if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-145"]="Who that cares much to know the history of man, and how the mysterious mixture behaves under the varying experiments of Time, has not dwelt, at least briefly, on the life of Saint Theresa, has not smiled with some gentleness at the thought of the little girl walking forth one morning hand-in-hand with her still smaller brother, to go and seek martyrdom in the country of the Moors? Out they toddled from rugged Avila, wide-eyed and helpless-looking as two fawns, but with human hearts, already beating to a national idea; until domestic reality met them in the shape of uncles, and turned them back from their great resolve. That child-pilgrimage was a fit beginning. Theresa’s passionate, ideal nature demanded an epic life: what were many-volumed romances of chivalry and the social conquests of a brilliant girl to her? Her flame quickly burned up that light fuel; and, fed from within, soared after some illimitable satisfaction, some object which would never justify weariness, which would reconcile self-despair with the rapturous consciousness of life beyond self. She found her epos in the reform of a religious order.\n\nThat Spanish woman who lived three hundred years ago, was certainly not the last of her kind. Many Theresas have been born who found for themselves no epic life wherein there was a constant unfolding of far-resonant action; perhaps only a life of mistakes, the offspring of a certain spiritual grandeur ill-matched with the meanness of opportunity; perhaps a tragic failure which found no sacred poet and sank unwept into oblivion. With dim lights and tangled circumstance they tried to shape their thought and deed in noble agreement; but after all, to common eyes their struggles seemed mere inconsistency and formlessness; for these later-born Theresas were helped by no coherent social faith and order which could perform the function of knowledge for the ardently willing soul. Their ardor alternated between a vague ideal and the common yearning of womanhood; so that the one was disapproved as extravagance, and the other condemned as a lapse.\n\nSome have felt that these blundering lives are due to the inconvenient indefiniteness with which the Supreme Power has fashioned the natures of women: if there were one level of feminine incompetence as strict as the ability to count three and no more, the social lot of women might be treated with scientific certitude. Meanwhile the indefiniteness remains, and the limits of variation are really much wider than any one would imagine from the sameness of women’s coiffure and the favorite love-stories in prose and verse. Here and there a cygnet is reared uneasily among the ducklings in the brown pond, and never finds the living stream in fellowship with its own oary-footed kind. Here and there is born a Saint Theresa, foundress of nothing, whose loving heart-beats and sobs after an unattained goodness tremble off and are dispersed among hindrances, instead of centring in some long-recognizable deed.\n\n# BOOK I. MISS BROOKE. CHAPTER I.\n\nSince I can do no good because a woman, Reach constantly at something that is near it. —The Maid’s Tragedy: BEAUMONT AND FLETCHER.\n\nMiss Brooke had that kind of beauty which seems to be thrown into relief by poor dress. Her hand and wrist were so finely formed that she could wear sleeves not less bare of style than those in which the Blessed Virgin appeared to Italian painters; and her profile as well as her stature and bearing seemed to gain the more dignity from her plain garments, which by the side of provincial fashion gave her the impressiveness of a fine quotation from the Bible,—or from one of our elder poets,—in a paragraph of to-day’s newspaper. She was usually spoken of as being remarkably clever, but with the addition that her sister Celia had more common-sense. Nevertheless, Celia wore scarcely more trimmings; and it was only to close observers that her dress differed from her sister’s, and had a shade of coquetry in its arrangements; for Miss Brooke’s plain dressing was due to mixed conditions, in most of which her sister shared. The pride of being ladies had something to do with it: the Brooke connections, though not exactly aristocratic, were unquestionably “good:” if you inquired backward for a generation or two, you would not find any yard-measuring or parcel-tying forefathers—anything lower than an admiral or a clergyman; and there was even an ancestor discernible as a Puritan gentleman who served under Cromwell, but afterwards conformed, and managed to come out of all political troubles as the proprietor of a respectable family estate. Young women of such birth, living in a quiet country-house, and attending a village church hardly larger than a parlor, naturally regarded frippery as the ambition of a huckster’s daughter. Then there was well-bred economy, which in those days made show in dress the first item to be deducted from, when any margin was required for expenses more distinctive of rank. Such reasons would have been enough to account for plain dress, quite apart from religious feeling; but in Miss Brooke’s case, religion alone would have determined it; and Celia mildly acquiesced in all her sister’s sentiments, only infusing them with that common-sense which is able to accept momentous doctrines without any eccentric agitation. Dorothea knew many passages of Pascal’s Pensees and of Jeremy Taylor by heart; and to her the destinies of mankind, seen by the light of Christianity, made the solicitudes of feminine fashion appear an occupation for Bedlam. She could not reconcile the anxieties of a spiritual life involving eternal consequences, with a keen interest in gimp and artificial protrusions of drapery. Her mind was theoretic, and yearned by its nature after some lofty conception of the world which might frankly include the parish of Tipton and her own rule of conduct there; she was enamoured of intensity and greatness, and rash in embracing whatever seemed to her to have those aspects; likely to seek martyrdom, to make retractations, and then to incur martyrdom after all in a quarter where she had not sought it. Certainly such elements in the character of a marriageable girl tended to interfere with her lot, and hinder it from being decided according to custom, by good looks, vanity, and merely canine affection. With all this, she, the elder of the sisters, was not yet twenty, and they had both been educated, since they were about twelve years old and had lost their parents, on plans at once narrow and promiscuous, first in an English family and afterwards in a Swiss family at Lausanne, their bachelor uncle and guardian trying in this way to remedy the disadvantages of their orphaned condition.\n\nIt was hardly a year since they had come to live at Tipton Grange with their uncle, a man nearly sixty, of acquiescent temper, miscellaneous opinions, and uncertain vote. He had travelled in his younger years, and was held in this part of the county to have contracted a too rambling habit of mind. Mr. Brooke’s conclusions were as difficult to predict as the weather: it was only safe to say that he would act with benevolent intentions, and that he would spend as little money as possible in carrying them out. For the most glutinously indefinite minds enclose some hard grains of habit; and a man has been seen lax about all his own interests except the retention of his snuff-box, concerning which he was watchful, suspicious, and greedy of clutch.\n\nIn Mr. Brooke the hereditary strain of Puritan energy was clearly in abeyance; but in his niece Dorothea it glowed alike through faults and virtues, turning sometimes into impatience of her uncle’s talk or his way of “letting things be” on his estate, and making her long all the more for the time when she would be of age and have some command of money for generous schemes. She was regarded as an heiress; for not only had the sisters seven hundred a-year each from their parents, but if Dorothea married and had a son, that son would inherit Mr. Brooke’s estate, presumably worth about three thousand a-year—a rental which seemed wealth to provincial families, still discussing Mr. Peel’s late conduct on the Catholic question, innocent of future gold-fields, and of that gorgeous plutocracy which has so nobly exalted the necessities of genteel life.\n\nAnd how should Dorothea not marry?—a girl so handsome and with such prospects? Nothing could hinder it but her love of extremes, and her insistence on regulating life according to notions which might cause a wary man to hesitate before he made her an offer, or even might lead her at last to refuse all offers. A young lady of some birth and fortune, who knelt suddenly down on a brick floor by the side of a sick laborer and prayed fervidly as if she thought herself living in the time of the Apostles—who had strange whims of fasting like a Papist, and of sitting up at night to read old theological books! Such a wife might awaken you some fine morning with a new scheme for the application of her income which would interfere with political economy and the keeping of saddle-horses: a man would naturally think twice before he risked himself in such fellowship. Women were expected to have weak opinions; but the great safeguard of society and of domestic life was, that opinions were not acted on. Sane people did what their neighbors did, so that if any lunatics were at large, one might know and avoid them.\n\nThe rural opinion about the new young ladies, even among the cottagers, was generally in favor of Celia, as being so amiable and innocent-looking, while Miss Brooke’s large eyes seemed, like her religion, too unusual and striking. Poor Dorothea! compared with her, the innocent-looking Celia was knowing and worldly-wise; so much subtler is a human mind than the outside tissues which make a sort of blazonry or clock-face for it.\n\nYet those who approached Dorothea, though prejudiced against her by this alarming hearsay, found that she had a charm unaccountably reconcilable with it. Most men thought her bewitching when she was on horseback. She loved the fresh air and the various aspects of the country, and when her eyes and cheeks glowed with mingled pleasure she looked very little like a devotee. Riding was an indulgence which she allowed herself in spite of conscientious qualms; she felt that she enjoyed it in a pagan sensuous way, and always looked forward to renouncing it.\n\nShe was open, ardent, and not in the least self-admiring; indeed, it was pretty to see how her imagination adorned her sister Celia with attractions altogether superior to her own, and if any gentleman appeared to come to the Grange from some other motive than that of seeing Mr. Brooke, she concluded that he must be in love with Celia: Sir James Chettam, for example, whom she constantly considered from Celia’s point of view, inwardly debating whether it would be good for Celia to accept him. That he should be regarded as a suitor to herself would have seemed to her a ridiculous irrelevance. Dorothea, with all her eagerness to know the truths of life, retained very childlike ideas about marriage. She felt sure that she would have accepted the judicious Hooker, if she had been born in time to save him from that wretched mistake he made in matrimony; or John Milton when his blindness had come on; or any of the other great men whose odd habits it would have been glorious piety to endure; but an amiable handsome baronet, who said “Exactly” to her remarks even when she expressed uncertainty,—how could he affect her as a lover? The really delightful marriage must be that where your husband was a sort of father, and could teach you even Hebrew, if you wished it.\n\nThese peculiarities of Dorothea’s character caused Mr. Brooke to be all the more blamed in neighboring families for not securing some middle-aged lady as guide and companion to his nieces. But he himself dreaded so much the sort of superior woman likely to be available for such a position, that he allowed himself to be dissuaded by Dorothea’s objections, and was in this case brave enough to defy the world—that is to say, Mrs. Cadwallader the Rector’s wife, and the small group of gentry with whom he visited in the northeast corner of Loamshire. So Miss Brooke presided in her uncle’s household, and did not at all dislike her new authority, with the homage that belonged to it.\n\nSir James Chettam was going to dine at the Grange to-day with another gentleman whom the girls had never seen, and about whom Dorothea felt some venerating expectation. This was the Reverend Edward Casaubon, noted in the county as a man of profound learning, understood for many years to be engaged on a great work concerning religious history; also as a man of wealth enough to give lustre to his piety, and having views of his own which were to be more clearly ascertained on the publication of his book. His very name carried an impressiveness hardly to be measured without a precise chronology of scholarship.\n\nEarly in the day Dorothea had returned from the infant school which she had set going in the village, and was taking her usual place in the pretty sitting-room which divided the bedrooms of the sisters, bent on finishing a plan for some buildings (a kind of work which she delighted in), when Celia, who had been watching her with a hesitating desire to propose something, said—\n\n“Dorothea, dear, if you don’t mind—if you are not very busy—suppose we looked at mamma’s jewels to-day, and divided them? It is exactly six months to-day since uncle gave them to you, and you have not looked at them yet.”\n\nCelia’s face had the shadow of a pouting expression in it, the full presence of the pout being kept back by an habitual awe of Dorothea and principle; two associated facts which might show a mysterious electricity if you touched them incautiously. To her relief, Dorothea’s eyes were full of laughter as she looked up.\n\n“What a wonderful little almanac you are, Celia! Is it six calendar or six lunar months?”\n\n“It is the last day of September now, and it was the first of April when uncle gave them to you. You know, he said that he had forgotten them till then. I believe you have never thought of them since you locked them up in the cabinet here.”\n\n“Well, dear, we should never wear them, you know.” Dorothea spoke in a full cordial tone, half caressing, half explanatory. She had her pencil in her hand, and was making tiny side-plans on a margin.\n\nCelia colored, and looked very grave. “I think, dear, we are wanting in respect to mamma’s memory, to put them by and take no notice of them. And,” she added, after hesitating a little, with a rising sob of mortification, “necklaces are quite usual now; and Madame Poincon, who was stricter in some things even than you are, used to wear ornaments. And Christians generally—surely there are women in heaven now who wore jewels.” Celia was conscious of some mental strength when she really applied herself to argument.\n\n“You would like to wear them?” exclaimed Dorothea, an air of astonished discovery animating her whole person with a dramatic action which she had caught from that very Madame Poincon who wore the ornaments. “Of course, then, let us have them out. Why did you not tell me before? But the keys, the keys!” She pressed her hands against the sides of her head and seemed to despair of her memory.\n\n“They are here,” said Celia, with whom this explanation had been long meditated and prearranged.\n\n“Pray open the large drawer of the cabinet and get out the jewel-box.”\n\nThe casket was soon open before them, and the various jewels spread out, making a bright parterre on the table. It was no great collection, but a few of the ornaments were really of remarkable beauty, the finest that was obvious at first being a necklace of purple amethysts set in exquisite gold work, and a pearl cross with five brilliants in it. Dorothea immediately took up the necklace and fastened it round her sister’s neck, where it fitted almost as closely as a bracelet; but the circle suited the Henrietta-Maria style of Celia’s head and neck, and she could see that it did, in the pier-glass opposite.\n\n“There, Celia! you can wear that with your Indian muslin. But this cross you must wear with your dark dresses.”\n\nCelia was trying not to smile with pleasure. “O Dodo, you must keep the cross yourself.”\n\n“No, no, dear, no,” said Dorothea, putting up her hand with careless deprecation.\n\n“Yes, indeed you must; it would suit you—in your black dress, now,” said Celia, insistingly. “You might wear that.”\n\n“Not for the world, not for the world. A cross is the last thing I would wear as a trinket.” Dorothea shuddered slightly.\n\n“Then you will think it wicked in me to wear it,” said Celia, uneasily.\n\n“No, dear, no,” said Dorothea, stroking her sister’s cheek. “Souls have complexions too: what will suit one will not suit another.”\n\n“But you might like to keep it for mamma’s sake.”\n\n“No, I have other things of mamma’s—her sandal-wood box which I am so fond of—plenty of things. In fact, they are all yours, dear. We need discuss them no longer. There—take away your property.”\n\nCelia felt a little hurt. There was a strong assumption of superiority in this Puritanic toleration, hardly less trying to the blond flesh of an unenthusiastic sister than a Puritanic persecution.\n\n“But how can I wear ornaments if you, who are the elder sister, will never wear them?”\n\n“Nay, Celia, that is too much to ask, that I should wear trinkets to keep you in countenance. If I were to put on such a necklace as that, I should feel as if I had been pirouetting. The world would go round with me, and I should not know how to walk.”\n\nCelia had unclasped the necklace and drawn it off. “It would be a little tight for your neck; something to lie down and hang would suit you better,” she said, with some satisfaction. The complete unfitness of the necklace from all points of view for Dorothea, made Celia happier in taking it. She was opening some ring-boxes, which disclosed a fine emerald with diamonds, and just then the sun passing beyond a cloud sent a bright gleam over the table.\n\n“How very beautiful these gems are!” said Dorothea, under a new current of feeling, as sudden as the gleam. “It is strange how deeply colors seem to penetrate one, like scent. I suppose that is the reason why gems are used as spiritual emblems in the Revelation of St. John. They look like fragments of heaven. I think that emerald is more beautiful than any of them.”\n\n“And there is a bracelet to match it,” said Celia. “We did not notice this at first.”\n\n“They are lovely,” said Dorothea, slipping the ring and bracelet on her finely turned finger and wrist, and holding them towards the window on a level with her eyes. All the while her thought was trying to justify her delight in the colors by merging them in her mystic religious joy.\n\n“You would like those, Dorothea,” said Celia, rather falteringly, beginning to think with wonder that her sister showed some weakness, and also that emeralds would suit her own complexion even better than purple amethysts. “You must keep that ring and bracelet—if nothing else. But see, these agates are very pretty and quiet.”\n\n“Yes! I will keep these—this ring and bracelet,” said Dorothea. Then, letting her hand fall on the table, she said in another tone—“Yet what miserable men find such things, and work at them, and sell them!” She paused again, and Celia thought that her sister was going to renounce the ornaments, as in consistency she ought to do.\n\n“Yes, dear, I will keep these,” said Dorothea, decidedly. “But take all the rest away, and the casket.”\n\nShe took up her pencil without removing the jewels, and still looking at them. She thought of often having them by her, to feed her eye at these little fountains of pure color.\n\n“Shall you wear them in company?” said Celia, who was watching her with real curiosity as to what she would do.\n\nDorothea glanced quickly at her sister. Across all her imaginative adornment of those whom she loved, there darted now and then a keen discernment, which was not without a scorching quality. If Miss Brooke ever attained perfect meekness, it would not be for lack of inward fire.\n\n“Perhaps,” she said, rather haughtily. “I cannot tell to what level I may sink.”\n\nCelia blushed, and was unhappy: she saw that she had offended her sister, and dared not say even anything pretty about the gift of the ornaments which she put back into the box and carried away. Dorothea too was unhappy, as she went on with her plan-drawing, questioning the purity of her own feeling and speech in the scene which had ended with that little explosion.\n\nCelia’s consciousness told her that she had not been at all in the wrong: it was quite natural and justifiable that she should have asked that question, and she repeated to herself that Dorothea was inconsistent: either she should have taken her full share of the jewels, or, after what she had said, she should have renounced them altogether.\n\n“I am sure—at least, I trust,” thought Celia, “that the wearing of a necklace will not interfere with my prayers. And I do not see that I should be bound by Dorothea’s opinions now we are going into society, though of course she herself ought to be bound by them. But Dorothea is not always consistent.”\n\nThus Celia, mutely bending over her tapestry, until she heard her sister calling her.\n\n“Here, Kitty, come and look at my plan; I shall think I am a great architect, if I have not got incompatible stairs and fireplaces.”\n\nAs Celia bent over the paper, Dorothea put her cheek against her sister’s arm caressingly. Celia understood the action. Dorothea saw that she had been in the wrong, and Celia pardoned her. Since they could remember, there had been a mixture of criticism and awe in the attitude of Celia’s mind towards her elder sister. The younger had always worn a yoke; but is there any yoked creature without its private opinions?\n\n# CHAPTER II.\n\n“‘Dime; no ves aquel caballero que hacia nosotros viene sobre un caballo rucio rodado que trae puesto en la cabeza un yelmo de oro?’ ‘Lo que veo y columbro,’ respondio Sancho, ‘no es sino un hombre sobre un as no pardo como el mio, que trae sobre la cabeza una cosa que relumbra.’ ‘Pues ese es el yelmo de Mambrino,’ dijo Don Quijote.”—CERVANTES.\n\n“‘Seest thou not yon cavalier who cometh toward us on a dapple-gray steed, and weareth a golden helmet?’ ‘What I see,’ answered Sancho, ‘is nothing but a man on a gray ass like my own, who carries something shiny on his head.’ ‘Just so,’ answered Don Quixote: ‘and that resplendent object is the helmet of Mambrino.’”\n\n“Sir Humphry Davy?” said Mr. Brooke, over the soup, in his easy smiling way, taking up Sir James Chettam’s remark that he was studying Davy’s Agricultural Chemistry. “Well, now, Sir Humphry Davy; I dined with him years ago at Cartwright’s, and Wordsworth was there too—the poet Wordsworth, you know. Now there was something singular. I was at Cambridge when Wordsworth was there, and I never met him—and I dined with him twenty years afterwards at Cartwright’s. There’s an oddity in things, now. But Davy was there: he was a poet too. Or, as I may say, Wordsworth was poet one, and Davy was poet two. That was true in every sense, you know.”\n\nDorothea felt a little more uneasy than usual. In the beginning of dinner, the party being small and the room still, these motes from the mass of a magistrate’s mind fell too noticeably. She wondered how a man like Mr. Casaubon would support such triviality. His manners, she thought, were very dignified; the set of his iron-gray hair and his deep eye-sockets made him resemble the portrait of Locke. He had the spare form and the pale complexion which became a student; as different as possible from the blooming Englishman of the red-whiskered type represented by Sir James Chettam.\n\n“I am reading the Agricultural Chemistry,” said this excellent baronet, “because I am going to take one of the farms into my own hands, and see if something cannot be done in setting a good pattern of farming among my tenants. Do you approve of that, Miss Brooke?”\n\n“A great mistake, Chettam,” interposed Mr. Brooke, “going into electrifying your land and that kind of thing, and making a parlor of your cow-house. It won’t do. I went into science a great deal myself at one time; but I saw it would not do. It leads to everything; you can let nothing alone. No, no—see that your tenants don’t sell their straw, and that kind of thing; and give them draining-tiles, you know. But your fancy farming will not do—the most expensive sort of whistle you can buy: you may as well keep a pack of hounds.”\n\n“Surely,” said Dorothea, “it is better to spend money in finding out how men can make the most of the land which supports them all, than in keeping dogs and horses only to gallop over it. It is not a sin to make yourself poor in performing experiments for the good of all.”\n\nShe spoke with more energy than is expected of so young a lady, but Sir James had appealed to her. He was accustomed to do so, and she had often thought that she could urge him to many good actions when he was her brother-in-law.\n\nMr. Casaubon turned his eyes very markedly on Dorothea while she was speaking, and seemed to observe her newly.\n\n“Young ladies don’t understand political economy, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, smiling towards Mr. Casaubon. “I remember when we were all reading Adam Smith. There is a book, now. I took in all the new ideas at one time—human perfectibility, now. But some say, history moves in circles; and that may be very well argued; I have argued it myself. The fact is, human reason may carry you a little too far—over the hedge, in fact. It carried me a good way at one time; but I saw it would not do. I pulled up; I pulled up in time. But not too hard. I have always been in favor of a little theory: we must have Thought; else we shall be landed back in the dark ages. But talking of books, there is Southey’s ‘Peninsular War.’ I am reading that of a morning. You know Southey?”\n\n“No,” said Mr. Casaubon, not keeping pace with Mr. Brooke’s impetuous reason, and thinking of the book only. “I have little leisure for such literature just now. I have been using up my eyesight on old characters lately; the fact is, I want a reader for my evenings; but I am fastidious in voices, and I cannot endure listening to an imperfect reader. It is a misfortune, in some senses: I feed too much on the inward sources; I live too much with the dead. My mind is something like the ghost of an ancient, wandering about the world and trying mentally to construct it as it used to be, in spite of ruin and confusing changes. But I find it necessary to use the utmost caution about my eyesight.”\n\nThis was the first time that Mr. Casaubon had spoken at any length. He delivered himself with precision, as if he had been called upon to make a public statement; and the balanced sing-song neatness of his speech, occasionally corresponded to by a movement of his head, was the more conspicuous from its contrast with good Mr. Brooke’s scrappy slovenliness. Dorothea said to herself that Mr. Casaubon was the most interesting man she had ever seen, not excepting even Monsieur Liret, the Vaudois clergyman who had given conferences on the history of the Waldenses. To reconstruct a past world, doubtless with a view to the highest purposes of truth—what a work to be in any way present at, to assist in, though only as a lamp-holder! This elevating thought lifted her above her annoyance at being twitted with her ignorance of political economy, that never-explained science which was thrust as an extinguisher over all her lights.\n\n“But you are fond of riding, Miss Brooke,” Sir James presently took an opportunity of saying. “I should have thought you would enter a little into the pleasures of hunting. I wish you would let me send over a chestnut horse for you to try. It has been trained for a lady. I saw you on Saturday cantering over the hill on a nag not worthy of you. My groom shall bring Corydon for you every day, if you will only mention the time.”\n\n“Thank you, you are very good. I mean to give up riding. I shall not ride any more,” said Dorothea, urged to this brusque resolution by a little annoyance that Sir James would be soliciting her attention when she wanted to give it all to Mr. Casaubon.\n\n“No, that is too hard,” said Sir James, in a tone of reproach that showed strong interest. “Your sister is given to self-mortification, is she not?” he continued, turning to Celia, who sat at his right hand.\n\n“I think she is,” said Celia, feeling afraid lest she should say something that would not please her sister, and blushing as prettily as possible above her necklace. “She likes giving up.”\n\n“If that were true, Celia, my giving-up would be self-indulgence, not self-mortification. But there may be good reasons for choosing not to do what is very agreeable,” said Dorothea.\n\nMr. Brooke was speaking at the same time, but it was evident that Mr. Casaubon was observing Dorothea, and she was aware of it.\n\n“Exactly,” said Sir James. “You give up from some high, generous motive.”\n\n“No, indeed, not exactly. I did not say that of myself,” answered Dorothea, reddening. Unlike Celia, she rarely blushed, and only from high delight or anger. At this moment she felt angry with the perverse Sir James. Why did he not pay attention to Celia, and leave her to listen to Mr. Casaubon?—if that learned man would only talk, instead of allowing himself to be talked to by Mr. Brooke, who was just then informing him that the Reformation either meant something or it did not, that he himself was a Protestant to the core, but that Catholicism was a fact; and as to refusing an acre of your ground for a Romanist chapel, all men needed the bridle of religion, which, properly speaking, was the dread of a Hereafter.\n\n“I made a great study of theology at one time,” said Mr. Brooke, as if to explain the insight just manifested. “I know something of all schools. I knew Wilberforce in his best days. Do you know Wilberforce?”\n\nMr. Casaubon said, “No.”\n\n“Well, Wilberforce was perhaps not enough of a thinker; but if I went into Parliament, as I have been asked to do, I should sit on the independent bench, as Wilberforce did, and work at philanthropy.”\n\nMr. Casaubon bowed, and observed that it was a wide field.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. Brooke, with an easy smile, “but I have documents. I began a long while ago to collect documents. They want arranging, but when a question has struck me, I have written to somebody and got an answer. I have documents at my back. But now, how do you arrange your documents?”\n\n“In pigeon-holes partly,” said Mr. Casaubon, with rather a startled air of effort.\n\n“Ah, pigeon-holes will not do. I have tried pigeon-holes, but everything gets mixed in pigeon-holes: I never know whether a paper is in A or Z.”\n\n“I wish you would let me sort your papers for you, uncle,” said Dorothea. “I would letter them all, and then make a list of subjects under each letter.”\n\nMr. Casaubon gravely smiled approval, and said to Mr. Brooke, “You have an excellent secretary at hand, you perceive.”\n\n“No, no,” said Mr. Brooke, shaking his head; “I cannot let young ladies meddle with my documents. Young ladies are too flighty.”\n\nDorothea felt hurt. Mr. Casaubon would think that her uncle had some special reason for delivering this opinion, whereas the remark lay in his mind as lightly as the broken wing of an insect among all the other fragments there, and a chance current had sent it alighting on her.\n\nWhen the two girls were in the drawing-room alone, Celia said—\n\n“How very ugly Mr. Casaubon is!”\n\n“Celia! He is one of the most distinguished-looking men I ever saw. He is remarkably like the portrait of Locke. He has the same deep eye-sockets.”\n\n“Had Locke those two white moles with hairs on them?”\n\n“Oh, I dare say! when people of a certain sort looked at him,” said Dorothea, walking away a little.\n\n“Mr. Casaubon is so sallow.”\n\n“All the better. I suppose you admire a man with the complexion of a cochon de lait.”\n\n“Dodo!” exclaimed Celia, looking after her in surprise. “I never heard you make such a comparison before.”\n\n“Why should I make it before the occasion came? It is a good comparison: the match is perfect.”\n\nMiss Brooke was clearly forgetting herself, and Celia thought so.\n\n“I wonder you show temper, Dorothea.”\n\n“It is so painful in you, Celia, that you will look at human beings as if they were merely animals with a toilet, and never see the great soul in a man’s face.”\n\n“Has Mr. Casaubon a great soul?” Celia was not without a touch of naive malice.\n\n“Yes, I believe he has,” said Dorothea, with the full voice of decision. “Everything I see in him corresponds to his pamphlet on Biblical Cosmology.”\n\n“He talks very little,” said Celia\n\n“There is no one for him to talk to.”\n\nCelia thought privately, “Dorothea quite despises Sir James Chettam; I believe she would not accept him.” Celia felt that this was a pity. She had never been deceived as to the object of the baronet’s interest. Sometimes, indeed, she had reflected that Dodo would perhaps not make a husband happy who had not her way of looking at things; and stifled in the depths of her heart was the feeling that her sister was too religious for family comfort. Notions and scruples were like spilt needles, making one afraid of treading, or sitting down, or even eating.\n\nWhen Miss Brooke was at the tea-table, Sir James came to sit down by her, not having felt her mode of answering him at all offensive. Why should he? He thought it probable that Miss Brooke liked him, and manners must be very marked indeed before they cease to be interpreted by preconceptions either confident or distrustful. She was thoroughly charming to him, but of course he theorized a little about his attachment. He was made of excellent human dough, and had the rare merit of knowing that his talents, even if let loose, would not set the smallest stream in the county on fire: hence he liked the prospect of a wife to whom he could say, “What shall we do?” about this or that; who could help her husband out with reasons, and would also have the property qualification for doing so. As to the excessive religiousness alleged against Miss Brooke, he had a very indefinite notion of what it consisted in, and thought that it would die out with marriage. In short, he felt himself to be in love in the right place, and was ready to endure a great deal of predominance, which, after all, a man could always put down when he liked. Sir James had no idea that he should ever like to put down the predominance of this handsome girl, in whose cleverness he delighted. Why not? A man’s mind—what there is of it—has always the advantage of being masculine,—as the smallest birch-tree is of a higher kind than the most soaring palm,—and even his ignorance is of a sounder quality. Sir James might not have originated this estimate; but a kind Providence furnishes the limpest personality with a little gum or starch in the form of tradition.\n\n“Let me hope that you will rescind that resolution about the horse, Miss Brooke,” said the persevering admirer. “I assure you, riding is the most healthy of exercises.”\n\n“I am aware of it,” said Dorothea, coldly. “I think it would do Celia good—if she would take to it.”\n\n“But you are such a perfect horsewoman.”\n\n“Excuse me; I have had very little practice, and I should be easily thrown.”\n\n“Then that is a reason for more practice. Every lady ought to be a perfect horsewoman, that she may accompany her husband.”\n\n“You see how widely we differ, Sir James. I have made up my mind that I ought not to be a perfect horsewoman, and so I should never correspond to your pattern of a lady.” Dorothea looked straight before her, and spoke with cold brusquerie, very much with the air of a handsome boy, in amusing contrast with the solicitous amiability of her admirer.\n\n“I should like to know your reasons for this cruel resolution. It is not possible that you should think horsemanship wrong.”\n\n“It is quite possible that I should think it wrong for me.”\n\n“Oh, why?” said Sir James, in a tender tone of remonstrance.\n\nMr. Casaubon had come up to the table, teacup in hand, and was listening.\n\n“We must not inquire too curiously into motives,” he interposed, in his measured way. “Miss Brooke knows that they are apt to become feeble in the utterance: the aroma is mixed with the grosser air. We must keep the germinating grain away from the light.”\n\nDorothea colored with pleasure, and looked up gratefully to the speaker. Here was a man who could understand the higher inward life, and with whom there could be some spiritual communion; nay, who could illuminate principle with the widest knowledge: a man whose learning almost amounted to a proof of whatever he believed!\n\nDorothea’s inferences may seem large; but really life could never have gone on at any period but for this liberal allowance of conclusions, which has facilitated marriage under the difficulties of civilization. Has any one ever pinched into its pilulous smallness the cobweb of pre-matrimonial acquaintanceship?\n\n“Certainly,” said good Sir James. “Miss Brooke shall not be urged to tell reasons she would rather be silent upon. I am sure her reasons would do her honor.”\n\nHe was not in the least jealous of the interest with which Dorothea had looked up at Mr. Casaubon: it never occurred to him that a girl to whom he was meditating an offer of marriage could care for a dried bookworm towards fifty, except, indeed, in a religious sort of way, as for a clergyman of some distinction.\n\nHowever, since Miss Brooke had become engaged in a conversation with Mr. Casaubon about the Vaudois clergy, Sir James betook himself to Celia, and talked to her about her sister; spoke of a house in town, and asked whether Miss Brooke disliked London. Away from her sister, Celia talked quite easily, and Sir James said to himself that the second Miss Brooke was certainly very agreeable as well as pretty, though not, as some people pretended, more clever and sensible than the elder sister. He felt that he had chosen the one who was in all respects the superior; and a man naturally likes to look forward to having the best. He would be the very Mawworm of bachelors who pretended not to expect it.\n\n# CHAPTER III.\n\n“Say, goddess, what ensued, when Raphael, The affable archangel . . . Eve The story heard attentive, and was filled With admiration, and deep muse, to hear Of things so high and strange.” —Paradise Lost, B. vii.\n\nIf it had really occurred to Mr. Casaubon to think of Miss Brooke as a suitable wife for him, the reasons that might induce her to accept him were already planted in her mind, and by the evening of the next day the reasons had budded and bloomed. For they had had a long conversation in the morning, while Celia, who did not like the company of Mr. Casaubon’s moles and sallowness, had escaped to the vicarage to play with the curate’s ill-shod but merry children.\n\nDorothea by this time had looked deep into the ungauged reservoir of Mr. Casaubon’s mind, seeing reflected there in vague labyrinthine extension every quality she herself brought; had opened much of her own experience to him, and had understood from him the scope of his great work, also of attractively labyrinthine extent. For he had been as instructive as Milton’s “affable archangel;” and with something of the archangelic manner he told her how he had undertaken to show (what indeed had been attempted before, but not with that thoroughness, justice of comparison, and effectiveness of arrangement at which Mr. Casaubon aimed) that all the mythical systems or erratic mythical fragments in the world were corruptions of a tradition originally revealed. Having once mastered the true position and taken a firm footing there, the vast field of mythical constructions became intelligible, nay, luminous with the reflected light of correspondences. But to gather in this great harvest of truth was no light or speedy work. His notes already made a formidable range of volumes, but the crowning task would be to condense these voluminous still-accumulating results and bring them, like the earlier vintage of Hippocratic books, to fit a little shelf. In explaining this to Dorothea, Mr. Casaubon expressed himself nearly as he would have done to a fellow-student, for he had not two styles of talking at command: it is true that when he used a Greek or Latin phrase he always gave the English with scrupulous care, but he would probably have done this in any case. A learned provincial clergyman is accustomed to think of his acquaintances as of “lords, knyghtes, and other noble and worthi men, that conne Latyn but lytille.”\n\nDorothea was altogether captivated by the wide embrace of this conception. Here was something beyond the shallows of ladies’ school literature: here was a living Bossuet, whose work would reconcile complete knowledge with devoted piety; here was a modern Augustine who united the glories of doctor and saint.\n\nThe sanctity seemed no less clearly marked than the learning, for when Dorothea was impelled to open her mind on certain themes which she could speak of to no one whom she had before seen at Tipton, especially on the secondary importance of ecclesiastical forms and articles of belief compared with that spiritual religion, that submergence of self in communion with Divine perfection which seemed to her to be expressed in the best Christian books of widely distant ages, she found in Mr. Casaubon a listener who understood her at once, who could assure her of his own agreement with that view when duly tempered with wise conformity, and could mention historical examples before unknown to her.\n\n“He thinks with me,” said Dorothea to herself, “or rather, he thinks a whole world of which my thought is but a poor twopenny mirror. And his feelings too, his whole experience—what a lake compared with my little pool!”\n\nMiss Brooke argued from words and dispositions not less unhesitatingly than other young ladies of her age. Signs are small measurable things, but interpretations are illimitable, and in girls of sweet, ardent nature, every sign is apt to conjure up wonder, hope, belief, vast as a sky, and colored by a diffused thimbleful of matter in the shape of knowledge. They are not always too grossly deceived; for Sinbad himself may have fallen by good-luck on a true description, and wrong reasoning sometimes lands poor mortals in right conclusions: starting a long way off the true point, and proceeding by loops and zigzags, we now and then arrive just where we ought to be. Because Miss Brooke was hasty in her trust, it is not therefore clear that Mr. Casaubon was unworthy of it.\n\nHe stayed a little longer than he had intended, on a slight pressure of invitation from Mr. Brooke, who offered no bait except his own documents on machine-breaking and rick-burning. Mr. Casaubon was called into the library to look at these in a heap, while his host picked up first one and then the other to read aloud from in a skipping and uncertain way, passing from one unfinished passage to another with a “Yes, now, but here!” and finally pushing them all aside to open the journal of his youthful Continental travels.\n\n“Look here—here is all about Greece. Rhamnus, the ruins of Rhamnus—you are a great Grecian, now. I don’t know whether you have given much study to the topography. I spent no end of time in making out these things—Helicon, now. Here, now!—‘We started the next morning for Parnassus, the double-peaked Parnassus.’ All this volume is about Greece, you know,” Mr. Brooke wound up, rubbing his thumb transversely along the edges of the leaves as he held the book forward.\n\nMr. Casaubon made a dignified though somewhat sad audience; bowed in the right place, and avoided looking at anything documentary as far as possible, without showing disregard or impatience; mindful that this desultoriness was associated with the institutions of the country, and that the man who took him on this severe mental scamper was not only an amiable host, but a landholder and custos rotulorum. Was his endurance aided also by the reflection that Mr. Brooke was the uncle of Dorothea?\n\nCertainly he seemed more and more bent on making her talk to him, on drawing her out, as Celia remarked to herself; and in looking at her his face was often lit up by a smile like pale wintry sunshine. Before he left the next morning, while taking a pleasant walk with Miss Brooke along the gravelled terrace, he had mentioned to her that he felt the disadvantage of loneliness, the need of that cheerful companionship with which the presence of youth can lighten or vary the serious toils of maturity. And he delivered this statement with as much careful precision as if he had been a diplomatic envoy whose words would be attended with results. Indeed, Mr. Casaubon was not used to expect that he should have to repeat or revise his communications of a practical or personal kind. The inclinations which he had deliberately stated on the 2d of October he would think it enough to refer to by the mention of that date; judging by the standard of his own memory, which was a volume where a vide supra could serve instead of repetitions, and not the ordinary long-used blotting-book which only tells of forgotten writing. But in this case Mr. Casaubon’s confidence was not likely to be falsified, for Dorothea heard and retained what he said with the eager interest of a fresh young nature to which every variety in experience is an epoch.\n\nIt was three o’clock in the beautiful breezy autumn day when Mr. Casaubon drove off to his Rectory at Lowick, only five miles from Tipton; and Dorothea, who had on her bonnet and shawl, hurried along the shrubbery and across the park that she might wander through the bordering wood with no other visible companionship than that of Monk, the Great St. Bernard dog, who always took care of the young ladies in their walks. There had risen before her the girl’s vision of a possible future for herself to which she looked forward with trembling hope, and she wanted to wander on in that visionary future without interruption. She walked briskly in the brisk air, the color rose in her cheeks, and her straw bonnet (which our contemporaries might look at with conjectural curiosity as at an obsolete form of basket) fell a little backward. She would perhaps be hardly characterized enough if it were omitted that she wore her brown hair flatly braided and coiled behind so as to expose the outline of her head in a daring manner at a time when public feeling required the meagreness of nature to be dissimulated by tall barricades of frizzed curls and bows, never surpassed by any great race except the Feejeean. This was a trait of Miss Brooke’s asceticism. But there was nothing of an ascetic’s expression in her bright full eyes, as she looked before her, not consciously seeing, but absorbing into the intensity of her mood, the solemn glory of the afternoon with its long swathes of light between the far-off rows of limes, whose shadows touched each other.\n\nAll people, young or old (that is, all people in those ante-reform times), would have thought her an interesting object if they had referred the glow in her eyes and cheeks to the newly awakened ordinary images of young love: the illusions of Chloe about Strephon have been sufficiently consecrated in poetry, as the pathetic loveliness of all spontaneous trust ought to be. Miss Pippin adoring young Pumpkin, and dreaming along endless vistas of unwearying companionship, was a little drama which never tired our fathers and mothers, and had been put into all costumes. Let but Pumpkin have a figure which would sustain the disadvantages of the shortwaisted swallow-tail, and everybody felt it not only natural but necessary to the perfection of womanhood, that a sweet girl should be at once convinced of his virtue, his exceptional ability, and above all, his perfect sincerity. But perhaps no persons then living—certainly none in the neighborhood of Tipton—would have had a sympathetic understanding for the dreams of a girl whose notions about marriage took their color entirely from an exalted enthusiasm about the ends of life, an enthusiasm which was lit chiefly by its own fire, and included neither the niceties of the trousseau, the pattern of plate, nor even the honors and sweet joys of the blooming matron.\n\nIt had now entered Dorothea’s mind that Mr. Casaubon might wish to make her his wife, and the idea that he would do so touched her with a sort of reverential gratitude. How good of him—nay, it would be almost as if a winged messenger had suddenly stood beside her path and held out his hand towards her! For a long while she had been oppressed by the indefiniteness which hung in her mind, like a thick summer haze, over all her desire to make her life greatly effective. What could she do, what ought she to do?—she, hardly more than a budding woman, but yet with an active conscience and a great mental need, not to be satisfied by a girlish instruction comparable to the nibblings and judgments of a discursive mouse. With some endowment of stupidity and conceit, she might have thought that a Christian young lady of fortune should find her ideal of life in village charities, patronage of the humbler clergy, the perusal of “Female Scripture Characters,” unfolding the private experience of Sara under the Old Dispensation, and Dorcas under the New, and the care of her soul over her embroidery in her own boudoir—with a background of prospective marriage to a man who, if less strict than herself, as being involved in affairs religiously inexplicable, might be prayed for and seasonably exhorted. From such contentment poor Dorothea was shut out. The intensity of her religious disposition, the coercion it exercised over her life, was but one aspect of a nature altogether ardent, theoretic, and intellectually consequent: and with such a nature struggling in the bands of a narrow teaching, hemmed in by a social life which seemed nothing but a labyrinth of petty courses, a walled-in maze of small paths that led no whither, the outcome was sure to strike others as at once exaggeration and inconsistency. The thing which seemed to her best, she wanted to justify by the completest knowledge; and not to live in a pretended admission of rules which were never acted on. Into this soul-hunger as yet all her youthful passion was poured; the union which attracted her was one that would deliver her from her girlish subjection to her own ignorance, and give her the freedom of voluntary submission to a guide who would take her along the grandest path.\n\n“I should learn everything then,” she said to herself, still walking quickly along the bridle road through the wood. “It would be my duty to study that I might help him the better in his great works. There would be nothing trivial about our lives. Every-day things with us would mean the greatest things. It would be like marrying Pascal. I should learn to see the truth by the same light as great men have seen it by. And then I should know what to do, when I got older: I should see how it was possible to lead a grand life here—now—in England. I don’t feel sure about doing good in any way now: everything seems like going on a mission to a people whose language I don’t know;—unless it were building good cottages—there can be no doubt about that. Oh, I hope I should be able to get the people well housed in Lowick! I will draw plenty of plans while I have time.”\n\nDorothea checked herself suddenly with self-rebuke for the presumptuous way in which she was reckoning on uncertain events, but she was spared any inward effort to change the direction of her thoughts by the appearance of a cantering horseman round a turning of the road. The well-groomed chestnut horse and two beautiful setters could leave no doubt that the rider was Sir James Chettam. He discerned Dorothea, jumped off his horse at once, and, having delivered it to his groom, advanced towards her with something white on his arm, at which the two setters were barking in an excited manner.\n\n“How delightful to meet you, Miss Brooke,” he said, raising his hat and showing his sleekly waving blond hair. “It has hastened the pleasure I was looking forward to.”\n\nMiss Brooke was annoyed at the interruption. This amiable baronet, really a suitable husband for Celia, exaggerated the necessity of making himself agreeable to the elder sister. Even a prospective brother-in-law may be an oppression if he will always be presupposing too good an understanding with you, and agreeing with you even when you contradict him. The thought that he had made the mistake of paying his addresses to herself could not take shape: all her mental activity was used up in persuasions of another kind. But he was positively obtrusive at this moment, and his dimpled hands were quite disagreeable. Her roused temper made her color deeply, as she returned his greeting with some haughtiness.\n\nSir James interpreted the heightened color in the way most gratifying to himself, and thought he never saw Miss Brooke looking so handsome.\n\n“I have brought a little petitioner,” he said, “or rather, I have brought him to see if he will be approved before his petition is offered.” He showed the white object under his arm, which was a tiny Maltese puppy, one of nature’s most naive toys.\n\n“It is painful to me to see these creatures that are bred merely as pets,” said Dorothea, whose opinion was forming itself that very moment (as opinions will) under the heat of irritation.\n\n“Oh, why?” said Sir James, as they walked forward.\n\n“I believe all the petting that is given them does not make them happy. They are too helpless: their lives are too frail. A weasel or a mouse that gets its own living is more interesting. I like to think that the animals about us have souls something like our own, and either carry on their own little affairs or can be companions to us, like Monk here. Those creatures are parasitic.”\n\n“I am so glad I know that you do not like them,” said good Sir James. “I should never keep them for myself, but ladies usually are fond of these Maltese dogs. Here, John, take this dog, will you?”\n\nThe objectionable puppy, whose nose and eyes were equally black and expressive, was thus got rid of, since Miss Brooke decided that it had better not have been born. But she felt it necessary to explain.\n\n“You must not judge of Celia’s feeling from mine. I think she likes these small pets. She had a tiny terrier once, which she was very fond of. It made me unhappy, because I was afraid of treading on it. I am rather short-sighted.”\n\n“You have your own opinion about everything, Miss Brooke, and it is always a good opinion.”\n\nWhat answer was possible to such stupid complimenting?\n\n“Do you know, I envy you that,” Sir James said, as they continued walking at the rather brisk pace set by Dorothea.\n\n“I don’t quite understand what you mean.”\n\n“Your power of forming an opinion. I can form an opinion of persons. I know when I like people. But about other matters, do you know, I have often a difficulty in deciding. One hears very sensible things said on opposite sides.”\n\n“Or that seem sensible. Perhaps we don’t always discriminate between sense and nonsense.”\n\nDorothea felt that she was rather rude.\n\n“Exactly,” said Sir James. “But you seem to have the power of discrimination.”\n\n“On the contrary, I am often unable to decide. But that is from ignorance. The right conclusion is there all the same, though I am unable to see it.”\n\n“I think there are few who would see it more readily. Do you know, Lovegood was telling me yesterday that you had the best notion in the world of a plan for cottages—quite wonderful for a young lady, he thought. You had a real genus, to use his expression. He said you wanted Mr. Brooke to build a new set of cottages, but he seemed to think it hardly probable that your uncle would consent. Do you know, that is one of the things I wish to do—I mean, on my own estate. I should be so glad to carry out that plan of yours, if you would let me see it. Of course, it is sinking money; that is why people object to it. Laborers can never pay rent to make it answer. But, after all, it is worth doing.”\n\n“Worth doing! yes, indeed,” said Dorothea, energetically, forgetting her previous small vexations. “I think we deserve to be beaten out of our beautiful houses with a scourge of small cords—all of us who let tenants live in such sties as we see round us. Life in cottages might be happier than ours, if they were real houses fit for human beings from whom we expect duties and affections.”\n\n“Will you show me your plan?”\n\n“Yes, certainly. I dare say it is very faulty. But I have been examining all the plans for cottages in Loudon’s book, and picked out what seem the best things. Oh what a happiness it would be to set the pattern about here! I think instead of Lazarus at the gate, we should put the pigsty cottages outside the park-gate.”\n\nDorothea was in the best temper now. Sir James, as brother in-law, building model cottages on his estate, and then, perhaps, others being built at Lowick, and more and more elsewhere in imitation—it would be as if the spirit of Oberlin had passed over the parishes to make the life of poverty beautiful!\n\nSir James saw all the plans, and took one away to consult upon with Lovegood. He also took away a complacent sense that he was making great progress in Miss Brooke’s good opinion. The Maltese puppy was not offered to Celia; an omission which Dorothea afterwards thought of with surprise; but she blamed herself for it. She had been engrossing Sir James. After all, it was a relief that there was no puppy to tread upon.\n\nCelia was present while the plans were being examined, and observed Sir James’s illusion. “He thinks that Dodo cares about him, and she only cares about her plans. Yet I am not certain that she would refuse him if she thought he would let her manage everything and carry out all her notions. And how very uncomfortable Sir James would be! I cannot bear notions.”\n\nIt was Celia’s private luxury to indulge in this dislike. She dared not confess it to her sister in any direct statement, for that would be laying herself open to a demonstration that she was somehow or other at war with all goodness. But on safe opportunities, she had an indirect mode of making her negative wisdom tell upon Dorothea, and calling her down from her rhapsodic mood by reminding her that people were staring, not listening. Celia was not impulsive: what she had to say could wait, and came from her always with the same quiet staccato evenness. When people talked with energy and emphasis she watched their faces and features merely. She never could understand how well-bred persons consented to sing and open their mouths in the ridiculous manner requisite for that vocal exercise.\n\nIt was not many days before Mr. Casaubon paid a morning visit, on which he was invited again for the following week to dine and stay the night. Thus Dorothea had three more conversations with him, and was convinced that her first impressions had been just. He was all she had at first imagined him to be: almost everything he had said seemed like a specimen from a mine, or the inscription on the door of a museum which might open on the treasures of past ages; and this trust in his mental wealth was all the deeper and more effective on her inclination because it was now obvious that his visits were made for her sake. This accomplished man condescended to think of a young girl, and take the pains to talk to her, not with absurd compliment, but with an appeal to her understanding, and sometimes with instructive correction. What delightful companionship! Mr. Casaubon seemed even unconscious that trivialities existed, and never handed round that small-talk of heavy men which is as acceptable as stale bride-cake brought forth with an odor of cupboard. He talked of what he was interested in, or else he was silent and bowed with sad civility. To Dorothea this was adorable genuineness, and religious abstinence from that artificiality which uses up the soul in the efforts of pretence. For she looked as reverently at Mr. Casaubon’s religious elevation above herself as she did at his intellect and learning. He assented to her expressions of devout feeling, and usually with an appropriate quotation; he allowed himself to say that he had gone through some spiritual conflicts in his youth; in short, Dorothea saw that here she might reckon on understanding, sympathy, and guidance. On one—only one—of her favorite themes she was disappointed. Mr. Casaubon apparently did not care about building cottages, and diverted the talk to the extremely narrow accommodation which was to be had in the dwellings of the ancient Egyptians, as if to check a too high standard. After he was gone, Dorothea dwelt with some agitation on this indifference of his; and her mind was much exercised with arguments drawn from the varying conditions of climate which modify human needs, and from the admitted wickedness of pagan despots. Should she not urge these arguments on Mr. Casaubon when he came again? But further reflection told her that she was presumptuous in demanding his attention to such a subject; he would not disapprove of her occupying herself with it in leisure moments, as other women expected to occupy themselves with their dress and embroidery—would not forbid it when—Dorothea felt rather ashamed as she detected herself in these speculations. But her uncle had been invited to go to Lowick to stay a couple of days: was it reasonable to suppose that Mr. Casaubon delighted in Mr. Brooke’s society for its own sake, either with or without documents?\n\nMeanwhile that little disappointment made her delight the more in Sir James Chettam’s readiness to set on foot the desired improvements. He came much oftener than Mr. Casaubon, and Dorothea ceased to find him disagreeable since he showed himself so entirely in earnest; for he had already entered with much practical ability into Lovegood’s estimates, and was charmingly docile. She proposed to build a couple of cottages, and transfer two families from their old cabins, which could then be pulled down, so that new ones could be built on the old sites. Sir James said “Exactly,” and she bore the word remarkably well.\n\nCertainly these men who had so few spontaneous ideas might be very useful members of society under good feminine direction, if they were fortunate in choosing their sisters-in-law! It is difficult to say whether there was or was not a little wilfulness in her continuing blind to the possibility that another sort of choice was in question in relation to her. But her life was just now full of hope and action: she was not only thinking of her plans, but getting down learned books from the library and reading many things hastily (that she might be a little less ignorant in talking to Mr. Casaubon), all the while being visited with conscientious questionings whether she were not exalting these poor doings above measure and contemplating them with that self-satisfaction which was the last doom of ignorance and folly.\n\n1st Gent. Our deeds are fetters that we forge ourselves.\n\n2d Gent. Ay, truly: but I think it is the world That brings the iron.\n\n“Sir James seems determined to do everything you wish,” said Celia, as they were driving home from an inspection of the new building-site.\n\n“He is a good creature, and more sensible than any one would imagine,” said Dorothea, inconsiderately.\n\n“You mean that he appears silly.”\n\n“No, no,” said Dorothea, recollecting herself, and laying her hand on her sister’s a moment, “but he does not talk equally well on all subjects.”\n\n“I should think none but disagreeable people do,” said Celia, in her usual purring way. “They must be very dreadful to live with. Only think! at breakfast, and always.”\n\nDorothea laughed. “O Kitty, you are a wonderful creature!” She pinched Celia’s chin, being in the mood now to think her very winning and lovely—fit hereafter to be an eternal cherub, and if it were not doctrinally wrong to say so, hardly more in need of salvation than a squirrel. “Of course people need not be always talking well. Only one tells the quality of their minds when they try to talk well.”\n\n“You mean that Sir James tries and fails.”\n\n“I was speaking generally. Why do you catechise me about Sir James? It is not the object of his life to please me.”\n\n“Now, Dodo, can you really believe that?”\n\n“Certainly. He thinks of me as a future sister—that is all.” Dorothea had never hinted this before, waiting, from a certain shyness on such subjects which was mutual between the sisters, until it should be introduced by some decisive event. Celia blushed, but said at once—\n\n“Pray do not make that mistake any longer, Dodo. When Tantripp was brushing my hair the other day, she said that Sir James’s man knew from Mrs. Cadwallader’s maid that Sir James was to marry the eldest Miss Brooke.”\n\n“How can you let Tantripp talk such gossip to you, Celia?” said Dorothea, indignantly, not the less angry because details asleep in her memory were now awakened to confirm the unwelcome revelation. “You must have asked her questions. It is degrading.”\n\n“I see no harm at all in Tantripp’s talking to me. It is better to hear what people say. You see what mistakes you make by taking up notions. I am quite sure that Sir James means to make you an offer; and he believes that you will accept him, especially since you have been so pleased with him about the plans. And uncle too—I know he expects it. Every one can see that Sir James is very much in love with you.”\n\nThe revulsion was so strong and painful in Dorothea’s mind that the tears welled up and flowed abundantly. All her dear plans were embittered, and she thought with disgust of Sir James’s conceiving that she recognized him as her lover. There was vexation too on account of Celia.\n\n“How could he expect it?” she burst forth in her most impetuous manner. “I have never agreed with him about anything but the cottages: I was barely polite to him before.”\n\n“But you have been so pleased with him since then; he has begun to feel quite sure that you are fond of him.”\n\n“Fond of him, Celia! How can you choose such odious expressions?” said Dorothea, passionately.\n\n“Dear me, Dorothea, I suppose it would be right for you to be fond of a man whom you accepted for a husband.”\n\n“It is offensive to me to say that Sir James could think I was fond of him. Besides, it is not the right word for the feeling I must have towards the man I would accept as a husband.”\n\n“Well, I am sorry for Sir James. I thought it right to tell you, because you went on as you always do, never looking just where you are, and treading in the wrong place. You always see what nobody else sees; it is impossible to satisfy you; yet you never see what is quite plain. That’s your way, Dodo.” Something certainly gave Celia unusual courage; and she was not sparing the sister of whom she was occasionally in awe. Who can tell what just criticisms Murr the Cat may be passing on us beings of wider speculation?\n\n“It is very painful,” said Dorothea, feeling scourged. “I can have no more to do with the cottages. I must be uncivil to him. I must tell him I will have nothing to do with them. It is very painful.” Her eyes filled again with tears.\n\n“Wait a little. Think about it. You know he is going away for a day or two to see his sister. There will be nobody besides Lovegood.” Celia could not help relenting. “Poor Dodo,” she went on, in an amiable staccato. “It is very hard: it is your favorite fad to draw plans.”\n\n“Fad to draw plans! Do you think I only care about my fellow-creatures’ houses in that childish way? I may well make mistakes. How can one ever do anything nobly Christian, living among people with such petty thoughts?”\n\nNo more was said; Dorothea was too much jarred to recover her temper and behave so as to show that she admitted any error in herself. She was disposed rather to accuse the intolerable narrowness and the purblind conscience of the society around her: and Celia was no longer the eternal cherub, but a thorn in her spirit, a pink-and-white nullifidian, worse than any discouraging presence in the “Pilgrim’s Progress.” The fad of drawing plans! What was life worth—what great faith was possible when the whole effect of one’s actions could be withered up into such parched rubbish as that? When she got out of the carriage, her cheeks were pale and her eyelids red. She was an image of sorrow, and her uncle who met her in the hall would have been alarmed, if Celia had not been close to her looking so pretty and composed, that he at once concluded Dorothea’s tears to have their origin in her excessive religiousness. He had returned, during their absence, from a journey to the county town, about a petition for the pardon of some criminal.\n\n“Well, my dears,” he said, kindly, as they went up to kiss him, “I hope nothing disagreeable has happened while I have been away.”\n\n“No, uncle,” said Celia, “we have been to Freshitt to look at the cottages. We thought you would have been at home to lunch.”\n\n“I came by Lowick to lunch—you didn’t know I came by Lowick. And I have brought a couple of pamphlets for you, Dorothea—in the library, you know; they lie on the table in the library.”\n\nIt seemed as if an electric stream went through Dorothea, thrilling her from despair into expectation. They were pamphlets about the early Church. The oppression of Celia, Tantripp, and Sir James was shaken off, and she walked straight to the library. Celia went up-stairs. Mr. Brooke was detained by a message, but when he re-entered the library, he found Dorothea seated and already deep in one of the pamphlets which had some marginal manuscript of Mr. Casaubon’s,—taking it in as eagerly as she might have taken in the scent of a fresh bouquet after a dry, hot, dreary walk.\n\nShe was getting away from Tipton and Freshitt, and her own sad liability to tread in the wrong places on her way to the New Jerusalem.\n\nMr. Brooke sat down in his arm-chair, stretched his legs towards the wood-fire, which had fallen into a wondrous mass of glowing dice between the dogs, and rubbed his hands gently, looking very mildly towards Dorothea, but with a neutral leisurely air, as if he had nothing particular to say. Dorothea closed her pamphlet, as soon as she was aware of her uncle’s presence, and rose as if to go. Usually she would have been interested about her uncle’s merciful errand on behalf of the criminal, but her late agitation had made her absent-minded.\n\n“I came back by Lowick, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, not as if with any intention to arrest her departure, but apparently from his usual tendency to say what he had said before. This fundamental principle of human speech was markedly exhibited in Mr. Brooke. “I lunched there and saw Casaubon’s library, and that kind of thing. There’s a sharp air, driving. Won’t you sit down, my dear? You look cold.”\n\nDorothea felt quite inclined to accept the invitation. Some times, when her uncle’s easy way of taking things did not happen to be exasperating, it was rather soothing. She threw off her mantle and bonnet, and sat down opposite to him, enjoying the glow, but lifting up her beautiful hands for a screen. They were not thin hands, or small hands; but powerful, feminine, maternal hands. She seemed to be holding them up in propitiation for her passionate desire to know and to think, which in the unfriendly mediums of Tipton and Freshitt had issued in crying and red eyelids.\n\nShe bethought herself now of the condemned criminal. “What news have you brought about the sheep-stealer, uncle?”\n\n“What, poor Bunch?—well, it seems we can’t get him off—he is to be hanged.”\n\nDorothea’s brow took an expression of reprobation and pity.\n\n“Hanged, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, with a quiet nod. “Poor Romilly! he would have helped us. I knew Romilly. Casaubon didn’t know Romilly. He is a little buried in books, you know, Casaubon is.”\n\n“When a man has great studies and is writing a great work, he must of course give up seeing much of the world. How can he go about making acquaintances?”\n\n“That’s true. But a man mopes, you know. I have always been a bachelor too, but I have that sort of disposition that I never moped; it was my way to go about everywhere and take in everything. I never moped: but I can see that Casaubon does, you know. He wants a companion—a companion, you know.”\n\n“It would be a great honor to any one to be his companion,” said Dorothea, energetically.\n\n“You like him, eh?” said Mr. Brooke, without showing any surprise, or other emotion. “Well, now, I’ve known Casaubon ten years, ever since he came to Lowick. But I never got anything out of him—any ideas, you know. However, he is a tiptop man and may be a bishop—that kind of thing, you know, if Peel stays in. And he has a very high opinion of you, my dear.”\n\nDorothea could not speak.\n\n“The fact is, he has a very high opinion indeed of you. And he speaks uncommonly well—does Casaubon. He has deferred to me, you not being of age. In short, I have promised to speak to you, though I told him I thought there was not much chance. I was bound to tell him that. I said, my niece is very young, and that kind of thing. But I didn’t think it necessary to go into everything. However, the long and the short of it is, that he has asked my permission to make you an offer of marriage—of marriage, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, with his explanatory nod. “I thought it better to tell you, my dear.”\n\nNo one could have detected any anxiety in Mr. Brooke’s manner, but he did really wish to know something of his niece’s mind, that, if there were any need for advice, he might give it in time. What feeling he, as a magistrate who had taken in so many ideas, could make room for, was unmixedly kind. Since Dorothea did not speak immediately, he repeated, “I thought it better to tell you, my dear.”\n\n“Thank you, uncle,” said Dorothea, in a clear unwavering tone. “I am very grateful to Mr. Casaubon. If he makes me an offer, I shall accept him. I admire and honor him more than any man I ever saw.”\n\nMr. Brooke paused a little, and then said in a lingering low tone, “Ah? … Well! He is a good match in some respects. But now, Chettam is a good match. And our land lies together. I shall never interfere against your wishes, my dear. People should have their own way in marriage, and that sort of thing—up to a certain point, you know. I have always said that, up to a certain point. I wish you to marry well; and I have good reason to believe that Chettam wishes to marry you. I mention it, you know.”\n\n“It is impossible that I should ever marry Sir James Chettam,” said Dorothea. “If he thinks of marrying me, he has made a great mistake.”\n\n“That is it, you see. One never knows. I should have thought Chettam was just the sort of man a woman would like, now.”\n\n“Pray do not mention him in that light again, uncle,” said Dorothea, feeling some of her late irritation revive.\n\nMr. Brooke wondered, and felt that women were an inexhaustible subject of study, since even he at his age was not in a perfect state of scientific prediction about them. Here was a fellow like Chettam with no chance at all.\n\n“Well, but Casaubon, now. There is no hurry—I mean for you. It’s true, every year will tell upon him. He is over five-and-forty, you know. I should say a good seven-and-twenty years older than you. To be sure,—if you like learning and standing, and that sort of thing, we can’t have everything. And his income is good—he has a handsome property independent of the Church—his income is good. Still he is not young, and I must not conceal from you, my dear, that I think his health is not over-strong. I know nothing else against him.”\n\n“I should not wish to have a husband very near my own age,” said Dorothea, with grave decision. “I should wish to have a husband who was above me in judgment and in all knowledge.”\n\nMr. Brooke repeated his subdued, “Ah?—I thought you had more of your own opinion than most girls. I thought you liked your own opinion—liked it, you know.”\n\n“I cannot imagine myself living without some opinions, but I should wish to have good reasons for them, and a wise man could help me to see which opinions had the best foundation, and would help me to live according to them.”\n\n“Very true. You couldn’t put the thing better—couldn’t put it better, beforehand, you know. But there are oddities in things,” continued Mr. Brooke, whose conscience was really roused to do the best he could for his niece on this occasion. “Life isn’t cast in a mould—not cut out by rule and line, and that sort of thing. I never married myself, and it will be the better for you and yours. The fact is, I never loved any one well enough to put myself into a noose for them. It is a noose, you know. Temper, now. There is temper. And a husband likes to be master.”\n\n“I know that I must expect trials, uncle. Marriage is a state of higher duties. I never thought of it as mere personal ease,” said poor Dorothea.\n\n“Well, you are not fond of show, a great establishment, balls, dinners, that kind of thing. I can see that Casaubon’s ways might suit you better than Chettam’s. And you shall do as you like, my dear. I would not hinder Casaubon; I said so at once; for there is no knowing how anything may turn out. You have not the same tastes as every young lady; and a clergyman and scholar—who may be a bishop—that kind of thing—may suit you better than Chettam. Chettam is a good fellow, a good sound-hearted fellow, you know; but he doesn’t go much into ideas. I did, when I was his age. But Casaubon’s eyes, now. I think he has hurt them a little with too much reading.”\n\n“I should be all the happier, uncle, the more room there was for me to help him,” said Dorothea, ardently.\n\n“You have quite made up your mind, I see. Well, my dear, the fact is, I have a letter for you in my pocket.” Mr. Brooke handed the letter to Dorothea, but as she rose to go away, he added, “There is not too much hurry, my dear. Think about it, you know.”\n\nWhen Dorothea had left him, he reflected that he had certainly spoken strongly: he had put the risks of marriage before her in a striking manner. It was his duty to do so. But as to pretending to be wise for young people,—no uncle, however much he had travelled in his youth, absorbed the new ideas, and dined with celebrities now deceased, could pretend to judge what sort of marriage would turn out well for a young girl who preferred Casaubon to Chettam. In short, woman was a problem which, since Mr. Brooke’s mind felt blank before it, could be hardly less complicated than the revolutions of an irregular solid.\n\n# CHAPTER V.\n\n“Hard students are commonly troubled with gowts, catarrhs, rheums, cachexia, bradypepsia, bad eyes, stone, and collick, crudities, oppilations, vertigo, winds, consumptions, and all such diseases as come by over-much sitting: they are most part lean, dry, ill-colored … and all through immoderate pains and extraordinary studies. If you will not believe the truth of this, look upon great Tostatus and Thomas Aquinas’ works; and tell me whether those men took pains.”—BURTON’S Anatomy of Melancholy, P. I, s. 2.\n\nThis was Mr. Casaubon’s letter.\n\nMY DEAR MISS BROOKE,—I have your guardian’s permission to address you on a subject than which I have none more at heart. I am not, I trust, mistaken in the recognition of some deeper correspondence than that of date in the fact that a consciousness of need in my own life had arisen contemporaneously with the possibility of my becoming acquainted with you. For in the first hour of meeting you, I had an impression of your eminent and perhaps exclusive fitness to supply that need (connected, I may say, with such activity of the affections as even the preoccupations of a work too special to be abdicated could not uninterruptedly dissimulate); and each succeeding opportunity for observation has given the impression an added depth by convincing me more emphatically of that fitness which I had preconceived, and thus evoking more decisively those affections to which I have but now referred. Our conversations have, I think, made sufficiently clear to you the tenor of my life and purposes: a tenor unsuited, I am aware, to the commoner order of minds. But I have discerned in you an elevation of thought and a capability of devotedness, which I had hitherto not conceived to be compatible either with the early bloom of youth or with those graces of sex that may be said at once to win and to confer distinction when combined, as they notably are in you, with the mental qualities above indicated. It was, I confess, beyond my hope to meet with this rare combination of elements both solid and attractive, adapted to supply aid in graver labors and to cast a charm over vacant hours; and but for the event of my introduction to you (which, let me again say, I trust not to be superficially coincident with foreshadowing needs, but providentially related thereto as stages towards the completion of a life’s plan), I should presumably have gone on to the last without any attempt to lighten my solitariness by a matrimonial union. Such, my dear Miss Brooke, is the accurate statement of my feelings; and I rely on your kind indulgence in venturing now to ask you how far your own are of a nature to confirm my happy presentiment. To be accepted by you as your husband and the earthly guardian of your welfare, I should regard as the highest of providential gifts. In return I can at least offer you an affection hitherto unwasted, and the faithful consecration of a life which, however short in the sequel, has no backward pages whereon, if you choose to turn them, you will find records such as might justly cause you either bitterness or shame. I await the expression of your sentiments with an anxiety which it would be the part of wisdom (were it possible) to divert by a more arduous labor than usual. But in this order of experience I am still young, and in looking forward to an unfavorable possibility I cannot but feel that resignation to solitude will be more difficult after the temporary illumination of hope.\n\nIn any case, I shall remain, Yours with sincere devotion, EDWARD CASAUBON.\n\nDorothea trembled while she read this letter; then she fell on her knees, buried her face, and sobbed. She could not pray: under the rush of solemn emotion in which thoughts became vague and images floated uncertainly, she could but cast herself, with a childlike sense of reclining, in the lap of a divine consciousness which sustained her own. She remained in that attitude till it was time to dress for dinner.\n\nHow could it occur to her to examine the letter, to look at it critically as a profession of love? Her whole soul was possessed by the fact that a fuller life was opening before her: she was a neophyte about to enter on a higher grade of initiation. She was going to have room for the energies which stirred uneasily under the dimness and pressure of her own ignorance and the petty peremptoriness of the world’s habits.\n\nNow she would be able to devote herself to large yet definite duties; now she would be allowed to live continually in the light of a mind that she could reverence. This hope was not unmixed with the glow of proud delight—the joyous maiden surprise that she was chosen by the man whom her admiration had chosen. All Dorothea’s passion was transfused through a mind struggling towards an ideal life; the radiance of her transfigured girlhood fell on the first object that came within its level. The impetus with which inclination became resolution was heightened by those little events of the day which had roused her discontent with the actual conditions of her life.\n\nAfter dinner, when Celia was playing an “air, with variations,” a small kind of tinkling which symbolized the aesthetic part of the young ladies’ education, Dorothea went up to her room to answer Mr. Casaubon’s letter. Why should she defer the answer? She wrote it over three times, not because she wished to change the wording, but because her hand was unusually uncertain, and she could not bear that Mr. Casaubon should think her handwriting bad and illegible. She piqued herself on writing a hand in which each letter was distinguishable without any large range of conjecture, and she meant to make much use of this accomplishment, to save Mr. Casaubon’s eyes. Three times she wrote.\n\nMY DEAR MR. CASAUBON,—I am very grateful to you for loving me, and thinking me worthy to be your wife. I can look forward to no better happiness than that which would be one with yours. If I said more, it would only be the same thing written out at greater length, for I cannot now dwell on any other thought than that I may be through life\n\nYours devotedly, DOROTHEA BROOKE.\n\nLater in the evening she followed her uncle into the library to give him the letter, that he might send it in the morning. He was surprised, but his surprise only issued in a few moments’ silence, during which he pushed about various objects on his writing-table, and finally stood with his back to the fire, his glasses on his nose, looking at the address of Dorothea’s letter.\n\n“Have you thought enough about this, my dear?” he said at last.\n\n“There was no need to think long, uncle. I know of nothing to make me vacillate. If I changed my mind, it must be because of something important and entirely new to me.”\n\n“Ah!—then you have accepted him? Then Chettam has no chance? Has Chettam offended you—offended you, you know? What is it you don’t like in Chettam?”\n\n“There is nothing that I like in him,” said Dorothea, rather impetuously.\n\nMr. Brooke threw his head and shoulders backward as if some one had thrown a light missile at him. Dorothea immediately felt some self-rebuke, and said—\n\n“I mean in the light of a husband. He is very kind, I think—really very good about the cottages. A well-meaning man.”\n\n“But you must have a scholar, and that sort of thing? Well, it lies a little in our family. I had it myself—that love of knowledge, and going into everything—a little too much—it took me too far; though that sort of thing doesn’t often run in the female-line; or it runs underground like the rivers in Greece, you know—it comes out in the sons. Clever sons, clever mothers. I went a good deal into that, at one time. However, my dear, I have always said that people should do as they like in these things, up to a certain point. I couldn’t, as your guardian, have consented to a bad match. But Casaubon stands well: his position is good. I am afraid Chettam will be hurt, though, and Mrs. Cadwallader will blame me.”\n\nThat evening, of course, Celia knew nothing of what had happened. She attributed Dorothea’s abstracted manner, and the evidence of further crying since they had got home, to the temper she had been in about Sir James Chettam and the buildings, and was careful not to give further offence: having once said what she wanted to say, Celia had no disposition to recur to disagreeable subjects. It had been her nature when a child never to quarrel with any one—only to observe with wonder that they quarrelled with her, and looked like turkey-cocks; whereupon she was ready to play at cat’s cradle with them whenever they recovered themselves. And as to Dorothea, it had always been her way to find something wrong in her sister’s words, though Celia inwardly protested that she always said just how things were, and nothing else: she never did and never could put words together out of her own head. But the best of Dodo was, that she did not keep angry for long together. Now, though they had hardly spoken to each other all the evening, yet when Celia put by her work, intending to go to bed, a proceeding in which she was always much the earlier, Dorothea, who was seated on a low stool, unable to occupy herself except in meditation, said, with the musical intonation which in moments of deep but quiet feeling made her speech like a fine bit of recitative—\n\n“Celia, dear, come and kiss me,” holding her arms open as she spoke.\n\nCelia knelt down to get the right level and gave her little butterfly kiss, while Dorothea encircled her with gentle arms and pressed her lips gravely on each cheek in turn.\n\n“Don’t sit up, Dodo, you are so pale to-night: go to bed soon,” said Celia, in a comfortable way, without any touch of pathos.\n\n“No, dear, I am very, very happy,” said Dorothea, fervently.\n\n“So much the better,” thought Celia. “But how strangely Dodo goes from one extreme to the other.”\n\nThe next day, at luncheon, the butler, handing something to Mr. Brooke, said, “Jonas is come back, sir, and has brought this letter.”\n\nMr. Brooke read the letter, and then, nodding toward Dorothea, said, “Casaubon, my dear: he will be here to dinner; he didn’t wait to write more—didn’t wait, you know.”\n\nIt could not seem remarkable to Celia that a dinner guest should be announced to her sister beforehand, but, her eyes following the same direction as her uncle’s, she was struck with the peculiar effect of the announcement on Dorothea. It seemed as if something like the reflection of a white sunlit wing had passed across her features, ending in one of her rare blushes. For the first time it entered into Celia’s mind that there might be something more between Mr. Casaubon and her sister than his delight in bookish talk and her delight in listening. Hitherto she had classed the admiration for this “ugly” and learned acquaintance with the admiration for Monsieur Liret at Lausanne, also ugly and learned. Dorothea had never been tired of listening to old Monsieur Liret when Celia’s feet were as cold as possible, and when it had really become dreadful to see the skin of his bald head moving about. Why then should her enthusiasm not extend to Mr. Casaubon simply in the same way as to Monsieur Liret? And it seemed probable that all learned men had a sort of schoolmaster’s view of young people.\n\nBut now Celia was really startled at the suspicion which had darted into her mind. She was seldom taken by surprise in this way, her marvellous quickness in observing a certain order of signs generally preparing her to expect such outward events as she had an interest in. Not that she now imagined Mr. Casaubon to be already an accepted lover: she had only begun to feel disgust at the possibility that anything in Dorothea’s mind could tend towards such an issue. Here was something really to vex her about Dodo: it was all very well not to accept Sir James Chettam, but the idea of marrying Mr. Casaubon! Celia felt a sort of shame mingled with a sense of the ludicrous. But perhaps Dodo, if she were really bordering on such an extravagance, might be turned away from it: experience had often shown that her impressibility might be calculated on. The day was damp, and they were not going to walk out, so they both went up to their sitting-room; and there Celia observed that Dorothea, instead of settling down with her usual diligent interest to some occupation, simply leaned her elbow on an open book and looked out of the window at the great cedar silvered with the damp. She herself had taken up the making of a toy for the curate’s children, and was not going to enter on any subject too precipitately.\n\nDorothea was in fact thinking that it was desirable for Celia to know of the momentous change in Mr. Casaubon’s position since he had last been in the house: it did not seem fair to leave her in ignorance of what would necessarily affect her attitude towards him; but it was impossible not to shrink from telling her. Dorothea accused herself of some meanness in this timidity: it was always odious to her to have any small fears or contrivances about her actions, but at this moment she was seeking the highest aid possible that she might not dread the corrosiveness of Celia’s pretty carnally minded prose. Her reverie was broken, and the difficulty of decision banished, by Celia’s small and rather guttural voice speaking in its usual tone, of a remark aside or a “by the bye.”\n\n“Is any one else coming to dine besides Mr. Casaubon?”\n\n“Not that I know of.”\n\n“I hope there is some one else. Then I shall not hear him eat his soup so.”\n\n“What is there remarkable about his soup-eating?”\n\n“Really, Dodo, can’t you hear how he scrapes his spoon? And he always blinks before he speaks. I don’t know whether Locke blinked, but I’m sure I am sorry for those who sat opposite to him if he did.”\n\n“Celia,” said Dorothea, with emphatic gravity, “pray don’t make any more observations of that kind.”\n\n“Why not? They are quite true,” returned Celia, who had her reasons for persevering, though she was beginning to be a little afraid.\n\n“Many things are true which only the commonest minds observe.”\n\n“Then I think the commonest minds must be rather useful. I think it is a pity Mr. Casaubon’s mother had not a commoner mind: she might have taught him better.” Celia was inwardly frightened, and ready to run away, now she had hurled this light javelin.\n\nDorothea’s feelings had gathered to an avalanche, and there could be no further preparation.\n\n“It is right to tell you, Celia, that I am engaged to marry Mr. Casaubon.”\n\nPerhaps Celia had never turned so pale before. The paper man she was making would have had his leg injured, but for her habitual care of whatever she held in her hands. She laid the fragile figure down at once, and sat perfectly still for a few moments. When she spoke there was a tear gathering.\n\n“Oh, Dodo, I hope you will be happy.” Her sisterly tenderness could not but surmount other feelings at this moment, and her fears were the fears of affection.\n\nDorothea was still hurt and agitated.\n\n“It is quite decided, then?” said Celia, in an awed under tone. “And uncle knows?”\n\n“I have accepted Mr. Casaubon’s offer. My uncle brought me the letter that contained it; he knew about it beforehand.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, if I have said anything to hurt you, Dodo,” said Celia, with a slight sob. She never could have thought that she should feel as she did. There was something funereal in the whole affair, and Mr. Casaubon seemed to be the officiating clergyman, about whom it would be indecent to make remarks.\n\n“Never mind, Kitty, do not grieve. We should never admire the same people. I often offend in something of the same way; I am apt to speak too strongly of those who don’t please me.”\n\nIn spite of this magnanimity Dorothea was still smarting: perhaps as much from Celia’s subdued astonishment as from her small criticisms. Of course all the world round Tipton would be out of sympathy with this marriage. Dorothea knew of no one who thought as she did about life and its best objects.\n\nNevertheless before the evening was at an end she was very happy. In an hour’s tête-à-tête with Mr. Casaubon she talked to him with more freedom than she had ever felt before, even pouring out her joy at the thought of devoting herself to him, and of learning how she might best share and further all his great ends. Mr. Casaubon was touched with an unknown delight (what man would not have been?) at this childlike unrestrained ardor: he was not surprised (what lover would have been?) that he should be the object of it.\n\n“My dear young lady—Miss Brooke—Dorothea!” he said, pressing her hand between his hands, “this is a happiness greater than I had ever imagined to be in reserve for me. That I should ever meet with a mind and person so rich in the mingled graces which could render marriage desirable, was far indeed from my conception. You have all—nay, more than all—those qualities which I have ever regarded as the characteristic excellences of womanhood. The great charm of your sex is its capability of an ardent self-sacrificing affection, and herein we see its fitness to round and complete the existence of our own. Hitherto I have known few pleasures save of the severer kind: my satisfactions have been those of the solitary student. I have been little disposed to gather flowers that would wither in my hand, but now I shall pluck them with eagerness, to place them in your bosom.”\n\nNo speech could have been more thoroughly honest in its intention: the frigid rhetoric at the end was as sincere as the bark of a dog, or the cawing of an amorous rook. Would it not be rash to conclude that there was no passion behind those sonnets to Delia which strike us as the thin music of a mandolin?\n\nDorothea’s faith supplied all that Mr. Casaubon’s words seemed to leave unsaid: what believer sees a disturbing omission or infelicity? The text, whether of prophet or of poet, expands for whatever we can put into it, and even his bad grammar is sublime.\n\n“I am very ignorant—you will quite wonder at my ignorance,” said Dorothea. “I have so many thoughts that may be quite mistaken; and now I shall be able to tell them all to you, and ask you about them. But,” she added, with rapid imagination of Mr. Casaubon’s probable feeling, “I will not trouble you too much; only when you are inclined to listen to me. You must often be weary with the pursuit of subjects in your own track. I shall gain enough if you will take me with you there.”\n\n“How should I be able now to persevere in any path without your companionship?” said Mr. Casaubon, kissing her candid brow, and feeling that heaven had vouchsafed him a blessing in every way suited to his peculiar wants. He was being unconsciously wrought upon by the charms of a nature which was entirely without hidden calculations either for immediate effects or for remoter ends. It was this which made Dorothea so childlike, and, according to some judges, so stupid, with all her reputed cleverness; as, for example, in the present case of throwing herself, metaphorically speaking, at Mr. Casaubon’s feet, and kissing his unfashionable shoe-ties as if he were a Protestant Pope. She was not in the least teaching Mr. Casaubon to ask if he were good enough for her, but merely asking herself anxiously how she could be good enough for Mr. Casaubon. Before he left the next day it had been decided that the marriage should take place within six weeks. Why not? Mr. Casaubon’s house was ready. It was not a parsonage, but a considerable mansion, with much land attached to it. The parsonage was inhabited by the curate, who did all the duty except preaching the morning sermon.\n\n# CHAPTER VI.\n\nMy lady’s tongue is like the meadow blades, That cut you stroking them with idle hand. Nice cutting is her function: she divides With spiritual edge the millet-seed, And makes intangible savings.\n\nAs Mr. Casaubon’s carriage was passing out of the gateway, it arrested the entrance of a pony phaeton driven by a lady with a servant seated behind. It was doubtful whether the recognition had been mutual, for Mr. Casaubon was looking absently before him; but the lady was quick-eyed, and threw a nod and a “How do you do?” in the nick of time. In spite of her shabby bonnet and very old Indian shawl, it was plain that the lodge-keeper regarded her as an important personage, from the low curtsy which was dropped on the entrance of the small phaeton.\n\n“Well, Mrs. Fitchett, how are your fowls laying now?” said the high-colored, dark-eyed lady, with the clearest chiselled utterance.\n\n“Pretty well for laying, madam, but they’ve ta’en to eating their eggs: I’ve no peace o’ mind with ’em at all.”\n\n“Oh, the cannibals! Better sell them cheap at once. What will you sell them a couple? One can’t eat fowls of a bad character at a high price.”\n\n“Well, madam, half-a-crown: I couldn’t let ’em go, not under.”\n\n“Half-a-crown, these times! Come now—for the Rector’s chicken-broth on a Sunday. He has consumed all ours that I can spare. You are half paid with the sermon, Mrs. Fitchett, remember that. Take a pair of tumbler-pigeons for them—little beauties. You must come and see them. You have no tumblers among your pigeons.”\n\n“Well, madam, Master Fitchett shall go and see ’em after work. He’s very hot on new sorts; to oblige you.”\n\n“Oblige me! It will be the best bargain he ever made. A pair of church pigeons for a couple of wicked Spanish fowls that eat their own eggs! Don’t you and Fitchett boast too much, that is all!”\n\nThe phaeton was driven onwards with the last words, leaving Mrs. Fitchett laughing and shaking her head slowly, with an interjectional “Surely, surely!”—from which it might be inferred that she would have found the country-side somewhat duller if the Rector’s lady had been less free-spoken and less of a skinflint. Indeed, both the farmers and laborers in the parishes of Freshitt and Tipton would have felt a sad lack of conversation but for the stories about what Mrs. Cadwallader said and did: a lady of immeasurably high birth, descended, as it were, from unknown earls, dim as the crowd of heroic shades—who pleaded poverty, pared down prices, and cut jokes in the most companionable manner, though with a turn of tongue that let you know who she was. Such a lady gave a neighborliness to both rank and religion, and mitigated the bitterness of uncommuted tithe. A much more exemplary character with an infusion of sour dignity would not have furthered their comprehension of the Thirty-nine Articles, and would have been less socially uniting.\n\nMr. Brooke, seeing Mrs. Cadwallader’s merits from a different point of view, winced a little when her name was announced in the library, where he was sitting alone.\n\n“I see you have had our Lowick Cicero here,” she said, seating herself comfortably, throwing back her wraps, and showing a thin but well-built figure. “I suspect you and he are brewing some bad polities, else you would not be seeing so much of the lively man. I shall inform against you: remember you are both suspicious characters since you took Peel’s side about the Catholic Bill. I shall tell everybody that you are going to put up for Middlemarch on the Whig side when old Pinkerton resigns, and that Casaubon is going to help you in an underhand manner: going to bribe the voters with pamphlets, and throw open the public-houses to distribute them. Come, confess!”\n\n“Nothing of the sort,” said Mr. Brooke, smiling and rubbing his eye-glasses, but really blushing a little at the impeachment. “Casaubon and I don’t talk politics much. He doesn’t care much about the philanthropic side of things; punishments, and that kind of thing. He only cares about Church questions. That is not my line of action, you know.”\n\n“Ra-a-ther too much, my friend. I have heard of your doings. Who was it that sold his bit of land to the Papists at Middlemarch? I believe you bought it on purpose. You are a perfect Guy Faux. See if you are not burnt in effigy this 5th of November coming. Humphrey would not come to quarrel with you about it, so I am come.”\n\n“Very good. I was prepared to be persecuted for not persecuting—not persecuting, you know.”\n\n“There you go! That is a piece of clap-trap you have got ready for the hustings. Now, do not let them lure you to the hustings, my dear Mr. Brooke. A man always makes a fool of himself, speechifying: there’s no excuse but being on the right side, so that you can ask a blessing on your humming and hawing. You will lose yourself, I forewarn you. You will make a Saturday pie of all parties’ opinions, and be pelted by everybody.”\n\n“That is what I expect, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, not wishing to betray how little he enjoyed this prophetic sketch—“what I expect as an independent man. As to the Whigs, a man who goes with the thinkers is not likely to be hooked on by any party. He may go with them up to a certain point—up to a certain point, you know. But that is what you ladies never understand.”\n\n“Where your certain point is? No. I should like to be told how a man can have any certain point when he belongs to no party—leading a roving life, and never letting his friends know his address. ‘Nobody knows where Brooke will be—there’s no counting on Brooke’—that is what people say of you, to be quite frank. Now, do turn respectable. How will you like going to Sessions with everybody looking shy on you, and you with a bad conscience and an empty pocket?”\n\n“I don’t pretend to argue with a lady on politics,” said Mr. Brooke, with an air of smiling indifference, but feeling rather unpleasantly conscious that this attack of Mrs. Cadwallader’s had opened the defensive campaign to which certain rash steps had exposed him. “Your sex are not thinkers, you know—varium et mutabile semper—that kind of thing. You don’t know Virgil. I knew”—Mr. Brooke reflected in time that he had not had the personal acquaintance of the Augustan poet—“I was going to say, poor Stoddart, you know. That was what he said. You ladies are always against an independent attitude—a man’s caring for nothing but truth, and that sort of thing. And there is no part of the county where opinion is narrower than it is here—I don’t mean to throw stones, you know, but somebody is wanted to take the independent line; and if I don’t take it, who will?”\n\n“Who? Why, any upstart who has got neither blood nor position. People of standing should consume their independent nonsense at home, not hawk it about. And you! who are going to marry your niece, as good as your daughter, to one of our best men. Sir James would be cruelly annoyed: it will be too hard on him if you turn round now and make yourself a Whig sign-board.”\n\nMr. Brooke again winced inwardly, for Dorothea’s engagement had no sooner been decided, than he had thought of Mrs. Cadwallader’s prospective taunts. It might have been easy for ignorant observers to say, “Quarrel with Mrs. Cadwallader;” but where is a country gentleman to go who quarrels with his oldest neighbors? Who could taste the fine flavor in the name of Brooke if it were delivered casually, like wine without a seal? Certainly a man can only be cosmopolitan up to a certain point.\n\n“I hope Chettam and I shall always be good friends; but I am sorry to say there is no prospect of his marrying my niece,” said Mr. Brooke, much relieved to see through the window that Celia was coming in.\n\n“Why not?” said Mrs. Cadwallader, with a sharp note of surprise. “It is hardly a fortnight since you and I were talking about it.”\n\n“My niece has chosen another suitor—has chosen him, you know. I have had nothing to do with it. I should have preferred Chettam; and I should have said Chettam was the man any girl would have chosen. But there is no accounting for these things. Your sex is capricious, you know.”\n\n“Why, whom do you mean to say that you are going to let her marry?” Mrs. Cadwallader’s mind was rapidly surveying the possibilities of choice for Dorothea.\n\nBut here Celia entered, blooming from a walk in the garden, and the greeting with her delivered Mr. Brooke from the necessity of answering immediately. He got up hastily, and saying, “By the way, I must speak to Wright about the horses,” shuffled quickly out of the room.\n\n“My dear child, what is this?—this about your sister’s engagement?” said Mrs. Cadwallader.\n\n“She is engaged to marry Mr. Casaubon,” said Celia, resorting, as usual, to the simplest statement of fact, and enjoying this opportunity of speaking to the Rector’s wife alone.\n\n“This is frightful. How long has it been going on?”\n\n“I only knew of it yesterday. They are to be married in six weeks.”\n\n“Well, my dear, I wish you joy of your brother-in-law.”\n\n“I am so sorry for Dorothea.”\n\n“Sorry! It is her doing, I suppose.”\n\n“Yes; she says Mr. Casaubon has a great soul.”\n\n“With all my heart.”\n\n“Oh, Mrs. Cadwallader, I don’t think it can be nice to marry a man with a great soul.”\n\n“Well, my dear, take warning. You know the look of one now; when the next comes and wants to marry you, don’t you accept him.”\n\n“I’m sure I never should.”\n\n“No; one such in a family is enough. So your sister never cared about Sir James Chettam? What would you have said to him for a brother-in-law?”\n\n“I should have liked that very much. I am sure he would have been a good husband. Only,” Celia added, with a slight blush (she sometimes seemed to blush as she breathed), “I don’t think he would have suited Dorothea.”\n\n“Not high-flown enough?”\n\n“Dodo is very strict. She thinks so much about everything, and is so particular about what one says. Sir James never seemed to please her.”\n\n“She must have encouraged him, I am sure. That is not very creditable.”\n\n“Please don’t be angry with Dodo; she does not see things. She thought so much about the cottages, and she was rude to Sir James sometimes; but he is so kind, he never noticed it.”\n\n“Well,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, putting on her shawl, and rising, as if in haste, “I must go straight to Sir James and break this to him. He will have brought his mother back by this time, and I must call. Your uncle will never tell him. We are all disappointed, my dear. Young people should think of their families in marrying. I set a bad example—married a poor clergyman, and made myself a pitiable object among the De Bracys—obliged to get my coals by stratagem, and pray to heaven for my salad oil. However, Casaubon has money enough; I must do him that justice. As to his blood, I suppose the family quarterings are three cuttle-fish sable, and a commentator rampant. By the bye, before I go, my dear, I must speak to your Mrs. Carter about pastry. I want to send my young cook to learn of her. Poor people with four children, like us, you know, can’t afford to keep a good cook. I have no doubt Mrs. Carter will oblige me. Sir James’s cook is a perfect dragon.”\n\nIn less than an hour, Mrs. Cadwallader had circumvented Mrs. Carter and driven to Freshitt Hall, which was not far from her own parsonage, her husband being resident in Freshitt and keeping a curate in Tipton.\n\nSir James Chettam had returned from the short journey which had kept him absent for a couple of days, and had changed his dress, intending to ride over to Tipton Grange. His horse was standing at the door when Mrs. Cadwallader drove up, and he immediately appeared there himself, whip in hand. Lady Chettam had not yet returned, but Mrs. Cadwallader’s errand could not be despatched in the presence of grooms, so she asked to be taken into the conservatory close by, to look at the new plants; and on coming to a contemplative stand, she said—\n\n“I have a great shock for you; I hope you are not so far gone in love as you pretended to be.”\n\nIt was of no use protesting against Mrs. Cadwallader’s way of putting things. But Sir James’s countenance changed a little. He felt a vague alarm.\n\n“I do believe Brooke is going to expose himself after all. I accused him of meaning to stand for Middlemarch on the Liberal side, and he looked silly and never denied it—talked about the independent line, and the usual nonsense.”\n\n“Is that all?” said Sir James, much relieved.\n\n“Why,” rejoined Mrs. Cadwallader, with a sharper note, “you don’t mean to say that you would like him to turn public man in that way—making a sort of political Cheap Jack of himself?”\n\n“He might be dissuaded, I should think. He would not like the expense.”\n\n“That is what I told him. He is vulnerable to reason there—always a few grains of common-sense in an ounce of miserliness. Miserliness is a capital quality to run in families; it’s the safe side for madness to dip on. And there must be a little crack in the Brooke family, else we should not see what we are to see.”\n\n“What? Brooke standing for Middlemarch?”\n\n“Worse than that. I really feel a little responsible. I always told you Miss Brooke would be such a fine match. I knew there was a great deal of nonsense in her—a flighty sort of Methodistical stuff. But these things wear out of girls. However, I am taken by surprise for once.”\n\n“What do you mean, Mrs. Cadwallader?” said Sir James. His fear lest Miss Brooke should have run away to join the Moravian Brethren, or some preposterous sect unknown to good society, was a little allayed by the knowledge that Mrs. Cadwallader always made the worst of things. “What has happened to Miss Brooke? Pray speak out.”\n\n“Very well. She is engaged to be married.” Mrs. Cadwallader paused a few moments, observing the deeply hurt expression in her friend’s face, which he was trying to conceal by a nervous smile, while he whipped his boot; but she soon added, “Engaged to Casaubon.”\n\nSir James let his whip fall and stooped to pick it up. Perhaps his face had never before gathered so much concentrated disgust as when he turned to Mrs. Cadwallader and repeated, “Casaubon?”\n\n“Even so. You know my errand now.”\n\n“Good God! It is horrible! He is no better than a mummy!” (The point of view has to be allowed for, as that of a blooming and disappointed rival.)\n\n“She says, he is a great soul.—A great bladder for dried peas to rattle in!” said Mrs. Cadwallader.\n\n“What business has an old bachelor like that to marry?” said Sir James. “He has one foot in the grave.”\n\n“He means to draw it out again, I suppose.”\n\n“Brooke ought not to allow it: he should insist on its being put off till she is of age. She would think better of it then. What is a guardian for?”\n\n“As if you could ever squeeze a resolution out of Brooke!”\n\n“Cadwallader might talk to him.”\n\n“Not he! Humphrey finds everybody charming. I never can get him to abuse Casaubon. He will even speak well of the bishop, though I tell him it is unnatural in a beneficed clergyman; what can one do with a husband who attends so little to the decencies? I hide it as well as I can by abusing everybody myself. Come, come, cheer up! you are well rid of Miss Brooke, a girl who would have been requiring you to see the stars by daylight. Between ourselves, little Celia is worth two of her, and likely after all to be the better match. For this marriage to Casaubon is as good as going to a nunnery.”\n\n“Oh, on my own account—it is for Miss Brooke’s sake I think her friends should try to use their influence.”\n\n“Well, Humphrey doesn’t know yet. But when I tell him, you may depend on it he will say, ‘Why not? Casaubon is a good fellow—and young—young enough.’ These charitable people never know vinegar from wine till they have swallowed it and got the colic. However, if I were a man I should prefer Celia, especially when Dorothea was gone. The truth is, you have been courting one and have won the other. I can see that she admires you almost as much as a man expects to be admired. If it were any one but me who said so, you might think it exaggeration. Good-by!”\n\nSir James handed Mrs. Cadwallader to the phaeton, and then jumped on his horse. He was not going to renounce his ride because of his friend’s unpleasant news—only to ride the faster in some other direction than that of Tipton Grange.\n\nNow, why on earth should Mrs. Cadwallader have been at all busy about Miss Brooke’s marriage; and why, when one match that she liked to think she had a hand in was frustrated, should she have straightway contrived the preliminaries of another? Was there any ingenious plot, any hide-and-seek course of action, which might be detected by a careful telescopic watch? Not at all: a telescope might have swept the parishes of Tipton and Freshitt, the whole area visited by Mrs. Cadwallader in her phaeton, without witnessing any interview that could excite suspicion, or any scene from which she did not return with the same unperturbed keenness of eye and the same high natural color. In fact, if that convenient vehicle had existed in the days of the Seven Sages, one of them would doubtless have remarked, that you can know little of women by following them about in their pony-phaetons. Even with a microscope directed on a water-drop we find ourselves making interpretations which turn out to be rather coarse; for whereas under a weak lens you may seem to see a creature exhibiting an active voracity into which other smaller creatures actively play as if they were so many animated tax-pennies, a stronger lens reveals to you certain tiniest hairlets which make vortices for these victims while the swallower waits passively at his receipt of custom. In this way, metaphorically speaking, a strong lens applied to Mrs. Cadwallader’s match-making will show a play of minute causes producing what may be called thought and speech vortices to bring her the sort of food she needed. Her life was rurally simple, quite free from secrets either foul, dangerous, or otherwise important, and not consciously affected by the great affairs of the world. All the more did the affairs of the great world interest her, when communicated in the letters of high-born relations: the way in which fascinating younger sons had gone to the dogs by marrying their mistresses; the fine old-blooded idiocy of young Lord Tapir, and the furious gouty humors of old Lord Megatherium; the exact crossing of genealogies which had brought a coronet into a new branch and widened the relations of scandal,—these were topics of which she retained details with the utmost accuracy, and reproduced them in an excellent pickle of epigrams, which she herself enjoyed the more because she believed as unquestionably in birth and no-birth as she did in game and vermin. She would never have disowned any one on the ground of poverty: a De Bracy reduced to take his dinner in a basin would have seemed to her an example of pathos worth exaggerating, and I fear his aristocratic vices would not have horrified her. But her feeling towards the vulgar rich was a sort of religious hatred: they had probably made all their money out of high retail prices, and Mrs. Cadwallader detested high prices for everything that was not paid in kind at the Rectory: such people were no part of God’s design in making the world; and their accent was an affliction to the ears. A town where such monsters abounded was hardly more than a sort of low comedy, which could not be taken account of in a well-bred scheme of the universe. Let any lady who is inclined to be hard on Mrs. Cadwallader inquire into the comprehensiveness of her own beautiful views, and be quite sure that they afford accommodation for all the lives which have the honor to coexist with hers.\n\nWith such a mind, active as phosphorus, biting everything that came near into the form that suited it, how could Mrs. Cadwallader feel that the Miss Brookes and their matrimonial prospects were alien to her? especially as it had been the habit of years for her to scold Mr. Brooke with the friendliest frankness, and let him know in confidence that she thought him a poor creature. From the first arrival of the young ladies in Tipton she had prearranged Dorothea’s marriage with Sir James, and if it had taken place would have been quite sure that it was her doing: that it should not take place after she had preconceived it, caused her an irritation which every thinker will sympathize with. She was the diplomatist of Tipton and Freshitt, and for anything to happen in spite of her was an offensive irregularity. As to freaks like this of Miss Brooke’s, Mrs. Cadwallader had no patience with them, and now saw that her opinion of this girl had been infected with some of her husband’s weak charitableness: those Methodistical whims, that air of being more religious than the rector and curate together, came from a deeper and more constitutional disease than she had been willing to believe.\n\n“However,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, first to herself and afterwards to her husband, “I throw her over: there was a chance, if she had married Sir James, of her becoming a sane, sensible woman. He would never have contradicted her, and when a woman is not contradicted, she has no motive for obstinacy in her absurdities. But now I wish her joy of her hair shirt.”\n\nIt followed that Mrs. Cadwallader must decide on another match for Sir James, and having made up her mind that it was to be the younger Miss Brooke, there could not have been a more skilful move towards the success of her plan than her hint to the baronet that he had made an impression on Celia’s heart. For he was not one of those gentlemen who languish after the unattainable Sappho’s apple that laughs from the topmost bough—the charms which\n\n“Smile like the knot of cowslips on the cliff, Not to be come at by the willing hand.”\n\nHe had no sonnets to write, and it could not strike him agreeably that he was not an object of preference to the woman whom he had preferred. Already the knowledge that Dorothea had chosen Mr. Casaubon had bruised his attachment and relaxed its hold. Although Sir James was a sportsman, he had some other feelings towards women than towards grouse and foxes, and did not regard his future wife in the light of prey, valuable chiefly for the excitements of the chase. Neither was he so well acquainted with the habits of primitive races as to feel that an ideal combat for her, tomahawk in hand, so to speak, was necessary to the historical continuity of the marriage-tie. On the contrary, having the amiable vanity which knits us to those who are fond of us, and disinclines us to those who are indifferent, and also a good grateful nature, the mere idea that a woman had a kindness towards him spun little threads of tenderness from out his heart towards hers.\n\nThus it happened, that after Sir James had ridden rather fast for half an hour in a direction away from Tipton Grange, he slackened his pace, and at last turned into a road which would lead him back by a shorter cut. Various feelings wrought in him the determination after all to go to the Grange to-day as if nothing new had happened. He could not help rejoicing that he had never made the offer and been rejected; mere friendly politeness required that he should call to see Dorothea about the cottages, and now happily Mrs. Cadwallader had prepared him to offer his congratulations, if necessary, without showing too much awkwardness. He really did not like it: giving up Dorothea was very painful to him; but there was something in the resolve to make this visit forthwith and conquer all show of feeling, which was a sort of file-biting and counter-irritant. And without his distinctly recognizing the impulse, there certainly was present in him the sense that Celia would be there, and that he should pay her more attention than he had done before.\n\nWe mortals, men and women, devour many a disappointment between breakfast and dinner-time; keep back the tears and look a little pale about the lips, and in answer to inquiries say, “Oh, nothing!” Pride helps us; and pride is not a bad thing when it only urges us to hide our own hurts—not to hurt others.\n\n“Piacer e popone Vuol la sua stagione.” —Italian Proverb.\nMr. Casaubon, as might be expected, spent a great deal of his time at the Grange in these weeks, and the hindrance which courtship occasioned to the progress of his great work—the Key to all Mythologies—naturally made him look forward the more eagerly to the happy termination of courtship. But he had deliberately incurred the hindrance, having made up his mind that it was now time for him to adorn his life with the graces of female companionship, to irradiate the gloom which fatigue was apt to hang over the intervals of studious labor with the play of female fancy, and to secure in this, his culminating age, the solace of female tendance for his declining years. Hence he determined to abandon himself to the stream of feeling, and perhaps was surprised to find what an exceedingly shallow rill it was. As in droughty regions baptism by immersion could only be performed symbolically, Mr. Casaubon found that sprinkling was the utmost approach to a plunge which his stream would afford him; and he concluded that the poets had much exaggerated the force of masculine passion. Nevertheless, he observed with pleasure that Miss Brooke showed an ardent submissive affection which promised to fulfil his most agreeable previsions of marriage. It had once or twice crossed his mind that possibly there was some deficiency in Dorothea to account for the moderation of his abandonment; but he was unable to discern the deficiency, or to figure to himself a woman who would have pleased him better; so that there was clearly no reason to fall back upon but the exaggerations of human tradition.\n\n“Could I not be preparing myself now to be more useful?” said Dorothea to him, one morning, early in the time of courtship; “could I not learn to read Latin and Greek aloud to you, as Milton’s daughters did to their father, without understanding what they read?”\n\n“I fear that would be wearisome to you,” said Mr. Casaubon, smiling; “and, indeed, if I remember rightly, the young women you have mentioned regarded that exercise in unknown tongues as a ground for rebellion against the poet.”\n\n“Yes; but in the first place they were very naughty girls, else they would have been proud to minister to such a father; and in the second place they might have studied privately and taught themselves to understand what they read, and then it would have been interesting. I hope you don’t expect me to be naughty and stupid?”\n\n“I expect you to be all that an exquisite young lady can be in every possible relation of life. Certainly it might be a great advantage if you were able to copy the Greek character, and to that end it were well to begin with a little reading.”\n\nDorothea seized this as a precious permission. She would not have asked Mr. Casaubon at once to teach her the languages, dreading of all things to be tiresome instead of helpful; but it was not entirely out of devotion to her future husband that she wished to know Latin and Greek. Those provinces of masculine knowledge seemed to her a standing-ground from which all truth could be seen more truly. As it was, she constantly doubted her own conclusions, because she felt her own ignorance: how could she be confident that one-roomed cottages were not for the glory of God, when men who knew the classics appeared to conciliate indifference to the cottages with zeal for the glory? Perhaps even Hebrew might be necessary—at least the alphabet and a few roots—in order to arrive at the core of things, and judge soundly on the social duties of the Christian. And she had not reached that point of renunciation at which she would have been satisfied with having a wise husband: she wished, poor child, to be wise herself. Miss Brooke was certainly very naive with all her alleged cleverness. Celia, whose mind had never been thought too powerful, saw the emptiness of other people’s pretensions much more readily. To have in general but little feeling, seems to be the only security against feeling too much on any particular occasion.\n\nHowever, Mr. Casaubon consented to listen and teach for an hour together, like a schoolmaster of little boys, or rather like a lover, to whom a mistress’s elementary ignorance and difficulties have a touching fitness. Few scholars would have disliked teaching the alphabet under such circumstances. But Dorothea herself was a little shocked and discouraged at her own stupidity, and the answers she got to some timid questions about the value of the Greek accents gave her a painful suspicion that here indeed there might be secrets not capable of explanation to a woman’s reason.\n\nMr. Brooke had no doubt on that point, and expressed himself with his usual strength upon it one day that he came into the library while the reading was going forward.\n\n“Well, but now, Casaubon, such deep studies, classics, mathematics, that kind of thing, are too taxing for a woman—too taxing, you know.”\n\n“Dorothea is learning to read the characters simply,” said Mr. Casaubon, evading the question. “She had the very considerate thought of saving my eyes.”\n\n“Ah, well, without understanding, you know—that may not be so bad. But there is a lightness about the feminine mind—a touch and go—music, the fine arts, that kind of thing—they should study those up to a certain point, women should; but in a light way, you know. A woman should be able to sit down and play you or sing you a good old English tune. That is what I like; though I have heard most things—been at the opera in Vienna: Gluck, Mozart, everything of that sort. But I’m a conservative in music—it’s not like ideas, you know. I stick to the good old tunes.”\n\n“Mr. Casaubon is not fond of the piano, and I am very glad he is not,” said Dorothea, whose slight regard for domestic music and feminine fine art must be forgiven her, considering the small tinkling and smearing in which they chiefly consisted at that dark period. She smiled and looked up at her betrothed with grateful eyes. If he had always been asking her to play the “Last Rose of Summer,” she would have required much resignation. “He says there is only an old harpsichord at Lowick, and it is covered with books.”\n\n“Ah, there you are behind Celia, my dear. Celia, now, plays very prettily, and is always ready to play. However, since Casaubon does not like it, you are all right. But it’s a pity you should not have little recreations of that sort, Casaubon: the bow always strung—that kind of thing, you know—will not do.”\n\n“I never could look on it in the light of a recreation to have my ears teased with measured noises,” said Mr. Casaubon. “A tune much iterated has the ridiculous effect of making the words in my mind perform a sort of minuet to keep time—an effect hardly tolerable, I imagine, after boyhood. As to the grander forms of music, worthy to accompany solemn celebrations, and even to serve as an educating influence according to the ancient conception, I say nothing, for with these we are not immediately concerned.”\n\n“No; but music of that sort I should enjoy,” said Dorothea. “When we were coming home from Lausanne my uncle took us to hear the great organ at Freiberg, and it made me sob.”\n\n“That kind of thing is not healthy, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke. “Casaubon, she will be in your hands now: you must teach my niece to take things more quietly, eh, Dorothea?”\n\nHe ended with a smile, not wishing to hurt his niece, but really thinking that it was perhaps better for her to be early married to so sober a fellow as Casaubon, since she would not hear of Chettam.\n\n“It is wonderful, though,” he said to himself as he shuffled out of the room—“it is wonderful that she should have liked him. However, the match is good. I should have been travelling out of my brief to have hindered it, let Mrs. Cadwallader say what she will. He is pretty certain to be a bishop, is Casaubon. That was a very seasonable pamphlet of his on the Catholic Question:—a deanery at least. They owe him a deanery.”\n\nAnd here I must vindicate a claim to philosophical reflectiveness, by remarking that Mr. Brooke on this occasion little thought of the Radical speech which, at a later period, he was led to make on the incomes of the bishops. What elegant historian would neglect a striking opportunity for pointing out that his heroes did not foresee the history of the world, or even their own actions?—For example, that Henry of Navarre, when a Protestant baby, little thought of being a Catholic monarch; or that Alfred the Great, when he measured his laborious nights with burning candles, had no idea of future gentlemen measuring their idle days with watches. Here is a mine of truth, which, however vigorously it may be worked, is likely to outlast our coal.\n\nBut of Mr. Brooke I make a further remark perhaps less warranted by precedent—namely, that if he had foreknown his speech, it might not have made any great difference. To think with pleasure of his niece’s husband having a large ecclesiastical income was one thing—to make a Liberal speech was another thing; and it is a narrow mind which cannot look at a subject from various points of view.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII.\n\n“Oh, rescue her! I am her brother now, And you her father. Every gentle maid Should have a guardian in each gentleman.”\n\nIt was wonderful to Sir James Chettam how well he continued to like going to the Grange after he had once encountered the difficulty of seeing Dorothea for the first time in the light of a woman who was engaged to another man. Of course the forked lightning seemed to pass through him when he first approached her, and he remained conscious throughout the interview of hiding uneasiness; but, good as he was, it must be owned that his uneasiness was less than it would have been if he had thought his rival a brilliant and desirable match. He had no sense of being eclipsed by Mr. Casaubon; he was only shocked that Dorothea was under a melancholy illusion, and his mortification lost some of its bitterness by being mingled with compassion.\n\nNevertheless, while Sir James said to himself that he had completely resigned her, since with the perversity of a Desdemona she had not affected a proposed match that was clearly suitable and according to nature; he could not yet be quite passive under the idea of her engagement to Mr. Casaubon. On the day when he first saw them together in the light of his present knowledge, it seemed to him that he had not taken the affair seriously enough. Brooke was really culpable; he ought to have hindered it. Who could speak to him? Something might be done perhaps even now, at least to defer the marriage. On his way home he turned into the Rectory and asked for Mr. Cadwallader. Happily, the Rector was at home, and his visitor was shown into the study, where all the fishing tackle hung. But he himself was in a little room adjoining, at work with his turning apparatus, and he called to the baronet to join him there. The two were better friends than any other landholder and clergyman in the county—a significant fact which was in agreement with the amiable expression of their faces.\n\nMr. Cadwallader was a large man, with full lips and a sweet smile; very plain and rough in his exterior, but with that solid imperturbable ease and good-humor which is infectious, and like great grassy hills in the sunshine, quiets even an irritated egoism, and makes it rather ashamed of itself. “Well, how are you?” he said, showing a hand not quite fit to be grasped. “Sorry I missed you before. Is there anything particular? You look vexed.”\n\nSir James’s brow had a little crease in it, a little depression of the eyebrow, which he seemed purposely to exaggerate as he answered.\n\n“It is only this conduct of Brooke’s. I really think somebody should speak to him.”\n\n“What? meaning to stand?” said Mr. Cadwallader, going on with the arrangement of the reels which he had just been turning. “I hardly think he means it. But where’s the harm, if he likes it? Any one who objects to Whiggery should be glad when the Whigs don’t put up the strongest fellow. They won’t overturn the Constitution with our friend Brooke’s head for a battering ram.”\n\n“Oh, I don’t mean that,” said Sir James, who, after putting down his hat and throwing himself into a chair, had begun to nurse his leg and examine the sole of his boot with much bitterness. “I mean this marriage. I mean his letting that blooming young girl marry Casaubon.”\n\n“What is the matter with Casaubon? I see no harm in him—if the girl likes him.”\n\n“She is too young to know what she likes. Her guardian ought to interfere. He ought not to allow the thing to be done in this headlong manner. I wonder a man like you, Cadwallader—a man with daughters, can look at the affair with indifference: and with such a heart as yours! Do think seriously about it.”\n\n“I am not joking; I am as serious as possible,” said the Rector, with a provoking little inward laugh. “You are as bad as Elinor. She has been wanting me to go and lecture Brooke; and I have reminded her that her friends had a very poor opinion of the match she made when she married me.”\n\n“But look at Casaubon,” said Sir James, indignantly. “He must be fifty, and I don’t believe he could ever have been much more than the shadow of a man. Look at his legs!”\n\n“Confound you handsome young fellows! you think of having it all your own way in the world. You don’t understand women. They don’t admire you half so much as you admire yourselves. Elinor used to tell her sisters that she married me for my ugliness—it was so various and amusing that it had quite conquered her prudence.”\n\n“You! it was easy enough for a woman to love you. But this is no question of beauty. I don’t like Casaubon.” This was Sir James’s strongest way of implying that he thought ill of a man’s character.\n\n“Why? what do you know against him?” said the Rector laying down his reels, and putting his thumbs into his armholes with an air of attention.\n\nSir James paused. He did not usually find it easy to give his reasons: it seemed to him strange that people should not know them without being told, since he only felt what was reasonable. At last he said—\n\n“Now, Cadwallader, has he got any heart?”\n\n“Well, yes. I don’t mean of the melting sort, but a sound kernel, that you may be sure of. He is very good to his poor relations: pensions several of the women, and is educating a young fellow at a good deal of expense. Casaubon acts up to his sense of justice. His mother’s sister made a bad match—a Pole, I think—lost herself—at any rate was disowned by her family. If it had not been for that, Casaubon would not have had so much money by half. I believe he went himself to find out his cousins, and see what he could do for them. Every man would not ring so well as that, if you tried his metal. You would, Chettam; but not every man.”\n\n“I don’t know,” said Sir James, coloring. “I am not so sure of myself.” He paused a moment, and then added, “That was a right thing for Casaubon to do. But a man may wish to do what is right, and yet be a sort of parchment code. A woman may not be happy with him. And I think when a girl is so young as Miss Brooke is, her friends ought to interfere a little to hinder her from doing anything foolish. You laugh, because you fancy I have some feeling on my own account. But upon my honor, it is not that. I should feel just the same if I were Miss Brooke’s brother or uncle.”\n\n“Well, but what should you do?”\n\n“I should say that the marriage must not be decided on until she was of age. And depend upon it, in that case, it would never come off. I wish you saw it as I do—I wish you would talk to Brooke about it.”\n\nSir James rose as he was finishing his sentence, for he saw Mrs. Cadwallader entering from the study. She held by the hand her youngest girl, about five years old, who immediately ran to papa, and was made comfortable on his knee.\n\n“I hear what you are talking about,” said the wife. “But you will make no impression on Humphrey. As long as the fish rise to his bait, everybody is what he ought to be. Bless you, Casaubon has got a trout-stream, and does not care about fishing in it himself: could there be a better fellow?”\n\n“Well, there is something in that,” said the Rector, with his quiet, inward laugh. “It is a very good quality in a man to have a trout-stream.”\n\n“But seriously,” said Sir James, whose vexation had not yet spent itself, “don’t you think the Rector might do some good by speaking?”\n\n“Oh, I told you beforehand what he would say,” answered Mrs. Cadwallader, lifting up her eyebrows. “I have done what I could: I wash my hands of the marriage.”\n\n“In the first place,” said the Rector, looking rather grave, “it would be nonsensical to expect that I could convince Brooke, and make him act accordingly. Brooke is a very good fellow, but pulpy; he will run into any mould, but he won’t keep shape.”\n\n“He might keep shape long enough to defer the marriage,” said Sir James.\n\n“But, my dear Chettam, why should I use my influence to Casaubon’s disadvantage, unless I were much surer than I am that I should be acting for the advantage of Miss Brooke? I know no harm of Casaubon. I don’t care about his Xisuthrus and Fee-fo-fum and the rest; but then he doesn’t care about my fishing-tackle. As to the line he took on the Catholic Question, that was unexpected; but he has always been civil to me, and I don’t see why I should spoil his sport. For anything I can tell, Miss Brooke may be happier with him than she would be with any other man.”\n\n“Humphrey! I have no patience with you. You know you would rather dine under the hedge than with Casaubon alone. You have nothing to say to each other.”\n\n“What has that to do with Miss Brooke’s marrying him? She does not do it for my amusement.”\n\n“He has got no good red blood in his body,” said Sir James.\n\n“No. Somebody put a drop under a magnifying-glass and it was all semicolons and parentheses,” said Mrs. Cadwallader.\n\n“Why does he not bring out his book, instead of marrying,” said Sir James, with a disgust which he held warranted by the sound feeling of an English layman.\n\n“Oh, he dreams footnotes, and they run away with all his brains. They say, when he was a little boy, he made an abstract of ‘Hop o’ my Thumb,’ and he has been making abstracts ever since. Ugh! And that is the man Humphrey goes on saying that a woman may be happy with.”\n\n“Well, he is what Miss Brooke likes,” said the Rector. “I don’t profess to understand every young lady’s taste.”\n\n“But if she were your own daughter?” said Sir James.\n\n“That would be a different affair. She is not my daughter, and I don’t feel called upon to interfere. Casaubon is as good as most of us. He is a scholarly clergyman, and creditable to the cloth. Some Radical fellow speechifying at Middlemarch said Casaubon was the learned straw-chopping incumbent, and Freke was the brick-and-mortar incumbent, and I was the angling incumbent. And upon my word, I don’t see that one is worse or better than the other.” The Rector ended with his silent laugh. He always saw the joke of any satire against himself. His conscience was large and easy, like the rest of him: it did only what it could do without any trouble.\n\nClearly, there would be no interference with Miss Brooke’s marriage through Mr. Cadwallader; and Sir James felt with some sadness that she was to have perfect liberty of misjudgment. It was a sign of his good disposition that he did not slacken at all in his intention of carrying out Dorothea’s design of the cottages. Doubtless this persistence was the best course for his own dignity: but pride only helps us to be generous; it never makes us so, any more than vanity makes us witty. She was now enough aware of Sir James’s position with regard to her, to appreciate the rectitude of his perseverance in a landlord’s duty, to which he had at first been urged by a lover’s complaisance, and her pleasure in it was great enough to count for something even in her present happiness. Perhaps she gave to Sir James Chettam’s cottages all the interest she could spare from Mr. Casaubon, or rather from the symphony of hopeful dreams, admiring trust, and passionate self devotion which that learned gentleman had set playing in her soul. Hence it happened that in the good baronet’s succeeding visits, while he was beginning to pay small attentions to Celia, he found himself talking with more and more pleasure to Dorothea. She was perfectly unconstrained and without irritation towards him now, and he was gradually discovering the delight there is in frank kindness and companionship between a man and a woman who have no passion to hide or confess.\n\n# CHAPTER IX.\n\n1st Gent. An ancient land in ancient oracles Is called “law-thirsty”: all the struggle there Was after order and a perfect rule. Pray, where lie such lands now? . . .\n\n2d Gent. Why, where they lay of old—in human souls.\n\nMr. Casaubon’s behavior about settlements was highly satisfactory to Mr. Brooke, and the preliminaries of marriage rolled smoothly along, shortening the weeks of courtship. The betrothed bride must see her future home, and dictate any changes that she would like to have made there. A woman dictates before marriage in order that she may have an appetite for submission afterwards. And certainly, the mistakes that we male and female mortals make when we have our own way might fairly raise some wonder that we are so fond of it.\n\nOn a gray but dry November morning Dorothea drove to Lowick in company with her uncle and Celia. Mr. Casaubon’s home was the manor-house. Close by, visible from some parts of the garden, was the little church, with the old parsonage opposite. In the beginning of his career, Mr. Casaubon had only held the living, but the death of his brother had put him in possession of the manor also. It had a small park, with a fine old oak here and there, and an avenue of limes towards the southwest front, with a sunk fence between park and pleasure-ground, so that from the drawing-room windows the glance swept uninterruptedly along a slope of greensward till the limes ended in a level of corn and pastures, which often seemed to melt into a lake under the setting sun. This was the happy side of the house, for the south and east looked rather melancholy even under the brightest morning. The grounds here were more confined, the flower-beds showed no very careful tendance, and large clumps of trees, chiefly of sombre yews, had risen high, not ten yards from the windows. The building, of greenish stone, was in the old English style, not ugly, but small-windowed and melancholy-looking: the sort of house that must have children, many flowers, open windows, and little vistas of bright things, to make it seem a joyous home. In this latter end of autumn, with a sparse remnant of yellow leaves falling slowly athwart the dark evergreens in a stillness without sunshine, the house too had an air of autumnal decline, and Mr. Casaubon, when he presented himself, had no bloom that could be thrown into relief by that background.\n\n“Oh dear!” Celia said to herself, “I am sure Freshitt Hall would have been pleasanter than this.” She thought of the white freestone, the pillared portico, and the terrace full of flowers, Sir James smiling above them like a prince issuing from his enchantment in a rose-bush, with a handkerchief swiftly metamorphosed from the most delicately odorous petals—Sir James, who talked so agreeably, always about things which had common-sense in them, and not about learning! Celia had those light young feminine tastes which grave and weatherworn gentlemen sometimes prefer in a wife; but happily Mr. Casaubon’s bias had been different, for he would have had no chance with Celia.\n\nDorothea, on the contrary, found the house and grounds all that she could wish: the dark book-shelves in the long library, the carpets and curtains with colors subdued by time, the curious old maps and bird’s-eye views on the walls of the corridor, with here and there an old vase below, had no oppression for her, and seemed more cheerful than the casts and pictures at the Grange, which her uncle had long ago brought home from his travels—they being probably among the ideas he had taken in at one time. To poor Dorothea these severe classical nudities and smirking Renaissance-Correggiosities were painfully inexplicable, staring into the midst of her Puritanic conceptions: she had never been taught how she could bring them into any sort of relevance with her life. But the owners of Lowick apparently had not been travellers, and Mr. Casaubon’s studies of the past were not carried on by means of such aids.\n\nDorothea walked about the house with delightful emotion. Everything seemed hallowed to her: this was to be the home of her wifehood, and she looked up with eyes full of confidence to Mr. Casaubon when he drew her attention specially to some actual arrangement and asked her if she would like an alteration. All appeals to her taste she met gratefully, but saw nothing to alter. His efforts at exact courtesy and formal tenderness had no defect for her. She filled up all blanks with unmanifested perfections, interpreting him as she interpreted the works of Providence, and accounting for seeming discords by her own deafness to the higher harmonies. And there are many blanks left in the weeks of courtship which a loving faith fills with happy assurance.\n\n“Now, my dear Dorothea, I wish you to favor me by pointing out which room you would like to have as your boudoir,” said Mr. Casaubon, showing that his views of the womanly nature were sufficiently large to include that requirement.\n\n“It is very kind of you to think of that,” said Dorothea, “but I assure you I would rather have all those matters decided for me. I shall be much happier to take everything as it is—just as you have been used to have it, or as you will yourself choose it to be. I have no motive for wishing anything else.”\n\n“Oh, Dodo,” said Celia, “will you not have the bow-windowed room up-stairs?”\n\nMr. Casaubon led the way thither. The bow-window looked down the avenue of limes; the furniture was all of a faded blue, and there were miniatures of ladies and gentlemen with powdered hair hanging in a group. A piece of tapestry over a door also showed a blue-green world with a pale stag in it. The chairs and tables were thin-legged and easy to upset. It was a room where one might fancy the ghost of a tight-laced lady revisiting the scene of her embroidery. A light bookcase contained duodecimo volumes of polite literature in calf, completing the furniture.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. Brooke, “this would be a pretty room with some new hangings, sofas, and that sort of thing. A little bare now.”\n\n“No, uncle,” said Dorothea, eagerly. “Pray do not speak of altering anything. There are so many other things in the world that want altering—I like to take these things as they are. And you like them as they are, don’t you?” she added, looking at Mr. Casaubon. “Perhaps this was your mother’s room when she was young.”\n\n“It was,” he said, with his slow bend of the head.\n\n“This is your mother,” said Dorothea, who had turned to examine the group of miniatures. “It is like the tiny one you brought me; only, I should think, a better portrait. And this one opposite, who is this?”\n\n“Her elder sister. They were, like you and your sister, the only two children of their parents, who hang above them, you see.”\n\n“The sister is pretty,” said Celia, implying that she thought less favorably of Mr. Casaubon’s mother. It was a new opening to Celia’s imagination, that he came of a family who had all been young in their time—the ladies wearing necklaces.\n\n“It is a peculiar face,” said Dorothea, looking closely. “Those deep gray eyes rather near together—and the delicate irregular nose with a sort of ripple in it—and all the powdered curls hanging backward. Altogether it seems to me peculiar rather than pretty. There is not even a family likeness between her and your mother.”\n\n“No. And they were not alike in their lot.”\n\n“You did not mention her to me,” said Dorothea.\n\n“My aunt made an unfortunate marriage. I never saw her.”\n\nDorothea wondered a little, but felt that it would be indelicate just then to ask for any information which Mr. Casaubon did not proffer, and she turned to the window to admire the view. The sun had lately pierced the gray, and the avenue of limes cast shadows.\n\n“Shall we not walk in the garden now?” said Dorothea.\n\n“And you would like to see the church, you know,” said Mr. Brooke. “It is a droll little church. And the village. It all lies in a nut-shell. By the way, it will suit you, Dorothea; for the cottages are like a row of alms-houses—little gardens, gilly-flowers, that sort of thing.”\n\n“Yes, please,” said Dorothea, looking at Mr. Casaubon, “I should like to see all that.” She had got nothing from him more graphic about the Lowick cottages than that they were “not bad.”\n\nThey were soon on a gravel walk which led chiefly between grassy borders and clumps of trees, this being the nearest way to the church, Mr. Casaubon said. At the little gate leading into the churchyard there was a pause while Mr. Casaubon went to the parsonage close by to fetch a key. Celia, who had been hanging a little in the rear, came up presently, when she saw that Mr. Casaubon was gone away, and said in her easy staccato, which always seemed to contradict the suspicion of any malicious intent—\n\n“Do you know, Dorothea, I saw some one quite young coming up one of the walks.”\n\n“Is that astonishing, Celia?”\n\n“There may be a young gardener, you know—why not?” said Mr. Brooke. “I told Casaubon he should change his gardener.”\n\n“No, not a gardener,” said Celia; “a gentleman with a sketch-book. He had light-brown curls. I only saw his back. But he was quite young.”\n\n“The curate’s son, perhaps,” said Mr. Brooke. “Ah, there is Casaubon again, and Tucker with him. He is going to introduce Tucker. You don’t know Tucker yet.”\n\nMr. Tucker was the middle-aged curate, one of the “inferior clergy,” who are usually not wanting in sons. But after the introduction, the conversation did not lead to any question about his family, and the startling apparition of youthfulness was forgotten by every one but Celia. She inwardly declined to believe that the light-brown curls and slim figure could have any relationship to Mr. Tucker, who was just as old and musty-looking as she would have expected Mr. Casaubon’s curate to be; doubtless an excellent man who would go to heaven (for Celia wished not to be unprincipled), but the corners of his mouth were so unpleasant. Celia thought with some dismalness of the time she should have to spend as bridesmaid at Lowick, while the curate had probably no pretty little children whom she could like, irrespective of principle.\n\nMr. Tucker was invaluable in their walk; and perhaps Mr. Casaubon had not been without foresight on this head, the curate being able to answer all Dorothea’s questions about the villagers and the other parishioners. Everybody, he assured her, was well off in Lowick: not a cottager in those double cottages at a low rent but kept a pig, and the strips of garden at the back were well tended. The small boys wore excellent corduroy, the girls went out as tidy servants, or did a little straw-plaiting at home: no looms here, no Dissent; and though the public disposition was rather towards laying by money than towards spirituality, there was not much vice. The speckled fowls were so numerous that Mr. Brooke observed, “Your farmers leave some barley for the women to glean, I see. The poor folks here might have a fowl in their pot, as the good French king used to wish for all his people. The French eat a good many fowls—skinny fowls, you know.”\n\n“I think it was a very cheap wish of his,” said Dorothea, indignantly. “Are kings such monsters that a wish like that must be reckoned a royal virtue?”\n\n“And if he wished them a skinny fowl,” said Celia, “that would not be nice. But perhaps he wished them to have fat fowls.”\n\n“Yes, but the word has dropped out of the text, or perhaps was subauditum; that is, present in the king’s mind, but not uttered,” said Mr. Casaubon, smiling and bending his head towards Celia, who immediately dropped backward a little, because she could not bear Mr. Casaubon to blink at her.\n\nDorothea sank into silence on the way back to the house. She felt some disappointment, of which she was yet ashamed, that there was nothing for her to do in Lowick; and in the next few minutes her mind had glanced over the possibility, which she would have preferred, of finding that her home would be in a parish which had a larger share of the world’s misery, so that she might have had more active duties in it. Then, recurring to the future actually before her, she made a picture of more complete devotion to Mr. Casaubon’s aims in which she would await new duties. Many such might reveal themselves to the higher knowledge gained by her in that companionship.\n\nMr. Tucker soon left them, having some clerical work which would not allow him to lunch at the Hall; and as they were re-entering the garden through the little gate, Mr. Casaubon said—\n\n“You seem a little sad, Dorothea. I trust you are pleased with what you have seen.”\n\n“I am feeling something which is perhaps foolish and wrong,” answered Dorothea, with her usual openness—“almost wishing that the people wanted more to be done for them here. I have known so few ways of making my life good for anything. Of course, my notions of usefulness must be narrow. I must learn new ways of helping people.”\n\n“Doubtless,” said Mr. Casaubon. “Each position has its corresponding duties. Yours, I trust, as the mistress of Lowick, will not leave any yearning unfulfilled.”\n\n“Indeed, I believe that,” said Dorothea, earnestly. “Do not suppose that I am sad.”\n\n“That is well. But, if you are not tired, we will take another way to the house than that by which we came.”\n\nDorothea was not at all tired, and a little circuit was made towards a fine yew-tree, the chief hereditary glory of the grounds on this side of the house. As they approached it, a figure, conspicuous on a dark background of evergreens, was seated on a bench, sketching the old tree. Mr. Brooke, who was walking in front with Celia, turned his head, and said—\n\n“Who is that youngster, Casaubon?”\n\nThey had come very near when Mr. Casaubon answered—\n\n“That is a young relative of mine, a second cousin: the grandson, in fact,” he added, looking at Dorothea, “of the lady whose portrait you have been noticing, my aunt Julia.”\n\nThe young man had laid down his sketch-book and risen. His bushy light-brown curls, as well as his youthfulness, identified him at once with Celia’s apparition.\n\n“Dorothea, let me introduce to you my cousin, Mr. Ladislaw. Will, this is Miss Brooke.”\n\nThe cousin was so close now, that, when he lifted his hat, Dorothea could see a pair of gray eyes rather near together, a delicate irregular nose with a little ripple in it, and hair falling backward; but there was a mouth and chin of a more prominent, threatening aspect than belonged to the type of the grandmother’s miniature. Young Ladislaw did not feel it necessary to smile, as if he were charmed with this introduction to his future second cousin and her relatives; but wore rather a pouting air of discontent.\n\n“You are an artist, I see,” said Mr. Brooke, taking up the sketch-book and turning it over in his unceremonious fashion.\n\n“No, I only sketch a little. There is nothing fit to be seen there,” said young Ladislaw, coloring, perhaps with temper rather than modesty.\n\n“Oh, come, this is a nice bit, now. I did a little in this way myself at one time, you know. Look here, now; this is what I call a nice thing, done with what we used to call brio.” Mr. Brooke held out towards the two girls a large colored sketch of stony ground and trees, with a pool.\n\n“I am no judge of these things,” said Dorothea, not coldly, but with an eager deprecation of the appeal to her. “You know, uncle, I never see the beauty of those pictures which you say are so much praised. They are a language I do not understand. I suppose there is some relation between pictures and nature which I am too ignorant to feel—just as you see what a Greek sentence stands for which means nothing to me.” Dorothea looked up at Mr. Casaubon, who bowed his head towards her, while Mr. Brooke said, smiling nonchalantly—\n\n“Bless me, now, how different people are! But you had a bad style of teaching, you know—else this is just the thing for girls—sketching, fine art and so on. But you took to drawing plans; you don’t understand morbidezza, and that kind of thing. You will come to my house, I hope, and I will show you what I did in this way,” he continued, turning to young Ladislaw, who had to be recalled from his preoccupation in observing Dorothea. Ladislaw had made up his mind that she must be an unpleasant girl, since she was going to marry Casaubon, and what she said of her stupidity about pictures would have confirmed that opinion even if he had believed her. As it was, he took her words for a covert judgment, and was certain that she thought his sketch detestable. There was too much cleverness in her apology: she was laughing both at her uncle and himself. But what a voice! It was like the voice of a soul that had once lived in an Aeolian harp. This must be one of Nature’s inconsistencies. There could be no sort of passion in a girl who would marry Casaubon. But he turned from her, and bowed his thanks for Mr. Brooke’s invitation.\n\n“We will turn over my Italian engravings together,” continued that good-natured man. “I have no end of those things, that I have laid by for years. One gets rusty in this part of the country, you know. Not you, Casaubon; you stick to your studies; but my best ideas get undermost—out of use, you know. You clever young men must guard against indolence. I was too indolent, you know: else I might have been anywhere at one time.”\n\n“That is a seasonable admonition,” said Mr. Casaubon; “but now we will pass on to the house, lest the young ladies should be tired of standing.”\n\nWhen their backs were turned, young Ladislaw sat down to go on with his sketching, and as he did so his face broke into an expression of amusement which increased as he went on drawing, till at last he threw back his head and laughed aloud. Partly it was the reception of his own artistic production that tickled him; partly the notion of his grave cousin as the lover of that girl; and partly Mr. Brooke’s definition of the place he might have held but for the impediment of indolence. Mr. Will Ladislaw’s sense of the ludicrous lit up his features very agreeably: it was the pure enjoyment of comicality, and had no mixture of sneering and self-exaltation.\n\n“What is your nephew going to do with himself, Casaubon?” said Mr. Brooke, as they went on.\n\n“My cousin, you mean—not my nephew.”\n\n“Yes, yes, cousin. But in the way of a career, you know.”\n\n“The answer to that question is painfully doubtful. On leaving Rugby he declined to go to an English university, where I would gladly have placed him, and chose what I must consider the anomalous course of studying at Heidelberg. And now he wants to go abroad again, without any special object, save the vague purpose of what he calls culture, preparation for he knows not what. He declines to choose a profession.”\n\n“He has no means but what you furnish, I suppose.”\n\n“I have always given him and his friends reason to understand that I would furnish in moderation what was necessary for providing him with a scholarly education, and launching him respectably. I am therefore bound to fulfil the expectation so raised,” said Mr. Casaubon, putting his conduct in the light of mere rectitude: a trait of delicacy which Dorothea noticed with admiration.\n\n“He has a thirst for travelling; perhaps he may turn out a Bruce or a Mungo Park,” said Mr. Brooke. “I had a notion of that myself at one time.”\n\n“No, he has no bent towards exploration, or the enlargement of our geognosis: that would be a special purpose which I could recognize with some approbation, though without felicitating him on a career which so often ends in premature and violent death. But so far is he from having any desire for a more accurate knowledge of the earth’s surface, that he said he should prefer not to know the sources of the Nile, and that there should be some unknown regions preserved as hunting grounds for the poetic imagination.”\n\n“Well, there is something in that, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, who had certainly an impartial mind.\n\n“It is, I fear, nothing more than a part of his general inaccuracy and indisposition to thoroughness of all kinds, which would be a bad augury for him in any profession, civil or sacred, even were he so far submissive to ordinary rule as to choose one.”\n\n“Perhaps he has conscientious scruples founded on his own unfitness,” said Dorothea, who was interesting herself in finding a favorable explanation. “Because the law and medicine should be very serious professions to undertake, should they not? People’s lives and fortunes depend on them.”\n\n“Doubtless; but I fear that my young relative Will Ladislaw is chiefly determined in his aversion to these callings by a dislike to steady application, and to that kind of acquirement which is needful instrumentally, but is not charming or immediately inviting to self-indulgent taste. I have insisted to him on what Aristotle has stated with admirable brevity, that for the achievement of any work regarded as an end there must be a prior exercise of many energies or acquired facilities of a secondary order, demanding patience. I have pointed to my own manuscript volumes, which represent the toil of years preparatory to a work not yet accomplished. But in vain. To careful reasoning of this kind he replies by calling himself Pegasus, and every form of prescribed work ‘harness.’”\n\nCelia laughed. She was surprised to find that Mr. Casaubon could say something quite amusing.\n\n“Well, you know, he may turn out a Byron, a Chatterton, a Churchill—that sort of thing—there’s no telling,” said Mr. Brooke. “Shall you let him go to Italy, or wherever else he wants to go?”\n\n“Yes; I have agreed to furnish him with moderate supplies for a year or so; he asks no more. I shall let him be tried by the test of freedom.”\n\n“That is very kind of you,” said Dorothea, looking up at Mr. Casaubon with delight. “It is noble. After all, people may really have in them some vocation which is not quite plain to themselves, may they not? They may seem idle and weak because they are growing. We should be very patient with each other, I think.”\n\n“I suppose it is being engaged to be married that has made you think patience good,” said Celia, as soon as she and Dorothea were alone together, taking off their wrappings.\n\n“You mean that I am very impatient, Celia.”\n\n“Yes; when people don’t do and say just what you like.” Celia had become less afraid of “saying things” to Dorothea since this engagement: cleverness seemed to her more pitiable than ever.\n\n# CHAPTER X.\n\n“He had catched a great cold, had he had no other clothes to wear than the skin of a bear not yet killed.”—FULLER.\n\nYoung Ladislaw did not pay that visit to which Mr. Brooke had invited him, and only six days afterwards Mr. Casaubon mentioned that his young relative had started for the Continent, seeming by this cold vagueness to waive inquiry. Indeed, Will had declined to fix on any more precise destination than the entire area of Europe. Genius, he held, is necessarily intolerant of fetters: on the one hand it must have the utmost play for its spontaneity; on the other, it may confidently await those messages from the universe which summon it to its peculiar work, only placing itself in an attitude of receptivity towards all sublime chances. The attitudes of receptivity are various, and Will had sincerely tried many of them. He was not excessively fond of wine, but he had several times taken too much, simply as an experiment in that form of ecstasy; he had fasted till he was faint, and then supped on lobster; he had made himself ill with doses of opium. Nothing greatly original had resulted from these measures; and the effects of the opium had convinced him that there was an entire dissimilarity between his constitution and De Quincey’s. The superadded circumstance which would evolve the genius had not yet come; the universe had not yet beckoned. Even Caesar’s fortune at one time was but a grand presentiment. We know what a masquerade all development is, and what effective shapes may be disguised in helpless embryos. In fact, the world is full of hopeful analogies and handsome dubious eggs called possibilities. Will saw clearly enough the pitiable instances of long incubation producing no chick, and but for gratitude would have laughed at Casaubon, whose plodding application, rows of note-books, and small taper of learned theory exploring the tossed ruins of the world, seemed to enforce a moral entirely encouraging to Will’s generous reliance on the intentions of the universe with regard to himself. He held that reliance to be a mark of genius; and certainly it is no mark to the contrary; genius consisting neither in self-conceit nor in humility, but in a power to make or do, not anything in general, but something in particular. Let him start for the Continent, then, without our pronouncing on his future. Among all forms of mistake, prophecy is the most gratuitous.\n\nBut at present this caution against a too hasty judgment interests me more in relation to Mr. Casaubon than to his young cousin. If to Dorothea Mr. Casaubon had been the mere occasion which had set alight the fine inflammable material of her youthful illusions, does it follow that he was fairly represented in the minds of those less impassioned personages who have hitherto delivered their judgments concerning him? I protest against any absolute conclusion, any prejudice derived from Mrs. Cadwallader’s contempt for a neighboring clergyman’s alleged greatness of soul, or Sir James Chettam’s poor opinion of his rival’s legs,—from Mr. Brooke’s failure to elicit a companion’s ideas, or from Celia’s criticism of a middle-aged scholar’s personal appearance. I am not sure that the greatest man of his age, if ever that solitary superlative existed, could escape these unfavorable reflections of himself in various small mirrors; and even Milton, looking for his portrait in a spoon, must submit to have the facial angle of a bumpkin. Moreover, if Mr. Casaubon, speaking for himself, has rather a chilling rhetoric, it is not therefore certain that there is no good work or fine feeling in him. Did not an immortal physicist and interpreter of hieroglyphs write detestable verses? Has the theory of the solar system been advanced by graceful manners and conversational tact? Suppose we turn from outside estimates of a man, to wonder, with keener interest, what is the report of his own consciousness about his doings or capacity: with what hindrances he is carrying on his daily labors; what fading of hopes, or what deeper fixity of self-delusion the years are marking off within him; and with what spirit he wrestles against universal pressure, which will one day be too heavy for him, and bring his heart to its final pause. Doubtless his lot is important in his own eyes; and the chief reason that we think he asks too large a place in our consideration must be our want of room for him, since we refer him to the Divine regard with perfect confidence; nay, it is even held sublime for our neighbor to expect the utmost there, however little he may have got from us. Mr. Casaubon, too, was the centre of his own world; if he was liable to think that others were providentially made for him, and especially to consider them in the light of their fitness for the author of a “Key to all Mythologies,” this trait is not quite alien to us, and, like the other mendicant hopes of mortals, claims some of our pity.\n\nCertainly this affair of his marriage with Miss Brooke touched him more nearly than it did any one of the persons who have hitherto shown their disapproval of it, and in the present stage of things I feel more tenderly towards his experience of success than towards the disappointment of the amiable Sir James. For in truth, as the day fixed for his marriage came nearer, Mr. Casaubon did not find his spirits rising; nor did the contemplation of that matrimonial garden scene, where, as all experience showed, the path was to be bordered with flowers, prove persistently more enchanting to him than the accustomed vaults where he walked taper in hand. He did not confess to himself, still less could he have breathed to another, his surprise that though he had won a lovely and noble-hearted girl he had not won delight,—which he had also regarded as an object to be found by search. It is true that he knew all the classical passages implying the contrary; but knowing classical passages, we find, is a mode of motion, which explains why they leave so little extra force for their personal application.\n\nPoor Mr. Casaubon had imagined that his long studious bachelorhood had stored up for him a compound interest of enjoyment, and that large drafts on his affections would not fail to be honored; for we all of us, grave or light, get our thoughts entangled in metaphors, and act fatally on the strength of them. And now he was in danger of being saddened by the very conviction that his circumstances were unusually happy: there was nothing external by which he could account for a certain blankness of sensibility which came over him just when his expectant gladness should have been most lively, just when he exchanged the accustomed dulness of his Lowick library for his visits to the Grange. Here was a weary experience in which he was as utterly condemned to loneliness as in the despair which sometimes threatened him while toiling in the morass of authorship without seeming nearer to the goal. And his was that worst loneliness which would shrink from sympathy. He could not but wish that Dorothea should think him not less happy than the world would expect her successful suitor to be; and in relation to his authorship he leaned on her young trust and veneration, he liked to draw forth her fresh interest in listening, as a means of encouragement to himself: in talking to her he presented all his performance and intention with the reflected confidence of the pedagogue, and rid himself for the time of that chilling ideal audience which crowded his laborious uncreative hours with the vaporous pressure of Tartarean shades.\n\nFor to Dorothea, after that toy-box history of the world adapted to young ladies which had made the chief part of her education, Mr. Casaubon’s talk about his great book was full of new vistas; and this sense of revelation, this surprise of a nearer introduction to Stoics and Alexandrians, as people who had ideas not totally unlike her own, kept in abeyance for the time her usual eagerness for a binding theory which could bring her own life and doctrine into strict connection with that amazing past, and give the remotest sources of knowledge some bearing on her actions. That more complete teaching would come—Mr. Casaubon would tell her all that: she was looking forward to higher initiation in ideas, as she was looking forward to marriage, and blending her dim conceptions of both. It would be a great mistake to suppose that Dorothea would have cared about any share in Mr. Casaubon’s learning as mere accomplishment; for though opinion in the neighborhood of Freshitt and Tipton had pronounced her clever, that epithet would not have described her to circles in whose more precise vocabulary cleverness implies mere aptitude for knowing and doing, apart from character. All her eagerness for acquirement lay within that full current of sympathetic motive in which her ideas and impulses were habitually swept along. She did not want to deck herself with knowledge—to wear it loose from the nerves and blood that fed her action; and if she had written a book she must have done it as Saint Theresa did, under the command of an authority that constrained her conscience. But something she yearned for by which her life might be filled with action at once rational and ardent; and since the time was gone by for guiding visions and spiritual directors, since prayer heightened yearning but not instruction, what lamp was there but knowledge? Surely learned men kept the only oil; and who more learned than Mr. Casaubon?\n\nThus in these brief weeks Dorothea’s joyous grateful expectation was unbroken, and however her lover might occasionally be conscious of flatness, he could never refer it to any slackening of her affectionate interest.\n\nThe season was mild enough to encourage the project of extending the wedding journey as far as Rome, and Mr. Casaubon was anxious for this because he wished to inspect some manuscripts in the Vatican.\n\n“I still regret that your sister is not to accompany us,” he said one morning, some time after it had been ascertained that Celia objected to go, and that Dorothea did not wish for her companionship. “You will have many lonely hours, Dorothea, for I shall be constrained to make the utmost use of my time during our stay in Rome, and I should feel more at liberty if you had a companion.”\n\nThe words “I should feel more at liberty” grated on Dorothea. For the first time in speaking to Mr. Casaubon she colored from annoyance.\n\n“You must have misunderstood me very much,” she said, “if you think I should not enter into the value of your time—if you think that I should not willingly give up whatever interfered with your using it to the best purpose.”\n\n“That is very amiable in you, my dear Dorothea,” said Mr. Casaubon, not in the least noticing that she was hurt; “but if you had a lady as your companion, I could put you both under the care of a cicerone, and we could thus achieve two purposes in the same space of time.”\n\n“I beg you will not refer to this again,” said Dorothea, rather haughtily. But immediately she feared that she was wrong, and turning towards him she laid her hand on his, adding in a different tone, “Pray do not be anxious about me. I shall have so much to think of when I am alone. And Tantripp will be a sufficient companion, just to take care of me. I could not bear to have Celia: she would be miserable.”\n\nIt was time to dress. There was to be a dinner-party that day, the last of the parties which were held at the Grange as proper preliminaries to the wedding, and Dorothea was glad of a reason for moving away at once on the sound of the bell, as if she needed more than her usual amount of preparation. She was ashamed of being irritated from some cause she could not define even to herself; for though she had no intention to be untruthful, her reply had not touched the real hurt within her. Mr. Casaubon’s words had been quite reasonable, yet they had brought a vague instantaneous sense of aloofness on his part.\n\n“Surely I am in a strangely selfish weak state of mind,” she said to herself. “How can I have a husband who is so much above me without knowing that he needs me less than I need him?”\n\nHaving convinced herself that Mr. Casaubon was altogether right, she recovered her equanimity, and was an agreeable image of serene dignity when she came into the drawing-room in her silver-gray dress—the simple lines of her dark-brown hair parted over her brow and coiled massively behind, in keeping with the entire absence from her manner and expression of all search after mere effect. Sometimes when Dorothea was in company, there seemed to be as complete an air of repose about her as if she had been a picture of Santa Barbara looking out from her tower into the clear air; but these intervals of quietude made the energy of her speech and emotion the more remarked when some outward appeal had touched her.\n\nShe was naturally the subject of many observations this evening, for the dinner-party was large and rather more miscellaneous as to the male portion than any which had been held at the Grange since Mr. Brooke’s nieces had resided with him, so that the talking was done in duos and trios more or less inharmonious. There was the newly elected mayor of Middlemarch, who happened to be a manufacturer; the philanthropic banker his brother-in-law, who predominated so much in the town that some called him a Methodist, others a hypocrite, according to the resources of their vocabulary; and there were various professional men. In fact, Mrs. Cadwallader said that Brooke was beginning to treat the Middlemarchers, and that she preferred the farmers at the tithe-dinner, who drank her health unpretentiously, and were not ashamed of their grandfathers’ furniture. For in that part of the country, before reform had done its notable part in developing the political consciousness, there was a clearer distinction of ranks and a dimmer distinction of parties; so that Mr. Brooke’s miscellaneous invitations seemed to belong to that general laxity which came from his inordinate travel and habit of taking too much in the form of ideas.\n\nAlready, as Miss Brooke passed out of the dining-room, opportunity was found for some interjectional “asides.”\n\n“A fine woman, Miss Brooke! an uncommonly fine woman, by God!” said Mr. Standish, the old lawyer, who had been so long concerned with the landed gentry that he had become landed himself, and used that oath in a deep-mouthed manner as a sort of armorial bearings, stamping the speech of a man who held a good position.\n\nMr. Bulstrode, the banker, seemed to be addressed, but that gentleman disliked coarseness and profanity, and merely bowed. The remark was taken up by Mr. Chichely, a middle-aged bachelor and coursing celebrity, who had a complexion something like an Easter egg, a few hairs carefully arranged, and a carriage implying the consciousness of a distinguished appearance.\n\n“Yes, but not my style of woman: I like a woman who lays herself out a little more to please us. There should be a little filigree about a woman—something of the coquette. A man likes a sort of challenge. The more of a dead set she makes at you the better.”\n\n“There’s some truth in that,” said Mr. Standish, disposed to be genial. “And, by God, it’s usually the way with them. I suppose it answers some wise ends: Providence made them so, eh, Bulstrode?”\n\n“I should be disposed to refer coquetry to another source,” said Mr. Bulstrode. “I should rather refer it to the devil.”\n\n“Ay, to be sure, there should be a little devil in a woman,” said Mr. Chichely, whose study of the fair sex seemed to have been detrimental to his theology. “And I like them blond, with a certain gait, and a swan neck. Between ourselves, the mayor’s daughter is more to my taste than Miss Brooke or Miss Celia either. If I were a marrying man I should choose Miss Vincy before either of them.”\n\n“Well, make up, make up,” said Mr. Standish, jocosely; “you see the middle-aged fellows carry the day.”\n\nMr. Chichely shook his head with much meaning: he was not going to incur the certainty of being accepted by the woman he would choose.\n\nThe Miss Vincy who had the honor of being Mr. Chichely’s ideal was of course not present; for Mr. Brooke, always objecting to go too far, would not have chosen that his nieces should meet the daughter of a Middlemarch manufacturer, unless it were on a public occasion. The feminine part of the company included none whom Lady Chettam or Mrs. Cadwallader could object to; for Mrs. Renfrew, the colonel’s widow, was not only unexceptionable in point of breeding, but also interesting on the ground of her complaint, which puzzled the doctors, and seemed clearly a case wherein the fulness of professional knowledge might need the supplement of quackery. Lady Chettam, who attributed her own remarkable health to home-made bitters united with constant medical attendance, entered with much exercise of the imagination into Mrs. Renfrew’s account of symptoms, and into the amazing futility in her case of all strengthening medicines.\n\n“Where can all the strength of those medicines go, my dear?” said the mild but stately dowager, turning to Mrs. Cadwallader reflectively, when Mrs. Renfrew’s attention was called away.\n\n“It strengthens the disease,” said the Rector’s wife, much too well-born not to be an amateur in medicine. “Everything depends on the constitution: some people make fat, some blood, and some bile—that’s my view of the matter; and whatever they take is a sort of grist to the mill.”\n\n“Then she ought to take medicines that would reduce—reduce the disease, you know, if you are right, my dear. And I think what you say is reasonable.”\n\n“Certainly it is reasonable. You have two sorts of potatoes, fed on the same soil. One of them grows more and more watery—”\n\n“Ah! like this poor Mrs. Renfrew—that is what I think. Dropsy! There is no swelling yet—it is inward. I should say she ought to take drying medicines, shouldn’t you?—or a dry hot-air bath. Many things might be tried, of a drying nature.”\n\n“Let her try a certain person’s pamphlets,” said Mrs. Cadwallader in an undertone, seeing the gentlemen enter. “He does not want drying.”\n\n“Who, my dear?” said Lady Chettam, a charming woman, not so quick as to nullify the pleasure of explanation.\n\n“The bridegroom—Casaubon. He has certainly been drying up faster since the engagement: the flame of passion, I suppose.”\n\n“I should think he is far from having a good constitution,” said Lady Chettam, with a still deeper undertone. “And then his studies—so very dry, as you say.”\n\n“Really, by the side of Sir James, he looks like a death’s head skinned over for the occasion. Mark my words: in a year from this time that girl will hate him. She looks up to him as an oracle now, and by-and-by she will be at the other extreme. All flightiness!”\n\n“How very shocking! I fear she is headstrong. But tell me—you know all about him—is there anything very bad? What is the truth?”\n\n“The truth? he is as bad as the wrong physic—nasty to take, and sure to disagree.”\n\n“There could not be anything worse than that,” said Lady Chettam, with so vivid a conception of the physic that she seemed to have learned something exact about Mr. Casaubon’s disadvantages. “However, James will hear nothing against Miss Brooke. He says she is the mirror of women still.”\n\n“That is a generous make-believe of his. Depend upon it, he likes little Celia better, and she appreciates him. I hope you like my little Celia?”\n\n“Certainly; she is fonder of geraniums, and seems more docile, though not so fine a figure. But we were talking of physic. Tell me about this new young surgeon, Mr. Lydgate. I am told he is wonderfully clever: he certainly looks it—a fine brow indeed.”\n\n“He is a gentleman. I heard him talking to Humphrey. He talks well.”\n\n“Yes. Mr. Brooke says he is one of the Lydgates of Northumberland, really well connected. One does not expect it in a practitioner of that kind. For my own part, I like a medical man more on a footing with the servants; they are often all the cleverer. I assure you I found poor Hicks’s judgment unfailing; I never knew him wrong. He was coarse and butcher-like, but he knew my constitution. It was a loss to me his going off so suddenly. Dear me, what a very animated conversation Miss Brooke seems to be having with this Mr. Lydgate!”\n\n“She is talking cottages and hospitals with him,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, whose ears and power of interpretation were quick. “I believe he is a sort of philanthropist, so Brooke is sure to take him up.”\n\n“James,” said Lady Chettam when her son came near, “bring Mr. Lydgate and introduce him to me. I want to test him.”\n\nThe affable dowager declared herself delighted with this opportunity of making Mr. Lydgate’s acquaintance, having heard of his success in treating fever on a new plan.\n\nMr. Lydgate had the medical accomplishment of looking perfectly grave whatever nonsense was talked to him, and his dark steady eyes gave him impressiveness as a listener. He was as little as possible like the lamented Hicks, especially in a certain careless refinement about his toilet and utterance. Yet Lady Chettam gathered much confidence in him. He confirmed her view of her own constitution as being peculiar, by admitting that all constitutions might be called peculiar, and he did not deny that hers might be more peculiar than others. He did not approve of a too lowering system, including reckless cupping, nor, on the other hand, of incessant port wine and bark. He said “I think so” with an air of so much deference accompanying the insight of agreement, that she formed the most cordial opinion of his talents.\n\n“I am quite pleased with your protege,” she said to Mr. Brooke before going away.\n\n“My protege?—dear me!—who is that?” said Mr. Brooke.\n\n“This young Lydgate, the new doctor. He seems to me to understand his profession admirably.”\n\n“Oh, Lydgate! he is not my protege, you know; only I knew an uncle of his who sent me a letter about him. However, I think he is likely to be first-rate—has studied in Paris, knew Broussais; has ideas, you know—wants to raise the profession.”\n\n“Lydgate has lots of ideas, quite new, about ventilation and diet, that sort of thing,” resumed Mr. Brooke, after he had handed out Lady Chettam, and had returned to be civil to a group of Middlemarchers.\n\n“Hang it, do you think that is quite sound?—upsetting the old treatment, which has made Englishmen what they are?” said Mr. Standish.\n\n“Medical knowledge is at a low ebb among us,” said Mr. Bulstrode, who spoke in a subdued tone, and had rather a sickly air. “I, for my part, hail the advent of Mr. Lydgate. I hope to find good reason for confiding the new hospital to his management.”\n\n“That is all very fine,” replied Mr. Standish, who was not fond of Mr. Bulstrode; “if you like him to try experiments on your hospital patients, and kill a few people for charity I have no objection. But I am not going to hand money out of my purse to have experiments tried on me. I like treatment that has been tested a little.”\n\n“Well, you know, Standish, every dose you take is an experiment-an experiment, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, nodding towards the lawyer.\n\n“Oh, if you talk in that sense!” said Mr. Standish, with as much disgust at such non-legal quibbling as a man can well betray towards a valuable client.\n\n“I should be glad of any treatment that would cure me without reducing me to a skeleton, like poor Grainger,” said Mr. Vincy, the mayor, a florid man, who would have served for a study of flesh in striking contrast with the Franciscan tints of Mr. Bulstrode. “It’s an uncommonly dangerous thing to be left without any padding against the shafts of disease, as somebody said,—and I think it a very good expression myself.”\n\nMr. Lydgate, of course, was out of hearing. He had quitted the party early, and would have thought it altogether tedious but for the novelty of certain introductions, especially the introduction to Miss Brooke, whose youthful bloom, with her approaching marriage to that faded scholar, and her interest in matters socially useful, gave her the piquancy of an unusual combination.\n\n“She is a good creature—that fine girl—but a little too earnest,” he thought. “It is troublesome to talk to such women. They are always wanting reasons, yet they are too ignorant to understand the merits of any question, and usually fall back on their moral sense to settle things after their own taste.”\n\nEvidently Miss Brooke was not Mr. Lydgate’s style of woman any more than Mr. Chichely’s. Considered, indeed, in relation to the latter, whose mind was matured, she was altogether a mistake, and calculated to shock his trust in final causes, including the adaptation of fine young women to purplefaced bachelors. But Lydgate was less ripe, and might possibly have experience before him which would modify his opinion as to the most excellent things in woman.\n\nMiss Brooke, however, was not again seen by either of these gentlemen under her maiden name. Not long after that dinner-party she had become Mrs. Casaubon, and was on her way to Rome.\n\n# CHAPTER XI.\n\nBut deeds and language such as men do use, And persons such as comedy would choose, When she would show an image of the times, And sport with human follies, not with crimes. —BEN JONSON.\n\nLydgate, in fact, was already conscious of being fascinated by a woman strikingly different from Miss Brooke: he did not in the least suppose that he had lost his balance and fallen in love, but he had said of that particular woman, “She is grace itself; she is perfectly lovely and accomplished. That is what a woman ought to be: she ought to produce the effect of exquisite music.” Plain women he regarded as he did the other severe facts of life, to be faced with philosophy and investigated by science. But Rosamond Vincy seemed to have the true melodic charm; and when a man has seen the woman whom he would have chosen if he had intended to marry speedily, his remaining a bachelor will usually depend on her resolution rather than on his. Lydgate believed that he should not marry for several years: not marry until he had trodden out a good clear path for himself away from the broad road which was quite ready made. He had seen Miss Vincy above his horizon almost as long as it had taken Mr. Casaubon to become engaged and married: but this learned gentleman was possessed of a fortune; he had assembled his voluminous notes, and had made that sort of reputation which precedes performance,—often the larger part of a man’s fame. He took a wife, as we have seen, to adorn the remaining quadrant of his course, and be a little moon that would cause hardly a calculable perturbation. But Lydgate was young, poor, ambitious. He had his half-century before him instead of behind him, and he had come to Middlemarch bent on doing many things that were not directly fitted to make his fortune or even secure him a good income. To a man under such circumstances, taking a wife is something more than a question of adornment, however highly he may rate this; and Lydgate was disposed to give it the first place among wifely functions. To his taste, guided by a single conversation, here was the point on which Miss Brooke would be found wanting, notwithstanding her undeniable beauty. She did not look at things from the proper feminine angle. The society of such women was about as relaxing as going from your work to teach the second form, instead of reclining in a paradise with sweet laughs for bird-notes, and blue eyes for a heaven.\n\nCertainly nothing at present could seem much less important to Lydgate than the turn of Miss Brooke’s mind, or to Miss Brooke than the qualities of the woman who had attracted this young surgeon. But any one watching keenly the stealthy convergence of human lots, sees a slow preparation of effects from one life on another, which tells like a calculated irony on the indifference or the frozen stare with which we look at our unintroduced neighbor. Destiny stands by sarcastic with our dramatis personae folded in her hand.\n\nOld provincial society had its share of this subtle movement: had not only its striking downfalls, its brilliant young professional dandies who ended by living up an entry with a drab and six children for their establishment, but also those less marked vicissitudes which are constantly shifting the boundaries of social intercourse, and begetting new consciousness of interdependence. Some slipped a little downward, some got higher footing: people denied aspirates, gained wealth, and fastidious gentlemen stood for boroughs; some were caught in political currents, some in ecclesiastical, and perhaps found themselves surprisingly grouped in consequence; while a few personages or families that stood with rocky firmness amid all this fluctuation, were slowly presenting new aspects in spite of solidity, and altering with the double change of self and beholder. Municipal town and rural parish gradually made fresh threads of connection—gradually, as the old stocking gave way to the savings-bank, and the worship of the solar guinea became extinct; while squires and baronets, and even lords who had once lived blamelessly afar from the civic mind, gathered the faultiness of closer acquaintanceship. Settlers, too, came from distant counties, some with an alarming novelty of skill, others with an offensive advantage in cunning. In fact, much the same sort of movement and mixture went on in old England as we find in older Herodotus, who also, in telling what had been, thought it well to take a woman’s lot for his starting-point; though Io, as a maiden apparently beguiled by attractive merchandise, was the reverse of Miss Brooke, and in this respect perhaps bore more resemblance to Rosamond Vincy, who had excellent taste in costume, with that nymph-like figure and pure blondness which give the largest range to choice in the flow and color of drapery. But these things made only part of her charm. She was admitted to be the flower of Mrs. Lemon’s school, the chief school in the county, where the teaching included all that was demanded in the accomplished female—even to extras, such as the getting in and out of a carriage. Mrs. Lemon herself had always held up Miss Vincy as an example: no pupil, she said, exceeded that young lady for mental acquisition and propriety of speech, while her musical execution was quite exceptional. We cannot help the way in which people speak of us, and probably if Mrs. Lemon had undertaken to describe Juliet or Imogen, these heroines would not have seemed poetical. The first vision of Rosamond would have been enough with most judges to dispel any prejudice excited by Mrs. Lemon’s praise.\n\nLydgate could not be long in Middlemarch without having that agreeable vision, or even without making the acquaintance of the Vincy family; for though Mr. Peacock, whose practice he had paid something to enter on, had not been their doctor (Mrs. Vincy not liking the lowering system adopted by him), he had many patients among their connections and acquaintances. For who of any consequence in Middlemarch was not connected or at least acquainted with the Vincys? They were old manufacturers, and had kept a good house for three generations, in which there had naturally been much intermarrying with neighbors more or less decidedly genteel. Mr. Vincy’s sister had made a wealthy match in accepting Mr. Bulstrode, who, however, as a man not born in the town, and altogether of dimly known origin, was considered to have done well in uniting himself with a real Middlemarch family; on the other hand, Mr. Vincy had descended a little, having taken an innkeeper’s daughter. But on this side too there was a cheering sense of money; for Mrs. Vincy’s sister had been second wife to rich old Mr. Featherstone, and had died childless years ago, so that her nephews and nieces might be supposed to touch the affections of the widower. And it happened that Mr. Bulstrode and Mr. Featherstone, two of Peacock’s most important patients, had, from different causes, given an especially good reception to his successor, who had raised some partisanship as well as discussion. Mr. Wrench, medical attendant to the Vincy family, very early had grounds for thinking lightly of Lydgate’s professional discretion, and there was no report about him which was not retailed at the Vincys’, where visitors were frequent. Mr. Vincy was more inclined to general good-fellowship than to taking sides, but there was no need for him to be hasty in making any new man acquaintance. Rosamond silently wished that her father would invite Mr. Lydgate. She was tired of the faces and figures she had always been used to—the various irregular profiles and gaits and turns of phrase distinguishing those Middlemarch young men whom she had known as boys. She had been at school with girls of higher position, whose brothers, she felt sure, it would have been possible for her to be more interested in, than in these inevitable Middlemarch companions. But she would not have chosen to mention her wish to her father; and he, for his part, was in no hurry on the subject. An alderman about to be mayor must by-and-by enlarge his dinner-parties, but at present there were plenty of guests at his well-spread table.\n\nThat table often remained covered with the relics of the family breakfast long after Mr. Vincy had gone with his second son to the warehouse, and when Miss Morgan was already far on in morning lessons with the younger girls in the schoolroom. It awaited the family laggard, who found any sort of inconvenience (to others) less disagreeable than getting up when he was called. This was the case one morning of the October in which we have lately seen Mr. Casaubon visiting the Grange; and though the room was a little overheated with the fire, which had sent the spaniel panting to a remote corner, Rosamond, for some reason, continued to sit at her embroidery longer than usual, now and then giving herself a little shake, and laying her work on her knee to contemplate it with an air of hesitating weariness. Her mamma, who had returned from an excursion to the kitchen, sat on the other side of the small work-table with an air of more entire placidity, until, the clock again giving notice that it was going to strike, she looked up from the lace-mending which was occupying her plump fingers and rang the bell.\n\n“Knock at Mr. Fred’s door again, Pritchard, and tell him it has struck half-past ten.”\n\nThis was said without any change in the radiant good-humor of Mrs. Vincy’s face, in which forty-five years had delved neither angles nor parallels; and pushing back her pink capstrings, she let her work rest on her lap, while she looked admiringly at her daughter.\n\n“Mamma,” said Rosamond, “when Fred comes down I wish you would not let him have red herrings. I cannot bear the smell of them all over the house at this hour of the morning.”\n\n“Oh, my dear, you are so hard on your brothers! It is the only fault I have to find with you. You are the sweetest temper in the world, but you are so tetchy with your brothers.”\n\n“Not tetchy, mamma: you never hear me speak in an unladylike way.”\n\n“Well, but you want to deny them things.”\n\n“Brothers are so unpleasant.”\n\n“Oh, my dear, you must allow for young men. Be thankful if they have good hearts. A woman must learn to put up with little things. You will be married some day.”\n\n“Not to any one who is like Fred.”\n\n“Don’t decry your own brother, my dear. Few young men have less against them, although he couldn’t take his degree—I’m sure I can’t understand why, for he seems to me most clever. And you know yourself he was thought equal to the best society at college. So particular as you are, my dear, I wonder you are not glad to have such a gentlemanly young man for a brother. You are always finding fault with Bob because he is not Fred.”\n\n“Oh no, mamma, only because he is Bob.”\n\n“Well, my dear, you will not find any Middlemarch young man who has not something against him.”\n\n“But”—here Rosamond’s face broke into a smile which suddenly revealed two dimples. She herself thought unfavorably of these dimples and smiled little in general society. “But I shall not marry any Middlemarch young man.”\n\n“So it seems, my love, for you have as good as refused the pick of them; and if there’s better to be had, I’m sure there’s no girl better deserves it.”\n\n“Excuse me, mamma—I wish you would not say, ‘the pick of them.’”\n\n“Why, what else are they?”\n\n“I mean, mamma, it is rather a vulgar expression.”\n\n“Very likely, my dear; I never was a good speaker. What should I say?”\n\n“The best of them.”\n\n“Why, that seems just as plain and common. If I had had time to think, I should have said, ‘the most superior young men.’ But with your education you must know.”\n\n“What must Rosy know, mother?” said Mr. Fred, who had slid in unobserved through the half-open door while the ladies were bending over their work, and now going up to the fire stood with his back towards it, warming the soles of his slippers.\n\n“Whether it’s right to say ‘superior young men,’” said Mrs. Vincy, ringing the bell.\n\n“Oh, there are so many superior teas and sugars now. Superior is getting to be shopkeepers’ slang.”\n\n“Are you beginning to dislike slang, then?” said Rosamond, with mild gravity.\n\n“Only the wrong sort. All choice of words is slang. It marks a class.”\n\n“There is correct English: that is not slang.”\n\n“I beg your pardon: correct English is the slang of prigs who write history and essays. And the strongest slang of all is the slang of poets.”\n\n“You will say anything, Fred, to gain your point.”\n\n“Well, tell me whether it is slang or poetry to call an ox a leg-plaiter.”\n\n“Of course you can call it poetry if you like.”\n\n“Aha, Miss Rosy, you don’t know Homer from slang. I shall invent a new game; I shall write bits of slang and poetry on slips, and give them to you to separate.”\n\n“Dear me, how amusing it is to hear young people talk!” said Mrs. Vincy, with cheerful admiration.\n\n“Have you got nothing else for my breakfast, Pritchard?” said Fred, to the servant who brought in coffee and buttered toast; while he walked round the table surveying the ham, potted beef, and other cold remnants, with an air of silent rejection, and polite forbearance from signs of disgust.\n\n“Should you like eggs, sir?”\n\n“Eggs, no! Bring me a grilled bone.”\n\n“Really, Fred,” said Rosamond, when the servant had left the room, “if you must have hot things for breakfast, I wish you would come down earlier. You can get up at six o’clock to go out hunting; I cannot understand why you find it so difficult to get up on other mornings.”\n\n“That is your want of understanding, Rosy. I can get up to go hunting because I like it.”\n\n“What would you think of me if I came down two hours after every one else and ordered grilled bone?”\n\n“I should think you were an uncommonly fast young lady,” said Fred, eating his toast with the utmost composure.\n\n“I cannot see why brothers are to make themselves disagreeable, any more than sisters.”\n\n“I don’t make myself disagreeable; it is you who find me so. Disagreeable is a word that describes your feelings and not my actions.”\n\n“I think it describes the smell of grilled bone.”\n\n“Not at all. It describes a sensation in your little nose associated with certain finicking notions which are the classics of Mrs. Lemon’s school. Look at my mother; you don’t see her objecting to everything except what she does herself. She is my notion of a pleasant woman.”\n\n“Bless you both, my dears, and don’t quarrel,” said Mrs. Vincy, with motherly cordiality. “Come, Fred, tell us all about the new doctor. How is your uncle pleased with him?”\n\n“Pretty well, I think. He asks Lydgate all sorts of questions and then screws up his face while he hears the answers, as if they were pinching his toes. That’s his way. Ah, here comes my grilled bone.”\n\n“But how came you to stay out so late, my dear? You only said you were going to your uncle’s.”\n\n“Oh, I dined at Plymdale’s. We had whist. Lydgate was there too.”\n\n“And what do you think of him? He is very gentlemanly, I suppose. They say he is of excellent family—his relations quite county people.”\n\n“Yes,” said Fred. “There was a Lydgate at John’s who spent no end of money. I find this man is a second cousin of his. But rich men may have very poor devils for second cousins.”\n\n“It always makes a difference, though, to be of good family,” said Rosamond, with a tone of decision which showed that she had thought on this subject. Rosamond felt that she might have been happier if she had not been the daughter of a Middlemarch manufacturer. She disliked anything which reminded her that her mother’s father had been an innkeeper. Certainly any one remembering the fact might think that Mrs. Vincy had the air of a very handsome good-humored landlady, accustomed to the most capricious orders of gentlemen.\n\n“I thought it was odd his name was Tertius,” said the bright-faced matron, “but of course it’s a name in the family. But now, tell us exactly what sort of man he is.”\n\n“Oh, tallish, dark, clever—talks well—rather a prig, I think.”\n\n“I never can make out what you mean by a prig,” said Rosamond.\n\n“A fellow who wants to show that he has opinions.”\n\n“Why, my dear, doctors must have opinions,” said Mrs. Vincy. “What are they there for else?”\n\n“Yes, mother, the opinions they are paid for. But a prig is a fellow who is always making you a present of his opinions.”\n\n“I suppose Mary Garth admires Mr. Lydgate,” said Rosamond, not without a touch of innuendo.\n\n“Really, I can’t say.” said Fred, rather glumly, as he left the table, and taking up a novel which he had brought down with him, threw himself into an arm-chair. “If you are jealous of her, go oftener to Stone Court yourself and eclipse her.”\n\n“I wish you would not be so vulgar, Fred. If you have finished, pray ring the bell.”\n\n“It is true, though—what your brother says, Rosamond,” Mrs. Vincy began, when the servant had cleared the table. “It is a thousand pities you haven’t patience to go and see your uncle more, so proud of you as he is, and wanted you to live with him. There’s no knowing what he might have done for you as well as for Fred. God knows, I’m fond of having you at home with me, but I can part with my children for their good. And now it stands to reason that your uncle Featherstone will do something for Mary Garth.”\n\n“Mary Garth can bear being at Stone Court, because she likes that better than being a governess,” said Rosamond, folding up her work. “I would rather not have anything left to me if I must earn it by enduring much of my uncle’s cough and his ugly relations.”\n\n“He can’t be long for this world, my dear; I wouldn’t hasten his end, but what with asthma and that inward complaint, let us hope there is something better for him in another. And I have no ill-will towards Mary Garth, but there’s justice to be thought of. And Mr. Featherstone’s first wife brought him no money, as my sister did. Her nieces and nephews can’t have so much claim as my sister’s. And I must say I think Mary Garth a dreadful plain girl—more fit for a governess.”\n\n“Every one would not agree with you there, mother,” said Fred, who seemed to be able to read and listen too.\n\n“Well, my dear,” said Mrs. Vincy, wheeling skilfully, “if she had some fortune left her,—a man marries his wife’s relations, and the Garths are so poor, and live in such a small way. But I shall leave you to your studies, my dear; for I must go and do some shopping.”\n\n“Fred’s studies are not very deep,” said Rosamond, rising with her mamma, “he is only reading a novel.”\n\n“Well, well, by-and-by he’ll go to his Latin and things,” said Mrs. Vincy, soothingly, stroking her son’s head. “There’s a fire in the smoking-room on purpose. It’s your father’s wish, you know—Fred, my dear—and I always tell him you will be good, and go to college again to take your degree.”\n\nFred drew his mother’s hand down to his lips, but said nothing.\n\n“I suppose you are not going out riding to-day?” said Rosamond, lingering a little after her mamma was gone.\n\n“No; why?”\n\n“Papa says I may have the chestnut to ride now.”\n\n“You can go with me to-morrow, if you like. Only I am going to Stone Court, remember.”\n\n“I want to ride so much, it is indifferent to me where we go.” Rosamond really wished to go to Stone Court, of all other places.\n\n“Oh, I say, Rosy,” said Fred, as she was passing out of the room, “if you are going to the piano, let me come and play some airs with you.”\n\n“Pray do not ask me this morning.”\n\n“Why not this morning?”\n\n“Really, Fred, I wish you would leave off playing the flute. A man looks very silly playing the flute. And you play so out of tune.”\n\n“When next any one makes love to you, Miss Rosamond, I will tell him how obliging you are.”\n\n“Why should you expect me to oblige you by hearing you play the flute, any more than I should expect you to oblige me by not playing it?”\n\n“And why should you expect me to take you out riding?”\n\nThis question led to an adjustment, for Rosamond had set her mind on that particular ride.\n\nSo Fred was gratified with nearly an hour’s practice of “Ar hyd y nos,” “Ye banks and braes,” and other favorite airs from his “Instructor on the Flute;” a wheezy performance, into which he threw much ambition and an irrepressible hopefulness.\n\n# CHAPTER XII.\n\nHe had more tow on his distaffe Than Gerveis knew. —CHAUCER.\n\nThe ride to Stone Court, which Fred and Rosamond took the next morning, lay through a pretty bit of midland landscape, almost all meadows and pastures, with hedgerows still allowed to grow in bushy beauty and to spread out coral fruit for the birds. Little details gave each field a particular physiognomy, dear to the eyes that have looked on them from childhood: the pool in the corner where the grasses were dank and trees leaned whisperingly; the great oak shadowing a bare place in mid-pasture; the high bank where the ash-trees grew; the sudden slope of the old marl-pit making a red background for the burdock; the huddled roofs and ricks of the homestead without a traceable way of approach; the gray gate and fences against the depths of the bordering wood; and the stray hovel, its old, old thatch full of mossy hills and valleys with wondrous modulations of light and shadow such as we travel far to see in later life, and see larger, but not more beautiful. These are the things that make the gamut of joy in landscape to midland-bred souls—the things they toddled among, or perhaps learned by heart standing between their father’s knees while he drove leisurely.\n\nBut the road, even the byroad, was excellent; for Lowick, as we have seen, was not a parish of muddy lanes and poor tenants; and it was into Lowick parish that Fred and Rosamond entered after a couple of miles’ riding. Another mile would bring them to Stone Court, and at the end of the first half, the house was already visible, looking as if it had been arrested in its growth toward a stone mansion by an unexpected budding of farm-buildings on its left flank, which had hindered it from becoming anything more than the substantial dwelling of a gentleman farmer. It was not the less agreeable an object in the distance for the cluster of pinnacled corn-ricks which balanced the fine row of walnuts on the right.\n\nPresently it was possible to discern something that might be a gig on the circular drive before the front door.\n\n“Dear me,” said Rosamond, “I hope none of my uncle’s horrible relations are there.”\n\n“They are, though. That is Mrs. Waule’s gig—the last yellow gig left, I should think. When I see Mrs. Waule in it, I understand how yellow can have been worn for mourning. That gig seems to me more funereal than a hearse. But then Mrs. Waule always has black crape on. How does she manage it, Rosy? Her friends can’t always be dying.”\n\n“I don’t know at all. And she is not in the least evangelical,” said Rosamond, reflectively, as if that religious point of view would have fully accounted for perpetual crape. “And, not poor,” she added, after a moment’s pause.\n\n“No, by George! They are as rich as Jews, those Waules and Featherstones; I mean, for people like them, who don’t want to spend anything. And yet they hang about my uncle like vultures, and are afraid of a farthing going away from their side of the family. But I believe he hates them all.”\n\nThe Mrs. Waule who was so far from being admirable in the eyes of these distant connections, had happened to say this very morning (not at all with a defiant air, but in a low, muffled, neutral tone, as of a voice heard through cotton wool) that she did not wish “to enjoy their good opinion.” She was seated, as she observed, on her own brother’s hearth, and had been Jane Featherstone five-and-twenty years before she had been Jane Waule, which entitled her to speak when her own brother’s name had been made free with by those who had no right to it.\n\n“What are you driving at there?” said Mr. Featherstone, holding his stick between his knees and settling his wig, while he gave her a momentary sharp glance, which seemed to react on him like a draught of cold air and set him coughing.\n\nMrs. Waule had to defer her answer till he was quiet again, till Mary Garth had supplied him with fresh syrup, and he had begun to rub the gold knob of his stick, looking bitterly at the fire. It was a bright fire, but it made no difference to the chill-looking purplish tint of Mrs. Waule’s face, which was as neutral as her voice; having mere chinks for eyes, and lips that hardly moved in speaking.\n\n“The doctors can’t master that cough, brother. It’s just like what I have; for I’m your own sister, constitution and everything. But, as I was saying, it’s a pity Mrs. Vincy’s family can’t be better conducted.”\n\n“Tchah! you said nothing o’ the sort. You said somebody had made free with my name.”\n\n“And no more than can be proved, if what everybody says is true. My brother Solomon tells me it’s the talk up and down in Middlemarch how unsteady young Vincy is, and has been forever gambling at billiards since home he came.”\n\n“Nonsense! What’s a game at billiards? It’s a good gentlemanly game; and young Vincy is not a clodhopper. If your son John took to billiards, now, he’d make a fool of himself.”\n\n“Your nephew John never took to billiards or any other game, brother, and is far from losing hundreds of pounds, which, if what everybody says is true, must be found somewhere else than out of Mr. Vincy the father’s pocket. For they say he’s been losing money for years, though nobody would think so, to see him go coursing and keeping open house as they do. And I’ve heard say Mr. Bulstrode condemns Mrs. Vincy beyond anything for her flightiness, and spoiling her children so.”\n\n“What’s Bulstrode to me? I don’t bank with him.”\n\n“Well, Mrs. Bulstrode is Mr. Vincy’s own sister, and they do say that Mr. Vincy mostly trades on the Bank money; and you may see yourself, brother, when a woman past forty has pink strings always flying, and that light way of laughing at everything, it’s very unbecoming. But indulging your children is one thing, and finding money to pay their debts is another. And it’s openly said that young Vincy has raised money on his expectations. I don’t say what expectations. Miss Garth hears me, and is welcome to tell again. I know young people hang together.”\n\n“No, thank you, Mrs. Waule,” said Mary Garth. “I dislike hearing scandal too much to wish to repeat it.”\n\nMr. Featherstone rubbed the knob of his stick and made a brief convulsive show of laughter, which had much the same genuineness as an old whist-player’s chuckle over a bad hand. Still looking at the fire, he said—\n\n“And who pretends to say Fred Vincy hasn’t got expectations? Such a fine, spirited fellow is like enough to have ’em.”\n\nThere was a slight pause before Mrs. Waule replied, and when she did so, her voice seemed to be slightly moistened with tears, though her face was still dry.\n\n“Whether or no, brother, it is naturally painful to me and my brother Solomon to hear your name made free with, and your complaint being such as may carry you off sudden, and people who are no more Featherstones than the Merry-Andrew at the fair, openly reckoning on your property coming to them. And me your own sister, and Solomon your own brother! And if that’s to be it, what has it pleased the Almighty to make families for?” Here Mrs. Waule’s tears fell, but with moderation.\n\n“Come, out with it, Jane!” said Mr. Featherstone, looking at her. “You mean to say, Fred Vincy has been getting somebody to advance him money on what he says he knows about my will, eh?”\n\n“I never said so, brother” (Mrs. Waule’s voice had again become dry and unshaken). “It was told me by my brother Solomon last night when he called coming from market to give me advice about the old wheat, me being a widow, and my son John only three-and-twenty, though steady beyond anything. And he had it from most undeniable authority, and not one, but many.”\n\n“Stuff and nonsense! I don’t believe a word of it. It’s all a got-up story. Go to the window, missy; I thought I heard a horse. See if the doctor’s coming.”\n\n“Not got up by me, brother, nor yet by Solomon, who, whatever else he may be—and I don’t deny he has oddities—has made his will and parted his property equal between such kin as he’s friends with; though, for my part, I think there are times when some should be considered more than others. But Solomon makes it no secret what he means to do.”\n\n“The more fool he!” said Mr. Featherstone, with some difficulty; breaking into a severe fit of coughing that required Mary Garth to stand near him, so that she did not find out whose horses they were which presently paused stamping on the gravel before the door.\n\nBefore Mr. Featherstone’s cough was quiet, Rosamond entered, bearing up her riding-habit with much grace. She bowed ceremoniously to Mrs. Waule, who said stiffly, “How do you do, miss?” smiled and nodded silently to Mary, and remained standing till the coughing should cease, and allow her uncle to notice her.\n\n“Heyday, miss!” he said at last, “you have a fine color. Where’s Fred?”\n\n“Seeing about the horses. He will be in presently.”\n\n“Sit down, sit down. Mrs. Waule, you’d better go.”\n\nEven those neighbors who had called Peter Featherstone an old fox, had never accused him of being insincerely polite, and his sister was quite used to the peculiar absence of ceremony with which he marked his sense of blood-relationship. Indeed, she herself was accustomed to think that entire freedom from the necessity of behaving agreeably was included in the Almighty’s intentions about families. She rose slowly without any sign of resentment, and said in her usual muffled monotone, “Brother, I hope the new doctor will be able to do something for you. Solomon says there’s great talk of his cleverness. I’m sure it’s my wish you should be spared. And there’s none more ready to nurse you than your own sister and your own nieces, if you’d only say the word. There’s Rebecca, and Joanna, and Elizabeth, you know.”\n\n“Ay, ay, I remember—you’ll see I’ve remembered ’em all—all dark and ugly. They’d need have some money, eh? There never was any beauty in the women of our family; but the Featherstones have always had some money, and the Waules too. Waule had money too. A warm man was Waule. Ay, ay; money’s a good egg; and if you’ve got money to leave behind you, lay it in a warm nest. Good-by, Mrs. Waule.” Here Mr. Featherstone pulled at both sides of his wig as if he wanted to deafen himself, and his sister went away ruminating on this oracular speech of his. Notwithstanding her jealousy of the Vincys and of Mary Garth, there remained as the nethermost sediment in her mental shallows a persuasion that her brother Peter Featherstone could never leave his chief property away from his blood-relations:—else, why had the Almighty carried off his two wives both childless, after he had gained so much by manganese and things, turning up when nobody expected it?—and why was there a Lowick parish church, and the Waules and Powderells all sitting in the same pew for generations, and the Featherstone pew next to them, if, the Sunday after her brother Peter’s death, everybody was to know that the property was gone out of the family? The human mind has at no period accepted a moral chaos; and so preposterous a result was not strictly conceivable. But we are frightened at much that is not strictly conceivable.\n\nWhen Fred came in the old man eyed him with a peculiar twinkle, which the younger had often had reason to interpret as pride in the satisfactory details of his appearance.\n\n“You two misses go away,” said Mr. Featherstone. “I want to speak to Fred.”\n\n“Come into my room, Rosamond, you will not mind the cold for a little while,” said Mary. The two girls had not only known each other in childhood, but had been at the same provincial school together (Mary as an articled pupil), so that they had many memories in common, and liked very well to talk in private. Indeed, this tête-à-tête was one of Rosamond’s objects in coming to Stone Court.\n\nOld Featherstone would not begin the dialogue till the door had been closed. He continued to look at Fred with the same twinkle and with one of his habitual grimaces, alternately screwing and widening his mouth; and when he spoke, it was in a low tone, which might be taken for that of an informer ready to be bought off, rather than for the tone of an offended senior. He was not a man to feel any strong moral indignation even on account of trespasses against himself. It was natural that others should want to get an advantage over him, but then, he was a little too cunning for them.\n\n“So, sir, you’ve been paying ten per cent for money which you’ve promised to pay off by mortgaging my land when I’m dead and gone, eh? You put my life at a twelvemonth, say. But I can alter my will yet.”\n\nFred blushed. He had not borrowed money in that way, for excellent reasons. But he was conscious of having spoken with some confidence (perhaps with more than he exactly remembered) about his prospect of getting Featherstone’s land as a future means of paying present debts.\n\n“I don’t know what you refer to, sir. I have certainly never borrowed any money on such an insecurity. Please do explain.”\n\n“No, sir, it’s you must explain. I can alter my will yet, let me tell you. I’m of sound mind—can reckon compound interest in my head, and remember every fool’s name as well as I could twenty years ago. What the deuce? I’m under eighty. I say, you must contradict this story.”\n\n“I have contradicted it, sir,” Fred answered, with a touch of impatience, not remembering that his uncle did not verbally discriminate contradicting from disproving, though no one was further from confounding the two ideas than old Featherstone, who often wondered that so many fools took his own assertions for proofs. “But I contradict it again. The story is a silly lie.”\n\n“Nonsense! you must bring dockiments. It comes from authority.”\n\n“Name the authority, and make him name the man of whom I borrowed the money, and then I can disprove the story.”\n\n“It’s pretty good authority, I think—a man who knows most of what goes on in Middlemarch. It’s that fine, religious, charitable uncle o’ yours. Come now!” Here Mr. Featherstone had his peculiar inward shake which signified merriment.\n\n“Mr. Bulstrode?”\n\n“Who else, eh?”\n\n“Then the story has grown into this lie out of some sermonizing words he may have let fall about me. Do they pretend that he named the man who lent me the money?”\n\n“If there is such a man, depend upon it Bulstrode knows him. But, supposing you only tried to get the money lent, and didn’t get it—Bulstrode ’ud know that too. You bring me a writing from Bulstrode to say he doesn’t believe you’ve ever promised to pay your debts out o’ my land. Come now!”\n\nMr. Featherstone’s face required its whole scale of grimaces as a muscular outlet to his silent triumph in the soundness of his faculties.\n\nFred felt himself to be in a disgusting dilemma.\n\n“You must be joking, sir. Mr. Bulstrode, like other men, believes scores of things that are not true, and he has a prejudice against me. I could easily get him to write that he knew no facts in proof of the report you speak of, though it might lead to unpleasantness. But I could hardly ask him to write down what he believes or does not believe about me.” Fred paused an instant, and then added, in politic appeal to his uncle’s vanity, “That is hardly a thing for a gentleman to ask.” But he was disappointed in the result.\n\n“Ay, I know what you mean. You’d sooner offend me than Bulstrode. And what’s he?—he’s got no land hereabout that ever I heard tell of. A speckilating fellow! He may come down any day, when the devil leaves off backing him. And that’s what his religion means: he wants God A’mighty to come in. That’s nonsense! There’s one thing I made out pretty clear when I used to go to church—and it’s this: God A’mighty sticks to the land. He promises land, and He gives land, and He makes chaps rich with corn and cattle. But you take the other side. You like Bulstrode and speckilation better than Featherstone and land.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, sir,” said Fred, rising, standing with his back to the fire and beating his boot with his whip. “I like neither Bulstrode nor speculation.” He spoke rather sulkily, feeling himself stalemated.\n\n“Well, well, you can do without me, that’s pretty clear,” said old Featherstone, secretly disliking the possibility that Fred would show himself at all independent. “You neither want a bit of land to make a squire of you instead of a starving parson, nor a lift of a hundred pound by the way. It’s all one to me. I can make five codicils if I like, and I shall keep my bank-notes for a nest-egg. It’s all one to me.”\n\nFred colored again. Featherstone had rarely given him presents of money, and at this moment it seemed almost harder to part with the immediate prospect of bank-notes than with the more distant prospect of the land.\n\n“I am not ungrateful, sir. I never meant to show disregard for any kind intentions you might have towards me. On the contrary.”\n\n“Very good. Then prove it. You bring me a letter from Bulstrode saying he doesn’t believe you’ve been cracking and promising to pay your debts out o’ my land, and then, if there’s any scrape you’ve got into, we’ll see if I can’t back you a bit. Come now! That’s a bargain. Here, give me your arm. I’ll try and walk round the room.”\n\nFred, in spite of his irritation, had kindness enough in him to be a little sorry for the unloved, unvenerated old man, who with his dropsical legs looked more than usually pitiable in walking. While giving his arm, he thought that he should not himself like to be an old fellow with his constitution breaking up; and he waited good-temperedly, first before the window to hear the wonted remarks about the guinea-fowls and the weather-cock, and then before the scanty book-shelves, of which the chief glories in dark calf were Josephus, Culpepper, Klopstock’s “Messiah,” and several volumes of the “Gentleman’s Magazine.”\n\n“Read me the names o’ the books. Come now! you’re a college man.”\n\nFred gave him the titles.\n\n“What did missy want with more books? What must you be bringing her more books for?”\n\n“They amuse her, sir. She is very fond of reading.”\n\n“A little too fond,” said Mr. Featherstone, captiously. “She was for reading when she sat with me. But I put a stop to that. She’s got the newspaper to read out loud. That’s enough for one day, I should think. I can’t abide to see her reading to herself. You mind and not bring her any more books, do you hear?”\n\n“Yes, sir, I hear.” Fred had received this order before, and had secretly disobeyed it. He intended to disobey it again.\n\n“Ring the bell,” said Mr. Featherstone; “I want missy to come down.”\n\nRosamond and Mary had been talking faster than their male friends. They did not think of sitting down, but stood at the toilet-table near the window while Rosamond took off her hat, adjusted her veil, and applied little touches of her finger-tips to her hair—hair of infantine fairness, neither flaxen nor yellow. Mary Garth seemed all the plainer standing at an angle between the two nymphs—the one in the glass, and the one out of it, who looked at each other with eyes of heavenly blue, deep enough to hold the most exquisite meanings an ingenious beholder could put into them, and deep enough to hide the meanings of the owner if these should happen to be less exquisite. Only a few children in Middlemarch looked blond by the side of Rosamond, and the slim figure displayed by her riding-habit had delicate undulations. In fact, most men in Middlemarch, except her brothers, held that Miss Vincy was the best girl in the world, and some called her an angel. Mary Garth, on the contrary, had the aspect of an ordinary sinner: she was brown; her curly dark hair was rough and stubborn; her stature was low; and it would not be true to declare, in satisfactory antithesis, that she had all the virtues. Plainness has its peculiar temptations and vices quite as much as beauty; it is apt either to feign amiability, or, not feigning it, to show all the repulsiveness of discontent: at any rate, to be called an ugly thing in contrast with that lovely creature your companion, is apt to produce some effect beyond a sense of fine veracity and fitness in the phrase. At the age of two-and-twenty Mary had certainly not attained that perfect good sense and good principle which are usually recommended to the less fortunate girl, as if they were to be obtained in quantities ready mixed, with a flavor of resignation as required. Her shrewdness had a streak of satiric bitterness continually renewed and never carried utterly out of sight, except by a strong current of gratitude towards those who, instead of telling her that she ought to be contented, did something to make her so. Advancing womanhood had tempered her plainness, which was of a good human sort, such as the mothers of our race have very commonly worn in all latitudes under a more or less becoming headgear. Rembrandt would have painted her with pleasure, and would have made her broad features look out of the canvas with intelligent honesty. For honesty, truth-telling fairness, was Mary’s reigning virtue: she neither tried to create illusions, nor indulged in them for her own behoof, and when she was in a good mood she had humor enough in her to laugh at herself. When she and Rosamond happened both to be reflected in the glass, she said, laughingly—\n\n“What a brown patch I am by the side of you, Rosy! You are the most unbecoming companion.”\n\n“Oh no! No one thinks of your appearance, you are so sensible and useful, Mary. Beauty is of very little consequence in reality,” said Rosamond, turning her head towards Mary, but with eyes swerving towards the new view of her neck in the glass.\n\n“You mean my beauty,” said Mary, rather sardonically.\n\nRosamond thought, “Poor Mary, she takes the kindest things ill.” Aloud she said, “What have you been doing lately?”\n\n“I? Oh, minding the house—pouring out syrup—pretending to be amiable and contented—learning to have a bad opinion of everybody.”\n\n“It is a wretched life for you.”\n\n“No,” said Mary, curtly, with a little toss of her head. “I think my life is pleasanter than your Miss Morgan’s.”\n\n“Yes; but Miss Morgan is so uninteresting, and not young.”\n\n“She is interesting to herself, I suppose; and I am not at all sure that everything gets easier as one gets older.”\n\n“No,” said Rosamond, reflectively; “one wonders what such people do, without any prospect. To be sure, there is religion as a support. But,” she added, dimpling, “it is very different with you, Mary. You may have an offer.”\n\n“Has any one told you he means to make me one?”\n\n“Of course not. I mean, there is a gentleman who may fall in love with you, seeing you almost every day.”\n\nA certain change in Mary’s face was chiefly determined by the resolve not to show any change.\n\n“Does that always make people fall in love?” she answered, carelessly; “it seems to me quite as often a reason for detesting each other.”\n\n“Not when they are interesting and agreeable. I hear that Mr. Lydgate is both.”\n\n“Oh, Mr. Lydgate!” said Mary, with an unmistakable lapse into indifference. “You want to know something about him,” she added, not choosing to indulge Rosamond’s indirectness.\n\n“Merely, how you like him.”\n\n“There is no question of liking at present. My liking always wants some little kindness to kindle it. I am not magnanimous enough to like people who speak to me without seeming to see me.”\n\n“Is he so haughty?” said Rosamond, with heightened satisfaction. “You know that he is of good family?”\n\n“No; he did not give that as a reason.”\n\n“Mary! you are the oddest girl. But what sort of looking man is he? Describe him to me.”\n\n“How can one describe a man? I can give you an inventory: heavy eyebrows, dark eyes, a straight nose, thick dark hair, large solid white hands—and—let me see—oh, an exquisite cambric pocket-handkerchief. But you will see him. You know this is about the time of his visits.”\n\nRosamond blushed a little, but said, meditatively, “I rather like a haughty manner. I cannot endure a rattling young man.”\n\n“I did not tell you that Mr. Lydgate was haughty; but il y en a pour tous les goûts, as little Mamselle used to say, and if any girl can choose the particular sort of conceit she would like, I should think it is you, Rosy.”\n\n“Haughtiness is not conceit; I call Fred conceited.”\n\n“I wish no one said any worse of him. He should be more careful. Mrs. Waule has been telling uncle that Fred is very unsteady.” Mary spoke from a girlish impulse which got the better of her judgment. There was a vague uneasiness associated with the word “unsteady” which she hoped Rosamond might say something to dissipate. But she purposely abstained from mentioning Mrs. Waule’s more special insinuation.\n\n“Oh, Fred is horrid!” said Rosamond. She would not have allowed herself so unsuitable a word to any one but Mary.\n\n“What do you mean by horrid?”\n\n“He is so idle, and makes papa so angry, and says he will not take orders.”\n\n“I think Fred is quite right.”\n\n“How can you say he is quite right, Mary? I thought you had more sense of religion.”\n\n“He is not fit to be a clergyman.”\n\n“But he ought to be fit.”—“Well, then, he is not what he ought to be. I know some other people who are in the same case.”\n\n“But no one approves of them. I should not like to marry a clergyman; but there must be clergymen.”\n\n“It does not follow that Fred must be one.”\n\n“But when papa has been at the expense of educating him for it! And only suppose, if he should have no fortune left him?”\n\n“I can suppose that very well,” said Mary, dryly.\n\n“Then I wonder you can defend Fred,” said Rosamond, inclined to push this point.\n\n“I don’t defend him,” said Mary, laughing; “I would defend any parish from having him for a clergyman.”\n\n“But of course if he were a clergyman, he must be different.”\n\n“Yes, he would be a great hypocrite; and he is not that yet.”\n\n“It is of no use saying anything to you, Mary. You always take Fred’s part.”\n\n“Why should I not take his part?” said Mary, lighting up. “He would take mine. He is the only person who takes the least trouble to oblige me.”\n\n“You make me feel very uncomfortable, Mary,” said Rosamond, with her gravest mildness; “I would not tell mamma for the world.”\n\n“What would you not tell her?” said Mary, angrily.\n\n“Pray do not go into a rage, Mary,” said Rosamond, mildly as ever.\n\n“If your mamma is afraid that Fred will make me an offer, tell her that I would not marry him if he asked me. But he is not going to do so, that I am aware. He certainly never has asked me.”\n\n“Mary, you are always so violent.”\n\n“And you are always so exasperating.”\n\n“I? What can you blame me for?”\n\n“Oh, blameless people are always the most exasperating. There is the bell—I think we must go down.”\n\n“I did not mean to quarrel,” said Rosamond, putting on her hat.\n\n“Quarrel? Nonsense; we have not quarrelled. If one is not to get into a rage sometimes, what is the good of being friends?”\n\n“Am I to repeat what you have said?”\n\n“Just as you please. I never say what I am afraid of having repeated. But let us go down.”\n\nMr. Lydgate was rather late this morning, but the visitors stayed long enough to see him; for Mr. Featherstone asked Rosamond to sing to him, and she herself was so kind as to propose a second favorite song of his—“Flow on, thou shining river”—after she had sung “Home, sweet home” (which she detested). This hard-headed old Overreach approved of the sentimental song, as the suitable garnish for girls, and also as fundamentally fine, sentiment being the right thing for a song.\n\nMr. Featherstone was still applauding the last performance, and assuring missy that her voice was as clear as a blackbird’s, when Mr. Lydgate’s horse passed the window.\n\nHis dull expectation of the usual disagreeable routine with an aged patient—who can hardly believe that medicine would not “set him up” if the doctor were only clever enough—added to his general disbelief in Middlemarch charms, made a doubly effective background to this vision of Rosamond, whom old Featherstone made haste ostentatiously to introduce as his niece, though he had never thought it worth while to speak of Mary Garth in that light. Nothing escaped Lydgate in Rosamond’s graceful behavior: how delicately she waived the notice which the old man’s want of taste had thrust upon her by a quiet gravity, not showing her dimples on the wrong occasion, but showing them afterwards in speaking to Mary, to whom she addressed herself with so much good-natured interest, that Lydgate, after quickly examining Mary more fully than he had done before, saw an adorable kindness in Rosamond’s eyes. But Mary from some cause looked rather out of temper.\n\n“Miss Rosy has been singing me a song—you’ve nothing to say against that, eh, doctor?” said Mr. Featherstone. “I like it better than your physic.”\n\n“That has made me forget how the time was going,” said Rosamond, rising to reach her hat, which she had laid aside before singing, so that her flower-like head on its white stem was seen in perfection above her riding-habit. “Fred, we must really go.”\n\n“Very good,” said Fred, who had his own reasons for not being in the best spirits, and wanted to get away.\n\n“Miss Vincy is a musician?” said Lydgate, following her with his eyes. (Every nerve and muscle in Rosamond was adjusted to the consciousness that she was being looked at. She was by nature an actress of parts that entered into her physique: she even acted her own character, and so well, that she did not know it to be precisely her own.)\n\n“The best in Middlemarch, I’ll be bound,” said Mr. Featherstone, “let the next be who she will. Eh, Fred? Speak up for your sister.”\n\n“I’m afraid I’m out of court, sir. My evidence would be good for nothing.”\n\n“Middlemarch has not a very high standard, uncle,” said Rosamond, with a pretty lightness, going towards her whip, which lay at a distance.\n\nLydgate was quick in anticipating her. He reached the whip before she did, and turned to present it to her. She bowed and looked at him: he of course was looking at her, and their eyes met with that peculiar meeting which is never arrived at by effort, but seems like a sudden divine clearance of haze. I think Lydgate turned a little paler than usual, but Rosamond blushed deeply and felt a certain astonishment. After that, she was really anxious to go, and did not know what sort of stupidity her uncle was talking of when she went to shake hands with him.\n\nYet this result, which she took to be a mutual impression, called falling in love, was just what Rosamond had contemplated beforehand. Ever since that important new arrival in Middlemarch she had woven a little future, of which something like this scene was the necessary beginning. Strangers, whether wrecked and clinging to a raft, or duly escorted and accompanied by portmanteaus, have always had a circumstantial fascination for the virgin mind, against which native merit has urged itself in vain. And a stranger was absolutely necessary to Rosamond’s social romance, which had always turned on a lover and bridegroom who was not a Middlemarcher, and who had no connections at all like her own: of late, indeed, the construction seemed to demand that he should somehow be related to a baronet. Now that she and the stranger had met, reality proved much more moving than anticipation, and Rosamond could not doubt that this was the great epoch of her life. She judged of her own symptoms as those of awakening love, and she held it still more natural that Mr. Lydgate should have fallen in love at first sight of her. These things happened so often at balls, and why not by the morning light, when the complexion showed all the better for it? Rosamond, though no older than Mary, was rather used to being fallen in love with; but she, for her part, had remained indifferent and fastidiously critical towards both fresh sprig and faded bachelor. And here was Mr. Lydgate suddenly corresponding to her ideal, being altogether foreign to Middlemarch, carrying a certain air of distinction congruous with good family, and possessing connections which offered vistas of that middle-class heaven, rank; a man of talent, also, whom it would be especially delightful to enslave: in fact, a man who had touched her nature quite newly, and brought a vivid interest into her life which was better than any fancied “might-be” such as she was in the habit of opposing to the actual.\n\nThus, in riding home, both the brother and the sister were preoccupied and inclined to be silent. Rosamond, whose basis for her structure had the usual airy slightness, was of remarkably detailed and realistic imagination when the foundation had been once presupposed; and before they had ridden a mile she was far on in the costume and introductions of her wedded life, having determined on her house in Middlemarch, and foreseen the visits she would pay to her husband’s high-bred relatives at a distance, whose finished manners she could appropriate as thoroughly as she had done her school accomplishments, preparing herself thus for vaguer elevations which might ultimately come. There was nothing financial, still less sordid, in her previsions: she cared about what were considered refinements, and not about the money that was to pay for them.\n\nFred’s mind, on the other hand, was busy with an anxiety which even his ready hopefulness could not immediately quell. He saw no way of eluding Featherstone’s stupid demand without incurring consequences which he liked less even than the task of fulfilling it. His father was already out of humor with him, and would be still more so if he were the occasion of any additional coolness between his own family and the Bulstrodes. Then, he himself hated having to go and speak to his uncle Bulstrode, and perhaps after drinking wine he had said many foolish things about Featherstone’s property, and these had been magnified by report. Fred felt that he made a wretched figure as a fellow who bragged about expectations from a queer old miser like Featherstone, and went to beg for certificates at his bidding. But—those expectations! He really had them, and he saw no agreeable alternative if he gave them up; besides, he had lately made a debt which galled him extremely, and old Featherstone had almost bargained to pay it off. The whole affair was miserably small: his debts were small, even his expectations were not anything so very magnificent. Fred had known men to whom he would have been ashamed of confessing the smallness of his scrapes. Such ruminations naturally produced a streak of misanthropic bitterness. To be born the son of a Middlemarch manufacturer, and inevitable heir to nothing in particular, while such men as Mainwaring and Vyan—certainly life was a poor business, when a spirited young fellow, with a good appetite for the best of everything, had so poor an outlook.\n\nIt had not occurred to Fred that the introduction of Bulstrode’s name in the matter was a fiction of old Featherstone’s; nor could this have made any difference to his position. He saw plainly enough that the old man wanted to exercise his power by tormenting him a little, and also probably to get some satisfaction out of seeing him on unpleasant terms with Bulstrode. Fred fancied that he saw to the bottom of his uncle Featherstone’s soul, though in reality half what he saw there was no more than the reflex of his own inclinations. The difficult task of knowing another soul is not for young gentlemen whose consciousness is chiefly made up of their own wishes.\n\nFred’s main point of debate with himself was, whether he should tell his father, or try to get through the affair without his father’s knowledge. It was probably Mrs. Waule who had been talking about him; and if Mary Garth had repeated Mrs. Waule’s report to Rosamond, it would be sure to reach his father, who would as surely question him about it. He said to Rosamond, as they slackened their pace—\n\n“Rosy, did Mary tell you that Mrs. Waule had said anything about me?”\n\n“Yes, indeed, she did.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“That you were very unsteady.”\n\n“Was that all?”\n\n“I should think that was enough, Fred.”\n\n“You are sure she said no more?”\n\n“Mary mentioned nothing else. But really, Fred, I think you ought to be ashamed.”\n\n“Oh, fudge! Don’t lecture me. What did Mary say about it?”\n\n“I am not obliged to tell you. You care so very much what Mary says, and you are too rude to allow me to speak.”\n\n“Of course I care what Mary says. She is the best girl I know.”\n\n“I should never have thought she was a girl to fall in love with.”\n\n“How do you know what men would fall in love with? Girls never know.”\n\n“At least, Fred, let me advise you not to fall in love with her, for she says she would not marry you if you asked her.”\n\n“She might have waited till I did ask her.”\n\n“I knew it would nettle you, Fred.”\n\n“Not at all. She would not have said so if you had not provoked her.” Before reaching home, Fred concluded that he would tell the whole affair as simply as possible to his father, who might perhaps take on himself the unpleasant business of speaking to Bulstrode.\n\n# BOOK II. OLD AND YOUNG. CHAPTER XIII.\n\n1st Gent. How class your man?—as better than the most, Or, seeming better, worse beneath that cloak? As saint or knave, pilgrim or hypocrite?\n\n2d Gent. Nay, tell me how you class your wealth of books The drifted relics of all time. As well sort them at once by size and livery: Vellum, tall copies, and the common calf Will hardly cover more diversity Than all your labels cunningly devised To class your unread authors.\n\nIn consequence of what he had heard from Fred, Mr. Vincy determined to speak with Mr. Bulstrode in his private room at the Bank at half-past one, when he was usually free from other callers. But a visitor had come in at one o’clock, and Mr. Bulstrode had so much to say to him, that there was little chance of the interview being over in half an hour. The banker’s speech was fluent, but it was also copious, and he used up an appreciable amount of time in brief meditative pauses. Do not imagine his sickly aspect to have been of the yellow, black-haired sort: he had a pale blond skin, thin gray-besprinkled brown hair, light-gray eyes, and a large forehead. Loud men called his subdued tone an undertone, and sometimes implied that it was inconsistent with openness; though there seems to be no reason why a loud man should not be given to concealment of anything except his own voice, unless it can be shown that Holy Writ has placed the seat of candor in the lungs. Mr. Bulstrode had also a deferential bending attitude in listening, and an apparently fixed attentiveness in his eyes which made those persons who thought themselves worth hearing infer that he was seeking the utmost improvement from their discourse. Others, who expected to make no great figure, disliked this kind of moral lantern turned on them. If you are not proud of your cellar, there is no thrill of satisfaction in seeing your guest hold up his wine-glass to the light and look judicial. Such joys are reserved for conscious merit. Hence Mr. Bulstrode’s close attention was not agreeable to the publicans and sinners in Middlemarch; it was attributed by some to his being a Pharisee, and by others to his being Evangelical. Less superficial reasoners among them wished to know who his father and grandfather were, observing that five-and-twenty years ago nobody had ever heard of a Bulstrode in Middlemarch. To his present visitor, Lydgate, the scrutinizing look was a matter of indifference: he simply formed an unfavorable opinion of the banker’s constitution, and concluded that he had an eager inward life with little enjoyment of tangible things.\n\n“I shall be exceedingly obliged if you will look in on me here occasionally, Mr. Lydgate,” the banker observed, after a brief pause. “If, as I dare to hope, I have the privilege of finding you a valuable coadjutor in the interesting matter of hospital management, there will be many questions which we shall need to discuss in private. As to the new hospital, which is nearly finished, I shall consider what you have said about the advantages of the special destination for fevers. The decision will rest with me, for though Lord Medlicote has given the land and timber for the building, he is not disposed to give his personal attention to the object.”\n\n“There are few things better worth the pains in a provincial town like this,” said Lydgate. “A fine fever hospital in addition to the old infirmary might be the nucleus of a medical school here, when once we get our medical reforms; and what would do more for medical education than the spread of such schools over the country? A born provincial man who has a grain of public spirit as well as a few ideas, should do what he can to resist the rush of everything that is a little better than common towards London. Any valid professional aims may often find a freer, if not a richer field, in the provinces.”\n\nOne of Lydgate’s gifts was a voice habitually deep and sonorous, yet capable of becoming very low and gentle at the right moment. About his ordinary bearing there was a certain fling, a fearless expectation of success, a confidence in his own powers and integrity much fortified by contempt for petty obstacles or seductions of which he had had no experience. But this proud openness was made lovable by an expression of unaffected good-will. Mr. Bulstrode perhaps liked him the better for the difference between them in pitch and manners; he certainly liked him the better, as Rosamond did, for being a stranger in Middlemarch. One can begin so many things with a new person!—even begin to be a better man.\n\n“I shall rejoice to furnish your zeal with fuller opportunities,” Mr. Bulstrode answered; “I mean, by confiding to you the superintendence of my new hospital, should a maturer knowledge favor that issue, for I am determined that so great an object shall not be shackled by our two physicians. Indeed, I am encouraged to consider your advent to this town as a gracious indication that a more manifest blessing is now to be awarded to my efforts, which have hitherto been much withstood. With regard to the old infirmary, we have gained the initial point—I mean your election. And now I hope you will not shrink from incurring a certain amount of jealousy and dislike from your professional brethren by presenting yourself as a reformer.”\n\n“I will not profess bravery,” said Lydgate, smiling, “but I acknowledge a good deal of pleasure in fighting, and I should not care for my profession, if I did not believe that better methods were to be found and enforced there as well as everywhere else.”\n\n“The standard of that profession is low in Middlemarch, my dear sir,” said the banker. “I mean in knowledge and skill; not in social status, for our medical men are most of them connected with respectable townspeople here. My own imperfect health has induced me to give some attention to those palliative resources which the divine mercy has placed within our reach. I have consulted eminent men in the metropolis, and I am painfully aware of the backwardness under which medical treatment labors in our provincial districts.”\n\n“Yes;—with our present medical rules and education, one must be satisfied now and then to meet with a fair practitioner. As to all the higher questions which determine the starting-point of a diagnosis—as to the philosophy of medical evidence—any glimmering of these can only come from a scientific culture of which country practitioners have usually no more notion than the man in the moon.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode, bending and looking intently, found the form which Lydgate had given to his agreement not quite suited to his comprehension. Under such circumstances a judicious man changes the topic and enters on ground where his own gifts may be more useful.\n\n“I am aware,” he said, “that the peculiar bias of medical ability is towards material means. Nevertheless, Mr. Lydgate, I hope we shall not vary in sentiment as to a measure in which you are not likely to be actively concerned, but in which your sympathetic concurrence may be an aid to me. You recognize, I hope; the existence of spiritual interests in your patients?”\n\n“Certainly I do. But those words are apt to cover different meanings to different minds.”\n\n“Precisely. And on such subjects wrong teaching is as fatal as no teaching. Now a point which I have much at heart to secure is a new regulation as to clerical attendance at the old infirmary. The building stands in Mr. Farebrother’s parish. You know Mr. Farebrother?”\n\n“I have seen him. He gave me his vote. I must call to thank him. He seems a very bright pleasant little fellow. And I understand he is a naturalist.”\n\n“Mr. Farebrother, my dear sir, is a man deeply painful to contemplate. I suppose there is not a clergyman in this country who has greater talents.” Mr. Bulstrode paused and looked meditative.\n\n“I have not yet been pained by finding any excessive talent in Middlemarch,” said Lydgate, bluntly.\n\n“What I desire,” Mr. Bulstrode continued, looking still more serious, “is that Mr. Farebrother’s attendance at the hospital should be superseded by the appointment of a chaplain—of Mr. Tyke, in fact—and that no other spiritual aid should be called in.”\n\n“As a medical man I could have no opinion on such a point unless I knew Mr. Tyke, and even then I should require to know the cases in which he was applied.” Lydgate smiled, but he was bent on being circumspect.\n\n“Of course you cannot enter fully into the merits of this measure at present. But”—here Mr. Bulstrode began to speak with a more chiselled emphasis—“the subject is likely to be referred to the medical board of the infirmary, and what I trust I may ask of you is, that in virtue of the cooperation between us which I now look forward to, you will not, so far as you are concerned, be influenced by my opponents in this matter.”\n\n“I hope I shall have nothing to do with clerical disputes,” said Lydgate. “The path I have chosen is to work well in my own profession.”\n\n“My responsibility, Mr. Lydgate, is of a broader kind. With me, indeed, this question is one of sacred accountableness; whereas with my opponents, I have good reason to say that it is an occasion for gratifying a spirit of worldly opposition. But I shall not therefore drop one iota of my convictions, or cease to identify myself with that truth which an evil generation hates. I have devoted myself to this object of hospital-improvement, but I will boldly confess to you, Mr. Lydgate, that I should have no interest in hospitals if I believed that nothing more was concerned therein than the cure of mortal diseases. I have another ground of action, and in the face of persecution I will not conceal it.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode’s voice had become a loud and agitated whisper as he said the last words.\n\n“There we certainly differ,” said Lydgate. But he was not sorry that the door was now opened, and Mr. Vincy was announced. That florid sociable personage was become more interesting to him since he had seen Rosamond. Not that, like her, he had been weaving any future in which their lots were united; but a man naturally remembers a charming girl with pleasure, and is willing to dine where he may see her again. Before he took leave, Mr. Vincy had given that invitation which he had been “in no hurry about,” for Rosamond at breakfast had mentioned that she thought her uncle Featherstone had taken the new doctor into great favor.\n\nMr. Bulstrode, alone with his brother-in-law, poured himself out a glass of water, and opened a sandwich-box.\n\n“I cannot persuade you to adopt my regimen, Vincy?”\n\n“No, no; I’ve no opinion of that system. Life wants padding,” said Mr. Vincy, unable to omit his portable theory. “However,” he went on, accenting the word, as if to dismiss all irrelevance, “what I came here to talk about was a little affair of my young scapegrace, Fred’s.”\n\n“That is a subject on which you and I are likely to take quite as different views as on diet, Vincy.”\n\n“I hope not this time.” (Mr. Vincy was resolved to be good-humored.) “The fact is, it’s about a whim of old Featherstone’s. Somebody has been cooking up a story out of spite, and telling it to the old man, to try to set him against Fred. He’s very fond of Fred, and is likely to do something handsome for him; indeed he has as good as told Fred that he means to leave him his land, and that makes other people jealous.”\n\n“Vincy, I must repeat, that you will not get any concurrence from me as to the course you have pursued with your eldest son. It was entirely from worldly vanity that you destined him for the Church: with a family of three sons and four daughters, you were not warranted in devoting money to an expensive education which has succeeded in nothing but in giving him extravagant idle habits. You are now reaping the consequences.”\n\nTo point out other people’s errors was a duty that Mr. Bulstrode rarely shrank from, but Mr. Vincy was not equally prepared to be patient. When a man has the immediate prospect of being mayor, and is ready, in the interests of commerce, to take up a firm attitude on politics generally, he has naturally a sense of his importance to the framework of things which seems to throw questions of private conduct into the background. And this particular reproof irritated him more than any other. It was eminently superfluous to him to be told that he was reaping the consequences. But he felt his neck under Bulstrode’s yoke; and though he usually enjoyed kicking, he was anxious to refrain from that relief.\n\n“As to that, Bulstrode, it’s no use going back. I’m not one of your pattern men, and I don’t pretend to be. I couldn’t foresee everything in the trade; there wasn’t a finer business in Middlemarch than ours, and the lad was clever. My poor brother was in the Church, and would have done well—had got preferment already, but that stomach fever took him off: else he might have been a dean by this time. I think I was justified in what I tried to do for Fred. If you come to religion, it seems to me a man shouldn’t want to carve out his meat to an ounce beforehand:—one must trust a little to Providence and be generous. It’s a good British feeling to try and raise your family a little: in my opinion, it’s a father’s duty to give his sons a fine chance.”\n\n“I don’t wish to act otherwise than as your best friend, Vincy, when I say that what you have been uttering just now is one mass of worldliness and inconsistent folly.”\n\n“Very well,” said Mr. Vincy, kicking in spite of resolutions, “I never professed to be anything but worldly; and, what’s more, I don’t see anybody else who is not worldly. I suppose you don’t conduct business on what you call unworldly principles. The only difference I see is that one worldliness is a little bit honester than another.”\n\n“This kind of discussion is unfruitful, Vincy,” said Mr. Bulstrode, who, finishing his sandwich, had thrown himself back in his chair, and shaded his eyes as if weary. “You had some more particular business.”\n\n“Yes, yes. The long and short of it is, somebody has told old Featherstone, giving you as the authority, that Fred has been borrowing or trying to borrow money on the prospect of his land. Of course you never said any such nonsense. But the old fellow will insist on it that Fred should bring him a denial in your handwriting; that is, just a bit of a note saying you don’t believe a word of such stuff, either of his having borrowed or tried to borrow in such a fool’s way. I suppose you can have no objection to do that.”\n\n“Pardon me. I have an objection. I am by no means sure that your son, in his recklessness and ignorance—I will use no severer word—has not tried to raise money by holding out his future prospects, or even that some one may not have been foolish enough to supply him on so vague a presumption: there is plenty of such lax money-lending as of other folly in the world.”\n\n“But Fred gives me his honor that he has never borrowed money on the pretence of any understanding about his uncle’s land. He is not a liar. I don’t want to make him better than he is. I have blown him up well—nobody can say I wink at what he does. But he is not a liar. And I should have thought—but I may be wrong—that there was no religion to hinder a man from believing the best of a young fellow, when you don’t know worse. It seems to me it would be a poor sort of religion to put a spoke in his wheel by refusing to say you don’t believe such harm of him as you’ve got no good reason to believe.”\n\n“I am not at all sure that I should be befriending your son by smoothing his way to the future possession of Featherstone’s property. I cannot regard wealth as a blessing to those who use it simply as a harvest for this world. You do not like to hear these things, Vincy, but on this occasion I feel called upon to tell you that I have no motive for furthering such a disposition of property as that which you refer to. I do not shrink from saying that it will not tend to your son’s eternal welfare or to the glory of God. Why then should you expect me to pen this kind of affidavit, which has no object but to keep up a foolish partiality and secure a foolish bequest?”\n\n“If you mean to hinder everybody from having money but saints and evangelists, you must give up some profitable partnerships, that’s all I can say,” Mr. Vincy burst out very bluntly. “It may be for the glory of God, but it is not for the glory of the Middlemarch trade, that Plymdale’s house uses those blue and green dyes it gets from the Brassing manufactory; they rot the silk, that’s all I know about it. Perhaps if other people knew so much of the profit went to the glory of God, they might like it better. But I don’t mind so much about that—I could get up a pretty row, if I chose.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode paused a little before he answered. “You pain me very much by speaking in this way, Vincy. I do not expect you to understand my grounds of action—it is not an easy thing even to thread a path for principles in the intricacies of the world—still less to make the thread clear for the careless and the scoffing. You must remember, if you please, that I stretch my tolerance towards you as my wife’s brother, and that it little becomes you to complain of me as withholding material help towards the worldly position of your family. I must remind you that it is not your own prudence or judgment that has enabled you to keep your place in the trade.”\n\n“Very likely not; but you have been no loser by my trade yet,” said Mr. Vincy, thoroughly nettled (a result which was seldom much retarded by previous resolutions). “And when you married Harriet, I don’t see how you could expect that our families should not hang by the same nail. If you’ve changed your mind, and want my family to come down in the world, you’d better say so. I’ve never changed; I’m a plain Churchman now, just as I used to be before doctrines came up. I take the world as I find it, in trade and everything else. I’m contented to be no worse than my neighbors. But if you want us to come down in the world, say so. I shall know better what to do then.”\n\n“You talk unreasonably. Shall you come down in the world for want of this letter about your son?”\n\n“Well, whether or not, I consider it very unhandsome of you to refuse it. Such doings may be lined with religion, but outside they have a nasty, dog-in-the-manger look. You might as well slander Fred: it comes pretty near to it when you refuse to say you didn’t set a slander going. It’s this sort of thing—this tyrannical spirit, wanting to play bishop and banker everywhere—it’s this sort of thing makes a man’s name stink.”\n\n“Vincy, if you insist on quarrelling with me, it will be exceedingly painful to Harriet as well as myself,” said Mr. Bulstrode, with a trifle more eagerness and paleness than usual.\n\n“I don’t want to quarrel. It’s for my interest—and perhaps for yours too—that we should be friends. I bear you no grudge; I think no worse of you than I do of other people. A man who half starves himself, and goes the length in family prayers, and so on, that you do, believes in his religion whatever it may be: you could turn over your capital just as fast with cursing and swearing:—plenty of fellows do. You like to be master, there’s no denying that; you must be first chop in heaven, else you won’t like it much. But you’re my sister’s husband, and we ought to stick together; and if I know Harriet, she’ll consider it your fault if we quarrel because you strain at a gnat in this way, and refuse to do Fred a good turn. And I don’t mean to say I shall bear it well. I consider it unhandsome.”\n\nMr. Vincy rose, began to button his great-coat, and looked steadily at his brother-in-law, meaning to imply a demand for a decisive answer.\n\nThis was not the first time that Mr. Bulstrode had begun by admonishing Mr. Vincy, and had ended by seeing a very unsatisfactory reflection of himself in the coarse unflattering mirror which that manufacturer’s mind presented to the subtler lights and shadows of his fellow-men; and perhaps his experience ought to have warned him how the scene would end. But a full-fed fountain will be generous with its waters even in the rain, when they are worse than useless; and a fine fount of admonition is apt to be equally irrepressible.\n\nIt was not in Mr. Bulstrode’s nature to comply directly in consequence of uncomfortable suggestions. Before changing his course, he always needed to shape his motives and bring them into accordance with his habitual standard. He said, at last—\n\n“I will reflect a little, Vincy. I will mention the subject to Harriet. I shall probably send you a letter.”\n\n“Very well. As soon as you can, please. I hope it will all be settled before I see you to-morrow.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIV.\n\n“Follows here the strict receipt For that sauce to dainty meat, Named Idleness, which many eat By preference, and call it sweet: First watch for morsels, like a hound Mix well with buffets, stir them round With good thick oil of flatteries, And froth with mean self-lauding lies. Serve warm: the vessels you must choose To keep it in are dead men’s shoes.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode’s consultation of Harriet seemed to have had the effect desired by Mr. Vincy, for early the next morning a letter came which Fred could carry to Mr. Featherstone as the required testimony.\n\nThe old gentleman was staying in bed on account of the cold weather, and as Mary Garth was not to be seen in the sitting-room, Fred went up-stairs immediately and presented the letter to his uncle, who, propped up comfortably on a bed-rest, was not less able than usual to enjoy his consciousness of wisdom in distrusting and frustrating mankind. He put on his spectacles to read the letter, pursing up his lips and drawing down their corners.\n\n“Under the circumstances I will not decline to state my conviction—tchah! what fine words the fellow puts! He’s as fine as an auctioneer—that your son Frederic has not obtained any advance of money on bequests promised by Mr. Featherstone—promised? who said I had ever promised? I promise nothing—I shall make codicils as long as I like—and that considering the nature of such a proceeding, it is unreasonable to presume that a young man of sense and character would attempt it—ah, but the gentleman doesn’t say you are a young man of sense and character, mark you that, sir!—As to my own concern with any report of such a nature, I distinctly affirm that I never made any statement to the effect that your son had borrowed money on any property that might accrue to him on Mr. Featherstone’s demise—bless my heart! ‘property’—accrue—demise! Lawyer Standish is nothing to him. He couldn’t speak finer if he wanted to borrow. Well,” Mr. Featherstone here looked over his spectacles at Fred, while he handed back the letter to him with a contemptuous gesture, “you don’t suppose I believe a thing because Bulstrode writes it out fine, eh?”\n\nFred colored. “You wished to have the letter, sir. I should think it very likely that Mr. Bulstrode’s denial is as good as the authority which told you what he denies.”\n\n“Every bit. I never said I believed either one or the other. And now what d’ you expect?” said Mr. Featherstone, curtly, keeping on his spectacles, but withdrawing his hands under his wraps.\n\n“I expect nothing, sir.” Fred with difficulty restrained himself from venting his irritation. “I came to bring you the letter. If you like I will bid you good morning.”\n\n“Not yet, not yet. Ring the bell; I want missy to come.”\n\nIt was a servant who came in answer to the bell.\n\n“Tell missy to come!” said Mr. Featherstone, impatiently. “What business had she to go away?” He spoke in the same tone when Mary came.\n\n“Why couldn’t you sit still here till I told you to go? I want my waistcoat now. I told you always to put it on the bed.”\n\nMary’s eyes looked rather red, as if she had been crying. It was clear that Mr. Featherstone was in one of his most snappish humors this morning, and though Fred had now the prospect of receiving the much-needed present of money, he would have preferred being free to turn round on the old tyrant and tell him that Mary Garth was too good to be at his beck. Though Fred had risen as she entered the room, she had barely noticed him, and looked as if her nerves were quivering with the expectation that something would be thrown at her. But she never had anything worse than words to dread. When she went to reach the waistcoat from a peg, Fred went up to her and said, “Allow me.”\n\n“Let it alone! You bring it, missy, and lay it down here,” said Mr. Featherstone. “Now you go away again till I call you,” he added, when the waistcoat was laid down by him. It was usual with him to season his pleasure in showing favor to one person by being especially disagreeable to another, and Mary was always at hand to furnish the condiment. When his own relatives came she was treated better. Slowly he took out a bunch of keys from the waistcoat pocket, and slowly he drew forth a tin box which was under the bed-clothes.\n\n“You expect I am going to give you a little fortune, eh?” he said, looking above his spectacles and pausing in the act of opening the lid.\n\n“Not at all, sir. You were good enough to speak of making me a present the other day, else, of course, I should not have thought of the matter.” But Fred was of a hopeful disposition, and a vision had presented itself of a sum just large enough to deliver him from a certain anxiety. When Fred got into debt, it always seemed to him highly probable that something or other—he did not necessarily conceive what—would come to pass enabling him to pay in due time. And now that the providential occurrence was apparently close at hand, it would have been sheer absurdity to think that the supply would be short of the need: as absurd as a faith that believed in half a miracle for want of strength to believe in a whole one.\n\nThe deep-veined hands fingered many bank-notes one after the other, laying them down flat again, while Fred leaned back in his chair, scorning to look eager. He held himself to be a gentleman at heart, and did not like courting an old fellow for his money. At last, Mr. Featherstone eyed him again over his spectacles and presented him with a little sheaf of notes: Fred could see distinctly that there were but five, as the less significant edges gaped towards him. But then, each might mean fifty pounds. He took them, saying—\n\n“I am very much obliged to you, sir,” and was going to roll them up without seeming to think of their value. But this did not suit Mr. Featherstone, who was eying him intently.\n\n“Come, don’t you think it worth your while to count ’em? You take money like a lord; I suppose you lose it like one.”\n\n“I thought I was not to look a gift-horse in the mouth, sir. But I shall be very happy to count them.”\n\nFred was not so happy, however, after he had counted them. For they actually presented the absurdity of being less than his hopefulness had decided that they must be. What can the fitness of things mean, if not their fitness to a man’s expectations? Failing this, absurdity and atheism gape behind him. The collapse for Fred was severe when he found that he held no more than five twenties, and his share in the higher education of this country did not seem to help him. Nevertheless he said, with rapid changes in his fair complexion—\n\n“It is very handsome of you, sir.”\n\n“I should think it is,” said Mr. Featherstone, locking his box and replacing it, then taking off his spectacles deliberately, and at length, as if his inward meditation had more deeply convinced him, repeating, “I should think it handsome.”\n\n“I assure you, sir, I am very grateful,” said Fred, who had had time to recover his cheerful air.\n\n“So you ought to be. You want to cut a figure in the world, and I reckon Peter Featherstone is the only one you’ve got to trust to.” Here the old man’s eyes gleamed with a curiously mingled satisfaction in the consciousness that this smart young fellow relied upon him, and that the smart young fellow was rather a fool for doing so.\n\n“Yes, indeed: I was not born to very splendid chances. Few men have been more cramped than I have been,” said Fred, with some sense of surprise at his own virtue, considering how hardly he was dealt with. “It really seems a little too bad to have to ride a broken-winded hunter, and see men, who, are not half such good judges as yourself, able to throw away any amount of money on buying bad bargains.”\n\n“Well, you can buy yourself a fine hunter now. Eighty pound is enough for that, I reckon—and you’ll have twenty pound over to get yourself out of any little scrape,” said Mr. Featherstone, chuckling slightly.\n\n“You are very good, sir,” said Fred, with a fine sense of contrast between the words and his feeling.\n\n“Ay, rather a better uncle than your fine uncle Bulstrode. You won’t get much out of his spekilations, I think. He’s got a pretty strong string round your father’s leg, by what I hear, eh?”\n\n“My father never tells me anything about his affairs, sir.”\n\n“Well, he shows some sense there. But other people find ’em out without his telling. He’ll never have much to leave you: he’ll most-like die without a will—he’s the sort of man to do it—let ’em make him mayor of Middlemarch as much as they like. But you won’t get much by his dying without a will, though you are the eldest son.”\n\nFred thought that Mr. Featherstone had never been so disagreeable before. True, he had never before given him quite so much money at once.\n\n“Shall I destroy this letter of Mr. Bulstrode’s, sir?” said Fred, rising with the letter as if he would put it in the fire.\n\n“Ay, ay, I don’t want it. It’s worth no money to me.”\n\nFred carried the letter to the fire, and thrust the poker through it with much zest. He longed to get out of the room, but he was a little ashamed before his inner self, as well as before his uncle, to run away immediately after pocketing the money. Presently, the farm-bailiff came up to give his master a report, and Fred, to his unspeakable relief, was dismissed with the injunction to come again soon.\n\nHe had longed not only to be set free from his uncle, but also to find Mary Garth. She was now in her usual place by the fire, with sewing in her hands and a book open on the little table by her side. Her eyelids had lost some of their redness now, and she had her usual air of self-command.\n\n“Am I wanted up-stairs?” she said, half rising as Fred entered.\n\n“No; I am only dismissed, because Simmons is gone up.”\n\nMary sat down again, and resumed her work. She was certainly treating him with more indifference than usual: she did not know how affectionately indignant he had felt on her behalf up-stairs.\n\n“May I stay here a little, Mary, or shall I bore you?”\n\n“Pray sit down,” said Mary; “you will not be so heavy a bore as Mr. John Waule, who was here yesterday, and he sat down without asking my leave.”\n\n“Poor fellow! I think he is in love with you.”\n\n“I am not aware of it. And to me it is one of the most odious things in a girl’s life, that there must always be some supposition of falling in love coming between her and any man who is kind to her, and to whom she is grateful. I should have thought that I, at least, might have been safe from all that. I have no ground for the nonsensical vanity of fancying everybody who comes near me is in love with me.”\n\nMary did not mean to betray any feeling, but in spite of herself she ended in a tremulous tone of vexation.\n\n“Confound John Waule! I did not mean to make you angry. I didn’t know you had any reason for being grateful to me. I forgot what a great service you think it if any one snuffs a candle for you.” Fred also had his pride, and was not going to show that he knew what had called forth this outburst of Mary’s.\n\n“Oh, I am not angry, except with the ways of the world. I do like to be spoken to as if I had common-sense. I really often feel as if I could understand a little more than I ever hear even from young gentlemen who have been to college.” Mary had recovered, and she spoke with a suppressed rippling under-current of laughter pleasant to hear.\n\n“I don’t care how merry you are at my expense this morning,” said Fred, “I thought you looked so sad when you came up-stairs. It is a shame you should stay here to be bullied in that way.”\n\n“Oh, I have an easy life—by comparison. I have tried being a teacher, and I am not fit for that: my mind is too fond of wandering on its own way. I think any hardship is better than pretending to do what one is paid for, and never really doing it. Everything here I can do as well as any one else could; perhaps better than some—Rosy, for example. Though she is just the sort of beautiful creature that is imprisoned with ogres in fairy tales.”\n\n“Rosy!” cried Fred, in a tone of profound brotherly scepticism.\n\n“Come, Fred!” said Mary, emphatically; “you have no right to be so critical.”\n\n“Do you mean anything particular—just now?”\n\n“No, I mean something general—always.”\n\n“Oh, that I am idle and extravagant. Well, I am not fit to be a poor man. I should not have made a bad fellow if I had been rich.”\n\n“You would have done your duty in that state of life to which it has not pleased God to call you,” said Mary, laughing.\n\n“Well, I couldn’t do my duty as a clergyman, any more than you could do yours as a governess. You ought to have a little fellow-feeling there, Mary.”\n\n“I never said you ought to be a clergyman. There are other sorts of work. It seems to me very miserable not to resolve on some course and act accordingly.”\n\n“So I could, if—” Fred broke off, and stood up, leaning against the mantel-piece.\n\n“If you were sure you should not have a fortune?”\n\n“I did not say that. You want to quarrel with me. It is too bad of you to be guided by what other people say about me.”\n\n“How can I want to quarrel with you? I should be quarrelling with all my new books,” said Mary, lifting the volume on the table. “However naughty you may be to other people, you are good to me.”\n\n“Because I like you better than any one else. But I know you despise me.”\n\n“Yes, I do—a little,” said Mary, nodding, with a smile.\n\n“You would admire a stupendous fellow, who would have wise opinions about everything.”\n\n“Yes, I should.” Mary was sewing swiftly, and seemed provokingly mistress of the situation. When a conversation has taken a wrong turn for us, we only get farther and farther into the swamp of awkwardness. This was what Fred Vincy felt.\n\n“I suppose a woman is never in love with any one she has always known—ever since she can remember; as a man often is. It is always some new fellow who strikes a girl.”\n\n“Let me see,” said Mary, the corners of her mouth curling archly; “I must go back on my experience. There is Juliet—she seems an example of what you say. But then Ophelia had probably known Hamlet a long while; and Brenda Troil—she had known Mordaunt Merton ever since they were children; but then he seems to have been an estimable young man; and Minna was still more deeply in love with Cleveland, who was a stranger. Waverley was new to Flora MacIvor; but then she did not fall in love with him. And there are Olivia and Sophia Primrose, and Corinne—they may be said to have fallen in love with new men. Altogether, my experience is rather mixed.”\n\nMary looked up with some roguishness at Fred, and that look of hers was very dear to him, though the eyes were nothing more than clear windows where observation sat laughingly. He was certainly an affectionate fellow, and as he had grown from boy to man, he had grown in love with his old playmate, notwithstanding that share in the higher education of the country which had exalted his views of rank and income.\n\n“When a man is not loved, it is no use for him to say that he could be a better fellow—could do anything—I mean, if he were sure of being loved in return.”\n\n“Not of the least use in the world for him to say he could be better. Might, could, would—they are contemptible auxiliaries.”\n\n“I don’t see how a man is to be good for much unless he has some one woman to love him dearly.”\n\n“I think the goodness should come before he expects that.”\n\n“You know better, Mary. Women don’t love men for their goodness.”\n\n“Perhaps not. But if they love them, they never think them bad.”\n\n“It is hardly fair to say I am bad.”\n\n“I said nothing at all about you.”\n\n“I never shall be good for anything, Mary, if you will not say that you love me—if you will not promise to marry me—I mean, when I am able to marry.”\n\n“If I did love you, I would not marry you: I would certainly not promise ever to marry you.”\n\n“I think that is quite wicked, Mary. If you love me, you ought to promise to marry me.”\n\n“On the contrary, I think it would be wicked in me to marry you even if I did love you.”\n\n“You mean, just as I am, without any means of maintaining a wife. Of course: I am but three-and-twenty.”\n\n“In that last point you will alter. But I am not so sure of any other alteration. My father says an idle man ought not to exist, much less, be married.”\n\n“Then I am to blow my brains out?”\n\n“No; on the whole I should think you would do better to pass your examination. I have heard Mr. Farebrother say it is disgracefully easy.”\n\n“That is all very fine. Anything is easy to him. Not that cleverness has anything to do with it. I am ten times cleverer than many men who pass.”\n\n“Dear me!” said Mary, unable to repress her sarcasm; “that accounts for the curates like Mr. Crowse. Divide your cleverness by ten, and the quotient—dear me!—is able to take a degree. But that only shows you are ten times more idle than the others.”\n\n“Well, if I did pass, you would not want me to go into the Church?”\n\n“That is not the question—what I want you to do. You have a conscience of your own, I suppose. There! there is Mr. Lydgate. I must go and tell my uncle.”\n\n“Mary,” said Fred, seizing her hand as she rose; “if you will not give me some encouragement, I shall get worse instead of better.”\n\n“I will not give you any encouragement,” said Mary, reddening. “Your friends would dislike it, and so would mine. My father would think it a disgrace to me if I accepted a man who got into debt, and would not work!”\n\nFred was stung, and released her hand. She walked to the door, but there she turned and said: “Fred, you have always been so good, so generous to me. I am not ungrateful. But never speak to me in that way again.”\n\n“Very well,” said Fred, sulkily, taking up his hat and whip. His complexion showed patches of pale pink and dead white. Like many a plucked idle young gentleman, he was thoroughly in love, and with a plain girl, who had no money! But having Mr. Featherstone’s land in the background, and a persuasion that, let Mary say what she would, she really did care for him, Fred was not utterly in despair.\n\nWhen he got home, he gave four of the twenties to his mother, asking her to keep them for him. “I don’t want to spend that money, mother. I want it to pay a debt with. So keep it safe away from my fingers.”\n\n“Bless you, my dear,” said Mrs. Vincy. She doted on her eldest son and her youngest girl (a child of six), whom others thought her two naughtiest children. The mother’s eyes are not always deceived in their partiality: she at least can best judge who is the tender, filial-hearted child. And Fred was certainly very fond of his mother. Perhaps it was his fondness for another person also that made him particularly anxious to take some security against his own liability to spend the hundred pounds. For the creditor to whom he owed a hundred and sixty held a firmer security in the shape of a bill signed by Mary’s father.\n\n# CHAPTER XV.\n\n“Black eyes you have left, you say, Blue eyes fail to draw you; Yet you seem more rapt to-day, Than of old we saw you.\n\n“Oh, I track the fairest fair Through new haunts of pleasure; Footprints here and echoes there Guide me to my treasure:\n\n“Lo! she turns—immortal youth Wrought to mortal stature, Fresh as starlight’s aged truth— Many-namèd Nature!”\n\nA great historian, as he insisted on calling himself, who had the happiness to be dead a hundred and twenty years ago, and so to take his place among the colossi whose huge legs our living pettiness is observed to walk under, glories in his copious remarks and digressions as the least imitable part of his work, and especially in those initial chapters to the successive books of his history, where he seems to bring his armchair to the proscenium and chat with us in all the lusty ease of his fine English. But Fielding lived when the days were longer (for time, like money, is measured by our needs), when summer afternoons were spacious, and the clock ticked slowly in the winter evenings. We belated historians must not linger after his example; and if we did so, it is probable that our chat would be thin and eager, as if delivered from a campstool in a parrot-house. I at least have so much to do in unraveling certain human lots, and seeing how they were woven and interwoven, that all the light I can command must be concentrated on this particular web, and not dispersed over that tempting range of relevancies called the universe.\n\nAt present I have to make the new settler Lydgate better known to any one interested in him than he could possibly be even to those who had seen the most of him since his arrival in Middlemarch. For surely all must admit that a man may be puffed and belauded, envied, ridiculed, counted upon as a tool and fallen in love with, or at least selected as a future husband, and yet remain virtually unknown—known merely as a cluster of signs for his neighbors’ false suppositions. There was a general impression, however, that Lydgate was not altogether a common country doctor, and in Middlemarch at that time such an impression was significant of great things being expected from him. For everybody’s family doctor was remarkably clever, and was understood to have immeasurable skill in the management and training of the most skittish or vicious diseases. The evidence of his cleverness was of the higher intuitive order, lying in his lady-patients’ immovable conviction, and was unassailable by any objection except that their intuitions were opposed by others equally strong; each lady who saw medical truth in Wrench and “the strengthening treatment” regarding Toller and “the lowering system” as medical perdition. For the heroic times of copious bleeding and blistering had not yet departed, still less the times of thorough-going theory, when disease in general was called by some bad name, and treated accordingly without shilly-shally—as if, for example, it were to be called insurrection, which must not be fired on with blank-cartridge, but have its blood drawn at once. The strengtheners and the lowerers were all “clever” men in somebody’s opinion, which is really as much as can be said for any living talents. Nobody’s imagination had gone so far as to conjecture that Mr. Lydgate could know as much as Dr. Sprague and Dr. Minchin, the two physicians, who alone could offer any hope when danger was extreme, and when the smallest hope was worth a guinea. Still, I repeat, there was a general impression that Lydgate was something rather more uncommon than any general practitioner in Middlemarch. And this was true. He was but seven-and-twenty, an age at which many men are not quite common—at which they are hopeful of achievement, resolute in avoidance, thinking that Mammon shall never put a bit in their mouths and get astride their backs, but rather that Mammon, if they have anything to do with him, shall draw their chariot.\n\nHe had been left an orphan when he was fresh from a public school. His father, a military man, had made but little provision for three children, and when the boy Tertius asked to have a medical education, it seemed easier to his guardians to grant his request by apprenticing him to a country practitioner than to make any objections on the score of family dignity. He was one of the rarer lads who early get a decided bent and make up their minds that there is something particular in life which they would like to do for its own sake, and not because their fathers did it. Most of us who turn to any subject with love remember some morning or evening hour when we got on a high stool to reach down an untried volume, or sat with parted lips listening to a new talker, or for very lack of books began to listen to the voices within, as the first traceable beginning of our love. Something of that sort happened to Lydgate. He was a quick fellow, and when hot from play, would toss himself in a corner, and in five minutes be deep in any sort of book that he could lay his hands on: if it were Rasselas or Gulliver, so much the better, but Bailey’s Dictionary would do, or the Bible with the Apocrypha in it. Something he must read, when he was not riding the pony, or running and hunting, or listening to the talk of men. All this was true of him at ten years of age; he had then read through “Chrysal, or the Adventures of a Guinea,” which was neither milk for babes, nor any chalky mixture meant to pass for milk, and it had already occurred to him that books were stuff, and that life was stupid. His school studies had not much modified that opinion, for though he “did” his classics and mathematics, he was not pre-eminent in them. It was said of him, that Lydgate could do anything he liked, but he had certainly not yet liked to do anything remarkable. He was a vigorous animal with a ready understanding, but no spark had yet kindled in him an intellectual passion; knowledge seemed to him a very superficial affair, easily mastered: judging from the conversation of his elders, he had apparently got already more than was necessary for mature life. Probably this was not an exceptional result of expensive teaching at that period of short-waisted coats, and other fashions which have not yet recurred. But, one vacation, a wet day sent him to the small home library to hunt once more for a book which might have some freshness for him: in vain! unless, indeed, he took down a dusty row of volumes with gray-paper backs and dingy labels—the volumes of an old Cyclopaedia which he had never disturbed. It would at least be a novelty to disturb them. They were on the highest shelf, and he stood on a chair to get them down. But he opened the volume which he first took from the shelf: somehow, one is apt to read in a makeshift attitude, just where it might seem inconvenient to do so. The page he opened on was under the head of Anatomy, and the first passage that drew his eyes was on the valves of the heart. He was not much acquainted with valves of any sort, but he knew that valvae were folding-doors, and through this crevice came a sudden light startling him with his first vivid notion of finely adjusted mechanism in the human frame. A liberal education had of course left him free to read the indecent passages in the school classics, but beyond a general sense of secrecy and obscenity in connection with his internal structure, had left his imagination quite unbiassed, so that for anything he knew his brains lay in small bags at his temples, and he had no more thought of representing to himself how his blood circulated than how paper served instead of gold. But the moment of vocation had come, and before he got down from his chair, the world was made new to him by a presentiment of endless processes filling the vast spaces planked out of his sight by that wordy ignorance which he had supposed to be knowledge. From that hour Lydgate felt the growth of an intellectual passion.\n\nWe are not afraid of telling over and over again how a man comes to fall in love with a woman and be wedded to her, or else be fatally parted from her. Is it due to excess of poetry or of stupidity that we are never weary of describing what King James called a woman’s “makdom and her fairnesse,” never weary of listening to the twanging of the old Troubadour strings, and are comparatively uninterested in that other kind of “makdom and fairnesse” which must be wooed with industrious thought and patient renunciation of small desires? In the story of this passion, too, the development varies: sometimes it is the glorious marriage, sometimes frustration and final parting. And not seldom the catastrophe is bound up with the other passion, sung by the Troubadours. For in the multitude of middle-aged men who go about their vocations in a daily course determined for them much in the same way as the tie of their cravats, there is always a good number who once meant to shape their own deeds and alter the world a little. The story of their coming to be shapen after the average and fit to be packed by the gross, is hardly ever told even in their consciousness; for perhaps their ardor in generous unpaid toil cooled as imperceptibly as the ardor of other youthful loves, till one day their earlier self walked like a ghost in its old home and made the new furniture ghastly. Nothing in the world more subtle than the process of their gradual change! In the beginning they inhaled it unknowingly: you and I may have sent some of our breath towards infecting them, when we uttered our conforming falsities or drew our silly conclusions: or perhaps it came with the vibrations from a woman’s glance.\n\nLydgate did not mean to be one of those failures, and there was the better hope of him because his scientific interest soon took the form of a professional enthusiasm: he had a youthful belief in his bread-winning work, not to be stifled by that initiation in makeshift called his ’prentice days; and he carried to his studies in London, Edinburgh, and Paris, the conviction that the medical profession as it might be was the finest in the world; presenting the most perfect interchange between science and art; offering the most direct alliance between intellectual conquest and the social good. Lydgate’s nature demanded this combination: he was an emotional creature, with a flesh-and-blood sense of fellowship which withstood all the abstractions of special study. He cared not only for “cases,” but for John and Elizabeth, especially Elizabeth.\n\nThere was another attraction in his profession: it wanted reform, and gave a man an opportunity for some indignant resolve to reject its venal decorations and other humbug, and to be the possessor of genuine though undemanded qualifications. He went to study in Paris with the determination that when he came home again he would settle in some provincial town as a general practitioner, and resist the irrational severance between medical and surgical knowledge in the interest of his own scientific pursuits, as well as of the general advance: he would keep away from the range of London intrigues, jealousies, and social truckling, and win celebrity, however slowly, as Jenner had done, by the independent value of his work. For it must be remembered that this was a dark period; and in spite of venerable colleges which used great efforts to secure purity of knowledge by making it scarce, and to exclude error by a rigid exclusiveness in relation to fees and appointments, it happened that very ignorant young gentlemen were promoted in town, and many more got a legal right to practise over large areas in the country. Also, the high standard held up to the public mind by the College of Physicians, which gave its peculiar sanction to the expensive and highly rarefied medical instruction obtained by graduates of Oxford and Cambridge, did not hinder quackery from having an excellent time of it; for since professional practice chiefly consisted in giving a great many drugs, the public inferred that it might be better off with more drugs still, if they could only be got cheaply, and hence swallowed large cubic measures of physic prescribed by unscrupulous ignorance which had taken no degrees. Considering that statistics had not yet embraced a calculation as to the number of ignorant or canting doctors which absolutely must exist in the teeth of all changes, it seemed to Lydgate that a change in the units was the most direct mode of changing the numbers. He meant to be a unit who would make a certain amount of difference towards that spreading change which would one day tell appreciably upon the averages, and in the mean time have the pleasure of making an advantageous difference to the viscera of his own patients. But he did not simply aim at a more genuine kind of practice than was common. He was ambitious of a wider effect: he was fired with the possibility that he might work out the proof of an anatomical conception and make a link in the chain of discovery.\n\nDoes it seem incongruous to you that a Middlemarch surgeon should dream of himself as a discoverer? Most of us, indeed, know little of the great originators until they have been lifted up among the constellations and already rule our fates. But that Herschel, for example, who “broke the barriers of the heavens”—did he not once play a provincial church-organ, and give music-lessons to stumbling pianists? Each of those Shining Ones had to walk on the earth among neighbors who perhaps thought much more of his gait and his garments than of anything which was to give him a title to everlasting fame: each of them had his little local personal history sprinkled with small temptations and sordid cares, which made the retarding friction of his course towards final companionship with the immortals. Lydgate was not blind to the dangers of such friction, but he had plenty of confidence in his resolution to avoid it as far as possible: being seven-and-twenty, he felt himself experienced. And he was not going to have his vanities provoked by contact with the showy worldly successes of the capital, but to live among people who could hold no rivalry with that pursuit of a great idea which was to be a twin object with the assiduous practice of his profession. There was fascination in the hope that the two purposes would illuminate each other: the careful observation and inference which was his daily work, the use of the lens to further his judgment in special cases, would further his thought as an instrument of larger inquiry. Was not this the typical pre-eminence of his profession? He would be a good Middlemarch doctor, and by that very means keep himself in the track of far-reaching investigation. On one point he may fairly claim approval at this particular stage of his career: he did not mean to imitate those philanthropic models who make a profit out of poisonous pickles to support themselves while they are exposing adulteration, or hold shares in a gambling-hell that they may have leisure to represent the cause of public morality. He intended to begin in his own case some particular reforms which were quite certainly within his reach, and much less of a problem than the demonstrating of an anatomical conception. One of these reforms was to act stoutly on the strength of a recent legal decision, and simply prescribe, without dispensing drugs or taking percentage from druggists. This was an innovation for one who had chosen to adopt the style of general practitioner in a country town, and would be felt as offensive criticism by his professional brethren. But Lydgate meant to innovate in his treatment also, and he was wise enough to see that the best security for his practising honestly according to his belief was to get rid of systematic temptations to the contrary.\n\nPerhaps that was a more cheerful time for observers and theorizers than the present; we are apt to think it the finest era of the world when America was beginning to be discovered, when a bold sailor, even if he were wrecked, might alight on a new kingdom; and about 1829 the dark territories of Pathology were a fine America for a spirited young adventurer. Lydgate was ambitious above all to contribute towards enlarging the scientific, rational basis of his profession. The more he became interested in special questions of disease, such as the nature of fever or fevers, the more keenly he felt the need for that fundamental knowledge of structure which just at the beginning of the century had been illuminated by the brief and glorious career of Bichat, who died when he was only one-and-thirty, but, like another Alexander, left a realm large enough for many heirs. That great Frenchman first carried out the conception that living bodies, fundamentally considered, are not associations of organs which can be understood by studying them first apart, and then as it were federally; but must be regarded as consisting of certain primary webs or tissues, out of which the various organs—brain, heart, lungs, and so on—are compacted, as the various accommodations of a house are built up in various proportions of wood, iron, stone, brick, zinc, and the rest, each material having its peculiar composition and proportions. No man, one sees, can understand and estimate the entire structure or its parts—what are its frailties and what its repairs, without knowing the nature of the materials. And the conception wrought out by Bichat, with his detailed study of the different tissues, acted necessarily on medical questions as the turning of gas-light would act on a dim, oil-lit street, showing new connections and hitherto hidden facts of structure which must be taken into account in considering the symptoms of maladies and the action of medicaments. But results which depend on human conscience and intelligence work slowly, and now at the end of 1829, most medical practice was still strutting or shambling along the old paths, and there was still scientific work to be done which might have seemed to be a direct sequence of Bichat’s. This great seer did not go beyond the consideration of the tissues as ultimate facts in the living organism, marking the limit of anatomical analysis; but it was open to another mind to say, have not these structures some common basis from which they have all started, as your sarsnet, gauze, net, satin, and velvet from the raw cocoon? Here would be another light, as of oxy-hydrogen, showing the very grain of things, and revising all former explanations. Of this sequence to Bichat’s work, already vibrating along many currents of the European mind, Lydgate was enamoured; he longed to demonstrate the more intimate relations of living structure, and help to define men’s thought more accurately after the true order. The work had not yet been done, but only prepared for those who knew how to use the preparation. What was the primitive tissue? In that way Lydgate put the question—not quite in the way required by the awaiting answer; but such missing of the right word befalls many seekers. And he counted on quiet intervals to be watchfully seized, for taking up the threads of investigation—on many hints to be won from diligent application, not only of the scalpel, but of the microscope, which research had begun to use again with new enthusiasm of reliance. Such was Lydgate’s plan of his future: to do good small work for Middlemarch, and great work for the world.\n\nHe was certainly a happy fellow at this time: to be seven-and-twenty, without any fixed vices, with a generous resolution that his action should be beneficent, and with ideas in his brain that made life interesting quite apart from the cultus of horseflesh and other mystic rites of costly observance, which the eight hundred pounds left him after buying his practice would certainly not have gone far in paying for. He was at a starting-point which makes many a man’s career a fine subject for betting, if there were any gentlemen given to that amusement who could appreciate the complicated probabilities of an arduous purpose, with all the possible thwartings and furtherings of circumstance, all the niceties of inward balance, by which a man swims and makes his point or else is carried headlong. The risk would remain even with close knowledge of Lydgate’s character; for character too is a process and an unfolding. The man was still in the making, as much as the Middlemarch doctor and immortal discoverer, and there were both virtues and faults capable of shrinking or expanding. The faults will not, I hope, be a reason for the withdrawal of your interest in him. Among our valued friends is there not some one or other who is a little too self-confident and disdainful; whose distinguished mind is a little spotted with commonness; who is a little pinched here and protuberant there with native prejudices; or whose better energies are liable to lapse down the wrong channel under the influence of transient solicitations? All these things might be alleged against Lydgate, but then, they are the periphrases of a polite preacher, who talks of Adam, and would not like to mention anything painful to the pew-renters. The particular faults from which these delicate generalities are distilled have distinguishable physiognomies, diction, accent, and grimaces; filling up parts in very various dramas. Our vanities differ as our noses do: all conceit is not the same conceit, but varies in correspondence with the minutiae of mental make in which one of us differs from another. Lydgate’s conceit was of the arrogant sort, never simpering, never impertinent, but massive in its claims and benevolently contemptuous. He would do a great deal for noodles, being sorry for them, and feeling quite sure that they could have no power over him: he had thought of joining the Saint Simonians when he was in Paris, in order to turn them against some of their own doctrines. All his faults were marked by kindred traits, and were those of a man who had a fine baritone, whose clothes hung well upon him, and who even in his ordinary gestures had an air of inbred distinction. Where then lay the spots of commonness? says a young lady enamoured of that careless grace. How could there be any commonness in a man so well-bred, so ambitious of social distinction, so generous and unusual in his views of social duty? As easily as there may be stupidity in a man of genius if you take him unawares on the wrong subject, or as many a man who has the best will to advance the social millennium might be ill-inspired in imagining its lighter pleasures; unable to go beyond Offenbach’s music, or the brilliant punning in the last burlesque. Lydgate’s spots of commonness lay in the complexion of his prejudices, which, in spite of noble intention and sympathy, were half of them such as are found in ordinary men of the world: that distinction of mind which belonged to his intellectual ardor, did not penetrate his feeling and judgment about furniture, or women, or the desirability of its being known (without his telling) that he was better born than other country surgeons. He did not mean to think of furniture at present; but whenever he did so it was to be feared that neither biology nor schemes of reform would lift him above the vulgarity of feeling that there would be an incompatibility in his furniture not being of the best.\n\nAs to women, he had once already been drawn headlong by impetuous folly, which he meant to be final, since marriage at some distant period would of course not be impetuous. For those who want to be acquainted with Lydgate it will be good to know what was that case of impetuous folly, for it may stand as an example of the fitful swerving of passion to which he was prone, together with the chivalrous kindness which helped to make him morally lovable. The story can be told without many words. It happened when he was studying in Paris, and just at the time when, over and above his other work, he was occupied with some galvanic experiments. One evening, tired with his experimenting, and not being able to elicit the facts he needed, he left his frogs and rabbits to some repose under their trying and mysterious dispensation of unexplained shocks, and went to finish his evening at the theatre of the Porte Saint Martin, where there was a melodrama which he had already seen several times; attracted, not by the ingenious work of the collaborating authors, but by an actress whose part it was to stab her lover, mistaking him for the evil-designing duke of the piece. Lydgate was in love with this actress, as a man is in love with a woman whom he never expects to speak to. She was a Provencale, with dark eyes, a Greek profile, and rounded majestic form, having that sort of beauty which carries a sweet matronliness even in youth, and her voice was a soft cooing. She had but lately come to Paris, and bore a virtuous reputation, her husband acting with her as the unfortunate lover. It was her acting which was “no better than it should be,” but the public was satisfied. Lydgate’s only relaxation now was to go and look at this woman, just as he might have thrown himself under the breath of the sweet south on a bank of violets for a while, without prejudice to his galvanism, to which he would presently return. But this evening the old drama had a new catastrophe. At the moment when the heroine was to act the stabbing of her lover, and he was to fall gracefully, the wife veritably stabbed her husband, who fell as death willed. A wild shriek pierced the house, and the Provencale fell swooning: a shriek and a swoon were demanded by the play, but the swooning too was real this time. Lydgate leaped and climbed, he hardly knew how, on to the stage, and was active in help, making the acquaintance of his heroine by finding a contusion on her head and lifting her gently in his arms. Paris rang with the story of this death:—was it a murder? Some of the actress’s warmest admirers were inclined to believe in her guilt, and liked her the better for it (such was the taste of those times); but Lydgate was not one of these. He vehemently contended for her innocence, and the remote impersonal passion for her beauty which he had felt before, had passed now into personal devotion, and tender thought of her lot. The notion of murder was absurd: no motive was discoverable, the young couple being understood to dote on each other; and it was not unprecedented that an accidental slip of the foot should have brought these grave consequences. The legal investigation ended in Madame Laure’s release. Lydgate by this time had had many interviews with her, and found her more and more adorable. She talked little; but that was an additional charm. She was melancholy, and seemed grateful; her presence was enough, like that of the evening light. Lydgate was madly anxious about her affection, and jealous lest any other man than himself should win it and ask her to marry him. But instead of reopening her engagement at the Porte Saint Martin, where she would have been all the more popular for the fatal episode, she left Paris without warning, forsaking her little court of admirers. Perhaps no one carried inquiry far except Lydgate, who felt that all science had come to a stand-still while he imagined the unhappy Laure, stricken by ever-wandering sorrow, herself wandering, and finding no faithful comforter. Hidden actresses, however, are not so difficult to find as some other hidden facts, and it was not long before Lydgate gathered indications that Laure had taken the route to Lyons. He found her at last acting with great success at Avignon under the same name, looking more majestic than ever as a forsaken wife carrying her child in her arms. He spoke to her after the play, was received with the usual quietude which seemed to him beautiful as clear depths of water, and obtained leave to visit her the next day; when he was bent on telling her that he adored her, and on asking her to marry him. He knew that this was like the sudden impulse of a madman—incongruous even with his habitual foibles. No matter! It was the one thing which he was resolved to do. He had two selves within him apparently, and they must learn to accommodate each other and bear reciprocal impediments. Strange, that some of us, with quick alternate vision, see beyond our infatuations, and even while we rave on the heights, behold the wide plain where our persistent self pauses and awaits us.\n\nTo have approached Laure with any suit that was not reverentially tender would have been simply a contradiction of his whole feeling towards her.\n\n“You have come all the way from Paris to find me?” she said to him the next day, sitting before him with folded arms, and looking at him with eyes that seemed to wonder as an untamed ruminating animal wonders. “Are all Englishmen like that?”\n\n“I came because I could not live without trying to see you. You are lonely; I love you; I want you to consent to be my wife; I will wait, but I want you to promise that you will marry me—no one else.”\n\nLaure looked at him in silence with a melancholy radiance from under her grand eyelids, until he was full of rapturous certainty, and knelt close to her knees.\n\n“I will tell you something,” she said, in her cooing way, keeping her arms folded. “My foot really slipped.”\n\n“I know, I know,” said Lydgate, deprecatingly. “It was a fatal accident—a dreadful stroke of calamity that bound me to you the more.”\n\nAgain Laure paused a little and then said, slowly, “I meant to do it.”\n\nLydgate, strong man as he was, turned pale and trembled: moments seemed to pass before he rose and stood at a distance from her.\n\n“There was a secret, then,” he said at last, even vehemently. “He was brutal to you: you hated him.”\n\n“No! he wearied me; he was too fond: he would live in Paris, and not in my country; that was not agreeable to me.”\n\n“Great God!” said Lydgate, in a groan of horror. “And you planned to murder him?”\n\n“I did not plan: it came to me in the play—I meant to do it.”\n\nLydgate stood mute, and unconsciously pressed his hat on while he looked at her. He saw this woman—the first to whom he had given his young adoration—amid the throng of stupid criminals.\n\n“You are a good young man,” she said. “But I do not like husbands. I will never have another.”\n\nThree days afterwards Lydgate was at his galvanism again in his Paris chambers, believing that illusions were at an end for him. He was saved from hardening effects by the abundant kindness of his heart and his belief that human life might be made better. But he had more reason than ever for trusting his judgment, now that it was so experienced; and henceforth he would take a strictly scientific view of woman, entertaining no expectations but such as were justified beforehand.\n\nNo one in Middlemarch was likely to have such a notion of Lydgate’s past as has here been faintly shadowed, and indeed the respectable townsfolk there were not more given than mortals generally to any eager attempt at exactness in the representation to themselves of what did not come under their own senses. Not only young virgins of that town, but gray-bearded men also, were often in haste to conjecture how a new acquaintance might be wrought into their purposes, contented with very vague knowledge as to the way in which life had been shaping him for that instrumentality. Middlemarch, in fact, counted on swallowing Lydgate and assimilating him very comfortably.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI.\n\n“All that in woman is adored In thy fair self I find— For the whole sex can but afford The handsome and the kind.” —SIR CHARLES SEDLEY.\n\nThe question whether Mr. Tyke should be appointed as salaried chaplain to the hospital was an exciting topic to the Middlemarchers; and Lydgate heard it discussed in a way that threw much light on the power exercised in the town by Mr. Bulstrode. The banker was evidently a ruler, but there was an opposition party, and even among his supporters there were some who allowed it to be seen that their support was a compromise, and who frankly stated their impression that the general scheme of things, and especially the casualties of trade, required you to hold a candle to the devil.\n\nMr. Bulstrode’s power was not due simply to his being a country banker, who knew the financial secrets of most traders in the town and could touch the springs of their credit; it was fortified by a beneficence that was at once ready and severe—ready to confer obligations, and severe in watching the result. He had gathered, as an industrious man always at his post, a chief share in administering the town charities, and his private charities were both minute and abundant. He would take a great deal of pains about apprenticing Tegg the shoemaker’s son, and he would watch over Tegg’s church-going; he would defend Mrs. Strype the washerwoman against Stubbs’s unjust exaction on the score of her drying-ground, and he would himself scrutinize a calumny against Mrs. Strype. His private minor loans were numerous, but he would inquire strictly into the circumstances both before and after. In this way a man gathers a domain in his neighbors’ hope and fear as well as gratitude; and power, when once it has got into that subtle region, propagates itself, spreading out of all proportion to its external means. It was a principle with Mr. Bulstrode to gain as much power as possible, that he might use it for the glory of God. He went through a great deal of spiritual conflict and inward argument in order to adjust his motives, and make clear to himself what God’s glory required. But, as we have seen, his motives were not always rightly appreciated. There were many crass minds in Middlemarch whose reflective scales could only weigh things in the lump; and they had a strong suspicion that since Mr. Bulstrode could not enjoy life in their fashion, eating and drinking so little as he did, and worreting himself about everything, he must have a sort of vampire’s feast in the sense of mastery.\n\nThe subject of the chaplaincy came up at Mr. Vincy’s table when Lydgate was dining there, and the family connection with Mr. Bulstrode did not, he observed, prevent some freedom of remark even on the part of the host himself, though his reasons against the proposed arrangement turned entirely on his objection to Mr. Tyke’s sermons, which were all doctrine, and his preference for Mr. Farebrother, whose sermons were free from that taint. Mr. Vincy liked well enough the notion of the chaplain’s having a salary, supposing it were given to Farebrother, who was as good a little fellow as ever breathed, and the best preacher anywhere, and companionable too.\n\n“What line shall you take, then?” said Mr. Chichely, the coroner, a great coursing comrade of Mr. Vincy’s.\n\n“Oh, I’m precious glad I’m not one of the Directors now. I shall vote for referring the matter to the Directors and the Medical Board together. I shall roll some of my responsibility on your shoulders, Doctor,” said Mr. Vincy, glancing first at Dr. Sprague, the senior physician of the town, and then at Lydgate who sat opposite. “You medical gentlemen must consult which sort of black draught you will prescribe, eh, Mr. Lydgate?”\n\n“I know little of either,” said Lydgate; “but in general, appointments are apt to be made too much a question of personal liking. The fittest man for a particular post is not always the best fellow or the most agreeable. Sometimes, if you wanted to get a reform, your only way would be to pension off the good fellows whom everybody is fond of, and put them out of the question.”\n\nDr. Sprague, who was considered the physician of most “weight,” though Dr. Minchin was usually said to have more “penetration,” divested his large heavy face of all expression, and looked at his wine-glass while Lydgate was speaking. Whatever was not problematical and suspected about this young man—for example, a certain showiness as to foreign ideas, and a disposition to unsettle what had been settled and forgotten by his elders—was positively unwelcome to a physician whose standing had been fixed thirty years before by a treatise on Meningitis, of which at least one copy marked “own” was bound in calf. For my part I have some fellow-feeling with Dr. Sprague: one’s self-satisfaction is an untaxed kind of property which it is very unpleasant to find deprecated.\n\nLydgate’s remark, however, did not meet the sense of the company. Mr. Vincy said, that if he could have his way, he would not put disagreeable fellows anywhere.\n\n“Hang your reforms!” said Mr. Chichely. “There’s no greater humbug in the world. You never hear of a reform, but it means some trick to put in new men. I hope you are not one of the ‘Lancet’s’ men, Mr. Lydgate—wanting to take the coronership out of the hands of the legal profession: your words appear to point that way.”\n\n“I disapprove of Wakley,” interposed Dr. Sprague, “no man more: he is an ill-intentioned fellow, who would sacrifice the respectability of the profession, which everybody knows depends on the London Colleges, for the sake of getting some notoriety for himself. There are men who don’t mind about being kicked blue if they can only get talked about. But Wakley is right sometimes,” the Doctor added, judicially. “I could mention one or two points in which Wakley is in the right.”\n\n“Oh, well,” said Mr. Chichely, “I blame no man for standing up in favor of his own cloth; but, coming to argument, I should like to know how a coroner is to judge of evidence if he has not had a legal training?”\n\n“In my opinion,” said Lydgate, “legal training only makes a man more incompetent in questions that require knowledge of another kind. People talk about evidence as if it could really be weighed in scales by a blind Justice. No man can judge what is good evidence on any particular subject, unless he knows that subject well. A lawyer is no better than an old woman at a post-mortem examination. How is he to know the action of a poison? You might as well say that scanning verse will teach you to scan the potato crops.”\n\n“You are aware, I suppose, that it is not the coroner’s business to conduct the post-mortem, but only to take the evidence of the medical witness?” said Mr. Chichely, with some scorn.\n\n“Who is often almost as ignorant as the coroner himself,” said Lydgate. “Questions of medical jurisprudence ought not to be left to the chance of decent knowledge in a medical witness, and the coroner ought not to be a man who will believe that strychnine will destroy the coats of the stomach if an ignorant practitioner happens to tell him so.”\n\nLydgate had really lost sight of the fact that Mr. Chichely was his Majesty’s coroner, and ended innocently with the question, “Don’t you agree with me, Dr. Sprague?”\n\n“To a certain extent—with regard to populous districts, and in the metropolis,” said the Doctor. “But I hope it will be long before this part of the country loses the services of my friend Chichely, even though it might get the best man in our profession to succeed him. I am sure Vincy will agree with me.”\n\n“Yes, yes, give me a coroner who is a good coursing man,” said Mr. Vincy, jovially. “And in my opinion, you’re safest with a lawyer. Nobody can know everything. Most things are ‘visitation of God.’ And as to poisoning, why, what you want to know is the law. Come, shall we join the ladies?”\n\nLydgate’s private opinion was that Mr. Chichely might be the very coroner without bias as to the coats of the stomach, but he had not meant to be personal. This was one of the difficulties of moving in good Middlemarch society: it was dangerous to insist on knowledge as a qualification for any salaried office. Fred Vincy had called Lydgate a prig, and now Mr. Chichely was inclined to call him prick-eared; especially when, in the drawing-room, he seemed to be making himself eminently agreeable to Rosamond, whom he had easily monopolized in a tête-à-tête, since Mrs. Vincy herself sat at the tea-table. She resigned no domestic function to her daughter; and the matron’s blooming good-natured face, with the two volatile pink strings floating from her fine throat, and her cheery manners to husband and children, was certainly among the great attractions of the Vincy house—attractions which made it all the easier to fall in love with the daughter. The tinge of unpretentious, inoffensive vulgarity in Mrs. Vincy gave more effect to Rosamond’s refinement, which was beyond what Lydgate had expected.\n\nCertainly, small feet and perfectly turned shoulders aid the impression of refined manners, and the right thing said seems quite astonishingly right when it is accompanied with exquisite curves of lip and eyelid. And Rosamond could say the right thing; for she was clever with that sort of cleverness which catches every tone except the humorous. Happily she never attempted to joke, and this perhaps was the most decisive mark of her cleverness.\n\nShe and Lydgate readily got into conversation. He regretted that he had not heard her sing the other day at Stone Court. The only pleasure he allowed himself during the latter part of his stay in Paris was to go and hear music.\n\n“You have studied music, probably?” said Rosamond.\n\n“No, I know the notes of many birds, and I know many melodies by ear; but the music that I don’t know at all, and have no notion about, delights me—affects me. How stupid the world is that it does not make more use of such a pleasure within its reach!”\n\n“Yes, and you will find Middlemarch very tuneless. There are hardly any good musicians. I only know two gentlemen who sing at all well.”\n\n“I suppose it is the fashion to sing comic songs in a rhythmic way, leaving you to fancy the tune—very much as if it were tapped on a drum?”\n\n“Ah, you have heard Mr. Bowyer,” said Rosamond, with one of her rare smiles. “But we are speaking very ill of our neighbors.”\n\nLydgate was almost forgetting that he must carry on the conversation, in thinking how lovely this creature was, her garment seeming to be made out of the faintest blue sky, herself so immaculately blond, as if the petals of some gigantic flower had just opened and disclosed her; and yet with this infantine blondness showing so much ready, self-possessed grace. Since he had had the memory of Laure, Lydgate had lost all taste for large-eyed silence: the divine cow no longer attracted him, and Rosamond was her very opposite. But he recalled himself.\n\n“You will let me hear some music to-night, I hope.”\n\n“I will let you hear my attempts, if you like,” said Rosamond. “Papa is sure to insist on my singing. But I shall tremble before you, who have heard the best singers in Paris. I have heard very little: I have only once been to London. But our organist at St. Peter’s is a good musician, and I go on studying with him.”\n\n“Tell me what you saw in London.”\n\n“Very little.” (A more naive girl would have said, “Oh, everything!” But Rosamond knew better.) “A few of the ordinary sights, such as raw country girls are always taken to.”\n\n“Do you call yourself a raw country girl?” said Lydgate, looking at her with an involuntary emphasis of admiration, which made Rosamond blush with pleasure. But she remained simply serious, turned her long neck a little, and put up her hand to touch her wondrous hair-plaits—an habitual gesture with her as pretty as any movements of a kitten’s paw. Not that Rosamond was in the least like a kitten: she was a sylph caught young and educated at Mrs. Lemon’s.\n\n“I assure you my mind is raw,” she said immediately; “I pass at Middlemarch. I am not afraid of talking to our old neighbors. But I am really afraid of you.”\n\n“An accomplished woman almost always knows more than we men, though her knowledge is of a different sort. I am sure you could teach me a thousand things—as an exquisite bird could teach a bear if there were any common language between them. Happily, there is a common language between women and men, and so the bears can get taught.”\n\n“Ah, there is Fred beginning to strum! I must go and hinder him from jarring all your nerves,” said Rosamond, moving to the other side of the room, where Fred having opened the piano, at his father’s desire, that Rosamond might give them some music, was parenthetically performing “Cherry Ripe!” with one hand. Able men who have passed their examinations will do these things sometimes, not less than the plucked Fred.\n\n“Fred, pray defer your practising till to-morrow; you will make Mr. Lydgate ill,” said Rosamond. “He has an ear.”\n\nFred laughed, and went on with his tune to the end.\n\nRosamond turned to Lydgate, smiling gently, and said, “You perceive, the bears will not always be taught.”\n\n“Now then, Rosy!” said Fred, springing from the stool and twisting it upward for her, with a hearty expectation of enjoyment. “Some good rousing tunes first.”\n\nRosamond played admirably. Her master at Mrs. Lemon’s school (close to a county town with a memorable history that had its relics in church and castle) was one of those excellent musicians here and there to be found in our provinces, worthy to compare with many a noted Kapellmeister in a country which offers more plentiful conditions of musical celebrity. Rosamond, with the executant’s instinct, had seized his manner of playing, and gave forth his large rendering of noble music with the precision of an echo. It was almost startling, heard for the first time. A hidden soul seemed to be flowing forth from Rosamond’s fingers; and so indeed it was, since souls live on in perpetual echoes, and to all fine expression there goes somewhere an originating activity, if it be only that of an interpreter. Lydgate was taken possession of, and began to believe in her as something exceptional. After all, he thought, one need not be surprised to find the rare conjunctions of nature under circumstances apparently unfavorable: come where they may, they always depend on conditions that are not obvious. He sat looking at her, and did not rise to pay her any compliments, leaving that to others, now that his admiration was deepened.\n\nHer singing was less remarkable, but also well trained, and sweet to hear as a chime perfectly in tune. It is true she sang “Meet me by moonlight,” and “I’ve been roaming”; for mortals must share the fashions of their time, and none but the ancients can be always classical. But Rosamond could also sing “Black-eyed Susan” with effect, or Haydn’s canzonets, or “Voi, che sapete,” or “Batti, batti”—she only wanted to know what her audience liked.\n\nHer father looked round at the company, delighting in their admiration. Her mother sat, like a Niobe before her troubles, with her youngest little girl on her lap, softly beating the child’s hand up and down in time to the music. And Fred, notwithstanding his general scepticism about Rosy, listened to her music with perfect allegiance, wishing he could do the same thing on his flute. It was the pleasantest family party that Lydgate had seen since he came to Middlemarch. The Vincys had the readiness to enjoy, the rejection of all anxiety, and the belief in life as a merry lot, which made a house exceptional in most county towns at that time, when Evangelicalism had cast a certain suspicion as of plague-infection over the few amusements which survived in the provinces. At the Vincys’ there was always whist, and the card-tables stood ready now, making some of the company secretly impatient of the music. Before it ceased Mr. Farebrother came in—a handsome, broad-chested but otherwise small man, about forty, whose black was very threadbare: the brilliancy was all in his quick gray eyes. He came like a pleasant change in the light, arresting little Louisa with fatherly nonsense as she was being led out of the room by Miss Morgan, greeting everybody with some special word, and seeming to condense more talk into ten minutes than had been held all through the evening. He claimed from Lydgate the fulfilment of a promise to come and see him. “I can’t let you off, you know, because I have some beetles to show you. We collectors feel an interest in every new man till he has seen all we have to show him.”\n\nBut soon he swerved to the whist-table, rubbing his hands and saying, “Come now, let us be serious! Mr. Lydgate? not play? Ah! you are too young and light for this kind of thing.”\n\nLydgate said to himself that the clergyman whose abilities were so painful to Mr. Bulstrode, appeared to have found an agreeable resort in this certainly not erudite household. He could half understand it: the good-humor, the good looks of elder and younger, and the provision for passing the time without any labor of intelligence, might make the house beguiling to people who had no particular use for their odd hours.\n\nEverything looked blooming and joyous except Miss Morgan, who was brown, dull, and resigned, and altogether, as Mrs. Vincy often said, just the sort of person for a governess. Lydgate did not mean to pay many such visits himself. They were a wretched waste of the evenings; and now, when he had talked a little more to Rosamond, he meant to excuse himself and go.\n\n“You will not like us at Middlemarch, I feel sure,” she said, when the whist-players were settled. “We are very stupid, and you have been used to something quite different.”\n\n“I suppose all country towns are pretty much alike,” said Lydgate. “But I have noticed that one always believes one’s own town to be more stupid than any other. I have made up my mind to take Middlemarch as it comes, and shall be much obliged if the town will take me in the same way. I have certainly found some charms in it which are much greater than I had expected.”\n\n“You mean the rides towards Tipton and Lowick; every one is pleased with those,” said Rosamond, with simplicity.\n\n“No, I mean something much nearer to me.”\n\nRosamond rose and reached her netting, and then said, “Do you care about dancing at all? I am not quite sure whether clever men ever dance.”\n\n“I would dance with you if you would allow me.”\n\n“Oh!” said Rosamond, with a slight deprecatory laugh. “I was only going to say that we sometimes have dancing, and I wanted to know whether you would feel insulted if you were asked to come.”\n\n“Not on the condition I mentioned.”\n\nAfter this chat Lydgate thought that he was going, but on moving towards the whist-tables, he got interested in watching Mr. Farebrother’s play, which was masterly, and also his face, which was a striking mixture of the shrewd and the mild. At ten o’clock supper was brought in (such were the customs of Middlemarch) and there was punch-drinking; but Mr. Farebrother had only a glass of water. He was winning, but there seemed to be no reason why the renewal of rubbers should end, and Lydgate at last took his leave.\n\nBut as it was not eleven o’clock, he chose to walk in the brisk air towards the tower of St. Botolph’s, Mr. Farebrother’s church, which stood out dark, square, and massive against the starlight. It was the oldest church in Middlemarch; the living, however, was but a vicarage worth barely four hundred a-year. Lydgate had heard that, and he wondered now whether Mr. Farebrother cared about the money he won at cards; thinking, “He seems a very pleasant fellow, but Bulstrode may have his good reasons.” Many things would be easier to Lydgate if it should turn out that Mr. Bulstrode was generally justifiable. “What is his religious doctrine to me, if he carries some good notions along with it? One must use such brains as are to be found.”\n\nThese were actually Lydgate’s first meditations as he walked away from Mr. Vincy’s, and on this ground I fear that many ladies will consider him hardly worthy of their attention. He thought of Rosamond and her music only in the second place; and though, when her turn came, he dwelt on the image of her for the rest of his walk, he felt no agitation, and had no sense that any new current had set into his life. He could not marry yet; he wished not to marry for several years; and therefore he was not ready to entertain the notion of being in love with a girl whom he happened to admire. He did admire Rosamond exceedingly; but that madness which had once beset him about Laure was not, he thought, likely to recur in relation to any other woman. Certainly, if falling in love had been at all in question, it would have been quite safe with a creature like this Miss Vincy, who had just the kind of intelligence one would desire in a woman—polished, refined, docile, lending itself to finish in all the delicacies of life, and enshrined in a body which expressed this with a force of demonstration that excluded the need for other evidence. Lydgate felt sure that if ever he married, his wife would have that feminine radiance, that distinctive womanhood which must be classed with flowers and music, that sort of beauty which by its very nature was virtuous, being moulded only for pure and delicate joys.\n\nBut since he did not mean to marry for the next five years—his more pressing business was to look into Louis’ new book on Fever, which he was specially interested in, because he had known Louis in Paris, and had followed many anatomical demonstrations in order to ascertain the specific differences of typhus and typhoid. He went home and read far into the smallest hour, bringing a much more testing vision of details and relations into this pathological study than he had ever thought it necessary to apply to the complexities of love and marriage, these being subjects on which he felt himself amply informed by literature, and that traditional wisdom which is handed down in the genial conversation of men. Whereas Fever had obscure conditions, and gave him that delightful labor of the imagination which is not mere arbitrariness, but the exercise of disciplined power—combining and constructing with the clearest eye for probabilities and the fullest obedience to knowledge; and then, in yet more energetic alliance with impartial Nature, standing aloof to invent tests by which to try its own work.\n\nMany men have been praised as vividly imaginative on the strength of their profuseness in indifferent drawing or cheap narration:—reports of very poor talk going on in distant orbs; or portraits of Lucifer coming down on his bad errands as a large ugly man with bat’s wings and spurts of phosphorescence; or exaggerations of wantonness that seem to reflect life in a diseased dream. But these kinds of inspiration Lydgate regarded as rather vulgar and vinous compared with the imagination that reveals subtle actions inaccessible by any sort of lens, but tracked in that outer darkness through long pathways of necessary sequence by the inward light which is the last refinement of Energy, capable of bathing even the ethereal atoms in its ideally illuminated space. He for his part had tossed away all cheap inventions where ignorance finds itself able and at ease: he was enamoured of that arduous invention which is the very eye of research, provisionally framing its object and correcting it to more and more exactness of relation; he wanted to pierce the obscurity of those minute processes which prepare human misery and joy, those invisible thoroughfares which are the first lurking-places of anguish, mania, and crime, that delicate poise and transition which determine the growth of happy or unhappy consciousness.\n\nAs he threw down his book, stretched his legs towards the embers in the grate, and clasped his hands at the back of his head, in that agreeable afterglow of excitement when thought lapses from examination of a specific object into a suffusive sense of its connections with all the rest of our existence—seems, as it were, to throw itself on its back after vigorous swimming and float with the repose of unexhausted strength—Lydgate felt a triumphant delight in his studies, and something like pity for those less lucky men who were not of his profession.\n\n“If I had not taken that turn when I was a lad,” he thought, “I might have got into some stupid draught-horse work or other, and lived always in blinkers. I should never have been happy in any profession that did not call forth the highest intellectual strain, and yet keep me in good warm contact with my neighbors. There is nothing like the medical profession for that: one can have the exclusive scientific life that touches the distance and befriend the old fogies in the parish too. It is rather harder for a clergyman: Farebrother seems to be an anomaly.”\n\nThis last thought brought back the Vincys and all the pictures of the evening. They floated in his mind agreeably enough, and as he took up his bed-candle his lips were curled with that incipient smile which is apt to accompany agreeable recollections. He was an ardent fellow, but at present his ardor was absorbed in love of his work and in the ambition of making his life recognized as a factor in the better life of mankind—like other heroes of science who had nothing but an obscure country practice to begin with.\n\nPoor Lydgate! or shall I say, Poor Rosamond! Each lived in a world of which the other knew nothing. It had not occurred to Lydgate that he had been a subject of eager meditation to Rosamond, who had neither any reason for throwing her marriage into distant perspective, nor any pathological studies to divert her mind from that ruminating habit, that inward repetition of looks, words, and phrases, which makes a large part in the lives of most girls. He had not meant to look at her or speak to her with more than the inevitable amount of admiration and compliment which a man must give to a beautiful girl; indeed, it seemed to him that his enjoyment of her music had remained almost silent, for he feared falling into the rudeness of telling her his great surprise at her possession of such accomplishment. But Rosamond had registered every look and word, and estimated them as the opening incidents of a preconceived romance—incidents which gather value from the foreseen development and climax. In Rosamond’s romance it was not necessary to imagine much about the inward life of the hero, or of his serious business in the world: of course, he had a profession and was clever, as well as sufficiently handsome; but the piquant fact about Lydgate was his good birth, which distinguished him from all Middlemarch admirers, and presented marriage as a prospect of rising in rank and getting a little nearer to that celestial condition on earth in which she would have nothing to do with vulgar people, and perhaps at last associate with relatives quite equal to the county people who looked down on the Middlemarchers. It was part of Rosamond’s cleverness to discern very subtly the faintest aroma of rank, and once when she had seen the Miss Brookes accompanying their uncle at the county assizes, and seated among the aristocracy, she had envied them, notwithstanding their plain dress.\n\nIf you think it incredible that to imagine Lydgate as a man of family could cause thrills of satisfaction which had anything to do with the sense that she was in love with him, I will ask you to use your power of comparison a little more effectively, and consider whether red cloth and epaulets have never had an influence of that sort. Our passions do not live apart in locked chambers, but, dressed in their small wardrobe of notions, bring their provisions to a common table and mess together, feeding out of the common store according to their appetite.\n\nRosamond, in fact, was entirely occupied not exactly with Tertius Lydgate as he was in himself, but with his relation to her; and it was excusable in a girl who was accustomed to hear that all young men might, could, would be, or actually were in love with her, to believe at once that Lydgate could be no exception. His looks and words meant more to her than other men’s, because she cared more for them: she thought of them diligently, and diligently attended to that perfection of appearance, behavior, sentiments, and all other elegancies, which would find in Lydgate a more adequate admirer than she had yet been conscious of.\n\nFor Rosamond, though she would never do anything that was disagreeable to her, was industrious; and now more than ever she was active in sketching her landscapes and market-carts and portraits of friends, in practising her music, and in being from morning till night her own standard of a perfect lady, having always an audience in her own consciousness, with sometimes the not unwelcome addition of a more variable external audience in the numerous visitors of the house. She found time also to read the best novels, and even the second best, and she knew much poetry by heart. Her favorite poem was “Lalla Rookh.”\n\n“The best girl in the world! He will be a happy fellow who gets her!” was the sentiment of the elderly gentlemen who visited the Vincys; and the rejected young men thought of trying again, as is the fashion in country towns where the horizon is not thick with coming rivals. But Mrs. Plymdale thought that Rosamond had been educated to a ridiculous pitch, for what was the use of accomplishments which would be all laid aside as soon as she was married? While her aunt Bulstrode, who had a sisterly faithfulness towards her brother’s family, had two sincere wishes for Rosamond—that she might show a more serious turn of mind, and that she might meet with a husband whose wealth corresponded to her habits.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII.\n\n“The clerkly person smiled and said Promise was a pretty maid, But being poor she died unwed.”\nThe Rev. Camden Farebrother, whom Lydgate went to see the next evening, lived in an old parsonage, built of stone, venerable enough to match the church which it looked out upon. All the furniture too in the house was old, but with another grade of age—that of Mr. Farebrother’s father and grandfather. There were painted white chairs, with gilding and wreaths on them, and some lingering red silk damask with slits in it. There were engraved portraits of Lord Chancellors and other celebrated lawyers of the last century; and there were old pier-glasses to reflect them, as well as the little satin-wood tables and the sofas resembling a prolongation of uneasy chairs, all standing in relief against the dark wainscot. This was the physiognomy of the drawing-room into which Lydgate was shown; and there were three ladies to receive him, who were also old-fashioned, and of a faded but genuine respectability: Mrs. Farebrother, the Vicar’s white-haired mother, befrilled and kerchiefed with dainty cleanliness, upright, quick-eyed, and still under seventy; Miss Noble, her sister, a tiny old lady of meeker aspect, with frills and kerchief decidedly more worn and mended; and Miss Winifred Farebrother, the Vicar’s elder sister, well-looking like himself, but nipped and subdued as single women are apt to be who spend their lives in uninterrupted subjection to their elders. Lydgate had not expected to see so quaint a group: knowing simply that Mr. Farebrother was a bachelor, he had thought of being ushered into a snuggery where the chief furniture would probably be books and collections of natural objects. The Vicar himself seemed to wear rather a changed aspect, as most men do when acquaintances made elsewhere see them for the first time in their own homes; some indeed showing like an actor of genial parts disadvantageously cast for the curmudgeon in a new piece. This was not the case with Mr. Farebrother: he seemed a trifle milder and more silent, the chief talker being his mother, while he only put in a good-humored moderating remark here and there. The old lady was evidently accustomed to tell her company what they ought to think, and to regard no subject as quite safe without her steering. She was afforded leisure for this function by having all her little wants attended to by Miss Winifred. Meanwhile tiny Miss Noble carried on her arm a small basket, into which she diverted a bit of sugar, which she had first dropped in her saucer as if by mistake; looking round furtively afterwards, and reverting to her teacup with a small innocent noise as of a tiny timid quadruped. Pray think no ill of Miss Noble. That basket held small savings from her more portable food, destined for the children of her poor friends among whom she trotted on fine mornings; fostering and petting all needy creatures being so spontaneous a delight to her, that she regarded it much as if it had been a pleasant vice that she was addicted to. Perhaps she was conscious of being tempted to steal from those who had much that she might give to those who had nothing, and carried in her conscience the guilt of that repressed desire. One must be poor to know the luxury of giving!\n\nMrs. Farebrother welcomed the guest with a lively formality and precision. She presently informed him that they were not often in want of medical aid in that house. She had brought up her children to wear flannel and not to over-eat themselves, which last habit she considered the chief reason why people needed doctors. Lydgate pleaded for those whose fathers and mothers had over-eaten themselves, but Mrs. Farebrother held that view of things dangerous: Nature was more just than that; it would be easy for any felon to say that his ancestors ought to have been hanged instead of him. If those who had bad fathers and mothers were bad themselves, they were hanged for that. There was no need to go back on what you couldn’t see.\n\n“My mother is like old George the Third,” said the Vicar, “she objects to metaphysics.”\n\n“I object to what is wrong, Camden. I say, keep hold of a few plain truths, and make everything square with them. When I was young, Mr. Lydgate, there never was any question about right and wrong. We knew our catechism, and that was enough; we learned our creed and our duty. Every respectable Church person had the same opinions. But now, if you speak out of the Prayer-book itself, you are liable to be contradicted.”\n\n“That makes rather a pleasant time of it for those who like to maintain their own point,” said Lydgate.\n\n“But my mother always gives way,” said the Vicar, slyly.\n\n“No, no, Camden, you must not lead Mr. Lydgate into a mistake about me. I shall never show that disrespect to my parents, to give up what they taught me. Any one may see what comes of turning. If you change once, why not twenty times?”\n\n“A man might see good arguments for changing once, and not see them for changing again,” said Lydgate, amused with the decisive old lady.\n\n“Excuse me there. If you go upon arguments, they are never wanting, when a man has no constancy of mind. My father never changed, and he preached plain moral sermons without arguments, and was a good man—few better. When you get me a good man made out of arguments, I will get you a good dinner with reading you the cookery-book. That’s my opinion, and I think anybody’s stomach will bear me out.”\n\n“About the dinner certainly, mother,” said Mr. Farebrother.\n\n“It is the same thing, the dinner or the man. I am nearly seventy, Mr. Lydgate, and I go upon experience. I am not likely to follow new lights, though there are plenty of them here as elsewhere. I say, they came in with the mixed stuffs that will neither wash nor wear. It was not so in my youth: a Churchman was a Churchman, and a clergyman, you might be pretty sure, was a gentleman, if nothing else. But now he may be no better than a Dissenter, and want to push aside my son on pretence of doctrine. But whoever may wish to push him aside, I am proud to say, Mr. Lydgate, that he will compare with any preacher in this kingdom, not to speak of this town, which is but a low standard to go by; at least, to my thinking, for I was born and bred at Exeter.”\n\n“A mother is never partial,” said Mr. Farebrother, smiling. “What do you think Tyke’s mother says about him?”\n\n“Ah, poor creature! what indeed?” said Mrs. Farebrother, her sharpness blunted for the moment by her confidence in maternal judgments. “She says the truth to herself, depend upon it.”\n\n“And what is the truth?” said Lydgate. “I am curious to know.”\n\n“Oh, nothing bad at all,” said Mr. Farebrother. “He is a zealous fellow: not very learned, and not very wise, I think—because I don’t agree with him.”\n\n“Why, Camden!” said Miss Winifred, “Griffin and his wife told me only to-day, that Mr. Tyke said they should have no more coals if they came to hear you preach.”\n\nMrs. Farebrother laid down her knitting, which she had resumed after her small allowance of tea and toast, and looked at her son as if to say “You hear that?” Miss Noble said, “Oh poor things! poor things!” in reference, probably, to the double loss of preaching and coal. But the Vicar answered quietly—\n\n“That is because they are not my parishioners. And I don’t think my sermons are worth a load of coals to them.”\n\n“Mr. Lydgate,” said Mrs. Farebrother, who could not let this pass, “you don’t know my son: he always undervalues himself. I tell him he is undervaluing the God who made him, and made him a most excellent preacher.”\n\n“That must be a hint for me to take Mr. Lydgate away to my study, mother,” said the Vicar, laughing. “I promised to show you my collection,” he added, turning to Lydgate; “shall we go?”\n\nAll three ladies remonstrated. Mr. Lydgate ought not to be hurried away without being allowed to accept another cup of tea: Miss Winifred had abundance of good tea in the pot. Why was Camden in such haste to take a visitor to his den? There was nothing but pickled vermin, and drawers full of blue-bottles and moths, with no carpet on the floor. Mr. Lydgate must excuse it. A game at cribbage would be far better. In short, it was plain that a vicar might be adored by his womankind as the king of men and preachers, and yet be held by them to stand in much need of their direction. Lydgate, with the usual shallowness of a young bachelor, wondered that Mr. Farebrother had not taught them better.\n\n“My mother is not used to my having visitors who can take any interest in my hobbies,” said the Vicar, as he opened the door of his study, which was indeed as bare of luxuries for the body as the ladies had implied, unless a short porcelain pipe and a tobacco-box were to be excepted.\n\n“Men of your profession don’t generally smoke,” he said. Lydgate smiled and shook his head. “Nor of mine either, properly, I suppose. You will hear that pipe alleged against me by Bulstrode and Company. They don’t know how pleased the devil would be if I gave it up.”\n\n“I understand. You are of an excitable temper and want a sedative. I am heavier, and should get idle with it. I should rush into idleness, and stagnate there with all my might.”\n\n“And you mean to give it all to your work. I am some ten or twelve years older than you, and have come to a compromise. I feed a weakness or two lest they should get clamorous. See,” continued the Vicar, opening several small drawers, “I fancy I have made an exhaustive study of the entomology of this district. I am going on both with the fauna and flora; but I have at least done my insects well. We are singularly rich in orthoptera: I don’t know whether—Ah! you have got hold of that glass jar—you are looking into that instead of my drawers. You don’t really care about these things?”\n\n“Not by the side of this lovely anencephalous monster. I have never had time to give myself much to natural history. I was early bitten with an interest in structure, and it is what lies most directly in my profession. I have no hobby besides. I have the sea to swim in there.”\n\n“Ah! you are a happy fellow,” said Mr. Farebrother, turning on his heel and beginning to fill his pipe. “You don’t know what it is to want spiritual tobacco—bad emendations of old texts, or small items about a variety of Aphis Brassicae, with the well-known signature of Philomicron, for the ‘Twaddler’s Magazine;’ or a learned treatise on the entomology of the Pentateuch, including all the insects not mentioned, but probably met with by the Israelites in their passage through the desert; with a monograph on the Ant, as treated by Solomon, showing the harmony of the Book of Proverbs with the results of modern research. You don’t mind my fumigating you?”\n\nLydgate was more surprised at the openness of this talk than at its implied meaning—that the Vicar felt himself not altogether in the right vocation. The neat fitting-up of drawers and shelves, and the bookcase filled with expensive illustrated books on Natural History, made him think again of the winnings at cards and their destination. But he was beginning to wish that the very best construction of everything that Mr. Farebrother did should be the true one. The Vicar’s frankness seemed not of the repulsive sort that comes from an uneasy consciousness seeking to forestall the judgment of others, but simply the relief of a desire to do with as little pretence as possible. Apparently he was not without a sense that his freedom of speech might seem premature, for he presently said—\n\n“I have not yet told you that I have the advantage of you, Mr. Lydgate, and know you better than you know me. You remember Trawley who shared your apartment at Paris for some time? I was a correspondent of his, and he told me a good deal about you. I was not quite sure when you first came that you were the same man. I was very glad when I found that you were. Only I don’t forget that you have not had the like prologue about me.”\n\nLydgate divined some delicacy of feeling here, but did not half understand it. “By the way,” he said, “what has become of Trawley? I have quite lost sight of him. He was hot on the French social systems, and talked of going to the Backwoods to found a sort of Pythagorean community. Is he gone?”\n\n“Not at all. He is practising at a German bath, and has married a rich patient.”\n\n“Then my notions wear the best, so far,” said Lydgate, with a short scornful laugh. “He would have it, the medical profession was an inevitable system of humbug. I said, the fault was in the men—men who truckle to lies and folly. Instead of preaching against humbug outside the walls, it might be better to set up a disinfecting apparatus within. In short—I am reporting my own conversation—you may be sure I had all the good sense on my side.”\n\n“Your scheme is a good deal more difficult to carry out than the Pythagorean community, though. You have not only got the old Adam in yourself against you, but you have got all those descendants of the original Adam who form the society around you. You see, I have paid twelve or thirteen years more than you for my knowledge of difficulties. But”—Mr. Farebrother broke off a moment, and then added, “you are eying that glass vase again. Do you want to make an exchange? You shall not have it without a fair barter.”\n\n“I have some sea-mice—fine specimens—in spirits. And I will throw in Robert Brown’s new thing—‘Microscopic Observations on the Pollen of Plants’—if you don’t happen to have it already.”\n\n“Why, seeing how you long for the monster, I might ask a higher price. Suppose I ask you to look through my drawers and agree with me about all my new species?” The Vicar, while he talked in this way, alternately moved about with his pipe in his mouth, and returned to hang rather fondly over his drawers. “That would be good discipline, you know, for a young doctor who has to please his patients in Middlemarch. You must learn to be bored, remember. However, you shall have the monster on your own terms.”\n\n“Don’t you think men overrate the necessity for humoring everybody’s nonsense, till they get despised by the very fools they humor?” said Lydgate, moving to Mr. Farebrother’s side, and looking rather absently at the insects ranged in fine gradation, with names subscribed in exquisite writing. “The shortest way is to make your value felt, so that people must put up with you whether you flatter them or not.”\n\n“With all my heart. But then you must be sure of having the value, and you must keep yourself independent. Very few men can do that. Either you slip out of service altogether, and become good for nothing, or you wear the harness and draw a good deal where your yoke-fellows pull you. But do look at these delicate orthoptera!”\n\nLydgate had after all to give some scrutiny to each drawer, the Vicar laughing at himself, and yet persisting in the exhibition.\n\n“Apropos of what you said about wearing harness,” Lydgate began, after they had sat down, “I made up my mind some time ago to do with as little of it as possible. That was why I determined not to try anything in London, for a good many years at least. I didn’t like what I saw when I was studying there—so much empty bigwiggism, and obstructive trickery. In the country, people have less pretension to knowledge, and are less of companions, but for that reason they affect one’s amour-propre less: one makes less bad blood, and can follow one’s own course more quietly.”\n\n“Yes—well—you have got a good start; you are in the right profession, the work you feel yourself most fit for. Some people miss that, and repent too late. But you must not be too sure of keeping your independence.”\n\n“You mean of family ties?” said Lydgate, conceiving that these might press rather tightly on Mr. Farebrother.\n\n“Not altogether. Of course they make many things more difficult. But a good wife—a good unworldly woman—may really help a man, and keep him more independent. There’s a parishioner of mine—a fine fellow, but who would hardly have pulled through as he has done without his wife. Do you know the Garths? I think they were not Peacock’s patients.”\n\n“No; but there is a Miss Garth at old Featherstone’s, at Lowick.”\n\n“Their daughter: an excellent girl.”\n\n“She is very quiet—I have hardly noticed her.”\n\n“She has taken notice of you, though, depend upon it.”\n\n“I don’t understand,” said Lydgate; he could hardly say “Of course.”\n\n“Oh, she gauges everybody. I prepared her for confirmation—she is a favorite of mine.”\n\nMr. Farebrother puffed a few moments in silence, Lydgate not caring to know more about the Garths. At last the Vicar laid down his pipe, stretched out his legs, and turned his bright eyes with a smile towards Lydgate, saying—\n\n“But we Middlemarchers are not so tame as you take us to be. We have our intrigues and our parties. I am a party man, for example, and Bulstrode is another. If you vote for me you will offend Bulstrode.”\n\n“What is there against Bulstrode?” said Lydgate, emphatically.\n\n“I did not say there was anything against him except that. If you vote against him you will make him your enemy.”\n\n“I don’t know that I need mind about that,” said Lydgate, rather proudly; “but he seems to have good ideas about hospitals, and he spends large sums on useful public objects. He might help me a good deal in carrying out my ideas. As to his religious notions—why, as Voltaire said, incantations will destroy a flock of sheep if administered with a certain quantity of arsenic. I look for the man who will bring the arsenic, and don’t mind about his incantations.”\n\n“Very good. But then you must not offend your arsenic-man. You will not offend me, you know,” said Mr. Farebrother, quite unaffectedly. “I don’t translate my own convenience into other people’s duties. I am opposed to Bulstrode in many ways. I don’t like the set he belongs to: they are a narrow ignorant set, and do more to make their neighbors uncomfortable than to make them better. Their system is a sort of worldly-spiritual cliqueism: they really look on the rest of mankind as a doomed carcass which is to nourish them for heaven. But,” he added, smilingly, “I don’t say that Bulstrode’s new hospital is a bad thing; and as to his wanting to oust me from the old one—why, if he thinks me a mischievous fellow, he is only returning a compliment. And I am not a model clergyman—only a decent makeshift.”\n\nLydgate was not at all sure that the Vicar maligned himself. A model clergyman, like a model doctor, ought to think his own profession the finest in the world, and take all knowledge as mere nourishment to his moral pathology and therapeutics. He only said, “What reason does Bulstrode give for superseding you?”\n\n“That I don’t teach his opinions—which he calls spiritual religion; and that I have no time to spare. Both statements are true. But then I could make time, and I should be glad of the forty pounds. That is the plain fact of the case. But let us dismiss it. I only wanted to tell you that if you vote for your arsenic-man, you are not to cut me in consequence. I can’t spare you. You are a sort of circumnavigator come to settle among us, and will keep up my belief in the antipodes. Now tell me all about them in Paris.”\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII.\n\n“Oh, sir, the loftiest hopes on earth Draw lots with meaner hopes: heroic breasts, Breathing bad air, run risk of pestilence; Or, lacking lime-juice when they cross the Line, May languish with the scurvy.”\n\nSome weeks passed after this conversation before the question of the chaplaincy gathered any practical import for Lydgate, and without telling himself the reason, he deferred the predetermination on which side he should give his vote. It would really have been a matter of total indifference to him—that is to say, he would have taken the more convenient side, and given his vote for the appointment of Tyke without any hesitation—if he had not cared personally for Mr. Farebrother.\n\nBut his liking for the Vicar of St. Botolph’s grew with growing acquaintanceship. That, entering into Lydgate’s position as a new-comer who had his own professional objects to secure, Mr. Farebrother should have taken pains rather to warn off than to obtain his interest, showed an unusual delicacy and generosity, which Lydgate’s nature was keenly alive to. It went along with other points of conduct in Mr. Farebrother which were exceptionally fine, and made his character resemble those southern landscapes which seem divided between natural grandeur and social slovenliness. Very few men could have been as filial and chivalrous as he was to the mother, aunt, and sister, whose dependence on him had in many ways shaped his life rather uneasily for himself; few men who feel the pressure of small needs are so nobly resolute not to dress up their inevitably self-interested desires in a pretext of better motives. In these matters he was conscious that his life would bear the closest scrutiny; and perhaps the consciousness encouraged a little defiance towards the critical strictness of persons whose celestial intimacies seemed not to improve their domestic manners, and whose lofty aims were not needed to account for their actions. Then, his preaching was ingenious and pithy, like the preaching of the English Church in its robust age, and his sermons were delivered without book. People outside his parish went to hear him; and, since to fill the church was always the most difficult part of a clergyman’s function, here was another ground for a careless sense of superiority. Besides, he was a likable man: sweet-tempered, ready-witted, frank, without grins of suppressed bitterness or other conversational flavors which make half of us an affliction to our friends. Lydgate liked him heartily, and wished for his friendship.\n\nWith this feeling uppermost, he continued to waive the question of the chaplaincy, and to persuade himself that it was not only no proper business of his, but likely enough never to vex him with a demand for his vote. Lydgate, at Mr. Bulstrode’s request, was laying down plans for the internal arrangements of the new hospital, and the two were often in consultation. The banker was always presupposing that he could count in general on Lydgate as a coadjutor, but made no special recurrence to the coming decision between Tyke and Farebrother. When the General Board of the Infirmary had met, however, and Lydgate had notice that the question of the chaplaincy was thrown on a council of the directors and medical men, to meet on the following Friday, he had a vexed sense that he must make up his mind on this trivial Middlemarch business. He could not help hearing within him the distinct declaration that Bulstrode was prime minister, and that the Tyke affair was a question of office or no office; and he could not help an equally pronounced dislike to giving up the prospect of office. For his observation was constantly confirming Mr. Farebrother’s assurance that the banker would not overlook opposition. “Confound their petty politics!” was one of his thoughts for three mornings in the meditative process of shaving, when he had begun to feel that he must really hold a court of conscience on this matter. Certainly there were valid things to be said against the election of Mr. Farebrother: he had too much on his hands already, especially considering how much time he spent on non-clerical occupations. Then again it was a continually repeated shock, disturbing Lydgate’s esteem, that the Vicar should obviously play for the sake of money, liking the play indeed, but evidently liking some end which it served. Mr. Farebrother contended on theory for the desirability of all games, and said that Englishmen’s wit was stagnant for want of them; but Lydgate felt certain that he would have played very much less but for the money. There was a billiard-room at the Green Dragon, which some anxious mothers and wives regarded as the chief temptation in Middlemarch. The Vicar was a first-rate billiard-player, and though he did not frequent the Green Dragon, there were reports that he had sometimes been there in the daytime and had won money. And as to the chaplaincy, he did not pretend that he cared for it, except for the sake of the forty pounds. Lydgate was no Puritan, but he did not care for play, and winning money at it had always seemed a meanness to him; besides, he had an ideal of life which made this subservience of conduct to the gaining of small sums thoroughly hateful to him. Hitherto in his own life his wants had been supplied without any trouble to himself, and his first impulse was always to be liberal with half-crowns as matters of no importance to a gentleman; it had never occurred to him to devise a plan for getting half-crowns. He had always known in a general way that he was not rich, but he had never felt poor, and he had no power of imagining the part which the want of money plays in determining the actions of men. Money had never been a motive to him. Hence he was not ready to frame excuses for this deliberate pursuit of small gains. It was altogether repulsive to him, and he never entered into any calculation of the ratio between the Vicar’s income and his more or less necessary expenditure. It was possible that he would not have made such a calculation in his own case.\n\nAnd now, when the question of voting had come, this repulsive fact told more strongly against Mr. Farebrother than it had done before. One would know much better what to do if men’s characters were more consistent, and especially if one’s friends were invariably fit for any function they desired to undertake! Lydgate was convinced that if there had been no valid objection to Mr. Farebrother, he would have voted for him, whatever Bulstrode might have felt on the subject: he did not intend to be a vassal of Bulstrode’s. On the other hand, there was Tyke, a man entirely given to his clerical office, who was simply curate at a chapel of ease in St. Peter’s parish, and had time for extra duty. Nobody had anything to say against Mr. Tyke, except that they could not bear him, and suspected him of cant. Really, from his point of view, Bulstrode was thoroughly justified.\n\nBut whichever way Lydgate began to incline, there was something to make him wince; and being a proud man, he was a little exasperated at being obliged to wince. He did not like frustrating his own best purposes by getting on bad terms with Bulstrode; he did not like voting against Farebrother, and helping to deprive him of function and salary; and the question occurred whether the additional forty pounds might not leave the Vicar free from that ignoble care about winning at cards. Moreover, Lydgate did not like the consciousness that in voting for Tyke he should be voting on the side obviously convenient for himself. But would the end really be his own convenience? Other people would say so, and would allege that he was currying favor with Bulstrode for the sake of making himself important and getting on in the world. What then? He for his own part knew that if his personal prospects simply had been concerned, he would not have cared a rotten nut for the banker’s friendship or enmity. What he really cared for was a medium for his work, a vehicle for his ideas; and after all, was he not bound to prefer the object of getting a good hospital, where he could demonstrate the specific distinctions of fever and test therapeutic results, before anything else connected with this chaplaincy? For the first time Lydgate was feeling the hampering threadlike pressure of small social conditions, and their frustrating complexity. At the end of his inward debate, when he set out for the hospital, his hope was really in the chance that discussion might somehow give a new aspect to the question, and make the scale dip so as to exclude the necessity for voting. I think he trusted a little also to the energy which is begotten by circumstances—some feeling rushing warmly and making resolve easy, while debate in cool blood had only made it more difficult. However it was, he did not distinctly say to himself on which side he would vote; and all the while he was inwardly resenting the subjection which had been forced upon him. It would have seemed beforehand like a ridiculous piece of bad logic that he, with his unmixed resolutions of independence and his select purposes, would find himself at the very outset in the grasp of petty alternatives, each of which was repugnant to him. In his student’s chambers, he had prearranged his social action quite differently.\n\nLydgate was late in setting out, but Dr. Sprague, the two other surgeons, and several of the directors had arrived early; Mr. Bulstrode, treasurer and chairman, being among those who were still absent. The conversation seemed to imply that the issue was problematical, and that a majority for Tyke was not so certain as had been generally supposed. The two physicians, for a wonder, turned out to be unanimous, or rather, though of different minds, they concurred in action. Dr. Sprague, the rugged and weighty, was, as every one had foreseen, an adherent of Mr. Farebrother. The Doctor was more than suspected of having no religion, but somehow Middlemarch tolerated this deficiency in him as if he had been a Lord Chancellor; indeed it is probable that his professional weight was the more believed in, the world-old association of cleverness with the evil principle being still potent in the minds even of lady-patients who had the strictest ideas of frilling and sentiment. It was perhaps this negation in the Doctor which made his neighbors call him hard-headed and dry-witted; conditions of texture which were also held favorable to the storing of judgments connected with drugs. At all events, it is certain that if any medical man had come to Middlemarch with the reputation of having very definite religious views, of being given to prayer, and of otherwise showing an active piety, there would have been a general presumption against his medical skill.\n\nOn this ground it was (professionally speaking) fortunate for Dr. Minchin that his religious sympathies were of a general kind, and such as gave a distant medical sanction to all serious sentiment, whether of Church or Dissent, rather than any adhesion to particular tenets. If Mr. Bulstrode insisted, as he was apt to do, on the Lutheran doctrine of justification, as that by which a Church must stand or fall, Dr. Minchin in return was quite sure that man was not a mere machine or a fortuitous conjunction of atoms; if Mrs. Wimple insisted on a particular providence in relation to her stomach complaint, Dr. Minchin for his part liked to keep the mental windows open and objected to fixed limits; if the Unitarian brewer jested about the Athanasian Creed, Dr. Minchin quoted Pope’s “Essay on Man.” He objected to the rather free style of anecdote in which Dr. Sprague indulged, preferring well-sanctioned quotations, and liking refinement of all kinds: it was generally known that he had some kinship to a bishop, and sometimes spent his holidays at “the palace.”\n\nDr. Minchin was soft-handed, pale-complexioned, and of rounded outline, not to be distinguished from a mild clergyman in appearance: whereas Dr. Sprague was superfluously tall; his trousers got creased at the knees, and showed an excess of boot at a time when straps seemed necessary to any dignity of bearing; you heard him go in and out, and up and down, as if he had come to see after the roofing. In short, he had weight, and might be expected to grapple with a disease and throw it; while Dr. Minchin might be better able to detect it lurking and to circumvent it. They enjoyed about equally the mysterious privilege of medical reputation, and concealed with much etiquette their contempt for each other’s skill. Regarding themselves as Middlemarch institutions, they were ready to combine against all innovators, and against non-professionals given to interference. On this ground they were both in their hearts equally averse to Mr. Bulstrode, though Dr. Minchin had never been in open hostility with him, and never differed from him without elaborate explanation to Mrs. Bulstrode, who had found that Dr. Minchin alone understood her constitution. A layman who pried into the professional conduct of medical men, and was always obtruding his reforms,—though he was less directly embarrassing to the two physicians than to the surgeon-apothecaries who attended paupers by contract, was nevertheless offensive to the professional nostril as such; and Dr. Minchin shared fully in the new pique against Bulstrode, excited by his apparent determination to patronize Lydgate. The long-established practitioners, Mr. Wrench and Mr. Toller; were just now standing apart and having a friendly colloquy, in which they agreed that Lydgate was a jackanapes, just made to serve Bulstrode’s purpose. To non-medical friends they had already concurred in praising the other young practitioner, who had come into the town on Mr. Peacock’s retirement without further recommendation than his own merits and such argument for solid professional acquirement as might be gathered from his having apparently wasted no time on other branches of knowledge. It was clear that Lydgate, by not dispensing drugs, intended to cast imputations on his equals, and also to obscure the limit between his own rank as a general practitioner and that of the physicians, who, in the interest of the profession, felt bound to maintain its various grades,—especially against a man who had not been to either of the English universities and enjoyed the absence of anatomical and bedside study there, but came with a libellous pretension to experience in Edinburgh and Paris, where observation might be abundant indeed, but hardly sound.\n\nThus it happened that on this occasion Bulstrode became identified with Lydgate, and Lydgate with Tyke; and owing to this variety of interchangeable names for the chaplaincy question, diverse minds were enabled to form the same judgment concerning it.\n\nDr. Sprague said at once bluntly to the group assembled when he entered, “I go for Farebrother. A salary, with all my heart. But why take it from the Vicar? He has none too much—has to insure his life, besides keeping house, and doing a vicar’s charities. Put forty pounds in his pocket and you’ll do no harm. He’s a good fellow, is Farebrother, with as little of the parson about him as will serve to carry orders.”\n\n“Ho, ho! Doctor,” said old Mr. Powderell, a retired iron-monger of some standing—his interjection being something between a laugh and a Parliamentary disapproval; “we must let you have your say. But what we have to consider is not anybody’s income—it’s the souls of the poor sick people”—here Mr. Powderell’s voice and face had a sincere pathos in them. “He is a real Gospel preacher, is Mr. Tyke. I should vote against my conscience if I voted against Mr. Tyke—I should indeed.”\n\n“Mr. Tyke’s opponents have not asked any one to vote against his conscience, I believe,” said Mr. Hackbutt, a rich tanner of fluent speech, whose glittering spectacles and erect hair were turned with some severity towards innocent Mr. Powderell. “But in my judgment it behoves us, as Directors, to consider whether we will regard it as our whole business to carry out propositions emanating from a single quarter. Will any member of the committee aver that he would have entertained the idea of displacing the gentleman who has always discharged the function of chaplain here, if it had not been suggested to him by parties whose disposition it is to regard every institution of this town as a machinery for carrying out their own views? I tax no man’s motives: let them lie between himself and a higher Power; but I do say, that there are influences at work here which are incompatible with genuine independence, and that a crawling servility is usually dictated by circumstances which gentlemen so conducting themselves could not afford either morally or financially to avow. I myself am a layman, but I have given no inconsiderable attention to the divisions in the Church and—”\n\n“Oh, damn the divisions!” burst in Mr. Frank Hawley, lawyer and town-clerk, who rarely presented himself at the board, but now looked in hurriedly, whip in hand. “We have nothing to do with them here. Farebrother has been doing the work—what there was—without pay, and if pay is to be given, it should be given to him. I call it a confounded job to take the thing away from Farebrother.”\n\n“I think it would be as well for gentlemen not to give their remarks a personal bearing,” said Mr. Plymdale. “I shall vote for the appointment of Mr. Tyke, but I should not have known, if Mr. Hackbutt hadn’t hinted it, that I was a Servile Crawler.”\n\n“I disclaim any personalities. I expressly said, if I may be allowed to repeat, or even to conclude what I was about to say—”\n\n“Ah, here’s Minchin!” said Mr. Frank Hawley; at which everybody turned away from Mr. Hackbutt, leaving him to feel the uselessness of superior gifts in Middlemarch. “Come, Doctor, I must have you on the right side, eh?”\n\n“I hope so,” said Dr. Minchin, nodding and shaking hands here and there; “at whatever cost to my feelings.”\n\n“If there’s any feeling here, it should be feeling for the man who is turned out, I think,” said Mr. Frank Hawley.\n\n“I confess I have feelings on the other side also. I have a divided esteem,” said Dr. Minchin, rubbing his hands. “I consider Mr. Tyke an exemplary man—none more so—and I believe him to be proposed from unimpeachable motives. I, for my part, wish that I could give him my vote. But I am constrained to take a view of the case which gives the preponderance to Mr. Farebrother’s claims. He is an amiable man, an able preacher, and has been longer among us.”\n\nOld Mr. Powderell looked on, sad and silent. Mr. Plymdale settled his cravat, uneasily.\n\n“You don’t set up Farebrother as a pattern of what a clergyman ought to be, I hope,” said Mr. Larcher, the eminent carrier, who had just come in. “I have no ill-will towards him, but I think we owe something to the public, not to speak of anything higher, in these appointments. In my opinion Farebrother is too lax for a clergyman. I don’t wish to bring up particulars against him; but he will make a little attendance here go as far as he can.”\n\n“And a devilish deal better than too much,” said Mr. Hawley, whose bad language was notorious in that part of the county. “Sick people can’t bear so much praying and preaching. And that methodistical sort of religion is bad for the spirits—bad for the inside, eh?” he added, turning quickly round to the four medical men who were assembled.\n\nBut any answer was dispensed with by the entrance of three gentlemen, with whom there were greetings more or less cordial. These were the Reverend Edward Thesiger, Rector of St. Peter’s, Mr. Bulstrode, and our friend Mr. Brooke of Tipton, who had lately allowed himself to be put on the board of directors in his turn, but had never before attended, his attendance now being due to Mr. Bulstrode’s exertions. Lydgate was the only person still expected.\n\nEvery one now sat down, Mr. Bulstrode presiding, pale and self-restrained as usual. Mr. Thesiger, a moderate evangelical, wished for the appointment of his friend Mr. Tyke, a zealous able man, who, officiating at a chapel of ease, had not a cure of souls too extensive to leave him ample time for the new duty. It was desirable that chaplaincies of this kind should be entered on with a fervent intention: they were peculiar opportunities for spiritual influence; and while it was good that a salary should be allotted, there was the more need for scrupulous watching lest the office should be perverted into a mere question of salary. Mr. Thesiger’s manner had so much quiet propriety that objectors could only simmer in silence.\n\nMr. Brooke believed that everybody meant well in the matter. He had not himself attended to the affairs of the Infirmary, though he had a strong interest in whatever was for the benefit of Middlemarch, and was most happy to meet the gentlemen present on any public question—“any public question, you know,” Mr. Brooke repeated, with his nod of perfect understanding. “I am a good deal occupied as a magistrate, and in the collection of documentary evidence, but I regard my time as being at the disposal of the public—and, in short, my friends have convinced me that a chaplain with a salary—a salary, you know—is a very good thing, and I am happy to be able to come here and vote for the appointment of Mr. Tyke, who, I understand, is an unexceptionable man, apostolic and eloquent and everything of that kind—and I am the last man to withhold my vote—under the circumstances, you know.”\n\n“It seems to me that you have been crammed with one side of the question, Mr. Brooke,” said Mr. Frank Hawley, who was afraid of nobody, and was a Tory suspicious of electioneering intentions. “You don’t seem to know that one of the worthiest men we have has been doing duty as chaplain here for years without pay, and that Mr. Tyke is proposed to supersede him.”\n\n“Excuse me, Mr. Hawley,” said Mr. Bulstrode. “Mr. Brooke has been fully informed of Mr. Farebrother’s character and position.”\n\n“By his enemies,” flashed out Mr. Hawley.\n\n“I trust there is no personal hostility concerned here,” said Mr. Thesiger.\n\n“I’ll swear there is, though,” retorted Mr. Hawley.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said Mr. Bulstrode, in a subdued tone, “the merits of the question may be very briefly stated, and if any one present doubts that every gentleman who is about to give his vote has not been fully informed, I can now recapitulate the considerations that should weigh on either side.”\n\n“I don’t see the good of that,” said Mr. Hawley. “I suppose we all know whom we mean to vote for. Any man who wants to do justice does not wait till the last minute to hear both sides of the question. I have no time to lose, and I propose that the matter be put to the vote at once.”\n\nA brief but still hot discussion followed before each person wrote “Tyke” or “Farebrother” on a piece of paper and slipped it into a glass tumbler; and in the mean time Mr. Bulstrode saw Lydgate enter.\n\n“I perceive that the votes are equally divided at present,” said Mr. Bulstrode, in a clear biting voice. Then, looking up at Lydgate—\n\n“There is a casting-vote still to be given. It is yours, Mr. Lydgate: will you be good enough to write?”\n\n“The thing is settled now,” said Mr. Wrench, rising. “We all know how Mr. Lydgate will vote.”\n\n“You seem to speak with some peculiar meaning, sir,” said Lydgate, rather defiantly, and keeping his pencil suspended.\n\n“I merely mean that you are expected to vote with Mr. Bulstrode. Do you regard that meaning as offensive?”\n\n“It may be offensive to others. But I shall not desist from voting with him on that account.” Lydgate immediately wrote down “Tyke.”\n\nSo the Rev. Walter Tyke became chaplain to the Infirmary, and Lydgate continued to work with Mr. Bulstrode. He was really uncertain whether Tyke were not the more suitable candidate, and yet his consciousness told him that if he had been quite free from indirect bias he should have voted for Mr. Farebrother. The affair of the chaplaincy remained a sore point in his memory as a case in which this petty medium of Middlemarch had been too strong for him. How could a man be satisfied with a decision between such alternatives and under such circumstances? No more than he can be satisfied with his hat, which he has chosen from among such shapes as the resources of the age offer him, wearing it at best with a resignation which is chiefly supported by comparison.\n\nBut Mr. Farebrother met him with the same friendliness as before. The character of the publican and sinner is not always practically incompatible with that of the modern Pharisee, for the majority of us scarcely see more distinctly the faultiness of our own conduct than the faultiness of our own arguments, or the dulness of our own jokes. But the Vicar of St. Botolph’s had certainly escaped the slightest tincture of the Pharisee, and by dint of admitting to himself that he was too much as other men were, he had become remarkably unlike them in this—that he could excuse others for thinking slightly of him, and could judge impartially of their conduct even when it told against him.\n\n“The world has been too strong for me, I know,” he said one day to Lydgate. “But then I am not a mighty man—I shall never be a man of renown. The choice of Hercules is a pretty fable; but Prodicus makes it easy work for the hero, as if the first resolves were enough. Another story says that he came to hold the distaff, and at last wore the Nessus shirt. I suppose one good resolve might keep a man right if everybody else’s resolve helped him.”\n\nThe Vicar’s talk was not always inspiriting: he had escaped being a Pharisee, but he had not escaped that low estimate of possibilities which we rather hastily arrive at as an inference from our own failure. Lydgate thought that there was a pitiable infirmity of will in Mr. Farebrother.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX.\n\n“L’ altra vedete ch’ha fatto alla guancia Della sua palma, sospirando, letto.” —Purgatorio, vii.\nWhen George the Fourth was still reigning over the privacies of Windsor, when the Duke of Wellington was Prime Minister, and Mr. Vincy was mayor of the old corporation in Middlemarch, Mrs. Casaubon, born Dorothea Brooke, had taken her wedding journey to Rome. In those days the world in general was more ignorant of good and evil by forty years than it is at present. Travellers did not often carry full information on Christian art either in their heads or their pockets; and even the most brilliant English critic of the day mistook the flower-flushed tomb of the ascended Virgin for an ornamental vase due to the painter’s fancy. Romanticism, which has helped to fill some dull blanks with love and knowledge, had not yet penetrated the times with its leaven and entered into everybody’s food; it was fermenting still as a distinguishable vigorous enthusiasm in certain long-haired German artists at Rome, and the youth of other nations who worked or idled near them were sometimes caught in the spreading movement.\n\nOne fine morning a young man whose hair was not immoderately long, but abundant and curly, and who was otherwise English in his equipment, had just turned his back on the Belvedere Torso in the Vatican and was looking out on the magnificent view of the mountains from the adjoining round vestibule. He was sufficiently absorbed not to notice the approach of a dark-eyed, animated German who came up to him and placing a hand on his shoulder, said with a strong accent, “Come here, quick! else she will have changed her pose.”\n\nQuickness was ready at the call, and the two figures passed lightly along by the Meleager, towards the hall where the reclining Ariadne, then called the Cleopatra, lies in the marble voluptuousness of her beauty, the drapery folding around her with a petal-like ease and tenderness. They were just in time to see another figure standing against a pedestal near the reclining marble: a breathing blooming girl, whose form, not shamed by the Ariadne, was clad in Quakerish gray drapery; her long cloak, fastened at the neck, was thrown backward from her arms, and one beautiful ungloved hand pillowed her cheek, pushing somewhat backward the white beaver bonnet which made a sort of halo to her face around the simply braided dark-brown hair. She was not looking at the sculpture, probably not thinking of it: her large eyes were fixed dreamily on a streak of sunlight which fell across the floor. But she became conscious of the two strangers who suddenly paused as if to contemplate the Cleopatra, and, without looking at them, immediately turned away to join a maid-servant and courier who were loitering along the hall at a little distance off.\n\n“What do you think of that for a fine bit of antithesis?” said the German, searching in his friend’s face for responding admiration, but going on volubly without waiting for any other answer. “There lies antique beauty, not corpse-like even in death, but arrested in the complete contentment of its sensuous perfection: and here stands beauty in its breathing life, with the consciousness of Christian centuries in its bosom. But she should be dressed as a nun; I think she looks almost what you call a Quaker; I would dress her as a nun in my picture. However, she is married; I saw her wedding-ring on that wonderful left hand, otherwise I should have thought the sallow Geistlicher was her father. I saw him parting from her a good while ago, and just now I found her in that magnificent pose. Only think! he is perhaps rich, and would like to have her portrait taken. Ah! it is no use looking after her—there she goes! Let us follow her home!”\n\n“No, no,” said his companion, with a little frown.\n\n“You are singular, Ladislaw. You look struck together. Do you know her?”\n\n“I know that she is married to my cousin,” said Will Ladislaw, sauntering down the hall with a preoccupied air, while his German friend kept at his side and watched him eagerly.\n\n“What! the Geistlicher? He looks more like an uncle—a more useful sort of relation.”\n\n“He is not my uncle. I tell you he is my second cousin,” said Ladislaw, with some irritation.\n\n“Schön, schön. Don’t be snappish. You are not angry with me for thinking Mrs. Second-Cousin the most perfect young Madonna I ever saw?”\n\n“Angry? nonsense. I have only seen her once before, for a couple of minutes, when my cousin introduced her to me, just before I left England. They were not married then. I didn’t know they were coming to Rome.”\n\n“But you will go to see them now—you will find out what they have for an address—since you know the name. Shall we go to the post? And you could speak about the portrait.”\n\n“Confound you, Naumann! I don’t know what I shall do. I am not so brazen as you.”\n\n“Bah! that is because you are dilettantish and amateurish. If you were an artist, you would think of Mistress Second-Cousin as antique form animated by Christian sentiment—a sort of Christian Antigone—sensuous force controlled by spiritual passion.”\n\n“Yes, and that your painting her was the chief outcome of her existence—the divinity passing into higher completeness and all but exhausted in the act of covering your bit of canvas. I am amateurish if you like: I do not think that all the universe is straining towards the obscure significance of your pictures.”\n\n“But it is, my dear!—so far as it is straining through me, Adolf Naumann: that stands firm,” said the good-natured painter, putting a hand on Ladislaw’s shoulder, and not in the least disturbed by the unaccountable touch of ill-humor in his tone. “See now! My existence presupposes the existence of the whole universe—does it not? and my function is to paint—and as a painter I have a conception which is altogether genialisch, of your great-aunt or second grandmother as a subject for a picture; therefore, the universe is straining towards that picture through that particular hook or claw which it puts forth in the shape of me—not true?”\n\n“But how if another claw in the shape of me is straining to thwart it?—the case is a little less simple then.”\n\n“Not at all: the result of the struggle is the same thing—picture or no picture—logically.”\n\nWill could not resist this imperturbable temper, and the cloud in his face broke into sunshiny laughter.\n\n“Come now, my friend—you will help?” said Naumann, in a hopeful tone.\n\n“No; nonsense, Naumann! English ladies are not at everybody’s service as models. And you want to express too much with your painting. You would only have made a better or worse portrait with a background which every connoisseur would give a different reason for or against. And what is a portrait of a woman? Your painting and Plastik are poor stuff after all. They perturb and dull conceptions instead of raising them. Language is a finer medium.”\n\n“Yes, for those who can’t paint,” said Naumann. “There you have perfect right. I did not recommend you to paint, my friend.”\n\nThe amiable artist carried his sting, but Ladislaw did not choose to appear stung. He went on as if he had not heard.\n\n“Language gives a fuller image, which is all the better for being vague. After all, the true seeing is within; and painting stares at you with an insistent imperfection. I feel that especially about representations of women. As if a woman were a mere colored superficies! You must wait for movement and tone. There is a difference in their very breathing: they change from moment to moment.—This woman whom you have just seen, for example: how would you paint her voice, pray? But her voice is much diviner than anything you have seen of her.”\n\n“I see, I see. You are jealous. No man must presume to think that he can paint your ideal. This is serious, my friend! Your great-aunt! ‘Der Neffe als Onkel’ in a tragic sense—ungeheuer!”\n\n“You and I shall quarrel, Naumann, if you call that lady my aunt again.”\n\n“How is she to be called then?”\n\n“Mrs. Casaubon.”\n\n“Good. Suppose I get acquainted with her in spite of you, and find that she very much wishes to be painted?”\n\n“Yes, suppose!” said Will Ladislaw, in a contemptuous undertone, intended to dismiss the subject. He was conscious of being irritated by ridiculously small causes, which were half of his own creation. Why was he making any fuss about Mrs. Casaubon? And yet he felt as if something had happened to him with regard to her. There are characters which are continually creating collisions and nodes for themselves in dramas which nobody is prepared to act with them. Their susceptibilities will clash against objects that remain innocently quiet.\n\n# CHAPTER XX.\n\n“A child forsaken, waking suddenly, Whose gaze afeard on all things round doth rove, And seeth only that it cannot see The meeting eyes of love.”\n\nTwo hours later, Dorothea was seated in an inner room or boudoir of a handsome apartment in the Via Sistina.\n\nI am sorry to add that she was sobbing bitterly, with such abandonment to this relief of an oppressed heart as a woman habitually controlled by pride on her own account and thoughtfulness for others will sometimes allow herself when she feels securely alone. And Mr. Casaubon was certain to remain away for some time at the Vatican.\n\nYet Dorothea had no distinctly shapen grievance that she could state even to herself; and in the midst of her confused thought and passion, the mental act that was struggling forth into clearness was a self-accusing cry that her feeling of desolation was the fault of her own spiritual poverty. She had married the man of her choice, and with the advantage over most girls that she had contemplated her marriage chiefly as the beginning of new duties: from the very first she had thought of Mr. Casaubon as having a mind so much above her own, that he must often be claimed by studies which she could not entirely share; moreover, after the brief narrow experience of her girlhood she was beholding Rome, the city of visible history, where the past of a whole hemisphere seems moving in funeral procession with strange ancestral images and trophies gathered from afar.\n\nBut this stupendous fragmentariness heightened the dreamlike strangeness of her bridal life. Dorothea had now been five weeks in Rome, and in the kindly mornings when autumn and winter seemed to go hand in hand like a happy aged couple one of whom would presently survive in chiller loneliness, she had driven about at first with Mr. Casaubon, but of late chiefly with Tantripp and their experienced courier. She had been led through the best galleries, had been taken to the chief points of view, had been shown the grandest ruins and the most glorious churches, and she had ended by oftenest choosing to drive out to the Campagna where she could feel alone with the earth and sky, away-from the oppressive masquerade of ages, in which her own life too seemed to become a masque with enigmatical costumes.\n\nTo those who have looked at Rome with the quickening power of a knowledge which breathes a growing soul into all historic shapes, and traces out the suppressed transitions which unite all contrasts, Rome may still be the spiritual centre and interpreter of the world. But let them conceive one more historical contrast: the gigantic broken revelations of that Imperial and Papal city thrust abruptly on the notions of a girl who had been brought up in English and Swiss Puritanism, fed on meagre Protestant histories and on art chiefly of the hand-screen sort; a girl whose ardent nature turned all her small allowance of knowledge into principles, fusing her actions into their mould, and whose quick emotions gave the most abstract things the quality of a pleasure or a pain; a girl who had lately become a wife, and from the enthusiastic acceptance of untried duty found herself plunged in tumultuous preoccupation with her personal lot. The weight of unintelligible Rome might lie easily on bright nymphs to whom it formed a background for the brilliant picnic of Anglo-foreign society; but Dorothea had no such defence against deep impressions. Ruins and basilicas, palaces and colossi, set in the midst of a sordid present, where all that was living and warm-blooded seemed sunk in the deep degeneracy of a superstition divorced from reverence; the dimmer but yet eager Titanic life gazing and struggling on walls and ceilings; the long vistas of white forms whose marble eyes seemed to hold the monotonous light of an alien world: all this vast wreck of ambitious ideals, sensuous and spiritual, mixed confusedly with the signs of breathing forgetfulness and degradation, at first jarred her as with an electric shock, and then urged themselves on her with that ache belonging to a glut of confused ideas which check the flow of emotion. Forms both pale and glowing took possession of her young sense, and fixed themselves in her memory even when she was not thinking of them, preparing strange associations which remained through her after-years. Our moods are apt to bring with them images which succeed each other like the magic-lantern pictures of a doze; and in certain states of dull forlornness Dorothea all her life continued to see the vastness of St. Peter’s, the huge bronze canopy, the excited intention in the attitudes and garments of the prophets and evangelists in the mosaics above, and the red drapery which was being hung for Christmas spreading itself everywhere like a disease of the retina.\n\nNot that this inward amazement of Dorothea’s was anything very exceptional: many souls in their young nudity are tumbled out among incongruities and left to “find their feet” among them, while their elders go about their business. Nor can I suppose that when Mrs. Casaubon is discovered in a fit of weeping six weeks after her wedding, the situation will be regarded as tragic. Some discouragement, some faintness of heart at the new real future which replaces the imaginary, is not unusual, and we do not expect people to be deeply moved by what is not unusual. That element of tragedy which lies in the very fact of frequency, has not yet wrought itself into the coarse emotion of mankind; and perhaps our frames could hardly bear much of it. If we had a keen vision and feeling of all ordinary human life, it would be like hearing the grass grow and the squirrel’s heart beat, and we should die of that roar which lies on the other side of silence. As it is, the quickest of us walk about well wadded with stupidity.\n\nHowever, Dorothea was crying, and if she had been required to state the cause, she could only have done so in some such general words as I have already used: to have been driven to be more particular would have been like trying to give a history of the lights and shadows, for that new real future which was replacing the imaginary drew its material from the endless minutiae by which her view of Mr. Casaubon and her wifely relation, now that she was married to him, was gradually changing with the secret motion of a watch-hand from what it had been in her maiden dream. It was too early yet for her fully to recognize or at least admit the change, still more for her to have readjusted that devotedness which was so necessary a part of her mental life that she was almost sure sooner or later to recover it. Permanent rebellion, the disorder of a life without some loving reverent resolve, was not possible to her; but she was now in an interval when the very force of her nature heightened its confusion. In this way, the early months of marriage often are times of critical tumult—whether that of a shrimp-pool or of deeper waters—which afterwards subsides into cheerful peace.\n\nBut was not Mr. Casaubon just as learned as before? Had his forms of expression changed, or his sentiments become less laudable? Oh waywardness of womanhood! did his chronology fail him, or his ability to state not only a theory but the names of those who held it; or his provision for giving the heads of any subject on demand? And was not Rome the place in all the world to give free play to such accomplishments? Besides, had not Dorothea’s enthusiasm especially dwelt on the prospect of relieving the weight and perhaps the sadness with which great tasks lie on him who has to achieve them?— And that such weight pressed on Mr. Casaubon was only plainer than before.\n\nAll these are crushing questions; but whatever else remained the same, the light had changed, and you cannot find the pearly dawn at noonday. The fact is unalterable, that a fellow-mortal with whose nature you are acquainted solely through the brief entrances and exits of a few imaginative weeks called courtship, may, when seen in the continuity of married companionship, be disclosed as something better or worse than what you have preconceived, but will certainly not appear altogether the same. And it would be astonishing to find how soon the change is felt if we had no kindred changes to compare with it. To share lodgings with a brilliant dinner-companion, or to see your favorite politician in the Ministry, may bring about changes quite as rapid: in these cases too we begin by knowing little and believing much, and we sometimes end by inverting the quantities.\n\nStill, such comparisons might mislead, for no man was more incapable of flashy make-believe than Mr. Casaubon: he was as genuine a character as any ruminant animal, and he had not actively assisted in creating any illusions about himself. How was it that in the weeks since her marriage, Dorothea had not distinctly observed but felt with a stifling depression, that the large vistas and wide fresh air which she had dreamed of finding in her husband’s mind were replaced by anterooms and winding passages which seemed to lead nowhither? I suppose it was that in courtship everything is regarded as provisional and preliminary, and the smallest sample of virtue or accomplishment is taken to guarantee delightful stores which the broad leisure of marriage will reveal. But the door-sill of marriage once crossed, expectation is concentrated on the present. Having once embarked on your marital voyage, it is impossible not to be aware that you make no way and that the sea is not within sight—that, in fact, you are exploring an enclosed basin.\n\nIn their conversation before marriage, Mr. Casaubon had often dwelt on some explanation or questionable detail of which Dorothea did not see the bearing; but such imperfect coherence seemed due to the brokenness of their intercourse, and, supported by her faith in their future, she had listened with fervid patience to a recitation of possible arguments to be brought against Mr. Casaubon’s entirely new view of the Philistine god Dagon and other fish-deities, thinking that hereafter she should see this subject which touched him so nearly from the same high ground whence doubtless it had become so important to him. Again, the matter-of-course statement and tone of dismissal with which he treated what to her were the most stirring thoughts, was easily accounted for as belonging to the sense of haste and preoccupation in which she herself shared during their engagement. But now, since they had been in Rome, with all the depths of her emotion roused to tumultuous activity, and with life made a new problem by new elements, she had been becoming more and more aware, with a certain terror, that her mind was continually sliding into inward fits of anger and repulsion, or else into forlorn weariness. How far the judicious Hooker or any other hero of erudition would have been the same at Mr. Casaubon’s time of life, she had no means of knowing, so that he could not have the advantage of comparison; but her husband’s way of commenting on the strangely impressive objects around them had begun to affect her with a sort of mental shiver: he had perhaps the best intention of acquitting himself worthily, but only of acquitting himself. What was fresh to her mind was worn out to his; and such capacity of thought and feeling as had ever been stimulated in him by the general life of mankind had long shrunk to a sort of dried preparation, a lifeless embalmment of knowledge.\n\nWhen he said, “Does this interest you, Dorothea? Shall we stay a little longer? I am ready to stay if you wish it,”—it seemed to her as if going or staying were alike dreary. Or, “Should you like to go to the Farnesina, Dorothea? It contains celebrated frescos designed or painted by Raphael, which most persons think it worth while to visit.”\n\n“But do you care about them?” was always Dorothea’s question.\n\n“They are, I believe, highly esteemed. Some of them represent the fable of Cupid and Psyche, which is probably the romantic invention of a literary period, and cannot, I think, be reckoned as a genuine mythical product. But if you like these wall-paintings we can easily drive thither; and you will then, I think, have seen the chief works of Raphael, any of which it were a pity to omit in a visit to Rome. He is the painter who has been held to combine the most complete grace of form with sublimity of expression. Such at least I have gathered to be the opinion of cognoscenti.”\n\nThis kind of answer given in a measured official tone, as of a clergyman reading according to the rubric, did not help to justify the glories of the Eternal City, or to give her the hope that if she knew more about them the world would be joyously illuminated for her. There is hardly any contact more depressing to a young ardent creature than that of a mind in which years full of knowledge seem to have issued in a blank absence of interest or sympathy.\n\nOn other subjects indeed Mr. Casaubon showed a tenacity of occupation and an eagerness which are usually regarded as the effect of enthusiasm, and Dorothea was anxious to follow this spontaneous direction of his thoughts, instead of being made to feel that she dragged him away from it. But she was gradually ceasing to expect with her former delightful confidence that she should see any wide opening where she followed him. Poor Mr. Casaubon himself was lost among small closets and winding stairs, and in an agitated dimness about the Cabeiri, or in an exposure of other mythologists’ ill-considered parallels, easily lost sight of any purpose which had prompted him to these labors. With his taper stuck before him he forgot the absence of windows, and in bitter manuscript remarks on other men’s notions about the solar deities, he had become indifferent to the sunlight.\n\nThese characteristics, fixed and unchangeable as bone in Mr. Casaubon, might have remained longer unfelt by Dorothea if she had been encouraged to pour forth her girlish and womanly feeling—if he would have held her hands between his and listened with the delight of tenderness and understanding to all the little histories which made up her experience, and would have given her the same sort of intimacy in return, so that the past life of each could be included in their mutual knowledge and affection—or if she could have fed her affection with those childlike caresses which are the bent of every sweet woman, who has begun by showering kisses on the hard pate of her bald doll, creating a happy soul within that woodenness from the wealth of her own love. That was Dorothea’s bent. With all her yearning to know what was afar from her and to be widely benignant, she had ardor enough for what was near, to have kissed Mr. Casaubon’s coat-sleeve, or to have caressed his shoe-latchet, if he would have made any other sign of acceptance than pronouncing her, with his unfailing propriety, to be of a most affectionate and truly feminine nature, indicating at the same time by politely reaching a chair for her that he regarded these manifestations as rather crude and startling. Having made his clerical toilet with due care in the morning, he was prepared only for those amenities of life which were suited to the well-adjusted stiff cravat of the period, and to a mind weighted with unpublished matter.\n\nAnd by a sad contradiction Dorothea’s ideas and resolves seemed like melting ice floating and lost in the warm flood of which they had been but another form. She was humiliated to find herself a mere victim of feeling, as if she could know nothing except through that medium: all her strength was scattered in fits of agitation, of struggle, of despondency, and then again in visions of more complete renunciation, transforming all hard conditions into duty. Poor Dorothea! she was certainly troublesome—to herself chiefly; but this morning for the first time she had been troublesome to Mr. Casaubon.\n\nShe had begun, while they were taking coffee, with a determination to shake off what she inwardly called her selfishness, and turned a face all cheerful attention to her husband when he said, “My dear Dorothea, we must now think of all that is yet left undone, as a preliminary to our departure. I would fain have returned home earlier that we might have been at Lowick for the Christmas; but my inquiries here have been protracted beyond their anticipated period. I trust, however, that the time here has not been passed unpleasantly to you. Among the sights of Europe, that of Rome has ever been held one of the most striking and in some respects edifying. I well remember that I considered it an epoch in my life when I visited it for the first time; after the fall of Napoleon, an event which opened the Continent to travellers. Indeed I think it is one among several cities to which an extreme hyperbole has been applied—‘See Rome and die:’ but in your case I would propose an emendation and say, See Rome as a bride, and live henceforth as a happy wife.”\n\nMr. Casaubon pronounced this little speech with the most conscientious intention, blinking a little and swaying his head up and down, and concluding with a smile. He had not found marriage a rapturous state, but he had no idea of being anything else than an irreproachable husband, who would make a charming young woman as happy as she deserved to be.\n\n“I hope you are thoroughly satisfied with our stay—I mean, with the result so far as your studies are concerned,” said Dorothea, trying to keep her mind fixed on what most affected her husband.\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. Casaubon, with that peculiar pitch of voice which makes the word half a negative. “I have been led farther than I had foreseen, and various subjects for annotation have presented themselves which, though I have no direct need of them, I could not pretermit. The task, notwithstanding the assistance of my amanuensis, has been a somewhat laborious one, but your society has happily prevented me from that too continuous prosecution of thought beyond the hours of study which has been the snare of my solitary life.”\n\n“I am very glad that my presence has made any difference to you,” said Dorothea, who had a vivid memory of evenings in which she had supposed that Mr. Casaubon’s mind had gone too deep during the day to be able to get to the surface again. I fear there was a little temper in her reply. “I hope when we get to Lowick, I shall be more useful to you, and be able to enter a little more into what interests you.”\n\n“Doubtless, my dear,” said Mr. Casaubon, with a slight bow. “The notes I have here made will want sifting, and you can, if you please, extract them under my direction.”\n\n“And all your notes,” said Dorothea, whose heart had already burned within her on this subject, so that now she could not help speaking with her tongue. “All those rows of volumes—will you not now do what you used to speak of?—will you not make up your mind what part of them you will use, and begin to write the book which will make your vast knowledge useful to the world? I will write to your dictation, or I will copy and extract what you tell me: I can be of no other use.” Dorothea, in a most unaccountable, darkly feminine manner, ended with a slight sob and eyes full of tears.\n\nThe excessive feeling manifested would alone have been highly disturbing to Mr. Casaubon, but there were other reasons why Dorothea’s words were among the most cutting and irritating to him that she could have been impelled to use. She was as blind to his inward troubles as he to hers: she had not yet learned those hidden conflicts in her husband which claim our pity. She had not yet listened patiently to his heartbeats, but only felt that her own was beating violently. In Mr. Casaubon’s ear, Dorothea’s voice gave loud emphatic iteration to those muffled suggestions of consciousness which it was possible to explain as mere fancy, the illusion of exaggerated sensitiveness: always when such suggestions are unmistakably repeated from without, they are resisted as cruel and unjust. We are angered even by the full acceptance of our humiliating confessions—how much more by hearing in hard distinct syllables from the lips of a near observer, those confused murmurs which we try to call morbid, and strive against as if they were the oncoming of numbness! And this cruel outward accuser was there in the shape of a wife—nay, of a young bride, who, instead of observing his abundant pen-scratches and amplitude of paper with the uncritical awe of an elegant-minded canary-bird, seemed to present herself as a spy watching everything with a malign power of inference. Here, towards this particular point of the compass, Mr. Casaubon had a sensitiveness to match Dorothea’s, and an equal quickness to imagine more than the fact. He had formerly observed with approbation her capacity for worshipping the right object; he now foresaw with sudden terror that this capacity might be replaced by presumption, this worship by the most exasperating of all criticism,—that which sees vaguely a great many fine ends, and has not the least notion what it costs to reach them.\n\nFor the first time since Dorothea had known him, Mr. Casaubon’s face had a quick angry flush upon it.\n\n“My love,” he said, with irritation reined in by propriety, “you may rely upon me for knowing the times and the seasons, adapted to the different stages of a work which is not to be measured by the facile conjectures of ignorant onlookers. It had been easy for me to gain a temporary effect by a mirage of baseless opinion; but it is ever the trial of the scrupulous explorer to be saluted with the impatient scorn of chatterers who attempt only the smallest achievements, being indeed equipped for no other. And it were well if all such could be admonished to discriminate judgments of which the true subject-matter lies entirely beyond their reach, from those of which the elements may be compassed by a narrow and superficial survey.”\n\nThis speech was delivered with an energy and readiness quite unusual with Mr. Casaubon. It was not indeed entirely an improvisation, but had taken shape in inward colloquy, and rushed out like the round grains from a fruit when sudden heat cracks it. Dorothea was not only his wife: she was a personification of that shallow world which surrounds the appreciated or desponding author.\n\nDorothea was indignant in her turn. Had she not been repressing everything in herself except the desire to enter into some fellowship with her husband’s chief interests?\n\n“My judgment was a very superficial one—such as I am capable of forming,” she answered, with a prompt resentment, that needed no rehearsal. “You showed me the rows of notebooks—you have often spoken of them—you have often said that they wanted digesting. But I never heard you speak of the writing that is to be published. Those were very simple facts, and my judgment went no farther. I only begged you to let me be of some good to you.”\n\nDorothea rose to leave the table and Mr. Casaubon made no reply, taking up a letter which lay beside him as if to reperuse it. Both were shocked at their mutual situation—that each should have betrayed anger towards the other. If they had been at home, settled at Lowick in ordinary life among their neighbors, the clash would have been less embarrassing: but on a wedding journey, the express object of which is to isolate two people on the ground that they are all the world to each other, the sense of disagreement is, to say the least, confounding and stultifying. To have changed your longitude extensively and placed yourselves in a moral solitude in order to have small explosions, to find conversation difficult and to hand a glass of water without looking, can hardly be regarded as satisfactory fulfilment even to the toughest minds. To Dorothea’s inexperienced sensitiveness, it seemed like a catastrophe, changing all prospects; and to Mr. Casaubon it was a new pain, he never having been on a wedding journey before, or found himself in that close union which was more of a subjection than he had been able to imagine, since this charming young bride not only obliged him to much consideration on her behalf (which he had sedulously given), but turned out to be capable of agitating him cruelly just where he most needed soothing. Instead of getting a soft fence against the cold, shadowy, unapplausive audience of his life, had he only given it a more substantial presence?\n\nNeither of them felt it possible to speak again at present. To have reversed a previous arrangement and declined to go out would have been a show of persistent anger which Dorothea’s conscience shrank from, seeing that she already began to feel herself guilty. However just her indignation might be, her ideal was not to claim justice, but to give tenderness. So when the carriage came to the door, she drove with Mr. Casaubon to the Vatican, walked with him through the stony avenue of inscriptions, and when she parted with him at the entrance to the Library, went on through the Museum out of mere listlessness as to what was around her. She had not spirit to turn round and say that she would drive anywhere. It was when Mr. Casaubon was quitting her that Naumann had first seen her, and he had entered the long gallery of sculpture at the same time with her; but here Naumann had to await Ladislaw with whom he was to settle a bet of champagne about an enigmatical mediaeval-looking figure there. After they had examined the figure, and had walked on finishing their dispute, they had parted, Ladislaw lingering behind while Naumann had gone into the Hall of Statues where he again saw Dorothea, and saw her in that brooding abstraction which made her pose remarkable. She did not really see the streak of sunlight on the floor more than she saw the statues: she was inwardly seeing the light of years to come in her own home and over the English fields and elms and hedge-bordered highroads; and feeling that the way in which they might be filled with joyful devotedness was not so clear to her as it had been. But in Dorothea’s mind there was a current into which all thought and feeling were apt sooner or later to flow—the reaching forward of the whole consciousness towards the fullest truth, the least partial good. There was clearly something better than anger and despondency.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI.\n\n“Hire facounde eke full womanly and plain, No contrefeted termes had she To semen wise.” —CHAUCER.\n\nIt was in that way Dorothea came to be sobbing as soon as she was securely alone. But she was presently roused by a knock at the door, which made her hastily dry her eyes before saying, “Come in.” Tantripp had brought a card, and said that there was a gentleman waiting in the lobby. The courier had told him that only Mrs. Casaubon was at home, but he said he was a relation of Mr. Casaubon’s: would she see him?\n\n“Yes,” said Dorothea, without pause; “show him into the salon.” Her chief impressions about young Ladislaw were that when she had seen him at Lowick she had been made aware of Mr. Casaubon’s generosity towards him, and also that she had been interested in his own hesitation about his career. She was alive to anything that gave her an opportunity for active sympathy, and at this moment it seemed as if the visit had come to shake her out of her self-absorbed discontent—to remind her of her husband’s goodness, and make her feel that she had now the right to be his helpmate in all kind deeds. She waited a minute or two, but when she passed into the next room there were just signs enough that she had been crying to make her open face look more youthful and appealing than usual. She met Ladislaw with that exquisite smile of good-will which is unmixed with vanity, and held out her hand to him. He was the elder by several years, but at that moment he looked much the younger, for his transparent complexion flushed suddenly, and he spoke with a shyness extremely unlike the ready indifference of his manner with his male companion, while Dorothea became all the calmer with a wondering desire to put him at ease.\n\n“I was not aware that you and Mr. Casaubon were in Rome, until this morning, when I saw you in the Vatican Museum,” he said. “I knew you at once—but—I mean, that I concluded Mr. Casaubon’s address would be found at the Poste Restante, and I was anxious to pay my respects to him and you as early as possible.”\n\n“Pray sit down. He is not here now, but he will be glad to hear of you, I am sure,” said Dorothea, seating herself unthinkingly between the fire and the light of the tall window, and pointing to a chair opposite, with the quietude of a benignant matron. The signs of girlish sorrow in her face were only the more striking. “Mr. Casaubon is much engaged; but you will leave your address—will you not?—and he will write to you.”\n\n“You are very good,” said Ladislaw, beginning to lose his diffidence in the interest with which he was observing the signs of weeping which had altered her face. “My address is on my card. But if you will allow me I will call again to-morrow at an hour when Mr. Casaubon is likely to be at home.”\n\n“He goes to read in the Library of the Vatican every day, and you can hardly see him except by an appointment. Especially now. We are about to leave Rome, and he is very busy. He is usually away almost from breakfast till dinner. But I am sure he will wish you to dine with us.”\n\nWill Ladislaw was struck mute for a few moments. He had never been fond of Mr. Casaubon, and if it had not been for the sense of obligation, would have laughed at him as a Bat of erudition. But the idea of this dried-up pedant, this elaborator of small explanations about as important as the surplus stock of false antiquities kept in a vendor’s back chamber, having first got this adorable young creature to marry him, and then passing his honeymoon away from her, groping after his mouldy futilities (Will was given to hyperbole)—this sudden picture stirred him with a sort of comic disgust: he was divided between the impulse to laugh aloud and the equally unseasonable impulse to burst into scornful invective.\n\nFor an instant he felt that the struggle was causing a queer contortion of his mobile features, but with a good effort he resolved it into nothing more offensive than a merry smile.\n\nDorothea wondered; but the smile was irresistible, and shone back from her face too. Will Ladislaw’s smile was delightful, unless you were angry with him beforehand: it was a gush of inward light illuminating the transparent skin as well as the eyes, and playing about every curve and line as if some Ariel were touching them with a new charm, and banishing forever the traces of moodiness. The reflection of that smile could not but have a little merriment in it too, even under dark eyelashes still moist, as Dorothea said inquiringly, “Something amuses you?”\n\n“Yes,” said Will, quick in finding resources. “I am thinking of the sort of figure I cut the first time I saw you, when you annihilated my poor sketch with your criticism.”\n\n“My criticism?” said Dorothea, wondering still more. “Surely not. I always feel particularly ignorant about painting.”\n\n“I suspected you of knowing so much, that you knew how to say just what was most cutting. You said—I dare say you don’t remember it as I do—that the relation of my sketch to nature was quite hidden from you. At least, you implied that.” Will could laugh now as well as smile.\n\n“That was really my ignorance,” said Dorothea, admiring Will’s good-humor. “I must have said so only because I never could see any beauty in the pictures which my uncle told me all judges thought very fine. And I have gone about with just the same ignorance in Rome. There are comparatively few paintings that I can really enjoy. At first when I enter a room where the walls are covered with frescos, or with rare pictures, I feel a kind of awe—like a child present at great ceremonies where there are grand robes and processions; I feel myself in the presence of some higher life than my own. But when I begin to examine the pictures one by one the life goes out of them, or else is something violent and strange to me. It must be my own dulness. I am seeing so much all at once, and not understanding half of it. That always makes one feel stupid. It is painful to be told that anything is very fine and not be able to feel that it is fine—something like being blind, while people talk of the sky.”\n\n“Oh, there is a great deal in the feeling for art which must be acquired,” said Will. (It was impossible now to doubt the directness of Dorothea’s confession.) “Art is an old language with a great many artificial affected styles, and sometimes the chief pleasure one gets out of knowing them is the mere sense of knowing. I enjoy the art of all sorts here immensely; but I suppose if I could pick my enjoyment to pieces I should find it made up of many different threads. There is something in daubing a little one’s self, and having an idea of the process.”\n\n“You mean perhaps to be a painter?” said Dorothea, with a new direction of interest. “You mean to make painting your profession? Mr. Casaubon will like to hear that you have chosen a profession.”\n\n“No, oh no,” said Will, with some coldness. “I have quite made up my mind against it. It is too one-sided a life. I have been seeing a great deal of the German artists here: I travelled from Frankfort with one of them. Some are fine, even brilliant fellows—but I should not like to get into their way of looking at the world entirely from the studio point of view.”\n\n“That I can understand,” said Dorothea, cordially. “And in Rome it seems as if there were so many things which are more wanted in the world than pictures. But if you have a genius for painting, would it not be right to take that as a guide? Perhaps you might do better things than these—or different, so that there might not be so many pictures almost all alike in the same place.”\n\nThere was no mistaking this simplicity, and Will was won by it into frankness. “A man must have a very rare genius to make changes of that sort. I am afraid mine would not carry me even to the pitch of doing well what has been done already, at least not so well as to make it worth while. And I should never succeed in anything by dint of drudgery. If things don’t come easily to me I never get them.”\n\n“I have heard Mr. Casaubon say that he regrets your want of patience,” said Dorothea, gently. She was rather shocked at this mode of taking all life as a holiday.\n\n“Yes, I know Mr. Casaubon’s opinion. He and I differ.”\n\nThe slight streak of contempt in this hasty reply offended Dorothea. She was all the more susceptible about Mr. Casaubon because of her morning’s trouble.\n\n“Certainly you differ,” she said, rather proudly. “I did not think of comparing you: such power of persevering devoted labor as Mr. Casaubon’s is not common.”\n\nWill saw that she was offended, but this only gave an additional impulse to the new irritation of his latent dislike towards Mr. Casaubon. It was too intolerable that Dorothea should be worshipping this husband: such weakness in a woman is pleasant to no man but the husband in question. Mortals are easily tempted to pinch the life out of their neighbor’s buzzing glory, and think that such killing is no murder.\n\n“No, indeed,” he answered, promptly. “And therefore it is a pity that it should be thrown away, as so much English scholarship is, for want of knowing what is being done by the rest of the world. If Mr. Casaubon read German he would save himself a great deal of trouble.”\n\n“I do not understand you,” said Dorothea, startled and anxious.\n\n“I merely mean,” said Will, in an offhand way, “that the Germans have taken the lead in historical inquiries, and they laugh at results which are got by groping about in woods with a pocket-compass while they have made good roads. When I was with Mr. Casaubon I saw that he deafened himself in that direction: it was almost against his will that he read a Latin treatise written by a German. I was very sorry.”\n\nWill only thought of giving a good pinch that would annihilate that vaunted laboriousness, and was unable to imagine the mode in which Dorothea would be wounded. Young Mr. Ladislaw was not at all deep himself in German writers; but very little achievement is required in order to pity another man’s shortcomings.\n\nPoor Dorothea felt a pang at the thought that the labor of her husband’s life might be void, which left her no energy to spare for the question whether this young relative who was so much obliged to him ought not to have repressed his observation. She did not even speak, but sat looking at her hands, absorbed in the piteousness of that thought.\n\nWill, however, having given that annihilating pinch, was rather ashamed, imagining from Dorothea’s silence that he had offended her still more; and having also a conscience about plucking the tail-feathers from a benefactor.\n\n“I regretted it especially,” he resumed, taking the usual course from detraction to insincere eulogy, “because of my gratitude and respect towards my cousin. It would not signify so much in a man whose talents and character were less distinguished.”\n\nDorothea raised her eyes, brighter than usual with excited feeling, and said in her saddest recitative, “How I wish I had learned German when I was at Lausanne! There were plenty of German teachers. But now I can be of no use.”\n\nThere was a new light, but still a mysterious light, for Will in Dorothea’s last words. The question how she had come to accept Mr. Casaubon—which he had dismissed when he first saw her by saying that she must be disagreeable in spite of appearances—was not now to be answered on any such short and easy method. Whatever else she might be, she was not disagreeable. She was not coldly clever and indirectly satirical, but adorably simple and full of feeling. She was an angel beguiled. It would be a unique delight to wait and watch for the melodious fragments in which her heart and soul came forth so directly and ingenuously. The Aeolian harp again came into his mind.\n\nShe must have made some original romance for herself in this marriage. And if Mr. Casaubon had been a dragon who had carried her off to his lair with his talons simply and without legal forms, it would have been an unavoidable feat of heroism to release her and fall at her feet. But he was something more unmanageable than a dragon: he was a benefactor with collective society at his back, and he was at that moment entering the room in all the unimpeachable correctness of his demeanor, while Dorothea was looking animated with a newly roused alarm and regret, and Will was looking animated with his admiring speculation about her feelings.\n\nMr. Casaubon felt a surprise which was quite unmixed with pleasure, but he did not swerve from his usual politeness of greeting, when Will rose and explained his presence. Mr. Casaubon was less happy than usual, and this perhaps made him look all the dimmer and more faded; else, the effect might easily have been produced by the contrast of his young cousin’s appearance. The first impression on seeing Will was one of sunny brightness, which added to the uncertainty of his changing expression. Surely, his very features changed their form, his jaw looked sometimes large and sometimes small; and the little ripple in his nose was a preparation for metamorphosis. When he turned his head quickly his hair seemed to shake out light, and some persons thought they saw decided genius in this coruscation. Mr. Casaubon, on the contrary, stood rayless.\n\nAs Dorothea’s eyes were turned anxiously on her husband she was perhaps not insensible to the contrast, but it was only mingled with other causes in making her more conscious of that new alarm on his behalf which was the first stirring of a pitying tenderness fed by the realities of his lot and not by her own dreams. Yet it was a source of greater freedom to her that Will was there; his young equality was agreeable, and also perhaps his openness to conviction. She felt an immense need of some one to speak to, and she had never before seen any one who seemed so quick and pliable, so likely to understand everything.\n\nMr. Casaubon gravely hoped that Will was passing his time profitably as well as pleasantly in Rome—had thought his intention was to remain in South Germany—but begged him to come and dine to-morrow, when he could converse more at large: at present he was somewhat weary. Ladislaw understood, and accepting the invitation immediately took his leave.\n\nDorothea’s eyes followed her husband anxiously, while he sank down wearily at the end of a sofa, and resting his elbow supported his head and looked on the floor. A little flushed, and with bright eyes, she seated herself beside him, and said—\n\n“Forgive me for speaking so hastily to you this morning. I was wrong. I fear I hurt you and made the day more burdensome.”\n\n“I am glad that you feel that, my dear,” said Mr. Casaubon. He spoke quietly and bowed his head a little, but there was still an uneasy feeling in his eyes as he looked at her.\n\n“But you do forgive me?” said Dorothea, with a quick sob. In her need for some manifestation of feeling she was ready to exaggerate her own fault. Would not love see returning penitence afar off, and fall on its neck and kiss it?\n\n“My dear Dorothea—‘who with repentance is not satisfied, is not of heaven nor earth:’—you do not think me worthy to be banished by that severe sentence,” said Mr. Casaubon, exerting himself to make a strong statement, and also to smile faintly.\n\nDorothea was silent, but a tear which had come up with the sob would insist on falling.\n\n“You are excited, my dear. And I also am feeling some unpleasant consequences of too much mental disturbance,” said Mr. Casaubon. In fact, he had it in his thought to tell her that she ought not to have received young Ladislaw in his absence: but he abstained, partly from the sense that it would be ungracious to bring a new complaint in the moment of her penitent acknowledgment, partly because he wanted to avoid further agitation of himself by speech, and partly because he was too proud to betray that jealousy of disposition which was not so exhausted on his scholarly compeers that there was none to spare in other directions. There is a sort of jealousy which needs very little fire: it is hardly a passion, but a blight bred in the cloudy, damp despondency of uneasy egoism.\n\n“I think it is time for us to dress,” he added, looking at his watch. They both rose, and there was never any further allusion between them to what had passed on this day.\n\nBut Dorothea remembered it to the last with the vividness with which we all remember epochs in our experience when some dear expectation dies, or some new motive is born. Today she had begun to see that she had been under a wild illusion in expecting a response to her feeling from Mr. Casaubon, and she had felt the waking of a presentiment that there might be a sad consciousness in his life which made as great a need on his side as on her own.\n\nWe are all of us born in moral stupidity, taking the world as an udder to feed our supreme selves: Dorothea had early begun to emerge from that stupidity, but yet it had been easier to her to imagine how she would devote herself to Mr. Casaubon, and become wise and strong in his strength and wisdom, than to conceive with that distinctness which is no longer reflection but feeling—an idea wrought back to the directness of sense, like the solidity of objects—that he had an equivalent centre of self, whence the lights and shadows must always fall with a certain difference.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII.\n\n“Nous câusames longtemps; elle était simple et bonne. Ne sachant pas le mal, elle faisait le bien; Des richesses du coeur elle me fit l’aumône, Et tout en écoutant comme le coeur se donne, Sans oser y penser je lui donnai le mien; Elle emporta ma vie, et n’en sut jamais rien.” —ALFRED DE MUSSET.\n\nWill Ladislaw was delightfully agreeable at dinner the next day, and gave no opportunity for Mr. Casaubon to show disapprobation. On the contrary it seemed to Dorothea that Will had a happier way of drawing her husband into conversation and of deferentially listening to him than she had ever observed in any one before. To be sure, the listeners about Tipton were not highly gifted! Will talked a good deal himself, but what he said was thrown in with such rapidity, and with such an unimportant air of saying something by the way, that it seemed a gay little chime after the great bell. If Will was not always perfect, this was certainly one of his good days. He described touches of incident among the poor people in Rome, only to be seen by one who could move about freely; he found himself in agreement with Mr. Casaubon as to the unsound opinions of Middleton concerning the relations of Judaism and Catholicism; and passed easily to a half-enthusiastic half-playful picture of the enjoyment he got out of the very miscellaneousness of Rome, which made the mind flexible with constant comparison, and saved you from seeing the world’s ages as a set of box-like partitions without vital connection. Mr. Casaubon’s studies, Will observed, had always been of too broad a kind for that, and he had perhaps never felt any such sudden effect, but for himself he confessed that Rome had given him quite a new sense of history as a whole: the fragments stimulated his imagination and made him constructive. Then occasionally, but not too often, he appealed to Dorothea, and discussed what she said, as if her sentiment were an item to be considered in the final judgment even of the Madonna di Foligno or the Laocoon. A sense of contributing to form the world’s opinion makes conversation particularly cheerful; and Mr. Casaubon too was not without his pride in his young wife, who spoke better than most women, as indeed he had perceived in choosing her.\n\nSince things were going on so pleasantly, Mr. Casaubon’s statement that his labors in the Library would be suspended for a couple of days, and that after a brief renewal he should have no further reason for staying in Rome, encouraged Will to urge that Mrs. Casaubon should not go away without seeing a studio or two. Would not Mr. Casaubon take her? That sort of thing ought not to be missed: it was quite special: it was a form of life that grew like a small fresh vegetation with its population of insects on huge fossils. Will would be happy to conduct them—not to anything wearisome, only to a few examples.\n\nMr. Casaubon, seeing Dorothea look earnestly towards him, could not but ask her if she would be interested in such visits: he was now at her service during the whole day; and it was agreed that Will should come on the morrow and drive with them.\n\nWill could not omit Thorwaldsen, a living celebrity about whom even Mr. Casaubon inquired, but before the day was far advanced he led the way to the studio of his friend Adolf Naumann, whom he mentioned as one of the chief renovators of Christian art, one of those who had not only revived but expanded that grand conception of supreme events as mysteries at which the successive ages were spectators, and in relation to which the great souls of all periods became as it were contemporaries. Will added that he had made himself Naumann’s pupil for the nonce.\n\n“I have been making some oil-sketches under him,” said Will. “I hate copying. I must put something of my own in. Naumann has been painting the Saints drawing the Car of the Church, and I have been making a sketch of Marlowe’s Tamburlaine Driving the Conquered Kings in his Chariot. I am not so ecclesiastical as Naumann, and I sometimes twit him with his excess of meaning. But this time I mean to outdo him in breadth of intention. I take Tamburlaine in his chariot for the tremendous course of the world’s physical history lashing on the harnessed dynasties. In my opinion, that is a good mythical interpretation.” Will here looked at Mr. Casaubon, who received this offhand treatment of symbolism very uneasily, and bowed with a neutral air.\n\n“The sketch must be very grand, if it conveys so much,” said Dorothea. “I should need some explanation even of the meaning you give. Do you intend Tamburlaine to represent earthquakes and volcanoes?”\n“Oh yes,” said Will, laughing, “and migrations of races and clearings of forests—and America and the steam-engine. Everything you can imagine!”\n\n“What a difficult kind of shorthand!” said Dorothea, smiling towards her husband. “It would require all your knowledge to be able to read it.”\n\nMr. Casaubon blinked furtively at Will. He had a suspicion that he was being laughed at. But it was not possible to include Dorothea in the suspicion.\n\nThey found Naumann painting industriously, but no model was present; his pictures were advantageously arranged, and his own plain vivacious person set off by a dove-colored blouse and a maroon velvet cap, so that everything was as fortunate as if he had expected the beautiful young English lady exactly at that time.\n\nThe painter in his confident English gave little dissertations on his finished and unfinished subjects, seeming to observe Mr. Casaubon as much as he did Dorothea. Will burst in here and there with ardent words of praise, marking out particular merits in his friend’s work; and Dorothea felt that she was getting quite new notions as to the significance of Madonnas seated under inexplicable canopied thrones with the simple country as a background, and of saints with architectural models in their hands, or knives accidentally wedged in their skulls. Some things which had seemed monstrous to her were gathering intelligibility and even a natural meaning: but all this was apparently a branch of knowledge in which Mr. Casaubon had not interested himself.\n\n“I think I would rather feel that painting is beautiful than have to read it as an enigma; but I should learn to understand these pictures sooner than yours with the very wide meaning,” said Dorothea, speaking to Will.\n\n“Don’t speak of my painting before Naumann,” said Will. “He will tell you, it is all pfuscherei, which is his most opprobrious word!”\n\n“Is that true?” said Dorothea, turning her sincere eyes on Naumann, who made a slight grimace and said—\n\n“Oh, he does not mean it seriously with painting. His walk must be belles-lettres. That is wi-ide.”\n\nNaumann’s pronunciation of the vowel seemed to stretch the word satirically. Will did not half like it, but managed to laugh: and Mr. Casaubon, while he felt some disgust at the artist’s German accent, began to entertain a little respect for his judicious severity.\n\nThe respect was not diminished when Naumann, after drawing Will aside for a moment and looking, first at a large canvas, then at Mr. Casaubon, came forward again and said—\n\n“My friend Ladislaw thinks you will pardon me, sir, if I say that a sketch of your head would be invaluable to me for the St. Thomas Aquinas in my picture there. It is too much to ask; but I so seldom see just what I want—the idealistic in the real.”\n\n“You astonish me greatly, sir,” said Mr. Casaubon, his looks improved with a glow of delight; “but if my poor physiognomy, which I have been accustomed to regard as of the commonest order, can be of any use to you in furnishing some traits for the angelical doctor, I shall feel honored. That is to say, if the operation will not be a lengthy one; and if Mrs. Casaubon will not object to the delay.”\n\nAs for Dorothea, nothing could have pleased her more, unless it had been a miraculous voice pronouncing Mr. Casaubon the wisest and worthiest among the sons of men. In that case her tottering faith would have become firm again.\n\nNaumann’s apparatus was at hand in wonderful completeness, and the sketch went on at once as well as the conversation. Dorothea sat down and subsided into calm silence, feeling happier than she had done for a long while before. Every one about her seemed good, and she said to herself that Rome, if she had only been less ignorant, would have been full of beauty: its sadness would have been winged with hope. No nature could be less suspicious than hers: when she was a child she believed in the gratitude of wasps and the honorable susceptibility of sparrows, and was proportionately indignant when their baseness was made manifest.\n\nThe adroit artist was asking Mr. Casaubon questions about English polities, which brought long answers, and, Will meanwhile had perched himself on some steps in the background overlooking all.\n\nPresently Naumann said—“Now if I could lay this by for half an hour and take it up again—come and look, Ladislaw—I think it is perfect so far.”\n\nWill vented those adjuring interjections which imply that admiration is too strong for syntax; and Naumann said in a tone of piteous regret—\n\n“Ah—now—if I could but have had more—but you have other engagements—I could not ask it—or even to come again to-morrow.”\n\n“Oh, let us stay!” said Dorothea. “We have nothing to do to-day except go about, have we?” she added, looking entreatingly at Mr. Casaubon. “It would be a pity not to make the head as good as possible.”\n\n“I am at your service, sir, in the matter,” said Mr. Casaubon, with polite condescension. “Having given up the interior of my head to idleness, it is as well that the exterior should work in this way.”\n\n“You are unspeakably good—now I am happy!” said Naumann, and then went on in German to Will, pointing here and there to the sketch as if he were considering that. Putting it aside for a moment, he looked round vaguely, as if seeking some occupation for his visitors, and afterwards turning to Mr. Casaubon, said—\n\n“Perhaps the beautiful bride, the gracious lady, would not be unwilling to let me fill up the time by trying to make a slight sketch of her—not, of course, as you see, for that picture—only as a single study.”\n\nMr. Casaubon, bowing, doubted not that Mrs. Casaubon would oblige him, and Dorothea said, at once, “Where shall I put myself?”\n\nNaumann was all apologies in asking her to stand, and allow him to adjust her attitude, to which she submitted without any of the affected airs and laughs frequently thought necessary on such occasions, when the painter said, “It is as Santa Clara that I want you to stand—leaning so, with your cheek against your hand—so—looking at that stool, please, so!”\n\nWill was divided between the inclination to fall at the Saint’s feet and kiss her robe, and the temptation to knock Naumann down while he was adjusting her arm. All this was impudence and desecration, and he repented that he had brought her.\n\nThe artist was diligent, and Will recovering himself moved about and occupied Mr. Casaubon as ingeniously as he could; but he did not in the end prevent the time from seeming long to that gentleman, as was clear from his expressing a fear that Mrs. Casaubon would be tired. Naumann took the hint and said—\n\n“Now, sir, if you can oblige me again; I will release the lady-wife.”\n\nSo Mr. Casaubon’s patience held out further, and when after all it turned out that the head of Saint Thomas Aquinas would be more perfect if another sitting could be had, it was granted for the morrow. On the morrow Santa Clara too was retouched more than once. The result of all was so far from displeasing to Mr. Casaubon, that he arranged for the purchase of the picture in which Saint Thomas Aquinas sat among the doctors of the Church in a disputation too abstract to be represented, but listened to with more or less attention by an audience above. The Santa Clara, which was spoken of in the second place, Naumann declared himself to be dissatisfied with—he could not, in conscience, engage to make a worthy picture of it; so about the Santa Clara the arrangement was conditional.\n\nI will not dwell on Naumann’s jokes at the expense of Mr. Casaubon that evening, or on his dithyrambs about Dorothea’s charm, in all which Will joined, but with a difference. No sooner did Naumann mention any detail of Dorothea’s beauty, than Will got exasperated at his presumption: there was grossness in his choice of the most ordinary words, and what business had he to talk of her lips? She was not a woman to be spoken of as other women were. Will could not say just what he thought, but he became irritable. And yet, when after some resistance he had consented to take the Casaubons to his friend’s studio, he had been allured by the gratification of his pride in being the person who could grant Naumann such an opportunity of studying her loveliness—or rather her divineness, for the ordinary phrases which might apply to mere bodily prettiness were not applicable to her. (Certainly all Tipton and its neighborhood, as well as Dorothea herself, would have been surprised at her beauty being made so much of. In that part of the world Miss Brooke had been only a “fine young woman.”)\n\n“Oblige me by letting the subject drop, Naumann. Mrs. Casaubon is not to be talked of as if she were a model,” said Will. Naumann stared at him.\n\n“Schön! I will talk of my Aquinas. The head is not a bad type, after all. I dare say the great scholastic himself would have been flattered to have his portrait asked for. Nothing like these starchy doctors for vanity! It was as I thought: he cared much less for her portrait than his own.”\n\n“He’s a cursed white-blooded pedantic coxcomb,” said Will, with gnashing impetuosity. His obligations to Mr. Casaubon were not known to his hearer, but Will himself was thinking of them, and wishing that he could discharge them all by a check.\n\nNaumann gave a shrug and said, “It is good they go away soon, my dear. They are spoiling your fine temper.”\n\nAll Will’s hope and contrivance were now concentrated on seeing Dorothea when she was alone. He only wanted her to take more emphatic notice of him; he only wanted to be something more special in her remembrance than he could yet believe himself likely to be. He was rather impatient under that open ardent good-will, which he saw was her usual state of feeling. The remote worship of a woman throned out of their reach plays a great part in men’s lives, but in most cases the worshipper longs for some queenly recognition, some approving sign by which his soul’s sovereign may cheer him without descending from her high place. That was precisely what Will wanted. But there were plenty of contradictions in his imaginative demands. It was beautiful to see how Dorothea’s eyes turned with wifely anxiety and beseeching to Mr. Casaubon: she would have lost some of her halo if she had been without that duteous preoccupation; and yet at the next moment the husband’s sandy absorption of such nectar was too intolerable; and Will’s longing to say damaging things about him was perhaps not the less tormenting because he felt the strongest reasons for restraining it.\n\nWill had not been invited to dine the next day. Hence he persuaded himself that he was bound to call, and that the only eligible time was the middle of the day, when Mr. Casaubon would not be at home.\n\nDorothea, who had not been made aware that her former reception of Will had displeased her husband, had no hesitation about seeing him, especially as he might be come to pay a farewell visit. When he entered she was looking at some cameos which she had been buying for Celia. She greeted Will as if his visit were quite a matter of course, and said at once, having a cameo bracelet in her hand—\n\n“I am so glad you are come. Perhaps you understand all about cameos, and can tell me if these are really good. I wished to have you with us in choosing them, but Mr. Casaubon objected: he thought there was not time. He will finish his work to-morrow, and we shall go away in three days. I have been uneasy about these cameos. Pray sit down and look at them.”\n\n“I am not particularly knowing, but there can be no great mistake about these little Homeric bits: they are exquisitely neat. And the color is fine: it will just suit you.”\n\n“Oh, they are for my sister, who has quite a different complexion. You saw her with me at Lowick: she is light-haired and very pretty—at least I think so. We were never so long away from each other in our lives before. She is a great pet and never was naughty in her life. I found out before I came away that she wanted me to buy her some cameos, and I should be sorry for them not to be good—after their kind.” Dorothea added the last words with a smile.\n\n“You seem not to care about cameos,” said Will, seating himself at some distance from her, and observing her while she closed the cases.\n\n“No, frankly, I don’t think them a great object in life,” said Dorothea.\n\n“I fear you are a heretic about art generally. How is that? I should have expected you to be very sensitive to the beautiful everywhere.”\n\n“I suppose I am dull about many things,” said Dorothea, simply. “I should like to make life beautiful—I mean everybody’s life. And then all this immense expense of art, that seems somehow to lie outside life and make it no better for the world, pains one. It spoils my enjoyment of anything when I am made to think that most people are shut out from it.”\n\n“I call that the fanaticism of sympathy,” said Will, impetuously. “You might say the same of landscape, of poetry, of all refinement. If you carried it out you ought to be miserable in your own goodness, and turn evil that you might have no advantage over others. The best piety is to enjoy—when you can. You are doing the most then to save the earth’s character as an agreeable planet. And enjoyment radiates. It is of no use to try and take care of all the world; that is being taken care of when you feel delight—in art or in anything else. Would you turn all the youth of the world into a tragic chorus, wailing and moralizing over misery? I suspect that you have some false belief in the virtues of misery, and want to make your life a martyrdom.” Will had gone further than he intended, and checked himself. But Dorothea’s thought was not taking just the same direction as his own, and she answered without any special emotion—\n\n“Indeed you mistake me. I am not a sad, melancholy creature. I am never unhappy long together. I am angry and naughty—not like Celia: I have a great outburst, and then all seems glorious again. I cannot help believing in glorious things in a blind sort of way. I should be quite willing to enjoy the art here, but there is so much that I don’t know the reason of—so much that seems to me a consecration of ugliness rather than beauty. The painting and sculpture may be wonderful, but the feeling is often low and brutal, and sometimes even ridiculous. Here and there I see what takes me at once as noble—something that I might compare with the Alban Mountains or the sunset from the Pincian Hill; but that makes it the greater pity that there is so little of the best kind among all that mass of things over which men have toiled so.”\n\n“Of course there is always a great deal of poor work: the rarer things want that soil to grow in.”\n\n“Oh dear,” said Dorothea, taking up that thought into the chief current of her anxiety; “I see it must be very difficult to do anything good. I have often felt since I have been in Rome that most of our lives would look much uglier and more bungling than the pictures, if they could be put on the wall.”\n\nDorothea parted her lips again as if she were going to say more, but changed her mind and paused.\n\n“You are too young—it is an anachronism for you to have such thoughts,” said Will, energetically, with a quick shake of the head habitual to him. “You talk as if you had never known any youth. It is monstrous—as if you had had a vision of Hades in your childhood, like the boy in the legend. You have been brought up in some of those horrible notions that choose the sweetest women to devour—like Minotaurs. And now you will go and be shut up in that stone prison at Lowick: you will be buried alive. It makes me savage to think of it! I would rather never have seen you than think of you with such a prospect.”\n\nWill again feared that he had gone too far; but the meaning we attach to words depends on our feeling, and his tone of angry regret had so much kindness in it for Dorothea’s heart, which had always been giving out ardor and had never been fed with much from the living beings around her, that she felt a new sense of gratitude and answered with a gentle smile—\n\n“It is very good of you to be anxious about me. It is because you did not like Lowick yourself: you had set your heart on another kind of life. But Lowick is my chosen home.”\n\nThe last sentence was spoken with an almost solemn cadence, and Will did not know what to say, since it would not be useful for him to embrace her slippers, and tell her that he would die for her: it was clear that she required nothing of the sort; and they were both silent for a moment or two, when Dorothea began again with an air of saying at last what had been in her mind beforehand.\n\n“I wanted to ask you again about something you said the other day. Perhaps it was half of it your lively way of speaking: I notice that you like to put things strongly; I myself often exaggerate when I speak hastily.”\n\n“What was it?” said Will, observing that she spoke with a timidity quite new in her. “I have a hyperbolical tongue: it catches fire as it goes. I dare say I shall have to retract.”\n\n“I mean what you said about the necessity of knowing German—I mean, for the subjects that Mr. Casaubon is engaged in. I have been thinking about it; and it seems to me that with Mr. Casaubon’s learning he must have before him the same materials as German scholars—has he not?” Dorothea’s timidity was due to an indistinct consciousness that she was in the strange situation of consulting a third person about the adequacy of Mr. Casaubon’s learning.\n\n“Not exactly the same materials,” said Will, thinking that he would be duly reserved. “He is not an Orientalist, you know. He does not profess to have more than second-hand knowledge there.”\n\n“But there are very valuable books about antiquities which were written a long while ago by scholars who knew nothing about these modern things; and they are still used. Why should Mr. Casaubon’s not be valuable, like theirs?” said Dorothea, with more remonstrant energy. She was impelled to have the argument aloud, which she had been having in her own mind.\n\n“That depends on the line of study taken,” said Will, also getting a tone of rejoinder. “The subject Mr. Casaubon has chosen is as changing as chemistry: new discoveries are constantly making new points of view. Who wants a system on the basis of the four elements, or a book to refute Paracelsus? Do you not see that it is no use now to be crawling a little way after men of the last century—men like Bryant—and correcting their mistakes?—living in a lumber-room and furbishing up broken-legged theories about Chus and Mizraim?”\n\n“How can you bear to speak so lightly?” said Dorothea, with a look between sorrow and anger. “If it were as you say, what could be sadder than so much ardent labor all in vain? I wonder it does not affect you more painfully, if you really think that a man like Mr. Casaubon, of so much goodness, power, and learning, should in any way fail in what has been the labor of his best years.” She was beginning to be shocked that she had got to such a point of supposition, and indignant with Will for having led her to it.\n\n“You questioned me about the matter of fact, not of feeling,” said Will. “But if you wish to punish me for the fact, I submit. I am not in a position to express my feeling toward Mr. Casaubon: it would be at best a pensioner’s eulogy.”\n\n“Pray excuse me,” said Dorothea, coloring deeply. “I am aware, as you say, that I am in fault in having introduced the subject. Indeed, I am wrong altogether. Failure after long perseverance is much grander than never to have a striving good enough to be called a failure.”\n\n“I quite agree with you,” said Will, determined to change the situation—“so much so that I have made up my mind not to run that risk of never attaining a failure. Mr. Casaubon’s generosity has perhaps been dangerous to me, and I mean to renounce the liberty it has given me. I mean to go back to England shortly and work my own way—depend on nobody else than myself.”\n\n“That is fine—I respect that feeling,” said Dorothea, with returning kindness. “But Mr. Casaubon, I am sure, has never thought of anything in the matter except what was most for your welfare.”\n\n“She has obstinacy and pride enough to serve instead of love, now she has married him,” said Will to himself. Aloud he said, rising—\n\n“I shall not see you again.”\n\n“Oh, stay till Mr. Casaubon comes,” said Dorothea, earnestly. “I am so glad we met in Rome. I wanted to know you.”\n\n“And I have made you angry,” said Will. “I have made you think ill of me.”\n\n“Oh no. My sister tells me I am always angry with people who do not say just what I like. But I hope I am not given to think ill of them. In the end I am usually obliged to think ill of myself for being so impatient.”\n\n“Still, you don’t like me; I have made myself an unpleasant thought to you.”\n\n“Not at all,” said Dorothea, with the most open kindness. “I like you very much.”\n\nWill was not quite contented, thinking that he would apparently have been of more importance if he had been disliked. He said nothing, but looked dull, not to say sulky.\n\n“And I am quite interested to see what you will do,” Dorothea went on cheerfully. “I believe devoutly in a natural difference of vocation. If it were not for that belief, I suppose I should be very narrow—there are so many things, besides painting, that I am quite ignorant of. You would hardly believe how little I have taken in of music and literature, which you know so much of. I wonder what your vocation will turn out to be: perhaps you will be a poet?”\n\n“That depends. To be a poet is to have a soul so quick to discern that no shade of quality escapes it, and so quick to feel, that discernment is but a hand playing with finely ordered variety on the chords of emotion—a soul in which knowledge passes instantaneously into feeling, and feeling flashes back as a new organ of knowledge. One may have that condition by fits only.”\n\n“But you leave out the poems,” said Dorothea. “I think they are wanted to complete the poet. I understand what you mean about knowledge passing into feeling, for that seems to be just what I experience. But I am sure I could never produce a poem.”\n\n“You are a poem—and that is to be the best part of a poet—what makes up the poet’s consciousness in his best moods,” said Will, showing such originality as we all share with the morning and the spring-time and other endless renewals.\n\n“I am very glad to hear it,” said Dorothea, laughing out her words in a bird-like modulation, and looking at Will with playful gratitude in her eyes. “What very kind things you say to me!”\n\n“I wish I could ever do anything that would be what you call kind—that I could ever be of the slightest service to you. I fear I shall never have the opportunity.” Will spoke with fervor.\n\n“Oh yes,” said Dorothea, cordially. “It will come; and I shall remember how well you wish me. I quite hoped that we should be friends when I first saw you—because of your relationship to Mr. Casaubon.” There was a certain liquid brightness in her eyes, and Will was conscious that his own were obeying a law of nature and filling too. The allusion to Mr. Casaubon would have spoiled all if anything at that moment could have spoiled the subduing power, the sweet dignity, of her noble unsuspicious inexperience.\n\n“And there is one thing even now that you can do,” said Dorothea, rising and walking a little way under the strength of a recurring impulse. “Promise me that you will not again, to any one, speak of that subject—I mean about Mr. Casaubon’s writings—I mean in that kind of way. It was I who led to it. It was my fault. But promise me.”\n\nShe had returned from her brief pacing and stood opposite Will, looking gravely at him.\n\n“Certainly, I will promise you,” said Will, reddening however. If he never said a cutting word about Mr. Casaubon again and left off receiving favors from him, it would clearly be permissible to hate him the more. The poet must know how to hate, says Goethe; and Will was at least ready with that accomplishment. He said that he must go now without waiting for Mr. Casaubon, whom he would come to take leave of at the last moment. Dorothea gave him her hand, and they exchanged a simple “Good-by.”\n\nBut going out of the porte cochere he met Mr. Casaubon, and that gentleman, expressing the best wishes for his cousin, politely waived the pleasure of any further leave-taking on the morrow, which would be sufficiently crowded with the preparations for departure.\n\n“I have something to tell you about our cousin Mr. Ladislaw, which I think will heighten your opinion of him,” said Dorothea to her husband in the course of the evening. She had mentioned immediately on his entering that Will had just gone away, and would come again, but Mr. Casaubon had said, “I met him outside, and we made our final adieux, I believe,” saying this with the air and tone by which we imply that any subject, whether private or public, does not interest us enough to wish for a further remark upon it. So Dorothea had waited.\n\n“What is that, my love?” said Mr Casaubon (he always said “my love” when his manner was the coldest).\n\n“He has made up his mind to leave off wandering at once, and to give up his dependence on your generosity. He means soon to go back to England, and work his own way. I thought you would consider that a good sign,” said Dorothea, with an appealing look into her husband’s neutral face.\n\n“Did he mention the precise order of occupation to which he would addict himself?”\n\n“No. But he said that he felt the danger which lay for him in your generosity. Of course he will write to you about it. Do you not think better of him for his resolve?”\n\n“I shall await his communication on the subject,” said Mr. Casaubon.\n\n“I told him I was sure that the thing you considered in all you did for him was his own welfare. I remembered your goodness in what you said about him when I first saw him at Lowick,” said Dorothea, putting her hand on her husband’s.\n\n“I had a duty towards him,” said Mr. Casaubon, laying his other hand on Dorothea’s in conscientious acceptance of her caress, but with a glance which he could not hinder from being uneasy. “The young man, I confess, is not otherwise an object of interest to me, nor need we, I think, discuss his future course, which it is not ours to determine beyond the limits which I have sufficiently indicated.” Dorothea did not mention Will again.\n\n# BOOK III. WAITING FOR DEATH. CHAPTER XXIII.\n\n“Your horses of the Sun,” he said, “And first-rate whip Apollo! Whate’er they be, I’ll eat my head, But I will beat them hollow.”\n\nFred Vincy, we have seen, had a debt on his mind, and though no such immaterial burthen could depress that buoyant-hearted young gentleman for many hours together, there were circumstances connected with this debt which made the thought of it unusually importunate. The creditor was Mr. Bambridge, a horse-dealer of the neighborhood, whose company was much sought in Middlemarch by young men understood to be “addicted to pleasure.” During the vacations Fred had naturally required more amusements than he had ready money for, and Mr. Bambridge had been accommodating enough not only to trust him for the hire of horses and the accidental expense of ruining a fine hunter, but also to make a small advance by which he might be able to meet some losses at billiards. The total debt was a hundred and sixty pounds. Bambridge was in no alarm about his money, being sure that young Vincy had backers; but he had required something to show for it, and Fred had at first given a bill with his own signature. Three months later he had renewed this bill with the signature of Caleb Garth. On both occasions Fred had felt confident that he should meet the bill himself, having ample funds at disposal in his own hopefulness. You will hardly demand that his confidence should have a basis in external facts; such confidence, we know, is something less coarse and materialistic: it is a comfortable disposition leading us to expect that the wisdom of providence or the folly of our friends, the mysteries of luck or the still greater mystery of our high individual value in the universe, will bring about agreeable issues, such as are consistent with our good taste in costume, and our general preference for the best style of thing. Fred felt sure that he should have a present from his uncle, that he should have a run of luck, that by dint of “swapping” he should gradually metamorphose a horse worth forty pounds into a horse that would fetch a hundred at any moment—“judgment” being always equivalent to an unspecified sum in hard cash. And in any case, even supposing negations which only a morbid distrust could imagine, Fred had always (at that time) his father’s pocket as a last resource, so that his assets of hopefulness had a sort of gorgeous superfluity about them. Of what might be the capacity of his father’s pocket, Fred had only a vague notion: was not trade elastic? And would not the deficiencies of one year be made up for by the surplus of another? The Vincys lived in an easy profuse way, not with any new ostentation, but according to the family habits and traditions, so that the children had no standard of economy, and the elder ones retained some of their infantine notion that their father might pay for anything if he would. Mr. Vincy himself had expensive Middlemarch habits—spent money on coursing, on his cellar, and on dinner-giving, while mamma had those running accounts with tradespeople, which give a cheerful sense of getting everything one wants without any question of payment. But it was in the nature of fathers, Fred knew, to bully one about expenses: there was always a little storm over his extravagance if he had to disclose a debt, and Fred disliked bad weather within doors. He was too filial to be disrespectful to his father, and he bore the thunder with the certainty that it was transient; but in the mean time it was disagreeable to see his mother cry, and also to be obliged to look sulky instead of having fun; for Fred was so good-tempered that if he looked glum under scolding, it was chiefly for propriety’s sake. The easier course plainly, was to renew the bill with a friend’s signature. Why not? With the superfluous securities of hope at his command, there was no reason why he should not have increased other people’s liabilities to any extent, but for the fact that men whose names were good for anything were usually pessimists, indisposed to believe that the universal order of things would necessarily be agreeable to an agreeable young gentleman.\n\nWith a favor to ask we review our list of friends, do justice to their more amiable qualities, forgive their little offenses, and concerning each in turn, try to arrive at the conclusion that he will be eager to oblige us, our own eagerness to be obliged being as communicable as other warmth. Still there is always a certain number who are dismissed as but moderately eager until the others have refused; and it happened that Fred checked off all his friends but one, on the ground that applying to them would be disagreeable; being implicitly convinced that he at least (whatever might be maintained about mankind generally) had a right to be free from anything disagreeable. That he should ever fall into a thoroughly unpleasant position—wear trousers shrunk with washing, eat cold mutton, have to walk for want of a horse, or to “duck under” in any sort of way—was an absurdity irreconcilable with those cheerful intuitions implanted in him by nature. And Fred winced under the idea of being looked down upon as wanting funds for small debts. Thus it came to pass that the friend whom he chose to apply to was at once the poorest and the kindest—namely, Caleb Garth.\n\nThe Garths were very fond of Fred, as he was of them; for when he and Rosamond were little ones, and the Garths were better off, the slight connection between the two families through Mr. Featherstone’s double marriage (the first to Mr. Garth’s sister, and the second to Mrs. Vincy’s) had led to an acquaintance which was carried on between the children rather than the parents: the children drank tea together out of their toy teacups, and spent whole days together in play. Mary was a little hoyden, and Fred at six years old thought her the nicest girl in the world, making her his wife with a brass ring which he had cut from an umbrella. Through all the stages of his education he had kept his affection for the Garths, and his habit of going to their house as a second home, though any intercourse between them and the elders of his family had long ceased. Even when Caleb Garth was prosperous, the Vincys were on condescending terms with him and his wife, for there were nice distinctions of rank in Middlemarch; and though old manufacturers could not any more than dukes be connected with none but equals, they were conscious of an inherent social superiority which was defined with great nicety in practice, though hardly expressible theoretically. Since then Mr. Garth had failed in the building business, which he had unfortunately added to his other avocations of surveyor, valuer, and agent, had conducted that business for a time entirely for the benefit of his assignees, and had been living narrowly, exerting himself to the utmost that he might after all pay twenty shillings in the pound. He had now achieved this, and from all who did not think it a bad precedent, his honorable exertions had won him due esteem; but in no part of the world is genteel visiting founded on esteem, in the absence of suitable furniture and complete dinner-service. Mrs. Vincy had never been at her ease with Mrs. Garth, and frequently spoke of her as a woman who had had to work for her bread—meaning that Mrs. Garth had been a teacher before her marriage; in which case an intimacy with Lindley Murray and Mangnall’s Questions was something like a draper’s discrimination of calico trademarks, or a courier’s acquaintance with foreign countries: no woman who was better off needed that sort of thing. And since Mary had been keeping Mr. Featherstone’s house, Mrs. Vincy’s want of liking for the Garths had been converted into something more positive, by alarm lest Fred should engage himself to this plain girl, whose parents “lived in such a small way.” Fred, being aware of this, never spoke at home of his visits to Mrs. Garth, which had of late become more frequent, the increasing ardor of his affection for Mary inclining him the more towards those who belonged to her.\n\nMr. Garth had a small office in the town, and to this Fred went with his request. He obtained it without much difficulty, for a large amount of painful experience had not sufficed to make Caleb Garth cautious about his own affairs, or distrustful of his fellow-men when they had not proved themselves untrustworthy; and he had the highest opinion of Fred, was “sure the lad would turn out well—an open affectionate fellow, with a good bottom to his character—you might trust him for anything.” Such was Caleb’s psychological argument. He was one of those rare men who are rigid to themselves and indulgent to others. He had a certain shame about his neighbors’ errors, and never spoke of them willingly; hence he was not likely to divert his mind from the best mode of hardening timber and other ingenious devices in order to preconceive those errors. If he had to blame any one, it was necessary for him to move all the papers within his reach, or describe various diagrams with his stick, or make calculations with the odd money in his pocket, before he could begin; and he would rather do other men’s work than find fault with their doing. I fear he was a bad disciplinarian.\n\nWhen Fred stated the circumstances of his debt, his wish to meet it without troubling his father, and the certainty that the money would be forthcoming so as to cause no one any inconvenience, Caleb pushed his spectacles upward, listened, looked into his favorite’s clear young eyes, and believed him, not distinguishing confidence about the future from veracity about the past; but he felt that it was an occasion for a friendly hint as to conduct, and that before giving his signature he must give a rather strong admonition. Accordingly, he took the paper and lowered his spectacles, measured the space at his command, reached his pen and examined it, dipped it in the ink and examined it again, then pushed the paper a little way from him, lifted up his spectacles again, showed a deepened depression in the outer angle of his bushy eyebrows, which gave his face a peculiar mildness (pardon these details for once—you would have learned to love them if you had known Caleb Garth), and said in a comfortable tone,—\n\n“It was a misfortune, eh, that breaking the horse’s knees? And then, these exchanges, they don’t answer when you have ’cute jockeys to deal with. You’ll be wiser another time, my boy.”\n\nWhereupon Caleb drew down his spectacles, and proceeded to write his signature with the care which he always gave to that performance; for whatever he did in the way of business he did well. He contemplated the large well-proportioned letters and final flourish, with his head a trifle on one side for an instant, then handed it to Fred, said “Good-by,” and returned forthwith to his absorption in a plan for Sir James Chettam’s new farm-buildings.\n\nEither because his interest in this work thrust the incident of the signature from his memory, or for some reason of which Caleb was more conscious, Mrs. Garth remained ignorant of the affair.\n\nSince it occurred, a change had come over Fred’s sky, which altered his view of the distance, and was the reason why his uncle Featherstone’s present of money was of importance enough to make his color come and go, first with a too definite expectation, and afterwards with a proportionate disappointment. His failure in passing his examination, had made his accumulation of college debts the more unpardonable by his father, and there had been an unprecedented storm at home. Mr. Vincy had sworn that if he had anything more of that sort to put up with, Fred should turn out and get his living how he could; and he had never yet quite recovered his good-humored tone to his son, who had especially enraged him by saying at this stage of things that he did not want to be a clergyman, and would rather not “go on with that.” Fred was conscious that he would have been yet more severely dealt with if his family as well as himself had not secretly regarded him as Mr. Featherstone’s heir; that old gentleman’s pride in him, and apparent fondness for him, serving in the stead of more exemplary conduct—just as when a youthful nobleman steals jewellery we call the act kleptomania, speak of it with a philosophical smile, and never think of his being sent to the house of correction as if he were a ragged boy who had stolen turnips. In fact, tacit expectations of what would be done for him by uncle Featherstone determined the angle at which most people viewed Fred Vincy in Middlemarch; and in his own consciousness, what uncle Featherstone would do for him in an emergency, or what he would do simply as an incorporated luck, formed always an immeasurable depth of aerial perspective. But that present of bank-notes, once made, was measurable, and being applied to the amount of the debt, showed a deficit which had still to be filled up either by Fred’s “judgment” or by luck in some other shape. For that little episode of the alleged borrowing, in which he had made his father the agent in getting the Bulstrode certificate, was a new reason against going to his father for money towards meeting his actual debt. Fred was keen enough to foresee that anger would confuse distinctions, and that his denial of having borrowed expressly on the strength of his uncle’s will would be taken as a falsehood. He had gone to his father and told him one vexatious affair, and he had left another untold: in such cases the complete revelation always produces the impression of a previous duplicity. Now Fred piqued himself on keeping clear of lies, and even fibs; he often shrugged his shoulders and made a significant grimace at what he called Rosamond’s fibs (it is only brothers who can associate such ideas with a lovely girl); and rather than incur the accusation of falsehood he would even incur some trouble and self-restraint. It was under strong inward pressure of this kind that Fred had taken the wise step of depositing the eighty pounds with his mother. It was a pity that he had not at once given them to Mr. Garth; but he meant to make the sum complete with another sixty, and with a view to this, he had kept twenty pounds in his own pocket as a sort of seed-corn, which, planted by judgment, and watered by luck, might yield more than threefold—a very poor rate of multiplication when the field is a young gentleman’s infinite soul, with all the numerals at command.\n\nFred was not a gambler: he had not that specific disease in which the suspension of the whole nervous energy on a chance or risk becomes as necessary as the dram to the drunkard; he had only the tendency to that diffusive form of gambling which has no alcoholic intensity, but is carried on with the healthiest chyle-fed blood, keeping up a joyous imaginative activity which fashions events according to desire, and having no fears about its own weather, only sees the advantage there must be to others in going aboard with it. Hopefulness has a pleasure in making a throw of any kind, because the prospect of success is certain; and only a more generous pleasure in offering as many as possible a share in the stake. Fred liked play, especially billiards, as he liked hunting or riding a steeple-chase; and he only liked it the better because he wanted money and hoped to win. But the twenty pounds’ worth of seed-corn had been planted in vain in the seductive green plot—all of it at least which had not been dispersed by the roadside—and Fred found himself close upon the term of payment with no money at command beyond the eighty pounds which he had deposited with his mother. The broken-winded horse which he rode represented a present which had been made to him a long while ago by his uncle Featherstone: his father always allowed him to keep a horse, Mr. Vincy’s own habits making him regard this as a reasonable demand even for a son who was rather exasperating. This horse, then, was Fred’s property, and in his anxiety to meet the imminent bill he determined to sacrifice a possession without which life would certainly be worth little. He made the resolution with a sense of heroism—heroism forced on him by the dread of breaking his word to Mr. Garth, by his love for Mary and awe of her opinion. He would start for Houndsley horse-fair which was to be held the next morning, and—simply sell his horse, bringing back the money by coach?—Well, the horse would hardly fetch more than thirty pounds, and there was no knowing what might happen; it would be folly to balk himself of luck beforehand. It was a hundred to one that some good chance would fall in his way; the longer he thought of it, the less possible it seemed that he should not have a good chance, and the less reasonable that he should not equip himself with the powder and shot for bringing it down. He would ride to Houndsley with Bambridge and with Horrock “the vet,” and without asking them anything expressly, he should virtually get the benefit of their opinion. Before he set out, Fred got the eighty pounds from his mother.\n\nMost of those who saw Fred riding out of Middlemarch in company with Bambridge and Horrock, on his way of course to Houndsley horse-fair, thought that young Vincy was pleasure-seeking as usual; and but for an unwonted consciousness of grave matters on hand, he himself would have had a sense of dissipation, and of doing what might be expected of a gay young fellow. Considering that Fred was not at all coarse, that he rather looked down on the manners and speech of young men who had not been to the university, and that he had written stanzas as pastoral and unvoluptuous as his flute-playing, his attraction towards Bambridge and Horrock was an interesting fact which even the love of horse-flesh would not wholly account for without that mysterious influence of Naming which determinates so much of mortal choice. Under any other name than “pleasure” the society of Messieurs Bambridge and Horrock must certainly have been regarded as monotonous; and to arrive with them at Houndsley on a drizzling afternoon, to get down at the Red Lion in a street shaded with coal-dust, and dine in a room furnished with a dirt-enamelled map of the county, a bad portrait of an anonymous horse in a stable, His Majesty George the Fourth with legs and cravat, and various leaden spittoons, might have seemed a hard business, but for the sustaining power of nomenclature which determined that the pursuit of these things was “gay.”\n\nIn Mr. Horrock there was certainly an apparent unfathomableness which offered play to the imagination. Costume, at a glance, gave him a thrilling association with horses (enough to specify the hat-brim which took the slightest upward angle just to escape the suspicion of bending downwards), and nature had given him a face which by dint of Mongolian eyes, and a nose, mouth, and chin seeming to follow his hat-brim in a moderate inclination upwards, gave the effect of a subdued unchangeable sceptical smile, of all expressions the most tyrannous over a susceptible mind, and, when accompanied by adequate silence, likely to create the reputation of an invincible understanding, an infinite fund of humor—too dry to flow, and probably in a state of immovable crust,—and a critical judgment which, if you could ever be fortunate enough to know it, would be the thing and no other. It is a physiognomy seen in all vocations, but perhaps it has never been more powerful over the youth of England than in a judge of horses.\n\nMr. Horrock, at a question from Fred about his horse’s fetlock, turned sideways in his saddle, and watched the horse’s action for the space of three minutes, then turned forward, twitched his own bridle, and remained silent with a profile neither more nor less sceptical than it had been.\n\nThe part thus played in dialogue by Mr. Horrock was terribly effective. A mixture of passions was excited in Fred—a mad desire to thrash Horrock’s opinion into utterance, restrained by anxiety to retain the advantage of his friendship. There was always the chance that Horrock might say something quite invaluable at the right moment.\n\nMr. Bambridge had more open manners, and appeared to give forth his ideas without economy. He was loud, robust, and was sometimes spoken of as being “given to indulgence”—chiefly in swearing, drinking, and beating his wife. Some people who had lost by him called him a vicious man; but he regarded horse-dealing as the finest of the arts, and might have argued plausibly that it had nothing to do with morality. He was undeniably a prosperous man, bore his drinking better than others bore their moderation, and, on the whole, flourished like the green bay-tree. But his range of conversation was limited, and like the fine old tune, “Drops of brandy,” gave you after a while a sense of returning upon itself in a way that might make weak heads dizzy. But a slight infusion of Mr. Bambridge was felt to give tone and character to several circles in Middlemarch; and he was a distinguished figure in the bar and billiard-room at the Green Dragon. He knew some anecdotes about the heroes of the turf, and various clever tricks of Marquesses and Viscounts which seemed to prove that blood asserted its pre-eminence even among black-legs; but the minute retentiveness of his memory was chiefly shown about the horses he had himself bought and sold; the number of miles they would trot you in no time without turning a hair being, after the lapse of years, still a subject of passionate asseveration, in which he would assist the imagination of his hearers by solemnly swearing that they never saw anything like it. In short, Mr. Bambridge was a man of pleasure and a gay companion.\n\nFred was subtle, and did not tell his friends that he was going to Houndsley bent on selling his horse: he wished to get indirectly at their genuine opinion of its value, not being aware that a genuine opinion was the last thing likely to be extracted from such eminent critics. It was not Mr. Bambridge’s weakness to be a gratuitous flatterer. He had never before been so much struck with the fact that this unfortunate bay was a roarer to a degree which required the roundest word for perdition to give you any idea of it.\n\n“You made a bad hand at swapping when you went to anybody but me, Vincy! Why, you never threw your leg across a finer horse than that chestnut, and you gave him for this brute. If you set him cantering, he goes on like twenty sawyers. I never heard but one worse roarer in my life, and that was a roan: it belonged to Pegwell, the corn-factor; he used to drive him in his gig seven years ago, and he wanted me to take him, but I said, ‘Thank you, Peg, I don’t deal in wind-instruments.’ That was what I said. It went the round of the country, that joke did. But, what the hell! the horse was a penny trumpet to that roarer of yours.”\n\n“Why, you said just now his was worse than mine,” said Fred, more irritable than usual.\n\n“I said a lie, then,” said Mr. Bambridge, emphatically. “There wasn’t a penny to choose between ’em.”\n\nFred spurred his horse, and they trotted on a little way. When they slackened again, Mr. Bambridge said—\n\n“Not but what the roan was a better trotter than yours.”\n\n“I’m quite satisfied with his paces, I know,” said Fred, who required all the consciousness of being in gay company to support him; “I say his trot is an uncommonly clean one, eh, Horrock?”\n\nMr. Horrock looked before him with as complete a neutrality as if he had been a portrait by a great master.\n\nFred gave up the fallacious hope of getting a genuine opinion; but on reflection he saw that Bambridge’s depreciation and Horrock’s silence were both virtually encouraging, and indicated that they thought better of the horse than they chose to say.\n\nThat very evening, indeed, before the fair had set in, Fred thought he saw a favorable opening for disposing advantageously of his horse, but an opening which made him congratulate himself on his foresight in bringing with him his eighty pounds. A young farmer, acquainted with Mr. Bambridge, came into the Red Lion, and entered into conversation about parting with a hunter, which he introduced at once as Diamond, implying that it was a public character. For himself he only wanted a useful hack, which would draw upon occasion; being about to marry and to give up hunting. The hunter was in a friend’s stable at some little distance; there was still time for gentlemen to see it before dark. The friend’s stable had to be reached through a back street where you might as easily have been poisoned without expense of drugs as in any grim street of that unsanitary period. Fred was not fortified against disgust by brandy, as his companions were, but the hope of having at last seen the horse that would enable him to make money was exhilarating enough to lead him over the same ground again the first thing in the morning. He felt sure that if he did not come to a bargain with the farmer, Bambridge would; for the stress of circumstances, Fred felt, was sharpening his acuteness and endowing him with all the constructive power of suspicion. Bambridge had run down Diamond in a way that he never would have done (the horse being a friend’s) if he had not thought of buying it; every one who looked at the animal—even Horrock—was evidently impressed with its merit. To get all the advantage of being with men of this sort, you must know how to draw your inferences, and not be a spoon who takes things literally. The color of the horse was a dappled gray, and Fred happened to know that Lord Medlicote’s man was on the look-out for just such a horse. After all his running down, Bambridge let it out in the course of the evening, when the farmer was absent, that he had seen worse horses go for eighty pounds. Of course he contradicted himself twenty times over, but when you know what is likely to be true you can test a man’s admissions. And Fred could not but reckon his own judgment of a horse as worth something. The farmer had paused over Fred’s respectable though broken-winded steed long enough to show that he thought it worth consideration, and it seemed probable that he would take it, with five-and-twenty pounds in addition, as the equivalent of Diamond. In that case Fred, when he had parted with his new horse for at least eighty pounds, would be fifty-five pounds in pocket by the transaction, and would have a hundred and thirty-five pounds towards meeting the bill; so that the deficit temporarily thrown on Mr. Garth would at the utmost be twenty-five pounds. By the time he was hurrying on his clothes in the morning, he saw so clearly the importance of not losing this rare chance, that if Bambridge and Horrock had both dissuaded him, he would not have been deluded into a direct interpretation of their purpose: he would have been aware that those deep hands held something else than a young fellow’s interest. With regard to horses, distrust was your only clew. But scepticism, as we know, can never be thoroughly applied, else life would come to a standstill: something we must believe in and do, and whatever that something may be called, it is virtually our own judgment, even when it seems like the most slavish reliance on another. Fred believed in the excellence of his bargain, and even before the fair had well set in, had got possession of the dappled gray, at the price of his old horse and thirty pounds in addition—only five pounds more than he had expected to give.\n\nBut he felt a little worried and wearied, perhaps with mental debate, and without waiting for the further gayeties of the horse-fair, he set out alone on his fourteen miles’ journey, meaning to take it very quietly and keep his horse fresh.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV.\n\n“The offender’s sorrow brings but small relief To him who wears the strong offence’s cross.” —SHAKESPEARE: Sonnets.\n\nI am sorry to say that only the third day after the propitious events at Houndsley Fred Vincy had fallen into worse spirits than he had known in his life before. Not that he had been disappointed as to the possible market for his horse, but that before the bargain could be concluded with Lord Medlicote’s man, this Diamond, in which hope to the amount of eighty pounds had been invested, had without the slightest warning exhibited in the stable a most vicious energy in kicking, had just missed killing the groom, and had ended in laming himself severely by catching his leg in a rope that overhung the stable-board. There was no more redress for this than for the discovery of bad temper after marriage—which of course old companions were aware of before the ceremony. For some reason or other, Fred had none of his usual elasticity under this stroke of ill-fortune: he was simply aware that he had only fifty pounds, that there was no chance of his getting any more at present, and that the bill for a hundred and sixty would be presented in five days. Even if he had applied to his father on the plea that Mr. Garth should be saved from loss, Fred felt smartingly that his father would angrily refuse to rescue Mr. Garth from the consequence of what he would call encouraging extravagance and deceit. He was so utterly downcast that he could frame no other project than to go straight to Mr. Garth and tell him the sad truth, carrying with him the fifty pounds, and getting that sum at least safely out of his own hands. His father, being at the warehouse, did not yet know of the accident: when he did, he would storm about the vicious brute being brought into his stable; and before meeting that lesser annoyance Fred wanted to get away with all his courage to face the greater. He took his father’s nag, for he had made up his mind that when he had told Mr. Garth, he would ride to Stone Court and confess all to Mary. In fact, it is probable that but for Mary’s existence and Fred’s love for her, his conscience would have been much less active both in previously urging the debt on his thought and impelling him not to spare himself after his usual fashion by deferring an unpleasant task, but to act as directly and simply as he could. Even much stronger mortals than Fred Vincy hold half their rectitude in the mind of the being they love best. “The theatre of all my actions is fallen,” said an antique personage when his chief friend was dead; and they are fortunate who get a theatre where the audience demands their best. Certainly it would have made a considerable difference to Fred at that time if Mary Garth had had no decided notions as to what was admirable in character.\n\nMr. Garth was not at the office, and Fred rode on to his house, which was a little way outside the town—a homely place with an orchard in front of it, a rambling, old-fashioned, half-timbered building, which before the town had spread had been a farm-house, but was now surrounded with the private gardens of the townsmen. We get the fonder of our houses if they have a physiognomy of their own, as our friends have. The Garth family, which was rather a large one, for Mary had four brothers and one sister, were very fond of their old house, from which all the best furniture had long been sold. Fred liked it too, knowing it by heart even to the attic which smelt deliciously of apples and quinces, and until to-day he had never come to it without pleasant expectations; but his heart beat uneasily now with the sense that he should probably have to make his confession before Mrs. Garth, of whom he was rather more in awe than of her husband. Not that she was inclined to sarcasm and to impulsive sallies, as Mary was. In her present matronly age at least, Mrs. Garth never committed herself by over-hasty speech; having, as she said, borne the yoke in her youth, and learned self-control. She had that rare sense which discerns what is unalterable, and submits to it without murmuring. Adoring her husband’s virtues, she had very early made up her mind to his incapacity of minding his own interests, and had met the consequences cheerfully. She had been magnanimous enough to renounce all pride in teapots or children’s frilling, and had never poured any pathetic confidences into the ears of her feminine neighbors concerning Mr. Garth’s want of prudence and the sums he might have had if he had been like other men. Hence these fair neighbors thought her either proud or eccentric, and sometimes spoke of her to their husbands as “your fine Mrs. Garth.” She was not without her criticism of them in return, being more accurately instructed than most matrons in Middlemarch, and—where is the blameless woman?—apt to be a little severe towards her own sex, which in her opinion was framed to be entirely subordinate. On the other hand, she was disproportionately indulgent towards the failings of men, and was often heard to say that these were natural. Also, it must be admitted that Mrs. Garth was a trifle too emphatic in her resistance to what she held to be follies: the passage from governess into housewife had wrought itself a little too strongly into her consciousness, and she rarely forgot that while her grammar and accent were above the town standard, she wore a plain cap, cooked the family dinner, and darned all the stockings. She had sometimes taken pupils in a peripatetic fashion, making them follow her about in the kitchen with their book or slate. She thought it good for them to see that she could make an excellent lather while she corrected their blunders “without looking,”—that a woman with her sleeves tucked up above her elbows might know all about the Subjunctive Mood or the Torrid Zone—that, in short, she might possess “education” and other good things ending in “tion,” and worthy to be pronounced emphatically, without being a useless doll. When she made remarks to this edifying effect, she had a firm little frown on her brow, which yet did not hinder her face from looking benevolent, and her words which came forth like a procession were uttered in a fervid agreeable contralto. Certainly, the exemplary Mrs. Garth had her droll aspects, but her character sustained her oddities, as a very fine wine sustains a flavor of skin.\n\nTowards Fred Vincy she had a motherly feeling, and had always been disposed to excuse his errors, though she would probably not have excused Mary for engaging herself to him, her daughter being included in that more rigorous judgment which she applied to her own sex. But this very fact of her exceptional indulgence towards him made it the harder to Fred that he must now inevitably sink in her opinion. And the circumstances of his visit turned out to be still more unpleasant than he had expected; for Caleb Garth had gone out early to look at some repairs not far off. Mrs. Garth at certain hours was always in the kitchen, and this morning she was carrying on several occupations at once there—making her pies at the well-scoured deal table on one side of that airy room, observing Sally’s movements at the oven and dough-tub through an open door, and giving lessons to her youngest boy and girl, who were standing opposite to her at the table with their books and slates before them. A tub and a clothes-horse at the other end of the kitchen indicated an intermittent wash of small things also going on.\n\nMrs. Garth, with her sleeves turned above her elbows, deftly handling her pastry—applying her rolling-pin and giving ornamental pinches, while she expounded with grammatical fervor what were the right views about the concord of verbs and pronouns with “nouns of multitude or signifying many,” was a sight agreeably amusing. She was of the same curly-haired, square-faced type as Mary, but handsomer, with more delicacy of feature, a pale skin, a solid matronly figure, and a remarkable firmness of glance. In her snowy-frilled cap she reminded one of that delightful Frenchwoman whom we have all seen marketing, basket on arm. Looking at the mother, you might hope that the daughter would become like her, which is a prospective advantage equal to a dowry—the mother too often standing behind the daughter like a malignant prophecy—“Such as I am, she will shortly be.”\n\n“Now let us go through that once more,” said Mrs. Garth, pinching an apple-puff which seemed to distract Ben, an energetic young male with a heavy brow, from due attention to the lesson. “‘Not without regard to the import of the word as conveying unity or plurality of idea’—tell me again what that means, Ben.”\n\n(Mrs. Garth, like more celebrated educators, had her favorite ancient paths, and in a general wreck of society would have tried to hold her “Lindley Murray” above the waves.)\n\n“Oh—it means—you must think what you mean,” said Ben, rather peevishly. “I hate grammar. What’s the use of it?”\n\n“To teach you to speak and write correctly, so that you can be understood,” said Mrs. Garth, with severe precision. “Should you like to speak as old Job does?”\n\n“Yes,” said Ben, stoutly; “it’s funnier. He says, ‘Yo goo’—that’s just as good as ‘You go.’”\n\n“But he says, ‘A ship’s in the garden,’ instead of ‘a sheep,’” said Letty, with an air of superiority. “You might think he meant a ship off the sea.”\n\n“No, you mightn’t, if you weren’t silly,” said Ben. “How could a ship off the sea come there?”\n\n“These things belong only to pronunciation, which is the least part of grammar,” said Mrs. Garth. “That apple-peel is to be eaten by the pigs, Ben; if you eat it, I must give them your piece of pasty. Job has only to speak about very plain things. How do you think you would write or speak about anything more difficult, if you knew no more of grammar than he does? You would use wrong words, and put words in the wrong places, and instead of making people understand you, they would turn away from you as a tiresome person. What would you do then?”\n\n“I shouldn’t care, I should leave off,” said Ben, with a sense that this was an agreeable issue where grammar was concerned.\n\n“I see you are getting tired and stupid, Ben,” said Mrs. Garth, accustomed to these obstructive arguments from her male offspring. Having finished her pies, she moved towards the clothes-horse, and said, “Come here and tell me the story I told you on Wednesday, about Cincinnatus.”\n\n“I know! he was a farmer,” said Ben.\n\n“Now, Ben, he was a Roman—let me tell,” said Letty, using her elbow contentiously.\n\n“You silly thing, he was a Roman farmer, and he was ploughing.”\n\n“Yes, but before that—that didn’t come first—people wanted him,” said Letty.\n\n“Well, but you must say what sort of a man he was first,” insisted Ben. “He was a wise man, like my father, and that made the people want his advice. And he was a brave man, and could fight. And so could my father—couldn’t he, mother?”\n\n“Now, Ben, let me tell the story straight on, as mother told it us,” said Letty, frowning. “Please, mother, tell Ben not to speak.”\n\n“Letty, I am ashamed of you,” said her mother, wringing out the caps from the tub. “When your brother began, you ought to have waited to see if he could not tell the story. How rude you look, pushing and frowning, as if you wanted to conquer with your elbows! Cincinnatus, I am sure, would have been sorry to see his daughter behave so.” (Mrs. Garth delivered this awful sentence with much majesty of enunciation, and Letty felt that between repressed volubility and general disesteem, that of the Romans inclusive, life was already a painful affair.) “Now, Ben.”\n\n“Well—oh—well—why, there was a great deal of fighting, and they were all blockheads, and—I can’t tell it just how you told it—but they wanted a man to be captain and king and everything—”\n\n“Dictator, now,” said Letty, with injured looks, and not without a wish to make her mother repent.\n\n“Very well, dictator!” said Ben, contemptuously. “But that isn’t a good word: he didn’t tell them to write on slates.”\n\n“Come, come, Ben, you are not so ignorant as that,” said Mrs. Garth, carefully serious. “Hark, there is a knock at the door! Run, Letty, and open it.”\n\nThe knock was Fred’s; and when Letty said that her father was not in yet, but that her mother was in the kitchen, Fred had no alternative. He could not depart from his usual practice of going to see Mrs. Garth in the kitchen if she happened to be at work there. He put his arm round Letty’s neck silently, and led her into the kitchen without his usual jokes and caresses.\n\nMrs. Garth was surprised to see Fred at this hour, but surprise was not a feeling that she was given to express, and she only said, quietly continuing her work—\n\n“You, Fred, so early in the day? You look quite pale. Has anything happened?”\n\n“I want to speak to Mr. Garth,” said Fred, not yet ready to say more—“and to you also,” he added, after a little pause, for he had no doubt that Mrs. Garth knew everything about the bill, and he must in the end speak of it before her, if not to her solely.\n\n“Caleb will be in again in a few minutes,” said Mrs. Garth, who imagined some trouble between Fred and his father. “He is sure not to be long, because he has some work at his desk that must be done this morning. Do you mind staying with me, while I finish my matters here?”\n\n“But we needn’t go on about Cincinnatus, need we?” said Ben, who had taken Fred’s whip out of his hand, and was trying its efficiency on the cat.\n\n“No, go out now. But put that whip down. How very mean of you to whip poor old Tortoise! Pray take the whip from him, Fred.”\n\n“Come, old boy, give it me,” said Fred, putting out his hand.\n\n“Will you let me ride on your horse to-day?” said Ben, rendering up the whip, with an air of not being obliged to do it.\n\n“Not to-day—another time. I am not riding my own horse.”\n\n“Shall you see Mary to-day?”\n\n“Yes, I think so,” said Fred, with an unpleasant twinge.\n\n“Tell her to come home soon, and play at forfeits, and make fun.”\n\n“Enough, enough, Ben! run away,” said Mrs. Garth, seeing that Fred was teased.\n\n“Are Letty and Ben your only pupils now, Mrs. Garth?” said Fred, when the children were gone and it was needful to say something that would pass the time. He was not yet sure whether he should wait for Mr. Garth, or use any good opportunity in conversation to confess to Mrs. Garth herself, give her the money and ride away.\n\n“One—only one. Fanny Hackbutt comes at half past eleven. I am not getting a great income now,” said Mrs. Garth, smiling. “I am at a low ebb with pupils. But I have saved my little purse for Alfred’s premium: I have ninety-two pounds. He can go to Mr. Hanmer’s now; he is just at the right age.”\n\nThis did not lead well towards the news that Mr. Garth was on the brink of losing ninety-two pounds and more. Fred was silent. “Young gentlemen who go to college are rather more costly than that,” Mrs. Garth innocently continued, pulling out the edging on a cap-border. “And Caleb thinks that Alfred will turn out a distinguished engineer: he wants to give the boy a good chance. There he is! I hear him coming in. We will go to him in the parlor, shall we?”\n\nWhen they entered the parlor Caleb had thrown down his hat and was seated at his desk.\n\n“What! Fred, my boy!” he said, in a tone of mild surprise, holding his pen still undipped; “you are here betimes.” But missing the usual expression of cheerful greeting in Fred’s face, he immediately added, “Is there anything up at home?—anything the matter?”\n\n“Yes, Mr. Garth, I am come to tell something that I am afraid will give you a bad opinion of me. I am come to tell you and Mrs. Garth that I can’t keep my word. I can’t find the money to meet the bill after all. I have been unfortunate; I have only got these fifty pounds towards the hundred and sixty.”\n\nWhile Fred was speaking, he had taken out the notes and laid them on the desk before Mr. Garth. He had burst forth at once with the plain fact, feeling boyishly miserable and without verbal resources. Mrs. Garth was mutely astonished, and looked at her husband for an explanation. Caleb blushed, and after a little pause said—\n\n“Oh, I didn’t tell you, Susan: I put my name to a bill for Fred; it was for a hundred and sixty pounds. He made sure he could meet it himself.”\n\nThere was an evident change in Mrs. Garth’s face, but it was like a change below the surface of water which remains smooth. She fixed her eyes on Fred, saying—\n\n“I suppose you have asked your father for the rest of the money and he has refused you.”\n\n“No,” said Fred, biting his lip, and speaking with more difficulty; “but I know it will be of no use to ask him; and unless it were of use, I should not like to mention Mr. Garth’s name in the matter.”\n\n“It has come at an unfortunate time,” said Caleb, in his hesitating way, looking down at the notes and nervously fingering the paper, “Christmas upon us—I’m rather hard up just now. You see, I have to cut out everything like a tailor with short measure. What can we do, Susan? I shall want every farthing we have in the bank. It’s a hundred and ten pounds, the deuce take it!”\n\n“I must give you the ninety-two pounds that I have put by for Alfred’s premium,” said Mrs. Garth, gravely and decisively, though a nice ear might have discerned a slight tremor in some of the words. “And I have no doubt that Mary has twenty pounds saved from her salary by this time. She will advance it.”\n\nMrs. Garth had not again looked at Fred, and was not in the least calculating what words she should use to cut him the most effectively. Like the eccentric woman she was, she was at present absorbed in considering what was to be done, and did not fancy that the end could be better achieved by bitter remarks or explosions. But she had made Fred feel for the first time something like the tooth of remorse. Curiously enough, his pain in the affair beforehand had consisted almost entirely in the sense that he must seem dishonorable, and sink in the opinion of the Garths: he had not occupied himself with the inconvenience and possible injury that his breach might occasion them, for this exercise of the imagination on other people’s needs is not common with hopeful young gentlemen. Indeed we are most of us brought up in the notion that the highest motive for not doing a wrong is something irrespective of the beings who would suffer the wrong. But at this moment he suddenly saw himself as a pitiful rascal who was robbing two women of their savings.\n\n“I shall certainly pay it all, Mrs. Garth—ultimately,” he stammered out.\n\n“Yes, ultimately,” said Mrs. Garth, who having a special dislike to fine words on ugly occasions, could not now repress an epigram. “But boys cannot well be apprenticed ultimately: they should be apprenticed at fifteen.” She had never been so little inclined to make excuses for Fred.\n\n“I was the most in the wrong, Susan,” said Caleb. “Fred made sure of finding the money. But I’d no business to be fingering bills. I suppose you have looked all round and tried all honest means?” he added, fixing his merciful gray eyes on Fred. Caleb was too delicate to specify Mr. Featherstone.\n\n“Yes, I have tried everything—I really have. I should have had a hundred and thirty pounds ready but for a misfortune with a horse which I was about to sell. My uncle had given me eighty pounds, and I paid away thirty with my old horse in order to get another which I was going to sell for eighty or more—I meant to go without a horse—but now it has turned out vicious and lamed itself. I wish I and the horses too had been at the devil, before I had brought this on you. There’s no one else I care so much for: you and Mrs. Garth have always been so kind to me. However, it’s no use saying that. You will always think me a rascal now.”\n\nFred turned round and hurried out of the room, conscious that he was getting rather womanish, and feeling confusedly that his being sorry was not of much use to the Garths. They could see him mount, and quickly pass through the gate.\n\n“I am disappointed in Fred Vincy,” said Mrs. Garth. “I would not have believed beforehand that he would have drawn you into his debts. I knew he was extravagant, but I did not think that he would be so mean as to hang his risks on his oldest friend, who could the least afford to lose.”\n\n“I was a fool, Susan.”\n\n“That you were,” said the wife, nodding and smiling. “But I should not have gone to publish it in the market-place. Why should you keep such things from me? It is just so with your buttons: you let them burst off without telling me, and go out with your wristband hanging. If I had only known I might have been ready with some better plan.”\n\n“You are sadly cut up, I know, Susan,” said Caleb, looking feelingly at her. “I can’t abide your losing the money you’ve scraped together for Alfred.”\n\n“It is very well that I had scraped it together; and it is you who will have to suffer, for you must teach the boy yourself. You must give up your bad habits. Some men take to drinking, and you have taken to working without pay. You must indulge yourself a little less in that. And you must ride over to Mary, and ask the child what money she has.”\n\nCaleb had pushed his chair back, and was leaning forward, shaking his head slowly, and fitting his finger-tips together with much nicety.\n\n“Poor Mary!” he said. “Susan,” he went on in a lowered tone, “I’m afraid she may be fond of Fred.”\n\n“Oh no! She always laughs at him; and he is not likely to think of her in any other than a brotherly way.”\n\nCaleb made no rejoinder, but presently lowered his spectacles, drew up his chair to the desk, and said, “Deuce take the bill—I wish it was at Hanover! These things are a sad interruption to business!”\n\nThe first part of this speech comprised his whole store of maledictory expression, and was uttered with a slight snarl easy to imagine. But it would be difficult to convey to those who never heard him utter the word “business,” the peculiar tone of fervid veneration, of religious regard, in which he wrapped it, as a consecrated symbol is wrapped in its gold-fringed linen.\n\nCaleb Garth often shook his head in meditation on the value, the indispensable might of that myriad-headed, myriad-handed labor by which the social body is fed, clothed, and housed. It had laid hold of his imagination in boyhood. The echoes of the great hammer where roof or keel were a-making, the signal-shouts of the workmen, the roar of the furnace, the thunder and plash of the engine, were a sublime music to him; the felling and lading of timber, and the huge trunk vibrating star-like in the distance along the highway, the crane at work on the wharf, the piled-up produce in warehouses, the precision and variety of muscular effort wherever exact work had to be turned out,—all these sights of his youth had acted on him as poetry without the aid of the poets, had made a philosophy for him without the aid of philosophers, a religion without the aid of theology. His early ambition had been to have as effective a share as possible in this sublime labor, which was peculiarly dignified by him with the name of “business;” and though he had only been a short time under a surveyor, and had been chiefly his own teacher, he knew more of land, building, and mining than most of the special men in the county.\n\nHis classification of human employments was rather crude, and, like the categories of more celebrated men, would not be acceptable in these advanced times. He divided them into “business, politics, preaching, learning, and amusement.” He had nothing to say against the last four; but he regarded them as a reverential pagan regarded other gods than his own. In the same way, he thought very well of all ranks, but he would not himself have liked to be of any rank in which he had not such close contact with “business” as to get often honorably decorated with marks of dust and mortar, the damp of the engine, or the sweet soil of the woods and fields. Though he had never regarded himself as other than an orthodox Christian, and would argue on prevenient grace if the subject were proposed to him, I think his virtual divinities were good practical schemes, accurate work, and the faithful completion of undertakings: his prince of darkness was a slack workman. But there was no spirit of denial in Caleb, and the world seemed so wondrous to him that he was ready to accept any number of systems, like any number of firmaments, if they did not obviously interfere with the best land-drainage, solid building, correct measuring, and judicious boring (for coal). In fact, he had a reverential soul with a strong practical intelligence. But he could not manage finance: he knew values well, but he had no keenness of imagination for monetary results in the shape of profit and loss: and having ascertained this to his cost, he determined to give up all forms of his beloved “business” which required that talent. He gave himself up entirely to the many kinds of work which he could do without handling capital, and was one of those precious men within his own district whom everybody would choose to work for them, because he did his work well, charged very little, and often declined to charge at all. It is no wonder, then, that the Garths were poor, and “lived in a small way.” However, they did not mind it.\n\n# CHAPTER XXV.\n\n“Love seeketh not itself to please, Nor for itself hath any care But for another gives its ease And builds a heaven in hell’s despair. . . . . . . . Love seeketh only self to please, To bind another to its delight, Joys in another’s loss of ease, And builds a hell in heaven’s despite.” —W. BLAKE: Songs of Experience.\n\nFred Vincy wanted to arrive at Stone Court when Mary could not expect him, and when his uncle was not downstairs: in that case she might be sitting alone in the wainscoted parlor. He left his horse in the yard to avoid making a noise on the gravel in front, and entered the parlor without other notice than the noise of the door-handle. Mary was in her usual corner, laughing over Mrs. Piozzi’s recollections of Johnson, and looked up with the fun still in her face. It gradually faded as she saw Fred approach her without speaking, and stand before her with his elbow on the mantel-piece, looking ill. She too was silent, only raising her eyes to him inquiringly.\n\n“Mary,” he began, “I am a good-for-nothing blackguard.”\n\n“I should think one of those epithets would do at a time,” said Mary, trying to smile, but feeling alarmed.\n\n“I know you will never think well of me any more. You will think me a liar. You will think me dishonest. You will think I didn’t care for you, or your father and mother. You always do make the worst of me, I know.”\n\n“I cannot deny that I shall think all that of you, Fred, if you give me good reasons. But please to tell me at once what you have done. I would rather know the painful truth than imagine it.”\n\n“I owed money—a hundred and sixty pounds. I asked your father to put his name to a bill. I thought it would not signify to him. I made sure of paying the money myself, and I have tried as hard as I could. And now, I have been so unlucky—a horse has turned out badly—I can only pay fifty pounds. And I can’t ask my father for the money: he would not give me a farthing. And my uncle gave me a hundred a little while ago. So what can I do? And now your father has no ready money to spare, and your mother will have to pay away her ninety-two pounds that she has saved, and she says your savings must go too. You see what a—”\n\n“Oh, poor mother, poor father!” said Mary, her eyes filling with tears, and a little sob rising which she tried to repress. She looked straight before her and took no notice of Fred, all the consequences at home becoming present to her. He too remained silent for some moments, feeling more miserable than ever. “I wouldn’t have hurt you for the world, Mary,” he said at last. “You can never forgive me.”\n\n“What does it matter whether I forgive you?” said Mary, passionately. “Would that make it any better for my mother to lose the money she has been earning by lessons for four years, that she might send Alfred to Mr. Hanmer’s? Should you think all that pleasant enough if I forgave you?”\n\n“Say what you like, Mary. I deserve it all.”\n\n“I don’t want to say anything,” said Mary, more quietly, “and my anger is of no use.” She dried her eyes, threw aside her book, rose and fetched her sewing.\n\nFred followed her with his eyes, hoping that they would meet hers, and in that way find access for his imploring penitence. But no! Mary could easily avoid looking upward.\n\n“I do care about your mother’s money going,” he said, when she was seated again and sewing quickly. “I wanted to ask you, Mary—don’t you think that Mr. Featherstone—if you were to tell him—tell him, I mean, about apprenticing Alfred—would advance the money?”\n\n“My family is not fond of begging, Fred. We would rather work for our money. Besides, you say that Mr. Featherstone has lately given you a hundred pounds. He rarely makes presents; he has never made presents to us. I am sure my father will not ask him for anything; and even if I chose to beg of him, it would be of no use.”\n\n“I am so miserable, Mary—if you knew how miserable I am, you would be sorry for me.”\n\n“There are other things to be more sorry for than that. But selfish people always think their own discomfort of more importance than anything else in the world. I see enough of that every day.”\n\n“It is hardly fair to call me selfish. If you knew what things other young men do, you would think me a good way off the worst.”\n\n“I know that people who spend a great deal of money on themselves without knowing how they shall pay, must be selfish. They are always thinking of what they can get for themselves, and not of what other people may lose.”\n\n“Any man may be unfortunate, Mary, and find himself unable to pay when he meant it. There is not a better man in the world than your father, and yet he got into trouble.”\n\n“How dare you make any comparison between my father and you, Fred?” said Mary, in a deep tone of indignation. “He never got into trouble by thinking of his own idle pleasures, but because he was always thinking of the work he was doing for other people. And he has fared hard, and worked hard to make good everybody’s loss.”\n\n“And you think that I shall never try to make good anything, Mary. It is not generous to believe the worst of a man. When you have got any power over him, I think you might try and use it to make him better; but that is what you never do. However, I’m going,” Fred ended, languidly. “I shall never speak to you about anything again. I’m very sorry for all the trouble I’ve caused—that’s all.”\n\nMary had dropped her work out of her hand and looked up. There is often something maternal even in a girlish love, and Mary’s hard experience had wrought her nature to an impressibility very different from that hard slight thing which we call girlishness. At Fred’s last words she felt an instantaneous pang, something like what a mother feels at the imagined sobs or cries of her naughty truant child, which may lose itself and get harm. And when, looking up, her eyes met his dull despairing glance, her pity for him surmounted her anger and all her other anxieties.\n\n“Oh, Fred, how ill you look! Sit down a moment. Don’t go yet. Let me tell uncle that you are here. He has been wondering that he has not seen you for a whole week.” Mary spoke hurriedly, saying the words that came first without knowing very well what they were, but saying them in a half-soothing half-beseeching tone, and rising as if to go away to Mr. Featherstone. Of course Fred felt as if the clouds had parted and a gleam had come: he moved and stood in her way.\n\n“Say one word, Mary, and I will do anything. Say you will not think the worst of me—will not give me up altogether.”\n\n“As if it were any pleasure to me to think ill of you,” said Mary, in a mournful tone. “As if it were not very painful to me to see you an idle frivolous creature. How can you bear to be so contemptible, when others are working and striving, and there are so many things to be done—how can you bear to be fit for nothing in the world that is useful? And with so much good in your disposition, Fred,—you might be worth a great deal.”\n\n“I will try to be anything you like, Mary, if you will say that you love me.”\n\n“I should be ashamed to say that I loved a man who must always be hanging on others, and reckoning on what they would do for him. What will you be when you are forty? Like Mr. Bowyer, I suppose—just as idle, living in Mrs. Beck’s front parlor—fat and shabby, hoping somebody will invite you to dinner—spending your morning in learning a comic song—oh no! learning a tune on the flute.”\n\nMary’s lips had begun to curl with a smile as soon as she had asked that question about Fred’s future (young souls are mobile), and before she ended, her face had its full illumination of fun. To him it was like the cessation of an ache that Mary could laugh at him, and with a passive sort of smile he tried to reach her hand; but she slipped away quickly towards the door and said, “I shall tell uncle. You must see him for a moment or two.”\n\nFred secretly felt that his future was guaranteed against the fulfilment of Mary’s sarcastic prophecies, apart from that “anything” which he was ready to do if she would define it. He never dared in Mary’s presence to approach the subject of his expectations from Mr. Featherstone, and she always ignored them, as if everything depended on himself. But if ever he actually came into the property, she must recognize the change in his position. All this passed through his mind somewhat languidly, before he went up to see his uncle. He stayed but a little while, excusing himself on the ground that he had a cold; and Mary did not reappear before he left the house. But as he rode home, he began to be more conscious of being ill, than of being melancholy.\n\nWhen Caleb Garth arrived at Stone Court soon after dusk, Mary was not surprised, although he seldom had leisure for paying her a visit, and was not at all fond of having to talk with Mr. Featherstone. The old man, on the other hand, felt himself ill at ease with a brother-in-law whom he could not annoy, who did not mind about being considered poor, had nothing to ask of him, and understood all kinds of farming and mining business better than he did. But Mary had felt sure that her parents would want to see her, and if her father had not come, she would have obtained leave to go home for an hour or two the next day. After discussing prices during tea with Mr. Featherstone, Caleb rose to bid him good-by, and said, “I want to speak to you, Mary.”\n\nShe took a candle into another large parlor, where there was no fire, and setting down the feeble light on the dark mahogany table, turned round to her father, and putting her arms round his neck kissed him with childish kisses which he delighted in,—the expression of his large brows softening as the expression of a great beautiful dog softens when it is caressed. Mary was his favorite child, and whatever Susan might say, and right as she was on all other subjects, Caleb thought it natural that Fred or any one else should think Mary more lovable than other girls.\n\n“I’ve got something to tell you, my dear,” said Caleb in his hesitating way. “No very good news; but then it might be worse.”\n\n“About money, father? I think I know what it is.”\n\n“Ay? how can that be? You see, I’ve been a bit of a fool again, and put my name to a bill, and now it comes to paying; and your mother has got to part with her savings, that’s the worst of it, and even they won’t quite make things even. We wanted a hundred and ten pounds: your mother has ninety-two, and I have none to spare in the bank; and she thinks that you have some savings.”\n\n“Oh yes; I have more than four-and-twenty pounds. I thought you would come, father, so I put it in my bag. See! beautiful white notes and gold.”\n\nMary took out the folded money from her reticule and put it into her father’s hand.\n\n“Well, but how—we only want eighteen—here, put the rest back, child,—but how did you know about it?” said Caleb, who, in his unconquerable indifference to money, was beginning to be chiefly concerned about the relation the affair might have to Mary’s affections.\n\n“Fred told me this morning.”\n\n“Ah! Did he come on purpose?”\n\n“Yes, I think so. He was a good deal distressed.”\n\n“I’m afraid Fred is not to be trusted, Mary,” said the father, with hesitating tenderness. “He means better than he acts, perhaps. But I should think it a pity for any body’s happiness to be wrapped up in him, and so would your mother.”\n\n“And so should I, father,” said Mary, not looking up, but putting the back of her father’s hand against her cheek.\n\n“I don’t want to pry, my dear. But I was afraid there might be something between you and Fred, and I wanted to caution you. You see, Mary”—here Caleb’s voice became more tender; he had been pushing his hat about on the table and looking at it, but finally he turned his eyes on his daughter—“a woman, let her be as good as she may, has got to put up with the life her husband makes for her. Your mother has had to put up with a good deal because of me.”\n\nMary turned the back of her father’s hand to her lips and smiled at him.\n\n“Well, well, nobody’s perfect, but”—here Mr. Garth shook his head to help out the inadequacy of words—“what I am thinking of is—what it must be for a wife when she’s never sure of her husband, when he hasn’t got a principle in him to make him more afraid of doing the wrong thing by others than of getting his own toes pinched. That’s the long and the short of it, Mary. Young folks may get fond of each other before they know what life is, and they may think it all holiday if they can only get together; but it soon turns into working day, my dear. However, you have more sense than most, and you haven’t been kept in cotton-wool: there may be no occasion for me to say this, but a father trembles for his daughter, and you are all by yourself here.”\n\n“Don’t fear for me, father,” said Mary, gravely meeting her father’s eyes; “Fred has always been very good to me; he is kind-hearted and affectionate, and not false, I think, with all his self-indulgence. But I will never engage myself to one who has no manly independence, and who goes on loitering away his time on the chance that others will provide for him. You and my mother have taught me too much pride for that.”\n\n“That’s right—that’s right. Then I am easy,” said Mr. Garth, taking up his hat. “But it’s hard to run away with your earnings, eh child.”\n\n“Father!” said Mary, in her deepest tone of remonstrance. “Take pocketfuls of love besides to them all at home,” was her last word before he closed the outer door on himself.\n\n“I suppose your father wanted your earnings,” said old Mr. Featherstone, with his usual power of unpleasant surmise, when Mary returned to him. “He makes but a tight fit, I reckon. You’re of age now; you ought to be saving for yourself.”\n\n“I consider my father and mother the best part of myself, sir,” said Mary, coldly.\n\nMr. Featherstone grunted: he could not deny that an ordinary sort of girl like her might be expected to be useful, so he thought of another rejoinder, disagreeable enough to be always apropos. “If Fred Vincy comes to-morrow, now, don’t you keep him chattering: let him come up to me.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI.\n\nHe beats me and I rail at him: O worthy satisfaction! would it were otherwise—that I could beat him while he railed at me.—Troilus and Cressida.\n\nBut Fred did not go to Stone Court the next day, for reasons that were quite peremptory. From those visits to unsanitary Houndsley streets in search of Diamond, he had brought back not only a bad bargain in horse-flesh, but the further misfortune of some ailment which for a day or two had deemed mere depression and headache, but which got so much worse when he returned from his visit to Stone Court that, going into the dining-room, he threw himself on the sofa, and in answer to his mother’s anxious question, said, “I feel very ill: I think you must send for Wrench.”\n\nWrench came, but did not apprehend anything serious, spoke of a “slight derangement,” and did not speak of coming again on the morrow. He had a due value for the Vincys’ house, but the wariest men are apt to be dulled by routine, and on worried mornings will sometimes go through their business with the zest of the daily bell-ringer. Mr. Wrench was a small, neat, bilious man, with a well-dressed wig: he had a laborious practice, an irascible temper, a lymphatic wife and seven children; and he was already rather late before setting out on a four-miles drive to meet Dr. Minchin on the other side of Tipton, the decease of Hicks, a rural practitioner, having increased Middlemarch practice in that direction. Great statesmen err, and why not small medical men? Mr. Wrench did not neglect sending the usual white parcels, which this time had black and drastic contents. Their effect was not alleviating to poor Fred, who, however, unwilling as he said to believe that he was “in for an illness,” rose at his usual easy hour the next morning and went down-stairs meaning to breakfast, but succeeded in nothing but in sitting and shivering by the fire. Mr. Wrench was again sent for, but was gone on his rounds, and Mrs. Vincy seeing her darling’s changed looks and general misery, began to cry and said she would send for Dr. Sprague.\n\n“Oh, nonsense, mother! It’s nothing,” said Fred, putting out his hot dry hand to her, “I shall soon be all right. I must have taken cold in that nasty damp ride.”\n\n“Mamma!” said Rosamond, who was seated near the window (the dining-room windows looked on that highly respectable street called Lowick Gate), “there is Mr. Lydgate, stopping to speak to some one. If I were you I would call him in. He has cured Ellen Bulstrode. They say he cures every one.”\n\nMrs. Vincy sprang to the window and opened it in an instant, thinking only of Fred and not of medical etiquette. Lydgate was only two yards off on the other side of some iron palisading, and turned round at the sudden sound of the sash, before she called to him. In two minutes he was in the room, and Rosamond went out, after waiting just long enough to show a pretty anxiety conflicting with her sense of what was becoming.\n\nLydgate had to hear a narrative in which Mrs. Vincy’s mind insisted with remarkable instinct on every point of minor importance, especially on what Mr. Wrench had said and had not said about coming again. That there might be an awkward affair with Wrench, Lydgate saw at once; but the case was serious enough to make him dismiss that consideration: he was convinced that Fred was in the pink-skinned stage of typhoid fever, and that he had taken just the wrong medicines. He must go to bed immediately, must have a regular nurse, and various appliances and precautions must be used, about which Lydgate was particular. Poor Mrs. Vincy’s terror at these indications of danger found vent in such words as came most easily. She thought it “very ill usage on the part of Mr. Wrench, who had attended their house so many years in preference to Mr. Peacock, though Mr. Peacock was equally a friend. Why Mr. Wrench should neglect her children more than others, she could not for the life of her understand. He had not neglected Mrs. Larcher’s when they had the measles, nor indeed would Mrs. Vincy have wished that he should. And if anything should happen—”\n\nHere poor Mrs. Vincy’s spirit quite broke down, and her Niobe throat and good-humored face were sadly convulsed. This was in the hall out of Fred’s hearing, but Rosamond had opened the drawing-room door, and now came forward anxiously. Lydgate apologized for Mr. Wrench, said that the symptoms yesterday might have been disguising, and that this form of fever was very equivocal in its beginnings: he would go immediately to the druggist’s and have a prescription made up in order to lose no time, but he would write to Mr. Wrench and tell him what had been done.\n\n“But you must come again—you must go on attending Fred. I can’t have my boy left to anybody who may come or not. I bear nobody ill-will, thank God, and Mr. Wrench saved me in the pleurisy, but he’d better have let me die—if—if—”\n\n“I will meet Mr. Wrench here, then, shall I?” said Lydgate, really believing that Wrench was not well prepared to deal wisely with a case of this kind.\n\n“Pray make that arrangement, Mr. Lydgate,” said Rosamond, coming to her mother’s aid, and supporting her arm to lead her away.\n\nWhen Mr. Vincy came home he was very angry with Wrench, and did not care if he never came into his house again. Lydgate should go on now, whether Wrench liked it or not. It was no joke to have fever in the house. Everybody must be sent to now, not to come to dinner on Thursday. And Pritchard needn’t get up any wine: brandy was the best thing against infection. “I shall drink brandy,” added Mr. Vincy, emphatically—as much as to say, this was not an occasion for firing with blank-cartridges. “He’s an uncommonly unfortunate lad, is Fred. He’d need have some luck by and by to make up for all this—else I don’t know who’d have an eldest son.”\n\n“Don’t say so, Vincy,” said the mother, with a quivering lip, “if you don’t want him to be taken from me.”\n\n“It will worret you to death, Lucy; that I can see,” said Mr. Vincy, more mildly. “However, Wrench shall know what I think of the matter.” (What Mr. Vincy thought confusedly was, that the fever might somehow have been hindered if Wrench had shown the proper solicitude about his—the Mayor’s—family.) “I’m the last man to give in to the cry about new doctors, or new parsons either—whether they’re Bulstrode’s men or not. But Wrench shall know what I think, take it as he will.”\n\nWrench did not take it at all well. Lydgate was as polite as he could be in his offhand way, but politeness in a man who has placed you at a disadvantage is only an additional exasperation, especially if he happens to have been an object of dislike beforehand. Country practitioners used to be an irritable species, susceptible on the point of honor; and Mr. Wrench was one of the most irritable among them. He did not refuse to meet Lydgate in the evening, but his temper was somewhat tried on the occasion. He had to hear Mrs. Vincy say—\n\n“Oh, Mr. Wrench, what have I ever done that you should use me so?— To go away, and never to come again! And my boy might have been stretched a corpse!”\n\nMr. Vincy, who had been keeping up a sharp fire on the enemy Infection, and was a good deal heated in consequence, started up when he heard Wrench come in, and went into the hall to let him know what he thought.\n\n“I’ll tell you what, Wrench, this is beyond a joke,” said the Mayor, who of late had had to rebuke offenders with an official air, and now broadened himself by putting his thumbs in his armholes. “To let fever get unawares into a house like this. There are some things that ought to be actionable, and are not so— that’s my opinion.”\n\nBut irrational reproaches were easier to bear than the sense of being instructed, or rather the sense that a younger man, like Lydgate, inwardly considered him in need of instruction, for “in point of fact,” Mr. Wrench afterwards said, Lydgate paraded flighty, foreign notions, which would not wear. He swallowed his ire for the moment, but he afterwards wrote to decline further attendance in the case. The house might be a good one, but Mr. Wrench was not going to truckle to anybody on a professional matter. He reflected, with much probability on his side, that Lydgate would by-and-by be caught tripping too, and that his ungentlemanly attempts to discredit the sale of drugs by his professional brethren, would by-and-by recoil on himself. He threw out biting remarks on Lydgate’s tricks, worthy only of a quack, to get himself a factitious reputation with credulous people. That cant about cures was never got up by sound practitioners.\n\nThis was a point on which Lydgate smarted as much as Wrench could desire. To be puffed by ignorance was not only humiliating, but perilous, and not more enviable than the reputation of the weather-prophet. He was impatient of the foolish expectations amidst which all work must be carried on, and likely enough to damage himself as much as Mr. Wrench could wish, by an unprofessional openness.\n\nHowever, Lydgate was installed as medical attendant on the Vincys, and the event was a subject of general conversation in Middlemarch. Some said, that the Vincys had behaved scandalously, that Mr. Vincy had threatened Wrench, and that Mrs. Vincy had accused him of poisoning her son. Others were of opinion that Mr. Lydgate’s passing by was providential, that he was wonderfully clever in fevers, and that Bulstrode was in the right to bring him forward. Many people believed that Lydgate’s coming to the town at all was really due to Bulstrode; and Mrs. Taft, who was always counting stitches and gathered her information in misleading fragments caught between the rows of her knitting, had got it into her head that Mr. Lydgate was a natural son of Bulstrode’s, a fact which seemed to justify her suspicions of evangelical laymen.\n\nShe one day communicated this piece of knowledge to Mrs. Farebrother, who did not fail to tell her son of it, observing—\n\n“I should not be surprised at anything in Bulstrode, but I should be sorry to think it of Mr. Lydgate.”\n\n“Why, mother,” said Mr. Farebrother, after an explosive laugh, “you know very well that Lydgate is of a good family in the North. He never heard of Bulstrode before he came here.”\n\n“That is satisfactory so far as Mr. Lydgate is concerned, Camden,” said the old lady, with an air of precision.—“But as to Bulstrode—the report may be true of some other son.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXVII.\n\nLet the high Muse chant loves Olympian: We are but mortals, and must sing of man.\n\nAn eminent philosopher among my friends, who can dignify even your ugly furniture by lifting it into the serene light of science, has shown me this pregnant little fact. Your pier-glass or extensive surface of polished steel made to be rubbed by a housemaid, will be minutely and multitudinously scratched in all directions; but place now against it a lighted candle as a centre of illumination, and lo! the scratches will seem to arrange themselves in a fine series of concentric circles round that little sun. It is demonstrable that the scratches are going everywhere impartially and it is only your candle which produces the flattering illusion of a concentric arrangement, its light falling with an exclusive optical selection. These things are a parable. The scratches are events, and the candle is the egoism of any person now absent—of Miss Vincy, for example. Rosamond had a Providence of her own who had kindly made her more charming than other girls, and who seemed to have arranged Fred’s illness and Mr. Wrench’s mistake in order to bring her and Lydgate within effective proximity. It would have been to contravene these arrangements if Rosamond had consented to go away to Stone Court or elsewhere, as her parents wished her to do, especially since Mr. Lydgate thought the precaution needless. Therefore, while Miss Morgan and the children were sent away to a farmhouse the morning after Fred’s illness had declared itself, Rosamond refused to leave papa and mamma.\n\nPoor mamma indeed was an object to touch any creature born of woman; and Mr. Vincy, who doted on his wife, was more alarmed on her account than on Fred’s. But for his insistence she would have taken no rest: her brightness was all bedimmed; unconscious of her costume which had always been so fresh and gay, she was like a sick bird with languid eye and plumage ruffled, her senses dulled to the sights and sounds that used most to interest her. Fred’s delirium, in which he seemed to be wandering out of her reach, tore her heart. After her first outburst against Mr. Wrench she went about very quietly: her one low cry was to Lydgate. She would follow him out of the room and put her hand on his arm moaning out, “Save my boy.” Once she pleaded, “He has always been good to me, Mr. Lydgate: he never had a hard word for his mother,”—as if poor Fred’s suffering were an accusation against him. All the deepest fibres of the mother’s memory were stirred, and the young man whose voice took a gentler tone when he spoke to her, was one with the babe whom she had loved, with a love new to her, before he was born.\n\n“I have good hope, Mrs. Vincy,” Lydgate would say. “Come down with me and let us talk about the food.” In that way he led her to the parlor where Rosamond was, and made a change for her, surprising her into taking some tea or broth which had been prepared for her. There was a constant understanding between him and Rosamond on these matters. He almost always saw her before going to the sickroom, and she appealed to him as to what she could do for mamma. Her presence of mind and adroitness in carrying out his hints were admirable, and it is not wonderful that the idea of seeing Rosamond began to mingle itself with his interest in the case. Especially when the critical stage was passed, and he began to feel confident of Fred’s recovery. In the more doubtful time, he had advised calling in Dr. Sprague (who, if he could, would rather have remained neutral on Wrench’s account); but after two consultations, the conduct of the case was left to Lydgate, and there was every reason to make him assiduous. Morning and evening he was at Mr. Vincy’s, and gradually the visits became cheerful as Fred became simply feeble, and lay not only in need of the utmost petting but conscious of it, so that Mrs. Vincy felt as if, after all, the illness had made a festival for her tenderness.\n\nBoth father and mother held it an added reason for good spirits, when old Mr. Featherstone sent messages by Lydgate, saying that Fred must make haste and get well, as he, Peter Featherstone, could not do without him, and missed his visits sadly. The old man himself was getting bedridden. Mrs. Vincy told these messages to Fred when he could listen, and he turned towards her his delicate, pinched face, from which all the thick blond hair had been cut away, and in which the eyes seemed to have got larger, yearning for some word about Mary—wondering what she felt about his illness. No word passed his lips; but “to hear with eyes belongs to love’s rare wit,” and the mother in the fulness of her heart not only divined Fred’s longing, but felt ready for any sacrifice in order to satisfy him.\n\n“If I can only see my boy strong again,” she said, in her loving folly; “and who knows?—perhaps master of Stone Court! and he can marry anybody he likes then.”\n\n“Not if they won’t have me, mother,” said Fred. The illness had made him childish, and tears came as he spoke.\n\n“Oh, take a bit of jelly, my dear,” said Mrs. Vincy, secretly incredulous of any such refusal.\n\nShe never left Fred’s side when her husband was not in the house, and thus Rosamond was in the unusual position of being much alone. Lydgate, naturally, never thought of staying long with her, yet it seemed that the brief impersonal conversations they had together were creating that peculiar intimacy which consists in shyness. They were obliged to look at each other in speaking, and somehow the looking could not be carried through as the matter of course which it really was. Lydgate began to feel this sort of consciousness unpleasant and one day looked down, or anywhere, like an ill-worked puppet. But this turned out badly: the next day, Rosamond looked down, and the consequence was that when their eyes met again, both were more conscious than before. There was no help for this in science, and as Lydgate did not want to flirt, there seemed to be no help for it in folly. It was therefore a relief when neighbors no longer considered the house in quarantine, and when the chances of seeing Rosamond alone were very much reduced.\n\nBut that intimacy of mutual embarrassment, in which each feels that the other is feeling something, having once existed, its effect is not to be done away with. Talk about the weather and other well-bred topics is apt to seem a hollow device, and behavior can hardly become easy unless it frankly recognizes a mutual fascination—which of course need not mean anything deep or serious. This was the way in which Rosamond and Lydgate slid gracefully into ease, and made their intercourse lively again. Visitors came and went as usual, there was once more music in the drawing-room, and all the extra hospitality of Mr. Vincy’s mayoralty returned. Lydgate, whenever he could, took his seat by Rosamond’s side, and lingered to hear her music, calling himself her captive—meaning, all the while, not to be her captive. The preposterousness of the notion that he could at once set up a satisfactory establishment as a married man was a sufficient guarantee against danger. This play at being a little in love was agreeable, and did not interfere with graver pursuits. Flirtation, after all, was not necessarily a singeing process. Rosamond, for her part, had never enjoyed the days so much in her life before: she was sure of being admired by some one worth captivating, and she did not distinguish flirtation from love, either in herself or in another. She seemed to be sailing with a fair wind just whither she would go, and her thoughts were much occupied with a handsome house in Lowick Gate which she hoped would by-and-by be vacant. She was quite determined, when she was married, to rid herself adroitly of all the visitors who were not agreeable to her at her father’s; and she imagined the drawing-room in her favorite house with various styles of furniture.\n\nCertainly her thoughts were much occupied with Lydgate himself; he seemed to her almost perfect: if he had known his notes so that his enchantment under her music had been less like an emotional elephant’s, and if he had been able to discriminate better the refinements of her taste in dress, she could hardly have mentioned a deficiency in him. How different he was from young Plymdale or Mr. Caius Larcher! Those young men had not a notion of French, and could speak on no subject with striking knowledge, except perhaps the dyeing and carrying trades, which of course they were ashamed to mention; they were Middlemarch gentry, elated with their silver-headed whips and satin stocks, but embarrassed in their manners, and timidly jocose: even Fred was above them, having at least the accent and manner of a university man. Whereas Lydgate was always listened to, bore himself with the careless politeness of conscious superiority, and seemed to have the right clothes on by a certain natural affinity, without ever having to think about them. Rosamond was proud when he entered the room, and when he approached her with a distinguishing smile, she had a delicious sense that she was the object of enviable homage. If Lydgate had been aware of all the pride he excited in that delicate bosom, he might have been just as well pleased as any other man, even the most densely ignorant of humoral pathology or fibrous tissue: he held it one of the prettiest attitudes of the feminine mind to adore a man’s pre-eminence without too precise a knowledge of what it consisted in. But Rosamond was not one of those helpless girls who betray themselves unawares, and whose behavior is awkwardly driven by their impulses, instead of being steered by wary grace and propriety. Do you imagine that her rapid forecast and rumination concerning house-furniture and society were ever discernible in her conversation, even with her mamma? On the contrary, she would have expressed the prettiest surprise and disapprobation if she had heard that another young lady had been detected in that immodest prematureness—indeed, would probably have disbelieved in its possibility. For Rosamond never showed any unbecoming knowledge, and was always that combination of correct sentiments, music, dancing, drawing, elegant note-writing, private album for extracted verse, and perfect blond loveliness, which made the irresistible woman for the doomed man of that date. Think no unfair evil of her, pray: she had no wicked plots, nothing sordid or mercenary; in fact, she never thought of money except as something necessary which other people would always provide. She was not in the habit of devising falsehoods, and if her statements were no direct clew to fact, why, they were not intended in that light—they were among her elegant accomplishments, intended to please. Nature had inspired many arts in finishing Mrs. Lemon’s favorite pupil, who by general consent (Fred’s excepted) was a rare compound of beauty, cleverness, and amiability.\n\nLydgate found it more and more agreeable to be with her, and there was no constraint now, there was a delightful interchange of influence in their eyes, and what they said had that superfluity of meaning for them, which is observable with some sense of flatness by a third person; still they had no interviews or asides from which a third person need have been excluded. In fact, they flirted; and Lydgate was secure in the belief that they did nothing else. If a man could not love and be wise, surely he could flirt and be wise at the same time? Really, the men in Middlemarch, except Mr. Farebrother, were great bores, and Lydgate did not care about commercial politics or cards: what was he to do for relaxation? He was often invited to the Bulstrodes’; but the girls there were hardly out of the schoolroom; and Mrs. Bulstrode’s naive way of conciliating piety and worldliness, the nothingness of this life and the desirability of cut glass, the consciousness at once of filthy rags and the best damask, was not a sufficient relief from the weight of her husband’s invariable seriousness. The Vincys’ house, with all its faults, was the pleasanter by contrast; besides, it nourished Rosamond—sweet to look at as a half-opened blush-rose, and adorned with accomplishments for the refined amusement of man.\n\nBut he made some enemies, other than medical, by his success with Miss Vincy. One evening he came into the drawing-room rather late, when several other visitors were there. The card-table had drawn off the elders, and Mr. Ned Plymdale (one of the good matches in Middlemarch, though not one of its leading minds) was in tête-à-tête with Rosamond. He had brought the last “Keepsake,” the gorgeous watered-silk publication which marked modern progress at that time; and he considered himself very fortunate that he could be the first to look over it with her, dwelling on the ladies and gentlemen with shiny copper-plate cheeks and copper-plate smiles, and pointing to comic verses as capital and sentimental stories as interesting. Rosamond was gracious, and Mr. Ned was satisfied that he had the very best thing in art and literature as a medium for “paying addresses”—the very thing to please a nice girl. He had also reasons, deep rather than ostensible, for being satisfied with his own appearance. To superficial observers his chin had too vanishing an aspect, looking as if it were being gradually reabsorbed. And it did indeed cause him some difficulty about the fit of his satin stocks, for which chins were at that time useful.\n\n“I think the Honorable Mrs. S. is something like you,” said Mr. Ned. He kept the book open at the bewitching portrait, and looked at it rather languishingly.\n\n“Her back is very large; she seems to have sat for that,” said Rosamond, not meaning any satire, but thinking how red young Plymdale’s hands were, and wondering why Lydgate did not come. She went on with her tatting all the while.\n\n“I did not say she was as beautiful as you are,” said Mr. Ned, venturing to look from the portrait to its rival.\n\n“I suspect you of being an adroit flatterer,” said Rosamond, feeling sure that she should have to reject this young gentleman a second time.\n\nBut now Lydgate came in; the book was closed before he reached Rosamond’s corner, and as he took his seat with easy confidence on the other side of her, young Plymdale’s jaw fell like a barometer towards the cheerless side of change. Rosamond enjoyed not only Lydgate’s presence but its effect: she liked to excite jealousy.\n\n“What a late comer you are!” she said, as they shook hands. “Mamma had given you up a little while ago. How do you find Fred?”\n\n“As usual; going on well, but slowly. I want him to go away—to Stone Court, for example. But your mamma seems to have some objection.”\n\n“Poor fellow!” said Rosamond, prettily. “You will see Fred so changed,” she added, turning to the other suitor; “we have looked to Mr. Lydgate as our guardian angel during this illness.”\n\nMr. Ned smiled nervously, while Lydgate, drawing the “Keepsake” towards him and opening it, gave a short scornful laugh and tossed up his chin, as if in wonderment at human folly.\n\n“What are you laughing at so profanely?” said Rosamond, with bland neutrality.\n\n“I wonder which would turn out to be the silliest—the engravings or the writing here,” said Lydgate, in his most convinced tone, while he turned over the pages quickly, seeming to see all through the book in no time, and showing his large white hands to much advantage, as Rosamond thought. “Do look at this bridegroom coming out of church: did you ever see such a ‘sugared invention’—as the Elizabethans used to say? Did any haberdasher ever look so smirking? Yet I will answer for it the story makes him one of the first gentlemen in the land.”\n\n“You are so severe, I am frightened at you,” said Rosamond, keeping her amusement duly moderate. Poor young Plymdale had lingered with admiration over this very engraving, and his spirit was stirred.\n\n“There are a great many celebrated people writing in the ‘Keepsake,’ at all events,” he said, in a tone at once piqued and timid. “This is the first time I have heard it called silly.”\n\n“I think I shall turn round on you and accuse you of being a Goth,” said Rosamond, looking at Lydgate with a smile. “I suspect you know nothing about Lady Blessington and L. E. L.” Rosamond herself was not without relish for these writers, but she did not readily commit herself by admiration, and was alive to the slightest hint that anything was not, according to Lydgate, in the very highest taste.\n\n“But Sir Walter Scott—I suppose Mr. Lydgate knows him,” said young Plymdale, a little cheered by this advantage.\n\n“Oh, I read no literature now,” said Lydgate, shutting the book, and pushing it away. “I read so much when I was a lad, that I suppose it will last me all my life. I used to know Scott’s poems by heart.”\n\n“I should like to know when you left off,” said Rosamond, “because then I might be sure that I knew something which you did not know.”\n\n“Mr. Lydgate would say that was not worth knowing,” said Mr. Ned, purposely caustic.\n\n“On the contrary,” said Lydgate, showing no smart; but smiling with exasperating confidence at Rosamond. “It would be worth knowing by the fact that Miss Vincy could tell it me.”\n\nYoung Plymdale soon went to look at the whist-playing, thinking that Lydgate was one of the most conceited, unpleasant fellows it had ever been his ill-fortune to meet.\n\n“How rash you are!” said Rosamond, inwardly delighted. “Do you see that you have given offence?”\n\n“What! is it Mr. Plymdale’s book? I am sorry. I didn’t think about it.”\n\n“I shall begin to admit what you said of yourself when you first came here—that you are a bear, and want teaching by the birds.”\n\n“Well, there is a bird who can teach me what she will. Don’t I listen to her willingly?”\n\nTo Rosamond it seemed as if she and Lydgate were as good as engaged. That they were some time to be engaged had long been an idea in her mind; and ideas, we know, tend to a more solid kind of existence, the necessary materials being at hand. It is true, Lydgate had the counter-idea of remaining unengaged; but this was a mere negative, a shadow cast by other resolves which themselves were capable of shrinking. Circumstance was almost sure to be on the side of Rosamond’s idea, which had a shaping activity and looked through watchful blue eyes, whereas Lydgate’s lay blind and unconcerned as a jelly-fish which gets melted without knowing it.\n\nThat evening when he went home, he looked at his phials to see how a process of maceration was going on, with undisturbed interest; and he wrote out his daily notes with as much precision as usual. The reveries from which it was difficult for him to detach himself were ideal constructions of something else than Rosamond’s virtues, and the primitive tissue was still his fair unknown. Moreover, he was beginning to feel some zest for the growing though half-suppressed feud between him and the other medical men, which was likely to become more manifest, now that Bulstrode’s method of managing the new hospital was about to be declared; and there were various inspiriting signs that his non-acceptance by some of Peacock’s patients might be counterbalanced by the impression he had produced in other quarters. Only a few days later, when he had happened to overtake Rosamond on the Lowick road and had got down from his horse to walk by her side until he had quite protected her from a passing drove, he had been stopped by a servant on horseback with a message calling him in to a house of some importance where Peacock had never attended; and it was the second instance of this kind. The servant was Sir James Chettam’s, and the house was Lowick Manor.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII.\n\n1st Gent. All times are good to seek your wedded home Bringing a mutual delight.\n\n2d Gent. Why, true. The calendar hath not an evil day For souls made one by love, and even death Were sweetness, if it came like rolling waves While they two clasped each other, and foresaw No life apart.\n\nMr. and Mrs. Casaubon, returning from their wedding journey, arrived at Lowick Manor in the middle of January. A light snow was falling as they descended at the door, and in the morning, when Dorothea passed from her dressing-room into the blue-green boudoir that we know of, she saw the long avenue of limes lifting their trunks from a white earth, and spreading white branches against the dun and motionless sky. The distant flat shrank in uniform whiteness and low-hanging uniformity of cloud. The very furniture in the room seemed to have shrunk since she saw it before: the stag in the tapestry looked more like a ghost in his ghostly blue-green world; the volumes of polite literature in the bookcase looked more like immovable imitations of books. The bright fire of dry oak-boughs burning on the logs seemed an incongruous renewal of life and glow—like the figure of Dorothea herself as she entered carrying the red-leather cases containing the cameos for Celia.\n\nShe was glowing from her morning toilet as only healthful youth can glow: there was gem-like brightness on her coiled hair and in her hazel eyes; there was warm red life in her lips; her throat had a breathing whiteness above the differing white of the fur which itself seemed to wind about her neck and cling down her blue-gray pelisse with a tenderness gathered from her own, a sentient commingled innocence which kept its loveliness against the crystalline purity of the outdoor snow. As she laid the cameo-cases on the table in the bow-window, she unconsciously kept her hands on them, immediately absorbed in looking out on the still, white enclosure which made her visible world.\n\nMr. Casaubon, who had risen early complaining of palpitation, was in the library giving audience to his curate Mr. Tucker. By-and-by Celia would come in her quality of bridesmaid as well as sister, and through the next weeks there would be wedding visits received and given; all in continuance of that transitional life understood to correspond with the excitement of bridal felicity, and keeping up the sense of busy ineffectiveness, as of a dream which the dreamer begins to suspect. The duties of her married life, contemplated as so great beforehand, seemed to be shrinking with the furniture and the white vapor-walled landscape. The clear heights where she expected to walk in full communion had become difficult to see even in her imagination; the delicious repose of the soul on a complete superior had been shaken into uneasy effort and alarmed with dim presentiment. When would the days begin of that active wifely devotion which was to strengthen her husband’s life and exalt her own? Never perhaps, as she had preconceived them; but somehow—still somehow. In this solemnly pledged union of her life, duty would present itself in some new form of inspiration and give a new meaning to wifely love.\n\nMeanwhile there was the snow and the low arch of dun vapor—there was the stifling oppression of that gentlewoman’s world, where everything was done for her and none asked for her aid—where the sense of connection with a manifold pregnant existence had to be kept up painfully as an inward vision, instead of coming from without in claims that would have shaped her energies.— “What shall I do?” “Whatever you please, my dear:” that had been her brief history since she had left off learning morning lessons and practising silly rhythms on the hated piano. Marriage, which was to bring guidance into worthy and imperative occupation, had not yet freed her from the gentlewoman’s oppressive liberty: it had not even filled her leisure with the ruminant joy of unchecked tenderness. Her blooming full-pulsed youth stood there in a moral imprisonment which made itself one with the chill, colorless, narrowed landscape, with the shrunken furniture, the never-read books, and the ghostly stag in a pale fantastic world that seemed to be vanishing from the daylight.\n\nIn the first minutes when Dorothea looked out she felt nothing but the dreary oppression; then came a keen remembrance, and turning away from the window she walked round the room. The ideas and hopes which were living in her mind when she first saw this room nearly three months before were present now only as memories: she judged them as we judge transient and departed things. All existence seemed to beat with a lower pulse than her own, and her religious faith was a solitary cry, the struggle out of a nightmare in which every object was withering and shrinking away from her. Each remembered thing in the room was disenchanted, was deadened as an unlit transparency, till her wandering gaze came to the group of miniatures, and there at last she saw something which had gathered new breath and meaning: it was the miniature of Mr. Casaubon’s aunt Julia, who had made the unfortunate marriage—of Will Ladislaw’s grandmother. Dorothea could fancy that it was alive now—the delicate woman’s face which yet had a headstrong look, a peculiarity difficult to interpret. Was it only her friends who thought her marriage unfortunate? or did she herself find it out to be a mistake, and taste the salt bitterness of her tears in the merciful silence of the night? What breadths of experience Dorothea seemed to have passed over since she first looked at this miniature! She felt a new companionship with it, as if it had an ear for her and could see how she was looking at it. Here was a woman who had known some difficulty about marriage. Nay, the colors deepened, the lips and chin seemed to get larger, the hair and eyes seemed to be sending out light, the face was masculine and beamed on her with that full gaze which tells her on whom it falls that she is too interesting for the slightest movement of her eyelid to pass unnoticed and uninterpreted. The vivid presentation came like a pleasant glow to Dorothea: she felt herself smiling, and turning from the miniature sat down and looked up as if she were again talking to a figure in front of her. But the smile disappeared as she went on meditating, and at last she said aloud—\n\n“Oh, it was cruel to speak so! How sad—how dreadful!”\n\nShe rose quickly and went out of the room, hurrying along the corridor, with the irresistible impulse to go and see her husband and inquire if she could do anything for him. Perhaps Mr. Tucker was gone and Mr. Casaubon was alone in the library. She felt as if all her morning’s gloom would vanish if she could see her husband glad because of her presence.\n\nBut when she reached the head of the dark oak there was Celia coming up, and below there was Mr. Brooke, exchanging welcomes and congratulations with Mr. Casaubon.\n\n“Dodo!” said Celia, in her quiet staccato; then kissed her sister, whose arms encircled her, and said no more. I think they both cried a little in a furtive manner, while Dorothea ran down-stairs to greet her uncle.\n\n“I need not ask how you are, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, after kissing her forehead. “Rome has agreed with you, I see—happiness, frescos, the antique—that sort of thing. Well, it’s very pleasant to have you back again, and you understand all about art now, eh? But Casaubon is a little pale, I tell him—a little pale, you know. Studying hard in his holidays is carrying it rather too far. I overdid it at one time”—Mr. Brooke still held Dorothea’s hand, but had turned his face to Mr. Casaubon—“about topography, ruins, temples—I thought I had a clew, but I saw it would carry me too far, and nothing might come of it. You may go any length in that sort of thing, and nothing may come of it, you know.”\n\nDorothea’s eyes also were turned up to her husband’s face with some anxiety at the idea that those who saw him afresh after absence might be aware of signs which she had not noticed.\n\n“Nothing to alarm you, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, observing her expression. “A little English beef and mutton will soon make a difference. It was all very well to look pale, sitting for the portrait of Aquinas, you know—we got your letter just in time. But Aquinas, now—he was a little too subtle, wasn’t he? Does anybody read Aquinas?”\n\n“He is not indeed an author adapted to superficial minds,” said Mr. Casaubon, meeting these timely questions with dignified patience.\n\n“You would like coffee in your own room, uncle?” said Dorothea, coming to the rescue.\n\n“Yes; and you must go to Celia: she has great news to tell you, you know. I leave it all to her.”\n\nThe blue-green boudoir looked much more cheerful when Celia was seated there in a pelisse exactly like her sister’s, surveying the cameos with a placid satisfaction, while the conversation passed on to other topics.\n\n“Do you think it nice to go to Rome on a wedding journey?” said Celia, with her ready delicate blush which Dorothea was used to on the smallest occasions.\n\n“It would not suit all—not you, dear, for example,” said Dorothea, quietly. No one would ever know what she thought of a wedding journey to Rome.\n\n“Mrs. Cadwallader says it is nonsense, people going a long journey when they are married. She says they get tired to death of each other, and can’t quarrel comfortably, as they would at home. And Lady Chettam says she went to Bath.” Celia’s color changed again and again—seemed\n\n“To come and go with tidings from the heart, As it a running messenger had been.”\n\nIt must mean more than Celia’s blushing usually did.\n\n“Celia! has something happened?” said Dorothea, in a tone full of sisterly feeling. “Have you really any great news to tell me?”\n\n“It was because you went away, Dodo. Then there was nobody but me for Sir James to talk to,” said Celia, with a certain roguishness in her eyes.\n\n“I understand. It is as I used to hope and believe,” said Dorothea, taking her sister’s face between her hands, and looking at her half anxiously. Celia’s marriage seemed more serious than it used to do.\n\n“It was only three days ago,” said Celia. “And Lady Chettam is very kind.”\n\n“And you are very happy?”\n\n“Yes. We are not going to be married yet. Because every thing is to be got ready. And I don’t want to be married so very soon, because I think it is nice to be engaged. And we shall be married all our lives after.”\n\n“I do believe you could not marry better, Kitty. Sir James is a good, honorable man,” said Dorothea, warmly.\n\n“He has gone on with the cottages, Dodo. He will tell you about them when he comes. Shall you be glad to see him?”\n\n“Of course I shall. How can you ask me?”\n\n“Only I was afraid you would be getting so learned,” said Celia, regarding Mr. Casaubon’s learning as a kind of damp which might in due time saturate a neighboring body.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX.\n\nI found that no genius in another could please me. My unfortunate paradoxes had entirely dried up that source of comfort.—GOLDSMITH.\n\nOne morning, some weeks after her arrival at Lowick, Dorothea—but why always Dorothea? Was her point of view the only possible one with regard to this marriage? I protest against all our interest, all our effort at understanding being given to the young skins that look blooming in spite of trouble; for these too will get faded, and will know the older and more eating griefs which we are helping to neglect. In spite of the blinking eyes and white moles objectionable to Celia, and the want of muscular curve which was morally painful to Sir James, Mr. Casaubon had an intense consciousness within him, and was spiritually a-hungered like the rest of us. He had done nothing exceptional in marrying—nothing but what society sanctions, and considers an occasion for wreaths and bouquets. It had occurred to him that he must not any longer defer his intention of matrimony, and he had reflected that in taking a wife, a man of good position should expect and carefully choose a blooming young lady—the younger the better, because more educable and submissive—of a rank equal to his own, of religious principles, virtuous disposition, and good understanding. On such a young lady he would make handsome settlements, and he would neglect no arrangement for her happiness: in return, he should receive family pleasures and leave behind him that copy of himself which seemed so urgently required of a man—to the sonneteers of the sixteenth century. Times had altered since then, and no sonneteer had insisted on Mr. Casaubon’s leaving a copy of himself; moreover, he had not yet succeeded in issuing copies of his mythological key; but he had always intended to acquit himself by marriage, and the sense that he was fast leaving the years behind him, that the world was getting dimmer and that he felt lonely, was a reason to him for losing no more time in overtaking domestic delights before they too were left behind by the years.\n\nAnd when he had seen Dorothea he believed that he had found even more than he demanded: she might really be such a helpmate to him as would enable him to dispense with a hired secretary, an aid which Mr. Casaubon had never yet employed and had a suspicious dread of. (Mr. Casaubon was nervously conscious that he was expected to manifest a powerful mind.) Providence, in its kindness, had supplied him with the wife he needed. A wife, a modest young lady, with the purely appreciative, unambitious abilities of her sex, is sure to think her husband’s mind powerful. Whether Providence had taken equal care of Miss Brooke in presenting her with Mr. Casaubon was an idea which could hardly occur to him. Society never made the preposterous demand that a man should think as much about his own qualifications for making a charming girl happy as he thinks of hers for making himself happy. As if a man could choose not only his wife but his wife’s husband! Or as if he were bound to provide charms for his posterity in his own person!— When Dorothea accepted him with effusion, that was only natural; and Mr. Casaubon believed that his happiness was going to begin.\n\nHe had not had much foretaste of happiness in his previous life. To know intense joy without a strong bodily frame, one must have an enthusiastic soul. Mr. Casaubon had never had a strong bodily frame, and his soul was sensitive without being enthusiastic: it was too languid to thrill out of self-consciousness into passionate delight; it went on fluttering in the swampy ground where it was hatched, thinking of its wings and never flying. His experience was of that pitiable kind which shrinks from pity, and fears most of all that it should be known: it was that proud narrow sensitiveness which has not mass enough to spare for transformation into sympathy, and quivers thread-like in small currents of self-preoccupation or at best of an egoistic scrupulosity. And Mr. Casaubon had many scruples: he was capable of a severe self-restraint; he was resolute in being a man of honor according to the code; he would be unimpeachable by any recognized opinion. In conduct these ends had been attained; but the difficulty of making his Key to all Mythologies unimpeachable weighed like lead upon his mind; and the pamphlets—or “Parerga” as he called them—by which he tested his public and deposited small monumental records of his march, were far from having been seen in all their significance. He suspected the Archdeacon of not having read them; he was in painful doubt as to what was really thought of them by the leading minds of Brasenose, and bitterly convinced that his old acquaintance Carp had been the writer of that depreciatory recension which was kept locked in a small drawer of Mr. Casaubon’s desk, and also in a dark closet of his verbal memory. These were heavy impressions to struggle against, and brought that melancholy embitterment which is the consequence of all excessive claim: even his religious faith wavered with his wavering trust in his own authorship, and the consolations of the Christian hope in immortality seemed to lean on the immortality of the still unwritten Key to all Mythologies. For my part I am very sorry for him. It is an uneasy lot at best, to be what we call highly taught and yet not to enjoy: to be present at this great spectacle of life and never to be liberated from a small hungry shivering self—never to be fully possessed by the glory we behold, never to have our consciousness rapturously transformed into the vividness of a thought, the ardor of a passion, the energy of an action, but always to be scholarly and uninspired, ambitious and timid, scrupulous and dim-sighted. Becoming a dean or even a bishop would make little difference, I fear, to Mr. Casaubon’s uneasiness. Doubtless some ancient Greek has observed that behind the big mask and the speaking-trumpet, there must always be our poor little eyes peeping as usual and our timorous lips more or less under anxious control.\n\nTo this mental estate mapped out a quarter of a century before, to sensibilities thus fenced in, Mr. Casaubon had thought of annexing happiness with a lovely young bride; but even before marriage, as we have seen, he found himself under a new depression in the consciousness that the new bliss was not blissful to him. Inclination yearned back to its old, easier custom. And the deeper he went in domesticity the more did the sense of acquitting himself and acting with propriety predominate over any other satisfaction. Marriage, like religion and erudition, nay, like authorship itself, was fated to become an outward requirement, and Edward Casaubon was bent on fulfilling unimpeachably all requirements. Even drawing Dorothea into use in his study, according to his own intention before marriage, was an effort which he was always tempted to defer, and but for her pleading insistence it might never have begun. But she had succeeded in making it a matter of course that she should take her place at an early hour in the library and have work either of reading aloud or copying assigned her. The work had been easier to define because Mr. Casaubon had adopted an immediate intention: there was to be a new Parergon, a small monograph on some lately traced indications concerning the Egyptian mysteries whereby certain assertions of Warburton’s could be corrected. References were extensive even here, but not altogether shoreless; and sentences were actually to be written in the shape wherein they would be scanned by Brasenose and a less formidable posterity. These minor monumental productions were always exciting to Mr. Casaubon; digestion was made difficult by the interference of citations, or by the rivalry of dialectical phrases ringing against each other in his brain. And from the first there was to be a Latin dedication about which everything was uncertain except that it was not to be addressed to Carp: it was a poisonous regret to Mr. Casaubon that he had once addressed a dedication to Carp in which he had numbered that member of the animal kingdom among the viros nullo ævo perituros, a mistake which would infallibly lay the dedicator open to ridicule in the next age, and might even be chuckled over by Pike and Tench in the present.\n\nThus Mr. Casaubon was in one of his busiest epochs, and as I began to say a little while ago, Dorothea joined him early in the library where he had breakfasted alone. Celia at this time was on a second visit to Lowick, probably the last before her marriage, and was in the drawing-room expecting Sir James.\n\nDorothea had learned to read the signs of her husband’s mood, and she saw that the morning had become more foggy there during the last hour. She was going silently to her desk when he said, in that distant tone which implied that he was discharging a disagreeable duty—\n\n“Dorothea, here is a letter for you, which was enclosed in one addressed to me.”\n\nIt was a letter of two pages, and she immediately looked at the signature.\n\n“Mr. Ladislaw! What can he have to say to me?” she exclaimed, in a tone of pleased surprise. “But,” she added, looking at Mr. Casaubon, “I can imagine what he has written to you about.”\n\n“You can, if you please, read the letter,” said Mr. Casaubon, severely pointing to it with his pen, and not looking at her. “But I may as well say beforehand, that I must decline the proposal it contains to pay a visit here. I trust I may be excused for desiring an interval of complete freedom from such distractions as have been hitherto inevitable, and especially from guests whose desultory vivacity makes their presence a fatigue.”\n\nThere had been no clashing of temper between Dorothea and her husband since that little explosion in Rome, which had left such strong traces in her mind that it had been easier ever since to quell emotion than to incur the consequence of venting it. But this ill-tempered anticipation that she could desire visits which might be disagreeable to her husband, this gratuitous defence of himself against selfish complaint on her part, was too sharp a sting to be meditated on until after it had been resented. Dorothea had thought that she could have been patient with John Milton, but she had never imagined him behaving in this way; and for a moment Mr. Casaubon seemed to be stupidly undiscerning and odiously unjust. Pity, that “new-born babe” which was by-and-by to rule many a storm within her, did not “stride the blast” on this occasion. With her first words, uttered in a tone that shook him, she startled Mr. Casaubon into looking at her, and meeting the flash of her eyes.\n\n“Why do you attribute to me a wish for anything that would annoy you? You speak to me as if I were something you had to contend against. Wait at least till I appear to consult my own pleasure apart from yours.”\n\n“Dorothea, you are hasty,” answered Mr. Casaubon, nervously.\n\nDecidedly, this woman was too young to be on the formidable level of wifehood—unless she had been pale and featureless and taken everything for granted.\n\n“I think it was you who were first hasty in your false suppositions about my feeling,” said Dorothea, in the same tone. The fire was not dissipated yet, and she thought it was ignoble in her husband not to apologize to her.\n\n“We will, if you please, say no more on this subject, Dorothea. I have neither leisure nor energy for this kind of debate.”\n\nHere Mr. Casaubon dipped his pen and made as if he would return to his writing, though his hand trembled so much that the words seemed to be written in an unknown character. There are answers which, in turning away wrath, only send it to the other end of the room, and to have a discussion coolly waived when you feel that justice is all on your own side is even more exasperating in marriage than in philosophy.\n\nDorothea left Ladislaw’s two letters unread on her husband’s writing-table and went to her own place, the scorn and indignation within her rejecting the reading of these letters, just as we hurl away any trash towards which we seem to have been suspected of mean cupidity. She did not in the least divine the subtle sources of her husband’s bad temper about these letters: she only knew that they had caused him to offend her. She began to work at once, and her hand did not tremble; on the contrary, in writing out the quotations which had been given to her the day before, she felt that she was forming her letters beautifully, and it seemed to her that she saw the construction of the Latin she was copying, and which she was beginning to understand, more clearly than usual. In her indignation there was a sense of superiority, but it went out for the present in firmness of stroke, and did not compress itself into an inward articulate voice pronouncing the once “affable archangel” a poor creature.\n\nThere had been this apparent quiet for half an hour, and Dorothea had not looked away from her own table, when she heard the loud bang of a book on the floor, and turning quickly saw Mr. Casaubon on the library steps clinging forward as if he were in some bodily distress. She started up and bounded towards him in an instant: he was evidently in great straits for breath. Jumping on a stool she got close to his elbow and said with her whole soul melted into tender alarm—\n\n“Can you lean on me, dear?”\n\nHe was still for two or three minutes, which seemed endless to her, unable to speak or move, gasping for breath. When at last he descended the three steps and fell backward in the large chair which Dorothea had drawn close to the foot of the ladder, he no longer gasped but seemed helpless and about to faint. Dorothea rang the bell violently, and presently Mr. Casaubon was helped to the couch: he did not faint, and was gradually reviving, when Sir James Chettam came in, having been met in the hall with the news that Mr. Casaubon had “had a fit in the library.”\n\n“Good God! this is just what might have been expected,” was his immediate thought. If his prophetic soul had been urged to particularize, it seemed to him that “fits” would have been the definite expression alighted upon. He asked his informant, the butler, whether the doctor had been sent for. The butler never knew his master to want the doctor before; but would it not be right to send for a physician?\n\nWhen Sir James entered the library, however, Mr. Casaubon could make some signs of his usual politeness, and Dorothea, who in the reaction from her first terror had been kneeling and sobbing by his side now rose and herself proposed that some one should ride off for a medical man.\n\n“I recommend you to send for Lydgate,” said Sir James. “My mother has called him in, and she has found him uncommonly clever. She has had a poor opinion of the physicians since my father’s death.”\n\nDorothea appealed to her husband, and he made a silent sign of approval. So Mr. Lydgate was sent for and he came wonderfully soon, for the messenger, who was Sir James Chettam’s man and knew Mr. Lydgate, met him leading his horse along the Lowick road and giving his arm to Miss Vincy.\n\nCelia, in the drawing-room, had known nothing of the trouble till Sir James told her of it. After Dorothea’s account, he no longer considered the illness a fit, but still something “of that nature.”\n\n“Poor dear Dodo—how dreadful!” said Celia, feeling as much grieved as her own perfect happiness would allow. Her little hands were clasped, and enclosed by Sir James’s as a bud is enfolded by a liberal calyx. “It is very shocking that Mr. Casaubon should be ill; but I never did like him. And I think he is not half fond enough of Dorothea; and he ought to be, for I am sure no one else would have had him—do you think they would?”\n\n“I always thought it a horrible sacrifice of your sister,” said Sir James.\n\n“Yes. But poor Dodo never did do what other people do, and I think she never will.”\n\n“She is a noble creature,” said the loyal-hearted Sir James. He had just had a fresh impression of this kind, as he had seen Dorothea stretching her tender arm under her husband’s neck and looking at him with unspeakable sorrow. He did not know how much penitence there was in the sorrow.\n\n“Yes,” said Celia, thinking it was very well for Sir James to say so, but he would not have been comfortable with Dodo. “Shall I go to her? Could I help her, do you think?”\n\n“I think it would be well for you just to go and see her before Lydgate comes,” said Sir James, magnanimously. “Only don’t stay long.”\n\nWhile Celia was gone he walked up and down remembering what he had originally felt about Dorothea’s engagement, and feeling a revival of his disgust at Mr. Brooke’s indifference. If Cadwallader—if every one else had regarded the affair as he, Sir James, had done, the marriage might have been hindered. It was wicked to let a young girl blindly decide her fate in that way, without any effort to save her. Sir James had long ceased to have any regrets on his own account: his heart was satisfied with his engagement to Celia. But he had a chivalrous nature (was not the disinterested service of woman among the ideal glories of old chivalry?): his disregarded love had not turned to bitterness; its death had made sweet odors—floating memories that clung with a consecrating effect to Dorothea. He could remain her brotherly friend, interpreting her actions with generous trustfulness.\n\nQui veut délasser hors de propos, lasse.—PASCAL.\nMr. Casaubon had no second attack of equal severity with the first, and in a few days began to recover his usual condition. But Lydgate seemed to think the case worth a great deal of attention. He not only used his stethoscope (which had not become a matter of course in practice at that time), but sat quietly by his patient and watched him. To Mr. Casaubon’s questions about himself, he replied that the source of the illness was the common error of intellectual men—a too eager and monotonous application: the remedy was, to be satisfied with moderate work, and to seek variety of relaxation. Mr. Brooke, who sat by on one occasion, suggested that Mr. Casaubon should go fishing, as Cadwallader did, and have a turning-room, make toys, table-legs, and that kind of thing.\n\n“In short, you recommend me to anticipate the arrival of my second childhood,” said poor Mr. Casaubon, with some bitterness. “These things,” he added, looking at Lydgate, “would be to me such relaxation as tow-picking is to prisoners in a house of correction.”\n\n“I confess,” said Lydgate, smiling, “amusement is rather an unsatisfactory prescription. It is something like telling people to keep up their spirits. Perhaps I had better say, that you must submit to be mildly bored rather than to go on working.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Mr. Brooke. “Get Dorothea to play backgammon with you in the evenings. And shuttlecock, now—I don’t know a finer game than shuttlecock for the daytime. I remember it all the fashion. To be sure, your eyes might not stand that, Casaubon. But you must unbend, you know. Why, you might take to some light study: conchology, now: I always think that must be a light study. Or get Dorothea to read you light things, Smollett—‘Roderick Random,’ ‘Humphry Clinker:’ they are a little broad, but she may read anything now she’s married, you know. I remember they made me laugh uncommonly—there’s a droll bit about a postilion’s breeches. We have no such humor now. I have gone through all these things, but they might be rather new to you.”\n\n“As new as eating thistles,” would have been an answer to represent Mr. Casaubon’s feelings. But he only bowed resignedly, with due respect to his wife’s uncle, and observed that doubtless the works he mentioned had “served as a resource to a certain order of minds.”\n\n“You see,” said the able magistrate to Lydgate, when they were outside the door, “Casaubon has been a little narrow: it leaves him rather at a loss when you forbid him his particular work, which I believe is something very deep indeed—in the line of research, you know. I would never give way to that; I was always versatile. But a clergyman is tied a little tight. If they would make him a bishop, now!—he did a very good pamphlet for Peel. He would have more movement then, more show; he might get a little flesh. But I recommend you to talk to Mrs. Casaubon. She is clever enough for anything, is my niece. Tell her, her husband wants liveliness, diversion: put her on amusing tactics.”\n\nWithout Mr. Brooke’s advice, Lydgate had determined on speaking to Dorothea. She had not been present while her uncle was throwing out his pleasant suggestions as to the mode in which life at Lowick might be enlivened, but she was usually by her husband’s side, and the unaffected signs of intense anxiety in her face and voice about whatever touched his mind or health, made a drama which Lydgate was inclined to watch. He said to himself that he was only doing right in telling her the truth about her husband’s probable future, but he certainly thought also that it would be interesting to talk confidentially with her. A medical man likes to make psychological observations, and sometimes in the pursuit of such studies is too easily tempted into momentous prophecy which life and death easily set at nought. Lydgate had often been satirical on this gratuitous prediction, and he meant now to be guarded.\n\nHe asked for Mrs. Casaubon, but being told that she was out walking, he was going away, when Dorothea and Celia appeared, both glowing from their struggle with the March wind. When Lydgate begged to speak with her alone, Dorothea opened the library door which happened to be the nearest, thinking of nothing at the moment but what he might have to say about Mr. Casaubon. It was the first time she had entered this room since her husband had been taken ill, and the servant had chosen not to open the shutters. But there was light enough to read by from the narrow upper panes of the windows.\n\n“You will not mind this sombre light,” said Dorothea, standing in the middle of the room. “Since you forbade books, the library has been out of the question. But Mr. Casaubon will soon be here again, I hope. Is he not making progress?”\n\n“Yes, much more rapid progress than I at first expected. Indeed, he is already nearly in his usual state of health.”\n\n“You do not fear that the illness will return?” said Dorothea, whose quick ear had detected some significance in Lydgate’s tone.\n\n“Such cases are peculiarly difficult to pronounce upon,” said Lydgate. “The only point on which I can be confident is that it will be desirable to be very watchful on Mr. Casaubon’s account, lest he should in any way strain his nervous power.”\n\n“I beseech you to speak quite plainly,” said Dorothea, in an imploring tone. “I cannot bear to think that there might be something which I did not know, and which, if I had known it, would have made me act differently.” The words came out like a cry: it was evident that they were the voice of some mental experience which lay not very far off.\n\n“Sit down,” she added, placing herself on the nearest chair, and throwing off her bonnet and gloves, with an instinctive discarding of formality where a great question of destiny was concerned.\n\n“What you say now justifies my own view,” said Lydgate. “I think it is one’s function as a medical man to hinder regrets of that sort as far as possible. But I beg you to observe that Mr. Casaubon’s case is precisely of the kind in which the issue is most difficult to pronounce upon. He may possibly live for fifteen years or more, without much worse health than he has had hitherto.”\n\nDorothea had turned very pale, and when Lydgate paused she said in a low voice, “You mean if we are very careful.”\n\n“Yes—careful against mental agitation of all kinds, and against excessive application.”\n\n“He would be miserable, if he had to give up his work,” said Dorothea, with a quick prevision of that wretchedness.\n\n“I am aware of that. The only course is to try by all means, direct and indirect, to moderate and vary his occupations. With a happy concurrence of circumstances, there is, as I said, no immediate danger from that affection of the heart, which I believe to have been the cause of his late attack. On the other hand, it is possible that the disease may develop itself more rapidly: it is one of those cases in which death is sometimes sudden. Nothing should be neglected which might be affected by such an issue.”\n\nThere was silence for a few moments, while Dorothea sat as if she had been turned to marble, though the life within her was so intense that her mind had never before swept in brief time over an equal range of scenes and motives.\n\n“Help me, pray,” she said, at last, in the same low voice as before. “Tell me what I can do.”\n\n“What do you think of foreign travel? You have been lately in Rome, I think.”\n\nThe memories which made this resource utterly hopeless were a new current that shook Dorothea out of her pallid immobility.\n\n“Oh, that would not do—that would be worse than anything,” she said with a more childlike despondency, while the tears rolled down. “Nothing will be of any use that he does not enjoy.”\n\n“I wish that I could have spared you this pain,” said Lydgate, deeply touched, yet wondering about her marriage. Women just like Dorothea had not entered into his traditions.\n\n“It was right of you to tell me. I thank you for telling me the truth.”\n\n“I wish you to understand that I shall not say anything to enlighten Mr. Casaubon himself. I think it desirable for him to know nothing more than that he must not overwork himself, and must observe certain rules. Anxiety of any kind would be precisely the most unfavorable condition for him.”\n\nLydgate rose, and Dorothea mechanically rose at the same time, unclasping her cloak and throwing it off as if it stifled her. He was bowing and quitting her, when an impulse which if she had been alone would have turned into a prayer, made her say with a sob in her voice—\n\n“Oh, you are a wise man, are you not? You know all about life and death. Advise me. Think what I can do. He has been laboring all his life and looking forward. He minds about nothing else.— And I mind about nothing else—”\n\nFor years after Lydgate remembered the impression produced in him by this involuntary appeal—this cry from soul to soul, without other consciousness than their moving with kindred natures in the same embroiled medium, the same troublous fitfully illuminated life. But what could he say now except that he should see Mr. Casaubon again to-morrow?\n\nWhen he was gone, Dorothea’s tears gushed forth, and relieved her stifling oppression. Then she dried her eyes, reminded that her distress must not be betrayed to her husband; and looked round the room thinking that she must order the servant to attend to it as usual, since Mr. Casaubon might now at any moment wish to enter. On his writing-table there were letters which had lain untouched since the morning when he was taken ill, and among them, as Dorothea well remembered, there were young Ladislaw’s letters, the one addressed to her still unopened. The associations of these letters had been made the more painful by that sudden attack of illness which she felt that the agitation caused by her anger might have helped to bring on: it would be time enough to read them when they were again thrust upon her, and she had had no inclination to fetch them from the library. But now it occurred to her that they should be put out of her husband’s sight: whatever might have been the sources of his annoyance about them, he must, if possible, not be annoyed again; and she ran her eyes first over the letter addressed to him to assure herself whether or not it would be necessary to write in order to hinder the offensive visit.\n\nWill wrote from Rome, and began by saying that his obligations to Mr. Casaubon were too deep for all thanks not to seem impertinent. It was plain that if he were not grateful, he must be the poorest-spirited rascal who had ever found a generous friend. To expand in wordy thanks would be like saying, “I am honest.” But Will had come to perceive that his defects—defects which Mr. Casaubon had himself often pointed to—needed for their correction that more strenuous position which his relative’s generosity had hitherto prevented from being inevitable. He trusted that he should make the best return, if return were possible, by showing the effectiveness of the education for which he was indebted, and by ceasing in future to need any diversion towards himself of funds on which others might have a better claim. He was coming to England, to try his fortune, as many other young men were obliged to do whose only capital was in their brains. His friend Naumann had desired him to take charge of the “Dispute”—the picture painted for Mr. Casaubon, with whose permission, and Mrs. Casaubon’s, Will would convey it to Lowick in person. A letter addressed to the Poste Restante in Paris within the fortnight would hinder him, if necessary, from arriving at an inconvenient moment. He enclosed a letter to Mrs. Casaubon in which he continued a discussion about art, begun with her in Rome.\n\nOpening her own letter Dorothea saw that it was a lively continuation of his remonstrance with her fanatical sympathy and her want of sturdy neutral delight in things as they were—an outpouring of his young vivacity which it was impossible to read just now. She had immediately to consider what was to be done about the other letter: there was still time perhaps to prevent Will from coming to Lowick. Dorothea ended by giving the letter to her uncle, who was still in the house, and begging him to let Will know that Mr. Casaubon had been ill, and that his health would not allow the reception of any visitors.\n\nNo one more ready than Mr. Brooke to write a letter: his only difficulty was to write a short one, and his ideas in this case expanded over the three large pages and the inward foldings. He had simply said to Dorothea—\n\n“To be sure, I will write, my dear. He’s a very clever young fellow—this young Ladislaw—I dare say will be a rising young man. It’s a good letter—marks his sense of things, you know. However, I will tell him about Casaubon.”\n\nBut the end of Mr. Brooke’s pen was a thinking organ, evolving sentences, especially of a benevolent kind, before the rest of his mind could well overtake them. It expressed regrets and proposed remedies, which, when Mr. Brooke read them, seemed felicitously worded—surprisingly the right thing, and determined a sequel which he had never before thought of. In this case, his pen found it such a pity young Ladislaw should not have come into the neighborhood just at that time, in order that Mr. Brooke might make his acquaintance more fully, and that they might go over the long-neglected Italian drawings together—it also felt such an interest in a young man who was starting in life with a stock of ideas—that by the end of the second page it had persuaded Mr. Brooke to invite young Ladislaw, since he could not be received at Lowick, to come to Tipton Grange. Why not? They could find a great many things to do together, and this was a period of peculiar growth—the political horizon was expanding, and—in short, Mr. Brooke’s pen went off into a little speech which it had lately reported for that imperfectly edited organ the “Middlemarch Pioneer.” While Mr. Brooke was sealing this letter, he felt elated with an influx of dim projects:—a young man capable of putting ideas into form, the “Pioneer” purchased to clear the pathway for a new candidate, documents utilized—who knew what might come of it all? Since Celia was going to marry immediately, it would be very pleasant to have a young fellow at table with him, at least for a time.\n\nBut he went away without telling Dorothea what he had put into the letter, for she was engaged with her husband, and—in fact, these things were of no importance to her.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXI.\n\nHow will you know the pitch of that great bell Too large for you to stir? Let but a flute Play ’neath the fine-mixed metal: listen close Till the right note flows forth, a silvery rill: Then shall the huge bell tremble—then the mass With myriad waves concurrent shall respond In low soft unison.\n\nLydgate that evening spoke to Miss Vincy of Mrs. Casaubon, and laid some emphasis on the strong feeling she appeared to have for that formal studious man thirty years older than herself.\n\n“Of course she is devoted to her husband,” said Rosamond, implying a notion of necessary sequence which the scientific man regarded as the prettiest possible for a woman; but she was thinking at the same time that it was not so very melancholy to be mistress of Lowick Manor with a husband likely to die soon. “Do you think her very handsome?”\n\n“She certainly is handsome, but I have not thought about it,” said Lydgate.\n“I suppose it would be unprofessional,” said Rosamond, dimpling. “But how your practice is spreading! You were called in before to the Chettams, I think; and now, the Casaubons.”\n\n“Yes,” said Lydgate, in a tone of compulsory admission. “But I don’t really like attending such people so well as the poor. The cases are more monotonous, and one has to go through more fuss and listen more deferentially to nonsense.”\n\n“Not more than in Middlemarch,” said Rosamond. “And at least you go through wide corridors and have the scent of rose-leaves everywhere.”\n\n“That is true, Mademoiselle de Montmorenci,” said Lydgate, just bending his head to the table and lifting with his fourth finger her delicate handkerchief which lay at the mouth of her reticule, as if to enjoy its scent, while he looked at her with a smile.\n\nBut this agreeable holiday freedom with which Lydgate hovered about the flower of Middlemarch, could not continue indefinitely. It was not more possible to find social isolation in that town than elsewhere, and two people persistently flirting could by no means escape from “the various entanglements, weights, blows, clashings, motions, by which things severally go on.” Whatever Miss Vincy did must be remarked, and she was perhaps the more conspicuous to admirers and critics because just now Mrs. Vincy, after some struggle, had gone with Fred to stay a little while at Stone Court, there being no other way of at once gratifying old Featherstone and keeping watch against Mary Garth, who appeared a less tolerable daughter-in-law in proportion as Fred’s illness disappeared.\n\nAunt Bulstrode, for example, came a little oftener into Lowick Gate to see Rosamond, now she was alone. For Mrs. Bulstrode had a true sisterly feeling for her brother; always thinking that he might have married better, but wishing well to the children. Now Mrs. Bulstrode had a long-standing intimacy with Mrs. Plymdale. They had nearly the same preferences in silks, patterns for underclothing, china-ware, and clergymen; they confided their little troubles of health and household management to each other, and various little points of superiority on Mrs. Bulstrode’s side, namely, more decided seriousness, more admiration for mind, and a house outside the town, sometimes served to give color to their conversation without dividing them—well-meaning women both, knowing very little of their own motives.\n\nMrs. Bulstrode, paying a morning visit to Mrs. Plymdale, happened to say that she could not stay longer, because she was going to see poor Rosamond.\n\n“Why do you say ‘poor Rosamond’?” said Mrs. Plymdale, a round-eyed sharp little woman, like a tamed falcon.\n\n“She is so pretty, and has been brought up in such thoughtlessness. The mother, you know, had always that levity about her, which makes me anxious for the children.”\n\n“Well, Harriet, if I am to speak my mind,” said Mrs. Plymdale, with emphasis, “I must say, anybody would suppose you and Mr. Bulstrode would be delighted with what has happened, for you have done everything to put Mr. Lydgate forward.”\n\n“Selina, what do you mean?” said Mrs. Bulstrode, in genuine surprise.\n\n“Not but what I am truly thankful for Ned’s sake,” said Mrs. Plymdale. “He could certainly better afford to keep such a wife than some people can; but I should wish him to look elsewhere. Still a mother has anxieties, and some young men would take to a bad life in consequence. Besides, if I was obliged to speak, I should say I was not fond of strangers coming into a town.”\n\n“I don’t know, Selina,” said Mrs. Bulstrode, with a little emphasis in her turn. “Mr. Bulstrode was a stranger here at one time. Abraham and Moses were strangers in the land, and we are told to entertain strangers. And especially,” she added, after a slight pause, “when they are unexceptionable.”\n\n“I was not speaking in a religious sense, Harriet. I spoke as a mother.”\n\n“Selina, I am sure you have never heard me say anything against a niece of mine marrying your son.”\n\n“Oh, it is pride in Miss Vincy—I am sure it is nothing else,” said Mrs. Plymdale, who had never before given all her confidence to “Harriet” on this subject. “No young man in Middlemarch was good enough for her: I have heard her mother say as much. That is not a Christian spirit, I think. But now, from all I hear, she has found a man as proud as herself.”\n\n“You don’t mean that there is anything between Rosamond and Mr. Lydgate?” said Mrs. Bulstrode, rather mortified at finding out her own ignorance.\n\n“Is it possible you don’t know, Harriet?”\n\n“Oh, I go about so little; and I am not fond of gossip; I really never hear any. You see so many people that I don’t see. Your circle is rather different from ours.”\n\n“Well, but your own niece and Mr. Bulstrode’s great favorite—and yours too, I am sure, Harriet! I thought, at one time, you meant him for Kate, when she is a little older.”\n\n“I don’t believe there can be anything serious at present,” said Mrs. Bulstrode. “My brother would certainly have told me.”\n\n“Well, people have different ways, but I understand that nobody can see Miss Vincy and Mr. Lydgate together without taking them to be engaged. However, it is not my business. Shall I put up the pattern of mittens?”\n\nAfter this Mrs. Bulstrode drove to her niece with a mind newly weighted. She was herself handsomely dressed, but she noticed with a little more regret than usual that Rosamond, who was just come in and met her in walking-dress, was almost as expensively equipped. Mrs. Bulstrode was a feminine smaller edition of her brother, and had none of her husband’s low-toned pallor. She had a good honest glance and used no circumlocution.\n\n“You are alone, I see, my dear,” she said, as they entered the drawing-room together, looking round gravely. Rosamond felt sure that her aunt had something particular to say, and they sat down near each other. Nevertheless, the quilling inside Rosamond’s bonnet was so charming that it was impossible not to desire the same kind of thing for Kate, and Mrs. Bulstrode’s eyes, which were rather fine, rolled round that ample quilled circuit, while she spoke.\n\n“I have just heard something about you that has surprised me very much, Rosamond.”\n\n“What is that, aunt?” Rosamond’s eyes also were roaming over her aunt’s large embroidered collar.\n\n“I can hardly believe it—that you should be engaged without my knowing it—without your father’s telling me.” Here Mrs. Bulstrode’s eyes finally rested on Rosamond’s, who blushed deeply, and said—\n\n“I am not engaged, aunt.”\n\n“How is it that every one says so, then—that it is the town’s talk?”\n\n“The town’s talk is of very little consequence, I think,” said Rosamond, inwardly gratified.\n\n“Oh, my dear, be more thoughtful; don’t despise your neighbors so. Remember you are turned twenty-two now, and you will have no fortune: your father, I am sure, will not be able to spare you anything. Mr. Lydgate is very intellectual and clever; I know there is an attraction in that. I like talking to such men myself; and your uncle finds him very useful. But the profession is a poor one here. To be sure, this life is not everything; but it is seldom a medical man has true religious views—there is too much pride of intellect. And you are not fit to marry a poor man.\n\n“Mr. Lydgate is not a poor man, aunt. He has very high connections.”\n\n“He told me himself he was poor.”\n\n“That is because he is used to people who have a high style of living.”\n\n“My dear Rosamond, you must not think of living in high style.”\n\nRosamond looked down and played with her reticule. She was not a fiery young lady and had no sharp answers, but she meant to live as she pleased.\n\n“Then it is really true?” said Mrs. Bulstrode, looking very earnestly at her niece. “You are thinking of Mr. Lydgate—there is some understanding between you, though your father doesn’t know. Be open, my dear Rosamond: Mr. Lydgate has really made you an offer?”\n\nPoor Rosamond’s feelings were very unpleasant. She had been quite easy as to Lydgate’s feeling and intention, but now when her aunt put this question she did not like being unable to say Yes. Her pride was hurt, but her habitual control of manner helped her.\n\n“Pray excuse me, aunt. I would rather not speak on the subject.”\n\n“You would not give your heart to a man without a decided prospect, I trust, my dear. And think of the two excellent offers I know of that you have refused!—and one still within your reach, if you will not throw it away. I knew a very great beauty who married badly at last, by doing so. Mr. Ned Plymdale is a nice young man—some might think good-looking; and an only son; and a large business of that kind is better than a profession. Not that marrying is everything. I would have you seek first the kingdom of God. But a girl should keep her heart within her own power.”\n\n“I should never give it to Mr. Ned Plymdale, if it were. I have already refused him. If I loved, I should love at once and without change,” said Rosamond, with a great sense of being a romantic heroine, and playing the part prettily.\n\n“I see how it is, my dear,” said Mrs. Bulstrode, in a melancholy voice, rising to go. “You have allowed your affections to be engaged without return.”\n\n“No, indeed, aunt,” said Rosamond, with emphasis.\n\n“Then you are quite confident that Mr. Lydgate has a serious attachment to you?”\n\nRosamond’s cheeks by this time were persistently burning, and she felt much mortification. She chose to be silent, and her aunt went away all the more convinced.\n\nMr. Bulstrode in things worldly and indifferent was disposed to do what his wife bade him, and she now, without telling her reasons, desired him on the next opportunity to find out in conversation with Mr. Lydgate whether he had any intention of marrying soon. The result was a decided negative. Mr. Bulstrode, on being cross-questioned, showed that Lydgate had spoken as no man would who had any attachment that could issue in matrimony. Mrs. Bulstrode now felt that she had a serious duty before her, and she soon managed to arrange a tête-à-tête with Lydgate, in which she passed from inquiries about Fred Vincy’s health, and expressions of her sincere anxiety for her brother’s large family, to general remarks on the dangers which lay before young people with regard to their settlement in life. Young men were often wild and disappointing, making little return for the money spent on them, and a girl was exposed to many circumstances which might interfere with her prospects.\n\n“Especially when she has great attractions, and her parents see much company,” said Mrs. Bulstrode. “Gentlemen pay her attention, and engross her all to themselves, for the mere pleasure of the moment, and that drives off others. I think it is a heavy responsibility, Mr. Lydgate, to interfere with the prospects of any girl.” Here Mrs. Bulstrode fixed her eyes on him, with an unmistakable purpose of warning, if not of rebuke.\n\n“Clearly,” said Lydgate, looking at her—perhaps even staring a little in return. “On the other hand, a man must be a great coxcomb to go about with a notion that he must not pay attention to a young lady lest she should fall in love with him, or lest others should think she must.”\n\n“Oh, Mr. Lydgate, you know well what your advantages are. You know that our young men here cannot cope with you. Where you frequent a house it may militate very much against a girl’s making a desirable settlement in life, and prevent her from accepting offers even if they are made.”\n\nLydgate was less flattered by his advantage over the Middlemarch Orlandos than he was annoyed by the perception of Mrs. Bulstrode’s meaning. She felt that she had spoken as impressively as it was necessary to do, and that in using the superior word “militate” she had thrown a noble drapery over a mass of particulars which were still evident enough.\n\nLydgate was fuming a little, pushed his hair back with one hand, felt curiously in his waistcoat-pocket with the other, and then stooped to beckon the tiny black spaniel, which had the insight to decline his hollow caresses. It would not have been decent to go away, because he had been dining with other guests, and had just taken tea. But Mrs. Bulstrode, having no doubt that she had been understood, turned the conversation.\n\nSolomon’s Proverbs, I think, have omitted to say, that as the sore palate findeth grit, so an uneasy consciousness heareth innuendoes. The next day Mr. Farebrother, parting from Lydgate in the street, supposed that they should meet at Vincy’s in the evening. Lydgate answered curtly, no—he had work to do—he must give up going out in the evening.\n\n“What! you are going to get lashed to the mast, eh, and are stopping your ears?” said the Vicar. “Well, if you don’t mean to be won by the sirens, you are right to take precautions in time.”\n\nA few days before, Lydgate would have taken no notice of these words as anything more than the Vicar’s usual way of putting things. They seemed now to convey an innuendo which confirmed the impression that he had been making a fool of himself and behaving so as to be misunderstood: not, he believed, by Rosamond herself; she, he felt sure, took everything as lightly as he intended it. She had an exquisite tact and insight in relation to all points of manners; but the people she lived among were blunderers and busybodies. However, the mistake should go no farther. He resolved—and kept his resolution—that he would not go to Mr. Vincy’s except on business.\n\nRosamond became very unhappy. The uneasiness first stirred by her aunt’s questions grew and grew till at the end of ten days that she had not seen Lydgate, it grew into terror at the blank that might possibly come—into foreboding of that ready, fatal sponge which so cheaply wipes out the hopes of mortals. The world would have a new dreariness for her, as a wilderness that a magician’s spells had turned for a little while into a garden. She felt that she was beginning to know the pang of disappointed love, and that no other man could be the occasion of such delightful aerial building as she had been enjoying for the last six months. Poor Rosamond lost her appetite and felt as forlorn as Ariadne—as a charming stage Ariadne left behind with all her boxes full of costumes and no hope of a coach.\n\nThere are many wonderful mixtures in the world which are all alike called love, and claim the privileges of a sublime rage which is an apology for everything (in literature and the drama). Happily Rosamond did not think of committing any desperate act: she plaited her fair hair as beautifully as usual, and kept herself proudly calm. Her most cheerful supposition was that her aunt Bulstrode had interfered in some way to hinder Lydgate’s visits: everything was better than a spontaneous indifference in him. Any one who imagines ten days too short a time—not for falling into leanness, lightness, or other measurable effects of passion, but—for the whole spiritual circuit of alarmed conjecture and disappointment, is ignorant of what can go on in the elegant leisure of a young lady’s mind.\n\nOn the eleventh day, however, Lydgate when leaving Stone Court was requested by Mrs. Vincy to let her husband know that there was a marked change in Mr. Featherstone’s health, and that she wished him to come to Stone Court on that day. Now Lydgate might have called at the warehouse, or might have written a message on a leaf of his pocket-book and left it at the door. Yet these simple devices apparently did not occur to him, from which we may conclude that he had no strong objection to calling at the house at an hour when Mr. Vincy was not at home, and leaving the message with Miss Vincy. A man may, from various motives, decline to give his company, but perhaps not even a sage would be gratified that nobody missed him. It would be a graceful, easy way of piecing on the new habits to the old, to have a few playful words with Rosamond about his resistance to dissipation, and his firm resolve to take long fasts even from sweet sounds. It must be confessed, also, that momentary speculations as to all the possible grounds for Mrs. Bulstrode’s hints had managed to get woven like slight clinging hairs into the more substantial web of his thoughts.\n\nMiss Vincy was alone, and blushed so deeply when Lydgate came in that he felt a corresponding embarrassment, and instead of any playfulness, he began at once to speak of his reason for calling, and to beg her, almost formally, to deliver the message to her father. Rosamond, who at the first moment felt as if her happiness were returning, was keenly hurt by Lydgate’s manner; her blush had departed, and she assented coldly, without adding an unnecessary word, some trivial chain-work which she had in her hands enabling her to avoid looking at Lydgate higher than his chin. In all failures, the beginning is certainly the half of the whole. After sitting two long moments while he moved his whip and could say nothing, Lydgate rose to go, and Rosamond, made nervous by her struggle between mortification and the wish not to betray it, dropped her chain as if startled, and rose too, mechanically. Lydgate instantaneously stooped to pick up the chain. When he rose he was very near to a lovely little face set on a fair long neck which he had been used to see turning about under the most perfect management of self-contented grace. But as he raised his eyes now he saw a certain helpless quivering which touched him quite newly, and made him look at Rosamond with a questioning flash. At this moment she was as natural as she had ever been when she was five years old: she felt that her tears had risen, and it was no use to try to do anything else than let them stay like water on a blue flower or let them fall over her cheeks, even as they would.\n\nThat moment of naturalness was the crystallizing feather-touch: it shook flirtation into love. Remember that the ambitious man who was looking at those Forget-me-nots under the water was very warm-hearted and rash. He did not know where the chain went; an idea had thrilled through the recesses within him which had a miraculous effect in raising the power of passionate love lying buried there in no sealed sepulchre, but under the lightest, easily pierced mould. His words were quite abrupt and awkward; but the tone made them sound like an ardent, appealing avowal.\n\n“What is the matter? you are distressed. Tell me, pray.”\n\nRosamond had never been spoken to in such tones before. I am not sure that she knew what the words were: but she looked at Lydgate and the tears fell over her cheeks. There could have been no more complete answer than that silence, and Lydgate, forgetting everything else, completely mastered by the outrush of tenderness at the sudden belief that this sweet young creature depended on him for her joy, actually put his arms round her, folding her gently and protectingly—he was used to being gentle with the weak and suffering—and kissed each of the two large tears. This was a strange way of arriving at an understanding, but it was a short way. Rosamond was not angry, but she moved backward a little in timid happiness, and Lydgate could now sit near her and speak less incompletely. Rosamond had to make her little confession, and he poured out words of gratitude and tenderness with impulsive lavishment. In half an hour he left the house an engaged man, whose soul was not his own, but the woman’s to whom he had bound himself.\n\nHe came again in the evening to speak with Mr. Vincy, who, just returned from Stone Court, was feeling sure that it would not be long before he heard of Mr. Featherstone’s demise. The felicitous word “demise,” which had seasonably occurred to him, had raised his spirits even above their usual evening pitch. The right word is always a power, and communicates its definiteness to our action. Considered as a demise, old Featherstone’s death assumed a merely legal aspect, so that Mr. Vincy could tap his snuff-box over it and be jovial, without even an intermittent affectation of solemnity; and Mr. Vincy hated both solemnity and affectation. Who was ever awe struck about a testator, or sang a hymn on the title to real property? Mr. Vincy was inclined to take a jovial view of all things that evening: he even observed to Lydgate that Fred had got the family constitution after all, and would soon be as fine a fellow as ever again; and when his approbation of Rosamond’s engagement was asked for, he gave it with astonishing facility, passing at once to general remarks on the desirableness of matrimony for young men and maidens, and apparently deducing from the whole the appropriateness of a little more punch.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII.\n\nThey’ll take suggestion as a cat laps milk. —SHAKESPEARE: Tempest.\n\nThe triumphant confidence of the Mayor founded on Mr. Featherstone’s insistent demand that Fred and his mother should not leave him, was a feeble emotion compared with all that was agitating the breasts of the old man’s blood-relations, who naturally manifested more their sense of the family tie and were more visibly numerous now that he had become bedridden. Naturally: for when “poor Peter” had occupied his arm-chair in the wainscoted parlor, no assiduous beetles for whom the cook prepares boiling water could have been less welcome on a hearth which they had reasons for preferring, than those persons whose Featherstone blood was ill-nourished, not from penuriousness on their part, but from poverty. Brother Solomon and Sister Jane were rich, and the family candor and total abstinence from false politeness with which they were always received seemed to them no argument that their brother in the solemn act of making his will would overlook the superior claims of wealth. Themselves at least he had never been unnatural enough to banish from his house, and it seemed hardly eccentric that he should have kept away Brother Jonah, Sister Martha, and the rest, who had no shadow of such claims. They knew Peter’s maxim, that money was a good egg, and should be laid in a warm nest.\n\nBut Brother Jonah, Sister Martha, and all the needy exiles, held a different point of view. Probabilities are as various as the faces to be seen at will in fretwork or paper-hangings: every form is there, from Jupiter to Judy, if you only look with creative inclination. To the poorer and least favored it seemed likely that since Peter had done nothing for them in his life, he would remember them at the last. Jonah argued that men liked to make a surprise of their wills, while Martha said that nobody need be surprised if he left the best part of his money to those who least expected it. Also it was not to be thought but that an own brother “lying there” with dropsy in his legs must come to feel that blood was thicker than water, and if he didn’t alter his will, he might have money by him. At any rate some blood-relations should be on the premises and on the watch against those who were hardly relations at all. Such things had been known as forged wills and disputed wills, which seemed to have the golden-hazy advantage of somehow enabling non-legatees to live out of them. Again, those who were no blood-relations might be caught making away with things—and poor Peter “lying there” helpless! Somebody should be on the watch. But in this conclusion they were at one with Solomon and Jane; also, some nephews, nieces, and cousins, arguing with still greater subtilty as to what might be done by a man able to “will away” his property and give himself large treats of oddity, felt in a handsome sort of way that there was a family interest to be attended to, and thought of Stone Court as a place which it would be nothing but right for them to visit. Sister Martha, otherwise Mrs. Cranch, living with some wheeziness in the Chalky Flats, could not undertake the journey; but her son, as being poor Peter’s own nephew, could represent her advantageously, and watch lest his uncle Jonah should make an unfair use of the improbable things which seemed likely to happen. In fact there was a general sense running in the Featherstone blood that everybody must watch everybody else, and that it would be well for everybody else to reflect that the Almighty was watching him.\n\nThus Stone Court continually saw one or other blood-relation alighting or departing, and Mary Garth had the unpleasant task of carrying their messages to Mr. Featherstone, who would see none of them, and sent her down with the still more unpleasant task of telling them so. As manager of the household she felt bound to ask them in good provincial fashion to stay and eat; but she chose to consult Mrs. Vincy on the point of extra down-stairs consumption now that Mr. Featherstone was laid up.\n\n“Oh, my dear, you must do things handsomely where there’s last illness and a property. God knows, I don’t grudge them every ham in the house—only, save the best for the funeral. Have some stuffed veal always, and a fine cheese in cut. You must expect to keep open house in these last illnesses,” said liberal Mrs. Vincy, once more of cheerful note and bright plumage.\n\nBut some of the visitors alighted and did not depart after the handsome treating to veal and ham. Brother Jonah, for example (there are such unpleasant people in most families; perhaps even in the highest aristocracy there are Brobdingnag specimens, gigantically in debt and bloated at greater expense)—Brother Jonah, I say, having come down in the world, was mainly supported by a calling which he was modest enough not to boast of, though it was much better than swindling either on exchange or turf, but which did not require his presence at Brassing so long as he had a good corner to sit in and a supply of food. He chose the kitchen-corner, partly because he liked it best, and partly because he did not want to sit with Solomon, concerning whom he had a strong brotherly opinion. Seated in a famous arm-chair and in his best suit, constantly within sight of good cheer, he had a comfortable consciousness of being on the premises, mingled with fleeting suggestions of Sunday and the bar at the Green Man; and he informed Mary Garth that he should not go out of reach of his brother Peter while that poor fellow was above ground. The troublesome ones in a family are usually either the wits or the idiots. Jonah was the wit among the Featherstones, and joked with the maid-servants when they came about the hearth, but seemed to consider Miss Garth a suspicious character, and followed her with cold eyes.\n\nMary would have borne this one pair of eyes with comparative ease, but unfortunately there was young Cranch, who, having come all the way from the Chalky Flats to represent his mother and watch his uncle Jonah, also felt it his duty to stay and to sit chiefly in the kitchen to give his uncle company. Young Cranch was not exactly the balancing point between the wit and the idiot,—verging slightly towards the latter type, and squinting so as to leave everything in doubt about his sentiments except that they were not of a forcible character. When Mary Garth entered the kitchen and Mr. Jonah Featherstone began to follow her with his cold detective eyes, young Cranch turning his head in the same direction seemed to insist on it that she should remark how he was squinting, as if he did it with design, like the gypsies when Borrow read the New Testament to them. This was rather too much for poor Mary; sometimes it made her bilious, sometimes it upset her gravity. One day that she had an opportunity she could not resist describing the kitchen scene to Fred, who would not be hindered from immediately going to see it, affecting simply to pass through. But no sooner did he face the four eyes than he had to rush through the nearest door which happened to lead to the dairy, and there under the high roof and among the pans he gave way to laughter which made a hollow resonance perfectly audible in the kitchen. He fled by another doorway, but Mr. Jonah, who had not before seen Fred’s white complexion, long legs, and pinched delicacy of face, prepared many sarcasms in which these points of appearance were wittily combined with the lowest moral attributes.\n\n“Why, Tom, you don’t wear such gentlemanly trousers—you haven’t got half such fine long legs,” said Jonah to his nephew, winking at the same time, to imply that there was something more in these statements than their undeniableness. Tom looked at his legs, but left it uncertain whether he preferred his moral advantages to a more vicious length of limb and reprehensible gentility of trouser.\n\nIn the large wainscoted parlor too there were constantly pairs of eyes on the watch, and own relatives eager to be “sitters-up.” Many came, lunched, and departed, but Brother Solomon and the lady who had been Jane Featherstone for twenty-five years before she was Mrs. Waule found it good to be there every day for hours, without other calculable occupation than that of observing the cunning Mary Garth (who was so deep that she could be found out in nothing) and giving occasional dry wrinkly indications of crying—as if capable of torrents in a wetter season—at the thought that they were not allowed to go into Mr. Featherstone’s room. For the old man’s dislike of his own family seemed to get stronger as he got less able to amuse himself by saying biting things to them. Too languid to sting, he had the more venom refluent in his blood.\n\nNot fully believing the message sent through Mary Garth, they had presented themselves together within the door of the bedroom, both in black—Mrs. Waule having a white handkerchief partially unfolded in her hand—and both with faces in a sort of half-mourning purple; while Mrs. Vincy with her pink cheeks and pink ribbons flying was actually administering a cordial to their own brother, and the light-complexioned Fred, his short hair curling as might be expected in a gambler’s, was lolling at his ease in a large chair.\n\nOld Featherstone no sooner caught sight of these funereal figures appearing in spite of his orders than rage came to strengthen him more successfully than the cordial. He was propped up on a bed-rest, and always had his gold-headed stick lying by him. He seized it now and swept it backwards and forwards in as large an area as he could, apparently to ban these ugly spectres, crying in a hoarse sort of screech—\n\n“Back, back, Mrs. Waule! Back, Solomon!”\n\n“Oh, Brother. Peter,” Mrs. Waule began—but Solomon put his hand before her repressingly. He was a large-cheeked man, nearly seventy, with small furtive eyes, and was not only of much blander temper but thought himself much deeper than his brother Peter; indeed not likely to be deceived in any of his fellow-men, inasmuch as they could not well be more greedy and deceitful than he suspected them of being. Even the invisible powers, he thought, were likely to be soothed by a bland parenthesis here and there—coming from a man of property, who might have been as impious as others.\n\n“Brother Peter,” he said, in a wheedling yet gravely official tone, “It’s nothing but right I should speak to you about the Three Crofts and the Manganese. The Almighty knows what I’ve got on my mind—”\n\n“Then he knows more than I want to know,” said Peter, laying down his stick with a show of truce which had a threat in it too, for he reversed the stick so as to make the gold handle a club in case of closer fighting, and looked hard at Solomon’s bald head.\n\n“There’s things you might repent of, Brother, for want of speaking to me,” said Solomon, not advancing, however. “I could sit up with you to-night, and Jane with me, willingly, and you might take your own time to speak, or let me speak.”\n\n“Yes, I shall take my own time—you needn’t offer me yours,” said Peter.\n\n“But you can’t take your own time to die in, Brother,” began Mrs. Waule, with her usual woolly tone. “And when you lie speechless you may be tired of having strangers about you, and you may think of me and my children”—but here her voice broke under the touching thought which she was attributing to her speechless brother; the mention of ourselves being naturally affecting.\n\n“No, I shan’t,” said old Featherstone, contradictiously. “I shan’t think of any of you. I’ve made my will, I tell you, I’ve made my will.” Here he turned his head towards Mrs. Vincy, and swallowed some more of his cordial.\n\n“Some people would be ashamed to fill up a place belonging by rights to others,” said Mrs. Waule, turning her narrow eyes in the same direction.\n\n“Oh, sister,” said Solomon, with ironical softness, “you and me are not fine, and handsome, and clever enough: we must be humble and let smart people push themselves before us.”\n\nFred’s spirit could not bear this: rising and looking at Mr. Featherstone, he said, “Shall my mother and I leave the room, sir, that you may be alone with your friends?”\n\n“Sit down, I tell you,” said old Featherstone, snappishly. “Stop where you are. Good-by, Solomon,” he added, trying to wield his stick again, but failing now that he had reversed the handle. “Good-by, Mrs. Waule. Don’t you come again.”\n\n“I shall be down-stairs, Brother, whether or no,” said Solomon. “I shall do my duty, and it remains to be seen what the Almighty will allow.”\n\n“Yes, in property going out of families,” said Mrs. Waule, in continuation,—“and where there’s steady young men to carry on. But I pity them who are not such, and I pity their mothers. Good-by, Brother Peter.”\n\n“Remember, I’m the eldest after you, Brother, and prospered from the first, just as you did, and have got land already by the name of Featherstone,” said Solomon, relying much on that reflection, as one which might be suggested in the watches of the night. “But I bid you good-by for the present.”\n\nTheir exit was hastened by their seeing old Mr. Featherstone pull his wig on each side and shut his eyes with his mouth-widening grimace, as if he were determined to be deaf and blind.\n\nNone the less they came to Stone Court daily and sat below at the post of duty, sometimes carrying on a slow dialogue in an undertone in which the observation and response were so far apart, that any one hearing them might have imagined himself listening to speaking automata, in some doubt whether the ingenious mechanism would really work, or wind itself up for a long time in order to stick and be silent. Solomon and Jane would have been sorry to be quick: what that led to might be seen on the other side of the wall in the person of Brother Jonah.\n\nBut their watch in the wainscoted parlor was sometimes varied by the presence of other guests from far or near. Now that Peter Featherstone was up-stairs, his property could be discussed with all that local enlightenment to be found on the spot: some rural and Middlemarch neighbors expressed much agreement with the family and sympathy with their interest against the Vincys, and feminine visitors were even moved to tears, in conversation with Mrs. Waule, when they recalled the fact that they themselves had been disappointed in times past by codicils and marriages for spite on the part of ungrateful elderly gentlemen, who, it might have been supposed, had been spared for something better. Such conversation paused suddenly, like an organ when the bellows are let drop, if Mary Garth came into the room; and all eyes were turned on her as a possible legatee, or one who might get access to iron chests.\n\nBut the younger men who were relatives or connections of the family, were disposed to admire her in this problematic light, as a girl who showed much conduct, and who among all the chances that were flying might turn out to be at least a moderate prize. Hence she had her share of compliments and polite attentions.\n\nEspecially from Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, a distinguished bachelor and auctioneer of those parts, much concerned in the sale of land and cattle: a public character, indeed, whose name was seen on widely distributed placards, and who might reasonably be sorry for those who did not know of him. He was second cousin to Peter Featherstone, and had been treated by him with more amenity than any other relative, being useful in matters of business; and in that programme of his funeral which the old man had himself dictated, he had been named as a Bearer. There was no odious cupidity in Mr. Borthrop Trumbull—nothing more than a sincere sense of his own merit, which, he was aware, in case of rivalry might tell against competitors; so that if Peter Featherstone, who so far as he, Trumbull, was concerned, had behaved like as good a soul as ever breathed, should have done anything handsome by him, all he could say was, that he had never fished and fawned, but had advised him to the best of his experience, which now extended over twenty years from the time of his apprenticeship at fifteen, and was likely to yield a knowledge of no surreptitious kind. His admiration was far from being confined to himself, but was accustomed professionally as well as privately to delight in estimating things at a high rate. He was an amateur of superior phrases, and never used poor language without immediately correcting himself—which was fortunate, as he was rather loud, and given to predominate, standing or walking about frequently, pulling down his waistcoat with the air of a man who is very much of his own opinion, trimming himself rapidly with his fore-finger, and marking each new series in these movements by a busy play with his large seals. There was occasionally a little fierceness in his demeanor, but it was directed chiefly against false opinion, of which there is so much to correct in the world that a man of some reading and experience necessarily has his patience tried. He felt that the Featherstone family generally was of limited understanding, but being a man of the world and a public character, took everything as a matter of course, and even went to converse with Mr. Jonah and young Cranch in the kitchen, not doubting that he had impressed the latter greatly by his leading questions concerning the Chalky Flats. If anybody had observed that Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, being an auctioneer, was bound to know the nature of everything, he would have smiled and trimmed himself silently with the sense that he came pretty near that. On the whole, in an auctioneering way, he was an honorable man, not ashamed of his business, and feeling that “the celebrated Peel, now Sir Robert,” if introduced to him, would not fail to recognize his importance.\n\n“I don’t mind if I have a slice of that ham, and a glass of that ale, Miss Garth, if you will allow me,” he said, coming into the parlor at half-past eleven, after having had the exceptional privilege of seeing old Featherstone, and standing with his back to the fire between Mrs. Waule and Solomon.\n\n“It’s not necessary for you to go out;—let me ring the bell.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Mary, “I have an errand.”\n\n“Well, Mr. Trumbull, you’re highly favored,” said Mrs. Waule.\n\n“What! seeing the old man?” said the auctioneer, playing with his seals dispassionately. “Ah, you see he has relied on me considerably.” Here he pressed his lips together, and frowned meditatively.\n\n“Might anybody ask what their brother has been saying?” said Solomon, in a soft tone of humility, in which he had a sense of luxurious cunning, he being a rich man and not in need of it.\n\n“Oh yes, anybody may ask,” said Mr. Trumbull, with loud and good-humored though cutting sarcasm. “Anybody may interrogate. Any one may give their remarks an interrogative turn,” he continued, his sonorousness rising with his style. “This is constantly done by good speakers, even when they anticipate no answer. It is what we call a figure of speech—speech at a high figure, as one may say.” The eloquent auctioneer smiled at his own ingenuity.\n\n“I shouldn’t be sorry to hear he’d remembered you, Mr. Trumbull,” said Solomon. “I never was against the deserving. It’s the undeserving I’m against.”\n\n“Ah, there it is, you see, there it is,” said Mr. Trumbull, significantly. “It can’t be denied that undeserving people have been legatees, and even residuary legatees. It is so, with testamentary dispositions.” Again he pursed up his lips and frowned a little.\n\n“Do you mean to say for certain, Mr. Trumbull, that my brother has left his land away from our family?” said Mrs. Waule, on whom, as an unhopeful woman, those long words had a depressing effect.\n\n“A man might as well turn his land into charity land at once as leave it to some people,” observed Solomon, his sister’s question having drawn no answer.\n\n“What, Blue-Coat land?” said Mrs. Waule, again. “Oh, Mr. Trumbull, you never can mean to say that. It would be flying in the face of the Almighty that’s prospered him.”\n\nWhile Mrs. Waule was speaking, Mr. Borthrop Trumbull walked away from the fireplace towards the window, patrolling with his fore-finger round the inside of his stock, then along his whiskers and the curves of his hair. He now walked to Miss Garth’s work-table, opened a book which lay there and read the title aloud with pompous emphasis as if he were offering it for sale:\n\n“‘Anne of Geierstein’ (pronounced Jeersteen) or the ‘Maiden of the Mist, by the author of Waverley.’” Then turning the page, he began sonorously—“The course of four centuries has well-nigh elapsed since the series of events which are related in the following chapters took place on the Continent.” He pronounced the last truly admirable word with the accent on the last syllable, not as unaware of vulgar usage, but feeling that this novel delivery enhanced the sonorous beauty which his reading had given to the whole.\n\nAnd now the servant came in with the tray, so that the moments for answering Mrs. Waule’s question had gone by safely, while she and Solomon, watching Mr. Trumbull’s movements, were thinking that high learning interfered sadly with serious affairs. Mr. Borthrop Trumbull really knew nothing about old Featherstone’s will; but he could hardly have been brought to declare any ignorance unless he had been arrested for misprision of treason.\n\n“I shall take a mere mouthful of ham and a glass of ale,” he said, reassuringly. “As a man with public business, I take a snack when I can. I will back this ham,” he added, after swallowing some morsels with alarming haste, “against any ham in the three kingdoms. In my opinion it is better than the hams at Freshitt Hall—and I think I am a tolerable judge.”\n\n“Some don’t like so much sugar in their hams,” said Mrs. Waule. “But my poor brother would always have sugar.”\n\n“If any person demands better, he is at liberty to do so; but, God bless me, what an aroma! I should be glad to buy in that quality, I know. There is some gratification to a gentleman”—here Mr. Trumbull’s voice conveyed an emotional remonstrance—“in having this kind of ham set on his table.”\n\nHe pushed aside his plate, poured out his glass of ale and drew his chair a little forward, profiting by the occasion to look at the inner side of his legs, which he stroked approvingly—Mr. Trumbull having all those less frivolous airs and gestures which distinguish the predominant races of the north.\n\n“You have an interesting work there, I see, Miss Garth,” he observed, when Mary re-entered. “It is by the author of ‘Waverley’: that is Sir Walter Scott. I have bought one of his works myself—a very nice thing, a very superior publication, entitled ‘Ivanhoe.’ You will not get any writer to beat him in a hurry, I think—he will not, in my opinion, be speedily surpassed. I have just been reading a portion at the commencement of ‘Anne of Jeersteen.’ It commences well.” (Things never began with Mr. Borthrop Trumbull: they always commenced, both in private life and on his handbills.) “You are a reader, I see. Do you subscribe to our Middlemarch library?”\n\n“No,” said Mary. “Mr. Fred Vincy brought this book.”\n\n“I am a great bookman myself,” returned Mr. Trumbull. “I have no less than two hundred volumes in calf, and I flatter myself they are well selected. Also pictures by Murillo, Rubens, Teniers, Titian, Vandyck, and others. I shall be happy to lend you any work you like to mention, Miss Garth.”\n\n“I am much obliged,” said Mary, hastening away again, “but I have little time for reading.”\n\n“I should say my brother has done something for her in his will,” said Mr. Solomon, in a very low undertone, when she had shut the door behind her, pointing with his head towards the absent Mary.\n\n“His first wife was a poor match for him, though,” said Mrs. Waule. “She brought him nothing: and this young woman is only her niece,—and very proud. And my brother has always paid her wage.”\n\n“A sensible girl though, in my opinion,” said Mr. Trumbull, finishing his ale and starting up with an emphatic adjustment of his waistcoat. “I have observed her when she has been mixing medicine in drops. She minds what she is doing, sir. That is a great point in a woman, and a great point for our friend up-stairs, poor dear old soul. A man whose life is of any value should think of his wife as a nurse: that is what I should do, if I married; and I believe I have lived single long enough not to make a mistake in that line. Some men must marry to elevate themselves a little, but when I am in need of that, I hope some one will tell me so—I hope some individual will apprise me of the fact. I wish you good morning, Mrs. Waule. Good morning, Mr. Solomon. I trust we shall meet under less melancholy auspices.”\n\nWhen Mr. Trumbull had departed with a fine bow, Solomon, leaning forward, observed to his sister, “You may depend, Jane, my brother has left that girl a lumping sum.”\n\n“Anybody would think so, from the way Mr. Trumbull talks,” said Jane. Then, after a pause, “He talks as if my daughters wasn’t to be trusted to give drops.”\n\n“Auctioneers talk wild,” said Solomon. “Not but what Trumbull has made money.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII.\n\n“Close up his eyes and draw the curtain close; And let us all to meditation.” —2 Henry VI.\n\nThat night after twelve o’clock Mary Garth relieved the watch in Mr. Featherstone’s room, and sat there alone through the small hours. She often chose this task, in which she found some pleasure, notwithstanding the old man’s testiness whenever he demanded her attentions. There were intervals in which she could sit perfectly still, enjoying the outer stillness and the subdued light. The red fire with its gently audible movement seemed like a solemn existence calmly independent of the petty passions, the imbecile desires, the straining after worthless uncertainties, which were daily moving her contempt. Mary was fond of her own thoughts, and could amuse herself well sitting in twilight with her hands in her lap; for, having early had strong reason to believe that things were not likely to be arranged for her peculiar satisfaction, she wasted no time in astonishment and annoyance at that fact. And she had already come to take life very much as a comedy in which she had a proud, nay, a generous resolution not to act the mean or treacherous part. Mary might have become cynical if she had not had parents whom she honored, and a well of affectionate gratitude within her, which was all the fuller because she had learned to make no unreasonable claims.\n\nShe sat to-night revolving, as she was wont, the scenes of the day, her lips often curling with amusement at the oddities to which her fancy added fresh drollery: people were so ridiculous with their illusions, carrying their fool’s caps unawares, thinking their own lies opaque while everybody else’s were transparent, making themselves exceptions to everything, as if when all the world looked yellow under a lamp they alone were rosy. Yet there were some illusions under Mary’s eyes which were not quite comic to her. She was secretly convinced, though she had no other grounds than her close observation of old Featherstone’s nature, that in spite of his fondness for having the Vincys about him, they were as likely to be disappointed as any of the relations whom he kept at a distance. She had a good deal of disdain for Mrs. Vincy’s evident alarm lest she and Fred should be alone together, but it did not hinder her from thinking anxiously of the way in which Fred would be affected, if it should turn out that his uncle had left him as poor as ever. She could make a butt of Fred when he was present, but she did not enjoy his follies when he was absent.\n\nYet she liked her thoughts: a vigorous young mind not overbalanced by passion, finds a good in making acquaintance with life, and watches its own powers with interest. Mary had plenty of merriment within.\n\nHer thought was not veined by any solemnity or pathos about the old man on the bed: such sentiments are easier to affect than to feel about an aged creature whose life is not visibly anything but a remnant of vices. She had always seen the most disagreeable side of Mr. Featherstone: he was not proud of her, and she was only useful to him. To be anxious about a soul that is always snapping at you must be left to the saints of the earth; and Mary was not one of them. She had never returned him a harsh word, and had waited on him faithfully: that was her utmost. Old Featherstone himself was not in the least anxious about his soul, and had declined to see Mr. Tucker on the subject.\n\nTo-night he had not snapped, and for the first hour or two he lay remarkably still, until at last Mary heard him rattling his bunch of keys against the tin box which he always kept in the bed beside him. About three o’clock he said, with remarkable distinctness, “Missy, come here!”\n\nMary obeyed, and found that he had already drawn the tin box from under the clothes, though he usually asked to have this done for him; and he had selected the key. He now unlocked the box, and, drawing from it another key, looked straight at her with eyes that seemed to have recovered all their sharpness and said, “How many of ’em are in the house?”\n\n“You mean of your own relations, sir,” said Mary, well used to the old man’s way of speech. He nodded slightly and she went on.\n\n“Mr. Jonah Featherstone and young Cranch are sleeping here.”\n\n“Oh ay, they stick, do they? and the rest—they come every day, I’ll warrant—Solomon and Jane, and all the young uns? They come peeping, and counting and casting up?”\n\n“Not all of them every day. Mr. Solomon and Mrs. Waule are here every day, and the others come often.”\n\nThe old man listened with a grimace while she spoke, and then said, relaxing his face, “The more fools they. You hearken, missy. It’s three o’clock in the morning, and I’ve got all my faculties as well as ever I had in my life. I know all my property, and where the money’s put out, and everything. And I’ve made everything ready to change my mind, and do as I like at the last. Do you hear, missy? I’ve got my faculties.”\n\n“Well, sir?” said Mary, quietly.\n\nHe now lowered his tone with an air of deeper cunning. “I’ve made two wills, and I’m going to burn one. Now you do as I tell you. This is the key of my iron chest, in the closet there. You push well at the side of the brass plate at the top, till it goes like a bolt: then you can put the key in the front lock and turn it. See and do that; and take out the topmost paper—Last Will and Testament—big printed.”\n\n“No, sir,” said Mary, in a firm voice, “I cannot do that.”\n\n“Not do it? I tell you, you must,” said the old man, his voice beginning to shake under the shock of this resistance.\n\n“I cannot touch your iron chest or your will. I must refuse to do anything that might lay me open to suspicion.”\n\n“I tell you, I’m in my right mind. Shan’t I do as I like at the last? I made two wills on purpose. Take the key, I say.”\n\n“No, sir, I will not,” said Mary, more resolutely still. Her repulsion was getting stronger.\n\n“I tell you, there’s no time to lose.”\n\n“I cannot help that, sir. I will not let the close of your life soil the beginning of mine. I will not touch your iron chest or your will.” She moved to a little distance from the bedside.\n\nThe old man paused with a blank stare for a little while, holding the one key erect on the ring; then with an agitated jerk he began to work with his bony left hand at emptying the tin box before him.\n\n“Missy,” he began to say, hurriedly, “look here! take the money—the notes and gold—look here—take it—you shall have it all—do as I tell you.”\n\nHe made an effort to stretch out the key towards her as far as possible, and Mary again retreated.\n\n“I will not touch your key or your money, sir. Pray don’t ask me to do it again. If you do, I must go and call your brother.”\n\nHe let his hand fall, and for the first time in her life Mary saw old Peter Featherstone begin to cry childishly. She said, in as gentle a tone as she could command, “Pray put up your money, sir;” and then went away to her seat by the fire, hoping this would help to convince him that it was useless to say more. Presently he rallied and said eagerly—\n\n“Look here, then. Call the young chap. Call Fred Vincy.”\n\nMary’s heart began to beat more quickly. Various ideas rushed through her mind as to what the burning of a second will might imply. She had to make a difficult decision in a hurry.\n\n“I will call him, if you will let me call Mr. Jonah and others with him.”\n\n“Nobody else, I say. The young chap. I shall do as I like.”\n\n“Wait till broad daylight, sir, when every one is stirring. Or let me call Simmons now, to go and fetch the lawyer? He can be here in less than two hours.”\n\n“Lawyer? What do I want with the lawyer? Nobody shall know—I say, nobody shall know. I shall do as I like.”\n\n“Let me call some one else, sir,” said Mary, persuasively. She did not like her position—alone with the old man, who seemed to show a strange flaring of nervous energy which enabled him to speak again and again without falling into his usual cough; yet she desired not to push unnecessarily the contradiction which agitated him. “Let me, pray, call some one else.”\n\n“You let me alone, I say. Look here, missy. Take the money. You’ll never have the chance again. It’s pretty nigh two hundred—there’s more in the box, and nobody knows how much there was. Take it and do as I tell you.”\n\nMary, standing by the fire, saw its red light falling on the old man, propped up on his pillows and bed-rest, with his bony hand holding out the key, and the money lying on the quilt before him. She never forgot that vision of a man wanting to do as he liked at the last. But the way in which he had put the offer of the money urged her to speak with harder resolution than ever.\n\n“It is of no use, sir. I will not do it. Put up your money. I will not touch your money. I will do anything else I can to comfort you; but I will not touch your keys or your money.”\n\n“Anything else—anything else!” said old Featherstone, with hoarse rage, which, as if in a nightmare, tried to be loud, and yet was only just audible. “I want nothing else. You come here—you come here.”\n\nMary approached him cautiously, knowing him too well. She saw him dropping his keys and trying to grasp his stick, while he looked at her like an aged hyena, the muscles of his face getting distorted with the effort of his hand. She paused at a safe distance.\n\n“Let me give you some cordial,” she said, quietly, “and try to compose yourself. You will perhaps go to sleep. And to-morrow by daylight you can do as you like.”\n\nHe lifted the stick, in spite of her being beyond his reach, and threw it with a hard effort which was but impotence. It fell, slipping over the foot of the bed. Mary let it lie, and retreated to her chair by the fire. By-and-by she would go to him with the cordial. Fatigue would make him passive. It was getting towards the chillest moment of the morning, the fire had got low, and she could see through the chink between the moreen window-curtains the light whitened by the blind. Having put some wood on the fire and thrown a shawl over her, she sat down, hoping that Mr. Featherstone might now fall asleep. If she went near him the irritation might be kept up. He had said nothing after throwing the stick, but she had seen him taking his keys again and laying his right hand on the money. He did not put it up, however, and she thought that he was dropping off to sleep.\n\nBut Mary herself began to be more agitated by the remembrance of what she had gone through, than she had been by the reality—questioning those acts of hers which had come imperatively and excluded all question in the critical moment.\n\nPresently the dry wood sent out a flame which illuminated every crevice, and Mary saw that the old man was lying quietly with his head turned a little on one side. She went towards him with inaudible steps, and thought that his face looked strangely motionless; but the next moment the movement of the flame communicating itself to all objects made her uncertain. The violent beating of her heart rendered her perceptions so doubtful that even when she touched him and listened for his breathing, she could not trust her conclusions. She went to the window and gently propped aside the curtain and blind, so that the still light of the sky fell on the bed.\n\nThe next moment she ran to the bell and rang it energetically. In a very little while there was no longer any doubt that Peter Featherstone was dead, with his right hand clasping the keys, and his left hand lying on the heap of notes and gold.\n\n# BOOK IV. THREE LOVE PROBLEMS. CHAPTER XXXIV.\n\n“1st Gent. Such men as this are feathers, chips, and straws, Carry no weight, no force.\n\n2d Gent. But levity Is causal too, and makes the sum of weight. For power finds its place in lack of power; Advance is cession, and the driven ship May run aground because the helmsman’s thought Lacked force to balance opposites.”\n\nIt was on a morning of May that Peter Featherstone was buried. In the prosaic neighborhood of Middlemarch, May was not always warm and sunny, and on this particular morning a chill wind was blowing the blossoms from the surrounding gardens on to the green mounds of Lowick churchyard. Swiftly moving clouds only now and then allowed a gleam to light up any object, whether ugly or beautiful, that happened to stand within its golden shower. In the churchyard the objects were remarkably various, for there was a little country crowd waiting to see the funeral. The news had spread that it was to be a “big burying;” the old gentleman had left written directions about everything and meant to have a funeral “beyond his betters.” This was true; for old Featherstone had not been a Harpagon whose passions had all been devoured by the ever-lean and ever-hungry passion of saving, and who would drive a bargain with his undertaker beforehand. He loved money, but he also loved to spend it in gratifying his peculiar tastes, and perhaps he loved it best of all as a means of making others feel his power more or less uncomfortably. If any one will here contend that there must have been traits of goodness in old Featherstone, I will not presume to deny this; but I must observe that goodness is of a modest nature, easily discouraged, and when much privacy, elbowed in early life by unabashed vices, is apt to retire into extreme privacy, so that it is more easily believed in by those who construct a selfish old gentleman theoretically, than by those who form the narrower judgments based on his personal acquaintance. In any case, he had been bent on having a handsome funeral, and on having persons “bid” to it who would rather have stayed at home. He had even desired that female relatives should follow him to the grave, and poor sister Martha had taken a difficult journey for this purpose from the Chalky Flats. She and Jane would have been altogether cheered (in a tearful manner) by this sign that a brother who disliked seeing them while he was living had been prospectively fond of their presence when he should have become a testator, if the sign had not been made equivocal by being extended to Mrs. Vincy, whose expense in handsome crape seemed to imply the most presumptuous hopes, aggravated by a bloom of complexion which told pretty plainly that she was not a blood-relation, but of that generally objectionable class called wife’s kin.\n\nWe are all of us imaginative in some form or other, for images are the brood of desire; and poor old Featherstone, who laughed much at the way in which others cajoled themselves, did not escape the fellowship of illusion. In writing the programme for his burial he certainly did not make clear to himself that his pleasure in the little drama of which it formed a part was confined to anticipation. In chuckling over the vexations he could inflict by the rigid clutch of his dead hand, he inevitably mingled his consciousness with that livid stagnant presence, and so far as he was preoccupied with a future life, it was with one of gratification inside his coffin. Thus old Featherstone was imaginative, after his fashion.\n\nHowever, the three mourning-coaches were filled according to the written orders of the deceased. There were pall-bearers on horseback, with the richest scarfs and hatbands, and even the under-bearers had trappings of woe which were of a good well-priced quality. The black procession, when dismounted, looked the larger for the smallness of the churchyard; the heavy human faces and the black draperies shivering in the wind seemed to tell of a world strangely incongruous with the lightly dropping blossoms and the gleams of sunshine on the daisies. The clergyman who met the procession was Mr. Cadwallader—also according to the request of Peter Featherstone, prompted as usual by peculiar reasons. Having a contempt for curates, whom he always called understrappers, he was resolved to be buried by a beneficed clergyman. Mr. Casaubon was out of the question, not merely because he declined duty of this sort, but because Featherstone had an especial dislike to him as the rector of his own parish, who had a lien on the land in the shape of tithe, also as the deliverer of morning sermons, which the old man, being in his pew and not at all sleepy, had been obliged to sit through with an inward snarl. He had an objection to a parson stuck up above his head preaching to him. But his relations with Mr. Cadwallader had been of a different kind: the trout-stream which ran through Mr. Casaubon’s land took its course through Featherstone’s also, so that Mr. Cadwallader was a parson who had had to ask a favor instead of preaching. Moreover, he was one of the high gentry living four miles away from Lowick, and was thus exalted to an equal sky with the sheriff of the county and other dignities vaguely regarded as necessary to the system of things. There would be a satisfaction in being buried by Mr. Cadwallader, whose very name offered a fine opportunity for pronouncing wrongly if you liked.\n\nThis distinction conferred on the Rector of Tipton and Freshitt was the reason why Mrs. Cadwallader made one of the group that watched old Featherstone’s funeral from an upper window of the manor. She was not fond of visiting that house, but she liked, as she said, to see collections of strange animals such as there would be at this funeral; and she had persuaded Sir James and the young Lady Chettam to drive the Rector and herself to Lowick in order that the visit might be altogether pleasant.\n“I will go anywhere with you, Mrs. Cadwallader,” Celia had said; “but I don’t like funerals.”\n\n“Oh, my dear, when you have a clergyman in your family you must accommodate your tastes: I did that very early. When I married Humphrey I made up my mind to like sermons, and I set out by liking the end very much. That soon spread to the middle and the beginning, because I couldn’t have the end without them.”\n\n“No, to be sure not,” said the Dowager Lady Chettam, with stately emphasis.\n\nThe upper window from which the funeral could be well seen was in the room occupied by Mr. Casaubon when he had been forbidden to work; but he had resumed nearly his habitual style of life now in spite of warnings and prescriptions, and after politely welcoming Mrs. Cadwallader had slipped again into the library to chew a cud of erudite mistake about Cush and Mizraim.\n\nBut for her visitors Dorothea too might have been shut up in the library, and would not have witnessed this scene of old Featherstone’s funeral, which, aloof as it seemed to be from the tenor of her life, always afterwards came back to her at the touch of certain sensitive points in memory, just as the vision of St. Peter’s at Rome was inwoven with moods of despondency. Scenes which make vital changes in our neighbors’ lot are but the background of our own, yet, like a particular aspect of the fields and trees, they become associated for us with the epochs of our own history, and make a part of that unity which lies in the selection of our keenest consciousness.\n\nThe dream-like association of something alien and ill-understood with the deepest secrets of her experience seemed to mirror that sense of loneliness which was due to the very ardor of Dorothea’s nature. The country gentry of old time lived in a rarefied social air: dotted apart on their stations up the mountain they looked down with imperfect discrimination on the belts of thicker life below. And Dorothea was not at ease in the perspective and chilliness of that height.\n\n“I shall not look any more,” said Celia, after the train had entered the church, placing herself a little behind her husband’s elbow so that she could slyly touch his coat with her cheek. “I dare say Dodo likes it: she is fond of melancholy things and ugly people.”\n\n“I am fond of knowing something about the people I live among,” said Dorothea, who had been watching everything with the interest of a monk on his holiday tour. “It seems to me we know nothing of our neighbors, unless they are cottagers. One is constantly wondering what sort of lives other people lead, and how they take things. I am quite obliged to Mrs. Cadwallader for coming and calling me out of the library.”\n\n“Quite right to feel obliged to me,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “Your rich Lowick farmers are as curious as any buffaloes or bisons, and I dare say you don’t half see them at church. They are quite different from your uncle’s tenants or Sir James’s—monsters—farmers without landlords—one can’t tell how to class them.”\n\n“Most of these followers are not Lowick people,” said Sir James; “I suppose they are legatees from a distance, or from Middlemarch. Lovegood tells me the old fellow has left a good deal of money as well as land.”\n\n“Think of that now! when so many younger sons can’t dine at their own expense,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “Ah,” turning round at the sound of the opening door, “here is Mr. Brooke. I felt that we were incomplete before, and here is the explanation. You are come to see this odd funeral, of course?”\n\n“No, I came to look after Casaubon—to see how he goes on, you know. And to bring a little news—a little news, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, nodding at Dorothea as she came towards him. “I looked into the library, and I saw Casaubon over his books. I told him it wouldn’t do: I said, ‘This will never do, you know: think of your wife, Casaubon.’ And he promised me to come up. I didn’t tell him my news: I said, he must come up.”\n\n“Ah, now they are coming out of church,” Mrs. Cadwallader exclaimed. “Dear me, what a wonderfully mixed set! Mr. Lydgate as doctor, I suppose. But that is really a good looking woman, and the fair young man must be her son. Who are they, Sir James, do you know?”\n\n“I see Vincy, the Mayor of Middlemarch; they are probably his wife and son,” said Sir James, looking interrogatively at Mr. Brooke, who nodded and said—\n\n“Yes, a very decent family—a very good fellow is Vincy; a credit to the manufacturing interest. You have seen him at my house, you know.”\n\n“Ah, yes: one of your secret committee,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, provokingly.\n\n“A coursing fellow, though,” said Sir James, with a fox-hunter’s disgust.\n\n“And one of those who suck the life out of the wretched handloom weavers in Tipton and Freshitt. That is how his family look so fair and sleek,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “Those dark, purple-faced people are an excellent foil. Dear me, they are like a set of jugs! Do look at Humphrey: one might fancy him an ugly archangel towering above them in his white surplice.”\n\n“It’s a solemn thing, though, a funeral,” said Mr. Brooke, “if you take it in that light, you know.”\n\n“But I am not taking it in that light. I can’t wear my solemnity too often, else it will go to rags. It was time the old man died, and none of these people are sorry.”\n\n“How piteous!” said Dorothea. “This funeral seems to me the most dismal thing I ever saw. It is a blot on the morning. I cannot bear to think that any one should die and leave no love behind.”\n\nShe was going to say more, but she saw her husband enter and seat himself a little in the background. The difference his presence made to her was not always a happy one: she felt that he often inwardly objected to her speech.\n\n“Positively,” exclaimed Mrs. Cadwallader, “there is a new face come out from behind that broad man queerer than any of them: a little round head with bulging eyes—a sort of frog-face—do look. He must be of another blood, I think.”\n\n“Let me see!” said Celia, with awakened curiosity, standing behind Mrs. Cadwallader and leaning forward over her head. “Oh, what an odd face!” Then with a quick change to another sort of surprised expression, she added, “Why, Dodo, you never told me that Mr. Ladislaw was come again!”\n\nDorothea felt a shock of alarm: every one noticed her sudden paleness as she looked up immediately at her uncle, while Mr. Casaubon looked at her.\n\n“He came with me, you know; he is my guest—puts up with me at the Grange,” said Mr. Brooke, in his easiest tone, nodding at Dorothea, as if the announcement were just what she might have expected. “And we have brought the picture at the top of the carriage. I knew you would be pleased with the surprise, Casaubon. There you are to the very life—as Aquinas, you know. Quite the right sort of thing. And you will hear young Ladislaw talk about it. He talks uncommonly well—points out this, that, and the other—knows art and everything of that kind—companionable, you know—is up with you in any track—what I’ve been wanting a long while.”\n\nMr. Casaubon bowed with cold politeness, mastering his irritation, but only so far as to be silent. He remembered Will’s letter quite as well as Dorothea did; he had noticed that it was not among the letters which had been reserved for him on his recovery, and secretly concluding that Dorothea had sent word to Will not to come to Lowick, he had shrunk with proud sensitiveness from ever recurring to the subject. He now inferred that she had asked her uncle to invite Will to the Grange; and she felt it impossible at that moment to enter into any explanation.\n\nMrs. Cadwallader’s eyes, diverted from the churchyard, saw a good deal of dumb show which was not so intelligible to her as she could have desired, and could not repress the question, “Who is Mr. Ladislaw?”\n\n“A young relative of Mr. Casaubon’s,” said Sir James, promptly. His good-nature often made him quick and clear-seeing in personal matters, and he had divined from Dorothea’s glance at her husband that there was some alarm in her mind.\n\n“A very nice young fellow—Casaubon has done everything for him,” explained Mr. Brooke. “He repays your expense in him, Casaubon,” he went on, nodding encouragingly. “I hope he will stay with me a long while and we shall make something of my documents. I have plenty of ideas and facts, you know, and I can see he is just the man to put them into shape—remembers what the right quotations are, omne tulit punctum, and that sort of thing—gives subjects a kind of turn. I invited him some time ago when you were ill, Casaubon; Dorothea said you couldn’t have anybody in the house, you know, and she asked me to write.”\n\nPoor Dorothea felt that every word of her uncle’s was about as pleasant as a grain of sand in the eye to Mr. Casaubon. It would be altogether unfitting now to explain that she had not wished her uncle to invite Will Ladislaw. She could not in the least make clear to herself the reasons for her husband’s dislike to his presence—a dislike painfully impressed on her by the scene in the library; but she felt the unbecomingness of saying anything that might convey a notion of it to others. Mr. Casaubon, indeed, had not thoroughly represented those mixed reasons to himself; irritated feeling with him, as with all of us, seeking rather for justification than for self-knowledge. But he wished to repress outward signs, and only Dorothea could discern the changes in her husband’s face before he observed with more of dignified bending and sing-song than usual—\n\n“You are exceedingly hospitable, my dear sir; and I owe you acknowledgments for exercising your hospitality towards a relative of mine.”\n\nThe funeral was ended now, and the churchyard was being cleared.\n\n“Now you can see him, Mrs. Cadwallader,” said Celia. “He is just like a miniature of Mr. Casaubon’s aunt that hangs in Dorothea’s boudoir—quite nice-looking.”\n\n“A very pretty sprig,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, dryly. “What is your nephew to be, Mr. Casaubon?”\n\n“Pardon me, he is not my nephew. He is my cousin.”\n\n“Well, you know,” interposed Mr. Brooke, “he is trying his wings. He is just the sort of young fellow to rise. I should be glad to give him an opportunity. He would make a good secretary, now, like Hobbes, Milton, Swift—that sort of man.”\n\n“I understand,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “One who can write speeches.”\n\n“I’ll fetch him in now, eh, Casaubon?” said Mr. Brooke. “He wouldn’t come in till I had announced him, you know. And we’ll go down and look at the picture. There you are to the life: a deep subtle sort of thinker with his fore-finger on the page, while Saint Bonaventure or somebody else, rather fat and florid, is looking up at the Trinity. Everything is symbolical, you know—the higher style of art: I like that up to a certain point, but not too far—it’s rather straining to keep up with, you know. But you are at home in that, Casaubon. And your painter’s flesh is good—solidity, transparency, everything of that sort. I went into that a great deal at one time. However, I’ll go and fetch Ladislaw.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV.\n\n“Non, je ne comprends pas de plus charmant plaisir Que de voir d’héritiers une troupe affligée Le maintien interdit, et la mine allongée, Lire un long testament où pales, étonnés On leur laisse un bonsoir avec un pied de nez. Pour voir au naturel leur tristesse profonde Je reviendrais, je crois, exprès de l’autre monde.” —REGNARD: Le Légataire Universel.\n\nWhen the animals entered the Ark in pairs, one may imagine that allied species made much private remark on each other, and were tempted to think that so many forms feeding on the same store of fodder were eminently superfluous, as tending to diminish the rations. (I fear the part played by the vultures on that occasion would be too painful for art to represent, those birds being disadvantageously naked about the gullet, and apparently without rites and ceremonies.)\n\nThe same sort of temptation befell the Christian Carnivora who formed Peter Featherstone’s funeral procession; most of them having their minds bent on a limited store which each would have liked to get the most of. The long-recognized blood-relations and connections by marriage made already a goodly number, which, multiplied by possibilities, presented a fine range for jealous conjecture and pathetic hopefulness. Jealousy of the Vincys had created a fellowship in hostility among all persons of the Featherstone blood, so that in the absence of any decided indication that one of themselves was to have more than the rest, the dread lest that long-legged Fred Vincy should have the land was necessarily dominant, though it left abundant feeling and leisure for vaguer jealousies, such as were entertained towards Mary Garth. Solomon found time to reflect that Jonah was undeserving, and Jonah to abuse Solomon as greedy; Jane, the elder sister, held that Martha’s children ought not to expect so much as the young Waules; and Martha, more lax on the subject of primogeniture, was sorry to think that Jane was so “having.” These nearest of kin were naturally impressed with the unreasonableness of expectations in cousins and second cousins, and used their arithmetic in reckoning the large sums that small legacies might mount to, if there were too many of them. Two cousins were present to hear the will, and a second cousin besides Mr. Trumbull. This second cousin was a Middlemarch mercer of polite manners and superfluous aspirates. The two cousins were elderly men from Brassing, one of them conscious of claims on the score of inconvenient expense sustained by him in presents of oysters and other eatables to his rich cousin Peter; the other entirely saturnine, leaning his hands and chin on a stick, and conscious of claims based on no narrow performance but on merit generally: both blameless citizens of Brassing, who wished that Jonah Featherstone did not live there. The wit of a family is usually best received among strangers.\n\n“Why, Trumbull himself is pretty sure of five hundred—that you may depend,—I shouldn’t wonder if my brother promised him,” said Solomon, musing aloud with his sisters, the evening before the funeral.\n\n“Dear, dear!” said poor sister Martha, whose imagination of hundreds had been habitually narrowed to the amount of her unpaid rent.\n\nBut in the morning all the ordinary currents of conjecture were disturbed by the presence of a strange mourner who had plashed among them as if from the moon. This was the stranger described by Mrs. Cadwallader as frog-faced: a man perhaps about two or three and thirty, whose prominent eyes, thin-lipped, downward-curved mouth, and hair sleekly brushed away from a forehead that sank suddenly above the ridge of the eyebrows, certainly gave his face a batrachian unchangeableness of expression. Here, clearly, was a new legatee; else why was he bidden as a mourner? Here were new possibilities, raising a new uncertainty, which almost checked remark in the mourning-coaches. We are all humiliated by the sudden discovery of a fact which has existed very comfortably and perhaps been staring at us in private while we have been making up our world entirely without it. No one had seen this questionable stranger before except Mary Garth, and she knew nothing more of him than that he had twice been to Stone Court when Mr. Featherstone was down-stairs, and had sat alone with him for several hours. She had found an opportunity of mentioning this to her father, and perhaps Caleb’s were the only eyes, except the lawyer’s, which examined the stranger with more of inquiry than of disgust or suspicion. Caleb Garth, having little expectation and less cupidity, was interested in the verification of his own guesses, and the calmness with which he half smilingly rubbed his chin and shot intelligent glances much as if he were valuing a tree, made a fine contrast with the alarm or scorn visible in other faces when the unknown mourner, whose name was understood to be Rigg, entered the wainscoted parlor and took his seat near the door to make part of the audience when the will should be read. Just then Mr. Solomon and Mr. Jonah were gone up-stairs with the lawyer to search for the will; and Mrs. Waule, seeing two vacant seats between herself and Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, had the spirit to move next to that great authority, who was handling his watch-seals and trimming his outlines with a determination not to show anything so compromising to a man of ability as wonder or surprise.\n\n“I suppose you know everything about what my poor brother’s done, Mr. Trumbull,” said Mrs. Waule, in the lowest of her woolly tones, while she turned her crape-shadowed bonnet towards Mr. Trumbull’s ear.\n\n“My good lady, whatever was told me was told in confidence,” said the auctioneer, putting his hand up to screen that secret.\n\n“Them who’ve made sure of their good-luck may be disappointed yet,” Mrs. Waule continued, finding some relief in this communication.\n\n“Hopes are often delusive,” said Mr. Trumbull, still in confidence.\n\n“Ah!” said Mrs. Waule, looking across at the Vincys, and then moving back to the side of her sister Martha.\n\n“It’s wonderful how close poor Peter was,” she said, in the same undertones. “We none of us know what he might have had on his mind. I only hope and trust he wasn’t a worse liver than we think of, Martha.”\n\nPoor Mrs. Cranch was bulky, and, breathing asthmatically, had the additional motive for making her remarks unexceptionable and giving them a general bearing, that even her whispers were loud and liable to sudden bursts like those of a deranged barrel-organ.\n\n“I never was covetous, Jane,” she replied; “but I have six children and have buried three, and I didn’t marry into money. The eldest, that sits there, is but nineteen—so I leave you to guess. And stock always short, and land most awkward. But if ever I’ve begged and prayed; it’s been to God above; though where there’s one brother a bachelor and the other childless after twice marrying—anybody might think!”\n\nMeanwhile, Mr. Vincy had glanced at the passive face of Mr. Rigg, and had taken out his snuff-box and tapped it, but had put it again unopened as an indulgence which, however clarifying to the judgment, was unsuited to the occasion. “I shouldn’t wonder if Featherstone had better feelings than any of us gave him credit for,” he observed, in the ear of his wife. “This funeral shows a thought about everybody: it looks well when a man wants to be followed by his friends, and if they are humble, not to be ashamed of them. I should be all the better pleased if he’d left lots of small legacies. They may be uncommonly useful to fellows in a small way.”\n\n“Everything is as handsome as could be, crape and silk and everything,” said Mrs. Vincy, contentedly.\n\nBut I am sorry to say that Fred was under some difficulty in repressing a laugh, which would have been more unsuitable than his father’s snuff-box. Fred had overheard Mr. Jonah suggesting something about a “love-child,” and with this thought in his mind, the stranger’s face, which happened to be opposite him, affected him too ludicrously. Mary Garth, discerning his distress in the twitchings of his mouth, and his recourse to a cough, came cleverly to his rescue by asking him to change seats with her, so that he got into a shadowy corner. Fred was feeling as good-naturedly as possible towards everybody, including Rigg; and having some relenting towards all these people who were less lucky than he was aware of being himself, he would not for the world have behaved amiss; still, it was particularly easy to laugh.\n\nBut the entrance of the lawyer and the two brothers drew every one’s attention. The lawyer was Mr. Standish, and he had come to Stone Court this morning believing that he knew thoroughly well who would be pleased and who disappointed before the day was over. The will he expected to read was the last of three which he had drawn up for Mr. Featherstone. Mr. Standish was not a man who varied his manners: he behaved with the same deep-voiced, off-hand civility to everybody, as if he saw no difference in them, and talked chiefly of the hay-crop, which would be “very fine, by God!” of the last bulletins concerning the King, and of the Duke of Clarence, who was a sailor every inch of him, and just the man to rule over an island like Britain.\n\nOld Featherstone had often reflected as he sat looking at the fire that Standish would be surprised some day: it is true that if he had done as he liked at the last, and burnt the will drawn up by another lawyer, he would not have secured that minor end; still he had had his pleasure in ruminating on it. And certainly Mr. Standish was surprised, but not at all sorry; on the contrary, he rather enjoyed the zest of a little curiosity in his own mind, which the discovery of a second will added to the prospective amazement on the part of the Featherstone family.\n\nAs to the sentiments of Solomon and Jonah, they were held in utter suspense: it seemed to them that the old will would have a certain validity, and that there might be such an interlacement of poor Peter’s former and latter intentions as to create endless “lawing” before anybody came by their own—an inconvenience which would have at least the advantage of going all round. Hence the brothers showed a thoroughly neutral gravity as they re-entered with Mr. Standish; but Solomon took out his white handkerchief again with a sense that in any case there would be affecting passages, and crying at funerals, however dry, was customarily served up in lawn.\n\nPerhaps the person who felt the most throbbing excitement at this moment was Mary Garth, in the consciousness that it was she who had virtually determined the production of this second will, which might have momentous effects on the lot of some persons present. No soul except herself knew what had passed on that final night.\n\n“The will I hold in my hand,” said Mr. Standish, who, seated at the table in the middle of the room, took his time about everything, including the coughs with which he showed a disposition to clear his voice, “was drawn up by myself and executed by our deceased friend on the 9th of August, 1825. But I find that there is a subsequent instrument hitherto unknown to me, bearing date the 20th of July, 1826, hardly a year later than the previous one. And there is farther, I see”—Mr. Standish was cautiously travelling over the document with his spectacles—“a codicil to this latter will, bearing date March 1, 1828.”\n\n“Dear, dear!” said sister Martha, not meaning to be audible, but driven to some articulation under this pressure of dates.\n\n“I shall begin by reading the earlier will,” continued Mr. Standish, “since such, as appears by his not having destroyed the document, was the intention of the deceased.”\n\nThe preamble was felt to be rather long, and several besides Solomon shook their heads pathetically, looking on the ground: all eyes avoided meeting other eyes, and were chiefly fixed either on the spots in the table-cloth or on Mr. Standish’s bald head; excepting Mary Garth’s. When all the rest were trying to look nowhere in particular, it was safe for her to look at them. And at the sound of the first “give and bequeath” she could see all complexions changing subtly, as if some faint vibration were passing through them, save that of Mr. Rigg. He sat in unaltered calm, and, in fact, the company, preoccupied with more important problems, and with the complication of listening to bequests which might or might not be revoked, had ceased to think of him. Fred blushed, and Mr. Vincy found it impossible to do without his snuff-box in his hand, though he kept it closed.\n\nThe small bequests came first, and even the recollection that there was another will and that poor Peter might have thought better of it, could not quell the rising disgust and indignation. One likes to be done well by in every tense, past, present, and future. And here was Peter capable five years ago of leaving only two hundred apiece to his own brothers and sisters, and only a hundred apiece to his own nephews and nieces: the Garths were not mentioned, but Mrs. Vincy and Rosamond were each to have a hundred. Mr. Trumbull was to have the gold-headed cane and fifty pounds; the other second cousins and the cousins present were each to have the like handsome sum, which, as the saturnine cousin observed, was a sort of legacy that left a man nowhere; and there was much more of such offensive dribbling in favor of persons not present—problematical, and, it was to be feared, low connections. Altogether, reckoning hastily, here were about three thousand disposed of. Where then had Peter meant the rest of the money to go—and where the land? and what was revoked and what not revoked—and was the revocation for better or for worse? All emotion must be conditional, and might turn out to be the wrong thing. The men were strong enough to bear up and keep quiet under this confused suspense; some letting their lower lip fall, others pursing it up, according to the habit of their muscles. But Jane and Martha sank under the rush of questions, and began to cry; poor Mrs. Cranch being half moved with the consolation of getting any hundreds at all without working for them, and half aware that her share was scanty; whereas Mrs. Waule’s mind was entirely flooded with the sense of being an own sister and getting little, while somebody else was to have much. The general expectation now was that the “much” would fall to Fred Vincy, but the Vincys themselves were surprised when ten thousand pounds in specified investments were declared to be bequeathed to him:—was the land coming too? Fred bit his lips: it was difficult to help smiling, and Mrs. Vincy felt herself the happiest of women—possible revocation shrinking out of sight in this dazzling vision.\n\nThere was still a residue of personal property as well as the land, but the whole was left to one person, and that person was—O possibilities! O expectations founded on the favor of “close” old gentlemen! O endless vocatives that would still leave expression slipping helpless from the measurement of mortal folly!—that residuary legatee was Joshua Rigg, who was also sole executor, and who was to take thenceforth the name of Featherstone.\n\nThere was a rustling which seemed like a shudder running round the room. Every one stared afresh at Mr. Rigg, who apparently experienced no surprise.\n\n“A most singular testamentary disposition!” exclaimed Mr. Trumbull, preferring for once that he should be considered ignorant in the past. “But there is a second will—there is a further document. We have not yet heard the final wishes of the deceased.”\n\nMary Garth was feeling that what they had yet to hear were not the final wishes. The second will revoked everything except the legacies to the low persons before mentioned (some alterations in these being the occasion of the codicil), and the bequest of all the land lying in Lowick parish with all the stock and household furniture, to Joshua Rigg. The residue of the property was to be devoted to the erection and endowment of almshouses for old men, to be called Featherstone’s Alms-Houses, and to be built on a piece of land near Middlemarch already bought for the purpose by the testator, he wishing—so the document declared—to please God Almighty. Nobody present had a farthing; but Mr. Trumbull had the gold-headed cane. It took some time for the company to recover the power of expression. Mary dared not look at Fred.\n\nMr. Vincy was the first to speak—after using his snuff-box energetically—and he spoke with loud indignation. “The most unaccountable will I ever heard! I should say he was not in his right mind when he made it. I should say this last will was void,” added Mr. Vincy, feeling that this expression put the thing in the true light. “Eh Standish?”\n\n“Our deceased friend always knew what he was about, I think,” said Mr. Standish. “Everything is quite regular. Here is a letter from Clemmens of Brassing tied with the will. He drew it up. A very respectable solicitor.”\n\n“I never noticed any alienation of mind—any aberration of intellect in the late Mr. Featherstone,” said Borthrop Trumbull, “but I call this will eccentric. I was always willingly of service to the old soul; and he intimated pretty plainly a sense of obligation which would show itself in his will. The gold-headed cane is farcical considered as an acknowledgment to me; but happily I am above mercenary considerations.”\n\n“There’s nothing very surprising in the matter that I can see,” said Caleb Garth. “Anybody might have had more reason for wondering if the will had been what you might expect from an open-minded straightforward man. For my part, I wish there was no such thing as a will.”\n\n“That’s a strange sentiment to come from a Christian man, by God!” said the lawyer. “I should like to know how you will back that up, Garth!”\n\n“Oh,” said Caleb, leaning forward, adjusting his finger-tips with nicety and looking meditatively on the ground. It always seemed to him that words were the hardest part of “business.”\n\nBut here Mr. Jonah Featherstone made himself heard. “Well, he always was a fine hypocrite, was my brother Peter. But this will cuts out everything. If I’d known, a wagon and six horses shouldn’t have drawn me from Brassing. I’ll put a white hat and drab coat on to-morrow.”\n\n“Dear, dear,” wept Mrs. Cranch, “and we’ve been at the expense of travelling, and that poor lad sitting idle here so long! It’s the first time I ever heard my brother Peter was so wishful to please God Almighty; but if I was to be struck helpless I must say it’s hard—I can think no other.”\n\n“It’ll do him no good where he’s gone, that’s my belief,” said Solomon, with a bitterness which was remarkably genuine, though his tone could not help being sly. “Peter was a bad liver, and almshouses won’t cover it, when he’s had the impudence to show it at the last.”\n\n“And all the while had got his own lawful family—brothers and sisters and nephews and nieces—and has sat in church with ’em whenever he thought well to come,” said Mrs. Waule. “And might have left his property so respectable, to them that’s never been used to extravagance or unsteadiness in no manner of way—and not so poor but what they could have saved every penny and made more of it. And me—the trouble I’ve been at, times and times, to come here and be sisterly—and him with things on his mind all the while that might make anybody’s flesh creep. But if the Almighty’s allowed it, he means to punish him for it. Brother Solomon, I shall be going, if you’ll drive me.”\n\n“I’ve no desire to put my foot on the premises again,” said Solomon. “I’ve got land of my own and property of my own to will away.”\n\n“It’s a poor tale how luck goes in the world,” said Jonah. “It never answers to have a bit of spirit in you. You’d better be a dog in the manger. But those above ground might learn a lesson. One fool’s will is enough in a family.”\n\n“There’s more ways than one of being a fool,” said Solomon. “I shan’t leave my money to be poured down the sink, and I shan’t leave it to foundlings from Africay. I like Featherstones that were brewed such, and not turned Featherstones with sticking the name on ’em.”\n\nSolomon addressed these remarks in a loud aside to Mrs. Waule as he rose to accompany her. Brother Jonah felt himself capable of much more stinging wit than this, but he reflected that there was no use in offending the new proprietor of Stone Court, until you were certain that he was quite without intentions of hospitality towards witty men whose name he was about to bear.\n\nMr. Joshua Rigg, in fact, appeared to trouble himself little about any innuendoes, but showed a notable change of manner, walking coolly up to Mr. Standish and putting business questions with much coolness. He had a high chirping voice and a vile accent. Fred, whom he no longer moved to laughter, thought him the lowest monster he had ever seen. But Fred was feeling rather sick. The Middlemarch mercer waited for an opportunity of engaging Mr. Rigg in conversation: there was no knowing how many pairs of legs the new proprietor might require hose for, and profits were more to be relied on than legacies. Also, the mercer, as a second cousin, was dispassionate enough to feel curiosity.\n\nMr. Vincy, after his one outburst, had remained proudly silent, though too much preoccupied with unpleasant feelings to think of moving, till he observed that his wife had gone to Fred’s side and was crying silently while she held her darling’s hand. He rose immediately, and turning his back on the company while he said to her in an undertone,—“Don’t give way, Lucy; don’t make a fool of yourself, my dear, before these people,” he added in his usual loud voice—“Go and order the phaeton, Fred; I have no time to waste.”\n\nMary Garth had before this been getting ready to go home with her father. She met Fred in the hall, and now for the first time had the courage to look at him. He had that withered sort of paleness which will sometimes come on young faces, and his hand was very cold when she shook it. Mary too was agitated; she was conscious that fatally, without will of her own, she had perhaps made a great difference to Fred’s lot.\n\n“Good-by,” she said, with affectionate sadness. “Be brave, Fred. I do believe you are better without the money. What was the good of it to Mr. Featherstone?”\n\n“That’s all very fine,” said Fred, pettishly. “What is a fellow to do? I must go into the Church now.” (He knew that this would vex Mary: very well; then she must tell him what else he could do.) “And I thought I should be able to pay your father at once and make everything right. And you have not even a hundred pounds left you. What shall you do now, Mary?”\n\n“Take another situation, of course, as soon as I can get one. My father has enough to do to keep the rest, without me. Good-by.”\n\nIn a very short time Stone Court was cleared of well-brewed Featherstones and other long-accustomed visitors. Another stranger had been brought to settle in the neighborhood of Middlemarch, but in the case of Mr. Rigg Featherstone there was more discontent with immediate visible consequences than speculation as to the effect which his presence might have in the future. No soul was prophetic enough to have any foreboding as to what might appear on the trial of Joshua Rigg.\n\nAnd here I am naturally led to reflect on the means of elevating a low subject. Historical parallels are remarkably efficient in this way. The chief objection to them is, that the diligent narrator may lack space, or (what is often the same thing) may not be able to think of them with any degree of particularity, though he may have a philosophical confidence that if known they would be illustrative. It seems an easier and shorter way to dignity, to observe that—since there never was a true story which could not be told in parables, where you might put a monkey for a margrave, and vice versa—whatever has been or is to be narrated by me about low people, may be ennobled by being considered a parable; so that if any bad habits and ugly consequences are brought into view, the reader may have the relief of regarding them as not more than figuratively ungenteel, and may feel himself virtually in company with persons of some style. Thus while I tell the truth about loobies, my reader’s imagination need not be entirely excluded from an occupation with lords; and the petty sums which any bankrupt of high standing would be sorry to retire upon, may be lifted to the level of high commercial transactions by the inexpensive addition of proportional ciphers.\n\nAs to any provincial history in which the agents are all of high moral rank, that must be of a date long posterior to the first Reform Bill, and Peter Featherstone, you perceive, was dead and buried some months before Lord Grey came into office.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVI.\n\n’T is strange to see the humors of these men, These great aspiring spirits, that should be wise: . . . . . . . . For being the nature of great spirits to love To be where they may be most eminent; They, rating of themselves so farre above Us in conceit, with whom they do frequent, Imagine how we wonder and esteeme All that they do or say; which makes them strive To make our admiration more extreme, Which they suppose they cannot, ’less they give Notice of their extreme and highest thoughts. —DANIEL: Tragedy of Philotas.\n\nMr. Vincy went home from the reading of the will with his point of view considerably changed in relation to many subjects. He was an open-minded man, but given to indirect modes of expressing himself: when he was disappointed in a market for his silk braids, he swore at the groom; when his brother-in-law Bulstrode had vexed him, he made cutting remarks on Methodism; and it was now apparent that he regarded Fred’s idleness with a sudden increase of severity, by his throwing an embroidered cap out of the smoking-room on to the hall-floor.\n\n“Well, sir,” he observed, when that young gentleman was moving off to bed, “I hope you’ve made up your mind now to go up next term and pass your examination. I’ve taken my resolution, so I advise you to lose no time in taking yours.”\n\nFred made no answer: he was too utterly depressed. Twenty-four hours ago he had thought that instead of needing to know what he should do, he should by this time know that he needed to do nothing: that he should hunt in pink, have a first-rate hunter, ride to cover on a fine hack, and be generally respected for doing so; moreover, that he should be able at once to pay Mr. Garth, and that Mary could no longer have any reason for not marrying him. And all this was to have come without study or other inconvenience, purely by the favor of providence in the shape of an old gentleman’s caprice. But now, at the end of the twenty-four hours, all those firm expectations were upset. It was “rather hard lines” that while he was smarting under this disappointment he should be treated as if he could have helped it. But he went away silently and his mother pleaded for him.\n\n“Don’t be hard on the poor boy, Vincy. He’ll turn out well yet, though that wicked man has deceived him. I feel as sure as I sit here, Fred will turn out well—else why was he brought back from the brink of the grave? And I call it a robbery: it was like giving him the land, to promise it; and what is promising, if making everybody believe is not promising? And you see he did leave him ten thousand pounds, and then took it away again.”\n\n“Took it away again!” said Mr. Vincy, pettishly. “I tell you the lad’s an unlucky lad, Lucy. And you’ve always spoiled him.”\n\n“Well, Vincy, he was my first, and you made a fine fuss with him when he came. You were as proud as proud,” said Mrs. Vincy, easily recovering her cheerful smile.\n\n“Who knows what babies will turn to? I was fool enough, I dare say,” said the husband—more mildly, however.\n\n“But who has handsomer, better children than ours? Fred is far beyond other people’s sons: you may hear it in his speech, that he has kept college company. And Rosamond—where is there a girl like her? She might stand beside any lady in the land, and only look the better for it. You see—Mr. Lydgate has kept the highest company and been everywhere, and he fell in love with her at once. Not but what I could have wished Rosamond had not engaged herself. She might have met somebody on a visit who would have been a far better match; I mean at her schoolfellow Miss Willoughby’s. There are relations in that family quite as high as Mr. Lydgate’s.”\n\n“Damn relations!” said Mr. Vincy; “I’ve had enough of them. I don’t want a son-in-law who has got nothing but his relations to recommend him.”\n\n“Why, my dear,” said Mrs. Vincy, “you seemed as pleased as could be about it. It’s true, I wasn’t at home; but Rosamond told me you hadn’t a word to say against the engagement. And she has begun to buy in the best linen and cambric for her underclothing.”\n\n“Not by my will,” said Mr. Vincy. “I shall have enough to do this year, with an idle scamp of a son, without paying for wedding-clothes. The times are as tight as can be; everybody is being ruined; and I don’t believe Lydgate has got a farthing. I shan’t give my consent to their marrying. Let ’em wait, as their elders have done before ’em.”\n\n“Rosamond will take it hard, Vincy, and you know you never could bear to cross her.”\n\n“Yes, I could. The sooner the engagement’s off, the better. I don’t believe he’ll ever make an income, the way he goes on. He makes enemies; that’s all I hear of his making.”\n\n“But he stands very high with Mr. Bulstrode, my dear. The marriage would please him, I should think.”\n\n“Please the deuce!” said Mr. Vincy. “Bulstrode won’t pay for their keep. And if Lydgate thinks I’m going to give money for them to set up housekeeping, he’s mistaken, that’s all. I expect I shall have to put down my horses soon. You’d better tell Rosy what I say.”\n\nThis was a not infrequent procedure with Mr. Vincy—to be rash in jovial assent, and on becoming subsequently conscious that he had been rash, to employ others in making the offensive retractation. However, Mrs. Vincy, who never willingly opposed her husband, lost no time the next morning in letting Rosamond know what he had said. Rosamond, examining some muslin-work, listened in silence, and at the end gave a certain turn of her graceful neck, of which only long experience could teach you that it meant perfect obstinacy.\n\n“What do you say, my dear?” said her mother, with affectionate deference.\n\n“Papa does not mean anything of the kind,” said Rosamond, quite calmly. “He has always said that he wished me to marry the man I loved. And I shall marry Mr. Lydgate. It is seven weeks now since papa gave his consent. And I hope we shall have Mrs. Bretton’s house.”\n\n“Well, my dear, I shall leave you to manage your papa. You always do manage everybody. But if we ever do go and get damask, Sadler’s is the place—far better than Hopkins’s. Mrs. Bretton’s is very large, though: I should love you to have such a house; but it will take a great deal of furniture—carpeting and everything, besides plate and glass. And you hear, your papa says he will give no money. Do you think Mr. Lydgate expects it?”\n\n“You cannot imagine that I should ask him, mamma. Of course he understands his own affairs.”\n\n“But he may have been looking for money, my dear, and we all thought of your having a pretty legacy as well as Fred;—and now everything is so dreadful—there’s no pleasure in thinking of anything, with that poor boy disappointed as he is.”\n\n“That has nothing to do with my marriage, mamma. Fred must leave off being idle. I am going up-stairs to take this work to Miss Morgan: she does the open hemming very well. Mary Garth might do some work for me now, I should think. Her sewing is exquisite; it is the nicest thing I know about Mary. I should so like to have all my cambric frilling double-hemmed. And it takes a long time.”\n\nMrs. Vincy’s belief that Rosamond could manage her papa was well founded. Apart from his dinners and his coursing, Mr. Vincy, blustering as he was, had as little of his own way as if he had been a prime minister: the force of circumstances was easily too much for him, as it is for most pleasure-loving florid men; and the circumstance called Rosamond was particularly forcible by means of that mild persistence which, as we know, enables a white soft living substance to make its way in spite of opposing rock. Papa was not a rock: he had no other fixity than that fixity of alternating impulses sometimes called habit, and this was altogether unfavorable to his taking the only decisive line of conduct in relation to his daughter’s engagement—namely, to inquire thoroughly into Lydgate’s circumstances, declare his own inability to furnish money, and forbid alike either a speedy marriage or an engagement which must be too lengthy. That seems very simple and easy in the statement; but a disagreeable resolve formed in the chill hours of the morning had as many conditions against it as the early frost, and rarely persisted under the warming influences of the day. The indirect though emphatic expression of opinion to which Mr. Vincy was prone suffered much restraint in this case: Lydgate was a proud man towards whom innuendoes were obviously unsafe, and throwing his hat on the floor was out of the question. Mr. Vincy was a little in awe of him, a little vain that he wanted to marry Rosamond, a little indisposed to raise a question of money in which his own position was not advantageous, a little afraid of being worsted in dialogue with a man better educated and more highly bred than himself, and a little afraid of doing what his daughter would not like. The part Mr. Vincy preferred playing was that of the generous host whom nobody criticises. In the earlier half of the day there was business to hinder any formal communication of an adverse resolve; in the later there was dinner, wine, whist, and general satisfaction. And in the mean while the hours were each leaving their little deposit and gradually forming the final reason for inaction, namely, that action was too late. The accepted lover spent most of his evenings in Lowick Gate, and a love-making not at all dependent on money-advances from fathers-in-law, or prospective income from a profession, went on flourishingly under Mr. Vincy’s own eyes. Young love-making—that gossamer web! Even the points it clings to—the things whence its subtle interlacings are swung—are scarcely perceptible: momentary touches of fingertips, meetings of rays from blue and dark orbs, unfinished phrases, lightest changes of cheek and lip, faintest tremors. The web itself is made of spontaneous beliefs and indefinable joys, yearnings of one life towards another, visions of completeness, indefinite trust. And Lydgate fell to spinning that web from his inward self with wonderful rapidity, in spite of experience supposed to be finished off with the drama of Laure—in spite too of medicine and biology; for the inspection of macerated muscle or of eyes presented in a dish (like Santa Lucia’s), and other incidents of scientific inquiry, are observed to be less incompatible with poetic love than a native dulness or a lively addiction to the lowest prose. As for Rosamond, she was in the water-lily’s expanding wonderment at its own fuller life, and she too was spinning industriously at the mutual web. All this went on in the corner of the drawing-room where the piano stood, and subtle as it was, the light made it a sort of rainbow visible to many observers besides Mr. Farebrother. The certainty that Miss Vincy and Mr. Lydgate were engaged became general in Middlemarch without the aid of formal announcement.\n\nAunt Bulstrode was again stirred to anxiety; but this time she addressed herself to her brother, going to the warehouse expressly to avoid Mrs. Vincy’s volatility. His replies were not satisfactory.\n\n“Walter, you never mean to tell me that you have allowed all this to go on without inquiry into Mr. Lydgate’s prospects?” said Mrs. Bulstrode, opening her eyes with wider gravity at her brother, who was in his peevish warehouse humor. “Think of this girl brought up in luxury—in too worldly a way, I am sorry to say—what will she do on a small income?”\n\n“Oh, confound it, Harriet! What can I do when men come into the town without any asking of mine? Did you shut your house up against Lydgate? Bulstrode has pushed him forward more than anybody. I never made any fuss about the young fellow. You should go and talk to your husband about it, not me.”\n\n“Well, really, Walter, how can Mr. Bulstrode be to blame? I am sure he did not wish for the engagement.”\n\n“Oh, if Bulstrode had not taken him by the hand, I should never have invited him.”\n\n“But you called him in to attend on Fred, and I am sure that was a mercy,” said Mrs. Bulstrode, losing her clew in the intricacies of the subject.\n\n“I don’t know about mercy,” said Mr. Vincy, testily. “I know I am worried more than I like with my family. I was a good brother to you, Harriet, before you married Bulstrode, and I must say he doesn’t always show that friendly spirit towards your family that might have been expected of him.” Mr. Vincy was very little like a Jesuit, but no accomplished Jesuit could have turned a question more adroitly. Harriet had to defend her husband instead of blaming her brother, and the conversation ended at a point as far from the beginning as some recent sparring between the brothers-in-law at a vestry meeting.\n\nMrs. Bulstrode did not repeat her brother’s complaints to her husband, but in the evening she spoke to him of Lydgate and Rosamond. He did not share her warm interest, however; and only spoke with resignation of the risks attendant on the beginning of medical practice and the desirability of prudence.\n\n“I am sure we are bound to pray for that thoughtless girl—brought up as she has been,” said Mrs. Bulstrode, wishing to rouse her husband’s feelings.\n\n“Truly, my dear,” said Mr. Bulstrode, assentingly. “Those who are not of this world can do little else to arrest the errors of the obstinately worldly. That is what we must accustom ourselves to recognize with regard to your brother’s family. I could have wished that Mr. Lydgate had not entered into such a union; but my relations with him are limited to that use of his gifts for God’s purposes which is taught us by the divine government under each dispensation.”\n\nMrs. Bulstrode said no more, attributing some dissatisfaction which she felt to her own want of spirituality. She believed that her husband was one of those men whose memoirs should be written when they died.\n\nAs to Lydgate himself, having been accepted, he was prepared to accept all the consequences which he believed himself to foresee with perfect clearness. Of course he must be married in a year—perhaps even in half a year. This was not what he had intended; but other schemes would not be hindered: they would simply adjust themselves anew. Marriage, of course, must be prepared for in the usual way. A house must be taken instead of the rooms he at present occupied; and Lydgate, having heard Rosamond speak with admiration of old Mrs. Bretton’s house (situated in Lowick Gate), took notice when it fell vacant after the old lady’s death, and immediately entered into treaty for it.\n\nHe did this in an episodic way, very much as he gave orders to his tailor for every requisite of perfect dress, without any notion of being extravagant. On the contrary, he would have despised any ostentation of expense; his profession had familiarized him with all grades of poverty, and he cared much for those who suffered hardships. He would have behaved perfectly at a table where the sauce was served in a jug with the handle off, and he would have remembered nothing about a grand dinner except that a man was there who talked well. But it had never occurred to him that he should live in any other than what he would have called an ordinary way, with green glasses for hock, and excellent waiting at table. In warming himself at French social theories he had brought away no smell of scorching. We may handle even extreme opinions with impunity while our furniture, our dinner-giving, and preference for armorial bearings in our own case, link us indissolubly with the established order. And Lydgate’s tendency was not towards extreme opinions: he would have liked no barefooted doctrines, being particular about his boots: he was no radical in relation to anything but medical reform and the prosecution of discovery. In the rest of practical life he walked by hereditary habit; half from that personal pride and unreflecting egoism which I have already called commonness, and half from that naivete which belonged to preoccupation with favorite ideas.\n\nAny inward debate Lydgate had as to the consequences of this engagement which had stolen upon him, turned on the paucity of time rather than of money. Certainly, being in love and being expected continually by some one who always turned out to be prettier than memory could represent her to be, did interfere with the diligent use of spare hours which might serve some “plodding fellow of a German” to make the great, imminent discovery. This was really an argument for not deferring the marriage too long, as he implied to Mr. Farebrother, one day that the Vicar came to his room with some pond-products which he wanted to examine under a better microscope than his own, and, finding Lydgate’s tableful of apparatus and specimens in confusion, said sarcastically—\n\n“Eros has degenerated; he began by introducing order and harmony, and now he brings back chaos.”\n\n“Yes, at some stages,” said Lydgate, lifting his brows and smiling, while he began to arrange his microscope. “But a better order will begin after.”\n\n“Soon?” said the Vicar.\n\n“I hope so, really. This unsettled state of affairs uses up the time, and when one has notions in science, every moment is an opportunity. I feel sure that marriage must be the best thing for a man who wants to work steadily. He has everything at home then—no teasing with personal speculations—he can get calmness and freedom.”\n\n“You are an enviable dog,” said the Vicar, “to have such a prospect—Rosamond, calmness and freedom, all to your share. Here am I with nothing but my pipe and pond-animalcules. Now, are you ready?”\n\nLydgate did not mention to the Vicar another reason he had for wishing to shorten the period of courtship. It was rather irritating to him, even with the wine of love in his veins, to be obliged to mingle so often with the family party at the Vincys’, and to enter so much into Middlemarch gossip, protracted good cheer, whist-playing, and general futility. He had to be deferential when Mr. Vincy decided questions with trenchant ignorance, especially as to those liquors which were the best inward pickle, preserving you from the effects of bad air. Mrs. Vincy’s openness and simplicity were quite unstreaked with suspicion as to the subtle offence she might give to the taste of her intended son-in-law; and altogether Lydgate had to confess to himself that he was descending a little in relation to Rosamond’s family. But that exquisite creature herself suffered in the same sort of way:—it was at least one delightful thought that in marrying her, he could give her a much-needed transplantation.\n\n“Dear!” he said to her one evening, in his gentlest tone, as he sat down by her and looked closely at her face—\n\nBut I must first say that he had found her alone in the drawing-room, where the great old-fashioned window, almost as large as the side of the room, was opened to the summer scents of the garden at the back of the house. Her father and mother were gone to a party, and the rest were all out with the butterflies.\n\n“Dear! your eyelids are red.”\n\n“Are they?” said Rosamond. “I wonder why.” It was not in her nature to pour forth wishes or grievances. They only came forth gracefully on solicitation.\n\n“As if you could hide it from me!” said Lydgate, laying his hand tenderly on both of hers. “Don’t I see a tiny drop on one of the lashes? Things trouble you, and you don’t tell me. That is unloving.”\n\n“Why should I tell you what you cannot alter? They are every-day things:—perhaps they have been a little worse lately.”\n\n“Family annoyances. Don’t fear speaking. I guess them.”\n\n“Papa has been more irritable lately. Fred makes him angry, and this morning there was a fresh quarrel because Fred threatens to throw his whole education away, and do something quite beneath him. And besides—”\n\nRosamond hesitated, and her cheeks were gathering a slight flush. Lydgate had never seen her in trouble since the morning of their engagement, and he had never felt so passionately towards her as at this moment. He kissed the hesitating lips gently, as if to encourage them.\n\n“I feel that papa is not quite pleased about our engagement,” Rosamond continued, almost in a whisper; “and he said last night that he should certainly speak to you and say it must be given up.”\n\n“Will you give it up?” said Lydgate, with quick energy—almost angrily.\n\n“I never give up anything that I choose to do,” said Rosamond, recovering her calmness at the touching of this chord.\n\n“God bless you!” said Lydgate, kissing her again. This constancy of purpose in the right place was adorable. He went on:—\n\n“It is too late now for your father to say that our engagement must be given up. You are of age, and I claim you as mine. If anything is done to make you unhappy,—that is a reason for hastening our marriage.”\n\nAn unmistakable delight shone forth from the blue eyes that met his, and the radiance seemed to light up all his future with mild sunshine. Ideal happiness (of the kind known in the Arabian Nights, in which you are invited to step from the labor and discord of the street into a paradise where everything is given to you and nothing claimed) seemed to be an affair of a few weeks’ waiting, more or less.\n\n“Why should we defer it?” he said, with ardent insistence. “I have taken the house now: everything else can soon be got ready—can it not? You will not mind about new clothes. Those can be bought afterwards.”\n\n“What original notions you clever men have!” said Rosamond, dimpling with more thorough laughter than usual at this humorous incongruity. “This is the first time I ever heard of wedding-clothes being bought after marriage.”\n\n“But you don’t mean to say you would insist on my waiting months for the sake of clothes?” said Lydgate, half thinking that Rosamond was tormenting him prettily, and half fearing that she really shrank from speedy marriage. “Remember, we are looking forward to a better sort of happiness even than this—being continually together, independent of others, and ordering our lives as we will. Come, dear, tell me how soon you can be altogether mine.”\n\nThere was a serious pleading in Lydgate’s tone, as if he felt that she would be injuring him by any fantastic delays. Rosamond became serious too, and slightly meditative; in fact, she was going through many intricacies of lace-edging and hosiery and petticoat-tucking, in order to give an answer that would at least be approximative.\n\n“Six weeks would be ample—say so, Rosamond,” insisted Lydgate, releasing her hands to put his arm gently round her.\n\nOne little hand immediately went to pat her hair, while she gave her neck a meditative turn, and then said seriously—\n\n“There would be the house-linen and the furniture to be prepared. Still, mamma could see to those while we were away.”\n\n“Yes, to be sure. We must be away a week or so.”\n\n“Oh, more than that!” said Rosamond, earnestly. She was thinking of her evening dresses for the visit to Sir Godwin Lydgate’s, which she had long been secretly hoping for as a delightful employment of at least one quarter of the honeymoon, even if she deferred her introduction to the uncle who was a doctor of divinity (also a pleasing though sober kind of rank, when sustained by blood). She looked at her lover with some wondering remonstrance as she spoke, and he readily understood that she might wish to lengthen the sweet time of double solitude.\n\n“Whatever you wish, my darling, when the day is fixed. But let us take a decided course, and put an end to any discomfort you may be suffering. Six weeks!—I am sure they would be ample.”\n\n“I could certainly hasten the work,” said Rosamond. “Will you, then, mention it to papa?—I think it would be better to write to him.” She blushed and looked at him as the garden flowers look at us when we walk forth happily among them in the transcendent evening light: is there not a soul beyond utterance, half nymph, half child, in those delicate petals which glow and breathe about the centres of deep color?\n\nHe touched her ear and a little bit of neck under it with his lips, and they sat quite still for many minutes which flowed by them like a small gurgling brook with the kisses of the sun upon it. Rosamond thought that no one could be more in love than she was; and Lydgate thought that after all his wild mistakes and absurd credulity, he had found perfect womanhood—felt as if already breathed upon by exquisite wedded affection such as would be bestowed by an accomplished creature who venerated his high musings and momentous labors and would never interfere with them; who would create order in the home and accounts with still magic, yet keep her fingers ready to touch the lute and transform life into romance at any moment; who was instructed to the true womanly limit and not a hair’s-breadth beyond—docile, therefore, and ready to carry out behests which came from that limit. It was plainer now than ever that his notion of remaining much longer a bachelor had been a mistake: marriage would not be an obstruction but a furtherance. And happening the next day to accompany a patient to Brassing, he saw a dinner-service there which struck him as so exactly the right thing that he bought it at once. It saved time to do these things just when you thought of them, and Lydgate hated ugly crockery. The dinner-service in question was expensive, but that might be in the nature of dinner-services. Furnishing was necessarily expensive; but then it had to be done only once.\n\n“It must be lovely,” said Mrs. Vincy, when Lydgate mentioned his purchase with some descriptive touches. “Just what Rosy ought to have. I trust in heaven it won’t be broken!”\n\n“One must hire servants who will not break things,” said Lydgate. (Certainly, this was reasoning with an imperfect vision of sequences. But at that period there was no sort of reasoning which was not more or less sanctioned by men of science.)\n\nOf course it was unnecessary to defer the mention of anything to mamma, who did not readily take views that were not cheerful, and being a happy wife herself, had hardly any feeling but pride in her daughter’s marriage. But Rosamond had good reasons for suggesting to Lydgate that papa should be appealed to in writing. She prepared for the arrival of the letter by walking with her papa to the warehouse the next morning, and telling him on the way that Mr. Lydgate wished to be married soon.\n\n“Nonsense, my dear!” said Mr. Vincy. “What has he got to marry on? You’d much better give up the engagement. I’ve told you so pretty plainly before this. What have you had such an education for, if you are to go and marry a poor man? It’s a cruel thing for a father to see.”\n\n“Mr. Lydgate is not poor, papa. He bought Mr. Peacock’s practice, which, they say, is worth eight or nine hundred a-year.”\n\n“Stuff and nonsense! What’s buying a practice? He might as well buy next year’s swallows. It’ll all slip through his fingers.”\n\n“On the contrary, papa, he will increase the practice. See how he has been called in by the Chettams and Casaubons.”\n\n“I hope he knows I shan’t give anything—with this disappointment about Fred, and Parliament going to be dissolved, and machine-breaking everywhere, and an election coming on—”\n\n“Dear papa! what can that have to do with my marriage?”\n\n“A pretty deal to do with it! We may all be ruined for what I know—the country’s in that state! Some say it’s the end of the world, and be hanged if I don’t think it looks like it! Anyhow, it’s not a time for me to be drawing money out of my business, and I should wish Lydgate to know that.”\n\n“I am sure he expects nothing, papa. And he has such very high connections: he is sure to rise in one way or another. He is engaged in making scientific discoveries.”\n\nMr. Vincy was silent.\n\n“I cannot give up my only prospect of happiness, papa. Mr. Lydgate is a gentleman. I could never love any one who was not a perfect gentleman. You would not like me to go into a consumption, as Arabella Hawley did. And you know that I never change my mind.”\n\nAgain papa was silent.\n\n“Promise me, papa, that you will consent to what we wish. We shall never give each other up; and you know that you have always objected to long courtships and late marriages.”\n\nThere was a little more urgency of this kind, till Mr. Vincy said, “Well, well, child, he must write to me first before I can answer him,”—and Rosamond was certain that she had gained her point.\n\nMr. Vincy’s answer consisted chiefly in a demand that Lydgate should insure his life—a demand immediately conceded. This was a delightfully reassuring idea supposing that Lydgate died, but in the mean time not a self-supporting idea. However, it seemed to make everything comfortable about Rosamond’s marriage; and the necessary purchases went on with much spirit. Not without prudential considerations, however. A bride (who is going to visit at a baronet’s) must have a few first-rate pocket-handkerchiefs; but beyond the absolutely necessary half-dozen, Rosamond contented herself without the very highest style of embroidery and Valenciennes. Lydgate also, finding that his sum of eight hundred pounds had been considerably reduced since he had come to Middlemarch, restrained his inclination for some plate of an old pattern which was shown to him when he went into Kibble’s establishment at Brassing to buy forks and spoons. He was too proud to act as if he presupposed that Mr. Vincy would advance money to provide furniture; and though, since it would not be necessary to pay for everything at once, some bills would be left standing over, he did not waste time in conjecturing how much his father-in-law would give in the form of dowry, to make payment easy. He was not going to do anything extravagant, but the requisite things must be bought, and it would be bad economy to buy them of a poor quality. All these matters were by the bye. Lydgate foresaw that science and his profession were the objects he should alone pursue enthusiastically; but he could not imagine himself pursuing them in such a home as Wrench had—the doors all open, the oil-cloth worn, the children in soiled pinafores, and lunch lingering in the form of bones, black-handled knives, and willow-pattern. But Wrench had a wretched lymphatic wife who made a mummy of herself indoors in a large shawl; and he must have altogether begun with an ill-chosen domestic apparatus.\n\nRosamond, however, was on her side much occupied with conjectures, though her quick imitative perception warned her against betraying them too crudely.\n\n“I shall like so much to know your family,” she said one day, when the wedding journey was being discussed. “We might perhaps take a direction that would allow us to see them as we returned. Which of your uncles do you like best?”\n\n“Oh,—my uncle Godwin, I think. He is a good-natured old fellow.”\n\n“You were constantly at his house at Quallingham, when you were a boy, were you not? I should so like to see the old spot and everything you were used to. Does he know you are going to be married?”\n\n“No,” said Lydgate, carelessly, turning in his chair and rubbing his hair up.\n\n“Do send him word of it, you naughty undutiful nephew. He will perhaps ask you to take me to Quallingham; and then you could show me about the grounds, and I could imagine you there when you were a boy. Remember, you see me in my home, just as it has been since I was a child. It is not fair that I should be so ignorant of yours. But perhaps you would be a little ashamed of me. I forgot that.”\n\nLydgate smiled at her tenderly, and really accepted the suggestion that the proud pleasure of showing so charming a bride was worth some trouble. And now he came to think of it, he would like to see the old spots with Rosamond.\n\n“I will write to him, then. But my cousins are bores.”\n\nIt seemed magnificent to Rosamond to be able to speak so slightingly of a baronet’s family, and she felt much contentment in the prospect of being able to estimate them contemptuously on her own account.\n\nBut mamma was near spoiling all, a day or two later, by saying—\n\n“I hope your uncle Sir Godwin will not look down on Rosy, Mr. Lydgate. I should think he would do something handsome. A thousand or two can be nothing to a baronet.”\n\n“Mamma!” said Rosamond, blushing deeply; and Lydgate pitied her so much that he remained silent and went to the other end of the room to examine a print curiously, as if he had been absent-minded. Mamma had a little filial lecture afterwards, and was docile as usual. But Rosamond reflected that if any of those high-bred cousins who were bores, should be induced to visit Middlemarch, they would see many things in her own family which might shock them. Hence it seemed desirable that Lydgate should by-and-by get some first-rate position elsewhere than in Middlemarch; and this could hardly be difficult in the case of a man who had a titled uncle and could make discoveries. Lydgate, you perceive, had talked fervidly to Rosamond of his hopes as to the highest uses of his life, and had found it delightful to be listened to by a creature who would bring him the sweet furtherance of satisfying affection—beauty—repose—such help as our thoughts get from the summer sky and the flower-fringed meadows.\n\nLydgate relied much on the psychological difference between what for the sake of variety I will call goose and gander: especially on the innate submissiveness of the goose as beautifully corresponding to the strength of the gander.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVII.\n\nThrice happy she that is so well assured Unto herself and settled so in heart That neither will for better be allured Ne fears to worse with any chance to start, But like a steddy ship doth strongly part The raging waves and keeps her course aright; Ne aught for tempest doth from it depart, Ne aught for fairer weather’s false delight. Such self-assurance need not fear the spight Of grudging foes; ne favour seek of friends; But in the stay of her own stedfast might Neither to one herself nor other bends. Most happy she that most assured doth rest, But he most happy who such one loves best. —SPENSER.\nThe doubt hinted by Mr. Vincy whether it were only the general election or the end of the world that was coming on, now that George the Fourth was dead, Parliament dissolved, Wellington and Peel generally depreciated and the new King apologetic, was a feeble type of the uncertainties in provincial opinion at that time. With the glow-worm lights of country places, how could men see which were their own thoughts in the confusion of a Tory Ministry passing Liberal measures, of Tory nobles and electors being anxious to return Liberals rather than friends of the recreant Ministers, and of outcries for remedies which seemed to have a mysteriously remote bearing on private interest, and were made suspicious by the advocacy of disagreeable neighbors? Buyers of the Middlemarch newspapers found themselves in an anomalous position: during the agitation on the Catholic Question many had given up the “Pioneer”—which had a motto from Charles James Fox and was in the van of progress—because it had taken Peel’s side about the Papists, and had thus blotted its Liberalism with a toleration of Jesuitry and Baal; but they were ill-satisfied with the “Trumpet,” which—since its blasts against Rome, and in the general flaccidity of the public mind (nobody knowing who would support whom)—had become feeble in its blowing.\n\nIt was a time, according to a noticeable article in the “Pioneer,” when the crying needs of the country might well counteract a reluctance to public action on the part of men whose minds had from long experience acquired breadth as well as concentration, decision of judgment as well as tolerance, dispassionateness as well as energy—in fact, all those qualities which in the melancholy experience of mankind have been the least disposed to share lodgings.\n\nMr. Hackbutt, whose fluent speech was at that time floating more widely than usual, and leaving much uncertainty as to its ultimate channel, was heard to say in Mr. Hawley’s office that the article in question “emanated” from Brooke of Tipton, and that Brooke had secretly bought the “Pioneer” some months ago.\n\n“That means mischief, eh?” said Mr. Hawley. “He’s got the freak of being a popular man now, after dangling about like a stray tortoise. So much the worse for him. I’ve had my eye on him for some time. He shall be prettily pumped upon. He’s a damned bad landlord. What business has an old county man to come currying favor with a low set of dark-blue freemen? As to his paper, I only hope he may do the writing himself. It would be worth our paying for.”\n\n“I understand he has got a very brilliant young fellow to edit it, who can write the highest style of leading article, quite equal to anything in the London papers. And he means to take very high ground on Reform.”\n\n“Let Brooke reform his rent-roll. He’s a cursed old screw, and the buildings all over his estate are going to rack. I suppose this young fellow is some loose fish from London.”\n\n“His name is Ladislaw. He is said to be of foreign extraction.”\n\n“I know the sort,” said Mr. Hawley; “some emissary. He’ll begin with flourishing about the Rights of Man and end with murdering a wench. That’s the style.”\n“You must concede that there are abuses, Hawley,” said Mr. Hackbutt, foreseeing some political disagreement with his family lawyer. “I myself should never favor immoderate views—in fact I take my stand with Huskisson—but I cannot blind myself to the consideration that the non-representation of large towns—”\n\n“Large towns be damned!” said Mr. Hawley, impatient of exposition. “I know a little too much about Middlemarch elections. Let ’em quash every pocket borough to-morrow, and bring in every mushroom town in the kingdom—they’ll only increase the expense of getting into Parliament. I go upon facts.”\n\nMr. Hawley’s disgust at the notion of the “Pioneer” being edited by an emissary, and of Brooke becoming actively political—as if a tortoise of desultory pursuits should protrude its small head ambitiously and become rampant—was hardly equal to the annoyance felt by some members of Mr. Brooke’s own family. The result had oozed forth gradually, like the discovery that your neighbor has set up an unpleasant kind of manufacture which will be permanently under your nostrils without legal remedy. The “Pioneer” had been secretly bought even before Will Ladislaw’s arrival, the expected opportunity having offered itself in the readiness of the proprietor to part with a valuable property which did not pay; and in the interval since Mr. Brooke had written his invitation, those germinal ideas of making his mind tell upon the world at large which had been present in him from his younger years, but had hitherto lain in some obstruction, had been sprouting under cover.\n\nThe development was much furthered by a delight in his guest which proved greater even than he had anticipated. For it seemed that Will was not only at home in all those artistic and literary subjects which Mr. Brooke had gone into at one time, but that he was strikingly ready at seizing the points of the political situation, and dealing with them in that large spirit which, aided by adequate memory, lends itself to quotation and general effectiveness of treatment.\n\n“He seems to me a kind of Shelley, you know,” Mr. Brooke took an opportunity of saying, for the gratification of Mr. Casaubon. “I don’t mean as to anything objectionable—laxities or atheism, or anything of that kind, you know—Ladislaw’s sentiments in every way I am sure are good—indeed, we were talking a great deal together last night. But he has the same sort of enthusiasm for liberty, freedom, emancipation—a fine thing under guidance—under guidance, you know. I think I shall be able to put him on the right tack; and I am the more pleased because he is a relation of yours, Casaubon.”\n\nIf the right tack implied anything more precise than the rest of Mr. Brooke’s speech, Mr. Casaubon silently hoped that it referred to some occupation at a great distance from Lowick. He had disliked Will while he helped him, but he had begun to dislike him still more now that Will had declined his help. That is the way with us when we have any uneasy jealousy in our disposition: if our talents are chiefly of the burrowing kind, our honey-sipping cousin (whom we have grave reasons for objecting to) is likely to have a secret contempt for us, and any one who admires him passes an oblique criticism on ourselves. Having the scruples of rectitude in our souls, we are above the meanness of injuring him—rather we meet all his claims on us by active benefits; and the drawing of cheques for him, being a superiority which he must recognize, gives our bitterness a milder infusion. Now Mr. Casaubon had been deprived of that superiority (as anything more than a remembrance) in a sudden, capricious manner. His antipathy to Will did not spring from the common jealousy of a winter-worn husband: it was something deeper, bred by his lifelong claims and discontents; but Dorothea, now that she was present—Dorothea, as a young wife who herself had shown an offensive capability of criticism, necessarily gave concentration to the uneasiness which had before been vague.\n\nWill Ladislaw on his side felt that his dislike was flourishing at the expense of his gratitude, and spent much inward discourse in justifying the dislike. Casaubon hated him—he knew that very well; on his first entrance he could discern a bitterness in the mouth and a venom in the glance which would almost justify declaring war in spite of past benefits. He was much obliged to Casaubon in the past, but really the act of marrying this wife was a set-off against the obligation. It was a question whether gratitude which refers to what is done for one’s self ought not to give way to indignation at what is done against another. And Casaubon had done a wrong to Dorothea in marrying her. A man was bound to know himself better than that, and if he chose to grow gray crunching bones in a cavern, he had no business to be luring a girl into his companionship. “It is the most horrible of virgin-sacrifices,” said Will; and he painted to himself what were Dorothea’s inward sorrows as if he had been writing a choric wail. But he would never lose sight of her: he would watch over her—if he gave up everything else in life he would watch over her, and she should know that she had one slave in the world. Will had—to use Sir Thomas Browne’s phrase—a “passionate prodigality” of statement both to himself and others. The simple truth was that nothing then invited him so strongly as the presence of Dorothea.\n\nInvitations of the formal kind had been wanting, however, for Will had never been asked to go to Lowick. Mr. Brooke, indeed, confident of doing everything agreeable which Casaubon, poor fellow, was too much absorbed to think of, had arranged to bring Ladislaw to Lowick several times (not neglecting meanwhile to introduce him elsewhere on every opportunity as “a young relative of Casaubon’s”). And though Will had not seen Dorothea alone, their interviews had been enough to restore her former sense of young companionship with one who was cleverer than herself, yet seemed ready to be swayed by her. Poor Dorothea before her marriage had never found much room in other minds for what she cared most to say; and she had not, as we know, enjoyed her husband’s superior instruction so much as she had expected. If she spoke with any keenness of interest to Mr. Casaubon, he heard her with an air of patience as if she had given a quotation from the Delectus familiar to him from his tender years, and sometimes mentioned curtly what ancient sects or personages had held similar ideas, as if there were too much of that sort in stock already; at other times he would inform her that she was mistaken, and reassert what her remark had questioned.\n\nBut Will Ladislaw always seemed to see more in what she said than she herself saw. Dorothea had little vanity, but she had the ardent woman’s need to rule beneficently by making the joy of another soul. Hence the mere chance of seeing Will occasionally was like a lunette opened in the wall of her prison, giving her a glimpse of the sunny air; and this pleasure began to nullify her original alarm at what her husband might think about the introduction of Will as her uncle’s guest. On this subject Mr. Casaubon had remained dumb.\n\nBut Will wanted to talk with Dorothea alone, and was impatient of slow circumstance. However slight the terrestrial intercourse between Dante and Beatrice or Petrarch and Laura, time changes the proportion of things, and in later days it is preferable to have fewer sonnets and more conversation. Necessity excused stratagem, but stratagem was limited by the dread of offending Dorothea. He found out at last that he wanted to take a particular sketch at Lowick; and one morning when Mr. Brooke had to drive along the Lowick road on his way to the county town, Will asked to be set down with his sketch-book and camp-stool at Lowick, and without announcing himself at the Manor settled himself to sketch in a position where he must see Dorothea if she came out to walk—and he knew that she usually walked an hour in the morning.\n\nBut the stratagem was defeated by the weather. Clouds gathered with treacherous quickness, the rain came down, and Will was obliged to take shelter in the house. He intended, on the strength of relationship, to go into the drawing-room and wait there without being announced; and seeing his old acquaintance the butler in the hall, he said, “Don’t mention that I am here, Pratt; I will wait till luncheon; I know Mr. Casaubon does not like to be disturbed when he is in the library.”\n\n“Master is out, sir; there’s only Mrs. Casaubon in the library. I’d better tell her you’re here, sir,” said Pratt, a red-cheeked man given to lively converse with Tantripp, and often agreeing with her that it must be dull for Madam.\n\n“Oh, very well; this confounded rain has hindered me from sketching,” said Will, feeling so happy that he affected indifference with delightful ease.\n\nIn another minute he was in the library, and Dorothea was meeting him with her sweet unconstrained smile.\n\n“Mr. Casaubon has gone to the Archdeacon’s,” she said, at once. “I don’t know whether he will be at home again long before dinner. He was uncertain how long he should be. Did you want to say anything particular to him?”\n\n“No; I came to sketch, but the rain drove me in. Else I would not have disturbed you yet. I supposed that Mr. Casaubon was here, and I know he dislikes interruption at this hour.”\n\n“I am indebted to the rain, then. I am so glad to see you.” Dorothea uttered these common words with the simple sincerity of an unhappy child, visited at school.\n\n“I really came for the chance of seeing you alone,” said Will, mysteriously forced to be just as simple as she was. He could not stay to ask himself, why not? “I wanted to talk about things, as we did in Rome. It always makes a difference when other people are present.”\n\n“Yes,” said Dorothea, in her clear full tone of assent. “Sit down.” She seated herself on a dark ottoman with the brown books behind her, looking in her plain dress of some thin woollen-white material, without a single ornament on her besides her wedding-ring, as if she were under a vow to be different from all other women; and Will sat down opposite her at two yards’ distance, the light falling on his bright curls and delicate but rather petulant profile, with its defiant curves of lip and chin. Each looked at the other as if they had been two flowers which had opened then and there. Dorothea for the moment forgot her husband’s mysterious irritation against Will: it seemed fresh water at her thirsty lips to speak without fear to the one person whom she had found receptive; for in looking backward through sadness she exaggerated a past solace.\n\n“I have often thought that I should like to talk to you again,” she said, immediately. “It seems strange to me how many things I said to you.”\n\n“I remember them all,” said Will, with the unspeakable content in his soul of feeling that he was in the presence of a creature worthy to be perfectly loved. I think his own feelings at that moment were perfect, for we mortals have our divine moments, when love is satisfied in the completeness of the beloved object.\n\n“I have tried to learn a great deal since we were in Rome,” said Dorothea. “I can read Latin a little, and I am beginning to understand just a little Greek. I can help Mr. Casaubon better now. I can find out references for him and save his eyes in many ways. But it is very difficult to be learned; it seems as if people were worn out on the way to great thoughts, and can never enjoy them because they are too tired.”\n\n“If a man has a capacity for great thoughts, he is likely to overtake them before he is decrepit,” said Will, with irrepressible quickness. But through certain sensibilities Dorothea was as quick as he, and seeing her face change, he added, immediately, “But it is quite true that the best minds have been sometimes overstrained in working out their ideas.”\n\n“You correct me,” said Dorothea. “I expressed myself ill. I should have said that those who have great thoughts get too much worn in working them out. I used to feel about that, even when I was a little girl; and it always seemed to me that the use I should like to make of my life would be to help some one who did great works, so that his burthen might be lighter.”\n\nDorothea was led on to this bit of autobiography without any sense of making a revelation. But she had never before said anything to Will which threw so strong a light on her marriage. He did not shrug his shoulders; and for want of that muscular outlet he thought the more irritably of beautiful lips kissing holy skulls and other emptinesses ecclesiastically enshrined. Also he had to take care that his speech should not betray that thought.\n\n“But you may easily carry the help too far,” he said, “and get over-wrought yourself. Are you not too much shut up? You already look paler. It would be better for Mr. Casaubon to have a secretary; he could easily get a man who would do half his work for him. It would save him more effectually, and you need only help him in lighter ways.”\n\n“How can you think of that?” said Dorothea, in a tone of earnest remonstrance. “I should have no happiness if I did not help him in his work. What could I do? There is no good to be done in Lowick. The only thing I desire is to help him more. And he objects to a secretary: please not to mention that again.”\n\n“Certainly not, now I know your feeling. But I have heard both Mr. Brooke and Sir James Chettam express the same wish.”\n\n“Yes,” said Dorothea, “but they don’t understand—they want me to be a great deal on horseback, and have the garden altered and new conservatories, to fill up my days. I thought you could understand that one’s mind has other wants,” she added, rather impatiently—“besides, Mr. Casaubon cannot bear to hear of a secretary.”\n\n“My mistake is excusable,” said Will. “In old days I used to hear Mr. Casaubon speak as if he looked forward to having a secretary. Indeed he held out the prospect of that office to me. But I turned out to be—not good enough for it.”\n\nDorothea was trying to extract out of this an excuse for her husband’s evident repulsion, as she said, with a playful smile, “You were not a steady worker enough.”\n\n“No,” said Will, shaking his head backward somewhat after the manner of a spirited horse. And then, the old irritable demon prompting him to give another good pinch at the moth-wings of poor Mr. Casaubon’s glory, he went on, “And I have seen since that Mr. Casaubon does not like any one to overlook his work and know thoroughly what he is doing. He is too doubtful—too uncertain of himself. I may not be good for much, but he dislikes me because I disagree with him.”\n\nWill was not without his intentions to be always generous, but our tongues are little triggers which have usually been pulled before general intentions can be brought to bear. And it was too intolerable that Casaubon’s dislike of him should not be fairly accounted for to Dorothea. Yet when he had spoken he was rather uneasy as to the effect on her.\n\nBut Dorothea was strangely quiet—not immediately indignant, as she had been on a like occasion in Rome. And the cause lay deep. She was no longer struggling against the perception of facts, but adjusting herself to their clearest perception; and now when she looked steadily at her husband’s failure, still more at his possible consciousness of failure, she seemed to be looking along the one track where duty became tenderness. Will’s want of reticence might have been met with more severity, if he had not already been recommended to her mercy by her husband’s dislike, which must seem hard to her till she saw better reason for it.\n\nShe did not answer at once, but after looking down ruminatingly she said, with some earnestness, “Mr. Casaubon must have overcome his dislike of you so far as his actions were concerned: and that is admirable.”\n\n“Yes; he has shown a sense of justice in family matters. It was an abominable thing that my grandmother should have been disinherited because she made what they called a mesalliance, though there was nothing to be said against her husband except that he was a Polish refugee who gave lessons for his bread.”\n\n“I wish I knew all about her!” said Dorothea. “I wonder how she bore the change from wealth to poverty: I wonder whether she was happy with her husband! Do you know much about them?”\n\n“No; only that my grandfather was a patriot—a bright fellow—could speak many languages—musical—got his bread by teaching all sorts of things. They both died rather early. And I never knew much of my father, beyond what my mother told me; but he inherited the musical talents. I remember his slow walk and his long thin hands; and one day remains with me when he was lying ill, and I was very hungry, and had only a little bit of bread.”\n\n“Ah, what a different life from mine!” said Dorothea, with keen interest, clasping her hands on her lap. “I have always had too much of everything. But tell me how it was—Mr. Casaubon could not have known about you then.”\n\n“No; but my father had made himself known to Mr. Casaubon, and that was my last hungry day. My father died soon after, and my mother and I were well taken care of. Mr. Casaubon always expressly recognized it as his duty to take care of us because of the harsh injustice which had been shown to his mother’s sister. But now I am telling you what is not new to you.”\n\nIn his inmost soul Will was conscious of wishing to tell Dorothea what was rather new even in his own construction of things—namely, that Mr. Casaubon had never done more than pay a debt towards him. Will was much too good a fellow to be easy under the sense of being ungrateful. And when gratitude has become a matter of reasoning there are many ways of escaping from its bonds.\n\n“No,” answered Dorothea; “Mr. Casaubon has always avoided dwelling on his own honorable actions.” She did not feel that her husband’s conduct was depreciated; but this notion of what justice had required in his relations with Will Ladislaw took strong hold on her mind. After a moment’s pause, she added, “He had never told me that he supported your mother. Is she still living?”\n\n“No; she died by an accident—a fall—four years ago. It is curious that my mother, too, ran away from her family, but not for the sake of her husband. She never would tell me anything about her family, except that she forsook them to get her own living—went on the stage, in fact. She was a dark-eyed creature, with crisp ringlets, and never seemed to be getting old. You see I come of rebellious blood on both sides,” Will ended, smiling brightly at Dorothea, while she was still looking with serious intentness before her, like a child seeing a drama for the first time.\n\nBut her face, too, broke into a smile as she said, “That is your apology, I suppose, for having yourself been rather rebellious; I mean, to Mr. Casaubon’s wishes. You must remember that you have not done what he thought best for you. And if he dislikes you—you were speaking of dislike a little while ago—but I should rather say, if he has shown any painful feelings towards you, you must consider how sensitive he has become from the wearing effect of study. Perhaps,” she continued, getting into a pleading tone, “my uncle has not told you how serious Mr. Casaubon’s illness was. It would be very petty of us who are well and can bear things, to think much of small offences from those who carry a weight of trial.”\n\n“You teach me better,” said Will. “I will never grumble on that subject again.” There was a gentleness in his tone which came from the unutterable contentment of perceiving—what Dorothea was hardly conscious of—that she was travelling into the remoteness of pure pity and loyalty towards her husband. Will was ready to adore her pity and loyalty, if she would associate himself with her in manifesting them. “I have really sometimes been a perverse fellow,” he went on, “but I will never again, if I can help it, do or say what you would disapprove.”\n\n“That is very good of you,” said Dorothea, with another open smile. “I shall have a little kingdom then, where I shall give laws. But you will soon go away, out of my rule, I imagine. You will soon be tired of staying at the Grange.”\n\n“That is a point I wanted to mention to you—one of the reasons why I wished to speak to you alone. Mr. Brooke proposes that I should stay in this neighborhood. He has bought one of the Middlemarch newspapers, and he wishes me to conduct that, and also to help him in other ways.”\n\n“Would not that be a sacrifice of higher prospects for you?” said Dorothea.\n\n“Perhaps; but I have always been blamed for thinking of prospects, and not settling to anything. And here is something offered to me. If you would not like me to accept it, I will give it up. Otherwise I would rather stay in this part of the country than go away. I belong to nobody anywhere else.”\n\n“I should like you to stay very much,” said Dorothea, at once, as simply and readily as she had spoken at Rome. There was not the shadow of a reason in her mind at the moment why she should not say so.\n\n“Then I will stay,” said Ladislaw, shaking his head backward, rising and going towards the window, as if to see whether the rain had ceased.\n\nBut the next moment, Dorothea, according to a habit which was getting continually stronger, began to reflect that her husband felt differently from herself, and she colored deeply under the double embarrassment of having expressed what might be in opposition to her husband’s feeling, and of having to suggest this opposition to Will. His face was not turned towards her, and this made it easier to say—\n\n“But my opinion is of little consequence on such a subject. I think you should be guided by Mr. Casaubon. I spoke without thinking of anything else than my own feeling, which has nothing to do with the real question. But it now occurs to me—perhaps Mr. Casaubon might see that the proposal was not wise. Can you not wait now and mention it to him?”\n\n“I can’t wait to-day,” said Will, inwardly seared by the possibility that Mr. Casaubon would enter. “The rain is quite over now. I told Mr. Brooke not to call for me: I would rather walk the five miles. I shall strike across Halsell Common, and see the gleams on the wet grass. I like that.”\n\nHe approached her to shake hands quite hurriedly, longing but not daring to say, “Don’t mention the subject to Mr. Casaubon.” No, he dared not, could not say it. To ask her to be less simple and direct would be like breathing on the crystal that you want to see the light through. And there was always the other great dread—of himself becoming dimmed and forever ray-shorn in her eyes.\n\n“I wish you could have stayed,” said Dorothea, with a touch of mournfulness, as she rose and put out her hand. She also had her thought which she did not like to express:—Will certainly ought to lose no time in consulting Mr. Casaubon’s wishes, but for her to urge this might seem an undue dictation.\n\nSo they only said “Good-by,” and Will quitted the house, striking across the fields so as not to run any risk of encountering Mr. Casaubon’s carriage, which, however, did not appear at the gate until four o’clock. That was an unpropitious hour for coming home: it was too early to gain the moral support under ennui of dressing his person for dinner, and too late to undress his mind of the day’s frivolous ceremony and affairs, so as to be prepared for a good plunge into the serious business of study. On such occasions he usually threw into an easy-chair in the library, and allowed Dorothea to read the London papers to him, closing his eyes the while. To-day, however, he declined that relief, observing that he had already had too many public details urged upon him; but he spoke more cheerfully than usual, when Dorothea asked about his fatigue, and added with that air of formal effort which never forsook him even when he spoke without his waistcoat and cravat—\n\n“I have had the gratification of meeting my former acquaintance, Dr. Spanning, to-day, and of being praised by one who is himself a worthy recipient of praise. He spoke very handsomely of my late tractate on the Egyptian Mysteries,—using, in fact, terms which it would not become me to repeat.” In uttering the last clause, Mr. Casaubon leaned over the elbow of his chair, and swayed his head up and down, apparently as a muscular outlet instead of that recapitulation which would not have been becoming.\n\n“I am very glad you have had that pleasure,” said Dorothea, delighted to see her husband less weary than usual at this hour. “Before you came I had been regretting that you happened to be out to-day.”\n\n“Why so, my dear?” said Mr. Casaubon, throwing himself backward again.\n\n“Because Mr. Ladislaw has been here; and he has mentioned a proposal of my uncle’s which I should like to know your opinion of.” Her husband she felt was really concerned in this question. Even with her ignorance of the world she had a vague impression that the position offered to Will was out of keeping with his family connections, and certainly Mr. Casaubon had a claim to be consulted. He did not speak, but merely bowed.\n\n“Dear uncle, you know, has many projects. It appears that he has bought one of the Middlemarch newspapers, and he has asked Mr. Ladislaw to stay in this neighborhood and conduct the paper for him, besides helping him in other ways.”\n\nDorothea looked at her husband while she spoke, but he had at first blinked and finally closed his eyes, as if to save them; while his lips became more tense. “What is your opinion?” she added, rather timidly, after a slight pause.\n\n“Did Mr. Ladislaw come on purpose to ask my opinion?” said Mr. Casaubon, opening his eyes narrowly with a knife-edged look at Dorothea. She was really uncomfortable on the point he inquired about, but she only became a little more serious, and her eyes did not swerve.\n\n“No,” she answered immediately, “he did not say that he came to ask your opinion. But when he mentioned the proposal, he of course expected me to tell you of it.”\n\nMr. Casaubon was silent.\n\n“I feared that you might feel some objection. But certainly a young man with so much talent might be very useful to my uncle—might help him to do good in a better way. And Mr. Ladislaw wishes to have some fixed occupation. He has been blamed, he says, for not seeking something of that kind, and he would like to stay in this neighborhood because no one cares for him elsewhere.”\n\nDorothea felt that this was a consideration to soften her husband. However, he did not speak, and she presently recurred to Dr. Spanning and the Archdeacon’s breakfast. But there was no longer sunshine on these subjects.\n\nThe next morning, without Dorothea’s knowledge, Mr. Casaubon despatched the following letter, beginning “Dear Mr. Ladislaw” (he had always before addressed him as “Will”):—\n\n“Mrs. Casaubon informs me that a proposal has been made to you, and (according to an inference by no means stretched) has on your part been in some degree entertained, which involves your residence in this neighborhood in a capacity which I am justified in saying touches my own position in such a way as renders it not only natural and warrantable in me when that effect is viewed under the influence of legitimate feeling, but incumbent on me when the same effect is considered in the light of my responsibilities, to state at once that your acceptance of the proposal above indicated would be highly offensive to me. That I have some claim to the exercise of a veto here, would not, I believe, be denied by any reasonable person cognizant of the relations between us: relations which, though thrown into the past by your recent procedure, are not thereby annulled in their character of determining antecedents. I will not here make reflections on any person’s judgment. It is enough for me to point out to yourself that there are certain social fitnesses and proprieties which should hinder a somewhat near relative of mine from becoming any wise conspicuous in this vicinity in a status not only much beneath my own, but associated at best with the sciolism of literary or political adventurers. At any rate, the contrary issue must exclude you from further reception at my house.\n\nYours faithfully, “EDWARD CASAUBON.”\n\nMeanwhile Dorothea’s mind was innocently at work towards the further embitterment of her husband; dwelling, with a sympathy that grew to agitation, on what Will had told her about his parents and grandparents. Any private hours in her day were usually spent in her blue-green boudoir, and she had come to be very fond of its pallid quaintness. Nothing had been outwardly altered there; but while the summer had gradually advanced over the western fields beyond the avenue of elms, the bare room had gathered within it those memories of an inward life which fill the air as with a cloud of good or bad angels, the invisible yet active forms of our spiritual triumphs or our spiritual falls. She had been so used to struggle for and to find resolve in looking along the avenue towards the arch of western light that the vision itself had gained a communicating power. Even the pale stag seemed to have reminding glances and to mean mutely, “Yes, we know.” And the group of delicately touched miniatures had made an audience as of beings no longer disturbed about their own earthly lot, but still humanly interested. Especially the mysterious “Aunt Julia” about whom Dorothea had never found it easy to question her husband.\n\nAnd now, since her conversation with Will, many fresh images had gathered round that Aunt Julia who was Will’s grandmother; the presence of that delicate miniature, so like a living face that she knew, helping to concentrate her feelings. What a wrong, to cut off the girl from the family protection and inheritance only because she had chosen a man who was poor! Dorothea, early troubling her elders with questions about the facts around her, had wrought herself into some independent clearness as to the historical, political reasons why eldest sons had superior rights, and why land should be entailed: those reasons, impressing her with a certain awe, might be weightier than she knew, but here was a question of ties which left them uninfringed. Here was a daughter whose child—even according to the ordinary aping of aristocratic institutions by people who are no more aristocratic than retired grocers, and who have no more land to “keep together” than a lawn and a paddock—would have a prior claim. Was inheritance a question of liking or of responsibility? All the energy of Dorothea’s nature went on the side of responsibility—the fulfilment of claims founded on our own deeds, such as marriage and parentage.\n\nIt was true, she said to herself, that Mr. Casaubon had a debt to the Ladislaws—that he had to pay back what the Ladislaws had been wronged of. And now she began to think of her husband’s will, which had been made at the time of their marriage, leaving the bulk of his property to her, with proviso in case of her having children. That ought to be altered; and no time ought to be lost. This very question which had just arisen about Will Ladislaw’s occupation, was the occasion for placing things on a new, right footing. Her husband, she felt sure, according to all his previous conduct, would be ready to take the just view, if she proposed it—she, in whose interest an unfair concentration of the property had been urged. His sense of right had surmounted and would continue to surmount anything that might be called antipathy. She suspected that her uncle’s scheme was disapproved by Mr. Casaubon, and this made it seem all the more opportune that a fresh understanding should be begun, so that instead of Will’s starting penniless and accepting the first function that offered itself, he should find himself in possession of a rightful income which should be paid by her husband during his life, and, by an immediate alteration of the will, should be secured at his death. The vision of all this as what ought to be done seemed to Dorothea like a sudden letting in of daylight, waking her from her previous stupidity and incurious self-absorbed ignorance about her husband’s relation to others. Will Ladislaw had refused Mr. Casaubon’s future aid on a ground that no longer appeared right to her; and Mr. Casaubon had never himself seen fully what was the claim upon him. “But he will!” said Dorothea. “The great strength of his character lies here. And what are we doing with our money? We make no use of half of our income. My own money buys me nothing but an uneasy conscience.”\n\nThere was a peculiar fascination for Dorothea in this division of property intended for herself, and always regarded by her as excessive. She was blind, you see, to many things obvious to others—likely to tread in the wrong places, as Celia had warned her; yet her blindness to whatever did not lie in her own pure purpose carried her safely by the side of precipices where vision would have been perilous with fear.\n\nThe thoughts which had gathered vividness in the solitude of her boudoir occupied her incessantly through the day on which Mr. Casaubon had sent his letter to Will. Everything seemed hindrance to her till she could find an opportunity of opening her heart to her husband. To his preoccupied mind all subjects were to be approached gently, and she had never since his illness lost from her consciousness the dread of agitating him. But when young ardor is set brooding over the conception of a prompt deed, the deed itself seems to start forth with independent life, mastering ideal obstacles. The day passed in a sombre fashion, not unusual, though Mr. Casaubon was perhaps unusually silent; but there were hours of the night which might be counted on as opportunities of conversation; for Dorothea, when aware of her husband’s sleeplessness, had established a habit of rising, lighting a candle, and reading him to sleep again. And this night she was from the beginning sleepless, excited by resolves. He slept as usual for a few hours, but she had risen softly and had sat in the darkness for nearly an hour before he said—\n\n“Dorothea, since you are up, will you light a candle?”\n\n“Do you feel ill, dear?” was her first question, as she obeyed him.\n\n“No, not at all; but I shall be obliged, since you are up, if you will read me a few pages of Lowth.”\n\n“May I talk to you a little instead?” said Dorothea.\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“I have been thinking about money all day—that I have always had too much, and especially the prospect of too much.”\n\n“These, my dear Dorothea, are providential arrangements.”\n\n“But if one has too much in consequence of others being wronged, it seems to me that the divine voice which tells us to set that wrong right must be obeyed.”\n\n“What, my love, is the bearing of your remark?”\n\n“That you have been too liberal in arrangements for me—I mean, with regard to property; and that makes me unhappy.”\n\n“How so? I have none but comparatively distant connections.”\n\n“I have been led to think about your aunt Julia, and how she was left in poverty only because she married a poor man, an act which was not disgraceful, since he was not unworthy. It was on that ground, I know, that you educated Mr. Ladislaw and provided for his mother.”\n\nDorothea waited a few moments for some answer that would help her onward. None came, and her next words seemed the more forcible to her, falling clear upon the dark silence.\n\n“But surely we should regard his claim as a much greater one, even to the half of that property which I know that you have destined for me. And I think he ought at once to be provided for on that understanding. It is not right that he should be in the dependence of poverty while we are rich. And if there is any objection to the proposal he mentioned, the giving him his true place and his true share would set aside any motive for his accepting it.”\n\n“Mr. Ladislaw has probably been speaking to you on this subject?” said Mr. Casaubon, with a certain biting quickness not habitual to him.\n\n“Indeed, no!” said Dorothea, earnestly. “How can you imagine it, since he has so lately declined everything from you? I fear you think too hardly of him, dear. He only told me a little about his parents and grandparents, and almost all in answer to my questions. You are so good, so just—you have done everything you thought to be right. But it seems to me clear that more than that is right; and I must speak about it, since I am the person who would get what is called benefit by that ‘more’ not being done.”\n\nThere was a perceptible pause before Mr. Casaubon replied, not quickly as before, but with a still more biting emphasis.\n\n“Dorothea, my love, this is not the first occasion, but it were well that it should be the last, on which you have assumed a judgment on subjects beyond your scope. Into the question how far conduct, especially in the matter of alliances, constitutes a forfeiture of family claims, I do not now enter. Suffice it, that you are not here qualified to discriminate. What I now wish you to understand is, that I accept no revision, still less dictation within that range of affairs which I have deliberated upon as distinctly and properly mine. It is not for you to interfere between me and Mr. Ladislaw, and still less to encourage communications from him to you which constitute a criticism on my procedure.”\n\nPoor Dorothea, shrouded in the darkness, was in a tumult of conflicting emotions. Alarm at the possible effect on himself of her husband’s strongly manifested anger, would have checked any expression of her own resentment, even if she had been quite free from doubt and compunction under the consciousness that there might be some justice in his last insinuation. Hearing him breathe quickly after he had spoken, she sat listening, frightened, wretched—with a dumb inward cry for help to bear this nightmare of a life in which every energy was arrested by dread. But nothing else happened, except that they both remained a long while sleepless, without speaking again.\n\nThe next day, Mr. Casaubon received the following answer from Will Ladislaw:—\n\n“DEAR MR. CASAUBON,—I have given all due consideration to your letter of yesterday, but I am unable to take precisely your view of our mutual position. With the fullest acknowledgment of your generous conduct to me in the past, I must still maintain that an obligation of this kind cannot fairly fetter me as you appear to expect that it should. Granted that a benefactor’s wishes may constitute a claim; there must always be a reservation as to the quality of those wishes. They may possibly clash with more imperative considerations. Or a benefactor’s veto might impose such a negation on a man’s life that the consequent blank might be more cruel than the benefaction was generous. I am merely using strong illustrations. In the present case I am unable to take your view of the bearing which my acceptance of occupation—not enriching certainly, but not dishonorable—will have on your own position which seems to me too substantial to be affected in that shadowy manner. And though I do not believe that any change in our relations will occur (certainly none has yet occurred) which can nullify the obligations imposed on me by the past, pardon me for not seeing that those obligations should restrain me from using the ordinary freedom of living where I choose, and maintaining myself by any lawful occupation I may choose. Regretting that there exists this difference between us as to a relation in which the conferring of benefits has been entirely on your side—\n\nI remain, yours with persistent obligation, WILL LADISLAW.”\n\nPoor Mr. Casaubon felt (and must not we, being impartial, feel with him a little?) that no man had juster cause for disgust and suspicion than he. Young Ladislaw, he was sure, meant to defy and annoy him, meant to win Dorothea’s confidence and sow her mind with disrespect, and perhaps aversion, towards her husband. Some motive beneath the surface had been needed to account for Will’s sudden change of course in rejecting Mr. Casaubon’s aid and quitting his travels; and this defiant determination to fix himself in the neighborhood by taking up something so much at variance with his former choice as Mr. Brooke’s Middlemarch projects, revealed clearly enough that the undeclared motive had relation to Dorothea. Not for one moment did Mr. Casaubon suspect Dorothea of any doubleness: he had no suspicions of her, but he had (what was little less uncomfortable) the positive knowledge that her tendency to form opinions about her husband’s conduct was accompanied with a disposition to regard Will Ladislaw favorably and be influenced by what he said. His own proud reticence had prevented him from ever being undeceived in the supposition that Dorothea had originally asked her uncle to invite Will to his house.\n\nAnd now, on receiving Will’s letter, Mr. Casaubon had to consider his duty. He would never have been easy to call his action anything else than duty; but in this case, contending motives thrust him back into negations.\n\nShould he apply directly to Mr. Brooke, and demand of that troublesome gentleman to revoke his proposal? Or should he consult Sir James Chettam, and get him to concur in remonstrance against a step which touched the whole family? In either case Mr. Casaubon was aware that failure was just as probable as success. It was impossible for him to mention Dorothea’s name in the matter, and without some alarming urgency Mr. Brooke was as likely as not, after meeting all representations with apparent assent, to wind up by saying, “Never fear, Casaubon! Depend upon it, young Ladislaw will do you credit. Depend upon it, I have put my finger on the right thing.” And Mr. Casaubon shrank nervously from communicating on the subject with Sir James Chettam, between whom and himself there had never been any cordiality, and who would immediately think of Dorothea without any mention of her.\n\nPoor Mr. Casaubon was distrustful of everybody’s feeling towards him, especially as a husband. To let any one suppose that he was jealous would be to admit their (suspected) view of his disadvantages: to let them know that he did not find marriage particularly blissful would imply his conversion to their (probably) earlier disapproval. It would be as bad as letting Carp, and Brasenose generally, know how backward he was in organizing the matter for his “Key to all Mythologies.” All through his life Mr. Casaubon had been trying not to admit even to himself the inward sores of self-doubt and jealousy. And on the most delicate of all personal subjects, the habit of proud suspicious reticence told doubly.\n\nThus Mr. Casaubon remained proudly, bitterly silent. But he had forbidden Will to come to Lowick Manor, and he was mentally preparing other measures of frustration.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII.\n\n“C’est beaucoup que le jugement des hommes sur les actions humaines; tôt ou tard il devient efficace.”—GUIZOT.\n\nSir James Chettam could not look with any satisfaction on Mr. Brooke’s new courses; but it was easier to object than to hinder. Sir James accounted for his having come in alone one day to lunch with the Cadwalladers by saying—\n\n“I can’t talk to you as I want, before Celia: it might hurt her. Indeed, it would not be right.”\n\n“I know what you mean—the ‘Pioneer’ at the Grange!” darted in Mrs. Cadwallader, almost before the last word was off her friend’s tongue. “It is frightful—this taking to buying whistles and blowing them in everybody’s hearing. Lying in bed all day and playing at dominoes, like poor Lord Plessy, would be more private and bearable.”\n\n“I see they are beginning to attack our friend Brooke in the ‘Trumpet,’” said the Rector, lounging back and smiling easily, as he would have done if he had been attacked himself. “There are tremendous sarcasms against a landlord not a hundred miles from Middlemarch, who receives his own rents, and makes no returns.”\n\n“I do wish Brooke would leave that off,” said Sir James, with his little frown of annoyance.\n\n“Is he really going to be put in nomination, though?” said Mr. Cadwallader. “I saw Farebrother yesterday—he’s Whiggish himself, hoists Brougham and Useful Knowledge; that’s the worst I know of him;—and he says that Brooke is getting up a pretty strong party. Bulstrode, the banker, is his foremost man. But he thinks Brooke would come off badly at a nomination.”\n\n“Exactly,” said Sir James, with earnestness. “I have been inquiring into the thing, for I’ve never known anything about Middlemarch politics before—the county being my business. What Brooke trusts to, is that they are going to turn out Oliver because he is a Peelite. But Hawley tells me that if they send up a Whig at all it is sure to be Bagster, one of those candidates who come from heaven knows where, but dead against Ministers, and an experienced Parliamentary man. Hawley’s rather rough: he forgot that he was speaking to me. He said if Brooke wanted a pelting, he could get it cheaper than by going to the hustings.”\n\n“I warned you all of it,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, waving her hands outward. “I said to Humphrey long ago, Mr. Brooke is going to make a splash in the mud. And now he has done it.”\n\n“Well, he might have taken it into his head to marry,” said the Rector. “That would have been a graver mess than a little flirtation with politics.”\n\n“He may do that afterwards,” said Mrs. Cadwallader—“when he has come out on the other side of the mud with an ague.”\n\n“What I care for most is his own dignity,” said Sir James. “Of course I care the more because of the family. But he’s getting on in life now, and I don’t like to think of his exposing himself. They will be raking up everything against him.”\n\n“I suppose it’s no use trying any persuasion,” said the Rector. “There’s such an odd mixture of obstinacy and changeableness in Brooke. Have you tried him on the subject?”\n\n“Well, no,” said Sir James; “I feel a delicacy in appearing to dictate. But I have been talking to this young Ladislaw that Brooke is making a factotum of. Ladislaw seems clever enough for anything. I thought it as well to hear what he had to say; and he is against Brooke’s standing this time. I think he’ll turn him round: I think the nomination may be staved off.”\n\n“I know,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, nodding. “The independent member hasn’t got his speeches well enough by heart.”\n\n“But this Ladislaw—there again is a vexatious business,” said Sir James. “We have had him two or three times to dine at the Hall (you have met him, by the bye) as Brooke’s guest and a relation of Casaubon’s, thinking he was only on a flying visit. And now I find he’s in everybody’s mouth in Middlemarch as the editor of the ‘Pioneer.’ There are stories going about him as a quill-driving alien, a foreign emissary, and what not.”\n\n“Casaubon won’t like that,” said the Rector.\n\n“There is some foreign blood in Ladislaw,” returned Sir James. “I hope he won’t go into extreme opinions and carry Brooke on.”\n\n“Oh, he’s a dangerous young sprig, that Mr. Ladislaw,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, “with his opera songs and his ready tongue. A sort of Byronic hero—an amorous conspirator, it strikes me. And Thomas Aquinas is not fond of him. I could see that, the day the picture was brought.”\n\n“I don’t like to begin on the subject with Casaubon,” said Sir James. “He has more right to interfere than I. But it’s a disagreeable affair all round. What a character for anybody with decent connections to show himself in!—one of those newspaper fellows! You have only to look at Keck, who manages the ‘Trumpet.’ I saw him the other day with Hawley. His writing is sound enough, I believe, but he’s such a low fellow, that I wished he had been on the wrong side.”\n\n“What can you expect with these peddling Middlemarch papers?” said the Rector. “I don’t suppose you could get a high style of man anywhere to be writing up interests he doesn’t really care about, and for pay that hardly keeps him in at elbows.”\n\n“Exactly: that makes it so annoying that Brooke should have put a man who has a sort of connection with the family in a position of that kind. For my part, I think Ladislaw is rather a fool for accepting.”\n\n“It is Aquinas’s fault,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “Why didn’t he use his interest to get Ladislaw made an attache or sent to India? That is how families get rid of troublesome sprigs.”\n\n“There is no knowing to what lengths the mischief may go,” said Sir James, anxiously. “But if Casaubon says nothing, what can I do?”\n\n“Oh my dear Sir James,” said the Rector, “don’t let us make too much of all this. It is likely enough to end in mere smoke. After a month or two Brooke and this Master Ladislaw will get tired of each other; Ladislaw will take wing; Brooke will sell the ‘Pioneer,’ and everything will settle down again as usual.”\n\n“There is one good chance—that he will not like to feel his money oozing away,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “If I knew the items of election expenses I could scare him. It’s no use plying him with wide words like Expenditure: I wouldn’t talk of phlebotomy, I would empty a pot of leeches upon him. What we good stingy people don’t like, is having our sixpences sucked away from us.”\n\n“And he will not like having things raked up against him,” said Sir James. “There is the management of his estate. They have begun upon that already. And it really is painful for me to see. It is a nuisance under one’s very nose. I do think one is bound to do the best for one’s land and tenants, especially in these hard times.”\n\n“Perhaps the ‘Trumpet’ may rouse him to make a change, and some good may come of it all,” said the Rector. “I know I should be glad. I should hear less grumbling when my tithe is paid. I don’t know what I should do if there were not a modus in Tipton.”\n\n“I want him to have a proper man to look after things—I want him to take on Garth again,” said Sir James. “He got rid of Garth twelve years ago, and everything has been going wrong since. I think of getting Garth to manage for me—he has made such a capital plan for my buildings; and Lovegood is hardly up to the mark. But Garth would not undertake the Tipton estate again unless Brooke left it entirely to him.”\n\n“In the right of it too,” said the Rector. “Garth is an independent fellow: an original, simple-minded fellow. One day, when he was doing some valuation for me, he told me point-blank that clergymen seldom understood anything about business, and did mischief when they meddled; but he said it as quietly and respectfully as if he had been talking to me about sailors. He would make a different parish of Tipton, if Brooke would let him manage. I wish, by the help of the ‘Trumpet,’ you could bring that round.”\n\n“If Dorothea had kept near her uncle, there would have been some chance,” said Sir James. “She might have got some power over him in time, and she was always uneasy about the estate. She had wonderfully good notions about such things. But now Casaubon takes her up entirely. Celia complains a good deal. We can hardly get her to dine with us, since he had that fit.” Sir James ended with a look of pitying disgust, and Mrs. Cadwallader shrugged her shoulders as much as to say that she was not likely to see anything new in that direction.\n\n“Poor Casaubon!” the Rector said. “That was a nasty attack. I thought he looked shattered the other day at the Archdeacon’s.”\n\n“In point of fact,” resumed Sir James, not choosing to dwell on “fits,” “Brooke doesn’t mean badly by his tenants or any one else, but he has got that way of paring and clipping at expenses.”\n\n“Come, that’s a blessing,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “That helps him to find himself in a morning. He may not know his own opinions, but he does know his own pocket.”\n\n“I don’t believe a man is in pocket by stinginess on his land,” said Sir James.\n\n“Oh, stinginess may be abused like other virtues: it will not do to keep one’s own pigs lean,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, who had risen to look out of the window. “But talk of an independent politician and he will appear.”\n\n“What! Brooke?” said her husband.\n\n“Yes. Now, you ply him with the ‘Trumpet,’ Humphrey; and I will put the leeches on him. What will you do, Sir James?”\n\n“The fact is, I don’t like to begin about it with Brooke, in our mutual position; the whole thing is so unpleasant. I do wish people would behave like gentlemen,” said the good baronet, feeling that this was a simple and comprehensive programme for social well-being.\n\n“Here you all are, eh?” said Mr. Brooke, shuffling round and shaking hands. “I was going up to the Hall by-and-by, Chettam. But it’s pleasant to find everybody, you know. Well, what do you think of things?—going on a little fast! It was true enough, what Lafitte said—‘Since yesterday, a century has passed away:’—they’re in the next century, you know, on the other side of the water. Going on faster than we are.”\n\n“Why, yes,” said the Rector, taking up the newspaper. “Here is the ‘Trumpet’ accusing you of lagging behind—did you see?”\n\n“Eh? no,” said Mr. Brooke, dropping his gloves into his hat and hastily adjusting his eye-glass. But Mr. Cadwallader kept the paper in his hand, saying, with a smile in his eyes—\n\n“Look here! all this is about a landlord not a hundred miles from Middlemarch, who receives his own rents. They say he is the most retrogressive man in the county. I think you must have taught them that word in the ‘Pioneer.’”\n\n“Oh, that is Keck—an illiterate fellow, you know. Retrogressive, now! Come, that’s capital. He thinks it means destructive: they want to make me out a destructive, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, with that cheerfulness which is usually sustained by an adversary’s ignorance.\n\n“I think he knows the meaning of the word. Here is a sharp stroke or two. If we had to describe a man who is retrogressive in the most evil sense of the word—we should say, he is one who would dub himself a reformer of our constitution, while every interest for which he is immediately responsible is going to decay: a philanthropist who cannot bear one rogue to be hanged, but does not mind five honest tenants being half-starved: a man who shrieks at corruption, and keeps his farms at rack-rent: who roars himself red at rotten boroughs, and does not mind if every field on his farms has a rotten gate: a man very open-hearted to Leeds and Manchester, no doubt; he would give any number of representatives who will pay for their seats out of their own pockets: what he objects to giving, is a little return on rent-days to help a tenant to buy stock, or an outlay on repairs to keep the weather out at a tenant’s barn-door or make his house look a little less like an Irish cottier’s. But we all know the wag’s definition of a philanthropist: a man whose charity increases directly as the square of the distance. And so on. All the rest is to show what sort of legislator a philanthropist is likely to make,” ended the Rector, throwing down the paper, and clasping his hands at the back of his head, while he looked at Mr. Brooke with an air of amused neutrality.\n\n“Come, that’s rather good, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, taking up the paper and trying to bear the attack as easily as his neighbor did, but coloring and smiling rather nervously; “that about roaring himself red at rotten boroughs—I never made a speech about rotten boroughs in my life. And as to roaring myself red and that kind of thing—these men never understand what is good satire. Satire, you know, should be true up to a certain point. I recollect they said that in ‘The Edinburgh’ somewhere—it must be true up to a certain point.”\n\n“Well, that is really a hit about the gates,” said Sir James, anxious to tread carefully. “Dagley complained to me the other day that he hadn’t got a decent gate on his farm. Garth has invented a new pattern of gate—I wish you would try it. One ought to use some of one’s timber in that way.”\n\n“You go in for fancy farming, you know, Chettam,” said Mr. Brooke, appearing to glance over the columns of the “Trumpet.” “That’s your hobby, and you don’t mind the expense.”\n\n“I thought the most expensive hobby in the world was standing for Parliament,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “They said the last unsuccessful candidate at Middlemarch—Giles, wasn’t his name?—spent ten thousand pounds and failed because he did not bribe enough. What a bitter reflection for a man!”\n\n“Somebody was saying,” said the Rector, laughingly, “that East Retford was nothing to Middlemarch, for bribery.”\n\n“Nothing of the kind,” said Mr. Brooke. “The Tories bribe, you know: Hawley and his set bribe with treating, hot codlings, and that sort of thing; and they bring the voters drunk to the poll. But they are not going to have it their own way in future—not in future, you know. Middlemarch is a little backward, I admit—the freemen are a little backward. But we shall educate them—we shall bring them on, you know. The best people there are on our side.”\n\n“Hawley says you have men on your side who will do you harm,” remarked Sir James. “He says Bulstrode the banker will do you harm.”\n\n“And that if you got pelted,” interposed Mrs. Cadwallader, “half the rotten eggs would mean hatred of your committee-man. Good heavens! Think what it must be to be pelted for wrong opinions. And I seem to remember a story of a man they pretended to chair and let him fall into a dust-heap on purpose!”\n\n“Pelting is nothing to their finding holes in one’s coat,” said the Rector. “I confess that’s what I should be afraid of, if we parsons had to stand at the hustings for preferment. I should be afraid of their reckoning up all my fishing days. Upon my word, I think the truth is the hardest missile one can be pelted with.”\n\n“The fact is,” said Sir James, “if a man goes into public life he must be prepared for the consequences. He must make himself proof against calumny.”\n\n“My dear Chettam, that is all very fine, you know,” said Mr. Brooke. “But how will you make yourself proof against calumny? You should read history—look at ostracism, persecution, martyrdom, and that kind of thing. They always happen to the best men, you know. But what is that in Horace?—fiat justitia, ruat … something or other.”\n\n“Exactly,” said Sir James, with a little more heat than usual. “What I mean by being proof against calumny is being able to point to the fact as a contradiction.”\n\n“And it is not martyrdom to pay bills that one has run into one’s self,” said Mrs. Cadwallader.\n\nBut it was Sir James’s evident annoyance that most stirred Mr. Brooke. “Well, you know, Chettam,” he said, rising, taking up his hat and leaning on his stick, “you and I have a different system. You are all for outlay with your farms. I don’t want to make out that my system is good under all circumstances—under all circumstances, you know.”\n\n“There ought to be a new valuation made from time to time,” said Sir James. “Returns are very well occasionally, but I like a fair valuation. What do you say, Cadwallader?”\n\n“I agree with you. If I were Brooke, I would choke the ‘Trumpet’ at once by getting Garth to make a new valuation of the farms, and giving him carte blanche about gates and repairs: that’s my view of the political situation,” said the Rector, broadening himself by sticking his thumbs in his armholes, and laughing towards Mr. Brooke.\n\n“That’s a showy sort of thing to do, you know,” said Mr. Brooke. “But I should like you to tell me of another landlord who has distressed his tenants for arrears as little as I have. I let the old tenants stay on. I’m uncommonly easy, let me tell you, uncommonly easy. I have my own ideas, and I take my stand on them, you know. A man who does that is always charged with eccentricity, inconsistency, and that kind of thing. When I change my line of action, I shall follow my own ideas.”\n\nAfter that, Mr. Brooke remembered that there was a packet which he had omitted to send off from the Grange, and he bade everybody hurriedly good-by.\n\n“I didn’t want to take a liberty with Brooke,” said Sir James; “I see he is nettled. But as to what he says about old tenants, in point of fact no new tenant would take the farms on the present terms.”\n\n“I have a notion that he will be brought round in time,” said the Rector. “But you were pulling one way, Elinor, and we were pulling another. You wanted to frighten him away from expense, and we want to frighten him into it. Better let him try to be popular and see that his character as a landlord stands in his way. I don’t think it signifies two straws about the ‘Pioneer,’ or Ladislaw, or Brooke’s speechifying to the Middlemarchers. But it does signify about the parishioners in Tipton being comfortable.”\n\n“Excuse me, it is you two who are on the wrong tack,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “You should have proved to him that he loses money by bad management, and then we should all have pulled together. If you put him a-horseback on politics, I warn you of the consequences. It was all very well to ride on sticks at home and call them ideas.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX.\n\n“If, as I have, you also doe, Vertue attired in woman see, And dare love that, and say so too, And forget the He and She;\n\nAnd if this love, though placed so, From prophane men you hide, Which will no faith on this bestow, Or, if they doe, deride:\n\nThen you have done a braver thing Than all the Worthies did, And a braver thence will spring, Which is, to keep that hid.” —DR. DONNE.\n\nSir James Chettam’s mind was not fruitful in devices, but his growing anxiety to “act on Brooke,” once brought close to his constant belief in Dorothea’s capacity for influence, became formative, and issued in a little plan; namely, to plead Celia’s indisposition as a reason for fetching Dorothea by herself to the Hall, and to leave her at the Grange with the carriage on the way, after making her fully aware of the situation concerning the management of the estate.\n\nIn this way it happened that one day near four o’clock, when Mr. Brooke and Ladislaw were seated in the library, the door opened and Mrs. Casaubon was announced.\n\nWill, the moment before, had been low in the depths of boredom, and, obliged to help Mr. Brooke in arranging “documents” about hanging sheep-stealers, was exemplifying the power our minds have of riding several horses at once by inwardly arranging measures towards getting a lodging for himself in Middlemarch and cutting short his constant residence at the Grange; while there flitted through all these steadier images a tickling vision of a sheep-stealing epic written with Homeric particularity. When Mrs. Casaubon was announced he started up as from an electric shock, and felt a tingling at his finger-ends. Any one observing him would have seen a change in his complexion, in the adjustment of his facial muscles, in the vividness of his glance, which might have made them imagine that every molecule in his body had passed the message of a magic touch. And so it had. For effective magic is transcendent nature; and who shall measure the subtlety of those touches which convey the quality of soul as well as body, and make a man’s passion for one woman differ from his passion for another as joy in the morning light over valley and river and white mountain-top differs from joy among Chinese lanterns and glass panels? Will, too, was made of very impressible stuff. The bow of a violin drawn near him cleverly, would at one stroke change the aspect of the world for him, and his point of view shifted as easily as his mood. Dorothea’s entrance was the freshness of morning.\n\n“Well, my dear, this is pleasant, now,” said Mr. Brooke, meeting and kissing her. “You have left Casaubon with his books, I suppose. That’s right. We must not have you getting too learned for a woman, you know.”\n“There is no fear of that, uncle,” said Dorothea, turning to Will and shaking hands with open cheerfulness, while she made no other form of greeting, but went on answering her uncle. “I am very slow. When I want to be busy with books, I am often playing truant among my thoughts. I find it is not so easy to be learned as to plan cottages.”\n\nShe seated herself beside her uncle opposite to Will, and was evidently preoccupied with something that made her almost unmindful of him. He was ridiculously disappointed, as if he had imagined that her coming had anything to do with him.\n\n“Why, yes, my dear, it was quite your hobby to draw plans. But it was good to break that off a little. Hobbies are apt to run away with us, you know; it doesn’t do to be run away with. We must keep the reins. I have never let myself be run away with; I always pulled up. That is what I tell Ladislaw. He and I are alike, you know: he likes to go into everything. We are working at capital punishment. We shall do a great deal together, Ladislaw and I.”\n\n“Yes,” said Dorothea, with characteristic directness, “Sir James has been telling me that he is in hope of seeing a great change made soon in your management of the estate—that you are thinking of having the farms valued, and repairs made, and the cottages improved, so that Tipton may look quite another place. Oh, how happy!”—she went on, clasping her hands, with a return to that more childlike impetuous manner, which had been subdued since her marriage. “If I were at home still, I should take to riding again, that I might go about with you and see all that! And you are going to engage Mr. Garth, who praised my cottages, Sir James says.”\n\n“Chettam is a little hasty, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, coloring slightly; “a little hasty, you know. I never said I should do anything of the kind. I never said I should not do it, you know.”\n\n“He only feels confident that you will do it,” said Dorothea, in a voice as clear and unhesitating as that of a young chorister chanting a credo, “because you mean to enter Parliament as a member who cares for the improvement of the people, and one of the first things to be made better is the state of the land and the laborers. Think of Kit Downes, uncle, who lives with his wife and seven children in a house with one sitting room and one bedroom hardly larger than this table!—and those poor Dagleys, in their tumble-down farmhouse, where they live in the back kitchen and leave the other rooms to the rats! That is one reason why I did not like the pictures here, dear uncle—which you think me stupid about. I used to come from the village with all that dirt and coarse ugliness like a pain within me, and the simpering pictures in the drawing-room seemed to me like a wicked attempt to find delight in what is false, while we don’t mind how hard the truth is for the neighbors outside our walls. I think we have no right to come forward and urge wider changes for good, until we have tried to alter the evils which lie under our own hands.”\n\nDorothea had gathered emotion as she went on, and had forgotten everything except the relief of pouring forth her feelings, unchecked: an experience once habitual with her, but hardly ever present since her marriage, which had been a perpetual struggle of energy with fear. For the moment, Will’s admiration was accompanied with a chilling sense of remoteness. A man is seldom ashamed of feeling that he cannot love a woman so well when he sees a certain greatness in her: nature having intended greatness for men. But nature has sometimes made sad oversights in carrying out her intention; as in the case of good Mr. Brooke, whose masculine consciousness was at this moment in rather a stammering condition under the eloquence of his niece. He could not immediately find any other mode of expressing himself than that of rising, fixing his eye-glass, and fingering the papers before him. At last he said—\n\n“There is something in what you say, my dear, something in what you say—but not everything—eh, Ladislaw? You and I don’t like our pictures and statues being found fault with. Young ladies are a little ardent, you know—a little one-sided, my dear. Fine art, poetry, that kind of thing, elevates a nation—emollit mores—you understand a little Latin now. But—eh? what?”\n\nThese interrogatives were addressed to the footman who had come in to say that the keeper had found one of Dagley’s boys with a leveret in his hand just killed.\n\n“I’ll come, I’ll come. I shall let him off easily, you know,” said Mr. Brooke aside to Dorothea, shuffling away very cheerfully.\n\n“I hope you feel how right this change is that I—that Sir James wishes for,” said Dorothea to Will, as soon as her uncle was gone.\n\n“I do, now I have heard you speak about it. I shall not forget what you have said. But can you think of something else at this moment? I may not have another opportunity of speaking to you about what has occurred,” said Will, rising with a movement of impatience, and holding the back of his chair with both hands.\n\n“Pray tell me what it is,” said Dorothea, anxiously, also rising and going to the open window, where Monk was looking in, panting and wagging his tail. She leaned her back against the window-frame, and laid her hand on the dog’s head; for though, as we know, she was not fond of pets that must be held in the hands or trodden on, she was always attentive to the feelings of dogs, and very polite if she had to decline their advances.\n\nWill followed her only with his eyes and said, “I presume you know that Mr. Casaubon has forbidden me to go to his house.”\n\n“No, I did not,” said Dorothea, after a moment’s pause. She was evidently much moved. “I am very, very sorry,” she added, mournfully. She was thinking of what Will had no knowledge of—the conversation between her and her husband in the darkness; and she was anew smitten with hopelessness that she could influence Mr. Casaubon’s action. But the marked expression of her sorrow convinced Will that it was not all given to him personally, and that Dorothea had not been visited by the idea that Mr. Casaubon’s dislike and jealousy of him turned upon herself. He felt an odd mixture of delight and vexation: of delight that he could dwell and be cherished in her thought as in a pure home, without suspicion and without stint—of vexation because he was of too little account with her, was not formidable enough, was treated with an unhesitating benevolence which did not flatter him. But his dread of any change in Dorothea was stronger than his discontent, and he began to speak again in a tone of mere explanation.\n\n“Mr. Casaubon’s reason is, his displeasure at my taking a position here which he considers unsuited to my rank as his cousin. I have told him that I cannot give way on this point. It is a little too hard on me to expect that my course in life is to be hampered by prejudices which I think ridiculous. Obligation may be stretched till it is no better than a brand of slavery stamped on us when we were too young to know its meaning. I would not have accepted the position if I had not meant to make it useful and honorable. I am not bound to regard family dignity in any other light.”\n\nDorothea felt wretched. She thought her husband altogether in the wrong, on more grounds than Will had mentioned.\n\n“It is better for us not to speak on the subject,” she said, with a tremulousness not common in her voice, “since you and Mr. Casaubon disagree. You intend to remain?” She was looking out on the lawn, with melancholy meditation.\n\n“Yes; but I shall hardly ever see you now,” said Will, in a tone of almost boyish complaint.\n\n“No,” said Dorothea, turning her eyes full upon him, “hardly ever. But I shall hear of you. I shall know what you are doing for my uncle.”\n\n“I shall know hardly anything about you,” said Will. “No one will tell me anything.”\n\n“Oh, my life is very simple,” said Dorothea, her lips curling with an exquisite smile, which irradiated her melancholy. “I am always at Lowick.”\n\n“That is a dreadful imprisonment,” said Will, impetuously.\n\n“No, don’t think that,” said Dorothea. “I have no longings.”\n\nHe did not speak, but she replied to some change in his expression. “I mean, for myself. Except that I should like not to have so much more than my share without doing anything for others. But I have a belief of my own, and it comforts me.”\n\n“What is that?” said Will, rather jealous of the belief.\n\n“That by desiring what is perfectly good, even when we don’t quite know what it is and cannot do what we would, we are part of the divine power against evil—widening the skirts of light and making the struggle with darkness narrower.”\n\n“That is a beautiful mysticism—it is a—”\n\n“Please not to call it by any name,” said Dorothea, putting out her hands entreatingly. “You will say it is Persian, or something else geographical. It is my life. I have found it out, and cannot part with it. I have always been finding out my religion since I was a little girl. I used to pray so much—now I hardly ever pray. I try not to have desires merely for myself, because they may not be good for others, and I have too much already. I only told you, that you might know quite well how my days go at Lowick.”\n\n“God bless you for telling me!” said Will, ardently, and rather wondering at himself. They were looking at each other like two fond children who were talking confidentially of birds.\n\n“What is your religion?” said Dorothea. “I mean—not what you know about religion, but the belief that helps you most?”\n\n“To love what is good and beautiful when I see it,” said Will. “But I am a rebel: I don’t feel bound, as you do, to submit to what I don’t like.”\n\n“But if you like what is good, that comes to the same thing,” said Dorothea, smiling.\n\n“Now you are subtle,” said Will.\n\n“Yes; Mr. Casaubon often says I am too subtle. I don’t feel as if I were subtle,” said Dorothea, playfully. “But how long my uncle is! I must go and look for him. I must really go on to the Hall. Celia is expecting me.”\n\nWill offered to tell Mr. Brooke, who presently came and said that he would step into the carriage and go with Dorothea as far as Dagley’s, to speak about the small delinquent who had been caught with the leveret. Dorothea renewed the subject of the estate as they drove along, but Mr. Brooke, not being taken unawares, got the talk under his own control.\n\n“Chettam, now,” he replied; “he finds fault with me, my dear; but I should not preserve my game if it were not for Chettam, and he can’t say that that expense is for the sake of the tenants, you know. It’s a little against my feeling:—poaching, now, if you come to look into it—I have often thought of getting up the subject. Not long ago, Flavell, the Methodist preacher, was brought up for knocking down a hare that came across his path when he and his wife were walking out together. He was pretty quick, and knocked it on the neck.”\n\n“That was very brutal, I think,” said Dorothea.\n\n“Well, now, it seemed rather black to me, I confess, in a Methodist preacher, you know. And Johnson said, ‘You may judge what a hypocrite he is.’ And upon my word, I thought Flavell looked very little like ‘the highest style of man’—as somebody calls the Christian—Young, the poet Young, I think—you know Young? Well, now, Flavell in his shabby black gaiters, pleading that he thought the Lord had sent him and his wife a good dinner, and he had a right to knock it down, though not a mighty hunter before the Lord, as Nimrod was—I assure you it was rather comic: Fielding would have made something of it—or Scott, now—Scott might have worked it up. But really, when I came to think of it, I couldn’t help liking that the fellow should have a bit of hare to say grace over. It’s all a matter of prejudice—prejudice with the law on its side, you know—about the stick and the gaiters, and so on. However, it doesn’t do to reason about things; and law is law. But I got Johnson to be quiet, and I hushed the matter up. I doubt whether Chettam would not have been more severe, and yet he comes down on me as if I were the hardest man in the county. But here we are at Dagley’s.”\n\nMr. Brooke got down at a farmyard-gate, and Dorothea drove on. It is wonderful how much uglier things will look when we only suspect that we are blamed for them. Even our own persons in the glass are apt to change their aspect for us after we have heard some frank remark on their less admirable points; and on the other hand it is astonishing how pleasantly conscience takes our encroachments on those who never complain or have nobody to complain for them. Dagley’s homestead never before looked so dismal to Mr. Brooke as it did today, with his mind thus sore about the fault-finding of the “Trumpet,” echoed by Sir James.\n\nIt is true that an observer, under that softening influence of the fine arts which makes other people’s hardships picturesque, might have been delighted with this homestead called Freeman’s End: the old house had dormer-windows in the dark red roof, two of the chimneys were choked with ivy, the large porch was blocked up with bundles of sticks, and half the windows were closed with gray worm-eaten shutters about which the jasmine-boughs grew in wild luxuriance; the mouldering garden wall with hollyhocks peeping over it was a perfect study of highly mingled subdued color, and there was an aged goat (kept doubtless on interesting superstitious grounds) lying against the open back-kitchen door. The mossy thatch of the cow-shed, the broken gray barn-doors, the pauper laborers in ragged breeches who had nearly finished unloading a wagon of corn into the barn ready for early thrashing; the scanty dairy of cows being tethered for milking and leaving one half of the shed in brown emptiness; the very pigs and white ducks seeming to wander about the uneven neglected yard as if in low spirits from feeding on a too meagre quality of rinsings,—all these objects under the quiet light of a sky marbled with high clouds would have made a sort of picture which we have all paused over as a “charming bit,” touching other sensibilities than those which are stirred by the depression of the agricultural interest, with the sad lack of farming capital, as seen constantly in the newspapers of that time. But these troublesome associations were just now strongly present to Mr. Brooke, and spoiled the scene for him. Mr. Dagley himself made a figure in the landscape, carrying a pitchfork and wearing his milking-hat—a very old beaver flattened in front. His coat and breeches were the best he had, and he would not have been wearing them on this weekday occasion if he had not been to market and returned later than usual, having given himself the rare treat of dining at the public table of the Blue Bull. How he came to fall into this extravagance would perhaps be matter of wonderment to himself on the morrow; but before dinner something in the state of the country, a slight pause in the harvest before the Far Dips were cut, the stories about the new King and the numerous handbills on the walls, had seemed to warrant a little recklessness. It was a maxim about Middlemarch, and regarded as self-evident, that good meat should have good drink, which last Dagley interpreted as plenty of table ale well followed up by rum-and-water. These liquors have so far truth in them that they were not false enough to make poor Dagley seem merry: they only made his discontent less tongue-tied than usual. He had also taken too much in the shape of muddy political talk, a stimulant dangerously disturbing to his farming conservatism, which consisted in holding that whatever is, is bad, and any change is likely to be worse. He was flushed, and his eyes had a decidedly quarrelsome stare as he stood still grasping his pitchfork, while the landlord approached with his easy shuffling walk, one hand in his trouser-pocket and the other swinging round a thin walking-stick.\n\n“Dagley, my good fellow,” began Mr. Brooke, conscious that he was going to be very friendly about the boy.\n\n“Oh, ay, I’m a good feller, am I? Thank ye, sir, thank ye,” said Dagley, with a loud snarling irony which made Fag the sheep-dog stir from his seat and prick his ears; but seeing Monk enter the yard after some outside loitering, Fag seated himself again in an attitude of observation. “I’m glad to hear I’m a good feller.”\n\nMr. Brooke reflected that it was market-day, and that his worthy tenant had probably been dining, but saw no reason why he should not go on, since he could take the precaution of repeating what he had to say to Mrs. Dagley.\n\n“Your little lad Jacob has been caught killing a leveret, Dagley: I have told Johnson to lock him up in the empty stable an hour or two, just to frighten him, you know. But he will be brought home by-and-by, before night: and you’ll just look after him, will you, and give him a reprimand, you know?”\n\n“No, I woon’t: I’ll be dee’d if I’ll leather my boy to please you or anybody else, not if you was twenty landlords istid o’ one, and that a bad un.”\n\nDagley’s words were loud enough to summon his wife to the back-kitchen door—the only entrance ever used, and one always open except in bad weather—and Mr. Brooke, saying soothingly, “Well, well, I’ll speak to your wife—I didn’t mean beating, you know,” turned to walk to the house. But Dagley, only the more inclined to “have his say” with a gentleman who walked away from him, followed at once, with Fag slouching at his heels and sullenly evading some small and probably charitable advances on the part of Monk.\n\n“How do you do, Mrs. Dagley?” said Mr. Brooke, making some haste. “I came to tell you about your boy: I don’t want you to give him the stick, you know.” He was careful to speak quite plainly this time.\n\nOverworked Mrs. Dagley—a thin, worn woman, from whose life pleasure had so entirely vanished that she had not even any Sunday clothes which could give her satisfaction in preparing for church—had already had a misunderstanding with her husband since he had come home, and was in low spirits, expecting the worst. But her husband was beforehand in answering.\n\n“No, nor he woon’t hev the stick, whether you want it or no,” pursued Dagley, throwing out his voice, as if he wanted it to hit hard. “You’ve got no call to come an’ talk about sticks o’ these primises, as you woon’t give a stick tow’rt mending. Go to Middlemarch to ax for your charrickter.”\n\n“You’d far better hold your tongue, Dagley,” said the wife, “and not kick your own trough over. When a man as is father of a family has been an’ spent money at market and made himself the worse for liquor, he’s done enough mischief for one day. But I should like to know what my boy’s done, sir.”\n\n“Niver do you mind what he’s done,” said Dagley, more fiercely, “it’s my business to speak, an’ not yourn. An’ I wull speak, too. I’ll hev my say—supper or no. An’ what I say is, as I’ve lived upo’ your ground from my father and grandfather afore me, an’ hev dropped our money into’t, an’ me an’ my children might lie an’ rot on the ground for top-dressin’ as we can’t find the money to buy, if the King wasn’t to put a stop.”\n\n“My good fellow, you’re drunk, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, confidentially but not judiciously. “Another day, another day,” he added, turning as if to go.\n\nBut Dagley immediately fronted him, and Fag at his heels growled low, as his master’s voice grew louder and more insulting, while Monk also drew close in silent dignified watch. The laborers on the wagon were pausing to listen, and it seemed wiser to be quite passive than to attempt a ridiculous flight pursued by a bawling man.\n\n“I’m no more drunk nor you are, nor so much,” said Dagley. “I can carry my liquor, an’ I know what I meean. An’ I meean as the King ’ull put a stop to ’t, for them say it as knows it, as there’s to be a Rinform, and them landlords as never done the right thing by their tenants ’ull be treated i’ that way as they’ll hev to scuttle off. An’ there’s them i’ Middlemarch knows what the Rinform is—an’ as knows who’ll hev to scuttle. Says they, ‘I know who your landlord is.’ An’ says I, ‘I hope you’re the better for knowin’ him, I arn’t.’ Says they, ‘He’s a close-fisted un.’ ‘Ay ay,’ says I. ‘He’s a man for the Rinform,’ says they. That’s what they says. An’ I made out what the Rinform were—an’ it were to send you an’ your likes a-scuttlin’ an’ wi’ pretty strong-smellin’ things too. An’ you may do as you like now, for I’m none afeard on you. An’ you’d better let my boy aloan, an’ look to yoursen, afore the Rinform has got upo’ your back. That’s what I’n got to say,” concluded Mr. Dagley, striking his fork into the ground with a firmness which proved inconvenient as he tried to draw it up again.\n\nAt this last action Monk began to bark loudly, and it was a moment for Mr. Brooke to escape. He walked out of the yard as quickly as he could, in some amazement at the novelty of his situation. He had never been insulted on his own land before, and had been inclined to regard himself as a general favorite (we are all apt to do so, when we think of our own amiability more than of what other people are likely to want of us). When he had quarrelled with Caleb Garth twelve years before he had thought that the tenants would be pleased at the landlord’s taking everything into his own hands.\n\nSome who follow the narrative of his experience may wonder at the midnight darkness of Mr. Dagley; but nothing was easier in those times than for an hereditary farmer of his grade to be ignorant, in spite somehow of having a rector in the twin parish who was a gentleman to the backbone, a curate nearer at hand who preached more learnedly than the rector, a landlord who had gone into everything, especially fine art and social improvement, and all the lights of Middlemarch only three miles off. As to the facility with which mortals escape knowledge, try an average acquaintance in the intellectual blaze of London, and consider what that eligible person for a dinner-party would have been if he had learned scant skill in “summing” from the parish-clerk of Tipton, and read a chapter in the Bible with immense difficulty, because such names as Isaiah or Apollos remained unmanageable after twice spelling. Poor Dagley read a few verses sometimes on a Sunday evening, and the world was at least not darker to him than it had been before. Some things he knew thoroughly, namely, the slovenly habits of farming, and the awkwardness of weather, stock and crops, at Freeman’s End—so called apparently by way of sarcasm, to imply that a man was free to quit it if he chose, but that there was no earthly “beyond” open to him.\n\n# CHAPTER XL.\n\nWise in his daily work was he: To fruits of diligence, And not to faiths or polity, He plied his utmost sense. These perfect in their little parts, Whose work is all their prize— Without them how could laws, or arts, Or towered cities rise?\n\nIn watching effects, if only of an electric battery, it is often necessary to change our place and examine a particular mixture or group at some distance from the point where the movement we are interested in was set up. The group I am moving towards is at Caleb Garth’s breakfast-table in the large parlor where the maps and desk were: father, mother, and five of the children. Mary was just now at home waiting for a situation, while Christy, the boy next to her, was getting cheap learning and cheap fare in Scotland, having to his father’s disappointment taken to books instead of that sacred calling “business.”\n\nThe letters had come—nine costly letters, for which the postman had been paid three and twopence, and Mr. Garth was forgetting his tea and toast while he read his letters and laid them open one above the other, sometimes swaying his head slowly, sometimes screwing up his mouth in inward debate, but not forgetting to cut off a large red seal unbroken, which Letty snatched up like an eager terrier.\n\nThe talk among the rest went on unrestrainedly, for nothing disturbed Caleb’s absorption except shaking the table when he was writing.\n\nTwo letters of the nine had been for Mary. After reading them, she had passed them to her mother, and sat playing with her tea-spoon absently, till with a sudden recollection she returned to her sewing, which she had kept on her lap during breakfast.\n\n“Oh, don’t sew, Mary!” said Ben, pulling her arm down. “Make me a peacock with this bread-crumb.” He had been kneading a small mass for the purpose.\n\n“No, no, Mischief!” said Mary, good-humoredly, while she pricked his hand lightly with her needle. “Try and mould it yourself: you have seen me do it often enough. I must get this sewing done. It is for Rosamond Vincy: she is to be married next week, and she can’t be married without this handkerchief.” Mary ended merrily, amused with the last notion.\n\n“Why can’t she, Mary?” said Letty, seriously interested in this mystery, and pushing her head so close to her sister that Mary now turned the threatening needle towards Letty’s nose.\n\n“Because this is one of a dozen, and without it there would only be eleven,” said Mary, with a grave air of explanation, so that Letty sank back with a sense of knowledge.\n\n“Have you made up your mind, my dear?” said Mrs. Garth, laying the letters down.\n\n“I shall go to the school at York,” said Mary. “I am less unfit to teach in a school than in a family. I like to teach classes best. And, you see, I must teach: there is nothing else to be done.”\n\n“Teaching seems to me the most delightful work in the world,” said Mrs. Garth, with a touch of rebuke in her tone. “I could understand your objection to it if you had not knowledge enough, Mary, or if you disliked children.”\n\n“I suppose we never quite understand why another dislikes what we like, mother,” said Mary, rather curtly. “I am not fond of a schoolroom: I like the outside world better. It is a very inconvenient fault of mine.”\n\n“It must be very stupid to be always in a girls’ school,” said Alfred. “Such a set of nincompoops, like Mrs. Ballard’s pupils walking two and two.”\n\n“And they have no games worth playing at,” said Jim. “They can neither throw nor leap. I don’t wonder at Mary’s not liking it.”\n\n“What is that Mary doesn’t like, eh?” said the father, looking over his spectacles and pausing before he opened his next letter.\n\n“Being among a lot of nincompoop girls,” said Alfred.\n\n“Is it the situation you had heard of, Mary?” said Caleb, gently, looking at his daughter.\n\n“Yes, father: the school at York. I have determined to take it. It is quite the best. Thirty-five pounds a-year, and extra pay for teaching the smallest strummers at the piano.”\n\n“Poor child! I wish she could stay at home with us, Susan,” said Caleb, looking plaintively at his wife.\n\n“Mary would not be happy without doing her duty,” said Mrs. Garth, magisterially, conscious of having done her own.\n\n“It wouldn’t make me happy to do such a nasty duty as that,” said Alfred—at which Mary and her father laughed silently, but Mrs. Garth said, gravely—\n\n“Do find a fitter word than nasty, my dear Alfred, for everything that you think disagreeable. And suppose that Mary could help you to go to Mr. Hanmer’s with the money she gets?”\n\n“That seems to me a great shame. But she’s an old brick,” said Alfred, rising from his chair, and pulling Mary’s head backward to kiss her.\n\nMary colored and laughed, but could not conceal that the tears were coming. Caleb, looking on over his spectacles, with the angles of his eyebrows falling, had an expression of mingled delight and sorrow as he returned to the opening of his letter; and even Mrs. Garth, her lips curling with a calm contentment, allowed that inappropriate language to pass without correction, although Ben immediately took it up, and sang, “She’s an old brick, old brick, old brick!” to a cantering measure, which he beat out with his fist on Mary’s arm.\n\nBut Mrs. Garth’s eyes were now drawn towards her husband, who was already deep in the letter he was reading. His face had an expression of grave surprise, which alarmed her a little, but he did not like to be questioned while he was reading, and she remained anxiously watching till she saw him suddenly shaken by a little joyous laugh as he turned back to the beginning of the letter, and looking at her above his spectacles, said, in a low tone, “What do you think, Susan?”\n\nShe went and stood behind him, putting her hand on his shoulder, while they read the letter together. It was from Sir James Chettam, offering to Mr. Garth the management of the family estates at Freshitt and elsewhere, and adding that Sir James had been requested by Mr. Brooke of Tipton to ascertain whether Mr. Garth would be disposed at the same time to resume the agency of the Tipton property. The Baronet added in very obliging words that he himself was particularly desirous of seeing the Freshitt and Tipton estates under the same management, and he hoped to be able to show that the double agency might be held on terms agreeable to Mr. Garth, whom he would be glad to see at the Hall at twelve o’clock on the following day.\n\n“He writes handsomely, doesn’t he, Susan?” said Caleb, turning his eyes upward to his wife, who raised her hand from his shoulder to his ear, while she rested her chin on his head. “Brooke didn’t like to ask me himself, I can see,” he continued, laughing silently.\n\n“Here is an honor to your father, children,” said Mrs. Garth, looking round at the five pair of eyes, all fixed on the parents. “He is asked to take a post again by those who dismissed him long ago. That shows that he did his work well, so that they feel the want of him.”\n\n“Like Cincinnatus—hooray!” said Ben, riding on his chair, with a pleasant confidence that discipline was relaxed.\n\n“Will they come to fetch him, mother?” said Letty, thinking of the Mayor and Corporation in their robes.\n\nMrs. Garth patted Letty’s head and smiled, but seeing that her husband was gathering up his letters and likely soon to be out of reach in that sanctuary “business,” she pressed his shoulder and said emphatically—\n\n“Now, mind you ask fair pay, Caleb.”\n\n“Oh yes,” said Caleb, in a deep voice of assent, as if it would be unreasonable to suppose anything else of him. “It’ll come to between four and five hundred, the two together.” Then with a little start of remembrance he said, “Mary, write and give up that school. Stay and help your mother. I’m as pleased as Punch, now I’ve thought of that.”\n\nNo manner could have been less like that of Punch triumphant than Caleb’s, but his talents did not lie in finding phrases, though he was very particular about his letter-writing, and regarded his wife as a treasury of correct language.\n\nThere was almost an uproar among the children now, and Mary held up the cambric embroidery towards her mother entreatingly, that it might be put out of reach while the boys dragged her into a dance. Mrs. Garth, in placid joy, began to put the cups and plates together, while Caleb pushing his chair from the table, as if he were going to move to the desk, still sat holding his letters in his hand and looking on the ground meditatively, stretching out the fingers of his left hand, according to a mute language of his own. At last he said—\n\n“It’s a thousand pities Christy didn’t take to business, Susan. I shall want help by-and-by. And Alfred must go off to the engineering—I’ve made up my mind to that.” He fell into meditation and finger-rhetoric again for a little while, and then continued: “I shall make Brooke have new agreements with the tenants, and I shall draw up a rotation of crops. And I’ll lay a wager we can get fine bricks out of the clay at Bott’s corner. I must look into that: it would cheapen the repairs. It’s a fine bit of work, Susan! A man without a family would be glad to do it for nothing.”\n\n“Mind you don’t, though,” said his wife, lifting up her finger.\n\n“No, no; but it’s a fine thing to come to a man when he’s seen into the nature of business: to have the chance of getting a bit of the country into good fettle, as they say, and putting men into the right way with their farming, and getting a bit of good contriving and solid building done—that those who are living and those who come after will be the better for. I’d sooner have it than a fortune. I hold it the most honorable work that is.” Here Caleb laid down his letters, thrust his fingers between the buttons of his waistcoat, and sat upright, but presently proceeded with some awe in his voice and moving his head slowly aside—“It’s a great gift of God, Susan.”\n\n“That it is, Caleb,” said his wife, with answering fervor. “And it will be a blessing to your children to have had a father who did such work: a father whose good work remains though his name may be forgotten.” She could not say any more to him then about the pay.\n\nIn the evening, when Caleb, rather tired with his day’s work, was seated in silence with his pocket-book open on his knee, while Mrs. Garth and Mary were at their sewing, and Letty in a corner was whispering a dialogue with her doll, Mr. Farebrother came up the orchard walk, dividing the bright August lights and shadows with the tufted grass and the apple-tree boughs. We know that he was fond of his parishioners the Garths, and had thought Mary worth mentioning to Lydgate. He used to the full the clergyman’s privilege of disregarding the Middlemarch discrimination of ranks, and always told his mother that Mrs. Garth was more of a lady than any matron in the town. Still, you see, he spent his evenings at the Vincys’, where the matron, though less of a lady, presided over a well-lit drawing-room and whist. In those days human intercourse was not determined solely by respect. But the Vicar did heartily respect the Garths, and a visit from him was no surprise to that family. Nevertheless he accounted for it even while he was shaking hands, by saying, “I come as an envoy, Mrs. Garth: I have something to say to you and Garth on behalf of Fred Vincy. The fact is, poor fellow,” he continued, as he seated himself and looked round with his bright glance at the three who were listening to him, “he has taken me into his confidence.”\n\nMary’s heart beat rather quickly: she wondered how far Fred’s confidence had gone.\n\n“We haven’t seen the lad for months,” said Caleb. “I couldn’t think what was become of him.”\n\n“He has been away on a visit,” said the Vicar, “because home was a little too hot for him, and Lydgate told his mother that the poor fellow must not begin to study yet. But yesterday he came and poured himself out to me. I am very glad he did, because I have seen him grow up from a youngster of fourteen, and I am so much at home in the house that the children are like nephews and nieces to me. But it is a difficult case to advise upon. However, he has asked me to come and tell you that he is going away, and that he is so miserable about his debt to you, and his inability to pay, that he can’t bear to come himself even to bid you good by.”\n\n“Tell him it doesn’t signify a farthing,” said Caleb, waving his hand. “We’ve had the pinch and have got over it. And now I’m going to be as rich as a Jew.”\n\n“Which means,” said Mrs. Garth, smiling at the Vicar, “that we are going to have enough to bring up the boys well and to keep Mary at home.”\n\n“What is the treasure-trove?” said Mr. Farebrother.\n\n“I’m going to be agent for two estates, Freshitt and Tipton; and perhaps for a pretty little bit of land in Lowick besides: it’s all the same family connection, and employment spreads like water if it’s once set going. It makes me very happy, Mr. Farebrother”—here Caleb threw back his head a little, and spread his arms on the elbows of his chair—“that I’ve got an opportunity again with the letting of the land, and carrying out a notion or two with improvements. It’s a most uncommonly cramping thing, as I’ve often told Susan, to sit on horseback and look over the hedges at the wrong thing, and not be able to put your hand to it to make it right. What people do who go into politics I can’t think: it drives me almost mad to see mismanagement over only a few hundred acres.”\n\nIt was seldom that Caleb volunteered so long a speech, but his happiness had the effect of mountain air: his eyes were bright, and the words came without effort.\n\n“I congratulate you heartily, Garth,” said the Vicar. “This is the best sort of news I could have had to carry to Fred Vincy, for he dwelt a good deal on the injury he had done you in causing you to part with money—robbing you of it, he said—which you wanted for other purposes. I wish Fred were not such an idle dog; he has some very good points, and his father is a little hard upon him.”\n\n“Where is he going?” said Mrs. Garth, rather coldly.\n\n“He means to try again for his degree, and he is going up to study before term. I have advised him to do that. I don’t urge him to enter the Church—on the contrary. But if he will go and work so as to pass, that will be some guarantee that he has energy and a will; and he is quite at sea; he doesn’t know what else to do. So far he will please his father, and I have promised in the mean time to try and reconcile Vincy to his son’s adopting some other line of life. Fred says frankly he is not fit for a clergyman, and I would do anything I could to hinder a man from the fatal step of choosing the wrong profession. He quoted to me what you said, Miss Garth—do you remember it?” (Mr. Farebrother used to say “Mary” instead of “Miss Garth,” but it was part of his delicacy to treat her with the more deference because, according to Mrs. Vincy’s phrase, she worked for her bread.)\n\nMary felt uncomfortable, but, determined to take the matter lightly, answered at once, “I have said so many impertinent things to Fred—we are such old playfellows.”\n\n“You said, according to him, that he would be one of those ridiculous clergymen who help to make the whole clergy ridiculous. Really, that was so cutting that I felt a little cut myself.”\n\nCaleb laughed. “She gets her tongue from you, Susan,” he said, with some enjoyment.\n\n“Not its flippancy, father,” said Mary, quickly, fearing that her mother would be displeased. “It is rather too bad of Fred to repeat my flippant speeches to Mr. Farebrother.”\n\n“It was certainly a hasty speech, my dear,” said Mrs. Garth, with whom speaking evil of dignities was a high misdemeanor. “We should not value our Vicar the less because there was a ridiculous curate in the next parish.”\n\n“There’s something in what she says, though,” said Caleb, not disposed to have Mary’s sharpness undervalued. “A bad workman of any sort makes his fellows mistrusted. Things hang together,” he added, looking on the floor and moving his feet uneasily with a sense that words were scantier than thoughts.\n\n“Clearly,” said the Vicar, amused. “By being contemptible we set men’s minds to the tune of contempt. I certainly agree with Miss Garth’s view of the matter, whether I am condemned by it or not. But as to Fred Vincy, it is only fair he should be excused a little: old Featherstone’s delusive behavior did help to spoil him. There was something quite diabolical in not leaving him a farthing after all. But Fred has the good taste not to dwell on that. And what he cares most about is having offended you, Mrs. Garth; he supposes you will never think well of him again.”\n\n“I have been disappointed in Fred,” said Mrs. Garth, with decision. “But I shall be ready to think well of him again when he gives me good reason to do so.”\n\nAt this point Mary went out of the room, taking Letty with her.\n\n“Oh, we must forgive young people when they’re sorry,” said Caleb, watching Mary close the door. “And as you say, Mr. Farebrother, there was the very devil in that old man. Now Mary’s gone out, I must tell you a thing—it’s only known to Susan and me, and you’ll not tell it again. The old scoundrel wanted Mary to burn one of the wills the very night he died, when she was sitting up with him by herself, and he offered her a sum of money that he had in the box by him if she would do it. But Mary, you understand, could do no such thing—would not be handling his iron chest, and so on. Now, you see, the will he wanted burnt was this last, so that if Mary had done what he wanted, Fred Vincy would have had ten thousand pounds. The old man did turn to him at the last. That touches poor Mary close; she couldn’t help it—she was in the right to do what she did, but she feels, as she says, much as if she had knocked down somebody’s property and broken it against her will, when she was rightfully defending herself. I feel with her, somehow, and if I could make any amends to the poor lad, instead of bearing him a grudge for the harm he did us, I should be glad to do it. Now, what is your opinion, sir? Susan doesn’t agree with me; she says—tell what you say, Susan.”\n\n“Mary could not have acted otherwise, even if she had known what would be the effect on Fred,” said Mrs. Garth, pausing from her work, and looking at Mr. Farebrother.\n\n“And she was quite ignorant of it. It seems to me, a loss which falls on another because we have done right is not to lie upon our conscience.”\n\nThe Vicar did not answer immediately, and Caleb said, “It’s the feeling. The child feels in that way, and I feel with her. You don’t mean your horse to tread on a dog when you’re backing out of the way; but it goes through you, when it’s done.”\n\n“I am sure Mrs. Garth would agree with you there,” said Mr. Farebrother, who for some reason seemed more inclined to ruminate than to speak. “One could hardly say that the feeling you mention about Fred is wrong—or rather, mistaken—though no man ought to make a claim on such feeling.”\n\n“Well, well,” said Caleb, “it’s a secret. You will not tell Fred.”\n\n“Certainly not. But I shall carry the other good news—that you can afford the loss he caused you.”\n\nMr. Farebrother left the house soon after, and seeing Mary in the orchard with Letty, went to say good-by to her. They made a pretty picture in the western light which brought out the brightness of the apples on the old scant-leaved boughs—Mary in her lavender gingham and black ribbons holding a basket, while Letty in her well-worn nankin picked up the fallen apples. If you want to know more particularly how Mary looked, ten to one you will see a face like hers in the crowded street to-morrow, if you are there on the watch: she will not be among those daughters of Zion who are haughty, and walk with stretched-out necks and wanton eyes, mincing as they go: let all those pass, and fix your eyes on some small plump brownish person of firm but quiet carriage, who looks about her, but does not suppose that anybody is looking at her. If she has a broad face and square brow, well-marked eyebrows and curly dark hair, a certain expression of amusement in her glance which her mouth keeps the secret of, and for the rest features entirely insignificant—take that ordinary but not disagreeable person for a portrait of Mary Garth. If you made her smile, she would show you perfect little teeth; if you made her angry, she would not raise her voice, but would probably say one of the bitterest things you have ever tasted the flavor of; if you did her a kindness, she would never forget it. Mary admired the keen-faced handsome little Vicar in his well-brushed threadbare clothes more than any man she had had the opportunity of knowing. She had never heard him say a foolish thing, though she knew that he did unwise ones; and perhaps foolish sayings were more objectionable to her than any of Mr. Farebrother’s unwise doings. At least, it was remarkable that the actual imperfections of the Vicar’s clerical character never seemed to call forth the same scorn and dislike which she showed beforehand for the predicted imperfections of the clerical character sustained by Fred Vincy. These irregularities of judgment, I imagine, are found even in riper minds than Mary Garth’s: our impartiality is kept for abstract merit and demerit, which none of us ever saw. Will any one guess towards which of those widely different men Mary had the peculiar woman’s tenderness?—the one she was most inclined to be severe on, or the contrary?\n\n“Have you any message for your old playfellow, Miss Garth?” said the Vicar, as he took a fragrant apple from the basket which she held towards him, and put it in his pocket. “Something to soften down that harsh judgment? I am going straight to see him.”\n\n“No,” said Mary, shaking her head, and smiling. “If I were to say that he would not be ridiculous as a clergyman, I must say that he would be something worse than ridiculous. But I am very glad to hear that he is going away to work.”\n\n“On the other hand, I am very glad to hear that you are not going away to work. My mother, I am sure, will be all the happier if you will come to see her at the vicarage: you know she is fond of having young people to talk to, and she has a great deal to tell about old times. You will really be doing a kindness.”\n\n“I should like it very much, if I may,” said Mary. “Everything seems too happy for me all at once. I thought it would always be part of my life to long for home, and losing that grievance makes me feel rather empty: I suppose it served instead of sense to fill up my mind?”\n\n“May I go with you, Mary?” whispered Letty—a most inconvenient child, who listened to everything. But she was made exultant by having her chin pinched and her cheek kissed by Mr. Farebrother—an incident which she narrated to her mother and father.\n\nAs the Vicar walked to Lowick, any one watching him closely might have seen him twice shrug his shoulders. I think that the rare Englishmen who have this gesture are never of the heavy type—for fear of any lumbering instance to the contrary, I will say, hardly ever; they have usually a fine temperament and much tolerance towards the smaller errors of men (themselves inclusive). The Vicar was holding an inward dialogue in which he told himself that there was probably something more between Fred and Mary Garth than the regard of old playfellows, and replied with a question whether that bit of womanhood were not a great deal too choice for that crude young gentleman. The rejoinder to this was the first shrug. Then he laughed at himself for being likely to have felt jealous, as if he had been a man able to marry, which, added he, it is as clear as any balance-sheet that I am not. Whereupon followed the second shrug.\n\nWhat could two men, so different from each other, see in this “brown patch,” as Mary called herself? It was certainly not her plainness that attracted them (and let all plain young ladies be warned against the dangerous encouragement given them by Society to confide in their want of beauty). A human being in this aged nation of ours is a very wonderful whole, the slow creation of long interchanging influences: and charm is a result of two such wholes, the one loving and the one loved.\n\nWhen Mr. and Mrs. Garth were sitting alone, Caleb said, “Susan, guess what I’m thinking of.”\n\n“The rotation of crops,” said Mrs. Garth, smiling at him, above her knitting, “or else the back-doors of the Tipton cottages.”\n\n“No,” said Caleb, gravely; “I am thinking that I could do a great turn for Fred Vincy. Christy’s gone, Alfred will be gone soon, and it will be five years before Jim is ready to take to business. I shall want help, and Fred might come in and learn the nature of things and act under me, and it might be the making of him into a useful man, if he gives up being a parson. What do you think?”\n\n“I think, there is hardly anything honest that his family would object to more,” said Mrs. Garth, decidedly.\n\n“What care I about their objecting?” said Caleb, with a sturdiness which he was apt to show when he had an opinion. “The lad is of age and must get his bread. He has sense enough and quickness enough; he likes being on the land, and it’s my belief that he could learn business well if he gave his mind to it.”\n\n“But would he? His father and mother wanted him to be a fine gentleman, and I think he has the same sort of feeling himself. They all think us beneath them. And if the proposal came from you, I am sure Mrs. Vincy would say that we wanted Fred for Mary.”\n\n“Life is a poor tale, if it is to be settled by nonsense of that sort,” said Caleb, with disgust.\n\n“Yes, but there is a certain pride which is proper, Caleb.”\n\n“I call it improper pride to let fools’ notions hinder you from doing a good action. There’s no sort of work,” said Caleb, with fervor, putting out his hand and moving it up and down to mark his emphasis, “that could ever be done well, if you minded what fools say. You must have it inside you that your plan is right, and that plan you must follow.”\n\n“I will not oppose any plan you have set your mind on, Caleb,” said Mrs. Garth, who was a firm woman, but knew that there were some points on which her mild husband was yet firmer. “Still, it seems to be fixed that Fred is to go back to college: will it not be better to wait and see what he will choose to do after that? It is not easy to keep people against their will. And you are not yet quite sure enough of your own position, or what you will want.”\n\n“Well, it may be better to wait a bit. But as to my getting plenty of work for two, I’m pretty sure of that. I’ve always had my hands full with scattered things, and there’s always something fresh turning up. Why, only yesterday—bless me, I don’t think I told you!—it was rather odd that two men should have been at me on different sides to do the same bit of valuing. And who do you think they were?” said Caleb, taking a pinch of snuff and holding it up between his fingers, as if it were a part of his exposition. He was fond of a pinch when it occurred to him, but he usually forgot that this indulgence was at his command.\n\nHis wife held down her knitting and looked attentive.\n\n“Why, that Rigg, or Rigg Featherstone, was one. But Bulstrode was before him, so I’m going to do it for Bulstrode. Whether it’s mortgage or purchase they’re going for, I can’t tell yet.”\n\n“Can that man be going to sell the land just left him—which he has taken the name for?” said Mrs. Garth.\n\n“Deuce knows,” said Caleb, who never referred the knowledge of discreditable doings to any higher power than the deuce. “But Bulstrode has long been wanting to get a handsome bit of land under his fingers—that I know. And it’s a difficult matter to get, in this part of the country.”\n\nCaleb scattered his snuff carefully instead of taking it, and then added, “The ins and outs of things are curious. Here is the land they’ve been all along expecting for Fred, which it seems the old man never meant to leave him a foot of, but left it to this side-slip of a son that he kept in the dark, and thought of his sticking there and vexing everybody as well as he could have vexed ’em himself if he could have kept alive. I say, it would be curious if it got into Bulstrode’s hands after all. The old man hated him, and never would bank with him.”\n\n“What reason could the miserable creature have for hating a man whom he had nothing to do with?” said Mrs. Garth.\n\n“Pooh! where’s the use of asking for such fellows’ reasons? The soul of man,” said Caleb, with the deep tone and grave shake of the head which always came when he used this phrase—“The soul of man, when it gets fairly rotten, will bear you all sorts of poisonous toad-stools, and no eye can see whence came the seed thereof.”\n\nIt was one of Caleb’s quaintnesses, that in his difficulty of finding speech for his thought, he caught, as it were, snatches of diction which he associated with various points of view or states of mind; and whenever he had a feeling of awe, he was haunted by a sense of Biblical phraseology, though he could hardly have given a strict quotation.\n\n# CHAPTER XLI.\n\nBy swaggering could I never thrive, For the rain it raineth every day. —Twelfth Night.\n\nThe transactions referred to by Caleb Garth as having gone forward between Mr. Bulstrode and Mr. Joshua Rigg Featherstone concerning the land attached to Stone Court, had occasioned the interchange of a letter or two between these personages.\n\nWho shall tell what may be the effect of writing? If it happens to have been cut in stone, though it lie face down-most for ages on a forsaken beach, or “rest quietly under the drums and tramplings of many conquests,” it may end by letting us into the secret of usurpations and other scandals gossiped about long empires ago:—this world being apparently a huge whispering-gallery. Such conditions are often minutely represented in our petty lifetimes. As the stone which has been kicked by generations of clowns may come by curious little links of effect under the eyes of a scholar, through whose labors it may at last fix the date of invasions and unlock religions, so a bit of ink and paper which has long been an innocent wrapping or stop-gap may at last be laid open under the one pair of eyes which have knowledge enough to turn it into the opening of a catastrophe. To Uriel watching the progress of planetary history from the sun, the one result would be just as much of a coincidence as the other.\n\nHaving made this rather lofty comparison I am less uneasy in calling attention to the existence of low people by whose interference, however little we may like it, the course of the world is very much determined. It would be well, certainly, if we could help to reduce their number, and something might perhaps be done by not lightly giving occasion to their existence. Socially speaking, Joshua Rigg would have been generally pronounced a superfluity. But those who like Peter Featherstone never had a copy of themselves demanded, are the very last to wait for such a request either in prose or verse. The copy in this case bore more of outside resemblance to the mother, in whose sex frog-features, accompanied with fresh-colored cheeks and a well-rounded figure, are compatible with much charm for a certain order of admirers. The result is sometimes a frog-faced male, desirable, surely, to no order of intelligent beings. Especially when he is suddenly brought into evidence to frustrate other people’s expectations—the very lowest aspect in which a social superfluity can present himself.\n\nBut Mr. Rigg Featherstone’s low characteristics were all of the sober, water-drinking kind. From the earliest to the latest hour of the day he was always as sleek, neat, and cool as the frog he resembled, and old Peter had secretly chuckled over an offshoot almost more calculating, and far more imperturbable, than himself. I will add that his finger-nails were scrupulously attended to, and that he meant to marry a well-educated young lady (as yet unspecified) whose person was good, and whose connections, in a solid middle-class way, were undeniable. Thus his nails and modesty were comparable to those of most gentlemen; though his ambition had been educated only by the opportunities of a clerk and accountant in the smaller commercial houses of a seaport. He thought the rural Featherstones very simple absurd people, and they in their turn regarded his “bringing up” in a seaport town as an exaggeration of the monstrosity that their brother Peter, and still more Peter’s property, should have had such belongings.\n\nThe garden and gravel approach, as seen from the two windows of the wainscoted parlor at Stone Court, were never in better trim than now, when Mr. Rigg Featherstone stood, with his hands behind him, looking out on these grounds as their master. But it seemed doubtful whether he looked out for the sake of contemplation or of turning his back to a person who stood in the middle of the room, with his legs considerably apart and his hands in his trouser-pockets: a person in all respects a contrast to the sleek and cool Rigg. He was a man obviously on the way towards sixty, very florid and hairy, with much gray in his bushy whiskers and thick curly hair, a stoutish body which showed to disadvantage the somewhat worn joinings of his clothes, and the air of a swaggerer, who would aim at being noticeable even at a show of fireworks, regarding his own remarks on any other person’s performance as likely to be more interesting than the performance itself.\n\nHis name was John Raffles, and he sometimes wrote jocosely W.A.G. after his signature, observing when he did so, that he was once taught by Leonard Lamb of Finsbury who wrote B.A. after his name, and that he, Raffles, originated the witticism of calling that celebrated principal Ba-Lamb. Such were the appearance and mental flavor of Mr. Raffles, both of which seemed to have a stale odor of travellers’ rooms in the commercial hotels of that period.\n\n“Come, now, Josh,” he was saying, in a full rumbling tone, “look at it in this light: here is your poor mother going into the vale of years, and you could afford something handsome now to make her comfortable.”\n\n“Not while you live. Nothing would make her comfortable while you live,” returned Rigg, in his cool high voice. “What I give her, you’ll take.”\n\n“You bear me a grudge, Josh, that I know. But come, now—as between man and man—without humbug—a little capital might enable me to make a first-rate thing of the shop. The tobacco trade is growing. I should cut my own nose off in not doing the best I could at it. I should stick to it like a flea to a fleece for my own sake. I should always be on the spot. And nothing would make your poor mother so happy. I’ve pretty well done with my wild oats—turned fifty-five. I want to settle down in my chimney-corner. And if I once buckled to the tobacco trade, I could bring an amount of brains and experience to bear on it that would not be found elsewhere in a hurry. I don’t want to be bothering you one time after another, but to get things once for all into the right channel. Consider that, Josh—as between man and man—and with your poor mother to be made easy for her life. I was always fond of the old woman, by Jove!”\n\n“Have you done?” said Mr. Rigg, quietly, without looking away from the window.\n\n“Yes, I’ve done,” said Raffles, taking hold of his hat which stood before him on the table, and giving it a sort of oratorical push.\n\n“Then just listen to me. The more you say anything, the less I shall believe it. The more you want me to do a thing, the more reason I shall have for never doing it. Do you think I mean to forget your kicking me when I was a lad, and eating all the best victual away from me and my mother? Do you think I forget your always coming home to sell and pocket everything, and going off again leaving us in the lurch? I should be glad to see you whipped at the cart-tail. My mother was a fool to you: she’d no right to give me a father-in-law, and she’s been punished for it. She shall have her weekly allowance paid and no more: and that shall be stopped if you dare to come on to these premises again, or to come into this country after me again. The next time you show yourself inside the gates here, you shall be driven off with the dogs and the wagoner’s whip.”\n\nAs Rigg pronounced the last words he turned round and looked at Raffles with his prominent frozen eyes. The contrast was as striking as it could have been eighteen years before, when Rigg was a most unengaging kickable boy, and Raffles was the rather thick-set Adonis of bar-rooms and back-parlors. But the advantage now was on the side of Rigg, and auditors of this conversation might probably have expected that Raffles would retire with the air of a defeated dog. Not at all. He made a grimace which was habitual with him whenever he was “out” in a game; then subsided into a laugh, and drew a brandy-flask from his pocket.\n\n“Come, Josh,” he said, in a cajoling tone, “give us a spoonful of brandy, and a sovereign to pay the way back, and I’ll go. Honor bright! I’ll go like a bullet, by Jove!”\n\n“Mind,” said Rigg, drawing out a bunch of keys, “if I ever see you again, I shan’t speak to you. I don’t own you any more than if I saw a crow; and if you want to own me you’ll get nothing by it but a character for being what you are—a spiteful, brassy, bullying rogue.”\n\n“That’s a pity, now, Josh,” said Raffles, affecting to scratch his head and wrinkle his brows upward as if he were nonplussed. “I’m very fond of you; by Jove, I am! There’s nothing I like better than plaguing you—you’re so like your mother, and I must do without it. But the brandy and the sovereign’s a bargain.”\n\nHe jerked forward the flask and Rigg went to a fine old oaken bureau with his keys. But Raffles had reminded himself by his movement with the flask that it had become dangerously loose from its leather covering, and catching sight of a folded paper which had fallen within the fender, he took it up and shoved it under the leather so as to make the glass firm.\n\nBy that time Rigg came forward with a brandy-bottle, filled the flask, and handed Raffles a sovereign, neither looking at him nor speaking to him. After locking up the bureau again, he walked to the window and gazed out as impassibly as he had done at the beginning of the interview, while Raffles took a small allowance from the flask, screwed it up, and deposited it in his side-pocket, with provoking slowness, making a grimace at his stepson’s back.\n\n“Farewell, Josh—and if forever!” said Raffles, turning back his head as he opened the door.\n\nRigg saw him leave the grounds and enter the lane. The gray day had turned to a light drizzling rain, which freshened the hedgerows and the grassy borders of the by-roads, and hastened the laborers who were loading the last shocks of corn. Raffles, walking with the uneasy gait of a town loiterer obliged to do a bit of country journeying on foot, looked as incongruous amid this moist rural quiet and industry as if he had been a baboon escaped from a menagerie. But there were none to stare at him except the long-weaned calves, and none to show dislike of his appearance except the little water-rats which rustled away at his approach.\n\nHe was fortunate enough when he got on to the highroad to be overtaken by the stage-coach, which carried him to Brassing; and there he took the new-made railway, observing to his fellow-passengers that he considered it pretty well seasoned now it had done for Huskisson. Mr. Raffles on most occasions kept up the sense of having been educated at an academy, and being able, if he chose, to pass well everywhere; indeed, there was not one of his fellow-men whom he did not feel himself in a position to ridicule and torment, confident of the entertainment which he thus gave to all the rest of the company.\n\nHe played this part now with as much spirit as if his journey had been entirely successful, resorting at frequent intervals to his flask. The paper with which he had wedged it was a letter signed Nicholas Bulstrode, but Raffles was not likely to disturb it from its present useful position.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII.\n\nHow much, methinks, I could despise this man Were I not bound in charity against it! —SHAKESPEARE: Henry VIII.\n\nOne of the professional calls made by Lydgate soon after his return from his wedding-journey was to Lowick Manor, in consequence of a letter which had requested him to fix a time for his visit.\n\nMr. Casaubon had never put any question concerning the nature of his illness to Lydgate, nor had he even to Dorothea betrayed any anxiety as to how far it might be likely to cut short his labors or his life. On this point, as on all others, he shrank from pity; and if the suspicion of being pitied for anything in his lot surmised or known in spite of himself was embittering, the idea of calling forth a show of compassion by frankly admitting an alarm or a sorrow was necessarily intolerable to him. Every proud mind knows something of this experience, and perhaps it is only to be overcome by a sense of fellowship deep enough to make all efforts at isolation seem mean and petty instead of exalting.\n\nBut Mr. Casaubon was now brooding over something through which the question of his health and life haunted his silence with a more harassing importunity even than through the autumnal unripeness of his authorship. It is true that this last might be called his central ambition; but there are some kinds of authorship in which by far the largest result is the uneasy susceptibility accumulated in the consciousness of the author—one knows of the river by a few streaks amid a long-gathered deposit of uncomfortable mud. That was the way with Mr. Casaubon’s hard intellectual labors. Their most characteristic result was not the “Key to all Mythologies,” but a morbid consciousness that others did not give him the place which he had not demonstrably merited—a perpetual suspicious conjecture that the views entertained of him were not to his advantage—a melancholy absence of passion in his efforts at achievement, and a passionate resistance to the confession that he had achieved nothing.\n\nThus his intellectual ambition which seemed to others to have absorbed and dried him, was really no security against wounds, least of all against those which came from Dorothea. And he had begun now to frame possibilities for the future which were somehow more embittering to him than anything his mind had dwelt on before.\n\nAgainst certain facts he was helpless: against Will Ladislaw’s existence, his defiant stay in the neighborhood of Lowick, and his flippant state of mind with regard to the possessors of authentic, well-stamped erudition: against Dorothea’s nature, always taking on some new shape of ardent activity, and even in submission and silence covering fervid reasons which it was an irritation to think of: against certain notions and likings which had taken possession of her mind in relation to subjects that he could not possibly discuss with her. There was no denying that Dorothea was as virtuous and lovely a young lady as he could have obtained for a wife; but a young lady turned out to be something more troublesome than he had conceived. She nursed him, she read to him, she anticipated his wants, and was solicitous about his feelings; but there had entered into the husband’s mind the certainty that she judged him, and that her wifely devotedness was like a penitential expiation of unbelieving thoughts—was accompanied with a power of comparison by which himself and his doings were seen too luminously as a part of things in general. His discontent passed vapor-like through all her gentle loving manifestations, and clung to that inappreciative world which she had only brought nearer to him.\n\nPoor Mr. Casaubon! This suffering was the harder to bear because it seemed like a betrayal: the young creature who had worshipped him with perfect trust had quickly turned into the critical wife; and early instances of criticism and resentment had made an impression which no tenderness and submission afterwards could remove. To his suspicious interpretation Dorothea’s silence now was a suppressed rebellion; a remark from her which he had not in any way anticipated was an assertion of conscious superiority; her gentle answers had an irritating cautiousness in them; and when she acquiesced it was a self-approved effort of forbearance. The tenacity with which he strove to hide this inward drama made it the more vivid for him; as we hear with the more keenness what we wish others not to hear.\n\nInstead of wondering at this result of misery in Mr. Casaubon, I think it quite ordinary. Will not a tiny speck very close to our vision blot out the glory of the world, and leave only a margin by which we see the blot? I know no speck so troublesome as self. And who, if Mr. Casaubon had chosen to expound his discontents—his suspicions that he was not any longer adored without criticism—could have denied that they were founded on good reasons? On the contrary, there was a strong reason to be added, which he had not himself taken explicitly into account—namely, that he was not unmixedly adorable. He suspected this, however, as he suspected other things, without confessing it, and like the rest of us, felt how soothing it would have been to have a companion who would never find it out.\n\nThis sore susceptibility in relation to Dorothea was thoroughly prepared before Will Ladislaw had returned to Lowick, and what had occurred since then had brought Mr. Casaubon’s power of suspicious construction into exasperated activity. To all the facts which he knew, he added imaginary facts both present and future which became more real to him than those because they called up a stronger dislike, a more predominating bitterness. Suspicion and jealousy of Will Ladislaw’s intentions, suspicion and jealousy of Dorothea’s impressions, were constantly at their weaving work. It would be quite unjust to him to suppose that he could have entered into any coarse misinterpretation of Dorothea: his own habits of mind and conduct, quite as much as the open elevation of her nature, saved him from any such mistake. What he was jealous of was her opinion, the sway that might be given to her ardent mind in its judgments, and the future possibilities to which these might lead her. As to Will, though until his last defiant letter he had nothing definite which he would choose formally to allege against him, he felt himself warranted in believing that he was capable of any design which could fascinate a rebellious temper and an undisciplined impulsiveness. He was quite sure that Dorothea was the cause of Will’s return from Rome, and his determination to settle in the neighborhood; and he was penetrating enough to imagine that Dorothea had innocently encouraged this course. It was as clear as possible that she was ready to be attached to Will and to be pliant to his suggestions: they had never had a tête-à-tête without her bringing away from it some new troublesome impression, and the last interview that Mr. Casaubon was aware of (Dorothea, on returning from Freshitt Hall, had for the first time been silent about having seen Will) had led to a scene which roused an angrier feeling against them both than he had ever known before. Dorothea’s outpouring of her notions about money, in the darkness of the night, had done nothing but bring a mixture of more odious foreboding into her husband’s mind.\n\nAnd there was the shock lately given to his health always sadly present with him. He was certainly much revived; he had recovered all his usual power of work: the illness might have been mere fatigue, and there might still be twenty years of achievement before him, which would justify the thirty years of preparation. That prospect was made the sweeter by a flavor of vengeance against the hasty sneers of Carp & Company; for even when Mr. Casaubon was carrying his taper among the tombs of the past, those modern figures came athwart the dim light, and interrupted his diligent exploration. To convince Carp of his mistake, so that he would have to eat his own words with a good deal of indigestion, would be an agreeable accident of triumphant authorship, which the prospect of living to future ages on earth and to all eternity in heaven could not exclude from contemplation. Since, thus, the prevision of his own unending bliss could not nullify the bitter savors of irritated jealousy and vindictiveness, it is the less surprising that the probability of a transient earthly bliss for other persons, when he himself should have entered into glory, had not a potently sweetening effect. If the truth should be that some undermining disease was at work within him, there might be large opportunity for some people to be the happier when he was gone; and if one of those people should be Will Ladislaw, Mr. Casaubon objected so strongly that it seemed as if the annoyance would make part of his disembodied existence.\n\nThis is a very bare and therefore a very incomplete way of putting the case. The human soul moves in many channels, and Mr. Casaubon, we know, had a sense of rectitude and an honorable pride in satisfying the requirements of honor, which compelled him to find other reasons for his conduct than those of jealousy and vindictiveness. The way in which Mr. Casaubon put the case was this:—“In marrying Dorothea Brooke I had to care for her well-being in case of my death. But well-being is not to be secured by ample, independent possession of property; on the contrary, occasions might arise in which such possession might expose her to the more danger. She is ready prey to any man who knows how to play adroitly either on her affectionate ardor or her Quixotic enthusiasm; and a man stands by with that very intention in his mind—a man with no other principle than transient caprice, and who has a personal animosity towards me—I am sure of it—an animosity which is fed by the consciousness of his ingratitude, and which he has constantly vented in ridicule of which I am as well assured as if I had heard it. Even if I live I shall not be without uneasiness as to what he may attempt through indirect influence. This man has gained Dorothea’s ear: he has fascinated her attention; he has evidently tried to impress her mind with the notion that he has claims beyond anything I have done for him. If I die—and he is waiting here on the watch for that—he will persuade her to marry him. That would be calamity for her and success for him. She would not think it calamity: he would make her believe anything; she has a tendency to immoderate attachment which she inwardly reproaches me for not responding to, and already her mind is occupied with his fortunes. He thinks of an easy conquest and of entering into my nest. That I will hinder! Such a marriage would be fatal to Dorothea. Has he ever persisted in anything except from contradiction? In knowledge he has always tried to be showy at small cost. In religion he could be, as long as it suited him, the facile echo of Dorothea’s vagaries. When was sciolism ever dissociated from laxity? I utterly distrust his morals, and it is my duty to hinder to the utmost the fulfilment of his designs.”\n\nThe arrangements made by Mr. Casaubon on his marriage left strong measures open to him, but in ruminating on them his mind inevitably dwelt so much on the probabilities of his own life that the longing to get the nearest possible calculation had at last overcome his proud reticence, and had determined him to ask Lydgate’s opinion as to the nature of his illness.\n\nHe had mentioned to Dorothea that Lydgate was coming by appointment at half-past three, and in answer to her anxious question, whether he had felt ill, replied,—“No, I merely wish to have his opinion concerning some habitual symptoms. You need not see him, my dear. I shall give orders that he may be sent to me in the Yew-tree Walk, where I shall be taking my usual exercise.”\n\nWhen Lydgate entered the Yew-tree Walk he saw Mr. Casaubon slowly receding with his hands behind him according to his habit, and his head bent forward. It was a lovely afternoon; the leaves from the lofty limes were falling silently across the sombre evergreens, while the lights and shadows slept side by side: there was no sound but the cawing of the rooks, which to the accustomed ear is a lullaby, or that last solemn lullaby, a dirge. Lydgate, conscious of an energetic frame in its prime, felt some compassion when the figure which he was likely soon to overtake turned round, and in advancing towards him showed more markedly than ever the signs of premature age—the student’s bent shoulders, the emaciated limbs, and the melancholy lines of the mouth. “Poor fellow,” he thought, “some men with his years are like lions; one can tell nothing of their age except that they are full grown.”\n\n“Mr. Lydgate,” said Mr. Casaubon, with his invariably polite air, “I am exceedingly obliged to you for your punctuality. We will, if you please, carry on our conversation in walking to and fro.”\n\n“I hope your wish to see me is not due to the return of unpleasant symptoms,” said Lydgate, filling up a pause.\n\n“Not immediately—no. In order to account for that wish I must mention—what it were otherwise needless to refer to—that my life, on all collateral accounts insignificant, derives a possible importance from the incompleteness of labors which have extended through all its best years. In short, I have long had on hand a work which I would fain leave behind me in such a state, at least, that it might be committed to the press by—others. Were I assured that this is the utmost I can reasonably expect, that assurance would be a useful circumscription of my attempts, and a guide in both the positive and negative determination of my course.”\n\nHere Mr. Casaubon paused, removed one hand from his back and thrust it between the buttons of his single-breasted coat. To a mind largely instructed in the human destiny hardly anything could be more interesting than the inward conflict implied in his formal measured address, delivered with the usual sing-song and motion of the head. Nay, are there many situations more sublimely tragic than the struggle of the soul with the demand to renounce a work which has been all the significance of its life—a significance which is to vanish as the waters which come and go where no man has need of them? But there was nothing to strike others as sublime about Mr. Casaubon, and Lydgate, who had some contempt at hand for futile scholarship, felt a little amusement mingling with his pity. He was at present too ill acquainted with disaster to enter into the pathos of a lot where everything is below the level of tragedy except the passionate egoism of the sufferer.\n\n“You refer to the possible hindrances from want of health?” he said, wishing to help forward Mr. Casaubon’s purpose, which seemed to be clogged by some hesitation.\n\n“I do. You have not implied to me that the symptoms which—I am bound to testify—you watched with scrupulous care, were those of a fatal disease. But were it so, Mr. Lydgate, I should desire to know the truth without reservation, and I appeal to you for an exact statement of your conclusions: I request it as a friendly service. If you can tell me that my life is not threatened by anything else than ordinary casualties, I shall rejoice, on grounds which I have already indicated. If not, knowledge of the truth is even more important to me.”\n\n“Then I can no longer hesitate as to my course,” said Lydgate; “but the first thing I must impress on you is that my conclusions are doubly uncertain—uncertain not only because of my fallibility, but because diseases of the heart are eminently difficult to found predictions on. In any case, one can hardly increase appreciably the tremendous uncertainty of life.”\n\nMr. Casaubon winced perceptibly, but bowed.\n\n“I believe that you are suffering from what is called fatty degeneration of the heart, a disease which was first divined and explored by Laennec, the man who gave us the stethoscope, not so very many years ago. A good deal of experience—a more lengthened observation—is wanting on the subject. But after what you have said, it is my duty to tell you that death from this disease is often sudden. At the same time, no such result can be predicted. Your condition may be consistent with a tolerably comfortable life for another fifteen years, or even more. I could add no information to this beyond anatomical or medical details, which would leave expectation at precisely the same point.” Lydgate’s instinct was fine enough to tell him that plain speech, quite free from ostentatious caution, would be felt by Mr. Casaubon as a tribute of respect.\n\n“I thank you, Mr. Lydgate,” said Mr. Casaubon, after a moment’s pause. “One thing more I have still to ask: did you communicate what you have now told me to Mrs. Casaubon?”\n\n“Partly—I mean, as to the possible issues.” Lydgate was going to explain why he had told Dorothea, but Mr. Casaubon, with an unmistakable desire to end the conversation, waved his hand slightly, and said again, “I thank you,” proceeding to remark on the rare beauty of the day.\n\nLydgate, certain that his patient wished to be alone, soon left him; and the black figure with hands behind and head bent forward continued to pace the walk where the dark yew-trees gave him a mute companionship in melancholy, and the little shadows of bird or leaf that fleeted across the isles of sunlight, stole along in silence as in the presence of a sorrow. Here was a man who now for the first time found himself looking into the eyes of death—who was passing through one of those rare moments of experience when we feel the truth of a commonplace, which is as different from what we call knowing it, as the vision of waters upon the earth is different from the delirious vision of the water which cannot be had to cool the burning tongue. When the commonplace “We must all die” transforms itself suddenly into the acute consciousness “I must die—and soon,” then death grapples us, and his fingers are cruel; afterwards, he may come to fold us in his arms as our mother did, and our last moment of dim earthly discerning may be like the first. To Mr. Casaubon now, it was as if he suddenly found himself on the dark river-brink and heard the plash of the oncoming oar, not discerning the forms, but expecting the summons. In such an hour the mind does not change its lifelong bias, but carries it onward in imagination to the other side of death, gazing backward—perhaps with the divine calm of beneficence, perhaps with the petty anxieties of self-assertion. What was Mr. Casaubon’s bias his acts will give us a clew to. He held himself to be, with some private scholarly reservations, a believing Christian, as to estimates of the present and hopes of the future. But what we strive to gratify, though we may call it a distant hope, is an immediate desire: the future estate for which men drudge up city alleys exists already in their imagination and love. And Mr. Casaubon’s immediate desire was not for divine communion and light divested of earthly conditions; his passionate longings, poor man, clung low and mist-like in very shady places.\n\nDorothea had been aware when Lydgate had ridden away, and she had stepped into the garden, with the impulse to go at once to her husband. But she hesitated, fearing to offend him by obtruding herself; for her ardor, continually repulsed, served, with her intense memory, to heighten her dread, as thwarted energy subsides into a shudder; and she wandered slowly round the nearer clumps of trees until she saw him advancing. Then she went towards him, and might have represented a heaven-sent angel coming with a promise that the short hours remaining should yet be filled with that faithful love which clings the closer to a comprehended grief. His glance in reply to hers was so chill that she felt her timidity increased; yet she turned and passed her hand through his arm.\n\nMr. Casaubon kept his hands behind him and allowed her pliant arm to cling with difficulty against his rigid arm.\n\nThere was something horrible to Dorothea in the sensation which this unresponsive hardness inflicted on her. That is a strong word, but not too strong: it is in these acts called trivialities that the seeds of joy are forever wasted, until men and women look round with haggard faces at the devastation their own waste has made, and say, the earth bears no harvest of sweetness—calling their denial knowledge. You may ask why, in the name of manliness, Mr. Casaubon should have behaved in that way. Consider that his was a mind which shrank from pity: have you ever watched in such a mind the effect of a suspicion that what is pressing it as a grief may be really a source of contentment, either actual or future, to the being who already offends by pitying? Besides, he knew little of Dorothea’s sensations, and had not reflected that on such an occasion as the present they were comparable in strength to his own sensibilities about Carp’s criticisms.\n\nDorothea did not withdraw her arm, but she could not venture to speak. Mr. Casaubon did not say, “I wish to be alone,” but he directed his steps in silence towards the house, and as they entered by the glass door on this eastern side, Dorothea withdrew her arm and lingered on the matting, that she might leave her husband quite free. He entered the library and shut himself in, alone with his sorrow.\n\nShe went up to her boudoir. The open bow-window let in the serene glory of the afternoon lying in the avenue, where the lime-trees cast long shadows. But Dorothea knew nothing of the scene. She threw herself on a chair, not heeding that she was in the dazzling sun-rays: if there were discomfort in that, how could she tell that it was not part of her inward misery?\n\nShe was in the reaction of a rebellious anger stronger than any she had felt since her marriage. Instead of tears there came words:—\n\n“What have I done—what am I—that he should treat me so? He never knows what is in my mind—he never cares. What is the use of anything I do? He wishes he had never married me.”\n\nShe began to hear herself, and was checked into stillness. Like one who has lost his way and is weary, she sat and saw as in one glance all the paths of her young hope which she should never find again. And just as clearly in the miserable light she saw her own and her husband’s solitude—how they walked apart so that she was obliged to survey him. If he had drawn her towards him, she would never have surveyed him—never have said, “Is he worth living for?” but would have felt him simply a part of her own life. Now she said bitterly, “It is his fault, not mine.” In the jar of her whole being, Pity was overthrown. Was it her fault that she had believed in him—had believed in his worthiness?—And what, exactly, was he?— She was able enough to estimate him—she who waited on his glances with trembling, and shut her best soul in prison, paying it only hidden visits, that she might be petty enough to please him. In such a crisis as this, some women begin to hate.\n\nThe sun was low when Dorothea was thinking that she would not go down again, but would send a message to her husband saying that she was not well and preferred remaining up-stairs. She had never deliberately allowed her resentment to govern her in this way before, but she believed now that she could not see him again without telling him the truth about her feeling, and she must wait till she could do it without interruption. He might wonder and be hurt at her message. It was good that he should wonder and be hurt. Her anger said, as anger is apt to say, that God was with her—that all heaven, though it were crowded with spirits watching them, must be on her side. She had determined to ring her bell, when there came a rap at the door.\n\nMr. Casaubon had sent to say that he would have his dinner in the library. He wished to be quite alone this evening, being much occupied.\n\n“I shall not dine, then, Tantripp.”\n\n“Oh, madam, let me bring you a little something?”\n\n“No; I am not well. Get everything ready in my dressing room, but pray do not disturb me again.”\n\nDorothea sat almost motionless in her meditative struggle, while the evening slowly deepened into night. But the struggle changed continually, as that of a man who begins with a movement towards striking and ends with conquering his desire to strike. The energy that would animate a crime is not more than is wanted to inspire a resolved submission, when the noble habit of the soul reasserts itself. That thought with which Dorothea had gone out to meet her husband—her conviction that he had been asking about the possible arrest of all his work, and that the answer must have wrung his heart, could not be long without rising beside the image of him, like a shadowy monitor looking at her anger with sad remonstrance. It cost her a litany of pictured sorrows and of silent cries that she might be the mercy for those sorrows—but the resolved submission did come; and when the house was still, and she knew that it was near the time when Mr. Casaubon habitually went to rest, she opened her door gently and stood outside in the darkness waiting for his coming up-stairs with a light in his hand. If he did not come soon she thought that she would go down and even risk incurring another pang. She would never again expect anything else. But she did hear the library door open, and slowly the light advanced up the staircase without noise from the footsteps on the carpet. When her husband stood opposite to her, she saw that his face was more haggard. He started slightly on seeing her, and she looked up at him beseechingly, without speaking.\n\n“Dorothea!” he said, with a gentle surprise in his tone. “Were you waiting for me?”\n\n“Yes, I did not like to disturb you.”\n\n“Come, my dear, come. You are young, and need not to extend your life by watching.”\n\nWhen the kind quiet melancholy of that speech fell on Dorothea’s ears, she felt something like the thankfulness that might well up in us if we had narrowly escaped hurting a lamed creature. She put her hand into her husband’s, and they went along the broad corridor together.\n\n# BOOK V. THE DEAD HAND. CHAPTER XLIII.\n\n“This figure hath high price: ’t was wrought with love Ages ago in finest ivory; Nought modish in it, pure and noble lines Of generous womanhood that fits all time That too is costly ware; majolica Of deft design, to please a lordly eye: The smile, you see, is perfect—wonderful As mere Faience! a table ornament To suit the richest mounting.”\n\nDorothea seldom left home without her husband, but she did occasionally drive into Middlemarch alone, on little errands of shopping or charity such as occur to every lady of any wealth when she lives within three miles of a town. Two days after that scene in the Yew-tree Walk, she determined to use such an opportunity in order if possible to see Lydgate, and learn from him whether her husband had really felt any depressing change of symptoms which he was concealing from her, and whether he had insisted on knowing the utmost about himself. She felt almost guilty in asking for knowledge about him from another, but the dread of being without it—the dread of that ignorance which would make her unjust or hard—overcame every scruple. That there had been some crisis in her husband’s mind she was certain: he had the very next day begun a new method of arranging his notes, and had associated her quite newly in carrying out his plan. Poor Dorothea needed to lay up stores of patience.\n\nIt was about four o’clock when she drove to Lydgate’s house in Lowick Gate, wishing, in her immediate doubt of finding him at home, that she had written beforehand. And he was not at home.\n\n“Is Mrs. Lydgate at home?” said Dorothea, who had never, that she knew of, seen Rosamond, but now remembered the fact of the marriage. Yes, Mrs. Lydgate was at home.\n\n“I will go in and speak to her, if she will allow me. Will you ask her if she can see me—see Mrs. Casaubon, for a few minutes?”\n\nWhen the servant had gone to deliver that message, Dorothea could hear sounds of music through an open window—a few notes from a man’s voice and then a piano bursting into roulades. But the roulades broke off suddenly, and then the servant came back saying that Mrs. Lydgate would be happy to see Mrs. Casaubon.\n\nWhen the drawing-room door opened and Dorothea entered, there was a sort of contrast not infrequent in country life when the habits of the different ranks were less blent than now. Let those who know, tell us exactly what stuff it was that Dorothea wore in those days of mild autumn—that thin white woollen stuff soft to the touch and soft to the eye. It always seemed to have been lately washed, and to smell of the sweet hedges—was always in the shape of a pelisse with sleeves hanging all out of the fashion. Yet if she had entered before a still audience as Imogene or Cato’s daughter, the dress might have seemed right enough: the grace and dignity were in her limbs and neck; and about her simply parted hair and candid eyes the large round poke which was then in the fate of women, seemed no more odd as a head-dress than the gold trencher we call a halo. By the present audience of two persons, no dramatic heroine could have been expected with more interest than Mrs. Casaubon. To Rosamond she was one of those county divinities not mixing with Middlemarch mortality, whose slightest marks of manner or appearance were worthy of her study; moreover, Rosamond was not without satisfaction that Mrs. Casaubon should have an opportunity of studying her. What is the use of being exquisite if you are not seen by the best judges? and since Rosamond had received the highest compliments at Sir Godwin Lydgate’s, she felt quite confident of the impression she must make on people of good birth. Dorothea put out her hand with her usual simple kindness, and looked admiringly at Lydgate’s lovely bride—aware that there was a gentleman standing at a distance, but seeing him merely as a coated figure at a wide angle. The gentleman was too much occupied with the presence of the one woman to reflect on the contrast between the two—a contrast that would certainly have been striking to a calm observer. They were both tall, and their eyes were on a level; but imagine Rosamond’s infantine blondness and wondrous crown of hair-plaits, with her pale-blue dress of a fit and fashion so perfect that no dressmaker could look at it without emotion, a large embroidered collar which it was to be hoped all beholders would know the price of, her small hands duly set off with rings, and that controlled self-consciousness of manner which is the expensive substitute for simplicity.\n\n“Thank you very much for allowing me to interrupt you,” said Dorothea, immediately. “I am anxious to see Mr. Lydgate, if possible, before I go home, and I hoped that you might possibly tell me where I could find him, or even allow me to wait for him, if you expect him soon.”\n\n“He is at the New Hospital,” said Rosamond; “I am not sure how soon he will come home. But I can send for him.”\n\n“Will you let me go and fetch him?” said Will Ladislaw, coming forward. He had already taken up his hat before Dorothea entered. She colored with surprise, but put out her hand with a smile of unmistakable pleasure, saying—\n\n“I did not know it was you: I had no thought of seeing you here.”\n\n“May I go to the Hospital and tell Mr. Lydgate that you wish to see him?” said Will.\n\n“It would be quicker to send the carriage for him,” said Dorothea, “if you will be kind enough to give the message to the coachman.”\n\nWill was moving to the door when Dorothea, whose mind had flashed in an instant over many connected memories, turned quickly and said, “I will go myself, thank you. I wish to lose no time before getting home again. I will drive to the Hospital and see Mr. Lydgate there. Pray excuse me, Mrs. Lydgate. I am very much obliged to you.”\n\nHer mind was evidently arrested by some sudden thought, and she left the room hardly conscious of what was immediately around her—hardly conscious that Will opened the door for her and offered her his arm to lead her to the carriage. She took the arm but said nothing. Will was feeling rather vexed and miserable, and found nothing to say on his side. He handed her into the carriage in silence, they said good-by, and Dorothea drove away.\n\nIn the five minutes’ drive to the Hospital she had time for some reflections that were quite new to her. Her decision to go, and her preoccupation in leaving the room, had come from the sudden sense that there would be a sort of deception in her voluntarily allowing any further intercourse between herself and Will which she was unable to mention to her husband, and already her errand in seeking Lydgate was a matter of concealment. That was all that had been explicitly in her mind; but she had been urged also by a vague discomfort. Now that she was alone in her drive, she heard the notes of the man’s voice and the accompanying piano, which she had not noted much at the time, returning on her inward sense; and she found herself thinking with some wonder that Will Ladislaw was passing his time with Mrs. Lydgate in her husband’s absence. And then she could not help remembering that he had passed some time with her under like circumstances, so why should there be any unfitness in the fact? But Will was Mr. Casaubon’s relative, and one towards whom she was bound to show kindness. Still there had been signs which perhaps she ought to have understood as implying that Mr. Casaubon did not like his cousin’s visits during his own absence. “Perhaps I have been mistaken in many things,” said poor Dorothea to herself, while the tears came rolling and she had to dry them quickly. She felt confusedly unhappy, and the image of Will which had been so clear to her before was mysteriously spoiled. But the carriage stopped at the gate of the Hospital. She was soon walking round the grass plots with Lydgate, and her feelings recovered the strong bent which had made her seek for this interview.\n\nWill Ladislaw, meanwhile, was mortified, and knew the reason of it clearly enough. His chances of meeting Dorothea were rare; and here for the first time there had come a chance which had set him at a disadvantage. It was not only, as it had been hitherto, that she was not supremely occupied with him, but that she had seen him under circumstances in which he might appear not to be supremely occupied with her. He felt thrust to a new distance from her, amongst the circles of Middlemarchers who made no part of her life. But that was not his fault: of course, since he had taken his lodgings in the town, he had been making as many acquaintances as he could, his position requiring that he should know everybody and everything. Lydgate was really better worth knowing than any one else in the neighborhood, and he happened to have a wife who was musical and altogether worth calling upon. Here was the whole history of the situation in which Diana had descended too unexpectedly on her worshipper. It was mortifying. Will was conscious that he should not have been at Middlemarch but for Dorothea; and yet his position there was threatening to divide him from her with those barriers of habitual sentiment which are more fatal to the persistence of mutual interest than all the distance between Rome and Britain. Prejudices about rank and status were easy enough to defy in the form of a tyrannical letter from Mr. Casaubon; but prejudices, like odorous bodies, have a double existence both solid and subtle—solid as the pyramids, subtle as the twentieth echo of an echo, or as the memory of hyacinths which once scented the darkness. And Will was of a temperament to feel keenly the presence of subtleties: a man of clumsier perceptions would not have felt, as he did, that for the first time some sense of unfitness in perfect freedom with him had sprung up in Dorothea’s mind, and that their silence, as he conducted her to the carriage, had had a chill in it. Perhaps Casaubon, in his hatred and jealousy, had been insisting to Dorothea that Will had slid below her socially. Confound Casaubon!\n\nWill re-entered the drawing-room, took up his hat, and looking irritated as he advanced towards Mrs. Lydgate, who had seated herself at her work-table, said—\n\n“It is always fatal to have music or poetry interrupted. May I come another day and just finish about the rendering of ‘Lungi dal caro bene’?”\n\n“I shall be happy to be taught,” said Rosamond. “But I am sure you admit that the interruption was a very beautiful one. I quite envy your acquaintance with Mrs. Casaubon. Is she very clever? She looks as if she were.”\n\n“Really, I never thought about it,” said Will, sulkily.\n\n“That is just the answer Tertius gave me, when I first asked him if she were handsome. What is it that you gentlemen are thinking of when you are with Mrs. Casaubon?”\n\n“Herself,” said Will, not indisposed to provoke the charming Mrs. Lydgate. “When one sees a perfect woman, one never thinks of her attributes—one is conscious of her presence.”\n\n“I shall be jealous when Tertius goes to Lowick,” said Rosamond, dimpling, and speaking with aery lightness. “He will come back and think nothing of me.”\n\n“That does not seem to have been the effect on Lydgate hitherto. Mrs. Casaubon is too unlike other women for them to be compared with her.”\n\n“You are a devout worshipper, I perceive. You often see her, I suppose.”\n\n“No,” said Will, almost pettishly. “Worship is usually a matter of theory rather than of practice. But I am practising it to excess just at this moment—I must really tear myself away.”\n\n“Pray come again some evening: Mr. Lydgate will like to hear the music, and I cannot enjoy it so well without him.”\n\nWhen her husband was at home again, Rosamond said, standing in front of him and holding his coat-collar with both her hands, “Mr. Ladislaw was here singing with me when Mrs. Casaubon came in. He seemed vexed. Do you think he disliked her seeing him at our house? Surely your position is more than equal to his—whatever may be his relation to the Casaubons.”\n\n“No, no; it must be something else if he were really vexed. Ladislaw is a sort of gypsy; he thinks nothing of leather and prunella.”\n\n“Music apart, he is not always very agreeable. Do you like him?”\n\n“Yes: I think he is a good fellow: rather miscellaneous and bric-a-brac, but likable.”\n\n“Do you know, I think he adores Mrs. Casaubon.”\n\n“Poor devil!” said Lydgate, smiling and pinching his wife’s ears.\n\nRosamond felt herself beginning to know a great deal of the world, especially in discovering what when she was in her unmarried girlhood had been inconceivable to her except as a dim tragedy in by-gone costumes—that women, even after marriage, might make conquests and enslave men. At that time young ladies in the country, even when educated at Mrs. Lemon’s, read little French literature later than Racine, and public prints had not cast their present magnificent illumination over the scandals of life. Still, vanity, with a woman’s whole mind and day to work in, can construct abundantly on slight hints, especially on such a hint as the possibility of indefinite conquests. How delightful to make captives from the throne of marriage with a husband as crown-prince by your side—himself in fact a subject—while the captives look up forever hopeless, losing their rest probably, and if their appetite too, so much the better! But Rosamond’s romance turned at present chiefly on her crown-prince, and it was enough to enjoy his assured subjection. When he said, “Poor devil!” she asked, with playful curiosity—\n\n“Why so?”\n\n“Why, what can a man do when he takes to adoring one of you mermaids? He only neglects his work and runs up bills.”\n\n“I am sure you do not neglect your work. You are always at the Hospital, or seeing poor patients, or thinking about some doctor’s quarrel; and then at home you always want to pore over your microscope and phials. Confess you like those things better than me.”\n\n“Haven’t you ambition enough to wish that your husband should be something better than a Middlemarch doctor?” said Lydgate, letting his hands fall on to his wife’s shoulders, and looking at her with affectionate gravity. “I shall make you learn my favorite bit from an old poet—\n\n‘Why should our pride make such a stir to be And be forgot? What good is like to this, To do worthy the writing, and to write Worthy the reading and the worlds delight?’\n\nWhat I want, Rosy, is to do worthy the writing,—and to write out myself what I have done. A man must work, to do that, my pet.”\n\n“Of course, I wish you to make discoveries: no one could more wish you to attain a high position in some better place than Middlemarch. You cannot say that I have ever tried to hinder you from working. But we cannot live like hermits. You are not discontented with me, Tertius?”\n\n“No, dear, no. I am too entirely contented.”\n\n“But what did Mrs. Casaubon want to say to you?”\n\n“Merely to ask about her husband’s health. But I think she is going to be splendid to our New Hospital: I think she will give us two hundred a-year.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV.\n\nI would not creep along the coast but steer Out in mid-sea, by guidance of the stars.\n\nWhen Dorothea, walking round the laurel-planted plots of the New Hospital with Lydgate, had learned from him that there were no signs of change in Mr. Casaubon’s bodily condition beyond the mental sign of anxiety to know the truth about his illness, she was silent for a few moments, wondering whether she had said or done anything to rouse this new anxiety. Lydgate, not willing to let slip an opportunity of furthering a favorite purpose, ventured to say—\n\n“I don’t know whether your or Mr. Casaubon’s attention has been drawn to the needs of our New Hospital. Circumstances have made it seem rather egotistic in me to urge the subject; but that is not my fault: it is because there is a fight being made against it by the other medical men. I think you are generally interested in such things, for I remember that when I first had the pleasure of seeing you at Tipton Grange before your marriage, you were asking me some questions about the way in which the health of the poor was affected by their miserable housing.”\n\n“Yes, indeed,” said Dorothea, brightening. “I shall be quite grateful to you if you will tell me how I can help to make things a little better. Everything of that sort has slipped away from me since I have been married. I mean,” she said, after a moment’s hesitation, “that the people in our village are tolerably comfortable, and my mind has been too much taken up for me to inquire further. But here—in such a place as Middlemarch—there must be a great deal to be done.”\n\n“There is everything to be done,” said Lydgate, with abrupt energy. “And this Hospital is a capital piece of work, due entirely to Mr. Bulstrode’s exertions, and in a great degree to his money. But one man can’t do everything in a scheme of this sort. Of course he looked forward to help. And now there’s a mean, petty feud set up against the thing in the town, by certain persons who want to make it a failure.”\n\n“What can be their reasons?” said Dorothea, with naive surprise.\n\n“Chiefly Mr. Bulstrode’s unpopularity, to begin with. Half the town would almost take trouble for the sake of thwarting him. In this stupid world most people never consider that a thing is good to be done unless it is done by their own set. I had no connection with Bulstrode before I came here. I look at him quite impartially, and I see that he has some notions—that he has set things on foot—which I can turn to good public purpose. If a fair number of the better educated men went to work with the belief that their observations might contribute to the reform of medical doctrine and practice, we should soon see a change for the better. That’s my point of view. I hold that by refusing to work with Mr. Bulstrode I should be turning my back on an opportunity of making my profession more generally serviceable.”\n\n“I quite agree with you,” said Dorothea, at once fascinated by the situation sketched in Lydgate’s words. “But what is there against Mr. Bulstrode? I know that my uncle is friendly with him.”\n\n“People don’t like his religious tone,” said Lydgate, breaking off there.\n\n“That is all the stronger reason for despising such an opposition,” said Dorothea, looking at the affairs of Middlemarch by the light of the great persecutions.\n\n“To put the matter quite fairly, they have other objections to him:—he is masterful and rather unsociable, and he is concerned with trade, which has complaints of its own that I know nothing about. But what has that to do with the question whether it would not be a fine thing to establish here a more valuable hospital than any they have in the county? The immediate motive to the opposition, however, is the fact that Bulstrode has put the medical direction into my hands. Of course I am glad of that. It gives me an opportunity of doing some good work,—and I am aware that I have to justify his choice of me. But the consequence is, that the whole profession in Middlemarch have set themselves tooth and nail against the Hospital, and not only refuse to cooperate themselves, but try to blacken the whole affair and hinder subscriptions.”\n\n“How very petty!” exclaimed Dorothea, indignantly.\n\n“I suppose one must expect to fight one’s way: there is hardly anything to be done without it. And the ignorance of people about here is stupendous. I don’t lay claim to anything else than having used some opportunities which have not come within everybody’s reach; but there is no stifling the offence of being young, and a new-comer, and happening to know something more than the old inhabitants. Still, if I believe that I can set going a better method of treatment—if I believe that I can pursue certain observations and inquiries which may be a lasting benefit to medical practice, I should be a base truckler if I allowed any consideration of personal comfort to hinder me. And the course is all the clearer from there being no salary in question to put my persistence in an equivocal light.”\n\n“I am glad you have told me this, Mr. Lydgate,” said Dorothea, cordially. “I feel sure I can help a little. I have some money, and don’t know what to do with it—that is often an uncomfortable thought to me. I am sure I can spare two hundred a-year for a grand purpose like this. How happy you must be, to know things that you feel sure will do great good! I wish I could awake with that knowledge every morning. There seems to be so much trouble taken that one can hardly see the good of!”\n\nThere was a melancholy cadence in Dorothea’s voice as she spoke these last words. But she presently added, more cheerfully, “Pray come to Lowick and tell us more of this. I will mention the subject to Mr. Casaubon. I must hasten home now.”\n\nShe did mention it that evening, and said that she should like to subscribe two hundred a-year—she had seven hundred a-year as the equivalent of her own fortune, settled on her at her marriage. Mr. Casaubon made no objection beyond a passing remark that the sum might be disproportionate in relation to other good objects, but when Dorothea in her ignorance resisted that suggestion, he acquiesced. He did not care himself about spending money, and was not reluctant to give it. If he ever felt keenly any question of money it was through the medium of another passion than the love of material property.\n\nDorothea told him that she had seen Lydgate, and recited the gist of her conversation with him about the Hospital. Mr. Casaubon did not question her further, but he felt sure that she had wished to know what had passed between Lydgate and himself. “She knows that I know,” said the ever-restless voice within; but that increase of tacit knowledge only thrust further off any confidence between them. He distrusted her affection; and what loneliness is more lonely than distrust?\n\n# CHAPTER XLV.\n\nIt is the humor of many heads to extol the days of their forefathers, and declaim against the wickedness of times present. Which notwithstanding they cannot handsomely do, without the borrowed help and satire of times past; condemning the vices of their own times, by the expressions of vices in times which they commend, which cannot but argue the community of vice in both. Horace, therefore, Juvenal, and Persius, were no prophets, although their lines did seem to indigitate and point at our times.—SIR THOMAS BROWNE: Pseudodoxia Epidemica.\n\nThat opposition to the New Fever Hospital which Lydgate had sketched to Dorothea was, like other oppositions, to be viewed in many different lights. He regarded it as a mixture of jealousy and dunderheaded prejudice. Mr. Bulstrode saw in it not only medical jealousy but a determination to thwart himself, prompted mainly by a hatred of that vital religion of which he had striven to be an effectual lay representative—a hatred which certainly found pretexts apart from religion such as were only too easy to find in the entanglements of human action. These might be called the ministerial views. But oppositions have the illimitable range of objections at command, which need never stop short at the boundary of knowledge, but can draw forever on the vasts of ignorance. What the opposition in Middlemarch said about the New Hospital and its administration had certainly a great deal of echo in it, for heaven has taken care that everybody shall not be an originator; but there were differences which represented every social shade between the polished moderation of Dr. Minchin and the trenchant assertion of Mrs. Dollop, the landlady of the Tankard in Slaughter Lane.\n\nMrs. Dollop became more and more convinced by her own asseveration, that Dr. Lydgate meant to let the people die in the Hospital, if not to poison them, for the sake of cutting them up without saying by your leave or with your leave; for it was a known “fac” that he had wanted to cut up Mrs. Goby, as respectable a woman as any in Parley Street, who had money in trust before her marriage—a poor tale for a doctor, who if he was good for anything should know what was the matter with you before you died, and not want to pry into your inside after you were gone. If that was not reason, Mrs. Dollop wished to know what was; but there was a prevalent feeling in her audience that her opinion was a bulwark, and that if it were overthrown there would be no limits to the cutting-up of bodies, as had been well seen in Burke and Hare with their pitch-plaisters—such a hanging business as that was not wanted in Middlemarch!\n\nAnd let it not be supposed that opinion at the Tankard in Slaughter Lane was unimportant to the medical profession: that old authentic public-house—the original Tankard, known by the name of Dollop’s—was the resort of a great Benefit Club, which had some months before put to the vote whether its long-standing medical man, “Doctor Gambit,” should not be cashiered in favor of “this Doctor Lydgate,” who was capable of performing the most astonishing cures, and rescuing people altogether given up by other practitioners. But the balance had been turned against Lydgate by two members, who for some private reasons held that this power of resuscitating persons as good as dead was an equivocal recommendation, and might interfere with providential favors. In the course of the year, however, there had been a change in the public sentiment, of which the unanimity at Dollop’s was an index.\n\nA good deal more than a year ago, before anything was known of Lydgate’s skill, the judgments on it had naturally been divided, depending on a sense of likelihood, situated perhaps in the pit of the stomach or in the pineal gland, and differing in its verdicts, but not the less valuable as a guide in the total deficit of evidence. Patients who had chronic diseases or whose lives had long been worn threadbare, like old Featherstone’s, had been at once inclined to try him; also, many who did not like paying their doctor’s bills, thought agreeably of opening an account with a new doctor and sending for him without stint if the children’s temper wanted a dose, occasions when the old practitioners were often crusty; and all persons thus inclined to employ Lydgate held it likely that he was clever. Some considered that he might do more than others “where there was liver;”—at least there would be no harm in getting a few bottles of “stuff” from him, since if these proved useless it would still be possible to return to the Purifying Pills, which kept you alive if they did not remove the yellowness. But these were people of minor importance. Good Middlemarch families were of course not going to change their doctor without reason shown; and everybody who had employed Mr. Peacock did not feel obliged to accept a new man merely in the character of his successor, objecting that he was “not likely to be equal to Peacock.”\n\nBut Lydgate had not been long in the town before there were particulars enough reported of him to breed much more specific expectations and to intensify differences into partisanship; some of the particulars being of that impressive order of which the significance is entirely hidden, like a statistical amount without a standard of comparison, but with a note of exclamation at the end. The cubic feet of oxygen yearly swallowed by a full-grown man—what a shudder they might have created in some Middlemarch circles! “Oxygen! nobody knows what that may be—is it any wonder the cholera has got to Dantzic? And yet there are people who say quarantine is no good!”\n\nOne of the facts quickly rumored was that Lydgate did not dispense drugs. This was offensive both to the physicians whose exclusive distinction seemed infringed on, and to the surgeon-apothecaries with whom he ranged himself; and only a little while before, they might have counted on having the law on their side against a man who without calling himself a London-made M.D. dared to ask for pay except as a charge on drugs. But Lydgate had not been experienced enough to foresee that his new course would be even more offensive to the laity; and to Mr. Mawmsey, an important grocer in the Top Market, who, though not one of his patients, questioned him in an affable manner on the subject, he was injudicious enough to give a hasty popular explanation of his reasons, pointing out to Mr. Mawmsey that it must lower the character of practitioners, and be a constant injury to the public, if their only mode of getting paid for their work was by their making out long bills for draughts, boluses, and mixtures.\n\n“It is in that way that hard-working medical men may come to be almost as mischievous as quacks,” said Lydgate, rather thoughtlessly. “To get their own bread they must overdose the king’s lieges; and that’s a bad sort of treason, Mr. Mawmsey—undermines the constitution in a fatal way.”\n\nMr. Mawmsey was not only an overseer (it was about a question of outdoor pay that he was having an interview with Lydgate), he was also asthmatic and had an increasing family: thus, from a medical point of view, as well as from his own, he was an important man; indeed, an exceptional grocer, whose hair was arranged in a flame-like pyramid, and whose retail deference was of the cordial, encouraging kind—jocosely complimentary, and with a certain considerate abstinence from letting out the full force of his mind. It was Mr. Mawmsey’s friendly jocoseness in questioning him which had set the tone of Lydgate’s reply. But let the wise be warned against too great readiness at explanation: it multiplies the sources of mistake, lengthening the sum for reckoners sure to go wrong.\n\nLydgate smiled as he ended his speech, putting his foot into the stirrup, and Mr. Mawmsey laughed more than he would have done if he had known who the king’s lieges were, giving his “Good morning, sir, good-morning, sir,” with the air of one who saw everything clearly enough. But in truth his views were perturbed. For years he had been paying bills with strictly made items, so that for every half-crown and eighteen-pence he was certain something measurable had been delivered. He had done this with satisfaction, including it among his responsibilities as a husband and father, and regarding a longer bill than usual as a dignity worth mentioning. Moreover, in addition to the massive benefit of the drugs to “self and family,” he had enjoyed the pleasure of forming an acute judgment as to their immediate effects, so as to give an intelligent statement for the guidance of Mr. Gambit—a practitioner just a little lower in status than Wrench or Toller, and especially esteemed as an accoucheur, of whose ability Mr. Mawmsey had the poorest opinion on all other points, but in doctoring, he was wont to say in an undertone, he placed Gambit above any of them.\n\nHere were deeper reasons than the superficial talk of a new man, which appeared still flimsier in the drawing-room over the shop, when they were recited to Mrs. Mawmsey, a woman accustomed to be made much of as a fertile mother,—generally under attendance more or less frequent from Mr. Gambit, and occasionally having attacks which required Dr. Minchin.\n\n“Does this Mr. Lydgate mean to say there is no use in taking medicine?” said Mrs. Mawmsey, who was slightly given to drawling. “I should like him to tell me how I could bear up at Fair time, if I didn’t take strengthening medicine for a month beforehand. Think of what I have to provide for calling customers, my dear!”—here Mrs. Mawmsey turned to an intimate female friend who sat by—“a large veal pie—a stuffed fillet—a round of beef—ham, tongue, et cetera, et cetera! But what keeps me up best is the pink mixture, not the brown. I wonder, Mr. Mawmsey, with your experience, you could have patience to listen. I should have told him at once that I knew a little better than that.”\n\n“No, no, no,” said Mr. Mawmsey; “I was not going to tell him my opinion. Hear everything and judge for yourself is my motto. But he didn’t know who he was talking to. I was not to be turned on his finger. People often pretend to tell me things, when they might as well say, ‘Mawmsey, you’re a fool.’ But I smile at it: I humor everybody’s weak place. If physic had done harm to self and family, I should have found it out by this time.”\n\nThe next day Mr. Gambit was told that Lydgate went about saying physic was of no use.\n\n“Indeed!” said he, lifting his eyebrows with cautious surprise. (He was a stout husky man with a large ring on his fourth finger.) “How will he cure his patients, then?”\n\n“That is what I say,” returned Mrs. Mawmsey, who habitually gave weight to her speech by loading her pronouns. “Does he suppose that people will pay him only to come and sit with them and go away again?”\n\nMrs. Mawmsey had had a great deal of sitting from Mr. Gambit, including very full accounts of his own habits of body and other affairs; but of course he knew there was no innuendo in her remark, since his spare time and personal narrative had never been charged for. So he replied, humorously—\n\n“Well, Lydgate is a good-looking young fellow, you know.”\n\n“Not one that I would employ,” said Mrs. Mawmsey. “Others may do as they please.”\n\nHence Mr. Gambit could go away from the chief grocer’s without fear of rivalry, but not without a sense that Lydgate was one of those hypocrites who try to discredit others by advertising their own honesty, and that it might be worth some people’s while to show him up. Mr. Gambit, however, had a satisfactory practice, much pervaded by the smells of retail trading which suggested the reduction of cash payments to a balance. And he did not think it worth his while to show Lydgate up until he knew how. He had not indeed great resources of education, and had had to work his own way against a good deal of professional contempt; but he made none the worse accoucheur for calling the breathing apparatus “longs.”\n\nOther medical men felt themselves more capable. Mr. Toller shared the highest practice in the town and belonged to an old Middlemarch family: there were Tollers in the law and everything else above the line of retail trade. Unlike our irascible friend Wrench, he had the easiest way in the world of taking things which might be supposed to annoy him, being a well-bred, quietly facetious man, who kept a good house, was very fond of a little sporting when he could get it, very friendly with Mr. Hawley, and hostile to Mr. Bulstrode. It may seem odd that with such pleasant habits he should have been given to the heroic treatment, bleeding and blistering and starving his patients, with a dispassionate disregard to his personal example; but the incongruity favored the opinion of his ability among his patients, who commonly observed that Mr. Toller had lazy manners, but his treatment was as active as you could desire: no man, said they, carried more seriousness into his profession: he was a little slow in coming, but when he came, he did something. He was a great favorite in his own circle, and whatever he implied to any one’s disadvantage told doubly from his careless ironical tone.\n\nHe naturally got tired of smiling and saying, “Ah!” when he was told that Mr. Peacock’s successor did not mean to dispense medicines; and Mr. Hackbutt one day mentioning it over the wine at a dinner-party, Mr. Toller said, laughingly, “Dibbitts will get rid of his stale drugs, then. I’m fond of little Dibbitts—I’m glad he’s in luck.”\n\n“I see your meaning, Toller,” said Mr. Hackbutt, “and I am entirely of your opinion. I shall take an opportunity of expressing myself to that effect. A medical man should be responsible for the quality of the drugs consumed by his patients. That is the rationale of the system of charging which has hitherto obtained; and nothing is more offensive than this ostentation of reform, where there is no real amelioration.”\n\n“Ostentation, Hackbutt?” said Mr. Toller, ironically. “I don’t see that. A man can’t very well be ostentatious of what nobody believes in. There’s no reform in the matter: the question is, whether the profit on the drugs is paid to the medical man by the druggist or by the patient, and whether there shall be extra pay under the name of attendance.”\n\n“Ah, to be sure; one of your damned new versions of old humbug,” said Mr. Hawley, passing the decanter to Mr. Wrench.\n\nMr. Wrench, generally abstemious, often drank wine rather freely at a party, getting the more irritable in consequence.\n\n“As to humbug, Hawley,” he said, “that’s a word easy to fling about. But what I contend against is the way medical men are fouling their own nest, and setting up a cry about the country as if a general practitioner who dispenses drugs couldn’t be a gentleman. I throw back the imputation with scorn. I say, the most ungentlemanly trick a man can be guilty of is to come among the members of his profession with innovations which are a libel on their time-honored procedure. That is my opinion, and I am ready to maintain it against any one who contradicts me.” Mr. Wrench’s voice had become exceedingly sharp.\n\n“I can’t oblige you there, Wrench,” said Mr. Hawley, thrusting his hands into his trouser-pockets.\n\n“My dear fellow,” said Mr. Toller, striking in pacifically, and looking at Mr. Wrench, “the physicians have their toes trodden on more than we have. If you come to dignity it is a question for Minchin and Sprague.”\n\n“Does medical jurisprudence provide nothing against these infringements?” said Mr. Hackbutt, with a disinterested desire to offer his lights. “How does the law stand, eh, Hawley?”\n\n“Nothing to be done there,” said Mr. Hawley. “I looked into it for Sprague. You’d only break your nose against a damned judge’s decision.”\n\n“Pooh! no need of law,” said Mr. Toller. “So far as practice is concerned the attempt is an absurdity. No patient will like it—certainly not Peacock’s, who have been used to depletion. Pass the wine.”\n\nMr. Toller’s prediction was partly verified. If Mr. and Mrs. Mawmsey, who had no idea of employing Lydgate, were made uneasy by his supposed declaration against drugs, it was inevitable that those who called him in should watch a little anxiously to see whether he did “use all the means he might use” in the case. Even good Mr. Powderell, who in his constant charity of interpretation was inclined to esteem Lydgate the more for what seemed a conscientious pursuit of a better plan, had his mind disturbed with doubts during his wife’s attack of erysipelas, and could not abstain from mentioning to Lydgate that Mr. Peacock on a similar occasion had administered a series of boluses which were not otherwise definable than by their remarkable effect in bringing Mrs. Powderell round before Michaelmas from an illness which had begun in a remarkably hot August. At last, indeed, in the conflict between his desire not to hurt Lydgate and his anxiety that no “means” should be lacking, he induced his wife privately to take Widgeon’s Purifying Pills, an esteemed Middlemarch medicine, which arrested every disease at the fountain by setting to work at once upon the blood. This co-operative measure was not to be mentioned to Lydgate, and Mr. Powderell himself had no certain reliance on it, only hoping that it might be attended with a blessing.\n\nBut in this doubtful stage of Lydgate’s introduction he was helped by what we mortals rashly call good fortune. I suppose no doctor ever came newly to a place without making cures that surprised somebody—cures which may be called fortune’s testimonials, and deserve as much credit as the written or printed kind. Various patients got well while Lydgate was attending them, some even of dangerous illnesses; and it was remarked that the new doctor with his new ways had at least the merit of bringing people back from the brink of death. The trash talked on such occasions was the more vexatious to Lydgate, because it gave precisely the sort of prestige which an incompetent and unscrupulous man would desire, and was sure to be imputed to him by the simmering dislike of the other medical men as an encouragement on his own part of ignorant puffing. But even his proud outspokenness was checked by the discernment that it was as useless to fight against the interpretations of ignorance as to whip the fog; and “good fortune” insisted on using those interpretations.\n\nMrs. Larcher having just become charitably concerned about alarming symptoms in her charwoman, when Dr. Minchin called, asked him to see her then and there, and to give her a certificate for the Infirmary; whereupon after examination he wrote a statement of the case as one of tumor, and recommended the bearer Nancy Nash as an out-patient. Nancy, calling at home on her way to the Infirmary, allowed the stay maker and his wife, in whose attic she lodged, to read Dr. Minchin’s paper, and by this means became a subject of compassionate conversation in the neighboring shops of Churchyard Lane as being afflicted with a tumor at first declared to be as large and hard as a duck’s egg, but later in the day to be about the size of “your fist.” Most hearers agreed that it would have to be cut out, but one had known of oil and another of “squitchineal” as adequate to soften and reduce any lump in the body when taken enough of into the inside—the oil by gradually “soopling,” the squitchineal by eating away.\n\nMeanwhile when Nancy presented herself at the Infirmary, it happened to be one of Lydgate’s days there. After questioning and examining her, Lydgate said to the house-surgeon in an undertone, “It’s not tumor: it’s cramp.” He ordered her a blister and some steel mixture, and told her to go home and rest, giving her at the same time a note to Mrs. Larcher, who, she said, was her best employer, to testify that she was in need of good food.\n\nBut by-and-by Nancy, in her attic, became portentously worse, the supposed tumor having indeed given way to the blister, but only wandered to another region with angrier pain. The staymaker’s wife went to fetch Lydgate, and he continued for a fortnight to attend Nancy in her own home, until under his treatment she got quite well and went to work again. But the case continued to be described as one of tumor in Churchyard Lane and other streets—nay, by Mrs. Larcher also; for when Lydgate’s remarkable cure was mentioned to Dr. Minchin, he naturally did not like to say, “The case was not one of tumor, and I was mistaken in describing it as such,” but answered, “Indeed! ah! I saw it was a surgical case, not of a fatal kind.” He had been inwardly annoyed, however, when he had asked at the Infirmary about the woman he had recommended two days before, to hear from the house-surgeon, a youngster who was not sorry to vex Minchin with impunity, exactly what had occurred: he privately pronounced that it was indecent in a general practitioner to contradict a physician’s diagnosis in that open manner, and afterwards agreed with Wrench that Lydgate was disagreeably inattentive to etiquette. Lydgate did not make the affair a ground for valuing himself or (very particularly) despising Minchin, such rectification of misjudgments often happening among men of equal qualifications. But report took up this amazing case of tumor, not clearly distinguished from cancer, and considered the more awful for being of the wandering sort; till much prejudice against Lydgate’s method as to drugs was overcome by the proof of his marvellous skill in the speedy restoration of Nancy Nash after she had been rolling and rolling in agonies from the presence of a tumor both hard and obstinate, but nevertheless compelled to yield.\n\nHow could Lydgate help himself? It is offensive to tell a lady when she is expressing her amazement at your skill, that she is altogether mistaken and rather foolish in her amazement. And to have entered into the nature of diseases would only have added to his breaches of medical propriety. Thus he had to wince under a promise of success given by that ignorant praise which misses every valid quality.\n\nIn the case of a more conspicuous patient, Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, Lydgate was conscious of having shown himself something better than an every-day doctor, though here too it was an equivocal advantage that he won. The eloquent auctioneer was seized with pneumonia, and having been a patient of Mr. Peacock’s, sent for Lydgate, whom he had expressed his intention to patronize. Mr Trumbull was a robust man, a good subject for trying the expectant theory upon—watching the course of an interesting disease when left as much as possible to itself, so that the stages might be noted for future guidance; and from the air with which he described his sensations Lydgate surmised that he would like to be taken into his medical man’s confidence, and be represented as a partner in his own cure. The auctioneer heard, without much surprise, that his was a constitution which (always with due watching) might be left to itself, so as to offer a beautiful example of a disease with all its phases seen in clear delineation, and that he probably had the rare strength of mind voluntarily to become the test of a rational procedure, and thus make the disorder of his pulmonary functions a general benefit to society.\n\nMr. Trumbull acquiesced at once, and entered strongly into the view that an illness of his was no ordinary occasion for medical science.\n\n“Never fear, sir; you are not speaking to one who is altogether ignorant of the vis medicatrix,” said he, with his usual superiority of expression, made rather pathetic by difficulty of breathing. And he went without shrinking through his abstinence from drugs, much sustained by application of the thermometer which implied the importance of his temperature, by the sense that he furnished objects for the microscope, and by learning many new words which seemed suited to the dignity of his secretions. For Lydgate was acute enough to indulge him with a little technical talk.\n\nIt may be imagined that Mr. Trumbull rose from his couch with a disposition to speak of an illness in which he had manifested the strength of his mind as well as constitution; and he was not backward in awarding credit to the medical man who had discerned the quality of patient he had to deal with. The auctioneer was not an ungenerous man, and liked to give others their due, feeling that he could afford it. He had caught the words “expectant method,” and rang chimes on this and other learned phrases to accompany the assurance that Lydgate “knew a thing or two more than the rest of the doctors—was far better versed in the secrets of his profession than the majority of his compeers.”\n\nThis had happened before the affair of Fred Vincy’s illness had given to Mr. Wrench’s enmity towards Lydgate more definite personal ground. The new-comer already threatened to be a nuisance in the shape of rivalry, and was certainly a nuisance in the shape of practical criticism or reflections on his hard-driven elders, who had had something else to do than to busy themselves with untried notions. His practice had spread in one or two quarters, and from the first the report of his high family had led to his being pretty generally invited, so that the other medical men had to meet him at dinner in the best houses; and having to meet a man whom you dislike is not observed always to end in a mutual attachment. There was hardly ever so much unanimity among them as in the opinion that Lydgate was an arrogant young fellow, and yet ready for the sake of ultimately predominating to show a crawling subservience to Bulstrode. That Mr. Farebrother, whose name was a chief flag of the anti-Bulstrode party, always defended Lydgate and made a friend of him, was referred to Farebrother’s unaccountable way of fighting on both sides.\n\nHere was plenty of preparation for the outburst of professional disgust at the announcement of the laws Mr. Bulstrode was laying down for the direction of the New Hospital, which were the more exasperating because there was no present possibility of interfering with his will and pleasure, everybody except Lord Medlicote having refused help towards the building, on the ground that they preferred giving to the Old Infirmary. Mr. Bulstrode met all the expenses, and had ceased to be sorry that he was purchasing the right to carry out his notions of improvement without hindrance from prejudiced coadjutors; but he had had to spend large sums, and the building had lingered. Caleb Garth had undertaken it, had failed during its progress, and before the interior fittings were begun had retired from the management of the business; and when referring to the Hospital he often said that however Bulstrode might ring if you tried him, he liked good solid carpentry and masonry, and had a notion both of drains and chimneys. In fact, the Hospital had become an object of intense interest to Bulstrode, and he would willingly have continued to spare a large yearly sum that he might rule it dictatorially without any Board; but he had another favorite object which also required money for its accomplishment: he wished to buy some land in the neighborhood of Middlemarch, and therefore he wished to get considerable contributions towards maintaining the Hospital. Meanwhile he framed his plan of management. The Hospital was to be reserved for fever in all its forms; Lydgate was to be chief medical superintendent, that he might have free authority to pursue all comparative investigations which his studies, particularly in Paris, had shown him the importance of, the other medical visitors having a consultative influence, but no power to contravene Lydgate’s ultimate decisions; and the general management was to be lodged exclusively in the hands of five directors associated with Mr. Bulstrode, who were to have votes in the ratio of their contributions, the Board itself filling up any vacancy in its numbers, and no mob of small contributors being admitted to a share of government.\n\nThere was an immediate refusal on the part of every medical man in the town to become a visitor at the Fever Hospital.\n\n“Very well,” said Lydgate to Mr. Bulstrode, “we have a capital house-surgeon and dispenser, a clear-headed, neat-handed fellow; we’ll get Webbe from Crabsley, as good a country practitioner as any of them, to come over twice a-week, and in case of any exceptional operation, Protheroe will come from Brassing. I must work the harder, that’s all, and I have given up my post at the Infirmary. The plan will flourish in spite of them, and then they’ll be glad to come in. Things can’t last as they are: there must be all sorts of reform soon, and then young fellows may be glad to come and study here.” Lydgate was in high spirits.\n\n“I shall not flinch, you may depend upon it, Mr. Lydgate,” said Mr. Bulstrode. “While I see you carrying out high intentions with vigor, you shall have my unfailing support. And I have humble confidence that the blessing which has hitherto attended my efforts against the spirit of evil in this town will not be withdrawn. Suitable directors to assist me I have no doubt of securing. Mr. Brooke of Tipton has already given me his concurrence, and a pledge to contribute yearly: he has not specified the sum—probably not a great one. But he will be a useful member of the board.”\n\nA useful member was perhaps to be defined as one who would originate nothing, and always vote with Mr. Bulstrode.\n\nThe medical aversion to Lydgate was hardly disguised now. Neither Dr. Sprague nor Dr. Minchin said that he disliked Lydgate’s knowledge, or his disposition to improve treatment: what they disliked was his arrogance, which nobody felt to be altogether deniable. They implied that he was insolent, pretentious, and given to that reckless innovation for the sake of noise and show which was the essence of the charlatan.\n\nThe word charlatan once thrown on the air could not be let drop. In those days the world was agitated about the wondrous doings of Mr. St. John Long, “noblemen and gentlemen” attesting his extraction of a fluid like mercury from the temples of a patient.\n\nMr. Toller remarked one day, smilingly, to Mrs. Taft, that “Bulstrode had found a man to suit him in Lydgate; a charlatan in religion is sure to like other sorts of charlatans.”\n\n“Yes, indeed, I can imagine,” said Mrs. Taft, keeping the number of thirty stitches carefully in her mind all the while; “there are so many of that sort. I remember Mr. Cheshire, with his irons, trying to make people straight when the Almighty had made them crooked.”\n\n“No, no,” said Mr. Toller, “Cheshire was all right—all fair and above board. But there’s St. John Long—that’s the kind of fellow we call a charlatan, advertising cures in ways nobody knows anything about: a fellow who wants to make a noise by pretending to go deeper than other people. The other day he was pretending to tap a man’s brain and get quicksilver out of it.”\n\n“Good gracious! what dreadful trifling with people’s constitutions!” said Mrs. Taft.\n\nAfter this, it came to be held in various quarters that Lydgate played even with respectable constitutions for his own purposes, and how much more likely that in his flighty experimenting he should make sixes and sevens of hospital patients. Especially it was to be expected, as the landlady of the Tankard had said, that he would recklessly cut up their dead bodies. For Lydgate having attended Mrs. Goby, who died apparently of a heart-disease not very clearly expressed in the symptoms, too daringly asked leave of her relatives to open the body, and thus gave an offence quickly spreading beyond Parley Street, where that lady had long resided on an income such as made this association of her body with the victims of Burke and Hare a flagrant insult to her memory.\n\nAffairs were in this stage when Lydgate opened the subject of the Hospital to Dorothea. We see that he was bearing enmity and silly misconception with much spirit, aware that they were partly created by his good share of success.\n\n“They will not drive me away,” he said, talking confidentially in Mr. Farebrother’s study. “I have got a good opportunity here, for the ends I care most about; and I am pretty sure to get income enough for our wants. By-and-by I shall go on as quietly as possible: I have no seductions now away from home and work. And I am more and more convinced that it will be possible to demonstrate the homogeneous origin of all the tissues. Raspail and others are on the same track, and I have been losing time.”\n\n“I have no power of prophecy there,” said Mr. Farebrother, who had been puffing at his pipe thoughtfully while Lydgate talked; “but as to the hostility in the town, you’ll weather it if you are prudent.”\n\n“How am I to be prudent?” said Lydgate, “I just do what comes before me to do. I can’t help people’s ignorance and spite, any more than Vesalius could. It isn’t possible to square one’s conduct to silly conclusions which nobody can foresee.”\n\n“Quite true; I didn’t mean that. I meant only two things. One is, keep yourself as separable from Bulstrode as you can: of course, you can go on doing good work of your own by his help; but don’t get tied. Perhaps it seems like personal feeling in me to say so—and there’s a good deal of that, I own—but personal feeling is not always in the wrong if you boil it down to the impressions which make it simply an opinion.”\n\n“Bulstrode is nothing to me,” said Lydgate, carelessly, “except on public grounds. As to getting very closely united to him, I am not fond enough of him for that. But what was the other thing you meant?” said Lydgate, who was nursing his leg as comfortably as possible, and feeling in no great need of advice.\n\n“Why, this. Take care—experto crede—take care not to get hampered about money matters. I know, by a word you let fall one day, that you don’t like my playing at cards so much for money. You are right enough there. But try and keep clear of wanting small sums that you haven’t got. I am perhaps talking rather superfluously; but a man likes to assume superiority over himself, by holding up his bad example and sermonizing on it.”\n\nLydgate took Mr. Farebrother’s hints very cordially, though he would hardly have borne them from another man. He could not help remembering that he had lately made some debts, but these had seemed inevitable, and he had no intention now to do more than keep house in a simple way. The furniture for which he owed would not want renewing; nor even the stock of wine for a long while.\n\nMany thoughts cheered him at that time—and justly. A man conscious of enthusiasm for worthy aims is sustained under petty hostilities by the memory of great workers who had to fight their way not without wounds, and who hover in his mind as patron saints, invisibly helping. At home, that same evening when he had been chatting with Mr. Farebrother, he had his long legs stretched on the sofa, his head thrown back, and his hands clasped behind it according to his favorite ruminating attitude, while Rosamond sat at the piano, and played one tune after another, of which her husband only knew (like the emotional elephant he was!) that they fell in with his mood as if they had been melodious sea-breezes.\n\nThere was something very fine in Lydgate’s look just then, and any one might have been encouraged to bet on his achievement. In his dark eyes and on his mouth and brow there was that placidity which comes from the fulness of contemplative thought—the mind not searching, but beholding, and the glance seeming to be filled with what is behind it.\n\nPresently Rosamond left the piano and seated herself on a chair close to the sofa and opposite her husband’s face.\n\n“Is that enough music for you, my lord?” she said, folding her hands before her and putting on a little air of meekness.\n\n“Yes, dear, if you are tired,” said Lydgate, gently, turning his eyes and resting them on her, but not otherwise moving. Rosamond’s presence at that moment was perhaps no more than a spoonful brought to the lake, and her woman’s instinct in this matter was not dull.\n\n“What is absorbing you?” she said, leaning forward and bringing her face nearer to his.\n\nHe moved his hands and placed them gently behind her shoulders.\n\n“I am thinking of a great fellow, who was about as old as I am three hundred years ago, and had already begun a new era in anatomy.”\n\n“I can’t guess,” said Rosamond, shaking her head. “We used to play at guessing historical characters at Mrs. Lemon’s, but not anatomists.”\n\n“I’ll tell you. His name was Vesalius. And the only way he could get to know anatomy as he did, was by going to snatch bodies at night, from graveyards and places of execution.”\n\n“Oh!” said Rosamond, with a look of disgust on her pretty face, “I am very glad you are not Vesalius. I should have thought he might find some less horrible way than that.”\n\n“No, he couldn’t,” said Lydgate, going on too earnestly to take much notice of her answer. “He could only get a complete skeleton by snatching the whitened bones of a criminal from the gallows, and burying them, and fetching them away by bits secretly, in the dead of night.”\n\n“I hope he is not one of your great heroes,” said Rosamond, half playfully, half anxiously, “else I shall have you getting up in the night to go to St. Peter’s churchyard. You know how angry you told me the people were about Mrs. Goby. You have enemies enough already.”\n\n“So had Vesalius, Rosy. No wonder the medical fogies in Middlemarch are jealous, when some of the greatest doctors living were fierce upon Vesalius because they had believed in Galen, and he showed that Galen was wrong. They called him a liar and a poisonous monster. But the facts of the human frame were on his side; and so he got the better of them.”\n\n“And what happened to him afterwards?” said Rosamond, with some interest.\n\n“Oh, he had a good deal of fighting to the last. And they did exasperate him enough at one time to make him burn a good deal of his work. Then he got shipwrecked just as he was coming from Jerusalem to take a great chair at Padua. He died rather miserably.”\n\nThere was a moment’s pause before Rosamond said, “Do you know, Tertius, I often wish you had not been a medical man.”\n\n“Nay, Rosy, don’t say that,” said Lydgate, drawing her closer to him. “That is like saying you wish you had married another man.”\n\n“Not at all; you are clever enough for anything: you might easily have been something else. And your cousins at Quallingham all think that you have sunk below them in your choice of a profession.”\n\n“The cousins at Quallingham may go to the devil!” said Lydgate, with scorn. “It was like their impudence if they said anything of the sort to you.”\n\n“Still,” said Rosamond, “I do not think it is a nice profession, dear.” We know that she had much quiet perseverance in her opinion.\n\n“It is the grandest profession in the world, Rosamond,” said Lydgate, gravely. “And to say that you love me without loving the medical man in me, is the same sort of thing as to say that you like eating a peach but don’t like its flavor. Don’t say that again, dear, it pains me.”\n\n“Very well, Doctor Grave-face,” said Rosy, dimpling, “I will declare in future that I dote on skeletons, and body-snatchers, and bits of things in phials, and quarrels with everybody, that end in your dying miserably.”\n\n“No, no, not so bad as that,” said Lydgate, giving up remonstrance and petting her resignedly.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVI.\n\nPues no podemos haber aquello que queremos, queramos aquello que podremos.\n\nSince we cannot get what we like, let us like what we can get.—Spanish Proverb.\n\nWhile Lydgate, safely married and with the Hospital under his command, felt himself struggling for Medical Reform against Middlemarch, Middlemarch was becoming more and more conscious of the national struggle for another kind of Reform.\n\nBy the time that Lord John Russell’s measure was being debated in the House of Commons, there was a new political animation in Middlemarch, and a new definition of parties which might show a decided change of balance if a new election came. And there were some who already predicted this event, declaring that a Reform Bill would never be carried by the actual Parliament. This was what Will Ladislaw dwelt on to Mr. Brooke as a reason for congratulation that he had not yet tried his strength at the hustings.\n\n“Things will grow and ripen as if it were a comet year,” said Will. “The public temper will soon get to a cometary heat, now the question of Reform has set in. There is likely to be another election before long, and by that time Middlemarch will have got more ideas into its head. What we have to work at now is the ‘Pioneer’ and political meetings.”\n\n“Quite right, Ladislaw; we shall make a new thing of opinion here,” said Mr. Brooke. “Only I want to keep myself independent about Reform, you know; I don’t want to go too far. I want to take up Wilberforce’s and Romilly’s line, you know, and work at Negro Emancipation, Criminal Law—that kind of thing. But of course I should support Grey.”\n\n“If you go in for the principle of Reform, you must be prepared to take what the situation offers,” said Will. “If everybody pulled for his own bit against everybody else, the whole question would go to tatters.”\n\n“Yes, yes, I agree with you—I quite take that point of view. I should put it in that light. I should support Grey, you know. But I don’t want to change the balance of the constitution, and I don’t think Grey would.”\n\n“But that is what the country wants,” said Will. “Else there would be no meaning in political unions or any other movement that knows what it’s about. It wants to have a House of Commons which is not weighted with nominees of the landed class, but with representatives of the other interests. And as to contending for a reform short of that, it is like asking for a bit of an avalanche which has already begun to thunder.”\n\n“That is fine, Ladislaw: that is the way to put it. Write that down, now. We must begin to get documents about the feeling of the country, as well as the machine-breaking and general distress.”\n\n“As to documents,” said Will, “a two-inch card will hold plenty. A few rows of figures are enough to deduce misery from, and a few more will show the rate at which the political determination of the people is growing.”\n\n“Good: draw that out a little more at length, Ladislaw. That is an idea, now: write it out in the ‘Pioneer.’ Put the figures and deduce the misery, you know; and put the other figures and deduce—and so on. You have a way of putting things. Burke, now:—when I think of Burke, I can’t help wishing somebody had a pocket-borough to give you, Ladislaw. You’d never get elected, you know. And we shall always want talent in the House: reform as we will, we shall always want talent. That avalanche and the thunder, now, was really a little like Burke. I want that sort of thing—not ideas, you know, but a way of putting them.”\n\n“Pocket-boroughs would be a fine thing,” said Ladislaw, “if they were always in the right pocket, and there were always a Burke at hand.”\n\nWill was not displeased with that complimentary comparison, even from Mr. Brooke; for it is a little too trying to human flesh to be conscious of expressing one’s self better than others and never to have it noticed, and in the general dearth of admiration for the right thing, even a chance bray of applause falling exactly in time is rather fortifying. Will felt that his literary refinements were usually beyond the limits of Middlemarch perception; nevertheless, he was beginning thoroughly to like the work of which when he began he had said to himself rather languidly, “Why not?”—and he studied the political situation with as ardent an interest as he had ever given to poetic metres or mediaevalism. It is undeniable that but for the desire to be where Dorothea was, and perhaps the want of knowing what else to do, Will would not at this time have been meditating on the needs of the English people or criticising English statesmanship: he would probably have been rambling in Italy sketching plans for several dramas, trying prose and finding it too jejune, trying verse and finding it too artificial, beginning to copy “bits” from old pictures, leaving off because they were “no good,” and observing that, after all, self-culture was the principal point; while in politics he would have been sympathizing warmly with liberty and progress in general. Our sense of duty must often wait for some work which shall take the place of dilettanteism and make us feel that the quality of our action is not a matter of indifference.\n\nLadislaw had now accepted his bit of work, though it was not that indeterminate loftiest thing which he had once dreamed of as alone worthy of continuous effort. His nature warmed easily in the presence of subjects which were visibly mixed with life and action, and the easily stirred rebellion in him helped the glow of public spirit. In spite of Mr. Casaubon and the banishment from Lowick, he was rather happy; getting a great deal of fresh knowledge in a vivid way and for practical purposes, and making the “Pioneer” celebrated as far as Brassing (never mind the smallness of the area; the writing was not worse than much that reaches the four corners of the earth).\n\nMr. Brooke was occasionally irritating; but Will’s impatience was relieved by the division of his time between visits to the Grange and retreats to his Middlemarch lodgings, which gave variety to his life.\n\n“Shift the pegs a little,” he said to himself, “and Mr. Brooke might be in the Cabinet, while I was Under-Secretary. That is the common order of things: the little waves make the large ones and are of the same pattern. I am better here than in the sort of life Mr. Casaubon would have trained me for, where the doing would be all laid down by a precedent too rigid for me to react upon. I don’t care for prestige or high pay.”\n\nAs Lydgate had said of him, he was a sort of gypsy, rather enjoying the sense of belonging to no class; he had a feeling of romance in his position, and a pleasant consciousness of creating a little surprise wherever he went. That sort of enjoyment had been disturbed when he had felt some new distance between himself and Dorothea in their accidental meeting at Lydgate’s, and his irritation had gone out towards Mr. Casaubon, who had declared beforehand that Will would lose caste. “I never had any caste,” he would have said, if that prophecy had been uttered to him, and the quick blood would have come and gone like breath in his transparent skin. But it is one thing to like defiance, and another thing to like its consequences.\n\nMeanwhile, the town opinion about the new editor of the “Pioneer” was tending to confirm Mr. Casaubon’s view. Will’s relationship in that distinguished quarter did not, like Lydgate’s high connections, serve as an advantageous introduction: if it was rumored that young Ladislaw was Mr. Casaubon’s nephew or cousin, it was also rumored that “Mr. Casaubon would have nothing to do with him.”\n\n“Brooke has taken him up,” said Mr. Hawley, “because that is what no man in his senses could have expected. Casaubon has devilish good reasons, you may be sure, for turning the cold shoulder on a young fellow whose bringing-up he paid for. Just like Brooke—one of those fellows who would praise a cat to sell a horse.”\n\nAnd some oddities of Will’s, more or less poetical, appeared to support Mr. Keck, the editor of the “Trumpet,” in asserting that Ladislaw, if the truth were known, was not only a Polish emissary but crack-brained, which accounted for the preternatural quickness and glibness of his speech when he got on to a platform—as he did whenever he had an opportunity, speaking with a facility which cast reflections on solid Englishmen generally. It was disgusting to Keck to see a strip of a fellow, with light curls round his head, get up and speechify by the hour against institutions “which had existed when he was in his cradle.” And in a leading article of the “Trumpet,” Keck characterized Ladislaw’s speech at a Reform meeting as “the violence of an energumen—a miserable effort to shroud in the brilliancy of fireworks the daring of irresponsible statements and the poverty of a knowledge which was of the cheapest and most recent description.”\n\n“That was a rattling article yesterday, Keck,” said Dr. Sprague, with sarcastic intentions. “But what is an energumen?”\n\n“Oh, a term that came up in the French Revolution,” said Keck.\n\nThis dangerous aspect of Ladislaw was strangely contrasted with other habits which became matter of remark. He had a fondness, half artistic, half affectionate, for little children—the smaller they were on tolerably active legs, and the funnier their clothing, the better Will liked to surprise and please them. We know that in Rome he was given to ramble about among the poor people, and the taste did not quit him in Middlemarch.\n\nHe had somehow picked up a troop of droll children, little hatless boys with their galligaskins much worn and scant shirting to hang out, little girls who tossed their hair out of their eyes to look at him, and guardian brothers at the mature age of seven. This troop he had led out on gypsy excursions to Halsell Wood at nutting-time, and since the cold weather had set in he had taken them on a clear day to gather sticks for a bonfire in the hollow of a hillside, where he drew out a small feast of gingerbread for them, and improvised a Punch-and-Judy drama with some private home-made puppets. Here was one oddity. Another was, that in houses where he got friendly, he was given to stretch himself at full length on the rug while he talked, and was apt to be discovered in this attitude by occasional callers for whom such an irregularity was likely to confirm the notions of his dangerously mixed blood and general laxity.\n\nBut Will’s articles and speeches naturally recommended him in families which the new strictness of party division had marked off on the side of Reform. He was invited to Mr. Bulstrode’s; but here he could not lie down on the rug, and Mrs. Bulstrode felt that his mode of talking about Catholic countries, as if there were any truce with Antichrist, illustrated the usual tendency to unsoundness in intellectual men.\n\nAt Mr. Farebrother’s, however, whom the irony of events had brought on the same side with Bulstrode in the national movement, Will became a favorite with the ladies; especially with little Miss Noble, whom it was one of his oddities to escort when he met her in the street with her little basket, giving her his arm in the eyes of the town, and insisting on going with her to pay some call where she distributed her small filchings from her own share of sweet things.\n\nBut the house where he visited oftenest and lay most on the rug was Lydgate’s. The two men were not at all alike, but they agreed none the worse. Lydgate was abrupt but not irritable, taking little notice of megrims in healthy people; and Ladislaw did not usually throw away his susceptibilities on those who took no notice of them. With Rosamond, on the other hand, he pouted and was wayward—nay, often uncomplimentary, much to her inward surprise; nevertheless he was gradually becoming necessary to her entertainment by his companionship in her music, his varied talk, and his freedom from the grave preoccupation which, with all her husband’s tenderness and indulgence, often made his manners unsatisfactory to her, and confirmed her dislike of the medical profession.\n\nLydgate, inclined to be sarcastic on the superstitious faith of the people in the efficacy of “the bill,” while nobody cared about the low state of pathology, sometimes assailed Will with troublesome questions. One evening in March, Rosamond in her cherry-colored dress with swansdown trimming about the throat sat at the tea-table; Lydgate, lately come in tired from his outdoor work, was seated sideways on an easy-chair by the fire with one leg over the elbow, his brow looking a little troubled as his eyes rambled over the columns of the “Pioneer,” while Rosamond, having noticed that he was perturbed, avoided looking at him, and inwardly thanked heaven that she herself had not a moody disposition. Will Ladislaw was stretched on the rug contemplating the curtain-pole abstractedly, and humming very low the notes of “When first I saw thy face;” while the house spaniel, also stretched out with small choice of room, looked from between his paws at the usurper of the rug with silent but strong objection.\n\nRosamond bringing Lydgate his cup of tea, he threw down the paper, and said to Will, who had started up and gone to the table—\n\n“It’s no use your puffing Brooke as a reforming landlord, Ladislaw: they only pick the more holes in his coat in the ‘Trumpet.’”\n\n“No matter; those who read the ‘Pioneer’ don’t read the ‘Trumpet,’” said Will, swallowing his tea and walking about. “Do you suppose the public reads with a view to its own conversion? We should have a witches’ brewing with a vengeance then—‘Mingle, mingle, mingle, mingle, You that mingle may’—and nobody would know which side he was going to take.”\n\n“Farebrother says, he doesn’t believe Brooke would get elected if the opportunity came: the very men who profess to be for him would bring another member out of the bag at the right moment.”\n\n“There’s no harm in trying. It’s good to have resident members.”\n\n“Why?” said Lydgate, who was much given to use that inconvenient word in a curt tone.\n\n“They represent the local stupidity better,” said Will, laughing, and shaking his curls; “and they are kept on their best behavior in the neighborhood. Brooke is not a bad fellow, but he has done some good things on his estate that he never would have done but for this Parliamentary bite.”\n\n“He’s not fitted to be a public man,” said Lydgate, with contemptuous decision. “He would disappoint everybody who counted on him: I can see that at the Hospital. Only, there Bulstrode holds the reins and drives him.”\n\n“That depends on how you fix your standard of public men,” said Will. “He’s good enough for the occasion: when the people have made up their mind as they are making it up now, they don’t want a man—they only want a vote.”\n\n“That is the way with you political writers, Ladislaw—crying up a measure as if it were a universal cure, and crying up men who are a part of the very disease that wants curing.”\n\n“Why not? Men may help to cure themselves off the face of the land without knowing it,” said Will, who could find reasons impromptu, when he had not thought of a question beforehand.\n\n“That is no excuse for encouraging the superstitious exaggeration of hopes about this particular measure, helping the cry to swallow it whole and to send up voting popinjays who are good for nothing but to carry it. You go against rottenness, and there is nothing more thoroughly rotten than making people believe that society can be cured by a political hocus-pocus.”\n\n“That’s very fine, my dear fellow. But your cure must begin somewhere, and put it that a thousand things which debase a population can never be reformed without this particular reform to begin with. Look what Stanley said the other day—that the House had been tinkering long enough at small questions of bribery, inquiring whether this or that voter has had a guinea when everybody knows that the seats have been sold wholesale. Wait for wisdom and conscience in public agents—fiddlestick! The only conscience we can trust to is the massive sense of wrong in a class, and the best wisdom that will work is the wisdom of balancing claims. That’s my text—which side is injured? I support the man who supports their claims; not the virtuous upholder of the wrong.”\n\n“That general talk about a particular case is mere question begging, Ladislaw. When I say, I go in for the dose that cures, it doesn’t follow that I go in for opium in a given case of gout.”\n\n“I am not begging the question we are upon—whether we are to try for nothing till we find immaculate men to work with. Should you go on that plan? If there were one man who would carry you a medical reform and another who would oppose it, should you inquire which had the better motives or even the better brains?”\n\n“Oh, of course,” said Lydgate, seeing himself checkmated by a move which he had often used himself, “if one did not work with such men as are at hand, things must come to a dead-lock. Suppose the worst opinion in the town about Bulstrode were a true one, that would not make it less true that he has the sense and the resolution to do what I think ought to be done in the matters I know and care most about; but that is the only ground on which I go with him,” Lydgate added rather proudly, bearing in mind Mr. Farebrother’s remarks. “He is nothing to me otherwise; I would not cry him up on any personal ground—I would keep clear of that.”\n\n“Do you mean that I cry up Brooke on any personal ground?” said Will Ladislaw, nettled, and turning sharp round. For the first time he felt offended with Lydgate; not the less so, perhaps, because he would have declined any close inquiry into the growth of his relation to Mr. Brooke.\n\n“Not at all,” said Lydgate, “I was simply explaining my own action. I meant that a man may work for a special end with others whose motives and general course are equivocal, if he is quite sure of his personal independence, and that he is not working for his private interest—either place or money.”\n\n“Then, why don’t you extend your liberality to others?” said Will, still nettled. “My personal independence is as important to me as yours is to you. You have no more reason to imagine that I have personal expectations from Brooke, than I have to imagine that you have personal expectations from Bulstrode. Motives are points of honor, I suppose—nobody can prove them. But as to money and place in the world,” Will ended, tossing back his head, “I think it is pretty clear that I am not determined by considerations of that sort.”\n\n“You quite mistake me, Ladislaw,” said Lydgate, surprised. He had been preoccupied with his own vindication, and had been blind to what Ladislaw might infer on his own account. “I beg your pardon for unintentionally annoying you. In fact, I should rather attribute to you a romantic disregard of your own worldly interests. On the political question, I referred simply to intellectual bias.”\n\n“How very unpleasant you both are this evening!” said Rosamond. “I cannot conceive why money should have been referred to. Politics and Medicine are sufficiently disagreeable to quarrel upon. You can both of you go on quarrelling with all the world and with each other on those two topics.”\n\nRosamond looked mildly neutral as she said this, rising to ring the bell, and then crossing to her work-table.\n\n“Poor Rosy!” said Lydgate, putting out his hand to her as she was passing him. “Disputation is not amusing to cherubs. Have some music. Ask Ladislaw to sing with you.”\n\nWhen Will was gone Rosamond said to her husband, “What put you out of temper this evening, Tertius?”\n\n“Me? It was Ladislaw who was out of temper. He is like a bit of tinder.”\n\n“But I mean, before that. Something had vexed you before you came in, you looked cross. And that made you begin to dispute with Mr. Ladislaw. You hurt me very much when you look so, Tertius.”\n\n“Do I? Then I am a brute,” said Lydgate, caressing her penitently.\n\n“What vexed you?”\n\n“Oh, outdoor things—business.” It was really a letter insisting on the payment of a bill for furniture. But Rosamond was expecting to have a baby, and Lydgate wished to save her from any perturbation.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII.\n\nWas never true love loved in vain, For truest love is highest gain. No art can make it: it must spring Where elements are fostering. So in heaven’s spot and hour Springs the little native flower, Downward root and upward eye, Shapen by the earth and sky.\n\nIt happened to be on a Saturday evening that Will Ladislaw had that little discussion with Lydgate. Its effect when he went to his own rooms was to make him sit up half the night, thinking over again, under a new irritation, all that he had before thought of his having settled in Middlemarch and harnessed himself with Mr. Brooke. Hesitations before he had taken the step had since turned into susceptibility to every hint that he would have been wiser not to take it; and hence came his heat towards Lydgate—a heat which still kept him restless. Was he not making a fool of himself?—and at a time when he was more than ever conscious of being something better than a fool? And for what end?\n\nWell, for no definite end. True, he had dreamy visions of possibilities: there is no human being who having both passions and thoughts does not think in consequence of his passions—does not find images rising in his mind which soothe the passion with hope or sting it with dread. But this, which happens to us all, happens to some with a wide difference; and Will was not one of those whose wit “keeps the roadway:” he had his bypaths where there were little joys of his own choosing, such as gentlemen cantering on the highroad might have thought rather idiotic. The way in which he made a sort of happiness for himself out of his feeling for Dorothea was an example of this. It may seem strange, but it is the fact, that the ordinary vulgar vision of which Mr. Casaubon suspected him—namely, that Dorothea might become a widow, and that the interest he had established in her mind might turn into acceptance of him as a husband—had no tempting, arresting power over him; he did not live in the scenery of such an event, and follow it out, as we all do with that imagined “otherwise” which is our practical heaven. It was not only that he was unwilling to entertain thoughts which could be accused of baseness, and was already uneasy in the sense that he had to justify himself from the charge of ingratitude—the latent consciousness of many other barriers between himself and Dorothea besides the existence of her husband, had helped to turn away his imagination from speculating on what might befall Mr. Casaubon. And there were yet other reasons. Will, we know, could not bear the thought of any flaw appearing in his crystal: he was at once exasperated and delighted by the calm freedom with which Dorothea looked at him and spoke to him, and there was something so exquisite in thinking of her just as she was, that he could not long for a change which must somehow change her. Do we not shun the street version of a fine melody?—or shrink from the news that the rarity—some bit of chiselling or engraving perhaps—which we have dwelt on even with exultation in the trouble it has cost us to snatch glimpses of it, is really not an uncommon thing, and may be obtained as an every-day possession? Our good depends on the quality and breadth of our emotion; and to Will, a creature who cared little for what are called the solid things of life and greatly for its subtler influences, to have within him such a feeling as he had towards Dorothea, was like the inheritance of a fortune. What others might have called the futility of his passion, made an additional delight for his imagination: he was conscious of a generous movement, and of verifying in his own experience that higher love-poetry which had charmed his fancy. Dorothea, he said to himself, was forever enthroned in his soul: no other woman could sit higher than her footstool; and if he could have written out in immortal syllables the effect she wrought within him, he might have boasted after the example of old Drayton, that,—\n\n“Queens hereafter might be glad to live Upon the alms of her superfluous praise.”\n\nBut this result was questionable. And what else could he do for Dorothea? What was his devotion worth to her? It was impossible to tell. He would not go out of her reach. He saw no creature among her friends to whom he could believe that she spoke with the same simple confidence as to him. She had once said that she would like him to stay; and stay he would, whatever fire-breathing dragons might hiss around her.\n\nThis had always been the conclusion of Will’s hesitations. But he was not without contradictoriness and rebellion even towards his own resolve. He had often got irritated, as he was on this particular night, by some outside demonstration that his public exertions with Mr. Brooke as a chief could not seem as heroic as he would like them to be, and this was always associated with the other ground of irritation—that notwithstanding his sacrifice of dignity for Dorothea’s sake, he could hardly ever see her. Whereupon, not being able to contradict these unpleasant facts, he contradicted his own strongest bias and said, “I am a fool.”\n\nNevertheless, since the inward debate necessarily turned on Dorothea, he ended, as he had done before, only by getting a livelier sense of what her presence would be to him; and suddenly reflecting that the morrow would be Sunday, he determined to go to Lowick Church and see her. He slept upon that idea, but when he was dressing in the rational morning light, Objection said—\n\n“That will be a virtual defiance of Mr. Casaubon’s prohibition to visit Lowick, and Dorothea will be displeased.”\n\n“Nonsense!” argued Inclination, “it would be too monstrous for him to hinder me from going out to a pretty country church on a spring morning. And Dorothea will be glad.”\n\n“It will be clear to Mr. Casaubon that you have come either to annoy him or to see Dorothea.”\n\n“It is not true that I go to annoy him, and why should I not go to see Dorothea? Is he to have everything to himself and be always comfortable? Let him smart a little, as other people are obliged to do. I have always liked the quaintness of the church and congregation; besides, I know the Tuckers: I shall go into their pew.”\n\nHaving silenced Objection by force of unreason, Will walked to Lowick as if he had been on the way to Paradise, crossing Halsell Common and skirting the wood, where the sunlight fell broadly under the budding boughs, bringing out the beauties of moss and lichen, and fresh green growths piercing the brown. Everything seemed to know that it was Sunday, and to approve of his going to Lowick Church. Will easily felt happy when nothing crossed his humor, and by this time the thought of vexing Mr. Casaubon had become rather amusing to him, making his face break into its merry smile, pleasant to see as the breaking of sunshine on the water—though the occasion was not exemplary. But most of us are apt to settle within ourselves that the man who blocks our way is odious, and not to mind causing him a little of the disgust which his personality excites in ourselves. Will went along with a small book under his arm and a hand in each side-pocket, never reading, but chanting a little, as he made scenes of what would happen in church and coming out. He was experimenting in tunes to suit some words of his own, sometimes trying a ready-made melody, sometimes improvising. The words were not exactly a hymn, but they certainly fitted his Sunday experience:—\n\n“O me, O me, what frugal cheer My love doth feed upon! A touch, a ray, that is not here, A shadow that is gone:\n\n“A dream of breath that might be near, An inly-echoed tone, The thought that one may think me dear, The place where one was known,\n\n“The tremor of a banished fear, An ill that was not done— O me, O me, what frugal cheer My love doth feed upon!”\n\nSometimes, when he took off his hat, shaking his head backward, and showing his delicate throat as he sang, he looked like an incarnation of the spring whose spirit filled the air—a bright creature, abundant in uncertain promises.\n\nThe bells were still ringing when he got to Lowick, and he went into the curate’s pew before any one else arrived there. But he was still left alone in it when the congregation had assembled. The curate’s pew was opposite the rector’s at the entrance of the small chancel, and Will had time to fear that Dorothea might not come while he looked round at the group of rural faces which made the congregation from year to year within the white-washed walls and dark old pews, hardly with more change than we see in the boughs of a tree which breaks here and there with age, but yet has young shoots. Mr. Rigg’s frog-face was something alien and unaccountable, but notwithstanding this shock to the order of things, there were still the Waules and the rural stock of the Powderells in their pews side by side; brother Samuel’s cheek had the same purple round as ever, and the three generations of decent cottagers came as of old with a sense of duty to their betters generally—the smaller children regarding Mr. Casaubon, who wore the black gown and mounted to the highest box, as probably the chief of all betters, and the one most awful if offended. Even in 1831 Lowick was at peace, not more agitated by Reform than by the solemn tenor of the Sunday sermon. The congregation had been used to seeing Will at church in former days, and no one took much note of him except the choir, who expected him to make a figure in the singing.\n\nDorothea did at last appear on this quaint background, walking up the short aisle in her white beaver bonnet and gray cloak—the same she had worn in the Vatican. Her face being, from her entrance, towards the chancel, even her shortsighted eyes soon discerned Will, but there was no outward show of her feeling except a slight paleness and a grave bow as she passed him. To his own surprise Will felt suddenly uncomfortable, and dared not look at her after they had bowed to each other. Two minutes later, when Mr. Casaubon came out of the vestry, and, entering the pew, seated himself in face of Dorothea, Will felt his paralysis more complete. He could look nowhere except at the choir in the little gallery over the vestry-door: Dorothea was perhaps pained, and he had made a wretched blunder. It was no longer amusing to vex Mr. Casaubon, who had the advantage probably of watching him and seeing that he dared not turn his head. Why had he not imagined this beforehand?—but he could not expect that he should sit in that square pew alone, unrelieved by any Tuckers, who had apparently departed from Lowick altogether, for a new clergyman was in the desk. Still he called himself stupid now for not foreseeing that it would be impossible for him to look towards Dorothea—nay, that she might feel his coming an impertinence. There was no delivering himself from his cage, however; and Will found his places and looked at his book as if he had been a school-mistress, feeling that the morning service had never been so immeasurably long before, that he was utterly ridiculous, out of temper, and miserable. This was what a man got by worshipping the sight of a woman! The clerk observed with surprise that Mr. Ladislaw did not join in the tune of Hanover, and reflected that he might have a cold.\n\nMr. Casaubon did not preach that morning, and there was no change in Will’s situation until the blessing had been pronounced and every one rose. It was the fashion at Lowick for “the betters” to go out first. With a sudden determination to break the spell that was upon him, Will looked straight at Mr. Casaubon. But that gentleman’s eyes were on the button of the pew-door, which he opened, allowing Dorothea to pass, and following her immediately without raising his eyelids. Will’s glance had caught Dorothea’s as she turned out of the pew, and again she bowed, but this time with a look of agitation, as if she were repressing tears. Will walked out after them, but they went on towards the little gate leading out of the churchyard into the shrubbery, never looking round.\n\nIt was impossible for him to follow them, and he could only walk back sadly at mid-day along the same road which he had trodden hopefully in the morning. The lights were all changed for him both without and within.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII.\n\nSurely the golden hours are turning gray And dance no more, and vainly strive to run: I see their white locks streaming in the wind— Each face is haggard as it looks at me, Slow turning in the constant clasping round Storm-driven.\n\nDorothea’s distress when she was leaving the church came chiefly from the perception that Mr. Casaubon was determined not to speak to his cousin, and that Will’s presence at church had served to mark more strongly the alienation between them. Will’s coming seemed to her quite excusable, nay, she thought it an amiable movement in him towards a reconciliation which she herself had been constantly wishing for. He had probably imagined, as she had, that if Mr. Casaubon and he could meet easily, they would shake hands and friendly intercourse might return. But now Dorothea felt quite robbed of that hope. Will was banished further than ever, for Mr. Casaubon must have been newly embittered by this thrusting upon him of a presence which he refused to recognize.\n\nHe had not been very well that morning, suffering from some difficulty in breathing, and had not preached in consequence; she was not surprised, therefore, that he was nearly silent at luncheon, still less that he made no allusion to Will Ladislaw. For her own part she felt that she could never again introduce that subject. They usually spent apart the hours between luncheon and dinner on a Sunday; Mr. Casaubon in the library dozing chiefly, and Dorothea in her boudoir, where she was wont to occupy herself with some of her favorite books. There was a little heap of them on the table in the bow-window—of various sorts, from Herodotus, which she was learning to read with Mr. Casaubon, to her old companion Pascal, and Keble’s “Christian Year.” But to-day she opened one after another, and could read none of them. Everything seemed dreary: the portents before the birth of Cyrus—Jewish antiquities—oh dear!—devout epigrams—the sacred chime of favorite hymns—all alike were as flat as tunes beaten on wood: even the spring flowers and the grass had a dull shiver in them under the afternoon clouds that hid the sun fitfully; even the sustaining thoughts which had become habits seemed to have in them the weariness of long future days in which she would still live with them for her sole companions. It was another or rather a fuller sort of companionship that poor Dorothea was hungering for, and the hunger had grown from the perpetual effort demanded by her married life. She was always trying to be what her husband wished, and never able to repose on his delight in what she was. The thing that she liked, that she spontaneously cared to have, seemed to be always excluded from her life; for if it was only granted and not shared by her husband it might as well have been denied. About Will Ladislaw there had been a difference between them from the first, and it had ended, since Mr. Casaubon had so severely repulsed Dorothea’s strong feeling about his claims on the family property, by her being convinced that she was in the right and her husband in the wrong, but that she was helpless. This afternoon the helplessness was more wretchedly benumbing than ever: she longed for objects who could be dear to her, and to whom she could be dear. She longed for work which would be directly beneficent like the sunshine and the rain, and now it appeared that she was to live more and more in a virtual tomb, where there was the apparatus of a ghastly labor producing what would never see the light. Today she had stood at the door of the tomb and seen Will Ladislaw receding into the distant world of warm activity and fellowship—turning his face towards her as he went.\n\nBooks were of no use. Thinking was of no use. It was Sunday, and she could not have the carriage to go to Celia, who had lately had a baby. There was no refuge now from spiritual emptiness and discontent, and Dorothea had to bear her bad mood, as she would have borne a headache.\n\nAfter dinner, at the hour when she usually began to read aloud, Mr. Casaubon proposed that they should go into the library, where, he said, he had ordered a fire and lights. He seemed to have revived, and to be thinking intently.\n\nIn the library Dorothea observed that he had newly arranged a row of his note-books on a table, and now he took up and put into her hand a well-known volume, which was a table of contents to all the others.\n\n“You will oblige me, my dear,” he said, seating himself, “if instead of other reading this evening, you will go through this aloud, pencil in hand, and at each point where I say ‘mark,’ will make a cross with your pencil. This is the first step in a sifting process which I have long had in view, and as we go on I shall be able to indicate to you certain principles of selection whereby you will, I trust, have an intelligent participation in my purpose.”\n\nThis proposal was only one more sign added to many since his memorable interview with Lydgate, that Mr. Casaubon’s original reluctance to let Dorothea work with him had given place to the contrary disposition, namely, to demand much interest and labor from her.\n\nAfter she had read and marked for two hours, he said, “We will take the volume up-stairs—and the pencil, if you please—and in case of reading in the night, we can pursue this task. It is not wearisome to you, I trust, Dorothea?”\n\n“I prefer always reading what you like best to hear,” said Dorothea, who told the simple truth; for what she dreaded was to exert herself in reading or anything else which left him as joyless as ever.\n\nIt was a proof of the force with which certain characteristics in Dorothea impressed those around her, that her husband, with all his jealousy and suspicion, had gathered implicit trust in the integrity of her promises, and her power of devoting herself to her idea of the right and best. Of late he had begun to feel that these qualities were a peculiar possession for himself, and he wanted to engross them.\n\nThe reading in the night did come. Dorothea in her young weariness had slept soon and fast: she was awakened by a sense of light, which seemed to her at first like a sudden vision of sunset after she had climbed a steep hill: she opened her eyes and saw her husband wrapped in his warm gown seating himself in the arm-chair near the fire-place where the embers were still glowing. He had lit two candles, expecting that Dorothea would awake, but not liking to rouse her by more direct means.\n\n“Are you ill, Edward?” she said, rising immediately.\n\n“I felt some uneasiness in a reclining posture. I will sit here for a time.” She threw wood on the fire, wrapped herself up, and said, “You would like me to read to you?”\n\n“You would oblige me greatly by doing so, Dorothea,” said Mr. Casaubon, with a shade more meekness than usual in his polite manner. “I am wakeful: my mind is remarkably lucid.”\n\n“I fear that the excitement may be too great for you,” said Dorothea, remembering Lydgate’s cautions.\n\n“No, I am not conscious of undue excitement. Thought is easy.” Dorothea dared not insist, and she read for an hour or more on the same plan as she had done in the evening, but getting over the pages with more quickness. Mr. Casaubon’s mind was more alert, and he seemed to anticipate what was coming after a very slight verbal indication, saying, “That will do—mark that”—or “Pass on to the next head—I omit the second excursus on Crete.” Dorothea was amazed to think of the bird-like speed with which his mind was surveying the ground where it had been creeping for years. At last he said—\n\n“Close the book now, my dear. We will resume our work to-morrow. I have deferred it too long, and would gladly see it completed. But you observe that the principle on which my selection is made, is to give adequate, and not disproportionate illustration to each of the theses enumerated in my introduction, as at present sketched. You have perceived that distinctly, Dorothea?”\n\n“Yes,” said Dorothea, rather tremulously. She felt sick at heart.\n\n“And now I think that I can take some repose,” said Mr. Casaubon. He laid down again and begged her to put out the lights. When she had lain down too, and there was a darkness only broken by a dull glow on the hearth, he said—\n\n“Before I sleep, I have a request to make, Dorothea.”\n\n“What is it?” said Dorothea, with dread in her mind.\n\n“It is that you will let me know, deliberately, whether, in case of my death, you will carry out my wishes: whether you will avoid doing what I should deprecate, and apply yourself to do what I should desire.”\n\nDorothea was not taken by surprise: many incidents had been leading her to the conjecture of some intention on her husband’s part which might make a new yoke for her. She did not answer immediately.\n\n“You refuse?” said Mr. Casaubon, with more edge in his tone.\n\n“No, I do not yet refuse,” said Dorothea, in a clear voice, the need of freedom asserting itself within her; “but it is too solemn—I think it is not right—to make a promise when I am ignorant what it will bind me to. Whatever affection prompted I would do without promising.”\n\n“But you would use your own judgment: I ask you to obey mine; you refuse.”\n\n“No, dear, no!” said Dorothea, beseechingly, crushed by opposing fears. “But may I wait and reflect a little while? I desire with my whole soul to do what will comfort you; but I cannot give any pledge suddenly—still less a pledge to do I know not what.”\n\n“You cannot then confide in the nature of my wishes?”\n\n“Grant me till to-morrow,” said Dorothea, beseechingly.\n\n“Till to-morrow then,” said Mr. Casaubon.\n\nSoon she could hear that he was sleeping, but there was no more sleep for her. While she constrained herself to lie still lest she should disturb him, her mind was carrying on a conflict in which imagination ranged its forces first on one side and then on the other. She had no presentiment that the power which her husband wished to establish over her future action had relation to anything else than his work. But it was clear enough to her that he would expect her to devote herself to sifting those mixed heaps of material, which were to be the doubtful illustration of principles still more doubtful. The poor child had become altogether unbelieving as to the trustworthiness of that Key which had made the ambition and the labor of her husband’s life. It was not wonderful that, in spite of her small instruction, her judgment in this matter was truer than his: for she looked with unbiassed comparison and healthy sense at probabilities on which he had risked all his egoism. And now she pictured to herself the days, and months, and years which she must spend in sorting what might be called shattered mummies, and fragments of a tradition which was itself a mosaic wrought from crushed ruins—sorting them as food for a theory which was already withered in the birth like an elfin child. Doubtless a vigorous error vigorously pursued has kept the embryos of truth a-breathing: the quest of gold being at the same time a questioning of substances, the body of chemistry is prepared for its soul, and Lavoisier is born. But Mr. Casaubon’s theory of the elements which made the seed of all tradition was not likely to bruise itself unawares against discoveries: it floated among flexible conjectures no more solid than those etymologies which seemed strong because of likeness in sound until it was shown that likeness in sound made them impossible: it was a method of interpretation which was not tested by the necessity of forming anything which had sharper collisions than an elaborate notion of Gog and Magog: it was as free from interruption as a plan for threading the stars together. And Dorothea had so often had to check her weariness and impatience over this questionable riddle-guessing, as it revealed itself to her instead of the fellowship in high knowledge which was to make life worthier! She could understand well enough now why her husband had come to cling to her, as possibly the only hope left that his labors would ever take a shape in which they could be given to the world. At first it had seemed that he wished to keep even her aloof from any close knowledge of what he was doing; but gradually the terrible stringency of human need—the prospect of a too speedy death—\n\nAnd here Dorothea’s pity turned from her own future to her husband’s past—nay, to his present hard struggle with a lot which had grown out of that past: the lonely labor, the ambition breathing hardly under the pressure of self-distrust; the goal receding, and the heavier limbs; and now at last the sword visibly trembling above him! And had she not wished to marry him that she might help him in his life’s labor?—But she had thought the work was to be something greater, which she could serve in devoutly for its own sake. Was it right, even to soothe his grief—would it be possible, even if she promised—to work as in a treadmill fruitlessly?\n\nAnd yet, could she deny him? Could she say, “I refuse to content this pining hunger?” It would be refusing to do for him dead, what she was almost sure to do for him living. If he lived as Lydgate had said he might, for fifteen years or more, her life would certainly be spent in helping him and obeying him.\n\nStill, there was a deep difference between that devotion to the living and that indefinite promise of devotion to the dead. While he lived, he could claim nothing that she would not still be free to remonstrate against, and even to refuse. But—the thought passed through her mind more than once, though she could not believe in it—might he not mean to demand something more from her than she had been able to imagine, since he wanted her pledge to carry out his wishes without telling her exactly what they were? No; his heart was bound up in his work only: that was the end for which his failing life was to be eked out by hers.\n\nAnd now, if she were to say, “No! if you die, I will put no finger to your work”—it seemed as if she would be crushing that bruised heart.\n\nFor four hours Dorothea lay in this conflict, till she felt ill and bewildered, unable to resolve, praying mutely. Helpless as a child which has sobbed and sought too long, she fell into a late morning sleep, and when she waked Mr. Casaubon was already up. Tantripp told her that he had read prayers, breakfasted, and was in the library.\n\n“I never saw you look so pale, madam,” said Tantripp, a solid-figured woman who had been with the sisters at Lausanne.\n\n“Was I ever high-colored, Tantripp?” said Dorothea, smiling faintly.\n\n“Well, not to say high-colored, but with a bloom like a Chiny rose. But always smelling those leather books, what can be expected? Do rest a little this morning, madam. Let me say you are ill and not able to go into that close library.”\n\n“Oh no, no! let me make haste,” said Dorothea. “Mr. Casaubon wants me particularly.”\n\nWhen she went down she felt sure that she should promise to fulfil his wishes; but that would be later in the day—not yet.\n\nAs Dorothea entered the library, Mr. Casaubon turned round from the table where he had been placing some books, and said—\n\n“I was waiting for your appearance, my dear. I had hoped to set to work at once this morning, but I find myself under some indisposition, probably from too much excitement yesterday. I am going now to take a turn in the shrubbery, since the air is milder.”\n\n“I am glad to hear that,” said Dorothea. “Your mind, I feared, was too active last night.”\n\n“I would fain have it set at rest on the point I last spoke of, Dorothea. You can now, I hope, give me an answer.”\n\n“May I come out to you in the garden presently?” said Dorothea, winning a little breathing space in that way.\n\n“I shall be in the Yew-tree Walk for the next half-hour,” said Mr. Casaubon, and then he left her.\n\nDorothea, feeling very weary, rang and asked Tantripp to bring her some wraps. She had been sitting still for a few minutes, but not in any renewal of the former conflict: she simply felt that she was going to say “Yes” to her own doom: she was too weak, too full of dread at the thought of inflicting a keen-edged blow on her husband, to do anything but submit completely. She sat still and let Tantripp put on her bonnet and shawl, a passivity which was unusual with her, for she liked to wait on herself.\n\n“God bless you, madam!” said Tantripp, with an irrepressible movement of love towards the beautiful, gentle creature for whom she felt unable to do anything more, now that she had finished tying the bonnet.\n\nThis was too much for Dorothea’s highly-strung feeling, and she burst into tears, sobbing against Tantripp’s arm. But soon she checked herself, dried her eyes, and went out at the glass door into the shrubbery.\n\n“I wish every book in that library was built into a caticom for your master,” said Tantripp to Pratt, the butler, finding him in the breakfast-room. She had been at Rome, and visited the antiquities, as we know; and she always declined to call Mr. Casaubon anything but “your master,” when speaking to the other servants.\n\nPratt laughed. He liked his master very well, but he liked Tantripp better.\n\nWhen Dorothea was out on the gravel walks, she lingered among the nearer clumps of trees, hesitating, as she had done once before, though from a different cause. Then she had feared lest her effort at fellowship should be unwelcome; now she dreaded going to the spot where she foresaw that she must bind herself to a fellowship from which she shrank. Neither law nor the world’s opinion compelled her to this—only her husband’s nature and her own compassion, only the ideal and not the real yoke of marriage. She saw clearly enough the whole situation, yet she was fettered: she could not smite the stricken soul that entreated hers. If that were weakness, Dorothea was weak. But the half-hour was passing, and she must not delay longer. When she entered the Yew-tree Walk she could not see her husband; but the walk had bends, and she went, expecting to catch sight of his figure wrapped in a blue cloak, which, with a warm velvet cap, was his outer garment on chill days for the garden. It occurred to her that he might be resting in the summer-house, towards which the path diverged a little. Turning the angle, she could see him seated on the bench, close to a stone table. His arms were resting on the table, and his brow was bowed down on them, the blue cloak being dragged forward and screening his face on each side.\n\n“He exhausted himself last night,” Dorothea said to herself, thinking at first that he was asleep, and that the summer-house was too damp a place to rest in. But then she remembered that of late she had seen him take that attitude when she was reading to him, as if he found it easier than any other; and that he would sometimes speak, as well as listen, with his face down in that way. She went into the summerhouse and said, “I am come, Edward; I am ready.”\n\nHe took no notice, and she thought that he must be fast asleep. She laid her hand on his shoulder, and repeated, “I am ready!” Still he was motionless; and with a sudden confused fear, she leaned down to him, took off his velvet cap, and leaned her cheek close to his head, crying in a distressed tone—\n\n“Wake, dear, wake! Listen to me. I am come to answer.” But Dorothea never gave her answer.\n\nLater in the day, Lydgate was seated by her bedside, and she was talking deliriously, thinking aloud, and recalling what had gone through her mind the night before. She knew him, and called him by his name, but appeared to think it right that she should explain everything to him; and again, and again, begged him to explain everything to her husband.\n\n“Tell him I shall go to him soon: I am ready to promise. Only, thinking about it was so dreadful—it has made me ill. Not very ill. I shall soon be better. Go and tell him.”\n\nBut the silence in her husband’s ear was never more to be broken.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX.\n\n“A task too strong for wizard spells This squire had brought about; ’T is easy dropping stones in wells, But who shall get them out?”\n\n“I wish to God we could hinder Dorothea from knowing this,” said Sir James Chettam, with a little frown on his brow, and an expression of intense disgust about his mouth.\n\nHe was standing on the hearth-rug in the library at Lowick Grange, and speaking to Mr. Brooke. It was the day after Mr. Casaubon had been buried, and Dorothea was not yet able to leave her room.\n\n“That would be difficult, you know, Chettam, as she is an executrix, and she likes to go into these things—property, land, that kind of thing. She has her notions, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, sticking his eye-glasses on nervously, and exploring the edges of a folded paper which he held in his hand; “and she would like to act—depend upon it, as an executrix Dorothea would want to act. And she was twenty-one last December, you know. I can hinder nothing.”\n\nSir James looked at the carpet for a minute in silence, and then lifting his eyes suddenly fixed them on Mr. Brooke, saying, “I will tell you what we can do. Until Dorothea is well, all business must be kept from her, and as soon as she is able to be moved she must come to us. Being with Celia and the baby will be the best thing in the world for her, and will pass away the time. And meanwhile you must get rid of Ladislaw: you must send him out of the country.” Here Sir James’s look of disgust returned in all its intensity.\n\nMr. Brooke put his hands behind him, walked to the window and straightened his back with a little shake before he replied.\n\n“That is easily said, Chettam, easily said, you know.”\n\n“My dear sir,” persisted Sir James, restraining his indignation within respectful forms, “it was you who brought him here, and you who keep him here—I mean by the occupation you give him.”\n\n“Yes, but I can’t dismiss him in an instant without assigning reasons, my dear Chettam. Ladislaw has been invaluable, most satisfactory. I consider that I have done this part of the country a service by bringing him—by bringing him, you know.” Mr. Brooke ended with a nod, turning round to give it.\n\n“It’s a pity this part of the country didn’t do without him, that’s all I have to say about it. At any rate, as Dorothea’s brother-in-law, I feel warranted in objecting strongly to his being kept here by any action on the part of her friends. You admit, I hope, that I have a right to speak about what concerns the dignity of my wife’s sister?”\n\nSir James was getting warm.\n\n“Of course, my dear Chettam, of course. But you and I have different ideas—different—”\n\n“Not about this action of Casaubon’s, I should hope,” interrupted Sir James. “I say that he has most unfairly compromised Dorothea. I say that there never was a meaner, more ungentlemanly action than this—a codicil of this sort to a will which he made at the time of his marriage with the knowledge and reliance of her family—a positive insult to Dorothea!”\n\n“Well, you know, Casaubon was a little twisted about Ladislaw. Ladislaw has told me the reason—dislike of the bent he took, you know—Ladislaw didn’t think much of Casaubon’s notions, Thoth and Dagon—that sort of thing: and I fancy that Casaubon didn’t like the independent position Ladislaw had taken up. I saw the letters between them, you know. Poor Casaubon was a little buried in books—he didn’t know the world.”\n\n“It’s all very well for Ladislaw to put that color on it,” said Sir James. “But I believe Casaubon was only jealous of him on Dorothea’s account, and the world will suppose that she gave him some reason; and that is what makes it so abominable—coupling her name with this young fellow’s.”\n\n“My dear Chettam, it won’t lead to anything, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, seating himself and sticking on his eye-glass again. “It’s all of a piece with Casaubon’s oddity. This paper, now, ‘Synoptical Tabulation’ and so on, ‘for the use of Mrs. Casaubon,’ it was locked up in the desk with the will. I suppose he meant Dorothea to publish his researches, eh? and she’ll do it, you know; she has gone into his studies uncommonly.”\n\n“My dear sir,” said Sir James, impatiently, “that is neither here nor there. The question is, whether you don’t see with me the propriety of sending young Ladislaw away?”\n\n“Well, no, not the urgency of the thing. By-and-by, perhaps, it may come round. As to gossip, you know, sending him away won’t hinder gossip. People say what they like to say, not what they have chapter and verse for,” said Mr Brooke, becoming acute about the truths that lay on the side of his own wishes. “I might get rid of Ladislaw up to a certain point—take away the ‘Pioneer’ from him, and that sort of thing; but I couldn’t send him out of the country if he didn’t choose to go—didn’t choose, you know.”\n\nMr. Brooke, persisting as quietly as if he were only discussing the nature of last year’s weather, and nodding at the end with his usual amenity, was an exasperating form of obstinacy.\n\n“Good God!” said Sir James, with as much passion as he ever showed, “let us get him a post; let us spend money on him. If he could go in the suite of some Colonial Governor! Grampus might take him—and I could write to Fulke about it.”\n\n“But Ladislaw won’t be shipped off like a head of cattle, my dear fellow; Ladislaw has his ideas. It’s my opinion that if he were to part from me to-morrow, you’d only hear the more of him in the country. With his talent for speaking and drawing up documents, there are few men who could come up to him as an agitator—an agitator, you know.”\n\n“Agitator!” said Sir James, with bitter emphasis, feeling that the syllables of this word properly repeated were a sufficient exposure of its hatefulness.\n\n“But be reasonable, Chettam. Dorothea, now. As you say, she had better go to Celia as soon as possible. She can stay under your roof, and in the mean time things may come round quietly. Don’t let us be firing off our guns in a hurry, you know. Standish will keep our counsel, and the news will be old before it’s known. Twenty things may happen to carry off Ladislaw—without my doing anything, you know.”\n\n“Then I am to conclude that you decline to do anything?”\n\n“Decline, Chettam?—no—I didn’t say decline. But I really don’t see what I could do. Ladislaw is a gentleman.”\n\n“I am glad to hear it!” said Sir James, his irritation making him forget himself a little. “I am sure Casaubon was not.”\n\n“Well, it would have been worse if he had made the codicil to hinder her from marrying again at all, you know.”\n\n“I don’t know that,” said Sir James. “It would have been less indelicate.”\n\n“One of poor Casaubon’s freaks! That attack upset his brain a little. It all goes for nothing. She doesn’t want to marry Ladislaw.”\n\n“But this codicil is framed so as to make everybody believe that she did. I don’t believe anything of the sort about Dorothea,” said Sir James—then frowningly, “but I suspect Ladislaw. I tell you frankly, I suspect Ladislaw.”\n\n“I couldn’t take any immediate action on that ground, Chettam. In fact, if it were possible to pack him off—send him to Norfolk Island—that sort of thing—it would look all the worse for Dorothea to those who knew about it. It would seem as if we distrusted her—distrusted her, you know.”\n\nThat Mr. Brooke had hit on an undeniable argument, did not tend to soothe Sir James. He put out his hand to reach his hat, implying that he did not mean to contend further, and said, still with some heat—\n\n“Well, I can only say that I think Dorothea was sacrificed once, because her friends were too careless. I shall do what I can, as her brother, to protect her now.”\n\n“You can’t do better than get her to Freshitt as soon as possible, Chettam. I approve that plan altogether,” said Mr. Brooke, well pleased that he had won the argument. It would have been highly inconvenient to him to part with Ladislaw at that time, when a dissolution might happen any day, and electors were to be convinced of the course by which the interests of the country would be best served. Mr. Brooke sincerely believed that this end could be secured by his own return to Parliament: he offered the forces of his mind honestly to the nation.\n\n# CHAPTER L.\n\n“This Loller here wol precilen us somewhat.” “Nay by my father’s soule! that schal he nat,” Sayde the Schipman, ‘here schal he not preche, We schal no gospel glosen here ne teche. We leven all in the gret God,’ quod he. He wolden sowen some diffcultee.”—Canterbury Tales.\n\nDorothea had been safe at Freshitt Hall nearly a week before she had asked any dangerous questions. Every morning now she sat with Celia in the prettiest of up-stairs sitting-rooms, opening into a small conservatory—Celia all in white and lavender like a bunch of mixed violets, watching the remarkable acts of the baby, which were so dubious to her inexperienced mind that all conversation was interrupted by appeals for their interpretation made to the oracular nurse. Dorothea sat by in her widow’s dress, with an expression which rather provoked Celia, as being much too sad; for not only was baby quite well, but really when a husband had been so dull and troublesome while he lived, and besides that had—well, well! Sir James, of course, had told Celia everything, with a strong representation how important it was that Dorothea should not know it sooner than was inevitable.\n\nBut Mr. Brooke had been right in predicting that Dorothea would not long remain passive where action had been assigned to her; she knew the purport of her husband’s will made at the time of their marriage, and her mind, as soon as she was clearly conscious of her position, was silently occupied with what she ought to do as the owner of Lowick Manor with the patronage of the living attached to it.\n\nOne morning when her uncle paid his usual visit, though with an unusual alacrity in his manner which he accounted for by saying that it was now pretty certain Parliament would be dissolved forthwith, Dorothea said—\n\n“Uncle, it is right now that I should consider who is to have the living at Lowick. After Mr. Tucker had been provided for, I never heard my husband say that he had any clergyman in his mind as a successor to himself. I think I ought to have the keys now and go to Lowick to examine all my husband’s papers. There may be something that would throw light on his wishes.”\n\n“No hurry, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, quietly. “By-and-by, you know, you can go, if you like. But I cast my eyes over things in the desks and drawers—there was nothing—nothing but deep subjects, you know—besides the will. Everything can be done by-and-by. As to the living, I have had an application for interest already—I should say rather good. Mr. Tyke has been strongly recommended to me—I had something to do with getting him an appointment before. An apostolic man, I believe—the sort of thing that would suit you, my dear.”\n\n“I should like to have fuller knowledge about him, uncle, and judge for myself, if Mr. Casaubon has not left any expression of his wishes. He has perhaps made some addition to his will—there may be some instructions for me,” said Dorothea, who had all the while had this conjecture in her mind with relation to her husband’s work.\n\n“Nothing about the rectory, my dear—nothing,” said Mr. Brooke, rising to go away, and putting out his hand to his nieces: “nor about his researches, you know. Nothing in the will.”\n\nDorothea’s lip quivered.\n\n“Come, you must not think of these things yet, my dear. By-and-by, you know.”\n\n“I am quite well now, uncle; I wish to exert myself.”\n\n“Well, well, we shall see. But I must run away now—I have no end of work now—it’s a crisis—a political crisis, you know. And here is Celia and her little man—you are an aunt, you know, now, and I am a sort of grandfather,” said Mr. Brooke, with placid hurry, anxious to get away and tell Chettam that it would not be his (Mr. Brooke’s) fault if Dorothea insisted on looking into everything.\n\nDorothea sank back in her chair when her uncle had left the room, and cast her eyes down meditatively on her crossed hands.\n\n“Look, Dodo! look at him! Did you ever see anything like that?” said Celia, in her comfortable staccato.\n\n“What, Kitty?” said Dorothea, lifting her eyes rather absently.\n\n“What? why, his upper lip; see how he is drawing it down, as if he meant to make a face. Isn’t it wonderful! He may have his little thoughts. I wish nurse were here. Do look at him.”\n\nA large tear which had been for some time gathering, rolled down Dorothea’s cheek as she looked up and tried to smile.\n\n“Don’t be sad, Dodo; kiss baby. What are you brooding over so? I am sure you did everything, and a great deal too much. You should be happy now.”\n\n“I wonder if Sir James would drive me to Lowick. I want to look over everything—to see if there were any words written for me.”\n\n“You are not to go till Mr. Lydgate says you may go. And he has not said so yet (here you are, nurse; take baby and walk up and down the gallery). Besides, you have got a wrong notion in your head as usual, Dodo—I can see that: it vexes me.”\n\n“Where am I wrong, Kitty?” said Dorothea, quite meekly. She was almost ready now to think Celia wiser than herself, and was really wondering with some fear what her wrong notion was. Celia felt her advantage, and was determined to use it. None of them knew Dodo as well as she did, or knew how to manage her. Since Celia’s baby was born, she had had a new sense of her mental solidity and calm wisdom. It seemed clear that where there was a baby, things were right enough, and that error, in general, was a mere lack of that central poising force.\n\n“I can see what you are thinking of as well as can be, Dodo,” said Celia. “You are wanting to find out if there is anything uncomfortable for you to do now, only because Mr. Casaubon wished it. As if you had not been uncomfortable enough before. And he doesn’t deserve it, and you will find that out. He has behaved very badly. James is as angry with him as can be. And I had better tell you, to prepare you.”\n\n“Celia,” said Dorothea, entreatingly, “you distress me. Tell me at once what you mean.” It glanced through her mind that Mr. Casaubon had left the property away from her—which would not be so very distressing.\n\n“Why, he has made a codicil to his will, to say the property was all to go away from you if you married—I mean—”\n\n“That is of no consequence,” said Dorothea, breaking in impetuously.\n\n“But if you married Mr. Ladislaw, not anybody else,” Celia went on with persevering quietude. “Of course that is of no consequence in one way—you never would marry Mr. Ladislaw; but that only makes it worse of Mr. Casaubon.”\n\nThe blood rushed to Dorothea’s face and neck painfully. But Celia was administering what she thought a sobering dose of fact. It was taking up notions that had done Dodo’s health so much harm. So she went on in her neutral tone, as if she had been remarking on baby’s robes.\n\n“James says so. He says it is abominable, and not like a gentleman. And there never was a better judge than James. It is as if Mr. Casaubon wanted to make people believe that you would wish to marry Mr. Ladislaw—which is ridiculous. Only James says it was to hinder Mr. Ladislaw from wanting to marry you for your money—just as if he ever would think of making you an offer. Mrs. Cadwallader said you might as well marry an Italian with white mice! But I must just go and look at baby,” Celia added, without the least change of tone, throwing a light shawl over her, and tripping away.\n\nDorothea by this time had turned cold again, and now threw herself back helplessly in her chair. She might have compared her experience at that moment to the vague, alarmed consciousness that her life was taking on a new form, that she was undergoing a metamorphosis in which memory would not adjust itself to the stirring of new organs. Everything was changing its aspect: her husband’s conduct, her own duteous feeling towards him, every struggle between them—and yet more, her whole relation to Will Ladislaw. Her world was in a state of convulsive change; the only thing she could say distinctly to herself was, that she must wait and think anew. One change terrified her as if it had been a sin; it was a violent shock of repulsion from her departed husband, who had had hidden thoughts, perhaps perverting everything she said and did. Then again she was conscious of another change which also made her tremulous; it was a sudden strange yearning of heart towards Will Ladislaw. It had never before entered her mind that he could, under any circumstances, be her lover: conceive the effect of the sudden revelation that another had thought of him in that light—that perhaps he himself had been conscious of such a possibility,—and this with the hurrying, crowding vision of unfitting conditions, and questions not soon to be solved.\n\nIt seemed a long while—she did not know how long—before she heard Celia saying, “That will do, nurse; he will be quiet on my lap now. You can go to lunch, and let Garratt stay in the next room. What I think, Dodo,” Celia went on, observing nothing more than that Dorothea was leaning back in her chair, and likely to be passive, “is that Mr. Casaubon was spiteful. I never did like him, and James never did. I think the corners of his mouth were dreadfully spiteful. And now he has behaved in this way, I am sure religion does not require you to make yourself uncomfortable about him. If he has been taken away, that is a mercy, and you ought to be grateful. We should not grieve, should we, baby?” said Celia confidentially to that unconscious centre and poise of the world, who had the most remarkable fists all complete even to the nails, and hair enough, really, when you took his cap off, to make—you didn’t know what:—in short, he was Bouddha in a Western form.\n\nAt this crisis Lydgate was announced, and one of the first things he said was, “I fear you are not so well as you were, Mrs. Casaubon; have you been agitated? allow me to feel your pulse.” Dorothea’s hand was of a marble coldness.\n\n“She wants to go to Lowick, to look over papers,” said Celia. “She ought not, ought she?”\n\nLydgate did not speak for a few moments. Then he said, looking at Dorothea. “I hardly know. In my opinion Mrs. Casaubon should do what would give her the most repose of mind. That repose will not always come from being forbidden to act.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Dorothea, exerting herself, “I am sure that is wise. There are so many things which I ought to attend to. Why should I sit here idle?” Then, with an effort to recall subjects not connected with her agitation, she added, abruptly, “You know every one in Middlemarch, I think, Mr. Lydgate. I shall ask you to tell me a great deal. I have serious things to do now. I have a living to give away. You know Mr. Tyke and all the—” But Dorothea’s effort was too much for her; she broke off and burst into sobs.\n\nLydgate made her drink a dose of sal volatile.\n\n“Let Mrs. Casaubon do as she likes,” he said to Sir James, whom he asked to see before quitting the house. “She wants perfect freedom, I think, more than any other prescription.”\n\nHis attendance on Dorothea while her brain was excited, had enabled him to form some true conclusions concerning the trials of her life. He felt sure that she had been suffering from the strain and conflict of self-repression; and that she was likely now to feel herself only in another sort of pinfold than that from which she had been released.\n\nLydgate’s advice was all the easier for Sir James to follow when he found that Celia had already told Dorothea the unpleasant fact about the will. There was no help for it now—no reason for any further delay in the execution of necessary business. And the next day Sir James complied at once with her request that he would drive her to Lowick.\n\n“I have no wish to stay there at present,” said Dorothea; “I could hardly bear it. I am much happier at Freshitt with Celia. I shall be able to think better about what should be done at Lowick by looking at it from a distance. And I should like to be at the Grange a little while with my uncle, and go about in all the old walks and among the people in the village.”\n\n“Not yet, I think. Your uncle is having political company, and you are better out of the way of such doings,” said Sir James, who at that moment thought of the Grange chiefly as a haunt of young Ladislaw’s. But no word passed between him and Dorothea about the objectionable part of the will; indeed, both of them felt that the mention of it between them would be impossible. Sir James was shy, even with men, about disagreeable subjects; and the one thing that Dorothea would have chosen to say, if she had spoken on the matter at all, was forbidden to her at present because it seemed to be a further exposure of her husband’s injustice. Yet she did wish that Sir James could know what had passed between her and her husband about Will Ladislaw’s moral claim on the property: it would then, she thought, be apparent to him as it was to her, that her husband’s strange indelicate proviso had been chiefly urged by his bitter resistance to that idea of claim, and not merely by personal feelings more difficult to talk about. Also, it must be admitted, Dorothea wished that this could be known for Will’s sake, since her friends seemed to think of him as simply an object of Mr. Casaubon’s charity. Why should he be compared with an Italian carrying white mice? That word quoted from Mrs. Cadwallader seemed like a mocking travesty wrought in the dark by an impish finger.\n\nAt Lowick Dorothea searched desk and drawer—searched all her husband’s places of deposit for private writing, but found no paper addressed especially to her, except that “Synoptical Tabulation,” which was probably only the beginning of many intended directions for her guidance. In carrying out this bequest of labor to Dorothea, as in all else, Mr. Casaubon had been slow and hesitating, oppressed in the plan of transmitting his work, as he had been in executing it, by the sense of moving heavily in a dim and clogging medium: distrust of Dorothea’s competence to arrange what he had prepared was subdued only by distrust of any other redactor. But he had come at last to create a trust for himself out of Dorothea’s nature: she could do what she resolved to do: and he willingly imagined her toiling under the fetters of a promise to erect a tomb with his name upon it. (Not that Mr. Casaubon called the future volumes a tomb; he called them the Key to all Mythologies.) But the months gained on him and left his plans belated: he had only had time to ask for that promise by which he sought to keep his cold grasp on Dorothea’s life.\n\nThe grasp had slipped away. Bound by a pledge given from the depths of her pity, she would have been capable of undertaking a toil which her judgment whispered was vain for all uses except that consecration of faithfulness which is a supreme use. But now her judgment, instead of being controlled by duteous devotion, was made active by the imbittering discovery that in her past union there had lurked the hidden alienation of secrecy and suspicion. The living, suffering man was no longer before her to awaken her pity: there remained only the retrospect of painful subjection to a husband whose thoughts had been lower than she had believed, whose exorbitant claims for himself had even blinded his scrupulous care for his own character, and made him defeat his own pride by shocking men of ordinary honor. As for the property which was the sign of that broken tie, she would have been glad to be free from it and have nothing more than her original fortune which had been settled on her, if there had not been duties attached to ownership, which she ought not to flinch from. About this property many troublous questions insisted on rising: had she not been right in thinking that the half of it ought to go to Will Ladislaw?—but was it not impossible now for her to do that act of justice? Mr. Casaubon had taken a cruelly effective means of hindering her: even with indignation against him in her heart, any act that seemed a triumphant eluding of his purpose revolted her.\n\nAfter collecting papers of business which she wished to examine, she locked up again the desks and drawers—all empty of personal words for her—empty of any sign that in her husband’s lonely brooding his heart had gone out to her in excuse or explanation; and she went back to Freshitt with the sense that around his last hard demand and his last injurious assertion of his power, the silence was unbroken.\n\nDorothea tried now to turn her thoughts towards immediate duties, and one of these was of a kind which others were determined to remind her of. Lydgate’s ear had caught eagerly her mention of the living, and as soon as he could, he reopened the subject, seeing here a possibility of making amends for the casting-vote he had once given with an ill-satisfied conscience. “Instead of telling you anything about Mr. Tyke,” he said, “I should like to speak of another man—Mr. Farebrother, the Vicar of St. Botolph’s. His living is a poor one, and gives him a stinted provision for himself and his family. His mother, aunt, and sister all live with him, and depend upon him. I believe he has never married because of them. I never heard such good preaching as his—such plain, easy eloquence. He would have done to preach at St. Paul’s Cross after old Latimer. His talk is just as good about all subjects: original, simple, clear. I think him a remarkable fellow: he ought to have done more than he has done.”\n\n“Why has he not done more?” said Dorothea, interested now in all who had slipped below their own intention.\n\n“That’s a hard question,” said Lydgate. “I find myself that it’s uncommonly difficult to make the right thing work: there are so many strings pulling at once. Farebrother often hints that he has got into the wrong profession; he wants a wider range than that of a poor clergyman, and I suppose he has no interest to help him on. He is very fond of Natural History and various scientific matters, and he is hampered in reconciling these tastes with his position. He has no money to spare—hardly enough to use; and that has led him into card-playing—Middlemarch is a great place for whist. He does play for money, and he wins a good deal. Of course that takes him into company a little beneath him, and makes him slack about some things; and yet, with all that, looking at him as a whole, I think he is one of the most blameless men I ever knew. He has neither venom nor doubleness in him, and those often go with a more correct outside.”\n\n“I wonder whether he suffers in his conscience because of that habit,” said Dorothea; “I wonder whether he wishes he could leave it off.”\n\n“I have no doubt he would leave it off, if he were transplanted into plenty: he would be glad of the time for other things.”\n\n“My uncle says that Mr. Tyke is spoken of as an apostolic man,” said Dorothea, meditatively. She was wishing it were possible to restore the times of primitive zeal, and yet thinking of Mr. Farebrother with a strong desire to rescue him from his chance-gotten money.\n\n“I don’t pretend to say that Farebrother is apostolic,” said Lydgate. “His position is not quite like that of the Apostles: he is only a parson among parishioners whose lives he has to try and make better. Practically I find that what is called being apostolic now, is an impatience of everything in which the parson doesn’t cut the principal figure. I see something of that in Mr. Tyke at the Hospital: a good deal of his doctrine is a sort of pinching hard to make people uncomfortably aware of him. Besides, an apostolic man at Lowick!—he ought to think, as St. Francis did, that it is needful to preach to the birds.”\n\n“True,” said Dorothea. “It is hard to imagine what sort of notions our farmers and laborers get from their teaching. I have been looking into a volume of sermons by Mr. Tyke: such sermons would be of no use at Lowick—I mean, about imputed righteousness and the prophecies in the Apocalypse. I have always been thinking of the different ways in which Christianity is taught, and whenever I find one way that makes it a wider blessing than any other, I cling to that as the truest—I mean that which takes in the most good of all kinds, and brings in the most people as sharers in it. It is surely better to pardon too much, than to condemn too much. But I should like to see Mr. Farebrother and hear him preach.”\n\n“Do,” said Lydgate; “I trust to the effect of that. He is very much beloved, but he has his enemies too: there are always people who can’t forgive an able man for differing from them. And that money-winning business is really a blot. You don’t, of course, see many Middlemarch people: but Mr. Ladislaw, who is constantly seeing Mr. Brooke, is a great friend of Mr. Farebrother’s old ladies, and would be glad to sing the Vicar’s praises. One of the old ladies—Miss Noble, the aunt—is a wonderfully quaint picture of self-forgetful goodness, and Ladislaw gallants her about sometimes. I met them one day in a back street: you know Ladislaw’s look—a sort of Daphnis in coat and waistcoat; and this little old maid reaching up to his arm—they looked like a couple dropped out of a romantic comedy. But the best evidence about Farebrother is to see him and hear him.”\n\nHappily Dorothea was in her private sitting-room when this conversation occurred, and there was no one present to make Lydgate’s innocent introduction of Ladislaw painful to her. As was usual with him in matters of personal gossip, Lydgate had quite forgotten Rosamond’s remark that she thought Will adored Mrs. Casaubon. At that moment he was only caring for what would recommend the Farebrother family; and he had purposely given emphasis to the worst that could be said about the Vicar, in order to forestall objections. In the weeks since Mr. Casaubon’s death he had hardly seen Ladislaw, and he had heard no rumor to warn him that Mr. Brooke’s confidential secretary was a dangerous subject with Mrs. Casaubon. When he was gone, his picture of Ladislaw lingered in her mind and disputed the ground with that question of the Lowick living. What was Will Ladislaw thinking about her? Would he hear of that fact which made her cheeks burn as they never used to do? And how would he feel when he heard it?—But she could see as well as possible how he smiled down at the little old maid. An Italian with white mice!—on the contrary, he was a creature who entered into every one’s feelings, and could take the pressure of their thought instead of urging his own with iron resistance.\n\n# CHAPTER LI.\n\nParty is Nature too, and you shall see By force of Logic how they both agree: The Many in the One, the One in Many; All is not Some, nor Some the same as Any: Genus holds species, both are great or small; One genus highest, one not high at all; Each species has its differentia too, This is not That, and He was never You, Though this and that are AYES, and you and he Are like as one to one, or three to three.\n\nNo gossip about Mr. Casaubon’s will had yet reached Ladislaw: the air seemed to be filled with the dissolution of Parliament and the coming election, as the old wakes and fairs were filled with the rival clatter of itinerant shows; and more private noises were taken little notice of. The famous “dry election” was at hand, in which the depths of public feeling might be measured by the low flood-mark of drink. Will Ladislaw was one of the busiest at this time; and though Dorothea’s widowhood was continually in his thought, he was so far from wishing to be spoken to on the subject, that when Lydgate sought him out to tell him what had passed about the Lowick living, he answered rather waspishly—\n\n“Why should you bring me into the matter? I never see Mrs. Casaubon, and am not likely to see her, since she is at Freshitt. I never go there. It is Tory ground, where I and the ‘Pioneer’ are no more welcome than a poacher and his gun.”\n\nThe fact was that Will had been made the more susceptible by observing that Mr. Brooke, instead of wishing him, as before, to come to the Grange oftener than was quite agreeable to himself, seemed now to contrive that he should go there as little as possible. This was a shuffling concession of Mr. Brooke’s to Sir James Chettam’s indignant remonstrance; and Will, awake to the slightest hint in this direction, concluded that he was to be kept away from the Grange on Dorothea’s account. Her friends, then, regarded him with some suspicion? Their fears were quite superfluous: they were very much mistaken if they imagined that he would put himself forward as a needy adventurer trying to win the favor of a rich woman.\n\nUntil now Will had never fully seen the chasm between himself and Dorothea—until now that he was come to the brink of it, and saw her on the other side. He began, not without some inward rage, to think of going away from the neighborhood: it would be impossible for him to show any further interest in Dorothea without subjecting himself to disagreeable imputations—perhaps even in her mind, which others might try to poison.\n\n“We are forever divided,” said Will. “I might as well be at Rome; she would be no farther from me.” But what we call our despair is often only the painful eagerness of unfed hope. There were plenty of reasons why he should not go—public reasons why he should not quit his post at this crisis, leaving Mr. Brooke in the lurch when he needed “coaching” for the election, and when there was so much canvassing, direct and indirect, to be carried on. Will could not like to leave his own chessmen in the heat of a game; and any candidate on the right side, even if his brain and marrow had been as soft as was consistent with a gentlemanly bearing, might help to turn a majority. To coach Mr. Brooke and keep him steadily to the idea that he must pledge himself to vote for the actual Reform Bill, instead of insisting on his independence and power of pulling up in time, was not an easy task. Mr. Farebrother’s prophecy of a fourth candidate “in the bag” had not yet been fulfilled, neither the Parliamentary Candidate Society nor any other power on the watch to secure a reforming majority seeing a worthy nodus for interference while there was a second reforming candidate like Mr. Brooke, who might be returned at his own expense; and the fight lay entirely between Pinkerton the old Tory member, Bagster the new Whig member returned at the last election, and Brooke the future independent member, who was to fetter himself for this occasion only. Mr. Hawley and his party would bend all their forces to the return of Pinkerton, and Mr. Brooke’s success must depend either on plumpers which would leave Bagster in the rear, or on the new minting of Tory votes into reforming votes. The latter means, of course, would be preferable.\n\nThis prospect of converting votes was a dangerous distraction to Mr. Brooke: his impression that waverers were likely to be allured by wavering statements, and also the liability of his mind to stick afresh at opposing arguments as they turned up in his memory, gave Will Ladislaw much trouble.\n\n“You know there are tactics in these things,” said Mr. Brooke; “meeting people half-way—tempering your ideas—saying, ‘Well now, there’s something in that,’ and so on. I agree with you that this is a peculiar occasion—the country with a will of its own—political unions—that sort of thing—but we sometimes cut with rather too sharp a knife, Ladislaw. These ten-pound householders, now: why ten? Draw the line somewhere—yes: but why just at ten? That’s a difficult question, now, if you go into it.”\n\n“Of course it is,” said Will, impatiently. “But if you are to wait till we get a logical Bill, you must put yourself forward as a revolutionist, and then Middlemarch would not elect you, I fancy. As for trimming, this is not a time for trimming.”\n\nMr. Brooke always ended by agreeing with Ladislaw, who still appeared to him a sort of Burke with a leaven of Shelley; but after an interval the wisdom of his own methods reasserted itself, and he was again drawn into using them with much hopefulness. At this stage of affairs he was in excellent spirits, which even supported him under large advances of money; for his powers of convincing and persuading had not yet been tested by anything more difficult than a chairman’s speech introducing other orators, or a dialogue with a Middlemarch voter, from which he came away with a sense that he was a tactician by nature, and that it was a pity he had not gone earlier into this kind of thing. He was a little conscious of defeat, however, with Mr. Mawmsey, a chief representative in Middlemarch of that great social power, the retail trader, and naturally one of the most doubtful voters in the borough—willing for his own part to supply an equal quality of teas and sugars to reformer and anti-reformer, as well as to agree impartially with both, and feeling like the burgesses of old that this necessity of electing members was a great burthen to a town; for even if there were no danger in holding out hopes to all parties beforehand, there would be the painful necessity at last of disappointing respectable people whose names were on his books. He was accustomed to receive large orders from Mr. Brooke of Tipton; but then, there were many of Pinkerton’s committee whose opinions had a great weight of grocery on their side. Mr. Mawmsey thinking that Mr. Brooke, as not too “clever in his intellects,” was the more likely to forgive a grocer who gave a hostile vote under pressure, had become confidential in his back parlor.\n\n“As to Reform, sir, put it in a family light,” he said, rattling the small silver in his pocket, and smiling affably. “Will it support Mrs. Mawmsey, and enable her to bring up six children when I am no more? I put the question fictiously, knowing what must be the answer. Very well, sir. I ask you what, as a husband and a father, I am to do when gentlemen come to me and say, ‘Do as you like, Mawmsey; but if you vote against us, I shall get my groceries elsewhere: when I sugar my liquor I like to feel that I am benefiting the country by maintaining tradesmen of the right color.’ Those very words have been spoken to me, sir, in the very chair where you are now sitting. I don’t mean by your honorable self, Mr. Brooke.”\n\n“No, no, no—that’s narrow, you know. Until my butler complains to me of your goods, Mr. Mawmsey,” said Mr. Brooke, soothingly, “until I hear that you send bad sugars, spices—that sort of thing—I shall never order him to go elsewhere.”\n\n“Sir, I am your humble servant, and greatly obliged,” said Mr. Mawmsey, feeling that politics were clearing up a little. “There would be some pleasure in voting for a gentleman who speaks in that honorable manner.”\n“Well, you know, Mr. Mawmsey, you would find it the right thing to put yourself on our side. This Reform will touch everybody by-and-by—a thoroughly popular measure—a sort of A, B, C, you know, that must come first before the rest can follow. I quite agree with you that you’ve got to look at the thing in a family light: but public spirit, now. We’re all one family, you know—it’s all one cupboard. Such a thing as a vote, now: why, it may help to make men’s fortunes at the Cape—there’s no knowing what may be the effect of a vote,” Mr. Brooke ended, with a sense of being a little out at sea, though finding it still enjoyable. But Mr. Mawmsey answered in a tone of decisive check.\n\n“I beg your pardon, sir, but I can’t afford that. When I give a vote I must know what I am doing; I must look to what will be the effects on my till and ledger, speaking respectfully. Prices, I’ll admit, are what nobody can know the merits of; and the sudden falls after you’ve bought in currants, which are a goods that will not keep—I’ve never; myself seen into the ins and outs there; which is a rebuke to human pride. But as to one family, there’s debtor and creditor, I hope; they’re not going to reform that away; else I should vote for things staying as they are. Few men have less need to cry for change than I have, personally speaking—that is, for self and family. I am not one of those who have nothing to lose: I mean as to respectability both in parish and private business, and noways in respect of your honorable self and custom, which you was good enough to say you would not withdraw from me, vote or no vote, while the article sent in was satisfactory.”\n\nAfter this conversation Mr. Mawmsey went up and boasted to his wife that he had been rather too many for Brooke of Tipton, and that he didn’t mind so much now about going to the poll.\n\nMr. Brooke on this occasion abstained from boasting of his tactics to Ladislaw, who for his part was glad enough to persuade himself that he had no concern with any canvassing except the purely argumentative sort, and that he worked no meaner engine than knowledge. Mr. Brooke, necessarily, had his agents, who understood the nature of the Middlemarch voter and the means of enlisting his ignorance on the side of the Bill—which were remarkably similar to the means of enlisting it on the side against the Bill. Will stopped his ears. Occasionally Parliament, like the rest of our lives, even to our eating and apparel, could hardly go on if our imaginations were too active about processes. There were plenty of dirty-handed men in the world to do dirty business; and Will protested to himself that his share in bringing Mr. Brooke through would be quite innocent.\n\nBut whether he should succeed in that mode of contributing to the majority on the right side was very doubtful to him. He had written out various speeches and memoranda for speeches, but he had begun to perceive that Mr. Brooke’s mind, if it had the burthen of remembering any train of thought, would let it drop, run away in search of it, and not easily come back again. To collect documents is one mode of serving your country, and to remember the contents of a document is another. No! the only way in which Mr. Brooke could be coerced into thinking of the right arguments at the right time was to be well plied with them till they took up all the room in his brain. But here there was the difficulty of finding room, so many things having been taken in beforehand. Mr. Brooke himself observed that his ideas stood rather in his way when he was speaking.\n\nHowever, Ladislaw’s coaching was forthwith to be put to the test, for before the day of nomination Mr. Brooke was to explain himself to the worthy electors of Middlemarch from the balcony of the White Hart, which looked out advantageously at an angle of the market-place, commanding a large area in front and two converging streets. It was a fine May morning, and everything seemed hopeful: there was some prospect of an understanding between Bagster’s committee and Brooke’s, to which Mr. Bulstrode, Mr. Standish as a Liberal lawyer, and such manufacturers as Mr. Plymdale and Mr. Vincy, gave a solidity which almost counterbalanced Mr. Hawley and his associates who sat for Pinkerton at the Green Dragon. Mr. Brooke, conscious of having weakened the blasts of the “Trumpet” against him, by his reforms as a landlord in the last half year, and hearing himself cheered a little as he drove into the town, felt his heart tolerably light under his buff-colored waistcoat. But with regard to critical occasions, it often happens that all moments seem comfortably remote until the last.\n\n“This looks well, eh?” said Mr. Brooke as the crowd gathered. “I shall have a good audience, at any rate. I like this, now—this kind of public made up of one’s own neighbors, you know.”\n\nThe weavers and tanners of Middlemarch, unlike Mr. Mawmsey, had never thought of Mr. Brooke as a neighbor, and were not more attached to him than if he had been sent in a box from London. But they listened without much disturbance to the speakers who introduced the candidate, one of them—a political personage from Brassing, who came to tell Middlemarch its duty—spoke so fully, that it was alarming to think what the candidate could find to say after him. Meanwhile the crowd became denser, and as the political personage neared the end of his speech, Mr. Brooke felt a remarkable change in his sensations while he still handled his eye-glass, trifled with documents before him, and exchanged remarks with his committee, as a man to whom the moment of summons was indifferent.\n\n“I’ll take another glass of sherry, Ladislaw,” he said, with an easy air, to Will, who was close behind him, and presently handed him the supposed fortifier. It was ill-chosen; for Mr. Brooke was an abstemious man, and to drink a second glass of sherry quickly at no great interval from the first was a surprise to his system which tended to scatter his energies instead of collecting them. Pray pity him: so many English gentlemen make themselves miserable by speechifying on entirely private grounds! whereas Mr. Brooke wished to serve his country by standing for Parliament—which, indeed, may also be done on private grounds, but being once undertaken does absolutely demand some speechifying.\n\nIt was not about the beginning of his speech that Mr. Brooke was at all anxious; this, he felt sure, would be all right; he should have it quite pat, cut out as neatly as a set of couplets from Pope. Embarking would be easy, but the vision of open sea that might come after was alarming. “And questions, now,” hinted the demon just waking up in his stomach, “somebody may put questions about the schedules.—Ladislaw,” he continued, aloud, “just hand me the memorandum of the schedules.”\n\nWhen Mr. Brooke presented himself on the balcony, the cheers were quite loud enough to counterbalance the yells, groans, brayings, and other expressions of adverse theory, which were so moderate that Mr. Standish (decidedly an old bird) observed in the ear next to him, “This looks dangerous, by God! Hawley has got some deeper plan than this.” Still, the cheers were exhilarating, and no candidate could look more amiable than Mr. Brooke, with the memorandum in his breast-pocket, his left hand on the rail of the balcony, and his right trifling with his eye-glass. The striking points in his appearance were his buff waistcoat, short-clipped blond hair, and neutral physiognomy. He began with some confidence.\n\n“Gentlemen—Electors of Middlemarch!”\n\nThis was so much the right thing that a little pause after it seemed natural.\n\n“I’m uncommonly glad to be here—I was never so proud and happy in my life—never so happy, you know.”\n\nThis was a bold figure of speech, but not exactly the right thing; for, unhappily, the pat opening had slipped away—even couplets from Pope may be but “fallings from us, vanishings,” when fear clutches us, and a glass of sherry is hurrying like smoke among our ideas. Ladislaw, who stood at the window behind the speaker, thought, “it’s all up now. The only chance is that, since the best thing won’t always do, floundering may answer for once.” Mr. Brooke, meanwhile, having lost other clews, fell back on himself and his qualifications—always an appropriate graceful subject for a candidate.\n\n“I am a close neighbor of yours, my good friends—you’ve known me on the bench a good while—I’ve always gone a good deal into public questions—machinery, now, and machine-breaking—you’re many of you concerned with machinery, and I’ve been going into that lately. It won’t do, you know, breaking machines: everything must go on—trade, manufactures, commerce, interchange of staples—that kind of thing—since Adam Smith, that must go on. We must look all over the globe:—‘Observation with extensive view,’ must look everywhere, ‘from China to Peru,’ as somebody says—Johnson, I think, ‘The Rambler,’ you know. That is what I have done up to a certain point—not as far as Peru; but I’ve not always stayed at home—I saw it wouldn’t do. I’ve been in the Levant, where some of your Middlemarch goods go—and then, again, in the Baltic. The Baltic, now.”\n\nPlying among his recollections in this way, Mr. Brooke might have got along, easily to himself, and would have come back from the remotest seas without trouble; but a diabolical procedure had been set up by the enemy. At one and the same moment there had risen above the shoulders of the crowd, nearly opposite Mr. Brooke, and within ten yards of him, the effigy of himself: buff-colored waistcoat, eye-glass, and neutral physiognomy, painted on rag; and there had arisen, apparently in the air, like the note of the cuckoo, a parrot-like, Punch-voiced echo of his words. Everybody looked up at the open windows in the houses at the opposite angles of the converging streets; but they were either blank, or filled by laughing listeners. The most innocent echo has an impish mockery in it when it follows a gravely persistent speaker, and this echo was not at all innocent; if it did not follow with the precision of a natural echo, it had a wicked choice of the words it overtook. By the time it said, “The Baltic, now,” the laugh which had been running through the audience became a general shout, and but for the sobering effects of party and that great public cause which the entanglement of things had identified with “Brooke of Tipton,” the laugh might have caught his committee. Mr. Bulstrode asked, reprehensively, what the new police was doing; but a voice could not well be collared, and an attack on the effigy of the candidate would have been too equivocal, since Hawley probably meant it to be pelted.\n\nMr. Brooke himself was not in a position to be quickly conscious of anything except a general slipping away of ideas within himself: he had even a little singing in the ears, and he was the only person who had not yet taken distinct account of the echo or discerned the image of himself. Few things hold the perceptions more thoroughly captive than anxiety about what we have got to say. Mr. Brooke heard the laughter; but he had expected some Tory efforts at disturbance, and he was at this moment additionally excited by the tickling, stinging sense that his lost exordium was coming back to fetch him from the Baltic.\n\n“That reminds me,” he went on, thrusting a hand into his side-pocket, with an easy air, “if I wanted a precedent, you know—but we never want a precedent for the right thing—but there is Chatham, now; I can’t say I should have supported Chatham, or Pitt, the younger Pitt—he was not a man of ideas, and we want ideas, you know.”\n\n“Blast your ideas! we want the Bill,” said a loud rough voice from the crowd below.\n\nImmediately the invisible Punch, who had hitherto followed Mr. Brooke, repeated, “Blast your ideas! we want the Bill.” The laugh was louder than ever, and for the first time Mr. Brooke being himself silent, heard distinctly the mocking echo. But it seemed to ridicule his interrupter, and in that light was encouraging; so he replied with amenity—\n\n“There is something in what you say, my good friend, and what do we meet for but to speak our minds—freedom of opinion, freedom of the press, liberty—that kind of thing? The Bill, now—you shall have the Bill”—here Mr. Brooke paused a moment to fix on his eye-glass and take the paper from his breast-pocket, with a sense of being practical and coming to particulars. The invisible Punch followed:—\n\n“You shall have the Bill, Mr. Brooke, per electioneering contest, and a seat outside Parliament as delivered, five thousand pounds, seven shillings, and fourpence.”\n\nMr. Brooke, amid the roars of laughter, turned red, let his eye-glass fall, and looking about him confusedly, saw the image of himself, which had come nearer. The next moment he saw it dolorously bespattered with eggs. His spirit rose a little, and his voice too.\n\n“Buffoonery, tricks, ridicule the test of truth—all that is very well”—here an unpleasant egg broke on Mr. Brooke’s shoulder, as the echo said, “All that is very well;” then came a hail of eggs, chiefly aimed at the image, but occasionally hitting the original, as if by chance. There was a stream of new men pushing among the crowd; whistles, yells, bellowings, and fifes made all the greater hubbub because there was shouting and struggling to put them down. No voice would have had wing enough to rise above the uproar, and Mr. Brooke, disagreeably anointed, stood his ground no longer. The frustration would have been less exasperating if it had been less gamesome and boyish: a serious assault of which the newspaper reporter “can aver that it endangered the learned gentleman’s ribs,” or can respectfully bear witness to “the soles of that gentleman’s boots having been visible above the railing,” has perhaps more consolations attached to it.\n\nMr. Brooke re-entered the committee-room, saying, as carelessly as he could, “This is a little too bad, you know. I should have got the ear of the people by-and-by—but they didn’t give me time. I should have gone into the Bill by-and-by, you know,” he added, glancing at Ladislaw. “However, things will come all right at the nomination.”\n\nBut it was not resolved unanimously that things would come right; on the contrary, the committee looked rather grim, and the political personage from Brassing was writing busily, as if he were brewing new devices.\n\n“It was Bowyer who did it,” said Mr. Standish, evasively. “I know it as well as if he had been advertised. He’s uncommonly good at ventriloquism, and he did it uncommonly well, by God! Hawley has been having him to dinner lately: there’s a fund of talent in Bowyer.”\n\n“Well, you know, you never mentioned him to me, Standish, else I would have invited him to dine,” said poor Mr. Brooke, who had gone through a great deal of inviting for the good of his country.\n\n“There’s not a more paltry fellow in Middlemarch than Bowyer,” said Ladislaw, indignantly, “but it seems as if the paltry fellows were always to turn the scale.”\n\nWill was thoroughly out of temper with himself as well as with his “principal,” and he went to shut himself in his rooms with a half-formed resolve to throw up the “Pioneer” and Mr. Brooke together. Why should he stay? If the impassable gulf between himself and Dorothea were ever to be filled up, it must rather be by his going away and getting into a thoroughly different position than by staying here and slipping into deserved contempt as an understrapper of Brooke’s. Then came the young dream of wonders that he might do—in five years, for example: political writing, political speaking, would get a higher value now public life was going to be wider and more national, and they might give him such distinction that he would not seem to be asking Dorothea to step down to him. Five years:—if he could only be sure that she cared for him more than for others; if he could only make her aware that he stood aloof until he could tell his love without lowering himself—then he could go away easily, and begin a career which at five-and-twenty seemed probable enough in the inward order of things, where talent brings fame, and fame everything else which is delightful. He could speak and he could write; he could master any subject if he chose, and he meant always to take the side of reason and justice, on which he would carry all his ardor. Why should he not one day be lifted above the shoulders of the crowd, and feel that he had won that eminence well? Without doubt he would leave Middlemarch, go to town, and make himself fit for celebrity by “eating his dinners.”\n\nBut not immediately: not until some kind of sign had passed between him and Dorothea. He could not be satisfied until she knew why, even if he were the man she would choose to marry, he would not marry her. Hence he must keep his post and bear with Mr. Brooke a little longer.\n\nBut he soon had reason to suspect that Mr. Brooke had anticipated him in the wish to break up their connection. Deputations without and voices within had concurred in inducing that philanthropist to take a stronger measure than usual for the good of mankind; namely, to withdraw in favor of another candidate, to whom he left the advantages of his canvassing machinery. He himself called this a strong measure, but observed that his health was less capable of sustaining excitement than he had imagined.\n\n“I have felt uneasy about the chest—it won’t do to carry that too far,” he said to Ladislaw in explaining the affair. “I must pull up. Poor Casaubon was a warning, you know. I’ve made some heavy advances, but I’ve dug a channel. It’s rather coarse work—this electioneering, eh, Ladislaw? dare say you are tired of it. However, we have dug a channel with the ‘Pioneer’—put things in a track, and so on. A more ordinary man than you might carry it on now—more ordinary, you know.”\n\n“Do you wish me to give it up?” said Will, the quick color coming in his face, as he rose from the writing-table, and took a turn of three steps with his hands in his pockets. “I am ready to do so whenever you wish it.”\n\n“As to wishing, my dear Ladislaw, I have the highest opinion of your powers, you know. But about the ‘Pioneer,’ I have been consulting a little with some of the men on our side, and they are inclined to take it into their hands—indemnify me to a certain extent—carry it on, in fact. And under the circumstances, you might like to give up—might find a better field. These people might not take that high view of you which I have always taken, as an alter ego, a right hand—though I always looked forward to your doing something else. I think of having a run into France. But I’ll write you any letters, you know—to Althorpe and people of that kind. I’ve met Althorpe.”\n\n“I am exceedingly obliged to you,” said Ladislaw, proudly. “Since you are going to part with the ‘Pioneer,’ I need not trouble you about the steps I shall take. I may choose to continue here for the present.”\n\nAfter Mr. Brooke had left him Will said to himself, “The rest of the family have been urging him to get rid of me, and he doesn’t care now about my going. I shall stay as long as I like. I shall go of my own movements and not because they are afraid of me.”\n\n# CHAPTER LII.\n\n“His heart The lowliest duties on itself did lay.” —WORDSWORTH.\n\nOn that June evening when Mr. Farebrother knew that he was to have the Lowick living, there was joy in the old fashioned parlor, and even the portraits of the great lawyers seemed to look on with satisfaction. His mother left her tea and toast untouched, but sat with her usual pretty primness, only showing her emotion by that flush in the cheeks and brightness in the eyes which give an old woman a touching momentary identity with her far-off youthful self, and saying decisively—\n\n“The greatest comfort, Camden, is that you have deserved it.”\n\n“When a man gets a good berth, mother, half the deserving must come after,” said the son, brimful of pleasure, and not trying to conceal it. The gladness in his face was of that active kind which seems to have energy enough not only to flash outwardly, but to light up busy vision within: one seemed to see thoughts, as well as delight, in his glances.\n\n“Now, aunt,” he went on, rubbing his hands and looking at Miss Noble, who was making tender little beaver-like noises, “There shall be sugar-candy always on the table for you to steal and give to the children, and you shall have a great many new stockings to make presents of, and you shall darn your own more than ever!”\n\nMiss Noble nodded at her nephew with a subdued half-frightened laugh, conscious of having already dropped an additional lump of sugar into her basket on the strength of the new preferment.\n\n“As for you, Winny”—the Vicar went on—“I shall make no difficulty about your marrying any Lowick bachelor—Mr. Solomon Featherstone, for example, as soon as I find you are in love with him.”\n\nMiss Winifred, who had been looking at her brother all the while and crying heartily, which was her way of rejoicing, smiled through her tears and said, “You must set me the example, Cam: you must marry now.”\n\n“With all my heart. But who is in love with me? I am a seedy old fellow,” said the Vicar, rising, pushing his chair away and looking down at himself. “What do you say, mother?”\n\n“You are a handsome man, Camden: though not so fine a figure of a man as your father,” said the old lady.\n\n“I wish you would marry Miss Garth, brother,” said Miss Winifred. “She would make us so lively at Lowick.”\n\n“Very fine! You talk as if young women were tied up to be chosen, like poultry at market; as if I had only to ask and everybody would have me,” said the Vicar, not caring to specify.\n\n“We don’t want everybody,” said Miss Winifred. “But you would like Miss Garth, mother, shouldn’t you?”\n\n“My son’s choice shall be mine,” said Mrs. Farebrother, with majestic discretion, “and a wife would be most welcome, Camden. You will want your whist at home when we go to Lowick, and Henrietta Noble never was a whist-player.” (Mrs. Farebrother always called her tiny old sister by that magnificent name.)\n\n“I shall do without whist now, mother.”\n\n“Why so, Camden? In my time whist was thought an undeniable amusement for a good churchman,” said Mrs. Farebrother, innocent of the meaning that whist had for her son, and speaking rather sharply, as at some dangerous countenancing of new doctrine.\n\n“I shall be too busy for whist; I shall have two parishes,” said the Vicar, preferring not to discuss the virtues of that game.\n\nHe had already said to Dorothea, “I don’t feel bound to give up St. Botolph’s. It is protest enough against the pluralism they want to reform if I give somebody else most of the money. The stronger thing is not to give up power, but to use it well.”\n\n“I have thought of that,” said Dorothea. “So far as self is concerned, I think it would be easier to give up power and money than to keep them. It seems very unfitting that I should have this patronage, yet I felt that I ought not to let it be used by some one else instead of me.”\n\n“It is I who am bound to act so that you will not regret your power,” said Mr. Farebrother.\n\nHis was one of the natures in which conscience gets the more active when the yoke of life ceases to gall them. He made no display of humility on the subject, but in his heart he felt rather ashamed that his conduct had shown laches which others who did not get benefices were free from.\n\n“I used often to wish I had been something else than a clergyman,” he said to Lydgate, “but perhaps it will be better to try and make as good a clergyman out of myself as I can. That is the well-beneficed point of view, you perceive, from which difficulties are much simplified,” he ended, smiling.\n\nThe Vicar did feel then as if his share of duties would be easy. But Duty has a trick of behaving unexpectedly—something like a heavy friend whom we have amiably asked to visit us, and who breaks his leg within our gates.\n\nHardly a week later, Duty presented itself in his study under the disguise of Fred Vincy, now returned from Omnibus College with his bachelor’s degree.\n\n“I am ashamed to trouble you, Mr. Farebrother,” said Fred, whose fair open face was propitiating, “but you are the only friend I can consult. I told you everything once before, and you were so good that I can’t help coming to you again.”\n\n“Sit down, Fred, I’m ready to hear and do anything I can,” said the Vicar, who was busy packing some small objects for removal, and went on with his work.\n\n“I wanted to tell you—” Fred hesitated an instant and then went on plungingly, “I might go into the Church now; and really, look where I may, I can’t see anything else to do. I don’t like it, but I know it’s uncommonly hard on my father to say so, after he has spent a good deal of money in educating me for it.” Fred paused again an instant, and then repeated, “and I can’t see anything else to do.”\n\n“I did talk to your father about it, Fred, but I made little way with him. He said it was too late. But you have got over one bridge now: what are your other difficulties?”\n\n“Merely that I don’t like it. I don’t like divinity, and preaching, and feeling obliged to look serious. I like riding across country, and doing as other men do. I don’t mean that I want to be a bad fellow in any way; but I’ve no taste for the sort of thing people expect of a clergyman. And yet what else am I to do? My father can’t spare me any capital, else I might go into farming. And he has no room for me in his trade. And of course I can’t begin to study for law or physic now, when my father wants me to earn something. It’s all very well to say I’m wrong to go into the Church; but those who say so might as well tell me to go into the backwoods.”\n\nFred’s voice had taken a tone of grumbling remonstrance, and Mr. Farebrother might have been inclined to smile if his mind had not been too busy in imagining more than Fred told him.\n\n“Have you any difficulties about doctrines—about the Articles?” he said, trying hard to think of the question simply for Fred’s sake.\n\n“No; I suppose the Articles are right. I am not prepared with any arguments to disprove them, and much better, cleverer fellows than I am go in for them entirely. I think it would be rather ridiculous in me to urge scruples of that sort, as if I were a judge,” said Fred, quite simply.\n\n“I suppose, then, it has occurred to you that you might be a fair parish priest without being much of a divine?”\n\n“Of course, if I am obliged to be a clergyman, I shall try and do my duty, though I mayn’t like it. Do you think any body ought to blame me?”\n\n“For going into the Church under the circumstances? That depends on your conscience, Fred—how far you have counted the cost, and seen what your position will require of you. I can only tell you about myself, that I have always been too lax, and have been uneasy in consequence.”\n\n“But there is another hindrance,” said Fred, coloring. “I did not tell you before, though perhaps I may have said things that made you guess it. There is somebody I am very fond of: I have loved her ever since we were children.”\n\n“Miss Garth, I suppose?” said the Vicar, examining some labels very closely.\n\n“Yes. I shouldn’t mind anything if she would have me. And I know I could be a good fellow then.”\n\n“And you think she returns the feeling?”\n\n“She never will say so; and a good while ago she made me promise not to speak to her about it again. And she has set her mind especially against my being a clergyman; I know that. But I can’t give her up. I do think she cares about me. I saw Mrs. Garth last night, and she said that Mary was staying at Lowick Rectory with Miss Farebrother.”\n\n“Yes, she is very kindly helping my sister. Do you wish to go there?”\n\n“No, I want to ask a great favor of you. I am ashamed to bother you in this way; but Mary might listen to what you said, if you mentioned the subject to her—I mean about my going into the Church.”\n\n“That is rather a delicate task, my dear Fred. I shall have to presuppose your attachment to her; and to enter on the subject as you wish me to do, will be asking her to tell me whether she returns it.”\n\n“That is what I want her to tell you,” said Fred, bluntly. “I don’t know what to do, unless I can get at her feeling.”\n\n“You mean that you would be guided by that as to your going into the Church?”\n\n“If Mary said she would never have me I might as well go wrong in one way as another.”\n\n“That is nonsense, Fred. Men outlive their love, but they don’t outlive the consequences of their recklessness.”\n\n“Not my sort of love: I have never been without loving Mary. If I had to give her up, it would be like beginning to live on wooden legs.”\n\n“Will she not be hurt at my intrusion?”\n\n“No, I feel sure she will not. She respects you more than any one, and she would not put you off with fun as she does me. Of course I could not have told any one else, or asked any one else to speak to her, but you. There is no one else who could be such a friend to both of us.” Fred paused a moment, and then said, rather complainingly, “And she ought to acknowledge that I have worked in order to pass. She ought to believe that I would exert myself for her sake.”\n\nThere was a moment’s silence before Mr. Farebrother laid down his work, and putting out his hand to Fred said—\n\n“Very well, my boy. I will do what you wish.”\n\nThat very day Mr. Farebrother went to Lowick parsonage on the nag which he had just set up. “Decidedly I am an old stalk,” he thought, “the young growths are pushing me aside.”\n\nHe found Mary in the garden gathering roses and sprinkling the petals on a sheet. The sun was low, and tall trees sent their shadows across the grassy walks where Mary was moving without bonnet or parasol. She did not observe Mr. Farebrother’s approach along the grass, and had just stooped down to lecture a small black-and-tan terrier, which would persist in walking on the sheet and smelling at the rose-leaves as Mary sprinkled them. She took his fore-paws in one hand, and lifted up the forefinger of the other, while the dog wrinkled his brows and looked embarrassed. “Fly, Fly, I am ashamed of you,” Mary was saying in a grave contralto. “This is not becoming in a sensible dog; anybody would think you were a silly young gentleman.”\n\n“You are unmerciful to young gentlemen, Miss Garth,” said the Vicar, within two yards of her.\n\nMary started up and blushed. “It always answers to reason with Fly,” she said, laughingly.\n\n“But not with young gentlemen?”\n\n“Oh, with some, I suppose; since some of them turn into excellent men.”\n\n“I am glad of that admission, because I want at this very moment to interest you in a young gentleman.”\n\n“Not a silly one, I hope,” said Mary, beginning to pluck the roses again, and feeling her heart beat uncomfortably.\n\n“No; though perhaps wisdom is not his strong point, but rather affection and sincerity. However, wisdom lies more in those two qualities than people are apt to imagine. I hope you know by those marks what young gentleman I mean.”\n\n“Yes, I think I do,” said Mary, bravely, her face getting more serious, and her hands cold; “it must be Fred Vincy.”\n\n“He has asked me to consult you about his going into the Church. I hope you will not think that I consented to take a liberty in promising to do so.”\n\n“On the contrary, Mr. Farebrother,” said Mary, giving up the roses, and folding her arms, but unable to look up, “whenever you have anything to say to me I feel honored.”\n\n“But before I enter on that question, let me just touch a point on which your father took me into confidence; by the way, it was that very evening on which I once before fulfilled a mission from Fred, just after he had gone to college. Mr. Garth told me what happened on the night of Featherstone’s death—how you refused to burn the will; and he said that you had some heart-prickings on that subject, because you had been the innocent means of hindering Fred from getting his ten thousand pounds. I have kept that in mind, and I have heard something that may relieve you on that score—may show you that no sin-offering is demanded from you there.”\n\nMr. Farebrother paused a moment and looked at Mary. He meant to give Fred his full advantage, but it would be well, he thought, to clear her mind of any superstitions, such as women sometimes follow when they do a man the wrong of marrying him as an act of atonement. Mary’s cheeks had begun to burn a little, and she was mute.\n\n“I mean, that your action made no real difference to Fred’s lot. I find that the first will would not have been legally good after the burning of the last; it would not have stood if it had been disputed, and you may be sure it would have been disputed. So, on that score, you may feel your mind free.”\n\n“Thank you, Mr. Farebrother,” said Mary, earnestly. “I am grateful to you for remembering my feelings.”\n\n“Well, now I may go on. Fred, you know, has taken his degree. He has worked his way so far, and now the question is, what is he to do? That question is so difficult that he is inclined to follow his father’s wishes and enter the Church, though you know better than I do that he was quite set against that formerly. I have questioned him on the subject, and I confess I see no insuperable objection to his being a clergyman, as things go. He says that he could turn his mind to doing his best in that vocation, on one condition. If that condition were fulfilled I would do my utmost in helping Fred on. After a time—not, of course, at first—he might be with me as my curate, and he would have so much to do that his stipend would be nearly what I used to get as vicar. But I repeat that there is a condition without which all this good cannot come to pass. He has opened his heart to me, Miss Garth, and asked me to plead for him. The condition lies entirely in your feeling.”\n\nMary looked so much moved, that he said after a moment, “Let us walk a little;” and when they were walking he added, “To speak quite plainly, Fred will not take any course which would lessen the chance that you would consent to be his wife; but with that prospect, he will try his best at anything you approve.”\n\n“I cannot possibly say that I will ever be his wife, Mr. Farebrother: but I certainly never will be his wife if he becomes a clergyman. What you say is most generous and kind; I don’t mean for a moment to correct your judgment. It is only that I have my girlish, mocking way of looking at things,” said Mary, with a returning sparkle of playfulness in her answer which only made its modesty more charming.\n\n“He wishes me to report exactly what you think,” said Mr. Farebrother.\n\n“I could not love a man who is ridiculous,” said Mary, not choosing to go deeper. “Fred has sense and knowledge enough to make him respectable, if he likes, in some good worldly business, but I can never imagine him preaching and exhorting, and pronouncing blessings, and praying by the sick, without feeling as if I were looking at a caricature. His being a clergyman would be only for gentility’s sake, and I think there is nothing more contemptible than such imbecile gentility. I used to think that of Mr. Crowse, with his empty face and neat umbrella, and mincing little speeches. What right have such men to represent Christianity—as if it were an institution for getting up idiots genteelly—as if—” Mary checked herself. She had been carried along as if she had been speaking to Fred instead of Mr. Farebrother.\n\n“Young women are severe: they don’t feel the stress of action as men do, though perhaps I ought to make you an exception there. But you don’t put Fred Vincy on so low a level as that?”\n\n“No, indeed, he has plenty of sense, but I think he would not show it as a clergyman. He would be a piece of professional affectation.”\n\n“Then the answer is quite decided. As a clergyman he could have no hope?”\n\nMary shook her head.\n\n“But if he braved all the difficulties of getting his bread in some other way—will you give him the support of hope? May he count on winning you?”\n\n“I think Fred ought not to need telling again what I have already said to him,” Mary answered, with a slight resentment in her manner. “I mean that he ought not to put such questions until he has done something worthy, instead of saying that he could do it.”\n\nMr. Farebrother was silent for a minute or more, and then, as they turned and paused under the shadow of a maple at the end of a grassy walk, said, “I understand that you resist any attempt to fetter you, but either your feeling for Fred Vincy excludes your entertaining another attachment, or it does not: either he may count on your remaining single until he shall have earned your hand, or he may in any case be disappointed. Pardon me, Mary—you know I used to catechise you under that name—but when the state of a woman’s affections touches the happiness of another life—of more lives than one—I think it would be the nobler course for her to be perfectly direct and open.”\n\nMary in her turn was silent, wondering not at Mr. Farebrother’s manner but at his tone, which had a grave restrained emotion in it. When the strange idea flashed across her that his words had reference to himself, she was incredulous, and ashamed of entertaining it. She had never thought that any man could love her except Fred, who had espoused her with the umbrella ring, when she wore socks and little strapped shoes; still less that she could be of any importance to Mr. Farebrother, the cleverest man in her narrow circle. She had only time to feel that all this was hazy and perhaps illusory; but one thing was clear and determined—her answer.\n\n“Since you think it my duty, Mr. Farebrother, I will tell you that I have too strong a feeling for Fred to give him up for any one else. I should never be quite happy if I thought he was unhappy for the loss of me. It has taken such deep root in me—my gratitude to him for always loving me best, and minding so much if I hurt myself, from the time when we were very little. I cannot imagine any new feeling coming to make that weaker. I should like better than anything to see him worthy of every one’s respect. But please tell him I will not promise to marry him till then: I should shame and grieve my father and mother. He is free to choose some one else.”\n\n“Then I have fulfilled my commission thoroughly,” said Mr. Farebrother, putting out his hand to Mary, “and I shall ride back to Middlemarch forthwith. With this prospect before him, we shall get Fred into the right niche somehow, and I hope I shall live to join your hands. God bless you!”\n\n“Oh, please stay, and let me give you some tea,” said Mary. Her eyes filled with tears, for something indefinable, something like the resolute suppression of a pain in Mr. Farebrother’s manner, made her feel suddenly miserable, as she had once felt when she saw her father’s hands trembling in a moment of trouble.\n\n“No, my dear, no. I must get back.”\n\nIn three minutes the Vicar was on horseback again, having gone magnanimously through a duty much harder than the renunciation of whist, or even than the writing of penitential meditations.\n\n# CHAPTER LIII.\n\nIt is but a shallow haste which concludeth insincerity from what outsiders call inconsistency—putting a dead mechanism of “ifs” and “therefores” for the living myriad of hidden suckers whereby the belief and the conduct are wrought into mutual sustainment.\n\nMr. Bulstrode, when he was hoping to acquire a new interest in Lowick, had naturally had an especial wish that the new clergyman should be one whom he thoroughly approved; and he believed it to be a chastisement and admonition directed to his own shortcomings and those of the nation at large, that just about the time when he came in possession of the deeds which made him the proprietor of Stone Court, Mr. Farebrother “read himself” into the quaint little church and preached his first sermon to the congregation of farmers, laborers, and village artisans. It was not that Mr. Bulstrode intended to frequent Lowick Church or to reside at Stone Court for a good while to come: he had bought the excellent farm and fine homestead simply as a retreat which he might gradually enlarge as to the land and beautify as to the dwelling, until it should be conducive to the divine glory that he should enter on it as a residence, partially withdrawing from his present exertions in the administration of business, and throwing more conspicuously on the side of Gospel truth the weight of local landed proprietorship, which Providence might increase by unforeseen occasions of purchase. A strong leading in this direction seemed to have been given in the surprising facility of getting Stone Court, when every one had expected that Mr. Rigg Featherstone would have clung to it as the Garden of Eden. That was what poor old Peter himself had expected; having often, in imagination, looked up through the sods above him, and, unobstructed by perspective, seen his frog-faced legatee enjoying the fine old place to the perpetual surprise and disappointment of other survivors.\n\nBut how little we know what would make paradise for our neighbors! We judge from our own desires, and our neighbors themselves are not always open enough even to throw out a hint of theirs. The cool and judicious Joshua Rigg had not allowed his parent to perceive that Stone Court was anything less than the chief good in his estimation, and he had certainly wished to call it his own. But as Warren Hastings looked at gold and thought of buying Daylesford, so Joshua Rigg looked at Stone Court and thought of buying gold. He had a very distinct and intense vision of his chief good, the vigorous greed which he had inherited having taken a special form by dint of circumstance: and his chief good was to be a moneychanger. From his earliest employment as an errand-boy in a seaport, he had looked through the windows of the moneychangers as other boys look through the windows of the pastry-cooks; the fascination had wrought itself gradually into a deep special passion; he meant, when he had property, to do many things, one of them being to marry a genteel young person; but these were all accidents and joys that imagination could dispense with. The one joy after which his soul thirsted was to have a money-changer’s shop on a much-frequented quay, to have locks all round him of which he held the keys, and to look sublimely cool as he handled the breeding coins of all nations, while helpless Cupidity looked at him enviously from the other side of an iron lattice. The strength of that passion had been a power enabling him to master all the knowledge necessary to gratify it. And when others were thinking that he had settled at Stone Court for life, Joshua himself was thinking that the moment now was not far off when he should settle on the North Quay with the best appointments in safes and locks.\n\nEnough. We are concerned with looking at Joshua Rigg’s sale of his land from Mr. Bulstrode’s point of view, and he interpreted it as a cheering dispensation conveying perhaps a sanction to a purpose which he had for some time entertained without external encouragement; he interpreted it thus, but not too confidently, offering up his thanksgiving in guarded phraseology. His doubts did not arise from the possible relations of the event to Joshua Rigg’s destiny, which belonged to the unmapped regions not taken under the providential government, except perhaps in an imperfect colonial way; but they arose from reflecting that this dispensation too might be a chastisement for himself, as Mr. Farebrother’s induction to the living clearly was.\n\nThis was not what Mr. Bulstrode said to any man for the sake of deceiving him: it was what he said to himself—it was as genuinely his mode of explaining events as any theory of yours may be, if you happen to disagree with him. For the egoism which enters into our theories does not affect their sincerity; rather, the more our egoism is satisfied, the more robust is our belief.\n\nHowever, whether for sanction or for chastisement, Mr. Bulstrode, hardly fifteen months after the death of Peter Featherstone, had become the proprietor of Stone Court, and what Peter would say “if he were worthy to know,” had become an inexhaustible and consolatory subject of conversation to his disappointed relatives. The tables were now turned on that dear brother departed, and to contemplate the frustration of his cunning by the superior cunning of things in general was a cud of delight to Solomon. Mrs. Waule had a melancholy triumph in the proof that it did not answer to make false Featherstones and cut off the genuine; and Sister Martha receiving the news in the Chalky Flats said, “Dear, dear! then the Almighty could have been none so pleased with the almshouses after all.”\n\nAffectionate Mrs. Bulstrode was particularly glad of the advantage which her husband’s health was likely to get from the purchase of Stone Court. Few days passed without his riding thither and looking over some part of the farm with the bailiff, and the evenings were delicious in that quiet spot, when the new hay-ricks lately set up were sending forth odors to mingle with the breath of the rich old garden. One evening, while the sun was still above the horizon and burning in golden lamps among the great walnut boughs, Mr. Bulstrode was pausing on horseback outside the front gate waiting for Caleb Garth, who had met him by appointment to give an opinion on a question of stable drainage, and was now advising the bailiff in the rick-yard.\n\nMr. Bulstrode was conscious of being in a good spiritual frame and more than usually serene, under the influence of his innocent recreation. He was doctrinally convinced that there was a total absence of merit in himself; but that doctrinal conviction may be held without pain when the sense of demerit does not take a distinct shape in memory and revive the tingling of shame or the pang of remorse. Nay, it may be held with intense satisfaction when the depth of our sinning is but a measure for the depth of forgiveness, and a clenching proof that we are peculiar instruments of the divine intention. The memory has as many moods as the temper, and shifts its scenery like a diorama. At this moment Mr. Bulstrode felt as if the sunshine were all one with that of far-off evenings when he was a very young man and used to go out preaching beyond Highbury. And he would willingly have had that service of exhortation in prospect now. The texts were there still, and so was his own facility in expounding them. His brief reverie was interrupted by the return of Caleb Garth, who also was on horseback, and was just shaking his bridle before starting, when he exclaimed—\n\n“Bless my heart! what’s this fellow in black coming along the lane? He’s like one of those men one sees about after the races.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode turned his horse and looked along the lane, but made no reply. The comer was our slight acquaintance Mr. Raffles, whose appearance presented no other change than such as was due to a suit of black and a crape hat-band. He was within three yards of the horseman now, and they could see the flash of recognition in his face as he whirled his stick upward, looking all the while at Mr. Bulstrode, and at last exclaiming:—\n\n“By Jove, Nick, it’s you! I couldn’t be mistaken, though the five-and-twenty years have played old Boguy with us both! How are you, eh? you didn’t expect to see me here. Come, shake us by the hand.” To say that Mr. Raffles’ manner was rather excited would be only one mode of saying that it was evening. Caleb Garth could see that there was a moment of struggle and hesitation in Mr. Bulstrode, but it ended in his putting out his hand coldly to Raffles and saying—\n\n“I did not indeed expect to see you in this remote country place.”\n\n“Well, it belongs to a stepson of mine,” said Raffles, adjusting himself in a swaggering attitude. “I came to see him here before. I’m not so surprised at seeing you, old fellow, because I picked up a letter—what you may call a providential thing. It’s uncommonly fortunate I met you, though; for I don’t care about seeing my stepson: he’s not affectionate, and his poor mother’s gone now. To tell the truth, I came out of love to you, Nick: I came to get your address, for—look here!” Raffles drew a crumpled paper from his pocket.\n\nAlmost any other man than Caleb Garth might have been tempted to linger on the spot for the sake of hearing all he could about a man whose acquaintance with Bulstrode seemed to imply passages in the banker’s life so unlike anything that was known of him in Middlemarch that they must have the nature of a secret to pique curiosity. But Caleb was peculiar: certain human tendencies which are commonly strong were almost absent from his mind; and one of these was curiosity about personal affairs. Especially if there was anything discreditable to be found out concerning another man, Caleb preferred not to know it; and if he had to tell anybody under him that his evil doings were discovered, he was more embarrassed than the culprit. He now spurred his horse, and saying, “I wish you good evening, Mr. Bulstrode; I must be getting home,” set off at a trot.\n\n“You didn’t put your full address to this letter,” Raffles continued. “That was not like the first-rate man of business you used to be. ‘The Shrubs,’—they may be anywhere: you live near at hand, eh?—have cut the London concern altogether—perhaps turned country squire—have a rural mansion to invite me to. Lord, how many years it is ago! The old lady must have been dead a pretty long while—gone to glory without the pain of knowing how poor her daughter was, eh? But, by Jove! you’re very pale and pasty, Nick. Come, if you’re going home, I’ll walk by your side.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode’s usual paleness had in fact taken an almost deathly hue. Five minutes before, the expanse of his life had been submerged in its evening sunshine which shone backward to its remembered morning: sin seemed to be a question of doctrine and inward penitence, humiliation an exercise of the closet, the bearing of his deeds a matter of private vision adjusted solely by spiritual relations and conceptions of the divine purposes. And now, as if by some hideous magic, this loud red figure had risen before him in unmanageable solidity—an incorporate past which had not entered into his imagination of chastisements. But Mr. Bulstrode’s thought was busy, and he was not a man to act or speak rashly.\n\n“I was going home,” he said, “but I can defer my ride a little. And you can, if you please, rest here.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Raffles, making a grimace. “I don’t care now about seeing my stepson. I’d rather go home with you.”\n\n“Your stepson, if Mr. Rigg Featherstone was he, is here no longer. I am master here now.”\n\nRaffles opened wide eyes, and gave a long whistle of surprise, before he said, “Well then, I’ve no objection. I’ve had enough walking from the coach-road. I never was much of a walker, or rider either. What I like is a smart vehicle and a spirited cob. I was always a little heavy in the saddle. What a pleasant surprise it must be to you to see me, old fellow!” he continued, as they turned towards the house. “You don’t say so; but you never took your luck heartily—you were always thinking of improving the occasion—you’d such a gift for improving your luck.”\n\nMr. Raffles seemed greatly to enjoy his own wit, and swung his leg in a swaggering manner which was rather too much for his companion’s judicious patience.\n\n“If I remember rightly,” Mr. Bulstrode observed, with chill anger, “our acquaintance many years ago had not the sort of intimacy which you are now assuming, Mr. Raffles. Any services you desire of me will be the more readily rendered if you will avoid a tone of familiarity which did not lie in our former intercourse, and can hardly be warranted by more than twenty years of separation.”\n\n“You don’t like being called Nick? Why, I always called you Nick in my heart, and though lost to sight, to memory dear. By Jove! my feelings have ripened for you like fine old cognac. I hope you’ve got some in the house now. Josh filled my flask well the last time.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode had not yet fully learned that even the desire for cognac was not stronger in Raffles than the desire to torment, and that a hint of annoyance always served him as a fresh cue. But it was at least clear that further objection was useless, and Mr. Bulstrode, in giving orders to the housekeeper for the accommodation of the guest, had a resolute air of quietude.\n\nThere was the comfort of thinking that this housekeeper had been in the service of Rigg also, and might accept the idea that Mr. Bulstrode entertained Raffles merely as a friend of her former master.\n\nWhen there was food and drink spread before his visitor in the wainscoted parlor, and no witness in the room, Mr. Bulstrode said—\n\n“Your habits and mine are so different, Mr. Raffles, that we can hardly enjoy each other’s society. The wisest plan for both of us will therefore be to part as soon as possible. Since you say that you wished to meet me, you probably considered that you had some business to transact with me. But under the circumstances I will invite you to remain here for the night, and I will myself ride over here early to-morrow morning—before breakfast, in fact—when I can receive any communication you have to make to me.”\n\n“With all my heart,” said Raffles; “this is a comfortable place—a little dull for a continuance; but I can put up with it for a night, with this good liquor and the prospect of seeing you again in the morning. You’re a much better host than my stepson was; but Josh owed me a bit of a grudge for marrying his mother; and between you and me there was never anything but kindness.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode, hoping that the peculiar mixture of joviality and sneering in Raffles’ manner was a good deal the effect of drink, had determined to wait till he was quite sober before he spent more words upon him. But he rode home with a terribly lucid vision of the difficulty there would be in arranging any result that could be permanently counted on with this man. It was inevitable that he should wish to get rid of John Raffles, though his reappearance could not be regarded as lying outside the divine plan. The spirit of evil might have sent him to threaten Mr. Bulstrode’s subversion as an instrument of good; but the threat must have been permitted, and was a chastisement of a new kind. It was an hour of anguish for him very different from the hours in which his struggle had been securely private, and which had ended with a sense that his secret misdeeds were pardoned and his services accepted. Those misdeeds even when committed—had they not been half sanctified by the singleness of his desire to devote himself and all he possessed to the furtherance of the divine scheme? And was he after all to become a mere stone of stumbling and a rock of offence? For who would understand the work within him? Who would not, when there was the pretext of casting disgrace upon him, confound his whole life and the truths he had espoused, in one heap of obloquy?\n\nIn his closest meditations the life-long habit of Mr. Bulstrode’s mind clad his most egoistic terrors in doctrinal references to superhuman ends. But even while we are talking and meditating about the earth’s orbit and the solar system, what we feel and adjust our movements to is the stable earth and the changing day. And now within all the automatic succession of theoretic phrases—distinct and inmost as the shiver and the ache of oncoming fever when we are discussing abstract pain, was the forecast of disgrace in the presence of his neighbors and of his own wife. For the pain, as well as the public estimate of disgrace, depends on the amount of previous profession. To men who only aim at escaping felony, nothing short of the prisoner’s dock is disgrace. But Mr. Bulstrode had aimed at being an eminent Christian.\n\nIt was not more than half-past seven in the morning when he again reached Stone Court. The fine old place never looked more like a delightful home than at that moment; the great white lilies were in flower, the nasturtiums, their pretty leaves all silvered with dew, were running away over the low stone wall; the very noises all around had a heart of peace within them. But everything was spoiled for the owner as he walked on the gravel in front and awaited the descent of Mr. Raffles, with whom he was condemned to breakfast.\n\nIt was not long before they were seated together in the wainscoted parlor over their tea and toast, which was as much as Raffles cared to take at that early hour. The difference between his morning and evening self was not so great as his companion had imagined that it might be; the delight in tormenting was perhaps even the stronger because his spirits were rather less highly pitched. Certainly his manners seemed more disagreeable by the morning light.\n\n“As I have little time to spare, Mr. Raffles,” said the banker, who could hardly do more than sip his tea and break his toast without eating it, “I shall be obliged if you will mention at once the ground on which you wished to meet with me. I presume that you have a home elsewhere and will be glad to return to it.”\n\n“Why, if a man has got any heart, doesn’t he want to see an old friend, Nick?—I must call you Nick—we always did call you young Nick when we knew you meant to marry the old widow. Some said you had a handsome family likeness to old Nick, but that was your mother’s fault, calling you Nicholas. Aren’t you glad to see me again? I expected an invite to stay with you at some pretty place. My own establishment is broken up now my wife’s dead. I’ve no particular attachment to any spot; I would as soon settle hereabout as anywhere.”\n\n“May I ask why you returned from America? I considered that the strong wish you expressed to go there, when an adequate sum was furnished, was tantamount to an engagement that you would remain there for life.”\n\n“Never knew that a wish to go to a place was the same thing as a wish to stay. But I did stay a matter of ten years; it didn’t suit me to stay any longer. And I’m not going again, Nick.” Here Mr. Raffles winked slowly as he looked at Mr. Bulstrode.\n\n“Do you wish to be settled in any business? What is your calling now?”\n\n“Thank you, my calling is to enjoy myself as much as I can. I don’t care about working any more. If I did anything it would be a little travelling in the tobacco line—or something of that sort, which takes a man into agreeable company. But not without an independence to fall back upon. That’s what I want: I’m not so strong as I was, Nick, though I’ve got more color than you. I want an independence.”\n\n“That could be supplied to you, if you would engage to keep at a distance,” said Mr. Bulstrode, perhaps with a little too much eagerness in his undertone.\n\n“That must be as it suits my convenience,” said Raffles coolly. “I see no reason why I shouldn’t make a few acquaintances hereabout. I’m not ashamed of myself as company for anybody. I dropped my portmanteau at the turnpike when I got down—change of linen—genuine—honor bright—more than fronts and wristbands; and with this suit of mourning, straps and everything, I should do you credit among the nobs here.” Mr. Raffles had pushed away his chair and looked down at himself, particularly at his straps. His chief intention was to annoy Bulstrode, but he really thought that his appearance now would produce a good effect, and that he was not only handsome and witty, but clad in a mourning style which implied solid connections.\n\n“If you intend to rely on me in any way, Mr. Raffles,” said Bulstrode, after a moment’s pause, “you will expect to meet my wishes.”\n\n“Ah, to be sure,” said Raffles, with a mocking cordiality. “Didn’t I always do it? Lord, you made a pretty thing out of me, and I got but little. I’ve often thought since, I might have done better by telling the old woman that I’d found her daughter and her grandchild: it would have suited my feelings better; I’ve got a soft place in my heart. But you’ve buried the old lady by this time, I suppose—it’s all one to her now. And you’ve got your fortune out of that profitable business which had such a blessing on it. You’ve taken to being a nob, buying land, being a country bashaw. Still in the Dissenting line, eh? Still godly? Or taken to the Church as more genteel?”\n\nThis time Mr. Raffles’ slow wink and slight protrusion of his tongue was worse than a nightmare, because it held the certitude that it was not a nightmare, but a waking misery. Mr. Bulstrode felt a shuddering nausea, and did not speak, but was considering diligently whether he should not leave Raffles to do as he would, and simply defy him as a slanderer. The man would soon show himself disreputable enough to make people disbelieve him. “But not when he tells any ugly-looking truth about you,” said discerning consciousness. And again: it seemed no wrong to keep Raffles at a distance, but Mr. Bulstrode shrank from the direct falsehood of denying true statements. It was one thing to look back on forgiven sins, nay, to explain questionable conformity to lax customs, and another to enter deliberately on the necessity of falsehood.\n\nBut since Bulstrode did not speak, Raffles ran on, by way of using time to the utmost.\n\n“I’ve not had such fine luck as you, by Jove! Things went confoundedly with me in New York; those Yankees are cool hands, and a man of gentlemanly feelings has no chance with them. I married when I came back—a nice woman in the tobacco trade—very fond of me—but the trade was restricted, as we say. She had been settled there a good many years by a friend; but there was a son too much in the case. Josh and I never hit it off. However, I made the most of the position, and I’ve always taken my glass in good company. It’s been all on the square with me; I’m as open as the day. You won’t take it ill of me that I didn’t look you up before. I’ve got a complaint that makes me a little dilatory. I thought you were trading and praying away in London still, and didn’t find you there. But you see I was sent to you, Nick—perhaps for a blessing to both of us.”\n\nMr. Raffles ended with a jocose snuffle: no man felt his intellect more superior to religious cant. And if the cunning which calculates on the meanest feelings in men could be called intellect, he had his share, for under the blurting rallying tone with which he spoke to Bulstrode, there was an evident selection of statements, as if they had been so many moves at chess. Meanwhile Bulstrode had determined on his move, and he said, with gathered resolution—\n\n“You will do well to reflect, Mr. Raffles, that it is possible for a man to overreach himself in the effort to secure undue advantage. Although I am not in any way bound to you, I am willing to supply you with a regular annuity—in quarterly payments—so long as you fulfil a promise to remain at a distance from this neighborhood. It is in your power to choose. If you insist on remaining here, even for a short time, you will get nothing from me. I shall decline to know you.”\n\n“Ha, ha!” said Raffles, with an affected explosion, “that reminds me of a droll dog of a thief who declined to know the constable.”\n\n“Your allusions are lost on me sir,” said Bulstrode, with white heat; “the law has no hold on me either through your agency or any other.”\n\n“You can’t understand a joke, my good fellow. I only meant that I should never decline to know you. But let us be serious. Your quarterly payment won’t quite suit me. I like my freedom.”\n\nHere Raffles rose and stalked once or twice up and down the room, swinging his leg, and assuming an air of masterly meditation. At last he stopped opposite Bulstrode, and said, “I’ll tell you what! Give us a couple of hundreds—come, that’s modest—and I’ll go away—honor bright!—pick up my portmanteau and go away. But I shall not give up my liberty for a dirty annuity. I shall come and go where I like. Perhaps it may suit me to stay away, and correspond with a friend; perhaps not. Have you the money with you?”\n\n“No, I have one hundred,” said Bulstrode, feeling the immediate riddance too great a relief to be rejected on the ground of future uncertainties. “I will forward you the other if you will mention an address.”\n\n“No, I’ll wait here till you bring it,” said Raffles. “I’ll take a stroll and have a snack, and you’ll be back by that time.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode’s sickly body, shattered by the agitations he had gone through since the last evening, made him feel abjectly in the power of this loud invulnerable man. At that moment he snatched at a temporary repose to be won on any terms. He was rising to do what Raffles suggested, when the latter said, lifting up his finger as if with a sudden recollection—\n\n“I did have another look after Sarah again, though I didn’t tell you; I’d a tender conscience about that pretty young woman. I didn’t find her, but I found out her husband’s name, and I made a note of it. But hang it, I lost my pocketbook. However, if I heard it, I should know it again. I’ve got my faculties as if I was in my prime, but names wear out, by Jove! Sometimes I’m no better than a confounded tax-paper before the names are filled in. However, if I hear of her and her family, you shall know, Nick. You’d like to do something for her, now she’s your step-daughter.”\n\n“Doubtless,” said Mr. Bulstrode, with the usual steady look of his light-gray eyes; “though that might reduce my power of assisting you.”\n\nAs he walked out of the room, Raffles winked slowly at his back, and then turned towards the window to watch the banker riding away—virtually at his command. His lips first curled with a smile and then opened with a short triumphant laugh.\n\n“But what the deuce was the name?” he presently said, half aloud, scratching his head, and wrinkling his brows horizontally. He had not really cared or thought about this point of forgetfulness until it occurred to him in his invention of annoyances for Bulstrode.\n\n“It began with L; it was almost all l’s I fancy,” he went on, with a sense that he was getting hold of the slippery name. But the hold was too slight, and he soon got tired of this mental chase; for few men were more impatient of private occupation or more in need of making themselves continually heard than Mr. Raffles. He preferred using his time in pleasant conversation with the bailiff and the housekeeper, from whom he gathered as much as he wanted to know about Mr. Bulstrode’s position in Middlemarch.\n\nAfter all, however, there was a dull space of time which needed relieving with bread and cheese and ale, and when he was seated alone with these resources in the wainscoted parlor, he suddenly slapped his knee, and exclaimed, “Ladislaw!” That action of memory which he had tried to set going, and had abandoned in despair, had suddenly completed itself without conscious effort—a common experience, agreeable as a completed sneeze, even if the name remembered is of no value. Raffles immediately took out his pocket-book, and wrote down the name, not because he expected to use it, but merely for the sake of not being at a loss if he ever did happen to want it. He was not going to tell Bulstrode: there was no actual good in telling, and to a mind like that of Mr. Raffles there is always probable good in a secret.\n\nHe was satisfied with his present success, and by three o’clock that day he had taken up his portmanteau at the turnpike and mounted the coach, relieving Mr. Bulstrode’s eyes of an ugly black spot on the landscape at Stone Court, but not relieving him of the dread that the black spot might reappear and become inseparable even from the vision of his hearth.\n\n# BOOK VI. THE WIDOW AND THE WIFE. CHAPTER LIV.\n\n“Negli occhi porta la mia donna Amore; Per che si fa gentil ciò ch’ella mira: Ov’ella passa, ogni uom ver lei si gira, E cui saluta fa tremar lo core.\n\nSicchè, bassando il viso, tutto smore, E d’ogni suo difetto allor sospira: Fuggon dinanzi a lei Superbia ed Ira: Aiutatemi, donne, a farle onore.\n\nOgni dolcezza, ogni pensiero umile Nasce nel core a chi parlar la sente; Ond’è beato chi prima la vide. Quel ch’ella par quand’ un poco sorride, Non si può dicer, nè tener a mente, Si è nuovo miracolo gentile.” —DANTE: La Vita Nuova.\n\nBy that delightful morning when the hay-ricks at Stone Court were scenting the air quite impartially, as if Mr. Raffles had been a guest worthy of finest incense, Dorothea had again taken up her abode at Lowick Manor. After three months Freshitt had become rather oppressive: to sit like a model for Saint Catherine looking rapturously at Celia’s baby would not do for many hours in the day, and to remain in that momentous babe’s presence with persistent disregard was a course that could not have been tolerated in a childless sister. Dorothea would have been capable of carrying baby joyfully for a mile if there had been need, and of loving it the more tenderly for that labor; but to an aunt who does not recognize her infant nephew as Bouddha, and has nothing to do for him but to admire, his behavior is apt to appear monotonous, and the interest of watching him exhaustible. This possibility was quite hidden from Celia, who felt that Dorothea’s childless widowhood fell in quite prettily with the birth of little Arthur (baby was named after Mr. Brooke).\n\n“Dodo is just the creature not to mind about having anything of her own—children or anything!” said Celia to her husband. “And if she had had a baby, it never could have been such a dear as Arthur. Could it, James?\n\n“Not if it had been like Casaubon,” said Sir James, conscious of some indirectness in his answer, and of holding a strictly private opinion as to the perfections of his first-born.\n\n“No! just imagine! Really it was a mercy,” said Celia; “and I think it is very nice for Dodo to be a widow. She can be just as fond of our baby as if it were her own, and she can have as many notions of her own as she likes.”\n\n“It is a pity she was not a queen,” said the devout Sir James.\n\n“But what should we have been then? We must have been something else,” said Celia, objecting to so laborious a flight of imagination. “I like her better as she is.”\n\nHence, when she found that Dorothea was making arrangements for her final departure to Lowick, Celia raised her eyebrows with disappointment, and in her quiet unemphatic way shot a needle-arrow of sarcasm.\n\n“What will you do at Lowick, Dodo? You say yourself there is nothing to be done there: everybody is so clean and well off, it makes you quite melancholy. And here you have been so happy going all about Tipton with Mr. Garth into the worst backyards. And now uncle is abroad, you and Mr. Garth can have it all your own way; and I am sure James does everything you tell him.”\n\n“I shall often come here, and I shall see how baby grows all the better,” said Dorothea.\n\n“But you will never see him washed,” said Celia; “and that is quite the best part of the day.” She was almost pouting: it did seem to her very hard in Dodo to go away from the baby when she might stay.\n\n“Dear Kitty, I will come and stay all night on purpose,” said Dorothea; “but I want to be alone now, and in my own home. I wish to know the Farebrothers better, and to talk to Mr. Farebrother about what there is to be done in Middlemarch.”\n\nDorothea’s native strength of will was no longer all converted into resolute submission. She had a great yearning to be at Lowick, and was simply determined to go, not feeling bound to tell all her reasons. But every one around her disapproved. Sir James was much pained, and offered that they should all migrate to Cheltenham for a few months with the sacred ark, otherwise called a cradle: at that period a man could hardly know what to propose if Cheltenham were rejected.\n\nThe Dowager Lady Chettam, just returned from a visit to her daughter in town, wished, at least, that Mrs. Vigo should be written to, and invited to accept the office of companion to Mrs. Casaubon: it was not credible that Dorothea as a young widow would think of living alone in the house at Lowick. Mrs. Vigo had been reader and secretary to royal personages, and in point of knowledge and sentiments even Dorothea could have nothing to object to her.\n\nMrs. Cadwallader said, privately, “You will certainly go mad in that house alone, my dear. You will see visions. We have all got to exert ourselves a little to keep sane, and call things by the same names as other people call them by. To be sure, for younger sons and women who have no money, it is a sort of provision to go mad: they are taken care of then. But you must not run into that. I dare say you are a little bored here with our good dowager; but think what a bore you might become yourself to your fellow-creatures if you were always playing tragedy queen and taking things sublimely. Sitting alone in that library at Lowick you may fancy yourself ruling the weather; you must get a few people round you who wouldn’t believe you if you told them. That is a good lowering medicine.”\n\n“I never called everything by the same name that all the people about me did,” said Dorothea, stoutly.\n\n“But I suppose you have found out your mistake, my dear,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, “and that is a proof of sanity.”\n\nDorothea was aware of the sting, but it did not hurt her. “No,” she said, “I still think that the greater part of the world is mistaken about many things. Surely one may be sane and yet think so, since the greater part of the world has often had to come round from its opinion.”\n\nMrs. Cadwallader said no more on that point to Dorothea, but to her husband she remarked, “It will be well for her to marry again as soon as it is proper, if one could get her among the right people. Of course the Chettams would not wish it. But I see clearly a husband is the best thing to keep her in order. If we were not so poor I would invite Lord Triton. He will be marquis some day, and there is no denying that she would make a good marchioness: she looks handsomer than ever in her mourning.”\n\n“My dear Elinor, do let the poor woman alone. Such contrivances are of no use,” said the easy Rector.\n\n“No use? How are matches made, except by bringing men and women together? And it is a shame that her uncle should have run away and shut up the Grange just now. There ought to be plenty of eligible matches invited to Freshitt and the Grange. Lord Triton is precisely the man: full of plans for making the people happy in a soft-headed sort of way. That would just suit Mrs. Casaubon.”\n\n“Let Mrs. Casaubon choose for herself, Elinor.”\n\n“That is the nonsense you wise men talk! How can she choose if she has no variety to choose from? A woman’s choice usually means taking the only man she can get. Mark my words, Humphrey. If her friends don’t exert themselves, there will be a worse business than the Casaubon business yet.”\n\n“For heaven’s sake don’t touch on that topic, Elinor! It is a very sore point with Sir James. He would be deeply offended if you entered on it to him unnecessarily.”\n\n“I have never entered on it,” said Mrs Cadwallader, opening her hands. “Celia told me all about the will at the beginning, without any asking of mine.”\n\n“Yes, yes; but they want the thing hushed up, and I understand that the young fellow is going out of the neighborhood.”\n\nMrs. Cadwallader said nothing, but gave her husband three significant nods, with a very sarcastic expression in her dark eyes.\n\nDorothea quietly persisted in spite of remonstrance and persuasion. So by the end of June the shutters were all opened at Lowick Manor, and the morning gazed calmly into the library, shining on the rows of note-books as it shines on the weary waste planted with huge stones, the mute memorial of a forgotten faith; and the evening laden with roses entered silently into the blue-green boudoir where Dorothea chose oftenest to sit. At first she walked into every room, questioning the eighteen months of her married life, and carrying on her thoughts as if they were a speech to be heard by her husband. Then, she lingered in the library and could not be at rest till she had carefully ranged all the note-books as she imagined that he would wish to see them, in orderly sequence. The pity which had been the restraining compelling motive in her life with him still clung about his image, even while she remonstrated with him in indignant thought and told him that he was unjust. One little act of hers may perhaps be smiled at as superstitious. The Synoptical Tabulation for the use of Mrs. Casaubon, she carefully enclosed and sealed, writing within the envelope, “I could not use it. Do you not see now that I could not submit my soul to yours, by working hopelessly at what I have no belief in—Dorothea?” Then she deposited the paper in her own desk.\n\nThat silent colloquy was perhaps only the more earnest because underneath and through it all there was always the deep longing which had really determined her to come to Lowick. The longing was to see Will Ladislaw. She did not know any good that could come of their meeting: she was helpless; her hands had been tied from making up to him for any unfairness in his lot. But her soul thirsted to see him. How could it be otherwise? If a princess in the days of enchantment had seen a four-footed creature from among those which live in herds come to her once and again with a human gaze which rested upon her with choice and beseeching, what would she think of in her journeying, what would she look for when the herds passed her? Surely for the gaze which had found her, and which she would know again. Life would be no better than candle-light tinsel and daylight rubbish if our spirits were not touched by what has been, to issues of longing and constancy. It was true that Dorothea wanted to know the Farebrothers better, and especially to talk to the new rector, but also true that remembering what Lydgate had told her about Will Ladislaw and little Miss Noble, she counted on Will’s coming to Lowick to see the Farebrother family. The very first Sunday, before she entered the church, she saw him as she had seen him the last time she was there, alone in the clergyman’s pew; but when she entered his figure was gone.\n\nIn the week-days when she went to see the ladies at the Rectory, she listened in vain for some word that they might let fall about Will; but it seemed to her that Mrs. Farebrother talked of every one else in the neighborhood and out of it.\n\n“Probably some of Mr. Farebrother’s Middlemarch hearers may follow him to Lowick sometimes. Do you not think so?” said Dorothea, rather despising herself for having a secret motive in asking the question.\n\n“If they are wise they will, Mrs. Casaubon,” said the old lady. “I see that you set a right value on my son’s preaching. His grandfather on my side was an excellent clergyman, but his father was in the law:—most exemplary and honest nevertheless, which is a reason for our never being rich. They say Fortune is a woman and capricious. But sometimes she is a good woman and gives to those who merit, which has been the case with you, Mrs. Casaubon, who have given a living to my son.”\n\nMrs. Farebrother recurred to her knitting with a dignified satisfaction in her neat little effort at oratory, but this was not what Dorothea wanted to hear. Poor thing! she did not even know whether Will Ladislaw was still at Middlemarch, and there was no one whom she dared to ask, unless it were Lydgate. But just now she could not see Lydgate without sending for him or going to seek him. Perhaps Will Ladislaw, having heard of that strange ban against him left by Mr. Casaubon, had felt it better that he and she should not meet again, and perhaps she was wrong to wish for a meeting that others might find many good reasons against. Still “I do wish it” came at the end of those wise reflections as naturally as a sob after holding the breath. And the meeting did happen, but in a formal way quite unexpected by her.\n\nOne morning, about eleven, Dorothea was seated in her boudoir with a map of the land attached to the manor and other papers before her, which were to help her in making an exact statement for herself of her income and affairs. She had not yet applied herself to her work, but was seated with her hands folded on her lap, looking out along the avenue of limes to the distant fields. Every leaf was at rest in the sunshine, the familiar scene was changeless, and seemed to represent the prospect of her life, full of motiveless ease—motiveless, if her own energy could not seek out reasons for ardent action. The widow’s cap of those times made an oval frame for the face, and had a crown standing up; the dress was an experiment in the utmost laying on of crape; but this heavy solemnity of clothing made her face look all the younger, with its recovered bloom, and the sweet, inquiring candor of her eyes.\n\nHer reverie was broken by Tantripp, who came to say that Mr. Ladislaw was below, and begged permission to see Madam if it were not too early.\n\n“I will see him,” said Dorothea, rising immediately. “Let him be shown into the drawing-room.”\n\nThe drawing-room was the most neutral room in the house to her—the one least associated with the trials of her married life: the damask matched the wood-work, which was all white and gold; there were two tall mirrors and tables with nothing on them—in brief, it was a room where you had no reason for sitting in one place rather than in another. It was below the boudoir, and had also a bow-window looking out on the avenue. But when Pratt showed Will Ladislaw into it the window was open; and a winged visitor, buzzing in and out now and then without minding the furniture, made the room look less formal and uninhabited.\n\n“Glad to see you here again, sir,” said Pratt, lingering to adjust a blind.\n\n“I am only come to say good-by, Pratt,” said Will, who wished even the butler to know that he was too proud to hang about Mrs. Casaubon now she was a rich widow.\n\n“Very sorry to hear it, sir,” said Pratt, retiring. Of course, as a servant who was to be told nothing, he knew the fact of which Ladislaw was still ignorant, and had drawn his inferences; indeed, had not differed from his betrothed Tantripp when she said, “Your master was as jealous as a fiend—and no reason. Madam would look higher than Mr. Ladislaw, else I don’t know her. Mrs. Cadwallader’s maid says there’s a lord coming who is to marry her when the mourning’s over.”\n\nThere were not many moments for Will to walk about with his hat in his hand before Dorothea entered. The meeting was very different from that first meeting in Rome when Will had been embarrassed and Dorothea calm. This time he felt miserable but determined, while she was in a state of agitation which could not be hidden. Just outside the door she had felt that this longed-for meeting was after all too difficult, and when she saw Will advancing towards her, the deep blush which was rare in her came with painful suddenness. Neither of them knew how it was, but neither of them spoke. She gave her hand for a moment, and then they went to sit down near the window, she on one settee and he on another opposite. Will was peculiarly uneasy: it seemed to him not like Dorothea that the mere fact of her being a widow should cause such a change in her manner of receiving him; and he knew of no other condition which could have affected their previous relation to each other—except that, as his imagination at once told him, her friends might have been poisoning her mind with their suspicions of him.\n\n“I hope I have not presumed too much in calling,” said Will; “I could not bear to leave the neighborhood and begin a new life without seeing you to say good-by.”\n\n“Presumed? Surely not. I should have thought it unkind if you had not wished to see me,” said Dorothea, her habit of speaking with perfect genuineness asserting itself through all her uncertainty and agitation. “Are you going away immediately?”\n\n“Very soon, I think. I intend to go to town and eat my dinners as a barrister, since, they say, that is the preparation for all public business. There will be a great deal of political work to be done by-and-by, and I mean to try and do some of it. Other men have managed to win an honorable position for themselves without family or money.”\n\n“And that will make it all the more honorable,” said Dorothea, ardently. “Besides, you have so many talents. I have heard from my uncle how well you speak in public, so that every one is sorry when you leave off, and how clearly you can explain things. And you care that justice should be done to every one. I am so glad. When we were in Rome, I thought you only cared for poetry and art, and the things that adorn life for us who are well off. But now I know you think about the rest of the world.”\n\nWhile she was speaking Dorothea had lost her personal embarrassment, and had become like her former self. She looked at Will with a direct glance, full of delighted confidence.\n\n“You approve of my going away for years, then, and never coming here again till I have made myself of some mark in the world?” said Will, trying hard to reconcile the utmost pride with the utmost effort to get an expression of strong feeling from Dorothea.\n\nShe was not aware how long it was before she answered. She had turned her head and was looking out of the window on the rose-bushes, which seemed to have in them the summers of all the years when Will would be away. This was not judicious behavior. But Dorothea never thought of studying her manners: she thought only of bowing to a sad necessity which divided her from Will. Those first words of his about his intentions had seemed to make everything clear to her: he knew, she supposed, all about Mr. Casaubon’s final conduct in relation to him, and it had come to him with the same sort of shock as to herself. He had never felt more than friendship for her—had never had anything in his mind to justify what she felt to be her husband’s outrage on the feelings of both: and that friendship he still felt. Something which may be called an inward silent sob had gone on in Dorothea before she said with a pure voice, just trembling in the last words as if only from its liquid flexibility—\n\n“Yes, it must be right for you to do as you say. I shall be very happy when I hear that you have made your value felt. But you must have patience. It will perhaps be a long while.”\n\nWill never quite knew how it was that he saved himself from falling down at her feet, when the “long while” came forth with its gentle tremor. He used to say that the horrible hue and surface of her crape dress was most likely the sufficient controlling force. He sat still, however, and only said—\n\n“I shall never hear from you. And you will forget all about me.”\n\n“No,” said Dorothea, “I shall never forget you. I have never forgotten any one whom I once knew. My life has never been crowded, and seems not likely to be so. And I have a great deal of space for memory at Lowick, haven’t I?” She smiled.\n\n“Good God!” Will burst out passionately, rising, with his hat still in his hand, and walking away to a marble table, where he suddenly turned and leaned his back against it. The blood had mounted to his face and neck, and he looked almost angry. It had seemed to him as if they were like two creatures slowly turning to marble in each other’s presence, while their hearts were conscious and their eyes were yearning. But there was no help for it. It should never be true of him that in this meeting to which he had come with bitter resolution he had ended by a confession which might be interpreted into asking for her fortune. Moreover, it was actually true that he was fearful of the effect which such confessions might have on Dorothea herself.\n\nShe looked at him from that distance in some trouble, imagining that there might have been an offence in her words. But all the while there was a current of thought in her about his probable want of money, and the impossibility of her helping him. If her uncle had been at home, something might have been done through him! It was this preoccupation with the hardship of Will’s wanting money, while she had what ought to have been his share, which led her to say, seeing that he remained silent and looked away from her—\n\n“I wonder whether you would like to have that miniature which hangs up-stairs—I mean that beautiful miniature of your grandmother. I think it is not right for me to keep it, if you would wish to have it. It is wonderfully like you.”\n\n“You are very good,” said Will, irritably. “No; I don’t mind about it. It is not very consoling to have one’s own likeness. It would be more consoling if others wanted to have it.”\n\n“I thought you would like to cherish her memory—I thought—” Dorothea broke off an instant, her imagination suddenly warning her away from Aunt Julia’s history—“you would surely like to have the miniature as a family memorial.”\n\n“Why should I have that, when I have nothing else! A man with only a portmanteau for his stowage must keep his memorials in his head.”\n\nWill spoke at random: he was merely venting his petulance; it was a little too exasperating to have his grandmother’s portrait offered him at that moment. But to Dorothea’s feeling his words had a peculiar sting. She rose and said with a touch of indignation as well as hauteur—\n\n“You are much the happier of us two, Mr. Ladislaw, to have nothing.”\n\nWill was startled. Whatever the words might be, the tone seemed like a dismissal; and quitting his leaning posture, he walked a little way towards her. Their eyes met, but with a strange questioning gravity. Something was keeping their minds aloof, and each was left to conjecture what was in the other. Will had really never thought of himself as having a claim of inheritance on the property which was held by Dorothea, and would have required a narrative to make him understand her present feeling.\n\n“I never felt it a misfortune to have nothing till now,” he said. “But poverty may be as bad as leprosy, if it divides us from what we most care for.”\n\nThe words cut Dorothea to the heart, and made her relent. She answered in a tone of sad fellowship.\n\n“Sorrow comes in so many ways. Two years ago I had no notion of that—I mean of the unexpected way in which trouble comes, and ties our hands, and makes us silent when we long to speak. I used to despise women a little for not shaping their lives more, and doing better things. I was very fond of doing as I liked, but I have almost given it up,” she ended, smiling playfully.\n\n“I have not given up doing as I like, but I can very seldom do it,” said Will. He was standing two yards from her with his mind full of contradictory desires and resolves—desiring some unmistakable proof that she loved him, and yet dreading the position into which such a proof might bring him. “The thing one most longs for may be surrounded with conditions that would be intolerable.”\n\nAt this moment Pratt entered and said, “Sir James Chettam is in the library, madam.”\n\n“Ask Sir James to come in here,” said Dorothea, immediately. It was as if the same electric shock had passed through her and Will. Each of them felt proudly resistant, and neither looked at the other, while they awaited Sir James’s entrance.\n\nAfter shaking hands with Dorothea, he bowed as slightly as possible to Ladislaw, who repaid the slightness exactly, and then going towards Dorothea, said—\n\n“I must say good-by, Mrs. Casaubon; and probably for a long while.”\n\nDorothea put out her hand and said her good-by cordially. The sense that Sir James was depreciating Will, and behaving rudely to him, roused her resolution and dignity: there was no touch of confusion in her manner. And when Will had left the room, she looked with such calm self-possession at Sir James, saying, “How is Celia?” that he was obliged to behave as if nothing had annoyed him. And what would be the use of behaving otherwise? Indeed, Sir James shrank with so much dislike from the association even in thought of Dorothea with Ladislaw as her possible lover, that he would himself have wished to avoid an outward show of displeasure which would have recognized the disagreeable possibility. If any one had asked him why he shrank in that way, I am not sure that he would at first have said anything fuller or more precise than “That Ladislaw!”—though on reflection he might have urged that Mr. Casaubon’s codicil, barring Dorothea’s marriage with Will, except under a penalty, was enough to cast unfitness over any relation at all between them. His aversion was all the stronger because he felt himself unable to interfere.\n\nBut Sir James was a power in a way unguessed by himself. Entering at that moment, he was an incorporation of the strongest reasons through which Will’s pride became a repellent force, keeping him asunder from Dorothea.\n\n# CHAPTER LV.\n\nHath she her faults? I would you had them too. They are the fruity must of soundest wine; Or say, they are regenerating fire Such as hath turned the dense black element Into a crystal pathway for the sun.\n\nIf youth is the season of hope, it is often so only in the sense that our elders are hopeful about us; for no age is so apt as youth to think its emotions, partings, and resolves are the last of their kind. Each crisis seems final, simply because it is new. We are told that the oldest inhabitants in Peru do not cease to be agitated by the earthquakes, but they probably see beyond each shock, and reflect that there are plenty more to come.\n\nTo Dorothea, still in that time of youth when the eyes with their long full lashes look out after their rain of tears unsoiled and unwearied as a freshly opened passion-flower, that morning’s parting with Will Ladislaw seemed to be the close of their personal relations. He was going away into the distance of unknown years, and if ever he came back he would be another man. The actual state of his mind—his proud resolve to give the lie beforehand to any suspicion that he would play the needy adventurer seeking a rich woman—lay quite out of her imagination, and she had interpreted all his behavior easily enough by her supposition that Mr. Casaubon’s codicil seemed to him, as it did to her, a gross and cruel interdict on any active friendship between them. Their young delight in speaking to each other, and saying what no one else would care to hear, was forever ended, and become a treasure of the past. For this very reason she dwelt on it without inward check. That unique happiness too was dead, and in its shadowed silent chamber she might vent the passionate grief which she herself wondered at. For the first time she took down the miniature from the wall and kept it before her, liking to blend the woman who had been too hardly judged with the grandson whom her own heart and judgment defended. Can any one who has rejoiced in woman’s tenderness think it a reproach to her that she took the little oval picture in her palm and made a bed for it there, and leaned her cheek upon it, as if that would soothe the creatures who had suffered unjust condemnation? She did not know then that it was Love who had come to her briefly, as in a dream before awaking, with the hues of morning on his wings—that it was Love to whom she was sobbing her farewell as his image was banished by the blameless rigor of irresistible day. She only felt that there was something irrevocably amiss and lost in her lot, and her thoughts about the future were the more readily shapen into resolve. Ardent souls, ready to construct their coming lives, are apt to commit themselves to the fulfilment of their own visions.\n\nOne day that she went to Freshitt to fulfil her promise of staying all night and seeing baby washed, Mrs. Cadwallader came to dine, the Rector being gone on a fishing excursion. It was a warm evening, and even in the delightful drawing-room, where the fine old turf sloped from the open window towards a lilied pool and well-planted mounds, the heat was enough to make Celia in her white muslin and light curls reflect with pity on what Dodo must feel in her black dress and close cap. But this was not until some episodes with baby were over, and had left her mind at leisure. She had seated herself and taken up a fan for some time before she said, in her quiet guttural—\n\n“Dear Dodo, do throw off that cap. I am sure your dress must make you feel ill.”\n\n“I am so used to the cap—it has become a sort of shell,” said Dorothea, smiling. “I feel rather bare and exposed when it is off.”\n\n“I must see you without it; it makes us all warm,” said Celia, throwing down her fan, and going to Dorothea. It was a pretty picture to see this little lady in white muslin unfastening the widow’s cap from her more majestic sister, and tossing it on to a chair. Just as the coils and braids of dark-brown hair had been set free, Sir James entered the room. He looked at the released head, and said, “Ah!” in a tone of satisfaction.\n\n“It was I who did it, James,” said Celia. “Dodo need not make such a slavery of her mourning; she need not wear that cap any more among her friends.”\n\n“My dear Celia,” said Lady Chettam, “a widow must wear her mourning at least a year.”\n\n“Not if she marries again before the end of it,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, who had some pleasure in startling her good friend the Dowager. Sir James was annoyed, and leaned forward to play with Celia’s Maltese dog.\n\n“That is very rare, I hope,” said Lady Chettam, in a tone intended to guard against such events. “No friend of ours ever committed herself in that way except Mrs. Beevor, and it was very painful to Lord Grinsell when she did so. Her first husband was objectionable, which made it the greater wonder. And severely she was punished for it. They said Captain Beevor dragged her about by the hair, and held up loaded pistols at her.”\n\n“Oh, if she took the wrong man!” said Mrs. Cadwallader, who was in a decidedly wicked mood. “Marriage is always bad then, first or second. Priority is a poor recommendation in a husband if he has got no other. I would rather have a good second husband than an indifferent first.”\n\n“My dear, your clever tongue runs away with you,” said Lady Chettam. “I am sure you would be the last woman to marry again prematurely, if our dear Rector were taken away.”\n\n“Oh, I make no vows; it might be a necessary economy. It is lawful to marry again, I suppose; else we might as well be Hindoos instead of Christians. Of course if a woman accepts the wrong man, she must take the consequences, and one who does it twice over deserves her fate. But if she can marry blood, beauty, and bravery—the sooner the better.”\n\n“I think the subject of our conversation is very ill-chosen,” said Sir James, with a look of disgust. “Suppose we change it.”\n\n“Not on my account, Sir James,” said Dorothea, determined not to lose the opportunity of freeing herself from certain oblique references to excellent matches. “If you are speaking on my behalf, I can assure you that no question can be more indifferent and impersonal to me than second marriage. It is no more to me than if you talked of women going fox-hunting: whether it is admirable in them or not, I shall not follow them. Pray let Mrs. Cadwallader amuse herself on that subject as much as on any other.”\n\n“My dear Mrs. Casaubon,” said Lady Chettam, in her stateliest way, “you do not, I hope, think there was any allusion to you in my mentioning Mrs. Beevor. It was only an instance that occurred to me. She was step-daughter to Lord Grinsell: he married Mrs. Teveroy for his second wife. There could be no possible allusion to you.”\n\n“Oh no,” said Celia. “Nobody chose the subject; it all came out of Dodo’s cap. Mrs. Cadwallader only said what was quite true. A woman could not be married in a widow’s cap, James.”\n\n“Hush, my dear!” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “I will not offend again. I will not even refer to Dido or Zenobia. Only what are we to talk about? I, for my part, object to the discussion of Human Nature, because that is the nature of rectors’ wives.”\n\nLater in the evening, after Mrs. Cadwallader was gone, Celia said privately to Dorothea, “Really, Dodo, taking your cap off made you like yourself again in more ways than one. You spoke up just as you used to do, when anything was said to displease you. But I could hardly make out whether it was James that you thought wrong, or Mrs. Cadwallader.”\n\n“Neither,” said Dorothea. “James spoke out of delicacy to me, but he was mistaken in supposing that I minded what Mrs. Cadwallader said. I should only mind if there were a law obliging me to take any piece of blood and beauty that she or anybody else recommended.”\n\n“But you know, Dodo, if you ever did marry, it would be all the better to have blood and beauty,” said Celia, reflecting that Mr. Casaubon had not been richly endowed with those gifts, and that it would be well to caution Dorothea in time.\n\n“Don’t be anxious, Kitty; I have quite other thoughts about my life. I shall never marry again,” said Dorothea, touching her sister’s chin, and looking at her with indulgent affection. Celia was nursing her baby, and Dorothea had come to say good-night to her.\n\n“Really—quite?” said Celia. “Not anybody at all—if he were very wonderful indeed?”\n\nDorothea shook her head slowly. “Not anybody at all. I have delightful plans. I should like to take a great deal of land, and drain it, and make a little colony, where everybody should work, and all the work should be done well. I should know every one of the people and be their friend. I am going to have great consultations with Mr. Garth: he can tell me almost everything I want to know.”\n\n“Then you will be happy, if you have a plan, Dodo?” said Celia. “Perhaps little Arthur will like plans when he grows up, and then he can help you.”\n\nSir James was informed that same night that Dorothea was really quite set against marrying anybody at all, and was going to take to “all sorts of plans,” just like what she used to have. Sir James made no remark. To his secret feeling there was something repulsive in a woman’s second marriage, and no match would prevent him from feeling it a sort of desecration for Dorothea. He was aware that the world would regard such a sentiment as preposterous, especially in relation to a woman of one-and-twenty; the practice of “the world” being to treat of a young widow’s second marriage as certain and probably near, and to smile with meaning if the widow acts accordingly. But if Dorothea did choose to espouse her solitude, he felt that the resolution would well become her.\n\n# CHAPTER LVI.\n\n“How happy is he born and taught That serveth not another’s will; Whose armor is his honest thought, And simple truth his only skill! . . . . . . . This man is freed from servile bands Of hope to rise or fear to fall; Lord of himself though not of lands; And having nothing yet hath all.” —SIR HENRY WOTTON.\n\nDorothea’s confidence in Caleb Garth’s knowledge, which had begun on her hearing that he approved of her cottages, had grown fast during her stay at Freshitt, Sir James having induced her to take rides over the two estates in company with himself and Caleb, who quite returned her admiration, and told his wife that Mrs. Casaubon had a head for business most uncommon in a woman. It must be remembered that by “business” Caleb never meant money transactions, but the skilful application of labor.\n\n“Most uncommon!” repeated Caleb. “She said a thing I often used to think myself when I was a lad:—‘Mr. Garth, I should like to feel, if I lived to be old, that I had improved a great piece of land and built a great many good cottages, because the work is of a healthy kind while it is being done, and after it is done, men are the better for it.’ Those were the very words: she sees into things in that way.”\n\n“But womanly, I hope,” said Mrs. Garth, half suspecting that Mrs. Casaubon might not hold the true principle of subordination.\n\n“Oh, you can’t think!” said Caleb, shaking his head. “You would like to hear her speak, Susan. She speaks in such plain words, and a voice like music. Bless me! it reminds me of bits in the ‘Messiah’—‘and straightway there appeared a multitude of the heavenly host, praising God and saying;’ it has a tone with it that satisfies your ear.”\n\nCaleb was very fond of music, and when he could afford it went to hear an oratorio that came within his reach, returning from it with a profound reverence for this mighty structure of tones, which made him sit meditatively, looking on the floor and throwing much unutterable language into his outstretched hands.\n\nWith this good understanding between them, it was natural that Dorothea asked Mr. Garth to undertake any business connected with the three farms and the numerous tenements attached to Lowick Manor; indeed, his expectation of getting work for two was being fast fulfilled. As he said, “Business breeds.” And one form of business which was beginning to breed just then was the construction of railways. A projected line was to run through Lowick parish where the cattle had hitherto grazed in a peace unbroken by astonishment; and thus it happened that the infant struggles of the railway system entered into the affairs of Caleb Garth, and determined the course of this history with regard to two persons who were dear to him. The submarine railway may have its difficulties; but the bed of the sea is not divided among various landed proprietors with claims for damages not only measurable but sentimental. In the hundred to which Middlemarch belonged railways were as exciting a topic as the Reform Bill or the imminent horrors of Cholera, and those who held the most decided views on the subject were women and landholders. Women both old and young regarded travelling by steam as presumptuous and dangerous, and argued against it by saying that nothing should induce them to get into a railway carriage; while proprietors, differing from each other in their arguments as much as Mr. Solomon Featherstone differed from Lord Medlicote, were yet unanimous in the opinion that in selling land, whether to the Enemy of mankind or to a company obliged to purchase, these pernicious agencies must be made to pay a very high price to landowners for permission to injure mankind.\n\nBut the slower wits, such as Mr. Solomon and Mrs. Waule, who both occupied land of their own, took a long time to arrive at this conclusion, their minds halting at the vivid conception of what it would be to cut the Big Pasture in two, and turn it into three-cornered bits, which would be “nohow;” while accommodation-bridges and high payments were remote and incredible.\n\n“The cows will all cast their calves, brother,” said Mrs. Waule, in a tone of deep melancholy, “if the railway comes across the Near Close; and I shouldn’t wonder at the mare too, if she was in foal. It’s a poor tale if a widow’s property is to be spaded away, and the law say nothing to it. What’s to hinder ’em from cutting right and left if they begin? It’s well known, I can’t fight.”\n\n“The best way would be to say nothing, and set somebody on to send ’em away with a flea in their ear, when they came spying and measuring,” said Solomon. “Folks did that about Brassing, by what I can understand. It’s all a pretence, if the truth was known, about their being forced to take one way. Let ’em go cutting in another parish. And I don’t believe in any pay to make amends for bringing a lot of ruffians to trample your crops. Where’s a company’s pocket?”\n\n“Brother Peter, God forgive him, got money out of a company,” said Mrs. Waule. “But that was for the manganese. That wasn’t for railways to blow you to pieces right and left.”\n\n“Well, there’s this to be said, Jane,” Mr. Solomon concluded, lowering his voice in a cautious manner—“the more spokes we put in their wheel, the more they’ll pay us to let ’em go on, if they must come whether or not.”\n\nThis reasoning of Mr. Solomon’s was perhaps less thorough than he imagined, his cunning bearing about the same relation to the course of railways as the cunning of a diplomatist bears to the general chill or catarrh of the solar system. But he set about acting on his views in a thoroughly diplomatic manner, by stimulating suspicion. His side of Lowick was the most remote from the village, and the houses of the laboring people were either lone cottages or were collected in a hamlet called Frick, where a water-mill and some stone-pits made a little centre of slow, heavy-shouldered industry.\n\nIn the absence of any precise idea as to what railways were, public opinion in Frick was against them; for the human mind in that grassy corner had not the proverbial tendency to admire the unknown, holding rather that it was likely to be against the poor man, and that suspicion was the only wise attitude with regard to it. Even the rumor of Reform had not yet excited any millennial expectations in Frick, there being no definite promise in it, as of gratuitous grains to fatten Hiram Ford’s pig, or of a publican at the “Weights and Scales” who would brew beer for nothing, or of an offer on the part of the three neighboring farmers to raise wages during winter. And without distinct good of this kind in its promises, Reform seemed on a footing with the bragging of pedlers, which was a hint for distrust to every knowing person. The men of Frick were not ill-fed, and were less given to fanaticism than to a strong muscular suspicion; less inclined to believe that they were peculiarly cared for by heaven, than to regard heaven itself as rather disposed to take them in—a disposition observable in the weather.\n\nThus the mind of Frick was exactly of the sort for Mr. Solomon Featherstone to work upon, he having more plenteous ideas of the same order, with a suspicion of heaven and earth which was better fed and more entirely at leisure. Solomon was overseer of the roads at that time, and on his slow-paced cob often took his rounds by Frick to look at the workmen getting the stones there, pausing with a mysterious deliberation, which might have misled you into supposing that he had some other reason for staying than the mere want of impulse to move. After looking for a long while at any work that was going on, he would raise his eyes a little and look at the horizon; finally he would shake his bridle, touch his horse with the whip, and get it to move slowly onward. The hour-hand of a clock was quick by comparison with Mr. Solomon, who had an agreeable sense that he could afford to be slow. He was in the habit of pausing for a cautious, vaguely designing chat with every hedger or ditcher on his way, and was especially willing to listen even to news which he had heard before, feeling himself at an advantage over all narrators in partially disbelieving them. One day, however, he got into a dialogue with Hiram Ford, a wagoner, in which he himself contributed information. He wished to know whether Hiram had seen fellows with staves and instruments spying about: they called themselves railroad people, but there was no telling what they were or what they meant to do. The least they pretended was that they were going to cut Lowick Parish into sixes and sevens.\n\n“Why, there’ll be no stirrin’ from one pla-ace to another,” said Hiram, thinking of his wagon and horses.\n\n“Not a bit,” said Mr. Solomon. “And cutting up fine land such as this parish! Let ’em go into Tipton, say I. But there’s no knowing what there is at the bottom of it. Traffic is what they put for’ard; but it’s to do harm to the land and the poor man in the long-run.”\n\n“Why, they’re Lunnon chaps, I reckon,” said Hiram, who had a dim notion of London as a centre of hostility to the country.\n\n“Ay, to be sure. And in some parts against Brassing, by what I’ve heard say, the folks fell on ’em when they were spying, and broke their peep-holes as they carry, and drove ’em away, so as they knew better than come again.”\n\n“It war good foon, I’d be bound,” said Hiram, whose fun was much restricted by circumstances.\n\n“Well, I wouldn’t meddle with ’em myself,” said Solomon. “But some say this country’s seen its best days, and the sign is, as it’s being overrun with these fellows trampling right and left, and wanting to cut it up into railways; and all for the big traffic to swallow up the little, so as there shan’t be a team left on the land, nor a whip to crack.”\n\n“I’ll crack my whip about their ear’n, afore they bring it to that, though,” said Hiram, while Mr. Solomon, shaking his bridle, moved onward.\n\nNettle-seed needs no digging. The ruin of this countryside by railroads was discussed, not only at the “Weights and Scales,” but in the hay-field, where the muster of working hands gave opportunities for talk such as were rarely had through the rural year.\n\nOne morning, not long after that interview between Mr. Farebrother and Mary Garth, in which she confessed to him her feeling for Fred Vincy, it happened that her father had some business which took him to Yoddrell’s farm in the direction of Frick: it was to measure and value an outlying piece of land belonging to Lowick Manor, which Caleb expected to dispose of advantageously for Dorothea (it must be confessed that his bias was towards getting the best possible terms from railroad companies). He put up his gig at Yoddrell’s, and in walking with his assistant and measuring-chain to the scene of his work, he encountered the party of the company’s agents, who were adjusting their spirit-level. After a little chat he left them, observing that by-and-by they would reach him again where he was going to measure. It was one of those gray mornings after light rains, which become delicious about twelve o’clock, when the clouds part a little, and the scent of the earth is sweet along the lanes and by the hedgerows.\n\nThe scent would have been sweeter to Fred Vincy, who was coming along the lanes on horseback, if his mind had not been worried by unsuccessful efforts to imagine what he was to do, with his father on one side expecting him straightway to enter the Church, with Mary on the other threatening to forsake him if he did enter it, and with the working-day world showing no eager need whatever of a young gentleman without capital and generally unskilled. It was the harder to Fred’s disposition because his father, satisfied that he was no longer rebellious, was in good humor with him, and had sent him on this pleasant ride to see after some greyhounds. Even when he had fixed on what he should do, there would be the task of telling his father. But it must be admitted that the fixing, which had to come first, was the more difficult task:—what secular avocation on earth was there for a young man (whose friends could not get him an “appointment”) which was at once gentlemanly, lucrative, and to be followed without special knowledge? Riding along the lanes by Frick in this mood, and slackening his pace while he reflected whether he should venture to go round by Lowick Parsonage to call on Mary, he could see over the hedges from one field to another. Suddenly a noise roused his attention, and on the far side of a field on his left hand he could see six or seven men in smock-frocks with hay-forks in their hands making an offensive approach towards the four railway agents who were facing them, while Caleb Garth and his assistant were hastening across the field to join the threatened group. Fred, delayed a few moments by having to find the gate, could not gallop up to the spot before the party in smock-frocks, whose work of turning the hay had not been too pressing after swallowing their mid-day beer, were driving the men in coats before them with their hay-forks; while Caleb Garth’s assistant, a lad of seventeen, who had snatched up the spirit-level at Caleb’s order, had been knocked down and seemed to be lying helpless. The coated men had the advantage as runners, and Fred covered their retreat by getting in front of the smock-frocks and charging them suddenly enough to throw their chase into confusion. “What do you confounded fools mean?” shouted Fred, pursuing the divided group in a zigzag, and cutting right and left with his whip. “I’ll swear to every one of you before the magistrate. You’ve knocked the lad down and killed him, for what I know. You’ll every one of you be hanged at the next assizes, if you don’t mind,” said Fred, who afterwards laughed heartily as he remembered his own phrases.\n\nThe laborers had been driven through the gate-way into their hay-field, and Fred had checked his horse, when Hiram Ford, observing himself at a safe challenging distance, turned back and shouted a defiance which he did not know to be Homeric.\n\n“Yo’re a coward, yo are. Yo git off your horse, young measter, and I’ll have a round wi’ ye, I wull. Yo daredn’t come on wi’out your hoss an’ whip. I’d soon knock the breath out on ye, I would.”\n\n“Wait a minute, and I’ll come back presently, and have a round with you all in turn, if you like,” said Fred, who felt confidence in his power of boxing with his dearly beloved brethren. But just now he wanted to hasten back to Caleb and the prostrate youth.\n\nThe lad’s ankle was strained, and he was in much pain from it, but he was no further hurt, and Fred placed him on the horse that he might ride to Yoddrell’s and be taken care of there.\n\n“Let them put the horse in the stable, and tell the surveyors they can come back for their traps,” said Fred. “The ground is clear now.”\n\n“No, no,” said Caleb, “here’s a breakage. They’ll have to give up for to-day, and it will be as well. Here, take the things before you on the horse, Tom. They’ll see you coming, and they’ll turn back.”\n\n“I’m glad I happened to be here at the right moment, Mr. Garth,” said Fred, as Tom rode away. “No knowing what might have happened if the cavalry had not come up in time.”\n\n“Ay, ay, it was lucky,” said Caleb, speaking rather absently, and looking towards the spot where he had been at work at the moment of interruption. “But—deuce take it—this is what comes of men being fools—I’m hindered of my day’s work. I can’t get along without somebody to help me with the measuring-chain. However!” He was beginning to move towards the spot with a look of vexation, as if he had forgotten Fred’s presence, but suddenly he turned round and said quickly, “What have you got to do to-day, young fellow?”\n\n“Nothing, Mr. Garth. I’ll help you with pleasure—can I?” said Fred, with a sense that he should be courting Mary when he was helping her father.\n\n“Well, you mustn’t mind stooping and getting hot.”\n\n“I don’t mind anything. Only I want to go first and have a round with that hulky fellow who turned to challenge me. It would be a good lesson for him. I shall not be five minutes.”\n\n“Nonsense!” said Caleb, with his most peremptory intonation. “I shall go and speak to the men myself. It’s all ignorance. Somebody has been telling them lies. The poor fools don’t know any better.”\n\n“I shall go with you, then,” said Fred.\n\n“No, no; stay where you are. I don’t want your young blood. I can take care of myself.”\n\nCaleb was a powerful man and knew little of any fear except the fear of hurting others and the fear of having to speechify. But he felt it his duty at this moment to try and give a little harangue. There was a striking mixture in him—which came from his having always been a hard-working man himself—of rigorous notions about workmen and practical indulgence towards them. To do a good day’s work and to do it well, he held to be part of their welfare, as it was the chief part of his own happiness; but he had a strong sense of fellowship with them. When he advanced towards the laborers they had not gone to work again, but were standing in that form of rural grouping which consists in each turning a shoulder towards the other, at a distance of two or three yards. They looked rather sulkily at Caleb, who walked quickly with one hand in his pocket and the other thrust between the buttons of his waistcoat, and had his every-day mild air when he paused among them.\n\n“Why, my lads, how’s this?” he began, taking as usual to brief phrases, which seemed pregnant to himself, because he had many thoughts lying under them, like the abundant roots of a plant that just manages to peep above the water. “How came you to make such a mistake as this? Somebody has been telling you lies. You thought those men up there wanted to do mischief.”\n\n“Aw!” was the answer, dropped at intervals by each according to his degree of unreadiness.\n\n“Nonsense! No such thing! They’re looking out to see which way the railroad is to take. Now, my lads, you can’t hinder the railroad: it will be made whether you like it or not. And if you go fighting against it, you’ll get yourselves into trouble. The law gives those men leave to come here on the land. The owner has nothing to say against it, and if you meddle with them you’ll have to do with the constable and Justice Blakesley, and with the handcuffs and Middlemarch jail. And you might be in for it now, if anybody informed against you.”\n\nCaleb paused here, and perhaps the greatest orator could not have chosen either his pause or his images better for the occasion.\n\n“But come, you didn’t mean any harm. Somebody told you the railroad was a bad thing. That was a lie. It may do a bit of harm here and there, to this and to that; and so does the sun in heaven. But the railway’s a good thing.”\n\n“Aw! good for the big folks to make money out on,” said old Timothy Cooper, who had stayed behind turning his hay while the others had been gone on their spree;—“I’n seen lots o’ things turn up sin’ I war a young un—the war an’ the peace, and the canells, an’ the oald King George, an’ the Regen’, an’ the new King George, an’ the new un as has got a new ne-ame—an’ it’s been all aloike to the poor mon. What’s the canells been t’ him? They’n brought him neyther me-at nor be-acon, nor wage to lay by, if he didn’t save it wi’ clemmin’ his own inside. Times ha’ got wusser for him sin’ I war a young un. An’ so it’ll be wi’ the railroads. They’ll on’y leave the poor mon furder behind. But them are fools as meddle, and so I told the chaps here. This is the big folks’s world, this is. But yo’re for the big folks, Muster Garth, yo are.”\n\nTimothy was a wiry old laborer, of a type lingering in those times—who had his savings in a stocking-foot, lived in a lone cottage, and was not to be wrought on by any oratory, having as little of the feudal spirit, and believing as little, as if he had not been totally unacquainted with the Age of Reason and the Rights of Man. Caleb was in a difficulty known to any person attempting in dark times and unassisted by miracle to reason with rustics who are in possession of an undeniable truth which they know through a hard process of feeling, and can let it fall like a giant’s club on your neatly carved argument for a social benefit which they do not feel. Caleb had no cant at command, even if he could have chosen to use it; and he had been accustomed to meet all such difficulties in no other way than by doing his “business” faithfully. He answered—\n\n“If you don’t think well of me, Tim, never mind; that’s neither here nor there now. Things may be bad for the poor man—bad they are; but I want the lads here not to do what will make things worse for themselves. The cattle may have a heavy load, but it won’t help ’em to throw it over into the roadside pit, when it’s partly their own fodder.”\n\n“We war on’y for a bit o’ foon,” said Hiram, who was beginning to see consequences. “That war all we war arter.”\n\n“Well, promise me not to meddle again, and I’ll see that nobody informs against you.”\n\n“I’n ne’er meddled, an’ I’n no call to promise,” said Timothy.\n\n“No, but the rest. Come, I’m as hard at work as any of you to-day, and I can’t spare much time. Say you’ll be quiet without the constable.”\n\n“Aw, we wooant meddle—they may do as they loike for oos”—were the forms in which Caleb got his pledges; and then he hastened back to Fred, who had followed him, and watched him in the gateway.\n\nThey went to work, and Fred helped vigorously. His spirits had risen, and he heartily enjoyed a good slip in the moist earth under the hedgerow, which soiled his perfect summer trousers. Was it his successful onset which had elated him, or the satisfaction of helping Mary’s father? Something more. The accidents of the morning had helped his frustrated imagination to shape an employment for himself which had several attractions. I am not sure that certain fibres in Mr. Garth’s mind had not resumed their old vibration towards the very end which now revealed itself to Fred. For the effective accident is but the touch of fire where there is oil and tow; and it always appeared to Fred that the railway brought the needed touch. But they went on in silence except when their business demanded speech. At last, when they had finished and were walking away, Mr. Garth said—\n\n“A young fellow needn’t be a B. A. to do this sort of work, eh, Fred?”\n\n“I wish I had taken to it before I had thought of being a B. A.,” said Fred. He paused a moment, and then added, more hesitatingly, “Do you think I am too old to learn your business, Mr. Garth?”\n\n“My business is of many sorts, my boy,” said Mr. Garth, smiling. “A good deal of what I know can only come from experience: you can’t learn it off as you learn things out of a book. But you are young enough to lay a foundation yet.” Caleb pronounced the last sentence emphatically, but paused in some uncertainty. He had been under the impression lately that Fred had made up his mind to enter the Church.\n\n“You do think I could do some good at it, if I were to try?” said Fred, more eagerly.\n\n“That depends,” said Caleb, turning his head on one side and lowering his voice, with the air of a man who felt himself to be saying something deeply religious. “You must be sure of two things: you must love your work, and not be always looking over the edge of it, wanting your play to begin. And the other is, you must not be ashamed of your work, and think it would be more honorable to you to be doing something else. You must have a pride in your own work and in learning to do it well, and not be always saying, There’s this and there’s that—if I had this or that to do, I might make something of it. No matter what a man is—I wouldn’t give twopence for him”—here Caleb’s mouth looked bitter, and he snapped his fingers—“whether he was the prime minister or the rick-thatcher, if he didn’t do well what he undertook to do.”\n\n“I can never feel that I should do that in being a clergyman,” said Fred, meaning to take a step in argument.\n\n“Then let it alone, my boy,” said Caleb, abruptly, “else you’ll never be easy. Or, if you are easy, you’ll be a poor stick.”\n\n“That is very nearly what Mary thinks about it,” said Fred, coloring. “I think you must know what I feel for Mary, Mr. Garth: I hope it does not displease you that I have always loved her better than any one else, and that I shall never love any one as I love her.”\n\nThe expression of Caleb’s face was visibly softening while Fred spoke. But he swung his head with a solemn slowness, and said—\n\n“That makes things more serious, Fred, if you want to take Mary’s happiness into your keeping.”\n\n“I know that, Mr. Garth,” said Fred, eagerly, “and I would do anything for her. She says she will never have me if I go into the Church; and I shall be the most miserable devil in the world if I lose all hope of Mary. Really, if I could get some other profession, business—anything that I am at all fit for, I would work hard, I would deserve your good opinion. I should like to have to do with outdoor things. I know a good deal about land and cattle already. I used to believe, you know—though you will think me rather foolish for it—that I should have land of my own. I am sure knowledge of that sort would come easily to me, especially if I could be under you in any way.”\n\n“Softly, my boy,” said Caleb, having the image of “Susan” before his eyes. “What have you said to your father about all this?”\n\n“Nothing, yet; but I must tell him. I am only waiting to know what I can do instead of entering the Church. I am very sorry to disappoint him, but a man ought to be allowed to judge for himself when he is four-and-twenty. How could I know when I was fifteen, what it would be right for me to do now? My education was a mistake.”\n\n“But hearken to this, Fred,” said Caleb. “Are you sure Mary is fond of you, or would ever have you?”\n\n“I asked Mr. Farebrother to talk to her, because she had forbidden me—I didn’t know what else to do,” said Fred, apologetically. “And he says that I have every reason to hope, if I can put myself in an honorable position—I mean, out of the Church. I dare say you think it unwarrantable in me, Mr. Garth, to be troubling you and obtruding my own wishes about Mary, before I have done anything at all for myself. Of course I have not the least claim—indeed, I have already a debt to you which will never be discharged, even when I have been able to pay it in the shape of money.”\n\n“Yes, my boy, you have a claim,” said Caleb, with much feeling in his voice. “The young ones have always a claim on the old to help them forward. I was young myself once and had to do without much help; but help would have been welcome to me, if it had been only for the fellow-feeling’s sake. But I must consider. Come to me to-morrow at the office, at nine o’clock. At the office, mind.”\n\nMr. Garth would take no important step without consulting Susan, but it must be confessed that before he reached home he had taken his resolution. With regard to a large number of matters about which other men are decided or obstinate, he was the most easily manageable man in the world. He never knew what meat he would choose, and if Susan had said that they ought to live in a four-roomed cottage, in order to save, he would have said, “Let us go,” without inquiring into details. But where Caleb’s feeling and judgment strongly pronounced, he was a ruler; and in spite of his mildness and timidity in reproving, every one about him knew that on the exceptional occasions when he chose, he was absolute. He never, indeed, chose to be absolute except on some one else’s behalf. On ninety-nine points Mrs. Garth decided, but on the hundredth she was often aware that she would have to perform the singularly difficult task of carrying out her own principle, and to make herself subordinate.\n\n“It is come round as I thought, Susan,” said Caleb, when they were seated alone in the evening. He had already narrated the adventure which had brought about Fred’s sharing in his work, but had kept back the further result. “The children are fond of each other—I mean, Fred and Mary.”\n\nMrs. Garth laid her work on her knee, and fixed her penetrating eyes anxiously on her husband.\n\n“After we’d done our work, Fred poured it all out to me. He can’t bear to be a clergyman, and Mary says she won’t have him if he is one; and the lad would like to be under me and give his mind to business. And I’ve determined to take him and make a man of him.”\n\n“Caleb!” said Mrs. Garth, in a deep contralto, expressive of resigned astonishment.\n\n“It’s a fine thing to do,” said Mr. Garth, settling himself firmly against the back of his chair, and grasping the elbows. “I shall have trouble with him, but I think I shall carry it through. The lad loves Mary, and a true love for a good woman is a great thing, Susan. It shapes many a rough fellow.”\n\n“Has Mary spoken to you on the subject?” said Mrs Garth, secretly a little hurt that she had to be informed on it herself.\n\n“Not a word. I asked her about Fred once; I gave her a bit of a warning. But she assured me she would never marry an idle self-indulgent man—nothing since. But it seems Fred set on Mr. Farebrother to talk to her, because she had forbidden him to speak himself, and Mr. Farebrother has found out that she is fond of Fred, but says he must not be a clergyman. Fred’s heart is fixed on Mary, that I can see: it gives me a good opinion of the lad—and we always liked him, Susan.”\n\n“It is a pity for Mary, I think,” said Mrs. Garth.\n\n“Why—a pity?”\n\n“Because, Caleb, she might have had a man who is worth twenty Fred Vincy’s.”\n\n“Ah?” said Caleb, with surprise.\n\n“I firmly believe that Mr. Farebrother is attached to her, and meant to make her an offer; but of course, now that Fred has used him as an envoy, there is an end to that better prospect.” There was a severe precision in Mrs. Garth’s utterance. She was vexed and disappointed, but she was bent on abstaining from useless words.\n\nCaleb was silent a few moments under a conflict of feelings. He looked at the floor and moved his head and hands in accompaniment to some inward argumentation. At last he said—\n\n“That would have made me very proud and happy, Susan, and I should have been glad for your sake. I’ve always felt that your belongings have never been on a level with you. But you took me, though I was a plain man.”\n\n“I took the best and cleverest man I had ever known,” said Mrs. Garth, convinced that she would never have loved any one who came short of that mark.\n\n“Well, perhaps others thought you might have done better. But it would have been worse for me. And that is what touches me close about Fred. The lad is good at bottom, and clever enough to do, if he’s put in the right way; and he loves and honors my daughter beyond anything, and she has given him a sort of promise according to what he turns out. I say, that young man’s soul is in my hand; and I’ll do the best I can for him, so help me God! It’s my duty, Susan.”\n\nMrs. Garth was not given to tears, but there was a large one rolling down her face before her husband had finished. It came from the pressure of various feelings, in which there was much affection and some vexation. She wiped it away quickly, saying—\n\n“Few men besides you would think it a duty to add to their anxieties in that way, Caleb.”\n\n“That signifies nothing—what other men would think. I’ve got a clear feeling inside me, and that I shall follow; and I hope your heart will go with me, Susan, in making everything as light as can be to Mary, poor child.”\n\nCaleb, leaning back in his chair, looked with anxious appeal towards his wife. She rose and kissed him, saying, “God bless you, Caleb! Our children have a good father.”\n\nBut she went out and had a hearty cry to make up for the suppression of her words. She felt sure that her husband’s conduct would be misunderstood, and about Fred she was rational and unhopeful. Which would turn out to have the more foresight in it—her rationality or Caleb’s ardent generosity?\n\nWhen Fred went to the office the next morning, there was a test to be gone through which he was not prepared for.\n\n“Now Fred,” said Caleb, “you will have some desk-work. I have always done a good deal of writing myself, but I can’t do without help, and as I want you to understand the accounts and get the values into your head, I mean to do without another clerk. So you must buckle to. How are you at writing and arithmetic?”\n\nFred felt an awkward movement of the heart; he had not thought of desk-work; but he was in a resolute mood, and not going to shrink. “I’m not afraid of arithmetic, Mr. Garth: it always came easily to me. I think you know my writing.”\n\n“Let us see,” said Caleb, taking up a pen, examining it carefully and handing it, well dipped, to Fred with a sheet of ruled paper. “Copy me a line or two of that valuation, with the figures at the end.”\n\nAt that time the opinion existed that it was beneath a gentleman to write legibly, or with a hand in the least suitable to a clerk. Fred wrote the lines demanded in a hand as gentlemanly as that of any viscount or bishop of the day: the vowels were all alike and the consonants only distinguishable as turning up or down, the strokes had a blotted solidity and the letters disdained to keep the line—in short, it was a manuscript of that venerable kind easy to interpret when you know beforehand what the writer means.\n\nAs Caleb looked on, his visage showed a growing depression, but when Fred handed him the paper he gave something like a snarl, and rapped the paper passionately with the back of his hand. Bad work like this dispelled all Caleb’s mildness.\n\n“The deuce!” he exclaimed, snarlingly. “To think that this is a country where a man’s education may cost hundreds and hundreds, and it turns you out this!” Then in a more pathetic tone, pushing up his spectacles and looking at the unfortunate scribe, “The Lord have mercy on us, Fred, I can’t put up with this!”\n\n“What can I do, Mr. Garth?” said Fred, whose spirits had sunk very low, not only at the estimate of his handwriting, but at the vision of himself as liable to be ranked with office clerks.\n\n“Do? Why, you must learn to form your letters and keep the line. What’s the use of writing at all if nobody can understand it?” asked Caleb, energetically, quite preoccupied with the bad quality of the work. “Is there so little business in the world that you must be sending puzzles over the country? But that’s the way people are brought up. I should lose no end of time with the letters some people send me, if Susan did not make them out for me. It’s disgusting.” Here Caleb tossed the paper from him.\n\nAny stranger peeping into the office at that moment might have wondered what was the drama between the indignant man of business, and the fine-looking young fellow whose blond complexion was getting rather patchy as he bit his lip with mortification. Fred was struggling with many thoughts. Mr. Garth had been so kind and encouraging at the beginning of their interview, that gratitude and hopefulness had been at a high pitch, and the downfall was proportionate. He had not thought of desk-work—in fact, like the majority of young gentlemen, he wanted an occupation which should be free from disagreeables. I cannot tell what might have been the consequences if he had not distinctly promised himself that he would go to Lowick to see Mary and tell her that he was engaged to work under her father. He did not like to disappoint himself there.\n\n“I am very sorry,” were all the words that he could muster. But Mr. Garth was already relenting.\n\n“We must make the best of it, Fred,” he began, with a return to his usual quiet tone. “Every man can learn to write. I taught myself. Go at it with a will, and sit up at night if the day-time isn’t enough. We’ll be patient, my boy. Callum shall go on with the books for a bit, while you are learning. But now I must be off,” said Caleb, rising. “You must let your father know our agreement. You’ll save me Callum’s salary, you know, when you can write; and I can afford to give you eighty pounds for the first year, and more after.”\n\nWhen Fred made the necessary disclosure to his parents, the relative effect on the two was a surprise which entered very deeply into his memory. He went straight from Mr. Garth’s office to the warehouse, rightly feeling that the most respectful way in which he could behave to his father was to make the painful communication as gravely and formally as possible. Moreover, the decision would be more certainly understood to be final, if the interview took place in his father’s gravest hours, which were always those spent in his private room at the warehouse.\n\nFred entered on the subject directly, and declared briefly what he had done and was resolved to do, expressing at the end his regret that he should be the cause of disappointment to his father, and taking the blame on his own deficiencies. The regret was genuine, and inspired Fred with strong, simple words.\n\nMr. Vincy listened in profound surprise without uttering even an exclamation, a silence which in his impatient temperament was a sign of unusual emotion. He had not been in good spirits about trade that morning, and the slight bitterness in his lips grew intense as he listened. When Fred had ended, there was a pause of nearly a minute, during which Mr. Vincy replaced a book in his desk and turned the key emphatically. Then he looked at his son steadily, and said—\n\n“So you’ve made up your mind at last, sir?”\n\n“Yes, father.”\n\n“Very well; stick to it. I’ve no more to say. You’ve thrown away your education, and gone down a step in life, when I had given you the means of rising, that’s all.”\n\n“I am very sorry that we differ, father. I think I can be quite as much of a gentleman at the work I have undertaken, as if I had been a curate. But I am grateful to you for wishing to do the best for me.”\n\n“Very well; I have no more to say. I wash my hands of you. I only hope, when you have a son of your own he will make a better return for the pains you spend on him.”\n\nThis was very cutting to Fred. His father was using that unfair advantage possessed by us all when we are in a pathetic situation and see our own past as if it were simply part of the pathos. In reality, Mr. Vincy’s wishes about his son had had a great deal of pride, inconsiderateness, and egoistic folly in them. But still the disappointed father held a strong lever; and Fred felt as if he were being banished with a malediction.\n\n“I hope you will not object to my remaining at home, sir?” he said, after rising to go; “I shall have a sufficient salary to pay for my board, as of course I should wish to do.”\n\n“Board be hanged!” said Mr. Vincy, recovering himself in his disgust at the notion that Fred’s keep would be missed at his table. “Of course your mother will want you to stay. But I shall keep no horse for you, you understand; and you will pay your own tailor. You will do with a suit or two less, I fancy, when you have to pay for ’em.”\n\nFred lingered; there was still something to be said. At last it came.\n\n“I hope you will shake hands with me, father, and forgive me the vexation I have caused you.”\n\nMr. Vincy from his chair threw a quick glance upward at his son, who had advanced near to him, and then gave his hand, saying hurriedly, “Yes, yes, let us say no more.”\n\nFred went through much more narrative and explanation with his mother, but she was inconsolable, having before her eyes what perhaps her husband had never thought of, the certainty that Fred would marry Mary Garth, that her life would henceforth be spoiled by a perpetual infusion of Garths and their ways, and that her darling boy, with his beautiful face and stylish air “beyond anybody else’s son in Middlemarch,” would be sure to get like that family in plainness of appearance and carelessness about his clothes. To her it seemed that there was a Garth conspiracy to get possession of the desirable Fred, but she dared not enlarge on this opinion, because a slight hint of it had made him “fly out” at her as he had never done before. Her temper was too sweet for her to show any anger, but she felt that her happiness had received a bruise, and for several days merely to look at Fred made her cry a little as if he were the subject of some baleful prophecy. Perhaps she was the slower to recover her usual cheerfulness because Fred had warned her that she must not reopen the sore question with his father, who had accepted his decision and forgiven him. If her husband had been vehement against Fred, she would have been urged into defence of her darling. It was the end of the fourth day when Mr. Vincy said to her—\n\n“Come, Lucy, my dear, don’t be so down-hearted. You always have spoiled the boy, and you must go on spoiling him.”\n\n“Nothing ever did cut me so before, Vincy,” said the wife, her fair throat and chin beginning to tremble again, “only his illness.”\n\n“Pooh, pooh, never mind! We must expect to have trouble with our children. Don’t make it worse by letting me see you out of spirits.”\n\n“Well, I won’t,” said Mrs. Vincy, roused by this appeal and adjusting herself with a little shake as of a bird which lays down its ruffled plumage.\n\n“It won’t do to begin making a fuss about one,” said Mr. Vincy, wishing to combine a little grumbling with domestic cheerfulness. “There’s Rosamond as well as Fred.”\n\n“Yes, poor thing. I’m sure I felt for her being disappointed of her baby; but she got over it nicely.”\n\n“Baby, pooh! I can see Lydgate is making a mess of his practice, and getting into debt too, by what I hear. I shall have Rosamond coming to me with a pretty tale one of these days. But they’ll get no money from me, I know. Let his family help him. I never did like that marriage. But it’s no use talking. Ring the bell for lemons, and don’t look dull any more, Lucy. I’ll drive you and Louisa to Riverston to-morrow.”\n\n# CHAPTER LVII.\n\nThey numbered scarce eight summers when a name Rose on their souls and stirred such motions there As thrill the buds and shape their hidden frame At penetration of the quickening air: His name who told of loyal Evan Dhu, Of quaint Bradwardine, and Vich Ian Vor, Making the little world their childhood knew Large with a land of mountain lake and scaur, And larger yet with wonder, love, belief Toward Walter Scott who living far away Sent them this wealth of joy and noble grief. The book and they must part, but day by day, In lines that thwart like portly spiders ran They wrote the tale, from Tully Veolan.\n\nThe evening that Fred Vincy walked to Lowick parsonage (he had begun to see that this was a world in which even a spirited young man must sometimes walk for want of a horse to carry him) he set out at five o’clock and called on Mrs. Garth by the way, wishing to assure himself that she accepted their new relations willingly.\n\nHe found the family group, dogs and cats included, under the great apple-tree in the orchard. It was a festival with Mrs. Garth, for her eldest son, Christy, her peculiar joy and pride, had come home for a short holiday—Christy, who held it the most desirable thing in the world to be a tutor, to study all literatures and be a regenerate Porson, and who was an incorporate criticism on poor Fred, a sort of object-lesson given to him by the educational mother. Christy himself, a square-browed, broad-shouldered masculine edition of his mother not much higher than Fred’s shoulder—which made it the harder that he should be held superior—was always as simple as possible, and thought no more of Fred’s disinclination to scholarship than of a giraffe’s, wishing that he himself were more of the same height. He was lying on the ground now by his mother’s chair, with his straw hat laid flat over his eyes, while Jim on the other side was reading aloud from that beloved writer who has made a chief part in the happiness of many young lives. The volume was “Ivanhoe,” and Jim was in the great archery scene at the tournament, but suffered much interruption from Ben, who had fetched his own old bow and arrows, and was making himself dreadfully disagreeable, Letty thought, by begging all present to observe his random shots, which no one wished to do except Brownie, the active-minded but probably shallow mongrel, while the grizzled Newfoundland lying in the sun looked on with the dull-eyed neutrality of extreme old age. Letty herself, showing as to her mouth and pinafore some slight signs that she had been assisting at the gathering of the cherries which stood in a coral-heap on the tea-table, was now seated on the grass, listening open-eyed to the reading.\n\nBut the centre of interest was changed for all by the arrival of Fred Vincy. When, seating himself on a garden-stool, he said that he was on his way to Lowick Parsonage, Ben, who had thrown down his bow, and snatched up a reluctant half-grown kitten instead, strode across Fred’s outstretched leg, and said “Take me!”\n\n“Oh, and me too,” said Letty.\n\n“You can’t keep up with Fred and me,” said Ben.\n\n“Yes, I can. Mother, please say that I am to go,” urged Letty, whose life was much checkered by resistance to her depreciation as a girl.\n\n“I shall stay with Christy,” observed Jim; as much as to say that he had the advantage of those simpletons; whereupon Letty put her hand up to her head and looked with jealous indecision from the one to the other.\n\n“Let us all go and see Mary,” said Christy, opening his arms.\n“No, my dear child, we must not go in a swarm to the parsonage. And that old Glasgow suit of yours would never do. Besides, your father will come home. We must let Fred go alone. He can tell Mary that you are here, and she will come back to-morrow.”\n\nChristy glanced at his own threadbare knees, and then at Fred’s beautiful white trousers. Certainly Fred’s tailoring suggested the advantages of an English university, and he had a graceful way even of looking warm and of pushing his hair back with his handkerchief.\n\n“Children, run away,” said Mrs. Garth; “it is too warm to hang about your friends. Take your brother and show him the rabbits.”\n\nThe eldest understood, and led off the children immediately. Fred felt that Mrs. Garth wished to give him an opportunity of saying anything he had to say, but he could only begin by observing—\n\n“How glad you must be to have Christy here!”\n\n“Yes; he has come sooner than I expected. He got down from the coach at nine o’clock, just after his father went out. I am longing for Caleb to come and hear what wonderful progress Christy is making. He has paid his expenses for the last year by giving lessons, carrying on hard study at the same time. He hopes soon to get a private tutorship and go abroad.”\n\n“He is a great fellow,” said Fred, to whom these cheerful truths had a medicinal taste, “and no trouble to anybody.” After a slight pause, he added, “But I fear you will think that I am going to be a great deal of trouble to Mr. Garth.”\n\n“Caleb likes taking trouble: he is one of those men who always do more than any one would have thought of asking them to do,” answered Mrs. Garth. She was knitting, and could either look at Fred or not, as she chose—always an advantage when one is bent on loading speech with salutary meaning; and though Mrs. Garth intended to be duly reserved, she did wish to say something that Fred might be the better for.\n\n“I know you think me very undeserving, Mrs. Garth, and with good reason,” said Fred, his spirit rising a little at the perception of something like a disposition to lecture him. “I happen to have behaved just the worst to the people I can’t help wishing for the most from. But while two men like Mr. Garth and Mr. Farebrother have not given me up, I don’t see why I should give myself up.” Fred thought it might be well to suggest these masculine examples to Mrs. Garth.\n\n“Assuredly,” said she, with gathering emphasis. “A young man for whom two such elders had devoted themselves would indeed be culpable if he threw himself away and made their sacrifices vain.”\n\nFred wondered a little at this strong language, but only said, “I hope it will not be so with me, Mrs. Garth, since I have some encouragement to believe that I may win Mary. Mr. Garth has told you about that? You were not surprised, I dare say?” Fred ended, innocently referring only to his own love as probably evident enough.\n\n“Not surprised that Mary has given you encouragement?” returned Mrs. Garth, who thought it would be well for Fred to be more alive to the fact that Mary’s friends could not possibly have wished this beforehand, whatever the Vincys might suppose. “Yes, I confess I was surprised.”\n\n“She never did give me any—not the least in the world, when I talked to her myself,” said Fred, eager to vindicate Mary. “But when I asked Mr. Farebrother to speak for me, she allowed him to tell me there was a hope.”\n\nThe power of admonition which had begun to stir in Mrs. Garth had not yet discharged itself. It was a little too provoking even for her self-control that this blooming youngster should flourish on the disappointments of sadder and wiser people—making a meal of a nightingale and never knowing it—and that all the while his family should suppose that hers was in eager need of this sprig; and her vexation had fermented the more actively because of its total repression towards her husband. Exemplary wives will sometimes find scapegoats in this way. She now said with energetic decision, “You made a great mistake, Fred, in asking Mr. Farebrother to speak for you.”\n\n“Did I?” said Fred, reddening instantaneously. He was alarmed, but at a loss to know what Mrs. Garth meant, and added, in an apologetic tone, “Mr. Farebrother has always been such a friend of ours; and Mary, I knew, would listen to him gravely; and he took it on himself quite readily.”\n\n“Yes, young people are usually blind to everything but their own wishes, and seldom imagine how much those wishes cost others,” said Mrs. Garth. She did not mean to go beyond this salutary general doctrine, and threw her indignation into a needless unwinding of her worsted, knitting her brow at it with a grand air.\n\n“I cannot conceive how it could be any pain to Mr. Farebrother,” said Fred, who nevertheless felt that surprising conceptions were beginning to form themselves.\n\n“Precisely; you cannot conceive,” said Mrs. Garth, cutting her words as neatly as possible.\n\nFor a moment Fred looked at the horizon with a dismayed anxiety, and then turning with a quick movement said almost sharply—\n\n“Do you mean to say, Mrs. Garth, that Mr. Farebrother is in love with Mary?”\n\n“And if it were so, Fred, I think you are the last person who ought to be surprised,” returned Mrs. Garth, laying her knitting down beside her and folding her arms. It was an unwonted sign of emotion in her that she should put her work out of her hands. In fact her feelings were divided between the satisfaction of giving Fred his discipline and the sense of having gone a little too far. Fred took his hat and stick and rose quickly.\n\n“Then you think I am standing in his way, and in Mary’s too?” he said, in a tone which seemed to demand an answer.\n\nMrs. Garth could not speak immediately. She had brought herself into the unpleasant position of being called on to say what she really felt, yet what she knew there were strong reasons for concealing. And to her the consciousness of having exceeded in words was peculiarly mortifying. Besides, Fred had given out unexpected electricity, and he now added, “Mr. Garth seemed pleased that Mary should be attached to me. He could not have known anything of this.”\n\nMrs. Garth felt a severe twinge at this mention of her husband, the fear that Caleb might think her in the wrong not being easily endurable. She answered, wanting to check unintended consequences—\n\n“I spoke from inference only. I am not aware that Mary knows anything of the matter.”\n\nBut she hesitated to beg that he would keep entire silence on a subject which she had herself unnecessarily mentioned, not being used to stoop in that way; and while she was hesitating there was already a rush of unintended consequences under the apple-tree where the tea-things stood. Ben, bouncing across the grass with Brownie at his heels, and seeing the kitten dragging the knitting by a lengthening line of wool, shouted and clapped his hands; Brownie barked, the kitten, desperate, jumped on the tea-table and upset the milk, then jumped down again and swept half the cherries with it; and Ben, snatching up the half-knitted sock-top, fitted it over the kitten’s head as a new source of madness, while Letty arriving cried out to her mother against this cruelty—it was a history as full of sensation as “This is the house that Jack built.” Mrs. Garth was obliged to interfere, the other young ones came up and the tête-à-tête with Fred was ended. He got away as soon as he could, and Mrs. Garth could only imply some retractation of her severity by saying “God bless you” when she shook hands with him.\n\nShe was unpleasantly conscious that she had been on the verge of speaking as “one of the foolish women speaketh”—telling first and entreating silence after. But she had not entreated silence, and to prevent Caleb’s blame she determined to blame herself and confess all to him that very night. It was curious what an awful tribunal the mild Caleb’s was to her, whenever he set it up. But she meant to point out to him that the revelation might do Fred Vincy a great deal of good.\n\nNo doubt it was having a strong effect on him as he walked to Lowick. Fred’s light hopeful nature had perhaps never had so much of a bruise as from this suggestion that if he had been out of the way Mary might have made a thoroughly good match. Also he was piqued that he had been what he called such a stupid lout as to ask that intervention from Mr. Farebrother. But it was not in a lover’s nature—it was not in Fred’s, that the new anxiety raised about Mary’s feeling should not surmount every other. Notwithstanding his trust in Mr. Farebrother’s generosity, notwithstanding what Mary had said to him, Fred could not help feeling that he had a rival: it was a new consciousness, and he objected to it extremely, not being in the least ready to give up Mary for her good, being ready rather to fight for her with any man whatsoever. But the fighting with Mr. Farebrother must be of a metaphorical kind, which was much more difficult to Fred than the muscular. Certainly this experience was a discipline for Fred hardly less sharp than his disappointment about his uncle’s will. The iron had not entered into his soul, but he had begun to imagine what the sharp edge would be. It did not once occur to Fred that Mrs. Garth might be mistaken about Mr. Farebrother, but he suspected that she might be wrong about Mary. Mary had been staying at the parsonage lately, and her mother might know very little of what had been passing in her mind.\n\nHe did not feel easier when he found her looking cheerful with the three ladies in the drawing-room. They were in animated discussion on some subject which was dropped when he entered, and Mary was copying the labels from a heap of shallow cabinet drawers, in a minute handwriting which she was skilled in. Mr. Farebrother was somewhere in the village, and the three ladies knew nothing of Fred’s peculiar relation to Mary: it was impossible for either of them to propose that they should walk round the garden, and Fred predicted to himself that he should have to go away without saying a word to her in private. He told her first of Christy’s arrival and then of his own engagement with her father; and he was comforted by seeing that this latter news touched her keenly. She said hurriedly, “I am so glad,” and then bent over her writing to hinder any one from noticing her face. But here was a subject which Mrs. Farebrother could not let pass.\n\n“You don’t mean, my dear Miss Garth, that you are glad to hear of a young man giving up the Church for which he was educated: you only mean that things being so, you are glad that he should be under an excellent man like your father.”\n\n“No, really, Mrs. Farebrother, I am glad of both, I fear,” said Mary, cleverly getting rid of one rebellious tear. “I have a dreadfully secular mind. I never liked any clergyman except the Vicar of Wakefield and Mr. Farebrother.”\n\n“Now why, my dear?” said Mrs. Farebrother, pausing on her large wooden knitting-needles and looking at Mary. “You have always a good reason for your opinions, but this astonishes me. Of course I put out of the question those who preach new doctrine. But why should you dislike clergymen?”\n\n“Oh dear,” said Mary, her face breaking into merriment as she seemed to consider a moment, “I don’t like their neckcloths.”\n\n“Why, you don’t like Camden’s, then,” said Miss Winifred, in some anxiety.\n\n“Yes, I do,” said Mary. “I don’t like the other clergymen’s neckcloths, because it is they who wear them.”\n\n“How very puzzling!” said Miss Noble, feeling that her own intellect was probably deficient.\n\n“My dear, you are joking. You would have better reasons than these for slighting so respectable a class of men,” said Mrs. Farebrother, majestically.\n\n“Miss Garth has such severe notions of what people should be that it is difficult to satisfy her,” said Fred.\n\n“Well, I am glad at least that she makes an exception in favor of my son,” said the old lady.\n\nMary was wondering at Fred’s piqued tone, when Mr. Farebrother came in and had to hear the news about the engagement under Mr. Garth. At the end he said with quiet satisfaction, “That is right;” and then bent to look at Mary’s labels and praise her handwriting. Fred felt horribly jealous—was glad, of course, that Mr. Farebrother was so estimable, but wished that he had been ugly and fat as men at forty sometimes are. It was clear what the end would be, since Mary openly placed Farebrother above everybody, and these women were all evidently encouraging the affair. He was feeling sure that he should have no chance of speaking to Mary, when Mr. Farebrother said—\n\n“Fred, help me to carry these drawers back into my study—you have never seen my fine new study. Pray come too, Miss Garth. I want you to see a stupendous spider I found this morning.”\n\nMary at once saw the Vicar’s intention. He had never since the memorable evening deviated from his old pastoral kindness towards her, and her momentary wonder and doubt had quite gone to sleep. Mary was accustomed to think rather rigorously of what was probable, and if a belief flattered her vanity she felt warned to dismiss it as ridiculous, having early had much exercise in such dismissals. It was as she had foreseen: when Fred had been asked to admire the fittings of the study, and she had been asked to admire the spider, Mr. Farebrother said—\n\n“Wait here a minute or two. I am going to look out an engraving which Fred is tall enough to hang for me. I shall be back in a few minutes.” And then he went out. Nevertheless, the first word Fred said to Mary was—\n\n“It is of no use, whatever I do, Mary. You are sure to marry Farebrother at last.” There was some rage in his tone.\n\n“What do you mean, Fred?” Mary exclaimed indignantly, blushing deeply, and surprised out of all her readiness in reply.\n\n“It is impossible that you should not see it all clearly enough—you who see everything.”\n\n“I only see that you are behaving very ill, Fred, in speaking so of Mr. Farebrother after he has pleaded your cause in every way. How can you have taken up such an idea?”\n\nFred was rather deep, in spite of his irritation. If Mary had really been unsuspicious, there was no good in telling her what Mrs. Garth had said.\n\n“It follows as a matter of course,” he replied. “When you are continually seeing a man who beats me in everything, and whom you set up above everybody, I can have no fair chance.”\n\n“You are very ungrateful, Fred,” said Mary. “I wish I had never told Mr. Farebrother that I cared for you in the least.”\n\n“No, I am not ungrateful; I should be the happiest fellow in the world if it were not for this. I told your father everything, and he was very kind; he treated me as if I were his son. I could go at the work with a will, writing and everything, if it were not for this.”\n\n“For this? for what?” said Mary, imagining now that something specific must have been said or done.\n\n“This dreadful certainty that I shall be bowled out by Farebrother.” Mary was appeased by her inclination to laugh.\n\n“Fred,” she said, peeping round to catch his eyes, which were sulkily turned away from her, “you are too delightfully ridiculous. If you were not such a charming simpleton, what a temptation this would be to play the wicked coquette, and let you suppose that somebody besides you has made love to me.”\n\n“Do you really like me best, Mary?” said Fred, turning eyes full of affection on her, and trying to take her hand.\n\n“I don’t like you at all at this moment,” said Mary, retreating, and putting her hands behind her. “I only said that no mortal ever made love to me besides you. And that is no argument that a very wise man ever will,” she ended, merrily.\n\n“I wish you would tell me that you could not possibly ever think of him,” said Fred.\n\n“Never dare to mention this any more to me, Fred,” said Mary, getting serious again. “I don’t know whether it is more stupid or ungenerous in you not to see that Mr. Farebrother has left us together on purpose that we might speak freely. I am disappointed that you should be so blind to his delicate feeling.”\n\nThere was no time to say any more before Mr. Farebrother came back with the engraving; and Fred had to return to the drawing-room still with a jealous dread in his heart, but yet with comforting arguments from Mary’s words and manner. The result of the conversation was on the whole more painful to Mary: inevitably her attention had taken a new attitude, and she saw the possibility of new interpretations. She was in a position in which she seemed to herself to be slighting Mr. Farebrother, and this, in relation to a man who is much honored, is always dangerous to the firmness of a grateful woman. To have a reason for going home the next day was a relief, for Mary earnestly desired to be always clear that she loved Fred best. When a tender affection has been storing itself in us through many of our years, the idea that we could accept any exchange for it seems to be a cheapening of our lives. And we can set a watch over our affections and our constancy as we can over other treasures.\n\n“Fred has lost all his other expectations; he must keep this,” Mary said to herself, with a smile curling her lips. It was impossible to help fleeting visions of another kind—new dignities and an acknowledged value of which she had often felt the absence. But these things with Fred outside them, Fred forsaken and looking sad for the want of her, could never tempt her deliberate thought.\n\n# CHAPTER LVIII.\n\n“For there can live no hatred in thine eye, Therefore in that I cannot know thy change: In many’s looks the false heart’s history Is writ in moods and frowns and wrinkles strange: But Heaven in thy creation did decree That in thy face sweet love should ever dwell: Whate’er thy thoughts or thy heart’s workings be Thy looks should nothing thence but sweetness tell.” —SHAKESPEARE: Sonnets.\n\nAt the time when Mr. Vincy uttered that presentiment about Rosamond, she herself had never had the idea that she should be driven to make the sort of appeal which he foresaw. She had not yet had any anxiety about ways and means, although her domestic life had been expensive as well as eventful. Her baby had been born prematurely, and all the embroidered robes and caps had to be laid by in darkness. This misfortune was attributed entirely to her having persisted in going out on horseback one day when her husband had desired her not to do so; but it must not be supposed that she had shown temper on the occasion, or rudely told him that she would do as she liked.\n\nWhat led her particularly to desire horse-exercise was a visit from Captain Lydgate, the baronet’s third son, who, I am sorry to say, was detested by our Tertius of that name as a vapid fop “parting his hair from brow to nape in a despicable fashion” (not followed by Tertius himself), and showing an ignorant security that he knew the proper thing to say on every topic. Lydgate inwardly cursed his own folly that he had drawn down this visit by consenting to go to his uncle’s on the wedding-tour, and he made himself rather disagreeable to Rosamond by saying so in private. For to Rosamond this visit was a source of unprecedented but gracefully concealed exultation. She was so intensely conscious of having a cousin who was a baronet’s son staying in the house, that she imagined the knowledge of what was implied by his presence to be diffused through all other minds; and when she introduced Captain Lydgate to her guests, she had a placid sense that his rank penetrated them as if it had been an odor. The satisfaction was enough for the time to melt away some disappointment in the conditions of marriage with a medical man even of good birth: it seemed now that her marriage was visibly as well as ideally floating her above the Middlemarch level, and the future looked bright with letters and visits to and from Quallingham, and vague advancement in consequence for Tertius. Especially as, probably at the Captain’s suggestion, his married sister, Mrs. Mengan, had come with her maid, and stayed two nights on her way from town. Hence it was clearly worth while for Rosamond to take pains with her music and the careful selection of her lace.\n\nAs to Captain Lydgate himself, his low brow, his aquiline nose bent on one side, and his rather heavy utterance, might have been disadvantageous in any young gentleman who had not a military bearing and mustache to give him what is doted on by some flower-like blond heads as “style.” He had, moreover, that sort of high-breeding which consists in being free from the petty solicitudes of middle-class gentility, and he was a great critic of feminine charms. Rosamond delighted in his admiration now even more than she had done at Quallingham, and he found it easy to spend several hours of the day in flirting with her. The visit altogether was one of the pleasantest larks he had ever had, not the less so perhaps because he suspected that his queer cousin Tertius wished him away: though Lydgate, who would rather (hyperbolically speaking) have died than have failed in polite hospitality, suppressed his dislike, and only pretended generally not to hear what the gallant officer said, consigning the task of answering him to Rosamond. For he was not at all a jealous husband, and preferred leaving a feather-headed young gentleman alone with his wife to bearing him company.\n\n“I wish you would talk more to the Captain at dinner, Tertius,” said Rosamond, one evening when the important guest was gone to Loamford to see some brother officers stationed there. “You really look so absent sometimes—you seem to be seeing through his head into something behind it, instead of looking at him.”\n\n“My dear Rosy, you don’t expect me to talk much to such a conceited ass as that, I hope,” said Lydgate, brusquely. “If he got his head broken, I might look at it with interest, not before.”\n“I cannot conceive why you should speak of your cousin so contemptuously,” said Rosamond, her fingers moving at her work while she spoke with a mild gravity which had a touch of disdain in it.\n\n“Ask Ladislaw if he doesn’t think your Captain the greatest bore he ever met with. Ladislaw has almost forsaken the house since he came.”\nRosamond thought she knew perfectly well why Mr. Ladislaw disliked the Captain: he was jealous, and she liked his being jealous.\n\n“It is impossible to say what will suit eccentric persons,” she answered, “but in my opinion Captain Lydgate is a thorough gentleman, and I think you ought not, out of respect to Sir Godwin, to treat him with neglect.”\n\n“No, dear; but we have had dinners for him. And he comes in and goes out as he likes. He doesn’t want me.”\n\n“Still, when he is in the room, you might show him more attention. He may not be a phoenix of cleverness in your sense; his profession is different; but it would be all the better for you to talk a little on his subjects. I think his conversation is quite agreeable. And he is anything but an unprincipled man.”\n\n“The fact is, you would wish me to be a little more like him, Rosy,” said Lydgate, in a sort of resigned murmur, with a smile which was not exactly tender, and certainly not merry. Rosamond was silent and did not smile again; but the lovely curves of her face looked good-tempered enough without smiling.\n\nThose words of Lydgate’s were like a sad milestone marking how far he had travelled from his old dreamland, in which Rosamond Vincy appeared to be that perfect piece of womanhood who would reverence her husband’s mind after the fashion of an accomplished mermaid, using her comb and looking-glass and singing her song for the relaxation of his adored wisdom alone. He had begun to distinguish between that imagined adoration and the attraction towards a man’s talent because it gives him prestige, and is like an order in his button-hole or an Honorable before his name.\n\nIt might have been supposed that Rosamond had travelled too, since she had found the pointless conversation of Mr. Ned Plymdale perfectly wearisome; but to most mortals there is a stupidity which is unendurable and a stupidity which is altogether acceptable—else, indeed, what would become of social bonds? Captain Lydgate’s stupidity was delicately scented, carried itself with “style,” talked with a good accent, and was closely related to Sir Godwin. Rosamond found it quite agreeable and caught many of its phrases.\n\nTherefore since Rosamond, as we know, was fond of horseback, there were plenty of reasons why she should be tempted to resume her riding when Captain Lydgate, who had ordered his man with two horses to follow him and put up at the “Green Dragon,” begged her to go out on the gray which he warranted to be gentle and trained to carry a lady—indeed, he had bought it for his sister, and was taking it to Quallingham. Rosamond went out the first time without telling her husband, and came back before his return; but the ride had been so thorough a success, and she declared herself so much the better in consequence, that he was informed of it with full reliance on his consent that she should go riding again.\n\nOn the contrary Lydgate was more than hurt—he was utterly confounded that she had risked herself on a strange horse without referring the matter to his wish. After the first almost thundering exclamations of astonishment, which sufficiently warned Rosamond of what was coming, he was silent for some moments.\n\n“However, you have come back safely,” he said, at last, in a decisive tone. “You will not go again, Rosy; that is understood. If it were the quietest, most familiar horse in the world, there would always be the chance of accident. And you know very well that I wished you to give up riding the roan on that account.”\n\n“But there is the chance of accident indoors, Tertius.”\n\n“My darling, don’t talk nonsense,” said Lydgate, in an imploring tone; “surely I am the person to judge for you. I think it is enough that I say you are not to go again.”\n\nRosamond was arranging her hair before dinner, and the reflection of her head in the glass showed no change in its loveliness except a little turning aside of the long neck. Lydgate had been moving about with his hands in his pockets, and now paused near her, as if he awaited some assurance.\n\n“I wish you would fasten up my plaits, dear,” said Rosamond, letting her arms fall with a little sigh, so as to make a husband ashamed of standing there like a brute. Lydgate had often fastened the plaits before, being among the deftest of men with his large finely formed fingers. He swept up the soft festoons of plaits and fastened in the tall comb (to such uses do men come!); and what could he do then but kiss the exquisite nape which was shown in all its delicate curves? But when we do what we have done before, it is often with a difference. Lydgate was still angry, and had not forgotten his point.\n\n“I shall tell the Captain that he ought to have known better than offer you his horse,” he said, as he moved away.\n\n“I beg you will not do anything of the kind, Tertius,” said Rosamond, looking at him with something more marked than usual in her speech. “It will be treating me as if I were a child. Promise that you will leave the subject to me.”\n\nThere did seem to be some truth in her objection. Lydgate said, “Very well,” with a surly obedience, and thus the discussion ended with his promising Rosamond, and not with her promising him.\n\nIn fact, she had been determined not to promise. Rosamond had that victorious obstinacy which never wastes its energy in impetuous resistance. What she liked to do was to her the right thing, and all her cleverness was directed to getting the means of doing it. She meant to go out riding again on the gray, and she did go on the next opportunity of her husband’s absence, not intending that he should know until it was late enough not to signify to her. The temptation was certainly great: she was very fond of the exercise, and the gratification of riding on a fine horse, with Captain Lydgate, Sir Godwin’s son, on another fine horse by her side, and of being met in this position by any one but her husband, was something as good as her dreams before marriage: moreover she was riveting the connection with the family at Quallingham, which must be a wise thing to do.\n\nBut the gentle gray, unprepared for the crash of a tree that was being felled on the edge of Halsell wood, took fright, and caused a worse fright to Rosamond, leading finally to the loss of her baby. Lydgate could not show his anger towards her, but he was rather bearish to the Captain, whose visit naturally soon came to an end.\n\nIn all future conversations on the subject, Rosamond was mildly certain that the ride had made no difference, and that if she had stayed at home the same symptoms would have come on and would have ended in the same way, because she had felt something like them before.\n\nLydgate could only say, “Poor, poor darling!”—but he secretly wondered over the terrible tenacity of this mild creature. There was gathering within him an amazed sense of his powerlessness over Rosamond. His superior knowledge and mental force, instead of being, as he had imagined, a shrine to consult on all occasions, was simply set aside on every practical question. He had regarded Rosamond’s cleverness as precisely of the receptive kind which became a woman. He was now beginning to find out what that cleverness was—what was the shape into which it had run as into a close network aloof and independent. No one quicker than Rosamond to see causes and effects which lay within the track of her own tastes and interests: she had seen clearly Lydgate’s preeminence in Middlemarch society, and could go on imaginatively tracing still more agreeable social effects when his talent should have advanced him; but for her, his professional and scientific ambition had no other relation to these desirable effects than if they had been the fortunate discovery of an ill-smelling oil. And that oil apart, with which she had nothing to do, of course she believed in her own opinion more than she did in his. Lydgate was astounded to find in numberless trifling matters, as well as in this last serious case of the riding, that affection did not make her compliant. He had no doubt that the affection was there, and had no presentiment that he had done anything to repel it. For his own part he said to himself that he loved her as tenderly as ever, and could make up his mind to her negations; but—well! Lydgate was much worried, and conscious of new elements in his life as noxious to him as an inlet of mud to a creature that has been used to breathe and bathe and dart after its illuminated prey in the clearest of waters.\n\nRosamond was soon looking lovelier than ever at her worktable, enjoying drives in her father’s phaeton and thinking it likely that she might be invited to Quallingham. She knew that she was a much more exquisite ornament to the drawing-room there than any daughter of the family, and in reflecting that the gentlemen were aware of that, did not perhaps sufficiently consider whether the ladies would be eager to see themselves surpassed.\n\nLydgate, relieved from anxiety about her, relapsed into what she inwardly called his moodiness—a name which to her covered his thoughtful preoccupation with other subjects than herself, as well as that uneasy look of the brow and distaste for all ordinary things as if they were mixed with bitter herbs, which really made a sort of weather-glass to his vexation and foreboding. These latter states of mind had one cause amongst others, which he had generously but mistakenly avoided mentioning to Rosamond, lest it should affect her health and spirits. Between him and her indeed there was that total missing of each other’s mental track, which is too evidently possible even between persons who are continually thinking of each other. To Lydgate it seemed that he had been spending month after month in sacrificing more than half of his best intent and best power to his tenderness for Rosamond; bearing her little claims and interruptions without impatience, and, above all, bearing without betrayal of bitterness to look through less and less of interfering illusion at the blank unreflecting surface her mind presented to his ardor for the more impersonal ends of his profession and his scientific study, an ardor which he had fancied that the ideal wife must somehow worship as sublime, though not in the least knowing why. But his endurance was mingled with a self-discontent which, if we know how to be candid, we shall confess to make more than half our bitterness under grievances, wife or husband included. It always remains true that if we had been greater, circumstance would have been less strong against us. Lydgate was aware that his concessions to Rosamond were often little more than the lapse of slackening resolution, the creeping paralysis apt to seize an enthusiasm which is out of adjustment to a constant portion of our lives. And on Lydgate’s enthusiasm there was constantly pressing not a simple weight of sorrow, but the biting presence of a petty degrading care, such as casts the blight of irony over all higher effort.\n\nThis was the care which he had hitherto abstained from mentioning to Rosamond; and he believed, with some wonder, that it had never entered her mind, though certainly no difficulty could be less mysterious. It was an inference with a conspicuous handle to it, and had been easily drawn by indifferent observers, that Lydgate was in debt; and he could not succeed in keeping out of his mind for long together that he was every day getting deeper into that swamp, which tempts men towards it with such a pretty covering of flowers and verdure. It is wonderful how soon a man gets up to his chin there—in a condition in which, in spite of himself, he is forced to think chiefly of release, though he had a scheme of the universe in his soul.\n\nEighteen months ago Lydgate was poor, but had never known the eager want of small sums, and felt rather a burning contempt for any one who descended a step in order to gain them. He was now experiencing something worse than a simple deficit: he was assailed by the vulgar hateful trials of a man who has bought and used a great many things which might have been done without, and which he is unable to pay for, though the demand for payment has become pressing.\n\nHow this came about may be easily seen without much arithmetic or knowledge of prices. When a man in setting up a house and preparing for marriage finds that his furniture and other initial expenses come to between four and five hundred pounds more than he has capital to pay for; when at the end of a year it appears that his household expenses, horses and et caeteras, amount to nearly a thousand, while the proceeds of the practice reckoned from the old books to be worth eight hundred per annum have sunk like a summer pond and make hardly five hundred, chiefly in unpaid entries, the plain inference is that, whether he minds it or not, he is in debt. Those were less expensive times than our own, and provincial life was comparatively modest; but the ease with which a medical man who had lately bought a practice, who thought that he was obliged to keep two horses, whose table was supplied without stint, and who paid an insurance on his life and a high rent for house and garden, might find his expenses doubling his receipts, can be conceived by any one who does not think these details beneath his consideration. Rosamond, accustomed from her childhood to an extravagant household, thought that good housekeeping consisted simply in ordering the best of everything—nothing else “answered;” and Lydgate supposed that “if things were done at all, they must be done properly”—he did not see how they were to live otherwise. If each head of household expenditure had been mentioned to him beforehand, he would have probably observed that “it could hardly come to much,” and if any one had suggested a saving on a particular article—for example, the substitution of cheap fish for dear—it would have appeared to him simply a penny-wise, mean notion. Rosamond, even without such an occasion as Captain Lydgate’s visit, was fond of giving invitations, and Lydgate, though he often thought the guests tiresome, did not interfere. This sociability seemed a necessary part of professional prudence, and the entertainment must be suitable. It is true Lydgate was constantly visiting the homes of the poor and adjusting his prescriptions of diet to their small means; but, dear me! has it not by this time ceased to be remarkable—is it not rather that we expect in men, that they should have numerous strands of experience lying side by side and never compare them with each other? Expenditure—like ugliness and errors—becomes a totally new thing when we attach our own personality to it, and measure it by that wide difference which is manifest (in our own sensations) between ourselves and others. Lydgate believed himself to be careless about his dress, and he despised a man who calculated the effects of his costume; it seemed to him only a matter of course that he had abundance of fresh garments—such things were naturally ordered in sheaves. It must be remembered that he had never hitherto felt the check of importunate debt, and he walked by habit, not by self-criticism. But the check had come.\n\nIts novelty made it the more irritating. He was amazed, disgusted that conditions so foreign to all his purposes, so hatefully disconnected with the objects he cared to occupy himself with, should have lain in ambush and clutched him when he was unaware. And there was not only the actual debt; there was the certainty that in his present position he must go on deepening it. Two furnishing tradesmen at Brassing, whose bills had been incurred before his marriage, and whom uncalculated current expenses had ever since prevented him from paying, had repeatedly sent him unpleasant letters which had forced themselves on his attention. This could hardly have been more galling to any disposition than to Lydgate’s, with his intense pride—his dislike of asking a favor or being under an obligation to any one. He had scorned even to form conjectures about Mr. Vincy’s intentions on money matters, and nothing but extremity could have induced him to apply to his father-in-law, even if he had not been made aware in various indirect ways since his marriage that Mr. Vincy’s own affairs were not flourishing, and that the expectation of help from him would be resented. Some men easily trust in the readiness of friends; it had never in the former part of his life occurred to Lydgate that he should need to do so: he had never thought what borrowing would be to him; but now that the idea had entered his mind, he felt that he would rather incur any other hardship. In the mean time he had no money or prospects of money; and his practice was not getting more lucrative.\n\nNo wonder that Lydgate had been unable to suppress all signs of inward trouble during the last few months, and now that Rosamond was regaining brilliant health, he meditated taking her entirely into confidence on his difficulties. New conversance with tradesmen’s bills had forced his reasoning into a new channel of comparison: he had begun to consider from a new point of view what was necessary and unnecessary in goods ordered, and to see that there must be some change of habits. How could such a change be made without Rosamond’s concurrence? The immediate occasion of opening the disagreeable fact to her was forced upon him.\n\nHaving no money, and having privately sought advice as to what security could possibly be given by a man in his position, Lydgate had offered the one good security in his power to the less peremptory creditor, who was a silversmith and jeweller, and who consented to take on himself the upholsterer’s credit also, accepting interest for a given term. The security necessary was a bill of sale on the furniture of his house, which might make a creditor easy for a reasonable time about a debt amounting to less than four hundred pounds; and the silversmith, Mr. Dover, was willing to reduce it by taking back a portion of the plate and any other article which was as good as new. “Any other article” was a phrase delicately implying jewellery, and more particularly some purple amethysts costing thirty pounds, which Lydgate had bought as a bridal present.\n\nOpinions may be divided as to his wisdom in making this present: some may think that it was a graceful attention to be expected from a man like Lydgate, and that the fault of any troublesome consequences lay in the pinched narrowness of provincial life at that time, which offered no conveniences for professional people whose fortune was not proportioned to their tastes; also, in Lydgate’s ridiculous fastidiousness about asking his friends for money.\n\nHowever, it had seemed a question of no moment to him on that fine morning when he went to give a final order for plate: in the presence of other jewels enormously expensive, and as an addition to orders of which the amount had not been exactly calculated, thirty pounds for ornaments so exquisitely suited to Rosamond’s neck and arms could hardly appear excessive when there was no ready cash for it to exceed. But at this crisis Lydgate’s imagination could not help dwelling on the possibility of letting the amethysts take their place again among Mr. Dover’s stock, though he shrank from the idea of proposing this to Rosamond. Having been roused to discern consequences which he had never been in the habit of tracing, he was preparing to act on this discernment with some of the rigor (by no means all) that he would have applied in pursuing experiment. He was nerving himself to this rigor as he rode from Brassing, and meditated on the representations he must make to Rosamond.\n\nIt was evening when he got home. He was intensely miserable, this strong man of nine-and-twenty and of many gifts. He was not saying angrily within himself that he had made a profound mistake; but the mistake was at work in him like a recognized chronic disease, mingling its uneasy importunities with every prospect, and enfeebling every thought. As he went along the passage to the drawing-room, he heard the piano and singing. Of course, Ladislaw was there. It was some weeks since Will had parted from Dorothea, yet he was still at the old post in Middlemarch. Lydgate had no objection in general to Ladislaw’s coming, but just now he was annoyed that he could not find his hearth free. When he opened the door the two singers went on towards the key-note, raising their eyes and looking at him indeed, but not regarding his entrance as an interruption. To a man galled with his harness as poor Lydgate was, it is not soothing to see two people warbling at him, as he comes in with the sense that the painful day has still pains in store. His face, already paler than usual, took on a scowl as he walked across the room and flung himself into a chair.\n\nThe singers feeling themselves excused by the fact that they had only three bars to sing, now turned round.\n\n“How are you, Lydgate?” said Will, coming forward to shake hands.\n\nLydgate took his hand, but did not think it necessary to speak.\n\n“Have you dined, Tertius? I expected you much earlier,” said Rosamond, who had already seen that her husband was in a “horrible humor.” She seated herself in her usual place as she spoke.\n\n“I have dined. I should like some tea, please,” said Lydgate, curtly, still scowling and looking markedly at his legs stretched out before him.\n\nWill was too quick to need more. “I shall be off,” he said, reaching his hat.\n\n“Tea is coming,” said Rosamond; “pray don’t go.”\n\n“Yes, Lydgate is bored,” said Will, who had more comprehension of Lydgate than Rosamond had, and was not offended by his manner, easily imagining outdoor causes of annoyance.\n\n“There is the more need for you to stay,” said Rosamond, playfully, and in her lightest accent; “he will not speak to me all the evening.”\n\n“Yes, Rosamond, I shall,” said Lydgate, in his strong baritone. “I have some serious business to speak to you about.”\n\nNo introduction of the business could have been less like that which Lydgate had intended; but her indifferent manner had been too provoking.\n\n“There! you see,” said Will. “I’m going to the meeting about the Mechanics’ Institute. Good-by;” and he went quickly out of the room.\n\nRosamond did not look at her husband, but presently rose and took her place before the tea-tray. She was thinking that she had never seen him so disagreeable. Lydgate turned his dark eyes on her and watched her as she delicately handled the tea-service with her taper fingers, and looked at the objects immediately before her with no curve in her face disturbed, and yet with an ineffable protest in her air against all people with unpleasant manners. For the moment he lost the sense of his wound in a sudden speculation about this new form of feminine impassibility revealing itself in the sylph-like frame which he had once interpreted as the sign of a ready intelligent sensitiveness. His mind glancing back to Laure while he looked at Rosamond, he said inwardly, “Would she kill me because I wearied her?” and then, “It is the way with all women.” But this power of generalizing which gives men so much the superiority in mistake over the dumb animals, was immediately thwarted by Lydgate’s memory of wondering impressions from the behavior of another woman—from Dorothea’s looks and tones of emotion about her husband when Lydgate began to attend him—from her passionate cry to be taught what would best comfort that man for whose sake it seemed as if she must quell every impulse in her except the yearnings of faithfulness and compassion. These revived impressions succeeded each other quickly and dreamily in Lydgate’s mind while the tea was being brewed. He had shut his eyes in the last instant of reverie while he heard Dorothea saying, “Advise me—think what I can do—he has been all his life laboring and looking forward. He minds about nothing else—and I mind about nothing else.”\n\nThat voice of deep-souled womanhood had remained within him as the enkindling conceptions of dead and sceptred genius had remained within him (is there not a genius for feeling nobly which also reigns over human spirits and their conclusions?); the tones were a music from which he was falling away—he had really fallen into a momentary doze, when Rosamond said in her silvery neutral way, “Here is your tea, Tertius,” setting it on the small table by his side, and then moved back to her place without looking at him. Lydgate was too hasty in attributing insensibility to her; after her own fashion, she was sensitive enough, and took lasting impressions. Her impression now was one of offence and repulsion. But then, Rosamond had no scowls and had never raised her voice: she was quite sure that no one could justly find fault with her.\n\nPerhaps Lydgate and she had never felt so far off each other before; but there were strong reasons for not deferring his revelation, even if he had not already begun it by that abrupt announcement; indeed some of the angry desire to rouse her into more sensibility on his account which had prompted him to speak prematurely, still mingled with his pain in the prospect of her pain. But he waited till the tray was gone, the candles were lit, and the evening quiet might be counted on: the interval had left time for repelled tenderness to return into the old course. He spoke kindly.\n\n“Dear Rosy, lay down your work and come to sit by me,” he said, gently, pushing away the table, and stretching out his arm to draw a chair near his own.\n\nRosamond obeyed. As she came towards him in her drapery of transparent faintly tinted muslin, her slim yet round figure never looked more graceful; as she sat down by him and laid one hand on the elbow of his chair, at last looking at him and meeting his eyes, her delicate neck and cheek and purely cut lips never had more of that untarnished beauty which touches as in spring-time and infancy and all sweet freshness. It touched Lydgate now, and mingled the early moments of his love for her with all the other memories which were stirred in this crisis of deep trouble. He laid his ample hand softly on hers, saying—\n\n“Dear!” with the lingering utterance which affection gives to the word. Rosamond too was still under the power of that same past, and her husband was still in part the Lydgate whose approval had stirred delight. She put his hair lightly away from his forehead, then laid her other hand on his, and was conscious of forgiving him.\n\n“I am obliged to tell you what will hurt you, Rosy. But there are things which husband and wife must think of together. I dare say it has occurred to you already that I am short of money.”\n\nLydgate paused; but Rosamond turned her neck and looked at a vase on the mantel-piece.\n\n“I was not able to pay for all the things we had to get before we were married, and there have been expenses since which I have been obliged to meet. The consequence is, there is a large debt at Brassing—three hundred and eighty pounds—which has been pressing on me a good while, and in fact we are getting deeper every day, for people don’t pay me the faster because others want the money. I took pains to keep it from you while you were not well; but now we must think together about it, and you must help me.”\n\n“What can I do, Tertius?” said Rosamond, turning her eyes on him again. That little speech of four words, like so many others in all languages, is capable by varied vocal inflections of expressing all states of mind from helpless dimness to exhaustive argumentative perception, from the completest self-devoting fellowship to the most neutral aloofness. Rosamond’s thin utterance threw into the words “What can—I—do!” as much neutrality as they could hold. They fell like a mortal chill on Lydgate’s roused tenderness. He did not storm in indignation—he felt too sad a sinking of the heart. And when he spoke again it was more in the tone of a man who forces himself to fulfil a task.\n\n“It is necessary for you to know, because I have to give security for a time, and a man must come to make an inventory of the furniture.”\n\nRosamond colored deeply. “Have you not asked papa for money?” she said, as soon as she could speak.\n\n“No.”\n“Then I must ask him!” she said, releasing her hands from Lydgate’s, and rising to stand at two yards’ distance from him.\n\n“No, Rosy,” said Lydgate, decisively. “It is too late to do that. The inventory will be begun to-morrow. Remember it is a mere security: it will make no difference: it is a temporary affair. I insist upon it that your father shall not know, unless I choose to tell him,” added Lydgate, with a more peremptory emphasis.\n\nThis certainly was unkind, but Rosamond had thrown him back on evil expectation as to what she would do in the way of quiet steady disobedience. The unkindness seemed unpardonable to her: she was not given to weeping and disliked it, but now her chin and lips began to tremble and the tears welled up. Perhaps it was not possible for Lydgate, under the double stress of outward material difficulty and of his own proud resistance to humiliating consequences, to imagine fully what this sudden trial was to a young creature who had known nothing but indulgence, and whose dreams had all been of new indulgence, more exactly to her taste. But he did wish to spare her as much as he could, and her tears cut him to the heart. He could not speak again immediately; but Rosamond did not go on sobbing: she tried to conquer her agitation and wiped away her tears, continuing to look before her at the mantel-piece.\n\n“Try not to grieve, darling,” said Lydgate, turning his eyes up towards her. That she had chosen to move away from him in this moment of her trouble made everything harder to say, but he must absolutely go on. “We must brace ourselves to do what is necessary. It is I who have been in fault: I ought to have seen that I could not afford to live in this way. But many things have told against me in my practice, and it really just now has ebbed to a low point. I may recover it, but in the mean time we must pull up—we must change our way of living. We shall weather it. When I have given this security I shall have time to look about me; and you are so clever that if you turn your mind to managing you will school me into carefulness. I have been a thoughtless rascal about squaring prices—but come, dear, sit down and forgive me.”\n\nLydgate was bowing his neck under the yoke like a creature who had talons, but who had Reason too, which often reduces us to meekness. When he had spoken the last words in an imploring tone, Rosamond returned to the chair by his side. His self-blame gave her some hope that he would attend to her opinion, and she said—\n\n“Why can you not put off having the inventory made? You can send the men away to-morrow when they come.”\n\n“I shall not send them away,” said Lydgate, the peremptoriness rising again. Was it of any use to explain?\n\n“If we left Middlemarch? there would of course be a sale, and that would do as well.”\n\n“But we are not going to leave Middlemarch.”\n\n“I am sure, Tertius, it would be much better to do so. Why can we not go to London? Or near Durham, where your family is known?”\n\n“We can go nowhere without money, Rosamond.”\n\n“Your friends would not wish you to be without money. And surely these odious tradesmen might be made to understand that, and to wait, if you would make proper representations to them.”\n\n“This is idle Rosamond,” said Lydgate, angrily. “You must learn to take my judgment on questions you don’t understand. I have made necessary arrangements, and they must be carried out. As to friends, I have no expectations whatever from them, and shall not ask them for anything.”\n\nRosamond sat perfectly still. The thought in her mind was that if she had known how Lydgate would behave, she would never have married him.\n\n“We have no time to waste now on unnecessary words, dear,” said Lydgate, trying to be gentle again. “There are some details that I want to consider with you. Dover says he will take a good deal of the plate back again, and any of the jewellery we like. He really behaves very well.”\n\n“Are we to go without spoons and forks then?” said Rosamond, whose very lips seemed to get thinner with the thinness of her utterance. She was determined to make no further resistance or suggestions.\n\n“Oh no, dear!” said Lydgate. “But look here,” he continued, drawing a paper from his pocket and opening it; “here is Dover’s account. See, I have marked a number of articles, which if we returned them would reduce the amount by thirty pounds and more. I have not marked any of the jewellery.” Lydgate had really felt this point of the jewellery very bitter to himself; but he had overcome the feeling by severe argument. He could not propose to Rosamond that she should return any particular present of his, but he had told himself that he was bound to put Dover’s offer before her, and her inward prompting might make the affair easy.\n\n“It is useless for me to look, Tertius,” said Rosamond, calmly; “you will return what you please.” She would not turn her eyes on the paper, and Lydgate, flushing up to the roots of his hair, drew it back and let it fall on his knee. Meanwhile Rosamond quietly went out of the room, leaving Lydgate helpless and wondering. Was she not coming back? It seemed that she had no more identified herself with him than if they had been creatures of different species and opposing interests. He tossed his head and thrust his hands deep into his pockets with a sort of vengeance. There was still science—there were still good objects to work for. He must give a tug still—all the stronger because other satisfactions were going.\n\nBut the door opened and Rosamond re-entered. She carried the leather box containing the amethysts, and a tiny ornamental basket which contained other boxes, and laying them on the chair where she had been sitting, she said, with perfect propriety in her air—\n\n“This is all the jewellery you ever gave me. You can return what you like of it, and of the plate also. You will not, of course, expect me to stay at home to-morrow. I shall go to papa’s.”\n\nTo many women the look Lydgate cast at her would have been more terrible than one of anger: it had in it a despairing acceptance of the distance she was placing between them.\n\n“And when shall you come back again?” he said, with a bitter edge on his accent.\n\n“Oh, in the evening. Of course I shall not mention the subject to mamma.” Rosamond was convinced that no woman could behave more irreproachably than she was behaving; and she went to sit down at her work-table. Lydgate sat meditating a minute or two, and the result was that he said, with some of the old emotion in his tone—\n\n“Now we have been united, Rosy, you should not leave me to myself in the first trouble that has come.”\n\n“Certainly not,” said Rosamond; “I shall do everything it becomes me to do.”\n\n“It is not right that the thing should be left to servants, or that I should have to speak to them about it. And I shall be obliged to go out—I don’t know how early. I understand your shrinking from the humiliation of these money affairs. But, my dear Rosamond, as a question of pride, which I feel just as much as you can, it is surely better to manage the thing ourselves, and let the servants see as little of it as possible; and since you are my wife, there is no hindering your share in my disgraces—if there were disgraces.”\n\nRosamond did not answer immediately, but at last she said, “Very well, I will stay at home.”\n\n“I shall not touch these jewels, Rosy. Take them away again. But I will write out a list of plate that we may return, and that can be packed up and sent at once.”\n\n“The servants will know that,” said Rosamond, with the slightest touch of sarcasm.\n\n“Well, we must meet some disagreeables as necessities. Where is the ink, I wonder?” said Lydgate, rising, and throwing the account on the larger table where he meant to write.\n\nRosamond went to reach the inkstand, and after setting it on the table was going to turn away, when Lydgate, who was standing close by, put his arm round her and drew her towards him, saying—\n\n“Come, darling, let us make the best of things. It will only be for a time, I hope, that we shall have to be stingy and particular. Kiss me.”\n\nHis native warm-heartedness took a great deal of quenching, and it is a part of manliness for a husband to feel keenly the fact that an inexperienced girl has got into trouble by marrying him. She received his kiss and returned it faintly, and in this way an appearance of accord was recovered for the time. But Lydgate could not help looking forward with dread to the inevitable future discussions about expenditure and the necessity for a complete change in their way of living.\n\n# CHAPTER LIX.\n\n“They said of old the Soul had human shape, But smaller, subtler than the fleshly self, So wandered forth for airing when it pleased. And see! beside her cherub-face there floats A pale-lipped form aerial whispering Its promptings in that little shell her ear.”\n\nNews is often dispersed as thoughtlessly and effectively as that pollen which the bees carry off (having no idea how powdery they are) when they are buzzing in search of their particular nectar. This fine comparison has reference to Fred Vincy, who on that evening at Lowick Parsonage heard a lively discussion among the ladies on the news which their old servant had got from Tantripp concerning Mr. Casaubon’s strange mention of Mr. Ladislaw in a codicil to his will made not long before his death. Miss Winifred was astounded to find that her brother had known the fact before, and observed that Camden was the most wonderful man for knowing things and not telling them; whereupon Mary Garth said that the codicil had perhaps got mixed up with the habits of spiders, which Miss Winifred never would listen to. Mrs. Farebrother considered that the news had something to do with their having only once seen Mr. Ladislaw at Lowick, and Miss Noble made many small compassionate mewings.\n\nFred knew little and cared less about Ladislaw and the Casaubons, and his mind never recurred to that discussion till one day calling on Rosamond at his mother’s request to deliver a message as he passed, he happened to see Ladislaw going away. Fred and Rosamond had little to say to each other now that marriage had removed her from collision with the unpleasantness of brothers, and especially now that he had taken what she held the stupid and even reprehensible step of giving up the Church to take to such a business as Mr. Garth’s. Hence Fred talked by preference of what he considered indifferent news, and “a propos of that young Ladislaw” mentioned what he had heard at Lowick Parsonage.\n\nNow Lydgate, like Mr. Farebrother, knew a great deal more than he told, and when he had once been set thinking about the relation between Will and Dorothea his conjectures had gone beyond the fact. He imagined that there was a passionate attachment on both sides, and this struck him as much too serious to gossip about. He remembered Will’s irritability when he had mentioned Mrs. Casaubon, and was the more circumspect. On the whole his surmises, in addition to what he knew of the fact, increased his friendliness and tolerance towards Ladislaw, and made him understand the vacillation which kept him at Middlemarch after he had said that he should go away. It was significant of the separateness between Lydgate’s mind and Rosamond’s that he had no impulse to speak to her on the subject; indeed, he did not quite trust her reticence towards Will. And he was right there; though he had no vision of the way in which her mind would act in urging her to speak.\n\nWhen she repeated Fred’s news to Lydgate, he said, “Take care you don’t drop the faintest hint to Ladislaw, Rosy. He is likely to fly out as if you insulted him. Of course it is a painful affair.”\n\nRosamond turned her neck and patted her hair, looking the image of placid indifference. But the next time Will came when Lydgate was away, she spoke archly about his not going to London as he had threatened.\n\n“I know all about it. I have a confidential little bird,” said she, showing very pretty airs of her head over the bit of work held high between her active fingers. “There is a powerful magnet in this neighborhood.”\n\n“To be sure there is. Nobody knows that better than you,” said Will, with light gallantry, but inwardly prepared to be angry.\n\n“It is really the most charming romance: Mr. Casaubon jealous, and foreseeing that there was no one else whom Mrs. Casaubon would so much like to marry, and no one who would so much like to marry her as a certain gentleman; and then laying a plan to spoil all by making her forfeit her property if she did marry that gentleman—and then—and then—and then—oh, I have no doubt the end will be thoroughly romantic.”\n\n“Great God! what do you mean?” said Will, flushing over face and ears, his features seeming to change as if he had had a violent shake. “Don’t joke; tell me what you mean.”\n\n“You don’t really know?” said Rosamond, no longer playful, and desiring nothing better than to tell in order that she might evoke effects.\n\n“No!” he returned, impatiently.\n\n“Don’t know that Mr. Casaubon has left it in his will that if Mrs. Casaubon marries you she is to forfeit all her property?”\n\n“How do you know that it is true?” said Will, eagerly.\n\n“My brother Fred heard it from the Farebrothers.” Will started up from his chair and reached his hat.\n\n“I dare say she likes you better than the property,” said Rosamond, looking at him from a distance.\n\n“Pray don’t say any more about it,” said Will, in a hoarse undertone extremely unlike his usual light voice. “It is a foul insult to her and to me.” Then he sat down absently, looking before him, but seeing nothing.\n\n“Now you are angry with me,” said Rosamond. “It is too bad to bear me malice. You ought to be obliged to me for telling you.”\n\n“So I am,” said Will, abruptly, speaking with that kind of double soul which belongs to dreamers who answer questions.\n\n“I expect to hear of the marriage,” said Rosamond, playfully.\n\n“Never! You will never hear of the marriage!”\n\nWith those words uttered impetuously, Will rose, put out his hand to Rosamond, still with the air of a somnambulist, and went away.\n\nWhen he was gone, Rosamond left her chair and walked to the other end of the room, leaning when she got there against a chiffonniere, and looking out of the window wearily. She was oppressed by ennui, and by that dissatisfaction which in women’s minds is continually turning into a trivial jealousy, referring to no real claims, springing from no deeper passion than the vague exactingness of egoism, and yet capable of impelling action as well as speech. “There really is nothing to care for much,” said poor Rosamond inwardly, thinking of the family at Quallingham, who did not write to her; and that perhaps Tertius when he came home would tease her about expenses. She had already secretly disobeyed him by asking her father to help them, and he had ended decisively by saying, “I am more likely to want help myself.”\n\n# CHAPTER LX.\n\nGood phrases are surely, and ever were, very commendable. —Justice Shallow.\n\nA few days afterwards—it was already the end of August—there was an occasion which caused some excitement in Middlemarch: the public, if it chose, was to have the advantage of buying, under the distinguished auspices of Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, the furniture, books, and pictures which anybody might see by the handbills to be the best in every kind, belonging to Edwin Larcher, Esq. This was not one of the sales indicating the depression of trade; on the contrary, it was due to Mr. Larcher’s great success in the carrying business, which warranted his purchase of a mansion near Riverston already furnished in high style by an illustrious Spa physician—furnished indeed with such large framefuls of expensive flesh-painting in the dining-room, that Mrs. Larcher was nervous until reassured by finding the subjects to be Scriptural. Hence the fine opportunity to purchasers which was well pointed out in the handbills of Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, whose acquaintance with the history of art enabled him to state that the hall furniture, to be sold without reserve, comprised a piece of carving by a contemporary of Gibbons.\n\nAt Middlemarch in those times a large sale was regarded as a kind of festival. There was a table spread with the best cold eatables, as at a superior funeral; and facilities were offered for that generous-drinking of cheerful glasses which might lead to generous and cheerful bidding for undesirable articles. Mr. Larcher’s sale was the more attractive in the fine weather because the house stood just at the end of the town, with a garden and stables attached, in that pleasant issue from Middlemarch called the London Road, which was also the road to the New Hospital and to Mr. Bulstrode’s retired residence, known as the Shrubs. In short, the auction was as good as a fair, and drew all classes with leisure at command: to some, who risked making bids in order simply to raise prices, it was almost equal to betting at the races. The second day, when the best furniture was to be sold, “everybody” was there; even Mr. Thesiger, the rector of St. Peter’s, had looked in for a short time, wishing to buy the carved table, and had rubbed elbows with Mr. Bambridge and Mr. Horrock. There was a wreath of Middlemarch ladies accommodated with seats round the large table in the dining-room, where Mr. Borthrop Trumbull was mounted with desk and hammer; but the rows chiefly of masculine faces behind were often varied by incomings and outgoings both from the door and the large bow-window opening on to the lawn.\n\n“Everybody” that day did not include Mr. Bulstrode, whose health could not well endure crowds and draughts. But Mrs. Bulstrode had particularly wished to have a certain picture—a “Supper at Emmaus,” attributed in the catalogue to Guido; and at the last moment before the day of the sale Mr. Bulstrode had called at the office of the “Pioneer,” of which he was now one of the proprietors, to beg of Mr. Ladislaw as a great favor that he would obligingly use his remarkable knowledge of pictures on behalf of Mrs. Bulstrode, and judge of the value of this particular painting—“if,” added the scrupulously polite banker, “attendance at the sale would not interfere with the arrangements for your departure, which I know is imminent.”\n\nThis proviso might have sounded rather satirically in Will’s ear if he had been in a mood to care about such satire. It referred to an understanding entered into many weeks before with the proprietors of the paper, that he should be at liberty any day he pleased to hand over the management to the subeditor whom he had been training; since he wished finally to quit Middlemarch. But indefinite visions of ambition are weak against the ease of doing what is habitual or beguilingly agreeable; and we all know the difficulty of carrying out a resolve when we secretly long that it may turn out to be unnecessary. In such states of mind the most incredulous person has a private leaning towards miracle: impossible to conceive how our wish could be fulfilled, still—very wonderful things have happened! Will did not confess this weakness to himself, but he lingered. What was the use of going to London at that time of the year? The Rugby men who would remember him were not there; and so far as political writing was concerned, he would rather for a few weeks go on with the “Pioneer.” At the present moment, however, when Mr. Bulstrode was speaking to him, he had both a strengthened resolve to go and an equally strong resolve not to go till he had once more seen Dorothea. Hence he replied that he had reasons for deferring his departure a little, and would be happy to go to the sale.\n\nWill was in a defiant mood, his consciousness being deeply stung with the thought that the people who looked at him probably knew a fact tantamount to an accusation against him as a fellow with low designs which were to be frustrated by a disposal of property. Like most people who assert their freedom with regard to conventional distinction, he was prepared to be sudden and quick at quarrel with any one who might hint that he had personal reasons for that assertion—that there was anything in his blood, his bearing, or his character to which he gave the mask of an opinion. When he was under an irritating impression of this kind he would go about for days with a defiant look, the color changing in his transparent skin as if he were on the qui vive, watching for something which he had to dart upon.\n\nThis expression was peculiarly noticeable in him at the sale, and those who had only seen him in his moods of gentle oddity or of bright enjoyment would have been struck with a contrast. He was not sorry to have this occasion for appearing in public before the Middlemarch tribes of Toller, Hackbutt, and the rest, who looked down on him as an adventurer, and were in a state of brutal ignorance about Dante—who sneered at his Polish blood, and were themselves of a breed very much in need of crossing. He stood in a conspicuous place not far from the auctioneer, with a fore-finger in each side-pocket and his head thrown backward, not caring to speak to anybody, though he had been cordially welcomed as a connoissure by Mr. Trumbull, who was enjoying the utmost activity of his great faculties.\n\nAnd surely among all men whose vocation requires them to exhibit their powers of speech, the happiest is a prosperous provincial auctioneer keenly alive to his own jokes and sensible of his encyclopedic knowledge. Some saturnine, sour-blooded persons might object to be constantly insisting on the merits of all articles from boot-jacks to “Berghems;” but Mr. Borthrop Trumbull had a kindly liquid in his veins; he was an admirer by nature, and would have liked to have the universe under his hammer, feeling that it would go at a higher figure for his recommendation.\n\nMeanwhile Mrs. Larcher’s drawing-room furniture was enough for him. When Will Ladislaw had come in, a second fender, said to have been forgotten in its right place, suddenly claimed the auctioneer’s enthusiasm, which he distributed on the equitable principle of praising those things most which were most in need of praise. The fender was of polished steel, with much lancet-shaped open-work and a sharp edge.\n\n“Now, ladies,” said he, “I shall appeal to you. Here is a fender which at any other sale would hardly be offered with out reserve, being, as I may say, for quality of steel and quaintness of design, a kind of thing”—here Mr. Trumbull dropped his voice and became slightly nasal, trimming his outlines with his left finger—“that might not fall in with ordinary tastes. Allow me to tell you that by-and-by this style of workmanship will be the only one in vogue—half-a-crown, you said? thank you—going at half-a-crown, this characteristic fender; and I have particular information that the antique style is very much sought after in high quarters. Three shillings—three-and-sixpence—hold it well up, Joseph! Look, ladies, at the chastity of the design—I have no doubt myself that it was turned out in the last century! Four shillings, Mr. Mawmsey?—four shillings.”\n\n“It’s not a thing I would put in my drawing-room,” said Mrs. Mawmsey, audibly, for the warning of the rash husband. “I wonder at Mrs. Larcher. Every blessed child’s head that fell against it would be cut in two. The edge is like a knife.”\n\n“Quite true,” rejoined Mr. Trumbull, quickly, “and most uncommonly useful to have a fender at hand that will cut, if you have a leather shoe-tie or a bit of string that wants cutting and no knife at hand: many a man has been left hanging because there was no knife to cut him down. Gentlemen, here’s a fender that if you had the misfortune to hang yourselves would cut you down in no time—with astonishing celerity—four-and-sixpence—five—five-and-sixpence—an appropriate thing for a spare bedroom where there was a four-poster and a guest a little out of his mind—six shillings—thank you, Mr. Clintup—going at six shillings—going—gone!” The auctioneer’s glance, which had been searching round him with a preternatural susceptibility to all signs of bidding, here dropped on the paper before him, and his voice too dropped into a tone of indifferent despatch as he said, “Mr. Clintup. Be handy, Joseph.”\n\n“It was worth six shillings to have a fender you could always tell that joke on,” said Mr. Clintup, laughing low and apologetically to his next neighbor. He was a diffident though distinguished nurseryman, and feared that the audience might regard his bid as a foolish one.\n\nMeanwhile Joseph had brought a trayful of small articles. “Now, ladies,” said Mr. Trumbull, taking up one of the articles, “this tray contains a very recherchy lot—a collection of trifles for the drawing-room table—and trifles make the sum of human things—nothing more important than trifles—(yes, Mr. Ladislaw, yes, by-and-by)—but pass the tray round, Joseph—these bijoux must be examined, ladies. This I have in my hand is an ingenious contrivance—a sort of practical rebus, I may call it: here, you see, it looks like an elegant heart-shaped box, portable—for the pocket; there, again, it becomes like a splendid double flower—an ornament for the table; and now”—Mr. Trumbull allowed the flower to fall alarmingly into strings of heart-shaped leaves—“a book of riddles! No less than five hundred printed in a beautiful red. Gentlemen, if I had less of a conscience, I should not wish you to bid high for this lot—I have a longing for it myself. What can promote innocent mirth, and I may say virtue, more than a good riddle?—it hinders profane language, and attaches a man to the society of refined females. This ingenious article itself, without the elegant domino-box, card-basket, &c., ought alone to give a high price to the lot. Carried in the pocket it might make an individual welcome in any society. Four shillings, sir?—four shillings for this remarkable collection of riddles with the et caeteras. Here is a sample: ‘How must you spell honey to make it catch lady-birds? Answer—money.’ You hear?—lady-birds—honey money. This is an amusement to sharpen the intellect; it has a sting—it has what we call satire, and wit without indecency. Four-and-sixpence—five shillings.”\n\nThe bidding ran on with warming rivalry. Mr. Bowyer was a bidder, and this was too exasperating. Bowyer couldn’t afford it, and only wanted to hinder every other man from making a figure. The current carried even Mr. Horrock with it, but this committal of himself to an opinion fell from him with so little sacrifice of his neutral expression, that the bid might not have been detected as his but for the friendly oaths of Mr. Bambridge, who wanted to know what Horrock would do with blasted stuff only fit for haberdashers given over to that state of perdition which the horse-dealer so cordially recognized in the majority of earthly existences. The lot was finally knocked down at a guinea to Mr. Spilkins, a young Slender of the neighborhood, who was reckless with his pocket-money and felt his want of memory for riddles.\n\n“Come, Trumbull, this is too bad—you’ve been putting some old maid’s rubbish into the sale,” murmured Mr. Toller, getting close to the auctioneer. “I want to see how the prints go, and I must be off soon.”\n\n“Immediately, Mr. Toller. It was only an act of benevolence which your noble heart would approve. Joseph! quick with the prints—Lot 235. Now, gentlemen, you who are connoissures, you are going to have a treat. Here is an engraving of the Duke of Wellington surrounded by his staff on the Field of Waterloo; and notwithstanding recent events which have, as it were, enveloped our great Hero in a cloud, I will be bold to say—for a man in my line must not be blown about by political winds—that a finer subject—of the modern order, belonging to our own time and epoch—the understanding of man could hardly conceive: angels might, perhaps, but not men, sirs, not men.”\n\n“Who painted it?” said Mr. Powderell, much impressed.\n\n“It is a proof before the letter, Mr. Powderell—the painter is not known,” answered Trumbull, with a certain gaspingness in his last words, after which he pursed up his lips and stared round him.\n\n“I’ll bid a pound!” said Mr. Powderell, in a tone of resolved emotion, as of a man ready to put himself in the breach. Whether from awe or pity, nobody raised the price on him.\n\nNext came two Dutch prints which Mr. Toller had been eager for, and after he had secured them he went away. Other prints, and afterwards some paintings, were sold to leading Middlemarchers who had come with a special desire for them, and there was a more active movement of the audience in and out; some, who had bought what they wanted, going away, others coming in either quite newly or from a temporary visit to the refreshments which were spread under the marquee on the lawn. It was this marquee that Mr. Bambridge was bent on buying, and he appeared to like looking inside it frequently, as a foretaste of its possession. On the last occasion of his return from it he was observed to bring with him a new companion, a stranger to Mr. Trumbull and every one else, whose appearance, however, led to the supposition that he might be a relative of the horse-dealer’s—also “given to indulgence.” His large whiskers, imposing swagger, and swing of the leg, made him a striking figure; but his suit of black, rather shabby at the edges, caused the prejudicial inference that he was not able to afford himself as much indulgence as he liked.\n\n“Who is it you’ve picked up, Bam?” said Mr. Horrock, aside.\n\n“Ask him yourself,” returned Mr. Bambridge. “He said he’d just turned in from the road.”\n\nMr. Horrock eyed the stranger, who was leaning back against his stick with one hand, using his toothpick with the other, and looking about him with a certain restlessness apparently under the silence imposed on him by circumstances.\n\nAt length the “Supper at Emmaus” was brought forward, to Will’s immense relief, for he was getting so tired of the proceedings that he had drawn back a little and leaned his shoulder against the wall just behind the auctioneer. He now came forward again, and his eye caught the conspicuous stranger, who, rather to his surprise, was staring at him markedly. But Will was immediately appealed to by Mr. Trumbull.\n\n“Yes, Mr. Ladislaw, yes; this interests you as a connoissure, I think. It is some pleasure,” the auctioneer went on with a rising fervor, “to have a picture like this to show to a company of ladies and gentlemen—a picture worth any sum to an individual whose means were on a level with his judgment. It is a painting of the Italian school—by the celebrated Guydo, the greatest painter in the world, the chief of the Old Masters, as they are called—I take it, because they were up to a thing or two beyond most of us—in possession of secrets now lost to the bulk of mankind. Let me tell you, gentlemen, I have seen a great many pictures by the Old Masters, and they are not all up to this mark—some of them are darker than you might like and not family subjects. But here is a Guydo—the frame alone is worth pounds—which any lady might be proud to hang up—a suitable thing for what we call a refectory in a charitable institution, if any gentleman of the Corporation wished to show his munificence. Turn it a little, sir? yes. Joseph, turn it a little towards Mr. Ladislaw—Mr. Ladislaw, having been abroad, understands the merit of these things, you observe.”\n\nAll eyes were for a moment turned towards Will, who said, coolly, “Five pounds.” The auctioneer burst out in deep remonstrance.\n\n“Ah! Mr. Ladislaw! the frame alone is worth that. Ladies and gentlemen, for the credit of the town! Suppose it should be discovered hereafter that a gem of art has been amongst us in this town, and nobody in Middlemarch awake to it. Five guineas—five seven-six—five ten. Still, ladies, still! It is a gem, and ‘Full many a gem,’ as the poet says, has been allowed to go at a nominal price because the public knew no better, because it was offered in circles where there was—I was going to say a low feeling, but no!—Six pounds—six guineas—a Guydo of the first order going at six guineas—it is an insult to religion, ladies; it touches us all as Christians, gentlemen, that a subject like this should go at such a low figure—six pounds ten—seven—”\n\nThe bidding was brisk, and Will continued to share in it, remembering that Mrs. Bulstrode had a strong wish for the picture, and thinking that he might stretch the price to twelve pounds. But it was knocked down to him at ten guineas, whereupon he pushed his way towards the bow-window and went out. He chose to go under the marquee to get a glass of water, being hot and thirsty: it was empty of other visitors, and he asked the woman in attendance to fetch him some fresh water; but before she was well gone he was annoyed to see entering the florid stranger who had stared at him. It struck Will at this moment that the man might be one of those political parasitic insects of the bloated kind who had once or twice claimed acquaintance with him as having heard him speak on the Reform question, and who might think of getting a shilling by news. In this light his person, already rather heating to behold on a summer’s day, appeared the more disagreeable; and Will, half-seated on the elbow of a garden-chair, turned his eyes carefully away from the comer. But this signified little to our acquaintance Mr. Raffles, who never hesitated to thrust himself on unwilling observation, if it suited his purpose to do so. He moved a step or two till he was in front of Will, and said with full-mouthed haste, “Excuse me, Mr. Ladislaw—was your mother’s name Sarah Dunkirk?”\n\nWill, starting to his feet, moved backward a step, frowning, and saying with some fierceness, “Yes, sir, it was. And what is that to you?”\n\nIt was in Will’s nature that the first spark it threw out was a direct answer of the question and a challenge of the consequences. To have said, “What is that to you?” in the first instance, would have seemed like shuffling—as if he minded who knew anything about his origin!\n\nRaffles on his side had not the same eagerness for a collision which was implied in Ladislaw’s threatening air. The slim young fellow with his girl’s complexion looked like a tiger-cat ready to spring on him. Under such circumstances Mr. Raffles’s pleasure in annoying his company was kept in abeyance.\n\n“No offence, my good sir, no offence! I only remember your mother—knew her when she was a girl. But it is your father that you feature, sir. I had the pleasure of seeing your father too. Parents alive, Mr. Ladislaw?”\n\n“No!” thundered Will, in the same attitude as before.\n\n“Should be glad to do you a service, Mr. Ladislaw—by Jove, I should! Hope to meet again.”\n\nHereupon Raffles, who had lifted his hat with the last words, turned himself round with a swing of his leg and walked away. Will looked after him a moment, and could see that he did not re-enter the auction-room, but appeared to be walking towards the road. For an instant he thought that he had been foolish not to let the man go on talking;—but no! on the whole he preferred doing without knowledge from that source.\n\nLater in the evening, however, Raffles overtook him in the street, and appearing either to have forgotten the roughness of his former reception or to intend avenging it by a forgiving familiarity, greeted him jovially and walked by his side, remarking at first on the pleasantness of the town and neighborhood. Will suspected that the man had been drinking and was considering how to shake him off when Raffles said—\n\n“I’ve been abroad myself, Mr. Ladislaw—I’ve seen the world—used to parley-vous a little. It was at Boulogne I saw your father—a most uncommon likeness you are of him, by Jove! mouth—nose—eyes—hair turned off your brow just like his—a little in the foreign style. John Bull doesn’t do much of that. But your father was very ill when I saw him. Lord, lord! hands you might see through. You were a small youngster then. Did he get well?”\n\n“No,” said Will, curtly.\n\n“Ah! Well! I’ve often wondered what became of your mother. She ran away from her friends when she was a young lass—a proud-spirited lass, and pretty, by Jove! I knew the reason why she ran away,” said Raffles, winking slowly as he looked sideways at Will.\n\n“You know nothing dishonorable of her, sir,” said Will, turning on him rather savagely. But Mr. Raffles just now was not sensitive to shades of manner.\n\n“Not a bit!” said he, tossing his head decisively. “She was a little too honorable to like her friends—that was it!” Here Raffles again winked slowly. “Lord bless you, I knew all about ’em—a little in what you may call the respectable thieving line—the high style of receiving-house—none of your holes and corners—first-rate. Slap-up shop, high profits and no mistake. But Lord! Sarah would have known nothing about it—a dashing young lady she was—fine boarding-school—fit for a lord’s wife—only Archie Duncan threw it at her out of spite, because she would have nothing to do with him. And so she ran away from the whole concern. I travelled for ’em, sir, in a gentlemanly way—at a high salary. They didn’t mind her running away at first—godly folks, sir, very godly—and she was for the stage. The son was alive then, and the daughter was at a discount. Hallo! here we are at the Blue Bull. What do you say, Mr. Ladislaw?—shall we turn in and have a glass?”\n\n“No, I must say good evening,” said Will, dashing up a passage which led into Lowick Gate, and almost running to get out of Raffles’s reach.\n\nHe walked a long while on the Lowick road away from the town, glad of the starlit darkness when it came. He felt as if he had had dirt cast on him amidst shouts of scorn. There was this to confirm the fellow’s statement—that his mother never would tell him the reason why she had run away from her family.\n\nWell! what was he, Will Ladislaw, the worse, supposing the truth about that family to be the ugliest? His mother had braved hardship in order to separate herself from it. But if Dorothea’s friends had known this story—if the Chettams had known it—they would have had a fine color to give their suspicions a welcome ground for thinking him unfit to come near her. However, let them suspect what they pleased, they would find themselves in the wrong. They would find out that the blood in his veins was as free from the taint of meanness as theirs.\n\n# CHAPTER LXI.\n\n“Inconsistencies,” answered Imlac, “cannot both be right, but imputed to man they may both be true.”—Rasselas.\n\nThe same night, when Mr. Bulstrode returned from a journey to Brassing on business, his good wife met him in the entrance-hall and drew him into his private sitting-room.\n\n“Nicholas,” she said, fixing her honest eyes upon him anxiously, “there has been such a disagreeable man here asking for you—it has made me quite uncomfortable.”\n\n“What kind of man, my dear,” said Mr. Bulstrode, dreadfully certain of the answer.\n\n“A red-faced man with large whiskers, and most impudent in his manner. He declared he was an old friend of yours, and said you would be sorry not to see him. He wanted to wait for you here, but I told him he could see you at the Bank to-morrow morning. Most impudent he was!—stared at me, and said his friend Nick had luck in wives. I don’t believe he would have gone away, if Blucher had not happened to break his chain and come running round on the gravel—for I was in the garden; so I said, ‘You’d better go away—the dog is very fierce, and I can’t hold him.’ Do you really know anything of such a man?”\n\n“I believe I know who he is, my dear,” said Mr. Bulstrode, in his usual subdued voice, “an unfortunate dissolute wretch, whom I helped too much in days gone by. However, I presume you will not be troubled by him again. He will probably come to the Bank—to beg, doubtless.”\n\nNo more was said on the subject until the next day, when Mr. Bulstrode had returned from the town and was dressing for dinner. His wife, not sure that he was come home, looked into his dressing-room and saw him with his coat and cravat off, leaning one arm on a chest of drawers and staring absently at the ground. He started nervously and looked up as she entered.\n\n“You look very ill, Nicholas. Is there anything the matter?”\n\n“I have a good deal of pain in my head,” said Mr. Bulstrode, who was so frequently ailing that his wife was always ready to believe in this cause of depression.\n\n“Sit down and let me sponge it with vinegar.”\n\nPhysically Mr. Bulstrode did not want the vinegar, but morally the affectionate attention soothed him. Though always polite, it was his habit to receive such services with marital coolness, as his wife’s duty. But to-day, while she was bending over him, he said, “You are very good, Harriet,” in a tone which had something new in it to her ear; she did not know exactly what the novelty was, but her woman’s solicitude shaped itself into a darting thought that he might be going to have an illness.\n\n“Has anything worried you?” she said. “Did that man come to you at the Bank?”\n\n“Yes; it was as I had supposed. He is a man who at one time might have done better. But he has sunk into a drunken debauched creature.”\n\n“Is he quite gone away?” said Mrs. Bulstrode, anxiously; but for certain reasons she refrained from adding, “It was very disagreeable to hear him calling himself a friend of yours.” At that moment she would not have liked to say anything which implied her habitual consciousness that her husband’s earlier connections were not quite on a level with her own. Not that she knew much about them. That her husband had at first been employed in a bank, that he had afterwards entered into what he called city business and gained a fortune before he was three-and-thirty, that he had married a widow who was much older than himself—a Dissenter, and in other ways probably of that disadvantageous quality usually perceptible in a first wife if inquired into with the dispassionate judgment of a second—was almost as much as she had cared to learn beyond the glimpses which Mr. Bulstrode’s narrative occasionally gave of his early bent towards religion, his inclination to be a preacher, and his association with missionary and philanthropic efforts. She believed in him as an excellent man whose piety carried a peculiar eminence in belonging to a layman, whose influence had turned her own mind toward seriousness, and whose share of perishable good had been the means of raising her own position. But she also liked to think that it was well in every sense for Mr. Bulstrode to have won the hand of Harriet Vincy; whose family was undeniable in a Middlemarch light—a better light surely than any thrown in London thoroughfares or dissenting chapel-yards. The unreformed provincial mind distrusted London; and while true religion was everywhere saving, honest Mrs. Bulstrode was convinced that to be saved in the Church was more respectable. She so much wished to ignore towards others that her husband had ever been a London Dissenter, that she liked to keep it out of sight even in talking to him. He was quite aware of this; indeed in some respects he was rather afraid of this ingenuous wife, whose imitative piety and native worldliness were equally sincere, who had nothing to be ashamed of, and whom he had married out of a thorough inclination still subsisting. But his fears were such as belong to a man who cares to maintain his recognized supremacy: the loss of high consideration from his wife, as from every one else who did not clearly hate him out of enmity to the truth, would be as the beginning of death to him. When she said—\n\n“Is he quite gone away?”\n\n“Oh, I trust so,” he answered, with an effort to throw as much sober unconcern into his tone as possible!\n\nBut in truth Mr. Bulstrode was very far from a state of quiet trust. In the interview at the Bank, Raffles had made it evident that his eagerness to torment was almost as strong in him as any other greed. He had frankly said that he had turned out of the way to come to Middlemarch, just to look about him and see whether the neighborhood would suit him to live in. He had certainly had a few debts to pay more than he expected, but the two hundred pounds were not gone yet: a cool five-and-twenty would suffice him to go away with for the present. What he had wanted chiefly was to see his friend Nick and family, and know all about the prosperity of a man to whom he was so much attached. By-and-by he might come back for a longer stay. This time Raffles declined to be “seen off the premises,” as he expressed it—declined to quit Middlemarch under Bulstrode’s eyes. He meant to go by coach the next day—if he chose.\n\nBulstrode felt himself helpless. Neither threats nor coaxing could avail: he could not count on any persistent fear nor on any promise. On the contrary, he felt a cold certainty at his heart that Raffles—unless providence sent death to hinder him—would come back to Middlemarch before long. And that certainty was a terror.\n\nIt was not that he was in danger of legal punishment or of beggary: he was in danger only of seeing disclosed to the judgment of his neighbors and the mournful perception of his wife certain facts of his past life which would render him an object of scorn and an opprobrium of the religion with which he had diligently associated himself. The terror of being judged sharpens the memory: it sends an inevitable glare over that long-unvisited past which has been habitually recalled only in general phrases. Even without memory, the life is bound into one by a zone of dependence in growth and decay; but intense memory forces a man to own his blameworthy past. With memory set smarting like a reopened wound, a man’s past is not simply a dead history, an outworn preparation of the present: it is not a repented error shaken loose from the life: it is a still quivering part of himself, bringing shudders and bitter flavors and the tinglings of a merited shame.\n\nInto this second life Bulstrode’s past had now risen, only the pleasures of it seeming to have lost their quality. Night and day, without interruption save of brief sleep which only wove retrospect and fear into a fantastic present, he felt the scenes of his earlier life coming between him and everything else, as obstinately as when we look through the window from a lighted room, the objects we turn our backs on are still before us, instead of the grass and the trees. The successive events inward and outward were there in one view: though each might be dwelt on in turn, the rest still kept their hold in the consciousness.\n\nOnce more he saw himself the young banker’s clerk, with an agreeable person, as clever in figures as he was fluent in speech and fond of theological definition: an eminent though young member of a Calvinistic dissenting church at Highbury, having had striking experience in conviction of sin and sense of pardon. Again he heard himself called for as Brother Bulstrode in prayer meetings, speaking on religious platforms, preaching in private houses. Again he felt himself thinking of the ministry as possibly his vocation, and inclined towards missionary labor. That was the happiest time of his life: that was the spot he would have chosen now to awake in and find the rest a dream. The people among whom Brother Bulstrode was distinguished were very few, but they were very near to him, and stirred his satisfaction the more; his power stretched through a narrow space, but he felt its effect the more intensely. He believed without effort in the peculiar work of grace within him, and in the signs that God intended him for special instrumentality.\n\nThen came the moment of transition; it was with the sense of promotion he had when he, an orphan educated at a commercial charity-school, was invited to a fine villa belonging to Mr. Dunkirk, the richest man in the congregation. Soon he became an intimate there, honored for his piety by the wife, marked out for his ability by the husband, whose wealth was due to a flourishing city and west-end trade. That was the setting-in of a new current for his ambition, directing his prospects of “instrumentality” towards the uniting of distinguished religious gifts with successful business.\n\nBy-and-by came a decided external leading: a confidential subordinate partner died, and nobody seemed to the principal so well fitted to fill the severely felt vacancy as his young friend Bulstrode, if he would become confidential accountant. The offer was accepted. The business was a pawnbroker’s, of the most magnificent sort both in extent and profits; and on a short acquaintance with it Bulstrode became aware that one source of magnificent profit was the easy reception of any goods offered, without strict inquiry as to where they came from. But there was a branch house at the west end, and no pettiness or dinginess to give suggestions of shame.\n\nHe remembered his first moments of shrinking. They were private, and were filled with arguments; some of these taking the form of prayer. The business was established and had old roots; is it not one thing to set up a new gin-palace and another to accept an investment in an old one? The profits made out of lost souls—where can the line be drawn at which they begin in human transactions? Was it not even God’s way of saving His chosen? “Thou knowest,”—the young Bulstrode had said then, as the older Bulstrode was saying now—“Thou knowest how loose my soul sits from these things—how I view them all as implements for tilling Thy garden rescued here and there from the wilderness.”\n\nMetaphors and precedents were not wanting; peculiar spiritual experiences were not wanting which at last made the retention of his position seem a service demanded of him: the vista of a fortune had already opened itself, and Bulstrode’s shrinking remained private. Mr. Dunkirk had never expected that there would be any shrinking at all: he had never conceived that trade had anything to do with the scheme of salvation. And it was true that Bulstrode found himself carrying on two distinct lives; his religious activity could not be incompatible with his business as soon as he had argued himself into not feeling it incompatible.\n\nMentally surrounded with that past again, Bulstrode had the same pleas—indeed, the years had been perpetually spinning them into intricate thickness, like masses of spider-web, padding the moral sensibility; nay, as age made egoism more eager but less enjoying, his soul had become more saturated with the belief that he did everything for God’s sake, being indifferent to it for his own. And yet—if he could be back in that far-off spot with his youthful poverty—why, then he would choose to be a missionary.\n\nBut the train of causes in which he had locked himself went on. There was trouble in the fine villa at Highbury. Years before, the only daughter had run away, defied her parents, and gone on the stage; and now the only boy died, and after a short time Mr. Dunkirk died also. The wife, a simple pious woman, left with all the wealth in and out of the magnificent trade, of which she never knew the precise nature, had come to believe in Bulstrode, and innocently adore him as women often adore their priest or “man-made” minister. It was natural that after a time marriage should have been thought of between them. But Mrs. Dunkirk had qualms and yearnings about her daughter, who had long been regarded as lost both to God and her parents. It was known that the daughter had married, but she was utterly gone out of sight. The mother, having lost her boy, imagined a grandson, and wished in a double sense to reclaim her daughter. If she were found, there would be a channel for property—perhaps a wide one—in the provision for several grandchildren. Efforts to find her must be made before Mrs. Dunkirk would marry again. Bulstrode concurred; but after advertisement as well as other modes of inquiry had been tried, the mother believed that her daughter was not to be found, and consented to marry without reservation of property.\n\nThe daughter had been found; but only one man besides Bulstrode knew it, and he was paid for keeping silence and carrying himself away.\n\nThat was the bare fact which Bulstrode was now forced to see in the rigid outline with which acts present themselves to onlookers. But for himself at that distant time, and even now in burning memory, the fact was broken into little sequences, each justified as it came by reasonings which seemed to prove it righteous. Bulstrode’s course up to that time had, he thought, been sanctioned by remarkable providences, appearing to point the way for him to be the agent in making the best use of a large property and withdrawing it from perversion. Death and other striking dispositions, such as feminine trustfulness, had come; and Bulstrode would have adopted Cromwell’s words—“Do you call these bare events? The Lord pity you!” The events were comparatively small, but the essential condition was there—namely, that they were in favor of his own ends. It was easy for him to settle what was due from him to others by inquiring what were God’s intentions with regard to himself. Could it be for God’s service that this fortune should in any considerable proportion go to a young woman and her husband who were given up to the lightest pursuits, and might scatter it abroad in triviality—people who seemed to lie outside the path of remarkable providences? Bulstrode had never said to himself beforehand, “The daughter shall not be found”—nevertheless when the moment came he kept her existence hidden; and when other moments followed, he soothed the mother with consolation in the probability that the unhappy young woman might be no more.\n\nThere were hours in which Bulstrode felt that his action was unrighteous; but how could he go back? He had mental exercises, called himself nought, laid hold on redemption, and went on in his course of instrumentality. And after five years Death again came to widen his path, by taking away his wife. He did gradually withdraw his capital, but he did not make the sacrifices requisite to put an end to the business, which was carried on for thirteen years afterwards before it finally collapsed. Meanwhile Nicholas Bulstrode had used his hundred thousand discreetly, and was become provincially, solidly important—a banker, a Churchman, a public benefactor; also a sleeping partner in trading concerns, in which his ability was directed to economy in the raw material, as in the case of the dyes which rotted Mr. Vincy’s silk. And now, when this respectability had lasted undisturbed for nearly thirty years—when all that preceded it had long lain benumbed in the consciousness—that past had risen and immersed his thought as if with the terrible irruption of a new sense overburthening the feeble being.\n\nMeanwhile, in his conversation with Raffles, he had learned something momentous, something which entered actively into the struggle of his longings and terrors. There, he thought, lay an opening towards spiritual, perhaps towards material rescue.\n\nThe spiritual kind of rescue was a genuine need with him. There may be coarse hypocrites, who consciously affect beliefs and emotions for the sake of gulling the world, but Bulstrode was not one of them. He was simply a man whose desires had been stronger than his theoretic beliefs, and who had gradually explained the gratification of his desires into satisfactory agreement with those beliefs. If this be hypocrisy, it is a process which shows itself occasionally in us all, to whatever confession we belong, and whether we believe in the future perfection of our race or in the nearest date fixed for the end of the world; whether we regard the earth as a putrefying nidus for a saved remnant, including ourselves, or have a passionate belief in the solidarity of mankind.\n\nThe service he could do to the cause of religion had been through life the ground he alleged to himself for his choice of action: it had been the motive which he had poured out in his prayers. Who would use money and position better than he meant to use them? Who could surpass him in self-abhorrence and exaltation of God’s cause? And to Mr. Bulstrode God’s cause was something distinct from his own rectitude of conduct: it enforced a discrimination of God’s enemies, who were to be used merely as instruments, and whom it would be as well if possible to keep out of money and consequent influence. Also, profitable investments in trades where the power of the prince of this world showed its most active devices, became sanctified by a right application of the profits in the hands of God’s servant.\n\nThis implicit reasoning is essentially no more peculiar to evangelical belief than the use of wide phrases for narrow motives is peculiar to Englishmen. There is no general doctrine which is not capable of eating out our morality if unchecked by the deep-seated habit of direct fellow-feeling with individual fellow-men.\n\nBut a man who believes in something else than his own greed, has necessarily a conscience or standard to which he more or less adapts himself. Bulstrode’s standard had been his serviceableness to God’s cause: “I am sinful and nought—a vessel to be consecrated by use—but use me!”—had been the mould into which he had constrained his immense need of being something important and predominating. And now had come a moment in which that mould seemed in danger of being broken and utterly cast away.\n\nWhat if the acts he had reconciled himself to because they made him a stronger instrument of the divine glory, were to become the pretext of the scoffer, and a darkening of that glory? If this were to be the ruling of Providence, he was cast out from the temple as one who had brought unclean offerings.\n\nHe had long poured out utterances of repentance. But today a repentance had come which was of a bitterer flavor, and a threatening Providence urged him to a kind of propitiation which was not simply a doctrinal transaction. The divine tribunal had changed its aspect for him; self-prostration was no longer enough, and he must bring restitution in his hand. It was really before his God that Bulstrode was about to attempt such restitution as seemed possible: a great dread had seized his susceptible frame, and the scorching approach of shame wrought in him a new spiritual need. Night and day, while the resurgent threatening past was making a conscience within him, he was thinking by what means he could recover peace and trust—by what sacrifice he could stay the rod. His belief in these moments of dread was, that if he spontaneously did something right, God would save him from the consequences of wrong-doing. For religion can only change when the emotions which fill it are changed; and the religion of personal fear remains nearly at the level of the savage.\n\nHe had seen Raffles actually going away on the Brassing coach, and this was a temporary relief; it removed the pressure of an immediate dread, but did not put an end to the spiritual conflict and the need to win protection. At last he came to a difficult resolve, and wrote a letter to Will Ladislaw, begging him to be at the Shrubs that evening for a private interview at nine o’clock. Will had felt no particular surprise at the request, and connected it with some new notions about the “Pioneer;” but when he was shown into Mr. Bulstrode’s private room, he was struck with the painfully worn look on the banker’s face, and was going to say, “Are you ill?” when, checking himself in that abruptness, he only inquired after Mrs. Bulstrode, and her satisfaction with the picture bought for her.\n\n“Thank you, she is quite satisfied; she has gone out with her daughters this evening. I begged you to come, Mr. Ladislaw, because I have a communication of a very private—indeed, I will say, of a sacredly confidential nature, which I desire to make to you. Nothing, I dare say, has been farther from your thoughts than that there had been important ties in the past which could connect your history with mine.”\n\nWill felt something like an electric shock. He was already in a state of keen sensitiveness and hardly allayed agitation on the subject of ties in the past, and his presentiments were not agreeable. It seemed like the fluctuations of a dream—as if the action begun by that loud bloated stranger were being carried on by this pale-eyed sickly looking piece of respectability, whose subdued tone and glib formality of speech were at this moment almost as repulsive to him as their remembered contrast. He answered, with a marked change of color—\n\n“No, indeed, nothing.”\n\n“You see before you, Mr. Ladislaw, a man who is deeply stricken. But for the urgency of conscience and the knowledge that I am before the bar of One who seeth not as man seeth, I should be under no compulsion to make the disclosure which has been my object in asking you to come here to-night. So far as human laws go, you have no claim on me whatever.”\n\nWill was even more uncomfortable than wondering. Mr. Bulstrode had paused, leaning his head on his hand, and looking at the floor. But he now fixed his examining glance on Will and said—\n\n“I am told that your mother’s name was Sarah Dunkirk, and that she ran away from her friends to go on the stage. Also, that your father was at one time much emaciated by illness. May I ask if you can confirm these statements?”\n\n“Yes, they are all true,” said Will, struck with the order in which an inquiry had come, that might have been expected to be preliminary to the banker’s previous hints. But Mr. Bulstrode had to-night followed the order of his emotions; he entertained no doubt that the opportunity for restitution had come, and he had an overpowering impulse towards the penitential expression by which he was deprecating chastisement.\n\n“Do you know any particulars of your mother’s family?” he continued.\n\n“No; she never liked to speak of them. She was a very generous, honorable woman,” said Will, almost angrily.\n\n“I do not wish to allege anything against her. Did she never mention her mother to you at all?”\n\n“I have heard her say that she thought her mother did not know the reason of her running away. She said ‘poor mother’ in a pitying tone.”\n\n“That mother became my wife,” said Bulstrode, and then paused a moment before he added, “you have a claim on me, Mr. Ladislaw: as I said before, not a legal claim, but one which my conscience recognizes. I was enriched by that marriage—a result which would probably not have taken place—certainly not to the same extent—if your grandmother could have discovered her daughter. That daughter, I gather, is no longer living!”\n\n“No,” said Will, feeling suspicion and repugnance rising so strongly within him, that without quite knowing what he did, he took his hat from the floor and stood up. The impulse within him was to reject the disclosed connection.\n\n“Pray be seated, Mr. Ladislaw,” said Bulstrode, anxiously. “Doubtless you are startled by the suddenness of this discovery. But I entreat your patience with one who is already bowed down by inward trial.”\n\nWill reseated himself, feeling some pity which was half contempt for this voluntary self-abasement of an elderly man.\n\n“It is my wish, Mr. Ladislaw, to make amends for the deprivation which befell your mother. I know that you are without fortune, and I wish to supply you adequately from a store which would have probably already been yours had your grandmother been certain of your mother’s existence and been able to find her.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode paused. He felt that he was performing a striking piece of scrupulosity in the judgment of his auditor, and a penitential act in the eyes of God. He had no clew to the state of Will Ladislaw’s mind, smarting as it was from the clear hints of Raffles, and with its natural quickness in construction stimulated by the expectation of discoveries which he would have been glad to conjure back into darkness. Will made no answer for several moments, till Mr. Bulstrode, who at the end of his speech had cast his eyes on the floor, now raised them with an examining glance, which Will met fully, saying—\n\n“I suppose you did know of my mother’s existence, and knew where she might have been found.”\n\nBulstrode shrank—there was a visible quivering in his face and hands. He was totally unprepared to have his advances met in this way, or to find himself urged into more revelation than he had beforehand set down as needful. But at that moment he dared not tell a lie, and he felt suddenly uncertain of his ground which he had trodden with some confidence before.\n\n“I will not deny that you conjecture rightly,” he answered, with a faltering in his tone. “And I wish to make atonement to you as the one still remaining who has suffered a loss through me. You enter, I trust, into my purpose, Mr. Ladislaw, which has a reference to higher than merely human claims, and as I have already said, is entirely independent of any legal compulsion. I am ready to narrow my own resources and the prospects of my family by binding myself to allow you five hundred pounds yearly during my life, and to leave you a proportional capital at my death—nay, to do still more, if more should be definitely necessary to any laudable project on your part.” Mr. Bulstrode had gone on to particulars in the expectation that these would work strongly on Ladislaw, and merge other feelings in grateful acceptance.\n\nBut Will was looking as stubborn as possible, with his lip pouting and his fingers in his side-pockets. He was not in the least touched, and said firmly,—\n\n“Before I make any reply to your proposition, Mr. Bulstrode, I must beg you to answer a question or two. Were you connected with the business by which that fortune you speak of was originally made?”\n\nMr. Bulstrode’s thought was, “Raffles has told him.” How could he refuse to answer when he had volunteered what drew forth the question? He answered, “Yes.”\n\n“And was that business—or was it not—a thoroughly dishonorable one—nay, one that, if its nature had been made public, might have ranked those concerned in it with thieves and convicts?”\n\nWill’s tone had a cutting bitterness: he was moved to put his question as nakedly as he could.\n\nBulstrode reddened with irrepressible anger. He had been prepared for a scene of self-abasement, but his intense pride and his habit of supremacy overpowered penitence, and even dread, when this young man, whom he had meant to benefit, turned on him with the air of a judge.\n\n“The business was established before I became connected with it, sir; nor is it for you to institute an inquiry of that kind,” he answered, not raising his voice, but speaking with quick defiantness.\n\n“Yes, it is,” said Will, starting up again with his hat in his hand. “It is eminently mine to ask such questions, when I have to decide whether I will have transactions with you and accept your money. My unblemished honor is important to me. It is important to me to have no stain on my birth and connections. And now I find there is a stain which I can’t help. My mother felt it, and tried to keep as clear of it as she could, and so will I. You shall keep your ill-gotten money. If I had any fortune of my own, I would willingly pay it to any one who could disprove what you have told me. What I have to thank you for is that you kept the money till now, when I can refuse it. It ought to lie with a man’s self that he is a gentleman. Good-night, sir.”\n\nBulstrode was going to speak, but Will, with determined quickness, was out of the room in an instant, and in another the hall-door had closed behind him. He was too strongly possessed with passionate rebellion against this inherited blot which had been thrust on his knowledge to reflect at present whether he had not been too hard on Bulstrode—too arrogantly merciless towards a man of sixty, who was making efforts at retrieval when time had rendered them vain.\n\nNo third person listening could have thoroughly understood the impetuosity of Will’s repulse or the bitterness of his words. No one but himself then knew how everything connected with the sentiment of his own dignity had an immediate bearing for him on his relation to Dorothea and to Mr. Casaubon’s treatment of him. And in the rush of impulses by which he flung back that offer of Bulstrode’s there was mingled the sense that it would have been impossible for him ever to tell Dorothea that he had accepted it.\n\nAs for Bulstrode—when Will was gone he suffered a violent reaction, and wept like a woman. It was the first time he had encountered an open expression of scorn from any man higher than Raffles; and with that scorn hurrying like venom through his system, there was no sensibility left to consolations. But the relief of weeping had to be checked. His wife and daughters soon came home from hearing the address of an Oriental missionary, and were full of regret that papa had not heard, in the first instance, the interesting things which they tried to repeat to him.\n\nPerhaps, through all other hidden thoughts, the one that breathed most comfort was, that Will Ladislaw at least was not likely to publish what had taken place that evening.\n\n# CHAPTER LXII.\n\nHe was a squyer of lowe degre, That loved the king’s daughter of Hungrie. —Old Romance.\n\nWill Ladislaw’s mind was now wholly bent on seeing Dorothea again, and forthwith quitting Middlemarch. The morning after his agitating scene with Bulstrode he wrote a brief letter to her, saying that various causes had detained him in the neighborhood longer than he had expected, and asking her permission to call again at Lowick at some hour which she would mention on the earliest possible day, he being anxious to depart, but unwilling to do so until she had granted him an interview. He left the letter at the office, ordering the messenger to carry it to Lowick Manor, and wait for an answer.\n\nLadislaw felt the awkwardness of asking for more last words. His former farewell had been made in the hearing of Sir James Chettam, and had been announced as final even to the butler. It is certainly trying to a man’s dignity to reappear when he is not expected to do so: a first farewell has pathos in it, but to come back for a second lends an opening to comedy, and it was possible even that there might be bitter sneers afloat about Will’s motives for lingering. Still it was on the whole more satisfactory to his feeling to take the directest means of seeing Dorothea, than to use any device which might give an air of chance to a meeting of which he wished her to understand that it was what he earnestly sought. When he had parted from her before, he had been in ignorance of facts which gave a new aspect to the relation between them, and made a more absolute severance than he had then believed in. He knew nothing of Dorothea’s private fortune, and being little used to reflect on such matters, took it for granted that according to Mr. Casaubon’s arrangement marriage to him, Will Ladislaw, would mean that she consented to be penniless. That was not what he could wish for even in his secret heart, or even if she had been ready to meet such hard contrast for his sake. And then, too, there was the fresh smart of that disclosure about his mother’s family, which if known would be an added reason why Dorothea’s friends should look down upon him as utterly below her. The secret hope that after some years he might come back with the sense that he had at least a personal value equal to her wealth, seemed now the dreamy continuation of a dream. This change would surely justify him in asking Dorothea to receive him once more.\n\nBut Dorothea on that morning was not at home to receive Will’s note. In consequence of a letter from her uncle announcing his intention to be at home in a week, she had driven first to Freshitt to carry the news, meaning to go on to the Grange to deliver some orders with which her uncle had intrusted her—thinking, as he said, “a little mental occupation of this sort good for a widow.”\n\nIf Will Ladislaw could have overheard some of the talk at Freshitt that morning, he would have felt all his suppositions confirmed as to the readiness of certain people to sneer at his lingering in the neighborhood. Sir James, indeed, though much relieved concerning Dorothea, had been on the watch to learn Ladislaw’s movements, and had an instructed informant in Mr. Standish, who was necessarily in his confidence on this matter. That Ladislaw had stayed in Middlemarch nearly two months after he had declared that he was going immediately, was a fact to embitter Sir James’s suspicions, or at least to justify his aversion to a “young fellow” whom he represented to himself as slight, volatile, and likely enough to show such recklessness as naturally went along with a position unriveted by family ties or a strict profession. But he had just heard something from Standish which, while it justified these surmises about Will, offered a means of nullifying all danger with regard to Dorothea.\n\nUnwonted circumstances may make us all rather unlike ourselves: there are conditions under which the most majestic person is obliged to sneeze, and our emotions are liable to be acted on in the same incongruous manner. Good Sir James was this morning so far unlike himself that he was irritably anxious to say something to Dorothea on a subject which he usually avoided as if it had been a matter of shame to them both. He could not use Celia as a medium, because he did not choose that she should know the kind of gossip he had in his mind; and before Dorothea happened to arrive he had been trying to imagine how, with his shyness and unready tongue, he could ever manage to introduce his communication. Her unexpected presence brought him to utter hopelessness in his own power of saying anything unpleasant; but desperation suggested a resource; he sent the groom on an unsaddled horse across the park with a pencilled note to Mrs. Cadwallader, who already knew the gossip, and would think it no compromise of herself to repeat it as often as required.\n\nDorothea was detained on the good pretext that Mr. Garth, whom she wanted to see, was expected at the hall within the hour, and she was still talking to Caleb on the gravel when Sir James, on the watch for the rector’s wife, saw her coming and met her with the needful hints.\n\n“Enough! I understand,”—said Mrs. Cadwallader. “You shall be innocent. I am such a blackamoor that I cannot smirch myself.”\n\n“I don’t mean that it’s of any consequence,” said Sir James, disliking that Mrs. Cadwallader should understand too much. “Only it is desirable that Dorothea should know there are reasons why she should not receive him again; and I really can’t say so to her. It will come lightly from you.”\n\nIt came very lightly indeed. When Dorothea quitted Caleb and turned to meet them, it appeared that Mrs. Cadwallader had stepped across the park by the merest chance in the world, just to chat with Celia in a matronly way about the baby. And so Mr. Brooke was coming back? Delightful!—coming back, it was to be hoped, quite cured of Parliamentary fever and pioneering. Apropos of the “Pioneer”—somebody had prophesied that it would soon be like a dying dolphin, and turn all colors for want of knowing how to help itself, because Mr. Brooke’s protege, the brilliant young Ladislaw, was gone or going. Had Sir James heard that?\n\nThe three were walking along the gravel slowly, and Sir James, turning aside to whip a shrub, said he had heard something of that sort.\n\n“All false!” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “He is not gone, or going, apparently; the ‘Pioneer’ keeps its color, and Mr. Orlando Ladislaw is making a sad dark-blue scandal by warbling continually with your Mr. Lydgate’s wife, who they tell me is as pretty as pretty can be. It seems nobody ever goes into the house without finding this young gentleman lying on the rug or warbling at the piano. But the people in manufacturing towns are always disreputable.”\n\n“You began by saying that one report was false, Mrs. Cadwallader, and I believe this is false too,” said Dorothea, with indignant energy; “at least, I feel sure it is a misrepresentation. I will not hear any evil spoken of Mr. Ladislaw; he has already suffered too much injustice.”\n\nDorothea when thoroughly moved cared little what any one thought of her feelings; and even if she had been able to reflect, she would have held it petty to keep silence at injurious words about Will from fear of being herself misunderstood. Her face was flushed and her lip trembled.\n\nSir James, glancing at her, repented of his stratagem; but Mrs. Cadwallader, equal to all occasions, spread the palms of her hands outward and said—“Heaven grant it, my dear!—I mean that all bad tales about anybody may be false. But it is a pity that young Lydgate should have married one of these Middlemarch girls. Considering he’s a son of somebody, he might have got a woman with good blood in her veins, and not too young, who would have put up with his profession. There’s Clara Harfager, for instance, whose friends don’t know what to do with her; and she has a portion. Then we might have had her among us. However!—it’s no use being wise for other people. Where is Celia? Pray let us go in.”\n\n“I am going on immediately to Tipton,” said Dorothea, rather haughtily. “Good-by.”\n\nSir James could say nothing as he accompanied her to the carriage. He was altogether discontented with the result of a contrivance which had cost him some secret humiliation beforehand.\n\nDorothea drove along between the berried hedgerows and the shorn corn-fields, not seeing or hearing anything around. The tears came and rolled down her cheeks, but she did not know it. The world, it seemed, was turning ugly and hateful, and there was no place for her trustfulness. “It is not true—it is not true!” was the voice within her that she listened to; but all the while a remembrance to which there had always clung a vague uneasiness would thrust itself on her attention—the remembrance of that day when she had found Will Ladislaw with Mrs. Lydgate, and had heard his voice accompanied by the piano.\n\n“He said he would never do anything that I disapproved—I wish I could have told him that I disapproved of that,” said poor Dorothea, inwardly, feeling a strange alternation between anger with Will and the passionate defence of him. “They all try to blacken him before me; but I will care for no pain, if he is not to blame. I always believed he was good.”—These were her last thoughts before she felt that the carriage was passing under the archway of the lodge-gate at the Grange, when she hurriedly pressed her handkerchief to her face and began to think of her errands. The coachman begged leave to take out the horses for half an hour as there was something wrong with a shoe; and Dorothea, having the sense that she was going to rest, took off her gloves and bonnet, while she was leaning against a statue in the entrance-hall, and talking to the housekeeper. At last she said—\n\n“I must stay here a little, Mrs. Kell. I will go into the library and write you some memoranda from my uncle’s letter, if you will open the shutters for me.”\n\n“The shutters are open, madam,” said Mrs. Kell, following Dorothea, who had walked along as she spoke. “Mr. Ladislaw is there, looking for something.”\n\n(Will had come to fetch a portfolio of his own sketches which he had missed in the act of packing his movables, and did not choose to leave behind.)\n\nDorothea’s heart seemed to turn over as if it had had a blow, but she was not perceptibly checked: in truth, the sense that Will was there was for the moment all-satisfying to her, like the sight of something precious that one has lost. When she reached the door she said to Mrs. Kell—\n\n“Go in first, and tell him that I am here.”\n\nWill had found his portfolio, and had laid it on the table at the far end of the room, to turn over the sketches and please himself by looking at the memorable piece of art which had a relation to nature too mysterious for Dorothea. He was smiling at it still, and shaking the sketches into order with the thought that he might find a letter from her awaiting him at Middlemarch, when Mrs. Kell close to his elbow said—\n\n“Mrs. Casaubon is coming in, sir.”\n\nWill turned round quickly, and the next moment Dorothea was entering. As Mrs. Kell closed the door behind her they met: each was looking at the other, and consciousness was overflowed by something that suppressed utterance. It was not confusion that kept them silent, for they both felt that parting was near, and there is no shamefacedness in a sad parting.\n\nShe moved automatically towards her uncle’s chair against the writing-table, and Will, after drawing it out a little for her, went a few paces off and stood opposite to her.\n\n“Pray sit down,” said Dorothea, crossing her hands on her lap; “I am very glad you were here.” Will thought that her face looked just as it did when she first shook hands with him in Rome; for her widow’s cap, fixed in her bonnet, had gone off with it, and he could see that she had lately been shedding tears. But the mixture of anger in her agitation had vanished at the sight of him; she had been used, when they were face to face, always to feel confidence and the happy freedom which comes with mutual understanding, and how could other people’s words hinder that effect on a sudden? Let the music which can take possession of our frame and fill the air with joy for us, sound once more—what does it signify that we heard it found fault with in its absence?\n\n“I have sent a letter to Lowick Manor to-day, asking leave to see you,” said Will, seating himself opposite to her. “I am going away immediately, and I could not go without speaking to you again.”\n\n“I thought we had parted when you came to Lowick many weeks ago—you thought you were going then,” said Dorothea, her voice trembling a little.\n\n“Yes; but I was in ignorance then of things which I know now—things which have altered my feelings about the future. When I saw you before, I was dreaming that I might come back some day. I don’t think I ever shall—now.” Will paused here.\n\n“You wished me to know the reasons?” said Dorothea, timidly.\n\n“Yes,” said Will, impetuously, shaking his head backward, and looking away from her with irritation in his face. “Of course I must wish it. I have been grossly insulted in your eyes and in the eyes of others. There has been a mean implication against my character. I wish you to know that under no circumstances would I have lowered myself by—under no circumstances would I have given men the chance of saying that I sought money under the pretext of seeking—something else. There was no need of other safeguard against me—the safeguard of wealth was enough.”\n\nWill rose from his chair with the last word and went—he hardly knew where; but it was to the projecting window nearest him, which had been open as now about the same season a year ago, when he and Dorothea had stood within it and talked together. Her whole heart was going out at this moment in sympathy with Will’s indignation: she only wanted to convince him that she had never done him injustice, and he seemed to have turned away from her as if she too had been part of the unfriendly world.\n\n“It would be very unkind of you to suppose that I ever attributed any meanness to you,” she began. Then in her ardent way, wanting to plead with him, she moved from her chair and went in front of him to her old place in the window, saying, “Do you suppose that I ever disbelieved in you?”\n\nWhen Will saw her there, he gave a start and moved backward out of the window, without meeting her glance. Dorothea was hurt by this movement following up the previous anger of his tone. She was ready to say that it was as hard on her as on him, and that she was helpless; but those strange particulars of their relation which neither of them could explicitly mention kept her always in dread of saying too much. At this moment she had no belief that Will would in any case have wanted to marry her, and she feared using words which might imply such a belief. She only said earnestly, recurring to his last word—\n\n“I am sure no safeguard was ever needed against you.”\n\nWill did not answer. In the stormy fluctuation of his feelings these words of hers seemed to him cruelly neutral, and he looked pale and miserable after his angry outburst. He went to the table and fastened up his portfolio, while Dorothea looked at him from the distance. They were wasting these last moments together in wretched silence. What could he say, since what had got obstinately uppermost in his mind was the passionate love for her which he forbade himself to utter? What could she say, since she might offer him no help—since she was forced to keep the money that ought to have been his?—since to-day he seemed not to respond as he used to do to her thorough trust and liking?\n\nBut Will at last turned away from his portfolio and approached the window again.\n\n“I must go,” he said, with that peculiar look of the eyes which sometimes accompanies bitter feeling, as if they had been tired and burned with gazing too close at a light.\n\n“What shall you do in life?” said Dorothea, timidly. “Have your intentions remained just the same as when we said good-by before?”\n\n“Yes,” said Will, in a tone that seemed to waive the subject as uninteresting. “I shall work away at the first thing that offers. I suppose one gets a habit of doing without happiness or hope.”\n\n“Oh, what sad words!” said Dorothea, with a dangerous tendency to sob. Then trying to smile, she added, “We used to agree that we were alike in speaking too strongly.”\n\n“I have not spoken too strongly now,” said Will, leaning back against the angle of the wall. “There are certain things which a man can only go through once in his life; and he must know some time or other that the best is over with him. This experience has happened to me while I am very young—that is all. What I care more for than I can ever care for anything else is absolutely forbidden to me—I don’t mean merely by being out of my reach, but forbidden me, even if it were within my reach, by my own pride and honor—by everything I respect myself for. Of course I shall go on living as a man might do who had seen heaven in a trance.”\n\nWill paused, imagining that it would be impossible for Dorothea to misunderstand this; indeed he felt that he was contradicting himself and offending against his self-approval in speaking to her so plainly; but still—it could not be fairly called wooing a woman to tell her that he would never woo her. It must be admitted to be a ghostly kind of wooing.\n\nBut Dorothea’s mind was rapidly going over the past with quite another vision than his. The thought that she herself might be what Will most cared for did throb through her an instant, but then came doubt: the memory of the little they had lived through together turned pale and shrank before the memory which suggested how much fuller might have been the intercourse between Will and some one else with whom he had had constant companionship. Everything he had said might refer to that other relation, and whatever had passed between him and herself was thoroughly explained by what she had always regarded as their simple friendship and the cruel obstruction thrust upon it by her husband’s injurious act. Dorothea stood silent, with her eyes cast down dreamily, while images crowded upon her which left the sickening certainty that Will was referring to Mrs. Lydgate. But why sickening? He wanted her to know that here too his conduct should be above suspicion.\n\nWill was not surprised at her silence. His mind also was tumultuously busy while he watched her, and he was feeling rather wildly that something must happen to hinder their parting—some miracle, clearly nothing in their own deliberate speech. Yet, after all, had she any love for him?—he could not pretend to himself that he would rather believe her to be without that pain. He could not deny that a secret longing for the assurance that she loved him was at the root of all his words.\n\nNeither of them knew how long they stood in that way. Dorothea was raising her eyes, and was about to speak, when the door opened and her footman came to say—\n\n“The horses are ready, madam, whenever you like to start.”\n\n“Presently,” said Dorothea. Then turning to Will, she said, “I have some memoranda to write for the housekeeper.”\n\n“I must go,” said Will, when the door had closed again—advancing towards her. “The day after to-morrow I shall leave Middlemarch.”\n\n“You have acted in every way rightly,” said Dorothea, in a low tone, feeling a pressure at her heart which made it difficult to speak.\n\nShe put out her hand, and Will took it for an instant without speaking, for her words had seemed to him cruelly cold and unlike herself. Their eyes met, but there was discontent in his, and in hers there was only sadness. He turned away and took his portfolio under his arm.\n\n“I have never done you injustice. Please remember me,” said Dorothea, repressing a rising sob.\n\n“Why should you say that?” said Will, with irritation. “As if I were not in danger of forgetting everything else.”\n\nHe had really a movement of anger against her at that moment, and it impelled him to go away without pause. It was all one flash to Dorothea—his last words—his distant bow to her as he reached the door—the sense that he was no longer there. She sank into the chair, and for a few moments sat like a statue, while images and emotions were hurrying upon her. Joy came first, in spite of the threatening train behind it—joy in the impression that it was really herself whom Will loved and was renouncing, that there was really no other love less permissible, more blameworthy, which honor was hurrying him away from. They were parted all the same, but—Dorothea drew a deep breath and felt her strength return—she could think of him unrestrainedly. At that moment the parting was easy to bear: the first sense of loving and being loved excluded sorrow. It was as if some hard icy pressure had melted, and her consciousness had room to expand: her past was come back to her with larger interpretation. The joy was not the less—perhaps it was the more complete just then—because of the irrevocable parting; for there was no reproach, no contemptuous wonder to imagine in any eye or from any lips. He had acted so as to defy reproach, and make wonder respectful.\n\nAny one watching her might have seen that there was a fortifying thought within her. Just as when inventive power is working with glad ease some small claim on the attention is fully met as if it were only a cranny opened to the sunlight, it was easy now for Dorothea to write her memoranda. She spoke her last words to the housekeeper in cheerful tones, and when she seated herself in the carriage her eyes were bright and her cheeks blooming under the dismal bonnet. She threw back the heavy “weepers,” and looked before her, wondering which road Will had taken. It was in her nature to be proud that he was blameless, and through all her feelings there ran this vein—“I was right to defend him.”\n\nThe coachman was used to drive his grays at a good pace, Mr. Casaubon being unenjoying and impatient in everything away from his desk, and wanting to get to the end of all journeys; and Dorothea was now bowled along quickly. Driving was pleasant, for rain in the night had laid the dust, and the blue sky looked far off, away from the region of the great clouds that sailed in masses. The earth looked like a happy place under the vast heavens, and Dorothea was wishing that she might overtake Will and see him once more.\n\nAfter a turn of the road, there he was with the portfolio under his arm; but the next moment she was passing him while he raised his hat, and she felt a pang at being seated there in a sort of exaltation, leaving him behind. She could not look back at him. It was as if a crowd of indifferent objects had thrust them asunder, and forced them along different paths, taking them farther and farther away from each other, and making it useless to look back. She could no more make any sign that would seem to say, “Need we part?” than she could stop the carriage to wait for him. Nay, what a world of reasons crowded upon her against any movement of her thought towards a future that might reverse the decision of this day!\n\n“I only wish I had known before—I wish he knew—then we could be quite happy in thinking of each other, though we are forever parted. And if I could but have given him the money, and made things easier for him!”—were the longings that came back the most persistently. And yet, so heavily did the world weigh on her in spite of her independent energy, that with this idea of Will as in need of such help and at a disadvantage with the world, there came always the vision of that unfittingness of any closer relation between them which lay in the opinion of every one connected with her. She felt to the full all the imperativeness of the motives which urged Will’s conduct. How could he dream of her defying the barrier that her husband had placed between them?—how could she ever say to herself that she would defy it?\n\nWill’s certainty as the carriage grew smaller in the distance, had much more bitterness in it. Very slight matters were enough to gall him in his sensitive mood, and the sight of Dorothea driving past him while he felt himself plodding along as a poor devil seeking a position in a world which in his present temper offered him little that he coveted, made his conduct seem a mere matter of necessity, and took away the sustainment of resolve. After all, he had no assurance that she loved him: could any man pretend that he was simply glad in such a case to have the suffering all on his own side?\n\nThat evening Will spent with the Lydgates; the next evening he was gone.\n\nThese little things are great to little man.—GOLDSMITH.\n\n“Have you seen much of your scientific phoenix, Lydgate, lately?” said Mr. Toller at one of his Christmas dinner-parties, speaking to Mr. Farebrother on his right hand.\n\n“Not much, I am sorry to say,” answered the Vicar, accustomed to parry Mr. Toller’s banter about his belief in the new medical light. “I am out of the way and he is too busy.”\n\n“Is he? I am glad to hear it,” said Dr. Minchin, with mingled suavity and surprise.\n\n“He gives a great deal of time to the New Hospital,” said Mr. Farebrother, who had his reasons for continuing the subject: “I hear of that from my neighbor, Mrs. Casaubon, who goes there often. She says Lydgate is indefatigable, and is making a fine thing of Bulstrode’s institution. He is preparing a new ward in case of the cholera coming to us.”\n\n“And preparing theories of treatment to try on the patients, I suppose,” said Mr. Toller.\n\n“Come, Toller, be candid,” said Mr. Farebrother. “You are too clever not to see the good of a bold fresh mind in medicine, as well as in everything else; and as to cholera, I fancy, none of you are very sure what you ought to do. If a man goes a little too far along a new road, it is usually himself that he harms more than any one else.”\n\n“I am sure you and Wrench ought to be obliged to him,” said Dr. Minchin, looking towards Toller, “for he has sent you the cream of Peacock’s patients.”\n\n“Lydgate has been living at a great rate for a young beginner,” said Mr. Harry Toller, the brewer. “I suppose his relations in the North back him up.”\n\n“I hope so,” said Mr. Chichely, “else he ought not to have married that nice girl we were all so fond of. Hang it, one has a grudge against a man who carries off the prettiest girl in the town.”\n\n“Ay, by God! and the best too,” said Mr. Standish.\n\n“My friend Vincy didn’t half like the marriage, I know that,” said Mr. Chichely. “He wouldn’t do much. How the relations on the other side may have come down I can’t say.” There was an emphatic kind of reticence in Mr. Chichely’s manner of speaking.\n\n“Oh, I shouldn’t think Lydgate ever looked to practice for a living,” said Mr. Toller, with a slight touch of sarcasm, and there the subject was dropped.\n\nThis was not the first time that Mr. Farebrother had heard hints of Lydgate’s expenses being obviously too great to be met by his practice, but he thought it not unlikely that there were resources or expectations which excused the large outlay at the time of Lydgate’s marriage, and which might hinder any bad consequences from the disappointment in his practice. One evening, when he took the pains to go to Middlemarch on purpose to have a chat with Lydgate as of old, he noticed in him an air of excited effort quite unlike his usual easy way of keeping silence or breaking it with abrupt energy whenever he had anything to say. Lydgate talked persistently when they were in his work-room, putting arguments for and against the probability of certain biological views; but he had none of those definite things to say or to show which give the waymarks of a patient uninterrupted pursuit, such as he used himself to insist on, saying that “there must be a systole and diastole in all inquiry,” and that “a man’s mind must be continually expanding and shrinking between the whole human horizon and the horizon of an object-glass.” That evening he seemed to be talking widely for the sake of resisting any personal bearing; and before long they went into the drawing room, where Lydgate, having asked Rosamond to give them music, sank back in his chair in silence, but with a strange light in his eyes. “He may have been taking an opiate,” was a thought that crossed Mr. Farebrother’s mind—“tic-douloureux perhaps—or medical worries.”\n\nIt did not occur to him that Lydgate’s marriage was not delightful: he believed, as the rest did, that Rosamond was an amiable, docile creature, though he had always thought her rather uninteresting—a little too much the pattern-card of the finishing-school; and his mother could not forgive Rosamond because she never seemed to see that Henrietta Noble was in the room. “However, Lydgate fell in love with her,” said the Vicar to himself, “and she must be to his taste.”\n\nMr. Farebrother was aware that Lydgate was a proud man, but having very little corresponding fibre in himself, and perhaps too little care about personal dignity, except the dignity of not being mean or foolish, he could hardly allow enough for the way in which Lydgate shrank, as from a burn, from the utterance of any word about his private affairs. And soon after that conversation at Mr. Toller’s, the Vicar learned something which made him watch the more eagerly for an opportunity of indirectly letting Lydgate know that if he wanted to open himself about any difficulty there was a friendly ear ready.\n\nThe opportunity came at Mr. Vincy’s, where, on New Year’s Day, there was a party, to which Mr. Farebrother was irresistibly invited, on the plea that he must not forsake his old friends on the first new year of his being a greater man, and Rector as well as Vicar. And this party was thoroughly friendly: all the ladies of the Farebrother family were present; the Vincy children all dined at the table, and Fred had persuaded his mother that if she did not invite Mary Garth, the Farebrothers would regard it as a slight to themselves, Mary being their particular friend. Mary came, and Fred was in high spirits, though his enjoyment was of a checkered kind—triumph that his mother should see Mary’s importance with the chief personages in the party being much streaked with jealousy when Mr. Farebrother sat down by her. Fred used to be much more easy about his own accomplishments in the days when he had not begun to dread being “bowled out by Farebrother,” and this terror was still before him. Mrs. Vincy, in her fullest matronly bloom, looked at Mary’s little figure, rough wavy hair, and visage quite without lilies and roses, and wondered; trying unsuccessfully to fancy herself caring about Mary’s appearance in wedding clothes, or feeling complacency in grandchildren who would “feature” the Garths. However, the party was a merry one, and Mary was particularly bright; being glad, for Fred’s sake, that his friends were getting kinder to her, and being also quite willing that they should see how much she was valued by others whom they must admit to be judges.\n\nMr. Farebrother noticed that Lydgate seemed bored, and that Mr. Vincy spoke as little as possible to his son-in-law. Rosamond was perfectly graceful and calm, and only a subtle observation such as the Vicar had not been roused to bestow on her would have perceived the total absence of that interest in her husband’s presence which a loving wife is sure to betray, even if etiquette keeps her aloof from him. When Lydgate was taking part in the conversation, she never looked towards him any more than if she had been a sculptured Psyche modelled to look another way: and when, after being called out for an hour or two, he re-entered the room, she seemed unconscious of the fact, which eighteen months before would have had the effect of a numeral before ciphers. In reality, however, she was intensely aware of Lydgate’s voice and movements; and her pretty good-tempered air of unconsciousness was a studied negation by which she satisfied her inward opposition to him without compromise of propriety. When the ladies were in the drawing-room after Lydgate had been called away from the dessert, Mrs. Farebrother, when Rosamond happened to be near her, said—“You have to give up a great deal of your husband’s society, Mrs. Lydgate.”\n\n“Yes, the life of a medical man is very arduous: especially when he is so devoted to his profession as Mr. Lydgate is,” said Rosamond, who was standing, and moved easily away at the end of this correct little speech.\n\n“It is dreadfully dull for her when there is no company,” said Mrs. Vincy, who was seated at the old lady’s side. “I am sure I thought so when Rosamond was ill, and I was staying with her. You know, Mrs. Farebrother, ours is a cheerful house. I am of a cheerful disposition myself, and Mr. Vincy always likes something to be going on. That is what Rosamond has been used to. Very different from a husband out at odd hours, and never knowing when he will come home, and of a close, proud disposition, I think”—indiscreet Mrs. Vincy did lower her tone slightly with this parenthesis. “But Rosamond always had an angel of a temper; her brothers used very often not to please her, but she was never the girl to show temper; from a baby she was always as good as good, and with a complexion beyond anything. But my children are all good-tempered, thank God.”\n\nThis was easily credible to any one looking at Mrs. Vincy as she threw back her broad cap-strings, and smiled towards her three little girls, aged from seven to eleven. But in that smiling glance she was obliged to include Mary Garth, whom the three girls had got into a corner to make her tell them stories. Mary was just finishing the delicious tale of Rumpelstiltskin, which she had well by heart, because Letty was never tired of communicating it to her ignorant elders from a favorite red volume. Louisa, Mrs. Vincy’s darling, now ran to her with wide-eyed serious excitement, crying, “Oh mamma, mamma, the little man stamped so hard on the floor he couldn’t get his leg out again!”\n\n“Bless you, my cherub!” said mamma; “you shall tell me all about it to-morrow. Go and listen!” and then, as her eyes followed Louisa back towards the attractive corner, she thought that if Fred wished her to invite Mary again she would make no objection, the children being so pleased with her.\n\nBut presently the corner became still more animated, for Mr. Farebrother came in, and seating himself behind Louisa, took her on his lap; whereupon the girls all insisted that he must hear Rumpelstiltskin, and Mary must tell it over again. He insisted too, and Mary, without fuss, began again in her neat fashion, with precisely the same words as before. Fred, who had also seated himself near, would have felt unmixed triumph in Mary’s effectiveness if Mr. Farebrother had not been looking at her with evident admiration, while he dramatized an intense interest in the tale to please the children.\n\n“You will never care any more about my one-eyed giant, Loo,” said Fred at the end.\n\n“Yes, I shall. Tell about him now,” said Louisa.\n\n“Oh, I dare say; I am quite cut out. Ask Mr. Farebrother.”\n\n“Yes,” added Mary; “ask Mr. Farebrother to tell you about the ants whose beautiful house was knocked down by a giant named Tom, and he thought they didn’t mind because he couldn’t hear them cry, or see them use their pocket-handkerchiefs.”\n\n“Please,” said Louisa, looking up at the Vicar.\n\n“No, no, I am a grave old parson. If I try to draw a story out of my bag a sermon comes instead. Shall I preach you a sermon?” said he, putting on his short-sighted glasses, and pursing up his lips.\n\n“Yes,” said Louisa, falteringly.\n\n“Let me see, then. Against cakes: how cakes are bad things, especially if they are sweet and have plums in them.”\n\nLouisa took the affair rather seriously, and got down from the Vicar’s knee to go to Fred.\n\n“Ah, I see it will not do to preach on New Year’s Day,” said Mr. Farebrother, rising and walking away. He had discovered of late that Fred had become jealous of him, and also that he himself was not losing his preference for Mary above all other women.\n\n“A delightful young person is Miss Garth,” said Mrs. Farebrother, who had been watching her son’s movements.\n\n“Yes,” said Mrs. Vincy, obliged to reply, as the old lady turned to her expectantly. “It is a pity she is not better-looking.”\n\n“I cannot say that,” said Mrs. Farebrother, decisively. “I like her countenance. We must not always ask for beauty, when a good God has seen fit to make an excellent young woman without it. I put good manners first, and Miss Garth will know how to conduct herself in any station.”\n\nThe old lady was a little sharp in her tone, having a prospective reference to Mary’s becoming her daughter-in-law; for there was this inconvenience in Mary’s position with regard to Fred, that it was not suitable to be made public, and hence the three ladies at Lowick Parsonage were still hoping that Camden would choose Miss Garth.\n\nNew visitors entered, and the drawing-room was given up to music and games, while whist-tables were prepared in the quiet room on the other side of the hall. Mr. Farebrother played a rubber to satisfy his mother, who regarded her occasional whist as a protest against scandal and novelty of opinion, in which light even a revoke had its dignity. But at the end he got Mr. Chichely to take his place, and left the room. As he crossed the hall, Lydgate had just come in and was taking off his great-coat.\n\n“You are the man I was going to look for,” said the Vicar; and instead of entering the drawing-room, they walked along the hall and stood against the fireplace, where the frosty air helped to make a glowing bank. “You see, I can leave the whist-table easily enough,” he went on, smiling at Lydgate, “now I don’t play for money. I owe that to you, Mrs. Casaubon says.”\n\n“How?” said Lydgate, coldly.\n\n“Ah, you didn’t mean me to know it; I call that ungenerous reticence. You should let a man have the pleasure of feeling that you have done him a good turn. I don’t enter into some people’s dislike of being under an obligation: upon my word, I prefer being under an obligation to everybody for behaving well to me.”\n\n“I can’t tell what you mean,” said Lydgate, “unless it is that I once spoke of you to Mrs. Casaubon. But I did not think that she would break her promise not to mention that I had done so,” said Lydgate, leaning his back against the corner of the mantel-piece, and showing no radiance in his face.\n\n“It was Brooke who let it out, only the other day. He paid me the compliment of saying that he was very glad I had the living though you had come across his tactics, and had praised me up as a Ken and a Tillotson, and that sort of thing, till Mrs. Casaubon would hear of no one else.”\n\n“Oh, Brooke is such a leaky-minded fool,” said Lydgate, contemptuously.\n\n“Well, I was glad of the leakiness then. I don’t see why you shouldn’t like me to know that you wished to do me a service, my dear fellow. And you certainly have done me one. It’s rather a strong check to one’s self-complacency to find how much of one’s right doing depends on not being in want of money. A man will not be tempted to say the Lord’s Prayer backward to please the devil, if he doesn’t want the devil’s services. I have no need to hang on the smiles of chance now.”\n\n“I don’t see that there’s any money-getting without chance,” said Lydgate; “if a man gets it in a profession, it’s pretty sure to come by chance.”\n\nMr. Farebrother thought he could account for this speech, in striking contrast with Lydgate’s former way of talking, as the perversity which will often spring from the moodiness of a man ill at ease in his affairs. He answered in a tone of good-humored admission—\n\n“Ah, there’s enormous patience wanted with the way of the world. But it is the easier for a man to wait patiently when he has friends who love him, and ask for nothing better than to help him through, so far as it lies in their power.”\n\n“Oh yes,” said Lydgate, in a careless tone, changing his attitude and looking at his watch. “People make much more of their difficulties than they need to do.”\n\nHe knew as distinctly as possible that this was an offer of help to himself from Mr. Farebrother, and he could not bear it. So strangely determined are we mortals, that, after having been long gratified with the sense that he had privately done the Vicar a service, the suggestion that the Vicar discerned his need of a service in return made him shrink into unconquerable reticence. Besides, behind all making of such offers what else must come?—that he should “mention his case,” imply that he wanted specific things. At that moment, suicide seemed easier.\n\nMr. Farebrother was too keen a man not to know the meaning of that reply, and there was a certain massiveness in Lydgate’s manner and tone, corresponding with his physique, which if he repelled your advances in the first instance seemed to put persuasive devices out of question.\n\n“What time are you?” said the Vicar, devouring his wounded feeling.\n\n“After eleven,” said Lydgate. And they went into the drawing-room.\n\n# CHAPTER LXIV.\n\n1st Gent. Where lies the power, there let the blame lie too.\n\n2d Gent. Nay, power is relative; you cannot fright The coming pest with border fortresses, Or catch your carp with subtle argument. All force is twain in one: cause is not cause Unless effect be there; and action’s self Must needs contain a passive. So command Exists but with obedience.\n\nEven if Lydgate had been inclined to be quite open about his affairs, he knew that it would have hardly been in Mr. Farebrother’s power to give him the help he immediately wanted. With the year’s bills coming in from his tradesmen, with Dover’s threatening hold on his furniture, and with nothing to depend on but slow dribbling payments from patients who must not be offended—for the handsome fees he had had from Freshitt Hall and Lowick Manor had been easily absorbed—nothing less than a thousand pounds would have freed him from actual embarrassment, and left a residue which, according to the favorite phrase of hopefulness in such circumstances, would have given him “time to look about him.”\n\nNaturally, the merry Christmas bringing the happy New Year, when fellow-citizens expect to be paid for the trouble and goods they have smilingly bestowed on their neighbors, had so tightened the pressure of sordid cares on Lydgate’s mind that it was hardly possible for him to think unbrokenly of any other subject, even the most habitual and soliciting. He was not an ill-tempered man; his intellectual activity, the ardent kindness of his heart, as well as his strong frame, would always, under tolerably easy conditions, have kept him above the petty uncontrolled susceptibilities which make bad temper. But he was now a prey to that worst irritation which arises not simply from annoyances, but from the second consciousness underlying those annoyances, of wasted energy and a degrading preoccupation, which was the reverse of all his former purposes. “This is what I am thinking of; and that is what I might have been thinking of,” was the bitter incessant murmur within him, making every difficulty a double goad to impatience.\n\nSome gentlemen have made an amazing figure in literature by general discontent with the universe as a trap of dulness into which their great souls have fallen by mistake; but the sense of a stupendous self and an insignificant world may have its consolations. Lydgate’s discontent was much harder to bear: it was the sense that there was a grand existence in thought and effective action lying around him, while his self was being narrowed into the miserable isolation of egoistic fears, and vulgar anxieties for events that might allay such fears. His troubles will perhaps appear miserably sordid, and beneath the attention of lofty persons who can know nothing of debt except on a magnificent scale. Doubtless they were sordid; and for the majority, who are not lofty, there is no escape from sordidness but by being free from money-craving, with all its base hopes and temptations, its watching for death, its hinted requests, its horse-dealer’s desire to make bad work pass for good, its seeking for function which ought to be another’s, its compulsion often to long for Luck in the shape of a wide calamity.\n\nIt was because Lydgate writhed under the idea of getting his neck beneath this vile yoke that he had fallen into a bitter moody state which was continually widening Rosamond’s alienation from him. After the first disclosure about the bill of sale, he had made many efforts to draw her into sympathy with him about possible measures for narrowing their expenses, and with the threatening approach of Christmas his propositions grew more and more definite. “We two can do with only one servant, and live on very little,” he said, “and I shall manage with one horse.” For Lydgate, as we have seen, had begun to reason, with a more distinct vision, about the expenses of living, and any share of pride he had given to appearances of that sort was meagre compared with the pride which made him revolt from exposure as a debtor, or from asking men to help him with their money.\n\n“Of course you can dismiss the other two servants, if you like,” said Rosamond; “but I should have thought it would be very injurious to your position for us to live in a poor way. You must expect your practice to be lowered.”\n\n“My dear Rosamond, it is not a question of choice. We have begun too expensively. Peacock, you know, lived in a much smaller house than this. It is my fault: I ought to have known better, and I deserve a thrashing—if there were anybody who had a right to give it me—for bringing you into the necessity of living in a poorer way than you have been used to. But we married because we loved each other, I suppose. And that may help us to pull along till things get better. Come, dear, put down that work and come to me.”\n\nHe was really in chill gloom about her at that moment, but he dreaded a future without affection, and was determined to resist the oncoming of division between them. Rosamond obeyed him, and he took her on his knee, but in her secret soul she was utterly aloof from him. The poor thing saw only that the world was not ordered to her liking, and Lydgate was part of that world. But he held her waist with one hand and laid the other gently on both of hers; for this rather abrupt man had much tenderness in his manners towards women, seeming to have always present in his imagination the weakness of their frames and the delicate poise of their health both in body and mind. And he began again to speak persuasively.\n\n“I find, now I look into things a little, Rosy, that it is wonderful what an amount of money slips away in our housekeeping. I suppose the servants are careless, and we have had a great many people coming. But there must be many in our rank who manage with much less: they must do with commoner things, I suppose, and look after the scraps. It seems, money goes but a little way in these matters, for Wrench has everything as plain as possible, and he has a very large practice.”\n\n“Oh, if you think of living as the Wrenches do!” said Rosamond, with a little turn of her neck. “But I have heard you express your disgust at that way of living.”\n\n“Yes, they have bad taste in everything—they make economy look ugly. We needn’t do that. I only meant that they avoid expenses, although Wrench has a capital practice.”\n\n“Why should not you have a good practice, Tertius? Mr. Peacock had. You should be more careful not to offend people, and you should send out medicines as the others do. I am sure you began well, and you got several good houses. It cannot answer to be eccentric; you should think what will be generally liked,” said Rosamond, in a decided little tone of admonition.\n\nLydgate’s anger rose: he was prepared to be indulgent towards feminine weakness, but not towards feminine dictation. The shallowness of a waternixie’s soul may have a charm until she becomes didactic. But he controlled himself, and only said, with a touch of despotic firmness—\n\n“What I am to do in my practice, Rosy, it is for me to judge. That is not the question between us. It is enough for you to know that our income is likely to be a very narrow one—hardly four hundred, perhaps less, for a long time to come, and we must try to re-arrange our lives in accordance with that fact.”\n\nRosamond was silent for a moment or two, looking before her, and then said, “My uncle Bulstrode ought to allow you a salary for the time you give to the Hospital: it is not right that you should work for nothing.”\n\n“It was understood from the beginning that my services would be gratuitous. That, again, need not enter into our discussion. I have pointed out what is the only probability,” said Lydgate, impatiently. Then checking himself, he went on more quietly—\n\n“I think I see one resource which would free us from a good deal of the present difficulty. I hear that young Ned Plymdale is going to be married to Miss Sophy Toller. They are rich, and it is not often that a good house is vacant in Middlemarch. I feel sure that they would be glad to take this house from us with most of our furniture, and they would be willing to pay handsomely for the lease. I can employ Trumbull to speak to Plymdale about it.”\n\nRosamond left her husband’s knee and walked slowly to the other end of the room; when she turned round and walked towards him it was evident that the tears had come, and that she was biting her under-lip and clasping her hands to keep herself from crying. Lydgate was wretched—shaken with anger and yet feeling that it would be unmanly to vent the anger just now.\n\n“I am very sorry, Rosamond; I know this is painful.”\n\n“I thought, at least, when I had borne to send the plate back and have that man taking an inventory of the furniture—I should have thought that would suffice.”\n\n“I explained it to you at the time, dear. That was only a security and behind that security there is a debt. And that debt must be paid within the next few months, else we shall have our furniture sold. If young Plymdale will take our house and most of our furniture, we shall be able to pay that debt, and some others too, and we shall be quit of a place too expensive for us. We might take a smaller house: Trumbull, I know, has a very decent one to let at thirty pounds a-year, and this is ninety.” Lydgate uttered this speech in the curt hammering way with which we usually try to nail down a vague mind to imperative facts. Tears rolled silently down Rosamond’s cheeks; she just pressed her handkerchief against them, and stood looking at the large vase on the mantel-piece. It was a moment of more intense bitterness than she had ever felt before. At last she said, without hurry and with careful emphasis—\n\n“I never could have believed that you would like to act in that way.”\n\n“Like it?” burst out Lydgate, rising from his chair, thrusting his hands in his pockets and stalking away from the hearth; “it’s not a question of liking. Of course, I don’t like it; it’s the only thing I can do.” He wheeled round there, and turned towards her.\n\n“I should have thought there were many other means than that,” said Rosamond. “Let us have a sale and leave Middlemarch altogether.”\n\n“To do what? What is the use of my leaving my work in Middlemarch to go where I have none? We should be just as penniless elsewhere as we are here,” said Lydgate still more angrily.\n\n“If we are to be in that position it will be entirely your own doing, Tertius,” said Rosamond, turning round to speak with the fullest conviction. “You will not behave as you ought to do to your own family. You offended Captain Lydgate. Sir Godwin was very kind to me when we were at Quallingham, and I am sure if you showed proper regard to him and told him your affairs, he would do anything for you. But rather than that, you like giving up our house and furniture to Mr. Ned Plymdale.”\n\nThere was something like fierceness in Lydgate’s eyes, as he answered with new violence, “Well, then, if you will have it so, I do like it. I admit that I like it better than making a fool of myself by going to beg where it’s of no use. Understand then, that it is what I like to do.”\n\nThere was a tone in the last sentence which was equivalent to the clutch of his strong hand on Rosamond’s delicate arm. But for all that, his will was not a whit stronger than hers. She immediately walked out of the room in silence, but with an intense determination to hinder what Lydgate liked to do.\n\nHe went out of the house, but as his blood cooled he felt that the chief result of the discussion was a deposit of dread within him at the idea of opening with his wife in future subjects which might again urge him to violent speech. It was as if a fracture in delicate crystal had begun, and he was afraid of any movement that might make it fatal. His marriage would be a mere piece of bitter irony if they could not go on loving each other. He had long ago made up his mind to what he thought was her negative character—her want of sensibility, which showed itself in disregard both of his specific wishes and of his general aims. The first great disappointment had been borne: the tender devotedness and docile adoration of the ideal wife must be renounced, and life must be taken up on a lower stage of expectation, as it is by men who have lost their limbs. But the real wife had not only her claims, she had still a hold on his heart, and it was his intense desire that the hold should remain strong. In marriage, the certainty, “She will never love me much,” is easier to bear than the fear, “I shall love her no more.” Hence, after that outburst, his inward effort was entirely to excuse her, and to blame the hard circumstances which were partly his fault. He tried that evening, by petting her, to heal the wound he had made in the morning, and it was not in Rosamond’s nature to be repellent or sulky; indeed, she welcomed the signs that her husband loved her and was under control. But this was something quite distinct from loving him. Lydgate would not have chosen soon to recur to the plan of parting with the house; he was resolved to carry it out, and say as little more about it as possible. But Rosamond herself touched on it at breakfast by saying, mildly—\n\n“Have you spoken to Trumbull yet?”\n\n“No,” said Lydgate, “but I shall call on him as I go by this morning. No time must be lost.” He took Rosamond’s question as a sign that she withdrew her inward opposition, and kissed her head caressingly when he got up to go away.\n\nAs soon as it was late enough to make a call, Rosamond went to Mrs. Plymdale, Mr. Ned’s mother, and entered with pretty congratulations into the subject of the coming marriage. Mrs. Plymdale’s maternal view was, that Rosamond might possibly now have retrospective glimpses of her own folly; and feeling the advantages to be at present all on the side of her son, was too kind a woman not to behave graciously.\n\n“Yes, Ned is most happy, I must say. And Sophy Toller is all I could desire in a daughter-in-law. Of course her father is able to do something handsome for her—that is only what would be expected with a brewery like his. And the connection is everything we should desire. But that is not what I look at. She is such a very nice girl—no airs, no pretensions, though on a level with the first. I don’t mean with the titled aristocracy. I see very little good in people aiming out of their own sphere. I mean that Sophy is equal to the best in the town, and she is contented with that.”\n\n“I have always thought her very agreeable,” said Rosamond.\n\n“I look upon it as a reward for Ned, who never held his head too high, that he should have got into the very best connection,” continued Mrs. Plymdale, her native sharpness softened by a fervid sense that she was taking a correct view. “And such particular people as the Tollers are, they might have objected because some of our friends are not theirs. It is well known that your aunt Bulstrode and I have been intimate from our youth, and Mr. Plymdale has been always on Mr. Bulstrode’s side. And I myself prefer serious opinions. But the Tollers have welcomed Ned all the same.”\n\n“I am sure he is a very deserving, well-principled young man,” said Rosamond, with a neat air of patronage in return for Mrs. Plymdale’s wholesome corrections.\n\n“Oh, he has not the style of a captain in the army, or that sort of carriage as if everybody was beneath him, or that showy kind of talking, and singing, and intellectual talent. But I am thankful he has not. It is a poor preparation both for here and Hereafter.”\n\n“Oh dear, yes; appearances have very little to do with happiness,” said Rosamond. “I think there is every prospect of their being a happy couple. What house will they take?”\n\n“Oh, as for that, they must put up with what they can get. They have been looking at the house in St. Peter’s Place, next to Mr. Hackbutt’s; it belongs to him, and he is putting it nicely in repair. I suppose they are not likely to hear of a better. Indeed, I think Ned will decide the matter to-day.”\n\n“I should think it is a nice house; I like St. Peter’s Place.”\n\n“Well, it is near the Church, and a genteel situation. But the windows are narrow, and it is all ups and downs. You don’t happen to know of any other that would be at liberty?” said Mrs. Plymdale, fixing her round black eyes on Rosamond with the animation of a sudden thought in them.\n\n“Oh no; I hear so little of those things.”\n\nRosamond had not foreseen that question and answer in setting out to pay her visit; she had simply meant to gather any information which would help her to avert the parting with her own house under circumstances thoroughly disagreeable to her. As to the untruth in her reply, she no more reflected on it than she did on the untruth there was in her saying that appearances had very little to do with happiness. Her object, she was convinced, was thoroughly justifiable: it was Lydgate whose intention was inexcusable; and there was a plan in her mind which, when she had carried it out fully, would prove how very false a step it would have been for him to have descended from his position.\n\nShe returned home by Mr. Borthrop Trumbull’s office, meaning to call there. It was the first time in her life that Rosamond had thought of doing anything in the form of business, but she felt equal to the occasion. That she should be obliged to do what she intensely disliked, was an idea which turned her quiet tenacity into active invention. Here was a case in which it could not be enough simply to disobey and be serenely, placidly obstinate: she must act according to her judgment, and she said to herself that her judgment was right—“indeed, if it had not been, she would not have wished to act on it.”\n\nMr. Trumbull was in the back-room of his office, and received Rosamond with his finest manners, not only because he had much sensibility to her charms, but because the good-natured fibre in him was stirred by his certainty that Lydgate was in difficulties, and that this uncommonly pretty woman—this young lady with the highest personal attractions—was likely to feel the pinch of trouble—to find herself involved in circumstances beyond her control. He begged her to do him the honor to take a seat, and stood before her trimming and comporting himself with an eager solicitude, which was chiefly benevolent. Rosamond’s first question was, whether her husband had called on Mr. Trumbull that morning, to speak about disposing of their house.\n\n“Yes, ma’am, yes, he did; he did so,” said the good auctioneer, trying to throw something soothing into his iteration. “I was about to fulfil his order, if possible, this afternoon. He wished me not to procrastinate.”\n\n“I called to tell you not to go any further, Mr. Trumbull; and I beg of you not to mention what has been said on the subject. Will you oblige me?”\n\n“Certainly I will, Mrs. Lydgate, certainly. Confidence is sacred with me on business or any other topic. I am then to consider the commission withdrawn?” said Mr. Trumbull, adjusting the long ends of his blue cravat with both hands, and looking at Rosamond deferentially.\n\n“Yes, if you please. I find that Mr. Ned Plymdale has taken a house—the one in St. Peter’s Place next to Mr. Hackbutt’s. Mr. Lydgate would be annoyed that his orders should be fulfilled uselessly. And besides that, there are other circumstances which render the proposal unnecessary.”\n\n“Very good, Mrs. Lydgate, very good. I am at your commands, whenever you require any service of me,” said Mr. Trumbull, who felt pleasure in conjecturing that some new resources had been opened. “Rely on me, I beg. The affair shall go no further.”\n\nThat evening Lydgate was a little comforted by observing that Rosamond was more lively than she had usually been of late, and even seemed interested in doing what would please him without being asked. He thought, “If she will be happy and I can rub through, what does it all signify? It is only a narrow swamp that we have to pass in a long journey. If I can get my mind clear again, I shall do.”\n\nHe was so much cheered that he began to search for an account of experiments which he had long ago meant to look up, and had neglected out of that creeping self-despair which comes in the train of petty anxieties. He felt again some of the old delightful absorption in a far-reaching inquiry, while Rosamond played the quiet music which was as helpful to his meditation as the plash of an oar on the evening lake. It was rather late; he had pushed away all the books, and was looking at the fire with his hands clasped behind his head in forgetfulness of everything except the construction of a new controlling experiment, when Rosamond, who had left the piano and was leaning back in her chair watching him, said—\n\n“Mr. Ned Plymdale has taken a house already.”\n\nLydgate, startled and jarred, looked up in silence for a moment, like a man who has been disturbed in his sleep. Then flushing with an unpleasant consciousness, he asked—\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“I called at Mrs. Plymdale’s this morning, and she told me that he had taken the house in St. Peter’s Place, next to Mr. Hackbutt’s.”\n\nLydgate was silent. He drew his hands from behind his head and pressed them against the hair which was hanging, as it was apt to do, in a mass on his forehead, while he rested his elbows on his knees. He was feeling bitter disappointment, as if he had opened a door out of a suffocating place and had found it walled up; but he also felt sure that Rosamond was pleased with the cause of his disappointment. He preferred not looking at her and not speaking, until he had got over the first spasm of vexation. After all, he said in his bitterness, what can a woman care about so much as house and furniture? a husband without them is an absurdity. When he looked up and pushed his hair aside, his dark eyes had a miserable blank non-expectance of sympathy in them, but he only said, coolly—\n\n“Perhaps some one else may turn up. I told Trumbull to be on the look-out if he failed with Plymdale.”\n\nRosamond made no remark. She trusted to the chance that nothing more would pass between her husband and the auctioneer until some issue should have justified her interference; at any rate, she had hindered the event which she immediately dreaded. After a pause, she said—\n\n“How much money is it that those disagreeable people want?”\n\n“What disagreeable people?”\n\n“Those who took the list—and the others. I mean, how much money would satisfy them so that you need not be troubled any more?”\n\nLydgate surveyed her for a moment, as if he were looking for symptoms, and then said, “Oh, if I could have got six hundred from Plymdale for furniture and as premium, I might have managed. I could have paid off Dover, and given enough on account to the others to make them wait patiently, if we contracted our expenses.”\n\n“But I mean how much should you want if we stayed in this house?”\n\n“More than I am likely to get anywhere,” said Lydgate, with rather a grating sarcasm in his tone. It angered him to perceive that Rosamond’s mind was wandering over impracticable wishes instead of facing possible efforts.\n\n“Why should you not mention the sum?” said Rosamond, with a mild indication that she did not like his manners.\n\n“Well,” said Lydgate in a guessing tone, “it would take at least a thousand to set me at ease. But,” he added, incisively, “I have to consider what I shall do without it, not with it.”\n\nRosamond said no more.\n\nBut the next day she carried out her plan of writing to Sir Godwin Lydgate. Since the Captain’s visit, she had received a letter from him, and also one from Mrs. Mengan, his married sister, condoling with her on the loss of her baby, and expressing vaguely the hope that they should see her again at Quallingham. Lydgate had told her that this politeness meant nothing; but she was secretly convinced that any backwardness in Lydgate’s family towards him was due to his cold and contemptuous behavior, and she had answered the letters in her most charming manner, feeling some confidence that a specific invitation would follow. But there had been total silence. The Captain evidently was not a great penman, and Rosamond reflected that the sisters might have been abroad. However, the season was come for thinking of friends at home, and at any rate Sir Godwin, who had chucked her under the chin, and pronounced her to be like the celebrated beauty, Mrs. Croly, who had made a conquest of him in 1790, would be touched by any appeal from her, and would find it pleasant for her sake to behave as he ought to do towards his nephew. Rosamond was naively convinced of what an old gentleman ought to do to prevent her from suffering annoyance. And she wrote what she considered the most judicious letter possible—one which would strike Sir Godwin as a proof of her excellent sense—pointing out how desirable it was that Tertius should quit such a place as Middlemarch for one more fitted to his talents, how the unpleasant character of the inhabitants had hindered his professional success, and how in consequence he was in money difficulties, from which it would require a thousand pounds thoroughly to extricate him. She did not say that Tertius was unaware of her intention to write; for she had the idea that his supposed sanction of her letter would be in accordance with what she did say of his great regard for his uncle Godwin as the relative who had always been his best friend. Such was the force of Poor Rosamond’s tactics now she applied them to affairs.\n\nThis had happened before the party on New Year’s Day, and no answer had yet come from Sir Godwin. But on the morning of that day Lydgate had to learn that Rosamond had revoked his order to Borthrop Trumbull. Feeling it necessary that she should be gradually accustomed to the idea of their quitting the house in Lowick Gate, he overcame his reluctance to speak to her again on the subject, and when they were breakfasting said—\n\n“I shall try to see Trumbull this morning, and tell him to advertise the house in the ‘Pioneer’ and the ‘Trumpet.’ If the thing were advertised, some one might be inclined to take it who would not otherwise have thought of a change. In these country places many people go on in their old houses when their families are too large for them, for want of knowing where they can find another. And Trumbull seems to have got no bite at all.”\n\nRosamond knew that the inevitable moment was come. “I ordered Trumbull not to inquire further,” she said, with a careful calmness which was evidently defensive.\n\nLydgate stared at her in mute amazement. Only half an hour before he had been fastening up her plaits for her, and talking the “little language” of affection, which Rosamond, though not returning it, accepted as if she had been a serene and lovely image, now and then miraculously dimpling towards her votary. With such fibres still astir in him, the shock he received could not at once be distinctly anger; it was confused pain. He laid down the knife and fork with which he was carving, and throwing himself back in his chair, said at last, with a cool irony in his tone—\n\n“May I ask when and why you did so?”\n\n“When I knew that the Plymdales had taken a house, I called to tell him not to mention ours to them; and at the same time I told him not to let the affair go on any further. I knew that it would be very injurious to you if it were known that you wished to part with your house and furniture, and I had a very strong objection to it. I think that was reason enough.”\n\n“It was of no consequence then that I had told you imperative reasons of another kind; of no consequence that I had come to a different conclusion, and given an order accordingly?” said Lydgate, bitingly, the thunder and lightning gathering about his brow and eyes.\n\nThe effect of any one’s anger on Rosamond had always been to make her shrink in cold dislike, and to become all the more calmly correct, in the conviction that she was not the person to misbehave whatever others might do. She replied—\n\n“I think I had a perfect right to speak on a subject which concerns me at least as much as you.”\n\n“Clearly—you had a right to speak, but only to me. You had no right to contradict my orders secretly, and treat me as if I were a fool,” said Lydgate, in the same tone as before. Then with some added scorn, “Is it possible to make you understand what the consequences will be? Is it of any use for me to tell you again why we must try to part with the house?”\n\n“It is not necessary for you to tell me again,” said Rosamond, in a voice that fell and trickled like cold water-drops. “I remembered what you said. You spoke just as violently as you do now. But that does not alter my opinion that you ought to try every other means rather than take a step which is so painful to me. And as to advertising the house, I think it would be perfectly degrading to you.”\n\n“And suppose I disregard your opinion as you disregard mine?”\n\n“You can do so, of course. But I think you ought to have told me before we were married that you would place me in the worst position, rather than give up your own will.”\n\nLydgate did not speak, but tossed his head on one side, and twitched the corners of his mouth in despair. Rosamond, seeing that he was not looking at her, rose and set his cup of coffee before him; but he took no notice of it, and went on with an inward drama and argument, occasionally moving in his seat, resting one arm on the table, and rubbing his hand against his hair. There was a conflux of emotions and thoughts in him that would not let him either give thorough way to his anger or persevere with simple rigidity of resolve. Rosamond took advantage of his silence.\n\n“When we were married everyone felt that your position was very high. I could not have imagined then that you would want to sell our furniture, and take a house in Bride Street, where the rooms are like cages. If we are to live in that way let us at least leave Middlemarch.”\n\n“These would be very strong considerations,” said Lydgate, half ironically—still there was a withered paleness about his lips as he looked at his coffee, and did not drink—“these would be very strong considerations if I did not happen to be in debt.”\n\n“Many persons must have been in debt in the same way, but if they are respectable, people trust them. I am sure I have heard papa say that the Torbits were in debt, and they went on very well. It cannot be good to act rashly,” said Rosamond, with serene wisdom.\n\nLydgate sat paralyzed by opposing impulses: since no reasoning he could apply to Rosamond seemed likely to conquer her assent, he wanted to smash and grind some object on which he could at least produce an impression, or else to tell her brutally that he was master, and she must obey. But he not only dreaded the effect of such extremities on their mutual life—he had a growing dread of Rosamond’s quiet elusive obstinacy, which would not allow any assertion of power to be final; and again, she had touched him in a spot of keenest feeling by implying that she had been deluded with a false vision of happiness in marrying him. As to saying that he was master, it was not the fact. The very resolution to which he had wrought himself by dint of logic and honorable pride was beginning to relax under her torpedo contact. He swallowed half his cup of coffee, and then rose to go.\n\n“I may at least request that you will not go to Trumbull at present—until it has been seen that there are no other means,” said Rosamond. Although she was not subject to much fear, she felt it safer not to betray that she had written to Sir Godwin. “Promise me that you will not go to him for a few weeks, or without telling me.”\n\nLydgate gave a short laugh. “I think it is I who should exact a promise that you will do nothing without telling me,” he said, turning his eyes sharply upon her, and then moving to the door.\n\n“You remember that we are going to dine at papa’s,” said Rosamond, wishing that he should turn and make a more thorough concession to her. But he only said “Oh yes,” impatiently, and went away. She held it to be very odious in him that he did not think the painful propositions he had had to make to her were enough, without showing so unpleasant a temper. And when she put the moderate request that he would defer going to Trumbull again, it was cruel in him not to assure her of what he meant to do. She was convinced of her having acted in every way for the best; and each grating or angry speech of Lydgate’s served only as an addition to the register of offences in her mind. Poor Rosamond for months had begun to associate her husband with feelings of disappointment, and the terribly inflexible relation of marriage had lost its charm of encouraging delightful dreams. It had freed her from the disagreeables of her father’s house, but it had not given her everything that she had wished and hoped. The Lydgate with whom she had been in love had been a group of airy conditions for her, most of which had disappeared, while their place had been taken by every-day details which must be lived through slowly from hour to hour, not floated through with a rapid selection of favorable aspects. The habits of Lydgate’s profession, his home preoccupation with scientific subjects, which seemed to her almost like a morbid vampire’s taste, his peculiar views of things which had never entered into the dialogue of courtship—all these continually alienating influences, even without the fact of his having placed himself at a disadvantage in the town, and without that first shock of revelation about Dover’s debt, would have made his presence dull to her. There was another presence which ever since the early days of her marriage, until four months ago, had been an agreeable excitement, but that was gone: Rosamond would not confess to herself how much the consequent blank had to do with her utter ennui; and it seemed to her (perhaps she was right) that an invitation to Quallingham, and an opening for Lydgate to settle elsewhere than in Middlemarch—in London, or somewhere likely to be free from unpleasantness—would satisfy her quite well, and make her indifferent to the absence of Will Ladislaw, towards whom she felt some resentment for his exaltation of Mrs. Casaubon.\n\nThat was the state of things with Lydgate and Rosamond on the New Year’s Day when they dined at her father’s, she looking mildly neutral towards him in remembrance of his ill-tempered behavior at breakfast, and he carrying a much deeper effect from the inward conflict in which that morning scene was only one of many epochs. His flushed effort while talking to Mr. Farebrother—his effort after the cynical pretence that all ways of getting money are essentially the same, and that chance has an empire which reduces choice to a fool’s illusion—was but the symptom of a wavering resolve, a benumbed response to the old stimuli of enthusiasm.\n\nWhat was he to do? He saw even more keenly than Rosamond did the dreariness of taking her into the small house in Bride Street, where she would have scanty furniture around her and discontent within: a life of privation and life with Rosamond were two images which had become more and more irreconcilable ever since the threat of privation had disclosed itself. But even if his resolves had forced the two images into combination, the useful preliminaries to that hard change were not visibly within reach. And though he had not given the promise which his wife had asked for, he did not go again to Trumbull. He even began to think of taking a rapid journey to the North and seeing Sir Godwin. He had once believed that nothing would urge him into making an application for money to his uncle, but he had not then known the full pressure of alternatives yet more disagreeable. He could not depend on the effect of a letter; it was only in an interview, however disagreeable this might be to himself, that he could give a thorough explanation and could test the effectiveness of kinship. No sooner had Lydgate begun to represent this step to himself as the easiest than there was a reaction of anger that he—he who had long ago determined to live aloof from such abject calculations, such self-interested anxiety about the inclinations and the pockets of men with whom he had been proud to have no aims in common—should have fallen not simply to their level, but to the level of soliciting them.\n\n# CHAPTER LXV.\n\nOne of us two must bowen douteless, And, sith a man is more reasonable Than woman is, ye [men] moste be suffrable. —CHAUCER: Canterbury Tales.\n\nThe bias of human nature to be slow in correspondence triumphs even over the present quickening in the general pace of things: what wonder then that in 1832 old Sir Godwin Lydgate was slow to write a letter which was of consequence to others rather than to himself? Nearly three weeks of the new year were gone, and Rosamond, awaiting an answer to her winning appeal, was every day disappointed. Lydgate, in total ignorance of her expectations, was seeing the bills come in, and feeling that Dover’s use of his advantage over other creditors was imminent. He had never mentioned to Rosamond his brooding purpose of going to Quallingham: he did not want to admit what would appear to her a concession to her wishes after indignant refusal, until the last moment; but he was really expecting to set off soon. A slice of the railway would enable him to manage the whole journey and back in four days.\n\nBut one morning after Lydgate had gone out, a letter came addressed to him, which Rosamond saw clearly to be from Sir Godwin. She was full of hope. Perhaps there might be a particular note to her enclosed; but Lydgate was naturally addressed on the question of money or other aid, and the fact that he was written to, nay, the very delay in writing at all, seemed to certify that the answer was thoroughly compliant. She was too much excited by these thoughts to do anything but light stitching in a warm corner of the dining-room, with the outside of this momentous letter lying on the table before her. About twelve she heard her husband’s step in the passage, and tripping to open the door, she said in her lightest tones, “Tertius, come in here—here is a letter for you.”\n\n“Ah?” he said, not taking off his hat, but just turning her round within his arm to walk towards the spot where the letter lay. “My uncle Godwin!” he exclaimed, while Rosamond reseated herself, and watched him as he opened the letter. She had expected him to be surprised.\n\nWhile Lydgate’s eyes glanced rapidly over the brief letter, she saw his face, usually of a pale brown, taking on a dry whiteness; with nostrils and lips quivering he tossed down the letter before her, and said violently—\n\n“It will be impossible to endure life with you, if you will always be acting secretly—acting in opposition to me and hiding your actions.”\n\nHe checked his speech and turned his back on her—then wheeled round and walked about, sat down, and got up again restlessly, grasping hard the objects deep down in his pockets. He was afraid of saying something irremediably cruel.\n\nRosamond too had changed color as she read. The letter ran in this way:—\n\n“DEAR TERTIUS,—Don’t set your wife to write to me when you have anything to ask. It is a roundabout wheedling sort of thing which I should not have credited you with. I never choose to write to a woman on matters of business. As to my supplying you with a thousand pounds, or only half that sum, I can do nothing of the sort. My own family drains me to the last penny. With two younger sons and three daughters, I am not likely to have cash to spare. You seem to have got through your own money pretty quickly, and to have made a mess where you are; the sooner you go somewhere else the better. But I have nothing to do with men of your profession, and can’t help you there. I did the best I could for you as guardian, and let you have your own way in taking to medicine. You might have gone into the army or the Church. Your money would have held out for that, and there would have been a surer ladder before you. Your uncle Charles has had a grudge against you for not going into his profession, but not I. I have always wished you well, but you must consider yourself on your own legs entirely now.\n\nYour affectionate uncle, GODWIN LYDGATE.”\n\nWhen Rosamond had finished reading the letter she sat quite still, with her hands folded before her, restraining any show of her keen disappointment, and intrenching herself in quiet passivity under her husband’s wrath. Lydgate paused in his movements, looked at her again, and said, with biting severity—\n\n“Will this be enough to convince you of the harm you may do by secret meddling? Have you sense enough to recognize now your incompetence to judge and act for me—to interfere with your ignorance in affairs which it belongs to me to decide on?”\n\nThe words were hard; but this was not the first time that Lydgate had been frustrated by her. She did not look at him, and made no reply.\n\n“I had nearly resolved on going to Quallingham. It would have cost me pain enough to do it, yet it might have been of some use. But it has been of no use for me to think of anything. You have always been counteracting me secretly. You delude me with a false assent, and then I am at the mercy of your devices. If you mean to resist every wish I express, say so and defy me. I shall at least know what I am doing then.”\n\nIt is a terrible moment in young lives when the closeness of love’s bond has turned to this power of galling. In spite of Rosamond’s self-control a tear fell silently and rolled over her lips. She still said nothing; but under that quietude was hidden an intense effect: she was in such entire disgust with her husband that she wished she had never seen him. Sir Godwin’s rudeness towards her and utter want of feeling ranged him with Dover and all other creditors—disagreeable people who only thought of themselves, and did not mind how annoying they were to her. Even her father was unkind, and might have done more for them. In fact there was but one person in Rosamond’s world whom she did not regard as blameworthy, and that was the graceful creature with blond plaits and with little hands crossed before her, who had never expressed herself unbecomingly, and had always acted for the best—the best naturally being what she best liked.\n\nLydgate pausing and looking at her began to feel that half-maddening sense of helplessness which comes over passionate people when their passion is met by an innocent-looking silence whose meek victimized air seems to put them in the wrong, and at last infects even the justest indignation with a doubt of its justice. He needed to recover the full sense that he was in the right by moderating his words.\n\n“Can you not see, Rosamond,” he began again, trying to be simply grave and not bitter, “that nothing can be so fatal as a want of openness and confidence between us? It has happened again and again that I have expressed a decided wish, and you have seemed to assent, yet after that you have secretly disobeyed my wish. In that way I can never know what I have to trust to. There would be some hope for us if you would admit this. Am I such an unreasonable, furious brute? Why should you not be open with me?” Still silence.\n\n“Will you only say that you have been mistaken, and that I may depend on your not acting secretly in future?” said Lydgate, urgently, but with something of request in his tone which Rosamond was quick to perceive. She spoke with coolness.\n\n“I cannot possibly make admissions or promises in answer to such words as you have used towards me. I have not been accustomed to language of that kind. You have spoken of my ‘secret meddling,’ and my ‘interfering ignorance,’ and my ‘false assent.’ I have never expressed myself in that way to you, and I think that you ought to apologize. You spoke of its being impossible to live with me. Certainly you have not made my life pleasant to me of late. I think it was to be expected that I should try to avert some of the hardships which our marriage has brought on me.” Another tear fell as Rosamond ceased speaking, and she pressed it away as quietly as the first.\n\nLydgate flung himself into a chair, feeling checkmated. What place was there in her mind for a remonstrance to lodge in? He laid down his hat, flung an arm over the back of his chair, and looked down for some moments without speaking. Rosamond had the double purchase over him of insensibility to the point of justice in his reproach, and of sensibility to the undeniable hardships now present in her married life. Although her duplicity in the affair of the house had exceeded what he knew, and had really hindered the Plymdales from knowing of it, she had no consciousness that her action could rightly be called false. We are not obliged to identify our own acts according to a strict classification, any more than the materials of our grocery and clothes. Rosamond felt that she was aggrieved, and that this was what Lydgate had to recognize.\n\nAs for him, the need of accommodating himself to her nature, which was inflexible in proportion to its negations, held him as with pincers. He had begun to have an alarmed foresight of her irrevocable loss of love for him, and the consequent dreariness of their life. The ready fulness of his emotions made this dread alternate quickly with the first violent movements of his anger. It would assuredly have been a vain boast in him to say that he was her master.\n\n“You have not made my life pleasant to me of late”—“the hardships which our marriage has brought on me”—these words were stinging his imagination as a pain makes an exaggerated dream. If he were not only to sink from his highest resolve, but to sink into the hideous fettering of domestic hate?\n\n“Rosamond,” he said, turning his eyes on her with a melancholy look, “you should allow for a man’s words when he is disappointed and provoked. You and I cannot have opposite interests. I cannot part my happiness from yours. If I am angry with you, it is that you seem not to see how any concealment divides us. How could I wish to make anything hard to you either by my words or conduct? When I hurt you, I hurt part of my own life. I should never be angry with you if you would be quite open with me.”\n\n“I have only wished to prevent you from hurrying us into wretchedness without any necessity,” said Rosamond, the tears coming again from a softened feeling now that her husband had softened. “It is so very hard to be disgraced here among all the people we know, and to live in such a miserable way. I wish I had died with the baby.”\n\nShe spoke and wept with that gentleness which makes such words and tears omnipotent over a loving-hearted man. Lydgate drew his chair near to hers and pressed her delicate head against his cheek with his powerful tender hand. He only caressed her; he did not say anything; for what was there to say? He could not promise to shield her from the dreaded wretchedness, for he could see no sure means of doing so. When he left her to go out again, he told himself that it was ten times harder for her than for him: he had a life away from home, and constant appeals to his activity on behalf of others. He wished to excuse everything in her if he could—but it was inevitable that in that excusing mood he should think of her as if she were an animal of another and feebler species. Nevertheless she had mastered him.\n\n# CHAPTER LXVI.\n\n’Tis one thing to be tempted, Escalus, Another thing to fall. —Measure for Measure.\nLydgate certainly had good reason to reflect on the service his practice did him in counteracting his personal cares. He had no longer free energy enough for spontaneous research and speculative thinking, but by the bedside of patients, the direct external calls on his judgment and sympathies brought the added impulse needed to draw him out of himself. It was not simply that beneficent harness of routine which enables silly men to live respectably and unhappy men to live calmly—it was a perpetual claim on the immediate fresh application of thought, and on the consideration of another’s need and trial. Many of us looking back through life would say that the kindest man we have ever known has been a medical man, or perhaps that surgeon whose fine tact, directed by deeply informed perception, has come to us in our need with a more sublime beneficence than that of miracle-workers. Some of that twice-blessed mercy was always with Lydgate in his work at the Hospital or in private houses, serving better than any opiate to quiet and sustain him under his anxieties and his sense of mental degeneracy.\n\nMr. Farebrother’s suspicion as to the opiate was true, however. Under the first galling pressure of foreseen difficulties, and the first perception that his marriage, if it were not to be a yoked loneliness, must be a state of effort to go on loving without too much care about being loved, he had once or twice tried a dose of opium. But he had no hereditary constitutional craving after such transient escapes from the hauntings of misery. He was strong, could drink a great deal of wine, but did not care about it; and when the men round him were drinking spirits, he took sugar and water, having a contemptuous pity even for the earliest stages of excitement from drink. It was the same with gambling. He had looked on at a great deal of gambling in Paris, watching it as if it had been a disease. He was no more tempted by such winning than he was by drink. He had said to himself that the only winning he cared for must be attained by a conscious process of high, difficult combination tending towards a beneficent result. The power he longed for could not be represented by agitated fingers clutching a heap of coin, or by the half-barbarous, half-idiotic triumph in the eyes of a man who sweeps within his arms the ventures of twenty chapfallen companions.\n\nBut just as he had tried opium, so his thought now began to turn upon gambling—not with appetite for its excitement, but with a sort of wistful inward gaze after that easy way of getting money, which implied no asking and brought no responsibility. If he had been in London or Paris at that time, it is probable that such thoughts, seconded by opportunity, would have taken him into a gambling-house, no longer to watch the gamblers, but to watch with them in kindred eagerness. Repugnance would have been surmounted by the immense need to win, if chance would be kind enough to let him. An incident which happened not very long after that airy notion of getting aid from his uncle had been excluded, was a strong sign of the effect that might have followed any extant opportunity of gambling.\n\nThe billiard-room at the Green Dragon was the constant resort of a certain set, most of whom, like our acquaintance Mr. Bambridge, were regarded as men of pleasure. It was here that poor Fred Vincy had made part of his memorable debt, having lost money in betting, and been obliged to borrow of that gay companion. It was generally known in Middlemarch that a good deal of money was lost and won in this way; and the consequent repute of the Green Dragon as a place of dissipation naturally heightened in some quarters the temptation to go there. Probably its regular visitants, like the initiates of freemasonry, wished that there were something a little more tremendous to keep to themselves concerning it; but they were not a closed community, and many decent seniors as well as juniors occasionally turned into the billiard-room to see what was going on. Lydgate, who had the muscular aptitude for billiards, and was fond of the game, had once or twice in the early days after his arrival in Middlemarch taken his turn with the cue at the Green Dragon; but afterwards he had no leisure for the game, and no inclination for the socialities there. One evening, however, he had occasion to seek Mr. Bambridge at that resort. The horsedealer had engaged to get him a customer for his remaining good horse, for which Lydgate had determined to substitute a cheap hack, hoping by this reduction of style to get perhaps twenty pounds; and he cared now for every small sum, as a help towards feeding the patience of his tradesmen. To run up to the billiard-room, as he was passing, would save time.\n\nMr. Bambridge was not yet come, but would be sure to arrive by-and-by, said his friend Mr. Horrock; and Lydgate stayed, playing a game for the sake of passing the time. That evening he had the peculiar light in the eyes and the unusual vivacity which had been once noticed in him by Mr. Farebrother. The exceptional fact of his presence was much noticed in the room, where there was a good deal of Middlemarch company; and several lookers-on, as well as some of the players, were betting with animation. Lydgate was playing well, and felt confident; the bets were dropping round him, and with a swift glancing thought of the probable gain which might double the sum he was saving from his horse, he began to bet on his own play, and won again and again. Mr. Bambridge had come in, but Lydgate did not notice him. He was not only excited with his play, but visions were gleaming on him of going the next day to Brassing, where there was gambling on a grander scale to be had, and where, by one powerful snatch at the devil’s bait, he might carry it off without the hook, and buy his rescue from his daily solicitings.\n\nHe was still winning when two new visitors entered. One of them was a young Hawley, just come from his law studies in town, and the other was Fred Vincy, who had spent several evenings of late at this old haunt of his. Young Hawley, an accomplished billiard-player, brought a cool fresh hand to the cue. But Fred Vincy, startled at seeing Lydgate, and astonished to see him betting with an excited air, stood aside, and kept out of the circle round the table.\n\nFred had been rewarding resolution by a little laxity of late. He had been working heartily for six months at all outdoor occupations under Mr. Garth, and by dint of severe practice had nearly mastered the defects of his handwriting, this practice being, perhaps, a little the less severe that it was often carried on in the evening at Mr. Garth’s under the eyes of Mary. But the last fortnight Mary had been staying at Lowick Parsonage with the ladies there, during Mr. Farebrother’s residence in Middlemarch, where he was carrying out some parochial plans; and Fred, not seeing anything more agreeable to do, had turned into the Green Dragon, partly to play at billiards, partly to taste the old flavor of discourse about horses, sport, and things in general, considered from a point of view which was not strenuously correct. He had not been out hunting once this season, had had no horse of his own to ride, and had gone from place to place chiefly with Mr. Garth in his gig, or on the sober cob which Mr. Garth could lend him. It was a little too bad, Fred began to think, that he should be kept in the traces with more severity than if he had been a clergyman. “I will tell you what, Mistress Mary—it will be rather harder work to learn surveying and drawing plans than it would have been to write sermons,” he had said, wishing her to appreciate what he went through for her sake; “and as to Hercules and Theseus, they were nothing to me. They had sport, and never learned to write a bookkeeping hand.” And now, Mary being out of the way for a little while, Fred, like any other strong dog who cannot slip his collar, had pulled up the staple of his chain and made a small escape, not of course meaning to go fast or far. There could be no reason why he should not play at billiards, but he was determined not to bet. As to money just now, Fred had in his mind the heroic project of saving almost all of the eighty pounds that Mr. Garth offered him, and returning it, which he could easily do by giving up all futile money-spending, since he had a superfluous stock of clothes, and no expense in his board. In that way he could, in one year, go a good way towards repaying the ninety pounds of which he had deprived Mrs. Garth, unhappily at a time when she needed that sum more than she did now. Nevertheless, it must be acknowledged that on this evening, which was the fifth of his recent visits to the billiard-room, Fred had, not in his pocket, but in his mind, the ten pounds which he meant to reserve for himself from his half-year’s salary (having before him the pleasure of carrying thirty to Mrs. Garth when Mary was likely to be come home again)—he had those ten pounds in his mind as a fund from which he might risk something, if there were a chance of a good bet. Why? Well, when sovereigns were flying about, why shouldn’t he catch a few? He would never go far along that road again; but a man likes to assure himself, and men of pleasure generally, what he could do in the way of mischief if he chose, and that if he abstains from making himself ill, or beggaring himself, or talking with the utmost looseness which the narrow limits of human capacity will allow, it is not because he is a spooney. Fred did not enter into formal reasons, which are a very artificial, inexact way of representing the tingling returns of old habit, and the caprices of young blood: but there was lurking in him a prophetic sense that evening, that when he began to play he should also begin to bet—that he should enjoy some punch-drinking, and in general prepare himself for feeling “rather seedy” in the morning. It is in such indefinable movements that action often begins.\n\nBut the last thing likely to have entered Fred’s expectation was that he should see his brother-in-law Lydgate—of whom he had never quite dropped the old opinion that he was a prig, and tremendously conscious of his superiority—looking excited and betting, just as he himself might have done. Fred felt a shock greater than he could quite account for by the vague knowledge that Lydgate was in debt, and that his father had refused to help him; and his own inclination to enter into the play was suddenly checked. It was a strange reversal of attitudes: Fred’s blond face and blue eyes, usually bright and careless, ready to give attention to anything that held out a promise of amusement, looking involuntarily grave and almost embarrassed as if by the sight of something unfitting; while Lydgate, who had habitually an air of self-possessed strength, and a certain meditativeness that seemed to lie behind his most observant attention, was acting, watching, speaking with that excited narrow consciousness which reminds one of an animal with fierce eyes and retractile claws.\n\nLydgate, by betting on his own strokes, had won sixteen pounds; but young Hawley’s arrival had changed the poise of things. He made first-rate strokes himself, and began to bet against Lydgate’s strokes, the strain of whose nerves was thus changed from simple confidence in his own movements to defying another person’s doubt in them. The defiance was more exciting than the confidence, but it was less sure. He continued to bet on his own play, but began often to fail. Still he went on, for his mind was as utterly narrowed into that precipitous crevice of play as if he had been the most ignorant lounger there. Fred observed that Lydgate was losing fast, and found himself in the new situation of puzzling his brains to think of some device by which, without being offensive, he could withdraw Lydgate’s attention, and perhaps suggest to him a reason for quitting the room. He saw that others were observing Lydgate’s strange unlikeness to himself, and it occurred to him that merely to touch his elbow and call him aside for a moment might rouse him from his absorption. He could think of nothing cleverer than the daring improbability of saying that he wanted to see Rosy, and wished to know if she were at home this evening; and he was going desperately to carry out this weak device, when a waiter came up to him with a message, saying that Mr. Farebrother was below, and begged to speak with him.\n\nFred was surprised, not quite comfortably, but sending word that he would be down immediately, he went with a new impulse up to Lydgate, said, “Can I speak to you a moment?” and drew him aside.\n\n“Farebrother has just sent up a message to say that he wants to speak to me. He is below. I thought you might like to know he was there, if you had anything to say to him.”\n\nFred had simply snatched up this pretext for speaking, because he could not say, “You are losing confoundedly, and are making everybody stare at you; you had better come away.” But inspiration could hardly have served him better. Lydgate had not before seen that Fred was present, and his sudden appearance with an announcement of Mr. Farebrother had the effect of a sharp concussion.\n\n“No, no,” said Lydgate; “I have nothing particular to say to him. But—the game is up—I must be going—I came in just to see Bambridge.”\n\n“Bambridge is over there, but he is making a row—I don’t think he’s ready for business. Come down with me to Farebrother. I expect he is going to blow me up, and you will shield me,” said Fred, with some adroitness.\n\nLydgate felt shame, but could not bear to act as if he felt it, by refusing to see Mr. Farebrother; and he went down. They merely shook hands, however, and spoke of the frost; and when all three had turned into the street, the Vicar seemed quite willing to say good-by to Lydgate. His present purpose was clearly to talk with Fred alone, and he said, kindly, “I disturbed you, young gentleman, because I have some pressing business with you. Walk with me to St. Botolph’s, will you?”\n\nIt was a fine night, the sky thick with stars, and Mr. Farebrother proposed that they should make a circuit to the old church by the London road. The next thing he said was—\n\n“I thought Lydgate never went to the Green Dragon?”\n\n“So did I,” said Fred. “But he said that he went to see Bambridge.”\n\n“He was not playing, then?”\n\nFred had not meant to tell this, but he was obliged now to say, “Yes, he was. But I suppose it was an accidental thing. I have never seen him there before.”\n\n“You have been going often yourself, then, lately?”\n\n“Oh, about five or six times.”\n\n“I think you had some good reason for giving up the habit of going there?”\n\n“Yes. You know all about it,” said Fred, not liking to be catechised in this way. “I made a clean breast to you.”\n\n“I suppose that gives me a warrant to speak about the matter now. It is understood between us, is it not?—that we are on a footing of open friendship: I have listened to you, and you will be willing to listen to me. I may take my turn in talking a little about myself?”\n\n“I am under the deepest obligation to you, Mr. Farebrother,” said Fred, in a state of uncomfortable surmise.\n\n“I will not affect to deny that you are under some obligation to me. But I am going to confess to you, Fred, that I have been tempted to reverse all that by keeping silence with you just now. When somebody said to me, ‘Young Vincy has taken to being at the billiard-table every night again—he won’t bear the curb long;’ I was tempted to do the opposite of what I am doing—to hold my tongue and wait while you went down the ladder again, betting first and then—”\n\n“I have not made any bets,” said Fred, hastily.\n\n“Glad to hear it. But I say, my prompting was to look on and see you take the wrong turning, wear out Garth’s patience, and lose the best opportunity of your life—the opportunity which you made some rather difficult effort to secure. You can guess the feeling which raised that temptation in me—I am sure you know it. I am sure you know that the satisfaction of your affections stands in the way of mine.”\n\nThere was a pause. Mr. Farebrother seemed to wait for a recognition of the fact; and the emotion perceptible in the tones of his fine voice gave solemnity to his words. But no feeling could quell Fred’s alarm.\n\n“I could not be expected to give her up,” he said, after a moment’s hesitation: it was not a case for any pretence of generosity.\n\n“Clearly not, when her affection met yours. But relations of this sort, even when they are of long standing, are always liable to change. I can easily conceive that you might act in a way to loosen the tie she feels towards you—it must be remembered that she is only conditionally bound to you—and that in that case, another man, who may flatter himself that he has a hold on her regard, might succeed in winning that firm place in her love as well as respect which you had let slip. I can easily conceive such a result,” repeated Mr. Farebrother, emphatically. “There is a companionship of ready sympathy, which might get the advantage even over the longest associations.” It seemed to Fred that if Mr. Farebrother had had a beak and talons instead of his very capable tongue, his mode of attack could hardly be more cruel. He had a horrible conviction that behind all this hypothetic statement there was a knowledge of some actual change in Mary’s feeling.\n\n“Of course I know it might easily be all up with me,” he said, in a troubled voice. “If she is beginning to compare—” He broke off, not liking to betray all he felt, and then said, by the help of a little bitterness, “But I thought you were friendly to me.”\n\n“So I am; that is why we are here. But I have had a strong disposition to be otherwise. I have said to myself, ‘If there is a likelihood of that youngster doing himself harm, why should you interfere? Aren’t you worth as much as he is, and don’t your sixteen years over and above his, in which you have gone rather hungry, give you more right to satisfaction than he has? If there’s a chance of his going to the dogs, let him—perhaps you could nohow hinder it—and do you take the benefit.’”\n\nThere was a pause, in which Fred was seized by a most uncomfortable chill. What was coming next? He dreaded to hear that something had been said to Mary—he felt as if he were listening to a threat rather than a warning. When the Vicar began again there was a change in his tone like the encouraging transition to a major key.\n\n“But I had once meant better than that, and I am come back to my old intention. I thought that I could hardly secure myself in it better, Fred, than by telling you just what had gone on in me. And now, do you understand me? I want you to make the happiness of her life and your own, and if there is any chance that a word of warning from me may turn aside any risk to the contrary—well, I have uttered it.”\n\nThere was a drop in the Vicar’s voice when he spoke the last words. He paused—they were standing on a patch of green where the road diverged towards St. Botolph’s, and he put out his hand, as if to imply that the conversation was closed. Fred was moved quite newly. Some one highly susceptible to the contemplation of a fine act has said, that it produces a sort of regenerating shudder through the frame, and makes one feel ready to begin a new life. A good degree of that effect was just then present in Fred Vincy.\n\n“I will try to be worthy,” he said, breaking off before he could say “of you as well as of her.” And meanwhile Mr. Farebrother had gathered the impulse to say something more.\n\n“You must not imagine that I believe there is at present any decline in her preference of you, Fred. Set your heart at rest, that if you keep right, other things will keep right.”\n\n“I shall never forget what you have done,” Fred answered. “I can’t say anything that seems worth saying—only I will try that your goodness shall not be thrown away.”\n\n“That’s enough. Good-by, and God bless you.”\n\nIn that way they parted. But both of them walked about a long while before they went out of the starlight. Much of Fred’s rumination might be summed up in the words, “It certainly would have been a fine thing for her to marry Farebrother—but if she loves me best and I am a good husband?”\n\nPerhaps Mr. Farebrother’s might be concentrated into a single shrug and one little speech. “To think of the part one little woman can play in the life of a man, so that to renounce her may be a very good imitation of heroism, and to win her may be a discipline!”\n\n# CHAPTER LXVII.\n\nNow is there civil war within the soul: Resolve is thrust from off the sacred throne By clamorous Needs, and Pride the grand-vizier Makes humble compact, plays the supple part Of envoy and deft-tongued apologist For hungry rebels.\n\nHappily Lydgate had ended by losing in the billiard-room, and brought away no encouragement to make a raid on luck. On the contrary, he felt unmixed disgust with himself the next day when he had to pay four or five pounds over and above his gains, and he carried about with him a most unpleasant vision of the figure he had made, not only rubbing elbows with the men at the Green Dragon but behaving just as they did. A philosopher fallen to betting is hardly distinguishable from a Philistine under the same circumstances: the difference will chiefly be found in his subsequent reflections, and Lydgate chewed a very disagreeable cud in that way. His reason told him how the affair might have been magnified into ruin by a slight change of scenery—if it had been a gambling-house that he had turned into, where chance could be clutched with both hands instead of being picked up with thumb and fore-finger. Nevertheless, though reason strangled the desire to gamble, there remained the feeling that, with an assurance of luck to the needful amount, he would have liked to gamble, rather than take the alternative which was beginning to urge itself as inevitable.\n\nThat alternative was to apply to Mr. Bulstrode. Lydgate had so many times boasted both to himself and others that he was totally independent of Bulstrode, to whose plans he had lent himself solely because they enabled him to carry out his own ideas of professional work and public benefit—he had so constantly in their personal intercourse had his pride sustained by the sense that he was making a good social use of this predominating banker, whose opinions he thought contemptible and whose motives often seemed to him an absurd mixture of contradictory impressions—that he had been creating for himself strong ideal obstacles to the proffering of any considerable request to him on his own account.\n\nStill, early in March his affairs were at that pass in which men begin to say that their oaths were delivered in ignorance, and to perceive that the act which they had called impossible to them is becoming manifestly possible. With Dover’s ugly security soon to be put in force, with the proceeds of his practice immediately absorbed in paying back debts, and with the chance, if the worst were known, of daily supplies being refused on credit, above all with the vision of Rosamond’s hopeless discontent continually haunting him, Lydgate had begun to see that he should inevitably bend himself to ask help from somebody or other. At first he had considered whether he should write to Mr. Vincy; but on questioning Rosamond he found that, as he had suspected, she had already applied twice to her father, the last time being since the disappointment from Sir Godwin; and papa had said that Lydgate must look out for himself. “Papa said he had come, with one bad year after another, to trade more and more on borrowed capital, and had had to give up many indulgences; he could not spare a single hundred from the charges of his family. He said, let Lydgate ask Bulstrode: they have always been hand and glove.”\n\nIndeed, Lydgate himself had come to the conclusion that if he must end by asking for a free loan, his relations with Bulstrode, more at least than with any other man, might take the shape of a claim which was not purely personal. Bulstrode had indirectly helped to cause the failure of his practice, and had also been highly gratified by getting a medical partner in his plans:—but who among us ever reduced himself to the sort of dependence in which Lydgate now stood, without trying to believe that he had claims which diminished the humiliation of asking? It was true that of late there had seemed to be a new languor of interest in Bulstrode about the Hospital; but his health had got worse, and showed signs of a deep-seated nervous affection. In other respects he did not appear to be changed: he had always been highly polite, but Lydgate had observed in him from the first a marked coldness about his marriage and other private circumstances, a coldness which he had hitherto preferred to any warmth of familiarity between them. He deferred the intention from day to day, his habit of acting on his conclusions being made infirm by his repugnance to every possible conclusion and its consequent act. He saw Mr. Bulstrode often, but he did not try to use any occasion for his private purpose. At one moment he thought, “I will write a letter: I prefer that to any circuitous talk;” at another he thought, “No; if I were talking to him, I could make a retreat before any signs of disinclination.”\n\nStill the days passed and no letter was written, no special interview sought. In his shrinking from the humiliation of a dependent attitude towards Bulstrode, he began to familiarize his imagination with another step even more unlike his remembered self. He began spontaneously to consider whether it would be possible to carry out that puerile notion of Rosamond’s which had often made him angry, namely, that they should quit Middlemarch without seeing anything beyond that preface. The question came—“Would any man buy the practice of me even now, for as little as it is worth? Then the sale might happen as a necessary preparation for going away.”\n\nBut against his taking this step, which he still felt to be a contemptible relinquishment of present work, a guilty turning aside from what was a real and might be a widening channel for worthy activity, to start again without any justified destination, there was this obstacle, that the purchaser, if procurable at all, might not be quickly forthcoming. And afterwards? Rosamond in a poor lodging, though in the largest city or most distant town, would not find the life that could save her from gloom, and save him from the reproach of having plunged her into it. For when a man is at the foot of the hill in his fortunes, he may stay a long while there in spite of professional accomplishment. In the British climate there is no incompatibility between scientific insight and furnished lodgings: the incompatibility is chiefly between scientific ambition and a wife who objects to that kind of residence.\n\nBut in the midst of his hesitation, opportunity came to decide him. A note from Mr. Bulstrode requested Lydgate to call on him at the Bank. A hypochondriacal tendency had shown itself in the banker’s constitution of late; and a lack of sleep, which was really only a slight exaggeration of an habitual dyspeptic symptom, had been dwelt on by him as a sign of threatening insanity. He wanted to consult Lydgate without delay on that particular morning, although he had nothing to tell beyond what he had told before. He listened eagerly to what Lydgate had to say in dissipation of his fears, though this too was only repetition; and this moment in which Bulstrode was receiving a medical opinion with a sense of comfort, seemed to make the communication of a personal need to him easier than it had been in Lydgate’s contemplation beforehand. He had been insisting that it would be well for Mr. Bulstrode to relax his attention to business.\n\n“One sees how any mental strain, however slight, may affect a delicate frame,” said Lydgate at that stage of the consultation when the remarks tend to pass from the personal to the general, “by the deep stamp which anxiety will make for a time even on the young and vigorous. I am naturally very strong; yet I have been thoroughly shaken lately by an accumulation of trouble.”\n\n“I presume that a constitution in the susceptible state in which mine at present is, would be especially liable to fall a victim to cholera, if it visited our district. And since its appearance near London, we may well besiege the Mercy-seat for our protection,” said Mr. Bulstrode, not intending to evade Lydgate’s allusion, but really preoccupied with alarms about himself.\n\n“You have at all events taken your share in using good practical precautions for the town, and that is the best mode of asking for protection,” said Lydgate, with a strong distaste for the broken metaphor and bad logic of the banker’s religion, somewhat increased by the apparent deafness of his sympathy. But his mind had taken up its long-prepared movement towards getting help, and was not yet arrested. He added, “The town has done well in the way of cleansing, and finding appliances; and I think that if the cholera should come, even our enemies will admit that the arrangements in the Hospital are a public good.”\n\n“Truly,” said Mr. Bulstrode, with some coldness. “With regard to what you say, Mr. Lydgate, about the relaxation of my mental labor, I have for some time been entertaining a purpose to that effect—a purpose of a very decided character. I contemplate at least a temporary withdrawal from the management of much business, whether benevolent or commercial. Also I think of changing my residence for a time: probably I shall close or let ‘The Shrubs,’ and take some place near the coast—under advice of course as to salubrity. That would be a measure which you would recommend?”\n\n“Oh yes,” said Lydgate, falling backward in his chair, with ill-repressed impatience under the banker’s pale earnest eyes and intense preoccupation with himself.\n\n“I have for some time felt that I should open this subject with you in relation to our Hospital,” continued Bulstrode. “Under the circumstances I have indicated, of course I must cease to have any personal share in the management, and it is contrary to my views of responsibility to continue a large application of means to an institution which I cannot watch over and to some extent regulate. I shall therefore, in case of my ultimate decision to leave Middlemarch, consider that I withdraw other support to the New Hospital than that which will subsist in the fact that I chiefly supplied the expenses of building it, and have contributed further large sums to its successful working.”\n\nLydgate’s thought, when Bulstrode paused according to his wont, was, “He has perhaps been losing a good deal of money.” This was the most plausible explanation of a speech which had caused rather a startling change in his expectations. He said in reply—\n\n“The loss to the Hospital can hardly be made up, I fear.”\n\n“Hardly,” returned Bulstrode, in the same deliberate, silvery tone; “except by some changes of plan. The only person who may be certainly counted on as willing to increase her contributions is Mrs. Casaubon. I have had an interview with her on the subject, and I have pointed out to her, as I am about to do to you, that it will be desirable to win a more general support to the New Hospital by a change of system.” Another pause, but Lydgate did not speak.\n\n“The change I mean is an amalgamation with the Infirmary, so that the New Hospital shall be regarded as a special addition to the elder institution, having the same directing board. It will be necessary, also, that the medical management of the two shall be combined. In this way any difficulty as to the adequate maintenance of our new establishment will be removed; the benevolent interests of the town will cease to be divided.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode had lowered his eyes from Lydgate’s face to the buttons of his coat as he again paused.\n\n“No doubt that is a good device as to ways and means,” said Lydgate, with an edge of irony in his tone. “But I can’t be expected to rejoice in it at once, since one of the first results will be that the other medical men will upset or interrupt my methods, if it were only because they are mine.”\n\n“I myself, as you know, Mr. Lydgate, highly valued the opportunity of new and independent procedure which you have diligently employed: the original plan, I confess, was one which I had much at heart, under submission to the Divine Will. But since providential indications demand a renunciation from me, I renounce.”\n\nBulstrode showed a rather exasperating ability in this conversation. The broken metaphor and bad logic of motive which had stirred his hearer’s contempt were quite consistent with a mode of putting the facts which made it difficult for Lydgate to vent his own indignation and disappointment. After some rapid reflection, he only asked—\n\n“What did Mrs. Casaubon say?”\n\n“That was the further statement which I wished to make to you,” said Bulstrode, who had thoroughly prepared his ministerial explanation. “She is, you are aware, a woman of most munificent disposition, and happily in possession—not I presume of great wealth, but of funds which she can well spare. She has informed me that though she has destined the chief part of those funds to another purpose, she is willing to consider whether she cannot fully take my place in relation to the Hospital. But she wishes for ample time to mature her thoughts on the subject, and I have told her that there is no need for haste—that, in fact, my own plans are not yet absolute.”\n\nLydgate was ready to say, “If Mrs. Casaubon would take your place, there would be gain, instead of loss.” But there was still a weight on his mind which arrested this cheerful candor. He replied, “I suppose, then, that I may enter into the subject with Mrs. Casaubon.”\n\n“Precisely; that is what she expressly desires. Her decision, she says, will much depend on what you can tell her. But not at present: she is, I believe, just setting out on a journey. I have her letter here,” said Mr. Bulstrode, drawing it out, and reading from it. “‘I am immediately otherwise engaged,’ she says. ‘I am going into Yorkshire with Sir James and Lady Chettam; and the conclusions I come to about some land which I am to see there may affect my power of contributing to the Hospital.’ Thus, Mr. Lydgate, there is no haste necessary in this matter; but I wished to apprise you beforehand of what may possibly occur.”\n\nMr. Bulstrode returned the letter to his side-pocket, and changed his attitude as if his business were closed. Lydgate, whose renewed hope about the Hospital only made him more conscious of the facts which poisoned his hope, felt that his effort after help, if made at all, must be made now and vigorously.\n\n“I am much obliged to you for giving me full notice,” he said, with a firm intention in his tone, yet with an interruptedness in his delivery which showed that he spoke unwillingly. “The highest object to me is my profession, and I had identified the Hospital with the best use I can at present make of my profession. But the best use is not always the same with monetary success. Everything which has made the Hospital unpopular has helped with other causes—I think they are all connected with my professional zeal—to make me unpopular as a practitioner. I get chiefly patients who can’t pay me. I should like them best, if I had nobody to pay on my own side.” Lydgate waited a little, but Bulstrode only bowed, looking at him fixedly, and he went on with the same interrupted enunciation—as if he were biting an objectional leek.\n\n“I have slipped into money difficulties which I can see no way out of, unless some one who trusts me and my future will advance me a sum without other security. I had very little fortune left when I came here. I have no prospects of money from my own family. My expenses, in consequence of my marriage, have been very much greater than I had expected. The result at this moment is that it would take a thousand pounds to clear me. I mean, to free me from the risk of having all my goods sold in security of my largest debt—as well as to pay my other debts—and leave anything to keep us a little beforehand with our small income. I find that it is out of the question that my wife’s father should make such an advance. That is why I mention my position to—to the only other man who may be held to have some personal connection with my prosperity or ruin.”\n\nLydgate hated to hear himself. But he had spoken now, and had spoken with unmistakable directness. Mr. Bulstrode replied without haste, but also without hesitation.\n\n“I am grieved, though, I confess, not surprised by this information, Mr. Lydgate. For my own part, I regretted your alliance with my brother-in-law’s family, which has always been of prodigal habits, and which has already been much indebted to me for sustainment in its present position. My advice to you, Mr. Lydgate, would be, that instead of involving yourself in further obligations, and continuing a doubtful struggle, you should simply become a bankrupt.”\n\n“That would not improve my prospect,” said Lydgate, rising and speaking bitterly, “even if it were a more agreeable thing in itself.”\n\n“It is always a trial,” said Mr. Bulstrode; “but trial, my dear sir, is our portion here, and is a needed corrective. I recommend you to weigh the advice I have given.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Lydgate, not quite knowing what he said. “I have occupied you too long. Good-day.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXVIII.\n\nWhat suit of grace hath Virtue to put on If Vice shall wear as good, and do as well? If Wrong, if Craft, if Indiscretion Act as fair parts with ends as laudable? Which all this mighty volume of events The world, the universal map of deeds, Strongly controls, and proves from all descents, That the directest course still best succeeds. For should not grave and learn’d Experience That looks with the eyes of all the world beside, And with all ages holds intelligence, Go safer than Deceit without a guide! —DANIEL: Musophilus.\n\nThat change of plan and shifting of interest which Bulstrode stated or betrayed in his conversation with Lydgate, had been determined in him by some severe experience which he had gone through since the epoch of Mr. Larcher’s sale, when Raffles had recognized Will Ladislaw, and when the banker had in vain attempted an act of restitution which might move Divine Providence to arrest painful consequences.\n\nHis certainty that Raffles, unless he were dead, would return to Middlemarch before long, had been justified. On Christmas Eve he had reappeared at The Shrubs. Bulstrode was at home to receive him, and hinder his communication with the rest of the family, but he could not altogether hinder the circumstances of the visit from compromising himself and alarming his wife. Raffles proved more unmanageable than he had shown himself to be in his former appearances, his chronic state of mental restlessness, the growing effect of habitual intemperance, quickly shaking off every impression from what was said to him. He insisted on staying in the house, and Bulstrode, weighing two sets of evils, felt that this was at least not a worse alternative than his going into the town. He kept him in his own room for the evening and saw him to bed, Raffles all the while amusing himself with the annoyance he was causing this decent and highly prosperous fellow-sinner, an amusement which he facetiously expressed as sympathy with his friend’s pleasure in entertaining a man who had been serviceable to him, and who had not had all his earnings. There was a cunning calculation under this noisy joking—a cool resolve to extract something the handsomer from Bulstrode as payment for release from this new application of torture. But his cunning had a little overcast its mark.\n\nBulstrode was indeed more tortured than the coarse fibre of Raffles could enable him to imagine. He had told his wife that he was simply taking care of this wretched creature, the victim of vice, who might otherwise injure himself; he implied, without the direct form of falsehood, that there was a family tie which bound him to this care, and that there were signs of mental alienation in Raffles which urged caution. He would himself drive the unfortunate being away the next morning. In these hints he felt that he was supplying Mrs. Bulstrode with precautionary information for his daughters and servants, and accounting for his allowing no one but himself to enter the room even with food and drink. But he sat in an agony of fear lest Raffles should be overheard in his loud and plain references to past facts—lest Mrs. Bulstrode should be even tempted to listen at the door. How could he hinder her, how betray his terror by opening the door to detect her? She was a woman of honest direct habits, and little likely to take so low a course in order to arrive at painful knowledge; but fear was stronger than the calculation of probabilities.\n\nIn this way Raffles had pushed the torture too far, and produced an effect which had not been in his plan. By showing himself hopelessly unmanageable he had made Bulstrode feel that a strong defiance was the only resource left. After taking Raffles to bed that night the banker ordered his closed carriage to be ready at half-past seven the next morning. At six o’clock he had already been long dressed, and had spent some of his wretchedness in prayer, pleading his motives for averting the worst evil if in anything he had used falsity and spoken what was not true before God. For Bulstrode shrank from a direct lie with an intensity disproportionate to the number of his more indirect misdeeds. But many of these misdeeds were like the subtle muscular movements which are not taken account of in the consciousness, though they bring about the end that we fix our mind on and desire. And it is only what we are vividly conscious of that we can vividly imagine to be seen by Omniscience.\n\nBulstrode carried his candle to the bedside of Raffles, who was apparently in a painful dream. He stood silent, hoping that the presence of the light would serve to waken the sleeper gradually and gently, for he feared some noise as the consequence of a too sudden awakening. He had watched for a couple of minutes or more the shudderings and pantings which seemed likely to end in waking, when Raffles, with a long half-stifled moan, started up and stared round him in terror, trembling and gasping. But he made no further noise, and Bulstrode, setting down the candle, awaited his recovery.\n\nIt was a quarter of an hour later before Bulstrode, with a cold peremptoriness of manner which he had not before shown, said, “I came to call you thus early, Mr. Raffles, because I have ordered the carriage to be ready at half-past seven, and intend myself to conduct you as far as Ilsely, where you can either take the railway or await a coach.” Raffles was about to speak, but Bulstrode anticipated him imperiously with the words, “Be silent, sir, and hear what I have to say. I shall supply you with money now, and I will furnish you with a reasonable sum from time to time, on your application to me by letter; but if you choose to present yourself here again, if you return to Middlemarch, if you use your tongue in a manner injurious to me, you will have to live on such fruits as your malice can bring you, without help from me. Nobody will pay you well for blasting my name: I know the worst you can do against me, and I shall brave it if you dare to thrust yourself upon me again. Get up, sir, and do as I order you, without noise, or I will send for a policeman to take you off my premises, and you may carry your stories into every pothouse in the town, but you shall have no sixpence from me to pay your expenses there.”\n\nBulstrode had rarely in his life spoken with such nervous energy: he had been deliberating on this speech and its probable effects through a large part of the night; and though he did not trust to its ultimately saving him from any return of Raffles, he had concluded that it was the best throw he could make. It succeeded in enforcing submission from the jaded man this morning: his empoisoned system at this moment quailed before Bulstrode’s cold, resolute bearing, and he was taken off quietly in the carriage before the family breakfast time. The servants imagined him to be a poor relation, and were not surprised that a strict man like their master, who held his head high in the world, should be ashamed of such a cousin and want to get rid of him. The banker’s drive of ten miles with his hated companion was a dreary beginning of the Christmas day; but at the end of the drive, Raffles had recovered his spirits, and parted in a contentment for which there was the good reason that the banker had given him a hundred pounds. Various motives urged Bulstrode to this open-handedness, but he did not himself inquire closely into all of them. As he had stood watching Raffles in his uneasy sleep, it had certainly entered his mind that the man had been much shattered since the first gift of two hundred pounds.\n\nHe had taken care to repeat the incisive statement of his resolve not to be played on any more; and had tried to penetrate Raffles with the fact that he had shown the risks of bribing him to be quite equal to the risks of defying him. But when, freed from his repulsive presence, Bulstrode returned to his quiet home, he brought with him no confidence that he had secured more than a respite. It was as if he had had a loathsome dream, and could not shake off its images with their hateful kindred of sensations—as if on all the pleasant surroundings of his life a dangerous reptile had left his slimy traces.\nWho can know how much of his most inward life is made up of the thoughts he believes other men to have about him, until that fabric of opinion is threatened with ruin?\n\nBulstrode was only the more conscious that there was a deposit of uneasy presentiment in his wife’s mind, because she carefully avoided any allusion to it. He had been used every day to taste the flavor of supremacy and the tribute of complete deference: and the certainty that he was watched or measured with a hidden suspicion of his having some discreditable secret, made his voice totter when he was speaking to edification. Foreseeing, to men of Bulstrode’s anxious temperament, is often worse than seeing; and his imagination continually heightened the anguish of an imminent disgrace. Yes, imminent; for if his defiance of Raffles did not keep the man away—and though he prayed for this result he hardly hoped for it—the disgrace was certain. In vain he said to himself that, if permitted, it would be a divine visitation, a chastisement, a preparation; he recoiled from the imagined burning; and he judged that it must be more for the Divine glory that he should escape dishonor. That recoil had at last urged him to make preparations for quitting Middlemarch. If evil truth must be reported of him, he would then be at a less scorching distance from the contempt of his old neighbors; and in a new scene, where his life would not have gathered the same wide sensibility, the tormentor, if he pursued him, would be less formidable. To leave the place finally would, he knew, be extremely painful to his wife, and on other grounds he would have preferred to stay where he had struck root. Hence he made his preparations at first in a conditional way, wishing to leave on all sides an opening for his return after brief absence, if any favorable intervention of Providence should dissipate his fears. He was preparing to transfer his management of the Bank, and to give up any active control of other commercial affairs in the neighborhood, on the ground of his failing health, but without excluding his future resumption of such work. The measure would cause him some added expense and some diminution of income beyond what he had already undergone from the general depression of trade; and the Hospital presented itself as a principal object of outlay on which he could fairly economize.\n\nThis was the experience which had determined his conversation with Lydgate. But at this time his arrangements had most of them gone no farther than a stage at which he could recall them if they proved to be unnecessary. He continually deferred the final steps; in the midst of his fears, like many a man who is in danger of shipwreck or of being dashed from his carriage by runaway horses, he had a clinging impression that something would happen to hinder the worst, and that to spoil his life by a late transplantation might be over-hasty—especially since it was difficult to account satisfactorily to his wife for the project of their indefinite exile from the only place where she would like to live.\n\nAmong the affairs Bulstrode had to care for, was the management of the farm at Stone Court in case of his absence; and on this as well as on all other matters connected with any houses and land he possessed in or about Middlemarch, he had consulted Caleb Garth. Like every one else who had business of that sort, he wanted to get the agent who was more anxious for his employer’s interests than his own. With regard to Stone Court, since Bulstrode wished to retain his hold on the stock, and to have an arrangement by which he himself could, if he chose, resume his favorite recreation of superintendence, Caleb had advised him not to trust to a mere bailiff, but to let the land, stock, and implements yearly, and take a proportionate share of the proceeds.\n\n“May I trust to you to find me a tenant on these terms, Mr. Garth?” said Bulstrode. “And will you mention to me the yearly sum which would repay you for managing these affairs which we have discussed together?”\n“I’ll think about it,” said Caleb, in his blunt way. “I’ll see how I can make it out.”\n\nIf it had not been that he had to consider Fred Vincy’s future, Mr. Garth would not probably have been glad of any addition to his work, of which his wife was always fearing an excess for him as he grew older. But on quitting Bulstrode after that conversation, a very alluring idea occurred to him about this said letting of Stone Court. What if Bulstrode would agree to his placing Fred Vincy there on the understanding that he, Caleb Garth, should be responsible for the management? It would be an excellent schooling for Fred; he might make a modest income there, and still have time left to get knowledge by helping in other business. He mentioned his notion to Mrs. Garth with such evident delight that she could not bear to chill his pleasure by expressing her constant fear of his undertaking too much.\n\n“The lad would be as happy as two,” he said, throwing himself back in his chair, and looking radiant, “if I could tell him it was all settled. Think; Susan! His mind had been running on that place for years before old Featherstone died. And it would be as pretty a turn of things as could be that he should hold the place in a good industrious way after all—by his taking to business. For it’s likely enough Bulstrode might let him go on, and gradually buy the stock. He hasn’t made up his mind, I can see, whether or not he shall settle somewhere else as a lasting thing. I never was better pleased with a notion in my life. And then the children might be married by-and-by, Susan.”\n\n“You will not give any hint of the plan to Fred, until you are sure that Bulstrode would agree to the plan?” said Mrs. Garth, in a tone of gentle caution. “And as to marriage, Caleb, we old people need not help to hasten it.”\n\n“Oh, I don’t know,” said Caleb, swinging his head aside. “Marriage is a taming thing. Fred would want less of my bit and bridle. However, I shall say nothing till I know the ground I’m treading on. I shall speak to Bulstrode again.”\n\nHe took his earliest opportunity of doing so. Bulstrode had anything but a warm interest in his nephew Fred Vincy, but he had a strong wish to secure Mr. Garth’s services on many scattered points of business at which he was sure to be a considerable loser, if they were under less conscientious management. On that ground he made no objection to Mr. Garth’s proposal; and there was also another reason why he was not sorry to give a consent which was to benefit one of the Vincy family. It was that Mrs. Bulstrode, having heard of Lydgate’s debts, had been anxious to know whether her husband could not do something for poor Rosamond, and had been much troubled on learning from him that Lydgate’s affairs were not easily remediable, and that the wisest plan was to let them “take their course.” Mrs. Bulstrode had then said for the first time, “I think you are always a little hard towards my family, Nicholas. And I am sure I have no reason to deny any of my relatives. Too worldly they may be, but no one ever had to say that they were not respectable.”\n\n“My dear Harriet,” said Mr. Bulstrode, wincing under his wife’s eyes, which were filling with tears, “I have supplied your brother with a great deal of capital. I cannot be expected to take care of his married children.”\n\nThat seemed to be true, and Mrs. Bulstrode’s remonstrance subsided into pity for poor Rosamond, whose extravagant education she had always foreseen the fruits of.\n\nBut remembering that dialogue, Mr. Bulstrode felt that when he had to talk to his wife fully about his plan of quitting Middlemarch, he should be glad to tell her that he had made an arrangement which might be for the good of her nephew Fred. At present he had merely mentioned to her that he thought of shutting up The Shrubs for a few months, and taking a house on the Southern Coast.\n\nHence Mr. Garth got the assurance he desired, namely, that in case of Bulstrode’s departure from Middlemarch for an indefinite time, Fred Vincy should be allowed to have the tenancy of Stone Court on the terms proposed.\n\nCaleb was so elated with his hope of this “neat turn” being given to things, that if his self-control had not been braced by a little affectionate wifely scolding, he would have betrayed everything to Mary, wanting “to give the child comfort.” However, he restrained himself, and kept in strict privacy from Fred certain visits which he was making to Stone Court, in order to look more thoroughly into the state of the land and stock, and take a preliminary estimate. He was certainly more eager in these visits than the probable speed of events required him to be; but he was stimulated by a fatherly delight in occupying his mind with this bit of probable happiness which he held in store like a hidden birthday gift for Fred and Mary.\n\n“But suppose the whole scheme should turn out to be a castle in the air?” said Mrs. Garth.\n\n“Well, well,” replied Caleb; “the castle will tumble about nobody’s head.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXIX.\n\n“If thou hast heard a word, let it die with thee.” —Ecclesiasticus.\n\nMr. Bulstrode was still seated in his manager’s room at the Bank, about three o’clock of the same day on which he had received Lydgate there, when the clerk entered to say that his horse was waiting, and also that Mr. Garth was outside and begged to speak with him.\n\n“By all means,” said Bulstrode; and Caleb entered. “Pray sit down, Mr. Garth,” continued the banker, in his suavest tone.\n\n“I am glad that you arrived just in time to find me here. I know you count your minutes.”\n\n“Oh,” said Caleb, gently, with a slow swing of his head on one side, as he seated himself and laid his hat on the floor.\n\nHe looked at the ground, leaning forward and letting his long fingers droop between his legs, while each finger moved in succession, as if it were sharing some thought which filled his large quiet brow.\n\nMr. Bulstrode, like every one else who knew Caleb, was used to his slowness in beginning to speak on any topic which he felt to be important, and rather expected that he was about to recur to the buying of some houses in Blindman’s Court, for the sake of pulling them down, as a sacrifice of property which would be well repaid by the influx of air and light on that spot. It was by propositions of this kind that Caleb was sometimes troublesome to his employers; but he had usually found Bulstrode ready to meet him in projects of improvement, and they had got on well together. When he spoke again, however, it was to say, in rather a subdued voice—\n\n“I have just come away from Stone Court, Mr. Bulstrode.”\n\n“You found nothing wrong there, I hope,” said the banker; “I was there myself yesterday. Abel has done well with the lambs this year.”\n\n“Why, yes,” said Caleb, looking up gravely, “there is something wrong—a stranger, who is very ill, I think. He wants a doctor, and I came to tell you of that. His name is Raffles.”\n\nHe saw the shock of his words passing through Bulstrode’s frame. On this subject the banker had thought that his fears were too constantly on the watch to be taken by surprise; but he had been mistaken.\n\n“Poor wretch!” he said in a compassionate tone, though his lips trembled a little. “Do you know how he came there?”\n\n“I took him myself,” said Caleb, quietly—“took him up in my gig. He had got down from the coach, and was walking a little beyond the turning from the toll-house, and I overtook him. He remembered seeing me with you once before, at Stone Court, and he asked me to take him on. I saw he was ill: it seemed to me the right thing to do, to carry him under shelter. And now I think you should lose no time in getting advice for him.” Caleb took up his hat from the floor as he ended, and rose slowly from his seat.\n\n“Certainly,” said Bulstrode, whose mind was very active at this moment. “Perhaps you will yourself oblige me, Mr. Garth, by calling at Mr. Lydgate’s as you pass—or stay! he may at this hour probably be at the Hospital. I will first send my man on the horse there with a note this instant, and then I will myself ride to Stone Court.”\n\nBulstrode quickly wrote a note, and went out himself to give the commission to his man. When he returned, Caleb was standing as before with one hand on the back of the chair, holding his hat with the other. In Bulstrode’s mind the dominant thought was, “Perhaps Raffles only spoke to Garth of his illness. Garth may wonder, as he must have done before, at this disreputable fellow’s claiming intimacy with me; but he will know nothing. And he is friendly to me—I can be of use to him.”\n\nHe longed for some confirmation of this hopeful conjecture, but to have asked any question as to what Raffles had said or done would have been to betray fear.\n\n“I am exceedingly obliged to you, Mr. Garth,” he said, in his usual tone of politeness. “My servant will be back in a few minutes, and I shall then go myself to see what can be done for this unfortunate man. Perhaps you had some other business with me? If so, pray be seated.”\n\n“Thank you,” said Caleb, making a slight gesture with his right hand to waive the invitation. “I wish to say, Mr. Bulstrode, that I must request you to put your business into some other hands than mine. I am obliged to you for your handsome way of meeting me—about the letting of Stone Court, and all other business. But I must give it up.” A sharp certainty entered like a stab into Bulstrode’s soul.\n\n“This is sudden, Mr. Garth,” was all he could say at first.\n\n“It is,” said Caleb; “but it is quite fixed. I must give it up.”\n\nHe spoke with a firmness which was very gentle, and yet he could see that Bulstrode seemed to cower under that gentleness, his face looking dried and his eyes swerving away from the glance which rested on him. Caleb felt a deep pity for him, but he could have used no pretexts to account for his resolve, even if they would have been of any use.\n\n“You have been led to this, I apprehend, by some slanders concerning me uttered by that unhappy creature,” said Bulstrode, anxious now to know the utmost.\n\n“That is true. I can’t deny that I act upon what I heard from him.”\n\n“You are a conscientious man, Mr. Garth—a man, I trust, who feels himself accountable to God. You would not wish to injure me by being too ready to believe a slander,” said Bulstrode, casting about for pleas that might be adapted to his hearer’s mind. “That is a poor reason for giving up a connection which I think I may say will be mutually beneficial.”\n\n“I would injure no man if I could help it,” said Caleb; “even if I thought God winked at it. I hope I should have a feeling for my fellow-creature. But, sir—I am obliged to believe that this Raffles has told me the truth. And I can’t be happy in working with you, or profiting by you. It hurts my mind. I must beg you to seek another agent.”\n\n“Very well, Mr. Garth. But I must at least claim to know the worst that he has told you. I must know what is the foul speech that I am liable to be the victim of,” said Bulstrode, a certain amount of anger beginning to mingle with his humiliation before this quiet man who renounced his benefits.\n\n“That’s needless,” said Caleb, waving his hand, bowing his head slightly, and not swerving from the tone which had in it the merciful intention to spare this pitiable man. “What he has said to me will never pass from my lips, unless something now unknown forces it from me. If you led a harmful life for gain, and kept others out of their rights by deceit, to get the more for yourself, I dare say you repent—you would like to go back, and can’t: that must be a bitter thing”—Caleb paused a moment and shook his head—“it is not for me to make your life harder to you.”\n\n“But you do—you do make it harder to me,” said Bulstrode constrained into a genuine, pleading cry. “You make it harder to me by turning your back on me.”\n\n“That I’m forced to do,” said Caleb, still more gently, lifting up his hand. “I am sorry. I don’t judge you and say, he is wicked, and I am righteous. God forbid. I don’t know everything. A man may do wrong, and his will may rise clear out of it, though he can’t get his life clear. That’s a bad punishment. If it is so with you,—well, I’m very sorry for you. But I have that feeling inside me, that I can’t go on working with you. That’s all, Mr. Bulstrode. Everything else is buried, so far as my will goes. And I wish you good-day.”\n\n“One moment, Mr. Garth!” said Bulstrode, hurriedly. “I may trust then to your solemn assurance that you will not repeat either to man or woman what—even if it have any degree of truth in it—is yet a malicious representation?” Caleb’s wrath was stirred, and he said, indignantly—\n\n“Why should I have said it if I didn’t mean it? I am in no fear of you. Such tales as that will never tempt my tongue.”\n\n“Excuse me—I am agitated—I am the victim of this abandoned man.”\n\n“Stop a bit! you have got to consider whether you didn’t help to make him worse, when you profited by his vices.”\n\n“You are wronging me by too readily believing him,” said Bulstrode, oppressed, as by a nightmare, with the inability to deny flatly what Raffles might have said; and yet feeling it an escape that Caleb had not so stated it to him as to ask for that flat denial.\n\n“No,” said Caleb, lifting his hand deprecatingly; “I am ready to believe better, when better is proved. I rob you of no good chance. As to speaking, I hold it a crime to expose a man’s sin unless I’m clear it must be done to save the innocent. That is my way of thinking, Mr. Bulstrode, and what I say, I’ve no need to swear. I wish you good-day.”\n\nSome hours later, when he was at home, Caleb said to his wife, incidentally, that he had had some little differences with Bulstrode, and that in consequence, he had given up all notion of taking Stone Court, and indeed had resigned doing further business for him.\n\n“He was disposed to interfere too much, was he?” said Mrs. Garth, imagining that her husband had been touched on his sensitive point, and not been allowed to do what he thought right as to materials and modes of work.\n\n“Oh,” said Caleb, bowing his head and waving his hand gravely. And Mrs. Garth knew that this was a sign of his not intending to speak further on the subject.\n\nAs for Bulstrode, he had almost immediately mounted his horse and set off for Stone Court, being anxious to arrive there before Lydgate.\n\nHis mind was crowded with images and conjectures, which were a language to his hopes and fears, just as we hear tones from the vibrations which shake our whole system. The deep humiliation with which he had winced under Caleb Garth’s knowledge of his past and rejection of his patronage, alternated with and almost gave way to the sense of safety in the fact that Garth, and no other, had been the man to whom Raffles had spoken. It seemed to him a sort of earnest that Providence intended his rescue from worse consequences; the way being thus left open for the hope of secrecy. That Raffles should be afflicted with illness, that he should have been led to Stone Court rather than elsewhere—Bulstrode’s heart fluttered at the vision of probabilities which these events conjured up. If it should turn out that he was freed from all danger of disgrace—if he could breathe in perfect liberty—his life should be more consecrated than it had ever been before. He mentally lifted up this vow as if it would urge the result he longed for—he tried to believe in the potency of that prayerful resolution—its potency to determine death. He knew that he ought to say, “Thy will be done;” and he said it often. But the intense desire remained that the will of God might be the death of that hated man.\n\nYet when he arrived at Stone Court he could not see the change in Raffles without a shock. But for his pallor and feebleness, Bulstrode would have called the change in him entirely mental. Instead of his loud tormenting mood, he showed an intense, vague terror, and seemed to deprecate Bulstrode’s anger, because the money was all gone—he had been robbed—it had half of it been taken from him. He had only come here because he was ill and somebody was hunting him—somebody was after him, he had told nobody anything, he had kept his mouth shut. Bulstrode, not knowing the significance of these symptoms, interpreted this new nervous susceptibility into a means of alarming Raffles into true confessions, and taxed him with falsehood in saying that he had not told anything, since he had just told the man who took him up in his gig and brought him to Stone Court. Raffles denied this with solemn adjurations; the fact being that the links of consciousness were interrupted in him, and that his minute terror-stricken narrative to Caleb Garth had been delivered under a set of visionary impulses which had dropped back into darkness.\n\nBulstrode’s heart sank again at this sign that he could get no grasp over the wretched man’s mind, and that no word of Raffles could be trusted as to the fact which he most wanted to know, namely, whether or not he had really kept silence to every one in the neighborhood except Caleb Garth. The housekeeper had told him without the least constraint of manner that since Mr. Garth left, Raffles had asked her for beer, and after that had not spoken, seeming very ill. On that side it might be concluded that there had been no betrayal. Mrs. Abel thought, like the servants at The Shrubs, that the strange man belonged to the unpleasant “kin” who are among the troubles of the rich; she had at first referred the kinship to Mr. Rigg, and where there was property left, the buzzing presence of such large blue-bottles seemed natural enough. How he could be “kin” to Bulstrode as well was not so clear, but Mrs. Abel agreed with her husband that there was “no knowing,” a proposition which had a great deal of mental food for her, so that she shook her head over it without further speculation.\n\nIn less than an hour Lydgate arrived. Bulstrode met him outside the wainscoted parlor, where Raffles was, and said—\n\n“I have called you in, Mr. Lydgate, to an unfortunate man who was once in my employment, many years ago. Afterwards he went to America, and returned I fear to an idle dissolute life. Being destitute, he has a claim on me. He was slightly connected with Rigg, the former owner of this place, and in consequence found his way here. I believe he is seriously ill: apparently his mind is affected. I feel bound to do the utmost for him.”\n\nLydgate, who had the remembrance of his last conversation with Bulstrode strongly upon him, was not disposed to say an unnecessary word to him, and bowed slightly in answer to this account; but just before entering the room he turned automatically and said, “What is his name?”—to know names being as much a part of the medical man’s accomplishment as of the practical politician’s.\n\n“Raffles, John Raffles,” said Bulstrode, who hoped that whatever became of Raffles, Lydgate would never know any more of him.\n\nWhen he had thoroughly examined and considered the patient, Lydgate ordered that he should go to bed, and be kept there in as complete quiet as possible, and then went with Bulstrode into another room.\n\n“It is a serious case, I apprehend,” said the banker, before Lydgate began to speak.\n\n“No—and yes,” said Lydgate, half dubiously. “It is difficult to decide as to the possible effect of long-standing complications; but the man had a robust constitution to begin with. I should not expect this attack to be fatal, though of course the system is in a ticklish state. He should be well watched and attended to.”\n\n“I will remain here myself,” said Bulstrode. “Mrs. Abel and her husband are inexperienced. I can easily remain here for the night, if you will oblige me by taking a note for Mrs. Bulstrode.”\n\n“I should think that is hardly necessary,” said Lydgate. “He seems tame and terrified enough. He might become more unmanageable. But there is a man here—is there not?”\n\n“I have more than once stayed here a few nights for the sake of seclusion,” said Bulstrode, indifferently; “I am quite disposed to do so now. Mrs. Abel and her husband can relieve or aid me, if necessary.”\n\n“Very well. Then I need give my directions only to you,” said Lydgate, not feeling surprised at a little peculiarity in Bulstrode.\n\n“You think, then, that the case is hopeful?” said Bulstrode, when Lydgate had ended giving his orders.\n\n“Unless there turn out to be further complications, such as I have not at present detected—yes,” said Lydgate. “He may pass on to a worse stage; but I should not wonder if he got better in a few days, by adhering to the treatment I have prescribed. There must be firmness. Remember, if he calls for liquors of any sort, not to give them to him. In my opinion, men in his condition are oftener killed by treatment than by the disease. Still, new symptoms may arise. I shall come again to-morrow morning.”\n\nAfter waiting for the note to be carried to Mrs. Bulstrode, Lydgate rode away, forming no conjectures, in the first instance, about the history of Raffles, but rehearsing the whole argument, which had lately been much stirred by the publication of Dr. Ware’s abundant experience in America, as to the right way of treating cases of alcoholic poisoning such as this. Lydgate, when abroad, had already been interested in this question: he was strongly convinced against the prevalent practice of allowing alcohol and persistently administering large doses of opium; and he had repeatedly acted on this conviction with a favorable result.\n\n“The man is in a diseased state,” he thought, “but there’s a good deal of wear in him still. I suppose he is an object of charity to Bulstrode. It is curious what patches of hardness and tenderness lie side by side in men’s dispositions. Bulstrode seems the most unsympathetic fellow I ever saw about some people, and yet he has taken no end of trouble, and spent a great deal of money, on benevolent objects. I suppose he has some test by which he finds out whom Heaven cares for—he has made up his mind that it doesn’t care for me.”\n\nThis streak of bitterness came from a plenteous source, and kept widening in the current of his thought as he neared Lowick Gate. He had not been there since his first interview with Bulstrode in the morning, having been found at the Hospital by the banker’s messenger; and for the first time he was returning to his home without the vision of any expedient in the background which left him a hope of raising money enough to deliver him from the coming destitution of everything which made his married life tolerable—everything which saved him and Rosamond from that bare isolation in which they would be forced to recognize how little of a comfort they could be to each other. It was more bearable to do without tenderness for himself than to see that his own tenderness could make no amends for the lack of other things to her. The sufferings of his own pride from humiliations past and to come were keen enough, yet they were hardly distinguishable to himself from that more acute pain which dominated them—the pain of foreseeing that Rosamond would come to regard him chiefly as the cause of disappointment and unhappiness to her. He had never liked the makeshifts of poverty, and they had never before entered into his prospects for himself; but he was beginning now to imagine how two creatures who loved each other, and had a stock of thoughts in common, might laugh over their shabby furniture, and their calculations how far they could afford butter and eggs. But the glimpse of that poetry seemed as far off from him as the carelessness of the golden age; in poor Rosamond’s mind there was not room enough for luxuries to look small in. He got down from his horse in a very sad mood, and went into the house, not expecting to be cheered except by his dinner, and reflecting that before the evening closed it would be wise to tell Rosamond of his application to Bulstrode and its failure. It would be well not to lose time in preparing her for the worst.\n\nBut his dinner waited long for him before he was able to eat it. For on entering he found that Dover’s agent had already put a man in the house, and when he asked where Mrs. Lydgate was, he was told that she was in her bedroom. He went up and found her stretched on the bed pale and silent, without an answer even in her face to any word or look of his. He sat down by the bed and leaning over her said with almost a cry of prayer—\n\n“Forgive me for this misery, my poor Rosamond! Let us only love one another.”\n\nShe looked at him silently, still with the blank despair on her face; but then the tears began to fill her blue eyes, and her lip trembled. The strong man had had too much to bear that day. He let his head fall beside hers and sobbed.\n\nHe did not hinder her from going to her father early in the morning—it seemed now that he ought not to hinder her from doing as she pleased. In half an hour she came back, and said that papa and mamma wished her to go and stay with them while things were in this miserable state. Papa said he could do nothing about the debt—if he paid this, there would be half-a-dozen more. She had better come back home again till Lydgate had got a comfortable home for her. “Do you object, Tertius?”\n\n“Do as you like,” said Lydgate. “But things are not coming to a crisis immediately. There is no hurry.”\n\n“I should not go till to-morrow,” said Rosamond; “I shall want to pack my clothes.”\n\n“Oh, I would wait a little longer than to-morrow—there is no knowing what may happen,” said Lydgate, with bitter irony. “I may get my neck broken, and that may make things easier to you.”\n\nIt was Lydgate’s misfortune and Rosamond’s too, that his tenderness towards her, which was both an emotional prompting and a well-considered resolve, was inevitably interrupted by these outbursts of indignation either ironical or remonstrant. She thought them totally unwarranted, and the repulsion which this exceptional severity excited in her was in danger of making the more persistent tenderness unacceptable.\n\n“I see you do not wish me to go,” she said, with chill mildness; “why can you not say so, without that kind of violence? I shall stay until you request me to do otherwise.”\n\nLydgate said no more, but went out on his rounds. He felt bruised and shattered, and there was a dark line under his eyes which Rosamond had not seen before. She could not bear to look at him. Tertius had a way of taking things which made them a great deal worse for her.\n\n# CHAPTER LXX.\n\n“Our deeds still travel with us from afar, And what we have been makes us what we are.”\n\nBulstrode’s first object after Lydgate had left Stone Court was to examine Raffles’s pockets, which he imagined were sure to carry signs in the shape of hotel-bills of the places he had stopped in, if he had not told the truth in saying that he had come straight from Liverpool because he was ill and had no money. There were various bills crammed into his pocketbook, but none of a later date than Christmas at any other place, except one, which bore date that morning. This was crumpled up with a hand-bill about a horse-fair in one of his tail-pockets, and represented the cost of three days’ stay at an inn at Bilkley, where the fair was held—a town at least forty miles from Middlemarch. The bill was heavy, and since Raffles had no luggage with him, it seemed probable that he had left his portmanteau behind in payment, in order to save money for his travelling fare; for his purse was empty, and he had only a couple of sixpences and some loose pence in his pockets.\n\nBulstrode gathered a sense of safety from these indications that Raffles had really kept at a distance from Middlemarch since his memorable visit at Christmas. At a distance and among people who were strangers to Bulstrode, what satisfaction could there be to Raffles’s tormenting, self-magnifying vein in telling old scandalous stories about a Middlemarch banker? And what harm if he did talk? The chief point now was to keep watch over him as long as there was any danger of that intelligible raving, that unaccountable impulse to tell, which seemed to have acted towards Caleb Garth; and Bulstrode felt much anxiety lest some such impulse should come over him at the sight of Lydgate. He sat up alone with him through the night, only ordering the housekeeper to lie down in her clothes, so as to be ready when he called her, alleging his own indisposition to sleep, and his anxiety to carry out the doctor’s orders. He did carry them out faithfully, although Raffles was incessantly asking for brandy, and declaring that he was sinking away—that the earth was sinking away from under him. He was restless and sleepless, but still quailing and manageable. On the offer of the food ordered by Lydgate, which he refused, and the denial of other things which he demanded, he seemed to concentrate all his terror on Bulstrode, imploringly deprecating his anger, his revenge on him by starvation, and declaring with strong oaths that he had never told any mortal a word against him. Even this Bulstrode felt that he would not have liked Lydgate to hear; but a more alarming sign of fitful alternation in his delirium was, that in-the morning twilight Raffles suddenly seemed to imagine a doctor present, addressing him and declaring that Bulstrode wanted to starve him to death out of revenge for telling, when he never had told.\n\nBulstrode’s native imperiousness and strength of determination served him well. This delicate-looking man, himself nervously perturbed, found the needed stimulus in his strenuous circumstances, and through that difficult night and morning, while he had the air of an animated corpse returned to movement without warmth, holding the mastery by its chill impassibility, his mind was intensely at work thinking of what he had to guard against and what would win him security. Whatever prayers he might lift up, whatever statements he might inwardly make of this man’s wretched spiritual condition, and the duty he himself was under to submit to the punishment divinely appointed for him rather than to wish for evil to another—through all this effort to condense words into a solid mental state, there pierced and spread with irresistible vividness the images of the events he desired. And in the train of those images came their apology. He could not but see the death of Raffles, and see in it his own deliverance. What was the removal of this wretched creature? He was impenitent—but were not public criminals impenitent?—yet the law decided on their fate. Should Providence in this case award death, there was no sin in contemplating death as the desirable issue—if he kept his hands from hastening it—if he scrupulously did what was prescribed. Even here there might be a mistake: human prescriptions were fallible things: Lydgate had said that treatment had hastened death,—why not his own method of treatment? But of course intention was everything in the question of right and wrong.\n\nAnd Bulstrode set himself to keep his intention separate from his desire. He inwardly declared that he intended to obey orders. Why should he have got into any argument about the validity of these orders? It was only the common trick of desire—which avails itself of any irrelevant scepticism, finding larger room for itself in all uncertainty about effects, in every obscurity that looks like the absence of law. Still, he did obey the orders.\n\nHis anxieties continually glanced towards Lydgate, and his remembrance of what had taken place between them the morning before was accompanied with sensibilities which had not been roused at all during the actual scene. He had then cared but little about Lydgate’s painful impressions with regard to the suggested change in the Hospital, or about the disposition towards himself which what he held to be his justifiable refusal of a rather exorbitant request might call forth. He recurred to the scene now with a perception that he had probably made Lydgate his enemy, and with an awakened desire to propitiate him, or rather to create in him a strong sense of personal obligation. He regretted that he had not at once made even an unreasonable money-sacrifice. For in case of unpleasant suspicions, or even knowledge gathered from the raving of Raffles, Bulstrode would have felt that he had a defence in Lydgate’s mind by having conferred a momentous benefit on him. But the regret had perhaps come too late.\n\nStrange, piteous conflict in the soul of this unhappy man, who had longed for years to be better than he was—who had taken his selfish passions into discipline and clad them in severe robes, so that he had walked with them as a devout choir, till now that a terror had risen among them, and they could chant no longer, but threw out their common cries for safety.\n\nIt was nearly the middle of the day before Lydgate arrived: he had meant to come earlier, but had been detained, he said; and his shattered looks were noticed by Balstrode. But he immediately threw himself into the consideration of the patient, and inquired strictly into all that had occurred. Raffles was worse, would take hardly any food, was persistently wakeful and restlessly raving; but still not violent. Contrary to Bulstrode’s alarmed expectation, he took little notice of Lydgate’s presence, and continued to talk or murmur incoherently.\n\n“What do you think of him?” said Bulstrode, in private.\n\n“The symptoms are worse.”\n\n“You are less hopeful?”\n\n“No; I still think he may come round. Are you going to stay here yourself?” said Lydgate, looking at Bulstrode with an abrupt question, which made him uneasy, though in reality it was not due to any suspicious conjecture.\n\n“Yes, I think so,” said Bulstrode, governing himself and speaking with deliberation. “Mrs. Bulstrode is advised of the reasons which detain me. Mrs. Abel and her husband are not experienced enough to be left quite alone, and this kind of responsibility is scarcely included in their service of me. You have some fresh instructions, I presume.”\n\nThe chief new instruction that Lydgate had to give was on the administration of extremely moderate doses of opium, in case of the sleeplessness continuing after several hours. He had taken the precaution of bringing opium in his pocket, and he gave minute directions to Bulstrode as to the doses, and the point at which they should cease. He insisted on the risk of not ceasing; and repeated his order that no alcohol should be given.\n\n“From what I see of the case,” he ended, “narcotism is the only thing I should be much afraid of. He may wear through even without much food. There’s a good deal of strength in him.”\n\n“You look ill yourself, Mr. Lydgate—a most unusual, I may say unprecedented thing in my knowledge of you,” said Bulstrode, showing a solicitude as unlike his indifference the day before, as his present recklessness about his own fatigue was unlike his habitual self-cherishing anxiety. “I fear you are harassed.”\n\n“Yes, I am,” said Lydgate, brusquely, holding his hat, and ready to go.\n\n“Something new, I fear,” said Bulstrode, inquiringly. “Pray be seated.”\n\n“No, thank you,” said Lydgate, with some hauteur. “I mentioned to you yesterday what was the state of my affairs. There is nothing to add, except that the execution has since then been actually put into my house. One can tell a good deal of trouble in a short sentence. I will say good morning.”\n\n“Stay, Mr. Lydgate, stay,” said Bulstrode; “I have been reconsidering this subject. I was yesterday taken by surprise, and saw it superficially. Mrs. Bulstrode is anxious for her niece, and I myself should grieve at a calamitous change in your position. Claims on me are numerous, but on reconsideration, I esteem it right that I should incur a small sacrifice rather than leave you unaided. You said, I think, that a thousand pounds would suffice entirely to free you from your burthens, and enable you to recover a firm stand?”\n\n“Yes,” said Lydgate, a great leap of joy within him surmounting every other feeling; “that would pay all my debts, and leave me a little on hand. I could set about economizing in our way of living. And by-and-by my practice might look up.”\n\n“If you will wait a moment, Mr. Lydgate, I will draw a check to that amount. I am aware that help, to be effectual in these cases, should be thorough.”\n\nWhile Bulstrode wrote, Lydgate turned to the window thinking of his home—thinking of his life with its good start saved from frustration, its good purposes still unbroken.\n\n“You can give me a note of hand for this, Mr. Lydgate,” said the banker, advancing towards him with the check. “And by-and-by, I hope, you may be in circumstances gradually to repay me. Meanwhile, I have pleasure in thinking that you will be released from further difficulty.”\n\n“I am deeply obliged to you,” said Lydgate. “You have restored to me the prospect of working with some happiness and some chance of good.”\n\nIt appeared to him a very natural movement in Bulstrode that he should have reconsidered his refusal: it corresponded with the more munificent side of his character. But as he put his hack into a canter, that he might get the sooner home, and tell the good news to Rosamond, and get cash at the bank to pay over to Dover’s agent, there crossed his mind, with an unpleasant impression, as from a dark-winged flight of evil augury across his vision, the thought of that contrast in himself which a few months had brought—that he should be overjoyed at being under a strong personal obligation—that he should be overjoyed at getting money for himself from Bulstrode.\n\nThe banker felt that he had done something to nullify one cause of uneasiness, and yet he was scarcely the easier. He did not measure the quantity of diseased motive which had made him wish for Lydgate’s good-will, but the quantity was none the less actively there, like an irritating agent in his blood. A man vows, and yet will not cast away the means of breaking his vow. Is it that he distinctly means to break it? Not at all; but the desires which tend to break it are at work in him dimly, and make their way into his imagination, and relax his muscles in the very moments when he is telling himself over again the reasons for his vow. Raffles, recovering quickly, returning to the free use of his odious powers—how could Bulstrode wish for that? Raffles dead was the image that brought release, and indirectly he prayed for that way of release, beseeching that, if it were possible, the rest of his days here below might be freed from the threat of an ignominy which would break him utterly as an instrument of God’s service. Lydgate’s opinion was not on the side of promise that this prayer would be fulfilled; and as the day advanced, Bulstrode felt himself getting irritated at the persistent life in this man, whom he would fain have seen sinking into the silence of death: imperious will stirred murderous impulses towards this brute life, over which will, by itself, had no power. He said inwardly that he was getting too much worn; he would not sit up with the patient to-night, but leave him to Mrs. Abel, who, if necessary, could call her husband.\n\nAt six o’clock, Raffles, having had only fitful perturbed snatches of sleep, from which he waked with fresh restlessness and perpetual cries that he was sinking away, Bulstrode began to administer the opium according to Lydgate’s directions. At the end of half an hour or more he called Mrs. Abel and told her that he found himself unfit for further watching. He must now consign the patient to her care; and he proceeded to repeat to her Lydgate’s directions as to the quantity of each dose. Mrs. Abel had not before known anything of Lydgate’s prescriptions; she had simply prepared and brought whatever Bulstrode ordered, and had done what he pointed out to her. She began now to ask what else she should do besides administering the opium.\n\n“Nothing at present, except the offer of the soup or the soda-water: you can come to me for further directions. Unless there is any important change, I shall not come into the room again to-night. You will ask your husband for help if necessary. I must go to bed early.”\n\n“You’ve much need, sir, I’m sure,” said Mrs. Abel, “and to take something more strengthening than what you’ve done.”\n\nBulstrode went away now without anxiety as to what Raffles might say in his raving, which had taken on a muttering incoherence not likely to create any dangerous belief. At any rate he must risk this. He went down into the wainscoted parlor first, and began to consider whether he would not have his horse saddled and go home by the moonlight, and give up caring for earthly consequences. Then, he wished that he had begged Lydgate to come again that evening. Perhaps he might deliver a different opinion, and think that Raffles was getting into a less hopeful state. Should he send for Lydgate? If Raffles were really getting worse, and slowly dying, Bulstrode felt that he could go to bed and sleep in gratitude to Providence. But was he worse? Lydgate might come and simply say that he was going on as he expected, and predict that he would by-and-by fall into a good sleep, and get well. What was the use of sending for him? Bulstrode shrank from that result. No ideas or opinions could hinder him from seeing the one probability to be, that Raffles recovered would be just the same man as before, with his strength as a tormentor renewed, obliging him to drag away his wife to spend her years apart from her friends and native place, carrying an alienating suspicion against him in her heart.\n\nHe had sat an hour and a half in this conflict by the firelight only, when a sudden thought made him rise and light the bed-candle, which he had brought down with him. The thought was, that he had not told Mrs. Abel when the doses of opium must cease.\n\nHe took hold of the candlestick, but stood motionless for a long while. She might already have given him more than Lydgate had prescribed. But it was excusable in him, that he should forget part of an order, in his present wearied condition. He walked up-stairs, candle in hand, not knowing whether he should straightway enter his own room and go to bed, or turn to the patient’s room and rectify his omission. He paused in the passage, with his face turned towards Raffles’s room, and he could hear him moaning and murmuring. He was not asleep, then. Who could know that Lydgate’s prescription would not be better disobeyed than followed, since there was still no sleep?\n\nHe turned into his own room. Before he had quite undressed, Mrs. Abel rapped at the door; he opened it an inch, so that he could hear her speak low.\n\n“If you please, sir, should I have no brandy nor nothing to give the poor creetur? He feels sinking away, and nothing else will he swaller—and but little strength in it, if he did—only the opium. And he says more and more he’s sinking down through the earth.”\n\nTo her surprise, Mr. Bulstrode did not answer. A struggle was going on within him.\n\n“I think he must die for want o’ support, if he goes on in that way. When I nursed my poor master, Mr. Robisson, I had to give him port-wine and brandy constant, and a big glass at a time,” added Mrs. Abel, with a touch of remonstrance in her tone.\n\nBut again Mr. Bulstrode did not answer immediately, and she continued, “It’s not a time to spare when people are at death’s door, nor would you wish it, sir, I’m sure. Else I should give him our own bottle o’ rum as we keep by us. But a sitter-up so as you’ve been, and doing everything as laid in your power—”\n\nHere a key was thrust through the inch of doorway, and Mr. Bulstrode said huskily, “That is the key of the wine-cooler. You will find plenty of brandy there.”\n\nEarly in the morning—about six—Mr. Bulstrode rose and spent some time in prayer. Does any one suppose that private prayer is necessarily candid—necessarily goes to the roots of action? Private prayer is inaudible speech, and speech is representative: who can represent himself just as he is, even in his own reflections? Bulstrode had not yet unravelled in his thought the confused promptings of the last four-and-twenty hours.\n\nHe listened in the passage, and could hear hard stertorous breathing. Then he walked out in the garden, and looked at the early rime on the grass and fresh spring leaves. When he re-entered the house, he felt startled at the sight of Mrs. Abel.\n\n“How is your patient—asleep, I think?” he said, with an attempt at cheerfulness in his tone.\n\n“He’s gone very deep, sir,” said Mrs. Abel. “He went off gradual between three and four o’clock. Would you please to go and look at him? I thought it no harm to leave him. My man’s gone afield, and the little girl’s seeing to the kettles.”\n\nBulstrode went up. At a glance he knew that Raffles was not in the sleep which brings revival, but in the sleep which streams deeper and deeper into the gulf of death.\n\nHe looked round the room and saw a bottle with some brandy in it, and the almost empty opium phial. He put the phial out of sight, and carried the brandy-bottle down-stairs with him, locking it again in the wine-cooler.\n\nWhile breakfasting he considered whether he should ride to Middlemarch at once, or wait for Lydgate’s arrival. He decided to wait, and told Mrs. Abel that she might go about her work—he could watch in the bed-chamber.\n\nAs he sat there and beheld the enemy of his peace going irrevocably into silence, he felt more at rest than he had done for many months. His conscience was soothed by the enfolding wing of secrecy, which seemed just then like an angel sent down for his relief. He drew out his pocket-book to review various memoranda there as to the arrangements he had projected and partly carried out in the prospect of quitting Middlemarch, and considered how far he would let them stand or recall them, now that his absence would be brief. Some economies which he felt desirable might still find a suitable occasion in his temporary withdrawal from management, and he hoped still that Mrs. Casaubon would take a large share in the expenses of the Hospital. In that way the moments passed, until a change in the stertorous breathing was marked enough to draw his attention wholly to the bed, and forced him to think of the departing life, which had once been subservient to his own—which he had once been glad to find base enough for him to act on as he would. It was his gladness then which impelled him now to be glad that the life was at an end.\n\nAnd who could say that the death of Raffles had been hastened? Who knew what would have saved him?\n\nLydgate arrived at half-past ten, in time to witness the final pause of the breath. When he entered the room Bulstrode observed a sudden expression in his face, which was not so much surprise as a recognition that he had not judged correctly. He stood by the bed in silence for some time, with his eyes turned on the dying man, but with that subdued activity of expression which showed that he was carrying on an inward debate.\n\n“When did this change begin?” said he, looking at Bulstrode.\n\n“I did not watch by him last night,” said Bulstrode. “I was over-worn, and left him under Mrs. Abel’s care. She said that he sank into sleep between three and four o’clock. When I came in before eight he was nearly in this condition.”\n\nLydgate did not ask another question, but watched in silence until he said, “It’s all over.”\n\nThis morning Lydgate was in a state of recovered hope and freedom. He had set out on his work with all his old animation, and felt himself strong enough to bear all the deficiencies of his married life. And he was conscious that Bulstrode had been a benefactor to him. But he was uneasy about this case. He had not expected it to terminate as it had done. Yet he hardly knew how to put a question on the subject to Bulstrode without appearing to insult him; and if he examined the housekeeper—why, the man was dead. There seemed to be no use in implying that somebody’s ignorance or imprudence had killed him. And after all, he himself might be wrong.\n\nHe and Bulstrode rode back to Middlemarch together, talking of many things—chiefly cholera and the chances of the Reform Bill in the House of Lords, and the firm resolve of the political Unions. Nothing was said about Raffles, except that Bulstrode mentioned the necessity of having a grave for him in Lowick churchyard, and observed that, so far as he knew, the poor man had no connections, except Rigg, whom he had stated to be unfriendly towards him.\n\nOn returning home Lydgate had a visit from Mr. Farebrother. The Vicar had not been in the town the day before, but the news that there was an execution in Lydgate’s house had got to Lowick by the evening, having been carried by Mr. Spicer, shoemaker and parish-clerk, who had it from his brother, the respectable bell-hanger in Lowick Gate. Since that evening when Lydgate had come down from the billiard room with Fred Vincy, Mr. Farebrother’s thoughts about him had been rather gloomy. Playing at the Green Dragon once or oftener might have been a trifle in another man; but in Lydgate it was one of several signs that he was getting unlike his former self. He was beginning to do things for which he had formerly even an excessive scorn. Whatever certain dissatisfactions in marriage, which some silly tinklings of gossip had given him hints of, might have to do with this change, Mr. Farebrother felt sure that it was chiefly connected with the debts which were being more and more distinctly reported, and he began to fear that any notion of Lydgate’s having resources or friends in the background must be quite illusory. The rebuff he had met with in his first attempt to win Lydgate’s confidence, disinclined him to a second; but this news of the execution being actually in the house, determined the Vicar to overcome his reluctance.\n\nLydgate had just dismissed a poor patient, in whom he was much interested, and he came forward to put out his hand—with an open cheerfulness which surprised Mr. Farebrother. Could this too be a proud rejection of sympathy and help? Never mind; the sympathy and help should be offered.\n\n“How are you, Lydgate? I came to see you because I had heard something which made me anxious about you,” said the Vicar, in the tone of a good brother, only that there was no reproach in it. They were both seated by this time, and Lydgate answered immediately—\n\n“I think I know what you mean. You had heard that there was an execution in the house?”\n\n“Yes; is it true?”\n\n“It was true,” said Lydgate, with an air of freedom, as if he did not mind talking about the affair now. “But the danger is over; the debt is paid. I am out of my difficulties now: I shall be freed from debts, and able, I hope, to start afresh on a better plan.”\n\n“I am very thankful to hear it,” said the Vicar, falling back in his chair, and speaking with that low-toned quickness which often follows the removal of a load. “I like that better than all the news in the ‘Times.’ I confess I came to you with a heavy heart.”\n\n“Thank you for coming,” said Lydgate, cordially. “I can enjoy the kindness all the more because I am happier. I have certainly been a good deal crushed. I’m afraid I shall find the bruises still painful by-and by,” he added, smiling rather sadly; “but just now I can only feel that the torture-screw is off.”\n\nMr. Farebrother was silent for a moment, and then said earnestly, “My dear fellow, let me ask you one question. Forgive me if I take a liberty.”\n\n“I don’t believe you will ask anything that ought to offend me.”\n\n“Then—this is necessary to set my heart quite at rest—you have not—have you?—in order to pay your debts, incurred another debt which may harass you worse hereafter?”\n\n“No,” said Lydgate, coloring slightly. “There is no reason why I should not tell you—since the fact is so—that the person to whom I am indebted is Bulstrode. He has made me a very handsome advance—a thousand pounds—and he can afford to wait for repayment.”\n\n“Well, that is generous,” said Mr. Farebrother, compelling himself to approve of the man whom he disliked. His delicate feeling shrank from dwelling even in his thought on the fact that he had always urged Lydgate to avoid any personal entanglement with Bulstrode. He added immediately, “And Bulstrode must naturally feel an interest in your welfare, after you have worked with him in a way which has probably reduced your income instead of adding to it. I am glad to think that he has acted accordingly.”\n\nLydgate felt uncomfortable under these kindly suppositions. They made more distinct within him the uneasy consciousness which had shown its first dim stirrings only a few hours before, that Bulstrode’s motives for his sudden beneficence following close upon the chillest indifference might be merely selfish. He let the kindly suppositions pass. He could not tell the history of the loan, but it was more vividly present with him than ever, as well as the fact which the Vicar delicately ignored—that this relation of personal indebtedness to Bulstrode was what he had once been most resolved to avoid.\n\nHe began, instead of answering, to speak of his projected economies, and of his having come to look at his life from a different point of view.\n\n“I shall set up a surgery,” he said. “I really think I made a mistaken effort in that respect. And if Rosamond will not mind, I shall take an apprentice. I don’t like these things, but if one carries them out faithfully they are not really lowering. I have had a severe galling to begin with: that will make the small rubs seem easy.”\n\nPoor Lydgate! the “if Rosamond will not mind,” which had fallen from him involuntarily as part of his thought, was a significant mark of the yoke he bore. But Mr. Farebrother, whose hopes entered strongly into the same current with Lydgate’s, and who knew nothing about him that could now raise a melancholy presentiment, left him with affectionate congratulation.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXI.\n\nClown. . . . ’Twas in the Bunch of Grapes, where, indeed, you have a delight to sit, have you not? Froth. I have so: because it is an open room, and good for winter. Clo. Why, very well then: I hope here be truths. —Measure for Measure.\n\nFive days after the death of Raffles, Mr. Bambridge was standing at his leisure under the large archway leading into the yard of the Green Dragon. He was not fond of solitary contemplation, but he had only just come out of the house, and any human figure standing at ease under the archway in the early afternoon was as certain to attract companionship as a pigeon which has found something worth pecking at. In this case there was no material object to feed upon, but the eye of reason saw a probability of mental sustenance in the shape of gossip. Mr. Hopkins, the meek-mannered draper opposite, was the first to act on this inward vision, being the more ambitious of a little masculine talk because his customers were chiefly women. Mr. Bambridge was rather curt to the draper, feeling that Hopkins was of course glad to talk to him, but that he was not going to waste much of his talk on Hopkins. Soon, however, there was a small cluster of more important listeners, who were either deposited from the passers-by, or had sauntered to the spot expressly to see if there were anything going on at the Green Dragon; and Mr. Bambridge was finding it worth his while to say many impressive things about the fine studs he had been seeing and the purchases he had made on a journey in the north from which he had just returned. Gentlemen present were assured that when they could show him anything to cut out a blood mare, a bay, rising four, which was to be seen at Doncaster if they chose to go and look at it, Mr. Bambridge would gratify them by being shot “from here to Hereford.” Also, a pair of blacks which he was going to put into the break recalled vividly to his mind a pair which he had sold to Faulkner in ’19, for a hundred guineas, and which Faulkner had sold for a hundred and sixty two months later—any gent who could disprove this statement being offered the privilege of calling Mr. Bambridge by a very ugly name until the exercise made his throat dry.\n\nWhen the discourse was at this point of animation, came up Mr. Frank Hawley. He was not a man to compromise his dignity by lounging at the Green Dragon, but happening to pass along the High Street and seeing Bambridge on the other side, he took some of his long strides across to ask the horsedealer whether he had found the first-rate gig-horse which he had engaged to look for. Mr. Hawley was requested to wait until he had seen a gray selected at Bilkley: if that did not meet his wishes to a hair, Bambridge did not know a horse when he saw it, which seemed to be the highest conceivable unlikelihood. Mr. Hawley, standing with his back to the street, was fixing a time for looking at the gray and seeing it tried, when a horseman passed slowly by.\n\n“Bulstrode!” said two or three voices at once in a low tone, one of them, which was the draper’s, respectfully prefixing the “Mr.;” but nobody having more intention in this interjectural naming than if they had said “the Riverston coach” when that vehicle appeared in the distance. Mr. Hawley gave a careless glance round at Bulstrode’s back, but as Bambridge’s eyes followed it he made a sarcastic grimace.\n\n“By jingo! that reminds me,” he began, lowering his voice a little, “I picked up something else at Bilkley besides your gig-horse, Mr. Hawley. I picked up a fine story about Bulstrode. Do you know how he came by his fortune? Any gentleman wanting a bit of curious information, I can give it him free of expense. If everybody got their deserts, Bulstrode might have had to say his prayers at Botany Bay.”\n\n“What do you mean?” said Mr. Hawley, thrusting his hands into his pockets, and pushing a little forward under the archway. If Bulstrode should turn out to be a rascal, Frank Hawley had a prophetic soul.\n\n“I had it from a party who was an old chum of Bulstrode’s. I’ll tell you where I first picked him up,” said Bambridge, with a sudden gesture of his fore-finger. “He was at Larcher’s sale, but I knew nothing of him then—he slipped through my fingers—was after Bulstrode, no doubt. He tells me he can tap Bulstrode to any amount, knows all his secrets. However, he blabbed to me at Bilkley: he takes a stiff glass. Damme if I think he meant to turn king’s evidence; but he’s that sort of bragging fellow, the bragging runs over hedge and ditch with him, till he’d brag of a spavin as if it ’ud fetch money. A man should know when to pull up.” Mr. Bambridge made this remark with an air of disgust, satisfied that his own bragging showed a fine sense of the marketable.\n\n“What’s the man’s name? Where can he be found?” said Mr. Hawley.\n\n“As to where he is to be found, I left him to it at the Saracen’s Head; but his name is Raffles.”\n\n“Raffles!” exclaimed Mr. Hopkins. “I furnished his funeral yesterday. He was buried at Lowick. Mr. Bulstrode followed him. A very decent funeral.” There was a strong sensation among the listeners. Mr. Bambridge gave an ejaculation in which “brimstone” was the mildest word, and Mr. Hawley, knitting his brows and bending his head forward, exclaimed, “What?—where did the man die?”\n\n“At Stone Court,” said the draper. “The housekeeper said he was a relation of the master’s. He came there ill on Friday.”\n\n“Why, it was on Wednesday I took a glass with him,” interposed Bambridge.\n\n“Did any doctor attend him?” said Mr. Hawley\n\n“Yes. Mr. Lydgate. Mr. Bulstrode sat up with him one night. He died the third morning.”\n\n“Go on, Bambridge,” said Mr. Hawley, insistently. “What did this fellow say about Bulstrode?”\n\nThe group had already become larger, the town-clerk’s presence being a guarantee that something worth listening to was going on there; and Mr. Bambridge delivered his narrative in the hearing of seven. It was mainly what we know, including the fact about Will Ladislaw, with some local color and circumstance added: it was what Bulstrode had dreaded the betrayal of—and hoped to have buried forever with the corpse of Raffles—it was that haunting ghost of his earlier life which as he rode past the archway of the Green Dragon he was trusting that Providence had delivered him from. Yes, Providence. He had not confessed to himself yet that he had done anything in the way of contrivance to this end; he had accepted what seemed to have been offered. It was impossible to prove that he had done anything which hastened the departure of that man’s soul.\n\nBut this gossip about Bulstrode spread through Middlemarch like the smell of fire. Mr. Frank Hawley followed up his information by sending a clerk whom he could trust to Stone Court on a pretext of inquiring about hay, but really to gather all that could be learned about Raffles and his illness from Mrs. Abel. In this way it came to his knowledge that Mr. Garth had carried the man to Stone Court in his gig; and Mr. Hawley in consequence took an opportunity of seeing Caleb, calling at his office to ask whether he had time to undertake an arbitration if it were required, and then asking him incidentally about Raffles. Caleb was betrayed into no word injurious to Bulstrode beyond the fact which he was forced to admit, that he had given up acting for him within the last week. Mr Hawley drew his inferences, and feeling convinced that Raffles had told his story to Garth, and that Garth had given up Bulstrode’s affairs in consequence, said so a few hours later to Mr. Toller. The statement was passed on until it had quite lost the stamp of an inference, and was taken as information coming straight from Garth, so that even a diligent historian might have concluded Caleb to be the chief publisher of Bulstrode’s misdemeanors.\n\nMr. Hawley was not slow to perceive that there was no handle for the law either in the revelations made by Raffles or in the circumstances of his death. He had himself ridden to Lowick village that he might look at the register and talk over the whole matter with Mr. Farebrother, who was not more surprised than the lawyer that an ugly secret should have come to light about Bulstrode, though he had always had justice enough in him to hinder his antipathy from turning into conclusions. But while they were talking another combination was silently going forward in Mr. Farebrother’s mind, which foreshadowed what was soon to be loudly spoken of in Middlemarch as a necessary “putting of two and two together.” With the reasons which kept Bulstrode in dread of Raffles there flashed the thought that the dread might have something to do with his munificence towards his medical man; and though he resisted the suggestion that it had been consciously accepted in any way as a bribe, he had a foreboding that this complication of things might be of malignant effect on Lydgate’s reputation. He perceived that Mr. Hawley knew nothing at present of the sudden relief from debt, and he himself was careful to glide away from all approaches towards the subject.\n\n“Well,” he said, with a deep breath, wanting to wind up the illimitable discussion of what might have been, though nothing could be legally proven, “it is a strange story. So our mercurial Ladislaw has a queer genealogy! A high-spirited young lady and a musical Polish patriot made a likely enough stock for him to spring from, but I should never have suspected a grafting of the Jew pawnbroker. However, there’s no knowing what a mixture will turn out beforehand. Some sorts of dirt serve to clarify.”\n\n“It’s just what I should have expected,” said Mr. Hawley, mounting his horse. “Any cursed alien blood, Jew, Corsican, or Gypsy.”\n\n“I know he’s one of your black sheep, Hawley. But he is really a disinterested, unworldly fellow,” said Mr. Farebrother, smiling.\n\n“Ay, ay, that is your Whiggish twist,” said Mr. Hawley, who had been in the habit of saying apologetically that Farebrother was such a damned pleasant good-hearted fellow you would mistake him for a Tory.\n\nMr. Hawley rode home without thinking of Lydgate’s attendance on Raffles in any other light than as a piece of evidence on the side of Bulstrode. But the news that Lydgate had all at once become able not only to get rid of the execution in his house but to pay all his debts in Middlemarch was spreading fast, gathering round it conjectures and comments which gave it new body and impetus, and soon filling the ears of other persons besides Mr. Hawley, who were not slow to see a significant relation between this sudden command of money and Bulstrode’s desire to stifle the scandal of Raffles. That the money came from Bulstrode would infallibly have been guessed even if there had been no direct evidence of it; for it had beforehand entered into the gossip about Lydgate’s affairs, that neither his father-in-law nor his own family would do anything for him, and direct evidence was furnished not only by a clerk at the Bank, but by innocent Mrs. Bulstrode herself, who mentioned the loan to Mrs. Plymdale, who mentioned it to her daughter-in-law of the house of Toller, who mentioned it generally. The business was felt to be so public and important that it required dinners to feed it, and many invitations were just then issued and accepted on the strength of this scandal concerning Bulstrode and Lydgate; wives, widows, and single ladies took their work and went out to tea oftener than usual; and all public conviviality, from the Green Dragon to Dollop’s, gathered a zest which could not be won from the question whether the Lords would throw out the Reform Bill.\n\nFor hardly anybody doubted that some scandalous reason or other was at the bottom of Bulstrode’s liberality to Lydgate. Mr. Hawley indeed, in the first instance, invited a select party, including the two physicians, with Mr Toller and Mr. Wrench, expressly to hold a close discussion as to the probabilities of Raffles’s illness, reciting to them all the particulars which had been gathered from Mrs. Abel in connection with Lydgate’s certificate, that the death was due to delirium tremens; and the medical gentlemen, who all stood undisturbedly on the old paths in relation to this disease, declared that they could see nothing in these particulars which could be transformed into a positive ground of suspicion. But the moral grounds of suspicion remained: the strong motives Bulstrode clearly had for wishing to be rid of Raffles, and the fact that at this critical moment he had given Lydgate the help which he must for some time have known the need for; the disposition, moreover, to believe that Bulstrode would be unscrupulous, and the absence of any indisposition to believe that Lydgate might be as easily bribed as other haughty-minded men when they have found themselves in want of money. Even if the money had been given merely to make him hold his tongue about the scandal of Bulstrode’s earlier life, the fact threw an odious light on Lydgate, who had long been sneered at as making himself subservient to the banker for the sake of working himself into predominance, and discrediting the elder members of his profession. Hence, in spite of the negative as to any direct sign of guilt in relation to the death at Stone Court, Mr. Hawley’s select party broke up with the sense that the affair had “an ugly look.”\n\nBut this vague conviction of indeterminable guilt, which was enough to keep up much head-shaking and biting innuendo even among substantial professional seniors, had for the general mind all the superior power of mystery over fact. Everybody liked better to conjecture how the thing was, than simply to know it; for conjecture soon became more confident than knowledge, and had a more liberal allowance for the incompatible. Even the more definite scandal concerning Bulstrode’s earlier life was, for some minds, melted into the mass of mystery, as so much lively metal to be poured out in dialogue, and to take such fantastic shapes as heaven pleased.\n\nThis was the tone of thought chiefly sanctioned by Mrs. Dollop, the spirited landlady of the Tankard in Slaughter Lane, who had often to resist the shallow pragmatism of customers disposed to think that their reports from the outer world were of equal force with what had “come up” in her mind. How it had been brought to her she didn’t know, but it was there before her as if it had been “scored with the chalk on the chimney-board—” as Bulstrode should say, “his inside was that black as if the hairs of his head knowed the thoughts of his heart, he’d tear ’em up by the roots.”\n\n“That’s odd,” said Mr. Limp, a meditative shoemaker, with weak eyes and a piping voice. “Why, I read in the ‘Trumpet’ that was what the Duke of Wellington said when he turned his coat and went over to the Romans.”\n\n“Very like,” said Mrs. Dollop. “If one raskill said it, it’s more reason why another should. But hypocrite as he’s been, and holding things with that high hand, as there was no parson i’ the country good enough for him, he was forced to take Old Harry into his counsel, and Old Harry’s been too many for him.”\n\n“Ay, ay, he’s a ’complice you can’t send out o’ the country,” said Mr. Crabbe, the glazier, who gathered much news and groped among it dimly. “But by what I can make out, there’s them says Bulstrode was for running away, for fear o’ being found out, before now.”\n\n“He’ll be drove away, whether or no,” said Mr. Dill, the barber, who had just dropped in. “I shaved Fletcher, Hawley’s clerk, this morning—he’s got a bad finger—and he says they’re all of one mind to get rid of Bulstrode. Mr. Thesiger is turned against him, and wants him out o’ the parish. And there’s gentlemen in this town says they’d as soon dine with a fellow from the hulks. ‘And a deal sooner I would,’ says Fletcher; ‘for what’s more against one’s stomach than a man coming and making himself bad company with his religion, and giving out as the Ten Commandments are not enough for him, and all the while he’s worse than half the men at the tread-mill?’ Fletcher said so himself.”\n\n“It’ll be a bad thing for the town though, if Bulstrode’s money goes out of it,” said Mr. Limp, quaveringly.\n\n“Ah, there’s better folks spend their money worse,” said a firm-voiced dyer, whose crimson hands looked out of keeping with his good-natured face.\n\n“But he won’t keep his money, by what I can make out,” said the glazier. “Don’t they say as there’s somebody can strip it off him? By what I can understan’, they could take every penny off him, if they went to lawing.”\n\n“No such thing!” said the barber, who felt himself a little above his company at Dollop’s, but liked it none the worse. “Fletcher says it’s no such thing. He says they might prove over and over again whose child this young Ladislaw was, and they’d do no more than if they proved I came out of the Fens—he couldn’t touch a penny.”\n\n“Look you there now!” said Mrs. Dollop, indignantly. “I thank the Lord he took my children to Himself, if that’s all the law can do for the motherless. Then by that, it’s o’ no use who your father and mother is. But as to listening to what one lawyer says without asking another—I wonder at a man o’ your cleverness, Mr. Dill. It’s well known there’s always two sides, if no more; else who’d go to law, I should like to know? It’s a poor tale, with all the law as there is up and down, if it’s no use proving whose child you are. Fletcher may say that if he likes, but I say, don’t Fletcher me!”\n\nMr. Dill affected to laugh in a complimentary way at Mrs. Dollop, as a woman who was more than a match for the lawyers; being disposed to submit to much twitting from a landlady who had a long score against him.\n\n“If they come to lawing, and it’s all true as folks say, there’s more to be looked to nor money,” said the glazier. “There’s this poor creetur as is dead and gone; by what I can make out, he’d seen the day when he was a deal finer gentleman nor Bulstrode.”\n\n“Finer gentleman! I’ll warrant him,” said Mrs. Dollop; “and a far personabler man, by what I can hear. As I said when Mr. Baldwin, the tax-gatherer, comes in, a-standing where you sit, and says, ‘Bulstrode got all his money as he brought into this town by thieving and swindling,’—I said, ‘You don’t make me no wiser, Mr. Baldwin: it’s set my blood a-creeping to look at him ever sin’ here he came into Slaughter Lane a-wanting to buy the house over my head: folks don’t look the color o’ the dough-tub and stare at you as if they wanted to see into your backbone for nothingk.’ That was what I said, and Mr. Baldwin can bear me witness.”\n\n“And in the rights of it too,” said Mr. Crabbe. “For by what I can make out, this Raffles, as they call him, was a lusty, fresh-colored man as you’d wish to see, and the best o’ company—though dead he lies in Lowick churchyard sure enough; and by what I can understan’, there’s them knows more than they should know about how he got there.”\n\n“I’ll believe you!” said Mrs. Dollop, with a touch of scorn at Mr. Crabbe’s apparent dimness. “When a man’s been ’ticed to a lone house, and there’s them can pay for hospitals and nurses for half the country-side choose to be sitters-up night and day, and nobody to come near but a doctor as is known to stick at nothingk, and as poor as he can hang together, and after that so flush o’ money as he can pay off Mr. Byles the butcher as his bill has been running on for the best o’ joints since last Michaelmas was a twelvemonth—I don’t want anybody to come and tell me as there’s been more going on nor the Prayer-book’s got a service for—I don’t want to stand winking and blinking and thinking.”\n\nMrs. Dollop looked round with the air of a landlady accustomed to dominate her company. There was a chorus of adhesion from the more courageous; but Mr. Limp, after taking a draught, placed his flat hands together and pressed them hard between his knees, looking down at them with blear-eyed contemplation, as if the scorching power of Mrs. Dollop’s speech had quite dried up and nullified his wits until they could be brought round again by further moisture.\n\n“Why shouldn’t they dig the man up and have the Crowner?” said the dyer. “It’s been done many and many’s the time. If there’s been foul play they might find it out.”\n\n“Not they, Mr. Jonas!” said Mrs Dollop, emphatically. “I know what doctors are. They’re a deal too cunning to be found out. And this Doctor Lydgate that’s been for cutting up everybody before the breath was well out o’ their body—it’s plain enough what use he wanted to make o’ looking into respectable people’s insides. He knows drugs, you may be sure, as you can neither smell nor see, neither before they’re swallowed nor after. Why, I’ve seen drops myself ordered by Doctor Gambit, as is our club doctor and a good charikter, and has brought more live children into the world nor ever another i’ Middlemarch—I say I’ve seen drops myself as made no difference whether they was in the glass or out, and yet have griped you the next day. So I’ll leave your own sense to judge. Don’t tell me! All I say is, it’s a mercy they didn’t take this Doctor Lydgate on to our club. There’s many a mother’s child might ha’ rued it.”\n\nThe heads of this discussion at “Dollop’s” had been the common theme among all classes in the town, had been carried to Lowick Parsonage on one side and to Tipton Grange on the other, had come fully to the ears of the Vincy family, and had been discussed with sad reference to “poor Harriet” by all Mrs. Bulstrode’s friends, before Lydgate knew distinctly why people were looking strangely at him, and before Bulstrode himself suspected the betrayal of his secrets. He had not been accustomed to very cordial relations with his neighbors, and hence he could not miss the signs of cordiality; moreover, he had been taking journeys on business of various kinds, having now made up his mind that he need not quit Middlemarch, and feeling able consequently to determine on matters which he had before left in suspense.\n\n“We will make a journey to Cheltenham in the course of a month or two,” he had said to his wife. “There are great spiritual advantages to be had in that town along with the air and the waters, and six weeks there will be eminently refreshing to us.”\n\nHe really believed in the spiritual advantages, and meant that his life henceforth should be the more devoted because of those later sins which he represented to himself as hypothetic, praying hypothetically for their pardon:—“if I have herein transgressed.”\n\nAs to the Hospital, he avoided saying anything further to Lydgate, fearing to manifest a too sudden change of plans immediately on the death of Raffles. In his secret soul he believed that Lydgate suspected his orders to have been intentionally disobeyed, and suspecting this he must also suspect a motive. But nothing had been betrayed to him as to the history of Raffles, and Bulstrode was anxious not to do anything which would give emphasis to his undefined suspicions. As to any certainty that a particular method of treatment would either save or kill, Lydgate himself was constantly arguing against such dogmatism; he had no right to speak, and he had every motive for being silent. Hence Bulstrode felt himself providentially secured. The only incident he had strongly winced under had been an occasional encounter with Caleb Garth, who, however, had raised his hat with mild gravity.\n\nMeanwhile, on the part of the principal townsmen a strong determination was growing against him.\n\nA meeting was to be held in the Town-Hall on a sanitary question which had risen into pressing importance by the occurrence of a cholera case in the town. Since the Act of Parliament, which had been hurriedly passed, authorizing assessments for sanitary measures, there had been a Board for the superintendence of such measures appointed in Middlemarch, and much cleansing and preparation had been concurred in by Whigs and Tories. The question now was, whether a piece of ground outside the town should be secured as a burial-ground by means of assessment or by private subscription. The meeting was to be open, and almost everybody of importance in the town was expected to be there.\n\nMr. Bulstrode was a member of the Board, and just before twelve o’clock he started from the Bank with the intention of urging the plan of private subscription. Under the hesitation of his projects, he had for some time kept himself in the background, and he felt that he should this morning resume his old position as a man of action and influence in the public affairs of the town where he expected to end his days. Among the various persons going in the same direction, he saw Lydgate; they joined, talked over the object of the meeting, and entered it together.\n\nIt seemed that everybody of mark had been earlier than they. But there were still spaces left near the head of the large central table, and they made their way thither. Mr. Farebrother sat opposite, not far from Mr. Hawley; all the medical men were there; Mr. Thesiger was in the chair, and Mr. Brooke of Tipton was on his right hand.\n\nLydgate noticed a peculiar interchange of glances when he and Bulstrode took their seats.\n\nAfter the business had been fully opened by the chairman, who pointed out the advantages of purchasing by subscription a piece of ground large enough to be ultimately used as a general cemetery, Mr. Bulstrode, whose rather high-pitched but subdued and fluent voice the town was used to at meetings of this sort, rose and asked leave to deliver his opinion. Lydgate could see again the peculiar interchange of glances before Mr. Hawley started up, and said in his firm resonant voice, “Mr. Chairman, I request that before any one delivers his opinion on this point I may be permitted to speak on a question of public feeling, which not only by myself, but by many gentlemen present, is regarded as preliminary.”\n\nMr. Hawley’s mode of speech, even when public decorum repressed his “awful language,” was formidable in its curtness and self-possession. Mr. Thesiger sanctioned the request, Mr. Bulstrode sat down, and Mr. Hawley continued.\n\n“In what I have to say, Mr. Chairman, I am not speaking simply on my own behalf: I am speaking with the concurrence and at the express request of no fewer than eight of my fellow-townsmen, who are immediately around us. It is our united sentiment that Mr. Bulstrode should be called upon—and I do now call upon him—to resign public positions which he holds not simply as a tax-payer, but as a gentleman among gentlemen. There are practices and there are acts which, owing to circumstances, the law cannot visit, though they may be worse than many things which are legally punishable. Honest men and gentlemen, if they don’t want the company of people who perpetrate such acts, have got to defend themselves as they best can, and that is what I and the friends whom I may call my clients in this affair are determined to do. I don’t say that Mr. Bulstrode has been guilty of shameful acts, but I call upon him either publicly to deny and confute the scandalous statements made against him by a man now dead, and who died in his house—the statement that he was for many years engaged in nefarious practices, and that he won his fortune by dishonest procedures—or else to withdraw from positions which could only have been allowed him as a gentleman among gentlemen.”\n\nAll eyes in the room were turned on Mr. Bulstrode, who, since the first mention of his name, had been going through a crisis of feeling almost too violent for his delicate frame to support. Lydgate, who himself was undergoing a shock as from the terrible practical interpretation of some faint augury, felt, nevertheless, that his own movement of resentful hatred was checked by that instinct of the Healer which thinks first of bringing rescue or relief to the sufferer, when he looked at the shrunken misery of Bulstrode’s livid face.\n\nThe quick vision that his life was after all a failure, that he was a dishonored man, and must quail before the glance of those towards whom he had habitually assumed the attitude of a reprover—that God had disowned him before men and left him unscreened to the triumphant scorn of those who were glad to have their hatred justified—the sense of utter futility in that equivocation with his conscience in dealing with the life of his accomplice, an equivocation which now turned venomously upon him with the full-grown fang of a discovered lie:—all this rushed through him like the agony of terror which fails to kill, and leaves the ears still open to the returning wave of execration. The sudden sense of exposure after the re-established sense of safety came—not to the coarse organization of a criminal, but to the susceptible nerve of a man whose intensest being lay in such mastery and predominance as the conditions of his life had shaped for him.\n\nBut in that intense being lay the strength of reaction. Through all his bodily infirmity there ran a tenacious nerve of ambitious self-preserving will, which had continually leaped out like a flame, scattering all doctrinal fears, and which, even while he sat an object of compassion for the merciful, was beginning to stir and glow under his ashy paleness. Before the last words were out of Mr. Hawley’s mouth, Bulstrode felt that he should answer, and that his answer would be a retort. He dared not get up and say, “I am not guilty, the whole story is false”—even if he had dared this, it would have seemed to him, under his present keen sense of betrayal, as vain as to pull, for covering to his nakedness, a frail rag which would rend at every little strain.\n\nFor a few moments there was total silence, while every man in the room was looking at Bulstrode. He sat perfectly still, leaning hard against the back of his chair; he could not venture to rise, and when he began to speak he pressed his hands upon the seat on each side of him. But his voice was perfectly audible, though hoarser than usual, and his words were distinctly pronounced, though he paused between sentence as if short of breath. He said, turning first toward Mr. Thesiger, and then looking at Mr. Hawley—\n\n“I protest before you, sir, as a Christian minister, against the sanction of proceedings towards me which are dictated by virulent hatred. Those who are hostile to me are glad to believe any libel uttered by a loose tongue against me. And their consciences become strict against me. Say that the evil-speaking of which I am to be made the victim accuses me of malpractices—” here Bulstrode’s voice rose and took on a more biting accent, till it seemed a low cry—“who shall be my accuser? Not men whose own lives are unchristian, nay, scandalous—not men who themselves use low instruments to carry out their ends—whose profession is a tissue of chicanery—who have been spending their income on their own sensual enjoyments, while I have been devoting mine to advance the best objects with regard to this life and the next.”\n\nAfter the word chicanery there was a growing noise, half of murmurs and half of hisses, while four persons started up at once—Mr. Hawley, Mr. Toller, Mr. Chichely, and Mr. Hackbutt; but Mr. Hawley’s outburst was instantaneous, and left the others behind in silence.\n\n“If you mean me, sir, I call you and every one else to the inspection of my professional life. As to Christian or unchristian, I repudiate your canting palavering Christianity; and as to the way in which I spend my income, it is not my principle to maintain thieves and cheat offspring of their due inheritance in order to support religion and set myself up as a saintly Killjoy. I affect no niceness of conscience—I have not found any nice standards necessary yet to measure your actions by, sir. And I again call upon you to enter into satisfactory explanations concerning the scandals against you, or else to withdraw from posts in which we at any rate decline you as a colleague. I say, sir, we decline to co-operate with a man whose character is not cleared from infamous lights cast upon it, not only by reports but by recent actions.”\n\n“Allow me, Mr. Hawley,” said the chairman; and Mr. Hawley, still fuming, bowed half impatiently, and sat down with his hands thrust deep in his pockets.\n\n“Mr. Bulstrode, it is not desirable, I think, to prolong the present discussion,” said Mr. Thesiger, turning to the pallid trembling man; “I must so far concur with what has fallen from Mr. Hawley in expression of a general feeling, as to think it due to your Christian profession that you should clear yourself, if possible, from unhappy aspersions. I for my part should be willing to give you full opportunity and hearing. But I must say that your present attitude is painfully inconsistent with those principles which you have sought to identify yourself with, and for the honor of which I am bound to care. I recommend you at present, as your clergyman, and one who hopes for your reinstatement in respect, to quit the room, and avoid further hindrance to business.”\n\nBulstrode, after a moment’s hesitation, took his hat from the floor and slowly rose, but he grasped the corner of the chair so totteringly that Lydgate felt sure there was not strength enough in him to walk away without support. What could he do? He could not see a man sink close to him for want of help. He rose and gave his arm to Bulstrode, and in that way led him out of the room; yet this act, which might have been one of gentle duty and pure compassion, was at this moment unspeakably bitter to him. It seemed as if he were putting his sign-manual to that association of himself with Bulstrode, of which he now saw the full meaning as it must have presented itself to other minds. He now felt the conviction that this man who was leaning tremblingly on his arm, had given him the thousand pounds as a bribe, and that somehow the treatment of Raffles had been tampered with from an evil motive. The inferences were closely linked enough; the town knew of the loan, believed it to be a bribe, and believed that he took it as a bribe.\n\nPoor Lydgate, his mind struggling under the terrible clutch of this revelation, was all the while morally forced to take Mr. Bulstrode to the Bank, send a man off for his carriage, and wait to accompany him home.\n\nMeanwhile the business of the meeting was despatched, and fringed off into eager discussion among various groups concerning this affair of Bulstrode—and Lydgate.\n\nMr. Brooke, who had before heard only imperfect hints of it, and was very uneasy that he had “gone a little too far” in countenancing Bulstrode, now got himself fully informed, and felt some benevolent sadness in talking to Mr. Farebrother about the ugly light in which Lydgate had come to be regarded. Mr. Farebrother was going to walk back to Lowick.\n\n“Step into my carriage,” said Mr. Brooke. “I am going round to see Mrs. Casaubon. She was to come back from Yorkshire last night. She will like to see me, you know.”\n\nSo they drove along, Mr. Brooke chatting with good-natured hope that there had not really been anything black in Lydgate’s behavior—a young fellow whom he had seen to be quite above the common mark, when he brought a letter from his uncle Sir Godwin. Mr. Farebrother said little: he was deeply mournful: with a keen perception of human weakness, he could not be confident that under the pressure of humiliating needs Lydgate had not fallen below himself.\n\nWhen the carriage drove up to the gate of the Manor, Dorothea was out on the gravel, and came to greet them.\n\n“Well, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, “we have just come from a meeting—a sanitary meeting, you know.”\n\n“Was Mr. Lydgate there?” said Dorothea, who looked full of health and animation, and stood with her head bare under the gleaming April lights. “I want to see him and have a great consultation with him about the Hospital. I have engaged with Mr. Bulstrode to do so.”\n\n“Oh, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, “we have been hearing bad news—bad news, you know.”\n\nThey walked through the garden towards the churchyard gate, Mr. Farebrother wanting to go on to the parsonage; and Dorothea heard the whole sad story.\n\nShe listened with deep interest, and begged to hear twice over the facts and impressions concerning Lydgate. After a short silence, pausing at the churchyard gate, and addressing Mr. Farebrother, she said energetically—\n\n“You don’t believe that Mr. Lydgate is guilty of anything base? I will not believe it. Let us find out the truth and clear him!”\n\n# BOOK VIII. SUNSET AND SUNRISE. CHAPTER LXXII.\n\nFull souls are double mirrors, making still An endless vista of fair things before, Repeating things behind.\n\nDorothea’s impetuous generosity, which would have leaped at once to the vindication of Lydgate from the suspicion of having accepted money as a bribe, underwent a melancholy check when she came to consider all the circumstances of the case by the light of Mr. Farebrother’s experience.\n\n“It is a delicate matter to touch,” he said. “How can we begin to inquire into it? It must be either publicly by setting the magistrate and coroner to work, or privately by questioning Lydgate. As to the first proceeding there is no solid ground to go upon, else Hawley would have adopted it; and as to opening the subject with Lydgate, I confess I should shrink from it. He would probably take it as a deadly insult. I have more than once experienced the difficulty of speaking to him on personal matters. And—one should know the truth about his conduct beforehand, to feel very confident of a good result.”\n\n“I feel convinced that his conduct has not been guilty: I believe that people are almost always better than their neighbors think they are,” said Dorothea. Some of her intensest experience in the last two years had set her mind strongly in opposition to any unfavorable construction of others; and for the first time she felt rather discontented with Mr. Farebrother. She disliked this cautious weighing of consequences, instead of an ardent faith in efforts of justice and mercy, which would conquer by their emotional force. Two days afterwards, he was dining at the Manor with her uncle and the Chettams, and when the dessert was standing uneaten, the servants were out of the room, and Mr. Brooke was nodding in a nap, she returned to the subject with renewed vivacity.\n\n“Mr. Lydgate would understand that if his friends hear a calumny about him their first wish must be to justify him. What do we live for, if it is not to make life less difficult to each other? I cannot be indifferent to the troubles of a man who advised me in my trouble, and attended me in my illness.”\n\nDorothea’s tone and manner were not more energetic than they had been when she was at the head of her uncle’s table nearly three years before, and her experience since had given her more right to express a decided opinion. But Sir James Chettam was no longer the diffident and acquiescent suitor: he was the anxious brother-in-law, with a devout admiration for his sister, but with a constant alarm lest she should fall under some new illusion almost as bad as marrying Casaubon. He smiled much less; when he said “Exactly” it was more often an introduction to a dissentient opinion than in those submissive bachelor days; and Dorothea found to her surprise that she had to resolve not to be afraid of him—all the more because he was really her best friend. He disagreed with her now.\n\n“But, Dorothea,” he said, remonstrantly, “you can’t undertake to manage a man’s life for him in that way. Lydgate must know—at least he will soon come to know how he stands. If he can clear himself, he will. He must act for himself.”\n\n“I think his friends must wait till they find an opportunity,” added Mr. Farebrother. “It is possible—I have often felt so much weakness in myself that I can conceive even a man of honorable disposition, such as I have always believed Lydgate to be, succumbing to such a temptation as that of accepting money which was offered more or less indirectly as a bribe to insure his silence about scandalous facts long gone by. I say, I can conceive this, if he were under the pressure of hard circumstances—if he had been harassed as I feel sure Lydgate has been. I would not believe anything worse of him except under stringent proof. But there is the terrible Nemesis following on some errors, that it is always possible for those who like it to interpret them into a crime: there is no proof in favor of the man outside his own consciousness and assertion.”\n\n“Oh, how cruel!” said Dorothea, clasping her hands. “And would you not like to be the one person who believed in that man’s innocence, if the rest of the world belied him? Besides, there is a man’s character beforehand to speak for him.”\n\n“But, my dear Mrs. Casaubon,” said Mr. Farebrother, smiling gently at her ardor, “character is not cut in marble—it is not something solid and unalterable. It is something living and changing, and may become diseased as our bodies do.”\n\n“Then it may be rescued and healed,” said Dorothea “I should not be afraid of asking Mr. Lydgate to tell me the truth, that I might help him. Why should I be afraid? Now that I am not to have the land, James, I might do as Mr. Bulstrode proposed, and take his place in providing for the Hospital; and I have to consult Mr. Lydgate, to know thoroughly what are the prospects of doing good by keeping up the present plans. There is the best opportunity in the world for me to ask for his confidence; and he would be able to tell me things which might make all the circumstances clear. Then we would all stand by him and bring him out of his trouble. People glorify all sorts of bravery except the bravery they might show on behalf of their nearest neighbors.” Dorothea’s eyes had a moist brightness in them, and the changed tones of her voice roused her uncle, who began to listen.\n\n“It is true that a woman may venture on some efforts of sympathy which would hardly succeed if we men undertook them,” said Mr. Farebrother, almost converted by Dorothea’s ardor.\n\n“Surely, a woman is bound to be cautious and listen to those who know the world better than she does.” said Sir James, with his little frown. “Whatever you do in the end, Dorothea, you should really keep back at present, and not volunteer any meddling with this Bulstrode business. We don’t know yet what may turn up. You must agree with me?” he ended, looking at Mr. Farebrother.\n\n“I do think it would be better to wait,” said the latter.\n\n“Yes, yes, my dear,” said Mr. Brooke, not quite knowing at what point the discussion had arrived, but coming up to it with a contribution which was generally appropriate. “It is easy to go too far, you know. You must not let your ideas run away with you. And as to being in a hurry to put money into schemes—it won’t do, you know. Garth has drawn me in uncommonly with repairs, draining, that sort of thing: I’m uncommonly out of pocket with one thing or another. I must pull up. As for you, Chettam, you are spending a fortune on those oak fences round your demesne.”\n\nDorothea, submitting uneasily to this discouragement, went with Celia into the library, which was her usual drawing-room.\n\n“Now, Dodo, do listen to what James says,” said Celia, “else you will be getting into a scrape. You always did, and you always will, when you set about doing as you please. And I think it is a mercy now after all that you have got James to think for you. He lets you have your plans, only he hinders you from being taken in. And that is the good of having a brother instead of a husband. A husband would not let you have your plans.”\n\n“As if I wanted a husband!” said Dorothea. “I only want not to have my feelings checked at every turn.” Mrs. Casaubon was still undisciplined enough to burst into angry tears.\n\n“Now, really, Dodo,” said Celia, with rather a deeper guttural than usual, “you are contradictory: first one thing and then another. You used to submit to Mr. Casaubon quite shamefully: I think you would have given up ever coming to see me if he had asked you.”\n\n“Of course I submitted to him, because it was my duty; it was my feeling for him,” said Dorothea, looking through the prism of her tears.\n\n“Then why can’t you think it your duty to submit a little to what James wishes?” said Celia, with a sense of stringency in her argument. “Because he only wishes what is for your own good. And, of course, men know best about everything, except what women know better.” Dorothea laughed and forgot her tears.\n\n“Well, I mean about babies and those things,” explained Celia. “I should not give up to James when I knew he was wrong, as you used to do to Mr. Casaubon.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXXIII.\n\nPity the laden one; this wandering woe May visit you and me.\n\nWhen Lydgate had allayed Mrs. Bulstrode’s anxiety by telling her that her husband had been seized with faintness at the meeting, but that he trusted soon to see him better and would call again the next day, unless she sent for him earlier, he went directly home, got on his horse, and rode three miles out of the town for the sake of being out of reach.\n\nHe felt himself becoming violent and unreasonable as if raging under the pain of stings: he was ready to curse the day on which he had come to Middlemarch. Everything that had happened to him there seemed a mere preparation for this hateful fatality, which had come as a blight on his honorable ambition, and must make even people who had only vulgar standards regard his reputation as irrevocably damaged. In such moments a man can hardly escape being unloving. Lydgate thought of himself as the sufferer, and of others as the agents who had injured his lot. He had meant everything to turn out differently; and others had thrust themselves into his life and thwarted his purposes. His marriage seemed an unmitigated calamity; and he was afraid of going to Rosamond before he had vented himself in this solitary rage, lest the mere sight of her should exasperate him and make him behave unwarrantably. There are episodes in most men’s lives in which their highest qualities can only cast a deterring shadow over the objects that fill their inward vision: Lydgate’s tenderheartedness was present just then only as a dread lest he should offend against it, not as an emotion that swayed him to tenderness. For he was very miserable. Only those who know the supremacy of the intellectual life—the life which has a seed of ennobling thought and purpose within it—can understand the grief of one who falls from that serene activity into the absorbing soul-wasting struggle with worldly annoyances.\n\nHow was he to live on without vindicating himself among people who suspected him of baseness? How could he go silently away from Middlemarch as if he were retreating before a just condemnation? And yet how was he to set about vindicating himself?\n\nFor that scene at the meeting, which he had just witnessed, although it had told him no particulars, had been enough to make his own situation thoroughly clear to him. Bulstrode had been in dread of scandalous disclosures on the part of Raffles. Lydgate could now construct all the probabilities of the case. “He was afraid of some betrayal in my hearing: all he wanted was to bind me to him by a strong obligation: that was why he passed on a sudden from hardness to liberality. And he may have tampered with the patient—he may have disobeyed my orders. I fear he did. But whether he did or not, the world believes that he somehow or other poisoned the man and that I winked at the crime, if I didn’t help in it. And yet—and yet he may not be guilty of the last offence; and it is just possible that the change towards me may have been a genuine relenting—the effect of second thoughts such as he alleged. What we call the ‘just possible’ is sometimes true and the thing we find it easier to believe is grossly false. In his last dealings with this man Bulstrode may have kept his hands pure, in spite of my suspicion to the contrary.”\n\nThere was a benumbing cruelty in his position. Even if he renounced every other consideration than that of justifying himself—if he met shrugs, cold glances, and avoidance as an accusation, and made a public statement of all the facts as he knew them, who would be convinced? It would be playing the part of a fool to offer his own testimony on behalf of himself, and say, “I did not take the money as a bribe.” The circumstances would always be stronger than his assertion. And besides, to come forward and tell everything about himself must include declarations about Bulstrode which would darken the suspicions of others against him. He must tell that he had not known of Raffles’s existence when he first mentioned his pressing need of money to Bulstrode, and that he took the money innocently as a result of that communication, not knowing that a new motive for the loan might have arisen on his being called in to this man. And after all, the suspicion of Bulstrode’s motives might be unjust.\n\nBut then came the question whether he should have acted in precisely the same way if he had not taken the money? Certainly, if Raffles had continued alive and susceptible of further treatment when he arrived, and he had then imagined any disobedience to his orders on the part of Bulstrode, he would have made a strict inquiry, and if his conjecture had been verified he would have thrown up the case, in spite of his recent heavy obligation. But if he had not received any money—if Bulstrode had never revoked his cold recommendation of bankruptcy—would he, Lydgate, have abstained from all inquiry even on finding the man dead?—would the shrinking from an insult to Bulstrode—would the dubiousness of all medical treatment and the argument that his own treatment would pass for the wrong with most members of his profession—have had just the same force or significance with him?\n\nThat was the uneasy corner of Lydgate’s consciousness while he was reviewing the facts and resisting all reproach. If he had been independent, this matter of a patient’s treatment and the distinct rule that he must do or see done that which he believed best for the life committed to him, would have been the point on which he would have been the sturdiest. As it was, he had rested in the consideration that disobedience to his orders, however it might have arisen, could not be considered a crime, that in the dominant opinion obedience to his orders was just as likely to be fatal, and that the affair was simply one of etiquette. Whereas, again and again, in his time of freedom, he had denounced the perversion of pathological doubt into moral doubt and had said—“the purest experiment in treatment may still be conscientious: my business is to take care of life, and to do the best I can think of for it. Science is properly more scrupulous than dogma. Dogma gives a charter to mistake, but the very breath of science is a contest with mistake, and must keep the conscience alive.” Alas! the scientific conscience had got into the debasing company of money obligation and selfish respects.\n\n“Is there a medical man of them all in Middlemarch who would question himself as I do?” said poor Lydgate, with a renewed outburst of rebellion against the oppression of his lot. “And yet they will all feel warranted in making a wide space between me and them, as if I were a leper! My practice and my reputation are utterly damned—I can see that. Even if I could be cleared by valid evidence, it would make little difference to the blessed world here. I have been set down as tainted and should be cheapened to them all the same.”\n\nAlready there had been abundant signs which had hitherto puzzled him, that just when he had been paying off his debts and getting cheerfully on his feet, the townsmen were avoiding him or looking strangely at him, and in two instances it came to his knowledge that patients of his had called in another practitioner. The reasons were too plain now. The general black-balling had begun.\n\nNo wonder that in Lydgate’s energetic nature the sense of a hopeless misconstruction easily turned into a dogged resistance. The scowl which occasionally showed itself on his square brow was not a meaningless accident. Already when he was re-entering the town after that ride taken in the first hours of stinging pain, he was setting his mind on remaining in Middlemarch in spite of the worst that could be done against him. He would not retreat before calumny, as if he submitted to it. He would face it to the utmost, and no act of his should show that he was afraid. It belonged to the generosity as well as defiant force of his nature that he resolved not to shrink from showing to the full his sense of obligation to Bulstrode. It was true that the association with this man had been fatal to him—true that if he had had the thousand pounds still in his hands with all his debts unpaid he would have returned the money to Bulstrode, and taken beggary rather than the rescue which had been sullied with the suspicion of a bribe (for, remember, he was one of the proudest among the sons of men)—nevertheless, he would not turn away from this crushed fellow-mortal whose aid he had used, and make a pitiful effort to get acquittal for himself by howling against another. “I shall do as I think right, and explain to nobody. They will try to starve me out, but—” he was going on with an obstinate resolve, but he was getting near home, and the thought of Rosamond urged itself again into that chief place from which it had been thrust by the agonized struggles of wounded honor and pride.\n\nHow would Rosamond take it all? Here was another weight of chain to drag, and poor Lydgate was in a bad mood for bearing her dumb mastery. He had no impulse to tell her the trouble which must soon be common to them both. He preferred waiting for the incidental disclosure which events must soon bring about.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXIV.\n\n“Mercifully grant that we may grow aged together.” —BOOK OF TOBIT: Marriage Prayer.\n\nIn Middlemarch a wife could not long remain ignorant that the town held a bad opinion of her husband. No feminine intimate might carry her friendship so far as to make a plain statement to the wife of the unpleasant fact known or believed about her husband; but when a woman with her thoughts much at leisure got them suddenly employed on something grievously disadvantageous to her neighbors, various moral impulses were called into play which tended to stimulate utterance. Candor was one. To be candid, in Middlemarch phraseology, meant, to use an early opportunity of letting your friends know that you did not take a cheerful view of their capacity, their conduct, or their position; and a robust candor never waited to be asked for its opinion. Then, again, there was the love of truth—a wide phrase, but meaning in this relation, a lively objection to seeing a wife look happier than her husband’s character warranted, or manifest too much satisfaction in her lot—the poor thing should have some hint given her that if she knew the truth she would have less complacency in her bonnet, and in light dishes for a supper-party. Stronger than all, there was the regard for a friend’s moral improvement, sometimes called her soul, which was likely to be benefited by remarks tending to gloom, uttered with the accompaniment of pensive staring at the furniture and a manner implying that the speaker would not tell what was on her mind, from regard to the feelings of her hearer. On the whole, one might say that an ardent charity was at work setting the virtuous mind to make a neighbor unhappy for her good.\n\nThere were hardly any wives in Middlemarch whose matrimonial misfortunes would in different ways be likely to call forth more of this moral activity than Rosamond and her aunt Bulstrode. Mrs. Bulstrode was not an object of dislike, and had never consciously injured any human being. Men had always thought her a handsome comfortable woman, and had reckoned it among the signs of Bulstrode’s hypocrisy that he had chosen a red-blooded Vincy, instead of a ghastly and melancholy person suited to his low esteem for earthly pleasure. When the scandal about her husband was disclosed they remarked of her—“Ah, poor woman! She’s as honest as the day—she never suspected anything wrong in him, you may depend on it.” Women, who were intimate with her, talked together much of “poor Harriet,” imagined what her feelings must be when she came to know everything, and conjectured how much she had already come to know. There was no spiteful disposition towards her; rather, there was a busy benevolence anxious to ascertain what it would be well for her to feel and do under the circumstances, which of course kept the imagination occupied with her character and history from the times when she was Harriet Vincy till now. With the review of Mrs. Bulstrode and her position it was inevitable to associate Rosamond, whose prospects were under the same blight with her aunt’s. Rosamond was more severely criticised and less pitied, though she too, as one of the good old Vincy family who had always been known in Middlemarch, was regarded as a victim to marriage with an interloper. The Vincys had their weaknesses, but then they lay on the surface: there was never anything bad to be “found out” concerning them. Mrs. Bulstrode was vindicated from any resemblance to her husband. Harriet’s faults were her own.\n\n“She has always been showy,” said Mrs. Hackbutt, making tea for a small party, “though she has got into the way of putting her religion forward, to conform to her husband; she has tried to hold her head up above Middlemarch by making it known that she invites clergymen and heaven-knows-who from Riverston and those places.”\n\n“We can hardly blame her for that,” said Mrs. Sprague; “because few of the best people in the town cared to associate with Bulstrode, and she must have somebody to sit down at her table.”\n\n“Mr. Thesiger has always countenanced him,” said Mrs. Hackbutt. “I think he must be sorry now.”\n\n“But he was never fond of him in his heart—that every one knows,” said Mrs. Tom Toller. “Mr. Thesiger never goes into extremes. He keeps to the truth in what is evangelical. It is only clergymen like Mr. Tyke, who want to use Dissenting hymn-books and that low kind of religion, who ever found Bulstrode to their taste.”\n\n“I understand, Mr. Tyke is in great distress about him,” said Mrs. Hackbutt. “And well he may be: they say the Bulstrodes have half kept the Tyke family.”\n\n“And of course it is a discredit to his doctrines,” said Mrs. Sprague, who was elderly, and old-fashioned in her opinions.\n\n“People will not make a boast of being methodistical in Middlemarch for a good while to come.”\n\n“I think we must not set down people’s bad actions to their religion,” said falcon-faced Mrs. Plymdale, who had been listening hitherto.\n\n“Oh, my dear, we are forgetting,” said Mrs. Sprague. “We ought not to be talking of this before you.”\n\n“I am sure I have no reason to be partial,” said Mrs. Plymdale, coloring. “It’s true Mr. Plymdale has always been on good terms with Mr. Bulstrode, and Harriet Vincy was my friend long before she married him. But I have always kept my own opinions and told her where she was wrong, poor thing. Still, in point of religion, I must say, Mr. Bulstrode might have done what he has, and worse, and yet have been a man of no religion. I don’t say that there has not been a little too much of that—I like moderation myself. But truth is truth. The men tried at the assizes are not all over-religious, I suppose.”\n\n“Well,” said Mrs. Hackbutt, wheeling adroitly, “all I can say is, that I think she ought to separate from him.”\n\n“I can’t say that,” said Mrs. Sprague. “She took him for better or worse, you know.”\n\n“But ‘worse’ can never mean finding out that your husband is fit for Newgate,” said Mrs. Hackbutt. “Fancy living with such a man! I should expect to be poisoned.”\n\n“Yes, I think myself it is an encouragement to crime if such men are to be taken care of and waited on by good wives,” said Mrs. Tom Toller.\n\n“And a good wife poor Harriet has been,” said Mrs. Plymdale. “She thinks her husband the first of men. It’s true he has never denied her anything.”\n\n“Well, we shall see what she will do,” said Mrs. Hackbutt. “I suppose she knows nothing yet, poor creature. I do hope and trust I shall not see her, for I should be frightened to death lest I should say anything about her husband. Do you think any hint has reached her?”\n\n“I should hardly think so,” said Mrs. Tom Toller. “We hear that he is ill, and has never stirred out of the house since the meeting on Thursday; but she was with her girls at church yesterday, and they had new Tuscan bonnets. Her own had a feather in it. I have never seen that her religion made any difference in her dress.”\n\n“She wears very neat patterns always,” said Mrs. Plymdale, a little stung. “And that feather I know she got dyed a pale lavender on purpose to be consistent. I must say it of Harriet that she wishes to do right.”\n\n“As to her knowing what has happened, it can’t be kept from her long,” said Mrs. Hackbutt. “The Vincys know, for Mr. Vincy was at the meeting. It will be a great blow to him. There is his daughter as well as his sister.”\n\n“Yes, indeed,” said Mrs. Sprague. “Nobody supposes that Mr. Lydgate can go on holding up his head in Middlemarch, things look so black about the thousand pounds he took just at that man’s death. It really makes one shudder.”\n\n“Pride must have a fall,” said Mrs. Hackbutt.\n\n“I am not so sorry for Rosamond Vincy that was as I am for her aunt,” said Mrs. Plymdale. “She needed a lesson.”\n\n“I suppose the Bulstrodes will go and live abroad somewhere,” said Mrs. Sprague. “That is what is generally done when there is anything disgraceful in a family.”\n\n“And a most deadly blow it will be to Harriet,” said Mrs. Plymdale. “If ever a woman was crushed, she will be. I pity her from my heart. And with all her faults, few women are better. From a girl she had the neatest ways, and was always good-hearted, and as open as the day. You might look into her drawers when you would—always the same. And so she has brought up Kate and Ellen. You may think how hard it will be for her to go among foreigners.”\n\n“The doctor says that is what he should recommend the Lydgates to do,” said Mrs. Sprague. “He says Lydgate ought to have kept among the French.”\n\n“That would suit her well enough, I dare say,” said Mrs. Plymdale; “there is that kind of lightness about her. But she got that from her mother; she never got it from her aunt Bulstrode, who always gave her good advice, and to my knowledge would rather have had her marry elsewhere.”\n\nMrs. Plymdale was in a situation which caused her some complication of feeling. There had been not only her intimacy with Mrs. Bulstrode, but also a profitable business relation of the great Plymdale dyeing house with Mr. Bulstrode, which on the one hand would have inclined her to desire that the mildest view of his character should be the true one, but on the other, made her the more afraid of seeming to palliate his culpability. Again, the late alliance of her family with the Tollers had brought her in connection with the best circle, which gratified her in every direction except in the inclination to those serious views which she believed to be the best in another sense. The sharp little woman’s conscience was somewhat troubled in the adjustment of these opposing “bests,” and of her griefs and satisfactions under late events, which were likely to humble those who needed humbling, but also to fall heavily on her old friend whose faults she would have preferred seeing on a background of prosperity.\n\nPoor Mrs. Bulstrode, meanwhile, had been no further shaken by the oncoming tread of calamity than in the busier stirring of that secret uneasiness which had always been present in her since the last visit of Raffles to The Shrubs. That the hateful man had come ill to Stone Court, and that her husband had chosen to remain there and watch over him, she allowed to be explained by the fact that Raffles had been employed and aided in earlier-days, and that this made a tie of benevolence towards him in his degraded helplessness; and she had been since then innocently cheered by her husband’s more hopeful speech about his own health and ability to continue his attention to business. The calm was disturbed when Lydgate had brought him home ill from the meeting, and in spite of comforting assurances during the next few days, she cried in private from the conviction that her husband was not suffering from bodily illness merely, but from something that afflicted his mind. He would not allow her to read to him, and scarcely to sit with him, alleging nervous susceptibility to sounds and movements; yet she suspected that in shutting himself up in his private room he wanted to be busy with his papers. Something, she felt sure, had happened. Perhaps it was some great loss of money; and she was kept in the dark. Not daring to question her husband, she said to Lydgate, on the fifth day after the meeting, when she had not left home except to go to church—\n\n“Mr. Lydgate, pray be open with me: I like to know the truth. Has anything happened to Mr. Bulstrode?”\n\n“Some little nervous shock,” said Lydgate, evasively. He felt that it was not for him to make the painful revelation.\n\n“But what brought it on?” said Mrs. Bulstrode, looking directly at him with her large dark eyes.\n\n“There is often something poisonous in the air of public rooms,” said Lydgate. “Strong men can stand it, but it tells on people in proportion to the delicacy of their systems. It is often impossible to account for the precise moment of an attack—or rather, to say why the strength gives way at a particular moment.”\n\nMrs. Bulstrode was not satisfied with this answer. There remained in her the belief that some calamity had befallen her husband, of which she was to be kept in ignorance; and it was in her nature strongly to object to such concealment. She begged leave for her daughters to sit with their father, and drove into the town to pay some visits, conjecturing that if anything were known to have gone wrong in Mr. Bulstrode’s affairs, she should see or hear some sign of it.\n\nShe called on Mrs. Thesiger, who was not at home, and then drove to Mrs. Hackbutt’s on the other side of the churchyard. Mrs. Hackbutt saw her coming from an up-stairs window, and remembering her former alarm lest she should meet Mrs. Bulstrode, felt almost bound in consistency to send word that she was not at home; but against that, there was a sudden strong desire within her for the excitement of an interview in which she was quite determined not to make the slightest allusion to what was in her mind.\n\nHence Mrs. Bulstrode was shown into the drawing-room, and Mrs. Hackbutt went to her, with more tightness of lip and rubbing of her hands than was usually observable in her, these being precautions adopted against freedom of speech. She was resolved not to ask how Mr. Bulstrode was.\n\n“I have not been anywhere except to church for nearly a week,” said Mrs. Bulstrode, after a few introductory remarks. “But Mr. Bulstrode was taken so ill at the meeting on Thursday that I have not liked to leave the house.”\n\nMrs. Hackbutt rubbed the back of one hand with the palm of the other held against her chest, and let her eyes ramble over the pattern on the rug.\n\n“Was Mr. Hackbutt at the meeting?” persevered Mrs. Bulstrode.\n\n“Yes, he was,” said Mrs. Hackbutt, with the same attitude. “The land is to be bought by subscription, I believe.”\n\n“Let us hope that there will be no more cases of cholera to be buried in it,” said Mrs. Bulstrode. “It is an awful visitation. But I always think Middlemarch a very healthy spot. I suppose it is being used to it from a child; but I never saw the town I should like to live at better, and especially our end.”\n\n“I am sure I should be glad that you always should live at Middlemarch, Mrs. Bulstrode,” said Mrs. Hackbutt, with a slight sigh. “Still, we must learn to resign ourselves, wherever our lot may be cast. Though I am sure there will always be people in this town who will wish you well.”\n\nMrs. Hackbutt longed to say, “if you take my advice you will part from your husband,” but it seemed clear to her that the poor woman knew nothing of the thunder ready to bolt on her head, and she herself could do no more than prepare her a little. Mrs. Bulstrode felt suddenly rather chill and trembling: there was evidently something unusual behind this speech of Mrs. Hackbutt’s; but though she had set out with the desire to be fully informed, she found herself unable now to pursue her brave purpose, and turning the conversation by an inquiry about the young Hackbutts, she soon took her leave saying that she was going to see Mrs. Plymdale. On her way thither she tried to imagine that there might have been some unusually warm sparring at the meeting between Mr. Bulstrode and some of his frequent opponents—perhaps Mr. Hackbutt might have been one of them. That would account for everything.\n\nBut when she was in conversation with Mrs. Plymdale that comforting explanation seemed no longer tenable. “Selina” received her with a pathetic affectionateness and a disposition to give edifying answers on the commonest topics, which could hardly have reference to an ordinary quarrel of which the most important consequence was a perturbation of Mr. Bulstrode’s health. Beforehand Mrs. Bulstrode had thought that she would sooner question Mrs. Plymdale than any one else; but she found to her surprise that an old friend is not always the person whom it is easiest to make a confidant of: there was the barrier of remembered communication under other circumstances—there was the dislike of being pitied and informed by one who had been long wont to allow her the superiority. For certain words of mysterious appropriateness that Mrs. Plymdale let fall about her resolution never to turn her back on her friends, convinced Mrs. Bulstrode that what had happened must be some kind of misfortune, and instead of being able to say with her native directness, “What is it that you have in your mind?” she found herself anxious to get away before she had heard anything more explicit. She began to have an agitating certainty that the misfortune was something more than the mere loss of money, being keenly sensitive to the fact that Selina now, just as Mrs. Hackbutt had done before, avoided noticing what she said about her husband, as they would have avoided noticing a personal blemish.\n\nShe said good-by with nervous haste, and told the coachman to drive to Mr. Vincy’s warehouse. In that short drive her dread gathered so much force from the sense of darkness, that when she entered the private counting-house where her brother sat at his desk, her knees trembled and her usually florid face was deathly pale. Something of the same effect was produced in him by the sight of her: he rose from his seat to meet her, took her by the hand, and said, with his impulsive rashness—\n\n“God help you, Harriet! you know all.”\n\nThat moment was perhaps worse than any which came after. It contained that concentrated experience which in great crises of emotion reveals the bias of a nature, and is prophetic of the ultimate act which will end an intermediate struggle. Without that memory of Raffles she might still have thought only of monetary ruin, but now along with her brother’s look and words there darted into her mind the idea of some guilt in her husband—then, under the working of terror came the image of her husband exposed to disgrace—and then, after an instant of scorching shame in which she felt only the eyes of the world, with one leap of her heart she was at his side in mournful but unreproaching fellowship with shame and isolation. All this went on within her in a mere flash of time—while she sank into the chair, and raised her eyes to her brother, who stood over her. “I know nothing, Walter. What is it?” she said, faintly.\n\nHe told her everything, very inartificially, in slow fragments, making her aware that the scandal went much beyond proof, especially as to the end of Raffles.\n\n“People will talk,” he said. “Even if a man has been acquitted by a jury, they’ll talk, and nod and wink—and as far as the world goes, a man might often as well be guilty as not. It’s a breakdown blow, and it damages Lydgate as much as Bulstrode. I don’t pretend to say what is the truth. I only wish we had never heard the name of either Bulstrode or Lydgate. You’d better have been a Vincy all your life, and so had Rosamond.” Mrs. Bulstrode made no reply.\n\n“But you must bear up as well as you can, Harriet. People don’t blame you. And I’ll stand by you whatever you make up your mind to do,” said the brother, with rough but well-meaning affectionateness.\n\n“Give me your arm to the carriage, Walter,” said Mrs. Bulstrode. “I feel very weak.”\n\nAnd when she got home she was obliged to say to her daughter, “I am not well, my dear; I must go and lie down. Attend to your papa. Leave me in quiet. I shall take no dinner.”\n\nShe locked herself in her room. She needed time to get used to her maimed consciousness, her poor lopped life, before she could walk steadily to the place allotted her. A new searching light had fallen on her husband’s character, and she could not judge him leniently: the twenty years in which she had believed in him and venerated him by virtue of his concealments came back with particulars that made them seem an odious deceit. He had married her with that bad past life hidden behind him, and she had no faith left to protest his innocence of the worst that was imputed to him. Her honest ostentatious nature made the sharing of a merited dishonor as bitter as it could be to any mortal.\n\nBut this imperfectly taught woman, whose phrases and habits were an odd patchwork, had a loyal spirit within her. The man whose prosperity she had shared through nearly half a life, and who had unvaryingly cherished her—now that punishment had befallen him it was not possible to her in any sense to forsake him. There is a forsaking which still sits at the same board and lies on the same couch with the forsaken soul, withering it the more by unloving proximity. She knew, when she locked her door, that she should unlock it ready to go down to her unhappy husband and espouse his sorrow, and say of his guilt, I will mourn and not reproach. But she needed time to gather up her strength; she needed to sob out her farewell to all the gladness and pride of her life. When she had resolved to go down, she prepared herself by some little acts which might seem mere folly to a hard onlooker; they were her way of expressing to all spectators visible or invisible that she had begun a new life in which she embraced humiliation. She took off all her ornaments and put on a plain black gown, and instead of wearing her much-adorned cap and large bows of hair, she brushed her hair down and put on a plain bonnet-cap, which made her look suddenly like an early Methodist.\n\nBulstrode, who knew that his wife had been out and had come in saying that she was not well, had spent the time in an agitation equal to hers. He had looked forward to her learning the truth from others, and had acquiesced in that probability, as something easier to him than any confession. But now that he imagined the moment of her knowledge come, he awaited the result in anguish. His daughters had been obliged to consent to leave him, and though he had allowed some food to be brought to him, he had not touched it. He felt himself perishing slowly in unpitied misery. Perhaps he should never see his wife’s face with affection in it again. And if he turned to God there seemed to be no answer but the pressure of retribution.\n\nIt was eight o’clock in the evening before the door opened and his wife entered. He dared not look up at her. He sat with his eyes bent down, and as she went towards him she thought he looked smaller—he seemed so withered and shrunken. A movement of new compassion and old tenderness went through her like a great wave, and putting one hand on his which rested on the arm of the chair, and the other on his shoulder, she said, solemnly but kindly—\n\n“Look up, Nicholas.”\n\nHe raised his eyes with a little start and looked at her half amazed for a moment: her pale face, her changed, mourning dress, the trembling about her mouth, all said, “I know;” and her hands and eyes rested gently on him. He burst out crying and they cried together, she sitting at his side. They could not yet speak to each other of the shame which she was bearing with him, or of the acts which had brought it down on them. His confession was silent, and her promise of faithfulness was silent. Open-minded as she was, she nevertheless shrank from the words which would have expressed their mutual consciousness, as she would have shrunk from flakes of fire. She could not say, “How much is only slander and false suspicion?” and he did not say, “I am innocent.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXXV.\n\n“Le sentiment de la fausseté des plaisirs présents, et l’ignorance de la vanité des plaisirs absents causent l’inconstance.”—PASCAL.\nRosamond had a gleam of returning cheerfulness when the house was freed from the threatening figure, and when all the disagreeable creditors were paid. But she was not joyous: her married life had fulfilled none of her hopes, and had been quite spoiled for her imagination. In this brief interval of calm, Lydgate, remembering that he had often been stormy in his hours of perturbation, and mindful of the pain Rosamond had had to bear, was carefully gentle towards her; but he, too, had lost some of his old spirit, and he still felt it necessary to refer to an economical change in their way of living as a matter of course, trying to reconcile her to it gradually, and repressing his anger when she answered by wishing that he would go to live in London. When she did not make this answer, she listened languidly, and wondered what she had that was worth living for. The hard and contemptuous words which had fallen from her husband in his anger had deeply offended that vanity which he had at first called into active enjoyment; and what she regarded as his perverse way of looking at things, kept up a secret repulsion, which made her receive all his tenderness as a poor substitute for the happiness he had failed to give her. They were at a disadvantage with their neighbors, and there was no longer any outlook towards Quallingham—there was no outlook anywhere except in an occasional letter from Will Ladislaw. She had felt stung and disappointed by Will’s resolution to quit Middlemarch, for in spite of what she knew and guessed about his admiration for Dorothea, she secretly cherished the belief that he had, or would necessarily come to have, much more admiration for herself; Rosamond being one of those women who live much in the idea that each man they meet would have preferred them if the preference had not been hopeless. Mrs. Casaubon was all very well; but Will’s interest in her dated before he knew Mrs. Lydgate. Rosamond took his way of talking to herself, which was a mixture of playful fault-finding and hyperbolical gallantry, as the disguise of a deeper feeling; and in his presence she felt that agreeable titillation of vanity and sense of romantic drama which Lydgate’s presence had no longer the magic to create. She even fancied—what will not men and women fancy in these matters?—that Will exaggerated his admiration for Mrs. Casaubon in order to pique herself. In this way poor Rosamond’s brain had been busy before Will’s departure. He would have made, she thought, a much more suitable husband for her than she had found in Lydgate. No notion could have been falser than this, for Rosamond’s discontent in her marriage was due to the conditions of marriage itself, to its demand for self-suppression and tolerance, and not to the nature of her husband; but the easy conception of an unreal Better had a sentimental charm which diverted her ennui. She constructed a little romance which was to vary the flatness of her life: Will Ladislaw was always to be a bachelor and live near her, always to be at her command, and have an understood though never fully expressed passion for her, which would be sending out lambent flames every now and then in interesting scenes. His departure had been a proportionate disappointment, and had sadly increased her weariness of Middlemarch; but at first she had the alternative dream of pleasures in store from her intercourse with the family at Quallingham. Since then the troubles of her married life had deepened, and the absence of other relief encouraged her regretful rumination over that thin romance which she had once fed on. Men and women make sad mistakes about their own symptoms, taking their vague uneasy longings, sometimes for genius, sometimes for religion, and oftener still for a mighty love. Will Ladislaw had written chatty letters, half to her and half to Lydgate, and she had replied: their separation, she felt, was not likely to be final, and the change she now most longed for was that Lydgate should go to live in London; everything would be agreeable in London; and she had set to work with quiet determination to win this result, when there came a sudden, delightful promise which inspirited her.\n\nIt came shortly before the memorable meeting at the town-hall, and was nothing less than a letter from Will Ladislaw to Lydgate, which turned indeed chiefly on his new interest in plans of colonization, but mentioned incidentally, that he might find it necessary to pay a visit to Middlemarch within the next few weeks—a very pleasant necessity, he said, almost as good as holidays to a schoolboy. He hoped there was his old place on the rug, and a great deal of music in store for him. But he was quite uncertain as to the time. While Lydgate was reading the letter to Rosamond, her face looked like a reviving flower—it grew prettier and more blooming. There was nothing unendurable now: the debts were paid, Mr. Ladislaw was coming, and Lydgate would be persuaded to leave Middlemarch and settle in London, which was “so different from a provincial town.”\n\nThat was a bright bit of morning. But soon the sky became black over poor Rosamond. The presence of a new gloom in her husband, about which he was entirely reserved towards her—for he dreaded to expose his lacerated feeling to her neutrality and misconception—soon received a painfully strange explanation, alien to all her previous notions of what could affect her happiness. In the new gayety of her spirits, thinking that Lydgate had merely a worse fit of moodiness than usual, causing him to leave her remarks unanswered, and evidently to keep out of her way as much as possible, she chose, a few days after the meeting, and without speaking to him on the subject, to send out notes of invitation for a small evening party, feeling convinced that this was a judicious step, since people seemed to have been keeping aloof from them, and wanted restoring to the old habit of intercourse. When the invitations had been accepted, she would tell Lydgate, and give him a wise admonition as to how a medical man should behave to his neighbors; for Rosamond had the gravest little airs possible about other people’s duties. But all the invitations were declined, and the last answer came into Lydgate’s hands.\n\n“This is Chichely’s scratch. What is he writing to you about?” said Lydgate, wonderingly, as he handed the note to her. She was obliged to let him see it, and, looking at her severely, he said—\n\n“Why on earth have you been sending out invitations without telling me, Rosamond? I beg, I insist that you will not invite any one to this house. I suppose you have been inviting others, and they have refused too.” She said nothing.\n\n“Do you hear me?” thundered Lydgate.\n\n“Yes, certainly I hear you,” said Rosamond, turning her head aside with the movement of a graceful long-necked bird.\n\nLydgate tossed his head without any grace and walked out of the room, feeling himself dangerous. Rosamond’s thought was, that he was getting more and more unbearable—not that there was any new special reason for this peremptoriness. His indisposition to tell her anything in which he was sure beforehand that she would not be interested was growing into an unreflecting habit, and she was in ignorance of everything connected with the thousand pounds except that the loan had come from her uncle Bulstrode. Lydgate’s odious humors and their neighbors’ apparent avoidance of them had an unaccountable date for her in their relief from money difficulties. If the invitations had been accepted she would have gone to invite her mamma and the rest, whom she had seen nothing of for several days; and she now put on her bonnet to go and inquire what had become of them all, suddenly feeling as if there were a conspiracy to leave her in isolation with a husband disposed to offend everybody. It was after the dinner hour, and she found her father and mother seated together alone in the drawing-room. They greeted her with sad looks, saying “Well, my dear!” and no more. She had never seen her father look so downcast; and seating herself near him she said—\n\n“Is there anything the matter, papa?”\n\nHe did not answer, but Mrs. Vincy said, “Oh, my dear, have you heard nothing? It won’t be long before it reaches you.”\n\n“Is it anything about Tertius?” said Rosamond, turning pale. The idea of trouble immediately connected itself with what had been unaccountable to her in him.\n\n“Oh, my dear, yes. To think of your marrying into this trouble. Debt was bad enough, but this will be worse.”\n\n“Stay, stay, Lucy,” said Mr. Vincy. “Have you heard nothing about your uncle Bulstrode, Rosamond?”\n\n“No, papa,” said the poor thing, feeling as if trouble were not anything she had before experienced, but some invisible power with an iron grasp that made her soul faint within her.\n\nHer father told her everything, saying at the end, “It’s better for you to know, my dear. I think Lydgate must leave the town. Things have gone against him. I dare say he couldn’t help it. I don’t accuse him of any harm,” said Mr. Vincy. He had always before been disposed to find the utmost fault with Lydgate.\n\nThe shock to Rosamond was terrible. It seemed to her that no lot could be so cruelly hard as hers to have married a man who had become the centre of infamous suspicions. In many cases it is inevitable that the shame is felt to be the worst part of crime; and it would have required a great deal of disentangling reflection, such as had never entered into Rosamond’s life, for her in these moments to feel that her trouble was less than if her husband had been certainly known to have done something criminal. All the shame seemed to be there. And she had innocently married this man with the belief that he and his family were a glory to her! She showed her usual reticence to her parents, and only said, that if Lydgate had done as she wished he would have left Middlemarch long ago.\n\n“She bears it beyond anything,” said her mother when she was gone.\n\n“Ah, thank God!” said Mr. Vincy, who was much broken down.\n\nBut Rosamond went home with a sense of justified repugnance towards her husband. What had he really done—how had he really acted? She did not know. Why had he not told her everything? He did not speak to her on the subject, and of course she could not speak to him. It came into her mind once that she would ask her father to let her go home again; but dwelling on that prospect made it seem utter dreariness to her: a married woman gone back to live with her parents—life seemed to have no meaning for her in such a position: she could not contemplate herself in it.\n\nThe next two days Lydgate observed a change in her, and believed that she had heard the bad news. Would she speak to him about it, or would she go on forever in the silence which seemed to imply that she believed him guilty? We must remember that he was in a morbid state of mind, in which almost all contact was pain. Certainly Rosamond in this case had equal reason to complain of reserve and want of confidence on his part; but in the bitterness of his soul he excused himself;—was he not justified in shrinking from the task of telling her, since now she knew the truth she had no impulse to speak to him? But a deeper-lying consciousness that he was in fault made him restless, and the silence between them became intolerable to him; it was as if they were both adrift on one piece of wreck and looked away from each other.\n\nHe thought, “I am a fool. Haven’t I given up expecting anything? I have married care, not help.” And that evening he said—\n\n“Rosamond, have you heard anything that distresses you?”\n\n“Yes,” she answered, laying down her work, which she had been carrying on with a languid semi-consciousness, most unlike her usual self.\n\n“What have you heard?”\n\n“Everything, I suppose. Papa told me.”\n\n“That people think me disgraced?”\n\n“Yes,” said Rosamond, faintly, beginning to sew again automatically.\n\nThere was silence. Lydgate thought, “If she has any trust in me—any notion of what I am, she ought to speak now and say that she does not believe I have deserved disgrace.”\n\nBut Rosamond on her side went on moving her fingers languidly. Whatever was to be said on the subject she expected to come from Tertius. What did she know? And if he were innocent of any wrong, why did he not do something to clear himself?\n\nThis silence of hers brought a new rush of gall to that bitter mood in which Lydgate had been saying to himself that nobody believed in him—even Farebrother had not come forward. He had begun to question her with the intent that their conversation should disperse the chill fog which had gathered between them, but he felt his resolution checked by despairing resentment. Even this trouble, like the rest, she seemed to regard as if it were hers alone. He was always to her a being apart, doing what she objected to. He started from his chair with an angry impulse, and thrusting his hands in his pockets, walked up and down the room. There was an underlying consciousness all the while that he should have to master this anger, and tell her everything, and convince her of the facts. For he had almost learned the lesson that he must bend himself to her nature, and that because she came short in her sympathy, he must give the more. Soon he recurred to his intention of opening himself: the occasion must not be lost. If he could bring her to feel with some solemnity that here was a slander which must be met and not run away from, and that the whole trouble had come out of his desperate want of money, it would be a moment for urging powerfully on her that they should be one in the resolve to do with as little money as possible, so that they might weather the bad time and keep themselves independent. He would mention the definite measures which he desired to take, and win her to a willing spirit. He was bound to try this—and what else was there for him to do?\n\nHe did not know how long he had been walking uneasily backwards and forwards, but Rosamond felt that it was long, and wished that he would sit down. She too had begun to think this an opportunity for urging on Tertius what he ought to do. Whatever might be the truth about all this misery, there was one dread which asserted itself.\n\nLydgate at last seated himself, not in his usual chair, but in one nearer to Rosamond, leaning aside in it towards her, and looking at her gravely before he reopened the sad subject. He had conquered himself so far, and was about to speak with a sense of solemnity, as on an occasion which was not to be repeated. He had even opened his lips, when Rosamond, letting her hands fall, looked at him and said—\n\n“Surely, Tertius—”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Surely now at last you have given up the idea of staying in Middlemarch. I cannot go on living here. Let us go to London. Papa, and every one else, says you had better go. Whatever misery I have to put up with, it will be easier away from here.”\n\nLydgate felt miserably jarred. Instead of that critical outpouring for which he had prepared himself with effort, here was the old round to be gone through again. He could not bear it. With a quick change of countenance he rose and went out of the room.\n\nPerhaps if he had been strong enough to persist in his determination to be the more because she was less, that evening might have had a better issue. If his energy could have borne down that check, he might still have wrought on Rosamond’s vision and will. We cannot be sure that any natures, however inflexible or peculiar, will resist this effect from a more massive being than their own. They may be taken by storm and for the moment converted, becoming part of the soul which enwraps them in the ardor of its movement. But poor Lydgate had a throbbing pain within him, and his energy had fallen short of its task.\n\nThe beginning of mutual understanding and resolve seemed as far off as ever; nay, it seemed blocked out by the sense of unsuccessful effort. They lived on from day to day with their thoughts still apart, Lydgate going about what work he had in a mood of despair, and Rosamond feeling, with some justification, that he was behaving cruelly. It was of no use to say anything to Tertius; but when Will Ladislaw came, she was determined to tell him everything. In spite of her general reticence, she needed some one who would recognize her wrongs.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXVI.\n\nTo mercy, pity, peace, and love All pray in their distress, And to these virtues of delight, Return their thankfulness. . . . . . . For Mercy has a human heart, Pity a human face; And Love, the human form divine; And Peace, the human dress. —WILLIAM BLAKE: Songs of Innocence.\n\nSome days later, Lydgate was riding to Lowick Manor, in consequence of a summons from Dorothea. The summons had not been unexpected, since it had followed a letter from Mr. Bulstrode, in which he stated that he had resumed his arrangements for quitting Middlemarch, and must remind Lydgate of his previous communications about the Hospital, to the purport of which he still adhered. It had been his duty, before taking further steps, to reopen the subject with Mrs. Casaubon, who now wished, as before, to discuss the question with Lydgate. “Your views may possibly have undergone some change,” wrote Mr. Bulstrode; “but, in that case also, it is desirable that you should lay them before her.”\n\nDorothea awaited his arrival with eager interest. Though, in deference to her masculine advisers, she had refrained from what Sir James had called “interfering in this Bulstrode business,” the hardship of Lydgate’s position was continually in her mind, and when Bulstrode applied to her again about the hospital, she felt that the opportunity was come to her which she had been hindered from hastening. In her luxurious home, wandering under the boughs of her own great trees, her thought was going out over the lot of others, and her emotions were imprisoned. The idea of some active good within her reach, “haunted her like a passion,” and another’s need having once come to her as a distinct image, preoccupied her desire with the yearning to give relief, and made her own ease tasteless. She was full of confident hope about this interview with Lydgate, never heeding what was said of his personal reserve; never heeding that she was a very young woman. Nothing could have seemed more irrelevant to Dorothea than insistence on her youth and sex when she was moved to show her human fellowship.\n\nAs she sat waiting in the library, she could do nothing but live through again all the past scenes which had brought Lydgate into her memories. They all owed their significance to her marriage and its troubles—but no; there were two occasions in which the image of Lydgate had come painfully in connection with his wife and some one else. The pain had been allayed for Dorothea, but it had left in her an awakened conjecture as to what Lydgate’s marriage might be to him, a susceptibility to the slightest hint about Mrs. Lydgate. These thoughts were like a drama to her, and made her eyes bright, and gave an attitude of suspense to her whole frame, though she was only looking out from the brown library on to the turf and the bright green buds which stood in relief against the dark evergreens.\n\nWhen Lydgate came in, she was almost shocked at the change in his face, which was strikingly perceptible to her who had not seen him for two months. It was not the change of emaciation, but that effect which even young faces will very soon show from the persistent presence of resentment and despondency. Her cordial look, when she put out her hand to him, softened his expression, but only with melancholy.\n\n“I have wished very much to see you for a long while, Mr. Lydgate,” said Dorothea when they were seated opposite each other; “but I put off asking you to come until Mr. Bulstrode applied to me again about the Hospital. I know that the advantage of keeping the management of it separate from that of the Infirmary depends on you, or, at least, on the good which you are encouraged to hope for from having it under your control. And I am sure you will not refuse to tell me exactly what you think.”\n\n“You want to decide whether you should give a generous support to the Hospital,” said Lydgate. “I cannot conscientiously advise you to do it in dependence on any activity of mine. I may be obliged to leave the town.”\nHe spoke curtly, feeling the ache of despair as to his being able to carry out any purpose that Rosamond had set her mind against.\n\n“Not because there is no one to believe in you?” said Dorothea, pouring out her words in clearness from a full heart. “I know the unhappy mistakes about you. I knew them from the first moment to be mistakes. You have never done anything vile. You would not do anything dishonorable.”\n\nIt was the first assurance of belief in him that had fallen on Lydgate’s ears. He drew a deep breath, and said, “Thank you.” He could say no more: it was something very new and strange in his life that these few words of trust from a woman should be so much to him.\n\n“I beseech you to tell me how everything was,” said Dorothea, fearlessly. “I am sure that the truth would clear you.”\n\nLydgate started up from his chair and went towards the window, forgetting where he was. He had so often gone over in his mind the possibility of explaining everything without aggravating appearances that would tell, perhaps unfairly, against Bulstrode, and had so often decided against it—he had so often said to himself that his assertions would not change people’s impressions—that Dorothea’s words sounded like a temptation to do something which in his soberness he had pronounced to be unreasonable.\n\n“Tell me, pray,” said Dorothea, with simple earnestness; “then we can consult together. It is wicked to let people think evil of any one falsely, when it can be hindered.”\n\nLydgate turned, remembering where he was, and saw Dorothea’s face looking up at him with a sweet trustful gravity. The presence of a noble nature, generous in its wishes, ardent in its charity, changes the lights for us: we begin to see things again in their larger, quieter masses, and to believe that we too can be seen and judged in the wholeness of our character. That influence was beginning to act on Lydgate, who had for many days been seeing all life as one who is dragged and struggling amid the throng. He sat down again, and felt that he was recovering his old self in the consciousness that he was with one who believed in it.\n\n“I don’t want,” he said, “to bear hard on Bulstrode, who has lent me money of which I was in need—though I would rather have gone without it now. He is hunted down and miserable, and has only a poor thread of life in him. But I should like to tell you everything. It will be a comfort to me to speak where belief has gone beforehand, and where I shall not seem to be offering assertions of my own honesty. You will feel what is fair to another, as you feel what is fair to me.”\n\n“Do trust me,” said Dorothea; “I will not repeat anything without your leave. But at the very least, I could say that you have made all the circumstances clear to me, and that I know you are not in any way guilty. Mr. Farebrother would believe me, and my uncle, and Sir James Chettam. Nay, there are persons in Middlemarch to whom I could go; although they don’t know much of me, they would believe me. They would know that I could have no other motive than truth and justice. I would take any pains to clear you. I have very little to do. There is nothing better that I can do in the world.”\n\nDorothea’s voice, as she made this childlike picture of what she would do, might have been almost taken as a proof that she could do it effectively. The searching tenderness of her woman’s tones seemed made for a defence against ready accusers. Lydgate did not stay to think that she was Quixotic: he gave himself up, for the first time in his life, to the exquisite sense of leaning entirely on a generous sympathy, without any check of proud reserve. And he told her everything, from the time when, under the pressure of his difficulties, he unwillingly made his first application to Bulstrode; gradually, in the relief of speaking, getting into a more thorough utterance of what had gone on in his mind—entering fully into the fact that his treatment of the patient was opposed to the dominant practice, into his doubts at the last, his ideal of medical duty, and his uneasy consciousness that the acceptance of the money had made some difference in his private inclination and professional behavior, though not in his fulfilment of any publicly recognized obligation.\n\n“It has come to my knowledge since,” he added, “that Hawley sent some one to examine the housekeeper at Stone Court, and she said that she gave the patient all the opium in the phial I left, as well as a good deal of brandy. But that would not have been opposed to ordinary prescriptions, even of first-rate men. The suspicions against me had no hold there: they are grounded on the knowledge that I took money, that Bulstrode had strong motives for wishing the man to die, and that he gave me the money as a bribe to concur in some malpractices or other against the patient—that in any case I accepted a bribe to hold my tongue. They are just the suspicions that cling the most obstinately, because they lie in people’s inclination and can never be disproved. How my orders came to be disobeyed is a question to which I don’t know the answer. It is still possible that Bulstrode was innocent of any criminal intention—even possible that he had nothing to do with the disobedience, and merely abstained from mentioning it. But all that has nothing to do with the public belief. It is one of those cases on which a man is condemned on the ground of his character—it is believed that he has committed a crime in some undefined way, because he had the motive for doing it; and Bulstrode’s character has enveloped me, because I took his money. I am simply blighted—like a damaged ear of corn—the business is done and can’t be undone.”\n\n“Oh, it is hard!” said Dorothea. “I understand the difficulty there is in your vindicating yourself. And that all this should have come to you who had meant to lead a higher life than the common, and to find out better ways—I cannot bear to rest in this as unchangeable. I know you meant that. I remember what you said to me when you first spoke to me about the hospital. There is no sorrow I have thought more about than that—to love what is great, and try to reach it, and yet to fail.”\n\n“Yes,” said Lydgate, feeling that here he had found room for the full meaning of his grief. “I had some ambition. I meant everything to be different with me. I thought I had more strength and mastery. But the most terrible obstacles are such as nobody can see except oneself.”\n\n“Suppose,” said Dorothea, meditatively,—“suppose we kept on the Hospital according to the present plan, and you stayed here though only with the friendship and support of a few, the evil feeling towards you would gradually die out; there would come opportunities in which people would be forced to acknowledge that they had been unjust to you, because they would see that your purposes were pure. You may still win a great fame like the Louis and Laennec I have heard you speak of, and we shall all be proud of you,” she ended, with a smile.\n\n“That might do if I had my old trust in myself,” said Lydgate, mournfully. “Nothing galls me more than the notion of turning round and running away before this slander, leaving it unchecked behind me. Still, I can’t ask any one to put a great deal of money into a plan which depends on me.”\n\n“It would be quite worth my while,” said Dorothea, simply. “Only think. I am very uncomfortable with my money, because they tell me I have too little for any great scheme of the sort I like best, and yet I have too much. I don’t know what to do. I have seven hundred a-year of my own fortune, and nineteen hundred a-year that Mr. Casaubon left me, and between three and four thousand of ready money in the bank. I wished to raise money and pay it off gradually out of my income which I don’t want, to buy land with and found a village which should be a school of industry; but Sir James and my uncle have convinced me that the risk would be too great. So you see that what I should most rejoice at would be to have something good to do with my money: I should like it to make other people’s lives better to them. It makes me very uneasy—coming all to me who don’t want it.”\n\nA smile broke through the gloom of Lydgate’s face. The childlike grave-eyed earnestness with which Dorothea said all this was irresistible—blent into an adorable whole with her ready understanding of high experience. (Of lower experience such as plays a great part in the world, poor Mrs. Casaubon had a very blurred shortsighted knowledge, little helped by her imagination.) But she took the smile as encouragement of her plan.\n\n“I think you see now that you spoke too scrupulously,” she said, in a tone of persuasion. “The hospital would be one good; and making your life quite whole and well again would be another.”\n\nLydgate’s smile had died away. “You have the goodness as well as the money to do all that; if it could be done,” he said. “But—”\n\nHe hesitated a little while, looking vaguely towards the window; and she sat in silent expectation. At last he turned towards her and said impetuously—\n\n“Why should I not tell you?—you know what sort of bond marriage is. You will understand everything.”\n\nDorothea felt her heart beginning to beat faster. Had he that sorrow too? But she feared to say any word, and he went on immediately.\n\n“It is impossible for me now to do anything—to take any step without considering my wife’s happiness. The thing that I might like to do if I were alone, is become impossible to me. I can’t see her miserable. She married me without knowing what she was going into, and it might have been better for her if she had not married me.”\n\n“I know, I know—you could not give her pain, if you were not obliged to do it,” said Dorothea, with keen memory of her own life.\n\n“And she has set her mind against staying. She wishes to go. The troubles she has had here have wearied her,” said Lydgate, breaking off again, lest he should say too much.\n\n“But when she saw the good that might come of staying—” said Dorothea, remonstrantly, looking at Lydgate as if he had forgotten the reasons which had just been considered. He did not speak immediately.\n\n“She would not see it,” he said at last, curtly, feeling at first that this statement must do without explanation. “And, indeed, I have lost all spirit about carrying on my life here.” He paused a moment and then, following the impulse to let Dorothea see deeper into the difficulty of his life, he said, “The fact is, this trouble has come upon her confusedly. We have not been able to speak to each other about it. I am not sure what is in her mind about it: she may fear that I have really done something base. It is my fault; I ought to be more open. But I have been suffering cruelly.”\n\n“May I go and see her?” said Dorothea, eagerly. “Would she accept my sympathy? I would tell her that you have not been blamable before any one’s judgment but your own. I would tell her that you shall be cleared in every fair mind. I would cheer her heart. Will you ask her if I may go to see her? I did see her once.”\n\n“I am sure you may,” said Lydgate, seizing the proposition with some hope. “She would feel honored—cheered, I think, by the proof that you at least have some respect for me. I will not speak to her about your coming—that she may not connect it with my wishes at all. I know very well that I ought not to have left anything to be told her by others, but—”\n\nHe broke off, and there was a moment’s silence. Dorothea refrained from saying what was in her mind—how well she knew that there might be invisible barriers to speech between husband and wife. This was a point on which even sympathy might make a wound. She returned to the more outward aspect of Lydgate’s position, saying cheerfully—\n\n“And if Mrs. Lydgate knew that there were friends who would believe in you and support you, she might then be glad that you should stay in your place and recover your hopes—and do what you meant to do. Perhaps then you would see that it was right to agree with what I proposed about your continuing at the Hospital. Surely you would, if you still have faith in it as a means of making your knowledge useful?”\n\nLydgate did not answer, and she saw that he was debating with himself.\n\n“You need not decide immediately,” she said, gently. “A few days hence it will be early enough for me to send my answer to Mr. Bulstrode.”\n\nLydgate still waited, but at last turned to speak in his most decisive tones.\n\n“No; I prefer that there should be no interval left for wavering. I am no longer sure enough of myself—I mean of what it would be possible for me to do under the changed circumstances of my life. It would be dishonorable to let others engage themselves to anything serious in dependence on me. I might be obliged to go away after all; I see little chance of anything else. The whole thing is too problematic; I cannot consent to be the cause of your goodness being wasted. No—let the new Hospital be joined with the old Infirmary, and everything go on as it might have done if I had never come. I have kept a valuable register since I have been there; I shall send it to a man who will make use of it,” he ended bitterly. “I can think of nothing for a long while but getting an income.”\n\n“It hurts me very much to hear you speak so hopelessly,” said Dorothea. “It would be a happiness to your friends, who believe in your future, in your power to do great things, if you would let them save you from that. Think how much money I have; it would be like taking a burthen from me if you took some of it every year till you got free from this fettering want of income. Why should not people do these things? It is so difficult to make shares at all even. This is one way.”\n\n“God bless you, Mrs. Casaubon!” said Lydgate, rising as if with the same impulse that made his words energetic, and resting his arm on the back of the great leather chair he had been sitting in. “It is good that you should have such feelings. But I am not the man who ought to allow himself to benefit by them. I have not given guarantees enough. I must not at least sink into the degradation of being pensioned for work that I never achieved. It is very clear to me that I must not count on anything else than getting away from Middlemarch as soon as I can manage it. I should not be able for a long while, at the very best, to get an income here, and—and it is easier to make necessary changes in a new place. I must do as other men do, and think what will please the world and bring in money; look for a little opening in the London crowd, and push myself; set up in a watering-place, or go to some southern town where there are plenty of idle English, and get myself puffed,—that is the sort of shell I must creep into and try to keep my soul alive in.”\n\n“Now that is not brave,” said Dorothea,—“to give up the fight.”\n\n“No, it is not brave,” said Lydgate, “but if a man is afraid of creeping paralysis?” Then, in another tone, “Yet you have made a great difference in my courage by believing in me. Everything seems more bearable since I have talked to you; and if you can clear me in a few other minds, especially in Farebrother’s, I shall be deeply grateful. The point I wish you not to mention is the fact of disobedience to my orders. That would soon get distorted. After all, there is no evidence for me but people’s opinion of me beforehand. You can only repeat my own report of myself.”\n\n“Mr. Farebrother will believe—others will believe,” said Dorothea. “I can say of you what will make it stupidity to suppose that you would be bribed to do a wickedness.”\n\n“I don’t know,” said Lydgate, with something like a groan in his voice. “I have not taken a bribe yet. But there is a pale shade of bribery which is sometimes called prosperity. You will do me another great kindness, then, and come to see my wife?”\n\n“Yes, I will. I remember how pretty she is,” said Dorothea, into whose mind every impression about Rosamond had cut deep. “I hope she will like me.”\n\nAs Lydgate rode away, he thought, “This young creature has a heart large enough for the Virgin Mary. She evidently thinks nothing of her own future, and would pledge away half her income at once, as if she wanted nothing for herself but a chair to sit in from which she can look down with those clear eyes at the poor mortals who pray to her. She seems to have what I never saw in any woman before—a fountain of friendship towards men—a man can make a friend of her. Casaubon must have raised some heroic hallucination in her. I wonder if she could have any other sort of passion for a man? Ladislaw?—there was certainly an unusual feeling between them. And Casaubon must have had a notion of it. Well—her love might help a man more than her money.”\n\nDorothea on her side had immediately formed a plan of relieving Lydgate from his obligation to Bulstrode, which she felt sure was a part, though small, of the galling pressure he had to bear. She sat down at once under the inspiration of their interview, and wrote a brief note, in which she pleaded that she had more claim than Mr. Bulstrode had to the satisfaction of providing the money which had been serviceable to Lydgate—that it would be unkind in Lydgate not to grant her the position of being his helper in this small matter, the favor being entirely to her who had so little that was plainly marked out for her to do with her superfluous money. He might call her a creditor or by any other name if it did but imply that he granted her request. She enclosed a check for a thousand pounds, and determined to take the letter with her the next day when she went to see Rosamond.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXVII.\n\n“And thus thy fall hath left a kind of blot, To mark the full-fraught man and best indued With some suspicion.” —Henry V.\nThe next day Lydgate had to go to Brassing, and told Rosamond that he should be away until the evening. Of late she had never gone beyond her own house and garden, except to church, and once to see her papa, to whom she said, “If Tertius goes away, you will help us to move, will you not, papa? I suppose we shall have very little money. I am sure I hope some one will help us.” And Mr. Vincy had said, “Yes, child, I don’t mind a hundred or two. I can see the end of that.” With these exceptions she had sat at home in languid melancholy and suspense, fixing her mind on Will Ladislaw’s coming as the one point of hope and interest, and associating this with some new urgency on Lydgate to make immediate arrangements for leaving Middlemarch and going to London, till she felt assured that the coming would be a potent cause of the going, without at all seeing how. This way of establishing sequences is too common to be fairly regarded as a peculiar folly in Rosamond. And it is precisely this sort of sequence which causes the greatest shock when it is sundered: for to see how an effect may be produced is often to see possible missings and checks; but to see nothing except the desirable cause, and close upon it the desirable effect, rids us of doubt and makes our minds strongly intuitive. That was the process going on in poor Rosamond, while she arranged all objects around her with the same nicety as ever, only with more slowness—or sat down to the piano, meaning to play, and then desisting, yet lingering on the music stool with her white fingers suspended on the wooden front, and looking before her in dreamy ennui. Her melancholy had become so marked that Lydgate felt a strange timidity before it, as a perpetual silent reproach, and the strong man, mastered by his keen sensibilities towards this fair fragile creature whose life he seemed somehow to have bruised, shrank from her look, and sometimes started at her approach, fear of her and fear for her rushing in only the more forcibly after it had been momentarily expelled by exasperation.\n\nBut this morning Rosamond descended from her room upstairs—where she sometimes sat the whole day when Lydgate was out—equipped for a walk in the town. She had a letter to post—a letter addressed to Mr. Ladislaw and written with charming discretion, but intended to hasten his arrival by a hint of trouble. The servant-maid, their sole house-servant now, noticed her coming down-stairs in her walking dress, and thought “there never did anybody look so pretty in a bonnet poor thing.”\n\nMeanwhile Dorothea’s mind was filled with her project of going to Rosamond, and with the many thoughts, both of the past and the probable future, which gathered round the idea of that visit. Until yesterday when Lydgate had opened to her a glimpse of some trouble in his married life, the image of Mrs. Lydgate had always been associated for her with that of Will Ladislaw. Even in her most uneasy moments—even when she had been agitated by Mrs. Cadwallader’s painfully graphic report of gossip—her effort, nay, her strongest impulsive prompting, had been towards the vindication of Will from any sullying surmises; and when, in her meeting with him afterwards, she had at first interpreted his words as a probable allusion to a feeling towards Mrs. Lydgate which he was determined to cut himself off from indulging, she had had a quick, sad, excusing vision of the charm there might be in his constant opportunities of companionship with that fair creature, who most likely shared his other tastes as she evidently did his delight in music. But there had followed his parting words—the few passionate words in which he had implied that she herself was the object of whom his love held him in dread, that it was his love for her only which he was resolved not to declare but to carry away into banishment. From the time of that parting, Dorothea, believing in Will’s love for her, believing with a proud delight in his delicate sense of honor and his determination that no one should impeach him justly, felt her heart quite at rest as to the regard he might have for Mrs. Lydgate. She was sure that the regard was blameless.\n\nThere are natures in which, if they love us, we are conscious of having a sort of baptism and consecration: they bind us over to rectitude and purity by their pure belief about us; and our sins become that worst kind of sacrilege which tears down the invisible altar of trust. “If you are not good, none is good”—those little words may give a terrific meaning to responsibility, may hold a vitriolic intensity for remorse.\n\nDorothea’s nature was of that kind: her own passionate faults lay along the easily counted open channels of her ardent character; and while she was full of pity for the visible mistakes of others, she had not yet any material within her experience for subtle constructions and suspicions of hidden wrong. But that simplicity of hers, holding up an ideal for others in her believing conception of them, was one of the great powers of her womanhood. And it had from the first acted strongly on Will Ladislaw. He felt, when he parted from her, that the brief words by which he had tried to convey to her his feeling about herself and the division which her fortune made between them, would only profit by their brevity when Dorothea had to interpret them: he felt that in her mind he had found his highest estimate.\n\nAnd he was right there. In the months since their parting Dorothea had felt a delicious though sad repose in their relation to each other, as one which was inwardly whole and without blemish. She had an active force of antagonism within her, when the antagonism turned on the defence either of plans or persons that she believed in; and the wrongs which she felt that Will had received from her husband, and the external conditions which to others were grounds for slighting him, only gave the more tenacity to her affection and admiring judgment. And now with the disclosures about Bulstrode had come another fact affecting Will’s social position, which roused afresh Dorothea’s inward resistance to what was said about him in that part of her world which lay within park palings.\n\n“Young Ladislaw the grandson of a thieving Jew pawnbroker” was a phrase which had entered emphatically into the dialogues about the Bulstrode business, at Lowick, Tipton, and Freshitt, and was a worse kind of placard on poor Will’s back than the “Italian with white mice.” Upright Sir James Chettam was convinced that his own satisfaction was righteous when he thought with some complacency that here was an added league to that mountainous distance between Ladislaw and Dorothea, which enabled him to dismiss any anxiety in that direction as too absurd. And perhaps there had been some pleasure in pointing Mr. Brooke’s attention to this ugly bit of Ladislaw’s genealogy, as a fresh candle for him to see his own folly by. Dorothea had observed the animus with which Will’s part in the painful story had been recalled more than once; but she had uttered no word, being checked now, as she had not been formerly in speaking of Will, by the consciousness of a deeper relation between them which must always remain in consecrated secrecy. But her silence shrouded her resistant emotion into a more thorough glow; and this misfortune in Will’s lot which, it seemed, others were wishing to fling at his back as an opprobrium, only gave something more of enthusiasm to her clinging thought.\n\nShe entertained no visions of their ever coming into nearer union, and yet she had taken no posture of renunciation. She had accepted her whole relation to Will very simply as part of her marriage sorrows, and would have thought it very sinful in her to keep up an inward wail because she was not completely happy, being rather disposed to dwell on the superfluities of her lot. She could bear that the chief pleasures of her tenderness should lie in memory, and the idea of marriage came to her solely as a repulsive proposition from some suitor of whom she at present knew nothing, but whose merits, as seen by her friends, would be a source of torment to her:—“somebody who will manage your property for you, my dear,” was Mr. Brooke’s attractive suggestion of suitable characteristics. “I should like to manage it myself, if I knew what to do with it,” said Dorothea. No—she adhered to her declaration that she would never be married again, and in the long valley of her life which looked so flat and empty of waymarks, guidance would come as she walked along the road, and saw her fellow-passengers by the way.\n\nThis habitual state of feeling about Will Ladislaw had been strong in all her waking hours since she had proposed to pay a visit to Mrs. Lydgate, making a sort of background against which she saw Rosamond’s figure presented to her without hindrances to her interest and compassion. There was evidently some mental separation, some barrier to complete confidence which had arisen between this wife and the husband who had yet made her happiness a law to him. That was a trouble which no third person must directly touch. But Dorothea thought with deep pity of the loneliness which must have come upon Rosamond from the suspicions cast on her husband; and there would surely be help in the manifestation of respect for Lydgate and sympathy with her.\n\n“I shall talk to her about her husband,” thought Dorothea, as she was being driven towards the town. The clear spring morning, the scent of the moist earth, the fresh leaves just showing their creased-up wealth of greenery from out their half-opened sheaths, seemed part of the cheerfulness she was feeling from a long conversation with Mr. Farebrother, who had joyfully accepted the justifying explanation of Lydgate’s conduct. “I shall take Mrs. Lydgate good news, and perhaps she will like to talk to me and make a friend of me.”\n\nDorothea had another errand in Lowick Gate: it was about a new fine-toned bell for the school-house, and as she had to get out of her carriage very near to Lydgate’s, she walked thither across the street, having told the coachman to wait for some packages. The street door was open, and the servant was taking the opportunity of looking out at the carriage which was pausing within sight when it became apparent to her that the lady who “belonged to it” was coming towards her.\n\n“Is Mrs. Lydgate at home?” said Dorothea.\n\n“I’m not sure, my lady; I’ll see, if you’ll please to walk in,” said Martha, a little confused on the score of her kitchen apron, but collected enough to be sure that “mum” was not the right title for this queenly young widow with a carriage and pair. “Will you please to walk in, and I’ll go and see.”\n\n“Say that I am Mrs. Casaubon,” said Dorothea, as Martha moved forward intending to show her into the drawing-room and then to go up-stairs to see if Rosamond had returned from her walk.\n\nThey crossed the broader part of the entrance-hall, and turned up the passage which led to the garden. The drawing-room door was unlatched, and Martha, pushing it without looking into the room, waited for Mrs. Casaubon to enter and then turned away, the door having swung open and swung back again without noise.\n\nDorothea had less of outward vision than usual this morning, being filled with images of things as they had been and were going to be. She found herself on the other side of the door without seeing anything remarkable, but immediately she heard a voice speaking in low tones which startled her as with a sense of dreaming in daylight, and advancing unconsciously a step or two beyond the projecting slab of a bookcase, she saw, in the terrible illumination of a certainty which filled up all outlines, something which made her pause, motionless, without self-possession enough to speak.\n\nSeated with his back towards her on a sofa which stood against the wall on a line with the door by which she had entered, she saw Will Ladislaw: close by him and turned towards him with a flushed tearfulness which gave a new brilliancy to her face sat Rosamond, her bonnet hanging back, while Will leaning towards her clasped both her upraised hands in his and spoke with low-toned fervor.\n\nRosamond in her agitated absorption had not noticed the silently advancing figure; but when Dorothea, after the first immeasurable instant of this vision, moved confusedly backward and found herself impeded by some piece of furniture, Rosamond was suddenly aware of her presence, and with a spasmodic movement snatched away her hands and rose, looking at Dorothea who was necessarily arrested. Will Ladislaw, starting up, looked round also, and meeting Dorothea’s eyes with a new lightning in them, seemed changing to marble. But she immediately turned them away from him to Rosamond and said in a firm voice—\n\n“Excuse me, Mrs. Lydgate, the servant did not know that you were here. I called to deliver an important letter for Mr. Lydgate, which I wished to put into your own hands.”\n\nShe laid down the letter on the small table which had checked her retreat, and then including Rosamond and Will in one distant glance and bow, she went quickly out of the room, meeting in the passage the surprised Martha, who said she was sorry the mistress was not at home, and then showed the strange lady out with an inward reflection that grand people were probably more impatient than others.\n\nDorothea walked across the street with her most elastic step and was quickly in her carriage again.\n\n“Drive on to Freshitt Hall,” she said to the coachman, and any one looking at her might have thought that though she was paler than usual she was never animated by a more self-possessed energy. And that was really her experience. It was as if she had drunk a great draught of scorn that stimulated her beyond the susceptibility to other feelings. She had seen something so far below her belief, that her emotions rushed back from it and made an excited throng without an object. She needed something active to turn her excitement out upon. She felt power to walk and work for a day, without meat or drink. And she would carry out the purpose with which she had started in the morning, of going to Freshitt and Tipton to tell Sir James and her uncle all that she wished them to know about Lydgate, whose married loneliness under his trial now presented itself to her with new significance, and made her more ardent in readiness to be his champion. She had never felt anything like this triumphant power of indignation in the struggle of her married life, in which there had always been a quickly subduing pang; and she took it as a sign of new strength.\n\n“Dodo, how very bright your eyes are!” said Celia, when Sir James was gone out of the room. “And you don’t see anything you look at, Arthur or anything. You are going to do something uncomfortable, I know. Is it all about Mr. Lydgate, or has something else happened?” Celia had been used to watch her sister with expectation.\n\n“Yes, dear, a great many things have happened,” said Dodo, in her full tones.\n\n“I wonder what,” said Celia, folding her arms cozily and leaning forward upon them.\n\n“Oh, all the troubles of all people on the face of the earth,” said Dorothea, lifting her arms to the back of her head.\n\n“Dear me, Dodo, are you going to have a scheme for them?” said Celia, a little uneasy at this Hamlet-like raving.\n\nBut Sir James came in again, ready to accompany Dorothea to the Grange, and she finished her expedition well, not swerving in her resolution until she descended at her own door.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXVIII.\n\n“Would it were yesterday and I i’ the grave, With her sweet faith above for monument.”\n\nRosamond and Will stood motionless—they did not know how long—he looking towards the spot where Dorothea had stood, and she looking towards him with doubt. It seemed an endless time to Rosamond, in whose inmost soul there was hardly so much annoyance as gratification from what had just happened. Shallow natures dream of an easy sway over the emotions of others, trusting implicitly in their own petty magic to turn the deepest streams, and confident, by pretty gestures and remarks, of making the thing that is not as though it were. She knew that Will had received a severe blow, but she had been little used to imagining other people’s states of mind except as a material cut into shape by her own wishes; and she believed in her own power to soothe or subdue. Even Tertius, that most perverse of men, was always subdued in the long-run: events had been obstinate, but still Rosamond would have said now, as she did before her marriage, that she never gave up what she had set her mind on.\n\nShe put out her arm and laid the tips of her fingers on Will’s coat-sleeve.\n\n“Don’t touch me!” he said, with an utterance like the cut of a lash, darting from her, and changing from pink to white and back again, as if his whole frame were tingling with the pain of the sting. He wheeled round to the other side of the room and stood opposite to her, with the tips of his fingers in his pockets and his head thrown back, looking fiercely not at Rosamond but at a point a few inches away from her.\n\nShe was keenly offended, but the signs she made of this were such as only Lydgate was used to interpret. She became suddenly quiet and seated herself, untying her hanging bonnet and laying it down with her shawl. Her little hands which she folded before her were very cold.\n\nIt would have been safer for Will in the first instance to have taken up his hat and gone away; but he had felt no impulse to do this; on the contrary, he had a horrible inclination to stay and shatter Rosamond with his anger. It seemed as impossible to bear the fatality she had drawn down on him without venting his fury as it would be to a panther to bear the javelin-wound without springing and biting. And yet—how could he tell a woman that he was ready to curse her? He was fuming under a repressive law which he was forced to acknowledge: he was dangerously poised, and Rosamond’s voice now brought the decisive vibration. In flute-like tones of sarcasm she said—\n\n“You can easily go after Mrs. Casaubon and explain your preference.”\n\n“Go after her!” he burst out, with a sharp edge in his voice. “Do you think she would turn to look at me, or value any word I ever uttered to her again at more than a dirty feather?—Explain! How can a man explain at the expense of a woman?”\n\n“You can tell her what you please,” said Rosamond with more tremor.\n\n“Do you suppose she would like me better for sacrificing you? She is not a woman to be flattered because I made myself despicable—to believe that I must be true to her because I was a dastard to you.”\n\nHe began to move about with the restlessness of a wild animal that sees prey but cannot reach it. Presently he burst out again—\n\n“I had no hope before—not much—of anything better to come. But I had one certainty—that she believed in me. Whatever people had said or done about me, she believed in me.—That’s gone! She’ll never again think me anything but a paltry pretence—too nice to take heaven except upon flattering conditions, and yet selling myself for any devil’s change by the sly. She’ll think of me as an incarnate insult to her, from the first moment we—”\n\nWill stopped as if he had found himself grasping something that must not be thrown and shattered. He found another vent for his rage by snatching up Rosamond’s words again, as if they were reptiles to be throttled and flung off.\n\n“Explain! Tell a man to explain how he dropped into hell! Explain my preference! I never had a preference for her, any more than I have a preference for breathing. No other woman exists by the side of her. I would rather touch her hand if it were dead, than I would touch any other woman’s living.”\n\nRosamond, while these poisoned weapons were being hurled at her, was almost losing the sense of her identity, and seemed to be waking into some new terrible existence. She had no sense of chill resolute repulsion, of reticent self-justification such as she had known under Lydgate’s most stormy displeasure: all her sensibility was turned into a bewildering novelty of pain; she felt a new terrified recoil under a lash never experienced before. What another nature felt in opposition to her own was being burnt and bitten into her consciousness. When Will had ceased to speak she had become an image of sickened misery: her lips were pale, and her eyes had a tearless dismay in them. If it had been Tertius who stood opposite to her, that look of misery would have been a pang to him, and he would have sunk by her side to comfort her, with that strong-armed comfort which she had often held very cheap.\n\nLet it be forgiven to Will that he had no such movement of pity. He had felt no bond beforehand to this woman who had spoiled the ideal treasure of his life, and he held himself blameless. He knew that he was cruel, but he had no relenting in him yet.\n\nAfter he had done speaking, he still moved about, half in absence of mind, and Rosamond sat perfectly still. At length Will, seeming to bethink himself, took up his hat, yet stood some moments irresolute. He had spoken to her in a way that made a phrase of common politeness difficult to utter; and yet, now that he had come to the point of going away from her without further speech, he shrank from it as a brutality; he felt checked and stultified in his anger. He walked towards the mantel-piece and leaned his arm on it, and waited in silence for—he hardly knew what. The vindictive fire was still burning in him, and he could utter no word of retractation; but it was nevertheless in his mind that having come back to this hearth where he had enjoyed a caressing friendship he had found calamity seated there—he had had suddenly revealed to him a trouble that lay outside the home as well as within it. And what seemed a foreboding was pressing upon him as with slow pincers:—that his life might come to be enslaved by this helpless woman who had thrown herself upon him in the dreary sadness of her heart. But he was in gloomy rebellion against the fact that his quick apprehensiveness foreshadowed to him, and when his eyes fell on Rosamond’s blighted face it seemed to him that he was the more pitiable of the two; for pain must enter into its glorified life of memory before it can turn into compassion.\n\nAnd so they remained for many minutes, opposite each other, far apart, in silence; Will’s face still possessed by a mute rage, and Rosamond’s by a mute misery. The poor thing had no force to fling out any passion in return; the terrible collapse of the illusion towards which all her hope had been strained was a stroke which had too thoroughly shaken her: her little world was in ruins, and she felt herself tottering in the midst as a lonely bewildered consciousness.\n\nWill wished that she would speak and bring some mitigating shadow across his own cruel speech, which seemed to stand staring at them both in mockery of any attempt at revived fellowship. But she said nothing, and at last with a desperate effort over himself, he asked, “Shall I come in and see Lydgate this evening?”\n\n“If you like,” Rosamond answered, just audibly.\n\nAnd then Will went out of the house, Martha never knowing that he had been in.\n\nAfter he was gone, Rosamond tried to get up from her seat, but fell back fainting. When she came to herself again, she felt too ill to make the exertion of rising to ring the bell, and she remained helpless until the girl, surprised at her long absence, thought for the first time of looking for her in all the down-stairs rooms. Rosamond said that she had felt suddenly sick and faint, and wanted to be helped up-stairs. When there she threw herself on the bed with her clothes on, and lay in apparent torpor, as she had done once before on a memorable day of grief.\n\nLydgate came home earlier than he had expected, about half-past five, and found her there. The perception that she was ill threw every other thought into the background. When he felt her pulse, her eyes rested on him with more persistence than they had done for a long while, as if she felt some content that he was there. He perceived the difference in a moment, and seating himself by her put his arm gently under her, and bending over her said, “My poor Rosamond! has something agitated you?” Clinging to him she fell into hysterical sobbings and cries, and for the next hour he did nothing but soothe and tend her. He imagined that Dorothea had been to see her, and that all this effect on her nervous system, which evidently involved some new turning towards himself, was due to the excitement of the new impressions which that visit had raised.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXIX.\n\n“Now, I saw in my dream, that just as they had ended their talk, they drew nigh to a very miry slough, that was in the midst of the plain; and they, being heedless, did both fall suddenly into the bog. The name of the slough was Despond.”—BUNYAN.\n\nWhen Rosamond was quiet, and Lydgate had left her, hoping that she might soon sleep under the effect of an anodyne, he went into the drawing-room to fetch a book which he had left there, meaning to spend the evening in his work-room, and he saw on the table Dorothea’s letter addressed to him. He had not ventured to ask Rosamond if Mrs. Casaubon had called, but the reading of this letter assured him of the fact, for Dorothea mentioned that it was to be carried by herself.\n\nWhen Will Ladislaw came in a little later Lydgate met him with a surprise which made it clear that he had not been told of the earlier visit, and Will could not say, “Did not Mrs. Lydgate tell you that I came this morning?”\n\n“Poor Rosamond is ill,” Lydgate added immediately on his greeting.\n\n“Not seriously, I hope,” said Will.\n\n“No—only a slight nervous shock—the effect of some agitation. She has been overwrought lately. The truth is, Ladislaw, I am an unlucky devil. We have gone through several rounds of purgatory since you left, and I have lately got on to a worse ledge of it than ever. I suppose you are only just come down—you look rather battered—you have not been long enough in the town to hear anything?”\n\n“I travelled all night and got to the White Hart at eight o’clock this morning. I have been shutting myself up and resting,” said Will, feeling himself a sneak, but seeing no alternative to this evasion.\n\nAnd then he heard Lydgate’s account of the troubles which Rosamond had already depicted to him in her way. She had not mentioned the fact of Will’s name being connected with the public story—this detail not immediately affecting her—and he now heard it for the first time.\n\n“I thought it better to tell you that your name is mixed up with the disclosures,” said Lydgate, who could understand better than most men how Ladislaw might be stung by the revelation. “You will be sure to hear it as soon as you turn out into the town. I suppose it is true that Raffles spoke to you.”\n\n“Yes,” said Will, sardonically. “I shall be fortunate if gossip does not make me the most disreputable person in the whole affair. I should think the latest version must be, that I plotted with Raffles to murder Bulstrode, and ran away from Middlemarch for the purpose.”\n\nHe was thinking “Here is a new ring in the sound of my name to recommend it in her hearing; however—what does it signify now?”\n\nBut he said nothing of Bulstrode’s offer to him. Will was very open and careless about his personal affairs, but it was among the more exquisite touches in nature’s modelling of him that he had a delicate generosity which warned him into reticence here. He shrank from saying that he had rejected Bulstrode’s money, in the moment when he was learning that it was Lydgate’s misfortune to have accepted it.\n\nLydgate too was reticent in the midst of his confidence. He made no allusion to Rosamond’s feeling under their trouble, and of Dorothea he only said, “Mrs. Casaubon has been the one person to come forward and say that she had no belief in any of the suspicions against me.” Observing a change in Will’s face, he avoided any further mention of her, feeling himself too ignorant of their relation to each other not to fear that his words might have some hidden painful bearing on it. And it occurred to him that Dorothea was the real cause of the present visit to Middlemarch.\n\nThe two men were pitying each other, but it was only Will who guessed the extent of his companion’s trouble. When Lydgate spoke with desperate resignation of going to settle in London, and said with a faint smile, “We shall have you again, old fellow,” Will felt inexpressibly mournful, and said nothing. Rosamond had that morning entreated him to urge this step on Lydgate; and it seemed to him as if he were beholding in a magic panorama a future where he himself was sliding into that pleasureless yielding to the small solicitations of circumstance, which is a commoner history of perdition than any single momentous bargain.\n\nWe are on a perilous margin when we begin to look passively at our future selves, and see our own figures led with dull consent into insipid misdoing and shabby achievement. Poor Lydgate was inwardly groaning on that margin, and Will was arriving at it. It seemed to him this evening as if the cruelty of his outburst to Rosamond had made an obligation for him, and he dreaded the obligation: he dreaded Lydgate’s unsuspecting good-will: he dreaded his own distaste for his spoiled life, which would leave him in motiveless levity.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXX.\n\nStern lawgiver! yet thou dost wear The Godhead’s most benignant grace; Nor know we anything so fair As is the smile upon thy face; Flowers laugh before thee on their beds, And fragrance in thy footing treads; Thou dost preserve the Stars from wrong; And the most ancient Heavens, through thee, are fresh and strong. —WORDSWORTH: Ode to Duty.\n\nWhen Dorothea had seen Mr. Farebrother in the morning, she had promised to go and dine at the parsonage on her return from Freshitt. There was a frequent interchange of visits between her and the Farebrother family, which enabled her to say that she was not at all lonely at the Manor, and to resist for the present the severe prescription of a lady companion. When she reached home and remembered her engagement, she was glad of it; and finding that she had still an hour before she could dress for dinner, she walked straight to the schoolhouse and entered into a conversation with the master and mistress about the new bell, giving eager attention to their small details and repetitions, and getting up a dramatic sense that her life was very busy. She paused on her way back to talk to old Master Bunney who was putting in some garden-seeds, and discoursed wisely with that rural sage about the crops that would make the most return on a perch of ground, and the result of sixty years’ experience as to soils—namely, that if your soil was pretty mellow it would do, but if there came wet, wet, wet to make it all of a mummy, why then—\n\nFinding that the social spirit had beguiled her into being rather late, she dressed hastily and went over to the parsonage rather earlier than was necessary. That house was never dull, Mr. Farebrother, like another White of Selborne, having continually something new to tell of his inarticulate guests and proteges, whom he was teaching the boys not to torment; and he had just set up a pair of beautiful goats to be pets of the village in general, and to walk at large as sacred animals. The evening went by cheerfully till after tea, Dorothea talking more than usual and dilating with Mr. Farebrother on the possible histories of creatures that converse compendiously with their antennae, and for aught we know may hold reformed parliaments; when suddenly some inarticulate little sounds were heard which called everybody’s attention.\n\n“Henrietta Noble,” said Mrs. Farebrother, seeing her small sister moving about the furniture-legs distressfully, “what is the matter?”\n\n“I have lost my tortoise-shell lozenge-box. I fear the kitten has rolled it away,” said the tiny old lady, involuntarily continuing her beaver-like notes.\n“Is it a great treasure, aunt?” said Mr. Farebrother, putting up his glasses and looking at the carpet.\n\n“Mr. Ladislaw gave it me,” said Miss Noble. “A German box—very pretty, but if it falls it always spins away as far as it can.”\n“Oh, if it is Ladislaw’s present,” said Mr. Farebrother, in a deep tone of comprehension, getting up and hunting. The box was found at last under a chiffonier, and Miss Noble grasped it with delight, saying, “it was under a fender the last time.”\n\n“That is an affair of the heart with my aunt,” said Mr. Farebrother, smiling at Dorothea, as he reseated himself.\n\n“If Henrietta Noble forms an attachment to any one, Mrs. Casaubon,” said his mother, emphatically,—“she is like a dog—she would take their shoes for a pillow and sleep the better.”\n\n“Mr. Ladislaw’s shoes, I would,” said Henrietta Noble.\n\nDorothea made an attempt at smiling in return. She was surprised and annoyed to find that her heart was palpitating violently, and that it was quite useless to try after a recovery of her former animation. Alarmed at herself—fearing some further betrayal of a change so marked in its occasion, she rose and said in a low voice with undisguised anxiety, “I must go; I have overtired myself.”\n\nMr. Farebrother, quick in perception, rose and said, “It is true; you must have half-exhausted yourself in talking about Lydgate. That sort of work tells upon one after the excitement is over.”\n\nHe gave her his arm back to the Manor, but Dorothea did not attempt to speak, even when he said good-night.\n\nThe limit of resistance was reached, and she had sunk back helpless within the clutch of inescapable anguish. Dismissing Tantripp with a few faint words, she locked her door, and turning away from it towards the vacant room she pressed her hands hard on the top of her head, and moaned out—\n\nThen came the hour in which the waves of suffering shook her too thoroughly to leave any power of thought. She could only cry in loud whispers, between her sobs, after her lost belief which she had planted and kept alive from a very little seed since the days in Rome—after her lost joy of clinging with silent love and faith to one who, misprized by others, was worthy in her thought—after her lost woman’s pride of reigning in his memory—after her sweet dim perspective of hope, that along some pathway they should meet with unchanged recognition and take up the backward years as a yesterday.\n\nIn that hour she repeated what the merciful eyes of solitude have looked on for ages in the spiritual struggles of man—she besought hardness and coldness and aching weariness to bring her relief from the mysterious incorporeal might of her anguish: she lay on the bare floor and let the night grow cold around her; while her grand woman’s frame was shaken by sobs as if she had been a despairing child.\n\nThere were two images—two living forms that tore her heart in two, as if it had been the heart of a mother who seems to see her child divided by the sword, and presses one bleeding half to her breast while her gaze goes forth in agony towards the half which is carried away by the lying woman that has never known the mother’s pang.\n\nHere, with the nearness of an answering smile, here within the vibrating bond of mutual speech, was the bright creature whom she had trusted—who had come to her like the spirit of morning visiting the dim vault where she sat as the bride of a worn-out life; and now, with a full consciousness which had never awakened before, she stretched out her arms towards him and cried with bitter cries that their nearness was a parting vision: she discovered her passion to herself in the unshrinking utterance of despair.\n\nAnd there, aloof, yet persistently with her, moving wherever she moved, was the Will Ladislaw who was a changed belief exhausted of hope, a detected illusion—no, a living man towards whom there could not yet struggle any wail of regretful pity, from the midst of scorn and indignation and jealous offended pride. The fire of Dorothea’s anger was not easily spent, and it flamed out in fitful returns of spurning reproach. Why had he come obtruding his life into hers, hers that might have been whole enough without him? Why had he brought his cheap regard and his lip-born words to her who had nothing paltry to give in exchange? He knew that he was deluding her—wished, in the very moment of farewell, to make her believe that he gave her the whole price of her heart, and knew that he had spent it half before. Why had he not stayed among the crowd of whom she asked nothing—but only prayed that they might be less contemptible?\n\nBut she lost energy at last even for her loud-whispered cries and moans: she subsided into helpless sobs, and on the cold floor she sobbed herself to sleep.\n\nIn the chill hours of the morning twilight, when all was dim around her, she awoke—not with any amazed wondering where she was or what had happened, but with the clearest consciousness that she was looking into the eyes of sorrow. She rose, and wrapped warm things around her, and seated herself in a great chair where she had often watched before. She was vigorous enough to have borne that hard night without feeling ill in body, beyond some aching and fatigue; but she had waked to a new condition: she felt as if her soul had been liberated from its terrible conflict; she was no longer wrestling with her grief, but could sit down with it as a lasting companion and make it a sharer in her thoughts. For now the thoughts came thickly. It was not in Dorothea’s nature, for longer than the duration of a paroxysm, to sit in the narrow cell of her calamity, in the besotted misery of a consciousness that only sees another’s lot as an accident of its own.\n\nShe began now to live through that yesterday morning deliberately again, forcing herself to dwell on every detail and its possible meaning. Was she alone in that scene? Was it her event only? She forced herself to think of it as bound up with another woman’s life—a woman towards whom she had set out with a longing to carry some clearness and comfort into her beclouded youth. In her first outleap of jealous indignation and disgust, when quitting the hateful room, she had flung away all the mercy with which she had undertaken that visit. She had enveloped both Will and Rosamond in her burning scorn, and it seemed to her as if Rosamond were burned out of her sight forever. But that base prompting which makes a women more cruel to a rival than to a faithless lover, could have no strength of recurrence in Dorothea when the dominant spirit of justice within her had once overcome the tumult and had once shown her the truer measure of things. All the active thought with which she had before been representing to herself the trials of Lydgate’s lot, and this young marriage union which, like her own, seemed to have its hidden as well as evident troubles—all this vivid sympathetic experience returned to her now as a power: it asserted itself as acquired knowledge asserts itself and will not let us see as we saw in the day of our ignorance. She said to her own irremediable grief, that it should make her more helpful, instead of driving her back from effort.\n\nAnd what sort of crisis might not this be in three lives whose contact with hers laid an obligation on her as if they had been suppliants bearing the sacred branch? The objects of her rescue were not to be sought out by her fancy: they were chosen for her. She yearned towards the perfect Right, that it might make a throne within her, and rule her errant will. “What should I do—how should I act now, this very day, if I could clutch my own pain, and compel it to silence, and think of those three?”\n\nIt had taken long for her to come to that question, and there was light piercing into the room. She opened her curtains, and looked out towards the bit of road that lay in view, with fields beyond outside the entrance-gates. On the road there was a man with a bundle on his back and a woman carrying her baby; in the field she could see figures moving—perhaps the shepherd with his dog. Far off in the bending sky was the pearly light; and she felt the largeness of the world and the manifold wakings of men to labor and endurance. She was a part of that involuntary, palpitating life, and could neither look out on it from her luxurious shelter as a mere spectator, nor hide her eyes in selfish complaining.\n\nWhat she would resolve to do that day did not yet seem quite clear, but something that she could achieve stirred her as with an approaching murmur which would soon gather distinctness. She took off the clothes which seemed to have some of the weariness of a hard watching in them, and began to make her toilet. Presently she rang for Tantripp, who came in her dressing-gown.\n\n“Why, madam, you’ve never been in bed this blessed night,” burst out Tantripp, looking first at the bed and then at Dorothea’s face, which in spite of bathing had the pale cheeks and pink eyelids of a mater dolorosa. “You’ll kill yourself, you will. Anybody might think now you had a right to give yourself a little comfort.”\n\n“Don’t be alarmed, Tantripp,” said Dorothea, smiling. “I have slept; I am not ill. I shall be glad of a cup of coffee as soon as possible. And I want you to bring me my new dress; and most likely I shall want my new bonnet to-day.”\n\n“They’ve lain there a month and more ready for you, madam, and most thankful I shall be to see you with a couple o’ pounds’ worth less of crape,” said Tantripp, stooping to light the fire. “There’s a reason in mourning, as I’ve always said; and three folds at the bottom of your skirt and a plain quilling in your bonnet—and if ever anybody looked like an angel, it’s you in a net quilling—is what’s consistent for a second year. At least, that’s my thinking,” ended Tantripp, looking anxiously at the fire; “and if anybody was to marry me flattering himself I should wear those hijeous weepers two years for him, he’d be deceived by his own vanity, that’s all.”\n\n“The fire will do, my good Tan,” said Dorothea, speaking as she used to do in the old Lausanne days, only with a very low voice; “get me the coffee.”\n\nShe folded herself in the large chair, and leaned her head against it in fatigued quiescence, while Tantripp went away wondering at this strange contrariness in her young mistress—that just the morning when she had more of a widow’s face than ever, she should have asked for her lighter mourning which she had waived before. Tantripp would never have found the clew to this mystery. Dorothea wished to acknowledge that she had not the less an active life before her because she had buried a private joy; and the tradition that fresh garments belonged to all initiation, haunting her mind, made her grasp after even that slight outward help towards calm resolve. For the resolve was not easy.\n\nNevertheless at eleven o’clock she was walking towards Middlemarch, having made up her mind that she would make as quietly and unnoticeably as possible her second attempt to see and save Rosamond.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXI.\n\nDu Erde warst auch diese Nacht beständig, Und athmest neu erquickt zu meinen Füssen, Beginnest schon mit Lust mich zu umgeben, Du regst und rührst ein kräftiges Beschliessen Zum höchsten Dasein immerfort zu streben. —Faust: 2r Theil.\n\nWhen Dorothea was again at Lydgate’s door speaking to Martha, he was in the room close by with the door ajar, preparing to go out. He heard her voice, and immediately came to her.\n\n“Do you think that Mrs. Lydgate can receive me this morning?” she said, having reflected that it would be better to leave out all allusion to her previous visit.\n\n“I have no doubt she will,” said Lydgate, suppressing his thought about Dorothea’s looks, which were as much changed as Rosamond’s, “if you will be kind enough to come in and let me tell her that you are here. She has not been very well since you were here yesterday, but she is better this morning, and I think it is very likely that she will be cheered by seeing you again.”\n\nIt was plain that Lydgate, as Dorothea had expected, knew nothing about the circumstances of her yesterday’s visit; nay, he appeared to imagine that she had carried it out according to her intention. She had prepared a little note asking Rosamond to see her, which she would have given to the servant if he had not been in the way, but now she was in much anxiety as to the result of his announcement.\n\nAfter leading her into the drawing-room, he paused to take a letter from his pocket and put it into her hands, saying, “I wrote this last night, and was going to carry it to Lowick in my ride. When one is grateful for something too good for common thanks, writing is less unsatisfactory than speech—one does not at least hear how inadequate the words are.”\n\nDorothea’s face brightened. “It is I who have most to thank for, since you have let me take that place. You have consented?” she said, suddenly doubting.\n\n“Yes, the check is going to Bulstrode to-day.”\n\nHe said no more, but went up-stairs to Rosamond, who had but lately finished dressing herself, and sat languidly wondering what she should do next, her habitual industry in small things, even in the days of her sadness, prompting her to begin some kind of occupation, which she dragged through slowly or paused in from lack of interest. She looked ill, but had recovered her usual quietude of manner, and Lydgate had feared to disturb her by any questions. He had told her of Dorothea’s letter containing the check, and afterwards he had said, “Ladislaw is come, Rosy; he sat with me last night; I dare say he will be here again to-day. I thought he looked rather battered and depressed.” And Rosamond had made no reply.\n\nNow, when he came up, he said to her very gently, “Rosy, dear, Mrs. Casaubon is come to see you again; you would like to see her, would you not?” That she colored and gave rather a startled movement did not surprise him after the agitation produced by the interview yesterday—a beneficent agitation, he thought, since it seemed to have made her turn to him again.\n\nRosamond dared not say no. She dared not with a tone of her voice touch the facts of yesterday. Why had Mrs. Casaubon come again? The answer was a blank which Rosamond could only fill up with dread, for Will Ladislaw’s lacerating words had made every thought of Dorothea a fresh smart to her. Nevertheless, in her new humiliating uncertainty she dared do nothing but comply. She did not say yes, but she rose and let Lydgate put a light shawl over her shoulders, while he said, “I am going out immediately.” Then something crossed her mind which prompted her to say, “Pray tell Martha not to bring any one else into the drawing-room.” And Lydgate assented, thinking that he fully understood this wish. He led her down to the drawing-room door, and then turned away, observing to himself that he was rather a blundering husband to be dependent for his wife’s trust in him on the influence of another woman.\n\nRosamond, wrapping her soft shawl around her as she walked towards Dorothea, was inwardly wrapping her soul in cold reserve. Had Mrs. Casaubon come to say anything to her about Will? If so, it was a liberty that Rosamond resented; and she prepared herself to meet every word with polite impassibility. Will had bruised her pride too sorely for her to feel any compunction towards him and Dorothea: her own injury seemed much the greater. Dorothea was not only the “preferred” woman, but had also a formidable advantage in being Lydgate’s benefactor; and to poor Rosamond’s pained confused vision it seemed that this Mrs. Casaubon—this woman who predominated in all things concerning her—must have come now with the sense of having the advantage, and with animosity prompting her to use it. Indeed, not Rosamond only, but any one else, knowing the outer facts of the case, and not the simple inspiration on which Dorothea acted, might well have wondered why she came.\n\nLooking like the lovely ghost of herself, her graceful slimness wrapped in her soft white shawl, the rounded infantine mouth and cheek inevitably suggesting mildness and innocence, Rosamond paused at three yards’ distance from her visitor and bowed. But Dorothea, who had taken off her gloves, from an impulse which she could never resist when she wanted a sense of freedom, came forward, and with her face full of a sad yet sweet openness, put out her hand. Rosamond could not avoid meeting her glance, could not avoid putting her small hand into Dorothea’s, which clasped it with gentle motherliness; and immediately a doubt of her own prepossessions began to stir within her. Rosamond’s eye was quick for faces; she saw that Mrs. Casaubon’s face looked pale and changed since yesterday, yet gentle, and like the firm softness of her hand. But Dorothea had counted a little too much on her own strength: the clearness and intensity of her mental action this morning were the continuance of a nervous exaltation which made her frame as dangerously responsive as a bit of finest Venetian crystal; and in looking at Rosamond, she suddenly found her heart swelling, and was unable to speak—all her effort was required to keep back tears. She succeeded in that, and the emotion only passed over her face like the spirit of a sob; but it added to Rosamond’s impression that Mrs. Casaubon’s state of mind must be something quite different from what she had imagined.\n\nSo they sat down without a word of preface on the two chairs that happened to be nearest, and happened also to be close together; though Rosamond’s notion when she first bowed was that she should stay a long way off from Mrs. Casaubon. But she ceased thinking how anything would turn out—merely wondering what would come. And Dorothea began to speak quite simply, gathering firmness as she went on.\n\n“I had an errand yesterday which I did not finish; that is why I am here again so soon. You will not think me too troublesome when I tell you that I came to talk to you about the injustice that has been shown towards Mr. Lydgate. It will cheer you—will it not?—to know a great deal about him, that he may not like to speak about himself just because it is in his own vindication and to his own honor. You will like to know that your husband has warm friends, who have not left off believing in his high character? You will let me speak of this without thinking that I take a liberty?”\n\nThe cordial, pleading tones which seemed to flow with generous heedlessness above all the facts which had filled Rosamond’s mind as grounds of obstruction and hatred between her and this woman, came as soothingly as a warm stream over her shrinking fears. Of course Mrs. Casaubon had the facts in her mind, but she was not going to speak of anything connected with them. That relief was too great for Rosamond to feel much else at the moment. She answered prettily, in the new ease of her soul—\n\n“I know you have been very good. I shall like to hear anything you will say to me about Tertius.”\n\n“The day before yesterday,” said Dorothea, “when I had asked him to come to Lowick to give me his opinion on the affairs of the Hospital, he told me everything about his conduct and feelings in this sad event which has made ignorant people cast suspicions on him. The reason he told me was because I was very bold and asked him. I believed that he had never acted dishonorably, and I begged him to tell me the history. He confessed to me that he had never told it before, not even to you, because he had a great dislike to say, ‘I was not wrong,’ as if that were proof, when there are guilty people who will say so. The truth is, he knew nothing of this man Raffles, or that there were any bad secrets about him; and he thought that Mr. Bulstrode offered him the money because he repented, out of kindness, of having refused it before. All his anxiety about his patient was to treat him rightly, and he was a little uncomfortable that the case did not end as he had expected; but he thought then and still thinks that there may have been no wrong in it on any one’s part. And I have told Mr. Farebrother, and Mr. Brooke, and Sir James Chettam: they all believe in your husband. That will cheer you, will it not? That will give you courage?”\n\nDorothea’s face had become animated, and as it beamed on Rosamond very close to her, she felt something like bashful timidity before a superior, in the presence of this self-forgetful ardor. She said, with blushing embarrassment, “Thank you: you are very kind.”\n\n“And he felt that he had been so wrong not to pour out everything about this to you. But you will forgive him. It was because he feels so much more about your happiness than anything else—he feels his life bound into one with yours, and it hurts him more than anything, that his misfortunes must hurt you. He could speak to me because I am an indifferent person. And then I asked him if I might come to see you; because I felt so much for his trouble and yours. That is why I came yesterday, and why I am come to-day. Trouble is so hard to bear, is it not?— How can we live and think that any one has trouble—piercing trouble—and we could help them, and never try?”\n\nDorothea, completely swayed by the feeling that she was uttering, forgot everything but that she was speaking from out the heart of her own trial to Rosamond’s. The emotion had wrought itself more and more into her utterance, till the tones might have gone to one’s very marrow, like a low cry from some suffering creature in the darkness. And she had unconsciously laid her hand again on the little hand that she had pressed before.\n\nRosamond, with an overmastering pang, as if a wound within her had been probed, burst into hysterical crying as she had done the day before when she clung to her husband. Poor Dorothea was feeling a great wave of her own sorrow returning over her—her thought being drawn to the possible share that Will Ladislaw might have in Rosamond’s mental tumult. She was beginning to fear that she should not be able to suppress herself enough to the end of this meeting, and while her hand was still resting on Rosamond’s lap, though the hand underneath it was withdrawn, she was struggling against her own rising sobs. She tried to master herself with the thought that this might be a turning-point in three lives—not in her own; no, there the irrevocable had happened, but—in those three lives which were touching hers with the solemn neighborhood of danger and distress. The fragile creature who was crying close to her—there might still be time to rescue her from the misery of false incompatible bonds; and this moment was unlike any other: she and Rosamond could never be together again with the same thrilling consciousness of yesterday within them both. She felt the relation between them to be peculiar enough to give her a peculiar influence, though she had no conception that the way in which her own feelings were involved was fully known to Mrs. Lydgate.\n\nIt was a newer crisis in Rosamond’s experience than even Dorothea could imagine: she was under the first great shock that had shattered her dream-world in which she had been easily confident of herself and critical of others; and this strange unexpected manifestation of feeling in a woman whom she had approached with a shrinking aversion and dread, as one who must necessarily have a jealous hatred towards her, made her soul totter all the more with a sense that she had been walking in an unknown world which had just broken in upon her.\n\nWhen Rosamond’s convulsed throat was subsiding into calm, and she withdrew the handkerchief with which she had been hiding her face, her eyes met Dorothea’s as helplessly as if they had been blue flowers. What was the use of thinking about behavior after this crying? And Dorothea looked almost as childish, with the neglected trace of a silent tear. Pride was broken down between these two.\n\n“We were talking about your husband,” Dorothea said, with some timidity. “I thought his looks were sadly changed with suffering the other day. I had not seen him for many weeks before. He said he had been feeling very lonely in his trial; but I think he would have borne it all better if he had been able to be quite open with you.”\n\n“Tertius is so angry and impatient if I say anything,” said Rosamond, imagining that he had been complaining of her to Dorothea. “He ought not to wonder that I object to speak to him on painful subjects.”\n\n“It was himself he blamed for not speaking,” said Dorothea. “What he said of you was, that he could not be happy in doing anything which made you unhappy—that his marriage was of course a bond which must affect his choice about everything; and for that reason he refused my proposal that he should keep his position at the Hospital, because that would bind him to stay in Middlemarch, and he would not undertake to do anything which would be painful to you. He could say that to me, because he knows that I had much trial in my marriage, from my husband’s illness, which hindered his plans and saddened him; and he knows that I have felt how hard it is to walk always in fear of hurting another who is tied to us.”\n\nDorothea waited a little; she had discerned a faint pleasure stealing over Rosamond’s face. But there was no answer, and she went on, with a gathering tremor, “Marriage is so unlike everything else. There is something even awful in the nearness it brings. Even if we loved some one else better than—than those we were married to, it would be no use”—poor Dorothea, in her palpitating anxiety, could only seize her language brokenly—“I mean, marriage drinks up all our power of giving or getting any blessedness in that sort of love. I know it may be very dear—but it murders our marriage—and then the marriage stays with us like a murder—and everything else is gone. And then our husband—if he loved and trusted us, and we have not helped him, but made a curse in his life—”\n\nHer voice had sunk very low: there was a dread upon her of presuming too far, and of speaking as if she herself were perfection addressing error. She was too much preoccupied with her own anxiety, to be aware that Rosamond was trembling too; and filled with the need to express pitying fellowship rather than rebuke, she put her hands on Rosamond’s, and said with more agitated rapidity,—“I know, I know that the feeling may be very dear—it has taken hold of us unawares—it is so hard, it may seem like death to part with it—and we are weak—I am weak—”\n\nThe waves of her own sorrow, from out of which she was struggling to save another, rushed over Dorothea with conquering force. She stopped in speechless agitation, not crying, but feeling as if she were being inwardly grappled. Her face had become of a deathlier paleness, her lips trembled, and she pressed her hands helplessly on the hands that lay under them.\n\nRosamond, taken hold of by an emotion stronger than her own—hurried along in a new movement which gave all things some new, awful, undefined aspect—could find no words, but involuntarily she put her lips to Dorothea’s forehead which was very near her, and then for a minute the two women clasped each other as if they had been in a shipwreck.\n\n“You are thinking what is not true,” said Rosamond, in an eager half-whisper, while she was still feeling Dorothea’s arms round her—urged by a mysterious necessity to free herself from something that oppressed her as if it were blood guiltiness.\n\nThey moved apart, looking at each other.\n\n“When you came in yesterday—it was not as you thought,” said Rosamond in the same tone.\n\nThere was a movement of surprised attention in Dorothea. She expected a vindication of Rosamond herself.\n\n“He was telling me how he loved another woman, that I might know he could never love me,” said Rosamond, getting more and more hurried as she went on. “And now I think he hates me because—because you mistook him yesterday. He says it is through me that you will think ill of him—think that he is a false person. But it shall not be through me. He has never had any love for me—I know he has not—he has always thought slightly of me. He said yesterday that no other woman existed for him beside you. The blame of what happened is entirely mine. He said he could never explain to you—because of me. He said you could never think well of him again. But now I have told you, and he cannot reproach me any more.”\n\nRosamond had delivered her soul under impulses which she had not known before. She had begun her confession under the subduing influence of Dorothea’s emotion; and as she went on she had gathered the sense that she was repelling Will’s reproaches, which were still like a knife-wound within her.\n\nThe revulsion of feeling in Dorothea was too strong to be called joy. It was a tumult in which the terrible strain of the night and morning made a resistant pain:—she could only perceive that this would be joy when she had recovered her power of feeling it. Her immediate consciousness was one of immense sympathy without check; she cared for Rosamond without struggle now, and responded earnestly to her last words—\n\n“No, he cannot reproach you any more.”\n\nWith her usual tendency to over-estimate the good in others, she felt a great outgoing of her heart towards Rosamond, for the generous effort which had redeemed her from suffering, not counting that the effort was a reflex of her own energy. After they had been silent a little, she said—\n\n“You are not sorry that I came this morning?”\n\n“No, you have been very good to me,” said Rosamond. “I did not think that you would be so good. I was very unhappy. I am not happy now. Everything is so sad.”\n\n“But better days will come. Your husband will be rightly valued. And he depends on you for comfort. He loves you best. The worst loss would be to lose that—and you have not lost it,” said Dorothea.\n\nShe tried to thrust away the too overpowering thought of her own relief, lest she should fail to win some sign that Rosamond’s affection was yearning back towards her husband.\n\n“Tertius did not find fault with me, then?” said Rosamond, understanding now that Lydgate might have said anything to Mrs. Casaubon, and that she certainly was different from other women. Perhaps there was a faint taste of jealousy in the question. A smile began to play over Dorothea’s face as she said—\n\n“No, indeed! How could you imagine it?” But here the door opened, and Lydgate entered.\n\n“I am come back in my quality of doctor,” he said. “After I went away, I was haunted by two pale faces: Mrs. Casaubon looked as much in need of care as you, Rosy. And I thought that I had not done my duty in leaving you together; so when I had been to Coleman’s I came home again. I noticed that you were walking, Mrs. Casaubon, and the sky has changed—I think we may have rain. May I send some one to order your carriage to come for you?”\n\n“Oh, no! I am strong: I need the walk,” said Dorothea, rising with animation in her face. “Mrs. Lydgate and I have chatted a great deal, and it is time for me to go. I have always been accused of being immoderate and saying too much.”\n\nShe put out her hand to Rosamond, and they said an earnest, quiet good-by without kiss or other show of effusion: there had been between them too much serious emotion for them to use the signs of it superficially.\n\nAs Lydgate took her to the door she said nothing of Rosamond, but told him of Mr. Farebrother and the other friends who had listened with belief to his story.\n\nWhen he came back to Rosamond, she had already thrown herself on the sofa, in resigned fatigue.\n\n“Well, Rosy,” he said, standing over her, and touching her hair, “what do you think of Mrs. Casaubon now you have seen so much of her?”\n\n“I think she must be better than any one,” said Rosamond, “and she is very beautiful. If you go to talk to her so often, you will be more discontented with me than ever!”\n\nLydgate laughed at the “so often.” “But has she made you any less discontented with me?”\n\n“I think she has,” said Rosamond, looking up in his face. “How heavy your eyes are, Tertius—and do push your hair back.” He lifted up his large white hand to obey her, and felt thankful for this little mark of interest in him. Poor Rosamond’s vagrant fancy had come back terribly scourged—meek enough to nestle under the old despised shelter. And the shelter was still there: Lydgate had accepted his narrowed lot with sad resignation. He had chosen this fragile creature, and had taken the burthen of her life upon his arms. He must walk as he could, carrying that burthen pitifully.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXII.\n\n“My grief lies onward and my joy behind.” —SHAKESPEARE: Sonnets.\nExiles notoriously feed much on hopes, and are unlikely to stay in banishment unless they are obliged. When Will Ladislaw exiled himself from Middlemarch he had placed no stronger obstacle to his return than his own resolve, which was by no means an iron barrier, but simply a state of mind liable to melt into a minuet with other states of mind, and to find itself bowing, smiling, and giving place with polite facility. As the months went on, it had seemed more and more difficult to him to say why he should not run down to Middlemarch—merely for the sake of hearing something about Dorothea; and if on such a flying visit he should chance by some strange coincidence to meet with her, there was no reason for him to be ashamed of having taken an innocent journey which he had beforehand supposed that he should not take. Since he was hopelessly divided from her, he might surely venture into her neighborhood; and as to the suspicious friends who kept a dragon watch over her—their opinions seemed less and less important with time and change of air.\n\nAnd there had come a reason quite irrespective of Dorothea, which seemed to make a journey to Middlemarch a sort of philanthropic duty. Will had given a disinterested attention to an intended settlement on a new plan in the Far West, and the need for funds in order to carry out a good design had set him on debating with himself whether it would not be a laudable use to make of his claim on Bulstrode, to urge the application of that money which had been offered to himself as a means of carrying out a scheme likely to be largely beneficial. The question seemed a very dubious one to Will, and his repugnance to again entering into any relation with the banker might have made him dismiss it quickly, if there had not arisen in his imagination the probability that his judgment might be more safely determined by a visit to Middlemarch.\n\nThat was the object which Will stated to himself as a reason for coming down. He had meant to confide in Lydgate, and discuss the money question with him, and he had meant to amuse himself for the few evenings of his stay by having a great deal of music and badinage with fair Rosamond, without neglecting his friends at Lowick Parsonage:—if the Parsonage was close to the Manor, that was no fault of his. He had neglected the Farebrothers before his departure, from a proud resistance to the possible accusation of indirectly seeking interviews with Dorothea; but hunger tames us, and Will had become very hungry for the vision of a certain form and the sound of a certain voice. Nothing had done instead—not the opera, or the converse of zealous politicians, or the flattering reception (in dim corners) of his new hand in leading articles.\n\nThus he had come down, foreseeing with confidence how almost everything would be in his familiar little world; fearing, indeed, that there would be no surprises in his visit. But he had found that humdrum world in a terribly dynamic condition, in which even badinage and lyrism had turned explosive; and the first day of this visit had become the most fatal epoch of his life. The next morning he felt so harassed with the nightmare of consequences—he dreaded so much the immediate issues before him—that seeing while he breakfasted the arrival of the Riverston coach, he went out hurriedly and took his place on it, that he might be relieved, at least for a day, from the necessity of doing or saying anything in Middlemarch. Will Ladislaw was in one of those tangled crises which are commoner in experience than one might imagine, from the shallow absoluteness of men’s judgments. He had found Lydgate, for whom he had the sincerest respect, under circumstances which claimed his thorough and frankly declared sympathy; and the reason why, in spite of that claim, it would have been better for Will to have avoided all further intimacy, or even contact, with Lydgate, was precisely of the kind to make such a course appear impossible. To a creature of Will’s susceptible temperament—without any neutral region of indifference in his nature, ready to turn everything that befell him into the collisions of a passionate drama—the revelation that Rosamond had made her happiness in any way dependent on him was a difficulty which his outburst of rage towards her had immeasurably increased for him. He hated his own cruelty, and yet he dreaded to show the fulness of his relenting: he must go to her again; the friendship could not be put to a sudden end; and her unhappiness was a power which he dreaded. And all the while there was no more foretaste of enjoyment in the life before him than if his limbs had been lopped off and he was making his fresh start on crutches. In the night he had debated whether he should not get on the coach, not for Riverston, but for London, leaving a note to Lydgate which would give a makeshift reason for his retreat. But there were strong cords pulling him back from that abrupt departure: the blight on his happiness in thinking of Dorothea, the crushing of that chief hope which had remained in spite of the acknowledged necessity for renunciation, was too fresh a misery for him to resign himself to it and go straightway into a distance which was also despair.\n\nThus he did nothing more decided than taking the Riverston coach. He came back again by it while it was still daylight, having made up his mind that he must go to Lydgate’s that evening. The Rubicon, we know, was a very insignificant stream to look at; its significance lay entirely in certain invisible conditions. Will felt as if he were forced to cross his small boundary ditch, and what he saw beyond it was not empire, but discontented subjection.\n\nBut it is given to us sometimes even in our every-day life to witness the saving influence of a noble nature, the divine efficacy of rescue that may lie in a self-subduing act of fellowship. If Dorothea, after her night’s anguish, had not taken that walk to Rosamond—why, she perhaps would have been a woman who gained a higher character for discretion, but it would certainly not have been as well for those three who were on one hearth in Lydgate’s house at half-past seven that evening.\n\nRosamond had been prepared for Will’s visit, and she received him with a languid coldness which Lydgate accounted for by her nervous exhaustion, of which he could not suppose that it had any relation to Will. And when she sat in silence bending over a bit of work, he innocently apologized for her in an indirect way by begging her to lean backward and rest. Will was miserable in the necessity for playing the part of a friend who was making his first appearance and greeting to Rosamond, while his thoughts were busy about her feeling since that scene of yesterday, which seemed still inexorably to enclose them both, like the painful vision of a double madness. It happened that nothing called Lydgate out of the room; but when Rosamond poured out the tea, and Will came near to fetch it, she placed a tiny bit of folded paper in his saucer. He saw it and secured it quickly, but as he went back to his inn he had no eagerness to unfold the paper. What Rosamond had written to him would probably deepen the painful impressions of the evening. Still, he opened and read it by his bed-candle. There were only these few words in her neatly flowing hand:—\n\n“I have told Mrs. Casaubon. She is not under any mistake about you. I told her because she came to see me and was very kind. You will have nothing to reproach me with now. I shall not have made any difference to you.”\n\nThe effect of these words was not quite all gladness. As Will dwelt on them with excited imagination, he felt his cheeks and ears burning at the thought of what had occurred between Dorothea and Rosamond—at the uncertainty how far Dorothea might still feel her dignity wounded in having an explanation of his conduct offered to her. There might still remain in her mind a changed association with him which made an irremediable difference—a lasting flaw. With active fancy he wrought himself into a state of doubt little more easy than that of the man who has escaped from wreck by night and stands on unknown ground in the darkness. Until that wretched yesterday—except the moment of vexation long ago in the very same room and in the very same presence—all their vision, all their thought of each other, had been as in a world apart, where the sunshine fell on tall white lilies, where no evil lurked, and no other soul entered. But now—would Dorothea meet him in that world again?\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXIII.\n\n“And now good-morrow to our waking souls Which watch not one another out of fear; For love all love of other sights controls, And makes one little room, an everywhere.” —DR. DONNE.\n\nOn the second morning after Dorothea’s visit to Rosamond, she had had two nights of sound sleep, and had not only lost all traces of fatigue, but felt as if she had a great deal of superfluous strength—that is to say, more strength than she could manage to concentrate on any occupation. The day before, she had taken long walks outside the grounds, and had paid two visits to the Parsonage; but she never in her life told any one the reason why she spent her time in that fruitless manner, and this morning she was rather angry with herself for her childish restlessness. To-day was to be spent quite differently. What was there to be done in the village? Oh dear! nothing. Everybody was well and had flannel; nobody’s pig had died; and it was Saturday morning, when there was a general scrubbing of doors and door-stones, and when it was useless to go into the school. But there were various subjects that Dorothea was trying to get clear upon, and she resolved to throw herself energetically into the gravest of all. She sat down in the library before her particular little heap of books on political economy and kindred matters, out of which she was trying to get light as to the best way of spending money so as not to injure one’s neighbors, or—what comes to the same thing—so as to do them the most good. Here was a weighty subject which, if she could but lay hold of it, would certainly keep her mind steady. Unhappily her mind slipped off it for a whole hour; and at the end she found herself reading sentences twice over with an intense consciousness of many things, but not of any one thing contained in the text. This was hopeless. Should she order the carriage and drive to Tipton? No; for some reason or other she preferred staying at Lowick. But her vagrant mind must be reduced to order: there was an art in self-discipline; and she walked round and round the brown library considering by what sort of manoeuvre she could arrest her wandering thoughts. Perhaps a mere task was the best means—something to which she must go doggedly. Was there not the geography of Asia Minor, in which her slackness had often been rebuked by Mr. Casaubon? She went to the cabinet of maps and unrolled one: this morning she might make herself finally sure that Paphlagonia was not on the Levantine coast, and fix her total darkness about the Chalybes firmly on the shores of the Euxine. A map was a fine thing to study when you were disposed to think of something else, being made up of names that would turn into a chime if you went back upon them. Dorothea set earnestly to work, bending close to her map, and uttering the names in an audible, subdued tone, which often got into a chime. She looked amusingly girlish after all her deep experience—nodding her head and marking the names off on her fingers, with a little pursing of her lip, and now and then breaking off to put her hands on each side of her face and say, “Oh dear! oh dear!”\n\nThere was no reason why this should end any more than a merry-go-round; but it was at last interrupted by the opening of the door and the announcement of Miss Noble.\n\nThe little old lady, whose bonnet hardly reached Dorothea’s shoulder, was warmly welcomed, but while her hand was being pressed she made many of her beaver-like noises, as if she had something difficult to say.\n\n“Do sit down,” said Dorothea, rolling a chair forward. “Am I wanted for anything? I shall be so glad if I can do anything.”\n“I will not stay,” said Miss Noble, putting her hand into her small basket, and holding some article inside it nervously; “I have left a friend in the churchyard.” She lapsed into her inarticulate sounds, and unconsciously drew forth the article which she was fingering. It was the tortoise-shell lozenge-box, and Dorothea felt the color mounting to her cheeks.\n\n“Mr. Ladislaw,” continued the timid little woman. “He fears he has offended you, and has begged me to ask if you will see him for a few minutes.”\n\nDorothea did not answer on the instant: it was crossing her mind that she could not receive him in this library, where her husband’s prohibition seemed to dwell. She looked towards the window. Could she go out and meet him in the grounds? The sky was heavy, and the trees had begun to shiver as at a coming storm. Besides, she shrank from going out to him.\n\n“Do see him, Mrs. Casaubon,” said Miss Noble, pathetically; “else I must go back and say No, and that will hurt him.”\n“Yes, I will see him,” said Dorothea. “Pray tell him to come.”\n\nWhat else was there to be done? There was nothing that she longed for at that moment except to see Will: the possibility of seeing him had thrust itself insistently between her and every other object; and yet she had a throbbing excitement like an alarm upon her—a sense that she was doing something daringly defiant for his sake.\n\nWhen the little lady had trotted away on her mission, Dorothea stood in the middle of the library with her hands falling clasped before her, making no attempt to compose herself in an attitude of dignified unconsciousness. What she was least conscious of just then was her own body: she was thinking of what was likely to be in Will’s mind, and of the hard feelings that others had had about him. How could any duty bind her to hardness? Resistance to unjust dispraise had mingled with her feeling for him from the very first, and now in the rebound of her heart after her anguish the resistance was stronger than ever. “If I love him too much it is because he has been used so ill:”—there was a voice within her saying this to some imagined audience in the library, when the door was opened, and she saw Will before her.\n\nShe did not move, and he came towards her with more doubt and timidity in his face than she had ever seen before. He was in a state of uncertainty which made him afraid lest some look or word of his should condemn him to a new distance from her; and Dorothea was afraid of her own emotion. She looked as if there were a spell upon her, keeping her motionless and hindering her from unclasping her hands, while some intense, grave yearning was imprisoned within her eyes. Seeing that she did not put out her hand as usual, Will paused a yard from her and said with embarrassment, “I am so grateful to you for seeing me.”\n\n“I wanted to see you,” said Dorothea, having no other words at command. It did not occur to her to sit down, and Will did not give a cheerful interpretation to this queenly way of receiving him; but he went on to say what he had made up his mind to say.\n\n“I fear you think me foolish and perhaps wrong for coming back so soon. I have been punished for my impatience. You know—every one knows now—a painful story about my parentage. I knew of it before I went away, and I always meant to tell you of it if—if we ever met again.”\n\nThere was a slight movement in Dorothea, and she unclasped her hands, but immediately folded them over each other.\n\n“But the affair is matter of gossip now,” Will continued. “I wished you to know that something connected with it—something which happened before I went away, helped to bring me down here again. At least I thought it excused my coming. It was the idea of getting Bulstrode to apply some money to a public purpose—some money which he had thought of giving me. Perhaps it is rather to Bulstrode’s credit that he privately offered me compensation for an old injury: he offered to give me a good income to make amends; but I suppose you know the disagreeable story?”\n\nWill looked doubtfully at Dorothea, but his manner was gathering some of the defiant courage with which he always thought of this fact in his destiny. He added, “You know that it must be altogether painful to me.”\n\n“Yes—yes—I know,” said Dorothea, hastily.\n\n“I did not choose to accept an income from such a source. I was sure that you would not think well of me if I did so,” said Will. Why should he mind saying anything of that sort to her now? She knew that he had avowed his love for her. “I felt that”—he broke off, nevertheless.\n\n“You acted as I should have expected you to act,” said Dorothea, her face brightening and her head becoming a little more erect on its beautiful stem.\n\n“I did not believe that you would let any circumstance of my birth create a prejudice in you against me, though it was sure to do so in others,” said Will, shaking his head backward in his old way, and looking with a grave appeal into her eyes.\n\n“If it were a new hardship it would be a new reason for me to cling to you,” said Dorothea, fervidly. “Nothing could have changed me but—” her heart was swelling, and it was difficult to go on; she made a great effort over herself to say in a low tremulous voice, “but thinking that you were different—not so good as I had believed you to be.”\n\n“You are sure to believe me better than I am in everything but one,” said Will, giving way to his own feeling in the evidence of hers. “I mean, in my truth to you. When I thought you doubted of that, I didn’t care about anything that was left. I thought it was all over with me, and there was nothing to try for—only things to endure.”\n\n“I don’t doubt you any longer,” said Dorothea, putting out her hand; a vague fear for him impelling her unutterable affection.\n\nHe took her hand and raised it to his lips with something like a sob. But he stood with his hat and gloves in the other hand, and might have done for the portrait of a Royalist. Still it was difficult to loose the hand, and Dorothea, withdrawing it in a confusion that distressed her, looked and moved away.\n\n“See how dark the clouds have become, and how the trees are tossed,” she said, walking towards the window, yet speaking and moving with only a dim sense of what she was doing.\n\nWill followed her at a little distance, and leaned against the tall back of a leather chair, on which he ventured now to lay his hat and gloves, and free himself from the intolerable durance of formality to which he had been for the first time condemned in Dorothea’s presence. It must be confessed that he felt very happy at that moment leaning on the chair. He was not much afraid of anything that she might feel now.\n\nThey stood silent, not looking at each other, but looking at the evergreens which were being tossed, and were showing the pale underside of their leaves against the blackening sky. Will never enjoyed the prospect of a storm so much: it delivered him from the necessity of going away. Leaves and little branches were hurled about, and the thunder was getting nearer. The light was more and more sombre, but there came a flash of lightning which made them start and look at each other, and then smile. Dorothea began to say what she had been thinking of.\n\n“That was a wrong thing for you to say, that you would have had nothing to try for. If we had lost our own chief good, other people’s good would remain, and that is worth trying for. Some can be happy. I seemed to see that more clearly than ever, when I was the most wretched. I can hardly think how I could have borne the trouble, if that feeling had not come to me to make strength.”\n\n“You have never felt the sort of misery I felt,” said Will; “the misery of knowing that you must despise me.”\n\n“But I have felt worse—it was worse to think ill—” Dorothea had begun impetuously, but broke off.\n\nWill colored. He had the sense that whatever she said was uttered in the vision of a fatality that kept them apart. He was silent a moment, and then said passionately—\n\n“We may at least have the comfort of speaking to each other without disguise. Since I must go away—since we must always be divided—you may think of me as one on the brink of the grave.”\n\nWhile he was speaking there came a vivid flash of lightning which lit each of them up for the other—and the light seemed to be the terror of a hopeless love. Dorothea darted instantaneously from the window; Will followed her, seizing her hand with a spasmodic movement; and so they stood, with their hands clasped, like two children, looking out on the storm, while the thunder gave a tremendous crack and roll above them, and the rain began to pour down. Then they turned their faces towards each other, with the memory of his last words in them, and they did not loose each other’s hands.\n\n“There is no hope for me,” said Will. “Even if you loved me as well as I love you—even if I were everything to you—I shall most likely always be very poor: on a sober calculation, one can count on nothing but a creeping lot. It is impossible for us ever to belong to each other. It is perhaps base of me to have asked for a word from you. I meant to go away into silence, but I have not been able to do what I meant.”\n\n“Don’t be sorry,” said Dorothea, in her clear tender tones. “I would rather share all the trouble of our parting.”\n\nHer lips trembled, and so did his. It was never known which lips were the first to move towards the other lips; but they kissed tremblingly, and then they moved apart.\n\nThe rain was dashing against the window-panes as if an angry spirit were within it, and behind it was the great swoop of the wind; it was one of those moments in which both the busy and the idle pause with a certain awe.\n\nDorothea sat down on the seat nearest to her, a long low ottoman in the middle of the room, and with her hands folded over each other on her lap, looked at the drear outer world. Will stood still an instant looking at her, then seated himself beside her, and laid his hand on hers, which turned itself upward to be clasped. They sat in that way without looking at each other, until the rain abated and began to fall in stillness. Each had been full of thoughts which neither of them could begin to utter.\n\nBut when the rain was quiet, Dorothea turned to look at Will. With passionate exclamation, as if some torture screw were threatening him, he started up and said, “It is impossible!”\n\nHe went and leaned on the back of the chair again, and seemed to be battling with his own anger, while she looked towards him sadly.\n\n“It is as fatal as a murder or any other horror that divides people,” he burst out again; “it is more intolerable—to have our life maimed by petty accidents.”\n\n“No—don’t say that—your life need not be maimed,” said Dorothea, gently.\n\n“Yes, it must,” said Will, angrily. “It is cruel of you to speak in that way—as if there were any comfort. You may see beyond the misery of it, but I don’t. It is unkind—it is throwing back my love for you as if it were a trifle, to speak in that way in the face of the fact. We can never be married.”\n\n“Some time—we might,” said Dorothea, in a trembling voice.\n\n“When?” said Will, bitterly. “What is the use of counting on any success of mine? It is a mere toss up whether I shall ever do more than keep myself decently, unless I choose to sell myself as a mere pen and a mouthpiece. I can see that clearly enough. I could not offer myself to any woman, even if she had no luxuries to renounce.”\n\nThere was silence. Dorothea’s heart was full of something that she wanted to say, and yet the words were too difficult. She was wholly possessed by them: at that moment debate was mute within her. And it was very hard that she could not say what she wanted to say. Will was looking out of the window angrily. If he would have looked at her and not gone away from her side, she thought everything would have been easier. At last he turned, still resting against the chair, and stretching his hand automatically towards his hat, said with a sort of exasperation, “Good-by.”\n\n“Oh, I cannot bear it—my heart will break,” said Dorothea, starting from her seat, the flood of her young passion bearing down all the obstructions which had kept her silent—the great tears rising and falling in an instant: “I don’t mind about poverty—I hate my wealth.”\n\nIn an instant Will was close to her and had his arms round her, but she drew her head back and held his away gently that she might go on speaking, her large tear-filled eyes looking at his very simply, while she said in a sobbing childlike way, “We could live quite well on my own fortune—it is too much—seven hundred a-year—I want so little—no new clothes—and I will learn what everything costs.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXIV.\n\n“Though it be songe of old and yonge, That I sholde be to blame, Theyrs be the charge, that spoke so large In hurtynge of my name.” —The Not-Browne Mayde.\nIt was just after the Lords had thrown out the Reform Bill: that explains how Mr. Cadwallader came to be walking on the slope of the lawn near the great conservatory at Freshitt Hall, holding the “Times” in his hands behind him, while he talked with a trout-fisher’s dispassionateness about the prospects of the country to Sir James Chettam. Mrs. Cadwallader, the Dowager Lady Chettam, and Celia were sometimes seated on garden-chairs, sometimes walking to meet little Arthur, who was being drawn in his chariot, and, as became the infantine Bouddha, was sheltered by his sacred umbrella with handsome silken fringe.\n\nThe ladies also talked politics, though more fitfully. Mrs. Cadwallader was strong on the intended creation of peers: she had it for certain from her cousin that Truberry had gone over to the other side entirely at the instigation of his wife, who had scented peerages in the air from the very first introduction of the Reform question, and would sign her soul away to take precedence of her younger sister, who had married a baronet. Lady Chettam thought that such conduct was very reprehensible, and remembered that Mrs. Truberry’s mother was a Miss Walsingham of Melspring. Celia confessed it was nicer to be “Lady” than “Mrs.,” and that Dodo never minded about precedence if she could have her own way. Mrs. Cadwallader held that it was a poor satisfaction to take precedence when everybody about you knew that you had not a drop of good blood in your veins; and Celia again, stopping to look at Arthur, said, “It would be very nice, though, if he were a Viscount—and his lordship’s little tooth coming through! He might have been, if James had been an Earl.”\n\n“My dear Celia,” said the Dowager, “James’s title is worth far more than any new earldom. I never wished his father to be anything else than Sir James.”\n\n“Oh, I only meant about Arthur’s little tooth,” said Celia, comfortably. “But see, here is my uncle coming.”\n\nShe tripped off to meet her uncle, while Sir James and Mr. Cadwallader came forward to make one group with the ladies. Celia had slipped her arm through her uncle’s, and he patted her hand with a rather melancholy “Well, my dear!” As they approached, it was evident that Mr. Brooke was looking dejected, but this was fully accounted for by the state of politics; and as he was shaking hands all round without more greeting than a “Well, you’re all here, you know,” the Rector said, laughingly—\n\n“Don’t take the throwing out of the Bill so much to heart, Brooke; you’ve got all the riff-raff of the country on your side.”\n\n“The Bill, eh? ah!” said Mr. Brooke, with a mild distractedness of manner. “Thrown out, you know, eh? The Lords are going too far, though. They’ll have to pull up. Sad news, you know. I mean, here at home—sad news. But you must not blame me, Chettam.”\n\n“What is the matter?” said Sir James. “Not another gamekeeper shot, I hope? It’s what I should expect, when a fellow like Trapping Bass is let off so easily.”\n\n“Gamekeeper? No. Let us go in; I can tell you all in the house, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, nodding at the Cadwalladers, to show that he included them in his confidence. “As to poachers like Trapping Bass, you know, Chettam,” he continued, as they were entering, “when you are a magistrate, you’ll not find it so easy to commit. Severity is all very well, but it’s a great deal easier when you’ve got somebody to do it for you. You have a soft place in your heart yourself, you know—you’re not a Draco, a Jeffreys, that sort of thing.”\n\nMr. Brooke was evidently in a state of nervous perturbation. When he had something painful to tell, it was usually his way to introduce it among a number of disjointed particulars, as if it were a medicine that would get a milder flavor by mixing. He continued his chat with Sir James about the poachers until they were all seated, and Mrs. Cadwallader, impatient of this drivelling, said—\n\n“I’m dying to know the sad news. The gamekeeper is not shot: that is settled. What is it, then?”\n\n“Well, it’s a very trying thing, you know,” said Mr. Brooke. “I’m glad you and the Rector are here; it’s a family matter—but you will help us all to bear it, Cadwallader. I’ve got to break it to you, my dear.” Here Mr. Brooke looked at Celia—“You’ve no notion what it is, you know. And, Chettam, it will annoy you uncommonly—but, you see, you have not been able to hinder it, any more than I have. There’s something singular in things: they come round, you know.”\n\n“It must be about Dodo,” said Celia, who had been used to think of her sister as the dangerous part of the family machinery. She had seated herself on a low stool against her husband’s knee.\n\n“For God’s sake let us hear what it is!” said Sir James.\n\n“Well, you know, Chettam, I couldn’t help Casaubon’s will: it was a sort of will to make things worse.”\n\n“Exactly,” said Sir James, hastily. “But what is worse?”\n\n“Dorothea is going to be married again, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, nodding towards Celia, who immediately looked up at her husband with a frightened glance, and put her hand on his knee. Sir James was almost white with anger, but he did not speak.\n\n“Merciful heaven!” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “Not to young Ladislaw?”\n\nMr. Brooke nodded, saying, “Yes; to Ladislaw,” and then fell into a prudential silence.\n\n“You see, Humphrey!” said Mrs. Cadwallader, waving her arm towards her husband. “Another time you will admit that I have some foresight; or rather you will contradict me and be just as blind as ever. You supposed that the young gentleman was gone out of the country.”\n\n“So he might be, and yet come back,” said the Rector, quietly.\n\n“When did you learn this?” said Sir James, not liking to hear any one else speak, though finding it difficult to speak himself.\n\n“Yesterday,” said Mr. Brooke, meekly. “I went to Lowick. Dorothea sent for me, you know. It had come about quite suddenly—neither of them had any idea two days ago—not any idea, you know. There’s something singular in things. But Dorothea is quite determined—it is no use opposing. I put it strongly to her. I did my duty, Chettam. But she can act as she likes, you know.”\n\n“It would have been better if I had called him out and shot him a year ago,” said Sir James, not from bloody-mindedness, but because he needed something strong to say.\n\n“Really, James, that would have been very disagreeable,” said Celia.\n\n“Be reasonable, Chettam. Look at the affair more quietly,” said Mr. Cadwallader, sorry to see his good-natured friend so overmastered by anger.\n\n“That is not so very easy for a man of any dignity—with any sense of right—when the affair happens to be in his own family,” said Sir James, still in his white indignation. “It is perfectly scandalous. If Ladislaw had had a spark of honor he would have gone out of the country at once, and never shown his face in it again. However, I am not surprised. The day after Casaubon’s funeral I said what ought to be done. But I was not listened to.”\n\n“You wanted what was impossible, you know, Chettam,” said Mr. Brooke. “You wanted him shipped off. I told you Ladislaw was not to be done as we liked with: he had his ideas. He was a remarkable fellow—I always said he was a remarkable fellow.”\n\n“Yes,” said Sir James, unable to repress a retort, “it is rather a pity you formed that high opinion of him. We are indebted to that for his being lodged in this neighborhood. We are indebted to that for seeing a woman like Dorothea degrading herself by marrying him.” Sir James made little stoppages between his clauses, the words not coming easily. “A man so marked out by her husband’s will, that delicacy ought to have forbidden her from seeing him again—who takes her out of her proper rank—into poverty—has the meanness to accept such a sacrifice—has always had an objectionable position—a bad origin—and, I believe, is a man of little principle and light character. That is my opinion.” Sir James ended emphatically, turning aside and crossing his leg.\n\n“I pointed everything out to her,” said Mr. Brooke, apologetically—“I mean the poverty, and abandoning her position. I said, ‘My dear, you don’t know what it is to live on seven hundred a-year, and have no carriage, and that kind of thing, and go amongst people who don’t know who you are.’ I put it strongly to her. But I advise you to talk to Dorothea herself. The fact is, she has a dislike to Casaubon’s property. You will hear what she says, you know.”\n\n“No—excuse me—I shall not,” said Sir James, with more coolness. “I cannot bear to see her again; it is too painful. It hurts me too much that a woman like Dorothea should have done what is wrong.”\n\n“Be just, Chettam,” said the easy, large-lipped Rector, who objected to all this unnecessary discomfort. “Mrs. Casaubon may be acting imprudently: she is giving up a fortune for the sake of a man, and we men have so poor an opinion of each other that we can hardly call a woman wise who does that. But I think you should not condemn it as a wrong action, in the strict sense of the word.”\n\n“Yes, I do,” answered Sir James. “I think that Dorothea commits a wrong action in marrying Ladislaw.”\n\n“My dear fellow, we are rather apt to consider an act wrong because it is unpleasant to us,” said the Rector, quietly. Like many men who take life easily, he had the knack of saying a home truth occasionally to those who felt themselves virtuously out of temper. Sir James took out his handkerchief and began to bite the corner.\n\n“It is very dreadful of Dodo, though,” said Celia, wishing to justify her husband. “She said she never would marry again—not anybody at all.”\n\n“I heard her say the same thing myself,” said Lady Chettam, majestically, as if this were royal evidence.\n\n“Oh, there is usually a silent exception in such cases,” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “The only wonder to me is, that any of you are surprised. You did nothing to hinder it. If you would have had Lord Triton down here to woo her with his philanthropy, he might have carried her off before the year was over. There was no safety in anything else. Mr. Casaubon had prepared all this as beautifully as possible. He made himself disagreeable—or it pleased God to make him so—and then he dared her to contradict him. It’s the way to make any trumpery tempting, to ticket it at a high price in that way.”\n\n“I don’t know what you mean by wrong, Cadwallader,” said Sir James, still feeling a little stung, and turning round in his chair towards the Rector. “He’s not a man we can take into the family. At least, I must speak for myself,” he continued, carefully keeping his eyes off Mr. Brooke. “I suppose others will find his society too pleasant to care about the propriety of the thing.”\n\n“Well, you know, Chettam,” said Mr. Brooke, good-humoredly, nursing his leg, “I can’t turn my back on Dorothea. I must be a father to her up to a certain point. I said, ‘My dear, I won’t refuse to give you away.’ I had spoken strongly before. But I can cut off the entail, you know. It will cost money and be troublesome; but I can do it, you know.”\n\nMr. Brooke nodded at Sir James, and felt that he was both showing his own force of resolution and propitiating what was just in the Baronet’s vexation. He had hit on a more ingenious mode of parrying than he was aware of. He had touched a motive of which Sir James was ashamed. The mass of his feeling about Dorothea’s marriage to Ladislaw was due partly to excusable prejudice, or even justifiable opinion, partly to a jealous repugnance hardly less in Ladislaw’s case than in Casaubon’s. He was convinced that the marriage was a fatal one for Dorothea. But amid that mass ran a vein of which he was too good and honorable a man to like the avowal even to himself: it was undeniable that the union of the two estates—Tipton and Freshitt—lying charmingly within a ring-fence, was a prospect that flattered him for his son and heir. Hence when Mr. Brooke noddingly appealed to that motive, Sir James felt a sudden embarrassment; there was a stoppage in his throat; he even blushed. He had found more words than usual in the first jet of his anger, but Mr. Brooke’s propitiation was more clogging to his tongue than Mr. Cadwallader’s caustic hint.\n\nBut Celia was glad to have room for speech after her uncle’s suggestion of the marriage ceremony, and she said, though with as little eagerness of manner as if the question had turned on an invitation to dinner, “Do you mean that Dodo is going to be married directly, uncle?”\n\n“In three weeks, you know,” said Mr. Brooke, helplessly. “I can do nothing to hinder it, Cadwallader,” he added, turning for a little countenance toward the Rector, who said—\n\n“I should not make any fuss about it. If she likes to be poor, that is her affair. Nobody would have said anything if she had married the young fellow because he was rich. Plenty of beneficed clergy are poorer than they will be. Here is Elinor,” continued the provoking husband; “she vexed her friends by me: I had hardly a thousand a-year—I was a lout—nobody could see anything in me—my shoes were not the right cut—all the men wondered how a woman could like me. Upon my word, I must take Ladislaw’s part until I hear more harm of him.”\n\n“Humphrey, that is all sophistry, and you know it,” said his wife. “Everything is all one—that is the beginning and end with you. As if you had not been a Cadwallader! Does any one suppose that I would have taken such a monster as you by any other name?”\n\n“And a clergyman too,” observed Lady Chettam with approbation. “Elinor cannot be said to have descended below her rank. It is difficult to say what Mr. Ladislaw is, eh, James?”\n\nSir James gave a small grunt, which was less respectful than his usual mode of answering his mother. Celia looked up at him like a thoughtful kitten.\n\n“It must be admitted that his blood is a frightful mixture!” said Mrs. Cadwallader. “The Casaubon cuttle-fish fluid to begin with, and then a rebellious Polish fiddler or dancing-master, was it?—and then an old clo—”\n\n“Nonsense, Elinor,” said the Rector, rising. “It is time for us to go.”\n\n“After all, he is a pretty sprig,” said Mrs. Cadwallader, rising too, and wishing to make amends. “He is like the fine old Crichley portraits before the idiots came in.”\n\n“I’ll go with you,” said Mr. Brooke, starting up with alacrity. “You must all come and dine with me to-morrow, you know—eh, Celia, my dear?”\n\n“You will, James—won’t you?” said Celia, taking her husband’s hand.\n\n“Oh, of course, if you like,” said Sir James, pulling down his waistcoat, but unable yet to adjust his face good-humoredly. “That is to say, if it is not to meet anybody else.”\n\n“No, no, no,” said Mr. Brooke, understanding the condition. “Dorothea would not come, you know, unless you had been to see her.”\n\nWhen Sir James and Celia were alone, she said, “Do you mind about my having the carriage to go to Lowick, James?”\n\n“What, now, directly?” he answered, with some surprise.\n\n“Yes, it is very important,” said Celia.\n\n“Remember, Celia, I cannot see her,” said Sir James.\n\n“Not if she gave up marrying?”\n\n“What is the use of saying that?—however, I’m going to the stables. I’ll tell Briggs to bring the carriage round.”\n\nCelia thought it was of great use, if not to say that, at least to take a journey to Lowick in order to influence Dorothea’s mind. All through their girlhood she had felt that she could act on her sister by a word judiciously placed—by opening a little window for the daylight of her own understanding to enter among the strange colored lamps by which Dodo habitually saw. And Celia the matron naturally felt more able to advise her childless sister. How could any one understand Dodo so well as Celia did or love her so tenderly?\n\nDorothea, busy in her boudoir, felt a glow of pleasure at the sight of her sister so soon after the revelation of her intended marriage. She had prefigured to herself, even with exaggeration, the disgust of her friends, and she had even feared that Celia might be kept aloof from her.\n\n“O Kitty, I am delighted to see you!” said Dorothea, putting her hands on Celia’s shoulders, and beaming on her. “I almost thought you would not come to me.”\n\n“I have not brought Arthur, because I was in a hurry,” said Celia, and they sat down on two small chairs opposite each other, with their knees touching.\n\n“You know, Dodo, it is very bad,” said Celia, in her placid guttural, looking as prettily free from humors as possible. “You have disappointed us all so. And I can’t think that it ever will be—you never can go and live in that way. And then there are all your plans! You never can have thought of that. James would have taken any trouble for you, and you might have gone on all your life doing what you liked.”\n\n“On the contrary, dear,” said Dorothea, “I never could do anything that I liked. I have never carried out any plan yet.”\n\n“Because you always wanted things that wouldn’t do. But other plans would have come. And how can you marry Mr. Ladislaw, that we none of us ever thought you could marry? It shocks James so dreadfully. And then it is all so different from what you have always been. You would have Mr. Casaubon because he had such a great soul, and was so old and dismal and learned; and now, to think of marrying Mr. Ladislaw, who has got no estate or anything. I suppose it is because you must be making yourself uncomfortable in some way or other.”\n\nDorothea laughed.\n\n“Well, it is very serious, Dodo,” said Celia, becoming more impressive. “How will you live? and you will go away among queer people. And I shall never see you—and you won’t mind about little Arthur—and I thought you always would—”\n\nCelia’s rare tears had got into her eyes, and the corners of her mouth were agitated.\n\n“Dear Celia,” said Dorothea, with tender gravity, “if you don’t ever see me, it will not be my fault.”\n\n“Yes, it will,” said Celia, with the same touching distortion of her small features. “How can I come to you or have you with me when James can’t bear it?—that is because he thinks it is not right—he thinks you are so wrong, Dodo. But you always were wrong: only I can’t help loving you. And nobody can think where you will live: where can you go?”\n\n“I am going to London,” said Dorothea.\n\n“How can you always live in a street? And you will be so poor. I could give you half my things, only how can I, when I never see you?”\n\n“Bless you, Kitty,” said Dorothea, with gentle warmth. “Take comfort: perhaps James will forgive me some time.”\n\n“But it would be much better if you would not be married,” said Celia, drying her eyes, and returning to her argument; “then there would be nothing uncomfortable. And you would not do what nobody thought you could do. James always said you ought to be a queen; but this is not at all being like a queen. You know what mistakes you have always been making, Dodo, and this is another. Nobody thinks Mr. Ladislaw a proper husband for you. And you said you would never be married again.”\n\n“It is quite true that I might be a wiser person, Celia,” said Dorothea, “and that I might have done something better, if I had been better. But this is what I am going to do. I have promised to marry Mr. Ladislaw; and I am going to marry him.”\n\nThe tone in which Dorothea said this was a note that Celia had long learned to recognize. She was silent a few moments, and then said, as if she had dismissed all contest, “Is he very fond of you, Dodo?”\n\n“I hope so. I am very fond of him.”\n\n“That is nice,” said Celia, comfortably. “Only I would rather you had such a sort of husband as James is, with a place very near, that I could drive to.”\n\nDorothea smiled, and Celia looked rather meditative. Presently she said, “I cannot think how it all came about.” Celia thought it would be pleasant to hear the story.\n\n“I dare say not,” said Dorothea, pinching her sister’s chin. “If you knew how it came about, it would not seem wonderful to you.”\n\n“Can’t you tell me?” said Celia, settling her arms cozily.\n\n“No, dear, you would have to feel with me, else you would never know.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXV.\n\n“Then went the jury out whose names were Mr. Blindman, Mr. No-good, Mr. Malice, Mr. Love-lust, Mr. Live-loose, Mr. Heady, Mr. High-mind, Mr. Enmity, Mr. Liar, Mr. Cruelty, Mr. Hate-light, Mr. Implacable, who every one gave in his private verdict against him among themselves, and afterwards unanimously concluded to bring him in guilty before the judge. And first among themselves, Mr. Blindman, the foreman, said, I see clearly that this man is a heretic. Then said Mr. No-good, Away with such a fellow from the earth! Ay, said Mr. Malice, for I hate the very look of him. Then said Mr. Love-lust, I could never endure him. Nor I, said Mr. Live-loose; for he would be always condemning my way. Hang him, hang him, said Mr. Heady. A sorry scrub, said Mr. High-mind. My heart riseth against him, said Mr. Enmity. He is a rogue, said Mr. Liar. Hanging is too good for him, said Mr. Cruelty. Let us despatch him out of the way said Mr. Hate-light. Then said Mr. Implacable, Might I have all the world given me, I could not be reconciled to him; therefore let us forthwith bring him in guilty of death.”—Pilgrim’s Progress.\n\nWhen immortal Bunyan makes his picture of the persecuting passions bringing in their verdict of guilty, who pities Faithful? That is a rare and blessed lot which some greatest men have not attained, to know ourselves guiltless before a condemning crowd—to be sure that what we are denounced for is solely the good in us. The pitiable lot is that of the man who could not call himself a martyr even though he were to persuade himself that the men who stoned him were but ugly passions incarnate—who knows that he is stoned, not for professing the Right, but for not being the man he professed to be.\n\nThis was the consciousness that Bulstrode was withering under while he made his preparations for departing from Middlemarch, and going to end his stricken life in that sad refuge, the indifference of new faces. The duteous merciful constancy of his wife had delivered him from one dread, but it could not hinder her presence from being still a tribunal before which he shrank from confession and desired advocacy. His equivocations with himself about the death of Raffles had sustained the conception of an Omniscience whom he prayed to, yet he had a terror upon him which would not let him expose them to judgment by a full confession to his wife: the acts which he had washed and diluted with inward argument and motive, and for which it seemed comparatively easy to win invisible pardon—what name would she call them by? That she should ever silently call his acts Murder was what he could not bear. He felt shrouded by her doubt: he got strength to face her from the sense that she could not yet feel warranted in pronouncing that worst condemnation on him. Some time, perhaps—when he was dying—he would tell her all: in the deep shadow of that time, when she held his hand in the gathering darkness, she might listen without recoiling from his touch. Perhaps: but concealment had been the habit of his life, and the impulse to confession had no power against the dread of a deeper humiliation.\n\nHe was full of timid care for his wife, not only because he deprecated any harshness of judgment from her, but because he felt a deep distress at the sight of her suffering. She had sent her daughters away to board at a school on the coast, that this crisis might be hidden from them as far as possible. Set free by their absence from the intolerable necessity of accounting for her grief or of beholding their frightened wonder, she could live unconstrainedly with the sorrow that was every day streaking her hair with whiteness and making her eyelids languid.\n\n“Tell me anything that you would like to have me do, Harriet,” Bulstrode had said to her; “I mean with regard to arrangements of property. It is my intention not to sell the land I possess in this neighborhood, but to leave it to you as a safe provision. If you have any wish on such subjects, do not conceal it from me.”\n\nA few days afterwards, when she had returned from a visit to her brother’s, she began to speak to her husband on a subject which had for some time been in her mind.\n\n“I should like to do something for my brother’s family, Nicholas; and I think we are bound to make some amends to Rosamond and her husband. Walter says Mr. Lydgate must leave the town, and his practice is almost good for nothing, and they have very little left to settle anywhere with. I would rather do without something for ourselves, to make some amends to my poor brother’s family.”\n\nMrs. Bulstrode did not wish to go nearer to the facts than in the phrase “make some amends;” knowing that her husband must understand her. He had a particular reason, which she was not aware of, for wincing under her suggestion. He hesitated before he said—\n\n“It is not possible to carry out your wish in the way you propose, my dear. Mr. Lydgate has virtually rejected any further service from me. He has returned the thousand pounds which I lent him. Mrs. Casaubon advanced him the sum for that purpose. Here is his letter.”\n\nThe letter seemed to cut Mrs. Bulstrode severely. The mention of Mrs. Casaubon’s loan seemed a reflection of that public feeling which held it a matter of course that every one would avoid a connection with her husband. She was silent for some time; and the tears fell one after the other, her chin trembling as she wiped them away. Bulstrode, sitting opposite to her, ached at the sight of that grief-worn face, which two months before had been bright and blooming. It had aged to keep sad company with his own withered features. Urged into some effort at comforting her, he said—\n\n“There is another means, Harriet, by which I might do a service to your brother’s family, if you like to act in it. And it would, I think, be beneficial to you: it would be an advantageous way of managing the land which I mean to be yours.”\n\nShe looked attentive.\n\n“Garth once thought of undertaking the management of Stone Court in order to place your nephew Fred there. The stock was to remain as it is, and they were to pay a certain share of the profits instead of an ordinary rent. That would be a desirable beginning for the young man, in conjunction with his employment under Garth. Would it be a satisfaction to you?”\n\n“Yes, it would,” said Mrs. Bulstrode, with some return of energy. “Poor Walter is so cast down; I would try anything in my power to do him some good before I go away. We have always been brother and sister.”\n\n“You must make the proposal to Garth yourself, Harriet,” said Mr. Bulstrode, not liking what he had to say, but desiring the end he had in view, for other reasons besides the consolation of his wife. “You must state to him that the land is virtually yours, and that he need have no transactions with me. Communications can be made through Standish. I mention this, because Garth gave up being my agent. I can put into your hands a paper which he himself drew up, stating conditions; and you can propose his renewed acceptance of them. I think it is not unlikely that he will accept when you propose the thing for the sake of your nephew.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXVI.\n\n“Le cœur se sature d’amour comme d’un sel divin qui le conserve; de là l’incorruptible adhérence de ceux qui se sont aimés dès l’aube de la vie, et la fraîcheur des vielles amours prolongées. Il existe un embaumement d’amour. C’est de Daphnis et Chloé que sont faits Philémon et Baucis. Cette vieillesse-là, ressemblance du soir avec l’aurore.”—VICTOR HUGO: L’homme qui rit.\n\nMrs. Garth, hearing Caleb enter the passage about tea-time, opened the parlor-door and said, “There you are, Caleb. Have you had your dinner?” (Mr. Garth’s meals were much subordinated to “business.”)\n\n“Oh yes, a good dinner—cold mutton and I don’t know what. Where is Mary?”\n\n“In the garden with Letty, I think.”\n\n“Fred is not come yet?”\n\n“No. Are you going out again without taking tea, Caleb?” said Mrs. Garth, seeing that her absent-minded husband was putting on again the hat which he had just taken off.\n\n“No, no; I’m only going to Mary a minute.”\n\nMary was in a grassy corner of the garden, where there was a swing loftily hung between two pear-trees. She had a pink kerchief tied over her head, making a little poke to shade her eyes from the level sunbeams, while she was giving a glorious swing to Letty, who laughed and screamed wildly.\n\nSeeing her father, Mary left the swing and went to meet him, pushing back the pink kerchief and smiling afar off at him with the involuntary smile of loving pleasure.\n\n“I came to look for you, Mary,” said Mr. Garth. “Let us walk about a bit.”\n\nMary knew quite well that her father had something particular to say: his eyebrows made their pathetic angle, and there was a tender gravity in his voice: these things had been signs to her when she was Letty’s age. She put her arm within his, and they turned by the row of nut-trees.\n\n“It will be a sad while before you can be married, Mary,” said her father, not looking at her, but at the end of the stick which he held in his other hand.\n\n“Not a sad while, father—I mean to be merry,” said Mary, laughingly. “I have been single and merry for four-and-twenty years and more: I suppose it will not be quite as long again as that.” Then, after a little pause, she said, more gravely, bending her face before her father’s, “If you are contented with Fred?”\n\nCaleb screwed up his mouth and turned his head aside wisely.\n\n“Now, father, you did praise him last Wednesday. You said he had an uncommon notion of stock, and a good eye for things.”\n\n“Did I?” said Caleb, rather slyly.\n\n“Yes, I put it all down, and the date, anno Domini, and everything,” said Mary. “You like things to be neatly booked. And then his behavior to you, father, is really good; he has a deep respect for you; and it is impossible to have a better temper than Fred has.”\n\n“Ay, ay; you want to coax me into thinking him a fine match.”\n\n“No, indeed, father. I don’t love him because he is a fine match.”\n\n“What for, then?”\n\n“Oh, dear, because I have always loved him. I should never like scolding any one else so well; and that is a point to be thought of in a husband.”\n\n“Your mind is quite settled, then, Mary?” said Caleb, returning to his first tone. “There’s no other wish come into it since things have been going on as they have been of late?” (Caleb meant a great deal in that vague phrase;) “because, better late than never. A woman must not force her heart—she’ll do a man no good by that.”\n\n“My feelings have not changed, father,” said Mary, calmly. “I shall be constant to Fred as long as he is constant to me. I don’t think either of us could spare the other, or like any one else better, however much we might admire them. It would make too great a difference to us—like seeing all the old places altered, and changing the name for everything. We must wait for each other a long while; but Fred knows that.”\n\nInstead of speaking immediately, Caleb stood still and screwed his stick on the grassy walk. Then he said, with emotion in his voice, “Well, I’ve got a bit of news. What do you think of Fred going to live at Stone Court, and managing the land there?”\n\n“How can that ever be, father?” said Mary, wonderingly.\n\n“He would manage it for his aunt Bulstrode. The poor woman has been to me begging and praying. She wants to do the lad good, and it might be a fine thing for him. With saving, he might gradually buy the stock, and he has a turn for farming.”\n\n“Oh, Fred would be so happy! It is too good to believe.”\n\n“Ah, but mind you,” said Caleb, turning his head warningly, “I must take it on my shoulders, and be responsible, and see after everything; and that will grieve your mother a bit, though she mayn’t say so. Fred had need be careful.”\n\n“Perhaps it is too much, father,” said Mary, checked in her joy. “There would be no happiness in bringing you any fresh trouble.”\n\n“Nay, nay; work is my delight, child, when it doesn’t vex your mother. And then, if you and Fred get married,” here Caleb’s voice shook just perceptibly, “he’ll be steady and saving; and you’ve got your mother’s cleverness, and mine too, in a woman’s sort of way; and you’ll keep him in order. He’ll be coming by-and-by, so I wanted to tell you first, because I think you’d like to tell him by yourself. After that, I could talk it well over with him, and we could go into business and the nature of things.”\n\n“Oh, you dear good father!” cried Mary, putting her hands round her father’s neck, while he bent his head placidly, willing to be caressed. “I wonder if any other girl thinks her father the best man in the world!”\n\n“Nonsense, child; you’ll think your husband better.”\n\n“Impossible,” said Mary, relapsing into her usual tone; “husbands are an inferior class of men, who require keeping in order.”\n\nWhen they were entering the house with Letty, who had run to join them, Mary saw Fred at the orchard-gate, and went to meet him.\n\n“What fine clothes you wear, you extravagant youth!” said Mary, as Fred stood still and raised his hat to her with playful formality. “You are not learning economy.”\n\n“Now that is too bad, Mary,” said Fred. “Just look at the edges of these coat-cuffs! It is only by dint of good brushing that I look respectable. I am saving up three suits—one for a wedding-suit.”\n\n“How very droll you will look!—like a gentleman in an old fashion-book.”\n\n“Oh no, they will keep two years.”\n\n“Two years! be reasonable, Fred,” said Mary, turning to walk. “Don’t encourage flattering expectations.”\n\n“Why not? One lives on them better than on unflattering ones. If we can’t be married in two years, the truth will be quite bad enough when it comes.”\n\n“I have heard a story of a young gentleman who once encouraged flattering expectations, and they did him harm.”\n\n“Mary, if you’ve got something discouraging to tell me, I shall bolt; I shall go into the house to Mr. Garth. I am out of spirits. My father is so cut up—home is not like itself. I can’t bear any more bad news.”\n\n“Should you call it bad news to be told that you were to live at Stone Court, and manage the farm, and be remarkably prudent, and save money every year till all the stock and furniture were your own, and you were a distinguished agricultural character, as Mr. Borthrop Trumbull says—rather stout, I fear, and with the Greek and Latin sadly weather-worn?”\n\n“You don’t mean anything except nonsense, Mary?” said Fred, coloring slightly nevertheless.\n\n“That is what my father has just told me of as what may happen, and he never talks nonsense,” said Mary, looking up at Fred now, while he grasped her hand as they walked, till it rather hurt her; but she would not complain.\n\n“Oh, I could be a tremendously good fellow then, Mary, and we could be married directly.”\n\n“Not so fast, sir; how do you know that I would not rather defer our marriage for some years? That would leave you time to misbehave, and then if I liked some one else better, I should have an excuse for jilting you.”\n\n“Pray don’t joke, Mary,” said Fred, with strong feeling. “Tell me seriously that all this is true, and that you are happy because of it—because you love me best.”\n\n“It is all true, Fred, and I am happy because of it—because I love you best,” said Mary, in a tone of obedient recitation.\n\nThey lingered on the door-step under the steep-roofed porch, and Fred almost in a whisper said—\n\n“When we were first engaged, with the umbrella-ring, Mary, you used to—”\n\nThe spirit of joy began to laugh more decidedly in Mary’s eyes, but the fatal Ben came running to the door with Brownie yapping behind him, and, bouncing against them, said—\n\n“Fred and Mary! are you ever coming in?—or may I eat your cake?”\n\nEvery limit is a beginning as well as an ending. Who can quit young lives after being long in company with them, and not desire to know what befell them in their after-years? For the fragment of a life, however typical, is not the sample of an even web: promises may not be kept, and an ardent outset may be followed by declension; latent powers may find their long-waited opportunity; a past error may urge a grand retrieval.\n\nMarriage, which has been the bourne of so many narratives, is still a great beginning, as it was to Adam and Eve, who kept their honeymoon in Eden, but had their first little one among the thorns and thistles of the wilderness. It is still the beginning of the home epic—the gradual conquest or irremediable loss of that complete union which makes the advancing years a climax, and age the harvest of sweet memories in common.\n\nSome set out, like Crusaders of old, with a glorious equipment of hope and enthusiasm and get broken by the way, wanting patience with each other and the world.\n\nAll who have cared for Fred Vincy and Mary Garth will like to know that these two made no such failure, but achieved a solid mutual happiness. Fred surprised his neighbors in various ways. He became rather distinguished in his side of the county as a theoretic and practical farmer, and produced a work on the “Cultivation of Green Crops and the Economy of Cattle-Feeding” which won him high congratulations at agricultural meetings. In Middlemarch admiration was more reserved: most persons there were inclined to believe that the merit of Fred’s authorship was due to his wife, since they had never expected Fred Vincy to write on turnips and mangel-wurzel.\n\nBut when Mary wrote a little book for her boys, called “Stories of Great Men, taken from Plutarch,” and had it printed and published by Gripp & Co., Middlemarch, every one in the town was willing to give the credit of this work to Fred, observing that he had been to the University, “where the ancients were studied,” and might have been a clergyman if he had chosen.\n\nIn this way it was made clear that Middlemarch had never been deceived, and that there was no need to praise anybody for writing a book, since it was always done by somebody else.\n\nMoreover, Fred remained unswervingly steady. Some years after his marriage he told Mary that his happiness was half owing to Farebrother, who gave him a strong pull-up at the right moment. I cannot say that he was never again misled by his hopefulness: the yield of crops or the profits of a cattle sale usually fell below his estimate; and he was always prone to believe that he could make money by the purchase of a horse which turned out badly—though this, Mary observed, was of course the fault of the horse, not of Fred’s judgment. He kept his love of horsemanship, but he rarely allowed himself a day’s hunting; and when he did so, it was remarkable that he submitted to be laughed at for cowardliness at the fences, seeming to see Mary and the boys sitting on the five-barred gate, or showing their curly heads between hedge and ditch.\n\nThere were three boys: Mary was not discontented that she brought forth men-children only; and when Fred wished to have a girl like her, she said, laughingly, “that would be too great a trial to your mother.” Mrs. Vincy in her declining years, and in the diminished lustre of her housekeeping, was much comforted by her perception that two at least of Fred’s boys were real Vincys, and did not “feature the Garths.” But Mary secretly rejoiced that the youngest of the three was very much what her father must have been when he wore a round jacket, and showed a marvellous nicety of aim in playing at marbles, or in throwing stones to bring down the mellow pears.\n\nBen and Letty Garth, who were uncle and aunt before they were well in their teens, disputed much as to whether nephews or nieces were more desirable; Ben contending that it was clear girls were good for less than boys, else they would not be always in petticoats, which showed how little they were meant for; whereupon Letty, who argued much from books, got angry in replying that God made coats of skins for both Adam and Eve alike—also it occurred to her that in the East the men too wore petticoats. But this latter argument, obscuring the majesty of the former, was one too many, for Ben answered contemptuously, “The more spooneys they!” and immediately appealed to his mother whether boys were not better than girls. Mrs. Garth pronounced that both were alike naughty, but that boys were undoubtedly stronger, could run faster, and throw with more precision to a greater distance. With this oracular sentence Ben was well satisfied, not minding the naughtiness; but Letty took it ill, her feeling of superiority being stronger than her muscles.\n\nFred never became rich—his hopefulness had not led him to expect that; but he gradually saved enough to become owner of the stock and furniture at Stone Court, and the work which Mr. Garth put into his hands carried him in plenty through those “bad times” which are always present with farmers. Mary, in her matronly days, became as solid in figure as her mother; but, unlike her, gave the boys little formal teaching, so that Mrs. Garth was alarmed lest they should never be well grounded in grammar and geography. Nevertheless, they were found quite forward enough when they went to school; perhaps, because they had liked nothing so well as being with their mother. When Fred was riding home on winter evenings he had a pleasant vision beforehand of the bright hearth in the wainscoted parlor, and was sorry for other men who could not have Mary for their wife; especially for Mr. Farebrother. “He was ten times worthier of you than I was,” Fred could now say to her, magnanimously. “To be sure he was,” Mary answered; “and for that reason he could do better without me. But you—I shudder to think what you would have been—a curate in debt for horse-hire and cambric pocket-handkerchiefs!”\n\nOn inquiry it might possibly be found that Fred and Mary still inhabit Stone Court—that the creeping plants still cast the foam of their blossoms over the fine stone-wall into the field where the walnut-trees stand in stately row—and that on sunny days the two lovers who were first engaged with the umbrella-ring may be seen in white-haired placidity at the open window from which Mary Garth, in the days of old Peter Featherstone, had often been ordered to look out for Mr. Lydgate.\n\nLydgate’s hair never became white. He died when he was only fifty, leaving his wife and children provided for by a heavy insurance on his life. He had gained an excellent practice, alternating, according to the season, between London and a Continental bathing-place; having written a treatise on Gout, a disease which has a good deal of wealth on its side. His skill was relied on by many paying patients, but he always regarded himself as a failure: he had not done what he once meant to do. His acquaintances thought him enviable to have so charming a wife, and nothing happened to shake their opinion. Rosamond never committed a second compromising indiscretion. She simply continued to be mild in her temper, inflexible in her judgment, disposed to admonish her husband, and able to frustrate him by stratagem. As the years went on he opposed her less and less, whence Rosamond concluded that he had learned the value of her opinion; on the other hand, she had a more thorough conviction of his talents now that he gained a good income, and instead of the threatened cage in Bride Street provided one all flowers and gilding, fit for the bird of paradise that she resembled. In brief, Lydgate was what is called a successful man. But he died prematurely of diphtheria, and Rosamond afterwards married an elderly and wealthy physician, who took kindly to her four children. She made a very pretty show with her daughters, driving out in her carriage, and often spoke of her happiness as “a reward”—she did not say for what, but probably she meant that it was a reward for her patience with Tertius, whose temper never became faultless, and to the last occasionally let slip a bitter speech which was more memorable than the signs he made of his repentance. He once called her his basil plant; and when she asked for an explanation, said that basil was a plant which had flourished wonderfully on a murdered man’s brains. Rosamond had a placid but strong answer to such speeches. Why then had he chosen her? It was a pity he had not had Mrs. Ladislaw, whom he was always praising and placing above her. And thus the conversation ended with the advantage on Rosamond’s side. But it would be unjust not to tell, that she never uttered a word in depreciation of Dorothea, keeping in religious remembrance the generosity which had come to her aid in the sharpest crisis of her life.\n\nDorothea herself had no dreams of being praised above other women, feeling that there was always something better which she might have done, if she had only been better and known better. Still, she never repented that she had given up position and fortune to marry Will Ladislaw, and he would have held it the greatest shame as well as sorrow to him if she had repented. They were bound to each other by a love stronger than any impulses which could have marred it. No life would have been possible to Dorothea which was not filled with emotion, and she had now a life filled also with a beneficent activity which she had not the doubtful pains of discovering and marking out for herself. Will became an ardent public man, working well in those times when reforms were begun with a young hopefulness of immediate good which has been much checked in our days, and getting at last returned to Parliament by a constituency who paid his expenses. Dorothea could have liked nothing better, since wrongs existed, than that her husband should be in the thick of a struggle against them, and that she should give him wifely help. Many who knew her, thought it a pity that so substantive and rare a creature should have been absorbed into the life of another, and be only known in a certain circle as a wife and mother. But no one stated exactly what else that was in her power she ought rather to have done—not even Sir James Chettam, who went no further than the negative prescription that she ought not to have married Will Ladislaw.\n\nBut this opinion of his did not cause a lasting alienation; and the way in which the family was made whole again was characteristic of all concerned. Mr. Brooke could not resist the pleasure of corresponding with Will and Dorothea; and one morning when his pen had been remarkably fluent on the prospects of Municipal Reform, it ran off into an invitation to the Grange, which, once written, could not be done away with at less cost than the sacrifice (hardly to be conceived) of the whole valuable letter. During the months of this correspondence Mr. Brooke had continually, in his talk with Sir James Chettam, been presupposing or hinting that the intention of cutting off the entail was still maintained; and the day on which his pen gave the daring invitation, he went to Freshitt expressly to intimate that he had a stronger sense than ever of the reasons for taking that energetic step as a precaution against any mixture of low blood in the heir of the Brookes.\n\nBut that morning something exciting had happened at the Hall. A letter had come to Celia which made her cry silently as she read it; and when Sir James, unused to see her in tears, asked anxiously what was the matter, she burst out in a wail such as he had never heard from her before.\n\n“Dorothea has a little boy. And you will not let me go and see her. And I am sure she wants to see me. And she will not know what to do with the baby—she will do wrong things with it. And they thought she would die. It is very dreadful! Suppose it had been me and little Arthur, and Dodo had been hindered from coming to see me! I wish you would be less unkind, James!”\n\n“Good heavens, Celia!” said Sir James, much wrought upon, “what do you wish? I will do anything you like. I will take you to town to-morrow if you wish it.” And Celia did wish it.\n\nIt was after this that Mr. Brooke came, and meeting the Baronet in the grounds, began to chat with him in ignorance of the news, which Sir James for some reason did not care to tell him immediately. But when the entail was touched on in the usual way, he said, “My dear sir, it is not for me to dictate to you, but for my part I would let that alone. I would let things remain as they are.”\n\nMr. Brooke felt so much surprised that he did not at once find out how much he was relieved by the sense that he was not expected to do anything in particular.\n\nSuch being the bent of Celia’s heart, it was inevitable that Sir James should consent to a reconciliation with Dorothea and her husband. Where women love each other, men learn to smother their mutual dislike. Sir James never liked Ladislaw, and Will always preferred to have Sir James’s company mixed with another kind: they were on a footing of reciprocal tolerance which was made quite easy only when Dorothea and Celia were present.\n\nIt became an understood thing that Mr. and Mrs. Ladislaw should pay at least two visits during the year to the Grange, and there came gradually a small row of cousins at Freshitt who enjoyed playing with the two cousins visiting Tipton as much as if the blood of these cousins had been less dubiously mixed.\n\nMr. Brooke lived to a good old age, and his estate was inherited by Dorothea’s son, who might have represented Middlemarch, but declined, thinking that his opinions had less chance of being stifled if he remained out of doors.\n\nSir James never ceased to regard Dorothea’s second marriage as a mistake; and indeed this remained the tradition concerning it in Middlemarch, where she was spoken of to a younger generation as a fine girl who married a sickly clergyman, old enough to be her father, and in little more than a year after his death gave up her estate to marry his cousin—young enough to have been his son, with no property, and not well-born. Those who had not seen anything of Dorothea usually observed that she could not have been “a nice woman,” else she would not have married either the one or the other.\n\nCertainly those determining acts of her life were not ideally beautiful. They were the mixed result of young and noble impulse struggling amidst the conditions of an imperfect social state, in which great feelings will often take the aspect of error, and great faith the aspect of illusion. For there is no creature whose inward being is so strong that it is not greatly determined by what lies outside it. A new Theresa will hardly have the opportunity of reforming a conventual life, any more than a new Antigone will spend her heroic piety in daring all for the sake of a brother’s burial: the medium in which their ardent deeds took shape is forever gone. But we insignificant people with our daily words and acts are preparing the lives of many Dorotheas, some of which may present a far sadder sacrifice than that of the Dorothea whose story we know.\n\nHer finely touched spirit had still its fine issues, though they were not widely visible. Her full nature, like that river of which Cyrus broke the strength, spent itself in channels which had no great name on the earth. But the effect of her being on those around her was incalculably diffusive: for the growing good of the world is partly dependent on unhistoric acts; and that things are not so ill with you and me as they might have been, is half owing to the number who lived faithfully a hidden life, and rest in unvisited tombs.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-145"]="인간의 역사에, 그리고 그 신비로운 혼합물이 시간의 다양한 실험 아래서 어떻게 행동하는지에 관심을 두는 사람이라면, 적어도 잠시나마 성녀 테레사의 생애에 마음을 머문 적이 없을까, 어느 날 아침 자기보다 더 작은 남동생의 손을 잡고 무어인의 땅으로 순교를 찾아 길을 나선 어린 소녀를 떠올리며 다소 부드럽게 미소 짓지 않은 사람이 있을까? 두 아이는 험준한 아빌라에서 아장아장 걸어 나왔으니, 두 마리 새끼 사슴처럼 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 어찌할 바를 모르는 듯 보였지만, 이미 하나의 국가적 이상을 향해 고동치는 인간의 심장을 지니고 있었다. 그러다 삼촌들의 모습을 한 가정의 현실이 그들을 가로막아 그 위대한 결심으로부터 돌려세웠다. 그 어린 시절의 순례는 어울리는 시작이었다. 테레사의 열정적이고 이상주의적인 천성은 서사시적인 삶을 요구했으니, 여러 권에 달하는 기사도 연애담이나 한 명석한 소녀가 거두는 사교계의 정복 따위가 그녀에게 무슨 의미가 있었겠는가? 그녀의 불꽃은 그런 가벼운 연료를 순식간에 태워 버렸고, 내부로부터 양분을 받아 어떤 무한한 만족을, 결코 권태를 정당화하지 않을 어떤 대상을, 자기 자신을 넘어선 삶의 황홀한 의식과 자기 절망을 화해시켜 줄 어떤 대상을 향해 솟아올랐다. 그녀는 한 종교 교단의 개혁에서 자신의 서사시를 발견했다.\n\n삼백 년 전에 살았던 그 스페인 여인은 분명 자기 부류의 마지막은 아니었다. 많은 테레사들이 태어났으나, 그들은 멀리까지 울려 퍼지는 행위가 끊임없이 펼쳐지는 서사시적인 삶을 스스로 찾아내지 못했다. 어쩌면 그것은 단지 실수의 연속인 삶, 어떤 정신적 위대함이 기회의 보잘것없음과 어긋나게 짝지어진 데서 태어난 자식 같은 삶이었을 것이다. 어쩌면 거룩한 시인을 만나지 못한 채 아무도 울어 주지 않는 망각 속으로 가라앉아 버린 비극적 실패였을 것이다. 그들은 어슴푸레한 빛과 뒤엉킨 환경 속에서 자신의 생각과 행위를 고결한 조화 속에 빚어내려 애썼다. 하지만 결국 평범한 눈에는 그들의 분투가 한낱 일관성 없음과 형태 없음으로 보일 따름이었다. 후대에 태어난 이 테레사들은, 열렬히 의지하는 영혼을 위해 지식의 기능을 수행해 줄 수 있는 일관된 사회적 신앙과 질서의 도움을 전혀 받지 못했기 때문이다. 그들의 열정은 막연한 이상과 여성다움의 평범한 갈망 사이를 오갔으니, 그리하여 전자는 지나친 일탈로 못마땅하게 여겨졌고, 후자는 타락으로 단죄되었다.\n\n어떤 이들은 이렇게 더듬거리는 삶이 지고한 권능이 여성의 본성을 빚어낼 때 보인, 곤혹스러울 만큼 명확하지 못한 솜씨 탓이라고 느껴 왔다. 만약 셋까지 셀 수 있되 그 이상은 셀 수 없는 능력처럼 엄격하게 정해진 단 하나의 여성적 무능의 수준이 있다면, 여성의 사회적 운명도 과학적 확실성을 가지고 다룰 수 있으리라. 하지만 그동안에도 그 불명확함은 여전히 남아 있고, 그 변이의 한계는 사실 여성들의 한결같은 머리 모양이나 산문과 운문으로 즐겨 읽히는 연애담에서 누구든 상상할 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 넓다. 여기저기서 새끼 백조 한 마리가 흙탕물 연못의 새끼 오리들 틈에서 불안하게 길러지지만, 끝내 제 발에 물갈퀴를 단 동족과 어울려 노니는 살아 흐르는 물줄기를 찾지 못한다. 여기저기서 아무것도 세우지 못한 창립자, 한 명의 성녀 테레사가 태어나지만, 이루지 못한 선을 향한 그녀의 사랑에 찬 심장의 고동과 흐느낌은 어떤 오래 기억될 행위로 모이지 못한 채, 갖가지 장애물 사이에서 떨리며 흩어져 버린다.\n\n# BOOK I. MISS BROOKE. CHAPTER I.\n\n그 가까이에 있는 무언가에 끊임없이 손을 뻗네.\n— 『처녀의 비극』, 보몬트와 플레처\n\n브룩 양은 초라한 옷차림 덕분에 도리어 돋보이는 듯한 종류의 아름다움을 지니고 있었다. 그녀의 손과 손목은 어찌나 곱게 빚어졌던지, 이탈리아 화가들이 그린 성모 마리아가 입은 것 못지않게 멋이라곤 없는 소매를 걸쳐도 좋을 정도였다. 그녀의 옆모습과 키, 자태 또한 수수한 옷차림에서 한층 더 기품을 얻는 것 같았으니, 지방의 유행과 나란히 놓이면 그 옷차림은 오늘 자 신문 한 문단 속에 인용된 성경의 한 구절—아니면 우리 선대 시인 가운데 한 사람의 한 구절—같은 인상적인 느낌을 그녀에게 주었다. 사람들은 흔히 그녀가 놀랄 만큼 영리하다고 말했지만, 동생 실리아가 분별력은 더 낫다는 말을 덧붙이곤 했다. 그렇지만 실리아의 옷에도 장식은 별로 더 붙어 있지 않았으니, 그녀의 옷차림이 언니의 것과 다르고 그 매무새에 약간의 교태가 어려 있다는 사실은 눈여겨보는 이들에게만 보였다. 브룩 양의 수수한 옷차림은 여러 가지 사정에서 비롯된 것이었고, 그 사정의 대부분을 동생도 함께 나누고 있었기 때문이다. 숙녀라는 자부심도 거기에 한몫했다. 브룩 가문의 인척들은 정확히 귀족이랄 수는 없어도 의심할 바 없이 \"훌륭한\" 집안이었다. 한두 세대 거슬러 올라가 보아도 자를 들고 물건을 재거나 꾸러미를 묶던 조상은 찾아볼 수 없었으니, 제독이나 성직자보다 낮은 신분은 없었다. 심지어 크롬웰 휘하에서 복무했으나 나중에 국교에 순응하여 갖가지 정치적 풍파를 헤치고 어엿한 가문 사유지의 소유주로 빠져나온 청교도 신사 한 사람이 선조로 식별되기까지 했다. 이런 가문에서 태어나 조용한 시골 저택에 살며 응접실보다 별로 크지도 않은 마을 교회에 다니는 젊은 여성들이라면, 당연히 화려한 치장을 잡화상 딸의 야심쯤으로 여겼다. 게다가 가문 좋은 집안의 검약 정신이라는 것이 있었으니, 그 시절에는 신분에 더 어울리는 지출을 위한 여유가 필요할 때면 옷치레가 가장 먼저 줄여야 할 항목이었다. 이런 이유들만으로도 종교적 감정과는 별개로 수수한 옷차림은 충분히 설명될 수 있었다. 하지만 브룩 양의 경우라면 종교만으로도 그 옷차림이 결정되었을 것이다. 실리아는 언니의 모든 정서를 온순하게 따랐으나, 다만 거기에 분별력을 불어넣었으니, 그 분별력이란 별난 동요 없이도 중대한 교리를 받아들일 줄 아는 것이었다. 도러시아는 파스칼의 『팡세』와 제러미 테일러의 글 가운데 여러 구절을 외고 있었다. 그녀에게는 기독교의 빛으로 바라본 인류의 운명 앞에서, 여성스러운 유행에 마음 쓰는 일이란 정신병원에나 어울리는 소일거리로 보였다. 그녀는 영원한 결과를 초래하는 영적 삶의 근심을, 가두리 장식과 인공적으로 부풀린 옷자락에 대한 예리한 관심과 도무지 조화시킬 수 없었다. 그녀의 정신은 이론적이어서, 본성상 어떤 고결한 세계관을 갈망했으니, 그 세계관은 팁턴 교구와 그곳에서의 자기 행동 규범까지도 거리낌 없이 품을 수 있는 것이어야 했다. 그녀는 강렬함과 위대함에 매혹되어, 그런 면모를 지닌 듯 보이는 것이라면 무엇이든 무모하게 끌어안았다. 순교를 추구하다가, 그것을 철회하고, 끝내는 자기가 찾지도 않았던 곳에서 순교를 당하기 십상인 사람이었다. 결혼 적령기 처녀의 성격에 이런 요소들이 깃들어 있으면 분명 그녀의 운명에 지장을 주어, 미모와 허영심과 한낱 개 같은 애정에 따라 관습대로 정해지는 것을 가로막기 마련이었다. 이 모든 면모를 지녔으면서도, 자매 가운데 언니인 그녀는 아직 스무 살이 채 되지 않았다. 두 사람은 열두 살 무렵 부모를 여읜 뒤로, 한편으로는 편협하고 한편으로는 두서없는 방침에 따라 교육을 받았으니, 처음에는 어느 영국 가정에서, 이후에는 로잔의 어느 스위스 가정에서였다. 독신인 숙부이자 후견인이 이런 식으로 고아가 된 처지의 불리함을 메워 주려 애쓴 것이었다.\n\n자매가 숙부와 함께 팁턴 그레인지에 와서 산 지는 일 년이 채 되지 않았다. 숙부는 예순에 가까운 사람으로, 순응적인 기질에, 잡다한 의견을 지녔으며, 표를 어디에 던질지도 종잡을 수 없는 인물이었다. 그는 젊은 시절에 여행을 다녔던 까닭에, 이 지방에서는 지나치게 산만한 사고 습관이 몸에 밴 사람으로 여겨졌다. 브룩 씨의 결론은 날씨만큼이나 예측하기 어려웠으니, 그가 자비로운 의도로 행동하리라는 것, 그리고 그것을 실행에 옮기는 데에는 가능한 한 돈을 적게 쓰리라는 것만은 안심하고 말할 수 있었다. 아무리 끈적끈적하니 종잡을 수 없는 정신이라도 그 안에는 단단한 습관의 알갱이가 몇 알쯤 들어 있는 법이니, 자기 코담뱃갑을 지키는 일을 제외하고는 자신의 모든 이해관계에 무심하면서도 그 갑에 대해서만은 경계하고 의심하며 움켜쥐려 탐내는 사람도 본 적이 있다.\n\n브룩 씨에게는 청교도적 정력의 유전적 기질이 분명 잠들어 있었다. 하지만 조카 도러시아에게서는 그 기질이 결점과 미덕 양쪽으로 똑같이 타올라, 때로는 숙부의 이야기나 사유지를 \"되는대로 내버려 두는\" 방식에 대한 조바심으로 변하기도 했고, 또 자기가 성년이 되어 돈을 어느 정도 마음대로 쓰며 너그러운 계획들을 펼칠 수 있게 될 날을 한층 더 갈망하게 만들기도 했다. 그녀는 상속녀로 여겨졌으니, 자매가 각각 부모에게서 연간 칠백 파운드씩을 받을 뿐 아니라, 만약 도러시아가 결혼해 아들을 낳으면 그 아들이 브룩 씨의 사유지를 물려받을 것이었기 때문이다. 그 사유지는 연간 삼천 파운드쯤의 가치가 있는 것으로 짐작되었는데, 그런 지대 수입은 지방 가문들에게는 부유함으로 보였으니, 그들은 아직도 가톨릭 문제에 대한 필 씨의 근래 처신을 논하면서, 장차 닥칠 금광에 대해서도, 또한 점잖은 생활의 필수품을 그토록 고귀하게 드높여 놓은 저 휘황찬란한 금권 계층에 대해서도 까맣게 모르고 있었다.\n\n그러니 도러시아가 어찌 결혼하지 않을 수 있겠는가? 그토록 아름답고 그토록 전망이 밝은 처녀가? 그 무엇도 그것을 막을 수 없었으니, 다만 극단을 좋아하는 그녀의 성향과, 신중한 남자라면 청혼을 하기 전에 망설이게 만들 법한, 아니 끝내 그녀로 하여금 모든 청혼을 거절하게 만들지도 모를 그런 관념들에 따라 삶을 다스리겠다는 고집만은 예외였다. 좋은 가문과 재산을 갖춘 젊은 숙녀가 병든 일꾼 곁의 벽돌 바닥에 느닷없이 무릎을 꿇고, 마치 자기가 사도들의 시대에 살고 있다고 여기기라도 하듯 열렬히 기도를 올린다니. 천주교도처럼 단식을 한다는 둥, 밤을 새워 낡은 신학 서적을 읽는다는 둥, 이상한 변덕을 부리는 처녀라니! 그런 아내라면 어느 화창한 아침에 당신을 깨워 자기 수입의 사용처에 관한 새로운 계획을 들이밀어, 정치경제학과 승마용 말 키우는 일에 지장을 줄지도 모른다. 그러니 남자라면 당연히 그런 동반자 관계에 자신을 내맡기기 전에 두 번은 생각해 볼 일이다. 여자란 의견이 약하리라고 으레 기대되었지만, 사회와 가정생활을 지키는 큰 안전판은 그 의견이 실행에 옮겨지지 않는다는 데 있었다. 제정신인 사람들은 이웃이 하는 대로 했으니, 그래야 행여 미치광이가 돌아다닌다 해도 그가 누구인지 알아보고 피할 수 있는 것이었다.\n\n새로 온 두 아가씨에 대한 시골 사람들의 의견은, 심지어 오두막에 사는 이들 사이에서도, 대체로 실리아 쪽이 우세했으니, 그녀가 무척 상냥하고 천진해 보였기 때문이다. 반면 브룩 양의 커다란 눈은 그녀의 신앙과 마찬가지로 너무 별나고 강렬해 보였다. 가엾은 도러시아! 그녀에 비하면 천진해 보이는 실리아가 오히려 영악하고 세상 물정에 밝은 셈이었다. 인간의 정신이란 그것의 일종의 문장(紋章)이나 시계 문자판 노릇을 하는 겉껍질보다 그토록 미묘한 법이다.\n\n그렇지만 이 섬뜩한 풍문 탓에 그녀에게 편견을 품고 다가간 이들조차도, 막상 그 풍문과 어찌 된 영문인지 어우러지는 매력을 그녀에게서 발견하곤 했다. 대다수 남자들은 그녀가 말을 탄 모습을 보면 넋을 잃었다. 그녀는 신선한 공기와 시골의 다채로운 풍경을 사랑했으니, 두 눈과 두 뺨이 뒤섞인 기쁨으로 빛날 때면 신앙에 헌신하는 사람으로는 도무지 보이지 않았다. 승마는 그녀가 양심의 가책을 무릅쓰고 자신에게 허락한 즐거움이었다. 그녀는 자기가 그것을 이교도처럼 관능적인 방식으로 즐긴다고 느꼈고, 늘 그것을 단념할 날을 고대했다.\n\n그녀는 솔직하고 열정적이며 조금도 자기를 우러러보지 않았다. 실제로 그녀의 상상이 동생 실리아를 자기보다 한결 뛰어난 매력으로 꾸며 놓는 모습은 보기에 사랑스러웠다. 또 어느 신사가 브룩 씨를 만나는 것 말고 다른 동기로 그레인지를 찾는 듯하면, 그녀는 그가 분명 실리아를 사랑하는 것이라고 결론지었다. 이를테면 제임스 체텀 경이 그러했으니, 그녀는 늘 실리아의 관점에서 그를 헤아리며, 실리아가 그의 청혼을 받아들이는 것이 과연 좋을지 속으로 따져 보곤 했다. 그가 자기 자신에게 구혼하는 사람으로 여겨지리라는 생각은 그녀에게는 우스꽝스러울 만큼 엉뚱한 일로 보였을 것이다. 도러시아는 삶의 진리를 알고자 그토록 열망하면서도, 결혼에 대해서는 무척이나 어린아이 같은 관념을 지니고 있었다. 그녀는 만약 자기가 제때 태어나 분별 있는 후커를 그 결혼이라는 비참한 실수에서 구할 수 있었더라면 그를 받아들였으리라고 확신했다. 아니면 실명이 찾아온 존 밀턴을, 아니면 그 기이한 습관을 견뎌 내는 것이 영광스러운 신심이 되었을 다른 위인들 가운데 누구라도 받아들였으리라고. 하지만 그녀가 불확실함을 내비칠 때조차 그녀의 말에 \"지당하신 말씀입니다\"라고 답하는 상냥하고 잘생긴 준남작이라니, 그런 사람이 어떻게 그녀에게 연인으로서 마음을 움직일 수 있겠는가? 정말로 흐뭇한 결혼이란, 남편이 일종의 아버지 같아서, 원한다면 히브리어까지도 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 그런 결혼이어야 했다.\n\n도러시아의 성격에 깃든 이런 별난 점들 때문에, 브룩 씨는 조카들의 길잡이이자 동무가 되어 줄 중년 부인을 들이지 않았다는 이유로 이웃 가문들로부터 한층 더 비난을 받았다. 하지만 정작 그 자신은 그런 자리에 들일 만한 종류의 잘난 여인을 몹시 두려워한 나머지, 도러시아의 반대에 못 이겨 단념하고 말았으니, 이 일에서만큼은 세상에 맞설 만큼 용감했다. 다시 말해 교구 목사의 아내인 캐드월러더 부인과, 그가 롬셔 북동쪽 모퉁이에서 왕래하던 소수의 지주 무리에게 맞선 셈이었다. 그리하여 브룩 양은 숙부의 집안을 도맡아 다스렸고, 거기에 따르는 존경과 더불어 자신의 새로운 권한을 조금도 싫어하지 않았다.\n\n제임스 체텀 경은 오늘 그레인지에서 만찬을 들기로 되어 있었는데, 자매가 한 번도 본 적 없는 또 다른 신사도 함께였으니, 도러시아는 그 사람에 대해 어떤 경외에 찬 기대를 품고 있었다. 그가 바로 에드워드 캐소본 목사로, 이 지방에서 심오한 학식을 지닌 사람으로 이름이 높았으며, 여러 해 동안 종교사에 관한 위대한 저작에 매달려 있다고 알려진 인물이었다. 또한 그 신심에 광채를 더해 줄 만큼 재산이 넉넉한 사람이었으며, 자신의 견해를 지니고 있어 그 책이 출간되면 그 견해가 한층 분명히 드러나리라고 했다. 그의 이름 자체가, 학문의 정확한 연대기 없이는 도무지 가늠할 수 없을 만큼 묵직한 인상을 풍겼다.\n\n그날 일찍 도러시아는 자기가 마을에 세워 운영하던 유아 학교에서 돌아와, 자매의 침실 사이에 자리한 아담한 거실에서 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아, 어떤 건물의 설계도를 마무리하는 데 몰두해 있었다(그녀가 즐기는 종류의 일이었다). 그때 무언가를 제안하고 싶어 망설이며 그녀를 지켜보던 실리아가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"언니, 괜찮으면—그러니까 별로 바쁘지 않으면—오늘 엄마 보석을 꺼내 보고, 나눠 가지면 어떨까? 숙부님이 그걸 언니한테 주신 지 오늘로 꼭 여섯 달이 됐는데, 언니는 아직 한 번도 들여다보지 않았잖아.\"\n\n실리아의 얼굴에는 입을 삐죽거리는 듯한 표정의 그림자가 어려 있었으나, 그것이 온전히 드러나지 못한 것은 도러시아에 대한 습관적인 경외와 신념 때문이었다. 이 둘은 함께 얽힌 사실이어서, 자칫 조심성 없이 건드리면 어떤 신비로운 전류를 내비칠지도 몰랐다. 다행스럽게도 도러시아가 고개를 들었을 때 그 눈에는 웃음이 가득했다.\n\n\"넌 정말 놀라운 꼬마 달력이로구나, 실리아! 그게 양력으로 여섯 달이니, 아니면 음력으로 여섯 달이니?\"\n\n\"지금이 9월 마지막 날이고, 숙부님이 언니한테 그걸 주신 게 4월 초하루였잖아. 그날까지 잊고 있었다고 숙부님이 말씀하신 거 기억하지. 언니는 여기 장식장에 넣고 잠가 둔 뒤로 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없을걸.\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘, 우리가 그걸 차고 다닐 일이야 없잖니.\" 도러시아는 반은 어르듯, 반은 설명하듯, 다정함이 가득한 목소리로 말했다. 손에는 연필을 쥐고서, 여백에 자그마한 곁그림들을 그리고 있었다.\n\n실리아는 얼굴을 붉히고 무척 진지한 표정을 지었다. \"내 생각엔, 언니, 우리가 그걸 치워 두고 거들떠보지도 않는 건 엄마를 추억하는 마음에 소홀한 거야. 그리고,\" 그녀는 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 분한 마음에 울먹임이 차오르는 채로 덧붙였다. \"요즘은 목걸이를 흔히들 하잖아. 그리고 어떤 일에서는 언니보다도 더 엄격했던 푸앵송 부인도 장신구를 차곤 했어. 게다가 기독교인들도 으레—분명 지금 천국에도 보석을 찼던 여인들이 있을 거야.\" 실리아는 정말로 작정하고 따지고 들 때면 자기에게 어떤 지적인 힘이 있음을 의식했다.\n\n\"넌 그걸 차고 싶은 거니?\" 도러시아가 외쳤다. 놀라운 발견을 한 듯한 기색이 그녀의 온몸에 생기를 불어넣었으니, 그 극적인 몸짓은 바로 그 장신구를 차고 다녔던 푸앵송 부인에게서 배운 것이었다. \"그렇다면야 당연히 꺼내 봐야지. 왜 진작 말하지 않았어? 그런데 열쇠, 열쇠가!\" 그녀는 두 손을 머리 양옆에 갖다 대며 자기 기억력에 절망하는 듯한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"여기 있어.\" 실리아가 말했다. 이 설명만큼은 그녀가 오랫동안 곱씹으며 미리 준비해 둔 것이었다.\n\n\"장식장의 큰 서랍을 열고 보석 상자를 꺼내 줘.\"\n\n이내 작은 상자가 그들 앞에 열렸고, 갖가지 보석이 펼쳐져 식탁 위에 화사한 화단을 이루었다. 그리 많은 수집품은 아니었지만, 장신구 가운데 몇몇은 정말로 빼어나게 아름다웠으니, 처음에 한눈에 드러나는 가장 훌륭한 것은 정교한 금세공에 박힌 자줏빛 자수정 목걸이와, 다섯 개의 작은 다이아몬드가 박힌 진주 십자가였다. 도러시아는 곧바로 목걸이를 집어 들어 동생의 목에 걸어 주었는데, 거의 팔찌만큼이나 꼭 맞게 둘러졌다. 그 둥근 모양은 실리아의 머리와 목이 지닌 헨리에타 마리아풍 분위기에 잘 어울렸으니, 맞은편 큰 거울로 그녀 스스로도 그것이 어울린다는 것을 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"자, 실리아! 이건 네 인도 모슬린 옷에 차면 되겠다. 그런데 이 십자가는 너의 짙은 색 드레스에 차야 해.\"\n\n실리아는 기쁨에 겨워 새어 나오려는 미소를 애써 참았다. \"어머, 도도 언니, 십자가는 언니가 가져야 해.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야, 얘, 안 돼.\" 도러시아가 무심하게 사양하며 손을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"아니, 정말 언니가 가져야 해. 언니한테 어울릴 거야—이제 언니의 검은 드레스에 말이야.\" 실리아가 고집스레 말했다. \"언니라면 그건 차도 되잖아.\"\n\n\"절대로, 절대로 안 돼. 십자가야말로 내가 노리개로 차고 다닐 마지막 물건이야.\" 도러시아는 살짝 몸서리를 쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 언니는 내가 그걸 차면 사악하다고 여기겠네.\" 실리아가 불안한 듯 말했다.\n\n\"아니야, 얘, 그렇지 않아.\" 도러시아가 동생의 뺨을 어루만지며 말했다. \"영혼에도 저마다 어울리는 빛깔이 있는 법이야. 어떤 사람에게 어울리는 게 다른 사람에게는 어울리지 않거든.\"\n\n\"하지만 언니도 엄마를 생각해서 그걸 간직하고 싶을지 모르잖아.\"\n\n\"아니야, 난 엄마의 다른 물건들이 있어—내가 그토록 아끼는 백단향 상자며, 그밖에도 많아. 사실 이건 다 네 거야, 얘. 더 이상 이걸로 왈가왈부할 것 없어. 자, 네 것을 챙겨 가렴.\"\n\n실리아는 마음이 조금 상했다. 이 청교도적인 너그러움 속에는 우월함을 강하게 내세우는 데가 있었으니, 그것은 그다지 열성적이지 못한 동생의 금빛 살결에는 청교도적인 박해 못지않게 견디기 힘든 것이었다.\n\n\"하지만 언니가, 언니가 손위인데도 절대로 장신구를 차지 않으면, 내가 어떻게 그걸 차겠어?\"\n\n\"얘, 실리아, 너를 거들어 주려고 내가 노리개를 차야 한다니, 그건 너무 무리한 부탁이야. 만약 내가 그런 목걸이를 차고 다닌다면, 마치 발끝으로 빙글빙글 돌고 있는 것 같은 기분일 거야. 세상이 빙빙 돌아서, 어떻게 걸어야 할지도 모를걸.\"\n\n실리아는 목걸이의 잠금쇠를 풀어 벗겨 냈다. \"이건 언니 목에는 좀 조일 거야. 언니한테는 길게 늘어뜨려 드리워지는 게 더 어울려.\" 그녀가 다소 흡족한 듯 말했다. 그 목걸이가 어느 모로 보나 도러시아에게는 도무지 어울리지 않는다는 사실이, 그것을 차지하는 실리아를 한결 즐겁게 했다. 그녀가 반지 상자 몇 개를 열자 다이아몬드가 박힌 고운 에메랄드가 드러났는데, 바로 그때 구름을 벗어난 해가 식탁 위로 환한 빛줄기를 보냈다.\n\n\"이 보석들 정말 아름답구나!\" 그 빛줄기처럼 갑작스레 솟구친 새로운 감정에 휩싸여 도러시아가 말했다. \"빛깔이라는 게 마치 향기처럼 그토록 깊이 사람한테 스며드는 것이 신기해. 그래서 「요한 계시록」에서 보석을 영적인 상징으로 쓴 게 아닐까 싶어. 보석은 천국의 조각들 같아. 내 생각엔 저 에메랄드가 그 가운데 어느 것보다도 아름다운 것 같아.\"\n\n\"그리고 거기 어울리는 팔찌도 있어.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"처음엔 우리가 이걸 못 봤네.\"\n\n\"정말 사랑스러워.\" 도러시아가 반지와 팔찌를 곱게 빠진 손가락과 손목에 끼우고 차고는, 눈높이로 들어 창 쪽을 향해 비춰 보며 말했다. 그러는 내내 그녀의 생각은, 그 빛깔에 대한 자기 기쁨을 신비로운 종교적 환희 속에 녹여 넣음으로써 정당화하려 애쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"언니도 그건 마음에 드는구나.\" 실리아가 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다. 그녀는 언니가 어떤 약점을 드러낸다는 것에 놀라워하기 시작했고, 또 에메랄드가 자줏빛 자수정보다도 자기 살결에 더 잘 어울리리라는 생각도 들었다. \"그 반지와 팔찌는 언니가 가져—다른 건 다 그만두더라도. 그런데 봐, 이 마노들도 참 곱고 차분하다.\"\n\n\"그래! 이건 내가 가질게—이 반지와 팔찌 말이야.\" 도러시아가 말했다. 그러다 손을 식탁 위에 떨어뜨리며, 다른 어조로 말했다. \"하지만 얼마나 비참한 사람들이 이런 것을 찾아내고, 다듬고, 또 팔고 있을까!\" 그녀가 다시 말을 멈추자, 실리아는 언니가 일관성을 지키려면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 장신구를 단념하려는 것이라고 생각했다.\n\n\"그래, 얘, 이건 내가 가질게.\" 도러시아가 단호하게 말했다. \"하지만 나머지는 다 가져가고, 상자도 가져가렴.\"\n\n그녀는 보석을 치우지 않은 채, 여전히 그것을 바라보며 연필을 다시 집어 들었다. 그것을 자주 곁에 두고서, 이 자그마한 순수한 빛깔의 샘들로 눈을 적시는 모습을 그려 보았다.\n\n\"이걸 사람들 앞에서 차고 다닐 거야?\" 실리아가 언니가 어떻게 할지 정말로 궁금해하며 지켜보다가 물었다.\n\n도러시아가 동생을 휙 돌아보았다. 자기가 사랑하는 이들을 상상으로 꾸며 주는 그 모든 마음을 가로질러, 이따금 예리한 통찰이 번뜩였으니, 거기에는 사람을 그슬릴 듯한 데가 없지 않았다. 만약 브룩 양이 언젠가 완벽한 온순함에 이른다면, 그것은 내면의 불길이 모자라서는 결코 아닐 것이었다.\n\n\"어쩌면.\" 그녀가 다소 도도하게 말했다. \"내가 어느 수준까지 추락할지는 나도 모르겠어.\"\n\n실리아는 얼굴을 붉히며 마음이 언짢아졌다. 자기가 언니의 기분을 상하게 했음을 알아챈 것이다. 그래서 장신구라는 선물에 대해 듣기 좋은 말 한마디조차 감히 꺼내지 못한 채, 그것을 다시 상자에 넣어 들고 나갔다. 도러시아 역시 마음이 언짢았으니, 설계도를 계속 그리면서, 그 작은 폭발로 끝나 버린 장면에서 자신의 감정과 말이 과연 순수했는지를 곱씹었다.\n\n실리아의 양심은 자기가 조금도 잘못하지 않았다고 일러 주었다. 그런 질문을 한 것은 지극히 당연하고 정당한 일이었다고. 그러고는 도러시아야말로 일관성이 없다고 속으로 되뇌었다. 보석을 자기 몫만큼 다 가졌어야 하든지, 아니면 그렇게 말한 이상 아예 전부 단념했어야 했다고.\n\n\"분명히—적어도, 난 그렇게 믿어.\" 실리아는 생각했다. \"목걸이를 찬다고 해서 내 기도에 지장이 생기지는 않을 거야. 그리고 이제 우리가 사교계에 나가는 마당에, 내가 도러시아 언니의 의견에 매여야 할 이유는 모르겠어. 물론 언니 자신이야 당연히 그 의견에 매여야겠지만 말이야. 하지만 도러시아 언니는 늘 한결같은 건 아니거든.\"\n\n이렇게 실리아는 잠자코 자수에 몸을 숙이고 있었다. 언니가 부르는 소리가 들릴 때까지.\n\n\"이리 와, 키티, 와서 내 설계도 좀 봐 줘. 계단이랑 벽난로가 서로 안 맞게 안 그려졌으면, 난 내가 대단한 건축가라고 여기게 될 거야.\"\n\n실리아가 종이 위로 몸을 숙이자, 도러시아는 동생의 팔에 다정하게 뺨을 갖다 댔다. 실리아는 그 몸짓을 이해했다. 도러시아는 자기가 잘못했음을 알았고, 실리아는 언니를 용서했다. 기억하는 한 언제나, 손위 언니를 향한 실리아의 마음가짐에는 비판과 경외가 뒤섞여 있었다. 손아래 동생은 늘 멍에를 짊어지고 있었던 셈이다. 하지만 멍에를 진 짐승치고 제 나름의 속생각이 없는 것이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n# CHAPTER II.\n\n“‘Dime; no ves aquel caballero que hacia nosotros viene sobre un caballo rucio rodado que trae puesto en la cabeza un yelmo de oro?’ ‘Lo que veo y columbro,’ respondio Sancho, ‘no es sino un hombre sobre un as no pardo como el mio, que trae sobre la cabeza una cosa que relumbra.’ ‘Pues ese es el yelmo de Mambrino,’ dijo Don Quijote.”—세르반테스.\n\n“‘저기 얼룩무늬 잿빛 준마를 타고 황금 투구를 머리에 쓴 채 우리를 향해 다가오는 기사가 보이지 않느냐?’ ‘제 눈에 보이는 것은,’ 산초가 대답했다. ‘제 것과 같은 잿빛 당나귀를 탄 한 사내가 머리에 뭔가 번쩍이는 것을 얹고 있는 것뿐인뎁쇼.’ ‘바로 그것이니라,’ 돈키호테가 대답했다. ‘그 찬란하게 빛나는 물건이 바로 맘브리노의 투구이니라.’”\n\n“험프리 데이비 경 말입니까?” 자기가 데이비의 『농경 화학』을 공부하고 있다는 제임스 체텀 경의 말을 받아, 브룩 씨가 수프를 사이에 두고 느긋하게 미소 지으며 말했다. “자, 그런데 험프리 데이비 경이라, 나도 여러 해 전에 카트라이트 댁에서 그 사람과 식사를 한 적이 있지, 그리고 워즈워스도 거기 있었어. 시인 워즈워스 말이야, 알 테지. 그런데 거기에 묘한 점이 있단 말이지. 워즈워스가 케임브리지에 다닐 때 나도 거기 있었는데, 한 번도 그를 만난 적이 없었어. 그런데 이십 년이 지나 카트라이트 댁에서 그와 함께 식사를 한 거지. 세상일에는 참 이상한 데가 있어, 정말로. 아무튼 데이비도 거기 있었어. 그 사람도 시인이었지. 아니, 이렇게 말할 수도 있겠군. 워즈워스가 첫째 시인이고 데이비가 둘째 시인이었다고. 그게 모든 의미에서 사실이었어, 알 테지.”\n\n도러시아는 평소보다 조금 더 불편함을 느꼈다. 식사가 시작될 무렵, 일행이 적고 방이 고요한 탓에 한 치안 판사의 정신이라는 덩어리에서 떨어져 나오는 이런 티끌들이 지나치게 두드러지게 들렸다. 그녀는 캐소본 씨 같은 사람이 어떻게 이런 사소함을 견딜 수 있을지 의아했다. 그의 태도는 무척 품위 있어 보였다. 철회색 머리카락의 매무새와 깊이 팬 눈자위는 그를 로크의 초상화와 닮아 보이게 했다. 그에게는 학자에게 어울리는 마른 체구와 창백한 안색이 있었으니, 제임스 체텀 경으로 대표되는 불그스레한 구레나룻에 혈색 좋은 영국 신사와는 더없이 대조적이었다.\n\n“제가 『농경 화학』을 읽고 있는 것은,” 이 훌륭한 준남작이 말했다. “농장 하나를 직접 맡아 경영하면서, 소작인들 사이에 좋은 영농의 본보기를 세우는 데 무언가 할 수 있는 일이 없을지 알아보려는 겁니다. 그 일에 찬성하십니까, 브룩 양?”\n\n“그건 큰 실수일세, 체텀.” 브룩 씨가 끼어들었다. “땅에 전기를 통하게 한다느니 그런 일에 손을 대고, 외양간을 응접실로 만드는 짓 말이야. 그래 봤자 소용없어. 나도 한때 과학에 꽤나 깊이 들어가 봤지만, 소용없다는 걸 알았지. 그게 온갖 일로 이어지거든. 그 무엇 하나 가만 둘 수가 없게 돼. 아니, 아닐세. 소작인들이 짚을 내다 팔지 않도록 단속하고, 그런 류의 일이나 챙기게. 그리고 배수용 기와나 대주게나, 알겠나. 하지만 그런 멋부리는 영농은 소용없어. 살 수 있는 호각 중에서도 가장 비싼 호각이지. 차라리 사냥개 떼나 기르는 게 나을 걸세.”\n\n“하지만 분명,” 도러시아가 말했다. “모두를 부양하는 땅을 사람들이 어떻게 하면 최대한 활용할 수 있을지 알아내는 데 돈을 쓰는 것이, 그저 그 위를 달리려고 개와 말을 기르는 것보다 낫지 않겠어요. 모두의 이익을 위해 실험을 하다가 가난해지는 것은 죄가 아니에요.”\n\n그녀는 그토록 어린 숙녀에게 기대되는 것보다 더 힘주어 말했지만, 제임스 경이 그녀에게 물었기 때문이었다. 그는 으레 그렇게 묻곤 했고, 그녀는 그가 형부가 되면 여러 선한 행동을 하도록 권할 수 있으리라고 종종 생각했다.\n\n캐소본 씨는 그녀가 말하는 동안 도러시아에게 눈길을 매우 뚜렷이 돌렸고, 그녀를 새삼스레 살펴보는 것 같았다.\n\n“젊은 숙녀들은 정치 경제학을 이해하지 못하지요, 알 테지요.” 브룩 씨가 캐소본 씨를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다. “우리 모두 애덤 스미스를 읽던 때가 생각나는군요. 자, 그게 바로 책이라는 거지요. 나도 한때 새로운 사상이란 사상은 다 받아들였어요. 인간 완전성이라는 것 말이오. 하지만 어떤 이들은 역사가 순환한다고 하지요. 그것도 충분히 논할 만한 이야기고, 나 자신도 그렇게 논해 봤어요. 사실은, 인간의 이성이라는 게 사람을 좀 지나치게 멀리 데려가기도 하지요. 실은 울타리를 넘어가도록 말이오. 한때는 나도 그게 나를 꽤 멀리 데려갔지만, 소용없다는 걸 알았어요. 멈춰 섰지요. 제때에 멈춰 섰어요. 하지만 너무 매몰차게는 아니고. 나는 늘 약간의 이론에는 찬성하는 쪽이었어요. 우리에게는 사상이 있어야지요. 그러지 않으면 다시 암흑시대로 떨어지고 말 테니까요. 그런데 책 이야기가 나왔으니, 사우디의 『반도 전쟁』이라는 게 있지요. 나는 그걸 아침나절에 읽고 있어요. 사우디를 아십니까?”\n\n“모릅니다.” 캐소본 씨가 브룩 씨의 거침없는 이성의 행보를 따라가지 못하고 그 책만을 생각하며 대답했다. “요즈음은 그런 문헌을 읽을 여유가 별로 없습니다. 근래에는 옛 문자에 시력을 다 써 버리고 있어서요. 사실은 저녁에 책을 읽어 줄 사람이 필요합니다만, 목소리에 까다로워서 서툰 낭독자가 읽는 것을 도무지 견딜 수가 없습니다. 어떤 의미에서는 불운한 일이지요. 저는 내면의 원천에서 지나치게 많은 양분을 얻고, 죽은 자들과 지나치게 많이 함께 살고 있으니까요. 제 정신은 어쩐지 옛사람의 유령 같아서, 세상을 떠돌며 폐허와 어지러운 변화에도 불구하고 그것이 본래 어떠했는지를 마음속으로 다시 세워 보려고 애쓰고 있습니다. 하지만 제 시력에 대해서는 더없이 조심할 필요가 있다는 걸 알게 됩니다.”\n\n캐소본 씨가 어느 정도 길게 말한 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그는 마치 공적인 진술을 하라는 요청을 받기라도 한 듯 정확하게 자신을 표현했다. 그리고 이따금 머리의 움직임이 이를 뒷받침하는, 균형 잡힌 가락처럼 단정한 그의 말투는 선량한 브룩 씨의 토막토막 끊기는 너저분함과 대조되어 한층 두드러졌다. 도러시아는 캐소본 씨가 자신이 지금껏 본 가장 흥미로운 사람이라고 속으로 생각했는데, 발도파의 역사에 관해 강연을 했던 보드의 성직자 리레 씨조차 예외가 아니었다. 과거의 세계를 다시 세우는 일, 그것도 분명 진리의 가장 드높은 목적을 위해서라니, 비록 등불을 들고 있는 사람으로서일 뿐일지라도 어떤 식으로든 그 자리에 함께하며 거들 수 있다면 얼마나 멋진 일인가! 이 고양된 생각은 정치 경제학에 무지하다고 놀림받은 데서 온 짜증 위로 그녀를 들어 올렸다. 그 끝내 설명되지 않는 학문은 그녀의 모든 빛 위로 덮어씌워진 촛불 끄개와도 같았다.\n\n“하지만 브룩 양은 승마를 좋아하시지요.” 제임스 경이 곧 기회를 잡아 말했다. “사냥의 즐거움에도 조금은 빠져 보실 줄 알았는데요. 한번 타 보시도록 밤색 말을 한 마리 보내 드리고 싶습니다. 숙녀용으로 길들인 놈입니다. 토요일에 양에게 어울리지 않는 조랑말을 타고 언덕을 가볍게 달리시는 걸 봤어요. 시간만 일러 주시면 제 마부가 코리던을 날마다 끌고 오게 하겠습니다.”\n\n“고맙습니다, 정말 친절하시군요. 하지만 저는 승마를 그만둘 생각이에요. 더는 말을 타지 않을 거예요.” 도러시아가 말했는데, 캐소본 씨에게 온 주의를 기울이고 싶은 마당에 제임스 경이 자꾸 관심을 청하는 것이 조금 짜증스러워 이런 퉁명스러운 결심으로 내몰린 것이었다.\n\n“아니, 그건 너무 가혹하군요.” 제임스 경이 강한 관심을 드러내는 책망의 어조로 말했다. “양의 언니께서는 자기 절제에 잘 빠지시는 편이지요?” 그가 오른편에 앉은 실리아 쪽을 돌아보며 말을 이었다.\n\n“그런 것 같아요.” 실리아가 언니의 마음에 들지 않을 말을 할까 봐 두려워하며, 목걸이 위로 더없이 곱게 얼굴을 붉히면서 말했다. “언니는 무언가 포기하는 걸 좋아하거든요.”\n\n“그게 사실이라면, 실리아, 내가 포기하는 건 자기 절제가 아니라 자기 탐닉이겠지. 하지만 무척 즐거운 일을 하지 않기로 택하는 데에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있을 수도 있어.” 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n브룩 씨도 같은 때에 말하고 있었지만, 캐소본 씨가 도러시아를 관찰하고 있는 것은 분명했고, 그녀도 그것을 알고 있었다.\n\n“바로 그겁니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “양은 무언가 드높고 너그러운 동기에서 포기하시는 거지요.”\n\n“아니에요, 정말이지, 꼭 그런 건 아니에요. 저는 제 자신에 대해 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.” 도러시아가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. 실리아와 달리 그녀는 좀처럼 얼굴을 붉히지 않았고, 오직 큰 기쁨이나 분노에서만 그랬다. 이 순간 그녀는 심술궂은 제임스 경에게 화가 났다. 어째서 그는 실리아에게 주의를 기울이고, 그녀는 캐소본 씨의 말에 귀 기울이도록 내버려 두지 않는 것일까? 그 박식한 사람이 브룩 씨에게 말을 듣고만 있지 말고 입을 열어 주기만 한다면 좋으련만. 그때 브룩 씨는 종교 개혁이란 무언가를 의미하든가 아니든가 둘 중 하나이며, 자기 자신은 골수까지 신교도이지만 가톨릭교도 하나의 사실이라고, 그리고 가톨릭교도의 예배당을 위해 제 땅 한 에이커를 내주기를 거절하는 문제로 말하자면, 모든 사람에게는 종교라는 고삐가 필요한데 그것은 정확히 말하자면 내세에 대한 두려움이라고 그에게 일러 주고 있었다.\n\n“나도 한때 신학을 크게 연구한 적이 있어요.” 방금 드러낸 통찰을 설명이라도 하려는 듯 브룩 씨가 말했다. “모든 학파에 대해 조금씩은 알지요. 윌버포스가 한창때일 적에 그를 알았어요. 윌버포스를 아십니까?”\n\n캐소본 씨가 “모릅니다.”라고 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 윌버포스는 어쩌면 사상가라 하기엔 좀 부족했을지도 모르지요. 하지만 내가 청을 받은 대로 의회에 들어간다면, 윌버포스가 그랬듯이 무소속 의석에 앉아 박애 사업에 힘쓸 겁니다.”\n\n캐소본 씨가 고개를 숙이며, 그것은 광대한 분야라고 말했다.\n\n“그렇지요.” 브룩 씨가 느긋하게 미소 지으며 말했다. “하지만 나에게는 자료가 있어요. 오래전부터 자료를 모으기 시작했지요. 정리는 좀 해야 하지만, 어떤 문제가 떠오를 때마다 누군가에게 편지를 써서 답을 받아 두었거든요. 내 뒤에는 자료가 있어요. 그런데 선생은 자료를 어떻게 정리하십니까?”\n\n“부분적으로는 서류함에 넣어 둡니다.” 캐소본 씨가 다소 놀란 듯 애쓰는 기색으로 말했다.\n\n“아, 서류함은 안 돼요. 나도 서류함을 써 봤지만, 서류함에서는 모든 게 뒤섞여 버려요. 어떤 종이가 에이 칸에 있는지 제트 칸에 있는지 도무지 알 수가 없거든요.”\n\n“삼촌, 제가 서류를 정리해 드리면 좋겠어요.” 도러시아가 말했다. “전부 글자를 매기고, 그런 다음 각 글자 아래에 주제 목록을 만들겠어요.”\n\n캐소본 씨가 엄숙하게 미소 지으며 찬성을 표하고는, 브룩 씨에게 말했다. “보십시오, 가까이에 훌륭한 비서를 두고 계시는군요.”\n\n“아니요, 아닙니다.” 브룩 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “젊은 숙녀들이 내 자료에 손대게 둘 수는 없어요. 젊은 숙녀들은 너무 변덕스럽거든요.”\n\n도러시아는 마음이 상했다. 캐소본 씨는 삼촌이 이런 의견을 내놓은 데에 무슨 특별한 이유가 있으리라 여길 테지만, 사실 그 말은 그의 마음속에서 그곳에 흩어진 다른 온갖 조각들 사이에 끼어 있는 곤충의 부러진 날개만큼이나 가볍게 자리하고 있었고, 우연한 기류가 그것을 하필 그녀에게 내려앉게 했을 뿐이었다.\n\n두 아가씨가 객실에 단둘이 있게 되자, 실리아가 말했다.\n\n“캐소본 씨는 정말 못생겼어!”\n\n“실리아! 그분은 내가 본 사람 중에 가장 기품 있어 보이는 분이야. 로크의 초상화와 놀랄 만큼 닮았어. 똑같이 눈자위가 깊이 패었잖아.”\n\n“로크에게도 그렇게 털 난 흰 사마귀 두 개가 있었나?”\n\n“글쎄, 그랬을지도 모르지! 어떤 부류의 사람들이 그를 들여다봤다면 말이야.” 도러시아가 조금 멀어지며 말했다.\n\n“캐소본 씨는 안색이 너무 누렇잖아.”\n\n“그게 더 나아. 너는 새끼 돼지 같은 안색을 가진 남자를 좋아하나 보구나.”\n\n“도도!” 실리아가 놀라 언니를 바라보며 외쳤다. “언니가 그런 비유를 하는 건 전에는 한 번도 못 들었어.”\n\n“그럴 일이 생기지도 않았는데 내가 왜 그런 비유를 했겠니? 좋은 비유잖아. 딱 들어맞으니까.”\n\n브룩 양은 분명 자신을 잊고 있었고, 실리아도 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n“언니가 화를 내다니 의외야, 도러시아.”\n\n“네가 그러는 게 너무 마음 아파, 실리아. 너는 인간을 마치 몸치장한 동물 정도로만 바라보고, 사람의 얼굴에서 위대한 영혼을 결코 보지 못하니 말이야.”\n\n“캐소본 씨에게 위대한 영혼이 있어?” 실리아에게는 순진한 짓궂음이 없지 않았다.\n\n“그래, 나는 그렇다고 믿어.” 도러시아가 단호한 목소리로 힘주어 말했다. “내가 그분에게서 보는 모든 것이 성서 우주론에 관한 그분의 소책자와 일치해.”\n\n“그분은 말이 무척 적던데.” 실리아가 말했다.\n\n“그분이 말을 나눌 상대가 없으니까 그렇지.”\n\n실리아는 속으로 생각했다. ‘언니는 제임스 체텀 경을 아주 경멸해. 청혼해도 받아들이지 않을 것 같아.’ 실리아는 그것이 안타까운 일이라고 느꼈다. 그녀는 준남작이 누구에게 관심을 두고 있는지에 대해서는 한 번도 속은 적이 없었다. 사실 그녀는 도도가 자기처럼 사물을 보지 않는 남편이라면 어쩌면 그를 행복하게 해 주지 못할지도 모른다고 이따금 생각하기도 했다. 그리고 마음 깊은 곳에는, 언니가 가정의 안락에 비해 너무 종교적이라는 느낌이 짓눌려 있었다. 관념과 양심의 가책이란 흩뿌려진 바늘 같아서, 밟지도, 앉지도, 심지어 먹지도 못하게 사람을 겁먹게 했다.\n\n브룩 양이 찻상 앞에 앉자, 제임스 경이 그녀 곁에 와 앉았는데, 그녀가 자기에게 대답한 방식을 조금도 무례하다고 느끼지 않았기 때문이었다. 어째서 그가 그렇게 느끼겠는가? 그는 브룩 양이 자기를 좋아할 것이 분명하다고 생각했고, 사람의 태도라는 것은 자신만만하든 미심쩍어하든 선입견으로 해석되지 않을 만큼 정말로 두드러진 경우가 아니고서는 늘 그렇게 받아들여지기 마련이었다. 그녀는 그에게 더없이 매력적인 사람이었지만, 물론 그는 자신의 연정에 대해 조금은 이론을 세워 보기도 했다. 그는 더없이 훌륭한 인간의 반죽으로 빚어진 사람이었고, 자신의 재능이란 풀어 놓아 본들 이 고장의 가장 작은 시냇물조차 불사르지 못하리라는 것을 아는 보기 드문 미덕을 지니고 있었다. 그래서 그는 이런저런 일에 대해 “우리 어떻게 할까?” 하고 물을 수 있는 아내, 남편을 거들어 논거를 대 줄 수 있고 또 그럴 만한 재산상의 자격까지 갖춘 아내를 맞이할 전망이 마음에 들었다. 브룩 양에게 씌워진 지나친 신앙심으로 말하자면, 그는 그것이 무엇으로 이루어진 것인지에 대해 매우 막연한 관념밖에 없었고, 그것이 결혼과 함께 사그라지리라 생각했다. 요컨대 그는 자신이 제대로 된 상대에게 사랑에 빠졌다고 느꼈고, 적잖은 우위까지도 기꺼이 견딜 작정이었는데, 어쨌거나 남자란 마음만 먹으면 언제든 그것을 눌러 버릴 수 있는 법이었다. 제임스 경은 자기가 그 영리함을 즐거워하는 이 아름다운 아가씨의 우위를 언젠가 눌러 버리고 싶어지리라고는 전혀 생각하지 않았다. 어찌 그렇지 않겠는가? 남자의 정신이란, 그것이 얼마만큼 있든 간에, 늘 남성적이라는 이점을 지니고 있으니, 가장 작은 자작나무가 가장 드높이 솟은 야자수보다 더 높은 부류이듯이 말이다. 게다가 남자의 무지조차 더 건실한 품질의 것이었다. 제임스 경이 이런 평가를 처음 만들어 낸 것은 아닐 테지만, 자비로운 섭리는 가장 흐물거리는 인격에도 전통이라는 형태로 약간의 풀기나 빳빳함을 보태 주는 법이다.\n\n“말에 관한 그 결심을 거두어 주시기를 바랍니다, 브룩 양.” 끈질긴 숭배자가 말했다. “장담컨대 승마는 가장 건강에 좋은 운동입니다.”\n\n“알고 있어요.” 도러시아가 차갑게 말했다. “실리아에게 좋을 것 같아요. 그 애가 승마를 시작한다면 말이지요.”\n\n“하지만 양은 그토록 완벽한 여기수이신데요.”\n\n“죄송하지만, 저는 연습을 거의 한 적이 없어서 쉽게 낙마할 거예요.”\n\n“그렇다면 그건 더 많이 연습해야 할 이유가 되지요. 숙녀라면 누구나 남편과 동행할 수 있도록 완벽한 여기수가 되어야 합니다.”\n\n“보세요, 우리는 이렇게 생각이 크게 다르군요, 제임스 경. 저는 완벽한 여기수가 되어서는 안 된다고 마음을 정했으니, 결코 당신이 그리는 숙녀의 본보기에는 들어맞지 않을 거예요.” 도러시아는 곧장 앞을 바라보며 차갑고 퉁명스럽게, 꼭 잘생긴 소년 같은 태도로 말했는데, 이는 그녀의 숭배자가 보이는 안달하는 다정함과 우습게도 대조를 이루었다.\n\n“그토록 매정한 결심을 하신 이유를 알고 싶군요. 양께서 승마를 그릇된 일이라 여기실 리는 없을 테니까요.”\n\n“저로서는 그것이 그릇된 일이라고 충분히 여길 수 있지요.”\n\n“오, 어째서요?” 제임스 경이 부드럽게 항의하는 어조로 말했다.\n\n캐소본 씨가 찻잔을 손에 들고 찻상으로 다가와 듣고 있었다.\n\n“동기를 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐물어서는 안 되지요.” 그가 차분한 어조로 끼어들었다. “브룩 양은 동기란 입 밖으로 내면 약해지기 쉽다는 걸 압니다. 그 향기가 더 거친 공기와 뒤섞이는 거지요. 우리는 움트는 씨앗을 빛으로부터 멀리 두어야 합니다.”\n\n도러시아는 기쁨으로 얼굴을 붉히며 고마운 마음으로 말한 이를 올려다보았다. 여기, 더 높은 내면의 삶을 이해할 수 있고, 어떤 영적인 교감을 나눌 수 있는 사람이 있었다. 아니, 가장 넓은 지식으로 원칙을 밝혀 줄 수 있는 사람, 그 학식이 그가 믿는 무엇이든 거의 증명에 이르게 하는 사람이 있었다!\n\n도러시아의 추론은 거창해 보일지 모른다. 하지만 사실 이렇게 결론을 너그럽게 허용하는 일이 없었다면 그 어느 시대에도 삶은 이어질 수 없었을 것이니, 그것이 문명의 어려움 속에서도 결혼을 수월하게 만들어 온 것이다. 결혼 전의 교제라는 거미줄을 알약만 한 작은 크기로 꼬집어 압축해 본 사람이 누가 있던가?\n\n“물론입니다.” 선량한 제임스 경이 말했다. “브룩 양께서 침묵하고 싶어 하시는 이유를 굳이 말하시도록 재촉하지는 않겠습니다. 양의 이유라면 분명 양께 명예가 될 만한 것이겠지요.”\n\n그는 도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 올려다본 그 관심에 대해 조금도 질투하지 않았다. 자기가 청혼을 생각하고 있는 아가씨가 쉰을 바라보는 메마른 책벌레에게 마음을 둘 수도 있으리라고는, 물론 어느 정도 명망 있는 성직자에게 그러듯 종교적인 의미에서가 아니라면, 그로서는 도무지 떠오르지 않는 일이었다.\n\n그렇지만 브룩 양이 캐소본 씨와 보드의 성직자에 관한 대화에 빠져들자, 제임스 경은 실리아에게로 가서 그녀의 언니에 관해 이야기를 나누었다. 그는 시내의 집 이야기를 꺼내며, 브룩 양이 런던을 싫어하는지 물었다. 언니에게서 떨어져 나오자 실리아는 아주 편안하게 이야기했고, 제임스 경은 속으로, 둘째 브룩 양은 비록 어떤 이들이 떠벌리듯 언니보다 더 영리하고 분별 있는 것은 아닐지라도 분명 예쁘기도 하거니와 무척 상냥하다고 생각했다. 그는 자기가 모든 면에서 더 뛰어난 쪽을 택했다고 느꼈고, 남자란 본디 최고의 것을 누릴 일을 내다보기를 좋아하는 법이었다. 그것을 기대하지 않는 척하는 자가 있다면 그야말로 독신자들 가운데 위선의 화신일 터였다.\n\n# CHAPTER III.\n\n“말하라, 여신이여, 그 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를, 라파엘이,\n그 상냥한 대천사가……\n\n이브는\n그 이야기를 주의 깊게 들으며, 가득 차올랐으니\n\n감탄으로, 그리고 깊은 명상으로, 그토록 드높고\n기이한 것들에 대해 들으면서.”\n\n—『실낙원』, 제7편.\n캐소본 씨가 정말로 브룩 양을 자신에게 어울리는 아내로 떠올려 본 적이 있었다면, 그녀로 하여금 그를 받아들이게 할 만한 이유들은 이미 그녀의 마음속에 심어져 있었고, 이튿날 저녁 무렵에는 그 이유들이 싹을 틔우고 꽃을 피웠다. 캐소본 씨의 사마귀와 누르께한 안색을 좋아하지 않던 실리아가 부목사의 신발도 변변찮으나 명랑한 아이들과 놀려고 사제관으로 도망친 사이, 아침 내내 두 사람은 긴 대화를 나누었기 때문이다.\n\n이 무렵 도러시아는 캐소본 씨의 마음이라는, 가늠할 수 없는 저수지를 깊이 들여다보았고, 거기에 자신이 지닌 온갖 자질이 어렴풋이 미궁처럼 확장되어 비치는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 자기 경험의 많은 부분을 그에게 털어놓았고, 그로부터 그의 위대한 저작의 범위를, 역시 매력적이게도 미궁처럼 펼쳐진 그 규모를 이해하게 되었다. 그는 밀턴의 “상냥한 대천사”만큼이나 가르침을 주는 사람이었으니, 다소 대천사 같은 태도로 그는 그녀에게 자신이 무엇을 보여 주려 하는지를 들려주었다. 세상의 모든 신화 체계나 산만한 신화의 단편들이 본래 계시된 하나의 전통이 변질된 것임을 증명하는 일이었다(이는 사실 이전에도 시도된 바 있었으나, 캐소본 씨가 지향하는 그러한 철저함이나 비교의 공정함, 배열의 효율성을 갖추지는 못했다). 일단 참된 입지를 터득하고 거기에 확고히 발을 디디면, 신화적 구조물의 광대한 들판이 이해 가능해지고, 아니, 상응 관계가 비추는 빛으로 환히 밝아진다는 것이었다. 그러나 이 위대한 진리의 수확을 거두어들이는 일은 결코 가볍거나 빠른 작업이 아니었다. 그의 노트는 이미 어마어마한 분량의 여러 권을 이루고 있었지만, 그 절정의 과업은 이 방대하고도 여전히 쌓여 가는 결과물을 응축하여, 히포크라테스 저작들의 초기 수확처럼, 작은 선반 하나에 들어맞게 만드는 일이었다. 이것을 도러시아에게 설명하면서 캐소본 씨는 마치 동료 학자에게 말하듯 자신을 표현했으니, 그에게는 두 가지 말투가 따로 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그가 그리스어나 라틴어 구절을 쓸 때면 언제나 세심한 주의를 기울여 영어로도 옮겨 주었던 것은 사실이지만, 그것은 아마 어떤 경우에도 그렇게 했을 터였다. 학식 있는 지방 성직자란 자기 지인들을 “라틴어를 거의 알지 못하는 영주들, 기사들, 그 밖의 고귀하고 훌륭한 사람들”로 여기는 데 익숙한 법이다.\n\n도러시아는 이 구상의 폭넓은 포용에 완전히 사로잡혔다. 여기에 숙녀들의 학교 문학이라는 얕은 물웅덩이를 넘어서는 무언가가 있었다. 여기에 살아 있는 보쉬에가 있었으니, 그의 저작은 완전한 지식과 헌신적인 경건을 화해시킬 것이었다. 여기에 현대의 아우구스티누스가 있었으니, 그는 학자와 성인의 영광을 하나로 결합한 사람이었다.\n\n그 거룩함은 학식 못지않게 뚜렷이 드러나 보였다. 도러시아가 티프턴에서 이전에 만난 어느 누구에게도 말할 수 없었던 어떤 주제들에 대해, 특히 멀리 떨어진 시대들의 가장 훌륭한 기독교 저서들에 표현되어 있는 듯한 그 영적인 신앙, 곧 신성한 완전함과의 교감 속에서 자아를 침잠시키는 일에 비하면 교회의 형식이나 신앙 조항들이 부차적 중요성밖에 지니지 못한다는 생각을 털어놓고 싶은 충동을 느꼈을 때, 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게서 자신을 단번에 이해해 주는 청자를, 현명한 순응으로 적절히 다듬어진다면 그 견해에 자신도 동의한다고 확언해 줄 수 있고, 그녀가 미처 알지 못했던 역사적 사례들을 들어 줄 수 있는 청자를 발견했다.\n“그분은 나와 똑같이 생각하셔.” 도러시아는 혼잣말을 했다. “아니, 오히려 그분은 하나의 온전한 세계를 생각하시고, 내 생각은 그 세계를 비추는 한낱 보잘것없는 싸구려 거울에 지나지 않아. 그분의 감정도, 그분의 온 경험도—내 작은 물웅덩이에 비하면 얼마나 큰 호수인지!”\n\n브룩 양은 또래의 다른 젊은 숙녀들 못지않게 거침없이 말과 성향으로부터 추론을 끌어냈다. 징표란 작고 측정할 수 있는 것이지만, 해석이란 한계가 없는 법이며, 다정하고 열정적인 천성을 지닌 소녀들에게는 모든 징표가 하늘만큼 광대한 경이와 희망과 믿음을 불러일으키기 쉽고, 그것은 지식이라는 형태로 흩뿌려진 골무 하나만큼의 재료로 채색되곤 한다. 그들이 늘 너무 심하게 속는 것은 아니다. 신드바드 자신도 운 좋게 어쩌다 참된 묘사에 도달했을 수 있고, 그릇된 추론이 때로는 가련한 인간들을 옳은 결론으로 데려다주기도 하니, 참된 지점에서 한참 벗어난 곳에서 출발하여 고리를 그리고 갈지자로 나아가다가도 우리는 이따금 마땅히 있어야 할 바로 그곳에 다다른다. 브룩 양이 성급하게 신뢰했다고 해서, 캐소본 씨가 그 신뢰를 받을 자격이 없다는 것이 분명해지는 것은 아니다.\n\n그는 의도했던 것보다 조금 더 오래 머물렀으니, 기계 파괴와 건초더미 방화에 관한 자신의 문서밖에는 아무 미끼도 내놓지 않은 브룩 씨의 가벼운 권유 때문이었다. 캐소본 씨는 그것들을 무더기로 살펴보라며 서재로 불려 들어갔고, 그동안 주인은 이것저것 집어 들며 건너뛰는 듯 종잡을 수 없는 방식으로 소리 내어 읽다가, “그래, 자, 그런데 여기 보게나!” 하면서 끝나지 않은 한 구절에서 다른 구절로 옮겨 다니더니, 마침내 그것들을 모조리 옆으로 밀쳐 두고 자신의 젊은 시절 유럽 대륙 여행 일기를 펼쳤다.\n\n“여기 보게나—여기 그리스 이야기가 전부 있다네. 람누스, 람누스의 폐허—자네야 그리스에 정통한 사람이지. 자네가 지형학을 많이 공부했는지는 모르겠네만. 나는 이런 것들을 알아내느라 한없이 시간을 보냈지—이것 보게, 헬리콘 말이야. 자, 여기!—‘우리는 이튿날 아침 파르나소스로 떠났다, 두 봉우리의 파르나소스로.’ 이 책은 죄다 그리스 이야기일세, 알겠나.” 브룩 씨는 책을 앞으로 내밀며 엄지손가락을 책장 가장자리를 따라 가로로 문지르면서 말을 맺었다.\n\n캐소본 씨는 위엄 있되 다소 서글픈 청중 노릇을 했으니, 적절한 대목에서 고개를 숙였고, 무시나 조바심을 드러내지 않으면서 되도록 어떤 문서에도 눈길을 주지 않으려 했다. 이 두서없음이 이 나라의 제도와 결부되어 있다는 것, 그리고 자신을 이 혹독한 정신적 질주로 끌고 다니는 이 사람이 상냥한 주인일 뿐만 아니라 지주이자 기록 보관관이라는 것을 그는 유념하고 있었다. 브룩 씨가 도러시아의 숙부라는 생각이 그의 인내를 거들기도 했을까?\n분명 그는 점점 더 그녀에게 자신과 이야기하도록 만들고, 그녀의 속내를 이끌어 내는 일에 열중하는 것 같았다고, 실리아는 속으로 생각했다. 그리고 그녀를 바라볼 때면 그의 얼굴은 창백한 겨울 햇살 같은 미소로 환히 밝아지곤 했다. 이튿날 아침 떠나기 전, 브룩 양과 함께 자갈 깔린 테라스를 따라 즐거운 산책을 하면서, 그는 고독의 불리함을, 젊음의 존재가 원숙한 시절의 진지한 노고를 가볍게 하거나 변화시켜 줄 그 명랑한 동반자에 대한 필요를 느낀다고 그녀에게 언급했다. 그리고 그는 마치 그 말이 어떤 결과로 이어질 외교 사절이라도 되는 듯, 그토록 신중한 정확성을 기울여 이 진술을 전했다. 사실 캐소본 씨는 실무적이거나 개인적인 종류의 의사를 되풀이하거나 고쳐 말해야 하리라고는 예상하는 데 익숙하지 않았다. 10월 2일에 신중하게 밝힌 의향은 그 날짜를 언급하는 것만으로 충분히 가리킬 수 있다고 그는 여겼을 터였으니, 그가 판단의 기준으로 삼은 자신의 기억력이란 반복 대신 “위 참조”라는 한마디로 족한 책 같은 것이었지 잊힌 글씨에 대해서나 알려 주는, 오래 써서 닳은 흔한 압지첩 같은 것은 아니었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 경우 캐소본 씨의 확신은 어긋날 것 같지 않았으니, 도러시아는 그가 한 말을, 경험의 온갖 변화가 하나의 획기적 사건이 되는 풋풋하고 젊은 천성다운 열렬한 관심으로 듣고 마음에 새겼기 때문이다.\n\n캐소본 씨가 티프턴에서 겨우 5마일 떨어진 로윅의 사제관으로 마차를 몰고 떠난 것은 산들바람 부는 아름다운 가을날 오후 세 시였다. 보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두른 도러시아는, 산책할 때 늘 젊은 숙녀들을 보살펴 주는 세인트버나드종 대형견 멍크 말고는 눈에 띄는 다른 동반자 없이 가장자리 숲을 거닐고 싶어서, 관목 숲을 따라 서둘러 걸어 정원을 가로질렀다. 그녀 앞에는 떨리는 희망으로 기대하는, 자신의 가능한 미래에 대한 소녀의 환영이 떠올라 있었고, 그녀는 그 환영의 미래 속을 방해받지 않고 거닐고 싶었다. 그녀는 상쾌한 공기 속을 활기차게 걸었고, 뺨에는 홍조가 떠올랐으며, 밀짚 보닛은(우리 시대 사람들이라면 한물간 바구니 모양이라도 보듯 추측 어린 호기심으로 바라볼 만한 것이었다) 조금 뒤로 젖혀졌다. 그녀가 갈색 머리카락을 납작하게 땋아 뒤로 둘둘 감아 올려 머리 윤곽을 대담하게 드러냈다는 점을 빼놓는다면, 그녀의 성격을 충분히 그려 냈다고 하기 어려울 것이다. 당시의 대중적 감정은 곱슬곱슬하게 지진 고수머리와 리본의 높다란 바리케이드로 타고난 빈약함을 감추기를 요구했으니, 피지섬 사람들 말고는 어떤 위대한 종족도 그것을 능가한 적이 없었다. 이것은 브룩 양의 금욕주의의 한 특징이었다. 그러나 멀리 늘어선 보리수 사이로 그 그림자들이 서로 맞닿은, 길게 드리운 빛줄기를 거느린 오후의 엄숙한 영광을, 의식적으로 바라보는 것이 아니라 자기 기분의 강렬함 속으로 빨아들이며 앞을 응시하는 그녀의 밝고 충만한 눈에는 금욕주의자다운 표정이라곤 없었다.\n\n만약 사람들이 그녀의 눈과 뺨에 어린 그 광채를, 갓 눈뜬 평범한 풋사랑의 이미지로 돌려서 보았다면, 늙은이든 젊은이든 모든 사람이(다시 말해, 그 개혁 이전 시대의 모든 사람이) 그녀를 흥미로운 대상으로 여겼을 것이다. 스트레폰을 향한 클로에의 환상은, 자발적인 모든 신뢰가 지닌 애처로운 사랑스러움이 마땅히 그래야 하듯, 시 속에서 충분히 신성하게 여겨져 왔다. 어린 펌프킨을 흠모하며 지치지 않는 동반의 끝없는 전망을 따라 꿈꾸는 피핀 양이란, 우리 아버지 어머니들을 결코 싫증나게 하지 않았고 온갖 의상을 갈아입혀 온 작은 드라마였다. 짧은 허리의 연미복이라는 불리함을 견뎌 낼 만한 풍채를 펌프킨이 지니기만 하면, 어떤 사랑스러운 소녀가 그의 미덕과 비범한 능력, 무엇보다 그의 완벽한 진실함을 단번에 확신하는 것이야말로 자연스러울 뿐 아니라 여성성의 완성을 위해 필수적이라고 누구나 여겼다. 그러나 그 시절에 살던 사람들 중에는—적어도 티프턴 근방에는 분명히 아무도—결혼에 대한 관념이 전적으로 삶의 목적에 대한 고양된 열정에서 빛깔을 끌어낸 소녀의 꿈을, 주로 자기 자신의 불꽃으로 타오르며 혼수의 자질구레함도, 식기 무늬도, 심지어 활짝 핀 주부가 누리는 영예와 달콤한 기쁨도 포함하지 않는 그런 열정을 지닌 소녀의 꿈을 공감 어린 마음으로 이해해 줄 사람이 아마 아무도 없었을 것이다.\n\n캐소본 씨가 그녀를 자신의 아내로 삼고 싶어 할지도 모른다는 생각이 이제 도러시아의 마음속에 들어와 있었고, 그가 그렇게 하리라는 생각은 일종의 경건한 감사의 마음으로 그녀를 어루만졌다. 얼마나 고마운 일인가—아니, 그것은 마치 날개 달린 사자가 갑자기 그녀가 걷는 길 옆에 나타나 그녀를 향해 손을 내미는 것이나 다름없으리라! 오랫동안 그녀는 자신의 삶을 크게 보람 있게 만들고 싶은 모든 열망을 짙은 여름 안개처럼 뒤덮은 채 마음속에 걸려 있던 그 막연함에 짓눌려 있었다. 그녀가 무엇을 할 수 있을까, 무엇을 해야 할까?—이제 막 피어나는 여인이나 다름없으면서도, 그래도 활동적인 양심과 큰 정신적 갈망을 지녀, 산만한 생쥐가 갉작거리고 판단을 내리는 정도에 견줄 만한 소녀다운 교육으로는 만족할 수 없는 그녀가. 어리석음과 자만심을 얼마간 타고났더라면, 그녀는 재산 있는 기독교도 젊은 숙녀란 마을 자선 사업과 신분 낮은 성직자들에 대한 후원, 옛 율법 시대 사라의 사사로운 경험과 새 시대 도르가의 그것을 펼쳐 보이는 『성서 속 여성들』의 정독, 그리고 자기 방 안에서 자수를 놓으며 영혼을 돌보는 일에서 삶의 이상을 찾아야 한다고 생각했을지도 모른다—그 배경에는, 자신만큼 엄격하지는 못하더라도 종교적으로 설명할 수 없는 일들에 얽혀 있는 까닭에 그를 위해 기도하고 때맞춰 권면할 수 있는 남자와의 장래 결혼이 깔려 있을 터였다. 가련한 도러시아는 그러한 만족으로부터 차단되어 있었다. 그녀의 종교적 기질의 강렬함, 그것이 그녀의 삶에 행사하는 강제력이란, 전적으로 열정적이고 이론적이며 지적으로 일관된 천성의 한 단면일 뿐이었다. 그리고 그러한 천성이 편협한 가르침의 끈에 묶여 몸부림치고, 자질구레한 행로들의 미궁, 아무 데로도 이어지지 않는 작은 길들이 벽으로 둘러싸인 미로에 지나지 않는 듯한 사교 생활에 갇혀 있을 때, 그 결과는 다른 사람들에게 과장인 동시에 모순으로 비치기 마련이었다. 그녀에게 가장 좋아 보이는 것을, 그녀는 가장 완전한 지식으로 정당화하고 싶었으며, 결코 실천되지 않는 규칙들을 인정하는 척하며 살고 싶지 않았다. 그녀의 젊은 열정은 아직 모두 이 영혼의 굶주림 속에 쏟아부어지고 있었다. 그녀를 끌어당기는 그 결합은 자기 자신의 무지에 대한 소녀다운 예속에서 그녀를 해방시켜 주고, 가장 웅대한 길로 그녀를 데려가 줄 안내자에게 자발적으로 복종하는 자유를 그녀에게 줄 그런 것이었다.\n\n“그러면 나는 모든 것을 배우게 되겠지.” 그녀는 숲속의 승마로를 따라 여전히 빠르게 걸으며 혼잣말을 했다. “그분의 위대한 저작을 더 잘 도울 수 있도록 공부하는 것이 내 의무가 될 거야. 우리 삶에는 하찮은 것이라곤 없겠지. 우리에게는 일상적인 일들이 가장 위대한 일들을 뜻하게 될 거야. 파스칼과 결혼하는 것이나 마찬가지일 거야. 나는 위대한 사람들이 진리를 본 것과 똑같은 빛으로 진리를 보는 법을 배우게 되겠지. 그러면 나이가 들었을 때 무엇을 해야 할지 알게 될 거야. 어떻게 하면 여기서—지금—영국에서 웅대한 삶을 이끌어 갈 수 있는지 알게 될 거야. 지금은 내가 어떤 식으로든 선을 행할 수 있을지 확신이 서지 않아. 모든 것이 내가 모르는 언어를 쓰는 사람들에게로 선교를 떠나는 것만 같아.—좋은 오두막을 짓는 일이라면 모를까—그것만은 의심의 여지가 없지. 아, 로윅 사람들이 제대로 된 집에 살게 해 줄 수 있다면 좋겠어! 시간이 있을 때 설계도를 잔뜩 그려 둬야지.”\n\n도러시아는 불확실한 일들을 헤아리는 자신의 주제넘은 태도를 자책하며 문득 스스로를 멈춰 세웠으나, 길모퉁이를 돌아 천천히 말을 몰고 나타난 기수의 모습 덕분에 생각의 방향을 바꾸려는 내적 노력은 덜게 되었다. 잘 손질된 밤색 말과 두 마리의 아름다운 세터를 보니 그 기수가 제임스 체텀 경이라는 데 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그는 도러시아를 알아보고 즉시 말에서 뛰어내렸고, 말을 마부에게 넘긴 뒤 팔에 무언가 하얀 것을 안고 그녀에게로 다가왔는데, 그것을 향해 두 마리 세터가 흥분한 듯 짖어 대고 있었다.\n\n“브룩 양, 이렇게 만나다니 정말 반갑습니다.” 그는 모자를 들어 올리며 매끄럽게 물결치는 금발을 드러내고 말했다. “고대하던 즐거움을 앞당겨 주셨군요.”\n\n브룩 양은 방해받은 것이 짜증스러웠다. 정말로 실리아에게 어울리는 남편감인 이 상냥한 준남작은 손위 언니에게 자신을 호감 가게 보일 필요성을 지나치게 부풀리고 있었다. 장래의 제부라 할지라도, 늘 당신과 너무 좋은 양해가 이루어진 것을 전제하고, 당신이 반박할 때조차 당신에게 동의한다면, 하나의 압박이 될 수 있는 법이다. 그가 자기 자신에게 구애하는 실수를 저질렀다는 생각은 도무지 형체를 갖추지 못했으니, 그녀의 정신적 활동은 온통 다른 종류의 설득에 쓰이고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 순간 그는 확실히 거슬렸고, 보조개 진 그의 두 손은 영 못마땅했다. 돋워진 성미 탓에 그녀는 다소 거만하게 그의 인사에 답하면서 얼굴을 짙게 붉혔다.\n\n제임스 경은 그 짙어진 홍조를 자신에게 가장 흡족한 방식으로 해석했고, 브룩 양이 그토록 아름다워 보인 적이 없었다고 생각했다.\n\n“제가 작은 청원자를 하나 데려왔습니다.” 그가 말했다. “아니, 정확히는 청원을 올리기 전에 받아들여질지를 보려고 데려왔지요.” 그가 팔 밑의 하얀 물체를 내보이니, 그것은 자연이 빚어낸 가장 천진난만한 장난감 중 하나인 자그마한 몰티즈 강아지였다.\n\n“저는 그저 애완용으로 길러진 이런 짐승들을 보면 마음이 아파요.” 도러시아가 말했는데, 그녀의 의견은 바로 그 순간 (의견이란 으레 그러하듯) 짜증의 열기 속에서 형성되고 있었다.\n\n“아니, 어째서요?” 두 사람이 앞으로 걸어가며 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n“저는 이 짐승들에게 주어지는 그 모든 귀여움이 그들을 행복하게 해 주지는 못한다고 생각해요. 너무 무력하잖아요. 그들의 생명은 너무 연약해요. 스스로 살아가는 족제비나 생쥐가 더 흥미로워요. 저는 우리 주위의 동물들이 우리 자신과 비슷한 영혼을 지니고서, 여기 멍크처럼 자기네 작은 일들을 꾸려 가거나 우리의 동반자가 될 수 있다고 생각하는 게 좋아요. 저런 짐승들은 기생적이에요.”\n\n“당신이 그것들을 좋아하지 않는다는 걸 알게 되어 정말 기쁩니다.” 마음씨 좋은 제임스 경이 말했다. “저야 결코 직접 기르지는 않겠지만, 숙녀분들은 대개 이 몰티즈 개를 좋아하시지요. 자, 존, 이 개를 좀 받아 주겠나?”\n\n코와 눈이 똑같이 검고 표정이 풍부한 그 못마땅한 강아지는 이렇게 치워졌으니, 브룩 양이 그것은 태어나지 않는 편이 나았으리라고 결론 내렸기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀는 설명할 필요를 느꼈다.\n\n“제 마음으로 실리아의 마음을 판단하시면 안 돼요. 그 애는 이런 작은 애완동물을 좋아하는 것 같아요. 한번은 아주 작은 테리어를 길렀는데, 무척 아꼈지요. 저는 그게 마음에 걸렸어요, 밟을까 봐 겁이 났거든요. 제가 좀 근시라서요.”\n\n“브룩 양, 당신은 모든 일에 자기만의 의견을 갖고 계시고, 그건 언제나 훌륭한 의견이지요.”\n\n이런 어리석은 찬사에 무슨 대꾸가 가능하겠는가?\n\n“그거 아십니까, 저는 당신의 그런 점이 부럽습니다.” 도러시아가 정해 놓은 다소 빠른 걸음으로 계속 걸으며 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n“무슨 말씀이신지 잘 모르겠어요.”\n\n“의견을 형성하는 능력 말입니다. 저는 사람에 대해서는 의견을 세울 수 있어요. 제가 누군가를 좋아하는지는 알지요. 하지만 다른 문제들에 대해서는, 그거 아십니까, 결정하기가 어려울 때가 많습니다. 양쪽에서 다 그럴듯한 말을 하는 걸 듣게 되거든요.”\n\n“그럴듯해 보이는 말이겠지요. 어쩌면 우리가 늘 분별과 무분별을 구분하지는 못하는지도 몰라요.”\n\n도러시아는 자신이 다소 무례했다고 느꼈다.\n\n“바로 그렇습니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “하지만 당신에게는 분별하는 능력이 있는 것 같군요.”\n\n“오히려 저는 결정을 내리지 못할 때가 많아요. 하지만 그건 무지에서 비롯된 거예요. 올바른 결론은 어김없이 거기 있는데, 제가 그것을 보지 못하는 거지요.”\n\n“그것을 당신보다 더 선뜻 알아볼 사람은 거의 없을 겁니다. 그거 아십니까, 어제 러브굿이 그러더군요, 당신이 오두막 설계에 관해 세상에서 가장 좋은 발상을 갖고 있다고요—젊은 숙녀로서는 참으로 놀라운 일이라고 생각하더군요. 그의 표현을 빌리자면, 당신에게 진짜 ‘제누스’가 있다고요. 당신이 브룩 씨에게 새 오두막을 한 채 지어 달라고 했는데, 숙부께서 동의하실 것 같지는 않다고 여기는 듯했습니다. 그거 아십니까, 그게 바로 제가 하고 싶은 일들 가운데 하나예요—제 말은, 제 영지에서 말입니다. 당신만 보여 주신다면, 그 설계를 실행에 옮길 수 있다면 정말 기쁘겠습니다. 물론 그건 돈을 까먹는 일이지요. 그래서 사람들이 반대하는 거고요. 일꾼들은 그게 수지맞을 만큼 지대를 낼 수 없으니까요. 하지만 어쨌든 할 만한 가치가 있는 일입니다.”\n\n“할 만한 가치라니요! 정말, 그렇고말고요.” 앞서의 사소한 짜증을 잊은 채 도러시아가 힘차게 말했다. “저는 우리가 가는 노끈 채찍으로 우리의 아름다운 집에서 쫓겨나 마땅하다고 생각해요—주위에서 보는 것 같은 그런 돼지우리에 소작인들을 살게 하는 우리 모두가요. 오두막에서의 삶이 우리에게 의무와 애정을 기대받는 인간에게 어울리는 진짜 집이라면, 우리 삶보다 더 행복할지도 몰라요.”\n\n“당신의 설계를 제게 보여 주시겠습니까?”\n\n“네, 물론이지요. 분명 결함이 많을 거예요. 하지만 저는 라우든의 책에 실린 오두막 설계를 죄다 살펴보고 가장 좋아 보이는 것들을 골라냈어요. 아, 이 근방에 그 본보기를 세울 수 있다면 얼마나 행복할까요! 저는 대문 앞의 나사로 대신에, 그 돼지우리 같은 오두막들을 정원 대문 밖에 두어야 한다고 생각해요.”\n\n이제 도러시아는 더없이 좋은 기분이었다. 처남으로서 제임스 경이 자기 영지에 본보기가 될 오두막을 짓고, 그러면 어쩌면 로윅에도 다른 오두막들이 지어지고, 그것을 본떠 다른 곳곳에 점점 더 많이 지어진다면—그것은 마치 오베를랭의 정신이 여러 교구를 지나가며 가난한 이들의 삶을 아름답게 만들어 주는 것이나 다름없으리라!\n\n제임스 경은 설계를 모두 보았고, 러브굿과 의논하기 위해 하나를 가져갔다. 또한 그는 브룩 양의 호감 속에서 자신이 크게 진전을 이루고 있다는 흡족한 느낌도 함께 가져갔다. 그 몰티즈 강아지는 실리아에게 건네지지 않았는데, 그 누락을 도러시아는 나중에 놀라움과 함께 떠올렸으나, 그것을 자기 탓으로 돌렸다. 그녀가 제임스 경을 독차지하고 있었던 것이다. 어쨌든 밟을 강아지가 없다는 것은 다행스러운 일이었다.\n\n설계를 살펴보는 동안 실리아도 그 자리에 있었고, 제임스 경의 착각을 알아차렸다. ‘저 사람은 도도가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다고 생각하지만, 도도는 오로지 자기 설계만 마음에 두고 있는걸. 그래도 만약 저 사람이 모든 걸 도도 마음대로 꾸려 나가고 도도의 온갖 생각을 실행하게 해 주리라고 도도가 여긴다면, 도도가 저 사람을 거절할지 확신이 안 서. 그렇게 되면 제임스 경은 또 얼마나 불편할까! 나는 그런 생각들을 견딜 수가 없어.’\n\n이런 반감에 젖어 드는 것은 실리아의 은밀한 사치였다. 그녀는 그것을 어떤 직접적인 진술로도 언니에게 감히 고백하지 못했으니, 그랬다가는 자신이 어떻게든 온갖 선과 맞서 싸우고 있다는 것을 입증당하는 빌미를 주는 셈이었기 때문이다. 그러나 안전한 기회가 있을 때면, 그녀는 자신의 소극적인 지혜를 도러시아에게 에둘러 일깨우는 방식으로, 사람들이 귀 기울여 듣는 것이 아니라 빤히 쳐다보고 있다는 사실을 상기시켜 언니를 그 황홀한 기분에서 끌어내리곤 했다. 실리아는 충동적이지 않았다. 할 말이 있으면 기다릴 수 있었고, 그 말은 언제나 똑같이 조용하고 또박또박 끊어지는 한결같음으로 그녀에게서 나왔다. 사람들이 활기차게 힘주어 이야기할 때면 그녀는 그저 그들의 얼굴과 표정을 살필 뿐이었다. 점잖은 사람들이 어떻게 그런 발성 연습에 필요한 우스꽝스러운 모양으로 입을 벌리고 노래 부르기를 마다하지 않는지 그녀는 도무지 이해할 수 없었다.\n\n며칠 지나지 않아 캐소본 씨는 오전 방문을 했고, 그 자리에서 다음 주에 다시 와서 만찬을 들고 하룻밤 묵으라는 초대를 받았다. 그리하여 도러시아는 그와 세 번 더 대화를 나누었고, 자신의 첫인상이 옳았다고 확신하게 되었다. 그는 그녀가 처음에 상상했던 그대로의 사람이었다. 그가 한 말은 거의 모두가 광맥에서 캐낸 표본 같았고, 과거 시대의 보물로 통하는 박물관 문에 새겨진 글귀 같았다. 그리고 그의 정신적 풍요로움에 대한 이 신뢰는, 그의 방문이 그녀를 위한 것임이 이제 분명해졌기에, 그녀의 마음에 더욱 깊이, 더욱 효과적으로 작용했다. 이 학식 높은 남자가 어린 소녀를 떠올리고는 어처구니없는 찬사가 아니라 그녀의 이해력에 호소하며, 때로는 가르침을 주는 정정을 곁들여 그녀에게 말을 거는 수고를 마다하지 않은 것이다. 얼마나 즐거운 동반인가! 캐소본 씨는 사소한 것들이 존재한다는 것조차 의식하지 못하는 듯했고, 찬장 냄새를 풍기며 내놓는 곰팡내 나는 결혼식 케이크만큼이나 달갑잖은, 둔중한 남자들이 으레 돌리는 그런 잡담을 결코 늘어놓지 않았다. 그는 자신이 관심 있는 것에 대해 이야기했고, 그렇지 않을 때는 침묵하며 서글픈 정중함으로 고개를 숙였다. 도러시아에게 이것은 사랑스러운 진실함이었고, 가식의 노력으로 영혼을 소진시키는 그 인위성에 대한 종교적 절제였다. 그녀는 그의 지성과 학식을 우러러보았던 것 못지않게 그가 자신보다 높이 선 종교적 고양도 경건하게 우러러보았기 때문이다. 그는 그녀의 경건한 감정 표현에 동의했는데, 대개 적절한 인용을 곁들였다. 그는 젊은 시절 얼마간 영적 갈등을 겪었노라고 스스로 말하기도 했다. 요컨대 도러시아는 여기서 이해와 공감과 인도를 기대할 수 있겠다고 보았다. 다만 그녀가 좋아하는 주제들 가운데 단 하나—오직 하나—에 대해서는 실망했다. 캐소본 씨는 오두막을 짓는 일에는 전혀 관심이 없는 듯했고, 마치 지나치게 높은 기준을 억누르려는 듯, 고대 이집트인들의 거처에서 누릴 수 있었던 극도로 협소한 주거 공간으로 이야기를 돌렸다. 그가 떠난 뒤 도러시아는 그의 이러한 무관심을 다소 동요하며 곱씹었고, 인간의 필요를 변화시키는 기후의 다양한 조건에서 끌어낸 논거와, 이교도 폭군들의 익히 알려진 사악함에서 끌어낸 논거로 마음을 한껏 굴렸다. 캐소본 씨가 다시 오면 그에게 이런 논거들을 내세워야 하지 않을까? 그러나 더 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그런 주제에 그의 주의를 요구하는 것은 주제넘은 일이라는 생각이 들었다. 다른 여자들이 옷차림이나 자수로 시간을 보내려 하듯, 그녀가 한가한 틈에 그 일에 몰두하는 것을 그가 못마땅해하지는 않으리라—언젠가는 금하지 않으리라—이런 억측에 빠진 자신을 알아채고 도러시아는 다소 부끄러워졌다. 그런데 그녀의 숙부가 며칠 묵으러 로윅으로 오라는 초대를 받았다. 캐소본 씨가 문서가 있든 없든 브룩 씨의 교제 그 자체를 즐거워한다고 추측하는 것이 과연 합리적일까?\n\n한편 그 작은 실망은 그녀가 바라던 개량 사업에 선뜻 착수하려는 제임스 체텀 경의 태도를 더욱 기쁘게 여기도록 만들었다. 그는 캐소본 씨보다 훨씬 더 자주 찾아왔고, 그가 그토록 진심을 다하는 모습을 보이자 도러시아는 더 이상 그를 못마땅하게 여기지 않게 되었다. 그는 이미 상당한 실무 능력을 발휘해 러브굿의 견적에 파고들었고, 더없이 고분고분했다. 그녀는 오두막 두 채를 짓고, 두 가족을 그들의 낡은 오두막에서 옮긴 다음, 그 낡은 집은 헐어 버려 옛터에 새집을 지을 수 있게 하자고 제안했다. 제임스 경은 “바로 그렇습니다”라고 말했고, 그녀는 그 말을 놀라우리만치 잘 견뎌 냈다.\n\n확실히 스스로 떠올리는 생각이 그토록 적은 이런 남자들도, 처형이나 처제를 잘 고르는 행운만 따른다면, 훌륭한 여성의 지도 아래 사회의 매우 유용한 일원이 될 수 있으리라! 그녀와 관련하여 또 다른 종류의 선택이 문제되고 있을 가능성에 그녀가 계속 눈멀어 있었던 데에 약간의 고집이 있었는지 없었는지는 말하기 어렵다. 그러나 그녀의 삶은 바로 지금 희망과 활동으로 가득 차 있었다. 그녀는 자기 설계만 생각하고 있었던 것이 아니라, 서재에서 학술서들을 꺼내 와 많은 것을 서둘러 읽고 있었으니(캐소본 씨와 이야기할 때 조금이라도 덜 무지하기 위해서였다), 그러는 내내 자신이 이 보잘것없는 행위들을 분에 넘치게 떠받들며, 무지와 어리석음에 내려지는 최후의 형벌인 그 자기만족으로 그것들을 바라보고 있는 것은 아닌지 양심의 가책 어린 물음에 시달리고 있었다.\n\n신사 1. 우리의 행위는 우리 스스로 벼려 낸 족쇄다.\n\n신사 2. 그렇소, 참으로. 하지만 그 쇠붙이를 가져오는 것은 세상이라고 나는 생각하오.\n\n\"제임스 경은 언니가 바라는 일이라면 무엇이든 해 주기로 작정한 것 같아.\" 새 건축 부지를 둘러보고 집으로 돌아오는 마차 안에서 실리아가 말했다.\n\n\"그분은 선량한 사람이고, 누가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 분별 있는 분이야.\" 도러시아가 무심코 말했다.\n\n\"그분이 어리석어 보인다는 말이지.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 도러시아는 정신을 차리고 잠시 동생의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"그저 모든 주제에 똑같이 잘 말하지는 못한다는 거지.\"\n\n\"불쾌한 사람들만 그렇게 모든 주제에 잘 말하지 않을까.\" 실리아가 늘 그렇듯 가르랑거리는 투로 말했다. \"그런 사람들과 함께 사는 건 정말 끔찍할 거야. 생각해 봐! 아침 식사 자리에서도, 언제나 그러는 거잖아.\"\n\n도러시아는 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"오, 키티, 너는 정말 놀라운 아이야!\" 그녀는 실리아의 턱을 살짝 꼬집었다. 이제 동생이 무척 사랑스럽고 매력적으로 여겨지는 기분이었으니, 훗날 영원한 천사가 되기에 알맞고, 교리상 이렇게 말하는 것이 잘못만 아니라면 다람쥐만큼이나 구원이 필요 없는 아이로 보였다. \"물론 사람이 언제나 말을 잘할 필요는 없지. 다만 말을 잘하려고 애쓸 때 그 사람 정신의 자질이 드러나는 거야.\"\n\n\"제임스 경이 애를 쓰지만 실패한다는 말이지.\"\n\n\"나는 일반적으로 말한 거야. 왜 제임스 경에 대해 나를 추궁하니? 나를 기쁘게 하는 게 그분 인생의 목적은 아니잖아.\"\n\n\"그러면, 도도, 정말로 그렇게 믿을 수 있어?\"\n\n\"물론이지. 그분은 나를 미래의 처형으로 생각해. 그게 다야.\" 도러시아는 전에 이런 말을 비친 적이 없었다. 이런 주제에 대해 자매가 서로 느끼는 어떤 수줍음 때문에, 결정적인 어떤 사건으로 그 화제가 꺼내어질 때까지 기다려 온 것이었다. 실리아는 얼굴을 붉혔지만 곧바로 말했다.\n\n\"제발 더는 그런 착각 하지 마, 도도. 며칠 전에 탠트립이 내 머리를 빗겨 주면서 그랬는데, 제임스 경의 하인이 캐드월러더 부인의 하녀한테서 들었다지 뭐야. 제임스 경이 브룩 댁의 맏딸과 결혼하려 한다고 말이야.\"\n\n\"실리아, 어떻게 탠트립이 그런 험담을 너한테 늘어놓도록 내버려 둘 수 있니?\" 도러시아가 분개하며 말했다. 기억 속에 잠들어 있던 세세한 일들이 이제 깨어나 그 달갑잖은 폭로를 뒷받침해 주었기에 그녀의 화는 한층 더 컸다. \"네가 그 애한테 이것저것 물어봤겠지. 천박한 짓이야.\"\n\n\"탠트립이 나한테 이야기하는 게 나쁠 게 하나도 없어. 사람들이 하는 말을 듣는 게 좋은 거야. 봐, 언니는 혼자 생각에 사로잡혀서 얼마나 착각을 많이 하는지. 나는 제임스 경이 언니한테 청혼하려 한다고 확신해. 그리고 그분은 언니가 받아들일 거라고 믿고 있어. 특히 언니가 그 설계도 때문에 그분을 그렇게 마음에 들어 했으니 말이야. 그리고 큰아버지도, 내가 알기로 그걸 기대하고 계셔. 제임스 경이 언니한테 홀딱 반한 건 누구나 알 수 있다고.\"\n\n도러시아의 마음속에서 그 반발이 너무도 강하고 고통스러워 눈물이 솟아 하염없이 흘러내렸다. 그녀의 소중한 계획들이 죄다 씁쓸해졌고, 제임스 경이 자신을 연인으로 여긴다고 받아들였다고 생각하는 것이 혐오스러웠다. 실리아 때문에 짜증도 났다.\n\n\"어떻게 그분이 그런 걸 기대할 수 있어?\" 그녀가 더없이 격렬한 태도로 쏟아 냈다. \"나는 그분과 그 오두막들 말고는 어떤 일에도 의견이 맞은 적이 없어. 전에는 그분한테 겨우 예의나 차렸을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 이후로 언니가 그분을 그렇게 마음에 들어 했잖아. 그분은 언니가 자기를 좋아한다고 꽤 확신하기 시작한 거야.\"\n\n\"좋아한다니, 실리아! 어떻게 그런 끔찍한 표현을 골라 쓸 수 있니?\" 도러시아가 격앙되어 말했다.\n\n\"어머나, 도러시아, 남편으로 받아들이려는 남자라면 좋아하는 게 당연히 옳지 않을까.\"\n\n\"제임스 경이 내가 자기를 좋아한다고 생각할 수 있다는 말이 나는 모욕적이야. 게다가 그건 내가 남편으로 받아들일 남자에게 가져야 할 감정에 어울리는 말도 아니야.\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 제임스 경이 안됐어. 언니한테 말해 주는 게 옳다고 생각했어. 왜냐하면 언니는 늘 그러듯이, 지금 자기가 어디에 있는지는 한 번도 살피지 않고 엉뚱한 데를 밟으면서 막무가내로 나아가니까. 언니는 늘 아무도 보지 못하는 걸 보지. 언니를 만족시키기란 불가능해. 그러면서도 정작 빤히 보이는 건 결코 보지 못하잖아. 그게 언니 방식이야, 도도.\" 무엇인가가 실리아에게 분명 평소답지 않은 용기를 주었던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 이따금 외경심마저 품던 언니에게도 거침이 없었다. 우리보다 더 폭넓게 사색하는 존재인 우리에게, 고양이 무르가 어떤 정당한 비판을 던지고 있을지 누가 알겠는가?\n\n\"정말 괴로운 일이야.\" 도러시아가 채찍질을 당하는 기분으로 말했다. \"이제 그 오두막들과는 더 이상 관계할 수 없어. 그분한테 무례하게 굴어야겠지. 그 일과는 아무것도 함께하지 않겠다고 말해야겠어. 정말 괴로운 일이야.\" 그녀의 눈에 다시 눈물이 고였다.\n\n\"조금만 기다려 봐. 생각을 좀 해 봐. 알다시피 그분은 누이를 만나러 하루 이틀쯤 떠나 계실 거야. 러브굿 말고는 아무도 없을 거고.\" 실리아도 누그러지지 않을 수 없었다. \"가엾은 도도.\" 그녀가 다정한 끊긴 말투로 이어 갔다. \"정말 안됐어. 설계도 그리는 건 언니가 제일 좋아하는 별난 취미잖아.\"\n\n\"별난 취미라고! 너는 내가 그렇게 유치한 식으로 이웃들의 집에나 신경 쓴다고 생각하는 거니? 나도 충분히 실수할 수 있지. 이렇게 옹졸한 생각만 하는 사람들 틈에서 살면서 어떻게 고결하게 기독교인다운 일을 할 수 있겠어?\"\n\n더 이상 말은 없었다. 도러시아는 너무 격앙되어 마음을 가라앉히고 자신에게 어떤 잘못이 있음을 인정하는 태도를 보이지 못했다. 그녀는 오히려 자기를 둘러싼 사회의 견디기 힘든 편협함과 반쯤 눈먼 양심을 탓하고 싶은 심정이었다. 그리고 실리아는 더 이상 영원한 천사가 아니라 그녀의 정신을 찌르는 가시, 분홍빛과 흰빛의 신앙 없는 자, 『천로역정』에 나오는 그 어떤 낙담시키는 존재보다도 더 고약한 존재였다. 설계도를 그리는 별난 취미라니! 한 사람의 행위가 빚어내는 그 모든 효과가 그토록 메마른 잡동사니로 바싹 말라 시들어 버릴 수 있다면, 인생에 무슨 가치가 있으며, 어떤 위대한 신앙이 가능하겠는가? 마차에서 내릴 때 그녀의 뺨은 창백했고 눈꺼풀은 붉었다. 슬픔의 화신 같은 모습이었으니, 현관에서 그녀를 맞이한 큰아버지도 놀랐을 테지만, 옆에 바짝 붙어 선 실리아가 어찌나 예쁘고 차분해 보였던지 그는 곧바로 도러시아의 눈물이 그 지나친 신앙심에서 비롯된 것이라고 단정해 버렸다. 그는 그들이 없는 동안 어느 범죄자의 사면을 청원하는 일로 주도(州都)에 다녀온 참이었다.\n\n\"그래, 얘들아.\" 그가 다정하게 말했다. 두 사람이 그에게 입맞춤을 하러 다가가자 그가 말을 이었다. \"내가 없는 동안 불쾌한 일은 없었기를 바란다.\"\n\n\"없었어요, 큰아버지.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"오두막을 보러 프레시트에 다녀왔어요. 큰아버지가 점심때 댁에 계실 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"점심은 로윅에 들러서 먹었지. 너희는 내가 로윅에 들른 걸 몰랐지. 그리고 너한테 줄 소책자를 두어 권 가져왔다, 도러시아. 서재에 있단다. 서재 탁자 위에 놓여 있어.\"\n\n마치 전류가 도러시아를 관통하며 절망에서 기대로 그녀를 떨리게 한 것 같았다. 그것은 초기 교회에 관한 소책자들이었다. 실리아와 탠트립과 제임스 경의 압박이 떨쳐졌고, 그녀는 곧장 서재로 걸어갔다. 실리아는 이층으로 올라갔다. 브룩 씨는 전갈 때문에 붙들렸으나, 다시 서재로 들어와 보니 도러시아가 이미 자리에 앉아 캐소본 씨의 여백 필사 메모가 적힌 소책자 한 권에 깊이 빠져 있었다. 마치 메마르고 무더우며 따분한 산책 끝에 신선한 꽃다발의 향기를 들이마시듯, 그렇게 열렬히 그것을 빨아들이고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 티프턴과 프레시트에서, 그리고 새 예루살렘으로 가는 길에 엉뚱한 데를 밟고 마는 자신의 서글픈 버릇에서 벗어나고 있었다.\n\n브룩 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 장작불을 향해 다리를 뻗었다. 불은 장작 받침쇠 사이에서 빨갛게 달아오른 주사위 같은 놀라운 덩어리로 무너져 내려 있었다. 그는 두 손을 가만히 비비며 도러시아 쪽을 무척 온화하게 바라보았으나, 딱히 할 말이 있는 것은 아니라는 듯 무심하고 한가로운 태도였다. 도러시아는 큰아버지가 있다는 것을 알아채자마자 소책자를 덮고 가려는 듯 일어섰다. 평소라면 큰아버지가 범죄자를 위해 베푼 자비로운 일에 관심을 가졌을 테지만, 조금 전의 동요로 그녀는 정신이 산만했다.\n\n\"내가 로윅을 거쳐 돌아왔단다, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 그녀를 붙잡으려는 의도라기보다는, 분명 앞서 한 말을 또 하는 그의 평소 버릇에서 말했다. 인간 언어의 이 근본 원리가 브룩 씨에게서 두드러지게 드러났다. \"거기서 점심을 먹고 캐소본의 서재며 뭐 그런 것들을 봤지. 마차를 모니 공기가 매섭더구나. 앉지 그러니, 얘야? 추워 보이는데.\"\n\n도러시아는 그 권유를 받아들이고 싶은 마음이 들었다. 큰아버지의 느긋한 처사가 짜증스럽지 않을 때면, 그것은 오히려 마음을 달래 주기도 했다. 그녀는 망토와 보닛을 벗고 그의 맞은편에 앉아 그 불기운을 즐기면서도 아름다운 두 손을 들어 올려 가림막으로 삼았다. 가늘거나 작은 손이 아니라, 힘이 있고 여성스러우며 모성적인 손이었다. 그녀는 알고 사유하려는 자신의 열정적인 갈망을 달래 보려는 듯 그 손을 들어 올린 것 같았으니, 티프턴과 프레시트의 비우호적인 환경 속에서 그 갈망은 결국 눈물과 붉은 눈꺼풀로 터져 나왔던 것이다.\n\n이제 그녀는 그 사형수가 생각났다. \"양 도둑에 관해서는 어떤 소식을 가져오셨어요, 큰아버지?\"\n\n\"뭐, 가엾은 번치 말이냐? 글쎄, 그 사람을 살려 낼 수는 없을 것 같구나. 교수형에 처해질 거야.\"\n\n도러시아의 이마에 비난과 연민의 표정이 어렸다.\n\n\"교수형이지, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 조용히 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"가엾은 로밀리! 그 사람이라면 우리를 도와주었을 텐데. 나는 로밀리를 알았지. 캐소본은 로밀리를 몰랐고. 캐소본은, 알다시피, 책 속에 조금 파묻혀 사는 사람이거든.\"\n\n\"위대한 연구를 하고 위대한 저작을 쓰는 사람이라면, 물론 세상과 많이 어울리는 일은 포기해야겠지요. 어떻게 여기저기 다니며 사람을 사귀겠어요?\"\n\n\"그건 사실이지. 하지만 사람이 그러면 우울해지거든, 알다시피. 나도 줄곧 독신으로 살아왔지만, 나는 천성이 그래서 한 번도 우울해진 적이 없어. 어디든 돌아다니며 모든 걸 받아들이는 게 내 방식이었지. 나는 우울해진 적이 없어. 하지만 캐소본은 그렇다는 걸 알 수 있어, 알다시피. 그 사람은 동반자가 필요해. 동반자 말이다, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"그분의 동반자가 된다는 건 누구에게나 큰 영광일 거예요.\" 도러시아가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"네가 그 사람을 좋아하는 게로구나, 응?\" 브룩 씨가 별다른 놀라움이나 다른 감정도 내비치지 않고 말했다. \"그래, 자, 나는 캐소본을 십 년 동안 알아 왔어. 그 사람이 로윅에 온 이후로 줄곧 말이야. 하지만 그 사람한테서 뭔가를 얻어 낸 적은 없었지. 무슨 생각거리 같은 것 말이다, 알다시피. 그래도 그 사람은 일류 인물이고, 주교가 될지도 몰라. 뭐 그런 거 있잖니, 알다시피, 필이 자리에 남아 있는다면 말이야. 그리고 그 사람은 너를 아주 높이 평가한단다, 얘야.\"\n\n도러시아는 말을 할 수가 없었다.\n\n\"실은, 그 사람이 너를 정말이지 아주 높이 평가하거든. 그리고 캐소본은 말을 유난히 잘하지. 그 사람이 나한테 미뤄 두었어. 네가 아직 성년이 아니니까 말이야. 요컨대, 나는 너한테 말을 전하겠다고 약속했단다. 가능성이 그리 크지 않을 것 같다고 그 사람한테 말하긴 했지만 말이야. 나로서는 그 말을 해 주어야 했어. 내 조카는 아주 어리다, 뭐 그런 식으로 말했지. 하지만 모든 걸 다 들춰낼 필요는 없다고 생각했어. 어쨌든 결론을 말하자면, 그 사람이 너한테 결혼 신청을 하게 해 달라고 나한테 허락을 구했단다. 결혼 말이다, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 설명하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"너한테 말해 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었지, 얘야.\"\n\n브룩 씨의 태도에서는 그 누구도 불안의 기색을 알아챌 수 없었을 테지만, 그는 정말로 조카의 마음을 좀 알고 싶었다. 만일 조언이 필요하다면 제때 해 주려는 것이었다. 그토록 많은 생각을 받아들여 온 치안 판사인 그가 품을 수 있는 감정이라야 순전히 다정한 것뿐이었다. 도러시아가 곧바로 말을 하지 않자 그가 다시 말했다. \"너한테 말해 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었어, 얘야.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 큰아버지.\" 도러시아가 또렷하고 흔들림 없는 어조로 말했다. \"저는 캐소본 씨께 정말 감사드려요. 그분이 청혼하신다면 받아들이겠어요. 저는 지금껏 본 그 누구보다도 그분을 존경하고 흠모해요.\"\n\n브룩 씨는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 낮게 끄는 어조로 말했다. \"아? …… 그래! 그 사람은 어떤 면에서는 좋은 혼처지. 하지만 말이다, 체텀도 좋은 혼처야. 게다가 우리 땅이 서로 붙어 있잖니. 나는 결코 네 뜻을 거슬러 간섭하지 않을 거다, 얘야. 결혼이란 자기 뜻대로 해야 하는 거지, 뭐 그런 거 말이다. 어느 정도까지는 말이야, 알다시피. 나는 늘 그렇게 말해 왔어, 어느 정도까지는 말이지. 나는 네가 좋은 결혼을 하기를 바라. 그리고 체텀이 너와 결혼하고 싶어 한다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 나한테는 있단다. 그냥 말해 두는 거야, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"제가 제임스 체텀 경과 결혼하는 일은 결코 있을 수 없어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"만일 그분이 저와 결혼할 생각을 한다면, 그건 큰 착각을 한 거예요.\"\n\n\"바로 그거란다, 알다시피. 사람 일이란 도무지 알 수가 없어. 나는 체텀이야말로 요즘 여자가 좋아할 만한 부류의 남자라고 생각했는데 말이지.\"\n\n\"제발 그분을 다시는 그런 식으로 언급하지 마세요, 큰아버지.\" 도러시아가 조금 전의 짜증이 되살아나는 것을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n브룩 씨는 의아해하며, 여자란 무궁무진한 연구 대상이라고 느꼈다. 이 나이의 자기조차도 여자에 대해서는 완벽하게 과학적으로 예측할 수 있는 상태가 아니었으니 말이다. 여기 체텀 같은 사내가 아무런 가망도 없는 것이다.\n\n\"그래, 그런데 이제 캐소본 말이다. 서두를 건 없어. 내 말은 너로서는 말이지. 하긴 해마다 그 사람한테는 세월이 표가 날 거야. 그 사람은 마흔다섯이 넘었거든, 알다시피. 너보다 족히 스물일곱 살은 위라고 봐야지. 하긴, 네가 학식이며 지위며 뭐 그런 걸 좋아한다면, 모든 걸 다 가질 수는 없는 노릇이지. 그리고 그 사람 수입도 괜찮아. 교회 직책과는 별개로 상당한 재산을 가지고 있거든. 수입이 좋아. 그래도 그 사람이 젊지는 않으니, 얘야, 너한테 숨길 수는 없구나. 내 생각에 그 사람 건강이 그리 튼튼한 편은 아닌 것 같아. 그 사람한테 흠잡을 거라곤 그것밖에 모르겠다.\"\n\n\"저는 제 또래에 아주 가까운 남편을 원하지는 않아요.\" 도러시아가 진지하게 단언하듯 말했다. \"저는 판단력과 모든 지식에서 저보다 뛰어난 남편을 갖고 싶어요.\"\n\n브룩 씨가 다시 한번 나직이 되뇌었다. \"아? 나는 네가 여느 여자애들보다 자기 의견이 더 뚜렷하다고 생각했는데. 네가 자기 의견을 좋아한다고 생각했지. 좋아한다고 말이다, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"저는 어떤 의견도 없이 사는 제 모습을 상상할 수가 없어요. 하지만 저는 그 의견에 대한 타당한 근거를 갖고 싶고, 현명한 사람이라면 어떤 의견이 가장 튼튼한 바탕을 지녔는지 제가 알아보도록 도와줄 수 있을 거예요. 그리고 그 의견에 따라 살아가도록 도와줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"지당한 말이야. 그보다 더 잘 표현할 수는 없겠구나. 미리야 그보다 더 잘 말할 수는 없지, 알다시피. 하지만 세상일에는 묘한 데가 있단다.\" 이번 일에서만큼은 조카를 위해 최선을 다하려는 양심이 정말로 움직인 브룩 씨가 말을 이었다. \"인생은 틀에 부어 만들어지는 게 아니야. 자와 선을 대고 재단되는 것도 아니고, 뭐 그런 거 말이다. 나 자신은 결혼하지 않았는데, 그게 너랑 네 식구들한테는 더 잘된 일이지. 실은, 나는 누구한테든 올가미에 목을 들이밀 만큼 깊이 사랑해 본 적이 없었어. 그건 올가미거든, 알다시피. 자, 성미라는 게 있잖니. 성미가 있단 말이야. 게다가 남편이란 주인 노릇을 하고 싶어 하는 법이지.\"\n\n\"저도 시련을 각오해야 한다는 건 알아요, 큰아버지. 결혼이란 더 높은 의무를 지는 상태잖아요. 저는 한 번도 그것을 단지 개인적인 안락으로 생각한 적이 없어요.\" 가엾은 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 너는 겉치레며 큰 살림이며 무도회며 만찬회며 뭐 그런 걸 좋아하지 않지. 캐소본의 생활 방식이 체텀의 방식보다 너한테 더 맞을 거라는 건 알겠다. 그러니 네 마음대로 하렴, 얘야. 나는 캐소본을 막지 않을 거야. 곧바로 그렇게 말했지. 무슨 일이든 어떻게 풀릴지는 모르는 법이니까. 너는 여느 젊은 아가씨들과 취향이 같지 않잖니. 그러니 성직자이자 학자인 데다 주교가 될지도 모르는, 뭐 그런 사람이 체텀보다 너한테 더 맞을 수도 있지. 체텀은 좋은 친구야. 마음씨 곧고 건전한 친구지, 알다시피. 하지만 생각거리 같은 데로는 그리 파고들지 않아. 나는 그 사람 나이 때 그랬는데 말이야. 그런데 캐소본의 눈은, 글쎄. 너무 많이 읽어서 눈을 좀 상한 것 같더구나.\"\n\n\"저한테 그분을 도울 여지가 많을수록 저는 그만큼 더 행복할 거예요, 큰아버지.\" 도러시아가 열렬히 말했다.\n\n\"네가 마음을 단단히 정한 게로구나, 알겠다. 그래, 얘야, 실은 내 주머니에 너한테 줄 편지가 한 통 있단다.\" 브룩 씨가 도러시아에게 편지를 건넸으나, 그녀가 일어나 나가려 하자 그가 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 서두를 건 없다, 얘야. 곰곰이 생각해 보렴, 알다시피.\"\n\n도러시아가 그를 떠난 뒤, 그는 자신이 분명 강하게 말했다고 돌이켜 보았다. 결혼의 위험들을 그녀 앞에 인상 깊은 방식으로 펼쳐 보였으니 말이다. 그렇게 하는 것이 그의 의무였다. 하지만 젊은이들을 위해 현명한 척하는 것으로 말하자면, 아무리 젊은 시절에 여행을 많이 다니고 새로운 사상을 흡수하고 지금은 고인이 된 명사들과 식사를 함께했던 큰아버지라 한들, 체텀보다 캐소본을 좋아하는 어린 처녀에게 어떤 결혼이 잘 풀릴지 어떻게 판단하는 척할 수 있겠는가. 요컨대 여자란 하나의 문제였으니, 브룩 씨의 정신이 그 앞에서 막막함을 느끼는 터라, 불규칙한 입체의 회전운동에 못지않게 복잡한 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER V.\n\n\"열심히 공부하는 학생들은 흔히 통풍, 카타르, 점액병, 악액질, 소화불량, 눈병, 결석, 산통, 미숙증, 폐색증, 현기증, 위창자증, 폐결핵, 그리고 지나치게 오래 앉아 있어 생기는 온갖 질병에 시달린다. 그들은 대개 야위고 메마르며 안색이 나쁘다…… 모두가 도를 넘은 노고와 비상한 학구열 탓이다. 이 말의 진실을 믿지 못하겠거든 위대한 토스타투스와 토마스 아퀴나스의 저작을 보라. 그러고 나서 그 사람들이 고생을 했는지 어쨌는지 내게 말해 보라.\" — 버턴, 『우울의 해부』, 제1부, 2절.\n\n이것이 캐소본 씨의 편지였다.\n\n친애하는 브룩 양에게. 저는 무엇보다도 제 마음에 가장 가까이 두고 있는 한 가지 문제에 관해 당신께 말씀드릴 수 있는 후견인의 허락을 받았습니다. 제 삶에서 어떤 필요에 대한 자각이 당신과 알고 지내게 될 가능성과 때를 같이하여 솟아났다는 사실 속에, 단순한 시기의 일치를 넘어서는 더 깊은 상응을 알아본 것이 잘못은 아니리라 믿습니다. 당신을 처음 만난 그 순간에, 저는 당신이 그 필요(말씀드리자면, 너무도 특수하여 결코 포기할 수 없는 작업의 몰두조차 끊임없이 감추어 둘 수는 없었던 그런 애정의 활동과 결부된 필요입니다)를 채워 줄, 더없이 뛰어나고 어쩌면 유일무이한 적임자라는 인상을 받았습니다. 그리고 관찰의 기회가 거듭될 때마다 그 인상은 한층 더 깊어졌으니, 제가 미리 그려 두었던 그 적합함을 더욱 분명하게 확신하게 되었고, 그리하여 방금 말씀드린 그 애정이 한결 결연하게 일깨워졌기 때문입니다. 우리의 대화는, 제 생각에, 제 삶과 목적의 방향을 당신께 충분히 분명하게 전했으리라 봅니다. 그것은 제가 알기로 평범한 부류의 정신에는 어울리지 않는 방향입니다. 하지만 저는 당신에게서 고결한 사유와 헌신의 역량을 발견했으니, 그것은 제가 여태껏 젊음의 이른 만개나, 위에서 말한 정신적 자질과 결합될 때(당신에게서 두드러지게 그러하듯) 비로소 품위를 얻는 동시에 부여하는 그 여성다운 우아함과 양립한다고는 미처 생각하지 못했던 것입니다. 저는 고백하건대 견실하면서도 매력적인 요소들이 이토록 드물게 어우러진 사람을, 곧 더 진지한 노고를 돕고 빈 시간에 매혹을 드리울 수 있는 사람을 만나리라고는 감히 바라지도 못했습니다. 그리고 당신과 인연을 맺게 된 그 사건이 아니었다면(다시 말씀드리지만, 저는 그것이 예고된 필요와 피상적으로 우연히 겹친 것이 아니라, 한 생애의 계획이 완성되어 가는 단계로서 섭리에 따라 그것과 이어져 있다고 믿습니다), 저는 아마도 결혼이라는 결합으로 제 고독을 덜어 보려는 어떤 시도조차 없이 끝까지 살아갔을 것입니다.\n친애하는 브룩 양, 이것이 제 감정에 대한 정확한 진술입니다. 그리고 이제 감히 여쭙고자 하니, 부디 너그러이 헤아려 주시기를 믿으며, 당신 자신의 감정은 제 행복한 예감을 어느 정도까지 확증해 줄 성질의 것인지 묻습니다. 당신이 저를 남편으로, 그리고 당신의 안녕을 지키는 지상의 보호자로 받아들여 주신다면, 저는 그것을 섭리가 내려 주는 가장 높은 선물로 여길 것입니다. 그 대가로 저는 적어도 여태껏 헛되이 소모하지 않은 애정과, 비록 앞으로 짧을지라도 당신이 들춰 본다 해도 쓰라림이나 수치를 정당하게 안길 만한 기록이라곤 없는 한 생애의 신실한 헌신을 바칠 수 있습니다. 저는 당신의 마음을 전해 들을 때까지 불안에 휩싸여 기다리고 있으니, 그것을 (가능하다면) 평소보다 더 고된 노고로 돌리는 것이 지혜로운 일이겠지요. 하지만 이런 종류의 경험에서 저는 아직 미숙하며, 불리한 가능성을 내다볼 때, 희망이 잠시 비춘 빛 뒤에 다시 고독에 체념하기란 한층 더 어려우리라 느끼지 않을 수 없습니다.\n\n어떤 경우에도, 저는 여전히\n진심 어린 헌신을 담아, 에드워드 캐소본 올림.\n\n도러시아는 이 편지를 읽으며 떨었다. 그러다 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 묻은 채 흐느꼈다. 기도할 수 없었다. 생각이 흐릿해지고 형상들이 정처 없이 떠도는 엄숙한 감정의 격류 속에서, 그녀는 그저 어린아이가 기대듯 자신을 지탱해 주는 신성한 의식의 품에 자신을 내맡길 수 있을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입을 시간이 될 때까지 그 자세로 머물러 있었다.\n\n그 편지를 따져 보거나, 사랑의 고백으로서 비판적으로 들여다볼 생각이 어떻게 그녀에게 떠오를 수 있었겠는가? 그녀의 온 영혼은 더 충만한 삶이 자기 앞에 열리고 있다는 사실에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그녀는 더 높은 입문의 단계로 들어서려는 초심자였다. 그녀는 자신의 무지가 안기는 어둠과 압박, 그리고 세상 관습의 사소한 강제 아래 불안스레 꿈틀거리던 그 에너지를 펼칠 공간을 갖게 될 참이었다.\n\n이제 그녀는 크면서도 분명한 의무에 자신을 바칠 수 있을 것이었다. 이제 그녀는 자신이 경외할 수 있는 한 정신의 빛 속에서 끊임없이 살아갈 수 있을 것이었다. 이 희망에는 자랑스러운 기쁨의 광채가, 곧 자신이 흠모해 마지않던 그 남자에게서 선택받았다는, 처녀다운 즐거운 놀라움이 섞이지 않은 것이 아니었다. 도러시아의 온 열정은 이상적인 삶을 향해 분투하는 한 정신을 거쳐 흘러나왔다. 변모한 그녀의 처녀 시절이 발하는 광휘가 그 시선 안에 들어온 첫 대상 위로 떨어졌다. 마음의 기울어짐이 결심으로 바뀌는 그 추진력은, 그날 그녀의 삶의 실제 처지에 대한 불만을 일깨운 그 자질구레한 사건들 때문에 한층 강해졌다.\n\n저녁 식사 후, 실리아가 \"변주가 딸린 소품\"을, 곧 젊은 숙녀들이 받는 교육의 미적인 부분을 상징하는 작은 딸랑거림을 연주하고 있을 때, 도러시아는 캐소본 씨의 편지에 답장을 쓰러 자기 방으로 올라갔다. 답장을 미룰 까닭이 어디 있겠는가? 그녀는 그것을 세 번이나 고쳐 썼는데, 표현을 바꾸고 싶어서가 아니라, 손이 유난히 떨려서였다. 그리고 캐소본 씨가 자기 필체를 보기 흉하고 알아보기 어렵다고 여길까 봐 견딜 수 없었던 것이다. 그녀는 한 글자 한 글자가 큰 추측 없이도 또렷이 구별되는 필체로 글씨 쓰는 것을 자랑으로 여겼고, 캐소본 씨의 눈을 아끼고자 이 솜씨를 한껏 활용할 작정이었다. 세 번을 그녀는 다시 썼다.\n\n친애하는 캐소본 씨께. 저를 사랑해 주시고, 당신의 아내가 될 자격이 있다고 여겨 주셔서 진심으로 감사드립니다. 저는 당신의 행복과 하나가 될 행복보다 더 나은 행복은 바랄 수 없습니다. 제가 더 말씀드린다 해도, 그것은 같은 말을 더 길게 늘어놓는 것일 뿐이니, 지금 제가 평생토록 당신의 사람이 될 수 있으리라는 생각 외에 다른 어떤 생각에도 머물 수 없기 때문입니다.\n\n헌신을 담아, 도러시아 브룩 올림.\n\n저녁 늦게 그녀는 삼촌을 따라 서재로 들어가, 이튿날 아침에 부쳐 주십사 하고 그 편지를 건넸다. 그는 놀랐지만, 그 놀라움은 잠시의 침묵으로 그쳤을 뿐이었다. 그 동안 그는 글 쓰는 탁자 위의 여러 물건을 이리저리 밀어 보다가, 마침내 안경을 코에 걸친 채 벽난로를 등지고 서서 도러시아의 편지에 적힌 수신인 주소를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이 일에 대해 충분히 생각해 봤느냐, 얘야?\" 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n\"오래 생각할 필요는 없었어요, 삼촌. 저를 망설이게 할 만한 게 아무것도 없으니까요. 제가 마음을 바꾼다면, 그건 분명 무언가 중대하고 제게 전혀 새로운 일 때문일 거예요.\"\n\n\"아!—그럼 그를 받아들였다는 게냐? 그럼 체텀에게는 가망이 없는 거고? 체텀이 너를 언짢게라도 했느냐—언짢게 말이다, 알겠지? 체텀에게서 마음에 들지 않는 게 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"그 사람에게는 제 마음에 드는 게 아무것도 없어요.\" 도러시아가 다소 격하게 말했다.\n\n브룩 씨는 누가 가벼운 물건이라도 던진 듯 머리와 어깨를 뒤로 젖혔다. 도러시아는 곧 얼마간 스스로를 나무라는 마음이 들어 말했다.\n\n\"남편으로서 말이에요. 그분은 무척 친절한 분이에요, 정말로. 오두막집 일에는 정말 잘해 주시고요. 선의를 지닌 분이죠.\"\n\n\"하지만 너는 학자라든가, 그런 부류여야 한다는 거지? 음, 그런 게 우리 집안에 좀 흐르긴 하지. 나도 그랬으니까—그 지식에 대한 사랑, 모든 걸 파고드는 것 말이다—좀 지나쳤지—나를 너무 멀리 끌고 갔어. 하긴 그런 건 여자 쪽으로는 잘 흐르지 않지. 아니면 그리스의 강처럼 땅속으로 흐르다가 말이다—아들들에게서 솟아나는 거지. 영리한 아들에 영리한 어머니. 나도 한때 그 문제를 꽤 파고들었단다. 하지만 얘야, 나는 늘 이런 일에서는 어느 정도까지는 사람이 좋을 대로 해야 한다고 말해 왔다. 후견인으로서 나쁜 혼처에 동의할 수는 없었을 게다. 하지만 캐소본은 평판이 좋아. 처지가 괜찮지. 다만 체텀이 상처를 받을까 걱정이고, 캐드월러더 부인이 나를 탓할까 걱정이로구나.\"\n\n물론 그날 저녁 실리아는 무슨 일이 있었는지 전혀 알지 못했다. 그녀는 도러시아의 멍한 태도와, 집에 돌아온 뒤로 다시 운 흔적을, 제임스 체텀 경과 건물 일로 언니가 빠져 있던 언짢은 기분 탓으로 돌렸고, 더 이상 기분을 상하게 하지 않으려 조심했다. 일단 하고 싶은 말을 하고 나면, 실리아는 불쾌한 화제로 되돌아가는 성미가 아니었다. 어릴 적부터 그녀의 천성은 누구와도 다투지 않는 것이었다. 그저 사람들이 자기와 다투며 칠면조 수컷처럼 구는 것을 의아하게 바라볼 뿐이었고, 그들이 마음을 가라앉히기만 하면 언제든 함께 실뜨기 놀이를 할 준비가 되어 있었다. 그리고 도러시아로 말하자면, 동생의 말에서 늘 무언가 잘못된 점을 찾아내는 것이 그녀의 버릇이었다. 비록 실리아는 자기가 늘 있는 그대로의 사실만 말할 뿐 다른 건 말하지 않는다고 속으로 항변했지만 말이다. 그녀는 제 머릿속에서 지어낸 말을 엮어 본 적도 없고 엮을 줄도 몰랐다. 그래도 도도의 가장 좋은 점은 오래 화를 품고 있지 않는다는 것이었다. 이제 둘은 저녁 내내 거의 말을 주고받지 않았지만, 실리아가 일감을 치우며—잠자리에 드는 일에서는 언제나 그녀가 훨씬 일렀다—자러 가려 할 때, 명상 말고는 달리 할 일이 없어 낮은 의자에 앉아 있던 도러시아가, 깊지만 잔잔한 감정의 순간이면 그녀의 말을 한 가락 아름다운 영창처럼 만드는 그 음악적인 억양으로 말했다.\n\n\"실리아, 얘, 이리 와서 입맞춰 주렴.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하며 두 팔을 벌렸다.\n\n실리아는 알맞은 높이를 맞추려 무릎을 꿇고 나비처럼 가벼운 입맞춤을 했고, 그동안 도러시아는 부드러운 팔로 동생을 감싸 안고 양 볼에 차례로 진지하게 입술을 댔다.\n\n\"늦게까지 앉아 있지 마, 도도, 오늘 밤은 너무 창백해 보여. 어서 자.\" 실리아가 애틋한 기색이라곤 없이 편안한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"아냐, 얘, 난 아주, 아주 행복해.\" 도러시아가 열렬하게 말했다.\n\n'그렇다면 더 잘됐지.' 실리아는 생각했다. '하지만 도도는 어쩌면 저리도 이상하게 한 극단에서 다른 극단으로 오가는 걸까.'\n\n이튿날 점심때, 집사가 브룩 씨에게 무언가를 건네며 말했다. \"조나스가 돌아왔습니다, 나리, 이 편지를 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n브룩 씨는 편지를 읽고 나서 도러시아 쪽으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"캐소본이다, 얘야. 저녁 식사 때 여기 온다는구나. 더 길게 쓸 겨를도 없었던 게야—겨를이 없었던 거지, 알겠지.\"\n\n저녁 손님이 언니에게 미리 알려진다는 것이 실리아에게 별스럽게 보일 리는 없었지만, 삼촌과 같은 방향으로 시선을 따라가던 그녀는 그 통고가 도러시아에게 미친 묘한 효과에 놀랐다. 마치 햇빛에 빛나는 하얀 날개의 반영 같은 것이 언니의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나가, 좀처럼 보기 드문 그녀의 홍조 가운데 하나로 끝맺는 것 같았다. 캐소본 씨와 언니 사이에, 그가 책 이야기를 좋아하고 언니가 그것을 듣기 좋아하는 것 이상의 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 실리아의 마음에 처음으로 떠올랐다. 여태껏 그녀는 이 \"못생기고\" 학식 있는 지인에 대한 흠모를, 로잔에서 만난 리레 씨, 역시 못생기고 학식 있던 그 사람에 대한 흠모와 같은 부류로 분류해 왔던 것이다. 실리아의 발이 더없이 시릴 때에도, 그리고 그 대머리 살갗이 움찔거리는 꼴을 보기가 정말이지 끔찍해질 때에도, 도러시아는 늙은 리레 씨의 말을 듣는 데 결코 싫증을 내지 않았다. 그렇다면 어째서 그녀의 열광이 리레 씨에게 향한 것과 똑같은 식으로 캐소본 씨에게도 그저 뻗어 나가지 못하겠는가? 그리고 학식 있는 사람이란 다들 젊은이들을 일종의 학교 선생 같은 눈으로 본다는 것도 충분히 있을 법한 일이었다.\n\n하지만 이제 실리아는 마음속에 번뜩 떠오른 그 의혹에 정말로 흠칫 놀랐다. 그녀가 이런 식으로 불시에 놀라는 일은 드물었으니, 어떤 종류의 징후를 관찰하는 데 비상하게 빠른 덕분에 대개는 자신이 관심을 둔 외적인 일들을 미리 예상할 수 있었던 것이다. 그렇다고 그녀가 지금 캐소본 씨를 이미 받아들여진 구혼자라고 상상한 것은 아니었다. 다만 도러시아의 마음속 무언가가 그런 결말을 향해 기울 수도 있다는 가능성에 비로소 역겨움을 느끼기 시작했을 뿐이다. 이거야말로 도도에 대해 정말로 속상한 일이었다. 제임스 체텀 경을 받아들이지 않는 것이야 그렇다 쳐도, 캐소본 씨와 결혼한다는 생각이라니! 실리아는 일종의 수치심에 우스꽝스럽다는 느낌이 뒤섞인 기분이었다. 하지만 어쩌면 도도가 정말로 그런 터무니없는 일에 발을 들이려 한다 해도, 거기서 돌이켜 세울 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 언니가 곧잘 마음이 흔들린다는 것은 경험으로 자주 드러난 바였으니까. 날이 눅눅하여 산책을 나갈 참은 아니었으므로, 둘은 함께 거실로 올라갔다. 그리고 거기서 실리아는, 도러시아가 평소처럼 부지런한 흥미를 가지고 무슨 일에 자리를 잡는 대신, 그저 펼쳐진 책에 팔꿈치를 괴고 창밖으로 눅눅한 기운에 은빛으로 물든 커다란 삼나무를 내다보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀 자신은 보좌 신부의 아이들에게 줄 장난감을 만들기 시작했고, 어떤 화제든 너무 성급하게 꺼낼 생각은 없었다.\n\n사실 도러시아는, 캐소본 씨가 지난번 이 집에 다녀간 이후로 그의 처지에 일어난 중대한 변화를 실리아가 아는 것이 바람직하다고 생각하고 있었다. 동생이 그를 대하는 태도에 필연적으로 영향을 미칠 일을 모르는 채로 두는 것은 공정하지 않게 여겨졌던 것이다. 하지만 그 말을 꺼내기를 움츠리지 않을 수 없었다. 도러시아는 이 소심함 속에서 어떤 비겁함을 들어 자신을 책망했다. 자기 행동에 대해 조그만 두려움이나 잔꾀를 품는 것은 그녀에게 늘 혐오스러운 일이었으나, 바로 이 순간 그녀는 실리아의 예쁘장하고 속되리만치 현세적인 산문이 지닌 부식성을 두려워하지 않으려, 가능한 한 가장 높은 도움을 구하고 있었다. 그녀의 몽상은 깨어졌고, 결단을 내리기 어렵던 망설임도 사라졌으니, 실리아가 평소의 어조로, 곁들이는 말이나 \"그러고 보니\" 하고 던지는 한마디처럼, 작고 다소 목구멍에서 울리는 목소리로 입을 열었기 때문이다.\n\n\"캐소본 씨 말고 또 누가 저녁 먹으러 오나?\"\n\n\"내가 알기로는 없어.\"\n\n\"누가 또 왔으면 좋겠는데. 그러면 그분이 수프 드시는 소리를 그렇게 안 들어도 될 테니까.\"\n\n\"그분이 수프 드시는 게 뭐가 그리 별스럽다는 거니?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 도도, 언니는 그분이 숟가락 긁는 소리가 안 들려? 게다가 말하기 전에 늘 눈을 깜박이잖아. 로크가 눈을 깜박였는지는 모르겠지만, 만약 그랬다면 그분 맞은편에 앉았던 사람들이 참 딱했겠다 싶어.\"\n\n\"실리아.\" 도러시아가 단호하고 엄숙하게 말했다. \"제발 그런 식의 말은 더 이상 하지 마라.\"\n\n\"왜 안 돼? 다 사실인걸.\" 실리아가 받아쳤다. 슬슬 좀 겁이 나기 시작했지만, 그녀에게는 굽히지 않을 나름의 이유가 있었다.\n\n\"가장 평범한 정신만이 보아 내는 것 중에도 사실인 것이 많지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 가장 평범한 정신도 꽤 쓸모가 있나 봐. 캐소본 씨의 어머니가 좀 더 평범한 정신을 가지지 못했던 게 안타까워. 그랬으면 아들을 좀 더 잘 가르쳤을 텐데.\" 이 가벼운 투창을 던지고 나자 실리아는 속으로 겁이 나서 달아날 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n도러시아의 감정은 눈사태처럼 쌓여 있었고, 더 이상 준비할 여지는 없었다.\n\n\"네게 알려 주는 것이 옳겠구나, 실리아. 나는 캐소본 씨와 결혼하기로 약혼했어.\"\n\n아마 실리아는 그토록 창백해진 적이 없었을 것이다. 그녀가 만들고 있던 종이 인형은, 손에 쥔 것은 무엇이든 조심하는 그녀의 습관이 아니었다면 다리가 상했을 것이다. 그녀는 그 부서지기 쉬운 인형을 곧장 내려놓고 잠시 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다. 다시 입을 열었을 때 눈에는 눈물이 고이고 있었다.\n\n\"오, 도도, 언니가 행복하길 바라.\" 이 순간 그녀의 자매다운 다정함은 다른 감정들을 누르지 않을 수 없었고, 그녀의 두려움은 애정에서 나온 두려움이었다.\n\n도러시아는 여전히 상처받고 동요한 상태였다.\n\n\"그럼 완전히 정해진 거야?\" 실리아가 외경에 찬 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"삼촌도 아셔?\"\n\n\"나는 캐소본 씨의 청혼을 받아들였어. 삼촌이 그 청혼이 담긴 편지를 가져다주셨고, 미리 알고 계셨어.\"\n\n\"내가 언니 마음을 아프게 한 말을 했다면 미안해, 도도.\" 실리아가 가볍게 흐느끼며 말했다. 그녀는 자기가 이런 기분을 느끼게 되리라곤 결코 생각지 못했다. 이 모든 일에는 어딘가 장례식 같은 데가 있었고, 캐소본 씨는 그 예식을 집전하는 성직자 같아서, 그에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 말하는 것은 무례한 일처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마, 키티, 슬퍼하지 마. 우리가 같은 사람을 좋아할 수는 없는 노릇이지. 나도 곧잘 비슷한 식으로 누구의 비위를 거스르곤 해. 마음에 들지 않는 사람들에 대해서는 너무 심하게 말하는 버릇이 있거든.\"\n\n이런 관대함에도 불구하고 도러시아는 여전히 쓰라렸다. 어쩌면 실리아의 자질구레한 비판 못지않게, 그녀의 억눌린 놀라움 때문이었는지도 모른다. 물론 팁턴 일대의 온 세상이 이 결혼에 공감하지 않을 것이었다. 도러시아는 삶과 그 가장 고귀한 목적에 대해 자기처럼 생각하는 사람을 단 한 명도 알지 못했다.\n\n그럼에도 그날 저녁이 끝나기 전에 그녀는 무척 행복했다. 캐소본 씨와 단둘이 보낸 한 시간 동안, 그녀는 일찍이 느껴 본 적 없는 자유로움으로 그에게 이야기했고, 그에게 자신을 바치겠다는 생각에서, 그리고 그의 위대한 목적들을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 나누고 진척시킬 수 있을지 배우겠다는 생각에서 우러나는 기쁨을 쏟아 내기까지 했다. 캐소본 씨는 이 어린아이같이 거리낌 없는 열정에 미지의 기쁨을 느끼며 감동했고(어느 남자인들 그러지 않았겠는가?), 자신이 그 열정의 대상이 되었다는 데에는 놀라지 않았다(어느 연인인들 놀랐겠는가?).\n\n\"내 사랑하는 아가씨—브룩 양—도러시아!\" 그가 그녀의 손을 두 손 사이에 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"이것은 제가 저를 위해 마련되어 있으리라고는 일찍이 상상도 못 했던 더없이 큰 행복입니다. 결혼을 바람직하게 만들어 줄 그 어우러진 우아함을 정신과 인품에 이토록 풍성하게 갖춘 분을 만나리라고는, 참으로 제 머릿속에 떠올려 보지도 못한 일이었습니다. 당신은 제가 여태껏 여성다움의 특징적인 미덕이라 여겨 온 그 모든 자질을—아니, 그 이상을—지니고 계십니다. 여성의 위대한 매력은 열렬하고 자기희생적인 애정을 발휘할 수 있는 능력에 있으니, 바로 여기서 우리는 그것이 우리 남성의 존재를 완전하게 둥글려 채워 주기에 알맞다는 것을 봅니다. 여태껏 저는 더 엄격한 종류의 즐거움 말고는 거의 알지 못했습니다. 제 만족이라곤 고독한 학자의 만족이었지요. 손안에서 시들어 버릴 꽃을 따 모으려는 마음은 별로 없었습니다만, 이제는 그것을 당신의 가슴에 꽂아 드리려 기꺼이 따겠습니다.\"\n\n이보다 더 철저하게 그 의도에 있어 정직한 말은 없었을 것이다. 그 끝의 차가운 수사조차도 개가 짖는 소리나 사랑에 빠진 띠까마귀의 울음소리만큼이나 진실했다. 만돌린의 가냘픈 가락처럼 우리에게 들리는, 델리아에게 바친 그 소네트들 뒤에 아무런 열정도 없었다고 결론짓는 것은 경솔한 일이 아니겠는가?\n\n도러시아의 믿음은 캐소본 씨의 말이 미처 다 하지 못한 듯한 모든 것을 채워 넣었다. 신앙을 가진 자가 거슬리는 누락이나 어설픔을 알아채는 일이 어디 있겠는가? 예언자의 것이든 시인의 것이든, 글이란 우리가 그 안에 무엇을 담아 넣든 그만큼 넓어지는 법이며, 심지어 그의 어설픈 문법조차 숭고하게 느껴지는 것이다.\n\n\"저는 아주 무지해요—제 무지함에 당신은 무척 놀라실 거예요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"온통 잘못되었을지도 모를 생각이 너무 많거든요. 하지만 이제 저는 그 모든 것을 당신께 말씀드리고, 여쭤 볼 수 있겠지요. 그래도,\" 그녀는 캐소본 씨의 마음을 빠르게 헤아리며 덧붙였다. \"당신을 너무 성가시게 하지는 않을 거예요. 당신이 제 말에 귀 기울이고 싶으실 때만요. 당신은 자기 길에서 주제들을 좇느라 자주 지치실 테니까요. 당신이 저를 거기 데려가 주신다면, 저는 그것으로 충분합니다.\"\n\n\"이제 당신과 함께하지 않고서야 제가 어떻게 어떤 길에서든 견뎌 나갈 수 있겠습니까?\" 캐소본 씨가 그녀의 솔직한 이마에 입 맞추며 말했다. 하늘이 자신의 특별한 필요에 모든 면에서 들어맞는 축복을 내려 주었다고 느끼면서였다. 그는 당장의 효과를 위해서든 더 먼 목적을 위해서든 아무런 숨은 계산도 없는 한 천성의 매력에 자신도 모르게 감화되고 있었다. 바로 이것이, 그토록 영리하다는 평판에도 불구하고 도러시아를 어린아이같이 만들고, 어떤 사람들이 보기에는 어리석게까지 보이도록 만든 것이었다. 예컨대 지금의 경우처럼, 비유적으로 말하자면 캐소본 씨의 발치에 몸을 던지고, 마치 그가 신교의 교황이라도 되는 양 그의 유행 지난 신발 끈에 입 맞추는 것이 그러했다. 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게 자기에게 어울릴 만큼 그가 충분히 훌륭한지 자문하도록 가르치기는커녕, 그저 자신이 어떻게 하면 캐소본 씨에게 어울릴 만큼 충분히 훌륭할 수 있을지 노심초사 자문하고 있을 뿐이었다. 그가 이튿날 떠나기 전에 결혼식을 여섯 주 안에 올리기로 정해졌다. 안 될 이유가 어디 있겠는가? 캐소본 씨의 집은 준비되어 있었다. 그것은 사제관이 아니라, 딸린 땅도 넓은 상당한 규모의 저택이었다. 사제관에는 보좌 신부가 살고 있었는데, 그는 아침 설교만 빼고 모든 직무를 맡고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI.\n\n무심한 손으로 쓰다듬다가는 베이고 만다.\n정교하게 베는 것이 그녀의 본분이라, 그녀는\n\n영적인 칼날로 좁쌀 한 알을 가르고,\n만질 수 없는 절약을 이루어 낸다.\n\n캐소본 씨의 마차가 정문을 빠져나오던 참에, 그것이 한 부인이 모는, 하인 하나가 뒤에 앉은 조랑말 사륜마차의 진입을 가로막았다. 서로 알아보았는지는 확실치 않았으니, 캐소본 씨는 멍하니 앞만 바라보고 있었기 때문이다. 하지만 그 부인은 눈치가 빨라서 때를 놓치지 않고 고개를 까딱하며 \"안녕하세요?\" 하고 던졌다. 초라한 보닛과 무척 낡은 인도산 숄을 걸쳤음에도, 문지기가 그 작은 사륜마차의 진입에 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 절하는 것으로 보아, 그녀를 중요한 인물로 여기고 있음이 분명했다.\n\n\"그래, 피칫 부인, 요즘 닭들이 알은 잘 낳나요?\" 혈색 좋고 검은 눈을 가진 부인이 더없이 또렷하게 깎아 낸 듯한 발음으로 말했다.\n\n\"알은 그런대로 잘 낳습니다요, 마님, 한데 제 알을 쪼아 먹는 버릇이 들어서요. 도무지 마음이 편치가 않아요.\"\n\"오, 식인종 같으니! 차라리 당장 싸게 팔아 버리는 게 낫겠네요. 한 쌍에 얼마에 팔겠어요? 행실 나쁜 닭을 비싼 값에 먹을 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 마님, 반 크라운입죠. 그 아래로는 못 내놓습니다요.\"\n\n\"이런 시절에 반 크라운이라니! 자, 이러면 어떨까요. 일요일에 목사님 닭 수프로 쓰게요. 우리 집 닭은 내가 내줄 만한 건 그분이 다 자셨거든요. 피칫 부인, 설교로 절반은 받은 셈이니, 그 점을 기억하세요. 대신 공중제비비둘기 한 쌍을 받아요. 어찌나 어여쁜지. 와서 꼭 봐야 해요. 댁 비둘기 중에는 공중제비비둘기가 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 마님, 피칫이 일 끝나고 보러 가게 하겠습니다요. 그이가 새로운 종류라면 사족을 못 쓰니까요. 마님 좋으시라고요.\"\n\n\"내가 좋으라고요! 그이가 여태껏 한 거래 중에서 제일 남는 장사일 텐데요. 제 알을 쪼아 먹는 못된 스페인 닭 두 마리 대신 교회 비둘기 한 쌍이라니! 피칫 부부, 너무 자랑하지나 말아요. 그것뿐이에요!\"\n\n마지막 말과 함께 사륜마차가 앞으로 나아갔고, 피칫 부인은 웃으며 천천히 고개를 가로젓고는 \"그러믄요, 그러믄요!\" 하고 감탄사를 내뱉으며 남겨졌다. 이로 미루어 보아, 만약 목사님 부인이 그처럼 거리낌 없이 말하지 않고 그처럼 인색하지 않았더라면 그녀는 시골 생활이 다소 따분하게 느껴졌으리라 짐작할 만했다. 사실 프레시트와 티프턴 교구의 농부들과 일꾼들도 캐드월러더 부인이 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 했는지에 관한 이야기가 없었더라면 대화거리가 몹시 아쉬웠을 것이다. 그녀는 헤아릴 수 없이 고귀한 가문에서 태어난 부인으로, 영웅적인 망령들의 무리처럼 어렴풋한, 이름 모를 백작들의 후예라 할 만했는데, 가난을 호소하고 값을 깎고 더없이 다정스러운 태도로 농담을 던졌으되, 그 말투에는 자신이 누구인지를 알리는 무언가가 있었다. 그런 부인은 신분에도 종교에도 친근함을 더해 주었고, 면제받지 못한 십일조의 쓰라림을 덜어 주었다. 시큼한 위엄이 가미된 훨씬 모범적인 인물이었다면 그들이 39개 신조를 이해하는 데 도움이 되지 못했을 것이고, 사람들을 사회적으로 결속시키는 힘도 덜했을 것이다.\n\n* 39개 신조: 영국 국교회의 기본 교리를 정리한 신앙 조항.\n캐드월러더 부인의 장점을 다른 관점에서 보고 있던 브룩 씨는, 혼자 앉아 있던 서재에 그녀의 이름이 전해지자 약간 움찔했다.\n\n\"여기 우리 로윅의 키케로가 다녀갔나 보군요.\" 그녀는 편안하게 자리를 잡고 앉아 겉옷을 뒤로 젖히며, 가냘프지만 잘 짜인 몸매를 드러내고 말했다. \"당신과 그 사람이 무슨 못된 정략을 꾸미고 있는 게 아닌가 싶어요. 안 그러면 그 활달한 양반을 이렇게 자주 만날 리가 없잖아요. 당신을 고발해야겠어요. 가톨릭 법안 때 두 분 다 필 편을 들었으니 의심스러운 인물이라는 걸 잊지 마세요. 늙은 핑커턴이 사임하면 당신이 휘그당 쪽에서 미들마치 의원에 출마할 거고, 캐소본이 뒤에서 은밀히 당신을 도울 거라고 다들한테 말하고 다니겠어요. 유권자들을 소책자로 매수하고, 그걸 나눠 주려고 술집 문을 활짝 열어 줄 거라고요. 자, 자백하세요!\"\n\n\"전혀 그런 게 아니오.\" 브룩 씨가 미소 지으며 안경을 닦았으나, 사실 그 탄핵에 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"캐소본과 나는 정치 이야기는 별로 안 하오. 그는 일의 자선적인 측면에는 관심이 별로 없소. 형벌이나 그런 것 말이오. 그는 오로지 교회 문제에만 관심이 있소. 그건 내 활동 분야가 아니라는 걸 당신도 알잖소.\"\n\n\"너무하시네요, 친애하는 양반. 당신이 한 일들을 들었어요. 미들마치에서 가톨릭교도들한테 자기 땅을 판 게 누구였더라? 일부러 그걸 사들였다고 믿어요. 당신은 완벽한 가이 포크스예요. 다가오는 11월 5일에 허수아비로 화형당하지 않나 두고 보세요. 험프리는 이 일로 당신과 다투러 오지 않을 테니, 그래서 내가 온 거예요.\"\n\n\"아주 좋소. 박해하지 않는 죄로 박해받을 각오는 되어 있었으니까. 박해하지 않는 죄로 말이오.\"\n\n\"또 시작이군요! 그건 당신이 유세장에서 쓰려고 준비해 둔 입버릇이로군요. 자, 친애하는 브룩 씨, 절대로 그들이 당신을 유세장으로 꾀어내게 두지 마세요. 사람이 연설을 하면 늘 바보 꼴이 되고 마니까요. 옳은 편에 서서 당신의 우물쭈물 더듬거림에 축복을 빌 수 있는 경우가 아니라면 변명의 여지가 없어요. 당신은 길을 잃고 말 거예요, 미리 경고하지요. 온갖 정파의 의견을 잡탕 파이로 만들어서 모두에게 돌팔매질당할 거예요.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 내가 기대하는 바요.\" 브룩 씨는 이 예언적 묘사가 얼마나 달갑지 않은지 드러내고 싶지 않아서 말했다. \"독립적인 인간으로서 내가 기대하는 바란 말이오. 휘그당으로 말하자면, 사상가들과 뜻을 같이하는 사람은 어느 정파에도 낚여 들어가지 않는 법이오. 어느 지점까지는 그들과 함께 갈 수 있소. 어느 지점까지는 말이오. 하지만 그게 바로 당신네 부인들은 도무지 이해하지 못하는 거요.\"\n\n\"당신의 그 어느 지점이 어디냐고요? 모르겠네요. 어느 정파에도 속하지 않고, 떠돌이 생활을 하면서 친구들한테 주소조차 알리지 않는 사람이 어떻게 어느 지점이라는 걸 가질 수 있는지 누가 좀 알려 줬으면 좋겠어요. '브룩이 어디 있을지는 아무도 몰라. 브룩은 도무지 믿을 수가 없어.' 솔직히 말하면 사람들이 당신을 두고 하는 말이 그래요. 자, 이제 점잖아지세요. 다들 당신을 못마땅하게 쳐다보고, 당신은 양심에 가책을 느끼며 빈 주머니를 하고서 재판소에 가는 게 좋겠어요?\"\n\n\"부인을 상대로 정치를 논할 생각은 없소.\" 브룩 씨가 미소 띤 무관심한 태도로 말했으나, 캐드월러더 부인의 이 공격이 자신의 경솔한 몇몇 행보가 자초한 방어전의 막을 열었다는 것을 다소 불쾌하게 의식하고 있었다. \"당신네 성은 사상가가 아니니까. 바리움 엣 무타빌레 셈페르라, 뭐 그런 거지. 당신은 베르길리우스를 모르잖소. 내가 알기로는….\" 브룩 씨는 자신이 그 아우구스투스 시대 시인과 개인적으로 친분이 있었던 게 아님을 때맞춰 깨달았다. \"내가 하려던 말은, 가엾은 스토더트 말이오. 그 사람이 한 말이었소. 당신네 부인들은 늘 독립적인 태도에 반대하지요. 사람이 오로지 진리만을 소중히 여기는 것, 뭐 그런 것 말이오. 그리고 이 고장만큼 여론이 편협한 곳도 없소. 돌을 던지려는 건 아니오만, 누군가는 독립적인 노선을 취해야 하고, 내가 안 하면 누가 하겠소?\"\n* 바리움 엣 무타빌레 셈페르: \"여자는 언제나 변덕스럽고 변하기 쉽다\"는 뜻의 라틴어로, 베르길리우스의 『아이네이스』에 나오는 구절.\n\n\"누가 하겠냐고요? 그야 혈통도 지위도 없는 벼락출세꾼이라면 누구든 하겠지요. 신분 있는 사람들은 독립적이라는 헛소리를 집 안에서나 소비할 일이지 떠벌리고 다닐 일이 아니에요. 그런데 당신은! 딸이나 다름없는 조카딸을 우리 고장 최고의 신사 가운데 한 사람에게 시집보내려는 사람이! 제임스 경이 몹시 언짢아할 거예요. 당신이 이제 와서 입장을 바꿔 휘그당 간판이 된다면 그 사람에게 너무 가혹한 일이 될 거라고요.\"\n\n브룩 씨는 다시 속으로 움찔했으니, 도러시아의 약혼이 결정되자마자 캐드월러더 부인이 앞으로 퍼부을 빈정거림을 떠올렸기 때문이었다. 무지한 구경꾼이라면 \"캐드월러더 부인과 절교하시오\"라고 쉽게 말할 수 있겠지만, 가장 오랜 이웃과 다툰 시골 신사가 대체 어디로 갈 수 있단 말인가? 봉인 없는 포도주처럼 무심코 전해진다면, 브룩이라는 이름의 그 그윽한 풍미를 누가 음미할 수 있겠는가? 분명 사람이란 어느 지점까지만 세계주의자가 될 수 있는 법이다.\n\n\"채텀과 나는 늘 좋은 친구로 지내기를 바라오만, 안타깝게도 그가 내 조카딸과 결혼할 전망은 없소.\" 브룩 씨는 창밖으로 실리아가 들어오는 것이 보여 크게 안도하며 말했다.\n\n\"왜 안 되는데요?\" 캐드월러더 부인이 놀란 듯 날카롭게 물었다. \"당신과 내가 그 일을 이야기한 게 채 보름도 안 됐는데요.\"\n\n\"내 조카딸이 다른 구혼자를 골랐소. 그를 골랐단 말이오. 나는 거기 아무 상관도 없소. 나는 채텀이 더 좋았을 거요. 어떤 처녀라도 채텀을 고를 거라고 말했을 거요. 하지만 이런 일은 도무지 설명할 수가 없소. 당신네 성은 변덕스러우니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼 조카딸을 누구한테 시집보낼 작정이라는 거예요?\" 캐드월러더 부인의 마음은 도러시아가 고를 만한 가능성들을 재빠르게 훑고 있었다.\n\n하지만 바로 그때 실리아가 정원 산책으로 발그레해진 얼굴로 들어왔고, 그녀와 인사를 나누느라 브룩 씨는 당장 대답해야 할 처지에서 벗어났다. 그는 허둥지둥 일어나 \"그러고 보니 말에 관해 라이트와 얘기를 좀 해야겠군\" 하고는 잰걸음으로 방을 빠져나갔다.\n\n\"얘야, 이게 무슨 일이니? 네 언니의 약혼 말이다.\" 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"언니는 캐소본 씨와 결혼하기로 약혼했어요.\" 실리아는 여느 때처럼 가장 단순한 사실 진술에 의지하면서, 목사님 부인과 단둘이 이야기할 이 기회를 즐기며 말했다.\n\n\"끔찍하구나. 얼마나 오래된 일이니?\"\n\n\"저도 어제야 알았어요. 6주 후에 결혼한대요.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 형부 생기는 거 축하한다.\"\n\n\"저는 언니가 너무 안됐어요.\"\n\n\"안됐다고! 그 애가 한 일이잖니.\"\n\n\"네. 언니는 캐소본 씨가 위대한 영혼을 지녔대요.\"\n\n\"진심으로 그러길 바란다.\"\n\n\"오, 캐드월러더 부인, 위대한 영혼을 지닌 사람과 결혼하는 게 좋을 리 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 명심하렴. 이제 그런 사람이 어떻게 생겼는지 알았으니, 다음에 그런 사람이 와서 너랑 결혼하자고 하면 절대 받아 주지 마라.\"\n\n\"전 정말이지 절대 그러지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래. 한 집안에 그런 사람 하나면 충분하지. 그래, 네 언니는 제임스 채텀 경한테 도통 마음이 없었던 거니? 형부로 그분을 맞았다면 너는 뭐라고 했겠니?\"\n\n\"저는 무척 좋았을 거예요. 분명 좋은 남편이 되셨을 거예요. 다만,\" 실리아는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 덧붙였다(그녀는 때때로 숨을 쉬듯 자연스럽게 얼굴을 붉히는 듯했다). \"그분이 언니한테 어울렸을 것 같지는 않아요.\"\n\n\"충분히 고상하지 못해서?\"\n\n\"도도는 무척 엄격해요. 모든 일을 너무 깊이 생각하고, 남이 하는 말에 무척 까다로워요. 제임스 경은 한 번도 언니 마음에 든 적이 없는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 분명 부추겼겠지. 그건 별로 떳떳한 일이 아니구나.\"\n\n\"도도한테 화내지 마세요. 언니는 그런 걸 잘 못 봐요. 오두막집들 생각에만 너무 골몰해서 가끔 제임스 경한테 무례하게 굴기도 했지만, 그분은 워낙 친절해서 한 번도 그걸 눈치채지 못했어요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 캐드월러더 부인이 숄을 두르고 서두르듯 일어서며 말했다. \"곧장 제임스 경한테 가서 이 일을 알려야겠다. 지금쯤이면 어머니를 모시고 돌아왔을 테니, 찾아가야겠어. 네 삼촌은 절대 그분한테 말하지 않을 테니까. 얘야, 우리 모두 실망했단다. 젊은 사람들은 결혼할 때 가문을 생각해야 하는데. 나야 나쁜 본보기지. 가난한 목사와 결혼해서 드 브레이시 집안에서 가엾은 신세가 됐으니까. 석탄도 꾀를 부려 구해야 하고, 샐러드 기름은 하늘에 기도해야 할 판이지. 하지만 캐소본은 돈은 넉넉하니, 그 점만큼은 공정하게 인정해 줘야겠지. 혈통으로 말하자면, 가문 문장이 검은 오징어 세 마리에 앞발을 든 주석가 한 마리쯤이려나. 그건 그렇고, 가기 전에 얘야, 네 집 카터 부인한테 페이스트리 만드는 얘기를 좀 해야겠다. 우리 집 젊은 요리사를 보내서 배우게 하고 싶거든. 우리처럼 아이가 넷이나 되는 가난한 사람들은 좋은 요리사를 둘 형편이 못 되잖니. 카터 부인은 틀림없이 들어줄 거야. 제임스 경네 요리사는 완전히 용 같으니까.\"\n\n한 시간도 채 안 되어 캐드월러더 부인은 카터 부인을 구워삶고는, 자기 목사관에서 멀지 않은 프레시트 홀로 마차를 몰았다. 그녀의 남편은 프레시트에 거주하면서 티프턴에는 부목사를 두고 있었다.\n\n제임스 채텀 경은 이틀간 자리를 비우게 했던 짧은 여행에서 돌아와, 티프턴 그레인지로 말을 타고 갈 작정으로 옷을 갈아입은 참이었다. 캐드월러더 부인이 마차를 몰고 왔을 때 그의 말이 문 앞에 서 있었고, 그도 곧 채찍을 손에 든 채 직접 그 앞에 나타났다. 채텀 부인은 아직 돌아오지 않았지만, 캐드월러더 부인의 용건은 마부들 앞에서 처리할 수 있는 것이 아니었으므로, 그녀는 새로 들여온 식물들을 구경하게 가까운 온실로 안내해 달라고 청했다. 그리고 생각에 잠긴 듯 걸음을 멈추고는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"당신한테 큰 충격을 줄 일이 있어요. 당신이 짐짓 그런 척했던 만큼 사랑에 깊이 빠진 건 아니길 바라요.\"\n\n캐드월러더 부인의 화법에 항의해 봐야 소용없는 일이었다. 하지만 제임스 경의 얼굴빛이 약간 변했다. 그는 막연한 불안을 느꼈다.\n\n\"아무래도 브룩이 결국 망신을 살 모양이에요. 내가 그 사람더러 자유당 쪽에서 미들마치 의원에 출마할 생각이라고 다그쳤더니, 멍청한 표정만 짓고 부인은 절대 안 하더군요. 독립적인 노선이니 늘 하던 헛소리만 늘어놓았어요.\"\n\n\"그게 다입니까?\" 제임스 경이 크게 안도하며 말했다.\n\n\"아니,\" 캐드월러더 부인이 더 날카로운 어조로 받아쳤다. \"설마 그 사람이 그런 식으로 공인이 되는 게 좋다는 말은 아니겠지요? 일종의 정치판 싸구려 장사꾼이 되는 게 말이에요.\"\n\n\"단념시킬 수 있을 것 같은데요. 그 사람은 돈 드는 걸 싫어하니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 사람한테 한 말이 바로 그거예요. 거기서는 이치가 통하지요. 한 온스의 인색함 속에도 늘 몇 알의 상식은 들어 있으니까요. 인색함은 집안 내력으로 이어지기에 더없이 좋은 자질이지요. 광기가 빠져들 때 안전한 쪽이거든요. 그리고 브룩 집안에는 분명 조금 금이 가 있을 거예요. 안 그러면 우리가 곧 보게 될 일은 보이지 않겠지요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 브룩이 미들마치 의원에 출마한다고요?\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나쁜 일이에요. 정말이지 제 책임도 좀 느껴요. 내가 늘 당신한테 브룩 양이 그렇게 좋은 짝이 될 거라고 했잖아요. 그 애한테 헛된 구석이 많다는 건 알고 있었어요. 변덕스러운 감리교도 같은 면 말이에요. 하지만 그런 건 처녀들한테서 닳아 없어지는 법이니까요. 그런데 이번만큼은 내가 깜짝 놀라고 말았어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 캐드월러더 부인?\" 제임스 경이 물었다. 브룩 양이 모라비아 형제단이나 점잖은 사회에는 알려지지 않은 어떤 터무니없는 종파에 들어가려고 달아난 게 아닐까 하는 그의 두려움은, 캐드월러더 부인이 늘 일을 최악으로 말하곤 한다는 것을 알기에 조금 누그러졌다. \"브룩 양한테 무슨 일이 생긴 겁니까? 제발 분명히 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"좋아요. 그 애가 결혼하기로 약혼했어요.\" 캐드월러더 부인은 잠시 말을 멈추고, 친구가 긴장된 미소로 감추려 애쓰며 부츠를 채찍으로 치는 동안, 그의 얼굴에 깊이 상처받은 표정이 떠오르는 것을 살펴보았다. 그러나 곧 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"캐소본과 약혼했어요.\"\n\n제임스 경은 채찍을 떨어뜨리고 그것을 주우려 몸을 숙였다. 그가 캐드월러더 부인을 돌아보며 \"캐소본이라고요?\"라고 되뇌었을 때만큼 그의 얼굴에 응축된 혐오가 떠오른 적은 아마 일찍이 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"바로 그래요. 이제 내가 온 용건을 알겠지요.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 끔찍해요! 그자는 미라나 다를 바 없는데!\" (이는 한창 혈기 왕성한, 실연당한 연적의 관점임을 감안해 들어야 한다.)\n\n\"그 애는 그자가 위대한 영혼을 지녔다고 하더군요. 마른 완두콩이 달그락거리는 커다란 오줌통 같은 영혼이겠지요!\" 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"저런 늙은 독신자가 결혼은 무슨 결혼이람?\" 제임스 경이 말했다. \"한 발은 이미 무덤에 들여놓은 주제에.\"\n\n\"그 발을 다시 빼낼 작정인가 보지요.\"\n\n\"브룩은 이걸 허락하면 안 돼요. 그 애가 성년이 될 때까지 미루도록 우겨야 해요. 그러면 그때는 다시 생각할 거예요. 후견인이 뭐 하러 있는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"브룩한테서 결단이라는 걸 짜낼 수 있을 것 같아요?\"\n\n\"캐드월러더 씨가 그 사람한테 말해 볼 수 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그이는 안 돼요! 험프리는 누구든 다 매력적으로 본다니까요. 도무지 그이가 캐소본을 욕하게 만들 수가 없어요. 심지어 주교까지도 좋게 말하는걸요. 성직록을 받는 목사가 그러는 건 부자연스럽다고 내가 말해도요. 체면치레에 그렇게 무심한 남편을 데리고 뭘 어쩌겠어요? 그래서 내가 직접 모두를 욕하는 걸로 어떻게든 그걸 감추고 있지요. 자, 자, 기운 내세요! 브룩 양과 인연이 끊긴 건 잘된 일이에요. 그 애는 당신더러 대낮에 별을 보라고 요구했을 처녀니까요. 우리끼리 얘기지만, 어린 실리아가 그 애보다 두 배는 나아요. 결국엔 더 좋은 짝이 될 것 같고요. 캐소본과의 이 결혼은 수녀원에 들어가는 거나 마찬가지니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 제 입장에서 하는 말이 아니에요. 브룩 양을 위해서 그 애 친지들이 영향력을 행사해 봐야 한다는 거지요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 험프리는 아직 몰라요. 하지만 내가 말하면 틀림없이 이럴 거예요. '왜 안 돼? 캐소본은 좋은 사람이고, 젊잖아. 충분히 젊어.' 그런 인심 좋은 사람들은 식초를 삼키고 배앓이를 해 봐야 비로소 그게 포도주가 아니란 걸 알지요. 어쨌든 내가 남자라면 실리아를 택하겠어요. 특히 도러시아가 가 버린 마당에는요. 사실은, 당신이 한 사람한테 구애해서 다른 사람의 마음을 얻은 격이에요. 그 애가 남자가 받기를 기대하는 만큼이나 당신을 흠모한다는 게 내 눈엔 보여요. 나 아닌 다른 사람이 이런 말을 했다면 과장이라고 여길지도 모르지만요. 그럼 안녕히!\"\n\n제임스 경은 캐드월러더 부인을 사륜마차에 태워 주고 나서 자기 말에 뛰어올랐다. 그는 친구의 달갑지 않은 소식 때문에 말 타는 것을 그만둘 생각은 없었다. 다만 티프턴 그레인지가 아닌 다른 방향으로 더 빨리 달릴 뿐이었다.\n\n그런데 대체 무엇 때문에 캐드월러더 부인은 브룩 양의 결혼에 그토록 부산을 떨었을까? 그리고 자신이 거든 셈 치고 싶었던 한 혼사가 틀어지자, 왜 곧장 또 다른 혼사의 사전 작업을 꾸몄을까? 세심하게 망원경으로 감시하면 포착할 만한 어떤 교묘한 음모나 숨바꼭질 같은 행동이 있었을까? 전혀 그렇지 않았다. 망원경으로 티프턴과 프레시트 교구, 즉 캐드월러더 부인이 사륜마차로 다니는 구역 전체를 훑어보아도 의심을 살 만한 어떤 만남도, 그녀가 한결같이 흔들림 없는 예리한 눈빛과 한결같이 높다란 천연 혈색을 잃은 채 돌아오는 어떤 장면도 목격하지 못했을 것이다. 사실 그런 편리한 탈것이 일곱 현인의 시대에 있었더라면, 그중 한 사람은 분명 이렇게 말했을 것이다. 여인들을 조랑말 사륜마차로 따라다녀 봐야 그들에 관해 알 수 있는 것은 거의 없다고. 물 한 방울에 현미경을 들이대도 우리는 다소 조잡한 것으로 드러나는 해석을 하기 마련이다. 약한 렌즈로 보면 어떤 생물이 활발한 탐식성을 발휘하고 그 속으로 더 작은 생물들이 마치 살아 움직이는 세금 동전이라도 되는 양 활발히 빨려 드는 것처럼 보일 테지만, 더 강한 렌즈를 대면 그 희생자들을 향해 소용돌이를 일으키는 아주 미세한 잔털들이 드러나고, 정작 삼키는 자는 세금 거두는 자리에서 수동적으로 기다리고 있을 뿐이다. 비유하자면 이런 식으로, 캐드월러더 부인의 중매질에 강한 렌즈를 들이대면, 그녀가 필요로 하는 종류의 양식을 가져다주는, 이른바 사고와 언어의 소용돌이를 만들어 내는 미세한 원인들의 작용이 드러날 것이다. 그녀의 삶은 시골답게 단순했고, 추잡하거나 위험하거나 그 밖에 중요한 어떤 비밀도 없었으며, 세상의 큰일들에 의식적으로 영향받지도 않았다. 그렇기에 고귀한 친척들이 보낸 편지로 전해지는 큰 세상의 일들에 그녀는 더더욱 흥미를 느꼈다. 매력적인 차남들이 정부와 결혼해 신세를 망친 사연이며, 젊은 테이퍼 경의 고상한 명문가다운 백치 같은 짓이며, 늙은 메가테리엄 경의 통풍에 시달리는 격노한 성미며, 어느 가문의 가계도가 정확히 어떻게 교차하여 새로운 가지로 백작 작위가 넘어가고 추문의 친척 관계가 더 넓어졌는가 하는 것들이 그러한 화제였는데, 그녀는 그 세부 사항들을 더없이 정확하게 기억해 두었다가 절묘하게 절인 경구로 재현해 냈다. 그녀 자신은 사냥감과 해로운 짐승을 믿듯이 좋은 혈통과 미천한 혈통을 의심 없이 믿었기에 그것을 더더욱 즐겼다. 그녀는 결코 가난을 이유로 누군가를 외면하지는 않았을 것이다. 대야에 밥을 받아먹을 만큼 영락한 드 브레이시 집안 사람이라면 과장할 가치가 있는 비애의 본보기로 여겼을 테고, 그의 귀족적인 악덕에도 질겁하지 않았으리라. 하지만 천박한 부자들을 향한 그녀의 감정은 일종의 종교적 증오였다. 그들은 아마도 높은 소매가로 그 돈을 다 벌었을 텐데, 캐드월러더 부인은 목사관에서 현물로 지불하지 않는 모든 것의 높은 가격을 혐오했던 것이다. 그런 자들은 신이 세상을 만들 때 의도한 설계의 일부가 아니었고, 그들의 말투는 귀에 고통스러운 것이었다. 그런 괴물들이 우글거리는 도시란 점잖은 우주의 체계에서는 고려할 가치도 없는, 일종의 저급한 희극에 지나지 않았다. 캐드월러더 부인에게 가혹하게 굴고 싶은 부인이 있다면, 자기 자신의 아름다운 견해가 얼마나 포용적인지 따져 보고, 그것이 자기와 함께 살아가는 영광을 누리는 모든 삶을 받아들일 자리를 마련해 주는지 분명히 확인해 보시라.\n\n인 가까이 다가오는 것은 무엇이든 자기에게 맞는 형태로 깨물어 버리는, 인처럼 활동적인 그런 정신을 지닌 캐드월러더 부인이 어떻게 브룩 자매와 그들의 결혼 전망을 자신과 무관한 일로 느낄 수 있었겠는가? 더구나 그녀는 더없이 다정한 솔직함으로 브룩 씨를 꾸짖고, 은밀히 그가 변변찮은 인간이라 생각한다는 것을 알려 주는 일을 여러 해 동안 습관처럼 해 왔으니 말이다. 이 젊은 아가씨들이 티프턴에 처음 왔을 때부터 그녀는 도러시아와 제임스 경의 결혼을 미리 짜 두었고, 만약 그것이 성사되었더라면 그것이 자기가 한 일이라고 확신했을 것이다. 미리 구상해 둔 그 일이 성사되지 않게 되자, 그녀는 모든 사색가가 공감할 만한 짜증을 느꼈다. 그녀는 티프턴과 프레시트의 외교관이었으니, 무슨 일이든 자기 뜻을 거슬러 일어나는 것은 불쾌한 비정상이었다. 브룩 양의 이런 변덕으로 말하자면, 캐드월러더 부인은 도무지 참을 수가 없었고, 이제 이 처녀에 대한 자신의 평가가 남편의 무른 자비심에 다소 물들었었다는 것을 깨달았다. 저 감리교도 같은 변덕, 목사와 부목사를 합친 것보다 더 신앙심 깊은 척하는 태도는, 그녀가 믿고 싶었던 것보다 더 깊고 더 체질적인 병에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 캐드월러더 부인은 처음에는 혼잣말로, 나중에는 남편에게 말했다. \"나는 그 애를 포기하겠어요. 제임스 경과 결혼했더라면 그 애도 제정신의 분별 있는 여자가 될 가능성이 있었지요. 그분은 절대 그 애 말을 반박하지 않았을 테고, 여자란 반박당하지 않으면 자기 황당한 짓을 고집할 동기가 없는 법이니까요. 하지만 이제는 그 애가 고행자의 거친 셔츠를 입게 된 걸 축복해 줘야겠네요.\"\n\n그리하여 캐드월러더 부인은 제임스 경을 위해 또 다른 짝을 정해야 했고, 그것이 동생 브룩 양이어야 한다고 마음을 굳힌 이상, 그가 실리아의 마음에 깊은 인상을 남겼다고 준남작에게 넌지시 일러 준 것보다 더 솜씨 좋은 수는 없었을 것이다. 왜냐하면 그는 가장 높은 가지에서 비웃는, 손에 닿지 않는 사포의 사과를 애타게 그리워하는 그런 신사가 아니었기 때문이다. 그 매력이란\n\n\"절벽 위의 앵초 꽃송이처럼 미소 짓되,\n기꺼이 뻗은 손으로는 닿을 수 없는 것.\"\n\n그는 쓸 소네트도 없었고, 자기가 마음에 둔 여인에게 자신이 선택받지 못했다는 사실이 그에게 기분 좋게 와닿을 리도 없었다. 도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 골랐다는 것을 안 것만으로도 이미 그의 애정은 멍이 들고 그 힘이 느슨해진 터였다. 제임스 경은 사냥을 즐기는 사람이었으나, 여자에 대해서는 뇌조나 여우에 대한 것과는 다른 감정을 품고 있었고, 미래의 아내를 주로 추격의 흥분 때문에 가치 있는 사냥감으로 여기지도 않았다. 또한 그는 원시 부족의 풍습에 정통하여, 말하자면 도끼를 손에 들고 그녀를 두고 이상적인 결투를 벌이는 것이 결혼이라는 결속의 역사적 연속성에 필요하다고 느낄 만큼은 아니었다. 오히려 그는 우리를 우리에게 호의를 품은 사람과 이어 주고 무심한 사람으로부터 멀어지게 하는 그 사랑스러운 허영심을 지녔고, 더불어 감사할 줄 아는 선량한 천성을 지녔기에, 한 여인이 자신에게 호감을 품고 있다는 생각만으로도 그의 마음에서 그녀의 마음을 향해 가느다란 다정함의 실들이 자아져 나왔다.\n\n그리하여 제임스 경은 티프턴 그레인지에서 멀어지는 방향으로 반 시간쯤 제법 빠르게 말을 달린 끝에 속도를 늦추었고, 마침내 지름길로 되돌아가게 해 줄 길로 접어들게 되었다. 여러 감정이 그의 안에서 작용하여, 결국 아무 새로운 일도 일어나지 않은 것처럼 오늘 그레인지를 찾아가기로 결심하게 만들었다. 자신이 청혼을 했다가 거절당한 적이 없다는 것이 다행스럽게 여겨지는 마음을 그는 어쩔 수 없었다. 단순한 우호적 예의만으로도 오두막집들 문제로 도러시아를 만나러 가야 마땅했고, 이제 다행히도 캐드월러더 부인 덕분에 필요하다면 너무 어색해 보이지 않고 축하의 말을 건넬 채비가 되어 있었다. 사실 그는 마음이 편치 않았다. 도러시아를 단념하는 것은 그에게 무척 고통스러운 일이었다. 하지만 이 방문을 당장 해치우고 온갖 감정의 기색을 억누르겠다는 결심에는 일종의 줄칼질 같은, 자극에는 자극으로 맞서는 무언가가 있었다. 그리고 그 충동을 뚜렷이 자각하지는 못했지만, 실리아가 거기 있으리라는 것, 그리고 전보다 그녀에게 더 관심을 기울이게 되리라는 의식이 분명 그의 안에 자리 잡고 있었다.\n\n우리 인간은, 남자든 여자든, 아침과 저녁 식사 시간 사이에 숱한 실망을 삼킨다. 눈물을 참고 입술 언저리를 약간 창백하게 한 채, 무슨 일이냐는 물음에는 \"오, 아무것도 아니에요!\"라고 대답한다. 자존심이 우리를 돕는다. 그리고 자존심이란 그저 우리 자신의 상처를 감추라고만 부추길 뿐 남에게 상처 주지 말라고 한다면, 결코 나쁜 것이 아니다.\n\n예상할 수 있듯이, 캐소본 씨는 이 몇 주 동안 그레인지에서 많은 시간을 보냈고, 구애가 그의 위대한 저작—모든 신화의 열쇠—의 진척에 끼치는 방해는 자연히 구애의 행복한 종결을 한층 더 간절히 고대하게 만들었다. 하지만 그는 그 방해를 의도적으로 자초한 것이었으니, 이제 여성의 동반이라는 우아함으로 자신의 삶을 꾸밀 때가 되었다고, 학구적 노동의 틈새에 피로가 드리우기 쉬운 우울을 여성적 공상의 유희로 밝힐 때가 되었다고, 그리하여 이 절정의 나이에 노년을 위한 여성의 보살핌이라는 위안을 확보할 때가 되었다고 마음을 정했기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 감정의 물결에 몸을 맡기기로 결심했는데, 아마도 그 물결이 얼마나 지극히 얕은 실개천인지를 발견하고는 놀랐을 것이다. 가뭄이 든 지방에서는 침례를 상징적으로만 행할 수 있듯이, 캐소본 씨는 자신의 시냇물이 베풀어 줄 수 있는, 뛰어듦에 가장 근접한 행위가 물을 뿌리는 정도임을 깨달았다. 그리하여 그는 시인들이 남성적 열정의 힘을 크게 과장했다고 결론지었다. 그럼에도 그는 브룩 양이 결혼에 대한 자신의 가장 흡족한 예상을 채워 줄 듯한 열렬하고 순종적인 애정을 보이는 것을 흐뭇하게 지켜보았다. 그가 몸을 맡긴 정도가 그토록 절제된 까닭을 설명해 줄 어떤 결함이 도로테아에게 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 한두 번 스친 적은 있었다. 그러나 그는 그 결함을 가려낼 수도, 자신을 더 기쁘게 해 줄 만한 여인을 머릿속에 그려 볼 수도 없었다. 그러니 인간이 물려받은 전통의 과장 말고는 의지할 만한 이유가 분명 없었다.\n\n“이제 제가 좀 더 쓸모 있는 사람이 되도록 준비할 수는 없을까요?” 구애가 시작된 초기의 어느 날 아침, 도로테아가 그에게 말했다. “밀턴의 딸들이 자기 아버지에게 그랬듯이, 읽는 내용을 이해하지 못하더라도 라틴어와 그리스어를 소리 내어 읽어 드리는 법을 배울 수는 없을까요?”\n\n“그건 당신에게 지루한 일일까 봐 걱정스럽군요.” 캐소본 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. “게다가, 내 기억이 옳다면, 당신이 언급한 그 젊은 여인들은 모르는 언어로 하는 그 작업을 시인에게 반항할 빌미로 여겼지요.”\n\n“네, 하지만 우선 그들은 아주 못된 처녀들이었어요. 그렇지 않았다면 그런 아버지를 섬기는 것을 자랑스러워했을 테니까요. 그리고 둘째로, 그들은 따로 공부해서 읽는 내용을 이해하도록 스스로를 가르칠 수도 있었을 거예요. 그랬다면 흥미로웠겠지요. 제가 못되고 어리석은 사람이 되리라고 기대하시는 건 아니겠지요?”\n\n“나는 당신이 빼어난 젊은 숙녀가 삶의 모든 가능한 관계 속에서 될 수 있는 그 모든 것이 되기를 기대합니다. 확실히 당신이 그리스 문자를 베껴 쓸 수 있다면 큰 도움이 되겠지요. 그러려면 약간의 읽기부터 시작하는 것이 좋겠습니다.”\n\n도로테아는 이것을 소중한 허락으로 붙들었다. 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게 당장 그 언어들을 가르쳐 달라고 청하지는 않았을 것이다. 무엇보다 도움이 되기는커녕 성가신 존재가 될까 봐 두려웠기 때문이다. 하지만 그녀가 라틴어와 그리스어를 알고 싶어 한 것이 전적으로 미래의 남편에 대한 헌신 때문만은 아니었다. 남성적 지식의 그 영역들은 그녀에게 모든 진리를 더 진실되게 바라볼 수 있는 발판처럼 여겨졌다. 사실 그녀는 자신의 무지를 느꼈기에 끊임없이 자신의 결론을 의심했다. 고전을 아는 사람들이 오두막에 대한 무관심과 신의 영광에 대한 열의를 조화시킬 줄 아는 것처럼 보이는데, 방 하나짜리 오두막이 신의 영광을 위한 것이 아니라고 어떻게 확신할 수 있겠는가? 사물의 핵심에 이르러 기독교인의 사회적 의무를 건전하게 판단하려면 어쩌면 히브리어조차 필요할지도 몰랐다—적어도 알파벳과 어근 몇 개쯤은. 그리고 그녀는 현명한 남편을 둔 것으로 만족할 만한 그런 체념의 경지에는 이르지 못했다. 가엾은 아이여, 그녀는 자기 자신이 현명해지기를 바랐다. 브룩 양은 그 알려진 영민함에도 불구하고 분명 매우 순진했다. 결코 너무 뛰어나다고 여겨진 적 없는 머리를 가진 실리아는 다른 사람들의 허세가 텅 비어 있음을 훨씬 더 쉽게 알아보았다. 대체로 감정이 적은 것이야말로 어떤 특정한 경우에 지나치게 많이 느끼지 않도록 막아 주는 유일한 안전장치인 듯하다.\n\n어쨌든 캐소본 씨는 한 시간씩 함께 듣고 가르치는 일에 동의했으니, 마치 어린 소년들을 가르치는 교사처럼, 아니 그보다는 연인처럼, 그에게는 사랑하는 여인의 초보적인 무지와 어려움이 가슴 뭉클하게 어울리는 것이었다. 이런 상황에서라면 알파벳 가르치기를 싫어할 학자는 거의 없으리라. 하지만 도로테아 자신은 자신의 어리석음에 다소 충격을 받고 낙담했으며, 그리스어 강세의 가치에 관해 머뭇거리며 던진 몇몇 질문에 돌아온 대답은, 정말로 여기에는 여자의 이성으로는 설명할 수 없는 비밀이 있을지도 모른다는 괴로운 의혹을 그녀에게 안겨 주었다.\n\n브룩 씨는 그 점에 대해서는 아무런 의심이 없었고, 읽기 수업이 진행되던 어느 날 서재에 들어와서는 평소의 단호함으로 자신의 견해를 표명했다.\n\n“그런데 말이오, 캐소본, 그런 깊은 학문, 고전이니 수학이니 하는 그런 것들은 여자에게는 너무 과한 부담이라오—너무 과해요, 알다시피.”\n\n“도로테아는 그저 문자를 읽는 법을 배우고 있을 뿐입니다.” 캐소본 씨가 질문을 비껴가며 말했다. “내 눈을 아껴 주려는 아주 사려 깊은 생각을 해 준 거지요.”\n\n“아, 그래요, 이해하지는 못한 채로 말이지, 알다시피—그거라면 그리 나쁘진 않겠군. 하지만 여성의 정신에는 가벼움이 있어요—툭 건드리고 지나가는 식이지—음악이니 미술이니 하는 그런 것들 말이오—그런 것들은 어느 정도까지는 공부해야지, 여자들은. 하지만 가볍게, 알다시피. 여자라면 자리에 앉아서 멋진 옛 영국 곡조 하나쯤 연주하거나 노래할 줄 알아야지. 내가 좋아하는 게 바로 그거요. 하긴 나도 웬만한 건 다 들어 봤소—빈에서 오페라도 봤지. 글루크, 모차르트, 뭐 그런 부류 다 말이오. 하지만 음악에 관해서라면 나는 보수주의자라오—그건 사상하고는 다르거든, 알다시피. 나는 좋은 옛 곡조를 고수하지.”\n\n“캐소본 씨는 피아노를 좋아하지 않으세요. 그리고 저는 그분이 좋아하지 않으셔서 무척 기뻐요.” 도로테아가 말했다. 그 음울한 시대에 가정 음악과 여성적 미술이라는 것이 주로 자잘하게 댕그랑거리고 어설프게 칠해 대는 것으로 이루어져 있었음을 감안한다면, 그녀가 그것들을 대수롭지 않게 여긴 것은 용서받아야 할 일이다. 그녀는 미소 지으며 고마운 눈빛으로 약혼자를 올려다보았다. 만약 그가 늘 「여름의 마지막 장미」를 연주하라고 청해 왔다면, 그녀는 상당한 인내심이 필요했을 것이다. “로윅에는 낡은 하프시코드 한 대밖에 없는데, 그것도 책으로 뒤덮여 있다고 하시네요.”\n\n“아, 거기서는 네가 실리아만 못하구나, 얘야. 실리아는 아주 어여쁘게 연주하고, 언제든 기꺼이 연주하거든. 하지만 캐소본이 좋아하지 않으니, 너는 괜찮겠다. 그래도 자네가 그런 자그마한 오락을 누리지 못한다니 안타깝군, 캐소본. 활시위를 늘 팽팽하게 당겨 두는 일은—뭐 그런 거 말이오, 알다시피—좋지 않아요.”\n\n“가락 맞춰 내는 소음에 귀가 시달리는 일을 나는 도무지 오락으로 여길 수가 없습니다.” 캐소본 씨가 말했다. “여러 번 되풀이되는 곡조는 내 머릿속의 단어들이 박자를 맞추느라 일종의 미뉴에트를 추게 만드는 우스꽝스러운 효과를 내지요—소년기를 지난 뒤로는 견디기 힘든 효과라고 봅니다. 엄숙한 의식을 동반할 만한, 그리고 고대의 관념에 따라 교육적 영향력으로 기능할 만한 더 웅장한 형식의 음악에 대해서라면 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다. 그것은 지금 우리가 직접 관련된 문제가 아니니까요.”\n\n“그렇지요. 하지만 그런 종류의 음악이라면 저도 즐길 것 같아요.” 도로테아가 말했다. “로잔에서 집으로 돌아오던 길에 삼촌이 저희를 프라이베르크의 그 거대한 오르간 소리를 들으러 데려가 주셨는데, 저는 그만 흐느껴 울었어요.”\n\n“그런 건 건강에 좋지 않다, 얘야.” 브룩 씨가 말했다. “캐소본, 이 아이는 이제 자네 손에 맡겨졌네. 자네가 내 조카에게 매사를 좀 더 차분하게 받아들이도록 가르쳐 줘야 해, 안 그러냐, 도로테아?”\n\n그는 조카의 마음을 상하게 할 생각은 없이 미소로 말을 맺었지만, 그녀가 체텀의 이야기는 들으려고도 하지 않으니, 캐소본처럼 진중한 사람과 일찌감치 결혼하는 편이 어쩌면 그녀에게 더 나을지도 모른다고 진심으로 생각하고 있었다.\n\n“하지만 놀라운 일이야.” 그는 발을 끌며 방을 나서면서 혼잣말을 했다. “그 애가 저 사람을 좋아하게 되었다니 놀라운 일이지. 그래도 좋은 짝이야. 캐드월래더 부인이 뭐라고 하든, 이 일을 막았다면 나는 분수에 넘치는 짓을 한 셈이 됐을 거야. 캐소본은 십중팔구 주교가 될 사람이지. 가톨릭 문제에 관한 그 소책자는 아주 시의적절했어—적어도 주임 사제 자리는 받을 거야. 사람들이 그에게 주임 사제 자리쯤은 빚지고 있는 셈이지.”\n\n여기서 나는 철학적 성찰력에 대한 권리를 입증해야겠다. 브룩 씨는 이 자리에서, 훗날 주교들의 수입에 관해 자신이 하게 될 급진적 연설을 거의 떠올리지 못했음을 지적함으로써 말이다. 어느 우아한 역사가가 자신의 영웅들이 세계의 역사를, 심지어 자기 자신의 행동조차 예견하지 못했다는 사실을 짚어 줄 인상적인 기회를 놓치겠는가?—이를테면 나바르의 앙리가 신교도 갓난아기였을 때 가톨릭 군주가 되리라고는 거의 생각지 못했다거나, 알프레드 대왕이 타오르는 촛불로 자신의 고된 밤을 측정하던 시절에 훗날의 신사들이 회중시계로 자신의 한가한 낮을 측정하리라고는 짐작도 못 했다는 사실 말이다. 여기 진리의 광맥이 있으니, 아무리 힘차게 캐낸들 우리의 석탄보다 더 오래 남을 법한 것이다.\n\n하지만 브룩 씨에 대해 나는 선례로 정당화되기에는 다소 미흡할지도 모를 한 가지 견해를 더 덧붙이려 한다—즉, 그가 자신의 연설을 미리 알았더라도 큰 차이는 없었으리라는 것이다. 조카의 남편이 막대한 성직 수입을 누리게 되리라는 것을 흐뭇하게 생각하는 일은 별개의 문제였고, 자유주의적 연설을 하는 일은 또 다른 문제였다. 하나의 주제를 여러 관점에서 바라보지 못하는 것은 좁은 마음이니 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII.\n\n신사들 하나하나를 후견인으로 두어야 마땅하니.」\n\n도러시아를 다른 남자와 약혼한 여인으로 처음 마주하는 곤란을 일단 한 번 겪고 난 뒤에도, 제임스 체텀 경은 자신이 여전히 그레인지를 즐겨 찾는다는 사실이 스스로도 놀라웠다. 물론 처음 그녀에게 다가갔을 때는 갈라진 번개가 온몸을 관통하는 듯했고, 면담 내내 그는 불안을 감추고 있다는 의식을 떨치지 못했다. 그러나 선량한 그였지만, 그의 불안은 만약 자신의 연적이 눈부시고 탐낼 만한 혼처라고 여겼더라면 느꼈을 것보다 덜했음을 인정해야 한다. 그는 캐소본 씨에게 빛이 가려진다는 느낌이 전혀 없었으니, 다만 도러시아가 우울한 환상에 사로잡혀 있다는 사실에 충격을 받았을 뿐이며, 그의 굴욕감은 연민과 뒤섞임으로써 그 쓰라림을 다소 덜어 냈다.\n\n그렇지만 제임스 경은 스스로에게 자신이 그녀를 완전히 단념했노라고 말하면서도—명백히 적합하고 자연의 이치에 부합하는 청혼을 데스데모나 같은 심술로 받아들이지 않았으니—캐소본 씨와의 약혼이라는 생각 앞에서 아직은 도무지 무심할 수 없었다. 지금 알게 된 사실에 비추어 두 사람을 처음으로 함께 본 날, 그는 자신이 그 일을 충분히 진지하게 받아들이지 않았던 것 같다는 생각이 들었다. 브룩은 정말로 책임이 있었으니, 그가 그 일을 막았어야 했다. 누가 그에게 말할 수 있을까? 어쩌면 지금이라도 무언가 할 수 있을지 몰랐으니, 적어도 결혼을 미루는 일이라도. 집으로 돌아가는 길에 그는 목사관에 들러 캐드월래더 씨를 찾았다. 다행히 목사는 집에 있었고, 손님은 온갖 낚시 도구가 걸려 있는 서재로 안내되었다. 하지만 목사 본인은 그 옆에 딸린 작은 방에서 선반(旋盤) 장치로 일을 하고 있었고, 그는 준남작에게 그쪽으로 오라고 불렀다. 두 사람은 그 지방의 다른 어떤 지주와 성직자보다도 사이가 좋은 친구였으니—이는 그들 얼굴에 어린 온화한 표정과 잘 어울리는 의미심장한 사실이었다.\n\n캐드월래더 씨는 도톰한 입술에 다정한 미소를 띤 덩치 큰 남자였다. 겉모습은 무척 수수하고 거칠었으나, 전염성이 있는 그 단단하고 흔들림 없는 여유와 쾌활함을 지니고 있어서, 햇살 속의 광활한 풀 언덕처럼, 짜증 난 이기심마저 가라앉히고 오히려 스스로 부끄러워하게 만드는 데가 있었다. 「자, 잘 지냈소?」 그가 차마 맞잡기에는 어울리지 않는 손을 내보이며 말했다. 「아까 못 만나서 미안하오. 무슨 특별한 일이라도 있소? 언짢아 보이는구려.」\n\n제임스 경의 이마에는 작은 주름이 잡혀 있었고, 눈썹이 살짝 처져 있었는데, 그는 대답하면서 그것을 일부러 과장하는 듯했다.\n\n「그저 브룩의 처사 때문이오. 정말이지 누군가 그에게 한마디 해야 할 것 같소.」\n\n「뭐 말이오? 출마하겠다는 거 말이오?」 캐드월래더 씨가 방금 돌리던 낚싯줄 얼레를 정리하며 말했다. 「난 그가 진심이라곤 별로 생각지 않소. 하지만 본인이 좋다는데 무슨 해가 되겠소? 휘그당을 못마땅하게 여기는 사람이라면 휘그당이 가장 강한 자를 내세우지 않을 때 오히려 반가워해야지. 우리 친구 브룩의 머리를 공성 망치 삼아서야 헌정을 뒤엎지는 못할 테니.」\n\n「오, 그 말이 아니오.」 제임스 경이 모자를 내려놓고 의자에 몸을 던지더니, 다리를 끌어안고는 부츠 밑창을 무척 씁쓸한 표정으로 살피기 시작하면서 말했다. 「이 결혼 말이오. 그가 그 한창 피어나는 어린 처녀를 캐소본과 결혼하게 내버려 두는 것 말이오.」\n\n「캐소본이 뭐가 어떻다는 거요? 난 그에게서 아무 해도 못 보겠는데—처녀가 그를 좋아한다면야.」\n\n「그녀는 자기가 뭘 좋아하는지 알기엔 너무 어리오. 후견인이 나서야지. 이런 식으로 무턱대고 일을 해치우도록 내버려 둬서는 안 되오. 당신 같은 사람이, 캐드월래더—딸을 둔 사람이, 이 일을 그토록 무심하게 바라볼 수 있다니 놀랍소. 게다가 당신처럼 마음씨 좋은 사람이! 진지하게 한번 생각해 보시오.」\n\n「농담하는 게 아니오. 나는 더없이 진지하오.」 목사가 사람을 약 올리는 작은 속웃음을 흘리며 말했다. 「당신은 엘리너만큼이나 못 말리겠소. 그 사람도 내게 브룩한테 가서 훈계를 좀 하라고 졸랐다오. 그래서 내가 일깨워 줬지, 그 사람 친구들은 그가 나와 결혼해서 맺은 인연을 아주 형편없는 혼처로 여겼다고 말이오.」\n\n「하지만 캐소본을 좀 보시오.」 제임스 경이 분개하며 말했다. 「틀림없이 쉰은 됐을 거고, 한 번이라도 사내의 그림자 이상이었던 적이 있었을지 의심스럽소. 그 다리를 좀 보시오!」\n\n「잘난 미남 청년들 같으니라고! 자네들은 세상만사가 제 뜻대로 되리라 생각하지. 자네들은 여자를 몰라. 여자들은 자네들이 스스로를 흠모하는 것의 절반만큼도 자네들을 흠모하지 않아. 엘리너는 제 자매들한테 늘 그러더군, 내 못생긴 얼굴 때문에 나와 결혼했다고—그게 어찌나 가지각색이고 재미있었던지 신중함이 아주 무너져 버렸다나.」\n\n「당신이야! 여자가 당신을 사랑하기란 쉬웠지. 하지만 이건 미모의 문제가 아니오. 난 캐소본이 마음에 들지 않소.」 이것이 어떤 사내의 인품을 나쁘게 본다는 뜻을 내비치는 제임스 경의 가장 강한 표현이었다.\n\n「왜요? 그에 대해 뭐 안 좋은 거라도 아시오?」 목사가 얼레를 내려놓고는, 주의를 기울이는 태도로 엄지손가락을 겨드랑이 안쪽에 끼우며 말했다.\n\n제임스 경은 말을 멈추었다. 그는 평소 자기 이유를 대는 일이 쉽지 않았으니, 자신은 그저 이치에 맞는 바를 느낄 뿐인데 사람들이 굳이 말하지 않아도 그것을 알지 못한다는 게 이상하게 여겨졌다. 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n「자, 캐드월래더, 그에게 도대체 마음이란 게 있소?」\n\n「글쎄, 있지요. 눈 녹듯 무르녹는 그런 종류는 아니지만, 속알맹이는 멀쩡하다오, 그건 장담해도 좋소. 그는 가난한 친척들에게 무척 잘하오. 여자 몇몇에게는 생활비를 대 주고, 한 젊은이는 적잖은 비용을 들여 교육시키고 있소. 캐소본은 자기 정의감에 따라 행동하는 사람이오. 그의 이모가 잘못된 결혼을 했는데—폴란드 사람이었던 것 같소—몸을 망쳤지요, 어쨌든 집안에서 의절당했소. 그 일이 아니었다면 캐소본은 지금 가진 재산의 절반도 갖지 못했을 거요. 그는 친히 사촌들을 찾아 나서서 자기가 그들을 위해 뭘 할 수 있는지 알아봤다고 하오. 쇠붙이를 두드려 보면, 누구나 그렇게 좋은 소리를 내진 못하오. 당신이라면 그러겠지, 체텀. 하지만 누구나 그렇지는 않다오.」\n\n「모르겠소.」 제임스 경이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 「나 자신도 그리 자신은 없소.」 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. 「그건 캐소본이 한 옳은 일이오. 하지만 사람이 옳은 일을 하고자 하면서도 일종의 양피지 법전 같은 존재일 수도 있소. 여자가 그와 행복하지 못할 수도 있단 말이오. 그리고 내 생각엔 브룩 양처럼 어린 처녀라면, 어리석은 짓을 못 하도록 친구들이 좀 나서야 마땅하오. 당신은 내가 사사로운 감정이 있어서 그러는 줄 알고 웃는구려. 하지만 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 그게 아니오. 내가 브룩 양의 오라비나 삼촌이라 해도 똑같이 느꼈을 거요.」\n\n「그럼, 그래서 당신이라면 어떻게 하겠소?」\n\n「그녀가 성년이 될 때까지는 결혼을 결정해서는 안 된다고 하겠소. 그리고 분명히 말하건대, 그렇게 되면 결혼은 결코 성사되지 않을 거요. 당신도 나처럼 봐 주면 좋겠소—브룩에게 이 일을 이야기해 주면 좋겠소.」\n\n제임스 경은 문장을 마치며 자리에서 일어났으니, 캐드월래더 부인이 서재 쪽에서 들어오는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그녀는 다섯 살쯤 된 막내딸의 손을 잡고 있었는데, 아이는 곧장 아빠에게로 달려가 무릎 위에 편안히 자리를 잡았다.\n\n「무슨 얘기를 나누는지 들었어요.」 아내가 말했다. 「하지만 험프리한테는 아무런 영향도 못 줄 거예요. 물고기가 미끼를 물어 주기만 하면, 그이한테는 누구나 마땅히 있어야 할 모습 그대로거든요. 세상에, 캐소본은 송어가 사는 개울을 가졌으면서도 거기서 손수 낚시할 생각은 안 한다지요. 이보다 더 좋은 사람이 어디 있겠어요?」\n\n「글쎄, 그 말에도 일리가 있소.」 목사가 조용한 속웃음을 지으며 말했다. 「송어 개울을 가졌다는 건 사람에게 아주 좋은 자질이지.」\n\n「하지만 진지하게 말해서.」 아직 분이 다 풀리지 않은 제임스 경이 말했다. 「목사님이 한마디 하면 무슨 도움이 되지 않겠소?」\n\n「오, 그이가 무슨 말을 할지 내가 미리 일러 줬잖아요.」 캐드월래더 부인이 눈썹을 치켜올리며 대꾸했다. 「난 할 수 있는 건 다 했어요. 이 결혼에서 난 손을 떼겠어요.」\n\n「우선 말이오.」 목사가 다소 진지한 표정으로 말했다. 「내가 브룩을 설득해서 그대로 움직이게 할 수 있으리라 기대하는 건 어불성설이오. 브룩은 아주 좋은 친구지만, 물렁물렁하단 말이오. 어떤 틀에든 흘러 들어가지만, 모양을 그대로 지키지는 못하지.」\n\n「결혼을 미룰 만큼만은 모양을 지킬 수도 있잖소.」 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n「하지만, 체텀, 내가 브룩 양에게 이로운 쪽으로 행동하는 거라고 지금보다 훨씬 더 확신하지 않는 한, 어째서 내가 내 영향력을 캐소본에게 불리하게 써야 한단 말이오? 난 캐소본에게서 아무 잘못도 모르오. 나는 그의 크시수트로스니 피포펌이니 하는 것들에는 관심 없소. 하지만 그렇다고 그가 내 낚시 도구에 관심을 두는 것도 아니지 않소. 가톨릭 문제에서 그가 취한 입장은 뜻밖이었지만, 그는 늘 내게 예의 발랐고, 그러니 내가 어째서 그의 즐거움을 망쳐야 하는지 모르겠소. 내가 알 수 있는 한, 브룩 양은 다른 어떤 남자와 사는 것보다 그와 함께 더 행복할지도 모르오.」\n\n「험프리! 당신한텐 정말 인내심이 바닥나요. 당신은 캐소본과 단둘이 식사하느니 차라리 울타리 밑에서 먹는 편이 낫다고 여기잖아요. 두 사람은 서로 할 말이 하나도 없으면서.」\n\n「그게 브룩 양이 그와 결혼하는 것과 무슨 상관이오? 그녀가 내 즐거움을 위해 결혼하는 것도 아니잖소.」\n\n「그자 몸에는 좋은 붉은 피라곤 한 방울도 없소.」 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n「없고말고요. 누가 한 방울을 확대경 아래 놓고 봤더니 온통 세미콜론하고 괄호뿐이었다지요.」 캐드월래더 부인이 말했다.\n\n「결혼할 게 아니라 어째서 자기 책을 내놓지 않는 거요?」 제임스 경이, 영국 평신도다운 건전한 감정으로 정당하다 여기는 혐오감을 담아 말했다.\n\n「오, 그이는 각주를 꿈꾸고, 그 각주들이 그이의 머릿속을 통째로 끌고 달아나 버린답니다. 어릴 적에 「엄지 동자」 이야기의 개요를 만들었다나요, 그 뒤로 줄곧 개요만 만들고 있다지요. 어휴! 그런 사람과 여자가 행복할 수 있다고 험프리는 자꾸만 우긴다니까요.」\n\n「뭐, 그가 바로 브룩 양이 좋아하는 사람이오.」 목사가 말했다. 「나는 젊은 아가씨들의 취향을 죄다 이해한다고 자처하지는 않소.」\n\n「하지만 그녀가 당신 친딸이라면 어떻겠소?」 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n「그건 또 다른 문제겠지요. 그녀는 내 딸이 아니고, 나는 나서야 할 처지라고 느끼지 않소. 캐소본은 우리 대부분만큼은 괜찮은 사람이오. 학식 있는 성직자이고, 성직자 신분에 걸맞은 인물이지. 미들마치에서 어떤 급진파 친구가 일장 연설을 하면서, 캐소본은 학식 깊은 지푸라기나 써는 교구 목사요, 프리크는 벽돌과 회반죽이나 다루는 교구 목사요, 나는 낚시질이나 하는 교구 목사라고 했다오. 그런데 정말이지, 나는 그중 누가 누구보다 더 못하거나 더 낫다고는 보지 않소.」 목사는 그 소리 없는 웃음으로 말을 맺었다. 그는 언제나 자신을 겨눈 풍자의 우스운 대목을 알아챘다. 그의 양심은 나머지 모든 면이 그러하듯 넓고 느긋했으니, 아무런 수고 없이도 할 수 있는 일만 했다.\n\n분명, 캐드월래더 씨를 통해 브룩 양의 결혼에 끼어들 길은 없었다. 제임스 경은 그녀가 잘못 판단할 완벽한 자유를 누리게 되리라는 생각에 어느 정도 슬픔을 느꼈다. 그가 도러시아의 오두막 설계를 실행하려는 의도를 조금도 늦추지 않은 것은 그의 선량한 성품을 보여 주는 증표였다. 분명 이런 끈기는 그 자신의 품위를 위해서도 최선의 길이었으리라. 하지만 자긍심은 우리가 너그러워지도록 도울 뿐, 결코 우리를 너그럽게 만들지는 못하니, 허영이 우리를 재치 있게 만들지 못하는 것과 마찬가지다. 그녀는 이제 자신을 향한 제임스 경의 처지를 충분히 알게 되어, 처음에는 연인다운 호의에서 떠밀려 시작했으나 이제는 지주로서의 의무를 꿋꿋이 지켜 나가는 그의 올곧음을 높이 살 수 있었다. 그리고 거기서 얻는 그녀의 기쁨은 지금의 행복 속에서도 한몫을 차지할 만큼 컸다. 어쩌면 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게서, 아니 오히려 그 학식 있는 신사가 그녀의 영혼 속에 울려 퍼지게 한 희망찬 꿈과 흠모하는 신뢰와 열정적인 자기 헌신의 교향곡에서 떼어 낼 수 있는 만큼의 관심을 제임스 체텀 경의 오두막에 쏟았는지도 모른다. 그리하여 선량한 준남작이 잇따라 방문하는 동안, 그는 실리아에게 자잘한 관심을 기울이기 시작하면서도, 점점 더 큰 즐거움을 느끼며 도러시아와 이야기를 나누는 자신을 발견하게 되었다. 이제 그녀는 그를 향해 더없이 거리낌이 없었고 짜증도 내지 않았으니, 그는 감추거나 고백할 정념이 없는 남자와 여자 사이에 오가는 솔직한 친절과 우정에 어떤 기쁨이 깃들어 있는지를 차츰 깨달아 가고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX.\n\n질서와 완벽한 통치를 향한 것이었다.\n\n청컨대, 그런 나라들이 지금은 어디에 있는가?……\n\n제2신사. 어찌 묻는가, 그 나라들이 옛적에 있던 곳에—인간의 영혼 속에 있지 않은가.\n재산 분배에 관한 캐소본 씨의 처신은 브룩 씨에게 매우 흡족했고, 결혼의 사전 절차는 순조롭게 진행되어 구애의 시간을 단축시켰다. 약혼한 신부는 자기가 살게 될 집을 둘러보고, 거기서 고치고 싶은 점이 있으면 무엇이든 지시해야 했다. 여자가 결혼 전에 이것저것 지시하는 것은, 결혼 후에 복종에 대한 입맛을 갖기 위해서이다. 그리고 분명, 우리 남녀 인간들이 제 뜻대로 할 때 저지르는 실수들을 생각하면, 우리가 그토록 제 뜻대로 하기를 좋아한다는 사실에 적이 의아함을 품을 만도 하다.\n\n잿빛이지만 건조한 어느 11월 아침, 도러시아는 삼촌과 실리아와 함께 로윅으로 마차를 몰았다. 캐소본 씨의 집은 영주관이었다. 바로 가까이에, 정원의 어느 지점에서는 보이는 작은 교회가 있었고, 그 맞은편에는 오래된 사제관이 있었다. 캐소본 씨는 처음 성직 생활을 시작했을 무렵에는 그 성직록만을 받았으나, 형의 죽음으로 영주관까지 소유하게 되었다. 거기에는 작은 사슴 공원이 딸려 있어 여기저기 멋진 고목 떡갈나무가 서 있었고, 남서쪽 정면을 향해 보리수 가로수길이 나 있었으며, 공원과 정원 사이에는 우묵하게 판 울타리가 있어, 응접실 창문에서 바라보면 시선이 푸른 잔디 비탈을 따라 거침없이 미끄러져 내려가다가 보리수가 끝나는 곳에서 옥수수밭과 목초지의 평지로 이어졌고, 그곳은 지는 해 아래서 흔히 호수로 녹아드는 듯 보였다. 이쪽이 그 집의 밝은 쪽이었으니, 남쪽과 동쪽은 가장 화창한 아침에도 다소 우울해 보였기 때문이다. 이쪽의 부지는 더 비좁았고, 화단은 그다지 정성 들여 가꾼 흔적이 없었으며, 주로 음울한 주목으로 이루어진 큰 나무 무더기가 창문에서 채 열 야드도 안 되는 곳에 높이 솟아 있었다. 푸르스름한 돌로 지은 건물은 옛 영국식이었는데, 보기 흉하지는 않았으나 창이 작고 우울해 보였다. 즐거운 가정처럼 보이려면 아이들과 많은 꽃과 열린 창과 밝은 것들이 펼쳐지는 자그마한 전망이 반드시 있어야 할 그런 종류의 집이었다. 햇빛 한 점 없는 고요 속에서 노랗게 남은 몇 안 되는 잎들이 짙은 상록수 사이로 천천히 비껴 떨어지는 이 늦가을 무렵, 그 집도 가을의 쇠락 같은 분위기를 띠고 있었고, 캐소본 씨가 모습을 드러냈을 때, 그 배경을 받아 도드라질 만한 생기는 그에게 조금도 없었다.\n\n\"어머나!\" 실리아는 속으로 말했다. \"이보다는 프레시트 홀이 훨씬 더 기분 좋았을 텐데.\" 그녀는 하얀 사암과 기둥이 늘어선 주랑 현관과 꽃이 가득한 테라스를 떠올렸고, 그 위에서 마치 장미 덤불의 마법에서 풀려나온 왕자처럼 미소 짓는 제임스 경을, 그리고 더없이 향기로운 꽃잎에서 순식간에 손수건으로 바뀌어 나오는 모습을 떠올렸다. 늘 학문 따위가 아니라 상식이 담긴 이야기를, 그토록 유쾌하게 들려주는 제임스 경을! 실리아는 진지하고 풍상에 닳은 신사들이 때때로 아내에게서 선호하는, 그 가볍고 어린 여성적인 취향을 지니고 있었다. 하지만 다행히도 캐소본 씨의 성향은 달랐으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그는 실리아에게는 가망이 없었을 것이다.\n\n도러시아는 반대로, 그 집과 부지가 자기가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이라고 여겼다. 기다란 서재의 어두운 책장들, 세월에 색이 가라앉은 양탄자와 커튼, 복도 벽에 걸린 진기한 옛 지도와 조감도들, 그리고 그 아래 여기저기 놓인 오래된 꽃병들은 그녀에게 아무런 답답함도 주지 않았고, 오래전 삼촌이 여행에서 가져온 그레인지의 석고상이며 그림들보다 더 명랑해 보였다. 그것들은 아마 삼촌이 한때 받아들였던 여러 관념들에 속한 것이리라. 가엾은 도러시아에게 그 엄숙한 고전적 나신상들과 능글맞게 웃는 르네상스풍의 코레조 같은 것들은 그녀의 청교도적 관념 한복판을 빤히 노려보는, 고통스러우리만치 이해할 수 없는 것이었으니, 그녀는 그것들을 자기 삶과 어떤 식으로든 관련지을 수 있는 방법을 한 번도 배운 적이 없었다. 그러나 로윅의 주인들은 보아하니 여행을 다닌 사람들이 아니었고, 캐소본 씨의 과거 연구도 그런 보조물의 힘을 빌려 이루어진 것이 아니었다.\n\n도러시아는 즐거운 감정에 젖어 집안을 거닐었다. 모든 것이 그녀에게는 신성해 보였다. 이곳이 그녀의 아내로서의 보금자리가 될 것이었기에, 캐소본 씨가 어떤 실제 배치를 특별히 일러 주며 고치고 싶은지 물을 때면 그녀는 신뢰가 가득한 눈으로 그를 올려다보았다. 자기 취향을 묻는 모든 청에 그녀는 고맙게 응했으나, 고칠 만한 것은 아무것도 없다고 여겼다. 정확한 예의와 격식 있는 다정함을 갖추려는 그의 노력은 그녀에게 아무런 결함도 없어 보였다. 그녀는 모든 빈자리를 드러나지 않은 완전함으로 채웠으니, 섭리의 작품을 해석하듯 그를 해석했고, 어긋나 보이는 불협화음은 자신이 더 높은 화음을 듣지 못하는 탓으로 돌렸다. 그리고 구애의 시간에는 사랑에 찬 믿음이 행복한 확신으로 채워 넣는 많은 빈자리들이 남아 있는 법이다.\n\n\"자, 사랑하는 도러시아, 어느 방을 당신의 규방으로 쓰고 싶은지 일러 주면 고맙겠소.\" 캐소본 씨는 여성의 본성에 관한 그의 견해가 그러한 요구까지 포함할 만큼 충분히 너그럽다는 것을 내보이며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각해 주시다니 정말 친절하세요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"하지만 정말이지 저는 그런 일들은 모두 정해 주시는 편이 더 좋아요. 모든 것을 있는 그대로—당신이 늘 써 오시던 그대로, 혹은 당신이 직접 정하시는 그대로 받아들이는 편이 훨씬 더 행복할 거예요. 달리 무엇을 바랄 까닭이 제겐 없답니다.\"\n\n\"어머, 도도 언니,\" 실리아가 말했다. \"위층에 그 활 모양 창이 난 방을 갖지 않을 거야?\"\n\n캐소본 씨가 그곳으로 앞장섰다. 활 모양 창은 보리수 가로수길을 내려다보고 있었고, 가구는 모두 빛바랜 푸른색이었으며, 분을 바른 머리를 한 신사 숙녀들의 세밀화가 한데 모여 걸려 있었다. 어느 문 위에 걸린 한 폭의 태피스트리에도 창백한 수사슴 한 마리가 있는 청록색 세계가 펼쳐져 있었다. 의자와 탁자는 다리가 가늘어 넘어지기 쉬웠다. 코르셋을 꽉 졸라맨 어느 부인의 유령이 자기가 수를 놓던 자리를 다시 찾아오는 모습을 상상해 봄 직한 방이었다. 가벼운 책장에는 송아지 가죽으로 장정한 12절판의 교양 문학책들이 꽂혀 있어, 가구를 마저 채우고 있었다.\n\n\"그래,\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"여기에 새 휘장과 소파 같은 것들을 좀 들이면 예쁜 방이 되겠구나. 지금은 좀 휑하고 말이야.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 삼촌,\" 도러시아가 간곡히 말했다. \"제발 무엇을 고친다는 말씀은 마세요. 세상에는 고쳐야 할 다른 것들이 너무도 많은걸요—저는 이런 것들을 있는 그대로 받아들이는 게 좋아요. 그리고 당신도 이대로가 좋으시죠, 그렇죠?\" 그녀는 캐소본 씨를 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"어쩌면 이 방은 어머님이 젊으셨을 때 쓰시던 방이었겠네요.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 그는 천천히 고개를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n\"이분이 어머님이시군요.\" 세밀화 무리를 살펴보려고 몸을 돌렸던 도러시아가 말했다. \"당신이 제게 주신 작은 그림과 닮았어요. 다만 이게 더 잘 그린 초상화 같네요. 그리고 맞은편의 이분은, 누구신가요?\"\n\n\"그분의 언니요. 두 분도 당신과 동생처럼 부모님의 자식이라곤 둘뿐이었소. 보다시피 그 위에 부모님이 걸려 계시지.\"\n\n\"언니분이 예쁘네요.\" 실리아가, 캐소본 씨의 어머니는 그만 못하다고 여기는 속내를 내비치며 말했다. 그가 한때 모두 젊었던—그 숙녀들이 목걸이를 둘렀던—집안 출신이라는 사실은, 실리아의 상상력에 새로운 문을 열어 주었다.\n\n\"독특한 얼굴이네요.\" 도러시아가 가까이 들여다보며 말했다. \"다소 가까이 붙은 저 짙은 잿빛 눈동자며—잔물결 같은 게 진 섬세하고 고르지 않은 코며—뒤로 넘긴 분 바른 곱슬머리며. 통틀어 보면 예쁘다기보다는 독특해 보여요. 어머님과는 가족이라 할 만한 닮은 데조차 없네요.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그리고 두 분은 운명도 닮지 않았소.\"\n\n\"이분에 대해선 제게 말씀해 주신 적이 없으셨어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"내 이모는 불행한 결혼을 했소. 나는 그분을 한 번도 뵌 적이 없소.\"\n\n도러시아는 조금 의아했으나, 그 순간 캐소본 씨가 자청해서 말하지 않은 것을 더 캐묻는 것은 무례한 일이라 느꼈고, 그래서 창가로 돌아서서 전망을 감탄하며 바라보았다. 어느새 햇살이 잿빛 하늘을 꿰뚫고 나와, 보리수 가로수길에 그림자를 드리우고 있었다.\n\n\"이제 정원을 거닐까요?\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"교회도 보고 싶을 거다, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"익살맞은 작은 교회지. 그리고 마을도 말이야. 모든 게 호두 껍데기 안에 들어앉은 듯 자그마하단다. 그건 그렇고, 네 마음에 들 거다, 도러시아. 오두막들이 양로원 줄지어 선 것 같거든—작은 정원이며 비단향꽃무며, 그런 것들 말이다.\"\n\n\"네, 부탁드려요.\" 도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 바라보며 말했다. \"그 모든 걸 보고 싶어요.\" 그녀는 그에게서 로윅의 오두막들에 관해 \"나쁘지 않다\"는 말보다 더 생생한 이야기를 들은 적이 없었다.\n\n이내 그들은 주로 풀밭 가장자리와 나무 무더기 사이로 난 자갈길에 들어섰는데, 캐소본 씨의 말로는 이것이 교회로 가는 가장 가까운 길이었다. 교회 묘지로 통하는 작은 문 앞에서, 캐소본 씨가 바로 옆 사제관으로 열쇠를 가지러 간 동안 잠시 멈춰 섰다. 조금 뒤처져 있던 실리아가, 캐소본 씨가 자리를 비운 것을 보고는 곧 다가와, 어떤 악의의 의심도 늘 부인하는 듯한 그 편안한 또박또박한 말투로 말했다—\n\n\"있잖아, 언니, 산책로 한쪽으로 올라오는 아주 젊은 사람을 봤어.\"\n\n\"그게 놀랄 일이니, 실리아?\"\n\n\"젊은 정원사가 있을지도 모르지—안 그래?\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"내가 캐소본에게 정원사를 바꾸라고 했지.\"\n\n\"아니, 정원사가 아니었어요.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"스케치북을 든 신사였어요. 연한 갈색 곱슬머리였고요. 뒷모습만 봤지만요. 그래도 꽤 젊었어요.\"\n\n\"아마 부목사의 아들이겠지.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"아, 저기 캐소본이 다시 오는구나, 터커도 함께 오고. 터커를 소개하려나 보다. 너희들은 아직 터커를 모르지.\"\n\n터커 씨는 중년의 부목사로, 대개 아들이 부족하지 않은 부류인 \"하급 성직자\" 가운데 하나였다. 그러나 소개가 끝난 뒤에도 대화는 그의 가족에 관한 어떤 물음으로도 이어지지 않았고, 그 깜짝 놀랄 만한 젊음의 환영은 실리아를 제외한 모두에게서 잊혔다. 그녀는 그 연한 갈색 곱슬머리와 호리호리한 모습이 터커 씨와 무슨 관계가 있을 리 없다고 속으로 부인했으니, 터커 씨는 그녀가 캐소본 씨의 부목사라면 그러려니 했던 그대로 늙고 케케묵어 보이는 사람이었다. 분명 천국에 갈 훌륭한 분이겠지만(실리아는 신념 없는 사람이 되고 싶지 않았으므로), 그 입꼬리가 어찌나 불쾌하던지. 실리아는 로윅에서 신부 들러리로 지내야 할 시간을 다소 침울하게 떠올렸으니, 그 부목사에게는 신념과 상관없이 그녀가 좋아할 만한 예쁘고 어린 자식들도 아마 없을 터였다.\n\n터커 씨는 그들의 산책에 더없이 요긴했다. 어쩌면 캐소본 씨가 이 점에 관해 미리 내다본 바가 없지 않았을 텐데, 그 부목사는 마을 사람들과 다른 교구민들에 관한 도러시아의 모든 물음에 답할 수 있었기 때문이다. 로윅에서는 모두가 형편이 넉넉하다고 그는 장담했다. 싼 집세로 그 연립 오두막들에 사는 이들 가운데 돼지를 치지 않는 농가가 없었고, 뒤편의 텃밭들도 잘 가꾸어져 있었다. 사내아이들은 훌륭한 코듀로이를 입었고, 계집아이들은 단정한 하녀로 나가거나 집에서 짚을 엮는 일을 조금씩 했다. 이곳에는 베틀도 없고 비국교도도 없었다. 그리고 사람들의 성향이 영성보다는 돈을 모으는 쪽으로 다소 기울긴 했어도, 악덕은 그리 많지 않았다. 얼룩진 닭들이 어찌나 많은지 브룩 씨가 한마디 했다. \"여기 농부들은 여인들이 이삭을 주울 수 있도록 보리를 좀 남겨 두는군. 보아하니 말이야. 그 어진 프랑스 왕이 자기 백성 모두가 그러길 바랐듯, 여기 가난한 사람들도 냄비에 닭 한 마리쯤은 넣을 수 있겠어. 프랑스 사람들은 닭을 꽤 많이 먹지—비쩍 마른 닭들이긴 하지만 말이야.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 그건 그 왕이 참 값싸게 한 소원이었어요.\" 도러시아가 분개하며 말했다. \"왕들이 그처럼 괴물 같은 존재라서, 그런 소원조차 왕다운 미덕으로 쳐 줘야 하나요?\"\n\n\"그리고 만약 그가 비쩍 마른 닭을 바란 거라면,\" 실리아가 말했다. \"그건 좋지 않았을 거예요. 하지만 어쩌면 그들이 살진 닭을 갖길 바랐는지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 그 단어가 본문에서 빠졌거나, 아니면 어쩌면 함축되었던 거지. 즉, 왕의 마음속에는 있었으나 입 밖에 내지 않은 것이오.\" 캐소본 씨가 미소 지으며 실리아 쪽으로 고개를 숙이며 말했고, 실리아는 캐소본 씨가 자기에게 눈을 깜박이는 것을 견딜 수 없어 곧장 조금 뒤로 물러났다.\n\n집으로 돌아가는 길에 도러시아는 침묵에 잠겼다. 그녀는 로윅에서는 자기가 할 일이 아무것도 없다는 데에 어떤 실망을 느꼈고, 그러면서도 그런 실망이 부끄러웠다. 그리고 다음 몇 분 동안 그녀의 마음은, 차라리 자기가 더 좋아했을 가능성을—세상의 비참함을 더 많이 떠안은 교구에 자기 보금자리가 자리하여, 그 안에서 더 능동적인 의무를 갖게 되는 가능성을—훑어 보았다. 그러다 실제로 자기 앞에 놓인 미래로 되돌아와, 캐소본 씨의 목표에 더욱 온전히 헌신하며 새로운 의무들을 기다리는 자기 모습을 그려 보았다. 그러한 의무들 가운데 많은 것이, 그 동반의 삶 속에서 그녀가 얻게 될 더 높은 지식에 비로소 드러날지도 몰랐다.\n\n터커 씨는 영주관에서 점심을 함께할 수 없게 만드는 성직 업무가 있어 이내 그들 곁을 떠났다. 그리고 그들이 작은 문을 지나 다시 정원으로 들어설 때, 캐소본 씨가 말했다—\n\n\"좀 슬퍼 보이는구려, 도러시아. 본 것들이 마음에 들었기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 어리석고 잘못된 어떤 감정을 느끼고 있는 것 같아요.\" 도러시아가 늘 그렇듯 솔직하게 대답했다. \"이곳 사람들이 해 줄 일을 좀 더 필요로 했으면, 하고 바랄 뻔했거든요. 저는 제 삶을 무언가에 쓸모 있게 만드는 방법을 너무도 적게밖에 알지 못해요. 물론 쓸모에 관한 제 생각이 좁기야 하겠죠. 사람들을 돕는 새로운 방법들을 배워야겠어요.\"\n\n\"분명 그럴 거요.\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다. \"각 위치에는 그에 걸맞은 의무가 있는 법이오. 로윅의 안주인으로서 당신의 의무도, 바라건대 어떤 갈망도 채워지지 않은 채 남겨 두지는 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 저도 그렇게 믿어요.\" 도러시아가 진심으로 말했다. \"제가 슬퍼한다고는 여기지 마세요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좋소. 그런데 피곤하지 않다면, 우리가 온 길과는 다른 길로 집에 갑시다.\"\n\n도러시아는 조금도 피곤하지 않았고, 그래서 그들은 멋진 주목 한 그루를 향해 조금 에둘러 갔으니, 그것은 집의 이쪽 부지가 자랑하는 으뜸가는 가보였다. 그들이 가까이 다가가자, 상록수의 어두운 배경 속에 또렷이 드러나는 한 사람이 벤치에 앉아 그 고목을 스케치하고 있었다. 실리아와 함께 앞서 걷던 브룩 씨가 고개를 돌리며 말했다—\n\n\"저 젊은이는 누구요, 캐소본?\"\n\n그들이 아주 가까이 다가갔을 때 캐소본 씨가 대답했다—\n\n\"저 사람은 내 젊은 친척이오, 육촌이지. 사실은 손자라오.\" 그가 도러시아를 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"당신이 눈여겨보던 초상화의 그 부인, 내 이모 줄리아의 손자 말이오.\"\n\n그 젊은이는 스케치북을 내려놓고 일어섰다. 그의 숱 많은 연한 갈색 곱슬머리는, 젊음과 더불어 그를 단번에 실리아가 본 그 환영과 일치시켰다.\n\n\"도러시아, 내 사촌인 래디슬로 군을 소개하겠소. 윌, 이쪽은 브룩 양일세.\"\n\n이제 그 사촌이 어찌나 가까이 있던지, 그가 모자를 들어 올렸을 때 도러시아는 다소 가까이 붙은 한 쌍의 잿빛 눈동자와, 잔물결이 살짝 진 섬세하고 고르지 않은 코와, 뒤로 넘긴 머리카락을 볼 수 있었다. 다만 그 입과 턱은 할머니의 세밀화가 보여 주는 모습보다 더 도드라지고 위협적인 인상을 풍겼다. 젊은 래디슬로는, 미래의 육촌 형수와 그 친척들에게 소개되는 것이 마치 마음에 든다는 듯 미소 지을 필요를 느끼지 않았고, 오히려 입을 삐죽 내민 불만스러운 기색을 띠고 있었다.\n\n\"화가시군요.\" 브룩 씨가 스케치북을 집어 들고 거리낌 없는 그 특유의 방식으로 이리저리 넘기며 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 그저 조금 끄적거릴 뿐입니다. 거기엔 봐 줄 만한 게 아무것도 없어요.\" 젊은 래디슬로가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했는데, 어쩌면 겸손이라기보다는 성미 탓이었을 것이다.\n\n\"오, 무슨 말을, 이건 멋진 솜씨인데. 나도 한때 이쪽으로 좀 했지, 알다시피. 자, 여기 보게나. 이게 바로 우리가 예전에 '브리오'라고 부르던 솜씨로 그린, 내가 멋지다고 하는 것일세.\" 브룩 씨가 두 처녀를 향해 돌투성이 땅과 나무들과 못이 그려진 커다란 채색 스케치를 내밀었다.\n\n\"저는 그런 것들을 판단할 줄 몰라요.\" 도러시아가, 차갑지는 않으나 자기에게 묻는 것을 간곡히 사양하며 말했다. \"아시잖아요, 삼촌, 저는 삼촌이 그토록 칭송받는다고 하시는 그림들의 아름다움을 도무지 알아보지 못하는걸요. 그건 제가 이해하지 못하는 언어예요. 아마 그림과 자연 사이에 어떤 관계가 있을 텐데, 제가 너무 무지해서 느끼지 못하는 거겠죠—마치 삼촌은 제겐 아무 의미도 없는 그리스어 문장이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알아보시듯이요.\" 도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 올려다보자 그는 그녀 쪽으로 고개를 숙였고, 그동안 브룩 씨가 무심히 미소 지으며 말했다—\n\n\"이런, 사람들이 어찌 이리도 다른지! 하지만 너는 배운 방식이 나빴던 거다, 알다시피—그렇지 않았다면 이건 딱 여자아이들에게 어울리는 것인데—스케치, 미술 같은 것 말이다. 그런데 너는 설계도를 그리는 쪽으로 빠졌지. 너는 '모르비데차' 같은 것을 이해하지 못해.\" 그가 젊은 래디슬로에게로 돌아서며 말을 이었는데, 래디슬로는 도러시아를 관찰하는 데 골몰해 있다가 불려 나와야 했다. \"우리 집에 와 주게나, 부디. 내가 이쪽으로 한 것들을 보여 주겠네.\" 래디슬로는 캐소본과 결혼할 여자라면 분명 불쾌한 처녀일 거라고 마음을 굳혔던 터라, 그림에 관한 자기 어리석음을 두고 그녀가 한 말은, 설령 그가 그 말을 곧이곧대로 믿었다 해도 그 견해를 더욱 굳혀 주었을 것이다. 실상 그는 그녀의 말을 은근한 평가로 받아들였고, 그녀가 자기 스케치를 형편없다고 여긴다고 확신했다. 그녀의 변명에는 영리함이 너무 많이 배어 있었으니, 그녀는 자기 삼촌과 그를 함께 비웃고 있는 것이었다. 하지만 그 목소리란! 그것은 한때 아이올로스의 하프 속에 깃들어 살았던 어느 영혼의 목소리 같았다. 이것은 자연의 모순 가운데 하나임이 틀림없었다. 캐소본과 결혼할 처녀에게 어떤 종류의 열정도 있을 리 없었다. 그러나 그는 그녀에게서 몸을 돌려, 브룩 씨의 초대에 고개 숙여 감사를 표했다.\n\n\"내 이탈리아 동판화들을 함께 넘겨 보세나.\" 그 마음씨 좋은 사람이 말을 이었다. \"그런 게 끝도 없이 많아, 몇 년이나 처박아 둔 것들이지. 이런 시골에서는 녹슬기 마련이거든, 알다시피. 자네 말고, 캐소본은 빼고 말이야. 자네야 연구를 붙들고 있으니. 하지만 내 가장 좋은 생각들은 자꾸 밑바닥으로 가라앉아—쓰이지 않고 말이야. 자네같이 영리한 젊은이들은 게으름을 경계해야 하네. 나는 너무 게을렀거든, 알다시피. 안 그랬으면 한때 어디든 됐을 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n\"때맞은 훈계로군요.\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 이제 집으로 갑시다, 아가씨들이 서 있느라 지치지 않도록 말이오.\"\n\n그들이 등을 돌리자, 젊은 래디슬로는 다시 자리에 앉아 스케치를 계속했고, 그러면서 그의 얼굴에는 재미있어하는 표정이 번지더니 그림을 그려 나갈수록 점점 짙어져, 마침내 고개를 젖히고 소리 내어 웃었다. 한편으로는 자기 작품이 받은 대접이 그를 간질였고, 한편으로는 그 엄숙한 사촌이 저 처녀의 연인이라는 발상이, 그리고 한편으로는 게으름이라는 걸림돌만 없었다면 자기가 차지했을 자리에 관한 브룩 씨의 정의가 그러했다. 윌 래디슬로 군의 우스꽝스러운 것을 알아보는 감각은 그의 이목구비를 매우 보기 좋게 환히 밝혔으니, 그것은 익살에 대한 순수한 즐거움일 뿐, 비웃음이나 자기 과시는 조금도 섞여 있지 않았다.\n\n\"자네 조카는 뭘 하며 지내려는 건가, 캐소본?\" 그들이 걸어가는 동안 브룩 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내 사촌 말이지요—조카가 아니라.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 사촌. 하지만 직업을 두고 하는 말일세, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"그 물음에 대한 답은 고통스러우리만치 불확실합니다. 럭비를 졸업하고 나서 그는 내가 기꺼이 보내 주려던 영국 대학에 가기를 거절하고, 내가 변칙적이라 여길 수밖에 없는 길, 즉 하이델베르크에서 공부하는 길을 택했습니다. 그리고 이제 그는 다시 외국에 나가고 싶어 하는데, 무엇을 위함인지 그 자신도 모를 준비, 이른바 교양이라는 막연한 목적 말고는 아무런 특별한 목표도 없습니다. 그는 직업을 정하기를 거절합니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 대 주는 것 말고는 다른 수입이 없겠지, 아마?\"\n\n\"나는 늘 그에게나 그의 지인들에게, 그를 학자다운 교육으로 길러 내고 번듯하게 출발시키는 데 필요한 것을 적당히 대 주겠다고 알려 왔습니다. 그러니 그렇게 품게 한 기대를 채워 줄 의무가 내게 있는 것이지요.\" 캐소본 씨가 자신의 처신을 한낱 올바름의 빛 아래 놓으며 말했으니, 도러시아는 그 섬세함을 감탄하며 눈여겨보았다.\n\n\"그 친구가 여행에 목말라 있군. 어쩌면 브루스나 멍고 파크 같은 인물이 될지도 모르지.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"나도 한때 그런 생각을 품었거든.\"\n\n\"아니요, 그는 탐험이나 우리의 지질 지식을 넓히는 쪽으로는 소질이 없습니다. 그거야 내가 어느 정도 인정하며 받아들일 수 있는 특별한 목적이겠지요. 비록 그런 경력이 흔히 때 이른 비명횡사로 끝나는 것을 두고 그를 축하해 줄 수는 없겠지만 말입니다. 그런데 그는 지구 표면을 더 정확히 알고자 하는 욕구가 어찌나 없는지, 차라리 나일강의 수원을 모르는 편이 좋다고, 시적 상상력의 사냥터로 보존될 미지의 영역이 좀 있어야 한다고 말하더군요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 거기엔 일리가 있어, 알다시피.\" 분명 공평한 마음을 지닌 브룩 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 유감스럽게도, 온갖 종류의 철저함을 꺼리는 그의 전반적인 부정확함과 부주의함의 한 부분에 지나지 않을 거라 봅니다. 그것은 그가 어떤 직업, 세속의 것이든 신성한 것이든, 그가 통상의 규칙에 순응하여 하나를 택할 만큼 고분고분하다 해도, 그에게 나쁜 징조가 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그분은 자기가 적합하지 않다는 데서 비롯된 양심상의 거리낌을 품고 있는지도 몰라요.\" 도러시아가, 호의적인 해석을 찾는 데 흥미를 느끼며 말했다. \"법률과 의학은 떠맡기에 아주 진지한 직업이어야 하니까요, 그렇지 않나요? 사람들의 생명과 재산이 거기에 달려 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"분명 그렇소. 하지만 나는 내 젊은 친척 윌 래디슬로가 그런 직업을 꺼리는 까닭이, 주로 꾸준한 노력과, 도구로서는 필요하나 제멋대로인 취향에는 매력적이거나 곧장 끌리지 않는 그런 종류의 습득을 싫어하는 데 있을까 두렵소. 나는 그에게, 아리스토텔레스가 감탄스러우리만치 간결하게 진술한 바를 누차 강조했소. 즉, 목적으로 여겨지는 어떤 일을 이루려면, 그에 앞서 인내를 요하는 이차적인 여러 능력이나 습득된 재능을 먼저 발휘해야 한다는 것이오. 나는 아직 완성하지 못한 한 저작을 위해 여러 해의 노고를 담은 내 친필 원고집을 그에게 가리켜 보였소. 하지만 헛수고였소. 이런 식의 신중한 논리에 그는 자신을 페가수스라 칭하고, 정해진 모든 형태의 일을 '굴레'라 부르며 응수할 뿐이오.\"\n\n실리아가 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 캐소본 씨가 꽤 재미있는 말을 할 수 있다는 것을 알고 놀랐던 것이다.\n\n\"글쎄, 알다시피, 그가 바이런이나 채터턴이나 처칠 같은 인물이 될지도 모르지—그런 부류 말이야—누가 알겠나.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"그를 이탈리아든, 가고 싶어 하는 다른 어디든 보내 줄 텐가?\"\n\n\"네. 한 일 년쯤 적당한 비용을 대 주기로 했습니다. 그도 그 이상은 청하지 않더군요. 자유라는 시험으로 그를 한번 겪어 보게 할 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"참 친절하시네요.\" 도러시아가 기쁨에 차서 캐소본 씨를 올려다보며 말했다. \"고귀한 일이에요. 결국 사람들은 정말로 자기 안에, 자신에게조차 분명히 드러나지 않은 어떤 천직을 지니고 있을 수 있잖아요, 안 그래요? 한창 자라고 있기에 게으르고 약해 보일 수도 있고요. 우리는 서로에게 무척 인내심을 가져야 한다고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"인내를 좋게 여기게 된 건 약혼을 했기 때문인가 봐.\" 둘만 남아 겉옷을 벗는 사이에 실리아가 곧장 말했다.\n\n\"내가 무척 참을성이 없다는 뜻이구나, 실리아.\"\n\n\"응. 사람들이 언니 마음에 꼭 드는 대로 행동하고 말하지 않을 때 말이야.\" 이번 약혼 이후로 실리아는 도러시아에게 \"이런저런 말을 하는 것\"을 전보다 덜 두려워하게 되었으니, 영리함이라는 것이 그녀에게는 그 어느 때보다 가엾어 보였던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER X.\n\n「죽이지도 않은 곰의 가죽 말고는 입을 옷이 없었더라면, 그는 심한 감기에 걸리고 말았으리라.」—풀러.\n\n젊은 래디슬로는 브룩 씨가 청한 그 방문을 끝내 하지 않았고, 불과 엿새 뒤에 캐소본 씨는 자신의 젊은 친척이 대륙으로 떠났다고 언급했는데, 그 차갑고 모호한 어조로써 더 이상의 질문을 사양하는 듯했다. 사실 윌은 유럽 전역이라는 것 외에 더 명확한 행선지를 정하기를 거부했던 것이다. 그는 천재성이란 필연적으로 속박을 견디지 못한다고 여겼다. 한편으로 그것은 자발성을 위해 최대한의 활동 공간을 가져야 하고, 다른 한편으로는 그것을 그 고유의 과업으로 부르는 우주로부터의 전언을, 모든 숭고한 기회에 대해 오로지 수용의 자세를 취하기만 하면, 자신만만하게 기다릴 수 있다는 것이었다. 수용의 자세는 다양하며, 윌은 그중 많은 것을 진지하게 시도해 보았다. 그는 포도주를 지나치게 좋아하지는 않았으나, 단지 그러한 형태의 황홀경을 실험해 보려고 몇 번 과음한 적이 있었다. 또 기진맥진할 때까지 단식했다가 바닷가재로 저녁을 들었고, 아편을 복용하여 몸을 병들게 한 적도 있었다. 이런 방법들로부터 그다지 독창적인 결과는 나오지 않았으며, 아편의 효과는 그의 체질과 드 퀸시의 체질이 전혀 다르다는 사실을 그에게 확신시켜 주었다. 천재성을 펼쳐 낼 그 부가적인 상황은 아직 찾아오지 않았고, 우주는 아직 손짓하지 않았다. 카이사르의 운명조차 한때는 한낱 거창한 예감에 불과했다. 우리는 모든 발달이 얼마나 가면극 같은 것인지, 그리고 무력한 배아 속에 얼마나 강력한 형상이 변장하고 숨어 있을 수 있는지를 안다. 사실 세상은 희망에 찬 비유들과, 가능성이라 불리는 의심스럽고 그럴싸한 알들로 가득 차 있다. 윌은 오랜 부화 끝에도 병아리 한 마리 나오지 않는 가련한 사례들을 충분히 똑똑히 보았으며, 만일 감사하는 마음이 아니었다면 캐소본을 비웃었을 것이다. 캐소본의 우직한 노력, 줄지어 늘어선 공책들, 그리고 세상의 흩어진 폐허를 더듬어 가는 학문적 이론의 자그마한 촛불은, 자기 자신에 관한 우주의 의도에 대한 윌의 너그러운 신뢰를 전적으로 북돋워 주는 교훈을 강요하는 듯했다. 그는 그러한 신뢰를 천재의 표지로 여겼으며, 분명 그것이 그 반대의 표지는 아니다. 천재성이란 자만에도 겸손에도 있지 않고, 무언가를 일반적으로가 아니라 어떤 특정한 것을 만들거나 해내는 능력에 있는 것이다. 그러니 우리가 그의 미래에 대해 단언하지 않은 채로, 그를 대륙으로 떠나게 하자. 온갖 형태의 오류 가운데 예언이야말로 가장 부질없는 것이다.\n\n그러나 지금으로서는 너무 성급한 판단에 대한 이 경계가 그의 젊은 사촌보다는 캐소본 씨와 관련하여 나의 흥미를 더 끈다. 도러시아에게 캐소본 씨가 그저 그녀의 젊은 환상이라는 잘 타오르는 재료에 불을 댕긴 한낱 계기였다고 해서, 지금까지 그에 관한 판단을 내려 온 덜 열정적인 인물들의 마음속에 그가 공정하게 그려졌다는 결론이 따라 나오는가? 나는 어떠한 절대적인 결론에도, 이웃 성직자의 위대한 영혼이라는 주장에 대한 캐드월래더 부인의 경멸이나 자기 연적의 다리에 대한 제임스 체텀 경의 형편없는 평가에서 비롯된 어떠한 편견에도, 또한 동행의 생각을 끌어내지 못한 브룩 씨의 실패나 중년 학자의 외모에 대한 실리아의 비판에서 비롯된 어떠한 편견에도 항의한다. 만일 그러한 고독한 최상급이 존재한 적이 있다 한들, 당대의 가장 위대한 인물조차 갖가지 작은 거울들에 비친 이런 불리한 자기 모습을 피할 수 있었을지 나는 확신하지 못한다. 밀턴조차 숟가락에서 제 초상을 찾으려 하면 시골뜨기의 안면 각도를 감수해야 하리라. 더욱이 캐소본 씨가 스스로를 두고 말할 때 다소 냉랭한 화법을 쓴다 해서, 그에게 훌륭한 작업이나 고운 감정이 없다고 단정할 수는 없다. 불멸의 어느 물리학자이자 상형문자 해독자가 가증스러운 시를 쓰지 않았던가? 태양계 이론이 우아한 태도와 능란한 화술로 진전된 적이 있는가? 한 인간에 대한 외부의 평가에서 눈을 돌려, 더 예리한 흥미로써, 그 자신의 의식이 자기 행위나 능력에 관해 보내는 보고는 어떠한지, 그가 어떤 장애 속에서 나날의 노고를 이어 가고 있는지, 세월이 그의 내부에 어떤 희망의 시듦이나 자기기만의 더 깊은 고착을 새기고 있는지, 그리고 언젠가는 그에게 너무 무거워져 그의 심장을 마지막 멈춤으로 데려갈 보편적인 압력에 맞서 그가 어떤 정신으로 분투하고 있는지를 헤아려 본다고 가정해 보자. 의심할 바 없이 그의 처지는 그 자신의 눈에는 중요한 것이다. 그리고 우리가 그를 위한 자리가 모자라기에 그가 우리의 고려 속에서 너무 큰 자리를 요구한다고 생각하는 것이 그 주된 이유임에 틀림없으니, 우리는 그를 완전한 확신을 가지고 신의 보살핌에 맡기는 까닭이다. 아니, 이웃이 우리에게서 받은 것이 아무리 적다 해도, 그가 거기서 최대한의 것을 기대하는 일은 오히려 숭고하다고까지 여겨진다. 캐소본 씨 역시 자기 자신의 세계의 중심이었다. 그가 다른 이들이 섭리에 의해 자기를 위해 만들어졌다고 생각하기 쉬웠다 한들, 그리고 특히 그들을 「모든 신화의 열쇠」의 저자에게 얼마나 알맞은가 하는 관점에서 가늠하기 쉬웠다 한들, 이 특성은 우리와 그리 동떨어진 것이 아니며, 인간이 지닌 다른 구걸하는 희망들처럼, 우리의 연민을 얼마쯤 요구한다.\n\n분명 미스 브룩과의 이 결혼이라는 사건은, 지금까지 그 일에 못마땅한 기색을 보여 온 그 어느 누구보다도 그에게 더 절실하게 와닿았으며, 지금 단계에서 나는 상냥한 제임스 경의 실망보다는 그가 거둔 성공의 경험에 대해 더 따뜻한 마음이 든다. 사실 결혼식으로 정해진 날이 가까워질수록 캐소본 씨는 자기 마음이 들뜨는 것을 느끼지 못했으니, 또한 모든 경험이 보여 주듯 길가에 꽃이 피어 있게 마련인 그 부부의 정원 풍경을 떠올리는 것이, 그가 촛불을 손에 들고 거닐던 익숙한 지하 납골당보다 그에게 한결같이 더 매혹적이지도 않았던 것이다. 그는 사랑스럽고 고결한 마음을 지닌 처녀를 얻었으면서도 환희는—그가 역시 찾아내야 할 대상으로 여겼던 그 환희는—얻지 못했다는 자신의 놀라움을 스스로에게 고백하지 못했으며, 하물며 남에게 입 밖에 낼 수도 없었다. 그가 그 반대를 함의하는 모든 고전 구절을 알고 있었던 것은 사실이다. 그러나 고전 구절을 안다는 것은, 우리가 알다시피, 일종의 운동 방식이니, 그것이 어째서 그 구절들을 개인적으로 적용할 여분의 힘을 거의 남기지 않는지를 설명해 준다.\n\n가엾은 캐소본 씨는 자신의 오랜 학구적 독신 생활이 그를 위해 향락의 복리(複利)를 쌓아 두었으며, 자기 애정에 대해 큰 액수를 인출하더라도 어김없이 지불되리라고 상상해 왔다. 우리는 모두, 진중한 사람이든 가벼운 사람이든, 자기 생각을 비유 속에 얽어 넣고서 그 힘에 기대어 치명적으로 행동하는 까닭이다. 그리고 이제 그는 자기 처지가 유달리 행복하다는 바로 그 확신 때문에 슬퍼질 위험에 처해 있었다. 기대에 찬 기쁨이 가장 생생해야 할 바로 그때, 그가 로윅 서재의 익숙한 따분함을 그레인지 방문과 맞바꾸던 바로 그때 그에게 엄습하는 어떤 감수성의 공허함을 설명해 줄 외적인 것이 아무것도 없었다. 여기에 있는 것은, 목표에 더 가까워지는 기색도 없이 저술이라는 수렁 속에서 애쓰는 동안 이따금 그를 위협하던 절망 속에서와 마찬가지로, 그가 철저히 고독에 처해진 지긋지긋한 경험이었다. 게다가 그의 고독은 동정으로부터 움츠러드는 그 최악의 고독이었다. 그는 도러시아가 그를 세상이 그녀의 성공한 구혼자에게 기대할 만한 것에 못지않게 행복하다고 여겨 주기를 바라지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 자신의 저술과 관련하여 그는 그녀의 젊은 신뢰와 흠모에 기댔으며, 자기 자신을 격려하는 수단으로서 그녀가 귀 기울여 듣는 신선한 관심을 끌어내기를 좋아했다. 그녀에게 이야기할 때면 그는 자신의 모든 성과와 의도를 교사다운 반사된 자신감으로 펼쳐 보였고, 그 힘겹고 비창조적인 시간들을 타르타로스의 망령들이 풍기는 안개 같은 압박으로 가득 채우던 그 냉랭한 상상 속의 청중으로부터 그 동안만은 벗어났다.\n\n도러시아에게는, 그녀가 받은 교육의 주된 부분을 이루었던, 어린 숙녀들에게 맞춰진 그 장난감 상자 같은 세계사 이후로, 자신의 위대한 책에 관한 캐소본 씨의 이야기는 새로운 전망으로 가득했던 까닭이다. 그리고 이 계시의 감각, 스토아학파와 알렉산드리아 학자들을—자기 자신의 생각과 전혀 다르지 않은 사상을 지녔던 사람들로서—한층 가까이 소개받는 이 놀라움은, 자신의 삶과 신조를 그 경이로운 과거와 엄밀히 연결 짓고 가장 먼 지식의 원천에 자신의 행위와 관련된 의미를 부여해 줄, 어떤 구속력 있는 이론에 대한 그녀의 평소 열망을 한동안 보류시켰다. 그 더 완전한 가르침은 다가올 것이고, 캐소본 씨가 그녀에게 그 모든 것을 일러 주리라. 그녀는 결혼을 고대하듯 사상의 더 높은 입문을 고대했으며, 그 둘에 대한 어렴풋한 관념을 한데 뒤섞고 있었다. 도러시아가 캐소본 씨의 학식에 어떤 식으로든 참여하는 것을 한낱 교양으로서 마음에 두었으리라고 추측한다면 그것은 큰 잘못이리라. 비록 프레시트와 팁턴 인근의 여론이 그녀를 영리하다고 평했지만, 그 형용사는 영리함이 인격과는 별개로 단지 알고 행하는 적성을 뜻하는, 더 정밀한 어휘를 쓰는 부류의 사람들에게는 그녀를 제대로 묘사하지 못했을 것이다. 무언가를 습득하려는 그녀의 모든 열망은, 그녀의 사상과 충동이 습관적으로 휩쓸려 흐르던 공감적 동기의 그 충만한 흐름 안에 있었다. 그녀는 지식으로 자신을 치장하기를—자기 행위를 먹여 살리는 신경과 피로부터 떼어 낸 채 그것을 헐겁게 걸치기를—원하지 않았다. 만일 그녀가 책을 썼다면, 그녀는 성녀 테레사가 했던 것처럼, 자기 양심을 강제하는 권위의 명령 아래에서 썼을 것임에 틀림없다. 그러나 그녀가 갈망한 것은, 그것으로써 자기 삶이 이성적이면서도 동시에 열렬한 행위로 채워질 무언가였다. 그리고 인도하는 환상과 영적 지도자의 시대는 지나갔고, 기도는 갈망은 드높이되 가르침을 주지는 않았으니, 지식 말고 무슨 등불이 있었겠는가? 분명 학식 있는 사람들이 유일한 기름을 간직하고 있었으며, 캐소본 씨보다 더 학식 있는 자가 누구이겠는가?\n\n이렇듯 이 짧은 몇 주 동안 도러시아의 기쁘고 감사에 찬 기대는 흐트러지지 않았으며, 그녀의 연인이 이따금 무미건조함을 의식했을지언정, 그는 결코 그것을 그녀의 애정 어린 관심이 식은 탓으로 돌릴 수가 없었다.\n\n계절이 신혼여행을 로마까지 늘리려는 계획을 부추길 만큼 온화했으며, 캐소본 씨는 바티칸의 어떤 필사본들을 살펴보고 싶었기에 이 일을 간절히 바랐다.\n\n\"자네 동생이 우리와 동행하지 않게 된 것이 여전히 유감이오.\" 어느 날 아침, 실리아가 가기를 꺼린다는 것과 도러시아도 그녀의 동행을 원하지 않는다는 것이 확인되고 얼마쯤 지난 뒤에 그가 말했다. \"당신은 외로운 시간을 많이 보내게 될 거요, 도러시아. 로마에 머무는 동안 나는 부득이 내 시간을 최대한 활용해야 할 테니, 당신에게 말동무가 있다면 내가 한결 홀가분할 것 같소.\"\n\n\"내가 한결 홀가분할 것 같소\"라는 말이 도러시아의 마음에 거슬렸다. 캐소본 씨에게 말할 때 그녀가 언짢음으로 얼굴을 붉힌 것은 그때가 처음이었다.\n\n\"당신은 나를 몹시 오해하셨나 봐요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 당신 시간의 가치를 헤아리지 못하리라고 생각하신다면—당신이 그 시간을 가장 알맞게 쓰는 데 방해가 되는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 포기하지 않으리라고 생각하신다면 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그건 참으로 갸륵한 마음이오, 사랑하는 도러시아.\" 캐소본 씨는 그녀가 상처받았다는 것을 조금도 알아차리지 못한 채 말했다. \"하지만 당신에게 숙녀 말동무가 있다면, 내가 두 사람을 안내인에게 맡길 수 있을 테고, 그러면 우리가 같은 시간 안에 두 가지 목적을 이룰 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"제발 이 일을 다시는 입에 올리지 말아 주세요.\" 도러시아가 다소 도도하게 말했다. 그러나 곧바로 그녀는 자신이 잘못했을까 봐 두려워졌고, 그를 향해 돌아서서 그의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹으며 다른 어조로 덧붙였다. \"제발 저 때문에 염려하지 마세요. 혼자 있을 때 생각할 것이 무척 많을 거예요. 그리고 그저 저를 돌봐 줄 사람으로는 탠트립이면 충분한 말동무가 될 거예요. 실리아를 데려가는 건 견딜 수가 없어요. 그 애는 비참해할 테니까요.\"\n\n옷을 갈아입을 시간이었다. 그날은 만찬회가 열릴 예정이었는데, 그것은 결혼에 앞선 의례로서 그레인지에서 열린 만찬회들 중 마지막 것이었으며, 도러시아는 종소리가 울리자 즉시 자리를 뜰 구실이 생긴 것이, 마치 자신이 평소보다 더 많은 채비가 필요한 것처럼 보일 수 있어 다행스러웠다. 그녀는 자기 자신에게조차 규명할 수 없는 어떤 이유로 짜증이 난 것이 부끄러웠다. 거짓말을 할 생각은 없었지만, 그녀의 대답은 자기 안의 진짜 상처를 건드리지 못했던 것이다. 캐소본 씨의 말은 지극히 타당했으나, 그럼에도 그것은 그의 편에서 비롯된 어떤 막연하고 순간적인 거리감을 가져다주었다.\n\n\"분명 나는 이상하리만치 이기적이고 나약한 마음 상태에 빠져 있어.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"나보다 그토록 뛰어난 남편을 맞이하면서, 그가 내가 그를 필요로 하는 것보다 나를 덜 필요로 한다는 사실을 모를 수가 있겠는가?\"\n\n캐소본 씨가 전적으로 옳다고 스스로 납득하고 나자, 그녀는 평정을 되찾았고, 은회색 드레스를 입고 응접실로 들어설 때 고요한 기품의 흐뭇한 모습을 띠고 있었다—이마 위로 가르마를 타고 뒤로 묵직하게 틀어 올린 짙은 갈색 머리의 단순한 선은, 한낱 효과만을 좇는 기색이 그녀의 태도와 표정에서 전혀 보이지 않는 것과 잘 어울렸다. 때때로 도러시아가 사람들과 함께 있을 때면, 마치 탑에서 맑은 대기를 내다보는 성녀 바르바라의 그림인 양 그녀를 감도는 평온의 기운이 완연한 것 같았다. 그러나 이런 고요의 막간들은, 어떤 외적인 호소가 그녀를 건드렸을 때 그녀의 말과 감정의 활력을 한층 더 두드러지게 했다.\n\n이날 저녁 그녀는 자연히 많은 관찰의 대상이 되었으니, 만찬회가 규모도 컸을뿐더러 남성 쪽으로 보자면 브룩 씨의 조카딸들이 그와 함께 거주하게 된 이래로 그레인지에서 열린 어느 만찬회보다도 더 잡다한 편이어서, 대화가 다소 어울리지 않는 두세 사람씩의 무리로 이루어진 까닭이다. 그곳에는 새로 선출된 미들마치 시장이 있었는데, 공교롭게도 제조업자였다. 그의 처남인 자선가 은행가도 있었는데, 그는 그 읍내에서 어찌나 위세를 떨쳤는지 어휘력이 닿는 대로 어떤 이들은 그를 감리교도라 불렀고 어떤 이들은 위선자라 불렀다. 또한 여러 전문직 인사들도 있었다. 사실 캐드월래더 부인은 브룩이 미들마치 사람들을 대접하기 시작했다고 말하면서, 자기는 십일조 만찬에 오는 농부들이 더 좋다고, 그들은 가식 없이 자기 건강을 위해 축배를 들고 자기 할아버지 대의 가구를 부끄러워하지 않는다고 했다. 그 지방에서는 개혁이 정치적 의식을 발달시키는 그 주목할 만한 역할을 하기 전까지는, 신분의 구별은 더 뚜렷하고 당파의 구별은 더 흐릿했던 까닭에, 브룩 씨의 잡다한 초대는 그의 지나친 여행과 사상이라는 형태로 너무 많은 것을 받아들이는 습관에서 비롯된 그 전반적인 헐거움에 속하는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n이미, 미스 브룩이 식당을 나서자, 짤막한 \"방백(傍白)\"을 주고받을 기회가 생겼다.\n\n\"훌륭한 여인이오, 미스 브룩은! 보기 드물게 훌륭한 여인이지, 정말이지!\" 늙은 변호사 스탠디시 씨가 말했는데, 그는 워낙 오랫동안 지주 계층과 관계를 맺어 와서 자신도 지주가 되어 버린 사람이었고, 그 욕설을 묵직한 어조로 일종의 문장(紋章)처럼 내뱉어, 좋은 지위에 있는 사람의 말투에 도장을 찍었다.\n\n은행가 벌스트로드 씨를 향한 말 같았으나, 그 신사는 상스러움과 불경함을 싫어하여 그저 고개만 숙였다. 그 말을 받은 것은 치철리 씨로, 그는 중년의 독신남이자 토끼 사냥으로 이름난 인물이었으며, 부활절 달걀 같은 안색에, 몇 가닥 안 되는 머리카락을 정성스레 매만지고, 자신이 빼어난 외모를 지녔음을 의식한 태도를 풍기는 사람이었다.\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 내 취향의 여자는 아니오. 나는 우리를 즐겁게 해 주려고 좀 더 애쓰는 여자가 좋단 말이오. 여자에게는 좀 섬세한 장식 같은 게 있어야 하지—교태 같은 거 말이오. 남자는 일종의 도전을 좋아하니까. 여자가 사내를 노골적으로 노릴수록 더 좋은 거요.\"\n\n\"거기엔 일리가 있군.\" 상냥하게 굴 마음이 든 스탠디시 씨가 말했다. \"그리고, 정말이지, 여자들이란 대개 그렇지. 그게 무슨 현명한 목적에 들어맞는 모양이오. 섭리가 여자들을 그렇게 만든 거지, 안 그렇소, 벌스트로드?\"\n\n\"나라면 교태를 다른 원천으로 돌리고 싶군요.\" 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다. \"차라리 그것을 악마에게로 돌리겠소.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고, 여자에게는 약간의 악마성이 있어야지.\" 여성에 대한 연구가 자신의 신학에 해로웠던 듯한 치철리 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 금발에, 어떤 걸음걸이를 갖추고, 백조 같은 목을 한 여자가 좋소. 우리끼리 얘기지만, 시장의 딸이 미스 브룩이나 미스 실리아보다 내 취향에 더 맞아요. 내가 결혼할 사람이라면, 그 둘 중 누구보다도 미스 빈시를 택하겠소.\"\n\n\"이런, 어서 다가서 보시오, 어서.\" 스탠디시 씨가 익살스럽게 말했다. \"보다시피 중년 사내들이 이기는 법이니까.\"\n\n치철리 씨는 자못 의미심장하게 고개를 저었다. 그는 자기가 택할 여자에게 틀림없이 받아들여질 위험을 무릅쓸 생각이 없었던 것이다.\n\n치철리 씨의 이상이 되는 영광을 누린 그 미스 빈시는 물론 그 자리에 없었으니, 언제나 도를 넘는 것을 꺼리는 브룩 씨가, 공적인 자리가 아니라면 자기 조카딸들이 미들마치 제조업자의 딸과 만나게 하기를 택하지 않았을 것이기 때문이다. 모인 사람들 중 여성 쪽에는 체텀 부인이나 캐드월래더 부인이 트집 잡을 만한 이가 아무도 없었다. 대령의 미망인인 렌프루 부인은 가문으로 보아 나무랄 데 없을뿐더러, 의사들을 당혹케 하는 그녀의 병 때문에도 흥미로운 인물이었는데, 그 병은 분명 전문적 지식의 충만함이 돌팔이 치료의 보충을 필요로 할 법한 사례로 보였다. 자기 자신의 놀라운 건강을 집에서 만든 쓴 약과 끊임없는 진료의 결합 덕분이라고 여기는 체텀 부인은, 렌프루 부인의 증상 설명에, 그리고 그녀의 경우 온갖 강장제가 놀랍도록 효과 없다는 이야기에 상상력을 한껏 발휘하며 빠져들었다.\n\n\"그 약들의 그 모든 약효가 다 어디로 가는 걸까요, 부인?\" 온화하면서도 위엄 있는 노부인이, 렌프루 부인의 주의가 다른 데로 돌려졌을 때, 캐드월래더 부인을 향해 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"병을 튼튼하게 만드는 거죠.\" 의술의 아마추어가 되기에 충분할 만큼 좋은 집안에서 태어난 교구 목사 부인이 말했다. \"모든 것은 체질에 달려 있어요. 어떤 사람은 살을 만들고, 어떤 사람은 피를 만들고, 또 어떤 사람은 담즙을 만들지요—제 견해로는 그래요. 그러니 그들이 무엇을 먹든 그건 일종의 방앗간에 들어가는 곡물 같은 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 부인은, 부인 말씀이 옳다면, 줄여 주는—병을 줄여 주는 약을 먹어야겠군요, 안 그래요. 그리고 부인 말씀이 일리가 있다고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"분명 일리가 있지요. 같은 흙에서 기른 감자가 두 종류 있다고 쳐요. 그중 하나는 점점 더 물기가 많아지고—\"\n\n\"아! 이 가엾은 렌프루 부인처럼 말이지요—제 생각도 그래요. 수종(水腫)이에요! 아직 붓지는 않았지만—속으로 그런 거지요. 제 생각엔 말리는 약을 먹어야 할 것 같아요, 안 그래요?—아니면 건조한 열풍 목욕이라든가. 말리는 성질의 것이라면 여러 가지를 시도해 볼 만하겠지요.\"\n\n\"어떤 분의 소책자들을 한번 읽어 보시라고 하지요.\" 신사들이 들어오는 것을 보며 캐드월래더 부인이 나직이 말했다. \"그분은 말릴 필요가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"누구 말씀이에요, 부인?\" 매력적인 여성이지만, 설명하는 즐거움을 앗아 갈 만큼 눈치가 빠르지는 않은 체텀 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"신랑요—캐소본 말이에요. 그분은 약혼 이래로 확실히 더 빨리 말라 가고 있어요. 정열의 불꽃 탓이겠지요.\"\n\n\"그분은 결코 좋은 체질을 가졌다고 할 수 없을 것 같아요.\" 체텀 부인이 한층 더 나직한 어조로 말했다. \"게다가 그 연구라는 것도—부인 말씀처럼, 어찌나 메마른지.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 제임스 경 곁에 서 있으면 그분은 마치 이 자리를 위해 가죽을 씌워 놓은 해골 같아 보여요. 제 말을 새겨 두세요. 지금부터 일 년 뒤면 저 아가씨는 그분을 미워하게 될 거예요. 지금은 그분을 신탁(神託)인 양 우러러보지만, 머지않아 정반대 극단으로 치닫게 될 거예요. 죄다 변덕이지요!\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 충격적인 말씀을! 그 애가 고집이 세지 않을까 걱정이에요. 하지만 말씀해 보세요—부인은 그분에 대해 다 아시잖아요—뭔가 아주 나쁜 점이라도 있나요? 진실이 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"진실이요? 그분은 잘못 처방된 약만큼이나 나쁜 사람이에요—먹기에 역겹고, 틀림없이 몸에 안 맞지요.\"\n\n\"그보다 더 나쁜 건 없겠군요.\" 체텀 부인이, 그 약에 대해 어찌나 생생하게 떠올렸던지 캐소본 씨의 단점에 관해 무언가 정확한 것을 알게 된 양 말했다. \"하지만 제임스는 미스 브룩에 대해 험담은 한마디도 들으려 하지 않아요. 여전히 그 애가 여성의 귀감이라고 한답니다.\"\n\n\"그건 그 사람의 너그러운 자기기만이지요. 틀림없이 그 사람은 어린 실리아를 더 좋아하고, 그 애는 그를 알아본답니다. 제 어린 실리아를 마음에 들어 하시기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"물론이지요. 그 애는 제라늄을 더 좋아하고, 더 온순해 보여요. 비록 그만큼 빼어난 자태는 아니지만요. 그런데 우리가 약 얘기를 하고 있었지요. 이 새로 온 젊은 외과의 리드게이트 씨에 대해 말씀해 주세요. 놀랄 만큼 영리하다고들 하던데요. 확실히 그렇게 보여요—참으로 훌륭한 이마예요.\"\n\n\"신사예요. 그분이 험프리와 얘기하는 걸 들었어요. 말을 잘하더군요.\"\n\n\"그래요. 브룩 씨 말씀이 그분은 노섬벌랜드의 리드게이트 가문 사람으로, 정말 좋은 집안과 연결되어 있다더군요. 그런 부류의 개업의에게서는 기대하기 어려운 일이지요. 저로 말하자면, 하인들과 좀 더 비슷한 처지의 의사가 좋아요. 그런 이들이 흔히 오히려 더 영리하거든요. 정말이지, 저는 가엾은 힉스의 판단이 틀림이 없다는 걸 알았어요. 그가 틀리는 걸 본 적이 없답니다. 그는 거칠고 푸주한 같았지만, 제 체질을 잘 알았어요. 그가 그토록 갑작스레 세상을 떠난 건 제겐 손실이었지요. 어머나, 미스 브룩이 이 리드게이트 씨와 어찌나 활기차게 대화를 나누는지 좀 보세요!\"\n\n\"그분과 오두막이며 병원 얘기를 하고 있는 거예요.\" 귀와 해석하는 능력이 빠른 캐드월래더 부인이 말했다. \"내 생각엔 그분이 일종의 자선가인 모양인데, 그러니 브룩이 틀림없이 그를 후원하려 들 거예요.\"\n\n\"제임스.\" 아들이 가까이 오자 체텀 부인이 말했다. \"리드게이트 씨를 데려와 내게 소개해 다오. 그 사람을 시험해 보고 싶구나.\"\n\n붙임성 좋은 노부인은 리드게이트 씨가 새로운 방식으로 열병을 치료해 성공을 거두었다는 이야기를 들은 터라, 그와 알게 될 이 기회가 무척 기쁘다고 밝혔다.\n\n리드게이트 씨는 자신에게 어떤 허튼소리를 늘어놓든 완벽하게 진지한 표정을 짓는 의료인다운 소양을 갖추고 있었고, 그의 검고 흔들림 없는 눈은 그를 인상적인 경청자로 만들어 주었다. 그는 애석한 힉스와는 가능한 한 닮은 데가 없었으니, 특히 몸단장과 말투에 어린 어떤 무심한 세련됨에서 그러했다. 그럼에도 체텀 부인은 그에게서 큰 신뢰를 느꼈다. 그는 모든 체질이 특이하다고 불릴 수 있다고 인정함으로써 그녀 자신의 체질이 특이하다는 그녀의 견해를 확인해 주었으며, 그녀의 체질이 다른 이들보다 더 특이할지도 모른다는 것을 부인하지 않았다. 그는 무모한 부항을 포함한 지나치게 기력을 떨어뜨리는 요법도, 다른 한편으로 끊임없는 포트와인과 기나피(幾那皮)도 찬성하지 않았다. 그는 \"저도 그렇게 생각합니다\"라고, 동의라는 통찰에 곁들여 깍듯한 공손함을 어찌나 풍기며 말했던지, 그녀는 그의 재능에 대해 더없이 호의적인 견해를 갖게 되었다.\n\n\"당신의 보호 대상에게 아주 만족하고 있어요.\" 그녀가 떠나기 전에 브룩 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"내 보호 대상이라니?—이런!—그게 누구요?\" 브룩 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이 젊은 리드게이트, 새로 온 의사 말이에요. 제가 보기에 그 사람은 자기 직업을 훌륭히 이해하고 있는 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"오, 리드게이트! 그는 내 보호 대상이 아니오, 알다시피. 다만 그에 관해 내게 편지를 보낸 그의 삼촌을 내가 알았을 뿐이지. 그래도 그는 일류가 될 가능성이 있을 것 같소—파리에서 공부했고, 브루세를 알았다지. 사상이 있단 말이오, 알다시피—이 직업의 수준을 끌어올리려 하지.\"\n\n\"리드게이트는 환기며 식이며, 뭐 그런 것들에 관해 아주 새로운 사상을 잔뜩 갖고 있소.\" 브룩 씨가 체텀 부인을 배웅하고 나서 한 무리의 미들마치 사람들에게 예의를 갖추려고 돌아온 뒤에 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제기랄, 그게 과연 온당하다고 보시오?—잉글랜드 사람들을 지금의 그들로 만들어 준 그 옛 치료법을 뒤엎는 게 말이오?\" 스탠디시 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"의학 지식은 우리 사이에서 바닥을 치고 있소이다.\" 가라앉은 어조로 말하는, 다소 병약한 기색의 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다. \"나로 말하자면, 리드게이트 씨의 출현을 환영하오. 그에게 새 병원의 운영을 맡길 만한 좋은 이유를 찾게 되기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"그것 참 그럴듯한 말이오만.\" 벌스트로드 씨를 좋아하지 않는 스탠디시 씨가 대꾸했다. \"당신이 그더러 당신 병원 환자들을 상대로 실험을 해 보게 하고, 자선이랍시고 몇 사람 죽이는 거야 나는 이의 없소. 하지만 나는 내 돈을 지갑에서 꺼내 나를 상대로 실험하게 할 생각은 없소이다. 나는 어느 정도 검증된 치료가 좋단 말이오.\"\n\n\"이보시오, 알다시피, 스탠디시, 당신이 먹는 약 한 알 한 알이 다 실험이오—실험이란 말이오, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 변호사를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그런 식으로 말씀하신다면야!\" 스탠디시 씨가, 소중한 의뢰인에게 사람이 드러낼 수 있는 한껏, 그런 비법률적인 궤변에 대한 역겨움을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 그레인저처럼 나를 해골로 만들어 놓지 않고 낫게 해 주는 치료라면 무엇이든 반갑겠소.\" 시장인 빈시 씨가 말했는데, 그는 혈색 좋은 사람으로, 벌스트로드 씨의 프란체스코회 수도사 같은 빛깔과 두드러지게 대조되는 살집의 표본으로 쓸 만한 인물이었다. \"누군가 말했듯이, 병의 화살에 맞서 아무런 완충도 없이 남겨진다는 건 유달리 위험한 일이오—나도 그게 아주 좋은 표현이라고 생각하오만.\"\n\n물론 리드게이트 씨는 그 말을 듣지 못했다. 그는 일찍이 그 모임을 떠났고, 몇몇 사람을 새로 소개받은 신선함이 아니었다면, 특히 미스 브룩을 소개받은 것이 아니었다면, 그 자리를 완전히 따분하게 여겼을 것이다. 그녀의 젊은 꽃다움은, 저 빛바랜 학자와의 임박한 결혼과 사회적으로 유용한 일에 대한 관심과 어우러져, 그녀에게 보기 드문 조합의 묘미를 부여했다.\n\n'착한 사람이야—저 멋진 아가씨는—하지만 좀 지나치게 진지해.' 그는 생각했다. '그런 여자들과 이야기하는 건 성가신 일이야. 그들은 늘 이유를 알고 싶어 하면서도, 정작 어떤 문제의 핵심을 이해하기엔 너무 무지해서, 결국 자기 입맛대로 일을 매듭짓느라 도덕 감각에 기대고 마니까.'\n\n명백히 미스 브룩은 치철리 씨에게나 마찬가지로 리드게이트 씨의 취향의 여자도 아니었다. 사실 정신이 무르익은 후자와의 관계에서 보자면, 그녀는 전적으로 하나의 실수였으며, 자줏빛 얼굴의 독신남에게 어울리도록 빚어진 멋진 젊은 여성을 비롯한 목적인(目的因)에 대한 그의 신뢰를 뒤흔들기에 알맞았다. 그러나 리드게이트는 덜 무르익은 사람이었기에, 어쩌면 그의 앞에는 여성에게서 가장 빼어난 것에 관한 그의 견해를 바꿔 놓을 경험이 놓여 있을지도 몰랐다.\n\n그러나 미스 브룩은 이 두 신사 중 누구에게도 다시는 처녀 적 이름으로 보이지 않았다. 그 만찬회로부터 그리 오래지 않아 그녀는 캐소본 부인이 되었고, 로마로 가는 길에 올랐던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XI.\n\n죄악이 아닌 인간의 어리석음과 희롱하려 할 때.\n—벤 존슨.\n\n리드게이트는 사실 브룩 양과는 현저히 다른 한 여인에게 이미 매혹되었음을 의식하고 있었다. 그는 자신이 평정을 잃고 사랑에 빠졌다고는 조금도 생각하지 않았지만, 그 특정한 여인에 대해 이렇게 말했다. \"그녀는 우아함 그 자체다. 더없이 사랑스럽고 교양 있다. 여자란 마땅히 그래야 하는 법이다. 여자는 절묘한 음악 같은 효과를 자아내야 한다.\" 그는 평범하게 생긴 여자들을 인생의 다른 가혹한 사실들과 마찬가지로, 철학으로 마주하고 과학으로 탐구해야 할 대상으로 여겼다. 그러나 로저먼드 빈시는 진정한 선율의 매력을 지닌 듯 보였다. 그리고 어떤 남자가 만약 서둘러 결혼할 작정이었다면 골랐을 법한 여인을 본 경우, 그가 계속 독신으로 남느냐는 대개 그 자신의 결심보다 그녀의 결심에 달려 있는 법이다. 리드게이트는 자신이 몇 해 동안은 결혼하지 않으리라 믿었다. 이미 닦여 있는 널찍한 큰길을 벗어나 자기만의 또렷한 길을 다져 놓기 전에는 결혼하지 않으리라고. 그가 빈시 양을 자신의 지평선 위로 바라본 기간은, 캐소본이 약혼하고 결혼하기까지 걸린 시간과 거의 맞먹었다. 하지만 그 학식 있는 신사는 재산을 지니고 있었고, 방대한 노트를 모아 두었으며, 업적에 앞서는 그런 종류의 명성을 쌓아 두었다. 그것은 흔히 한 인간이 누리는 명성의 대부분을 차지한다. 그는 우리가 본 대로, 자기 인생행로의 남은 사분면을 장식해 줄, 거의 계산할 가치도 없을 만큼 미미한 섭동을 일으킬 작은 달 하나로서 아내를 맞이했다. 그러나 리드게이트는 젊고 가난하며 야심에 차 있었다. 그에게는 반세기의 세월이 등 뒤가 아니라 눈앞에 놓여 있었으니, 그는 자기 재산을 늘리거나 심지어 넉넉한 수입을 확보하는 데 직접 어울리지도 않는 수많은 일들을 해내겠다는 결심을 품고 미들마치로 왔던 것이다. 이런 처지에 놓인 남자에게 아내를 맞이하는 일은, 그가 아무리 장식의 가치를 높이 평가한다 해도, 단순한 장식의 문제 이상이다. 그런데도 리드게이트는 아내의 역할 중에서 그것을 첫째 자리에 두려는 쪽이었다. 단 한 번의 대화로 다듬어진 그의 취향으로 보건대, 바로 이 점에서 브룩 양은 부정할 수 없는 미모에도 불구하고 부족함이 드러날 터였다. 그녀는 여성에게 마땅한 각도에서 사물을 바라보지 않았다. 그런 여자들과 어울리는 것은, 새소리 같은 달콤한 웃음과 천국 같은 푸른 눈에 둘러싸여 낙원에 비스듬히 누워 쉬는 것이 아니라, 일터를 떠나 이학년 학생들을 가르치러 가는 것만큼이나 마음을 편안하게 풀어 주는 일이었다.\n\n분명 지금 이 순간 리드게이트에게 브룩 양의 마음의 성향만큼 사소해 보이는 것도 없었고, 브룩 양에게 이 젊은 외과 의사를 사로잡은 여인의 자질만큼 하찮아 보이는 것도 없었다. 하지만 인간의 운명이 은밀히 수렴해 가는 모습을 예리하게 지켜보는 사람이라면, 한 생애가 다른 생애에 미치는 영향이 더디게 준비되는 광경을 보게 된다. 그것은 마치 우리가 미처 소개받지 못한 이웃을 무관심하게, 혹은 얼어붙은 시선으로 바라볼 때 계산된 아이러니처럼 느껴지는 것이다. 운명은 우리의 등장인물들을 손안에 접어 쥔 채, 비웃듯이 곁에 서 있다.\n\n이 오래된 지방 사회에도 그 미묘한 움직임의 몫이 있었다. 거기에는 눈에 띄는 몰락도 있었으니, 화려하던 젊은 전문직 멋쟁이가 결국 칙칙한 여자와 여섯 아이를 살림이랍시고 거느린 채 골목 단칸방에서 연명하는 신세로 끝나기도 했다. 그러나 그뿐 아니라, 사교의 경계를 끊임없이 옮겨 놓으며 상호 의존이라는 새로운 의식을 낳는, 그보다 덜 두드러진 부침도 있었다. 어떤 이는 약간 아래로 미끄러졌고, 어떤 이는 더 높은 발판을 얻었다. 어떤 이들은 무성음을 제대로 발음하지 못하면서도 부를 거머쥐었고, 까다로운 신사들은 자치 도시의 의원으로 출마했다. 어떤 이들은 정치의 물결에, 어떤 이들은 교회의 물결에 휩쓸렸고, 그 결과 의외의 무리로 분류된 자신을 발견하기도 했다. 한편 이 모든 변동 속에서도 바위처럼 굳건히 버티고 선 몇몇 인물이나 가문은, 그 견고함에도 불구하고 서서히 새로운 면모를 드러냈으니, 자신과 보는 이가 함께 변하는 이중의 변화 속에서 달라져 갔다. 도시의 자치구와 농촌의 교구는 차츰 새로운 연결의 실을 자아냈다. 그것은 더디게 이루어졌으니, 옛날의 양말 속 비상금이 저축은행에 자리를 내주고, 황금빛 기니화를 숭배하던 풍습이 사라져 갈 무렵의 일이었다. 한편 한때 도시민의 의식과는 흠 잡힐 일 없이 멀리 떨어져 살던 향사와 준남작들, 심지어 영주들까지도 가까이 교류하면서 생기는 결점을 떠안게 되었다. 게다가 먼 주에서 정착민들이 찾아왔으니, 어떤 이는 놀랄 만큼 새로운 기술을 지녔고, 어떤 이는 교활함이라는 거슬리는 이점을 지녔다. 사실 옛 잉글랜드에서도 더 오래된 헤로도토스에게서 발견되는 것과 거의 같은 종류의 움직임과 뒤섞임이 일어났던 것이다. 그 또한 지난 일을 이야기하면서 한 여인의 운명을 출발점으로 삼는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 다만 이오는 매력적인 상품에 홀린 듯한 처녀였으니 브룩 양과는 정반대였고, 이 점에서는 오히려 로저먼드 빈시와 더 닮았는지도 모른다. 로저먼드는 의상에 탁월한 안목을 지녔고, 휘감기는 천의 흐름과 빛깔을 가장 폭넓게 고를 수 있게 해 주는 그 요정 같은 몸매와 순결한 금발을 지니고 있었다. 그러나 이런 것들은 그녀가 지닌 매력의 일부에 지나지 않았다. 그녀는 그 주에서 으뜸가는 학교인 레먼 부인의 학교에서도 꽃으로 인정받았으니, 그곳의 교육은 교양 있는 여성에게 요구되는 모든 것을 망라했다. 마차에 오르내리는 법 같은 부수적인 항목까지도. 레먼 부인 자신은 늘 빈시 양을 모범으로 내세웠다. 그녀의 말로는, 지식의 습득과 말씨의 단정함에서 그 아가씨를 능가하는 학생은 없었으며, 음악 연주 솜씨는 그야말로 발군이었다. 사람들이 우리를 두고 어떻게 말하는지를 우리가 어찌할 수는 없는 노릇이다. 아마 레먼 부인이 줄리엣이나 이모진을 묘사하는 일을 맡았더라면, 그 여주인공들조차 시적으로 보이지는 않았으리라. 로저먼드를 처음 본 광경이면 대부분의 판단자에게는 레먼 부인의 칭찬이 불러일으킨 어떤 편견도 말끔히 씻어 내기에 충분했을 것이다.\n\n리드게이트가 미들마치에 머무는 동안, 그 흐뭇한 광경을 보지 않고서는, 아니 심지어 빈시 가족과 알고 지내지 않고서는 오래 지낼 수가 없었다. 그가 얼마간 돈을 치르고 진료를 넘겨받은 피콕 씨가 빈시 가의 주치의는 아니었지만—빈시 부인이 그의 쇠약 요법을 좋아하지 않았던 까닭이었다—그래도 빈시 가의 친척과 지인 중에는 그의 환자가 많았다. 미들마치에서 웬만큼 비중 있는 사람치고 빈시 가와 인척이거나 적어도 알고 지내지 않는 이가 어디 있겠는가? 그들은 오래된 제조업자 집안으로 삼 대에 걸쳐 번듯한 살림을 꾸려 왔고, 그 살림 안에서는 자연히 다소 분명한 양반 행세를 하는 이웃들과 통혼하는 일이 잦았다. 빈시 씨의 누이는 불스트로드 씨를 받아들임으로써 부유한 혼처와 맺어졌는데, 다만 불스트로드 씨는 이 도시 태생이 아니고 출신도 도무지 알 수 없는 인물이었던 터라, 진짜 미들마치 가문과 결합한 것은 그로서는 잘한 일로 여겨졌다. 반면 빈시 씨는 여관 주인의 딸을 아내로 맞았으니 신분이 조금 내려간 셈이었다. 그러나 이쪽에도 흐뭇한 재물의 기운이 감돌았다. 빈시 부인의 언니가 부유한 노인 페더스톤 씨의 후처였다가 여러 해 전에 자식 없이 세상을 떠난 까닭에, 그 조카들이 홀아비가 된 노인의 애정을 건드릴 수 있으리라 여겨졌던 것이다. 그런데 마침 피콕의 가장 중요한 환자 둘인 불스트로드 씨와 페더스톤 씨가 저마다 다른 이유로 그의 후임자를 유달리 호의적으로 맞아들였으니, 그 후임자는 논쟁뿐 아니라 어느 정도 편가르기까지 불러일으킨 인물이었다. 빈시 가의 주치의인 렌치 씨는 일찌감치 리드게이트의 직업적 분별을 가볍게 여길 만한 근거를 얻었고, 손님이 잦은 빈시 가에서 그에 관한 소문치고 입에 오르내리지 않는 것이 없었다. 빈시 씨는 어느 편을 드는 쪽보다 두루 사람 좋게 지내는 쪽으로 기우는 사람이었지만, 그렇다고 새 인물과 굳이 서둘러 사귈 필요도 없었다. 로저먼드는 아버지가 리드게이트 씨를 초대해 주기를 속으로 바랐다. 그녀는 늘 보아 온 얼굴과 모습들에—소년 시절부터 알고 지낸 그 미들마치 청년들의 제각각 고르지 못한 옆모습이며 걸음걸이며 말버릇에 싫증이 나 있었다. 그녀는 학교에서 더 지체 높은 집안의 아가씨들과 함께 지냈는데, 그들의 오라비라면 이 어쩔 수 없이 가까운 미들마치 친구들보다 자신이 더 관심을 가질 수 있었으리라고 확신했다. 그러나 그런 바람을 아버지에게 입 밖에 내려고는 하지 않았고, 아버지는 아버지대로 그 문제에 전혀 서두르지 않았다. 곧 시장이 될 시 참사회원이라면 머지않아 만찬 모임을 늘려야 할 테지만, 당장은 그의 푸짐한 식탁에 손님이 차고 넘쳤다.\n\n그 식탁은 흔히 빈시 씨가 둘째 아들을 데리고 창고로 떠난 뒤에도, 그리고 모건 양이 교실에서 어린 딸들과 함께 오전 수업을 한참 진행하고 있을 때에도, 가족의 아침 식사 흔적으로 덮인 채 그대로 놓여 있었다. 식탁은 집안의 늦잠꾸러기를 기다리고 있었으니, 그는 부르는 소리에 일어나는 것보다는 (남들에게) 온갖 불편을 끼치는 편이 덜 불쾌하다고 여겼다. 우리가 얼마 전 캐소본 씨가 그레인지를 방문하는 모습을 보았던 바로 그 시월의 어느 아침이 그러했다. 방 안은 난롯불로 약간 후끈하여 스패니얼이 헐떡이며 멀찍한 구석으로 물러갔는데도, 로저먼드는 무슨 까닭에서인지 평소보다 오래 자수 앞에 앉아 있었다. 이따금 몸을 가볍게 떨고는, 일감을 무릎에 내려놓고 망설이는 듯한 권태로운 기색으로 그것을 바라보았다. 부엌에 잠깐 다녀온 그녀의 어머니는 한결 온전히 평온한 기색으로 작은 작업 탁자 맞은편에 앉아 있다가, 시계가 다시 종을 칠 참이라고 알리자, 통통한 손가락으로 매만지던 레이스 수선에서 눈을 들어 종을 울렸다.\n\n\"프리처드, 프레드 도련님 방문을 다시 두드려서, 벌써 열 시 반이 쳤다고 일러 주게.\"\n\n이 말은 빈시 부인의 환하게 좋은 기분이 어린 얼굴에 아무런 변화도 일으키지 않은 채 흘러나왔다. 그 얼굴에는 사십오 년 세월이 모난 주름이나 나란한 골을 새겨 놓지 않았다. 그녀는 분홍빛 모자 끈을 뒤로 밀어 넘기고 일감을 무릎에 내려놓은 채, 흐뭇한 눈길로 딸을 바라보았다.\n\n\"엄마,\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"프레드가 내려오면 청어구이는 못 먹게 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요. 아침 이 시각에 온 집 안에 그 냄새가 진동하는 걸 견딜 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"오, 얘야, 너는 오라비들한테 어쩜 그리 모질더냐! 너한테서 흠잡을 데라고는 그것 하나뿐이란다. 너는 세상에서 제일 다정한 성품이면서도, 오라비들한테는 어찌나 까다롭게 구는지.\"\n\n\"까다로운 게 아니에요, 엄마. 제가 숙녀답지 못하게 말하는 건 한 번도 들어 보신 적 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 하지만 너는 오라비들이 뭘 하려고 하면 안 된다고 막으려 들잖니.\"\n\n\"오라비들은 너무 거북한걸요.\"\n\n\"오, 얘야, 젊은 남자들이려니 하고 봐줘야지. 마음씨만 착하면 고맙게 여길 일이야. 여자는 사소한 일쯤은 참아 넘기는 법을 배워야 한단다. 너도 언젠가는 결혼할 텐데.\"\n\n\"프레드 같은 사람한테는 안 해요.\"\n\n\"제 오라비를 깎아내리지 말려무나, 얘야. 학위를 못 딴 것만 빼면 그 애만큼 나무랄 데 적은 젊은이도 드물단다—왜 못 땄는지 나는 도무지 알 수가 없구나, 내가 보기엔 더없이 영리한 아이인데 말이야. 너도 알다시피 그 애는 대학에서 최상류 사교계에도 손색없다고들 했잖니. 너처럼 까다로운 애가, 그렇게 신사다운 청년을 오라비로 두고도 기뻐하지 않다니 의아하구나. 너는 늘 밥이 프레드가 아니라고 트집을 잡잖니.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 엄마. 그저 밥은 밥이라서 그런 거예요.\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야, 미들마치 청년치고 흠 한 가지 없는 사람은 못 찾을 거다.\"\n\n\"하지만—\" 여기서 로저먼드의 얼굴이 미소로 활짝 피어나며 보조개 두 개가 불쑥 드러났다. 그녀 자신은 이 보조개를 못마땅하게 여겨 사람들 앞에서는 좀처럼 웃지 않았다. \"하지만 저는 미들마치 청년하고는 결혼하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런 듯하구나, 얘야. 그중 으뜸가는 사람들도 사실상 마다했으니 말이다. 그리고 더 나은 사람이 있다면야, 너만큼 그걸 누릴 자격이 있는 아가씨도 없고말고.\"\n\n\"죄송하지만 엄마—'그중 으뜸'이라는 말은 안 하셨으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼 달리 뭐라 하겠니?\"\n\n\"그러니까 엄마, 그건 좀 천박한 표현이에요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지, 얘야. 나는 워낙 말솜씨가 변변치 못했으니까. 그럼 뭐라고 해야 하느냐?\"\n\n\"'그중 가장 나은 사람들'이라고요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그것도 똑같이 밋밋하고 흔해 빠진 말 같은데. 내가 생각할 틈이 있었다면 '가장 뛰어난 청년들'이라고 했을 거다. 하지만 네가 받은 교육이 있으니 네가 더 잘 알겠지.\"\n\n\"엄마, 로지가 뭘 더 잘 안다는 거예요?\" 프레드 씨가 말했다. 그는 부인들이 일감 위로 몸을 숙이고 있는 사이에 반쯤 열린 문으로 슬그머니 들어왔고, 이제 난롯가로 가서 등을 불 쪽으로 향한 채 슬리퍼 바닥을 데우며 서 있었다.\n\n\"'뛰어난 청년들'이라고 말하는 게 옳은지를 말이다,\" 빈시 부인이 종을 울리며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 요즘은 '뛰어난' 차에 '뛰어난' 설탕이 어찌나 많은지. 뛰어나다는 말은 이제 가게 주인들의 은어가 돼 가고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 너도 은어가 싫어지기 시작한 거니?\" 로저먼드가 온화하면서도 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"잘못 쓰는 종류만요. 말을 골라 쓰는 건 죄다 은어예요. 그게 계층을 드러내거든요.\"\n\n\"올바른 영어라는 게 있잖아. 그건 은어가 아니야.\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 올바른 영어란 역사책이며 수필을 쓰는 잘난 척하는 자들의 은어야. 그리고 그중에서도 가장 지독한 은어는 시인들의 은어고.\"\n\n\"프레드, 너는 자기 주장을 관철하려고 무슨 말이든 다 하는구나.\"\n\n\"그럼 황소를 두고 '다리를 엮는 짐승'이라 부르는 게 은어인지 시인지 말해 봐.\"\n\n\"물론 네가 시라고 부르고 싶으면 시겠지.\"\n\n\"하하, 로지 양, 너는 호메로스와 은어도 구별 못 하는구나. 새 놀이를 하나 만들어야겠다. 종이쪽지에 은어 토막이랑 시 토막을 적어서 너한테 줄 테니 가려내 보라고 해야지.\"\n\n\"어머나, 젊은 사람들 이야기를 듣고 있으면 어쩜 이리 재미있을까!\" 빈시 부인이 흐뭇하게 감탄하며 말했다.\n\n\"프리처드, 내 아침으로 줄 게 이것 말고는 없나?\" 프레드가 커피와 버터 바른 토스트를 가져온 하인에게 말하면서, 햄이며 항아리에 담은 쇠고기며 그 밖의 식은 음식들을 둘러보며 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌았다. 말없이 거절하는 기색이었지만, 역겨워하는 내색은 점잖게 삼갔다.\n\n\"달걀은 어떠시겠어요, 도련님?\"\n\n\"달걀, 안 돼! 뼈에 붙은 고기를 구워서 가져와.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 프레드,\" 하인이 방을 나가자 로저먼드가 말했다. \"기어이 아침으로 뜨거운 걸 먹어야겠으면, 좀 일찍 내려오면 좋겠어. 사냥하러 나가려고 여섯 시에는 일어나면서, 다른 날 아침엔 왜 그렇게 일어나기를 힘들어하는지 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"그건 네가 이해를 못 해서 그래, 로지. 사냥하러 갈 때는 내가 좋아서 일어나는 거니까.\"\n\n\"내가 만약 다른 사람들보다 두 시간이나 늦게 내려와서 뼈구이를 시킨다면 나를 어떻게 생각하겠어?\"\n\n\"보기 드물게 행실이 헤픈 아가씨라고 생각하겠지,\" 프레드가 더없이 태연하게 토스트를 먹으며 말했다.\n\n\"오라비들이 누이들보다 더 거북하게 굴어야 할 이유를 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"내가 거북하게 구는 게 아니라, 네가 나를 거북하게 여기는 거야. 거북하다는 건 내 행동이 아니라 네 기분을 설명하는 말이라고.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 그게 뼈구이 냄새를 설명하는 말 같은데.\"\n\n\"천만에. 그건 네 조그만 코의 어떤 감각을 설명하는 말이지. 레먼 부인 학교의 고전이라 할 만한 그 까탈스러운 관념들과 결부된 감각 말이야. 우리 어머니를 봐. 어머니는 당신이 직접 하는 일 말고는 무엇에도 트집을 잡지 않으시잖아. 어머니야말로 내가 생각하는 유쾌한 여성이야.\"\n\n\"오누이 둘 다 복 받으렴, 얘들아, 그리고 다투지들 마라,\" 빈시 부인이 어머니다운 다정함으로 말했다. \"자, 프레드, 새로 온 의사 이야기나 다 들려주렴. 네 외삼촌은 그 사람을 얼마나 마음에 들어 하시던?\"\n\n\"꽤 마음에 들어 하시는 것 같아요. 리드게이트한테 온갖 질문을 던져 놓고는, 대답을 듣는 동안 마치 누가 발가락이라도 꼬집는 것처럼 얼굴을 잔뜩 찡그리시죠. 그게 외삼촌 방식이에요. 아, 여기 내 뼈구이가 오는군.\"\n\n\"그런데 얘야, 어쩌다 그리 늦게까지 밖에 있었니? 외삼촌 댁에 간다고만 했잖니.\"\n\n\"오, 플림데일 댁에서 저녁을 먹었어요. 휘스트를 했죠. 리드게이트도 거기 있었어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 사람을 어떻게 생각하니? 무척 신사답겠지, 아마. 사람들 말로는 훌륭한 집안 출신이라던데—친척들이 다 지체 높은 시골 양반들이라고.\"\n\n\"네,\" 프레드가 말했다. \"존스 칼리지에 리드게이트라는 사람이 있었는데 돈을 펑펑 썼어요. 알고 보니 이 사람이 그 사람의 육촌이더라고요. 하지만 부자라고 해서 육촌까지 다 형편이 좋으란 법은 없죠.\"\n\n\"그래도 좋은 집안 출신이라는 건 늘 차이가 나는 법이지,\" 로저먼드가 단호한 어조로 말했는데, 그 어조는 그녀가 이 문제를 곰곰 생각해 보았음을 드러냈다. 로저먼드는 자신이 미들마치 제조업자의 딸이 아니었더라면 더 행복했으리라고 느꼈다. 그녀는 외할아버지가 여관 주인이었다는 사실을 떠올리게 하는 것이라면 무엇이든 싫어했다. 분명 그 사실을 기억하는 사람이라면 누구든, 빈시 부인이 신사들의 변덕스럽기 짝이 없는 주문에 이골이 난, 매우 잘생기고 쾌활한 여관 안주인 같은 분위기를 풍긴다고 여길 만했다.\n\n\"그 사람 이름이 터티어스라는 게 이상하다 싶었는데,\" 환한 얼굴의 부인이 말했다. \"물론 집안 내림 이름이겠지. 그건 그렇고, 그 사람이 정확히 어떤 사람인지 말해 보렴.\"\n\n\"오, 키가 큰 편이고, 거무스름하고, 영리하고—말도 잘하고—제법 잘난 척하는 친구예요.\"\n\n\"네가 잘난 척한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 모르겠어,\" 로저먼드가 말했다.\n\n\"자기한테 의견이 있다는 걸 내보이고 싶어 하는 사람이지.\"\n\n\"아니, 얘야, 의사라면 의견이 있어야지,\" 빈시 부인이 말했다. \"그게 아니면 의사가 무엇하러 있겠니?\"\n\n\"그래요, 어머니, 돈을 받고 내놓는 의견이라면 그렇죠. 하지만 잘난 척하는 사람이란 늘 자기 의견을 선물처럼 안겨 주는 사람을 말해요.\"\n\n\"메리 가스가 리드게이트 씨를 흠모하는 모양이지,\" 로저먼드가 은근히 빈정대는 기색을 섞어 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠는데.\" 프레드가 다소 시무룩하게 말하며 식탁에서 일어나, 가지고 내려온 소설책을 집어 들고는 안락의자에 몸을 던졌다. \"그 애가 질투 나거든 네가 직접 스톤 코트에 더 자주 가서 빛이 안 나게 눌러 버리든가.\"\n\n\"제발 그렇게 천박하게 굴지 좀 마, 프레드. 다 먹었으면 종이나 좀 울려 줘.\"\n\n\"하지만 네 오라비 말이 맞다, 로저먼드,\" 하인이 식탁을 치우고 나자 빈시 부인이 입을 열었다. \"네가 외삼촌을 더 자주 찾아뵐 인내심이 없다는 건 천번만번 아까운 일이야. 그분이 너를 그토록 자랑스러워하시고, 너더러 같이 살자고까지 하셨는데. 프레드뿐 아니라 너한테도 무엇을 해 주셨을지 누가 알겠니. 하느님은 아시겠지만, 나야 너를 곁에 두고 사는 게 좋단다. 그래도 자식들 잘되라고 떠나보낼 수는 있는 거야. 그리고 이제 외삼촌 페더스톤 씨가 메리 가스한테 무언가 해 주실 게 뻔한 노릇이고.\"\n\n\"메리 가스는 스톤 코트에 있는 걸 견딜 수 있죠. 가정 교사 노릇 하는 것보다 그게 더 좋으니까요,\" 로저먼드가 일감을 개키며 말했다. \"외삼촌의 기침이며 그 흉한 친척들을 한참 참아 가며 얻어 내야 한다면, 저는 차라리 아무것도 안 물려받는 편이 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"그분이 이 세상에 오래 계실 수는 없을 거다, 얘야. 내가 그분 임종을 재촉할 생각이야 없지만, 천식에다 그 속병까지 있으니, 저세상에서나마 더 나은 것이 그분을 기다리고 있기를 바라야지. 그리고 나는 메리 가스한테 악감정은 없다만, 공평함이라는 것도 생각해야지. 게다가 페더스톤 씨의 첫 부인은 우리 언니처럼 그분께 재산을 가져다주지도 않았잖니. 그 조카들이 우리 언니 조카들만큼 권리를 주장할 수는 없는 노릇이지. 그리고 솔직히 말해서 나는 메리 가스가 정말 못생긴 처녀라고 생각한다—가정 교사가 차라리 더 어울려.\"\n\n\"누구나 그 점에서 어머니 의견에 동의하지는 않을걸요,\" 책을 읽으면서도 귀를 기울일 수 있는 모양인 프레드가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야,\" 빈시 부인이 능숙하게 말머리를 돌리며 말했다. \"그 애한테 재산이라도 좀 남겨진다면야—남자란 아내의 친척들까지 맞아들이는 법이고, 가스 집안은 어찌나 가난한지, 그렇게 옹색하게 살잖니. 하지만 나는 네 공부에 방해되지 않게 자리를 비켜 주마, 얘야. 장도 좀 보러 가야 하니.\"\n\n\"프레드의 공부는 그리 깊지 않아요, 엄마,\" 로저먼드가 어머니를 따라 일어서며 말했다. \"그냥 소설을 읽고 있을 뿐인걸요.\"\n\n\"자, 자, 머지않아 라틴어 공부 같은 걸 할 거란다,\" 빈시 부인이 아들의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 달래듯 말했다. \"그러라고 흡연실에 일부러 불을 지펴 놓았잖니. 너도 알다시피 그게 네 아버지 바람이란다—프레드, 얘야—나는 늘 아버지한테 네가 착하게 굴고 다시 대학에 가서 학위를 딸 거라고 말씀드린단다.\"\n\n프레드는 어머니의 손을 끌어다 입술에 갖다 댔지만, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"오늘은 말 타러 안 나갈 거지?\" 어머니가 나간 뒤 로저먼드가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"안 나가. 왜?\"\n\n\"아빠가 이제 그 밤색 말을 내가 타도 된다고 하셨거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 내일 나랑 같이 가도 돼, 네가 원하면. 단, 나는 스톤 코트에 갈 거란 걸 기억해 둬.\"\n\n\"나는 말이 어찌나 타고 싶은지, 어디로 가든 상관없어.\" 로저먼드는 사실 다른 어떤 곳보다도 스톤 코트에 가고 싶었다.\n\n\"아, 참, 로지,\" 그녀가 방을 나서려 할 때 프레드가 말했다. \"피아노 치러 갈 거면, 나도 가서 너랑 같이 몇 곡 연주하게 해 줘.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침엔 제발 그런 부탁 하지 마.\"\n\n\"왜 오늘 아침은 안 되는데?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 프레드, 너 플루트 좀 그만 불었으면 좋겠어. 남자가 플루트를 부는 모습은 너무 우스꽝스러워. 게다가 너는 음정도 그렇게 틀리잖아.\"\n\n\"다음번에 누가 너한테 구애하거든, 로저먼드 양, 네가 얼마나 상냥한지 그 사람한테 일러바칠 테다.\"\n\n\"내가 네 플루트 연주를 들어 줘서 너를 기쁘게 해 주리라고 기대할 이유가 어디 있어? 내가 너더러 플루트를 불지 말아 달라고 해서 나를 기쁘게 해 주리라고 기대하지 않는 것과 마찬가지로 말이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 너를 말 타러 데리고 나가 주리라고 기대할 이유는 또 어디 있고?\"\n\n이 물음은 합의로 이어졌으니, 로저먼드가 그 특정한 승마에 마음을 단단히 두고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 프레드는 「밤이 새도록」과 「그대 강둑과 언덕이여」를 비롯해 자신의 『플루트 교본』에 실린 다른 애창곡들을 거의 한 시간 가까이 연습하는 흐뭇함을 누렸다. 그것은 쌕쌕거리는 연주였으나, 그는 거기에 잔뜩 욕심을 부리며 억누를 수 없는 희망을 쏟아부었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII.\n\n그는 거베이스가 아는 것보다\n더 많은 실을 물레에 감고 있었다.\n\n— 초서\n다음 날 아침 프레드와 로저먼드가 스톤 코트로 말을 달려간 길은 중부 지방의 아름다운 풍경 한 자락을 가로질렀으니, 거의 온통 풀밭과 목초지였고, 산울타리들은 여전히 무성하게 자라 그 아름다움을 뽐내며 새들에게 산호빛 열매를 펼쳐 보이도록 허락받고 있었다. 자잘한 세부들이 들판마다 저마다의 인상을 부여했으니, 어린 시절부터 그것들을 바라보아 온 눈에는 더없이 정겨운 것들이었다. 풀이 축축하고 나무들이 속삭이듯 기울어진 모퉁이의 웅덩이, 목초지 한가운데 휑한 곳에 그늘을 드리운 커다란 떡갈나무, 물푸레나무가 자라는 높은 둑, 우엉을 위해 붉은 배경을 만들어 주는 오래된 이회토 채굴장의 가파른 비탈, 다가갈 길조차 가늠할 수 없는 농가의 옹기종기 모인 지붕들과 낟가리들, 경계를 이루는 깊은 숲을 등진 회색 대문과 울타리, 그리고 한 채 외따로 떨어진 오두막, 그 아주아주 오래된 초가지붕은 이끼 낀 언덕과 골짜기로 가득해 훗날의 삶에서 멀리까지 찾아가 보게 되는, 더 크긴 하지만 더 아름답지는 않은 빛과 그림자의 경이로운 변주를 품고 있었다. 이런 것들이야말로 중부 지방에서 자란 영혼들에게 풍경 속 기쁨의 음계를 이루는 것들이다. 그들이 아장아장 그 사이를 걸었던 것들, 혹은 아버지가 한가로이 마차를 모는 동안 그 무릎 사이에 서서 마음속에 새겨 두었던 것들이다.\n\n그러나 길은, 심지어 샛길조차도 훌륭했다. 우리가 보았듯이 로윅은 진창 골목과 가난한 소작인의 교구가 아니었기 때문이다. 그리고 프레드와 로저먼드가 두 마일쯤 말을 달린 끝에 들어선 곳이 바로 로윅 교구였다. 한 마일을 더 가면 스톤 코트에 닿을 텐데, 그 첫 반 마일이 끝나는 곳에 이르자 집은 이미 눈에 들어왔다. 그 집은 석조 저택으로 자라나려던 차에, 왼쪽 측면에서 예기치 못하게 농가 건물들이 돋아나는 바람에 그 성장이 멈춰 버린 듯, 한 신사 농부의 견실한 거처 이상으로는 더 자라나지 못한 모습이었다. 그렇다 해도 오른쪽에 늘어선 멋진 호두나무들과 균형을 이루는, 첨탑 같은 곡식 낟가리들이 무리 지어 있어, 멀리서 바라보기에 결코 덜 정겨운 풍경은 아니었다.\n\n이윽고 현관문 앞 둥근 진입로에 마차 같은 무언가가 서 있는 것을 분간할 수 있게 되었다.\n\n\"어머나,\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"외삼촌의 그 끔찍한 친척들이 와 있지 않았으면 좋겠는데.\"\n\n\"하지만 와 있는걸. 저건 월 부인의 이륜마차야. 내 생각엔 마지막으로 남은 노란 이륜마차일걸. 월 부인이 저걸 타고 있는 걸 보면, 어째서 노란색이 상복으로 쓰일 수 있었는지 이해가 돼. 저 마차는 나한테는 영구차보다 더 장례식 같아 보여. 하긴 월 부인은 늘 검은 크레이프를 두르고 다니지. 어떻게 그게 가능한 거지, 로지? 그 사람 친구들이 늘 죽어 나갈 수는 없잖아.\"\n\n\"나도 도무지 모르겠어. 게다가 그 사람은 조금도 복음주의적이지도 않은데.\" 로저먼드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했는데, 마치 그런 종교적 관점이라면 끊임없는 크레이프 차림을 충분히 설명해 주기라도 할 것처럼 들렸다. \"그리고 가난하지도 않고.\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그럼, 천만에! 그 월씨 집안이나 페더스톤 집안 사람들은 유대인처럼 부자야. 내 말은, 그 사람들처럼 아무것도 쓰려 들지 않는 부류치고는 말이지. 그러면서도 그들은 외삼촌 주위를 독수리처럼 맴돌면서, 자기네 쪽 집안에서 단 한 푼이라도 새어 나갈까 봐 전전긍긍하지. 하지만 내 생각엔 외삼촌이 그들 모두를 미워하는 것 같아.\"\n\n그 먼 친척들의 눈에 그토록 탐탁지 않았던 월 부인은, 바로 그날 아침에 (결코 도전적인 태도가 아니라, 솜을 통과해 들려오는 듯한 낮고 둔탁하며 무덤덤한 어조로) 자기는 \"그들의 좋은 평판을 누리고\" 싶지 않다고 말했던 참이었다. 그녀는 자기가 직접 말했듯이, 친오빠의 화롯가에 앉아 있었고, 제인 월이 되기 스물다섯 해 전에 이미 제인 페더스톤이었으니, 그 이름에 아무 권리도 없는 자들이 친오빠의 이름을 함부로 입에 올렸을 때 한마디 할 자격이 있는 사람이었다.\n\n\"거기서 무슨 소리를 하려는 게야?\" 페더스톤 씨가 무릎 사이에 지팡이를 끼우고 가발을 바로잡으며 말했다. 그러면서 그녀에게 잠깐 날카로운 시선을 던졌는데, 그것이 마치 찬 바람 한 줄기가 닥친 것처럼 그에게 되돌아와 기침을 터뜨리게 했다.\n\n월 부인은 그가 다시 잠잠해질 때까지, 메리 가스가 그에게 새 시럽을 갖다줄 때까지, 그리고 그가 불을 씁쓸하게 바라보며 지팡이의 금 손잡이를 문지르기 시작할 때까지 대답을 미뤄야 했다. 불은 환하게 타올랐지만, 월 부인의 차갑고 푸르스름한 얼굴빛에는 아무 변화도 주지 못했으니, 그 얼굴은 목소리만큼이나 무덤덤했고, 눈이라고는 가느다란 틈에 지나지 않았으며, 입술은 말할 때조차 거의 움직이지 않았다.\n\n\"의사들도 그 기침은 못 다스리네요, 오빠. 꼭 내 기침이랑 똑같아요. 나도 오빠의 친동생이니까 체질이고 뭐고 다 같지요. 하지만 아까도 말했다시피, 빈시 부인네 집안이 좀 더 단속이 안 되는 게 안타까운 일이에요.\"\n\n\"쳇! 넌 그런 말 한 적 없어. 누가 내 이름을 함부로 입에 올렸다고 했지.\"\n\n\"누구나 하는 말이 사실이라면, 증명할 수 있는 것만 말한 거예요. 솔로몬 오빠 말로는, 빈시네 그 젊은 녀석이 얼마나 행실이 헤픈지, 집에 돌아온 뒤로 줄곧 당구로 도박을 한다고 미들마치에서 위아래로 다들 떠들썩하다네요.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리! 당구 한 판이 뭐가 어떻다고? 그건 점잖은 신사다운 놀이야. 빈시네 젊은이는 촌뜨기가 아니라고. 만약 네 아들 존이 당구에 손을 댔다면, 그야말로 바보 같은 꼴이 됐겠지.\"\n\n\"오빠 조카 존은 당구든 다른 어떤 놀이든 손댄 적이 없어요, 오빠. 게다가 수백 파운드를 잃는 일과는 거리가 멀지요. 누구나 하는 말이 사실이라면, 그 돈은 분명 아버지 빈시 씨 호주머니가 아닌 다른 데서 나와야 할 거예요. 사람들 말로는 그가 몇 년째 돈을 잃고 있다는데, 그렇게 사냥개를 데리고 다니고 늘 손님을 치는 걸 보면 누구도 그렇게 생각하진 않겠지만요. 그리고 들리는 말로는 벌스트로드 씨가 빈시 부인을 두고, 그 들떠 있는 태도며 자식들을 그렇게 응석받이로 키우는 걸 무엇보다도 호되게 나무란다더군요.\"\n\n\"벌스트로드가 나하고 무슨 상관이야? 난 그자 은행에 거래도 안 해.\"\n\n\"그런데 벌스트로드 부인이 바로 빈시 씨의 친누이고, 사람들 말로는 빈시 씨가 거의 은행 돈으로 장사를 한다지요. 그리고 오빠도 직접 보시면 알겠지만, 마흔이 넘은 여자가 늘 분홍 리본을 나부끼고 다니면서 무슨 일에든 그렇게 가볍게 웃어 대는 건 아주 꼴사나운 일이에요. 하지만 자식들을 응석받이로 키우는 것과 그 빚을 갚을 돈을 마련하는 건 별개의 문제지요. 그리고 빈시네 젊은 녀석이 자기 유산 기대를 담보로 돈을 빌렸다는 게 공공연한 소문이에요. 무슨 유산 기대인지는 말하지 않겠어요. 가스 양이 듣고 있으니, 얼마든지 다시 옮겨도 좋아요. 젊은 사람들끼리는 한통속이라는 걸 잘 아니까요.\"\n\n\"고맙지만 사양할게요, 월 부인.\" 메리 가스가 말했다. \"저는 험담을 듣는 것 자체를 너무 싫어해서, 그걸 옮기고 싶은 마음 같은 건 없거든요.\"\n\n페더스톤 씨는 지팡이 손잡이를 문지르며 발작하듯 짧게 웃어 보였는데, 그 웃음은 패가 형편없을 때 흘리는 노련한 휘스트 노름꾼의 낄낄거림과 꼭 같은 진정성을 지니고 있었다. 여전히 불을 바라보며 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 프레드 빈시한테 유산 기대가 없다고 감히 떠들 사람이 누구야? 그렇게 잘생기고 패기 있는 녀석이라면 그런 기대쯤 있을 만도 하지.\"\n\n월 부인이 대답하기까지 잠깐의 사이가 있었는데, 마침내 입을 열었을 때 그녀의 목소리는 눈물에 살짝 젖은 듯했으나, 얼굴은 여전히 메말라 있었다.\n\n\"그렇든 아니든, 오빠, 오빠 이름이 함부로 입에 오르내리는 걸 듣는 건 나와 솔로몬 오빠한테는 당연히 가슴 아픈 일이에요. 게다가 오빠 병이 언제 갑자기 데려갈지 모를 그런 병인데, 장터의 어릿광대만큼이나 페더스톤 집안과는 아무 상관도 없는 사람들이 오빠 재산이 자기네 차지가 될 거라고 대놓고 셈을 하고 있다니. 나는 오빠 친동생이고, 솔로몬은 오빠 친동생인데! 정말 그렇게 될 거라면, 전능하신 하느님께서 대체 무엇 하러 가족을 만드신 거예요?\" 여기서 월 부인의 눈물이 떨어졌으나, 절제된 정도였다.\n\n\"자, 어서 털어놓아, 제인!\" 페더스톤 씨가 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"네 말은, 프레드 빈시가 내 유언장에 대해 안다고 떠벌리면서 그걸 빌미로 누군가한테 돈을 끌어다 썼다는 거지, 응?\"\n\n\"난 그런 말 한 적 없어요, 오빠.\" (월 부인의 목소리는 다시 메마르고 흔들림 없는 것으로 돌아와 있었다.) \"어젯밤에 솔로몬 오빠가 장에서 돌아오는 길에 들러서 묵은 밀에 대해 조언을 해 주면서 말해 준 거예요. 내가 과부고, 아들 존은 이제 겨우 스물셋이지만, 누구보다도 착실하니까요. 그리고 오빠는 그걸 더없이 확실한 출처에서 들었대요. 그것도 한 사람이 아니라 여러 사람한테서요.\"\n\n\"허튼소리! 난 한마디도 안 믿어. 죄다 꾸며 낸 이야기야. 창가로 가 보거라, 아가씨. 말 소리가 들린 것 같구나. 의사가 오는지 보거라.\"\n\n\"내가 꾸며 낸 게 아니에요, 오빠. 솔로몬 오빠도 아니고요. 솔로몬 오빠가 다른 면에서야 어떻든—별난 데가 있다는 건 나도 부인하지 않아요—이미 유언장을 작성해서, 친하게 지내는 친척들끼리 재산을 똑같이 나눠 두었어요. 하기야 내 생각으로는 어떤 사람은 다른 이들보다 더 대접받아야 할 때도 있다고 보지만요. 어쨌든 솔로몬 오빠는 자기가 뭘 할 작정인지 조금도 숨기지 않아요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 더 바보 천치지!\" 페더스톤 씨가 간신히 말을 내뱉으며 심한 기침 발작을 일으켰는데, 그 바람에 메리 가스가 그의 곁에 가까이 서 있어야 했고, 그래서 이윽고 문 앞 자갈밭에서 발굽 소리를 내며 멈춰 선 말이 누구의 것인지 알아보지 못했다.\n\n페더스톤 씨의 기침이 잦아들기도 전에, 로저먼드가 승마복 자락을 우아하게 추켜올리며 들어섰다. 그녀는 월 부인에게 격식을 갖춰 인사했고, 월 부인은 뻣뻣하게 \"안녕하세요, 아가씨?\"라고 답했다. 그녀는 메리에게는 말없이 미소 지으며 고갯짓을 했고, 기침이 멎어 외삼촌이 자기를 알아볼 수 있을 때까지 선 채로 기다렸다.\n\n\"이런, 아가씨!\" 마침내 그가 말했다. \"혈색이 참 좋구나. 프레드는 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n\"말을 살피고 있어요. 곧 들어올 거예요.\"\n\n\"앉거라, 앉아. 월 부인, 그만 가 보는 게 좋겠어.\"\n\n피터 페더스톤을 늙은 여우라고 불렀던 이웃들조차도, 그가 마음에 없는 예의를 차린다고 비난한 적은 결코 없었다. 그의 누이도 그가 혈연관계임을 드러낼 때 보이는 그 특유의 무례함에 이미 익숙해져 있었다. 실제로 그녀 자신도, 가족 사이에서는 듣기 좋게 굴어야 할 의무로부터 완전히 자유로운 것이 전능하신 하느님께서 가족에 대해 품으신 뜻에 포함되어 있다고 여기는 데 익숙했다. 그녀는 아무런 분개의 기색도 없이 천천히 일어나, 여느 때의 둔탁하고 단조로운 목소리로 말했다. \"오빠, 새로 온 의사가 오빠한테 뭔가 도움이 될 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 솔로몬 오빠 말로는 그 사람이 똑똑하다고 소문이 자자하다더군요. 나야 오빠가 오래 사시길 진심으로 바라지요. 그리고 한마디만 하시면, 오빠를 친동생과 친조카들만큼 정성껏 간병할 사람은 없어요. 알다시피 리베카도 있고, 조애나도 있고, 엘리자베스도 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 기억하지—내가 그 애들을 다 기억하고 있다는 걸 알게 될 게다—다들 거무튀튀하고 못생겼지. 그 애들은 돈이라도 좀 있어야겠어, 안 그래? 우리 집안 여자들한테 미모란 게 있었던 적이 없어. 하지만 페더스톤 집안은 늘 돈은 좀 있었고, 월 집안도 그랬지. 월도 돈은 있었어. 월은 잘사는 사람이었지. 그래, 그래. 돈이란 좋은 알이거든. 그리고 남길 돈이 있거든, 따뜻한 둥지에 넣어 두는 게 좋아. 잘 가게, 월 부인.\" 여기서 페더스톤 씨는 마치 제 귀를 먹먹하게 만들고 싶기라도 한 듯 가발을 양쪽으로 잡아당겼고, 그의 누이는 그 신탁 같은 말을 곱씹으며 떠나갔다. 빈시 집안과 메리 가스에 대한 질투에도 불구하고, 그녀의 얕은 정신의 밑바닥에는 침전물처럼 한 가지 확신이 남아 있었으니, 곧 오빠 피터 페더스톤이 자기 주요 재산을 혈육에게서 멀리 떼어 놓을 리가 결코 없으리라는 것이었다. 그렇지 않다면, 그가 아무도 예상치 못했을 때 망간이며 이런저런 것들로 그토록 큰돈을 거머쥔 뒤에, 전능하신 하느님께서는 왜 그의 두 아내를 모두 자식 없이 데려가셨겠는가? 또한 오빠 피터가 죽고 난 다음 일요일에, 그 재산이 집안 밖으로 새어 나가 버렸다는 사실을 모두가 알게 될 거라면, 로윅 교구 교회가 왜 있으며, 월 집안과 파우더렐 집안 사람들이 대대로 같은 신도석에 앉고, 그 옆에 페더스톤 집안 신도석이 왜 있겠는가? 인간의 정신은 어느 시대에도 도덕적 혼돈을 받아들인 적이 없으니, 그토록 터무니없는 결과란 도무지 상상조차 할 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 우리는 엄밀히 따지면 상상할 수도 없는 많은 것에 겁을 먹곤 한다.\n\n프레드가 들어서자 노인은 묘한 반짝임을 띤 눈으로 그를 훑어보았는데, 젊은이는 그것이 자기 외모의 흡족한 세부에 대한 자랑임을 종종 해석해 낼 수 있었다.\n\n\"너희 두 아가씨는 가 있거라.\" 페더스톤 씨가 말했다. \"프레드하고 할 얘기가 있어.\"\n\n\"내 방으로 가자, 로저먼드. 잠깐 추운 건 괜찮겠지.\" 메리가 말했다. 두 처녀는 어린 시절부터 알고 지냈을 뿐 아니라, 같은 지방 학교를 함께 다녔으니 (메리는 견습 학생이었다) 공유하는 추억이 많았고, 단둘이 이야기 나누기를 무척 좋아했다. 실제로 이 단둘만의 대화야말로 로저먼드가 스톤 코트에 온 목적 가운데 하나였다.\n\n늙은 페더스톤은 문이 닫히기 전까지는 이야기를 꺼내려 하지 않았다. 그는 여전히 그 반짝임을 띤 눈으로, 입을 번갈아 오므렸다 벌렸다 하는 버릇대로 얼굴을 찡그리며 프레드를 바라보았다. 그리고 입을 열었을 때, 그것은 낮은 어조였으니, 모욕당한 손윗사람의 어조라기보다는 차라리 돈을 받고 입을 다물 준비가 된 밀고자의 어조로 들릴 만했다. 그는 자기에게 가해진 잘못에 대해서조차 강한 도덕적 분노 따위는 느끼지 못하는 사람이었다. 남들이 자기에게서 이득을 취하려 드는 것이야 당연한 일이고, 다만 그 자신이 그들보다 약간 더 교활할 따름이었다.\n\n\"그래, 이 녀석아, 내가 죽고 나면 내 땅을 저당 잡혀서 갚겠다고 약속하고 돈을 빌리면서 10퍼센트 이자를 물고 있다지, 응? 내 목숨을 일 년짜리로 쳐 놓고 말이야. 하지만 난 아직 유언장을 바꿀 수 있어.\"\n\n프레드는 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그는 그런 식으로 돈을 빌린 적이 없었으니, 그럴 만한 더없이 타당한 이유가 있었다. 그러나 그는 페더스톤의 땅을 손에 넣어 그것을 현재의 빚을 갚을 미래의 수단으로 삼을 전망에 대해 어느 정도 자신만만하게 (어쩌면 자기가 정확히 기억하는 것보다 더) 떠벌렸던 것을 의식하고 있었다.\n\n\"무엇을 말씀하시는 건지 모르겠습니다, 외삼촌. 저는 분명 그런 불확실한 담보로 돈을 빌린 적이 없습니다. 부디 설명해 주세요.\"\n\n\"아니지, 이놈아, 설명해야 할 사람은 너야. 다시 말하지만, 난 아직 유언장을 바꿀 수 있어. 난 정신이 멀쩡하다고—머릿속으로 복리 계산도 할 수 있고, 어떤 바보 녀석 이름이든 이십 년 전만큼이나 또렷이 기억해. 대체 뭔 소리야? 난 아직 여든도 안 됐어. 분명히 말하는데, 이 이야기를 부인해 봐.\"\n\n\"이미 부인했습니다, 외삼촌.\" 프레드가 약간 짜증 섞인 기색으로 대답했는데, 그는 외삼촌이 부인하는 것과 반증하는 것을 말로는 구별하지 않는다는 사실을 미처 떠올리지 못했다. 사실 누구도 그 두 개념을 늙은 페더스톤만큼 혼동하지 않는 사람은 없었으니, 그는 그토록 많은 바보들이 자기 주장을 증거로 받아들이는 것을 종종 의아하게 여기곤 했다. \"하지만 다시 한번 부인하겠습니다. 그 이야기는 어리석은 거짓말이에요.\"\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리! 증서를 가져와야지. 그건 확실한 출처에서 나온 거란 말이야.\"\n\n\"그 출처가 누군지 대시고, 그 사람한테 제가 돈을 빌렸다는 그 상대가 누군지 대게 하세요. 그러면 제가 그 이야기를 반증할 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"꽤나 믿을 만한 출처지, 내 생각엔—미들마치에서 벌어지는 일은 거의 다 아는 사람이거든. 바로 그 잘나고 신앙심 깊고 자선 잘하는 네 외삼촌이야. 자, 어떠냐!\" 여기서 페더스톤 씨는 흥겨움을 뜻하는 그 특유의 속으로 떠는 몸짓을 했다.\n\n\"벌스트로드 외삼촌요?\"\n\n\"그럼 누구겠어, 응?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 이야기는 외삼촌이 저에 대해 설교조로 흘렸을 법한 몇 마디 말에서 부풀려져 이런 거짓말이 된 거예요. 그분이 저한테 돈을 빌려준 사람의 이름을 댔다고 그러기라도 하던가요?\"\n\n\"그런 사람이 있다면, 벌스트로드가 분명히 알 거다. 하지만 네가 돈을 빌리려고만 했고 실제로는 못 빌렸다 쳐도—벌스트로드는 그것도 알 게야. 벌스트로드한테서, 네가 내 땅으로 빚을 갚겠다고 약속한 적이 있다는 걸 자기는 믿지 않는다는 글을 한 장 받아 와. 자, 어떠냐!\"\n\n페더스톤 씨의 얼굴은, 자기 머리가 멀쩡하다는 데서 오는 말 없는 승리감을 근육으로 풀어내느라 온갖 찡그림을 총동원해야 했다.\n\n프레드는 자기가 역겨운 진퇴양난에 빠졌음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"농담하시는 거지요, 외삼촌. 벌스트로드 씨도 다른 사람들처럼 사실이 아닌 것을 수두룩하게 믿고 있고, 게다가 저에 대해서는 편견을 갖고 있어요. 외삼촌이 말씀하신 그 소문을 입증할 만한 사실을 자기는 전혀 모른다고 그분이 써 주게 하는 거야 쉽게 할 수 있겠지요. 비록 불쾌한 일로 번질 수는 있겠지만요. 하지만 저에 대해 그분이 무엇을 믿고 안 믿는지를 적어 달라고 부탁하기는 도무지 어렵습니다.\" 프레드는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가, 외삼촌의 허영심에 영리하게 호소하며 덧붙였다. \"그런 건 신사가 부탁할 만한 일이 아니지요.\" 그러나 그는 그 결과에 실망하고 말았다.\n\n\"그래, 네 속셈은 알겠다. 너는 벌스트로드보다 나를 거스르는 게 더 낫다는 거지. 그자가 뭐냐?—내가 들은 바로는 이 근방에 땅 한 뙈기도 없는 자야. 투기꾼 같은 녀석이지! 악마가 뒤를 봐주던 걸 그만두는 날엔 언제든 거꾸러질 수 있어. 그자의 신앙이란 게 바로 그런 거야. 전능하신 하느님께서 끼어들어 주시기를 바라는 거지. 그건 다 헛소리야! 내가 교회 다닐 적에 한 가지는 꽤 분명하게 깨달은 게 있는데, 그건 이거다. 전능하신 하느님은 땅에 매달리신다는 거야. 그분은 땅을 약속하시고, 땅을 주시며, 곡식과 가축으로 사람들을 부자로 만드시지. 그런데 너는 다른 편을 택하는 거다. 너는 페더스톤과 땅보다 벌스트로드와 투기를 더 좋아하는 거지.\"\n\n\"죄송하지만요, 외삼촌.\" 프레드가 일어서서 불을 등지고 서서는 채찍으로 장화를 탁탁 치며 말했다. \"저는 벌스트로드도 투기도 좋아하지 않습니다.\" 그는 자기가 옴짝달싹 못하게 됐다는 느낌에 다소 뚱한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"흠, 흠, 너는 나 없이도 잘해 나갈 수 있겠지, 그건 아주 분명하구나.\" 늙은 페더스톤이 말했는데, 프레드가 조금이라도 독립적인 태도를 보일 가능성이 내심 못마땅했던 것이다. \"너는 굶주리는 목사 대신 시골 지주가 되게 해 줄 땅 한 뙈기도 원하지 않고, 그 김에 백 파운드쯤 거들어 주는 것도 원하지 않는다는 거지. 나야 아무래도 상관없다. 내키면 유언 보충서를 다섯 장이라도 만들 수 있고, 내 은행권은 비상금으로 챙겨 둘 테니까. 나야 아무래도 상관없어.\"\n\n프레드는 다시 얼굴을 붉혔다. 페더스톤은 그에게 돈을 선물로 준 적이 거의 없었는데, 바로 이 순간 그 당장의 은행권 전망을 포기하는 것이 더 먼 땅에 대한 전망을 포기하는 것보다 거의 더 힘들게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"저는 은혜를 모르는 사람이 아닙니다, 외삼촌. 외삼촌이 저에게 품으셨을지 모를 그 어떤 호의에도 무관심한 태도를 보일 생각은 결코 없었어요. 오히려 그 반대지요.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그럼 그걸 증명해 봐. 벌스트로드한테서, 네가 내 땅으로 빚을 갚겠다고 떠벌리며 약속했다는 걸 자기는 믿지 않는다는 편지를 받아 와. 그러고 나서 네가 무슨 곤경에 빠진 게 있거든, 내가 너를 좀 도와줄 수 있을지 어디 보자꾸나. 자, 어떠냐! 이건 약속이다. 자, 내 팔을 잡거라. 방 안을 한 바퀴 걸어 보마.\"\n\n프레드는 짜증이 났음에도, 사랑받지도 존경받지도 못하는 그 노인이 부종이 든 다리로 걸을 때 여느 때보다 더 가련해 보이는 것을 안쓰러워할 만큼의 다정함은 마음속에 지니고 있었다. 그는 팔을 내주면서, 자기는 체질이 무너져 가는 늙은이가 되고 싶지는 않다고 생각했다. 그리고 그는 좋은 기분으로 기다려 주었으니, 먼저 창가에서 호로새와 풍향계에 관한 늘 듣던 말을 들어 주었고, 그다음에는 빈약한 책장 앞에 섰는데, 그 검은 송아지 가죽 표지의 주된 자랑거리는 요세푸스, 컬페퍼, 클롭슈토크의 『메시아』, 그리고 『신사의 잡지』 몇 권이었다.\n\n\"책 제목들을 읽어 보거라. 자, 어서! 너야 대학 다닌 사람이니까.\"\n\n프레드는 그에게 제목들을 읽어 주었다.\n\n\"저 아가씨가 책을 더 가져다 뭣에 쓴다는 게냐? 무엇 하러 그 애한테 책을 더 가져다주는 거야?\"\n\n\"책이 그 애한테 즐거움을 주니까요, 외삼촌. 메리는 독서를 무척 좋아해요.\"\n\n\"좀 지나치게 좋아하지.\" 페더스톤 씨가 트집을 잡듯 말했다. \"그 애는 나랑 같이 앉아 있을 때도 책을 읽으려 들더구나. 하지만 내가 그걸 못 하게 했지. 그 애한테는 큰 소리로 읽어 줄 신문이 있어. 그것만 해도 하루치로는 충분하다고 본다. 난 그 애가 혼자서 책 읽는 꼴은 도무지 못 봐주겠어. 명심하고 그 애한테 다시는 책을 가져다주지 마라, 알겠느냐?\"\n\n\"네, 외삼촌, 알겠습니다.\" 프레드는 전에도 이 명령을 받은 적이 있었고, 몰래 그것을 어겼었다. 그는 다시 어길 작정이었다.\n\n\"종을 울려라.\" 페더스톤 씨가 말했다. \"저 아가씨더러 내려오라고 해야겠다.\"\n\n로저먼드와 메리는 그들의 남자 친구들보다 더 빠르게 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그들은 앉을 생각도 없이, 창가 가까이 화장대 앞에 서 있었고, 그동안 로저먼드는 모자를 벗고 베일을 매만지며 손끝으로 머리를 살짝살짝 다듬었으니—아마빛도 노란빛도 아닌, 어린아이처럼 환한 머리카락이었다. 메리 가스는 두 요정 사이에 비스듬히 서 있어 더욱더 평범해 보였다. 한 요정은 거울 속에, 또 한 요정은 거울 밖에 있었고, 그들은 천상의 푸른빛 눈으로 서로를 바라보았으니, 그 눈은 재간 있는 보는 이가 거기에 담을 수 있는 가장 절묘한 의미들을 다 품을 만큼 깊었고, 또한 그 주인의 의미가 혹시라도 덜 절묘한 것일 경우 그것을 감추어 줄 만큼도 깊었다. 미들마치에서 로저먼드 곁에서 금발로 보일 아이는 몇 안 되었고, 그녀의 승마복이 드러내는 호리호리한 자태는 섬세한 굴곡을 지니고 있었다. 사실 미들마치의 남자들은, 그녀의 오빠들을 빼면 대부분 빈시 양이야말로 세상에서 가장 멋진 처녀라고 여겼고, 더러는 그녀를 천사라고 부르기도 했다. 반면에 메리 가스는 평범한 죄인의 모습을 하고 있었으니, 살결이 거무스름했고, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리카락은 거칠고 뻣뻣했으며, 키는 작았다. 그렇다고 흡족한 대조를 이루기 위해 그녀가 온갖 미덕을 다 갖추었다고 단언하는 것도 사실은 아닐 것이다. 평범함에도 아름다움 못지않게 그 나름의 독특한 유혹과 악덕이 따르는 법이니, 평범함은 상냥함을 꾸며 내기 쉽고, 그러지 않을 경우에는 불만의 온갖 혐오스러움을 다 드러내기 쉽다. 어쨌든 곁에 있는 그 사랑스러운 존재와 대비되어 못생긴 것이라 불리는 일은, 그 표현 속의 정밀한 진실성과 적절함을 느끼는 것 이상의 어떤 영향을 낳기 쉬운 법이다. 스물두 살의 나이에 메리는 분명, 덜 운 좋은 처녀에게 흔히 권해지는 그 완벽한 분별력과 훌륭한 원칙을 갖추지는 못했으니, 마치 그것들이 미리 적당히 배합되어, 필요에 따라 체념의 풍미까지 곁들여진 채로 얻어질 수 있기라도 한 듯이 말이다. 그녀의 영민함에는 끊임없이 되살아나는 풍자적 신랄함의 한 줄기가 깔려 있었고, 그것은 결코 완전히 자취를 감추는 법이 없었으니, 다만 그녀에게 만족해야 마땅하다고 말하는 대신 그녀를 만족시키기 위해 무언가를 해 주는 이들을 향한 강한 감사의 물결만이 그것을 가려 줄 뿐이었다. 성숙해 가는 여성다움이 그녀의 평범함을 누그러뜨려 주었으니, 그것은 우리 인류의 어머니들이 어느 위도에서나 다소 어울리는 머릿수건 아래 흔히 지녀 온, 인간미 넘치는 종류의 평범함이었다. 렘브란트라면 기꺼이 그녀를 그렸을 것이고, 그 넓적한 이목구비가 지적인 정직함을 띠고 화폭 밖을 내다보도록 만들었을 것이다. 정직함, 곧 진실을 말하는 올곧음이야말로 메리를 지배하는 미덕이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 환상을 만들어 내려 하지도 않았고, 자기 잇속을 위해 환상에 빠지지도 않았으며, 기분이 좋을 때면 자기 자신을 비웃을 만큼의 유머도 충분히 지니고 있었다. 그녀와 로저먼드가 거울에 함께 비친 순간, 그녀가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 네 옆에 있으니 얼마나 거무튀튀한 얼룩 같은지, 로지! 너야말로 가장 어울리지 않는 길동무야.\"\n\n\"어머, 아니야! 아무도 네 외모 같은 건 생각하지 않아. 너는 그렇게 분별 있고 쓸모 있는 사람이잖아, 메리. 미모란 사실 거의 아무 중요성도 없는걸.\" 로저먼드가 메리 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 말했는데, 그 눈길은 거울 속에 새로 비친 자기 목덜미 쪽으로 슬며시 빗겨 가고 있었다.\n\n\"내 미모 말이구나.\" 메리가 다소 빈정대듯 말했다.\n\n로저먼드는 생각했다. '가엾은 메리, 더없이 다정한 말도 나쁘게 받아들이다니.' 소리 내어서는 이렇게 말했다. \"요즘은 뭘 하면서 지내?\"\n\n\"나? 아, 집안일을 돌보고—시럽을 따라 주고—상냥하고 만족스러운 척하고—모든 사람을 나쁘게 보는 법을 배우고 있지.\"\n\n\"너한테는 비참한 삶이구나.\"\n\n\"아니야.\" 메리가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 퉁명스레 말했다. \"내 삶은 너희 집 모건 양의 삶보다는 즐겁다고 생각해.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 해. 하지만 모건 양은 너무 재미없고, 게다가 젊지도 않잖아.\"\n\n\"본인한테는 자기가 재미있겠지. 그리고 나이가 든다고 모든 게 더 쉬워지는 건지는 나도 전혀 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 로저먼드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"아무 전망도 없이 그런 사람들이 뭘 하며 사는지 궁금하긴 해. 하긴, 종교라는 버팀목이 있긴 하지. 하지만,\" 그녀가 보조개를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"너는 사정이 아주 다르잖아, 메리. 너는 청혼을 받을 수도 있으니까.\"\n\n\"누가 나한테 청혼하겠다고 너한테 말이라도 했어?\"\n\n\"그럴 리가. 내 말은, 너를 거의 날마다 보면서 너에게 반할지도 모르는 신사 한 분이 있다는 거지.\"\n\n메리의 얼굴에 어떤 변화가 일었는데, 그것은 무엇보다도 어떤 변화도 내보이지 않겠다는 결심에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n\"그렇게 본다고 사람들이 늘 사랑에 빠지나?\" 그녀가 아무렇지 않게 대답했다. \"내가 보기엔 오히려 그만큼 자주 서로를 싫어하게 되는 이유가 되던데.\"\n\n\"재미있고 호감 가는 사람이라면 그렇지 않지. 듣자 하니 리드게이트 씨가 그 두 가지를 다 갖췄다더라.\"\n\n\"아, 리드게이트 씨.\" 메리가 분명한 무관심으로 어조를 떨어뜨리며 말했다. \"그 사람에 대해 뭔가 알고 싶은 거구나.\" 그녀는 로저먼드의 에두르는 방식에 장단을 맞춰 줄 마음이 없어서 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그냥, 네가 그 사람을 어떻게 생각하는지.\"\n\n\"지금으로선 좋고 말고 할 게 없어. 내가 누군가를 좋아하려면 늘 약간의 친절이 그 마음에 불을 붙여 줘야 하거든. 나를 보는 것 같지도 않게 말을 거는 사람을 좋아할 만큼 나는 너그럽지 못해.\"\n\n\"그렇게 거만한가?\" 로저먼드가 한층 흡족해하며 말했다. \"그 사람이 좋은 집안 출신이라는 거 알아?\"\n\n\"아니. 그 사람이 그걸 이유로 댄 건 아니야.\"\n\n\"메리! 너는 정말 별난 애야. 그런데 그 사람 생김새는 어때? 나한테 묘사해 줘 봐.\"\n\n\"사람을 어떻게 묘사해? 목록이라면 줄 수 있지. 짙은 눈썹, 검은 눈, 곧은 코, 숱 많은 검은 머리, 크고 단단한 흰 손, 그리고—어디 보자—아, 더없이 고운 무명 손수건. 하지만 네가 직접 보게 될 거야. 알다시피 지금쯤이 그 사람이 방문하는 시간이거든.\"\n\n로저먼드는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"나는 거만한 태도가 오히려 좋아. 시끄럽게 떠벌리는 젊은 남자는 견딜 수가 없거든.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 씨가 거만하다고 한 건 아니야. 하지만 마드무아젤 선생님이 늘 말하셨듯이, 취향이란 사람마다 다 다른 법이지. 그리고 어떤 처녀든 자기가 좋아할 특정한 종류의 자만을 고를 수 있다면, 그건 바로 너일 거라고 생각해, 로지.\"\n\n\"거만함은 자만이 아니야. 자만이라면 나는 프레드를 두고 말하겠어.\"\n\n\"아무도 그 애를 그보다 더 나쁘게 말하지 않으면 좋으련만. 그 앤 좀 더 조심해야 해. 월 부인이 외삼촌한테, 프레드가 아주 행실이 헤프다고 말하던걸.\" 메리는 분별을 앞지른 처녀다운 충동에서 이렇게 말했다. \"헤프다\"는 말에는 어떤 막연한 불안이 얽혀 있었고, 그녀는 로저먼드가 무슨 말이든 해서 그것을 떨쳐 주기를 바랐다. 그러나 그녀는 월 부인의 더 특정한 암시는 일부러 입에 올리지 않았다.\n\n\"어머, 프레드는 정말 못 말려!\" 로저먼드가 말했다. 그녀는 메리가 아닌 다른 누구에게는 그토록 어울리지 않는 말을 결코 허용하지 않았을 것이다.\n\n\"못 말린다는 게 무슨 뜻이야?\"\n\n\"그렇게 게으르고, 아빠를 그렇게 화나게 하고, 게다가 성직도 받지 않겠다고 하니까.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 프레드가 아주 옳아.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그 애가 아주 옳다고 할 수 있어, 메리? 난 네가 좀 더 신앙심이 있는 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n\"그 앤 성직자가 되기에 적합하지 않아.\"\n\n\"하지만 적합해야 하잖아.\"—\"그럼, 그렇다면 그 애는 마땅히 되어야 할 사람이 못 되는 거지. 나는 그것과 똑같은 처지에 있는 다른 사람들도 좀 알고 있어.\"\n\n\"하지만 아무도 그 사람들을 좋게 보지 않잖아. 나라면 성직자랑 결혼하긴 싫지만, 그래도 성직자는 있어야 하는걸.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 해서 프레드가 꼭 그중 하나여야 하는 건 아니지.\"\n\n\"하지만 아빠가 그걸 위해 돈을 들여 교육까지 시켰는데! 게다가 한번 생각해 봐, 만약 그 애한테 물려받을 재산이 한 푼도 없다면?\"\n\n\"그거야 아주 쉽게 상상이 되네.\" 메리가 무덤덤하게 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 어떻게 네가 프레드를 두둔할 수 있는지 모르겠어.\" 로저먼드가 이 점을 밀어붙이고 싶어 하며 말했다.\n\n\"두둔하는 게 아니야.\" 메리가 웃으며 말했다. \"어느 교구든 그 애를 성직자로 두지 않도록 지켜 주고 싶을 뿐이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 물론 그 애가 성직자가 된다면야 달라지겠지.\"\n\n\"그래, 대단한 위선자가 되겠지. 그리고 그 앤 아직 그렇지는 않으니까.\"\n\n\"너한테는 무슨 말을 해도 소용없어, 메리. 너는 늘 프레드 편만 들잖아.\"\n\n\"내가 왜 그 애 편을 들면 안 되지?\" 메리가 발끈하며 말했다. \"그 앤 내 편을 들어 줄 테니까. 그 애야말로 나를 위해 조금이라도 수고를 해 주는 유일한 사람이거든.\"\n\n\"너 때문에 정말 마음이 불편해, 메리.\" 로저먼드가 더없이 진지한 온화함으로 말했다. \"엄마한테는 절대로 말 안 할게.\"\n\n\"뭘 말 안 한다는 거야?\" 메리가 성을 내며 말했다.\n\n\"제발 화내지 마, 메리.\" 로저먼드가 여전히 온화하게 말했다.\n\n\"네 엄마가 프레드가 나한테 청혼할까 봐 걱정하는 거라면, 설령 그 애가 청혼한다 해도 나는 그 애랑 결혼하지 않을 거라고 전해. 하지만 내가 아는 한 그 앤 그러지도 않을 거야. 분명 그 앤 나한테 청혼한 적도 없으니까.\"\n\n\"메리, 너는 늘 그렇게 격하다니까.\"\n\n\"그리고 너는 늘 그렇게 사람 약을 올리고.\"\n\n\"내가? 내가 뭘 잘못했다고?\"\n\n\"아, 흠잡을 데 없는 사람들이 늘 가장 약을 올리는 법이지. 종이 울리네—이제 내려가야 할 것 같아.\"\n\n\"싸우려던 건 아니었어.\" 로저먼드가 모자를 쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"싸워? 말도 안 돼. 우리는 싸운 적 없어. 가끔 발끈하지도 못한다면, 친구인 게 무슨 소용이야?\"\n\n\"네가 한 말을 내가 옮겨도 돼?\"\n\n\"좋을 대로 해. 나는 옮겨질까 봐 두려운 말 같은 건 절대 하지 않으니까. 그럼 이제 내려가자.\"\n\n리드게이트 씨는 이날 아침 다소 늦었지만, 방문객들은 그를 볼 만큼 오래 머물렀다. 페더스톤 씨가 로저먼드에게 노래를 불러 달라고 청했고, 그녀 자신도 친절하게도 그가 좋아하는 두 번째 노래—\"흘러라, 빛나는 강물이여\"—를 부르겠다고 제안했기 때문이다. 그것도 (자기는 질색하는) \"즐거운 나의 집\"을 부른 뒤에 말이다. 이 완고한 늙은 오버리치는 그 감상적인 노래를 흡족하게 여겼으니, 처녀들에게 어울리는 장식이라 여겼고, 또한 근본적으로 훌륭하다고 보았기 때문이다. 감상이란 노래에 딱 어울리는 것이니 말이다.\n\n리드게이트 씨의 말이 창가를 지나갈 때, 페더스톤 씨는 여전히 마지막 곡에 박수를 보내며 아가씨의 목소리가 검은지빠귀만큼이나 맑다고 장담하고 있던 참이었다.\n\n의사가 충분히 똑똑하기만 하다면 약이 자기를 \"벌떡 일으켜 세워\" 주리라는 것을 좀처럼 믿지 못하는 늙은 환자를 상대로, 늘 겪는 그 불쾌한 일과가 또 반복되리라는 따분한 예상은—미들마치의 매력 전반에 대한 그의 불신과 더해져—이 로저먼드라는 환상에 한층 효과적인 배경이 되어 주었다. 늙은 페더스톤은, 메리 가스에 대해서는 그런 식으로 소개할 가치조차 느낀 적이 없었으면서도, 부랴부랴 거창하게 로저먼드를 자기 조카딸이라고 소개했다. 로저먼드의 우아한 처신은 무엇 하나 리드게이트의 눈을 벗어나지 못했으니, 노인의 몰취미가 그녀에게 떠안긴 그 주목을 그녀가 얼마나 섬세하게 받아넘기는지—엉뚱한 때에는 보조개를 짓지 않다가, 메리에게 말을 걸 때에야 비로소 그것을 내보이며, 그토록 따뜻한 관심을 담아 메리에게 다정히 말을 건네는 모습을, 리드게이트는 전보다 더 자세히 메리를 재빨리 살펴본 뒤에, 로저먼드의 눈에서 사랑스러운 친절함을 보아 내었다. 그러나 메리는 무슨 까닭에선지 다소 언짢아 보였다.\n\n\"로지 양이 나한테 노래를 한 곡 불러 주었다네—그것까지 뭐라 할 말은 없겠지, 응, 의사 양반?\" 페더스톤 씨가 말했다. \"난 자네 약보다 그게 더 좋아.\"\n\n\"그 노래 때문에 시간 가는 걸 깜빡했네요.\" 로저먼드가 일어나 모자를 집으러 가며 말했다. 노래하기 전에 벗어 두었던 그 모자를 집으러 가는 동안, 흰 줄기 위에 핀 꽃 같은 그녀의 머리가 승마복 위로 완벽하게 드러나 보였다. \"프레드, 우리 정말 가야 해.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 프레드가 말했는데, 그는 기분이 그리 좋지 못할 나름의 이유가 있었고, 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶었다.\n\n\"빈시 양은 음악을 하시나요?\" 리드게이트가 눈으로 그녀를 좇으며 말했다. (로저먼드의 온 신경과 근육은 자기가 보여지고 있다는 의식에 맞춰 조율되어 있었다. 그녀는 천성적으로, 자기 육체에 배어든 배역을 연기하는 배우였다. 심지어 자기 자신의 성격마저도 연기했으니, 그것도 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 그것이 정확히 자기 본래의 성격임을 스스로도 알지 못했다.)\n\n\"미들마치에서 최고지, 내 장담하네.\" 페더스톤 씨가 말했다. \"그다음이 누구든 간에 말이야. 안 그러냐, 프레드? 네 누이를 두둔해 봐.\"\n\n\"제 말은 인정받기 어려울 것 같은데요, 외삼촌. 제 증언은 아무 쓸모가 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"미들마치는 그리 수준이 높지 않은걸요, 외삼촌.\" 로저먼드가 어여쁘게 가벼운 어조로 말하며, 멀찍이 놓여 있던 자기 채찍 쪽으로 다가갔다.\n\n리드게이트가 재빨리 그녀의 동작을 앞질렀다. 그가 그녀보다 먼저 채찍에 닿아, 그것을 그녀에게 건네려고 몸을 돌렸다. 그녀는 고개를 숙여 인사하며 그를 바라보았다. 그도 물론 그녀를 바라보고 있었고, 그렇게 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤으니, 그것은 애써서는 결코 다다를 수 없는, 마치 안개가 별안간 신성하게 걷히는 듯한 그 묘한 마주침이었다. 내 생각에 리드게이트는 여느 때보다 조금 창백해진 것 같았으나, 로저먼드는 깊이 얼굴을 붉히며 어떤 놀라움을 느꼈다. 그 일이 있고 나서 그녀는 정말로 어서 가고 싶어졌고, 외삼촌과 작별 인사를 하러 갔을 때는 그가 무슨 어리석은 소리를 하고 있는지조차 알지 못했다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 사랑에 빠지는 것이라 부르는, 서로의 인상이 통했다고 여긴 이 결과야말로, 로저먼드가 미리 마음속에 그려 두었던 바로 그것이었다. 미들마치에 그 중요한 새 인물이 도착한 이래로, 그녀는 자그마한 미래를 짜 내려 왔고, 이런 장면 같은 것이야말로 그 미래의 필수적인 시작이었다. 난파되어 뗏목에 매달려 있든, 정중한 수행원과 여행 가방들을 거느리고 오든, 낯선 이는 처녀의 마음에 늘 어떤 정황적인 매혹을 불러일으켜 왔으니, 그 앞에서는 토박이의 가치 따위가 아무리 애써도 헛될 뿐이었다. 그리고 낯선 이야말로 로저먼드의 사교적 로맨스에 절대적으로 필요한 존재였으니, 그 로맨스는 늘 미들마치 토박이가 아니면서 자기 집안 같은 연줄이라고는 전혀 없는 연인이자 신랑을 중심으로 펼쳐져 왔고, 게다가 근래에 와서는 그 구도가, 그가 어떻게든 준남작과 친척 관계여야 한다고 요구하는 듯했다. 이제 그녀와 그 낯선 이가 만났으니, 현실은 예상보다 훨씬 더 가슴 설레는 것으로 드러났고, 로저먼드는 이것이야말로 자기 생애의 위대한 전환점임을 의심할 수가 없었다. 그녀는 자기 자신의 징후를 깨어나는 사랑의 징후라고 판단했고, 리드게이트 씨가 그녀를 첫눈에 보고 사랑에 빠졌으리라는 것은 한층 더 당연하게 여겼다. 그런 일이야 무도회에서 그토록 자주 일어나는데, 게다가 안색이 한결 더 돋보이는 아침 햇살 속에서라고 안 될 이유가 어디 있겠는가? 로저먼드는 메리보다 나이가 많은 것도 아니었지만, 사랑의 대상이 되는 데에는 제법 익숙했다. 그러나 그녀로 말하자면, 풋풋한 젊은이든 한물간 노총각이든 양쪽 모두에게 무관심한 채 까다롭게 비판적인 태도를 지켜 왔다. 그런데 여기 리드게이트 씨야말로 별안간 그녀의 이상에 들어맞는 사람이었으니, 미들마치와는 전적으로 무관한 사람이고, 좋은 집안에 걸맞은 어떤 기품을 풍기며, 신분이라는 그 중산층의 천국으로 통하는 전망을 열어 줄 연줄을 지니고 있었다. 게다가 재능 있는 남자이기도 했으니, 그런 사람을 노예로 삼는 것이야말로 특히나 즐거운 일일 터였다. 요컨대 그는 그녀의 천성을 아주 새롭게 건드렸고, 그녀가 늘 현실에 맞세워 두곤 하던 어떤 가공의 \"있을 법한 일\"보다 더 나은, 생생한 흥미를 그녀의 삶에 가져다준 남자였다.\n\n이리하여 집으로 말을 달려 돌아가는 동안, 오빠도 누이도 저마다의 생각에 잠겨 말이 없으려 했다. 로저먼드는, 자기 구조물의 토대가 여느 때처럼 허황될 만큼 빈약했음에도, 일단 그 토대가 전제되고 나면 놀랄 만큼 세세하고 사실적인 상상력을 발휘하는 사람이었다. 그래서 그들이 채 한 마일도 달리기 전에 그녀는 이미 결혼 생활의 의상과 사교 소개에 한참 빠져들어, 미들마치에 마련할 자기 집을 정해 두었고, 멀리 떨어진 남편의 지체 높은 친척들을 찾아갈 일까지 내다보았으니, 그 세련된 예법을 그녀는 학창 시절의 교양을 익혔던 것만큼이나 철저히 제 것으로 만들 수 있을 터였고, 이렇게 해서 끝내 다가올지도 모를 한층 막연한 신분 상승에 미리 대비하는 셈이었다. 그녀의 예상에는 금전적인 것이라고는 전혀 없었고, 천박한 것은 더더욱 없었다. 그녀가 마음 쓰는 것은 세련됨이라 일컬어지는 것들이었지, 그것을 치를 돈이 아니었다.\n\n반면에 프레드의 마음은, 타고난 낙천성으로도 당장은 가라앉히지 못할 어떤 근심으로 분주했다. 그는 페더스톤의 어리석은 요구를 이행하는 일조차도 마뜩잖았지만, 그 요구를 피하려다가는 그보다 더 싫은 결과를 불러올 길밖에 보이지 않았다. 아버지는 이미 그에게 기분이 상해 있었고, 만약 그가 자기 집안과 벌스트로드 집안 사이에 어떤 추가적인 냉랭함이라도 불러오는 빌미가 된다면 더욱더 그러할 터였다. 게다가 그 자신도 외삼촌 벌스트로드를 찾아가 말을 거는 일이 끔찍이도 싫었고, 어쩌면 술을 마신 뒤에 페더스톤의 재산에 관해 어리석은 소리를 숱하게 늘어놓았을지도 모르며, 그것이 소문으로 부풀려졌을 수도 있었다. 프레드는 자기가 페더스톤 같은 별난 늙은 구두쇠한테서 받을 유산 기대를 떠벌리고 다니다가, 그 명령에 따라 보증서를 받으러 굽실거리러 가는 한심한 꼴이 되었다고 느꼈다. 하지만—그 유산 기대라니! 그는 정말로 그것을 품고 있었고, 그것을 포기한다면 마음에 드는 다른 대안이라고는 보이지 않았다. 게다가 그는 근래에 빚을 하나 졌는데, 그것이 그를 몹시 괴롭혔고, 늙은 페더스톤이 그것을 갚아 주겠다고 거의 흥정해 준 참이었다. 이 모든 일은 비참할 만큼 사소했다. 그의 빚도 적었고, 그의 유산 기대조차도 그리 대단한 것은 아니었다. 프레드는, 자기가 처한 곤경의 그 하찮음을 고백하기조차 부끄러울 만한 사람들을 알고 있었다. 이런 곱씹음은 자연히 한 줄기 인간 혐오의 신랄함을 자아냈다. 미들마치 제조업자의 아들로 태어나, 이렇다 할 무엇 하나 물려받지 못할 어쩔 수 없는 신세인데, 메인웨어링이나 바이언 같은 자들은 저렇건만—정녕 인생이란 보잘것없는 노릇이었다, 무엇이든 가장 좋은 것을 탐하는 왕성한 식욕을 지닌 패기 있는 젊은이에게 그토록 빈약한 앞날밖에 없다니.\n\n이 일에 벌스트로드의 이름을 끌어들인 것이 늙은 페더스톤의 꾸며 낸 이야기일 수도 있다는 생각은 프레드에게 떠오르지 않았다. 설령 그랬다 한들 그의 처지에는 아무 차이도 없었을 것이다. 그는, 노인이 자기를 조금 괴롭힘으로써 그 권력을 휘두르고 싶어 한다는 것, 또한 아마도 자기가 벌스트로드와 불편한 사이가 되는 꼴을 보면서 어떤 만족을 얻으려 한다는 것쯤은 충분히 꿰뚫어 보았다. 프레드는 자기가 외삼촌 페더스톤의 영혼 밑바닥까지 들여다본다고 자부했지만, 실은 거기서 본 것의 절반은 자기 자신의 성향이 반사된 것에 지나지 않았다. 다른 영혼을 안다는 그 어려운 일은, 의식의 대부분이 자기 욕망으로 이루어진 젊은 신사들의 몫이 아니다.\n\n프레드가 자기 자신과 벌인 논쟁의 주된 쟁점은, 이 일을 아버지에게 말해야 할 것인지, 아니면 아버지가 모르게 일을 처리하려 해야 할 것인지였다. 자기에 대해 떠들어 댄 사람은 아마 월 부인이었을 것이다. 그리고 만약 메리 가스가 월 부인의 그 소문을 로저먼드에게 옮겼다면, 그것은 틀림없이 아버지의 귀에까지 들어갈 테고, 아버지는 어김없이 그에게 그 일을 캐물을 것이었다. 그는 말의 속도를 늦추면서 로저먼드에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"로지, 월 부인이 나에 대해 뭔가 말했다고 메리가 너한테 그러던?\"\n\n\"응, 정말로 그랬어.\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\"\n\n\"오빠가 아주 행실이 헤프다고.\"\n\n\"그게 다였어?\"\n\n\"그 정도면 충분하다고 봐, 프레드.\"\n\n\"메리가 더 말한 건 없는 게 확실해?\"\n\n\"메리는 다른 건 아무 말도 안 했어. 하지만 정말이지, 프레드, 오빠는 부끄러운 줄 알아야 해.\"\n\n\"아, 무슨 헛소리! 나한테 설교하지 마. 그 일에 대해 메리가 뭐래?\"\n\n\"꼭 말해 줄 의무는 없는데. 오빠는 메리가 하는 말에 그렇게 끔찍이 신경 쓰면서, 정작 내가 말하게 가만두지도 않을 만큼 무례하니까.\"\n\n\"당연히 난 메리가 하는 말에 신경 써. 그 앤 내가 아는 가장 멋진 처녀야.\"\n\n\"난 그 애가 사랑에 빠질 만한 처녀라고는 한 번도 생각 안 해 봤는데.\"\n\n\"남자들이 어떤 처녀한테 반할지 네가 어떻게 알아? 처녀들은 절대 몰라.\"\n\n\"적어도, 프레드, 오빠한테 충고하자면 그 애한테 반하진 마. 그 애가 그러는데, 오빠가 청혼해도 자기는 결혼 안 할 거래.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 청혼할 때까지 기다리기라도 했어야지.\"\n\n\"오빠가 발끈할 줄 알았어, 프레드.\"\n\n\"전혀. 네가 약을 올리지 않았으면 그 앤 그런 말 안 했을 거야.\" 집에 닿기 전에 프레드는, 이 일 전부를 되도록 간단하게 아버지에게 털어놓기로 마음먹었으니, 어쩌면 아버지가 벌스트로드에게 말을 거는 그 불쾌한 일을 직접 떠맡아 줄지도 몰랐기 때문이다.\n\n# BOOK II. OLD AND YOUNG. CHAPTER XIII.\n\n늙음과 젊음\n첫째 신사. 당신은 그 사람을 어떻게 분류하시오?—대다수보다 나은 자로? 아니면 나아 보이지만 그 망토 아래 더 못된 자로? 성자로, 아니면 악당으로, 순례자로, 아니면 위선자로?\n\n둘째 신사. 아니, 그보다 당신의 그 책 더미는 어떻게 분류하는지부터 말해 보시오. 온 시대가 떠밀려 와 쌓인 잔해들 말이오. 차라리 크기와 장정으로 단번에 가르는 편이 나을 거요. 송아지 가죽, 키 큰 판본, 흔한 송아지 가죽본인들, 당신이 그 읽지도 않은 저자들을 분류하려고 교묘하게 고안해 낸 온갖 딱지보다 더 다양함을 덮어 주지는 못할 테니.\n\n프레드에게 들은 이야기 때문에, 빈시 씨는 한 시 반에 은행의 개인 사무실에서 불스트로드 씨와 이야기를 나누기로 마음먹었다. 그 시각이면 대개 다른 방문객이 없을 때였다. 그러나 한 시에 한 손님이 들어와 있었고, 불스트로드 씨가 그에게 할 말이 어찌나 많았던지 그 면담이 삼십 분 안에 끝날 가망은 거의 없어 보였다. 은행가의 화법은 유창했으나 또한 장황했고, 그는 짧은 명상적 침묵으로 적잖은 시간을 소모했다. 그의 병약한 외모가 누렇고 머리칼이 검은 부류였으리라고 상상하지는 마시라. 그는 핏기 없는 금발 피부에, 회색이 듬성듬성 섞인 가는 갈색 머리, 연한 회색 눈, 그리고 넓은 이마를 지니고 있었다. 목소리 큰 사람들은 그의 낮춘 어조를 두고 속삭임이라 불렀고, 때로는 그것이 솔직함과 양립하지 않는다는 뜻을 내비치기도 했다. 그러나 목소리 큰 사람이라고 해서 자기 목소리 말고 무언가를 감추지 못할 이유는 없어 보인다. 성경이 솔직함의 자리를 폐에 두었음을 증명할 수 있다면 모를까. 또한 불스트로드 씨는 남의 말을 들을 때 공손하게 몸을 숙이는 태도와, 눈에 어린 한결같이 집중하는 듯한 표정을 지니고 있었는데, 그 때문에 자신이 들을 만한 가치가 있다고 여기는 사람들은 그가 자신의 이야기에서 최대한의 유익을 구하고 있다고 짐작하게 되었다. 별로 대단한 인물이 못 되리라 여기는 다른 이들은 자기에게 비치는 이런 도덕적 등불을 싫어했다. 자기 술 창고가 자랑스럽지 않다면, 손님이 술잔을 빛에 비추어 들고서 감정관 같은 표정을 짓는 것을 보아도 흡족한 전율 따위는 없는 법이다. 그런 기쁨은 떳떳한 자부심을 지닌 자에게나 허락되는 것이다. 그리하여 불스트로드 씨의 면밀한 주의는 미들마치의 세리와 죄인들에게 달갑지 않았다. 어떤 이들은 그것을 그가 바리새인이기 때문이라 여겼고, 또 어떤 이들은 그가 복음주의자이기 때문이라 여겼다. 그들 가운데 좀 덜 피상적인 추론가들은 그의 아버지와 할아버지가 누구였는지 알고 싶어 했으니, 이십오 년 전만 해도 미들마치에서 불스트로드라는 이름을 들어 본 사람이 아무도 없었다는 점을 지적했다. 지금 그의 방문객인 리드게이트에게는 그 살피는 눈초리가 아무래도 상관없는 것이었다. 그는 그저 그 은행가의 체질에 대해 좋지 않은 견해를 품었고, 그가 손에 잡히는 것들을 거의 즐기지 못한 채 격렬한 내적 생활을 하고 있다고 결론지었다.\n\n“가끔 이곳으로 저를 찾아와 주신다면 더없이 고맙겠습니다, 리드게이트 선생.” 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 은행가가 말했다. “감히 바라는 대로, 병원 운영이라는 흥미로운 일에서 선생을 귀중한 협력자로 모시는 영광을 누리게 된다면, 우리가 사적으로 논의해야 할 문제가 많을 겁니다. 거의 완공된 새 병원에 관해서는, 열병 환자를 위한 특수 시설의 이점에 대해 선생이 하신 말씀을 고려해 보겠습니다. 결정은 제게 달려 있습니다. 메들리코트 경이 건물을 위해 땅과 목재를 내놓기는 했으나, 그 사업에 직접 관심을 기울일 뜻은 없으니까요.”\n\n“이런 지방 도시에서 그만큼 수고를 들일 가치가 있는 일도 드물지요.” 리드게이트가 말했다. “기존의 진료소에 더해 훌륭한 열병 병원이 있다면, 일단 의료 개혁이 이루어지고 나서는 이곳이 의학교의 중심이 될 수도 있을 겁니다. 그런 학교들이 전국에 퍼지는 것보다 의학 교육에 더 큰 도움이 될 일이 또 있겠습니까? 몇 가지 식견과 더불어 한 톨이라도 공공심을 지닌 타고난 지방 사람이라면, 평범한 것보다 조금이라도 나은 모든 것이 런던으로 몰려가는 흐름에 맞서 제가 할 수 있는 바를 해야 합니다. 어떤 정당한 직업적 목표든, 더 풍요롭지는 않을지언정 더 자유로운 무대를 지방에서 찾는 경우가 많지요.”\n\n리드게이트의 재능 가운데 하나는 평소 깊고 낭랑하면서도 적절한 순간에는 매우 낮고 부드러워질 줄 아는 목소리였다. 그의 일상적인 태도에는 어떤 호기로움이, 성공에 대한 두려움 없는 기대가, 자신의 능력과 진실성에 대한 자신감이 깃들어 있었는데, 그 자신감은 경험해 본 적 없는 사소한 장애나 유혹에 대한 경멸로 한층 굳건해진 것이었다. 그러나 이 당당한 솔직함은 꾸밈없는 선의의 표정 덕분에 사랑스러운 것이 되었다. 불스트로드 씨는 어쩌면 둘 사이의 어조와 태도의 차이 때문에 그를 더 좋아했는지도 모른다. 적어도 그가 미들마치에서 낯선 사람이라는 점 때문에, 로저먼드가 그러했듯 그를 더 좋아한 것만은 분명했다. 새로운 사람과는 참으로 많은 일을 시작할 수 있는 법이니!—심지어 더 나은 사람이 되기 시작할 수도 있는 것이다.\n\n“선생의 열정에 더 충분한 기회를 마련해 드리게 되어 기쁩니다.” 불스트로드 씨가 대답했다. “말하자면, 더 무르익은 식견이 그 결정을 뒷받침해 준다면 제 새 병원의 감독직을 선생께 맡기겠다는 뜻입니다. 저는 그토록 위대한 사업이 우리의 두 내과 의사에게 발목 잡히지 않도록 하기로 결심했으니까요. 사실, 선생이 이 도시에 오신 것을 두고 저는, 지금까지 적잖이 방해받아 온 제 노력에 이제 더 분명한 축복이 내리리라는 은혜로운 징표로 여기게 됩니다. 기존 진료소에 관해서는 우리가 첫 관문을 통과했습니다—선생의 선출 말입니다. 그리고 이제 저는 선생이 개혁가로 나섬으로써 동료 의사들에게서 어느 정도의 질시와 반감을 사게 되더라도 움츠러들지 않기를 바랍니다.”\n\n“용기가 있는 척하지는 않겠습니다.” 리드게이트가 미소 지으며 말했다. “하지만 싸움에서 적잖은 즐거움을 느낀다는 점은 인정합니다. 그리고 다른 모든 분야에서와 마찬가지로 의료에서도 더 나은 방법이 발견되고 시행될 수 있다고 믿지 않는다면, 저는 제 직업에 미련을 두지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“미들마치에서 그 직업의 수준은 낮습니다, 선생.” 은행가가 말했다. “지식과 기술 면에서 그렇다는 말이지, 사회적 지위에서 그렇다는 건 아닙니다. 우리 의사들은 대부분 이곳의 점잖은 시민들과 연이 닿아 있으니까요. 저 자신의 부실한 건강 탓에 저는 신의 자비가 우리 손이 닿는 곳에 마련해 둔 그 완화책들에 다소 관심을 기울이게 되었습니다. 수도의 저명한 분들과 상의해 보았고, 우리 지방 지역에서 의료가 시달리고 있는 그 낙후함을 뼈저리게 깨닫고 있습니다.”\n\n“그렇습니다—현재의 의료 규정과 교육으로는, 그저 어쩌다 한 번 괜찮은 개업의를 만나는 것에 만족해야 하지요. 진단의 출발점을 결정하는 온갖 고차원적 문제에 관해서는—의학적 증거의 철학에 관해서는—그것을 어렴풋이나마 이해하는 일조차 과학적 소양에서나 나올 수 있는데, 지방 개업의들은 대개 그런 것에 대해 달나라 사람이나 다름없이 아무 개념이 없습니다.”\n\n불스트로드 씨는 몸을 숙이고 골똘히 바라보면서, 리드게이트가 자신의 동의를 표현한 그 방식이 자기 이해력에 그리 잘 맞지 않음을 깨달았다. 그런 상황에서 분별 있는 사람은 화제를 바꾸어, 자신의 재능이 더 쓸모 있을 만한 영역으로 들어서는 법이다.\n\n“저도 압니다.” 그가 말했다. “의술의 특유한 성향이 물질적 수단 쪽으로 기운다는 것을요. 그렇지만, 리드게이트 선생, 선생이 적극적으로 관여할 일은 아니나 선생의 공감 어린 동의가 제게 도움이 될 만한 어떤 조치에 대해서는 우리의 생각이 어긋나지 않기를 바랍니다. 선생도 환자들에게 영적 관심사가 존재한다는 것은 인정하시리라 믿습니다만?”\n\n“물론 인정합니다. 하지만 그 말은 사람마다 다른 의미를 담기 마련이지요.”\n\n“바로 그렇습니다. 그리고 그런 문제에서는 잘못된 가르침이 가르침이 전혀 없는 것만큼이나 치명적입니다. 그런데 제가 꼭 이루고 싶어 하는 한 가지는 기존 진료소의 성직자 봉직에 관한 새 규정입니다. 그 건물은 페어브러더 씨의 교구 안에 있습니다. 페어브러더 씨를 아십니까?”\n\n“뵌 적이 있습니다. 제게 표를 주셨지요. 감사 인사를 드리러 찾아뵈어야 합니다. 무척 명랑하고 유쾌한 분 같더군요. 그리고 박물학자라고 들었습니다.”\n\n“페어브러더 씨는, 선생, 생각만 해도 몹시 가슴 아픈 사람입니다. 이 나라에 그보다 더 큰 재능을 지닌 성직자는 없으리라 생각합니다.” 불스트로드 씨는 말을 멈추고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯한 표정을 지었다.\n\n“저는 아직 미들마치에서 지나친 재능을 발견하여 가슴 아파한 적이 없습니다.” 리드게이트가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다.\n\n“제가 바라는 것은,” 불스트로드 씨가 한층 더 진지한 표정으로 말을 이었다. “페어브러더 씨의 병원 봉직을 전속 사제—실은 타이크 씨—의 임명으로 대체하는 것이며, 다른 어떤 영적 도움도 부르지 않는 것입니다.”\n\n“의사로서 저는 타이크 씨를 알지 못하는 한 그런 문제에 대해 의견을 가질 수 없으며, 설령 안다 해도 그가 어떤 경우에 부름받는지를 알아야 할 겁니다.” 리드게이트는 미소 지었으나, 신중을 기하려고 작정하고 있었다.\n\n“물론 지금 당장 이 조치의 시비를 온전히 따지실 수는 없겠지요. 하지만”—여기서 불스트로드 씨는 한층 또렷하게 힘주어 말하기 시작했다—“이 문제는 진료소의 의료 위원회에 회부될 가능성이 큽니다. 그래서 제가 선생께 청하고 싶은 것은, 제가 지금 기대하고 있는 우리 사이의 협력을 생각해서라도, 적어도 선생에 관한 한 이 문제에서 제 반대자들에게 휘둘리지 않으시리라는 점입니다.”\n\n“저는 성직자들의 분쟁에는 아무 관여도 하지 않기를 바랍니다.” 리드게이트가 말했다. “제가 택한 길은 제 직업 안에서 일을 잘 해내는 것입니다.”\n\n“제 책임은, 리드게이트 선생, 그보다 더 폭넓은 성질의 것입니다. 사실 제게 이 문제는 거룩한 책무에 관한 것입니다. 반면 제 반대자들에게는, 세속적 반목의 기질을 충족시키는 빌미라고 말할 만한 충분한 근거가 제게 있습니다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 저는 제 신념을 티끌만큼도 버리지 않을 것이며, 사악한 세대가 미워하는 그 진리와 저 자신을 동일시하기를 그만두지도 않을 것입니다. 저는 이 병원 개선 사업에 헌신해 왔습니다만, 리드게이트 선생, 솔직히 고백하건대 그 안에 죽음에 이르는 병의 치료 이상의 것이 걸려 있지 않다고 믿었다면 저는 병원에 아무 관심도 없었을 겁니다. 제게는 또 다른 행동의 근거가 있으며, 박해에 직면하더라도 저는 그것을 감추지 않겠습니다.”\n\n불스트로드 씨의 목소리는 그 마지막 말을 할 때 크고 격앙된 속삭임이 되어 있었다.\n\n“그 점에서는 우리가 분명 다릅니다.” 리드게이트가 말했다. 하지만 그는 이제 문이 열리고 빈시 씨가 들어왔다고 알려진 것이 싫지 않았다. 혈색 좋고 사교적인 그 인물은 로저먼드를 본 뒤로 그에게 더욱 흥미로운 존재가 되어 있었다. 그녀처럼 그가 두 사람의 운명이 하나로 엮이는 어떤 미래를 짜고 있었던 것은 아니지만, 남자란 매력적인 처녀를 기분 좋게 기억하기 마련이고, 그녀를 다시 볼 수 있는 곳이라면 기꺼이 만찬에 가는 법이다. 빈시 씨는 자리를 뜨기 전에, 그가 “전혀 서두르지 않았던” 그 초대를 건넸다. 로저먼드가 아침 식사 자리에서, 페더스톤 외삼촌이 그 새 의사를 무척 마음에 들어 하시는 것 같다고 말했기 때문이었다.\n\n불스트로드 씨는 처남과 단둘이 남게 되자 물 한 잔을 따르고 샌드위치 상자를 열었다.\n\n“자네에게 내 섭생법을 권해도 따르지 않겠지, 빈시?”\n\n“아니, 아니, 난 그런 방식엔 동의 못 하네. 인생엔 살집이 좀 있어야지.” 빈시 씨가 늘 지니고 다니는 지론을 빠뜨리지 못하고 말했다. “어쨌든,” 그는 무관한 이야기는 다 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 그 말에 힘을 주며 말을 이었다. “내가 여기 온 건 우리 집 망나니 녀석, 프레드에 관한 자그마한 일 때문일세.”\n\n“그건 자네와 내가, 식단 문제만큼이나 사뭇 다른 견해를 가질 법한 주제로군, 빈시.”\n\n“이번엔 아니길 바라네.” (빈시 씨는 좋은 기분을 유지하기로 작정하고 있었다.) “실은 늙은 페더스톤의 변덕 때문일세. 누군가가 앙심을 품고 이야기를 꾸며 내서는, 프레드와 사이를 틀어 놓으려고 그 노인에게 일러바쳤네. 영감은 프레드를 무척 아끼고, 그 애에게 무언가 후한 일을 해 줄 모양이야. 사실 자기 땅을 프레드에게 남기겠다고 그 애한테 거의 말한 거나 다름없고, 그래서 다른 사람들이 시샘하는 거지.”\n\n“빈시, 다시 한번 말하지만, 자네가 맏아들을 두고 취해 온 방침에 관해서는 나로부터 어떤 동의도 얻지 못할 걸세. 자네가 그 애를 성직자로 정한 것은 전적으로 세속적 허영심에서였네. 아들 셋에 딸 넷을 둔 처지에, 결국 그 애에게 사치스럽고 게으른 습관만 길러 주고 만 값비싼 교육에 돈을 들일 명분이 자네에겐 없었어. 이제 자네는 그 결과를 거두고 있는 걸세.”\n\n남의 잘못을 지적하는 일은 불스트로드 씨가 좀처럼 회피하지 않는 의무였으나, 빈시 씨는 그만큼 참을성 있게 받아들일 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 머지않아 시장이 될 전망을 가진 사람이, 상업의 이익을 위해 정치 전반에 단호한 태도를 취할 각오가 되어 있을 때면, 그는 자연히 자신이 세상의 틀에 중요한 존재라는 의식을 갖게 되고, 그런 의식은 개인적 처신의 문제 따위를 뒷전으로 밀어 버리는 듯하다. 그리고 이 특정한 질책은 다른 어떤 것보다도 그를 짜증나게 했다. 그가 그 결과를 거두고 있다는 말을 듣는 것은 그에게 더없이 쓸데없는 일이었다. 하지만 그는 자기 목이 불스트로드의 멍에 아래 놓여 있음을 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 평소 발길질을 즐기는 편이었으나, 이번에는 그 후련함을 자제하려고 안간힘을 썼다.\n\n“그 점에 관해서는, 불스트로드, 거슬러 따져 봤자 소용없네. 난 자네 같은 본보기 인간이 아니고, 그런 척하지도 않아. 장사에서 모든 걸 미리 내다볼 수는 없었네. 미들마치에 우리 가게보다 더 번창한 사업은 없었고, 그 녀석은 영리했지. 내 가엾은 형은 성직에 있었고, 잘 풀렸을 거야—이미 승진까지 했었는데, 그 위장병이 형을 데려갔지. 안 그랬으면 지금쯤 주임 사제가 됐을지도 몰라. 난 프레드를 위해 해 보려 한 일이 정당했다고 생각하네. 신앙 이야기를 꺼내자면, 사람이 제 먹을 고기를 한 온스까지 미리 잘라 두려 해서는 안 된다는 게 내 생각이야—섭리에 조금은 맡기고 너그러워야지. 제 가문을 조금이라도 일으켜 보려는 건 훌륭한 영국적 정서일세. 내 생각엔, 아들들에게 좋은 기회를 주는 게 아버지의 의무지.”\n\n“자네가 방금 늘어놓은 말이 온통 세속성과 모순된 어리석음 덩어리라고 내가 말하는 것은, 자네의 더없는 벗으로서 그러는 것일 뿐이네, 빈시.”\n\n“좋아.” 빈시 씨가 결심에도 불구하고 발길질을 하며 말했다. “난 세속적인 것 말고 다른 무엇인 척한 적이 없네. 게다가 세속적이지 않은 사람을 난 본 적이 없어. 자네라고 해서 이른바 비세속적인 원칙으로 사업을 하는 건 아닐 테지. 내가 보기에 유일한 차이라곤, 한쪽 세속성이 다른 쪽보다 조금 더 정직하다는 것뿐이야.”\n\n“이런 종류의 논의는 무익하네, 빈시.” 불스트로드 씨가 말했다. 그는 샌드위치를 다 먹고 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 피곤하다는 듯 손으로 눈을 가리고 있었다. “좀 더 구체적인 용건이 있었을 텐데.”\n\n“그래, 그래. 결론만 말하자면, 누군가가 자네를 출처로 대면서, 프레드가 자기 땅을 담보로 돈을 빌렸거나 빌리려 했다고 늙은 페더스톤에게 일러바쳤다네. 물론 자네가 그런 허튼소리를 한 적은 없겠지. 하지만 그 영감은, 프레드가 자네 필체로 된 부인서를 가져와야 한다고 우기네. 그러니까, 그 애가 그런 바보 같은 식으로 돈을 빌렸다거나 빌리려 했다거나 하는 그런 소리는 한마디도 믿지 않는다고 적은 짤막한 쪽지 말일세. 그 정도는 해 주는 데 거리낄 게 없겠지.”\n\n“미안하네. 거리끼는 게 있네. 나는 자네 아들이 그 무모함과 무지로—이보다 더 심한 말은 쓰지 않겠네—제 장래의 전망을 내세워 돈을 마련하려 들지 않았다고 결코 확신할 수 없고, 또 누군가가 그런 막연한 추정을 믿고 그 애에게 돈을 댈 만큼 어리석지 않았으리라고도 확신할 수 없네. 세상에는 다른 온갖 어리석음과 마찬가지로 그런 헤픈 대부 행위가 넘쳐 나니까.”\n\n“하지만 프레드는 외삼촌의 땅에 관한 어떤 묵계를 핑계로 돈을 빌린 적이 결단코 없다고 내게 명예를 걸고 말하네. 그 애는 거짓말쟁이가 아니야. 난 그 애를 실제보다 더 나은 사람으로 만들 생각은 없네. 난 그 애를 호되게 꾸짖었어—내가 그 애 행실을 못 본 척한다고는 아무도 못 할 거야. 하지만 그 애는 거짓말쟁이가 아닐세. 그리고 내 생각엔—내가 틀릴 수도 있지만—더 나쁜 사정을 알지 못하는 한, 젊은이를 좋게 믿어 주는 것을 가로막는 신앙 따위는 없을 줄 알았네. 믿을 만한 합당한 근거도 없는 해악을 그 애가 저질렀다고 믿지 않는다는 말조차 거부함으로써 그 애의 바퀴에 막대기를 끼우는 거라면, 그건 참 보잘것없는 신앙으로 보이는군.”\n\n“페더스톤의 재산을 장차 손에 넣도록 자네 아들의 앞길을 닦아 주는 것이 과연 그 애를 위하는 일인지 나는 도무지 확신할 수 없네. 나는 부를, 그것을 그저 이 세상의 수확으로만 쓰는 자들에게 축복이라고 여길 수 없어. 자네가 이런 말을 듣기 싫어하는 줄은 아네, 빈시. 하지만 이번만큼은 자네에게 말해야겠다는 사명감을 느끼네. 자네가 말하는 그런 식의 재산 처분을 거들 동기가 내겐 없네. 그것이 자네 아들의 영원한 복락에도, 신의 영광에도 이바지하지 않으리라고 나는 거리낌 없이 말하겠어. 그렇다면 자네는 어찌 내게, 어리석은 편애를 부추기고 어리석은 유증을 확보하는 것 말고는 아무 목적도 없는 그런 진술서를 쓰라고 기대하는 건가?”\n\n“성자와 복음 전도자 말고는 아무도 돈을 갖지 못하게 막을 작정이라면, 자네는 수지맞는 동업 몇 개는 포기해야 할 걸세. 내가 할 말은 그뿐이야.” 빈시 씨가 매우 퉁명스럽게 내뱉었다. “신의 영광을 위한 일일지는 몰라도, 플림데일네 가게가 브래싱 공장에서 들여오는 그 파랗고 푸른 염료를 쓰는 건 미들마치 상업의 영광을 위한 일이 아닐세. 그 염료는 비단을 썩게 하지, 내가 아는 건 그게 다야. 어쩌면 그 이익이 그렇게나 신의 영광으로 돌아간다는 걸 사람들이 안다면, 그걸 더 좋아할지도 모르지. 하지만 난 그런 건 별로 개의치 않네—내가 마음만 먹으면 제법 한바탕 소동을 일으킬 수도 있어.”\n\n불스트로드 씨는 대답하기 전에 잠시 뜸을 들였다. “자네가 그런 식으로 말하니 나는 몹시 가슴이 아프네, 빈시. 자네가 내 행동의 근거를 이해하리라고는 기대하지 않아—세상의 복잡한 얽힘 속에서 원칙이 나아갈 길을 한 가닥 더듬어 내는 일조차 쉽지 않은데, 하물며 그 가닥을 무심하고 비웃는 자들에게 또렷이 보여 주는 일이야 더더욱 그렇지. 부디 기억해 주게나, 나는 자네를 내 아내의 오라비로서 너그러이 대하고 있다는 것을, 그리고 내가 자네 가문의 세속적 지위를 위한 물질적 도움을 베풀지 않는다고 자네가 나를 탓하는 것은 자네에게 어울리지 않는다는 것을. 자네가 그 장사에서 자리를 지킬 수 있었던 것이 자네 자신의 분별이나 판단 덕분이 아니었음을 내가 일깨워야겠네.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 자넨 지금껏 내 장사로 손해 본 적이 없잖나.” 빈시 씨가 완전히 발끈해서 말했다. (그런 결과는 미리 한 결심으로도 좀처럼 오래 미뤄지지 않았다.) “그리고 자네가 해리엇과 결혼했을 때, 우리 두 집안이 같은 못에 함께 걸리지 않으리라고 어떻게 기대할 수 있었겠나? 자네 마음이 바뀌어서 우리 집안이 영락하기를 바란다면, 차라리 그렇게 말하게. 난 한 번도 바뀐 적이 없어. 난 지금도 그저 평범한 국교도일세, 교리 따위가 나오기 전에 그랬던 것처럼 말이야. 난 장사에서든 다른 무엇에서든, 세상을 있는 그대로 받아들이네. 난 이웃보다 나쁘지만 않으면 만족해. 하지만 자네가 우리를 영락시키고 싶다면, 그렇게 말하게. 그러면 난 어떻게 해야 할지 더 잘 알게 될 테니.”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리를 하는군. 자네 아들에 관한 이 편지가 없다고 자네 집안이 영락하기라도 한단 말인가?”\n\n“글쎄, 그러든 말든, 자네가 그걸 거절하는 건 매우 야박한 짓이라고 생각하네. 그런 짓이 겉으로는 신앙으로 안감을 댔을지 몰라도, 바깥에서 보면 고약하고 심술궂기 짝이 없어 보이네. 차라리 프레드를 중상모략하는 편이 낫겠어. 자네가 중상을 퍼뜨린 적 없다고 말하기를 거부하는 건 거의 그것과 마찬가지니까. 바로 이런 식이지—이 폭압적인 기질, 어디서나 주교 노릇에 은행가 노릇까지 하려 드는 이 기질—바로 이런 식이 사람의 이름에서 악취가 나게 만드는 걸세.”\n\n“빈시, 자네가 굳이 나와 다투려 든다면, 그건 나뿐 아니라 해리엇에게도 몹시 가슴 아픈 일이 될 걸세.” 불스트로드 씨가 평소보다 조금 더 다급하고 핏기 없는 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n“난 다투고 싶지 않네. 우리가 친구로 지내는 게 내게 이로운 일이고—어쩌면 자네에게도 그럴 테지. 난 자네에게 아무 원한도 없어. 난 자네를 다른 사람들보다 더 나쁘게 여기지 않네. 자네처럼 제 몸을 반쯤 굶기다시피 하고, 가족 기도며 뭐며 그렇게까지 하는 사람은, 그 신앙이 어떤 것이든 간에 자기 신앙을 믿는 거지. 자넨 욕설을 퍼붓고 악담을 하면서도 똑같이 빠르게 자본을 굴릴 수 있을 걸세—그렇게 하는 친구들이 수두룩하니까. 자네가 우두머리 노릇을 좋아한다는 건 부인할 수 없는 사실이지. 자넨 천국에서도 일등급이 되어야 할 거야, 안 그러면 별로 마음에 들지 않을 테니. 하지만 자넨 내 누이의 남편이고, 우린 서로 뭉쳐야 하네. 그리고 내가 해리엇을 안다면, 자네가 이런 식으로 하루살이를 걸러 내듯 까다롭게 굴고 프레드에게 호의 한 번 베풀기를 거부해서 우리가 다투게 된다면, 그건 자네 잘못이라고 여길 걸세. 그리고 난 그걸 의연하게 견디겠다고 말하진 못하겠네. 난 이게 야박한 짓이라고 생각해.”\n\n빈시 씨는 일어서서 외투 단추를 채우기 시작하며, 단호한 답을 요구한다는 뜻을 내비치려는 듯 처남을 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n불스트로드 씨가 빈시 씨를 훈계하는 것으로 시작했다가, 그 제조업자의 정신이 동료 인간들의 더 미묘한 빛과 그림자를 향해 들이대는 거칠고 아첨할 줄 모르는 거울 속에서 자기 자신의 몹시 못마땅한 모습을 보는 것으로 끝난 것이 이번이 처음은 아니었다. 어쩌면 그 경험이 이 장면이 어떻게 끝날지를 그에게 미리 일러 주었어야 했으리라. 그러나 가득 찬 샘은 그 물이 아무 쓸모도 없는 빗속에서조차 제 물을 아낌없이 내어 주는 법이고, 훌륭한 훈계의 샘 또한 그처럼 억누를 수 없기 마련이다.\n\n불편한 제안 때문에 곧바로 응하는 것은 불스트로드 씨의 천성에 맞지 않았다. 진로를 바꾸기 전에 그는 언제나 자신의 동기를 빚어내어 그것을 자신의 습관적인 기준에 부합시켜야 했다. 마침내 그가 말했다—\n\n“좀 생각해 보겠네, 빈시. 해리엇에게 이 문제를 이야기해 보지. 아마 자네에게 편지를 보내게 될 걸세.”\n\n“좋아. 되도록 빨리 부탁하네. 내일 자네를 만나기 전에 다 정리되었으면 좋겠군.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIV.\n\n여기 까다로운 입맛에 맞춘 그 소스의 정확한 비법이 있으니, 이름하여 게으름이라, 많은 이들이 즐겨 먹으며 달다고 일컫는 것. 먼저 사냥개처럼 한 입거리를 노리고, 주먹질을 잘 섞어 휘저으며, 걸쭉한 아첨의 기름을 넉넉히 두르고, 비열한 자화자찬의 거짓말로 거품을 낸다. 따뜻하게 내어라. 그것을 담아 둘 그릇으로는 죽은 자의 신발을 골라야 하느니.\n\n벌스트로드 씨가 해리엇에게 의논한 일은 빈시 씨가 바라던 효과를 거둔 듯했다. 이튿날 아침 일찍 편지 한 통이 도착했고, 프레드는 그것을 페더스톤 씨에게 가져가 요구된 증거로 내놓을 수 있었던 것이다.\n\n노신사는 추운 날씨 탓에 침대에 누워 있었는데, 메리 가스가 거실에 보이지 않자 프레드는 곧장 위층으로 올라가 외삼촌에게 편지를 내밀었다. 그는 등받이에 편안히 기대앉아, 평소와 다름없이 인류를 불신하고 좌절시키는 자신의 지혜를 의식하며 흐뭇해할 능력을 잃지 않은 상태였다. 그는 편지를 읽으려고 안경을 쓰면서 입술을 오므리고 그 양 끝을 아래로 끌어내렸다.\n\n\"'사정이 이러하므로 저의 확신을 밝히기를 거절하지 않겠습니다'—체! 이 친구가 무슨 거창한 말을 늘어놓는 거야! 경매인만큼이나 그럴듯하게 말하는군—'귀하의 아드님 프레더릭이 페더스톤 씨께서 약속하신 유증을 담보로 어떤 금전적 선급도 받은 바 없으며'—약속? 누가 내가 약속했다고 했나? 나는 아무것도 약속한 게 없어—나는 내가 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 유언 보충서를 만들 거야—'또한 그러한 행위의 성격을 고려할 때, 분별력과 인품을 갖춘 청년이 그것을 시도하리라고 추정하는 것은 불합리한 일입니다'—아, 하지만 이 신사 양반은 네가 분별력과 인품을 갖춘 청년이라고는 말하지 않는군, 그 점을 새겨 둬라, 이놈아!—'그러한 성격의 소문에 제가 관련되어 있다는 점에 관하여는, 귀하의 아드님이 페더스톤 씨 사후에 그에게 돌아올 어떤 재산을 담보로 돈을 빌렸다는 취지의 진술을 결코 한 적이 없음을 분명히 단언하는 바입니다'—이런 세상에! '재산'—'돌아올'—'사후'라니! 변호사 스탠디시도 이 친구에 비하면 아무것도 아니야. 돈을 빌리려 할 때조차도 이보다 더 그럴듯하게 말하지는 못할 거다. 자,\" 페더스톤 씨는 여기서 안경 너머로 프레드를 쳐다보며 경멸하는 몸짓으로 편지를 돌려주었다. \"벌스트로드가 그럴듯하게 써 놓았다고 해서 내가 그 말을 믿을 줄 아느냐, 응?\"\n\n프레드는 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"외삼촌께서 편지를 원하셨잖아요. 제 생각엔 벌스트로드 씨가 부인하는 말이, 그가 부인하는 그 내용을 외삼촌께 일러바친 사람의 권위만큼은 믿을 만한 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"꼭 그만큼이지. 나는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 믿는다고 말한 적이 없다. 그래, 이제 뭘 기대하느냐?\" 페더스톤 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 말하며 안경은 그대로 쓴 채 두 손을 이불 속으로 거두어들였다.\n\n\"아무것도 기대하지 않습니다, 외삼촌.\" 프레드는 짜증을 터뜨리고 싶은 마음을 가까스로 억눌렀다. \"편지를 가져다 드리려고 왔습니다. 원하신다면 이만 물러가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아직, 아직 아니다. 종을 울려라. 아가씨를 들어오게 해야겠다.\"\n\n종소리에 응해 들어온 것은 하인이었다.\n\n\"아가씨를 들어오라고 해!\" 페더스톤 씨가 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"도대체 무슨 일로 나가 있는 거야?\" 메리가 들어왔을 때도 그는 같은 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"내가 가라고 할 때까지 여기 가만히 앉아 있을 수는 없었더냐? 이제 조끼를 다오. 늘 침대 위에 올려놓으라고 일렀잖느냐.\"\n\n메리의 눈은 마치 울었던 것처럼 다소 붉어 보였다. 페더스톤 씨가 그날 아침 가장 까칠한 기분에 빠져 있는 것이 분명했고, 프레드는 이제 그토록 절실히 필요했던 돈을 선물로 받게 될 전망이었음에도, 차라리 자유로운 몸이 되어 그 늙은 폭군을 돌아보며 메리 가스는 당신이 부른다고 굽실거릴 사람이 아니라고 말해 주고 싶었다. 그녀가 방에 들어왔을 때 프레드가 일어섰지만, 그녀는 그를 거의 알아보지도 못했고, 무언가가 자기에게 날아오리라는 예감에 신경이 떨리는 듯한 표정이었다. 하지만 그녀가 두려워해야 할 것이라곤 말 이상의 것은 결코 없었다. 그녀가 못에서 조끼를 집으려 다가가자 프레드가 그녀에게 가서 말했다. \"제가 하지요.\"\n\n\"내버려 둬! 네가 가져와라, 아가씨, 여기 내려놔.\" 페더스톤 씨가 말했다. \"이제 다시 나가 있다가 내가 부르거든 오너라.\" 조끼가 그의 옆에 놓이자 그가 덧붙였다. 한 사람에게 호의를 베푸는 즐거움에다 다른 사람에게 유난히 못되게 구는 것으로 양념을 치는 것이 그의 버릇이었는데, 메리는 언제나 곁에 있어 그 양념거리를 제공해 주었다. 그의 친척들이 올 때면 그녀는 더 나은 대접을 받았다. 그는 천천히 조끼 주머니에서 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내고, 천천히 이불 밑에 있던 양철 상자를 끄집어냈다.\n\n\"내가 너한테 작은 재산이라도 줄 거라고 기대하느냐, 응?\" 그가 안경 너머로 올려다보며 뚜껑을 여는 동작을 멈춘 채 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 아닙니다, 외삼촌. 며칠 전에 친절하게도 제게 선물을 주겠다고 말씀하셨으니 드린 말씀이지, 그렇지 않았다면 물론 그 일을 생각조차 하지 않았을 겁니다.\" 하지만 프레드는 낙천적인 성품이었고, 어떤 걱정거리에서 자신을 벗어나게 해 줄 만큼 딱 충분한 액수의 환상이 그의 머릿속에 떠올라 있었다. 프레드는 빚을 지면 언제나, 무언가가—그것이 무엇인지 굳이 떠올리지는 않았지만—일어나 제때에 갚을 수 있게 되리라는 것이 지극히 그럴듯한 일로 여겨졌다. 그리고 이제 그 섭리적인 사건이 분명 코앞에 닥친 듯하니, 그 공급이 필요에 못 미치리라고 생각하는 것은 순전한 어리석음일 터였다. 온전한 기적을 믿을 힘이 없어 반쪽짜리 기적을 믿는 신앙만큼이나 어리석은 일이었다.\n\n깊은 핏줄이 도드라진 손이 지폐를 한 장 한 장 만지작거리다가 다시 평평하게 내려놓는 동안, 프레드는 안달하는 모습을 보이기를 경멸하며 의자에 등을 기대고 있었다. 그는 마음속으로 자신을 신사라 여겼고, 돈을 노리고 노인에게 굽실거리는 것을 좋아하지 않았다. 마침내 페더스톤 씨는 안경 너머로 다시 그를 살피더니 작은 지폐 뭉치를 건넸다. 프레드는 덜 중요한 가장자리들이 그를 향해 벌어져 있어 그것이 다섯 장뿐임을 또렷이 알 수 있었다. 하지만 그래도 한 장이 오십 파운드를 뜻할 수도 있었다. 그는 그것을 받으며 말했다.\n\n\"정말 감사합니다, 외삼촌.\" 그러고는 그 가치를 생각하지 않는 척하며 돌돌 말아 넣으려 했다. 하지만 그것은 그를 유심히 지켜보던 페더스톤 씨의 마음에 들지 않았다.\n\n\"이봐, 그걸 세어 볼 가치도 없다고 생각하느냐? 너는 영주처럼 돈을 받는구나. 영주처럼 잃어버리겠지, 아마.\"\n\n\"받은 말의 입 속을 들여다보지 말라*고 하지 않습니까, 외삼촌. 하지만 기꺼이 세어 보겠습니다.\"\n\n* 받은 말의 입 속을 들여다보지 말라: 선물의 흠을 따지지 말라는 뜻의 서양 속담. 말의 나이는 이빨로 가늠한다는 데서 유래했다.\n하지만 다 세어 보고 나니 프레드는 그렇게 기쁘지 않았다. 그것은 실제로 그의 낙관이 분명 그래야 한다고 결정해 두었던 액수보다 적다는 어처구니없음을 드러냈던 것이다. 사물의 합당함이란 사람의 기대에 들어맞는 합당함이 아니라면 대체 무엇을 뜻하겠는가? 그것이 어긋날 때 어처구니없음과 무신론이 그의 등 뒤에서 입을 벌린다. 자기가 쥔 것이 이십 파운드짜리 다섯 장에 지나지 않음을 알았을 때 프레드가 받은 충격은 가혹했고, 이 나라의 고등 교육에 그가 참여한 몫도 그에게 도움이 되어 주는 것 같지 않았다. 그럼에도 그는 고운 얼굴빛을 빠르게 바꾸어 가며 말했다.\n\n\"정말 후하십니다, 외삼촌.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고.\" 페더스톤 씨는 상자를 잠그고 제자리에 도로 넣은 뒤, 천천히 안경을 벗었다. 그러고는 마침내 마음속 명상이 그를 한층 더 깊이 납득시키기라도 한 듯 되풀이했다. \"후하다고 봐야지.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 외삼촌, 무척 감사드립니다.\" 명랑한 태도를 되찾을 시간을 가진 프레드가 말했다.\n\n\"그래야 마땅하지. 너는 세상에서 한자리 차지하고 싶어 하는데, 네가 믿을 만한 사람은 이 피터 페더스톤뿐이라고 나는 본다.\" 여기서 노인의 두 눈은 이 영리한 젊은이가 자기를 의지하고 있다는 의식과, 그렇게 의지하다니 이 영리한 젊은이가 어지간히 바보라는 의식이 묘하게 뒤섞인 만족감으로 번득였다.\n\n\"네, 정말 그렇습니다. 저는 그리 화려한 기회를 타고나지 못했지요. 저만큼 옹색하게 살아온 사람도 드물 겁니다.\" 프레드는 자기가 얼마나 가혹한 처지에 놓여 있는지를 생각하며, 자신의 덕성에 다소 놀라는 마음으로 말했다. \"숨이 가빠 헐떡이는 사냥말을 타야 한다는 것은, 그리고 외삼촌만큼 안목도 절반밖에 안 되는 자들이 형편없는 물건을 사들이느라 돈을 얼마든지 내던지는 꼴을 보아야 한다는 것은, 정말이지 너무하다 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"음, 이제 너도 좋은 사냥말을 한 마리 살 수 있겠구나. 그러기엔 팔십 파운드면 충분하다고 본다—그리고 이십 파운드가 남으니 무슨 자잘한 곤경에서든 빠져나올 수 있겠지.\" 페더스톤 씨가 살짝 킬킬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"정말 인자하십니다, 외삼촌.\" 프레드는 그 말과 자기 심정 사이의 그럴듯한 대조를 의식하며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 네 그 잘난 외삼촌 벌스트로드보다야 좀 나은 외삼촌이지. 그자의 투기로는 네가 별로 건질 게 없을걸. 내가 듣기로는 그자가 네 아버지 다리에 꽤 단단한 끈을 옭아매 놓았다던데, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"제 아버지는 사업 얘기를 제게 전혀 하지 않으십니다, 외삼촌.\"\n\n\"흠, 그 점에선 분별이 좀 있구나. 하지만 사람들은 그 양반이 말하지 않아도 다 알아낸단다. 그자가 네게 남길 재산은 별로 없을 거다. 십중팔구 유언도 없이 죽을 테고—그런 짓을 할 위인이지—사람들이 그자를 미들마치 시장으로 추대하든 말든 말이야. 하지만 그자가 유언 없이 죽는다 해도, 네가 비록 장남이긴 하다만, 별로 얻을 게 없을 거다.\"\n\n프레드는 페더스톤 씨가 이전에 이렇게까지 못마땅하게 군 적이 없었다고 생각했다. 하기야 그가 이전에 이렇게 한꺼번에 많은 돈을 준 적도 없긴 했다.\n\n\"이 벌스트로드 씨의 편지는 없애 버릴까요, 외삼촌?\" 프레드가 그것을 불 속에 넣기라도 할 것처럼 편지를 들고 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 나는 필요 없다. 내겐 한 푼 값어치도 없는 거야.\"\n\n프레드는 편지를 불가로 가져가 신이 나서 부지깽이로 푹 찔러 넣었다. 그는 방에서 나가고 싶어 안달이 났지만, 돈을 주머니에 넣자마자 곧장 달아나는 것이 외삼촌 앞에서뿐 아니라 자기 양심 앞에서도 다소 부끄러웠다. 이윽고 농장 관리인이 주인에게 보고하러 올라왔고, 프레드는 곧 또 오라는 분부를 받고 물러가게 되어 이루 말할 수 없는 안도를 느꼈다.\n\n그는 외삼촌에게서 풀려나기를 바랐을 뿐 아니라 메리 가스를 찾기를 바라기도 했다. 그녀는 이제 손에 바느질감을 들고 곁의 작은 탁자에 책을 펼쳐 둔 채, 늘 앉던 난롯가 자리에 있었다. 이제 그녀의 눈꺼풀은 붉은 기가 좀 가셨고, 평소의 자제하는 분위기를 되찾고 있었다.\n\n\"위층에서 저를 부르시나요?\" 프레드가 들어서자 그녀가 반쯤 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"아니요. 시먼스가 올라가서 저는 그냥 물러나라는 거예요.\"\n\n메리는 다시 앉아 일감을 이어 갔다. 그녀는 분명 평소보다 더 무심하게 그를 대하고 있었다. 그가 위층에서 그녀를 위해 얼마나 애정 어린 분노를 느꼈는지 그녀는 알지 못했다.\n\n\"여기 좀 있어도 될까요, 메리, 아니면 제가 지루하게 할까요?\"\n\n\"앉으세요.\" 메리가 말했다. \"어제 여기 왔던 존 워울 씨만큼 지루하게 굴진 않으실 테니까요. 그 사람은 제 허락도 구하지 않고 앉아 버리던걸요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 친구! 제 생각엔 그가 당신을 사랑하는 것 같던데요.\"\n\n\"저는 모르는 일이에요. 그리고 제겐, 친절하게 대해 주고 제가 고마워하는 어떤 남자와 저 사이에 늘 사랑에 빠진다는 가정이 끼어들어야 한다는 것이 여자의 삶에서 가장 역겨운 일 중 하나예요. 적어도 저만은 그런 일에서 안전할 줄 알았어요. 제게 가까이 오는 사람마다 저를 사랑한다고 상상하는 그런 터무니없는 허영을 부릴 근거가 제겐 없으니까요.\"\n\n메리는 어떤 감정도 드러낼 생각이 없었지만, 자기도 모르게 분함이 떨리는 어조로 끝맺고 말았다.\n\n\"존 워울 따위는 빌어먹을! 당신을 화나게 할 생각은 아니었어요. 당신이 제게 고마워할 이유가 있는 줄도 몰랐고요. 누가 당신을 위해 촛불 심지 하나 잘라 줘도 당신이 그걸 대단한 봉사로 여긴다는 걸 깜빡했네요.\" 프레드 역시 자존심이 있었던지라, 메리의 이 격한 토로를 무엇이 불러일으켰는지 자기가 안다는 것을 드러낼 생각이 없었다.\n\n\"아, 화난 건 아니에요. 세상 돌아가는 방식에 화가 났을 뿐이지요. 저는 제게 상식이라도 있는 사람인 양 말을 걸어 주는 게 정말 좋아요. 사실 저는 종종, 대학에 다녀온 젊은 신사분들에게서 듣는 것보다 조금은 더 많이 이해할 수 있을 것 같은 기분이 들거든요.\" 메리는 평정을 되찾았고, 듣기 좋게 잔물결처럼 일렁이는 웃음을 속으로 억누르며 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 당신이 저를 두고 아무리 즐거워해도 괜찮아요.\" 프레드가 말했다. \"당신이 위층에 올라왔을 때 무척 슬퍼 보였거든요. 당신이 여기 남아 그런 식으로 시달려야 한다는 건 정말 부끄러운 일이에요.\"\n\n\"아, 저는 비교적 편한 삶을 살고 있는걸요. 교사가 되어 보려고도 했지만 그 일엔 맞지 않더군요. 제 마음은 너무 제멋대로 떠돌기를 좋아해서요. 보수를 받는 일을 하는 척하면서 실제로는 결코 제대로 하지 않는 것보다는 어떤 고생이든 더 낫다고 생각해요. 여기서 하는 일은 무엇이든 저도 남들만큼 잘할 수 있어요. 어쩌면 어떤 이들보다는 더 잘하겠지요—이를테면 로지보다요. 비록 그 애는 동화 속에서 도깨비들과 함께 갇혀 있는, 바로 그런 아름다운 존재이긴 하지만요.\"\n\n\"로지가요!\" 프레드가 오빠다운 깊은 회의가 담긴 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"이봐요, 프레드!\" 메리가 단호하게 말했다. \"그렇게 비판할 권리는 당신에게 없어요.\"\n\n\"지금 무슨 특별한 걸 두고 하는 말인가요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 일반적인 걸 두고 하는 말이에요—늘 그렇다는 거죠.\"\n\n\"아, 내가 게으르고 헤프다는 거군요. 음, 나는 가난한 사람이 될 자질이 못 돼요. 부자였다면 나쁜 녀석은 되지 않았을 텐데.\"\n\n\"하느님이 당신을 불러 주시지 않은 그 처지에서나마 당신은 본분을 다했겠지요.\" 메리가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"음, 당신이 가정 교사로서 본분을 다할 수 없는 것이나 마찬가지로, 나도 성직자로서 본분을 다할 수 없어요. 그 점에선 당신도 조금은 동병상련을 느껴야 마땅하잖아요, 메리.\"\n\n\"당신이 성직자가 되어야 한다고는 한 번도 말한 적 없어요. 다른 종류의 일도 있잖아요. 어떤 길도 정하지 못하고 그에 따라 행동하지 못한다는 건 제겐 너무나 비참해 보여요.\"\n\n\"나도 할 수 있어요, 만약—\" 프레드는 말을 끊고 일어서서 벽난로 선반에 기댔다.\n\n\"재산을 물려받지 못하리라는 게 확실하다면 말이죠?\"\n\n\"그런 말이 아니에요. 당신은 나와 다투고 싶어 하는군요. 나에 대해 남들이 하는 말에 휘둘리다니 당신답지 않게 너무하네요.\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 당신과 다투고 싶어 하겠어요? 그러려면 내 새 책들 전부와 다퉈야 할 텐데.\" 메리가 탁자 위의 책을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"당신이 다른 사람들에게 아무리 못되게 굴어도, 제겐 좋은 사람이니까요.\"\n\n\"누구보다도 당신을 더 좋아하니까요. 하지만 당신이 나를 경멸한다는 건 알아요.\"\n\n\"네, 맞아요—조금은요.\" 메리가 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 모든 일에 현명한 견해를 지닌 굉장한 남자라면 우러러보겠지요.\"\n\n\"네, 그럴 거예요.\" 메리는 빠르게 바느질을 하고 있었는데, 얄미울 만큼 상황을 장악하고 있는 것 같았다. 대화가 우리에게 불리한 쪽으로 흘러가면, 우리는 그저 어색함의 수렁 속으로 점점 더 깊이 빠져들 뿐이다. 프레드 빈시가 느낀 것이 바로 그것이었다.\n\n\"여자는, 남자가 흔히 그러는 것과 달리, 늘 알고 지내 온 사람—기억할 수 있는 한 줄곧 알아 온 사람과는 결코 사랑에 빠지지 않는 모양이에요. 여자의 마음을 사로잡는 건 언제나 새로운 사내지요.\"\n\n\"어디 봐요.\" 메리가 입꼬리를 짓궂게 말아 올리며 말했다. \"제 경험을 되짚어 봐야겠네요. 줄리엣이 있군요—그 애는 당신 말의 예가 될 것 같아요. 하지만 오필리아는 아마 햄릿을 오랫동안 알았을 테고, 브렌다 트로일은 모돈트 머튼을 어릴 적부터 알았는데, 그래도 그는 훌륭한 청년이었던 것 같아요. 그리고 미나는 낯선 사람이었던 클리블런드를 더욱 깊이 사랑했지요. 웨이벌리는 플로라 매카이버에게 새로운 사람이었지만, 그래도 그녀는 그와 사랑에 빠지지 않았어요. 그리고 올리비아와 소피아 프림로즈가 있고, 코린이 있는데—이들은 새로운 남자들과 사랑에 빠졌다고 할 수 있겠네요. 통틀어 보면 제 경험은 꽤 뒤섞여 있군요.\"\n\n메리는 다소 장난스럽게 프레드를 올려다보았고, 그 눈길은 그에게 무척 소중했다. 비록 그 눈이라곤 관찰이 웃으며 앉아 있는 맑은 창에 지나지 않았지만 말이다. 그는 분명 다정한 친구였고, 소년에서 어른으로 자라면서 옛 놀이 동무에 대한 사랑도 함께 자라났다. 그가 신분과 수입에 대한 안목을 한껏 높여 준, 이 나라의 고등 교육에 참여한 그 몫에도 불구하고 말이다.\n\n\"사랑받지 못하는 남자가, 자기는 더 나은 사람이 될 수 있다고—무슨 일이든 할 수 있다고—그러니까 사랑이 보답으로 돌아오리라는 확신만 있다면 그럴 수 있다고 말해 봐야 아무 소용이 없겠죠.\"\n\n\"더 나아질 수 있다고 말해 봐야 세상에 아무짝에도 쓸모없죠. ~할지도 모른다, ~할 수 있다, ~할 텐데—그건 경멸스러운 조동사들이에요.\"\n\n\"한 여자가 자기를 진심으로 사랑해 주지 않는 한, 남자가 어떻게 무언가에 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 수 있을지 나는 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"그 선함이라는 건 그런 걸 기대하기 전에 먼저 와야 한다고 봐요.\"\n\n\"당신도 잘 알잖아요, 메리. 여자들은 남자의 선함을 보고 사랑하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠. 하지만 일단 사랑하게 되면, 결코 그 남자를 나쁘다고 여기지 않아요.\"\n\n\"나를 나쁘다고 하는 건 좀 공정치 못하네요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"나는 영영 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 사람이 될 거예요, 메리, 당신이 나를 사랑한다고 말해 주지 않는다면—나와 결혼하겠다고 약속해 주지 않는다면—그러니까, 내가 결혼할 수 있게 되었을 때 말이에요.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 사랑한다 해도, 저는 당신과 결혼하지 않겠어요. 분명코 당신과 결혼하겠다는 약속 따위는 결코 하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그건 정말 못된 거예요, 메리. 나를 사랑한다면 나와 결혼하겠다고 약속해야 마땅하잖아요.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 제가 당신을 사랑한다 해도 당신과 결혼하는 건 못된 짓일 거라고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"지금 이대로의 나, 아내를 부양할 아무런 방도도 없는 나를 두고 하는 말이군요. 물론이죠. 나는 겨우 스물셋이니까요.\"\n\n\"그 마지막 점은 당신이 달라지겠죠. 하지만 다른 어떤 변화에 대해서는 그리 확신할 수가 없어요. 제 아버지는 게으른 남자란 존재해서도 안 되고, 결혼은 더더욱 해서도 안 된다고 말씀하세요.\"\n\n\"그럼 내 머리에 총이라도 쏘란 말인가요?\"\n\n\"아니요. 전체적으로 보아 당신은 시험에 합격하는 편이 더 나을 것 같아요. 페어브러더 씨 말씀이 그게 창피할 만큼 쉽다고 하던걸요.\"\n\n\"그거 참 듣기 좋은 말이군요. 그분에겐 뭐든 쉽죠. 영리함과는 아무 상관도 없다는 게 아니라—나는 합격하는 많은 사람들보다 열 배는 더 영리하다고요.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 메리가 빈정거림을 억누르지 못하고 말했다. \"그러니 크라우스 씨 같은 부목사들이 나오는 거군요. 당신의 영리함을 열로 나누면, 그 몫이—어머나!—학위를 딸 수 있다는 거네요. 하지만 그건 당신이 다른 사람들보다 열 배는 더 게으르다는 걸 보여 줄 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"음, 내가 합격한다 해도, 당신은 내가 성직에 들어가길 바라지는 않겠죠?\"\n\n\"그건 문제가 아니에요—내가 당신이 무엇을 하길 바라는지는요. 당신에게도 양심은 있을 거 아니에요. 저런! 리드게이트 씨가 오시네요. 가서 외삼촌께 알려 드려야겠어요.\"\n\n\"메리.\" 프레드가 그녀가 일어서는 순간 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"당신이 내게 어떤 격려도 해 주지 않는다면, 나는 나아지기는커녕 더 나빠질 거예요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신에게 아무런 격려도 해 주지 않을 거예요.\" 메리가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"당신 친구들도 그걸 못마땅해할 테고, 제 식구들도 마찬가지일 거예요. 제 아버지는 빚을 지고도 일하려 하지 않는 남자를 제가 받아들인다면 그걸 제 수치로 여기실 거예요!\"\n\n프레드는 마음이 찔려 그녀의 손을 놓았다. 그녀는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔지만, 거기서 돌아서서 말했다. \"프레드, 당신은 늘 제게 무척 잘해 주고 너그러웠어요. 저는 은혜를 모르는 사람이 아니에요. 하지만 다시는 제게 그런 식으로 말하지 말아요.\"\n\n\"좋아요.\" 프레드가 시무룩하게 말하며 모자와 채찍을 집어 들었다. 그의 얼굴에는 옅은 분홍빛과 핏기 없는 흰빛이 군데군데 얼룩져 있었다. 시험에 떨어진 수많은 게으른 젊은 신사들처럼, 그는 철저히 사랑에 빠져 있었다. 그것도 돈 한 푼 없는 평범한 처녀에게! 하지만 페더스톤 씨의 토지가 배경에 버티고 있다는 것, 그리고 메리가 무어라 말하든 그녀가 정말로 자기를 마음에 두고 있다는 믿음이 있었기에, 프레드는 완전히 절망에 빠지지는 않았다.\n\n집에 돌아온 그는 이십 파운드짜리 가운데 네 장을 어머니에게 주며 자기 대신 맡아 달라고 부탁했다. \"이 돈은 쓰고 싶지 않아요, 어머니. 빚을 갚는 데 쓰려고요. 그러니 제 손이 닿지 않게 안전히 보관해 주세요.\"\n\n\"착하기도 하지, 우리 아들.\" 빈시 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 맏아들과 막내딸(여섯 살배기 아이)을 끔찍이 아꼈는데, 다른 사람들은 그 둘을 그녀의 가장 말썽꾸러기 자식들로 여겼다. 어머니의 눈이 그 편애 속에서 늘 속는 것은 아니다. 적어도 누가 다정하고 효심 깊은 자식인지는 어머니가 가장 잘 판단할 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 프레드는 분명 어머니를 무척 좋아했다. 어쩌면 또 다른 한 사람을 향한 애정 때문에, 백 파운드를 써 버리고 마는 자신의 약점에 대비해 무언가 안전장치를 마련해 두려고 그가 유난히 안달했는지도 모른다. 그가 백육십 파운드를 빚진 채권자는, 메리의 아버지가 서명한 어음의 형태로 더 단단한 담보를 쥐고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n# 제13장\n\n검은 눈동자는 이제 놓아 버렸다 그대 말하고, 파란 눈동자는 그대를 끌지 못한다 하나, 오늘 그대는 어느 때보다 넋을 빼앗긴 듯하니, 예전에 우리가 보던 그대보다 더하구나.\n\n오, 나는 가장 어여쁜 미인을 좇는다 쾌락의 새로운 은신처를 두루 누비며, 여기 발자국 저기 메아리를 따라 내 보물에게로 인도되어 가노라.\n\n보라! 그녀가 돌아선다—불멸의 젊음이 필멸의 몸으로 빚어졌으니, 별빛의 오래된 진리처럼 싱그럽도다— 여러 이름을 지닌 자연이여!\n\n스스로를 그렇게 부르기를 고집했던 한 위대한 역사가는 백이십 년 전에 세상을 떠나는 행복을 누렸으니, 그리하여 우리네 살아 있는 보잘것없음이 그 거대한 다리 밑을 걸어 다니는 모습으로 관찰되는 거인들 사이에 자리 잡았다. 그는 자신의 풍부한 논평과 여담을 작품에서 가장 모방하기 어려운 부분으로 자랑스러워했으며, 특히 그의 역사서 각 권의 첫머리에 놓인 도입 장들을 자랑했다. 그곳에서 그는 마치 자기 안락의자를 무대 앞으로 끌어다 놓고, 그 훌륭한 영어의 활기차고 편안한 어조로 우리와 한담을 나누는 듯하다. 하지만 필딩은 하루가 더 길던 시절에 살았으니(시간이란 돈과 마찬가지로 우리의 필요에 따라 가늠되는 것이므로), 여름 오후가 널찍하고 겨울 저녁이면 시계가 느릿느릿 째깍이던 때였다. 우리처럼 뒤늦게 온 역사가들은 그의 본보기를 따라 한가로이 머뭇거려서는 안 된다. 만일 우리가 그렇게 한다면, 우리의 한담은 앵무새 우리에서 접의자에 앉아 늘어놓는 듯, 빈약하고 조급한 것이 되기 십상일 터이다. 적어도 나는 어떤 인간들의 운명을 풀어내고, 그것들이 어떻게 짜이고 또 서로 엮였는지 살피는 데 할 일이 너무도 많아서, 내가 부릴 수 있는 모든 빛을 이 특정한 직물 하나에 집중해야지, 우주라 불리는 그 유혹적인 연관성의 영역에 흩뿌려서는 안 된다.\n\n지금 나는 새로 정착한 리드게이트를, 그에게 관심 있는 누구에게든, 그가 미들마치에 온 이래 그를 가장 많이 본 사람들조차 알 수 있었던 것보다 더 잘 알려야 한다. 분명 누구나 인정할 것이다. 한 사람이 추켜세워지고 칭송받고 시기당하고 조롱당하며, 도구로 점쳐지고 사랑받거나 적어도 미래의 남편감으로 선택되면서도, 사실상 알려지지 않은 채—이웃들의 그릇된 억측을 위한 한 무리의 기호로만 알려진 채 남아 있을 수 있다는 것을. 그렇지만 리드게이트가 아주 평범한 시골 의사는 아니라는 일반적인 인상이 있었고, 당시 미들마치에서 그런 인상은 그에게서 대단한 무언가가 기대된다는 의미였다. 누구네 집 주치의든 한결같이 놀랍도록 영리했고, 가장 변덕스럽거나 고약한 질병들을 다루고 길들이는 데 헤아릴 수 없는 솜씨를 지녔다고 여겨졌기 때문이다. 그 영리함의 증거는 보다 고차원적인 직관의 종류에 속하는 것이었으니, 그것은 그 부인 환자들의 흔들리지 않는 확신에 자리 잡고 있었고, 그들의 직관이 그에 못지않게 강한 다른 직관들과 맞부딪힌다는 반박을 제외하고는 어떤 이의도 제기될 수 없었다. 렌치와 \"강화 치료법\"에서 의학적 진리를 본 부인은 톨러와 \"약화 요법\"을 의학적 파멸로 여겼다. 풍성한 사혈과 발포(發疱)의 영웅적 시대는 아직 가시지 않았으며, 질병 일반에 어떤 고약한 이름을 붙이고 그에 따라 우물쭈물하지 않고 처치하던 철저한 이론의 시대는 더더욱 가시지 않았기 때문이다—예컨대 질병을 반란이라 부른다면, 공포탄으로 쏘아서는 안 되고 당장 그 피를 뽑아내야 하는 것처럼 말이다. 강화론자들과 약화론자들은 누군가의 의견 속에서는 모두 \"영리한\" 이들이었으니, 사실 이것은 살아 있는 어떤 재능에 대해서도 할 수 있는 말의 전부다. 리드게이트 씨가 두 내과 의사인 스프레이그 박사와 민친 박사만큼 많이 알 수 있으리라고 짐작하는 데까지 상상력을 뻗친 사람은 아무도 없었다. 위험이 극에 달하고 아주 작은 희망이라도 1기니의 값어치가 있을 때, 오직 그 두 사람만이 어떤 희망이라도 줄 수 있었다. 그래도 거듭 말하건대, 리드게이트가 미들마치의 어떤 일반 개업의보다는 다소 더 흔치 않은 인물이라는 일반적인 인상이 있었다. 그리고 그것은 사실이었다. 그는 겨우 스물일곱이었으니, 많은 남자들이 아직 그리 평범하지 않은 나이—성취를 희망하고 회피에 단호하며, 마몬이 결코 자기 입에 재갈을 물리고 등에 올라타지 못하리라, 오히려 마몬과 무슨 관계를 맺는다면 그가 자기 마차를 끌게 하리라 생각하는 나이였다.\n\n그는 퍼블릭 스쿨을 갓 나왔을 때 고아가 되었다. 군인이었던 그의 아버지는 세 자녀에게 거의 아무 대비도 마련해 두지 않았고, 소년 터티어스가 의학 교육을 받게 해 달라고 청했을 때, 그의 후견인들에게는 가문의 품위를 들어 반대하기보다 시골 개업의에게 도제로 보내 그 청을 들어주는 편이 더 쉬워 보였다. 그는 일찍이 뚜렷한 성향을 지니고, 인생에는 아버지가 했기 때문이 아니라 그 자체를 위해 자신이 하고 싶은 어떤 특별한 무언가가 있다고 마음을 정하는, 보기 드문 소년들 가운데 하나였다. 어떤 주제든 사랑으로 향하는 우리 대부분은, 시도해 본 적 없는 책을 꺼내려 높은 걸상에 올라섰던 어느 아침이나 저녁의 한때를, 혹은 입을 벌린 채 새로운 이야기꾼의 말에 귀 기울이며 앉아 있던 때를, 또는 책이 없어서 안에서 들려오는 목소리에 귀 기울이기 시작했던 때를, 우리 사랑의 추적할 수 있는 첫 시작으로 기억한다. 리드게이트에게도 그런 비슷한 일이 일어났다. 그는 영민한 녀석이었고, 놀다가 달아올랐을 때면 한구석에 몸을 던지고는 오 분 만에 손에 잡히는 어떤 책에든 깊이 빠져들곤 했다. 그것이 『라셀라스』나 『걸리버 여행기』라면 더할 나위 없었지만, 베일리의 사전이라도 좋았고, 외경이 들어 있는 성경이라도 괜찮았다. 조랑말을 타거나 뛰어다니며 사냥을 하거나 어른들의 이야기를 듣고 있지 않을 때면, 그는 무언가를 꼭 읽어야 했다. 이 모든 것이 그가 열 살 때 사실이었으니, 그는 그때 이미 『크리설, 혹은 어느 기니화의 모험』을 다 읽었는데, 그것은 갓난아기를 위한 젖도, 젖인 척 통하려는 백악(白堊) 섞인 혼합물도 아니었고, 책이란 쓸데없는 것이며 인생이란 어리석은 것이라는 생각이 그에게는 이미 떠올라 있었다. 학교 공부도 그 생각을 그리 바꾸어 놓지는 못했으니, 그는 고전과 수학을 \"해내기는\" 했어도 거기서 두각을 나타내지는 못했기 때문이다. 사람들은 그를 두고 리드게이트는 마음만 먹으면 무엇이든 할 수 있다고 말했지만, 그가 무언가 비범한 일을 하고 싶어 한 적은 분명 아직 없었다. 그는 이해가 빠른 활기찬 동물이었으나, 아직 어떤 불꽃도 그의 안에 지적 열정을 지피지 못했다. 그에게 지식이란 매우 피상적인 일이라 쉽게 통달할 수 있는 것으로 보였다. 손윗사람들의 대화로 판단하건대, 그는 이미 성숙한 인생에 필요한 것 이상을 분명 갖춘 듯했다. 짧은 허리의 코트와, 아직 다시 유행하지 않은 다른 풍속들의 그 시절에, 값비싼 교육이 빚어낸 이런 결과는 아마 예외적인 것이 아니었으리라. 하지만 어느 방학 때, 비 오는 날이 그를 자그마한 집 안 서재로 보내, 그에게 어떤 신선함이라도 줄 만한 책을 다시 한 번 찾게 했다. 헛수고였다! 정말로 그가 회색 종이 등표지에 칙칙한 라벨이 붙은 먼지투성이 책들의 줄을—한 번도 건드린 적 없던 낡은 백과사전 여러 권을—꺼내지 않는 한 말이다. 적어도 그것들을 건드리는 일은 새로운 시도이긴 할 터였다. 그것들은 맨 위 선반에 있어서, 그는 의자에 올라서서 내렸다. 그러나 그는 선반에서 처음 집어 든 권을 펼쳐 보았다. 어쩐 일인지 사람은 불편할 법한 바로 그 자리에서, 임시변통의 자세로 책을 읽기 마련이다. 그가 펼친 페이지는 해부학 항목 아래 있었고, 처음 그의 눈을 끈 대목은 심장의 판막에 관한 것이었다. 그는 어떤 종류의 판막에도 그리 익숙지 않았지만, 발바이(valvae)가 접이문이라는 것은 알고 있었고, 이 틈새로 갑작스러운 빛이 들어와 인체라는 구조물 속에 정교하게 조정된 기계 장치가 있다는 생생한 첫 관념으로 그를 깜짝 놀라게 했다. 자유로운 교양 교육은 물론 그가 학교 고전 속 음란한 대목들을 마음껏 읽도록 내버려 두었지만, 자기 신체 내부 구조와 결부된 비밀스러움과 외설스러움이라는 막연한 느낌 너머로는, 그의 상상력을 전혀 한쪽으로 치우치게 하지 않았다. 그래서 그가 아는 한 자기 뇌는 관자놀이께의 작은 주머니들 속에 들어 있는 셈이었고, 종이가 금화 대신 쓰인다는 것을 떠올리지 않는 것과 마찬가지로 자기 피가 어떻게 순환하는지 스스로에게 그려 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 소명의 순간이 찾아왔으니, 그가 의자에서 내려오기도 전에, 자신이 지식이라 여겨 왔던 그 말뿐인 무지가 가려 놓았던 광대한 공간들을 채우는 끝없는 과정에 대한 예감으로, 세상은 그에게 새롭게 만들어졌다. 그 시간 이후로 리드게이트는 지적 열정이 자라나는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n우리는 한 남자가 어떻게 한 여자와 사랑에 빠져 그녀와 맺어지거나, 그렇지 않으면 그녀와 운명적으로 갈라서게 되는지를, 거듭거듭 이야기하기를 두려워하지 않는다. 제임스 왕이 여인의 \"맵시와 어여쁨\"이라 불렀던 것을 묘사하는 데 결코 싫증 내지 않고, 옛 음유시인의 현이 울리는 소리를 듣는 데 결코 싫증 내지 않으면서, 부지런한 사색과 자잘한 욕망에 대한 끈기 있는 단념으로 구애해야 하는 저 다른 종류의 \"맵시와 어여쁨\"에는 비교적 무관심한 것은, 시정(詩情)이 지나친 탓인가 아니면 어리석음이 지나친 탓인가? 이 열정의 이야기에서도 그 전개는 다양하다. 어떤 때는 영광스러운 결혼이고, 어떤 때는 좌절과 끝내 갈라섬이다. 그리고 적지 않게 그 파국은 음유시인들이 노래한 저 다른 열정과 얽혀 있다. 자기 넥타이를 매는 방식과 거의 같은 방식으로 정해진 일상의 행로 속에서 자기 직업에 종사하는 수많은 중년 남자들 가운데에는, 한때 자기 행위를 스스로 빚어내고 세상을 조금쯤 바꾸어 보려 했던 이들이 언제나 상당수 있기 마련이다. 그들이 평균에 맞추어 빚어지고 다발로 묶여 포장되기에 알맞게 되어 가는 이야기는, 그들 자신의 의식 속에서조차 좀처럼 이야기되지 않는다. 아마도 너그러운 무보수 노고를 향한 그들의 열정은 다른 젊은 날 사랑의 열정만큼이나 알아채지 못할 만큼 서서히 식어 갔을 것이고, 그러다 어느 날 예전의 자아가 옛집을 거니는 유령처럼 나타나 새 가구를 으스스하게 만들었을 것이다. 세상에 그들의 점진적인 변화 과정보다 더 미묘한 것은 없다! 처음에 그들은 자기도 모르게 그것을 들이마셨다. 당신과 나도, 우리가 순응의 거짓말을 내뱉거나 어리석은 결론을 끌어냈을 때, 그들을 물들이는 데 우리 입김을 얼마쯤 보탰을지 모른다. 혹은 어쩌면 그것은 한 여자의 눈길에서 오는 떨림과 함께 왔을지도 모른다.\n\n리드게이트는 그런 실패자들 가운데 하나가 될 생각이 없었으며, 그에 대한 더 나은 희망이 있었던 것은 그의 과학적 관심이 곧 직업적 열정의 형태를 띠었기 때문이다. 그는 자신의 밥벌이 일에 대한 젊은이다운 믿음을 지녔으니, 도제 시절이라 불리던 그 임시변통의 입문기에도 질식되지 않은 믿음이었다. 그리고 그는 런던과 에든버러와 파리에서의 학업으로, 의술이란 본래의 모습대로라면 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 직업이라는 확신을 가져갔다. 그것은 과학과 기술 사이의 가장 완벽한 교류를 보여 주며, 지적 정복과 사회적 선 사이의 가장 직접적인 동맹을 제공한다는 것이었다. 리드게이트의 천성은 이 결합을 요구했다. 그는 정서가 풍부한 존재였으니, 특수한 연구의 온갖 추상에도 굴하지 않는, 피와 살로 이루어진 동료애의 감각을 지니고 있었다. 그는 \"증례\"뿐 아니라 존과 엘리자베스에게도, 특히 엘리자베스에게 마음을 썼다.\n\n그의 직업에는 또 다른 매력이 있었다. 그것은 개혁을 필요로 했으며, 한 사람에게 그 직업의 매수된 장식들과 다른 허튼소리를 분연히 거부하고, 요구되지 않을지언정 진정한 자격을 갖춘 자가 되겠노라 결의할 기회를 주었다. 그는 다시 고향에 돌아오면 어느 지방 도시에 일반 개업의로 정착하여, 자신의 과학적 탐구를 위해서나 전반적인 진보를 위해서나 내과적 지식과 외과적 지식 사이의 비합리적인 분리에 맞서겠다는 결심을 품고 파리로 공부하러 갔다. 그는 런던의 음모와 질시와 사교적 아첨의 영역에서 멀리 떨어져, 제너가 그랬듯이 아무리 더디더라도 자기 일의 독자적인 가치로 명성을 얻으려 했다. 이 시절이 어두운 시기였음을 기억해야 하기 때문이다. 지식을 희소하게 만들어 그 순수성을 지키고, 진료비와 임명에 관한 엄격한 배타성으로 오류를 배제하려 큰 노력을 기울인 유서 깊은 단과 대학들이 있었음에도, 매우 무지한 젊은 신사들이 도시에서 출세하고, 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 이들이 시골의 넓은 지역에서 개업할 법적 권리를 얻는 일이 벌어졌다. 또한 옥스퍼드와 케임브리지 졸업생들이 얻는 값비싸고 극도로 정제된 의학 교육에 특유의 인가를 부여하며 내과 의사 협회가 대중의 마음에 떠받쳐 놓은 그 높은 기준도, 돌팔이 의술이 호시절을 누리는 것을 막지는 못했다. 직업적 진료란 주로 수많은 약을 처방하는 데 있었으므로, 대중은 값만 싸게 구할 수 있다면 약이 더 많을수록 더 나으리라 추론했고, 그리하여 아무런 학위도 받지 않은 파렴치한 무지가 처방한 약물을 어마어마한 양으로 들이켰던 것이다. 어떤 변화에도 불구하고 반드시 존재할 수밖에 없는 무지하거나 위선적인 의사들의 수에 관한 계산을 통계학이 아직 포함하지 못했음을 생각하면, 리드게이트에게는 단위들을 바꾸는 것이 그 수를 바꾸는 가장 직접적인 방법으로 여겨졌다. 그는 언젠가 평균에 뚜렷이 영향을 미칠 그 확산되는 변화를 향해 일정한 차이를 만들어 낼 하나의 단위가 되고자 했고, 그러는 동안 자기 환자들의 내장에 유익한 차이를 만들어 내는 즐거움을 누리고자 했다. 그러나 그는 단지 흔한 진료보다 더 진정한 종류의 진료를 목표로 한 것만은 아니었다. 그는 더 폭넓은 영향력을 야심껏 바랐으니, 어떤 해부학적 착상의 증명을 이루어 내어 발견의 사슬에 한 고리를 잇게 될지도 모른다는 가능성에 불타올랐다.\n\n미들마치의 외과 의사가 자기 자신을 발견자로 꿈꾼다는 것이 당신에게는 어울리지 않게 보이는가? 우리 대부분은 실로 위대한 창시자들이 별자리들 사이로 들어 올려져 이미 우리 운명을 다스리게 되기 전까지는 그들에 대해 거의 알지 못한다. 그러나 예를 들어 \"하늘의 장벽을 깨뜨린\" 저 허셜은, 한때 지방 교회의 오르간을 연주하고 더듬거리는 피아노 연주자들에게 음악 교습을 하지 않았던가? 그 빛나는 이들은 저마다 이웃들 사이에서 땅 위를 걸어야 했으니, 그 이웃들은 어쩌면 그에게 영원한 명성의 자격을 부여할 무언가보다 그의 걸음걸이와 옷차림을 훨씬 더 마음에 두었을 것이다. 그들은 저마다 자잘한 유혹과 누추한 근심이 뿌려진 작은 지역적·개인적 내력을 지녔으며, 그것이 불멸의 존재들과 끝내 동무가 되는 길로 나아가는 그의 행로를 지체시키는 마찰이 되었다. 리드게이트는 그런 마찰의 위험에 눈멀어 있지 않았으나, 가능한 한 그것을 피하겠다는 자신의 결심에 충분한 자신이 있었다. 스물일곱이었으므로, 그는 스스로 경험이 많다고 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 수도의 화려한 세속적 성공과 접촉하여 자신의 허영심을 자극받지 않고, 자기 직업의 부지런한 실천과 더불어 쌍둥이 목표가 될 위대한 착상의 추구와 결코 경쟁할 수 없는 사람들 사이에서 살아가려 했다. 그 두 목표가 서로를 밝혀 주리라는 희망에는 매혹이 깃들어 있었다. 그의 일상 작업인 세심한 관찰과 추론, 특수한 증례들에서 판단을 돕기 위한 렌즈의 사용은, 더 큰 탐구의 도구로서 그의 사색을 진전시킬 터였다. 이것이야말로 그의 직업이 지닌 전형적인 탁월함이 아니던가? 그는 훌륭한 미들마치 의사가 될 것이고, 바로 그 수단으로 멀리까지 미치는 연구의 궤도에 자신을 머무르게 할 것이었다. 한 가지 점에서 그는 자기 경력의 바로 이 단계에서 정당히 인정을 주장할 만하다. 그는 불순물 섞기를 폭로하는 동안 제 생계를 꾸리려고 유독한 절임 식품으로 이익을 챙기거나, 공중도덕의 대의를 대변할 여유를 얻으려고 도박장 주식을 가지고 있는 저 박애주의적 본보기들을 흉내 낼 생각이 없었다. 그는 자기 경우에 한해서, 확실히 자신의 손이 닿는 범위 안에 있고 해부학적 착상을 입증하는 일보다 훨씬 덜 어려운, 몇 가지 특정한 개혁을 시작하려 했다. 그 개혁 가운데 하나는 최근의 한 법적 판결의 힘을 빌려 단호히 행동하는 것, 곧 약을 조제하거나 약제사에게서 수수료를 받지 않고 단지 처방만 하는 것이었다. 이것은 시골 도시에서 일반 개업의의 방식을 택하기로 한 사람에게는 하나의 혁신이었고, 동업하는 의사 형제들에게는 모욕적인 비판으로 받아들여질 터였다. 그러나 리드게이트는 자기 치료법에서도 혁신을 꾀할 작정이었고, 자신의 신념에 따라 정직하게 진료하는 데 가장 확실한 보장은 그에 어긋나는 체계적인 유혹들을 없애 버리는 것임을 알 만큼 현명했다.\n\n어쩌면 그때가 관찰자와 이론가에게는 지금보다 더 신나는 시절이었으리라. 우리는 아메리카가 처음 발견되기 시작했을 때, 대담한 뱃사람이 설령 난파했더라도 새로운 왕국에 발을 디딜 수 있었을 그때를 세상에서 가장 멋진 시대라고 생각하기 쉽다. 그리고 1829년 무렵, 병리학의 어두운 영역들은 기개 있는 젊은 모험가에게 멋진 아메리카였다. 리드게이트는 무엇보다 자기 직업의 과학적이고 합리적인 토대를 넓히는 데 이바지하려는 야심을 품었다. 열병 혹은 여러 열병들의 본질 같은 질병의 특수한 문제들에 관심을 가지면 가질수록, 그는 19세기 초에 비샤의 짧고도 영광스러운 생애로 환히 밝혀졌던 그 근본적인 구조 지식의 필요성을 더욱 절실히 느꼈다. 비샤는 겨우 서른한 살에 죽었지만, 또 한 명의 알렉산드로스처럼 수많은 후계자에게 충분할 만큼 넓은 영토를 남겼다. 그 위대한 프랑스인은, 살아 있는 신체란 근본적으로 보아 먼저 따로따로 연구한 다음 이를테면 연방처럼 합쳐서 이해할 수 있는 기관들의 집합이 아니라, 어떤 일차적인 망(網) 혹은 조직으로 이루어진 것으로 보아야 하며, 그것들로부터 뇌, 심장, 폐 따위의 여러 기관이—집의 여러 시설이 목재, 철, 돌, 벽돌, 아연 따위가 저마다 다른 비율로 짜여 세워지듯, 그리고 각 재료가 저마다 고유한 구성과 비율을 지니듯—압축되어 만들어진다는 착상을 처음으로 펼쳐 보였다. 보다시피, 어느 누구도 재료의 본질을 알지 못한 채로는 전체 구조나 그 부분들을—그 약한 곳이 무엇이고 그 보수할 곳이 무엇인지를—이해하고 가늠할 수 없다. 그리고 여러 조직에 대한 세밀한 연구로 비샤가 이루어 낸 그 착상은, 흐릿한 기름 등불의 거리에 가스등을 켜는 것이 그러하듯, 새로운 연관들과 그때까지 숨겨져 있던 구조의 사실들—질병의 증상과 약물의 작용을 고찰할 때 반드시 고려해야 할 사실들—을 드러내며, 필연적으로 의학적 문제들에 작용했다. 그러나 인간의 양심과 지성에 달린 결과들은 더디게 작동하는 법이어서, 이제 1829년이 끝나갈 무렵에도 대부분의 의료 행위는 여전히 옛 길을 으스대거나 비틀거리며 걷고 있었고, 비샤의 작업의 직접적인 후속으로 보였을 법한 과학적 작업이 여전히 행해져야 했다. 이 위대한 선견자는 살아 있는 유기체의 궁극적 사실로서 조직들을 고찰하는 것을 넘어서지는 않았으니, 해부학적 분석의 한계를 그어 놓은 셈이었다. 그러나 또 다른 정신이 이렇게 물을 여지가 있었다. 당신의 명주천, 거즈, 망사, 새틴, 벨벳이 모두 날것의 고치에서 비롯되듯, 이 구조들도 모두 거기서 출발한 어떤 공통의 토대를 지니지 않았는가? 여기에 또 다른 빛이, 산수소(酸水素) 불빛 같은 빛이 있어, 사물의 바로 그 결을 보여 주며 이전의 모든 설명을 수정할 터였다. 이미 유럽 정신의 수많은 흐름을 따라 떨려 오던 비샤의 작업에 대한 이 후속을 리드게이트는 사모했다. 그는 살아 있는 구조의 보다 내밀한 관계들을 입증하고, 참된 질서에 따라 인간의 사유를 더 정확하게 규정하는 데 보태고자 갈망했다. 그 작업은 아직 이루어지지 않았고, 다만 그 준비를 어떻게 쓸지 아는 이들을 위해 준비되어 있을 뿐이었다. 원초적 조직이란 무엇인가? 리드게이트는 그런 식으로 질문을 던졌으니—기다리는 답이 요구하는 방식과 꼭 들어맞지는 않았으나, 그렇게 옳은 말을 놓치는 일은 많은 탐구자에게 일어나는 법이다. 그리고 그는 조용한 틈들을 빈틈없이 붙잡아 탐구의 실마리를 다시 잡으리라 기대했고—메스뿐 아니라 현미경의 부지런한 사용에서 얻을 수많은 단서를 기대했으니, 그 현미경은 새로운 신뢰의 열의로 연구에 다시 쓰이기 시작하던 참이었다. 이것이 자기 미래에 대한 리드게이트의 계획이었다. 미들마치를 위해서는 훌륭한 작은 일을 하고, 세상을 위해서는 위대한 일을 하는 것이었다.\n\n이 무렵 그는 분명 행복한 녀석이었다. 정해진 악덕이라곤 없이 스물일곱이라는 나이로, 자기 행동이 이로운 것이어야 한다는 너그러운 결심을 품고, 말 다루는 일의 숭배나 값비싼 격식을 차리는 다른 신비로운 의례들—개업 비용을 치르고 그에게 남은 팔백 파운드로는 분명 그 비용을 대는 데 얼마 가지도 못했을 의례들—과는 전혀 별개로 인생을 흥미롭게 만드는 착상들을 머릿속에 지니고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 많은 남자들의 경력을 내기 좋은 멋진 대상으로 만들 만한 출발점에 서 있었다—만일 그런 오락을 즐기는 신사들 가운데, 온갖 상황의 방해와 도움, 그리고 한 사람이 헤엄쳐 목적지에 닿거나 아니면 곤두박질쳐 떠내려가게 되는 내적 균형의 온갖 미묘함과 더불어, 고된 목적이 지닌 복잡한 개연성을 음미할 줄 아는 이가 있다면 말이다. 그 위험은 리드게이트의 성품을 속속들이 안다 해도 여전히 남을 것이니, 성품 또한 하나의 과정이요 펼쳐짐이기 때문이다. 그 사람은 미들마치의 의사이자 불멸의 발견자만큼이나 아직 만들어지는 중이었고, 움츠러들 수도 부풀어 오를 수도 있는 미덕과 결점이 모두 있었다. 바라건대 그 결점들이 그에 대한 당신의 관심을 거두는 이유가 되지는 않기를. 우리가 아끼는 벗들 가운데, 좀 지나치게 자신만만하고 거만한 이가, 그 뛰어난 정신이 다소 평범함으로 얼룩진 이가, 타고난 편견으로 여기는 조금 옹색하고 저기는 불룩 튀어나온 이가, 혹은 그 더 나은 기력이 일시적인 유혹의 영향 아래 엉뚱한 물길로 흘러내리기 쉬운 이가 한둘쯤 있지 않은가? 이 모든 것이 리드게이트에게 들이댈 만한 것이었지만, 그런데 이것들은 아담을 입에 올리면서도 신도석을 빌린 이들에게 거북한 것은 무엇 하나 언급하고 싶어 하지 않는 점잖은 설교자의 에두른 표현일 뿐이다. 이 섬세한 일반론들이 증류되어 나온 그 특정한 결점들에는 분간할 수 있는 인상과 어법과 말투와 찡그린 표정이 있어, 매우 다양한 연극에서 여러 역을 맡는다. 우리의 허영은 우리의 코가 다르듯 저마다 다르다. 모든 자만이 같은 자만은 아니며, 우리 가운데 한 사람이 다른 사람과 구별되는 그 정신적 구성의 미세한 차이에 따라 달라진다. 리드게이트의 자만은 거만한 종류로, 결코 실실 웃지 않고 결코 무례하지 않으나, 그 주장에 있어 묵직하고 자비롭게 깔보는 것이었다. 그는 멍청이들을 딱하게 여기며 그들을 위해 많은 일을 해 줄 만했으니, 그들이 자신에게는 아무런 힘도 미치지 못하리라 꽤나 확신했기 때문이다. 그는 파리에 있을 때 생시몽주의자들에게 가담할 생각을 했었는데, 그들의 교리 가운데 일부에 그들 자신을 맞서게 하려는 의도에서였다. 그의 모든 결점에는 비슷한 특질이 새겨져 있었으니, 그것은 멋진 바리톤 음성을 지니고, 옷이 몸에 잘 어울리며, 평범한 몸짓에서조차 타고난 기품의 분위기를 풍기는 남자의 결점이었다. 그렇다면 그 평범함의 얼룩은 어디에 있었던가? 그 무심한 우아함에 반한 어느 젊은 아가씨는 이렇게 묻는다. 그토록 가문이 좋고, 사회적 명성을 그토록 야심 차게 바라며, 사회적 의무에 대한 견해가 그토록 너그럽고 남다른 남자에게 어떻게 평범함이라는 것이 있을 수 있겠는가? 천재인 사람이라도 그를 엉뚱한 주제로 불시에 붙잡으면 어리석을 수 있는 것만큼이나 쉽사리, 혹은 사회적 천년왕국을 진전시키려는 더없이 선한 의지를 지닌 많은 이가 그 가벼운 즐거움을 그려 내는 데서는 형편없는 착상에 빠질 수 있는 것만큼이나 쉽사리 그러하다—그들은 오펜바흐의 음악이나 최신 익살극의 현란한 말장난을 넘어서지 못하는 것이다. 리드게이트의 평범함의 얼룩은 그의 편견의 빛깔에 있었으니, 그 편견은 고결한 의도와 공감에도 불구하고 절반쯤은 세상 보통 사람들에게서 발견되는 것들이었다. 그의 지적 열의에 속했던 그 정신의 탁월함은, 가구나 여자에 대한, 혹은 그가 다른 시골 외과 의사들보다 태생이 더 낫다는 것이 (그가 말하지 않아도) 알려지면 좋겠다는 바람에 대한 그의 감정과 판단에는 스며들지 못했다. 그는 당장은 가구에 대해 생각할 마음이 없었지만, 언제든 그가 그렇게 할 때면, 생물학도 개혁의 구상도 그를 자기 가구가 최상의 것이 아니면 어딘가 어울리지 않으리라는 천박한 감정 위로 끌어올려 주지 못하리라는 것이 우려되는 일이었다.\n\n여자들에 관해 말하자면, 그는 이미 한 번 충동적인 어리석음에 휩쓸려 곤두박질친 적이 있었는데, 그것을 마지막으로 삼을 작정이었다. 먼 훗날 어느 때의 결혼이라면 물론 충동적이지 않을 터였으니 말이다. 리드게이트와 친해지고 싶은 이들에게는 그 충동적인 어리석음의 사례가 무엇이었는지 아는 것이 좋을 것이다. 그것은 그가 빠지기 쉬웠던 열정의 변덕스러운 일탈의, 그리고 그를 도덕적으로 사랑스럽게 만드는 데 한몫한 기사도적 다정함의 한 예가 될 수 있기 때문이다. 그 이야기는 많은 말 없이도 할 수 있다. 그것은 그가 파리에서 공부하던 때, 바로 다른 일에 더하여 어떤 갈바니 전기 실험에 몰두해 있던 무렵에 일어났다. 어느 저녁, 실험에 지치고 자신이 필요로 하는 사실을 끌어내지 못한 채, 그는 개구리와 토끼들을 설명되지 않는 충격이라는 그 가혹하고 신비로운 처분 아래 잠시 쉬게 두고, 포르트 생마르탱 극장에서 저녁 시간을 마저 보내러 갔다. 그곳에서는 그가 이미 여러 번 본 멜로드라마가 상연되고 있었는데, 그를 끌어당긴 것은 공동 집필한 작가들의 기발한 작품이 아니라, 작품 속의 악의에 찬 공작으로 착각하여 자기 연인을 칼로 찌르는 역을 맡은 한 여배우였다. 리드게이트는 결코 말을 걸어 볼 일이 없으리라 여기는 여자에게 남자가 사랑에 빠지듯, 이 여배우를 사랑했다. 그녀는 프로방스 여인으로, 검은 눈동자와 그리스풍의 옆얼굴, 둥그스름하고 위엄 있는 몸매를 지녔으며, 젊어서도 달콤한 주부다움을 풍기는 그런 종류의 아름다움을 지녔고, 그 목소리는 부드러운 비둘기 울음 같았다. 그녀는 파리에 온 지 얼마 되지 않았고 정숙하다는 평판을 지녔으며, 그녀의 남편이 불운한 연인 역으로 그녀와 함께 연기했다. \"딱히 나무랄 데 없다고는 할 수 없는\" 것은 그녀의 연기였으나, 관객은 만족했다. 이제 리드게이트의 유일한 휴식은 이 여자를 보러 가는 것이었으니, 마치 잠시 제비꽃 핀 둑에서 달콤한 남풍의 숨결 아래 몸을 던지면서도, 곧 돌아갈 자신의 갈바니 전기에는 아무런 손해를 끼치지 않는 것과 같았다. 그러나 이날 저녁, 그 낡은 극에는 새로운 파국이 있었다. 여주인공이 자기 연인을 칼로 찌르는 연기를 하고 그가 우아하게 쓰러지기로 한 순간, 아내가 정말로 남편을 칼로 찔렀고, 그는 죽음이 명한 대로 쓰러졌다. 거친 비명이 극장을 꿰뚫었고, 그 프로방스 여인은 졸도하여 쓰러졌다. 비명과 졸도는 극이 요구하는 것이었지만, 이번에는 졸도 또한 진짜였다. 리드게이트는 어찌했는지도 모르게 펄쩍 뛰어 무대 위로 기어올라가 분주히 거들었고, 그녀의 머리에서 타박상을 발견하고 두 팔로 그녀를 살며시 들어 올리며 자신의 여주인공과 알게 되었다. 파리는 이 죽음의 이야기로 떠들썩했다. 살인이었던가? 그 여배우의 가장 열렬한 숭배자들 가운데 일부는 그녀의 유죄를 믿고 싶어 했고, 그 때문에 그녀를 더욱 좋아했다(그 시절의 취향이 그러했다). 그러나 리드게이트는 그런 이들 가운데 하나가 아니었다. 그는 그녀의 무죄를 격렬히 변호했고, 전에 그가 느꼈던 그녀의 아름다움에 대한 멀고 비개인적인 열정은 이제 개인적인 헌신으로, 그녀의 처지에 대한 애틋한 생각으로 옮아갔다. 살인이라는 발상은 터무니없었다. 어떤 동기도 찾아낼 수 없었으니, 그 젊은 부부는 서로 끔찍이 아끼는 것으로 알려져 있었고, 우연히 발을 헛디딘 것이 이런 중대한 결과를 불러온 일은 전례가 없지도 않았다. 법적 조사는 로르 부인의 석방으로 끝났다. 리드게이트는 이 무렵 이미 그녀와 여러 차례 만나 이야기를 나누었고, 그녀가 점점 더 흠모할 만하다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그녀는 말이 적었지만, 그것이 오히려 또 하나의 매력이었다. 그녀는 우울해 보였고 고마워하는 것 같았으며, 그녀의 존재만으로 충분했으니, 마치 저녁 빛처럼 그러했다. 리드게이트는 그녀의 애정에 대해 미칠 듯이 노심초사했고, 자기 아닌 다른 어떤 남자가 그것을 얻어 그녀에게 청혼할까 봐 질투했다. 그러나 그녀는 그 치명적인 사건 덕분에 더욱 인기를 끌었을 포르트 생마르탱에서의 계약을 다시 트는 대신, 예고도 없이 파리를 떠나 자신의 작은 숭배자 무리를 저버렸다. 아마도 리드게이트 말고는 아무도 멀리까지 수소문하지 않았을 것이니, 그는 끊임없이 떠도는 슬픔에 짓눌린 불행한 로르가 스스로도 떠돌며 충실한 위로자를 찾지 못하는 모습을 상상하는 동안, 모든 과학이 멈춰 서 버린 듯 느꼈다. 그러나 자취를 감춘 여배우란 다른 숨은 사실들만큼 찾기 어렵지는 않은 법이어서, 리드게이트가 로르가 리옹으로 가는 길을 택했다는 단서들을 모으기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그는 마침내 같은 이름으로 아비뇽에서 큰 성공을 거두며 연기하고 있는 그녀를 찾아냈으니, 그녀는 아이를 품에 안은 버림받은 아내로서 어느 때보다 위엄 있어 보였다. 그는 공연이 끝난 뒤 그녀에게 말을 걸었고, 그에게는 맑은 물의 깊이처럼 아름답게 느껴지는 그 여느 때의 고요함으로 맞아들여졌으며, 이튿날 그녀를 방문해도 좋다는 허락을 얻었다. 그는 그녀를 흠모한다고 말하고 청혼할 작정이었다. 그는 이것이 미친 사람의 갑작스러운 충동과도 같다는 것을—그의 평소의 약점들과도 어울리지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었다. 상관없었다! 그것은 그가 하기로 결심한 단 하나의 일이었다. 그의 안에는 두 자아가 있는 것이 분명했고, 그것들은 서로 맞춰 가며 서로의 방해를 견디는 법을 배워야 했다. 기이하게도, 우리 가운데 어떤 이들은 번갈아 빠르게 바라보는 시야로 자신의 미혹 너머를 보며, 우리가 높은 곳에서 열에 들떠 떠들어 대는 동안에도, 우리의 끈질긴 자아가 멈춰 서서 우리를 기다리는 그 너른 평원을 바라본다.\n\n로르에게 경건하리만치 다정하지 않은 어떤 구애를 가지고 다가갔다면, 그것은 그녀를 향한 그의 온 감정에 대한 명백한 모순이었을 것이다.\n\n\"저를 찾으러 파리에서 그 먼 길을 오셨군요?\" 이튿날 그녀는 팔짱을 끼고 그의 앞에 앉아, 길들지 않은 채 되새김질하는 짐승이 의아해하듯 의아해하는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"영국 남자들은 다 그런가요?\"\n\n\"당신을 만나 보려 애쓰지 않고는 살 수가 없어서 왔습니다. 당신은 외롭고, 나는 당신을 사랑합니다. 당신이 내 아내가 되기로 승낙해 주기를 바랍니다. 나는 기다리겠습니다. 다만 당신이 나와 결혼하겠다고—다른 누구도 아닌 나와—약속해 주기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n로르는 그 큰 눈꺼풀 아래로 우울한 광채를 띤 채 말없이 그를 바라보았고, 마침내 그가 황홀한 확신에 가득 차 그녀의 무릎 가까이 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n\"당신에게 한 가지 말씀드릴 게 있어요.\" 그녀는 팔짱을 낀 채 그 비둘기 울음 같은 어조로 말했다. \"제 발이 정말로 미끄러진 거였어요.\"\n\n\"압니다, 알아요.\" 리드게이트가 만류하듯 말했다. \"그건 치명적인 사고였어요—나를 당신에게 더욱 묶어 놓은 끔찍한 재앙의 일격이었지요.\"\n\n로르는 다시 잠시 멈추었다가 천천히 말했다. \"나는 그걸 할 작정이었어요.\"\n\n강인한 사내였던 리드게이트는 창백해지며 몸을 떨었다. 그가 일어나 그녀에게서 떨어져 설 때까지 여러 순간이 지나가는 것 같았다.\n\n\"그렇다면 비밀이 있었군요.\" 마침내 그가, 사뭇 격렬하게 말했다. \"그가 당신에게 잔인하게 굴었군요. 당신은 그를 미워했고요.\"\n\n\"아니에요! 그가 나를 지치게 했어요. 그는 나를 너무 좋아했지요. 그는 내 고향이 아니라 파리에서 살려고 했어요. 그게 나는 못마땅했어요.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 리드게이트가 공포에 찬 신음으로 말했다. \"그래서 당신은 그를 죽이려고 계획했단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"계획한 게 아니에요. 연극 도중에 떠올랐던 거예요—나는 그걸 할 작정이었어요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 말없이 서서, 그녀를 바라보며 자기도 모르게 모자를 꾹 눌러썼다. 그는 이 여자를—자신이 젊은 흠모를 바친 첫 번째 여자를—어리석은 범죄자들의 무리 속에서 보았다.\n\n\"당신은 착한 청년이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 나는 남편이라는 게 싫어요. 두 번 다시 남편을 두지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n사흘 뒤 리드게이트는 파리의 자기 방에서 다시 갈바니 전기 실험에 매달렸으니, 자신에게 환상이란 이제 끝났다고 믿었다. 그는 풍성한 마음의 친절함과, 인간의 삶이 더 나아질 수 있으리라는 믿음 덕분에 냉혹해지는 영향에서 구원받았다. 그러나 그는 이제 자신의 판단이 그토록 경험을 쌓았으니 그 어느 때보다 그것을 신뢰할 이유가 있었고, 이후로 여자에 대해서는 엄격히 과학적인 관점을 취하여, 미리 정당화된 것 외에는 어떤 기대도 품지 않으리라 다짐했다.\n\n미들마치의 누구도 여기서 어렴풋이 그림자처럼 그려진 것과 같은 리드게이트의 과거에 대한 관념을 지닐 법하지 않았고, 실로 그곳의 점잖은 읍민들은 자기네 감각에 들어오지 않는 것을 스스로에게 정확히 그려 보려는 열성적인 시도에서 보통 사람들보다 더할 것이 없었다. 그 읍의 젊은 처녀들뿐 아니라 잿빛 수염의 남자들조차도, 한 인생이 그 도구적 쓸모를 위해 그를 어떻게 빚어 왔는가에 관해서는 매우 막연한 지식에 만족한 채, 새로 알게 된 사람이 어떻게 자기네 목적에 맞춰질 수 있을지를 추측하느라 흔히 서두르곤 했다. 사실 미들마치는 리드게이트를 삼켜 매우 편안하게 자기 것으로 동화시키리라 셈하고 있었다.\n\n# 제14장\n\n오직 아리따움과 다정함뿐이니.\nㅡ찰스 세들리 경\n\n타이크 씨를 병원의 봉급받는 원목으로 임명할 것인가 하는 문제는 미들마치 사람들에게 흥미진진한 화젯거리였다. 리드게이트는 그 논의가 오가는 방식을 들으면서 불스트로드 씨가 이 도시에서 행사하는 권력의 실체를 적잖이 깨달았다. 그 은행가는 분명 지배자였으나, 반대파가 있었고, 심지어 그의 지지자들 가운데서도 자신들의 지지가 하나의 타협임을 내비치는 이들이 있었으니, 이들은 세상사의 일반적인 형편, 특히 장사에서 닥치는 뜻밖의 사고들 때문에 악마에게도 촛불을 들어 줘야 한다는 인상을 솔직히 털어놓았다.\n\n불스트로드 씨의 권력은 단지 그가 시골 은행가로서 도시의 거의 모든 상인들의 재정적 비밀을 알고 그들의 신용의 발판을 건드릴 수 있다는 데서만 비롯된 것이 아니었다. 그것은 즉각적이면서도 준엄한 자선, 곧 기꺼이 은혜를 베풀되 그 결과를 준엄하게 감시하는 자선으로 한층 강화되었다. 그는 늘 제자리를 지키는 부지런한 사람으로서 도시의 자선 사업을 운영하는 데 주된 몫을 차지했으며, 사적인 자선도 세심하고 풍성했다. 그는 구두장이 테그의 아들을 도제로 들여보내는 데 큰 수고를 마다하지 않았고, 테그가 교회에 나가는지도 지켜보았다. 그는 세탁부 스트라이프 부인을 빨래 너는 땅을 두고 부당하게 돈을 뜯어내려는 스텁스로부터 보호해 주었고, 스트라이프 부인에 관한 중상에 대해서는 몸소 자세히 따져 보았다. 그의 사적인 소액 대출은 수없이 많았지만, 그는 대출 전후의 사정을 엄격하게 캐물었다. 이런 식으로 한 사람은 이웃들의 감사뿐 아니라 희망과 두려움 속에서도 자신의 영역을 넓혀 가는 법이다. 그리고 권력이란 일단 그런 미묘한 영역에 발을 들이면 스스로 번식하여, 외적인 수단에 전혀 걸맞지 않을 만큼 멀리 퍼져 나간다. 가능한 한 많은 권력을 얻어 그것을 하느님의 영광을 위해 쓰겠다는 것이 불스트로드 씨의 원칙이었다. 그는 자신의 동기를 조율하고 하느님의 영광이 무엇을 요구하는지 스스로 분명히 하기 위해 숱한 영적 갈등과 내면의 논쟁을 거쳤다. 그러나 우리가 보았듯이 그의 동기는 늘 올바르게 평가받지는 못했다. 미들마치에는 사물을 뭉뚱그려서만 저울질할 수 있는 무딘 사람들이 많았다. 그들은 불스트로드 씨가 자기들처럼 인생을 즐기지 못하고 그토록 적게 먹고 마시며 모든 일에 안달복달하니, 분명 지배감이라는 의미에서 일종의 흡혈귀 같은 향연을 누리고 있으리라고 강하게 의심했다.\n\n원목 임명 문제는 리드게이트가 빈시 씨 댁에서 저녁을 먹을 때 식탁에 올랐는데, 불스트로드 씨와의 인척 관계가 주인장 자신조차 어느 정도 자유로운 발언을 하는 데 걸림돌이 되지 않는다는 것을 그는 알아차렸다. 다만 빈시 씨가 그 제안에 반대하는 이유는 전적으로 온통 교리뿐인 타이크 씨의 설교를 못마땅하게 여기는 데, 그리고 그런 흠이 없는 페어브러더 씨를 선호하는 데 있었다. 빈시 씨는 원목에게 봉급을 준다는 발상 자체는 흡족해했으나, 그것이 페어브러더에게 돌아간다는 전제하에서였다. 페어브러더로 말하자면 둘도 없이 착한 친구이자 어디 내놔도 가장 뛰어난 설교자였고, 더구나 어울리기에도 좋은 사람이었다.\n\n\"그럼 자네는 어느 편에 서겠나?\" 빈시 씨와 사냥을 함께 즐기는 절친한 동무이자 검시관인 치철리 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 지금 내가 이사진의 한 사람이 아니라서 천만다행이지. 나는 그 문제를 이사진과 의무 위원회에 함께 회부하자는 데 표를 던질 걸세. 내 책임의 얼마쯤은 자네 어깨에 떠넘기겠네, 박사 양반.\" 빈시 씨는 먼저 도시의 선임 내과 의사인 스프레이그 박사를, 이어 맞은편에 앉은 리드게이트를 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"의사 양반들이 어떤 종류의 검은 물약을 처방할지 의논해 보셔야겠소, 안 그렇소, 리드게이트 씨?\"\n\n\"저는 그 어느 쪽에 대해서도 아는 게 별로 없습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"하지만 일반적으로 임명이라는 게 너무 개인적인 호감의 문제가 되기 십상이지요. 특정한 자리에 가장 적합한 사람이 늘 가장 착한 친구거나 가장 사람 좋은 인물은 아니니까요. 때로는 개혁을 이루고 싶으면, 누구나 좋아하는 그 착한 친구들에게 연금을 주어 물러나게 하고 아예 고려 대상에서 빼는 것이 유일한 길일 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n스프레이그 박사는, 비록 민친 박사가 흔히 더 \"통찰력\"이 있다고 일컬어지긴 했어도 가장 \"비중\" 있는 의사로 여겨졌는데, 리드게이트가 말하는 동안 그 크고 묵직한 얼굴에서 일체의 표정을 거두고 자기 포도주 잔만 바라보았다. 이 젊은이에 관해 문젯거리도 의심스러운 것도 아닌 점들ㅡ이를테면 외국 사상에 대한 어떤 과시벽, 그리고 손위 사람들이 이미 정해 두고 잊어버린 것을 다시 들쑤시려는 성향ㅡ은 삼십 년 전에 뇌막염에 관한 논문으로 자기 입지를 굳힌 의사에게는 단연코 달갑지 않은 것이었다. 그 논문은 적어도 \"본인 소장\"이라고 표시된 한 부가 송아지 가죽으로 장정되어 있었다. 나로서는 스프레이그 박사에게 어느 정도 동병상련을 느낀다. 자기만족이란 세금도 물지 않는 일종의 재산인데, 그것이 폄하당하는 것을 보는 일은 여간 불쾌하지 않으니까.\n\n그러나 리드게이트의 발언은 좌중의 정서와 들어맞지 않았다. 빈시 씨는 자기 뜻대로 할 수 있다면 못마땅한 친구들은 어디에도 앉히지 않을 거라고 말했다.\n\n\"개혁 따위는 집어치우게!\" 치철리 씨가 말했다. \"세상에 그보다 더한 사기는 없어. 개혁이라는 말이 나오면 그건 으레 새 사람을 들이려는 무슨 술책이라는 뜻이지. 자네가 그 『랜싯』 패거리의 한 사람은 아니길 바라네, 리드게이트 씨ㅡ검시관 자리를 법조계의 손에서 빼앗으려는 자들 말일세. 자네 말이 그쪽을 가리키는 것 같으니 하는 소릴세.\"\n\n\"나는 웨이클리를 인정하지 않소.\" 스프레이그 박사가 끼어들었다. \"그 누구보다도 말이오. 그자는 심보가 고약한 작자라, 누구나 알다시피 런던의 의학원들에 달려 있는 우리 직업의 품위를, 그저 제 한 몸 악명이나 떨치려고 희생시킬 인간이오. 입에만 오를 수 있다면 흠씬 두들겨 맞아 멍이 들어도 개의치 않는 자들이 있지. 하지만 웨이클리가 옳을 때도 있긴 하오.\" 박사는 판관처럼 신중하게 덧붙였다. \"웨이클리가 옳은 점을 한두 가지 들 수도 있소.\"\n\n\"아, 뭐,\" 치철리 씨가 말했다. \"자기 직업 편을 들고 나서는 거야 누구도 탓할 수 없지. 하지만 따지고 들자면, 법률 교육을 받지 못한 검시관이 대체 어떻게 증거를 판단한단 말인가, 그게 알고 싶군.\"\n\n\"제 생각으로는,\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"법률 교육은 다른 종류의 지식을 요하는 문제에서 오히려 사람을 더 무능하게 만들 뿐입니다. 사람들은 마치 눈먼 정의의 여신이 저울로 정말 달아 볼 수 있기라도 한 듯 증거를 입에 올리지요. 어떤 주제든 그것을 잘 알지 못하는 한 무엇이 좋은 증거인지 누구도 판단할 수 없습니다. 부검에 있어서는 변호사라도 노파보다 나을 게 없어요. 변호사가 독약의 작용을 어찌 알겠습니까? 그건 운율을 따질 줄 알면 감자 농사도 따질 수 있다고 말하는 거나 마찬가지지요.\"\n\n\"검시관의 일이란 부검을 직접 하는 게 아니라 그저 의학 증인의 증언을 받는 것뿐이라는 점은, 자네도 알 텐데?\" 치철리 씨가 다소 비웃듯이 말했다.\n\n\"그 의학 증인이라는 게 검시관 자신만큼이나 무지한 경우가 허다하지요.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"법의학 문제를 의학 증인이 어쩌다 가진 알량한 지식에 맡겨서는 안 됩니다. 그리고 무지한 개업의가 어쩌다 일러 준 말이라고 해서 스트리크닌이 위벽을 파괴한다고 곧이곧대로 믿을 사람을 검시관으로 앉혀서도 안 되고요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 치철리 씨가 다름 아닌 국왕 폐하의 검시관이라는 사실을 정작 까맣게 잊고서, 천연덕스럽게 이런 질문으로 말을 맺었다. \"박사님도 제 의견에 동의하시지요, 스프레이그 박사님?\"\n\n\"어느 정도까지는요ㅡ인구가 많은 지역이나 수도의 경우에는 말입니다.\" 박사가 말했다. \"하지만 우리 직업에서 가장 뛰어난 사람을 후임으로 들인다 할지라도, 이 고장이 내 친구 치철리의 봉사를 잃게 되는 일은 한참 뒤의 일이기를 바라오. 빈시도 분명 내 말에 동의할 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그렇고말고, 나는 사냥 잘하는 검시관이 좋소.\" 빈시 씨가 유쾌하게 말했다. \"내 생각에는 변호사를 두는 게 제일 안전하지. 아무도 모든 걸 다 알 수는 없는 법이오. 대개의 일들은 '하느님의 부르심'이니까. 그리고 독살로 말하자면, 결국 알아야 할 건 법이지. 자, 이제 부인들에게로 갈까요?\"\n\n리드게이트는 속으로 치철리 씨야말로 위벽에 관해 아무런 편견도 없는 바로 그런 검시관일지 모른다고 생각했으나, 그를 빗대어 한 말은 아니었다. 이것이 미들마치의 점잖은 사교계에서 처신하는 데 따르는 어려움 가운데 하나였다. 봉급받는 어떤 자리든 그 자격으로 지식을 고집하는 것은 위험한 일이었다. 프레드 빈시는 리드게이트를 잘난 척하는 샌님이라 불렀고, 이제 치철리 씨는 그를 건방진 풋내기라 부르고 싶어 했다. 특히 응접실에서 그가 로저먼드의 비위를 더없이 잘 맞추는 것처럼 보일 때 그러했다. 빈시 부인이 직접 차를 따르는 탁자에 앉아 있었던 터라, 그는 단둘이 마주 앉아 어렵잖게 로저먼드를 독차지하고 있었다. 빈시 부인은 어떤 살림의 소임도 딸에게 넘기지 않았다. 곱고 환한 목덜미에서 휘날리는 두 가닥 분홍빛 끈, 남편과 아이들을 대하는 쾌활한 태도, 그리고 그 활짝 핀 듯 인심 좋아 보이는 안주인의 얼굴은 분명 빈시가의 큰 매력 가운데 하나였으니ㅡ이런 매력은 그 집 딸과 사랑에 빠지기를 한결 쉽게 만들었다. 빈시 부인에게서 풍기는, 잘난 체하지 않고 거슬리지도 않는 약간의 천박함은 로저먼드의 세련됨을 더욱 돋보이게 했으며, 그 세련됨은 리드게이트가 기대했던 것 이상이었다.\n\n분명 작은 발과 완벽하게 빠진 어깨선은 세련된 몸가짐이라는 인상을 거들기 마련이고, 입술과 눈꺼풀의 더없이 아름다운 곡선이 함께할 때면 적절한 말 한마디가 놀랍도록 적절하게 들리는 법이다. 그런데 로저먼드는 적절한 말을 할 줄 알았다. 익살스러운 가락만 빼고 온갖 가락을 다 포착하는 그런 종류의 영민함을 지녔기 때문이다. 다행히도 그녀는 농담을 시도한 적이 한 번도 없었으니, 어쩌면 이것이야말로 그녀의 영민함을 가장 결정적으로 드러내는 표시였는지도 모른다.\n\n그녀와 리드게이트는 어렵잖게 대화에 빠져들었다. 그는 며칠 전 스톤 코트에서 그녀의 노래를 듣지 못한 것이 아쉽다고 했다. 그가 파리 체류 후반에 스스로에게 허락한 유일한 즐거움이 음악을 들으러 가는 것이었다.\n\n\"음악을 공부하셨나 보군요?\" 로저먼드가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 저는 새들의 지저귐을 많이 알고, 귀로 익힌 가락도 많지요. 하지만 제가 전혀 알지 못하고 아무 짐작도 없는 음악이 오히려 저를 즐겁게 합니다ㅡ저를 감동시키지요. 손 닿는 곳에 그런 즐거움이 있는데도 더 많이 누리지 못하다니, 세상이란 얼마나 어리석은지요!\"\n\n\"네, 그리고 미들마치가 무척 음악과 거리가 멀다는 걸 알게 되실 거예요. 변변한 음악가가 거의 없거든요. 제법 노래를 잘하는 신사라고는 두 분밖에 몰라요.\"\n\n\"아마 우스꽝스러운 노래를 마치 북을 두드리듯 박자만 맞춰 부르면서, 가락은 듣는 사람이 알아서 상상하라고 내버려 두는 것이 유행인가 보지요?\"\n\n\"아, 바우어 씨의 노래를 들으셨군요.\" 로저먼드가 좀체 보기 드문 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"그런데 우리가 이웃들 흉을 너무 보고 있네요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 이 가녀린 존재가 얼마나 사랑스러운지 헤아리느라 대화를 이어 가야 한다는 것마저 거의 잊을 지경이었다. 그녀의 옷은 더없이 옅은 푸른 하늘로 지은 듯했고, 그녀 자신은 어떤 거대한 꽃잎이 막 벌어지며 그 속에서 드러난 듯 티 없이 금빛으로 빛났다. 그러면서도 그 어린아이 같은 금빛에는 그토록 자연스럽고 침착한 우아함이 어려 있었다. 로르의 기억을 품게 된 뒤로 리드게이트는 눈만 큰 침묵에 대한 모든 취향을 잃어버렸다. 그 성스러운 암소는 더 이상 그를 끌어당기지 못했고, 로저먼드는 그와 정반대였다. 하지만 그는 정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 음악을 좀 들려주시겠지요.\"\n\n\"원하신다면 제 서툰 솜씨를 들려 드리지요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"아버지가 분명 제게 노래를 시키실 테니까요. 하지만 파리에서 최고의 가수들을 들으신 당신 앞에서는 떨릴 거예요. 저는 들은 게 거의 없거든요. 런던에도 딱 한 번 가 봤을 뿐이고요. 그래도 성 베드로 성당의 오르간 연주자가 훌륭한 음악가여서, 저는 그분께 계속 배우고 있어요.\"\n\n\"런던에서 무엇을 보셨는지 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"별것 없었어요.\" (좀 더 순진한 처녀였다면 \"아, 모든 걸요!\"라고 했으리라. 하지만 로저먼드는 그보다 영리했다.) \"촌스러운 시골 처녀들이 으레 구경하는 평범한 명소 몇 군데뿐이었지요.\"\n\n\"스스로를 촌스러운 시골 처녀라고 부르시는 겁니까?\" 리드게이트는 저도 모르게 감탄을 실어 그녀를 바라보며 말했고, 그 바람에 로저먼드는 기쁨에 얼굴을 붉혔다. 하지만 그녀는 그저 진지한 표정을 유지한 채 긴 목을 살짝 돌리고는 손을 들어 그 경탄스러운 땋은 머리채를 매만졌다ㅡ그녀의 버릇 같은 몸짓으로, 새끼 고양이의 앞발 놀림 못지않게 어여뻤다. 그렇다고 로저먼드가 조금이라도 새끼 고양이를 닮았다는 말은 아니다. 그녀는 어린 나이에 붙잡혀 레먼 부인의 학교에서 교육받은 한 마리 요정이었다.\n\n\"제 머릿속이 정말로 설익었다는 걸 장담해요.\" 그녀가 곧바로 말했다. \"미들마치에서야 그럭저럭 통하지요. 우리 오랜 이웃들과 이야기하는 건 두렵지 않아요. 하지만 당신은 정말로 두려워요.\"\n\n\"교양 있는 여인은 거의 언제나 우리 남자들보다 더 많이 압니다. 비록 그 지식의 종류가 다르긴 하지만요. 당신이라면 제게 수천 가지를 가르쳐 주실 수 있을 겁니다ㅡ아리따운 새가 곰에게 가르쳐 줄 수 있듯이요, 둘 사이에 공통의 언어만 있다면 말이지요. 다행히 여자와 남자 사이에는 공통의 언어가 있으니, 곰도 배울 수 있는 셈이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 저기 프레드가 또 건반을 두드리기 시작하네요! 가서 당신의 신경을 온통 거스르지 못하게 말려야겠어요.\" 로저먼드는 이렇게 말하며 방 저편으로 옮겨 갔다. 그곳에서는 프레드가, 로저먼드가 음악을 들려주도록 아버지가 청한 대로 피아노 뚜껑을 열어 놓고는, 곁다리 삼아 한 손으로 「체리 라이프」를 치고 있었다. 시험을 통과한 유능한 사람들도 이따금 이런 짓을 하니, 낙방한 프레드만 그러는 것은 아니다.\n\n\"프레드, 연습은 내일로 미뤄 줘. 리드게이트 씨를 병나게 만들겠어.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"이분은 귀가 예민하시거든.\"\n\n프레드는 웃으며 곡을 끝까지 마저 쳤다.\n\n로저먼드는 리드게이트를 돌아보며 부드럽게 미소 짓고는 말했다. \"보시다시피 곰이라고 늘 배워지는 건 아니랍니다.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 로지!\" 프레드가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 누나를 위해 그것을 위로 돌려 높이며, 한껏 즐길 기대에 부풀어 말했다. \"먼저 신나는 곡 몇 개부터.\"\n\n로저먼드의 연주는 훌륭했다. (교회와 성에 그 유물이 남아 있는, 기억할 만한 역사를 지닌 어느 주도 가까이에 있던) 레먼 부인 학교에서 그녀를 가르친 선생은, 우리 지방에서 여기저기 더러 만날 수 있는 그런 빼어난 음악가 가운데 하나로, 음악으로 이름을 떨칠 조건이 한결 풍부한 어느 나라의 이름난 악장 못지않게 견줄 만한 인물이었다. 연주자의 본능을 지닌 로저먼드는 그의 연주법을 그대로 붙잡아, 고결한 음악에 대한 그의 폭넓은 해석을 메아리처럼 정확하게 풀어냈다. 처음 들으면 거의 깜짝 놀랄 만했다. 로저먼드의 손가락에서 숨은 영혼이 흘러나오는 듯했다. 그리고 사실 그러했으니, 영혼이란 끊임없는 메아리 속에 살아남는 법이고, 모든 빼어난 표현에는 그것이 비록 해석자의 활동에 지나지 않을지라도 어딘가에 그것을 낳는 활동이 깃들어 있기 때문이다. 리드게이트는 사로잡혔고, 그녀를 무언가 예외적인 존재로 믿기 시작했다. 결국, 하고 그는 생각했다, 겉보기에 불리해 보이는 환경 아래에서 자연의 진귀한 조합을 발견한다 해도 놀랄 일은 아니다. 그것들은 어디에서 나타나든 늘 겉으로 드러나지 않는 조건에 달려 있는 법이니까. 그는 앉아서 그녀를 바라보았고, 일어나 찬사를 건네지도 않은 채 그것을 다른 이들에게 맡겨 두었으니, 이제 그의 감탄이 한층 깊어진 까닭이었다.\n\n그녀의 노래는 그만큼 빼어나지는 않았으나 역시 잘 다듬어져 있었고, 완벽하게 가락이 맞는 종소리처럼 듣기에 감미로웠다. 사실 그녀는 「달빛 속에서 나를 만나요」와 「나는 떠돌아다녔네」를 불렀다. 인간이란 자기 시대의 유행을 함께 나누기 마련이고, 오로지 옛사람만이 늘 고전적일 수 있기 때문이다. 하지만 로저먼드는 「검은 눈의 수전」도 멋지게 부를 수 있었고, 하이든의 칸초네타도, 「당신은 아시지요」도, 「때려요, 때려요」도 부를 수 있었으니ㅡ그저 청중이 무엇을 좋아하는지 알고 싶을 따름이었다.\n\n그녀의 아버지는 좌중을 둘러보며 그들의 감탄에 흐뭇해했다. 그녀의 어머니는 시련을 겪기 전의 니오베처럼 막내딸을 무릎에 앉히고서, 음악 박자에 맞춰 아이의 손을 부드럽게 위아래로 토닥이고 있었다. 그리고 프레드는, 로지에 대한 평소의 회의에도 불구하고 더없이 충성스럽게 그녀의 음악에 귀 기울이며, 자기도 플루트로 같은 것을 할 수 있다면 싶어 했다. 리드게이트가 미들마치에 온 이래로 본 가장 즐거운 가족 모임이었다. 빈시가 사람들에게는 즐길 줄 아는 천성, 일체의 근심을 떨쳐 내는 태도, 그리고 인생을 즐거운 운명으로 믿는 믿음이 있었으니, 이것이 그 무렵 대부분의 주도에서 그 집을 별난 집으로 만들었다. 그 시절은 복음주의가, 지방에 살아남은 몇 안 되는 오락거리에 마치 역병이 옮은 듯한 어떤 의혹의 그림자를 드리운 때였다. 빈시가에서는 늘 휘스트 놀이가 있었고, 지금도 카드 탁자가 준비되어 있어서 좌중의 몇몇은 속으로 음악이 어서 끝나기를 안달했다. 음악이 끝나기 전에 페어브러더 씨가 들어왔으니ㅡ잘생기고 가슴이 떡 벌어졌으되 그밖에는 자그마한, 마흔쯤 된 사내로, 검은 옷은 몹시 해져 있었다. 그 광채라는 광채는 죄다 그 재빠른 잿빛 눈에 깃들어 있었다. 그는 빛 속의 유쾌한 변화처럼 들어와, 모건 양에게 이끌려 방을 나서던 어린 루이자를 아버지 같은 너스레로 붙잡고, 모두에게 저마다 특별한 말을 건네며 인사했으니, 마치 그날 저녁 내내 오간 것보다 더 많은 이야기를 십 분 안에 압축해 넣는 듯했다. 그는 리드게이트에게 자신을 만나러 오겠다던 약속을 지키라고 졸랐다. \"놓아줄 수 없습니다, 알다시피, 보여 드릴 딱정벌레가 좀 있거든요. 우리 수집가들은 자기가 보여 줄 것을 다 보여 줄 때까지는 새로 온 사람마다 흥미를 느낀답니다.\"\n\n그러나 그는 이내 휘스트 탁자로 발길을 돌려, 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"자, 이제 진지해집시다! 리드게이트 씨? 안 하신다고요? 아! 당신은 이런 일을 하기엔 너무 젊고 가볍군요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 속으로, 불스트로드 씨에게 그토록 거슬리는 재능을 지닌 이 성직자가 학식이라고는 분명 없는 이 가정에서 마음에 드는 안식처를 찾은 모양이라고 생각했다. 그도 어느 정도는 이해할 수 있었다. 노소를 막론한 사람들의 좋은 기분과 빼어난 용모, 그리고 지성을 수고롭게 쓰지 않고도 시간을 보낼 수 있다는 점은, 남는 시간을 특별히 쓸 데 없는 이들에게 이 집을 매혹적인 곳으로 만들었으리라.\n\n모건 양만 빼고는 모든 것이 활짝 피어나고 즐거워 보였다. 모건 양은 거무튀튀하고 따분하고 체념한 듯한 사람으로, 빈시 부인이 곧잘 말하듯 한마디로 가정교사에 딱 어울리는 부류였다. 리드게이트로서는 이런 방문을 자주 할 생각이 없었다. 저녁 시간을 형편없이 낭비하는 일이었다. 그래서 그는 이제 로저먼드와 조금만 더 이야기를 나눈 다음, 양해를 구하고 자리를 뜰 작정이었다.\n\n\"미들마치에 사는 우리가 마음에 안 드시리라는 걸, 저는 분명히 느껴요.\" 휘스트 놀이꾼들이 자리를 잡고 앉자 그녀가 말했다. \"우리는 무척 따분한데, 당신은 아주 다른 것에 익숙하셨을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"주도라는 게 다 거기서 거기겠지요.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"하지만 사람은 누구나 자기가 사는 도시가 다른 어떤 곳보다 더 따분하다고 믿는다는 걸 저는 알아차렸습니다. 저는 미들마치를 있는 그대로 받아들이기로 마음먹었으니, 이 도시도 저를 똑같은 식으로 받아들여 준다면 무척 고맙겠습니다. 분명 저는 이곳에서 제가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 큰 매력을 몇 가지 발견했거든요.\"\n\n\"팁턴과 로윅 쪽으로 나가는 승마길을 말씀하시는 거군요. 누구나 그걸 좋아하지요.\" 로저먼드가 천연덕스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 제게 훨씬 더 가까운 무언가를 말하는 겁니다.\"\n\n로저먼드는 일어나 뜨개질감을 집어 들고는 말했다. \"춤에는 좀 관심이 있으세요? 영리한 남자들이 춤을 추기나 하는지 잘 모르겠어서요.\"\n\n\"허락만 해 주신다면 당신과 춤을 추겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어머!\" 로저먼드가 살짝 사양하는 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"저는 그저 우리가 가끔 춤추는 모임을 연다는 말씀을 드리려던 거예요. 그러니까 와 달라는 청을 받으면 모욕으로 여기실지 어떨지 알고 싶었던 거고요.\"\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 조건이라면 그럴 리 없지요.\"\n\n이런 대화를 나눈 뒤 리드게이트는 자리를 뜨려고 했으나, 휘스트 탁자 쪽으로 다가가다가 페어브러더 씨의 능란한 솜씨에, 그리고 빈틈없음과 온화함이 묘하게 어우러진 그의 얼굴에 흥미를 느껴 구경에 빠져들었다. 열 시에 (미들마치의 풍습이 그러했다) 야식이 들어왔고 펀치 술을 돌렸으나, 페어브러더 씨는 물 한 잔만 들었다. 그가 따고 있었지만 게임이 거듭되는 데 끝날 이유가 없어 보였으므로, 리드게이트는 마침내 작별을 고했다.\n\n하지만 아직 열한 시가 안 된 터라, 그는 상쾌한 공기를 가르며 페어브러더 씨의 교회인 성 보톨프 교회의 탑 쪽으로 걷기로 했다. 그 탑은 별빛을 배경으로 어둑하고 네모지고 육중하게 솟아 있었다. 미들마치에서 가장 오래된 교회였다. 그러나 그 성직록은 한 해에 사백 파운드도 채 안 되는 보좌신부직에 지나지 않았다. 리드게이트는 그 이야기를 들은 적이 있던지라, 이제 페어브러더 씨가 카드로 딴 돈을 마음에 두는 것은 아닐까 궁금해하며 생각했다. \"무척 유쾌한 친구 같긴 한데, 불스트로드에게도 그럴 만한 이유가 있을지 모르지.\" 불스트로드 씨가 대체로 정당하다는 것이 드러난다면 리드게이트에게는 많은 일이 한결 수월해질 터였다. \"그가 좋은 생각 몇 가지를 함께 지니고 있다면, 그의 종교적 교리 따위가 내게 무슨 상관이란 말인가? 사람은 구할 수 있는 만큼의 두뇌를 써야 하는 법이지.\"\n\n이것이 실은 리드게이트가 빈시 씨 댁을 나와 걸으며 가장 먼저 한 사색이었으니, 이런 점 때문에 많은 부인들은 그가 자기네 관심을 받을 자격이 거의 없다고 여길까 두렵다. 그는 로저먼드와 그녀의 음악을 두 번째로나 떠올렸다. 그리고 그녀의 차례가 돌아왔을 때는 남은 길을 걷는 내내 그녀의 모습에 마음을 머물렀으나, 아무런 동요도 느끼지 않았고, 자기 삶에 어떤 새로운 물결이 들이쳤다는 느낌도 없었다. 그는 아직 결혼할 수 없었다. 몇 해 동안은 결혼할 마음이 없었다. 그러니 어쩌다 감탄하게 된 처녀와 사랑에 빠진다는 생각을 받아들일 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그는 로저먼드에게 더없이 감탄했다. 하지만 한때 로르를 두고 그를 사로잡았던 그 광기가 다른 어떤 여자에게 되살아날 성싶지는 않다고 그는 생각했다. 분명, 사랑에 빠지는 일이 조금이라도 문제가 된다면, 이 빈시 양 같은 존재라면 더없이 안전할 터였다. 그녀에게는 남자가 여자에게 바랄 법한 바로 그런 종류의 지성ㅡ세련되고 정제되었으며 고분고분하고, 인생의 온갖 섬세한 일에 마무리를 더할 줄 알며, 그것을 더 이상의 증거가 필요 없을 만큼 분명하게 드러내는 몸에 깃든 지성이 있었기 때문이다. 리드게이트는 확신했다. 자기가 언젠가 결혼한다면 그의 아내는 그 여성다운 광채를, 꽃과 음악에 견주어야 할 그 독특한 여성성을, 본성상 순수하고 섬세한 기쁨만을 위해 빚어진 까닭에 그 자체로 미덕인 그런 종류의 아름다움을 지녔으리라고.\n\n그러나 그는 앞으로 다섯 해 동안은 결혼할 생각이 없었으므로ㅡ그에게 더 시급한 일은 루이의 새로운 열병 책을 살펴보는 것이었다. 그는 그 책에 특별히 관심이 있었으니, 파리에서 루이를 알고 지냈고, 발진티푸스와 장티푸스의 구체적인 차이를 규명하기 위해 숱한 해부 시연을 따라다녔기 때문이다. 그는 집으로 돌아가 한밤중이 깊도록 책을 읽으면서, 이 병리학 연구에는 사랑과 결혼의 복잡함에 적용해야 한다고 여겼던 것보다 훨씬 더 까다롭게 세부와 관계를 따져 보는 안목을 들이댔다. 사랑과 결혼이라는 주제에 관해서는, 문학과 남자들의 화기애애한 대화 속에 전해 내려오는 그 전통적 지혜로 이미 넉넉히 알고 있다고 그는 느꼈던 까닭이다. 반면 열병에는 모호한 조건들이 있었고, 그것은 그에게 한낱 자의가 아니라 단련된 능력의 발휘인 그 즐거운 상상의 노동을 안겨 주었다ㅡ개연성에 대한 더없이 또렷한 안목과 지식에 대한 더없이 완전한 복종으로 결합하고 구성하는 일, 그리고 다시 공정한 자연과 한층 힘차게 동맹하여, 한 발 물러선 채 제 작업을 시험할 검증법을 고안해 내는 일이었다.\n\n많은 사람들이, 그저 그런 그림이나 값싼 서술을 잔뜩 늘어놓은 것만으로 생생한 상상력을 지녔다고 칭송받아 왔다. 이를테면 먼 천체에서 오가는 형편없는 잡담을 전한다거나, 박쥐 날개를 달고 인광을 뿜으며 못된 심부름을 하러 내려오는 크고 흉측한 사내로 루시퍼를 그려 낸다거나, 마치 병든 꿈속의 삶을 비추는 듯한 음탕함을 과장한다거나 하는 것들 말이다. 하지만 리드게이트는 이런 종류의 영감을, 어떤 렌즈로도 닿을 수 없으나 그 바깥의 어둠 속에서 필연적 인과의 긴 행로를 따라 추적되는 미묘한 작용들을 드러내는 상상력에 견주면 다소 천박하고 술기운에 젖은 것으로 여겼다. 그 상상력은 에너지의 마지막 정수인 내면의 빛으로 추적하는 것이었으니, 그 빛은 이상적으로 밝혀진 공간 속에서 영묘한 원자들까지도 적실 수 있는 것이었다. 그로 말하자면, 무지가 스스로 능하고 편안하다고 여기는 그 모든 값싼 발상을 내던져 버렸다. 그는 연구의 바로 그 눈인 저 고된 발상에 매혹되어 있었으니, 그것은 잠정적으로 대상의 틀을 잡고는 점점 더 정확한 관계로 바로잡아 가는 것이었다. 그는 인간의 비참과 기쁨을 빚어내는 저 미세한 과정들의 모호함을, 고통과 광기와 범죄가 처음 숨어드는 잠복처인 저 보이지 않는 통로들을, 행복하거나 불행한 의식의 성장을 결정짓는 저 섬세한 균형과 전이를 꿰뚫고 싶었다.\n\n그는 책을 내려놓고 벽난로 쇠살대의 잉걸불 쪽으로 다리를 뻗은 채 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 끼었다. 사고가 어떤 특정한 대상에 대한 검토에서 벗어나 그것이 우리 존재의 나머지 전부와 맺는 관계에 대한 충만한 감각 속으로 풀려나는 그 흐뭇한 흥분의 여운 속에서ㅡ말하자면 힘차게 헤엄친 뒤 벌렁 드러누워, 아직 다 쓰지 않은 힘의 여유로움으로 둥둥 떠 있는 듯한 그 순간에ㅡ리드게이트는 자신의 연구에 의기양양한 기쁨을 느꼈고, 자기 직업에 속하지 못한 운 나쁜 사람들에게 일종의 연민 같은 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"소년 시절에 그 길로 접어들지 않았더라면,\" 하고 그는 생각했다. \"나는 무슨 멍청한 짐말 같은 일에나 빠져들어 늘 눈가리개를 한 채 살았을지도 몰라. 최고의 지적 긴장을 불러내면서도 이웃들과 따뜻하게 어울리게 해 주지 않는 직업에서는 결코 행복하지 못했을 거야. 그 점에서는 의학만 한 게 없지. 머나먼 곳에 닿는 그 순수한 과학적 삶을 누리면서도 교구의 케케묵은 노인네들과 벗할 수 있으니까. 성직자에게는 그게 좀 더 어렵지. 페어브러더는 별종인 모양이야.\"\n\n이 마지막 생각은 빈시가 사람들과 그날 저녁의 온갖 장면을 다시 떠올리게 했다. 그것들은 그의 마음속을 제법 흐뭇하게 떠다녔고, 그가 잠자리 촛대를 집어 들 때 그의 입술은 흐뭇한 회상에 으레 따르기 마련인 그 막 피어나려는 미소로 살짝 굽어 있었다. 그는 열정적인 사내였으나, 지금 그의 열정은 자기 일에 대한 사랑과, 변변찮은 시골 개업으로 시작할 수밖에 없었던 다른 과학의 영웅들처럼, 자기 삶을 인류의 더 나은 삶을 이루는 한 요소로 인정받게 하려는 야망에 온통 빨려들어 있었다.\n\n가엾은 리드게이트! 아니, 가엾은 로저먼드라고 해야 할까? 두 사람은 저마다 상대가 전혀 알지 못하는 세계 속에 살고 있었다. 리드게이트는 자신이 로저먼드의 열렬한 사색의 대상이 되어 왔다는 것을 떠올린 적이 없었다. 그녀로 말하자면 자신의 결혼을 먼 훗날로 미뤄 둘 까닭도 없었고, 대다수 처녀들의 삶에서 큰 몫을 차지하는 그 곱씹는 버릇ㅡ눈길과 말과 문구를 마음속으로 거듭 되뇌는 일에서 마음을 돌려놓을 병리학 연구 따위도 없었다. 그는 아름다운 처녀에게 남자가 으레 바쳐야 할 어쩔 수 없는 만큼의 감탄과 찬사 이상으로 그녀를 바라보거나 그녀에게 말을 건넬 생각이 없었다. 사실 그가 보기에는, 그녀의 음악을 즐긴 일조차 거의 입 밖에 내지 못한 채로 남아 있었다. 그녀가 그런 재능을 지닌 데 대한 자신의 큰 놀라움을 그녀에게 털어놓는 무례를 범할까 두려웠던 까닭이다. 하지만 로저먼드는 모든 눈길과 말을 빠짐없이 기록해 두고는, 그것들을 미리 그려 둔 어떤 연애담의 첫 장면들로ㅡ앞으로 펼쳐질 전개와 절정에서 가치를 얻게 될 장면들로 가늠했다. 로저먼드의 연애담에서는 주인공의 내면생활이나 그가 세상에서 하는 진지한 일에 관해 굳이 많이 상상할 필요가 없었다. 물론 그에게는 직업이 있었고 영리했으며 충분히 잘생기기도 했다. 하지만 리드게이트에 관한 짜릿한 사실은 그의 좋은 가문이었으니, 그것이 그를 미들마치의 모든 숭배자들과 구별 짓고, 결혼을 신분 상승의 전망으로ㅡ그녀가 천박한 사람들과는 아무 상관도 없게 될, 그리고 어쩌면 마침내 미들마치 사람들을 깔보는 그 군 유지들과 어깨를 나란히 할 만한 친척들과 어울리게 될, 지상에서의 그 천상 같은 경지에 조금이나마 더 가까이 다가서는 일로 제시했기 때문이다. 신분의 가장 희미한 향기까지도 무척 미묘하게 분별해 내는 것이 로저먼드의 영민함의 한 부분이었으니, 언젠가 브룩가 자매가 삼촌을 따라 순회 재판에 와서 귀족들 틈에 앉아 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀는 그들의 수수한 옷차림에도 불구하고 그들을 부러워했다.\n\n리드게이트를 가문 좋은 사내로 상상하는 일이 그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 느낌과 조금이라도 관련된 만족의 전율을 불러일으킬 수 있다는 것이 믿기지 않는다면, 나는 당신에게 비교의 능력을 좀 더 효과적으로 발휘해 보기를, 그리하여 붉은 군복과 견장이 그런 종류의 영향을 끼친 적이 정녕 없었는지 헤아려 보기를 청하겠다. 우리의 열정이란 잠긴 방 안에 따로따로 사는 것이 아니라, 저마다 관념이라는 자그마한 옷장의 옷을 차려입고 공동의 식탁으로 양식을 가져와 함께 어울려 식사하며, 제 식욕에 따라 공동의 곳간에서 양분을 먹는 법이다.\n\n사실 로저먼드는 테르티우스 리드게이트라는 사람 그 자체보다는, 정확히 말해 그와 자기 사이의 관계에 온통 마음을 빼앗기고 있었다. 모든 젊은 남자들이 자기에게 반할 수 있고, 반할 만하고, 반할 것이며, 실제로 반해 있다는 말을 늘 들으며 자란 처녀가 리드게이트도 예외일 수 없다고 대뜸 믿어 버린 것은 너그러이 봐줄 만한 일이었다. 그의 눈길과 말은 그녀에게 다른 남자들의 것보다 더 큰 의미가 있었으니, 그녀가 그것들을 더 마음에 두었기 때문이다. 그녀는 부지런히 그것들을 떠올렸고, 자신이 여태껏 의식했던 것보다 더 합당한 찬미자를 리드게이트에게서 찾아낼 그 완벽한 용모와 거동과 감정과 온갖 우아함을 부지런히 가다듬었다.\n\n로저먼드로 말하자면, 자기에게 거슬리는 일은 결코 하지 않으려 했으되 부지런한 처녀였다. 그리고 이제 그 어느 때보다도 그녀는 풍경화와 장터 수레와 친구들의 초상을 그리고, 음악을 연습하고, 아침부터 밤까지 완벽한 숙녀라는 자신의 본보기가 되기에 열심이었다. 그녀에게는 늘 자신의 의식이라는 청중이 있었고, 거기에 더러는 그 집을 드나드는 숱한 손님들이라는, 한결 변덕스러우나 결코 달갑지 않지는 않은 외부의 청중까지 더해졌다. 그녀는 또한 가장 좋은 소설, 심지어 둘째로 좋은 소설까지 읽을 짬도 냈고, 시도 많이 외우고 있었다. 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 시는 「랄라 루크」였다.\n\n\"세상에서 가장 좋은 처녀라니까! 저 애를 얻는 사내는 행복한 친구가 될 거야!\" 이것이 빈시가를 드나드는 노신사들의 마음이었다. 그리고 퇴짜 맞은 젊은이들은 다시 한번 도전해 볼까 생각했으니, 다가올 경쟁자들로 지평선이 빽빽하지 않은 주도에서는 으레 그러는 법이었다. 하지만 플림데일 부인은 로저먼드가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 과한 교육을 받았다고 여겼다. 결혼하고 나면 죄다 내팽개치게 될 재주가 무슨 소용이란 말인가? 한편 오라비의 가족에게 누이로서의 정성을 다하던 이모 불스트로드 부인은 로저먼드에 대해 두 가지 진심 어린 소망을 품고 있었으니ㅡ그 애가 좀 더 진지한 마음가짐을 보여 주는 것, 그리고 그 애의 습성에 걸맞은 재산을 지닌 남편을 만나는 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII.\n\n친애하는 카멘 페어브라더 목사를 리드게이트는 이튿날 저녁에 찾아갔는데, 그는 돌로 지은 오래된 목사관에 살고 있었으니, 그 건물은 마주 보이는 교회와 어울릴 만큼 위엄 있게 낡아 있었다. 집 안의 가구들도 모두 오래된 것이었지만, 그 낡음의 결은 또 달랐다. 페어브라더 씨의 부친과 조부 대의 것이었던 것이다. 도금과 화환 무늬가 새겨진, 하얗게 칠한 의자들이 있었고, 군데군데 째진 붉은 비단 다마스크 천이 아직 남아 있었다. 지난 세기의 대법관들과 그 밖에 유명한 법률가들의 동판 초상화가 걸려 있었고, 그것들을 비추는 오래된 벽거울들도 있었으며, 새틴우드로 만든 작은 탁자들과 불편한 의자를 길게 늘인 듯한 소파도 있었으니, 모두 짙은 빛깔의 벽널을 배경으로 도드라져 보였다. 이것이 리드게이트가 안내받아 들어선 응접실의 면모였다. 그를 맞이한 세 부인 역시 구식이었고, 빛바랬으되 진정한 품위를 지니고 있었다. 페어브라더 부인은 목사의 백발 모친으로, 정갈하고 깔끔하게 주름 장식과 머릿수건을 두른 채 꼿꼿한 자세에 눈빛이 또렷했으며 아직 칠십이 되지 않았다. 그녀의 여동생인 노블 양은 한결 온순해 보이는 자그마한 노부인으로, 주름 장식과 머릿수건은 확실히 더 닳고 기운 것이었다. 그리고 목사의 누이인 위니프레드 페어브라더 양은 동생과 마찬가지로 인상이 좋았으나, 평생 윗사람들에게 끊임없이 순종하며 살아온 독신 여성들이 흔히 그렇듯 위축되고 기가 꺾여 있었다. 리드게이트는 이토록 예스러운 무리를 만나리라고는 예상하지 못했다. 그저 페어브라더 씨가 독신이라는 것만 알고 있었던 터라, 그는 십중팔구 주된 가구라고는 책과 자연물 수집품일 아늑한 서재로 안내되리라 생각했던 것이다. 목사 자신도 오히려 다소 달라진 모습으로 보였으니, 다른 데서 사귄 지인이 자기 집에서 그를 처음 보는 경우 대부분의 남자가 그러하듯이 말이다. 사실 어떤 이들은 새 작품에서 심술쟁이 역할을 어울리지 않게 맡은, 본디 호인 역의 배우처럼 보이기도 한다. 페어브라더 씨는 그런 경우는 아니었다. 그는 조금 더 부드럽고 더 말이 없어 보였으며, 주로 이야기하는 사람은 그의 모친이었고, 그는 이따금 좋은 기분으로 누그러뜨리는 한마디를 끼워 넣을 뿐이었다. 노부인은 함께한 사람들에게 마땅히 무엇을 생각해야 하는지 일러 주는 데 익숙한 것이 분명했고, 자신의 키질 없이는 어떤 화제도 영 안전하지 않다고 여기는 듯했다. 그녀가 이런 소임을 다할 여유를 누릴 수 있었던 것은 자질구레한 시중을 모두 위니프레드 양이 들어 주었기 때문이다. 그러는 동안 자그마한 노블 양은 팔에 작은 바구니를 걸고 있었는데, 마치 실수인 양 먼저 받침 접시에 떨어뜨린 설탕 조각을 그 안으로 슬쩍 옮겨 넣고는, 그 뒤에 은밀히 주위를 둘러보고 나서 자그맣고 겁 많은 네발짐승처럼 작고 천진한 소리를 내며 다시 찻잔으로 돌아왔다. 부디 노블 양을 나쁘게 여기지 마시기를. 그 바구니에는 그녀가 비교적 옮기기 쉬운 음식에서 아껴 둔 것들이 담겨 있었으니, 화창한 아침이면 종종걸음으로 찾아가는 가난한 친구들의 아이들에게 줄 것이었다. 궁핍한 모든 것을 보살피고 어루만지는 일이 그녀에게는 너무도 자연스러운 기쁨이어서, 그녀는 그것을 마치 자기가 빠져 있는 어떤 즐거운 악덕쯤으로 여겼다. 어쩌면 그녀는 아무것도 가진 게 없는 이들에게 줄 것을 많이 가진 이들에게서 훔치고 싶은 유혹에 빠져 있음을 의식하고 있었는지도 모르고, 그 억눌린 욕망의 죄책감을 양심 속에 짊어지고 있었는지도 모른다. 베푸는 사치를 알려면 가난해야 하는 법이다!\n\n페어브라더 부인은 활기차면서도 격식 있고 또박또박한 태도로 손님을 맞았다. 그녀는 곧 이 집에서는 의술의 도움이 필요한 일이 흔치 않다고 그에게 알렸다. 그녀는 자식들을 플란넬을 입히고 과식하지 않도록 키웠는데, 그 마지막 습관이야말로 사람들이 의사를 필요로 하는 주된 이유라고 여겼다. 리드게이트가 부모가 과식한 탓에 고생하는 이들을 두고 변호하자, 페어브라더 부인은 그런 관점은 위험하다고 했다. 자연은 그보다 더 공정하다는 것이었다. 어떤 흉악범이든 자기 대신 조상이 교수형을 당했어야 한다고 말하기는 쉬운 일이 아니냐고 했다. 못된 부모를 둔 이들이 스스로도 못됐다면, 그것은 그 못됨 때문에 교수형을 당하는 것이라고 했다. 눈에 보이지도 않는 것까지 거슬러 올라갈 필요는 없다는 것이었다.\n\n\"우리 어머니는 늙은 조지 3세 같으시지요.\" 목사가 말했다. \"형이상학을 반대하시거든요.\"\n\n\"나는 그른 것을 반대하는 거다, 카멘. 내 말은, 몇 가지 명백한 진리를 붙들고서 모든 것을 거기에 맞춰 가라는 거야. 내가 젊었을 적엔, 리드게이트 선생, 옳고 그름에 대해 의문을 품는 일이라곤 없었어요. 우리는 교리문답을 알았고, 그것으로 충분했지요. 우리는 신조와 의무를 배웠어요. 점잖은 교회 신자라면 누구나 똑같은 의견을 가졌고요. 그런데 지금은 기도서 그 자체에서 인용해 말해도 반박당하기 십상이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자기 견해를 지키기 좋아하는 사람들에게는 오히려 즐거운 시절이겠군요.\" 리드게이트가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 우리 어머니는 늘 양보하시지요.\" 목사가 짓궂게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니다, 카멘, 리드게이트 선생이 나에 대해 오해하게 만들면 못 써. 나는 부모님이 가르쳐 주신 것을 저버리는 그런 불경을 절대 저지르지 않을 게다. 마음을 바꾸면 어찌 되는지는 누구나 알 수 있는 일이야. 한 번 바꾸면, 어찌 스무 번인들 못 바꾸겠니?\"\n\n\"한 번 바꾸는 데는 좋은 논거를 찾으면서도, 다시 바꾸는 데는 그 논거를 찾지 못할 수도 있지요.\" 리드게이트가 단호한 노부인에게 흥미를 느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"그 점은 실례지만 동의할 수 없어요. 논거로만 따진다면, 마음에 한결같음이 없는 사람에게는 논거란 결코 모자랄 일이 없거든. 우리 아버지는 절대 바꾸지 않으셨고, 논거 없이 평이한 도덕적 설교를 하셨으며, 훌륭한 분이셨어요. 그보다 나은 사람이 드물 정도로요. 논거로 빚어낸 훌륭한 사람을 데려오신다면, 나는 요리책을 읽어 드리고서 좋은 만찬을 차려 드리지요. 그게 내 의견이고, 누구의 위장이든 내 말이 옳다고 입증해 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"만찬에 관한 한은 분명 그렇겠지요, 어머니.\" 페어브라더 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"만찬이든 사람이든 매한가지야. 나는 칠십이 다 됐어요, 리드게이트 선생, 그래서 경험으로 판단하지요. 여기에도 다른 데처럼 새로운 깨달음을 외치는 자들이 수두룩하지만, 나는 그런 걸 따를 사람이 아니에요. 내 말로는, 그런 것들은 빨아도 못 견디고 입어도 못 견디는 혼방 천과 함께 들어온 거예요. 내 젊을 적엔 그렇지 않았어요. 교회 신자는 교회 신자였고, 성직자라면 다른 건 몰라도 신사임은 꽤 확신할 수 있었지요. 그런데 지금은 비국교도보다 나을 것도 없는 자가 교리를 빙자해 내 아들을 밀어내려 들어요. 하지만 누가 그 애를 밀어내려 하든, 자랑스럽게 말하건대, 리드게이트 선생, 내 아들은 이 왕국의 어떤 설교자와 견주어도 손색이 없어요. 이 도시 안에서야 말할 것도 없지만, 이 도시는 견줄 기준으로는 낮은 편이지요. 적어도 내 생각엔 그래요. 나는 엑서터에서 나고 자랐으니까요.\"\n\n\"어머니란 결코 공평하지 않은 법이지요.\" 페어브라더 씨가 웃으며 말했다. \"타이크의 어머니는 그분에 대해 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"아, 가엾은 사람! 정말이지 뭐라고 하겠니?\" 페어브라더 부인이 말했는데, 모성의 판단에 대한 그녀의 믿음 때문에 그 날카로움이 잠시 무뎌진 것이었다. \"분명 마음속으론 진실을 말할 게다.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 진실이란 게 뭡니까?\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"궁금하군요.\"\n\n\"오, 나쁜 거라곤 전혀 없어요.\" 페어브라더 씨가 말했다. \"그는 열성적인 사람이지요. 그리 박식하지도, 그리 지혜롭지도 않다고 나는 생각하는데, 그건 내가 그와 의견이 맞지 않기 때문일 거고요.\"\n\n\"어머나, 카멘!\" 위니프레드 양이 말했다. \"오늘에야 그리핀과 그 아내가 나한테 말해 줬는데, 타이크 씨가 그러더래요, 그 부부가 오빠 설교를 들으러 가면 석탄을 더는 안 주겠다고요.\"\n\n페어브라더 부인은 차와 토스트를 조금 든 뒤에 다시 시작했던 뜨개질을 내려놓고, \"들었지?\"라고 말하려는 듯 아들을 바라보았다. 노블 양은 \"오, 가엾어라! 가엾기도 하지!\" 하고 말했는데, 아마도 설교와 석탄을 둘 다 잃는 것을 두고 한 말이었으리라. 그러나 목사는 차분하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 그 사람들이 내 교구민이 아니어서 그런 거예요. 그리고 내 설교가 그 사람들한테 석탄 한 짐만 한 값어치가 있다고도 생각지 않고요.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 선생,\" 이 말을 그냥 넘길 수 없었던 페어브라더 부인이 말했다. \"당신은 내 아들을 몰라요. 저 애는 늘 자기를 낮춘다니까요. 나는 저 애한테 그러지요, 너를 만드시고 더없이 훌륭한 설교자로 만드신 하느님을 낮추는 거라고요.\"\n\n\"그건 제가 리드게이트 선생을 모시고 서재로 가라는 암시로군요, 어머니.\" 목사가 웃으며 말했다. \"수집품을 보여 드리겠다고 약속했지요.\" 그가 리드게이트를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"가 볼까요?\"\n\n세 부인 모두 만류했다. 리드게이트 씨가 차 한 잔 더 들 겨를도 없이 그렇게 서둘러 끌려가서는 안 된다는 것이었다. 위니프레드 양의 주전자에는 좋은 차가 넉넉히 있었다. 카멘은 무슨 까닭으로 손님을 자기 굴로 데려가지 못해 안달인가? 그곳에는 절여 둔 벌레들이며 청파리와 나방으로 가득 찬 서랍들밖에 없고, 바닥엔 양탄자도 깔려 있지 않았다. 리드게이트 씨가 양해해 줘야 할 형편이었다. 크리비지 한 판이 훨씬 나을 것이었다. 요컨대, 목사가 자기 집 여인들로부터 남자 중의 남자, 설교자 중의 으뜸으로 추앙받으면서도, 동시에 그들의 지도가 몹시 필요한 사람으로 여겨질 수 있다는 것이 분명했다. 리드게이트는 젊은 독신 남자 특유의 천박한 안목으로, 페어브라더 씨가 어찌 그들을 더 잘 가르쳐 두지 못했을까 의아해했다.\n\n\"우리 어머니는 내 취미에 조금이라도 관심을 가질 만한 손님을 맞는 데 익숙하지 않으시지요.\" 목사가 서재 문을 열며 말했는데, 그곳은 과연 부인들이 넌지시 일러 준 대로 짧은 자기 파이프와 담배통을 빼면 몸을 위한 사치라곤 찾아볼 수 없을 만큼 휑했다.\n\n\"선생 같은 직업을 가진 분들은 보통 담배를 안 피우지요.\" 그가 말했다. 리드게이트는 미소를 지으며 고개를 가로저었다. \"엄밀히 말하면 내 직업도 그렇겠지요. 벌스트로드 일당이 이 파이프를 두고 나를 헐뜯는 소리를 듣게 될 거예요. 그들은 내가 이걸 끊으면 악마가 얼마나 좋아할지 모르거든요.\"\n\n\"이해합니다. 선생은 흥분하기 쉬운 기질이라 진정제가 필요한 거지요. 저는 더 둔중한 편이어서, 그걸 하면 게을러질 겁니다. 나태함 속으로 곤두박질쳐서, 거기 고인 채 온 힘을 다해 정체해 버리겠지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 선생은 그 모든 걸 일에 쏟으려는 거군요. 나는 선생보다 한 열두어 살 위인데, 타협하기에 이르렀어요. 한두 가지 약점에 먹이를 주지요, 안 그러면 그것들이 아우성을 칠 테니까요. 보세요.\" 목사가 작은 서랍 여럿을 열며 말을 이었다. \"내가 이 지역의 곤충학을 철저히 연구했다고 자부합니다. 동물상과 식물상도 함께 다루고 있긴 하지만, 적어도 곤충만큼은 제대로 해 두었어요. 우리 지역은 직시류가 유난히 풍부하지요. 글쎄, 혹시 — 아! 그 유리병을 집어 드셨군요. 내 서랍 대신 그걸 들여다보고 계시는군. 이런 것들엔 정말로 관심이 없으신 거지요?\"\n\n\"이 사랑스러운 무뇌 기형 표본 옆에 두고 보면 그렇지요. 저는 박물학에 제 자신을 많이 쏟을 시간이 없었습니다. 일찍부터 구조에 흥미가 동했고, 그것이야말로 제 직업에 가장 직결되는 것이지요. 그 밖에 다른 취미는 없습니다. 저한테는 거기에 헤엄칠 바다가 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"아! 선생은 행복한 사람이군요.\" 페어브라더 씨가 발꿈치로 홱 돌아서며 파이프에 담배를 채우기 시작했다. \"선생은 영적인 담배를 갈망한다는 게 어떤 건지 모르지요. 옛 문헌의 서툰 교정이라든가, 『잡담쟁이 잡지』에 실으려고 필로마이크론이라는 잘 알려진 서명을 달아 양배추진딧물의 여러 변종에 관한 자질구레한 항목을 쓴다든가, 아니면 모세오경의 곤충학에 관한 학술 논문 — 거기 언급되지는 않았으되 이스라엘 백성이 광야를 지날 때 분명 마주쳤을 모든 곤충을 다루고, 솔로몬이 다룬 개미에 관한 단행본을 곁들여, 잠언서가 현대 연구 성과와 조화를 이룸을 보여 주는 식이지요. 내가 선생한테 연기를 뿜어 대도 괜찮겠어요?\"\n\n리드게이트는 이 이야기에 담긴 함의 — 목사가 스스로 썩 어울리는 소명을 얻지 못했다고 느끼고 있다는 것 — 보다도 그 이야기의 솔직함에 더 놀랐다. 깔끔하게 갖춰진 서랍과 선반, 그리고 값비싼 박물학 도판 서적으로 가득 찬 책장을 보자, 그는 다시금 카드놀이로 딴 돈과 그 돈의 쓰임새를 떠올렸다. 그러나 그는 페어브라더 씨가 하는 모든 일에 대한 가장 호의적인 해석이 진실이기를 바라기 시작하고 있었다. 목사의 솔직함은 남의 판단을 앞질러 막으려는 불안한 의식에서 나오는 그런 거슬리는 종류가 아니라, 그저 되도록 가식 없이 처신하고 싶은 욕구가 풀려 나온 것으로 보였다. 보아하니 그는 자기 발언의 거리낌 없음이 성급해 보일 수도 있다는 자각이 없지 않았으니, 곧 이렇게 말했던 것이다.\n\n\"내가 선생보다 유리한 점이 있고 선생이 나를 아는 것보다 내가 선생을 더 잘 안다는 것을, 아직 말씀드리지 않았군요, 리드게이트 선생. 파리에서 한동안 선생과 같은 방을 썼던 트롤리를 기억하시지요? 나는 그와 편지를 주고받던 사이였는데, 그가 선생에 대해 꽤 많은 이야기를 해 주었어요. 선생이 처음 왔을 때는 같은 사람인지 확신이 서지 않았어요. 같은 분임을 알고서 무척 반가웠지요. 다만 나는 선생에게는 나에 대한 그런 사전 설명이 없었다는 걸 잊지 않고 있습니다.\"\n\n리드게이트는 여기에 어떤 섬세한 감정이 있음을 알아차렸으나, 절반도 이해하지 못했다. \"그런데 말입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"트롤리는 어찌 됐습니까? 저는 그 친구 소식을 영 모르게 됐어요. 그는 프랑스 사회 체제에 열을 올렸고, 일종의 피타고라스식 공동체를 세우러 오지로 가겠다고 했지요. 떠났나요?\"\n\n\"전혀요. 그는 독일의 온천에서 개업하고 있고, 부유한 환자와 결혼했어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 지금까지는 제 생각이 더 잘 견디는 셈이군요.\" 리드게이트가 짧고 경멸 어린 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그는 의료라는 직업이 불가피한 사기 체계라고 우겼지요. 저는 잘못이 사람들에게 있다고 했고요. 거짓과 어리석음에 굴종하는 사람들 말입니다. 담장 밖에서 사기를 향해 설교할 게 아니라, 안에다 소독 장치를 설치하는 편이 나을지도 모른다고요. 요컨대 — 제가 제 말을 옮기고 있는 거지만 — 분명 옳은 분별은 다 제 쪽에 있었지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 선생의 계획은 피타고라스식 공동체보다 훨씬 더 실행하기 어렵지요. 선생은 자기 안의 옛 아담만 상대로 두고 있는 게 아니라, 선생 주위에서 사회를 이루고 있는 그 태초 아담의 후손들 전부를 상대로 두고 있으니까요. 보세요, 나는 어려움에 대한 지식을 위해 선생보다 열두세 해를 더 치렀어요. 한데\" — 페어브라더 씨가 잠시 말을 끊었다가 덧붙였다 — \"선생, 또 그 유리병을 노려보고 계시는군요. 맞바꾸고 싶으세요? 공정한 거래 없이는 못 드립니다.\"\n\n\"제게 알코올에 담근 바다쥐 표본이 — 훌륭한 표본들이 — 좀 있습니다. 그리고 로버트 브라운의 새 저작 『식물 화분에 관한 현미경 관찰』도 끼워 드리지요. 혹시 아직 안 가지고 계시다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 선생이 그 표본을 그토록 탐내는 걸 보니, 더 높은 값을 불러도 되겠는데요. 내 서랍을 다 살펴보고서 내 새로운 종들에 대해 모두 나와 의견을 같이해 달라고 하면 어떨까요?\" 목사는 이렇게 이야기하면서 파이프를 입에 문 채 이리저리 거닐었다가, 다시 돌아와 서랍 위로 자못 애틋하게 몸을 기울이곤 했다. \"미들마치에서 환자들을 만족시켜야 하는 젊은 의사한테는 그게 좋은 수련이 될 거예요. 지루함을 견디는 법을 배워 둬야지요, 명심하세요. 어쨌든 그 표본은 선생이 부른 조건대로 가지세요.\"\n\n\"사람들이 누구든의 허튼소리를 받아 주어야 할 필요를 지나치게 과대평가하다가, 결국 자기가 받아 준 바로 그 바보들한테 멸시당하게 되는 건 아닐까요?\" 리드게이트가 페어브라더 씨 곁으로 다가가며, 정교한 필체로 이름이 적힌 채 고운 단계에 따라 늘어선 곤충들을 다소 멍하니 바라보면서 말했다. \"가장 빠른 길은 자기 값어치를 느끼게 만드는 거지요. 그러면 사람들은 선생이 자기들한테 아첨을 하든 안 하든 선생을 받아들일 수밖에 없게 되니까요.\"\n\n\"전적으로 동감입니다. 하지만 그러려면 그 값어치를 확실히 갖추고 있어야 하고, 또 독립을 지켜야 해요. 그럴 수 있는 사람은 극히 드물지요. 아예 직무에서 미끄러져 나가 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 사람이 되거나, 아니면 멍에를 쓰고서 같이 멍에를 진 동료들이 끄는 대로 따라 끌려가거나 둘 중 하나예요. 한데 이 섬세한 직시류들 좀 보세요!\"\n\n리드게이트는 결국 서랍을 하나하나 어느 정도 들여다봐 줘야 했고, 목사는 스스로를 비웃으면서도 끈질기게 그것들을 펼쳐 보였다.\n\n\"멍에를 쓰는 일에 대해 선생이 하신 말씀과 관련해서요,\" 그들이 자리에 앉은 뒤에 리드게이트가 입을 열었다. \"저는 얼마 전에 그걸 되도록 적게 쓰고 살기로 마음먹었습니다. 그래서 적어도 앞으로 여러 해 동안은 런던에서 무엇이든 시도하지 않기로 결심했던 거고요. 거기서 공부할 때 본 것이 마음에 들지 않았거든요. 공허한 거드름이라든가, 앞길을 막는 술수가 너무 많았어요. 시골에서는 사람들이 지식을 내세우는 일도 덜하고, 좋은 벗이 되어 주는 일도 덜하지만, 바로 그 때문에 사람의 자존심을 덜 건드리지요. 나쁜 감정도 덜 생기고, 자기 길을 더 조용히 좇아갈 수 있고요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 음, 선생은 출발이 좋아요. 옳은 직업을 택했고, 스스로 가장 잘 맞는다고 느끼는 일을 하고 있으니까요. 어떤 사람들은 그걸 놓쳐 버리고는 너무 늦게야 후회하지요. 하지만 독립을 지킬 거라고 너무 확신하지는 마세요.\"\n\n\"가족이라는 굴레를 말씀하시는 거지요?\" 리드게이트가 말했는데, 그런 것들이 페어브라더 씨를 다소 옥죄고 있으리라 짐작하면서였다.\n\n\"꼭 그것만은 아니에요. 물론 그런 것들이 많은 일을 더 어렵게 만들긴 하지요. 하지만 좋은 아내 — 세속에 물들지 않은 좋은 여인은 — 정말로 남자를 도와줄 수 있고, 그를 더 독립적으로 지켜 줄 수 있어요. 내 교구민 중에 한 사람 — 훌륭한 친구지만 아내가 없었더라면 지금처럼 잘 헤쳐 나오지 못했을 사람이 있지요. 가스 일가를 아세요? 피콕의 환자는 아니었던 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"모릅니다. 하지만 로윅의 페더스톤 노인 댁에 가스 양이라는 분이 있더군요.\"\n\n\"그 집 딸이지요. 더없이 훌륭한 처녀예요.\"\n\n\"아주 조용한 분이라, 저는 거의 눈여겨보지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 처녀는 선생을 눈여겨봤을 거예요, 틀림없이.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠군요.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"당연하지요\"라고는 차마 말할 수 없었다.\n\n\"오, 그 처녀는 누구든 가늠한다니까요. 내가 그 애한테 견진성사 준비를 시켰지요. 내가 아끼는 아이예요.\"\n\n페어브라더 씨는 잠시 말없이 몇 모금 빨았고, 리드게이트는 가스 일가에 대해 더 알고 싶은 마음이 없었다. 마침내 목사는 파이프를 내려놓고 다리를 쭉 뻗더니, 빛나는 눈을 미소와 함께 리드게이트 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 우리 미들마치 사람들은 선생이 생각하시는 만큼 그렇게 순하지가 않아요. 우리에게도 우리 나름의 암투가 있고 파벌이 있지요. 가령 나도 파벌에 속한 사람이고, 벌스트로드도 다른 파벌 사람이에요. 선생이 나한테 표를 주면 벌스트로드의 비위를 거스르게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"벌스트로드에게 무슨 문제가 있나요?\" 리드게이트가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"그거 말고 그에게 무슨 문제가 있다고 한 건 아니에요. 그에게 반대표를 던지면 그를 적으로 만들게 된다는 거지요.\"\n\n\"그런 거라면 제가 굳이 신경 쓸 필요는 없을 듯합니다.\" 리드게이트가 다소 자존심 있게 말했다. \"하지만 그는 병원에 대해 좋은 생각을 가진 듯하고, 유용한 공공사업에 큰돈을 쓰지요. 제 구상을 실현하는 데 그가 저를 꽤 도와줄 수 있을 겁니다. 그의 종교적 신념으로 말하자면 — 그야, 볼테르가 말했듯이, 일정량의 비소와 함께 쓴다면 주문(呪文)도 양 떼를 죽일 수 있는 법이지요. 저는 비소를 가져올 사람을 찾는 것이고, 그의 주문 따위는 개의치 않습니다.\"\n\n\"아주 좋아요. 하지만 그렇다면 선생의 비소 담당자를 거스르면 안 되겠지요. 나야 거슬러도 괜찮아요, 아시다시피.\" 페어브라더 씨가 아주 꾸밈없이 말했다. \"나는 내 편의를 남의 의무로 둔갑시키지 않으니까요. 나는 여러모로 벌스트로드와 반대편에 서 있어요. 그가 속한 무리를 좋아하지 않거든요. 그들은 편협하고 무지한 무리인 데다, 이웃을 더 낫게 만드는 일보다 불편하게 만드는 일을 더 많이 하지요. 그들의 방식이란 일종의 세속적이면서 영적인 파벌주의예요. 그들은 정말이지 나머지 인류를 자기들이 천국에 가도록 자양분이 되어 줄, 죽을 운명의 송장쯤으로 여긴다니까요. 하지만,\" 그가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"벌스트로드의 새 병원이 나쁜 거라고 말하는 건 아니에요. 그리고 그가 나를 옛 병원에서 몰아내려 한다 해도 — 그야, 그가 나를 해로운 인간으로 여긴다면, 그저 인사치레를 되돌려 주는 것뿐이지요. 게다가 나는 모범적인 성직자가 아니에요. 그저 그럭저럭한 임시변통일 뿐이지요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 목사가 자신을 깎아내리고 있는 것인지 도무지 확신할 수 없었다. 모범적인 성직자란 모범적인 의사와 마찬가지로, 자기 직업을 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 것으로 여기고, 모든 지식을 자기 도덕의 병리학과 치료학에 바칠 단순한 자양분으로 받아들여야 마땅했다. 그는 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"벌스트로드가 선생을 갈아 치우려는 이유로 무엇을 댑니까?\"\n\n\"내가 그의 견해를 — 그가 영적 신앙이라 부르는 것을 — 가르치지 않는다는 것, 그리고 내게는 시간 여유가 없다는 것이지요. 두 가지 다 사실이에요. 하지만 그렇다 해도 나는 시간을 낼 수 있을 테고, 그 사십 파운드는 반가울 거예요. 그게 이 일의 명백한 실상이지요. 하지만 이 이야기는 그만둡시다. 나는 다만, 선생이 비소 담당자에게 표를 준다 해도 그 때문에 나와 인연을 끊어선 안 된다는 걸 말하고 싶었을 뿐이에요. 나는 선생을 놓칠 수가 없거든요. 선생은 우리 가운데 정착하러 온 일종의 세계 일주 항해자라, 내가 지구 반대편의 존재를 믿게끔 지켜 줄 사람이지요. 자, 이제 파리에 있는 그 사람들에 대해 다 말해 보세요.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII.\n\n더 비천한 희망들과 함께 제비를 뽑나니, 영웅적인 가슴도\n탁한 공기를 들이마시면 역병에 걸릴 위험을 무릅쓰고,\n\n적도를 건널 때 라임즙이 없으면\n괴혈병으로 시들 수도 있으니.」\n\n이 대화가 있고 나서 몇 주가 지나서야 비로소 예배당 목사직 문제가 리드게이트에게 실질적인 의미를 띠게 되었고, 그는 스스로 그 까닭을 인정하지는 않으면서도 어느 편에 표를 던질지 미리 정하는 일을 미루었다. 만약 그가 페어브라더 씨를 개인적으로 좋아하지 않았더라면, 그것은 정말이지 그에게 아무래도 좋은 문제였을 것이다. 다시 말해 그는 더 편한 쪽을 택해 망설임 없이 타이크의 임명에 표를 던졌을 것이다.\n그러나 세인트 보톨프의 교구 목사에 대한 그의 호감은 친분이 깊어 갈수록 커졌다. 자기 나름의 직업적 목표를 확보해야 하는 신참내기로서의 리드게이트의 처지를 헤아려, 페어브라더 씨가 그의 관심을 얻으려 하기보다는 오히려 멀리하도록 애써 주었다는 사실은, 리드게이트의 천성이 예민하게 감응하는 보기 드문 섬세함과 너그러움을 보여 주었다. 그것은 페어브라더 씨가 보이는 다른 면모들, 즉 유달리 훌륭하여 그의 성품을 자연의 웅장함과 사회적 너저분함으로 나뉜 듯한 저 남방의 풍경과 닮게 만드는 그런 행실들과 한데 어울렸다. 그처럼 자식 된 도리를 다하고 기사도적으로 어머니와 고모와 누이를 대할 수 있는 사람은 거의 없었으니, 그들이 그에게 의지한 탓에 그의 삶은 여러모로 그 자신에게는 다소 거북하게 빚어졌던 것이다. 자질구레한 필요에서 오는 압박을 느끼는 사람들 가운데 불가피하게 사사로운 욕망을 더 나은 동기라는 핑계로 치장하지 않으리라 그토록 고결하게 결심하는 이는 드물다. 이런 문제들에서 그는 자신의 삶이 어떤 면밀한 검증도 견뎌 내리라는 것을 의식하고 있었다. 그리고 어쩌면 그 의식이, 천상과의 친교가 가정에서의 행실은 조금도 나아지게 하지 못하는 듯하고 드높은 목표가 없어도 그들의 행동을 충분히 설명할 수 있는 그런 사람들의 까다로운 엄격함에 대해 약간의 반항심을 부추겼을지도 모른다. 게다가 그의 설교는 영국 교회가 건강하던 시절의 설교처럼 기발하고 함축적이었으며, 책 없이 전해졌다. 그의 교구 밖 사람들도 그의 설교를 들으러 왔다. 그리고 교회를 가득 채우는 일이 언제나 성직자의 직무 가운데 가장 어려운 부분이었으므로, 여기에 그가 무심한 우월감을 느낄 또 하나의 근거가 있었다. 게다가 그는 호감 가는 사람이었다. 성품이 온화하고 재치가 빠르며 솔직했고, 억눌린 쓰라림이 묻어나는 능글맞은 웃음이나 우리 가운데 절반을 친구들에게 골칫거리로 만드는 그 밖의 대화상의 버릇도 없었다. 리드게이트는 진심으로 그를 좋아했고, 그와 벗이 되기를 바랐다.\n\n이런 감정이 마음을 지배하는 가운데, 그는 계속해서 예배당 목사직 문제를 미뤄 두었고, 그것이 자기가 마땅히 관여할 일이 아닐뿐더러 자기에게 표를 요구하며 성가시게 하는 일도 결코 없으리라고 스스로를 설득했다. 리드게이트는 벌스트로드 씨의 요청으로 새 병원의 내부 배치 계획을 세우고 있었고, 두 사람은 자주 의논을 나누었다. 은행가는 늘 대체로 리드게이트를 협력자로 믿을 수 있으리라 전제했지만, 타이크와 페어브라더 사이에 다가올 결정에 대해서는 따로 언급하지 않았다. 그러나 병원 이사회 총회가 열리고, 예배당 목사직 문제가 다음 금요일에 모일 이사들과 의료진의 협의에 부쳐졌다는 통지를 받았을 때, 그는 이 사소한 미들마치의 일에 대해 마음을 정해야만 한다는 짜증스러운 기분이 들었다. 그는 마음속에서 벌스트로드가 수상이고 타이크 사건이 자리를 얻느냐 마느냐의 문제라는 뚜렷한 선언을 듣지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 자리에 대한 전망을 포기하기 싫다는 그에 못지않게 분명한 거부감도 어쩔 수 없었다. 그의 관찰이 은행가가 반대를 그냥 넘기지 않으리라는 페어브라더 씨의 장담을 끊임없이 확인해 주었기 때문이다. \"그들의 옹졸한 정치 따위, 빌어먹을!\" 이것이 그가 이 문제에 대해 정말로 양심의 법정을 열어야겠다고 느끼기 시작했을 무렵, 면도하며 사색에 잠겨 보낸 사흘 아침 동안 떠올린 생각 가운데 하나였다. 분명 페어브라더 씨의 선출에 반대해 할 수 있는 타당한 말들이 있었다. 그는 이미 너무 많은 일을 떠맡고 있었으니, 특히 성직과 무관한 일들에 얼마나 많은 시간을 쓰는지를 생각하면 그러했다. 게다가 그 목사가 명백히 돈을 위해 노름을 한다는 사실은, 정말로 노름 자체를 즐기기는 하지만 분명히 그것이 가져다주는 어떤 목적을 좋아한다는 사실은, 리드게이트의 존경심을 흔들며 거듭 충격을 주었다. 페어브라더 씨는 이론적으로 모든 놀이가 바람직하다고 주장했고, 영국인의 재치가 그런 것이 없어 정체되어 있다고 말했다. 그러나 리드게이트는 그가 돈이 아니었다면 훨씬 덜 노름을 했으리라 확신했다. 그린 드래건에는 당구장이 있었는데, 일부 노심초사하는 어머니들과 아내들은 그곳을 미들마치 최대의 유혹으로 여겼다. 그 목사는 일급 당구 선수였고, 그린 드래건에 자주 드나들지는 않았지만, 이따금 대낮에 그곳에 가서 돈을 땄다는 소문이 있었다. 그리고 예배당 목사직에 관해서는, 그 사십 파운드를 위해서가 아니라면 자기는 그 자리에 관심이 없다고 그는 굳이 꾸미려 들지 않았다. 리드게이트는 청교도는 아니었지만 노름을 좋아하지 않았고, 노름으로 돈을 따는 것은 늘 그에게 비열한 짓으로 여겨졌다. 게다가 그에게는 삶에 대한 어떤 이상이 있었으니, 행실을 푼돈벌이에 종속시키는 이런 일이 그에게는 더없이 혐오스럽게 느껴졌다. 지금껏 그 자신의 삶에서 그의 필요는 그 자신은 아무런 수고도 하지 않은 채 채워졌고, 그의 첫 충동은 언제나 신사에게는 대수롭지 않은 것이라며 반 크라운짜리 돈을 후하게 쓰는 것이었다. 반 크라운을 벌어들일 궁리를 짜내야겠다는 생각은 그에게 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그는 자신이 부자가 아니라는 것을 늘 대강은 알고 있었지만, 가난하다고 느낀 적은 없었고, 돈의 부족이 사람들의 행동을 결정하는 데 어떤 역할을 하는지 상상할 능력이 없었다. 돈은 그에게 결코 동기가 된 적이 없었다. 그래서 그는 이런 의도적인 푼돈 추구에 대해 변명을 마련해 줄 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그것은 그에게 그야말로 역겨운 일이었고, 그는 그 목사의 수입과 그의 다소간 필수적인 지출 사이의 비율을 따져 본 적도 없었다. 어쩌면 자기 자신의 경우라면 그런 계산을 하지 않았을 것이다.\n\n그런데 이제 투표 문제가 다가오자, 이 역겨운 사실은 전보다 더 강하게 페어브라더 씨에게 불리하게 작용했다. 사람들의 성품이 좀 더 한결같다면, 특히 친구들이 자기가 맡고자 하는 어떤 직무에든 어김없이 적합하다면, 우리는 어떻게 해야 할지 훨씬 더 잘 알 수 있으련만! 리드게이트는 페어브라더 씨에게 아무런 타당한 반대 사유가 없었다면, 벌스트로드가 그 문제에 대해 어떻게 느끼든 상관없이 그에게 표를 던졌으리라 확신했다. 그는 벌스트로드의 종복이 될 생각은 없었다. 다른 한편으로 타이크가 있었으니, 그는 전적으로 성직에 헌신하는 사람으로, 세인트 피터 교구의 한 부속 예배당의 보좌 사제에 지나지 않아 부수적인 직무를 맡을 시간이 있었다. 타이크 씨에 대해서는, 그를 도무지 견딜 수 없고 위선적인 말투를 쓴다고 의심한다는 것 말고는 누구도 흠잡을 말이 없었다. 정말이지, 그의 관점에서 보면 벌스트로드는 더없이 정당했다.\n\n그러나 리드게이트가 어느 쪽으로 기울기 시작하든, 그를 움찔하게 만드는 무언가가 있었다. 그리고 그는 자존심이 강한 사람이었던지라, 움찔할 수밖에 없는 처지가 다소 화가 났다. 그는 벌스트로드와 사이가 틀어져 자신의 가장 훌륭한 목표를 좌절시키고 싶지 않았다. 페어브라더에게 불리하게 표를 던져 그에게서 직무와 봉급을 빼앗는 데 거드는 것도 싫었다. 그리고 그 사십 파운드가 더 생긴다면 그 목사가 카드놀이에서 따려는 저 천박한 근심에서 벗어날 수 있지 않을까 하는 의문이 떠올랐다. 더욱이 리드게이트는 타이크에게 표를 던짐으로써 명백히 자기에게 편한 쪽에 표를 던지는 셈이 된다는 의식이 싫었다. 그러나 그 결과가 정말로 자기 편의를 위한 것일까? 다른 사람들은 그렇다고 말할 테고, 그가 자신을 중요한 인물로 만들어 출세하려고 벌스트로드의 비위를 맞춘다고 주장하리라. 그러면 또 어떤가? 그로서는 단지 자신의 개인적인 전망만이 걸린 문제였다면 은행가의 우정이든 적의든 썩은 호두 한 알만큼도 개의치 않았으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그가 정말로 마음을 쓴 것은 자기 일을 펼칠 매개이자 자기 생각을 실어 나를 수단이었다. 그러니 따지고 보면, 열병의 구체적인 구별을 입증하고 치료 결과를 시험할 수 있는 좋은 병원을 얻겠다는 목표를, 이 예배당 목사직과 관련된 그 무엇보다도 우선해야 마땅하지 않은가? 리드게이트는 처음으로 자질구레한 사회적 조건들이 실오라기처럼 옭아매는 압박과 그 좌절스러운 복잡함을 느끼고 있었다. 내적 토론 끝에 병원으로 출발할 때, 그의 희망은 사실 토론이 어떻게든 문제에 새로운 양상을 부여하여, 투표할 필요가 없도록 저울추를 기울게 해 주리라는 가능성에 걸려 있었다. 내 생각에 그는 또한 상황이 낳는 힘에도 약간 기대를 걸었던 것 같다. 어떤 감정이 따뜻하게 솟구쳐 결심을 쉽게 만들어 주리라는 기대 말이다. 냉정하게 따지는 토론은 그것을 오히려 더 어렵게 만들 뿐이었으니까. 어쨌든 그는 어느 편에 표를 던질지 스스로에게 분명히 말하지 않았다. 그리고 그러는 내내 그는 자기에게 강요된 굴종에 속으로 분개하고 있었다. 독립에 대한 순수한 결의와 선택받은 목표를 지닌 그가, 출발선에서부터 저마다 그에게 혐오스러운 옹졸한 양자택일의 손아귀에 붙들리게 되리라는 것은, 미리 보았다면 가소로울 만큼 어처구니없는 잘못된 논리처럼 여겨졌을 것이다. 학생 시절의 하숙방에서 그는 자신의 사회적 행보를 이와는 사뭇 다르게 미리 그려 보았던 것이다.\n\n리드게이트는 늦게 출발했지만, 스프레이그 박사와 다른 두 외과 의사, 그리고 몇몇 이사들은 일찍 도착해 있었다. 회계 담당이자 의장인 벌스트로드 씨는 아직 오지 않은 사람들 가운데 하나였다. 오가는 대화로 미루어 보아 결과가 불확실하고, 타이크 쪽의 다수가 일반적으로 짐작되던 것만큼 확실하지는 않은 듯했다. 놀랍게도 두 내과 의사는 의견이 일치한 것으로, 아니 오히려 생각은 달랐으나 행동에서는 뜻을 같이한 것으로 드러났다. 투박하고 묵직한 스프레이그 박사는 모두가 예견했던 대로 페어브라더 씨의 지지자였다. 박사는 종교가 없다는 의심을 단단히 받고 있었지만, 어찌 된 영문인지 미들마치는 마치 그가 대법관이라도 되는 양 그의 이 결함을 너그러이 보아 넘겼다. 실제로 영리함을 악의 원리와 결부 짓는 케케묵은 관념이, 주름 장식과 감상에 대해 가장 엄격한 관념을 지닌 부인 환자들의 마음속에서조차 여전히 힘을 떨치고 있었으니, 그의 직업적 무게는 오히려 그 때문에 더 믿어졌을 가능성이 크다. 어쩌면 박사의 이런 부정적인 면이 이웃들로 하여금 그를 냉철하고 무뚝뚝하게 재치 있는 사람이라 부르게 한 것인지도 모른다. 그런 기질의 결은 약물과 관련된 판단을 쌓아 두기에도 유리하다고 여겨졌다. 어쨌든 분명한 것은, 어떤 의사가 매우 확고한 종교적 견해를 지녔고 기도에 열심이며 그 밖에도 적극적인 신앙심을 드러낸다는 평판을 안고 미들마치에 왔더라면, 그의 의술에 대한 전반적인 의구심이 일었으리라는 점이다.\n\n이런 까닭에 민친 박사로서는 (직업적으로 말하자면) 그의 종교적 공감이 일반적인 종류여서, 특정 교리에 집착하기보다는 교회파든 비국교파든 모든 진지한 감정에 대해 멀찍이서 의학적 승인을 보내는 정도였다는 점이 다행스러웠다. 벌스트로드 씨가 곧잘 그러듯 교회의 존립이 걸린 것이라며 루터파의 칭의 교리를 고집하면, 민친 박사는 그에 맞서 인간이 한낱 기계나 우연히 모인 원자 덩어리가 아님을 자못 확신했다. 윔플 부인이 자신의 위장병과 관련해 특별한 섭리를 고집하면, 민친 박사는 자기로서는 정신의 창문을 열어 두기를 좋아하며 고정된 한계에는 반대한다고 했다. 유니테리언파 양조업자가 아타나시우스 신경을 두고 농을 던지면, 민친 박사는 포프의 「인간론」을 인용했다. 그는 스프레이그 박사가 즐기는 다소 거리낌 없는 일화 투의 화법에 반대했고, 권위 있는 인용구를 선호했으며 온갖 종류의 세련됨을 좋아했다. 그가 어느 주교와 친척 관계라는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이었고, 그는 이따금 \"주교관\"에서 휴일을 보내곤 했다.\n\n민친 박사는 손이 부드럽고 안색이 창백하며 윤곽이 둥글어서, 겉모습만으로는 온화한 성직자와 구별되지 않았다. 반면에 스프레이그 박사는 쓸데없이 키가 컸다. 그의 바지는 무릎이 구겨졌고, 단정한 차림새에 바짓단 끈이 필수처럼 여겨지던 시절에 부츠가 지나치게 드러나 보였다. 그가 드나들고 오르내리는 소리를 듣고 있노라면, 마치 지붕 잇는 일을 살피러 온 사람 같았다. 요컨대 그는 무게가 있어, 질병과 맞붙어 그것을 메다꽂으리라 기대할 만했고, 한편 민친 박사는 숨어 있는 질병을 찾아내 그것을 따돌리는 데 더 능할 법했다. 두 사람은 의학적 명성이라는 신비로운 특권을 거의 똑같이 누렸고, 서로의 의술에 대한 경멸을 깍듯한 예의로 감추었다. 자신들을 미들마치의 제도적 기둥으로 여기는 그들은, 모든 혁신가들에 맞서, 그리고 간섭하기 좋아하는 비전문가들에 맞서 손잡을 태세가 되어 있었다. 이런 점에서 두 사람은 마음속으로 똑같이 벌스트로드 씨를 꺼렸으니, 다만 민친 박사는 그와 공공연히 적대한 적이 없었고, 그와 의견이 다를 때면 언제나 벌스트로드 부인에게 정성껏 해명을 했는데, 그녀는 오직 민친 박사만이 자신의 체질을 이해한다고 여겼던 것이다. 의사들의 직업적 처신을 캐묻고 늘 자신의 개혁안을 들이미는 한 평신도는 — 계약을 맺고 극빈자들을 돌보는 외과 겸 약제사들에게보다는 이 두 내과 의사에게 직접적인 곤란을 덜 끼쳤지만 — 그럼에도 직업적 후각으로 보아 그 자체로 불쾌한 존재였다. 그리고 민친 박사는 벌스트로드가 리드게이트를 후원하기로 작심한 듯한 데서 비롯된, 그에 대한 새로운 반감에 충분히 동참하고 있었다. 오랜 세월 자리 잡은 개업의들인 렌치 씨와 톨러 씨는 마침 한쪽에 떨어져 다정하게 담소를 나누고 있었는데, 그 자리에서 그들은 리드게이트가 그야말로 벌스트로드의 속셈에 봉사하기 딱 알맞게 생겨먹은 건방진 애송이라는 데 의견을 모았다. 의료계 밖의 친구들에게 그들은 이미 입을 모아, 피콕 씨가 은퇴하면서 자기 자신의 자질과 다른 분야의 지식에 시간을 허비하지 않은 듯하다는 데서 끌어낼 수 있는 견실한 직업적 소양에 대한 논거 말고는 별다른 추천도 없이 이 도시에 들어온 그 다른 젊은 개업의를 칭찬했던 터였다. 리드게이트가 약을 조제하지 않음으로써 동료들에게 혐의를 뒤집어씌우려는 동시에, 일반 개업의로서의 자기 등급과 내과 의사들의 등급 사이의 경계를 흐리려 한다는 것은 분명했다. 내과 의사들은 직업의 이익을 위해 그 갖가지 등급을 지켜야 한다고 느꼈으니 — 특히 영국의 두 대학 어느 쪽에도 가 본 적 없어 그곳의 해부학 및 임상 수련의 결여라는 이점을 누리지 못한 채, 관찰이야 실로 풍부할지언정 도무지 건실하다고는 할 수 없는 에든버러와 파리에서 쌓았다는 비방거리가 될 만한 경험을 내세우며 온 사람에 대해서는 더욱 그러했다.\n\n이리하여 이번 일에서 벌스트로드는 리드게이트와 동일시되고, 리드게이트는 타이크와 동일시되었다. 그리고 예배당 목사직 문제를 두고 이렇게 서로 바꿔 쓸 수 있는 이름들이 다양했던 덕분에, 서로 다른 머리들이 그 문제에 대해 똑같은 판단에 이를 수 있었다.\n\n스프레이그 박사는 들어서면서 모여 있는 사람들에게 대뜸 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"나는 페어브라더에게 찬성이오. 봉급이야 진심으로 찬성하지요. 한데 어째서 그걸 목사한테서 빼앗는단 말이오? 그 사람은 가진 게 넉넉하지도 않은데 — 살림을 꾸리고 목사로서 자선을 베푸는 것 말고도 생명 보험까지 들어야 하지 않소. 그 주머니에 사십 파운드를 넣어 준대도 해 될 건 없소. 페어브라더는 좋은 친구요, 성직을 수행할 만큼만 목사티가 날 뿐 그 이상은 아니지요.\"\n\n\"허, 허! 박사 양반,\" 어느 정도 명망 있는 은퇴한 철물상 노인 파우더렐 씨가 말했는데, 그 감탄사는 웃음과 의회식 반대 표명의 중간쯤 되는 것이었다. \"그 말씀이야 하시게 둡시다. 하지만 우리가 고려해야 할 것은 누구의 수입이 아니라 — 가엾은 병든 사람들의 영혼이오.\" — 이 대목에서 파우더렐 씨의 목소리와 얼굴에는 진실한 비애가 어려 있었다. \"타이크 씨는 진정한 복음의 설교자요. 내가 타이크 씨를 반대하는 표를 던진다면 양심을 거스르는 게 될 거요 — 정말 그럴 거요.\"\n\n\"타이크 씨를 반대하는 쪽은 누구에게도 양심을 거슬러 표를 던지라고 한 적이 없을 텐데요.\" 유창한 화술의 부유한 무두장이 해크버트 씨가 말했는데, 번쩍이는 안경과 곧추선 머리카락이 다소 준엄하게 순진한 파우더렐 씨를 향하고 있었다. \"하지만 내 판단으로는, 우리는 이사로서 한쪽 진영에서만 나온 제안을 실행하는 것을 우리의 본분 전부로 여길지 말지를 숙고해야 마땅하오. 위원회의 어느 분이든, 이 도시의 모든 기관을 자기네 견해를 관철하기 위한 기계 장치쯤으로 여기는 성향을 지닌 자들이 부추기지 않았더라도, 여기서 늘 예배당 목사 직무를 수행해 온 신사분을 몰아낼 생각을 품었으리라고 단언할 수 있겠소? 나는 누구의 동기도 따지지 않겠소. 그건 그 사람 자신과 더 높은 권능 사이에 두라지요. 그러나 분명히 말하건대, 여기에는 진정한 독립과 양립할 수 없는 영향력이 작용하고 있으며, 비굴하게 굽실거리는 짓이란 으레, 그렇게 처신하는 신사들이 도덕적으로도 재정적으로도 차마 인정할 수 없는 사정에 따라 행해지는 법이오. 나 자신은 평신도이긴 하지만, 교회 내 분파에 대해서는 적잖이 관심을 기울여 왔고 —\"\n\n\"오, 그 분파들이고 뭐고 집어치우시오!\" 변호사 겸 읍 서기인 프랭크 홀리 씨가 불쑥 끼어들었는데, 그는 이사회에 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않다가 이제 채찍을 손에 든 채 바삐 들여다본 참이었다. \"여기서는 그런 것과 아무 상관 없소. 페어브라더가 — 있던 일이나마 — 보수도 없이 그 일을 해 왔고, 보수를 줄 거라면 마땅히 그에게 줘야 하오. 페어브라더에게서 그 일을 빼앗는 것은 그야말로 괘씸한 농간이라고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"신사 여러분께서 자신의 발언에 개인적인 빛깔을 입히지 않는 게 좋을 듯합니다.\" 플림데일 씨가 말했다. \"나는 타이크 씨의 임명에 찬성표를 던지겠소만, 해크버트 씨가 넌지시 일러 주지 않았더라면 내가 비굴하게 굽실거리는 자라는 걸 몰랐을 거요.\"\n\n\"나는 어떤 인신공격도 하지 않았소. 분명히 말했고, 다시 말하거나 하다 만 말을 마저 끝내도 된다면 —\"\n\n\"아, 여기 민친이 오는군요!\" 프랭크 홀리 씨가 말했다. 이에 모두가 해크버트 씨에게서 등을 돌렸고, 그는 미들마치에서 뛰어난 재능이란 부질없다는 것을 절감하게 되었다. \"어서 오시오, 박사. 당신은 꼭 옳은 편에 서 줘야겠소, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그러기를 바라오.\" 민친 박사가 이쪽저쪽으로 고개를 끄덕이고 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"내 감정이 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 말이오.\"\n\n\"여기에 감정이란 게 있다면, 그건 쫓겨나는 사람을 위한 감정이어야 한다고 보오만.\" 프랭크 홀리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히 나는 다른 쪽에도 감정이 있소. 내 존경심은 둘로 나뉘어 있지요.\" 민친 박사가 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"나는 타이크 씨를 모범적인 사람이라 여기오 — 그보다 더한 사람도 없지요 — 그리고 그가 흠잡을 데 없는 동기에서 추천되었다고 믿소. 나로서는 그에게 표를 줄 수 있다면 좋겠소. 하지만 나는 페어브라더 씨의 자격에 더 무게가 실리는 견해를 취하지 않을 수 없소. 그는 상냥한 사람이고 유능한 설교자이며 우리와 더 오래 함께해 왔으니 말이오.\"\n\n파우더렐 씨 노인은 슬프고 말없이 바라보았다. 플림데일 씨는 거북한 듯 자기 넥타이를 매만졌다.\n\n\"설마 페어브라더를 성직자의 본보기로 내세우시는 건 아니겠지요.\" 방금 들어온 저명한 운송업자 라처 씨가 말했다. \"나는 그에게 악감정은 없소만, 이런 임명에서는 더 높은 무엇은 차치하고라도 대중에 대해 우리가 빚진 바가 있다고 보오. 내 의견으로는 페어브라더는 성직자치고 너무 해이하오. 그를 두고 시시콜콜 들춰내고 싶지는 않소만, 그는 여기서도 출석이라는 걸 할 수 있는 한 적게 하려 들 거요.\"\n\n\"그게 지나친 것보다야 훨씬 낫지요.\" 그 일대에서 험한 말로 악명 높은 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"병든 사람들은 그렇게 많은 기도와 설교를 견뎌 내지 못하오. 게다가 그 감리교식 종교란 게 기분에 안 좋소 — 속에도 안 좋지요, 안 그렇소?\" 그는 모여 있는 네 명의 의사를 향해 휙 돌아서며 덧붙였다.\n\n그러나 세 신사가 들어서는 바람에 어떤 대답도 필요 없게 되었고, 그들과는 다소간 정중한 인사가 오갔다. 세 사람은 세인트 피터 교구의 주임 사제인 에드워드 데시저 목사와 벌스트로드 씨, 그리고 우리의 벗 티프턴의 브룩 씨였는데, 브룩 씨는 근래 자기 차례가 되어 이사진에 이름을 올리는 것을 허락한 터였으나 전에는 한 번도 출석한 적이 없었고, 이번 출석은 벌스트로드 씨의 노력 덕분이었다. 이제 기다리는 사람은 리드게이트뿐이었다.\n\n이제 모두 자리에 앉았고, 벌스트로드 씨가 여느 때처럼 창백하고 자제하는 모습으로 의장석에 앉았다. 온건한 복음주의자인 데시저 씨는 자기 벗인 타이크 씨의 임명을 바랐는데, 타이크 씨는 열성적이고 유능한 사람으로 부속 예배당에서 성직을 맡고 있어 돌볼 영혼이 그리 많지 않은 까닭에 새 직무에 쓸 시간이 넉넉했다. 이런 종류의 예배당 목사직은 열렬한 의도를 품고 맡아야 바람직했다. 그것은 영적 감화를 끼칠 특별한 기회였으니까. 그리고 봉급이 책정되는 것은 좋은 일이지만, 그만큼 이 직책이 한낱 봉급 문제로 변질되지 않도록 세심히 지켜보아야 할 필요가 있었다. 데시저 씨의 태도가 어찌나 조용한 예법을 갖추었던지 반대자들은 그저 잠자코 속으로 부글거릴 수밖에 없었다.\n\n브룩 씨는 이 문제에서 모두가 좋은 뜻을 품고 있다고 믿었다. 그 자신은 병원 일에 관여해 본 적이 없었지만, 미들마치에 이로운 것이라면 무엇이든 깊은 관심을 두었고, 어떤 공적 문제에 관해서든 자리에 모인 신사들을 만나게 되어 더없이 기뻤다 — \"어떤 공적 문제에 관해서든 말이오, 아시겠소.\" 브룩 씨는 완벽히 이해했다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 거듭 말했다. \"나는 치안 판사로서, 또 문서 증거를 수집하느라 꽤나 바쁘오만, 내 시간은 대중을 위해 쓸 수 있는 것이라 여기오 — 그리고 요컨대, 내 벗들이 봉급을 받는 예배당 목사 — 봉급 말이오, 아시겠소 — 가 아주 좋은 것이라고 나를 설득했고, 그래서 나는 이 자리에 와서 타이크 씨의 임명에 찬성표를 던질 수 있어 기쁘오. 내가 듣기로 그는 나무랄 데 없는 사람으로, 사도다운 데다 언변이 좋고 그 밖에도 그런 온갖 미덕을 갖춘 사람이라 하니 — 그러니 나는 결코 표를 아끼지 않을 사람이오 — 이런 상황에서는 말이오, 아시겠소.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔 브룩 씨께서 이 문제의 한쪽 이야기만 잔뜩 주입받으신 것 같소이다.\" 누구도 두려워하지 않으며 선거 운동의 속셈을 의심하는 토리당원인 프랭크 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"여기 우리 중에 가장 훌륭한 사람 하나가 여러 해 동안 보수도 없이 예배당 목사 직무를 해 왔는데, 그를 밀어내려고 타이크 씨가 추천되었다는 걸 모르시는 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 홀리 씨.\" 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다. \"브룩 씨는 페어브라더 씨의 성품과 처지에 대해 충분히 설명을 들으셨소.\"\n\n\"그의 적들한테서 말이지요.\" 홀리 씨가 발끈 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"여기에 개인적인 적대감이 끼어 있지 않기를 바라오.\" 데시저 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"끼어 있다는 데 맹세라도 하겠소이다.\" 홀리 씨가 되받아쳤다.\n\n\"신사 여러분,\" 벌스트로드 씨가 가라앉은 어조로 말했다. \"이 문제의 핵심은 아주 간략히 말씀드릴 수 있으며, 만약 표를 던지려는 신사 여러분 누구라도 충분한 설명을 듣지 못했다고 여기는 분이 있다면, 나는 지금이라도 양편에 두루 고려해야 할 점들을 다시 요약해 드릴 수 있소.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소용 있는지 모르겠소.\" 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"우리 모두 누구에게 표를 던질지 알고 있을 텐데 말이오. 정의를 행하고자 하는 사람이라면 마지막 순간까지 기다렸다가 양쪽 이야기를 듣는 법이 없소. 나는 허비할 시간이 없으니, 이 문제를 당장 표결에 부치자고 제안하오.\"\n\n각자가 종이쪽지에 \"타이크\"나 \"페어브라더\"라고 적어 유리잔에 밀어 넣기까지, 짧지만 그래도 후끈 달아오른 논의가 이어졌다. 그러는 사이 벌스트로드 씨는 리드게이트가 들어서는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"현재로서는 표가 똑같이 갈린 것으로 보입니다.\" 벌스트로드 씨가 또렷하고 쏘는 듯한 목소리로 말했다. 그러고는 리드게이트를 올려다보며 —\n\n\"아직 던질 결정표가 하나 남아 있습니다. 그건 당신 것이오, 리드게이트 씨. 부디 적어 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"이제 결판이 났군요.\" 렌치 씨가 자리에서 일어나며 말했다. \"리드게이트 씨가 어떻게 표를 던질지야 우리 모두 알고 있으니.\"\n\n\"어딘가 묘한 뜻을 담아 말씀하시는 것 같소이다.\" 리드게이트가 다소 도전적으로 말하며 연필을 든 채 멈추었다.\n\n\"단지 당신이 벌스트로드 씨와 같은 편에 표를 던지리라 예상된다는 뜻이오. 그 말이 모욕적으로 들리시오?\"\n\n\"다른 사람들에게는 모욕적일지도 모르지요. 하지만 그렇다고 해서 내가 그와 같은 편에 표를 던지기를 그만두지는 않을 거요.\" 리드게이트는 즉시 \"타이크\"라고 적어 넣었다.\n\n이리하여 월터 타이크 목사가 병원의 예배당 목사가 되었고, 리드게이트는 계속 벌스트로드 씨와 함께 일하게 되었다. 그는 타이크가 더 적합한 후보가 아닌지 정말로 확신이 서지 않았지만, 그러면서도 만약 자신이 간접적인 치우침에서 완전히 자유로웠다면 페어브라더 씨에게 표를 던졌으리라고 그의 양심은 일러 주었다. 예배당 목사직 일은, 미들마치의 이 옹졸한 매개가 그에게 너무도 버거웠던 사례로서 그의 기억 속에 쓰라린 응어리로 남았다. 어떻게 사람이 그런 양자택일 사이에서, 그런 상황 아래 내린 결정에 만족할 수 있겠는가? 그것은 시대의 형편이 그에게 내놓은 갖가지 모양 가운데서 골라 쓰는 모자에 만족할 수 없는 것이나 마찬가지였으니, 그 모자야 기껏해야 비교를 통해 겨우 지탱되는 체념으로나 쓰고 다닐 따름인 것이다.\n\n그러나 페어브라더 씨는 전과 다름없는 다정함으로 그를 대했다. 세리이자 죄인의 성품이 늘 현대판 바리새인의 성품과 실제로 양립할 수 없는 것은 아니니, 우리 대다수는 우리 자신의 논리의 허점이나 우리 자신의 농담의 시시함을 보지 못하는 것 못지않게 우리 자신의 행실의 흠도 좀처럼 또렷이 보지 못하기 때문이다. 그러나 세인트 보톨프의 교구 목사는 분명 바리새인의 기미를 털끝만큼도 띠지 않았으니, 자신도 다른 사람들과 너무나 다를 바 없다고 스스로 인정함으로써, 오히려 이 점에서 그들과 놀랍도록 달라진 것이다 — 즉, 그는 남들이 자기를 대수롭지 않게 여기는 것을 용서할 수 있었고, 자기에게 불리하게 작용하는 경우에조차 그들의 행실을 공정하게 판단할 수 있었다.\n\n\"세상이 나한테는 너무 강했지, 나도 알아.\" 어느 날 그가 리드게이트에게 말했다. \"하지만 따지고 보면 나는 대단한 인물이 아닐세 — 나는 결코 이름을 떨칠 사람이 못 돼. 헤라클레스의 선택이란 그럴듯한 우화지만, 프로디코스는 첫 결심만으로 충분하다는 듯 그 영웅에게 일을 쉽게 만들어 주지. 또 다른 이야기에서는 그가 실 잣는 물레를 잡게 되고, 끝내는 네소스의 옷을 입고 만다네. 모두의 결심이 한 사람을 도와준다면, 단 한 번의 굳은 결심이 그를 바른길에 붙들어 둘 수 있을지도 모르지.\"\n\n목사의 이야기가 늘 기운을 북돋아 주는 것은 아니었다. 그는 바리새인이 되는 것은 면했지만, 우리 자신의 실패에서 다소 성급하게 추론으로 끌어내는 저 가능성에 대한 낮은 평가만큼은 면하지 못했다. 리드게이트는 페어브라더 씨에게 가엾을 만큼 의지가 박약한 데가 있다고 생각했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX.\n\n조지 4세가 아직 윈저의 사사로운 영역을 다스리고 있었고, 웰링턴 공작이 수상이었으며, 빈시 씨가 미들마치 옛 자치 단체의 시장이던 시절, 도러시아 브룩으로 태어난 캐소본 부인은 신혼여행으로 로마를 찾았다. 그 시절 세상은 대체로 지금보다 사십 년만큼 선과 악에 더 무지했다. 여행자들은 머릿속에도 호주머니 속에도 기독교 미술에 관한 충분한 정보를 지니고 다니는 일이 드물었으며, 당대 가장 명석한 영국 비평가조차 승천하는 성모의 꽃으로 붉게 물든 무덤을 화가의 상상에서 비롯된 장식용 꽃병으로 오인할 정도였다. 어떤 무미건조한 공백을 사랑과 지식으로 채우는 데 일조한 낭만주의도 아직은 그 누룩으로 시대에 스며들어 모든 이의 양식 속으로 들어가지는 못했으니, 그것은 여전히 로마에 머무는 어떤 장발의 독일 화가들 사이에서 뚜렷이 구별되는 활기찬 열정으로 발효되고 있었고, 그들 곁에서 일하거나 빈둥거리던 다른 나라의 젊은이들이 이따금 그 번져 가는 운동에 휩쓸리곤 했다.\n\n어느 화창한 아침, 머리카락이 지나치게 길지는 않으나 숱이 많고 곱슬곱슬했으며 그 밖의 차림새는 영국식이던 한 젊은이가 바티칸의 벨베데레 토르소에서 막 등을 돌려, 인접한 둥근 현관에서 펼쳐지는 장엄한 산의 풍경을 내다보고 있었다. 그는 충분히 몰입해 있어서, 검은 눈에 활기 넘치는 한 독일인이 다가와 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 강한 억양으로 이렇게 말하는 것도 알아차리지 못했다. \"이리 와요, 빨리! 안 그러면 저 여자가 자세를 바꿔 버릴 테니.\"\n\n그 부름에 곧장 민첩함이 응했고, 두 사람은 멜레아그로스 상을 지나 가볍게 걸어, 그 무렵 클레오파트라라 불리던, 비스듬히 누운 아리아드네가 대리석의 관능적인 아름다움 속에 누워 있는 홀로 향했다. 옷자락이 꽃잎처럼 편안하고 부드럽게 그녀를 감싸고 있었다. 그들은 마침 그 누운 대리석상 가까이 받침대에 기대어 선 또 다른 형상을 볼 수 있었다. 숨 쉬며 피어나는 한 소녀, 그 자태는 아리아드네에 견주어도 부끄럽지 않았으며, 퀘이커교도 같은 회색 옷을 두르고 있었다. 목에서 여민 긴 망토는 양팔에서 뒤로 젖혀져 있었고, 장갑을 벗은 아름다운 한 손이 뺨을 받친 채, 소박하게 땋은 짙은 갈색 머리 둘레로 얼굴에 일종의 후광을 이루던 흰 비버 모자를 다소 뒤로 밀어 올리고 있었다. 그녀는 조각상을 바라보고 있지 않았고, 아마 그것을 생각하지도 않는 듯했다. 그녀의 커다란 눈은 바닥을 가로질러 비치는 한 줄기 햇살에 꿈꾸듯 고정되어 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 마치 클레오파트라를 응시하려는 듯 돌연 걸음을 멈춘 두 낯선 이를 의식하게 되었고, 그들을 바라보지도 않은 채 곧바로 몸을 돌려, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 홀을 따라 어슬렁거리던 하녀와 안내원에게로 향했다.\n\n\"저거야말로 멋진 대비라고 생각하지 않소?\" 독일인이 친구의 얼굴에서 공감 어린 감탄을 찾으면서도 다른 대답을 기다리지 않고 청산유수로 말을 이었다. \"저기 고대의 아름다움이 누워 있소. 죽어서도 시체 같지 않고, 관능적 완벽함의 충만한 만족 속에 멈춰 있지. 그리고 여기에는 숨 쉬는 생명 속의 아름다움이, 기독교 수 세기의 의식을 가슴에 품은 채 서 있소. 하지만 저 여자는 수녀처럼 차려입어야 해. 내가 보기엔 당신들이 퀘이커교도라 부르는 모습에 거의 가깝군. 내 그림에서는 그녀를 수녀로 입히겠소. 그렇지만 그녀는 결혼했어. 저 놀라운 왼손에 낀 결혼반지를 봤거든. 아니었으면 저 누르께한 성직자가 그녀의 아버지인 줄 알았을 거요. 한참 전에 그가 그녀와 헤어지는 걸 봤고, 방금 저 장엄한 자세로 있는 그녀를 발견했지. 생각해 보시오! 어쩌면 그는 부자이고, 그녀의 초상을 그리게 하고 싶어 할지도 모르오. 아! 그녀를 좇아 봐야 소용없군 — 저기 가 버리잖소! 그녀를 따라 집까지 갑시다!\"\n\n\"아니, 안 돼요.\" 그의 동행이 살짝 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n\"당신 별나군, 래디슬로. 충격이라도 받은 표정이야. 저 여자를 아시오?\"\n\n\"내 사촌과 결혼한 여자라는 건 압니다.\" 윌 래디슬로가 무언가에 골몰한 듯한 기색으로 홀을 따라 천천히 걸으며 말했고, 독일인 친구는 그의 곁에 붙어 열렬히 그를 살폈다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 그 성직자가? 그 사람은 외삼촌처럼 보이던데 — 좀 더 쓸모 있는 부류의 친척 말이오.\"\n\n\"그는 내 외삼촌이 아닙니다. 그는 내 육촌이라고 말했잖아요.\" 래디슬로가 다소 짜증스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"쇤, 쇤. 톡 쏘지 마시오. 내가 그 육촌 부인을 여태껏 본 가장 완벽한 젊은 마돈나라고 생각한다고 해서 화내는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"화내냐고요? 말도 안 돼요. 그녀를 본 건 전에 딱 한 번, 그것도 이삼 분뿐이었습니다. 내가 영국을 떠나기 직전에 사촌이 그녀를 소개해 줬을 때였죠. 그때는 결혼한 사이가 아니었어요. 그들이 로마에 오는 줄도 몰랐습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 이제 그들을 보러 가야지 — 이름을 아니까 주소가 뭔지 알아낼 수 있지 않소. 우체국에 가 볼까? 그러면 초상화 이야기를 꺼낼 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을, 나우만! 내가 어떻게 할지 나도 모르겠소. 난 당신만큼 뻔뻔하지 못하니까.\"\n\n\"흥! 그건 당신이 어설픈 호사가에 풋내기이기 때문이오. 당신이 화가였다면, 육촌 부인을 기독교적 정서로 생기를 얻은 고대의 형상으로 — 일종의 기독교적 안티고네로, 정신적 열정에 통제된 관능적 힘으로 여겼을 거요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그리고 당신이 그녀를 그리는 것이 그녀가 존재하는 주된 결실이라고도 여겼겠지 — 신성이 더 높은 완성으로 옮겨 가, 당신의 그 캔버스 조각을 덮는 행위에서 거의 소진되어 버린다고 말이오. 정 그렇다면 나는 풋내기로 두시오. 나는 온 우주가 당신 그림들의 모호한 의미를 향해 안간힘을 쓰고 있다고는 생각하지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇다오, 이 친구야! — 그것이 나 아돌프 나우만을 통해 안간힘을 쓰는 한에서는 말이지. 그건 확고하오.\" 사람 좋은 화가가 래디슬로의 어깨에 손을 얹으며, 상대의 말투에 깃든 까닭 모를 언짢음에는 조금도 동요하지 않고 말했다. \"자 보시오! 나의 존재는 온 우주의 존재를 전제로 하오 — 그렇지 않소? 그리고 내 본분은 그림을 그리는 것이고 — 화가로서 나는 당신의 큰이모님인지 둘째 할머니인지를 그림의 소재로 삼는, 전적으로 천재적인 착상을 품고 있소. 따라서 우주는 나라는 모양으로 내미는 바로 그 갈고리 혹은 발톱을 통해 그 그림을 향해 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 것이오 — 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그런데 나라는 모양을 한 또 다른 발톱이 그것을 방해하려고 안간힘을 쓴다면 어쩌겠소? — 그러면 문제가 좀 덜 단순해지지 않소.\"\n\n\"전혀 아니오. 그 다툼의 결과는 같은 것이오 — 그림이 되든 안 되든 — 논리적으로 말이지.\"\n\n윌은 이 흔들림 없는 기질에 당해 낼 수가 없었고, 얼굴에 드리웠던 먹구름이 환한 웃음으로 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"자 어서, 이 친구야 — 도와줄 거지?\" 나우만이 기대에 찬 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아니요. 말도 안 돼요, 나우만! 영국 숙녀들이 누구나 부른다고 모델이 되어 주는 건 아니오. 게다가 당신은 그림으로 너무 많은 걸 표현하려 들어요. 당신은 그저 어떤 감식가든 찬반의 이유를 제각기 댈 만한 배경을 깔아, 더 낫거나 못한 초상화 하나를 만들어 냈을 뿐일 거요. 게다가 한 여인의 초상이 다 무엇이란 말이오? 당신의 회화니 조형이니 하는 것은 결국 변변찮은 것들이오. 그것들은 관념을 고양하기는커녕 어지럽히고 무디게 만드니까. 언어가 더 섬세한 매체요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지, 그림을 못 그리는 사람들에게는 말이오.\" 나우만이 말했다. \"그 점에선 당신이 전적으로 옳소. 나는 당신에게 그림을 그리라고 권하지는 않았소, 친구.\"\n\n상냥한 화가는 가시를 품고 있었으나, 래디슬로는 찔린 티를 내려 하지 않았다. 그는 마치 듣지 못한 듯이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"언어는 더 충만한 이미지를 주는데, 모호한 까닭에 오히려 더 좋소. 결국 참된 봄이란 내면에 있고, 그림은 집요한 불완전함을 지닌 채 당신을 빤히 쳐다보지요. 나는 여인을 그린 것들에서 특히 그것을 느낍니다. 마치 여인이 한낱 색칠된 표면에 불과한 것처럼 말이오! 움직임과 음색을 기다려야 해요. 바로 그 숨결에도 차이가 있으니, 그들은 순간순간 달라지지요. 가령 방금 당신이 본 저 여인 말이오. 그녀의 목소리를 대체 어떻게 그리겠소? 한데 그녀의 목소리는 당신이 본 그녀의 그 무엇보다도 훨씬 더 신성하다오.\"\n\n\"알겠소, 알겠어. 당신은 질투하는 거요. 어느 누구도 당신의 이상을 그릴 수 있다고 감히 생각해선 안 되는 거지. 이거 심각하군, 친구! 당신의 큰이모라니! 비극적인 의미에서 '삼촌 노릇 하는 조카'라, 엄청나군!\"\n\n\"또 한 번 그 부인을 내 이모라고 부르면, 나우만, 우리 사이가 틀어질 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭐라고 불러야 하오?\"\n\n\"캐소본 부인이오.\"\n\n\"좋소. 당신이 반대하든 말든 내가 그녀와 알게 되어, 그녀가 자기를 그려 주기를 몹시 바란다는 걸 알아낸다면 어쩌겠소?\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇다면 어디 해 보시오!\" 윌 래디슬로가 그 화제를 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 경멸 어린 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 그는 우스울 만큼 사소한 일들에, 그것도 절반은 스스로 만들어 낸 일들에 짜증이 나 있음을 의식했다. 어째서 그는 캐소본 부인을 두고 이런 야단법석을 떨고 있단 말인가? 그러면서도 그는 그녀와 관련해 자신에게 무언가 일이 일어난 것 같은 느낌이 들었다. 누구도 함께 연기할 준비가 되어 있지 않은 극 속에서, 끊임없이 스스로를 위한 충돌과 매듭을 만들어 내는 성격들이 있다. 그들의 예민함은 천진하게 가만히 있을 뿐인 대상들에 부딪쳐 부서지는 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XX.\n\n버림받은 아이가 문득 깨어나 무서움에 찬 눈길로 주위 만물을 헤매다가 오직 보지 못함만을 볼 뿐, 사랑의 마주치는 눈길을.\n\n두 시간 뒤, 도러시아는 비아 시스티나에 있는 어느 멋진 거처의 안쪽 방, 곧 규방에 앉아 있었다.\n\n유감스럽게도 그녀는 비통하게 흐느끼고 있었다고 덧붙여야겠다. 자존심 때문에 자신을 늘 억제하고 남들을 배려하는 데 마음 쓰는 여인이, 안전하게 혼자라고 느낄 때 이따금 스스로에게 허락하는, 짓눌린 가슴을 풀어내는 그런 내맡김으로 말이다. 그리고 캐소본 씨는 한동안 바티칸에서 돌아오지 않을 것이 분명했다.\n\n하지만 도러시아에게는 스스로에게조차 분명히 말할 수 있는 또렷한 형태의 불만이 없었다. 혼란스러운 생각과 격정 한가운데서, 또렷함을 향해 솟아 나오려 애쓰던 정신적 행위는, 자신이 느끼는 황량함이 자신의 영적 빈곤 탓이라는 자책의 외침이었다. 그녀는 자신이 선택한 남자와 결혼했고, 대부분의 소녀들보다 유리한 점은 자신의 결혼을 무엇보다 새로운 의무의 시작으로 바라보았다는 데 있었다. 처음부터 그녀는 캐소본 씨가 자신보다 훨씬 높은 정신의 소유자라서, 자신이 온전히 함께할 수 없는 연구에 자주 매여 있을 수밖에 없으리라 여겼다. 게다가 짧고도 협소했던 처녀 시절의 경험을 지나, 이제 그녀는 눈에 보이는 역사의 도시 로마를 바라보고 있었으니, 그곳에서는 온 반구의 과거가 먼 곳에서 모아 온 기이한 조상들의 형상과 전리품을 거느리고 장송 행렬처럼 움직이는 듯했다.\n\n하지만 이 어마어마한 파편성은 그녀의 신혼 생활의 꿈결 같은 낯섦을 한층 더 짙게 만들었다. 도러시아는 이제 로마에 온 지 다섯 주가 되었고, 가을과 겨울이 행복한 노부부처럼 손을 맞잡고 함께 걷는 듯한—머지않아 그중 하나는 더 쌀쌀한 외로움 속에 홀로 남게 될—온화한 아침이면, 처음에는 캐소본 씨와 함께, 그러나 근래에는 주로 탠트립과 노련한 안내인과 함께 마차를 타고 돌아다녔다. 그녀는 최고의 미술관들을 두루 안내받았고, 주요한 전망 지점들로 인도되었으며, 가장 웅장한 폐허와 가장 영광스러운 교회들을 구경했지만, 결국 가장 자주 택하게 된 것은 마차를 몰아 캄파냐로 나가는 일이었다. 그곳에서는 여러 시대가 빚어내는 답답한 가장무도회에서 벗어나 대지와 하늘과 더불어 홀로 있는 듯한 느낌을 받을 수 있었으니, 그 가장무도회 속에서는 그녀 자신의 삶마저도 수수께끼 같은 의상을 걸친 가면극이 되어 버리는 것 같았다.\n\n모든 역사적 형상에 자라나는 영혼을 불어넣고, 모든 대조를 잇는 억눌린 이행의 자취를 더듬어 내는 지식의 생기 어린 힘으로 로마를 바라본 이들에게는, 로마가 여전히 세계의 영적 중심이자 해석자일 수 있으리라. 하지만 그들로 하여금 한 가지 역사적 대조를 더 떠올리게 하라. 그 제국과 교황의 도시가 보여 주는 거대하고 부서진 계시들이, 영국과 스위스의 청교도주의 속에서 자라났고, 빈약한 신교도 역사서와 주로 부채에 그려진 그림 같은 미술로 양육된 한 소녀의 관념 위로 느닷없이 들이닥치는 것을. 그 소녀는 열렬한 천성으로 자신이 가진 보잘것없는 지식의 몫을 모두 원칙으로 바꾸고, 자신의 행동을 그 틀 속으로 녹여 부었으며, 빠르게 솟는 감정으로 가장 추상적인 것들에까지 기쁨이나 고통의 성질을 부여하던 소녀였다. 또한 최근에 아내가 되어, 시험해 보지 않은 의무를 열정적으로 받아들였다가 어느새 자신의 개인적 운명에 대한 격동하는 사로잡힘 속으로 빠져든 소녀였다. 알 수 없는 로마의 무게는, 그곳을 영국과 외국 사교계가 펼치는 화려한 소풍의 배경쯤으로 여기는 명랑한 요정들에게라면 가볍게 얹혔을지도 모른다. 하지만 도러시아에게는 깊은 인상에 맞설 그런 방어막이 없었다. 폐허와 바실리카, 궁전과 거상들이, 살아 있고 따뜻한 피가 도는 모든 것이 경외심과 결별한 미신의 깊은 타락 속에 가라앉은 듯한 누추한 현재 한복판에 놓여 있었다. 벽과 천장 위에서는 한결 흐릿하지만 여전히 열렬한 타이탄적 생명이 응시하며 분투하고 있었고, 길게 늘어선 하얀 형상들의 대리석 눈은 낯선 세계의 단조로운 빛을 머금은 듯했다. 육체적이고 영적인 야심 찬 이상들이 빚어낸 이 거대한 잔해 전부가, 숨 쉬는 망각과 타락의 징표들과 어지러이 뒤섞여, 처음에는 전기 충격을 받은 듯 그녀를 거슬리게 했고, 이윽고 감정의 흐름을 막아 버리는 혼란스러운 관념의 과잉에 따르는 그 통증으로 그녀를 짓눌렀다. 창백하면서도 빛나는 형상들이 그녀의 젊은 감각을 사로잡았고, 그녀가 그것들을 생각하지 않을 때조차 기억 속에 박혀, 그녀의 훗날 여러 해 동안 남게 될 기이한 연상들을 마련하고 있었다. 우리의 기분은 선잠 속 환등기 그림처럼 연이어 떠오르는 심상들을 데려오기 마련이다. 도러시아는 평생토록, 어떤 둔탁하고 쓸쓸한 상태에 빠질 때면 성 베드로 대성당의 광대함과, 거대한 청동 천개와, 위쪽 모자이크 속 예언자들과 복음서 저자들의 자세와 의복에 깃든 흥분한 의도, 그리고 성탄절을 위해 걸리던 붉은 휘장이 망막의 질병처럼 사방에 번지던 모습을 계속 보게 되었다.\n\n도러시아의 이 내면적 경악이 그리 유별난 것은 아니었다. 많은 영혼들이 젊은 알몸 그대로 온갖 부조화 속으로 내던져진 채, 어른들이 제 일을 보러 다니는 동안 그것들 사이에서 \"제 발로 서는 법\"을 알아내도록 내버려진다. 또 캐소본 부인이 결혼 육 주 만에 울음을 터뜨린 모습으로 발견된다고 해서, 그 상황이 비극으로 여겨지리라고 나는 생각하지 않는다. 상상했던 미래를 대신하는 새로운 현실의 미래 앞에서 느끼는 약간의 낙담, 약간의 가슴 내려앉음은 드문 일이 아니며, 우리는 사람들이 드물지 않은 일에 깊이 동요하리라고 기대하지 않는다. 바로 그 빈번함이라는 사실 자체에 깃든 비극의 요소는, 아직 인류의 거친 감정 속으로 스며들지 못했다. 그리고 어쩌면 우리의 몸은 그것을 많이 견뎌 내기 어려울 것이다. 만약 우리가 모든 평범한 인간의 삶을 예리하게 보고 느낄 수 있다면, 그것은 풀이 자라는 소리와 다람쥐의 심장이 뛰는 소리를 듣는 것과 같으리니, 우리는 침묵의 저편에 도사린 그 굉음에 그만 죽고 말 것이다. 사정이 이러하므로, 우리 가운데 가장 영민한 자조차 어리석음으로 두툼하게 둘러싸인 채 살아간다.\n\n어쨌든 도러시아는 울고 있었다. 만약 그 이유를 대라고 했다면, 그녀는 내가 이미 사용한 것과 같은 두루뭉술한 말로밖에 대답할 수 없었으리라. 더 구체적으로 말하도록 떠밀렸다면 그것은 빛과 그림자의 내력을 들려주려 애쓰는 일과 같았을 터인데, 상상을 대신해 가던 그 새로운 현실의 미래는, 이제 그와 결혼한 처지에서 캐소본 씨와 아내로서의 관계를 바라보는 그녀의 시각이 처녀 시절의 꿈속에서와는 다른 것으로 시곗바늘의 은밀한 움직임처럼 차츰 변해 가게 만드는 끝없는 사소함들에서 그 재료를 끌어왔기 때문이다. 그 변화를 온전히 인식하거나 적어도 인정하기에는 아직 너무 일렀고, 그녀의 정신적 삶에 너무도 필수적인 부분이어서 조만간 분명 되찾으리라 거의 확신하던 그 헌신을 다시 가다듬기에는 더더욱 일렀다. 영속적인 반항, 사랑으로 가득한 경건한 결심이 없는 삶의 무질서란 그녀에게 가능한 일이 아니었다. 하지만 그녀는 지금 자기 천성의 바로 그 힘이 혼란을 한층 키우는 그런 간극에 놓여 있었다. 이런 식으로 결혼 초기의 몇 달은 흔히 위기의 격동기가 되는데—그것이 새우 웅덩이의 동요든 더 깊은 물의 동요든—이윽고 그것은 가라앉아 명랑한 평온으로 잦아든다.\n\n하지만 캐소본 씨가 예전만큼 박식하지 않았던가? 그의 표현 방식이 달라졌던가, 아니면 그의 감정이 덜 갸륵해졌던가? 오, 여인됨의 변덕이여! 그의 연대기 지식이 그를 저버렸던가, 아니면 어떤 이론뿐 아니라 그 이론을 주창한 이들의 이름까지 댈 줄 아는 능력이 사라졌던가, 아니면 요청만 하면 어떤 주제든 그 요점들을 짚어 줄 채비가 모자랐던가? 그리고 로마야말로 그러한 재능을 마음껏 펼칠 만한, 세상에 둘도 없는 곳이 아니었던가? 게다가 도러시아의 열정은, 위대한 과업이 그것을 이루어야 할 자에게 얹는 무게와 어쩌면 슬픔을 덜어 주리라는 전망에 특히 머물러 있지 않았던가? 그리고 그러한 무게가 캐소본 씨를 짓누르고 있다는 사실은 전보다 더욱 분명할 뿐이었다.\n\n이 모든 것은 사람을 짓누르는 물음들이다. 하지만 다른 무엇이 그대로 남아 있든, 빛이 달라져 버렸으니, 정오에 진주빛 새벽을 찾을 수는 없는 법이다. 변할 수 없는 사실인즉, 구애라 불리는 몇 주간의 상상에 찬 등장과 퇴장을 통해서만 그 천성을 알게 된 동료 인간은, 결혼 생활의 연속성 속에서 보았을 때 그대가 미리 그려 두었던 것보다 더 나은 무엇으로, 혹은 더 못한 무엇으로 드러날 수는 있어도, 결코 완전히 똑같이 보이지는 않으리라는 것이다. 그리고 만약 그것에 견줄 만한 비슷한 변화들을 우리가 겪어 보지 못했다면, 그 변화가 얼마나 빨리 느껴지는지를 알고 깜짝 놀랄 것이다. 명석한 만찬 동석자와 한 거처를 나누어 쓰거나, 좋아하던 정치가가 내각에 들어간 모습을 보는 일도 꼭 그만큼이나 빠른 변화를 가져올 수 있다. 이런 경우들에서도 우리는 거의 알지 못하면서 많이 믿는 데서 시작했다가, 때로는 그 양을 뒤집어 놓으며 끝나기도 한다.\n\n그렇더라도 그런 비교는 사람을 오도할 수 있으니, 캐소본 씨만큼 번지르르한 허울을 꾸며 낼 줄 모르는 사람도 없었기 때문이다. 그는 어떤 되새김질하는 짐승 못지않게 진실한 인물이었고, 자신에 관한 어떤 환상도 적극적으로 빚어내는 데 가담하지 않았다. 그렇다면 결혼 이후 몇 주 동안 도러시아가, 남편의 정신 속에서 발견하리라 꿈꾸었던 드넓은 전망과 광활하고 신선한 공기가, 어디로도 통하지 않는 듯한 대기실들과 구불구불한 통로들로 대체되었음을, 또렷이 관찰하지는 못했으되 숨 막히는 우울 속에서 느끼게 된 것은 어찌 된 일이었을까? 짐작건대 구애 기간에는 모든 것이 잠정적이고 예비적인 것으로 여겨지고, 미덕이나 재능의 가장 작은 표본조차 결혼이 주는 넉넉한 여유가 드러내 줄 황홀한 보고를 보증하는 것으로 받아들여지기 때문이리라. 하지만 결혼의 문지방을 일단 넘고 나면, 기대는 현재에 집중된다. 일단 결혼이라는 항해에 올라타고 나면, 자신이 한 발짝도 나아가지 못하고 바다는 시야에 들어오지도 않는다는 것을—사실은 자신이 닫힌 못 안을 탐사하고 있다는 것을—깨닫지 않을 도리가 없다.\n\n결혼 전 대화에서 캐소본 씨는 종종 도러시아가 그 의미를 알 수 없는 어떤 설명이나 의문스러운 세부에 머물러 있곤 했다. 하지만 그처럼 불완전한 일관성은 그들 교제의 단편적인 성격 탓인 듯했고, 그들의 미래에 대한 믿음에 기대어, 그녀는 캐소본 씨의 전적으로 새로운 견해—블레셋의 신 다곤과 그 밖의 물고기 신들에 관한 견해—에 맞서 제기될 수 있는 반론들을 늘어놓는 이야기를 열렬한 인내심으로 경청했다. 그를 그토록 절실히 사로잡는 이 주제를, 훗날 자신도 그것이 그에게 그토록 중요해진 그 높은 자리에서 똑같이 바라보게 되리라 생각하면서 말이다. 또한 그녀에게는 가장 가슴 떨리는 생각들을 그가 당연하다는 듯한 진술과 일축하는 어조로 다루는 것도, 약혼 기간에 그녀 자신도 함께 나누었던 서두름과 골몰의 감각에 속한 것으로 쉽게 설명되었다. 하지만 이제, 로마에 온 뒤로 그녀의 모든 감정의 심연이 격동하는 활동으로 일깨워지고, 새로운 요소들로 인해 삶이 새로운 문제로 변하면서, 그녀는 어떤 두려움과 더불어 점점 더 또렷이 깨닫게 되었다. 자기 마음이 끊임없이 내면적인 분노와 반감의 발작으로, 그렇지 않으면 쓸쓸한 권태로 미끄러져 들어가고 있다는 것을. 분별 있던 후커나 다른 어떤 박학의 영웅이라 해도 캐소본 씨 나이에 이르러서는 마찬가지였을지 그녀로서는 알 길이 없었으니, 그는 비교의 덕을 볼 수도 없었다. 다만 그들 주위의 기이하리만치 인상적인 대상들에 대해 남편이 논평하는 방식은, 그녀에게 일종의 정신적 전율을 일으키기 시작했다. 그는 어쩌면 자신의 소임을 훌륭히 해내려는 더없이 좋은 의도를 품었겠지만, 그것은 오직 제 소임만을 해내려는 것이었다. 그녀의 마음에는 신선한 것이 그에게는 닳아 버린 것이었다. 인류의 보편적 삶에 의해 그의 내면에서 한때 자극받았던 사유와 감정의 역량은, 이미 오래전에 일종의 메마른 표본으로, 생기 없이 박제된 지식으로 쪼그라들어 있었다.\n\n그가 \"이게 재미있소, 도러시아? 좀 더 머물까요? 당신이 원한다면 나는 머물 준비가 되어 있소\"라고 말했을 때, 그녀에게는 가는 것이나 머무는 것이나 똑같이 음울하게 느껴졌다. 혹은 \"파르네시나에 가 보겠소, 도러시아? 거기에는 라파엘로가 도안했거나 그렸다는 유명한 프레스코화가 있는데, 대다수 사람들이 방문할 만한 가치가 있다고 여기지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 그것들에 관심이 있나요?\"라는 것이 도러시아의 한결같은 물음이었다.\n\n\"내 생각에 그것들은 매우 높이 평가받고 있소. 그중 일부는 큐피드와 프시케의 우화를 묘사한 것인데, 그것은 아마 어느 문학 시대의 낭만적 창작일 뿐, 내 생각에는 진정한 신화적 산물로 칠 수는 없소. 하지만 당신이 이 벽화들을 좋아한다면 손쉽게 그리로 마차를 몰 수 있소. 그러면 당신은, 내 생각에, 라파엘로의 주요 작품들을 다 본 셈이 될 텐데, 로마를 방문하면서 그 어느 하나라도 빠뜨린다면 애석한 일이지요. 그는 가장 완벽한 형태의 우아함과 표현의 숭고함을 결합한 화가로 여겨져 왔소. 적어도 그것이 감식가들의 견해라고 나는 들었소.\"\n\n성직자가 전례서에 따라 낭독하듯 절도 있고 공적인 어조로 주어진 이런 종류의 대답은, 영원의 도시가 지닌 영광을 정당화하는 데에도, 또 그녀가 그것들에 대해 더 알게 되면 세상이 그녀를 위해 기쁘게 빛나리라는 희망을 주는 데에도 도움이 되지 못했다. 젊고 열정적인 존재에게 있어, 지식으로 가득 찬 세월이 흥미나 공감의 텅 빈 부재로 귀결된 듯한 정신과 접하는 것보다 더 의기소침하게 하는 일은 거의 없다.\n\n물론 다른 주제들에 대해서는 캐소본 씨도 끈질긴 몰두와, 흔히 열정의 효과로 여겨지는 열의를 보였고, 도러시아는 자신이 그를 그것에서 끌어낸다는 느낌을 받기보다는, 그의 생각이 저절로 향하는 그 방향을 따르고 싶어 안달했다. 하지만 그녀는 그를 따라가는 곳에서 어떤 드넓은 전망이 열리리라는, 예전의 즐거운 확신을 차츰 잃어 가고 있었다. 가엾은 캐소본 씨 자신도 작은 골방들과 구불구불한 계단 사이에서, 그리고 카비리에 관한 어수선한 모호함 속에서, 혹은 다른 신화학자들의 사려 깊지 못한 유비를 폭로하는 일에 빠져, 자신을 이런 노고로 몰아넣었던 목적을 손쉽게 놓쳐 버렸다. 가느다란 초를 앞에 켜 둔 채 그는 창문이 없다는 것을 잊었고, 태양신들에 관한 다른 이들의 견해에 신랄한 필사본 주석을 다는 동안, 그는 햇빛에 무관심해져 버렸다.\n\n캐소본 씨에게 뼈처럼 고착되어 변할 수 없는 이러한 특성들은, 만약 그가 도러시아로 하여금 처녀다운, 여인다운 감정을 쏟아 내도록 북돋아 주었더라면—만약 그가 그녀의 두 손을 자기 손 사이에 쥐고서, 그녀의 경험을 이루는 모든 자질구레한 사연들을 다정함과 이해의 기쁨으로 귀 기울여 들어 주고, 자기도 그녀에게 똑같은 종류의 친밀함을 되돌려 주어, 두 사람 각자의 지난 삶이 서로의 앎과 애정 속에 포함될 수 있게 해 주었더라면—혹은 그녀가, 대머리 인형의 딱딱한 머리통에 입맞춤을 퍼붓고 자신의 풍부한 사랑으로 그 나무토막 속에 행복한 영혼을 빚어내는 일로 시작하는 모든 다정한 여인의 천성인, 그러한 어린아이 같은 애무로 자신의 애정을 길러 낼 수 있었더라면, 도러시아에게 더 오래 느껴지지 않은 채 남아 있었을지도 모른다. 그것이 도러시아의 천성이었다. 멀리 있는 것을 알고 싶고 널리 자애로워지고 싶은 온갖 갈망에도 불구하고, 그녀에게는 가까이 있는 것을 향한 열정 또한 충분해서, 캐소본 씨가 받아들인다는 어떤 다른 표시만 보여 주었더라면 그의 외투 소매에 입을 맞추거나 그의 신발 끈을 어루만졌을 것이다. 하지만 그는 변함없는 예법으로, 그녀가 더없이 다정하고 진실로 여성적인 천성을 지녔노라고 선언하는 것 외에는 다른 표시를 하지 않았고, 동시에 그녀에게 정중히 의자를 끌어다 주는 것으로 자신이 그러한 표현들을 다소 거칠고 당혹스러운 것으로 여긴다는 것을 드러냈다. 아침에 성직자다운 몸단장을 빈틈없이 마친 그는, 그 시대의 잘 맞춰진 빳빳한 크라바트와 출판되지 않은 사안들로 무거운 정신에 어울리는 그런 생활의 예의범절에만 대비하고 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n그리고 슬픈 모순에 의해, 도러시아의 생각과 결심은 따뜻한 물결—본디 그것들도 그 물결의 또 다른 형태일 뿐이었던—속에 떠다니다 사라지는 녹아내리는 얼음 같았다. 그녀는 마치 그 매개를 통하지 않고는 아무것도 알 수 없다는 듯, 자신이 한낱 감정의 희생자임을 깨닫고 굴욕을 느꼈다. 그녀의 모든 힘은 동요와 분투와 낙담의 발작으로, 그러다가 또다시 더 완전한 체념의 환영—모든 가혹한 조건을 의무로 탈바꿈시키는—으로 흩어져 버렸다. 가엾은 도러시아! 그녀는 분명 성가신 존재였으니—주로 자기 자신에게 그러했다. 하지만 이날 아침 처음으로 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게 성가신 존재가 되었다.\n\n그녀는 커피를 마시는 동안, 스스로 마음속으로 이기심이라 부르던 것을 떨쳐 버리겠다는 결심으로 시작하여, 남편이 다음과 같이 말했을 때 온통 명랑한 주의로 가득 찬 얼굴을 그에게 돌렸다. \"사랑하는 도러시아, 이제 우리는 떠날 준비로서 아직 마치지 못한 모든 일을 생각해야 하오. 더 일찍 집으로 돌아가 성탄절을 로윅에서 보낼 수 있었으면 했지만, 이곳에서의 조사가 예상했던 기간을 넘겨 길어졌소. 그러나 이곳에서 보낸 시간이 당신에게 불쾌하지 않았기를 바라오. 유럽의 명소들 가운데 로마는 늘 가장 인상적이며 어떤 점에서는 교화적인 곳 가운데 하나로 여겨져 왔소. 나는 처음 이곳을 방문했을 때 그것을 내 생애의 한 획으로 여겼던 것을 잘 기억하고 있소. 나폴레옹의 몰락 이후였는데, 그것은 대륙을 여행자들에게 열어 준 사건이었지요. 사실 로마는 극단적인 과장이 적용된 몇몇 도시 가운데 하나라고 생각하오—'로마를 보고 죽으라'는 말 말이오. 하지만 당신의 경우에는 내가 수정을 제안하여 이렇게 말하고 싶소, 신부로서 로마를 보고, 이후로는 행복한 아내로 살라고.\"\n\n캐소본 씨는 이 짧은 연설을 더없이 양심적인 의도로 발음하면서, 눈을 조금 깜박이고 고개를 위아래로 끄덕이다가 미소로 마무리했다. 그는 결혼이 황홀한 상태임을 발견하지는 못했으나, 매력적인 젊은 여인을 그녀가 마땅히 누려야 할 만큼 행복하게 해 줄, 나무랄 데 없는 남편 외의 다른 무엇이 될 생각은 전혀 없었다.\n\n\"당신이 우리의 체류에 진정으로 만족하시길 바라요—제 말은, 당신의 연구에 관한 한 그동안의 성과에 말이에요.\" 도러시아는 남편에게 가장 절실한 것에 마음을 붙들어 두려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 캐소본 씨는 그 단어를 절반쯤 부정으로 만드는 그 독특한 음조로 말했다. \"예상했던 것보다 더 멀리까지 이끌려 갔고, 주석을 달 만한 갖가지 주제들이 떠올랐는데, 직접적으로 필요하지는 않았으나 그냥 지나칠 수가 없었소. 이 작업은 필사 조수의 도움에도 불구하고 다소 고된 일이었지만, 당신과 함께한 덕분에 다행히도 연구 시간을 넘겨서까지 너무 끊임없이 생각을 밀어붙이는 일은 막을 수 있었소. 그것은 내 고독한 삶의 덫이었지요.\"\n\n\"제가 곁에 있어서 당신에게 조금이나마 변화가 생겼다니 무척 기뻐요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. 그녀는 캐소본 씨의 정신이 낮 동안 너무 깊이 가라앉아 다시는 수면으로 떠오르지 못하리라 여겨졌던 여러 저녁들을 생생히 기억하고 있었다. 그녀의 대답에는 약간의 노여움이 섞여 있었던 듯하다. \"로윅에 가게 되면, 제가 당신에게 더 도움이 되고, 당신이 관심을 두는 일에 조금 더 들어갈 수 있게 되기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그럴 거요, 여보.\" 캐소본 씨가 가볍게 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"여기서 내가 적어 둔 노트들은 추려 낼 필요가 있을 텐데, 원한다면 내 지시에 따라 그것들을 발췌해도 좋소.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신의 그 모든 노트들 말이에요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. 이 주제에 대해 이미 그녀의 마음이 안에서 뜨겁게 타올랐던 터라, 이제는 입 밖으로 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"그 모든 책더미들—이제 당신이 늘 말씀하시던 그 일을 하지 않으시겠어요?—그것들 중 어느 부분을 쓸지 마음을 정하고, 당신의 방대한 지식을 세상에 유용하게 만들어 줄 그 책을 쓰기 시작하지 않으시겠어요? 제가 당신의 구술을 받아 적을게요, 아니면 당신이 일러 주시는 것을 옮겨 적고 발췌할게요. 저는 그것 말고는 아무 쓸모가 없으니까요.\" 도러시아는 도무지 설명할 길 없는, 어둑하게 여성적인 태도로, 가벼운 흐느낌과 눈물 가득한 눈으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n이렇게 드러난 지나친 감정만으로도 캐소본 씨에게는 몹시 거슬렸을 테지만, 도러시아의 말이 그가 들을 수 있었을 가장 날카롭고 짜증스러운 말 가운데 하나였던 데에는 다른 이유들도 있었다. 그가 그녀의 내면적 고뇌에 눈멀어 있듯, 그녀도 그의 고뇌에 눈멀어 있었다. 그녀는 우리의 연민을 요하는 남편 내면의 그 숨은 갈등들을 아직 알아채지 못했다. 그녀는 아직 그의 심장 박동에 인내심 있게 귀 기울인 적이 없었고, 그저 자기 심장이 격렬히 뛰는 것만을 느낄 뿐이었다. 캐소본 씨의 귀에는, 도러시아의 목소리가 의식의 그 어렴풋한 암시들—한낱 망상이라고, 과장된 예민함이 빚어낸 환각이라고 설명할 수도 있었던—을 큰 소리로 강조하며 되풀이하고 있었다. 그러한 암시들이 외부로부터 분명하게 되풀이될 때면, 우리는 언제나 그것들을 잔인하고 부당하다며 물리친다. 우리는 자신의 굴욕스러운 고백이 온전히 받아들여지는 것에도 화가 나는 법인데—하물며 가까이서 지켜보는 자의 입술로부터, 우리가 병적인 것이라 부르려 애쓰고 마비의 엄습인 양 맞서 싸우는 그 어수선한 웅얼거림을, 단단하고 또렷한 음절로 듣는 것은 얼마나 더 화가 나겠는가! 그런데 이 잔인한 외부의 고발자가 아내의 모습으로—아니, 젊은 신부의 모습으로 거기 있었다. 그 신부는 그의 무수한 펜 자국과 넉넉한 종이 더미를, 고상한 마음씨를 지닌 카나리아처럼 비판 없는 경외로 바라보기는커녕, 음험한 추론의 힘으로 모든 것을 감시하는 첩자로 제 모습을 드러내는 듯했다. 바로 이 특정한 방위를 향해서, 캐소본 씨는 도러시아에 못지않은 예민함과, 사실 이상을 상상해 내는 그녀와 똑같은 민첩함을 지니고 있었다. 그는 예전에 올바른 대상을 숭배할 줄 아는 그녀의 능력을 흡족하게 지켜보았으나, 이제 갑작스러운 두려움과 함께 예감했다. 그 능력이 주제넘음으로 대체되고, 그 숭배가 모든 비판 가운데 가장 부아를 돋우는 것—훌륭한 목표들을 막연하게 잔뜩 보면서도 그것에 이르는 데 무엇이 드는지는 조금도 알지 못하는 비판—으로 바뀔지도 모른다고.\n\n도러시아가 그를 알게 된 이래 처음으로, 캐소본 씨의 얼굴에 성난 홍조가 빠르게 번졌다.\n\n\"여보.\" 그가 예법으로 짜증을 억누른 채 말했다. \"무지한 구경꾼들의 섣부른 억측으로 가늠할 수 없는 작업의 여러 단계에 맞춰, 그 시기와 적기를 아는 일은 나를 믿고 맡겨도 좋소. 근거 없는 의견의 신기루로 일시적인 효과를 거두는 것쯤은 나로서도 쉬운 일이었소. 하지만 가장 작은 성취만을 시도하며—실로 그 외의 것에는 채비도 되어 있지 않은—수다쟁이들의 조급한 경멸을 받는 것은, 양심적인 탐구자가 늘 겪는 시련이오. 그리고 그러한 자들 모두가, 그 진정한 본질이 자기들 손이 닿는 범위를 완전히 벗어난 판단과, 그 요소들을 좁고 피상적인 일별로 파악할 수 있는 판단을 구별하도록 깨우침을 받는다면 좋으련만.\"\n\n이 연설은 캐소본 씨로서는 자못 이례적인 활기와 능란함으로 전달되었다. 사실 그것은 전적인 즉흥은 아니었고, 내면의 독백 속에서 형태를 갖추었다가, 갑작스러운 열기가 과일을 터뜨릴 때 둥근 씨앗들이 쏟아져 나오듯 튀어나온 것이었다. 도러시아는 그의 아내일 뿐만 아니라, 인정받는 작가나 의기소침한 작가를 에워싼 그 천박한 세상의 화신이었다.\n\n이번에는 도러시아가 분개했다. 그녀는 남편의 주된 관심사와 어떤 동지애로 결합되고 싶다는 소망 외에는 자기 안의 모든 것을 억눌러 오지 않았던가?\n\n\"제 판단은 정말이지 매우 피상적인 것이었어요—제가 형성할 수 있는 정도의 것이지요.\" 그녀는 미리 연습할 필요도 없었던 즉각적인 분노로 대답했다. \"당신은 제게 그 노트들의 줄지어 선 모습을 보여 주셨고—그것들에 대해 자주 말씀하셨고—그것들이 정리될 필요가 있다고 자주 말씀하셨어요. 하지만 저는 출판될 그 저술에 대해 당신이 말씀하시는 것은 한 번도 들은 적이 없어요. 그것들은 매우 단순한 사실들이었고, 제 판단은 그 이상으로 나아가지 않았어요. 저는 그저 제가 당신에게 조금이나마 도움이 되게 해 달라고 청했을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n도러시아가 식탁을 떠나려 일어섰고, 캐소본 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않은 채, 곁에 놓여 있던 편지를 다시 읽기라도 하려는 듯 집어 들었다. 두 사람 모두 서로 처한 상황에—각자가 상대에게 노여움을 드러냈다는 사실에—충격을 받았다. 만약 그들이 집에, 로윅에 자리 잡고 이웃들 사이에서 평범한 일상을 살고 있었다면, 그 충돌은 덜 거북했을 것이다. 하지만 신혼여행에서, 그 명백한 목적이 두 사람을 서로에게 온 세상이라는 이유로 따로 떼어 놓는 데 있는 여행에서, 의견이 어긋난다는 감각은 적어도 당혹스럽고 김빠지는 일이었다. 경도를 멀리까지 바꾸어 도덕적 고독 속에 자리 잡은 끝에, 작은 폭발들을 겪고, 대화가 어려워지고, 쳐다보지도 않은 채 물잔을 건네게 되는 것을, 아무리 강인한 정신의 소유자라 한들 만족스러운 성취라고 보기는 어렵다. 도러시아의 경험 없는 예민함에는 그것이 모든 전망을 뒤바꾸는 하나의 파국처럼 보였다. 캐소본 씨에게는 그것이 새로운 고통이었으니, 그는 전에 신혼여행을 한 적도, 그가 상상할 수 있었던 것보다 더 종속에 가까운 그 긴밀한 결합 속에 자신을 둔 적도 없었기 때문이다. 이 매력적인 젊은 신부는 그에게 자기를 위한 많은 배려를 요구할 뿐 아니라(그는 이를 부지런히 베풀어 왔다), 정작 그가 가장 위로를 필요로 하는 바로 그 지점에서 그를 잔인하게 동요시킬 줄 아는 존재로 드러났다. 자기 삶을 둘러싼 차갑고 그림자 같으며 갈채 없는 청중에 맞설 부드러운 울타리를 얻기는커녕, 그는 그저 그 청중에게 더 실질적인 존재감을 부여한 것에 지나지 않았던가?\n\n두 사람 중 누구도 지금 다시 입을 열 수 있다고 느끼지 못했다. 이미 정해 둔 일정을 뒤집어 외출하지 않겠다고 하는 것은, 끈질긴 노여움을 드러내 보이는 일이 되었을 텐데, 이미 자신이 죄책감을 느끼기 시작한 터라 도러시아의 양심은 그것을 꺼렸다. 자신의 분노가 아무리 정당하다 해도, 그녀의 이상은 정의를 주장하는 것이 아니라 다정함을 베푸는 것이었다. 그래서 마차가 문 앞에 오자, 그녀는 캐소본 씨와 함께 바티칸으로 갔고, 비문이 새겨진 돌투성이 통로를 그와 더불어 걸었으며, 도서관 입구에서 그와 헤어진 뒤에는, 주위에 무엇이 있든 그저 무기력하게 박물관을 가로질러 나아갔다. 그녀에게는 돌아서서 어디든 가고 싶다고 말할 기운조차 없었다. 캐소본 씨가 그녀와 헤어지던 바로 그때 나우만이 처음으로 그녀를 보았고, 그는 그녀와 동시에 긴 조각 회랑으로 들어섰다. 하지만 거기서 나우만은 라디슬로를 기다려야 했는데, 그곳에 있는 수수께끼 같은 중세풍 인물상을 두고 그와 샴페인 내기를 정리하기로 했기 때문이다. 그 인물상을 살펴보고, 논쟁을 마무리하며 걸어간 뒤 두 사람은 헤어졌고, 라디슬로는 뒤에 남아 머뭇거렸으며 나우만은 조각상의 전당으로 들어가 거기서 다시 도러시아를 보았다. 그녀의 자세를 두드러지게 만드는 그 골똘한 상념에 잠긴 모습으로 그녀를 본 것이다. 그녀는 조각상들을 보고 있지 않았던 만큼이나 바닥에 비친 한 줄기 햇빛도 실은 보고 있지 않았다. 그녀는 자기 집에서, 영국의 들판과 느릅나무들과 산울타리가 늘어선 큰길 위로 펼쳐질 다가올 세월의 빛을 내면으로 보고 있었고, 그 세월을 기쁨에 찬 헌신으로 채울 방도가 예전만큼 또렷하지 않다는 것을 느끼고 있었다. 하지만 도러시아의 마음속에는, 모든 생각과 감정이 조만간 흘러들기 마련인 하나의 흐름이 있었으니—그것은 가장 온전한 진리, 가장 덜 편파적인 선을 향해 의식 전체가 앞으로 내뻗는 움직임이었다. 분노와 낙담보다 더 나은 무언가가 분명히 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI.\n\n그녀의 언변 또한 매우 여성스럽고 꾸밈이 없으며, 현명해 보이려고 지어낸 말을 쓰지 않았다. — 초서\n\n도러시아가 안전하게 혼자가 되자마자 흐느끼게 된 것은 그런 까닭에서였다. 그러나 그녀는 곧 문을 두드리는 소리에 정신이 들었고, 그 바람에 \"들어오세요\"라고 말하기 전에 황급히 눈물을 닦았다. 탠트립이 명함을 가지고 와서, 어떤 신사가 현관에서 기다리고 있다고 말했다. 안내인이 그에게 캐소본 부인만 댁에 계신다고 했는데도, 그는 자신이 캐소본 씨의 친척이라며 부인을 뵐 수 있겠느냐고 했다는 것이다.\n\n\"네.\" 도러시아가 망설임 없이 말했다. \"응접실로 모시세요.\" 젊은 래디슬로에 대한 그녀의 주된 인상이라면, 로윅에서 그를 보았을 때 캐소본 씨가 그에게 베푼 관대함을 알게 되었다는 것, 그리고 그가 자신의 진로를 두고 망설이던 모습에 흥미를 느꼈다는 것이었다. 그녀는 적극적인 공감을 베풀 기회를 주는 것이라면 무엇에든 마음이 살아 움직였는데, 지금 이 순간에는 그 방문이 마치 자기 안에만 갇혀 있던 불만에서 그녀를 흔들어 깨우려 온 것처럼 — 남편의 선량함을 일깨워 주고, 이제 모든 선한 행위에서 그의 조력자가 될 권리가 자신에게 있음을 느끼게 해 주려고 온 것처럼 보였다. 그녀는 잠시 한두 순간 기다렸지만, 옆방으로 들어설 때 그녀의 얼굴에는 울었던 흔적이 꼭 그만큼만 남아 있어, 그 솔직한 얼굴이 평소보다 더 앳되고 호소력 있게 보였다. 그녀는 허영이 섞이지 않은 그 절묘한 선의의 미소로 래디슬로를 맞이하며 손을 내밀었다. 그는 그녀보다 몇 살 위였지만, 그 순간에는 훨씬 더 젊어 보였다. 투명한 얼굴빛이 갑자기 붉어졌고, 남자 동행과 있을 때의 그 거침없는 무심함과는 전혀 다른 수줍음을 띠며 말했기 때문이다. 그동안 도러시아는 그를 편안하게 해 주고 싶다는 의아한 마음에 점점 더 차분해졌다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 바티칸 박물관에서 뵙기 전까지는 당신과 캐소본 씨가 로마에 계신 줄 몰랐습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 곧바로 당신을 알아보았지만 — 하지만 — 제 말은, 캐소본 씨의 주소가 우편국 유치 창구에 있으리라 짐작했고, 가능한 한 빨리 그분과 당신께 인사를 드리고 싶었다는 뜻입니다.\"\n\n\"앉으세요. 그이는 지금 여기 없지만, 분명 당신 소식을 들으면 기뻐할 거예요.\" 도러시아는 무심코 벽난로와 높다란 창의 빛 사이에 앉으며, 자비로운 부인의 차분함으로 맞은편 의자를 가리키며 말했다. 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 소녀다운 슬픔의 흔적이 오히려 더 두드러져 보였다. \"캐소본 씨는 무척 바쁘답니다. 하지만 주소를 남겨 주시겠지요? 그러면 그이가 당신께 편지를 쓸 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시군요.\" 래디슬로가 말했다. 그녀의 얼굴을 달라지게 한 울음의 흔적을 흥미롭게 관찰하면서 차츰 소심함을 잃어 갔다. \"제 주소는 명함에 있습니다. 하지만 허락해 주신다면, 내일 캐소본 씨가 댁에 계실 만한 시간에 다시 찾아뵙겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그이는 매일 바티칸 도서관에 가서 글을 읽기 때문에, 약속을 하지 않고는 좀처럼 만나기 어려워요. 특히 지금은요. 우리는 곧 로마를 떠날 예정이라 그이가 무척 바쁘답니다. 보통 아침 식사 후부터 저녁 식사 때까지 거의 자리를 비워요. 하지만 그이도 분명 당신이 우리와 함께 저녁 식사를 하기를 바랄 거예요.\"\n\n윌 래디슬로는 몇 순간 말문이 막혔다. 그는 캐소본 씨를 좋아한 적이 없었고, 신세를 졌다는 의식이 없었더라면 박학다식한 박쥐 같은 인간이라고 비웃었을 것이다. 하지만 이 메마른 현학자, 골동품상이 뒷방에 쌓아 둔 가짜 골동품 재고만큼이나 하찮은 자질구레한 설명들을 공들여 다듬는 이 사내가, 우선 이 사랑스러운 젊은 여인을 자신과 결혼하게 만들어 놓고는, 신혼여행 기간을 그녀에게서 떨어져 곰팡내 나는 무익한 일들을 더듬으며 보내고 있다니 (윌은 과장에 능했다) — 이 돌연한 광경에 그는 일종의 우스꽝스러운 혐오감에 사로잡혔다. 큰 소리로 웃고 싶은 충동과, 마찬가지로 때에 맞지 않게 경멸 어린 욕설을 터뜨리고 싶은 충동 사이에서 그는 갈팡질팡했다.\n\n한순간 그는 그 갈등이 자신의 변화무쌍한 이목구비를 기묘하게 일그러뜨리고 있음을 느꼈지만, 애써 그것을 유쾌한 미소 정도로, 그 이상 거슬리지 않게 풀어냈다.\n\n도러시아는 의아했지만, 그 미소는 거부할 수 없는 것이어서 그녀의 얼굴에서도 되비쳐 빛났다. 윌 래디슬로의 미소는, 미리부터 그에게 화가 나 있지 않은 한, 사람을 즐겁게 했다. 그것은 내면의 빛이 솟구쳐 두 눈은 물론 투명한 살갗까지 환히 밝히며, 마치 어떤 아리엘이 새로운 매력으로 어루만지듯 모든 곡선과 선들 위에서 노닐어, 침울함의 자취를 영원히 쫓아내는 것이었다. 그 미소의 반영은, 아직 촉촉한 검은 속눈썹 아래에서일지라도, 조금의 유쾌함을 띠지 않을 수 없었으니, 도러시아는 묻듯이 말했다. \"무언가 재미있으신가요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 윌이 재빨리 둘러댈 거리를 찾아내며 말했다. \"처음 당신을 뵈었을 때 제가 했던 우스운 꼴을 생각하던 참입니다. 당신의 비평으로 제 보잘것없는 스케치를 단번에 무너뜨리셨을 때 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제 비평이라뇨?\" 도러시아가 더욱 의아해하며 말했다. \"그럴 리가요. 저는 그림에 관해서는 늘 유난히 무지하다고 느끼는걸요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신이 너무 많이 알아서, 가장 신랄한 말이 무엇인지 정확히 짚어 낼 줄 안다고 의심했습니다. 당신은 말씀하셨지요 — 저만큼 기억하지는 못하시겠지만 — 제 스케치가 자연과 어떤 관계를 맺는지 당신께는 전혀 보이지 않는다고요. 적어도 그런 뜻을 내비치셨습니다.\" 이제 윌은 미소뿐 아니라 웃을 수도 있었다.\n\n\"그건 정말 제 무지 탓이었어요.\" 도러시아가 윌의 호탕함에 감탄하며 말했다. \"제 삼촌이 모든 감식가들이 아주 훌륭하다고 평한다던 그림들에서 저는 도무지 아름다움을 볼 수 없었기에 그렇게 말한 게 분명해요. 그리고 로마에서도 똑같은 무지를 안고 다녔답니다. 제가 정말로 즐길 수 있는 그림은 비교적 적어요. 처음에 벽이 프레스코화나 진귀한 그림들로 뒤덮인 방에 들어서면, 저는 일종의 경외감을 느껴요 — 마치 화려한 예복과 행렬이 펼쳐지는 대단한 의식에 참석한 아이처럼요. 저 자신보다 더 높은 어떤 삶 앞에 서 있는 듯한 느낌이 들지요. 하지만 그림을 하나씩 들여다보기 시작하면 그 생명이 빠져나가 버리거나, 아니면 제게는 무언가 격렬하고 낯선 것이 되고 말아요. 분명 제 둔함 탓이겠지요. 한꺼번에 너무 많은 것을 보면서 절반도 이해하지 못하니까요. 그러면 늘 자신이 어리석게 느껴져요. 무언가가 매우 훌륭하다는 말을 들으면서도 그것이 훌륭하다고 느끼지 못하는 건 괴로운 일이에요 — 사람들이 하늘에 관해 이야기하는데 자기는 눈이 먼 것과도 같지요.\"\n\n\"오, 예술을 느끼는 감각에는 후천적으로 익혀야 하는 부분이 많습니다.\" 윌이 말했다. (이제 도러시아의 고백이 진솔하다는 것을 의심할 수 없었다.) \"예술은 인위적이고 꾸민 양식이 아주 많은 오래된 언어여서, 때로는 그것들을 알게 됨으로써 얻는 주된 즐거움이 그저 안다는 느낌일 뿐이지요. 저는 여기서 온갖 종류의 예술을 더없이 즐기고 있습니다. 하지만 제 즐거움을 낱낱이 분해해 본다면, 그것이 여러 가닥의 다른 실들로 짜여 있음을 알게 될 것 같아요. 자기도 조금쯤 서툴게나마 그려 보고 그 과정을 알고 있다는 데서 오는 무언가가 있거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어쩌면 화가가 되실 작정인가요?\" 도러시아가 새롭게 관심을 돌리며 말했다. \"그림을 직업으로 삼으실 생각이세요? 캐소본 씨도 당신이 직업을 정했다는 말을 들으면 좋아할 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 오, 아닙니다.\" 윌이 다소 냉랭하게 말했다. \"그쪽은 완전히 마음을 접었습니다. 너무 한쪽으로 치우친 삶이거든요. 저는 여기서 독일 화가들을 많이 보아 왔습니다. 프랑크푸르트에서 그들 중 한 명과 함께 여행을 했지요. 더러는 훌륭하고, 심지어 뛰어난 친구들도 있어요 — 하지만 세상을 온전히 화실의 관점에서만 바라보는 그들의 방식에 빠져들고 싶지는 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그 점은 이해할 수 있어요.\" 도러시아가 다정하게 말했다. \"그리고 로마에서는 그림보다 이 세상에 더 필요한 것들이 너무도 많은 것처럼 보여요. 하지만 당신께 그림에 대한 천부적 재능이 있다면, 그것을 길잡이로 삼는 것이 옳지 않을까요? 어쩌면 당신은 이런 것들보다 더 나은 것을 — 아니면 다른 것을 그려, 한 장소에 거의 다 비슷비슷한 그림들이 그렇게 많지 않도록 할 수도 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n이 순박함은 오해의 여지가 없었고, 윌은 그것에 마음이 끌려 솔직해졌다. \"그런 종류의 변화를 일으키려면 아주 드문 천재성이 있어야 합니다. 저의 재능은 이미 이루어진 것을 잘 해내는 수준에도, 적어도 해 볼 만한 가치가 있을 만큼 잘 해내는 수준에도 이르지 못할까 봐 걱정입니다. 그리고 저는 고된 노력으로는 어떤 것도 결코 이루지 못할 거예요. 무언가가 쉽게 다가오지 않으면 저는 절대 손에 넣지 못하거든요.\"\n\n\"캐소본 씨가 당신의 인내심 부족을 안타까워한다는 말을 들은 적이 있어요.\" 도러시아가 부드럽게 말했다. 그녀는 모든 삶을 휴일처럼 받아들이는 이런 태도에 다소 충격을 받았다.\n\n\"네, 캐소본 씨의 견해는 압니다. 그분과 저는 생각이 다릅니다.\"\n\n이 성급한 대답에 살짝 배어 있는 경멸의 기색이 도러시아의 비위를 거슬렀다. 아침의 괴로움 때문에 그녀는 캐소본 씨에 관한 일에 더욱 예민해져 있었다.\n\n\"확실히 두 분은 다르지요.\" 그녀가 다소 자랑스럽게 말했다. \"저는 당신을 그분과 비교할 생각은 없었어요. 캐소본 씨처럼 끈기 있고 헌신적으로 노력하는 힘은 흔한 게 아니거든요.\"\n\n윌은 그녀가 기분이 상한 것을 알아챘지만, 이는 오히려 캐소본 씨를 향한 그의 잠재된 반감이 새롭게 일으킨 짜증에 한층 더 박차를 가했을 뿐이었다. 도러시아가 이런 남편을 숭배하다니 참을 수 없는 노릇이었다. 여자의 그런 나약함은 다른 어떤 남자도 아닌 바로 그 남편에게만 흐뭇한 법이다. 인간은 이웃의 윙윙대는 영광에서 생명을 짓눌러 없애 버리고도, 그런 살해를 살인이 아니라고 여기는 유혹에 쉽게 빠진다.\n\n\"네, 정말 그렇습니다.\" 그가 즉각 대답했다. \"그래서 더욱 안타까운 일이지요. 세상의 다른 사람들이 무엇을 하고 있는지 모르는 탓에, 그토록 많은 영국 학문이 그러하듯이 그 노력이 헛되이 버려진다면 말입니다. 캐소본 씨가 독일어를 읽으신다면 많은 수고를 덜 수 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.\" 도러시아가 깜짝 놀라 불안해하며 말했다.\n\n\"제 말은 그저,\" 윌이 무심하게 말했다. \"역사 연구에서는 독일인들이 앞서가고 있고, 그들은 자기들이 잘 닦인 길을 만들어 놓은 마당에 누군가가 주머니 나침반을 들고 숲속을 더듬어 얻어 낸 결과를 비웃는다는 겁니다. 제가 캐소본 씨와 함께 있을 때 보니, 그분은 그쪽 방면에는 귀를 닫고 계셨습니다. 독일인이 쓴 라틴어 논문 한 편을 읽는 것조차 거의 마지못해 하셨지요. 저는 무척 안타까웠습니다.\"\n\n윌은 그저 그 자랑스러워하는 근면함을 무너뜨릴 통렬한 한 방을 먹일 생각만 했을 뿐, 도러시아가 어떤 식으로 상처받을지는 상상하지 못했다. 젊은 래디슬로 본인도 독일 저술가들에 그다지 정통한 것은 아니었다. 하지만 남의 결점을 가엾이 여기는 데는 아주 작은 성취만 있으면 족한 법이다.\n\n가엾은 도러시아는 남편이 평생 바쳐 온 노력이 헛수고일지도 모른다는 생각에 가슴이 아렸고, 그 통증에 짓눌려, 그토록 신세를 진 이 젊은 친척이 그런 말을 삼갔어야 하지 않았는가 하는 물음에 마음 쓸 여력이 없었다. 그녀는 말조차 하지 못하고, 그 처량한 생각에 사로잡힌 채 자기 두 손만 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n그러나 그 무너뜨리는 한 방을 먹인 윌은 자못 부끄러워졌으니, 도러시아의 침묵을 보고 그녀를 더욱 화나게 한 것이라 짐작한 데다, 은인의 꽁지깃을 뽑아 버린 데 대한 양심의 가책도 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"제가 특히 안타까워한 까닭은,\" 그가 헐뜯기에서 진심 아닌 찬사로 으레 옮겨 가며 말을 이었다. \"제 사촌에 대한 감사와 존경 때문입니다. 재능과 인품이 그만큼 빼어나지 않은 사람이라면 그리 큰 문제도 아닐 테니까요.\"\n\n도러시아는 격앙된 감정으로 평소보다 더 빛나는 눈을 들어, 더없이 서글픈 음조로 말했다. \"로잔에 있을 때 독일어를 배워 두었더라면 얼마나 좋았을까요! 독일어 선생이 얼마든지 있었는데요. 하지만 이제 저는 아무 도움도 될 수 없네요.\"\n\n윌에게 도러시아의 마지막 말은 새로운 빛, 그러나 여전히 알 수 없는 빛이었다. 그녀가 어떻게 캐소본 씨를 받아들이게 되었는가 하는 의문은 — 처음 그녀를 보았을 때는 겉모습과 달리 까다로운 사람일 게 분명하다고 일축해 버렸던 의문이었지만 — 이제 그렇게 간단하고 손쉬운 방법으로는 답할 수 없는 것이 되었다. 그녀가 다른 무엇이든 간에, 까다로운 사람은 아니었다. 그녀는 차갑게 영리하고 에둘러 풍자하는 사람이 아니라, 사랑스러우리만치 순박하고 감정이 충만한 사람이었다. 그녀는 속아 넘어간 천사였다. 그녀의 마음과 영혼이 그토록 직접적이고 천진하게 흘러나오는 그 가락 같은 단편들을 기다리며 지켜보는 것은 더없이 특별한 기쁨일 터였다. 다시금 그의 마음속에 아이올로스의 하프가 떠올랐다.\n\n그녀는 이 결혼에서 자기 나름의 독창적인 낭만을 지어냈음에 틀림없었다. 만약 캐소본 씨가 법적 절차도 없이 그저 발톱으로 그녀를 낚아채 자기 굴로 끌고 간 용이었다면, 그녀를 풀어 주고 그녀의 발치에 엎드리는 것이야말로 피할 수 없는 영웅적 위업이었으리라. 하지만 그는 용보다 더 다루기 힘든 존재였다. 그는 사회 집단 전체를 등에 업은 은인이었고, 바로 그 순간 흠잡을 데 없이 단정한 거동으로 방에 들어서고 있었다. 한편 도러시아는 새로 깨어난 불안과 회한으로 생기를 띠고 있었고, 윌은 그녀의 감정에 대한 흠모 어린 억측으로 생기를 띠고 있었다.\n\n캐소본 씨는 기쁨이라고는 전혀 섞이지 않은 놀라움을 느꼈지만, 윌이 일어나 자신이 찾아온 까닭을 설명하자 평소의 정중한 인사에서 벗어나지 않았다. 캐소본 씨는 평소보다 덜 행복했고, 어쩌면 그 때문에 한층 더 흐릿하고 빛바래 보였는지도 모른다. 그게 아니라면, 그런 인상은 젊은 사촌의 모습과 대비되어 손쉽게 빚어졌을 수도 있다. 윌을 처음 보았을 때 받는 인상은 햇살처럼 환한 밝음이었고, 이는 변화무쌍한 그의 표정이 주는 종잡을 수 없는 느낌을 더했다. 정말이지 그의 이목구비 자체가 모양을 바꾸는 듯했다. 그의 턱은 때로 커 보이다가 때로 작아 보였고, 콧등의 자그마한 잔주름은 무슨 변신을 준비하는 것 같았다. 그가 고개를 휙 돌리면 머리카락에서 빛이 흩날리는 듯했고, 어떤 사람들은 그 반짝임에서 분명한 천재성을 보았다고 여겼다. 이와 반대로 캐소본 씨는 광채라고는 없이 서 있었다.\n\n도러시아의 두 눈이 남편에게 불안하게 쏠려 있었으니, 어쩌면 그녀도 그 대비를 느끼지 못한 것은 아니었으리라. 하지만 그것은 그저 다른 여러 원인과 뒤섞여, 남편을 위한 그 새로운 불안을 그녀가 더욱 의식하게 만들었을 뿐이다. 그 불안은 자기 자신의 꿈이 아니라 남편이 처한 현실에서 자라난, 연민 어린 다정함의 첫 움직임이었다. 그렇지만 윌이 거기 있다는 것은 그녀에게 한결 더 큰 자유의 원천이었다. 그의 젊은 대등함이 유쾌했고, 또한 어쩌면 그가 설득에 열려 있다는 점도 그러했다. 그녀는 누군가에게 이야기하고 싶은 절실한 욕구를 느꼈는데, 이전까지 그토록 명민하고 유연한, 무엇이든 다 이해할 것 같은 사람은 본 적이 없었다.\n\n캐소본 씨는 윌이 로마에서 유익하고도 즐겁게 시간을 보내고 있기를 바란다고 점잖게 말하고는 — 윌의 의도가 남부 독일에 머무는 것인 줄 알았다고 하면서 — 내일 저녁 식사를 하러 와서 좀 더 폭넓게 이야기를 나누자고 청했다. 지금은 다소 피곤하다는 것이었다. 래디슬로는 알아차렸고, 초대를 받아들이고는 곧바로 작별을 고했다.\n\n캐소본 씨가 소파 끝에 지친 듯 주저앉아 팔꿈치를 괴고 머리를 받친 채 바닥을 내려다보는 동안, 도러시아의 눈은 불안하게 남편을 좇았다. 얼굴이 살짝 붉어지고 눈을 반짝이며 그녀는 그의 곁에 앉아 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 그렇게 성급하게 말씀드려서 용서해 주세요. 제가 잘못했어요. 당신께 상처를 드리고 하루를 더 무겁게 만든 것 같아 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"당신이 그렇게 느낀다니 기쁘구려, 여보.\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다. 그는 조용히 말하며 고개를 약간 숙였지만, 그녀를 바라보는 그의 눈에는 여전히 불편한 기색이 남아 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 저를 용서하시는 거지요?\" 도러시아가 흐느낌을 삼키며 재빨리 말했다. 어떤 감정의 표현을 갈구하던 그녀는 자기 잘못을 과장할 준비가 되어 있었다. 사랑이라면 멀리서 돌아오는 뉘우침을 알아보고, 그 목을 끌어안고 입맞추지 않겠는가?\n\n\"내 사랑 도러시아 — '뉘우침으로도 흡족하지 않은 자는 하늘에도 땅에도 속하지 못하나니' — 당신은 내가 그토록 엄한 선고로 추방당할 만한 사람이라고는 생각하지 않겠지.\" 캐소본 씨는 단호한 말을 하려고, 또한 희미하게나마 미소를 지으려고 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n도러시아는 잠자코 있었지만, 흐느낌과 함께 차올랐던 눈물 한 방울이 기어이 떨어지고야 말았다.\n\n\"당신은 흥분했구려, 여보. 나 또한 너무 큰 마음의 동요로 인해 좀 좋지 않은 결과를 느끼고 있다오.\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다. 사실 그는 자신이 없는 사이에 젊은 래디슬로를 맞아들이지 말았어야 했다고 그녀에게 말할 생각이 있었다. 하지만 그는 입을 다물었으니, 한편으로는 그녀가 뉘우치며 잘못을 시인하는 마당에 새로운 불평을 꺼내는 것은 무례한 일이라는 의식 때문이었고, 한편으로는 말로 자신을 더욱 동요시키는 것을 피하고 싶었기 때문이며, 또 한편으로는 학자 동료들에게 다 소진되지 않고 다른 방면에 쓸 만큼 남아 있던 그 시샘하는 기질을 드러내기에는 너무 자존심이 강했기 때문이었다. 아주 작은 불씨만으로도 충분한 질투가 있다. 그것은 정념이라기보다는, 불안한 자기중심주의의 흐리고 눅눅한 낙담 속에서 자라난 마름병에 가깝다.\n\n\"이제 옷을 갈아입을 시간인 것 같소.\" 그가 시계를 보며 덧붙였다. 두 사람은 모두 일어섰고, 그날 있었던 일에 관해서는 그 후로 결코 다시 언급하지 않았다.\n\n그러나 도러시아는 그 일을, 어떤 소중한 기대가 죽거나 어떤 새로운 동기가 태어나는, 우리 경험 속의 분기점을 누구나 기억하는 그 생생함으로 끝끝내 기억했다. 오늘 그녀는 자신이 캐소본 씨에게서 자기 감정에 대한 응답을 기대했던 것이 한낱 터무니없는 환상이었음을 깨닫기 시작했고, 그의 삶에도 어떤 슬픈 의식이 있어 그녀 못지않게 그에게도 절실한 갈망이 있을지 모른다는 예감이 깨어나는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n우리는 모두 도덕적 우둔함 속에서 태어나, 세상을 우리의 지고한 자아를 먹여 살리는 젖통쯤으로 여긴다. 도러시아는 일찍이 그 우둔함에서 벗어나기 시작했지만, 그럼에도 그녀에게는 자신이 어떻게 캐소본 씨에게 헌신하여 그의 힘과 지혜 안에서 현명하고 강해질 것인가를 상상하는 편이, 그에게도 자기 나름의 대등한 자아의 중심이 있어 거기서부터 빛과 그늘이 언제나 어떤 차이를 두고 비쳐 들 수밖에 없다는 사실을 — 더는 사색이 아니라 감정으로, 사물의 견고함처럼 감각의 직접성으로 되살아난 관념으로 — 그토록 또렷하게 인식하기보다 더 쉬웠던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII.\n\n우리는 오랫동안 이야기를 나누었네. 그녀는 단순하고 선했지.\n악을 알지 못했기에, 그녀는 선을 행했네.\n\n마음의 풍요로움으로 그녀는 내게 자비를 베풀었고,\n\n마음이 어떻게 주어지는지 귀 기울이는 동안,\n감히 생각할 겨를도 없이 나는 그녀에게 내 마음을 주었네.\n\n그녀는 내 삶을 가져갔으나, 끝내 그것을 알지 못했네.\n— 알프레드 드 뮈세\n\n윌 래디슬로는 다음 날 만찬 자리에서 더없이 유쾌하고 상냥하게 굴어서, 캐소본 씨가 못마땅함을 드러낼 만한 기회를 조금도 주지 않았다. 오히려 도러시아가 보기에 윌은 자기 남편을 대화로 끌어들이고 공손하게 그의 말에 귀 기울이는 데 있어, 지금껏 그녀가 누구에게서도 본 적 없는 더 행복한 솜씨를 지닌 것 같았다. 하긴 티프턴 근방의 청자들이라야 그리 뛰어난 사람들은 아니었다! 윌도 적잖이 말을 했지만, 그가 하는 말은 어찌나 빠르게, 그리고 무언가를 지나가는 길에 슬쩍 던진다는 듯 대수롭지 않은 태도로 끼어드는지, 마치 거대한 종소리 뒤에 울리는 경쾌하고 자그마한 종소리 같았다. 윌이 늘 완벽한 것은 아니었지만, 이날은 분명 그의 좋은 날들 가운데 하나였다. 그는 로마의 가난한 사람들 사이에서 일어나는 소소한 일들, 자유롭게 돌아다닐 수 있는 사람만이 볼 수 있는 장면들을 묘사했다. 그는 유대교와 가톨릭교의 관계에 관한 미들턴의 부당한 견해에 대해 캐소본 씨와 의견이 일치함을 알게 되었다. 그리고 자연스럽게, 로마의 바로 그 잡다함에서 얻는 즐거움을 절반은 열광적으로 절반은 장난스럽게 그려 보이는 데로 옮겨 갔는데, 그 잡다함이야말로 끊임없는 비교로 정신을 유연하게 만들고, 세계의 여러 시대를 생생한 연관 없이 칸칸이 나뉜 상자 같은 것으로 보지 않게 해 준다는 것이었다. 캐소본 씨의 연구는 언제나 그렇게 좁은 종류와는 거리가 멀 만큼 폭넓었으니, 어쩌면 그런 갑작스러운 효과를 한 번도 느껴 본 적이 없었을 거라고 윌은 말했다. 그러나 자기 자신으로 말하자면, 로마가 역사를 하나의 전체로 보는 아주 새로운 감각을 안겨 주었음을 고백한다고 했다. 그 단편들이 자기 상상력을 자극하여 건설적으로 만들었다는 것이다. 그러다 이따금, 그러나 지나치게 자주는 아니게, 그는 도러시아에게 의견을 구하고 그녀가 한 말을 논의했는데, 마치 그녀의 감상이 폴리뇨의 마돈나나 라오콘 군상에 대한 최종 판단에서조차 고려해야 할 한 항목이라도 되는 듯했다. 세계의 의견을 형성하는 데 기여한다는 느낌은 대화를 유난히 활기차게 만드는 법이다. 그리고 캐소본 씨 역시 자기 젊은 아내에 대한 자부심이 없지 않았으니, 그녀는 대부분의 여성보다 말을 잘했고, 그가 그녀를 택할 때 실제로 알아본 바이기도 했다.\n일이 이토록 즐겁게 풀려 가던 차에, 도서관에서의 작업을 이틀쯤 중단하겠으며 짧은 재개 후에는 더 이상 로마에 머물 이유가 없으리라는 캐소본 씨의 말은, 캐소본 부인이 화실 한두 군데는 보지 않고 떠나서는 안 된다고 권할 용기를 윌에게 주었다. 캐소본 씨가 그녀를 데려가지 않으시겠는가? 그런 것은 놓쳐서는 안 될 일이었다. 아주 특별한 것이었으니, 거대한 화석 위에 곤충 떼와 더불어 자라는 작고 싱싱한 식물 같은 삶의 한 형태였다. 윌이 기꺼이 안내하겠다고 했다. 따분한 것은 빼고, 그저 몇몇 본보기만 보여 주겠다는 것이었다.\n\n도러시아가 자기 쪽을 간절히 바라보는 것을 본 캐소본 씨는, 그런 방문에 관심이 있겠느냐고 그녀에게 묻지 않을 수 없었다. 이제 그는 하루 종일 그녀를 위해 시간을 낼 수 있었다. 그리하여 윌이 이튿날 와서 함께 마차를 타기로 합의되었다.\n윌은 살아 있는 명사인 토르발센을 빼놓을 수 없었으니, 캐소본 씨조차 그에 대해 물어볼 정도였다. 그러나 날이 그리 깊어지기 전에 그는 친구 아돌프 나우만의 화실로 길을 이끌었다. 그를 가리켜 윌은 기독교 미술을 되살린 주요 인물 가운데 하나, 지고한 사건들을 신비로 보는 저 웅대한 관념—거기서 잇따른 시대들이 관객이 되고, 그와 관련하여 모든 시대의 위대한 영혼들이 말하자면 동시대인이 되는—을 되살렸을 뿐 아니라 확장한 이들 중 하나라고 소개했다. 윌은 자기가 당분간 나우만의 제자로 들어가 있다고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그 사람 밑에서 유화 습작을 몇 점 그려 봤어요.\" 윌이 말했다. \"저는 모사가 싫습니다. 제 것을 뭔가 집어넣어야 직성이 풀려요. 나우만은 성인들이 교회의 수레를 끄는 그림을 그려 왔는데, 저는 말로의 탬벌레인이 정복당한 왕들을 자기 전차에 매어 몰고 가는 장면을 습작으로 그려 봤습니다. 저는 나우만만큼 교회적이지 않아서, 가끔은 의미가 너무 넘친다고 그를 놀리곤 하지요. 하지만 이번에는 의도의 폭에서 그를 능가할 작정입니다. 저는 전차에 탄 탬벌레인을, 멍에 메인 왕조들을 채찍질해 내모는 세계의 물리적 역사의 어마어마한 흐름으로 삼은 거예요. 제 생각에 그건 훌륭한 신화적 해석이죠.\" 윌은 여기서 캐소본 씨를 바라보았는데, 그는 상징을 이렇게 즉흥적으로 다루는 것을 몹시 불편하게 받아들이며 무덤덤한 태도로 고개를 까딱했다.\n\n\"그렇게나 많은 것을 담아낸다면 그 습작은 무척 웅대하겠어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"저로서는 당신이 부여한 의미조차 약간의 설명이 필요할 것 같아요. 탬벌레인이 지진과 화산을 나타내게 하실 생각인가요?\"\n\n\"오, 그렇습니다.\" 윌이 웃으며 말했다. \"그리고 종족의 이동과 숲의 개간도요. 또 아메리카와 증기 기관도. 당신이 상상할 수 있는 모든 것이죠!\"\n\n\"참으로 어려운 종류의 속기로군요!\" 도러시아가 남편 쪽을 보며 미소 지었다. \"그걸 읽어 내려면 당신의 온갖 지식이 다 필요하겠어요.\"\n캐소본 씨는 윌을 슬쩍 곁눈질했다. 그는 자기가 조롱당하고 있는 게 아닌가 의심했다. 그러나 그 의심에 도러시아까지 포함시키는 것은 가능한 일이 아니었다.\n\n그들이 들어섰을 때 나우만은 부지런히 그림을 그리고 있었으나, 모델은 한 사람도 와 있지 않았다. 그의 그림들은 보기 좋게 배치되어 있었고, 수수하나 생기 있는 그 자신의 풍채는 비둘기빛 작업복과 적갈색 벨벳 모자로 한층 돋보였으니, 마치 바로 그 시각에 아름다운 영국 젊은 부인이 오리라 예상이라도 한 듯 모든 것이 더없이 운 좋게 갖추어져 있었다.\n화가는 자신만만한 영어로 완성작과 미완성작에 관해 자그마한 강론을 늘어놓았는데, 도러시아 못지않게 캐소본 씨도 유심히 관찰하는 듯했다. 윌은 여기저기서 친구의 작품에서 두드러진 장점들을 짚어 가며 열렬한 찬사의 말을 불쑥불쑥 끼워 넣었다. 그러는 동안 도러시아는, 까닭 모를 닫집 씌운 옥좌 아래 소박한 시골을 배경 삼아 앉은 마돈나들의 의미에 대해, 그리고 손에 건축 모형을 든 성인들이나 두개골에 우연히 칼이 박힌 성인들의 의미에 대해 아주 새로운 관념을 얻고 있다는 느낌이 들었다. 그녀에게 괴이하게만 보였던 어떤 것들이 이해할 만한 것이 되어 가고, 심지어 자연스러운 의미마저 띠어 갔다. 그러나 이 모든 것은 분명 캐소본 씨가 관심을 두지 않았던 지식의 한 갈래였다.\n\n\"저는 그림을 수수께끼처럼 읽어 내야 하기보다는 차라리 아름답다고 느끼는 편이 좋아요. 하지만 매우 폭넓은 의미를 지닌 당신 그림보다는 이런 그림들을 더 빨리 이해하게 될 것 같아요.\" 도러시아가 윌에게 말했다.\n\n\"나우만 앞에서 제 그림 얘기는 하지 마세요.\" 윌이 말했다. \"그 친구가 그러는데, 그건 전부 푸셔라이라더군요. 그가 쓰는 가장 모욕적인 말이지요!\"\n\n\"그게 정말인가요?\" 도러시아가 진실한 눈길을 나우만에게로 돌리며 묻자, 그는 살짝 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그림에 대해서 진지하게 하는 말은 아닙니다. 저 사람이 갈 길은 문—학이지요. 그건 넓—습니다.\"\n\n나우만의 모음 발음이 그 말을 빈정대듯 늘이는 듯했다. 윌은 그것이 영 마음에 들지 않았으나 가까스로 웃어넘겼다. 그리고 캐소본 씨는 그 화가의 독일식 억양에 다소 거부감을 느끼면서도, 그의 사려 깊은 엄격함에 약간의 존경심을 품기 시작했다.\n\n나우만이 잠시 윌을 한쪽으로 끌고 가 먼저 커다란 화폭을, 그다음 캐소본 씨를 번갈아 보고 나서 다시 앞으로 나와 이렇게 말했을 때, 그 존경심은 줄어들지 않았다.\n\n\"제 친구 래디슬로 말로는, 제가 이런 말씀을 드려도 용서해 주시리라더군요. 선생님 두상을 습작으로 그릴 수 있다면 저기 제 그림 속 성 토마스 아퀴나스를 위해 더없이 귀중한 자료가 될 것입니다. 지나친 부탁이지요. 하지만 제가 원하는 바로 그것—실재 속의 이상적인 것을 이렇게 좀처럼 만나기 어려워서요.\"\n\n\"정말 놀라운 말씀이군요, 선생.\" 캐소본 씨가 기쁨의 홍조로 한결 나아진 얼굴로 말했다. \"하나 가장 평범한 부류라고 여겨 온 제 보잘것없는 용모가, 그 천사적 박사의 특징 몇 가지를 갖추는 데 조금이라도 쓸모가 있다면 영광으로 알겠습니다. 다만 그 작업이 오래 걸리지 않는다면, 그리고 캐소본 부인이 그 지체를 마다하지 않는다면 말이오.\"\n\n도러시아로 말하자면, 캐소본 씨가 인간의 아들들 가운데 가장 현명하고 가장 훌륭하다고 선포하는 기적의 음성이 아니고서야, 이보다 더 그녀를 기쁘게 할 수 있는 것은 없었다. 그런 일이 있었더라면 흔들리던 그녀의 믿음은 다시 굳건해졌으리라.\n\n나우만의 도구는 놀랄 만큼 완벽하게 가까이 갖추어져 있어서, 습작은 대화와 더불어 곧장 진행되었다. 도러시아는 앉아서 고요한 침묵 속으로 잦아들었으니, 오래도록 느껴 보지 못한 행복을 느끼고 있었다. 자기 주위의 모든 사람이 선해 보였고, 그녀는 속으로 자기가 그토록 무지하지만 않았던들 로마는 아름다움으로 가득했으리라고, 그 슬픔조차 희망의 날개를 달았으리라고 되뇌었다. 그녀만큼 의심을 모르는 천성도 없었을 것이다. 어린아이였을 때 그녀는 말벌의 고마움과 참새의 고결한 감수성을 믿었으며, 그것들의 비열함이 드러났을 때 그만큼 분개했다.\n\n그 능란한 화가는 캐소본 씨에게 영국 정치에 관한 질문들을 던졌고, 그것은 기나긴 답변을 끌어냈다. 그러는 동안 윌은 모든 것을 내려다볼 수 있는 뒤편의 어떤 계단 위에 걸터앉아 있었다.\n\n이윽고 나우만이 말했다. \"자, 이걸 반 시간쯤 미뤄 두었다가 다시 손댈 수 있다면—와서 보게, 래디슬로—여기까지는 완벽한 것 같은데.\"\n\n윌은 감탄이 너무 강렬해 문법을 갖출 수 없을 때 나오는 그런 애원조의 감탄사를 터뜨렸다. 그러자 나우만이 가련하게 안타까워하는 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아—이제—조금만 더 시간이 있었더라면—하지만 다른 약속이 있으시니—그걸 청할 수도 없고—내일 다시 와 주십사 할 수도 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"오, 그냥 더 있어요!\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"오늘은 여기저기 돌아다니는 것 말고는 할 일이 없잖아요, 그렇지요?\" 그녀가 캐소본 씨를 간청하듯 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"두상을 되도록 좋게 만들지 못한다면 안타까운 일이에요.\"\n\n\"그 일이라면 제가 시간을 내겠소, 선생.\" 캐소본 씨가 정중하게 겸양을 보이며 말했다. \"내 머릿속은 게으름에 내맡겨 두었으니, 그 바깥쪽이라도 이런 식으로 일하는 편이 좋겠지요.\"\n\n\"이루 말할 수 없이 친절하십니다—이제 저는 행복합니다!\" 나우만이 말하고는 독일어로 윌에게 무어라 말을 이어 가며, 마치 습작을 살펴보는 양 여기저기를 가리켰다. 잠시 그것을 옆으로 치워 두고 그는 손님들에게 무슨 소일거리라도 찾아 주려는 듯 막연히 둘러보더니, 이윽고 캐소본 씨를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"아마도 이 아름다운 신부님께서, 이 우아한 부인께서, 제가 그 시간을 채울 수 있도록 당신의 가벼운 습작 하나를 그려 보게 허락해 주실는지요—물론, 보시다시피, 저 그림을 위한 것은 아니고요—그저 단독 습작으로 말입니다.\"\n\n캐소본 씨는 고개를 숙이며 캐소본 부인이 호의를 베풀어 주리라 의심치 않는다고 했고, 도러시아는 곧바로 \"어디에 서면 될까요?\" 하고 물었다.\n\n나우만은 그녀에게 서 달라고 부탁하며, 자세를 잡아 줄 수 있게 해 달라고 연신 사과를 했다. 그녀는 그런 자리에서 흔히들 필요하다고 여기는 꾸민 태도나 웃음 따위는 전혀 없이 그에게 순순히 따랐고, 화가가 말했다. \"산타 클라라처럼 서 주셨으면 합니다—그렇게 기대어—손에 뺨을 괴고—그렇게—저 의자를 바라봐 주세요, 그렇게요!\"\n\n윌은 그 성녀의 발치에 엎드려 그 옷자락에 입 맞추고 싶은 마음과, 나우만이 그녀의 팔을 매만지는 동안 그를 때려눕히고 싶은 충동 사이에서 갈라져 있었다. 이 모든 것이 무엄하고 신성을 더럽히는 짓이었으니, 그는 그녀를 데려온 것을 후회했다.\n\n화가는 부지런했고, 윌은 마음을 추슬러 이리저리 움직이며 할 수 있는 한 솜씨껏 캐소본 씨를 상대해 주었다. 그러나 결국 그 신사에게 시간이 길게 느껴지는 것을 막지는 못했으니, 캐소본 부인이 피곤하지 않을까 염려된다고 그가 말한 데서 그 점이 분명히 드러났다. 나우만은 그 암시를 알아채고 말했다.\n\n\"자, 선생님, 다시 한 번 호의를 베풀어 주신다면, 사모님은 그만 놓아 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n그리하여 캐소본 씨의 인내심은 좀 더 버텨 주었고, 결국 한 번 더 앉아 줄 수 있다면 성 토마스 아퀴나스의 두상이 한결 완벽해지리라는 결론이 나자, 이튿날로 그것이 허락되었다. 이튿날 산타 클라라 역시 한 번 이상 수정되었다. 이 모든 것의 결과가 캐소본 씨에게는 그리 언짢지 않아서, 그는 성 토마스 아퀴나스가 교회의 박사들 사이에 앉아—그림으로 나타내기에는 너무 추상적인 논쟁을 벌이지만 위쪽의 청중이 다소간 주의를 기울여 경청하는—그 그림을 사들이기로 했다. 두 번째로 거론된 산타 클라라에 대해서는, 나우만이 스스로 만족스럽지 못하다고 밝혔다—양심상 그것으로 그럴듯한 그림을 만들겠다고 장담할 수가 없다는 것이었다. 그래서 산타 클라라에 관한 약정은 조건부로 남았다.\n\n그날 저녁 캐소본 씨를 안주 삼은 나우만의 농담들이나, 도러시아의 매력에 관한 그의 찬가에 대해서는 굳이 길게 늘어놓지 않겠다. 그 모든 데에 윌도 끼어들었으되, 결은 달랐다. 나우만이 도러시아의 아름다움을 한 가지라도 세세히 언급할라치면, 윌은 그 주제넘음에 분통이 치밀었다. 가장 평범한 말을 골라 쓰는 데에 천박함이 있었으니, 대체 그가 무슨 자격으로 그녀의 입술 따위를 입에 올린단 말인가? 그녀는 다른 여자들처럼 운위될 여자가 아니었다. 윌은 자기가 생각하는 바를 콕 집어 말할 수는 없었으나, 점점 신경이 곤두섰다. 그러면서도, 얼마간 버티다가 캐소본 부부를 친구의 화실로 데려가는 데 동의했을 때, 그는 나우만에게 그녀의 사랑스러움을—아니, 차라리 그녀의 신성함을, 한낱 육체의 어여쁨에나 어울릴 흔한 표현들은 그녀에게 들어맞지 않았으니—연구할 그런 기회를 베풀어 줄 수 있는 사람이라는 자부심의 충족에 끌렸던 것이었다. (티프턴과 그 인근 전체는 물론이고 도러시아 자신조차도, 그녀의 아름다움이 이토록 떠받들어지는 데 분명 놀랐을 것이다. 그 지방에서 브룩 양은 그저 \"잘난 젊은 여자\"였을 뿐이니까.)\n\n\"제발 그 얘기는 그만하게, 나우만. 캐소본 부인은 마치 모델이라도 되는 양 입에 올릴 분이 아니야.\" 윌이 말했다. 나우만은 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"쇤! 그럼 내 아퀴나스 얘기나 하지. 그 두상은 결국 나쁘지 않은 유형이야. 위대한 스콜라 학자 본인도 제 초상을 그려 달라는 청을 받으면 우쭐했을 게 분명해. 허영으로 치면 그 빳빳한 박사님들만 한 자들이 없거든! 내 생각대로였어. 그 사람은 제 초상보다 아내 초상에 훨씬 마음을 덜 쓰더군.\"\n\n\"그자는 빌어먹을, 피가 허옇게 식은 현학적인 멋쟁이 바보야.\" 윌이 이를 갈듯 격하게 말했다. 캐소본 씨에 대한 그의 빚을 그 청자는 알지 못했으나, 윌 자신은 그것을 떠올리며, 수표 한 장으로 그 모든 것을 청산할 수 있다면 좋겠다고 바라고 있었다.\n\n나우만은 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"저 사람들이 곧 떠나는 건 다행이야, 이 친구야. 자네의 그 좋은 성미를 망쳐 놓고 있으니 말이지.\"\n\n이제 윌의 온갖 희망과 궁리는 도러시아가 혼자 있을 때 그녀를 만나는 데 집중되었다. 그는 다만 그녀가 자기를 좀 더 또렷이 마음에 새겨 주기를 바랐다. 그는 다만 아직은 자기가 그러리라 믿기 어려운 무엇, 그녀의 기억 속에서 좀 더 특별한 무엇이 되기를 바랐다. 그녀의 그 솔직하고 열렬한 호의가—그것이 그녀의 평소 감정 상태임을 그는 알았다—그는 다소 견디기 어려웠다. 손이 닿지 않는 옥좌에 앉은 여인을 멀리서 흠모하는 일은 남자들의 삶에서 큰 몫을 차지하지만, 대개의 경우 흠모하는 자는 어떤 여왕다운 인정을, 자기 영혼의 군주가 그 높은 자리에서 내려오지 않은 채 자기를 격려해 줄 어떤 승인의 표시를 갈망한다. 윌이 바란 것이 바로 그것이었다. 그러나 그의 상상적 요구에는 모순이 가득했다. 도러시아의 두 눈이 아내다운 근심과 간청을 담아 캐소본 씨에게로 향하는 것을 보는 일은 아름다웠다. 그 의무에 찬 골몰함이 없었더라면 그녀는 후광의 일부를 잃었으리라. 그러면서도 바로 다음 순간, 그런 감로를 모래처럼 빨아들이는 남편의 모습은 도무지 참을 수가 없었다. 그래서 윌이 그를 헐뜯는 말을 하고 싶어 안달하는 마음은, 그것을 억눌러야 할 더없이 강력한 이유들을 느끼고 있었기에 어쩌면 그만큼 더 괴로운 것이었는지도 모른다.\n\n윌은 이튿날 만찬에 초대받지 못했다. 그래서 그는 자기가 마땅히 방문해야 한다고, 그리고 알맞은 시간은 캐소본 씨가 집에 없을 한낮뿐이라고 스스로를 설득했다.\n\n전에 윌을 맞이한 일이 남편의 심기를 거슬렀다는 것을 알지 못한 도러시아는, 그를 만나는 데 아무 망설임이 없었으니, 특히나 그가 작별 인사를 하러 온 것일 수도 있었기 때문이다. 그가 들어섰을 때 그녀는 실리아에게 주려고 사 둔 카메오 몇 점을 들여다보고 있었다. 그녀는 그의 방문이 지극히 당연한 일이라는 듯 윌을 맞이했고, 손에 카메오 팔찌를 든 채 곧바로 말했다.\n\n\"와 주셔서 정말 기뻐요. 어쩌면 카메오에 대해 잘 아실 테니, 이것들이 정말 좋은 건지 말씀해 주실 수 있겠네요. 고를 때 당신이 우리와 함께 있어 주기를 바랐는데, 캐소본 씨가 반대하셨어요. 시간이 없다고 생각하셨거든요. 그이는 내일 일을 끝낼 테고, 우리는 사흘 뒤에 떠나요. 이 카메오들 때문에 마음이 편치 않았어요. 부디 앉아서 좀 봐 주세요.\"\n\n\"제가 특별히 잘 아는 건 아니지만, 이 자그마한 호메로스풍 조각들에 대해서는 큰 실수가 있을 수 없어요. 더없이 정교하고 깔끔하니까요. 색도 좋고요. 부인께 꼭 어울릴 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오, 이것들은 제 동생 거예요. 그 애는 안색이 아주 다르거든요. 로윅에서 저와 함께 있는 걸 보셨지요. 머리가 밝은 빛이고 무척 예뻐요—적어도 제 생각엔 그래요. 우리는 평생 이렇게 오래 떨어져 지낸 적이 없었어요. 그 애는 정말 귀여운 아이고 한 번도 못된 짓을 한 적이 없답니다. 떠나오기 전에 그 애가 카메오를 좀 사다 주길 바란다는 걸 알게 됐는데, 이게—그 나름으로—좋은 것이 아니면 안타까울 것 같아요.\" 도러시아가 마지막 말을 미소와 함께 덧붙였다.\n\n\"카메오에는 별 관심이 없으신 모양이군요.\" 윌이 그녀에게서 얼마간 떨어진 자리에 앉아, 그녀가 갑들을 닫는 동안 그녀를 지켜보며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 솔직히 그게 인생에서 대단한 목표라고는 생각하지 않아요.\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"미술 전반에 대해 이단자이신 게 아닌가 싶군요. 어째서지요? 저는 부인이 어디서든 아름다운 것에 무척 민감하시리라 짐작했는데요.\"\n\n\"많은 것에 무딘가 봐요.\" 도러시아가 솔직하게 말했다. \"저는 삶을 아름답게 만들고 싶어요—모두의 삶을 말이에요. 그런데 미술에 들이는 이 엄청난 비용은, 어쩐지 삶의 바깥에 놓여 세상을 조금도 더 낫게 해 주지 못하는 것 같아서 마음이 아파요. 대다수의 사람들이 그것에서 배제되어 있다는 생각이 들면, 무엇을 즐기든 그 즐거움이 망가져 버려요.\"\n\n\"저는 그걸 동정의 광신이라고 부릅니다.\" 윌이 격하게 말했다. \"그런 식이면 풍경에 대해서도, 시에 대해서도, 온갖 세련된 것 전부에 대해서도 똑같이 말할 수 있겠지요. 그걸 끝까지 밀고 나간다면, 부인은 자기 자신의 선함 속에서 비참해야 하고, 남들보다 나은 점을 갖지 않으려고 스스로 악해져야 할 겁니다. 최선의 경건함은 즐기는 것입니다—즐길 수 있을 때 말이지요. 그래야 이 지구를 살 만한 행성으로 지키는 데 가장 크게 이바지하는 것이고요. 그리고 즐거움은 사방으로 퍼져 나갑니다. 온 세상을 다 돌보려 애써 봐야 소용없어요. 당신이—미술에서든 다른 무엇에서든—기쁨을 느낄 때, 그것이 곧 세상을 돌보는 일이니까요. 부인은 세상의 모든 젊은이를 비참함을 두고 통곡하며 설교하는 비극의 합창단으로 만들고 싶으신가요? 제 생각에 부인은 비참함의 미덕에 대해 무언가 그릇된 믿음을 품고 있고, 자기 삶을 순교로 만들고 싶어 하는 것 같습니다.\" 윌은 작정했던 것보다 더 멀리 나가 버려 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 도러시아의 생각은 그의 것과 똑같은 방향으로 흐르고 있지는 않았으므로, 그녀는 별다른 감정 없이 대답했다.\n\n\"정말이지 당신은 저를 오해하고 있어요. 저는 슬프고 우울한 사람이 아니에요. 오래도록 불행한 적은 한 번도 없어요. 저는 화도 잘 내고 못되게도 굴어요—실리아와는 다르죠. 한바탕 크게 터뜨리고 나면, 다시 모든 것이 찬란해 보여요. 저는 어쩐지 맹목적으로나마 찬란한 것들을 믿지 않을 수가 없어요. 이곳의 미술도 기꺼이 즐기고 싶지만, 까닭을 모를 것이 너무 많아요—아름다움이라기보다 추함을 봉헌한 것처럼 보이는 게 너무 많고요. 회화와 조각은 경이로울지 몰라도, 그 정서는 흔히 저열하고 잔혹하며, 때로는 우스꽝스럽기까지 해요. 여기저기서 단번에 저를 사로잡는 고귀한 것을—알반 산맥이나 핀치오 언덕에서 보는 석양에 견줄 만한 무언가를—보기도 해요. 하지만 그렇기에, 사람들이 그토록 애써 만들어 낸 그 숱한 것들 가운데 최상의 것이 그토록 적다는 게 더욱 안타까운 거예요.\"\n\n\"당연히 형편없는 작품은 늘 많기 마련이지요. 더 드문 것들은 그런 토양이 있어야 자라니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 저런.\" 도러시아가 그 생각을 자기 근심의 주된 흐름 속으로 끌어들이며 말했다. \"무언가 좋은 것을 해낸다는 게 무척 어려운 일이라는 걸 알겠어요. 로마에 와 있는 동안 저는, 우리 삶의 대부분이 벽에 걸어 놓을 수 있다면 그림보다 훨씬 더 추하고 서투르게 보이리라는 생각을 자주 했어요.\"\n\n도러시아는 무언가 더 말하려는 듯 다시 입술을 떼었으나, 마음을 바꾸어 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"부인은 너무 젊으세요—그런 생각을 품다니 시대착오예요.\" 윌이 그 특유의 빠르게 머리를 가로젓는 동작과 함께 힘주어 말했다. \"마치 젊음이라곤 한 번도 알아 본 적 없는 사람처럼 말씀하시는군요. 괴이한 일이에요—마치 전설 속 그 소년처럼 어린 시절에 하데스의 환영을 본 사람처럼 말이에요. 부인은 미노타우로스처럼 가장 사랑스러운 여인들을 골라 집어삼키는 그런 끔찍한 관념들 속에서 자라셨군요. 그런데 이제 로윅의 그 돌 감옥에 갇히러 가시겠다니. 산 채로 묻히는 거예요. 그 생각만 하면 사나워져요! 그런 앞날을 그리느니 차라리 부인을 만나지 않았던 편이 나았을 겁니다.\"\n\n윌은 또다시 너무 멀리 나갔다고 두려워했다. 그러나 말에 우리가 부여하는 의미는 우리의 감정에 달려 있는 법이라, 그 화난 듯 안타까워하는 어조에는 도러시아의 마음을 향한 다정함이 너무도 많이 담겨 있었으니—언제나 열정을 내어 주기만 했을 뿐 주위의 살아 있는 존재들로부터 그리 많이 받아 본 적 없는 그 마음에—그녀는 새삼스러운 고마움을 느끼며 부드러운 미소로 대답했다.\n\n\"저를 그토록 걱정해 주시다니 참 고마워요. 당신 자신이 로윅을 좋아하지 않았기 때문이겠지요. 당신은 다른 종류의 삶에 마음을 두었으니까요. 하지만 로윅은 제가 택한 보금자리예요.\"\n\n마지막 문장은 거의 엄숙한 가락으로 말해졌고, 윌은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그녀의 신발에 입 맞추며 그녀를 위해 죽겠노라 말해 봐야 그에게 아무 소용이 없을 테니까. 그녀가 그런 것을 조금도 요구하지 않는다는 것은 분명했다. 두 사람은 한두 순간 잠자코 있었는데, 이윽고 도러시아가 미리 마음에 두고 있던 것을 마침내 꺼내려는 기색으로 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"요전 날 당신이 하신 말씀에 대해 한 번 더 여쭤보고 싶었어요. 어쩌면 그건 절반쯤은 당신의 발랄한 말버릇이었겠지요. 당신이 무엇이든 강하게 표현하기를 좋아하신다는 걸 저도 알아요. 저 역시 급하게 말할 때면 곧잘 과장하니까요.\"\n\n\"무엇이었지요?\" 윌이, 그녀에게서 전에 없던 머뭇거림을 알아채며 말했다. \"제 혀가 과장이 심해서요. 굴러가면서 불이 붙거든요. 어쩌면 철회해야 할지도 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"독일어를 알아야 할 필요가 있다고 하신 말씀 말이에요—제 말은, 캐소본 씨가 몰두하고 계신 주제들에 대해서요. 그걸 곰곰이 생각해 봤는데, 캐소본 씨만한 학식이라면 독일 학자들과 같은 자료를 앞에 두고 계시지 않을까 싶어요—그렇지 않나요?\" 도러시아의 머뭇거림은, 자기가 제삼자에게 캐소본 씨의 학식이 충분한지를 의논하는 묘한 처지에 놓여 있다는 어렴풋한 자각에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n\"꼭 같은 자료는 아니지요.\" 윌이 마땅히 신중해야겠다고 생각하며 말했다. \"그분은 동양학자가 아니시잖아요. 그 분야에 대해서는 간접적인 지식 이상을 가졌다고 자처하지 않으시고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 고대에 관한 매우 귀중한 책들 중에는 이런 근래의 것들에 대해 아무것도 몰랐던 학자들이 아주 오래전에 쓴 것들도 있고, 그것들은 여전히 쓰이고 있잖아요. 어째서 캐소본 씨의 것은 그들의 것처럼 귀중하지 않다는 거죠?\" 도러시아가 한층 항변하는 기세로 말했다. 그녀는 마음속에서 벌여 온 그 논쟁을 소리 내어 펼치지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그건 어떤 연구 노선을 택하느냐에 달려 있어요.\" 윌도 받아치는 어조를 띠며 말했다. \"캐소본 씨가 택하신 주제는 화학만큼이나 변화무쌍합니다. 새로운 발견이 끊임없이 새로운 관점을 만들어 내니까요. 누가 이제 와서 사원소설에 기초한 체계를 원하겠어요, 아니면 파라켈수스를 반박하는 책을 원하겠습니까? 모르시겠어요? 브라이언트 같은 지난 세기 사람들의 뒤를 조금 기어가며 그들의 오류를 바로잡아 봐야 이제는 아무 소용이 없다는 걸요—잡동사니 방에 틀어박혀 쿠스니 미스라임이니 하는 다리 부러진 이론들을 손질하는 게 말이에요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그렇게 가볍게 말씀하실 수 있어요?\" 도러시아가 슬픔과 분노 사이의 표정으로 말했다. \"만약 당신 말대로라면, 그토록 열렬한 노고가 죄다 헛수고였다는 것보다 더 슬픈 일이 어디 있겠어요? 캐소본 씨처럼 선함과 능력과 학식을 그토록 많이 갖춘 분이, 평생 가장 좋은 시절을 바친 일에서 어떤 식으로든 실패하리라고 정말로 생각하신다면, 그게 당신에게 더 가슴 아프게 다가오지 않는다는 게 의아하군요.\" 그녀는 자기가 그런 가정의 지점에까지 이르렀다는 데 충격을 받기 시작했고, 자기를 거기까지 이끈 윌에게 분개했다.\n\n\"부인은 제게 사실에 관해 물으셨지, 감정에 관해 물으신 게 아니지요.\" 윌이 말했다. \"하지만 그 사실 때문에 저를 벌하고 싶으시다면, 받아들이겠습니다. 저는 캐소본 씨에 대한 제 감정을 표현할 처지가 못 됩니다. 기껏해야 식객의 찬사가 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"부디 용서하세요.\" 도러시아가 얼굴을 짙게 붉히며 말했다. \"말씀대로, 이 주제를 꺼낸 제게 잘못이 있다는 걸 알아요. 정말이지 제가 통째로 잘못했어요. 오랜 끈기 끝의 실패가, 실패라 부를 만큼도 되지 못하는 미약한 분투보다 훨씬 더 위대한 것인데요.\"\n\n\"전적으로 동감입니다.\" 윌이 상황을 바꾸기로 마음먹고 말했다. \"어찌나 동감인지, 저는 실패에조차 결코 이르지 못할 그 위험을 무릅쓰지 않기로 작정했답니다. 캐소본 씨의 관대함이 어쩌면 제게 위험한 것이었는지도 모르니, 그것이 제게 준 자유를 저는 단념하려 합니다. 머지않아 영국으로 돌아가 제 힘으로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 생각이에요—저 자신 말고는 누구에게도 기대지 않고요.\"\n\n\"훌륭해요—그런 마음 존경해요.\" 도러시아가 다시금 다정함을 되찾으며 말했다. \"하지만 캐소본 씨는, 분명히, 이 일에서 당신의 안녕에 가장 도움이 되는 것 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않으셨을 거예요.\"\n\n'이제 그자와 결혼했으니, 저 여자에겐 사랑 대신 노릇을 해 줄 만한 고집과 자존심이 넘치는군.' 윌은 속으로 중얼거렸다. 소리 내어서는, 자리에서 일어나며 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"다시는 부인을 뵙지 못하겠군요.\"\n\n\"오, 캐소본 씨가 오실 때까지 계세요.\" 도러시아가 간곡히 말했다. \"우리가 로마에서 만나게 되어 정말 기뻐요. 저는 당신을 알고 싶었어요.\"\n\n\"그런데 제가 부인을 화나게 만들었군요.\" 윌이 말했다. \"제가 부인이 저를 나쁘게 생각하시도록 만들었어요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요. 동생 말로는 제가 제 마음에 꼭 드는 말을 하지 않는 사람들에게 늘 화를 낸대요. 하지만 그 사람들을 나쁘게 생각하는 버릇은 없기를 바라요. 결국에는 늘, 제가 그토록 성급했던 걸 두고 제 자신을 나쁘게 생각하게 되거든요.\"\n\n\"그래도, 부인은 저를 좋아하지 않으시는군요. 제가 부인께 언짢은 생각거리가 되어 버렸어요.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 도러시아가 더없이 솔직한 다정함으로 말했다. \"저는 당신이 무척 좋아요.\"\n\n윌은 썩 만족스럽지 않았으니, 차라리 미움을 받았더라면 자기가 더 중요한 사람이었을 텐데 하는 생각이 들었기 때문이다. 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 시무룩하다고까지는 않더라도 멍한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"그리고 저는 당신이 무슨 일을 하게 될지 무척 궁금해요.\" 도러시아가 명랑하게 말을 이었다. \"저는 타고난 천직의 차이를 독실하게 믿어요. 그런 믿음이 없다면 저는 아마 무척 편협한 사람이 되었을 거예요—그림 말고도 제가 도무지 모르는 것이 너무 많거든요. 당신이 그토록 잘 아는 음악과 문학을 제가 얼마나 조금밖에 받아들이지 못했는지 들으면 좀처럼 믿기지 않으실 거예요. 당신의 천직이 무엇으로 드러날지 궁금해요. 어쩌면 시인이 되실까요?\"\n\n\"그건 경우에 따라 다르지요. 시인이 된다는 것은, 어떤 질의 미묘한 차이도 놓치지 않을 만큼 분별이 빠르고, 또 느낌이 빨라서, 그 분별이 곧 정교하게 배열된 다채로움을 감정의 현 위에서 켜는 손이 되는 그런 영혼을 갖는 것입니다—지식이 순식간에 느낌으로 옮겨 가고, 느낌이 다시 새로운 지식의 기관이 되어 번뜩이는 그런 영혼을요. 그런 상태는 발작적으로만 찾아올 수도 있고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 시는 빼놓으시는군요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"시인을 완성하려면 시가 필요할 것 같아요. 지식이 느낌으로 옮겨 간다는 그 말씀은 이해해요, 바로 그게 제가 경험하는 것 같으니까요. 하지만 저는 분명 시 한 편도 결코 지어내지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인 자신이 한 편의 시예요—그리고 그것이야말로 시인의 가장 좋은 부분, 최상의 순간에 시인의 의식을 이루는 것이지요.\" 윌은, 우리 모두가 아침이며 봄철이며 그 밖의 끝없는 갱신들과 더불어 나누어 갖는 그런 독창성을 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 정말 기뻐요.\" 도러시아가 새처럼 가락 있는 어조로 웃음 섞인 말을 내뱉으며, 장난스러운 고마움을 눈에 담아 윌을 바라보았다. \"저에게 어쩜 그렇게 다정한 말씀을 하세요!\"\n\n\"제가 부인이 다정하다 부르실 만한 일을 한 번이라도 할 수 있다면—부인께 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있다면 좋겠습니다. 그런 기회는 결코 오지 않을 것 같아 두렵지만요.\" 윌이 열정을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그래요.\" 도러시아가 진심으로 말했다. \"기회는 올 거예요. 그러면 저는 당신이 저를 얼마나 잘 되기를 바랐는지 떠올릴 거예요. 당신을 처음 봤을 때 저는 우리가 친구가 되기를 무척 바랐어요—당신이 캐소본 씨의 친척이니까요.\" 그녀의 두 눈에는 어떤 촉촉한 빛이 어렸고, 윌은 자기 눈도 자연의 법칙을 따라 그렁그렁 차오르는 것을 의식했다. 만약 그 순간 무언가가 그녀의 그 고결하고 의심을 모르는 순진함이 지닌 압도하는 힘과 감미로운 기품을 망쳐 놓을 수 있었다면, 캐소본 씨에 대한 그 언급이 모든 것을 망쳐 놓았으리라.\n\n\"그리고 지금 당장이라도 당신이 해 주실 수 있는 일이 하나 있어요.\" 도러시아가, 되살아나는 충동에 떠밀려 일어서서 몇 걸음 거닐며 말했다. \"다시는, 그 누구에게도, 그 주제에 대해—제 말은 캐소본 씨의 저작에 대해—그런 식으로 말하지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요. 그 얘기를 꺼낸 건 저였어요. 제 잘못이에요. 그래도 약속해 주세요.\"\n\n그녀는 짧은 거닐기를 마치고 돌아와 윌의 맞은편에 서서, 그를 진지하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"물론, 약속하지요.\" 윌이 그러면서도 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 다시는 캐소본 씨에 대해 모진 말을 하지 않고 그에게서 호의를 받는 일도 그만둔다면, 그를 더욱더 미워하는 것쯤은 분명 허용될 만한 일이었다. 시인은 미워할 줄 알아야 한다고 괴테는 말했으니, 윌은 적어도 그 재주만큼은 갖추고 있었다. 그는 캐소본 씨를 기다리지 않고 이제 가 봐야겠다고, 그분께는 떠나기 직전 마지막 순간에 작별 인사를 하러 오겠다고 말했다. 도러시아가 그에게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람은 간소한 \"안녕히\"를 주고받았다.\n\n그러나 정문을 나서다가 그는 캐소본 씨와 마주쳤고, 그 신사는 자기 사촌에게 더없는 행운을 빈다고 하면서, 출발 준비로 충분히 분주할 이튿날에 다시 작별 인사를 나누는 수고는 정중히 사양했다.\n\n\"우리 사촌 래디슬로 씨에 대해 당신에게 들려줄 이야기가 있어요. 그에 대한 당신의 평가가 한층 높아질 것 같은데요.\" 그날 저녁 도러시아가 남편에게 말했다. 그녀는 그가 들어서자마자 윌이 막 다녀갔으며 다시 오리라고 일러두었으나, 캐소본 씨는 \"밖에서 그를 만났소, 그리고 우리는 마지막 작별을 나눈 것 같소\" 하고 말했었다. 사적인 것이든 공적인 것이든 어떤 주제도 더 언급하고 싶을 만큼 흥미롭지는 않다는 뜻을 담은 그런 태도와 어조로 말이다. 그래서 도러시아는 기다렸던 것이다.\n\n\"무엇이오, 여보?\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다(그는 태도가 가장 차가울 때면 늘 \"여보\"라고 했다).\n\n\"그이는 이제 곧 떠돌이 생활을 그만두고, 당신의 관대함에 기대는 일도 그만두기로 마음먹었대요. 머지않아 영국으로 돌아가 제 힘으로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 생각이래요. 당신도 그걸 좋은 징조로 여기시리라 생각했어요.\" 도러시아가 남편의 무덤덤한 얼굴을 호소하듯 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그가 어떤 일에 몸 바칠지 그 정확한 종류를 언급하던가?\"\n\n\"아니요. 하지만 당신의 관대함 속에 자기에게 도사린 위험을 느낀다고 했어요. 물론 그 일에 대해 당신에게 편지를 쓰겠지요. 그의 결심을 보고 그를 더 좋게 생각하지 않으세요?\"\n\n\"그 문제에 관한 그의 연락을 기다려 보겠소.\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 그를 위해 하신 모든 일에서 염두에 두신 것은 그 자신의 안녕이었으리라 확신한다고 그에게 말했어요. 로윅에서 그를 처음 봤을 때 당신이 그에 대해 하신 말씀의 그 너그러움을 떠올렸거든요.\" 도러시아가 남편의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그에게 의무가 있었소.\" 캐소본 씨가 양심적으로 그녀의 애정 표시를 받아들이며 다른 한 손을 도러시아의 손 위에 얹었으나, 그 눈길만은 불안한 빛을 띠는 것을 어쩌지 못했다. \"그 젊은이가, 고백하건대, 그 밖의 점에서 내게 흥미를 끄는 대상은 아니오. 또 그의 앞날을 우리가 논할 필요도 없을 것 같소. 그것을 정하는 일은 내가 충분히 그어 놓은 한계를 넘어서서는 우리 몫이 아니니 말이오.\" 도러시아는 다시는 윌을 입에 올리지 않았다.\n\n# BOOK III. WAITING FOR DEATH. CHAPTER XXIII.\n\n“그리고 일류 채찍잡이 아폴로여!\n저들이 무엇이든, 내 목을 걸겠노라,\n\n내 저들을 완패시키고 말 테니.”\n앞서 보았듯이 프레드 빈시는 빚을 마음에 품고 있었다. 비록 그런 비물질적인 짐 따위가 이 들뜬 마음의 젊은 신사를 여러 시간 내내 짓누를 수는 없었지만, 이 빚에는 그 생각을 유난히 집요하게 만드는 사정들이 얽혀 있었다. 채권자는 인근의 말 거래상인 뱀브리지였는데, 미들마치에서 “쾌락에 빠졌다”고 알려진 젊은이들이 그의 교제를 무척 탐냈다. 방학 동안 프레드는 자연히 수중의 현금보다 더 많은 오락을 필요로 했고, 뱀브리지는 마음씨가 후하여 말을 빌려주거나 멋진 사냥말을 망가뜨려 생긴 우발적 비용을 외상으로 봐주었을 뿐 아니라, 당구에서 본 약간의 손실을 메울 수 있도록 소액을 미리 빌려주기까지 했다. 빚의 총액은 백육십 파운드였다. 뱀브리지는 자기 돈에 대해 전혀 걱정하지 않았으니, 빈시 청년에게 뒤를 봐줄 사람들이 있다고 확신했기 때문이다. 다만 그는 증서를 요구했고, 프레드는 처음에 자기 서명으로 어음을 끊어 주었다. 석 달 뒤 그는 케일럽 가스의 서명을 받아 이 어음을 갱신했다. 두 번 다 프레드는 자기가 직접 어음을 갚으리라 확신했으니, 자기 자신의 낙관 속에 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 넉넉한 자금을 두고 있었던 것이다. 그의 확신이 외적인 사실에 근거를 두기를 요구하지는 않으리라. 우리가 알다시피 그런 확신은 그보다 덜 거칠고 덜 물질적인 무엇이다. 그것은 섭리의 지혜나 우리 친구들의 어리석음, 행운의 신비나 우주 안에서 우리 개인의 높은 가치라는 더욱 큰 신비가, 우리의 의상 취향이나 가장 좋은 것을 선호하는 우리의 일반적 성향에 어울리는 흐뭇한 결말을 가져다주리라 기대하게 만드는, 편안한 기질인 것이다. 프레드는 자기가 삼촌에게서 선물을 받으리라고, 운이 트이리라고, “바꿔치기”를 거듭하다 보면 사십 파운드짜리 말이 언제든 백 파운드를 부를 만한 말로 점차 변신하리라고 확신했다 — “안목”이란 늘 액수를 정하지 않은 현금과 맞먹는 것이었으니까. 그리고 어떤 경우에도, 심지어 병적인 불신만이 상상할 법한 부정적 상황을 가정한다 해도, 프레드에게는 (그 무렵) 늘 최후의 수단으로 아버지의 지갑이 있었으니, 그의 낙관이라는 자산에는 일종의 호화로운 과잉이 깃들어 있었다. 아버지의 지갑이 어느 정도 능력을 지녔는지 프레드는 막연한 생각만 가졌을 뿐이었다. 장사란 탄력적인 것이 아니던가? 한 해의 부족분은 다른 해의 잉여로 메워지지 않겠는가? 빈시 집안은 여유롭고 풍족하게 살았는데, 무슨 새삼스러운 과시에서가 아니라 집안의 습관과 전통에 따른 것이었으므로, 아이들에게는 절약의 기준이라는 것이 없었고, 손위 아이들은 마음만 먹으면 아버지가 무엇이든 치러 줄 수 있다는 어린 시절의 관념을 어느 정도 간직하고 있었다. 빈시 씨 자신도 미들마치의 돈 드는 습관을 지니고 있었다 — 사냥개 경주에, 술 창고에, 만찬 베푸는 데 돈을 썼고, 어머니는 상인들과 외상 거래를 텄으니, 이는 지불 문제는 따지지 않고 원하는 것을 모두 손에 넣는다는 유쾌한 기분을 안겨 주었다. 그러나 비용 문제로 사람을 윽박지르는 것이 아버지들의 본성임을 프레드는 알고 있었다. 빚을 털어놓아야 할 때면 늘 그의 낭비를 두고 작은 폭풍이 일었고, 프레드는 집 안의 궂은 날씨를 싫어했다. 그는 너무도 효성스러워 아버지에게 불경하게 굴지 못했고, 그것이 일시적임을 확신하며 천둥을 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 그동안 어머니가 우는 것을 보거나 재미있게 놀지 못한 채 부루퉁한 표정을 지어야 하는 것은 영 달갑지 않았다. 프레드는 천성이 워낙 온순해서 꾸지람을 들으며 시무룩한 표정을 짓는다 해도 그것은 주로 체면을 차리기 위해서였다. 분명 더 쉬운 길은 친구의 서명을 받아 어음을 갱신하는 것이었다. 왜 안 되겠는가? 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 희망의 과잉 담보를 손에 쥔 그로서는, 남들의 채무를 얼마든지 늘리지 못할 이유가 없었다. 다만 이름값이 좀 나가는 사람들이 대개 비관주의자라서, 만물의 보편적 질서가 어느 호감 가는 젊은 신사에게 반드시 호의적이리라고는 믿으려 들지 않는다는 사실만 빼면 말이다.\n\n부탁할 일이 생기면 우리는 친구 목록을 점검하고, 그들의 더 사랑스러운 자질을 공정하게 평가하며, 그들의 사소한 잘못을 용서하고, 차례차례 한 사람씩 두고 그가 기꺼이 우리 청을 들어주리라는 결론에 이르려 애쓴다. 신세 지고 싶어 하는 우리 자신의 열의가 다른 온기와 마찬가지로 전염성을 지닌 까닭이다. 그래도 늘 일정한 수의 사람들은 다른 이들이 거절할 때까지는 기껏해야 미적지근하다고 제쳐 두기 마련이다. 그리하여 프레드는 한 사람만 빼고 모든 친구를 지워 버렸는데, 그들에게 부탁하는 것은 달갑지 않으리라는 이유에서였다. 그는 적어도 자기만큼은 (인류 일반에 대해서는 무어라 주장하든 간에) 달갑지 않은 일에서 면제받을 권리가 있다고 은연중에 확신하고 있었던 것이다. 자기가 정녕 철저히 불쾌한 처지에 빠진다는 것 — 빨아서 줄어든 바지를 입고, 식은 양고기를 먹으며, 말이 없어 걸어 다니거나 어떤 식으로든 “고개를 숙이고 들어가야” 한다는 것 — 은 천성이 그에게 심어 준 그 명랑한 직관과는 도무지 화해할 수 없는 부조리였다. 그리고 프레드는 사소한 빚을 갚을 자금조차 없는 자로 멸시당한다는 생각에 움찔했다. 이리하여 그가 부탁하기로 고른 친구는 가장 가난하면서 동시에 가장 친절한 사람, 곧 케일럽 가스가 되었다.\n\n가스 집안은 프레드를 무척 좋아했고, 프레드도 그들을 좋아했다. 그와 로저먼드가 어렸을 적, 가스 집안 형편이 더 나았을 때, 페더스톤 씨의 두 번 결혼(첫 번째는 가스 씨의 누이와, 두 번째는 빈시 부인과)을 통해 맺어진 두 집안의 희미한 인연이 아이들 사이에서 — 부모들보다는 — 이어지는 교분을 낳았던 것이다. 아이들은 장난감 찻잔으로 함께 차를 마셨고, 온종일 함께 놀며 지냈다. 메리는 자그마한 말괄량이였고, 여섯 살의 프레드는 그녀가 세상에서 가장 멋진 여자아이라고 여겨, 우산에서 잘라 낸 놋쇠 고리로 그녀를 아내로 삼았다. 교육의 온갖 단계를 거치는 내내 그는 가스 집안에 대한 애정을 간직했고, 그 집을 제2의 고향처럼 드나드는 버릇을 지켰으니, 그들과 그의 집안 어른들 사이의 왕래는 오래전에 끊겼는데도 그러했다. 케일럽 가스가 형편이 넉넉했던 시절에도 빈시 집안은 그와 그의 아내를 한 수 아래로 대했으니, 미들마치에는 신분의 미묘한 구별이 있었기 때문이다. 옛 제조업자들이 공작들과 마찬가지로 동등한 자들하고만 어울릴 수는 없었지만, 그들은 이론적으로는 거의 표현할 수 없되 실제로는 대단히 정교하게 구분되는, 타고난 사회적 우월감을 의식하고 있었다. 그 후 가스 씨는 건축업에서 실패했으니, 그는 불행히도 측량사, 감정사, 대리인이라는 다른 본업에 그것을 더해 두었던 것이다. 한동안은 오로지 채권 양수인들의 이익을 위해 그 사업을 꾸려야 했고, 결국 1파운드당 20실링을 다 갚으려고 안간힘을 쓰며 빠듯하게 살아왔다. 이제 그는 그것을 이루어 냈고, 그것을 나쁜 선례로 여기지 않는 모든 이들로부터 그의 명예로운 노력은 마땅한 존경을 얻었다. 그러나 세상 어디에서도 점잖은 방문 교제가, 알맞은 가구와 완비된 만찬 식기 없이 존경만으로 이루어지지는 않는다. 빈시 부인은 가스 부인 앞에서 한 번도 편안한 적이 없었고, 그녀를 두고 흔히 빵을 벌기 위해 일해야 했던 여자라고 말했다 — 가스 부인이 결혼 전에 교사였다는 뜻이었다. 그렇다면 린들리 머리와 맹널의 문제집에 정통한 것이란, 포목상이 옥양목 상표를 분간하는 것이나 안내인이 외국에 대해 아는 것과 비슷한 셈이었다. 형편이 더 나은 여자라면 그런 것이 필요할 리 없었으니까. 그리고 메리가 페더스톤 씨의 살림을 맡게 된 뒤로, 가스 집안에 대한 빈시 부인의 호감 부족은 더 적극적인 무언가로 바뀌었으니, 부모가 “그토록 가난하게 사는” 이 못생긴 여자아이에게 프레드가 약혼이라도 할까 봐 불안했던 것이다. 이를 알고 있던 프레드는 집에서 가스 부인을 찾아간 일을 결코 입에 올리지 않았는데, 그 방문은 근래 들어 더 잦아졌다. 메리에 대한 사랑이 점점 더 뜨거워지면서 그녀에게 속한 이들에게 그가 더욱 끌렸던 까닭이다.\n\n가스 씨는 읍내에 작은 사무실을 두고 있었고, 프레드는 부탁을 가지고 그곳을 찾아갔다. 그는 별 어려움 없이 부탁을 들어주었으니, 숱한 쓰라린 경험도 케일럽 가스로 하여금 제 일에 신중을 기하게 하거나, 아직 미덥지 못함이 드러나지 않은 동료들을 불신하게 하지는 못했기 때문이다. 게다가 그는 프레드를 더없이 높이 평가하여, “그 녀석은 분명 잘될 거야 — 솔직하고 다정한 친구인 데다, 사람 됨됨이의 바탕이 좋거든 — 무슨 일이든 믿고 맡길 만하지”라고 했다. 이것이 케일럽의 심리학적 논거였다. 그는 자기 자신에게는 엄격하면서 남에게는 너그러운 보기 드문 사람 가운데 하나였다. 그는 이웃의 잘못에 대해 일종의 부끄러움을 느꼈고, 그것을 기꺼이 입에 올리는 법이 없었다. 그러니 그는 목재를 가장 잘 단단하게 만드는 방법이나 다른 기발한 장치들에 쏠린 마음을 돌려, 그런 잘못을 미리 헤아릴 사람이 아니었다. 누군가를 책망해야 할 때면, 그는 시작도 하기 전에 손이 닿는 종이를 모조리 옮기거나, 지팡이로 갖가지 도면을 그리거나, 주머니 속 잔돈으로 계산을 해 봐야만 했다. 그리고 그는 남이 한 일을 흠잡기보다 차라리 남의 일을 대신 해 주려는 사람이었다. 안타깝게도 그는 서투른 훈육자였던 듯하다.\n\n프레드가 자기 빚의 사정과, 아버지를 번거롭게 하지 않고 그것을 갚고 싶다는 바람과, 누구에게도 불편을 끼치지 않을 만큼 돈이 마련되리라는 확신을 늘어놓자, 케일럽은 안경을 위로 밀어 올리고 귀를 기울이며, 자기가 아끼는 이 청년의 맑고 젊은 눈을 들여다보고는 그를 믿었으니, 미래에 대한 확신과 과거에 대한 진실성을 구별하지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 그는 이것이 처신에 관해 다정한 충고를 건넬 기회라고, 서명을 해 주기 전에 다소 강한 훈계를 해야겠다고 느꼈다. 그리하여 그는 종이를 집어 들고 안경을 내려 쓰고는, 자기가 쓸 자리를 가늠하고, 펜을 집어 살펴보고, 잉크에 적신 뒤 다시 살펴보고, 그런 다음 종이를 살짝 밀어 두고 다시 안경을 위로 올렸다. 그의 덥수룩한 눈썹 바깥쪽 끝이 한층 깊이 처지면서 그의 얼굴에 독특한 온화함을 자아냈으니(이 한 번만은 이런 자질구레함을 너그러이 봐주시기를 — 케일럽 가스를 알았더라면 당신도 그런 점을 사랑하게 되었으리라), 그는 편안한 어조로 말했다.\n\n“말 무릎을 부러뜨린 건, 음, 불운이었지? 그리고 이런 맞바꾸기란, 약삭빠른 말 장수들을 상대할 때는 영 수지가 안 맞아. 다음번엔 더 영리해질 게다, 얘야.”\n\n그러고 나서 케일럽은 안경을 내려 쓰고, 늘 그 일에 기울이던 정성을 다해 자기 서명을 써 내려갔다. 무릇 그는 일이라면 무엇이든 잘 해냈으니까. 그는 큼직하고 균형 잡힌 글자들과 마지막 장식 획을 잠시 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울인 채 들여다보더니, 그것을 프레드에게 건네고 “잘 가게”라고 말한 뒤, 곧장 제임스 체텀 경의 새 농장 건물 설계에 다시 몰두했다.\n\n이 일에 대한 관심이 서명이라는 사건을 그의 기억에서 밀어내 버렸기 때문인지, 아니면 케일럽 자신이 더 의식하고 있던 어떤 이유에서인지, 가스 부인은 그 일을 까맣게 모른 채 지냈다.\n\n그 일이 있은 뒤로 프레드의 하늘에 변화가 찾아왔으니, 그것은 그가 앞날을 바라보는 시야를 바꾸어 놓았고, 페더스톤 삼촌이 준 돈이 그의 얼굴빛을 변하게 할 만큼 — 처음엔 지나치게 분명한 기대로, 그다음엔 그에 걸맞은 실망으로 — 중요해진 까닭이었다. 시험에 통과하지 못한 일 탓에 그가 쌓은 대학 빚은 아버지로서 더욱 용서할 수 없는 것이 되었고, 집에서는 전에 없던 폭풍이 일었다. 빈시 씨는 그런 일을 한 번만 더 참아야 한다면 프레드를 내쫓아 알아서 먹고살게 하겠노라 호언했고, 아들에게 너그럽던 어조를 아직 온전히 되찾지 못했다. 더구나 프레드는 이 마당에 자기는 성직자가 되고 싶지 않으며 “그 일을 계속하지” 않겠다고 말해 그를 특히 격노케 했던 것이다. 프레드는 자기 자신뿐 아니라 가족까지도 그를 페더스톤 씨의 상속인으로 은근히 여기지 않았더라면 더욱 호되게 다루어졌으리라는 것을 의식하고 있었다. 그 노인이 그를 자랑스러워하고 그를 아끼는 듯 보이는 것이, 더 모범적인 처신을 대신해 준 셈이었으니 — 마치 젊은 귀족이 보석을 훔치면 우리는 그 행위를 도벽이라 부르며 철학적인 미소로 이야기할 뿐, 순무를 훔친 누더기 차림의 소년처럼 그를 교화원에 보낼 생각은 결코 하지 않는 것과 같았다. 사실 페더스톤 삼촌이 그에게 무엇을 해 줄 것인가에 대한 무언의 기대가, 미들마치에서 대부분의 사람들이 프레드 빈시를 바라보는 각도를 결정했다. 그리고 그 자신의 의식 속에서도, 위급할 때 페더스톤 삼촌이 무엇을 해 줄 것인가, 혹은 단지 하나의 화신화된 행운으로서 무엇을 해 줄 것인가 하는 것은, 늘 가늠할 수 없이 깊은 공중 원근법을 이루고 있었다. 그러나 일단 건네진 그 지폐 선물은 가늠할 수 있는 것이었고, 그것을 빚의 액수에 적용해 보니 여전히 메워야 할 부족분이 드러났는데, 그것은 프레드의 “안목”이나 다른 형태의 행운으로 채워져야 했다. 빌렸다고 하는 그 작은 일화 — 그가 아버지를 시켜 불스트로드의 증명서를 받게 했던 그 일 — 때문에, 실제 빚을 갚으려고 아버지에게 돈을 청하는 것이 새삼 꺼려졌던 것이다. 프레드는 분노가 구별을 흐려 놓으리라는 것, 그리고 자기가 삼촌의 유언을 명백한 담보로 삼아 돈을 빌린 적이 없다고 부인해 봐야 거짓말로 받아들여지리라는 것을 충분히 예견할 만큼 명민했다. 그는 아버지에게 가서 한 가지 골치 아픈 일은 털어놓고 다른 한 가지는 말하지 않은 채 두었던 것이다. 이런 경우 완전한 폭로란 늘 앞선 이중성이 있었다는 인상을 남기는 법이다. 그런데 프레드는 거짓말은 물론 사소한 허튼소리조차 피하는 것을 자랑으로 삼았다. 그는 로저먼드의 허튼소리라고 부르는 것을 두고 종종 어깨를 으쓱하며 의미심장하게 얼굴을 찌푸리곤 했다(이런 생각을 사랑스러운 처녀와 연결 지을 수 있는 것은 오직 오라비뿐이다). 그리고 거짓말쟁이라는 비난을 받느니 차라리 얼마간의 수고와 자제를 감수하려 했다. 프레드가 그 팔십 파운드를 어머니에게 맡기는 현명한 조치를 취한 것도 이런 강한 내적 압박 아래서였다. 그것을 곧장 가스 씨에게 건네지 않은 것은 아쉬운 일이었다. 그러나 그는 거기에 육십 파운드를 더해 액수를 채울 작정이었고, 이를 위해 이십 파운드를 일종의 씨앗 곡식처럼 제 주머니에 넣어 두었으니, 그것은 안목으로 심고 행운으로 물을 준다면 세 곱절 넘게 거둘 수 있을 터였다 — 밭이 온갖 숫자를 마음대로 부리는 젊은 신사의 무한한 영혼일 때, 그것은 참으로 보잘것없는 증식률이었지만.\n\n프레드는 도박꾼이 아니었다. 그는 우연이나 위험에 온 신경의 기운을 거는 일이 술꾼에게 한 모금 술과 같이 필요해지는, 그런 특정한 병을 앓지는 않았다. 그에게는 다만 그 산만한 형태의 도박 성향이 있을 뿐이었으니, 그것은 알코올 같은 격렬함은 없이 더없이 건강하고 영양을 잘 섭취한 피로 이루어지며, 욕망에 따라 사건을 빚어내는 즐겁고 상상력 넘치는 활동을 유지하고, 제 앞날의 날씨를 두려워하지 않은 채 함께 배에 올라타는 것이 남들에게 분명 이득이 되리라고만 여기는 것이었다. 낙관은 어떤 종류의 던지기든 즐거움을 느끼게 하니, 성공의 전망이 확실하기 때문이다. 그리고 되도록 많은 이에게 그 판돈의 몫을 나눠 주는 데서 한층 너그러운 즐거움을 느낄 뿐이다. 프레드는 놀이를, 특히 당구를 좋아했으니, 사냥이나 장애물 경마를 좋아하는 것과 같았다. 그리고 돈이 필요하고 따기를 바랐기에 그것을 더욱 좋아할 뿐이었다. 그러나 이십 파운드어치 씨앗 곡식은 그 유혹적인 초록 풀밭에 헛되이 뿌려졌으니 — 적어도 길가에 흩어지지 않은 부분은 모두 그러했고 — 프레드는 어머니에게 맡긴 팔십 파운드 말고는 마음대로 쓸 돈 한 푼 없이 지불 기한이 코앞에 닥친 처지가 되었다. 그가 타고 다니는 숨찬 말은 오래전 페더스톤 삼촌이 그에게 준 선물이었다. 아버지는 늘 그가 말 한 필을 두는 것을 허락했으니, 빈시 씨 자신의 습관 탓에 다소 사람 속을 뒤집는 아들에게도 그것만은 합당한 요구로 여겼던 것이다. 그러니 이 말은 프레드의 재산이었고, 임박한 어음을 갚아야 한다는 불안 속에서 그는 그것 없이는 삶이 정녕 무가치해질 소유물을 희생하기로 마음먹었다. 그는 영웅적인 기분으로 그 결심을 했다 — 가스 씨에게 한 약속을 어길까 하는 두려움과 메리에 대한 사랑, 그리고 그녀의 평가에 대한 경외감이 강요한 영웅심이었다. 그는 이튿날 아침 열리는 하운즐리 말 시장으로 출발해서 — 그저 말을 팔고 그 돈을 마차로 가져오면 되지 않을까? — 글쎄, 그 말은 삼십 파운드 이상 받기 어려울 테고, 무슨 일이 일어날지 알 수 없었다. 미리 행운을 가로막는 것은 어리석은 짓이리라. 좋은 기회 하나가 그의 앞에 떨어질 가능성은 백에 아흔아홉이었다. 생각하면 할수록 좋은 기회가 오지 않는다는 것은 더욱 있을 수 없는 일로 여겨졌고, 그것을 떨어뜨릴 화약과 산탄으로 무장하지 않는 것이 더욱 이치에 맞지 않게 느껴졌다. 그는 뱀브리지와 “수의사” 호럭과 함께 하운즐리로 말을 몰고 가서, 무엇 하나 대놓고 묻지 않더라도 사실상 그들의 견해라는 이득을 챙기게 될 것이었다. 길을 떠나기 전에 프레드는 어머니에게서 팔십 파운드를 받아 냈다.\n\n프레드가 뱀브리지와 호럭과 한 무리가 되어 — 물론 하운즐리 말 시장으로 가는 길에 — 미들마치를 빠져나가는 것을 본 대부분의 사람들은, 빈시 청년이 여느 때처럼 쾌락을 좇고 있다고 여겼다. 그리고 중대한 일이 걸려 있다는 낯선 의식만 없었더라면, 그 자신도 방탕에 빠진 듯한 기분, 명랑한 젊은이에게 기대됨 직한 일을 하고 있다는 기분을 느꼈을 것이다. 프레드가 결코 천박하지 않았다는 것, 대학에 다니지 않은 젊은이들의 몸가짐과 말씨를 다소 깔보았다는 것, 그리고 그의 플루트 연주만큼이나 목가적이고 관능과는 거리가 먼 시구를 지었다는 것을 생각하면, 뱀브리지와 호럭에게 끌렸다는 것은 흥미로운 사실이었으니, 인간의 선택을 그토록 많이 좌우하는 그 신비로운 명명(命名)의 영향 없이는 말에 대한 애착만으로 온전히 설명될 수 없는 일이었다. “쾌락” 말고 다른 어떤 이름이 붙었더라면 뱀브리지 씨와 호럭 씨의 교제는 분명 단조롭다고 여겨졌으리라. 그리고 보슬비 내리는 오후에 그들과 함께 하운즐리에 도착하여, 석탄 먼지에 그늘진 거리의 레드 라이언 여관에서 내려, 때에 절어 윤이 나는 그 지방 지도와, 마구간에 선 이름 모를 말의 형편없는 초상화와, 다리와 크라바트를 갖춘 국왕 조지 4세의 그림과, 갖가지 납 타구가 비치된 방에서 식사를 하는 것은, 그런 일을 좇는 것이 “명랑한” 일이라고 못 박은 명명법의 떠받치는 힘이 없었더라면 고된 일로 보였을 것이다.\n\n호럭 씨에게는 분명 상상력에 여지를 주는 헤아릴 수 없는 무언가가 있어 보였다. 차림새는 한눈에 그를 말과 짜릿하게 결부시켰고(아래로 굽었다는 의심을 가까스로 면할 만큼만 살짝 위로 각이 진 모자챙을 짚는 것으로 충분하리라), 또 자연은 그에게 몽골인 같은 눈과, 모자챙을 따라 적당히 위로 기우는 듯한 코와 입과 턱을 지닌 얼굴을 주어, 변치 않는 은근한 회의적 미소의 인상을 자아냈다. 모든 표정 가운데서도 그것은 감수성 예민한 마음을 가장 폭압적으로 지배하는 것이었고, 충분한 침묵이 따를 때면 천하무적의 분별력과, 흐르기엔 너무 메말라 아마도 꿈쩍 않는 딱지 상태에 있을 무진장한 유머와, 만에 하나 당신이 그것을 알 수 있는 행운을 누린다면 다른 어떤 것도 아닌 바로 그것이라 할 비평적 판단력을 갖췄다는 평판을 낳기 십상이었다. 그것은 온갖 직업에서 볼 수 있는 인상이지만, 어쩌면 말 감정가에게서만큼 잉글랜드 젊은이들을 강하게 사로잡은 적은 없었으리라.\n\n프레드가 자기 말의 발목 털에 대해 묻자, 호럭 씨는 안장에서 옆으로 몸을 틀어 삼 분 동안 말의 움직임을 지켜보더니, 다시 앞을 향하고 자기 고삐를 한 번 당긴 뒤, 조금도 더하지도 덜하지도 않게 여전히 회의적인 옆모습으로 침묵을 지켰다.\n\n이렇게 호럭 씨가 대화에서 맡은 역할은 무시무시하게 효과적이었다. 프레드의 마음속에는 갖가지 감정이 뒤섞여 일었다 — 호럭의 견해를 후려쳐서라도 입 밖에 내게 하고 싶은 미친 듯한 욕망과, 그 우정의 이점을 잃지 않으려는 불안이 그것을 억눌렀던 것이다. 호럭이 결정적인 순간에 더없이 값진 무언가를 말할 가능성은 늘 있었다.\n\n뱀브리지 씨는 더 솔직한 태도를 지녀, 자기 생각을 아낌없이 쏟아 내는 듯했다. 그는 목소리가 크고 다부졌으며, 때로는 “방종에 빠졌다”고 일컬어졌으니 — 주로 욕설과 술, 그리고 아내를 때리는 일에서였다. 그에게 손해를 본 어떤 사람들은 그를 악당이라 불렀다. 그러나 그는 말 거래를 가장 훌륭한 기예로 여겼고, 그것이 도덕과는 아무 상관이 없다고 그럴듯하게 주장할 법도 했다. 그가 번창하는 사람임은 부인할 수 없었으니, 남들이 절제를 견디는 것보다 그가 술을 견디는 솜씨가 나았고, 대체로 푸른 월계수처럼 무성하게 번성했다. 그러나 그의 화제의 폭은 한정되어 있어서, “브랜디 방울”이라는 그 오래되고 멋진 곡조처럼, 얼마 지나면 약한 머리는 어지러워질 만큼 같은 자리를 맴도는 느낌을 주었다. 그래도 뱀브리지 씨가 살짝 섞여 들면 미들마치의 여러 모임에 색조와 개성이 더해진다고들 여겼다. 그는 그린 드래건의 술집과 당구장에서 두드러진 인물이었다. 그는 경마계 영웅들에 관한 일화 몇 가지와, 후작과 자작들의 갖가지 교묘한 술책을 알고 있었으니, 그것은 사기 도박꾼들 사이에서도 혈통이 그 우월함을 주장한다는 것을 증명하는 듯했다. 그러나 그의 기억의 세세한 보존력이 주로 발휘되는 것은 자기가 직접 사고팔았던 말들에 관해서였다. 그 말들이 털끝 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 눈 깜짝할 새 몇 마일을 달리는지는, 여러 해가 지난 뒤에도 여전히 열렬한 단언의 주제였고, 그럴 때면 그는 그런 광경을 본 적이 없으리라고 엄숙히 맹세하여 듣는 이의 상상을 거들곤 했다. 요컨대 뱀브리지 씨는 쾌락을 아는 사내이자 명랑한 동행이었다.\n\n프레드는 약삭빨라서, 자기가 말을 팔 작정으로 하운즐리에 간다는 것을 친구들에게 말하지 않았다. 그는 말 값에 대한 그들의 진짜 견해를 에둘러 얻어 내고 싶었던 것이다. 그런 저명한 비평가들에게서 진짜 견해를 끌어낼 가능성이란 가장 희박하다는 것을 모른 채 말이다. 공짜로 아첨하는 것은 뱀브리지 씨의 약점이 아니었다. 그는 이 불행한 밤색 말이 천벌이라는 말의 가장 둥글둥글한 표현을 써야만 짐작이라도 갈 만큼 숨이 차는 말이라는 사실에, 그토록 강한 인상을 받은 적이 일찍이 없었다.\n\n“자네, 나 말고 딴 사람한테 가서 바꿔치기를 해서 헛수고를 했구먼, 빈시! 아니, 자네가 그 밤색 말보다 더 좋은 말에 다리를 걸쳐 본 적이 없었는데, 그걸 주고 이 짐승을 받았다니. 저놈을 가볍게 달리게 해 보면, 톱질꾼 스무 명이 한꺼번에 톱질하는 소리를 낸다고. 내 평생 저것보다 더 심하게 숨찬 말은 딱 한 마리 들어 봤는데, 그건 적갈색 말이었어. 곡물상 페그웰 거였지. 그 사람이 칠 년 전에 그놈을 이륜마차에 메고 다녔는데, 나더러 받으라고 하더라고. 그래서 내가 그랬지, ‘고맙네, 페그, 난 관악기는 안 다루네.’ 그렇게 말했지. 그 농담이 온 고을을 돌았어. 하지만 빌어먹을! 그 말은 자네 그 숨찬 놈에 대면 한 푼짜리 나팔에 불과했다고.”\n\n“아니, 방금은 그 말이 내 것보다 더 심하다고 했잖소.” 프레드가 평소보다 더 짜증스럽게 말했다.\n\n“그럼 내가 거짓말한 거지.” 뱀브리지 씨가 단호하게 말했다. “둘 사이엔 한 푼어치 차이도 없었으니까.”\n\n프레드가 말에 박차를 가하자 그들은 잠시 빠르게 나아갔다. 다시 속도를 늦추었을 때 뱀브리지 씨가 말했다.\n\n“그렇다고 그 적갈색 말이 자네 것보다 더 잘 달리는 놈이 아니었던 건 아니지만.”\n\n“난 이놈 걸음에 아주 만족하오, 분명히 말하건대.” 명랑한 무리와 함께 있다는 의식을 죄다 끌어모아야 버틸 수 있었던 프레드가 말했다. “이놈 걸음새는 유달리 깔끔하다니까, 안 그렇소, 호럭?”\n\n호럭 씨는 대가의 손으로 그린 초상화라도 되는 양 완벽한 중립을 지키며 앞만 바라보았다.\n\n프레드는 진짜 견해를 얻으려는 헛된 희망을 포기했다. 그러나 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 뱀브리지의 깎아내림과 호럭의 침묵이 둘 다 사실상 고무적이며, 그들이 입 밖에 내려는 것보다 그 말을 더 좋게 여기고 있음을 가리킨다는 것을 알 수 있었다.\n\n실로 바로 그날 저녁, 시장이 채 열리기도 전에, 프레드는 자기 말을 유리하게 처분할 좋은 기회가 보인다고 생각했다. 다만 그것은 팔십 파운드를 챙겨 온 자기 선견지명을 스스로 대견해할 만한 기회였다. 뱀브리지 씨와 아는 사이인 젊은 농부 하나가 레드 라이언에 들어와, 사냥말 한 마리를 처분하는 이야기를 꺼냈는데, 대뜸 그것을 다이아몬드라고 소개하여 마치 널리 알려진 명물인 양 암시했다. 자기로 말하자면 가끔 짐을 끌 수 있는 쓸 만한 보통 말이 한 마리 필요할 뿐이었으니, 곧 결혼을 앞두고 사냥을 그만둘 참이었던 것이다. 그 사냥말은 조금 떨어진 친구의 마구간에 있었다. 어두워지기 전에 신사분들이 보러 갈 시간은 아직 있었다. 친구의 마구간은 뒷골목을 지나야 닿을 수 있었는데, 그 비위생적인 시절의 어느 음산한 거리에서나 그렇듯, 약값 한 푼 들이지 않고도 손쉽게 독에 중독될 만한 곳이었다. 프레드는 동행들처럼 브랜디로 역겨움에 무장하지 못했지만, 마침내 돈을 벌게 해 줄 말을 보았다는 희망에 한껏 들떠, 이튿날 아침 제일 먼저 같은 길을 다시 밟게 되었다. 만약 자기가 그 농부와 흥정을 매듭짓지 못하면 뱀브리지가 매듭지으리라고 그는 확신했다. 상황의 압박이 자기의 명민함을 날카롭게 갈고, 의심이 지닌 온갖 구성력을 자기에게 부여하고 있다고 프레드는 느꼈던 것이다. 뱀브리지가 다이아몬드를 깎아내린 것은, 사려는 생각이 없었더라면 (그 말이 친구의 것이니) 결코 하지 않았을 짓이었다. 그 짐승을 본 사람이라면 누구나 — 심지어 호럭조차 — 분명 그 가치에 깊은 인상을 받았다. 이런 부류의 사람들과 어울리는 이점을 모조리 챙기려면, 추론하는 법을 알아야 하고, 무엇이든 곧이곧대로 받아들이는 얼간이가 되어서는 안 된다. 그 말의 빛깔은 얼룩진 회색이었는데, 마침 프레드는 메들리코트 경의 하인이 바로 그런 말을 찾고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그 모든 깎아내림 끝에, 농부가 자리를 비운 저녁나절에 뱀브리지는 그보다 못한 말도 팔십 파운드에 팔리는 것을 본 적이 있다고 무심코 흘렸다. 물론 그는 스무 번도 넘게 자기 말을 뒤집었지만, 무엇이 사실일 법한지를 알면 사람의 시인을 시험해 볼 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 프레드도 말에 대한 자기 안목이 어느 정도 값어치가 있다고 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 농부는 프레드의 점잖되 숨찬 말 앞에서 한참 머뭇거려, 그것을 고려할 만하다고 여기는 기색을 보였고, 거기에 이십오 파운드를 더 얹어 받고 다이아몬드의 대가로 그것을 가져갈 듯했다. 그렇게 되면 프레드는 새 말을 적어도 팔십 파운드에 처분했을 때, 이 거래로 오십오 파운드를 손에 쥐게 되고, 어음을 갚는 데 백삼십오 파운드를 보태게 되니, 가스 씨에게 임시로 떠넘겨질 부족분은 기껏해야 이십오 파운드일 터였다. 아침에 옷을 서둘러 챙겨 입을 무렵, 그는 이 드문 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 된다는 것을 너무도 분명히 깨달은 나머지, 설령 뱀브리지와 호럭이 둘 다 만류했더라도 그들의 의도를 곧이곧대로 해석하는 미혹에 빠지지는 않았을 것이다. 그 음흉한 자들이 젊은이의 이익 말고 딴것을 손에 쥐고 있다는 것을 알아챘으리라. 말에 관해서라면 불신이야말로 유일한 실마리였다. 그러나 우리가 알다시피 회의란 결코 철저히 적용될 수 없으니, 그렇지 않다면 삶은 멈춰 버릴 것이다. 무언가 우리는 믿고 행해야 하며, 그 무언가가 무어라 불리든 그것은 사실상 우리 자신의 판단이니, 남에게 가장 노예처럼 기대는 것처럼 보일 때조차 그러하다. 프레드는 자기 거래가 훌륭하다고 믿었고, 시장이 채 제대로 열리기도 전에 그 얼룩진 회색 말을, 옛 말에 삼십 파운드를 더 얹은 값에 손에 넣었으니 — 그가 치르리라 예상했던 것보다 고작 오 파운드 더 든 셈이었다.\n\n그러나 그는 아마도 마음속 논쟁 탓이었는지 약간 근심스럽고 지친 기분이 들어, 말 시장의 더한 흥겨움을 기다리지 않고 홀로 십사 마일의 여정에 올랐으니, 아주 느긋하게 가서 말을 싱싱하게 유지할 작정이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV.\n\n사랑하는 이가 저지른 죄의 슬픔은 그 죄의 무거운 십자가를 지고 있는 자에게 거의 위안이 되지 못한다. — 셰익스피어, 『소네트』\n\n유감스럽게도, 하운즐리에서 그 길조의 사건이 있은 지 사흘째 되던 날, 프레드 빈시는 생애 그 어느 때보다 더 침울한 기분에 빠지고 말았다. 자기 말을 팔 시장이 마땅치 않아 실망한 것은 아니었다. 다만 메들리코트 경의 사람과 흥정이 매듭지어지기 전에, 무려 팔십 파운드의 희망을 걸어 둔 그 다이아몬드라는 말이 아무런 예고도 없이 마구간에서 발길질을 해 대는 지독한 악성을 드러냈고, 하마터면 마부를 죽일 뻔하더니, 마침내 마구간 칸막이 위로 늘어진 밧줄에 다리가 걸려 스스로 심하게 절게 되고 만 것이다. 이것은 결혼 후에야 드러나는 못된 성미만큼이나 어찌해 볼 도리가 없는 일이었다. 물론 그 못된 성미라는 것은 식을 올리기 전부터 오랜 친구들은 이미 알고 있었겠지만 말이다. 무슨 까닭에선지 프레드는 이번 불운의 일격 앞에서 평소의 탄력성을 조금도 발휘하지 못했다. 그는 그저 자기에게 오십 파운드밖에 없고, 당장 더 마련할 가망이 없으며, 백육십 파운드짜리 어음이 닷새 후면 제시되리라는 것을 알 뿐이었다. 설령 개스 씨가 손해를 보지 않게 해 달라는 명목으로 아버지에게 부탁한다 해도, 아버지는 그것을 사치와 기만을 부추기는 짓이라 부르며 그 결과로부터 개스 씨를 구해 주는 것을 화를 내며 거절하리라는 것을 프레드는 쓰라리게 느꼈다. 그는 너무도 풀이 죽은 나머지, 곧장 개스 씨에게 가서 슬픈 진실을 털어놓는 것 외에는 다른 계획을 세울 수가 없었다. 오십 파운드를 들고 가서, 적어도 그 돈만큼은 자기 손에서 안전하게 떼어 놓겠다는 것이었다. 아버지는 창고에 가 있어서 아직 그 사고를 모르고 있었다. 알게 되면 그 못된 짐승을 마구간에 들였다고 노발대발할 터였다. 그 작은 골칫거리를 마주하기 전에 프레드는 온 용기를 끌어모아 더 큰일을 마주하러 떠나고 싶었다. 그는 아버지의 말을 탔는데, 개스 씨에게 이야기를 마치고 나면 스톤 코트로 말을 달려 메리에게 모든 것을 고백하기로 마음먹었기 때문이다. 사실 메리라는 존재와 그녀를 향한 프레드의 사랑이 없었더라면, 그의 양심은 미리 그 빚을 자꾸 떠올리게 하는 데서도, 또 불쾌한 일을 미루는 평소 버릇대로 자신을 봐주지 않고 되도록 곧장 단순하게 행동하도록 그를 떠미는 데서도 훨씬 덜 활발했을 것이다. 프레드 빈시보다 훨씬 강인한 사람들조차도 자신의 올바름 가운데 절반은 가장 사랑하는 사람의 마음속에 두고 있는 법이다. \"내 모든 행동의 무대가 무너졌다.\" 어느 고대인은 가장 가까운 벗이 죽었을 때 이렇게 말했다. 관객이 최선을 요구하는 무대를 얻은 사람은 행운이다. 만일 메리 개스가 인격에서 무엇이 훌륭한지에 관해 뚜렷한 생각이 없었더라면, 그 무렵 프레드에게는 분명 적잖은 차이가 생겼을 것이다.\n\n개스 씨는 사무소에 없었고, 프레드는 그의 집으로 말을 몰았다. 그 집은 마을에서 조금 벗어난 곳에 있었다. 앞쪽에 과수원이 딸린 소박한 곳으로, 두서없이 뻗은 구식의 반목조 건물이었는데, 마을이 퍼져 나가기 전에는 농가였으나 지금은 읍내 사람들의 개인 정원에 둘러싸여 있었다. 우리는 친구들이 그렇듯 집도 저마다 제 얼굴을 지니고 있으면 더욱 정이 가는 법이다. 메리에게는 남동생이 넷, 여동생이 하나 있어서 식구가 꽤 많은 편이었는데, 개스 가족은 그 낡은 집을 무척 사랑했다. 좋은 가구는 이미 오래전에 다 팔아 버린 집이었는데도 그러했다. 프레드도 그 집을 좋아했고, 사과와 모과 냄새가 향긋하게 풍기는 다락방까지 속속들이 알고 있었다. 오늘 전까지 그는 즐거운 기대 없이 그 집을 찾은 적이 없었다. 그러나 지금 그의 심장은 불안하게 뛰었으니, 아마도 개스 부인 앞에서 고백을 해야 할 것이라는 느낌 때문이었다. 그는 그녀의 남편보다 그녀를 다소 더 어렵게 여겼다. 그렇다고 그녀가 메리처럼 빈정거림이나 충동적인 말투에 빠지는 사람이라서는 아니었다. 적어도 지금의 부인다운 나이에 이르러서 개스 부인은 결코 성급한 말로 자신을 그르치는 일이 없었다. 그녀의 말마따나 젊은 시절에 멍에를 짊어지며 자제력을 익혔기 때문이다. 그녀에게는 바꿀 수 없는 것을 분별하여 불평 없이 받아들이는 드문 감각이 있었다. 남편의 미덕을 흠모하면서도, 그녀는 일찍이 남편이 자기 잇속을 챙기는 데는 무능하다고 마음을 정해 놓고, 그 결과를 쾌활하게 맞아들였다. 그녀는 찻주전자나 아이들 옷의 주름 장식에 대한 자부심을 모두 단념할 만큼 도량이 넓었고, 개스 씨가 분별이 없다거나, 다른 남자들처럼 했더라면 벌었을 돈이 얼마라거나 하는 따위의 애처로운 하소연을 이웃 여인네들의 귀에 쏟아 놓은 적도 결코 없었다. 그래서 이 점잖은 이웃들은 그녀를 거만하다거나 별나다고 여겼고, 때로는 자기 남편들에게 그녀를 두고 \"당신네 잘난 개스 부인\"이라고 말하곤 했다. 그녀 또한 그들에 대해 나름의 비판을 품지 않은 것은 아니었으니, 미들마치의 여느 부인들보다 더 정확히 배운 사람이었고, 또한—흠 없는 여자가 어디 있겠는가?—제 성(性)에 대해 조금은 엄격해지기 쉬웠다. 그녀가 보기에 여성이란 전적으로 종속되도록 빚어진 존재였던 것이다. 반면에 남자들의 결점에 대해서는 균형이 맞지 않을 만큼 너그러웠고, 그런 것은 자연스러운 일이라고 말하는 것을 사람들은 자주 들었다. 또한 인정해야 할 것은, 개스 부인이 어리석은 짓이라 여기는 것에 맞설 때면 다소 지나치게 단호했다는 점이다. 가정교사에서 주부로 넘어온 과정이 그녀의 의식 속에 조금은 너무 강하게 새겨졌던 터라, 그녀는 자기 문법과 발음이 읍내 수준을 넘어서면서도 수수한 모자를 쓰고 식구들 저녁을 짓고 양말을 모두 깁는다는 사실을 좀처럼 잊지 않았다. 그녀는 이따금 떠돌이식으로 학생들을 가르쳐서, 책이나 석판을 든 아이들을 부엌까지 졸졸 따라다니게 했다. 그녀는 아이들이, 자기가 \"쳐다보지 않고도\" 그들의 실수를 바로잡으면서 훌륭하게 비누 거품을 낼 수 있다는 것을, 다시 말해 소매를 팔꿈치 위로 걷어붙인 여자도 가정법이나 열대 지방에 관해 모든 것을 알 수 있다는 것을, 요컨대 쓸모없는 인형이 되지 않고서도 \"교육\"이라든가 힘주어 발음할 만한 다른 좋은 것들을 지닐 수 있다는 것을 보는 것이 아이들에게 유익하다고 생각했다. 이런 교훈적인 취지의 말을 할 때면 그녀의 이마에는 야무진 작은 주름이 잡혔지만, 그렇다고 해서 그녀의 얼굴이 인자해 보이는 것을 막지는 못했고, 행렬처럼 줄지어 나오는 그녀의 말들은 열정적이고 듣기 좋은 콘트랄토 음성으로 발화되었다. 분명 이 모범적인 개스 부인에게도 우스꽝스러운 구석이 있었지만, 그녀의 인격은 그 별난 점들을 떠받쳐 주었으니, 마치 아주 좋은 포도주가 껍질의 풍미마저 살려 내는 것과 같았다.\n\n프레드 빈시에 대해 그녀는 어머니 같은 감정을 품고 있어서, 늘 그의 잘못을 너그러이 봐주는 편이었다. 다만 메리가 그와 약혼하는 일만큼은 아마 용서하지 않았을 것이다. 자기 딸은 자기 성에 적용하는 더 엄격한 판단 속에 포함되어 있었으니까. 그러나 바로 이렇게 그를 예외적으로 너그럽게 대해 왔다는 사실 때문에, 이제 그녀의 평가 속에서 자신이 불가피하게 추락하리라는 것이 프레드에게는 더욱 견디기 힘들었다. 게다가 방문의 상황은 그가 예상했던 것보다 한층 더 불쾌하게 돌아갔으니, 케일럽 개스가 멀지 않은 곳의 수리 작업을 보러 일찍 나가 버렸기 때문이다. 개스 부인은 정해진 시간이면 늘 부엌에 있었고, 그날 아침 그녀는 그곳에서 여러 일을 한꺼번에 해 나가고 있었다. 그 환한 방 한쪽의 깨끗이 닦은 전나무 식탁에서 파이를 만들고, 열린 문 너머로 화덕과 반죽통 앞에서 움직이는 샐리를 살피며, 동시에 막내아들과 막내딸에게 수업을 하고 있었는데, 두 아이는 책과 석판을 앞에 놓고 식탁 맞은편에 서 있었다. 부엌 다른 쪽 끝의 빨래통과 빨래걸이는 자잘한 것들을 틈틈이 빨고 있다는 것을 말해 주었다.\n\n소매를 팔꿈치 위로 걷어붙이고 능숙하게 반죽을 다루며—밀방망이를 굴리고 장식 무늬를 집어 가며—\"집합 명사, 곧 다수를 뜻하는 명사\"와 동사·대명사의 일치에 관해 무엇이 옳은 견해인지를 문법적 열의를 가지고 설명하는 개스 부인의 모습은 보기에 즐거운 광경이었다. 그녀는 메리와 같은, 곱슬머리에 각진 얼굴형이었으나 더 잘생겼고, 이목구비가 더 섬세하며, 살결이 희고, 부인다운 단단한 체격에 눈빛이 놀랍도록 야무졌다. 눈처럼 흰 주름 장식 모자를 쓴 그녀의 모습은 팔에 바구니를 걸고 장을 보는, 우리 모두 한 번쯤 본 적 있는 그 사랑스러운 프랑스 여인을 떠올리게 했다. 그 어머니를 보면 딸도 그렇게 되리라는 희망을 품게 되는데, 이는 지참금에 맞먹는 장래의 이점이다. 너무도 자주 어머니는 딸 뒤에 악의에 찬 예언처럼 서 있곤 한다—\"지금의 나처럼, 머지않아 너도 이렇게 될 것이다.\"라고.\n\n\"자, 그걸 한 번 더 짚어 보자.\" 개스 부인이 사과 퍼프 하나를 집으며 말했다. 그 퍼프가 진중한 이마를 가진 혈기 왕성한 사내아이 벤의 주의를 수업에서 흐트러뜨리고 있는 듯 보였다. \"'관념의 단일성이나 복수성을 전달하는 그 단어의 의미를 고려하지 않고서는 안 된다'—그게 무슨 뜻인지 다시 말해 보렴, 벤.\"\n\n(개스 부인은 더 유명한 교육자들이 그러하듯 즐겨 다니는 옛길이 있었고, 사회가 송두리째 난파한다 해도 자신의 \"린들리 머리\"를 물결 위로 떠받치려 애썼을 것이다.)\n\n\"어—그건—제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 생각해야 한다는 거예요.\" 벤이 다소 짜증스레 말했다. \"전 문법이 싫어요. 그게 무슨 소용이에요?\"\n\n\"네 말과 글을 바르게 해서 남들이 알아듣게 하려는 거란다.\" 개스 부인이 엄격하고 정확하게 말했다. \"넌 늙은 욥처럼 말하고 싶니?\"\n\n\"네.\" 벤이 당차게 말했다. \"그게 더 재밌는걸요. 그 사람은 '너 가'라고 하잖아요—그게 '너 가거라'만큼이나 좋은데요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 사람은 '양'이라고 해야 할 걸 '배가 정원에 있다'고 하잖아.\" 레티가 우쭐대는 투로 말했다. \"바다에서 온 배를 말하는 줄 알겠어.\"\n\n\"아니, 안 그래. 네가 멍청이만 아니면.\" 벤이 말했다. \"어떻게 바다에서 온 배가 거기 들어와?\"\n\n\"이런 것들은 발음에만 관계되는 거란다. 발음은 문법에서 가장 사소한 부분이지.\" 개스 부인이 말했다. \"그 사과 껍질은 돼지가 먹을 거야, 벤. 네가 먹으면 네 파이 조각을 돼지한테 줘야겠다. 욥은 아주 평범한 것들에 관해서만 말하면 돼. 그런데 네가 문법을 그 사람만큼밖에 모른다면, 그보다 어려운 것에 관해 어떻게 쓰거나 말할 수 있겠니? 잘못된 단어를 쓰고, 단어를 엉뚱한 자리에 놓아서, 사람들이 너를 이해하기는커녕 지긋지긋한 사람이라며 등을 돌리겠지. 그러면 넌 어떡할래?\"\n\n\"전 상관없어요. 그만두면 되죠.\" 벤이, 문법에 관한 한 그것이야말로 반가운 결말이라는 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"네가 지치고 둔해지는 게 보이는구나, 벤.\" 사내아이들의 이런 방해성 논리에 익숙한 개스 부인이 말했다. 파이를 다 만든 그녀는 빨래걸이 쪽으로 가며 말했다. \"이리 와서, 수요일에 내가 들려준 신키나투스 이야기를 해 보렴.\"\n\n\"알아요! 그 사람 농부였어요.\" 벤이 말했다.\n\n\"아냐, 벤. 그 사람은 로마인이야—내가 말할래.\" 레티가 다투듯 팔꿈치를 들이밀며 말했다.\n\n\"바보 같긴, 그 사람은 로마의 농부였고, 밭을 갈고 있었어.\"\n\n\"맞아, 그런데 그 전에—그게 먼저가 아니야—사람들이 그 사람을 원했어.\" 레티가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그래도 그 사람이 어떤 사람이었는지부터 말해야지.\" 벤이 우겼다. \"그 사람은 우리 아빠처럼 현명한 사람이어서, 사람들이 그 사람한테 조언을 구한 거야. 그리고 용감한 사람이라 싸울 줄도 알았어. 우리 아빠도 그렇지—그렇죠, 엄마?\"\n\n\"자, 벤, 엄마가 우리한테 들려준 대로 내가 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 할래.\" 레티가 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"엄마, 제발 벤더러 말하지 말라고 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"레티, 엄마는 네가 부끄럽구나.\" 어머니가 통에서 모자들을 비틀어 짜며 말했다. \"오빠가 시작했으면 오빠가 이야기를 할 수 있는지 기다려 봤어야지. 팔꿈치로 밀치고 인상을 쓰면서, 마치 팔꿈치로 이기려는 것처럼 보이니 얼마나 무례하니! 신키나투스도 분명 자기 딸이 그렇게 행동하는 걸 보면 안타까워했을 거야.\" (개스 부인은 이 무시무시한 문장을 매우 위엄 있게 발음했고, 레티는 억눌린 말솜씨와, 로마인까지 포함한 전반적인 멸시 사이에서 인생이 벌써부터 괴로운 일이라고 느꼈다.) \"자, 벤.\"\n\n\"음—어—저—그러니까, 싸움이 엄청 많이 났고, 다들 멍청이였고, 그리고—엄마가 말한 그대로는 못 하겠지만—사람들이 어떤 사람을 대장이자 왕이자 뭐든 다 시키고 싶어 했는데—\"\n\n\"독재관, 이라니까.\" 레티가 상처받은 표정으로, 엄마가 후회하게 만들고 싶은 마음이 없지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n\"좋아, 독재관!\" 벤이 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그런데 그건 좋은 단어가 아니야. 그 사람은 사람들한테 석판에 받아쓰라고 시킨 게 아니거든.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 벤, 넌 그렇게까지 무지하진 않잖니.\" 개스 부인이 애써 진지하게 말했다. \"들어 봐, 문에 누가 노크를 하는구나! 뛰어가서 열어 드려라, 레티.\"\n\n노크한 사람은 프레드였다. 레티가 아버지는 아직 안 들어오셨지만 어머니는 부엌에 계신다고 말하자, 프레드에게는 달리 선택의 여지가 없었다. 그는 개스 부인이 부엌에서 일하고 있을 때면 으레 그리로 찾아가 인사하던 평소의 습관을 저버릴 수 없었다. 그는 말없이 레티의 목에 팔을 두르고, 평소의 농담이나 다정한 손짓도 없이 그 애를 부엌으로 데리고 들어갔다.\n\n개스 부인은 이런 시각에 프레드를 보고 놀랐지만, 놀라움이란 그녀가 곧잘 드러내는 감정은 아니어서, 그저 조용히 일을 계속하며 말할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"프레드, 이렇게 이른 시간에? 안색이 아주 창백하구나. 무슨 일이라도 있었니?\"\n\n\"개스 씨와 이야기를 좀 하고 싶어서요.\" 프레드가 아직 더 말할 준비가 되지 않은 채 말했다. \"그리고 부인과도요.\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그가 덧붙였다. 개스 부인이 어음에 관해 모든 것을 알고 있으리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없었고, 설령 그녀에게만 따로 말하는 것이 아니더라도 결국에는 그녀 앞에서 그 이야기를 해야 하리라 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n\"케일럽이 몇 분 안에 돌아올 거야.\" 프레드와 그의 아버지 사이에 무슨 곤란한 일이 생겼다고 짐작한 개스 부인이 말했다. \"오래 걸리진 않을 거야. 오늘 아침까지 책상에서 끝내야 할 일이 있거든. 내가 여기 일을 마치는 동안 나랑 같이 있어도 괜찮겠니?\"\n\n\"그런데 우리, 신키나투스 이야기는 계속 안 해도 되죠?\" 벤이 프레드의 손에서 채찍을 빼앗아 그것의 위력을 고양이한테 시험해 보며 말했다.\n\n\"안 해도 돼. 이제 나가렴. 그런데 그 채찍은 내려놓아라. 불쌍한 늙은 거북이를 채찍질하다니 어쩜 그렇게 짓궂니! 프레드, 미안하지만 그 애한테서 채찍을 좀 거둬 주렴.\"\n\n\"자, 꼬마야, 이리 줘.\" 프레드가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 형 말 타게 해 줄 거예요?\" 벤이 채찍을 건네면서도 마지못해서가 아니라는 듯한 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"오늘은 안 돼—다음에. 오늘은 내 말이 아니거든.\"\n\n\"오늘 메리 누나 봐요?\"\n\n\"응, 그럴 것 같다.\" 프레드가 불쾌한 통증을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"누나한테 얼른 집에 와서 벌금 놀이 하고 재밌게 놀자고 전해 줘요.\"\n\n\"그만, 그만하렴, 벤! 어서 가.\" 프레드가 시달리고 있는 것을 본 개스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"이제 부인의 학생은 레티와 벤뿐인가요?\" 아이들이 나가고, 시간을 보낼 만한 무슨 말이라도 해야 할 필요가 생기자 프레드가 말했다. 그는 아직 개스 씨를 기다려야 할지, 아니면 대화 중에 좋은 기회를 잡아 개스 부인에게 직접 고백하고 돈을 건넨 뒤 말을 타고 떠나야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하고 있었다.\n\n\"한 명—딱 한 명. 패니 핵벗이 열한 시 반에 온단다. 요즘은 수입이 변변치 않아.\" 개스 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"학생이 거의 없는 썰물 때거든. 그래도 앨프리드의 도제 수업료로 내 작은 지갑을 모아 두었지. 구십이 파운드란다. 이제 그 애는 한머 씨한테 갈 수 있어. 딱 알맞은 나이거든.\"\n\n이 말은 개스 씨가 구십이 파운드도 넘는 돈을 잃을 처지에 놓였다는 소식으로 이어지기에 좋은 길이 아니었다. 프레드는 입을 다물었다. \"대학에 가는 도련님들은 그보다 비용이 좀 더 들지.\" 개스 부인이 모자 가장자리의 장식을 잡아당기며 천연덕스럽게 이어 갔다. \"그리고 케일럽은 앨프리드가 뛰어난 기술자가 될 거라고 생각해. 그 애한테 좋은 기회를 주고 싶어 하지. 저기 오는구나! 들어오는 소리가 들려. 우리, 거실로 가서 보자, 그럴까?\"\n\n그들이 거실로 들어갔을 때 케일럽은 모자를 던져 놓고 책상 앞에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"아니! 프레드, 얘야!\" 그가 펜에 아직 잉크도 찍지 않은 채 들고서, 부드러운 놀라움이 담긴 어조로 말했다. \"이렇게 일찍 왔구나.\" 그러나 프레드의 얼굴에서 늘 보이던 명랑한 인사의 표정이 보이지 않자, 그는 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"집에 무슨 일이 있는 게냐?—뭐가 잘못됐니?\"\n\n\"네, 개스 씨, 아저씨께서 저를 나쁘게 생각하시게 될까 봐 걱정스러운 일을 말씀드리러 왔어요. 아저씨와 부인께, 제가 약속을 지킬 수 없게 됐다는 걸 말씀드리러 왔어요. 결국 어음을 막을 돈을 마련할 수가 없게 됐어요. 운이 나빴거든요. 백육십 파운드 중에 이 오십 파운드밖에 마련하지 못했어요.\"\n\n프레드는 말하는 동안 어음을 꺼내 개스 씨 앞 책상 위에 올려놓았다. 그는 소년처럼 비참한 심정에다 말주변도 없어서, 단번에 그 적나라한 사실을 쏟아 내고 말았다. 개스 부인은 말없이 놀라며 설명을 구하듯 남편을 바라보았다. 케일럽은 얼굴을 붉히더니, 잠시 뜸을 들인 후 말했다.\n\n\"아, 당신한테 말 안 했지, 수전. 내가 프레드의 어음에 보증을 섰어. 백육십 파운드짜리였어. 이 애는 자기가 직접 막을 수 있다고 확신했고.\"\n\n개스 부인의 얼굴에 분명한 변화가 일었지만, 그것은 표면은 여전히 잔잔한 채 수면 아래에서 일어나는 변화 같았다. 그녀는 프레드에게 시선을 고정하고 말했다.\n\n\"네 아버지께 나머지 돈을 부탁드렸는데 거절하신 거겠지.\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 프레드가 입술을 깨물며, 더 힘겹게 말했다. \"하지만 부탁드려 봐야 소용없을 거예요. 소용이 없을 바에는 이 일에 개스 아저씨의 이름을 꺼내고 싶지 않았고요.\"\n\n\"때가 안 좋게 닥쳤구나.\" 케일럽이 머뭇거리는 투로, 어음을 내려다보며 초조하게 종이를 만지작거리며 말했다. \"크리스마스가 코앞인데—요즘 형편이 좀 빠듯하단다. 알다시피 난 옷감이 모자라는 재단사처럼 모든 걸 잘라 맞춰야 해. 어쩌면 좋겠소, 수전? 은행에 있는 돈을 한 푼도 빠짐없이 써야겠어. 백십 파운드인데, 빌어먹을!\"\n\n\"앨프리드의 도제 수업료로 모아 둔 구십이 파운드를 당신한테 줘야겠어요.\" 개스 부인이 진지하고 단호하게 말했지만, 예민한 귀라면 몇몇 말끝에서 미세한 떨림을 알아챘을지도 모른다. \"그리고 메리도 이맘때면 봉급에서 이십 파운드쯤은 모아 두었을 거예요. 그 애가 보태 줄 거고요.\"\n\n개스 부인은 다시 프레드를 쳐다보지 않았고, 그에게 가장 효과적으로 상처를 입힐 말이 무엇일까 따위는 조금도 궁리하지 않았다. 별난 여자답게 그녀는 지금 무엇을 해야 할지를 골똘히 생각하는 데 몰두해 있었고, 쓰디쓴 말이나 폭발로 일이 더 잘 마무리될 수 있으리라고는 여기지 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 프레드에게 난생처음으로 후회의 이빨 같은 것을 느끼게 했다. 묘하게도, 그 일에 대해 미리 그가 느꼈던 고통은 거의 전적으로 자신이 명예롭지 못해 보이고 개스 가족의 평가 속에서 추락하리라는 의식에 있었다. 자기의 약속 위반이 그들에게 끼칠 불편이나 있을 법한 손해에는 마음을 쓰지 않았으니, 다른 사람의 곤궁에 이런 상상력을 발휘하는 것은 희망에 찬 젊은 도련님들에게 흔한 일이 아니다. 사실 우리 대부분은 잘못을 저지르지 않는 가장 높은 동기가 그 잘못으로 고통받을 존재와는 무관한 어떤 것이라는 생각 속에 자라난다. 그러나 이 순간 그는 문득 두 여인의 저축을 빼앗고 있는 가련한 악당으로서의 자기 모습을 보았다.\n\n\"틀림없이 다 갚을게요, 개스 부인—결국에는요.\" 그가 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 결국에는.\" 개스 부인이 말했다. 추한 자리에 번지르르한 말이 나오는 것을 유난히 싫어하는 그녀는 이번에는 경구 한마디를 억누를 수 없었다. \"하지만 소년들을 결국에 가서 도제로 보낼 수는 없는 노릇이지. 열다섯에 도제로 들어가야 하니까.\" 그녀가 프레드를 봐줄 마음이 이때만큼 적었던 적은 없었다.\n\n\"내 잘못이 제일 컸어, 수전.\" 케일럽이 말했다. \"프레드는 돈을 마련할 수 있다고 확신했고. 어음에 손을 댄 게 내 일이 아니었는데. 사방을 다 알아보고 정직한 수단은 다 써 봤겠지?\" 그가 자비로운 잿빛 눈을 프레드에게 고정하며 덧붙였다. 케일럽은 페더스톤 씨를 콕 집어 말하기에는 너무 섬세했다.\n\n\"네, 다 해 봤어요—정말로요. 막 팔려던 말에 불운이 닥치지만 않았어도 백삼십 파운드는 준비할 수 있었을 거예요. 삼촌이 저한테 팔십 파운드를 주셨고, 제 헌 말에 삼십 파운드를 보태서 다른 말을 한 마리 샀거든요. 그걸 팔십 파운드나 그 이상에 팔 작정이었고요—저는 말 없이 지낼 생각이었어요—그런데 그 말이 그만 사나운 놈으로 드러나서 스스로 다리를 절게 됐어요. 이 일로 아저씨께 짐을 지우느니, 저도 말들도 차라리 악마한테나 끌려갔으면 좋았을 텐데요. 제가 이만큼 아끼는 사람은 달리 없어요. 아저씨와 부인은 늘 저한테 그렇게 잘해 주셨잖아요. 하지만 그런 말을 해 봐야 소용없죠. 이제 두 분은 늘 저를 악당으로 여기실 테니까요.\"\n\n프레드는 돌아서서 방을 황급히 빠져나갔다. 자기가 다소 계집애처럼 굴고 있다는 것을 의식했고, 자기가 미안해한들 개스 가족에게 별 소용이 없다는 것을 어렴풋이 느꼈기 때문이다. 그들은 그가 말에 올라 재빨리 문을 빠져나가는 것을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"프레드 빈시한테 실망했어요.\" 개스 부인이 말했다. \"그 애가 당신을 자기 빚에 끌어들이리라고는 미리 알았어도 믿지 않았을 거예요. 그 애가 헤픈 건 알았지만, 가장 손해를 감당하기 어려운 가장 오랜 친구한테 자기 위험을 떠넘길 만큼 비열할 줄은 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"내가 바보였어, 수전.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 아내가 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었다. \"하지만 나라면 그걸 장터에 광고하러 다니진 않았을 거예요. 왜 그런 일들을 나한테 숨겨요? 당신 단추도 꼭 그래요. 떨어지게 두고도 나한테 말 안 하고, 소맷동을 늘어뜨린 채 나가잖아요. 진작 알았으면 더 나은 방도를 마련해 두었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"당신이 무척 속상해하는 거 알아, 수전.\" 케일럽이 정겹게 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"앨프리드를 위해 긁어모은 돈을 당신이 잃는 건 도저히 못 견디겠어.\"\n\n\"그걸 긁어모아 둔 게 천만다행이죠. 그리고 고생할 사람은 당신이에요. 그 애를 당신이 직접 가르쳐야 할 테니까요. 당신은 그 나쁜 버릇을 고쳐야 해요. 어떤 남자들은 술에 빠지는데, 당신은 보수 없이 일하는 데 빠졌어요. 그것 좀 덜 즐겨야 해요. 그리고 메리한테 말을 타고 가서, 그 애가 가진 돈이 얼마인지 물어봐야 하고요.\"\n\n케일럽은 의자를 뒤로 밀고 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채, 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 손끝을 아주 정성스럽게 맞붙이고 있었다.\n\n\"가엾은 메리!\" 그가 말했다. \"수전.\" 그가 목소리를 낮추며 이어 갔다. \"그 애가 프레드를 좋아하는 게 아닌가 걱정이오.\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요! 그 애는 늘 그 애를 비웃잖아요. 그리고 그 애도 메리를 오누이 사이가 아닌 다른 식으로 생각할 리 없고요.\"\n\n케일럽은 대꾸하지 않았으나, 이내 안경을 내려 쓰고 책상 쪽으로 의자를 당기며 말했다. \"빌어먹을 어음—하노버에나 가 있었으면! 이런 일들은 업무를 끔찍하게 방해한다니까!\"\n\n이 말의 앞부분은 그가 지닌 저주의 표현을 통틀어 망라한 것으로, 능히 상상할 만한 가벼운 으르렁거림과 함께 발화되었다. 그러나 그가 \"업무\"라는 말을 발음하는 것을 들어 본 적 없는 사람에게는, 그가 그 말을 감싸는 그 독특한 어조, 곧 뜨거운 숭배와 종교적 경의의 어조를 전하기란 어려운 일일 것이다. 마치 신성한 상징물을 금실로 가장자리를 두른 아마포로 감싸듯 그는 그 말을 감쌌다.\n\n케일럽 개스는 사회라는 몸을 먹이고 입히고 거처를 마련해 주는, 그 머리가 수만이요 손이 수만인 노동의 가치와 없어서는 안 될 위력을 곰곰이 생각하며 종종 고개를 가로젓곤 했다. 그것은 소년 시절부터 그의 상상력을 사로잡았다. 지붕이나 용골을 만드는 곳에서 울리는 큰 망치 소리의 메아리, 일꾼들이 신호로 외치는 함성, 용광로의 굉음, 기관의 우레 같은 소리와 철썩이는 물소리는 그에게 숭고한 음악이었다. 목재를 베어 싣는 일, 큰길을 따라 멀리서 별처럼 흔들리며 가는 거대한 통나무, 부두에서 일하는 기중기, 창고에 쌓인 산물들, 정확한 작업이 산출되어야 하는 곳이라면 어디서나 발휘되는 근육의 정밀하고 다채로운 노력—이 모든 어린 시절의 광경은 시인들의 도움 없이도 그에게 시로서 작용했고, 철학자들의 도움 없이도 그를 위한 철학을 빚어냈으며, 신학의 도움 없이도 하나의 종교가 되어 주었다. 그의 어릴 적 야심은 이 숭고한 노동에 되도록 효과적으로 한몫을 차지하는 것이었으니, 그는 그 노동을 유난히 \"업무\"라는 이름으로 품위 있게 불렀다. 비록 측량 기사 밑에서 짧은 기간 일했을 뿐이고 대개 독학으로 익혔지만, 그는 토지와 건축과 광업에 관해서라면 그 지방의 어지간한 전문가들보다 더 많이 알고 있었다.\n\n인간의 직업에 대한 그의 분류는 다소 거칠어서, 더 유명한 사람들의 범주가 그러하듯, 이 진보된 시대에는 받아들여지지 않을 것이었다. 그는 직업을 \"업무, 정치, 설교, 학문, 그리고 오락\"으로 나누었다. 뒤의 네 가지에 대해서는 그가 할 말이 없었다. 다만 그것들을, 경건한 이교도가 자기 신이 아닌 다른 신들을 대하듯 바라보았다. 같은 식으로, 그는 모든 신분을 매우 좋게 여겼지만, 자기 자신은 \"업무\"와 긴밀히 맞닿아 종종 먼지와 회반죽 자국이나 기관의 습기, 혹은 숲과 들의 향긋한 흙으로 명예롭게 치장되지 못할 신분에는 들고 싶어 하지 않았을 것이다. 그는 자신을 정통 기독교인이 아닌 다른 무엇으로 여긴 적이 없었고, 누가 그 주제를 꺼내면 선행 은총에 관해 논하기도 했겠지만, 내 생각에 그가 실질적으로 섬긴 신들은 훌륭한 실용적 계획, 정확한 작업, 그리고 맡은 일의 충실한 완수였다. 그의 암흑의 군주는 게으른 일꾼이었다. 그러나 케일럽에게는 부정의 정신이 없었고, 세상이 그에게는 너무도 경이로워 보였으므로, 그것들이 최선의 배수 작업이나 견고한 건축, 정확한 측량, 그리고 (석탄을 위한) 분별 있는 시추를 명백히 방해하지만 않는다면, 그는 얼마든지 많은 창공처럼 얼마든지 많은 체계라도 기꺼이 받아들일 사람이었다. 사실 그는 강한 실무적 지성을 갖춘 경건한 영혼의 소유자였다. 그러나 그는 재정만큼은 다룰 줄 몰랐다. 가치는 잘 알았지만, 손익이라는 형태의 금전적 결과를 그려 내는 예리한 상상력이 그에게는 없었다. 그리고 이 점을 비싼 대가를 치르며 깨달은 뒤로, 그는 그런 재능을 요하는 자신이 사랑하는 \"업무\"의 모든 형태를 단념하기로 마음먹었다. 그는 자본을 다루지 않고도 할 수 있는 온갖 종류의 일에 온전히 자신을 바쳤고, 자기 구역 안에서는 누구나 자기 일을 맡기고 싶어 하는 그 귀한 사람들 중 하나였다. 일을 잘하고, 비용을 아주 적게 청구하며, 때로는 아예 청구하기를 마다했기 때문이다. 그러니 개스 가족이 가난하고 \"옹색하게 살았던\" 것도 놀랄 일은 아니다. 하지만 그들은 그것을 개의치 않았다.\n\n# 제23장\n\n사랑은 자기를 기쁘게 하려 하지 않고, 자기를 위해 마음 쓰지도 않으며, 오직 남을 위해 제 안식을 내주고, 지옥의 절망 속에 천국을 세운다. . . . . . . . 사랑은 오직 자기만을 기쁘게 하려 하고, 남을 제 즐거움에 옭아매며, 남이 안식을 잃는 것을 기뻐하고, 천국을 거슬러 지옥을 세운다. ─W. 블레이크, 『경험의 노래』\n\n프레드 빈시는 메리가 그를 기다리지 않을 때, 그리고 삼촌이 아래층에 없을 때 스톤 코트에 닿고 싶었다. 그래야 메리가 징두리널을 댄 거실에 홀로 앉아 있을 테니까. 그는 앞마당의 자갈 위에서 소리를 내지 않으려고 말을 뜰에 매어 두고, 문손잡이 소리 말고는 아무 기별 없이 거실로 들어섰다. 메리는 늘 앉던 구석에서 존슨에 관한 피오치 부인의 회상록을 읽으며 웃고 있다가, 여전히 얼굴에 즐거움을 띤 채 고개를 들었다. 그 즐거움은 프레드가 말없이 다가와 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 괴고 아파 보이는 얼굴로 자기 앞에 서는 것을 보면서 차츰 사라졌다. 그녀 역시 아무 말 없이 묻는 듯한 눈길로 그를 올려다보기만 했다.\n\n\"메리.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"난 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 불한당이야.\"\n\n\"그런 호칭은 한 번에 하나씩이면 충분할 것 같은데.\" 메리는 미소를 지으려 애썼지만 불안한 마음이 들었다.\n\n\"넌 이제 다시는 날 좋게 생각하지 않겠지. 날 거짓말쟁이라고 여기겠지. 부정직한 인간이라고 생각할 거야. 내가 너나 네 아버지 어머니를 조금도 아끼지 않았다고 여기겠지. 넌 늘 날 최악으로 생각하니까, 알아.\"\n\n\"그럴 만한 이유를 댄다면 너에 대해 그 모든 걸 생각하게 되겠지, 부정할 수 없어, 프레드. 하지만 네가 무슨 일을 저질렀는지 어서 말해 줘. 괴로운 진실을 상상하기보다는 차라리 아는 편이 나아.\"\n\n\"빚을 졌어. 백육십 파운드를. 네 아버지께 어음에 이름을 적어 달라고 부탁드렸어. 아버님께는 별일 아닐 거라고 생각했지. 내가 직접 갚을 거라고 확신했고, 정말 할 수 있는 한 애썼어. 그런데 이제 와서 운이 너무 나빠서, 말 한 마리가 잘못돼서, 겨우 오십 파운드밖에 갚지 못해. 우리 아버지께는 돈을 청할 수도 없어. 한 푼도 주지 않으실 테니까. 삼촌은 얼마 전에 백 파운드를 주셨고. 그러니 내가 어쩌겠어? 그런데 이제 네 아버지께는 융통할 현금이 없고, 네 어머니는 모아 두신 구십이 파운드를 내놓으셔야 하고, 게다가 네 저금까지 보태야 한다고 하셔. 봐, 내가 얼마나─\"\n\n\"아, 가엾은 어머니, 가엾은 아버지!\" 메리가 말했다. 두 눈에 눈물이 차오르고, 억누르려 애쓰는 작은 흐느낌이 솟았다. 그녀는 똑바로 앞을 바라보며 프레드는 거들떠보지도 않았다. 집에서 벌어질 온갖 결과가 눈앞에 떠올랐던 것이다. 그도 한동안 말없이 그 어느 때보다 비참한 심정으로 있었다. \"절대로 너에게 상처 주고 싶지 않았어, 메리.\" 마침내 그가 말했다. \"넌 결코 날 용서할 수 없겠지.\"\n\n\"내가 널 용서하는 게 무슨 상관이야?\" 메리가 격하게 말했다. \"그렇다고 어머니가 사 년 동안 알프레드를 한머 씨에게 보내려고 과외 수업으로 벌어 모으신 돈을 잃는 게 조금이라도 나아져? 내가 널 용서하면 그 모든 게 충분히 흐뭇해질 것 같아?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이든 해, 메리. 다 들어 마땅하니까.\"\n\n\"난 아무 말도 하고 싶지 않아.\" 메리가 한결 차분하게 말했다. \"내가 화내 봤자 소용없으니까.\" 그녀는 눈물을 닦고 책을 옆으로 밀어 두고 일어나 바느질감을 가져왔다.\n\n프레드는 자기 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주치기를, 그리하여 애원하는 참회가 들어설 길을 찾기를 바라며 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다. 하지만 헛일이었다! 메리는 쉽사리 위를 올려다보지 않을 수 있었다.\n\n\"네 어머니 돈이 나가는 건 나도 정말 마음 쓰여.\" 그녀가 다시 앉아 빠르게 바느질을 시작했을 때 그가 말했다. \"물어보고 싶은 게 있어, 메리. 페더스톤 씨가, 네가 그분께 말씀드리면, 그러니까 알프레드를 도제로 보내는 일을 말씀드리면, 그 돈을 미리 내주시지 않을까?\"\n\n\"우리 식구는 구걸하는 걸 좋아하지 않아, 프레드. 차라리 일해서 돈을 버는 쪽이야. 게다가 너도 말했잖아, 페더스톤 씨가 최근에 너한테 백 파운드를 주셨다고. 그분은 좀처럼 선물을 하지 않으셔. 우리한테는 한 번도 선물을 주신 적이 없고. 우리 아버지는 분명 그분께 아무것도 청하지 않으실 거야. 설령 내가 그분께 구걸하려 든대도 소용없을 거고.\"\n\n\"난 너무 비참해, 메리. 내가 얼마나 비참한지 네가 안다면 날 가엾게 여길 텐데.\"\n\n\"그것보다 더 가여운 일들이 있어. 하지만 이기적인 사람은 늘 제 불편함을 세상 그 무엇보다 더 중요하게 여기지. 난 그런 걸 매일 충분히 보고 있어.\"\n\n\"날 이기적이라고 부르는 건 좀 부당해. 다른 젊은이들이 무슨 짓을 하는지 네가 안다면, 날 최악과는 한참 거리가 멀다고 생각할걸.\"\n\n\"갚을 방도도 모르면서 자기한테 큰돈을 쓰는 사람은 이기적일 수밖에 없다는 건 알아. 그런 사람은 늘 자기가 무엇을 얻을 수 있는지만 생각하지, 다른 사람이 무엇을 잃을지는 생각하지 않으니까.\"\n\n\"누구든 불운할 수 있어, 메리. 갚을 생각이었는데도 갚지 못하게 될 수 있다고. 세상에 네 아버지보다 더 훌륭한 분은 없지만, 그분도 곤경에 빠지셨잖아.\"\n\n\"감히 어떻게 우리 아버지와 너를 비교하는 거야, 프레드?\" 메리가 깊은 분노에 찬 목소리로 말했다. \"아버지는 한가한 쾌락을 생각하다가 곤경에 빠지신 게 아니라, 늘 남을 위해 하시던 일을 생각하셨기 때문에 그렇게 되신 거야. 그리고 모두의 손실을 메우려고 고생도 하셨고 열심히 일도 하셨어.\"\n\n\"그래, 넌 내가 결코 아무것도 메우려고 애쓰지 않을 거라고 생각하는구나, 메리. 한 사람을 두고 최악을 믿는 건 너그러운 일이 아니야. 그 사람에게 어떤 영향력을 가지게 됐다면, 그걸 써서 그를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 보려고 할 수도 있을 텐데, 넌 절대 그러지 않아. 어쨌든 난 갈게.\" 프레드가 맥없이 말을 맺었다. \"다시는 너한테 어떤 일에 대해서도 말하지 않을 거야. 내가 일으킨 온갖 말썽이 정말 미안해. 그게 다야.\"\n\n메리는 손에서 바느질감을 떨어뜨리고 고개를 들었다. 소녀다운 사랑에도 모성적인 무언가가 깃들어 있을 때가 많은데, 메리의 고된 경험은 그녀의 천성을, 우리가 소녀다움이라 부르는 딱딱하고 가벼운 것과는 사뭇 다른 감수성으로 빚어 놓았다. 프레드의 마지막 말에 그녀는 순간적인 아픔을 느꼈으니, 길을 잃고 해를 입을지도 모를 말썽꾸러기 가출한 아이의 상상 속 흐느낌이나 울음소리에 어머니가 느끼는 것과 비슷한 무엇이었다. 그리고 고개를 들었을 때 그녀의 눈이 그의 흐릿하게 절망한 눈길과 마주치자, 그에 대한 연민이 그녀의 분노와 다른 모든 근심을 넘어섰다.\n\n\"아, 프레드, 너 정말 아파 보여! 잠깐 앉아. 아직 가지 마. 네가 왔다고 삼촌께 말씀드릴게. 한 주 내내 널 못 봤다고 의아해하고 계셨거든.\" 메리는 무슨 말인지 잘 알지도 못한 채 먼저 떠오르는 말을 서둘러 내뱉었지만, 절반은 달래는 듯, 절반은 간청하는 듯한 어조로 말하며 페더스톤 씨에게 가려는 듯 일어섰다. 프레드는 당연히 구름이 갈라지고 한 줄기 빛이 비치는 것 같은 기분이 들었다. 그는 움직여 그녀의 길을 막아섰다.\n\n\"한마디만 해 줘, 메리. 그러면 뭐든 할게. 날 최악으로 생각하지는 않겠다고, 날 아주 단념하지는 않겠다고 말해 줘.\"\n\n\"널 나쁘게 생각하는 게 나한테 무슨 즐거움이라도 되는 것처럼.\" 메리가 서글픈 어조로 말했다. \"네가 게으르고 경박한 인간으로 지내는 걸 보는 게 나한테 몹시 괴롭지 않기라도 한 것처럼. 남들은 일하고 분투하고, 해야 할 일도 그토록 많은데, 어떻게 그렇게 한심하게 지낼 수 있어? 어떻게 세상의 쓸모 있는 일 가운데 아무것에도 어울리지 못하는 사람으로 견딜 수 있어? 게다가 넌 천성에 그토록 좋은 면이 많은데, 프레드, 넌 무척 가치 있는 사람이 될 수도 있을 텐데.\"\n\n\"네가 날 사랑한다고 말해 준다면, 메리, 네가 바라는 무엇이든 되려고 애쓸게.\"\n\n\"늘 남에게 매달리고, 남이 자기를 위해 무얼 해 줄지나 셈하는 남자를 사랑한다고 말하기는 부끄러울 거야. 마흔이 되면 넌 어떤 사람이 되어 있을까? 보이어 씨처럼 되겠지, 아마. 꼭 그렇게 게으르게, 벡 부인의 앞쪽 거실에 얹혀살면서, 뚱뚱하고 추레한 모습으로 누군가 저녁 식사에 초대해 주기를 바라며, 오전 내내 우스꽝스러운 노래나 익히면서, 아니, 플루트로 곡조나 익히면서.\"\n\n메리의 입술은 프레드의 미래에 관한 그 질문을 던지기 시작하자마자 미소로 말려 올라갔고(젊은 영혼은 변덕스러우니), 말을 끝맺기도 전에 그녀의 얼굴은 즐거움으로 환히 빛났다. 그에게는 메리가 자기를 보며 웃을 수 있다는 것이 마치 아픔이 멎는 것 같았고, 수동적인 미소를 띤 채 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다. 하지만 그녀는 재빨리 문 쪽으로 미끄러져 가며 말했다. \"삼촌께 말씀드릴게. 잠깐이라도 꼭 뵙고 가야 해.\"\n\n프레드는 자기가 정의해 주기만 하면 기꺼이 하겠다는 그 \"무엇이든\"과는 별개로, 자신의 미래가 메리의 빈정대는 예언이 실현되는 것에 맞서 보장되어 있다고 속으로 느꼈다. 그는 메리 앞에서는 감히 페더스톤 씨에게서 받을 유산에 대한 기대를 입에 올린 적이 없었고, 그녀는 마치 모든 것이 그 자신에게 달려 있다는 듯 늘 그 문제를 모른 체했다. 그러나 만일 그가 실제로 그 재산을 물려받는다면, 그녀도 그의 처지가 달라졌음을 인정해야 할 터였다. 이 모든 생각이 삼촌을 만나러 올라가기 전에 다소 나른하게 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다. 그는 감기에 걸렸다는 핑계를 대며 잠깐만 머물렀고, 그가 집을 나서기 전에 메리는 다시 나타나지 않았다. 그러나 말을 타고 집으로 가면서 그는 우울하다기보다 몸이 아프다는 것을 더 의식하기 시작했다.\n\n땅거미가 진 직후에 케일럽 가스가 스톤 코트에 도착했을 때, 메리는 놀라지 않았다. 그가 그녀를 찾아올 짬을 내는 일이 드물고, 페더스톤 씨와 이야기를 나눠야 하는 것을 전혀 좋아하지 않는데도 그러했다. 한편 노인은 처남이 영 거북했으니, 그는 약 올릴 수도 없고, 가난하게 여겨지는 것을 개의치 않으며, 청할 것도 없고, 게다가 온갖 농사일과 광산일을 자기보다 더 잘 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 메리는 부모가 자기를 보고 싶어 하리라는 것을 확신하고 있었고, 아버지가 오지 않았다면 이튿날 한두 시간쯤 집에 다녀올 허락을 받았을 터였다. 차를 마시며 페더스톤 씨와 시세를 논한 뒤, 케일럽은 일어나 작별 인사를 하고 말했다. \"너한테 할 말이 있다, 메리.\"\n\n그녀는 촛불을 들고 불기 없는 또 다른 큰 거실로 들어가, 거뭇한 마호가니 탁자 위에 그 희미한 빛을 내려놓고는, 아버지를 향해 돌아서서 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸고 어린아이처럼 입을 맞췄다. 그가 그토록 좋아하는 입맞춤이었다. 그의 넓은 이마의 표정이, 쓰다듬어 줄 때 크고 아름다운 개의 표정이 누그러지듯 부드러워졌다. 메리는 그가 가장 아끼는 자식이었고, 수전이 무어라 말하든, 다른 모든 일에서는 그녀가 옳다 해도, 케일럽은 프레드든 누구든 메리를 다른 여자아이들보다 더 사랑스럽게 여기는 것을 당연하게 생각했다.\n\n\"너한테 할 얘기가 좀 있단다, 얘야.\" 케일럽이 머뭇거리는 투로 말했다. \"그리 좋은 소식은 아니지만, 그래도 더 나쁠 수도 있었지.\"\n\n\"돈 얘기예요, 아버지? 무슨 일인지 알 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그래? 어떻게 알지? 그게 말이다, 내가 또 좀 어리석은 짓을 해서 어음에 이름을 적었는데, 이제 갚을 때가 됐단다. 그래서 네 어머니가 모아 둔 돈을 내놓아야 하게 됐지, 그게 제일 안된 일이야. 그런데도 그것으로 셈이 딱 맞아떨어지질 않는구나. 백십 파운드가 필요한데, 네 어머니한테 구십이 파운드가 있고, 내 은행에는 여유 돈이 없어. 그래서 네 어머니가 너한테도 모아 둔 게 좀 있을 거라고 하더구나.\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요. 이십사 파운드 넘게 있어요. 아버지가 오실 것 같아서 가방에 넣어 뒀어요. 보세요! 아름다운 흰 지폐랑 금화예요.\"\n\n메리는 손가방에서 접어 둔 돈을 꺼내 아버지의 손에 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"음, 하지만 어떻게, 우리한테 필요한 건 십팔 파운드뿐이란다. 자, 나머지는 도로 넣어 두렴, 얘야. 그런데 넌 이걸 어떻게 알았니?\" 케일럽이 물었다. 돈에는 도무지 어찌할 수 없이 무심한 그가, 이제 이 일이 메리의 마음과 어떤 관계가 있을지를 주로 염려하기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 프레드가 말해 줬어요.\"\n\n\"아! 일부러 온 거니?\"\n\n\"네, 그런 것 같아요. 무척 괴로워하더군요.\"\n\n\"프레드는 믿을 만한 사람이 못 되는 것 같아 걱정이구나, 메리.\" 아버지가 머뭇거리는 다정함으로 말했다. \"행동보다 마음은 더 나은 사람일지도 모르지. 하지만 누군가의 행복이 그 애한테 매여 있는 건 안타까운 일이라고 생각한다. 네 어머니도 그렇게 생각할 거고.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇게 생각해요, 아버지.\" 메리가 고개를 들지 않은 채 아버지의 손등을 자기 뺨에 갖다 대며 말했다.\n\n\"꼬치꼬치 캐묻고 싶지는 않다, 얘야. 다만 너와 프레드 사이에 무슨 일이 있을까 봐 염려스러워서 너한테 일러 두고 싶었단다. 그게 말이다, 메리.\" 여기서 케일럽의 목소리가 한결 다정해졌다. 그는 탁자 위에서 모자를 이리저리 밀며 그것을 바라보고 있다가, 마침내 눈을 들어 딸을 바라보았다. \"여자란, 아무리 착하다 해도, 남편이 만들어 주는 삶을 견디며 살아야 한단다. 네 어머니도 나 때문에 많은 걸 견뎌야 했지.\"\n\n메리는 아버지의 손등을 입술에 가져다 대고 그에게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 완벽한 사람은 없지, 하지만.\" 여기서 가스 씨는 말로 다 못 한 것을 거들려는 듯 고개를 저었다. \"내가 생각하는 건 이런 거란다. 아내가 남편을 한 번도 믿을 수 없을 때, 남편 마음속에 남에게 잘못하는 것을 제 발가락 짓밟히는 것보다 더 두려워하게 만들 원칙이라곤 없을 때, 그게 아내한테 어떤 일일까. 그게 결국 핵심이란다, 메리. 젊은 사람들은 삶이 뭔지 알기도 전에 서로 좋아하게 될 수 있고, 그저 함께만 있을 수 있으면 다 휴일 같을 거라고 여길지도 모르지. 하지만 그건 머잖아 일하는 날로 변하는 법이야, 얘야. 그래도 넌 누구보다 분별이 있고, 솜에 싸여 자라지도 않았으니, 내가 이런 말을 할 필요가 없을지도 모르겠다. 하지만 아버지란 딸을 위해 떨기 마련이고, 넌 여기 혼자 있으니 말이다.\"\n\n\"제 걱정은 마세요, 아버지.\" 메리가 진지하게 아버지의 눈을 마주 보며 말했다. \"프레드는 늘 저한테 무척 잘해 줬어요. 마음씨가 곱고 다정하고, 그 모든 방종에도 불구하고 거짓된 사람은 아니라고 생각해요. 하지만 저는 남자다운 자립심이라곤 없고, 남이 자기를 거둬 주리라는 요행만 바라며 시간을 허비하는 사람과는 절대로 약혼하지 않을 거예요. 아버지와 어머니가 그러기에는 제게 너무 큰 자긍심을 가르쳐 주셨거든요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇지, 그래. 그럼 난 마음이 놓이는구나.\" 가스 씨가 모자를 집어 들며 말했다. \"하지만 네가 번 돈을 가지고 도망쳐야 하다니 마음이 아프구나, 얘야.\"\n\n\"아버지!\" 메리가 더없이 깊은 만류의 어조로 말했다. \"집에 있는 모두에게 호주머니 가득 사랑도 함께 전해 주세요.\" 그가 바깥문을 닫고 나서기 전, 그녀의 마지막 말이었다.\n\n\"네 아버지가 네 돈을 원하셨던 모양이구나.\" 메리가 돌아오자 노인 페더스톤 씨가 늘 그렇듯 못마땅한 억측을 부리는 솜씨로 말했다. \"살림이 빠듯한 게지, 내 짐작에는. 넌 이제 성년이니, 네 자신을 위해 모아 둬야 할 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n\"저는 제 아버지와 어머니를 제 자신의 가장 좋은 부분으로 여깁니다, 어르신.\" 메리가 차갑게 말했다.\n\n페더스톤 씨는 끙 하고 앓는 소리를 냈다. 그녀 같은 평범한 부류의 여자아이가 쓸모 있기를 기대받는 것이야 부정할 수 없었으므로, 그는 언제 꺼내도 어울릴 만큼 충분히 고약한 다른 대꾸를 생각해 냈다. \"프레드 빈시가 내일 오거든, 그 애를 붙들고 수다나 떨게 하지 말고, 나한테로 올려 보내거라.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXVI.\n\n그는 나를 때리고 나는 그에게 욕설을 퍼붓는다. 오, 가치 있는 만족이여! 차라리 반대였다면—그가 내게 욕하는 동안 내가 그를 때릴 수 있다면.—『트로일러스와 크레시다』\n\n그러나 프레드는 다음 날 스톤 코트에 가지 않았으니, 그럴 만한 분명한 이유가 있었다. 다이아몬드를 찾아 위생 상태가 나쁜 하운즐리의 거리를 돌아다닌 그 방문에서 그는 마필 거래의 손해뿐만 아니라, 하루 이틀 동안은 단순한 우울과 두통이라 여겼던 어떤 병까지 함께 얻어 돌아왔다. 그런데 스톤 코트 방문에서 돌아오자 그 증세가 너무도 심해져서, 식당으로 들어가 소파에 몸을 던지고는 어머니의 근심 어린 물음에 이렇게 답했다. \"몹시 아파요. 렌치 선생을 불러 주셔야겠어요.\"\n\n렌치가 왔지만 심각한 것은 없다고 보았으며, \"가벼운 이상\"이라고 말했을 뿐, 다음 날 다시 오겠다는 말은 하지 않았다. 그는 빈시 집안을 응당 소중하게 여겼으나, 아무리 신중한 사람도 일상에 무뎌지기 쉬워서, 분주한 아침이면 종지기가 매일 종을 치듯 무심한 열의로 일을 해치우는 법이다. 렌치는 자그마하고 단정하며 신경질적인 사내로, 잘 손질한 가발을 쓰고 있었다. 그는 고된 진료에, 성마른 기질에, 림프질의 아내와 일곱 명의 아이를 두었다. 그리고 그날도 이미 다소 늦은 시각에야, 티프턴 건너편에서 민친 박사를 만나러 사 마일 떨어진 곳까지 마차를 몰고 떠나야 했으니, 시골 개업의 힉스가 사망한 탓에 그쪽 방면의 미들마치 진료가 늘어났던 것이다. 위대한 정치가도 그르치는데, 하물며 보잘것없는 의사라고 어찌 그러지 않겠는가? 렌치는 평소대로 흰 약 꾸러미를 보내는 일을 빠뜨리지 않았는데, 이번에는 그 안에 검고 독한 내용물이 들어 있었다. 그 효과는 가엾은 프레드를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 더 괴롭혔으나, 자신이 \"병에 걸렸다\"고는 믿고 싶지 않다던 그는 이튿날 아침 평소의 느긋한 시각에 일어나 아침을 먹으려고 아래층으로 내려갔지만, 난롯가에 앉아 떨고 있는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 못했다. 다시 렌치를 부르러 사람을 보냈으나 그는 왕진을 나간 뒤였고, 빈시 부인은 사랑하는 아들의 변한 안색과 전반적인 비참한 모습을 보고 울음을 터뜨리며 스프래그 박사를 부르겠다고 했다.\n\n\"오, 말도 안 돼요, 어머니! 아무것도 아니에요.\" 프레드가 뜨겁고 메마른 손을 어머니에게 내밀며 말했다. \"금방 괜찮아질 거예요. 그 고약하게 축축한 길을 달리다 감기에 걸렸나 봐요.\"\n\n\"엄마!\" 창가에 앉아 있던 로저먼드가 말했다(식당 창문은 로윅 게이트라 불리는 그 매우 점잖은 거리를 향해 있었다). \"리드게이트 선생님이 누군가와 이야기하려고 멈춰 서 계세요. 저라면 안으로 불러들이겠어요. 그분이 엘렌 불스트로드를 낫게 해 주셨대요. 누구든 다 고친다고들 해요.\"\n\n빈시 부인은 의료상의 예법 따위는 생각지도 않고 오직 프레드만을 떠올리며, 창가로 뛰어가 순식간에 창문을 열었다. 리드게이트는 철책 너머 불과 두 야드 떨어진 곳에 있었고, 그녀가 부르기도 전에 갑작스러운 창틀 소리에 돌아보았다. 이 분도 채 못 되어 그는 방 안에 들어와 있었고, 로저먼드는 적당한 예의에 대한 의식과 충돌하는 어여쁜 근심을 드러낼 만큼만 머물렀다가 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n리드게이트는 빈시 부인의 정신이 놀라운 본능으로 사소한 사항 하나하나에, 특히 렌치가 다시 오겠다고 했는지 안 했는지에 관해 고집스럽게 매달리는 이야기를 들어야 했다. 렌치와의 사이에 어색한 일이 생길 수도 있겠다는 것을 리드게이트는 단번에 알아차렸으나, 사태가 워낙 심각해서 그런 고려는 접어 둘 수밖에 없었다. 그는 프레드가 장티푸스의 발진기에 있으며, 바로 그 잘못된 약을 먹었다고 확신했다. 그는 즉시 침대에 누워야 하고, 전문 간호인을 두어야 하며, 여러 가지 기구와 예방 조치를 써야 했는데, 리드게이트는 이에 관해 까다로웠다. 가엾은 빈시 부인은 이러한 위험의 징후에 대한 공포를 가장 쉽게 떠오르는 말들로 쏟아냈다. 그녀는 그것이 \"렌치 선생의 매우 못된 처사\"라고 생각했다. 피콕 선생도 똑같이 친구였건만, 그는 여러 해 동안 피콕 선생보다 자기 집안을 더 챙겨 주지 않았던가. 어째서 렌치 선생이 남의 아이들보다 자기 아이들을 더 소홀히 대하는지 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다. 그는 라처 부인의 아이들이 홍역에 걸렸을 때 소홀히 하지 않았고, 사실 빈시 부인도 그가 그러기를 바라지는 않았다. 그런데 만약 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면—\n\n여기서 가엾은 빈시 부인의 기력이 완전히 꺾였고, 니오베 같은 목과 선량한 얼굴이 슬프게 일그러졌다. 이것은 프레드에게 들리지 않는 현관에서 일어난 일이었으나, 로저먼드가 응접실 문을 열고 근심스럽게 다가왔다. 리드게이트는 렌치를 변호하며, 어제의 증상은 분간하기 어려웠을 수 있고 이런 형태의 열병은 그 초기 단계에서 매우 모호하다고 말했다. 그는 지체 없이 즉시 약방으로 가서 처방을 조제하게 하겠지만, 렌치 선생에게 편지를 써서 어떤 조치를 했는지 알리겠다고 했다.\n\n\"하지만 선생님은 다시 와 주셔야 해요—계속 프레드를 봐 주셔야 해요. 제 아이를 올지 안 올지도 모르는 사람에게 맡길 수는 없어요. 저는 누구에게도 악감정을 품지 않아요, 정말이지요. 렌치 선생이 제 늑막염을 낫게 해 주긴 했지만, 차라리 저를 죽게 두는 편이 나았을 거예요—만약—만약—\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 여기서 렌치 선생을 만나겠습니다, 그래도 될까요?\" 리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 렌치가 이런 종류의 사례를 현명하게 다룰 준비가 제대로 되어 있지 않다고 진심으로 믿고 있었다.\n\n\"부디 그렇게 해 주세요, 리드게이트 선생님.\" 로저먼드가 어머니를 거들며 말했고, 어머니의 팔을 부축해 자리를 떠나도록 이끌었다.\n\n빈시 씨는 집에 돌아오자 렌치에게 몹시 화가 나서, 그가 다시는 자기 집에 발을 들이지 않는다 해도 상관없다고 했다. 이제는 렌치가 좋아하든 말든 리드게이트가 계속 봐야 했다. 집안에 열병이 든 것은 결코 농담거리가 아니었다. 이제 모두에게 사람을 보내 목요일 만찬에 오지 말라고 알려야 했다. 그리고 프리처드는 포도주를 준비할 필요가 없었으니, 전염병에는 브랜디가 최고였다. \"나는 브랜디를 마시겠어.\" 빈시 씨가 강조하며 덧붙였다—마치 지금은 빈 탄약으로 허세를 부릴 때가 아니라는 듯이. \"프레드 녀석은 유난히 운이 없는 놈이야. 이 모든 걸 메우려면 앞으로 운이 좀 따라 줘야 할 텐데—안 그러면 누가 장남을 두려고 하겠나.\"\n\n\"그런 말 마세요, 빈시.\" 어머니가 입술을 떨며 말했다. \"그 애를 제게서 빼앗기게 하고 싶지 않다면요.\"\n\n\"그러다 당신은 걱정으로 죽겠소, 루시. 그게 내 눈에 다 보인다오.\" 빈시 씨가 좀 더 부드럽게 말했다. \"어쨌든 렌치에게는 이 일에 대한 내 생각을 알려야겠어.\" (빈시 씨가 어렴풋이 생각한 바는, 렌치가 자기—시장의—집안에 대해 마땅한 정성을 보였더라면 어떻게든 이 열병을 막을 수 있었으리라는 것이었다.) \"나는 새 의사니 새 목사니 하는 떠들썩한 소리에 동조하는 마지막 사람일세—그들이 불스트로드의 사람이든 아니든 말이야. 하지만 렌치에게는 내 생각을 알릴 테니, 좋을 대로 받아들이라지.\"\n\n렌치는 그것을 전혀 좋게 받아들이지 않았다. 리드게이트는 무뚝뚝한 방식 안에서나마 최대한 정중했지만, 자신을 곤경에 빠뜨린 사람의 정중함이란 한층 더한 분노를 돋울 뿐이고, 더구나 미리부터 미움의 대상이었다면 더욱 그러했다. 시골 개업의들은 으레 성마른 부류라서 명예 문제에 예민했는데, 렌치는 그중에서도 가장 성마른 축에 들었다. 그는 저녁에 리드게이트를 만나는 것을 거절하지는 않았으나, 그 자리에서 그의 성미는 다소 시험에 들었다. 그는 빈시 부인이 이렇게 말하는 것을 들어야 했다—\n\n\"오, 렌치 선생, 제가 도대체 무슨 짓을 했기에 저를 이렇게 대하시나요?—가 버린 뒤에 다시는 오지 않으시다니! 제 아들이 시신이 되어 누워 있을 수도 있었어요!\"\n\n적군인 전염병을 향해 맹렬한 포화를 퍼붓느라 결과적으로 잔뜩 달아올라 있던 빈시 씨는, 렌치가 들어오는 소리를 듣자 벌떡 일어나 자기 생각을 알리려고 현관으로 나갔다.\n\n\"이보게, 렌치, 이건 농담을 넘어선 일일세.\" 시장이 말했다. 그는 요즘 들어 공직자다운 위엄으로 위반자들을 꾸짖어야 했던 터라, 이제 엄지손가락을 겨드랑이에 끼워 넣으며 몸을 한껏 부풀렸다. \"이런 집안에 열병이 모르는 새 들어오게 만들다니. 마땅히 고소할 만한 일인데도 그렇게 안 되는 일들이 있단 말이지—그게 내 생각일세.\"\n\n그러나 불합리한 비난은, 가르침을 받는다는 느낌, 아니 차라리 리드게이트 같은 젊은 자가 속으로 자기에게 가르침이 필요하다고 여긴다는 느낌보다는 견디기가 쉬웠다. \"사실인즉슨\" 나중에 렌치가 말한 바로는, 리드게이트가 변덕스럽고 외래적인 관념을 떠벌렸는데 그런 것은 오래갈 리 없다는 것이었다. 그는 순간의 노여움을 삼켰으나, 나중에 편지를 써서 이 사례를 더 이상 맡지 않겠다고 통보했다. 좋은 집안일지는 몰라도, 렌치는 전문적인 문제에서 누구에게도 굽실거릴 생각이 없었다. 그는 상당한 개연성을 자기편에 두고서, 리드게이트도 머지않아 실수를 들킬 것이며, 동업자들의 약품 판매를 깎아내리려는 그 신사답지 못한 시도가 머지않아 자신에게 되돌아오리라고 짚었다. 그는 리드게이트의 술수에 대해 신랄한 말을 던졌으니, 잘 속는 사람들 사이에서 거짓 평판을 얻으려는, 돌팔이에게나 어울리는 짓이라는 것이었다. 치료법에 관한 그런 허튼소리는 건실한 개업의가 결코 꾸며 내는 법이 없었다.\n\n이것은 렌치가 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 리드게이트를 아프게 찌르는 대목이었다. 무지에 의해 추켜세워지는 것은 굴욕적일 뿐만 아니라 위험하기까지 했고, 날씨 예언자의 평판보다 나을 게 없었다. 그는 모든 일이 진행되어야 하는 그 어리석은 기대들에 진절머리가 났고, 전문가답지 못한 솔직함으로 인해 렌치가 바랄 만큼이나 자기 자신을 해칠 가능성이 다분했다.\n\n그러나 리드게이트는 빈시 집안의 진료를 맡게 되었고, 그 일은 미들마치에서 널리 회자되는 화제가 되었다. 어떤 이들은 빈시 집안이 망신스럽게 처신했다고, 빈시 씨가 렌치를 협박했으며 빈시 부인은 그가 아들을 독살하려 했다고 비난했다고들 말했다. 또 어떤 이들은 리드게이트가 마침 그곳을 지나간 것이 하늘의 섭리였으며, 그가 열병에 놀랍도록 능하고, 불스트로드가 그를 내세운 것은 옳았다는 의견이었다. 많은 사람들은 리드게이트가 애초에 이 도시로 온 것 자체가 실은 불스트로드 덕분이라고 믿었다. 그리고 늘 뜨개질 코를 세느라 바빠서 뜨개질 줄 사이에서 주워들은 오해의 단편들로 정보를 그러모으던 태프트 부인은, 리드게이트가 불스트로드의 사생아라는 생각을 머릿속에 굳혀 버렸으니, 그것은 복음주의 평신도들에 대한 그녀의 의심을 정당화해 주는 듯한 사실이었다.\n\n그녀는 어느 날 페어브러더 부인에게 이 지식의 조각을 전했고, 부인은 빠짐없이 아들에게 그것을 일러 주며 한마디 덧붙였다—\n\n\"불스트로드에 관한 거라면 나는 무슨 일에도 놀라지 않겠다만, 리드게이트 선생에 대해 그런 생각을 한다는 건 안타까운 일이구나.\"\n\n\"아니, 어머니.\" 페어브러더가 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨린 뒤에 말했다. \"리드게이트가 북부의 훌륭한 집안 출신이라는 걸 잘 아시잖아요. 그는 여기 오기 전까지 불스트로드라는 이름을 들어 본 적도 없어요.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 선생에 관한 한 그것 참 다행이구나, 캠던.\" 노부인이 단호한 태도로 말했다. \"하지만 불스트로드로 말할 것 같으면—그 소문은 다른 어떤 아들에게는 사실일지도 모르지.\"\n\n# 제25장\n\n높은 뮤즈로 하여금 올림포스의 사랑을 노래하게 하라.\n우리는 한낱 인간일 뿐이니, 인간을 노래할 수밖에 없도다.\n\n내 친구들 가운데 한 저명한 철학자가 있는데, 그는 당신의 보잘것없는 가구조차도 과학의 청명한 빛 속으로 들어 올림으로써 품위를 부여할 줄 안다. 그가 내게 의미심장한 작은 사실 하나를 보여 주었다. 당신의 거울이나, 가정부가 닦도록 만들어진 광이 나는 강철의 널찍한 표면은, 사방으로 미세하고도 무수하게 긁혀 있을 것이다. 그러나 이제 그 앞에 불 켜진 양초 하나를 빛의 중심으로 놓아 보라, 그러면 보라! 그 긁힌 자국들은 그 작은 태양을 중심으로 동심원의 아름다운 무리를 이루며 스스로 정렬하는 듯 보일 것이다. 그 긁힌 자국들은 어디로든 공평하게 뻗어 있다는 사실이 입증될 수 있으며, 동심원 배열이라는 그 그럴듯한 환영을 만들어 내는 것은 오직 당신의 양초일 뿐, 그 빛이 배타적인 시각적 선택을 하며 떨어지는 것이다. 이런 것들은 하나의 우화다. 긁힌 자국들은 사건들이고, 양초는 지금 여기 없는 어떤 사람의, 이를테면 빈시 양의 이기심이다. 로저먼드에게는 자기만의 섭리가 있었으니, 그것은 친절하게도 그녀를 다른 처녀들보다 더 매력적으로 만들어 주었고, 그녀와 리드게이트를 효과적으로 가까운 거리에 데려다 놓으려고 프레드의 병과 렌치 씨의 실수까지도 마련해 놓은 듯 보였다. 부모가 바란 대로 로저먼드가 스톤 코트나 다른 곳으로 떠나는 데 동의했다면, 그것은 이러한 섭리에 어긋나는 일이 되었을 것이다. 더구나 리드게이트 씨가 그런 예방책은 불필요하다고 여겼으니 말이다. 그리하여 프레드의 병이 드러난 다음 날 아침에 모건 양과 아이들은 어느 농가로 보내졌지만, 로저먼드는 아빠와 엄마 곁을 떠나지 않겠다고 했다.\n\n가엾은 엄마는 참으로 여자에게서 태어난 그 누구의 마음이라도 움직일 만한 모습이었고, 아내를 끔찍이 사랑하는 빈시 씨는 프레드보다도 아내가 더 걱정스러웠다. 그가 고집하지 않았더라면 그녀는 한순간도 쉬지 않았을 것이다. 그녀의 환한 빛은 온통 흐려졌고, 늘 그토록 산뜻하고 화사하던 옷차림도 의식하지 못한 채, 그녀는 눈빛이 흐릿하고 깃털이 헝클어진 병든 새처럼 보였으며, 예전에 가장 흥미를 끌던 광경과 소리에도 감각이 무뎌져 있었다. 자기 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 헤매어 가는 듯한 프레드의 헛소리는 그녀의 가슴을 찢어 놓았다. 렌치 씨를 향한 첫 분노가 터져 나온 뒤로 그녀는 아주 조용히 지냈으니, 그녀가 낮게 부르짖는 단 하나의 호소는 리드게이트를 향한 것이었다. 그녀는 그를 방 밖으로 따라 나가 그의 팔에 손을 얹고 신음하듯 말하곤 했다. \"내 아들을 살려 주세요.\" 한번은 이렇게 간청했다. \"그 애는 늘 제게 잘했어요, 리드게이트 씨. 한 번도 제 어미한테 모진 말을 한 적이 없답니다.\" 마치 가엾은 프레드의 고통이 그를 향한 고발이라도 되는 듯이. 어머니의 기억 속 가장 깊은 가닥들이 모두 흔들렸고, 그녀에게 말을 건넬 때 한층 부드러운 어조를 띠던 그 젊은이는, 그녀가 그 아이를 낳기도 전에 새롭게 알게 된 사랑으로 사랑했던 그 갓난아기와 하나가 되었다.\n\n\"좋은 희망이 있습니다, 빈시 부인.\" 리드게이트는 말하곤 했다. \"저와 함께 아래층으로 내려가서 음식 이야기를 나눕시다.\" 그렇게 그는 로저먼드가 있는 거실로 그녀를 데려갔고, 그녀에게 변화를 주어, 그녀를 위해 마련해 둔 차나 죽을 뜻밖에도 들게 만들었다. 이런 일들에 관해서 그와 로저먼드 사이에는 한결같은 양해가 있었다. 그는 병실로 가기 전에 거의 언제나 그녀를 만났고, 그녀는 엄마를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있는지 그에게 도움을 청했다. 그의 지시를 실행하는 그녀의 침착함과 능란함은 감탄할 만한 것이었으니, 로저먼드를 본다는 생각이 그 병에 대한 그의 관심과 뒤섞이기 시작한 것도 이상한 일은 아니었다. 특히 위태로운 고비를 넘기고 프레드의 회복을 확신하기 시작했을 때는 더욱 그러했다. 더 불확실하던 시기에는 그가 스프레이그 박사를 부르자고 권한 적이 있었지만(그 박사는 가능하다면 렌치를 생각해서 차라리 중립을 지키고 싶어 했을 것이다), 두 번의 진찰 끝에 그 병의 처치는 리드게이트에게 맡겨졌으며, 그가 부지런히 매달릴 만한 이유는 충분했다. 아침저녁으로 그는 빈시 씨 댁에 있었고, 프레드가 그저 기력이 없을 뿐인 상태가 되어, 더할 나위 없는 보살핌을 필요로 할 뿐 아니라 그것을 의식하며 누워 있게 되자, 그 방문은 차츰 즐거워졌다. 그리하여 빈시 부인은 결국 그 병이 자기 애정을 위한 축제를 마련해 준 것 같은 기분이 들었다.\n\n늙은 페더스톤 씨가 리드게이트를 통해, 자기 피터 페더스톤은 프레드 없이는 지낼 수 없고 그의 방문이 무척 그립다며 프레드더러 어서 나으라는 전갈을 보냈을 때, 아버지와 어머니는 모두 그것을 기운을 낼 또 하나의 이유로 받아들였다. 그 노인 자신도 점점 자리보전을 하게 되고 있었다. 빈시 부인은 프레드가 들을 수 있게 되었을 때 이런 전갈들을 그에게 전했고, 그는 숱 많은 금발이 모조리 깎여 나간 섬세하고 야윈 얼굴을 그녀에게로 돌렸는데, 그 얼굴에서 두 눈은 한층 커진 듯 보였고, 메리에 관한 한마디 말을 갈망하며 그녀가 자기 병에 대해 어떻게 느끼는지 궁금해하는 듯했다. 그의 입술에서는 한마디도 새어 나오지 않았다. 그러나 \"눈으로 듣는 것은 사랑의 진귀한 재치에 속하나니\", 마음이 가득 차오른 어머니는 프레드의 갈망을 알아차렸을 뿐 아니라, 그를 만족시키기 위해서라면 어떤 희생이라도 치를 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"내 아들이 다시 튼튼해지는 것만 볼 수 있다면,\" 그녀는 사랑에 겨운 어리석음으로 말했다. \"또 누가 알겠니? 어쩌면 스톤 코트의 주인이 될지도! 그러면 그 애는 마음에 드는 누구하고든 결혼할 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"그쪽에서 저를 마다하면 안 되지요, 어머니.\" 프레드가 말했다. 병이 그를 어린애처럼 만들어, 그가 말하는 동안 눈물이 솟았다.\n\n\"오, 젤리 좀 들렴, 얘야.\" 빈시 부인이 말했는데, 속으로는 그런 거절이 있으리라고는 도무지 믿지 않았다.\n\n남편이 집에 없을 때면 그녀는 결코 프레드의 곁을 떠나지 않았고, 그래서 로저먼드는 혼자 있는 일이 많은 이례적인 처지에 놓이게 되었다. 리드게이트는 당연히 그녀와 오래 머물 생각은 전혀 하지 않았지만, 두 사람이 나누는 짧고 사사로움 없는 대화가 수줍음으로 이루어진 그 독특한 친밀감을 빚어내고 있는 듯했다. 그들은 말을 할 때 서로 마주 보지 않을 수 없었는데, 어쩐 일인지 그 마주 봄은 실제로 그러했던 것처럼 당연한 일로 끝까지 이어지지 못했다. 리드게이트는 이런 종류의 의식이 불쾌하게 느껴지기 시작해서, 어느 날은 잘못 다룬 꼭두각시처럼 아래쪽이나 아무 데나 내려다보았다. 그러나 이것은 좋지 않게 끝났다. 다음 날에는 로저먼드가 아래를 내려다보았고, 그 결과 두 사람의 눈이 다시 마주쳤을 때 둘 다 전보다 더 의식하게 되었던 것이다. 이 일에 과학으로는 도움 될 것이 없었고, 리드게이트는 시시덕거리고 싶지 않았으니 어리석음으로도 도움 될 것이 없어 보였다. 그러므로 이웃 사람들이 더 이상 그 집을 격리 상태로 여기지 않게 되고, 로저먼드를 혼자 만날 기회가 매우 줄어들었을 때는 오히려 마음이 놓였다.\n\n그러나 서로가 상대방이 무언가를 느끼고 있다고 느끼는 그 어색함의 친밀감은, 일단 생겨난 이상 그 효과를 없앨 수 없는 법이다. 날씨에 관한 이야기나 그 밖의 교양 있는 화제는 공허한 방편처럼 보이기 십상이고, 서로에게 끌린다는 사실을 솔직히 인정하지 않는 한 행동은 좀처럼 편안해지지 못하는데, 물론 그것이 무언가 깊거나 진지한 것을 뜻할 필요는 없었다. 로저먼드와 리드게이트가 우아하게 편안함으로 미끄러져 들어가, 그들의 교제를 다시 활기차게 만든 것도 바로 이런 식이었다. 손님들이 여느 때처럼 오가고, 응접실에서는 다시 음악이 흘렀으며, 빈시 씨의 시장직에 따르는 온갖 별스러운 환대가 되돌아왔다. 리드게이트는 기회가 닿을 때마다 로저먼드의 옆자리에 앉아, 그녀의 연주를 들으려고 머무르며 자신을 그녀의 포로라고 칭했지만, 정작 속으로는 줄곧 그녀의 포로가 되지 않을 작정이었다. 그가 당장 결혼한 남자로서 만족스러운 살림을 차릴 수 있다는 생각이 터무니없다는 사실 자체가 위험에 대한 충분한 보증이었다. 조금 사랑에 빠진 척하는 이 놀이는 즐거웠고, 더 진지한 일을 방해하지도 않았다. 어쨌거나 시시덕거림이 반드시 불에 데는 과정은 아니었다. 한편 로저먼드로 말하자면, 평생 그토록 즐거운 나날을 누려 본 적이 없었다. 매혹할 만한 가치가 있는 누군가에게 자신이 흠모받고 있다는 것이 확실했고, 자신에게서든 남에게서든 시시덕거림과 사랑을 구별하지 못했다. 그녀는 순풍을 받으며 가고 싶은 곳으로 곧장 항해하는 듯했고, 그녀의 생각은 로윅 게이트에 있는, 머지않아 비기를 바라는 어느 멋진 집에 온통 쏠려 있었다. 그녀는 결혼하면 아버지 집에서 마음에 들지 않던 모든 손님들을 능란하게 떨쳐 버리겠다고 단단히 작정했고, 자기가 좋아하는 그 집의 응접실을 갖가지 양식의 가구로 꾸민 모습을 상상했다.\n\n물론 그녀의 생각은 리드게이트 자신에게도 온통 쏠려 있었다. 그는 그녀에게 거의 완벽해 보였다. 그가 음표를 제대로 알아서 그녀의 음악에 빠져드는 그의 황홀함이 감정에 휩싸인 코끼리처럼 보이지만 않았더라면, 그리고 그가 옷차림에 관한 그녀의 세련된 취향을 좀 더 잘 분간할 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 그에게서 결점을 거의 짚어 낼 수 없었을 것이다. 그는 젊은 플림데일이나 케이어스 라처 씨와 얼마나 다른가! 그 젊은이들은 프랑스어라고는 한마디도 몰랐고, 어떤 주제에 관해서도 놀랄 만한 지식으로 이야기하지 못했는데, 다만 염색업이나 운송업에 관해서라면 모를까, 그런 것은 물론 입에 올리기 부끄러워했다. 그들은 미들마치의 신사 계층으로서, 은제 손잡이가 달린 채찍과 새틴 넥타이에 우쭐했지만 태도는 어색했고, 머뭇거리며 농담을 했다. 프레드조차도 그들보다는 나았으니, 적어도 대학 출신의 말투와 태도는 지니고 있었던 것이다. 반면에 리드게이트는 언제나 사람들이 귀를 기울였고, 우월함을 의식한 자의 무심한 정중함으로 처신했으며, 옷에 대해 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없으면서도 어떤 타고난 친화력으로 알맞은 옷을 걸친 듯 보였다. 그가 방에 들어서면 로저먼드는 자랑스러웠고, 그가 그녀를 알아보는 미소를 띠며 다가오면, 그녀는 자신이 부러움을 살 만한 경의의 대상이라는 감미로운 느낌에 젖었다. 만약 리드게이트가 자신이 그 섬세한 가슴속에 불러일으킨 그 모든 자부심을 알았더라면, 그는 다른 어떤 남자와도, 심지어 체액 병리학이나 섬유 조직에 대해 더없이 무지한 남자와도 마찬가지로 흡족해했을지 모른다. 그는 한 남자의 탁월함을 그것이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 너무 정확히 알지 못한 채 흠모하는 것을 여성 정신의 가장 어여쁜 자태 가운데 하나로 여겼다. 그러나 로저먼드는 자기도 모르게 속내를 드러내고, 신중한 우아함과 예법으로 키를 잡는 대신 충동에 떠밀려 어색하게 행동하는 그런 속수무책의 처녀들 가운데 하나가 아니었다. 집안 가구와 사교계에 관한 그녀의 재빠른 예측과 궁리가, 자기 엄마와 나누는 대화에서조차 드러난 적이 있었으리라고 상상하는가? 오히려 그녀는 다른 젊은 숙녀가 그런 점잖지 못한 조급함을 들켰다는 말을 들었다면 더없이 어여쁜 놀라움과 못마땅함을 드러냈을 것이고, 사실 그런 일이 있을 수 있다고는 아마 믿지 않았을 것이다. 로저먼드는 결코 어울리지 않는 지식을 내비치는 법이 없었고, 언제나 올바른 정서와 음악, 춤, 그림, 우아한 편지 쓰기, 시구를 가려 적어 두는 개인 앨범, 그리고 완벽한 금발의 사랑스러움이 어우러진 그런 여인이었으니, 그것은 그 시절 운명 지어진 남자에게 거역할 수 없는 여인을 만들어 내는 것이었다. 부디 그녀를 부당하게 나쁘게 여기지 말아 달라. 그녀에게는 사악한 음모도, 더럽거나 돈을 노리는 마음도 없었다. 사실 그녀는 돈이란 다른 사람들이 언제나 마련해 줄 어떤 필요한 것 정도로밖에는 생각하지 않았다. 그녀는 거짓을 꾸며 내는 버릇도 없었고, 설령 그녀의 말이 사실에 대한 직접적인 단서가 되지 못했다 한들, 그것은 본디 그런 의도로 한 말이 아니었으니, 그것은 그녀의 우아한 재주들 가운데 하나로서 남을 즐겁게 하려는 것이었다. 자연은 레먼 부인이 아끼던 제자를 완성하는 데 수많은 기예를 불어넣었으니, 그녀는 (프레드의 의견만 빼면) 모두가 인정하듯 아름다움과 영리함과 상냥함이 어우러진 보기 드문 결합체였다.\n\n리드게이트는 그녀와 함께 있는 것이 점점 더 흐뭇해졌고, 이제는 아무런 거북함도 없이 그들의 눈빛 속에서 영향을 주고받는 즐거운 교환이 있었으며, 그들이 나누는 말에는 제삼자가 다소 맥없게 느낄 만한 그런 넘쳐 나는 의미가 담겨 있었다. 그래도 제삼자를 굳이 배제해야 할 만한 만남이나 귓속말 같은 것은 없었다. 사실 그들은 시시덕거렸을 뿐이고, 리드게이트는 자기들이 그 이상 아무것도 하지 않는다는 믿음 속에서 안심하고 있었다. 한 남자가 사랑을 하면서 동시에 지혜로울 수 없다면, 적어도 시시덕거리면서 동시에 지혜로울 수는 있지 않겠는가? 정말이지 미들마치의 남자들은 페어브러더 씨를 빼면 지독히 따분한 자들이었고, 리드게이트는 상업 정치나 카드놀이에는 관심이 없었으니, 휴식을 위해 무엇을 한단 말인가? 그는 종종 불스트로드 댁에 초대받았다. 그러나 그 집 처녀들은 아직 교실을 갓 벗어났을 뿐이었고, 신앙과 세속, 이 삶의 덧없음과 컷글라스의 탐스러움, 더러운 누더기와 최고급 다마스크천을 동시에 의식하는 불스트로드 부인의 순진한 화해 방식은, 한결같이 엄숙한 그녀 남편의 무게로부터 충분한 위안이 되지 못했다. 온갖 결점에도 불구하고 빈시 씨네 집은 그에 비하면 더 유쾌했다. 게다가 그 집은 로저먼드를 길러 내고 있었으니, 그녀는 반쯤 핀 발그레한 장미처럼 바라보기에 사랑스러웠고, 남자의 세련된 즐거움을 위한 갖가지 재주로 단장되어 있었다.\n\n그러나 그는 빈시 양에게 거둔 성공으로 의료계 외에도 몇몇 적을 만들었다. 어느 날 저녁 그는 다른 손님 몇 명이 와 있을 때 다소 늦게 응접실로 들어섰다. 카드 탁자가 어른들을 끌어가 버려서, 네드 플림데일 씨(미들마치에서 손꼽히는 좋은 신랑감 가운데 하나였으나 손꼽히는 지성은 아니었다)가 로저먼드와 단둘이 마주 앉아 있었다. 그는 그 무렵 현대적 진보를 보여 주던 호화로운 물결무늬 비단 장정의 간행물인 최신호 「키프세이크」를 가져왔는데, 자기가 그것을 그녀와 함께 처음으로 들여다볼 수 있게 된 것을 무척 다행으로 여기며, 윤기 나는 동판화 같은 뺨과 동판화 같은 미소를 띤 신사 숙녀들에 대해 이런저런 말을 늘어놓고, 익살스러운 시구를 일품이라 가리키며 감상적인 이야기들을 흥미롭다고 가리켰다. 로저먼드는 상냥했고, 네드 씨는 자기가 \"구애를 하는\" 수단으로서 예술과 문학에서 최고의 것을, 그러니까 멋진 처녀를 즐겁게 할 바로 그것을 손에 쥐고 있다고 흡족해했다. 그는 또 자신의 외모에 만족할 만한, 겉으로 드러난다기보다는 깊은 이유들도 가지고 있었다. 겉만 보는 사람들에게는 그의 턱이 너무 희미한 데가 있어서, 마치 차츰 도로 빨려 들어가는 듯이 보였다. 그리고 실제로 그것 때문에 그는 새틴 넥타이를 매는 데 다소 애를 먹었으니, 그 시절에는 넥타이를 매는 데 턱이 요긴했던 것이다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 S. 부인 각하가 당신과 좀 닮은 것 같아요.\" 네드 씨가 말했다. 그는 그 매혹적인 초상화가 펼쳐진 채로 책을 들고서, 다소 애틋하게 그것을 바라보았다.\n\n\"등이 아주 넓은걸요. 저 자세로 앉아 있었나 봐요.\" 로저먼드는 무슨 풍자를 의도한 것은 아니었으나, 젊은 플림데일의 손이 어쩌면 저렇게 붉은가 생각하고 리드게이트는 왜 오지 않을까 궁금해하며 그렇게 말했다. 그녀는 그러는 내내 태팅 뜨개질을 계속하고 있었다.\n\n\"그분이 당신만큼 아름답다고 말한 건 아니에요.\" 네드 씨는 그 초상화에서 그 경쟁자에게로 감히 눈길을 옮기며 말했다.\n\n\"제 보기엔 능란한 아첨꾼이신 것 같은데요.\" 로저먼드는 이 젊은 신사를 두 번째로 거절해야 하리라는 것을 확신하며 말했다.\n\n그러나 이제 리드게이트가 들어왔다. 그가 로저먼드의 자리에 이르기 전에 책은 덮였고, 그가 편안한 자신감으로 그녀의 반대편에 자리를 잡자, 젊은 플림데일의 턱은 흐린 날을 향해 떨어지는 기압계처럼 내려앉았다. 로저먼드는 리드게이트가 와 있다는 사실뿐 아니라 그 효과까지도 즐겼다. 그녀는 질투를 불러일으키는 것을 좋아했다.\n\n\"정말 늦게도 오셨네요!\" 두 사람이 악수하는 동안 그녀가 말했다. \"엄마는 조금 전에 당신이 안 오는 줄 알고 단념하셨어요. 프레드는 어떤가요?\"\n\n\"여전합니다. 잘 회복되고 있지만 더디지요. 그를 떠나보내고 싶은데, 이를테면 스톤 코트로요. 그런데 어머님께서 좀 반대하시는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"가엾은 오빠!\" 로저먼드가 어여쁘게 말했다. \"프레드가 무척 달라진 걸 보시게 될 거예요.\" 그녀는 다른 구혼자 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"이번에 병을 앓는 동안 저희는 리드게이트 씨를 수호천사로 여겨 왔답니다.\"\n\n네드 씨는 초조하게 미소 지었고, 그동안 리드게이트는 「키프세이크」를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 펼치고는 짧고 경멸 어린 웃음을 지으며, 인간의 어리석음에 놀라기라도 한 듯 턱을 치켜올렸다.\n\n\"뭘 그렇게 불경스럽게 비웃으시는 거예요?\" 로저먼드가 부드러운 중립의 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"여기 있는 판화하고 글 중에 어느 쪽이 더 어리석은지 모르겠군요.\" 리드게이트는 더없이 확신에 찬 어조로 말하며, 책장을 빠르게 넘겨 순식간에 책 전체를 다 들여다보는 듯하면서, 로저먼드가 보기에 자기의 크고 흰 손을 한껏 돋보이게 내보였다. \"교회에서 나오는 이 신랑 좀 보세요. 엘리자베스 시대 사람들 말마따나 이렇게 '설탕을 입힌 고안물'을 본 적 있으세요? 잡화상이라도 이렇게 능글맞게 웃었을까요? 그런데도 장담컨대 이 이야기는 그를 이 나라 으뜸가는 신사들 가운데 하나로 만들어 놓았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 매정하세요, 무서울 지경이에요.\" 로저먼드는 자기의 재미를 알맞게 누그러뜨리며 말했다. 가엾은 젊은 플림데일은 바로 이 판화를 감탄하며 들여다보고 있던 참이라, 그의 심기가 발끈했다.\n\n\"어쨌든 「키프세이크」에는 유명한 사람들이 많이 글을 쓰는데요.\" 그는 발끈하면서도 머뭇거리는 어조로 말했다. \"이게 어리석다는 말은 처음 듣습니다.\"\n\n\"저는 거꾸로 당신한테 따져서 야만인이라고 몰아세울까 봐요.\" 로저먼드가 미소를 띠고 리드게이트를 바라보며 말했다. \"당신은 블레싱턴 부인이나 L. E. L.에 대해 아무것도 모르실 것 같은데요.\" 로저먼드 자신도 이런 작가들을 즐기지 않는 것은 아니었지만, 쉽사리 감탄을 드러내어 스스로를 드러내지 않았고, 무언가가 리드게이트의 기준에서 최고의 취향이 아니라는 아주 작은 기미에도 민감했다.\n\n\"하지만 월터 스콧 경이라면, 리드게이트 씨도 알고 계시겠지요.\" 젊은 플림데일이 이 우세에 다소 기운을 차리며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 저는 이제 문학이라곤 읽지 않습니다.\" 리드게이트가 책을 덮어 밀쳐 놓으며 말했다. \"소년 시절에 어찌나 많이 읽었던지, 그것으로 평생 우려먹을 것 같군요. 한때는 스콧의 시를 줄줄 외웠지요.\"\n\n\"언제 그만두셨는지 알고 싶네요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"그래야 당신이 모르는 무언가를 제가 안다고 확신할 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 씨라면 그런 건 알 가치도 없다고 하실걸요.\" 네드 씨가 일부러 가시 돋친 말을 했다.\n\n\"천만에요.\" 리드게이트는 아무런 자극도 받지 않은 채, 그러나 로저먼드를 향해 약 오를 만큼 자신만만하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"빈시 양이 제게 알려 줄 수 있다는 사실만으로도 알 가치가 있겠지요.\"\n\n젊은 플림데일은 이내 휘스트 판을 구경하러 갔는데, 리드게이트야말로 자기가 운수 사납게 만나 본 가장 자만심 강하고 불쾌한 작자들 가운데 하나라고 생각하면서였다.\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 경솔하세요!\" 로저먼드가 속으로는 기뻐하며 말했다. \"당신이 기분을 상하게 한 거 아세요?\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 그게 플림데일 씨 책이었나요? 미안하군요. 미처 생각을 못 했습니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 여기 처음 왔을 때 자신에 대해 한 말을 이제 인정하기 시작해야겠어요. 당신은 곰이라서 새들한테 가르침을 받아야 한다고 했잖아요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 마음대로 절 가르칠 수 있는 새가 한 마리 있긴 하지요. 제가 그 새의 말을 기꺼이 듣고 있지 않습니까?\"\n\n로저먼드에게는 자기와 리드게이트가 거의 약혼한 사이나 다름없는 듯이 여겨졌다. 언젠가 두 사람이 약혼하리라는 것은 오래전부터 그녀의 마음속에 자리 잡은 생각이었고, 우리도 알다시피, 필요한 재료가 갖춰져 있으면 생각이란 더 단단한 종류의 존재로 나아가는 법이다. 사실 리드게이트에게는 약혼하지 않은 채로 있겠다는 반대되는 생각이 있었다. 그러나 이것은 한낱 부정적인 것, 그 자체로 줄어들 수 있는 다른 결심들이 드리운 그림자에 불과했다. 정황은 거의 틀림없이 로저먼드의 생각 편에 서 있었으니, 그것은 형성하는 활동을 지녔고 주의 깊은 푸른 눈으로 내다보고 있었던 반면, 리드게이트의 생각은 자기도 모르는 사이에 녹아 버리는 해파리처럼 눈멀고 무심하게 놓여 있었다.\n\n그날 저녁 집으로 돌아갔을 때, 그는 침연 과정이 어떻게 진행되고 있는지 자기의 약병들을 들여다보며 흔들림 없는 흥미를 느꼈고, 여느 때와 다름없이 정확하게 그날의 기록을 적어 내려갔다. 그가 좀처럼 떨쳐 내기 어려웠던 몽상은 로저먼드의 미덕이 아닌 다른 무언가에 대한 이상적인 구상이었고, 원시 조직이 여전히 그의 아리따운 미지의 대상이었다. 더구나 그는 자기와 다른 의료인들 사이에서 자라나는, 반쯤 억눌린 그 반목에도 어떤 흥미를 느끼기 시작하고 있었는데, 그 반목은 불스트로드가 새 병원을 운영하는 방식이 곧 발표되려는 마당이라 한층 분명해질 듯했다. 그리고 피콕의 환자들 가운데 일부가 그를 받아들이지 않는 것이, 다른 곳에서 그가 남긴 인상으로 상쇄될지 모른다는 여러 고무적인 조짐도 있었다. 불과 며칠 뒤, 로윅 길에서 우연히 로저먼드를 따라잡아 말에서 내려, 지나가는 가축 떼로부터 그녀를 완전히 보호할 때까지 그녀의 곁에서 함께 걷고 있었는데, 말을 탄 어느 하인이 그를 불러 세우더니 피콕이 한 번도 진료한 적 없는 어느 지체 높은 집으로 와 달라는 전갈을 전했다. 그리고 그것은 이런 일이 두 번째였다. 그 하인은 제임스 체텀 경의 하인이었고, 그 집은 로윅 저택이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXVIII.\n\n첫 번째 신사. 결혼한 보금자리를 찾아가는 데에 좋지 않은 때란 없으니\n서로에게 기쁨을 가져다주는 일이로다.\n\n두 번째 신사. 옳거니, 참으로 그러하오.\n사랑으로 하나 된 영혼들에게\n\n달력에 흉한 날이란 없으며, 죽음조차도\n둘이 서로를 끌어안은 채\n\n헤어진 삶이란 없으리라 내다보는 동안\n굽이치는 파도처럼 다가온다면, 한낱 달콤함이리라.\n\n캐소본 부부는 신혼여행에서 돌아와 1월 중순에 로윅 영지에 다다랐다. 그들이 문 앞에서 마차에서 내릴 때 가벼운 눈발이 흩날리고 있었고, 아침에 도로시아가 화장실에서 우리가 익히 아는 그 청록색 내실로 건너갔을 때, 그녀는 보리수의 긴 가로수길이 하얀 대지에서 줄기를 일으켜 세우고 거무스름하니 미동도 없는 하늘을 향해 하얀 가지를 펼치고 있는 것을 보았다. 멀리 펼쳐진 평지는 한결같은 흰빛과 낮게 드리운 한결같은 구름 속으로 움츠러들어 있었다. 방 안의 가구마저 전에 보았을 때보다 줄어든 것 같았다. 태피스트리 속의 수사슴은 그 으스스한 청록색 세계 속에서 한층 더 유령처럼 보였고, 책장에 꽂힌 우아한 문학 전집들은 한층 더 옮길 수 없는 책의 모조품처럼 보였다. 장작 위에서 타오르는 마른 떡갈나무 가지의 환한 불은 어울리지 않게 되살아난 생명과 광채처럼 보였으니, 그것은 마치 실리아에게 줄 카메오가 든 붉은 가죽 상자들을 들고 들어서는 도로시아 자신의 모습과도 같았다.\n* 카메오: 마노·조개껍데기 따위에 양각으로 형상을 새긴 장식용 보석.\n\n그녀는 건강한 젊음만이 빛낼 수 있는 그런 빛으로 아침 단장에서 빛나고 있었다. 틀어 올린 머리칼과 담갈색 눈동자에는 보석 같은 광채가 있었고, 입술에는 따뜻하고 붉은 생기가 감돌았다. 모피의 흰빛과는 또 다른 흰빛 위로 그녀의 목은 숨 쉬듯 희게 드러나 있었는데, 그 모피 자체가 그녀의 목을 휘감고 청회색 외투를 따라 흘러내리며, 그녀에게서 모아 온 듯한 부드러움을, 바깥 눈의 수정 같은 순결함에 맞서 그 사랑스러움을 지키는 감성 어린 순진함을 한데 어우러뜨리고 있는 것 같았다. 그녀는 활 모양 창가의 탁자에 카메오 상자들을 내려놓으면서, 무심코 그 위에 손을 얹은 채, 곧장 자신의 눈에 보이는 세계를 이루는 고요하고 하얀 울안을 내다보는 데 몰입했다.\n가슴이 두근거린다고 호소하며 일찍 일어난 캐소본 씨는 서재에서 부목사 터커 씨를 맞이하고 있었다. 곧 실리아가 누이로서뿐 아니라 신부 들러리의 자격으로 찾아올 터였고, 다음 몇 주 동안에는 결혼 인사 방문을 주고받게 될 터였다. 이 모든 것이 신혼의 행복이 주는 흥분에 어울린다고 여겨지는 그 과도기적 삶의 연속이었으며, 꿈꾸는 자가 차츰 의심을 품기 시작하는 꿈처럼, 분주하지만 헛된 느낌을 이어 가는 것이었다. 미리 그토록 위대하게 그려 보았던 결혼 생활의 의무들은 가구며 흰 안개의 벽으로 둘러싸인 풍경과 더불어 움츠러드는 것 같았다. 그녀가 온전한 교감 속에 거닐기를 기대했던 그 맑은 높은 곳은 이제 상상 속에서조차 보기가 어려워졌고, 우월한 존재에게 온전히 기대어 누리리라던 영혼의 감미로운 안식은 불안한 노력으로 흔들리고 어렴풋한 예감으로 인해 놀라움에 사로잡혀 있었다. 남편의 삶을 굳건하게 하고 자기 자신의 삶을 드높여 줄 그 적극적인 아내로서의 헌신, 그 나날은 언제 시작될 것인가? 어쩌면 그녀가 미리 그려 본 모습 그대로는 결코 시작되지 않을지도 모른다. 하지만 어떻게든, 그래도 어떻게든. 엄숙하게 맹세한 이 삶의 결합 속에서, 의무는 어떤 새로운 영감의 형태로 모습을 드러내며 아내의 사랑에 새로운 의미를 부여할 것이었다.\n\n그러는 동안에도 눈이 있었고 거무스름한 안개의 나지막한 둥근 천장이 있었으며, 그 귀부인의 세계가 주는 숨 막히는 압박이 있었으니, 그곳에서는 모든 일이 그녀를 위해 처리되었고 누구도 그녀의 도움을 청하지 않았다. 그곳에서는 다채롭고 의미로 가득 찬 존재와의 연결감을, 그녀의 기운을 빚어 주었을 외부로부터의 요구로서 받는 대신, 내면의 환영으로서 애써 고통스럽게 지켜 가야 했다. \"내가 무엇을 하면 될까요?\" \"무엇이든 그대 좋을 대로 하시오, 여보.\" 이것이 그녀가 아침 공부를 그만두고 그 지긋지긋한 피아노로 어리석은 리듬을 연습하는 일을 그만둔 이래의 짧은 내력이었다. 가치 있고 절실한 일거리로 인도해 주리라던 결혼은 아직 그녀를 귀부인의 답답한 자유로부터 풀어 주지 못했다. 결혼은 그녀의 한가로움을 거리낌 없는 다정함이 주는 되새김의 기쁨으로 채워 주지조차 못했다. 활짝 피어나 힘차게 고동치는 그녀의 젊음은, 차갑고 빛깔 없이 좁아진 풍경과, 움츠러든 가구와, 한 번도 읽히지 않은 책들과, 환한 대낮으로부터 사라져 가는 듯한 창백하고 기괴한 세계 속의 유령 같은 수사슴과 하나가 되어 버린, 그러한 도덕적 감금 속에 서 있었다.\n도로시아가 바깥을 내다본 처음 몇 분 동안 그녀는 음울한 압박감만을 느꼈다. 그러다 날카로운 기억이 떠올랐고, 그녀는 창에서 몸을 돌려 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌았다. 거의 석 달 전에 이 방을 처음 보았을 때 그녀의 마음속에 살아 있던 생각과 희망들은 이제 한낱 기억으로서만 남아 있을 따름이었다. 그녀는 그것들을, 우리가 덧없이 지나가 버린 것들을 평가하듯이 판단했다. 모든 존재가 그녀 자신보다 더 낮은 맥박으로 고동치는 것 같았고, 그녀의 종교적 신앙은 고독한 외침이었으며, 모든 사물이 시들어 그녀에게서 움츠러들며 떨어져 나가는 악몽으로부터 벗어나려는 몸부림이었다. 방 안에서 기억나는 사물 하나하나가 마법이 풀린 채, 불 꺼진 투명체처럼 생기를 잃어 갔는데, 마침내 헤매던 그녀의 시선이 작은 초상화들이 모인 곳에 이르자, 거기서 비로소 새로운 숨결과 의미를 머금은 무언가를 보았다. 그것은 불행한 결혼을 했던 캐소본 씨의 고모 줄리아의 세밀화, 윌 래디슬로의 외할머니의 초상화였다. 도로시아는 그것이 지금 살아 있다고 상상할 수 있을 것 같았다. 그 섬세한 여인의 얼굴은 그러면서도 고집스러운 데가 있었는데, 그것은 해석하기 어려운 특이함이었다. 그녀의 결혼을 불행하다고 여긴 것은 다만 그녀의 친구들뿐이었을까? 아니면 그녀 자신도 그것이 잘못임을 깨닫고, 자비로운 밤의 침묵 속에서 짠 눈물의 쓰디씀을 맛보았을까? 도로시아가 이 세밀화를 처음 본 이래 얼마나 넓은 경험을 지나온 것 같았던가! 그녀는 그것에 대해 새로운 동반자 의식을 느꼈으니, 마치 그것이 그녀의 말에 귀 기울일 수 있고 그녀가 그것을 어떻게 바라보고 있는지 볼 수 있기라도 한 듯했다. 여기 결혼에 대해 어떤 어려움을 겪어 본 여인이 있었다. 아니, 빛깔이 짙어지고, 입술과 턱이 더 커지는 것 같았으며, 머리칼과 눈은 빛을 내뿜는 것 같았고, 얼굴은 남성적이었으며, 시선이 닿는 이로 하여금 자신이 너무도 흥미로운 존재여서 눈꺼풀의 아주 미세한 움직임 하나도 알아채지 못한 채 해석되지 않고 지나칠 리 없음을 일러 주는 그런 충만한 응시로 그녀를 비추었다. 그 생생한 현현은 도로시아에게 기분 좋은 온기처럼 다가왔다. 그녀는 자신이 미소 짓고 있음을 느꼈고, 세밀화에서 몸을 돌려 자리에 앉아, 마치 자기 앞에 있는 사람과 다시 이야기를 나누기라도 하듯 위를 올려다보았다. 하지만 골똘히 생각을 이어 가면서 미소는 사라졌고, 마침내 그녀는 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그렇게 말하다니 잔인했어! 어쩌면 그리도 슬픈가, 그리도 끔찍한가!\"\n\n그녀는 재빨리 일어나 방에서 나가, 남편에게 가서 그를 위해 무언가 할 수 있는 일이 없는지 묻고 싶은 억누를 수 없는 충동에 사로잡혀 복도를 서둘러 걸어갔다. 어쩌면 터커 씨는 떠났고 캐소본 씨가 서재에 홀로 있을지도 몰랐다. 자신의 존재로 인해 남편이 기뻐하는 모습을 볼 수만 있다면 아침의 우울이 모두 사라져 버릴 것 같았다.\n\n하지만 그녀가 검은 떡갈나무 계단 위에 이르렀을 때, 실리아가 올라오고 있었고, 아래쪽에서는 브룩 씨가 캐소본 씨와 환영과 축하의 인사를 주고받고 있었다.\n\n\"도도!\" 실리아가 특유의 조용하고 또박또박한 말투로 말했다. 그러고는 두 팔로 자신을 감싸 안는 언니에게 입을 맞추고는, 더는 말이 없었다. 내 생각엔 도로시아가 삼촌을 맞으러 아래층으로 달려가는 동안, 두 사람 다 남몰래 조금씩 운 것 같았다.\n\n\"어떻게 지냈는지 물을 필요도 없겠구나, 얘야.\" 브룩 씨가 그녀의 이마에 입을 맞추고 나서 말했다. \"로마가 네게 잘 맞은 모양이야. 보아하니, 행복이며, 프레스코화며, 고대 유물이며, 그런 것들 말이다. 그래, 네가 다시 돌아오니 참으로 반갑구나, 그래 이제 예술에 대해 죄다 알게 됐겠지, 응? 그런데 캐소본은 좀 창백해, 내 그에게도 말했다만, 좀 창백해 보여, 알겠지. 휴가 중에 그토록 열심히 공부하는 건 좀 지나친 일이야. 나도 한때 무리한 적이 있었지.\" 브룩 씨는 여전히 도로시아의 손을 잡은 채로, 얼굴은 캐소본 씨 쪽으로 돌렸다. \"지형이며, 유적이며, 신전이며, 실마리를 잡았다 싶었는데, 그게 나를 너무 멀리까지 끌고 갈 거고, 아무 결실도 없으리란 걸 알았지. 그런 일은 한정 없이 파고들 수 있지만, 아무 결실도 없을 수가 있어, 알겠나.\"\n\n도로시아의 눈길 또한 남편의 얼굴을 향해 올라가 있었으니, 한동안 떨어져 있다가 새로이 그를 보는 사람들이 그녀가 알아채지 못한 어떤 징후를 알아챘을지도 모른다는 생각에 다소 불안했던 것이다.\n\n\"놀랄 일은 없단다, 얘야.\" 브룩 씨가 그녀의 표정을 살피며 말했다. \"영국식 쇠고기와 양고기를 조금 먹으면 금세 달라질 게야. 아퀴나스의 초상화를 위해 모델로 앉아 있으려면 창백한 게 다 좋았겠지만 말이다, 알겠지, 네 편지를 마침맞게 받았단다. 그런데 아퀴나스라, 그자는 좀 지나치게 난해하지 않은가, 그렇지? 대체 누가 아퀴나스를 읽는단 말인가?\"\n* 아퀴나스: 13세기의 스콜라 철학자이자 신학자 토마스 아퀴나스.\n\n\"그는 참으로 피상적인 정신에 맞는 저술가는 아니지요.\" 캐소본 씨가 때마침 던져진 이 질문들을 위엄 있는 인내로 받아넘기며 말했다.\n\n\"삼촌, 방으로 커피를 가져다 드리는 게 좋겠지요?\" 도로시아가 거들고 나서며 말했다.\n\n\"그래. 너는 실리아에게 가 보아라. 그 애가 네게 전할 굉장한 소식이 있단다, 알겠지. 다 그 애한테 맡기마.\"\n\n실리아가 언니의 것과 똑같은 외투를 입고 그곳에 앉아, 차분한 만족감으로 카메오를 살펴보고 있고, 대화가 다른 화제로 옮겨 가니, 청록색 내실은 한결 더 밝아 보였다.\n\n\"신혼여행으로 로마에 가는 게 좋다고 생각해, 언니?\" 실리아가 도로시아가 아주 사소한 일에도 익히 보아 온 그 곱고 발그레한 홍조를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"모두에게 다 맞는 건 아니겠지, 가령 너한테는 안 맞을 거야, 얘야.\" 도로시아가 조용히 말했다. 로마로의 신혼여행을 그녀가 어떻게 생각하는지는 그 누구도 결코 알지 못할 것이었다.\n\n\"캐드월래더 부인은 그게 다 말도 안 된다고 해, 결혼한 사람들이 먼 길을 떠난다는 게. 서로한테 진절머리가 나도록 질려 버려서, 집에서처럼 마음 편히 다툴 수도 없다는 거야. 그리고 체텀 부인은 배스로 다녀왔대.\" 실리아의 얼굴빛은 또다시 변하고 또 변했으니,\n\n\"마치 달음박질하는 전령이라도 된 듯이\n마음의 소식을 안고 오갔다.\"\n\n이는 실리아의 평소 홍조가 뜻하는 것보다 더한 무언가를 의미하는 게 틀림없었다.\n\n\"실리아! 무슨 일이 있었니?\" 도로시아가 누이다운 정이 가득한 목소리로 말했다. \"정말 내게 전할 굉장한 소식이라도 있는 거야?\"\n\n\"언니가 떠나 버려서 그랬어, 도도. 그러고 나니 제임스 경이 이야기를 나눌 사람이라곤 나밖에 없었거든.\" 실리아가 두 눈에 다소 장난기를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"알겠다. 내가 바라고 믿어 왔던 그대로구나.\" 도로시아가 두 손으로 누이의 얼굴을 감싸며, 반쯤은 걱정스럽게 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. 실리아의 결혼은 예전보다 더 진지하게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"바로 사흘 전 일이야.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"그리고 체텀 부인은 무척 다정하셔.\"\n\n\"그래서 너는 아주 행복하니?\"\n\n\"응. 우린 아직 결혼하지 않을 거야. 모든 걸 다 준비해야 하거든. 그리고 난 그렇게 빨리 결혼하고 싶지 않아, 약혼해 있는 게 좋은 것 같으니까. 그러고 나서 우린 남은 평생 부부로 지낼 거잖아.\"\n\n\"네가 이보다 더 좋은 사람과 결혼할 수는 없을 거라고 정말 믿어, 키티. 제임스 경은 선량하고 명예로운 분이셔.\" 도로시아가 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"그분은 오두막 짓는 일을 계속하고 계셔, 도도. 오시면 그것에 대해 언니한테 말씀하실 거야. 언니, 그분을 만나면 기쁘겠어?\"\n\n\"물론 기쁘지. 그걸 어떻게 묻니?\"\n\n\"다만 언니가 너무 박식해지지나 않을까 걱정됐을 뿐이야.\" 실리아가 캐소본 씨의 학식을, 머지않아 이웃한 몸뚱이를 흠뻑 적셔 버릴지도 모를 일종의 습기처럼 여기며 말했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIX.\n\n또 다른 사람에게 깃든 천재성이 나를 즐겁게 할 수 없음을 나는 알았다. 나의 불행한 역설들이 그 위안의 샘을 완전히 말려 버린 것이다.—골드스미스.\n\n로윅에 도착한 지 몇 주가 지난 어느 날 아침, 도러시아는—그런데 왜 늘 도러시아인가? 이 결혼에 관해서 그녀의 관점만이 유일하게 가능한 것일까? 나는 우리의 모든 관심과 이해하려는 모든 노력이 곤경에도 불구하고 활짝 피어 보이는 젊은 살결에만 쏟아지는 것에 항의한다. 그들 역시 시들어 갈 것이고, 우리가 등한시하도록 거드는 더 오래되고 더 갉아먹는 슬픔을 알게 될 테니 말이다. 실리아에게 거슬렸던 깜박이는 눈과 흰 사마귀, 그리고 제임스 경에게 도덕적 고통을 안긴 근육의 곡선이 없음에도 불구하고, 캐소본 씨는 자기 안에 강렬한 의식을 지니고 있었고, 우리 모두와 마찬가지로 영적으로 굶주려 있었다. 그는 결혼함에 있어 별난 일을 한 것이 아니었다—사회가 승인하고, 화환과 꽃다발의 계기로 여기는 일을 했을 뿐이었다. 그는 결혼의 뜻을 더 이상 미뤄서는 안 되겠다는 생각이 들었고, 좋은 지위를 가진 남자라면 아내를 맞을 때 활짝 핀 젊은 숙녀를—어릴수록 좋은데, 그래야 더 교육하기 쉽고 순종적이기 때문이다—자신과 동등한 신분에, 종교적 원칙을 지니고, 덕성스러운 기질에, 분별력이 좋은 여성을 기대하고 신중히 골라야 한다고 생각했다. 그런 젊은 숙녀에게라면 그는 후한 재산을 정해 주고, 그녀의 행복을 위한 어떤 마련도 소홀히 하지 않을 터였다. 그 대가로 그는 가정의 즐거움을 누리고, 자신을 복제한 자식을 남길 것이었으니—16세기 소네트 시인들에게는 남자에게 그토록 절실히 요구되는 것으로 보였던 그것을 말이다. 그때 이후로 시대는 변했고, 어떤 소네트 시인도 캐소본 씨에게 자신의 복제본을 남기라고 강요하지 않았다. 게다가 그는 아직 자신의 신화 풀이 열쇠의 사본을 발행하는 데도 성공하지 못한 처지였다. 하지만 그는 늘 결혼으로 자기 의무를 다하리라 마음먹고 있었고, 자신이 빠르게 세월을 뒤로하고 있다는 것, 세상이 점점 흐려지고 외로움을 느낀다는 의식은, 가정의 즐거움마저 세월에 뒤처지기 전에 더 이상 시간을 허비하지 말고 그것을 따라잡아야 할 이유가 되었다.\n\n그리고 도러시아를 보았을 때 그는 자기가 요구한 것보다 더 많은 것을 발견했다고 믿었다. 그녀는 정말로 고용한 비서를 둘 필요가 없을 만큼 자신에게 알맞은 조력자가 되어 줄 듯했는데, 그 도움이란 캐소본 씨가 아직껏 한 번도 쓴 적이 없고 의심스럽게 두려워하던 것이었다. (캐소본 씨는 자신이 강력한 지성을 드러내 보여야 한다는 기대를 받고 있다는 것을 신경질적으로 의식하고 있었다.) 섭리는 그 자비로움 속에서 그에게 필요한 아내를 마련해 주었다. 아내, 즉 자기 성별 특유의 순전히 감상하기만 하고 야심 없는 능력을 지닌 정숙한 젊은 숙녀라면, 남편의 지성이 강력하다고 으레 생각하게 마련이다. 섭리가 브룩 양에게 캐소본 씨를 보내 줌으로써 그녀에게도 동등한 배려를 했는지 어떤지는, 그로서는 좀처럼 떠오를 수 없는 생각이었다. 사회는 한 남자가 매력적인 처녀를 행복하게 해 줄 자기 자질에 대해, 그녀가 자신을 행복하게 해 줄 자질을 헤아리는 만큼 헤아려야 한다는 터무니없는 요구를 결코 하지 않았다. 마치 남자가 자기 아내뿐 아니라 아내의 남편까지 고를 수 있기라도 한 듯이! 또는 마치 자신의 몸으로 후손을 위한 매력을 마련해 줄 의무라도 있는 듯이!— 도러시아가 감격에 차서 그를 받아들였을 때, 그것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다. 그리고 캐소본 씨는 자신의 행복이 이제 막 시작되려 한다고 믿었다.\n\n그는 이전의 삶에서 행복을 별로 맛보지 못했다. 튼튼한 육체 없이 강렬한 기쁨을 알려면 열정적인 영혼을 지녀야 한다. 캐소본 씨는 한 번도 튼튼한 육체를 가져 본 적이 없었고, 그의 영혼은 예민했으나 열정적이지는 않았다. 그것은 자의식에서 떨치고 나와 정열적인 환희로 전율하기에는 너무 무기력했다. 그것은 자기가 부화된 늪지에서 날개를 생각하면서도 결코 날지는 못한 채 계속 파닥거리기만 했다. 그의 경험은 연민에서 움츠러들고 무엇보다 알려지는 것을 두려워하는 가련한 종류의 것이었다. 그것은 동정으로 변모할 만큼의 충분한 무게를 지니지 못한 채, 자기 몰두의, 기껏해야 이기적인 결벽의 작은 물결 속에서 실처럼 떨리는, 자존심 강하고 옹졸한 예민함이었다. 그리고 캐소본 씨는 결벽이 많았다. 그는 엄격한 자기 절제를 할 수 있었고, 규범에 따른 명예로운 사람이 되기로 단호했으며, 어떤 공인된 견해로도 흠잡을 수 없는 사람이고자 했다. 행실에서 이러한 목표들은 달성되었다. 하지만 자신의 신화 풀이 열쇠를 흠잡을 수 없게 만드는 일의 어려움은 그의 마음을 납덩이처럼 짓눌렀다. 그리고 그가 대중을 시험하고 자신의 행진의 자그마한 기념비적 기록을 남겨 둔 소책자들—그가 부르는 이름으로는 '부록 논문들'—은 그 온전한 의미가 인정받기에는 한참 멀었다. 그는 부주교가 그것들을 읽지 않았으리라고 의심했다. 브레이즈노즈의 일류 지성들이 그것들을 진정 어떻게 생각하는지에 대해 고통스러운 의구심에 빠져 있었고, 캐소본 씨의 책상 작은 서랍 속에, 또한 언어 기억의 어두운 골방 속에 잠가 둔 그 깎아내리는 비평문이 옛 지인 카프의 손에서 나왔으리라고 쓰라리게 확신하고 있었다. 이것들은 맞서 싸우기에 무거운 인상들이었고, 모든 지나친 자부심의 결과인 그 우울한 비탄을 가져왔다. 자신의 저술에 대한 흔들리는 신뢰와 더불어 그의 종교적 신앙마저 흔들렸고, 불멸에 대한 기독교적 희망의 위안은 아직 쓰이지 않은 신화 풀이 열쇠의 불멸에 기대고 있는 듯 보였다. 나로서는 그가 무척 안쓰럽다. 우리가 이른바 고도로 교육받았다 하면서도 그것을 누리지 못하는 것은 기껏해야 불편한 운명이다. 삶이라는 이 거대한 장관에 참석하면서도 작고 굶주리고 떨리는 자아로부터 결코 해방되지 못하는 것, 우리가 바라보는 영광에 결코 온전히 사로잡히지 못하는 것, 우리의 의식이 한 사상의 생생함으로, 한 정열의 열기로, 한 행동의 활력으로 환희에 차 변모하지 못한 채, 늘 학구적이되 영감이 없고, 야심차되 소심하며, 결벽적이되 시야가 흐린 채로 있는 것 말이다. 학장이 되거나 심지어 주교가 된다 해도 캐소본 씨의 불편함에는 별 차이가 없으리라고, 나는 우려한다. 분명 어느 고대 그리스인이 일찍이 관찰했듯이, 그 커다란 가면과 확성용 나팔 뒤에는 언제나 평소처럼 빼꼼히 내다보는 우리의 가련하고 작은 눈과, 불안한 통제 아래 어느 정도 놓인 우리의 겁먹은 입술이 있게 마련이다.\n\n사반세기 전에 구획해 둔 이 정신적 영토에, 이렇게 울타리 쳐진 감수성에, 캐소본 씨는 사랑스러운 젊은 신부를 통해 행복을 보태리라 생각했다. 하지만 결혼하기도 전에, 우리가 이미 본 대로, 그는 그 새로운 지복이 자신에게는 지복답지 못하다는 의식 속에서 새로운 우울에 빠진 자신을 발견했다. 마음은 옛날의 더 편안한 습관으로 되돌아가기를 갈망했다. 그리고 그가 가정생활로 깊이 들어갈수록, 그 어떤 다른 만족보다도 자기 의무를 다하고 합당하게 처신한다는 의식이 우위를 점했다. 결혼은, 종교와 학식처럼, 아니, 저술 자체와 마찬가지로, 외적인 요구 사항이 될 운명이었고, 에드워드 캐소본은 모든 요구 사항을 흠잡을 데 없이 이행하기로 작정한 사람이었다. 결혼 전 자신의 의도대로 도러시아를 서재 일에 끌어들이는 것조차 그는 늘 미루고 싶은 유혹을 느끼는 노력이었으니, 그녀가 간청하며 고집하지 않았더라면 결코 시작되지 못했을지도 모른다. 하지만 그녀는 이른 시각에 서재에 자리를 잡고서, 소리 내어 읽거나 베껴 쓰는 일을 맡는 것을 당연한 일로 만드는 데 성공했다. 캐소본 씨가 당장의 의도 하나를 채택했기 때문에 일을 규정하기는 더 수월했다. 새로운 부록 논문이 나올 예정이었는데, 워버턴의 어떤 주장들을 바로잡을 수 있는, 이집트 신비 의식에 관해 최근 추적된 몇몇 단서들을 다룬 작은 단행본이었다. 여기서도 참고 문헌은 방대했으나, 아주 끝이 없을 정도는 아니었다. 그리고 브레이즈노즈와 그보다 덜 두려운 후세 사람들이 면밀히 살필 형태 그대로 실제 문장들이 쓰일 참이었다. 이러한 자잘한 기념비적 산물들은 늘 캐소본 씨를 흥분시켰다. 인용문의 간섭이나, 머릿속에서 서로 부딪쳐 울리는 변증법적 어구들의 경합 때문에 소화가 어려워지곤 했다. 그리고 처음부터 라틴어 헌사가 들어갈 예정이었는데, 그것이 카프에게 바쳐지지 않으리라는 점만 빼고는 모든 것이 불확실했다. 캐소본 씨에게는, 일찍이 카프에게 헌사를 바치면서 그 동물계의 일원을 가리켜 '어느 시대에도 사라지지 않을 사람들' 가운데 헤아렸던 일이 독을 머금은 후회로 남아 있었으니, 그것은 다음 시대에 헌사를 바친 자를 영락없이 조롱거리로 만들 실수였고, 어쩌면 현 시대에 파이크와 텐치가 키득거릴 거리가 될 수도 있었다.\n* '어느 시대에도 사라지지 않을 사람들'(viros nullo ævo perituros): \"결코 멸하지 않을 인물들\"이라는 라틴어 찬사. 카프(Carp)는 잉어, 파이크(Pike)는 강꼬치고기, 텐치(Tench)는 잉어과 민물고기로, 캐소본의 학계 지인들 이름이 모두 물고기여서 '동물계의 일원'이라는 빈정거림이 성립한다.\n\n이렇듯 캐소본 씨는 가장 바쁜 시기 가운데 하나를 보내고 있었는데, 조금 전에 내가 말하기 시작한 대로, 도러시아는 그가 홀로 아침을 든 서재로 일찍 그를 찾아왔다. 이 무렵 실리아는 두 번째로 로윅을 방문 중이었는데, 결혼 전으로는 아마 마지막 방문일 터였고, 응접실에서 제임스 경을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n도러시아는 남편의 기분을 나타내는 신호를 읽는 법을 익혀 두었기에, 지난 한 시간 사이 그곳의 아침 공기가 한층 더 안개 낀 듯 무거워졌음을 알아챘다. 그녀가 말없이 자기 책상으로 가려는데 그가 말했는데, 그것은 그가 불쾌한 의무를 이행하고 있음을 암시하는 그 멀찍한 어조였다—\n\n\"도러시아, 여기 당신에게 온 편지가 있소. 내게 온 편지 안에 동봉되어 있었소.\"\n\n두 장짜리 편지였고, 그녀는 곧바로 서명을 보았다.\n\n\"래디슬로 씨로군요! 그분이 내게 무슨 할 말이 있을까요?\" 그녀는 반가운 놀라움의 어조로 외쳤다. \"하지만,\" 그녀는 캐소본 씨를 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"그분이 당신께 무슨 일로 편지를 보냈는지는 짐작할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"원한다면 그 편지를 읽어 봐도 좋소.\" 캐소본 씨가 펜으로 그것을 엄하게 가리키면서, 그녀를 보지는 않은 채 말했다. \"하지만 미리 말해 두는 편이 낫겠소. 그 편지에 담긴, 이곳을 방문하겠다는 제안은 내가 거절해야겠소. 지금껏 불가피했던 그런 방해들로부터, 특히 종잡을 수 없는 활기로 그 존재만으로도 피로를 안기는 손님들로부터 완전히 자유로운 기간을 갖고 싶다는 내 바람이 양해되기를 바라오.\"\n\n로마에서의 그 작은 폭발 이후로 도러시아와 남편 사이에 기질의 충돌은 없었는데, 그 일은 그녀의 마음에 워낙 강한 자취를 남겨서 그 이후로는 감정을 터뜨려 그 결과를 떠안기보다는 감정을 억누르는 편이 더 쉬워졌던 것이다. 하지만 그녀가 남편에게 불쾌할 수도 있는 방문을 원할 거라는 이 심술궂은 넘겨짚음, 그녀 쪽의 이기적인 불평에 맞선 이 쓸데없는 자기 방어는, 분개하고 난 뒤에야 곱씹을 수 있을 만큼 너무 날카로운 가시였다. 도러시아는 자기가 존 밀턴에게라면 참을성을 보일 수 있었으리라 생각해 왔지만, 그가 이런 식으로 처신하는 모습은 한 번도 상상해 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 한순간 캐소본 씨는 어리석을 만큼 분별이 없고 가증스러울 만큼 부당해 보였다. 머지않아 그녀 안의 숱한 폭풍을 다스리게 될 그 \"갓 태어난 아기\"인 연민은, 이번만큼은 \"돌풍을 타고 넘지\" 않았다. 그를 떨게 만든 어조로 내뱉은 첫마디에서, 그녀는 캐소본 씨를 깜짝 놀라게 하여 그를 돌아보게 만들었고, 번득이는 그녀의 두 눈과 마주치게 했다.\n* \"갓 태어난 아기\"와 \"돌풍을 타고 넘지\": 셰익스피어 「맥베스」 1막 7장에서 연민(pity)을 \"갓 태어난 발가벗은 아기가 돌풍을 타고 넘는\" 모습에 비유한 구절을 인유한 것이다.\n\n\"어째서 당신을 짜증나게 할 일을 제가 바라리라고 단정하시나요? 마치 제가 당신이 맞서 싸워야 할 무엇이라도 되는 양 말씀하시는군요. 적어도 제가 당신과 별개로 제 즐거움만 챙기는 듯 보일 때까지는 기다려 주세요.\"\n\n\"도러시아, 당신은 성급하구려.\" 캐소본 씨가 신경질적으로 대답했다.\n\n확실히 이 여자는 결혼이라는 만만찮은 차원에 오르기에는 너무 젊었다—그녀가 창백하고 특색 없으며 모든 것을 당연하게 받아들이는 사람이 아니라면 말이다.\n\n\"제 감정에 대해 잘못된 억측을 하시며 먼저 성급하셨던 건 당신이라고 생각해요.\" 도러시아가 똑같은 어조로 말했다. 불길은 아직 가라앉지 않았고, 그녀는 남편이 자기에게 사과하지 않는 것이 비열하다고 생각했다.\n\n\"괜찮다면 이 문제에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다, 도러시아. 나는 이런 종류의 논쟁을 할 여유도 기운도 없소.\"\n\n여기서 캐소본 씨는 펜을 잉크에 적셔 글쓰기로 돌아가려는 듯한 시늉을 했지만, 그의 손이 어찌나 떨리는지 마치 알 수 없는 문자로 글이 쓰이는 것만 같았다. 분노를 돌려세우면서도 그저 그것을 방 저편 끝으로 보내 버릴 뿐인 대답들이 있는 법인데, 정의가 온전히 자기편에 있다고 느낄 때 토론이 냉랭하게 회피당하는 것은, 철학에서보다 결혼에서 한층 더 사람을 격분케 한다.\n\n도러시아는 래디슬로의 편지 두 장을 읽지 않은 채 남편의 책상 위에 남겨 두고 자기 자리로 갔는데, 그녀 안의 경멸과 분개는 그 편지들을 읽기를 거부했으니, 마치 우리가 비열한 탐욕을 품었다고 의심받은 듯한 어떤 쓰레기를 멀리 내던져 버리듯이 그러했다. 그녀는 이 편지들을 둘러싼 남편의 언짢은 기분의 미묘한 근원을 조금도 알아채지 못했다. 다만 그 편지들이 그로 하여금 자신을 불쾌하게 만들도록 했다는 것만 알았다. 그녀는 곧바로 일을 시작했고, 그녀의 손은 떨리지 않았다. 오히려 전날 받은 인용문들을 옮겨 적으면서 그녀는 자기가 글자를 아름답게 만들어 가고 있다고 느꼈고, 베끼고 있는, 그리고 이제 막 이해하기 시작한 라틴어의 구조가 평소보다 더 또렷이 보이는 것 같았다. 그녀의 분개 속에는 우월감이 깃들어 있었지만, 그것은 당장은 단호한 필치로 흘러나갔을 뿐, 한때 \"상냥한 대천사\"였던 이를 형편없는 인간이라 선언하는 또렷한 내면의 목소리로 응축되지는 않았다.\n* \"상냥한 대천사\"(affable archangel): 밀턴 「실낙원」에서 아담에게 친절히 가르침을 베푸는 대천사 라파엘을 가리키는 표현으로, 도러시아가 결혼 전 캐소본에게 품었던 이상을 빗댄다.\n\n이렇게 겉으로 고요한 상태가 반 시간쯤 이어졌고, 도러시아가 자기 책상에서 눈을 떼지 않고 있던 차에, 그녀는 책 한 권이 바닥에 쾅 하고 떨어지는 소리를 들었다. 얼른 돌아보니 캐소본 씨가 서재 사다리 위에서 무슨 육체적 고통에 시달리는 듯 앞으로 매달려 있는 것이 보였다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 순식간에 그에게로 달려갔다. 그는 분명 숨을 쉬느라 몹시 애를 먹고 있었다. 의자 위로 뛰어올라 그의 팔꿈치 가까이로 다가간 그녀는 온 영혼이 다정한 놀라움으로 녹아든 채 말했다—\n\n\"제게 기대실 수 있겠어요, 여보?\"\n\n그는 그녀에게는 끝없이 길게 느껴지는 이삼 분 동안 가만히 있었는데, 말도 못 하고 움직이지도 못한 채 숨을 헐떡거렸다. 마침내 그가 세 계단을 내려와, 도러시아가 사다리 발치 가까이 끌어다 놓은 큰 의자에 뒤로 쓰러지듯 앉았을 때, 그는 더는 헐떡이지 않았으나 무력하게 막 기절할 것처럼 보였다. 도러시아가 사납게 벨을 울리자 이내 캐소본 씨는 부축을 받아 긴 의자로 옮겨졌다. 그는 기절하지 않고 점차 되살아나고 있었는데, 바로 그때 캐소본 씨가 \"서재에서 발작을 일으켰다\"는 소식을 현관에서 전해 들은 제임스 체텀 경이 들어왔다.\n\n\"맙소사! 이건 충분히 예상할 수 있었던 일이잖아.\" 그것이 그의 즉각적인 생각이었다. 그의 예언자적 영혼이 좀 더 구체적으로 짚어 보라고 다그쳐졌다면, 그가 떠올렸을 명확한 표현은 \"발작\"이었으리라 여겨졌다. 그는 소식을 전한 집사에게 의사를 부르러 보냈는지 물었다. 집사는 주인이 전에 의사를 찾는 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 하지만 의사를 부르러 보내는 것이 옳지 않겠는가?\n\n그러나 제임스 경이 서재로 들어섰을 때, 캐소본 씨는 평소의 예의를 나타내는 몇 가지 몸짓을 할 수 있었고, 처음의 공포에서 벗어난 반동으로 그의 곁에 무릎 꿇고 흐느끼던 도러시아는 이제 몸을 일으켜 직접, 누군가 말을 타고 의사를 부르러 가야 한다고 제안했다.\n\n\"리드게이트를 부르시기를 권하오.\" 제임스 경이 말했다. \"내 어머니가 그를 모셔 왔는데, 보기 드물게 영리하다고 보시더군요. 어머니는 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 의사들을 탐탁잖게 여기셨거든요.\"\n\n도러시아가 남편에게 의견을 구하자 그는 말없이 동의의 표시를 했다. 그리하여 리드게이트 씨를 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 그는 놀랄 만큼 금세 도착했다. 심부름꾼은 제임스 체텀 경의 하인으로 리드게이트 씨를 알고 있었는데, 마침 로윅 가도를 따라 말을 끌면서 빈시 양에게 팔을 내어 주고 있던 그를 만났던 것이다.\n\n응접실에 있던 실리아는 제임스 경이 알려 줄 때까지 그 소동을 전혀 모르고 있었다. 도러시아의 설명을 듣고 나서 그는 더 이상 그 병을 발작으로 여기지는 않았으나, 그래도 \"그런 종류의\" 무언가라고 여겼다.\n\n\"가엾은 도도—정말 끔찍해라!\" 실리아가, 자신의 더없는 행복이 허락하는 만큼 슬퍼하며 말했다. 그녀의 작은 두 손은 마주 잡혀 있었고, 봉오리가 넉넉한 꽃받침에 감싸이듯 제임스 경의 손에 감싸여 있었다. \"캐소본 씨가 편찮으시다니 정말 충격적이에요. 하지만 저는 그분을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없어요. 게다가 그분은 도러시아를 반만큼도 충분히 아끼지 않는 것 같아요. 마땅히 그래야 하는데도요. 그분과 결혼할 사람은 분명 언니 말고는 아무도 없었을 테니까요—그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\"\n\n\"나는 늘 그것이 처형의 끔찍한 희생이라고 생각했소.\" 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요. 하지만 가엾은 도도는 한 번도 남들 하는 대로 한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"처형은 고귀한 사람이오.\" 충직한 마음의 제임스 경이 말했다. 그는 방금 이런 종류의 인상을 새로이 받은 참이었는데, 도러시아가 다정한 팔을 남편의 목 아래로 뻗으며 형언할 수 없는 슬픔으로 그를 바라보는 모습을 보았기 때문이다. 그는 그 슬픔 속에 얼마나 많은 뉘우침이 깃들어 있는지는 알지 못했다.\n\n\"맞아요.\" 실리아가 말했는데, 제임스 경이 그렇게 말하는 거야 좋은 일이지만 그라면 도도와 함께 살면서 편안하지 못했을 거라고 생각하면서였다. \"제가 언니에게 가 볼까요? 제가 언니를 도울 수 있을까요, 그렇게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"리드게이트가 오기 전에 가서 처형을 잠깐 보고 오는 것이 좋을 것 같소.\" 제임스 경이 너그럽게 말했다. \"다만 오래 머물지는 마시오.\"\n\n실리아가 간 동안 그는 도러시아의 약혼에 대해 처음에 자기가 느꼈던 바를 떠올리며 이리저리 거닐었고, 브룩 씨의 무관심에 대한 혐오가 되살아나는 것을 느꼈다. 만약 캐드월래더가—만약 다른 모든 사람이 그 일을 자신, 곧 제임스 경이 보았던 대로 보았더라면, 그 결혼은 막을 수 있었을지도 모른다. 어린 처녀가 그런 식으로, 그녀를 구하려는 어떤 노력도 없이 눈먼 채 자기 운명을 결정하도록 내버려 둔 것은 사악한 일이었다. 제임스 경은 자기 자신을 위해서는 이미 오래전에 아무런 미련도 갖지 않게 되었다. 그의 마음은 실리아와의 약혼으로 흡족했다. 하지만 그는 기사도적인 천성을 지녔으니(여성에 대한 사심 없는 헌신이야말로 옛 기사도의 이상적인 영광 가운데 하나가 아니었던가?), 외면당한 그의 사랑은 쓰라림으로 변하지 않았다. 그 사랑의 죽음은 달콤한 향기를 빚어냈으니—도러시아에게 신성하게 어리는 효과로 들러붙어 떠도는 추억들이었다. 그는 그녀의 오라비 같은 벗으로 남아, 너그러운 믿음으로 그녀의 행동을 해석할 수 있었다.\n\n카소본 씨는 첫 번째 발작만큼 심한 두 번째 발작을 겪지 않았고, 며칠 만에 평소의 상태를 회복하기 시작했다. 그러나 리드게이트는 이 병을 상당히 주의 깊게 살펴야 한다고 여기는 것 같았다. 그는 청진기를 사용했을 뿐만 아니라(당시만 해도 진료에서 청진기가 당연한 일이 아니었다) 환자 곁에 조용히 앉아 그를 지켜보았다. 자신에 관한 카소본 씨의 질문에 그는, 병의 근원이 지적인 사람들의 흔한 잘못, 곧 너무 열렬하고 단조로운 정신 집중에 있다고 대답했다. 처방은 적당한 양의 일에 만족하고 다양한 휴식을 구하는 것이었다. 한번은 그 자리에 앉아 있던 브룩 씨가, 카소본 씨도 캐드월래더처럼 낚시를 하러 가고, 선반 작업실을 두어 장난감이며 식탁 다리며 그런 것들을 만들어 보면 어떻겠느냐고 권했다.\n\n\"요컨대 제 두 번째 어린 시절이 다가오기를 미리 맞이하라고 권하시는 거군요.\" 가엾은 카소본 씨가 다소 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"그런 것들은,\" 그는 리드게이트를 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"교화소의 죄수들이 노끈을 푸는 것이나 마찬가지로 제게 휴식이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"솔직히,\" 리드게이트가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"오락이란 다소 만족스럽지 못한 처방입니다. 사람들에게 기운을 내라고 말하는 것과 비슷하지요. 어쩌면 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠군요. 계속 일하시기보다는 가벼운 권태를 견디는 데 순응하셔야 한다고요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그렇고말고요.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"저녁이면 도러시아에게 백개먼을 같이 하자고 하시오. 그리고 셔틀콕도 말이지, 나는 낮 시간에 셔틀콕만큼 좋은 놀이를 모른다오. 한창 유행하던 시절이 기억나는군. 하기야 자네 눈이 그걸 견디지 못할지도 모르지, 카소본. 하지만 알다시피 좀 느긋해져야 하네. 아니면 뭔가 가벼운 공부를 해 보면 어떻겠나. 패류학(貝類學) 같은 것 말이지. 나는 늘 그게 가벼운 공부라고 생각해 왔다네. 아니면 도러시아한테 가벼운 책을 읽어 달라고 하든가, 스몰릿 말일세, '로더릭 랜덤'이나 '험프리 클링커' 같은 것. 좀 야하긴 하지만, 알다시피 이제 결혼했으니 무엇이든 읽어도 되지 않겠나. 그게 나를 유난히 웃겼던 게 기억나는군. 마부 바지에 관한 우스운 대목이 있지. 요즘은 그런 유머가 없어. 나는 그런 것들을 다 거쳐 왔지만, 자네에게는 좀 새로울지도 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"엉겅퀴를 먹는 것만큼이나 새롭겠지요\"가 카소본 씨의 심정을 대변할 만한 대답이었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그저 아내의 삼촌에 대한 마땅한 예의를 갖추어 체념한 듯 고개를 숙이고는, 그분이 언급한 작품들이 의심할 바 없이 \"어떤 부류의 사람들에게는 위안거리가 되어 왔겠지요\"라고만 말했다.\n\n\"보시다시피,\" 그 유능한 치안 판사는 문밖으로 나서자 리드게이트에게 말했다. \"카소본은 좀 편협한 데가 있었어요. 그래서 그 특유의 일을 못 하게 막으면 어찌할 바를 모르고 막막해하지요. 그 일이라는 게 참으로 심오한 것이긴 한 모양입니다만, 알다시피 연구 분야의 일 말이오. 나라면 결코 거기에 그렇게 빠지지 않았을 텐데. 나는 늘 다재다능했거든요. 하지만 성직자란 좀 옴짝달싹 못 하지요. 그를 주교로라도 만들어 준다면 좋으련만! 그는 필을 위해 아주 훌륭한 소책자를 써냈지요. 그러면 활동도 더 많아지고, 드러날 자리도 더 생기고, 살도 좀 붙을 텐데. 어쨌든 카소본 부인과 이야기해 보시길 권하오. 내 조카는 뭐든 해낼 만큼 영리한 아이라오. 남편에게는 활기와 기분 전환이 필요하다고 일러 주시오. 재미있는 방법을 한번 써 보라고 하시오.\"\n\n브룩 씨의 조언이 없었더라도 리드게이트는 도러시아에게 말하기로 작정한 터였다. 로윅의 생활을 어떻게 활기차게 할 수 있을지에 관해 삼촌이 유쾌한 제안들을 늘어놓는 동안 그녀는 그 자리에 없었지만, 평소에는 늘 남편 곁에 있었다. 남편의 정신이나 건강에 관계된 것이라면 무엇에든 그녀의 얼굴과 목소리에 드러나는 강렬한 불안의 꾸밈없는 기색은, 리드게이트가 지켜보고 싶어지는 하나의 드라마를 이루었다. 그는 남편의 앞날에 대한 진실을 그녀에게 말하는 것은 그저 옳은 일을 하는 것뿐이라고 스스로에게 말했지만, 한편으로 그녀와 은밀하게 이야기를 나누는 것이 흥미로우리라고도 분명히 생각했다. 의사란 심리적 관찰을 즐기는 법이고, 때로 그런 연구에 몰두하다 보면 삶과 죽음이 손쉽게 무위로 돌려 버리는 중대한 예언에 너무 쉽게 빠져들곤 한다. 리드게이트는 그런 근거 없는 예측을 두고 자주 빈정거려 왔던 터라, 이제는 신중하려고 마음먹었다.\n\n그는 카소본 부인을 찾았으나 그녀가 산책을 나갔다는 말에 막 돌아가려던 참에, 도러시아와 실리아가 나타났다. 두 사람 다 삼월의 바람과 씨름하느라 발갛게 상기되어 있었다. 리드게이트가 그녀와 단둘이 이야기하기를 청하자, 도러시아는 마침 가장 가까이 있던 서재 문을 열었다. 그 순간 그녀의 머릿속에는 그가 카소본 씨에 관해 무슨 말을 하려는 것일까 하는 생각뿐이었다. 남편이 병을 앓은 이래 그녀가 이 방에 들어온 것은 처음이었고, 하인은 덧창을 열지 않은 채로 두기로 한 모양이었다. 그래도 창문 위쪽의 좁은 유리창으로 들어오는 빛이 책을 읽을 만큼은 되었다.\n\n\"이 어슴푸레한 빛을 개의치 않으시겠지요.\" 도러시아가 방 한가운데 서서 말했다. \"선생님이 책을 금하신 뒤로는 서재를 쓸 일이 없었어요. 하지만 곧 카소본 씨가 다시 이곳에 오실 수 있겠지요. 차도가 있지 않나요?\"\n\n\"네, 처음 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 빠르게 나아지고 계십니다. 사실 이미 평소의 건강 상태에 거의 가까워지셨습니다.\"\n\n\"병이 재발할까 봐 염려하시는 건 아니지요?\" 도러시아가 말했다. 그녀의 예민한 귀는 리드게이트의 어조에서 어떤 의미를 알아챘던 것이다.\n\n\"이런 경우는 단정해 말씀드리기가 유난히 어렵습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"제가 확신할 수 있는 유일한 점은, 카소본 씨가 어떤 식으로든 신경의 기력을 무리하게 쓰지 않도록 매우 세심하게 살피는 것이 바람직하다는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"제발 아주 분명하게 말씀해 주세요.\" 도러시아가 애원하는 어조로 말했다. \"제가 알지 못하는 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 것, 그리고 만약 제가 그것을 알았더라면 다르게 행동했을 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 것을 견딜 수가 없어요.\" 그 말은 외침처럼 터져 나왔다. 그것이 멀지 않은 곳에 자리한 어떤 마음의 체험에서 우러난 목소리임이 분명했다.\n\n\"앉으세요.\" 그녀는 가장 가까운 의자에 앉으며 덧붙였다. 운명에 관한 중대한 문제가 걸려 있을 때 본능적으로 격식을 벗어 던지듯, 그녀는 보닛과 장갑을 벗어 던졌다.\n\n\"지금 하신 말씀이 제 생각이 옳았음을 입증해 줍니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"의사로서 그런 후회를 되도록 막아 드리는 것이 제 역할이라고 생각합니다. 하지만 카소본 씨의 경우는 바로 그 결과를 단정하기가 가장 어려운 부류에 속한다는 점을 유념해 주시기 바랍니다. 어쩌면 지금까지보다 그리 나빠지지 않은 건강으로 십오 년, 혹은 그 이상을 사실 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n도러시아는 몹시 창백해졌고, 리드게이트가 말을 멈추자 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"아주 조심한다면 그렇다는 말씀이군요.\"\n\n\"네, 온갖 종류의 정신적 동요를 막고, 지나친 몰두를 막는다면요.\"\n\n\"일을 포기해야 한다면 그분은 비참해하실 거예요.\" 도러시아가 그 비참함을 재빨리 내다보며 말했다.\n\n\"저도 알고 있습니다. 유일한 방법은 직접적이든 간접적이든 모든 수단을 다해 그분의 일을 절제하게 하고 다양하게 하는 것입니다. 상황이 순조롭게 맞아떨어진다면, 앞서 말씀드린 대로 지난번 발작의 원인이라고 제가 여기는 그 심장의 질환으로부터 당장의 위험은 없습니다. 한편으로는 병이 더 빠르게 진행될 가능성도 있습니다. 때로 갑작스러운 죽음이 찾아오는 그런 부류의 경우이지요. 그런 결말에 영향을 받을 만한 것은 무엇 하나 소홀히 해서는 안 됩니다.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 도러시아는 대리석으로 변해 버린 듯 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그녀 안의 생명은 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그녀의 마음은 그 짧은 시간 동안 일찍이 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 폭넓은 장면과 동기들을 휩쓸어 지나갔다.\n\n\"제발 저를 도와주세요.\" 그녀가 마침내, 아까와 같은 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"제가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"외국 여행은 어떻게 생각하십니까? 최근에 로마에 다녀오셨지요?\"\n\n이 방책을 도무지 가망 없게 만드는 기억들이 새로운 물결처럼 밀려와, 도러시아를 그 창백한 부동의 상태에서 흔들어 깨웠다.\n\n\"아, 그건 안 돼요. 그건 무엇보다 더 나쁠 거예요.\" 그녀가 한층 어린아이 같은 낙담에 잠겨 말하는 동안 눈물이 흘러내렸다. \"그분이 즐기지 못하는 것은 아무 소용이 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"이 고통을 덜어 드릴 수 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\" 리드게이트가 깊이 마음이 움직인 채 말했다. 그러면서도 그녀의 결혼이 의아하게 여겨졌다. 도러시아 같은 여인은 그가 지닌 관념의 틀에 들어 있지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"말씀해 주신 것은 옳은 일이었어요. 진실을 말씀해 주셔서 감사해요.\"\n\n\"카소본 씨 본인에게는 무엇도 일러 드리지 않을 것임을 이해해 주시기 바랍니다. 그분은 무리하게 일해서는 안 되며 몇 가지 규칙을 지켜야 한다는 것 외에는 더 아무것도 모르시는 편이 바람직하다고 생각합니다. 어떤 종류의 불안이든 그분에게는 바로 가장 좋지 않은 상태일 테니까요.\"\n\n리드게이트가 일어서자 도러시아도 무심결에 동시에 일어나, 숨이 막힌다는 듯 망토를 풀어 벗어 던졌다. 그가 인사를 하고 자리를 뜨려는데, 만약 그녀가 혼자였더라면 기도로 바뀌었을 어떤 충동이 그녀로 하여금 흐느낌 섞인 목소리로 이렇게 말하게 했다.\n\n\"아, 선생님은 현명한 분이시지요? 삶과 죽음에 관해 다 아시잖아요. 제게 조언해 주세요. 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 생각해 주세요. 그분은 평생을 애써 일하며 앞날을 바라보아 왔어요. 그분은 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 마음을 두지 않으세요. 그리고 저도 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 마음을 두지 않아요.\"\n\n그 후로 오랜 세월 동안 리드게이트는 이 무의식적인 호소가, 영혼에서 영혼으로 건너간 이 외침이 자기 안에 남긴 인상을 기억했다. 같은 뒤엉킨 매질 속에서, 같은 어수선하고 간헐적으로만 밝혀지는 삶 속에서 비슷한 천성을 지닌 채 함께 움직이고 있다는 의식 외에 다른 의식이라곤 없는 외침이었다. 그러나 지금 그가 내일 다시 카소본 씨를 보러 오겠다는 말 외에 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n그가 떠나자 도러시아의 눈물이 쏟아져 나왔고, 그녀의 숨 막히는 답답함을 덜어 주었다. 이윽고 그녀는 자신의 괴로움을 남편에게 들켜서는 안 된다는 것을 떠올리며 눈물을 닦았다. 그리고 카소본 씨가 이제 언제든 들어오고 싶어 할지도 모르니 하인에게 평소처럼 방을 정돈하라고 일러야겠다고 생각하며 방 안을 둘러보았다. 그의 책상 위에는 병으로 쓰러지던 날 아침 이래 손대지 않은 채로 놓여 있는 편지들이 있었고, 도러시아가 잘 기억하고 있듯이 그 가운데에는 젊은 래디슬로의 편지들이 있었으며, 그녀 앞으로 온 편지는 아직 봉투조차 뜯지 않은 채였다. 이 편지들과 얽힌 일들은, 자신의 분노가 일으킨 동요가 어느 정도 부추겼을지도 모른다고 그녀가 느끼는 그 갑작스러운 발작 때문에 한층 더 고통스러워졌다. 그것들이 다시 그녀 앞에 들이밀어질 때 읽어도 충분하리라 여겨, 그녀는 서재에서 그것들을 가져올 마음이 도무지 들지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그 편지들을 남편의 눈에 띄지 않게 치워야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그것들에 관한 그의 언짢음이 무엇에서 비롯되었든, 그가 다시는 언짢아지지 않게 해야 했다. 그래서 그녀는 그 불쾌한 방문을 막기 위해 편지를 써야 할지 어떨지 확인하려고, 먼저 그에게 온 편지를 훑어보았다.\n\n윌은 로마에서 편지를 썼는데, 카소본 씨에게 진 신세가 너무도 깊어서 어떤 감사의 말도 무례하게 보이지 않을 도리가 없다는 말로 시작했다. 만약 그가 감사할 줄 모른다면, 너그러운 벗을 만난 자 가운데 가장 비루한 정신의 악당일 것이 분명했다. 장황한 감사의 말을 늘어놓는 것은 \"나는 정직합니다\"라고 말하는 것과 같았다. 그러나 윌은, 자신의 결점들, 카소본 씨 자신도 자주 지적했던 그 결점들을 고치려면 더 분발하는 처지가 필요한데, 지금까지는 친척의 너그러움이 그것을 불가피하게 만들지 않았음을 깨닫게 되었다. 그는, 만약 보답이 가능하다면, 신세를 진 그 교육의 효과를 보여 주고, 앞으로는 다른 이들이 더 마땅한 권리를 가졌을지 모를 자금을 자기에게 돌리지 않도록 함으로써 최선의 보답을 하리라 믿었다. 그는 두뇌만이 유일한 밑천인 다른 많은 젊은이들이 그래야 하듯이, 자신의 운을 시험하러 영국으로 갈 참이었다. 그의 벗 나우만이 그에게 '논쟁'을, 곧 카소본 씨를 위해 그린 그림을 맡아 달라고 부탁했기에, 그분과 카소본 부인의 허락을 받아 윌이 직접 그것을 로윅으로 가져갈 것이었다. 보름 안에 파리의 유치(留置) 우편으로 편지를 보내면, 필요할 경우 그가 부적절한 시기에 도착하는 것을 막을 수 있을 터였다. 그는 카소본 부인 앞으로 보내는 편지를 동봉했는데, 그 안에서 로마에서 그녀와 시작했던 예술에 관한 논의를 이어 가고 있었다.\n\n자신에게 온 편지를 펴 보고 도러시아는, 그것이 그녀의 광적인 동정심과, 사물을 있는 그대로 받아들이며 누리는 굳건하고 중립적인 즐거움의 결여에 대해 그가 항변하던 것을 활기차게 이어 가고 있음을 알았다. 그것은 그의 젊은 발랄함이 쏟아져 나온 것이어서, 지금 당장은 도무지 읽을 수가 없었다. 그녀는 곧바로 다른 편지에 대해 어떻게 해야 할지 궁리해야 했다. 어쩌면 윌이 로윅에 오는 것을 막을 시간이 아직 있을지도 몰랐다. 도러시아는 결국 아직 집에 있던 삼촌에게 그 편지를 건네며, 카소본 씨가 병을 앓았고 건강 때문에 어떤 손님도 맞을 수 없게 되었다는 사실을 윌에게 알려 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n편지 쓰는 일이라면 브룩 씨만큼 기꺼운 사람도 없었다. 그의 유일한 어려움은 짧게 쓰는 것이었으니, 이 경우 그의 생각은 큼직한 종이 석 장과 안으로 접힌 부분까지 가득 펼쳐졌다. 그는 도러시아에게는 그저 이렇게만 말했다.\n\n\"물론 쓰고말고, 얘야. 아주 영리한 청년이지, 이 젊은 래디슬로 말이다. 장차 두각을 나타낼 젊은이일 게야. 잘 쓴 편지야, 알다시피 사물을 보는 분별이 드러나 있어. 어쨌든 카소본 일은 내가 알려 주마.\"\n\n그러나 브룩 씨의 펜 끝은 하나의 사고 기관이어서, 특히 자비로운 종류의 문장이라면 그의 나머지 정신이 미처 따라잡기도 전에 줄줄 빚어냈다. 그것은 유감을 표하고 해결책을 제안했는데, 브룩 씨가 읽어 보면 그 글들은 절묘하게 표현된 것처럼, 놀랍도록 적절한 것처럼 보였고, 그가 전에는 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없는 결말로 이어졌다. 이 경우 그의 펜은, 자신이 젊은 래디슬로와 더 충분히 사귀고, 오래 방치되어 온 이탈리아 그림들을 함께 살펴볼 수 있도록, 바로 그 시기에 그가 이 근방으로 오지 못하는 것이 참으로 안타까운 일이라고 느꼈다. 또한 한 보따리의 사상을 품고 인생을 시작하려는 젊은이에게 어찌나 흥미를 느꼈던지, 둘째 장의 끝에 이르렀을 무렵 그것은 브룩 씨를 설득하여, 로윅에서 맞이할 수 없다면 젊은 래디슬로를 티프턴 그레인지로 초대하게 만들었다. 안 될 게 무어란 말인가? 둘이서 함께 할 만한 일이 아주 많을 테고, 지금은 유난히 성장하는 시기였다. 정치적 지평이 넓어지고 있었으며, 요컨대 브룩 씨의 펜은 최근 그가 그 미흡하게 편집된 기관지 '미들마치 파이어니어'에 실으려고 기고했던 짤막한 연설로 흘러 들어갔다. 이 편지를 봉하면서 브룩 씨는 어렴풋한 계획들이 밀려드는 데 들떴다. 사상을 형태로 빚어낼 줄 아는 젊은이, 새 후보를 위해 길을 닦으려고 사들인 '파이어니어', 활용될 문서들, 이 모든 것에서 무슨 일이 생겨날지 누가 알겠는가? 실리아가 곧 결혼할 참이니, 적어도 한동안은 식탁에 젊은이 하나를 함께 두는 것이 무척 즐거운 일일 터였다.\n그러나 그는 도러시아에게 편지에 무엇을 써 넣었는지 말하지 않은 채 떠났다. 그녀가 남편을 돌보느라 바빴기 때문이기도 했고, 사실 이런 일들은 그녀에게 아무런 중요성도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n* 패류학: 조개류를 연구하는 학문. * 유치 우편: 수신인이 우체국에 직접 가서 받을 수 있도록 보관해 두는 우편물.\n\n# 제28장\n\n정교하게 섞인 금속 아래서 울리게 하라. 가만히 귀 기울여\n맞는 음이 흘러나오기를 기다리라, 은빛 시냇물처럼.\n\n그러면 그 거대한 종이 떨리리니—그러면 그 덩어리가\n함께 일렁이는 무수한 파동으로\n\n낮고 부드러운 화음으로 응답하리라.\n그날 저녁 리드게이트는 빈시 양에게 캐소본 부인 이야기를 했는데, 자기보다 서른 살이나 많은 그 격식 차린 학구적인 남자에게 그녀가 품은 듯한 강렬한 감정을 다소 강조했다.\n\n\"물론 그분은 남편에게 헌신적이지요.\" 로저먼드가 말했는데, 거기에는 어떤 필연적인 귀결의 관념이 담겨 있었으니, 그 과학자는 그것을 여자에게 더없이 어여쁜 태도로 여겼다. 그러나 그녀는 동시에, 머지않아 죽을 듯한 남편을 두고 로윅 영지의 안주인이 된다는 것이 그리 서글픈 일만은 아니라고 생각하고 있었다. \"그분이 아주 아름답다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"분명 아름다운 분이지요. 하지만 그 점을 생각해 본 적은 없습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다.\n\"그건 직업상 어울리지 않는 일이겠지요.\" 로저먼드가 보조개를 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 진료 범위가 정말 넓어지고 계시네요! 전에는 체텀 가에 불려 가셨던 것 같고, 이제는 캐소본 가까지.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 리드게이트가 마지못해 인정하는 어조로 말했다. \"하지만 사실 저는 그런 사람들을 진료하는 것이 가난한 이들을 돌보는 것만큼 좋지가 않습니다. 증상은 더 단조롭고, 더 많은 야단법석을 거쳐야 하고, 터무니없는 말에 더 공손히 귀를 기울여야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"미들마치에서보다 더하지는 않겠지요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"그래도 적어도 넓은 복도를 지나다니시고, 가는 곳마다 장미 잎 향기가 나잖아요.\"\n\"그건 사실입니다, 몽모랑시 양.\" 리드게이트가 탁자 쪽으로 살짝 머리를 숙이며, 마치 향기를 즐기려는 듯 넷째 손가락으로 그녀의 손가방 입구에 놓여 있던 섬세한 손수건을 들어 올리고는, 미소 띤 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n그러나 리드게이트가 미들마치의 꽃 주위를 맴돌며 누리던 이 유쾌한 휴일 같은 자유가 언제까지나 이어질 수는 없었다. 그 도시에서 사회적 고립을 찾기란 다른 어디서나 마찬가지로 가능하지 않은 일이었고, 끈질기게 시시덕거리는 두 사람이 \"여러 가지가 제각기 굴러가게 하는 갖가지 얽힘과 무게와 타격과 충돌과 운동\"에서 결코 벗어날 수는 없었다. 빈시 양이 무엇을 하든 다 사람들의 입에 오르내렸으니, 어쩌면 그녀는 지금 더더욱 찬미자들과 비평가들의 눈에 띄었을 것이다. 마침 이즈음 빈시 부인이 얼마간의 갈등 끝에 프레드를 데리고 스톤 코트에 잠시 머물러 있었기 때문이다. 늙은 페더스톤을 흡족하게 하는 동시에 메리 가스를 경계할 다른 방도가 없었으니, 메리 가스는 프레드의 병이 나아 갈수록 점점 더 며느릿감으로 탐탁지 않아 보였다.\n\n이를테면 벌스트로드 이모는 로저먼드가 이제 혼자 지내게 되자 그녀를 보러 로윅 게이트에 조금 더 자주 들렀다. 벌스트로드 부인은 오빠에 대해 진정 누이다운 애정을 품고 있었으니, 늘 오빠가 더 나은 결혼을 할 수도 있었으리라 생각하면서도 그 아이들에게는 잘되기를 바랐다. 그런데 벌스트로드 부인은 플림데일 부인과 오랜 친분이 있었다. 두 사람은 비단이며 속옷의 무늬며 도자기며 성직자에 대해 거의 같은 취향을 지녔고, 건강과 살림살이의 자잘한 고민을 서로 털어놓았다. 벌스트로드 부인 쪽의 몇몇 자잘한 우월함, 이를테면 더 확고한 진지함, 정신적인 것에 대한 더 깊은 경탄, 그리고 도시 밖에 있는 저택 같은 것은 이따금 두 사람의 대화에 활기를 더해 주면서도 둘 사이를 갈라놓지는 않았다. 둘 다 선의를 지닌 여자들이었고, 자기 자신의 동기에 대해서는 거의 알지 못했다.\n\n벌스트로드 부인은 플림데일 부인을 오전에 방문했다가, 가엾은 로저먼드를 보러 가야 하니 더 오래 머물 수 없다는 말을 우연히 꺼냈다.\n\n\"왜 '가엾은 로저먼드'라고 하세요?\" 길든 매처럼 동그란 눈을 한, 야무지고 자그마한 여인 플림데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그 아이가 워낙 예쁜 데다 너무 분별없이 자랐거든요. 알다시피 그 어머니가 늘 그런 경박함이 있어서, 그게 아이들 일로 저를 걱정하게 만든답니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 해리엇, 제 마음을 솔직히 말하라면,\" 플림데일 부인이 힘주어 말했다. \"이렇게 말할 수밖에 없네요. 누가 봐도 댁이며 벌스트로드 씨가 이번 일을 기뻐하실 거라고 생각할 거예요. 댁이야말로 리드게이트 씨를 내세우려고 온갖 일을 다 하셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"실리나, 무슨 말씀이세요?\" 벌스트로드 부인이 정말로 놀라서 말했다.\n\n\"네드를 생각하면 진심으로 고맙기는 하지만요.\" 플림데일 부인이 말했다. \"그 애야 분명 여느 사람들보다 그런 아내를 거뜬히 부양할 수 있겠지요. 그래도 저는 그 애가 다른 데를 알아보길 바라요. 그래도 어미한테는 걱정이 있는 법이고, 어떤 젊은이들은 그 일로 못된 길로 빠지기도 하니까요. 게다가 굳이 말하라면, 저는 외지 사람이 도시로 들어오는 게 마뜩잖다고 말하겠어요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 실리나.\" 이번에는 벌스트로드 부인이 조금 힘주어 말했다. \"벌스트로드 씨도 한때는 이곳에 외지 사람이었어요. 아브라함과 모세도 그 땅에서 나그네였고, 우리는 나그네를 후히 대접하라는 가르침을 받았잖아요. 더욱이,\" 그녀는 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"흠잡을 데 없는 사람이라면 말이지요.\"\n\n\"저는 종교적인 뜻으로 말한 게 아니에요, 해리엇. 어미로서 말한 거예요.\"\n\n\"실리나, 제가 제 조카가 댁의 아들과 결혼하는 것에 대해 무슨 반대하는 말을 한 적은 결코 없을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"아, 그건 빈시 양의 자존심이에요. 분명히 다른 게 아니라고요.\" 이 일에 대해 한 번도 '해리엇'에게 속내를 다 털어놓은 적이 없던 플림데일 부인이 말했다. \"미들마치의 어떤 젊은이도 그 애한테는 성에 차지 않았어요. 그 어미가 그렇게 말하는 걸 제가 들었거든요. 그건 그리스도교 정신이 아니라고 생각해요. 그런데 이제 제가 듣자 하니, 그 애가 저 못지않게 자존심 센 남자를 만났더군요.\"\n\n\"로저먼드와 리드게이트 씨 사이에 뭔가 있다는 말씀은 아니겠지요?\" 자기 자신의 무지를 깨닫고 다소 무안해진 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"설마 모르고 계셨던 거예요, 해리엇?\"\n\n\"아, 제가 워낙 바깥출입을 거의 안 하잖아요. 게다가 저는 남 얘기 하는 걸 좋아하지 않아서 정말 아무것도 못 들어요. 댁은 제가 못 보는 사람들을 무척 많이 만나시잖아요. 댁의 교제 범위는 우리와 좀 다르니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 댁의 친조카이자 벌스트로드 씨가 그렇게 아끼는 사람인데, 그리고 댁도 분명 아끼시고요, 해리엇! 저는 한때 케이트가 좀 더 크면 그 사람을 케이트한테 점찍어 두신 줄 알았다니까요.\"\n\n\"지금으로서는 무슨 진지한 일이 있을 리 없다고 봐요.\" 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 오라버니가 분명 제게 말했을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 사람마다 방식이 다르긴 하지만, 제가 알기로는 빈시 양과 리드게이트 씨가 함께 있는 걸 보고 약혼한 사이라고 여기지 않을 사람은 아무도 없어요. 하지만 제가 상관할 바는 아니지요. 이 손모아장갑 본 좀 챙겨 둘까요?\"\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 벌스트로드 부인은 새삼 무거워진 마음으로 마차를 몰아 조카에게 갔다. 그녀 자신도 곱게 차려입었지만, 마침 들어와서 외출복 차림으로 자신을 맞이한 로저먼드가 거의 자기만큼이나 값비싸게 차려입은 것을 평소보다 조금 더 안타까운 마음으로 알아보았다. 벌스트로드 부인은 오빠를 여성스럽게 축소해 놓은 판본 같았고, 남편의 그 나직한 창백함은 조금도 지니고 있지 않았다. 그녀의 눈길은 정직하고 선했으며, 그녀는 에두르는 법이 없었다.\n\n\"보니 너 혼자 있구나, 얘야.\" 그들이 함께 응접실로 들어서면서 그녀가 진지하게 둘러보며 말했다. 로저먼드는 이모가 무언가 특별히 할 말이 있음을 직감했고, 두 사람은 가까이 마주 앉았다. 그럼에도 로저먼드의 보닛 안쪽 주름 장식이 어찌나 매력적이던지, 케이트에게도 똑같은 것을 마련해 주고 싶다는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었다. 그래서 벌스트로드 부인은 꽤 고운 편인 눈으로 그 넓은 주름 장식의 둘레를 굴리며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"방금 너에 관해 아주 놀라운 이야기를 들었단다, 로저먼드.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 이모?\" 로저먼드의 눈길 역시 이모의 큼직한 자수 옷깃 위를 돌아다니고 있었다.\n\n\"도무지 믿을 수가 없구나. 네가 약혼을 했는데 내가 모르고 있다니, 네 아버지가 내게 말도 안 해 주다니.\" 여기서 벌스트로드 부인의 눈길이 마침내 로저먼드의 눈에 가 닿았고, 로저먼드는 얼굴이 빨개지며 말했다.\n\n\"저는 약혼하지 않았어요, 이모.\"\n\n\"그럼 어째서 다들 그렇게 말하지? 온 도시의 이야깃거리던데?\"\n\n\"온 도시의 이야깃거리야 별 대수롭지 않다고 생각해요.\" 로저먼드가 속으로는 흐뭇해하며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 얘야, 좀 더 분별 있게 굴어라. 이웃을 그렇게 깔보지 말고. 너도 이제 스물둘이 넘었고 재산도 없을 거란 걸 기억해라. 네 아버지가 너한테 무얼 떼어 줄 형편이 못 된다는 건 분명하니까. 리드게이트 씨는 무척 지적이고 영리하지. 거기에 끌리는 게 있다는 건 나도 알아. 나 자신도 그런 남자들과 이야기하는 걸 좋아하고, 네 이모부도 그 사람을 아주 요긴하게 여기니까. 하지만 여기서는 그 직업이 변변치 못해. 물론 이 세상살이가 전부는 아니지. 하지만 의사가 참된 신앙심을 지닌 경우는 드물단다. 지성에 대한 자만이 지나치거든. 그리고 너는 가난한 남자와 결혼하기에는 어울리지 않는 아이야.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 씨는 가난한 사람이 아니에요, 이모. 그분은 아주 훌륭한 집안과 연줄이 있어요.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 직접 자기가 가난하다고 내게 말했단다.\"\n\n\"그건 그분이 호화롭게 사는 사람들에게 익숙해서 그런 거예요.\"\n\n\"얘, 로저먼드, 너는 호화롭게 살 생각을 해선 안 된다.\"\n\n로저먼드는 눈을 내리깔고 손가방을 만지작거렸다. 그녀는 불같은 아가씨도 아니었고 날카롭게 대꾸할 줄도 몰랐지만, 자기 마음 내키는 대로 살 작정이었다.\n\n\"그럼 정말이로구나?\" 벌스트로드 부인이 조카를 매우 진지하게 바라보며 말했다. \"너는 리드게이트 씨를 마음에 두고 있고, 네 아버지는 모르지만 너희 사이에 무슨 약조가 있는 거야. 솔직히 말해 보렴, 얘 로저먼드. 리드게이트 씨가 정말로 너에게 청혼을 한 거니?\"\n\n가엾은 로저먼드의 심정은 몹시 언짢았다. 그녀는 리드게이트의 감정과 의향에 대해 마음을 아주 놓고 있었지만, 막상 이모가 이런 질문을 하자 그렇다고 대답할 수 없는 것이 싫었다. 자존심이 상했으나, 몸에 밴 태도의 절제가 그녀를 도왔다.\n\n\"용서하세요, 이모. 그 이야기는 하지 않는 편이 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"확실한 전망도 없는 남자에게 네 마음을 줄 리는 없겠지, 얘야. 그리고 내가 아는, 네가 거절한 그 훌륭한 청혼 두 건을 생각해 보렴! 그중 하나는 네가 차 버리지만 않는다면 아직 손이 닿는 곳에 있단다. 나는 그런 식으로 굴다가 끝내 결혼을 잘못한 굉장한 미인을 알고 있어. 네드 플림데일 씨는 좋은 청년이야. 어떤 이들은 잘생겼다고 여길 만하고, 외아들인 데다, 그런 큰 사업이 직업보다 낫지. 결혼이 전부라는 건 아니다. 나야 네가 무엇보다 먼저 하느님의 나라를 구하길 바라지. 하지만 처녀라면 제 마음을 제 손아귀에 두어야 하는 법이란다.\"\n\n\"설령 그렇대도 저는 네드 플림데일 씨에게는 결코 제 마음을 주지 않겠어요. 이미 거절했는걸요. 제가 사랑한다면, 단번에 변함없이 사랑할 거예요.\" 로저먼드가 자신이 낭만적인 여주인공이 된 듯한 기분에 흠뻑 젖어, 그 역할을 어여쁘게 연기하며 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 된 일인지 알겠구나, 얘야.\" 벌스트로드 부인이 자리에서 일어서며 서글픈 목소리로 말했다. \"보답도 받지 못한 채 네 마음을 내주어 버린 게로구나.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말로요, 이모.\" 로저먼드가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 리드게이트 씨가 너에게 진지한 애정을 품고 있다고 정말로 자신하는 거니?\"\n\n이때쯤 로저먼드의 두 뺨은 끈질기게 달아올라 있었고, 그녀는 깊은 무안함을 느꼈다. 그녀는 입을 다물기로 했고, 이모는 한결 더 확신한 채 자리를 떴다.\n\n벌스트로드 씨는 세속적이고 사소한 일에서는 아내가 시키는 대로 하는 편이었으므로, 아내는 이제 이유는 말하지 않은 채 다음 기회에 리드게이트 씨와 대화하면서 그가 곧 결혼할 의향이 있는지 알아봐 달라고 부탁했다. 그 결과는 분명한 부정이었다. 벌스트로드 씨가 꼬치꼬치 캐물어 보니, 리드게이트는 결혼으로 이어질 만한 애정을 품은 남자라면 결코 하지 않았을 법한 말투로 이야기했다. 이제 벌스트로드 부인은 자신 앞에 중대한 의무가 놓여 있다고 느꼈고, 곧 리드게이트와 단둘이 마주 앉을 자리를 마련했다. 그 자리에서 그녀는 프레드 빈시의 건강을 묻고 오빠의 대가족에 대한 진심 어린 염려를 표하는 데서 시작해, 젊은 사람들이 인생에서 자리를 잡는 일과 관련해 그들 앞에 놓인 위험에 관한 일반적인 이야기로 옮겨 갔다. 젊은 남자들은 흔히 방탕하고 사람을 실망시켜서, 자기에게 들인 돈에 비해 보답이 적은 법이며, 처녀는 자신의 앞날을 가로막을 수 있는 갖가지 상황에 노출되어 있다는 것이었다.\n\n\"특히 매력이 뛰어나고 부모가 손님을 많이 맞이하는 경우에는 더욱 그렇지요.\" 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다. \"신사들이 그저 한순간의 즐거움을 위해 그 처녀에게 관심을 쏟고 그녀를 독차지하면, 그게 다른 이들을 쫓아 버리거든요. 어떤 처녀의 앞날에 끼어든다는 것은 무거운 책임이라고 생각해요, 리드게이트 씨.\" 여기서 벌스트로드 부인은, 질책까지는 아니더라도 경고하려는 의도를 분명히 드러내며 그에게 눈길을 고정했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지요.\" 리드게이트가 그녀를 바라보며—아마 마주 보고 약간 빤히 응시하기까지 하며 말했다. \"하지만 한편으로, 어떤 처녀가 자기에게 반할까 봐, 또는 남들이 그 처녀가 그러리라 생각할까 봐 처녀에게 관심을 주어선 안 된다는 생각을 품고 다니는 남자라면 어지간한 멋쟁이 바보일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아, 리드게이트 씨, 당신은 자기 강점이 무엇인지 잘 알잖아요. 여기 우리 젊은이들이 당신과 겨룰 수 없다는 것도 알고요. 당신이 어느 집을 드나들면, 그게 한 처녀가 바람직한 혼처를 잡는 데 크게 방해가 되어, 청혼이 들어와도 받아들이지 못하게 만들 수 있어요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 미들마치의 올랜도들에 대해 자신이 지닌 우위에 으쓱하기보다는, 벌스트로드 부인의 속뜻을 알아챈 것이 더 거슬렸다. 그녀는 자신이 필요한 만큼 충분히 강한 인상을 주며 말했다고 느꼈고, '방해가 되다'라는 점잖은 단어를 씀으로써 여전히 충분히 빤히 드러나는 자질구레한 사정 덩어리에 고상한 휘장을 둘렀다고 느꼈다.\n\n리드게이트는 살짝 화가 치밀어, 한 손으로 머리카락을 뒤로 쓸어 넘기고 다른 손으로는 조끼 주머니를 괜히 더듬다가, 몸을 굽혀 자그마한 검은 스패니얼을 불렀는데, 그 개는 그의 건성인 쓰다듬음을 마다할 만큼 눈치가 있었다. 그는 다른 손님들과 함께 만찬을 들고 막 차를 마신 참이었으니, 자리를 뜨는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이었을 것이다. 그러나 벌스트로드 부인은 자기 말이 이해되었음을 의심치 않고 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n솔로몬의 잠언은, 헌 입천장이 모래알을 잘 느끼듯 켕기는 의식은 빗댄 말을 잘 알아듣는다는 것을 말하는 걸 빠뜨린 듯하다. 이튿날 페어브러더 씨가 거리에서 리드게이트와 헤어지면서, 저녁에 빈시 가에서 만나겠거니 했다. 리드게이트는 무뚝뚝하게 아니라고, 할 일이 있다고, 저녁 외출을 끊어야 한다고 대답했다.\n\"아니, 자네는 돛대에 몸을 묶고 귀를 막을 작정인가?\" 부목사가 말했다. \"글쎄, 세이렌에게 홀리지 않을 작정이라면, 제때에 대비책을 세우는 게 옳지.\"\n\n* 세이렌: 그리스 신화에서 아름다운 노래로 뱃사람을 홀려 죽음에 이르게 한 바다의 요정. 오디세우스는 부하들에게 자신을 돛대에 묶게 하고 부하들의 귀는 막아 그 유혹을 피했다.\n\n며칠 전이었다면 리드게이트는 이 말을, 부목사가 늘 말하는 방식 이상으로는 전혀 마음에 두지 않았을 것이다. 그런데 이제 그 말은 어떤 빗댐을 담은 듯했고, 그가 바보짓을 하며 오해를 살 만하게 처신해 왔다는 인상을 굳혀 주는 것 같았다. 다만 그가 믿기로는 로저먼드 자신에게 오해받은 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 분명 모든 것을 그가 의도한 만큼이나 가볍게 받아들였으리라 확신했다. 그녀는 예절의 모든 점에 관해 절묘한 재치와 통찰을 지니고 있었지만, 그녀가 어울려 사는 사람들은 멍청이들이고 참견꾼들이었다. 어쨌든 이 오해를 더 키워서는 안 되었다. 그는 용무가 아니고서는 빈시 씨 댁에 가지 않겠다고 결심했고, 그 결심을 지켰다.\n\n로저먼드는 무척 불행해졌다. 이모의 질문에 처음 일었던 불안은 점점 커지고 또 커져서, 리드게이트를 보지 못한 지 열흘이 다 되어 갈 무렵에는 어쩌면 닥칠지도 모를 그 공허함에 대한 공포로, 인간의 희망을 그토록 손쉽게 지워 버리는 그 날렵하고 치명적인 해면에 대한 불길한 예감으로 자라났다. 마법사의 주문이 잠시 정원으로 바꾸어 놓았던 황무지처럼, 세상은 그녀에게 새로운 황량함을 띠게 될 터였다. 그녀는 자신이 사랑에 좌절하는 아픔을 알기 시작했음을, 그리고 지난 여섯 달 동안 누려 온 것 같은 그토록 즐거운 공중누각을 어떤 다른 남자도 일으켜 줄 수 없으리라는 것을 느꼈다. 가엾은 로저먼드는 입맛을 잃었고, 의상이 가득 든 상자들만 남겨진 채 마차를 기대할 길 없는, 무대 위의 어여쁜 아리아드네처럼 버림받은 기분이 들었다.\n\n* 아리아드네: 그리스 신화에서 크레타의 공주. 테세우스를 도와 미궁을 빠져나오게 했으나, 함께 떠난 뒤 낙소스섬에 홀로 버려졌다.\n세상에는 한결같이 사랑이라 불리며, (문학과 연극에서) 모든 것을 변명해 주는 숭고한 격정의 특권을 주장하는 온갖 신기한 혼합물이 있다. 다행히 로저먼드는 무슨 절망적인 행동을 저지를 생각은 하지 않았다. 그녀는 여느 때처럼 아름답게 고운 머리를 땋았고, 의젓하게 평정을 유지했다. 그녀가 떠올린 가장 기운 나는 추측은, 벌스트로드 이모가 어떤 식으로든 끼어들어 리드게이트의 방문을 막았으리라는 것이었다. 무엇이든 그 사람의 자발적인 무관심보다는 나았으니까. 열흘이 너무 짧다고 여기는 사람은—여위거나 가벼워지거나 하는, 측정 가능한 격정의 효과를 위해서가 아니라, 놀란 억측과 실망이 한 바퀴 도는 정신의 온전한 회로를 위해서—한 젊은 아가씨의 우아한 한가로움 속에서 무슨 일이 벌어질 수 있는지를 모르는 사람이다.\n\n그러나 열하루째 되는 날, 리드게이트가 스톤 코트를 떠나려 할 때 빈시 부인이, 페더스톤 씨의 건강에 뚜렷한 변화가 생겼으니 그날 안으로 스톤 코트로 와 달라는 뜻을 남편에게 전해 달라고 그에게 부탁했다. 그런데 리드게이트는 창고로 찾아갈 수도 있었고, 수첩 한 장에 전갈을 적어 문간에 남겨 둘 수도 있었다. 그러나 이런 간단한 방편들이 분명 그에게는 떠오르지 않았으니, 이로 미루어 우리는 그가 빈시 씨가 집에 없는 시각에 그 집에 찾아가 빈시 양에게 전갈을 남기는 데에 별다른 거부감이 없었으리라고 짐작할 수 있다. 사람은 여러 가지 동기에서 누군가와 어울리기를 마다할 수 있지만, 아무도 자신을 그리워하지 않는다면 아마 현자라도 흐뭇해하지는 않으리라. 새 습관을 옛 습관에 이어 붙이는 우아하고 손쉬운 방법은, 방탕에 맞선 자신의 저항이며 달콤한 소리마저도 오래 끊는 단식을 하겠다는 자신의 굳은 결심에 대해 로저먼드와 몇 마디 장난스러운 말을 나누는 것이었다. 또한 고백하건대, 벌스트로드 부인의 암시가 어디에 근거를 두고 있을지에 대한 온갖 순간적인 억측이, 가느다란 잔털처럼 그의 더 단단한 생각의 직물에 어느새 짜여 들어 엉겨 붙어 있었다.\n\n빈시 양은 혼자 있었고, 리드게이트가 들어서자 어찌나 얼굴이 빨개지던지 그도 덩달아 당혹스러워져서, 무슨 장난기는커녕 곧장 자신이 찾아온 이유를 이야기하기 시작했고, 거의 격식을 차려 가며 그 전갈을 아버지에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 처음 순간에는 자신의 행복이 되돌아오는 듯 느꼈던 로저먼드는 리드게이트의 태도에 몹시 상처받았다. 그녀의 홍조는 가셨고, 그녀는 불필요한 말 한마디 덧붙이지 않은 채 차갑게 응했다. 손에 들고 있던 자질구레한 사슬 세공 덕분에 그녀는 리드게이트를 턱 위로는 올려다보지 않을 수 있었다. 모든 실패에서 시작은 분명 그 전체의 절반이다. 그가 채찍을 만지작거리며 아무 말도 못 한 채 두 번의 긴 순간을 앉아 있다가 리드게이트가 일어서서 가려 하자, 무안함과 그것을 드러내지 않으려는 마음 사이에서 갈등하느라 안절부절못하던 로저먼드는 놀란 듯 사슬을 떨어뜨리고는 자기도 기계적으로 일어섰다. 리드게이트가 즉각 몸을 굽혀 사슬을 집어 들었다. 그가 몸을 일으키자, 그는 곱고 긴 목 위에 얹힌 사랑스러운 작은 얼굴에 아주 가까이 다가가 있었으니, 그 얼굴은 그가 늘 더없이 완벽하게 다듬어진 자족적인 우아함으로 돌아가는 것을 보아 오던 것이었다. 그러나 이제 그가 눈을 들어 보니, 어떤 어찌할 바 모르는 떨림이 보였고, 그것이 아주 새삼스럽게 그의 마음을 건드려 그는 의아한 듯 번뜩이는 눈으로 로저먼드를 바라보게 되었다. 이 순간 그녀는 다섯 살 적의 그 어느 때만큼이나 꾸밈없었다. 그녀는 눈물이 차오른 것을 느꼈고, 그것을 파란 꽃 위에 맺힌 물방울처럼 그대로 두거나, 흐르고 싶은 대로 두 뺨 위로 흘러내리게 하는 것 외에 달리 어찌해 볼 도리가 없음을 느꼈다.\n\n그 꾸밈없는 순간이 결정을 굳히는 깃털의 한 스침이 되었으니, 그것이 시시덕거림을 흔들어 사랑으로 만들었다. 물 아래 그 물망초들을 들여다보던 그 야심 찬 남자가 매우 따뜻한 마음을 지녔고 또 무모했다는 것을 기억하라. 그는 사슬이 어디로 가는지도 몰랐다. 어떤 생각 하나가 그의 내면 깊은 곳을 꿰뚫고 전율처럼 지나갔는데, 그것은 봉인된 무덤 속이 아니라 더없이 가볍고 쉽게 뚫리는 흙더미 아래 묻혀 있던 열정적인 사랑의 힘을 일으켜 세우는 데에 기적 같은 효과를 발휘했다. 그의 말은 자못 불쑥 튀어나온 어색한 것이었지만, 그 어조는 그 말을 간절히 호소하는 열렬한 고백처럼 들리게 했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이지요? 괴로워하고 있군요. 부디 말해 주세요.\"\n\n로저먼드는 그런 어조로 누가 자신에게 말을 건넨 적이 한 번도 없었다. 그녀가 그 말이 무엇이었는지 알아들었는지조차 나는 잘 모르겠다. 그러나 그녀는 리드게이트를 바라보았고 눈물이 두 뺨 위로 흘러내렸다. 그 침묵보다 더 완전한 대답은 없었을 것이고, 리드게이트는 다른 모든 것을 잊은 채, 이 사랑스러운 젊은 아가씨가 자신의 기쁨을 자기에게 기대고 있다는 갑작스러운 믿음에서 솟구쳐 오른 애틋함에 완전히 사로잡혀, 정말로 그녀를 두 팔로 감싸 안고는—그는 약하고 고통받는 이들에게 다정하게 대하는 데 익숙했다—부드럽게 지켜 주듯 그녀를 안았고, 그 큼직한 두 방울의 눈물에 하나씩 입을 맞추었다. 이것은 서로의 마음을 확인하는 묘한 방식이었으나, 그것은 빠른 방식이었다. 로저먼드는 화내지 않았으나, 수줍은 행복에 잠겨 조금 뒤로 물러섰고, 이제 리드게이트는 그녀 곁에 앉아 좀 더 온전하게 이야기할 수 있었다. 로저먼드는 자신의 자그마한 고백을 해야 했고, 그는 충동적으로 흘러넘치게 감사와 애정의 말들을 쏟아 냈다. 반 시간 뒤 그는 약혼한 남자가 되어 그 집을 나섰으니, 그의 영혼은 더 이상 자신의 것이 아니라 자신이 매여 버린 여자의 것이었다.\n\n그는 저녁에 다시 와서 빈시 씨와 이야기를 나누었는데, 빈시 씨는 막 스톤 코트에서 돌아온 참으로, 머지않아 페더스톤 씨의 '서거' 소식을 듣게 되리라 확신하고 있었다. 때맞춰 떠오른 '서거'라는 그 절묘한 단어는 그의 기분을 평소 저녁의 들뜬 수준보다도 더 높여 놓았다. 적절한 단어는 언제나 하나의 힘이어서, 그 명료함을 우리의 행동에 옮겨 준다. 서거로 여기고 보니 늙은 페더스톤의 죽음은 한낱 법적인 양상을 띠게 되어, 빈시 씨는 그것을 두고 코담뱃갑을 톡톡 두드리며 명랑하게 굴 수 있었고, 잠깐씩이나마 엄숙한 척조차 할 필요가 없었다. 게다가 빈시 씨는 엄숙함도 가식도 다 싫어했다. 누가 유언자를 두고 경외감에 사로잡힌 적이 있으며, 부동산 권리증서를 두고 찬송가를 부른 적이 있던가? 빈시 씨는 그날 저녁 만사를 유쾌하게 보려는 기분이었다. 그는 리드게이트에게, 프레드가 결국 집안의 튼튼한 체질을 물려받았으니 곧 다시 더없이 건장한 청년이 될 거라고 말하기까지 했다. 그리고 로저먼드의 약혼에 대한 동의를 청하자 그는 놀라우리만치 선뜻 그것을 허락하고는, 곧장 젊은 남녀에게 결혼이란 바람직한 것이라는 일반적인 이야기로 넘어갔으며, 그 모든 것에서 펀치를 조금 더 마시는 것이 적절하다는 결론을 끌어내는 듯 보였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXII.\n\n그들은 고양이가 우유를 핥듯 암시를 받아들이리라.\n— 셰익스피어, 「폭풍우」\n\n페더스톤 씨가 프레드와 그의 어머니에게 곁을 떠나지 말라고 한사코 요구한 데 근거를 둔 시장의 의기양양한 자신감은, 그 노인의 혈족들 가슴속에서 들끓고 있던 온갖 감정에 비하면 미약한 정서에 지나지 않았다. 노인이 자리에 눕게 되자 그 혈족들은 자연히 가족의 유대 의식을 더 드러냈고 눈에 띄게 그 수가 늘어났다. 자연히 그럴 수밖에 없었다. \"불쌍한 피터\"가 징두리널을 댄 거실의 안락의자를 차지하고 있을 적에는, 요리사가 끓는 물을 준비해 두고 기다리는 부지런한 딱정벌레들조차도, 그들이 선호할 만한 이유가 있는 난롯가에서 그만큼 환영받지 못한 자들만큼 달갑지 않았으니, 페더스톤가의 피가 빈약하게 흐르던 그 사람들이 바로 그러했다. 그것은 그들 자신의 인색함 때문이 아니라 가난 때문이었다. 솔로몬 형제와 제인 자매는 부유했고, 그들을 맞이할 때마다 보이는 가족 특유의 솔직함과 거짓 예의의 완전한 결여를, 그들은 자기 형제가 유언을 작성하는 엄숙한 행위에서 재력의 우월한 자격을 무시하리라는 논거로 여기지 않았다. 적어도 그들 자신만큼은, 형제가 집에서 내쫓을 만큼 매정하게 군 적이 없었으니, 그런 자격의 그림자조차 없는 요나 형제, 마사 자매와 나머지 사람들을 멀리해 온 것이 별스러운 일로 보이지 않았다. 그들은 돈이란 좋은 알이어서 따뜻한 둥지에 품어 두어야 한다는 피터의 격언을 알고 있었다.\n\n그러나 요나 형제, 마사 자매와 궁핍한 추방자들 전부는 다른 관점을 지니고 있었다. 개연성이란 투조 세공이나 벽지 무늬에서 마음대로 보이는 얼굴들만큼이나 다양하다. 창조적인 성향을 가지고 보기만 하면, 거기에는 주피터부터 주디까지 온갖 형상이 다 들어 있다. 더 가난하고 덜 총애받는 자들에게는, 피터가 살아생전 그들을 위해 아무것도 해 주지 않았으니 마지막에 가서는 그들을 기억해 주리라는 것이 그럴듯해 보였다. 요나는 사람들이란 유언을 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것으로 만들기를 좋아한다고 주장했고, 마사는 가장 기대하지 않은 자들에게 재산의 대부분을 남긴다 해도 아무도 놀랄 것이 없다고 말했다. 게다가 다리에 수종을 앓으며 \"저기 누워 있는\" 친형제라면 피가 물보다 진하다는 것을 절감하게 마련이며, 유언을 바꾸지 않았다 해도 손에 쥔 돈이 있을지 모를 일이었다. 어쨌거나 혈족이라고 할 수도 없는 자들을 경계하기 위해서라도 혈족 몇은 그 집에 들어앉아 망을 보아야 했다. 위조된 유언이나 다툼이 벌어진 유언 같은 일이 세상에 알려진 바 있었으니, 그런 것에는 유산 상속인이 아닌 자들도 어떻게든 그것으로 먹고살 수 있게 해 주는 황금빛 아련한 이점이 있는 듯했다. 또한 혈족이 아닌 자들이 물건을 빼돌리다 붙잡힐 수도 있었다. 게다가 불쌍한 피터는 \"저기 누워\" 속수무책이 아닌가! 누군가는 망을 보아야 했다. 그러나 이 결론에서는 그들도 솔로몬, 제인과 한마음이었다. 더욱이 몇몇 조카와 조카딸, 사촌들은, 제 재산을 \"마음대로 물려줄\" 수 있고 스스로 별스러운 짓을 큼직하게 즐길 수 있는 사람이 어떤 일을 저지를지에 관해 한층 더 미묘하게 따져 가며, 챙겨야 할 가족의 이해관계가 있다고 멋들어지게 느꼈고, 스톤 코트야말로 자신들이 찾아가는 것이 당연한 곳이라고 여겼다. 마사 자매, 다른 이름으로는 크랜치 부인은 초크 플랫에서 색색거리는 숨을 쉬며 살고 있어 그 여행을 감당할 수 없었지만, 그녀의 아들은 불쌍한 피터의 친조카로서 그녀를 유리하게 대리할 수 있었고, 일어날 법해 보이는 그 있을 법하지 않은 일들을 외삼촌 요나가 부당하게 이용하지 못하도록 망을 볼 수 있었다. 사실 페더스톤가의 피에는 누구나 서로서로 감시해야 하며, 또한 다른 모든 사람은 전능하신 분이 자신을 지켜보고 계심을 명심하는 것이 좋으리라는 보편적인 의식이 흐르고 있었다.\n\n이리하여 스톤 코트에서는 끊임없이 어느 혈족인가가 마차에서 내리거나 떠나는 광경이 보였고, 메리 가스는 그들의 전갈을 페더스톤 씨에게 전해야 하는 달갑잖은 임무를 떠맡았는데, 노인은 그들 중 아무도 만나려 하지 않았고, 그녀를 아래층으로 내려보내 그 사실을 알리는 한층 더 달갑잖은 임무를 맡겼다. 그녀는 살림을 맡은 사람으로서 시골의 좋은 관습에 따라 그들에게 머물러 식사하기를 권해야 한다고 느꼈지만, 페더스톤 씨가 자리에 누운 지금 아래층에서 늘어날 식비 문제에 관해서는 빈시 부인과 상의하기로 했다.\n\n\"오, 얘야, 임종을 앞둔 병환에 재산까지 걸린 곳에서는 일을 후하게 해야 한단다. 하느님께 맹세코, 집 안의 햄을 죄다 내놓는 것도 아깝지 않아. 다만 제일 좋은 건 장례식을 위해 남겨 둬라. 송아지 고기 속을 채운 요리는 늘 준비해 두고, 좋은 치즈도 잘라서 내놓고. 이런 임종 병환 때에는 손님이 끊이지 않으리라 각오해야 한다.\" 후한 빈시 부인은 다시금 명랑한 어조에 화사한 깃털을 되찾은 채 말했다.\n\n그러나 일부 방문객들은 마차에서 내린 뒤 송아지 고기와 햄을 후하게 대접받고서도 떠나지 않았다. 예컨대 요나 형제가 그러했는데(대부분의 가문에는 그런 달갑잖은 사람들이 있게 마련이고, 어쩌면 최고의 귀족 가문에도 거대하게 빚을 지고 더 큰 비용을 들여 부풀어 오른 브롭딩낵의 표본들이 있을 것이다)—그러니까 내가 말하건대, 요나 형제는 영락한 처지가 되어, 주로 어떤 직업으로 생계를 꾸렸는데, 그것은 증권 거래나 경마장에서 사기를 치는 것보다는 훨씬 나았지만 그가 떠벌리지 않을 만큼은 겸손한 일이었고, 다만 앉아 있기 좋은 구석과 먹을 것만 넉넉하면 브래싱에 그가 있을 필요는 없는 일이었다. 그는 부엌 구석을 골랐는데, 한편으로는 그곳이 제일 마음에 들어서였고, 한편으로는 솔로몬과 함께 앉고 싶지 않아서였으니, 형제로서 그에 대해 강경한 견해를 품고 있었기 때문이다. 그 유명한 안락의자에 가장 좋은 양복을 입고 앉아, 좋은 음식이 늘 눈에 들어오는 곳에서, 그는 이 집에 들어앉아 있다는 편안한 의식을 품었고, 거기에는 일요일과 그린 맨 주점의 술집에 대한 덧없는 연상이 뒤섞여 있었다. 그는 메리 가스에게 그 불쌍한 친구가 땅 위에 있는 한 자신은 피터 형제의 손이 닿는 곳을 벗어나지 않겠노라고 알렸다. 가문에서 골칫거리인 자들은 대개 재담꾼 아니면 멍청이다. 요나는 페더스톤가에서 재담꾼이어서, 하녀들이 난롯가로 다가오면 농을 주고받았지만, 가스 양만큼은 수상쩍은 인물로 여기는 듯 차가운 눈초리로 그녀를 좇았다.\n\n메리로서는 이 한 쌍의 눈만이라면 비교적 수월하게 견뎌 냈을 테지만, 불행히도 젊은 크랜치가 있었으니, 그는 어머니를 대리하고 외삼촌 요나를 감시하려고 초크 플랫에서 머나먼 길을 와서는, 머물면서 주로 부엌에 앉아 삼촌의 말동무가 되어 주는 것을 의무로 느꼈다. 젊은 크랜치는 정확히 재담꾼과 멍청이의 중간점은 아니었다—약간 후자 쪽으로 기울어 있었고, 사팔뜨기여서 그의 심정이라고는, 강경한 성질의 것이 아니라는 점 말고는 모든 게 의심스럽게 남았다. 메리 가스가 부엌에 들어와 요나 페더스톤 씨가 차가운 형사 같은 눈으로 그녀를 좇기 시작하면, 젊은 크랜치는 같은 방향으로 고개를 돌리며, 마치 일부러 그러는 것처럼, 보로가 집시들에게 신약 성서를 읽어 줄 때의 그들처럼, 자신이 어떻게 사팔눈을 뜨는지 그녀가 주목하라고 우기는 듯했다. 이는 불쌍한 메리에게는 좀 지나친 일이었다. 어떤 때는 그 때문에 속이 메슥거렸고, 어떤 때는 진지함을 잃고 말았다. 어느 날 기회가 생기자 그녀는 부엌 광경을 프레드에게 묘사하지 않을 수 없었는데, 프레드는 당장 그것을 보러 가는 것을 막을 수 없어 그저 지나가는 척하려 했다. 그러나 그 네 개의 눈과 마주하자마자 그는 가장 가까운 문으로 황급히 달려 나가야 했고, 그 문은 마침 우유 저장실로 통해 있었으니, 그곳 높은 지붕 아래 우유통들 사이에서 그는 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았고, 그 웃음은 부엌에까지 또렷이 들릴 만큼 텅 빈 울림을 만들어 냈다. 그는 다른 문으로 달아났지만, 그전까지 프레드의 흰 안색과 긴 다리, 야무지게 가늘어진 얼굴을 본 적이 없던 요나 씨는, 이런 외모의 특징들을 가장 저열한 도덕적 속성들과 재치 있게 결합한 숱한 비아냥을 준비해 두었다.\n\n\"이봐 톰, 너는 그렇게 신사다운 바지를 입지 못하잖니—너는 그만큼 곱고 긴 다리를 절반도 갖지 못했고.\" 요나가 조카에게 말하며, 동시에 한쪽 눈을 찡긋했으니, 이 말들에는 부인할 수 없다는 점 말고도 무언가 더 있다는 뜻이었다. 톰은 제 다리를 내려다보았으나, 자신의 도덕적 우월함을 더 사악하게 긴 다리와 비난받아 마땅한 바지의 우아함보다 더 좋아하는지는 분명히 하지 않았다.\n\n징두리널을 댄 그 큰 거실에서도 늘 한 쌍씩의 눈이 망을 보고 있었으며, 친척들은 \"밤샘하는 사람\"이 되고 싶어 안달했다. 많은 이들이 와서 점심을 먹고 떠났지만, 솔로몬 형제와 와울 부인이 되기 전 스물다섯 해 동안 제인 페더스톤이었던 부인은 날마다 몇 시간씩 그곳에 머무는 것이 좋다고 여겼으니, 교활한 메리 가스를 관찰하는 일(그녀는 어찌나 속이 깊은지 무슨 일에서도 들통날 데가 없었다) 말고는 헤아릴 만한 다른 소일거리도 없이, 페더스톤 씨의 방에 들어가는 것이 허락되지 않는다는 생각에 이따금 메마르게 주름진 울음의 기색을 내비치곤 했다—더 축축한 계절이었더라면 눈물을 폭포처럼 쏟아 낼 능력이 있는 양. 노인이 제 가족을 싫어하는 마음은, 그들에게 모진 말을 던지며 스스로를 즐겁게 할 기력이 줄어들수록 도리어 강해지는 듯했다. 너무 무기력해 쏘아붙이지 못하니, 그 독은 그만큼 더 그의 핏속으로 역류했다.\n\n메리 가스를 통해 전해진 전갈을 온전히 믿지 못한 그들은, 둘 다 검은 옷을 입고—와울 부인은 흰 손수건을 반쯤 펼쳐 손에 들고서—둘 다 일종의 반쯤 상중인 듯한 자줏빛 얼굴을 한 채, 함께 침실 문 안에 모습을 드러냈다. 한편 빈시 부인은 발그레한 뺨에 분홍 리본을 나부끼며, 다름 아닌 그들의 친형제에게 강심제를 먹이고 있었고, 안색이 환한 프레드는 노름꾼답게 짧은 머리칼을 곱슬거리며 큰 의자에 느긋하게 기대어 있었다.\n\n페더스톤 노인은 자기 명령을 어기고 나타난 이 장례식 같은 형상들을 보자마자, 강심제보다 훨씬 효과적으로 그를 일으켜 세우는 분노가 솟구쳤다. 그는 등받이에 기대어 몸을 받치고 있었고, 늘 금 손잡이 지팡이를 곁에 두고 있었다. 이제 그는 그것을 움켜쥐고, 이 흉측한 망령들을 쫓아내려는 듯 할 수 있는 한 넓은 범위로 앞뒤로 휘두르며, 쉰 듯한 비명을 질러 댔다—\n\n\"물러가, 물러가, 와울 부인! 물러가, 솔로몬!\"\n\n\"오, 피터 형제.\" 와울 부인이 입을 떼었으나—솔로몬이 그녀 앞으로 손을 들어 제지했다. 그는 뺨이 큰 사내로, 일흔 가까운 나이에 작고 음흉한 눈을 지녔고, 형제 피터보다 훨씬 온화한 성정일 뿐 아니라 스스로를 훨씬 더 속이 깊다고 여겼으니, 실로 어떤 동료에게도 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니었다. 왜냐하면 그들이 그가 의심하는 것보다 더 탐욕스럽고 기만적일 수는 없을 터였기 때문이다. 그는 눈에 보이지 않는 권능들조차도, 남들 못지않게 불경할 수 있었던 재산가가 여기저기 온화한 말 한마디씩을 끼워 넣으면 누그러질 법하다고 생각했다.\n\n\"피터 형제.\" 그는 비위를 맞추면서도 진지하게 격식을 차린 어조로 말했다. \"스리 크로프츠와 망간 건에 관해 형제께 말씀드리는 게 마땅한 일입니다. 제 마음에 무엇이 걸려 있는지는 전능하신 분께서 아십니다—\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 알고 싶지 않은 것까지 그분은 아시겠구먼.\" 피터가 휴전을 내보이듯 지팡이를 내려놓으며 말했는데, 거기에는 위협도 깃들어 있었으니, 그는 더 가까운 싸움이 벌어질 경우 금 손잡이를 곤봉 삼으려고 지팡이를 거꾸로 돌려 쥔 채 솔로몬의 대머리를 매섭게 노려보았다.\n\n\"형제께서는 제게 말씀하지 않으셨다가 후회하실 일들이 있을 겁니다, 형제.\" 솔로몬이 말했으나, 그러면서도 앞으로 다가가지는 않았다. \"오늘 밤 제가 형제 곁을 지키고, 제인도 저와 함께 기꺼이 지키겠습니다. 그러면 형제께서 편하실 때 말씀하시거나, 제가 말하게 두셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 내가 편할 때 하겠다—네 시간을 내게 내줄 것 없어.\" 피터가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 형제께서 죽으실 때는 형제 편하실 때로 잡을 수가 없잖습니까, 형제.\" 와울 부인이 평소의 흐리멍덩한 어조로 입을 떼었다. \"말씀도 못 하시고 누워 계실 때면 낯선 사람들이 곁에 있는 게 지겨워지실 테고, 그러면 저와 제 자식들 생각이 나실 거예요\"—그러나 여기서 그녀의 목소리는, 말 못 하는 형제에게 자기가 갖다 붙인 그 감동적인 생각에 겨워 잠겼으니, 우리 자신을 언급하는 일이란 자연히 마음을 울리게 마련이었다.\n\n\"아니, 안 날 거다.\" 페더스톤 노인이 어깃장을 놓듯 말했다. \"너희 중 누구 생각도 안 할 거야. 유언장은 만들어 놨다고, 말했잖아, 유언장은 다 만들어 놨다고.\" 여기서 그는 빈시 부인 쪽으로 고개를 돌리고 강심제를 좀 더 들이켰다.\n\n\"어떤 사람들은 마땅히 남의 것인 자리를 차지하고 들어앉은 걸 부끄러워할 텐데요.\" 와울 부인이 가느다란 눈을 같은 방향으로 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 누이.\" 솔로몬이 비꼬듯 부드럽게 말했다. \"너랑 나는 곱지도, 잘나지도, 똑똑하지도 못하잖아. 우린 겸손하게 굴며 똑똑한 사람들이 우리 앞에 끼어들도록 내버려 둬야지.\"\n\n프레드는 이를 참을 수 없었다. 그는 일어나 페더스톤 씨를 바라보며 말했다. \"어르신, 친지분들과 단둘이 계시도록 저와 제 어머니가 자리를 비울까요?\"\n\n\"앉아 있으래도.\" 페더스톤 노인이 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"있는 자리에 그대로 있어. 잘 가게, 솔로몬.\" 그는 다시 지팡이를 휘두르려 했으나, 손잡이를 거꾸로 돌려 쥔 터라 이번에는 실패하며 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"잘 가게, 와울 부인. 다시는 오지 마.\"\n\n\"형제, 그래도 저는 아래층에 있겠습니다.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"제 도리를 다할 것이고, 전능하신 분께서 무엇을 허락하실지는 두고 볼 일이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요, 재산이 집안 밖으로 나가는 일에 대해서 말이지요.\" 와울 부인이 이어서 말했다. \"그리고 가업을 이어 갈 견실한 젊은이들이 있는 마당에 말이에요. 하지만 저는 그렇지 못한 자들이 가엾고, 그 어미들이 가엾어요. 잘 계세요, 피터 형제.\"\n\n\"명심하세요, 형제, 저는 형제 다음으로 맏이이고, 형제와 똑같이 처음부터 번창했으며, 페더스톤이라는 이름으로 이미 땅을 가지고 있다는 걸요.\" 솔로몬은 한밤중 잠 못 이루는 시각에 떠오를 법한 생각이라며 그 점에 크게 기대를 걸고 말했다. \"그러나 우선은 작별 인사를 드리지요.\"\n\n페더스톤 노인이 가발을 양옆으로 잡아당기고 입을 한껏 벌려 찡그리며 눈을 감는 것을, 마치 귀먹고 눈먼 사람이 되기로 작정한 듯한 그 모습을 보고서 그들은 서둘러 나갔다.\n\n그럼에도 그들은 날마다 스톤 코트에 와서 아래층 의무의 초소에 앉아, 때로는 낮은 목소리로 느릿느릿 대화를 이어 갔는데, 그 말과 응답이 어찌나 멀리 떨어져 있던지, 누군가 그것을 들었다면 자신이 말하는 자동인형의 소리에 귀 기울이고 있다고 여기며, 그 정교한 기계 장치가 정말로 작동할지, 아니면 한참 동안 태엽을 감다가 멈춰 버리고 잠잠해질지 미심쩍어했을 것이다. 솔로몬과 제인은 빨라지는 것을 한사코 꺼렸을 것이다. 그것이 무엇으로 이어지는지는 담장 너머 요나 형제라는 인물에게서 볼 수 있었으니까.\n\n그러나 징두리널을 댄 거실에서 그들이 망보는 일은 때때로 멀고 가까운 곳에서 온 다른 손님들의 존재로 변화를 맞았다. 피터 페더스톤이 위층에 있게 된 지금, 그의 재산은 현장에서 얻을 수 있는 온갖 지역적 식견을 동원하여 논의될 수 있었다. 시골과 미들마치의 이웃들 가운데 더러는 가족의 의견에 크게 동의하며 빈시가에 맞선 그들의 이해에 공감을 표했고, 여성 방문객들은 와울 부인과 이야기를 나누다, 마땅히 더 나은 일을 위해 명을 부지하고 있으리라 여겨질 법한 은혜를 모르는 노신사들이 심술궂게 유언 보충서를 만들거나 결혼을 해 버리는 통에 자기들도 지난날 실망했던 사실을 떠올리며 눈물을 흘리기까지 했다. 그런 대화는 메리 가스가 방에 들어오면, 풀무에서 손을 놓아 버린 오르간처럼 뚝 멎었고, 모든 눈이 잠재적 유산 상속인일지도 모를, 아니면 무쇠 궤짝에 접근할 수 있을지도 모를 사람으로서 그녀에게 쏠렸다.\n\n그러나 가족의 친척이거나 인척인 젊은 남자들은, 이런 미심쩍은 처지에 놓인 그녀를, 처신이 반듯하고, 이리저리 날아다니는 온갖 기회 가운데 적어도 적당한 상품쯤은 될지도 모를 처녀로 보아 흠모하는 쪽이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그녀 몫의 찬사와 정중한 관심을 받았다.\n\n특히 그 지방의 저명한 독신남이자 경매인인 보스럽 트럼불 씨가 그러했는데, 그는 토지와 가축의 매매에 깊이 관여한 인물이었다—실로 널리 뿌려진 벽보에서 그 이름을 볼 수 있는 공인이었으니, 자기를 모르는 자들을 딱하게 여길 만도 했다. 그는 피터 페더스톤의 육촌으로, 사업적인 일에 유용했던 까닭에 다른 어느 친척보다도 호의적인 대접을 받아 왔고, 노인이 직접 지시해 둔 장례식 절차에서 운구인으로 지명되어 있었다. 보스럽 트럼불 씨에게는 가증스러운 탐욕 따위는 없었다—자기 자신의 가치에 대한 진실한 의식 말고는 아무것도 없었는데, 그는 경쟁이 붙을 경우 그 가치가 경쟁자들에게 불리하게 작용하리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러니 자기, 트럼불에 관한 한 더없이 선량한 사람처럼 행동해 온 피터 페더스톤이 그에게 무언가 후한 처사를 베푼다 해도, 자기로서는 결코 알랑거리거나 아첨한 적 없이 그저 자신의 경험을 다해 그에게 조언했을 뿐이라고밖에 할 말이 없을 터였다. 그 경험은 이제 열다섯 살에 도제 생활을 시작한 때로부터 이십 년에 걸쳐 있었으니, 떳떳하지 못한 종류의 지식을 내놓을 리 없는 경험이었다. 그의 흠모는 결코 자기 자신에게만 국한되지 않았으니, 직업적으로나 사적으로나 무엇이든 높이 평가하기를 즐기는 데 익숙했다. 그는 고상한 표현의 애호가여서, 변변찮은 말을 쓸 때면 곧바로 스스로 고치곤 했다—이는 다행한 일이었으니, 그는 다소 목소리가 크고 좌중을 압도하는 경향이 있어, 자주 서거나 걸어 다니고, 자기 의견이 확고한 사람다운 태도로 조끼를 끌어내리며, 집게손가락으로 재빨리 옷매무새를 가다듬고, 이런 동작들의 새로운 단계마다 큼직한 인장들을 분주히 만지작거려 표시하곤 했다. 이따금 그의 태도에는 약간의 사나움이 깃들기도 했지만, 그것은 주로 그릇된 견해를 겨눈 것이었으니, 세상에는 바로잡아야 할 그릇된 견해가 어찌나 많은지 어지간히 책을 읽고 경험을 쌓은 사람이라면 필연코 그 인내심을 시험당하게 마련이었다. 그는 페더스톤 가문이 대체로 이해력이 한정된 사람들이라고 느꼈지만, 세상 물정에 밝은 공인답게 모든 것을 당연한 일로 받아들였고, 심지어 부엌으로 가서 요나 씨와 젊은 크랜치와 담소를 나누기까지 했는데, 초크 플랫에 관한 유도 질문들로 후자에게 크게 깊은 인상을 남겼다고 믿어 의심치 않았다. 만일 누군가가 보스럽 트럼불 씨는 경매인이니 무엇이든 그 본질을 알아야 마땅하다고 한다면, 그는 미소를 지으며 자기가 그에 꽤 가깝다는 의식과 함께 말없이 옷매무새를 가다듬었을 것이다. 대체로 그는 경매인다운 방식으로 명예로운 사람이었고, 자기 직업을 부끄러워하지 않았으며, \"이제는 로버트 경이 된 저 유명한 필\" 씨가 자신에게 소개된다면 틀림없이 자신의 중요함을 알아보리라고 여겼다.\n\n\"저 햄을 한 조각 먹고 저 에일을 한 잔 마셔도 괜찮다면, 가스 양, 그렇게 하겠소.\" 그는 예외적으로 페더스톤 노인을 보는 특권을 누린 뒤 열한 시 반에 거실로 들어와, 와울 부인과 솔로몬 사이에서 난롯불을 등지고 서서 말했다.\n\n\"나가실 필요 없습니다—제가 종을 울리지요.\"\n\n\"고맙소.\" 메리가 말했다. \"심부름 갈 일이 있어서요.\"\n\n\"흠, 트럼불 씨, 큰 은총을 입으셨군요.\" 와울 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요! 노인을 만난 것 말이오?\" 경매인이 무심하게 인장을 만지작거리며 말했다. \"아, 보시다시피 그분은 저를 상당히 의지하셨지요.\" 여기서 그는 입술을 꾹 다물고 생각에 잠긴 듯 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"형제분께서 무슨 말씀을 하셨는지 누구라도 여쭤봐도 될까요?\" 솔로몬이 부드럽고 겸손한 어조로 말했는데, 그 안에는 부유해서 그럴 필요도 없는 사람이 누리는 호사스러운 교활함의 의식이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n\"오, 그렇소, 누구든 물어도 좋소.\" 트럼불 씨가 크고 호탕하면서도 날카로운 비아냥을 담아 말했다. \"누구든 캐물어도 좋고. 누구든 자기 말을 의문형으로 돌려도 좋지요.\" 그는 어조에 따라 점점 더 낭랑해지며 말을 이었다. \"이는 훌륭한 연설가들이 답을 기대하지 않을 때에도 늘 하는 일이지요. 우리가 수사적 표현이라 부르는 겁니다—말하자면, 비싼 값으로 하는 표현 말이오.\" 능변의 경매인은 자기 재치에 흐뭇이 미소를 지었다.\n* 수사적 표현: 원문은 'figure of speech'를 'speech at a high figure'(높은 값으로 하는 말)로 비튼 말장난이다.\n\n\"형제분께서 당신을 기억해 주셨다는 말을 들어도 저는 섭섭지 않겠습니다, 트럼불 씨.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"저는 받을 만한 사람을 마다한 적이 없습니다. 제가 마다하는 건 받을 만하지 못한 자들이지요.\"\n\n\"아, 바로 그겁니다, 보세요, 바로 그거예요.\" 트럼불 씨가 의미심장하게 말했다. \"받을 만하지 못한 사람들이 유산 상속인이, 심지어 잔여 재산 상속인이 되어 온 것은 부인할 수 없는 사실이지요. 유언에 의한 처분이란 게 그런 겁니다.\" 그는 다시금 입술을 오므리고 살짝 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"트럼불 씨, 제 형제가 자기 땅을 우리 집안에서 떼어 내 남에게 남겼다는 걸 확실히 말씀하시는 건가요?\" 와울 부인이 말했는데, 희망 없는 여인인 그녀에게 그 긴 단어들은 의기소침하게 만드는 효과가 있었다.\n\n\"어떤 사람들에게 땅을 남기느니, 차라리 단번에 자선용 토지로 바꿔 버리는 편이 나을 거요.\" 솔로몬이, 누이의 물음에 아무 대답이 나오지 않자 한마디 했다.\n\n\"뭐라고요, 자선 학교 땅으로 말인가요?\" 와울 부인이 다시 말했다. \"오, 트럼불 씨, 설마 그런 말씀을 하시는 건 아니겠지요. 그건 그분을 번창케 하신 전능하신 분의 뜻을 거스르는 일이 될 거예요.\"\n\n와울 부인이 말하는 동안, 보스럽 트럼불 씨는 난롯가에서 창가 쪽으로 걸어가며, 집게손가락으로 옷깃 안쪽을 한 바퀴 돌리고, 이어 구레나룻과 곱슬머리의 굴곡을 따라 쓸어내렸다. 이제 그는 가스 양의 반짇고리 탁자로 걸어가, 거기 놓인 책 한 권을 펴서, 마치 그것을 경매에 내놓기라도 하는 듯 거드름을 피우며 제목을 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n\"'가이어슈타인의 안네'(지어스틴이라 발음하오) 혹은 '안개의 처녀, 「웨이벌리」의 저자 지음.'\" 그러고는 책장을 넘기며 낭랑하게 시작했다—\"다음 장들에서 서술되는 일련의 사건들이 대륙에서 일어난 이래 사백 년의 세월이 흐른 셈이다.\" 그는 마지막의 참으로 멋진 단어를 마지막 음절에 강세를 주어 발음했는데, 통속적인 발음을 모르는 게 아니라, 이 색다른 발음이 자기 낭독이 전체에 부여한 낭랑한 아름다움을 한층 돋우어 준다고 느꼈기 때문이다.\n* 대륙: 원문 'Continent'를 트럼불은 마지막 음절에 엉뚱하게 강세를 두어 'Continént'로 발음한다.\n\n마침 그때 하인이 쟁반을 들고 들어왔고, 그리하여 와울 부인의 물음에 답할 순간들은 무사히 지나가 버렸으며, 그녀와 솔로몬은 트럼불 씨의 동작을 지켜보며, 높은 학식이란 진지한 일에 딱하게도 방해가 되는구나 하고 생각하고 있었다. 보스럽 트럼불 씨는 페더스톤 노인의 유언에 관해 실은 아무것도 몰랐다. 그러나 반역 은닉죄로 체포되지 않는 한, 그가 무지를 고백하게 만들기란 도무지 불가능했을 것이다.\n\n\"햄은 그저 한 입, 에일은 한 잔만 들겠소.\" 그는 안심시키듯 말했다. \"공무를 보는 사람이라 틈날 때 요기를 해 두지요. 이 햄으로 말하자면,\" 그는 위태로울 만큼 황급히 몇 점을 삼키고 나서 덧붙였다, \"세 왕국의 어떤 햄과 견주어도 이 햄에 걸겠소. 내 보기엔 프레시트 홀의 햄보다도 낫소—그리고 내가 웬만큼은 감식가라고 생각하오만.\"\n\n\"어떤 사람들은 햄에 설탕을 그렇게 많이 넣는 걸 안 좋아하지요.\" 와울 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 제 가엾은 형제는 늘 설탕을 넣곤 했어요.\"\n\n\"더 나은 걸 요구하는 사람이 있다면 마음대로 하라지요. 하지만 맙소사, 이 향이라니! 정말이지 나라면 이런 품질을 사들이고 싶을 거요. 신사에게는 어떤 흡족함이 있단 말이오\"—여기서 트럼불 씨의 목소리는 감정이 북받친 항변을 담았다—\"이런 햄을 식탁에 차려 놓는 데에 말이오.\"\n\n그는 접시를 옆으로 밀어 놓고, 에일을 잔에 따른 뒤, 의자를 약간 앞으로 끌어당기며, 그 기회를 빌려 제 다리 안쪽을 들여다보고는 흡족하게 쓰다듬었다—트럼불 씨에게는 북방의 우월한 종족들을 구별 짓는, 그 모든 덜 경박한 태도와 몸짓이 두루 갖추어져 있었다.\n\n\"흥미로운 책을 가지고 계시는군요, 가스 양.\" 메리가 다시 들어오자 그가 말했다. \"이건 「웨이벌리」의 저자가 쓴 것이오. 다름 아닌 월터 스콧 경이지. 나도 그의 작품 하나를 사 둔 게 있소—아주 근사한 것으로, 매우 뛰어난 출판물인데, 제목이 「아이반호」요. 내 생각엔 당분간 그를 능가할 작가는 쉬 나오지 않을 거요—내 보기에 그는 머잖아 추월당하지는 않을 거란 말이오. 방금 막 「지어스틴의 안네」의 첫머리 한 대목을 읽던 참인데. 시작이 좋소.\" (보스럽 트럼불 씨에게는 무엇도 그저 '시작'되는 법이 없었다. 그것은 사생활에서나 그의 전단지에서나 늘 '개시'되었다.) \"보아하니 독서가시구려. 우리 미들마치 도서관에 가입하셨소?\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 메리가 말했다. \"이 책은 프레드 빈시 씨가 가져온 거예요.\"\n\n\"나로 말하면 굉장한 장서가요.\" 트럼불 씨가 대꾸했다. \"송아지 가죽으로 장정한 책이 자그마치 이백 권은 되고, 자부하건대 잘 골라 모은 것들이지. 게다가 무리요, 루벤스, 테니르스, 티치아노, 반다이크 등의 그림도 있소. 가스 양, 말씀만 하시면 어떤 작품이든 기꺼이 빌려 드리리다.\"\n\n\"대단히 감사합니다.\" 메리가 다시 서둘러 자리를 뜨며 말했다. \"하지만 저는 책 읽을 시간이 거의 없어서요.\"\n\n\"제 형제가 유언으로 저 여자에게 뭔가 해 줬을 것 같소만.\" 메리가 등 뒤로 문을 닫고 나가자, 솔로몬 씨가 자리에 없는 메리 쪽으로 고개를 까딱이며 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그래도 형제의 첫 부인은 그분에게 어울리지 않는 짝이었어요.\" 와울 부인이 말했다. \"아무것도 가져온 게 없었지요. 게다가 이 젊은 여자는 그 부인의 조카에 불과한데다—몹시 도도하고요. 그리고 제 형제는 늘 저 여자에게 품삯을 줘 왔어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 생각엔 분별 있는 처녀요.\" 트럼불 씨가 에일을 다 마시고는 조끼를 힘주어 매만지며 벌떡 일어나 말했다. \"약을 방울로 섞는 걸 내가 지켜본 적이 있소. 자기가 하는 일에 마음을 쓰더이다. 그건 여자에게 큰 장점이고, 위층의 우리 친구, 가엾고 다정한 노인분께도 큰 다행이지. 목숨에 조금이라도 가치가 있는 사내라면 아내를 간호사로 생각해야 하는 법이오. 나라면, 결혼한다면 그렇게 하겠소. 그리고 내 믿건대 나는 이 방면에서 실수하지 않을 만큼은 충분히 오래 독신으로 살아왔소. 어떤 사내들은 제 처지를 좀 끌어올리려고 결혼해야 하지만, 내가 그게 필요할 때면 누군가 내게 그렇다고 말해 주기를 바라오—누군가 그 사실을 내게 일러 주기를 바란단 말이오. 그럼 안녕히 계시오, 와울 부인. 안녕히 계시오, 솔로몬 씨. 다음번엔 덜 우울한 자리에서 뵙기를 바라오.\"\n\n트럼불 씨가 멋들어진 인사를 남기고 떠나자, 솔로몬은 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 누이에게 말했다. \"제인, 분명히 말하건대, 내 형제가 저 처녀한테 큰돈을 남겼어.\"\n\n\"트럼불 씨가 말하는 걸 보면 누구라도 그렇게 생각하겠지요.\" 제인이 말했다. 그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가, \"마치 내 딸들은 약방울 하나 맡길 수 없다는 식으로 말하던데요.\"\n\n\"경매인들은 말을 함부로 하지.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"하긴 트럼불이 돈을 벌긴 했다만.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIII.\n\n“그의 두 눈을 감기고 휘장을 단단히 드리워라.\n그리고 우리 모두 묵상에 잠기자.”\n\n—『헨리 6세』 제2부.\n그날 밤 자정이 지난 뒤 메리 가스는 페더스톤 씨의 방에서 간병을 교대하고는, 새벽의 그 적막한 시간 내내 홀로 그곳에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 종종 이 일을 자청했는데, 노인이 시중을 요구할 때마다 부려 대는 짜증에도 불구하고 거기서 어떤 즐거움을 찾았기 때문이다. 그녀가 완전히 가만히 앉아 바깥의 고요와 은은한 불빛을 만끽할 수 있는 짬도 있었다. 부드럽게 사각거리는 소리를 내며 타오르는 붉은 불은 그녀가 날마다 경멸하던 시시한 격정, 어리석은 욕망, 하잘것없는 불확실함을 향한 안간힘 따위로부터 침착하게 독립한, 어떤 엄숙한 존재처럼 보였다. 메리는 자기만의 생각을 즐겼고, 무릎에 손을 얹은 채 어스름 속에 앉아서도 능히 스스로를 즐겁게 할 수 있었다. 일찍이 세상사가 자신의 별난 만족에 맞게 배열되지는 않으리라고 믿을 만한 강력한 이유를 알게 된 터라, 그녀는 그 사실에 놀라거나 짜증을 내며 시간을 허비하지 않았다. 그리고 그녀는 이미 인생을 하나의 희극으로 받아들이게 되었으니, 그 안에서 자신은 비열하거나 배신적인 역할을 맡지 않겠다는 당당한, 아니 관대한 결심을 품고 있었다. 메리는 자신이 존경하는 부모와, 내면에 솟아나는 애정 어린 감사의 샘이 없었다면 냉소적인 사람이 되었을지도 모른다. 그 샘은 그녀가 부당한 요구를 하지 않는 법을 배웠기에 더욱 가득 차 있었다.\n\n오늘 밤 그녀는 늘 그러하듯 그날의 장면들을 곱씹으며 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 상상이 새로운 익살을 보태는 기묘한 일들에 종종 입가에 즐거운 미소를 띠었다. 사람들은 그 환상으로 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지, 자기도 모르게 바보의 고깔모자를 쓰고 다니며, 남의 거짓말은 다 들여다보이는데 제 거짓말만은 불투명하다고 여기고, 매사에 자신만은 예외인 것처럼 굴었다. 마치 등불 아래 온 세상이 노랗게 보일 때 저 혼자만 장밋빛이라는 듯이. 그러나 메리의 눈앞에는 그녀에게 그다지 우습지만은 않은 환상들도 있었다. 그녀는 늙은 페더스톤의 성정을 가까이서 관찰한 것 말고는 다른 근거가 없었음에도, 그가 빈시 식구들을 곁에 두기를 좋아하긴 해도 그들 또한 그가 멀리하는 다른 친척들과 다름없이 실망할 공산이 크다고 남몰래 확신하고 있었다. 그녀는 자신과 프레드가 단둘이 있을까 봐 빈시 부인이 노골적으로 경계하는 것을 상당히 경멸했지만, 그렇다고 해서 만일 삼촌이 프레드를 여전히 빈털터리로 남겨 둔 것으로 판명될 경우 그가 어떻게 영향을 받을지 근심스레 생각하는 것을 막지는 못했다. 그녀는 프레드가 곁에 있을 때면 그를 놀림감으로 삼을 수 있었지만, 그가 없을 때는 그의 어리석음을 즐기지 못했다.\n\n그래도 그녀는 자신의 생각을 좋아했다. 격정에 지나치게 휘둘리지 않는 활기찬 젊은 정신은 인생과 친분을 맺는 데서 보람을 찾고, 자기 자신의 역량을 흥미롭게 지켜보는 법이다. 메리의 내면에는 유쾌함이 가득했다.\n\n침대에 누운 노인에 대한 그녀의 생각에는 어떤 엄숙함이나 비애도 서려 있지 않았다. 그런 감정이란 삶이 보아하니 악덕의 잔재일 뿐인 늙은 존재에 대해 실제로 느끼기보다 꾸며 내기가 더 쉬운 법이다. 그녀는 늘 페더스톤 씨의 가장 못마땅한 면만을 보아 왔다. 그는 그녀를 자랑스러워하지 않았고, 그녀는 그에게 그저 쓸모 있는 존재일 뿐이었다. 언제나 당신에게 으르렁대는 영혼을 두고 근심하는 일은 이 세상의 성인들에게나 맡겨 둘 일이다. 그리고 메리는 그런 성인이 아니었다. 그녀는 그에게 한 번도 모진 말을 돌려준 적이 없었고, 충실히 시중을 들었다. 그것이 그녀가 할 수 있는 최선이었다. 늙은 페더스톤 자신은 제 영혼에 대해 조금도 근심하지 않았고, 그 문제로 터커 씨를 만나는 것도 거절했다.\n\n오늘 밤 그는 으르렁대지 않았고, 처음 한두 시간 동안은 놀라울 만큼 가만히 누워 있었다. 그러다 마침내 메리는 그가 늘 침대 곁에 두는 양철 상자에 열쇠 꾸러미를 절그럭거리며 부딪치는 소리를 들었다. 세 시쯤 그가 놀랄 만큼 또렷한 목소리로 말했다. “아가씨, 이리 와!”\n\n메리는 시키는 대로 했고, 그가 평소에는 누군가에게 시켜 꺼내 달라고 하던 양철 상자를 벌써 이불 밑에서 끌어내 놓았으며, 게다가 열쇠까지 골라 둔 것을 보았다. 그는 이제 상자를 열고 그 안에서 또 다른 열쇠를 꺼내더니, 본래의 날카로움을 모두 되찾은 듯한 눈으로 그녀를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. “이 집에 그것들이 몇이나 있느냐?”\n\n“어르신의 친척분들을 말씀하시는 거지요.” 노인의 말투에 익숙한 메리가 말했다. 그가 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이자 그녀는 말을 이었다.\n\n“조나 페더스톤 씨와 젊은 크랜치가 여기서 자고 있어요.”\n\n“오 그래, 그것들이 들러붙어 있단 말이지? 그리고 나머지는—날마다 오겠지, 내 장담하건대—솔로몬과 제인, 그리고 어린것들까지 죄다? 기웃거리며 와서 세어 보고 따져 보겠지?”\n\n“다들 매일 오는 건 아니에요. 솔로몬 씨와 와울 부인은 매일 오시고, 다른 분들은 자주 오세요.”\n\n노인은 그녀가 말하는 동안 얼굴을 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 듣더니, 이윽고 표정을 누그러뜨리며 말했다. “그것들 멍청하기도 하지. 잘 들어라, 아가씨. 지금 새벽 세 시인데, 나는 평생 어느 때 못지않게 정신이 또렷하다. 내 재산이 전부 어디에 있는지, 돈을 어디에 빌려줬는지, 그 모든 걸 다 알고 있어. 그리고 나는 마음을 바꿔서 마지막에 내 뜻대로 할 수 있도록 만반의 준비를 해 놓았다. 알아듣겠느냐, 아가씨? 나는 정신이 멀쩡해.”\n\n“네, 어르신?” 메리가 조용히 말했다.\n\n그는 이제 한층 음흉한 기색을 띠며 목소리를 낮추었다. “나는 유언장을 두 개 만들어 놓았고, 그중 하나를 태워 버릴 참이다. 자, 내가 시키는 대로 해라. 이건 저 벽장 안에 있는 내 쇠궤의 열쇠다. 맨 위에 있는 놋쇠 판의 옆쪽을 빗장처럼 미끄러질 때까지 잘 밀어라. 그러면 정면 자물쇠에 열쇠를 꽂아 돌릴 수 있다. 잘 보고 그대로 해라. 그리고 맨 위에 있는 서류를 꺼내라—유언장이라고, 큼직하게 인쇄된 거다.”\n\n“아니요, 어르신.” 메리가 단호한 목소리로 말했다. “저는 그 일을 할 수 없어요.”\n\n“못 하겠다고? 내가 말하잖느냐, 너는 해야 한다.” 노인의 목소리가 이 저항의 충격으로 떨리기 시작했다.\n\n“저는 어르신의 쇠궤도 유언장도 손댈 수 없어요. 저를 의심받게 할 만한 일은 무엇이든 거절하겠어요.”\n\n“내가 말하잖느냐, 나는 정신이 멀쩡하다. 마지막에 내 뜻대로 하지도 못한단 말이냐? 일부러 유언장을 두 개나 만들었어. 열쇠를 받아라, 어서.”\n\n“아니요, 어르신, 그러지 않겠어요.” 메리가 더욱 단호하게 말했다. 그녀의 거부감은 점점 강해지고 있었다.\n\n“말하잖느냐, 지체할 시간이 없다.”\n\n“그건 제가 어쩔 수 없어요, 어르신. 어르신 인생의 끝자락이 제 인생의 시작을 더럽히게 두지 않겠어요. 저는 어르신의 쇠궤도 유언장도 손대지 않겠어요.” 그녀는 침대 곁에서 조금 떨어진 곳으로 물러섰다.\n\n노인은 한동안 멍한 눈으로 말을 멈춘 채, 고리에 꿰인 열쇠 하나를 곧추세워 들고 있었다. 그러다 격하게 몸을 홱 움직이더니 앙상한 왼손으로 자기 앞의 양철 상자를 비우기 시작했다.\n\n“아가씨,” 그가 다급하게 말을 꺼냈다. “여기 봐라! 이 돈을 가져라—지폐와 금화를—여기 봐라—가지라니까—전부 다 네 것으로 해 주마—내가 시키는 대로 해라.”\n\n그는 가능한 한 멀리 그녀를 향해 열쇠를 내밀려고 안간힘을 썼고, 메리는 다시 물러섰다.\n\n“저는 어르신의 열쇠도 돈도 손대지 않겠어요, 어르신. 제발 다시는 그런 부탁 하지 마세요. 그러시면 저는 가서 동생분을 불러야 해요.”\n\n그가 손을 떨어뜨렸고, 메리는 난생처음으로 늙은 피터 페더스톤이 어린아이처럼 울기 시작하는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 자신이 낼 수 있는 한 부드러운 어조로 말했다. “제발 돈을 도로 넣어 두세요, 어르신.” 그러고는 더 말해 봐야 소용없다는 것을 그가 납득하는 데 도움이 되기를 바라며 난롯가 자기 자리로 돌아갔다. 이윽고 그가 기운을 차리더니 간절하게 말했다.\n\n“그럼 이거 봐라. 그 젊은 녀석을 불러라. 프레드 빈시를 불러.”\n\n메리의 심장이 더 빠르게 뛰기 시작했다. 두 번째 유언장을 태운다는 것이 무엇을 의미할지 갖가지 생각이 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다. 그녀는 급히 어려운 결정을 내려야 했다.\n\n“그 사람을 부르되, 조나 씨와 다른 분들도 함께 부르게 해 주신다면 부르겠어요.”\n\n“다른 누구도 안 돼, 내 말했다. 그 젊은 녀석만이다. 나는 내 뜻대로 할 거야.”\n\n“환한 대낮이 될 때까지, 다들 일어나 움직일 때까지 기다리세요, 어르신. 아니면 지금 시먼스를 불러 변호사를 데려오게 할까요? 두 시간도 안 되어 여기 올 수 있어요.”\n\n“변호사? 변호사가 내게 무슨 소용이냐? 아무도 알아선 안 돼—말하잖느냐, 아무도 몰라야 한다. 나는 내 뜻대로 할 거야.”\n\n“다른 누구라도 부르게 해 주세요, 어르신.” 메리가 달래듯 말했다. 그녀는 자신의 처지가 마음에 들지 않았다. 노인과 단둘이 있는데, 그는 평소의 기침에 빠지지 않고 거듭거듭 말을 할 수 있게 하는 어떤 기이한 신경의 불꽃을 내보이는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 동요시키는 반발을 공연히 부추기고 싶지 않았다. “제발, 다른 누구라도 부르게 해 주세요.”\n\n“나를 가만히 내버려 두래도. 이거 봐라, 아가씨. 이 돈을 가져라. 다시는 이런 기회가 없을 거다. 거의 이백 파운드는 될 거야—상자 안에 더 있고, 얼마나 들었는지는 아무도 몰라. 이걸 가지고 내가 시키는 대로 해라.”\n\n난롯가에 서 있던 메리는 붉은 불빛이, 베개와 등받이에 몸을 기댄 채 앙상한 손으로 열쇠를 내밀고 있는 노인과 그의 앞 누비이불 위에 놓인 돈 위로 떨어지는 것을 보았다. 마지막에 제 뜻대로 하고 싶어 하는 한 인간의 그 광경을 그녀는 결코 잊지 못했다. 그러나 그가 돈을 내미는 그 방식이 도리어 그녀를 그 어느 때보다 단호하게 말하도록 몰아붙였다.\n\n“소용없어요, 어르신. 저는 하지 않겠어요. 돈을 도로 넣으세요. 저는 어르신 돈에 손대지 않겠어요. 어르신을 위로하기 위해서라면 제가 할 수 있는 다른 일은 무엇이든 하겠어요. 하지만 어르신의 열쇠도 돈도 손대지 않겠어요.”\n\n“다른 일이라—다른 일이라니!” 늙은 페더스톤이 쉰 목소리로 격노하여 외쳤는데, 마치 악몽 속에서처럼 크게 소리치려 했으나 가까스로 들릴 정도에 그쳤다. “나는 다른 건 아무것도 바라지 않아. 이리 와—이리 오래도.”\n\n메리는 그를 너무도 잘 알기에 조심스럽게 다가갔다. 그가 열쇠를 떨어뜨리고 지팡이를 움켜쥐려 애쓰는 것이 보였고, 손에 힘을 주느라 얼굴 근육이 일그러진 채 늙은 하이에나처럼 그녀를 노려보았다. 그녀는 안전한 거리에서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n“강심제를 좀 드릴게요.” 그녀가 조용히 말했다. “그리고 마음을 진정시키려 해 보세요. 그러면 잠이 드실지도 몰라요. 그리고 내일 날이 밝으면 뜻대로 하시면 돼요.”\n\n그는 그녀가 자기 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있는데도 지팡이를 들어, 무력하기 짝이 없는 안간힘으로 그것을 던졌다. 지팡이는 침대 발치를 넘어 미끄러지며 떨어졌다. 메리는 그것을 그대로 둔 채 난롯가 의자로 물러났다. 잠시 후에 강심제를 가지고 그에게 갈 작정이었다. 피로가 그를 잠잠하게 만들 터였다. 아침 중 가장 쌀쌀한 순간이 다가오고 있었고, 불은 사그라들었으며, 모린 천 창문 휘장 사이의 틈으로 블라인드에 의해 희뿌예진 빛이 보였다. 불에 장작을 좀 얹고 어깨에 숄을 두른 다음, 그녀는 이제 페더스톤 씨가 잠들기를 바라며 자리에 앉았다. 그에게 가까이 가면 짜증이 계속될지도 몰랐다. 지팡이를 던진 후로 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 그녀는 그가 다시 열쇠를 집어 들고 오른손을 돈 위에 얹는 것을 보았다. 그러나 그는 돈을 도로 넣지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 잠에 빠져들고 있다고 생각했다.\n\n하지만 메리 자신은, 실제 겪을 때보다도 자신이 겪어 낸 일을 떠올리며 더 동요하기 시작했다. 그 위급한 순간에 어쩔 수 없이 닥쳐와 일체의 망설임을 배제했던 자신의 그 행동들을 이제 와 곱씹는 것이었다.\n\n이윽고 마른 장작이 불길을 피워 올려 온갖 틈새를 환히 비추었고, 메리는 노인이 고개를 한쪽으로 약간 돌린 채 조용히 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 발소리를 죽이며 그에게 다가갔고, 그의 얼굴이 기이할 만큼 미동도 없어 보인다고 생각했다. 그러나 다음 순간 모든 사물에 옮겨 붙은 불길의 일렁임 때문에 확신할 수 없었다. 심장이 격렬하게 뛰는 탓에 지각이 어찌나 미덥지 못했던지, 그를 만지고 숨소리에 귀를 기울일 때조차 그녀는 자신의 판단을 믿을 수 없었다. 그녀는 창가로 가서 휘장과 블라인드를 살며시 밀어젖혀, 고요한 하늘빛이 침대 위로 떨어지게 했다.\n\n다음 순간 그녀는 종으로 달려가 힘차게 울렸다. 아주 잠깐 사이에 피터 페더스톤이 죽었다는 사실에는 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그의 오른손은 열쇠를 움켜쥐고 있었고, 왼손은 지폐와 금화 무더기 위에 놓여 있었다.\n\n# BOOK IV. THREE LOVE PROBLEMS. CHAPTER XXXIV.\n\n“첫째 신사. 이런 자들은 깃털이요, 나뭇조각이며, 지푸라기라,\n무게도 지니지 않고, 힘도 없다네.\n\n둘째 신사. 허나 가벼움 또한\n하나의 원인이 되어, 무게의 총합을 이루나니.\n\n권능은 권능의 결핍 속에서 제자리를 찾고,\n나아감이란 곧 물러섬이라, 떠밀려 가는 배도\n\n좌초할 수 있으니, 키잡이의 생각이\n상반된 것을 저울질할 힘이 모자란 까닭이라.”\n\n피터 페더스톤이 묻힌 것은 어느 오월의 아침이었다. 따분한 미들마치 인근에서 오월이 늘 따뜻하고 화창한 것은 아니어서, 바로 그날 아침에는 차가운 바람이 둘레의 정원에서 꽃잎을 불어와 로윅 교회 묘지의 푸른 봉분 위로 흩뿌렸다. 빠르게 흘러가는 구름은 이따금씩만 한 줄기 빛을 허락하여, 그 황금빛 소나기 속에 우연히 서 있게 된 것이라면 추하든 아름답든 가리지 않고 비추었다. 교회 묘지에 서 있는 것들은 유난히 다채로웠으니, 장례식을 구경하려고 기다리는 시골 사람들이 자그마하게 무리를 이루고 있었기 때문이다. 그것이 “성대한 장례식”이 될 것이라는 소문이 퍼져 있었다. 노신사가 모든 일에 대해 글로 지시를 남겨 놓았고, “제 윗사람들을 능가하는” 장례식을 치를 작정이었던 것이다. 사실이 그러했다. 늙은 페더스톤은 저축이라는 늘 야위고 늘 굶주린 열정에 다른 모든 열정을 잡아먹힌 인색한 수전노 같은 자가 아니었고, 미리 장의사와 흥정을 벌일 위인도 아니었다. 그는 돈을 사랑했으나, 자신의 별난 취향을 만족시키는 데 돈을 쓰는 것도 사랑했고, 어쩌면 무엇보다도 남들이 자신의 권력을 다소 불편하게 느끼도록 만드는 수단으로서 돈을 가장 사랑했는지도 모른다. 여기서 누군가가 늙은 페더스톤에게도 선한 면모가 분명히 있었을 것이라고 주장한다면, 나는 감히 그것을 부정하지는 않겠다. 다만 선이란 본성이 겸손하여 쉽게 기가 꺾이는 것이고, 삶의 초기에 뻔뻔한 악덕들에게 밀려나 많은 은밀함 속에 갇히면 극단적인 은밀함 속으로 물러나 버리기 쉽다는 점은 짚어 두어야겠다. 그리하여 그 선은 이론적으로 이기적인 노신사를 구성해 보는 이들에게는 더 쉽게 믿어지지만, 그와 개인적으로 알고 지내며 더 좁은 판단을 내리는 이들에게는 그렇지 못하다. 어쨌거나 그는 번듯한 장례식을 치르겠다고, 그리고 차라리 집에 있고 싶어 할 사람들을 그 자리에 “초대해” 부르겠다고 마음먹고 있었다. 그는 심지어 여자 친척들이 자기를 무덤까지 따라오기를 바랐으니, 가엾은 누이 마사는 이 목적을 위해 초크 플랫에서 힘든 여정을 거쳐 왔다. 그녀와 제인은, 살아 있는 동안에는 그들을 보기 싫어하던 오라비가 유언자가 되었을 때를 미리 내다보며 그들의 참석을 흐뭇하게 여겼다는 이 표시에 (눈물을 머금은 채) 적잖이 위안을 받았을 것이다. 다만 그 표시가 빈시 부인에게까지 확대됨으로써 애매하게 흐려지지 않았더라면 말이다. 빈시 부인이 번듯한 검정 크레이프에 들인 비용은 더없이 주제넘은 기대를 암시하는 듯했고, 게다가 그 화사한 혈색은 그녀가 혈연이 아니라 흔히 못마땅하게 여겨지는 처가붙이 부류임을 꽤 분명하게 말해 주어 그 인상을 한층 더 짙게 했다.\n우리는 누구나 어떤 형태로든 상상력을 지니고 있으니, 이미지란 욕망이 낳은 새끼이기 때문이다. 그리고 남들이 스스로를 속이는 방식을 두고 그토록 비웃던 가엾은 늙은 페더스톤도 환상의 동지 관계에서 벗어나지는 못했다. 자기 장례식의 식순을 적으면서, 그는 그것이 일부를 이루는 그 작은 연극에서 느끼는 즐거움이 오로지 예상에만 국한된다는 사실을 분명히 깨닫지 못한 것이 확실하다. 죽은 손의 뻣뻣한 움켜쥠으로 가할 수 있는 괴로움들을 떠올리며 낄낄대는 동안, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 자신의 의식을 그 시퍼렇고 정체된 존재와 뒤섞었고, 그가 내세에 마음을 쓰는 한 그것은 자기 관 속에서 느끼는 만족의 내세였다. 이렇게 늙은 페더스톤은 그 나름의 방식으로 상상력이 풍부했다.\n\n어쨌거나 세 대의 상여 마차는 고인이 글로 남긴 지시에 따라 채워졌다. 말을 탄 운구자들은 더없이 화려한 띠와 모자 띠를 둘렀고, 아래쪽 운구꾼들조차 값나가는 좋은 품질의 애도용 장식을 갖추고 있었다. 그 검은 행렬은 말에서 내리자 교회 묘지가 작은 탓에 한층 커 보였다. 바람 속에 떨리는 묵직한 사람들의 얼굴과 검은 휘장들은, 가볍게 떨어지는 꽃잎이며 데이지 위에 반짝이는 햇살과는 묘하게 어울리지 않는 어떤 세계를 일러 주는 듯했다. 행렬을 맞이한 성직자는 캐드월러더 씨였는데, 이 역시 늘 그렇듯 별난 이유에서 비롯된 피터 페더스톤의 요청에 따른 것이었다. 그는 보좌 신부를 경멸하여 늘 “심부름꾼”이라 불렀던 터라, 성직록을 받는 정식 성직자의 손에 묻히기로 작정했던 것이다. 캐소본 씨는 애초에 고려 대상이 아니었으니, 그가 이런 종류의 직무를 거절했기 때문만이 아니라, 페더스톤이 자기 교구의 교구장으로서 십일조라는 형태로 토지에 대한 권리를 쥐고 있는 그를 특별히 싫어했기 때문이다. 또한 캐소본 씨는 아침 설교를 하는 사람이기도 했으니, 노인은 자기 자리에 앉아 조금도 졸리지 않은 채로 속으로 으르렁대며 그 설교를 끝까지 들어야만 했던 것이다. 그는 목사가 자기 머리 위에 우뚝 서서 설교하는 것을 못마땅하게 여겼다. 그러나 캐드월러더 씨와의 관계는 다른 종류였다. 캐소본 씨의 땅을 가로지르는 송어 개울이 페더스톤의 땅으로도 흘러갔으니, 그리하여 캐드월러더 씨는 설교를 하기는커녕 부탁을 해야 했던 목사였던 것이다. 게다가 그는 로윅에서 사 마일 떨어진 곳에 사는 상류 신사 중 한 사람이었으므로, 카운티의 주장관이나 사물의 질서에 막연히 필요하다고 여겨지는 다른 고관들과 동등한 하늘로 떠받들어졌다. 캐드월러더 씨의 손에 묻히는 데에는 만족스러운 구석이 있었으니, 그 이름 자체가 마음만 먹으면 틀리게 발음하기 좋은 멋진 기회를 제공했기 때문이다.\n\n팁턴과 프레시트의 교구장에게 부여된 이 영예야말로 캐드월러더 부인이 영지 저택의 위층 창가에서 늙은 페더스톤의 장례식을 지켜보는 무리에 끼게 된 이유였다. 그녀는 그 집을 방문하는 것을 좋아하지 않았으나, 본인 말마따나 이번 장례식에 모일 법한 별난 동물들의 진열을 구경하는 것은 즐겨 했다. 그래서 그녀는 제임스 경과 젊은 채텀 부인을 설득해, 목사와 자신을 로윅까지 마차로 태워다 달라고 하여 그 방문이 전적으로 즐거운 나들이가 되게 했다.\n\n“부인이 가시는 곳이라면 어디든 따라가겠어요, 캐드월러더 부인.” 실리아가 말했다. “하지만 저는 장례식이 싫어요.”\n\n“오, 얘야, 집안에 성직자가 있으면 취향을 맞춰 가야 한단다. 나는 아주 일찍부터 그렇게 했지. 험프리와 결혼했을 때 나는 설교를 좋아하기로 마음먹었고, 우선 끝부분을 무척 좋아하는 데서 출발했어. 그러다 보니 그게 곧 중간으로, 또 처음으로 번져 갔단다. 처음과 중간이 없으면 끝도 있을 수 없으니까 말이야.”\n“그렇고말고요, 정말 그렇지요.” 노부인 채텀이 위엄 있게 힘주어 말했다.\n\n장례식을 잘 볼 수 있는 그 위층 창문은 캐소본 씨가 일을 금지당했을 때 머물던 방에 있었다. 그러나 그는 이제 경고와 처방에도 아랑곳없이 거의 평소의 생활 방식을 다시 되찾았던 터라, 캐드월러더 부인을 정중히 맞이한 뒤 다시 서재로 슬그머니 빠져나가 구시와 미스라임에 관한 박학한 오류라는 새김질거리를 되씹고 있었다.\n손님들이 오지 않았더라면 도러시아 역시 서재에 갇혀 있었을 테고, 늙은 페더스톤의 장례식이라는 이 광경을 목격하지 못했을 것이다. 그 광경은 그녀의 삶의 흐름과는 동떨어진 것처럼 보였지만, 훗날 기억의 어떤 예민한 지점이 건드려질 때면 늘 그녀에게 되돌아왔으니, 마치 로마의 성 베드로 대성당의 환영이 낙담의 기분과 한데 짜여 있었던 것과도 같았다. 우리 이웃들의 운명에 결정적인 변화를 일으키는 장면들은 우리 자신에게는 한낱 배경에 지나지 않는다. 그러나 들판과 나무들의 어떤 특정한 모습처럼, 그것들은 우리에게 우리 자신의 역사의 한 시기와 결부되어, 가장 예리한 의식의 선택 속에 깃든 그 통일성의 일부를 이루게 된다.\n\n낯설고 잘 이해되지 않는 무언가가 그녀 경험의 가장 깊은 비밀과 꿈결처럼 결부되는 그 느낌은, 도러시아의 천성이 지닌 바로 그 열정에서 비롯된 고독감을 비추는 듯했다. 옛 시절의 시골 신사 계층은 희박한 사교의 공기 속에 살았으니, 산자락 위 저마다의 위치에 점점이 떨어져 자리 잡은 채, 아래쪽의 더 빽빽한 삶의 무리들을 불완전한 분별력으로 내려다보았다. 그리고 도러시아는 그 높이의 조망과 냉기 속에서 마음이 편치 않았다.\n\n“이제 더는 안 볼래요.” 행렬이 교회 안으로 들어간 뒤 실리아가 말하며, 남편의 팔꿈치 뒤쪽으로 조금 물러서서 그의 외투에 뺨을 슬쩍 댈 수 있게 자리를 잡았다. “도도는 좋아하겠죠. 언니는 우울한 것이랑 못생긴 사람들을 좋아하니까요.”\n\n“나는 내가 더불어 사는 사람들에 대해 뭔가 알게 되는 걸 좋아해.” 휴일을 맞은 수도사 같은 흥미로 모든 것을 지켜보던 도러시아가 말했다. “우리는 이웃이 오두막에 사는 소작인이 아니고서는 그들에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것 같아. 다른 사람들이 어떤 삶을 살고, 일을 어떻게 받아들이는지 끊임없이 궁금해지잖니. 캐드월러더 부인이 오셔서 나를 서재 밖으로 불러내 주셔서 정말 감사해요.”\n\n“나에게 감사하는 게 당연하지요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “댁의 부유한 로윅 농부들은 물소나 들소만큼이나 신기한 구경거리예요. 게다가 교회에서는 그들을 절반도 못 볼 게 분명하고요. 그들은 댁의 삼촌이나 제임스 경의 소작인들과는 아주 달라요. 괴물들이지요. 지주가 없는 농부들이라니. 어떻게 분류해야 할지 알 수가 없어요.”\n\n“이 조문객들 대부분은 로윅 사람이 아닙니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “먼 곳에서 왔거나, 미들마치에서 온 유산 상속인들이겠지요. 러브굿 말로는 그 노인이 토지뿐 아니라 상당한 돈도 남겼다더군요.”\n\n“생각 좀 해 보세요! 차남 이하의 아들들 가운데 제 돈으로 끼니도 못 챙기는 이가 그토록 많은데 말이에요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. 그러더니 문이 열리는 소리에 돌아서며, “아, 브룩 씨가 오셨네요. 아까 우리 모임이 어딘가 미흡하다 싶더니, 여기 그 까닭이 있었군요. 물론 이 별난 장례식을 구경하러 오신 거겠지요?”\n\n“아니요, 캐소본을 살피러 왔습니다. 그 사람이 어떻게 지내는지 보려고요. 그리고 자그마한 소식도 좀 전하려고요. 자그마한 소식 말이오, 얘야.” 브룩 씨가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 도러시아에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “서재를 들여다보니, 캐소본이 책에 파묻혀 있더구나. 그래서 그러면 안 된다고 했지. ‘이래선 절대 안 돼요. 아내를 생각하시오, 캐소본.’ 이렇게 말했지. 그러니까 그가 올라오겠다고 약속하더구나. 소식은 말하지 않았어. 그저 올라오셔야 한다고만 했지.”\n\n“아, 이제 사람들이 교회에서 나오는군요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 소리쳤다. “어머나, 정말 기막히게 뒤섞인 무리네요! 저 사람이 의사 리드게이트 씨겠지요. 그런데 저 여자는 정말 인물이 곱고, 저 잘생긴 젊은이는 그 여자의 아들이 분명해요. 저들은 누구인가요, 제임스 경, 아세요?”\n\n“미들마치 시장인 빈시가 보이는군요. 아마 그의 아내와 아들일 겁니다.” 제임스 경이 브룩 씨를 미심쩍은 듯 바라보며 말하자, 브룩 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n“그렇지요, 아주 점잖은 집안이에요. 빈시는 아주 좋은 사람이고요. 제조업계의 자랑이지요. 우리 집에서 보신 적 있을 거요.”\n\n“아, 그럼요. 댁의 비밀 위원회 사람 중 하나죠.” 캐드월러더 부인이 약을 올리듯 말했다.\n\n“하긴 사냥개로 토끼를 쫓아다니는 작자이긴 합니다만.” 제임스 경이 여우 사냥꾼다운 혐오감을 담아 말했다.\n\n“게다가 팁턴과 프레시트의 가엾은 수직기 직조공들의 생기를 쪽쪽 빨아먹는 자들 중 하나죠. 그 집안 식구들이 저렇게 곱고 윤기 나 보이는 게 다 그 덕분이고요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “저 거무튀튀하고 검붉은 얼굴의 사람들이 아주 훌륭한 들러리 노릇을 해 주네요. 어머나, 저들은 무슨 물주전자들 같아요! 험프리 좀 보세요. 흰 중백의를 입고 그들 위로 우뚝 솟은 모습이 못생긴 대천사라 해도 믿겠어요.”\n\n“그래도 장례식은 엄숙한 일이잖소.” 브룩 씨가 말했다. “그런 관점에서 보면 말이오.”\n\n“하지만 나는 그런 관점으로 보고 있지 않은걸요. 나는 내 엄숙함을 너무 자주 걸칠 수가 없어요. 안 그러면 다 해져 버릴 테니까요. 그 노인은 죽을 때가 됐고, 이 사람들 중 슬퍼하는 이는 하나도 없으니까요.”\n\n“어쩌면 그리 가엾을까요!” 도러시아가 말했다. “이 장례식은 제가 본 것 중 가장 음울한 것 같아요. 아침에 드리운 얼룩 같아요. 누군가 죽으면서 아무 사랑도 남기지 못한다는 걸 차마 생각할 수가 없어요.”\n\n그녀는 더 말하려 했으나, 남편이 들어와 조금 뒤편에 자리를 잡고 앉는 것을 보았다. 그의 존재가 그녀에게 가져오는 차이는 늘 행복한 것만은 아니었다. 그녀는 그가 자기 말에 속으로 못마땅해할 때가 많다고 느꼈다.\n\n“어머, 정말이지,” 캐드월러더 부인이 외쳤다. “저 떡 벌어진 남자 뒤에서 그들 누구보다도 더 괴상한 새 얼굴이 나왔어요. 자그맣고 둥근 머리에 눈이 불룩 튀어나온, 일종의 개구리 얼굴 같은. 좀 보세요. 저 사람은 다른 핏줄인 것 같아요.”\n\n“어디 봐요!” 호기심이 일어난 실리아가 캐드월러더 부인 뒤에 서서 그녀의 머리 너머로 몸을 내밀며 말했다. “어머, 정말 별난 얼굴이네요!” 그러더니 또 다른 종류의 놀란 표정으로 재빨리 바꾸며 덧붙였다. “어머, 도도, 래디슬로 씨가 다시 오셨다는 말은 한 번도 안 했잖아요!”\n\n도러시아는 깜짝 놀라 충격을 받았다. 그녀가 곧장 삼촌을 올려다보는 사이 캐소본 씨는 그녀를 바라보았고, 모두가 그녀의 갑작스러운 창백함을 알아차렸다.\n\n“그 청년은 나와 함께 왔단다. 내 손님이지. 그레인지에 묵고 있어.” 브룩 씨가 더없이 편안한 어조로, 마치 그 소식이 그녀가 충분히 예상할 만한 것이라는 듯 도러시아에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “게다가 그림을 마차 지붕에 싣고 왔다네. 자네가 이 깜짝 선물을 좋아할 줄 알았지, 캐소본. 거기 자네가 꼭 살아 있는 모습 그대로 그려졌어. 아퀴나스로 말이지. 더없이 어울리는 소재야. 그리고 젊은 래디슬로가 그것에 대해 이야기하는 걸 듣게 될 거요. 그 친구는 놀랄 만큼 말을 잘하거든. 이것저것 짚어 가면서 말이오. 미술이며 그런 종류의 것을 죄다 알고 있어. 좋은 말동무이고 말이야. 어떤 화제로든 자네와 척척 통하지. 내가 오래전부터 바라던 그런 사람이라네.”\n\n캐소본 씨는 짜증을 다스리며 차가운 정중함으로 고개를 숙였으나, 그 다스림은 침묵을 지키는 정도에 그쳤다. 그는 도러시아 못지않게 윌의 편지를 또렷이 기억하고 있었다. 그 편지가 회복 후 자신에게 따로 챙겨진 편지들 가운데 없다는 것을 그는 알아차렸고, 도러시아가 윌에게 로윅으로 오지 말라는 전갈을 보냈으리라고 속으로 단정한 터라, 자존심 강한 예민함으로 그 화제를 다시 꺼내기를 줄곧 꺼려 왔다. 이제 그는 그녀가 삼촌에게 부탁해 윌을 그레인지로 초대하게 한 것이라고 추측했다. 그리고 그녀는 그 순간 어떤 설명도 시작하기가 불가능하다고 느꼈다.\n\n교회 묘지에서 시선을 돌린 캐드월러더 부인의 눈에는 적잖은 무언극이 들어왔으나, 그녀가 바라는 만큼 알아볼 만한 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 “래디슬로 씨가 누구죠?”라는 질문을 억누를 수 없었다.\n\n“캐소본 씨의 젊은 친척입니다.” 제임스 경이 얼른 대답했다. 그는 사람 좋은 성품 덕분에 종종 개인사에 관해서는 재빠르고 명민하게 알아차렸으니, 도러시아가 남편을 흘낏 바라본 데서 그녀의 마음에 어떤 불안이 있음을 간파한 것이다.\n\n“아주 괜찮은 청년이지요. 캐소본이 그를 위해 온갖 일을 다 해 주었답니다.” 브룩 씨가 설명했다. “그가 자네가 그에게 들인 비용을 갚아 주고 있다네, 캐소본.” 그가 격려하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 이었다. “그가 나와 함께 오래 머물러서 내 자료들로 뭔가를 만들어 냈으면 좋겠어. 나한테는 아이디어며 사실이 넘쳐 나는데 말이오, 보아하니 그가 바로 그것들을 꼴을 갖춰 정리해 줄 사람이라네. 알맞은 인용구가 뭔지 기억해 내고, 라틴어로 ‘만장일치를 얻었도다’ 운운하는 식이지. 화제에 일종의 묘미를 더해 준다네. 자네가 아플 때 얼마 전에 그를 초대했어, 캐소본. 도러시아가 자네 집에는 아무도 들일 수 없다고 해서, 나더러 편지를 쓰라고 부탁했거든.”\n\n* 만장일치를 얻었도다: 라틴어 omne tulit punctum. 호라티우스의 시구로, ‘유익함과 즐거움을 두루 갖춰 모든 표를 얻었다’는 뜻.\n가엾은 도러시아는 삼촌의 말 한마디 한마디가 캐소본 씨에게는 눈에 들어간 모래알만큼이나 거슬릴 것이라고 느꼈다. 자신이 삼촌에게 윌 래디슬로를 초대해 달라고 바란 적이 없다고 지금 해명하는 것은 도무지 어울리지 않을 일이었다. 남편이 그의 존재를 싫어하는 까닭을 그녀는 스스로도 조금도 분명히 알 수 없었다. 서재에서의 그 장면을 통해 그 혐오감이 그녀에게 고통스럽게 각인되긴 했지만 말이다. 그러나 그녀는 그 혐오감을 다른 이들에게 짐작하게 할 만한 말을 하는 것이 온당치 못하다는 것을 느꼈다. 사실 캐소본 씨 자신도 그 뒤섞인 이유들을 스스로에게 철저히 표명해 본 적이 없었으니, 그에게도 우리 모두와 마찬가지로 짜증 난 감정이란 자기 인식보다는 자기 정당화를 구하는 법이었다. 하지만 그는 겉으로 드러나는 기색을 억누르고자 했고, 평소보다 더 위엄 있게 몸을 굽히고 가락 섞인 어조로 다음과 같이 말하기 전에, 남편 얼굴의 변화를 알아챌 수 있는 사람은 오직 도러시아뿐이었다.\n\n“참으로 후한 대접이시오, 친애하는 어른. 제 친척에게 환대를 베풀어 주신 데 대해 감사의 말씀을 드려야겠습니다.”\n\n장례식은 이제 끝났고, 교회 묘지에서는 사람들이 흩어지고 있었다.\n\n“이제 그분을 볼 수 있어요, 캐드월러더 부인.” 실리아가 말했다. “도러시아의 규방에 걸린 캐소본 씨의 고모 세밀화하고 꼭 닮았어요. 아주 잘생겼고요.”\n\n“참 어여쁜 잔가지로군요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. “댁의 조카는 장차 무엇이 될 사람인가요, 캐소본 씨?”\n\n“실례지만, 그는 제 조카가 아닙니다. 제 사촌이지요.”\n\n“음, 그러니까 말입니다.” 브룩 씨가 끼어들었다. “그는 지금 날개를 펼쳐 보는 중이에요. 출세할 만한 그런 청년이지요. 나는 기꺼이 그에게 기회를 주고 싶어요. 이를테면 홉스나 밀턴, 스위프트 같은, 그런 부류의 사람처럼 훌륭한 비서가 될 거요.”\n\n“알겠어요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “연설문을 써 줄 수 있는 사람 말이지요.”\n\n“이제 그를 데려올까, 응, 캐소본?” 브룩 씨가 말했다. “내가 알리기 전에는 들어오려 하지 않더군. 그러고 나서 우리 다 같이 내려가서 그림을 보세. 거기 자네가 살아 있는 듯 그려져 있어. 손가락을 책장에 얹은, 깊고 미묘한 사색가의 모습이지. 그동안 성 보나벤투라인지 누군지가, 다소 살집 있고 혈색 좋은 모습으로 삼위일체를 올려다보고 있고 말이야. 모든 것이 상징적이라네. 더 높은 양식의 미술이지. 나도 어느 정도까지는 그런 걸 좋아하지만, 너무 지나치면 안 돼. 따라가기가 다소 버겁거든, 알다시피. 하지만 자네는 그 분야에 정통하지 않은가, 캐소본. 그리고 자네 화가의 살결 표현이 훌륭해. 견고함이며 투명함이며, 그런 종류의 것이 다 있어. 한때 나도 그 분야를 꽤 깊이 파고들었지. 어쨌든, 내가 가서 래디슬로를 데려오겠네.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXV.\n\n아니, 슬픔에 잠긴 상속자 무리가 망연자실한 자세로 얼굴을 길게 늘어뜨린 채 긴 유언장을 읽으며, 창백하고 놀란 얼굴로 조롱과 함께 작별 인사나 받는 꼴을 보는 것보다 더 매력적인 즐거움을 나는 알지 못하노라. 그들의 깊은 슬픔을 있는 그대로 보기 위해서라면 나는 일부러 저세상에서라도 돌아오리라. —레냐르, 『만물의 상속자』\n\n짐승들이 짝을 지어 방주로 들어갔을 때, 가까운 종끼리는 서로를 두고 속으로 이런저런 품평을 하면서, 같은 사료 더미를 먹어 치우는 형태가 그토록 많다는 것은 결국 각자의 몫을 줄이는 데나 기여할 뿐이니 지극히 군더더기라고 여기고 싶은 유혹에 빠졌으리라고 상상해 볼 수 있다. (그때 독수리들이 맡았던 역할은 예술로 표현하기에는 너무 고통스러우리라 싶다. 그 새들은 모이주머니 근처가 보기 흉하게 벌거벗었고, 또 보아하니 아무런 격식이나 의식도 갖추지 않은 듯하니 말이다.)\n\n피터 페더스톤의 장례 행렬을 이룬 기독교도 육식 동물들에게도 똑같은 종류의 유혹이 닥쳤다. 그들 대부분은 한정된 재산에 마음이 쏠려 있었고, 저마다 그 가운데 가장 큰 몫을 차지하고 싶어 했다. 오래전부터 인정받아 온 혈족과 혼인으로 맺어진 인척만 해도 이미 상당한 수였고, 거기에 갖가지 가능성을 곱하니 시샘 어린 추측과 애처로운 기대가 펼쳐질 멋진 무대가 마련되었다. 빈시 일가에 대한 시샘은 페더스톤 가문의 피를 이은 모든 사람들 사이에 적개심의 동지애를 만들어 냈으므로, 그들 가운데 누군가가 나머지보다 더 많이 받으리라는 확실한 조짐이 없는 한, 그 다리 긴 프레드 빈시가 땅을 차지할지 모른다는 두려움이 자연히 지배적이었다. 그러면서도 메리 가스에게 품은 것 같은 막연한 시샘에 쏟을 감정과 여유는 넉넉히 남아 있었다. 솔로몬은 조나가 자격이 없다고 곱씹을 짬을 냈고, 조나는 솔로몬을 탐욕스럽다고 헐뜯었다. 큰누이 제인은 마사의 자식들이 어린 와울 가의 아이들만큼 기대해서는 안 된다고 여겼고, 장자 상속 문제에 좀 더 너그러운 마사는 제인이 그토록 \"욕심 사납다\"는 사실에 마음이 쓰라렸다. 이 가장 가까운 친족들은 당연히 사촌이며 육촌이 품는 기대가 터무니없다고 느꼈고, 작은 유산도 너무 여럿이면 큰 금액으로 불어날 수 있다는 셈을 셈하는 데 산수를 동원했다. 유언장 낭독을 들으러 온 사촌은 둘이었고, 트럼불 씨 말고도 육촌이 하나 있었다. 이 육촌은 미들마치의 포목상으로, 예의 바른 태도와 필요 이상으로 자주 새어 나오는 발음을 지닌 사람이었다. 두 사촌은 브래싱에서 온 노인들이었는데, 한 사람은 부유한 사촌 피터에게 굴이며 그 밖의 먹을거리를 선물하느라 적잖은 비용을 치렀다는 명목으로 권리를 주장할 만하다고 여겼고, 다른 한 사람은 더없이 음울한 표정으로 두 손과 턱을 지팡이에 괸 채, 어떤 좁은 공적이 아니라 전반적인 인덕에 근거한 권리를 주장할 만하다고 여겼다. 둘 다 흠잡을 데 없는 브래싱 시민으로서, 조나 페더스톤이 그곳에 살지 않았으면 좋겠다고 바라는 사람들이었다. 한 집안에서 익살이란 대개 낯선 사람들 사이에서 가장 환대받는 법이다.\n\n\"아니, 트럼불 본인도 오백 파운드는 거의 확실해. 그건 믿어도 돼. 형님이 약속했다 해도 난 놀라지 않을 거야.\" 장례식 전날 저녁, 솔로몬이 누이들과 함께 소리 내어 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다.\n\n\"저런, 저런!\" 가엾은 누이 마사가 말했다. 그녀의 수백 파운드에 대한 상상은 늘 밀린 집세 액수에 맞춰 좁아져 있었다.\n\n그러나 아침이 되자 추측의 모든 통상적인 흐름이, 마치 달에서 떨어진 듯 그들 사이에 첨벙 끼어든 낯선 조문객의 출현으로 어지러워졌다. 이 사람이 바로 캐드월러더 부인이 개구리 얼굴이라고 묘사했던 그 낯선 이였다. 서른두셋쯤 되어 보이는 남자로, 툭 튀어나온 눈, 입술이 얇고 아래로 처진 입, 눈썹 능선 위에서 갑자기 꺼져 들어간 이마에서 매끈하게 빗어 넘긴 머리카락이, 그의 얼굴에 분명 양서류처럼 변치 않는 표정을 부여하고 있었다. 여기 분명히 새로운 수유자가 있었다. 그렇지 않다면 어째서 그가 조문객으로 초대되었겠는가? 여기 새로운 가능성이 있어 새로운 불확실성을 불러일으켰고, 그 탓에 장례 마차 안에서는 말이 거의 끊겼다. 우리는 어떤 사실이 — 아주 편안하게 존재해 왔고 어쩌면 우리가 그것을 완전히 빼놓은 채 세계를 꾸려 가는 동안 은밀히 우리를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었을지도 모를 사실이 — 느닷없이 발견되면 누구나 굴욕을 느낀다. 메리 가스를 제외하고는 아무도 이 수상쩍은 낯선 이를 전에 본 적이 없었는데, 그녀가 그에 대해 아는 것이라곤 페더스톤 씨가 아래층에 내려와 있을 때 그가 스톤 코트에 두 번 찾아와 몇 시간씩 단둘이 앉아 있었다는 사실뿐이었다. 그녀는 이 일을 아버지에게 말할 기회를 얻었고, 변호사를 빼면 그 낯선 이를 혐오나 의심보다 호기심에 가까운 눈으로 살피는 눈은 어쩌면 케일럽의 눈뿐이었을 것이다. 케일럽 가스는 기대가 적었고 욕심은 더 적었으므로, 자기 짐작이 맞는지 확인하는 데 흥미가 있었다. 그가 차분하게 반쯤 미소 지으며 턱을 문지르고는, 마치 나무 한 그루의 값을 매기듯 영민한 눈길을 던지는 모습은, 리그라는 이름으로 알려진 그 낯선 조문객이 판벽을 두른 응접실로 들어와 유언장이 낭독될 때 청중의 한 사람이 되려고 문가에 자리를 잡았을 때 다른 얼굴들에 드러난 경악이나 경멸과 멋진 대조를 이루었다. 바로 그때 솔로몬 씨와 조나 씨는 변호사와 함께 유언장을 찾으러 위층으로 올라가 있었다. 와울 부인은 자신과 보스럽 트럼불 씨 사이에 빈자리가 둘 있는 것을 보고, 그 대단한 권위자 옆으로 옮겨 앉을 만한 배짱을 발휘했다. 트럼불 씨는 시곗줄 장식을 만지작거리며 옷매무새를 가다듬고 있었는데, 능력 있는 사람에게 경이나 놀라움처럼 체면을 손상시키는 것은 결코 드러내지 않겠다는 결의가 엿보였다.\n\n\"제 가엾은 오라버니가 무슨 일을 해 놓으셨는지 트럼불 씨는 다 아실 것 같은데요.\" 와울 부인이 가장 솜털 같은 낮은 목소리로 말하며, 검은 상장(喪章)으로 그늘진 보닛을 트럼불 씨의 귀 쪽으로 돌렸다.\n\n\"부인, 제가 들은 것이 무엇이든 그것은 비밀로 들은 것입니다.\" 경매인은 그 비밀을 가리려는 듯 손을 들어 올리며 말했다.\n\n\"제 행운을 확신했던 사람들이 끝내 실망하게 될지도 모르지요.\" 와울 부인은 이 말 속에서 얼마간 위안을 찾으며 계속했다.\n\n\"기대란 종종 헛된 것이지요.\" 트럼불 씨는 여전히 은밀한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 와울 부인은 맞은편의 빈시 일가를 건너다보고는, 다시 누이 마사의 곁으로 돌아가며 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 피터 오라버니가 얼마나 속을 안 보였는지, 참 놀라울 정도야.\" 그녀는 같은 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"그이가 마음속에 무얼 품고 있었는지 우리는 아무도 몰라. 그저 오라버니가 우리 생각보다 더 못된 사람은 아니었기만을 바라고 또 빌 뿐이야, 마사.\"\n\n가엾은 크랜치 부인은 몸집이 우람했고, 천식 환자처럼 숨을 쉬었기에, 자기 말이 흠잡을 데 없고 일반적인 취지를 띠도록 신경 써야 할 까닭이 하나 더 있었다. 그녀는 속삭임조차 컸으며, 고장 난 손풍금처럼 별안간 터져 나오기 십상이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"난 한 번도 욕심을 부린 적이 없어, 제인.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 난 자식이 여섯이고 셋을 묻었으며, 돈 있는 집에 시집간 것도 아니야. 저기 앉은 맏이가 이제 겨우 열아홉이니, 나머지는 알아서 짐작해 봐. 게다가 가축은 늘 모자라고 땅은 더없이 다루기 까다롭고. 하지만 내가 무언가를 빌고 또 빌었다면 그건 위에 계신 하느님께였어. 그래도 한 형제는 독신이고 다른 형제는 두 번 결혼하고도 자식이 없으니, 누구라도 그런 생각을 할 거 아냐!\"\n\n그동안 빈시 씨는 무표정한 리그 씨의 얼굴을 슬쩍 보고는 코담뱃갑을 꺼내 톡톡 두드렸지만, 판단을 아무리 또렷하게 해 준다 한들 이런 자리에는 어울리지 않는 호사라 여겨 열지 않은 채 도로 집어넣었다. \"페더스톤이 우리 누구도 인정해 주지 않았던 좋은 감정을 품고 있었다 해도 난 놀라지 않겠어.\" 그는 아내의 귀에 대고 말했다. \"이 장례식을 보면 모든 사람을 두루 생각했다는 걸 알 수 있잖아. 사람이 죽어서 친구들의 배웅을 받고 싶어 하고, 그들이 비천하더라도 부끄러워하지 않는 건 보기 좋은 일이지. 그 사람이 자잘한 유산을 잔뜩 남겨 줬으면 난 더없이 기뻤을 거야. 형편이 변변찮은 친구들한테는 그런 게 무척 요긴할 수 있으니까.\"\n\n\"모든 게 더할 나위 없이 훌륭해요. 상장이며 비단이며 죄다요.\" 빈시 부인이 흡족하게 말했다.\n\n그러나 안타깝게도 프레드는 웃음을 억누르는 데 적잖이 애를 먹고 있었는데, 그 웃음은 아버지의 코담뱃갑보다 더 어울리지 않는 것이었으리라. 프레드는 조나 씨가 \"사생아\" 어쩌고 하는 말을 우연히 엿들었고, 그런 생각이 머릿속에 박힌 채 마침 맞은편에 있는 낯선 이의 얼굴을 보니 너무도 우스꽝스럽게 느껴졌다. 메리 가스는 그의 입술이 씰룩거리고 헛기침으로 얼버무리는 것을 보고 그의 곤경을 알아챘고, 자리를 바꿔 앉자고 청하여 슬기롭게 그를 구해 주었다. 그리하여 그는 그늘진 구석으로 들어갈 수 있었다. 프레드는 리그를 비롯한 모든 사람에게 더없이 너그러운 심정이었다. 자신이 운 좋다는 것을 스스로 깨닫는 만큼 운이 덜한 이 모든 사람들에게 얼마간 동정을 품고 있었기에, 무슨 일이 있어도 무례하게 굴고 싶지 않았다. 그런데도 웃기는 유난히 쉬웠다.\n\n그러나 변호사와 두 형제가 들어서자 모두의 시선이 그쪽으로 쏠렸다. 변호사는 스탠디시 씨였는데, 그는 그날이 저물기 전에 누가 기뻐하고 누가 실망할지 자기가 속속들이 알고 있다고 믿으며 이날 아침 스톤 코트에 왔다. 그가 낭독하리라 예상한 유언장은 페더스톤 씨를 위해 작성한 세 통 가운데 마지막 것이었다. 스탠디시 씨는 태도를 바꾸는 사람이 아니었다. 그는 모든 사람에게 똑같이 굵은 목소리로 무뚝뚝하면서도 정중하게 대했는데, 마치 그들 사이에 아무런 차이도 보이지 않는다는 투였고, 주로 건초 작황 이야기를 했다. 올 작황은 \"맙소사, 아주 좋을 거\"라느니, 국왕에 관한 최근 소식이라느니, 클래런스 공작은 뼛속까지 뱃사람이라 영국 같은 섬나라를 다스리기에 꼭 알맞은 인물이라느니 하는 식이었다.\n\n늙은 페더스톤은 불을 바라보고 앉아 언젠가 스탠디시가 깜짝 놀라게 되리라고 종종 생각하곤 했다. 사실 그가 마지막에 자기 뜻대로 하여 다른 변호사가 작성한 유언장을 태워 버렸더라면 그 사소한 목적은 이루지 못했을 테지만, 그래도 그는 그 일을 곱씹으며 즐거움을 누렸다. 그리고 과연 스탠디시 씨는 놀랐으나 조금도 언짢지는 않았다. 오히려 그는 두 번째 유언장의 발견이 페더스톤 가문이 앞으로 받을 충격에 더해 준, 자기 마음속 작은 호기심의 묘미를 꽤 즐기는 편이었다.\n\n솔로몬과 조나의 심정으로 말하자면, 그들은 완전한 불안 속에 매여 있었다. 그들에게는 옛 유언장이 일정한 효력을 지닐 것 같았고, 가엾은 피터의 예전 의도와 나중 의도가 뒤엉켜, 아무도 자기 몫을 차지하기 전에 끝없는 \"소송\"이 벌어질지도 모른다고 여겨졌다. 그것은 적어도 모두에게 두루 돌아간다는 이점은 있는 골칫거리였다. 그래서 두 형제는 스탠디시 씨와 함께 다시 들어올 때 철저히 중립적인 근엄함을 보였다. 그러나 솔로몬은 어쨌든 사람의 마음을 흔드는 대목이 있으리라는 생각에 다시 흰 손수건을 꺼냈으니, 장례식에서 우는 일은 아무리 눈물이 메말랐다 한들 으레 고운 아마포에 받쳐 차려 내는 법이었기 때문이다.\n\n아마도 이 순간 가장 두근거리는 흥분을 느낀 사람은 메리 가스였을 것이다. 이 두 번째 유언장의 작성을 사실상 결정지은 사람이 바로 자신이라는 것을, 그리고 그것이 여기 모인 몇몇 사람의 운명에 중대한 영향을 미칠지도 모른다는 것을 의식하고 있었기 때문이다. 그 마지막 밤에 무슨 일이 있었는지 그녀 말고는 어느 누구도 알지 못했다.\n\n\"제 손에 들린 이 유언장은,\" 방 한가운데 탁자 앞에 앉은 스탠디시 씨가 말했다. 그는 목소리를 가다듬으려는 듯한 기침을 비롯하여 모든 일에 충분히 시간을 들이는 사람이었다. \"제가 직접 작성했고 우리의 고인 친구께서 1825년 8월 9일에 서명하신 것입니다. 그런데 보니 지금까지 제가 알지 못했던 그 후의 문서가 하나 더 있군요. 1826년 7월 20일 자로, 앞의 것보다 채 일 년도 지나지 않은 때입니다. 그리고 더 보니\" — 스탠디시 씨는 안경을 쓰고 문서를 조심스럽게 훑어 나갔다 — \"이 나중 유언장에는 1828년 3월 1일 자 추가 조항이 붙어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"저런, 저런!\" 누이 마사가 말했다. 소리 내려던 것은 아니었으나, 날짜가 쏟아지는 압박에 무어라도 입 밖으로 내고 만 것이었다.\n\n\"먼저 앞의 유언장부터 읽겠습니다.\" 스탠디시 씨가 계속했다. \"고인이 이 문서를 파기하지 않으신 것으로 보아 그것이 고인의 뜻인 듯하니 말입니다.\"\n\n서두는 꽤 길게 느껴졌고, 솔로몬 말고도 여럿이 땅을 내려다보며 애처롭게 고개를 가로저었다. 모든 눈이 다른 눈과 마주치기를 피했고, 주로 식탁보의 얼룩이나 스탠디시 씨의 벗어진 머리에 고정되었다. 메리 가스의 눈만은 예외였다. 다른 모든 이들이 어디에도 시선을 두지 않으려 애쓰는 동안, 그녀는 그들을 바라보아도 안전했다. 그리고 첫 번째 \"준다, 그리고 유증한다\"라는 소리가 들리자, 그녀는 리그 씨를 제외한 모두의 안색이, 마치 희미한 떨림이 그들을 관통하는 듯 미묘하게 변하는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그는 변함없이 평온하게 앉아 있었고, 사실 더 중요한 문제와 철회될 수도 철회되지 않을 수도 있는 유증을 듣느라 복잡한 마음에 사로잡힌 일동은 그를 더 이상 생각하지 않게 되었다. 프레드는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 빈시 씨는 닫아 둔 채로나마 코담뱃갑을 손에 쥐고 있지 않으면 견딜 수 없었다.\n\n자잘한 유증이 먼저 나왔는데, 또 다른 유언장이 있고 가엾은 피터가 마음을 고쳐먹었을지도 모른다는 생각을 떠올린들 솟구치는 혐오와 분노를 가라앉힐 수는 없었다. 사람이란 과거든 현재든 미래든 모든 시제에서 후하게 대접받기를 바라는 법이다. 그런데 여기 피터는 오 년 전에 자기 친형제자매에게 고작 이백 파운드씩, 친조카들에게는 고작 백 파운드씩만 남길 수 있었던 사람이었다. 가스 가는 언급조차 되지 않았으나, 빈시 부인과 로저먼드는 각각 백 파운드씩 받게 되어 있었다. 트럼불 씨에게는 금장 손잡이 지팡이와 오십 파운드가 돌아갔고, 다른 육촌들과 자리에 함께한 사촌들에게도 저마다 그와 같은 후한 액수가 돌아갔는데, 음울한 사촌이 한마디 했듯이 그것은 사람을 이도 저도 아닌 처지에 빠뜨리는 종류의 유산이었다. 게다가 그 자리에 없는 사람들 — 정체가 모호한, 게다가 유감스럽게도 천한 인척일 게 분명한 사람들 — 에게 그런 식으로 찔끔찔끔 베푸는 대목이 훨씬 더 많았다. 어림잡아 다 합치니 여기서 약 삼천 파운드가 처분되었다. 그렇다면 피터는 나머지 돈을 어디로 보내려 했단 말인가 — 그리고 땅은 어디로? 무엇이 철회되었고 무엇이 철회되지 않았는가 — 그리고 그 철회는 더 나은 쪽인가 더 나쁜 쪽인가? 모든 감정은 조건부일 수밖에 없었고, 엉뚱한 방향으로 드러날 수도 있었다. 남자들은 이 어수선한 불안 속에서도 버티며 잠자코 있을 만큼 강했다. 어떤 이는 아랫입술을 늘어뜨리고, 어떤 이는 그것을 오므렸는데, 저마다 제 근육의 습관에 따른 것이었다. 그러나 제인과 마사는 쏟아지는 의문의 격류에 무너져 울기 시작했다. 가엾은 크랜치 부인은 일하지 않고도 어쨌든 몇백 파운드를 얻는다는 위안에 반쯤 마음이 움직이면서도, 자기 몫이 변변찮다는 것을 반쯤 알아채고 있었다. 반면 와울 부인의 마음은 자신이 친누이인데도 적게 받고 다른 누군가는 많이 받게 된다는 생각으로 온통 넘쳐흘렀다. 이제 모두는 그 \"많은\" 몫이 프레드 빈시에게 돌아가리라 예상했으나, 정작 명시된 투자 자산으로 만 파운드가 그에게 유증된다고 선언되자 빈시 일가 자신들조차 놀랐다. 땅도 함께 오는 것일까? 프레드는 입술을 깨물었다. 미소를 참기가 어려웠고, 빈시 부인은 자신이 세상에서 가장 행복한 여인이라고 느꼈다. 이 눈부신 환상 속에서 철회의 가능성 따위는 시야에서 쪼그라들어 버렸다.\n\n땅 말고도 동산이 아직 얼마간 남아 있었지만, 그 전부가 한 사람에게 남겨졌으니, 그 사람은 — 오, 가능성이여! 오, \"속을 안 보이던\" 노인의 호의에 기댄 기대여! 오, 죽어야 할 인간의 어리석음을 재는 잣대로는 표현이 속절없이 미끄러질 뿐인, 그 끝없는 호격이여! — 그 잔여 재산의 수유자는 조슈아 리그였다. 그는 단독 유언 집행인이기도 했으며, 그때부터 페더스톤이라는 이름을 쓰게 되어 있었다.\n\n방 안을 한 바퀴 도는 전율처럼 보이는 술렁임이 일었다. 모두가 리그 씨를 다시 빤히 쳐다보았으나, 그는 도무지 놀라는 기색이 없었다.\n\n\"참으로 기이하기 짝이 없는 유산 처분입니다!\" 트럼불 씨가 외쳤다. 그는 이번만큼은 자기가 사정을 미리 몰랐던 사람으로 여겨지기를 바랐다. \"그러나 두 번째 유언장이 있습니다 — 또 다른 문서가 있어요. 우리는 아직 고인의 마지막 뜻을 듣지 못했습니다.\"\n\n메리 가스는 그들이 아직 들어야 할 것이 마지막 뜻은 아니라고 느끼고 있었다. 두 번째 유언장은 앞서 언급된 천한 사람들에게 주는 유산(이에 대한 몇 가지 변경이 추가 조항의 사유였다)과 로윅 교구에 자리한 모든 땅을 가축과 가재도구 전부와 함께 조슈아 리그에게 주는 유증을 제외하고는 모든 것을 철회했다. 나머지 재산은 노인들을 위한 자선 양로원을 짓고 기금을 대는 데 바쳐지게 되어 있었으며, 그 양로원은 페더스톤 양로원이라 불리고, 유언자가 이미 그 목적으로 사들인 미들마치 부근의 땅에 세워질 것이었다. 문서에 적힌 바에 따르면 그는 전능하신 하느님을 기쁘게 해 드리고자 그리한 것이었다. 자리에 함께한 누구도 한 푼 받지 못했다. 다만 트럼불 씨에게는 금장 손잡이 지팡이가 있었다. 일동이 말할 기운을 되찾기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다. 메리는 차마 프레드를 쳐다볼 수 없었다.\n\n가장 먼저 입을 연 사람은 빈시 씨였다. 코담뱃갑을 힘차게 사용한 뒤, 그는 큰 소리로 분개하며 말했다. \"이렇게 어처구니없는 유언장은 난생처음 들어 봅니다! 이걸 만들 때 제정신이 아니었을 거라고 봐요. 이 마지막 유언장은 무효라고 봅니다.\" 빈시 씨는 이 표현이 사태를 제대로 짚어 준다는 느낌에 덧붙였다. \"안 그렇소, 스탠디시?\"\n\n\"우리 고인 친구께서는 늘 자기가 무얼 하는지 아셨던 분이라고 봅니다.\" 스탠디시 씨가 말했다. \"모든 것이 아주 적법합니다. 여기 유언장에 묶인, 브래싱의 클레먼스가 보낸 편지가 있군요. 그가 작성했습니다. 아주 훌륭한 사무 변호사지요.\"\n\n\"저는 고(故) 페더스톤 씨에게서 정신의 이상이라든가 지성의 착란 같은 것을 전혀 알아채지 못했습니다.\" 보스럽 트럼불이 말했다. \"하지만 이 유언장은 별나다고 부르겠습니다. 저는 그 노인장께 늘 기꺼이 도움을 드렸고, 그분도 유언장에 드러날 만한 신세 진 마음을 꽤 분명하게 내비치셨지요. 금장 손잡이 지팡이라니, 제게 보답이랍시고 준 것치고는 가소롭습니다. 그러나 다행히도 저는 돈 따위에 연연하는 사람은 아니지요.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔 이 일에 그다지 놀랄 만한 건 없습니다.\" 케일럽 가스가 말했다. \"오히려 이 유언장이 트인 마음을 지닌 정직한 사람한테 기대할 법한 것이었다면, 누구든 더 의아해할 까닭이 있었겠지요. 저로서는 유언장이라는 게 아예 없었으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 기독교도 입에서 나올 말로는 참 별난 소감이로군!\" 변호사가 말했다. \"그걸 어떻게 뒷받침할 셈인지 들어 보고 싶구먼, 가스!\"\n\n\"아.\" 케일럽이 몸을 앞으로 숙이고 손가락 끝을 정성스레 매만지며 골똘히 땅을 바라보며 말했다. 그에게는 늘 말이야말로 \"일\"에서 가장 어려운 부분으로 여겨졌다.\n\n그러나 여기서 조나 페더스톤 씨가 목소리를 냈다. \"흥, 우리 형 피터는 늘 대단한 위선자였지. 하지만 이 유언장은 모든 걸 잘라 버리는군. 진작 알았더라면 짐마차에 말 여섯 마리를 매도 날 브래싱에서 끌어내지 못했을 거야. 내일은 흰 모자에 누런 코트를 걸쳐야겠어.\"\n\n\"저런, 저런.\" 크랜치 부인이 울먹였다. \"우리는 여비를 들여 왔고, 저 가엾은 애는 여기서 그토록 오래 빈둥대며 앉아 있었는데! 피터 오라버니가 전능하신 하느님을 그토록 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶어 했다는 말은 난생처음 들어 봐. 하지만 내가 그 자리에서 못 박힌 듯 굳어 버린다 해도, 이건 너무하다고 말할 수밖에 없어 — 달리는 생각할 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"그래 봤자 그가 간 곳에서는 아무 소용도 없을 거야, 내 장담하지.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. 그 쓰라림은 놀랄 만큼 진심이었으나, 그 어조는 어쩔 수 없이 음흉했다. \"피터는 못된 인생을 산 사람이었고, 마지막에 그걸 뻔뻔스럽게 드러낸 마당에 양로원 따위로 덮을 수 있는 게 아니야.\"\n\n\"게다가 그동안 줄곧 제 적법한 가족 — 형제자매며 조카들이 있었고, 그러고 싶을 때면 언제든 교회에서 우리와 함께 앉기도 했으면서.\" 와울 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 어떤 식으로도 사치나 방탕에 길든 적 없는 사람들에게 그토록 번듯하게 재산을 남길 수도 있었는데 — 게다가 그 사람들은 한 푼도 허투루 쓰지 않고 모아서 더 불릴 수 있을 만큼 변변치 못한 처지도 아니었는데 말이야. 그리고 나는 — 여기 와서 누이답게 굴려고 그 숱한 수고를 마다하지 않았건만 — 그동안 줄곧 누구든 소름 끼칠 만한 것을 마음에 품고 있었던 거야. 하지만 전능하신 분이 그걸 허락하셨다면, 그건 그 일로 그를 벌하시려는 게야. 솔로몬 오라버니, 태워다 주시면 저는 이만 가겠어요.\"\n\n\"나도 이 집에 다시는 발을 들여놓고 싶지 않아.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"나야 내 땅이 있고 물려줄 내 재산도 있으니까.\"\n\n\"세상에서 운이라는 게 어찌 돌아가는지 참 한심한 얘기야.\" 조나가 말했다. \"근성이라는 게 조금이라도 있으면 절대 보답을 못 받지. 차라리 구유 속의 개*가 되는 편이 나아. 하지만 아직 땅 위에 있는 사람들은 교훈을 얻을 수 있겠지. 한 집안에 바보 같은 유언장 하나면 충분하다는 거.\"\n\n\"바보가 되는 길도 한 가지만 있는 게 아니지.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"나는 내 돈을 하수구에 쏟아붓도록 남기지 않을 거고, 아프리카에서 온 업둥이한테 남기지도 않을 거야. 나는 그렇게 빚어진 페더스톤들이 좋지, 이름만 갖다 붙여 페더스톤이 된 자가 아니라.\"\n\n솔로몬은 와울 부인을 배웅하러 일어서면서 이 말들을 그녀에게 큰 소리로 곁말처럼 건넸다. 조나 형은 자신이 이보다 훨씬 더 쏘는 듯한 익살을 부릴 수 있다고 느꼈지만, 자신이 곧 이어받을 이름을 쓰게 될 익살꾼들에게 그 새 주인이 환대할 뜻이 전혀 없다고 확실히 알기 전까지는, 스톤 코트의 새 소유주의 비위를 거스를 까닭이 없다고 생각했다.\n\n사실 조슈아 리그 씨는 어떤 빈정거림에도 거의 신경 쓰지 않는 듯했으나, 눈에 띄게 태도를 바꾸어 스탠디시 씨에게 태연히 걸어가서는 사무적인 질문을 매우 침착하게 던졌다. 그는 높고 짹짹거리는 목소리에 천박한 말씨를 지니고 있었다. 더는 그를 보고 웃을 마음이 들지 않는 프레드는 그가 자기가 본 가장 천박한 괴물이라고 생각했다. 그러나 프레드는 속이 좀 메스꺼웠다. 미들마치의 포목상은 리그 씨와 대화를 나눌 기회를 노리고 있었다. 그 새 소유주가 몇 켤레나 되는 다리에 양말을 필요로 할지 알 수 없는 노릇이었고, 이문은 유산보다 더 믿을 만한 것이었으니 말이다. 게다가 포목상은 육촌으로서 호기심을 느낄 만큼 이해관계에서 벗어나 있기도 했다.\n\n빈시 씨는 한 차례 터뜨린 뒤로 거만하게 침묵을 지키고 있었다. 불쾌한 감정에 너무 사로잡혀 자리를 뜰 생각조차 못 하고 있다가, 아내가 프레드 곁으로 가서 사랑하는 아들의 손을 잡고 소리 없이 울고 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 곧바로 일어나 일동에게 등을 돌린 채 그녀에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"기죽지 마, 루시. 이 사람들 앞에서 바보처럼 굴지 마, 여보.\" 그러고는 평소의 큰 목소리로 덧붙였다. \"가서 사두마차를 대령하라 일러라, 프레드. 나는 허비할 시간이 없다.\"\n\n메리 가스는 그보다 앞서 아버지와 함께 집으로 갈 채비를 하고 있었다. 그녀는 현관에서 프레드와 마주쳤고, 이제야 비로소 그를 바라볼 용기가 났다. 그의 얼굴에는 젊은 얼굴에 이따금 떠오르곤 하는, 시든 듯한 창백함이 어려 있었고, 그녀가 그의 손을 잡았을 때 그 손은 몹시 차가웠다. 메리도 동요하고 있었다. 자기 뜻과는 무관하게, 운명적으로, 어쩌면 자신이 프레드의 운명에 커다란 차이를 빚어냈을지도 모른다는 것을 의식하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"잘 가요.\" 그녀가 애정 어린 슬픔을 담아 말했다. \"용기를 내요, 프레드. 난 정말로 당신이 그 돈이 없는 편이 더 낫다고 믿어요. 그 돈이 페더스톤 씨에게 무슨 소용이었어요?\"\n\n\"그거야 다 듣기 좋은 소리지.\" 프레드가 토라져 말했다. \"사내가 어떻게 해야 한단 말이오? 난 이제 성직자가 될 수밖에 없어.\" (그는 이 말이 메리의 비위를 거스르리라는 것을 알았다. 좋아, 그렇다면 그녀가 다른 무엇을 할 수 있는지 말해 보라지.) \"게다가 난 당신 아버지께 곧장 돈을 갚고 모든 걸 바로잡을 수 있으리라 생각했는데. 그런데 당신은 백 파운드조차 남겨 받지 못했잖아. 이제 어쩔 거요, 메리?\"\n\n\"물론 일자리를 구하는 대로 또 다른 자리로 가야지요. 우리 아버지는 저 없이도 식구들을 건사하느라 벅찬걸요. 잘 가요.\"\n\n아주 짧은 시간 만에 스톤 코트에서는 제대로 빚어진 페더스톤들과 그 밖에 오랫동안 드나들던 방문객들이 모두 빠져나갔다. 또 한 사람의 낯선 이가 미들마치 인근에 정착하게 되었으나, 리그 페더스톤 씨의 경우에는 그의 존재가 장차 미칠 영향에 대한 추측보다 당장 눈앞에 드러난 결과에 대한 불만이 더 컸다. 조슈아 리그가 시험대에 올랐을 때 무엇이 드러날지를 미리 점칠 만큼 선견지명을 지닌 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n그리고 여기서 나는 자연히 비천한 소재를 격상시키는 방법에 대해 생각하게 된다. 역사적 유추는 이 점에서 놀랄 만큼 효과적이다. 거기에 대한 주된 이견이라면, 부지런한 서술자가 지면이 부족하거나 (흔히 같은 말이지만) 그 유추를 충분히 구체적으로 떠올리지 못할 수 있다는 점이다. 비록 그것이 알려지기만 한다면 좋은 예증이 되리라는 철학적 확신은 품고 있을지라도 말이다. 좀 더 쉽고 빠른 품위의 길은 이렇게 말하는 것 같다. 즉, 후작 자리에 원숭이를 앉히고 그 반대로도 할 수 있는 우화로 풀어내지 못할 참된 이야기란 결코 없었으니, 내가 비천한 사람들에 관해 이야기했거나 앞으로 이야기할 무엇이든 우화로 여겨짐으로써 고상해질 수 있다는 것이다. 그리하여 어떤 나쁜 습관과 추한 결과가 눈앞에 펼쳐진다 하더라도, 독자는 그것을 비유적으로나 천박할 뿐이라고 여기는 위안을 얻을 수 있고, 자신이 사실상 얼마간 품격 있는 사람들과 어울리고 있다고 느낄 수 있다. 이렇게 하여 내가 멍청이들에 관한 진실을 이야기하는 동안에도, 독자의 상상력은 영주들과 함께하는 일에서 완전히 배제될 필요가 없으며, 지위 높은 어떤 파산자라도 그것만 쥐고 물러나기는 서글퍼할 자잘한 금액도, 비례하는 영(零)들을 비용 한 푼 들이지 않고 덧붙임으로써 거대한 상업 거래의 수준으로 끌어올릴 수 있다.\n\n* 구유 속의 개: 이솝 우화에서, 제 먹지도 못할 건초를 깔고 누워 소가 먹지도 못하게 하는 개. 자기에게는 쓸모없으면서 남도 누리지 못하게 하는 심술을 가리킨다.\n\n# 제33장\n\n마땅히 현명해야 할, 저 높이 솟구치는 위대한 영혼들이여.\n. . . . . . . .\n\n위대한 영혼들의 본성이란 자신이 가장\n빼어나 보일 수 있는 곳에 있기를 사랑하는 법,\n\n그들은 어울려 지내는 우리보다 자신을\n한껏 드높이 헤아리며,\n\n우리가 그들의 모든 언행을 얼마나 경탄하고\n우러르는가를 상상하나니, 그리하여 그들은\n\n우리의 찬탄을 더욱 극진히 만들고자 애쓰며,\n자신의 극단적이고 드높은 사상을 알리지 않고서는\n\n그리할 수 없다고 여기는 것이다.\n—대니얼, 『필로타스의 비극』\n\n빈시 씨는 유언장을 다 듣고 여러 문제에 대한 자신의 견해가 상당히 바뀐 채 집으로 돌아갔다. 그는 너그러운 사람이었으나 자기 생각을 에둘러 표현하는 버릇이 있었다. 비단 끈 시장에서 실망스러운 일이 생기면 마부에게 욕을 퍼부었고, 처남인 불스트로드가 그를 화나게 하면 감리교에 대해 신랄한 말을 늘어놓았다. 그리고 그가 자수 놓은 모자를 흡연실에서 현관 바닥으로 내던지는 것을 보면, 이제 프레드의 게으름을 갑작스레 한층 엄하게 여기고 있음이 분명했다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야.\" 그 젊은이가 자러 가려고 일어서자 그가 말했다. \"이제 다음 학기에 올라가서 시험을 통과하기로 마음을 굳혔기를 바란다. 나는 결심을 했으니, 너도 지체 없이 결심하기를 권한다.\"\n\n프레드는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 너무도 완전히 풀이 죽어 있었다. 스물네 시간 전만 해도 그는 자신이 무엇을 해야 할지 알아야 할 필요가 없을 뿐 아니라, 이제쯤이면 아무것도 할 필요가 없다는 것을 알게 되리라고 생각했었다. 분홍빛 사냥복을 입고 사냥을 다니고, 일류 사냥말을 가지고, 멋진 승용마를 타고 사냥터로 나가며, 그렇게 하는 것으로 두루 존경을 받으리라고. 게다가 곧바로 가스 씨에게 빚을 갚을 수 있을 테고, 그러면 메리도 더 이상 그와 결혼하지 않을 이유가 없으리라고. 그리고 이 모든 것이 공부나 다른 수고 없이, 순전히 한 노신사의 변덕이라는 형태로 찾아온 섭리의 은총에 의해 이루어질 터였다. 그런데 이제 스물네 시간이 지나자 그 확고했던 기대가 모조리 뒤집혀 버렸다. 이 실망으로 쓰라려하는 와중에, 마치 그것이 자기 탓이라도 되는 양 취급받는 것은 \"다소 가혹한 처사\"였다. 하지만 그는 말없이 자리를 떠났고, 그의 어머니가 그를 위해 변호하고 나섰다.\n\"불쌍한 아이에게 모질게 굴지 말아요, 여보. 저 사악한 자가 그 애를 속였다고는 해도, 그 애는 아직 잘될 거예요. 내가 여기 앉아 있는 것만큼이나 확실히 느껴져요, 프레드는 잘될 거라고요. 그렇지 않다면 왜 그 애가 무덤 가장자리에서 다시 살아 돌아왔겠어요? 그리고 나는 이걸 도둑질이라고 부르겠어요. 약속을 했으니 땅을 준 거나 마찬가지죠. 모두가 믿게 만드는 것이 약속이 아니라면 약속이 대체 뭐겠어요? 그리고 보세요, 그 사람은 그 애에게 만 파운드를 남기더니 다시 빼앗아 갔잖아요.\"\n\n\"다시 빼앗아 갔다고!\" 빈시 씨가 짜증스레 말했다. \"그 녀석은 운이 없는 녀석이오, 루시. 그리고 당신은 늘 그 애를 버릇없이 키웠지.\"\n\n\"하지만 여보, 그 애는 내 첫아이였고, 그 애가 태어났을 때 당신도 야단스럽게 좋아했잖아요. 어찌나 자랑스러워했는지.\" 빈시 부인이 손쉽게 명랑한 미소를 되찾으며 말했다.\n\"갓난아기가 어떻게 될지 누가 알겠소? 내가 어리석었던 게지.\" 남편이 말했으나, 그래도 한결 누그러진 어조였다.\n\n\"하지만 우리 애들보다 더 잘생기고 더 훌륭한 자식을 둔 사람이 어디 있어요? 프레드는 다른 집 아들들보다 훨씬 뛰어나요. 말씨를 들어 보면 대학에서 좋은 사람들과 어울렸다는 걸 알 수 있죠. 그리고 로저먼드는요, 그 애 같은 처녀가 어디 있겠어요? 이 나라 어느 귀부인 옆에 서더라도 오히려 더 돋보일 거예요. 보세요, 리드게이트 씨는 최상류층과 어울리며 안 가 본 데가 없는 사람인데도 그 애에게 단번에 반했잖아요. 하긴 로저먼드가 약혼하지 않았더라면 하는 마음도 있긴 해요. 손님으로 갔다가 훨씬 나은 혼처를 만났을지도 모르니까요. 그 애 학교 친구 윌러비 양 댁 말이에요. 그 집안에는 리드게이트 씨 못지않게 지체 높은 친척들이 있거든요.\"\n\n\"친척이고 뭐고 빌어먹을!\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. \"친척이라면 이제 지긋지긋하오. 나는 친척 말고는 내세울 게 없는 사위는 바라지 않소.\"\n\n\"어머나 여보, 당신은 그 일을 더없이 기뻐하는 것 같았는데요.\" 빈시 부인이 말했다. \"사실 그때 나는 집에 없었지만, 당신이 약혼에 대해 한마디도 반대하지 않았다고 로저먼드가 그러던걸요. 그리고 그 애는 속옷감으로 최고급 아마포와 고급 무명을 사들이기 시작했어요.\"\n\"내 뜻은 아니오.\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. \"올해는 게으른 망나니 아들놈 때문에라도 할 일이 태산인데, 혼수 옷감 값까지 치를 여유는 없소. 시절은 더없이 빡빡하고, 다들 망해 가고 있소. 그리고 나는 리드게이트가 한 푼이라도 가지고 있다고 믿지 않소. 나는 그들의 결혼에 동의해 주지 않겠소. 어른들이 전에 그랬던 것처럼 그들도 기다리게 합시다.\"\n\n\"로저먼드가 가슴 아파할 거예요, 여보. 당신은 그 애 뜻을 거스르는 걸 견디지 못하잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니, 견딜 수 있소. 약혼은 깨질수록 좋소. 저렇게 처신하는 걸 보면 평생 변변한 수입을 올릴 것 같지 않소. 적만 만들고 다닐 뿐이지. 내가 그 사람에 대해 듣는 거라곤 그게 전부요.\"\n\"하지만 그 사람은 불스트로드 씨에게 신망이 아주 두텁잖아요, 여보. 그 결혼은 그분도 좋아하실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"좋아하긴 무슨!\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. \"불스트로드가 그들 생활비를 대 주진 않을 거요. 그리고 리드게이트가 내가 그들 살림 차리는 데 돈을 내줄 거라고 생각한다면 오산이오, 그뿐이오. 나는 곧 말들을 처분해야 할 판이오. 로지에게 내 말을 전하는 게 좋겠소.\"\n이것은 빈시 씨에게 드물지 않은 행동 방식이었다. 유쾌하게 덜컥 동의해 놓고는, 나중에 자신이 경솔했음을 깨닫고는 그 무례한 철회를 다른 사람에게 떠맡기는 것이다. 어쨌거나 결코 남편에게 기꺼이 맞서는 법이 없는 빈시 부인은 이튿날 아침 지체 없이 로저먼드에게 그가 한 말을 전했다. 모슬린 자수 작업을 살펴보고 있던 로저먼드는 말없이 듣더니, 다 듣고 나서 우아한 목을 어떤 식으로 갸웃했는데, 그것이 완벽한 고집을 뜻한다는 것은 오랜 경험을 통해서만 알 수 있는 일이었다.\n\n\"넌 어떻게 생각하니, 얘야?\" 어머니가 애정 어린 조심스러움으로 물었다.\n\n\"아빠는 전혀 그런 뜻이 아니에요.\" 로저먼드가 아주 차분하게 말했다. \"아빠는 늘 제가 사랑하는 사람과 결혼하기를 바란다고 말씀하셨어요. 그리고 저는 리드게이트 씨와 결혼할 거예요. 아빠가 동의하신 지 벌써 칠 주가 지났어요. 그리고 저는 우리가 브레턴 부인 댁을 얻을 수 있기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 네 아빠 문제는 네가 알아서 하렴. 너는 늘 모두를 잘 다루니까. 하지만 우리가 정말 다마스크 천을 사러 가게 된다면, 새들러네가 좋단다. 홉킨스네보다 훨씬 낫지. 그런데 브레턴 부인 댁은 아주 크던데. 네가 그런 집을 갖는다면야 나도 정말 좋겠지만, 가구가 엄청나게 들 거야. 양탄자에 온갖 것들, 게다가 은식기와 유리그릇까지. 그리고 너도 들었다시피 네 아빠는 돈을 한 푼도 안 주겠다고 하시잖니. 리드게이트 씨가 그걸 기대하고 있을 것 같니?\"\n\n\"제가 그분께 그런 걸 부탁하리라고는 상상도 못 하시겠죠, 엄마. 물론 그분은 자기 일을 알아서 하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분도 돈을 기대하고 있었을지 모르잖니, 얘야. 우리도 다들 네가 프레드처럼 두둑한 유산을 받으리라 생각했고. 그런데 이제 모든 게 이렇게 끔찍하게 됐으니, 저 불쌍한 애가 저렇게 실망한 마당에 뭘 생각해도 기쁘지가 않구나.\"\n\n\"그건 제 결혼과는 아무 상관 없어요, 엄마. 프레드는 게으름을 그만둬야 해요. 저는 이 작업을 모건 양에게 가져다주러 위층에 올라갈 거예요. 그분은 오픈 헤밍을 아주 잘하시거든요. 메리 가스도 이제 제 일을 좀 해 줄 수 있을 것 같아요. 그 애 바느질 솜씨는 일품이죠. 메리에 대해 내가 아는 것 중 가장 좋은 점이에요. 제 고급 무명 주름 장식을 전부 이중으로 감치고 싶거든요. 그건 시간이 오래 걸려요.\"\n\n로저먼드가 아빠를 다룰 수 있다는 빈시 부인의 믿음은 근거가 충분했다. 만찬과 사냥을 빼면, 빈시 씨는 큰소리는 쳤어도 마치 총리대신이라도 된 양 제 마음대로 하는 일이 거의 없었다. 쾌락을 좋아하는 혈색 좋은 사내들 대부분이 그러하듯, 상황의 힘이 그에게는 쉽사리 감당하기 벅찼던 것이다. 그리고 로저먼드라는 이름의 상황은, 우리가 알다시피 부드러운 살아 있는 물질이 가로막는 바위를 뚫고 제 길을 내게 하는 그 온화한 끈기를 통해 특히나 강력했다. 아빠는 바위가 아니었다. 그에게는 때로 습관이라 불리는, 번갈아 이는 충동의 고정성 말고는 다른 고정성이 없었으니, 이는 딸의 약혼과 관련해 유일하게 단호한 행동 노선을 취하는 데 전혀 도움이 되지 않았다. 그 노선이란 곧 리드게이트의 형편을 철저히 알아보고, 자신은 돈을 댈 능력이 없음을 선언하며, 성급한 결혼이든 지나치게 길어질 약혼이든 똑같이 금지하는 것이었다. 말로 하면 아주 간단하고 쉬워 보인다. 하지만 아침의 쌀쌀한 시간에 세운 언짢은 결심은 이른 서리만큼이나 그것을 가로막는 조건들이 많아, 낮의 따스한 기운 아래서는 좀처럼 지속되지 못했다. 빈시 씨가 즐겨 쓰는, 에두르되 강조하는 의견 표현 방식도 이번 경우에는 크게 억제될 수밖에 없었다. 리드게이트는 자존심 강한 사내라 그에게 넌지시 빈정대는 것은 분명 안전하지 못했고, 모자를 바닥에 내던지는 것 따위는 어림도 없었다. 빈시 씨는 그를 조금 두려워했고, 그가 로저먼드와 결혼하고 싶어 한다는 데 조금 우쭐했으며, 자기 처지가 유리하지 못한 돈 문제를 꺼내기를 조금 꺼렸고, 자기보다 교육을 잘 받고 가문이 좋은 사내와 대화에서 밀릴까 조금 두려웠으며, 딸이 싫어할 일을 하는 것이 조금 무서웠다. 빈시 씨가 즐겨 맡고 싶어 한 역할은 누구도 흉보지 않는 너그러운 주인이라는 역할이었다. 하루의 전반부에는 반대 결심을 정식으로 전달하는 것을 가로막는 일거리가 있었고, 후반부에는 만찬과 술과 휘스트와 두루두루 흡족한 기분이 있었다. 그리고 그러는 사이에 시간은 저마다 작은 침전물을 남기며 점차 무위(無爲)의 최종적인 이유를 빚어냈으니, 곧 행동하기에는 이미 너무 늦었다는 것이었다. 약혼을 받아들여진 그 연인은 저녁 대부분을 로윅 게이트에서 보냈고, 장인의 자금 지원이나 직업에서 나올 장래 수입 따위에는 전혀 기대지 않는 사랑놀이가 빈시 씨의 두 눈앞에서 무성하게 진행되고 있었다. 젊은 날의 사랑놀이, 그 거미줄 같은 것이여! 그것이 매달리는 점들마저, 그 미묘한 얽힘이 걸려 흔들리는 그 지점들마저 거의 알아챌 수 없을 정도다. 손끝의 순간적인 스침, 푸른 눈동자와 검은 눈동자에서 나온 빛줄기들의 마주침, 끝맺지 못한 말들, 뺨과 입술의 더없이 가벼운 변화, 가장 희미한 떨림. 그 거미줄 자체는 자연스러운 믿음과 형언할 수 없는 기쁨, 한 생명이 다른 생명을 향해 품는 갈망, 온전함의 환상, 막연한 신뢰로 이루어진다. 그리고 리드게이트는 로르의 비극으로 끝났으리라 여겨지는 경험에도 불구하고, 또 의학과 생물학에도 불구하고, 자신의 내면에서 그 거미줄을 놀라운 속도로 자아내기 시작했다. 물에 불린 근육이나 접시에 담긴 눈알(성녀 루치아의 것처럼)을 살펴보는 일, 그 밖의 과학적 탐구의 사례들은, 타고난 둔감함이나 가장 저속한 산문에 대한 활기찬 탐닉보다는 시적인 사랑과 덜 양립 불가능한 것으로 관찰되기 때문이다. 로저먼드로 말하자면, 그녀는 제 충만해진 생명에 대한 수련의 활짝 피어나는 경이로움에 잠겨 있었고, 그녀 역시 부지런히 그 공동의 거미줄을 자아내고 있었다. 이 모든 일은 피아노가 놓인 응접실 구석에서 벌어졌고, 그것이 아무리 미묘했다 한들, 그 빛은 그것을 페어브러더 씨 말고도 많은 사람들의 눈에 보이는 일종의 무지개로 만들어 놓았다. 빈시 양과 리드게이트 씨가 약혼했다는 확실한 사실은 정식 발표의 도움 없이도 미들마치에 두루 퍼졌다.\n\n불스트로드 이모는 다시금 마음이 불안해졌다. 하지만 이번에는 빈시 부인의 변덕스러움을 일부러 피하려고 창고로 직접 찾아가 오빠에게 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그의 대답은 만족스럽지 못했다.\n\n\"월터, 설마 리드게이트 씨의 장래에 대해 알아보지도 않고 이 모든 일이 진행되도록 내버려 뒀다는 건 아니겠죠?\" 불스트로드 부인이 창고 일로 짜증이 나 있는 오빠를 향해 더욱 큰 진지함으로 눈을 크게 뜨며 말했다. \"사치스럽게, 미안한 말이지만 너무 세속적으로 자란 그 애를 생각해 봐요. 적은 수입으로 그 애가 어떻게 살아가겠어요?\"\n\n\"아, 빌어먹을, 해리엇! 내가 청하지도 않았는데 사내들이 이 동네로 들어오는데 나더러 어쩌란 말이냐? 너는 리드게이트한테 네 집 문을 닫아걸기라도 했더냐? 불스트로드가 누구보다도 그를 밀어 줬어. 나는 그 젊은이에 대해 야단법석을 떤 적이 없다. 그 문제는 나한테 올 게 아니라 네 남편한테 가서 이야기해야지.\"\n\n\"하지만 정말, 월터, 불스트로드 씨가 어떻게 잘못이 있겠어요? 그분은 분명히 이 약혼을 바라지 않으셨는데요.\"\n\n\"아, 불스트로드가 그를 거두지 않았더라면 나는 그를 초대하는 일도 결코 없었을 거다.\"\n\n\"하지만 오빠가 그분을 불러 프레드를 봐 달라고 했고, 저는 그게 다행한 일이었다고 생각해요.\" 불스트로드 부인이 복잡한 화제 속에서 갈피를 놓치며 말했다.\n\n\"다행인지 어떤지는 모르겠다.\" 빈시 씨가 신경질적으로 말했다. \"나는 내 식구들 때문에 내가 바라는 것보다 더 골치를 썩고 있다는 건 안다. 해리엇, 나는 네가 불스트로드와 결혼하기 전에 너에게 좋은 오빠였어. 그런데 그자는 네 식구한테 마땅히 기대할 법한 그런 우애 어린 마음을 늘 보여 주는 건 아니라고 말할 수밖에 없구나.\" 빈시 씨는 예수회 수도사와는 거리가 멀었으나, 아무리 노련한 예수회 수도사라도 화제를 그보다 더 능란하게 돌려놓지는 못했을 것이다. 해리엇은 오빠를 탓하는 대신 남편을 변호해야 했고, 대화는 시작점으로부터 멀찍이 떨어진 지점에서 끝나고 말았으니, 마치 최근에 교구위원회에서 두 사람이 벌인 옥신각신만큼이나 동떨어진 지점이었다.\n\n불스트로드 부인은 오빠의 불평을 남편에게 되풀이하지 않았으나, 저녁에 리드게이트와 로저먼드 이야기를 그에게 꺼냈다. 하지만 그는 그녀의 뜨거운 관심을 함께 나누지 않았고, 다만 의료 개업 초기에 따르는 위험과 신중함의 필요성에 대해 체념한 듯 말할 따름이었다.\n\n\"그렇게 자란 그 철없는 애를 위해 우리가 기도해야 한다는 건 분명해요.\" 불스트로드 부인이 남편의 감정을 일깨우고 싶어 하며 말했다.\n\n\"참으로 그렇소, 여보.\" 불스트로드 씨가 동의하며 말했다. \"이 세상에 속하지 않은 자들은 고집스레 세속적인 자들의 잘못을 막기 위해 달리 할 수 있는 일이 거의 없소. 당신 오빠 식구에 관해서는 그 점을 인정하는 데 우리 스스로 익숙해져야 하오. 나는 리드게이트 씨가 그런 혼인을 맺지 않았더라면 했소만, 그와 나의 관계는 그가 지닌 재능을 하느님의 목적을 위해 쓰는 일에 한정되어 있소. 그것은 각 시대의 섭리 아래 신의 통치가 우리에게 가르치는 바요.\"\n\n불스트로드 부인은 더 이상 말하지 않았고, 자신이 느낀 어떤 불만을 자기 자신의 영성이 부족한 탓으로 돌렸다. 그녀는 남편이 죽으면 그 회고록을 마땅히 써야 할 그런 사람들 중 하나라고 믿었다.\n\n리드게이트 자신으로 말하자면, 약혼이 받아들여진 이상, 그는 자신이 완벽하게 명료히 내다본다고 믿는 모든 결과를 받아들일 각오가 되어 있었다. 물론 그는 일 년 안에, 어쩌면 반년 안에라도 결혼해야 했다. 이것은 그가 의도했던 바가 아니었으나, 다른 계획들이 방해받지는 않을 터였다. 그저 새로이 조정되기만 하면 되었다. 결혼은 물론 통상적인 방식으로 준비해야 했다. 지금 쓰고 있는 셋방 대신 집 한 채를 얻어야 했고, 리드게이트는 로저먼드가 (로윅 게이트에 자리한) 늙은 브레턴 부인의 집을 감탄하며 말하는 것을 들었던 터라, 그 노부인이 죽고 집이 비자 그것을 눈여겨보았다가 즉시 매입 협상에 들어갔다.\n\n그는 이 일을 마치 곁다리로 처리하듯, 완벽한 의복에 필요한 일체를 재단사에게 주문할 때와 똑같이 했으니, 사치를 부린다는 생각 따위는 전혀 없었다. 오히려 그는 비용을 과시하는 짓은 경멸했을 것이다. 그의 직업은 그를 온갖 등급의 가난에 익숙해지게 했고, 그는 곤궁을 겪는 이들을 깊이 염려했다. 그는 손잡이가 떨어진 항아리에 소스가 담겨 나오는 식탁에서도 흠잡을 데 없이 처신했을 것이고, 성대한 만찬에 대해서도 말솜씨 좋은 사내가 한 사람 있었다는 것 말고는 아무것도 기억하지 못했을 것이다. 하지만 그는 자신이 이른바 평범한 방식 외에 다른 방식으로 살게 되리라고는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었으니, 그 평범한 방식이란 호크 포도주를 담는 초록빛 유리잔과 식탁에서의 훌륭한 시중을 갖춘 것이었다. 프랑스 사회 이론으로 몸을 녹이면서도 그는 그을린 냄새는 조금도 묻혀 오지 않았다. 우리의 가구와 우리가 베푸는 만찬, 그리고 자기 경우에는 문장(紋章)을 선호하는 취향이 우리를 기성 질서에 풀 수 없이 묶어 두는 한, 우리는 극단적인 의견조차 탈 없이 다룰 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 리드게이트의 성향은 극단적인 의견 쪽으로 기울지 않았다. 그는 자기 부츠에 까다로운 사람이었던 만큼 맨발의 교리 따위는 좋아하지 않았을 것이다. 그는 의학 개혁과 발견의 추구를 제외하고는 무엇에 관해서도 급진주의자가 아니었다. 나머지 실생활에서 그는 물려받은 습관에 따라 걸었으니, 그 절반은 내가 이미 통속성이라 일컬은 그 개인적 자존심과 무반성적인 이기심에서 비롯되었고, 나머지 절반은 좋아하는 생각에 골몰한 데서 나온 그 순진함에서 비롯되었다.\n\n자기도 모르게 슬그머니 다가든 이 약혼의 결과에 대해 리드게이트가 마음속으로 벌인 논쟁이 있었다면, 그것은 돈의 부족보다는 시간의 부족을 둘러싼 것이었다. 분명, 사랑에 빠져 있고, 기억이 그려 내는 것보다 늘 더 예뻐 보이는 어떤 사람이 끊임없이 자기를 기다리고 있다는 사실은, 임박한 위대한 발견을 어느 \"꾸준한 독일인 친구\"가 이뤄 내는 데 쓰일 법한 여가 시간을 부지런히 활용하는 일을 방해했다. 이것은 결혼을 너무 오래 미루지 말아야 할 진짜 이유였으니, 어느 날 목사가 자기 것보다 더 좋은 현미경으로 들여다보고 싶은 연못 생물을 가지고 그의 방에 들렀다가, 리드게이트의 탁자가 기구와 표본으로 어지러운 것을 보고는 비꼬듯 말했을 때 그는 그런 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"에로스가 타락했군. 처음에는 질서와 조화를 들여오더니, 이제는 혼돈을 도로 끌어오는구먼.\"\n\n\"네, 어떤 단계에서는요.\" 리드게이트가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 미소 짓고는 현미경을 정돈하기 시작하며 말했다. \"하지만 그 후에는 더 나은 질서가 시작될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"곧?\" 목사가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그러기를 바랍니다. 이 어수선한 상태가 시간을 잡아먹는데, 과학에 관해 어떤 착상을 품고 있을 때는 매 순간이 기회거든요. 저는 꾸준히 일하고 싶은 사람에게 결혼이 분명 가장 좋은 것이라고 확신합니다. 그러면 모든 것이 집 안에 갖춰져 있고, 개인적인 궁리로 시달릴 일도 없고, 평온과 자유를 얻을 수 있죠.\"\n\n\"자네는 부러운 친구로군.\" 목사가 말했다. \"그런 전망을 가졌으니 말이야. 로저먼드, 평온, 자유, 죄다 자네 몫이지 않은가. 그런데 나는 내 파이프와 연못의 미생물밖에 없으니. 자, 이제 준비됐나?\"\n\n리드게이트는 구애 기간을 단축하고 싶은 또 다른 이유를 목사에게 말하지 않았다. 사랑의 술이 핏줄을 타고 흐르는 와중에도, 빈시 가의 가족 모임에 그토록 자주 끼어들어, 미들마치의 잡담과 한없이 이어지는 환담과 휘스트 놀이와 두루 쓸데없는 짓에 그토록 깊이 빠져들어야 하는 것은 그에게 다소 짜증스러운 일이었다. 빈시 씨가 무지를 단칼에 휘두르며 문제들을 단정 지을 때, 특히 어떤 술이 나쁜 공기의 영향으로부터 사람을 지켜 주는 최고의 속 절임이 되느냐 하는 문제에 대해 그럴 때면, 그는 공손한 태도를 보여야 했다. 빈시 부인의 솔직함과 단순함은 자신이 사위 될 사람의 취향에 끼칠지도 모를 미묘한 거슬림에 대한 의심이라곤 한 가닥도 섞여 있지 않았다. 그래서 대체로 리드게이트는 로저먼드의 집안과 관련해서는 자신이 조금 격이 떨어진다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 하지만 그 빼어난 여인 자신도 같은 식으로 괴로움을 겪고 있었다. 그녀와 결혼함으로써 그녀에게 절실히 필요한 옮겨심기를 해 줄 수 있다는 것은 적어도 하나의 즐거운 생각이었다.\n\n\"여보!\" 어느 날 저녁, 그가 그녀 곁에 앉아 그녀의 얼굴을 가까이 들여다보며 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말했다.\n\n하지만 먼저 말해 두어야겠다. 그는 그녀가 응접실에 혼자 있는 것을 보았는데, 거의 방 한쪽 벽만큼이나 큰 그 구식의 커다란 창문은 집 뒤편 정원의 여름 향기를 향해 열려 있었다. 그녀의 아버지와 어머니는 파티에 가고 없었고, 나머지 식구들은 모두 나비를 쫓아 밖에 나가 있었다.\n\n\"여보! 눈꺼풀이 빨갛군요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"왜 그럴까요.\" 소망이나 불평을 쏟아 내는 것은 그녀의 천성이 아니었다. 그것들은 청을 받을 때라야 우아하게 흘러나왔다.\n\n\"나한테 그걸 숨길 수 있다는 듯이!\" 리드게이트가 그녀의 두 손 위에 다정하게 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"속눈썹 하나에 작은 물방울이 맺힌 게 안 보일 것 같소? 무언가 당신을 괴롭히는데도 나한테 말하지 않다니. 그건 사랑이 없는 거요.\"\n\n\"당신이 바꿀 수도 없는 걸 무엇하러 말하겠어요? 그건 날마다 있는 일들인걸요. 요즘 들어 좀 더 심해졌을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"집안의 골칫거리로군. 말하기를 두려워하지 마요. 짐작이 가니까.\"\n\n\"아빠가 요즘 더 신경질적이에요. 프레드가 아빠를 화나게 하고, 오늘 아침에도 또 새로 다툼이 있었어요. 프레드가 받은 교육을 전부 내팽개치고 자기 격에 한참 못 미치는 일을 하겠다고 으름장을 놓았거든요. 그리고 또—\"\n\n로저먼드가 머뭇거렸고, 그녀의 뺨에는 옅은 홍조가 번지고 있었다. 리드게이트는 약혼한 날 아침 이후로 그녀가 괴로워하는 모습을 본 적이 없었고, 이 순간만큼 그녀에게 열정적인 감정을 느껴 본 적도 없었다. 그는 마치 그 입술을 격려하기라도 하듯, 머뭇거리는 그 입술에 부드럽게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"아빠가 우리 약혼을 그다지 좋아하지 않으시는 것 같아요.\" 로저먼드가 거의 속삭이듯 말을 이었다. \"그리고 어젯밤에는 당신에게 꼭 말씀하셔서, 약혼을 깨야 한다고 하시겠다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그걸 깰 거요?\" 리드게이트가 빠르고 격하게, 거의 화난 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"저는 제가 하기로 마음먹은 일은 절대 포기하지 않아요.\" 로저먼드가 이 심금을 건드리는 말에 차분함을 되찾으며 말했다.\n\n\"신의 축복을 받기를!\" 리드게이트가 그녀에게 다시 입맞추며 말했다. 올바른 자리에서 발휘되는 이 한결같은 의지는 사랑스러웠다. 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이제 당신 아버지가 우리 약혼을 깨야 한다고 말하기에는 너무 늦었소. 당신은 성년이고, 나는 당신이 내 사람이라고 주장하오. 무엇이든 당신을 불행하게 만드는 일이 생긴다면, 그건 우리 결혼을 서둘러야 할 이유요.\"\n\n그의 눈과 마주친 푸른 눈동자에서 틀림없는 기쁨이 빛났고, 그 광채는 그의 모든 앞날을 온화한 햇살로 환히 비추는 듯했다. (『아라비안나이트』에 나오는 그런 종류의) 이상적인 행복, 곧 거리의 노동과 불화에서 벗어나 모든 것이 주어지되 아무것도 요구되지 않는 낙원으로 발을 들이라는 초대를 받는 그런 행복이, 길어야 몇 주를 기다리면 될 일처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n\"우리가 왜 그것을 미뤄야 하오?\" 그가 열렬히 우기며 말했다. \"이제 집도 얻었소. 나머지는 모두 곧 마련할 수 있을 거요. 그렇지 않소? 당신은 새 옷에 연연하지 않겠지. 그런 건 나중에 사면 되니까.\"\n\n\"똑똑한 분들은 어쩌면 그렇게 독창적인 생각을 하시는지!\" 로저먼드가 이 우스꽝스러운 엇박자에 평소보다 한결 흐드러진 웃음으로 보조개를 지으며 말했다. \"혼수 옷을 결혼한 뒤에 산다는 말은 난생처음 들어 봐요.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 설마 옷 때문에 나더러 몇 달이나 기다리라고 고집부리려는 건 아니겠지?\" 리드게이트가 로저먼드가 자기를 어여쁘게 애태우고 있는 거라고 반쯤 생각하면서도, 그녀가 정말로 성급한 결혼을 꺼리는 것은 아닐까 반쯤 두려워하며 말했다. \"기억해요, 우리는 이보다 더 나은 종류의 행복을 고대하고 있다는 걸. 늘 함께 있고, 남들에게 얽매이지 않고, 우리 뜻대로 삶을 꾸려 가는 행복 말이오. 자, 여보, 얼마나 빨리 온전히 내 사람이 될 수 있는지 말해 줘요.\"\n\n리드게이트의 어조에는 진지한 호소가 담겨 있었으니, 마치 그녀가 변덕스러운 지연으로 자기에게 해를 입히리라 느끼는 듯했다. 로저먼드도 진지해졌고, 약간 생각에 잠겼다. 사실 그녀는 적어도 어림셈이라도 될 만한 대답을 내놓기 위해, 레이스 가장자리며 양말이며 페티코트 주름잡기의 온갖 복잡한 사정을 머릿속으로 헤아리고 있었다.\n\n\"육 주면 넉넉할 거요. 그렇다고 말해 줘요, 로저먼드.\" 리드게이트가 그녀의 손을 놓고 그녀의 허리에 가만히 팔을 두르며 우겼다.\n\n작은 한 손이 곧바로 머리를 매만지러 올라갔고, 그녀는 생각에 잠겨 목을 갸웃하더니 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"집안 리넨이랑 가구를 마련해야 해요. 그래도 우리가 떠나 있는 동안 엄마가 그것들을 챙겨 주실 수 있겠죠.\"\n\n\"그럼, 물론이지. 우리는 일주일쯤 떠나 있어야 해요.\"\n\n\"어머, 그보다는 더요!\" 로저먼드가 간곡하게 말했다. 그녀는 신혼여행의 적어도 사 분의 일은 즐거운 일로 채워 줄 것이라며 오랫동안 속으로 바라 온, 고드윈 리드게이트 경 댁을 방문할 때 입을 야회복을 생각하고 있었다. 신학 박사이기도 한 그 백부에게 인사드리는 일은 미뤄 두더라도 말이다(혈통으로 뒷받침될 때, 그 또한 차분하긴 해도 흐뭇한 종류의 지위였다). 그녀는 그렇게 말하며 다소 의아한 항의의 눈길로 연인을 바라보았고, 그는 그녀가 둘만의 그 달콤한 고독의 시간을 늘리고 싶어 하는 것이리라고 선뜻 이해했다.\n\n\"날짜만 정해지면 당신이 바라는 대로 하리다, 내 사랑. 하지만 단호한 방침을 정해서, 당신이 겪고 있을지 모를 불편을 끝내도록 합시다. 육 주면! 정말 넉넉할 거요.\"\n\n\"준비를 서두를 수는 분명히 있을 거예요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"그럼 아빠에게 그 말씀을 드리시겠어요? 편지를 쓰시는 게 더 나을 것 같아요.\" 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 그를 바라보았는데, 그 모습은 우리가 더없이 찬란한 저녁 빛 속을 행복하게 거닐 때 정원의 꽃들이 우리를 바라보는 것 같았다. 깊은 빛깔의 중심부를 둘러싸고 환히 빛나며 숨 쉬는 그 가녀린 꽃잎들 속에는, 반은 요정이요 반은 어린아이인, 말로 다 할 수 없는 영혼이 깃들어 있지 않은가?\n\n그는 입술로 그녀의 귀와 그 아래 목의 작은 자리에 입을 맞추었고, 두 사람은 여러 분 동안 아주 가만히 앉아 있었으니, 그 시간은 햇살의 입맞춤을 받은 작은 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물처럼 그들 곁을 흘러갔다. 로저먼드는 자기만큼 사랑에 빠진 사람은 없으리라 생각했고, 리드게이트는 그 온갖 무모한 실수와 터무니없는 맹신 끝에 마침내 완벽한 여성성을 찾았노라고 생각했다. 자기의 드높은 사색과 중대한 노고를 우러르며 결코 그것을 방해하지 않을, 교양 있는 여인이 베풀어 줄 더없이 빼어난 부부의 애정이 벌써 자기에게 숨결처럼 와 닿는 것만 같았다. 그런 여인이라면 고요한 마법으로 집안과 가계부에 질서를 세우면서도, 류트를 어루만질 손가락은 언제든 준비해 두어 어느 순간에든 삶을 로맨스로 바꿔 놓을 것이고, 참된 여성의 한계까지, 그러나 터럭 한 올만큼도 그 너머로는 가지 않게끔 교육받았기에, 따라서 고분고분하고, 그 한계에서 비롯된 분부라면 기꺼이 받들 터였다. 자기가 훨씬 더 오래 독신으로 지내겠다던 생각이 잘못이었음이 이제 그 어느 때보다 분명해졌다. 결혼은 방해물이 아니라 진척을 돕는 것일 터였다. 그리고 이튿날 마침 환자 한 사람을 따라 브래싱에 갔다가, 그는 거기서 정말 안성맞춤이라 여겨지는 만찬용 식기 세트를 보고는 그 자리에서 사 버렸다. 생각났을 때 바로 이런 일들을 해 치우는 편이 시간을 아꼈고, 리드게이트는 보기 흉한 그릇을 싫어했다. 문제의 그 식기 세트는 값이 비쌌으나, 그것은 만찬용 식기 세트의 본성상 그런 것일 수도 있었다. 살림살이를 갖추는 데는 으레 돈이 들기 마련이었다. 하지만 그것은 단 한 번만 하면 되는 일이었다.\n\n\"분명 사랑스러울 거예요.\" 리드게이트가 자신이 산 물건을 몇 마디 묘사를 곁들여 이야기하자 빈시 부인이 말했다. \"우리 로지에게 꼭 어울리는 거죠. 부디 깨지지 않기를 빌어요!\"\n\n\"물건을 깨뜨리지 않을 하인을 들이면 됩니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. (분명 이것은 인과의 연쇄를 불완전하게 내다본 추론이었다. 하지만 그 시절에는 과학자들에게 다소간 용인되지 않는 추론이란 없었다.)\n\n물론 어머니에게는 무엇이든 알리기를 미룰 필요가 없었으니, 그녀는 명랑하지 않은 견해는 좀처럼 받아들이지 않았고, 자신이 행복한 아내였던 만큼 딸의 결혼에 대해서도 자랑스러운 마음 외에는 거의 다른 감정이 없었다. 하지만 로저먼드에게는 아빠에게 편지로 청해야 한다고 리드게이트에게 제안할 만한 그럴듯한 이유가 있었다. 그녀는 이튿날 아침 아빠와 함께 창고까지 걸어가면서, 가는 길에 리드게이트 씨가 곧 결혼하고 싶어 한다고 그에게 말함으로써 편지의 도착에 대비했다.\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리, 얘야!\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. \"그 사람이 무슨 밑천으로 결혼을 하겠다는 거냐? 너는 그 약혼을 그만두는 편이 훨씬 나아. 전에도 꽤 분명하게 말했잖니. 가난한 사람한테 시집가려고 한다면, 그런 교육은 무엇하러 받았단 말이냐? 아비로서 보기에 참 매정한 일이구나.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 씨는 가난하지 않아요, 아빠. 그분은 피콕 씨의 개업권을 사들였는데, 그게 연 팔구백 파운드의 값어치가 있다고들 해요.\"\n\n\"허튼소리 마라! 개업권을 사는 게 뭐 대수냐? 내년에 올 제비를 사는 거나 다름없다. 죄다 손가락 사이로 빠져나갈 게다.\"\n\n\"그 반대예요, 아빠. 그분은 개업을 키워 갈 거예요. 보세요, 체텀 가와 캐소본 가에서도 그분을 불렀잖아요.\"\n\n\"프레드 일로 이렇게 실망스러운 데다, 의회는 해산될 참이고, 사방에서 기계를 부수고 있고, 선거까지 다가오는 마당에, 내가 한 푼도 안 줄 거라는 걸 그 사람이 알고 있기를 바란다.\"\n\n\"아빠도 참! 그게 제 결혼과 무슨 상관이 있어요?\"\n\n\"상관이 아주 많지! 내가 알기로 우리 모두 거덜 날지도 몰라. 나라가 그 지경이라니까! 어떤 이들은 세상의 종말이라고도 하는데, 정말 그렇게 보이지 않으면 내 목을 내놓겠다! 어쨌든 지금은 내가 사업에서 돈을 빼낼 때가 아니고, 나는 리드게이트가 그걸 알았으면 한다.\"\n\n\"그분은 아무것도 기대하지 않으세요, 아빠. 그리고 그분은 인맥이 아주 좋아서, 어떻게든 출세하실 게 분명해요. 그분은 과학적인 발견에 매진하고 계세요.\"\n\n빈시 씨는 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"저는 제 유일한 행복의 전망을 포기할 수 없어요, 아빠. 리드게이트 씨는 신사예요. 저는 완벽한 신사가 아닌 사람은 결코 사랑할 수 없을 거예요. 제가 아라벨라 홀리처럼 폐병에 걸리는 걸 바라지는 않으시겠죠. 그리고 제가 한번 먹은 마음을 바꾸는 법이 없다는 걸 아시잖아요.\"\n\n다시 아빠는 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"약속해 주세요, 아빠, 우리가 바라는 대로 동의해 주시겠다고요. 우리는 결코 서로를 포기하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 아빠는 늘 긴 구애와 늦은 결혼에 반대하셨잖아요.\"\n\n이런 식의 채근이 조금 더 이어지다가, 빈시 씨가 \"그래, 그래, 얘야, 내가 답하기 전에 그 사람이 먼저 나한테 편지를 써야지\" 하고 말했고, 로저먼드는 자기가 뜻을 이루었음을 확신했다.\n\n빈시 씨의 답장은 주로 리드게이트가 생명보험에 가입해야 한다는 요구로 이루어졌고, 그 요구는 즉각 받아들여졌다. 이는 리드게이트가 죽는 경우를 상정한다면 더없이 안심되는 발상이었으나, 당장에는 스스로를 부양해 줄 발상은 아니었다. 그래도 그것은 로저먼드의 결혼에 관한 모든 일을 편안하게 만들어 주는 듯했고, 필요한 구매들은 한껏 활기차게 진행되었다. 그렇다고 신중한 고려가 없지는 않았다. 신부는 (준남작 댁을 방문하러 가는 신부는) 일류 손수건을 몇 장 갖추어야 한다. 하지만 반드시 필요한 여섯 장 외에는, 로저먼드도 최상급의 자수와 발랑시엔 레이스까지는 갖추지 않는 것으로 만족했다. 리드게이트 역시, 미들마치에 온 이후로 자신의 팔백 파운드가 상당히 줄어든 것을 알고는, 포크와 숟가락을 사러 브래싱의 키블 상점에 들어갔을 때 그에게 보여 준 고풍스러운 무늬의 은식기에 대한 욕심을 억눌렀다. 그는 자존심이 강해서, 빈시 씨가 가구를 마련하라고 돈을 대 주리라 미리 짐작하는 듯 행동할 수 없었다. 그리고 모든 것을 한꺼번에 치를 필요는 없을 것이므로 미결제로 남을 청구서도 좀 있겠지만, 그는 장인이 지불을 수월하게 해 주려고 지참금 형태로 얼마나 내놓을지 따위를 짐작하느라 시간을 허비하지 않았다. 그는 사치를 부릴 생각은 없었으나, 필요한 물건들은 사야 했고, 그것들을 질 낮은 것으로 사는 것은 잘못된 절약일 터였다. 이 모든 일은 곁가지에 불과했다. 리드게이트는 과학과 자신의 직업만이 자기가 열정적으로 추구해야 할 대상임을 내다보았다. 하지만 그는 렌치 같은 가정에서 그것들을 추구하는 자신을 도무지 상상할 수 없었으니, 그 집은 문이라는 문은 죄다 열려 있고, 방수포는 닳아 해졌으며, 아이들은 더러운 앞치마를 두르고, 점심은 뼈다귀며 검은 손잡이 칼이며 버드나무 무늬 그릇의 형태로 어수선하게 남아 있었다. 하지만 렌치에게는 큼지막한 숄을 두른 채 집 안에서 미라처럼 지내는 가련하고 림프질의 아내가 있었다. 그러니 그는 애초에 잘못 고른 살림 도구로 시작했음이 틀림없었다.\n\n그렇지만 로저먼드도 그녀대로 갖가지 추측에 골몰해 있었으니, 다만 흉내 내는 데 빠른 그녀의 감각은 그것들을 너무 노골적으로 드러내지 않도록 그녀에게 경계심을 일러 주었다.\n\n\"당신 가족을 알게 되면 정말 좋겠어요.\" 어느 날 신혼여행 이야기가 나왔을 때 그녀가 말했다. \"어쩌면 돌아오는 길에 그분들을 뵐 수 있는 쪽으로 방향을 잡을 수도 있겠죠. 백부님들 중에 누구를 가장 좋아하세요?\"\n\n\"아, 고드윈 백부님이지 싶소. 마음씨 좋은 노인이거든.\"\n\n\"어렸을 때 콸링엄의 그분 댁에 늘 가 계셨다면서요, 그렇죠? 그 옛 장소며 당신이 익숙하게 지내던 모든 걸 정말 보고 싶어요. 당신이 결혼한다는 걸 그분이 아시나요?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 리드게이트가 무심하게 말하며, 의자에서 몸을 돌려 머리를 위로 쓸어 넘겼다.\n\n\"꼭 알려 드리세요, 당신 이 못된 불효 조카님. 그분이 어쩌면 저를 콸링엄으로 데려오라고 하실지도 모르잖아요. 그러면 당신이 저에게 그 영지를 보여 주실 수 있고, 저는 거기 있던 어린 시절의 당신을 그려 볼 수 있겠죠. 기억하세요, 당신은 어릴 적부터 한결같은 모습 그대로의 제 집에서 저를 보고 있다는 걸요. 제가 당신의 집을 이토록 모르고 지내는 건 공평하지 않아요. 하지만 어쩌면 당신은 저를 좀 부끄러워하실지도 모르겠네요. 그걸 깜빡했어요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 그녀에게 다정하게 미소 지었고, 이토록 매혹적인 신부를 보여 주는 뿌듯한 즐거움이 약간의 수고를 들일 만한 가치가 있다는 그 제안을 정말로 받아들였다. 그리고 막상 그렇게 생각하고 보니, 그도 로저먼드와 함께 그 옛 장소들을 보고 싶었다.\n\n\"그럼 그분께 편지를 쓰겠소. 하지만 내 사촌들은 따분한 작자들이오.\"\n\n준남작의 가족을 그토록 깔보듯 말할 수 있다는 것이 로저먼드에게는 근사하게 여겨졌고, 자기 나름으로 그들을 경멸하듯 가늠할 수 있으리라는 전망에 그녀는 큰 만족을 느꼈다.\n\n하지만 하루 이틀 뒤에 엄마가 모든 것을 거의 망쳐 놓을 뻔했으니, 이렇게 말했던 것이다.\n\n\"백부님이신 고드윈 경께서 우리 로지를 깔보지 않으시기를 바라요, 리드게이트 씨. 그분이라면 뭔가 후하게 해 주실 것 같은데요. 천 파운드나 이천 파운드쯤이야 준남작에게는 아무것도 아닐 테니까요.\"\n\n\"엄마!\" 로저먼드가 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 말했다. 그리고 리드게이트는 그녀가 너무도 가여워서, 아무 말 없이 방 저쪽 끝으로 가서 마치 정신이 딴 데 팔린 양 판화 한 점을 신기한 듯 들여다보았다. 엄마는 그 후에 딸에게 약간의 잔소리를 들었고, 여느 때처럼 고분고분했다. 하지만 로저먼드는 따분한 작자들인 그 지체 높은 사촌들 가운데 누구라도 미들마치를 방문하게 된다면, 자기 집안에서 그들에게 충격을 줄 만한 것들을 많이 보게 되리라고 생각했다. 그러니 리드게이트가 머지않아 미들마치가 아닌 다른 곳에서 일류의 지위를 얻는 것이 바람직해 보였다. 그리고 작위를 가진 백부가 있고 발견을 해낼 수 있는 사람의 경우라면, 이는 그리 어려운 일이 아닐 터였다. 알다시피 리드게이트는 자기 삶의 가장 드높은 쓰임에 관한 희망을 로저먼드에게 열렬히 이야기했고, 흡족한 애정과 아름다움과 안식이라는 달콤한 진척을, 곧 여름 하늘과 꽃 두른 초원에서 우리의 생각이 얻는 그런 도움을 가져다줄 여인이 자기 말에 귀 기울여 주는 것을 즐거워했다.\n\n리드게이트는 변화를 위해 내가 암컷 거위와 수컷 거위라 부르겠다는 양자 사이의 심리적 차이에 크게 기대고 있었다. 특히 암컷 거위의 타고난 순종성이 수컷 거위의 힘에 더없이 아름답게 호응한다는 점에 말이다.\n\n# 제34장\n\n어떤 운명으로도 더 나쁜 데로 끌려갈까 두려워하지 않으며\n\n든든한 배처럼 굳세게 사나운 물결을\n가르고 제 항로를 곧게 지키는 그녀여.\n\n폭풍우에도 그 길에서 벗어나지 않고\n더 맑은 날씨의 거짓 기쁨에도 흔들리지 않으니.\n\n그런 자기 확신은 시샘하는 적들의 악의를\n\n두려워할 필요도, 벗들의 호의를 구할 필요도 없도다.\n오직 제 한결같은 힘에 의지하여\n\n누구에게도 자신을 굽히지 않으니.\n가장 든든히 쉬는 그녀가 가장 행복하나,\n\n그런 이를 가장 사랑하는 그가 가장 행복하리라.\n—스펜서.\n\n조지 4세가 죽고 의회가 해산되고 웰링턴과 필이 대체로 평가절하되고 새 왕이 사죄의 태도를 보이는 지금, 다가오는 것이 단지 총선거인지 아니면 세상의 종말인지 빈시 씨가 넌지시 던진 그 의문은 당시 지방 여론의 불확실성을 보여 주는 미약한 한 예에 지나지 않았다. 시골의 반딧불 같은 희미한 빛 속에서, 자유주의적 법안을 통과시키는 토리당 내각, 변절한 장관들의 친구보다 차라리 자유주의자를 의회에 보내고 싶어 안달하는 토리당 귀족과 유권자들, 그리고 사적인 이해와는 신비로울 만큼 동떨어진 듯하면서도 마뜩잖은 이웃이 옹호하는 까닭에 의심스러워진 개혁 요구의 외침이 뒤엉킨 혼란 속에서, 사람들이 어찌 어느 것이 자기 자신의 생각인지 분간할 수 있었겠는가? 미들마치의 신문을 사는 이들은 기묘한 처지에 놓여 있었다. 가톨릭 문제로 동요가 일던 동안 많은 이들이 「개척자」를 끊어 버렸는데—이 신문은 찰스 제임스 폭스의 경구를 표어로 내걸고 진보의 선봉에 서 있었으나—교황주의자들에 관해 필의 편을 들어 예수회주의와 바알에 대한 관용으로 자기 자유주의에 오점을 남겼기 때문이었다. 하지만 그들은 「나팔」에도 만족하지 못했으니, 로마를 향해 나팔을 불어 댄 이래로, 그리고 누가 누구를 지지할지 아무도 모르는 일반적인 여론의 무기력함 속에서 그 나팔 소리가 미약해져 버린 까닭이었다.\n\n「개척자」의 한 주목할 만한 기사에 따르면, 그것은 나라의 절박한 필요가, 오랜 경험을 통해 집중력뿐 아니라 폭넓음을, 관용뿐 아니라 판단의 결단력을, 정력뿐 아니라 냉정함을—사실상 인류의 우울한 경험 속에서 한 거처에 좀처럼 함께 머물려 하지 않았던 그 모든 자질을—갖춘 사람들이 공적 행동을 꺼리는 마음을 능히 상쇄할 법한 시기였다.\n당시 평소보다 더 널리 떠돌면서 그 최종 물길이 어디인지 매우 불확실하게 만들던 유창한 언변의 소유자 핵벗 씨는, 홀리 씨의 사무실에서 문제의 그 기사가 티프턴의 브룩에게서 \"흘러나온\" 것이며 브룩이 몇 달 전에 「개척자」를 은밀히 사들였다고 말하는 것이 들렸다.\n\n\"그건 말썽을 부리겠다는 거지, 응?\" 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"길 잃은 거북처럼 어슬렁거리더니 이제 인기 있는 사람이 되어 보겠다는 변덕이 든 거요. 그자한테는 그만큼 더 나쁜 일이지. 한동안 그자를 지켜봐 왔소. 톡톡히 망신을 당하게 될 거요. 그자는 지독하게 형편없는 지주거든. 오래된 시골 양반이 무슨 까닭으로 비천한 무리의 짙푸른 자유민들에게 알랑거리러 온단 말이오? 그 신문으로 말하자면, 그저 글을 직접 쓰기나 바랄 뿐이오. 그렇다면 우리가 돈을 낼 만한 가치가 있을 테니.\"\n\"듣자 하니 아주 영리한 젊은이를 편집자로 들였다는데, 런던 신문에 실린 어떤 것에도 뒤지지 않을 최고 수준의 사설을 쓸 수 있다는군요. 그리고 개혁에 관해 아주 높은 입장을 취할 작정이라고 하오.\"\n\n\"브룩더러 자기 소작료 장부나 개혁하라고 하시오. 그자는 빌어먹을 늙은 구두쇠고, 사유지 곳곳의 건물이 죄다 허물어져 가고 있소. 그 젊은이라는 작자도 런던에서 굴러온 어중이떠중이 한 명이겠지.\"\n\"이름이 래디슬로라고 합니다. 외국 혈통이라는 말이 있더군요.\"\n\n\"그런 부류는 내가 알지.\" 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"어떤 밀정 같은 자 말이오. 인권을 떠벌리며 시작했다가 끝에 가선 계집을 하나 죽이겠지. 그게 그 부류의 방식이오.\"\n\"폐단이 있다는 건 인정해야 하오, 홀리.\" 핵벗 씨가, 자기 집안의 변호사와 정치적 의견 충돌이 생기리라 예감하며 말했다. \"나 자신도 과격한 견해를 결코 옹호하지 않을 것이오—사실 나는 허스키슨과 입장을 같이하지만—큰 도시들이 대표되지 못한다는 점은 못 본 척할 수가 없단 말이오—\"\n\n\"큰 도시고 나발이고!\" 설명을 못 참는 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"나는 미들마치 선거에 대해 좀 지나치게 잘 알고 있소. 내일 당장 부패 선거구를 죄다 없애 버리고 왕국의 우후죽순 같은 신흥 도시를 죄다 끌어들여 보시오—의회에 들어가는 비용만 늘어날 뿐이오. 나는 사실에 근거해 말하는 거요.\"\n「개척자」가 밀정의 손에 편집된다는 생각과 브룩이 적극적으로 정치에 나선다는 생각에—마치 종잡을 수 없이 무얼 좇던 거북이 야심차게 작은 머리를 내밀고 날뛰기라도 하듯—홀리 씨가 느낀 혐오감은, 브룩 씨 자신의 집안 일부 구성원들이 느낀 짜증에는 거의 미치지 못했다. 그 결과는, 마치 이웃이 법적 구제 수단도 없이 영영 당신의 콧구멍 아래 자리할 불쾌한 종류의 공장을 차렸다는 사실을 알아차렸을 때처럼, 서서히 새어 나왔다. 「개척자」는 윌 래디슬로가 도착하기도 전에 이미 은밀히 사들여진 터였는데, 수익을 내지 못하는 값진 자산을 기꺼이 처분하려던 소유주의 의향 속에 기대하던 기회가 마련된 셈이었다. 그리고 브룩 씨가 초대 편지를 쓴 이후의 기간 동안, 젊은 시절부터 그의 안에 있었으나 지금껏 어떤 장애물에 가로막혀 있던, 세상 전반에 자기 정신을 떨치게 하려는 그 싹트는 생각들이 은밀히 움터 오르고 있었다.\n\n그 전개는, 그가 예상했던 것보다도 더 컸던 손님에 대한 기쁨으로 한껏 촉진되었다. 윌은 한때 브룩 씨가 파고들었던 그 모든 예술적·문학적 주제에 정통할 뿐 아니라, 정치적 정세의 요점을 놀랍도록 재빨리 포착하여, 충분한 기억력의 도움을 받아 인용에 능하고 다루는 솜씨가 전반적으로 효과적인, 그 폭넓은 정신으로 그것들을 다루는 듯했기 때문이다.\n\"내가 보기에 그는 일종의 셸리 같은 사람이오.\" 브룩 씨가 캐소본 씨를 흡족하게 해 주려고 기회를 잡아 말했다. \"못마땅한 점이 있다는 뜻은 아니오—방종이라든가 무신론이라든가 그런 것 말이오—래디슬로의 정서는 모든 면에서 분명 훌륭하오—실은 간밤에 우리가 함께 이야기를 많이 나눴소. 하지만 그는 자유, 해방, 속박에서 벗어남에 대해 같은 종류의 열정을 지녔는데—지도만 받는다면 훌륭한 것이지요—지도를 받는다면 말이오. 내가 그를 올바른 항로에 올려놓을 수 있을 것 같소. 게다가 그가 당신 친척이라 더욱 기쁘오, 캐소본.\"\n\n올바른 항로라는 것이 브룩 씨의 나머지 말보다 조금이라도 더 분명한 무언가를 뜻한다면, 캐소본 씨는 그것이 로윅에서 멀리 떨어진 어떤 일자리를 가리키기를 속으로 바랐다. 그는 윌을 도와주는 동안에도 그를 싫어했지만, 윌이 자신의 도움을 거절한 지금 그를 더욱 싫어하기 시작한 터였다. 우리 기질 속에 어떤 불안한 질투심이 있을 때 우리는 다 그렇게 되는 법이다. 우리의 재능이 주로 땅속을 파헤치는 종류라면, 우리의 꿀을 빨아 먹는 사촌(우리가 못마땅해할 중대한 이유를 지닌 그 사촌)은 우리를 남몰래 경멸하기 마련이고, 그를 흠모하는 자는 누구든 우리에게 비스듬한 비판을 던지는 셈이 된다. 우리 영혼에 올바름의 양심이 있는 까닭에, 우리는 그를 해치는 비열함보다는 높은 자리에 서서—오히려 우리에 대한 그의 모든 요구를 적극적인 혜택으로 갚는다. 그리고 그를 위해 수표를 끊어 주는 일은 그가 인정할 수밖에 없는 우월함인 만큼, 우리의 쓰라림에 한결 부드러운 약을 타 준다. 그런데 캐소본 씨는 그 우월함을(추억 이상의 무언가로서의 그것을) 별안간 변덕스러운 방식으로 빼앗긴 터였다. 윌에 대한 그의 반감은 겨울에 시든 남편의 흔한 질투에서 비롯된 것이 아니었다. 그것은 더 깊은 무엇으로, 평생에 걸친 그의 요구와 불만이 길러 낸 것이었다. 그러나 이제 그 자리에 있는 도러시아는—그녀 자신이 비판하는 능력을 보란 듯이 드러낸 적이 있는 젊은 아내로서—전에는 막연했던 그 불안에 필연적으로 초점을 맞춰 주었다.\n한편 윌 래디슬로는 자기 혐오가 감사의 마음을 희생시키며 무성해지고 있음을 느꼈고, 그 혐오를 정당화하느라 많은 내면의 담론에 빠져들었다. 캐소본은 자기를 미워한다—그는 그것을 아주 잘 알았다. 처음 들어섰을 때 그는 그 입가의 쓰라림과 눈빛 속의 독기를 알아차릴 수 있었는데, 그것은 과거의 은혜에도 불구하고 거의 전쟁 선포를 정당화할 만한 것이었다. 그는 과거에 캐소본에게 많은 신세를 졌지만, 사실 이 아내와 결혼한 행위는 그 빚에 대한 상쇄였다. 자기 자신을 위해 베풀어진 것에 대한 감사가, 다른 이에게 저질러진 것에 대한 분노에 마땅히 자리를 내주어야 하지 않을까 하는 것은 하나의 물음이었다. 그리고 캐소본은 도러시아와 결혼함으로써 그녀에게 잘못을 저질렀다. 사내라면 마땅히 자기 자신을 그보다는 더 잘 알아야 하는 법이고, 동굴 속에서 뼈다귀나 갉아 먹으며 백발이 되기를 택했다면, 한 처녀를 자기 동무로 꾀어들일 일은 아니었다. \"처녀를 제물로 바치는 가장 끔찍한 짓이야.\" 윌은 말했고, 마치 합창대의 통곡을 쓰기라도 하듯 도러시아의 내면의 슬픔이 어떠할지 스스로 그려 보았다. 하지만 그는 결코 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않으리라. 그녀를 지켜보리라—삶의 다른 모든 것을 포기한다 해도 그녀를 지켜보리라, 그리고 그녀는 이 세상에 자기를 위한 노예가 하나 있음을 알게 되리라. 윌은—토머스 브라운 경의 표현을 빌리자면—자기 자신과 남들 모두에게 진술의 \"열정적인 낭비벽\"을 지니고 있었다. 단순한 진실은, 그때 그를 그토록 강하게 끌어당기는 것은 도러시아의 존재만 한 게 없었다는 것이다.\n\n하지만 정식 초대 같은 것은 없었으니, 윌은 한 번도 로윅에 오라는 청을 받은 적이 없었기 때문이다. 사실 브룩 씨는, 가엾은 캐소본이 너무 몰두한 나머지 생각하지 못하는 유쾌한 일이라면 무엇이든 해내겠다는 자신감으로, 래디슬로를 여러 차례 로윅에 데려가기로 일을 꾸며 두었다(그러는 동안에도 기회 있을 때마다 그를 다른 곳에 \"캐소본의 젊은 친척\"이라고 소개하는 일을 게을리하지 않았다). 그리고 윌이 도러시아를 단둘이 만난 적은 없었지만, 그들의 만남은 자기보다 더 영리하면서도 기꺼이 자기에게 좌우되려는 듯 보이는 한 사람과 나누던 젊은 동무 의식을 그녀에게 되살려 주기에 충분했다. 가엾은 도러시아는 결혼 전에 자기가 가장 하고 싶었던 말을 받아 줄 자리를 다른 이들의 마음속에서 좀처럼 찾지 못했었다. 그리고 우리가 알다시피, 그녀는 남편의 우월한 가르침을 기대했던 만큼 누리지 못했다. 그녀가 조금이라도 예리한 관심을 담아 캐소본 씨에게 말하면, 그는 그녀가 자기에게는 어린 시절부터 익숙한 라틴어 독본에서 한 구절을 인용하기라도 한 듯 참는 기색으로 들었고, 때로는 이미 그런 부류는 충분히 비축되어 있다는 양 어떤 고대 종파나 인물이 비슷한 생각을 품었었는지 퉁명스레 언급했으며, 또 어떤 때는 그녀가 틀렸다고 알려 주고 그녀의 말이 의문시한 바를 다시 단언하곤 했다.\n\n하지만 윌 래디슬로는 언제나 그녀가 한 말 속에서 그녀 자신이 본 것보다 더 많은 것을 보는 듯했다. 도러시아에게는 허영심이 거의 없었지만, 다른 영혼의 기쁨을 만들어 냄으로써 자애롭게 다스리고자 하는 열렬한 여인의 욕구가 있었다. 그리하여 이따금 윌을 볼 수 있다는 단순한 기회만으로도 그것은 그녀의 감옥 벽에 뚫린 작은 채광창 같았으니, 햇살 가득한 공기를 잠깐 엿보게 해 주는 것이었다. 그리고 이 기쁨은 윌을 삼촌의 손님으로 소개하는 일에 대해 남편이 어떻게 생각할지를 두고 처음에 품었던 불안을 무효로 만들기 시작했다. 이 문제에 관해 캐소본 씨는 말이 없었다.\n하지만 윌은 도러시아와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶었고, 더디게 흐르는 정황을 참지 못했다. 단테와 베아트리체 사이의, 혹은 페트라르카와 라우라 사이의 현세적 교류가 아무리 미미했다 한들, 시간은 사물의 비율을 바꾸어 놓는 법이고, 훗날에는 소네트는 더 적고 대화는 더 많은 편이 나은 것이다. 필요가 책략을 변명해 주었지만, 책략은 도러시아의 기분을 상하게 할까 하는 두려움에 제한받았다. 마침내 그는 로윅에서 어떤 특정한 풍경을 스케치하고 싶다는 사실을 알아냈다. 그리고 어느 날 아침 브룩 씨가 군청 소재지로 가는 길에 로윅 가도를 따라 마차를 몰아야 했을 때, 윌은 스케치북과 접이의자를 들고 로윅에 내려 달라고 청했고, 저택에 자기 도착을 알리지도 않은 채, 도러시아가 산책하러 나온다면 반드시 그녀를 보게 될 자리를 잡고 스케치할 채비를 했다—그리고 그는 그녀가 보통 아침에 한 시간씩 산책한다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n하지만 그 책략은 날씨에 무산되었다. 구름이 의뭉스러울 만큼 빠르게 몰려들었고, 비가 내렸으며, 윌은 어쩔 수 없이 집 안으로 피신해야 했다. 그는 친척이라는 명분에 기대어, 응접실로 들어가 알리지도 않은 채 그곳에서 기다릴 작정이었다. 그리고 현관에서 오랜 지인인 집사를 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"내가 여기 있다는 말은 하지 마시오, 프랫. 점심때까지 기다릴 테니. 캐소본 씨가 서재에 계실 때는 방해받기 싫어하시는 걸 알고 있소.\"\n\n\"주인님은 출타 중이십니다, 나리. 서재에는 캐소본 부인만 계십니다. 나리께서 여기 계신다고 부인께 말씀드리는 편이 낫겠습니다, 나리.\" 프랫이 말했다. 그는 볼이 붉은 사내로, 탠트립과 활기차게 수다 떠는 것을 좋아했고, 마님이 따분하시겠다는 데 그녀와 자주 의견을 같이하곤 했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼 좋소. 이 망할 비 때문에 스케치를 못 하게 됐단 말이오.\" 윌이 말했는데, 어찌나 행복했던지 더없이 손쉽게 무심한 척했다.\n\n다음 순간 그는 서재에 있었고, 도러시아가 그 다정하고 거리낌 없는 미소로 그를 맞고 있었다.\n\n\"캐소본 씨는 부주교님 댁에 가셨어요.\" 그녀가 곧바로 말했다. \"저녁 식사 한참 전에 돌아오실지 어떨지 모르겠네요. 얼마나 걸릴지 분명치 않다고 하셨거든요. 그분께 무슨 특별히 하실 말씀이 있으셨나요?\"\n\n\"아니요. 스케치하러 왔다가 비 때문에 안으로 떠밀려 들어왔습니다. 아니었다면 아직 부인을 방해하지 않았을 겁니다. 캐소본 씨가 계실 줄 알았는데, 이 시간에 방해받는 걸 싫어하시는 걸 알거든요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 비에 신세를 졌네요. 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\" 도러시아는 학교로 면회 온 불행한 아이의 소박한 진심으로 이 평범한 말들을 했다.\n\n\"실은 부인을 단둘이 뵐 기회를 노리고 왔습니다.\" 윌이 말했는데, 알 수 없는 까닭에 그녀만큼이나 소박해질 수밖에 없었다. 그는 어째서 안 될 것이 있느냐고 자문할 겨를도 없었다. \"로마에서 그랬듯이 이런저런 것들에 관해 이야기하고 싶었어요. 다른 사람들이 있으면 늘 다르게 마련이니까요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 도러시아가 맑고 충만한 어조로 동의하며 말했다. \"앉으세요.\" 그녀는 갈색 책들을 등진 어두운 색 오토만에 앉았는데, 결혼반지 말고는 몸에 장신구 하나 없이 어떤 얇은 모직 흰색 천으로 지은 수수한 옷을 입은 그 모습은, 마치 다른 모든 여자와 달라야 한다는 서원이라도 한 사람 같았다. 윌은 두 야드쯤 떨어진 맞은편에 앉았고, 빛이 그의 빛나는 곱슬머리와, 입술과 턱의 도전적인 곡선이 어려 섬세하면서도 다소 토라진 듯한 옆얼굴에 떨어졌다. 두 사람은 마치 바로 그 자리에서 피어난 두 송이 꽃이라도 되는 양 서로를 바라보았다. 도러시아는 그 순간 윌에 대한 남편의 알 수 없는 노여움을 잊었다. 자기가 받아들여 준다고 여겼던 단 한 사람에게 두려움 없이 말할 수 있다는 것은 목마른 입술에 닿는 신선한 물 같았다. 슬픔을 통해 과거를 돌아보면서 그녀는 지난날의 위안을 과장했던 것이다.\n\n\"당신과 다시 이야기를 나눴으면 좋겠다고 자주 생각했어요.\" 그녀가 곧바로 말했다. \"제가 당신께 얼마나 많은 것들을 말했었는지 생각하면 이상해요.\"\n\n\"저는 그것들을 다 기억합니다.\" 윌이 말했는데, 완전히 사랑받을 만한 존재 앞에 있다는 느낌에 그의 영혼은 형언할 수 없는 만족으로 차 있었다. 내 생각에 그 순간 그 자신의 감정은 완전한 것이었으니, 우리 같은 한낱 인간에게도 사랑이 사랑하는 대상의 완전함 속에서 충족되는 신성한 순간이 있는 법이다.\n\n\"로마에 있던 이후로 저는 아주 많은 것을 배우려고 애써 왔어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"이젠 라틴어를 조금 읽을 수 있고, 그리스어도 아주 조금 이해하기 시작했어요. 이제 캐소본 씨를 더 잘 도와드릴 수 있어요. 그분을 위해 참고 문헌을 찾아드리고 여러 방법으로 눈을 아껴드릴 수 있거든요. 하지만 학식을 갖춘다는 건 무척 어려운 일이에요. 사람들이 위대한 사상에 이르는 길에서 지쳐 버려서, 너무 피곤한 나머지 결코 그것을 누리지 못하는 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"위대한 사상을 품을 능력이 있는 사람이라면, 노쇠해지기 전에 그것을 따라잡을 가능성이 높지요.\" 윌이 억누를 수 없는 민첩함으로 말했다. 하지만 어떤 감수성에서는 도러시아도 그만큼 민첩했기에, 그녀의 얼굴빛이 변하는 것을 보고 그는 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"하지만 가장 뛰어난 정신도 자기 생각을 풀어내느라 때로 과로했다는 건 분명한 사실입니다.\"\n\n\"제 말을 바로잡아 주시는군요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"제가 표현을 잘못했어요. 위대한 사상을 품은 이들이 그것을 풀어내느라 너무 지쳐 버린다고 말했어야 했어요. 저는 어린 소녀였을 때도 그것에 대해 늘 느끼곤 했어요. 그리고 제 삶을 쓸 수 있다면, 위대한 일을 하는 누군가를 도와 그의 짐을 좀 더 가볍게 해 주는 데 쓰고 싶다는 생각이 언제나 들었어요.\"\n\n도러시아는 무언가를 털어놓는다는 의식도 없이 이 짤막한 자기 고백으로 이끌려 갔다. 하지만 그녀가 윌에게 자기 결혼에 그토록 강한 빛을 던지는 말을 한 적은 전에 한 번도 없었다. 그는 어깨를 으쓱하지 않았다. 그리고 그 근육의 분출구가 없는 탓에, 아름다운 입술이 거룩한 해골이며 교회의 신전에 모셔진 다른 공허들에 입 맞추는 광경을 더욱 짜증스럽게 떠올렸다. 또한 그는 자기 말이 그 생각을 드러내지 않도록 조심해야 했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 도움을 너무 멀리까지 밀고 나가다가는 부인 자신이 과로하게 되기 쉽습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"너무 갇혀 지내시는 것 아닙니까? 벌써 안색이 더 창백해 보여요. 캐소본 씨께 비서를 두는 편이 나을 겁니다. 그분 일의 절반을 대신해 줄 사람을 쉽게 구하실 수 있을 거예요. 그게 그분을 더 효과적으로 아껴 드리는 길이고, 부인은 더 가벼운 일로만 도와드리면 될 테니까요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요?\" 도러시아가 진지하게 항의하는 어조로 말했다. \"그분의 일을 돕지 못한다면 제겐 아무 행복도 없을 거예요. 제가 무얼 할 수 있겠어요? 로윅에는 할 만한 좋은 일이 없는걸요. 제가 바라는 단 하나는 그분을 더 많이 돕는 거예요. 그리고 그분은 비서를 두는 걸 반대하세요. 부디 그 말씀은 다시 꺼내지 말아 주세요.\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 이제 부인의 마음을 알았으니까요. 하지만 브룩 씨와 제임스 체텀 경도 같은 바람을 말하는 것을 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"네.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"하지만 그분들은 이해하지 못하세요—제가 말을 많이 타기를, 정원을 고치고 새 온실을 짓기를, 그렇게 하루하루를 채우기를 바라시거든요. 저는 당신이라면 사람의 마음에 다른 욕구가 있다는 걸 이해하실 줄 알았어요.\" 그녀가 다소 안달하며 덧붙였다. \"게다가 캐소본 씨는 비서라는 말은 듣고도 못 견디세요.\"\n\n\"제 착오는 용서받을 만합니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"예전에는 캐소본 씨가 마치 비서를 두기를 고대하시는 듯 말씀하시는 걸 듣곤 했거든요. 사실 제게 그 자리를 맡을 가능성을 내비치셨고요. 하지만 알고 보니 저는—그 일에 적합할 만큼 훌륭하지 못했지요.\"\n\n도러시아는 이 말에서 남편의 명백한 거부감에 대한 변명을 끌어내려 애쓰며, 장난스러운 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"당신이 충분히 꾸준한 일꾼이 아니었던 거군요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 윌이 기운찬 말처럼 머리를 뒤로 흔들며 말했다. 그러고는 가엾은 캐소본 씨의 영광이라는 나방 날개를 한 번 더 톡 꼬집어 주라고 부추기는 그 옛 짜증의 악마에 이끌려 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그 후로 보니, 캐소본 씨는 누가 자기 일을 들여다보고 자기가 하는 일을 속속들이 아는 걸 좋아하지 않으시더군요. 그분은 너무 의심이 많아요—자기 자신을 너무 확신하지 못하세요. 제가 별 쓸모는 없을지 몰라도, 그분이 저를 싫어하는 건 제가 그분 의견에 동의하지 않기 때문이에요.\"\n\n윌에게 늘 너그럽고자 하는 의도가 없었던 건 아니지만, 우리의 혀란 보통 전반적인 의도가 작동되기도 전에 당겨지고 마는 작은 방아쇠인 것이다. 그리고 캐소본이 자기를 싫어하는 까닭이 도러시아에게 제대로 설명되지 않는다는 것은 너무 참을 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 말을 하고 나자 그는 그것이 그녀에게 미칠 영향에 대해 다소 불안해졌다.\n\n하지만 도러시아는 이상하리만치 조용했다—로마에서 비슷한 일이 있었을 때처럼 즉각 분개하지 않았다. 그리고 그 까닭은 깊은 데 있었다. 그녀는 이제 더 이상 사실에 대한 인식과 싸우지 않고, 그것을 더없이 또렷하게 인식하는 쪽으로 자신을 맞춰 가고 있었다. 그리하여 이제 남편의 실패를, 더욱이 그가 그 실패를 의식하고 있을지도 모른다는 사실을 똑바로 바라보았을 때, 그녀는 의무가 다정함이 되는 그 하나의 길을 따라가고 있는 듯했다. 윌의 신중하지 못함은 좀 더 엄하게 받아들여졌을지도 모른다, 만약 남편의 그 못마땅함이—그녀가 더 나은 이유를 알기 전까지는 가혹하게 느껴질 수밖에 없는 그 못마땅함이—이미 그를 그녀의 자비에 맡기지 않았더라면.\n\n그녀는 곧바로 대답하지 않고, 골똘히 생각하며 시선을 내리깐 뒤 다소 진지하게 말했다. \"캐소본 씨는 적어도 행동에 관한 한 당신에 대한 못마땅함을 극복하셨던 게 분명해요. 그리고 그건 훌륭한 일이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 그분은 집안일에서 정의감을 보여 주셨지요. 제 할머니가 이른바 신분이 안 맞는 결혼을 했다는 이유로 상속에서 제외되었다는 건 가증스러운 일이었어요. 할머니의 남편으로 말하자면, 빵을 벌기 위해 수업을 하던 폴란드 망명객이라는 점 말고는 흠잡을 데가 없었는데도 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그분에 대해 다 알았으면 좋겠어요!\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"그분이 부유함에서 가난으로의 변화를 어떻게 견디셨을지 궁금해요. 남편과 행복하셨을지 궁금하고요! 그분들에 대해 많이 아세요?\"\n\n\"아니요. 다만 제 할아버지가 애국자였다는 것—명석한 분이었고—여러 언어를 할 줄 알았고—음악에도 재능이 있었고—온갖 것을 가르쳐 빵을 벌었다는 것 정도예요. 두 분 다 비교적 일찍 돌아가셨어요. 그리고 저는 아버지에 대해서도 어머니가 들려주신 것 말고는 잘 몰라요. 다만 아버지는 그 음악적 재능을 물려받으셨지요. 저는 아버지의 느린 걸음걸이와 길고 가느다란 손을 기억해요. 그리고 아버지가 병석에 누워 계시고, 제가 무척 배가 고픈데도 빵 한 조각밖에 없던 어느 날이 제게 남아 있어요.\"\n\n\"아, 제 삶과는 어쩌면 그리 다른 삶일까요!\" 도러시아가 깊은 관심을 담아 무릎 위에 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"저는 늘 모든 게 너무 넘쳤어요. 하지만 어떻게 된 일인지 말해 주세요—그때는 캐소본 씨가 당신에 대해 아실 수 없었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 하지만 아버지가 캐소본 씨께 자기 존재를 알리셨고, 그날이 제가 굶주린 마지막 날이었어요. 아버지는 곧 돌아가셨고, 어머니와 저는 잘 보살핌을 받았지요. 캐소본 씨는 자기 어머니의 누이에게 가해진 모진 불의 때문에 우리를 보살피는 것이 자기 의무라고 늘 분명히 인정하셨어요. 하지만 지금 저는 부인께 새로울 것 없는 이야기를 하고 있군요.\"\n\n속마음으로 윌은 도러시아에게, 자기 자신의 해석에서조차 다소 새로운 무언가를 말하고 싶어 했다—다시 말해 캐소본 씨는 자기에게 빚을 갚은 것 이상의 일은 결코 한 적이 없다는 것을. 윌은 배은망덕하다는 느낌 아래 편할 수 있기에는 너무도 좋은 사내였다. 그리고 감사가 따지고 드는 문제가 되어 버리면, 그 속박에서 빠져나갈 길은 많은 법이다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 도러시아가 대답했다. \"캐소본 씨는 늘 자신의 명예로운 행동을 두고 길게 늘어놓는 걸 삼가셨어요.\" 그녀는 남편의 행실이 깎아내려졌다고 느끼지는 않았다. 하지만 윌 래디슬로와의 관계에서 정의가 무엇을 요구했었는가 하는 이 생각이 그녀의 마음을 강하게 사로잡았다. 잠시 멈춘 뒤 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그분이 당신 어머니를 부양하셨다는 건 제게 말씀하신 적이 없어요. 그분은 아직 살아 계신가요?\"\n\n\"아니요. 사 년 전에 사고로—넘어지셔서—돌아가셨어요. 묘한 건 제 어머니도 가족에게서 도망치셨다는 거예요. 다만 남편 때문은 아니었지요. 어머니는 가족에 대해 제게 아무 말도 하려 하지 않으셨어요. 다만 스스로 벌어먹기 위해 그들을 버렸다는 것—실은 무대에 서셨다는 것만 빼고요. 어머니는 검은 눈에 곱슬곱슬한 머리를 한 분이었고, 결코 늙어 가는 것 같지 않았어요. 보시다시피 저는 양쪽 다 반항적인 피를 타고났지요.\" 윌은 도러시아를 향해 환하게 미소 지으며 말을 맺었고, 그동안에도 그녀는 마치 처음으로 연극을 보는 아이처럼 진지한 골똘함으로 앞을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n하지만 그녀의 얼굴 역시 미소로 피어나며 말했다. \"그게 당신 자신이 다소 반항적이었던 것에 대한—제 말은, 캐소본 씨의 바람에 반항한 것에 대한 변명이로군요. 그분이 당신을 위해 가장 좋다고 여기신 일을 당신이 하지 않았다는 걸 잊으시면 안 돼요. 그리고 만약 그분이 당신을 싫어하신다면—조금 전에 당신이 싫어함을 말씀하셨지만—차라리 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠네요, 만약 그분이 당신에게 어떤 괴로운 감정을 내비치셨다면, 그분이 학문에 시달린 끝에 얼마나 예민해지셨는지를 헤아려 주셔야 해요. 어쩌면,\" 그녀가 호소하는 어조에 들어서며 말을 이었다. \"제 삼촌이 캐소본 씨의 병환이 얼마나 심각했는지 당신께 말씀하지 않으셨을지도 몰라요. 건강해서 여러 일을 견딜 수 있는 우리가, 시련의 무게를 짊어진 분들이 저지르는 사소한 잘못을 크게 여긴다면 무척 옹졸한 일일 거예요.\"\n\n\"부인 덕에 더 잘 배웁니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"다시는 그 일로 투덜대지 않겠습니다.\" 그의 어조에는 부드러움이 깃들어 있었는데, 그것은 도러시아 자신은 거의 의식하지 못하는 사실—그녀가 남편을 향한 순수한 연민과 충정의 아득한 곳으로 나아가고 있다는 사실—을 알아차린 데서 오는 말할 수 없는 만족에서 비롯된 것이었다. 윌은, 그녀가 그것을 드러내는 일에 자기를 함께해 준다면, 그녀의 연민과 충정을 흠모할 채비가 되어 있었다. \"저는 정말 가끔 비뚤어진 사람이었어요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 다시는, 제가 어쩔 수만 있다면, 부인이 못마땅해하실 일을 하거나 말하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"참 고마운 말씀이에요.\" 도러시아가 또 한 번 환한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그렇다면 제겐 작은 왕국이 하나 생기는 셈이네요. 거기서 제가 법을 내리겠지요. 하지만 당신은 곧 떠나, 제 통치 밖으로 나가시겠지요, 제 짐작에. 곧 그레인지에 머무는 게 싫증나실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 부인께 말씀드리고 싶었던 점이에요—부인을 단둘이 뵙고 싶었던 이유 중 하나지요. 브룩 씨가 제게 이 근방에 머물러 달라고 하셨어요. 미들마치 신문 중 하나를 사들이셨는데, 제가 그걸 운영하고 다른 일로도 도와주기를 바라세요.\"\n\n\"그건 당신에게 더 높은 전망을 희생하는 일이 되지 않을까요?\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면요. 하지만 저는 늘 전망만 생각하고 무엇 하나에도 정착하지 않는다고 비난받아 왔어요. 그런데 여기 제게 제안된 일이 있고요. 만약 부인이 제가 그 일을 받아들이는 걸 원치 않으신다면, 그만두겠습니다. 그게 아니라면 저는 떠나기보다는 이 고장에 머무는 편이 좋아요. 다른 어디에도 저는 누구의 사람도 아니거든요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신이 머무시기를 정말 바라요.\" 도러시아가, 로마에서 말했던 것처럼이나 소박하고 선뜻 곧바로 말했다. 그 순간 그녀의 마음속에는 그렇게 말해서는 안 될 이유가 그림자조차 없었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 머물겠습니다.\" 래디슬로가 머리를 뒤로 흔들며 말했고, 일어나 마치 비가 그쳤는지 보려는 듯 창가로 걸어갔다.\n\n하지만 다음 순간 도러시아는, 점점 더 강해지는 어떤 버릇에 따라, 남편이 자기와는 다르게 느낀다는 사실을 곱씹기 시작했고, 남편의 감정에 어긋날지도 모르는 것을 입 밖에 냈다는 것과 그 어긋남을 윌에게 넌지시 알려야 한다는 것, 그 이중의 당혹감에 얼굴이 깊이 붉어졌다. 그의 얼굴이 그녀 쪽을 향해 있지 않았고, 그래서 이렇게 말하기가 한결 쉬웠다—\n\n\"하지만 그런 문제에 제 의견은 별 중요하지 않아요. 당신은 캐소본 씨의 인도를 받으셔야 한다고 생각해요. 저는 제 자신의 감정 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않고 말했는데, 그건 정작 문제와는 아무 상관이 없어요. 하지만 이제 생각해 보니—어쩌면 캐소본 씨는 그 제안이 현명하지 못하다고 보실지도 몰라요. 지금은 기다렸다가 그분께 그 얘기를 꺼내면 안 될까요?\"\n\n\"오늘은 기다릴 수 없어요.\" 윌이, 캐소본 씨가 들어올지도 모른다는 가능성에 속으로 데인 채 말했다. \"비는 이제 완전히 그쳤어요. 브룩 씨께 저를 데리러 오시지 마시라고 일러두었거든요. 차라리 오 마일을 걷는 편이 좋아요. 핼셀 공유지를 가로질러 가면서 젖은 풀밭에 어른거리는 빛을 보겠습니다. 저는 그게 좋아요.\"\n\n그는 작별 악수를 하려고 꽤 서둘러 그녀에게 다가가면서, \"캐소본 씨께는 그 얘기를 꺼내지 마세요\"라고 말하고 싶었으나 감히 그러지 못했다. 아니, 그는 감히 그 말을 할 수 없었고, 할 수도 없었다. 그녀에게 덜 소박하고 덜 솔직해지라고 청하는 것은, 그 너머로 빛을 보고 싶은 수정에 입김을 부는 것과 같으리라. 그리고 거기엔 늘 또 다른 커다란 두려움이 있었으니—그녀의 눈에 비친 자기 자신이 흐려지고 영영 광채를 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움이었다.\n\n\"머무실 수 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\" 도러시아가 일어나 손을 내밀며 다소 애잔한 기색으로 말했다. 그녀에게도 입 밖에 내고 싶지 않은 생각이 있었으니—윌이 어김없이 지체 없이 캐소본 씨의 뜻을 여쭈어야 하지만, 그녀가 그것을 다그치는 것은 지나친 지시처럼 보일지도 모른다는 생각이었다.\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 그저 \"안녕히 가세요\"라고만 말했고, 윌은 집을 나서서 캐소본 씨의 마차와 마주칠 위험을 조금도 무릅쓰지 않으려고 들판을 가로질러 갔다. 그러나 그 마차는 네 시가 되어서야 대문에 나타났다. 그것은 귀가하기에 상서롭지 못한 시각이었다. 저녁 식사를 위해 몸단장을 함으로써 권태로부터 정신적 지지를 얻기에는 너무 일렀고, 하루의 하찮은 격식과 용무로부터 마음을 벗겨 내어 학문이라는 진지한 일에 제대로 뛰어들 채비를 하기에는 너무 늦은 때였다. 그런 때면 그는 보통 서재의 안락의자에 몸을 던지고, 그동안 눈을 감은 채 도러시아가 런던 신문을 읽어 주도록 하곤 했다. 그러나 오늘은 그 위안을 마다하며, 이미 공적인 자질구레한 이야기를 너무 많이 들었노라고 말했다. 하지만 도러시아가 피곤하냐고 묻자 평소보다 한결 명랑하게 말했고, 조끼와 크라바트를 벗고 말할 때조차 결코 그를 떠나지 않는 그 격식 차린 노력의 태도로 덧붙였다—\n\n\"오늘은 옛 지인인 스패닝 박사를 만나고, 그 자신이 칭찬받기에 합당한 사람에게서 칭찬을 받는 기쁨을 누렸소. 그가 이집트 신비 의식에 관한 내 근래의 소론을 무척 훌륭하게 평하더군—사실 내가 입에 담기에는 어울리지 않을 표현들을 써 가면서 말이오.\" 마지막 구절을 말하면서 캐소본 씨는 의자 팔걸이에 몸을 기대고 머리를 위아래로 끄덕였는데, 어울리지 않을 그 말의 되풀이 대신 분명 근육의 분출구를 삼는 듯했다.\n\n\"그런 기쁨을 누리셨다니 정말 기뻐요.\" 도러시아가, 이 시간에 남편이 평소보다 덜 지쳐 보이는 것을 보고 기뻐하며 말했다. \"당신이 오시기 전에 저는 마침 오늘 출타하셨던 게 아쉽다고 생각하고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"어째서요, 여보?\" 캐소본 씨가 다시 뒤로 몸을 젖히며 말했다.\n\n\"래디슬로 씨가 여기 다녀가셨거든요. 그분이 제 삼촌의 제안을 말씀하셨는데, 그것에 대한 당신 의견을 알고 싶어요.\" 그녀는 남편이 이 문제에 정말로 관계되어 있다고 느꼈다. 세상 물정에 어두운 그녀로서도, 윌에게 제안된 자리가 그의 집안 연줄과 어울리지 않는다는 막연한 인상은 있었고, 캐소본 씨에게는 분명 의견을 구할 만한 권리가 있었다. 그는 말하지 않고 그저 고개만 숙였다.\n\n\"아시다시피 삼촌은 여러 계획을 갖고 계세요. 보아하니 미들마치 신문 중 하나를 사들이셨는데, 래디슬로 씨에게 이 근방에 머무르며 그 신문을 운영해 주고, 그 밖의 다른 일로도 도와 달라고 청하셨대요.\"\n\n도러시아는 말하면서 남편을 바라보았으나, 그는 처음에 눈을 깜박이더니 마침내 마치 눈을 아끼려는 듯 감아 버렸다. 그러는 동안 입술은 더욱 굳어졌다. \"당신 의견은 어떠세요?\" 그녀가 잠시 멈춘 뒤 다소 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"래디슬로 씨가 일부러 내 의견을 물으러 왔소?\" 캐소본 씨가 도러시아를 칼날같이 날카로운 눈빛으로 보며 가늘게 눈을 뜨고 말했다. 그녀는 그가 물어본 그 점에 대해 정말로 마음이 불편했지만, 다만 조금 더 진지해졌을 뿐 눈길을 돌리지는 않았다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 그녀가 곧바로 대답했다. \"당신 의견을 물으러 왔다고는 하지 않으셨어요. 하지만 그 제안을 말씀하셨을 때, 당연히 제가 당신께 그것을 전하리라 기대하셨겠지요.\"\n\n캐소본 씨는 말이 없었다.\n\n\"당신이 어떤 반대를 느끼실까 봐 걱정스러웠어요. 하지만 그렇게 재능 많은 젊은이라면 분명 삼촌께 큰 도움이 될 수 있을 거예요—삼촌이 더 나은 방식으로 좋은 일을 하도록 도울 수 있을 테고요. 그리고 래디슬로 씨는 정해진 일자리를 갖고 싶어 해요. 그런 종류의 무언가를 찾지 않는다고 비난받아 왔다고 하더군요. 그리고 다른 곳에서는 아무도 자기를 마음에 두지 않으니 이 근방에 머물고 싶어 해요.\"\n\n도러시아는 이것이 남편의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 만한 고려 사항이라고 느꼈다. 하지만 그는 말이 없었고, 그녀는 이내 스패닝 박사와 부주교의 조찬 이야기로 돌아갔다. 그러나 이런 화제에는 더 이상 햇살이 비치지 않았다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 도러시아가 모르는 사이에 캐소본 씨는 다음과 같은 편지를 발송했는데, 첫머리를 \"친애하는 래디슬로 씨\"라고 시작했다(그는 전에는 늘 그를 \"윌\"이라고 불렀다)—\n\n\"캐소본 부인에게 들으니, 자네에게 어떤 제안이 들어왔고, (결코 무리하지 않은 추론에 따르건대) 자네 쪽에서도 어느 정도 그것을 마음에 두고 있는 듯하네. 그것은 자네가 이 근방에 거주하는 일을 수반하는데, 그 처지는 내 자신의 입장을 건드리는 것이라 내가 단언해도 무방한바, 그 영향을 정당한 감정의 견지에서 본다면 이렇게 말하는 것이 나로서 자연스럽고 정당할 뿐 아니라, 같은 영향을 내 책임의 견지에서 헤아린다면 즉시 다음을 밝히는 것이 내 의무이기도 하네. 즉, 위에서 언급한 제안을 자네가 수락한다면 내게 심히 불쾌할 것이라는 점일세. 이 일에 내가 거부권을 행사할 만한 약간의 권리가 있다는 점은, 우리 사이의 관계를 아는 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구도 부정하지 않으리라 믿네. 그 관계는 비록 자네의 최근 처신으로 과거의 일이 되었다 해도, 그렇다고 해서 결정적 선행 사실로서의 그 성격이 무효화되는 것은 아닐세. 나는 여기서 어떤 사람의 판단에 대해서도 논평하지 않겠네. 다만, 어떤 사회적 적절함과 예법이 있어서, 나의 다소 가까운 친척이 이 인근에서 나 자신의 지위보다 훨씬 낮을 뿐 아니라, 기껏해야 문필적이거나 정치적인 모험가들의 어설픈 지식과 결부된 신분으로 어떤 식으로든 두드러지는 것을 마땅히 막아야 한다는 점을 자네에게 지적하는 것으로 충분하네. 어쨌든 그와 반대되는 결말은 자네가 앞으로 내 집에서 환대받는 것을 배제할 수밖에 없을 것일세.\n\n이만 줄이네,\n에드워드 캐소본.\"\n\n한편 도러시아의 마음은 천진하게도 남편을 한층 더 쓰라리게 만드는 쪽으로 움직이고 있었다. 윌이 자기 부모와 조부모에 대해 들려준 이야기를 동요에 이를 만큼 깊어진 공감으로 곱씹고 있었던 것이다. 하루 중 사적인 시간이면 그녀는 보통 청록색 규방에서 보냈는데, 그 창백한 고풍스러움을 무척 좋아하게 된 터였다. 그곳에서 겉으로 달라진 것은 아무것도 없었다. 하지만 여름이 느릅나무 가로수 길 너머 서쪽 들판으로 서서히 번져 가는 동안, 그 휑한 방은 마치 선하거나 악한 천사들의 구름처럼 공기를 채우는 그 내면의 삶의 기억들, 우리 영혼의 승리나 영혼의 추락의 보이지 않으나 살아 움직이는 형상들을 그 안에 모아 들였다. 그녀는 가로수 길을 따라 서쪽 빛의 아치를 바라보며 결심을 짜내고 또 찾는 데 너무도 익숙해진 나머지, 그 광경 자체가 무언가를 전해 주는 힘을 얻게 되었던 것이다. 창백한 수사슴조차 일깨우는 듯한 눈길을 보내며 말없이 \"그래, 우리도 알아\"라고 말하는 것 같았다. 그리고 섬세하게 그려진 세밀화들의 무리는, 이제 더 이상 자기 자신의 세속적 운명에 대해 동요하지 않으면서도 여전히 인간적인 관심을 지닌 존재들의 청중 같은 것을 이루었다. 특히 도러시아가 남편에게 좀처럼 캐묻기 어려웠던 그 신비로운 \"줄리아 고모\"가 그러했다.\n\n그리고 이제, 윌과 대화를 나눈 이래로, 윌의 할머니인 그 줄리아 고모를 둘러싸고 많은 새로운 이미지가 모여들었다. 그녀가 아는 어느 살아 있는 얼굴과 그토록 닮은 그 섬세한 세밀화의 존재가, 그녀의 감정을 한데 모으는 데 도움을 주었다. 가난한 남자를 택했다는 이유만으로 그 처녀를 가족의 보호와 상속에서 끊어 낸다는 것은 얼마나 큰 잘못인가! 어린 시절부터 자기 주변의 사실들에 대해 질문을 던져 어른들을 곤란하게 했던 도러시아는, 어째서 장남이 우월한 권리를 갖는지, 어째서 토지가 한정 상속되어야 하는지에 대한 역사적·정치적 이유들을 어느 정도 독자적으로 명료하게 정리해 두고 있었다. 어떤 경외감으로 그녀에게 새겨진 그 이유들은 어쩌면 그녀가 아는 것보다 더 무게가 있을지도 몰랐다. 하지만 여기 있는 것은 그 이유들을 침해하지 않은 채로 남겨 두는 인연의 문제였다. 여기 있는 것은, 그 딸의 자식이—은퇴한 식료품상보다 더 귀족적일 것도 없고 잔디밭과 작은 목초지 말고는 \"한데 지킬\" 땅이라곤 없는 사람들이 귀족 제도를 천박하게 흉내 내는 통상적 관행에 따르더라도—우선권을 가질, 그런 딸의 경우였다. 상속이란 호불호의 문제인가, 아니면 책임의 문제인가? 도러시아의 천성이 지닌 온 힘은 책임의 편에 섰다—결혼이나 부모 됨처럼, 우리 자신의 행위에 근거한 요구들을 이행하는 편에.\n\n캐소본 씨가 래디슬로 가문에 빚을 지고 있다는 것, 래디슬로 가문이 부당하게 빼앗긴 것을 그가 갚아야 한다는 것은 사실이라고 그녀는 스스로 되뇌었다. 그리고 이제 그녀는, 결혼할 무렵에 작성되어 자기 재산의 대부분을 자녀가 생길 경우를 단서로 도러시아에게 남기도록 한 남편의 유언을 떠올리기 시작했다. 그것은 고쳐져야 마땅했다. 그리고 한시도 지체해서는 안 되었다. 방금 윌 래디슬로의 일자리를 두고 생긴 바로 이 문제야말로, 모든 것을 새롭고 올바른 토대 위에 놓을 계기였다. 남편은, 지금까지의 모든 처신으로 미루어 보건대, 그녀가 제안하기만 한다면 분명 정당한 견해를 기꺼이 받아들일 것이라고 그녀는 확신했다—재산의 부당한 집중이 바로 그녀의 이익을 위해 추진되었던 만큼. 그의 올바름에 대한 감각은 반감이라 부를 만한 어떤 것도 이겨 냈고 앞으로도 계속 이겨 낼 터였다. 그녀는 삼촌의 계획을 캐소본 씨가 못마땅해한다고 짐작했고, 그래서 새로운 양해가 시작되는 것이 더욱 시의적절해 보였다. 그리하여 윌이 무일푼으로 출발해 처음 제안된 일자리를 받아들이는 대신, 남편이 살아 있는 동안 지급하고 유언을 즉시 고쳐 그가 죽은 뒤에도 보장될, 정당한 수입을 손에 넣게 되도록 말이다. 마땅히 해야 할 일로서 이 모든 것을 떠올리는 것은 도러시아에게 마치 별안간 햇빛이 들이치는 것 같았고, 남편이 다른 이들과 맺은 관계에 대한 이전의 어리석음과, 호기심 없이 자기에게만 몰입했던 무지에서 그녀를 깨워 주었다. 윌 래디슬로는 이제 그녀에게는 더 이상 옳아 보이지 않는 근거에서 캐소본 씨의 장래의 도움을 거절했었다. 그리고 캐소본 씨 자신은 자기에게 지워진 그 요구가 무엇인지 결코 온전히 보지 못했었다. \"하지만 그분은 보실 거야!\" 도러시아는 말했다. \"그분 성품의 큰 힘이 바로 거기 있어. 그런데 우리는 우리 돈으로 무얼 하고 있지? 우리 수입의 절반도 쓰지 않잖아. 내 돈은 내게 불편한 양심 말고는 아무것도 사 주지 않아.\"\n\n도러시아에게는 자기 몫으로 정해진, 그리고 늘 지나치다고 여겨 왔던 이 재산 분배에 묘한 매혹이 있었다. 보다시피 그녀는 남들에게는 빤한 많은 것들에 눈멀어 있었다—실리아가 경고했듯이 엉뚱한 곳에 발을 디디기 십상이었다. 하지만 자기 자신의 순수한 목적 안에 있지 않은 것에 대한 그 눈멂이, 시야가 있었다면 두려움으로 위태로웠을 벼랑 가를 그녀가 무사히 지나가게 해 주었다.\n\n규방의 고독 속에서 생생함을 더해 갔던 그 생각들은, 캐소본 씨가 윌에게 편지를 보낸 그날 내내 그녀를 끊임없이 사로잡았다. 남편에게 마음을 열 기회를 찾기 전까지는 모든 것이 그녀에게 방해물처럼 여겨졌다. 그의 골몰한 마음에는 어떤 화제든 부드럽게 다가가야 했고, 그녀는 그의 병환 이래로 그를 동요시킬까 하는 두려움을 의식에서 한순간도 떨치지 못했다. 하지만 젊은 열정이 신속한 행위의 구상에 골몰하게 되면, 그 행위 자체가 독자적인 생명을 띠고 솟아나 관념적인 장애물들을 제압하는 듯하다. 그날은 드물지 않은 음울한 방식으로 지나갔는데, 캐소본 씨가 어쩌면 평소보다 더 말이 없긴 했다. 하지만 밤 시간에는 대화의 기회로 기대할 만한 때가 있었다. 도러시아는 남편이 잠 못 드는 것을 알아차렸을 때, 일어나 촛불을 켜고 그가 다시 잠들 때까지 책을 읽어 주는 버릇을 들여 두었기 때문이다. 그리고 이날 밤 그녀는 결심에 들떠 처음부터 잠을 이루지 못했다. 그는 평소처럼 몇 시간 잠들었지만, 그녀는 가만히 일어나 거의 한 시간 가까이 어둠 속에 앉아 있었고, 그제야 그가 입을 열었다—\n\n\"도러시아, 일어나 있으니, 촛불을 켜 주겠소?\"\n\n\"어디 편찮으세요, 여보?\" 그녀는 그의 말에 따르며 첫 물음을 던졌다.\n\n\"아니, 전혀 아니오. 다만 당신이 일어나 있으니, 라우스의 글을 몇 쪽 읽어 준다면 고맙겠소.\"\n\n\"대신 잠깐 이야기를 나눠도 될까요?\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이오.\"\n\n\"저는 하루 종일 돈에 대해 생각하고 있었어요—제가 늘 너무 많이 가졌다는 것, 특히 앞으로 너무 많이 갖게 되리라는 전망에 대해서요.\"\n\n\"여보 도러시아, 그런 것들은 신의 섭리에 따른 배치라오.\"\n\n\"하지만 다른 이들이 부당한 일을 당한 탓에 누군가 너무 많이 갖게 된 거라면, 그 잘못을 바로잡으라고 우리에게 이르는 신의 음성에 따라야 한다고 저는 생각해요.\"\n\n\"여보, 그 말의 요지가 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"당신이 저를 위해—제 말은, 재산에 관해서요—너무 후하게 마련해 두셨다는 거예요. 그게 저를 불행하게 만들어요.\"\n\n\"어째서요? 내게는 비교적 먼 친척 말고는 없는데.\"\n\n\"저는 당신의 줄리아 고모님에 대해, 그리고 그분이 가난한 남자와 결혼했다는 이유만으로 가난 속에 버려졌다는 것에 대해 생각하게 됐어요. 그 남편이 부족한 사람이 아니었으니, 그건 수치스러운 행위가 아니었지요. 제가 알기로 바로 그 때문에 당신이 래디슬로 씨를 교육시키고 그의 어머니를 부양하셨던 거고요.\"\n\n도러시아는 자기를 앞으로 이끌어 줄 어떤 대답을 잠시 기다렸다. 아무 대답도 없었고, 어두운 침묵 위로 또렷이 떨어지는 그녀의 다음 말은 그녀 자신에게 한층 더 힘 있게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"하지만 분명 우리는 그분의 권리를, 당신이 제게 정해 두셨다고 제가 알고 있는 그 재산의 절반에 대해서도, 훨씬 더 큰 것으로 여겨야 해요. 그리고 그런 양해 아래 그분에게 즉시 마련해 드려야 한다고 생각해요. 우리는 부유한데 그분이 가난에 매여 의존하며 사는 건 옳지 않아요. 그리고 그분이 말씀하신 그 제안에 무슨 반대가 있으시다면, 그분에게 정당한 자리와 정당한 몫을 드리는 것이 그분이 그 제안을 받아들일 어떤 동기도 없애 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"래디슬로 씨가 이 문제에 대해 당신에게 말한 모양이군?\" 캐소본 씨가, 그답지 않게 어떤 물어뜯는 듯한 신속함으로 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 아니에요!\" 도러시아가 진지하게 말했다. \"그분이 그토록 최근에 당신에게서 모든 걸 거절했는데 어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요? 저는 당신이 그분을 너무 모질게 여기시는 게 아닐까 걱정스러워요, 여보. 그분은 그저 자기 부모와 조부모에 대해 조금 들려주셨을 뿐이고, 그것도 거의 다 제 물음에 답하느라 그러신 거예요. 당신은 그토록 선하고, 그토록 정의로우셔서—옳다고 여기신 일은 다 하셨어요. 하지만 제겐 그 이상의 것이 옳다는 게 분명해 보여요. 그리고 저는 그것에 대해 말씀드려야 해요, 그 '이상의 것'이 행해지지 않음으로써 이른바 덕을 보게 될 사람이 바로 저니까요.\"\n\n캐소본 씨가 대답하기까지 눈에 띄는 침묵이 있었는데, 이번에는 앞서처럼 신속하지 않고, 한층 더 물어뜯는 듯한 강조를 담고 있었다.\n\n\"도러시아, 여보, 당신이 당신 능력 밖의 문제에 대해 판단을 내린 것이 이번이 처음은 아니지만, 이번이 마지막이기를 바라는 바이오. 처신이, 특히 혼인 관계의 문제에서, 어느 정도까지 가문에 대한 권리의 박탈을 구성하는가 하는 물음으로 나는 지금 들어가지 않겠소. 다만 당신이 그것을 분별할 자격이 여기서 없다는 것으로 충분하오. 지금 내가 당신에게 깨닫게 하고 싶은 것은, 내가 분명하고도 정당하게 내 것이라고 숙고해 온 그 영역의 일에 대해서는 어떤 수정도, 더더욱 지시 따위는 받아들이지 않겠다는 것이오. 나와 래디슬로 씨 사이에 끼어드는 것은 당신이 할 일이 아니며, 더욱이 내 처신에 대한 비판을 이루는 그의 전언이 당신에게 오도록 부추기는 것은 더더욱 할 일이 아니오.\"\n\n가엾은 도러시아는 어둠에 싸인 채 상충하는 감정들로 들끓고 있었다. 남편이 그토록 강하게 드러낸 분노가 그 자신에게 미칠 영향에 대한 불안은, 설령 그녀가 그의 마지막 빈정거림에 어떤 정당함이 있을지도 모른다는 의식 아래 의심과 가책에서 완전히 자유로웠다 해도, 자기 자신의 분개를 입 밖에 내는 것을 억눌렀을 것이다. 그가 말을 마친 뒤 가쁘게 숨 쉬는 소리를 들으며, 그녀는 겁에 질려 비참한 심정으로 앉아 귀를 기울였다—온갖 기운이 두려움에 가로막히는 이 악몽 같은 삶을 견디게 해 달라는, 도움을 구하는 말 없는 내면의 외침과 함께. 하지만 두 사람이 다시는 말을 나누지 않은 채 오랫동안 잠들지 못한 채로 있었다는 것 말고는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다.\n\n이튿날 캐소본 씨는 윌 래디슬로에게서 다음과 같은 답장을 받았다—\n\n\"친애하는 캐소본 씨, 어제 보내 주신 편지를 충분히 숙고했습니다만, 저는 우리 상호 간의 입장에 대해 선생님의 견해를 그대로 받아들일 수가 없습니다. 과거에 제게 베풀어 주신 너그러운 처신을 더없이 충분히 인정하면서도, 저는 여전히, 이런 종류의 의무가 선생님께서 기대하시는 듯한 방식으로 저를 부당하게 옭아맬 수는 없다고 주장해야겠습니다. 은인의 바람이 하나의 권리를 구성할 수 있다 치더라도, 그 바람의 성질에 관해서는 언제나 유보가 있어야 합니다. 그것은 더 절박한 고려 사항들과 충돌할 수도 있습니다. 혹은 은인의 거부권이 한 사람의 삶에 그러한 부정을 가하여, 그로 인한 공백이 그 은혜가 너그러웠던 것보다 더 잔인할 수도 있습니다. 저는 단지 강한 예시를 들고 있을 뿐입니다. 현재의 경우, 제가 어떤 일자리를—분명 부를 가져다주지는 않으나 불명예스럽지도 않은 일자리를—받아들이는 것이 선생님 자신의 입장에 미칠 영향에 대해, 저는 선생님의 견해를 받아들일 수가 없습니다. 그 입장은 제게는 너무도 견고해 보여서 그런 그림자 같은 방식으로 영향받을 것 같지 않기 때문입니다. 그리고 우리 관계에 과거가 제게 지운 의무를 무효화할 어떤 변화가 일어나리라고는 믿지 않습니다만(분명 아직 아무 변화도 일어나지 않았고요), 그 의무가 제가 원하는 곳에서 살고 제가 택할 수 있는 어떤 합법적인 일로 저 자신을 부양하는 통상적인 자유를 누리는 것을 막아야 한다고는 저로서는 보이지 않으니, 부디 용서해 주십시오. 혜택을 베푸는 일이 전적으로 선생님 쪽에만 있었던 그런 관계에 관해 우리 사이에 이런 견해차가 존재하게 된 것을 유감으로 여기며—\n\n변함없는 의무를 지닌 채 이만 줄입니다,\n윌 래디슬로.\"\n\n가엾은 캐소본 씨는 자기보다 더 정당한 혐오와 의심의 근거를 가진 사람은 없다고 느꼈다(공정하다면 우리도 그와 더불어 조금은 그렇게 느껴야 하지 않을까?). 그는 젊은 래디슬로가 자기에게 맞서고 성가시게 굴 작정이며, 도러시아의 신뢰를 얻어 그녀의 마음에 남편에 대한 무례함을, 어쩌면 혐오까지 뿌리려 한다고 확신했다. 윌이 캐소본 씨의 도움을 거부하고 여행을 그만둠으로써 별안간 진로를 바꾼 것을 설명하려면 표면 아래의 어떤 동기가 필요했다. 그리고 자신의 이전 선택과 그토록 어긋나는 브룩 씨의 미들마치 계획 같은 것을 떠맡으면서까지 이 근방에 자리 잡으려는 이 도전적인 결의는, 그 밝히지 않은 동기가 도러시아와 관련되어 있음을 충분히 분명하게 드러냈다. 캐소본 씨는 한순간도 도러시아를 어떤 이중성으로 의심하지 않았다. 그녀에 대한 의심은 없었으나, 그에게는 (그에 못지않게 불편한) 확실한 앎이 있었으니, 남편의 처신에 대해 의견을 형성하는 그녀의 성향이 윌 래디슬로를 호의적으로 여기고 그가 하는 말에 영향받는 기질을 동반하고 있다는 것이었다. 자존심에서 비롯된 그의 과묵함은, 애초에 도러시아가 삼촌에게 윌을 집에 초대해 달라고 청했다는 추측에서 그가 결코 벗어나지 못하게 만들었다.\n\n그리고 이제 윌의 편지를 받고서, 캐소본 씨는 자신의 의무를 고려해야 했다. 그는 자기 행동을 의무 말고 다른 무엇으로 부르는 것을 결코 마음 편히 여기지 못할 사람이었다. 하지만 이 경우에는 상충하는 동기들이 그를 부정의 상태로 밀어 넣었다.\n\n브룩 씨에게 직접 청하여, 그 성가신 신사에게 제안을 철회하라고 요구해야 할까? 아니면 제임스 체텀 경에게 상의하여, 온 집안과 관련된 그런 조치에 반대하는 항의에 동조하도록 해야 할까? 어느 경우든 캐소본 씨는 실패가 성공만큼이나 있을 법하다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그가 이 문제에서 도러시아의 이름을 입에 올리는 것은 불가능했고, 무언가 위급함을 알리지 않는 한 브룩 씨는 모든 진술에 겉으로는 동의하다가도, 마지막에 가서 \"걱정 마시오, 캐소본! 내 장담하건대 젊은 래디슬로는 당신 체면을 세워 줄 거요. 내 장담하건대 나는 딱 맞는 일을 짚어 냈소\"라고 말할 공산이 적지 않았다. 그리고 캐소본 씨는 제임스 체텀 경과 이 문제로 의논하는 것을 신경질적으로 꺼렸으니, 둘 사이에는 한 번도 살가움이 없었던 데다, 그는 도러시아의 이름을 입에 올리지 않아도 즉각 도러시아를 떠올릴 사람이었기 때문이다.\n\n가엾은 캐소본 씨는 자기를 향한 모든 이의 감정을, 특히 남편으로서의 자기를 향한 감정을 미덥지 않게 여겼다. 자기가 질투한다고 누군가 짐작하게 두는 것은, 자기 약점에 대한 그들의 (의심되는) 견해를 인정하는 셈이었다. 자기가 결혼을 그리 더없이 행복하다고 여기지 않는다는 것을 그들이 알게 두는 것은, 자기가 그들의 (아마도) 이전의 반대로 돌아섰음을 함축하는 셈이었다. 그것은 자기 「모든 신화의 열쇠」를 짜내는 일에서 자기가 얼마나 뒤처져 있는지를 카프와 브레이즈노스 사람들 전반에게 알게 두는 것만큼이나 나쁜 일이었다. 평생토록 캐소본 씨는 자기 의심과 질투라는 내면의 상처를 자기 자신에게조차 인정하지 않으려 애써 왔다. 그리고 모든 개인적 문제 가운데 가장 미묘한 이 문제에서, 자존심 강하고 의심 많은 과묵함의 습관은 이중으로 작용했다.\n\n이리하여 캐소본 씨는 자존심에 차서, 쓰라리게 침묵을 지켰다. 하지만 그는 윌이 로윅 저택에 오는 것을 금했고, 마음속으로는 다른 좌절의 방책들을 마련하고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXXVIII.\n\n“인간의 행위에 대한 사람들의 판단은 대단한 것이니, 늦든 빠르든 그것은 힘을 발휘하게 된다.” —기조\n\n* 기조: 프랑수아 기조(1787~1874), 프랑스의 역사가이자 정치가.\n제임스 체텀 경은 브룩 씨의 새로운 행보를 아무리 보아도 흡족할 수 없었다. 그러나 반대하기는 쉬워도 막기는 어려운 일이었다. 제임스 경은 어느 날 혼자서 캐드월러더 부부와 점심을 함께하러 온 까닭을 이렇게 설명했다.\n\n“실리아 앞에서는 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 수가 없습니다. 그 애가 상처받을지도 모르니까요. 사실, 그건 옳지 않은 일이지요.”\n\n“무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요. 그레인지의 그 『파이어니어』 말이지요!” 캐드월러더 부인이 친구의 입에서 마지막 단어가 떨어지기도 전에 끼어들었다. “정말 끔찍한 일이에요. 호루라기를 사들여서 아무나 다 듣게 불어대다니 말이에요. 가엾은 플레시 경처럼 온종일 침대에 누워 도미노 놀이나 하는 편이 그래도 더 은밀하고 견딜 만하겠어요.”\n\n“『트럼펫』에서 우리 친구 브룩을 공격하기 시작한 것 같던데요.” 목사가 의자에 느긋하게 몸을 기대며, 자신이 공격받기라도 한 듯 편안한 미소를 지으며 말했다. “미들마치에서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사는 어떤 지주를 두고 굉장한 풍자를 퍼붓고 있어요. 자기 지대는 꼬박꼬박 받으면서 환원은 전혀 하지 않는 지주라고 말이지요.”\n\n“브룩이 정말 그 짓을 그만두면 좋겠습니다.” 제임스 경이 짜증스러운 듯 살짝 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n“그런데 그 사람, 정말로 후보로 지명될 모양인가요?” 캐드월러더 씨가 물었다. “어제 페어브러더를 만났는데—그 사람도 휘그파라서 브로엄이며 ‘유익한 지식’ 따위를 떠받들지요. 내가 그에게서 알아낸 최악의 점이 그거예요—그가 말하길 브룩이 제법 강한 세력을 모으고 있다더군요. 은행가 벌스트로드가 그의 최측근이라고요. 하지만 그는 브룩이 지명에서 형편없이 될 거라고 보더군요.”\n\n“바로 그렇습니다.” 제임스 경이 진지하게 말했다. “나도 그 일을 알아보고 있었습니다. 전에는 미들마치 정치에 대해 아무것도 몰랐거든요—주(州) 일이 내 관할이니까요. 브룩이 믿고 있는 건, 올리버가 필파라서 사람들이 그를 끌어내리려 한다는 점입니다. 하지만 홀리가 내게 말하길, 만약 그들이 휘그 후보를 내세운다면 틀림없이 배그스터일 거라더군요. 어디서 굴러왔는지 알 수 없는 후보 중 하나지만, 내각에 결사반대하는 노련한 의회 인사라고요. 홀리는 좀 거친 사람이라, 자기가 누구에게 말하고 있는지 잊더군요. 브룩이 매질을 당하고 싶다면, 선거 연단에 나갈 것 없이 더 싸게 당할 수 있다고 하더이다.”\n\n“내가 그 모든 걸 다 경고했잖아요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 두 손을 바깥으로 내저으며 말했다. “오래전에 험프리에게 말했어요. 브룩 씨가 진창에서 첨벙대는 꼴을 보일 거라고요. 그리고 지금 정말 그렇게 됐잖아요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 차라리 결혼이나 할 생각을 했더라면 좋았을 텐데요.” 목사가 말했다. “그게 정치를 가지고 잠깐 추파를 던지는 것보다는 더 심각한 난장판이었겠지만요.”\n\n“그건 나중에 할 수도 있겠죠.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “학질에 걸린 채 진창 반대편으로 빠져나온 다음에 말이에요.”\n\n“내가 무엇보다 신경 쓰이는 건 그 사람 자신의 품위입니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “물론 가족 때문에 더 마음이 쓰이는 거지요. 하지만 그분은 이제 나이도 들어가는데, 스스로를 웃음거리로 만드는 걸 생각하면 마음이 좋지 않습니다. 사람들이 그분에 대한 온갖 일을 들춰낼 테니까요.”\n\n“설득을 해봐야 소용없겠지요.” 목사가 말했다. “브룩에게는 고집과 변덕이 어찌나 묘하게 뒤섞여 있는지요. 그 문제로 그분께 말씀해보셨나요?”\n\n“글쎄요, 아니요.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “지시하는 것처럼 보이는 게 좀 조심스러워서요. 하지만 브룩이 만능 심부름꾼처럼 부리는 그 젊은 래디슬로와는 얘기를 나눠봤습니다. 래디슬로는 무슨 일이든 해낼 만큼 영리해 보이더군요. 그가 뭐라고 하는지 들어보는 게 좋겠다 싶었어요. 그런데 그는 이번에 브룩이 출마하는 것에 반대합니다. 내 생각에 그가 그분 마음을 돌려놓을 것 같아요. 지명을 막을 수 있을 것 같습니다.”\n\n“그렇겠죠.” 캐드월러더 부인이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “그 무소속 의원 양반이 아직 연설을 충분히 외우지 못한 모양이니까요.”\n\n“그런데 이 래디슬로라는 자—그게 또 골치 아픈 문제예요.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “브룩의 손님이자 캐소본의 친척이라고 해서, 두세 번 저택 만찬에 그를 불렀거든요(그러고 보니 부인께서도 그를 만나셨지요). 그저 잠깐 들른 거라고만 생각했어요. 그런데 지금 보니 그가 『파이어니어』의 편집장이라고 미들마치 사람들 입에 다 오르내리고 있더군요. 펜대나 굴리는 외지인이니, 외국에서 온 밀정이니, 별의별 소문이 다 돌고 있습니다.”\n\n“캐소본이 그걸 달가워하지 않겠군요.” 목사가 말했다.\n\n“래디슬로에게는 분명 외국 피가 좀 섞여 있어요.” 제임스 경이 대답했다. “극단적인 견해에 빠져들어 브룩까지 끌고 가는 일이 없으면 좋겠는데요.”\n\n“오, 그 래디슬로 씨는 위험한 애송이예요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “오페라 곡조나 흥얼대고 입담도 청산유수지요. 일종의 바이런풍 영웅이랄까—내가 보기엔 호색적인 음모가예요. 게다가 토마스 아퀴나스도 그를 좋아하지 않아요. 그림이 도착하던 날 그걸 알아챘지요.”\n\n“캐소본과 이 문제를 꺼내기는 좀 꺼려집니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “나보다는 그가 끼어들 권리가 더 많으니까요. 하지만 어느 모로 보나 거북한 일이에요. 변변한 연고가 있는 사람이 그런 식으로 나서다니 무슨 꼴입니까!—그 신문쟁이들 중 하나라니요! 『트럼펫』을 운영하는 켁만 봐도 알 만하지요. 며칠 전에 홀리와 함께 있는 걸 봤어요. 글솜씨야 제법 탄탄한 것 같지만, 어찌나 천박한 작자인지, 차라리 반대편에 있었으면 싶더군요.”\n\n“이런 시시한 미들마치 신문들에서 뭘 기대하겠습니까?” 목사가 말했다. “정말로는 관심도 없는 이익을 떠받들고, 입에 풀칠하기도 빠듯한 보수를 받으면서 글을 쓸 고상한 인물을 어디서 구하겠어요.”\n\n“바로 그겁니다. 그래서 브룩이 가족과 어느 정도 연이 있는 사람을 그런 자리에 앉혔다는 게 그토록 짜증스러운 거지요. 내 생각엔 래디슬로도 그걸 받아들이다니 어리석은 사람이에요.”\n\n“그건 아퀴나스 잘못이에요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “자기 연줄을 써서 래디슬로를 외교관 수행원으로 만들거나 인도로 보내버리면 됐잖아요. 집안에서 골칫덩어리 애송이를 처리하는 방법이 그거 아니겠어요.”\n\n“이 말썽이 어디까지 번질지 알 수가 없어요.” 제임스 경이 근심스럽게 말했다. “하지만 캐소본이 아무 말도 하지 않는다면, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?”\n\n“오, 제임스 경.” 목사가 말했다. “이 일을 너무 대단하게 여기지는 맙시다. 그저 연기처럼 흩어져 끝날 공산이 큽니다. 한두 달 지나면 브룩과 이 래디슬로 군은 서로 싫증이 날 테고, 래디슬로는 날아가 버릴 거예요. 브룩은 『파이어니어』를 팔아치울 거고, 모든 게 다시 평소대로 가라앉겠지요.”\n\n“좋은 가능성이 하나 있어요. 그가 자기 돈이 줄줄 새어 나가는 걸 느끼고 싶어 하지 않으리란 거죠.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “내가 선거 비용 항목들을 알기만 한다면 그를 겁줄 수 있을 텐데요. ‘지출’ 같은 두루뭉술한 말로 다그쳐봐야 소용없어요. 나라면 사혈(瀉血) 같은 말은 꺼내지도 않고, 거머리가 든 단지를 통째로 그에게 쏟아붓겠어요. 우리처럼 알뜰한 사람들이 가장 싫어하는 건, 6펜스짜리 동전을 쪽쪽 빨려 나가는 거니까요.”\n\n“그리고 그는 자기에 대한 일들이 들춰지는 걸 싫어할 거예요.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “그분 사유지 관리만 봐도 그렇지요. 벌써 그걸 물고 늘어지기 시작했어요. 그리고 나로서는 정말 보기 괴롭습니다. 바로 코앞에서 벌어지는 골칫거리니까요. 사람은 누구나 자기 땅과 소작인들을 위해 최선을 다해야 한다고 봐요. 특히 이런 어려운 시절에는 말이지요.”\n\n“어쩌면 『트럼펫』이 그를 자극해서 변화를 일으키게 할지도 모르지요. 그러면 이 모든 일에서 무언가 좋은 게 나올 수도 있고요.” 목사가 말했다. “나야 그러면 기쁘겠지요. 십일조를 낼 때 불평을 덜 듣게 될 테니까요. 팁턴에 정액 대납 관행이 없었다면 어쩔 뻔했나 싶어요.”\n\n* 정액 대납: 십일조를 현물 대신 고정된 금액으로 대신 내는 관행.\n“나는 그분이 제대로 된 사람을 들여서 일을 맡기길 바랍니다—다시 가스를 고용하길 바라요.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “십이 년 전에 가스를 내보낸 뒤로 모든 게 엉망이 되어왔으니까요. 나는 가스에게 내 일을 맡길까 생각 중이에요—내 건물 일에 정말 훌륭한 설계를 해주었거든요. 그리고 러브굿은 기대에 좀 못 미쳐요. 하지만 가스는 브룩이 전권을 완전히 넘겨주지 않는 한 다시는 팁턴 사유지를 맡지 않을 겁니다.”\n\n“그것도 당연한 일이지요.” 목사가 말했다. “가스는 독립적인 사람이에요. 독창적이고 소박한 사람이죠. 언젠가 그가 내 일로 평가를 해주던 날, 성직자들은 사업에 대해 아는 게 거의 없으면서 끼어들어 일을 망친다고 대놓고 말하더군요. 하지만 마치 뱃사람 얘기라도 하듯 차분하고 정중하게 말했어요. 브룩이 그에게 관리를 맡기기만 한다면 팁턴을 전혀 다른 교구로 만들어놓을 겁니다. 『트럼펫』의 도움을 받아 그렇게 되도록 해주시면 좋겠어요.”\n\n“도러시아가 삼촌 곁에 머물렀다면 가능성이 좀 있었을 텐데요.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “시간이 지나면서 그분께 어느 정도 영향력을 가졌을지도 몰라요. 그 애는 늘 사유지를 걱정했으니까요. 그런 일에 놀랄 만큼 좋은 식견이 있었어요. 하지만 지금은 캐소본이 그 애를 온통 차지하고 있지요. 실리아도 불평이 이만저만이 아닙니다. 그분이 그 발작을 일으킨 뒤로는 그 애를 우리 집 만찬에 부르기도 어려워졌어요.” 제임스 경은 측은함과 역겨움이 뒤섞인 표정으로 말을 맺었고, 캐드월러더 부인은 어깨를 으쓱하며 자기는 그쪽에서 새로운 일이 생기리라고 보지 않는다는 뜻을 내비쳤다.\n\n“가엾은 캐소본!” 목사가 말했다. “고약한 발작이었지요. 며칠 전 부주교 댁에서 봤을 때 무척 쇠약해 보이더군요.”\n\n“사실은 말입니다.” 제임스 경이 ‘발작’ 이야기는 길게 끌고 싶지 않은 듯 말을 이었다. “브룩이 소작인들이나 다른 누구에게 악의가 있는 건 아니에요. 다만 비용을 깎고 잘라내는 그 버릇이 문제지요.”\n\n“어머, 그거야말로 다행이에요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “그 덕분에 아침마다 제정신을 차리는 거니까요. 자기 의견은 잘 모를지 몰라도 자기 주머니 사정만은 잘 아는 사람이거든요.”\n\n“땅에 인색하게 굴어서 돈을 버는 사람이 있다고는 생각하지 않습니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n“오, 인색함도 다른 미덕처럼 남용될 수 있지요. 자기 돼지를 비쩍 마르게 키우면 안 되는 법이니까요.” 창밖을 내다보려고 일어선 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “그런데 무소속 정치인 얘기를 하니 바로 그가 나타나는군요.”\n\n“뭐라고요! 브룩이?” 남편이 물었다.\n\n“네. 자, 험프리, 당신은 『트럼펫』으로 그를 다그치세요. 나는 그에게 거머리를 붙이겠어요. 제임스 경은 어떻게 하시겠어요?”\n\n“사실은, 서로 처지가 처지인지라 브룩과 그 얘기를 꺼내기가 영 내키지 않습니다. 모든 게 너무나 불쾌한 일이라서요. 사람들이 신사답게 처신해주면 좋으련만.” 마음씨 좋은 준남작이 말했다. 그는 이것이 사회의 안녕을 위한 간단하고도 포괄적인 강령이라고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n“다들 여기 계셨군요, 응?” 브룩 씨가 발을 끌며 둘러보고 악수를 청하며 말했다. “이따가 저택으로 갈 참이었네, 체텀. 그래도 다들 모인 걸 보니 반갑구먼, 알다시피. 자, 요즘 돌아가는 일들을 어떻게들 보시오?—좀 빠르게 굴러가고 있지! 라피트가 한 말이 딱 맞았어—‘어제 이후로 한 세기가 지나갔다’고 말이야. 알다시피 바다 건너 저쪽 사람들은 벌써 다음 세기에 살고 있지. 우리보다 빠르게 나아가고 있어.”\n\n“그렇고말고요.” 목사가 신문을 집어 들며 말했다. “여기 『트럼펫』에서 당신이 뒤처져 있다고 비난하는군요—보셨소?”\n\n“응? 아니.” 브룩 씨가 모자 안에 장갑을 떨어뜨리고 황급히 안경을 고쳐 쓰며 말했다. 그러나 캐드월러더 씨는 눈에 미소를 띤 채 신문을 손에 든 채로 말했다.\n\n“여기 보세요! 이게 전부 미들마치에서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사는, 자기 지대를 꼬박꼬박 받는 어떤 지주에 관한 얘기예요. 그를 두고 주 안에서 가장 퇴행적인 사람이라고 하는군요. 아마 당신이 『파이어니어』에서 그들에게 그 단어를 가르쳐준 모양이지요.”\n\n“오, 그건 켁이야—알다시피 무식한 작자지. 퇴행적이라니, 원! 거참, 걸작이로군. 그자는 그게 파괴적이란 뜻인 줄 아는 거야. 알다시피 나를 파괴분자로 몰아세우려는 거지.” 보통 적수의 무지에서 비롯되곤 하는 그 쾌활함을 띠고 브룩 씨가 말했다.\n\n“내 생각엔 그 사람도 그 단어 뜻을 알고 있어요. 여기 날카로운 일격이 한두 번 나오는군요. ‘우리가 가장 사악한 의미에서 퇴행적인 사람을 묘사해야 한다면, 그는 자신이 직접 책임지고 있는 모든 이익이 썩어가는 동안에도 스스로 우리 헌정의 개혁가를 자처하는 자라고 하겠다. 악당 하나가 교수형 당하는 것은 차마 못 보면서, 정직한 소작인 다섯이 반쯤 굶주리는 것은 개의치 않는 박애주의자. 부패를 두고 비명을 지르면서 자기 농장은 터무니없이 비싼 소작료로 묶어두는 자. 부패 선거구를 두고 얼굴이 벌게지도록 고함치면서, 정작 자기 농장의 들판마다 문짝이 썩어 있어도 아랑곳하지 않는 자. 그가 리즈와 맨체스터에는 더없이 너그러운 게 분명하다. 제 주머니를 털어 의석을 사들일 의원이라면 얼마든지 내어줄 테니까. 그가 내어주기 싫어하는 것은, 소작료 납부일에 소작인이 가축을 사는 데 보태라고 환원해주는 약간의 돈, 혹은 소작인의 헛간 문으로 비바람이 새어 들지 않게 하거나 그 집이 아일랜드 빈농의 오두막보다 조금이라도 덜 초라해 보이게 할 수리 비용이다. 하지만 박애주의자에 대한 익살꾼의 정의를 우리 모두 알지 않는가. 그 자선이 거리의 제곱에 정비례해 커지는 사람이라고.’ 이런 식이에요. 나머지는 전부 박애주의자가 어떤 입법자가 되기 십상인지를 보여주는 내용이고요.” 목사가 신문을 내던지고 두 손을 뒤통수에 깍지 낀 채, 재미있어하는 중립적인 태도로 브룩 씨를 바라보며 말을 맺었다.\n\n“거참, 제법 잘 썼군, 알다시피.” 브룩 씨가 신문을 집어 들며, 이웃이 그러했듯 그 공격을 태연히 받아넘기려 애썼으나 얼굴을 붉히고 다소 초조하게 미소 지으며 말했다. “부패 선거구를 두고 얼굴이 벌게지도록 고함친다는 그 대목—나는 평생 부패 선거구에 관해 연설한 적이 한 번도 없네. 얼굴이 벌게지도록 고함치니 뭐니 하는 것도 그래—이런 자들은 좋은 풍자가 뭔지 도무지 몰라. 알다시피 풍자란 어느 정도까지는 사실이어야 하는 거야. 『에든버러』 어딘가에서 그렇게 말한 걸 기억해—어느 정도까지는 사실이어야 한다고 말이야.”\n\n“글쎄요, 문짝에 관한 건 정말 정곡을 찌른 얘기예요.” 제임스 경이 조심스럽게 디디려 애쓰며 말했다. “요전 날 대글리가 내게 불평하기를, 자기 농장에 변변한 문짝 하나 없다더군요. 가스가 새로운 문짝 양식을 고안했는데—한번 써보시면 좋겠습니다. 목재는 마땅히 그런 데 좀 써야지요.”\n\n“자네는 별난 영농에 빠져 있잖나, 체텀, 알다시피.” 브룩 씨가 『트럼펫』의 지면을 훑어보는 척하며 말했다. “그게 자네 취미니까 비용은 개의치 않는 거지.”\n\n“세상에서 가장 돈이 많이 드는 취미는 의회 출마인 줄 알았는데요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “미들마치에서 지난번에 낙선한 후보가—이름이 자일스였나요?—만 파운드를 쓰고도 뇌물을 충분히 안 써서 떨어졌다더군요. 그 사람으로서는 얼마나 쓰라린 일이겠어요!”\n\n“누군가가 그러는데,” 목사가 웃으며 말했다. “뇌물 쓰는 걸로 치면 이스트 레트퍼드도 미들마치에 비하면 아무것도 아니라더군요.”\n\n“천만에.” 브룩 씨가 말했다. “알다시피 뇌물은 토리당이 쓰지. 홀리와 그 패거리는 향응이며 따끈한 군밤이며 그런 걸로 매수하고, 유권자들을 술에 취하게 해서 투표소로 끌고 간다네. 하지만 앞으로는 자기들 멋대로 하지 못할 거야—알다시피 앞으로는 말이지. 미들마치가 좀 뒤처져 있다는 건 인정해—자유민들이 좀 뒤처져 있지. 하지만 우리가 그들을 교육할 거야—알다시피 우리가 그들을 끌어올릴 거라고. 그곳의 가장 훌륭한 사람들은 우리 편이니까.”\n\n“홀리 말로는 당신 편에 당신에게 해를 끼칠 사람들이 있다더군요.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “은행가 벌스트로드가 당신에게 해가 될 거라고요.”\n\n“그리고 만약 당신이 매질을 당하게 되면,” 캐드월러더 부인이 끼어들었다. “던져지는 썩은 달걀의 절반은 당신 위원회 사람에 대한 증오를 뜻할 거예요. 세상에! 잘못된 견해 때문에 매질당하는 게 어떤 건지 생각해보세요. 그리고 사람들이 어떤 사람을 헹가래 치는 척하다가 일부러 쓰레기 더미에 떨어뜨렸다는 얘기를 들은 기억이 나네요!”\n\n“던져 맞는 거야 사람들이 외투에서 구멍을 찾아내는 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니지요.” 목사가 말했다. “솔직히 우리 목사들이 성직록을 얻으려고 선거 연단에 서야 한다면, 내가 두려워할 게 바로 그겁니다. 내가 낚시하러 다닌 날들을 죄다 헤아릴까 봐 겁이 나겠지요. 정말이지, 진실이야말로 사람이 던져 맞을 수 있는 가장 견디기 힘든 무기라고 생각해요.”\n\n“사실은 말입니다,” 제임스 경이 말했다. “공적인 삶에 발을 들이는 사람이라면 그 결과를 각오해야지요. 중상모략에 끄떡없도록 스스로를 단련해야 하고요.”\n\n“여보게 체텀, 그거야 다 듣기 좋은 말이지, 알다시피.” 브룩 씨가 말했다. “하지만 어떻게 중상모략에 끄떡없도록 자기를 단련하겠나? 역사를 읽어봐야 해—도편추방이며, 박해며, 순교며, 그런 걸 보라고. 알다시피 그런 일은 늘 가장 훌륭한 사람들에게 일어나는 법이야. 그런데 호라티우스에 나오는 그 말이 뭐였더라?—‘피아트 유스티티아, 루아트…’ 뭐라뭐라 하는 거 말일세.”\n* 피아트 유스티티아, 루아트 카일룸: ‘하늘이 무너져도 정의를 행하라’라는 뜻의 라틴어 격언.\n\n“바로 그겁니다.” 제임스 경이 평소보다 좀 더 열을 띠며 말했다. “내가 중상모략에 끄떡없다고 하는 건, 사실을 들어 그것이 거짓임을 입증할 수 있다는 뜻입니다.”\n\n“그리고 자기가 직접 진 빚을 갚는 건 순교가 아니지요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다.\n\n그러나 브룩 씨의 마음을 가장 크게 흔든 것은 제임스 경의 역력한 짜증이었다. “글쎄, 알다시피 체텀,” 그가 일어서서 모자를 집어 들고 지팡이에 몸을 기대며 말했다. “자네와 나는 방식이 달라. 자네는 농장에 죄다 비용을 들이려는 쪽이지. 내 방식이 어떤 상황에서나 좋다고 우길 생각은 없네—어떤 상황에서나 말일세, 알다시피.”\n\n“이따금 새로 평가를 해야 합니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “환원도 가끔은 좋지만, 나는 공정한 평가가 좋습니다. 어떻게 생각하시오, 캐드월러더?”\n\n“동감입니다. 내가 브룩이라면 당장 『트럼펫』의 입을 막아버리겠어요. 가스에게 농장을 새로 평가하게 하고, 문짝이며 수리에 대해 전권을 주는 거지요. 그게 정치 상황에 대한 내 견해예요.” 목사가 양 겨드랑이에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣고 몸을 떡 벌리며, 브룩 씨를 향해 웃으며 말했다.\n\n“그거야 알다시피 겉만 그럴듯한 짓이지.” 브룩 씨가 말했다. “하지만 나만큼 연체된 소작료로 소작인들을 닦달하지 않은 지주가 또 있으면 말해보게. 나는 오래된 소작인들을 그대로 두고 있어. 내가 보기 드물게 너그럽다는 걸 말해두지, 보기 드물게 너그럽다고. 나는 내 나름의 생각이 있고, 알다시피 그 생각을 고수하는 거야. 그런 사람은 늘 괴짜니, 일관성이 없느니 하는 소리를 듣게 마련이지. 내가 방침을 바꾼다면, 그건 내 나름의 생각을 따르는 게 될 걸세.”\n\n그러고 나서 브룩 씨는 그레인지에서 부치는 걸 깜빡한 꾸러미가 있다는 게 생각난 듯, 모두에게 황급히 작별 인사를 했다.\n\n“브룩에게 주제넘게 굴고 싶지 않았어요.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “그분이 발끈한 게 보이더군요. 하지만 오래된 소작인들에 관해 그분이 한 말로 말하자면, 사실 지금 같은 조건으로는 새 소작인이 그 농장들을 맡으려 하지 않을 거예요.”\n\n“내 생각엔 그분도 때가 되면 마음을 돌리게 될 것 같아요.” 목사가 말했다. “하지만 엘리너, 당신은 한쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었고, 우리는 다른 쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었던 거요. 당신은 그를 겁줘서 비용에서 멀어지게 하려 했고, 우리는 겁줘서 비용을 쓰게 하려는 거니까. 차라리 그가 인기를 얻으려 애쓰다가, 지주로서의 평판이 발목을 잡는다는 걸 깨닫게 두는 편이 나아요. 『파이어니어』니, 래디슬로니, 브룩이 미들마치 사람들에게 연설하는 거니 하는 건 지푸라기 두 개만큼도 중요하지 않다고 봐요. 하지만 팁턴의 교구민들이 편안하게 사는가 하는 건 정말 중요한 문제지요.”\n\n“미안하지만, 잘못된 방향으로 가고 있는 건 두 분이에요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “관리를 잘못해서 돈을 잃는다는 걸 그에게 입증했어야 해요. 그랬다면 우리 모두 한 방향으로 끌어당겼겠지요. 그를 정치라는 말에 태워놓으면, 그 결과가 어찌 될지 내가 경고하는 바예요. 집에서 막대기를 타고 다니며 그걸 사상이라 부르는 거야 무방했지만 말이에요.”\n\n# CHAPTER XXXIX.\n\n그가 남자인지 여자인지 잊는다면.\n또 이 사랑이, 그렇게 자리 잡았어도,\n\n그것에 아무 믿음도 두지 않거나,\n설령 믿더라도 비웃을 뿐인\n\n속된 사람들로부터 그대가 감춘다면.\n그렇다면 그대는 모든 위인들이 한 일보다\n\n더 용감한 일을 해낸 것이며,\n거기서 더 용감한 일이 솟아나리니,\n\n그것은 곧 그것을 감추는 일이라.\n—던 박사\n\n제임스 체텀 경의 머리는 책략을 풍부하게 짜내는 편이 아니었으나, \"브룩 씨에게 영향을 미치고\" 싶은 점점 커지는 그의 조바심이, 도러시아의 설득력에 대한 그의 변함없는 믿음과 한번 가까이 맞닿자 어떤 형태를 갖추기 시작하더니 마침내 작은 계획으로 결실을 맺었다. 즉 실리아가 몸이 좋지 않다는 것을 구실 삼아 도러시아만 따로 홀로 데려오되, 오는 길에 영지 관리에 관한 상황을 그녀에게 충분히 알린 뒤, 마차와 함께 그레인지에 그녀를 내려 준다는 것이었다.\n그리하여 어느 날 네 시 무렵, 브룩 씨와 래디슬로가 서재에 앉아 있을 때, 문이 열리고 캐소본 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 들어오는 일이 일어났다.\n\n윌은 바로 그 직전까지 권태의 밑바닥에 가라앉아 있었고, 양 도둑을 교수형에 처하는 일에 관한 \"서류\"를 정리하는 브룩 씨를 도와야 하는 처지에서, 미들마치에 자기 거처를 마련해 그레인지에 늘 머무는 것을 끝낼 방안을 속으로 궁리하며, 우리 마음이 한꺼번에 여러 마리 말을 몰 수 있는 힘을 몸소 보여 주고 있었다. 그러면서도 이 한층 진지한 심상들 사이로, 호메로스적인 세밀함으로 쓴 양 도둑 서사시라는 간질간질한 환상이 스쳐 지나가고 있었다. 캐소본 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 들어오자 그는 전기 충격이라도 받은 듯 벌떡 일어났고, 손끝이 짜릿해지는 것을 느꼈다. 누가 그를 지켜보았더라면 그의 안색이, 얼굴 근육의 자리잡음이, 눈빛의 생생함이 변하는 것을 보았을 테고, 그의 몸 안 모든 분자가 어떤 마법의 손길이 전하는 전갈을 받았으리라 상상했을 것이다. 실제로 그러했다. 효과적인 마법이란 초월적인 자연이니, 영혼의 자질을 육체의 자질과 더불어 전달하고, 한 남자가 한 여인에게 품는 열정을 다른 여인에게 품는 열정과 다르게 만드는—마치 골짜기와 강과 흰 산봉우리 위로 쏟아지는 아침 햇살 속의 기쁨이 중국식 등롱과 유리 칸막이 사이의 기쁨과 다르듯이—그런 손길의 섬세함을 누가 가늠할 수 있겠는가? 윌 또한 매우 예민한 재질로 빚어진 사람이었다. 가까이서 솜씨 좋게 그은 바이올린의 활은 단 한 번의 긋기로 그에게 세상의 양상을 바꿔 놓을 수 있었고, 그의 관점은 기분만큼이나 쉽게 옮겨 갔다. 도러시아의 등장은 아침의 신선함이었다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 이거 참 반갑구나.\" 브룩 씨가 그녀를 맞아 입맞춤하며 말했다. \"캐소본은 책과 함께 두고 왔겠지. 잘했다. 너를 여자치고 너무 박식하게 만들면 안 되니까 말이다.\"\n\n\"그럴 염려는 없어요, 외삼촌.\" 도러시아는 윌 쪽으로 돌아서서 거리낌 없이 명랑하게 악수를 하며 말했는데, 다른 인사말은 하지 않고 곧장 외삼촌에게 대답을 이어 갔다. \"저는 아주 느려요. 책에 열중하고 싶을 때면 자꾸만 제 생각들 사이로 딴청을 부리는걸요. 박식해지는 것이 오두막 설계도를 그리는 것만큼 쉽지가 않네요.\"\n그녀는 윌의 맞은편에 외삼촌 곁에 앉았는데, 무언가에 마음을 빼앗긴 듯 그를 거의 의식하지 않는 게 분명했다. 그는 마치 그녀가 온 것이 자기와 무슨 관계라도 있다고 상상했던 것처럼, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 실망했다.\n\n\"네.\" 도러시아가 특유의 직설적인 태도로 말했다. \"제임스 경이 말씀하시기를, 외삼촌의 영지 관리에 곧 큰 변화가 생기기를 바라고 계신대요. 농장을 평가하고, 수리를 하고, 오두막을 개선해서 팁턴이 아주 다른 곳처럼 보이도록 하실 생각이라고요. 아, 정말 기뻐요!\" 그녀는 두 손을 맞잡으며, 결혼 후로 가라앉아 있던 그 한층 어린아이 같고 격정적인 태도로 돌아가 말을 이었다. \"제가 아직 집에 있었더라면 다시 말을 타기 시작했을 거예요. 그래서 외삼촌과 함께 돌아다니며 그 모든 걸 보았을 텐데요! 그리고 제 오두막을 칭찬했던 가스 씨를 고용하실 거라고 제임스 경이 말씀하셨어요.\"\n\n\"체텀은 좀 성급하구나, 얘야.\" 브룩 씨가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"좀 성급해, 알다시피. 나는 그런 일을 하겠다고 말한 적이 없어. 그렇다고 안 하겠다고 말한 적도 없지만 말이다, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"그분은 외삼촌이 그렇게 하시리라 확신할 뿐이에요.\" 도러시아가 신경을 외는 어린 성가대원처럼 맑고 거침없는 목소리로 말했다. \"왜냐하면 외삼촌은 백성들의 향상을 염려하는 의원으로 의회에 들어가실 작정이시고, 가장 먼저 개선되어야 할 것 중 하나가 바로 땅과 노동자들의 처지니까요. 키트 다운스를 생각해 보세요, 외삼촌. 그는 아내와 일곱 아이와 함께 거실 하나, 그리고 이 탁자보다 별로 크지도 않은 침실 하나뿐인 집에서 살고 있어요! 그리고 저 가엾은 데글리네는 다 쓰러져 가는 농가에서, 뒷부엌에서 살며 다른 방들은 쥐들에게 내준 채 지내고 있고요! 그것이 바로 제가 이곳의 그림들을 좋아하지 않았던 한 가지 이유예요, 외삼촌. 외삼촌은 제가 그 일에 어리석다고 여기시지만요. 저는 그 온갖 더러움과 거친 추함을 가슴속 아픔처럼 안고 마을에서 돌아오곤 했는데, 그러면 응접실의 그 간드러진 그림들이 거짓된 것에서 즐거움을 찾으려는 사악한 시도처럼 보였어요. 우리 담장 밖 이웃들에게 진실이 얼마나 가혹한지는 아랑곳하지 않으면서 말이에요. 우리 손이 닿는 곳에 놓인 악들을 바꾸려고 애써 보지도 않은 채 앞에 나서서 더 넓은 선의 변화를 부르짖을 권리는 우리에게 없다고 생각해요.\"\n\n도러시아는 말을 이어 가면서 점점 감정에 사로잡혔고, 제지받지 않고 마음을 쏟아 내는 안도감 외에는 모든 것을 잊고 있었다. 그것은 한때 그녀에게 익숙했으나 결혼 후로는 거의 찾아오지 않은 경험이었으니, 그녀의 결혼 생활은 활력과 두려움 사이의 끊임없는 투쟁이었던 것이다. 그 순간 윌의 감탄에는 서늘한 거리감이 함께 깃들었다. 한 여인에게서 어떤 위대함을 볼 때, 그녀를 그만큼 잘 사랑할 수 없다고 느끼는 것을 부끄러워하는 남자는 드물다. 자연은 위대함을 남자의 몫으로 정해 두었으니까. 하지만 자연은 때때로 그 의도를 실행하면서 서글픈 실수를 저질러 왔다. 선량한 브룩 씨의 경우가 그러했으니, 그의 남성적 의식은 이 순간 조카딸의 웅변 앞에서 다소 더듬거리는 상태에 빠져 있었다. 그는 자리에서 일어나 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 앞에 놓인 서류들을 만지작거리는 것 말고는 당장 자기를 표현할 다른 방도를 찾지 못했다. 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네 말에 일리가 있다, 얘야, 네 말에 일리가 있어. 하지만 전부 옳은 건 아니지. 안 그런가, 래디슬로? 자네와 나는 우리 그림이며 조각이 흠잡히는 걸 좋아하지 않아. 젊은 아가씨들은 좀 열정적이거든, 알다시피. 좀 한쪽으로 치우치고 말이야, 얘야. 미술이라든가 시 같은 것은 한 나라를 고양시켜 주거든. 에몰리트 모레스(품성을 부드럽게 한다)지. 너도 이제 라틴어를 좀 알지. 그런데, 어? 뭐냐?\"\n\n이 물음들은 사냥터지기가 데글리네 아들 하나가 방금 잡은 새끼 산토끼를 손에 든 채 붙잡혔다고 전하러 들어온 하인을 향한 것이었다.\n\n\"가지, 가지. 가볍게 풀어 줄 거야, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 도러시아에게 슬쩍 말하고는 아주 흔쾌하게 발을 끌며 나갔다.\n\n\"제가, 아니 제임스 경이 바라는 이 변화가 얼마나 옳은 것인지 당신도 느끼시기를 바라요.\" 외삼촌이 나가자마자 도러시아가 윌에게 말했다.\n\n\"느낍니다, 이제 당신이 그것에 관해 말하는 걸 들었으니까요. 당신이 한 말을 잊지 않겠습니다. 그런데 지금 이 순간 다른 일을 좀 생각해 주실 수 있겠습니까? 일어난 일에 관해 당신과 이야기할 기회가 다시 없을지도 모르니까요.\" 윌이 조바심이 묻어나는 동작으로 일어나 두 손으로 의자 등받이를 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 무엇인지 말씀해 주세요.\" 도러시아가 걱정스레 말하며, 그녀 역시 일어나 열린 창가로 갔다. 거기서 멍크가 헐떡이며 꼬리를 흔들며 안을 들여다보고 있었다. 그녀는 창틀에 등을 기댄 채 개의 머리에 손을 얹었다. 우리도 알다시피 그녀는 손에 안고 다니거나 발치에서 밟히는 애완동물을 좋아하지 않았지만, 개들의 감정에는 늘 마음을 썼고, 그들의 접근을 거절해야 할 때면 무척 정중했던 것이다.\n\n윌은 눈으로만 그녀를 좇으며 말했다. \"캐소본 씨가 제게 자기 집에 오는 것을 금했다는 걸 당신도 아시리라 짐작합니다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 몰랐어요.\" 도러시아가 잠시 멈췄다가 말했다. 그녀는 분명 무척 동요한 듯했다. \"정말, 정말 안타까워요.\" 그녀가 슬프게 덧붙였다. 그녀는 윌이 알지 못하는 것을 떠올리고 있었다. 어둠 속에서 그녀와 남편 사이에 오갔던 대화를. 그리고 자신이 캐소본 씨의 행동에 영향을 미칠 수 있으리라는 희망 없음에 새삼 마음이 찔렸다. 그러나 그녀의 슬픔이 뚜렷이 드러난 표정을 보고 윌은, 그것이 모두 자기 개인을 향한 것이 아니며, 캐소본 씨가 그를 싫어하고 질투하는 것이 그녀 자신에게로 향한 것이라는 생각이 도러시아에게는 떠오른 적조차 없음을 확신하게 되었다. 그는 기쁨과 짜증이 묘하게 뒤섞인 감정을 느꼈다. 의심도 인색함도 없이, 순수한 보금자리에서처럼 그녀의 생각 속에 머물며 소중히 여겨질 수 있다는 기쁨, 그리고 그녀에게 자기가 너무도 하찮은 존재이며, 두려워할 만한 상대가 못 되고, 그를 우쭐하게 하지도 않는 거침없는 호의로 대접받고 있다는 짜증이었다. 그러나 도러시아에게 어떤 변화가 생길까 봐 두려운 마음이 불만보다 강했으므로, 그는 그저 설명하는 어조로 다시 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"캐소본 씨의 이유인즉, 제가 여기서 그의 사촌이라는 신분에 어울리지 않는다고 그가 여기는 자리를 맡은 것에 대한 불쾌함입니다. 저는 이 점에 관해서는 양보할 수 없다고 그에게 말했습니다. 제가 우스꽝스럽다고 여기는 편견 때문에 제 삶의 진로가 방해받기를 기대하는 것은 제게 좀 너무 가혹한 일이지요. 의무라는 것도 잡아 늘이다 보면, 우리가 그 의미를 알기에는 너무 어렸을 때 우리에게 찍힌 노예의 낙인이나 다름없는 것이 되어 버릴 수 있습니다. 저는 이 자리를 유용하고 명예롭게 만들 생각이 없었다면 받아들이지도 않았을 겁니다. 저는 가문의 위엄을 그 외의 다른 관점으로 존중할 의무가 없습니다.\"\n\n도러시아는 비참한 기분이 들었다. 그녀는 남편이 윌이 말한 것보다 더 여러 가지 점에서 완전히 잘못되었다고 생각했다.\n\n\"우리는 이 문제에 관해 이야기하지 않는 편이 좋겠어요.\" 그녀가 평소 그녀의 목소리에서는 보기 드문 떨림을 띠고 말했다. \"당신과 캐소본 씨가 의견이 다르니까요. 계속 여기 머무실 작정이세요?\" 그녀는 우울한 명상에 잠겨 잔디밭을 내다보고 있었다.\n\n\"네, 하지만 이제 당신을 거의 뵙지 못하겠지요.\" 윌이 거의 소년 같은 푸념조로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇겠죠.\" 도러시아가 그를 향해 눈을 가득 돌리며 말했다. \"거의 못 보겠죠. 하지만 당신 소식은 듣겠지요. 당신이 외삼촌을 위해 무얼 하는지 알게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신에 관해선 거의 아무것도 모를 텐데요.\" 윌이 말했다. \"아무도 제게 아무 말도 해 주지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 제 삶은 아주 단순해요.\" 도러시아가 입가에 더없이 고운 미소를 머금자, 그 미소가 그녀의 우울을 환히 밝혔다. \"저는 늘 로윅에 있어요.\"\n\n\"그건 끔찍한 감금이군요.\" 윌이 격하게 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 그렇게 생각하지 마세요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"저는 갈망하는 게 없어요.\"\n\n그는 말이 없었지만, 그녀는 그의 표정에 떠오른 어떤 변화에 답하듯 말했다. \"제 말은, 제 자신을 위해서는요. 다만 남들을 위해 아무것도 하지 않으면서 제 몫보다 그렇게 많이 갖지는 않았으면 좋겠다는 것뿐이에요. 하지만 저에게는 저 나름의 믿음이 있고, 그것이 저를 위로해 줘요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇입니까?\" 윌이 그 믿음을 다소 질투하듯 물었다.\n\n\"완전히 선한 것을 갈망함으로써, 비록 그것이 무엇인지 잘 모르고 우리가 바라는 바를 행할 수 없을 때조차, 우리는 악에 맞서는 신성한 권능의 일부가 된다는 거예요. 빛의 자락을 넓히고, 어둠과의 투쟁을 좁혀 가는 거죠.\"\n\n\"그건 아름다운 신비주의로군요. 그건 일종의—\"\n\n\"제발 그것에 무슨 이름을 붙이지 말아 주세요.\" 도러시아가 간청하듯 두 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"당신은 그게 페르시아 식이라느니, 뭔가 지리적인 무엇이라느니 하시겠죠. 그건 제 삶이에요. 제가 찾아낸 것이고, 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것이에요. 저는 어린 소녀 때부터 늘 제 신앙을 찾아오고 있었어요. 예전엔 기도를 그렇게 많이 했는데, 지금은 거의 기도하지 않아요. 저는 그저 제 자신만을 위한 욕망을 갖지 않으려고 애써요. 그건 남들에게는 좋지 않을 수도 있고, 저는 이미 너무 많이 가졌으니까요. 제가 로윅에서 어떻게 나날을 보내는지 당신이 잘 알 수 있도록 말씀드린 것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"말해 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다, 신의 축복이 있기를!\" 윌이 열렬하게, 그리고 스스로도 다소 놀라며 말했다. 두 사람은 새 이야기를 은밀히 주고받는 다정한 두 아이처럼 서로를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"당신의 신앙은 무엇인가요?\" 도러시아가 물었다. \"제 말은, 당신이 종교에 관해 무엇을 아는가가 아니라, 당신을 가장 많이 도와주는 믿음 말이에요.\"\n\n\"제 눈에 선하고 아름다운 것이 보일 때 그것을 사랑하는 것입니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 반항아예요. 당신처럼 마음에 들지 않는 것에 복종해야 한다고 느끼지 않거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신이 선한 것을 좋아한다면, 그건 결국 같은 거예요.\" 도러시아가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 당신이 미묘해지는군요.\" 윌이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 캐소본 씨도 제가 너무 미묘하다고 자주 말해요. 저는 제가 미묘하다고는 느껴지지 않는데요.\" 도러시아가 장난스럽게 말했다. \"그런데 외삼촌이 어쩜 이리 오래 걸리실까! 가서 찾아봐야겠어요. 정말 홀로 가야 해요. 실리아가 저를 기다리고 있거든요.\"\n\n윌이 브룩 씨에게 알려 드리겠다고 나섰는데, 브룩 씨는 곧 와서는 자기도 마차에 올라타 새끼 산토끼를 잡다 걸린 그 어린 범법자에 관해 이야기하러 데글리네까지 도러시아와 함께 가겠다고 말했다. 도러시아는 마차를 타고 가는 동안 영지 문제를 다시 꺼냈으나, 이번에는 허를 찔리지 않은 브룩 씨가 대화를 자기 뜻대로 끌고 갔다.\n\n\"체텀으로 말하자면 말이다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"그는 나를 흠잡는단다, 얘야. 하지만 체텀이 아니었더라면 나는 사냥감을 보존하지도 않았을 게다. 그런데 그 비용이 소작인들을 위한 것이라고는 말할 수 없거든, 알다시피. 그게 내 마음에는 좀 걸린단다. 밀렵 말이야, 따지고 보면 말이지, 나는 이 문제를 한번 다뤄 봐야겠다고 자주 생각했어. 얼마 전에 감리교 설교자인 플라벨이 아내와 함께 산책하던 중에 길을 가로질러 온 산토끼를 때려잡은 일로 끌려왔지. 그가 꽤 날쌔서 산토끼 목을 후려쳤거든.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 그건 아주 잔인한 짓이에요.\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 그래, 솔직히 내게도 그게 좀 떳떳지 못해 보이더구나, 감리교 설교자라는 사람이 말이야, 알다시피. 그래서 존슨이 그러더군. '저자가 얼마나 위선자인지 알 만하지 않습니까.' 정말이지 플라벨은 어떤 이가 그리스도인을 두고 일컬은 '인간의 가장 높은 경지'와는 영 거리가 멀어 보였어. 그렇게 일컬은 게 누구더라, 영, 시인 영이었던 것 같은데. 너 영을 아니? 아무튼, 플라벨이 그 다 해진 검은 각반 차림으로, 주님이 자기와 아내에게 좋은 저녁거리를 보내 주셨다고 생각했으니 그걸 때려잡을 권리가 있었다고 변명을 하더란 말이지, 비록 그가 니므롯처럼 주님 앞의 위대한 사냥꾼은 아니었지만 말이야. 정말이지 좀 우스꽝스러웠어. 필딩이라면 그걸 가지고 뭔가 만들어 냈을 테고, 아니면 스콧이라면 말이야, 스콧이라면 그걸 잘 다듬어 냈을 거야. 그런데 정말이지, 곰곰 생각해 보니 그 친구가 식전 기도를 올릴 산토끼 한 조각이라도 갖게 된 게 싫지가 않더구나. 그게 다 편견의 문제거든. 법이 제 편에 선 편견 말이야, 알다시피. 그 막대기니 각반이니 하는 것들에 관한 편견 말이다. 하지만 무슨 일이든 이치를 따져 봐야 소용없는 법이고, 법은 법이지. 어쨌든 나는 존슨을 조용히 시키고 그 일을 덮어 버렸어. 체텀이라면 더 엄하게 굴지 않았을까 싶기도 한데, 그런데도 그는 내가 이 지방에서 가장 모진 사람이라도 되는 양 몰아붙이거든. 자, 데글리네에 다 왔구나.\"\n\n브룩 씨가 농가 마당 문 앞에서 내리자 도러시아는 마차를 타고 계속 갔다. 어떤 일로 우리가 비난받고 있다고 의심만 하게 되어도 그 일이 얼마나 더 흉하게 보이는지 놀라울 정도다. 우리 자신의 모습조차도, 그 덜 훌륭한 점에 관해 누군가의 솔직한 말을 듣고 나면 거울 속에서 그 양상이 달라져 보이기 십상이다. 반면, 결코 불평하지 않거나 대신 불평해 줄 이가 없는 사람들에게 우리가 끼친 침해를 양심이 얼마나 편안하게 받아들이는지는 놀라운 일이다. 데글리네 농가가 오늘처럼 브룩 씨에게 음울하게 보인 적은 일찍이 없었으니, 그의 마음이 「트럼펫」지의 흠잡기로, 그리고 제임스 경이 되풀이한 그 흠잡기로 이렇게 쓰라렸던 것이다.\n\n물론, 남들의 고난을 그림처럼 보이게 하는 그 미술의 부드러운 영향 아래 있는 관찰자라면 프리먼스 엔드라 불리는 이 농가를 보고 흐뭇해했을지도 모른다. 그 오래된 집은 검붉은 지붕에 지붕창들이 나 있었고, 두 굴뚝은 담쟁이덩굴로 막혀 있었으며, 커다란 현관은 잔가지 다발들로 가로막혀 있었고, 창문의 절반은 벌레 먹은 회색 덧문으로 닫혀 있었는데 그 주위로 재스민 가지들이 거칠게 무성히 자라 있었다. 접시꽃이 너머로 빠끔히 내다보는 허물어져 가는 정원 담장은 한껏 어우러진 은은한 색채의 완벽한 습작이었고, (틀림없이 흥미로운 미신적 이유로 키우는) 늙은 염소 한 마리가 열린 뒷부엌 문에 기대 누워 있었다. 이끼 낀 소외양간의 초가지붕, 부서진 회색 헛간 문, 이른 타작을 위해 헛간에 옥수수 한 수레를 거의 다 부려 놓은, 누더기 반바지 차림의 가난한 일꾼들, 젖을 짜려고 매어 둔 빈약한 젖소 떼와 그 탓에 헛간 한쪽이 갈색의 텅 빈 공간으로 남은 모습, 너무 형편없는 구정물을 먹어 풀이 죽은 듯 울퉁불퉁한 버려진 마당을 어슬렁거리는 듯한 돼지들과 흰 오리들까지, 높은 구름이 대리석 무늬를 이룬 하늘의 고요한 빛 아래 이 모든 것은 우리 모두가 한 번쯤 \"근사한 한 폭\"이라 일컬으며 발길을 멈춘 그런 그림을 이루었을 테고, 그 시절 신문에 끊임없이 실리던 대로 농업 자본의 서글픈 부족과 더불어 농업의 침체가 불러일으키는 감정과는 다른 감수성을 건드렸을 것이다. 그러나 이 골치 아픈 연상들이 지금 브룩 씨의 마음에 강하게 떠올라 그에게 그 광경을 망쳐 놓았다. 데글리 씨 자신도 그 풍경의 한 인물을 이루었으니, 그는 쇠스랑을 든 채 젖 짤 때 쓰는 모자—앞부분이 납작해진 아주 낡은 비버 가죽 모자를 쓰고 있었다. 그가 입은 외투와 반바지는 가진 것 중 가장 좋은 것이어서, 이런 평일에는 입지 않았을 텐데, 장에 나갔다가 블루 불 식당의 공동 식탁에서 점심을 먹는 드문 호사를 누리느라 평소보다 늦게 돌아온 탓이었다. 그가 어쩌다 이런 사치에 빠졌는지는 어쩌면 그 자신도 이튿날 의아해할 일이었다. 하지만 점심을 들기 전에 나라 사정이라든가, 파 딥스 밭을 베기 전 추수의 짧은 휴지기라든가, 새 임금에 관한 이야기들과 벽에 잔뜩 붙은 전단들 같은 것들이 약간의 무모함을 정당화해 주는 듯 보였던 것이다. 좋은 고기에는 좋은 술이 따라야 한다는 것이 미들마치의 격언이자 자명한 이치로 통했는데, 데글리는 그것을 식탁용 에일을 넉넉히 마시고 럼주를 탄 물로 든든히 뒤를 받치는 것으로 해석했다. 이 술들에는 그나마 진실이 담겨 있었으니, 가엾은 데글리를 흥겨워 보이게 할 만큼 거짓되지는 않아, 다만 평소보다 그의 불만을 덜 굳게 만들었을 뿐이다. 그는 또한 흙탕물 같은 정치 이야기도 너무 많이 들이켰는데, 그것은 그의 농사꾼다운 보수성을 위험하게 흔드는 자극제였다. 그 보수성이란 무엇이 됐든 현재 있는 것은 나쁘고, 어떤 변화든 더 나빠지기 쉽다고 여기는 데에 있었다. 그는 얼굴이 벌게져 있었고, 쇠스랑을 꼭 쥔 채 가만히 서 있을 때 그의 눈에는 단연 싸움을 거는 듯한 눈빛이 서려 있었다. 그러는 사이 지주가 한 손은 바지 주머니에 찌르고 다른 손으로는 가느다란 지팡이를 빙빙 돌리며 느긋하게 발을 끌며 다가왔다.\n\n\"데글리, 이 사람아.\" 브룩 씨가 그 아이 일에 관해 아주 다정하게 굴리라 마음먹은 채 입을 열었다.\n\n\"오, 그렇죠, 제가 좋은 사람이라고요? 고맙수다, 나리, 고마워요.\" 데글리가 으르렁대는 듯한 비아냥을 큰 소리로 내뱉자, 양치기 개 패그가 앉은 자리에서 몸을 들썩이며 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다. 하지만 멍크가 바깥에서 어슬렁거리다가 마당으로 들어오는 것을 보고는 패그는 다시 자리에 앉아 살피는 자세를 취했다. \"내가 좋은 사람이란 말을 들으니 기쁘구먼요.\"\n\n브룩 씨는 장날인 데다 자기의 점잖은 소작인이 아마 점심을 든 모양이라고 헤아렸지만, 데글리 부인에게 해야 할 말을 되풀이하는 조처를 취하면 되니 말을 계속하지 못할 이유가 없다고 보았다.\n\n\"자네 아들 녀석 제이컵이 새끼 산토끼를 잡다가 붙잡혔다네, 데글리. 내가 존슨에게 일러서 그 애를 한두 시간 빈 마구간에 가둬 두라고 했어, 그저 겁을 좀 주려고 말이야, 알다시피. 하지만 이따가, 날 저물기 전에 집으로 데려다줄 걸세. 그러니 자네가 그 애를 잘 살펴서 따끔하게 야단을 좀 쳐 주게나, 알겠지?\"\n\n\"싫소. 나리든 그 누구든 비위 맞추자고 내 자식을 두들겨 패느니 차라리 뒈지고 말겠소. 나리가 한 사람이 아니라 스무 명 지주라 해도, 그것도 나쁜 지주라면 말이오.\"\n\n데글리의 말은 그의 아내를 뒷부엌 문으로 불러낼 만큼 큰 소리였으니, 그 문은 늘 쓰는 유일한 출입구로 궂은 날씨가 아니면 언제나 열려 있었다. 브룩 씨는 \"그래, 그래, 자네 아내에게 말하겠네. 때리라는 뜻은 아니었어, 알다시피.\" 하고 달래듯 말하며 집 쪽으로 걸어가려고 돌아섰다. 하지만 데글리는 자기를 두고 걸어가 버리는 신사에게 \"할 말을 다 하고\" 싶은 마음이 더욱 동했으므로 곧장 뒤따랐고, 패그는 그 발치에서 어슬렁거리며 멍크가 베푸는 작고 아마도 호의적인 접근들을 시무룩하게 피했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 데글리 부인?\" 브룩 씨가 다소 서둘러 말했다. \"댁의 아들 일로 말하러 왔소. 그 애에게 매를 들기를 바라지 않소, 알겠지.\" 그는 이번에는 아주 분명하게 말하려고 신경을 썼다.\n\n혹사당한 데글리 부인은 여위고 지친 여인으로, 그 삶에서 즐거움이 너무도 완전히 사라져 버려 교회에 갈 채비를 하며 만족감을 느낄 만한 나들이옷 한 벌조차 없는 처지였는데, 남편이 돌아온 뒤로 이미 그와 한바탕 말다툼을 벌인 터라 풀이 죽어 최악의 사태를 예상하고 있었다. 그러나 남편이 먼저 나서서 대답했다.\n\n\"안 되오, 그 애는 매를 안 맞을 거요, 나리가 원하든 말든.\" 데글리가 멀리까지 들리라는 듯 목소리를 내지르며 말을 이었다. \"나리는 이 집에 와서 매가 어떠니 말할 자격이 없소. 고치라고 막대기 하나 안 대 줄 거면서 말이오. 나리 평판이나 알아보러 미들마치에 가 보슈.\"\n\n\"입 좀 다무는 게 훨씬 낫겠수, 데글리.\" 아내가 말했다. \"제 밥그릇 제가 차지 말고요. 한 집안의 가장이란 사람이 장에 가서 돈을 펑펑 쓰고 술에 절어 망가져서 돌아왔으면, 하루치 못된 짓은 충분히 한 거예요. 하지만 우리 애가 무슨 짓을 했는지 저는 알고 싶네요, 나리.\"\n\n\"그 애가 뭘 했는지 당신은 신경 쓰지 마.\" 데글리가 더 사납게 말했다. \"말하는 건 내 일이지 당신 일이 아니야. 그리고 난 말할 거야. 할 말은 하고 말 거라고, 저녁이고 뭐고. 내가 하려는 말은 이거요. 나는 우리 아버지, 그 위로 할아버지 적부터 나리네 땅에 살면서 우리 돈을 그 땅에 떨어뜨려 왔단 말이오. 그런데 임금님이 막아 주지 않으면, 나하고 내 자식들은 거름 살 돈도 없어서 땅바닥에 누워 썩어 갈 판이오.\"\n\n\"이 사람아, 자네 취했구먼, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 은밀히, 그러나 분별없이 말했다. \"다음에, 다음에 하지.\" 그가 가려는 듯 돌아서며 덧붙였다.\n\n하지만 데글리가 즉시 그의 앞을 가로막았고, 주인의 목소리가 점점 커지고 더 모욕적으로 변하자 패그도 그 발치에서 낮게 으르렁거렸으며, 멍크 역시 가까이 다가와 조용히 위엄 있게 지켜보았다. 수레의 일꾼들도 귀를 기울이려 손을 멈추었으니, 고래고래 소리치는 사내에게 쫓기며 우스꽝스럽게 달아나려 하기보다 완전히 잠자코 있는 편이 현명해 보였다.\n\n\"나는 나리보다 더 취하지도 않았고, 그만큼도 안 취했소.\" 데글리가 말했다. \"나는 술을 견딜 줄 알고, 내가 무슨 말을 하는지도 안단 말이오. 내 말은 임금님이 그걸 막아 주실 거란 거요. 아는 사람들이 그러는데, 개혁이란 게 있을 거고, 소작인들한테 옳은 일을 한 번도 안 한 지주들은 줄행랑을 쳐야 할 만큼 혼이 날 거라고 말이오. 미들마치에는 개혁이 뭔지 아는 사람들이 있단 말이오. 누가 줄행랑을 쳐야 할지도 아는 사람들이. 그자들이 그래요. '난 네 지주가 누군지 알지.' 그래서 내가 그랬소. '그 사람을 안다고 당신들이 더 나아지길 바라오, 난 그렇질 못하니.' 그자들이 그래요. '그 사람 손이 야무진 구두쇠지.' '그렇소, 그렇고말고.' 하고 내가 그랬소. '그 사람은 개혁을 지지하는 사람이오.' 하고 그자들이 그래요. 그게 그자들이 한 말이오. 그래서 내가 개혁이 뭔지 알아냈는데, 그건 나리하고 나리 같은 사람들을 줄행랑치게 만드는 거였소. 그것도 아주 고약한 냄새 풍기는 것들로 말이오. 이제 나리 멋대로 하슈, 난 나리가 하나도 안 무서우니까. 그리고 내 자식은 내버려 두고, 개혁이 나리 등짝을 덮치기 전에 나리 앞가림이나 잘하슈. 내가 할 말은 이게 다요.\" 데글리 씨는 이렇게 말을 맺으며 쇠스랑을 단호하게 땅에 꽂았는데, 그 단호함은 다시 뽑아 올리려 할 때 불편하게 작용했다.\n\n이 마지막 동작에 멍크가 요란하게 짖어 대기 시작했고, 브룩 씨로서는 빠져나갈 순간이었다. 그는 자기 처지의 생소함에 다소 어리둥절해하며 되도록 빨리 마당에서 걸어 나왔다. 그는 자기 땅에서 모욕을 당해 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 스스로를 두루 사랑받는 사람으로 여기는 쪽이었다(우리는 남들이 우리에게서 바랄 법한 것보다 우리 자신의 상냥함을 더 떠올릴 때면 누구나 그렇게 여기기 마련이다). 십이 년 전 케일럽 가스와 다투었을 때, 그는 지주가 모든 것을 자기 손에 직접 거머쥐는 것을 소작인들이 좋아하리라 생각했었다.\n\n그의 경험담을 따라온 이들 중 누군가는 데글리 씨의 한밤중 같은 무지에 놀랄지도 모른다. 하지만 그 시절에는 그만한 처지의 세습 농사꾼이 무지한 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없었다. 어찌 된 일인지 쌍둥이 교구에는 뼛속까지 신사인 교구 목사가 있었고, 더 가까이에는 그 목사보다 더 박식하게 설교하는 부목사가 있었으며, 무엇이든, 특히 미술과 사회 개선에 파고든 지주가 있었고, 미들마치의 온갖 빛이 불과 삼 마일 떨어진 곳에 있었는데도 그러했다. 인간이 얼마나 쉽게 지식에서 벗어나는지로 말하자면, 런던의 지적인 광채 속에 있는 평범한 지인 하나를 시험 삼아 떠올려 보고, 그 만찬에 어울릴 법한 사람이 만약 팁턴의 교구 서기에게서 \"셈하기\"를 어설프게밖에 못 배웠고, 이사야니 아폴로니 하는 이름들이 두 번을 떠듬떠듬 읽고 나서도 도무지 다룰 수 없는 탓에 성경 한 장을 엄청나게 애를 써서 읽었다면 어떠했을지 생각해 보라. 가엾은 데글리는 일요일 저녁이면 가끔 몇 구절을 읽었고, 그에게 세상은 적어도 예전보다 더 어둡지는 않았다. 그가 속속들이 아는 것이 몇 가지 있었으니, 곧 엉성한 농사 습관이며, 프리먼스 엔드의 날씨와 가축과 작물의 까다로움이었다. 프리먼스 엔드라는 이름은 아무래도 빈정대는 뜻으로 붙인 듯했으니, 마음만 먹으면 이곳을 떠날 자유는 있으나 그에게 열려 있는 이 세상의 \"그 너머\"란 어디에도 없음을 빗댄 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XL.\n\n그는 매일의 일에 있어 현명했네.\n부지런함의 결실을 위해,\n\n신앙이나 정치를 위해서가 아니라,\n온 힘을 다해 분별을 기울였네.\n\n저마다 작은 몫에서 완벽한 이들,\n그 일이 곧 그들의 유일한 보상이니—\n\n그들이 없다면 어찌 법이나 예술이,\n혹은 탑 솟은 도시가 일어설 수 있으랴?\n\n어떤 작용을 관찰할 때면, 그것이 비록 전지(電池) 하나의 작용에 불과하더라도, 우리가 관심을 둔 운동이 시작된 지점에서 다소 떨어진 곳에 자리한 특정한 혼합물이나 무리를 살펴보기 위해 자리를 옮길 필요가 종종 있는 법이다. 내가 지금 옮겨 가려는 무리는 케일럽 가스의 아침 식탁, 곧 지도와 책상이 놓인 널찍한 거실에 있다. 아버지와 어머니, 그리고 다섯 아이들. 메리는 지금 일자리를 기다리며 집에 머물고 있었고, 그녀 바로 아래 사내아이인 크리스티는 스코틀랜드에서 값싼 학문과 값싼 끼니를 얻고 있었으니, 아버지를 실망시키게도 “사업”이라는 그 신성한 소명 대신 책에 빠져 버린 탓이었다.\n편지가 도착했다. 무려 아홉 통이나 되는 비싼 편지였고, 우편배달부에게 삼 실링 이 펜스를 치러야 했다. 가스 씨는 차와 토스트를 잊은 채 편지를 읽으며 한 통씩 위로 겹쳐 펼쳐 놓았는데, 때로는 천천히 고개를 갸웃하기도 하고, 때로는 속으로 따져 보느라 입을 오므리기도 했지만, 깨지지 않은 커다란 붉은 봉인을 잘라 내는 일만은 잊지 않았으니, 레티가 그것을 마치 열성적인 테리어처럼 낚아챘다.\n\n나머지 식구들 사이의 이야기는 거침없이 이어졌다. 케일럽이 글을 쓸 때 식탁을 흔드는 것 말고는 무엇도 그의 몰두를 방해하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n아홉 통 중 두 통은 메리에게 온 것이었다. 그것을 읽고 나서 그녀는 어머니에게 건넨 뒤, 멍하니 찻숟가락을 만지작거리며 앉아 있다가, 문득 생각이 나서 아침 식사 내내 무릎에 올려 두었던 바느질감으로 돌아갔다.\n“아, 메리, 바느질하지 마!” 벤이 그녀의 팔을 끌어내리며 말했다. “이 빵 부스러기로 공작새 좀 만들어 줘.” 그러려고 그는 작은 덩어리를 조몰락거리고 있던 참이었다.\n\n“안 돼, 안 돼, 이 장난꾸러기!” 메리가 명랑하게 말하며 바늘로 그의 손을 살짝 찔렀다. “네가 직접 빚어 봐. 내가 만드는 걸 충분히 봤잖니. 난 이 바느질을 끝내야 해. 로저먼드 빈시한테 줄 거야. 다음 주에 결혼하는데, 이 손수건이 없으면 결혼을 못 한대.” 메리는 마지막 말을 떠올리며 재미있어하면서 즐겁게 말을 맺었다.\n\n“왜 못 해, 메리?” 레티가 이 수수께끼에 진지하게 흥미를 느껴, 언니에게 바짝 머리를 들이밀자, 메리는 이제 위협적인 바늘을 레티의 코 쪽으로 돌렸다.\n“이게 열두 장 중 하나거든. 이게 없으면 열한 장밖에 안 되니까.” 메리가 자못 진지하게 설명하는 투로 말하자, 레티는 무언가 알게 되었다는 듯한 기분으로 물러앉았다.\n\n“마음을 정했니, 얘야?” 가스 부인이 편지를 내려놓으며 물었다.\n\n“요크에 있는 학교로 가겠어요.” 메리가 말했다. “저는 가정에서보다 학교에서 가르치는 편이 덜 부적합해요. 반을 맡아 가르치는 게 제일 좋고요. 그리고, 보세요, 저는 가르쳐야만 해요. 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없으니까요.”\n\n“내가 보기엔 가르치는 일이야말로 세상에서 가장 즐거운 일이란다.” 가스 부인이 약간 나무라는 어조로 말했다. “네게 지식이 부족하거나, 네가 아이들을 싫어한다면 네가 꺼리는 걸 이해할 수 있겠지만 말이야, 메리.”\n\n“우리는 남이 왜 우리가 좋아하는 걸 싫어하는지 결코 온전히 이해하지 못하는 것 같아요, 어머니.” 메리가 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다. “저는 교실이 좋지 않아요. 바깥세상이 더 좋아요. 그게 제 아주 곤란한 결점이죠.”\n\n“늘 여학교에만 틀어박혀 있으면 무척 답답할 거예요.” 앨프리드가 말했다. “꼭 밸러드 부인네 학생들이 둘씩 짝지어 걸어 다니는 것처럼, 그런 멍청이들이라니.”\n\n“게다가 걔들은 할 만한 놀이도 없어요.” 짐이 말했다. “던지지도, 뛰지도 못하니까요. 메리가 그걸 싫어하는 게 이상하지 않아요.”\n\n“메리가 싫어하는 게 뭐라고, 응?” 아버지가 안경 너머로 바라보며, 다음 편지를 열기 전에 잠시 멈추고 물었다.\n\n“멍청이 여자애들 사이에 끼어 있는 거요.” 앨프리드가 말했다.\n\n“네가 들었다던 그 일자리니, 메리?” 케일럽이 딸을 바라보며 부드럽게 물었다.\n\n“네, 아버지. 요크의 학교예요. 그걸 맡기로 정했어요. 가장 좋은 자리예요. 연봉이 삼십오 파운드인 데다, 가장 어린 피아노 초보들을 가르치면 수당도 따로 받아요.”\n\n“가엾은 것! 우리와 함께 집에 있을 수 있다면 좋으련만, 수전.” 케일럽이 아내를 애처롭게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“메리는 자기 의무를 다하지 않으면 행복하지 못할 거예요.” 자신의 의무를 다했다는 의식을 품고서 가스 부인이 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n“그런 끔찍한 의무를 다한다고 제가 행복해질 것 같진 않은데요.” 앨프리드가 말하자, 메리와 아버지는 소리 없이 웃었으나, 가스 부인은 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n“네가 못마땅하게 여기는 모든 것에 끔찍하다는 말보다 더 어울리는 단어를 좀 찾아보렴, 앨프리드. 그리고 메리가 버는 돈으로 너를 해머 씨네로 보내는 데 보태 줄 수도 있다고 생각해 보면 어떻겠니?”\n\n“그건 정말 부끄러운 일 같아요. 그래도 누난 정말 멋진 사람이에요.” 앨프리드가 의자에서 일어나 메리의 머리를 뒤로 젖혀 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n메리는 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었지만, 눈물이 차오르는 것을 감추지는 못했다. 안경 너머로 지켜보던 케일럽은 눈썹 끝이 처진 채, 편지를 마저 펼치며 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 표정을 지었다. 가스 부인조차도, 차분한 만족감에 입술을 살짝 말아 올린 채 그 부적절한 말을 바로잡지 않고 넘겼다. 다만 벤이 곧바로 그 말을 받아, “누난 멋진 사람, 멋진 사람, 멋진 사람!” 하고 경쾌한 박자에 맞춰 노래하며 주먹으로 메리의 팔을 두드려 박자를 맞추었다.\n\n그러나 이제 가스 부인의 시선은 남편에게로 향했다. 남편은 벌써 읽고 있던 편지에 깊이 빠져 있었다. 그의 얼굴에는 자못 놀란 표정이 떠올라 그녀를 조금 불안하게 했지만, 그는 읽는 도중에 질문받는 것을 좋아하지 않았으므로, 그녀는 초조하게 지켜보며 기다렸다. 그러다 그가 편지의 첫머리로 되돌아가며 작은 환희의 웃음에 문득 몸을 떨더니, 안경 너머로 그녀를 보며 낮은 목소리로 말하는 것을 보았다. “어떻게 생각하오, 수전?”\n\n그녀는 다가가 그의 뒤에 서서 어깨에 손을 얹었고, 두 사람은 함께 편지를 읽었다. 그것은 제임스 체텀 경이 가스 씨에게 프레시트와 그 밖의 곳에 있는 가문 사유지의 관리를 맡아 달라고 청하는 편지였고, 티프턴의 브룩 씨가 제임스 경에게 부탁해, 가스 씨가 동시에 티프턴 영지의 관리직도 다시 맡을 의향이 있는지 알아보려 한다는 말이 덧붙어 있었다. 준남작은 자신이 특히 프레시트와 티프턴 사유지가 같은 사람의 관리 아래 놓이기를 바란다고 매우 정중한 말로 적었고, 그 두 곳의 관리직이 가스 씨에게 흡족한 조건으로 유지될 수 있음을 보여 드리기를 바란다며, 이튿날 정오에 저택에서 그를 뵙기를 기쁘게 여기겠다고 했다.\n\n“근사하게 썼지 않소, 수전?” 케일럽이 아내를 올려다보며 말하자, 아내는 그의 머리에 턱을 괴며 어깨에 얹었던 손을 그의 귀로 올렸다. “브룩은 내게 직접 부탁하기가 싫었던 게로군, 알 만하지.” 그가 소리 없이 웃으며 말을 이었다.\n\n“얘들아, 이건 너희 아버지에게 큰 영광이란다.” 가스 부인이 부모에게 모두 고정된 다섯 쌍의 눈을 둘러보며 말했다. “오래전에 너희 아버지를 내보냈던 사람들이 다시 자리를 맡아 달라고 청하는 거야. 그건 아버지가 일을 잘 해내서, 그들이 아버지가 없는 걸 아쉬워한다는 뜻이지.”\n\n“킨킨나투스처럼요. 만세!” 벤이 규율이 느슨해졌다는 유쾌한 확신에 차서 의자를 말 타듯 흔들며 말했다.\n\n* 킨킨나투스: 고대 로마의 정치가로, 위기 때 부름을 받아 나라를 구하고 다시 농사일로 돌아간 인물.\n\n“그분들이 아버지를 모시러 와요, 어머니?” 레티가 예복을 차려입은 시장과 시 참사회를 떠올리며 물었다.\n가스 부인은 레티의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 미소 지었지만, 남편이 편지를 챙기며 곧 “사업”이라는 그 성소(聖所) 안으로 손이 닿지 않게 들어가 버릴 것 같자, 그의 어깨를 누르며 힘주어 말했다.\n\n“이봐요, 정당한 보수를 꼭 청하세요, 케일럽.”\n\n“오, 그럼.” 케일럽이, 자신에게 그 외의 다른 것을 기대하는 게 가당치 않다는 듯, 낮고 깊은 동의의 목소리로 말했다. “둘을 합치면 사백에서 오백 사이가 될 거요.” 그러더니 문득 무언가 떠오른 듯 흠칫하며 말했다. “메리, 편지를 써서 그 학교는 그만두겠다고 하렴. 집에 남아서 어머니를 도와다오. 그 생각을 하니, 나는 더없이 기쁘구나.”\n\n* 펀치: 영국 인형극의 주인공으로, 의기양양하게 떠벌리는 모습으로 유명하다.\n의기양양한 펀치의 모습과 케일럽만큼 동떨어진 것도 없었지만, 그의 재능은 표현을 찾아내는 데 있지 않았다. 비록 그는 편지 쓰는 일에 무척 까다로웠고, 아내를 올바른 언어의 보고(寶庫)로 여겼지만 말이다.\n\n이제 아이들 사이에서는 거의 한바탕 소동이 일었고, 메리는 사내아이들이 자기를 끌어다 춤을 추는 동안 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 치워 두려고 어머니에게 그 고운 무명 자수감을 애원하듯 내밀었다. 가스 부인은 잔잔한 기쁨에 잠겨 잔과 접시를 한데 모으기 시작했고, 케일럽은 책상으로 옮겨 가려는 듯 의자를 식탁에서 밀어냈으나, 여전히 손에 편지를 든 채 앉아서 생각에 잠겨 바닥을 바라보며, 자기만의 무언의 언어에 따라 왼손 손가락을 쭉 펴 보였다. 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n“크리스티가 사업에 뜻을 두지 않은 게 천추의 한이오, 수전. 머지않아 나는 일손이 필요할 거요. 그리고 앨프리드는 공학 쪽으로 보내야 해. 그건 이미 마음을 정했소.” 그는 잠시 다시 명상과 손가락 웅변에 빠져들었다가 말을 이었다. “브룩에게 소작인들과 새 계약을 맺게 할 거요. 그리고 윤작(輪作) 계획도 짤 거고. 보츠 모퉁이의 점토로 좋은 벽돌을 뽑아낼 수 있다는 데 내기를 걸지. 그건 내가 꼭 살펴봐야겠소. 수리비를 줄여 줄 테니까. 정말 훌륭한 일거리요, 수전! 식솔 없는 사람이라면 거저라도 기꺼이 할 일이지.”\n\n“그래도 당신은 그러지 마세요.” 아내가 손가락을 들어 보이며 말했다.\n\n“아니, 아니지. 하지만 사업의 본질을 꿰뚫어 본 사람에게 이런 일이 찾아온다는 건 멋진 일이오. 말하자면, 시골 한구석을 제대로 가꿀 기회를 얻고, 사람들이 농사를 옳은 방향으로 짓도록 이끌고, 알찬 설계와 견실한 건축을 해내서, 지금 사는 이들과 후세에 올 이들이 모두 더 나아지게 하는 일 말이오. 나는 그것을 한 재산보다 더 갖고 싶소. 나는 그것이 세상에서 가장 명예로운 일이라고 믿어요.” 여기서 케일럽은 편지를 내려놓고, 손가락을 조끼 단추 사이에 찔러 넣고 똑바로 앉았다가, 이내 목소리에 어떤 경외를 담아 천천히 고개를 옆으로 기울이며 말을 이었다. “이건 하느님의 크나큰 선물이오, 수전.”\n\n“정말 그래요, 케일럽.” 아내가 화답하는 열정으로 말했다. “그리고 이런 일을 해낸 아버지를 둔 것이 우리 아이들에게 축복이 될 거예요. 이름은 잊힐지언정 그 훌륭한 일은 남는, 그런 아버지를 둔 것 말이에요.” 그녀는 그때 그에게 보수에 관해서는 더 말할 수 없었다.\n\n저녁이 되어, 케일럽이 하루의 일로 다소 지친 채 무릎에 수첩을 펼쳐 놓고 말없이 앉아 있고, 가스 부인과 메리가 바느질을 하고, 레티가 한구석에서 인형과 속삭이는 대화를 나누고 있을 때, 페어브러더 씨가 무성한 풀숲과 사과나무 가지 사이로 팔월의 밝은 빛과 그늘을 가르며 과수원 길을 따라 올라왔다. 우리는 그가 자신의 교구민인 가스 가족을 좋아했고, 리드게이트에게 메리를 언급할 만하다고 여겼다는 것을 안다. 그는 미들마치의 신분 차별을 무시하는 성직자의 특권을 한껏 누렸으며, 자기 어머니에게 늘 가스 부인이 읍내의 어떤 부인보다도 더 숙녀답다고 말하곤 했다. 그렇지만, 보다시피, 그는 저녁이면 빈시네에서 시간을 보냈으니, 그 집 안주인은 숙녀다움은 덜할지언정 환하게 불 밝힌 응접실과 휘스트 놀이를 주관했다. 그 시절 사람들의 교제가 오로지 존경으로만 결정되던 것은 아니었다. 하지만 부목사는 진심으로 가스 가족을 존경했고, 그가 찾아온 것이 그 가족에게 놀랄 일은 아니었다. 그럼에도 그는 악수를 나누면서까지 그 방문의 까닭을 밝혔다. “저는 사절(使節)로 왔습니다, 가스 부인. 프레드 빈시를 대신해서 당신과 가스 씨에게 드릴 말씀이 있어서요. 실은, 가엾은 친구가,” 그가 자리에 앉아 자신의 말에 귀 기울이는 세 사람을 밝은 눈빛으로 둘러보며 말을 이었다. “저를 믿고 속내를 털어놓았답니다.”\n\n메리의 심장이 다소 빠르게 뛰었다. 그녀는 프레드의 고백이 어디까지 갔을까 궁금했다.\n\n“그 녀석을 몇 달째 못 봤소.” 케일럽이 말했다. “대체 어찌 된 영문인지 알 수가 없었지.”\n\n“다른 데 다녀왔습니다.” 부목사가 말했다. “집이 좀 견디기 힘들었던 데다, 리드게이트가 그 가엾은 친구는 아직 공부를 시작해서는 안 된다고 그 어머니에게 일렀거든요. 그런데 어제 그가 와서 제게 속을 다 쏟아 놓았습니다. 저는 그 일이 무척 다행스럽습니다. 그가 열네 살 풋내기일 때부터 자라는 걸 봐 왔고, 그 집에 워낙 편하게 드나들어서 아이들이 제겐 조카들이나 다름없으니까요. 하지만 조언하기 어려운 경우입니다. 어쨌든 그가 제게 부탁하기를, 와서 전해 달라더군요. 자기가 떠날 거라고, 그리고 당신에게 진 빚과 그걸 갚지 못하는 자신의 처지가 너무도 비참해서, 작별 인사를 하러 직접 찾아오는 것조차 견딜 수가 없다고요.”\n\n“그에게 한 푼어치도 문제될 게 없다고 전해 주시오.” 케일럽이 손을 내저으며 말했다. “우리도 쪼들렸지만 다 넘겼소. 게다가 이제 나는 유대인처럼 부자가 될 참이라오.”\n\n“그 말씀인즉,” 가스 부인이 부목사를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다. “아이들을 잘 키우고 메리를 집에 둘 만큼은 살림이 넉넉해진다는 거예요.”\n\n“그 횡재가 무엇입니까?” 페어브러더 씨가 물었다.\n\n“두 사유지의 관리인이 될 거요. 프레시트와 티프턴 말이오. 거기다 어쩌면 로윅에 있는 자그마하니 괜찮은 땅도요. 다 같은 집안 연줄이라, 일이란 한번 굴러가기 시작하면 물처럼 번지는 법이지요. 정말 기쁩니다, 페어브러더 씨.” 여기서 케일럽은 고개를 살짝 젖히고 의자 팔걸이에 두 팔을 벌려 놓았다. “땅을 임대하고, 개량에 대한 한두 가지 구상을 실행할 기회를 다시 얻게 되어서 말이오. 수전에게 자주 말했지만, 말 위에 앉아 울타리 너머로 잘못된 것을 보면서도 손을 대 바로잡지 못하는 건 여간 답답한 일이 아니거든요. 정치에 발을 들이는 사람들은 대체 무슨 마음인지 모르겠소. 나는 고작 몇백 에이커의 잘못된 관리만 봐도 거의 미칠 지경이니 말이오.”\n\n케일럽이 이토록 긴 말을 자청하는 일은 드물었지만, 그의 행복은 산속 공기 같은 효과를 냈다. 그의 눈은 빛났고, 말은 힘들이지 않고 흘러나왔다.\n\n“진심으로 축하드립니다, 가스.” 부목사가 말했다. “이건 제가 프레드 빈시에게 전할 수 있는 가장 좋은 소식이로군요. 그가 당신에게 끼친 피해를, 그러니까 다른 데 쓰려던 돈을 내놓게 한 일을, 그의 표현으로는 그 돈을 강탈한 일을 무척이나 마음에 두고 있었으니까요. 프레드가 저런 게으름뱅이만 아니라면 좋으련만. 그에게는 아주 좋은 점도 몇 가지 있는데, 그의 아버지는 그에게 좀 가혹하지요.”\n\n“그 애는 어디로 갑니까?” 가스 부인이 다소 차갑게 물었다.\n\n“다시 학위에 도전해 볼 생각이라, 학기 전에 공부하러 올라간답니다. 제가 그렇게 하라고 권했지요. 저는 그에게 성직에 들어서라고 다그치지는 않습니다. 오히려 그 반대지요. 하지만 그가 가서 시험에 통과할 만큼 노력한다면, 그건 그에게 기력과 의지가 있다는 어느 정도의 보증이 될 겁니다. 게다가 그는 완전히 막막한 처지라, 달리 무엇을 해야 할지 모르고 있어요. 그렇게라도 하면 그 아버지의 뜻에 어느 정도 맞을 테고, 저는 그동안 빈시 씨가 아들이 다른 길을 택하는 데 마음을 누그러뜨리도록 애써 보겠다고 약속했습니다. 프레드는 자기가 성직자에 맞지 않는다고 솔직히 말합니다. 저로서는 한 사람이 그릇된 직업을 택하는 치명적인 발걸음을 내딛는 걸 막기 위해서라면 무엇이든 하고 싶습니다. 그가 제게 당신이 했던 말을 인용하더군요, 가스 양. 기억하십니까?” (페어브러더 씨는 “가스 양” 대신 “메리”라고 부르곤 했지만, 빈시 부인의 표현대로 그녀가 빵벌이를 한다는 이유로 오히려 더 깍듯이 대하는 것이 그의 섬세함의 일면이었다.)\n\n메리는 불편했지만, 이 일을 가볍게 받아넘기기로 마음먹고 곧바로 대답했다. “저는 프레드에게 무례한 말을 어찌나 많이 했는지 몰라요. 우리는 워낙 오랜 소꿉동무라서요.”\n\n“그의 말로는, 당신이 그가 성직자 전체를 우스꽝스럽게 만드는 데 일조하는 그런 우스꽝스러운 성직자 중 하나가 될 거라고 했다더군요. 정말이지 그 말이 어찌나 날카로운지, 저까지도 좀 베인 기분이 들었습니다.”\n\n케일럽이 웃었다. “이 애는 말솜씨를 당신한테서 물려받았구려, 수전.” 그가 다소 흐뭇해하며 말했다.\n\n“그 입바른 가벼움은 아니에요, 아버지.” 메리가 어머니가 언짢아할까 봐 얼른 말했다. “제 경솔한 말을 페어브러더 씨께 옮기다니, 프레드도 참 너무했어요.”\n\n“확실히 성급한 말이었어, 얘야.” 가스 부인이 말했다. 그녀에게는 윗사람을 험담하는 것이 중대한 비행(非行)이었다. “옆 교구에 우스꽝스러운 부목사가 있다고 해서 우리 부목사님을 덜 귀하게 여길 일은 아니지 않니.”\n\n“그래도 그 애 말에 일리가 있소.” 케일럽이 메리의 날카로움이 깎아내려지는 것을 원치 않아 말했다. “어떤 일이든 솜씨 나쁜 일꾼 하나가 제 동료들을 의심받게 만드는 법이거든. 만사가 서로 얽혀 있으니까.” 그는 말이 생각만큼 넉넉지 못하다는 느낌에, 바닥을 내려다보며 발을 불편하게 움직이고는 덧붙였다.\n\n“분명히 그렇습니다.” 부목사가 재미있어하며 말했다. “경멸받을 만하게 굴어서 우리는 사람들의 마음을 경멸의 가락에 맞춰 놓는 셈이지요. 비록 제가 그로 인해 단죄될지라도, 저는 분명 가스 양의 견해에 동의합니다. 다만 프레드 빈시로 말하자면, 그를 조금은 너그러이 봐주는 것이 마땅합니다. 늙은 페더스톤의 사람을 속이는 행태가 그를 망쳐 놓는 데 한몫했으니까요. 결국 그에게 한 푼도 남기지 않은 것은 자못 악마 같은 짓이었지요. 하지만 프레드는 그 일을 곱씹지 않을 만큼은 분별이 있더군요. 그가 가장 마음 쓰는 건 당신의 노여움을 산 일입니다, 가스 부인. 당신이 다시는 자기를 좋게 보지 않으리라 여기더군요.”\n\n“저는 프레드에게 실망했어요.” 가스 부인이 단호하게 말했다. “하지만 그 애가 다시 좋게 볼 만한 분명한 이유를 보여 준다면, 저는 기꺼이 그렇게 할 거예요.”\n\n이 대목에서 메리는 레티를 데리고 방을 나갔다.\n\n“오, 젊은 사람들이 뉘우치면 용서해 줘야지요.” 케일럽이 메리가 문을 닫는 것을 지켜보며 말했다. “그리고 당신 말마따나, 페어브러더 씨, 그 늙은이한테는 정말 악마가 들어 있었소. 이제 메리가 나갔으니 한 가지 이야기를 해 드려야겠소. 이건 수전과 나만 아는 일이니, 다시는 입 밖에 내지 마시오. 그 늙은 악당이 죽던 바로 그날 밤, 메리가 혼자 곁을 지키고 있을 때, 유언장 하나를 태워 달라고 메리한테 청했답니다. 그러면 곁에 둔 상자 속 돈을 주겠다고 했지요. 하지만 메리는, 아시다시피, 그런 짓을 할 아이가 아니오. 그의 철궤에 손을 대거나 하지 않으려 했지요. 그런데, 보시오, 그가 태우려던 게 바로 그 마지막 유언장이었소. 그래서 메리가 그가 시키는 대로 했더라면 프레드 빈시가 만 파운드를 받았을 거란 말이오. 그 늙은이가 마지막엔 그 애한테 마음을 돌렸던 게지요. 그게 가엾은 메리의 마음을 깊이 찌르는 거요. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었고, 그 애가 한 일은 옳았지만, 그 애 말로는, 정당하게 자기를 지키다가 본의 아니게 남의 재산을 쓰러뜨려 부순 것 같은 기분이 든다는 거요. 나도 어쩐지 그 애와 같은 심정이라, 그 가엾은 녀석이 우리에게 끼친 해를 두고 원망하기보다, 만일 그 애한테 어떻게든 보상해 줄 수 있다면 기꺼이 그러고 싶소. 자, 당신 생각은 어떻소, 선생? 수전은 내 말에 동의하지 않는다오. 수전 말로는, 당신이 직접 말해 보구려, 수전.”\n\n“프레드에게 어떤 결과가 미칠지 알았더라도, 메리는 달리 행동할 수 없었을 거예요.” 가스 부인이 하던 일을 멈추고 페어브러더 씨를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“게다가 그 애는 그 일을 전혀 몰랐어요. 제 생각엔, 우리가 옳은 일을 했기에 남에게 손실이 돌아간 것이라면, 그것이 우리 양심에 걸려서는 안 되는 거예요.”\n\n부목사가 곧바로 대답하지 않자, 케일럽이 말했다. “그건 감정의 문제요. 그 애가 그런 식으로 느끼고, 나도 그 애와 같이 느끼니까. 길을 비키려고 말을 뒤로 물릴 때 개를 밟을 작정은 아니지만, 일단 그리 되고 나면 마음이 사무치는 법이지요.”\n\n“가스 부인께서도 그 점은 동의하시리라 확신합니다.” 무슨 까닭에선지 말하기보다 곱씹는 쪽으로 더 기우는 듯한 페어브러더 씨가 말했다. “프레드에 관해 당신이 말씀하신 그 감정이 그릇되었다고, 아니 차라리 잘못되었다고는 말하기 어렵겠지요. 비록 누구도 그런 감정에 기대어 권리를 주장해서는 안 되겠지만요.”\n\n“뭐, 그래요.” 케일럽이 말했다. “이건 비밀이오. 프레드에게는 말하지 마시오.”\n\n“물론입니다. 하지만 다른 좋은 소식은 전하겠습니다. 그가 끼친 손실을 당신이 감당할 수 있게 되었다는 것 말이지요.”\n\n페어브러더 씨는 곧이어 그 집을 나섰고, 과수원에서 레티와 함께 있는 메리를 보고는 작별 인사를 하러 다가갔다. 서쪽 햇살이 잎이 성긴 늙은 가지에 매달린 사과들의 윤기를 도드라지게 하는 가운데, 두 사람은 한 폭의 예쁜 그림을 이루었다. 라벤더빛 깅엄 옷에 검은 리본을 단 메리는 바구니를 들고 있었고, 닳고 닳은 무명옷을 입은 레티는 떨어진 사과를 줍고 있었다. 메리가 어떻게 생겼는지 더 자세히 알고 싶다면, 십중팔구 내일 붐비는 거리에서 그녀 같은 얼굴을 보게 될 것이다. 당신이 거기서 눈여겨본다면 말이다. 그녀는 거만하게 목을 빼고 음탕한 눈으로 아장아장 걸어 다니는, 시온의 그 오만한 딸들 축에는 들지 않을 것이다. 그런 이들은 다 지나치게 두고, 당당하되 차분한 몸가짐으로 주위를 둘러보면서도 아무도 자기를 보지 않으리라 여기는, 작고 통통하며 거무스름한 사람에게 눈길을 멈춰 보라. 그녀가 넓적한 얼굴에 네모진 이마, 또렷한 눈썹과 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 지녔고, 입은 비밀을 지키는데도 눈빛에는 어떤 유쾌함의 기색이 어리며, 그 밖의 이목구비는 도무지 대수롭지 않다면, 그 평범하되 밉지 않은 사람을 메리 가스의 초상이라 여겨도 좋다. 그녀를 웃게 만들면, 가지런한 작은 이를 보여 줄 것이다. 그녀를 화나게 만들면, 목소리를 높이지는 않되 십중팔구 당신이 일찍이 맛본 가장 쓰디쓴 말 한마디를 던질 것이다. 그녀에게 친절을 베풀면, 결코 잊지 않을 것이다. 메리는 솔질이 잘 된 낡은 옷을 걸친, 날카로운 얼굴의 잘생긴 그 자그마한 부목사를, 자신이 알게 된 그 어떤 남자보다도 더 존경했다. 그녀는 그가 어리석은 짓을 한다는 건 알았지만, 어리석은 말을 하는 것은 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 어쩌면 어리석은 말이, 페어브러더 씨의 그 어떤 분별없는 행동보다도 그녀에게 더 못마땅한 것이었는지도 모른다. 적어도, 주목할 만한 점은, 부목사의 성직자적 인품에 실제로 있는 결함들이, 프레드 빈시가 떠맡게 될 성직자적 인품의 예견된 결함들에 대해 그녀가 미리 드러냈던 것과 같은 경멸과 혐오를 결코 불러일으키지 않았다는 사실이다. 이런 판단의 편차는, 짐작건대, 메리 가스보다 더 원숙한 정신에서도 발견되는 법이다. 우리의 공정함이란 우리 가운데 누구도 본 적 없는 추상적 미덕과 결함을 위해서만 간직되는 것이니까. 이 판이하게 다른 두 남자 가운데 메리가 그 특유의 여성적 다정함을 품은 쪽이 누구일지, 누가 알아맞힐 수 있을까? 그녀가 가장 모질게 대하려 드는 쪽일까, 아니면 그 반대쪽일까?\n\n“옛 소꿉동무에게 전할 말이라도 있습니까, 가스 양?” 부목사가 그녀가 내미는 바구니에서 향긋한 사과 하나를 집어 주머니에 넣으며 말했다. “그 모진 판단을 좀 누그러뜨릴 말이라도? 저는 곧장 그를 만나러 가는 길입니다.”\n\n“없어요.” 메리가 고개를 저으며 미소 지었다. “그가 성직자로서 우스꽝스럽지 않을 거라고 말하려면, 우스꽝스러운 것보다 더 못한 무언가가 될 거라고 말해야 하니까요. 하지만 그가 일하러 떠난다니 정말 기뻐요.”\n\n“반면에, 저는 당신이 일하러 떠나지 않는다는 게 정말 기쁩니다. 우리 어머니께서도, 분명, 당신이 사제관으로 찾아와 주신다면 한결 더 행복해하실 거예요. 아시다시피 어머니는 젊은 사람들과 이야기 나누는 걸 좋아하시고, 옛날이야기도 들려주실 게 많거든요. 정말 친절을 베푸시는 셈이 될 겁니다.”\n\n“그래도 된다면, 정말 그러고 싶어요.” 메리가 말했다. “갑자기 모든 게 제겐 너무 행복해 보여요. 집을 그리워하는 게 늘 제 삶의 일부일 거라고 생각했는데, 그 불만이 사라지니 어쩐지 좀 허전해요. 그게 분별을 대신해서 제 마음을 채워 주었던 모양이지요?”\n\n“저도 같이 가도 돼요, 메리?” 레티가 속삭였다. 무엇이든 다 엿듣는, 여간 성가신 아이가 아니었다. 그러나 페어브러더 씨가 턱을 살짝 꼬집고 뺨에 입을 맞춰 주자 그녀는 의기양양해졌고, 그 일을 어머니와 아버지에게 떠들어 댔다.\n\n부목사가 로윅으로 걸어가는 동안, 그를 가까이서 지켜본 사람이라면 그가 두 번 어깨를 으쓱하는 것을 보았을지도 모른다. 내 생각에, 이런 몸짓을 하는 보기 드문 영국인들은 결코 둔중한 부류가 아니다. 혹시라도 굼뜬 반례가 있을까 저어되니, 거의 그렇지 않다고 해 두자. 그들은 대개 섬세한 기질을 지녔고, 사람들의 (자기 자신을 포함한) 사소한 과오에 너그러운 편이다. 부목사는 마음속으로 대화를 나누고 있었으니, 프레드와 메리 가스 사이에는 아마 옛 소꿉동무의 정 이상의 무언가가 있을 거라고 스스로에게 말하고는, 그 한 조각 여인다움이 저 풋내기 청년에게는 지나치게 과분한 것이 아니겠느냐고 자문하며 답했다. 이에 대한 응답이 바로 첫 번째 어깨 으쓱임이었다. 그러고는 자신이 질투를 느꼈을 법한 것이, 마치 결혼할 수 있는 남자라도 되는 양 굴었다며 스스로를 비웃었으니, 그가 덧붙이기를, 내가 그럴 처지가 못 된다는 건 어떤 대차대조표만큼이나 명백하지 않은가, 했다. 이에 두 번째 어깨 으쓱임이 뒤따랐다.\n\n이토록 서로 다른 두 남자가, 메리 스스로 일컫듯 이 “거무튀튀한 점박이”에게서 대체 무엇을 본 것일까? 그들을 끌어당긴 것이 그녀의 평범한 외모가 아닌 것만은 분명했다. (그러니 모든 평범한 처녀들이여, 미모가 없음을 믿어 보라며 사교계가 건네는 그 위험한 부추김을 경계할지어다.) 이 유서 깊은 우리 나라의 한 인간이란, 오랜 세월 서로 주고받은 영향들이 더디게 빚어낸, 참으로 경이로운 하나의 전체다. 그리고 매력이란 그러한 두 전체, 곧 사랑하는 자와 사랑받는 자가 어우러진 결과인 것이다.\n\n가스 부부가 단둘이 앉아 있을 때, 케일럽이 말했다. “수전, 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 맞혀 보오.”\n\n“윤작 계획이겠죠.” 가스 부인이 뜨개질감 너머로 그를 보며 미소 지었다. “아니면 티프턴 오두막들의 뒷문이거나요.”\n\n“아니오.” 케일럽이 진지하게 말했다. “나는 프레드 빈시를 위해 큰일을 해 줄 수 있겠다는 생각을 하고 있소. 크리스티는 떠났고, 앨프리드도 곧 떠날 테고, 짐이 사업을 맡을 만큼 자라려면 오 년은 걸릴 거요. 나는 일손이 필요할 테고, 프레드가 들어와서 일의 본질을 배우고 내 밑에서 일한다면, 만일 그가 목사 되기를 포기한다면, 그를 쓸모 있는 사람으로 만들어 줄 수도 있을 거요. 당신 생각은 어떻소?”\n\n“제 생각엔, 그 애 집안이 그보다 더 못마땅해할 정직한 일은 거의 없을 거예요.” 가스 부인이 단호하게 말했다.\n\n“그들이 못마땅해한들 내가 무슨 상관이오?” 케일럽이, 자신의 견해가 섰을 때면 곧잘 드러내는 그 완강함으로 말했다. “그 녀석은 성년이니 제 밥벌이를 해야 하오. 분별도 충분하고 머리 회전도 빠른 데다, 땅에서 일하는 걸 좋아하지요. 내가 보기엔 마음만 쏟으면 사업을 충분히 익힐 수 있을 거요.”\n\n“하지만 그 애가 그러려 들까요? 그 애 부모는 그가 번듯한 신사가 되기를 바랐고, 제 생각엔 그 애 자신도 같은 마음이에요. 그들은 다들 우리를 자기네보다 아래로 여기거든요. 게다가 그 제안이 당신한테서 나온다면, 빈시 부인은 분명 우리가 메리를 위해 프레드를 탐낸다고 말할 거예요.”\n\n“그런 따위 헛소리로 좌우될 거라면, 인생이란 참으로 하찮은 이야기겠구려.” 케일럽이 못마땅한 듯 말했다.\n\n“그렇지만 마땅히 지녀야 할 자존심이라는 것도 있는 법이에요, 케일럽.”\n\n“어리석은 자들의 생각 때문에 좋은 일을 못 하게 막는 거야말로 부당한 자존심이라 부르겠소. 어떤 종류의 일이든,” 케일럽이 손을 내밀어 위아래로 흔들며 강조하면서 열정적으로 말했다. “어리석은 자들의 말에 신경 쓴다면 결코 제대로 해낼 수 없는 법이오. 자기 계획이 옳다는 확신을 가슴속에 품어야 하고, 그 계획을 따라야만 하는 거지요.”\n\n“당신이 마음을 굳힌 계획에는 반대하지 않겠어요, 케일럽.” 가스 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 굳센 여인이었지만, 자기의 온순한 남편이 오히려 더 굳건한 몇몇 지점이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. “하지만 프레드가 대학으로 돌아가는 건 정해진 일 같으니, 그 후에 그가 무엇을 택할지 기다려 보는 게 낫지 않겠어요? 사람을 제 뜻에 거슬러 붙잡아 두기란 쉽지 않으니까요. 게다가 당신도 아직 자기 처지가, 또 무엇이 필요할지가 그리 확실치 않잖아요.”\n\n“그래, 좀 기다리는 게 나을지도 모르겠소. 하지만 두 사람 몫의 일거리를 넉넉히 구하는 거라면, 그건 꽤 자신 있소. 나는 늘 여기저기 흩어진 일들로 손이 가득 찼고, 언제나 새로운 일이 생기곤 하니까. 아니, 바로 어제만 해도, 저런, 당신한테 말 안 한 것 같은데! 두 사람이 서로 다른 쪽에서 같은 평가 일을 맡기겠다고 나를 찾아온 게 좀 묘한 일이었소. 그 둘이 누구였는지 알겠소?” 케일럽이 코담배 한 줌을 집어 마치 자기 설명의 일부라도 되는 양 손가락 사이에 들어 올리며 말했다. 그는 생각날 때면 코담배 한 줌을 즐겼지만, 대개 그 즐거움이 제 손안에 있다는 것을 잊곤 했다.\n\n아내는 뜨개질감을 내려놓고 귀를 기울였다.\n\n“글쎄, 그 리그, 아니 리그 페더스톤이 하나였소. 하지만 벌스트로드가 먼저였으니, 나는 벌스트로드 쪽 일을 해 줄 참이오. 그들이 노리는 게 저당인지 매입인지는 아직 모르겠소.”\n\n“그 사람, 방금 물려받은 땅을, 그 이름까지 따른 그 땅을 팔려는 걸까요?” 가스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“귀신이나 알겠지.” 케일럽이 말했다. 그는 떳떳지 못한 행실의 내막을 두고 ‘귀신’보다 더 높은 권능을 들먹이는 법이 없었다. “하지만 벌스트로드가 오래전부터 번듯한 땅을 손아귀에 넣고 싶어 했다는 건 내가 아오. 그리고 이 고장에서 그런 땅을 구하기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니지.”\n\n케일럽은 코담배를 들이마시는 대신 조심스레 흩뜨리고는 덧붙였다. “일이란 그 속내가 묘한 거요. 여기 다들 줄곧 프레드 몫이라 여겨 왔던 땅이 있는데, 정작 그 늙은이는 그에게 한 뼘도 남길 생각이 없었던 모양이오. 대신 남몰래 숨겨 두었던 곁가지 아들에게 물려주었으니, 그 아들이 거기 눌러앉아 자기가 살아생전 그러했을 만큼 모두를 약 올리길 바란 게지. 내 말하건대, 결국 그게 벌스트로드의 손에 들어간다면 묘한 일이 될 거요. 그 늙은이는 벌스트로드를 미워해서, 결코 그와 거래하려 들지 않았거든.”\n\n“그 가련한 작자가 아무 상관도 없는 사람을 미워할 무슨 까닭이 있었을까요?” 가스 부인이 말했다.\n\n“쳇! 그런 작자들의 까닭을 물어 무엇 하겠소? 인간의 영혼이란,” 케일럽이 이 표현을 쓸 때면 늘 나오는 그 깊은 어조와 진중하게 고개를 젓는 몸짓으로 말했다. “인간의 영혼이란, 일단 제대로 썩고 나면, 온갖 독버섯을 자네에게 피워 올리는 법이지. 그 씨앗이 어디서 왔는지는 어떤 눈으로도 볼 수 없거든.”\n\n케일럽의 별난 구석 가운데 하나는, 자기 생각을 말로 옮기기가 어려울 때면, 말하자면 갖가지 관점이나 마음 상태와 결부 지어 둔 표현의 토막들을 끌어다 쓴다는 것이었다. 그리고 경외감이 일 때면 언제나 성경 어투의 기억에 사로잡히곤 했는데, 정작 정확한 인용을 댈 수 있었던 적은 거의 없었다.\n\n# 제38장\n\n비는 날마다 내리는 것을.\n— 『십이야』.\n\n케일럽 가스가 스톤 코트에 딸린 토지와 관련해 불스트로드 씨와 조슈아 리그 페더스톤 씨 사이에 오갔다고 언급한 거래는, 이 두 사람 사이에 한두 통의 편지가 오가는 계기가 되었다.\n* 십이야: 셰익스피어의 희극.\n\n글로 쓴 것의 영향이 어떠할지 누가 말할 수 있겠는가? 그것이 돌에 새겨졌다면, 비록 버림받은 해변에서 몇 세대에 걸쳐 글자가 새겨진 면을 아래로 향한 채 누워 있거나, \"수많은 정복의 북소리와 짓밟힘 아래 고요히 쉰다\" 하더라도, 끝내는 오래전 제국들 시절에 입에 오르내리던 찬탈과 그 밖의 추문의 비밀을 우리에게 알려 줄지도 모른다. 이 세상은 분명 거대한 속삭임의 회랑인 듯하다. 그런 사정은 우리의 자질구레한 일생에서도 종종 세밀하게 재현된다. 여러 세대의 촌뜨기들에게 차이던 돌멩이가 기묘하게 이어지는 작은 인과의 고리들을 거쳐 한 학자의 눈앞에 놓이고, 그의 노고를 통해 마침내 침략의 연대를 확정하고 종교의 비밀을 열어 줄 수 있듯이, 오랫동안 무해한 포장지나 빈틈을 메우는 종잇조각에 불과했던 잉크와 종이 한 조각이 마침내, 그것을 재앙의 서막으로 바꿔 놓을 만한 지식을 갖춘 단 한 쌍의 눈 아래 펼쳐질 수 있다. 태양에서 행성의 역사가 흘러가는 것을 지켜보는 우리엘에게는, 그 어느 결과든 똑같이 우연일 뿐일 것이다.\n* 우리엘: 밀턴의 『실낙원』에서 태양을 다스리는 천사.\n\n이렇게 다소 거창한 비유를 늘어놓고 나니, 우리가 아무리 그것을 달가워하지 않더라도 그들의 간섭으로 세상의 흐름이 크게 좌우되는 천한 사람들의 존재로 관심을 돌리기가 한결 덜 거북하다. 물론 우리가 그들의 수를 줄이는 데 일조할 수 있다면 좋을 것이고, 어쩌면 그들의 존재에 빌미를 가벼이 주지 않음으로써 무언가 이룰 수도 있을 것이다. 사회적으로 말하자면, 조슈아 리그는 대체로 군더더기로 평가받았을 것이다. 그러나 피터 페더스톤처럼 자기 자신의 복제본을 만들어 달라는 요청을 한 번도 받아 본 적 없는 이들이야말로, 산문으로든 운문으로든 그런 요청을 가장 기다리지 않을 자들이다. 이 경우의 복제본은 외양으로는 어머니를 더 닮았는데, 여성의 경우 개구리 같은 이목구비가 싱그러운 혈색의 뺨과 둥글둥글한 몸매를 동반하면 어떤 부류의 숭배자들에게는 적잖은 매력으로 통할 수 있다. 그 결과는 때로 개구리 얼굴을 한 사내로 나타나는데, 이는 분명 어떤 지각 있는 존재에게도 탐탁할 리 없다. 더구나 그가 갑작스레 나타나 다른 사람들의 기대를 좌절시킬 때는 — 사회적 군더더기가 자신을 드러낼 수 있는 가장 천한 모습이다.\n\n그러나 리그 페더스톤 씨의 천한 특성들은 모두 술을 멀리하고 절제하는 종류의 것이었다. 하루 중 가장 이른 시각부터 가장 늦은 시각까지 그는 자신이 닮은 개구리처럼 늘 매끄럽고 단정하고 냉정했으며, 늙은 피터는 자기보다 거의 더 타산적이고 훨씬 더 흔들림 없는 자손을 두었다며 은근히 흐뭇해했다. 덧붙이자면 그는 손톱을 빈틈없이 손질했고, 인물이 반반하고 견실한 중산층의 방식으로 연줄이 흠잡을 데 없는, 교양 있는 젊은 숙녀(아직 누구인지 정해지지 않은)와 결혼할 작정이었다. 따라서 그의 손톱과 단정함은 여느 신사들의 그것에 견줄 만했다. 비록 그의 야망은 항구 도시의 작은 상점들에서 서기 겸 회계원으로 일하며 얻은 기회에 의해서만 길러진 것이었지만 말이다. 그는 시골의 페더스톤 일가가 매우 단순하고 우스꽝스러운 사람들이라 여겼고, 그들은 그들대로 그가 항구 도시에서 받은 \"양육\"을, 오라비 피터가, 더구나 피터의 재산이 그런 친족을 두었다는 괴이함을 더욱 과장한 것이라 여겼다.\n\n스톤 코트의 판벽을 두른 응접실 두 창문에서 내다보이는 정원과 자갈길은, 리그 페더스톤 씨가 뒷짐을 진 채 이 땅의 주인으로서 그 부지를 내다보며 서 있는 지금보다 더 잘 손질된 적이 없었다. 하지만 그가 사색을 위해 밖을 내다보는 것인지, 아니면 방 한가운데 두 다리를 상당히 벌리고 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 서 있는 사람에게 등을 돌리려고 그러는 것인지는 의심스러워 보였다. 그 사람은 모든 면에서 매끄럽고 냉정한 리그와 대조를 이루었다. 그는 분명 예순을 향해 가는 남자로, 얼굴이 몹시 불그레하고 털이 많았으며, 덥수룩한 구레나룻과 두툼하고 곱슬곱슬한 머리칼에 흰 빛이 많이 섞여 있었고, 약간 비대한 몸집은 다소 닳은 옷의 이음새를 불리하게 드러냈으며, 불꽃놀이 자리에서조차 눈에 띄려 하고 남이 하는 어떤 일에 대한 자신의 논평이 그 일 자체보다 더 흥미로우리라 여기는 허풍쟁이의 분위기를 풍겼다.\n\n그의 이름은 존 래플스였고, 때로 서명 뒤에 익살스럽게 W.A.G.라고 적었는데, 그럴 때면 자기가 한때 이름 뒤에 B.A.라고 적던 핀스베리의 레너드 램에게 배웠으며, 그 유명한 교장을 바 램이라 부르는 익살을 자기가, 곧 래플스가 처음 만들어 냈노라고 말하곤 했다. 래플스 씨의 외양과 정신적 풍취는 이러했으며, 그 둘 다 그 시절 상업 호텔의 여행객 휴게실 같은 케케묵은 냄새를 풍기는 듯했다.\n* W.A.G.: '익살꾼(wag)'을 뜻하는 말장난. B.A.는 학사 학위. 'Ba-Lamb'은 '아기 양(baa-lamb)'을 빗댄 익살.\n\n\"이봐, 조시,\" 그가 우렁우렁한 목소리로 말하고 있었다. \"이렇게 한번 생각해 봐. 자네 가엾은 어머니가 노년의 골짜기로 접어들고 있는데, 자네는 이제 어머니를 편히 모실 만큼 넉넉히 베풀 수 있잖아.\"\n\n\"당신이 살아 있는 한은 안 됩니다. 당신이 살아 있는 한 어머니는 결코 편할 수 없을 테니까요.\" 리그가 냉정하고 높은 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"내가 어머니께 드리는 걸 당신이 가로챌 테죠.\"\n\n\"자네가 날 원망하는 거 알아, 조시. 하지만 이봐, 이제 — 남자 대 남자로서 — 허튼소리 빼고 — 약간의 밑천만 있으면 그 가게로 제대로 한몫 잡을 수 있단 말이야. 담배 장사가 커지고 있어. 거기에 최선을 다하지 않으면 내 코를 베어 내는 격이지. 나를 위해서라도 양털에 붙은 벼룩처럼 거기 딱 붙어 있을 거야. 늘 그 자리를 지킬 거고. 그러면 자네 가엾은 어머니가 그보다 더 기뻐할 일이 없을 거야. 나도 젊은 날 객기는 거의 다 부렸어 — 쉰다섯을 넘겼으니까. 이제 난롯가에 자리 잡고 싶다고. 일단 담배 장사에 매달리기만 하면, 어디서도 쉽게 찾을 수 없는 두뇌와 경험을 거기에 쏟아부을 수 있어. 자네를 한 번 또 한 번 귀찮게 하고 싶진 않아, 단번에 일을 제대로 된 궤도에 올려놓고 싶은 거지. 그걸 생각해 봐, 조시 — 남자 대 남자로서 — 게다가 자네 가엾은 어머니가 평생 편히 지내게 되는 일이잖아. 나야 늘 그 노인네를 아꼈지, 정말이야!\"\n\n\"다 했소?\" 리그 씨가 창에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 나야 다 했지.\" 래플스가 자기 앞 탁자 위에 놓인 모자를 집어 들며, 연설하듯 한번 쓱 밀어 올리며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 내 말을 들으시오. 당신이 어떤 말을 하면 할수록, 난 그걸 더 안 믿을 거요. 당신이 내게 어떤 일을 시키고 싶어 하면 할수록, 난 그 일을 절대 하지 않을 이유가 더 늘어날 거고. 내가 어릴 때 당신이 날 발로 차고, 나하고 어머니 입에서 제일 좋은 음식을 죄다 빼앗아 먹은 걸 내가 잊을 성싶소? 당신이 늘 집에 와서는 죄다 팔아 챙겨 주머니에 넣고는, 우릴 곤경에 내버려 둔 채 다시 떠나 버린 걸 내가 잊을 성싶소? 난 당신이 짐수레 꽁무니에 묶여 채찍질당하는 꼴을 보면 좋겠소. 내 어머니는 당신한테 바보처럼 굴었지. 어머닌 내게 새아버지를 안겨 줄 권리가 없었고, 그 벌을 받아 온 거요. 어머닌 매주 받는 생활비를 받을 거고, 그 이상은 없소. 그리고 당신이 다시 이 집에 발을 들이거나, 다시 이 고장으로 날 쫓아온다면 그것마저 끊길 거요. 다음번에 당신이 이 문 안에 모습을 드러내면, 개들과 마부의 채찍에 쫓겨날 줄 아시오.\"\n\n리그는 마지막 말을 내뱉으며 돌아서서 툭 불거진 얼음장 같은 눈으로 래플스를 바라보았다. 그 대조는 십팔 년 전, 리그가 발로 차이기 딱 좋은 더없이 밉살스러운 소년이었고 래플스가 술집과 뒷방을 누비던 다소 다부진 미남이었을 때와 다름없이 뚜렷했다. 하지만 이제 우위는 리그 쪽에 있었으니, 이 대화를 듣는 이들이라면 아마 래플스가 패배한 개꼴로 물러나리라 예상했을 것이다. 천만에. 그는 노름판에서 \"졌을\" 때면 으레 짓던 찡그린 표정을 짓더니, 이내 웃음으로 누그러지며 주머니에서 브랜디 병을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"이봐, 조시,\" 그가 구슬리는 어조로 말했다. \"브랜디 한 모금하고 돌아갈 노자로 1소버린만 주면 갈게. 명예를 걸고! 총알처럼 사라질게, 정말이야!\"\n\n\"명심하시오,\" 리그가 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내며 말했다. \"또다시 당신을 보게 되더라도 난 당신한테 말을 걸지 않을 거요. 난 까마귀를 본 것이나 다름없이 당신을 내 사람으로 여기지 않으니까. 그리고 당신이 날 자기 사람이라 주장하려 든다면, 얻을 거라곤 당신이 본디 어떤 자인지에 대한 평판뿐일 거요 — 심술궂고 뻔뻔하고 으름장이나 놓는 악당이라는 평판 말이오.\"\n\n\"그것참 안타까운 일이군, 조시.\" 래플스가 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 듯 머리를 긁적이고 눈썹을 위로 찌푸리는 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"난 자네가 정말 좋아. 맹세코, 정말이야! 자네를 골탕 먹이는 것보다 더 좋은 게 없는데 말이야 — 자네가 어머니를 어찌나 닮았는지 — 이제 그것 없이 지내야 하다니. 하지만 브랜디하고 소버린은 약속한 거다.\"\n\n그가 병을 앞으로 쑥 내밀자 리그는 열쇠를 들고 멋들어진 낡은 떡갈나무 책상으로 갔다. 그런데 래플스는 병을 다루다가, 그 병이 가죽 덮개에서 위험할 만큼 헐거워졌다는 걸 떠올렸고, 난로 가리개 안으로 떨어져 있던 접힌 종이 한 장을 발견하고는 그것을 집어 가죽 밑으로 밀어 넣어 유리병을 단단히 고정시켰다.\n\n그때쯤 리그가 브랜디 병을 들고 와서 병을 채워 주고 래플스에게 1소버린을 건넸는데, 그를 쳐다보지도 말을 걸지도 않았다. 다시 책상을 잠근 뒤 그는 창가로 걸어가 면담 처음과 똑같이 무심하게 밖을 응시했고, 그동안 래플스는 병에서 조금 따라 마시고는 마개를 돌려 닫아 옆 주머니에 챙겨 넣으며, 약을 올리듯 느릿느릿 의붓아들의 등을 향해 얼굴을 찡그렸다.\n\n\"잘 있게, 조시 — 영영 헤어지는 거라 해도!\" 래플스가 문을 열며 고개를 돌려 말했다.\n\n리그는 그가 부지를 떠나 오솔길로 들어서는 것을 지켜보았다. 잿빛이던 하루는 가볍게 부슬부슬 내리는 비로 바뀌어 산울타리와 샛길의 풀 돋은 가장자리를 싱그럽게 적시고, 마지막 곡식 단을 거두어 싣던 일꾼들의 손길을 재촉했다. 도시의 한량이 시골길을 걸어가야 할 때 보일 법한 불편한 걸음걸이로 걷는 래플스는, 이 촉촉한 시골의 고요와 부지런함 한가운데서 마치 동물원에서 도망친 비비처럼 어울리지 않아 보였다. 하지만 그를 빤히 쳐다보는 것이라곤 젖 뗀 지 오래된 송아지들뿐이었고, 그의 모습을 싫어하는 기색을 보이는 것이라곤 그가 다가오자 바스락거리며 달아나는 작은 물쥐들뿐이었다.\n\n그는 큰길에 들어섰을 때 운 좋게도 역마차를 만나 그것을 타고 브래싱까지 갔다. 거기서 그는 새로 놓인 철도를 탔는데, 동승객들에게 이제 이 철도가 허스키슨까지 해치웠으니 꽤 길이 들었다 싶다고 말했다. 래플스 씨는 대개의 경우 자신이 학교에서 교육받았으며, 마음만 먹으면 어디서든 무난히 처신할 수 있다는 느낌을 유지했다. 실제로 그는 동료 인간들 가운데 자기가 조롱하고 괴롭힐 처지가 못 된다고 여기는 자가 하나도 없었으며, 그렇게 함으로써 나머지 일행 모두에게 즐거움을 준다고 자신했다.\n* 허스키슨: 윌리엄 허스키슨. 1830년 리버풀-맨체스터 철도 개통식에서 기차에 치여 숨진 정치가. 철도 사고 사망자의 대명사처럼 쓰인다.\n\n그는 지금도 마치 자기 여행이 완전히 성공한 양 그 역할을 한껏 신명 나게 해냈으며, 틈틈이 병에 손을 댔다. 그가 병을 고정시키는 데 끼워 둔 종이는 니컬러스 불스트로드라는 서명이 적힌 편지였지만, 래플스가 그것을 지금의 유용한 자리에서 끄집어낼 일은 없을 듯했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLII.\n\n생각해 보건대, 자비심에 매여 있지 않다면 내가 이 사람을 얼마나 경멸할 수 있으랴!\n— 셰익스피어, 『헨리 8세』*\n\n* 셰익스피어의 사극 『헨리 8세』 2막 1장에 나오는 대사.\n리드게이트가 신혼여행에서 돌아온 직후 직업상 방문한 곳 가운데 하나는 로윅 영주관이었는데, 이는 방문 시간을 정해 달라고 청하는 편지를 받았기 때문이었다.\n\n캐소본 씨는 리드게이트에게 자기 병의 성질에 관해 어떤 질문도 한 적이 없었고, 도러시아에게조차 그 병이 자신의 노작이나 생명을 얼마나 단축시킬지에 대한 어떤 불안도 내비친 적이 없었다. 이 점에서도, 다른 모든 점에서와 마찬가지로, 그는 동정을 받는 것을 꺼렸다. 자기 처지의 무언가가 본의 아니게 짐작되거나 알려져 동정의 대상이 되리라는 의심이 쓰라린 것이라면, 두려움이나 슬픔을 솔직히 인정함으로써 연민의 표시를 불러일으킨다는 생각은 그에게 도저히 견딜 수 없는 것일 수밖에 없었다. 자존심 강한 정신이라면 누구나 이런 경험을 어느 정도 알고 있으니, 어쩌면 그것은 고립을 위한 온갖 노력을 고결한 것이 아니라 비열하고 하찮은 것으로 보이게 할 만큼 깊은 동류의식에 의해서만 극복될 수 있는 것인지도 모른다.\n\n그러나 캐소본 씨는 이제 무언가를 곱씹고 있었으니, 그것을 통해 그의 건강과 생명의 문제가 그의 침묵을 괴롭혔던바, 그의 저술이 가을이 되어도 익지 못한 채라는 사실을 통한 것보다 한층 더 성가신 집요함으로 그를 따라다녔다. 사실 이 마지막 것은 그의 핵심적인 야심이라 불릴 만했다. 그러나 어떤 종류의 저술에서는 가장 큰 결과가 저자의 의식 속에 쌓인 불안정한 예민함뿐인 경우가 있으니 — 누군가는 오래 가라앉은 불쾌한 진흙 퇴적층 사이로 드러난 몇 줄기 흔적으로 강이 있음을 알아챈다. 캐소본 씨의 고된 지적 노작이 바로 그러했다. 그것의 가장 특징적인 결과는 『모든 신화의 열쇠』가 아니라, 다른 이들이 자기에게 합당한 자리를 — 입증할 수 없게도 받을 자격이 없는 자리를 — 주지 않는다는 병적인 의식이었고, 자기를 향한 견해가 자기에게 유리하지 않으리라는 끊임없는 의심에 찬 억측이었으며, 업적을 이루려는 노력에 열정이 우울하게 결여되어 있다는 사실과, 아무것도 이루지 못했다는 고백에 대한 열정적인 저항이었다.\n\n이리하여 다른 이들에게는 그를 송두리째 흡수해 메마르게 한 것처럼 보였던 그의 지적 야심은, 실은 상처를 막아 줄 아무런 방패가 되지 못했으니, 무엇보다도 도러시아에게서 오는 상처에 대해서는 더욱 그러했다. 그리고 그는 이제 미래에 대한 여러 가능성을 그려 보기 시작했는데, 그것은 어쩐지 그의 정신이 이전에 머물렀던 그 무엇보다도 그를 더욱 쓰라리게 했다.\n\n어떤 사실들 앞에서 그는 무력했다. 윌 래디슬로의 존재, 로윅 근방에 도전적으로 머무르는 그의 태도, 진정하고 확실하게 검증된 박식을 지닌 이들에 대한 그의 경박한 마음가짐 앞에서 그러했다. 끊임없이 열렬한 활동의 새로운 형태를 띠는, 그리고 순종과 침묵 속에서조차 생각만 해도 짜증스러운 열정적인 이유를 감추고 있는 도러시아의 천성 앞에서도 그러했다. 그가 그녀와 도저히 논할 수 없는 주제들과 관련하여 그녀의 마음을 사로잡은 어떤 관념과 호감 앞에서도 그러했다. 도러시아가 그가 아내로 얻을 수 있었던 만큼 정숙하고 사랑스러운 젊은 숙녀라는 것은 부인할 수 없었다. 그러나 한 젊은 숙녀란 그가 생각했던 것보다 더 성가신 존재로 드러났다. 그녀는 그를 간호했고, 그에게 책을 읽어 주었으며, 그의 필요를 미리 헤아렸고, 그의 감정에 마음을 썼다. 그러나 남편의 마음속에는 그녀가 자기를 판단하고 있다는 확신과, 그녀의 아내다운 헌신이 마치 믿음 없는 생각에 대한 참회의 속죄와도 같다는 확신 — 자기 자신과 자기가 하는 일이 전체 사물의 일부로서 너무나 환히 드러나 보이는 비교의 능력을 동반하고 있다는 확신이 들어와 있었다. 그의 불만은 그녀의 온갖 다정하고 사랑스러운 표현을 안개처럼 뚫고 지나가, 그녀가 그에게 더 가까이 끌어다 놓았을 뿐인 그 몰이해한 세계에 들러붙었다.\n\n가엾은 캐소본 씨! 이 고통은 배신처럼 느껴졌기에 견디기가 더욱 힘들었다. 완전한 신뢰로 그를 숭배하던 어린 존재가 어느새 비판적인 아내로 변해 버렸고, 비판과 분개의 초기 사례들이 새겨 놓은 인상은 그 후의 어떤 다정함과 순종으로도 지울 수 없었다. 그의 의심에 찬 해석으로는, 이제 도러시아의 침묵은 억눌린 반항이었고, 그가 전혀 예상치 못한 그녀의 말 한마디는 의식적인 우월감의 표명이었으며, 그녀의 부드러운 대답에는 짜증스러운 신중함이 깃들어 있었고, 그녀가 묵묵히 따를 때면 그것은 스스로 흡족해하는 인내의 노력이었다. 이 내면의 드라마를 감추려는 그의 끈질긴 애씀이 오히려 그것을 그에게 더욱 생생하게 만들었으니, 우리가 남들이 듣지 않기를 바라는 것일수록 더 예민하게 들리는 법이다.\n\n캐소본 씨에게서 비참함이 빚어낸 이런 결과를 의아하게 여기기보다, 나는 그것이 지극히 평범한 일이라고 생각한다. 우리 눈에 아주 가까이 붙은 작은 티끌 하나가 세계의 영광을 가려 버리고, 그 티끌을 보게 하는 가장자리만 남기지 않던가? 나는 자아만큼 성가신 티끌을 알지 못한다. 그리고 만약 캐소본 씨가 자기 불만을 — 자기가 더 이상 비판 없이 흠모받지 못한다는 의심을 — 펼쳐 보이기로 했다면, 그 의심이 그럴 만한 이유에 근거한 것임을 누가 부정할 수 있었겠는가? 오히려 그가 스스로 명확히 고려하지 못한 강력한 이유 하나를 덧붙일 수 있었으니 — 바로 그가 순전하게 흠모할 만한 사람은 아니었다는 점이다. 그러나 그는 다른 일들을 의심하듯 이 점도 고백하지 않은 채 의심하면서, 우리 나머지 사람들과 마찬가지로, 그것을 결코 알아채지 못할 동반자가 있었다면 얼마나 위안이 되었을까 하고 느꼈다.\n\n도러시아와 관련된 이 쓰라린 예민함은 윌 래디슬로가 로윅으로 돌아오기 전에 이미 충분히 준비되어 있었으니, 그 이후에 일어난 일들이 캐소본 씨의 의심에 찬 해석의 능력을 격앙된 활동으로 몰아넣었다. 그는 자기가 아는 모든 사실에다 현재와 미래의 상상의 사실들을 보태었는데, 이것들이 더 강한 혐오와 더 압도적인 쓰라림을 불러일으켰기에 실제 사실들보다 그에게는 더 현실적인 것이 되었다. 윌 래디슬로의 의도에 대한 의심과 질투, 도러시아의 인상에 대한 의심과 질투가 끊임없이 베틀을 짜고 있었다. 그가 도러시아에 대해 어떤 천박한 오해에 빠질 수 있었으리라고 가정하는 것은 그에게 매우 부당한 일이리라. 그녀의 천성이 지닌 공공연한 고결함 못지않게 그 자신의 정신과 행동의 습관이 그를 그런 잘못으로부터 지켜 주었다. 그가 질투한 것은 그녀의 견해였고, 판단에 있어 그녀의 열렬한 정신에 주어질지도 모를 영향력이었으며, 그것이 그녀를 이끌어 갈 미래의 가능성들이었다. 윌에 대해서는, 그의 마지막 도전적인 편지가 오기 전까지는 정식으로 그에게 들이댈 만한 명확한 것이 아무것도 없었지만, 그는 윌이 반항적인 기질과 절제되지 않은 충동성을 매혹할 수 있는 어떤 계략이든 꾸밀 수 있는 자라고 믿을 만한 근거가 자기에게 있다고 느꼈다. 그는 도러시아가 윌이 로마에서 돌아온 원인이며 근방에 정착하기로 한 결심의 원인이라고 확신했다. 그리고 그는 도러시아가 순진하게 이 행보를 부추겼으리라고 상상할 만큼 통찰력이 있었다. 그녀가 윌에게 마음을 주고 그의 제안에 유순하게 따를 준비가 되어 있다는 것은 더할 나위 없이 분명해 보였다. 두 사람이 단둘이 마주한 자리에서 그녀가 새로운 성가신 인상을 가지고 돌아오지 않은 적이 없었고, 캐소본 씨가 아는 마지막 만남은(도러시아는 프레시트 홀에서 돌아왔을 때 처음으로 윌을 만났다는 사실을 침묵했다) 그가 일찍이 알았던 것보다 더 분노에 찬 감정을 두 사람 모두에 대해 불러일으킨 장면으로 이어졌다. 한밤의 어둠 속에서 돈에 관한 자기 생각을 쏟아낸 도러시아의 말은 남편의 마음에 더욱 가증스러운 예감의 혼합물을 가져다주었을 뿐이었다.\n\n게다가 최근 건강에 받은 충격이 늘 슬프게도 그와 함께했다. 그는 분명 많이 회복되었다. 평소의 작업 능력을 모두 되찾았으니, 그 병은 단순한 피로였을지도 모르고, 그 앞에는 아직 이십 년의 업적이 남아 있어 삼십 년의 준비를 정당화해 줄지도 몰랐다. 그 전망은 카프 일당의 성급한 비웃음에 대한 복수의 맛으로 더욱 달콤해졌으니, 캐소본 씨가 과거의 무덤들 사이로 촛불을 들고 다닐 때조차도 그 현대의 인물들이 희미한 불빛을 가로질러 다가와 그의 부지런한 탐구를 방해했기 때문이다. 카프에게 그의 잘못을 깨닫게 하여 그가 적잖은 소화불량을 겪으며 자기 말을 도로 삼키게 만드는 것이야말로 의기양양한 저술이 가져다줄 유쾌한 부수입이 되리라. 지상에서 후대까지, 그리고 천국에서 영원토록 살리라는 전망조차도 그 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수는 없었다. 이렇듯 자기 자신의 끝없는 지복에 대한 예견조차 격앙된 질투와 복수심의 쓴맛을 없애지 못했으니, 그 자신이 영광에 들어선 뒤에 다른 사람들이 잠시 누릴 지상의 행복의 가능성이 강력하게 달콤한 효과를 발휘하지 못했다는 것은 그리 놀라운 일도 아니다. 만약 어떤 좀먹는 질병이 그의 몸속에서 작용하고 있다는 것이 사실이라면, 그가 떠난 뒤에 어떤 이들이 더 행복해질 큰 기회가 있을지도 몰랐다. 그리고 만약 그 이들 가운데 하나가 윌 래디슬로라면, 캐소본 씨가 어찌나 강하게 반대했던지 그 짜증이 그의 육신을 벗어난 존재의 일부가 되기라도 할 것 같았다.\n\n이것은 사정을 매우 앙상하게, 따라서 매우 불완전하게 진술하는 방식이다. 인간의 영혼은 수많은 물길로 움직이는바, 우리가 알다시피 캐소본 씨에게는 올바름에 대한 감각과 명예의 요구를 충족시키는 데서 오는 떳떳한 자존심이 있었고, 이것이 그로 하여금 자기 행동에 대해 질투와 복수심 이외의 다른 이유를 찾도록 강요했다. 캐소본 씨가 사정을 진술한 방식은 이러했다. \"나는 도러시아 브룩과 결혼하면서 내가 죽을 경우에 대비해 그녀의 안녕을 돌봐야 했다. 그러나 안녕이란 넉넉하고 독립적인 재산의 소유로 보장되는 것이 아니다. 오히려 그런 소유가 그녀를 더 큰 위험에 노출시킬 경우가 생길지도 모른다. 그녀는 그녀의 다정한 열정이나 돈키호테 같은 열의를 교묘하게 다룰 줄 아는 어떤 남자에게든 쉽사리 먹이가 되고 만다. 그런데 바로 그런 의도를 마음에 품고 곁에 버티고 선 남자가 있으니 — 일시적인 변덕 말고는 아무런 원칙도 없고, 나에게 개인적인 적의를 품은 남자 — 나는 확신한다 — 자기 배은망덕에 대한 의식으로 길러진 적의, 그가 끊임없이 조롱으로 터뜨려 온, 마치 내가 직접 들은 것처럼 확신하는 그 적의를. 설령 내가 산다 해도, 그가 간접적인 영향력을 통해 무엇을 꾀할지에 대한 불안에서 벗어나지 못하리라. 이 남자는 도러시아의 귀를 사로잡았고, 그녀의 주의를 매혹했으며, 내가 그를 위해 해 준 그 무엇보다도 더 큰 권리를 자기가 지녔다는 관념을 그녀의 마음에 심으려고 분명히 애썼다. 내가 죽으면 — 그리고 그는 여기서 그것을 노리며 기다리고 있다 — 그는 그녀를 설득해 자기와 결혼하게 할 것이다. 그것은 그녀에게는 재앙이요 그에게는 성공이리라. 그녀는 그것을 재앙으로 여기지 않을 것이다. 그는 그녀로 하여금 무엇이든 믿게 만들 테니까. 그녀에게는 내가 응답해 주지 않는다고 속으로 나를 책망하는 지나친 애착의 경향이 있고, 이미 그녀의 마음은 그의 처지로 가득 차 있다. 그는 손쉬운 정복과 내 둥지로 들어오는 것을 꿈꾼다. 그것을 나는 막으리라! 그런 결혼은 도러시아에게 치명적이리라. 그가 반발심에서가 아니고서 무언가를 끝까지 밀고 나간 적이 있던가? 지식에 있어 그는 늘 적은 밑천으로 화려하게 보이려 애써 왔다. 종교에 있어 그는, 자기에게 유리한 동안에는, 도러시아의 변덕에 대한 손쉬운 메아리가 될 수 있었다. 어쭙잖은 지식이 방종과 떨어진 적이 언제 있던가? 나는 그의 도덕성을 전혀 믿지 못하며, 그의 계략의 실현을 온 힘을 다해 막는 것이 나의 의무다.\"\n\n캐소본 씨가 결혼할 때 마련해 둔 조치들은 그에게 강력한 수단을 열어 두고 있었지만, 그것들을 곱씹는 동안 그의 마음은 어쩔 수 없이 자기 수명의 개연성에 너무나 많이 머물게 되어, 가능한 한 가장 정확한 계산을 얻고자 하는 갈망이 마침내 그의 자존심 어린 과묵함을 이기고 말았고, 리드게이트에게 자기 병의 성질에 대한 견해를 묻기로 결심하게 만들었다.\n\n그는 도러시아에게 리드게이트가 약속대로 세 시 반에 올 것이라고 말했고, 어디 편찮으신 거냐고 묻는 그녀의 불안한 질문에 이렇게 대답했다. \"아니오. 그저 몇 가지 습관적인 증상에 대해 그의 의견을 듣고 싶을 뿐이오. 당신은 그를 만날 필요가 없소, 여보. 내가 평소처럼 운동을 하고 있을 주목 산책로로 그를 보내라고 일러 두겠소.\"\n\n리드게이트가 주목 산책로에 들어섰을 때, 그는 캐소본 씨가 습관대로 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 고개를 앞으로 숙이고 천천히 멀어져 가는 것을 보았다. 사랑스러운 오후였다. 우뚝 솟은 보리수에서 잎들이 음울한 상록수를 가로질러 소리 없이 떨어지고 있었고, 빛과 그림자가 나란히 잠들어 있었다. 떼까마귀의 울음소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었으니, 익숙한 귀에는 그것이 자장가, 혹은 저 마지막 엄숙한 자장가인 만가와도 같았다. 한창때의 기운찬 신체를 의식하고 있던 리드게이트는 곧 따라잡을 듯한 그 인물이 돌아서서 자기 쪽으로 다가오며 그 어느 때보다도 뚜렷하게 노쇠의 징후를 — 학자의 굽은 어깨, 수척한 사지, 입가의 우울한 주름을 — 드러내는 것을 보고 다소간 연민을 느꼈다. \"가엾은 사람,\" 그는 생각했다. \"저 나이에 어떤 사람들은 사자 같아서, 다 자랐다는 것 말고는 나이를 짐작할 수 없는데.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 선생,\" 캐소본 씨가 변함없이 정중한 태도로 말했다. \"시간을 정확히 지켜 주셔서 대단히 고맙습니다. 괜찮으시다면 거닐면서 이야기를 나누도록 합시다.\"\n\n\"저를 만나시려는 것이 불쾌한 증상이 다시 나타난 탓은 아니길 바랍니다.\" 리드게이트가 침묵을 메우며 말했다.\n\n\"당장은 아니오 — 그렇소. 그 바람을 설명하자면, 그렇지 않으면 굳이 언급할 필요가 없을 무언가를 말해야겠소 — 즉, 모든 부수적인 면에서는 하찮은 내 삶이, 그 가장 좋은 시절 전체에 걸쳐 이어 온 노작이 미완으로 남았기에 어쩌면 중요성을 띨 수도 있다는 것 말이오. 요컨대 나는 오랫동안 어떤 저작에 매달려 왔는데, 적어도 다른 이들의 손으로 인쇄에 부쳐질 수 있을 만한 상태로는 남겨 두고 싶소. 만약 이것이 내가 합리적으로 기대할 수 있는 최대치라는 확신이 든다면, 그 확신은 내 시도를 유용하게 한정해 줄 것이고, 내 진로의 적극적, 소극적 결정 모두에서 길잡이가 되어 줄 것이오.\"\n\n여기서 캐소본 씨는 말을 멈추고, 한 손을 등에서 떼어 외짝 단추가 달린 외투의 단추 사이에 찔러 넣었다. 인간의 운명에 대해 풍부히 배운 정신에게는, 평소의 단조로운 어조와 고갯짓으로 전달된 그의 격식 차린 절제된 발언에 담긴 내면의 갈등만큼 흥미로운 것이 거의 없으리라. 아니, 그 영혼의 의의 전부였던 노작을 — 아무도 필요로 하지 않는 곳에서 오갔다 사라지는 물처럼 사라져 버릴 의의를 — 단념하라는 요구와 맞서는 영혼의 투쟁보다 더 숭고하게 비극적인 상황이 과연 많을까? 그러나 캐소본 씨에게는 남들에게 숭고하게 비칠 만한 것이 전혀 없었고, 헛된 학식에 대해 어느 정도 경멸감을 품고 있던 리드게이트는 연민에 약간의 재미가 뒤섞이는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 당시 재난에 너무나 어두웠기에, 고통받는 자의 열정적인 자기중심성만 제외하고는 모든 것이 비극의 수준에 못 미치는 그런 처지의 비애 속으로 들어갈 수 없었다.\n\n\"건강 부족에서 비롯될 수 있는 지장을 말씀하시는 것입니까?\" 그가 말했으니, 어떤 망설임에 막혀 있는 듯한 캐소본 씨의 목적을 거들고자 함이었다.\n\n\"그렇소. 당신은 — 내가 증언하건대 — 세심한 주의를 기울여 관찰한 그 증상들이 치명적인 병의 증상이라고 내게 시사한 적이 없소. 그러나 만약 그렇다면, 리드게이트 선생, 나는 가감 없이 진실을 알고 싶고, 당신의 결론을 정확히 진술해 주기를 청하오. 우정 어린 봉사로서 그것을 요청하는 바요. 만약 내 생명이 평범한 우연 외의 다른 무엇으로도 위협받지 않는다고 말해 줄 수 있다면, 나는 이미 밝힌 이유들로 기뻐할 것이오. 만약 그렇지 않다면, 진실을 아는 것이 내게는 더욱 중요하오.\"\n\n\"그러시다면 저도 제가 취할 길을 더는 망설이지 않겠습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"그러나 제가 먼저 강조해 드려야 할 것은 제 결론이 이중으로 불확실하다는 점입니다 — 제가 오류를 범할 수 있다는 점 때문만이 아니라, 심장 질환이란 예측의 근거로 삼기가 지극히 어렵기 때문에도 불확실합니다. 어쨌든 누구도 생명의 그 엄청난 불확실성을 눈에 띄게 더 키울 수는 없는 법입니다.\"\n\n캐소본 씨는 눈에 띄게 움찔했으나, 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"제 생각에 선생께서는 이른바 심장의 지방변성을 앓고 계십니다. 이는 그리 오래되지 않은 옛날에 청진기를 우리에게 준 사람인 라에네크가 처음으로 알아내고 탐구한 질병입니다. 이 주제에 관해서는 상당한 경험이 — 더 오랜 관찰이 — 아직 부족합니다. 그러나 선생께서 말씀하신 바에 비추어 보면, 이 병으로 인한 죽음이 흔히 갑작스럽다는 것을 말씀드리는 것이 제 의무입니다. 동시에 그런 결과를 예측할 수는 없습니다. 선생의 상태로 앞으로 십오 년, 혹은 그 이상도 그런대로 편안한 삶을 사실 수 있을 것입니다. 이에 덧붙일 수 있는 것은 해부학적, 의학적 세부 사항뿐인데, 그것은 예측을 정확히 같은 지점에 남겨 둘 따름입니다.\" 리드게이트의 본능은 충분히 섬세하여, 과시적인 신중함이 전혀 없는 솔직한 말이 캐소본 씨에게 경의의 표시로 느껴지리라는 것을 그에게 일러 주었다.\n\n\"고맙소, 리드게이트 선생.\" 캐소본 씨가 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다. \"한 가지 더 물어볼 것이 있소. 지금 내게 말한 것을 캐소본 부인에게 알렸소?\"\n\n\"부분적으로 — 그러니까, 일어날 수 있는 결과에 대해서 말입니다.\" 리드게이트는 자기가 왜 도러시아에게 말했는지 설명하려 했으나, 캐소본 씨는 대화를 끝내고 싶다는 분명한 욕구로 가볍게 손을 내저으며 \"고맙소\"라고 다시 말하고는, 그날의 보기 드문 아름다움에 대해 한마디 하기 시작했다.\n\n자기 환자가 혼자 있기를 바란다는 것을 확신한 리드게이트는 곧 그를 떠났다. 그리고 두 손을 등 뒤로 하고 고개를 앞으로 숙인 그 검은 인물은 어두운 주목들이 우울 속에서 말없는 벗이 되어 주는 산책로를 계속 거닐었고, 햇빛의 섬들을 가로질러 스쳐 가는 새나 잎의 작은 그림자들이 마치 슬픔 앞에 놓인 듯 소리 없이 살그머니 지나갔다. 여기, 이제 처음으로 죽음의 눈을 들여다보게 된 한 사람이 있었다 — 우리가 어떤 진부한 진리를 그저 안다고 부르는 것과는 전혀 다르게, 그 진리를 느끼게 되는 드문 경험의 순간 가운데 하나를 지나가는 한 사람이 있었으니, 그것은 땅 위의 물을 바라보는 것이 타들어 가는 혀를 식힐 수 없는 물을 향한 헛된 환영과 다른 것만큼이나 다르다. \"우리는 모두 죽는다\"라는 진부한 말이 별안간 \"나는 죽는다 — 그것도 곧\"이라는 날카로운 의식으로 변할 때, 그제야 죽음이 우리를 움켜쥐고, 그 손가락은 잔인하다. 그 뒤에 죽음은 우리 어머니가 그러했듯 우리를 품에 안아 줄지도 모르고, 지상의 것을 어렴풋이 분별하는 우리의 마지막 순간은 그 첫 순간과도 같을지 모른다. 이제 캐소본 씨에게는, 자기가 별안간 어두운 강가에 서서 다가오는 노 젓는 소리를 듣되 그 형체는 분간하지 못한 채 부름을 기다리는 것만 같았다. 그런 시간에 정신은 평생의 성향을 바꾸지 않고, 그것을 상상 속에서 죽음의 저편으로까지 이어 가, 뒤를 돌아본다 — 어쩌면 자비로움의 거룩한 평온함으로, 어쩌면 자기주장의 하찮은 불안으로. 캐소본 씨의 성향이 무엇이었는지는 그의 행위가 우리에게 실마리를 줄 것이다. 그는 현재에 대한 평가와 미래에 대한 희망에 있어, 약간의 사사로운 학자적 유보를 두면서, 자기가 신앙심 깊은 기독교인이라고 자처했다. 그러나 우리가 충족시키려 애쓰는 것은, 비록 그것을 먼 희망이라 부를지라도, 당장의 욕망이다. 사람들이 도시의 뒷골목을 헤매며 애써 마련하려는 미래의 재산은 이미 그들의 상상과 사랑 속에 존재한다. 그리고 캐소본 씨의 당장의 욕망은 지상의 조건을 벗어난 신과의 교감과 빛이 아니었으니, 그 가엾은 사람의 열정적인 갈망은 매우 그늘진 곳에 안개처럼 낮게 들러붙어 있었다.\n\n도러시아는 리드게이트가 말을 타고 떠났을 때 그것을 알아채고, 곧장 남편에게 가려는 충동에 정원으로 나섰다. 그러나 그녀는 자기를 들이밀어 그를 언짢게 할까 두려워 망설였다. 끊임없이 거부당한 그녀의 열정이, 그녀의 강렬한 기억과 더불어, 마치 좌절된 기운이 전율로 가라앉듯 그녀의 두려움을 한층 높였기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀는 가까운 나무 무리 사이를 천천히 거닐다가, 마침내 그가 다가오는 것을 보았다. 그러자 그녀는 그를 향해 나아갔으니, 그 모습은 남은 짧은 시간이나마 이해받은 슬픔에 더욱 가까이 매달리는 그 충실한 사랑으로 채워지리라는 약속을 들고 오는, 하늘이 보낸 천사를 떠올리게 했을지도 모른다. 그녀의 시선에 답하는 그의 눈길이 너무나 차가워서 그녀는 소심함이 더해지는 것을 느꼈다. 그래도 그녀는 몸을 돌려 그의 팔에 손을 끼웠다.\n\n캐소본 씨는 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채로, 그녀의 유순한 팔이 자기의 뻣뻣한 팔에 힘겹게 매달리도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n이 무반응한 단단함이 도러시아에게 안긴 그 느낌에는 무언가 끔찍한 데가 있었다. 강한 말이지만, 지나친 말은 아니다. 사람들이 사소하다고 부르는 바로 이런 행위들 속에서 기쁨의 씨앗이 영영 허비되고 마니, 마침내 남녀는 자기들이 빚어낸 황폐를 초췌한 얼굴로 둘러보며 대지가 달콤함의 수확을 맺지 못한다고 말하면서 — 그 부정을 두고 깨달음이라 부르는 것이다. 어째서 사내답게 굴려면, 캐소본 씨가 그런 식으로 행동했어야 하느냐고 물을지도 모른다. 그의 정신이 동정을 꺼리는 정신이었음을 생각해 보라. 그런 정신 속에서, 자기를 슬픔으로 짓누르는 것이 사실은 이미 동정함으로써 거슬리게 하는 그 존재에게 현재든 미래든 만족의 원천일지도 모른다는 의심이 미치는 영향을 지켜본 적이 있는가? 게다가 그는 도러시아의 감정에 대해 거의 알지 못했고, 지금 같은 경우에 그 감정이 카프의 비판에 대한 자기 자신의 예민함과 맞먹는 강도라는 것을 헤아려 보지도 못했다.\n\n도러시아는 팔을 빼지 않았지만, 감히 말을 꺼낼 엄두를 내지 못했다. 캐소본 씨는 \"혼자 있고 싶소\"라고 말하지는 않았으나, 말없이 집을 향해 걸음을 옮겼고, 두 사람이 이 동쪽 면의 유리문으로 들어설 때, 도러시아는 남편을 완전히 자유롭게 해 주려고 팔을 빼고 깔개 위에서 머뭇거렸다. 그는 서재로 들어가 문을 닫고, 자기 슬픔과 더불어 홀로 있었다.\n\n그녀는 자기 규방으로 올라갔다. 활짝 열린 곡선형 창으로 오후의 고요한 영광이 들어왔으니, 보리수가 긴 그림자를 드리운 진입로 위에 그 영광이 깃들어 있었다. 그러나 도러시아는 그 풍경을 전혀 알지 못했다. 그녀는 자기가 눈부신 햇살 속에 있다는 것도 개의치 않고 의자에 몸을 던졌다. 거기에 불편함이 있다 한들, 그것이 자기 내면의 비참함의 일부가 아니라고 어떻게 알 수 있었겠는가?\n\n그녀는 결혼한 이래 느꼈던 그 어떤 분노보다도 강한, 반항적인 분노의 반작용에 사로잡혀 있었다. 눈물 대신 말이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"내가 무엇을 했기에 — 내가 어떤 사람이기에 — 그가 나를 이렇게 대한단 말인가? 그는 내 마음속에 무엇이 있는지 결코 모르고 — 결코 신경 쓰지 않아. 내가 하는 일이 다 무슨 소용이람? 그는 나와 결혼하지 않았기를 바라고 있어.\"\n\n그녀는 자기 말을 듣기 시작했고, 그 소리에 제지당해 잠잠해졌다. 길을 잃고 지친 사람처럼, 그녀는 앉아서 다시는 찾지 못할 자기 젊은 희망의 온갖 길들을 단번에 보는 듯했다. 그리고 그 비참한 빛 속에서, 그녀는 자기 자신과 남편의 고독을 — 두 사람이 따로 떨어져 걸었기에 그녀가 어쩔 수 없이 그를 살펴봐야 했음을 — 마찬가지로 똑똑히 보았다. 만약 그가 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겼더라면, 그녀는 결코 그를 살펴보지도 — 결코 \"그가 위해서 살 만한 가치가 있는 사람인가?\"라고 묻지도 않았을 것이고, 그저 그를 자기 삶의 일부로 느꼈으리라. 이제 그녀는 쓰라리게 말했다. \"이건 내 잘못이 아니라 그의 잘못이야.\" 그녀의 온 존재가 진동하는 가운데 연민이 무너져 내렸다. 그를 믿었던 것이 — 그의 가치를 믿었던 것이 그녀의 잘못이었던가? 그리고 그는 정확히 어떤 사람인가? 그녀는 그를 평가할 만한 능력이 충분히 있었으니 — 떨면서 그의 눈길을 기다리고, 그를 기쁘게 하기에 충분히 하찮아지려고 자기의 가장 좋은 영혼을 감옥에 가두어 둔 채 그곳에 은밀한 방문만을 하던 그녀가 말이다. 이런 위기에서 어떤 여자들은 미워하기 시작한다.\n\n도러시아가 다시 내려가지 않고 남편에게 몸이 좋지 않아 위층에 머물고 싶다는 전갈을 보내야겠다고 생각했을 때, 해는 낮게 기울어 있었다. 그녀는 이전에는 결코 자기 원한이 이런 식으로 자기를 지배하도록 일부러 내버려 둔 적이 없었으나, 이제 자기 감정에 대한 진실을 그에게 말하지 않고서는 그를 다시 볼 수 없으리라고 믿었고, 방해받지 않고 그렇게 할 수 있을 때까지 기다려야 했다. 그가 자기 전갈에 의아해하고 상처받을지도 몰랐다. 그가 의아해하고 상처받는 것은 잘된 일이었다. 그녀의 분노는, 분노가 곧잘 그러하듯, 신이 그녀의 편이라고 — 비록 두 사람을 지켜보는 영들로 가득 차 있을지언정 온 천국이 그녀 편일 수밖에 없다고 말했다. 그녀가 종을 울리기로 마음먹었을 때, 문에서 노크 소리가 났다.\n\n캐소본 씨가 사람을 보내어, 저녁은 서재에서 들겠다고 전했다. 그는 할 일이 많아 오늘 저녁은 아주 혼자 있고 싶다는 것이었다.\n\n\"그러면 나는 저녁을 들지 않겠다, 탠트립.\"\n\n\"오, 마님, 조금이라도 뭘 좀 가져다드릴까요?\"\n\n\"아니야. 몸이 좋지 않구나. 화장실에 모든 것을 준비해 두되, 다시는 나를 방해하지 말아 다오.\"\n\n도러시아는 명상에 잠긴 채 거의 미동도 없이 앉아 있었고, 그동안 저녁은 천천히 짙어져 밤이 되었다. 그러나 그 투쟁은 끊임없이 변해 갔으니, 마치 처음에는 내리치려는 움직임으로 시작했다가 결국 내리치고 싶은 욕망을 이겨 내고 끝맺는 사람의 투쟁과도 같았다. 범죄에 생기를 불어넣을 기운은, 영혼의 고결한 습성이 다시 제 모습을 되찾을 때 결연한 순종을 불어넣는 데 필요한 기운보다 더 큰 것이 아니다. 도러시아가 남편을 만나러 나갈 때 품었던 그 생각 — 그가 자기 모든 노작이 중단될 가능성에 대해 물었으리라는 확신, 그리고 그 대답이 그의 가슴을 쥐어짰으리라는 확신은, 마치 슬픈 책망의 눈길로 그녀의 분노를 바라보는 어렴풋한 감독자처럼, 그의 모습 곁에 떠오르지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 그 슬픔들을 위한 자비가 될 수 있도록, 그것은 그녀에게 그려진 슬픔들과 소리 없는 부르짖음의 연속된 기도를 치르게 했다 — 그러나 결연한 순종은 끝내 찾아왔다. 그리고 집이 고요해지고, 캐소본 씨가 습관적으로 잠자리에 드는 시각이 가까워졌음을 알았을 때, 그녀는 가만히 문을 열고 어둠 속에서 밖에 서서, 손에 등불을 들고 위층으로 올라올 그를 기다렸다. 그가 곧 오지 않으면 그녀는 아래로 내려가, 또 한 번의 아픔을 무릅쓰는 한이 있더라도 그렇게 하리라 생각했다. 그녀는 다시는 다른 무엇도 기대하지 않으리라. 그러나 그녀는 정말로 서재 문이 열리는 소리를 들었고, 카펫 위의 발소리도 없이 등불이 천천히 계단을 올라왔다. 남편이 그녀의 맞은편에 섰을 때, 그녀는 그의 얼굴이 더욱 초췌해진 것을 보았다. 그는 그녀를 보고 살짝 움찔했고, 그녀는 말없이 애원하듯 그를 올려다보았다.\n\n\"도러시아!\" 그가 다정하게 놀란 어조로 말했다. \"나를 기다리고 있었소?\"\n\n\"네, 당신을 방해하고 싶지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"이리 와요, 여보, 이리 와요. 당신은 젊으니, 깨어 지켜보느라 수명을 늘릴 필요는 없소.\"\n\n그 다정하고 차분한 우수가 깃든 말이 도러시아의 귀에 와닿았을 때, 그녀는 절름거리는 짐승을 다치게 할 뻔하다가 간신히 모면했을 때 우리 안에서 솟구칠 법한 그런 감사와도 같은 무언가를 느꼈다. 그녀는 남편의 손에 자기 손을 얹었고, 두 사람은 함께 넓은 복도를 따라 걸어갔다.\n\n# BOOK V. THE DEAD HAND. CHAPTER XLIII.\n\n너그러운 여성성의 순수하고 고귀한 선이라.\n저것 또한 값진 물건, 솜씨 좋게 빚은\n\n마욜리카 도자기, 고귀한 눈을 즐겁게 하려는 것.\n보다시피 그 미소는 완벽하다, 한낱\n\n채색 도기의 솜씨로는 경이로울 만큼! 가장 호화로운\n장식대에 어울릴 식탁의 장식품이라.”\n\n도러시아는 좀처럼 남편 없이 집을 나서지 않았지만, 작은 쇼핑이나 자선 같은 용무로 이따금 혼자 미들마치까지 마차를 타고 가곤 했다. 도시에서 삼 마일 이내에 사는, 다소의 재산을 지닌 부인이라면 누구에게나 으레 생기는 일이었다. 주목 산책로에서의 그 장면이 있은 지 이틀 후, 그녀는 그런 기회를 이용해 가능하다면 리드게이트를 만나, 남편이 정말로 자신에게 숨기고 있는 증상의 어떤 우울한 변화를 느낀 적이 있는지, 그리고 자기 자신에 관해 최악의 것까지 알기를 고집했는지 그에게서 알아내기로 결심했다. 다른 사람에게 남편에 대한 정보를 구하는 것이 거의 죄스럽게 느껴졌지만, 그것을 모른 채 지내는 데 대한 두려움—자신을 부당하거나 모질게 만들지도 모를 그 무지에 대한 두려움이 모든 거리낌을 이겨 냈다. 남편의 마음속에 어떤 위기가 있었다는 것만은 확신했다. 바로 그 이튿날 그는 노트를 정리하는 새로운 방식을 시작했고, 자신의 계획을 수행하는 데 그녀를 아주 새롭게 끌어들였던 것이다. 가엾은 도러시아는 인내심을 비축해 둘 필요가 있었다.\n\n그녀가 로익 게이트에 있는 리드게이트의 집으로 마차를 몰고 간 것은 네 시쯤이었다. 그가 집에 있을지 당장 의심스러워, 미리 편지를 써 둘 걸 그랬다고 생각했다. 그리고 그는 집에 없었다.\n\n“리드게이트 부인은 계신가요?” 도러시아가 물었다. 그녀는 자신이 아는 한 로저먼드를 본 적이 없었으나, 이제야 그 결혼 사실이 떠올랐다. 그렇다고, 리드게이트 부인은 집에 있었다.\n“허락해 주신다면 들어가 말씀을 나누고 싶군요. 잠시 저를—케소번 부인을 만나 주실 수 있는지 여쭤봐 주시겠어요?”\n\n하인이 그 전갈을 전하러 가자, 도러시아는 열린 창문을 통해 음악 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 남자 목소리로 부른 몇 소절에 이어 피아노가 빠른 장식음을 쏟아 냈다. 그러나 그 장식음은 돌연 멈췄고, 곧 하인이 돌아와 리드게이트 부인이 케소번 부인을 기꺼이 만나겠다고 한다고 전했다.\n응접실 문이 열리고 도러시아가 들어서자, 신분이 다른 계층의 습관이 지금처럼 섞이지 않았던 시골 생활에서 드물지 않게 볼 수 있는 일종의 대조가 빚어졌다. 그 온화한 가을날에 도러시아가 입었던 옷이 정확히 어떤 천이었는지는, 아는 사람이 있다면 우리에게 말해 주기를 바란다—촉감도 부드럽고 눈에도 부드러운 그 얇은 흰 모직 천 말이다. 그 옷은 늘 막 세탁한 것처럼 보였고 향긋한 산울타리 냄새를 풍기는 듯했으며, 언제나 유행에서 완전히 벗어난 소매가 늘어진 외투 모양이었다. 그러나 만약 그녀가 이모진이나 카토의 딸로 분장하여 조용히 지켜보는 관객 앞에 등장했더라면, 그 옷차림이 충분히 어울려 보였을 것이다. 우아함과 기품이 그녀의 사지와 목에 깃들어 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 단정하게 가른 머리와 솔직한 눈 둘레에, 당시 여성들의 운명이었던 커다랗고 둥근 모자 챙은, 우리가 후광이라 부르는 황금빛 쟁반보다 더 이상해 보이지 않는 머리 장식이었다. 지금 두 사람으로 이루어진 관객에게, 케소번 부인보다 더 큰 관심을 기울일 만한 극중 여주인공은 없었을 것이다. 로저먼드에게 그녀는 미들마치의 속세와 어울리지 않는 그 시골의 여신 같은 존재 중 하나였으니, 그 태도나 외모의 사소한 특징조차 연구할 가치가 있는 대상이었다. 게다가 로저먼드는 케소번 부인이 자기를 연구할 기회를 갖게 된 것에 만족스럽지 않은 것도 아니었다. 가장 안목 있는 사람에게 보여지지 못한다면 우아하게 가꾼들 무슨 소용이겠는가? 또 로저먼드는 고드윈 리드게이트 경의 댁에서 최고의 찬사를 받았던 터라, 좋은 가문의 사람들에게 자신이 줄 인상에 대해 자못 자신만만했다. 도러시아는 늘 그렇듯 소박한 친절을 담아 손을 내밀며, 리드게이트의 사랑스러운 신부를 감탄하듯 바라보았다—멀리 신사 한 사람이 서 있다는 것은 알아챘으나, 그저 넓은 각도로 떨어진 외투 입은 형상으로만 보았다. 그 신사는 한 여인의 존재에 너무도 사로잡혀 두 여인 사이의 대조—차분한 관찰자라면 분명 인상적으로 느꼈을 그 대조—를 헤아릴 겨를이 없었다. 두 사람 다 키가 컸고 눈높이가 같았다. 그러나 로저먼드의 아기 같은 금발과 경탄스러운 머리 땋은 관을 상상해 보라. 어떤 양재사라도 감동 없이는 볼 수 없을 만큼 완벽하게 재단되고 멋스러운 연푸른 드레스를 입었고, 모든 구경꾼이 그 값을 알아주기를 바랄 만한 커다란 자수 깃을 둘렀으며, 작은 손은 반지로 한껏 돋보이게 했고, 소박함을 대신하는 값비싼 대용품인 그 절제된 자의식이 몸에 배어 있었다.\n\n“방해해도 좋다고 허락해 주셔서 정말 감사합니다.” 도러시아가 곧바로 말했다. “집에 돌아가기 전에 가능하면 리드게이트 씨를 꼭 만나 뵙고 싶은데, 혹시 어디서 그분을 찾을 수 있을지 알려 주시거나, 곧 오실 것 같으면 기다려도 좋을지 여쭤보고 싶었어요.”\n\n“그이는 새 병원에 있어요.” 로저먼드가 말했다. “언제 돌아올지는 잘 모르겠어요. 하지만 사람을 보내 부를 수 있어요.”\n\n“제가 가서 모셔 올까요?” 윌 라디슬로가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. 그는 도러시아가 들어오기 전에 이미 모자를 집어 든 상태였다. 그녀는 놀라서 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 틀림없는 기쁨의 미소를 띠며 손을 내밀고 말했다—\n\n“당신인 줄 몰랐어요. 여기서 만나리라고는 생각도 못 했어요.”\n\n“병원에 가서 리드게이트 씨에게 당신이 만나고 싶어 한다고 전할까요?” 윌이 말했다.\n\n“마차를 보내 그분을 모셔 오는 게 더 빠를 거예요.” 도러시아가 말했다. “수고스럽겠지만 마부에게 전갈을 전해 주신다면요.”\n\n윌이 문 쪽으로 움직일 때, 순간적으로 여러 연결된 기억들이 머릿속을 스쳐 간 도러시아가 재빨리 돌아서며 말했다. “제가 직접 가겠어요, 고마워요. 다시 집에 돌아가기 전에 시간을 허비하고 싶지 않아요. 병원으로 마차를 몰고 가서 거기서 리드게이트 씨를 뵙겠어요. 죄송해요, 리드게이트 부인. 정말 감사합니다.”\n\n그녀의 마음은 어떤 갑작스러운 생각에 분명히 사로잡혀 있었고, 그녀는 바로 주위에서 일어나는 일을 거의 의식하지 못한 채 방을 나섰다—윌이 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주고 마차까지 이끌려고 팔을 내밀었다는 것조차 거의 의식하지 못했다. 그녀는 팔을 잡았으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 윌은 다소 언짢고 비참한 기분이 들어, 자신도 할 말을 찾지 못했다. 그는 말없이 그녀를 마차에 태웠고, 두 사람은 작별 인사를 했으며, 도러시아는 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n병원까지 가는 오 분의 마차 길에서 그녀는 자신에게 아주 새로운 몇 가지 생각을 할 시간이 있었다. 가겠다는 결심과 방을 나서며 골몰했던 마음은, 남편에게 말할 수 없는 자신과 윌 사이의 어떤 추가적인 교류를 스스로 허용하는 데 일종의 기만이 있으리라는 갑작스러운 깨달음에서 비롯된 것이었고, 이미 리드게이트를 찾아 나선 그녀의 용무 자체가 숨겨야 할 일이었다. 마음속에 분명하게 떠오른 것은 그것뿐이었다. 그러나 어떤 막연한 불편함도 그녀를 부추겼다. 이제 마차 안에 홀로 있게 되자, 그때는 별로 주의를 기울이지 않았던 그 남자의 목소리와 함께한 피아노 소리가 내면의 감각으로 되살아났다. 그리고 그녀는 남편이 없는 사이에 윌 라디슬로가 리드게이트 부인과 함께 시간을 보내고 있었다는 사실에 다소 의아한 마음으로 생각에 잠긴 자신을 발견했다. 그러다 그가 비슷한 상황에서 자신과도 얼마간 시간을 보냈던 일을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으니, 그렇다면 그 사실에 무슨 부적절함이 있겠는가? 그러나 윌은 케소번 씨의 친척이었고, 그녀가 친절을 베풀어야 할 사람이었다. 그래도 어쩌면 케소번 씨가 자신이 없는 동안 사촌이 찾아오는 것을 좋아하지 않는다는 뜻으로 이해했어야 할 징후들이 있었다. “어쩌면 나는 여러 가지를 잘못 생각해 왔는지도 몰라.” 가엾은 도러시아는 속으로 중얼거렸고, 눈물이 흘러내려 얼른 닦아 내야 했다. 그녀는 혼란스럽게 불행한 기분이었고, 전에는 그토록 또렷했던 윌의 모습이 알 수 없이 흐려졌다. 그러나 마차가 병원 정문에 멈춰 섰다. 그녀는 곧 리드게이트와 함께 잔디밭을 거닐었고, 그녀의 감정은 이 만남을 청하게 만들었던 강한 의지를 되찾았다.\n\n한편 윌 라디슬로는 굴욕감을 느꼈고, 그 이유를 충분히 명확하게 알고 있었다. 도러시아를 만날 기회는 드물었는데, 여기서 처음으로 자신을 불리한 처지에 놓이게 한 기회가 찾아온 것이었다. 그것은 지금까지처럼 그녀가 그에게 온전히 마음을 쏟지 않고 있다는 것만이 아니라, 그가 그녀에게 온전히 마음을 쏟고 있지 않은 것처럼 보일 만한 상황에서 그녀가 그를 보았다는 것이었다. 그는 그녀의 삶의 일부를 이루지 못하는 미들마치 사람들의 무리 가운데서, 그녀로부터 새로운 거리만큼 떠밀려난 느낌이 들었다. 그러나 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니었다. 당연히 그는 이 도시에 거처를 정한 이상 가능한 한 많은 사람과 알고 지내 왔으니, 그의 지위가 누구든 무엇이든 다 알아야 할 것을 요구했기 때문이다. 리드게이트는 이 인근의 그 누구보다도 정말 알고 지낼 만한 사람이었고, 마침 음악에 조예가 있고 여러모로 방문할 만한 아내를 두고 있었다. 디아나가 자신을 숭배하는 자에게 너무도 예기치 않게 강림한 그 상황의 전모는 이러했다. 굴욕스러운 일이었다. 윌은 도러시아가 아니었다면 자신이 미들마치에 와 있지도 않았으리라는 것을 의식하고 있었다. 그런데도 그곳에서의 그의 처지는, 로마와 브리타니아 사이의 온갖 거리보다도 상호 관심의 지속에 더 치명적인, 그 습관적 감정의 장벽으로 그를 그녀와 갈라놓으려 하고 있었다. 신분과 지위에 관한 편견은 케소번 씨의 횡포한 편지 같은 형태로는 충분히 쉽게 거역할 수 있었다. 그러나 편견이란, 향기를 내뿜는 물체처럼, 견고하면서도 미묘한 이중의 존재를 지니는 법이다—피라미드처럼 견고하고, 메아리의 메아리가 스무 번째로 울리는 것처럼, 혹은 한때 어둠에 향기를 더했던 히아신스의 기억처럼 미묘하다. 그리고 윌은 그 미묘함의 존재를 예민하게 느끼는 기질이었다. 더 둔한 감각을 지닌 사람이라면, 도러시아의 마음속에 그와의 완전한 자유로움 속에서 처음으로 어떤 부적절함의 감각이 싹텄다는 것을, 그리고 그가 그녀를 마차로 이끌 때의 그 침묵에 냉기가 서려 있었다는 것을 그처럼 느끼지 못했을 것이다. 어쩌면 케소번이 증오와 질투에 사로잡혀, 윌이 사회적으로 그녀보다 낮은 곳으로 굴러떨어졌다고 도러시아에게 우겨 댔는지도 모른다. 케소번, 빌어먹을!\n\n윌은 응접실로 다시 들어가 모자를 집어 들고, 작업대 앞에 앉은 리드게이트 부인에게 다가가며 짜증스러운 기색으로 말했다—\n\n“음악이나 시가 중단되는 건 언제나 치명적이지요. 다른 날 다시 와서 ‘사랑하는 임에게서 멀리’의 해석을 마저 끝내도 될까요?”\n\n“가르침을 받을 수 있다면 기쁘겠어요.” 로저먼드가 말했다. “하지만 그 방해가 무척 아름다운 것이었다는 건 인정하시겠지요. 케소번 부인과 아는 사이라니 정말 부러워요. 그분은 무척 영리한가요? 그래 보이던데요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 그런 건 생각해 본 적이 없습니다.” 윌이 뚱하게 말했다.\n\n“그분이 미인이냐고 테르티우스에게 처음 물었을 때 그이가 한 대답이 꼭 그랬어요. 당신네 신사분들은 케소번 부인과 함께 있을 때 대체 무슨 생각을 하시나요?”\n\n“그분 자신이지요.” 윌이 매력적인 리드게이트 부인을 약 올리고 싶은 마음이 없지 않아 말했다. “완벽한 여인을 볼 때면 그 속성 따위는 결코 생각하지 않습니다—그저 그 존재를 의식할 뿐이지요.”\n\n“테르티우스가 로익에 가면 질투가 날 거예요.” 로저먼드가 보조개를 지으며 사뿐한 경쾌함으로 말했다. “그이는 돌아와서 저 따위는 거들떠보지도 않을걸요.”\n\n“지금껏 리드게이트에게 그런 영향을 미친 것 같지는 않은데요. 케소번 부인은 다른 여성들과는 너무 달라서 그들과 비교될 수가 없어요.”\n\n“보아하니 당신은 독실한 숭배자시군요. 그분을 자주 뵙겠지요.”\n\n“아니요.” 윌이 거의 토라진 듯 말했다. “숭배란 대개 실천이라기보다 이론의 문제니까요. 하지만 지금 이 순간 저는 그것을 지나치게 실천하고 있군요—정말로 떨쳐 일어나 가 봐야겠습니다.”\n\n“언제 저녁에 또 들러 주세요. 리드게이트 씨도 음악을 듣고 싶어 할 테고, 저도 그이 없이는 그만큼 즐길 수가 없으니까요.”\n\n남편이 다시 집에 돌아왔을 때, 로저먼드는 그의 앞에 서서 두 손으로 그의 외투 깃을 잡고 말했다. “케소번 부인이 들어왔을 때 라디슬로 씨가 여기서 저와 노래를 부르고 있었어요. 그분이 언짢아 보이더군요. 그분이 우리 집에서 자기 모습을 그 부인에게 보인 게 싫었던 걸까요? 케소번가와의 관계가 어떻든 간에, 당신 지위가 분명 그분 못지않을 텐데요.”\n\n“아니, 아니야. 정말로 언짢았다면 다른 이유 때문일 거야. 라디슬로는 일종의 집시 같은 친구야. 가죽이니 모직이니 하는 신분 따위는 아랑곳하지 않거든.”\n\n“음악을 빼면 그분이 늘 그렇게 유쾌한 건 아니에요. 당신은 그분이 좋아요?”\n\n“그럼. 괜찮은 친구라고 생각해. 좀 잡다하고 골동품 같은 데가 있긴 해도, 호감 가는 친구야.”\n\n“있잖아요, 그분이 케소번 부인을 흠모하는 것 같아요.”\n\n“가엾은 친구!” 리드게이트가 미소 지으며 아내의 귀를 살짝 꼬집고 말했다.\n\n로저먼드는 자신이 세상사를 많이 알게 되었다고 느끼기 시작했는데, 특히 처녀 시절에는 지나간 시대의 의상을 걸친 어슴푸레한 비극으로밖에는 상상할 수 없었던 것을 깨닫게 되면서 그러했다—여자란 결혼한 뒤에도 남자를 정복하고 노예로 삼을 수 있다는 사실 말이다. 그 시절 시골의 젊은 숙녀들은, 레먼 부인의 학교에서 교육받은 경우라 해도 라신보다 후대의 프랑스 문학은 거의 읽지 않았고, 출판물도 인생의 추문 위에 오늘날과 같은 찬란한 조명을 비추지 않았다. 그래도 허영심이란, 여자의 온 정신과 하루를 부려 쓸 수만 있다면, 사소한 암시만으로도, 특히 무한한 정복의 가능성 같은 암시만으로도 풍성하게 무언가를 지어낼 수 있는 법이다. 황태자 같은 남편을 곁에 두고—사실은 그 남편 자신이 신하인 셈인데—결혼의 옥좌에서 포로들을 사로잡는다는 것은 얼마나 즐거운 일인가. 그러는 동안 포로들은 영원히 가망 없이 우러러보며, 아마도 안식을 잃고, 입맛까지 잃는다면 더더욱 좋을 테고! 그러나 로저먼드의 낭만은 지금으로서는 주로 자신의 황태자에게 쏠려 있었고, 그가 확실하게 복종하고 있음을 누리는 것으로 충분했다. 그가 “가엾은 친구!”라고 말했을 때, 그녀는 장난스러운 호기심으로 물었다—\n\n“어째서요?”\n\n“글쎄, 당신 같은 인어 하나를 숭배하기 시작하면 남자가 뭘 할 수 있겠어? 그저 일을 소홀히 하고 빚이나 쌓아 올릴 뿐이지.”\n\n“당신은 분명 일을 소홀히 하지 않으시잖아요. 늘 병원에 가 있거나, 가난한 환자들을 보거나, 무슨 의사들 싸움을 생각하고 있고요. 그러다 집에 와서는 늘 현미경이랑 약병만 들여다보고 싶어 하시죠. 솔직히 그런 것들을 저보다 더 좋아한다고 인정하세요.”\n\n“당신은 남편이 한낱 미들마치의 의사보다 더 나은 사람이 되기를 바랄 만한 야심도 없어?” 리드게이트가 두 손을 아내의 어깨에 떨어뜨리며 애정 어린 진지함으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. “당신에게 옛 시인의 내가 좋아하는 구절을 외우게 해야겠군—\n‘어찌하여 우리의 자긍심은 그토록 부산을 떨며\n\n존재했다가 잊히려 하는가? 이만한 선이 또 어디 있으랴,\n기록할 가치가 있는 일을 행하고, 또 그것을\n\n읽을 가치가, 세상이 기뻐할 가치가 있게 적어 내는 것 말고는?’\n내가 바라는 건, 로지, 기록할 가치가 있는 일을 해내는 거야—그리고 내가 해낸 것을 내 손으로 직접 적어 내는 거지. 그러려면 사람은 일을 해야 해, 내 사랑.”\n\n“물론 당신이 발견을 이뤄 내기를 바라요. 미들마치보다 더 나은 곳에서 당신이 높은 지위에 오르기를 저보다 더 바랄 사람은 없을 거예요. 제가 당신의 일을 방해하려 한 적이 있다고는 말 못 하실 거예요. 하지만 우리가 은둔자처럼 살 수는 없잖아요. 저한테 불만이 있는 건 아니죠, 테르티우스?”\n\n“아니야, 여보, 아니야. 난 더할 나위 없이 만족하고 있어.”\n\n“그런데 케소번 부인이 당신에게 무슨 말을 하려던 거예요?”\n\n“그저 남편의 건강에 대해 묻고 싶었던 거야. 하지만 그 부인이 우리 새 병원에 훌륭한 도움을 줄 것 같아. 연 이백 파운드를 기부해 줄 것 같거든.”\n\n# CHAPTER XLIV.\n\n해안을 따라 살금살금 나아가지 않고, 별빛을 길잡이 삼아 바다 한가운데로 키를 잡으리라.\n\n도러시아가 리드게이트와 함께 새 병원의 월계수가 심긴 화단 주위를 거닐다가, 캐소본 씨의 병세에는 자신의 병에 관한 진실을 알고 싶어 하는 불안이라는 정신적 징후 외에 신체적 변화의 조짐이 없다는 말을 그에게서 들었을 때, 그녀는 몇 순간 잠자코, 혹시 자기가 이 새로운 불안을 불러일으킬 만한 말이나 행동을 하지는 않았을까 곰곰이 생각했다. 리드게이트는 평소 마음에 둔 목적을 진척시킬 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않아 용기를 내어 말했다.\n\n\"부인이나 캐소본 씨께서 우리 새 병원이 처한 어려움에 관심을 가지신 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다. 사정이 이렇다 보니 제가 이 문제를 거론하는 게 다소 이기적으로 비칠 것 같습니다만, 그건 제 잘못이 아닙니다. 다른 의료인들이 병원을 반대하며 싸움을 벌이고 있기 때문이지요. 부인께서는 대체로 그런 일에 관심을 두시는 것 같습니다. 결혼하시기 전 티프턴 그레인지에서 처음 뵙는 즐거움을 누렸을 때, 부인께서 빈민들의 비참한 주거가 그들의 건강에 어떤 영향을 미치는지 제게 몇 가지 질문하셨던 일이 기억나니까요.\"\n\n\"네, 정말 그랬지요.\" 도러시아가 환해진 얼굴로 말했다. \"상황을 조금이라도 나아지게 하려면 제가 어떻게 도울 수 있는지 말씀해 주신다면 정말 감사하겠어요. 결혼한 뒤로는 그런 일이 모두 제게서 멀어져 버렸어요. 제 말은,\" 그녀는 잠시 망설이다가 덧붙였다. \"우리 마을 사람들은 그런대로 편안하게 지내고, 또 제 마음이 너무 다른 데 쏠려 있어서 더는 알아볼 여유가 없었다는 뜻이에요. 하지만 이곳에서는, 미들마치 같은 곳에서는 할 일이 무척 많을 게 분명해요.\"\n\n\"해야 할 일은 전부라고 할 수 있습니다.\" 리드게이트가 대뜸 힘주어 말했다. \"그리고 이 병원은 훌륭한 사업이지요. 전적으로 불스트로드 씨의 노력 덕분이고, 상당 부분 그분의 돈으로 이루어진 일입니다. 하지만 이런 계획에서는 한 사람이 모든 일을 다 할 수는 없습니다. 물론 그분도 도움을 기대했지요. 그런데 이제 이 사업을 실패로 만들려는 몇몇 사람들이 마을에서 치사하고 옹졸한 반목을 일으키고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 이유로 그러는 걸까요?\" 도러시아가 순진하게 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"우선 무엇보다 불스트로드 씨의 인기 없음이 가장 큰 이유지요. 마을 사람 절반은 그분을 방해하기 위해서라면 거의 수고도 마다하지 않을 겁니다. 이 어리석은 세상에서 대부분의 사람들은 어떤 일이 자기 무리가 하는 일이 아니라면 좋은 일이라고 여기지 않거든요. 저는 이곳에 오기 전에 불스트로드와 아무런 인연이 없었습니다. 저는 그분을 아주 공정하게 봅니다. 그래서 그분에게 어떤 구상들이 있다는 걸, 그분이 어떤 일들을 시작해 놓았다는 걸 압니다. 저는 그것을 훌륭한 공공의 목적으로 돌릴 수 있고요. 만약 교육받은 사람들 가운데 상당수가 자신들의 관찰이 의학 이론과 진료의 개혁에 이바지할 수 있으리라는 믿음을 가지고 일에 나선다면, 우리는 머지않아 더 나은 변화를 보게 될 것입니다. 그게 제 관점입니다. 불스트로드 씨와 함께 일하기를 거부한다면, 저는 제 직업을 더 폭넓게 쓸모 있게 만들 기회에 등을 돌리는 셈이 되리라고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"전적으로 동감이에요.\" 리드게이트의 말이 그려 낸 상황에 단번에 매료된 도러시아가 말했다. \"그런데 불스트로드 씨에게 무슨 흠이 있다는 건가요? 저희 삼촌은 그분과 친하게 지내시는 걸로 아는데요.\"\n\n\"사람들은 그분의 종교적인 어조를 좋아하지 않습니다.\" 리드게이트가 거기서 말을 끊으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그런 반대를 경멸할 이유가 더욱 분명해지는군요.\" 도러시아가 위대한 박해의 빛에 비추어 미들마치의 일을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"공정하게 말씀드리자면, 사람들이 그분에게 다른 불만도 갖고 있긴 합니다. 그분은 위압적이고 다소 비사교적이며, 또 상업에 관여하고 있는데, 거기에는 제가 전혀 모르는 그 나름의 말썽들이 있지요. 하지만 그것이 이 고을에 있는 어떤 병원보다 더 가치 있는 병원을 여기에 세우는 것이 좋은 일인지 아닌지 하는 문제와 무슨 상관이 있겠습니까? 하지만 반대의 직접적인 동기는, 불스트로드가 의료 책임을 제 손에 맡겼다는 사실입니다. 물론 저는 그 점이 기쁩니다. 그것은 제게 훌륭한 일을 할 기회를 주니까요. 그리고 그분이 저를 택한 일이 옳았음을 제가 증명해야 한다는 것도 잘 알고 있습니다. 그런데 그 결과로 미들마치의 의료계 전체가 이 병원에 맹렬히 맞서고 나서서, 자기들이 협력하기를 거부할 뿐 아니라 이 일 전체를 깎아내리고 기부를 방해하려 들고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 옹졸할까요!\" 도러시아가 분개하여 외쳤다.\n\n\"제 길은 싸워서 헤쳐 나가야 한다고 각오해야겠지요. 그런 싸움 없이 이룰 수 있는 일은 거의 없으니까요. 게다가 이 근방 사람들의 무지는 엄청납니다. 저는 그저 누구에게나 주어지지는 않았던 몇 가지 기회를 활용했다는 것 말고는 아무것도 내세우지 않습니다. 하지만 젊다는 것, 외지에서 온 사람이라는 것, 그리고 우연히 오래 산 주민들보다 무언가를 조금 더 안다는 것은 도무지 잠재울 수 없는 괘씸한 죄가 되지요. 그래도 제가 더 나은 치료법을 시작할 수 있다고 믿는다면, 의료 진료에 오래도록 도움이 될 어떤 관찰과 탐구를 이어 갈 수 있다고 믿는다면, 개인적인 안락을 고려해 그것을 가로막도록 내버려둔다면 저는 비열한 아첨꾼이 되고 말 겁니다. 더구나 이 일에는 봉급이 걸려 있지 않아 제 고집을 의심스러운 빛으로 비추지 않으니, 제가 가야 할 길은 한층 더 분명합니다.\"\n\n\"이 이야기를 해 주셔서 기뻐요, 리드게이트 선생님.\" 도러시아가 진심을 담아 말했다. \"제가 조금은 도울 수 있을 거라고 확신해요. 제게는 돈이 좀 있는데 그걸 어떻게 써야 할지 모르겠어요. 그게 종종 마음을 불편하게 하는 생각이지요. 이렇게 훌륭한 목적이라면 일 년에 이백 파운드쯤은 분명히 내놓을 수 있어요. 큰 도움이 될 거라고 확신하는 일을 알고 계시다니, 얼마나 행복하시겠어요! 저도 매일 아침 그런 확신을 가지고 깨어날 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 정작 그 좋은 점은 좀처럼 보이지 않는데도 들이는 수고는 너무나 많은 것 같으니까요!\"\n\n이 마지막 말을 할 때 도러시아의 목소리에는 우울한 가락이 묻어 있었다. 하지만 그녀는 곧 한결 밝은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"부디 로윅에 오셔서 이 일에 관해 더 자세히 이야기해 주세요. 제가 캐소본 씨께 이 문제를 말씀드릴게요. 이제 서둘러 집으로 돌아가야겠어요.\"\n\n그녀는 그날 저녁 정말로 그 이야기를 꺼내며, 일 년에 이백 파운드를 기부하고 싶다고 말했다. 그녀는 결혼할 때 자기 몫의 재산에 해당하는 일 년 칠백 파운드를 받기로 정해져 있었다. 캐소본 씨는 그 액수가 다른 좋은 목적들에 비추어 균형이 맞지 않을지도 모른다고 지나가듯 한마디 했을 뿐 별다른 반대는 하지 않았는데, 도러시아가 그런 사정을 모른 채 그 제안에 맞서자 그는 묵묵히 동의했다. 그 자신은 돈 쓰는 일에 개의치 않았고, 돈을 내놓기를 꺼리지도 않았다. 만약 그가 돈에 관한 어떤 문제로 절실하게 마음을 쓴 적이 있다면, 그것은 물질적 재산에 대한 사랑이 아닌 다른 열정을 매개로 한 것이었다.\n\n도러시아는 그에게 리드게이트를 만났다고 말하고, 병원에 관해 그와 나눈 대화의 요지를 들려주었다. 캐소본 씨는 더 캐묻지 않았으나, 그녀가 리드게이트와 자기 사이에 오간 이야기를 알고 싶어 했으리라고 확신했다. \"내가 안다는 걸 그녀는 알고 있어.\" 늘 안절부절못하는 내면의 목소리가 말했다. 하지만 그렇게 말없는 앎이 늘어날수록 둘 사이의 어떤 신뢰도 점점 더 멀어질 뿐이었다. 그는 그녀의 애정을 의심했다. 그런데 의심보다 더 외로운 고독이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n# CHAPTER XLV.\n\n많은 사람들의 머릿속에는 선조들의 시대를 찬양하고 지금 시대의 사악함을 규탄하는 변덕이 들어 있다. 그럼에도 그들은 지나간 시대의 빌려 온 도움과 풍자가 없이는 그 일을 그럴듯하게 해내지 못한다. 그들은 자기 시대의 악덕을 단죄하되 자신들이 찬양하는 시대의 악덕을 표현한 말을 빌려 쓰는데, 이는 두 시대 모두에 악덕이 공통으로 존재함을 입증할 따름이다. 그러므로 호라티우스도, 유베날리스도, 페르시우스도 예언자가 아니었으니, 그들의 시구가 우리 시대를 가리키고 짚어 내는 듯 보였을지라도 그러하다.—토머스 브라운 경: 『유행하는 거짓 통념들』\n\n리드게이트가 도러시아에게 대략 설명했던 그 새 열병 병원에 대한 반대는, 다른 반대들과 마찬가지로 여러 다른 각도에서 바라볼 수 있는 것이었다. 그는 그것을 질투와 우둔한 편견의 혼합물로 여겼다. 불스트로드 씨는 그 안에서 의학적 질투뿐만 아니라 자신을 방해하려는 결의를 보았는데, 그것은 주로 자신이 유능한 평신도 대표가 되고자 애써 온 그 생생한 신앙에 대한 증오에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 증오는 분명 종교와 무관한 구실들도 찾아냈으니, 인간 행위의 얽힘 속에서는 그런 구실을 찾기가 너무도 쉬웠다. 이런 것들은 성직자적 견해라 부를 만했다. 그러나 반대라는 것은 마음대로 동원할 수 있는 무한한 범위의 이의들을 거느리고 있어서, 지식의 경계에서 멈출 필요가 결코 없으며 무지의 광대함에서 영원히 끌어다 쓸 수 있다. 미들마치의 반대 세력이 새 병원과 그 운영에 대해 한 말에는 분명 메아리가 많이 섞여 있었으니, 하늘은 모든 사람이 창안자가 되지는 못하도록 마련해 두었기 때문이다. 하지만 그 안에는 민친 박사의 세련된 절제로부터 슬로터 레인의 술집 탱커드의 여주인 돌롭 부인의 신랄한 단언에 이르기까지, 온갖 사회적 색조를 대표하는 차이들이 있었다.\n\n돌롭 부인은 자신의 거듭된 주장을 통해 점점 더 확신하게 되었으니, 리드게이트 박사가 사람들을 독살하지는 않더라도 병원에서 죽게 내버려 둘 작정인데, 그래야 양해를 구하거나 구하지 않고서 그들을 해부할 수 있기 때문이라는 것이었다. 그가 팔리 스트리트의 어떤 여인 못지않게 점잖은, 결혼 전 신탁된 재산을 가지고 있던 고비 부인을 해부하려 했다는 것은 누구나 아는 \"사실\"이었으니까. 의사라는 자가 그 모양이라니 한심한 노릇이었다. 의사가 쓸모 있는 자라면 환자가 죽기 전에 무엇이 문제인지 알아야지, 죽은 다음에 속을 들여다보려 들어서는 안 되는 법이었다. 그게 이유가 아니라면 도대체 무엇이 이유냐고 돌롭 부인은 따지고 싶어 했다. 그러나 그녀의 청중들 사이에는 그녀의 의견이 하나의 방벽이며, 그것이 무너진다면 시신 해부에 한계가 없어지리라는 정서가 널리 퍼져 있었으니, 버크와 헤어가 그들의 송진 고약을 가지고 한 짓에서 똑똑히 보지 않았던가. 그런 교수형 감의 일은 미들마치에서는 결코 바라지 않는 바였다!\n\n그리고 슬로터 레인의 탱커드에서의 여론이 의료계에 중요하지 않으리라고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 돌롭이라는 이름으로 알려진 그 유서 깊고 정통적인 술집, 원조 탱커드는 한 거대한 공제 조합의 단골 모임 장소였는데, 그 조합은 몇 달 전에 오랜 기간 그들의 주치의였던 \"갬빗 박사\"를 면직하고 그 대신 \"이 리드게이트 박사\"를 앉혀야 하지 않겠느냐를 두고 표결에 부친 적이 있었다. 리드게이트 박사는 가장 놀라운 치료를 해내고 다른 의사들이 완전히 포기한 사람들을 구해 낼 능력이 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 두 회원에 의해 저울추가 리드게이트에게 불리하게 기울었으니, 그들은 사적인 이유로 죽은 거나 다름없는 사람을 되살리는 그 능력이 모호한 추천 사유라고 여겼고, 섭리에 따른 은총을 방해할지도 모른다고 보았기 때문이다. 그러나 한 해가 지나는 동안 여론에 변화가 생겼고, 돌롭의 술집에서의 만장일치가 그 지표였다.\n\n한 해도 더 전, 리드게이트의 솜씨에 대해 아무것도 알려지지 않았을 때 그에 대한 판단은 자연히 갈렸는데, 그것은 어쩌면 명치께나 송과선에 자리 잡고 있을 개연성의 감각에 좌우되어 그 판결이 제각각이었으나, 증거가 전적으로 결핍된 상황에서는 그래도 길잡이로서 못지않은 가치가 있었다. 만성 질환을 앓거나 늙은 페더스톤처럼 오래도록 생명이 닳아 너덜너덜해진 환자들은 곧장 그를 시험해 보고 싶어 했다. 또한 의사 청구서 내는 것을 싫어하는 많은 이들은 새 의사와 거래를 트는 것을 기껍게 여겼는데, 아이들 성미가 약 한 첩을 필요로 할 때면—그런 경우 옛 의사들은 흔히 퉁명스러웠으므로—마음 놓고 그를 부를 수 있겠다 싶어서였다. 이처럼 리드게이트를 쓰고 싶어 한 사람들은 모두 그가 영리하리라고 여겼다. 어떤 이들은 \"간이 문제일 때\" 그가 남들보다 더 잘할지도 모른다고 생각했다. 적어도 그에게서 \"약\" 몇 병 얻어 둔다고 해될 것은 없었으니, 그것이 쓸모없다고 판명되더라도 정화 환약으로 돌아갈 수 있을 테고, 그 약은 누런 기를 없애 주지는 못해도 목숨은 부지하게 해 주었으니까. 그러나 이들은 별로 중요하지 않은 사람들이었다. 미들마치의 좋은 가문들은 물론 합당한 이유가 드러나지 않는 한 의사를 바꾸려 하지 않았으며, 피콕 씨를 써 온 사람은 누구든 그저 후계자라는 이유만으로 새 사람을 받아들여야 한다고 느끼지 않았으니, 그가 \"피콕에 필적할 것 같지 않다\"고 트집을 잡았다.\n\n그러나 리드게이트가 마을에 온 지 오래지 않아 그에 관한 시시콜콜한 소문이 충분히 퍼져 훨씬 더 구체적인 기대를 낳고 차이를 당파적 대립으로까지 격화시켰으니, 그 시시콜콜한 것들 중에는 비교 기준 없는 통계 수치처럼 의미가 완전히 감춰져 있되 끝에 느낌표가 붙은, 그런 깊은 인상을 주는 부류가 있었다. 다 자란 남자가 한 해에 들이마시는 산소의 세제곱피트 양이라니—그것이 미들마치의 어떤 모임에 얼마나 큰 전율을 불러일으켰을까! \"산소라! 그게 무엇일지 아무도 모르지—콜레라가 단치히까지 퍼진 게 뭐 놀랄 일인가? 그런데도 검역이 아무 소용없다는 사람들이 있다니!\"\n\n빠르게 퍼진 사실들 중 하나는 리드게이트가 약을 조제해 주지 않는다는 것이었다. 이는 자신들만의 고유한 특권이 침해당하는 듯 느낀 내과 의사들에게도, 그리고 리드게이트가 자신을 그 부류에 넣은 외과 겸 약제사들에게도 거슬리는 일이었다. 그리고 얼마 전까지만 해도 그들은, 런던에서 학위를 딴 의학 박사라고 자칭하지도 않으면서 감히 약값 외의 명목으로 보수를 요구하는 자에 맞서 법이 자기들 편이라고 기댈 수 있었다. 그러나 리드게이트는 자신의 새로운 방침이 일반 사람들에게는 더더욱 거슬리리라는 것을 내다볼 만큼 경험이 많지 않았다. 그래서 그의 환자도 아니면서 그 문제에 대해 상냥하게 그에게 물어 온, 윗 시장의 유력한 식료품상 마음지 씨에게, 그는 경솔하게도 자신의 이유를 성급하고 속된 방식으로 설명해 버렸다. 그는 마음지 씨에게, 의사들이 일한 대가를 받는 유일한 방법이 물약과 환약과 혼합제에 대해 긴 청구서를 작성하는 것뿐이라면, 그것은 틀림없이 의사들의 품격을 떨어뜨리고 대중에게 끊임없는 해가 되리라고 지적했다.\n\n\"바로 그런 식으로 부지런히 일하는 의사들이 돌팔이만큼이나 해로운 존재가 될 수 있는 겁니다.\" 리드게이트는 다소 생각 없이 말했다. \"제 밥벌이를 위해 그들은 임금님의 백성들에게 약을 과하게 먹여야 하지요. 그건 못된 종류의 반역입니다, 마음지 씨—치명적인 방식으로 국체를 좀먹는 짓이지요.\"\n\n마음지 씨는 빈민 구호 담당관이었을 뿐 아니라(그가 리드게이트와 면담하고 있던 것은 옥외 구호금에 관한 문제 때문이었다), 천식을 앓고 있었고 식구도 늘어 가고 있었다. 그리하여 의학적 관점에서나 그 자신의 관점에서나 그는 중요한 인물이었다. 실로 예외적인 식료품상이었으니, 머리카락을 불꽃 모양의 피라미드처럼 정돈했고, 소매상 특유의 깍듯함은 다정하고 격려하는 종류여서—농담조로 칭찬을 건네되, 자기 생각의 온전한 힘을 다 드러내지 않는 어떤 사려 깊은 절제가 있었다. 리드게이트의 대답에 그런 어조를 갖게 한 것은 그에게 질문을 던진 마음지 씨의 친근한 농담조였다. 그러나 현명한 자들은 너무 선뜻 설명하려 드는 일을 경계하라. 그것은 오해의 원천을 늘리고, 틀림없이 잘못 셈할 자들을 위해 셈해야 할 액수만 길게 늘려 놓을 뿐이니.\n\n리드게이트는 말을 마치며 등자에 발을 걸고 미소 지었고, 마음지 씨는 임금님의 백성이 누구인지 알았더라면 그랬을 것보다 더 크게 웃으며 \"안녕히 가세요, 선생님, 안녕히 가세요, 선생님\" 하고 모든 것을 명확히 다 이해했다는 듯한 태도로 인사했다. 그러나 사실 그의 생각은 혼란스러웠다. 그는 여러 해 동안 항목이 엄밀히 적힌 청구서로 값을 치러 왔고, 그래서 반 크라운이나 일 실링 육 펜스마다 측정 가능한 무언가가 분명히 제공되었다고 확신해 왔다. 그는 이 일을 만족스럽게 해 왔으며, 그것을 남편이자 아버지로서의 책무에 포함시켰고, 평소보다 긴 청구서를 언급할 만한 위엄으로 여겼다. 게다가 \"본인과 가족\"에게 약이 주는 막대한 혜택에 더하여, 그는 그 즉각적인 효과에 대해 예리한 판단을 내리는 즐거움을 누려 왔으니, 그리하여 갬빗 씨의 지침이 되도록 지적인 진술을 해 줄 수 있었다. 갬빗 씨는 렌치나 톨러보다 지위가 조금 낮은 의사로, 특히 산파술 의사로 존중받았는데, 마음지 씨는 다른 모든 점에서는 그의 능력을 가장 낮게 평가했으나, 진료에 있어서는 그가 누구보다 갬빗을 위에 둔다고 낮은 목소리로 말하곤 했다.\n\n여기에는 새 의사의 피상적인 말보다 더 깊은 이유들이 있었으니, 그 말은 가게 위층 응접실에서 마음지 부인에게 되풀이될 때 한층 더 빈약하게 들렸다. 마음지 부인은 다산의 어머니로서 떠받들어지는 데 익숙한 여인으로—대개 갬빗 씨의 잦은 진료를 받았고, 가끔은 민친 박사가 필요한 발작을 일으키곤 했다.\n\n\"이 리드게이트 씨라는 사람은 약을 먹는 게 아무 소용없다고 말하려는 건가요?\" 약간 느릿느릿 말하는 버릇이 있는 마음지 부인이 말했다. \"장날에 한 달 전부터 미리 강장제를 먹지 않는다면 내가 어떻게 버틸 수 있는지 그 사람이 한번 말해 줬으면 좋겠네요. 손님들을 부르려면 내가 무엇을 마련해야 하는지 생각해 보세요, 여보!\"—여기서 마음지 부인은 곁에 앉은 친한 여자 친구에게로 몸을 돌렸다—\"커다란 송아지 고기 파이며, 속 채운 살코기며, 소고기 등심 한 덩이며, 햄, 혓바닥 고기, 기타 등등, 기타 등등을요! 하지만 나를 가장 잘 버티게 해 주는 건 분홍색 혼합제예요, 갈색이 아니라. 마음지 씨, 당신쯤 되는 경험을 가진 분이 그런 말을 참고 들어 줬다니 이상하네요. 나라면 그 자리에서 내가 그보다는 좀 더 잘 안다고 말해 줬을 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\" 마음지 씨가 말했다. \"나는 그에게 내 의견을 말할 생각이 없었어. 모든 걸 듣고 스스로 판단하라, 그게 내 좌우명이지. 하지만 그는 자기가 누구한테 말하고 있는지 몰랐어. 나는 그자의 손가락에 놀아날 사람이 아니거든. 사람들은 흔히 나한테 뭔가 가르쳐 주는 척하는데, 그럴 바엔 차라리 '마음지, 당신은 바보야'라고 말하는 게 나을 거야. 하지만 나는 그저 빙그레 웃고 말지. 나는 누구나의 약점을 받아 주거든. 약이 나 자신과 가족에게 해를 끼쳤다면 지금쯤은 알아챘을 거야.\"\n\n이튿날 갬빗 씨는 리드게이트가 약이 아무 소용없다고 떠들고 다닌다는 말을 들었다.\n\n\"그래요!\" 그가 조심스러운 놀라움으로 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. (그는 넷째 손가락에 커다란 반지를 낀, 다부지고 쉰 목소리의 사내였다.) \"그럼 그자는 환자를 어떻게 고친답니까?\"\n\n\"제 말이 바로 그거예요.\" 습관적으로 대명사에 힘을 실어 자기 말에 무게를 더하는 마음지 부인이 대꾸했다. \"그자는 사람들이 그저 곁에 와서 앉았다가 도로 가 버리는 값으로 돈을 낼 거라고 생각하는 걸까요?\"\n\n마음지 부인은 갬빗 씨에게서 곁에 앉아 있는 진료를 많이 받았는데, 거기에는 그 자신의 신체 습관이나 기타 일들에 대한 아주 상세한 이야기도 포함되어 있었다. 그러나 물론 그는 그녀의 말에 비꼬는 뜻이 없음을 알았으니, 그의 한가한 시간이나 신상 이야기에는 값이 매겨진 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 익살스럽게 대답했다.\n\n\"음, 리드게이트는 잘생긴 젊은이지요, 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"내가 쓸 사람은 아니에요.\" 마음지 부인이 말했다. \"다른 사람들이야 좋을 대로 하라죠.\"\n\n이리하여 갬빗 씨는 경쟁에 대한 두려움 없이 그 으뜸가는 식료품상의 집을 떠날 수 있었으나, 리드게이트가 자기 정직함을 광고함으로써 남들의 신용을 떨어뜨리려 드는 그런 위선자들 가운데 하나이며, 어떤 사람들에게는 그의 정체를 폭로하는 것이 수고를 들일 만한 일이 될지도 모른다는 느낌이 없지 않았다. 그러나 갬빗 씨는 만족스러운 진료 일감을 가지고 있었는데, 거기에는 현금 지불을 차감 계산으로 줄이는 일을 떠올리게 하는 소매업의 냄새가 짙게 배어 있었다. 그리고 그는 어떻게 폭로할지를 알기 전까지는 리드게이트를 폭로하는 것이 수고를 들일 만한 일이라고 생각하지 않았다. 사실 그는 대단한 교육 밑천이 없어 적잖은 직업적 멸시에 맞서 제 힘으로 길을 헤쳐 와야 했지만, 호흡 기관을 \"허파\"라고 부른다고 해서 산파술 의사로서 못한 것은 결코 아니었다.\n\n다른 의사들은 자신이 더 유능하다고 느꼈다. 톨러 씨는 마을에서 가장 큰 진료 일감을 나눠 가졌으며 오래된 미들마치 가문 출신이었으니, 법조계를 비롯해 소매업 위쪽의 모든 분야에 톨러 집안 사람들이 있었다. 성마른 우리의 친구 렌치와는 달리, 그는 자신을 짜증나게 할 법한 일들을 세상없이 편안하게 받아들이는 사람이었다. 교양 있고 조용히 익살스러운 사내로, 좋은 집을 건사했고, 기회가 닿을 때면 소소한 사냥을 무척 즐겼으며, 홀리 씨와는 아주 친했고 불스트로드 씨에게는 적대적이었다. 그토록 유쾌한 습성을 가진 사람이 영웅적 치료법, 곧 환자에게 피를 뽑고 물집을 잡고 굶기는 치료를 즐겼다는 것이, 그것도 자신의 본보기에는 냉정하게 아랑곳하지 않으면서 그랬다는 것이 이상해 보일지도 모른다. 그러나 그 부조화가 환자들 사이에서 그의 능력에 대한 평판에 유리하게 작용했으니, 환자들은 흔히 톨러 씨가 태도는 게을러도 치료만큼은 누구라도 바랄 만큼 적극적이라고 말했다. 그만큼 자기 직업에 진지함을 쏟아붓는 사람이 없다고들 했다. 오는 것은 좀 굼떴어도 일단 오면 그는 정말 무언가를 했다는 것이다. 그는 자기 모임에서 대단한 인기인이었고, 누군가에게 불리한 암시를 흘릴 때면 그의 무심하고 반어적인 어조 탓에 그 효과가 두 배로 컸다.\n\n피콕 씨의 후계자가 약을 조제할 생각이 없다는 말을 들을 때마다 미소 지으며 \"허!\" 하고 받아넘기는 데 그가 자연히 싫증을 내게 된 것은 당연했다. 그래서 어느 날 만찬 자리에서 햄벗 씨가 포도주를 들며 그 얘기를 꺼냈을 때, 톨러 씨는 웃으며 말했다. \"그럼 디빗츠는 케케묵은 약을 처분하게 되겠군. 나는 그 작은 디빗츠를 좋아하지—그 친구가 운이 트여서 기쁘네.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻인지 알겠소, 톨러.\" 햄벗 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 나도 전적으로 같은 의견이오. 기회가 되는 대로 그 취지로 내 생각을 밝히겠소. 의사는 환자가 복용하는 약의 품질에 대해 책임을 져야 하오. 그것이 지금껏 통용되어 온 청구 제도의 근거지요. 그리고 실질적인 개선이라곤 없으면서 개혁이랍시고 과시하는 것보다 더 거슬리는 일은 없소.\"\n\n\"과시라고요, 햄벗?\" 톨러 씨가 반어적으로 말했다. \"난 그렇게 보지 않소. 아무도 믿지 않는 것을 두고 과시할 수는 없는 노릇이니까. 그 문제에 개혁이란 건 없소. 문제는 약에서 나는 이익을 약제사가 의사에게 지불하느냐 환자가 지불하느냐, 그리고 진료라는 이름으로 별도의 보수가 있어야 하느냐 하는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고. 케케묵은 헛소리를 새 판으로 짜낸 망할 놈의 변종 중 하나지.\" 홀리 씨가 렌치 씨에게 술병을 건네며 말했다.\n\n렌치 씨는 평소 술을 절제하는 편이었으나 모임에서는 곧잘 포도주를 꽤 자유롭게 마셨고, 그 결과 더욱 신경질적이 되었다.\n\n\"헛소리라니, 홀리.\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 함부로 내던지기 쉬운 말이오. 하지만 내가 맞서 싸우는 건, 의사들이 제 둥지를 더럽히고 온 고장에 떠들어 대는 그 방식이오. 마치 약을 조제하는 일반의는 신사가 될 수 없다는 듯이 말이오. 나는 그런 누명을 경멸하며 되받아치겠소. 한 사람이 저지를 수 있는 가장 신사답지 못한 짓은, 자기 직업 동료들 사이에 끼어들어 그들의 유서 깊은 관행을 모욕하는 혁신을 들고 나오는 것이라고 나는 말하겠소. 그게 내 의견이며, 나는 나에게 반박하는 그 누구에 맞서서라도 이를 지킬 각오가 되어 있소.\" 렌치 씨의 목소리는 몹시 날카로워져 있었다.\n\n\"그건 내가 들어줄 수 없겠는걸, 렌치.\" 홀리 씨가 바지 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣으며 말했다.\n\n\"여보게.\" 톨러 씨가 화해시키려는 듯 끼어들며 렌치 씨를 바라보고 말했다. \"우리보다 발등을 더 밟히는 건 내과 의사들이지. 위엄 문제를 따지자면 그건 민친과 스프레이그의 일일세.\"\n\n\"이런 침해에 맞서 의료 법학이 마련해 둔 게 아무것도 없소?\" 햄벗 씨가 자기 식견을 보태려는 사심 없는 욕심에서 말했다. \"법은 어떻게 되어 있소, 응, 홀리?\"\n\n\"거기서는 할 수 있는 게 없소.\" 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"스프레이그를 위해 알아봤었지. 망할 놈의 판사 판결에 코나 깨질 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"흥! 법은 필요 없소.\" 톨러 씨가 말했다. \"진료에 관한 한 그런 시도는 어불성설이오. 어떤 환자도 그걸 좋아하지 않을 테니까—사혈에 익숙해진 피콕의 환자들이야 더 말할 것도 없고. 술이나 돌리시오.\"\n\n톨러 씨의 예언은 부분적으로 들어맞았다. 리드게이트를 쓸 생각이 전혀 없던 마음지 씨 부부조차 약에 반대한다는 그의 추정된 선언에 불안해졌으니, 그를 불러 쓰는 사람들이 그가 그 환자의 경우에 \"쓸 수 있는 모든 수단을 다 쓰는지\" 다소 노심초사하며 지켜보는 것은 불가피한 일이었다. 매사를 너그럽게 해석하는 한결같은 자비심에서 더 나은 방침을 양심적으로 추구하는 듯한 점 때문에 리드게이트를 더욱 높이 평가하는 쪽이었던 선량한 파우더렐 씨조차도, 아내가 단독에 걸렸을 때 의혹으로 마음이 어지러웠다. 그래서 그는 리드게이트에게, 피콕 씨가 비슷한 경우에 환약을 연이어 처방했었는데, 그 환약이란 유난히 무더웠던 팔월에 시작된 병에서 파우더렐 부인을 성 미카엘 축일 전에 일으켜 세운 그 놀라운 효과 말고는 달리 설명할 길이 없는 것이었다고 말하지 않을 수 없었다. 마침내, 리드게이트를 상하게 하고 싶지 않은 마음과 어떤 \"수단\"도 빠뜨리지 말아야 한다는 불안 사이의 갈등 속에서, 그는 아내를 몰래 설득해 위전의 정화 환약을 먹게 했다. 미들마치에서 높이 평가받는 약으로, 곧장 피에 작용함으로써 모든 병을 근원에서 막아 준다는 것이었다. 이 협력적 조치는 리드게이트에게 알려서는 안 되는 것이었고, 파우더렐 씨 자신도 그것을 확실히 믿지는 못했으며, 그저 거기에 축복이 따르기를 바랄 뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 리드게이트가 자리를 잡아 가는 이 미심쩍은 단계에서, 그는 우리 인간이 경솔하게도 행운이라 부르는 것의 도움을 받았다. 짐작건대 어떤 의사도 새로 어딘가에 와서 누군가를 놀라게 하는 치료를 해내지 않고서 자리 잡은 적은 없을 것이다—그런 치료는 행운의 추천서라 부를 만하며, 글로 쓰거나 인쇄된 추천서 못지않은 신뢰를 받을 만하다. 여러 환자가 리드게이트의 진료를 받는 동안 나았으니, 그중에는 위험한 병을 앓던 이들도 있었다. 그래서 새로운 방식을 쓰는 새 의사가 적어도 사람들을 죽음의 문턱에서 되돌려 놓는 공만큼은 있다는 평이 돌았다. 그런 경우에 오가는 허튼소리는 리드게이트에게 더욱 짜증스러웠으니, 그것은 무능하고 양심 없는 자가 바랄 법한 바로 그런 종류의 명성을 안겨 주었고, 또 다른 의사들의 부글거리는 반감 탓에 그가 무지한 허풍을 스스로 부추긴 것으로 틀림없이 그에게 덮어씌워질 것이었기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 자존심 강한 직설조차도, 무지의 해석과 싸우는 것은 안개를 채찍질하는 것만큼이나 부질없다는 통찰 앞에서 멈칫했다. 게다가 \"행운\"은 한사코 그 해석들을 써먹으려 들었다.\n\n라처 부인이 마침 자기네 잡역부에게 나타난 심상찮은 증상을 자비롭게 걱정하던 차에 민친 박사가 들렀고, 그녀는 그 자리에서 바로 그 여자를 봐 달라고, 그리고 진료소에 보낼 진단서를 써 달라고 청했다. 그리하여 진찰을 마친 뒤 그는 그 병을 종양으로 진단하는 소견서를 작성하고, 그것을 지닌 낸시 내시를 외래 환자로 추천했다. 낸시는 진료소로 가는 길에 집에 들러, 자기가 다락방을 세 들어 사는 코르셋 제조공과 그 아내에게 민친 박사의 소견서를 읽게 했고, 그리하여 처치야드 레인의 인근 가게들에서 동정 어린 화젯거리가 되었다. 처음에는 오리 알만큼 크고 단단한 종양이라더니, 그날 하루가 저물어 갈 무렵에는 \"주먹\"만 한 크기라고들 했다. 듣는 이들 대부분은 그것을 잘라 내야 하리라는 데 동의했으나, 한 사람은 기름이, 또 한 사람은 \"코치닐\"이 몸속의 어떤 멍울이든 충분히 안에 넣고 먹으면 무르게 하고 줄어들게 하는 데 효험이 있다고 알고 있었다—기름은 차츰 \"물러지게\" 하고, 코치닐은 갉아먹어 들어가는 식으로 말이다.\n\n한편 낸시가 진료소에 모습을 드러낸 날은 마침 리드게이트가 그곳에 나오는 날 중 하루였다. 그녀에게 이것저것 묻고 진찰한 뒤, 리드게이트는 입주 외과의에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"종양이 아니야. 경련이야.\" 그는 그녀에게 물집 고약과 철분 혼합제를 처방하고, 집에 가서 쉬라고 일렀으며, 동시에 자기가 최고의 고용주라고 한 라처 부인에게 그녀가 좋은 음식이 필요한 처지임을 증명하는 쪽지를 써 주었다.\n\n그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 다락방의 낸시는 심상찮게 더 나빠졌다. 종양으로 추정되던 것이 과연 물집 고약 앞에서 물러가기는 했으나, 그저 더 사나운 통증과 함께 다른 부위로 옮겨 갔을 뿐이었다. 코르셋 제조공의 아내가 리드게이트를 데리러 갔고, 그는 보름 동안 낸시의 집을 다니며 그녀를 돌보았으니, 마침내 그의 치료 아래 그녀는 완전히 나아 다시 일하러 나갔다. 그러나 그 병은 처치야드 레인과 다른 거리들에서 여전히 종양 사례로 이야기되었으며—아니, 라처 부인까지도 그렇게 말했다. 리드게이트의 놀라운 치료가 민친 박사에게 언급되었을 때, 그는 당연히 \"그건 종양이 아니었고, 내가 그렇게 진단한 것은 잘못이었소\"라고 말하고 싶지 않았으므로 이렇게 대답했다. \"그래요! 아! 나는 그게 외과적 사례지 치명적인 종류는 아니라고 봤지요.\" 그러나 그는 이틀 전에 자기가 추천했던 그 여자에 대해 진료소에 물어보았을 때, 거리낌 없이 민친을 골탕 먹이는 것을 마다하지 않는 젊은 입주 외과의로부터 일어난 일을 그대로 전해 듣고는 속으로 언짢았다. 그는 일반의가 그런 식으로 대놓고 내과 의사의 진단을 반박하는 것은 점잖지 못한 짓이라고 사사로이 단정했고, 나중에 렌치와 함께 리드게이트가 예법에 거슬릴 만큼 무신경하다는 데 의견을 같이했다. 리드게이트는 이 일을 가지고 자만하거나 (특별히) 민친을 멸시할 거리로 삼지 않았으니, 이런 오진의 정정은 동등한 자격을 가진 사람들 사이에서도 흔히 일어나는 일이었기 때문이다. 그러나 소문은 암과 명확히 구별되지 않는 이 놀라운 종양 사례를 물고 늘어졌고, 그것이 옮겨 다니는 종류라는 점 때문에 한층 더 무서운 것으로 여겨졌다. 그리하여 단단하고 고집스러운 종양 때문에 고통 속에서 데굴데굴 구르던 낸시 내시를, 그래도 끝내 굴복할 수밖에 없게 만들어 신속히 회복시킨 그의 경이로운 솜씨가 입증되자, 약에 관한 리드게이트의 방식에 대한 많은 편견이 극복되었다.\n\n리드게이트인들 어쩌겠는가? 한 부인이 당신의 솜씨에 놀라움을 표하고 있을 때, 그녀가 완전히 잘못 알고 있으며 그 놀라움이 다소 어리석다고 말하는 것은 무례한 일이다. 그리고 병의 본질에 대해 설명에 들어가는 것은 그의 의료적 예법 위반을 더할 뿐이었으리라. 그리하여 그는 온갖 타당한 자질을 죄다 놓쳐 버리는 그 무지한 칭찬이 안겨 준 성공의 약속 아래서 움찔거려야 했다.\n\n좀 더 눈에 띄는 환자인 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 경우, 리드게이트는 자신이 그저 그런 보통 의사보다 나은 무언가를 보여 주었다는 자각이 있었으나, 여기서도 그가 얻은 것은 모호한 이점이었다. 그 달변의 경매인은 폐렴에 걸렸는데, 피콕 씨의 환자였던지라 리드게이트를 불렀으니, 그는 진작 리드게이트를 후원하겠다는 뜻을 밝혀 둔 터였다. 트럼불 씨는 건장한 사람이라, 흥미로운 병의 경과를 되도록 자연에 맡겨 두고 지켜보면서 앞으로의 지침을 위해 그 단계들을 기록하는 대기 요법을 시험해 보기에 좋은 대상이었다. 그리고 자기 증상을 묘사하는 태도로 보아, 리드게이트는 그가 자신의 의사로부터 신뢰를 받고 자기 자신의 치료에 동업자로 내세워지기를 바라리라고 짐작했다. 경매인은 자신의 체질이 (언제나 적절히 지켜본다는 조건 아래) 자연에 맡겨 두어도 좋을 만한 것이라, 병의 모든 국면이 명확한 윤곽으로 드러나는 아름다운 본보기를 제공할 수 있다는 말을, 또 자신이 자발적으로 합리적 치료의 시험 대상이 되어 자기 폐 기능의 장애를 사회 전체의 이익으로 삼을 만한 보기 드문 정신력을 아마도 지니고 있으리라는 말을 그다지 놀라지 않고 들었다.\n\n트럼불 씨는 즉시 수긍했으며, 자신의 병은 의학에 보통의 기회가 아니라는 견해에 열렬히 찬동했다.\n\n\"염려 마십시오, 선생. 당신은 자연 치유력에 대해 전혀 무지한 사람과 이야기하고 있는 게 아니니까요.\" 그가 늘 그렇듯 격조 있는 말투로 말했는데, 숨쉬기가 힘들어 그 말투가 다소 애처롭게 느껴졌다. 그리고 그는 약을 끊는 것을 움츠림 없이 견뎌 냈으니, 자기 체온의 중요성을 암시해 주는 체온계의 사용과, 자신이 현미경에 들여다볼 대상을 제공한다는 의식, 그리고 자기 분비물의 위엄에 어울려 보이는 많은 새 단어들을 배우는 데서 크게 힘을 얻었다. 리드게이트가 그를 약간의 전문적인 이야기로 즐겁게 해 줄 만큼 약삭빨랐기 때문이다.\n\n트럼불 씨가 침상에서 일어났을 때, 자신이 체질뿐 아니라 정신력까지 발휘한 병에 대해 이야기하고 싶어 했으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하다. 그리고 그는 자기가 상대해야 할 환자의 자질을 알아본 그 의사에게 공을 돌리는 데 인색하지 않았다. 경매인은 인색한 사람이 아니었고, 남에게 마땅한 몫을 주기를 좋아했으니, 자기에게 그럴 여유가 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 그는 \"대기 요법\"이라는 말을 주워들어, 리드게이트가 \"다른 의사들보다 한두 가지는 더 안다—직업의 비밀에 있어서 동료 대다수보다 훨씬 더 정통하다\"는 보증과 더불어, 이 말과 다른 유식한 어구들을 종처럼 울려 댔다.\n\n이 일은 프레드 빈시의 병이 리드게이트에 대한 렌치 씨의 적의에 더 분명한 개인적 근거를 주기 전에 일어났다. 이 새내기는 이미 경쟁의 형태로 골칫거리가 될 조짐을 보였고, 검증되지 않은 관념에 매달리느니 다른 할 일이 있던 고생에 찌든 선배들에 대한 실질적 비판이나 논평의 형태로는 분명 골칫거리였다. 그의 진료는 한두 군데에서 퍼져 나갔고, 처음부터 그의 명문가 출신이라는 소문 덕에 그는 두루 초대를 받았으니, 그리하여 다른 의사들도 좋은 집안의 만찬 자리에서 그를 마주쳐야 했다. 그런데 싫어하는 사람을 마주쳐야 하는 일이 언제나 서로의 애착으로 끝나는 것은 아니라고 흔히들 본다. 리드게이트가 오만한 젊은이라는 의견만큼 그들 사이에 의견 일치를 본 적이 거의 없었으니, 그러면서도 그가 결국 우위를 점하기 위해 불스트로드에게 비굴할 만큼 알랑거릴 준비가 되어 있다는 점에서도 그러했다. 반反불스트로드 파의 주된 깃발과도 같은 이름을 가진 페어브라더 씨가 늘 리드게이트를 변호하고 그를 벗으로 삼은 것은, 양편에서 싸우는 페어브라더 특유의 알 수 없는 방식 탓으로 돌려졌다.\n\n여기에는 불스트로드 씨가 새 병원의 운영을 위해 정해 둔 규칙들이 발표되었을 때 직업적 반감이 폭발할 만한 준비가 충분히 갖춰져 있었다. 그 규칙들은 더욱 격분을 자아냈으니, 그의 의지와 뜻에 당장 간섭할 길이 없었기 때문이다. 메들리코트 경을 제외한 모두가 건물 건립에 도움을 거절했는데, 옛 진료소에 기부하는 쪽을 택했다는 이유에서였다. 불스트로드 씨는 모든 비용을 댔으며, 편견에 사로잡힌 협력자들의 방해 없이 자기 개량 구상을 실행할 권리를 사들이는 셈인 것을 더는 유감으로 여기지 않게 되었다. 그러나 그는 거액을 써야 했고, 건물은 더디게 지어졌다. 케일럽 가스가 그 일을 맡았다가 진행 도중에 실패했고, 내부 마감 공사가 시작되기 전에 사업 관리에서 물러났다. 그리고 병원 이야기가 나오면 그는 흔히, 불스트로드가 시험해 보면 어떻게 쩌렁쩌렁 울려 댈지는 몰라도, 자기는 견실하고 튼튼한 목공과 석공 일을 좋아하며 배수로와 굴뚝에 대해서도 일가견이 있다고 말하곤 했다. 사실 병원은 불스트로드에게 지대한 관심의 대상이 되어 있었고, 그는 어떤 이사회도 없이 독단적으로 그것을 다스리기 위해서라면 기꺼이 매년 거액을 계속 내놓을 용의가 있었다. 그러나 그에게는 그 성취를 위해 역시 돈이 필요한 또 다른 애착의 대상이 있었으니, 미들마치 근방의 땅을 좀 사들이고 싶었던 것이다. 그래서 그는 병원 유지를 위한 상당한 기부금을 얻어 내고자 했다. 한편 그는 운영 계획을 짰다. 병원은 모든 형태의 열병을 위해 전용될 것이었다. 리드게이트가 의무 총책임자가 되어, 그의 연구가, 특히 파리에서의 연구가 그 중요성을 일깨워 준 온갖 비교 조사를 자유로이 추진할 권한을 갖게 하고, 다른 방문 의사들은 자문의 영향력은 가지되 리드게이트의 최종 결정을 뒤집을 권한은 없도록 했다. 그리고 전반적 관리는 불스트로드 씨와 결합된 다섯 이사의 손에 전적으로 맡겨졌으니, 그들은 기부액에 비례하여 표결권을 가졌고, 이사회 스스로가 결원을 채우며, 소액 기부자 무리는 운영에 참여하지 못하도록 했다.\n\n마을의 모든 의사가 즉각 열병 병원의 방문 의사가 되기를 거부했다.\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 리드게이트가 불스트로드 씨에게 말했다. \"우리에게는 명민하고 손놀림이 야무진 훌륭한 입주 외과의 겸 조제사가 있습니다. 크랩슬리에서 웨브를 데려오면 되겠지요. 그들 중 누구 못지않게 유능한 시골 의사니, 일주일에 두 번 건너오게 하고, 무슨 예외적인 수술이 있을 경우엔 브래싱에서 프로서로를 부르면 됩니다. 저야 더 열심히 일하면 되는 거지요, 그뿐입니다. 진료소 자리는 그만두었습니다. 그들이 어쩌든 이 계획은 번창할 것이고, 그러면 그들도 기꺼이 들어오려 할 겁니다. 일이 지금 이대로 갈 수는 없습니다. 머지않아 온갖 개혁이 일어날 테고, 그러면 젊은이들이 여기 와서 기꺼이 공부하려 들 겁니다.\" 리드게이트는 의기양양했다.\n\n\"내가 물러서는 일은 결코 없을 거요, 그것은 믿어도 좋소, 리드게이트 씨.\" 불스트로드 씨가 말했다. \"당신이 높은 뜻을 힘차게 펴 나가는 것을 보는 한, 당신은 나의 변함없는 지지를 받을 거요. 그리고 나는 이 마을의 악의 정신에 맞선 내 노력에 지금껏 함께해 온 축복이 거두어지지 않으리라고 겸허히 확신하오. 나를 도와줄 적절한 이사들을 확보하는 것은 의심치 않소. 팁턴의 브룩 씨가 이미 동의해 주었고, 매년 기부하겠다는 약속도 했소. 액수를 명시하지는 않았는데—아마 큰 액수는 아닐 거요. 하지만 그는 이사회의 쓸모 있는 일원이 될 거요.\"\n\n쓸모 있는 일원이란 어쩌면 아무것도 발의하지 않으면서 늘 불스트로드 씨에게 표를 던지는 사람이라고 정의할 수 있으리라.\n\n이제 리드게이트에 대한 의료계의 반감은 거의 감춰지지 않았다. 스프레이그 박사도 민친 박사도 리드게이트의 지식이나 치료를 개선하려는 그의 성향을 싫어한다고는 말하지 않았다. 그들이 싫어한 것은 그의 오만이었으니, 그것은 아무도 전적으로 부인할 수 없다고 느끼는 바였다. 그들은 그가 무례하고 잘난 체하며, 돌팔이의 본질인 그 소란과 과시를 위한 무모한 혁신에 빠져 있다고 넌지시 일렀다.\n\n돌팔이라는 말이 일단 허공에 던져지자 그것은 떨어진 채로 둘 수가 없었다. 그 무렵 세상은 세인트 존 롱 씨의 경이로운 행적으로 떠들썩했으니, \"귀족과 신사들\"이 그가 환자의 관자놀이에서 수은 같은 액체를 뽑아냈다고 증언하고 있었다.\n\n톨러 씨는 어느 날 태프트 부인에게 웃으며 말했다. \"불스트로드가 리드게이트라는, 자기한테 딱 맞는 사람을 찾아낸 거지요. 종교의 돌팔이는 다른 종류의 돌팔이도 으레 좋아하게 마련이니까요.\"\n\n\"네, 정말, 그럴 만하지요.\" 그러는 내내 서른 코의 뜨개질 수를 머릿속에 조심스레 세고 있던 태프트 부인이 말했다. \"그런 부류가 어찌나 많은지요. 전능하신 하느님이 굽게 만드신 사람들을, 쇠붙이로 곧게 펴 보겠다던 체셔 씨가 기억나네요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요.\" 톨러 씨가 말했다. \"체셔는 멀쩡했어요—다 정당하고 떳떳했지요. 하지만 세인트 존 롱 같은 자—그게 바로 우리가 돌팔이라고 부르는 부류예요. 아무도 영문을 모르는 방식으로 치료를 광고하고, 남들보다 더 깊이 파고드는 척해서 소란을 피우려는 자 말입니다. 며칠 전에는 사람 뇌를 두드려서 수은을 뽑아낸다고 우겼다지 뭡니까.\"\n\n\"세상에! 사람의 몸을 가지고 무슨 그런 끔찍한 장난을!\" 태프트 부인이 말했다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤로, 리드게이트가 점잖은 사람들의 몸까지 제 목적을 위해 가지고 논다는 생각이 여기저기서 굳어졌고, 그렇다면 그가 변덕스러운 실험을 하면서 병원 환자들을 엉망진창으로 만들 가능성은 또 얼마나 크겠는가. 특히 탱커드의 여주인이 말했듯이, 그가 무모하게 그들의 시신을 해부하리라는 것은 충분히 예상할 만한 일이었다. 리드게이트가 고비 부인을 돌보았는데, 그녀는 증상에 그리 명확히 드러나지 않은 심장병으로 죽은 듯했고, 그가 너무 대담하게도 유족에게 시신 해부를 허락해 달라고 청한 탓에, 그 일은 팔리 스트리트 너머로 빠르게 번지는 반감을 자아냈다. 그 부인은 오래도록 그곳에서 상당한 수입으로 살아왔으므로, 그녀의 시신을 버크와 헤어의 희생자들과 한데 엮는 것은 그녀의 명예에 대한 노골적인 모욕이었다.\n\n리드게이트가 도러시아에게 병원 문제를 꺼냈을 때 사정은 이런 단계에 있었다. 우리는 그가 적의와 어리석은 오해를 상당한 기개로 견뎌 내고 있었음을, 그리고 그것들이 어느 정도는 자신의 큰 성공에서 비롯되었음을 알고 있었음을 본다.\n\n\"그들이 나를 쫓아내지는 못할 겁니다.\" 그가 페어브라더 씨의 서재에서 흉금을 터놓고 이야기하며 말했다. \"여기서 나는 내가 가장 마음 쓰는 목적을 위해 좋은 기회를 잡았습니다. 그리고 우리에게 필요한 만큼의 수입은 거의 틀림없이 얻을 수 있을 겁니다. 머지않아 나는 되도록 조용히 일을 이어 갈 겁니다. 지금은 집과 일에서 나를 떼어 놓을 유혹이 없으니까요. 그리고 모든 조직의 기원이 동일하다는 것을 입증하는 일이 가능하리라는 확신이 점점 더 굳어집니다. 라스파이와 다른 이들도 같은 길을 가고 있는데, 나는 시간을 허비해 왔어요.\"\n\n\"그 점에 대해서는 내게 예언할 능력이 없네.\" 리드게이트가 말하는 동안 생각에 잠겨 파이프를 빨고 있던 페어브라더 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 마을의 적의에 관해서라면, 자네가 신중하기만 하다면 그건 견뎌 낼 걸세.\"\n\n\"제가 어떻게 신중해야 합니까?\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"저는 그저 제 앞에 닥친 일을 할 뿐입니다. 베살리우스가 그랬듯이, 저로서도 사람들의 무지와 악의를 어쩔 도리가 없습니다. 아무도 내다볼 수 없는 어리석은 결론에 자기 행동을 맞춘다는 건 불가능한 일이에요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말일세. 그런 뜻이 아니었네. 내 말은 단지 두 가지였네. 하나는, 불스트로드와는 되도록 떼어 놓을 수 있는 채로 자네 자신을 지키게. 물론 그의 도움으로 자네만의 훌륭한 일을 계속해 나갈 수야 있겠지. 하지만 묶이지는 말게. 어쩌면 이렇게 말하는 것이 내 사사로운 감정처럼 보일지도 모르고—사실 그런 게 상당히 있다는 건 인정하네—하지만 사사로운 감정도 그것을 그저 하나의 의견으로 만들어 주는 인상들로 끓여서 졸여 내면 늘 틀린 것만은 아니라네.\"\n\n\"불스트로드는 저에게 아무것도 아닙니다.\" 리드게이트가 무심하게 말했다. \"공적인 근거에서가 아니라면요. 그와 아주 긴밀히 결합되는 것으로 말하자면, 저는 그러기에는 그를 그리 좋아하지 않습니다. 그런데 선생님이 말씀하신 다른 한 가지는 무엇이었습니까?\" 다리를 되도록 편안하게 감싸 안고서 별로 충고가 필요하다고 느끼지 않던 리드게이트가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 이걸세. 조심하게—경험자의 말을 믿게—돈 문제로 발이 묶이지 않도록 조심하게. 언젠가 자네가 흘린 한마디로, 자네가 내가 돈을 걸고 카드놀이를 그렇게 많이 하는 걸 못마땅해한다는 걸 안다네. 그 점에선 자네가 옳네. 하지만 자네가 가지지 못한 적은 돈을 아쉬워하는 일이 없도록 애써 보게. 어쩌면 내가 쓸데없는 말을 하고 있는지도 모르지. 하지만 사람이란 제 나쁜 본보기를 치켜들고 그것을 가지고 설교함으로써 자기 자신에 대해 우월감을 누리고 싶어 하는 법이거든.\"\n\n리드게이트는 페어브라더 씨의 충고를 아주 정답게 받아들였으니, 다른 사람에게서였다면 좀처럼 견디지 못했을 것이다. 그는 자기가 근래에 빚을 좀 졌다는 것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으나, 그 빚은 불가피해 보였고, 이제 그는 검소하게 살림을 꾸려 가는 것 이상은 할 생각이 없었다. 빚을 진 가구는 새로 장만할 필요가 없을 것이고, 포도주 재고도 한참 동안은 그러할 것이었다.\n\n그 무렵 많은 생각들이 그에게 기운을 북돋아 주었으니—마땅한 일이었다. 가치 있는 목표에 대한 열정을 자각하는 사람은, 상처를 입어 가며 길을 헤쳐 나가야 했던, 그리고 그의 마음속에서 수호성인처럼 떠돌며 보이지 않게 도와주는 위대한 일꾼들의 기억에 힘입어 사소한 적의들을 견뎌 낸다. 페어브라더 씨와 담소를 나눈 바로 그날 저녁, 집에서 그는 즐겨 하는 사색의 자세대로 긴 다리를 소파에 뻗고 머리를 뒤로 젖힌 채 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 끼고 있었다. 그동안 로저먼드는 피아노 앞에 앉아 이런저런 곡을 잇따라 연주했는데, 그녀의 남편은 (그가 다정다감한 코끼리답게!) 그저 그 곡들이 마치 가락 고운 바닷바람처럼 자기 기분에 맞아떨어진다는 것만 알 뿐이었다.\n\n바로 그때 리드게이트의 모습에는 무언가 매우 멋진 데가 있어서, 누구라도 그의 성취에 기꺼이 돈을 걸 만했다. 그의 검은 눈과 입가와 이마에는 사색적 사유의 충만함에서 오는 그 평온함이 어려 있었으니—탐색하지 않고 바라보는 정신, 그리고 그 뒤에 있는 것으로 가득 찬 듯한 눈길이었다.\n\n이윽고 로저먼드는 피아노를 떠나 소파 가까이, 남편의 얼굴 맞은편 의자에 앉았다.\n\n\"이제 음악은 충분하신가요, 나리?\" 그녀가 두 손을 앞으로 모으고 약간 순종하는 척하는 태도를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 여보, 당신이 피곤하다면.\" 리드게이트가 다정하게 말하며, 다른 데는 꼼짝 않고 눈길만 돌려 그녀에게 머물렀다. 그 순간 로저먼드의 존재는 어쩌면 호수에 떠 온 한 숟갈의 물에 지나지 않았는데, 이 일에 관한 그녀의 여자다운 직감은 둔하지 않았다.\n\n\"무엇에 그리 빠져 계세요?\" 그녀가 몸을 앞으로 기울여 얼굴을 그의 얼굴 가까이 가져가며 말했다.\n\n그는 두 손을 풀어 그녀의 어깨 뒤에 가만히 갖다 대었다.\n\n\"삼백 년 전, 지금의 나만 한 나이였을 때 이미 해부학에 새 시대를 연 한 위대한 사람을 생각하고 있었소.\"\n\n\"못 맞히겠어요.\" 로저먼드가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"레먼 부인 댁에서 역사적 인물 알아맞히기 놀이는 했지만, 해부학자는 안 했거든요.\"\n\n\"내가 말해 주지. 그의 이름은 베살리우스였소. 그가 그렇게 해부학을 알게 된 유일한 길은, 밤에 묘지와 처형장에서 시신을 훔쳐 오는 것이었지.\"\n\n\"어머!\" 로저먼드가 예쁜 얼굴에 역겨운 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"당신이 베살리우스가 아니라서 정말 다행이에요. 그보다는 덜 끔찍한 방법을 찾아낼 수도 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럴 수 없었소.\" 리드게이트가 너무 열중한 나머지 그녀의 대답에는 별로 주의를 기울이지 않은 채 말을 이었다. \"완전한 골격을 얻을 수 있는 유일한 방법은 교수대에서 죄인의 허옇게 바랜 뼈를 훔쳐다 묻어 두고, 깊은 밤에 몰래 조금씩 가져오는 것뿐이었지.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 당신의 위대한 영웅들 가운데 하나가 아니길 바라요.\" 로저먼드가 반은 장난스럽게, 반은 걱정스럽게 말했다. \"안 그러면 당신이 한밤중에 일어나 성 베드로 묘지로 가게 될 테니까요. 사람들이 고비 부인 일로 얼마나 화가 났었는지 당신이 말해 줬잖아요. 당신은 이미 적이 충분히 많아요.\"\n\n\"베살리우스도 그랬소, 로지. 살아 있는 가장 위대한 의사들 가운데 일부가 베살리우스에게 사납게 굴었던 걸 생각하면, 미들마치의 케케묵은 의사 영감들이 질투하는 것도 놀랄 일이 아니지. 그들은 갈레노스를 믿고 있었는데, 베살리우스가 갈레노스가 틀렸다는 걸 보여 줬거든. 그들은 그를 거짓말쟁이요 독을 품은 괴물이라고 불렀지. 하지만 인체의 사실들이 그의 편이었고, 그래서 그는 그들을 이겨 냈소.\"\n\n\"그 뒤에 그는 어떻게 됐어요?\" 로저먼드가 약간 흥미를 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그는 끝까지 숱한 싸움을 치렀지. 그리고 한때는 그들이 그를 어찌나 격분시켰던지 그가 자기 저작의 상당 부분을 불태워 버렸을 정도였소. 그러고는 예루살렘에서 파도바의 큰 교수직을 맡으러 오던 길에 그만 배가 난파되었지. 그는 다소 비참하게 죽었소.\"\n\n로저먼드가 입을 열기까지 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. \"있잖아요, 터티어스, 나는 당신이 의사가 아니었더라면 하고 자주 바라요.\"\n\n\"아니, 로지, 그런 말 말아요.\" 리드게이트가 그녀를 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다. \"그건 당신이 다른 남자와 결혼했더라면 좋았겠다고 말하는 거나 마찬가지요.\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아요. 당신은 무엇이든 할 만큼 영리하니까, 얼마든지 다른 게 될 수도 있었을 거예요. 그리고 콸링엄에 있는 당신 사촌들도 모두, 당신이 그런 직업을 택해서 그들보다 아래로 떨어졌다고 생각하잖아요.\"\n\n\"콸링엄의 사촌들이야 악마한테나 가라지!\" 리드게이트가 경멸조로 말했다. \"그자들이 당신에게 그런 말을 했다면 그건 뻔뻔스러운 짓이오.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"나는 그게 멋진 직업이라고는 생각하지 않아요, 여보.\" 우리는 그녀가 자기 의견에 조용한 끈기를 상당히 지니고 있었음을 안다.\n\n\"그건 세상에서 가장 위대한 직업이오, 로저먼드.\" 리드게이트가 진지하게 말했다. \"그리고 내 안에 있는 의사는 사랑하지 않으면서 나를 사랑한다고 말하는 건, 복숭아 먹는 건 좋아하지만 그 맛은 싫다고 말하는 거나 마찬가지요. 다시는 그런 말 말아요, 여보. 그건 나를 아프게 하니까.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 근엄한 얼굴의 박사님.\" 로지가 보조개를 지으며 말했다. \"앞으로는 내가 해골이며, 시체 도둑이며, 약병에 든 것 쪼가리며, 비참하게 죽는 걸로 끝나는 모든 사람과의 다툼을 끔찍이 사랑한다고 선언할게요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 그렇게까지는 아니오.\" 리드게이트가 항변을 그만두고 체념한 듯 그녀를 어르며 말했다.\n\n# 제43장\n\n우리는 좋아하는 것을 얻을 수 없으니, 얻을 수 있는 것을 좋아하기로 하자.—스페인 속담*\n\n* 본문 첫머리의 스페인어와 영어는 같은 속담의 원어와 번역으로, \"우리는 우리가 원하는 것을 가질 수 없으니, 우리가 가질 수 있는 것을 원하자\"라는 뜻이다.\n\n리드게이트가 안전하게 결혼하고 병원을 자기 지휘 아래 둔 채 미들마치를 상대로 의료 개혁을 위해 분투하고 있다고 느끼는 동안, 미들마치는 또 다른 종류의 개혁을 위한 전국적 투쟁을 점점 더 의식하게 되었다.\n\n존 러셀 경의 법안이 하원에서 토의되고 있을 무렵, 미들마치에는 새로운 정치적 활기가 일었고, 새로운 선거가 치러진다면 세력의 뚜렷한 변화를 보여 줄 만한 새로운 정당 구분이 생겨났다. 그리고 이미 이런 일을 예언하며, 개혁 법안이 현 의회에서는 결코 통과되지 못할 것이라고 단언하는 이들도 있었다. 윌 래디슬로가 브룩 씨에게, 그가 아직 유세장에서 자기 힘을 시험해 보지 않은 것이 다행스러운 일이라며 강조한 것도 바로 이 점이었다.\n\n\"마치 혜성이 나타나는 해처럼 사태가 자라나고 무르익을 겁니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"개혁 문제가 본격화되었으니, 대중의 정서가 곧 혜성처럼 뜨겁게 달아오를 거예요. 머지않아 또 한 차례 선거가 있을 가능성이 높고, 그때쯤이면 미들마치도 머릿속에 더 많은 생각을 담게 될 겁니다. 지금 우리가 매달려야 할 일은 『파이어니어』와 정치 집회예요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말이네, 래디슬로. 우리는 여기서 여론을 새로운 것으로 만들어 낼 걸세.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"다만 나는 개혁에 관해서는 독자적인 입장을 지키고 싶네, 알겠나. 너무 멀리 나가고 싶지는 않아. 윌버포스와 로밀리의 노선을 따라, 흑인 해방이라든가 형법 같은 것에 힘을 쏟고 싶단 말일세. 하지만 물론 그레이는 지지해야겠지.\"\n\n\"개혁의 원칙을 지지하신다면, 상황이 내놓는 것을 받아들일 준비도 하셔야 합니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"모두가 저마다 제 몫을 챙기려고 서로 잡아당긴다면, 문제 전체가 갈가리 찢어지고 말 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 자네 말에 동의하네. 나도 그 관점을 충분히 받아들여. 나라면 그런 식으로 표현하겠지. 그레이를 지지하겠네, 알다시피. 하지만 헌정의 균형을 바꾸고 싶지는 않고, 그레이도 그러지는 않으리라고 보네.\"\n\n\"하지만 나라가 원하는 것이 바로 그것입니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"그렇지 않다면 정치 동맹이나 자기가 무엇을 하는지 아는 다른 어떤 운동도 무의미할 거예요. 나라는 지주 계급의 지명자들로 채워진 하원이 아니라, 다른 이해 집단의 대표들로 이루어진 하원을 원합니다. 그리고 거기에 못 미치는 개혁을 두고 다투는 것은, 이미 천둥처럼 굉음을 내며 흘러내리기 시작한 눈사태에서 한 조각만 떼어 달라고 요구하는 것과 같아요.\"\n\n\"훌륭하군, 래디슬로. 바로 그렇게 표현해야지. 자, 그걸 적어 두게. 우리는 기계 파괴나 전반적인 궁핍에 관한 것뿐 아니라 나라의 정서에 관한 자료도 모으기 시작해야 하네.\"\n\n\"자료라면,\" 윌이 말했다. \"가로세로 2인치짜리 카드 한 장이면 충분히 담을 수 있습니다. 몇 줄의 숫자만 있으면 거기서 빈곤을 추론하기에 족하고, 몇 줄을 더하면 국민의 정치적 결의가 자라나는 속도를 보여 줄 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그걸 좀 더 길게 풀어내 보게, 래디슬로. 자, 그게 바로 착상이야. 『파이어니어』에 써 보게. 숫자를 제시하고 빈곤을 추론하고, 알겠나. 또 다른 숫자를 제시하고 추론하고, 그런 식으로 말일세. 자네는 사물을 표현하는 재주가 있어. 자, 버크를 생각하면—버크를 떠올릴 때면, 누군가 자네에게 줄 수 있는 매수 선거구라도 하나 있었으면 하는 바람을 떨칠 수가 없네, 래디슬로. 자네는 도무지 당선될 사람이 아니거든, 알다시피. 그런데 우리는 의회에 늘 인재를 필요로 할 걸세. 아무리 개혁을 한다 해도, 우리는 늘 인재가 필요하단 말이지. 자, 그 눈사태와 천둥, 그건 정말이지 버크와 좀 닮았어. 나는 그런 것을 원해—착상이 아니라, 알겠나, 그걸 표현하는 방식 말일세.\"\n\n\"매수 선거구도 좋은 것이겠지요.\" 래디슬로가 말했다. \"그것이 언제나 올바른 주머니 속에 들어 있고, 언제나 가까이에 버크가 있다면 말입니다.\"*\n\n* 매수 선거구(pocket-borough): 선거구가 사실상 한 지주의 '주머니' 안에 있어 그의 뜻대로 의원을 앉힐 수 있던 부패한 선거구. 버크(Edmund Burke, 1729~1797)는 이런 선거구를 통해 의회에 진출한 대정치가다.\n윌은 비록 브룩 씨에게서 나온 것일지언정 그런 찬사 어린 비교가 싫지 않았다. 자기가 남들보다 더 잘 표현한다는 것을 스스로 의식하면서도 아무도 그것을 알아주지 않는다는 것은 인간의 본성으로서는 견디기에 다소 가혹한 일이고, 올바른 것에 대한 감탄이 전반적으로 메말라 있는 가운데, 우연히 박자에 딱 맞아떨어지는 한 가닥의 칭찬 소리는 오히려 사람을 다소 든든하게 해 주기 때문이다. 윌은 자기의 문학적 세련됨이 대개 미들마치의 인식 범위를 넘어선다고 느꼈다. 그럼에도 그는, 처음 시작할 때 다소 나른하게 \"안 될 것도 없지?\"라고 스스로에게 말했던 그 일을 점차 진심으로 좋아하게 되었고, 일찍이 시의 운율이나 중세 연구에 쏟았던 것 못지않게 열렬한 관심을 가지고 정치 상황을 연구했다. 도러시아가 있는 곳에 있고 싶다는 욕망이 없었다면, 그리고 어쩌면 달리 무엇을 해야 할지 몰랐던 탓이 아니었다면, 윌이 이 무렵 영국 국민의 필요에 대해 사색하거나 영국의 정치력을 비판하고 있지는 않았으리라는 것은 부인할 수 없다. 그랬다면 그는 아마도 이탈리아를 떠돌면서 여러 편의 희곡 구상을 스케치하고, 산문을 시도하다가 너무 무미건조하다고 여기고, 운문을 시도하다가 너무 인위적이라고 여기고, 옛 그림에서 \"부분\"을 베끼기 시작하다가 \"별 볼 일 없다\"고 그만두고, 결국 무엇보다 중요한 것은 자기 수양이라고 생각하고 있었을 것이다. 한편 정치에 있어서는 자유와 진보 일반에 대해 따뜻하게 공감하고 있었으리라. 우리의 의무감은 흔히 호사가적 취미를 대신할 어떤 일을, 우리의 행위의 질이 아무래도 좋은 문제가 아님을 느끼게 해 줄 어떤 일을 기다려야만 한다.\n\n래디슬로는 이제 자기 몫의 일을 받아들였다. 비록 그것이 한때 그가 지속적인 노력을 바칠 가치가 있는 유일한 것이라고 꿈꾸었던, 그 막연하고 더없이 고결한 무엇은 아니었지만 말이다. 그의 천성은 삶과 행동에 눈에 띄게 뒤섞인 주제 앞에서 쉽게 달아올랐고, 그의 안에 쉽사리 일어나는 반항심은 공공 정신의 열기를 북돋웠다. 캐소본 씨와 로윅으로부터의 추방에도 불구하고, 그는 오히려 행복한 편이었다. 새로운 지식을 생생하게, 그리고 실용적인 목적에 맞게 잔뜩 얻고 있었고, 『파이어니어』를 브래싱에 이르기까지 유명하게 만들고 있었으니 말이다(그 범위가 좁다고 해서 개의치 말 것. 그 글은 세상의 사방 구석구석까지 닿는 많은 글들보다 못하지 않았다).\n\n브룩 씨는 이따금 사람을 짜증나게 했다. 하지만 윌의 조바심은, 그가 그레인지를 방문하는 시간과 미들마치의 자기 하숙으로 물러나는 시간으로 나뉘어 있던 덕분에 누그러졌으니, 이것이 그의 생활에 변화를 주었다.\n\n\"못을 조금만 옮겨 박으면,\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"브룩 씨가 내각에 들어가고 내가 차관이 될 수도 있겠지. 그게 흔히 돌아가는 이치야. 작은 물결이 큰 물결을 만들고, 둘은 같은 무늬를 지녔으니까. 캐소본 씨가 나를 길들이려 했던 그런 삶보다는 여기가 낫다. 거기서라면 모든 일이 너무 경직된 전례에 따라 정해져 있어서 내가 거기에 반응할 여지조차 없었을 테니. 나는 명망이나 높은 보수 따위에는 관심 없어.\"\n\n리드게이트가 그에 대해 말했듯이, 그는 일종의 집시 같았고, 어느 계급에도 속하지 않는다는 느낌을 오히려 즐겼다. 그는 자기 처지에 낭만의 정취를 느꼈고, 가는 곳마다 약간의 놀라움을 일으킨다는 것을 유쾌하게 의식했다. 그런 즐거움은, 리드게이트의 집에서 우연히 도러시아를 만났을 때 그녀와 자기 사이에 어떤 새로운 거리감을 느끼면서 흐트러졌고, 그의 짜증은 캐소본 씨를 향했다. 윌이 신분을 잃게 될 것이라고 미리 단언했던 사람 말이다. \"나는 애초에 신분이라는 게 없었어.\" 만약 그 예언이 그에게 직접 발설되었더라면 그는 이렇게 말했을 것이고, 빠른 핏기가 그의 투명한 피부 위로 숨결처럼 오갔을 것이다. 그러나 반항을 좋아하는 것과 그 결과를 좋아하는 것은 별개의 일이다.\n\n한편 『파이어니어』의 새 편집인에 대한 읍내의 여론은 캐소본 씨의 견해를 굳히는 쪽으로 기울고 있었다. 그 명망 높은 집안과의 윌의 인척 관계는, 리드게이트의 높은 연줄과 달리 유리한 소개장 구실을 하지 못했다. 젊은 래디슬로가 캐소본 씨의 조카니 사촌이니 하는 소문이 돌면, \"캐소본 씨가 그와는 상종하려 하지 않는다\"는 소문도 함께 돌았던 것이다.\n\n\"브룩이 그자를 거둔 거지.\" 홀리 씨가 말했다. \"그게 바로 제정신인 사람이라면 아무도 예상치 못할 일이니까. 캐소본이 저가 양육비를 대 준 젊은이를 냉대하는 데는 분명 지독히도 그럴 만한 이유가 있을 거요. 딱 브룩다운 짓이지—말 한 마리 팔아먹으려고 고양이를 칭찬할 그런 작자들 중 하나란 말이오.\"\n\n그리고 다소간 시적인 윌의 몇몇 기벽은, 『트럼펫』의 편집인 케크 씨가 래디슬로에 대해 단언한 바를 뒷받침해 주는 듯했다. 즉 진실을 알고 보면 래디슬로는 폴란드의 밀정일 뿐 아니라 머리가 돈 자라는 것, 그래서 그가 연단에 오르기만 하면—기회가 있을 때마다 그는 그렇게 했는데—말이 그토록 초자연적으로 빠르고 유창하게 쏟아져 나오고, 그 능변이 견실한 영국인 전반에 그늘을 드리운다는 것이었다. 케크로서는, 머리에 옅은 곱슬머리가 둘린 풋내기 한 명이 일어서서 \"제가 요람에 있을 때 이미 존재하던\" 제도들을 향해 몇 시간씩 일장연설을 늘어놓는 꼴을 보는 것이 역겨웠다. 그리고 케크는 『트럼펫』의 한 사설에서, 어느 개혁 집회에서 행한 래디슬로의 연설을 두고 \"악령 들린 자의 광란—불꽃놀이의 현란함 속에 무책임한 주장의 만용과, 가장 값싸고 가장 최근의 부류에 속하는 지식의 빈곤을 가려 보려는 가련한 시도\"라고 규정했다.\n\n\"어제 그 사설은 아주 신나는 글이었소, 케크.\" 빈정대려는 의도로 스프레이그 박사가 말했다. \"그런데 '악령 들린 자'라는 게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"아, 프랑스 혁명 때 나온 용어요.\" 케크가 말했다.\n\n래디슬로의 이런 위험한 면모는, 사람들 입에 오르내리게 된 다른 습관들과 묘하게 대조를 이루었다. 그에게는 반은 예술적이고 반은 다정한, 어린아이들을 향한 애정이 있었다. 아이가 어지간히 활발히 걸어 다닐 만한 다리를 지니고서도 몸집이 작을수록, 그리고 옷차림이 우스꽝스러울수록, 윌은 그 아이를 놀래 주고 기쁘게 해 주는 것을 더 좋아했다. 로마에서 그가 가난한 사람들 사이를 곧잘 어슬렁거렸다는 것을 우리는 알고 있거니와, 그 취향은 미들마치에서도 그를 떠나지 않았다.\n\n그는 어찌어찌하여 우스꽝스러운 아이들 한 무리를 거두게 되었다. 모자도 안 쓰고 무릎바지가 닳을 대로 닳은, 셔츠 자락도 변변히 빼낼 게 없는 사내아이들, 그를 보려고 눈가의 머리카락을 휙 넘기는 계집아이들, 그리고 일곱 살이라는 성숙한 나이에 보호자 노릇을 하는 오빠들이었다. 그는 이 무리를 이끌고 도토리철이면 핼셀 숲으로 집시처럼 소풍을 나갔고, 추운 날씨가 시작된 뒤로는 맑은 날을 골라 산비탈 우묵한 곳에서 모닥불을 피울 나뭇가지를 모으러 데리고 갔는데, 거기서 아이들에게 생강 과자로 조촐한 잔치를 베풀고, 손수 만든 인형 몇 개로 즉석에서 펀치와 주디 인형극을 펼쳐 보였다.* 이것이 하나의 기벽이었다. 또 하나는, 그가 친해진 집에서는 이야기를 나누는 동안 난롯가 깔개 위에 길게 드러눕기를 즐겨서, 이따금 찾아오는 손님들에게 그런 자세로 발견되곤 했다는 것인데, 그런 손님들로서는 이런 무례함이 그의 위험하게 뒤섞인 핏줄과 전반적인 방종에 대한 관념을 굳히기 십상이었다.\n\n* 펀치와 주디(Punch-and-Judy): 영국의 전통 손인형 희극.\n하지만 윌의 기고문과 연설은, 새로운 당파 구분의 엄정함이 개혁 쪽으로 갈라놓은 가문들에서 그를 자연스럽게 환영받게 했다. 그는 벌스트로드 씨 댁에 초대받았다. 하지만 거기서는 깔개 위에 드러누울 수 없었고, 벌스트로드 부인은 그가 가톨릭 국가들에 대해 마치 적그리스도와 무슨 휴전이라도 있을 수 있다는 듯이 이야기하는 방식이, 지식인들에게 흔한 그 불건전한 경향을 잘 보여 준다고 느꼈다.\n\n그러나 사태의 아이러니로 인해 이 전국적 운동에서 벌스트로드와 같은 편에 서게 된 페어브러더 씨 댁에서는, 윌이 부인들의 총애를 받게 되었다. 특히 자그마한 노블 양에게 그러했는데, 그녀가 작은 바구니를 들고 거리를 지날 때 마주치면 그녀를 호위하는 것이 그의 기벽 중 하나였다. 그는 온 읍내가 보는 앞에서 그녀에게 팔을 내밀고, 그녀가 자기 몫의 단것에서 슬쩍 챙겨 둔 자그마한 것들을 나누어 주러 어딘가로 방문 가는 길에 굳이 따라나서곤 했다.\n\n하지만 그가 가장 자주 방문하고 깔개 위에 가장 많이 드러누운 집은 리드게이트의 집이었다. 두 사람은 조금도 닮지 않았지만, 그렇다고 사이가 나쁘지도 않았다. 리드게이트는 무뚝뚝하긴 해도 신경질적이지는 않아서, 건강한 사람들의 변덕에는 별로 신경 쓰지 않았다. 그리고 래디슬로는 보통 자기의 예민함을 그것에 무심한 사람들에게 함부로 낭비하지 않았다. 반면 로저먼드에게는 입을 삐죽이며 변덕을 부렸고—아니, 그녀가 속으로 적잖이 놀랄 만큼 종종 무례하기까지 했다. 그럼에도 그는 점차 그녀의 소일거리에 없어서는 안 될 존재가 되어 갔으니, 그녀의 음악에 동무가 되어 주고, 다채로운 이야기를 들려주고, 또 그 진지한 골몰함—남편이 아무리 다정하고 너그럽게 대해 주어도 그 골몰함이 종종 그의 태도를 그녀에게 불만스럽게 만들고 의사라는 직업에 대한 그녀의 혐오를 굳혀 놓았던 그것—에서 자유로웠기 때문이다.\n\n리드게이트는, 병리학의 낙후된 상태에는 아무도 관심을 두지 않으면서 \"그 법안\"의 효험에 대한 사람들의 미신적인 믿음을 비꼬는 쪽이었던 터라, 때로 윌에게 곤란한 질문을 던져 그를 공격하곤 했다. 3월의 어느 저녁, 로저먼드는 목둘레에 백조 깃털 장식을 두른 체리 빛 드레스를 입고 찻상 앞에 앉아 있었다. 바깥일을 하다가 막 지쳐서 들어온 리드게이트는 난롯가 안락의자에 비스듬히 한쪽 다리를 팔걸이에 걸치고 앉아, 눈으로 『파이어니어』의 지면을 훑으며 이마를 다소 찌푸리고 있었고, 한편 로저먼드는 그가 심란해하는 것을 눈치채고서 그를 쳐다보지 않으려 했으며, 속으로는 자기 자신이 침울한 성정이 아닌 것을 하늘에 감사했다. 윌 래디슬로는 깔개 위에 길게 드러누워 멍하니 커튼 봉을 바라보며 \"그대의 얼굴을 처음 보았을 때\"의 가락을 아주 낮게 흥얼거리고 있었다. 한편 집에서 기르는 스패니얼도 변변한 자리도 못 차지한 채 길게 늘어져서는, 앞발 사이로 깔개를 빼앗은 그 침입자를 말없이, 그러나 강하게 못마땅해하며 바라보고 있었다.\n\n로저먼드가 리드게이트에게 찻잔을 가져다주자, 그는 신문을 내던지고는, 벌떡 일어나 찻상으로 다가온 윌에게 말했다.\n\n\"브룩을 개혁적인 지주라고 추켜세워 봐야 소용없네, 래디슬로. 『트럼펫』에서 그자의 옷에 흠집만 더 후벼낼 뿐이니까.\"\n\n\"상관없어요. 『파이어니어』를 읽는 사람들은 『트럼펫』을 안 읽으니까요.\" 윌이 차를 들이켜고 이리저리 거닐며 말했다. \"대중이 자기를 회심시키려는 마음으로 읽는다고 생각하세요? 그렇다면 우리는 정말이지 마녀들이 끓이는 가마솥 꼴이 될 겁니다—'섞어라, 섞어라, 섞어라, 섞어라, 섞을 수 있는 너희들이여'—그리고 아무도 자기가 어느 편을 들지 모르게 되겠지요.\"*\n* \"섞어라, 섞어라…\": 셰익스피어의 『맥베스』에서 마녀들이 가마솥 주위를 돌며 주문을 외는 장면을 빗댄 표현.\n\n\"페어브러더 말로는, 기회가 와도 브룩이 당선될 것 같지는 않다더군. 그를 지지한다고 공언하는 바로 그 사람들이 결정적인 순간에 다른 후보를 자루에서 꺼내 들 거라고 말이야.\"\n\n\"시도해 봐서 나쁠 건 없죠. 지역에 거주하는 의원을 두는 건 좋은 일이고요.\"\n\n\"왜?\" 리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 그 거북한 말을 퉁명스러운 어조로 곧잘 쓰는 사람이었다.\n\n\"지역의 어리석음을 더 잘 대변하니까요.\" 윌이 곱슬머리를 흔들며 웃으면서 말했다. \"그리고 그런 의원들은 이웃 사람들 앞에서 행실을 가장 바르게 유지하게 되거든요. 브룩이 나쁜 사람은 아니에요. 다만 이 의회 욕심이 아니었다면 결코 하지 않았을 좋은 일들을 자기 사유지에서 몇 가지 해 놓았지요.\"\n\n\"그는 공인이 될 만한 사람이 아니야.\" 리드게이트가 경멸 섞인 단호함으로 말했다. \"자기를 믿었던 모든 사람을 실망시킬 거야. 나는 병원에서 그걸 알 수 있어. 다만 거기서는 벌스트로드가 고삐를 쥐고 그를 몰고 가지.\"\n\n\"그건 공인의 기준을 어디에 두느냐에 달렸죠.\" 윌이 말했다. \"이번 경우에는 그 정도면 충분합니다. 사람들이 지금처럼 마음을 굳히고 나면, 그들은 사람을 원하는 게 아니에요—그저 한 표를 원할 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 자네들 정치 평론가들의 방식이지, 래디슬로—어떤 법안을 만병통치약이라도 되는 양 떠받들고, 치유해야 할 바로 그 질병의 일부인 사람들을 추켜세우는 것 말일세.\"\n\n\"안 될 거 있나요? 사람들은 자기도 모르는 사이에 자신들을 이 땅에서 치유해 없앨 수도 있는 거예요.\" 윌이 말했다. 그는 미리 어떤 문제를 생각해 두지 않았을 때면 즉석에서 이유를 찾아낼 줄 아는 사람이었다.\n\n\"그렇다고 이 특정 법안에 대한 미신적이고 과장된 희망을 부추기는 것, 그것을 통째로 삼키라고 외치는 데 가세하고, 그걸 통과시키는 것 말고는 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 투표하는 앵무새들을 의회로 올려 보내는 것이 정당화되는 건 아니야. 자네는 부패에 맞선다고 하지만, 정치적 요술로 사회를 치유할 수 있다고 사람들을 믿게 만드는 것보다 더 철저히 썩은 건 없다네.\"\n\n\"아주 훌륭한 말씀이군요, 선생님. 하지만 선생님의 치료도 어딘가에서는 시작되어야 하고, 한 집단을 타락시키는 천 가지 것들이 우선 이 특정한 개혁 없이는 결코 바로잡힐 수 없다고 한번 가정해 보세요. 얼마 전 스탠리가 한 말을 보세요—의석이 통째로 팔려 나갔다는 걸 누구나 아는 마당에, 의회가 이런저런 유권자가 1기니를 받았느냐 아니냐를 따지면서, 매수라는 자질구레한 문제나 너무 오래 만지작거려 왔다고 말이지요. 공직자들에게서 지혜와 양심이 나오기를 기다린다고요—말도 안 되는 소리! 우리가 믿을 수 있는 유일한 양심은 한 계급의 거대한 부당함의 감각이고, 실제로 작동할 최선의 지혜는 여러 요구를 견주어 저울질하는 지혜입니다. 그게 제 주장의 요지예요—어느 쪽이 피해를 보는가? 저는 그들의 요구를 지지하는 사람을 지지합니다. 부당함을 떠받드는 점잖은 옹호자가 아니라 말이지요.\"\n\n\"특정한 사안을 두고 그런 일반론을 펴는 건 그저 논점을 슬쩍 회피하는 거야, 래디슬로. 내가 치유하는 약을 지지한다고 해서, 통풍이라는 특정한 경우에 아편을 지지한다는 결론이 나오는 건 아니란 말일세.\"\n\n\"저는 우리가 논하는 논점을 회피하는 게 아닙니다—즉, 함께 일할 흠 하나 없는 사람들을 찾을 때까지 아무것도 시도하지 말아야 하느냐 하는 점 말이지요. 선생님이라면 그런 방침대로 하시겠어요? 만약 선생님께 의료 개혁을 추진해 줄 사람 하나와 그것을 반대할 사람 하나가 있다면, 어느 쪽이 더 나은 동기를, 아니 더 나은 머리를 가졌는지 따지시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 물론 아니지.\" 리드게이트가, 자기도 종종 써먹던 수에 자기가 외통수에 걸린 것을 깨닫고 말했다. \"지금 곁에 있는 그런 사람들과 일하지 않는다면, 만사가 꽉 막혀 버리고 말 테니까. 벌스트로드에 대한 읍내의 가장 나쁜 평판이 사실이라고 한들, 내가 가장 잘 알고 가장 마음 쓰는 일들에서 마땅히 해야 한다고 생각하는 바를 행할 분별력과 결단력이 그에게 있다는 사실이 덜 진실해지지는 않아. 하지만 내가 그와 함께하는 건 오직 그 점 때문일세.\" 리드게이트는 페어브러더 씨의 말을 떠올리며 다소 자랑스럽게 덧붙였다. \"그 밖에 그는 내게 아무것도 아니야. 어떤 개인적인 이유로도 그를 추켜세우진 않을 거고—그런 건 멀리할 걸세.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 어떤 개인적인 이유로 브룩을 추켜세운다는 말씀입니까?\" 윌 래디슬로가 발끈하며 홱 돌아서서 말했다. 그가 리드게이트에게 기분이 상한 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 어쩌면 그가 브룩 씨와의 관계가 어떻게 자라 왔는지 면밀히 캐묻는 것을 사양했으리라는 점 때문에 더더욱 그러했는지도 모른다.\n\n\"전혀 아닐세.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"나는 그저 내 행동을 설명하고 있었네. 내 말은, 자기의 개인적 독립을 확신하고, 또 자기가 사사로운 이익—지위든 돈이든—을 위해 일하는 게 아니라는 것을 확신한다면, 동기와 전반적 행로가 미심쩍은 사람들과도 어떤 특정한 목적을 위해 함께 일할 수 있다는 것이었네.\"\n\n\"그럼, 그 너그러움을 왜 남들에게는 베풀지 않으십니까?\" 여전히 발끈한 채 윌이 말했다. \"제 개인적 독립은 선생님의 것이 선생님께 중요한 만큼이나 제게 중요합니다. 선생님께서 제가 브룩에게서 개인적인 기대를 품고 있다고 상상하실 이유는, 제가 선생님께서 벌스트로드에게서 개인적인 기대를 품고 있다고 상상할 이유보다 조금도 더 많지 않아요. 동기란 명예에 관한 문제겠지요—아무도 그것을 입증할 수는 없으니까요. 하지만 세상의 돈이나 지위로 말하자면,\" 윌이 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 끝맺었다. \"제가 그런 종류의 고려에 좌우되는 사람이 아니라는 건 꽤 분명하다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"자네는 나를 완전히 오해했네, 래디슬로.\" 리드게이트가 놀라서 말했다. 그는 자기 자신을 변호하는 데 골몰해 있었던 터라, 래디슬로가 자기 처지에 비추어 무엇을 추론할지 미처 보지 못했던 것이다. \"본의 아니게 자네를 화나게 해서 미안하네. 사실 나는 오히려 자네가 자기 자신의 세속적 이익을 낭만적으로 무시한다고 여기고 있어. 정치 문제에 관해서는, 나는 그저 지적인 편향을 두고 한 말이었네.\"\n\n\"두 분 다 오늘 저녁엔 어쩜 그렇게 불쾌하게 구세요!\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"왜 돈 이야기까지 나와야 하는지 도무지 모르겠어요. 정치와 의학만으로도 다투기엔 충분히 거북한 화제잖아요. 두 분 다 그 두 가지 주제를 가지고 온 세상과, 또 서로와 얼마든지 계속 다투세요.\"\n\n로저먼드는 이렇게 말하며 온화하게 중립을 지키는 표정으로 일어나 종을 울리고는, 자기 반짇고리 탁자 쪽으로 건너갔다.\n\n\"가엾은 로지!\" 리드게이트가, 그녀가 자기 곁을 지날 때 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"논쟁은 천사들에게는 재미없는 거지. 음악이나 좀 들려주구려. 래디슬로한테 같이 노래하자고 청해 봐요.\"\n\n윌이 가고 나자 로저먼드가 남편에게 말했다. \"오늘 저녁엔 무슨 일로 그렇게 언짢으셨어요, 터셔스?\"\n\n\"내가? 언짢았던 건 래디슬로였소. 그 친구는 부싯깃 같으니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 제 말은, 그 전에요. 들어오시기 전에 뭔가 당신을 화나게 한 일이 있었던 거예요. 못마땅한 표정이셨거든요. 그래서 래디슬로 씨와 언쟁을 시작하신 거고요. 당신이 그런 표정을 지으시면 저는 무척 마음이 아파요, 터셔스.\"\n\n\"그랬소? 그렇다면 내가 짐승 같은 놈이군.\" 리드게이트가 뉘우치듯 그녀를 어루만지며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일로 화나셨어요?\"\n\n\"아, 바깥일이오—사업 일.\" 사실은 가구값 청구서의 지불을 독촉하는 편지였다. 하지만 로저먼드는 아이를 가질 예정이었고, 리드게이트는 그녀가 어떤 동요도 겪지 않도록 지켜 주고 싶었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVII.\n\n가장 진실한 사랑이 가장 높은 보람이라.\n어떤 기교도 그것을 빚어내지 못하니, 반드시 솟아나야 하리,\n\n온갖 요소들이 길러 주는 그곳에서.\n그리하여 하늘이 정한 자리와 시각에\n\n작은 토종 꽃 한 송이 피어나니,\n\n뿌리는 아래로, 눈길은 위로 향한 채,\n땅과 하늘이 그 모양을 빚어내네.」\n\n윌 라디슬로가 리드게이트와 그 짧은 언쟁을 나눈 것은 어느 토요일 저녁의 일이었다. 자기 방으로 돌아갔을 때 그 언쟁의 여파로 그는 밤의 절반을 자지 않고 앉아, 새로운 짜증에 사로잡힌 채 미들마치에 정착하여 브룩 씨와 한 멍에를 진 일에 관해 전에 생각했던 모든 것을 다시금 곱씹었다. 그 발걸음을 내딛기 전의 망설임은 그 후로 차라리 그러지 않는 편이 현명했으리라는 온갖 암시에 예민하게 반응하는 마음으로 바뀌었고, 그래서 리드게이트를 향한 그의 격앙이 생겨난 것이었으니, 그 격앙은 여전히 그를 안절부절못하게 했다. 그는 자기 자신을 바보로 만들고 있는 것은 아닐까? 그것도 자신이 바보보다는 더 나은 존재라는 것을 그 어느 때보다 절실히 의식하던 때에? 게다가 무슨 목적을 위해서?\n\n그렇다, 어떤 명확한 목적도 없었다. 사실 그에게는 가능성에 대한 몽롱한 환상이 있었다. 열정과 사유를 함께 지닌 인간치고 자신의 열정의 결과로 사유하지 않는 자는 없으며, 그 열정을 희망으로 달래거나 두려움으로 찌르는 심상들이 마음속에 떠오르는 것을 경험하지 않는 자는 없다. 하지만 우리 모두에게 일어나는 이 일이 어떤 이들에게는 사뭇 다른 양상으로 일어나는 법이니, 윌은 그 기지가 \"큰길을 벗어나지 않는\" 부류의 사람이 아니었다. 그에게는 제 나름대로 고른 자그마한 기쁨이 깃든 샛길들이 있었는데, 큰길에서 말을 달리는 신사들이라면 다소 어리석다 여겼을 법한 것들이었다. 도러시아에 대한 감정으로부터 그가 일종의 행복을 빚어낸 방식이 바로 그런 예였다. 이상하게 들릴지 모르나 사실인즉, 캐소본 씨가 그에게 품었던 의심, 다시 말해 도러시아가 과부가 될 수도 있고 그녀의 마음속에 그가 심어 둔 관심이 그를 남편으로 받아들이는 데까지 이를 수도 있다는 그 흔하고 속된 상상은 그에게 아무런 유혹의 힘도, 사로잡는 힘도 없었다. 그는 우리 모두가 우리의 실질적인 천국인 상상 속의 \"만약 달리 되었더라면\"을 두고 그러하듯, 그러한 사건의 정경 속에 머무르며 그것을 끝까지 그려 나가지 않았다. 비난받을 만한 비열함이 깃든 생각을 품기를 꺼렸고, 배은망덕하다는 비난으로부터 스스로를 변호해야 한다는 의식에 이미 불편했기 때문만은 아니었다. 그녀의 남편이 존재한다는 것 말고도 자신과 도러시아 사이에 가로놓인 여러 다른 장벽에 대한 잠재된 의식이, 캐소본 씨에게 무슨 일이 일어날지 추측하는 데서 그의 상상력을 돌려세우는 데 일조했던 것이다. 게다가 또 다른 이유들도 있었다. 우리가 알다시피 윌은 자신의 수정에 어떤 흠이 나타난다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. 도러시아가 그를 바라보고 그에게 말을 거는 그 차분한 자유로움에 그는 격분하면서도 동시에 기뻐했으며, 그녀를 있는 그대로 떠올리는 데 너무도 그윽한 무언가가 있었기에, 어떻게든 그녀를 변하게 할 변화를 갈망할 수가 없었다. 우리는 아름다운 선율의 거리 버전을 피하지 않는가? 혹은 우리가 어쩌다 한 번 엿보려고 애쓴 그 수고를 도리어 기뻐하며 마음에 두고 음미해 온 진귀한 것, 어쩌면 조각이나 판화 한 점이 실은 흔한 것이며 누구나 일상적으로 소유할 수 있다는 소식 앞에서 움츠러들지 않는가? 우리의 행복은 우리 감정의 질과 폭에 달려 있는 법이니, 흔히 인생의 견실한 것들이라 불리는 것에는 거의 관심이 없고 그 미묘한 영향력에 크게 마음을 쓰는 윌 같은 사람에게, 도러시아를 향한 것과 같은 감정을 마음에 품는 것은 한 재산을 물려받는 것이나 다름없었다. 다른 사람들이라면 그의 열정의 부질없음이라 불렀을 것이, 그의 상상력에는 또 다른 기쁨을 더해 주었다. 그는 자신이 너그러운 마음의 움직임을 지녔음을 의식했고, 자신의 공상을 매혹했던 저 드높은 사랑의 시를 제 경험 속에서 확인하고 있음을 의식했다. 도러시아는, 그는 혼잣말을 했다, 그의 영혼 속에 영원히 군림하고 있다고. 다른 어떤 여인도 그녀의 발판보다 더 높이 앉을 수 없으리라고. 그리고 만약 그녀가 그의 내면에 일으킨 효과를 불멸의 음절로 적어 낼 수 있었다면, 그는 옛 드레이턴을 본받아 이렇게 자랑했을지도 모른다.\n「훗날의 여왕들이 기꺼이 살아가리라,\n\n그녀가 넘치도록 베푼 찬미의 적선에 기대어.」\n하지만 이런 결과는 의심스러웠다. 그렇다면 그가 도러시아를 위해 그 밖에 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가? 그의 헌신이 그녀에게 무슨 가치가 있단 말인가? 알 수 없는 일이었다. 그는 그녀의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 떠나지 않으리라. 그녀의 벗들 가운데 그녀가 자기에게 하듯 그토록 소박한 신뢰로 말을 건넬 만한 사람은 하나도 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 언젠가 그가 머물러 주었으면 좋겠다고 말한 적이 있었으니, 그는 머물 것이었다. 어떤 불을 뿜는 용들이 그녀의 주위에서 쉭쉭거린다 해도.\n\n이것이 언제나 윌의 망설임이 다다른 결론이었다. 하지만 그는 자신의 결심에 대해서조차 모순과 반항이 없지 않았다. 그날 밤 그러했듯이, 그는 종종 짜증이 나곤 했다. 브룩 씨를 우두머리로 삼은 자신의 공적인 노력이 그가 바라는 만큼 영웅적으로 보일 수 없다는 어떤 외부의 표시 때문에. 그리고 이것은 늘 또 다른 짜증의 근원과 결부되어 있었으니, 도러시아를 위해 품위를 희생했음에도 그녀를 좀처럼 볼 수 없다는 것이었다. 그리하여 이 불쾌한 사실들을 반박할 수 없게 되자, 그는 자기 자신의 가장 강한 성향을 반박하며 이렇게 말했다. \"나는 바보다.\"\n\n그럼에도 불구하고, 그 내면의 논쟁이 필연적으로 도러시아를 중심으로 돌았기에, 그는 전에 그랬던 것처럼 그녀의 존재가 자신에게 어떤 의미일지 더욱 생생하게 느끼게 되는 것으로 끝맺을 뿐이었다. 그러다 문득 내일이 일요일이라는 생각이 떠오르자, 그는 로윅 교회에 가서 그녀를 보기로 마음먹었다. 그는 그 생각을 품은 채 잠들었으나, 이성적인 아침 햇살 속에서 옷을 입을 때 반대의 목소리가 말했다.\n\"그건 로윅을 방문하지 말라는 캐소본 씨의 금지에 대한 사실상의 도전이 될 테고, 도러시아도 언짢아할 거야.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼!\" 욕망이 항변했다. \"봄날 아침에 어여쁜 시골 교회에 나가는 것조차 그가 막는다면 그건 너무 터무니없는 일이지. 그리고 도러시아도 기뻐할 거야.\"\n\n\"네가 그를 성가시게 하려고 왔거나 아니면 도러시아를 보려고 왔다는 게 캐소본 씨에게는 빤히 보일 거야.\"\n\"내가 그를 성가시게 하려고 간다는 건 사실이 아니고, 도러시아를 보러 가면 안 될 이유가 어디 있어? 그는 모든 걸 혼자 독차지하고 늘 편안해야만 하나? 다른 사람들이 그래야만 하듯, 그도 좀 따끔한 맛을 봐도 되지. 나는 늘 그 교회와 신도들의 예스러운 정취를 좋아했어. 게다가 나는 터커 일가를 알지. 그들의 자리에 들어가 앉으면 돼.\"\n\n비합리의 힘으로 반대의 목소리를 잠재운 뒤, 윌은 마치 천국으로 향하는 길에 오른 것처럼 로윅을 향해 걸어갔다. 그는 핼셀 공유지를 가로지르고 숲가를 따라 걸었는데, 그곳에는 움트는 가지들 아래로 햇빛이 넓게 쏟아져 이끼와 지의의 아름다움을, 그리고 갈색 흙을 뚫고 나온 싱그러운 초록의 새싹들을 도드라지게 드러내고 있었다. 만물이 그날이 일요일임을 아는 듯했고, 그가 로윅 교회에 가는 것을 흡족해하는 듯했다. 윌은 자신의 기분을 거스르는 것이 아무것도 없을 때면 쉽사리 행복해졌으니, 이즈음에는 캐소본 씨를 약 올린다는 생각이 도리어 꽤 즐거워져서, 그의 얼굴에 명랑한 미소가 번지게 했다. 물밑에서 햇살이 부서지는 것처럼 보기 좋은 미소였다. 비록 그 동기는 본받을 만한 것이 못 되었지만 말이다. 하지만 우리 대부분은 우리의 길을 가로막는 사람을 가증스럽다고 마음속으로 단정해 버리고, 그의 인품이 우리 안에 불러일으키는 그 혐오를 그에게 조금 안겨 주는 것을 개의치 않는 경향이 있다. 윌은 작은 책 한 권을 옆구리에 끼고 양손을 옆주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 걸어갔는데, 책을 읽기는커녕 교회 안에서 그리고 나올 때 벌어질 장면을 머릿속에 그리며 흥얼흥얼 작은 노래를 불렀다. 그는 자기가 지은 가사에 맞을 곡조를 이리저리 시험하고 있었으니, 때로는 기성의 선율을 붙여 보고 때로는 즉흥적으로 지어냈다. 그 가사는 정확히 찬송가는 아니었으나, 분명 그의 일요일 경험에는 들어맞았다.\n「오 나여, 오 나여, 어찌 이리 보잘것없는 양식을\n\n내 사랑은 먹고 사는가!\n여기에 없는 손길 하나, 빛줄기 하나,\n\n이미 사라진 그림자 하나.\n여기 있을지도 모를 숨결의 꿈 하나,\n\n마음속에 울리는 가락 하나,\n누군가 나를 소중히 여길지도 모른다는 생각,\n\n누군가에게 알려졌던 그 자리,\n물러간 두려움의 떨림 하나,\n\n저질러지지 않은 잘못 하나.\n오 나여, 오 나여, 어찌 이리 보잘것없는 양식을\n\n내 사랑은 먹고 사는가!」\n이따금 그가 모자를 벗고 고개를 뒤로 젖히며, 노래를 부르느라 가냘픈 목을 드러낼 때면, 그는 공기를 가득 채운 봄의 정령이 육화한 모습 같았으니, 불확실한 약속으로 충만한 빛나는 존재였다.\n\n그가 로윅에 이르렀을 때 종은 아직 울리고 있었고, 그는 다른 누구도 도착하기 전에 부목사석으로 들어갔다. 하지만 신도들이 다 모인 뒤에도 그는 여전히 그 자리에 홀로 남겨졌다. 부목사석은 작은 성단소 입구에서 교구 목사석과 마주 보고 있었으니, 윌은 도러시아가 오지 않을지도 모른다고 두려워할 겨를이 있었다. 그러는 동안 그는 회칠한 벽과 어두운 옛 신도석 안에서 해마다 회중을 이루는 시골 사람들의 얼굴 무리를 둘러보았는데, 그 변화란 늙어 여기저기 부러지면서도 여전히 어린 가지를 틔우는 나뭇가지에서 우리가 보는 것보다 그리 크지 않았다. 리그 씨의 개구리 같은 얼굴은 무언가 낯설고 영문 모를 것이었으나, 이렇게 사물의 질서에 가해진 충격에도 불구하고, 거기에는 여전히 월 일가와 파우더렐 집안의 시골 혈통이 나란히 자기 자리에 앉아 있었다. 동생 새뮤얼의 뺨에는 여전히 예전과 똑같은 자줏빛 둘레가 있었고, 점잖은 소작농의 삼 대가 옛날과 다름없이 자기네 윗사람들에 대한 전반적인 의무감을 품고 왔다. 어린아이들은 검은 가운을 입고 가장 높은 칸에 오르는 캐소본 씨를 아마도 모든 윗사람들 중의 으뜸이요, 노여움을 사면 가장 무시무시한 분이라 여겼다. 1831년에도 로윅은 평온했으니, 개혁이라는 것에도 일요일 설교의 엄숙한 가락에 흔들리는 것 이상으로 동요하지 않았다. 신도들은 예전에 윌이 교회에 오는 것을 보는 데 익숙했으므로, 그가 노래에서 한몫할 것을 기대한 성가대 말고는 아무도 그를 별로 눈여겨보지 않았다.\n도러시아가 마침내 이 예스러운 배경 속에 나타나, 흰 비버 가죽 보닛과 회색 망토 차림으로 짧은 통로를 걸어 올라왔으니, 바티칸에서 입었던 바로 그 차림이었다. 들어서는 순간부터 그녀의 얼굴이 성단소 쪽을 향하고 있었기에, 근시인 그녀의 눈도 이내 윌을 알아보았으나, 그녀의 감정이 겉으로 드러난 것이라곤 그를 지나칠 때의 가벼운 창백함과 진중한 목례뿐이었다. 윌은 자기 자신도 놀랍게 갑자기 거북해져서, 서로 목례를 나눈 뒤에는 감히 그녀를 쳐다보지 못했다. 이 분 후, 캐소본 씨가 제의실에서 나와 신도석으로 들어가 도러시아를 마주 보고 앉자, 윌은 자신의 마비가 한층 완전해지는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 제의실 문 위 작은 회랑의 성가대 말고는 어디도 쳐다볼 수 없었다. 도러시아는 어쩌면 마음 아파하고 있을지도 모르고, 그는 비참한 실수를 저지른 것이었다. 캐소본 씨를 약 올리는 것은 이제 더는 즐겁지 않았으니, 그 사람은 아마도 윌을 지켜보며 그가 감히 고개를 돌리지 못한다는 것을 알아챈 유리한 입장에 있었다. 그는 어째서 이것을 미리 상상하지 못했던가? 하지만 그가 그 네모난 신도석에 홀로 앉아 있게 되리라고는, 어떤 터커 일가의 사람도 곁에 없어 거북함을 덜어 주지 못하리라고는 예상할 수 없었다. 그들은 분명 로윅을 아주 떠난 모양이었으니, 새 성직자가 강독대에 서 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 그는 도러시아 쪽을 바라보는 일이 자신에게 불가능하리라는 것을, 아니 그녀가 자신이 온 것을 무례하다고 느낄지도 모른다는 것을 예견하지 못한 일로 이제 스스로를 어리석다 책망했다. 그러나 자신의 새장에서 빠져나올 길은 없었다. 윌은 마치 자기가 여교사라도 된 양 자기 차례의 구절들을 찾아 책을 들여다보면서, 아침 예배가 전에는 한 번도 이토록 한없이 길었던 적이 없었다고, 자기가 더없이 우스꽝스럽고, 기분이 상했으며, 비참하다고 느꼈다. 한 여인의 모습을 흠모하여 한 남자가 얻은 것이 고작 이것이었다! 교회지기는 라디슬로 씨가 하노버 곡조에 함께 부르지 않는 것을 놀랍게 여기고는, 그가 감기에 걸린 모양이라고 생각했다.\n\n캐소본 씨는 그날 아침 설교를 하지 않았으며, 축도가 끝나고 모두가 자리에서 일어날 때까지 윌의 처지에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 로윅에서는 \"윗사람들\"이 먼저 나가는 것이 관례였다. 자신을 짓누르는 주문을 깨뜨리겠다는 갑작스러운 결심으로, 윌은 캐소본 씨를 똑바로 쳐다보았다. 하지만 그 신사의 눈길은 신도석 문의 단추에 가 있었으니, 그는 그 문을 열어 도러시아를 먼저 지나가게 한 다음, 눈꺼풀을 들지도 않은 채 곧바로 그녀를 뒤따라갔다. 윌의 눈길은 도러시아가 신도석에서 나설 때 그녀의 눈길과 마주쳤고, 그녀는 다시 한번 목례했으나 이번에는 동요한 기색이었으니, 마치 눈물을 억누르고 있는 듯했다. 윌은 그들을 따라 걸어 나갔으나, 그들은 교회 묘지에서 관목 숲으로 통하는 작은 문 쪽으로 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않고 가 버렸다.\n그가 그들을 뒤따르기란 불가능했으니, 그는 그저 한낮에, 아침에 희망에 차서 밟았던 바로 그 길을 따라 쓸쓸히 걸어 돌아올 수 있을 뿐이었다. 밖에서도 안에서도 그의 빛은 모두 바뀌어 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLVIII.\n\n분명 황금빛 시간들은 잿빛으로 바뀌어 가고 더는 춤추지 않으며, 달려 보려 헛되이 애쓴다. 나는 그들의 흰 머리채가 바람에 흩날리는 것을 본다— 저마다의 얼굴은 나를 바라보며 초췌하고, 폭풍에 떠밀려 쉼 없이 맞잡고 도는 그 회전 속에서 천천히 돌아선다.\n\n도러시아가 교회를 나서며 느낀 괴로움은 무엇보다도 캐소본 씨가 사촌과 말을 섞지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었다는 것, 그리고 윌이 교회에 나타난 일이 두 사람 사이의 소원함을 한층 더 또렷하게 드러냈다는 것을 깨달은 데서 비롯되었다. 도러시아가 보기에 윌이 온 것은 충분히 변명될 만한 일이었고, 아니 오히려 그녀 자신이 줄곧 바라 왔던 화해를 향한 그의 다정한 몸짓이라고 그녀는 생각했다. 그도 아마 그녀처럼, 캐소본 씨와 자기가 무람없이 마주칠 수만 있다면 악수를 나누고 다정한 교류가 되살아나리라 상상했을 것이다. 하지만 이제 도러시아는 그 희망마저 송두리째 빼앗긴 기분이었다. 윌은 그 어느 때보다 멀리 추방당했으니, 캐소본 씨는 자신이 인정하기를 거부하는 존재가 이렇게 들이밀어진 일로 새로이 쓰라림을 느꼈을 것이 분명했다.\n\n그날 아침 그는 몸이 그리 좋지 못해 숨쉬기가 다소 힘들어 설교도 하지 못했다. 그러니 점심 식사 자리에서 그가 거의 말이 없었던 것도 그녀는 놀랍지 않았고, 윌 래디슬로에 관해 아무 언급도 하지 않은 것은 더더욱 그러했다. 그녀 자신으로서도 다시는 그 화제를 꺼낼 수 없으리라 느꼈다. 일요일이면 두 사람은 보통 점심과 저녁 사이의 시간을 따로 보냈다. 캐소본 씨는 서재에서 주로 졸았고, 도러시아는 자신의 거실에서 좋아하는 책 몇 권을 펼쳐 시간을 보내곤 했다. 활 모양 창가의 탁자 위에는 책이 자그마하게 쌓여 있었으니, 캐소본 씨와 함께 읽기 시작한 헤로도토스부터, 그녀의 오랜 벗인 파스칼, 그리고 키블의 『기독교의 한 해』에 이르기까지 종류가 다양했다. 하지만 오늘은 한 권 한 권 펼쳐 보아도 어느 것도 읽을 수가 없었다. 모든 것이 음울하게만 느껴졌다. 키루스의 탄생을 예고하는 전조들도, 유대의 고대 유물들도—아, 정말!—경건한 경구들도, 즐겨 부르던 찬송가의 거룩한 가락도, 모두 한결같이 나무를 두드리는 곡조처럼 밋밋하게만 들렸다. 봄꽃과 풀잎마저도 이따금 해를 가리는 오후의 구름 아래에서 칙칙하게 떨고 있었다. 습관이 되어 버린, 그녀를 지탱해 주던 생각들조차도, 앞으로 오랜 세월 그것들만을 유일한 벗으로 삼아 살아가야 한다는 권태를 머금고 있는 것 같았다. 가엾은 도러시아가 굶주리듯 갈망하던 것은 또 다른, 아니 좀 더 충만한 종류의 교제였고, 그 굶주림은 결혼 생활이 끊임없이 요구하는 노력에서 자라난 것이었다. 그녀는 늘 남편이 바라는 사람이 되려고 애썼으나, 있는 그대로의 자신을 그가 기뻐하는 것에 기댈 수는 한 번도 없었다. 자기가 좋아하는 것, 자발적으로 마음이 가는 것은 언제나 그녀의 삶에서 배제되는 것 같았다. 남편이 그저 허락만 하고 함께 나누지 않는다면, 그것은 거절당한 것이나 다름없었기 때문이다. 윌 래디슬로에 관해서는 처음부터 두 사람 사이에 견해 차이가 있었고, 캐소본 씨가 가문의 재산에 대한 윌의 권리를 둘러싼 도러시아의 강한 감정을 그토록 모질게 물리쳤기에, 그것은 결국 자기가 옳고 남편이 그르되 자신은 어쩔 도리가 없다는 확신으로 끝을 맺었다. 그날 오후, 그 무력감은 그 어느 때보다도 비참하게 그녀를 마비시켰다. 그녀는 자신에게 소중할 수 있는, 그리고 자신이 소중할 수 있는 대상들을 갈망했다. 햇빛과 비처럼 곧바로 은혜로운 일을 갈망했으나, 이제 보니 그녀는 점점 더 살아 있는 무덤 속에서 살아가야 할 형편이었다. 그 무덤에는 결코 빛을 보지 못할 것을 만들어 내는 음산한 노역의 장치가 갖추어져 있었다. 오늘 그녀는 그 무덤의 문턱에 서서, 윌 래디슬로가 따뜻한 활동과 사귐의 머나먼 세계로 물러가는 모습을, 떠나가면서도 그녀에게로 얼굴을 돌리는 모습을 지켜본 것이다.\n\n책은 아무 소용이 없었다. 생각도 아무 소용이 없었다. 일요일이었으니, 얼마 전 아기를 낳은 실리아에게 갈 마차를 부릴 수도 없었다. 이제 정신적 공허와 불만으로부터 피할 곳은 어디에도 없었고, 도러시아는 두통을 견디듯 자신의 침울한 기분을 견뎌야 했다.\n\n저녁 식사 후, 평소 그녀가 소리 내어 책을 읽기 시작하던 시각에, 캐소본 씨는 서재로 가자고 제안하며, 거기에 불과 등불을 준비해 두라고 일러 두었다고 말했다. 그는 기운을 되찾은 듯, 무언가를 골똘히 생각하는 것 같았다.\n\n서재에서 도러시아는 그가 탁자 위에 자신의 공책들을 한 줄로 새로 정리해 둔 것을 보았는데, 그는 이제 익히 알려진 한 권을 집어 들어 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주었으니, 그것은 다른 모든 공책의 목차에 해당하는 것이었다.\n\n\"여보, 한 가지 청을 들어주면 고맙겠소.\" 그가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"오늘 저녁에는 다른 책을 읽는 대신, 연필을 손에 들고 이것을 소리 내어 끝까지 읽으면서, 내가 '표시'라고 말하는 대목마다 연필로 십자 표시를 해 주오. 이것은 내가 오래전부터 마음에 두어 온 선별 작업의 첫 단계요. 우리가 진행해 나가다 보면 내가 그대에게 선택의 어떤 원칙들을 일러 줄 수 있을 테고, 그러면 그대도 내 목적에 지적으로 참여하게 되리라 믿소.\"\n\n이 제안은 리드게이트와의 잊지 못할 면담 이후로 여러 차례 나타난 징표에 하나가 더 보태진 것에 불과했으니, 곧 도러시아가 자신과 함께 일하는 것을 마뜩잖아 하던 캐소본 씨의 본래 태도가 그 반대로, 다시 말해 그녀에게 많은 관심과 노고를 요구하는 쪽으로 바뀌었다는 표지였다.\n\n두 시간 동안 그녀가 읽으며 표시를 한 뒤, 그가 말했다. \"이 책을 위층으로 가져갑시다—연필도, 괜찮다면—그래야 밤중에 책을 읽게 되더라도 이 일을 이어 갈 수 있으니. 그대에게 고되지는 않겠지, 도러시아?\"\n\n\"저는 언제나 당신이 가장 듣고 싶어 하시는 것을 읽는 편이 좋아요.\" 도러시아가 말했으니, 그것은 꾸밈없는 진실이었다. 그녀가 두려워하는 것은 책을 읽거나 그 무엇을 하느라 애쓰고도 그를 여전히 기쁨 없는 상태로 남겨 두는 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n도러시아의 어떤 특성들이 주위 사람들에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다는 것은, 온갖 질투와 의심을 품은 그녀의 남편마저도 그녀가 한 약속의 신실함과, 자기가 생각하는 옳고 가장 좋은 일에 헌신하는 그녀의 능력에 대해 절대적인 신뢰를 품게 되었다는 사실로 입증되었다. 근래에 그는 이런 자질들이 자기에게 속한 특별한 소유물이라고 느끼기 시작했고, 그것들을 독차지하고 싶어 했다.\n\n밤중의 독서는 정말로 찾아왔다. 젊은 몸의 피로에 도러시아는 일찍 깊이 잠들었다가, 어떤 빛의 감각에 깨어났으니, 처음에는 그것이 가파른 언덕을 오른 뒤에 문득 마주한 노을의 환영처럼 느껴졌다. 그녀가 눈을 뜨니, 따뜻한 가운을 두른 남편이 아직 잉걸불이 빨갛게 빛나는 벽난로 가까이의 안락의자에 앉으려는 것이 보였다. 그는 도러시아가 깰 것을 예상하면서도, 더 직접적인 방법으로 그녀를 깨우기는 싫어서 촛불 두 개를 켜 둔 것이었다.\n\n\"편찮으세요, 에드워드?\" 그녀가 곧바로 일어나며 말했다.\n\n\"누운 자세로 있으니 좀 불편하더군. 한동안 여기 앉아 있겠소.\" 그녀는 불에 장작을 던져 넣고, 몸을 감싼 뒤 말했다. \"제가 책을 읽어 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"그래 주면 정말 고맙겠소, 도러시아.\" 캐소본 씨가 평소의 정중한 태도에 평소보다 한층 부드러운 기색을 더해 말했다. \"잠이 오지 않는구려. 머리가 유난히 맑소.\"\n\n\"그 흥분이 당신께 너무 큰 부담이 될까 두려워요.\" 도러시아가 리드게이트의 당부를 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 나는 지나친 흥분을 느끼지 않소. 생각이 수월하게 흘러가오.\" 도러시아는 감히 고집을 부리지 못하고, 저녁에 했던 것과 똑같은 방식으로 한 시간 남짓 책을 읽었으나, 페이지를 더 빠르게 넘겨 나갔다. 캐소본 씨의 정신은 한결 또렷해서, 아주 짧은 말의 신호만으로도 다음에 올 내용을 예측하는 듯이 \"됐소—거기 표시하오\"라거나 \"다음 항목으로 넘어가오—크레타에 관한 두 번째 부론은 생략하겠소\"라고 말했다. 도러시아는 여러 해 동안 기어 다니듯 더듬어 온 영역을 그의 정신이 새처럼 빠른 속도로 훑어 나가는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 마침내 그가 말했다—\n\n\"이제 책을 덮어요, 여보. 우리 작업은 내일 다시 이어 갑시다. 너무 오래 미루어 왔으니, 마무리되는 것을 기쁘게 보고 싶구려. 하지만 알다시피, 내 선별의 원칙은 지금 잡아 둔 대로, 서론에 열거한 각 명제에 대해 적절하되 한쪽으로 치우치지 않는 예증을 제시하는 데 있소. 그것을 분명히 헤아렸지, 도러시아?\"\n\n\"네.\" 도러시아가 다소 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. 그녀는 가슴이 메스꺼울 만큼 괴로웠다.\n\n\"그러면 이제 좀 쉴 수 있을 것 같소.\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다. 그는 다시 자리에 누우며 등불을 꺼 달라고 청했다. 그녀도 자리에 눕고, 난로 위의 희미한 불빛만이 깨뜨리는 어둠이 깔리자, 그가 말했다—\n\n\"잠들기 전에, 그대에게 한 가지 청할 것이 있소, 도러시아.\"\n\n\"무엇인가요?\" 도러시아가 마음속에 두려움을 품은 채 말했다.\n\n\"내가 만일 죽게 되면 그대가 내 뜻을 받들어 줄지를, 곧 내가 못마땅해할 일은 하지 않고 내가 바라는 일에 전념해 줄지를, 신중히 생각하여 내게 알려 달라는 것이오.\"\n\n도러시아는 놀라지 않았다. 여러 가지 일이 있어 그녀에게 새로운 멍에가 될지도 모를 남편의 어떤 의도를 짐작하게 만들었던 것이다. 그녀는 곧바로 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"거절하는 거요?\" 캐소본 씨가 한층 날 선 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 아직 거절하는 건 아니에요.\" 도러시아가 또렷한 목소리로 말했으니, 자유에 대한 갈망이 그녀의 내부에서 고개를 든 것이었다. \"하지만 그것이 무엇에 저를 매어 놓을지도 모르는 채로 약속을 한다는 것은 너무나 엄숙한 일이에요—옳지 않다고 생각해요. 어떤 애정이 시키는 일이라면 저는 약속하지 않고도 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그대는 그대 나름의 판단을 따르겠다는 것이지. 나는 그대가 내 판단에 따르기를 청하는 거요. 그대는 거절하는 거요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 여보, 아니에요!\" 도러시아가 서로 맞서는 두려움에 짓눌려 애원하듯 말했다. \"하지만 잠시 기다리며 조금만 더 생각해 봐도 될까요? 저는 온 영혼을 다해 당신을 위로할 일을 하고 싶어요. 하지만 이렇게 갑작스레 어떤 서약도 드릴 수가 없어요—무엇을 하게 될지조차 모르는 일에 대한 서약은 더더욱요.\"\n\n\"그러면 그대는 내 뜻의 본질을 믿을 수 없다는 거요?\"\n\n\"내일까지만 시간을 주세요.\" 도러시아가 애원하듯 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 내일까지.\" 캐소본 씨가 말했다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는 그가 잠든 소리를 들을 수 있었으나, 그녀에게는 더 이상 잠이 오지 않았다. 그를 방해할까 봐 가만히 누워 있으려 애쓰는 동안, 그녀의 마음속에서는 상상력이 그 군대를 처음에는 한쪽에, 다음에는 다른 쪽에 배치하는 갈등이 벌어지고 있었다. 남편이 자신의 앞으로의 행동에 대해 확립하려는 그 권한이 그의 작업 말고 다른 무언가와 관련이 있으리라는 예감은 그녀에게 전혀 없었다. 하지만 그가, 더더욱 미덥지 못한 원칙들의 미심쩍은 예증이 될 그 뒤섞인 자료 더미를 추려 내는 일에 자신이 헌신하기를 기대하리라는 것만은 충분히 분명했다. 가엾은 아이는 남편의 일생의 야망이자 노고였던 그 '열쇠'의 신뢰성에 대해 이미 완전히 믿음을 잃은 터였다. 변변치 못한 배움에도 불구하고 이 문제에 관한 그녀의 판단이 그의 것보다 더 진실했던 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그녀는 그가 자신의 모든 자아를 걸었던 개연성들을, 편견 없는 비교와 건강한 분별로 바라보았기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 그녀는 산산이 부서진 미라라 부를 만한 것들, 그리고 그 자체가 부서진 폐허에서 짜낸 모자이크인 전통의 파편들을 분류하며 보내야 할 나날과 달과 해를 머릿속에 그려 보았으니—그것들을, 마치 요정의 아이처럼 태어나면서부터 이미 시들어 버린 어떤 이론의 양식으로 분류하는 일이었다. 의심할 바 없이, 활기차게 추구된 활기찬 오류가 진리의 배아를 살아 숨 쉬게 한 적은 있었다. 황금을 찾는 일이 동시에 물질을 탐구하는 일이 되어, 화학이라는 육체가 그 영혼을 맞이할 채비를 갖추고 라부아지에가 태어났듯이. 하지만 모든 전통의 씨앗을 이룬 요소들에 관한 캐소본 씨의 이론은 미처 알지 못한 채로 발견들과 부딪쳐 멍들 일이 없을 것 같았다. 그것은 소리의 유사함 때문에 강해 보이다가 끝내 소리의 유사함이 도리어 그것들을 불가능하게 만든다는 사실이 밝혀지는 어원풀이들만큼이나, 견고함이라곤 없는 유연한 추측들 사이를 떠다닐 뿐이었다. 그것은 곡과 마곡에 관한 정교한 관념보다 더 날카로운 충돌을 일으키는 무언가를 형성해야 할 필요에 의해 검증되지 않는 해석의 방법이었다. 그것은 별들을 한데 꿰어 보겠다는 계획만큼이나 아무런 방해도 받지 않았다. 그리고 도러시아는 삶을 더 가치 있게 만들어 줄 고매한 지식 속의 동반 관계 대신에 그녀에게 모습을 드러낸 이 미심쩍은 수수께끼 풀이에 대해, 얼마나 자주 자신의 권태와 조바심을 억눌러야 했던가! 이제 그녀는 남편이 왜 자신에게 매달리게 되었는지를 충분히 이해할 수 있었으니, 그것은 어쩌면 자신의 노고가 언젠가 세상에 내놓을 수 있는 형태를 갖추리라는 마지막 남은 희망이었기 때문이다. 처음에 그는 자신이 하는 일에 대한 어떤 깊은 앎으로부터 그녀마저 멀리 떼어 놓고 싶어 하는 듯 보였다. 하지만 차츰 인간적 필요의 무서운 절박함이—너무 이른 죽음의 전망이—\n\n그리고 여기에서 도러시아의 연민은 자신의 미래로부터 남편의 과거로—아니, 그 과거에서 자라난 운명과 벌이는 그의 현재의 고된 분투로 옮겨 갔다. 외로운 노고, 자기 불신의 압박 아래 가까스로 숨 쉬는 야망, 멀어져 가는 목표와 점점 무거워지는 사지, 그리고 마침내 그의 머리 위에서 눈에 보이게 떨고 있는 칼! 그녀가 그와 결혼하려 했던 것은 그의 일생의 노고를 돕기 위해서가 아니었던가?—하지만 그녀는 그 일이 무언가 더 위대한 것, 그 자체를 위해 경건하게 봉사할 수 있는 것이리라 여겼었다. 그의 슬픔을 달래기 위해서라 한들, 결실 없이 쳇바퀴 돌듯 일하는 것이—설령 약속을 한다 해도—과연 옳을까, 아니 가능하기나 할까?\n\n그렇다 한들, 그녀가 그를 거절할 수 있을까? \"이 애타는 굶주림을 채워 드리기를 거절하겠어요\"라고 말할 수 있을까? 그것은 그가 살아 있을 때 거의 틀림없이 해 줄 일을, 그가 죽었을 때 해 주기를 거절하는 셈이었다. 리드게이트가 말한 대로 그가 십오 년 혹은 그 이상을 산다면, 그녀의 삶은 분명 그를 돕고 그에게 순종하는 데 바쳐질 것이었다.\n\n그럼에도 살아 있는 이에게 바치는 헌신과 죽은 이에게 바치겠다는 막연한 약속 사이에는 깊은 차이가 있었다. 그가 살아 있는 동안에는, 그녀가 여전히 자유로이 항변하고 심지어 거절할 수 있는 것이 아닌 어떤 것도 그는 요구할 수 없었다. 하지만—그 생각이 그녀의 마음을 한 번 이상 스쳐 갔으나 그녀는 그것을 믿을 수가 없었다—그가 자기 뜻이 정확히 무엇인지 말해 주지도 않으면서 그것을 받들겠다는 서약을 원하는 것을 보면, 혹시 그녀가 상상할 수 있었던 것보다 더한 무언가를 그녀에게 요구하려는 것은 아닐까? 아니다. 그의 마음은 오직 자신의 작업에만 묶여 있었다. 그것이야말로 그의 꺼져 가는 생명을 그녀의 생명으로 이어 붙여 이루려는 목적이었다.\n\n그리고 이제 만일 그녀가 \"안 돼요! 당신이 죽으면 저는 당신의 작업에 손가락 하나 대지 않겠어요\"라고 말한다면—그것은 마치 그 멍든 가슴을 짓밟아 부수는 것만 같았다.\n\n네 시간 동안 도러시아는 이 갈등 속에 누워 있다가, 끝내 속이 메스껍고 정신이 어지러워, 아무것도 결정하지 못한 채 말없이 기도했다. 너무 오래 흐느끼며 무언가를 구하다 지친 아이처럼 어찌할 바를 몰라, 그녀는 아침 늦게야 잠이 들었고, 깨어났을 때 캐소본 씨는 이미 일어나 있었다. 탠트립이 그녀에게 그가 기도를 올리고 아침을 들고는 서재에 있다고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"마님께서 그렇게 창백하신 건 처음 봬요.\" 로잔에서부터 자매를 모셔 온, 다부진 몸집의 탠트립이 말했다.\n\n\"내가 혈색이 좋았던 적이 있던가, 탠트립?\" 도러시아가 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 혈색이 좋다고까지야 할 수 없겠지만, 차이나 장미 같은 발그레함은 도시던데요. 그렇지만 늘 그 가죽 책들 냄새만 맡고 계시니, 무얼 더 바라겠어요? 오늘 아침은 조금 쉬세요, 마님. 제가 마님께서 편찮으셔서 그 답답한 서재엔 못 들어가신다고 여쭐게요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야! 어서 가야지.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"캐소본 씨가 나를 특별히 찾으시잖니.\"\n\n아래층으로 내려가면서 그녀는 그의 뜻을 받들겠다고 약속하게 되리라 확신했다. 하지만 그것은 그날 늦게의 일이 될 테고—아직은 아니었다.\n\n도러시아가 서재로 들어서자, 책 몇 권을 정리하고 있던 탁자에서 캐소본 씨가 몸을 돌리며 말했다—\n\n\"그대가 오기를 기다리고 있었소, 여보. 오늘 아침은 곧바로 일을 시작하려 했는데, 아마도 어제 너무 흥분한 탓인지 몸이 좀 좋지 않구려. 공기가 좀 누그러졌으니, 이제 관목 정원이나 한 바퀴 거닐고 오겠소.\"\n\n\"잘됐네요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"어젯밤 당신의 정신이 너무 활발한 것 같아 걱정스러웠어요.\"\n\n\"내가 지난번에 말한 그 일을 마음 놓고 정리하고 싶구려, 도러시아. 이제는 그대가 내게 답을 줄 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"제가 잠시 후에 정원으로 나가도 될까요?\" 도러시아가 그런 식으로 잠시 숨 돌릴 틈을 얻으며 말했다.\n\n\"앞으로 반 시간 동안은 주목 산책로에 있겠소.\" 캐소본 씨가 말하고는 그녀를 떠났다.\n\n몹시 지친 도러시아는 종을 울려 탠트립에게 걸칠 것을 가져다 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 몇 분간 가만히 앉아 있었으나, 앞서의 갈등이 다시 일어난 것은 아니었다. 다만 자신이 스스로의 운명에 \"네\"라고 말하게 되리라는 것을 느낄 뿐이었다. 그녀는 너무도 약했고, 남편에게 날카로운 일격을 가한다는 생각에 너무도 두려움이 가득 차서, 온전히 굴복하는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수가 없었다. 그녀는 가만히 앉아 탠트립이 모자를 씌우고 숄을 둘러 주도록 내버려 두었으니, 이는 자기 일은 스스로 하기를 좋아하는 그녀에게는 흔치 않은 수동적인 태도였다.\n\n\"하느님께서 마님을 축복하시길!\" 탠트립이 말했으니, 이제 모자 끈을 다 매어 주고 나니 더는 해 드릴 것이 없다고 느낀 그 아름답고 온순한 사람을 향한, 억누를 수 없는 사랑의 움직임이었다.\n\n이 한마디가 도러시아의 팽팽히 곤두선 감정에는 너무 벅찬 것이어서, 그녀는 와락 눈물을 터뜨리며 탠트립의 팔에 기대어 흐느꼈다. 하지만 곧 자신을 다잡아 눈물을 닦고는 유리문을 지나 관목 정원으로 나갔다.\n\n\"저 서재의 책이란 책은 모조리 주인 어른의 지하 묘소로 쌓아 올려졌으면 좋겠어요.\" 탠트립이 아침 식사실에서 마주친 집사 프랫에게 말했다. 우리가 알다시피 그녀는 로마에 가 본 적이 있어 고대 유적들을 둘러보았던 것이다. 그리고 그녀는 다른 하인들에게 말할 때면 언제나 캐소본 씨를 \"주인 어른\" 말고 달리 부르기를 한사코 마다했다.\n\n프랫은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그는 주인을 무척 좋아했으나, 탠트립을 더 좋아했다.\n\n자갈길로 나선 도러시아는, 전에 한 번 그랬던 것처럼, 다만 이번에는 다른 까닭으로 망설이며 가까이 있는 나무 무리 사이에서 발걸음을 늦추었다. 그때 그녀는 동반의 노력이 환영받지 못할까 두려워했었다. 이제 그녀는 자신이 움츠러드는 어떤 동반 관계에 스스로를 매어야 하리라는 것을 예감하는 그 장소로 가는 일이 두려웠다. 법도, 세상의 평판도 그녀에게 이를 강요하지 않았으니—오직 남편의 천성과 그녀 자신의 연민만이, 오직 결혼의 실제 멍에가 아닌 이상적인 멍에만이 그러했다. 그녀는 상황 전체를 충분히 또렷이 보면서도 결박되어 있었다. 자기를 애원하는 그 상처 입은 영혼을 그녀는 내려칠 수가 없었다. 그것이 나약함이라면, 도러시아는 나약했다. 하지만 반 시간이 흘러가고 있었으니, 더는 지체해서는 안 되었다. 주목 산책로에 들어섰을 때 그녀는 남편을 볼 수 없었으나, 그 길에는 굽이가 있었으므로, 그녀는 푸른 망토를 두른 그의 모습을 곧 발견하리라 기대하며 걸음을 옮겼다. 그 망토는 따뜻한 벨벳 모자와 함께 쌀쌀한 날 정원에 나설 때 그가 걸치는 겉옷이었다. 길이 조금 갈라져 나간 정자 쪽에서 그가 쉬고 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 모퉁이를 돌자, 돌 탁자 가까이 의자에 앉아 있는 그가 보였다. 그의 두 팔은 탁자 위에 놓여 있었고, 이마는 그 위로 수그러져 있었으며, 푸른 망토는 앞으로 끌려 와 그의 얼굴을 양옆에서 가리고 있었다.\n\n\"어젯밤에 기진하셨구나.\" 도러시아는 처음에 그가 잠든 것이고, 정자란 곳이 쉬기에는 너무 눅눅하다고 여기며 혼잣말을 했다. 하지만 곧 그녀는 근래에 자기가 책을 읽어 줄 때 그가 그 자세를 취하던 것을 떠올렸으니, 마치 다른 어떤 자세보다 그것이 더 편하다는 듯했고, 더러는 그렇게 얼굴을 숙인 채 듣기도 하고 말하기도 했던 것이다. 그녀는 정자 안으로 들어가 말했다. \"왔어요, 에드워드. 저는 준비됐어요.\"\n\n그는 아무런 기척이 없었고, 그녀는 그가 깊이 잠든 것이 분명하다고 생각했다. 그녀는 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 다시 말했다. \"준비됐어요!\" 그래도 그는 꼼짝하지 않았다. 문득 일렁이는 혼란스러운 두려움에 그녀는 그에게로 몸을 숙여 벨벳 모자를 벗기고는 그의 머리에 뺨을 가까이 댄 채, 괴로운 어조로 외쳤다—\n\n\"깨세요, 여보, 깨세요! 제 말을 들어 주세요. 답을 드리러 왔어요.\" 하지만 도러시아는 끝내 그 답을 주지 못했다.\n\n그날 늦게, 리드게이트는 그녀의 침상 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그녀는 헛소리하듯 생각을 입 밖으로 내며, 전날 밤 자신의 마음을 스쳐 간 것들을 되새기고 있었다. 그녀는 그를 알아보고 이름을 부르면서도, 자기가 그에게 모든 것을 설명해야 옳다고 여기는 듯했고, 거듭거듭 그에게 남편에게 모든 것을 설명해 달라고 애원했다.\n\n\"제가 곧 그분께 가겠다고 전해 주세요. 저는 약속할 준비가 됐어요. 다만, 그걸 생각하는 것이 너무도 끔찍해서—그 때문에 제가 병이 났어요. 아주 아픈 건 아니에요. 저는 곧 나을 거예요. 가서 그분께 전해 주세요.\"\n\n하지만 그녀 남편의 귓가에 깃든 침묵은 다시는 깨어지지 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER XLIX.\n\n이 시골 양반이 저질러 놓았구나.\n우물에 돌을 던져 넣기는 쉬워도,\n\n대체 누가 그것을 끄집어낼 것인가?”\n“정말이지 도러시아가 이 일을 알지 못하게 막을 수만 있다면 좋겠소.” 제임스 체텀 경은 이마를 살짝 찌푸리고 입가에 극도의 혐오를 드러내며 말했다.\n\n그는 로윅 그레인지의 서재에서 벽난로 깔개 위에 서서 브룩 씨에게 말하고 있었다. 캐소본 씨를 매장한 다음 날이었고, 도러시아는 아직 방을 나설 수 없는 상태였다.\n\n“그게 어렵다는 걸 알지 않소, 체텀. 그 애는 유언 집행인인 데다, 그런 일들—재산이며 토지며 그런 종류의 일에 직접 관여하고 싶어 하니 말이오. 그 애에겐 나름의 생각이 있어요.” 브룩 씨는 안경을 신경질적으로 코에 걸치며, 손에 든 접힌 서류의 가장자리를 더듬으며 말했다. “게다가 그 애는 직접 처리하고 싶어 할 거요—분명히 말하지만, 유언 집행인으로서 도러시아는 나서고 싶어 할 거란 말이오. 그리고 지난 십이월에 스물한 살이 되었지 않소. 나로서는 아무것도 막을 수 없어요.”\n\n제임스 경은 잠시 말없이 양탄자를 바라보다가, 별안간 눈을 들어 브룩 씨를 똑바로 응시하며 말했다. “우리가 할 수 있는 일을 말씀드리지요. 도러시아가 회복될 때까지 모든 일을 그녀에게 알려서는 안 됩니다. 그리고 움직일 수 있게 되는 즉시 우리 집으로 와야 합니다. 실리아와 아기와 함께 지내는 것이 그녀에게 세상에서 가장 좋은 일일 테고, 그러면서 시간도 흘러갈 겁니다. 그리고 그동안 어르신은 래디슬로를 처리하셔야 합니다. 그를 이 고장에서 내보내셔야 해요.” 여기서 제임스 경의 혐오에 찬 표정이 다시금 온전한 강도로 되살아났다.\n\n브룩 씨는 두 손을 등 뒤로 모으고 창가로 걸어가서, 대답하기 전에 가볍게 몸을 한 번 흔들며 허리를 곧게 폈다.\n\n“그거야 말이야 쉽지, 체텀, 말이야 쉬운 일이오.”\n\n“이보세요, 어르신.” 제임스 경은 분노를 정중한 형식 안에 억누르며 끈질기게 말했다. “그를 이곳으로 데려온 것도 어르신이고, 그를 이곳에 붙잡아 두고 계신 것도 어르신입니다—제 말은, 그에게 일거리를 주심으로써 말입니다.”\n\n“그렇지, 하지만 이유도 대지 않고 단번에 그를 해고할 수는 없잖소, 이보게 체텀. 래디슬로는 더없이 소중한 존재였소, 아주 만족스러웠지. 나는 그를 데려온 것—그를 데려온 것으로 이 지방에 봉사했다고 생각하오, 알겠소.” 브룩 씨는 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 맺었는데, 일부러 몸을 돌려 그 끄덕임을 보여 주었다.\n\n“이 지방이 그 사람 없이 지냈더라면 좋았을 것을, 그것이 제가 드릴 말씀의 전부입니다. 어쨌든 저는 도러시아의 처남으로서, 그녀의 친지들의 어떤 조처로 그가 이곳에 붙잡혀 있는 데 강력히 반대할 자격이 있다고 느낍니다. 제 아내의 언니의 품위에 관한 일을 두고 제가 말할 권리가 있다는 것은, 부디 인정하시겠지요?”\n\n제임스 경은 점점 격해지고 있었다.\n\n“물론이오, 이보게 체텀, 물론이지. 하지만 자네와 나는 생각이 다르오—다르단 말이지—”\n\n“캐소본의 이 처사에 관해서는 다르지 않기를 바랍니다.” 제임스 경이 말을 가로막았다. “그는 도러시아를 더없이 부당하게 욕보였습니다. 이보다 더 비열하고 더 신사답지 못한 행위는 일찍이 없었다고 봅니다—그녀의 가족이 알고 신뢰한 가운데 결혼할 때 작성한 유언장에 이런 종류의 추가 조항을 붙이다니—이는 도러시아에 대한 명백한 모욕입니다!”\n* 추가 조항: 유언장에 덧붙이는 부가 조항(codicil)으로, 원래 유언의 일부를 변경하거나 보충하는 법적 문서.\n\n“글쎄, 알다시피 캐소본은 래디슬로에 관해 조금 비틀려 있었소. 래디슬로가 그 이유를 내게 말해 주었지—그가 택한 성향이 마음에 들지 않았던 거요—래디슬로는 캐소본의 생각들을, 토트니 다곤이니 하는 그런 것들을 대단치 않게 여겼거든. 그리고 내 생각엔 캐소본도 래디슬로가 취한 독립적인 처지가 마음에 들지 않았던 것 같소. 나는 둘 사이에 오간 편지들을 봤다오. 가엾은 캐소본은 책 속에 좀 파묻혀 있었어—세상 물정을 몰랐던 거지.”\n* 토트, 다곤: 토트는 고대 이집트의 지혜의 신, 다곤은 고대 셈족의 신. 캐소본의 신화 연구 대상을 가리킨다.\n\n“래디슬로가 그렇게 채색해서 말하는 거야 좋습니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “하지만 저는 캐소본이 오로지 도러시아 때문에 그를 질투한 것이라고 믿습니다. 그리고 세상 사람들은 그녀가 그에게 무슨 빌미를 주었으리라 짐작할 겁니다. 바로 그 점이 이 일을 이토록 가증스럽게 만드는 거지요—그녀의 이름을 이 젊은이의 이름과 엮어 놓다니 말입니다.”\n\n“이보게 체텀, 이건 아무 결과도 낳지 않을 거요.” 브룩 씨는 자리에 앉아 다시 안경을 걸치며 말했다. “이건 다 캐소본의 별난 성미와 한통속인 일이오. 자, 이 서류만 해도 그렇소, ‘개요 도표’니 뭐니 하고, ‘캐소본 부인의 사용을 위하여’라고 되어 있는데, 유언장과 함께 책상에 잠겨 있었다오. 내 짐작에 그는 도러시아가 자기 연구를 출판해 주기를 바란 게야, 안 그렇소? 그리고 그 애는 그렇게 할 거요, 알다시피. 그 애는 그의 연구에 유난히 깊이 파고들었으니까.”\n\n“이보세요, 어르신.” 제임스 경이 초조하게 말했다. “그건 이 일과 아무 상관도 없습니다. 문제는, 젊은 래디슬로를 멀리 보내는 것이 마땅하다는 데 어르신께서 저와 견해를 같이하시느냐는 겁니다.”\n\n“글쎄, 아니, 그렇게 급한 일은 아니오. 머지않아 어쩌면 일이 저절로 풀릴 수도 있고. 소문으로 말하자면, 알다시피 그를 멀리 보낸다고 소문이 막히는 것도 아니오. 사람들은 근거가 있는 말이 아니라 자기들이 하고 싶은 말을 하는 법이니까.” 브룩 씨는 자기 바람 쪽에 놓인 진실들에 관해서는 예리해지면서 말했다. “나는 어느 정도까지는 래디슬로를 떼어 놓을 수 있소—그에게서 ‘파이어니어’를 빼앗는다든가 그런 식으로 말이오. 하지만 그가 가지 않겠다고 한다면 그를 이 고장 밖으로 내보낼 수는 없어요—가지 않겠다고 하면 말이오.”\n* 파이어니어: 브룩 씨가 후원하는 지역 신문의 이름.\n\n작년 날씨가 어떠했는지나 논하는 듯이 차분하게 고집을 부리며, 끝에 가서는 늘 그렇듯 상냥하게 고개를 끄덕이는 브룩 씨의 모습은 사람의 속을 뒤집어 놓는 완고함의 한 형태였다.\n\n“맙소사!” 제임스 경은 평소 좀처럼 드러내지 않던 격정을 다해 말했다. “그에게 자리를 마련해 줍시다. 그를 위해 돈을 씁시다. 어느 식민지 총독의 수행원으로 갈 수만 있다면! 그램퍼스가 그를 받아 줄지도 모르고—제가 그 일로 풀크에게 편지를 쓸 수도 있습니다.”\n\n“하지만 래디슬로는 소 떼처럼 배에 실려 보내질 사람이 아니오, 이보게. 래디슬로에겐 나름의 생각이 있어. 내 생각엔 그가 내일 당장 나와 헤어진다 해도, 자네는 이 고장에서 그에 관한 소문을 더 많이 듣게 될 거요. 연설하고 문서를 작성하는 그 재능으로 말하자면, 선동가로서 그를 따라올 사람이 거의 없을 테니까—선동가로서 말이오, 알겠소.”\n\n“선동가라뇨!” 제임스 경이 쓰디쓴 어조로 강조하며 말했는데, 이 단어를 제대로 한 음절 한 음절 되뇌는 것만으로도 그 가증스러움을 충분히 드러낼 수 있다고 느끼는 듯했다.\n\n“하지만 사리를 따져 보세요, 체텀. 자, 도러시아 말이오. 자네 말대로 그 애는 가능한 한 빨리 실리아에게 가는 게 좋겠지. 자네 집 지붕 아래 머물 수 있을 테고, 그러는 사이에 일이 조용히 풀릴 수도 있을 거요. 알다시피 우리가 서둘러 대포를 쏘아 댈 건 없소. 스탠디시가 비밀을 지켜 줄 테고, 소문은 알려지기도 전에 낡은 이야기가 될 거요. 스무 가지나 되는 일이 일어나 래디슬로를 데려가 버릴 수도 있어—내가 아무것도 하지 않아도 말이오, 알겠소.”\n\n“그렇다면 어르신께서는 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 말씀으로 결론을 내려도 되겠습니까?”\n\n“거절이라니, 체텀?—아니—거절한다고 한 적은 없소. 하지만 정말이지 내가 무얼 할 수 있을지 모르겠단 말이오. 래디슬로는 신사잖소.”\n\n“그 말씀을 들으니 기쁘군요!” 제임스 경은 짜증이 치밀어 잠시 자제력을 잃고 말했다. “캐소본은 분명 신사가 아니었습니다.”\n\n“글쎄, 만약 그가 그 애가 아예 재혼하지 못하도록 추가 조항을 달았더라면 더 나빴을 거요, 알다시피.”\n\n“그건 모를 일입니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다. “그게 차라리 덜 무례했겠지요.”\n\n“가엾은 캐소본의 변덕 가운데 하나요! 그 발작이 그의 머리를 좀 흐트러뜨린 거지. 다 부질없는 일이오. 그 애는 래디슬로와 결혼하고 싶어 하지 않으니까.”\n\n“하지만 이 추가 조항은 모든 사람이 그녀가 그러고 싶어 했다고 믿게끔 짜여 있습니다. 저는 도러시아에 관해서는 그런 종류의 것을 조금도 믿지 않습니다.” 제임스 경은 이렇게 말하고 나서 이맛살을 찌푸리며 덧붙였다. “하지만 래디슬로는 의심스럽습니다. 솔직히 말씀드리지요, 저는 래디슬로를 의심합니다.”\n\n“그런 근거로는 내가 당장 어떤 조처도 취할 수 없소, 체텀. 사실, 만에 하나 그를 짐짝처럼 부쳐 보내는 게—그를 노퍽섬으로 보낸다든가 하는 그런 일이 가능하다 해도—그 내막을 아는 사람들에게는 도러시아에게 도리어 더 나쁘게 보일 거요. 마치 우리가 그 애를 못 믿는 것처럼—그 애를 의심하는 것처럼 보일 테니까, 알겠소.”\n\n* 노퍽섬: 당시 영국의 유배지로 쓰이던 남태평양의 섬.\n브룩 씨가 부인할 수 없는 논거를 짚어 냈다는 사실이 제임스 경의 마음을 누그러뜨리지는 못했다. 그는 더 이상 다툴 뜻이 없음을 내비치며 모자를 집으려 손을 뻗었고, 여전히 다소 열기를 띤 채 말했다—\n\n“그렇다면 저로서는 이 말밖에 드릴 게 없군요. 도러시아는 한 번 희생되었다고 생각합니다. 그녀의 친지들이 너무 무심했던 탓이지요. 이제 저는 그녀의 오빠로서 그녀를 지키기 위해 할 수 있는 일을 하겠습니다.”\n\n“그 애를 가능한 한 빨리 프레시트로 데려가는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없을 거요, 체텀. 그 계획에 나는 전적으로 찬성하오.” 브룩 씨는 논쟁에서 이긴 것이 흡족하여 말했다. 의회 해산이 언제 닥칠지 모르고, 나라의 이익에 가장 잘 부합하는 노선이 무엇인지 유권자들을 설득해야 하는 이 시기에 래디슬로와 헤어진다는 것은 그에게 몹시 곤란한 일이었을 터였다. 브룩 씨는 자신이 의회로 복귀함으로써 그 목적을 이룰 수 있다고 진심으로 믿었다. 그는 자기 정신의 힘을 나라에 정직하게 바쳤던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER L.\n\n“아니, 내 아버지의 영혼을 두고 맹세컨대! 그럴 수는 없지,”\n선장이 말했다. “여기서 설교 따위는 못 하리라,\n\n우리는 여기서 복음을 해석하거나 가르치지 않으리니,\n우리 모두는 위대하신 하느님을 믿노라.” 그가 말했다.\n\n“그자는 무언가 골치 아픈 문제를 뿌리려 하는 것이로다.” — 「캔터베리 이야기」\n도로시아는 프레시트 저택에서 거의 일주일을 무사히 지낸 뒤에야 위험한 질문들을 던지기 시작했다. 이제 그녀는 아침마다 작은 온실로 통하는 위층의 가장 예쁜 거실에서 실리아와 함께 앉아 있었다. 흰빛과 연보라빛으로 차려입은 실리아는 마치 여러 빛깔이 뒤섞인 제비꽃 한 다발 같았고, 아기의 놀라운 행동들을 지켜보고 있었는데, 그 행동들이 미숙한 그녀의 마음에는 너무도 모호해서 모든 대화가 신탁을 내리는 유모에게 그 해석을 청하는 일로 자꾸만 끊기곤 했다. 도로시아는 과부의 상복 차림으로 그 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그 표정이 실리아를 다소 언짢게 했으니, 지나치게 슬퍼 보였던 까닭이다. 아기는 더없이 건강했고, 게다가 살아 있는 동안 그토록 따분하고 성가셨던 남편이었으며, 또 그 위에 — 아무튼, 아무튼! 물론 제임스 경은 실리아에게 모든 것을 이야기해 주면서, 도로시아가 어쩔 수 없이 알게 되기 전에 미리 알아서는 안 된다고 강하게 당부했던 터였다.\n\n하지만 브룩 씨는 도로시아가 자기에게 행동이 맡겨진 자리에서 오래도록 수동적으로 머물러 있지 않으리라고 정확히 내다보았다. 그녀는 결혼 당시에 작성된 남편의 유언 내용을 알고 있었으니, 자신의 처지를 분명히 의식하게 되자마자 그녀의 마음은 로윅 영지의 소유주이자 거기 딸린 성직 추천권을 지닌 사람으로서 마땅히 해야 할 일을 두고 조용히 골몰하기 시작했다.\n\n어느 날 아침 삼촌이 평소처럼 찾아왔는데, 그날따라 태도에 유난히 활기가 넘쳤다. 그는 이제 의회가 곧 해산되리라는 것이 꽤 확실해졌기 때문이라고 그 까닭을 설명했다. 도로시아가 말했다.\n“삼촌, 이제 로윅의 성직을 누구에게 맡길지 제가 생각해 보아야 마땅하겠어요. 터커 씨에게 자리가 마련된 뒤로, 남편이 자기 후임으로 어떤 성직자를 염두에 두고 있다고 말하는 걸 한 번도 들은 적이 없거든요. 이제 제가 열쇠를 받아 로윅으로 가서 남편의 서류를 모두 살펴보아야 할 것 같아요. 그분의 뜻을 밝혀 줄 무언가가 있을지도 모르니까요.”\n\n“서두를 것 없다, 얘야.” 브룩 씨가 조용히 말했다. “이따가, 알다시피, 네가 원하면 가도 좋아. 하지만 내가 책상이며 서랍 속을 죽 훑어보았는데 — 아무것도 없더구나 — 알다시피 깊이 있는 주제들뿐, 그 밖에는 아무것도 없었어 — 유언장 말고는 말이지. 모든 건 이따가 하면 돼. 성직 자리로 말하자면, 이미 청탁을 하나 받았단다 — 꽤 괜찮다고 해야겠지. 타이크 씨를 강력히 추천받았어 — 전에 그자가 한 자리를 얻는 데 내가 좀 거든 적이 있지. 사도 같은 사람이라더구나 — 알다시피 네게 딱 어울릴 만한 부류 말이다, 얘야.”\n\n“캐소본 씨가 자기 뜻을 적어 남긴 게 없다면, 삼촌, 저는 그분에 대해 더 자세히 알아보고 스스로 판단하고 싶어요. 어쩌면 유언장에 무언가를 덧붙였을지도 모르고요 — 저를 위한 어떤 지시가 있을지도 모르잖아요.” 도로시아가 말했다. 그녀는 줄곧 남편의 저작과 관련해 이런 추측을 마음에 품고 있었던 것이다.\n\n“목사관에 대해서는 아무것도 없다, 얘야 — 아무것도.” 브룩 씨가 자리에서 일어나 떠나려고 조카딸들에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. “알다시피 그의 연구에 대해서도 마찬가지야. 유언장에는 아무것도 없어.”\n\n도로시아의 입술이 떨렸다.\n\n“자, 아직은 이런 일들을 생각해서는 안 된다, 얘야. 이따가, 알다시피.”\n\n“저는 이제 아주 멀쩡해요, 삼촌. 저 자신을 좀 부지런히 움직이고 싶어요.”\n\n“그래, 그래, 두고 보자꾸나. 하지만 난 지금 서둘러 가야겠다 — 지금 할 일이 끝도 없어 — 위기야 — 정치적 위기란다, 알다시피. 그리고 여기 실리아와 이 작은 사내가 있구나 — 너는 이제 이모가 되었고, 알다시피, 나는 일종의 할아버지가 된 셈이지.” 브룩 씨는 차분한 다급함 속에서 말했으니, 어서 빠져나가 채텀에게, 도로시아가 모든 일을 들여다보겠다고 고집한다면 그것은 자기(브룩 씨)의 잘못이 아니라고 말하고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다.\n\n삼촌이 방을 나가자 도로시아는 의자에 깊이 몸을 묻고, 깍지 낀 두 손으로 시선을 떨군 채 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n“봐, 도도! 얘 좀 봐! 이런 거 본 적 있어?” 실리아가 편안하고 또박또박한 말투로 말했다.\n\n“뭘, 키티?” 도로시아가 다소 멍하니 눈을 들며 말했다.\n\n“뭐긴? 아니, 얘 윗입술 말이야. 어떻게 끌어내리는지 봐, 마치 얼굴을 찡그리려는 것처럼. 신기하지 않아! 얘도 나름대로 생각이 있나 봐. 유모가 여기 있으면 좋을 텐데. 얘 좀 봐.”\n\n한동안 고여 있던 커다란 눈물 한 방울이 도로시아의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 그녀는 고개를 들어 미소 지으려 애썼다.\n\n“슬퍼하지 마, 도도. 아기에게 입 맞춰 줘. 뭘 그렇게 골똘히 생각해? 언니는 분명 할 수 있는 건 다 했어, 그것도 너무 지나치게 많이. 이제는 행복해야 해.”\n\n“제임스 경이 나를 로윅까지 태워다 줄 수 있을지 모르겠구나. 모든 걸 살펴보고 싶어 — 나를 위해 적어 둔 말이 있었는지 보고 싶어.”\n\n“리드게이트 씨가 가도 좋다고 할 때까지는 가면 안 돼. 아직 그렇게 말하지 않았잖아 (여기 왔구나, 유모. 아기를 받아 회랑을 오르내리며 걸어 줘요). 게다가 언니는 늘 그렇듯이 머릿속에 잘못된 생각을 품고 있어, 도도 — 난 알 수 있어. 그게 나를 속상하게 해.”\n\n“내가 어디가 잘못됐다는 거니, 키티?” 도로시아가 자못 온순하게 말했다. 그녀는 이제 거의 실리아가 자기보다 현명하다고 여길 지경이 되어, 자신의 잘못된 생각이 무엇일까 약간의 두려움을 품고 진심으로 궁금해하고 있었다. 실리아는 자기가 유리한 처지에 있음을 느꼈고, 그것을 활용하기로 마음먹었다. 식구들 중 누구도 자기만큼 도도를 잘 알거나 그녀를 잘 다룰 줄 모른다고 여겼다. 아기를 낳은 뒤로 실리아는 자신의 정신적 견고함과 차분한 지혜에 대한 새로운 자각을 갖게 되었다. 아기가 있는 곳에서는 만사가 충분히 제대로 돌아가고, 일반적으로 오류란 바로 그 중심을 잡아 주는 균형의 힘이 결여된 데서 비롯된다는 것이 분명해 보였다.\n\n“언니가 무슨 생각을 하는지 나는 더없이 잘 알겠어, 도도.” 실리아가 말했다. “언니는 지금 캐소본 씨가 바랐다는 이유만으로, 자기한테 거북한 일이 또 없는지 찾아내려는 거잖아. 마치 이제껏 충분히 거북하지 않았다는 듯이 말이야. 그런데 그분은 그럴 자격이 없어, 언니도 곧 알게 될 거야. 그분은 아주 못되게 굴었어. 제임스는 그분한테 이루 말할 수 없이 화가 나 있어. 그러니 마음의 준비를 하도록 차라리 내가 말해 주는 게 낫겠어.”\n\n“실리아,” 도로시아가 애원하듯 말했다. “네 말이 나를 괴롭게 하는구나. 무슨 뜻인지 당장 말해 줘.” 캐소본 씨가 재산을 자기에게서 거두어 갔을지도 모른다는 생각이 그녀의 머리를 스쳤지만 — 그것은 그리 괴로운 일은 아닐 터였다.\n\n“그게 말이야, 그분이 유언장에 추가 조항을 만들어서, 언니가 결혼하면 재산이 모두 언니에게서 거두어진다고 해 놨어 — 그러니까 내 말은 —”\n\n“그건 아무래도 상관없어.” 도로시아가 격하게 말을 가로막았다.\n\n“하지만 언니가 래디슬로 씨와 결혼할 경우에 그렇다는 거야, 다른 누구도 아니고.” 실리아가 끈질긴 침착함으로 말을 이었다. “물론 한편으로는 아무 상관없는 일이지 — 언니는 절대로 래디슬로 씨와 결혼하지 않을 테니까. 하지만 그래서 캐소본 씨가 더더욱 못된 거야.”\n\n피가 도로시아의 얼굴과 목으로 고통스럽게 솟구쳤다. 하지만 실리아는 자기가 정신을 차리게 해 줄 사실의 처방이라고 여기는 것을 베풀고 있는 중이었다. 도도의 건강을 그토록 망쳐 놓은 것은 바로 그런 잡념을 떠안는 짓이었다. 그래서 실리아는 마치 아기의 배냇저고리에 대해 이야기하기라도 하듯 무심한 어조로 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n“제임스가 그렇게 말해. 그건 가증스러운 짓이고, 신사답지 못한 짓이라고 했어. 그리고 제임스보다 더 나은 판단을 할 사람은 없어. 마치 캐소본 씨는 언니가 래디슬로 씨와 결혼하고 싶어 할 거라고 사람들이 믿게 만들려는 것 같아 — 우스꽝스러운 일이지. 다만 제임스 말로는, 래디슬로 씨가 언니 돈을 노리고 결혼하려는 걸 막으려는 거였대 — 마치 그 사람이 언니한테 청혼할 생각이라도 할 것처럼 말이야. 캐드월래더 부인은 차라리 흰쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람하고 결혼하는 게 낫겠다고 했어! 그건 그렇고 나는 아기를 좀 보러 가야겠어.” 실리아는 조금도 어조를 바꾸지 않은 채 이렇게 덧붙이고는, 가벼운 숄을 어깨에 두르고 종종걸음으로 사라졌다.\n\n이때쯤 도로시아는 다시 온몸이 싸늘해져 있었고, 이제는 무력하게 의자에 몸을 던지듯 기댔다. 그 순간의 자신의 경험을, 그녀는 자기 삶이 새로운 형태를 띠어 가고 있다는, 기억이 새로 꿈틀거리는 기관들의 움직임에 미처 적응하지 못하는 어떤 변태를 겪고 있다는, 막연하고 불안한 의식에 견줄 수도 있었으리라. 모든 것이 그 양상을 바꾸고 있었다. 남편의 행동, 그를 향한 자신의 의무감에 찬 마음, 두 사람 사이의 모든 갈등 — 그리고 그보다 더, 윌 래디슬로와의 관계 전부가. 그녀의 세계는 발작적인 변화의 와중에 있었다. 그녀가 자기 자신에게 분명히 말할 수 있는 단 한 가지는, 기다리며 새롭게 생각해 보아야 한다는 것뿐이었다. 한 가지 변화는 마치 그것이 죄이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 두렵게 했다. 그것은 세상을 떠난 남편으로부터의 격렬한 혐오의 충격이었으니, 남편은 숨겨진 생각들을 품고 있었고, 어쩌면 그녀의 말과 행동 모두를 왜곡해 받아들였을지도 몰랐다. 그러다가 그녀는 자신을 또한 떨리게 만드는 또 다른 변화를 의식했다. 그것은 윌 래디슬로를 향한 갑작스럽고 낯선 마음의 갈망이었다. 그가 어떤 상황에서든 자신의 연인이 될 수 있으리라는 생각은 이제껏 한 번도 그녀의 마음에 떠오른 적이 없었다. 그런데 다른 누군가가 그를 그런 시선으로 생각해 왔다는 — 어쩌면 그 자신도 그런 가능성을 의식하고 있었을지 모른다는 — 갑작스러운 폭로가 어떤 영향을 미쳤을지 헤아려 보라. 게다가 어울리지 않는 여러 조건들과 쉽사리 풀리지 않을 의문들이 황급히 한꺼번에 밀려드는 환영까지 더해진 채로 말이다.\n\n오랜 시간이 지난 것 같았다 — 얼마나 오래인지 그녀는 알 수 없었다 — 실리아가 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들려올 때까지는. “이제 됐어요, 유모. 얘는 내 무릎에서 얌전히 있을 거예요. 점심 들러 가요, 그리고 개럿더러 옆방에 있게 해 줘요. 내 생각은 말이야, 도도,” 실리아는 도로시아가 의자에 기대 앉아 잠자코 있을 듯하다는 것 말고는 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 채 말을 이었다. “캐소본 씨가 심술궂었다는 거야. 나는 그분을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없고, 제임스도 마찬가지였어. 내 생각엔 그분 입가가 끔찍하게 심술궂었던 것 같아. 그리고 이제 그분이 이런 식으로 굴었으니, 종교가 언니더러 그분 때문에 자기 마음을 괴롭히라고 요구하지는 않을 거야, 분명히. 그분이 데려가졌다면, 그건 자비고, 언니는 감사해야 마땅해. 우리는 슬퍼해서는 안 돼, 그렇지, 아가?” 실리아는 세상의 무의식적인 중심이자 균형추인 그 존재에게 은밀히 속삭이듯 말했으니, 그 존재는 손톱까지 완전히 갖춘 더없이 놀라운 두 주먹을 지녔고, 모자를 벗기면 정말이지 무언가를 — 무엇이라 할 수 없는 무언가를 만들기에 충분할 만큼의 머리카락이 나 있었다. 한마디로, 그는 서양식 형상을 한 부처였다.\n\n이 위기의 순간에 리드게이트가 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔고, 그가 처음 꺼낸 말 가운데 하나는 이러했다. “전보다 안색이 좋지 않으신 것 같군요, 캐소본 부인. 마음이 동요하셨습니까? 맥을 좀 짚어 보게 해 주십시오.” 도로시아의 손은 대리석처럼 차가웠다.\n\n“언니는 서류를 살펴보러 로윅에 가고 싶어 해요.” 실리아가 말했다. “가면 안 되겠죠, 그렇죠?”\n\n리드게이트는 잠시 말이 없었다. 그러다가 도로시아를 바라보며 말했다. “저도 잘 모르겠습니다. 제 생각에는 캐소본 부인께서 마음에 가장 큰 평온을 주는 일을 하셔야 할 것 같습니다. 그 평온이 행동을 금지당함으로써 늘 찾아오는 것은 아니니까요.”\n\n“고맙습니다.” 도로시아가 기운을 내며 말했다. “그게 현명한 말씀이라는 걸 잘 알겠어요. 제가 처리해야 할 일이 너무 많아요. 어째서 제가 여기 빈둥거리며 앉아 있어야 하나요?” 그러고 나서 자신의 동요와 무관한 화제를 떠올리려 애쓰며 그녀는 불쑥 덧붙였다. “리드게이트 씨, 당신은 미들마치 사람들을 모두 아실 것 같은데요. 제가 여쭤볼 게 많을 거예요. 이제 저는 진지하게 해야 할 일들이 있어요. 제게 맡겨야 할 성직 자리가 하나 있거든요. 타이크 씨도 아실 테고, 또 그 모든 —” 하지만 도로시아의 노력은 그녀에게 너무 벅찼다. 그녀는 말을 멈추고 흐느껴 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n리드게이트는 그녀에게 각성제 한 모금을 마시게 했다.\n\n“캐소본 부인이 하고 싶은 대로 하시게 두십시오.” 그는 집을 나서기 전에 만나기를 청했던 제임스 경에게 말했다. “제 생각에 그분께는 다른 어떤 처방보다도 완전한 자유가 필요합니다.”\n\n그녀의 머릿속이 흥분 상태에 있는 동안 그녀를 돌본 덕분에, 그는 그녀의 삶의 시련에 관해 몇 가지 참된 결론을 내릴 수 있었다. 그는 그녀가 자기 억제의 긴장과 갈등으로 고통받아 왔으며, 이제는 자신이 풀려나온 우리와는 또 다른 종류의 우리 안에 갇혀 있을 뿐이라고 느끼게 될 공산이 크다고 확신했다.\n\n실리아가 이미 도로시아에게 유언장에 관한 불쾌한 사실을 말해 버렸음을 알게 되자, 리드게이트의 충고는 제임스 경이 따르기에 한결 수월해졌다. 이제는 어쩔 도리가 없었고 — 필요한 일을 처리하는 것을 더 미룰 이유도 없었다. 그래서 이튿날 제임스 경은 자신을 로윅까지 태워다 달라는 그녀의 청에 즉시 응했다.\n\n“지금 당장은 거기 머무르고 싶지 않아요.” 도로시아가 말했다. “도저히 견디기 힘들 것 같아요. 저는 실리아와 함께 프레시트에 있는 게 훨씬 행복해요. 멀찍이서 바라보는 편이 로윅에서 무엇을 해야 할지 더 잘 생각할 수 있을 것 같아요. 그리고 그레인지에서 삼촌과 함께 얼마간 지내면서, 예전에 거닐던 길들과 마을 사람들 사이를 두루 돌아다니고 싶기도 해요.”\n\n“아직은 아닌 것 같습니다. 삼촌께서 정치적인 손님들을 맞이하고 계시니, 그런 일들에서 비켜나 계시는 편이 낫습니다.” 제임스 경이 말했으니, 그 순간 그는 그레인지를 주로 젊은 래디슬로가 드나드는 곳으로 떠올리고 있었다. 하지만 유언장의 거슬리는 부분에 대해서는 그와 도로시아 사이에 단 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 실로 두 사람 모두 그것을 서로 입에 올리는 일은 불가능하다고 느꼈다. 제임스 경은 남자들과 있을 때조차 불쾌한 화제에 대해서는 수줍어했고, 도로시아가 만약 그 일을 두고 입을 연다면 가장 하고 싶었을 그 한마디는, 지금으로서는 남편의 부당함을 한층 더 드러내는 일처럼 여겨졌기에 그녀에게 금지되어 있었다. 그렇지만 그녀는 윌 래디슬로의 재산에 대한 도의적 권리를 두고 자신과 남편 사이에 오갔던 일을 제임스 경이 알아주기를 바라기는 했다. 그리하면, 자신에게 그러하듯 그에게도 분명해지리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 즉, 남편의 그 기이하고 무례한 단서 조항은 주로 그 권리라는 생각에 대한 남편의 쓰라린 저항에서 비롯된 것이지, 단지 말로 꺼내기 더 어려운 사사로운 감정 때문만은 아니라는 것이. 또한, 인정하건대, 도로시아는 윌을 위해서도 이 사실이 알려지기를 바랐으니, 친구들이 그를 그저 캐소본 씨의 자선의 대상쯤으로만 여기는 듯했기 때문이다. 어째서 그가 흰쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람에 견주어져야 한단 말인가? 캐드월래더 부인에게서 인용된 그 말은, 마치 어둠 속에서 장난꾸러기 손가락이 빚어낸 조롱 섞인 우스꽝스러운 모사처럼 느껴졌다.\n\n로윅에서 도로시아는 책상과 서랍을 뒤졌다 — 남편이 사적인 글을 보관해 두던 모든 곳을 뒤졌지만, 자신에게 특별히 부친 종이라곤 그 “개요표” 말고는 찾지 못했는데, 그것은 아마도 그녀를 인도하기 위해 의도된 수많은 지시의 시작에 불과했을 것이다. 도로시아에게 노고를 유산으로 물려주는 이 일에서도, 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로, 캐소본 씨는 더디고 머뭇거렸으니, 자기 저작을 실행할 때 그랬듯이 그것을 물려주는 계획에서도 어둑하고 발목을 붙드는 매질 속을 무겁게 헤쳐 나가는 듯한 느낌에 짓눌렸던 것이다. 자기가 준비해 둔 것을 정리할 도로시아의 능력에 대한 불신은, 다른 어떤 편집자에 대한 불신에 의해서만 겨우 가라앉았다. 하지만 그는 마침내 도로시아의 천성에서 스스로 의지할 신뢰를 빚어내기에 이르렀으니, 그녀라면 자기가 하기로 결심한 일은 해낼 수 있으리라는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 그녀가 자기 이름이 새겨진 무덤을 세우겠다는 약속의 족쇄에 묶인 채 고되게 일하는 모습을 기꺼이 상상했다. (캐소본 씨가 그 앞으로 나올 권들을 무덤이라 불렀다는 말은 아니다. 그는 그것들을 모든 신화의 열쇠라 불렀다.) 하지만 세월은 그를 앞질러 가며 그의 계획을 뒤처지게 만들었으니, 그는 도로시아의 삶을 자신의 차가운 손아귀에 붙들어 두려고 그 약속만을 청할 시간밖에 없었던 것이다.\n\n그 손아귀는 미끄러져 빠져나갔다. 연민의 밑바닥에서 우러나온 맹세에 묶였더라면, 그녀는 자신의 판단이 충실함의 봉헌이라는 — 그 자체로 지고한 쓸모인 — 그것 말고는 어떤 쓸모도 없는 헛된 일이라고 속삭이는 그 노고를 떠맡을 수도 있었으리라. 하지만 이제 그녀의 판단은, 의무에 찬 헌신에 통제되는 대신, 지난날의 결합 속에 비밀과 의심이라는 숨겨진 소외가 도사리고 있었다는 쓰라린 발견에 의해 활동을 시작했다. 살아서 고통받는 남자는 더 이상 그녀 앞에 있지 않아 연민을 일깨우지 못했다. 남은 것은 오직, 그녀가 믿었던 것보다 생각이 더 낮았던, 자신을 위한 터무니없는 권리 주장이 자기 인격에 대한 그 세심한 배려마저 눈멀게 하여, 평범한 명예심을 지닌 사람들에게 충격을 줌으로써 스스로 자기 자존심을 꺾어 버리게 만든 한 남편에게 고통스럽게 복종했던 일에 대한 회고뿐이었다. 그 끊어진 인연의 표징인 재산으로 말하자면, 소유에 따르는 의무들만 없었다면 — 그녀가 마땅히 외면해서는 안 되는 그 의무들만 없었다면 — 그녀는 기꺼이 그것에서 벗어나, 자신에게 본래 정해져 있던 원래의 재산 말고는 아무것도 갖지 않기를 바랐을 것이다. 이 재산을 둘러싸고 골치 아픈 의문들이 한사코 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그것의 절반은 윌 래디슬로에게 돌아가야 마땅하다고 생각한 것이 옳지 않았던가? — 하지만 이제 그녀가 그 정의로운 행동을 하기란 불가능해지지 않았던가? 캐소본 씨는 그녀를 막을 잔인하리만치 효과적인 수단을 취해 놓았으니, 그를 향한 분노를 가슴에 품고 있으면서도, 그의 의도를 보란 듯이 따돌리는 것처럼 보이는 어떤 행동도 그녀에게는 역겹게 느껴졌다.\n\n살펴보고 싶은 사무 관련 서류들을 챙긴 뒤, 그녀는 다시 책상과 서랍을 잠갔다 — 그녀를 위한 사사로운 말이라곤 한 마디도 없이 텅 빈 채로 — 남편이 홀로 골똘히 생각에 잠겼을 때 그의 마음이 변명이나 해명으로 그녀에게 향한 적이 있었다는 어떤 흔적도 없이 텅 빈 채로. 그리고 그녀는 그의 마지막 모진 요구와 자기 권력에 대한 그의 마지막 해로운 주장 둘레에 침묵이 깨지지 않은 채 남아 있다는 느낌을 안고 프레시트로 돌아갔다.\n\n이제 도로시아는 당장의 의무들로 생각을 돌리려 애썼고, 그중 하나는 다른 이들이 기어이 그녀에게 일깨워 주려 작정한 종류의 것이었다. 리드게이트의 귀는 그녀가 성직 자리를 언급한 것을 놓치지 않고 열심히 붙들었으며, 그는 되도록 빨리 그 화제를 다시 꺼냈으니, 여기서 한때 만족스럽지 못한 양심으로 던졌던 그 결정표를 만회할 가능성을 보았던 것이다. “타이크 씨에 대해 무언가 말씀드리는 대신,” 그가 말했다. “저는 다른 사람에 대해 이야기하고 싶습니다 — 세인트 보톨프 교회의 교구 목사인 페어브라더 씨 말입니다. 그의 성직록은 보잘것없어서, 자신과 가족을 위한 옹색한 생계만을 마련해 줍니다. 그의 어머니와 이모, 누이가 모두 그와 함께 살며 그에게 기대고 있지요. 제 생각엔 그들 때문에 그가 결혼을 못 한 것 같습니다. 저는 그분의 설교만큼 훌륭한 설교를 들어 본 적이 없습니다 — 그토록 소박하고 거침없는 웅변을요. 그분이라면 옛 래티머의 뒤를 이어 세인트 폴 십자가에서 설교했어도 손색이 없었을 겁니다. 그의 이야기는 어떤 주제에 대해서든 꼭 그만큼 훌륭합니다 — 독창적이고, 단순하고, 명료하지요. 저는 그를 비범한 사람이라고 생각합니다. 그는 지금까지 해 온 것보다 더 많은 일을 했어야 마땅한 사람입니다.”\n\n“어째서 그분이 더 많은 일을 하지 못했나요?” 도로시아가 물었으니, 이제 그녀는 자신이 본래 품었던 뜻 아래로 미끄러져 내린 모든 이에게 관심이 있었다.\n\n“그건 어려운 질문입니다.” 리드게이트가 말했다. “저 자신도 옳은 일을 제대로 풀어 나가기란 유난히 어렵다는 걸 깨닫고 있습니다. 한꺼번에 잡아당기는 끈이 너무 많거든요. 페어브라더는 자기가 잘못된 직업을 택했다고 종종 넌지시 말합니다. 그는 가난한 성직자의 범위보다 더 넓은 영역을 원하는데, 짐작건대 그를 밀어 줄 연줄이 없는 것 같습니다. 그는 박물학과 여러 과학적 사안을 무척 좋아하는데, 이런 취향을 자기 처지와 조화시키는 데 애를 먹습니다. 그에게는 여유 자금이 없습니다 — 쓸 돈조차 빠듯하지요. 그리고 그것이 그를 카드놀이로 이끌었습니다 — 미들마치는 휘스트가 성행하는 곳이거든요. 그는 정말로 돈을 걸고 놀고, 꽤 많이 땁니다. 물론 그 때문에 자기보다 좀 못한 사람들과 어울리게 되고, 어떤 일들에는 소홀해지지요. 그럼에도 그 모든 것을 다 감안해 그를 전체로서 바라보면, 저는 그가 제가 알던 가장 흠 없는 사람들 중 하나라고 생각합니다. 그에게는 독기도 이중성도 없는데, 그런 것들은 흔히 더 반듯한 겉모습과 함께 가곤 하지요.”\n\n“그분이 그 습관 때문에 양심의 가책을 느끼는지 궁금하네요.” 도로시아가 말했다. “그것을 그만두기를 바라는지 궁금해요.”\n\n“풍족한 처지로 옮겨진다면 분명 그만둘 겁니다. 다른 일에 쓸 시간이 생겨서 기뻐하겠지요.”\n\n“삼촌은 타이크 씨가 사도 같은 사람으로 일컬어진다고 하셨어요.” 도로시아가 생각에 잠겨 말했다. 그녀는 원시적인 열정의 시대를 되살릴 수 있기를 바라면서도, 한편으로는 우연히 얻은 돈에서 페어브라더 씨를 구해 내고픈 강한 바람으로 그를 떠올리고 있었다.\n\n“페어브라더가 사도 같은 사람이라고는 감히 말하지 않겠습니다.” 리드게이트가 말했다. “그의 처지는 사도들의 처지와는 사뭇 다르니까요. 그는 그저 자신이 그 삶을 더 낫게 만들어 보려 애써야 하는 교구민들 가운데 있는 한 명의 목사일 뿐입니다. 실제로 제가 보기에 요즘 사도 같다고 일컬어지는 것은, 목사가 주역으로 나서지 않는 모든 일에 대한 조바심에 지나지 않습니다. 저는 병원에서 타이크 씨에게서 그런 모습을 좀 봅니다. 그의 교리 가운데 상당 부분은 사람들로 하여금 거북하게나마 자신을 의식하도록 몰아붙이는 일종의 옥죄기 같은 것이지요. 게다가 로윅에 사도 같은 사람이라니! — 그라면 성 프란체스코가 그랬듯이, 새들에게 설교해야 한다고 생각해야 할 겁니다.”\n\n“맞는 말씀이에요.” 도로시아가 말했다. “우리 농부들과 일꾼들이 그런 가르침에서 어떤 관념을 얻게 될지 상상하기 어렵네요. 저는 타이크 씨의 설교집 한 권을 들여다보고 있었어요. 그런 설교는 로윅에서는 아무 쓸모가 없을 거예요 — 그러니까 전가된 의나 요한 묵시록의 예언 같은 것 말이에요. 저는 늘 기독교가 가르쳐지는 여러 방식에 대해 생각해 왔는데, 어떤 한 방식이 다른 어떤 방식보다 더 넓은 축복이 되는 것을 발견할 때면, 저는 그것을 가장 참된 것으로 붙듭니다 — 그러니까 온갖 종류의 선을 가장 많이 품어 안고, 가장 많은 사람을 그 나눔에 끌어들이는 방식 말이에요. 너무 많이 단죄하기보다는 너무 많이 용서하는 편이 분명 더 낫지요. 하지만 저는 페어브라더 씨를 만나고 그분의 설교를 들어 보고 싶어요.”\n\n“그러십시오.” 리드게이트가 말했다. “저는 그 효과를 믿습니다. 그는 많은 사랑을 받지만, 그에게도 적이 있긴 합니다. 유능한 사람이 자기와 의견을 달리하는 것을 용서하지 못하는 사람들은 언제나 있는 법이니까요. 그리고 그 돈 따는 일은 정말이지 하나의 오점이지요. 물론 부인께서는 미들마치 사람들을 많이 만나지 않으시겠지만, 브룩 씨를 늘 만나는 래디슬로 씨는 페어브라더 씨네 노부인들과 절친한 사이여서, 그 목사를 기꺼이 칭송할 겁니다. 노부인들 중 한 분 — 노블 양이라는 이모 되시는 분은 — 자기를 잊은 선량함의 놀랍도록 진기한 한 폭의 그림 같은데, 래디슬로가 가끔 그분을 모시고 다니지요. 어느 날 뒷골목에서 그들을 마주친 적이 있습니다. 부인께서도 래디슬로의 모습을 아시잖습니까 — 코트와 조끼를 걸친 일종의 다프니스 같은 모습 말입니다. 그리고 이 자그마한 노처녀가 그의 팔에 닿으려 손을 뻗고 있었는데 — 두 사람은 마치 낭만 희극에서 빠져나온 한 쌍처럼 보였습니다. 하지만 페어브라더에 관한 가장 좋은 증거는 그를 만나 그의 말을 들어 보는 것이지요.”\n\n다행히도 이 대화가 오갈 때 도로시아는 자신의 사적인 거실에 있었고, 리드게이트가 별 뜻 없이 래디슬로를 입에 올린 것이 그녀를 괴롭게 하는 모습을 지켜볼 사람이 곁에 아무도 없었다. 사사로운 잡담에 관한 한 늘 그러하듯, 리드게이트는 윌이 캐소본 부인을 흠모하는 것 같다던 로저먼드의 말을 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그 순간 그는 오로지 페어브라더 가족을 천거하는 데에만 마음을 쓰고 있었고, 미리 반대를 막아 두려고 그 목사에 대해 할 수 있는 가장 나쁜 말을 일부러 강조했던 것이다. 캐소본 씨가 죽은 뒤 몇 주 동안 그는 래디슬로를 거의 만나지 못했고, 브룩 씨의 비밀 비서가 캐소본 부인에게 위험한 화제라는 것을 경고해 줄 어떤 소문도 듣지 못했다. 그가 떠난 뒤, 래디슬로에 대한 그의 묘사는 그녀의 마음속에 오래 머무르며 로윅 성직 문제와 그 자리를 다투었다. 윌 래디슬로는 자신에 대해 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까? 예전에는 결코 그러지 않던 식으로 자신의 두 뺨을 화끈거리게 만든 그 사실을 그가 듣게 될까? 그리고 그것을 들으면 그는 어떤 기분이 들까? — 하지만 그녀는 그가 그 자그마한 노처녀를 내려다보며 미소 짓는 모습을 더없이 또렷하게 그려 볼 수 있었다. 흰쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람이라니! — 오히려 그는 모든 사람의 감정 속으로 들어가, 무쇠 같은 저항으로 자기 생각을 밀어붙이는 대신 다른 이들의 생각의 무게를 함께 받아 줄 수 있는 사람이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LI.\n\n당파도 자연의 일부이니, 그대는 보리라\n논리의 힘으로 둘이 어떻게 일치하는지를.\n\n다수가 하나 안에, 하나가 다수 안에 있고,\n전체는 일부가 아니며, 일부는 임의의 것과 같지 않다.\n\n유는 종을 거느리니, 둘 다 크거나 작고,\n한 유는 가장 높되, 한 유는 전혀 높지 않다.\n\n각 종에는 또한 그 종차가 있으니,\n이것은 저것이 아니고, 그는 결코 그대가 아니었다.\n\n비록 이것과 저것이 모두 긍정이고, 그대와 그가\n하나 대 하나처럼, 혹은 셋 대 셋처럼 닮았을지라도.\n\n캐소본 씨의 유언에 관한 소문은 아직 래디슬로의 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 옛날 마을 축제와 장터가 떠돌이 공연단들이 다투어 떠드는 소리로 가득 찼듯이, 이제 대기는 의회 해산과 다가오는 선거로 가득 찬 듯했다. 그래서 좀 더 사사로운 소음들은 거의 주목을 받지 못했다. 그 유명한 \"건조한 선거\"가 코앞에 닥쳐 있었으니, 그 선거에서는 술의 낮은 수위로 대중 감정의 깊이를 가늠할 수 있었다. 윌 래디슬로는 이 무렵 가장 분주한 사람 중 하나였다. 그리고 도러시아의 과부살이가 끊임없이 그의 머릿속을 맴돌았지만, 그 일에 관해 누군가가 말을 거는 것을 너무도 원치 않았던 터라, 리드게이트가 그를 찾아와 로윅 성직록에 관해 있었던 일을 전하려 하자 그는 다소 쏘아붙이듯 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 나를 그 일에 끌어들이는 겁니까? 나는 캐소본 부인을 본 적도 없고, 앞으로 볼 일도 없을 것 같습니다. 그분은 프레시트에 계시니까요. 나는 거기에 가지 않습니다. 거기는 토리당의 땅이라, 나와 '파이어니어'는 밀렵꾼과 그 총만큼이나 환영받지 못하니까요.\"\n\n사실 윌은 브룩 씨가 예전처럼 자신에게 그가 편히 여기는 것 이상으로 그레인지에 자주 와 달라고 청하기는커녕, 이제는 가능한 한 그가 그곳에 적게 가도록 궁리하는 듯한 것을 알아채고 더욱 민감해진 상태였다. 이는 제임스 체텀 경의 분노에 찬 항의에 대한 브룩 씨의 어정쩡한 양보였다. 그리고 이런 방향의 아주 미세한 기미에도 깨어 있던 윌은, 자신이 도러시아 때문에 그레인지에서 멀어지게 되었다고 결론지었다. 그렇다면 그녀의 친지들이 자신을 다소 의심의 눈으로 본다는 말인가? 그들의 두려움은 전혀 쓸데없는 것이었다. 그가 부유한 여인의 호의를 얻으려는 궁색한 모험가로 나서리라고 상상한다면, 그들은 크게 잘못 짚은 것이었다.\n\n지금까지 윌은 자신과 도러시아 사이에 가로놓인 심연을 온전히 본 적이 없었다. 이제 그 가장자리에 다다라 건너편에 있는 그녀를 보고서야 비로소 보게 된 것이다. 그는 어느 정도 속에서 끓어오르는 분노와 함께 이 근방을 떠날 생각을 하기 시작했다. 불쾌한 누명을 스스로 뒤집어쓰지 않고서는 도러시아에게 더 이상의 관심을 보이는 일이 불가능할 터였다. 어쩌면 다른 이들이 독을 풀려 애쓸 그녀의 마음속에서조차 그러할지 몰랐다.\n\n\"우리는 영원히 갈라져 있다.\" 윌은 말했다. \"차라리 로마에 가 있어도 마찬가지일 것이다. 그래도 그녀가 지금보다 더 멀어지지는 않을 테니.\" 그러나 우리가 절망이라 부르는 것은 흔히 먹이를 얻지 못한 희망의 고통스러운 갈망에 지나지 않는다. 그가 떠나서는 안 될 이유는 충분했다. 이 위기의 순간에 자기 자리를 떠나서는 안 될 공적인 이유들이 있었으니, 브룩 씨가 선거를 위해 \"지도\"를 받아야 하고 직간접적인 유세를 그토록 많이 벌여야 하는 마당에 그를 곤경에 빠뜨려 둘 수는 없었다. 윌은 한창 열기가 오른 게임판에서 자기 장기 말을 두고 떠나기가 싫었다. 그리고 옳은 편에 선 후보라면, 비록 그 두뇌와 골수가 신사다운 풍모와 양립할 수 있는 한도까지 무르다 하더라도, 과반수를 뒤집는 데 한몫할 수 있었다. 브룩 씨를 지도하여, 자신의 독립성과 적절한 때 멈출 수 있는 능력을 고집하는 대신 실제 개혁 법안에 찬성표를 던지겠다고 서약해야 한다는 생각을 그가 꾸준히 견지하게 하는 것은 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 페어브러더 씨가 예언한 네 번째 후보가 \"주머니 속에\" 들어 있다는 말은 아직 실현되지 않았다. 의회 후보 협회도, 개혁파 과반수를 확보하려고 지켜보는 그 어떤 세력도, 자기 비용으로 출마할 수 있는 브룩 씨 같은 두 번째 개혁파 후보가 있는 한, 개입할 만한 가치 있는 매듭을 보지 못했기 때문이다. 그리하여 싸움은 전적으로 옛 토리당 의원인 핑커턴, 지난 선거에서 당선된 새 휘그당 의원 백스터, 그리고 이번 경우에만 자신을 옭아매기로 한 미래의 무소속 의원 브룩, 이 셋 사이에 놓여 있었다. 홀리 씨와 그 일파는 핑커턴의 당선에 온 힘을 쏟을 터였고, 브룩 씨의 성공은 백스터를 뒤로 처지게 할 단독 투표에 의존하거나, 토리당 표를 개혁파 표로 새로이 주조해 내는 데 달려 있었다. 물론 후자의 방법이 더 바람직할 터였다.\n\n이렇게 표를 전향시킬 수 있다는 전망은 브룩 씨에게 위험한 곁눈질거리였다. 흔들리는 자들은 흔들리는 발언에 끌려들기 쉽다는 그의 인상, 그리고 기억 속에서 반대 논거가 떠오를 때마다 그것에 다시 발목 잡히는 그의 정신적 성향이 윌 래디슬로에게 큰 골칫거리였다.\n\"이런 일에는 전술이라는 게 있는 걸 자네도 알잖나.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"사람들과 절반쯤 만나 주고, 자기 생각을 누그러뜨리고, '음, 그 말에도 일리가 있군' 하는 식으로 말하는 것 말일세. 이번이 특별한 경우라는 데는 나도 동의하네. 나라가 제 의지를 지니고 있고, 정치 결사며, 그런 종류의 것들이 있으니 말이야. 하지만 우리가 때로는 칼날을 너무 날카롭게 들이대기도 한단 말이지, 래디슬로. 이 십 파운드 가구주들 말인데, 왜 하필 십 파운드인가? 어딘가에 선을 긋긴 해야지. 그래. 하지만 왜 꼭 십에서 긋는가? 이건 파고들면 어려운 문제란 말일세.\"\n\n\"물론 그렇지요.\" 윌이 조바심을 내며 말했다. \"하지만 논리적으로 완벽한 법안이 나올 때까지 기다리시려면, 스스로 혁명가로 나서셔야 할 테고, 그러면 미들마치는 어르신을 뽑지 않을 겁니다. 그리고 어정쩡한 절충에 관해서라면, 지금은 절충할 때가 아닙니다.\"\n\n브룩 씨는 언제나 결국에는 래디슬로의 말에 동의하고 말았으니, 그에게는 여전히 윌이 셸리의 효모가 섞인 일종의 버크처럼 보였던 것이다. 그러나 얼마간 시간이 지나면 자기 방식의 지혜가 다시 고개를 들었고, 그는 또다시 큰 기대를 품고 그 방식을 쓰는 쪽으로 끌려들었다. 일이 이쯤 진행되던 무렵 그는 더없이 좋은 기분이었고, 그 기분은 거액의 자금을 선뜻 내놓는 와중에도 그를 떠받쳐 주었다. 그의 설득력과 설복하는 능력은 아직 다른 연사들을 소개하는 의장의 연설이나 미들마치 유권자와의 대화보다 더 어려운 무엇으로도 시험받은 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그런 대화를 마치고 나면 그는 자신이 천성적으로 전술가이며, 이런 종류의 일에 좀 더 일찍 뛰어들지 않은 것이 안타깝다는 느낌을 안고 돌아오곤 했다. 그렇지만 그는 마음지 씨에게는 다소 패배를 의식하고 있었다. 마음지 씨는 그 위대한 사회적 권력인 소매상인을 대표하는 미들마치의 유력 인사였고, 당연히 이 자치구에서 가장 향방을 알 수 없는 유권자 중 하나였다. 그는 자기 입장에서는 개혁파에게나 반개혁파에게나 똑같은 품질의 차와 설탕을 기꺼이 공급하려 했고, 또한 양쪽 모두에 공평하게 동의하려 했으며, 옛 자치 시민들처럼 이렇게 의원을 뽑아야 하는 일이 도시에 큰 짐이라고 느꼈다. 미리 모든 당파에게 희망을 품게 하는 데 위험이 없다 하더라도, 결국에는 자기 장부에 이름이 올라 있는 점잖은 사람들을 실망시켜야 하는 괴로운 일이 남을 터였기 때문이다. 그는 티프턴의 브룩 씨에게서 큰 주문을 받는 데 익숙했다. 하지만 핑커턴의 위원회에도 그 의견에 식료품의 무게가 상당히 실린 사람들이 많았다. 마음지 씨는 브룩 씨가 \"지적으로 그리 영리하지\" 못한 만큼, 압력에 못 이겨 적대적인 표를 던지는 식료품상을 더 너그러이 용서해 줄 사람이라고 여기고는, 자기 뒷방에서 흉금을 털어놓았다.\n\n\"개혁에 관해서라면 말입니다, 나리, 그걸 가정의 관점에서 봐 주십시오.\" 그는 주머니 속 잔돈을 짤랑거리며 상냥하게 웃으면서 말했다. \"그게 우리 마음지 부인을 부양해 주고, 제가 죽고 난 뒤 그 사람이 여섯 아이를 길러 낼 수 있게 해 줄까요? 답이 무엇일지 뻔히 알면서도 가정삼아 여쭙는 겁니다. 좋습니다, 나리. 그럼 여쭙겠습니다. 남편이자 아버지로서 제가 어떻게 해야겠습니까? 신사분들이 제게 와서 이렇게 말합니다. '마음대로 하시오, 마음지. 하지만 우리에게 반대표를 던진다면 식료품은 다른 데서 사겠소. 술에 설탕을 탈 때면 올바른 색깔의 상인을 떠받쳐 나라에 이바지하고 있다고 느끼고 싶으니까.' 바로 그 말을 들었습니다, 나리, 지금 나리께서 앉아 계신 바로 그 의자에서요. 존경하는 나리께서 그러셨다는 말씀은 아닙니다, 브룩 씨.\"\n\"아니, 아니, 아닐세. 그건 옹졸한 짓이지, 안 그런가. 내 집사가 자네 물건에 대해 불평할 때까지는 말일세, 마음지 씨.\" 브룩 씨가 달래듯 말했다. \"자네가 나쁜 설탕이며 향신료며 그런 것들을 보낸다는 말을 듣기 전까지는, 나는 결코 그에게 다른 데서 사라고 시키지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n\"나리, 저는 나리의 변변찮은 종이며, 깊이 감사드립니다.\" 마음지 씨는 정치 형편이 조금 맑아지는 듯한 느낌을 받으며 말했다. \"그렇게 존경스러운 태도로 말씀하시는 신사분께 표를 드린다면 다소나마 기쁨이 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"음, 그러니까 말일세, 마음지 씨, 자네는 우리 편에 서는 것이 옳은 일이라는 걸 알게 될 걸세. 이 개혁은 머지않아 모든 사람에게 영향을 미칠 거야. 철저히 대중적인 조치지. 말하자면 알파벳의 가, 나, 다 같은 것이라서, 나머지가 따라오려면 그것이 먼저 와야 한단 말일세. 자네가 그 일을 가정의 관점에서 봐야 한다는 데는 나도 전적으로 동의하네. 하지만 공공심이라는 게 있잖나. 우리는 다 한 가족이야, 알잖나. 다 한 찬장 속에 있는 거라고. 이를테면 표라는 것 말인데, 글쎄, 그게 케이프에서 사람들의 재산을 일구는 데 도움이 될지도 모르지. 표 한 장이 무슨 결과를 낳을지 누가 알겠나.\" 브룩 씨는 다소 망망대해에 떠 있는 듯한 느낌을 받으면서도 여전히 그것이 즐겁다고 여기며 말을 맺었다. 그러나 마음지 씨는 단호히 제동을 거는 어조로 대답했다.\n\"송구합니다만, 나리, 저는 그런 건 감당할 수 없습니다. 표를 던질 때는 제가 무얼 하는지 알아야 합니다. 외람된 말씀이오나, 그게 제 돈궤와 장부에 어떤 영향을 미칠지 살펴야 한단 말씀입니다. 가격이야, 인정하건대, 그 시비를 아무도 알 수 없는 것이지요. 그리고 보관이 안 되는 물건인 건포도를 사들이고 나면 값이 갑자기 떨어지곤 하는데, 저 자신도 그 속사정을 도무지 헤아려 본 적이 없습니다. 그건 인간의 자만심에 대한 질책이지요. 하지만 한 가족이라는 것에 관해서라면, 그래도 채무자와 채권자는 있겠지요. 설마 그것까지 개혁으로 없애지는 않을 테고요. 안 그렇다면 저는 만사가 지금 그대로 있는 쪽에 표를 던지겠습니다. 개인적으로 말하자면, 즉 저 자신과 가족을 두고 말하자면, 저만큼 변화를 부르짖을 필요가 적은 사람도 드뭅니다. 저는 잃을 게 없는 부류가 아니란 말씀입니다. 교구에서나 사적인 사업에서나 누리는 신망을 두고 하는 말이지, 결코 존경하는 나리와 그 단골 거래를 두고 하는 말은 아닙니다. 나리께서는 친절하게도, 보내 드린 물건이 만족스럽기만 하면 표를 주든 안 주든 그 거래를 끊지 않으시겠다고 말씀해 주셨으니까요.\"\n\n이 대화를 마친 뒤 마음지 씨는 위층으로 올라가 아내에게, 자기가 티프턴의 브룩한테 한 수 위였노라고, 이제는 투표하러 가는 일에 별로 개의치 않는다고 자랑했다.\n브룩 씨는 이번에는 자기 전술을 래디슬로에게 자랑하기를 삼갔고, 윌은 윌대로 자신이 순전히 논쟁적인 종류의 유세 말고는 어떤 유세와도 무관하며, 지식보다 더 비천한 도구는 부리지 않는다고 스스로를 설득할 수 있어 다행이라 여겼다. 브룩 씨에게는 필연적으로 자기 사람들이 있어서, 그들은 미들마치 유권자의 본성과 그 무지를 법안 편으로 끌어들이는 수단을 잘 알고 있었으니, 그 수단은 무지를 법안 반대편으로 끌어들이는 수단과 놀랍도록 비슷했다. 윌은 귀를 막았다. 때로는 의회도, 우리 삶의 나머지 부분이, 심지어 우리가 먹는 것과 입는 것까지도 그러하듯이, 우리의 상상이 그 과정에 너무 활발히 작동한다면 도무지 굴러가지 못할 지경이었다. 세상에는 더러운 일을 맡아 할 손이 더러운 자들이 얼마든지 있었다. 그리고 윌은 브룩 씨를 당선시키는 데서 자기가 맡은 몫만큼은 전적으로 결백하다고 스스로에게 항변했다.\n\n그러나 그가 그런 방식으로 옳은 편의 과반수에 기여하는 데 성공할지는 그 자신도 매우 의심스러웠다. 그는 갖가지 연설문과 연설용 메모를 작성해 두었지만, 브룩 씨의 정신이란 일단 어떤 사고의 흐름을 기억해야 하는 짐을 떠안으면 그것을 떨어뜨리고, 그것을 찾으러 달아났다가도 좀처럼 다시 돌아오지 못한다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다. 문서를 모으는 것은 나라에 봉사하는 한 가지 방법이고, 문서의 내용을 기억하는 것은 또 다른 방법이다. 아니! 브룩 씨가 적절한 때에 적절한 논거를 떠올리도록 강제할 유일한 방법은, 그 논거들이 그의 머릿속 공간을 전부 차지할 때까지 부지런히 들이붓는 것이었다. 그러나 여기에는 공간을 찾는 어려움이 있었으니, 미리 너무 많은 것이 들어차 있었기 때문이다. 브룩 씨 자신도 연설할 때면 자기 생각들이 다소 거치적거린다고 말했다.\n그렇지만 래디슬로의 지도는 곧 시험대에 오를 참이었다. 후보 지명일에 앞서 브룩 씨가 화이트 하트 여관의 발코니에서 미들마치의 존경할 만한 유권자들에게 자신의 입장을 밝히기로 되어 있었기 때문이다. 그 발코니는 장터 한 모퉁이에서 유리한 각도로 내다보여, 앞쪽의 너른 공간과 모여드는 두 갈래 거리를 한눈에 굽어볼 수 있었다. 화창한 5월의 아침이었고, 모든 것이 희망차 보였다. 백스터의 위원회와 브룩의 위원회 사이에 합의가 이루어질 가망이 좀 있었으니, 거기에는 불스트로드 씨와 자유주의 변호사인 스탠디시 씨, 그리고 플림데일 씨와 빈시 씨 같은 제조업자들이 견실함을 더해 주어, 그린 드래건에서 핑커턴을 위해 진을 친 홀리 씨와 그 일당과 거의 맞먹을 정도였다. 브룩 씨는 지난 반년간 지주로서 단행한 개혁으로 자신을 향한 \"트럼펫\"의 나팔 소리를 누그러뜨렸음을 의식하고 있었고, 시내로 마차를 몰고 들어오면서 자신에게 보내는 약간의 환호를 듣자, 황갈색 조끼 아래 마음이 제법 가벼워지는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 결정적인 순간이란, 흔히 마지막에 이르기 전까지는 모든 순간이 편안하게 멀리 있는 것처럼 여겨지는 법이다.\n\n\"이거 괜찮아 보이는데, 안 그런가?\" 군중이 모여들자 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"어쨌든 청중은 잘 모이겠어. 난 이런 게 좋단 말이지. 이렇게 자기 이웃들로 이루어진 청중 말일세, 알잖나.\"\n\n미들마치의 직조공들과 무두장이들은 마음지 씨와 달리 브룩 씨를 이웃으로 여겨 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 그가 런던에서 상자에 담겨 보내져 온 사람이라 한들 더 정 붙을 것도 없는 형편이었다. 그러나 그들은 후보를 소개하는 연사들의 말을 별다른 소란 없이 들었다. 그중 하나인 브래싱에서 온 어떤 정치 인사는 미들마치에 그 의무를 일러 주려고 왔는데, 어찌나 빠짐없이 말을 늘어놓는지, 그 뒤에 후보가 무슨 할 말을 찾아낼 수 있을지 생각하면 아찔할 지경이었다. 그사이 군중은 더욱 빽빽해졌고, 그 정치 인사가 연설의 끝에 가까워질수록 브룩 씨는 자기 감각에 놀라운 변화가 이는 것을 느꼈다. 그러면서도 그는 호출의 순간 따위는 아무렇지도 않다는 듯한 사람처럼, 여전히 안경을 만지작거리고 앞에 놓인 문서들을 매만지며 자기 위원회 사람들과 이런저런 말을 주고받았다.\n\n\"셰리주 한 잔 더 주게, 래디슬로.\" 그는 바로 뒤에 있던 윌에게 태연한 태도로 말했고, 윌은 곧 그 기운을 북돋워 주리라 여겨지는 술을 건넸다. 그것은 잘못된 선택이었다. 브룩 씨는 절제하는 사람이라, 첫 잔과 그리 멀지 않은 간격을 두고 셰리주 둘째 잔을 급히 마시는 것은 그의 신체에 충격을 주어, 기력을 모으기는커녕 오히려 흩어 놓는 경향이 있었기 때문이다. 부디 그를 가엾이 여기시라. 그토록 많은 영국 신사들이 순전히 사사로운 자리에서 연설을 하느라 스스로를 비참하게 만드는데! 그에 비하면 브룩 씨는 의회에 출마하여 나라에 봉사하고자 했던 것이다. 하기야 그 또한 사사로운 동기에서 할 수 있는 일이긴 하나, 일단 떠맡고 나면 어쨌든 얼마간의 연설을 절대적으로 요구하는 법이다.\n\n브룩 씨가 조금이라도 불안해한 것은 연설의 첫머리에 관해서가 아니었다. 그 부분이라면 틀림없이 잘되리라고, 포프의 대구 한 묶음처럼 말끔히 잘라 내어 척척 외울 수 있으리라고 확신했다. 출항은 쉬울 터였다. 그러나 그 뒤에 펼쳐질지 모를 망망대해의 환영이 두려웠다. \"그리고 질문 말인데,\" 그의 뱃속에서 막 깨어나는 악마가 속삭였다. \"누군가 부칙에 관해 질문할지도 모르지.—래디슬로,\" 그가 소리 내어 말을 이었다. \"부칙 메모 좀 건네주게.\"\n\n브룩 씨가 발코니에 모습을 드러내자 환호 소리가 제법 우렁차서, 야유며 신음 소리며 당나귀 울음 흉내며 그 밖의 반대 견해 표현을 충분히 상쇄하고도 남았다. 그 반대 소리들이 어찌나 미미했던지, 스탠디시 씨는 (확실히 노련한 사람이었다) 옆 사람의 귀에 대고 말했다. \"이거 위험해 보이는군, 정말이지! 홀리가 이보다 더 깊은 수를 숨겨 두고 있어.\" 그래도 환호는 신바람을 돋우었고, 가슴 주머니에 메모를 넣고 왼손을 발코니 난간에 올린 채 오른손으로 안경을 만지작거리는 브룩 씨보다 더 상냥해 보이는 후보는 없었다. 그의 외모에서 두드러진 점은 황갈색 조끼와 짧게 깎은 금발, 그리고 어느 쪽으로도 기울지 않은 인상이었다. 그는 어느 정도 자신감을 가지고 입을 열었다.\n\n\"신사 여러분—미들마치의 유권자 여러분!\"\n\n이것이야말로 더할 나위 없이 옳은 말이었기에, 그 뒤에 잠깐 사이를 두는 것이 자연스러워 보였다.\n\n\"이 자리에 서게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다. 제 평생 이렇게 자랑스럽고 행복한 적이 없었습니다. 이토록 행복한 적이 없어요, 알다시피.\"\n\n이것은 대담한 수사였으나 딱히 옳은 말은 아니었다. 불행히도 척척 외우려던 첫머리가 미끄러져 달아나 버렸기 때문이다. 두려움이 우리를 움켜쥐고 셰리주 한 잔이 우리 생각들 사이를 연기처럼 헤집고 다닐 때면, 포프의 대구마저도 한낱 \"우리에게서 떨어져 나가는 것, 사라져 가는 것\"에 지나지 않을 수 있다. 연사 뒤편 창가에 서 있던 래디슬로는 생각했다. \"이제 다 끝났군. 가장 좋은 방법이 늘 통하는 것은 아니니, 허우적거리는 게 이번 한 번은 먹힐지도 모른다는 것이 유일한 희망이야.\" 그사이 브룩 씨는 다른 실마리를 다 놓치고서 자기 자신과 자기 자격으로 되돌아왔으니, 후보에게는 언제나 적절하고도 우아한 화제였다.\n\n\"여러분, 저는 여러분의 가까운 이웃입니다. 좋은 친구 여러분, 여러분은 오랫동안 치안 판사석에 앉은 저를 보아 오셨지요. 저는 늘 공공의 문제에 깊이 관여해 왔습니다. 이를테면 기계며, 기계 파괴며 말입니다. 여러분 중 많은 분이 기계와 관련이 있으시고, 저도 요즘 그 문제를 파고들고 있습니다. 기계를 부수는 건 안 될 일이지요, 알다시피. 모든 게 굴러가야 하니까요. 무역이며, 제조며, 상업이며, 주요 산물의 교환이며, 그런 것들 말입니다. 애덤 스미스 이래로 그건 계속 굴러가야 합니다. 우리는 온 지구를 두루 살펴야 합니다. '드넓은 시야로 살피는 관찰' 말이지요, 어디든 두루 살펴야 합니다, '중국에서 페루까지' 말입니다, 누군가의 말마따나요. 존슨이었던 것 같군요, '램블러' 말입니다, 알다시피. 그게 바로 제가 어느 정도까지 해 온 일입니다. 페루까지는 아니지만 말이지요. 하지만 저는 늘 집에만 머물러 있지는 않았습니다. 그래선 안 된다는 걸 알았으니까요. 저는 레반트에 가 보았는데, 여러분 미들마치의 물건 일부가 거기로 가지요. 그리고 또, 발트해에도 가 보았습니다. 발트해 말입니다.\"\n\n이런 식으로 자기 추억들 사이를 헤집고 다니다 보면, 브룩 씨는 스스로는 편안히 잘 헤쳐 나가, 가장 먼 바다에서도 별 탈 없이 돌아왔을지 모른다. 그러나 적이 악마 같은 술책을 꾸며 놓았다. 바로 그 순간, 브룩 씨와 거의 마주 보는 자리, 그에게서 십 야드도 안 되는 곳에서 군중의 어깨 위로 그 자신의 형상이 솟아올랐다. 황갈색 조끼에 안경에 어느 쪽으로도 기울지 않은 인상이 헝겊에 그려져 있었다. 그리고 마치 뻐꾸기 울음소리처럼, 허공 어디선가 앵무새 같고 펀치 인형 같은 목소리로 그의 말을 흉내 내는 메아리가 일었다. 모두가 모여드는 두 거리의 맞은편 모퉁이 집들의 열린 창문을 올려다보았다. 그러나 그 창들은 텅 비어 있거나, 웃어 대는 구경꾼들로 차 있었다. 아무리 천진한 메아리라도 진지하게 끈질긴 연사를 뒤따를 때면 짓궂은 조롱이 깃들기 마련인데, 이 메아리는 도무지 천진하지가 않았다. 자연의 메아리처럼 정확하게 뒤따르지는 않았지만, 따라잡을 말을 사악하게 골라내는 솜씨가 있었다. 그것이 \"발트해 말입니다\"라고 했을 때쯤, 청중 사이를 훑고 다니던 웃음은 전체의 함성으로 번졌다. 그리고 당파와, 일의 얽힘으로 \"티프턴의 브룩\"과 동일시되어 버린 그 위대한 공적 대의가 분위기를 가라앉히지 않았더라면, 그 웃음은 그의 위원회마저 사로잡았을 것이다. 불스트로드 씨가 신설된 경찰이 뭘 하고 있느냐고 나무라듯 물었지만, 목소리를 멱살 잡을 수는 없는 노릇이었고, 후보의 형상을 공격하는 것도 너무 애매한 일이었으니, 홀리가 아마도 거기에 물건을 던지게 할 작정이었을 터이기 때문이다.\n\n브룩 씨 자신은 자기 안에서 생각들이 죄다 미끄러져 빠져나가는 것 말고는 무엇도 재빨리 의식할 수 있는 처지가 아니었다. 그는 귀에서 약간 윙윙거리는 소리까지 들렸고, 메아리를 똑똑히 알아차리지도, 자기 형상을 분간하지도 못한 유일한 사람이었다. 우리가 해야 할 말에 대한 불안만큼 우리의 지각을 철저히 사로잡는 것도 드물다. 브룩 씨는 웃음소리를 듣기는 했다. 그러나 그는 토리당 쪽에서 얼마간 훼방을 놓으리라 예상하고 있었고, 게다가 잃어버린 서두가 발트해에서 자기를 데리러 돌아오고 있다는 간질간질하고 따끔따끔한 느낌에 이 순간 한층 더 흥분해 있었다.\n\n\"그러고 보니 생각나는군요.\" 그는 태연한 태도로 한 손을 옆 주머니에 찔러 넣으며 말을 이었다. \"제가 선례가 필요하다면 말이지요, 알다시피. 하지만 우리는 옳은 일을 하는 데 선례가 필요한 적이 없습니다. 그래도 채텀이 있긴 하지요. 제가 채텀을 지지했으리라고는 말 못 하겠습니다. 피트, 그러니까 소小 피트도 마찬가지고요. 그는 이념을 지닌 사람이 아니었는데, 우리는 이념을 원하니까요, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"이념이고 나발이고! 우리가 원하는 건 법안이야!\" 아래쪽 군중에서 거칠고 큰 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n곧바로, 그때까지 브룩 씨를 뒤따르던 보이지 않는 펀치가 되받았다. \"이념이고 나발이고! 우리가 원하는 건 법안이야!\" 웃음소리는 그 어느 때보다 컸고, 브룩 씨는 자신이 입을 다물고 있던 터라 처음으로 그 조롱하는 메아리를 똑똑히 들었다. 그러나 그것은 자신의 말을 가로막은 자를 비웃는 것처럼 보였고, 그런 점에서는 오히려 고무적이었다. 그래서 그는 상냥하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"좋은 친구여, 당신 말에도 일리가 있습니다. 그리고 우리가 모인 것이 다 무엇 때문입니까, 우리 생각을 말하기 위해서가 아니겠습니까. 의견의 자유, 출판의 자유, 자유 말입니다, 그런 종류의 것 말이지요. 법안이라, 그래요, 여러분은 법안을 가지게 될 겁니다.\" 여기서 브룩 씨는 잠시 멈추어 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 가슴 주머니에서 종이를 꺼냈는데, 자신이 실무적이며 구체적인 사항으로 들어서고 있다는 느낌이 들었다. 보이지 않는 펀치가 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"여러분은 법안을 가지게 될 겁니다, 브룩 씨, 선거전 한 차례에, 의회 바깥의 의석 하나를 배달해 드리니, 도합 오천 파운드 칠 실링 사 펜스올시다.\"\n\n웃음이 와르르 터지는 가운데 브룩 씨는 얼굴이 붉어져 안경을 떨어뜨렸고, 어리둥절하여 주위를 둘러보다가 어느새 더 가까이 다가와 있는 자기 형상을 보았다. 다음 순간 그는 그것이 처량하게 달걀 범벅이 된 것을 보았다. 그의 기운이 조금 살아났고, 목소리도 그러했다.\n\n\"익살이며, 술책이며, 조롱이 진리의 시금석이라니, 그것도 다 좋습니다.\" 여기서 고약한 달걀 하나가 브룩 씨의 어깨에 깨어지는 가운데, 메아리가 말했다. \"그것도 다 좋습니다.\" 이어서 달걀이 우박처럼 쏟아졌는데, 주로 형상을 겨냥했으나 이따금 마치 우연인 양 원본을 맞히기도 했다. 새로운 사내들의 무리가 군중 사이를 밀치며 밀려들었다. 호루라기며 야유며 고함이며 피리 소리가 더욱 큰 소동을 일으켰으니, 그것들을 잠재우려고 또 외쳐 대고 몸싸움하는 소리가 더해진 탓이었다. 그 어떤 목소리도 그 소란을 뚫고 솟아오를 만큼의 날개를 갖지 못했을 터였고, 불쾌하게 기름칠당한 브룩 씨는 더는 자리를 지키지 못했다. 그 좌절은, 좀 덜 장난스럽고 덜 어린애 같았더라면 그토록 약 오르지는 않았을 것이다. 신문 기자가 \"그 일이 학식 높은 신사분의 갈비뼈를 위태롭게 했음을 단언할 수 있다\"고 쓰거나, \"그 신사분의 구두 밑창이 난간 위로 보였음\"을 정중히 증언할 수 있는 진지한 습격이라면, 어쩌면 거기에 딸린 위안이 더 클 법도 하다.\n\n브룩 씨는 위원회실로 돌아 들어오면서 되도록 아무렇지 않은 듯 말했다. \"이거 좀 너무하는군, 정말이지. 조금만 있으면 사람들의 귀를 사로잡을 수 있었을 텐데, 시간을 안 주잖나. 조금만 있었으면 법안으로 들어갔을 텐데 말이야, 알다시피.\" 그는 래디슬로를 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다. \"그렇지만, 후보 지명일에는 다 잘될 거야.\"\n\n그러나 일이 잘되리라는 데 만장일치로 합의가 이루어진 것은 아니었다. 오히려 위원회는 다소 침울한 표정이었고, 브래싱에서 온 그 정치 인사는 마치 새로운 술책이라도 빚어내는 듯 부지런히 무언가를 쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"그건 보이어의 짓입니다.\" 스탠디시 씨가 말끝을 흐리며 말했다. \"마치 광고라도 난 것처럼 분명히 압니다. 그자는 복화술에 유난히 능하고, 이번에도 유난히 잘해냈지요, 정말이지! 홀리가 요즘 그자를 만찬에 부르곤 했답니다. 보이어한테는 재주가 넘치거든요.\"\n\n\"음, 그러니까 말일세, 자네가 그자 얘기를 내게 한 적이 없잖나, 스탠디시. 안 그랬으면 그자를 만찬에 초대했을 텐데.\" 가엾은 브룩 씨가 말했으니, 그는 나라를 위해 그토록 많은 초대를 해 온 터였다.\n\n\"미들마치에 보이어보다 더 하찮은 자도 없습니다.\" 래디슬로가 분개하여 말했다. \"그런데도 늘 하찮은 자들이 저울추를 기울이는 것 같단 말이지요.\"\n\n윌은 자기 \"주군\"에 대해서뿐 아니라 자기 자신에 대해서도 단단히 부아가 치밀어 있었고, \"파이어니어\"와 브룩 씨를 함께 내던져 버릴까 하는 어렴풋한 결심을 품고서 자기 방에 틀어박히러 갔다. 무엇 때문에 그가 여기 머물러 있겠는가? 자신과 도러시아 사이에 건널 수 없이 가로놓인 심연을 언젠가 메울 수 있다면, 그것은 여기 머물러 브룩의 하수인으로서 마땅한 경멸 속으로 미끄러져 드는 것보다는, 차라리 떠나가 전혀 다른 위치에 올라서는 편이 나을 터였다. 그러자 자기가 이룰 수 있을 경이로운 일들에 대한 젊은이의 꿈이 찾아왔다. 이를테면 오 년 안에 말이다. 정치적 저술과 정치적 연설은, 이제 공적 생활이 더 넓어지고 더 전국적으로 되어 가는 만큼 더 높은 가치를 지니게 될 터였고, 그것들이 그에게 그런 명성을 안겨 주어, 그가 도러시아더러 자기에게 격을 낮추어 내려오라고 청하는 것처럼 보이지 않게 될지도 몰랐다. 오 년이라. 그녀가 다른 누구보다 자기를 더 마음에 두고 있다고 확신만 할 수 있다면, 자신이 스스로를 낮추지 않고 사랑을 고백할 수 있게 될 때까지는 거리를 두고 있는 것이라고 그녀에게 알릴 수만 있다면, 그러면 그는 수월히 떠나가, 스물다섯 살의 그에게는 사물의 내적 질서 속에서 충분히 있을 법해 보이던 그런 출세길에 오를 수 있을 터였다. 그 질서 속에서는 재능이 명성을 가져오고, 명성이 그 밖에 즐거운 모든 것을 가져다주니 말이다. 그는 말할 줄도 알았고 글 쓸 줄도 알았다. 마음만 먹으면 어떤 주제든 통달할 수 있었고, 언제나 이성과 정의의 편에 서서, 그쪽으로 자신의 온 열정을 쏟아부을 작정이었다. 어찌 언젠가 그가 군중의 어깨 위로 들어 올려져, 그 높은 자리를 떳떳이 얻었다고 느끼지 못하겠는가? 의심할 바 없이 그는 미들마치를 떠나 도시로 가서, \"법조인의 회식에 참석하며\" 명성에 어울리는 사람이 될 터였다.\n\n그러나 당장은 아니었다. 자신과 도러시아 사이에 어떤 종류든 신호가 오가기 전까지는 아니었다. 설령 자신이 그녀가 남편으로 택할 만한 사람이라 하더라도 왜 그녀와 결혼하지 않으려는지 그녀가 알기 전까지는 그는 만족할 수 없었다. 그러니 그는 자기 자리를 지키며 조금 더 브룩 씨를 견뎌 내야 했다.\n\n그러나 그는 곧, 브룩 씨가 둘의 관계를 끝내고 싶어 하는 마음에서 자기보다 한발 앞섰음을 의심할 만한 까닭을 얻게 되었다. 밖에서의 대표단들과 안에서의 목소리들이 합세하여 그 박애주의자로 하여금 인류의 안녕을 위해 평소보다 더 강력한 조치를 취하도록 부추긴 것이다. 다름 아니라, 다른 후보를 위해 사퇴하여 그에게 자기 유세 기구의 이점을 넘겨주는 일이었다. 그 자신은 이를 강력한 조치라 일컬었으나, 자기 건강이 생각했던 것만큼 흥분을 견뎌 낼 능력이 못 된다고 말했다.\n\n\"가슴이 좀 불편하단 말이야. 그걸 너무 무리하면 안 되지.\" 그는 그 일을 설명하면서 래디슬로에게 말했다. \"나는 멈춰야 해. 가엾은 캐소본이 경고가 됐잖나, 알다시피. 내가 적잖은 자금을 댔지만, 물길은 하나 파 놓은 셈이지. 이 선거 운동이라는 게 좀 거친 일이야, 안 그런가, 래디슬로? 자네도 진력이 났을 테지. 그래도 우리는 '파이어니어'로 물길을 하나 파 놓았어. 일을 궤도에 올려놓았다, 그런 거지. 이제는 자네보다 더 평범한 사람도 이걸 이어 갈 수 있을 거야. 더 평범한 사람 말일세, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"제가 그만두기를 바라십니까?\" 윌이 글 쓰던 탁자에서 일어서며 말했는데, 얼굴에 핏기가 확 올랐다. 그는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 세 걸음을 떼며 돌아섰다. \"원하신다면 언제든 그만둘 준비가 되어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"바라느냐고 하면, 이보게 래디슬로, 나는 자네 능력을 더없이 높이 평가하네, 알잖나. 하지만 '파이어니어' 일은, 내가 우리 편 사람들 몇과 좀 상의해 봤는데, 그들이 그걸 자기들 손에 넘겨받으려는 눈치야. 어느 정도까지 내게 손실을 보전해 주고, 실은 그걸 이어 가겠다는 거지. 그런 사정이니, 자네는 그만두고 싶을지도 몰라. 더 나은 무대를 찾을 수도 있고. 그 사람들은 내가 늘 자네를 보아 온 것만큼 자네를 높이 평가하지 않을지도 모르네. 나는 자네를 또 다른 나, 오른팔로 여겨 왔는데 말이야. 하기야 나는 늘 자네가 다른 무언가를 하리라 내다보긴 했지. 나는 프랑스에 한번 다녀올까 하네. 하지만 자네에게 추천서야 얼마든지 써 주겠네, 알잖나. 올소프며 그런 부류의 사람들에게 말이야. 나는 올소프를 만난 적이 있거든.\"\n\n\"대단히 감사합니다.\" 래디슬로가 자존심을 세우며 말했다. \"어르신께서 '파이어니어'를 손에서 놓으실 거라면, 제가 앞으로 취할 행보에 관해서는 어르신을 번거롭게 해 드릴 필요가 없겠지요. 저는 당분간 여기 계속 머무는 쪽을 택할지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n브룩 씨가 떠난 뒤 윌은 혼잣말을 했다. \"집안 식구들이 나를 떼어 내라고 그를 졸라 댔는데, 이제 그는 내가 떠나든 말든 개의치 않는군. 나는 내가 있고 싶은 만큼 있겠어. 떠난다면 그건 내 뜻으로 떠나는 거지, 그들이 나를 두려워해서가 아니야.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LII.\n\n가장 미천한 의무를 스스로 떠맡았네.\n—워즈워스\n\n페어브러더 씨가 로윅의 성직록을 받게 되리라는 것을 알게 된 그 유월 저녁, 고풍스러운 응접실에는 기쁨이 가득했고, 위대한 법률가들의 초상화마저 흡족하게 내려다보는 듯했다. 그의 어머니는 차와 토스트에 손도 대지 않은 채, 여느 때처럼 곱고 단정한 자세로 앉아 있었는데, 오직 두 뺨에 떠오른 홍조와 두 눈에 어린 광채로만 감정을 드러냈을 뿐이었다. 그것은 노부인에게 아득히 멀어진 젊은 날의 자신과 잠시나마 가슴 뭉클하게 겹치는 모습을 부여하는 것이었다. 그러더니 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"가장 큰 위안은, 캠던, 네가 그것을 받을 만한 자격이 있다는 거란다.\"\n\n\"어머니, 사람이 좋은 자리를 얻으면, 자격의 절반은 그 뒤에 갖춰지는 법이지요.\" 아들이 기쁨에 넘쳐 그것을 감추려 하지도 않고 말했다. 그의 얼굴에 어린 환희는 능동적인 종류의 것이어서, 밖으로 번뜩일 뿐만 아니라 안으로도 분주한 상상을 환히 밝힐 만한 에너지가 있는 듯했다. 그의 시선에는 기쁨뿐 아니라 갖가지 생각도 깃들어 있는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n\"자, 고모.\" 그는 두 손을 비비며 새끼 비버 같은 가냘프고 정겨운 소리를 내고 있는 노블 양을 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"이제 식탁에는 늘 사탕이 놓여 있을 테니, 마음껏 슬쩍해서 아이들에게 나눠 주세요. 그리고 선물로 줄 새 양말도 많이 갖게 되실 테고, 고모 양말도 어느 때보다 더 많이 기우실 거예요!\"\n\n노블 양은 조카를 향해 다소 겁먹은 듯 나직이 웃으며 고개를 끄덕였는데, 이미 그 새로운 성직 임명을 빌미로 설탕 한 덩이를 더 자기 바구니에 떨어뜨려 넣었다는 것을 의식하고 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 위니, 너로 말할 것 같으면\" 하고 사제는 말을 이었다. \"네가 로윅의 어느 독신 남자와 결혼한다 해도 나는 조금도 반대하지 않을 거다. 이를테면 솔로몬 페더스톤 씨 같은 사람 말이지. 네가 그 사람을 사랑한다는 걸 알게 되는 즉시 말이야.\"\n\n내내 오빠를 바라보며 진심으로 울고 있던 위니프레드 양은 — 그것이 그녀가 기쁨을 표현하는 방식이었다 — 눈물 사이로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"오빠가 먼저 본을 보여 줘야지, 캠. 이제 오빠가 결혼해야 해.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러마. 그런데 누가 나를 사랑하겠니? 나는 초라한 늙은이인걸.\" 사제는 일어서서 의자를 밀어내고 자기 모습을 내려다보며 말했다. \"어머니는 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"너는 잘생긴 남자란다, 캠던. 비록 네 아버지만큼 풍채가 훌륭하진 않지만 말이다.\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"오빠가 가스 양과 결혼하면 좋겠어.\" 위니프레드 양이 말했다. \"그분이라면 로윅 생활을 아주 활기차게 만들어 줄 텐데.\"\n\n\"거참 멋진 말이로구나! 너는 마치 젊은 여자들이 시장의 가금처럼 골라 가라고 묶여 있는 것처럼, 내가 청하기만 하면 누구든 나를 받아 줄 것처럼 말하는구나.\" 사제는 누구라고 콕 집어 말할 마음이 없는 채로 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 아무나 원하는 건 아니야.\" 위니프레드 양이 말했다. \"하지만 어머니, 어머니도 가스 양이 마음에 드시지요?\"\n\n\"내 아들의 선택이 곧 나의 선택이지.\" 페어브러더 부인이 위엄 있는 신중함으로 말했다. \"그리고 며느리는 더없이 환영이란다, 캠던. 우리가 로윅으로 가면 너는 집에서 휘스트를 하고 싶을 텐데, 헨리에타 노블은 한 번도 휘스트를 한 적이 없으니 말이다.\" (페어브러더 부인은 자그마한 노처녀 여동생을 늘 그 거창한 이름으로 불렀다.)\n\n\"이제 휘스트는 안 할 작정이에요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"어째서, 캠던? 내 시절에는 휘스트가 신앙심 깊은 교인에게도 나무랄 데 없는 오락으로 여겨졌단다.\" 페어브러더 부인이 휘스트가 아들에게 어떤 의미였는지는 전혀 모른 채, 무언가 새로운 교리를 위태롭게 두둔하는 데 대해서인 양 다소 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n\"휘스트를 할 만큼 한가하지 않을 거예요. 교구를 둘이나 맡게 될 테니까요.\" 사제는 그 노름의 미덕을 논하고 싶지 않아 그렇게 말했다.\n\n그는 이미 도러시아에게 이렇게 말한 바 있었다. \"성 보톨프 교회를 포기해야 한다고는 생각하지 않습니다. 제가 다른 누군가에게 그 수입의 대부분을 넘겨준다면, 그것만으로도 사람들이 개혁하려는 성직 겸임에 대한 충분한 항의가 되겠지요. 더 강한 일은 권한을 포기하는 것이 아니라 그것을 잘 쓰는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"저도 그 점을 생각했어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"저 자신에 관한 한, 권한과 돈은 그것을 지키는 것보다 포기하는 편이 더 쉬울 것 같아요. 제가 이런 성직 임명권을 갖는 것이 무척 어울리지 않는 것 같지만, 그래도 그것을 저 대신 다른 누군가가 쓰도록 내버려 두어서는 안 된다고 느꼈답니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 그 권한을 후회하지 않도록 처신해야 할 사람은 바로 저입니다.\" 페어브러더 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 삶의 멍에가 더 이상 자신을 짓누르지 않게 되자 양심이 오히려 더 활발해지는 부류의 사람이었다. 그는 그 문제에 관해 겸손을 드러내 보이지는 않았으나, 마음속으로는 성직록을 얻지 못한 다른 이들에게는 없는 태만함을 자신의 행실이 드러내 보였다는 것이 다소 부끄러웠다.\n\n\"성직자가 아닌 다른 무언가가 되었더라면 하고 자주 바라곤 했지요.\" 그는 리드게이트에게 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌면 나 자신을 최대한 훌륭한 성직자로 만들어 보는 편이 더 나을지도 모르겠습니다. 보시다시피, 이것은 좋은 성직록을 가진 자의 관점이라, 거기서는 온갖 어려움이 한결 단순해진답니다.\" 그는 미소 지으며 말을 맺었다.\n\n그때 사제는 정말이지 자기 몫의 의무가 수월하리라 느꼈다. 하지만 의무란 뜻밖의 방식으로 행동하는 버릇이 있다 — 우리가 다정하게 방문을 청해 놓았더니 우리 집 문 안에서 다리가 부러져 버리는 거추장스러운 친구처럼.\n\n채 일주일도 지나지 않아, 의무는 옴니버스 칼리지에서 학사 학위를 받고 막 돌아온 프레드 빈시로 변장한 채 그의 서재에 나타났다.\n\n\"이런 일로 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다, 페어브러더 씨.\" 프레드가 말했는데, 그 환하고 솔직한 얼굴이 호감을 자아냈다. \"하지만 제가 상의할 수 있는 친구는 선생님뿐입니다. 전에 한 번 모든 걸 다 말씀드렸을 때 선생님이 너무나 친절하게 대해 주셔서, 다시 찾아오지 않을 수가 없었어요.\"\n\n\"앉게, 프레드. 무슨 이야기든 듣고, 내가 할 수 있는 일이라면 뭐든 하겠네.\" 사제는 옮길 자잘한 물건들을 챙기느라 분주했고, 그러면서도 하던 일을 계속했다.\n\n\"말씀드리고 싶은 건—\" 프레드는 한순간 머뭇거리다가 단숨에 털어놓듯 말을 이었다. \"이제 성직에 들어갈 수도 있겠습니다. 그리고 솔직히 아무리 둘러봐도 달리 할 일이 보이지 않아요. 마음에 들진 않지만, 이런 말을 하는 게 아버지께 몹시 모진 일이라는 건 알아요. 아버지가 저를 그 길로 키우느라 적잖은 돈을 쓰셨으니까요.\" 프레드는 다시 한순간 멈췄다가 되풀이했다. \"그리고 달리 할 일이 보이질 않습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 자네 아버지께 그 일을 말씀드린 적이 있네, 프레드. 하지만 별 진전이 없었어. 너무 늦었다고 하시더군. 하지만 자네는 이제 다리 하나를 건넌 셈이야. 나머지 어려움이라면 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"그저 마음에 들지 않는다는 것뿐이에요. 신학도, 설교도, 진지한 표정을 지어야 한다는 것도 싫어요. 저는 들판을 가로질러 말을 달리고, 다른 사내들처럼 사는 게 좋습니다. 무슨 나쁜 사람이 되고 싶다는 뜻은 아니에요. 다만 사람들이 성직자에게 기대하는 그런 것에는 취미가 없다는 거죠. 그렇지만 달리 무엇을 하겠어요? 아버지가 제게 밑천을 떼어 주실 수 없으니, 안 그러면 농사라도 지어 볼 텐데요. 또 가게에는 제가 들어갈 자리도 없어요. 게다가 아버지가 제가 뭐라도 벌기를 바라시는 지금에 와서 법학이나 의학 공부를 새로 시작할 수도 없는 노릇이고요. 제가 성직에 들어가는 게 잘못이라고 말하기야 쉽지요. 하지만 그렇게 말하는 사람들은 차라리 저더러 오지로 떠나라고 하는 편이 나을 거예요.\"\n\n프레드의 목소리에는 투덜대는 항변의 어조가 배어 있었고, 페어브러더 씨는 만약 프레드가 말한 것 이상을 머릿속으로 그려 보느라 바쁘지만 않았더라면 빙그레 웃었을지도 모른다.\n\n\"교리에 관해서는 어려움이 없는가? 신앙 조항 말일세.\" 그는 오로지 프레드를 위해 그 물음을 단순하게 생각하려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"없습니다. 조항들은 옳다고 생각해요. 그것을 반박할 논거 같은 건 제겐 없고, 저보다 훨씬 훌륭하고 영리한 사람들이 전적으로 그것을 받아들이지 않습니까. 제가 마치 무슨 심판관이라도 되는 양 그런 거리낌을 들먹이는 건 오히려 우스운 일일 거예요.\" 프레드가 아주 솔직하게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 자네가 대단한 신학자가 아니더라도 그럭저럭 괜찮은 교구 사제는 될 수 있겠다고 생각해 본 적은 있겠군?\"\n\n\"물론, 어쩔 수 없이 성직자가 되어야 한다면, 마음에 들지 않더라도 제 의무를 다하려고 애쓸 겁니다. 누가 저를 탓할 거라고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"이런 형편에 성직에 들어가는 일을 두고 말인가? 그건 자네 양심에 달린 문제일세, 프레드. 자네가 그 대가를 얼마나 헤아렸는지, 자네의 자리가 자네에게 무엇을 요구할지 얼마나 내다봤는지에 달린 거지. 나로 말하자면, 나는 늘 너무 안일했고 그 결과로 마음이 편치 못했다는 것밖에는 자네에게 해 줄 말이 없네.\"\n\n\"하지만 또 다른 걸림돌이 있어요.\" 프레드가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"전에는 말씀드리지 않았지만, 어쩌면 선생님이 짐작하실 만한 말들을 흘렸을지도 모르겠습니다. 제가 무척 좋아하는 사람이 있어요. 어릴 때부터 줄곧 그녀를 사랑해 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"가스 양 말이겠지?\" 사제는 어떤 꼬리표들을 아주 유심히 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"네. 그녀가 저를 받아 주기만 한다면 다른 건 아무래도 좋아요. 그러면 저도 좋은 사람이 될 수 있을 거라고 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 그녀도 그 마음에 화답한다고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"그녀는 절대 그렇다고 말하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 한참 전에 다시는 그 얘길 꺼내지 않겠다고 그녀에게 약속하게 됐어요. 게다가 제가 성직자가 되는 것을 특히나 단호하게 반대하고 있고요. 그건 저도 압니다. 하지만 그녀를 포기할 수가 없어요. 그녀가 저를 마음에 두고 있다고 정말로 믿어요. 어젯밤 가스 부인을 뵈었는데, 메리가 페어브러더 양과 함께 로윅 사제관에 머물고 있다고 하시더군요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그녀가 내 누이를 친절히 도와주고 있다네. 자네는 그곳에 가 보고 싶은가?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님께 큰 부탁을 드리고 싶어요. 이렇게 폐를 끼치는 게 면목 없지만, 선생님이 메리에게 그 이야기를 꺼내 주시면 그녀가 선생님 말씀에는 귀를 기울일 것 같아서요. 그러니까 제가 성직에 들어가는 일 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그건 꽤 미묘한 임무로구나, 프레드. 자네가 그녀를 사모한다는 것을 전제로 삼아야 할 테고, 자네가 바라는 대로 그 이야기를 꺼낸다는 건 곧 그녀에게 그 마음에 화답하는지 어떤지를 말해 달라고 청하는 셈이 될 테니 말이야.\"\n\n\"바로 그걸 그녀가 선생님께 말해 주기를 바라는 거예요.\" 프레드가 단도직입적으로 말했다. \"그녀의 마음을 알아내지 못하면 저는 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"성직에 들어가는 일을 두고 그것에 따라 결정하겠다는 말인가?\"\n\n\"메리가 절대 저를 받아 주지 않겠다고 하면, 어느 쪽으로 잘못되든 매한가지일 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 말도 안 되는 소리야, 프레드. 사람은 사랑은 견뎌 내고 살아남지만, 자기 무모함의 결과까지 견뎌 내며 살아남지는 못한다네.\"\n\n\"제 사랑은 그렇지 않아요. 저는 한 번도 메리를 사랑하지 않은 적이 없어요. 만약 그녀를 포기해야 한다면, 그건 나무 의족을 짚고 살아가기 시작하는 것과 같을 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 끼어드는 것을 그녀가 언짢아하지는 않을까?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 그러지 않을 거라고 확신해요. 그녀는 누구보다 선생님을 존경하니, 저를 대할 때처럼 농담으로 얼버무리지는 않을 거예요. 물론 선생님 말고 다른 누구에게도 이런 이야기를 할 수도, 그녀에게 말해 달라고 부탁할 수도 없었어요. 우리 두 사람 모두에게 이만한 친구가 되어 줄 사람은 달리 없으니까요.\" 프레드는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 다소 푸념하듯 덧붙였다. \"그리고 그녀도 제가 시험에 통과하려고 애썼다는 걸 인정해 줘야 해요. 제가 그녀를 위해서라면 스스로 노력하리라는 걸 믿어 줘야 한다고요.\"\n\n페어브러더 씨가 하던 일을 내려놓고 프레드에게 손을 내밀며 말하기까지 한순간의 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"좋아, 이 사람아. 자네가 바라는 대로 해 주지.\"\n\n바로 그날 페어브러더 씨는 막 새로 마련한 조랑말을 타고 로윅 사제관으로 향했다. \"확실히 나는 늙은 줄기로구나.\" 그는 생각했다. \"젊은 싹들이 나를 옆으로 밀어내고 있어.\"\n\n그는 메리가 정원에서 장미를 따서 천 위에 꽃잎을 뿌리고 있는 것을 발견했다. 해는 낮게 기울었고, 키 큰 나무들이 잔디 길 위로 그림자를 드리웠는데, 메리는 모자도 양산도 없이 그 길을 거닐고 있었다. 그녀는 잔디를 가로질러 다가오는 페어브러더 씨를 알아채지 못했고, 마침 몸을 숙여 작은 검정 갈색 테리어를 타이르던 참이었다. 메리가 꽃잎을 뿌리는 동안 그 개가 한사코 천 위를 걸어 다니며 장미 잎의 냄새를 맡으려 들었기 때문이다. 그녀는 한 손으로 개의 앞발을 잡고 다른 손의 집게손가락을 치켜들었고, 개는 이마를 찌푸린 채 난처한 표정을 짓고 있었다. \"플라이, 플라이, 부끄러운 줄 알아.\" 메리가 묵직한 콘트랄토 음성으로 말하고 있었다. \"분별 있는 개라면 이래선 못쓰지. 누가 보면 너를 어리석은 젊은 신사로 여기겠다.\"\n\n\"가스 양은 젊은 신사들에게 자비가 없으시군요.\" 사제가 그녀에게서 두 야드도 안 되는 곳에서 말했다.\n\n메리는 화들짝 일어서며 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"플라이한테는 늘 타이르는 게 효과가 있어요.\" 그녀가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 젊은 신사들에게는 안 그렇고요?\"\n\n\"오, 더러는 효과가 있겠지요. 개중에는 훌륭한 사람이 되는 이도 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 인정해 주시니 다행입니다. 다름 아니라 지금 이 순간 어떤 젊은 신사에게 관심을 가져 주십사 하고 부탁드리려던 참이거든요.\"\n\n\"어리석은 사람은 아니길 바라요.\" 메리는 다시 장미를 따기 시작하며 가슴이 거북할 만큼 두근거리는 것을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다. 다만 지혜가 그의 강점은 아닐지 모르지요. 오히려 애정과 진실함이 그렇답니다. 하지만 사람들이 흔히 생각하는 것보다 지혜는 그 두 가지 자질 속에 더 많이 깃들어 있지요. 그 특징만으로도 내가 누구를 말하는지 아실 줄 압니다.\"\n\n\"네, 알 것 같아요.\" 메리가 용감하게 말했지만, 얼굴은 한층 진지해지고 두 손은 차가워졌다. \"프레드 빈시일 거예요.\"\n\n\"그가 자기 성직 진로에 관해 양에게 의논해 달라고 부탁했답니다. 내가 그러겠다고 약속한 것이 주제넘은 짓이라고 여기지는 않으시길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"오히려 그 반대예요, 페어브러더 씨.\" 메리가 장미를 내려놓고 두 팔을 모아 잡았으나 차마 고개를 들지는 못한 채 말했다. \"선생님이 제게 하실 말씀이 있으시다면, 저는 언제나 영광으로 여겨요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 문제로 들어가기 전에, 양의 아버님께서 내게 털어놓으셨던 한 가지를 잠깐 짚고 넘어가겠습니다. 그러고 보니 그것은 바로 전에 한 번 프레드의 부탁을 전했던 그 저녁이었지요. 그가 막 대학으로 떠난 직후 말입니다. 가스 씨가 페더스톤이 죽던 날 밤에 일어난 일을 내게 말해 주셨어요. 양이 유언장 태우기를 거절한 일 말입니다. 그리고 그 일로 양이 다소 마음의 가책을 느꼈다고 하시더군요. 죄 없이 프레드가 만 파운드를 받지 못하게 막은 셈이 되었으니까요. 나는 그 일을 마음에 새겨 두고 있었는데, 그 점에서 양의 마음을 덜어 줄 만한 이야기를 들었습니다. 거기에는 양이 치러야 할 어떤 속죄의 제물도 요구되지 않는다는 것을 알려 드릴 수 있겠습니다.\"\n\n페어브러더 씨는 한순간 멈추고 메리를 바라보았다. 그는 프레드에게 충분히 유리하도록 해 줄 작정이었으나, 여자들이 이따금 빠지곤 하는 어떤 미신적인 생각에서 그녀의 마음을 풀어 주는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 여자가 속죄의 행위로 어떤 남자와 결혼하여 그에게 잘못을 저지를 때 따르는 그런 생각 말이다. 메리의 두 뺨이 약간 달아오르기 시작했고, 그녀는 말이 없었다.\n\n\"내 말은, 양의 행동이 프레드의 운명에는 실제로 아무런 차이도 만들지 않았다는 겁니다. 알아보니 맨 마지막 유언장을 태웠다 해도 첫 번째 유언장은 법적으로 효력이 없었을 거예요. 이의가 제기되었더라면 그것은 인정받지 못했을 텐데, 분명 이의가 제기되었으리라는 점은 양도 확신해도 좋습니다. 그러니 그 점에서는 마음을 자유로이 가지셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 페어브러더 씨.\" 메리가 진심으로 말했다. \"제 마음을 헤아려 주셔서 감사드려요.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 본론을 계속하지요. 알다시피 프레드는 학위를 받았습니다. 여기까지 스스로 헤쳐 왔는데, 이제 문제는 그가 무엇을 할 것인가입니다. 그 문제가 워낙 어려운 터라, 그는 아버지의 뜻을 따라 성직에 들어가는 쪽으로 기울고 있어요. 양은 나보다 더 잘 알겠지만 그가 예전에는 그 길을 한사코 반대했었지요. 내가 그에게 그 문제를 캐물어 보았는데, 솔직히 요즘 형편으로 보면 그가 성직자가 되는 데 넘을 수 없는 반대 사유는 보이지 않습니다. 그는 한 가지 조건만 갖춰진다면 그 천직에서 최선을 다하는 데 마음을 쏟을 수 있다고 합니다. 만약 그 조건이 채워진다면, 나는 프레드를 돕는 데 온 힘을 기울일 거예요. 얼마간 시간이 지나면 — 물론 처음부터는 아니지요 — 그가 내 부사제로 함께 일할 수 있을 테고, 할 일이 워낙 많아서 그의 봉급이 내가 사제로 받던 것과 거의 맞먹게 될 겁니다. 하지만 거듭 말하지만, 이 모든 좋은 일이 이루어지려면 반드시 갖춰져야 할 한 가지 조건이 있어요. 그가 내게 마음을 열어 보였습니다, 가스 양, 그러고는 자기를 위해 변호해 달라고 부탁했지요. 그 조건은 전적으로 양의 마음에 달려 있습니다.\"\n\n메리가 무척 마음이 흔들린 듯 보이자, 그는 한순간 뒤에 \"조금 걸읍시다\" 하고 말했고, 둘이 걷기 시작하자 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"아주 솔직히 말하자면, 프레드는 양이 자기 아내가 되어 줄 가망을 줄일 만한 길은 결코 택하지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 그런 가망만 있다면, 그는 양이 인정하는 일이라면 무엇이든 최선을 다하려 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"제가 언젠가 그분의 아내가 되겠다고는 도저히 말할 수 없어요, 페어브러더 씨. 하지만 그분이 성직자가 된다면 결코 그분의 아내가 되지 않을 거예요. 선생님 말씀은 더없이 너그럽고 친절하세요. 저는 한순간도 선생님의 판단을 바로잡으려는 게 아니에요. 다만 제게는 소녀답게 비꼬는 식으로 사물을 바라보는 버릇이 있어서요.\" 메리가 대답에 다시 장난기 어린 반짝임을 띠고 말했는데, 그것이 오히려 그 겸손함을 한층 사랑스럽게 만들었다.\n\n\"그가 바라기로는, 양이 어떻게 생각하는지 그대로 전해 달라더군요.\" 페어브러더 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 우스꽝스러운 남자는 사랑할 수 없어요.\" 메리는 더 깊이 들어가지 않기로 하고 말했다. \"프레드는 마음만 먹으면 어떤 번듯한 세속적인 일에서 존경받을 만한 사람이 될 분별과 지식을 갖추고 있어요. 하지만 그분이 설교를 하고, 권면을 하고, 축복을 선언하고, 병자 곁에서 기도하는 모습은, 무슨 풍자화를 보고 있는 듯한 기분이 들지 않고서는 도무지 상상이 안 돼요. 그분이 성직자가 되는 건 오직 체면 때문일 텐데, 저는 그런 얼빠진 체면치레만큼 경멸스러운 게 없다고 생각해요. 크라우스 씨를 보면서 늘 그런 생각을 했어요. 멍한 얼굴에 말끔한 우산을 들고, 점잔 빼며 짤막한 말을 늘어놓는 그 사람 말이에요. 그런 사람들에게 무슨 자격이 있어서 기독교를 대표한단 말인가요. 마치 기독교가 멍청이들을 점잖게 길러 내는 기관이라도 되는 양, 마치—\" 메리는 말을 멈췄다. 그녀는 마치 페어브러더 씨가 아니라 프레드에게 말하고 있는 듯 격해져 버렸던 것이다.\n\n\"젊은 여인들은 가혹하군요. 남자들처럼 행동의 압박을 느끼지 않으니까요. 하기야 그 점에서는 양을 예외로 두어야 할지도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 프레드 빈시를 그토록 낮은 수준에 놓는 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"네, 정말 그렇지 않아요. 그분은 분별이 넘치는 사람이에요. 다만 성직자로서는 그것을 드러내지 못할 것 같아요. 직업적인 가식 덩어리가 되고 말 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 대답은 아주 분명하군요. 성직자로서는 아무런 희망이 없다는 거지요?\"\n\n메리는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"하지만 그가 다른 길로 생계를 꾸리는 온갖 어려움을 무릅쓴다면 — 그에게 희망이라는 힘을 주시겠습니까? 그가 양의 마음을 얻으리라 기대해도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제가 이미 그분에게 했던 말을 프레드에게 또 일러 줄 필요는 없다고 생각해요.\" 메리가 다소 언짢은 기색으로 대답했다. \"제 말은, 그분이 무언가 할 수 있다고 말만 할 게 아니라 무언가 가치 있는 일을 해낼 때까지는 그런 질문을 해서는 안 된다는 거예요.\"\n\n페어브러더 씨는 일 분 남짓 말이 없었고, 이윽고 두 사람이 잔디 길 끝의 단풍나무 그늘 아래에서 발걸음을 돌려 멈춰 섰을 때 이렇게 말했다. \"양이 어떤 식으로든 속박당하는 데 저항한다는 건 이해합니다. 하지만 양이 프레드 빈시에게 품은 감정이 다른 사람에게 마음을 두는 것을 가로막든가, 아니면 그렇지 않든가 둘 중 하나겠지요. 그가 양의 손을 얻을 자격을 갖출 때까지 양이 독신으로 지내리라 기대해도 되든가, 아니면 어떤 경우에도 그가 실망하게 되든가 말입니다. 용서하세요, 메리 — 알다시피 나는 예전에 그 이름으로 양에게 교리 문답을 가르쳤지요 — 하지만 한 여인의 마음 상태가 다른 한 생명의 행복에, 그것도 하나 이상의 생명의 행복에 닿아 있을 때는, 그 여인이 완전히 솔직하고 숨김없이 처신하는 것이 더 고결한 길이라고 생각합니다.\"\n\n이번에는 메리가 침묵했는데, 페어브러더 씨의 태도가 아니라 그 어조에 의아함을 느꼈다. 거기에는 진지하게 억눌린 감정이 담겨 있었기 때문이다. 그의 말이 그 자신을 가리키는 것일지도 모른다는 야릇한 생각이 문득 머리를 스쳤을 때, 그녀는 믿기지 않았고 그런 생각을 품은 것이 부끄러웠다. 그녀는 프레드 말고는 어떤 남자가 자기를 사랑할 수 있으리라고 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 프레드는 그녀가 양말에 끈 달린 작은 신을 신던 시절에 우산 고리로 그녀와 약혼한 사이였다. 더구나 자기가 좁은 교제 범위 안에서 가장 영리한 남자인 페어브러더 씨에게 무슨 중요한 존재일 수 있으리라고는 더더욱 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 이 모든 것이 어렴풋하고 어쩌면 헛된 것이라고 느낄 겨를밖에 없었으나, 한 가지만은 분명하고 확고했다 — 바로 그녀의 대답이었다.\n\n\"선생님이 그것을 제 도리라고 여기시니, 말씀드리겠어요, 페어브러더 씨. 저는 프레드에게 너무나 강한 감정을 품고 있어서 그분을 다른 누구를 위해서도 포기할 수 없어요. 그분이 저를 잃어 불행하다고 생각하면 저는 결코 온전히 행복할 수 없을 거예요. 그 감정은 제 안에 너무도 깊이 뿌리내렸어요 — 아주 어렸을 적부터 그분이 늘 저를 가장 사랑해 주고, 제가 다칠까 봐 그토록 마음 써 준 것에 대한 고마움 말이에요. 그 마음을 약하게 만들 새로운 감정이 찾아오리라고는 상상조차 할 수 없어요. 저는 무엇보다도 그분이 모두에게 존경받을 만한 사람이 되는 것을 보고 싶어요. 하지만 부디 그분에게 전해 주세요, 그때까지는 그분과 결혼하겠다고 약속하지 않겠다고요. 그러면 저는 아버지와 어머니를 부끄럽게 하고 슬프게 할 거예요. 그분은 자유롭게 다른 사람을 택해도 좋아요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 나는 내 임무를 빈틈없이 완수한 셈이군요.\" 페어브러더 씨가 메리에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"그러니 곧장 미들마치로 말을 달려 돌아가겠습니다. 그에게 이런 가망이 놓여 있으니, 우리는 어떻게든 프레드를 알맞은 자리에 앉히게 될 거고, 나는 두 사람의 손을 맺어 주는 자리에 함께할 만큼 오래 살았으면 합니다. 신의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n\"오, 부디 더 계세요. 차라도 한잔 드리게 해 주세요.\" 메리가 말했다. 그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐는데, 페어브러더 씨의 태도에 깃든 무어라 형언할 수 없는 것, 마치 고통을 단호히 억누르는 듯한 무언가가 그녀를 문득 비참하게 만들었던 것이다. 언젠가 곤경에 처한 순간 아버지의 손이 떨리는 것을 보았을 때 느꼈던 그런 비참함이었다.\n\n\"아니요, 아가씨, 됐습니다. 돌아가야 해요.\"\n\n삼 분 뒤 사제는 다시 말 위에 올라 있었다. 휘스트를 단념하는 것보다, 아니 참회의 명상록을 써 내려가는 것보다 훨씬 더 힘든 의무를 너그러이 치러 낸 채로.\n\n# CHAPTER LIII.\n\n외부인들이 일관성 없음이라 부르는 것에서 곧장 진실하지 못함을 끌어내는 것은 한낱 얄팍한 성급함일 뿐이다. 신앙과 행위가 서로를 떠받치도록 엮어 내는, 살아 있는 무수한 숨은 빨판들을, \"만일\"과 \"그러므로\"라는 죽은 기계 장치로 대체해 버리는 짓이니까.\n\n벌스트로드 씨는 로윅에 새로운 이권을 얻기를 바라던 무렵, 새로 부임할 성직자가 자신이 전적으로 인정할 수 있는 인물이기를 각별히 소망한 것이 당연했다. 그리고 그는 자신을 스톤 코트의 소유주로 만들어 줄 권리 증서를 손에 넣은 바로 그 시기에, 페어브러더 씨가 그 고풍스러운 작은 교회에 \"취임 선서를 읽고\" 농부와 일꾼, 마을 장인 들로 이루어진 신도들 앞에서 첫 설교를 한 것이, 자신의 결점과 나라 전체의 결점을 향한 징계이자 훈계라고 믿었다. 벌스트로드 씨가 로윅 교회에 자주 다니거나 한동안 스톤 코트에 거주할 의도가 있었던 것은 아니다. 그가 그 훌륭한 농장과 멋진 저택을 사들인 것은 그저 은거지로 삼아, 토지는 차츰 넓히고 거처는 아름답게 가꾸어, 마침내 그곳을 거처로 삼는 것이 신의 영광에 이바지하게 되었을 때, 현재의 사업 운영에 들이는 노고에서 어느 정도 물러나, 섭리가 예기치 못한 매입의 기회로 늘려 줄지도 모를 지역 토지 소유권의 무게를 복음의 진리 쪽에 더 두드러지게 실어 놓으려는 뜻에서였다. 모두가 리그 페더스톤 씨라면 에덴동산처럼 그곳에 매달리리라 예상했건만, 뜻밖에도 손쉽게 스톤 코트를 손에 넣게 된 데서 이 방향으로 강한 인도가 있었던 것처럼 보였다. 그것은 가엾은 노인 피터 자신이 예상했던 바이기도 했다. 그는 상상 속에서 자기 위에 덮인 잔디를 올려다보며, 원근에 가로막히지 않고서, 개구리 상판을 한 그 수유자가 다른 생존자들의 끝없는 놀라움과 실망 속에서 그 멋진 옛집을 즐기는 모습을 자주 그려 보았던 것이다.\n\n하지만 우리는 무엇이 이웃에게 낙원이 될지 얼마나 모르는가! 우리는 자신의 욕망으로 판단할 뿐이며, 이웃들 자신도 자기 욕망의 기미조차 드러낼 만큼 늘 솔직하지는 않다. 냉정하고 분별 있는 조슈아 리그는 부친에게 스톤 코트가 자기 평가에서 최고선보다 못한 무엇이라는 낌새를 결코 알아채게 하지 않았고, 분명 그곳을 제 것이라 부르고 싶어 했다. 그러나 워런 헤이스팅스가 금을 보며 데일스퍼드를 사들일 생각을 했듯, 조슈아 리그는 스톤 코트를 보며 금을 사들일 생각을 했다. 그는 제 최고선에 대해 매우 또렷하고 강렬한 환상을 품고 있었으니, 물려받은 왕성한 탐욕이 환경의 힘으로 특별한 형태를 띠게 된 것이었다. 그의 최고선이란 환전상이 되는 것이었다. 항구 도시에서 심부름꾼 소년으로 일하던 가장 어린 시절부터, 그는 다른 소년들이 제과점 진열창을 들여다보듯 환전상의 창문을 들여다보았다. 그 매혹은 차츰 깊고 특별한 열정으로 자라났다. 그는 재산이 생기면 여러 일을 할 작정이었고, 그중 하나는 점잖은 젊은 여자와 결혼하는 것이었다. 그러나 이런 것들은 모두 상상이 없이도 견딜 수 있는 우연이자 즐거움일 뿐이었다. 그의 영혼이 갈망하는 단 하나의 기쁨은, 사람들이 붐비는 부두에 환전상 가게를 차려, 자기가 열쇠를 쥔 자물쇠들로 둘러싸인 채, 만국의 새끼 치는 동전들을 다루며 숭고할 만큼 냉정한 표정을 짓고, 그동안 무력한 탐욕이 철 격자 너머에서 부러운 눈으로 자기를 바라보게 하는 것이었다. 그 열정의 힘은 그것을 충족시키는 데 필요한 모든 지식을 익히게 해 주는 동력이었다. 그래서 남들이 그가 스톤 코트에 평생 자리 잡았다고 여길 때, 조슈아 자신은 이제 머지않아 최고급 금고와 자물쇠를 갖추고 노스 키에 자리 잡을 순간이 오리라 생각하고 있었다.\n\n이만하면 됐다. 우리의 관심사는 조슈아 리그의 토지 매각을 벌스트로드 씨의 관점에서 바라보는 것이며, 그는 이를 한동안 외부의 격려 없이 품어 온 어떤 목적에 어쩌면 승인을 전하는, 고무적인 섭리의 처분으로 해석했다. 그는 그렇게 해석했으나 지나치게 자신만만하지는 않았고, 조심스러운 어구로 감사를 올렸다. 그의 의심은 이 사건이 조슈아 리그의 운명과 맺을지도 모를 관계에서 비롯된 것은 아니었으니, 그 운명은 어쩌면 불완전한 식민지적 방식으로나 모를까, 섭리의 통치 아래 들지 않은 미답의 영역에 속한 것이었다. 그의 의심은 페어브러더 씨가 성직록에 임명된 일이 분명 그러했듯, 이 처분 역시 자신에 대한 징계일지도 모른다는 생각에서 비롯되었다.\n\n이것은 벌스트로드 씨가 누군가를 속이려고 한 말이 아니었다. 그것은 그가 스스로에게 한 말이었다. 그것은, 당신이 그와 의견을 달리한다 해도, 당신의 어떤 이론 못지않게 진정으로 그가 사건을 설명하는 방식이었다. 우리의 이론에 끼어드는 이기심이 그 이론의 진실성을 해치는 것은 아니기 때문이다. 오히려 우리의 이기심이 충족될수록 우리의 신념은 더욱 굳건해진다.\n\n어쨌거나, 승인을 위해서든 징계를 위해서든, 벌스트로드 씨는 피터 페더스톤이 죽은 지 채 열다섯 달이 지나지 않아 스톤 코트의 소유주가 되었고, 피터가 \"알 자격이 있다면\" 무슨 말을 할까 하는 것은 실망한 친척들에게 끝없는 위안의 화젯거리가 되었다. 이제 그 사랑하는 고인이 된 형제에게 형세가 뒤바뀐 셈이었고, 그의 교활함이 만물 전반의 더 우월한 교활함에 좌절되는 모습을 곱씹는 것은 솔로몬에게 되새김질의 즐거움이었다. 월 부인은 가짜 페더스톤을 만들고 진짜를 잘라 낸 짓이 헛수고였다는 증거에서 우울한 승리감을 맛보았다. 그리고 초크 플래츠에서 그 소식을 받은 마사 누이는 이렇게 말했다. \"저런, 저런! 그러니 전능하신 하느님께서도 결국 그 양로원이 그리 마음에 드시지는 않았던 게로구나.\"\n\n다정한 벌스트로드 부인은 스톤 코트 매입으로 남편의 건강이 얻게 될 이점이 특히나 기뻤다. 그가 그곳까지 말을 타고 가서 관리인과 함께 농장 한구석을 둘러보지 않는 날이 드물었고, 새로 쌓아 올린 건초 더미가 풍요로운 옛 정원의 숨결과 어우러질 향기를 내뿜는 그 고요한 곳의 저녁은 감미로웠다. 어느 날 저녁, 해가 아직 지평선 위에서 커다란 호두나무 가지들 사이로 황금 등불처럼 타오르고 있을 때, 벌스트로드 씨는 정문 밖에서 말을 탄 채 멈춰, 약속에 따라 마구간 배수 문제에 대한 의견을 주러 와서는 지금 건초 마당에서 관리인에게 조언을 하고 있는 케일럽 가스를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n벌스트로드 씨는 자신이 좋은 영적 상태에 있으며, 그 무해한 휴양의 영향으로 평소보다 한결 평온하다고 의식하고 있었다. 그는 교리상 자기 안에 공덕이 전혀 없다고 확신하고 있었다. 그러나 그 교리상의 확신은, 죄의식이 기억 속에서 뚜렷한 형태를 띠며 수치의 따가움이나 회한의 아픔을 되살리지 않는 한, 고통 없이 품을 수 있는 것이다. 아니, 우리가 지은 죄의 깊이가 다만 용서의 깊이를 재는 척도가 되고, 우리가 신의 의도가 쓰는 특별한 도구임을 못 박는 증거가 될 때, 그 확신은 강렬한 만족과 함께 품을 수도 있는 것이다. 기억은 기질만큼이나 많은 분위기를 지니며, 디오라마처럼 그 배경을 바꾼다. 이 순간 벌스트로드 씨는 이 햇살이 자신이 아주 젊은 시절 하이버리 너머로 설교하러 나가곤 하던 머나먼 옛 저녁의 햇살과 온전히 하나인 것처럼 느꼈다. 그래서 그는 지금 그 권면의 봉사를 기꺼이 눈앞에 두고 싶었다. 성경 구절들은 여전히 거기 있었고, 그것을 풀어내는 그 자신의 능숙함도 여전했다. 그 짧은 몽상은 역시 말을 타고 막 떠나려고 고삐를 흔들던 케일럽 가스가 돌아오면서 깨졌으니, 그가 이렇게 외쳤던 것이다.\n\n\"이런, 맙소사! 저 길로 오는 검은 옷 입은 사내는 누구지? 경마 끝나고 어슬렁거리는 그런 부류 같은데.\"\n\n벌스트로드 씨는 말을 돌려 길을 따라 바라보았으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 다가오는 사내는 우리가 살짝 아는 래플스 씨였는데, 그 모습은 검은 정장과 검은 상장 모자 띠 말고는 달라진 데가 없었다. 이제 그는 말 탄 사람들로부터 삼 야드 안에 들어와 있었고, 그들은 그가 지팡이를 위로 빙 돌리면서 줄곧 벌스트로드 씨를 바라보다가 마침내 이렇게 외칠 때, 그의 얼굴에 떠오르는 알아봤다는 빛을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n\"이런 세상에, 닉, 너로구나! 잘못 봤을 리가 없지, 이십오 년 세월이 우리 둘 다 늙은 마귀처럼 갉아먹어 놨지만 말이야! 어떻게 지냈나, 응? 여기서 나를 보게 될 줄은 몰랐겠지. 자, 악수나 한번 하세.\" 래플스 씨의 태도가 다소 들떠 있었다고 말하는 것은, 그저 때가 저녁이었다고 말하는 한 가지 방식일 뿐이리라. 케일럽 가스는 벌스트로드 씨 안에 한순간의 갈등과 망설임이 있는 것을 볼 수 있었으나, 그것은 결국 그가 래플스에게 차갑게 손을 내밀며 이렇게 말하는 것으로 끝났다.\n\n\"이 외진 시골에서 자네를 만나리라곤 정말 예상치 못했네.\"\n\n\"뭐, 이곳은 내 의붓아들 소유라네.\" 래플스가 거들먹거리는 자세를 취하며 말했다. \"전에 그 애를 보러 여기 온 적이 있지. 자네를 보고는 그리 놀라지 않았네, 이 친구야, 편지를 한 장 주웠거든. 말하자면 섭리라고 할 만한 일이지. 그래도 자네를 만나다니 어지간히 운이 좋군. 사실 난 의붓아들 보는 데는 관심도 없으니까. 그 애는 다정하지가 않고, 이제 그 가엾은 어미도 세상을 떠났으니. 솔직히 말하면 난 자네가 그리워서 온 걸세, 닉. 자네 주소를 알아보려고 왔지. 이것 좀 보게!\" 래플스가 주머니에서 구겨진 종이를 꺼냈다.\n\n케일럽 가스가 아닌 다른 사람이었다면 거의 누구라도 그 자리에 머물며, 벌스트로드와의 교분이 미들마치에서 알려진 어떤 것과도 너무나 다른, 호기심을 자극하는 비밀의 성격을 띨 수밖에 없는, 은행가 인생의 어떤 일들을 암시하는 듯한 그 사내에 관해 들을 수 있는 모든 것을 듣고 싶은 유혹에 빠졌을지도 모른다. 그러나 케일럽은 별난 사람이었다. 흔히 강한 어떤 인간적 성향들이 그의 마음에는 거의 없었는데, 그중 하나가 남의 사사로운 일에 대한 호기심이었다. 특히 다른 사람에 관해 드러날 떳떳치 못한 무언가가 있다면, 케일럽은 차라리 모르는 편을 택했다. 그리고 아랫사람에게 그의 나쁜 짓이 발각되었음을 알려야 할 때면, 그는 죄지은 당사자보다도 더 난처해했다. 이제 그는 말에 박차를 가하며 \"그럼 안녕히 가십시오, 벌스트로드 씨. 저는 집에 가 봐야겠습니다.\"라고 말하고는 빠른 걸음으로 떠났다.\n\n\"이 편지에 자네 주소를 다 적지 않았더군.\" 래플스가 말을 이었다. \"예전에 자네가 보여 주던 일류 사업가다운 면모가 아니야. '슈러브스'라니, 그건 어디든 있을 수 있잖나. 자넨 근처에 사는 게로군, 응? 런던 사업은 통째로 접고, 아마 시골 지주가 된 게로군. 나를 초대할 시골 저택도 있고. 세상에, 얼마나 오랜 세월이 흘렀는지! 그 노부인은 진작에 돌아가신 지 꽤 됐겠군. 자기 딸이 얼마나 가난하게 사는지 알게 되는 고통도 없이 영광 속으로 가셨겠어, 응? 그런데 이런, 닉, 자네 안색이 아주 창백하고 누렇구먼. 자, 집에 가는 길이면 내가 옆에서 같이 걸어 주지.\"\n\n벌스트로드 씨의 평소 창백한 안색은 실로 거의 죽은 사람 같은 빛을 띠고 있었다. 오 분 전만 해도 그의 인생의 드넓은 폭은, 기억 속의 아침을 향해 거슬러 비치는 저녁 햇살에 잠겨 있었다. 죄란 교리와 내면의 참회의 문제로, 굴욕이란 골방에서 행하는 수련으로, 자기 행위의 무게란 오로지 영적 관계와 신의 목적에 대한 관념으로만 조율되는 사사로운 환영의 문제로 여겨졌다. 그런데 이제, 무슨 흉측한 마법에라도 걸린 듯, 이 떠들썩한 붉은 형상이 다루기 힘든 견고함으로 그의 앞에 솟아오른 것이다. 그가 상상하던 징계의 범주에는 들어 있지 않던, 육신을 입은 과거였다. 그러나 벌스트로드 씨의 생각은 분주히 움직였고, 그는 경솔하게 행동하거나 말할 사람이 아니었다.\n\n\"집에 가는 길이었네만, 좀 미뤄도 되겠지. 그리고 원한다면 여기서 좀 쉬어도 좋네.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"고맙군.\" 래플스가 얼굴을 찡그리며 말했다. \"이제 의붓아들 보는 데는 관심 없어. 차라리 자네와 함께 집으로 가는 편이 낫지.\"\n\n\"자네 의붓아들이 리그 페더스톤 씨라면, 그는 이제 여기 없네. 이제 이곳의 주인은 나야.\"\n\n래플스는 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 길게 휘파람을 불며 놀라움을 드러낸 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"그렇다면야, 나도 이의 없네. 큰길에서부터 걸어왔더니 이제 걷는 건 충분해. 난 원래 걷는 것도, 말 타는 것도 그리 즐기는 편이 아니었지. 내가 좋아하는 건 멋진 마차에 기운찬 조랑말이야. 난 늘 안장에 앉으면 좀 무거웠거든. 나를 보다니 자네한텐 얼마나 즐거운 뜻밖의 일이겠나, 이 친구야!\" 그들이 집 쪽으로 돌아서며 그가 말을 이었다. \"자넨 그렇다고 말은 안 하지만 말이야. 하지만 자넨 한 번도 행운을 마음껏 누린 적이 없었어. 늘 기회를 잘 써먹을 궁리만 했지. 자넨 행운을 잘 써먹는 데 그런 재주가 있었으니까.\"\n\n래플스 씨는 제 익살을 무척이나 즐기는 듯했고, 거들먹거리는 태도로 다리를 흔들어 댔으니, 그것은 그의 동행이 분별 있게 인내하기에는 다소 지나친 것이었다.\n\n\"내 기억이 옳다면, 래플스 씨.\" 벌스트로드 씨가 싸늘한 분노를 담아 말했다. \"여러 해 전 우리의 교분은 자네가 지금 가장하는 그런 종류의 친밀함은 아니었네. 자네가 내게 바라는 어떤 도움이든, 우리의 옛 교제에는 없었고 이십 년이 넘는 단절로 도무지 정당화될 수 없는 그런 허물없는 말투를 삼간다면 한결 기꺼이 베풀어질 걸세.\"\n\n\"닉이라고 불리는 게 싫은가? 아니, 난 마음속으로는 늘 자넬 닉이라 불렀어, 눈에서 멀어졌어도 기억엔 정겨운 사람이었지. 이런, 자네에 대한 내 감정은 잘 묵은 코냑처럼 농익었다네. 지금 집에 코냑이 좀 있으면 좋겠군. 지난번엔 조시가 내 술병을 가득 채워 줬는데 말이야.\"\n\n벌스트로드 씨는 래플스에게 코냑을 향한 욕망조차 남을 괴롭히려는 욕망보다 더 강하지는 않다는 것을, 그리고 짜증의 기미가 늘 그에게 새로운 신호 노릇을 한다는 것을 아직 충분히 깨닫지 못하고 있었다. 그러나 더 이상의 반대가 소용없다는 것만은 적어도 분명했고, 벌스트로드 씨는 손님을 묵게 하라고 가정부에게 지시하면서도 단호한 평정의 기색을 보였다.\n\n이 가정부가 리그를 모셨던 사람이기도 하니, 벌스트로드 씨가 단지 옛 주인의 친구로서 래플스를 대접하는 것이라고 여겨 줄지 모른다는 데에 위안이 있었다.\n\n징두리 판자를 댄 거실에 손님 앞으로 음식과 술이 차려지고 방 안에 다른 목격자가 없게 되자, 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자네와 나의 습성은 너무나 다르니, 래플스 씨, 우리는 서로의 교제를 즐기기 어렵네. 그러니 우리 둘 모두에게 가장 현명한 방책은 되도록 빨리 헤어지는 것일세. 자네가 나를 만나고 싶었다고 하니, 아마 나와 처리할 무슨 용무가 있다고 여긴 모양이지. 하지만 이런 사정이니, 오늘 밤은 여기 묵으라고 청하겠네. 그리고 내가 내일 아침 일찍, 사실은 조반 전에 직접 이곳으로 말을 타고 와서, 자네가 내게 할 어떤 이야기든 듣겠네.\"\n\n\"좋고말고.\" 래플스가 말했다. \"여긴 편안한 곳이군. 오래 있기엔 좀 따분하지만, 이 좋은 술이 있고 아침에 자넬 다시 볼 기약이 있으니 하룻밤쯤은 견딜 만하지. 자넨 내 의붓아들보다 훨씬 나은 주인이야. 하긴 조시는 내가 제 어미와 결혼한 것에 좀 앙심을 품었지. 그래도 자네와 나 사이엔 친절 말고는 아무것도 없었으니까.\"\n\n벌스트로드 씨는 래플스의 태도에 깃든 그 묘한 쾌활함과 냉소의 혼합이 상당 부분 술의 작용이기를 바라며, 그가 완전히 술이 깰 때까지 기다렸다가 더 말을 섞기로 작정했다. 그러나 집으로 말을 타고 가는 내내, 이 사내와는 영구히 믿고 의지할 수 있는 어떤 결말도 마련하기가 얼마나 어려울지 무섭도록 또렷한 환영에 시달렸다. 존 래플스의 재출현을 신의 계획 밖에 놓인 일로 볼 수는 없었지만, 그를 떼어 내고 싶은 마음만은 피할 수 없었다. 악의 영이 선의 도구인 벌스트로드 씨를 무너뜨리려고 위협하려 그를 보냈는지도 모른다. 그러나 그 위협은 허락된 것이 분명했고, 새로운 종류의 징계였다. 그것은 그에게 고뇌의 시간이었으니, 그의 갈등이 안전하게 사사로웠고 자신의 은밀한 비행이 용서받고 봉사가 받아들여졌다는 느낌으로 끝나곤 하던 그 시간들과는 사뭇 달랐다. 그 비행들조차도, 저질러질 당시에, 자기 자신과 자기가 가진 모든 것을 신의 계획을 진척시키는 데 바치려는 한결같은 열망으로 반쯤 성스러워진 것이 아니었던가? 그런데 결국 그가 한낱 걸림돌이요 거치는 바위가 되고 만단 말인가? 누가 그의 내면의 역사를 이해해 주겠는가? 그에게 치욕을 씌울 구실이 생긴다면, 누군들 그의 평생과 그가 신봉해 온 진리를 한 무더기의 오욕 속에 한데 뒤섞어 버리지 않겠는가?\n\n가장 은밀한 명상 속에서, 벌스트로드 씨의 마음이 평생 들인 습성은 그의 가장 이기적인 공포들을 초인간적 목적에 대한 교리적 언급으로 감쌌다. 그러나 우리가 지구의 궤도와 태양계에 관해 이야기하고 사색하는 동안에도, 우리가 실제로 느끼고 그에 맞추어 움직이는 것은 안정된 대지와 변해 가는 하루다. 그리고 이제, 그 모든 자동으로 이어지는 이론적 어구들 안쪽에는, 우리가 추상적 고통을 논할 때 닥쳐오는 열병의 오한과 통증처럼 또렷하고 깊숙한 것으로서, 이웃들과 제 아내 앞에서 치욕을 당하리라는 예감이 자리하고 있었다. 그 고통이란, 그리고 치욕에 대한 세간의 평가란, 앞서 내세운 표방의 양에 달려 있기 때문이다. 중죄를 면하는 것만을 목표로 삼는 사람들에게는 피고석에 서는 것 말고는 무엇도 치욕이 아니다. 그러나 벌스트로드 씨는 저명한 기독교인이 되는 것을 목표로 삼아 왔던 것이다.\n\n그가 다시 스톤 코트에 이른 것은 아침 일곱 시 반이 채 되지 않은 때였다. 그 멋진 옛집이 그 순간만큼 기쁨에 찬 보금자리처럼 보인 적이 없었다. 커다란 흰 백합이 꽃을 피웠고, 이슬에 온통 잎이 은빛으로 물든 어여쁜 한련화가 낮은 돌담 위로 뻗어 나가고 있었다. 사방의 온갖 소리마저 그 안에 평화의 심장을 품고 있었다. 그러나 앞뜰 자갈길을 거닐며, 함께 조반을 들도록 운명 지어진 래플스 씨가 내려오기를 기다리는 주인에게는 모든 것이 망가져 버렸다.\n\n오래지 않아 그들은 징두리 판자를 댄 거실에 함께 앉아 차와 토스트를 들었는데, 그것이 그 이른 시각에 래플스가 들고 싶어 한 전부였다. 그의 아침의 자아와 저녁의 자아의 차이는 그의 동행이 짐작했을 만큼 크지는 않았다. 어쩌면 기분이 다소 덜 들떠 있었던 탓에 괴롭히는 즐거움은 오히려 더 강했는지도 모른다. 확실히 그의 태도는 아침 햇살 아래서 더 불쾌해 보였다.\n\n\"내겐 시간이 별로 없으니, 래플스 씨.\" 차를 홀짝이고 토스트를 부수기만 할 뿐 먹지는 못하는 은행가가 말했다. \"자네가 나를 만나고 싶어 한 까닭을 곧바로 말해 준다면 고맙겠네. 자네에게 다른 곳에 거처가 있어 그리로 기꺼이 돌아가리라 짐작하네만.\"\n\n\"아니, 사람에게 마음이라는 게 있다면 옛 친구를 보고 싶지 않겠나, 닉? 자넬 닉이라고 불러야겠어. 자네가 그 늙은 과부와 결혼할 작정인 걸 알았을 때 우린 늘 자넬 젊은 닉이라고 불렀잖나. 어떤 이들은 자네가 늙은 닉 영감, 그러니까 악마와 잘생긴 집안 내력처럼 닮았다고들 했지만, 그건 자네 어머니 탓이지, 자넬 니콜라스라 이름 지으셨으니. 날 다시 만나서 기쁘지 않은가? 난 자네가 어느 근사한 곳에 와서 묵으라고 초대해 줄 줄 알았네. 내 살림은 이제 아내가 죽어서 다 흩어졌어. 난 어느 한 곳에 딱히 정붙인 데가 없으니, 어디든 마찬가지로 이 근방에 자리 잡아도 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"자네가 미국에서 돌아온 까닭을 물어도 되겠나? 자네가 충분한 돈을 받고 그리로 가겠다고 강하게 바랐을 때, 나는 그것이 거기서 평생 머물겠다는 약속과 다름없다고 여겼네.\"\n\n\"어느 곳에 가고 싶다는 게 거기 머물고 싶다는 것과 같은 말이라고는 들어 본 적이 없는데. 그래도 한 십 년쯤은 머물렀어. 더 오래 있는 건 내게 맞지 않더군. 그리고 다시는 안 갈 걸세, 닉.\" 여기서 래플스 씨는 벌스트로드 씨를 바라보며 천천히 윙크를 했다.\n\n\"무슨 사업에든 자리를 잡고 싶은 건가? 지금 자네 생업은 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"고맙네만, 내 생업은 되도록 즐기는 거라네. 이제 일하는 데는 관심 없어. 뭔가를 한다면 담배 거래로 좀 돌아다니는 정도, 아니면 그런 비슷한, 사람을 유쾌한 무리 속에 끼게 해 주는 일이겠지. 하지만 기댈 만한 자립 밑천 없이는 안 되지. 내가 원하는 게 바로 그걸세. 난 예전만큼 튼튼하지 않아, 닉. 자네보다 혈색은 더 좋지만 말이야. 난 자립 밑천이 필요하네.\"\n\n\"자네가 멀리 떨어져 있겠다고 약속한다면, 그것은 마련해 줄 수 있네.\" 벌스트로드 씨가, 낮은 목소리에 어쩌면 다소 지나친 열의를 담아 말했다.\n\n\"그건 내 편의에 맞아야지.\" 래플스가 차갑게 말했다. \"내가 이 근방에서 아는 사람 몇쯤 못 사귈 이유가 없잖나. 난 누구에게든 동행으로서 나 자신이 부끄럽지 않아. 내려서는 여행 가방을 통행료 징수소에 맡겨 뒀지. 갈아입을 속옷도 있어, 진짜야, 정말로 명예를 걸고, 셔츠 앞자락이며 소맷동 정도가 아니라네. 게다가 이 상복 차림에 끈이며 뭐며 다 갖췄으니, 여기 양반네들 사이에서도 자네 체면을 세워 주겠는걸.\" 래플스 씨는 의자를 뒤로 밀고 자기 몸을, 특히 바지의 끈을 내려다보았다. 그의 주된 의도는 벌스트로드를 괴롭히는 것이었지만, 그는 진심으로 자신의 지금 모습이 좋은 인상을 주리라고, 자신이 잘생기고 재치 있을 뿐 아니라 탄탄한 연줄을 암시하는 상복 차림을 하고 있다고 생각했다.\n\n\"자네가 어떤 식으로든 나를 의지할 작정이라면, 래플스 씨.\" 벌스트로드가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. \"자넨 내 뜻에 맞추기를 기대받을 걸세.\"\n\n\"아, 그야 물론이지.\" 래플스가 비웃는 듯한 다정함을 담아 말했다. \"내가 늘 그러지 않았나? 세상에, 자넨 날 가지고 짭짤하게 재미를 봤지만 난 받은 게 별로 없었지. 그 뒤로 종종 생각했네, 그 노파에게 딸과 손주를 찾았다고 일러 주는 편이 나았을 거라고. 그게 내 심정에는 더 맞았을 텐데. 난 마음 한구석이 무른 데가 있거든. 하지만 그 노부인은 지금쯤 자네가 묻어 드렸겠지. 이제 그분에겐 다 매한가지지만. 그리고 자넨 그토록 큰 축복을 받은 그 잘나가는 사업에서 재산을 일궜고. 양반 행세를 하며 땅을 사들이고 시골 영주가 됐구먼. 아직도 비국교도 노선인가, 응? 여전히 신앙심이 깊은가? 아니면 더 점잖다고 국교회로 갈아탔나?\"\n\n이번에 래플스 씨의 느린 윙크와 살짝 내민 혀는 악몽보다도 더 끔찍했으니, 그것이 악몽이 아니라 깨어 있는 채로 겪는 비참함이라는 확신을 담고 있었기 때문이다. 벌스트로드 씨는 진저리 치는 메스꺼움을 느꼈고, 말은 하지 않은 채, 차라리 래플스가 제멋대로 하게 내버려 두고 그를 한낱 비방꾼으로 몰아 맞서야 하지 않을까 부지런히 궁리하고 있었다. 그 사내는 머지않아 사람들이 그의 말을 믿지 않을 만큼 추잡한 자임을 스스로 드러낼 터였다. \"하지만 그자가 자네에 관한, 보기 흉한 진실을 말할 때는 그렇지 않지.\" 분별 있는 의식이 말했다. 그리고 또 이렇게도. 래플스를 멀리 떼어 두는 것은 잘못이 아닌 듯했으나, 벌스트로드 씨는 참된 진술을 부인하는 노골적인 거짓에는 움츠러들었다. 용서받은 죄들을 되돌아보는 것, 아니, 느슨한 관습에 마지못해 따른 미심쩍은 동조를 해명하는 것은 하나의 일이요, 거짓을 말해야 할 필요 속으로 짐짓 발을 들이는 것은 또 다른 일이었다.\n\n그러나 벌스트로드가 말을 하지 않자, 래플스는 시간을 최대한 써먹으려는 양 줄곧 떠들어 댔다.\n\n\"난 자네만큼 좋은 운을 누리진 못했어, 정말이지! 뉴욕에서 일이 지독하게 꼬였거든. 그 양키들은 보통내기가 아니라서, 신사다운 감정을 지닌 사람은 도무지 그들을 당해 낼 재간이 없어. 돌아와서 결혼했지. 담배 장사를 하는 참한 여자였는데, 날 무척 좋아했어. 그런데 그 장사가, 우리 말로 하자면, 제약이 있었지. 그 여잔 어떤 친구가 거기 오래전에 자리를 잡아 줬는데, 군더더기 같은 아들이 하나 딸려 있었거든. 조시와 나는 도무지 마음이 맞질 않았어. 그래도 난 그 처지에서 최대한 잘 지냈고, 늘 좋은 사람들과 어울려 한잔씩 걸쳤지. 난 매사에 떳떳했어. 대낮처럼 솔직한 사람이지. 진작 자넬 찾아오지 않았다고 언짢게 여기진 말게. 난 사람을 좀 굼뜨게 만드는 지병이 있거든. 자네가 아직 런던에서 장사하고 기도하며 지내는 줄 알았는데, 거기 없더군. 하지만 보다시피 난 자네에게로 보내진 걸세, 닉. 어쩌면 우리 둘 모두에게 축복이 되라고 말이야.\"\n\n래플스 씨는 우스꽝스러운 콧소리로 말을 맺었다. 종교적 위선보다 제 지성이 우월하다고 느끼는 데서 그를 따를 자가 없었다. 그리고 사람들의 가장 비루한 감정을 셈하는 교활함을 지성이라 부를 수 있다면, 그에게도 제 몫의 지성이 있었으니, 벌스트로드에게 불쑥불쑥 놀려 대는 말투 아래에는 마치 그 말들이 체스의 수인 양 분명한 진술의 선별이 깔려 있었기 때문이다. 그동안 벌스트로드는 자신의 수를 정한 터라, 가다듬은 결의를 담아 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 잘 헤아려 보는 게 좋을 걸세, 래플스 씨. 부당한 이득을 챙기려는 노력으로 사람이 도리어 제 발등을 찍을 수도 있는 법이니. 나는 자네에게 어떤 식으로도 매여 있지 않네만, 자네가 이 근방에서 멀리 떨어져 있겠다는 약속을 지키는 한, 분기마다 지급되는 정기 연금을 기꺼이 대 주겠네. 선택은 자네 손에 달렸어. 자네가 단 한동안이라도 여기 머물기를 고집한다면, 자넨 내게서 아무것도 얻지 못할 걸세. 나는 자네를 모르는 척하겠네.\"\n\n\"하, 하!\" 래플스가 짐짓 폭소를 터뜨리며 말했다. \"그 말을 들으니 순경 모르는 척한 우스운 도둑놈이 생각나는군.\"\n\n\"자네 빗댄 말은 내게 통하지 않네, 이 사람아.\" 벌스트로드가 새하얗게 달아오른 노기로 말했다. \"법은 자네를 통해서든 다른 무엇을 통해서든 나를 옭아맬 게 없어.\"\n\n\"농담도 못 알아듣는구먼, 이 친구야. 난 그저 절대로 자넬 모르는 척하지 않겠다고 말하려던 거야. 하지만 진지하게 얘기해 보세. 자네 분기 지급은 내게 영 맞질 않아. 난 내 자유가 좋거든.\"\n\n여기서 래플스는 일어나 다리를 흔들며 한두 차례 방 안을 거들먹거리며 거닐었고, 무언가 골똘히 사색하는 듯한 태도를 취했다. 마침내 그는 벌스트로드 맞은편에 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"이렇게 하지! 이백 파운드만 내놓게. 자, 이만하면 양심적이지. 그럼 떠나겠네, 명예를 걸고! 여행 가방을 챙겨서 떠나지. 하지만 더러운 연금 따위에 내 자유를 내주지는 않겠어. 난 내키는 데로 오가겠네. 어쩌면 멀리 떨어져 지내면서 친구와 편지나 주고받는 게 내게 맞을지도 모르고, 아닐지도 모르지. 그 돈, 지금 가지고 있나?\"\n\n\"아니, 백 파운드가 있네.\" 벌스트로드가 말했으니, 당장의 해방이 미래의 불확실함을 빌미로 마다하기엔 너무나 큰 안도였던 것이다. \"주소를 알려 주면 나머지는 부쳐 주겠네.\"\n\n\"아니, 자네가 가져올 때까지 여기서 기다리겠네.\" 래플스가 말했다. \"한 바퀴 거닐고 요기나 좀 하고 있으면 그때쯤 자네가 돌아오겠지.\"\n\n벌스트로드 씨의 병약한 몸은, 전날 저녁 이후 겪은 동요로 산산이 부서져, 이 떠들썩하고 끄떡없는 사내의 손아귀에 비굴하게 잡혀 있는 듯한 느낌을 그에게 안겼다. 그 순간 그는 어떤 조건으로든 얻어 낼 수 있는 한때의 휴식을 덥석 붙잡았다. 그가 래플스가 제안한 대로 하려고 일어서는데, 래플스가 무언가 문득 떠오른 듯 손가락을 치켜들며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 세라를 다시 한번 수소문해 봤어, 자네한텐 말 안 했지만. 그 어여쁜 젊은 여자에 대해선 나도 양심이 좀 찔렸거든. 그 여잘 찾진 못했지만, 남편 이름은 알아내서 적어 뒀지. 그런데 빌어먹을, 수첩을 잃어버렸어. 그래도 다시 들으면 알아볼 걸세. 난 정신만은 한창때처럼 멀쩡하지만, 이름이란 게 닳아 없어진단 말이야, 정말이지! 가끔은 이름이 채워지기 전의 그 빌어먹을 세금 고지서나 다름없다니까. 어쨌든 그 여자와 그 가족 소식을 들으면 자네한테 알려 줌세, 닉. 이제 그 여자가 자네 의붓딸이 됐으니, 뭔가 해 주고 싶겠지.\"\n\n\"틀림없지.\" 벌스트로드 씨가 옅은 잿빛 눈으로 평소의 흔들림 없는 시선을 보내며 말했다. \"그러면 자넬 도울 내 여력이 줄어들지도 모르네만.\"\n\n그가 방을 나서자, 래플스는 그의 등 뒤로 천천히 윙크를 했고, 이어 창문 쪽으로 돌아서서 사실상 제 명령에 따라 말을 타고 떠나가는 은행가를 지켜보았다. 그의 입술은 처음엔 미소로 비틀렸다가, 이내 벌어지며 짤막한 의기양양한 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"그런데 그 이름이 도대체 뭐였더라?\" 이윽고 그가 머리를 긁적이고 이마를 가로로 찌푸리며 반쯤 소리 내어 말했다. 그는 벌스트로드를 괴롭힐 거리를 궁리하다 떠올리기 전까지는 이 깜빡한 대목을 사실 마음에 두거나 생각해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"엘 자로 시작했는데. 거의 죄다 엘 자였던 것 같아.\" 그는 그 미끄러운 이름을 붙잡아 가는 듯한 느낌으로 말을 이었다. 그러나 잡은 것이 너무 어설펐고, 그는 곧 이 머릿속 추적에 싫증이 났다. 래플스 씨만큼 혼자 하는 일을 못 견뎌 하거나, 끊임없이 제 목소리를 남들에게 들려주어야 하는 사람도 드물었기 때문이다. 그는 자기 시간을 관리인과 가정부와의 유쾌한 대화에 쓰는 편을 택했고, 그들에게서 미들마치에서의 벌스트로드 씨의 처지에 관해 알고 싶은 만큼 알아냈다.\n\n그래도 결국엔 빵과 치즈와 에일로 달래야 할 따분한 시간이 있었고, 징두리 판자를 댄 거실에 이 양식들을 곁에 두고 홀로 앉아 있던 그는 별안간 제 무릎을 탁 치며 외쳤다. \"래디슬로!\" 그가 굴러가게 하려 애쓰다 절망 속에 내버려 둔 그 기억의 작용이, 의식적인 노력 없이 별안간 저절로 완성된 것이었다. 떠올린 이름이 아무런 값어치가 없다 해도, 다 끝낸 재채기처럼 흐뭇한, 흔한 경험이었다. 래플스는 곧장 수첩을 꺼내 그 이름을 적었으니, 그것을 쓸 작정이 있어서가 아니라 그저 언젠가 정말 필요해질 경우에 난감해지지 않으려는 셈이었다. 그는 벌스트로드에게 말할 생각이 없었다. 말해 봐야 실속이 없었고, 래플스 씨 같은 사람의 마음에는 비밀 속에 늘 그럴싸한 실속이 있는 법이었다.\n\n그는 지금의 성과에 만족했고, 그날 세 시 무렵 통행료 징수소에서 여행 가방을 챙겨 마차에 올랐으니, 스톤 코트의 풍경에 박힌 보기 흉한 검은 점을 벌스트로드 씨의 눈에서 거두어 갔다. 그러나 그 검은 점이 다시 나타나 제 화롯가의 환영에서마저 떼어 낼 수 없게 될지도 모른다는 두려움까지 거두어 가지는 못했다.\n\n# BOOK VI. THE WIDOW AND THE WIFE. CHAPTER LIV.\n\n내 여인은 그 두 눈에 사랑을 담고 다니니 그녀가 바라보는 것은 무엇이든 고결해지네. 그녀가 지나가는 곳마다 모든 이가 그녀를 향해 돌아서고, 그녀가 인사하는 이의 심장은 떨리네.\n\n그리하여 그는 얼굴을 숙인 채 온통 창백해지고, 자기의 온갖 결점을 그제야 한숨지으며 뉘우치네. 그녀 앞에서는 오만과 분노가 달아나니, 숙녀들이여, 나를 도와 그녀를 기리게 하소서.\n\n온갖 달콤함과 온갖 겸허한 생각이 그녀의 말을 듣는 이의 가슴속에서 피어나니, 그녀를 가장 먼저 본 이는 복되도다. 그녀가 살며시 미소 지을 때의 그 모습은 말로 할 수도, 마음에 담아 둘 수도 없으니, 그토록 새롭고 고결한 기적이라네.\n― 단테, 『신생』\n\n스톤 코트의 건초 더미가 마치 래플스 씨가 가장 고운 향을 받을 만한 손님이기라도 한 듯 공기 중에 아무런 차별 없이 향기를 뿜어내던 그 상쾌한 아침 무렵, 도로테아는 다시 로윅 저택에 거처를 정한 터였다. 석 달이 지나니 프레시트는 다소 답답하게 느껴졌다. 실리아의 아기를 황홀하게 바라보는 성 카타리나의 모델처럼 앉아 있는 일을 하루에 여러 시간씩 할 수는 없었고, 그 중대한 아기의 면전에서 한결같이 무관심한 태도로 머무는 것은 아이 없는 이모로서 용납될 수 없는 처사였다. 도로테아는 필요하다면 아기를 안고 1마일이라도 기쁘게 걸어갈 수 있었고, 그 수고 때문에 아기를 더욱 다정하게 사랑할 수도 있었을 것이다. 하지만 어린 조카를 부처로 받아들이지 않고, 그저 감탄하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없는 이모에게는, 아기의 행동이 단조롭게 보이고 그를 바라보는 흥미도 쉬이 바닥나기 마련이다. 이런 가능성은 실리아에게는 전혀 보이지 않았으니, 그녀는 도로테아의 아이 없는 과부 신세가 어린 아서의 탄생(아기는 브룩 씨의 이름을 따서 지어졌다)과 아주 그럴싸하게 맞아떨어진다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"도도는 자기 것을 가지는 일에는 도무지 마음 쓰지 않는 사람이야. 아이든 뭐든 말이야!\" 실리아가 남편에게 말했다. \"게다가 언니가 아기를 낳았다 해도 결코 아서만큼 사랑스럽지는 못했을 거예요. 안 그래요, 제임스?\"\n\n\"캐소본을 닮았다면 그랬겠지.\" 제임스 경은 자기 대답에 어딘가 에두른 데가 있음을, 그리고 자기 맏아들의 완벽함에 관해서는 엄격히 사적인 견해를 품고 있음을 의식하며 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요! 상상해 봐요! 정말이지 다행이었어요.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 도도가 과부인 게 아주 좋다고 생각해요. 언니는 우리 아기를 자기 아이처럼 사랑할 수 있고, 자기가 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 자기만의 생각을 품을 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"언니가 여왕이 아니라는 게 안타깝군.\" 경건한 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그러면 우리는 뭐가 됐을까요? 우리는 다른 무엇이 되어야 했을 거예요.\" 실리아가 그토록 수고로운 상상의 비약에 반대하며 말했다. \"나는 지금 그대로의 언니가 더 좋아요.\"\n\n그리하여 도로테아가 로윅으로 영영 떠날 채비를 하고 있음을 알았을 때, 실리아는 실망하여 눈썹을 치켜올리고는, 특유의 조용하고 강조 없는 투로 빈정거림의 바늘 화살을 쏘았다.\n\n\"로윅에서 뭘 하려는 거야, 도도? 언니 입으로도 거기서는 할 일이 없다고 했잖아. 다들 너무 깨끗하고 잘살아서 오히려 우울해진다고 말이야. 그런데 여기서는 가스 씨와 함께 팁턴을 두루 돌아다니며, 가장 형편없는 뒷마당까지 다니면서 그토록 행복해했으면서. 게다가 이제 삼촌도 외국에 가셨으니 언니랑 가스 씨가 마음대로 할 수 있잖아. 그리고 제임스는 언니가 시키는 건 뭐든 다 한다니까.\"\n\n\"여기에 자주 올 거야. 그러면 아기가 자라는 모습도 오히려 더 잘 볼 수 있을 거고.\" 도로테아가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 아기 목욕시키는 건 절대 못 볼 거야.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"그게 하루 중에 제일 좋은 부분인데.\" 그녀는 거의 입을 삐죽이고 있었다. 도도가 머물 수 있는데도 아기 곁을 떠나는 것이 정말이지 너무하다고 여겨졌던 것이다.\n\n\"키티야, 일부러라도 와서 하룻밤 묵을게.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"하지만 지금은 혼자, 내 집에 있고 싶어. 페어브러더 댁 사람들을 더 잘 알고 싶고, 미들마치에서 해야 할 일에 관해 페어브러더 씨와 이야기하고 싶어.\"\n\n도로테아의 타고난 강한 의지력은 더 이상 온전히 단호한 복종으로만 전환되지 않았다. 그녀는 로윅에 있고 싶은 간절한 바람이 있었고, 그저 가기로 마음먹었으며, 자기 이유를 모두 밝혀야 한다고 느끼지도 않았다. 하지만 주위 사람은 모두 반대했다. 제임스 경은 몹시 마음 아파하며, 다들 그 신성한 궤짝, 달리 말하면 요람을 들고 몇 달간 첼튼엄으로 옮겨 가자고 제안했다. 그 시절에는 첼튼엄이 거절당하면 남자로서 달리 무엇을 제안해야 할지 알기 어려웠다.\n\n도시에 사는 딸을 방문하고 막 돌아온 노부인 체텀 부인은, 적어도 비고 부인에게 편지를 써서 캐소본 부인의 말동무 자리를 맡아 달라고 청해야 한다고 했다. 젊은 과부인 도로테아가 로윅의 집에서 혼자 살 생각을 하다니 믿을 수 없는 일이었다. 비고 부인은 왕족의 낭독자이자 비서였던 사람으로, 지식과 정서 면에서는 도로테아조차 그녀에게 흠잡을 데가 없을 정도였다.\n\n캐드월래더 부인은 은밀히 말했다. \"그 집에서 혼자 지내면 자네는 분명 미쳐 버릴 거야, 그러면 헛것을 보게 될 테고. 우리는 모두 제정신을 유지하려고, 또 다른 사람들이 부르는 이름과 똑같이 사물을 부르려고 조금은 애써야 한다네. 물론 돈 없는 차남들이나 여자들에게는 미치는 게 일종의 살길이긴 하지. 그러면 보살핌을 받게 되니까. 하지만 자네는 그렇게 빠져들면 안 돼. 우리 마음씨 좋은 노부인 때문에 여기서 좀 지루할 거라는 건 인정하네. 하지만 자네가 늘 비극의 여왕을 연기하며 만사를 숭고하게 받아들인다면, 동료 인간들에게 자네 자신이 얼마나 지루한 존재가 될지 생각해 보게. 로윅의 그 서재에 홀로 앉아 있으면 자네가 날씨를 다스린다고 착각하게 될지도 몰라. 그러니 자네가 무슨 말을 해도 믿지 않을 사람들을 몇 명 곁에 두어야 해. 그게 좋은 진정제거든.\"\n\n\"저는 제 주위 사람들이 모든 것을 부르는 이름과 똑같이 부른 적이 한 번도 없는걸요.\" 도로테아가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 이제 자네의 실수를 깨달았겠지, 그렇지?\" 캐드월래더 부인이 말했다. \"그게 바로 제정신이라는 증거라네.\"\n\n도로테아는 그 가시를 의식했지만, 마음이 상하지는 않았다. \"아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 여전히 세상 대부분이 많은 것에 대해 잘못 생각하고 있다고 봐요. 분명 제정신이면서도 그렇게 생각할 수 있어요. 세상 대부분이 종종 자기 의견을 바꾸어야 했으니까요.\"\n\n캐드월래더 부인은 그 점에 대해 도로테아에게 더 말하지 않았으나, 남편에게는 이렇게 언급했다. \"적당한 때가 되면 가능한 한 빨리 다시 결혼하는 게 저 사람에게 좋을 거예요. 제대로 된 사람들 틈에 넣어 줄 수만 있다면 말이죠. 물론 체텀가에서는 원치 않겠지만. 하지만 저 사람을 다잡는 데는 남편이 제일이라는 게 내 눈에는 훤히 보여요. 우리가 이렇게 가난하지만 않다면 트라이턴 경을 초대하겠어요. 그는 언젠가 후작이 될 사람이고, 저 여자가 훌륭한 후작 부인이 될 거라는 건 부인할 수 없죠. 상복을 입으니 그 어느 때보다 아름다워 보이잖아요.\"\n\n\"여보 엘리너, 제발 그 가엾은 여자를 가만 좀 내버려 두구려. 그런 꾀를 부려 봐야 아무 소용 없소.\" 느긋한 교구 목사가 말했다.\n\n\"소용없다고요? 남자와 여자를 한자리에 모으는 것 말고 어떻게 혼사가 이루어지겠어요? 게다가 하필 지금 저 여자 삼촌이 달아나 그레인지를 닫아 버린 게 너무하지 뭐예요. 프레시트와 그레인지에 마땅한 혼처들을 잔뜩 초대해야 마땅한데. 트라이턴 경이 딱 알맞은 사람이에요. 좀 무른 데가 있긴 해도 사람들을 행복하게 해 줄 계획으로 가득한 사람이거든요. 그게 캐소본 부인에게 딱 어울려요.\"\n\n\"캐소본 부인이 스스로 고르도록 두구려, 엘리너.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 당신네 똑똑한 양반들이 늘어놓는 헛소리예요! 고를 만한 변변한 선택지가 없는데 어떻게 고른단 말예요? 여자의 선택이란 대개 손에 넣을 수 있는 단 하나의 남자를 받아들이는 거라고요. 내 말 명심해요, 험프리. 저 여자 친구들이 애쓰지 않으면 캐소본 사건보다 더 고약한 일이 또 벌어질 거예요.\"\n\n\"제발 그 이야기는 꺼내지 마시오, 엘리너! 제임스 경에게는 아주 아픈 대목이라오. 당신이 괜히 그 양반 앞에서 그 일을 들먹였다간 크게 노여워할 거요.\"\n\n\"나는 그 이야기를 꺼낸 적이 한 번도 없어요.\" 캐드월래더 부인이 두 손을 펼치며 말했다. \"실리아가 처음부터 유언장에 관해 다 이야기해 준 거예요. 내가 묻지도 않았는데 말이죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 하지만 그쪽에서는 그 일을 덮어 두고 싶어 하고, 내가 듣기로는 그 젊은 친구가 이 동네를 떠난다더군.\"\n\n캐드월래더 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 검은 두 눈에 몹시 빈정대는 표정을 띤 채 남편을 향해 의미심장하게 세 번 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n도로테아는 만류와 설득에도 불구하고 조용히 고집을 부렸다. 그리하여 6월 말이 되자 로윅 저택의 덧창이 모두 열렸고, 아침 햇살은 잊힌 신앙의 말 없는 기념물인 거대한 돌들이 심긴 황량하고 쓸쓸한 들판을 비추듯 차분하게 서재를 들여다보며 줄지어 늘어선 노트들 위를 비추었다. 그리고 장미꽃을 가득 실은 저녁은 도로테아가 가장 즐겨 앉던 청록색 내실로 소리 없이 스며들었다. 처음에 그녀는 방마다 들어가 보며 자신의 결혼 생활 열여덟 달을 되짚었고, 남편이 들어야 할 이야기인 양 생각을 이어 갔다. 그러다 서재에 머물렀으며, 남편이 보고 싶어 했으리라 짐작되는 대로 노트들을 모두 정연한 순서로 조심스레 정리해 놓고서야 마음이 놓였다. 그와 함께한 삶에서 억제하고 강제하는 동력이 되었던 그 연민은, 분개한 마음으로 그에게 항변하며 당신은 부당하다고 따지는 와중에도 여전히 그의 모습에 들러붙어 있었다. 그녀의 어떤 사소한 행동 하나는 어쩌면 미신적이라며 미소를 자아낼지도 모른다. 캐소본 부인이 쓰도록 마련된 그 개요표를, 그녀는 조심스럽게 봉투에 넣어 봉인하고는 봉투 안에 이렇게 적었다. \"저는 이것을 쓸 수 없었어요. 제가 믿지 않는 일에 가망 없이 매달림으로써 제 영혼을 당신의 영혼에 복종시킬 수 없었다는 걸 이제 아시겠지요? ― 도로테아.\" 그러고는 그 종이를 자기 책상에 넣어 두었다.\n\n그 말 없는 대화는 어쩌면 그 모든 것 아래로, 그리고 그것을 관통해 늘 흐르던 깊은 갈망, 진정으로 그녀를 로윅으로 오게 만든 그 갈망 때문에 한층 더 간절했는지도 모른다. 그 갈망은 윌 래디슬로를 보고 싶다는 것이었다. 그녀는 두 사람의 만남에서 무슨 좋은 일이 생길 수 있을지 알지 못했다. 그녀는 속수무책이었다. 그의 처지에 깃든 온갖 부당함을 보상해 줄 길은 손이 묶인 듯 막혀 있었다. 하지만 그녀의 영혼은 그를 보고 싶어 목말라했다. 어찌 그러지 않을 수 있겠는가? 마법의 시대에 어느 공주가, 무리 지어 사는 짐승들 가운데 한 마리가 자신을 택하여 간청하듯 머무는 인간의 눈빛을 띠고 거듭 자기에게 다가오는 것을 보았다면, 길을 가는 동안 무엇을 생각하고, 그 무리가 곁을 지날 때 무엇을 찾으려 하겠는가? 분명 자신을 알아본 그 눈빛, 다시 보면 알아볼 그 눈빛을 찾으려 할 것이다. 우리의 영혼이 지나간 것에 의해 갈망과 변함없음의 결말로 이끌리지 않는다면, 인생은 촛불 빛 장식이나 대낮의 쓰레기만도 못할 것이다. 도로테아가 페어브러더 댁 사람들을 더 잘 알고 싶어 했고 특히 새 교구 목사와 이야기하고 싶어 한 것은 사실이었다. 하지만 윌 래디슬로와 어린 노블 양에 관해 리드게이트가 들려준 이야기를 떠올리며, 윌이 페어브러더 가족을 보러 로윅에 오리라고 기대한 것 또한 사실이었다. 바로 첫 번째 일요일, 교회에 들어서기 전에 그녀는 지난번 그곳에 갔을 때 보았던 그 모습 그대로, 성직자석에 홀로 앉은 그를 보았다. 하지만 막상 안으로 들어서자 그의 모습은 사라지고 없었다.\n\n평일에 교구 목사관의 부인들을 만나러 갔을 때, 그녀는 그들이 윌에 관해 흘릴지도 모를 한마디를 헛되이 기다렸다. 하지만 그녀가 보기에 페어브러더 부인은 그 동네 안팎의 다른 사람들 이야기는 다 하면서도 정작 그 이야기는 하지 않는 듯했다.\n\n\"아마 페어브러더 씨의 미들마치 신도들 가운데 더러는 가끔 로윅까지 그분을 따라오기도 하겠지요. 그렇게 생각지 않으세요?\" 도로테아가 물으면서, 그런 질문에 은밀한 속셈을 품은 자신을 다소 경멸하며 말했다.\n\n\"현명한 사람들이라면 그러겠지요, 캐소본 부인.\" 노부인이 말했다. \"부인께서 제 아들의 설교에 마땅한 가치를 두어 주시니 알겠어요. 제 친정 쪽 할아버지는 훌륭한 성직자셨지만, 아들 아비는 법률 쪽이었답니다. 그럼에도 더없이 모범적이고 정직한 사람이었는데, 그게 바로 우리가 결코 부유해질 수 없었던 까닭이지요. 운명의 여신은 여자라서 변덕스럽다고들 하잖아요. 하지만 때로는 그분도 선량한 여인이 되어 마땅한 이들에게 베푸시는데, 제 아들에게 성직록을 주신 부인이 바로 그런 경우랍니다, 캐소본 부인.\"\n\n페어브러더 부인은 자신의 단정하고 자그마한 웅변 솜씨에 점잖은 만족을 느끼며 다시 뜨개질로 돌아갔으나, 이것은 도로테아가 듣고 싶어 한 말이 아니었다. 가엾은 여인! 그녀는 윌 래디슬로가 아직 미들마치에 있는지조차 알지 못했고, 리드게이트가 아니라면 감히 물어볼 사람이 아무도 없었다. 하지만 지금으로서는 그를 부르러 보내거나 직접 찾아가지 않고서는 리드게이트를 만날 수 없었다. 어쩌면 윌 래디슬로는 캐소본 씨가 자신에게 남긴 그 기이한 금지 조항을 전해 듣고서, 자기와 그녀가 다시 만나지 않는 편이 낫다고 느꼈는지도 몰랐다. 또 어쩌면 다른 이들이 반대할 그럴듯한 이유를 잔뜩 댈 만한 만남을 바라는 것은 그녀가 잘못한 일인지도 몰랐다. 그래도 그 모든 현명한 성찰의 끝에는 \"그래도 나는 그걸 바라\"라는 말이, 숨을 참다가 터져 나오는 흐느낌처럼 자연스레 따라 나왔다. 그리고 그 만남은 정말로 이루어졌으나, 그녀가 전혀 예상치 못한 격식 차린 방식으로였다.\n\n어느 날 아침 열한 시쯤, 도로테아는 자기 내실에 앉아 저택에 딸린 토지의 지도와 다른 서류들을 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 있었는데, 그것들은 자신의 수입과 재정 상태를 스스로 정확히 파악하는 데 도움이 될 것들이었다. 하지만 그녀는 아직 일에 손을 대지 못한 채, 두 손을 무릎에 포갠 채로 앉아, 보리수 가로수 길 너머 멀리 들판을 바라보고 있었다. 햇살 속에서 잎사귀 하나하나가 고요히 쉬고 있었고, 그 낯익은 풍경은 변함이 없었으며, 동기 없는 안락으로 가득한 그녀의 인생 전망을 나타내는 듯했다. 그녀 자신의 활력이 열렬한 행동의 이유를 찾아내지 못한다면, 동기 없는 안락 말이다. 그 시절의 과부 모자는 얼굴을 감싸는 타원형 테두리를 이루며 위로 솟은 관 모양을 하고 있었고, 드레스는 크레이프 천을 최대한 겹쳐 두른 시도와도 같았다. 하지만 이 묵직하고 엄숙한 의복은 오히려 그녀의 얼굴을, 되살아난 생기와 다정하고 묻는 듯한 솔직한 눈동자와 더불어 한층 더 앳되어 보이게 했다.\n\n그녀의 몽상은 탠트립이 들어와, 래디슬로 씨가 아래층에 와 계시는데 너무 이르지 않다면 마님을 뵙고 싶다고 청한다는 말을 전하면서 깨어졌다.\n\n\"만나뵐게요.\" 도로테아가 즉시 일어서며 말했다. \"응접실로 안내해 드려요.\"\n\n응접실은 그녀에게 집 안에서 가장 무난한 방, 결혼 생활의 시련과 가장 적게 결부된 방이었다. 다마스크 천은 온통 흰색과 금색으로 된 목조 부분과 잘 어우러졌고, 키 큰 거울이 두 개 있었으며, 아무것도 올려놓지 않은 탁자들이 있었다. 한마디로 어느 한자리에 앉을 까닭이 다른 자리보다 더 있는 것도 아닌 방이었다. 그 방은 내실 아래층에 있었고, 역시 가로수 길이 내다보이는 활 모양 내닫이창이 있었다. 하지만 프랫이 윌 래디슬로를 그 방으로 안내했을 때 창문은 열려 있었고, 날개 달린 손님 하나가 가구에는 아랑곳없이 이따금 윙윙대며 들락거려, 그 방을 덜 격식 차리고 사람이 살지 않는 듯한 느낌을 덜어 주었다.\n\n\"다시 뵙게 되어 반갑습니다, 나리.\" 블라인드를 매만지느라 머뭇거리며 프랫이 말했다.\n\n\"작별 인사를 하러 왔을 뿐이네, 프랫.\" 윌이 말했다. 그는 캐소본 부인이 부유한 과부가 된 지금, 자기가 그녀 주위를 맴돌기에는 너무 자존심이 강하다는 것을 집사에게까지 알리고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"그 말씀을 들으니 참으로 섭섭합니다, 나리.\" 프랫이 물러가며 말했다. 물론 아무것도 들어서는 안 될 하인으로서, 그는 래디슬로가 아직 모르는 사실을 알고 있었고 나름의 추론을 내려 둔 터였다. 실제로 그는 약혼녀 탠트립이 이렇게 말했을 때 이의를 달지 않았다. \"주인어른이 악마처럼 질투를 하셨다니까. 그것도 아무 이유 없이 말이야. 마님이라면 래디슬로 씨보다는 더 높은 데를 보실 분이지, 안 그러면 내가 마님을 모르는 거고. 캐드월래더 부인 댁 하녀 말로는, 상이 끝나면 마님과 결혼할 어떤 귀족 나리가 오신다더라고.\"\n\n도로테아가 들어오기 전, 윌이 손에 모자를 든 채 서성일 시간은 그리 많지 않았다. 그 만남은 윌이 당황했고 도로테아가 침착했던 로마에서의 첫 만남과는 사뭇 달랐다. 이번에는 그가 비참하면서도 단호했던 반면, 그녀는 감출 수 없는 동요의 상태에 있었다. 문밖에서 그녀는 그토록 고대하던 이 만남이 결국 너무나 어려운 일이라고 느꼈고, 윌이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀에게는 좀처럼 없던 깊은 홍조가 고통스러울 만큼 갑작스레 떠올랐다. 두 사람 다 어찌 된 영문인지 알지 못했으나, 둘 다 입을 열지 못했다. 그녀는 잠시 손을 내밀었고, 이윽고 두 사람은 창가로 가서 앉았는데, 그녀는 한쪽 긴 의자에, 그는 맞은편 다른 의자에 앉았다. 윌은 유난히 불안했다. 그저 그녀가 과부가 되었다는 사실만으로 자기를 맞이하는 태도가 이렇게 달라지다니, 그에게는 도로테아답지 않게 여겨졌다. 그리고 두 사람의 이전 관계에 영향을 줄 만한 다른 사정이라고는 알지 못했다. 다만 그의 상상력이 곧바로 일러 준 대로, 그녀의 친구들이 자신에 대한 의혹으로 그녀의 마음을 물들였을 가능성을 빼고는 말이다.\n\n\"제가 찾아온 것이 너무 주제넘은 일이 아니길 바랍니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"이 동네를 떠나 새 삶을 시작하면서, 부인을 뵙고 작별 인사를 드리지 못하는 것은 견딜 수가 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"주제넘다니요? 결코 아니에요. 만일 저를 보고 싶어 하지 않으셨다면 오히려 야박하다고 여겼을 거예요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. 더없이 진솔하게 말하는 그녀의 습관이 온갖 망설임과 동요를 뚫고 드러난 것이다. \"곧 떠나시나요?\"\n\n\"꽤 곧 떠날 것 같습니다. 도시로 가서 변호사로서 회식에 참석할 생각입니다. 그게 모든 공적인 일을 위한 준비라고들 하니까요. 머지않아 정치적인 일이 산더미처럼 쌓일 텐데, 그중 일부를 해 보려 합니다. 가문도 돈도 없이 스스로 명예로운 지위를 일군 사람들이 더러 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그러면 그건 더욱더 명예로운 일이 되겠지요.\" 도로테아가 열렬하게 말했다. \"게다가 당신은 재능이 그토록 많으시잖아요. 삼촌께 들었어요. 당신이 사람들 앞에서 어찌나 말을 잘하는지, 당신이 말을 그치면 모두가 아쉬워한다고요. 그리고 얼마나 명료하게 설명을 잘하는지도요. 그리고 당신은 모든 사람에게 정의가 실현되기를 바라시잖아요. 저는 정말 기뻐요. 로마에 있을 때 저는 당신이 그저 시와 예술, 그리고 형편이 넉넉한 우리네 삶을 꾸며 주는 것들에만 관심이 있는 줄 알았어요. 하지만 이제 당신이 나머지 세상에 대해서도 생각한다는 걸 알겠어요.\"\n\n말하는 동안 도로테아는 개인적인 당혹감을 잊고 예전의 자신으로 되돌아가 있었다. 그녀는 기쁜 신뢰로 가득한 시선으로 윌을 똑바로 바라보았다.\n\n\"그러면 부인께서는, 제가 몇 년간 떠나 있다가 세상에서 어느 정도 인정받기 전까지는 이곳에 다시 오지 않는 것을 찬성하시는 거군요?\" 윌이 더없는 자존심과, 도로테아에게서 강한 감정의 표현을 끌어내려는 더없는 노력을 어떻게든 양립시키려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 대답하기까지 얼마나 오랜 시간이 흘렀는지 의식하지 못했다. 그녀는 고개를 돌려 창밖의 장미 덤불을 바라보고 있었는데, 그 덤불 속에는 윌이 떠나 있을 그 모든 세월의 여름이 담겨 있는 듯했다. 이것은 분별 있는 처신이 아니었다. 하지만 도로테아는 자기 태도를 따져 볼 생각 따위는 결코 하지 않았다. 그녀는 다만 자신을 윌과 갈라놓는 그 슬픈 필연에 고개 숙일 생각만 했다. 자기 의도에 관한 그의 첫마디는 그녀에게 모든 것을 분명히 해 주는 듯했다. 그녀가 짐작하기로, 그는 자신과 관련된 캐소본 씨의 마지막 처사를 모두 알고 있었고, 그것이 그녀에게 그랬듯 그에게도 똑같은 충격으로 다가왔던 것이다. 그는 그녀에게 우정 이상의 감정을 결코 품은 적이 없었고, 두 사람의 감정에 대한 남편의 모욕이라고 그녀가 느낀 그 일을 정당화할 만한 것을 마음에 둔 적도 없었다. 그리고 그 우정만큼은 그가 여전히 느끼고 있었다. 도로테아의 내면에서는 내적이고 소리 없는 흐느낌이라 할 만한 무언가가 흘렀고, 이윽고 그녀는 맑은 목소리로, 그저 그 유려한 떨림 때문인 양 마지막 말들이 살짝 떨리는 채로 말했다.\n\n\"네, 당신 말씀대로 하는 것이 분명 옳을 거예요. 당신이 자기 가치를 인정받게 되었다는 소식을 들으면 저는 무척 행복할 거예요. 하지만 인내심을 가지셔야 해요. 어쩌면 아주 오랜 세월이 걸릴 테니까요.\"\n\n윌은 그 \"오랜 세월\"이라는 말이 부드러운 떨림과 함께 흘러나왔을 때, 자기가 그녀의 발치에 무너져 내리지 않은 것이 어떻게 가능했는지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그는 훗날, 아마도 그녀의 크레이프 드레스의 그 끔찍한 빛깔과 촉감이야말로 충분한 억제력이었으리라고 말하곤 했다. 그러나 그는 가만히 앉은 채 다만 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"저는 결코 부인의 소식을 듣지 못할 겁니다. 그리고 부인은 저에 관한 것을 모조리 잊으시겠지요.\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 도로테아가 말했다. \"저는 결코 당신을 잊지 않을 거예요. 저는 한번 알게 된 사람을 잊은 적이 없어요. 제 삶은 한 번도 북적인 적이 없었고, 앞으로도 그럴 것 같지 않으니까요. 게다가 로윅에는 기억을 위한 자리가 아주 넉넉하잖아요, 그렇지 않아요?\" 그녀가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 윌이 격정에 차서 외치며, 여전히 손에 모자를 든 채 일어나 대리석 탁자 쪽으로 걸어가더니, 별안간 돌아서서 거기에 등을 기댔다. 피가 그의 얼굴과 목까지 솟구쳐 올라, 그는 거의 화가 난 듯 보였다. 그에게는 두 사람이 마치, 심장은 의식하고 두 눈은 갈망하면서도 서로의 면전에서 천천히 대리석으로 굳어 가는 두 존재처럼 여겨졌다. 하지만 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 쓰라린 결심을 품고 찾아온 이 만남에서, 그가 결국 그녀의 재산을 달라고 청하는 것으로 해석될 수 있는 고백으로 끝맺었다는 말이, 자신에게는 결코 진실이 되어서는 안 되었다. 게다가 그런 고백이 도로테아 자신에게 미칠 영향이 두렵다는 것 또한 사실이었다.\n\n그녀는 그 거리에서 다소 곤혹스러워하며 그를 바라보았다. 자기 말에 무언가 거슬리는 데가 있었던 것은 아닐까 싶었다. 하지만 그 와중에도 그녀의 마음속에는 그가 돈이 궁할 법하다는 것, 그리고 자신이 그를 도울 길이 없다는 생각의 물줄기가 줄곧 흐르고 있었다. 삼촌이 집에 계셨다면 그분을 통해 무언가 할 수 있었을 텐데! 윌이 돈이 궁한 처지인데 정작 그의 몫이 되었어야 할 것을 자신이 가지고 있다는 그 안타까움에 사로잡혀 있던 까닭에, 그녀는 그가 여전히 입을 다문 채 시선을 돌리고 있는 것을 보며 이렇게 말하게 되었다.\n\n\"위층에 걸려 있는 그 세밀화 초상 말인데요. 당신 할머니의 그 아름다운 세밀화 초상을 가지고 싶지 않으세요? 당신이 그것을 갖기를 바라신다면, 제가 간직하고 있는 것은 옳지 않은 듯해요. 그것은 당신과 놀랍도록 닮았거든요.\"\n\n\"참 친절하시군요.\" 윌이 짜증스레 말했다. \"아니요, 저는 그것에 마음 두지 않습니다. 제 자신의 초상을 갖는 것은 그리 위안이 되지 않거든요. 다른 사람들이 그걸 갖고 싶어 한다면 더 큰 위안이 되겠지만요.\"\n\n\"당신이 할머니의 기억을 소중히 간직하고 싶어 하실 줄 알았어요. 저는 그저ㅡ\" 도로테아는 잠시 말을 멈추었다. 줄리아 고모의 내력 쪽으로는 가지 말라고 그녀의 상상력이 별안간 경고했던 것이다. \"그 세밀화를 가문의 기념물로 분명 갖고 싶어 하시리라 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"제가 그걸 왜 갖겠습니까, 다른 건 아무것도 없는 마당에! 짐이라곤 여행 가방 하나뿐인 사람은 기념물을 머릿속에 간직하는 수밖에요.\"\n\n윌은 닥치는 대로 말했다. 그는 그저 짜증을 토해 내고 있었을 뿐이다. 바로 그 순간에 할머니의 초상화를 받으라는 권유를 받는 것은 다소 지나치게 부아가 치미는 일이었다. 하지만 도로테아의 감정에 그의 말은 독특한 가시처럼 박혔다. 그녀는 일어서서 분개와 함께 거만함이 살짝 깃든 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"우리 둘 중에는 당신이 훨씬 더 행복한 사람이네요, 래디슬로 씨, 아무것도 없으니 말이에요.\"\n\n윌은 흠칫했다. 말이야 어떻든, 그 어조는 마치 내쫓는 것처럼 들렸다. 그는 기대고 있던 자세를 풀고 그녀 쪽으로 조금 다가갔다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤으나, 기이하게 묻는 듯한 진지함을 띤 채였다. 무언가가 두 사람의 마음을 서로 멀리 떼어 놓고 있었고, 각자는 상대의 마음속에 무엇이 있는지 추측하는 데에 내맡겨져 있었다. 윌은 도로테아가 보유한 재산에 대해 자신에게 상속권 같은 것이 있다고는 정말이지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었으므로, 그녀의 지금 심정을 이해하려면 사연을 처음부터 들어야 했을 것이다.\n\n\"지금까지는 아무것도 없는 것을 불행이라 느낀 적이 없었습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 가난이, 우리가 가장 아끼는 것으로부터 우리를 갈라놓는다면, 그것은 나병만큼이나 고약한 것일 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n그 말은 도로테아의 가슴을 찔렀고, 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨렸다. 그녀는 슬픈 동류의식이 깃든 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"슬픔은 참으로 여러 모습으로 찾아오지요. 이 년 전만 해도 저는 그런 것을 몰랐어요. 그러니까, 시련이 뜻밖의 방식으로 찾아와서 우리 손을 묶어 버리고, 말하고 싶을 때 우리를 침묵하게 만든다는 것을요. 저는 여자들이 자기 삶을 더 잘 빚어내지 못하고 더 나은 일을 하지 못한다고 조금은 경멸하곤 했어요. 저는 제 멋대로 하는 것을 무척 좋아했지만, 이제는 거의 그만두었답니다.\" 그녀가 장난스레 미소 지으며 끝맺었다.\n\n\"저는 제 멋대로 하는 것을 그만두지는 못했지만, 좀처럼 그렇게 할 수가 없습니다.\" 윌이 말했다. 그는 그녀에게서 두 야드쯤 떨어져 서서, 서로 모순되는 욕망과 결심으로 마음이 가득 차 있었다. 그녀가 자신을 사랑한다는 틀림없는 증거를 바라면서도, 그런 증거가 자신을 몰아넣을 처지를 두려워하는 마음이었다. \"가장 간절히 바라는 것이, 견딜 수 없는 조건들에 둘러싸여 있을 수도 있는 법이니까요.\"\n\n바로 그 순간 프랫이 들어와 말했다. \"제임스 체텀 경께서 서재에 와 계십니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"제임스 경께 이리로 들어오시라고 해요.\" 도로테아가 즉시 말했다. 마치 똑같은 전기 충격이 그녀와 윌을 동시에 관통한 것 같았다. 두 사람은 각자 자존심에 차서 저항감을 느꼈고, 제임스 경이 들어오기를 기다리는 동안 누구도 상대를 쳐다보지 않았다.\n\n도로테아와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 그는 래디슬로에게는 가능한 한 가볍게 고개를 숙였고, 래디슬로도 그 가벼움을 똑같이 되갚았다. 그러고는 도로테아 쪽으로 가며 말했다.\n\n\"작별 인사를 드려야겠습니다, 캐소본 부인. 아마도 꽤 오랫동안 못 뵐 것 같습니다.\"\n\n도로테아는 손을 내밀며 진심 어린 작별 인사를 건넸다. 제임스 경이 윌을 깎아내리고 그에게 무례하게 굴고 있다는 느낌은 그녀의 결의와 품위를 불러일으켰다. 그녀의 태도에는 조금의 혼란도 없었다. 그리고 윌이 방을 나가자, 그녀는 더없이 침착하게 자제력을 발휘하며 제임스 경을 바라보고 \"실리아는 어때요?\"라고 물었기에, 그는 마치 자기를 언짢게 한 일이라곤 아무것도 없었던 것처럼 처신할 수밖에 없었다. 게다가 달리 처신해 본들 무슨 소용이 있겠는가? 실로 제임스 경은 도로테아를 래디슬로와 연인 사이가 될 수 있는 관계로 결부시키는 것을 생각만으로도 너무나 싫어하여 몸서리쳤기에, 그 불쾌한 가능성을 인정하는 꼴이 될 불쾌감의 외적 표출은 스스로도 피하고 싶었던 것이다. 누군가 그에게 왜 그렇게 몸서리치느냐고 물었다면, 그가 처음에는 \"저 래디슬로 녀석이라니!\" 이상으로 더 충실하거나 정확한 말을 했을지 나로서는 장담할 수 없다. 다만 곰곰이 생각해 보면, 도로테아가 윌과 결혼하면 벌칙을 받도록 막아 놓은 캐소본 씨의 유언 추가 조항만으로도, 두 사람 사이의 그 어떤 관계에도 부적절함의 그늘을 드리우기에 충분하다고 그는 주장했을지도 모른다. 그의 혐오감은, 자기가 끼어들 수 없다는 것을 느꼈기에 더욱 강했다.\n\n하지만 제임스 경은 스스로도 짐작 못 한 방식으로 하나의 힘이었다. 바로 그 순간에 들어섬으로써, 그는 윌의 자존심을 도로테아에게서 그를 떼어 놓는 밀어내는 힘으로 만든 가장 강력한 이유들의 화신이 되었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER LV.\n\n그녀에게 결점이 있는가? 그대도 그런 결점을 지녔으면 좋으련만. 그것들은 더없이 순수한 포도주의 향기로운 발효즙이요, 아니, 거듭나게 하는 불길이라 할까, 짙고 검은 원소를 태워 태양을 위한 수정의 길로 바꾸어 놓은 그런 불길.\n\n젊음이 희망의 계절이라면, 그것은 흔히 우리의 윗사람들이 우리에게 희망을 거는 한에서만 그러하다. 자신의 감정과 이별, 결심이 같은 종류의 마지막 것이라고 여기기에 젊음만큼 쉬운 시기는 없으니 말이다. 모든 위기는 단지 새롭다는 이유만으로 마지막인 듯 보인다. 페루에서 가장 오래 산 주민들도 지진에 동요하기를 멈추지 않는다고들 하지만, 그들은 아마도 각각의 충격 너머를 내다보며 앞으로도 그런 일이 얼마든지 더 일어나리라 헤아릴 것이다.\n\n도러시아는 긴 속눈썹이 가득한 두 눈이 눈물의 비를 쏟은 뒤에도 갓 피어난 시계꽃처럼 더럽혀지지 않고 지치지 않은 채 세상을 바라보는, 여전히 그런 젊음의 시기에 있었으니, 그날 아침 윌 래디슬로와의 이별이 두 사람의 사적인 관계의 끝인 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그는 알 수 없는 세월의 먼 곳으로 떠나가고 있었고, 혹여 돌아온다 해도 다른 사람이 되어 있을 터였다. 그의 마음의 실제 상태는—부유한 여인을 좇는 궁색한 모험가 노릇을 하리라는 어떤 의심에도 미리 거짓말을 하리라는 그 도도한 결심은—그녀의 상상 밖에 완전히 놓여 있었고, 그녀는 그의 모든 행동을, 캐소본 씨의 추가 유언이 자신에게 그렇듯 그에게도 두 사람 사이의 그 어떤 적극적인 우정에 대한 거칠고 잔인한 금령으로 여겨지는 듯하다는 가정으로 충분히 쉽게 해석해 버렸다. 서로 이야기하며 다른 누구도 들으려 하지 않을 말을 나누던 그들의 젊은 기쁨은 영원히 끝나 버렸고, 과거의 보물이 되어 버렸다. 바로 그런 이유로 그녀는 내적인 거리낌 없이 그 기억에 머물렀다. 그 둘도 없는 행복마저 죽어 버렸고, 그 그늘진 고요한 방에서 그녀는 스스로도 놀랄 만큼 격렬한 슬픔을 토해 낼 수 있었다. 그녀는 처음으로 벽에서 그 세밀화를 떼어 내 자기 앞에 두었으니, 너무 가혹하게 판단되었던 여인과 자신의 마음과 판단이 옹호하는 그 손자를 한데 섞어 그려 보는 것이 좋았다. 여인의 다정함에서 기쁨을 누려 본 사람이라면, 그녀가 그 작은 타원형 그림을 손바닥에 받쳐 들고 거기에 잠자리를 만들어 주듯 뺨을 기댄 것을, 마치 그렇게 하면 부당한 단죄를 받은 이들을 달랠 수 있기라도 하다는 듯한 그 행동을 어찌 비난거리라 여길 수 있겠는가? 그녀는 그때 몰랐다. 그것이 잠에서 깨어나기 전의 꿈처럼, 날개에 아침의 빛깔을 띠고 잠시 자신을 찾아왔던 사랑이었음을—거역할 수 없는 낮의 흠 없는 엄격함이 그 형상을 내쫓을 때 그녀가 흐느끼며 작별을 고하던 상대가 바로 사랑이었음을. 그녀는 다만 자신의 운명에 무언가 돌이킬 수 없이 어긋나고 사라져 버린 것이 있다고 느꼈을 뿐이고, 그래서 미래에 대한 그녀의 생각은 더 쉽사리 결심의 형태로 빚어졌다. 다가올 삶을 짓고자 하는 열렬한 영혼은 자기 자신의 환상을 실현하는 일에 스스로를 내맡기기 쉬운 법이다.\n\n어느 날 그녀가 하룻밤 묵으며 아기 목욕시키는 것을 보겠다는 약속을 지키러 프레시트로 갔을 때, 마침 목사가 낚시 여행을 떠나고 없던 터라 커들래더 부인이 만찬에 왔다. 따뜻한 저녁이었고, 열린 창문에서 고운 옛 잔디밭이 백합이 핀 연못과 나무가 잘 심긴 둔덕을 향해 비탈져 내려가는 그 쾌적한 응접실에서조차 더위가 충분히 심해서, 흰 모슬린 옷에 가벼운 곱슬머리를 한 실리아는 검은 옷에 꼭 맞는 모자를 쓴 도도가 얼마나 답답할까 측은하게 생각할 정도였다. 하지만 그것은 아기를 둘러싼 몇몇 일들이 끝나고 그녀의 마음에 여유가 생긴 뒤의 일이었다. 그녀는 자리에 앉아 한동안 부채를 들고 있다가, 그 조용하고 낮은 목소리로 말했다—\n\n\"도도, 그 모자 좀 벗어요. 그 옷 때문에 분명 몸이 안 좋을 거예요.\"\n\n\"이 모자에 너무 익숙해져서—일종의 껍데기처럼 되어 버렸어.\" 도러시아가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"벗으면 오히려 헐벗고 드러난 것 같은 느낌이 들어.\"\n\n\"모자 벗은 모습을 봐야겠어요. 그것 때문에 우리 모두 덥잖아요.\" 실리아는 부채를 내려놓고 도러시아에게 다가가며 말했다. 흰 모슬린을 입은 이 자그마한 부인이 한층 위엄 있는 언니에게서 미망인의 모자를 풀어 의자 위로 던지는 모습은 예쁜 그림이었다. 짙은 갈색 머리의 타래와 땋은 머리가 막 풀려난 순간, 제임스 경이 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 풀려난 머리를 보더니 흡족한 어조로 \"아!\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"내가 한 거예요, 제임스.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"도도가 상복을 그렇게 노예처럼 입을 필요는 없잖아요. 친구들 사이에서까지 저 모자를 쓰고 있을 필요는 없어요.\"\n\n\"실리아야,\" 채텀 부인이 말했다. \"미망인은 적어도 일 년은 상복을 입어야 한단다.\"\n\n\"일 년이 끝나기 전에 재혼한다면 그럴 필요 없지요.\" 커들래더 부인이 말했는데, 그녀는 자신의 좋은 벗인 미망인 부인을 놀라게 하는 데 다소간 즐거움을 느꼈다. 제임스 경은 언짢아져서 몸을 앞으로 숙여 실리아의 몰타산 강아지와 장난을 쳤다.\n\n\"그런 일은 아주 드물기를 바라요.\" 채텀 부인이 그런 일을 막으려는 듯한 어조로 말했다. \"우리 친구 중에 그런 식으로 처신한 사람은 비버 부인뿐이었는데, 그녀가 그러자 그린셀 경이 무척 괴로워했지요. 그 부인의 첫 남편은 흠잡을 데가 많았던 사람이라 더욱 놀라운 일이었어요. 그리고 그녀는 그 일로 호되게 벌을 받았지요. 비버 대위가 그녀의 머리채를 잡고 끌고 다니며 장전한 권총을 들이댔다더군요.\"\n\n\"오, 그야 잘못된 남자를 골랐다면 그렇지요!\" 단연 심술궂은 기분에 빠져 있던 커들래더 부인이 말했다. \"그러면 첫 번째든 두 번째든 결혼은 언제나 나쁜 거예요. 다른 장점이 없는 남편이라면 먼저였다는 건 보잘것없는 추천장이지요. 저는 시원찮은 첫 남편보다는 좋은 두 번째 남편을 택하겠어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그 영리한 혀에 끌려다니는군요.\" 채텀 부인이 말했다. \"우리 목사님께 무슨 일이 생긴다 해도, 당신이야말로 성급하게 재혼할 마지막 여인일 텐데요.\"\n\n\"오, 저는 맹세 같은 건 안 해요. 어쩌면 꼭 필요한 절약일 수도 있으니까요. 재혼하는 건 율법에 어긋나지 않는 거겠지요. 그렇지 않다면 우리가 기독교인이 아니라 차라리 힌두교도여도 마찬가지일 테고요. 물론 여자가 잘못된 남자를 받아들이면 그 결과를 감수해야 하고, 그런 짓을 두 번이나 하는 사람은 그런 운명을 당해도 싸지요. 하지만 가문과 미모와 용기를 다 갖춘 남자와 결혼할 수 있다면—빠를수록 좋고말고요.\"\n\n\"우리 대화의 주제가 아주 잘못 골라진 것 같군요.\" 제임스 경이 역겨운 표정으로 말했다. \"주제를 바꾸는 게 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"저를 위해서라면 그러지 마세요, 제임스 경.\" 도러시아가 훌륭한 혼처에 관한 어떤 에두른 언급들로부터 자신을 해방시킬 기회를 놓치지 않겠다고 결심하며 말했다. \"저를 두고 하시는 말씀이라면, 분명히 말씀드리는데 제게 재혼만큼 무관심하고 남의 일 같은 문제는 없어요. 여자들이 여우 사냥을 다닌다는 이야기와 다를 바 없어요. 그게 그들에게 훌륭한 일인지 아닌지는 몰라도, 저는 그들을 따라가지 않을 테니까요. 커들래더 부인이 다른 어떤 주제에서나 마찬가지로 그 주제로도 마음껏 즐기시게 두세요.\"\n\n\"캐소본 부인,\" 채텀 부인이 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다. \"제가 비버 부인 이야기를 했다고 해서 거기에 당신을 빗댄 것이 있었다고는 생각지 않으시길 바라요. 그저 제게 떠오른 한 가지 예였을 뿐이에요. 그녀는 그린셀 경의 의붓딸이었지요. 경은 두 번째 부인으로 테버로이 부인과 결혼했고요. 당신을 빗댈 만한 것은 전혀 없었어요.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"아무도 그 주제를 고른 게 아니에요. 다 도도의 모자에서 나온 거지요. 커들래더 부인은 그저 아주 옳은 말씀을 하셨을 뿐이에요. 여자가 미망인의 모자를 쓴 채로 결혼할 수는 없잖아요, 제임스.\"\n\n\"쉿, 얘야!\" 커들래더 부인이 말했다. \"다시는 거슬리게 하지 않을게요. 디도나 제노비아 얘기도 꺼내지 않겠어요. 그런데 우리 대체 무슨 얘기를 해야 할까요? 저로 말하자면 인간 본성에 관한 논의는 사양하겠어요. 그건 목사 아내들의 본성이니까요.\"\n\n저녁 늦게 커들래더 부인이 돌아간 뒤, 실리아가 도러시아에게 조용히 말했다. \"정말이지, 도도, 모자를 벗으니 여러모로 다시 언니다워졌어요. 마음에 거슬리는 말이 나오면 예전에 그러던 것처럼 똑 부러지게 말하더군요. 그런데 언니가 잘못이라고 생각한 게 제임스인지 커들래더 부인인지 잘 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"둘 다 아니야.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"제임스는 나를 배려해서 한 말이지만, 내가 커들래더 부인의 말을 마음에 둔다고 생각한 건 잘못이었지. 그 부인이든 다른 누구든 추천하는 가문 좋고 잘생긴 남자를 받아들이라고 강요하는 법이 있다면, 그때나 신경 쓰일 거야.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 언니, 언니가 언젠가 결혼한다면 가문과 미모를 갖춘 게 훨씬 나을 거예요.\" 실리아는 캐소본 씨가 그런 자질을 풍부히 타고나지 못했다는 것을, 그리고 도러시아에게 미리 주의를 주는 게 좋겠다는 것을 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"걱정 마, 키티. 난 내 삶에 대해 전혀 다른 생각을 품고 있어. 다시는 결혼하지 않을 거야.\" 도러시아가 동생의 턱을 만지며 너그러운 애정으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. 실리아는 아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었고, 도러시아는 작별 인사를 하러 온 참이었다.\n\n\"정말—정말로요?\" 실리아가 말했다. \"아무도—그가 정말로 더없이 훌륭한 사람이라 해도요?\"\n\n도러시아가 천천히 고개를 저었다. \"아무하고도. 난 멋진 계획들이 있어. 넓은 땅을 얻어 물을 빼내고, 작은 마을을 하나 만들어서, 거기서는 모두가 일하고 모든 일이 훌륭하게 이루어지면 좋겠어. 거기 사람들을 한 명 한 명 다 알고 그들의 친구가 되고 싶어. 가스 씨와 큰 의논을 할 작정이야. 그분은 내가 알고 싶은 걸 거의 다 알려 줄 수 있거든.\"\n\n\"그럼 계획이 있으면 언니는 행복해질 거예요, 도도?\" 실리아가 말했다. \"어쩌면 우리 아서도 자라면 계획을 좋아하게 될지 몰라요. 그러면 그 애가 언니를 도울 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n그날 밤 제임스 경은 도러시아가 정말로 그 누구와도 결혼하지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹었으며, 예전에 늘 그러던 것처럼 \"온갖 계획들\"에 몰두하려 한다는 이야기를 전해 들었다. 제임스 경은 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그의 은밀한 감정으로는 여자의 재혼에는 무언가 혐오스러운 데가 있었고, 그 어떤 혼처라도 도러시아에게는 그것을 일종의 신성모독처럼 느끼는 마음을 막지 못할 것이었다. 그는 세상이 그런 감정을, 특히 스물한 살 여인에 관해서는 터무니없다고 여길 것임을 알고 있었다. \"세상\"의 관행이란 젊은 미망인의 재혼을 기정사실이며 아마도 임박한 일로 다루는 것이고, 미망인이 그에 맞게 처신하면 의미심장하게 미소 짓는 것이었으니 말이다. 하지만 도러시아가 정녕 자신의 고독을 택하기로 한다면, 그는 그 결심이 그녀에게 잘 어울리리라 느꼈다.\n\n# CHAPTER LVI.\n\n태어나고 길러진 자, 그 얼마나 행복한가!\n. . . . . . .\n\n이 사람은 출세하려는 희망의 노예 같은 속박에서도\n\n추락을 두려워하는 마음에서도 자유로우니\n땅의 주인은 아닐지언정 제 자신의 주인이요\n\n아무것도 가진 것 없으나 모든 것을 가졌도다.”\n— 헨리 워튼 경\n\n캘럽 가스의 식견에 대한 도로테아의 신뢰는 그가 자신의 오두막들을 인정한다는 말을 들으면서 시작되었는데, 프레시트에 머무는 동안 빠르게 자라났다. 제임스 경이 그녀를 권하여 자신과 캘럽과 함께 두 사유지를 둘러보는 승마에 나서게 했기 때문이었다. 캘럽 또한 그녀의 찬탄에 충분히 화답하여, 캐소본 부인은 여자로서는 보기 드문 사업적 두뇌를 지녔다고 아내에게 말했다. 다만 캘럽이 말하는 \"사업\"이란 돈을 주고받는 거래가 아니라 노동을 능숙하게 적용하는 일을 뜻했다는 점을 기억해 두어야 한다.\n\"정말 보기 드물지!\" 캘럽이 거듭 말했다. \"내가 어렸을 때 곧잘 혼자 생각하던 바로 그런 말을 그분이 하더란 말이오. ‘가스 씨, 나는 늙도록 산다면 큰 땅 한 뙈기를 개량하고 좋은 오두막을 많이 지었다는 느낌을 가지고 싶어요. 그런 일은 하는 동안에도 건강한 종류의 일이고, 다 하고 나면 사람들이 그 덕을 보게 되니까요.’ 바로 그 말 그대로였소. 그분은 그런 식으로 사물의 속을 들여다본단 말이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 여자다웠으면 좋겠어요.\" 가스 부인이 말했다. 캐소본 부인이 종속의 참된 원칙을 지키지 않을지도 모른다고 반쯤 의심하면서였다.\n\"오, 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요!\" 캘럽이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"당신도 그분이 말하는 걸 듣고 싶어 할 거요, 수전. 그토록 쉬운 말로 이야기하는데, 목소리는 음악 같다오. 세상에! 「메시아」의 한 구절이 떠오를 정도지. ‘그러자 곧 수많은 하늘의 무리가 나타나 하느님을 찬미하며 이르되.’ 귀를 만족시키는 가락이 깃들어 있단 말이오.\"\n\n캘럽은 음악을 무척 좋아해서, 형편이 닿을 때면 손이 미치는 곳에서 열리는 오라토리오를 들으러 갔다. 그러고 돌아오면 이 장엄한 가락의 구조물에 깊은 경외심을 품고서, 바닥을 바라보며 명상에 잠겨 앉아 내뻗은 두 손에 말로 다 할 수 없는 갖가지 언어를 쏟아붓곤 했다.\n이렇게 두 사람 사이에 좋은 이해가 자리 잡았으니, 도로테아가 가스 씨에게 로윅 저택에 딸린 세 농장과 수많은 소작지에 관련된 일을 맡아 달라고 부탁한 것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 실제로 두 사람 몫의 일거리를 얻게 되리라던 그의 기대는 빠르게 채워지고 있었다. 그의 말마따나, \"일은 일을 낳는다.\" 그리고 바로 그 무렵 새끼를 치기 시작한 사업의 한 형태가 철도 건설이었다. 한 노선이 로윅 교구를 가로질러 놓일 예정이었는데, 그곳에서는 그때까지 소들이 놀라움에 깨질 일 없는 평화 속에서 풀을 뜯고 있었다. 그리하여 철도 체제의 갓난아기 같은 분투가 캘럽 가스의 일에 끼어들었고, 그에게 소중한 두 사람과 관련하여 이 이야기의 흐름을 결정짓게 되었다. 해저 철도에는 나름의 어려움이 있을 것이다. 하지만 바다 밑바닥은 측정 가능할 뿐 아니라 감정적이기까지 한 손해 배상 청구를 내거는 갖가지 지주들 사이에서 나뉘어 있지는 않다. 미들마치가 속한 백호구에서 철도는 개혁 법안이나 임박한 콜레라의 공포만큼이나 흥미진진한 화제였고, 이 문제에 가장 단호한 견해를 지닌 이들은 여자들과 지주들이었다. 늙은 여자든 젊은 여자든 증기로 여행하는 것을 외람되고 위험한 짓이라 여겼고, 무슨 일이 있어도 철도 객차에 올라타지 않겠다고 말하며 그에 반대했다. 한편 지주들은 솔로몬 페더스톤 씨가 메들리코트 경과 다르듯 저마다 논리는 천차만별이었으나, 땅을 팔 때에는—그것이 인류의 적에게든 매입할 의무를 진 회사에게든—이 해로운 매개체들로 하여금 인류에게 해를 끼칠 허가의 대가로 지주들에게 아주 높은 값을 치르게 해야 한다는 의견에는 한결같이 일치했다.\n\n하지만 둘 다 제 땅을 가지고 있던 솔로몬 씨나 월 부인처럼 머리가 더딘 이들은 이 결론에 다다르는 데 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 그들의 마음은 큰 목초지를 둘로 갈라 세모꼴 조각들로 만들어 놓는다는 것이 \"말도 안 되는\" 일이라는 생생한 상념에 멈춰 있었고, 건널목 다리니 높은 보상금이니 하는 것은 까마득하고 믿기 어려운 일이었다.\n\n\"근목초지를 철도가 가로지르면 암소들이 죄다 새끼를 유산할 거예요, 오라버니.\" 월 부인이 깊은 우수에 잠긴 어조로 말했다. \"그 암말도, 새끼를 뱄다면 그러지 않을까 싶고요. 과부의 재산이 삽질에 깎여 나가는데 법이 아무 말도 안 한다면, 그건 딱한 노릇이지요. 시작했다 하면 좌우로 함부로 갈라 대는 걸 누가 막겠어요? 다들 알다시피 나는 싸울 줄을 모르니까요.\"\n\n\"제일 좋은 방법은 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 그자들이 염탐하고 측량하러 오면 누구 하나를 붙여서 귀에 따끔하게 한마디 해 쫓아 보내는 거지.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"브래싱 쪽에서는 사람들이 그랬다더군, 내가 알기로는. 진실을 알고 보면, 한 길로만 가야 한다는 건 다 핑계라고. 다른 교구로 가서 갈라 대라고 해. 그리고 한 무리 깡패들을 데려와 작물을 짓밟게 해 놓고 그 보상이랍시고 무슨 돈을 준다는 것도 나는 못 믿겠어. 회사 호주머니란 게 어디 있냐고?\"\n\n\"피터 오라버니는, 하느님이 용서해 주시기를, 회사에서 돈을 받아 냈지요.\" 월 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 그건 망간 때문이었잖아요. 좌우로 사람을 산산조각 낼 철도 때문이 아니라.\"\n\n\"하긴, 이런 말은 할 수 있지, 제인.\" 솔로몬 씨가 조심스러운 태도로 목소리를 낮추며 결론을 내렸다. \"이러나저러나 그자들이 올 수밖에 없다면, 우리가 그 바퀴에 살을 더 많이 끼워 넣을수록 계속 가게 해 달라고 우리한테 더 많은 값을 치를 거란 말이야.\"\n\n솔로몬 씨의 이런 추론은 어쩌면 그가 생각한 것만큼 철저하지는 못했으니, 그의 잔꾀가 철도의 진로에 미치는 영향이란 외교관의 잔꾀가 태양계 전체의 한기나 카타르에 미치는 영향과 엇비슷한 것이었다. 그러나 그는 의심을 부추기는, 지극히 외교적인 방식으로 자신의 견해를 실행에 옮기기 시작했다. 그가 가진 로윅 쪽 땅은 마을에서 가장 멀리 떨어진 곳이었고, 일꾼들의 집은 외딴 오두막이거나, 아니면 프릭이라 불리는 작은 마을에 모여 있었다. 그곳에서는 물레방앗간과 몇몇 채석장이 느릿느릿하고 어깨 무거운 산업의 작은 중심을 이루고 있었다.\n\n철도가 무엇인지 정확한 관념이 전혀 없는 까닭에, 프릭의 여론은 철도에 반대했다. 그 풀밭 한구석의 인간 정신에는 미지의 것을 찬탄하는 흔한 경향이 없었고, 오히려 그것이 가난한 사람에게 불리할 공산이 크다고 여겨, 의심만이 그에 대한 유일하게 현명한 태도라고 보았기 때문이다. 개혁의 소문조차 프릭에서는 아직 어떤 천년왕국의 기대도 불러일으키지 못했다. 거기에는 하이럼 포드의 돼지를 살찌울 공짜 곡물이라든가, \"저울대 주막\"에 공짜로 맥주를 빚어 줄 술집 주인이 생긴다든가, 이웃한 세 농부가 겨울 동안 품삯을 올려 주겠다고 제안한다든가 하는 명확한 약속이 전혀 없었기 때문이다. 이런 종류의 뚜렷한 이득이 약속에 담겨 있지 않으니, 개혁은 행상꾼의 허풍과 다를 바 없어 보였고, 그것은 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구나 불신해야 한다는 신호였다. 프릭의 남자들은 못 먹고 사는 처지는 아니었고, 광신에 빠지기보다는 근육질의 강인한 의심에 기우는 편이었다. 자신들이 하늘로부터 특별히 보살핌을 받는다고 믿기보다는, 하늘 자체가 오히려 자신들을 속여 넘기려는 쪽이라고 여겼으니—이는 날씨에서 능히 관찰할 수 있는 성향이었다.\n\n이렇듯 프릭의 정신은 솔로몬 페더스톤 씨가 작업을 걸기에 꼭 알맞은 종류였다. 그는 같은 종류의 관념을 더 풍성하게 지니고 있었으며, 더 잘 먹고 더 한가로이 무르익은, 하늘과 땅에 대한 의심을 품고 있었기 때문이다. 솔로몬은 당시 도로 감독관이었는데, 발걸음 느린 조랑말을 타고 곧잘 프릭 쪽으로 순찰을 돌며 그곳에서 돌을 캐는 일꾼들을 들여다보았다. 그러다 알 수 없는 신중함을 띠고 멈추어 서곤 했는데, 그 모습을 보면 단지 움직일 마음이 없어서가 아니라 머무를 다른 이유라도 있는 줄로 오해할 만했다. 진행 중인 어떤 일이든 한참을 들여다본 뒤, 그는 눈을 약간 들어 지평선을 바라보았다. 마침내 고삐를 흔들고 채찍으로 말을 건드려 천천히 앞으로 나아가게 했다. 솔로몬 씨에 비하면 시계의 시침마저 빠른 편이었으니, 그는 느릿느릿해도 괜찮다는 흐뭇한 여유를 누리고 있었다. 그는 길에서 마주치는 울타리꾼이나 도랑 파는 일꾼마다 붙잡고, 조심스럽고 어렴풋이 속셈을 품은 잡담을 나누는 버릇이 있었으며, 전에 들은 적 있는 소식이라도 기꺼이 귀 기울여 들었다. 그것을 절반쯤 믿지 않는다는 점에서 모든 이야기꾼보다 자신이 유리하다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 그러나 어느 날 그는 짐마차꾼 하이럼 포드와 대화를 나누게 되었는데, 이번에는 자신이 직접 정보를 제공했다. 그는 하이럼이 막대기며 기구를 들고 여기저기 염탐하는 자들을 보았는지 알고 싶어 했다. 그자들은 자기들을 철도 사람들이라 부르지만, 그게 뭔지, 무슨 짓을 하려는 건지 알 길이 없었다. 적어도 그자들이 내세우는 명목은 로윅 교구를 갈가리 토막 내겠다는 것이었다.\n\n\"아니, 그럼 한 군데서 다른 데로 옮길 수가 없잖여.\" 하이럼이 제 짐마차와 말들을 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 그렇지.\" 솔로몬 씨가 말했다. \"더구나 이 교구 같은 좋은 땅을 토막 낸다니! 티프턴으로나 가라지, 내 말은. 하지만 그 속에 뭐가 있는지는 알 수 없어. 교통이니 뭐니 앞세우지만, 결국 길게 보면 땅과 가난한 사람한테 해를 끼치자는 거지.\"\n\n\"아니, 그놈들은 런던 놈들이렷다.\" 하이럼이 말했다. 그는 런던을 시골에 적대적인 중심지쯤으로 어렴풋이 여기고 있었다.\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고. 그리고 내가 들은 바로는 브래싱 쪽 어디서는, 그놈들이 염탐하고 있을 때 사람들이 달려들어, 들고 다니는 들여다보는 기구를 부숴 버리고 쫓아내서, 다시는 올 엄두를 못 내게 만들었다더군.\"\n\n\"거 참 재밌었겄네, 틀림없이.\" 하이럼이 말했다. 형편상 그가 누릴 재미란 무척 제한된 것이었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 나 같으면 그놈들한테 손대진 않겠어.\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"하지만 누구는 이 나라가 좋은 시절은 다 보냈다고 하지. 그 징조가 바로, 이런 놈들이 좌우로 짓밟고 다니며 땅을 철도로 토막 내려고 온통 들끓는 거라는 거야. 그것도 다 큰 교통이 작은 교통을 집어삼키게 해서, 땅에 짐말 한 마리 남지 않고, 채찍 휘두를 일도 안 남게 하려는 수작이지.\"\n\n\"그 지경 만들기 전에, 그놈들 귓전에다 내 채찍을 한번 휘둘러 줄 테여.\" 하이럼이 말하는 동안, 솔로몬 씨는 고삐를 흔들며 앞으로 나아갔다.\n\n쐐기풀 씨앗은 땅 파 묻을 것도 없다. 철도가 이 시골을 망쳐 놓으리라는 이야기는 \"저울대 주막\"에서만이 아니라 건초밭에서도 오갔다. 그곳에서는 일손들이 한데 모이는 까닭에, 농촌의 한 해 동안 좀처럼 갖기 힘든 이야기 나눌 기회가 생기곤 했다.\n\n페어브라더 씨와 메리 가스가 만나, 그녀가 그에게 프레드 빈시를 향한 자신의 마음을 고백한 그 만남이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 아침, 그녀의 아버지는 일이 생겨 프릭 방향에 있는 요드렐의 농장으로 가게 되었다. 로윅 저택에 딸린 외곽의 한 땅뙈기를 측량하고 값을 매기는 일이었는데, 캘럽은 그것을 도로테아에게 유리하게 처분할 수 있으리라 내다보고 있었다.(고백하건대 그의 편향은 철도 회사들로부터 가능한 한 좋은 조건을 얻어 내자는 쪽이었다.) 그는 요드렐의 농장에 이륜마차를 세워 두고, 조수와 측량용 사슬을 들고 일할 곳으로 걸어가다가, 수준기를 맞추고 있던 회사 측 직원 일행과 마주쳤다. 잠시 잡담을 나눈 뒤 그는 그들을 떠나며, 이따가 자신이 측량하려는 곳에서 다시 마주치게 될 거라고 일러두었다. 가벼운 비가 지나간 뒤 잿빛으로 흐린, 그런 아침들 가운데 하나였다. 정오 무렵이 되면 구름이 조금 갈라지고 오솔길과 산울타리를 따라 흙 내음이 향긋해져 황홀해지는 그런 아침이었다.\n\n말을 타고 오솔길을 따라 오던 프레드 빈시에게도 그 향기는 더 향긋했을 것이다. 다만 그가 무엇을 해야 할지 그려 보려다 번번이 실패하는 헛된 노력에 마음이 시달리고 있지만 않았다면 말이다. 한쪽에서는 아버지가 곧장 성직에 들어서기를 바라고 있었고, 다른 한쪽에서는 메리가 그가 성직에 들어선다면 그를 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓고 있었으며, 일상의 노동 세계는 자본도 없고 대체로 기술도 없는 젊은 신사를 조금도 절실히 필요로 하지 않았다. 프레드의 기질에는 이것이 한층 더 견디기 힘든 일이었으니, 아버지는 그가 더는 반항하지 않는다는 데 만족하여 그에게 기분 좋게 대했고, 그레이하운드 몇 마리를 보러 가라며 이 즐거운 승마 길에 그를 내보냈기 때문이다. 무엇을 할지 정한다 해도, 그것을 아버지에게 알려야 하는 과제가 남아 있었다. 하지만 먼저 와야 할 그 정하는 일이야말로 더 어려운 과제였음을 인정해야 한다. 신사다우면서 동시에 돈벌이가 되고, 게다가 특별한 지식 없이도 따를 수 있는, 그런 세속의 생업이 이 세상에(\"임명직\"을 얻어 줄 만한 친구도 없는) 젊은이에게 도대체 어디 있단 말인가? 이런 심정으로 프릭 곁의 오솔길을 따라 말을 몰며, 로윅 목사관 쪽으로 돌아가 메리를 찾아볼 엄두를 내야 할지 곰곰이 생각하느라 걸음을 늦추던 그는, 산울타리 너머로 이 들판에서 저 들판까지 내다볼 수 있었다. 갑자기 어떤 소리가 그의 주의를 끌었다. 왼편 들판 저쪽 끝에서, 작업복 차림의 남자 예닐곱이 손에 건초용 갈퀴를 들고, 자기들을 마주하고 선 네 명의 철도 직원을 향해 공세적으로 다가가는 것이 보였고, 그동안 캘럽 가스와 그의 조수는 위협받는 무리에 합류하려고 들판을 가로질러 서둘러 달려가고 있었다. 프레드는 출입문을 찾느라 몇 순간 지체하는 바람에, 작업복 무리가 미처 그 자리에 다다르기 전에 말을 달려가지는 못했다. 정오의 맥주를 들이켜고 나서 건초 뒤집는 일이 그리 다급하지 않았던 그 작업복 무리는, 갈퀴를 휘둘러 외투 입은 사람들을 앞으로 몰아대고 있었다. 한편 캘럽 가스의 조수인 열일곱 살 소년은 캘럽의 지시로 수준기를 집어 든 참이었는데, 얻어맞고 쓰러져 속수무책으로 누워 있는 듯 보였다. 외투 입은 사람들은 달리기에 유리했고, 프레드는 작업복 무리 앞으로 끼어들어 느닷없이 돌격함으로써 그들의 추격을 혼란에 빠뜨려 후퇴를 엄호했다. \"이 빌어먹을 멍청이들아, 무슨 짓들이냐?\" 프레드가 갈라진 무리를 지그재그로 뒤쫓으며 채찍을 좌우로 휘두르며 소리쳤다. \"치안판사 앞에서 너희 하나하나를 다 고발해 버리겠다. 너희가 그 애를 때려눕혀서, 내가 알기로는 죽여 버린 거야. 조심하지 않으면 너희 모두 다음 순회재판 때 교수형을 당하게 될 거다.\" 프레드는 이렇게 말했는데, 나중에 자신이 내뱉은 표현들을 떠올리며 한바탕 유쾌하게 웃었다.\n\n일꾼들은 출입문을 통해 자기네 건초밭으로 쫓겨 들어갔고, 프레드는 말을 멈춰 세웠다. 그때 하이럼 포드가 안전하게 시비를 걸 만한 거리에 자신이 있음을 알아채고는 돌아서서, 자신은 호메로스풍인 줄 모르는 도전의 말을 외쳤다.\n\n\"넌 겁쟁이여, 그려. 말에서 내려와 봐, 젊은 도련님, 그럼 내 한판 붙어 주지, 암. 말이랑 채찍 없이는 덤빌 엄두도 못 내잖여. 단숨에 숨통을 끊어 놔 줄 텐디, 내가.\"\n\n\"잠깐만 기다려라. 곧 돌아와서 원한다면 너희 모두와 차례차례 한판씩 붙어 주마.\" 프레드가 말했다. 그는 자신이 끔찍이도 아끼는 형제들과 권투로 맞붙을 자신이 있었다. 하지만 지금 당장은 캘럽과 쓰러진 소년에게로 서둘러 돌아가고 싶었다.\n\n소년은 발목을 삐어 무척 아파했으나, 그 이상 다친 데는 없었다. 프레드는 소년을 말에 태워 요드렐의 농장으로 가서 거기서 돌봄을 받게 했다.\n\n\"말은 마구간에 들여놓게 하고, 측량 기사들한테는 와서 도구를 가져가도 된다고 전하렴.\" 프레드가 말했다. \"이제 땅은 비었으니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 캘럽이 말했다. \"여기 부서진 게 있어. 오늘은 그만둬야 할 테고, 그게 차라리 낫지. 자, 톰, 그 물건들은 말 위 네 앞에 얹어 가져가거라. 네가 오는 걸 보면 그 사람들도 돌아갈 게다.\"\n\n\"제가 마침 알맞은 때에 여기 있게 되어 다행입니다, 가스 씨.\" 톰이 말을 타고 떠나자 프레드가 말했다. \"기병대가 제때 나타나지 않았으면 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 모르죠.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 운이 좋았어.\" 캘럽이 다소 멍하니 말했다. 그러면서 일이 중단된 순간에 자신이 작업하던 자리 쪽을 바라보았다. \"하지만—제기랄—사람들이 어리석으니 이런 일이 생기는 거지—덕분에 오늘 일을 못 하게 됐어. 측량용 사슬을 도와줄 누군가가 없으면 일을 못 한단 말이야. 하지만 뭐!\" 그는 프레드가 있다는 것도 잊은 듯 짜증스러운 표정으로 그 자리를 향해 움직이려다가, 문득 돌아서서 빠르게 말했다. \"오늘 자네는 할 일이 있나, 젊은이?\"\n\n\"없습니다, 가스 씨. 기꺼이 도와 드리겠어요—그래도 될까요?\" 프레드가 말했다. 메리의 아버지를 돕는 것이 곧 메리에게 구애하는 일이라는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"그럼, 허리 굽히고 땀 흘리는 걸 마다하지 않아야 하네.\"\n\n\"저는 아무것도 마다하지 않습니다. 다만 먼저 가서, 돌아서서 저한테 도전한 그 덩치 큰 녀석과 한판 붙고 싶군요. 그놈한테 좋은 교훈이 될 겁니다. 오 분도 안 걸릴 거예요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리!\" 캘럽이 더없이 단호한 어조로 말했다. \"그 사람들한테는 내가 직접 가서 이야기하겠네. 다 무지에서 나온 짓이야. 누가 거짓말을 한 거지. 그 가엾은 멍청이들은 더 잘 알지를 못해.\"\n\n\"그럼 저도 함께 가겠습니다.\" 프레드가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 자네는 여기 있게. 자네의 젊은 혈기는 필요 없어. 나 혼자 알아서 할 수 있네.\"\n\n캘럽은 힘센 사람이었고, 남에게 상처 주는 것에 대한 두려움과 일장 연설을 해야 하는 것에 대한 두려움 말고는 두려움을 거의 몰랐다. 하지만 이 순간 그는 짤막한 일장 연설을 해 보는 것이 자신의 의무라고 느꼈다. 그에게는 두드러진 혼합이 있었으니—언제나 고된 노동을 해 온 사람이었던 데서 비롯된—일꾼들에 대한 엄격한 관념과 그들을 향한 현실적인 관용이 뒤섞여 있었다. 좋은 하루치 일을 해내는 것, 그것도 잘 해내는 것을 그는 그들의 복지의 일부라고 여겼으니, 그것이 자신의 행복에서도 가장 큰 부분을 차지하는 까닭이었다. 동시에 그는 그들과의 동류 의식도 강하게 지니고 있었다. 그가 일꾼들에게 다가갔을 때 그들은 다시 일하러 가지 않고, 서로에게 어깨를 돌린 채 두세 야드씩 떨어져 서 있는, 그 농촌 특유의 무리 짓는 모양새를 하고 있었다. 그들은 다소 뚱하니 캘럽을 바라보았다. 캘럽은 한 손은 주머니에 넣고 다른 손은 조끼 단추 사이에 찔러 넣은 채 빠르게 걸어와, 그들 사이에 멈춰 서며 여느 때처럼 온화한 기색을 띠었다.\n\n\"아니, 이보게들, 이게 어찌 된 일인가?\" 그는 여느 때처럼 짤막한 어구로 입을 떼었다. 그 어구들은 그 자신에게는 깊은 뜻을 품은 것으로 여겨졌으니, 그 아래에는 마치 겨우 수면 위로 머리를 내미는 식물의 무성한 뿌리처럼 많은 생각이 깔려 있었기 때문이다. \"어쩌다 이런 실수를 저질렀나? 누가 자네들한테 거짓말을 한 게야. 자네들은 저 위에 있는 저 사람들이 못된 짓을 하려는 줄로 알았겠지.\"\n\n\"어이!\" 저마다 굼뜬 정도에 따라 간격을 두고 던지는 대답이었다.\n\n\"무슨 소리! 그런 거 아닐세! 저 사람들은 철도가 어느 쪽으로 날지 살펴보는 거라네. 자, 이보게들, 자네들이 철도를 막을 수는 없어. 좋든 싫든 철도는 놓일 걸세. 그리고 자네들이 거기 맞서 싸우면, 자네들만 곤경에 빠질 게야. 법이 저 사람들한테 이 땅에 들어올 허가를 준 거란 말이야. 땅 주인도 거기 대해 할 말이 없고, 자네들이 저 사람들한테 손댔다간 순경하고 블레익슬리 치안판사, 게다가 수갑과 미들마치 감옥하고 상대해야 할 걸세. 그리고 누가 자네들을 고발하기라도 하면 지금 당장에라도 그렇게 될 수 있어.\"\n\n캘럽은 여기서 말을 멈추었다. 어쩌면 제아무리 위대한 웅변가라도 이 상황에 이보다 더 나은 멈춤이나 더 나은 이미지를 고를 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"하지만 이보게, 자네들이 나쁜 뜻이 있었던 건 아니야. 누가 자네들한테 철도가 나쁜 거라고 했겠지. 그건 거짓말이야. 여기저기, 이것저것에 조금 해를 끼칠 수도 있지. 하늘의 태양도 그러니까. 하지만 철도는 좋은 거라네.\"\n\n\"어이! 큰사람들이 돈 벌어 먹기에나 좋은 거지.\" 늙은 티머시 쿠퍼가 말했다. 그는 다른 사람들이 한바탕 소동을 벌이러 가 있는 동안 뒤에 남아 건초를 뒤집고 있던 참이었다. \"내 젊었을 적부텀 별 게 다 일어나는 걸 봤지—전쟁이며 평화며, 운하며, 옛 조지 왕이며, 섭정이며, 새 조지 왕이며, 새 이름 단 새 양반이며—그래도 가난한 사람한테는 죄다 매한가지였어. 운하가 가난한 사람한테 뭘 해 줬간디? 운하가 그자한테 고기 한 점, 베이컨 한 점 갖다준 적 없고, 제 뱃속 곯아 가며 아껴 모으지 않는 한 따로 떼어 둘 품삯도 못 줬어. 내 젊었을 적부텀 가난한 사람한테는 형편이 점점 더 나빠지기만 했어. 철도도 똑같을 거여. 가난한 사람을 더 뒤처지게 둘 뿐이지. 하지만 거기 끼어드는 놈들은 바보여, 그래서 여기 이 사람들한테도 그리 말했고. 이건 큰사람들의 세상이여, 이건. 그런디 당신은 큰사람들 편이여, 가스 나리, 당신은.\"\n\n티머시는 그 시절에도 드물게 남아 있던 부류에 속하는, 깡마르고 억센 늙은 일꾼이었다. 양말 발치에 저축을 모아 두고, 외딴 오두막에 살며, 그 어떤 웅변에도 흔들리지 않는 사람이었으니, 그에게는 봉건적 정신이라곤 거의 없고, 마치 이성의 시대니 인권이니 하는 것을 전혀 모르고 살았던 것이나 다름없을 만큼 믿는 것도 거의 없었다. 캘럽은, 어둠의 시대에 기적의 도움도 없이, 부인할 수 없는 진실을 — 고된 감정의 과정을 거쳐 알아낸 진실을 — 거머쥐고 있는 시골뜨기들과 따져 보려는 사람이라면 누구나 아는 곤경에 빠져 있었다. 그들은 자신들이 느끼지도 못하는 사회적 이익에 관한 당신의 깔끔하게 다듬어진 논거 위로, 그 진실을 거인의 몽둥이처럼 내리꽂을 수 있는 것이다. 캘럽에게는 입에 발린 상투어가 없었고, 설령 그것을 쓰기로 마음먹을 수 있었다 해도 마찬가지였다. 그는 그런 온갖 곤경을, 자기 \"일\"을 충실히 해내는 것 말고 다른 어떤 방식으로도 마주해 본 적이 없었다. 그는 대답했다—\n\n\"자네가 나를 좋게 여기지 않는다면, 팀, 괜찮아. 그건 지금 중요한 게 아니니까. 가난한 사람한테 형편이 나쁠 수도 있지—실제로 나쁘고. 하지만 나는 여기 이 사람들이 자기들 형편을 더 나쁘게 만들 짓은 안 했으면 하는 거야. 짐말이 무거운 짐을 졌다 해도, 그 짐을 길가 구덩이에 내던져 버려서야 도움이 안 되지. 그 짐엔 그놈들 먹이도 얼마쯤 들어 있는데 말이야.\"\n\n\"우린 그저 좀 재미 삼아 그런 거여.\" 하이럼이 말했다. 그는 결과를 깨닫기 시작하고 있었다. \"그게 다였어유.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 다시는 끼어들지 않겠다고 약속하게. 그럼 아무도 자네들을 고발하지 못하게 내가 살펴봄세.\"\n\n\"난 끼어든 적도 없은께, 약속할 까닭도 없어.\" 티머시가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 하지만 나머지는 하게. 자, 나도 오늘 자네들 누구 못지않게 고되게 일하는 중이라 시간을 많이 낼 수가 없어. 순경 부르는 일 없이 얌전히 있겠다고 말하게.\"\n\n\"어이, 우린 안 끼어들겄어—그자들이야 우리 알 바 아니니께 멋대로 하라지\"—이것이 캘럽이 받아 낸 약속의 형태였다. 그러고 나서 그는 프레드에게로 서둘러 돌아갔다. 프레드는 그를 따라와 출입문에서 그를 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n두 사람은 일에 착수했고, 프레드는 힘차게 도왔다. 그의 기분은 한껏 들떠 있었으니, 산울타리 아래 축축한 흙에서 한바탕 미끄러져 완벽하던 여름 바지가 더럽혀지는 것마저 진심으로 즐거웠다. 그를 들뜨게 한 것은 성공적인 돌격이었을까, 아니면 메리의 아버지를 돕는다는 만족감이었을까? 그 이상의 무언가였다. 그날 아침의 우연한 사건들은 좌절되어 있던 그의 상상력이, 여러모로 매력적인 일거리 하나를 자신을 위해 그려 내도록 거들어 주었다. 가스 씨의 마음속 어떤 섬유들도, 이제 프레드 앞에 모습을 드러낸 바로 그 목표를 향해 옛 진동을 되살린 것은 아닐까 싶다. 효과를 내는 우연이란 기름과 부싯깃이 있는 곳에 닿는 불씨에 지나지 않으니, 프레드에게는 언제나 철도가 바로 그 필요한 불씨를 가져다준 것으로 보였다. 하지만 두 사람은 일에 말이 필요할 때 말고는 잠자코 일을 계속했다. 마침내 일을 끝마치고 걸어 나오면서, 가스 씨가 말했다—\n\n\"이런 일 하는 데 젊은이가 학사일 필요는 없지, 안 그런가, 프레드?\"\n\n\"학사가 될 생각을 하기 전에 이 일에 손댔더라면 좋았을걸요.\" 프레드가 말했다. 그는 잠시 멈추었다가, 좀 더 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다. \"제가 가스 씨 일을 배우기에는 너무 나이가 많을까요?\"\n\n\"내 일은 여러 가지란다, 얘야.\" 가스 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내가 아는 것 가운데 상당수는 경험으로만 얻을 수 있는 거지. 책에서 배우듯 외워서 익힐 수 있는 게 아니야. 하지만 자네는 아직 기초를 닦을 만큼 젊어.\" 캘럽은 마지막 문장을 힘주어 말했으나, 어떤 망설임 속에서 멈추었다. 그는 요사이 프레드가 성직에 들어서기로 마음을 굳혔다는 인상을 받고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"제가 한번 해 본다면 이 일에서 뭔가 잘할 수 있으리라고 보시는 거죠?\" 프레드가 좀 더 간절하게 말했다.\n\n\"그건 경우에 따라 다르지.\" 캘럽이 고개를 한쪽으로 돌리고 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. 무언가 깊이 종교적인 말을 하고 있다고 스스로 느끼는 사람의 태도였다. \"자네는 두 가지를 확실히 해야 하네. 첫째, 자네 일을 사랑해야 하고, 늘 그 가장자리 너머만 바라보며 놀 시간이 오기만 기다려서는 안 돼. 그리고 다른 하나는, 자네 일을 부끄러워해서 다른 일을 하는 게 더 명예로울 거라고 여겨서는 안 된다는 거야. 자기 일에, 그리고 그것을 잘하는 법을 배우는 데 자부심을 가져야 하고, 늘 이렇다 저렇다—이런저런 일이 내게 주어졌더라면 뭔가 해냈을 텐데, 하고 말해서는 안 돼. 사람이 무엇이든 간에—나는 그런 사람한테는 두 푼도 안 주겠어\"—여기서 캘럽의 입가가 쓰디쓴 빛을 띠었고, 그는 손가락을 탁 튕겼다—\"그가 총리든 짚 이엉장이든, 자기가 맡은 일을 잘 해내지 못한다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"성직자가 되어서는 제가 그렇게 할 수 있으리라는 느낌이 도무지 들지 않아요.\" 프레드가 말했다. 논의를 한 걸음 진전시킬 작정이었다.\n\n\"그럼 그건 그만두게, 얘야.\" 캘럽이 불쑥 말했다. \"안 그러면 자네는 결코 마음 편할 수 없을 게야. 아니면, 설령 마음이 편하다 해도, 자네는 형편없는 인간이 되고 말 테지.\"\n\n\"메리가 그 일에 대해 생각하는 것도 거의 그와 같아요.\" 프레드가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"제가 메리에게 어떤 마음인지 가스 씨도 아셔야 할 것 같아요. 제가 늘 그 누구보다 메리를 더 사랑해 왔고, 또 메리를 사랑하는 것만큼 누군가를 사랑하는 일은 결코 없으리라는 게 가스 씨를 불쾌하게 하지 않기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n프레드가 말하는 동안 캘럽의 얼굴 표정이 눈에 띄게 부드러워지고 있었다. 하지만 그는 엄숙하고 느릿하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다—\n\n\"자네가 메리의 행복을 자네 손에 맡아 두려 한다면, 일이 더 심각해지는 거야, 프레드.\"\n\n\"저도 압니다, 가스 씨.\" 프레드가 간절히 말했다. \"그리고 저는 메리를 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠어요. 메리는 제가 성직에 들어가면 결코 저와 결혼하지 않겠다고 합니다. 그리고 메리에 대한 모든 희망을 잃는다면 저는 세상에서 가장 비참한 인간이 될 거예요. 정말이지, 제가 조금이라도 적성에 맞는 다른 직업이나 일을—무엇이든 얻을 수만 있다면, 저는 열심히 일해서 가스 씨의 좋은 평가를 받을 만한 사람이 되겠어요. 저는 야외에서 하는 일을 하고 싶어요. 땅과 가축에 대해서는 이미 꽤 많이 알고요. 아시다시피 저는 한때, 이런 걸로 저를 좀 어리석다 여기시겠지만, 제 땅을 가지게 될 거라고 믿었거든요. 분명 그런 종류의 지식이라면 저한테 쉽게 익혀질 거예요. 특히 어떤 식으로든 가스 씨 밑에서 일할 수 있다면요.\"\n\n\"천천히, 얘야.\" 캘럽이 말했다. 눈앞에 \"수전\"의 모습이 떠올랐던 것이다. \"이 모든 걸 자네 아버님께는 뭐라고 말씀드렸나?\"\n\n\"아직 아무 말도 못 했습니다. 하지만 말씀드려야겠죠. 다만 성직에 들어서는 대신 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 알고 나서 말씀드리려고 기다리는 중이에요. 아버지를 실망시켜 드려 정말 죄송하지만, 사람이 스물네 살이 되었으면 자기 일은 스스로 판단하도록 허락받아야 하지 않겠어요? 열다섯 살에 제가 어떻게 지금 무엇을 하는 게 옳을지 알 수 있었겠습니까? 제 교육이 잘못된 거였어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이걸 들어 보게, 프레드.\" 캘럽이 말했다. \"자네 메리가 자네를 좋아한다고, 또 자네를 받아들이리라고 확신하나?\"\n\n\"메리가 직접 말하는 걸 금지했기 때문에, 페어브라더 씨께 메리와 이야기를 좀 해 달라고 부탁드렸어요—달리 어쩔 도리를 몰랐거든요.\" 프레드가 변명하듯 말했다. \"그리고 그분 말씀이, 제가 명예로운 자리에—그러니까 성직 밖에서요—설 수만 있다면 충분히 희망을 가질 만하다고 하셨어요. 제가 저 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 해 놓은 게 없으면서 가스 씨를 귀찮게 하고 메리에 대한 제 바람을 들이미는 게 주제넘은 짓이라 여기시겠죠. 물론 저는 무슨 권리도 없습니다—사실, 이미 가스 씨께 진 빚이 있는데, 그건 제가 돈으로 갚을 수 있게 된 뒤라 해도 결코 다 갚지 못할 빚이지요.\"\n\n\"아니, 얘야, 자네한테는 권리가 있어.\" 캘럽이 목소리에 깊은 감정을 담아 말했다. \"젊은이들한테는 자기를 앞으로 이끌어 달라고 늙은이들한테 청할 권리가 언제나 있는 법이지. 나도 한때는 젊었고, 변변한 도움 없이 헤쳐 나가야 했어. 하지만 그저 동류 의식에서 나온 것이라도, 도움이 있었더라면 반가웠을 거야. 하지만 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠네. 내일 사무소로 아홉 시에 오게. 사무소로, 알겠지.\"\n\n가스 씨는 수전과 의논하지 않고는 중대한 결정을 내리는 법이 없었지만, 집에 다다르기도 전에 이미 결심을 굳혔음을 인정해야겠다. 다른 사람들이라면 단호하거나 고집스러울 수많은 문제에 대해, 그는 세상에서 가장 다루기 쉬운 사람이었다. 그는 어떤 고기를 고를지조차 모르는 사람이라, 만약 수전이 절약을 위해 방 네 칸짜리 오두막에서 살아야 한다고 말했다면, 그는 자초지종도 캐묻지 않고 \"그럼 가지\" 하고 말했을 것이다. 하지만 캘럽의 감정과 판단이 강하게 발언하는 문제에서는, 그가 지배자였다. 나무라는 데서는 온화하고 소심했음에도, 그가 마음먹는 예외적인 경우에는 절대적이라는 것을 주위 사람들 모두가 알았다. 사실 그는 다른 누군가를 위해서가 아니고는 결코 절대적이 되기를 택하지 않았다. 백 가지 가운데 아흔아홉 가지는 가스 부인이 결정했으나, 백 번째에 이르러서는, 자기 자신의 원칙을 실행하여 스스로를 종속시켜야 하는 유난히 어려운 과제를 떠맡아야 하리라는 것을 부인은 종종 깨닫곤 했다.\n\n\"내 생각대로 일이 돌아갔구려, 수전.\" 그날 저녁 둘만 마주 앉았을 때 캘럽이 말했다. 그는 프레드가 자기 일을 함께하게 된 계기가 된 모험은 이미 이야기했으나, 그 뒤에 이어진 결과는 말하지 않고 있었다. \"아이들이 정말로 서로 좋아한다오—프레드와 메리 말이오.\"\n\n가스 부인은 일감을 무릎에 내려놓고, 꿰뚫어 보는 눈으로 남편을 불안하게 응시했다.\n\n\"일을 다 마치고 나서, 프레드가 나한테 죄다 털어놓더군. 그 애는 성직자가 되는 걸 견딜 수 없어 하고, 메리는 그 애가 성직자가 되면 받아들이지 않겠다고 하고. 그 애는 내 밑에서 일하며 사업에 마음을 쏟고 싶어 해. 그래서 나는 그 애를 받아들여 어엿한 사람으로 만들어 주기로 결심했소.\"\n\n\"캘럽!\" 가스 부인이 체념 섞인 놀라움이 묻어나는 깊은 콘트랄토 음성으로 말했다.\n\n\"좋은 일이오.\" 가스 씨가 의자 등받이에 단단히 몸을 기대고 팔걸이를 거머쥐며 말했다. \"그 애 때문에 애를 먹기야 하겠지만, 그래도 끝까지 해낼 수 있을 것 같소. 그 애는 메리를 사랑하고, 좋은 여자를 향한 참된 사랑은 대단한 거라오, 수전. 그건 거친 녀석도 여럿 빚어 놓는 법이지.\"\n\n\"메리가 이 일에 대해 당신한테 말한 적이 있나요?\" 가스 부인이 말했다. 자기가 이 일을 남에게서 전해 들어야 한다는 데 속으로 조금 서운한 마음이 들었다.\n\n\"한마디도 없었소. 언젠가 프레드에 대해 그 애한테 물어본 적은 있지. 약간 주의를 주었고. 하지만 그 애는 게으르고 제멋대로인 남자와는 절대 결혼하지 않겠다고 다짐하더군—그 뒤로는 아무 말도. 그런데 듣자 하니, 메리가 직접 말하는 걸 금지하니까 프레드가 페어브라더 씨를 시켜서 메리한테 이야기하게 했고, 페어브라더 씨가 알아낸 바로는, 메리가 프레드를 좋아하긴 하는데 그 애가 성직자가 되어서는 안 된다고 한다는 거요. 프레드의 마음은 메리한테 단단히 가 있어, 그건 내 눈에 보여. 그래서 그 애를 좋게 보게 되었다오—우리는 늘 그 애를 좋아했잖소, 수전.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 메리한테는 안된 일이에요.\" 가스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"왜—안된 일이라니?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면, 캘럽, 메리는 프레드 빈시 같은 사람 스무 명의 값어치가 있는 남자를 얻을 수도 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 캘럽이 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"저는 페어브라더 씨가 메리한테 마음을 두고 있고 청혼할 작정이었다고 굳게 믿어요. 하지만 물론, 이제 프레드가 그분을 사자로 부려 먹었으니, 그 더 나은 가능성은 끝이 난 거죠.\" 가스 부인의 말투에는 준엄한 정확함이 깃들어 있었다. 그녀는 속상하고 실망스러웠으나, 쓸데없는 말은 삼가기로 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n캘럽은 갖가지 감정이 뒤얽힌 채 잠시 말이 없었다. 그는 바닥을 바라보며, 마음속 어떤 논쟁에 맞춰 고개와 손을 움직였다. 마침내 그가 말했다—\n\n\"그렇게 됐더라면 나도 무척 자랑스럽고 행복했을 거요, 수전. 당신을 위해서도 기뻤을 테고. 나는 늘 당신 집안 식구들이 당신과 어울리는 수준에 미치지 못했다고 느껴 왔소. 그런데도 당신은, 내가 보잘것없는 사람인데도 나를 택해 주었지.\"\n\n\"저는 제가 아는 가장 훌륭하고 가장 영리한 남자를 택한 거예요.\" 가스 부인이 말했다. 그 기준에 못 미치는 사람이라면 자기는 결코 사랑하지 않았으리라고 확신하며 한 말이었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 다른 사람들은 당신이 더 나은 선택을 할 수도 있었다고 여겼을지 모르지. 하지만 그랬다면 나한테는 더 못한 일이었을 거요. 그리고 바로 그 점이 프레드 일을 두고 내 마음에 사무친다오. 그 애는 바탕이 착하고, 제대로 길을 잡아 주면 일을 해낼 만큼 영리해. 게다가 내 딸을 무엇보다 사랑하고 떠받들고, 메리도 그 애가 어떤 사람이 되느냐에 따라 일종의 약속을 해 준 거지. 내 말하건대, 그 젊은이의 영혼이 내 손안에 있소. 그러니 나는 그 애를 위해 할 수 있는 최선을 다할 거요, 하느님 도와주소서! 그게 내 의무라오, 수전.\"\n\n가스 부인은 눈물을 잘 흘리는 사람이 아니었으나, 남편이 말을 마치기도 전에 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 그녀의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 그것은 갖가지 감정의 압박에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 그 안에는 깊은 애정과 약간의 속상함이 뒤섞여 있었다. 그녀는 얼른 눈물을 닦으며 말했다—\n\n\"당신 말고는, 그런 식으로 일부러 제 걱정거리를 늘리는 걸 의무라고 여길 사람은 거의 없을 거예요, 캘럽.\"\n\n\"다른 사람들이 어떻게 여길지는 아무 상관 없소. 내 안에는 분명한 느낌이 있고, 나는 그걸 따를 거요. 그리고 당신 마음도 나와 함께해 주기를 바라오, 수전. 가엾은 메리한테 모든 일을 되도록 가볍게 해 주는 데 말이오.\"\n\n캘럽은 의자에 등을 기대고, 불안한 호소를 담아 아내 쪽을 바라보았다. 그녀는 일어나 그에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"하느님이 당신을 축복하시기를, 캘럽! 우리 아이들은 좋은 아버지를 두었어요.\"\n\n하지만 그녀는 방을 나가, 억눌렀던 말들을 만회하기라도 하듯 한바탕 실컷 울었다. 그녀는 남편의 행동이 오해받으리라고 확신했고, 프레드에 대해서는 이성적이고 희망을 품지 않았다. 어느 쪽에 더 멀리 내다보는 안목이 있었던 것으로 판명될까—그녀의 이성일까, 캘럽의 뜨거운 너그러움일까?\n\n이튿날 아침 프레드가 사무소로 갔을 때, 그가 미처 예상하지 못한 시험을 거쳐야 했다.\n\n\"자, 프레드.\" 캘럽이 말했다. \"자네는 책상에서 하는 일을 좀 맡게 될 거야. 나도 늘 글 쓰는 일을 꽤 많이 해 왔지만, 도와줄 사람 없이는 안 되거든. 그리고 나는 자네가 회계를 이해하고 가치 평가를 머릿속에 익혔으면 하니까, 다른 사무원은 두지 않을 작정이야. 그러니 자네가 단단히 매달려야 해. 글씨와 셈은 어떤가?\"\n\n프레드는 어색하게 가슴이 철렁하는 것을 느꼈다. 책상 일은 생각도 못 했던 것이다. 하지만 그는 단단히 마음먹은 상태였고, 물러설 생각이 없었다. \"셈은 두렵지 않습니다, 가스 씨. 셈은 늘 쉽게 익혀졌어요. 제 글씨는 가스 씨도 아실 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"어디 보세.\" 캘럽이 펜을 집어 들고 꼼꼼히 살펴본 뒤, 잉크를 잘 묻혀 줄 그은 종이 한 장과 함께 프레드에게 건네며 말했다. \"그 가치 평가서를, 끝에 숫자까지 넣어서 한두 줄 베껴 보게.\"\n\n그 시절에는 신사라면 알아보기 쉽게, 혹은 조금이라도 사무원에게 어울릴 만한 필체로 글씨를 쓰는 것이 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일이라는 견해가 있었다. 프레드는 요구받은 줄들을, 당대의 어느 자작이나 주교 못지않게 신사다운 필체로 써 내려갔다. 모음들은 죄다 똑같이 생겼고, 자음들은 위로 올라갔느냐 아래로 내려갔느냐로나 겨우 구별되었으며, 획들은 번진 듯 뭉툭했고 글자들은 줄을 지키기를 경멸했다—한마디로, 글쓴이가 무슨 뜻으로 썼는지 미리 알고 있어야만 풀어낼 수 있는, 그런 유서 깊은 종류의 필적이었다.\n\n캘럽이 들여다보는 사이, 그의 얼굴에는 점점 짙어지는 침울함이 드러났다. 그러나 프레드가 종이를 건네자, 그는 으르렁거리는 듯한 소리를 내며 손등으로 종이를 격하게 탁 쳤다. 이런 형편없는 솜씨는 캘럽의 온화함을 죄다 흩어 버렸다.\n\n\"제기랄!\" 그가 으르렁거리듯 외쳤다. \"한 사람의 교육에 몇백 파운드, 몇백 파운드씩 들 수도 있는 나라에서, 그래 봐야 자네 같은 사람을 내놓는다니!\" 그러더니 좀 더 애처로운 어조로, 안경을 밀어 올리고 그 가엾은 필경사를 바라보며 말했다. \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서, 프레드, 나는 이건 참아 줄 수가 없어!\"\n\n\"제가 어떻게 하면 될까요, 가스 씨?\" 프레드가 말했다. 그의 기분은 바닥까지 가라앉아 있었으니, 자기 글씨에 대한 평가 때문만이 아니라, 자신이 사무소 사무원과 동급으로 매겨질 처지라는 상상 때문이기도 했다.\n\n\"어떻게 하다니? 아니, 글자를 제대로 만들고 줄을 지키는 법을 배워야지. 아무도 알아볼 수 없는데 글을 쓴들 무슨 소용인가?\" 캘럽이 그 형편없는 솜씨에 온통 정신이 팔린 채 기운차게 물었다. \"세상에 일거리가 그렇게도 없어서 온 나라에 수수께끼나 보내야 한단 말인가? 하지만 이게 다 사람들 키워진 방식이지. 어떤 이들이 나한테 보내는 편지는, 수전이 풀어 주지 않았다면 끝없이 시간을 잡아먹었을 거야. 정말 역겨운 일이지.\" 여기서 캘럽은 종이를 멀찍이 내던졌다.\n\n그 순간 사무소를 들여다본 낯선 이가 있었다면, 분개한 사업가와, 분해서 입술을 깨무느라 그 금발의 얼굴빛이 군데군데 얼룩져 가는 잘생긴 젊은이 사이에 어떤 연극이 벌어지고 있는지 궁금해했을 것이다. 프레드는 여러 생각과 씨름하고 있었다. 가스 씨가 면담 초반에는 어찌나 친절하고 격려해 주었던지, 감사와 희망이 한껏 드높았던 만큼 그 추락도 그에 비례했다. 그는 책상 일은 생각해 본 적이 없었다—사실, 대다수 젊은 신사들이 그렇듯, 그는 불쾌한 구석이 없는 일거리를 원했던 것이다. 만약 그가 로윅으로 메리를 만나러 가서, 그녀의 아버지 밑에서 일하기로 했다고 말하리라 스스로에게 분명히 약속해 두지 않았다면 어떤 결과가 빚어졌을지 나는 알 수 없다. 그 일에서만큼은 자신을 실망시키고 싶지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"정말 죄송합니다.\" 그가 그러모을 수 있는 말이라곤 그것뿐이었다. 하지만 가스 씨는 이미 누그러지고 있었다.\n\n\"되는대로 최선을 다해 보세, 프레드.\" 그가 평소의 차분한 어조로 돌아와 입을 열었다. \"누구나 글씨는 배울 수 있어. 나도 혼자 익혔으니까. 의지를 가지고 매달려서, 낮 동안만으로 모자라면 밤에도 앉아서 하게. 우리 참고 해 보세, 얘야. 자네가 배우는 동안은 칼럼이 한동안 장부를 계속 맡으면 돼. 하지만 이제 나는 가 봐야겠네.\" 캘럽이 일어서며 말했다. \"우리 합의는 자네 아버님께 알려 드리게. 자네가 글씨를 쓸 수 있게 되면, 알다시피, 칼럼한테 줄 봉급을 내가 아끼게 되는 거야. 그래서 첫해에는 자네한테 80파운드를 줄 수 있고, 그 뒤로는 더 줄 수 있어.\"\n\n프레드가 부모에게 그 불가피한 고백을 했을 때, 두 사람에게 그것이 미친 상대적인 효과는 그의 기억 속에 아주 깊이 박힌 하나의 놀라움이었다. 그는 가스 씨의 사무소에서 곧장 창고로 갔는데, 아버지에게 표할 수 있는 가장 정중한 태도는 이 고통스러운 통보를 되도록 진중하고 격식 있게 하는 것이라고 옳게 여겼기 때문이다. 게다가, 그 면담이 아버지의 가장 진중한 시간—언제나 창고의 개인 방에서 보내는 그 시간—에 이루어진다면, 그 결정이 최종적인 것으로 더 확실히 이해될 터였다.\n\n프레드는 곧장 본론으로 들어가, 자신이 한 일과 하기로 결심한 일을 간략히 밝히고, 마지막에는 자신이 아버지에게 실망을 안겨 드리는 원인이 된 것을 유감스러워하며, 자기 자신의 부족함에 그 탓을 돌렸다. 그 유감은 진심에서 우러난 것이었고, 프레드에게 강하고 소박한 말들을 불어넣어 주었다.\n\n빈시 씨는 깊은 놀라움 속에서, 탄성 한 번 내뱉지 않고 들었다. 성마른 그의 기질에서 그 침묵은 예사롭지 않은 격정의 표시였다. 그날 아침 그는 장사 일로 기분이 좋지 않았던 터라, 듣는 동안 그의 입가에 어린 옅은 쓴 빛이 점점 짙어졌다. 프레드가 말을 마치자, 거의 일 분 가까이 멈춤이 이어졌고, 그동안 빈시 씨는 책 한 권을 책상에 도로 넣고 단호하게 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔다. 그러고는 아들을 물끄러미 바라보며 말했다—\n\n\"그래, 마침내 마음을 굳혔단 말이지, 이놈?\"\n\n\"네, 아버지.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그럼 거기 매달려라. 더는 할 말 없다. 내가 너한테 출세할 수단을 마련해 줬건만, 너는 네 교육을 내팽개치고 한 단계 내려간 거다, 그게 다야.\"\n\n\"아버지와 생각이 다른 게 정말 죄송합니다. 제가 맡은 일을 하면서도 부목사가 되었을 때 못지않게 충분히 신사다울 수 있다고 생각해요. 하지만 저를 위해 최선을 다해 주려 하신 데 대해서는 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"좋아. 더는 할 말 없다. 너한테서 손 떼마. 다만, 네가 네 아들을 두게 되거든, 그 아이는 네가 쏟는 수고에 대해 너보다 더 나은 보답을 해 주기를 바랄 뿐이다.\"\n\n이 말은 프레드에게 무척 아프게 와닿았다. 그의 아버지는, 우리 모두가 애처로운 처지에 놓여 자신의 지난날을 마치 단순히 그 애처로움의 일부인 양 바라볼 때 누리게 되는, 그 부당한 우위를 써먹고 있었다. 실상 빈시 씨가 아들에 대해 품었던 바람에는 적잖은 자만과 무분별과 이기적인 어리석음이 담겨 있었다. 하지만 그래도 실망한 아버지는 강한 지렛대를 쥐고 있었으니, 프레드는 마치 저주와 함께 추방당하는 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"제가 집에 계속 머무는 걸 반대하지 않으시겠죠, 아버지?\" 일어나 나가려다가 그가 말했다. \"물론 마땅히 그러고 싶은 만큼, 식비를 낼 만큼의 봉급은 받게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"식비는 무슨!\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. 프레드의 밥값이 자기 식탁에서 아쉬울 것이라는 생각에 역겨워하며 마음을 추스른 것이었다. \"물론 네 어머니는 네가 머물기를 바랄 거다. 하지만 너한테 말 한 마리도 두지 않겠으니, 알아 두어라. 그리고 양복점 값은 네가 직접 치러야 해. 옷값을 직접 내야 할 처지가 되면, 양복 한두 벌쯤은 줄여서 지내게 되겠지, 아마.\"\n\n프레드는 머뭇거렸다. 아직 할 말이 남아 있었다. 마침내 그것이 나왔다.\n\n\"아버지, 저와 악수해 주시고, 제가 끼쳐 드린 속상함을 용서해 주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n빈시 씨는 의자에 앉은 채, 가까이 다가온 아들을 향해 재빨리 눈길을 위로 던지더니, 손을 내밀며 서둘러 말했다. \"그래, 그래, 더는 말하지 말자.\"\n\n프레드는 어머니에게는 훨씬 더 많은 이야기와 설명을 늘어놓았으나, 어머니는 도무지 위로받지 못했다. 어쩌면 남편은 한 번도 떠올리지 못했을 일들이 그녀의 눈앞에 어른거렸기 때문이다. 즉 프레드가 메리 가스와 결혼하리라는 것, 그리하여 자기 삶이 앞으로는 가스 집안 사람들과 그들의 방식이 끊임없이 흘러들어 망쳐지리라는 것, 그리고 그 아름다운 얼굴과 \"미들마치의 그 누구 아들보다도\" 멋스러운 풍모를 지닌 자신의 사랑하는 아들이, 그 집안처럼 외모는 수수해지고 옷차림에는 무심해지고 말리라는 확신이었다. 그녀에게는 마치 그 탐나는 프레드를 손에 넣으려는 가스 집안의 음모가 있는 듯 여겨졌으나, 그녀는 감히 이 견해를 늘어놓지 못했다. 그것을 살짝 비친 것만으로도 프레드가 전에 없이 그녀에게 \"발끈\" 했던 것이다. 그녀의 성정은 너무도 다정해서 어떤 노여움도 드러내지 못했으나, 그녀는 자신의 행복이 멍든 것을 느꼈고, 며칠 동안은 그저 프레드를 바라보기만 해도, 마치 그가 무슨 불길한 예언의 대상이라도 되는 양 조금씩 눈물을 흘렸다. 그녀가 평소의 명랑함을 되찾는 데 더 더뎠던 것은, 어쩌면 프레드가 그 아픈 문제를 아버지에게 다시 꺼내서는 안 된다고 그녀에게 미리 일러두었기 때문인지도 모른다. 아버지는 그의 결정을 받아들였고 그를 용서한 터였다. 만약 남편이 프레드에게 격하게 굴었다면, 그녀는 사랑하는 아들을 변호하느라 떠밀려 나섰을 것이다. 빈시 씨가 그녀에게 이렇게 말한 것은 나흘째 되던 날 저물녘이었다—\n\n\"자, 루시, 여보, 그렇게 기죽어 있지 말구려. 당신은 늘 그 애를 응석받이로 키웠으니, 앞으로도 계속 응석을 받아 줘야지.\"\n\n\"전에는 무슨 일도 이렇게까지 제 마음을 후벼 판 적이 없었어요, 빈시.\" 아내가 말했다. 그 고운 목과 턱이 다시 떨리기 시작했다. \"그 애가 아팠을 때 말고는요.\"\n\n\"쯧, 쯧, 신경 쓰지 마! 자식들 때문에 속 썩는 거야 으레 있는 일이려니 해야지. 당신이 기죽은 모습을 나한테 보여서 일을 더 나쁘게 만들지 말아요.\"\n\n\"네, 그러지 않을게요.\" 빈시 부인이 말했다. 이 호소에 마음을 추스르고, 헝클어진 깃털을 가다듬는 새처럼 살짝 몸을 떨며 자세를 바로잡았다.\n\n\"하나 가지고 호들갑을 떨기 시작하면 안 되지.\" 빈시 씨가 약간의 투덜거림에 가정적인 명랑함을 섞고 싶어 하며 말했다. \"프레드뿐 아니라 로저먼드도 있잖소.\"\n\n\"네, 가엾은 것. 그 애가 아기를 잃고 실망한 게 저도 정말 가슴 아팠어요. 그래도 잘 추스르긴 했지요.\"\n\n\"아기는 무슨! 내가 듣자 하니 리드게이트가 진료를 엉망으로 망쳐 가고 빚까지 지고 있는 모양이야. 머지않아 로저먼드가 그럴듯한 사정을 들고 나한테 찾아오겠지. 하지만 나한테서는 한 푼도 못 받을 거야, 분명히. 그 사람 집안더러 도우라 해. 나는 그 결혼이 도무지 마음에 든 적이 없었어. 하지만 말해 봐야 소용없지. 종을 울려 레몬을 가져오라 하고, 더는 시무룩한 얼굴 하지 말아요, 루시. 내일 당신하고 루이자를 데리고 리버스턴으로 마차나 몰고 갑시다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LVII.\n\n한 이름이 그들 영혼 위로 떠올라 그 안에서 그런 움직임을 일으켰으니\n생기를 불어넣는 공기가 스며들 때\n\n봉오리를 떨게 하고 그 숨은 형태를 빚는 것과 같았다.\n충직한 에번 두를, 기이한 브래드워딘과\n\n비치 이언 보어를 들려준 그 이의 이름,\n어린 시절 알던 작은 세계를\n\n산과 호수와 벼랑의 땅으로 넓혀 주고,\n경이와 사랑과 믿음으로 더욱 넓혀 준\n\n멀리 떨어져 살던 월터 스콧을 향한 믿음으로.\n\n그가 보낸 이 기쁨의 풍요와 고귀한 슬픔.\n책과 그들은 헤어져야 했지만, 날마다,\n\n뚱뚱한 거미처럼 비뚤비뚤 가로지르는 줄로\n그들은 그 이야기를 썼다, 털리 비올란에서부터.\n\n* 에번 두, 브래드워딘, 비치 이언 보어, 털리 비올란: 모두 월터 스콧의 소설 『웨이벌리』에 나오는 인물과 장소 이름.\n프레드 빈시가 로윅 목사관까지 걸어갔던 날 저녁(그는 이제 기개 있는 젊은이라 해도 자신을 태워 줄 말이 없으면 이따금 걸어야만 하는 세상에 살고 있음을 깨닫기 시작하던 참이었다) 그는 다섯 시에 길을 나섰고, 가스 부인이 두 사람의 새로운 관계를 기꺼이 받아들이는지 확인하고 싶은 마음에 도중에 그녀를 찾아갔다.\n\n그는 과수원의 커다란 사과나무 아래에서 개와 고양이까지 포함된 가족 무리를 발견했다. 가스 부인에게는 축제와도 같은 날이었으니, 그녀의 각별한 기쁨이자 자랑인 맏아들 크리스티가 짧은 휴가를 맞아 집에 돌아왔기 때문이었다. 크리스티는 가정교사가 되어 온갖 문학을 연구하고 다시 태어난 포슨이 되는 것이야말로 세상에서 가장 바람직한 일이라 여기는 청년이자, 가엾은 프레드에 대한 살아 있는 비판이요, 교육열에 찬 어머니가 프레드에게 들이미는 일종의 본보기였다. 정작 크리스티 자신은 어머니를 남성판으로 옮겨 놓은 듯 네모진 이마에 떡 벌어진 어깨를 가졌으되 키는 프레드의 어깨에 겨우 닿을 정도였는데—그래서 그가 더 우월하게 여겨진다는 사실이 한층 견디기 어려웠다—언제나 더할 나위 없이 소박했고, 프레드가 학문에 마음이 없다는 것을 기린이 학문에 마음이 없는 것 이상으로 대수롭게 여기지 않았으며, 다만 자신이 좀 더 그만큼 키가 컸으면 하고 바랄 뿐이었다. 지금 그는 어머니의 의자 곁 땅바닥에 누워 밀짚모자를 눈 위에 납작하게 얹고 있었고, 그 맞은편에서는 짐이 많은 젊은이의 행복에 큰 몫을 차지해 온 그 사랑받는 작가의 글을 소리 내어 읽고 있었다. 그 책은 『아이반호』였고, 짐은 마상 시합의 대단한 활쏘기 장면을 읽던 중이었으나, 자기 낡은 활과 화살을 가져온 벤 때문에 자주 방해를 받았다. 레티가 보기에 벤은 자기가 마구잡이로 쏘는 화살을 보아 달라고 거기 있는 모든 이에게 졸라 대며 지독히도 성가시게 굴고 있었는데, 활기는 넘치나 아마도 머리는 얕은 잡종견 브라우니 말고는 아무도 그러고 싶어 하지 않았고, 햇볕 아래 누운 잿빛 늙은 뉴펀들랜드는 극도의 노령다운 흐릿한 눈의 무관심으로 그것을 바라보고 있었다. 레티 자신은 입가와 앞치마에, 찻상 위 산호 더미처럼 쌓인 버찌를 따는 일을 거들었다는 약간의 흔적을 남긴 채, 지금은 풀밭에 앉아 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 책 읽는 소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다.\n\n그러나 프레드 빈시가 도착하자 모두에게 관심의 중심이 바뀌었다. 그가 정원 의자에 앉으며 로윅 목사관으로 가는 길이라고 말하자, 활을 내던지고 대신 마지못해하는 반쯤 자란 새끼 고양이를 움켜쥐고 있던 벤이 프레드가 뻗은 다리를 성큼 넘어와 \"나 데려가!\"라고 말했다.\n\"아, 나도요.\" 레티가 말했다.\n\n\"넌 나하고 프레드를 못 따라와.\" 벤이 말했다.\n\"아냐, 따라갈 수 있어. 엄마, 제발 나 가도 된다고 말해 줘요.\" 여자아이라고 깔보는 데 맞서느라 인생이 자못 얼룩져 온 레티가 졸랐다.\n\n\"난 크리스티 형하고 있을래.\" 짐이 말했다. 마치 자기가 저 어수룩한 아이들보다 유리하다는 듯한 말투였다. 그러자 레티는 손을 머리로 가져가며 질투 어린 망설임으로 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라보았다.\n\n\"우리 다 같이 메리를 보러 가자.\" 크리스티가 두 팔을 벌리며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 얘야, 우리가 떼를 지어 목사관에 몰려가서는 안 된다. 게다가 네 그 낡은 글래스고 양복으로는 도무지 안 되지. 그리고 네 아버지가 집에 오실 거야. 프레드 혼자 가게 두자. 프레드가 네가 여기 있다고 메리에게 전해 줄 테고, 메리는 내일 돌아올 게다.\"\n크리스티는 자기 닳아빠진 무릎을 흘긋 보았다가 프레드의 멋진 흰 바지를 보았다. 분명 프레드의 옷차림은 영국 대학의 이점을 떠올리게 했고, 그는 더워 보이는 것조차도, 손수건으로 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기는 것조차도 우아한 데가 있었다.\n\n\"얘들아, 저리 가렴.\" 가스 부인이 말했다. \"이렇게 더운데 친구 곁에 매달려 있으면 못쓴다. 형을 데리고 가서 토끼를 보여 드려라.\"\n맏이가 알아듣고 곧장 아이들을 이끌고 갔다. 프레드는 가스 부인이 자기가 할 말이 있으면 할 기회를 주려 한다는 것을 느꼈으나, 이렇게밖에 말을 꺼낼 수 없었다.\n\n\"크리스티가 와서 정말 기쁘시겠어요!\"\n\"그럼, 생각보다 일찍 왔지. 아홉 시에 마차에서 내렸는데, 마침 제 아버지가 막 나가신 뒤였어. 어서 케일럽이 와서 크리스티가 얼마나 놀랍게 발전했는지 들었으면 좋겠구나. 그 애는 지난 한 해 동안 수업을 해 가며 학비를 댔고, 동시에 힘든 공부도 계속했어. 곧 개인 가정교사 자리를 얻어 외국에 나가기를 바라고 있단다.\"\n\n\"훌륭한 친구죠.\" 프레드가 말했는데, 이런 명랑한 진실들이 그에게는 약처럼 쓴맛이 났다. \"누구에게도 폐를 끼치는 법이 없고요.\" 그는 잠시 뜸을 들이다 덧붙였다. \"하지만 제가 가스 씨께 무척 폐를 끼치게 될 거라고 생각하실까 걱정입니다.\"\n\"케일럽은 수고를 마다하지 않아. 누가 부탁할 생각조차 못 한 일까지 늘 더 해내는 그런 사람이지.\" 가스 부인이 대답했다. 그녀는 뜨개질을 하고 있어서, 마음 내키는 대로 프레드를 보기도 하고 보지 않기도 할 수 있었다—말에 유익한 의미를 담으려 작정한 사람에게는 언제나 유리한 일이었다. 가스 부인은 마땅히 삼가려는 마음이었으나, 프레드에게 도움이 될 만한 말을 하고 싶은 것도 사실이었다.\n\n\"제가 자격이 없다고 여기시는 걸 알아요, 가스 부인, 그럴 만한 이유도 있으시고요.\" 프레드가 자기를 훈계하려는 듯한 기색을 알아채자 약간 기운이 솟아 말했다. \"공교롭게도 저는 가장 많은 것을 바라지 않을 수 없는 사람들에게 하필 가장 못되게 굴어 왔지요. 하지만 가스 씨나 페어브러더 씨 같은 두 분이 저를 포기하지 않으셨는데, 제가 어찌 저 자신을 포기해야 하겠습니까.\" 프레드는 이런 남자다운 본보기를 가스 부인에게 들이미는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했다.\n\n\"물론이지.\" 그녀가 점점 힘주어 말했다. \"그렇게 어른 두 분이 헌신해 주신 젊은이가 자기 자신을 내던져 그분들의 희생을 헛되게 만든다면 정말이지 죄가 될 게야.\"\n프레드는 이 강한 말투에 약간 의아해했으나, \"저는 그렇게 되지 않기를 바랍니다, 가스 부인, 메리의 마음을 얻을 수 있으리라 믿을 만한 격려를 좀 받았으니까요. 가스 씨가 그 이야기를 해 드렸지요? 놀라지 않으셨겠지요?\"라고만 말했다. 프레드는 순진하게도 충분히 분명할 자기 사랑만을 두고 한 말이었다.\n\n\"메리가 너에게 격려를 보냈다는 게 놀랍지 않으냐고?\" 가스 부인이 되받았다. 그녀는 빈시 집안 사람들이 무어라 짐작하든, 메리의 가족이 이런 일을 미리 바랐을 리 만무하다는 사실을 프레드가 좀 더 의식하는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했다. \"그래, 솔직히 놀랐다.\"\n\n\"제가 직접 말했을 때 메리는 결코 제게 아무 격려도 하지 않았어요—조금도요.\" 메리를 변호하고 싶은 마음에 프레드가 말했다. \"하지만 제가 페어브러더 씨에게 저를 위해 말해 달라고 부탁했더니, 메리가 그분을 통해 제게 희망이 있다고 전하도록 허락했지요.\"\n\n가스 부인의 마음속에서 꿈틀거리기 시작한 훈계의 힘은 아직 다 풀려나오지 않은 터였다. 이 활짝 핀 풋내기가 더 슬프고 더 현명한 사람들의 실망을 양분 삼아 번성한다는 것은—나이팅게일을 한 끼 식사로 먹으면서도 그런 줄 전혀 모른다는 것은—그리고 그러는 내내 그 집안 사람들은 이쪽이 이 잔가지를 간절히 필요로 하리라 짐작한다는 것은, 그녀의 자제력으로도 좀 지나치게 부아가 치미는 노릇이었다. 남편을 향해서는 그 분함을 완전히 억눌렀던 탓에 그것이 한층 더 활발히 발효되었다. 모범적인 아내들은 이따금 이런 식으로 희생양을 찾는 법이다. 그녀는 이제 단호하고 힘 있게 말했다. \"큰 실수를 한 게야, 프레드, 페어브러더 씨에게 너를 위해 말해 달라고 부탁한 것은.\"\n\n\"제가요?\" 프레드가 즉각 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 그는 깜짝 놀랐으나, 가스 부인이 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 종잡을 수 없어, 변명하는 어조로 덧붙였다. \"페어브러더 씨는 늘 우리 집과 그렇게나 친한 분이셨고요. 그리고 저는 메리가 그분 말씀은 진지하게 들으리라는 걸 알았어요. 게다가 그분이 아주 선뜻 그 일을 맡아 주셨고요.\"\n\n\"그래, 젊은이들은 보통 자기 바람 말고는 아무것도 보지 못하고, 그 바람이 남에게 얼마나 큰 대가를 치르게 하는지는 좀처럼 상상하지 못하지.\" 가스 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 이 유익한 일반론 이상으로 나아갈 생각은 없었고, 분함을 공연히 털실을 풀어내는 데로 던지며 거창한 태도로 그것에 미간을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"페어브러더 씨에게 그게 어떻게 괴로움이 될 수 있는지 저는 짐작도 못 하겠습니다.\" 프레드가 말했으나, 그럼에도 놀라운 짐작들이 마음속에 형체를 잡아 가기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"바로 그거다, 너는 짐작도 못 하지.\" 가스 부인이 되도록 또박또박 말을 끊으며 말했다.\n\n잠시 프레드는 당혹스러운 불안에 휩싸여 지평선을 바라보다가, 이내 빠른 동작으로 돌아서며 거의 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까 가스 부인, 페어브러더 씨가 메리를 사랑한다는 말씀이십니까?\"\n\n\"설령 그렇다 한들, 프레드, 너야말로 놀라서는 안 될 마지막 사람이라고 나는 생각한다.\" 가스 부인이 뜨개질감을 곁에 내려놓고 팔짱을 끼며 대꾸했다. 그녀가 손에서 일감을 놓는다는 것은 그녀로서는 보기 드문 감정의 표시였다. 사실 그녀의 마음은 프레드를 훈계했다는 만족감과 좀 지나쳤다는 느낌 사이에서 갈라져 있었다. 프레드는 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들고 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"그럼 부인은 제가 그분의 앞길을, 그리고 메리의 앞길까지 가로막고 있다고 생각하시는군요?\" 그가 대답을 요구하는 듯한 어조로 말했다.\n\n가스 부인은 곧바로 말할 수 없었다. 그녀는 자기가 정말로 느끼는 바를, 그러나 감추어야 할 강력한 이유가 있다고 아는 바를 말하라고 요구받는 곤란한 처지에 스스로를 몰아넣은 것이었다. 그리고 그녀에게는 말이 지나쳤다는 의식이 유난히 굴욕적이었다. 게다가 프레드가 뜻밖의 전기를 내뿜었고, 그는 이제 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"가스 씨는 메리가 저를 좋아하는 걸 기뻐하시는 듯했어요. 그분은 이런 일을 전혀 모르셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n남편이 거론되자 가스 부인은 심한 통증을 느꼈으니, 케일럽이 자기가 잘못했다고 여길지 모른다는 두려움은 쉬이 견딜 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 그녀는 의도치 않은 결과를 막고 싶은 마음에 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 그저 추측으로 말한 거다. 메리가 그 일에 대해 무언가 안다고는 생각지 않아.\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 정작 자기가 불필요하게 꺼낸 화제를 두고 프레드에게 완전히 침묵해 달라고 부탁하기를 망설였으니, 그런 식으로 자세를 낮추는 데 익숙지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 그녀가 망설이는 사이, 찻상이 놓인 사과나무 아래에서는 이미 의도치 않은 결과가 한바탕 쏟아져 나오고 있었다. 벤이 브라우니를 뒤에 달고 풀밭을 가로질러 깡충거리다가, 새끼 고양이가 점점 길어지는 털실 줄로 뜨개질감을 끌고 가는 것을 보고는 소리치며 손뼉을 쳤다. 브라우니가 짖었고, 절박해진 새끼 고양이는 찻상으로 뛰어올라 우유를 엎질렀다가 다시 뛰어내리며 버찌를 절반이나 휩쓸어 떨어뜨렸다. 그러자 벤이 반쯤 뜬 양말목을 낚아채어 새로운 광란의 원천으로 삼고자 새끼 고양이 머리에 씌웠고, 마침 도착한 레티가 이런 잔인한 짓을 두고 어머니에게 울며 항의했다—그것은 \"이것은 잭이 지은 집이라\"만큼이나 사건으로 가득 찬 이야기였다. 가스 부인은 끼어들 수밖에 없었고, 다른 아이들도 다가왔으며, 프레드와의 단둘이 나누던 이야기는 끝나고 말았다. 그는 되도록 빨리 그곳을 떠났고, 가스 부인은 그와 악수하며 \"신의 가호가 있기를\"이라고 말하는 것으로 자신의 가혹함을 다소 거두어들였음을 내비칠 수 있을 뿐이었다.\n* \"이것은 잭이 지은 집이라\": 영국 전래 누적 동요로, 한 사건이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 이어지는 형식.\n\n그녀는 자기가 \"어리석은 여인의 말처럼\"—먼저 일러바치고 나서 침묵을 간청하는 식으로—말할 뻔했다는 것을 불쾌하게 의식했다. 그러나 그녀는 침묵을 간청하지 않았고, 케일럽의 비난을 막기 위해 스스로를 탓하고 바로 그날 밤 그에게 모든 것을 고백하기로 결심했다. 온화한 케일럽이 일단 재판정을 차리고 나면 그녀에게는 그것이 얼마나 무시무시한 법정이 되는지 참으로 묘한 일이었다. 그러나 그녀는 이 폭로가 프레드 빈시에게 큰 도움이 될 수도 있음을 그에게 일러 줄 작정이었다.\n\n의심할 바 없이 그것은 로윅으로 걸어가는 프레드에게 강한 영향을 미치고 있었다. 프레드의 가볍고 희망찬 천성이, 자기가 끼어들지 않았더라면 메리가 더할 나위 없이 좋은 혼처를 만났을지 모른다는 그 암시만큼 큰 멍이 든 적은 아마 없었을 것이다. 또한 그는 자신이 페어브러더 씨에게 그런 중재를 부탁한, 스스로 말하는 그런 멍청한 얼간이였다는 데 자존심이 상했다. 그러나 메리의 마음을 두고 새로 일어난 불안이 다른 모든 것을 압도하지 않는다는 것은 연인의 본성에—프레드의 본성에 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 페어브러더 씨의 너그러움을 믿었음에도, 메리가 자기에게 한 말이 있었음에도, 프레드는 자기에게 연적이 있다는 느낌을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그것은 새로운 의식이었고, 그는 그것이 몹시 못마땅했으니, 메리를 위해 메리를 단념할 마음은 조금도 없었고, 오히려 그 누구와도 그녀를 두고 싸울 각오가 되어 있었던 것이다. 그러나 페어브러더 씨와의 싸움은 비유적인 것일 수밖에 없었는데, 그것은 프레드에게 근육을 쓰는 싸움보다 훨씬 더 어려운 일이었다. 분명 이 경험은 프레드에게 큰아버지의 유언을 둘러싼 실망 못지않게 날카로운 단련이었다. 쇠가 아직 그의 영혼을 꿰뚫지는 않았으나, 그는 그 예리한 날이 어떨지를 상상하기 시작했다. 가스 부인이 페어브러더 씨에 관해 잘못 알고 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 프레드에게 한 번도 떠오르지 않았으나, 그녀가 메리에 관해서는 틀렸을지도 모른다고 의심했다. 메리는 요즘 목사관에 머물고 있었으니, 그녀의 어머니는 메리의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는지 거의 모를 수도 있었다.\n\n객실에서 세 부인과 함께 명랑해 보이는 그녀를 발견했을 때도 그는 마음이 한결 편해지지 않았다. 그가 들어서자 뚝 그쳐 버린 어떤 화제를 두고 그들은 활기차게 토론하던 중이었고, 메리는 얕은 진열장 서랍 한 무더기에서 꼬리표를 베껴 적고 있었는데, 그녀가 능숙한 그 자잘한 필체였다. 페어브러더 씨는 마을 어딘가에 있었고, 세 부인은 프레드와 메리의 특별한 관계에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다. 둘 중 누구도 정원을 한 바퀴 돌자고 제안할 수 없었으니, 프레드는 그녀에게 단둘이 한마디도 하지 못한 채 떠나야 하리라고 스스로 예언했다. 그는 먼저 크리스티가 도착했다는 소식을, 그다음에는 자기가 그녀의 아버지와 일하기로 했다는 소식을 전했다. 그리고 이 뒤의 소식이 그녀의 마음을 절절히 건드리는 것을 보고 위안을 얻었다. 그녀는 황급히 \"정말 기뻐요\"라고 말하고는, 누구도 자기 얼굴을 알아채지 못하도록 글씨 위로 몸을 숙였다. 그러나 이것은 페어브러더 부인이 그냥 넘길 수 없는 화제였다.\n\n\"가스 양, 설마 한 젊은이가 자기가 교육받은 성직을 포기한다는 소식이 기쁘다는 뜻은 아니겠지요. 사정이 그러하니 그가 가스 양 아버님 같은 훌륭한 분 밑에 있게 되어 기쁘다는 뜻일 뿐이겠지요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말로, 페어브러더 부인, 저는 두 가지 다 기뻐요, 유감스럽게도요.\" 메리가 솟구치는 눈물 한 방울을 영리하게 떨쳐 내며 말했다. \"저는 속물스럽기 짝이 없는 사람이거든요. 저는 『웨이크필드의 목사』와 페어브러더 씨 말고는 어떤 성직자도 좋아한 적이 없어요.\"\n\n* 『웨이크필드의 목사』: 올리버 골드스미스의 소설로, 그 주인공인 시골 목사를 가리킨다.\n\"아니 왜 그러니, 얘야?\" 페어브러더 부인이 커다란 나무 뜨개바늘을 멈추고 메리를 바라보며 말했다. \"넌 늘 네 의견에 그럴듯한 이유가 있더니, 이건 정말 놀랍구나. 물론 새로운 교리를 설교하는 자들은 논외로 친다만. 그런데 어째서 성직자를 싫어하는 게냐?\"\n\n\"어머나,\" 메리가 잠시 생각하는 듯하더니 얼굴에 명랑한 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"저는 그분들의 목깃이 마음에 안 들어요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼 캠던의 것도 마음에 안 든다는 게냐.\" 위니프레드 양이 다소 걱정스레 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 그건 마음에 들어요.\" 메리가 말했다. \"다른 성직자들의 목깃이 마음에 안 드는 건, 그걸 두른 사람이 그분들이기 때문이에요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 알쏭달쏭하니!\" 노블 양이 자기 머리가 아마 모자란 모양이라고 느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"얘야, 농담이로구나. 그렇게 훌륭한 부류의 사람들을 깔보는 데는 이보다 나은 이유가 있어야지.\" 페어브러더 부인이 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"가스 양은 사람이 어떠해야 하는지에 대해 너무 엄격한 관념을 가지고 있어서 만족시켜 드리기가 어렵답니다.\" 프레드가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 그래도 적어도 내 아들은 예외로 쳐 준다니 기쁘구나.\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n메리가 프레드의 부아 난 어조에 의아해하고 있는데, 페어브러더 씨가 들어와 메리가 가스 씨 밑에서 일하게 된다는 소식을 들어야 했다. 다 듣고 나서 그는 조용한 만족으로 말했다. \"그것 잘됐구나.\" 그러고는 몸을 숙여 메리의 꼬리표를 들여다보며 그녀의 필체를 칭찬했다. 프레드는 끔찍하게 질투가 났다—물론 페어브러더 씨가 그토록 존경할 만한 사람이라 기뻤으나, 마흔 살 남자들이 더러 그렇듯 그가 못생기고 뚱뚱했더라면 싶었다. 메리가 페어브러더를 누구보다 위에 두는 것을 공공연히 드러내고, 이 여인들이 하나같이 분명 그 일을 부추기는 터라, 결말이 어떻게 될지는 자명했다. 메리와 말할 기회는 없으리라 그가 확신하고 있는데, 페어브러더 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"프레드, 이 서랍들을 내 서재로 도로 옮기는 걸 좀 도와주게—내 멋진 새 서재를 아직 못 봤지. 가스 양도 부디 같이 와요. 오늘 아침에 찾아낸 굉장한 거미를 보여 주고 싶군요.\"\n\n메리는 곧 목사의 의도를 알아챘다. 그는 그 잊을 수 없는 저녁 이후로 그녀를 향한 옛 목회자다운 친절에서 한 번도 벗어난 적이 없었고, 그녀의 순간적인 의아함과 의심은 완전히 잠들어 버린 터였다. 메리는 무엇이 그럴듯한지를 꽤나 엄정하게 생각하는 데 익숙했고, 어떤 믿음이 자신의 허영심을 추켜세우면 그것을 우스꽝스럽다며 떨쳐 내야 한다고 경계하는 마음이 들었으니, 그런 떨쳐 냄을 일찍부터 많이 연습해 온 까닭이었다. 그것은 그녀가 내다본 대로였다. 프레드가 서재의 비품들을 감탄해 보라는 청을 받고, 그녀가 거미를 감탄해 보라는 청을 받고 나자, 페어브러더 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 잠깐만 기다려요. 프레드가 키가 충분히 크니 나 대신 걸어 줄 판화를 하나 찾아오겠소. 곧 돌아오리다.\" 그러고는 그가 나갔다. 그럼에도 프레드가 메리에게 처음으로 한 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"내가 무얼 하든 소용없어, 메리. 너는 결국 틀림없이 페어브러더와 결혼하게 될 거야.\" 그의 어조에는 다소 분노가 서려 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 말이야, 프레드?\" 메리가 발끈하여 외쳤고, 얼굴이 새빨개졌으며, 평소의 재치 있는 대꾸도 잊은 채 놀라 버렸다.\n\n\"네가 모든 걸 환히 보면서—모든 걸 다 보는 네가—이걸 못 본다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이야.\"\n\n\"내가 보기엔, 프레드, 페어브러더 씨가 온갖 방법으로 너를 위해 변호해 주신 마당에 그분에 대해 그렇게 말하는 네가 아주 못되게 굴고 있을 뿐이야. 어떻게 그런 생각을 다 했어?\"\n\n프레드는 짜증이 났음에도 자못 신중했다. 메리가 정말로 아무것도 의심하지 않았다면, 가스 부인이 한 말을 그녀에게 알려서 좋을 게 없었다.\n\n\"당연한 귀결이지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"매사에 나를 능가하고, 네가 누구보다 위에 모시는 사람을 네가 줄곧 만나고 있으니, 나에게는 공정한 기회가 있을 수 없어.\"\n\n\"넌 정말 배은망덕하구나, 프레드.\" 메리가 말했다. \"페어브러더 씨에게 내가 너를 조금이라도 마음에 둔다고 말한 걸 후회하게 되네.\"\n\n\"아니, 난 배은망덕한 게 아니야. 이 일만 아니라면 난 세상에서 가장 행복한 녀석일 거야. 난 네 아버지께 모든 걸 말씀드렸고, 그분은 무척 다정하셨어. 나를 마치 당신 아들처럼 대해 주셨지. 이 일만 아니라면 나는 글 쓰는 일이며 뭐며 의욕을 가지고 매달릴 수 있어.\"\n\n\"이 일? 무슨 일?\" 메리가 이제는 무언가 구체적인 말이나 행동이 있었던 게 틀림없다고 짐작하며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 페어브러더에게 밀려나고 말 거라는 이 끔찍한 확신 말이야.\" 메리는 웃음이 나오려는 마음에 누그러졌다.\n\n\"프레드,\" 그녀가 자기에게서 뾰로통하게 돌려진 그의 눈을 마주치려고 빙 돌아 들여다보며 말했다. \"너는 너무 사랑스럽게 우스꽝스러워. 네가 이렇게 매력적인 얼간이만 아니라면, 못된 요부 노릇을 하면서 너 말고도 누군가가 내게 구애했다고 믿게 만들고 싶은 유혹이 얼마나 컸겠니.\"\n\n\"정말 나를 제일 좋아하는 거야, 메리?\" 프레드가 애정 가득한 눈을 그녀에게 돌리며, 그녀의 손을 잡으려 하면서 말했다.\n\n\"지금 이 순간엔 너를 조금도 좋아하지 않아.\" 메리가 물러서며 두 손을 등 뒤로 감추고 말했다. \"나는 그저 너 말고는 살아 있는 그 누구도 내게 구애한 적이 없다고 말했을 뿐이야. 그렇다고 어떤 아주 현명한 사람이 언젠가 그러리라는 근거가 되는 건 아니지.\" 그녀가 명랑하게 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"네가 그 사람을 절대로 생각할 수 없다고 말해 주면 좋겠어.\" 프레드가 말했다.\n\n\"두 번 다시 이런 이야기를 내게 꺼낼 생각 마, 프레드.\" 메리가 다시 진지해지며 말했다. \"페어브러더 씨가 우리 둘이 자유롭게 이야기하라고 일부러 자리를 비켜 주신 걸 네가 못 보는 게 더 어리석은 건지 더 야박한 건지 모르겠어. 네가 그분의 섬세한 마음에 그렇게 눈멀어 있다니 실망이야.\"\n\n페어브러더 씨가 판화를 가지고 돌아오기 전에 더 말할 시간은 없었다. 프레드는 마음속에 여전히 질투 어린 두려움을 품은 채, 그러나 메리의 말과 태도에서 위안이 되는 근거도 얻은 채 객실로 돌아가야 했다. 그 대화의 결과는 전체적으로 메리에게 더 괴로운 것이었다. 어쩔 수 없이 그녀의 관심은 새로운 태도를 취하게 되었고, 그녀는 새로운 해석의 가능성을 보게 되었던 것이다. 그녀는 스스로 페어브러더 씨를 홀대하는 듯이 여겨지는 처지에 놓였는데, 이는 많은 존경을 받는 남자와 관련될 때 늘 고마움을 아는 여자의 굳건함에 위험한 일이었다. 다음 날 집에 갈 이유가 있다는 것이 안도가 되었으니, 메리는 자신이 프레드를 제일 사랑한다는 것을 늘 분명히 하고 싶은 마음이 간절했기 때문이다. 다정한 애정이 여러 해에 걸쳐 우리 안에 쌓여 왔을 때, 그것을 무언가와 맞바꿀 수 있다는 생각은 우리 삶을 헐값으로 만드는 일처럼 보인다. 그리고 우리는 다른 보물들을 지키듯이 우리의 애정과 변함없음에도 파수꾼을 세워 둘 수 있다.\n\n\"프레드는 다른 기대를 모두 잃었어. 이것만은 지켜야 해.\" 메리가 입가에 미소를 머금으며 혼잣말을 했다. 또 다른 종류의 덧없는 환상들이—그녀가 그 부재를 자주 느껴 온 새로운 품위와, 인정받는 가치가—떠오르는 것을 막을 도리는 없었다. 그러나 이런 것들이 프레드를 빼놓은 채라면, 프레드가 버림받아 자기를 그리워하며 슬퍼 보인다면, 그것은 결코 그녀의 깊은 생각을 유혹할 수 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LVIII.\n\n그러므로 나는 그대의 변심을 알아챌 길 없네.\n많은 이들의 표정에는 거짓된 마음의 내력이\n\n기이한 변덕과 찡그림과 주름으로 새겨지건만,\n하늘은 그대를 빚을 때 이렇게 정하셨으니\n\n그대 얼굴에는 언제나 달콤한 사랑이 머물러야 한다고.\n그대의 생각이 무엇이든, 그대 마음의 움직임이 어떠하든\n\n그대의 표정은 오직 달콤함 외에 아무것도 말하지 않으리.\n— 셰익스피어, 『소네트』\n\n빈시 씨가 로저먼드를 두고 그런 예감을 말했을 무렵, 정작 그녀 자신은 아버지가 예견한 그런 종류의 부탁을 하게 되리라고는 생각조차 해 본 적이 없었다. 그녀의 가정생활은 다사다난한 동시에 사치스럽기도 했지만, 그녀는 아직 돈을 마련하는 방도에 대해 아무런 불안도 느끼지 않았다. 아기는 달을 채우지 못하고 일찍 태어났고, 수놓인 배냇저고리와 모자는 모두 어둠 속에 치워 두어야 했다. 이 불운은 전적으로, 남편이 하지 말라고 당부했는데도 그녀가 어느 날 굳이 말을 타고 외출한 탓으로 돌려졌다. 그러나 그녀가 그 일로 성을 냈다거나, 자기 마음대로 하겠다고 무례하게 쏘아붙였다고 짐작해서는 안 된다.\n그녀가 특별히 승마를 하고 싶어 하게 된 것은 준남작의 셋째 아들인 리드게이트 대위의 방문 때문이었는데, 안타깝게도 같은 성을 지닌 우리의 터셔스는 그를 시시한 멋쟁이라며 몹시 싫어했다. \"이마에서 목덜미까지 가르마를 천박하게 타고는\"(터셔스 자신은 따르지 않는 방식이었다) 어떤 주제에 대해서든 적절히 말할 줄 안다는 무지한 자신감을 드러내는 자라는 것이었다. 리드게이트는 신혼여행 중에 숙부 댁에 가기로 응낙하는 바람에 이 방문을 끌어들였다고 자기 어리석음을 속으로 저주했고, 그 말을 사석에서 털어놓아 로저먼드에게 다소 거슬리는 사람이 되었다. 로저먼드에게는 이 방문이 전에 없던, 그러나 우아하게 감춰진 의기양양함의 원천이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 준남작의 아들인 사촌이 집에 머물고 있다는 사실을 너무도 강렬하게 의식한 나머지, 그의 존재가 함축하는 바를 다른 모든 사람의 마음도 알고 있으리라 상상했다. 그래서 리드게이트 대위를 손님들에게 소개할 때 그의 신분이 마치 향기처럼 그들에게 스며든다는 평온한 느낌을 받았다. 그 만족감은 한동안, 좋은 가문 출신이라 해도 의사와 결혼한 처지에 대한 약간의 실망을 녹여 버리기에 충분했다. 이제 그녀의 결혼은 관념적으로뿐 아니라 눈에 보이게도 그녀를 미들마치의 수준 위로 떠받쳐 주는 것 같았고, 퀄링엄과 주고받는 편지와 방문으로, 그리고 그에 따른 터셔스의 막연한 출세로 미래가 밝아 보였다. 더구나 아마도 대위의 권유였겠지만, 그의 출가한 누이인 멘건 부인이 하녀를 데리고 와서, 런던에서 돌아가는 길에 이틀 밤을 묵었다. 그러니 로저먼드가 음악에 공을 들이고 레이스를 신중히 고르는 데 정성을 쏟을 만한 가치가 분명히 있었다.\n\n리드게이트 대위로 말하자면, 낮은 이마와 한쪽으로 휜 매부리코와 다소 굼뜬 말투는, 군인다운 풍채와 콧수염이 없었더라면 어느 젊은 신사에게든 불리하게 작용했을 법한 것들이었다. 그 풍채와 콧수염이, 꽃처럼 어여쁜 금발 머리를 가진 어떤 여자들이 떠받드는 이른바 \"스타일\"을 그에게 부여해 주었던 것이다. 게다가 그는 중산층 신사 계층의 자질구레한 걱정에서 자유롭다는 점에서 고상한 가문 태생다운 면모를 지녔고, 여성의 매력에 대한 대단한 비평가였다. 로저먼드는 퀄링엄에서보다 지금 그의 찬탄을 더욱 기뻐했고, 그는 하루에 몇 시간씩 그녀와 시시덕거리며 보내기를 쉬운 일로 여겼다. 그 방문은 전체적으로 그가 누려 본 가장 즐거운 장난 가운데 하나였는데, 어쩌면 그 괴짜 사촌 터셔스가 자기를 쫓아내고 싶어 한다는 것을 눈치챘기 때문에 그 즐거움이 덜하지 않았을 것이다. 다만 (과장해서 말하자면) 정중한 환대를 그르치느니 차라리 죽고 싶어 했을 리드게이트는 그 혐오감을 억누른 채, 대체로 그 멋진 장교가 하는 말을 못 들은 척하면서 그에게 응답하는 일을 로저먼드에게 떠맡길 뿐이었다. 왜냐하면 그는 결코 질투하는 남편이 아니어서, 머리가 텅 빈 젊은 신사와 자리를 함께하느니 차라리 그를 아내와 단둘이 내버려 두는 편을 택했기 때문이다.\n\n\"터셔스, 저녁 식사 때 대위와 좀 더 이야기를 나누셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 어느 날 저녁, 그 중요한 손님이 그곳에 주둔한 동료 장교들을 만나러 로엄퍼드에 간 사이에 로저먼드가 말했다. \"당신은 정말이지 가끔 너무 멍해 보여요. 그를 바라보는 게 아니라 그의 머리를 꿰뚫고 그 뒤에 있는 무언가를 보고 있는 것 같다니까요.\"\n\n\"여보 로지, 설마 내가 저런 잘난 척하는 멍청이와 많은 이야기를 나누기를 바라는 건 아니겠지.\" 리드게이트가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"저 친구 머리가 깨지기라도 하면 흥미롭게 들여다볼지 몰라도, 그전에는 어림없어.\"\n\n\"왜 당신 사촌을 그토록 경멸하듯 말하는지 이해할 수가 없네요.\" 로저먼드가 말했는데, 그녀는 말하는 동안 손으로 바느질감을 놀리면서 약간의 멸시가 담긴 온화한 진지함을 보였다.\n\n\"래디슬로한테 당신 대위가 여태껏 만나 본 가장 지독한 따분쟁이가 아닌지 물어보구려. 그가 온 뒤로 래디슬로는 거의 이 집 발길을 끊다시피 했으니까.\"\n로저먼드는 래디슬로 씨가 왜 대위를 싫어하는지 자기가 완벽하게 알고 있다고 생각했다. 그는 질투하고 있었고, 그녀는 그가 질투하는 것이 마음에 들었다.\n\n\"별난 사람들에게 뭐가 맞는지는 알 수 없는 노릇이죠.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 제 생각에 리드게이트 대위는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 신사예요. 그리고 당신은 고드윈 경에 대한 예의로라도 그를 소홀히 대해서는 안 된다고 봐요.\"\n\"안 그래, 여보. 하지만 우리는 그를 위해 만찬을 여러 번 베풀었잖소. 게다가 그 친구는 제 마음대로 드나든다고. 나를 필요로 하지도 않고.\"\n\n\"그래도 그가 방에 있을 때는 좀 더 관심을 보일 수 있잖아요. 당신이 말하는 의미에서 그가 비범하게 영리한 사람은 아닐지 몰라요. 직업이 다르니까요. 하지만 당신이 그의 관심사에 대해 조금이라도 이야기를 나눈다면 당신에게도 더 좋을 거예요. 저는 그의 대화가 꽤 유쾌하다고 생각해요. 그리고 그는 결코 원칙 없는 사람이 아니에요.\"\n\"사실은 당신이 나도 그를 조금 닮기를 바라는 거로군, 로지.\" 리드게이트가 일종의 체념 어린 중얼거림으로, 그다지 다정하지도 않고 분명 유쾌하지도 않은 미소를 지으며 말했다. 로저먼드는 잠자코 다시 미소 짓지 않았다. 하지만 미소가 없어도 그녀의 얼굴에 흐르는 사랑스러운 곡선은 충분히 온화해 보였다.\n\n리드게이트의 그 말은, 그가 예전의 꿈나라에서 얼마나 멀리 떠나왔는지를 표시하는 서글픈 이정표 같은 것이었다. 그 꿈나라에서 로저먼드 빈시는, 자기 빗과 거울을 사용하고 오로지 남편의 흠모받는 지혜를 쉬게 하기 위해 노래를 부르는, 재주 많은 인어처럼 남편의 정신을 경배할 완벽한 여성의 표본으로 보였다. 그는 그 상상 속의 경배와, 한 남자의 재능이 그에게 명성을 가져다주기에—단춧구멍의 훈장이나 이름 앞에 붙는 경칭과 같기에—그 재능에 이끌리는 것을 구별하기 시작했다.\n\n로저먼드 역시 길을 떠나왔다고 짐작할 수도 있었을 것이다. 그녀는 네드 플림데일 씨의 핵심 없는 대화를 완전히 지겹게 여겼으니 말이다. 하지만 대다수 인간에게는 견딜 수 없는 어리석음과 전적으로 받아들일 만한 어리석음이 있는 법이다. 그렇지 않다면 사회적 유대가 도대체 어떻게 되겠는가? 리드게이트 대위의 어리석음은 은은한 향기를 풍겼고, \"스타일\"을 갖추고 있었으며, 좋은 억양으로 이야기했고, 고드윈 경과 가까운 친척이었다. 로저먼드는 그것을 꽤 유쾌하게 여겼고, 그 말투를 많이 받아들였다.\n그러므로 우리가 아는 대로 로저먼드는 승마를 좋아했으니, 리드게이트 대위가 다시 말을 타도록 그녀를 유혹할 만한 이유는 충분했다. 대위는 하인에게 말 두 필을 끌고 자기를 뒤따라와 \"그린 드래건\"에 묵으라고 일러 두었고, 그녀에게 그 회색 말을 타고 나가자고 청했다. 그는 그 말이 온순하며 숙녀를 태우도록 훈련되었음을 보장했다. 사실 그것은 누이를 위해 사들인 말로, 퀄링엄으로 데려가는 중이었다. 로저먼드는 처음에는 남편에게 알리지 않고 외출했다가 그가 돌아오기 전에 돌아왔다. 하지만 그 승마가 너무도 완벽한 성공이었고, 그 덕분에 자기 몸 상태가 훨씬 좋아졌다고 그녀가 단언했으므로, 그녀는 남편이 자기가 다시 말을 타러 가는 것에 동의해 주리라 전적으로 믿으며 그에게 그 사실을 알렸다.\n\n그러나 리드게이트는 단지 상처받은 정도가 아니었다. 그는 그녀가 자기 뜻을 물어보지도 않고 낯선 말에 위험을 무릅썼다는 사실에 완전히 어안이 벙벙했다. 다가올 일을 충분히 예고하는, 거의 천둥 같은 첫 경악의 외침을 쏟아 낸 뒤, 그는 잠시 말이 없었다.\n\n\"하지만 어쨌든 무사히 돌아왔으니.\" 마침내 그가 단호한 어조로 말했다. \"다시는 가지 않을 거요, 로지. 그렇게 알아 둬요. 세상에서 가장 얌전하고 가장 익숙한 말이라 해도 사고의 위험은 언제나 있는 법이오. 그래서 내가 당신더러 그 적갈색 말을 그만 타라고 한 걸 당신도 잘 알잖소.\"\n\n\"하지만 집 안에서도 사고의 위험은 있어요, 터셔스.\"\n\n\"여보, 말도 안 되는 소리 하지 마.\" 리드게이트가 애원하는 어조로 말했다. \"당신을 위해 판단해 줄 사람은 분명 나잖소. 다시는 가지 말라고 내가 말하는 것으로 충분하다고 보오.\"\n\n로저먼드는 저녁 식사 전에 머리를 매만지고 있었는데, 거울에 비친 그녀의 머리는 긴 목을 약간 옆으로 돌린 것 외에는 그 사랑스러움에 아무런 변화가 없었다. 리드게이트는 두 손을 주머니에 넣은 채 서성이다가 이제 그녀 가까이에서 멈춰 섰는데, 마치 어떤 다짐을 기다리는 것 같았다.\n\n\"여보, 땋은 머리를 좀 고정해 줘요.\" 로저먼드가 작게 한숨을 쉬며 두 팔을 떨어뜨리고 말했는데, 짐승처럼 우두커니 서 있는 남편이 부끄러워지게 만들 만한 모양새였다. 리드게이트는 크지만 곱게 빚어진 손가락을 지닌, 사내 중에서도 가장 솜씨 좋은 축에 들었기에 전에도 종종 그 땋은 머리를 고정해 주곤 했다. 그는 부드러운 머리 타래를 쓸어 올려 큼직한 빗으로 고정했다(사내가 이런 일에까지 이르다니!). 그러고 나서 섬세한 곡선을 그대로 드러낸 그 우아한 목덜미에 입 맞추는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 하지만 우리가 전에 했던 일을 다시 할 때는, 흔히 약간의 차이가 있는 법이다. 리드게이트는 여전히 화가 나 있었고, 자기 주장을 잊지 않고 있었다.\n\n\"대위한테 당신에게 말을 권하다니 분별이 없었다고 말해야겠소.\" 그가 물러나며 말했다.\n\n\"제발 그런 일은 하지 마세요, 터셔스.\" 로저먼드가 평소보다 한층 또렷한 말투로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"그건 저를 어린애 취급하는 거예요. 이 문제는 제게 맡기겠다고 약속해 주세요.\"\n\n그녀의 항변에는 어느 정도 진실이 있는 듯 보였다. 리드게이트는 무뚝뚝하게 복종하며 \"좋아\"라고 말했고, 그리하여 그 논쟁은 그녀가 그에게 약속하는 것이 아니라 그가 로저먼드에게 약속하는 것으로 끝났다.\n\n사실 그녀는 약속하지 않기로 작정하고 있었다. 로저먼드에게는 결코 충동적인 저항에 기운을 허비하지 않는 승리하는 고집이 있었다. 그녀가 하고 싶은 일은 그녀에게 곧 옳은 일이었고, 그녀의 모든 영리함은 그것을 할 수단을 손에 넣는 데로 향했다. 그녀는 다시 그 회색 말을 타고 나갈 작정이었고, 남편이 자리를 비운 다음 기회에 정말로 그렇게 했다. 자기에게 문제가 되지 않을 만큼 늦은 시점이 되기 전에는 남편이 알지 못하게 할 셈이었다. 유혹은 분명 컸다. 그녀는 그 운동을 무척 좋아했고, 멋진 말을 타고 그 옆에서 고드윈 경의 아들인 리드게이트 대위가 또 다른 멋진 말을 타고 가는 가운데, 남편이 아닌 누군가에게 그런 모습을 들킨다는 만족감은 결혼 전 그녀의 꿈만큼이나 좋은 것이었다. 게다가 그녀는 퀄링엄 가문과의 인연을 단단히 다지고 있었으니, 이는 분명 현명한 일임에 틀림없었다.\n\n하지만 그 온순한 회색 말은 핼셀 숲 가장자리에서 베어 넘어지는 나무가 쾅 하고 쓰러지는 소리에 미처 대비하지 못해 놀랐고, 그 바람에 로저먼드에게 더 큰 놀라움을 안겨, 결국 그녀가 아기를 잃는 결과로 이어졌다. 리드게이트는 그녀에게 화를 낼 수 없었지만, 대위에게는 다소 무뚝뚝하게 굴었고, 대위의 방문은 자연히 곧 끝났다.\n\n이후 그 일에 관한 모든 대화에서 로저먼드는, 그 승마가 아무런 영향을 끼치지 않았으며, 만약 자기가 집에 있었더라도 똑같은 증상이 나타나 똑같은 식으로 끝났을 거라고 온화하게 확신했다. 전에도 그와 비슷한 무언가를 느낀 적이 있었기 때문이라는 것이었다.\n\n리드게이트는 \"가엾고도 가엾은 내 사랑!\"이라고밖에 말할 수 없었다. 하지만 그는 이 온순한 사람의 무서운 끈질김을 속으로 의아해했다. 그의 안에서는 로저먼드에 대한 자신의 무력함을 깨닫는 놀라운 감각이 자라나고 있었다. 그의 뛰어난 지식과 정신력은, 그가 상상했던 것처럼 모든 일에서 상의할 성소가 되기는커녕, 실제적인 모든 문제에서 그저 한쪽으로 밀쳐졌다. 그는 로저먼드의 영리함을 바로 여자에게 어울리는 수용적인 종류의 것으로 여겨 왔다. 그는 이제 그 영리함이 어떤 것인지—그것이 어떤 형태로, 마치 동떨어지고 독립된 촘촘한 그물망처럼 굳어졌는지 알아 가기 시작했다. 자기 취향과 관심의 궤도 안에 놓인 원인과 결과를 보는 데 로저먼드보다 빠른 사람은 없었다. 그녀는 미들마치 사교계에서 리드게이트가 두드러진 존재임을 분명히 보았고, 그의 재능이 그를 출세시켰을 때 더욱 유쾌해질 사교적 결과들을 상상으로 계속 그려 나갈 수 있었다. 하지만 그녀에게 그의 직업적이고 과학적인 야망은, 마치 그것이 고약한 냄새가 나는 기름을 운 좋게 발견한 것에 지나지 않는 듯, 그 바람직한 결과들과 그 외의 다른 관계가 없었다. 그리고 자기와는 아무 상관도 없는 그 기름을 제쳐 둔다면, 그녀는 당연히 그의 의견보다 자기 의견을 더 믿었다. 리드게이트는 이번의 심각한 승마 사건뿐 아니라 수없이 사소한 문제에서도 애정이 그녀를 순순하게 만들지 못한다는 것을 알고 경악했다. 그는 애정이 거기 있다는 것을 의심하지 않았고, 자기가 그 애정을 밀어낼 어떤 일을 했으리라는 예감도 없었다. 그 자신으로 말하자면, 여전히 변함없이 다정하게 그녀를 사랑하며 그녀의 거부도 받아들이기로 마음먹을 수 있다고 스스로에게 말했다. 하지만—아아! 리드게이트는 무척 시달렸고, 자기 삶에 새로 들어온 요소들을 의식했다. 그것들은, 가장 맑은 물속에서 숨 쉬고 멱 감으며 햇빛에 반짝이는 먹이를 향해 쏜살같이 달려들곤 하던 생물에게 진흙탕이 흘러드는 것만큼이나 그에게 해로운 것이었다.\n\n로저먼드는 곧 자기 작업대 앞에서 그 어느 때보다 사랑스러워 보였고, 아버지의 사륜마차로 드라이브를 즐기며 퀄링엄에 초대받을지도 모른다고 생각했다. 그녀는 자기가 그곳 응접실에서 그 집안의 어떤 딸보다도 훨씬 더 정교한 장식물이라는 것을 알았고, 신사들이 그 점을 알고 있다고 생각하면서도, 부인들이 자기들이 능가당하는 꼴을 기꺼이 보려 할지는 아마 충분히 헤아리지 못했다.\n\n그녀에 대한 불안에서 벗어난 리드게이트는, 그녀가 속으로 그의 침울함이라 부르는 상태로 다시 빠져들었다. 그녀에게 그 이름은, 그가 자기 아닌 다른 주제에 사려 깊게 골몰하는 것뿐 아니라, 마치 평범한 모든 것에 쓴 약초가 섞이기라도 한 듯 그 모든 것을 못마땅해하는 불안한 이맛살을 가리키는 것이었다. 사실 그것은 그의 근심과 불길한 예감을 알려 주는 일종의 청우계 같은 것이었다. 이런 후자의 심적 상태에는 다른 여러 원인 가운데 하나가 있었는데, 그는 그녀의 건강과 기분에 영향을 줄까 봐 너그러우면서도 잘못된 마음으로 그것을 로저먼드에게 말하지 않고 있었다. 사실 그와 그녀 사이에는 서로의 정신적 궤도를 완전히 어긋나게 비껴가는 일이 있었으니, 이는 끊임없이 서로를 생각하는 사람들 사이에서조차 너무도 분명하게 가능한 일이다. 리드게이트에게는, 자기가 여러 달에 걸쳐 자기의 가장 좋은 의도와 가장 좋은 능력의 절반 이상을 로저먼드에 대한 다정함에 희생하며 보낸 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그녀의 자질구레한 요구와 방해를 짜증 내지 않고 견뎌 냈고, 무엇보다도 쓴 기색 한번 내비치지 않은 채, 점점 줄어드는 환상의 장막 너머로, 그의 직업과 과학 연구라는 한층 비개인적인 목적을 향한 열정에 그녀의 마음이 내미는 그 텅 비고 아무것도 비추지 않는 표면을 바라보며 견뎠다. 그는 이상적인 아내라면 그 열정을 어찌하든 숭고한 것으로 경배해야 한다고 상상했지만, 정작 그 이유는 조금도 알지 못했다. 그러나 그의 인내에는 자기 자신에 대한 불만이 뒤섞여 있었으니, 우리가 솔직해질 줄 안다면, 아내든 남편이든 불만스러운 처지에서 느끼는 쓰라림의 절반 이상을 만들어 내는 것이 바로 그 자기 불만임을 고백하게 될 것이다. 우리가 더 위대했더라면 환경이 우리에게 그토록 강하게 맞서지는 않았으리라는 것은 언제나 진실로 남는다. 리드게이트는 자기가 로저먼드에게 양보하는 것이 흔히, 느슨해진 결심이 무너지는 것에 지나지 않음을, 우리 삶의 일정 부분과 조화를 이루지 못하는 열정을 곧잘 사로잡는 슬금슬금 다가오는 마비 증세에 지나지 않음을 알고 있었다. 그리고 리드게이트의 열정 위에는 단순한 슬픔의 무게가 아니라, 모든 고상한 노력 위로 아이러니의 먹구름을 드리우는, 자질구레하고 비천한 근심거리가 따끔하게 짓누르고 있었다.\n\n이것이 바로 그가 지금껏 로저먼드에게 말하기를 삼가 온 근심이었다. 그리고 그는, 분명 이보다 덜 알기 어려운 곤경도 없을 텐데 그것이 그녀의 마음에는 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없는 듯하다는 사실을 다소 의아해하며 믿고 있었다. 그것은 손잡이가 빤히 드러난 추론이어서, 무심한 관찰자들조차 쉽게 끌어낼 수 있는 것, 곧 리드게이트가 빚을 지고 있다는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 자기가 날마다 그 늪 속으로 점점 더 깊이 빠져들고 있다는 생각을 오래도록 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 그 늪은 꽃과 푸른 풀로 어찌나 예쁘게 덮여 있는지 사람들을 그쪽으로 유혹하는 법이다. 사람이 그 속에서 턱밑까지 빠져드는 것은 놀랄 만큼 순식간의 일이다. 영혼 속에 우주의 구상을 품고 있던 사람이라 해도, 결국 어쩔 수 없이 빠져나올 궁리만을 주로 하게 되는 처지에 이르고 마는 것이다.\n\n열여덟 달 전만 해도 리드게이트는 가난했지만, 적은 액수의 돈을 애타게 아쉬워해 본 적이 없었고, 그런 돈을 얻으려 한 단계 몸을 낮추는 사람을 오히려 불같이 경멸했다. 그런데 이제 그는 단순한 부족보다 더 나쁜 무언가를 겪고 있었다. 없어도 되었을 물건을 잔뜩 사서 써 버리고는 값을 치를 수 없는데도 그 지불 요구가 절박해진 사람이 겪는, 천박하고 가증스러운 시련에 시달리고 있었던 것이다.\n\n이런 일이 어떻게 일어났는지는 많은 계산이나 물가에 관한 지식 없이도 쉽게 알 수 있다. 한 남자가 집을 마련하고 결혼을 준비하면서, 가구와 그 밖의 초기 비용이 자기가 지불할 자본보다 사오백 파운드나 더 든다는 것을 알게 되고, 한 해가 지난 끝에 가계 지출과 말과 그 밖의 잡다한 것들이 거의 천 파운드에 이르는데, 한편 옛 장부로 따져 연간 팔백 파운드의 가치가 있다던 개업의 수입은 여름 연못처럼 말라붙어 겨우 오백 파운드, 그것도 대부분 미수금으로 잡힌다면, 그가 그것을 마음에 두든 두지 않든 빚을 지고 있다는 것이 자명한 결론이다. 그때는 우리 시대보다 물가가 덜 비쌌고 지방 생활은 비교적 검소했다. 하지만 최근에 개업권을 사들였고, 말 두 필을 둬야 한다고 생각했으며, 아낌없이 차려진 식탁에, 생명 보험료를 내고 집과 정원에 비싼 집세를 내는 의사가 자기 지출이 수입의 두 배에 이르는 것을 얼마나 쉽게 발견할 수 있는지는, 이런 세부 사항을 자기가 헤아릴 일이 아니라고 여기지 않는 사람이라면 누구나 짐작할 수 있다. 어릴 적부터 사치스러운 가정에 익숙했던 로저먼드는, 살림을 잘하는 것이 그저 모든 것의 가장 좋은 것을 주문하는 데 있다고 생각했다. 그 밖의 다른 것은 \"성에 차지\" 않았던 것이다. 그리고 리드게이트는 \"어차피 할 바에는 제대로 해야 한다\"고 여겼다. 그는 그렇지 않고서 어떻게 살아갈 수 있는지 알지 못했다. 만약 가계 지출의 각 항목을 미리 그에게 일러 주었더라면, 그는 아마 \"그래 봐야 얼마 안 될 텐데\"라고 했을 것이고, 누군가가 어떤 특정 항목에서 절약하라고—이를테면 비싼 생선 대신 싼 생선을 쓰라고—권했더라면, 그것은 그에게 그저 푼돈을 아끼려는 인색한 발상으로만 보였을 것이다. 로저먼드는 리드게이트 대위의 방문 같은 계기가 없어도 손님을 초대하기를 좋아했고, 리드게이트는 그 손님들이 따분하다고 종종 생각하면서도 간섭하지 않았다. 이런 사교성은 직업적 신중함의 필수적인 일부로 여겨졌고, 또 그 접대는 마땅히 격에 맞아야 했다. 사실 리드게이트는 끊임없이 가난한 이들의 집을 찾아가 그들의 적은 형편에 맞게 식이 처방을 조정해 주었다. 하지만 저런! 사람이란 나란히 놓인 수많은 경험의 가닥들을 지니고 있으면서도 그것들을 서로 견주어 보지 않는다는 사실은, 이제는 더 이상 놀랄 일이 아니지 않은가—오히려 우리가 사람들에게 으레 기대하는 바가 아닌가? 지출이란—추함이나 잘못이 그렇듯이—우리가 거기에 우리 자신의 인격을 결부시키고, (우리 자신의 감각 안에서) 우리와 남들 사이에 뚜렷이 드러나는 그 커다란 차이로 그것을 가늠할 때, 전혀 새로운 것이 되어 버린다. 리드게이트는 자기가 옷차림에 무심하다고 믿었고, 옷의 효과를 계산하는 남자를 경멸했다. 자기에게 새 옷이 넉넉히 있는 것은 그에게 그저 당연한 일로만 여겨졌다. 그런 것들은 자연히 한 묶음씩 주문되는 법이었으니까. 그가 지금껏 빚의 성가신 독촉에 제동이 걸려 본 적이 없으며, 자기비판이 아니라 습관에 따라 걸어왔다는 것을 기억해 둬야 한다. 그러나 이제 그 제동이 걸린 것이다.\n\n그것이 새로웠던 까닭에 더욱 짜증스러웠다. 그는 자기의 모든 목적과 그토록 동떨어진, 자기가 몰두하고 싶어 하던 대상들과 그토록 가증스럽게 무관한 처지가 매복해 있다가 방심한 사이에 자기를 덮쳤다는 사실에 놀랐고, 또 진저리가 났다. 게다가 실제로 진 빚만 있는 것이 아니었다. 지금 처지에서는 그 빚을 점점 더 깊게 만들어 갈 수밖에 없다는 확실함도 있었다. 결혼 전에 빚진 청구서를 두고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 예상치 못한 일상 경비 때문에 갚지 못해 온 브래싱의 가구상 두 곳은, 그의 주의를 끌지 않을 수 없는 불쾌한 편지를 거듭 보내왔다. 강렬한 자존심을 지닌—호의를 청하거나 누군가에게 신세 지기를 싫어하는—리드게이트의 기질만큼 이런 일에 더 분통이 터지는 기질도 없었을 것이다. 그는 돈 문제에 관한 빈시 씨의 의향을 추측해 보는 것조차 경멸했고, 극단적인 상황이 아니고서는 결코 장인에게 부탁할 마음이 들지 않았을 것이다. 더구나 결혼 후로 그는 여러 간접적인 경로를 통해, 빈시 씨 자신의 형편도 번창하지 못하고 있으며 그에게서 도움을 기대하는 것이 못마땅하게 여겨지리라는 것을 이미 알게 되었던 터였다. 어떤 사람들은 친구들의 선뜻함을 쉽게 믿는다. 리드게이트는 자기 생애의 이전 시절에 그래야 할 필요가 있으리라고는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 돈을 빌리는 것이 자기에게 어떤 일일지 결코 생각해 보지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 이제 그 생각이 머릿속에 들어오자, 그는 다른 어떤 고생이라도 차라리 떠안는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다. 한편 그에게는 돈도, 돈이 들어올 가망도 없었고, 그의 개업은 더 수지맞게 되어 가지도 않았다.\n\n리드게이트가 지난 몇 달 동안 속내의 괴로움을 드러내는 온갖 기색을 다 억누를 수 없었던 것도 당연했다. 그리고 이제 로저먼드가 화사한 건강을 되찾고 있었으므로, 그는 자기 어려움을 그녀에게 전적으로 털어놓을까 곰곰이 생각했다. 상인들의 청구서를 새로이 접하게 되면서 그의 사고는 새로운 비교의 물길로 떠밀려 갔다. 그는 주문한 물건들 가운데 무엇이 필요하고 무엇이 불필요한지를 새로운 관점에서 따져 보기 시작했고, 생활 습관을 어느 정도 바꿔야만 한다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그런 변화를 로저먼드의 동의 없이 어떻게 이룰 수 있겠는가? 그 불쾌한 사실을 그녀에게 털어놓을 직접적인 계기가 그에게 닥쳐왔다.\n\n돈이 없었던 데다, 자기 처지의 사람이 도대체 무슨 담보를 제공할 수 있을지 은밀히 조언을 구한 끝에, 리드게이트는 자기 능력 안에서 댈 수 있는 단 하나의 확실한 담보를, 덜 강압적인 채권자에게 제시했다. 그 채권자는 은세공인이자 보석상이었는데, 가구상의 외상까지 자기가 떠맡되 일정 기간의 이자를 받기로 하는 데 동의했다. 필요한 담보는 그의 집 가구에 대한 매도 증서였고, 이는 사백 파운드에 못 미치는 빚에 대해 적당한 기간 동안 채권자를 안심시킬 만한 것이었다. 그리고 그 은세공인 도버 씨는 은제 식기 일부와 새것이나 다름없는 다른 물건들을 되받음으로써 빚을 줄여 줄 용의가 있었다. \"다른 물건들\"이란 보석류를, 더 구체적으로는 리드게이트가 신부 선물로 사 두었던 서른 파운드짜리 자수정 몇 개를 은근히 가리키는 표현이었다.\n\n그가 이 선물을 한 것이 현명했는지를 두고는 의견이 갈릴 수 있다. 어떤 이들은 그것이 리드게이트 같은 남자에게 기대할 만한 우아한 배려였으며, 골치 아픈 결과가 빚어진 잘못은 당시 지방 생활의 옹색한 궁색함에 있다고 생각할지 모른다. 그 생활은 자기 재산이 취향에 걸맞지 않은 전문직 사람들에게 아무런 편의도 제공하지 못했으니 말이다. 또 친구들에게 돈을 청하기를 꺼린 리드게이트의 우스꽝스러운 까다로움에 잘못이 있다고 여길 수도 있다.\n\n그러나 그가 식기를 마지막으로 주문하러 가던 그 화창한 아침에는, 그것이 그에게 조금도 대수롭지 않은 문제로 여겨졌다. 엄청나게 값비싼 다른 보석들 앞에서, 그것도 정확히 액수를 계산해 두지 않은 주문에 덧붙이는 것이었으니, 로저먼드의 목과 팔에 그토록 절묘하게 어울리는 장신구에 서른 파운드를 쓴다는 것이, 그것을 치를 현금이 애초에 없는 마당에 과하게 보일 리 없었던 것이다. 하지만 이 위기에 이르러 리드게이트의 상상은, 그 자수정을 다시 도버 씨의 재고로 되돌릴 수도 있다는 가능성에 머무르지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 그것을 로저먼드에게 제안한다는 생각만으로도 그는 움츠러들었지만 말이다. 지금껏 한 번도 더듬어 본 적 없는 결과들을 분별하도록 일깨워진 그는, 실험을 수행할 때라면 적용했을 그 엄격함의 일부(전부는 결코 아니었다)를 가지고 이 분별에 따라 행동하려 준비하고 있었다. 그는 브래싱에서 말을 타고 돌아오며 그 엄격함을 향해 마음을 다잡았고, 로저먼드에게 해야 할 이야기를 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n그가 집에 도착했을 때는 저녁이었다. 스물아홉 살에 많은 재능을 지닌 이 건장한 사내는 몹시 비참했다. 그는 자기가 심각한 실수를 저질렀다고 속으로 성내며 말하고 있지는 않았다. 하지만 그 실수는 인정된 만성 질환처럼 그의 안에서 작동하며, 그 불안한 독촉을 온갖 전망에 뒤섞고 모든 생각을 약하게 만들었다. 응접실로 가는 복도를 지나는데 피아노 소리와 노랫소리가 들렸다. 물론 래디슬로가 와 있었다. 윌이 도러시아와 헤어진 지 몇 주가 지났는데도, 그는 여전히 미들마치의 옛 자리를 지키고 있었다. 리드게이트는 대체로 래디슬로가 오는 것에 반대하지 않았지만, 바로 지금은 자기 집 난롯가를 비워 둔 채로 찾을 수 없다는 것이 못마땅했다. 그가 문을 열자 두 가수는 으뜸음을 향해 노래를 이어 가며, 눈을 들어 그를 바라보기는 했으나 그의 등장을 방해로 여기지는 않았다. 가엾은 리드게이트처럼 멍에에 쓸린 사내에게는, 고통스러운 하루가 아직 더 많은 고통을 예비하고 있다는 느낌으로 들어서는 마당에 두 사람이 자기를 향해 지저귀는 모습을 보는 것이 결코 마음을 달래 주는 일이 아니었다. 가뜩이나 평소보다 창백하던 그의 얼굴은 방을 가로질러 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지면서 잔뜩 찌푸려졌다.\n\n부를 소절이 세 마디밖에 남지 않았으니 양해되리라 여긴 두 가수는 이제 돌아섰다.\n\n\"잘 지냈나, 리드게이트?\" 윌이 악수하러 다가오며 말했다.\n\n리드게이트는 그의 손을 잡았지만 굳이 말을 할 필요는 느끼지 않았다.\n\n\"식사는 하셨어요, 터셔스? 훨씬 일찍 오실 줄 알았는데.\" 로저먼드가 말했는데, 그녀는 이미 남편이 \"끔찍한 기분\"임을 알아챈 터였다. 그녀는 말하면서 늘 앉던 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"식사는 했소. 차나 좀 마셨으면 좋겠군.\" 리드게이트가 무뚝뚝하게 말했는데, 여전히 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 앞으로 뻗은 자기 다리를 빤히 내려다보고 있었다.\n\n윌은 더 말할 필요도 없을 만큼 눈치가 빨랐다. \"전 가 보겠습니다.\" 그가 모자를 집으며 말했다.\n\n\"차가 오고 있어요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"제발 가지 마세요.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 리드게이트가 따분해하고 있어요.\" 윌이 말했다. 그는 로저먼드보다 리드게이트를 더 잘 이해했기에, 바깥일에서 비롯된 짜증의 원인을 쉽게 상상하며 그의 태도에 기분 상해 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그러니 더더욱 당신이 계셔야죠.\" 로저먼드가 장난스럽게, 더없이 가벼운 어조로 말했다. \"이이는 저녁 내내 저한테 한마디도 안 할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"아니, 로저먼드, 할 거요.\" 리드게이트가 그 힘찬 바리톤으로 말했다. \"당신과 의논할 진지한 일이 좀 있소.\"\n\n리드게이트가 의도했던 것과 이보다 덜 닮은 화제의 도입은 없었을 것이다. 하지만 그녀의 무심한 태도가 너무도 화를 돋우었던 것이다.\n\n\"거 보세요!\" 윌이 말했다. \"전 직공 학교 회의에 갑니다. 안녕히 계세요.\" 그러고는 재빨리 방을 나갔다.\n\n로저먼드는 남편을 바라보지 않았으나, 이윽고 일어나 찻쟁반 앞 자기 자리에 앉았다. 그녀는 남편이 이토록 무뚝뚝한 모습은 처음 본다고 생각하고 있었다. 리드게이트는 검은 눈을 그녀에게 돌려, 그녀가 가느다란 손가락으로 차 도구를 섬세하게 다루는 모습을, 얼굴의 곡선 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 바로 앞의 물건들을 바라보면서도 불쾌한 태도를 지닌 모든 사람을 향한 형언할 수 없는 항의를 그 분위기에 담고 있는 모습을 지켜보았다. 한순간 그는, 한때 민첩하고 영민한 감수성의 표시로 해석했던 그 요정 같은 몸매에서 새로이 드러나는 이 여성적 무감각의 형태에 대한 불현듯한 사색에 잠겨 자기 상처의 느낌을 잊었다. 로저먼드를 바라보는 동안 그의 마음은 로르에게로 흘끗 되돌아갔고, 그는 속으로 말했다. \"그녀라면 내가 자기를 싫증 나게 했다고 나를 죽이려 들었을까?\" 그리고 이어서, \"여자들은 다 그런 식이지.\" 그러나 인간에게 실수에 있어서만큼은 말 못 하는 짐승보다 그토록 우월한 지위를 안겨 주는 이 일반화의 능력은, 또 다른 여인의 행동에서 받은 경이로운 인상에 대한 리드게이트의 기억에 곧바로 가로막혔다. 그것은 리드게이트가 남편을 진료하기 시작했을 때 도러시아가 남편을 두고 보인 표정과 감정 어린 어조에서, 그리고 충실함과 연민의 갈망 외에 자기 안의 모든 충동을 억눌러야만 할 것 같던 그 남자를 가장 잘 위로할 방도를 가르쳐 달라던 그녀의 열정적인 외침에서 받은 인상이었다. 차가 우러나는 동안 이 되살아난 인상들이 리드게이트의 마음속에서 빠르게, 또 꿈결처럼 잇따라 떠올랐다. 그는 도러시아가 \"조언해 주세요—제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 생각해 주세요—그이는 평생을 애쓰며 앞날을 바라보아 왔어요. 그이는 다른 무엇에도 마음을 쓰지 않아요—저도 다른 무엇에도 마음을 쓰지 않고요\"라고 말하는 소리를 들으며, 그 공상의 마지막 순간에 눈을 감고 있었다.\n\n깊은 영혼을 지닌 여성성의 그 목소리는, 죽어 왕홀을 든 천재들의 불을 지피는 착상들이 그의 안에 남아 있었듯 그의 안에 남아 있었다(인간의 정신과 그 결론을 다스리는, 고귀하게 느끼는 천재성 또한 있지 않은가?). 그 음색은 그가 멀어져 가고 있던 음악이었다. 로저먼드가 그 은빛의 무심한 어조로 \"차 여기 있어요, 터셔스\"라고 말하며 그 곁의 작은 탁자에 찻잔을 내려놓고 그를 보지도 않은 채 제자리로 물러났을 때, 그는 실은 잠깐 졸음에 빠져 있었다. 리드게이트는 그녀에게 무감각함을 돌리는 데 너무 성급했다. 그녀는 그녀 나름대로 충분히 예민했고 오래가는 인상을 받았다. 지금 그녀가 받은 인상은 모욕감과 반감의 인상이었다. 그러나 로저먼드는 얼굴을 찌푸리지도 않았고 목소리를 높인 적도 없었다. 그녀는 누구도 자기에게서 정당하게 흠잡을 수 없으리라고 꽤 확신하고 있었다.\n\n어쩌면 리드게이트와 그녀가 서로를 이토록 멀게 느낀 적은 전에 없었을 것이다. 하지만 그가 비록 그 불쑥 던진 말로 이미 시작하지 않았더라도, 자기 고백을 미룰 수 없는 강력한 이유가 있었다. 실제로 자기를 위해 그녀를 좀 더 민감하게 일깨우고 싶은 성난 욕망—그 욕망이 그로 하여금 성급히 입을 열게 했던 것인데—이 일부, 그녀가 겪을 고통을 내다보는 그 자신의 고통에 여전히 뒤섞여 있었다. 하지만 그는 쟁반이 치워지고 촛불이 밝혀지며 저녁의 고요가 기대할 만해질 때까지 기다렸다. 그 간격은 밀려났던 다정함이 다시 옛 물길로 돌아올 시간을 남겨 주었다. 그는 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"여보 로지, 일감은 내려놓고 이리 와서 내 옆에 앉아요.\" 그가 탁자를 밀어내고 팔을 뻗어 의자를 자기 옆으로 끌어당기며 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n로저먼드는 그 말에 따랐다. 투명하고 옅게 물든 모슬린 옷을 두른 채 그에게 다가오는 그녀의 날씬하면서도 둥근 몸매는 그 어느 때보다 우아해 보였다. 그의 옆에 앉아 한 손을 그의 의자 팔걸이에 얹고는 마침내 그를 바라보며 그의 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그녀의 섬세한 목과 뺨과 또렷이 빚어진 입술은, 봄철과 유년기와 온갖 달콤한 싱그러움에서처럼 마음을 울리는 그 티 없는 아름다움을 그 어느 때보다 가득 머금고 있었다. 그것은 지금 리드게이트의 마음을 울렸고, 그녀를 향한 사랑의 초기 순간들을 이 깊은 곤경의 위기에 들끓는 다른 모든 기억과 뒤섞었다. 그는 자기의 큼직한 손을 그녀의 손 위에 부드럽게 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"여보!\" 애정이 그 말에 실어 주는, 여운을 끄는 어조였다. 로저먼드 역시 여전히 그 똑같은 과거의 힘 아래 있었고, 그녀의 남편은 여전히 일부, 그의 칭찬이 기쁨을 일으키곤 했던 그 리드게이트였다. 그녀는 그의 이마에서 머리카락을 가볍게 쓸어 넘기고는 다른 한 손을 그의 손 위에 얹었고, 그를 용서하고 있음을 의식했다.\n\n\"당신을 아프게 할 이야기를 해야겠소, 로지. 하지만 부부가 함께 생각해야만 하는 일들이 있는 법이오. 내가 돈이 궁하다는 걸 당신도 이미 알아챘으리라고 보오만.\"\n\n리드게이트는 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 로저먼드는 목을 돌려 벽난로 위 선반의 꽃병을 바라보았다.\n\n\"우리가 결혼하기 전에 마련해야 했던 모든 물건의 값을 다 치를 수가 없었고, 그 뒤로도 부득이 떠안아야 했던 지출이 있었소. 그 결과 브래싱에 큰 빚이 생겼는데—삼백팔십 파운드라오—한동안 나를 짓눌러 왔지. 게다가 사실 우리는 날마다 더 깊이 빠져들고 있소. 다른 이들이 돈을 필요로 한다고 해서 사람들이 내게 더 빨리 돈을 갚는 건 아니니까. 당신이 몸이 좋지 않은 동안에는 이걸 당신에게 숨기려 애썼소. 하지만 이제 우리는 함께 이 일을 생각해야 하고, 당신이 나를 도와줘야 하오.\"\n\n\"제가 뭘 할 수 있겠어요, 터셔스?\" 로저먼드가 다시 그에게 눈을 돌리며 말했다. 그 네 마디의 짧은 말은, 모든 언어의 수많은 다른 말들과 마찬가지로, 다양한 음성의 굴절을 통해 속수무책의 막막함에서부터 빈틈없이 따지는 논리적 통찰에 이르기까지, 자기를 온전히 바치는 동지애에서부터 더없이 무심한 초연함에 이르기까지, 모든 마음 상태를 표현할 수 있는 말이다. 로저먼드의 가느다란 발화는 \"제가—뭘—할 수 있겠어요!\"라는 말에, 그 말이 담을 수 있는 한껏의 무심함을 실었다. 그것은 들끓던 리드게이트의 다정함 위로 치명적인 한기처럼 떨어졌다. 그는 분개하여 노발대발하지 않았다. 너무도 서글픈 마음의 가라앉음을 느꼈던 것이다. 그리고 그가 다시 입을 열었을 때는, 억지로 임무를 다하려는 사람의 어조에 더 가까웠다.\n\n\"당신이 알아야만 하는 까닭은, 내가 한동안 담보를 제공해야 하고, 누군가 와서 가구 목록을 작성해야 하기 때문이오.\"\n\n로저먼드의 얼굴이 새빨개졌다. \"아버지께 돈을 부탁하지 않으셨어요?\" 그녀가 입을 떼자마자 말했다.\n\"안 했소.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 부탁해야겠네요!\" 그녀가 자기 손을 리드게이트의 손에서 빼내며 말했고, 일어나서 그에게서 두 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에 섰다.\n\n\"안 돼, 로지.\" 리드게이트가 단호하게 말했다. \"그러기엔 너무 늦었소. 목록 작성은 내일 시작될 거요. 그저 담보라는 걸 기억하구려. 그건 아무것도 달라지게 하지 않소. 일시적인 일이오. 내가 직접 말하기로 정하지 않는 한, 당신 아버지가 이 일을 알아서는 안 된다고 나는 고집하겠소.\" 리드게이트가 한층 단호한 강조로 덧붙였다.\n\n이것은 분명 야박한 말이었다. 하지만 로저먼드는 그녀가 조용하고 한결같은 불복종으로 무슨 짓을 할지에 관한 좋지 않은 예상으로 그를 도로 내몰았던 것이다. 그녀에게는 그 야박함이 용서할 수 없는 것으로 여겨졌다. 그녀는 우는 버릇이 없었고 우는 것을 싫어했지만, 이제 그녀의 턱과 입술이 떨리기 시작하더니 눈물이 솟아올랐다. 어쩌면 리드게이트로서는, 외적인 물질적 어려움과 굴욕적인 결과에 대한 자기 자신의 자존심 어린 저항이라는 이중의 압박 아래서, 이 갑작스러운 시련이 응석 외에는 아무것도 몰랐고 그 꿈이라곤 자기 취향에 한층 꼭 맞는 새로운 응석뿐이었던 한 어린 사람에게 어떤 것일지를 온전히 상상하기란 불가능했을 것이다. 하지만 그는 그녀를 되도록 아껴 주고 싶었고, 그녀의 눈물은 그의 가슴을 후벼 팠다. 그는 곧바로 다시 입을 열 수 없었다. 그러나 로저먼드는 계속 흐느끼지 않았다. 그녀는 자기 동요를 가라앉히려 애쓰며 눈물을 닦았고, 계속해서 앞에 있는 벽난로 선반을 바라보았다.\n\n\"슬퍼하지 마요, 여보.\" 리드게이트가 눈을 들어 그녀를 올려다보며 말했다. 그녀가 이 곤경의 순간에 자기에게서 떨어져 가기를 택했다는 사실이 모든 말을 더 하기 어렵게 만들었지만, 그는 기어이 말을 이어 가야 했다. \"우리는 필요한 일을 할 수 있도록 마음을 다잡아야 하오. 잘못한 사람은 나요. 이런 식으로 살 형편이 못 된다는 걸 진작 봤어야 했소. 하지만 내 개업에는 여러 가지가 불리하게 작용했고, 정말이지 지금은 그게 바닥까지 내려앉았소. 다시 회복할 수도 있겠지만, 그동안 우리는 졸라매야 하오—생활 방식을 바꿔야 하오. 우리는 이 일을 헤쳐 나갈 거요. 이 담보를 제공하고 나면 주위를 둘러볼 시간이 생길 거고, 당신은 워낙 영리하니 마음먹고 살림을 꾸려 나간다면 나를 조심성 있게 길들여 줄 거요. 나는 값을 맞추는 데 생각 없는 못난이였소—하지만 자, 여보, 앉아서 나를 용서해 줘요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 발톱을 지녔으면서도 이성 또한 지닌 짐승처럼 멍에 아래 목을 숙이고 있었으니, 그 이성이란 흔히 우리를 온순함으로 굴복시키는 법이다. 그가 애원하는 어조로 마지막 말을 했을 때, 로저먼드는 그의 곁 의자로 돌아왔다. 그의 자책은 그가 자기 의견에 귀 기울이리라는 약간의 희망을 그녀에게 안겨 주었고, 그녀는 말했다.\n\n\"목록 작성하는 걸 미룰 수는 없나요? 내일 사람들이 오면 돌려보낼 수 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"돌려보내지 않을 거요.\" 리드게이트가 단호함을 다시 드러내며 말했다. 설명한들 무슨 소용이 있을까?\n\n\"우리가 미들마치를 떠난다면요? 그러면 당연히 경매가 있을 테고, 그것도 마찬가지로 효과가 있겠죠.\"\n\n\"하지만 우린 미들마치를 떠나지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"분명히 말하지만, 터셔스, 그러는 편이 훨씬 나을 거예요. 우리가 런던으로 가면 안 되나요? 아니면 당신 가족이 알려진 더럼 근처라든가요?\"\n\n\"돈 없이는 우린 아무 데도 갈 수 없소, 로저먼드.\"\n\n\"당신 친구들은 당신이 돈 없이 지내기를 바라지 않을 거예요. 그리고 이 가증스러운 상인들도, 당신이 제대로 사정을 설명한다면 분명 그걸 이해하고 기다려 줄 수 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그건 부질없는 소리요, 로저먼드.\" 리드게이트가 성내며 말했다. \"당신은 당신이 이해하지 못하는 문제에서 내 판단을 받아들이는 법을 배워야 하오. 나는 필요한 조처를 해 두었고, 그것은 실행되어야만 하오. 친구들로 말하자면, 나는 그들에게 아무런 기대도 없으며, 그들에게 무엇도 청하지 않을 거요.\"\n\n로저먼드는 미동도 없이 앉아 있었다. 그녀 마음속의 생각은, 리드게이트가 이렇게 행동할 줄 알았더라면 결코 그와 결혼하지 않았으리라는 것이었다.\n\n\"이제 우리는 불필요한 말에 시간을 허비할 여유가 없소, 여보.\" 리드게이트가 다시 다정해지려 애쓰며 말했다. \"당신과 함께 고려하고 싶은 몇 가지 세부 사항이 있소. 도버는 은제 식기를 상당 부분 되받겠다고 하고, 보석류도 우리가 원하는 건 무엇이든 받겠다고 하오. 정말 무척 합당하게 처신하는 거지.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는 숟가락이랑 포크도 없이 지내야 하는 건가요?\" 로저먼드가 말했는데, 그 가느다란 발화만큼이나 입술 자체도 얇아지는 것 같았다. 그녀는 더 이상 저항하거나 제안하지 않기로 작정한 터였다.\n\n\"오, 아니오, 여보!\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"하지만 여길 봐요.\" 그가 주머니에서 종이를 꺼내 펼치며 말을 이었다. \"여기 도버의 명세서요. 보다시피 내가 몇몇 품목에 표시를 해 두었는데, 이것들을 돌려주면 액수가 서른 파운드 넘게 줄어들 거요. 보석류에는 아무것도 표시하지 않았소.\" 리드게이트는 사실 이 보석 문제가 자기에게 무척 쓰라리게 느껴졌지만, 엄정한 논리로 그 감정을 이겨 낸 터였다. 그는 로저먼드에게 자기가 준 특정 선물을 돌려 달라고 제안할 수는 없었다. 하지만 그는 도버의 제안을 그녀 앞에 내놓는 것이 자기의 도리라고 스스로에게 일렀고, 그녀의 내적인 움직임이 그 일을 수월하게 해 줄지도 모른다고 여겼다.\n\n\"제가 봐야 소용없어요, 터셔스.\" 로저먼드가 차분하게 말했다. \"당신이 원하는 걸 돌려주시면 되죠.\" 그녀는 그 종이로 눈을 돌리려 하지 않았고, 리드게이트는 머리카락 뿌리까지 붉어진 채 그것을 도로 거두어 무릎 위에 떨어뜨렸다. 그러는 사이에 로저먼드는 리드게이트를 무력하게, 어리둥절하게 내버려 둔 채 조용히 방을 나갔다. 그녀는 돌아오지 않으려는 걸까? 마치 그들이 서로 다른 종(種)에다 이해관계가 어긋나는 생물이기라도 한 듯, 그녀가 그와 자신을 동일시한 것이 더 이상 아닌 듯 보였다. 그는 머리를 홱 젖히고 일종의 분풀이로 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣었다. 그래도 과학은 여전히 있었다—여전히 힘써 일할 만한 좋은 목표들이 있었다. 그는 아직 한번 더 힘껏 잡아당겨야 했다—다른 만족들이 사라져 가고 있는 만큼 더욱 힘차게.\n\n그러나 문이 열리고 로저먼드가 다시 들어왔다. 그녀는 자수정이 담긴 가죽 상자와, 다른 상자들이 들어 있는 자그마한 장식 바구니를 들고 있었고, 그것들을 자기가 앉았던 의자 위에 내려놓으며 흠잡을 데 없이 단정한 분위기로 말했다.\n\n\"이게 당신이 제게 주신 보석 전부예요. 이 가운데 원하는 걸 돌려주시면 되고, 은제 식기도 마찬가지예요. 물론 당신은 제가 내일 집에 있으리라 기대하지는 않으시겠죠. 저는 아버지 댁에 가 있을 거예요.\"\n\n리드게이트가 그녀에게 던진 눈길은 많은 여자들에게는 성난 눈길보다 더 무서웠을 것이다. 그 눈길에는 그녀가 둘 사이에 두고 있는 거리를 절망적으로 받아들이는 빛이 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"그러면 언제 다시 돌아오겠소?\" 그가 어조에 쓰라린 날을 세우며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 저녁에요. 물론 어머니께는 이 일을 입에 올리지 않을 거예요.\" 로저먼드는 어떤 여자도 자기보다 더 나무랄 데 없이 처신할 수는 없으리라고 확신했다. 그러고는 자기 작업대로 가서 앉았다. 리드게이트는 잠시 한두 순간 깊이 생각하며 앉아 있었고, 그 결과 어조에 예전의 감정을 얼마간 실어 말했다.\n\n\"이제 우리는 부부가 되었으니, 로지, 첫 곤경이 닥쳤다고 나를 혼자 내버려 둬서는 안 되오.\"\n\n\"물론 그러지 않을 거예요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"저는 제가 해야 할 모든 일을 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"이 일을 하인들에게 맡겨 두거나, 내가 그들에게 이 일을 말해야 하는 건 옳지 않소. 게다가 나는 부득이 외출해야 하오—얼마나 일찍일지 모르겠소. 이런 돈 문제의 굴욕에서 당신이 움츠러드는 건 나도 이해하오. 하지만 여보 로저먼드, 당신 못지않게 나도 절감하는 자존심의 문제로 따지자면, 이 일을 우리가 직접 처리하고 하인들에게는 되도록 적게 보이게 하는 편이 분명 더 나으오. 그리고 당신은 내 아내이니, 내 치욕에 당신이 한몫을 지는 걸—치욕이 있다면 말이지만—막을 길은 없소.\"\n\n로저먼드는 곧바로 대답하지 않았으나, 마침내 말했다. \"좋아요, 집에 있을게요.\"\n\n\"이 보석들에는 손대지 않겠소, 로지. 도로 가져가요. 하지만 우리가 돌려줄 수 있는 은제 식기 목록을 적어 두겠소. 그러면 그건 곧바로 꾸려서 보낼 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"하인들이 그건 알게 되겠네요.\" 로저먼드가 아주 살짝 비꼬는 기색을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 우리는 어쩔 수 없는 일로 불쾌한 것들을 좀 마주해야 하오. 잉크가 어디 있더라?\" 리드게이트가 일어서서, 자기가 글을 쓰려는 더 큰 탁자 위로 명세서를 던지며 말했다.\n\n로저먼드는 잉크병을 가지러 갔고, 그것을 탁자에 내려놓은 뒤 돌아서려는데, 바로 곁에 서 있던 리드게이트가 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 여보, 형편이 닿는 데까지 최선을 다해 봅시다. 우리가 인색하게 굴고 일일이 따져야 하는 건 그저 한동안일 거요. 키스해 줘요.\"\n\n그가 타고난 따스한 마음은 좀처럼 꺼지지 않았다. 그리고 경험 없는 처녀가 자기와 결혼함으로써 곤경에 빠졌다는 사실을 절절히 느끼는 것은 남편으로서의 사내다움의 일부이다. 그녀는 그의 키스를 받고 희미하게 그것을 되돌려 주었으며, 이런 식으로 한동안 화합의 외양이 회복되었다. 하지만 리드게이트는, 지출에 관한, 또 생활 방식을 완전히 바꿔야 할 필요에 관한 피할 수 없는 앞으로의 의논을 두려움으로 내다보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LIX.\n\n마음 내키면 바람을 쐬러 밖으로 떠도노라고.\n보라! 그녀의 천사 같은 얼굴 곁에 떠도는\n\n창백한 입술의 공기 같은 형체가 속삭이며\n그 작은 조가비, 그녀의 귓속에 부추김을 흘려 넣는다.\n\n소식이라는 것은 흔히, 꿀벌이 제 좋아하는 꽃의 꿀을 찾아 윙윙거리며 날아다니다가 (제 몸에 얼마나 가루가 묻었는지도 모른 채) 옮겨 나르는 그 꽃가루처럼 무심하면서도 효과적으로 퍼져 나간다. 이 멋들어진 비유는 프레드 빈시와 관련된 것인데, 그는 그날 저녁 로윅 사제관에서 부인들이 한 가지 소식을 두고 활기차게 토론하는 것을 들었다. 그 집의 늙은 하녀가 탠트립에게서 들은 소식으로, 캐소본 씨가 죽기 얼마 전 작성한 유언 추가 조항에서 래디슬로 씨를 기묘하게 언급했다는 내용이었다. 위니프리드 양은 자기 오빠가 그 사실을 이미 알고 있었다는 데 깜짝 놀라며, 캠든은 어떤 일을 알면서도 말하지 않는 데서는 가장 놀라운 사람이라고 했다. 그러자 메리 가스는 그 추가 조항이 어쩌면 거미의 습성에 관한 이야기와 뒤섞였을 거라고 했는데, 위니프리드 양은 그 말을 도무지 들으려 하지 않았다. 페어브러더 부인은 그 소식이 그들이 래디슬로 씨를 로윅에서 단 한 번밖에 보지 못한 일과 무언가 관계가 있을 거라고 생각했고, 노블 양은 가엾다는 듯 작게 야옹대는 소리를 여러 번 냈다.\n\n프레드는 래디슬로나 캐소본 집안에 대해 아는 것도 거의 없고 관심도 더더욱 없었으므로, 그 토론이 그의 머릿속에 다시 떠오른 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그러던 어느 날, 어머니의 부탁으로 지나는 길에 전갈을 전하려고 로저먼드를 찾아갔다가, 마침 래디슬로가 돌아가는 것을 보게 되었다. 결혼이 로저먼드를 오빠들과 부딪치는 불쾌함에서 떼어 놓은 지금, 그리고 특히 프레드가 그녀로서는 어리석을뿐더러 비난받아 마땅하다고 여기는 행보, 즉 성직의 길을 포기하고 가스 씨 같은 사람이 하는 일에 뛰어든 지금, 두 사람은 서로 나눌 말이 별로 없었다. 그래서 프레드는 그가 대수롭지 않다고 여기는 소식을 즐겨 화제로 삼았고, \"그 젊은 래디슬로 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데\" 하며 로윅 사제관에서 들은 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n그런데 리드게이트는 페어브러더 씨처럼 말하는 것보다 훨씬 많이 알고 있었고, 한번 윌과 도러시아의 관계에 대해 생각하기 시작하자 그의 추측은 사실을 넘어서까지 뻗어 나갔다. 그는 양쪽 모두에 열정적인 애착이 있다고 상상했고, 이것은 가십거리로 삼기에는 너무도 심각한 일이라고 느꼈다. 그는 캐소본 부인을 언급했을 때 윌이 보였던 짜증을 기억했고, 그래서 더욱 조심스러웠다. 대체로 그의 추측은, 그가 알고 있던 사실에 더해져, 래디슬로에 대한 그의 호의와 관용을 키웠으며, 떠나겠다고 말해 놓고도 미들마치에 머물게 한 그 우유부단함을 이해하게 해 주었다. 그가 이 문제에 대해 그녀에게 말하고 싶은 충동을 전혀 느끼지 않았다는 것은 리드게이트의 마음과 로저먼드의 마음 사이에 놓인 거리를 의미하는 일이었다. 사실 그는 윌에 대한 그녀의 입조심을 그다지 신뢰하지 않았다. 그리고 그 점에서 그는 옳았다. 다만 그녀의 마음이 어떤 식으로 작동하여 그녀로 하여금 말하도록 부추길지는 그도 짐작하지 못했다.\n\n그녀가 프레드의 소식을 리드게이트에게 옮기자, 그가 말했다. \"로지, 래디슬로한테 아주 희미한 암시조차도 흘리지 않도록 조심해요. 그 사람은 당신이 모욕이라도 한 것처럼 발끈할 가능성이 높으니까. 물론 괴로운 일이지.\"\n\n로저먼드는 목을 돌리고 머리를 매만지며, 평온한 무관심의 화신 같은 표정을 지었다. 그러나 다음번에 리드게이트가 자리를 비웠을 때 윌이 찾아오자, 그녀는 그가 으름장 놓던 대로 런던에 가지 않은 것을 두고 짓궂게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"저는 다 알고 있어요. 제겐 비밀을 일러 주는 작은 새가 한 마리 있거든요.\" 그녀는 부지런히 놀리는 손가락 사이로 바느질감을 높이 들어 올린 채, 머리를 매우 예쁘게 갸웃거리며 말했다. \"이 동네에 강력한 자석이 하나 있더군요.\"\n\n\"물론 있고말고요. 그건 당신만큼 잘 아는 사람이 없지요.\" 윌은 가볍게 정중한 척하며 말했지만, 속으로는 화낼 준비를 하고 있었다.\n\n\"정말이지 더없이 매력적인 연애담이에요. 캐소본 씨는 질투를 했고, 캐소본 부인이 그토록 결혼하고 싶어 할 사람이, 또 그분과 그토록 결혼하고 싶어 할 어떤 신사가 달리 없으리라는 걸 내다보았죠. 그러고는 그분이 만약 그 신사와 결혼하면 재산을 몰수당하게 만들어 모든 걸 망쳐 놓을 계획을 세웠고요. 그리고 그다음엔, 그다음엔, 그다음엔, 아, 결말이 틀림없이 더할 나위 없이 낭만적일 거예요.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 그게 무슨 소리예요?\" 윌은 얼굴과 귀까지 벌게진 채 말했고, 격렬하게 흔들리기라도 한 듯 그의 이목구비가 일그러지는 것 같았다. \"농담은 그만두고, 무슨 뜻인지 말해 봐요.\"\n\n\"정말로 모르세요?\" 로저먼드는 이제 더는 장난스럽지 않은 태도로, 효과를 자아내려고 그저 말해 주고 싶은 마음뿐이었다.\n\n\"몰라요!\" 그가 조바심을 내며 대답했다.\n\n\"캐소본 씨가 유언장에, 만약 캐소본 부인이 당신과 결혼하면 부인의 재산을 전부 몰수당하게 된다고 남겨 놓았다는 걸 모르세요?\"\n\n\"그게 사실인 걸 어떻게 알죠?\" 윌이 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"제 오빠 프레드가 페어브러더 댁에서 들었어요.\" 윌은 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 부인이 재산보다 당신을 더 좋아할 거예요.\" 로저먼드가 멀찍이서 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"제발 그 일에 대해선 더 말하지 말아요.\" 윌이 평소의 가벼운 목소리와는 전혀 다른, 쉰 듯한 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"그건 그분에게도 나에게도 더러운 모욕이에요.\" 그러고는 멍하니 앉아 앞을 바라보았지만, 아무것도 보고 있지 않았다.\n\n\"이제 저한테 화가 나셨군요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"저한테 악감정을 품으시다니 너무하세요. 말해 드렸으니 저한테 고마워하셔야죠.\"\n\n\"고마워하고 있어요.\" 윌은 질문에 대답하는 몽상가 특유의, 그 두 갈래로 갈린 영혼으로 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"저는 그 결혼 소식을 듣게 되리라 기대하고 있어요.\" 로저먼드가 장난스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"절대로! 당신은 절대로 그 결혼 소식을 듣지 못할 거요!\"\n\n그 말을 격하게 내뱉고서 윌은 일어나, 여전히 몽유병자 같은 태도로 로저먼드에게 손을 내밀고는 떠나 버렸다.\n\n그가 가 버리자, 로저먼드는 의자에서 일어나 방 저쪽 끝으로 걸어가더니, 그곳에 다다라 작은 장식장에 몸을 기댄 채 지친 듯 창밖을 내다보았다. 그녀는 권태에 짓눌려 있었고, 또 여자들의 마음속에서 끊임없이 하찮은 질투로 변해 가는 그 불만에 짓눌려 있었다. 그것은 어떤 실제적인 권리에서 비롯된 것도 아니요, 자기중심주의의 막연한 까다로움보다 더 깊은 정념에서 솟아난 것도 아니었으나, 그러면서도 말뿐만 아니라 행동까지 부추길 수 있는 것이었다. \"정말이지 그다지 마음 쓸 만한 일이 없어.\" 가엾은 로저먼드는 속으로 그렇게 생각하며, 자기에게 편지 한 통 보내지 않는 콸링엄의 식구들을 떠올렸다. 그리고 어쩌면 테르티우스가 집에 돌아오면 지출 문제로 자기를 들볶을 거라는 생각도 했다. 그녀는 이미 그를 거역하고 몰래 아버지에게 도움을 청한 적이 있었는데, 아버지는 \"내가 오히려 도움이 필요할 판이다\"라는 말로 단호하게 끝맺어 버렸던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER LX.\n\n좋은 표현은 분명, 그리고 언제나, 매우 칭찬할 만한 것이었다.\n—섈로 판사*\n\n* 셰익스피어의 『헨리 4세 2부』에 등장하는 인물.\n며칠 뒤—이미 팔월 말이었다—미들마치에 적잖은 흥분을 불러일으킨 사건이 하나 있었다. 사람들은 원한다면 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 명망 높은 주관 아래, 가구와 책과 그림을 살 수 있는 이점을 누리게 되었으니, 그것들은 광고지에서 누구나 보았듯이 종류마다 최고의 물품으로 에드윈 라처 씨의 소유였다. 이것은 사업 부진을 나타내는 매각이 아니었다. 오히려 라처 씨가 운수업에서 크게 성공한 덕분이었으니, 그 성공으로 그는 어느 저명한 온천 의사가 이미 고급스럽게 꾸며 놓은 리버스턴 근처의 저택을 사들일 수 있었다—식당에는 값비싼 살빛 그림이 큼직한 액자에 가득 담겨 걸려 있어 라처 부인이 불안해할 정도였으나, 그 주제가 성서적인 것임을 알고서야 안심했다. 그리하여 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 광고지에 잘 명시된, 구매자들을 위한 절호의 기회가 생긴 것이다. 그는 미술사에 대한 지식 덕분에, 유보 없이 매각될 현관 가구에는 기번스와 동시대 인물이 만든 조각 작품이 포함되어 있다고 밝힐 수 있었다.\n\n당시 미들마치에서 대규모 경매는 일종의 축제로 여겨졌다. 격조 높은 장례식에서처럼 최고급 찬 음식이 차려진 식탁이 마련되었고, 흥겨운 잔을 후하게 들이켜기에 좋은 편의가 제공되었으니, 그 술기운은 별로 탐나지 않는 물건에도 후하고 명랑하게 값을 부르도록 이끌 법했다. 라처 씨의 경매는 날씨가 좋아 더욱 매력적이었는데, 집이 마침 마을 끝자락에 정원과 마구간을 거느리고 서 있었기 때문이다. 미들마치에서 빠져나가는 그 기분 좋은 길은 런던 가도라 불렸으니, 신축 병원과 더 슈럽스라 알려진 불스트로드 씨의 은거지로 가는 길이기도 했다. 요컨대 그 경매는 장날만큼이나 좋은 구경거리였고, 여유 있는 모든 계층의 사람들을 끌어모았다. 단지 값을 올리려고 값을 부르는 모험을 하는 이들에게는 경마장에서 거는 내기나 다름없었다. 둘째 날, 가장 좋은 가구가 매각되는 날에는 \"누구나\" 그곳에 있었다. 세인트피터스 교구의 주임 사제인 시지어 씨조차 조각 탁자를 사고 싶어 잠깐 들렀고, 뱀브리지 씨와 호록 씨와 어깨를 부딪쳤다. 식당의 큰 탁자 둘레에는 미들마치 부인들이 화환처럼 둘러앉아 자리를 차지했고, 그 한가운데에 보스럽 트럼불 씨가 책상과 망치를 갖추고 올라앉았다. 하지만 그 뒤로 주로 남자들의 얼굴이 늘어선 줄은, 문과 잔디밭으로 열린 큰 활 모양 창문을 통해 드나드는 사람들로 자주 흐트러졌다.\n\n그날 \"누구나\"에 불스트로드 씨는 포함되지 않았으니, 그의 건강은 군중과 외풍을 잘 견디지 못했던 것이다. 하지만 불스트로드 부인은 어떤 그림 하나를 특히 갖고 싶어 했다—목록에는 귀도의 작품으로 적힌 「엠마오의 만찬」이었다. 경매 당일 마지막 순간에 불스트로드 씨는, 이제 자신도 그 일부 소유주가 된 『파이어니어』의 사무실에 들러, 래디슬로 씨에게 큰 호의를 베푸는 셈치고 그의 뛰어난 그림 식견을 불스트로드 부인을 위해 발휘하여 이 특정 그림의 가치를 판단해 달라고 부탁했다—\"다만,\" 그 꼼꼼하고 정중한 은행가는 덧붙였다, \"경매 참석이 임박해 있다고 알고 있는 당신의 출발 준비에 지장을 주지 않는다면 말입니다.\"\n\n이 단서는, 윌이 그런 풍자에 마음 쓸 기분이었다면, 다소 비꼬는 소리로 들렸을지도 모른다. 그것은 여러 주 전에 신문사 소유주들과 맺은 합의를 가리키는 것이었으니, 그가 원하는 날 언제든 자신이 훈련시켜 온 부편집인에게 경영을 넘기고 자유로이 떠나도 좋다는 내용이었다. 그가 마침내 미들마치를 떠나고 싶어 했기 때문이다. 하지만 야망에 대한 막연한 환상은 익숙하거나 그럴듯하게 기분 좋은 일을 하는 편안함 앞에서 무력하다. 그리고 우리는 결심이 불필요한 것으로 드러나기를 은근히 바랄 때, 그것을 실행에 옮기기가 얼마나 어려운지 잘 안다. 그런 마음 상태에서는 가장 회의적인 사람조차 기적 쪽으로 은밀히 기울게 마련이다. 우리의 소망이 어떻게 이루어질 수 있을지 상상조차 할 수 없지만, 그래도—참으로 놀라운 일들이 일어나 왔으니! 윌은 이 약점을 스스로 인정하지 않았으나, 머뭇거렸다. 일 년 중 그런 시기에 런던에 가서 무슨 소용이 있겠는가? 그를 기억해 줄 럭비 학교 친구들은 그곳에 없었다. 정치 평론에 관한 한, 그는 차라리 몇 주 더 『파이어니어』 일을 계속하고 싶었다. 그러나 지금 이 순간, 불스트로드 씨가 그에게 말을 걸고 있을 때, 그에게는 떠나겠다는 굳어진 결심과 더불어, 도러시아를 한 번 더 보기 전까지는 떠나지 않겠다는 똑같이 강한 결심이 함께 있었다. 그리하여 그는 출발을 조금 미룰 이유가 있으며, 기꺼이 경매에 가겠다고 대답했다.\n\n윌은 반항적인 기분이었으니, 자신을 쳐다보는 사람들이 아마도 그를 향한 고발이나 다름없는 사실, 곧 그가 재산 처분으로 좌절될 비열한 속셈을 품은 작자라는 사실을 알고 있으리라는 생각이 그의 의식을 깊이 찔렀던 것이다. 관습적인 신분 구별에 맞서 자신의 자유를 주장하는 대부분의 사람들이 그러하듯, 그는 자신이 그런 주장을 펴는 데에 개인적인 이유가 있다고—자신의 혈통이나 처신이나 성품에 어떤 의견의 가면을 씌우고 있는 무언가가 있다고—넌지시 비치는 자가 있으면 누구와든 불쑥 빠르게 다투려고 벼르고 있었다. 그는 이런 종류의 자극적인 인상을 받으면 며칠 동안 반항적인 표정으로 돌아다녔고, 마치 경계 태세로 무언가를 노려 덮치려는 듯, 투명한 피부의 빛깔이 바뀌곤 했다.\n\n이 표정은 경매에서 그에게서 유난히 두드러졌으니, 그의 부드러운 괴벽이나 밝은 즐거움의 기분만 보아 온 이들이라면 그 대조에 놀랐을 것이다. 그는 톨러, 해크벗 등 미들마치 일족들 앞에 공개적으로 나설 이런 기회가 싫지 않았으니, 그들은 그를 모험가로 깔보았고 단테에 대해서는 야만적으로 무지한 자들이었다—그의 폴란드 혈통을 비웃었으나, 그들 자신이야말로 이종 교배가 몹시 필요한 혈통이었다. 그는 경매인에게서 멀지 않은 눈에 띄는 자리에, 양쪽 호주머니에 검지를 하나씩 찔러 넣고 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 서 있었다. 누구에게도 말을 걸 마음이 없었으나, 트럼불 씨는 그를 감식가로 따뜻이 맞아 주었고, 그 자신은 자신의 위대한 재능을 한껏 발휘하며 즐기고 있었다.\n\n그리고 분명 말솜씨를 발휘해야 하는 직업을 가진 모든 사람 중에서, 가장 행복한 자는 자기 농담에 예민하게 살아 있고 자신의 백과사전적 지식을 의식하는, 번창하는 지방 경매인이다. 침울하고 시큰둥한 성미의 어떤 이들은 장화 벗는 기구에서 \"베르헴\" 그림에 이르기까지 모든 물건의 장점을 끊임없이 역설하는 일에 반감을 가질지도 모른다. 하지만 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 핏줄에는 다정한 액체가 흘렀다. 그는 천성이 찬미자였고, 온 우주를 자신의 망치 아래 두고 싶어 했으니, 자신의 추천이라면 우주도 더 높은 값에 팔려 나가리라 여겼던 것이다.\n\n한편 라처 부인의 응접실 가구만으로도 그에게는 충분했다. 윌 래디슬로가 들어왔을 때, 제자리에 놓는 것을 깜빡했다는 두 번째 난로 망이 느닷없이 경매인의 열정을 사로잡았으니, 그는 가장 칭찬이 필요한 물건을 가장 많이 칭찬한다는 공정한 원칙에 따라 그 열정을 배분했다. 그 난로 망은 광을 낸 강철로, 창끝 모양으로 뚫린 무늬가 많고 날이 날카로웠다.\n\n\"자, 부인들,\" 그가 말했다, \"여러분께 호소하겠습니다. 여기 이 난로 망은 다른 경매라면 유보 없이 내놓기 어려울 물건이니, 강철의 품질과 디자인의 기묘함으로 보아 이를테면 일종의\"—여기서 트럼불 씨는 목소리를 낮추고 약간 콧소리를 내며 왼손가락으로 윤곽을 다듬었다—\"보통의 취향에는 맞지 않을 법한 물건입니다. 머지않아 이런 양식의 세공이 유일하게 유행하게 되리라는 점을 말씀드리지요—반 크라운이라 하셨습니까? 감사합니다—반 크라운에 나갑니다, 이 개성 있는 난로 망이. 그리고 저는 이 고풍스러운 양식이 상류층에서 대단히 추구되고 있다는 특별한 정보를 가지고 있습니다. 삼 실링—삼 실링 육 펜스—잘 들어 올려라, 조지프! 보십시오, 부인들, 이 디자인의 정결함을—저는 이것이 지난 세기에 만들어졌다고 확신합니다! 사 실링이십니까, 몸지 씨?—사 실링.\"\n\n\"저것은 제 응접실에 둘 만한 물건이 아니에요.\" 몸지 부인이, 경솔한 남편에게 경고하려는 듯 또렷이 말했다. \"라처 부인은 어쩌자고. 부딪치는 아이마다 머리가 둘로 갈라질 거예요. 날이 칼 같잖아요.\"\n\n\"지당하신 말씀입니다.\" 트럼불 씨가 재빨리 응수했다. \"그리고 가까이 둘 수 있는, 자를 수 있는 난로 망이란 더없이 유용하지요. 가죽 신발 끈이나 끊어야 할 노끈은 있는데 칼이 없을 때 말입니다. 끊어 줄 칼이 없어서 매달린 채 남겨진 사람이 한둘이 아닙니다. 신사 여러분, 여기 이 난로 망은, 만약 불행히도 목을 매신다면 눈 깜짝할 사이에 끊어 내려 줄 것입니다—놀라운 신속함으로요—사 실링 육 펜스—오 실링—오 실링 육 펜스—사주식 침대가 놓이고 손님이 다소 정신이 나간 손님방에 안성맞춤이지요—육 실링—감사합니다, 클린텁 씨—육 실링에 나갑니다—나갑니다—낙찰!\" 모든 호가의 기미에 초자연적인 예민함으로 주위를 살피던 경매인의 시선이 여기서 앞에 놓인 종이로 떨어졌고, 그의 목소리도 무심하게 처리하는 어조로 가라앉으며 그는 말했다, \"클린텁 씨. 잽싸게 해라, 조지프.\"\n\n\"저런 농담을 늘 곁들일 수 있는 난로 망이라면 육 실링 값은 하지.\" 클린텁 씨가 옆 사람에게 변명하듯 낮게 웃으며 말했다. 그는 수줍지만 명망 있는 묘목 상인이었고, 청중이 자신의 호가를 어리석은 것으로 여길까 봐 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n그사이 조지프가 자잘한 물건이 가득 담긴 쟁반을 가져왔다. \"자, 부인들,\" 트럼불 씨가 물건 하나를 집어 들며 말했다, \"이 쟁반에는 아주 별난 한 묶음이 담겨 있습니다—응접실 탁자를 위한 자잘한 소품 모음이지요—그리고 자잘한 것들이 인간 만사의 총합을 이룹니다—자잘한 것보다 더 중요한 것은 없지요—(예, 래디슬로 씨, 예, 곧 하겠습니다)—하지만 쟁반을 돌려라, 조지프—이 보석들은 살펴보셔야 합니다, 부인들. 제 손에 든 이것은 기발한 고안품입니다—말하자면 일종의 실용적인 그림 수수께끼라 할 수 있지요. 보십시오, 여기서는 우아한 하트 모양 상자처럼 보이고—휴대용으로, 주머니에 넣을 수 있습니다. 또 저기서는 화려한 겹꽃처럼 변합니다—탁자 장식품이지요. 그리고 이제는\"—트럼불 씨는 그 꽃을 하트 모양 잎사귀들의 줄로 아슬아슬하게 늘어뜨렸다—\"수수께끼 책입니다! 무려 오백 개가 아름다운 붉은 글씨로 인쇄되어 있지요. 신사 여러분, 제가 양심이 조금만 덜했다면 여러분께서 이 묶음에 높은 값을 부르시기를 바라지 않았을 것입니다—저 자신이 이것을 탐내고 있으니까요. 좋은 수수께끼만큼 순수한 즐거움과, 감히 말하건대 미덕을 북돋우는 것이 또 있겠습니까?—그것은 상스러운 말을 막아 주고, 사람을 교양 있는 여성들의 모임에 붙들어 둡니다. 이 기발한 물건 하나만으로도, 우아한 도미노 상자며 카드 바구니 따위는 빼고도, 이 묶음에 높은 값을 매겨 마땅합니다. 주머니에 넣고 다니면 어떤 모임에서든 환영받는 사람이 될 수 있지요. 사 실링이십니까, 선생님?—이 놀라운 수수께끼 모음에 기타 등등을 더해 사 실링. 여기 견본이 하나 있습니다. '꿀로 무당벌레를 잡으려면 꿀을 어떻게 써야 하는가? 답—돈이다.'* 들으셨습니까?—무당벌레—꿀과 돈. 이것은 지력을 날카롭게 하는 오락입니다. 가시가 있고—우리가 풍자라 부르는 것이 있으며, 음란함 없는 재치가 있지요. 사 실링 육 펜스—오 실링.\"\n* 영어 honey와 money의 발음 유사성을 이용한 말장난.\n\n호가는 점점 달아오르는 경쟁 속에 이어졌다. 보이어 씨도 값을 불렀는데, 이것은 너무나 약 오르는 일이었다. 보이어는 그것을 살 형편도 아니면서, 그저 다른 모든 사람이 잘난 척하는 것을 방해하고 싶을 뿐이었다. 그 흐름은 호록 씨마저 휩쓸어 갔으나, 의견에 대한 이 같은 그의 가담은 중립적인 표정을 거의 희생하지 않고 흘러나온지라, 뱀브리지 씨의 다정한 욕설이 아니었던들 그것이 그의 호가임을 아무도 알아채지 못했을 것이다. 뱀브리지 씨는 호록이, 이 말 장수가 지상 존재 대다수에게서 그토록 흔쾌히 인정하는 그 파멸 상태에 빠진 잡화상에게나 어울릴 빌어먹을 물건을 가지고 무엇을 하려는지 알고 싶어 했다. 그 묶음은 결국 일 기니에 스필킨스 씨에게 낙찰되었으니, 그는 동네의 젊은 슬렌더*로, 용돈을 함부로 써 대는 데다 수수께끼에 대한 기억력이 모자란다고 느끼는 사람이었다.\n* 셰익스피어의 『윈저의 즐거운 아낙네들』에 나오는 어리석은 젊은이.\n\n\"이봐, 트럼불, 이건 너무하잖나—노처녀의 잡동사니를 경매에 끼워 넣다니.\" 톨러 씨가 경매인에게 바싹 다가서며 중얼거렸다. \"판화가 어떻게 나가는지 보고 싶은데, 곧 가 봐야 한단 말일세.\"\n\n\"즉시 하겠습니다, 톨러 씨. 그것은 그저 당신의 고결한 마음도 인정하실 자선의 행위였을 뿐입니다. 조지프! 판화를 어서—235번 묶음입니다. 자, 감식안을 갖추신 신사 여러분, 이제 진수성찬을 맛보시게 됩니다. 여기 워털루 전장에서 참모들에게 둘러싸인 웰링턴 공작의 판화가 있습니다. 그리고 최근 이를테면 우리의 위대한 영웅을 구름 속에 가려 버린 사건들이 있었음에도—제 직업을 가진 사람은 정치적 바람에 흔들려서는 안 되니까요—저는 감히 말씀드리겠습니다. 이보다 더 훌륭한 주제는—우리 자신의 시대와 시기에 속한 현대적 종류의 주제는—인간의 지력으로 거의 떠올릴 수 없을 것이라고요. 천사라면 혹 모르나, 인간은 아닙니다, 여러분, 인간은 아니지요.\"\n\n\"누가 그렸소?\" 깊이 감동한 파우더렐 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이것은 글자가 새겨지기 전의 시쇄본입니다, 파우더렐 씨—화가는 알려져 있지 않지요.\" 트럼불이 마지막 말에 숨을 약간 헐떡이며 대답하고는, 입술을 오므린 채 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"일 파운드 부르겠소!\" 파우더렐 씨가, 무너진 성벽을 메우려 몸을 던지는 사람처럼 결연한 감정의 어조로 말했다. 경외 때문인지 연민 때문인지, 아무도 그에게 값을 더 올려 부르지 않았다.\n\n다음으로 톨러 씨가 간절히 원하던 네덜란드 판화 두 점이 나왔고, 그것을 손에 넣은 뒤 그는 떠났다. 다른 판화들, 그리고 그 뒤로 몇몇 그림이, 특별히 그것들을 원해서 온 미들마치의 유력 인사들에게 팔렸고, 청중의 출입이 더욱 활발해졌다. 원하던 것을 산 이들은 떠나고, 다른 이들은 아주 새로 들어오거나 잔디밭 천막 아래 차려진 다과를 잠시 즐기고 들어왔다. 뱀브리지 씨가 사려고 작정한 것이 바로 이 천막이었는데, 그는 소유의 전조를 맛보기라도 하듯 자주 그 안을 들여다보기를 좋아하는 것 같았다. 마지막으로 거기서 돌아왔을 때, 그가 새로운 동행을 데려오는 것이 눈에 띄었으니, 트럼불 씨를 비롯한 모두에게 낯선 사람이었다. 다만 그 외모로 보아 말 장수의 친척이 아닐까—역시 \"탐닉에 빠진\" 자가 아닐까 짐작되었다. 큼직한 구레나룻과 위세 있는 거들먹거림, 다리를 휘젓는 걸음걸이가 그를 눈에 띄는 인물로 만들었다. 하지만 가장자리가 다소 해진 검은 양복은, 그가 원하는 만큼 탐닉할 형편은 못 된다는 불리한 추측을 불러일으켰다.\n\n\"자네가 데려온 사람은 누구야, 뱀?\" 호록 씨가 곁에서 물었다.\n\n\"직접 물어봐.\" 뱀브리지 씨가 대꾸했다. \"길에서 막 들어왔다더군.\"\n\n호록 씨는 그 낯선 사람을 살펴보았는데, 그는 한 손으로 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 채 다른 손으로 이쑤시개를 쓰며, 상황이 강요한 침묵 속에서 어떤 안절부절못함을 띤 채 주위를 둘러보고 있었다.\n\n마침내 「엠마오의 만찬」이 앞으로 나왔으니, 윌로서는 더없는 안도였다. 그는 진행에 너무나 싫증이 나서 조금 물러나 경매인 바로 뒤의 벽에 어깨를 기대고 있었던 것이다. 이제 그는 다시 앞으로 나섰는데, 그 눈에 눈에 띄는 그 낯선 사람이 들어왔다. 다소 놀랍게도, 그자는 그를 노골적으로 빤히 쳐다보고 있었다. 하지만 윌은 곧바로 트럼불 씨의 호소를 받았다.\n\n\"예, 래디슬로 씨, 예. 감식가이신 당신께서는 이것에 관심이 있으실 줄 압니다. 이런 그림을 신사 숙녀 여러분께 보여 드릴 수 있다는 것은,\" 경매인이 고조되는 열정으로 말을 이었다, \"여간한 기쁨이 아닙니다—식견에 걸맞은 재력을 갖춘 분에게는 그 값을 매길 수 없는 그림이지요. 이것은 이탈리아 화파의 그림으로—세계 최고의 화가이자 이른바 옛 거장들의 우두머리인 저 유명한 귀도의 작품입니다—제 생각에 그들을 옛 거장이라 부르는 까닭은, 그들이 우리 대부분을 넘어서는 한두 가지 비결을, 이제는 인류 대다수에게 사라져 버린 비밀을 알고 있었기 때문이지요. 말씀드리건대, 신사 여러분, 저는 옛 거장들의 그림을 매우 많이 보았으나, 그것들이 다 이 수준에 이르는 것은 아닙니다—그중에는 여러분 마음에 들기에 너무 어둡고 가족용 주제가 아닌 것도 있지요. 하지만 여기 이것은 귀도입니다—액자만으로도 여러 파운드 값어치가 있는—어떤 부인이든 걸어 두고 자랑스러워할 그림이지요—만약 시 참사회의 어느 신사분께서 자신의 후덕함을 보이고 싶으시다면, 자선 기관의 이른바 식당에 어울리는 물건입니다. 조금 돌려 보겠습니까, 선생님? 그래. 조지프, 래디슬로 씨 쪽으로 조금 돌려라—보십시오, 래디슬로 씨께서는 외국에 다녀오신 분이라 이런 것들의 가치를 아십니다.\"\n\n잠시 모든 시선이 윌에게로 향했고, 그는 냉담하게 말했다, \"오 파운드.\" 경매인이 깊은 항의를 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"아! 래디슬로 씨! 액자만으로도 그만한 값어치가 있습니다. 신사 숙녀 여러분, 이 마을의 명예를 위해서라도! 훗날 이 마을에 예술의 보석이 우리 가운데 있었는데, 미들마치의 그 누구도 그것을 알아보지 못했다는 사실이 밝혀진다면 어쩌시겠습니까. 오 기니—오 기니 칠 실링 육 펜스—오 기니 십 실링. 그래도, 부인들, 그래도요! 이것은 보석입니다. 시인이 말했듯 '수많은 보석이' 헐값에 팔려 나가도록 방치되어 왔으니, 이는 대중이 알아보지 못했기 때문이요, 그것이—저열한 감정이라 말하려다 그만두겠습니다만—아무튼 그런 분위기의 자리에서 내놓였기 때문입니다—육 파운드—육 기니—일급 귀도가 육 기니에 나갑니다—이것은 종교에 대한 모독입니다, 부인들. 이런 주제가 그토록 헐값에 나가다니, 기독교도로서 우리 모두를 건드리는 일입니다, 신사 여러분—육 파운드 십 실링—칠—\"\n\n호가는 활발했고, 윌은 불스트로드 부인이 그 그림을 간절히 바란다는 것을 떠올리며 계속 거기에 동참했으니, 십이 파운드까지는 값을 올릴 수 있으리라 생각했다. 하지만 그것은 십 기니에 그에게 낙찰되었고, 그러자 그는 활 모양 창문 쪽으로 헤치고 나가 밖으로 나왔다. 그는 덥고 목이 말라 물 한 잔을 마시러 천막 안으로 들어가기로 했다. 천막에는 다른 방문객이 없었고, 그는 시중드는 여자에게 신선한 물을 좀 가져다 달라고 청했다. 하지만 그녀가 미처 나가기도 전에, 자신을 빤히 쳐다보던 그 혈색 좋은 낯선 사람이 들어오는 것이 보여 짜증이 났다. 그 순간 윌은 그자가, 자신이 개혁 문제에 관해 연설하는 것을 들었다며 한두 번 안면을 주장하고는, 소식거리로 한 실링이나 벌어 볼까 궁리하는, 그 부풀어 오른 종류의 정치적 기생충 같은 자들 중 하나일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 그렇게 보니, 여름날 보기만 해도 이미 다소 더워지는 그자의 모습이 더욱 불쾌하게 느껴졌다. 윌은 정원 의자 팔걸이에 반쯤 걸터앉은 채, 그 모퉁이에서 조심스레 눈을 돌렸다. 하지만 그것은 우리의 지인 래플스 씨에게는 별 의미가 없었으니, 그는 자기 목적에 맞기만 하면 내키지 않아 하는 시선 앞에 자신을 들이미는 데 결코 주저하는 법이 없었다. 그는 한두 걸음 옮겨 윌 앞에 서서, 입을 가득 채운 듯한 성급함으로 말했다, \"실례합니다, 래디슬로 씨—어머님 성함이 세라 던커크 아니었습니까?\"\n\n윌은 벌떡 일어나 한 걸음 물러서며, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 다소 사납게 말했다, \"그렇소, 선생. 그랬소. 그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오?\"\n\n처음 튀어나온 불꽃이 질문에 대한 직접적인 대답이자 그 결과에 대한 도전이라는 것이 윌의 천성이었다. 대뜸 \"그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오?\"라고 했다면, 회피하는 것처럼—마치 자신의 출신에 대해 누가 무엇을 알든 신경 쓰는 것처럼 보였을 테니까!\n\n래플스로 말하자면, 래디슬로의 위협적인 태도가 암시하는 충돌을 그만큼 바라지는 않았다. 소녀 같은 살결의 그 호리호리한 젊은이가 당장이라도 덮칠 듯한 살쾡이처럼 보였던 것이다. 이런 상황에서 일행을 괴롭히는 래플스 씨의 즐거움은 잠시 보류되었다.\n\n\"악의는 없습니다, 선생, 악의는 없어요! 저는 그저 어머님을 기억할 뿐입니다—소녀 시절에 알았지요. 하지만 당신이 닮은 것은 아버님입니다, 선생. 저는 아버님을 뵙는 기쁨도 누렸지요. 부모님은 살아 계십니까, 래디슬로 씨?\"\n\n\"아니!\" 윌이 아까와 같은 자세로 우레처럼 내뱉었다.\n\n\"기꺼이 도움을 드리고 싶습니다, 래디슬로 씨—맹세코 그렇고말고요! 다시 뵙기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 래플스는 마지막 말을 하며 모자를 들어 올렸다가, 다리를 휘저으며 돌아서서 걸어갔다. 윌은 잠시 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았는데, 그가 경매장으로 다시 들어가지 않고 길 쪽으로 걸어가는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 순간 그는 그자가 계속 말하도록 내버려 두지 않은 것이 어리석었다는 생각이 들었다—하지만 아니! 대체로 그는 그런 출처에서 나온 지식 없이 지내는 편이 나았다.\n\n그러나 저녁 늦게 래플스가 거리에서 그를 따라잡았으니, 앞서 받은 거친 대접을 잊은 듯도 하고 너그러운 친근함으로 그것을 갚으려는 듯도 하게, 그를 쾌활하게 맞이하며 곁에서 나란히 걸었다. 그는 처음에는 마을과 그 일대가 쾌적하다는 말을 했다. 윌은 그자가 술을 마신 것 같다고 의심하며 어떻게 떼어 낼지 궁리하고 있는데, 래플스가 말했다—\n\n\"저도 외국에 다녀왔지요, 래디슬로 씨—세상 구경을 했답니다—프랑스 말도 좀 했고요. 제가 아버님을 뵌 것은 불로뉴에서였습니다—맹세코, 아버님을 어쩌면 그리도 빼닮으셨는지! 입—코—눈—이마에서 넘긴 머리까지 꼭 그분 같군요—약간 외국식으로요. 영국 사람들은 그렇게 잘 안 하지요. 하지만 제가 뵈었을 때 아버님은 몹시 편찮으셨습니다. 저런, 저런! 손이 비칠 정도였지요. 그때 당신은 어린 꼬마였습니다. 그분은 회복되셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 윌이 퉁명스레 말했다.\n\n\"아! 그렇군요! 저는 어머님이 어찌 되셨는지 종종 궁금했습니다. 젊은 처녀 시절에 식구들에게서 도망쳤지요—기상이 높은 처녀였고, 맹세코, 예뻤습니다! 저는 그분이 왜 도망쳤는지 그 이유를 알지요.\" 래플스가 윌을 곁눈질하며 천천히 눈을 찡긋했다.\n\n\"당신은 어머니에 대해 떳떳치 못한 어떤 것도 알지 못하오, 선생.\" 윌이 다소 사납게 그에게 돌아서며 말했다. 하지만 래플스 씨는 지금 태도의 미묘한 차이에 둔감했다.\n\n\"천만에요!\" 그가 단호하게 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그분은 식구들을 좋아하기엔 좀 지나치게 떳떳한 분이었지요—그게 다입니다!\" 여기서 래플스가 다시 천천히 눈을 찡긋했다. \"이거 참, 저는 그들에 대해 다 알고 있었습니다—이를테면 점잖은 도둑질이라 할 만한, 고급 장물 취급소였지요—구질구질한 뒷골목 따위가 아니라—일류였습니다. 으리으리한 가게에, 이문도 두둑하고 틀림없었지요. 하지만 이거 참! 세라는 그런 건 아무것도 몰랐을 겁니다—멋들어진 젊은 아가씨였고—훌륭한 기숙 학교를 다녔으며—귀족의 아내가 되기에도 손색이 없었지요—다만 아치 던컨이 앙심을 품고 그 사실을 그분에게 들이댄 겁니다. 그분이 자기를 상대해 주지 않았으니까요. 그래서 그분은 그 모든 일에서 도망쳤습니다. 저는 그들을 위해 신사답게 외판을 다녔지요, 선생—높은 봉급을 받으며. 그들은 처음엔 그분이 도망친 것을 개의치 않았습니다—독실한 사람들이었거든요, 선생, 아주 독실했지요—그리고 그분은 무대에 서고자 했습니다. 그때는 아들이 살아 있었고, 딸은 푸대접을 받았지요. 어이쿠! 다 왔군요, 블루 불입니다. 어떻습니까, 래디슬로 씨?—들어가서 한잔하실까요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그만 작별 인사를 해야겠소.\" 윌이 로윅 게이트로 통하는 골목길로 휙 들어서며, 래플스의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 벗어나려 거의 뛰다시피 하며 말했다.\n\n그는 마을에서 멀어지는 로윅 가도를 오래도록 걸었고, 이윽고 찾아온 별빛 어린 어둠이 반가웠다. 그는 마치 조롱의 외침 속에서 자기 몸에 오물이 끼얹어진 것 같은 느낌이었다. 그자의 말을 뒷받침하는 것이 하나 있었으니—어머니가 자기 가족에게서 왜 도망쳤는지 그 이유를 결코 그에게 말해 주지 않았다는 사실이었다.\n\n좋다! 그 가족에 대한 진실이 가장 추악한 것이라 한들, 그, 윌 래디슬로가 그것으로 무엇이 더 나빠진단 말인가? 어머니는 그것에서 자신을 떼어 놓기 위해 고난을 무릅썼다. 하지만 도러시아의 친지들이 이 이야기를 알았더라면—체텀 집안이 알았더라면—그들은 자신들의 의심에 그럴듯한 빛깔을 입혀, 그가 그녀에게 가까이 갈 자격이 없다고 여길 반가운 근거로 삼았을 것이다. 그러나 그들이야 좋을 대로 의심하라지, 그들은 자신들이 틀렸음을 알게 될 터였다. 그들은 그의 핏줄에 흐르는 피가 자신들의 것만큼이나 비열함의 오점에서 자유롭다는 것을 알게 될 터였다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXI.\n\n“모순은,” 임락이 대답했다, “둘 다 옳을 수는 없지만, 인간에게 돌린다면 둘 다 참일 수는 있다네.”—『라셀라스』.\n\n* 『라셀라스』: 새뮤얼 존슨의 철학 소설 『아비시니아의 왕자 라셀라스 이야기』(1759).\n같은 날 밤, 불스트로드 씨가 사업차 브래싱에 다녀와 돌아왔을 때, 그의 착한 아내가 현관 홀에서 그를 맞이하여 그의 사적인 거실로 데리고 들어갔다.\n\n“니컬러스,” 그녀는 정직한 눈으로 그를 근심스럽게 바라보며 말했다. “여기 어떤 아주 불쾌한 남자가 와서 당신을 찾았어요. 그래서 제가 무척 불편했답니다.”\n\n“어떤 부류의 사람이었소, 여보,” 불스트로드 씨는 끔찍하게도 대답을 확신하며 말했다.\n\n“얼굴이 벌겋고 구레나룻이 큰 사람인데, 태도가 더없이 무례했어요. 자기가 당신의 오랜 친구라고 주장하면서, 자기를 만나지 못하면 당신이 섭섭해할 거라고 했어요. 여기서 당신을 기다리겠다고 하길래, 내일 아침 은행에서 만날 수 있을 거라고 제가 말해 줬지요. 어찌나 무례하던지!—저를 빤히 쳐다보면서, 자기 친구 닉은 아내 복이 있다고 하지 뭐예요. 마침 블뤼허가 사슬을 끊고 자갈밭으로 달려 나오지 않았더라면 그 사람은 가지 않았을 거예요—제가 정원에 있었거든요. 그래서 제가 ‘가시는 게 좋겠어요—개가 아주 사나운데, 제가 붙잡을 수가 없네요’라고 했지요. 정말로 그런 사람을 아세요?”\n\n“그가 누군지 알 것 같소, 여보,” 불스트로드 씨가 평소의 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다. “지난날 내가 너무 많이 도와주었던 불운하고 방탕한 가련한 자라오. 하지만 다시는 그자가 당신을 괴롭히지 않을 거요. 아마 은행으로 올 테지—구걸하러, 틀림없이.”\n\n이 일에 관해서는 다음 날까지 더 이상 말이 오가지 않았는데, 그날 불스트로드 씨가 읍내에서 돌아와 만찬을 위해 옷을 갈아입던 참이었다. 그의 아내는 그가 집에 돌아왔는지 확신하지 못하고 그의 화장실을 들여다보았다가, 그가 윗도리와 크라바트를 벗은 채 한쪽 팔을 서랍장에 기대고 멍하니 바닥을 응시하고 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀가 들어서자 그는 신경질적으로 흠칫 놀라며 고개를 들었다.\n\n“무척 안 좋아 보여요, 니컬러스. 무슨 일이라도 있어요?”\n\n“머리가 몹시 아프오,” 불스트로드 씨가 말했다. 그가 워낙 자주 앓았기에 그의 아내는 이런 우울의 원인을 늘 믿을 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n“앉으세요, 식초로 좀 닦아 드릴게요.”\n\n육체적으로는 불스트로드 씨가 식초를 원하지 않았으나, 정신적으로는 그 애정 어린 보살핌이 그를 달래 주었다. 늘 정중하기는 했으되, 그런 봉사를 부부간의 무덤덤함으로, 아내의 의무로 받아들이는 것이 그의 습관이었다. 그러나 오늘은, 그녀가 자기 위로 몸을 굽히고 있을 때, 그가 “당신은 참 착하구려, 해리엇,” 하고 말했는데, 그 어조에는 그녀의 귀에 무언가 새로운 것이 담겨 있었다. 그녀는 그 새로움이 정확히 무엇인지 알지 못했지만, 그녀의 여성다운 염려가 그가 병이 나려는 것일지도 모른다는 화살 같은 생각으로 빚어졌다.\n\n“무슨 걱정거리라도 있었어요?” 그녀가 말했다. “그 사람이 은행으로 당신을 찾아왔어요?”\n\n“그렇소. 내가 짐작했던 그대로였소. 한때는 더 나은 사람이 될 수도 있었을 자였소. 하지만 술에 절은 방탕한 자로 전락하고 말았소.”\n\n“완전히 떠나갔나요?” 불스트로드 부인이 근심스럽게 말했다. 하지만 어떤 이유에서 그녀는 “그가 자기를 당신의 친구라고 부르는 걸 듣기가 무척 불쾌했어요”라는 말을 덧붙이는 것을 삼갔다. 그 순간 그녀는 남편의 옛 인연들이 자기 쪽 사람들과 그리 동등하지 않다는 자신의 습관적인 의식을 내비치는 어떤 말도 하고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 사실 그녀가 그 인연들에 관해 많이 아는 것도 아니었다. 남편이 처음에는 은행에서 일했다는 것, 그 후 그가 도시 사업이라고 부르는 일에 뛰어들어 서른세 살이 되기 전에 재산을 모았다는 것, 그가 자기보다 훨씬 나이 많은 과부와 결혼했다는 것—그 여자는 비국교도였고, 다른 면에서도 아마 두 번째 아내의 냉정한 판단으로 따져 보면 첫 번째 아내에게서 으레 감지되는 그 불리한 자질을 지녔으리라는 것—이 정도가, 불스트로드 씨의 이야기가 이따금 비춰 준 어린 시절의 종교적 성향, 설교자가 되고 싶어 했던 그의 기질, 그리고 선교와 자선 활동과의 연관에 대한 단편들 외에, 그녀가 알고자 한 거의 전부였다. 그녀는 그를 훌륭한 사람으로 믿었으니, 평신도로서 그가 지닌 경건함은 특별한 탁월함을 띠었고, 그의 영향력이 그녀 자신의 마음을 진지함으로 돌려놓았으며, 그가 지닌 덧없는 재물의 몫이 그녀 자신의 지위를 끌어올리는 수단이 되었던 것이다. 하지만 그녀는 또한 불스트로드 씨가 해리엇 빈시의 손을 얻은 것이 모든 의미에서 잘된 일이라고 생각하기를 좋아했으니, 그녀의 집안은 미들마치의 시각에서 보면 부인할 수 없이 떳떳했고—그것은 분명 런던의 큰길이나 비국교도 예배당 뜰에 드리워진 그 어떤 빛보다도 더 나은 빛이었다. 개혁되지 않은 지방의 정신은 런던을 불신했고, 참된 종교는 어디서든 사람을 구원하는 것이지만, 정직한 불스트로드 부인은 국교회 안에서 구원받는 것이 더 점잖다고 확신했다. 그녀는 남편이 한때 런던의 비국교도였다는 사실을 남들에게 알리지 않고 싶은 마음이 너무도 컸기에, 그에게 이야기할 때조차 그것을 시야 밖에 두기를 좋아했다. 그도 이 점을 잘 알고 있었으니, 사실 어떤 면에서는 그가 이 순진한 아내를 다소 두려워하기까지 했던 것인데, 그녀의 모방적인 경건함과 타고난 세속성은 똑같이 진실했고, 그녀에게는 부끄러워할 것이 아무것도 없었으며, 그가 여전히 지속되는 진정한 애정에서 그녀와 결혼했던 것이다. 하지만 그의 두려움은 자신이 인정받는 우위를 유지하고 싶어 하는 사람에게 속하는 그런 종류였으니, 진리에 대한 적의에서 그를 분명히 미워하는 사람이 아닌 모든 이로부터 받는 것과 마찬가지로 아내로부터 받는 높은 존경을 잃는다면, 그것은 그에게 죽음의 시작과도 같을 것이었다. 그녀가 이렇게 말했을 때—\n\n“완전히 떠나갔나요?”\n\n“오, 그러기를 바라오,” 그가 가능한 한 차분한 무관심을 어조에 담으려 애쓰며 대답했다!\n\n하지만 진실로 불스트로드 씨는 고요한 신뢰의 상태와는 거리가 멀었다. 은행에서의 면담에서 래플스는 자신의 괴롭히려는 열망이 다른 어떤 탐욕 못지않게 강하다는 것을 분명히 보여 주었다. 그는 그저 주위를 둘러보고 이 동네가 자기가 살기에 어울리는지 살펴보려고 일부러 길을 돌아 미들마치로 왔노라고 솔직하게 말했다. 분명 그는 예상보다 갚아야 할 빚이 좀 더 있었지만, 이백 파운드가 아직 다 사라진 것은 아니었다. 당장은 떠날 밑천으로 깔끔하게 이십오 파운드면 충분할 터였다. 그가 무엇보다 원했던 것은 친구 닉과 그 가족을 보는 것, 그리고 자기가 그토록 애정을 품은 사람의 번영에 관해 죄다 아는 것이었다. 머지않아 더 오래 머물기 위해 다시 올지도 모를 일이었다. 이번에 래플스는 자기 표현대로 “부지 밖으로 배웅받기”를 거부했다—불스트로드의 눈앞에서 미들마치를 떠나기를 거부한 것이다. 그는 내키면 다음 날 마차로 떠날 작정이었다.\n\n불스트로드는 자신이 무력하다고 느꼈다. 위협도 회유도 소용이 없었다. 그는 어떤 지속적인 두려움도, 어떤 약속도 믿을 수가 없었다. 도리어 그는 래플스가—섭리가 죽음을 보내 그를 막지 않는 한—머지않아 미들마치로 돌아오리라는 차가운 확신을 가슴에 품었다. 그리고 그 확신은 공포였다.\n\n그가 법적 처벌이나 거지 신세의 위험에 처한 것은 아니었다. 그가 처한 위험은 오직 자기 과거 삶의 어떤 사실들이 이웃들의 판단과 아내의 슬픈 인식 앞에 드러나는 것이었으니, 그것은 그를 경멸의 대상으로, 그가 부지런히 결부해 온 종교의 치욕으로 만들어 버릴 터였다. 심판받는 공포는 기억을 날카롭게 한다. 그것은 오랫동안 일반적인 어구로만 습관적으로 떠올려 온 그 오래도록 찾지 않던 과거 위로 피할 수 없는 섬광을 던진다. 기억이 없더라도, 삶은 성장과 쇠퇴 속에서 의존의 띠로 하나로 엮인다. 하지만 강렬한 기억은 인간으로 하여금 자신의 비난받을 만한 과거를 인정하게 만든다. 다시 벌어진 상처처럼 쓰라리게 자극받은 기억과 함께, 인간의 과거는 단순히 죽은 역사가, 현재의 낡아 빠진 준비 과정이 아니다. 그것은 삶에서 떨어져 나간, 뉘우친 과오가 아니다. 그것은 여전히 떨고 있는 자기 자신의 일부로서, 전율과 쓰디쓴 맛과 마땅히 받아야 할 수치심의 따끔거림을 불러일으킨다.\n\n이 두 번째 삶 속으로 이제 불스트로드의 과거가 솟아올랐는데, 다만 그 쾌락들만은 본래의 성질을 잃어버린 듯했다. 짧은 잠—그조차도 회고와 두려움을 환상적인 현재 속으로 짜 넣을 뿐이었다—으로 끊기는 것 말고는 밤낮으로 쉼 없이, 그는 자신의 옛 삶의 장면들이 자기와 다른 모든 것 사이를 가로막고 들어서는 것을 느꼈다. 마치 불 켜진 방에서 창문을 통해 내다볼 때, 우리가 등을 돌린 사물들이 풀과 나무 대신 여전히 우리 앞에 어른거리듯, 그처럼 집요하게. 안팎으로 잇따른 사건들이 한눈에 펼쳐졌으니, 비록 그것들을 차례로 곱씹을 수는 있었어도 나머지는 여전히 의식 속에서 그 자리를 지키고 있었다.\n\n다시 한번 그는 자신이 호감 가는 풍채를 지닌 젊은 은행원이었던 시절을 보았다. 숫자에 영민한 만큼이나 말솜씨도 유창했고 신학적 정의를 즐겨 따지던, 죄에 대한 통회와 사면의 감각에서 놀라운 체험을 겪은, 하이베리의 한 칼뱅파 비국교회의 젊으면서도 저명한 회원이었던 자신을. 다시 그는 기도 모임에서 ‘불스트로드 형제’로 불려 나가, 종교 단상에서 연설하고, 개인 가정집에서 설교하는 자신의 목소리를 들었다. 다시 그는 성직이 어쩌면 자신의 천직일지도 모른다고 생각하며 선교 사역에 마음이 기울던 자신을 느꼈다. 그때가 그의 생애에서 가장 행복한 시절이었으니, 이제라면 그가 깨어나 나머지가 한낱 꿈이었음을 발견하고 싶은 곳도 바로 그 지점이었다. 불스트로드 형제가 두각을 나타내던 사람들은 아주 적었지만, 그들은 그에게 무척 가까웠고 그래서 그의 만족감을 더욱 자극했다. 그의 영향력은 좁은 공간에 미쳤지만, 그래서 그는 그 효과를 더욱 강렬하게 느꼈다. 그는 자기 안에서 이루어지는 은총의 독특한 역사(役事)를, 그리고 하느님이 자기를 특별한 도구로 삼으려 하신다는 징표를 힘들이지 않고 믿었다.\n\n이윽고 전환의 순간이 왔다. 상업 자선 학교에서 교육받은 고아였던 그가 신도들 가운데 가장 부유한 사람인 던커크 씨 소유의 멋진 별장으로 초대받았을 때, 그는 승진의 감각을 맛보았다. 곧 그는 그곳의 친밀한 사람이 되어, 아내에게는 경건함으로 존경받고, 번성하는 도심과 웨스트엔드 무역으로 부를 일군 남편에게는 능력으로 점찍혔다. 그것이 그의 야망에 새로운 물줄기가 흘러들기 시작한 계기였으니, ‘도구가 되리라’는 그의 전망을 빼어난 종교적 재능과 성공적인 사업의 결합 쪽으로 향하게 했다.\n\n머지않아 분명한 외적 인도가 찾아왔다. 신임받던 부수적 동업자가 죽었는데, 주인이 보기에 절실히 느껴지는 그 공백을 메우기에 젊은 친구 불스트로드만큼 적합한 사람이 없어 보였던 것이니, 다만 그가 신임받는 회계사가 되어 준다면 말이었다. 제안은 받아들여졌다. 그 사업은 전당포로서, 규모로 보나 수익으로 보나 더없이 호화로운 종류였다. 그리고 그것을 잠시 겪어 보고서 불스트로드는 그 호화로운 수익의 한 원천이, 물건이 어디서 왔는지 엄밀히 따지지 않고 제공되는 어떤 물건이든 손쉽게 받아들이는 데 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 하지만 웨스트엔드에 지점이 있었으니, 수치심을 떠올리게 할 옹졸함이나 추레함은 전혀 없었다.\n\n그는 처음 움츠러들던 순간들을 기억했다. 그것들은 은밀했고, 갖가지 논변으로 채워져 있었으니, 그중 일부는 기도의 형태를 띠었다. 사업은 이미 자리 잡았고 오래된 뿌리를 두고 있었다. 새 진 술집을 차리는 것과 오래된 진 술집에 투자를 받아들이는 것은 다른 일이 아니던가? 길 잃은 영혼들에게서 나온 수익—인간의 거래에서 그것이 시작되는 경계선을 어디에 그을 수 있단 말인가? 그것이야말로 하느님께서 당신의 선택받은 자들을 구원하시는 방식이 아니던가? “당신께서는 아시나이다”—젊은 불스트로드는 그때 이렇게 말했고, 나이 든 불스트로드도 지금 이렇게 말하고 있었다—“제 영혼이 이 모든 것들에서 얼마나 헐겁게 떨어져 앉아 있는지를, 제가 그것들을 모두 황무지에서 여기저기 구해 낸 당신의 정원을 일구는 도구로 여기고 있음을 당신께서는 아시나이다.”\n\n비유와 선례는 모자라지 않았고, 마침내 그 지위를 지키는 것이 자기에게 요구된 봉사처럼 보이게 만든 독특한 영적 체험들도 모자라지 않았다. 재산의 전망은 이미 펼쳐졌고, 불스트로드의 움츠러듦은 여전히 은밀한 것으로 남았다. 던커크 씨는 애초에 움츠러듦이라는 것이 조금이라도 있으리라고는 예상하지 못했으니, 그는 무역이 구원의 도식과 무슨 관계가 있다고는 결코 생각해 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 불스트로드가 두 개의 뚜렷이 구별되는 삶을 영위하고 있다는 것도 사실이었다. 일단 그것이 양립할 수 없다고 느끼지 않도록 스스로를 논변으로 설득해 버리자, 그의 종교 활동은 그의 사업과 양립할 수 없는 것이 아니게 되었다.\n\n마음속으로 다시 그 과거에 둘러싸인 불스트로드는 똑같은 변명들을 가지고 있었다—실로 세월은 거미줄 뭉치처럼 그것들을 끊임없이 복잡하게 두껍도록 자아내, 도덕적 감수성에 두툼한 덧대기를 해 왔던 것이다. 아니, 나이가 자아를 더 갈급하되 덜 즐기게 만들수록, 그의 영혼은 자기가 모든 것을 하느님을 위해 행한다는, 자기 자신을 위해서는 그것에 무관심하다는 믿음으로 더욱 흠뻑 젖어 들었다. 그러나—만약 그가 젊은 날의 가난과 함께 그 머나먼 지점으로 돌아갈 수 있다면—그렇다면, 그때는 그가 선교사가 되기를 택할 것이었다.\n\n하지만 그가 스스로를 가두어 넣은 원인들의 사슬은 계속 이어졌다. 하이베리의 멋진 별장에 우환이 생겼다. 여러 해 전, 외동딸이 부모에게 반항하며 달아나 무대에 섰고, 이제 외아들이 죽었으며, 얼마 후 던커크 씨마저 죽었다. 아내는 소박하고 경건한 여인으로, 그 정확한 성질을 결코 알지 못했던 그 호화로운 무역의 안팎에 든 온갖 재물과 함께 홀로 남겨졌는데, 그녀는 불스트로드를 신뢰하게 되었고, 여인들이 흔히 자기네 사제나 ‘사람이 세운’ 목사를 흠모하듯 순진하게 그를 흠모했다. 얼마 후 두 사람 사이에 결혼이 거론되게 된 것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 하지만 던커크 부인은 자기 딸에 대해 불안과 그리움을 품고 있었으니, 그 딸은 오래도록 하느님께도 부모에게도 잃어버린 자로 여겨져 왔다. 딸이 결혼했다는 것은 알려져 있었지만, 그녀는 완전히 종적을 감추었다. 아들을 잃은 어머니는 손자를 상상하며, 이중의 의미로 딸을 되찾기를 바랐다. 만약 딸을 찾는다면, 여러 손주들을 위한 마련으로—어쩌면 넓은—재산의 물길이 트일 터였다. 던커크 부인이 재혼하기 전에 반드시 딸을 찾으려는 노력이 이루어져야 했다. 불스트로드도 동의했다. 하지만 광고를 비롯한 다른 탐문 방법들이 시도된 끝에, 어머니는 딸을 찾을 수 없다고 믿게 되었고, 재산의 유보 없이 결혼하기로 승낙했다.\n\n그 딸은 찾아진 상태였다. 다만 불스트로드 외에 단 한 사람만이 그것을 알았고, 그는 침묵을 지키고 멀리 떠나는 대가로 돈을 받았다.\n\n그것이 이제 불스트로드가 보지 않을 수 없게 된 적나라한 사실이었으니, 행위가 구경꾼에게 제 모습을 드러낼 때 띠는 그 빳빳한 윤곽 그대로였다. 하지만 그 먼 시절의 그 자신에게는, 그리고 지금 불타는 기억 속에서조차도, 그 사실은 자잘한 토막들로 쪼개져 있었고, 토막마다 닥쳐올 때마다 그것이 의롭다고 입증하는 듯한 논리들로 정당화되었다. 그때까지 불스트로드가 걸어온 길은, 그가 생각하기에, 놀라운 섭리들로 재가받은 것이었으니, 그 섭리들은 큰 재산을 가장 잘 쓰이게 하고 그것을 그릇된 용도에서 거두어들이는 일에 그가 대리인이 되도록 길을 가리키는 듯했다. 죽음과 그 밖의 두드러진 처분들이, 이를테면 여성다운 신뢰 같은 것들이 찾아왔던 것이다. 불스트로드라면 크롬웰의 말을 받아들였을 것이었다—“이것들을 한낱 사건이라 부르는가? 주께서 그대를 가엾이 여기시기를!” 사건들은 비교적 작은 것이었지만, 본질적인 조건은 거기 있었으니—다시 말해, 그것들이 자기 자신의 목적에 유리했다는 것이다. 자기 자신에 관한 하느님의 뜻이 무엇인지 물어봄으로써 자기가 남들에게 갚아야 할 것이 무엇인지 정하는 일은 그에게 쉬웠다. 가장 가벼운 일들에 몸을 내맡긴, 그것을 하찮은 데 흩뿌려 버릴지도 모를 한 젊은 여인과 그 남편에게—놀라운 섭리의 길 밖에 놓여 있는 듯한 사람들에게—이 재산이 상당한 비율로 돌아가는 것이 하느님을 섬기는 일일 수 있겠는가? 불스트로드는 미리 “그 딸을 찾아내지 말지어다”라고 스스로에게 말한 적은 결코 없었다. 그럼에도 그 순간이 닥치자 그는 그녀의 존재를 숨겼고, 다른 순간들이 뒤따르자 그 불행한 젊은 여인이 더는 살아 있지 않을지도 모른다는 위안으로 어머니를 달랬다.\n\n불스트로드가 자기 행위가 의롭지 않다고 느끼는 시간들도 있었다. 하지만 어떻게 되돌아갈 수 있단 말인가? 그는 영적 수련을 했고, 스스로를 아무것도 아닌 자라 일컬었으며, 구속(救贖)을 붙들고, 도구가 되는 자기 길을 계속 걸었다. 그리고 오 년 후, 죽음이 다시 찾아와 그의 아내를 데려감으로써 그의 길을 넓혀 주었다. 그는 점차 자기 자본을 빼냈지만, 사업을 끝내는 데 필요한 희생은 치르지 않았으니, 그 사업은 그 후로도 십삼 년 동안 이어지다가 마침내 무너졌다. 그러는 동안 니컬러스 불스트로드는 자신의 십만 파운드를 신중하게 굴려, 지방에서 견실하게 중요한 인물이 되었다—은행가, 국교회 신자, 공공 자선가로. 또한 그는 여러 무역 사업의 익명 동업자이기도 했는데, 거기서 그의 능력은 원료의 비용 절감으로 향했으니, 빈시 씨의 비단을 썩게 만든 그 염료의 경우가 그러했다. 그리고 이제, 이 존경받는 처지가 거의 삼십 년 동안 흔들림 없이 지속되어 온 지금—그것에 앞섰던 모든 것이 오랫동안 의식 속에서 마비된 채 누워 있던 지금—그 과거가 솟아올라, 마치 연약한 존재를 짓누르는 새로운 감각의 끔찍한 침입이 일어난 것처럼 그의 생각을 잠기게 했다.\n\n그러는 동안, 래플스와의 대화에서 그는 무언가 중대한 것을, 그의 갈망과 공포의 싸움 속으로 능동적으로 끼어드는 무언가를 알게 되었다. 거기에, 그는 생각했다, 영적인—어쩌면 물질적인—구제로 향하는 길이 트여 있었다.\n\n영적인 종류의 구제는 그에게 진정한 필요였다. 세상을 속이기 위해 의식적으로 신앙과 감정을 꾸며 내는 조잡한 위선자들도 있을지 모르나, 불스트로드는 그런 사람이 아니었다. 그는 단지 자신의 욕망이 이론적 신념보다 더 강했던 사람, 그리고 자신의 욕망의 충족을 그 신념들과 흡족하게 일치하는 것으로 차츰 설명해 낸 사람이었을 뿐이다. 이것이 위선이라면, 그것은 우리가 어떤 신앙 고백에 속하든, 우리 종족의 미래의 완성을 믿든 세상의 종말로 정해진 가장 가까운 날짜를 믿든, 우리 자신을 포함한 구원받을 소수의 잔존자를 위한 썩어 가는 둥지로 지구를 여기든 인류의 연대성에 열렬한 믿음을 품든, 우리 모두에게서 이따금 드러나는 과정이다.\n\n종교의 대의에 그가 할 수 있는 봉사는 평생토록 그가 자신의 행동 선택의 근거로 스스로에게 내세운 것이었다. 그것은 그가 기도 속에 쏟아부은 동기였다. 그가 돈과 지위를 쓰려고 의도한 것보다 더 잘 쓸 사람이 누가 있겠는가? 자기혐오와 하느님의 대의를 드높이는 일에서 그를 능가할 자가 누가 있겠는가? 그리고 불스트로드 씨에게 하느님의 대의는 자기 자신의 행실의 올바름과는 구별되는 무언가였다. 그것은 하느님의 적들을 식별할 것을 요구했으니, 그들은 한낱 도구로만 쓰일 자들이었고, 가능하다면 돈과 그에 따르는 영향력에서 떼어 놓는 것이 좋을 자들이었다. 또한, 이 세상 군주의 권능이 가장 활발한 술책을 부리는 무역에 수익성 있게 투자하는 일도, 하느님의 종의 손에서 그 수익을 올바르게 쓰면 거룩해지는 것이었다.\n\n이런 암묵적 추론은, 좁은 동기를 넓은 어구로 둘러대는 일이 영국인에게만 특유한 것이 아니듯, 본질적으로 복음주의 신앙에만 특유한 것이 아니다. 개개인 동료 인간과 직접 공감하는 깊이 뿌리박힌 습관으로 제어되지 않는다면, 우리의 도덕성을 좀먹어 들어갈 수 없는 일반 교리란 없다.\n\n하지만 자기 자신의 탐욕 외에 다른 무언가를 믿는 사람은 필연적으로 양심을, 혹은 그가 어느 정도 자신을 맞추어 가는 기준을 가진다. 불스트로드의 기준은 하느님의 대의에 대한 자신의 쓸모였다. “저는 죄 많고 아무것도 아닌 자이옵니다—쓰임으로써 거룩해질 그릇이옵니다—하오나 저를 쓰소서!”—이것이 그가 중요하고 우월한 존재가 되고자 하는 자신의 거대한 욕구를 억지로 밀어 넣은 틀이었다. 그리고 이제 그 틀이 부서져 완전히 내던져질 위기에 처한 듯한 순간이 찾아온 것이다.\n\n만약 그가 자신을 신적 영광의 더 강한 도구로 만들어 준다는 이유로 스스로 받아들였던 그 행위들이, 도리어 조롱하는 자의 구실이 되고 그 영광을 어둡게 만들 것이라면? 만약 이것이 섭리의 결정이라면, 그는 부정한 제물을 바친 자로서 성전에서 쫓겨난 셈이었다.\n\n그는 오랫동안 회개의 말들을 쏟아부어 왔다. 하지만 오늘은 더 쓰디쓴 맛을 띤 회개가 찾아왔고, 위협하는 섭리가 그를 한낱 교리적 거래에 그치지 않는 어떤 속죄 행위로 몰아붙였다. 그에게는 신적 심판대의 양상이 달라져 있었으니, 자기 자신을 엎드리는 것만으로는 더 이상 충분치 않았고, 그는 손에 배상을 들고 와야 했다. 불스트로드가 가능해 보이는 그런 배상을 시도하려 한 것은 실로 자신의 하느님 앞에서였으니, 큰 두려움이 그의 예민한 육체를 사로잡았고, 수치심의 타는 듯한 다가옴이 그의 안에 새로운 영적 필요를 빚어냈다. 밤낮으로, 다시 솟아올라 위협하는 과거가 그의 안에 양심을 만들어 내는 동안, 그는 어떤 수단으로 평화와 신뢰를 되찾을 수 있을지를—어떤 희생으로 회초리를 멈추게 할 수 있을지를 궁리했다. 이 두려움의 순간들에 그가 품은 믿음은, 만약 자기가 자발적으로 무언가 올바른 일을 한다면 하느님께서 잘못된 행위의 결과로부터 자기를 구해 주시리라는 것이었다. 종교란 그것을 채우는 감정이 바뀔 때에만 바뀔 수 있는 것이기에, 그리고 개인적 두려움의 종교는 미개인의 수준에서 거의 벗어나지 못하기에.\n\n그는 래플스가 실제로 브래싱행 마차를 타고 떠나는 것을 보았고, 이것은 일시적인 위안이었다. 그것은 당장의 두려움의 압박을 덜어 주었지만, 영적 갈등과 보호를 얻어야 할 필요에 종지부를 찍지는 못했다. 마침내 그는 어려운 결심에 이르러, 윌 래디슬로에게 편지를 써서 그날 저녁 아홉 시에 슈러브스에서 사적인 면담을 갖도록 와 달라고 청했다. 윌은 그 요청에 별다른 놀라움을 느끼지 않았고, 그것을 『파이어니어』에 관한 어떤 새로운 구상과 연결 지어 생각했다. 하지만 불스트로드 씨의 사적인 방으로 안내되었을 때, 그는 그 은행가의 얼굴에 어린 고통스럽게 수척한 기색에 충격을 받아, “편찮으십니까?” 하고 말하려다가, 그런 무람없음을 스스로 억누르고는, 그저 불스트로드 부인과 그녀를 위해 사 준 그림에 대한 그녀의 만족을 물었을 뿐이다.\n\n“고맙네, 그 사람은 아주 만족하고 있다네. 오늘 저녁엔 딸들과 함께 외출했지. 자네를 오라고 청한 것은, 래디슬로 군, 자네에게 전하고 싶은 매우 사적인—실로, 신성하리만치 은밀한 성질의 이야기가 있어서라네. 자네 생각에서 가장 멀리 떨어져 있었던 것이, 자네의 내력을 나의 내력과 이어 줄 수 있는 중요한 인연이 과거에 있었다는 사실이리라고 짐작하네.”\n\n윌은 전기 충격 비슷한 무언가를 느꼈다. 그는 이미 과거의 인연이라는 주제에 대해 예민한 감수성과 좀처럼 가라앉지 않은 동요의 상태에 있었고, 그의 예감은 유쾌하지 않았다. 그것은 꿈의 출렁임 같았으니—마치 그 시끄럽고 부풀어 오른 낯선 자가 시작한 행위를, 이 핏기 없는 눈에 병약해 보이는 이 존경받는 인물이 이어 가고 있는 것 같았다. 그의 가라앉은 어조와 매끄러운 격식의 말투는 이 순간 그에게, 기억 속 그 둘의 대비 못지않게 거의 거슬렸다. 그는 얼굴빛이 뚜렷이 변하며 대답했다—\n\n“네, 정말이지, 전혀 없습니다.”\n\n“자네 앞에 있는 것은, 래디슬로 군, 깊은 타격을 입은 사람이라네. 양심의 절박함과, 내가 사람이 보는 것처럼 보지 않으시는 그분의 심판대 앞에 서 있다는 인식이 아니라면, 나는 오늘 밤 자네를 이리로 오라고 청한 나의 목적이었던 이 고백을 할 아무런 강제도 받지 않을 것이네. 인간의 법으로 따지자면, 자네는 나에게 그 어떤 권리도 없다네.”\n\n윌은 의아하다기보다도 한층 더 불편했다. 불스트로드 씨는 한쪽 손에 머리를 기대고 바닥을 바라보며 말을 멈추었다. 하지만 이제 그는 살피는 시선을 윌에게 고정하고 말했다—\n\n“자네 어머니의 이름이 세라 던커크였고, 친지들에게서 달아나 무대에 섰다고 들었네. 또한 자네 아버지가 한때 병으로 몹시 쇠약했었다고도. 이 진술들을 확인해 줄 수 있는지 물어봐도 되겠나?”\n\n“네, 모두 사실입니다,” 윌이 말했는데, 그는 은행가의 앞선 암시들에 앞서는 서두가 되었으리라 예상할 법한 탐문이 들어온 순서에 놀랐다. 하지만 불스트로드 씨는 오늘 밤 자신의 감정의 순서를 따랐던 것이다. 그는 배상의 기회가 왔다는 것을 추호도 의심하지 않았고, 자신이 징벌을 면하기를 간구하는 그 참회의 표현을 향한 압도적인 충동에 휩싸여 있었다.\n\n“자네 어머니의 집안에 대해 무슨 자세한 사정을 아는가?” 그가 계속했다.\n\n“아니요. 어머니는 그 이야기를 결코 하고 싶어 하지 않으셨습니다. 어머니는 매우 너그럽고 명예로운 분이셨습니다,” 윌이 거의 화난 듯이 말했다.\n\n“나는 그분에 대해 무엇 하나 탓하려는 게 아니라네. 어머니가 자네에게 자기 어머니에 대해 전혀 언급하신 적이 없는가?”\n\n“어머니가 당신의 어머니는 당신이 달아난 이유를 모르셨던 것 같다고 말씀하시는 걸 들은 적이 있습니다. 어머니는 가엾어하는 어조로 ‘불쌍한 어머니’라고 하셨지요.”\n\n“그 어머니가 나의 아내가 되었네,” 불스트로드가 말하고는, 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. “자네는 나에게 권리가 있다네, 래디슬로 군. 앞서 말했듯이, 법적인 권리는 아니지만, 나의 양심이 인정하는 권리라네. 나는 그 결혼으로 부유해졌네—만약 자네의 외할머니가 따님을 찾아낼 수 있었더라면 아마도 일어나지 않았을—분명 같은 정도로는 일어나지 않았을 결과였지. 그 따님은, 내가 알기로, 이제 살아 있지 않다네!”\n\n“네,” 윌이 말했는데, 의심과 혐오가 그의 안에서 너무도 강하게 솟구쳐, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 채 모른 채 바닥에서 모자를 집어 들고 일어섰다. 그의 안에 인 충동은 드러난 그 인연을 거부하라는 것이었다.\n\n“제발 앉게나, 래디슬로 군,” 불스트로드가 근심스럽게 말했다. “이 발견의 갑작스러움에 자네가 놀란 것은 틀림없는 일이지. 하지만 이미 내적 시련에 짓눌려 있는 한 사람에게 인내를 베풀어 주기를 간청하네.”\n\n윌은 다시 자리에 앉으며, 한 노인의 이 자발적인 자기 비하에 대해 절반은 경멸이 섞인 어떤 연민을 느꼈다.\n\n“나의 바람은, 래디슬로 군, 자네 어머니가 당한 박탈에 대해 보상하는 것이라네. 나는 자네가 재산이 없다는 것을 알고 있고, 만약 자네의 외할머니가 자네 어머니의 존재를 확신하고 그분을 찾아낼 수 있었더라면 아마 이미 자네 것이 되었을 재물에서 자네에게 충분히 마련해 주고 싶네.”\n\n불스트로드 씨는 말을 멈추었다. 그는 자신의 듣는 이의 판단으로 볼 때 자기가 두드러진 양심의 행위를 수행하고 있으며, 하느님의 눈으로 볼 때 참회의 행위를 하고 있다고 느꼈다. 그는 윌 래디슬로의 마음 상태에 대한 아무런 실마리도 갖지 못했으니, 그 마음은 래플스의 명백한 암시로 쓰라려하고 있었고, 그가 기꺼이 다시 어둠 속으로 불러들이고 싶어 했을 발견들에 대한 예상으로 추론하는 타고난 민첩함이 자극받은 상태였다. 윌은 몇 순간 동안 아무 대답이 없었는데, 말을 마치며 바닥으로 눈을 내리깔았던 불스트로드 씨가 이제 살피는 시선으로 눈을 들었고, 윌은 그 시선을 정면으로 마주하며 말했다—\n\n“선생님은 제 어머니의 존재를 알고 계셨고, 그분이 어디 계실지도 알고 계셨던 것 같군요.”\n\n불스트로드는 움츠러들었다—그의 얼굴과 손에 눈에 띄는 떨림이 일었다. 그는 자신의 제의가 이런 식으로 응대되리라고는, 혹은 자기가 미리 필요하다고 정해 둔 것보다 더 많은 폭로로 떠밀리리라고는 전혀 대비가 되어 있지 않았다. 하지만 그 순간 그는 감히 거짓말을 할 수 없었고, 앞서 어느 정도 자신 있게 밟아 왔던 자신의 발판이 갑자기 불확실하게 느껴졌다.\n\n“자네가 옳게 짐작했다는 것을 부인하지 않겠네,” 그가 더듬거리는 어조로 대답했다. “그래서 나는 나로 인해 손실을 입은, 아직 남아 있는 유일한 사람인 자네에게 속죄하고 싶은 것이라네. 자네는, 바라건대, 나의 목적을 이해해 주겠지, 래디슬로 군. 그 목적은 한낱 인간적 권리보다 더 높은 것과 관련이 있고, 이미 말했듯이, 그 어떤 법적 강제와도 전적으로 무관하다네. 나는 내 평생 동안 자네에게 해마다 오백 파운드를 주기로 스스로를 묶고, 내가 죽을 때 그에 비례하는 자본을 자네에게 남겨 줌으로써 나 자신의 재력과 내 가족의 전망을 줄일 준비가 되어 있네—아니, 자네 쪽의 어떤 칭찬할 만한 계획에 더 많은 것이 분명히 필요하다면, 그 이상까지도 할 준비가 되어 있네.” 불스트로드 씨는 이런 세부 사항들이 래디슬로에게 강하게 작용하여 다른 감정들을 감사에 찬 수락 속으로 가라앉히리라는 기대로 거기까지 나아갔던 것이다.\n\n하지만 윌은 입술을 삐죽 내밀고 두 손을 옆 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 더없이 완강한 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그는 조금도 마음이 움직이지 않았고, 단호하게 말했다—\n\n“선생님의 제의에 어떤 대답이라도 하기 전에, 불스트로드 씨, 한두 가지 질문에 답해 주시기를 청해야겠습니다. 선생님께서 말씀하신 그 재산이 애초에 만들어진 그 사업과 관련이 있으셨습니까?”\n\n불스트로드 씨의 생각은 ‘래플스가 그에게 말했구나’였다. 자기가 그 질문을 끌어낸 말을 자청해 놓고서 어떻게 대답을 거절할 수 있단 말인가? 그는 “그렇네”라고 대답했다.\n\n“그렇다면 그 사업은—혹은 그렇지 않았는지—철저히 떳떳치 못한 것이었습니까—아니, 그 성질이 공개되었더라면 거기 관련된 자들을 도둑이나 죄수와 같은 부류로 놓았을 만한 것이었습니까?”\n\n윌의 어조에는 에는 듯한 신랄함이 있었으니, 그는 자신의 질문을 할 수 있는 한 적나라하게 던지고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔다.\n\n불스트로드는 억누를 수 없는 분노로 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 그는 자기 비하의 장면에 대비하고 있었지만, 그가 은혜를 베풀려 했던 이 젊은이가 심판관 같은 태도로 자기에게 맞서자, 그의 격렬한 자존심과 우위에 익숙한 습관이 참회를, 심지어 두려움마저 압도해 버렸다.\n\n“그 사업은 내가 거기 관련되기 전에 이미 자리 잡혀 있었네, 군. 또한 그런 종류의 탐문을 시작하는 것은 자네가 할 일이 아니라네,” 그가 목소리를 높이지는 않았으되, 재빠른 반항기를 담아 말했다.\n\n“아니요, 제가 할 일입니다,” 윌이 손에 모자를 들고 다시 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. “선생님과 거래를 할 것인지, 선생님의 돈을 받을 것인지를 제가 결정해야 할 때, 그런 질문을 하는 것은 지극히 제가 할 일입니다. 저의 흠 없는 명예는 제게 중요합니다. 저의 출생과 인연에 아무런 오점이 없는 것이 제게 중요합니다. 그런데 이제 저는 제가 어쩔 수 없는 오점이 있다는 것을 알게 되었군요. 저의 어머니도 그것을 느끼셨고, 거기서 할 수 있는 한 멀리 떨어져 있으려 하셨습니다. 저도 그럴 것입니다. 선생님의 부정하게 얻은 돈은 선생님이 가지십시오. 만약 저에게 제 재산이 조금이라도 있다면, 선생님이 제게 말씀하신 것을 반증할 수 있는 누구에게든 그것을 기꺼이 내놓겠습니다. 제가 선생님께 감사드릴 것은, 제가 그 돈을 거절할 수 있는 지금까지 선생님이 그 돈을 간직해 두신 것입니다. 한 사람이 신사라는 것은 마땅히 그 자신에게 달려 있어야 합니다. 안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.”\n\n불스트로드는 막 말을 하려던 참이었으나, 윌은 단호한 민첩함으로 순식간에 방을 빠져나갔고, 다음 순간 현관문이 그의 등 뒤에서 닫혔다. 그는 자신의 인식 속으로 떠밀려 들어온 이 물려받은 오점에 대한 열정적인 반항에 너무도 강하게 사로잡혀 있어서, 자기가 불스트로드에게 너무 가혹했던 것은 아닌지—시간이 모든 것을 헛되게 만들어 버린 마당에 만회의 노력을 기울이고 있는 예순 살 노인에게 너무 오만하게 무자비했던 것은 아닌지—지금으로서는 돌이켜 볼 겨를이 없었다.\n\n제삼자가 귀 기울여 듣고 있었다 한들 윌의 거부가 지닌 격렬함이나 그 말들의 신랄함을 온전히 이해하지는 못했을 것이다. 자기 자신의 존엄이라는 감정과 결부된 모든 것이 그에게는 도러시아와의 관계, 그리고 캐소본 씨가 그를 대한 방식과 곧바로 연관된다는 것을, 그때 그 자신 말고는 아무도 알지 못했다. 그리고 불스트로드의 그 제의를 내던지게 한 충동들의 격류 속에는, 자기가 그것을 받아들였노라고 도러시아에게 결코 말할 수 없으리라는 의식이 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n불스트로드로 말하자면—윌이 떠나자 그는 격렬한 반작용을 겪으며, 여인처럼 울었다. 그가 래플스보다 더 지체 높은 누군가로부터 노골적인 경멸의 표현을 마주한 것은 그것이 처음이었다. 그리고 그 경멸이 독처럼 그의 온몸으로 서둘러 퍼지면서, 위안에 반응할 감수성은 조금도 남지 않았다. 하지만 우는 데서 오는 위안도 억눌러야 했다. 그의 아내와 딸들이 곧 어느 동방 선교사의 강연을 듣고 집으로 돌아왔고, 그들이 그에게 들려주려 애쓰는 그 흥미로운 이야기들을 아버지가 애초에 듣지 못한 것을 못내 아쉬워했다.\n\n어쩌면, 다른 모든 숨겨진 생각들을 통틀어, 가장 큰 위안을 내쉬게 한 생각은, 적어도 윌 래디슬로는 그날 저녁 일어난 일을 공표하지 않으리라는 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXII.\n\n헝가리 왕의 딸을 사랑했더라.\n—옛 로맨스\n\n윌 래디슬로의 마음은 이제 온통 도러시아를 다시 만나고 곧장 미들마치를 떠나는 일에 쏠려 있었다. 벌스트로드와 마음을 뒤흔드는 장면을 겪은 다음 날 아침, 그는 그녀에게 짧은 편지를 썼다. 여러 사정으로 예상보다 오래 이 근방에 머물게 되었다는 말과 함께, 가능한 한 빠른 날, 그녀가 정해 주는 시간에 다시 로윅으로 찾아가도 좋겠느냐고 허락을 구하는 내용이었다. 떠나고 싶은 마음은 간절하나, 그녀가 만남을 허락하기 전에는 떠날 수 없다고 적었다. 그는 그 편지를 사무소에 맡기고, 심부름꾼에게 로윅 저택으로 가져가 답을 기다리라고 일렀다.\n\n래디슬로는 다시 한번 마지막 작별의 말을 청하는 일이 어색하게 느껴졌다. 앞서의 작별은 제임스 체텀 경이 듣는 자리에서 이루어졌고, 집사에게까지 그것이 마지막이라고 공언된 터였다. 다시 나타나리라 예상하지 않은 자리에 도로 나타나는 것은 분명 한 남자의 품위에 거슬리는 일이었다. 첫 작별에는 애틋함이 있으나, 두 번째 작별을 위해 돌아오는 일은 희극의 빌미를 제공하는 법이며, 윌이 머뭇거리는 동기를 두고 신랄한 비웃음이 떠돌 가능성마저 있었다. 그래도 전체적으로 보면, 도러시아에게 자신이 그 만남을 진정으로 갈구했음을 이해시키고 싶었던 만큼, 우연을 가장하는 어떤 술책을 쓰기보다 그녀를 만날 가장 직접적인 방법을 택하는 편이 그의 감정에 더 흡족했다. 전에 그녀와 헤어졌을 때 그는 두 사람 사이의 관계에 새로운 국면을 부여하고, 그가 당시 믿었던 것보다 더 절대적인 단절을 짓는 사실들을 알지 못했다. 그는 도러시아의 개인적 재산에 관해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그런 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보는 데 익숙하지 않았기에, 캐소본 씨의 처분에 따르면 자신, 곧 윌 래디슬로와의 결혼은 그녀가 빈털터리가 되기를 동의하는 것을 의미한다고 당연히 여겼다. 그것은 그의 은밀한 마음속에서조차 바랄 수 있는 일이 아니었으니, 설령 그녀가 그를 위해 그처럼 가혹한 처지를 기꺼이 감수할 준비가 되어 있었다 해도 마찬가지였다. 게다가 어머니 가문에 관한 그 폭로의 새로운 아픔도 있었으니, 만약 그것이 알려진다면 도러시아의 친지들이 그를 그녀에게 도무지 어울리지 않는 천한 자로 깔보아야 할 또 하나의 이유가 될 터였다. 몇 해가 지난 뒤 적어도 그녀의 재산에 맞먹는 인간적 가치를 갖추었다는 자각을 품고 돌아올 수 있으리라던 은밀한 희망은, 이제 한낱 꿈의 꿈결 같은 연장처럼 보였다. 이 변화야말로 도러시아에게 한 번 더 만나 달라고 청하는 일을 정당화해 줄 것이 분명했다.\n\n그러나 그날 아침 도러시아는 윌의 편지를 받기에는 집에 없었다. 일주일 안에 집으로 돌아오겠다는 삼촌의 편지를 받은 까닭에, 그녀는 그 소식을 전하러 먼저 프레시트로 마차를 몰았고, 그다음에는 삼촌이 맡긴 몇 가지 지시를 전하러 그레인지로 갈 작정이었다. 삼촌은 \"이런 자질구레한 정신적 소일거리가 과부에게 좋은 법\"이라고 말했던 것이다.\n\n만약 윌 래디슬로가 그날 아침 프레시트에서 오간 이야기를 엿들을 수 있었다면, 그가 이 근방에 머무는 일을 두고 어떤 사람들이 기꺼이 비웃을 것이라던 자신의 모든 추측이 옳았음을 절감했을 것이다. 실제로 제임스 경은 도러시아에 관해서는 크게 안심했으나, 래디슬로의 동향을 알아내려 줄곧 주시하고 있었고, 이 일에 관해 필연적으로 비밀을 함께 나누고 있던 스탠디시 씨라는 정통한 정보원을 두고 있었다. 래디슬로가 곧 떠나겠다고 공언하고도 거의 두 달이나 미들마치에 머물렀다는 사실은 제임스 경의 의심을 더 쓰라리게 만들거나, 적어도 그가 그 \"젊은 친구\"에게 품은 반감을 정당화하기에 충분했다. 그는 그 청년을 가볍고 변덕스러우며, 가문의 인연이나 엄격한 직업으로 단단히 매여 있지 않은 처지에 으레 따르기 마련인 무분별을 보일 법한 자로 여기고 있었다. 그런데 그는 방금 스탠디시에게서 어떤 이야기를 들었는데, 그것은 윌에 관한 이런 억측을 정당화하는 한편, 도러시아와 관련된 모든 위험을 무화할 방도를 제시해 주는 것이었다.\n\n뜻하지 않은 상황은 우리 모두를 다소 평소답지 않게 만들곤 한다. 아무리 위엄 있는 사람이라도 재채기를 해야만 하는 처지가 있고, 우리의 감정도 그와 똑같이 어울리지 않는 방식으로 좌우될 수 있다. 선량한 제임스 경은 이날 아침 평소답지 않게도, 보통은 두 사람 모두에게 수치스러운 일이기라도 한 듯 피하던 어떤 화제에 관해 도러시아에게 무언가 말하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다. 그는 마음속에 품은 그런 종류의 소문을 실리아가 알기를 원치 않았으므로, 실리아를 매개로 삼을 수 없었다. 그래서 도러시아가 도착하기 전까지 그는, 수줍음 많고 말주변 없는 자신이 어떻게 그 이야기를 꺼낼 수 있을지 상상해 보려 애쓰고 있었다. 그녀가 뜻밖에 나타나자 그는 불쾌한 말을 꺼낼 능력에 관해 완전히 절망에 빠졌으나, 그 절망이 한 가지 방책을 일러 주었다. 그는 마구간지기를 안장 없는 말에 태워 공원을 가로질러 캐드월래더 부인에게 연필로 쓴 쪽지를 보냈다. 부인은 이미 그 소문을 알고 있었고, 필요한 만큼 몇 번이고 그것을 되풀이하는 일을 자기 체면을 손상하는 것으로 여기지 않을 사람이었다.\n\n도러시아는 만나고 싶어 하던 가스 씨가 한 시간 안에 저택에 들르기로 되어 있다는 그럴듯한 핑계로 붙잡혀 있었고, 자갈길에서 아직 케일럽과 이야기를 나누는 중이었다. 그때 목사 부인을 기다리며 주시하던 제임스 경이 그녀가 오는 것을 보고 마중 나가 필요한 귀띔을 했다.\n\n\"됐어요! 알겠습니다.\" 캐드월래더 부인이 말했다. \"당신은 결백하실 거예요. 저야 워낙 시커먼 사람이라 더 더러워질 것도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 중대한 일이라는 뜻은 아닙니다.\" 제임스 경은 캐드월래더 부인이 너무 많이 알아차리는 것이 못마땅하여 말했다. \"다만 도러시아가 그를 다시 받아들여서는 안 될 이유가 있다는 걸 알았으면 하는 겁니다. 그런데 나로서는 도무지 그녀에게 그렇게 말할 수가 없어요. 당신 입에서라면 가볍게 나올 테고요.\"\n\n과연 그것은 아주 가볍게 흘러나왔다. 도러시아가 케일럽 곁을 떠나 그들을 맞이하러 돌아섰을 때, 캐드월래더 부인은 세상에서 가장 우연한 발걸음으로 공원을 가로질러, 그저 실리아와 아기에 관해 부인네답게 한담이나 나누려 들른 듯이 보였다. 그래서, 브룩 씨가 돌아오신다고요? 반가운 일이로군요! 돌아오시면, 부디 의회 열병과 개척 사업에서 말끔히 나으셨기를. 그 「개척자」지 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 누군가는 그 신문이 곧 죽어 가는 돌고래 꼴이 되어 제 한 몸 어쩔 줄도 모른 채 온갖 빛깔로 변할 거라고 예언했다지요. 브룩 씨가 후원하던 그 명민한 청년 래디슬로가 떠났거나 떠나려 하니 말이에요. 제임스 경은 그 이야기를 들으셨던가요?\n\n세 사람은 자갈길을 천천히 거닐고 있었는데, 제임스 경은 곁의 관목을 채찍으로 후려치려 몸을 돌리며 그런 비슷한 이야기를 들은 적이 있다고 말했다.\n\n\"전부 거짓말이에요!\" 캐드월래더 부인이 말했다. \"그는 떠나지도, 떠나려는 기색도 보이지 않아요. 「개척자」지는 여전히 제 빛깔을 지키고 있고, 올랜도 래디슬로 씨는 당신네 리드게이트 씨의 부인과 끊임없이 노래나 불러 대며 참으로 짙푸른 추문을 빚어내고 있답니다. 듣자 하니 그 부인은 더없이 예쁘다더군요. 누구든 그 집에 들어가면 이 젊은 신사분이 양탄자에 누워 있거나 피아노 앞에서 노래하는 모습을 보지 않은 적이 없다지 뭐예요. 하기야 공장 도시 사람들이란 늘 평판이 좋지 못한 법이지요.\"\n\n\"부인께서는 처음에 어떤 소문 하나가 거짓이라고 하셨고, 저는 이것도 거짓이라고 믿습니다.\" 도러시아가 분개하는 기운을 띠고 말했다. \"적어도 잘못 전해진 이야기라고 확신해요. 저는 래디슬로 씨에 관해 어떤 험담도 듣지 않겠어요. 그분은 이미 너무 많은 부당함을 겪으셨으니까요.\"\n\n도러시아는 일단 진정으로 마음이 동하면 남이 자기 감정을 어떻게 생각하는지 거의 개의치 않았다. 설령 차분히 헤아릴 수 있었다 해도, 자신이 오해받을까 두려워 윌에 관한 해로운 말 앞에서 침묵을 지키는 일을 그녀는 옹졸하다고 여겼을 것이다. 그녀의 얼굴은 붉게 달아올랐고 입술은 떨렸다.\n\n제임스 경은 그녀를 흘끗 보며 자신의 책략을 후회했다. 그러나 어떤 상황에도 능란한 캐드월래더 부인은 두 손바닥을 바깥으로 펼쳐 보이며 말했다. \"부디 그러기를 바라요, 얘야! 누구에 관해서든 나쁜 이야기는 다 거짓이기를 바란다는 뜻이지. 하지만 젊은 리드게이트가 이런 미들마치 처녀들 가운데 하나와 결혼한 건 안타까운 일이야. 그래도 명색이 누군가의 아들인데, 핏줄에 좋은 피가 흐르고 너무 어리지도 않으며 그의 직업을 견뎌 줄 만한 여자를 얻을 수도 있었을 텐데. 이를테면 클라라 하파거 같은 여자 말이야. 친지들이 그 애를 어쩌지 못해 쩔쩔매는데, 게다가 지참금까지 있거든. 그랬다면 우리 곁에 그 애를 두었을 텐데. 하지만 어쩌겠어! 남을 위해 지혜로워 봐야 소용없는 일이지. 실리아는 어디 있나? 자, 어서 들어갑시다.\"\n\n\"저는 곧장 팁턴으로 가야 해요.\" 도러시아가 다소 거만하게 말했다. \"안녕히 계세요.\"\n\n제임스 경은 그녀를 마차까지 배웅하면서 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 그는 미리 은밀한 굴욕까지 치르며 꾸민 술책의 결과에 도무지 만족할 수 없었다.\n\n도러시아는 열매 맺힌 산울타리와 베어 낸 곡식 밭 사이로 마차를 달렸으나, 주위의 무엇도 보거나 듣지 못했다. 눈물이 솟아 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸지만 그녀는 그것을 알지 못했다. 세상이 추하고 가증스럽게 변해 가는 듯했고, 그녀의 믿음이 깃들 자리는 어디에도 없었다. \"그건 사실이 아니야, 사실이 아니야!\" 그녀가 귀 기울이는 것은 내면의 그 목소리였다. 그러나 그러는 내내 언제나 막연한 불안이 들러붙어 있던 어떤 기억이 자꾸만 그녀의 주의를 비집고 들어왔다. 윌 래디슬로가 리드게이트 부인과 함께 있던 그날, 피아노 반주에 맞춰 노래하는 그의 목소리를 들었던 그날의 기억이었다.\n\n\"그분은 내가 못마땅해하는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다고 했어. 그게 못마땅하다고 말해 줄 수 있었더라면.\" 가엾은 도러시아는 윌에 대한 분노와 그를 열렬히 변호하려는 마음 사이를 묘하게 오가며 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"다들 내 앞에서 그분을 헐뜯으려 하지만, 그분에게 잘못이 없다면 나는 어떤 아픔도 개의치 않겠어. 나는 늘 그분이 선한 사람이라고 믿었어.\" 마차가 그레인지 정문 관리소의 아치 밑을 지나고 있다는 것을 느낀 순간, 이것이 그녀의 마지막 생각이었다. 그녀는 황급히 손수건을 얼굴에 갖다 대고 자신이 할 일들을 떠올리기 시작했다. 마부는 말굽 하나에 문제가 생겼으니 삼십 분쯤 말을 풀어 놓아도 되겠느냐고 청했다. 도러시아는 쉴 수 있겠다는 생각에, 현관 홀의 조각상에 기대어 가정부와 이야기를 나누며 장갑과 보닛을 벗었다. 마침내 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 잠깐 머물러야겠어요, 켈 부인. 덧문을 열어 주시면 서재로 들어가 삼촌 편지에서 몇 가지 적어 드리겠어요.\"\n\n\"덧문은 열려 있습니다, 마님.\" 켈 부인이, 말하면서 이미 걸어가던 도러시아를 따라가며 말했다. \"래디슬로 씨가 거기서 뭔가를 찾고 계십니다.\"\n\n(윌은 짐을 꾸리던 중 빠뜨린 자기 그림이 든 화첩을 가지러 온 것이었고, 그것을 두고 떠나고 싶지 않았다.)\n\n도러시아의 심장은 한 대 얻어맞기라도 한 듯 뒤집히는 것 같았으나, 겉으로는 눈에 띄게 멈칫하지 않았다. 사실 윌이 그곳에 있다는 느낌은 그 순간 그녀에게 더없는 만족이었으니, 잃어버린 줄 알았던 소중한 것을 다시 본 것과도 같았다. 문 앞에 이르자 그녀가 켈 부인에게 말했다.\n\n\"먼저 들어가서, 제가 와 있다고 전해 주세요.\"\n\n윌은 화첩을 찾아내어 방 저쪽 끝 탁자 위에 올려놓고, 그림을 한 장씩 넘기며 도러시아에게는 너무도 신비로워 자연과의 관계를 알 수 없을, 그 잊지 못할 예술 작품을 바라보며 흐뭇해하던 참이었다. 그는 여전히 그것을 보며 미소 짓고 있었고, 미들마치에 돌아가면 그녀의 편지가 기다리고 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 그림들을 가지런히 추스르고 있었다. 그때 그의 팔꿈치 가까이에서 켈 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"캐소본 부인께서 들어오십니다, 나리.\"\n\n윌은 재빨리 돌아섰고, 다음 순간 도러시아가 들어서고 있었다. 켈 부인이 그녀의 뒤로 문을 닫자 두 사람은 마주했다. 서로가 서로를 바라보았고, 의식은 말문을 막아 버리는 무언가에 휩쓸려 넘쳤다. 그들을 침묵하게 한 것은 당혹스러움이 아니었으니, 두 사람 모두 작별이 가까웠음을 느꼈고, 슬픈 작별에는 부끄러움이란 없는 법이다.\n\n그녀는 무심결에 글쓰기 탁자 옆 삼촌의 의자로 다가갔고, 윌은 그 의자를 조금 빼 준 다음 몇 걸음 물러나 그녀 맞은편에 섰다.\n\n\"부디 앉으세요.\" 도러시아가 무릎 위에 두 손을 포개며 말했다. \"당신이 여기 계셔서 정말 기뻐요.\" 윌은 그녀의 얼굴이 로마에서 처음 자기와 악수하던 때와 꼭 같아 보인다고 생각했다. 보닛에 고정되어 있던 과부의 두건이 보닛과 함께 벗겨진 터라, 그는 그녀가 최근에 눈물을 흘렸다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다. 그러나 그녀의 동요에 섞여 있던 분노는 그를 본 순간 사라져 버렸다. 두 사람이 얼굴을 마주할 때면 그녀는 늘 신뢰와, 서로의 이해에서 오는 행복한 자유로움을 느끼는 데 익숙했으니, 남들의 말이 어찌 그 효과를 별안간 가로막을 수 있겠는가? 우리의 온몸을 사로잡고 우리를 위해 공기를 기쁨으로 채우는 음악이 다시 한번 울려 퍼진다면, 그것이 들리지 않을 때 누군가 그 음악을 흠잡는 것을 들었다 한들 무슨 상관이겠는가?\n\n\"오늘 로윅 저택으로 편지를 보내, 당신을 뵙게 해 달라고 청했습니다.\" 윌이 그녀 맞은편에 앉으며 말했다. \"저는 곧 떠납니다. 그런데 당신과 다시 이야기를 나누지 않고는 떠날 수 없었어요.\"\n\n\"몇 주 전에 당신이 로윅에 오셨을 때 우리는 작별한 줄 알았어요. 그때 떠나신다고 하셨으니까요.\" 도러시아가 약간 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"네. 하지만 그때 저는 지금 알게 된 일들을 몰랐습니다. 미래에 관한 제 감정을 바꿔 놓은 일들 말이에요. 전에 당신을 뵈었을 때, 저는 언젠가 다시 돌아올 수 있으리라는 꿈을 꾸고 있었습니다. 그런데 이제는 결코 그러지 못하리라는 생각이 듭니다.\" 여기서 윌은 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"제가 그 이유를 알기를 바라시나요?\" 도러시아가 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 윌은 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 얼굴에 짜증을 띤 채 그녀에게서 시선을 돌리고 격하게 말했다. \"물론 그러기를 바라야지요. 저는 당신의 눈에도, 다른 이들의 눈에도 심하게 모욕당했습니다. 제 인격에 대한 비열한 암시가 있었어요. 저는 당신이 알아주시길 바랍니다. 어떤 상황에서도 저는, 무언가 다른 것을 구한다는 핑계로 돈을 노렸다는 말을 사람들에게 할 빌미를 주는 식으로 결코 제 자신을 낮추지 않았으리라는 것을요. 저를 막아 줄 다른 안전장치 따위는 필요 없었습니다. 재산이라는 안전장치만으로 충분했으니까요.\"\n\n윌은 마지막 말과 함께 의자에서 일어나, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 발길을 옮겼다. 그러나 그곳은 그에게서 가장 가까운, 돌출된 창가였다. 그 창은 일 년 전 거의 같은 계절에 지금처럼 열려 있었고, 그때 그와 도러시아는 그 안에 서서 함께 이야기를 나누었었다. 이 순간 그녀의 온 마음은 윌의 분개에 공감하며 쏟아져 나가고 있었다. 그녀는 다만 자신이 그에게 결코 부당하게 군 적이 없음을 그에게 납득시키고 싶었을 뿐인데, 그는 마치 그녀마저 그 매정한 세상의 일부이기라도 한 듯 그녀에게서 등을 돌린 듯했다.\n\n\"제가 당신에게 어떤 비열함을 갖다 붙인 적이 있다고 생각하신다면, 그건 정말 무정한 일이에요.\" 그녀가 입을 열었다. 그러더니 그녀 특유의 열렬한 태도로, 그에게 호소하고 싶은 마음에 의자에서 일어나 그의 앞을 지나 창가의 옛 자리로 가서 말했다. \"제가 당신을 단 한 번이라도 믿지 않은 적이 있다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n윌은 거기 선 그녀를 보자 흠칫 놀라 그녀의 시선을 마주하지 않은 채 창에서 뒤로 물러났다. 도러시아는 앞서의 성난 어조에 뒤이은 이 몸짓에 상처받았다. 그녀는 그것이 그에게 못지않게 자신에게도 괴로운 일이며, 자신은 어찌할 도리가 없다고 말하고 싶었다. 그러나 두 사람 다 명시적으로 입에 올릴 수 없는 그들 관계의 그 묘한 사정들 탓에, 그녀는 늘 말을 너무 많이 할까 두려워했다. 이 순간 그녀는 윌이 어떤 경우에도 자신과 결혼하고 싶어 했으리라고는 믿지 않았으므로, 그런 믿음을 암시할 만한 말을 쓰기가 두려웠다. 그녀는 다만 그의 마지막 말로 되돌아가 간곡히 말했다.\n\n\"당신을 막을 안전장치 따위는 결코 필요하지 않았다고 저는 확신해요.\"\n\n윌은 대답하지 않았다. 격렬하게 출렁이는 감정 속에서 그녀의 이 말은 그에게 잔인할 만큼 무심하게 들렸고, 성난 폭발 끝에 그의 얼굴은 창백하고 비참해 보였다. 그가 탁자로 가서 화첩을 동여매는 동안, 도러시아는 멀찍이서 그를 바라보았다. 그들은 함께하는 이 마지막 순간들을 비참한 침묵 속에 허비하고 있었다. 그가 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그의 마음속에 완고하게 솟구쳐 오른 것은 차마 입 밖에 낼 수 없도록 스스로 금한, 그녀를 향한 열렬한 사랑이었으니. 그녀는 또 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그녀는 그에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없었으니, 마땅히 그의 것이어야 했던 돈을 어쩔 수 없이 지켜야 하는 처지였으니, 게다가 오늘 그는 그녀의 온전한 신뢰와 호의에 예전처럼 응답하지 않는 듯했으니.\n\n그러나 윌은 마침내 화첩에서 몸을 돌려 다시 창가로 다가왔다.\n\n\"저는 가야 합니다.\" 그는 쓰라린 감정이 이따금 동반하곤 하는 그 특유의 눈빛으로 말했으니, 마치 빛을 너무 가까이서 응시하다 지치고 타 버린 듯한 눈빛이었다.\n\n\"인생에서 무엇을 하실 건가요?\" 도러시아가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"전에 작별 인사를 나누었을 때와 계획이 그대로신가요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 윌이 그 화제를 흥미롭지 않다는 듯 밀쳐 내는 어조로 말했다. \"처음 주어지는 일에 매달려 일할 겁니다. 행복도 희망도 없이 지내는 습관이 드는 모양이지요.\"\n\n\"오, 너무 슬픈 말씀이에요!\" 도러시아가 위태롭게 흐느낌이 터질 듯한 기색으로 말했다. 그러더니 애써 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"우리는 둘 다 말을 너무 강하게 한다는 점에서 닮았다고 늘 의견이 일치했잖아요.\"\n\n\"지금은 말을 너무 강하게 한 게 아닙니다.\" 윌이 벽 모서리에 등을 기대며 말했다. \"한 인간이 평생 단 한 번밖에 겪을 수 없는 일들이 있지요. 그리고 그는 언젠가 자신에게 가장 좋은 것은 이미 끝났음을 알아야만 합니다. 이런 경험이 저에게는 아주 젊은 나이에 닥쳤다, 그뿐입니다. 제가 그 무엇보다도 더 마음 쓰는 것이 저에게는 절대적으로 금지되어 있습니다. 단지 제 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있어서가 아니라, 설령 제 손이 닿는 곳에 있다 해도 제 자존심과 명예가, 제가 스스로를 존중하는 그 모든 것이 저에게 금지하고 있다는 말입니다. 물론 저는 황홀경 속에서 천국을 본 사람이 살아갈 법한 방식으로 계속 살아가겠지요.\"\n\n윌은 도러시아가 이 말을 오해할 리 없으리라 여기며 말을 멈추었다. 사실 그는 이토록 솔직하게 그녀에게 말하는 것이 자기모순이며 스스로의 양심에 어긋나는 일이라고 느꼈다. 그래도, 결코 구애하지 않으리라고 한 여자에게 말하는 것을 공정하게 구애라 부를 수는 없었다. 그것은 유령 같은 구애라고 인정해야만 할 일이었다.\n\n그러나 도러시아의 마음은 그와는 전혀 다른 시각으로 빠르게 과거를 더듬고 있었다. 자신이야말로 윌이 가장 마음 쓰는 대상일지 모른다는 생각이 한순간 그녀를 관통하며 고동쳤으나, 곧 의혹이 뒤따랐다. 둘이 함께 겪은 그 짧은 시간의 기억은, 윌이 끊임없이 곁에 두고 지낸 다른 누군가와 얼마나 더 충만한 교제를 나누었을지를 일러 주는 기억 앞에서 창백해지며 움츠러들었다. 그가 한 말은 모두 그 다른 관계를 가리키는 것일 수 있었고, 그와 그녀 자신 사이에 오간 일은, 그녀가 늘 둘의 단순한 우정이라 여겨 온 것과 남편의 해로운 행위가 거기에 들이민 잔인한 방해로 충분히 설명되었다. 도러시아는 꿈결처럼 눈을 내리깐 채 말없이 서 있었고, 그러는 동안 윌이 리드게이트 부인을 두고 한 말이라는 메스꺼운 확신을 남기는 영상들이 그녀에게 밀려들었다. 그런데 어째서 메스꺼운가? 그는 여기서도 자신의 처신이 의심받을 데가 없기를 그녀가 알아주길 바랐을 뿐인데.\n\n윌은 그녀의 침묵에 놀라지 않았다. 그녀를 지켜보는 동안 그의 마음 역시 어지러이 분주했고, 두 사람의 작별을 가로막을 무언가가 일어나야 한다는 다소 격앙된 느낌에 사로잡혀 있었다. 어떤 기적, 분명 그들 자신의 신중한 말로는 이룰 수 없는 무엇이. 하지만 결국, 그녀가 그를 조금이라도 사랑하기는 했을까? 그녀가 그런 아픔을 겪지 않기를 차라리 바란다고 그는 스스로에게도 가장할 수 없었다. 그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 확신에 대한 은밀한 갈망이 그의 모든 말 밑바닥에 깔려 있음을 그는 부정할 수 없었다.\n\n두 사람은 그렇게 얼마나 오래 서 있었는지 알지 못했다. 도러시아가 눈을 들어 막 입을 열려는데 문이 열리고 하인이 들어와 말했다.\n\n\"말들이 준비되었습니다, 마님. 떠나고 싶으실 때 언제든지요.\"\n\n\"이따가요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. 그러더니 윌에게로 돌아서서 말했다. \"가정부에게 적어 줄 메모가 좀 있어요.\"\n\n\"저는 가야 합니다.\" 문이 다시 닫히자 윌이 그녀에게 다가서며 말했다. \"모레 저는 미들마치를 떠납니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 모든 면에서 옳게 처신하셨어요.\" 도러시아가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 가슴을 짓누르는 압박감에 말하기가 힘겨웠다.\n\n그녀가 손을 내밀자 윌은 잠시 아무 말 없이 그 손을 잡았으니, 그녀의 말이 그에게는 잔인할 만큼 차갑고 그녀답지 않게 들렸기 때문이다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤으나, 그의 눈에는 불만이, 그녀의 눈에는 오직 슬픔만이 어려 있었다. 그는 몸을 돌려 화첩을 옆구리에 끼었다.\n\n\"저는 당신에게 부당하게 군 적이 없어요. 부디 저를 기억해 주세요.\" 도러시아가 솟구치는 흐느낌을 억누르며 말했다.\n\n\"어째서 그런 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\" 윌이 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"마치 제가 다른 모든 걸 잊어버릴 위험에 처하지 않기라도 한 것처럼요.\"\n\n그 순간 그는 정말로 그녀에 대한 분노가 일었고, 그것이 그를 망설임 없이 떠나도록 떠밀었다. 도러시아에게는 모든 것이 한순간의 섬광 같았다. 그의 마지막 말, 문에 이르러 그녀에게 한 멀찍한 인사, 그가 더는 그곳에 없다는 느낌. 그녀는 의자에 주저앉아, 영상과 감정이 그녀에게 밀려드는 가운데 잠시 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다. 뒤따르는 위협적인 행렬에도 불구하고 먼저 찾아온 것은 기쁨이었다. 윌이 사랑하면서도 단념하는 대상이 정말로 그녀 자신이라는 인상에서 오는 기쁨, 명예가 그를 황급히 떼어 놓는, 더 용납될 수 없고 더 비난받을 다른 사랑 따위는 정말로 없었다는 인상에서 오는 기쁨이었다. 그래도 두 사람은 헤어졌지만, 도러시아는 깊이 숨을 들이쉬며 힘이 돌아오는 것을 느꼈다. 이제 그녀는 거리낌 없이 그를 생각할 수 있었다. 그 순간 작별은 견디기 쉬웠으니, 사랑하고 사랑받는다는 첫 자각이 슬픔을 몰아냈던 것이다. 마치 어떤 단단하고 차가운 압박이 녹아내려 그녀의 의식이 펼쳐질 공간을 얻은 듯했다. 그녀의 과거가 더 큰 의미를 띠고 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 그 기쁨은 돌이킬 수 없는 작별 때문에 덜하지 않았으니, 어쩌면 바로 그렇기에 그 순간 더욱 온전했는지도 모른다. 어떤 눈에서도, 어떤 입술에서도 책망이나 경멸 어린 의아함을 상상할 필요가 없었기 때문이다. 그는 책망을 거부하고, 의아함을 경의로 바꾸어 놓는 식으로 처신했던 것이다.\n\n누군가 그녀를 지켜보았다면 그녀 안에 마음을 굳건히 다지는 어떤 생각이 자리 잡았음을 알아보았을 것이다. 창조의 힘이 기쁜 수월함으로 작동할 때 사소한 주의의 요구쯤은, 마치 햇빛이 비쳐 드는 작은 틈에 지나지 않는 듯 충분히 채워지듯이, 이제 도러시아가 메모를 적는 일은 수월했다. 그녀는 가정부에게 명랑한 어조로 마지막 말을 건넸고, 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉았을 때 그녀의 눈은 빛났으며 음울한 보닛 아래 두 뺨은 발그레 피어났다. 그녀는 무거운 \"상장(喪章) 베일\"을 뒤로 젖히고 앞을 바라보며, 윌이 어느 길로 갔을지 궁금해했다. 그가 결백하다는 사실을 자랑스러워하는 것이 그녀의 천성이었고, 그녀의 모든 감정을 관통하며 이런 가락이 흘렀다. \"내가 그를 변호한 건 옳았어.\"\n\n마부는 회색 말들을 빠른 속도로 모는 데 익숙했다. 캐소본 씨는 책상을 떠난 모든 일에서 즐거움을 느끼지 못하고 조급해하며 어떤 여정이든 끝에 다다르고 싶어 했기 때문이다. 그래서 도러시아는 이제 빠르게 실려 갔다. 마차를 타는 것은 즐거웠으니, 밤사이 내린 비가 먼지를 가라앉혔고, 푸른 하늘은 무리 지어 떠가는 거대한 구름의 영역에서 멀리 떨어져 아득히 보였다. 광활한 하늘 아래 대지는 행복한 곳처럼 보였고, 도러시아는 윌을 따라잡아 한 번 더 그를 볼 수 있기를 바라고 있었다.\n\n길모퉁이를 돌자 그가 화첩을 옆구리에 낀 채 거기 있었다. 그러나 다음 순간 그녀는 그를 지나치고 있었고 그는 모자를 들어 인사했다. 그녀는 일종의 고양감에 젖어 거기 앉아 그를 뒤에 남겨 두고 떠난다는 데서 가슴이 저미는 것을 느꼈다. 그녀는 그를 돌아볼 수 없었다. 마치 무심한 사물들의 무리가 두 사람을 갈라놓고 서로 다른 길로 떠밀어, 점점 더 멀리 떼어 놓으며 돌아보는 일을 부질없게 만든 것 같았다. \"우리가 꼭 헤어져야 하나요?\" 하고 말하는 듯한 어떤 신호도 그녀는 보낼 수 없었으니, 마차를 멈추고 그를 기다릴 수 없는 것과 마찬가지였다. 아니, 오늘의 결정을 뒤집을지도 모를 미래로 그녀의 생각이 조금이라도 움직이는 것을 막는 온갖 이유들이 그 얼마나 많이 그녀에게 밀려들었던가!\n\n\"진작 알았더라면. 그분도 알았더라면. 그랬다면 우리는 영영 헤어졌어도 서로를 생각하며 더없이 행복할 수 있을 텐데. 그리고 그분에게 그 돈을 드려서, 그분의 처지를 좀 더 편하게 해 드릴 수 있었더라면!\" 가장 끈질기게 되돌아오는 것은 이런 갈망들이었다. 그러나 그녀의 독립적인 기개에도 불구하고 세상은 그녀를 무겁게 짓눌렀으므로, 윌이 그런 도움을 필요로 하며 세상에서 불리한 처지에 놓여 있다는 이 생각에는, 두 사람 사이의 어떤 더 가까운 관계도 어울리지 않는다는 영상이 늘 따라붙었다. 그것은 그녀와 관계된 모든 사람의 의견 속에 자리 잡고 있는 것이었다. 그녀는 윌의 처신을 떠민 동기들이 얼마나 절박한 것이었는지 온전히 느꼈다. 남편이 두 사람 사이에 놓아둔 장벽에 그녀가 맞설 것이라고 그가 어찌 꿈엔들 상상할 수 있었겠는가? 그녀가 그것에 맞서겠다고 자신에게 어찌 말할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n마차가 멀리서 점점 작아져 가는 동안 윌이 품은 확신에는 훨씬 더 많은 쓰라림이 깃들어 있었다. 예민해진 그의 기분에는 아주 사소한 일들도 그를 괴롭히기에 충분했다. 도러시아가 마차를 타고 자신을 지나쳐 가는 광경, 그러는 동안 자신은 지금의 심정으로는 탐낼 만한 것을 거의 내주지 않는 세상에서 한 자리를 구하는 가련한 신세로 터덜터덜 걸어가고 있다는 느낌, 그것은 그의 처신을 한낱 어쩔 수 없는 일로 보이게 했고 결심을 떠받치던 버팀목을 앗아 갔다. 결국 그는 그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 확신이 없었다. 이런 처지에서 그 고통을 죄다 자기 몫으로만 떠안고도 단지 기쁘다고 가장할 수 있는 남자가 어디 있겠는가?\n\n그날 저녁 윌은 리드게이트 부부와 함께 보냈고, 이튿날 저녁 그는 떠나고 없었다.\n\n이런 사소한 일들이 사소한 인간에게는 위대하다. — 골드스미스\n\n\"요즘 자네의 과학적 불사조인 리드게이트를 자주 보았나?\" 토러 씨가 크리스마스 만찬 모임에서 오른편에 앉은 페어브러더 씨에게 말했다.\n\n\"안타깝지만 자주 보지는 못했습니다.\" 새로운 의학적 빛에 대한 자신의 믿음을 두고 토러 씨가 던지는 농담을 받아넘기는 데 익숙한 부목사가 대답했다. \"저는 외진 곳에 있고 그는 너무 바쁘니까요.\"\n\n\"그래요? 그렇다니 다행입니다.\" 민친 박사가 정중함과 놀라움을 뒤섞어 말했다.\n\n\"그는 새 병원에 많은 시간을 쏟고 있습니다.\" 페어브러더 씨가 말했는데, 그가 이 화제를 이어 가는 데에는 나름의 이유가 있었다. \"그 이야기는 이웃인 캐소본 부인에게서 듣지요. 그분이 그곳에 자주 가시거든요. 부인 말씀으로는 리드게이트가 지칠 줄 모르고 일하며, 불스트로드의 시설을 훌륭하게 만들어 가고 있다더군요. 콜레라가 우리에게 닥칠 경우에 대비해 새 병동을 준비하고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 짐작건대 환자들에게 시험해 볼 치료 이론도 준비하고 있겠지요.\" 토러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 토러, 솔직해지십시오.\" 페어브러더 씨가 말했다. \"당신은 다른 모든 분야에서와 마찬가지로 의학에서도 대담하고 신선한 정신이 주는 이로움을 모를 만큼 어리석은 분이 아닙니다. 그리고 콜레라로 말하자면, 제 생각엔 여러분 누구도 마땅히 무엇을 해야 할지 확신하지 못하고 있어요. 어떤 사람이 새 길을 따라 조금 멀리 나아간다 해도, 대개는 다른 누구보다 자기 자신에게 가장 큰 해를 끼치는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"당신과 렌치는 분명 그에게 고마워해야 합니다.\" 민친 박사가 토러 쪽을 바라보며 말했다. \"그가 피콕의 환자 중에서도 알짜배기를 당신들에게 보내 주었으니까요.\"\n\n\"리드게이트는 풋내기 초년생치고는 무척 호화롭게 살고 있어요.\" 양조업자인 해리 토러 씨가 말했다. \"북부에 있는 친척들이 그를 뒷받침해 주는 모양이지요.\"\n\n\"그러길 바랍니다.\" 치철리 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지 않다면 우리 모두가 그토록 아끼던 그 예쁜 아가씨와 결혼하지 말았어야지요. 제기랄, 동네에서 가장 예쁜 아가씨를 채 가는 남자에게는 원한이 생기는 법이거든요.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 정말로! 게다가 가장 착한 아가씨이기도 하지요.\" 스탠디시 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"내 친구 빈시는 그 결혼을 절반도 마음에 들어 하지 않았어요. 그건 내가 압니다.\" 치철리 씨가 말했다. \"그 사람은 별로 해 주지 않을 거예요. 다른 쪽 친척들이 얼마나 보태 주었는지는 나도 알 수 없지만요.\" 치철리 씨의 말투에는 어딘가 의미심장한 함구의 기색이 있었다.\n\n\"오, 리드게이트가 생계를 위해 개업의 일에 기대를 걸었으리라고는 생각하지 않습니다.\" 토러 씨가 가벼운 빈정거림을 담아 말했고, 그쯤에서 화제는 사그라들었다.\n\n리드게이트의 씀씀이가 개업으로 감당하기에는 분명 지나치게 크다는 암시를 페어브러더 씨가 들은 것은 이번이 처음이 아니었다. 하지만 그는 리드게이트의 결혼 무렵 그 큰 지출을 정당화해 줄, 그리고 개업이 기대에 못 미치더라도 나쁜 결과를 막아 줄 만한 재력이나 전망이 있을 법하다고 생각했다. 어느 날 저녁 그가 옛날처럼 리드게이트와 한담을 나누려고 일부러 수고를 들여 미들마치까지 갔을 때, 그는 리드게이트에게서 평소의 모습과는 사뭇 다른, 흥분된 노력의 기색을 알아차렸다. 평소 리드게이트는 할 말이 있을 때면 침묵을 지키거나 불쑥 활기차게 그 침묵을 깨곤 했었다. 그들이 작업실에 있는 동안 리드게이트는 끈질기게 말을 이어, 어떤 생물학적 견해들의 개연성에 대한 찬반 논거를 늘어놓았다. 그러나 그에게는 끈기 있고 끊임없는 탐구의 이정표가 되어 줄 그런 명확한 이야기나 보여 줄 것이 전혀 없었다. 예전의 그라면 \"모든 탐구에는 수축기와 확장기가 있어야 한다\"고, \"사람의 정신은 인류 전체의 지평과 대물렌즈의 지평 사이에서 끊임없이 팽창하고 수축해야 한다\"고 주장하며 그런 것들을 고집했을 텐데 말이다. 그날 저녁 그는 어떤 개인적인 함의를 거부하기 위해 일부러 두루뭉술하게 말하는 듯 보였다. 그리고 얼마 지나지 않아 그들은 응접실로 들어갔는데, 리드게이트는 로저먼드에게 음악을 들려 달라고 청한 뒤 말없이 의자에 깊숙이 몸을 기댔으나, 그의 눈에는 기이한 빛이 어려 있었다. \"혹시 아편제를 복용했나\" 하는 생각이 페어브러더 씨의 머리를 스쳤다. \"어쩌면 안면 신경통일지도 — 아니면 의료상의 근심거리이거나.\"\n\n리드게이트의 결혼 생활이 즐겁지 않으리라는 생각은 그에게 떠오르지 않았다. 그는 나머지 사람들과 마찬가지로 로저먼드가 상냥하고 고분고분한 사람이라고 믿었다. 다만 그는 늘 그녀가 다소 흥미롭지 못하다고 — 마무리 학교의 견본 카드를 좀 지나치게 닮았다고 여겨 왔고, 그의 어머니는 로저먼드가 헨리에타 노블이 방 안에 있다는 사실을 도무지 알아차리는 법이 없다는 이유로 그녀를 용서하지 못했다. \"하지만 어쨌든 리드게이트가 그녀와 사랑에 빠졌으니,\" 부목사는 속으로 생각했다. \"그녀가 그의 취향에 맞는 사람일 테지.\"\n\n페어브러더 씨는 리드게이트가 자존심이 강한 사람임을 알고 있었지만, 자신에게는 그에 상응하는 기질이 거의 없었고, 어쩌면 비열하거나 어리석지 않다는 품위 외에는 개인적 위엄에 대한 관심이 너무 적었던 탓에, 리드게이트가 자기 사사로운 일에 관한 말이라면 무엇이든 화상이라도 입은 듯 움츠러드는 그 방식을 충분히 헤아리기 어려웠다. 그리고 토러 씨 댁에서의 그 대화가 있고 얼마 지나지 않아, 부목사는 어떤 사실을 알게 되었는데, 그로 인해 그는 리드게이트가 어떤 어려움에 대해 마음을 털어놓고 싶어 할 경우 기꺼이 들어 줄 다정한 귀가 준비되어 있다는 것을 넌지시 알릴 기회를 더욱 간절히 엿보게 되었다.\n\n그 기회는 빈시 씨 댁에서 찾아왔다. 새해 첫날 그곳에서 모임이 열렸는데, 페어브러더 씨는 더 높은 인물이자 부목사인 동시에 교구 목사가 된 첫 새해에 옛 친구들을 저버려서는 안 된다는 명분으로 거절할 수 없게 초대받았다. 그리고 이 모임은 더할 나위 없이 정겨운 자리였다. 페어브러더 집안의 여인들이 모두 참석했고, 빈시가의 아이들은 전부 식탁에서 함께 저녁을 먹었으며, 프레드는 어머니에게 메리 가스를 초대하지 않으면 페어브러더 집안 사람들이 자기들에 대한 결례로 여길 것이라고 설득했다. 메리가 그들의 각별한 친구였기 때문이다. 메리가 왔고, 프레드는 기분이 무척 좋았으나, 그 즐거움에는 얼룩이 섞여 있었다 — 어머니가 메리가 모임의 주요 인사들에게 얼마나 중요한 존재인지 보게 되리라는 의기양양함은, 페어브러더 씨가 메리 곁에 앉자 질투로 잔뜩 줄이 그어졌다. 프레드는 \"페어브러더에게 밀려날까\" 두려워하기 시작하기 전, 그러니까 자기 자질에 대해 훨씬 마음 편하던 시절이 있었는데, 이 공포는 여전히 그의 앞에 도사리고 있었다. 무르익은 부인의 자태를 한껏 뽐내는 빈시 부인은 메리의 자그마한 몸집과 거칠게 물결치는 머리카락, 백합도 장미도 없는 얼굴을 바라보며 의아해했다. 그녀는 메리가 혼례복을 입은 모습에 마음을 쓰는 자신을, 혹은 가스 집안을 \"빼닮은\" 손주들에게 흐뭇해하는 자신을 그려 보려 했으나 헛수고였다. 그래도 모임은 즐거웠고 메리는 유난히 환했다. 프레드를 위해 그의 친구들이 자기에게 점점 더 다정해지는 것이 기뻤고, 또한 그들이 인정할 수밖에 없는 안목 있는 다른 이들에게 자기가 얼마나 높이 평가받는지 그들이 보는 것도 전혀 마다하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n페어브러더 씨는 리드게이트가 지루해하는 듯 보인다는 것과 빈시 씨가 사위에게 되도록 말을 적게 건넨다는 것을 알아차렸다. 로저먼드는 더없이 우아하고 침착했으며, 부목사가 미처 그녀에게 기울이지 않은 종류의 섬세한 관찰력으로만 알아챌 수 있을, 남편의 존재에 대한 관심이 완전히 결여된 모습 — 사랑하는 아내라면 예의상 남편과 거리를 두더라도 반드시 드러내고야 마는 그 관심이 전혀 없다는 사실은 눈에 띄지 않았다. 리드게이트가 대화에 끼어들 때면 그녀는 마치 다른 곳을 바라보도록 빚어진 프시케 조각상이라도 된 듯 그를 한 번도 바라보지 않았다. 그리고 한두 시간 불려 나갔다가 그가 방으로 다시 들어왔을 때, 그녀는 그 사실을 의식조차 못 하는 듯했는데, 열여덟 달 전이라면 그의 등장은 영(零)들 앞에 놓인 숫자 같은 효과를 냈을 것이다. 그러나 실제로 그녀는 리드게이트의 목소리와 움직임을 강렬하게 의식하고 있었다. 의식하지 못하는 척하는 그 예쁘고 온화한 태도는, 예법을 어기지 않으면서 그를 향한 내면의 반감을 충족시키는, 계산된 부정이었다. 디저트 자리에서 리드게이트가 불려 나간 뒤 부인들이 응접실에 모여 있을 때, 페어브러더 부인은 마침 로저먼드가 가까이 있자 이렇게 말했다. \"남편과 함께하는 시간을 많이 포기해야겠군요, 리드게이트 부인.\"\n\n\"네, 의사의 삶은 무척 고되지요. 특히 리드게이트 씨처럼 자기 직업에 헌신적일 때는요.\" 서 있던 로저먼드는 이 반듯한 짧은 인사말을 끝내고는 가벼이 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"손님이 없을 때 저 애는 끔찍이도 따분해한답니다.\" 노부인 곁에 앉아 있던 빈시 부인이 말했다. \"로저먼드가 아파서 제가 함께 머무는 동안 정말 그렇게 느꼈지요. 아시다시피, 페어브러더 부인, 우리 집은 활기찬 집이에요. 저 자신이 명랑한 기질이고, 빈시 씨도 늘 뭔가 일이 벌어지는 걸 좋아하지요. 로저먼드는 그런 데 익숙해 있어요. 시도 때도 없이 밖에 나가 있고, 언제 집에 돌아올지 도무지 알 수 없고, 게다가 제 생각엔 속을 닫아 두는 자존심 센 기질인 남편하고는 아주 딴판이지요.\" 경솔한 빈시 부인은 이 곁들인 말을 할 때 목소리를 살짝 낮추긴 했다. \"하지만 로저먼드는 늘 천사 같은 성품이었답니다. 오빠들이 그 애를 곧잘 못마땅하게 굴어도 그 애는 한 번도 성을 내는 법이 없었어요. 갓난아기 때부터 더없이 착했고, 살결도 이루 말할 수 없이 고왔지요. 하지만 우리 아이들은 모두 성품이 좋아요, 하느님 감사하게도.\"\n\n빈시 부인이 넓은 모자 끈을 뒤로 젖히며 일곱 살에서 열한 살 사이의 어린 세 딸을 향해 미소 짓는 모습을 보는 누구에게나 이 말은 쉽사리 믿어질 만했다. 그러나 미소 띤 그 눈길 속에 그녀는 메리 가스도 어쩔 수 없이 포함시켜야 했는데, 세 소녀가 메리를 구석으로 데려가 이야기를 해 달라고 졸랐기 때문이다. 메리는 마침 룸펠슈틸츠헨의 그 흥미진진한 이야기를 막 끝내 가던 참이었다. 레티가 자기가 좋아하는 빨간 책에서 그 이야기를 가져다 아무것도 모르는 어른들에게 들려주기를 결코 지겨워하지 않았던 덕에, 메리는 그 이야기를 줄줄 외우고 있었다. 빈시 부인의 귀염둥이 루이자가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 진지한 흥분에 사로잡혀 어머니에게 달려와 외쳤다. \"오 엄마, 엄마, 그 난쟁이가 바닥을 어찌나 세게 굴렀던지 다리를 다시 빼낼 수가 없었대요!\"\n\n\"오냐, 내 천사야!\" 어머니가 말했다. \"내일 나한테 그 이야기 다 들려주렴. 가서 들어!\" 그러고는 루이자가 그 매력적인 구석 자리로 돌아가는 모습을 눈으로 좇으며, 그녀는 프레드가 메리를 다시 초대하기를 바란다면 반대하지 않으리라고 생각했다. 아이들이 메리를 그토록 좋아하니 말이다.\n\n그러나 이내 그 구석은 한층 더 활기를 띠었으니, 페어브러더 씨가 들어와 루이자 뒤에 앉아 그 아이를 무릎에 앉혔기 때문이다. 그러자 소녀들은 모두 그가 룸펠슈틸츠헨 이야기를 꼭 들어야 한다고, 메리가 그 이야기를 다시 해야 한다고 졸랐다. 그도 졸랐고, 메리는 야단스럽지 않게 단정한 솜씨로 앞서와 정확히 똑같은 말로 다시 이야기를 시작했다. 가까이 앉아 있던 프레드는, 페어브러더 씨가 아이들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 그 이야기에 깊은 흥미를 느끼는 양 연기하면서도 역력한 감탄의 눈길로 메리를 바라보지만 않았더라면, 메리의 솜씨에 순수한 의기양양함을 느꼈을 것이다.\n\n\"이제 너는 내 외눈박이 거인 이야기 따위는 더 이상 좋아하지 않겠구나, 루.\" 이야기가 끝나자 프레드가 말했다.\n\n\"아니야, 좋아할 거야. 지금 그 이야기 해 줘.\" 루이자가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럴 테지. 난 완전히 밀려났어. 페어브러더 씨께 부탁드려.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 메리가 거들었다. \"페어브러더 씨께 톰이라는 거인에게 아름다운 집이 부서진 개미들 이야기를 해 달라고 부탁드려 봐. 거인은 개미들이 우는 소리를 들을 수도, 손수건을 쓰는 모습을 볼 수도 없었기 때문에 개미들이 아무렇지 않은 줄 알았다는 이야기 말이야.\"\n\n\"부탁이에요.\" 루이자가 부목사를 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 나는 근엄한 늙은 목사란다. 내가 보따리에서 이야기를 꺼내려 하면 그 대신 설교가 나오지. 너희에게 설교를 한 자락 해 줄까?\" 그가 근시용 안경을 쓰고 입을 오므리며 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 루이자가 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 어디 보자. 과자를 경계하라는 설교다. 과자가 얼마나 나쁜 것인지, 특히 달콤하고 그 안에 자두가 들어 있으면 더욱 그렇다는 이야기지.\"\n\n루이자는 그 일을 꽤 진지하게 받아들이고는 부목사의 무릎에서 내려와 프레드에게로 갔다.\n\n\"아, 새해 첫날에 설교는 통하지 않겠구나.\" 페어브러더 씨가 일어나 자리를 옮기며 말했다. 그는 최근 프레드가 자기를 질투하게 되었다는 것을, 또한 자기 자신도 다른 어떤 여자보다 메리를 더 좋아하는 마음을 잃지 않고 있다는 것을 깨달은 터였다.\n\n\"가스 양은 참 사랑스러운 아가씨예요.\" 아들의 거동을 지켜보던 페어브러더 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 노부인이 기대에 차 자기를 돌아보자 대답하지 않을 수 없었던 빈시 부인이 말했다. \"더 예쁘지 못한 게 안타깝지요.\"\n\n\"저는 그렇게 말할 수 없군요.\" 페어브러더 부인이 단호하게 말했다. \"저는 그 아이의 얼굴이 좋아요. 자비로우신 하느님께서 미모 없이도 훌륭한 처녀를 만드는 것이 마땅하다고 보셨을 때, 우리가 늘 미모를 요구해서는 안 되지요. 저는 좋은 몸가짐을 첫째로 꼽는데, 가스 양은 어떤 처지에서든 처신할 줄 알 거예요.\"\n\n노부인의 어조는 다소 날카로웠는데, 메리가 자기 며느리가 되는 일을 내심 염두에 두고 있었기 때문이다. 프레드와 관련한 메리의 처지에는 이런 불편함이 있었으니, 그것을 공공연히 밝히는 것이 적절치 않다는 점이었다. 그래서 로윅 목사관의 세 여인은 여전히 캠던이 가스 양을 택하기를 바라고 있었다.\n\n새 손님들이 들어왔고, 응접실은 음악과 놀이에 내맡겨졌으며, 그동안 홀 건너편 조용한 방에는 휘스트 탁자가 마련되었다. 페어브러더 씨는 어머니의 뜻을 채워 드리려고 한 판을 쳤는데, 어머니는 자신이 이따금 휘스트를 치는 것을 추문과 새로운 견해에 대한 항의로 여겼고, 그런 견지에서는 카드를 잘못 내는 실수조차 나름의 품위를 지녔다. 그러나 한 판이 끝나자 그는 치철리 씨에게 자기 자리를 넘기고 방을 나섰다. 그가 홀을 가로지를 때, 리드게이트가 막 들어와 외투를 벗고 있었다.\n\n\"마침 자네를 찾으러 가려던 참이었네.\" 부목사가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 응접실로 들어가는 대신 홀을 따라 걸어가 벽난로 앞에 섰는데, 서리 내린 찬 공기 덕에 난롯불이 한층 발갛게 타오르는 듯했다. \"보다시피 나는 이제 휘스트 탁자를 아주 쉽게 떠날 수 있다네.\" 그가 리드게이트에게 미소 지으며 말을 이었다. \"이제 돈을 걸고 치지 않으니까. 캐소본 부인 말씀으로는 그게 다 자네 덕이라더군.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 리드게이트가 쌀쌀하게 말했다.\n\n\"아, 자네는 내가 그걸 알기를 바라지 않았겠지. 나는 그런 걸 인색한 함구라고 부른다네. 자네가 누군가에게 좋은 일을 해 주었다는 느낌을 그 사람도 누리게 해 줘야지. 나는 신세 지는 것을 싫어하는 어떤 사람들의 마음을 도무지 이해 못 하겠어. 정말이지 나는 나에게 잘 대해 준 모든 이에게 신세를 지는 편이 더 좋다네.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"제가 언젠가 캐소본 부인께 목사님에 대해 말씀드린 것을 두고 하시는 말씀이 아니라면 말이지요. 하지만 그 일을 발설하지 않겠다는 약속을 부인이 깨실 줄은 몰랐습니다.\" 리드게이트는 벽난로 선반 모서리에 등을 기댄 채 얼굴에 아무런 빛도 내비치지 않으며 말했다.\n\n\"그걸 흘린 건 바로 얼마 전 브룩이었네. 그가 나에게 칭찬을 하더군. 자네가 자기 계산을 헝클어 놓고는, 캐소본 부인이 다른 누구의 이야기도 들으려 하지 않을 만큼 나를 켄이니 틸럿슨이니 하는 부류로 한껏 추켜세우긴 했지만, 그래도 내가 그 성직록을 얻게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 말이야.\"\n\n\"오, 브룩은 입이 가벼운 멍청이지요.\" 리드게이트가 경멸하듯 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 그때만큼은 그 입 가벼움이 고마웠다네. 자네가 나에게 도움을 주고 싶어 했다는 것을 내가 아는 걸 자네가 어째서 싫어해야 하는지 나는 모르겠어, 이 사람아. 그리고 자네는 분명 나에게 도움을 주었네. 자신의 올바른 행실이 얼마나 많이 돈에 궁하지 않은 데 달려 있는지를 깨닫고 나면 자기만족에 꽤 강한 제동이 걸리지. 악마의 도움이 필요 없는 사람이라면, 악마를 기쁘게 하려고 주기도문을 거꾸로 외우라는 유혹에 빠지지 않을 거야. 나는 이제 우연의 미소에 매달릴 필요가 없다네.\"\n\n\"우연 없이 돈을 버는 길이 있다고는 생각하지 않습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"어떤 직업에서 돈을 번다 해도, 그것은 거의 틀림없이 우연으로 들어오는 것이니까요.\"\n\n페어브러더 씨는 리드게이트의 예전 말투와 뚜렷이 대비되는 이 말을, 자기 일이 뜻대로 안 되는 사람의 침울함에서 흔히 솟아나는 심술로 이해할 수 있으리라 생각했다. 그는 너그러운 수긍의 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 세상사 돌아가는 방식에는 엄청난 인내가 필요하지. 하지만 자기를 사랑하고 힘닿는 데까지 도와주는 것 외에 더 바랄 게 없는 친구들이 있다면, 인내하며 기다리기가 그만큼 더 쉬운 법이라네.\"\n\n\"오 그렇지요.\" 리드게이트가 무심한 어조로 자세를 바꾸고 회중시계를 들여다보며 말했다. \"사람들은 자기 어려움을 필요 이상으로 부풀리는 법이지요.\"\n\n이것이 페어브러더 씨가 자기에게 건네는 도움의 제안이라는 것을 그는 더없이 또렷이 알고 있었고, 그것을 견딜 수가 없었다. 우리 인간이란 참으로 기이하게도 고집스러운 존재여서, 오랫동안 자신이 남몰래 부목사에게 도움을 주었다는 느낌에 흐뭇해하던 그였건만, 부목사가 그 보답으로 도움이 필요한 자기 처지를 알아차렸다는 암시에 그는 도저히 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 함구 속으로 움츠러들었다. 게다가 그런 모든 제안의 뒤에 무엇이 따라와야 하겠는가? — 자기가 \"사정을 털어놓고\", 구체적으로 무언가가 필요하다는 것을 내비쳐야 한다는 것이었다. 그 순간에는 차라리 자살하는 편이 더 쉬워 보였다.\n\n페어브러더 씨는 그 대답의 의미를 모를 만큼 둔한 사람이 아니었고, 리드게이트의 태도와 어조에는 그의 체격에 어울리는 어떤 묵직함이 있어서, 그가 처음부터 상대의 접근을 물리치면 어떤 설득의 수단도 무용지물로 만들어 버리는 듯했다.\n\n\"몇 시인가?\" 부목사가 상처 입은 마음을 삼키며 말했다.\n\n\"열한 시가 넘었습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 응접실로 들어갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXIV.\n\n제1신사. 권력이 있는 곳, 그곳에 책임 또한 있게 하라.\n제2신사. 아니다, 권력이란 상대적인 것. 그대는 다가오는 역병을\n\n국경의 요새로 위협할 수 없고,\n교묘한 논변으로 잉어를 낚을 수도 없으니.\n\n모든 힘은 하나 속에 둘이다. 결과가 없으면\n원인은 원인이 아니며, 행위 자체도\n\n필연코 수동성을 품어야 한다. 그러므로 명령은\n오직 복종과 더불어서만 존재한다.\n\n리드게이트가 자신의 형편에 대해 아주 솔직하게 털어놓을 마음이 있었다 하더라도, 페어브러더 씨가 그에게 당장 필요한 도움을 줄 만한 처지가 못 된다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 상인들로부터 한 해 치 청구서가 들어오고 있었고, 도버가 그의 가구에 대한 압류를 들이밀며 위협하고 있었으며, 비위를 거스를 수 없는 환자들로부터 찔끔찔끔 들어오는 더딘 진료비 말고는 의지할 데가 없었다—프레시트 저택과 로윅 영지에서 받은 두둑한 사례비는 어느새 흔적도 없이 사라져 버렸으니—천 파운드는 족히 있어야 실질적인 곤경에서 벗어나, 그러한 상황에서 흔히 입에 올리곤 하는 희망 섞인 표현을 빌리자면 “주위를 둘러볼 여유”를 줄 만한 잔액이 남을 터였다.\n당연하게도, 즐거운 성탄절이 행복한 새해를 데려오고, 시민들이 이웃에게 웃으며 베푼 수고와 물품에 대한 대가를 받기를 기대하는 이 시기는, 리드게이트의 마음을 짓누르는 옹색한 근심의 압박을 한층 죄어 놓았으니, 그로서는 다른 어떤 주제에 대해서도, 아무리 습관적이고 마음을 잡아끄는 것일지라도 끊김 없이 생각하기란 거의 불가능했다. 그는 성품이 고약한 사람이 아니었다. 그의 지적 활동, 마음에서 우러나는 열렬한 친절, 그리고 튼튼한 체격은, 그런대로 편안한 조건 아래서라면 언제든 그를 고약한 성미를 빚어내는 사소하고 통제되지 않은 예민함 위로 올려 두었을 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 단순한 골칫거리에서가 아니라, 그 골칫거리 밑에 깔린 두 번째 의식, 곧 헛되이 낭비된 정력과 그의 모든 지난날의 목표와는 정반대인 비루한 골몰에 대한 의식에서 비롯되는, 그 가장 고약한 짜증의 먹이가 되어 있었다. “지금 내가 골몰하는 것은 바로 이것이다. 그리고 내가 골몰할 수도 있었던 것은 저것이다.”—이것이 그의 내면에서 끊임없이 흐르는 쓰라린 중얼거림이었고, 그것이 모든 곤란을 조바심을 향한 이중의 채찍으로 만들었다.\n\n어떤 신사들은 자기네 위대한 영혼이 실수로 빠져든 권태의 덫으로 우주를 여기는 전반적인 불만을 토로함으로써 문학에서 놀라운 명성을 떨치기도 했다. 그러나 어마어마한 자아와 보잘것없는 세계에 대한 감각에는 나름의 위안이 따를 수도 있다. 리드게이트의 불만은 견디기가 훨씬 더 어려웠다. 그것은 사유와 효과적인 행동 속에 펼쳐진 웅대한 존재가 자기 둘레에 놓여 있는데도, 정작 자기 자신은 이기적인 두려움과 그러한 두려움을 가라앉혀 줄 사건에 대한 천박한 불안의 비참한 고립 속으로 좁혀져 들고 있다는 감각이었다. 그의 고민거리는 어쩌면 비참할 만큼 옹색하게, 거창한 규모가 아니면 빚이라는 것을 도무지 알지 못하는 고결한 사람들의 관심에는 미치지 못하는 것으로 비칠지도 모른다. 분명 그것은 옹색한 것이었다. 그리고 고결하지 못한 대다수의 사람에게, 옹색함에서 벗어나는 길이란 오직 돈에 대한 갈망에서, 그 온갖 저열한 희망과 유혹에서, 누군가의 죽음을 기다리는 마음에서, 넌지시 던지는 부탁에서, 나쁜 물건을 좋은 것으로 둔갑시키려는 말 장수의 욕심에서, 마땅히 남의 몫이어야 할 자리를 노리는 마음에서, 그리고 흔히 닥쳐오는 폭넓은 재난의 모습을 한 행운을 갈망하지 않을 수 없게 만드는 강박에서 자유로워지는 것뿐이다.\n리드게이트가 쓰라리고 침울한 상태에 빠져 들어 로저먼드와의 소원함을 끊임없이 넓혀 가게 된 것은, 바로 이 비열한 멍에 아래로 목을 들이미는 일이라는 생각에 그가 몸부림쳤기 때문이었다. 매각 증서에 관한 첫 번째 고백이 있은 뒤로, 그는 지출을 줄일 만한 가능한 방책에 관해 아내가 자기에게 공감을 갖도록 끌어들이려 여러 차례 애를 썼고, 성탄절이 위협적으로 다가오면서 그의 제안은 점점 더 구체적으로 변해 갔다. “우리 둘은 하인 하나만으로도 꾸려 갈 수 있고, 아주 적은 돈으로 살 수 있어.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 나도 말 한 마리로 어떻게든 해 볼게.” 우리가 보아 온 대로, 리드게이트는 생활비에 관해 한층 또렷한 안목을 갖고 따져 보기 시작한 터였고, 그가 그런 외양에 들이던 자존심 따위는, 빚쟁이로 드러나는 일이나 사람들에게 돈을 빌려 달라고 청하는 일을 못 견뎌 하게 만드는 그 자존심에 비하면 보잘것없는 것이었다.\n\n“물론 당신이 원하면 다른 하인 둘은 내보낼 수 있어요.” 로저먼드가 말했다. “하지만 우리가 궁색하게 사는 건 당신 입지에 아주 해로울 거라고 생각했는데요. 진료가 줄어들 걸 각오하셔야 할 거예요.”\n\n“로저먼드, 여보, 이건 선택의 문제가 아니야. 우리는 너무 사치스럽게 시작했어. 피콕도, 알다시피 이 집보다 훨씬 작은 집에서 살았잖아. 내 잘못이야. 더 분별 있게 했어야 했고, 누군가 나를 매질할 권리가 있는 사람이 있다면 매를 맞아 마땅해—당신을 여태까지 익숙했던 것보다 더 궁색하게 살아야 하는 처지로 끌어들였으니. 하지만 우리는 서로 사랑해서 결혼했잖아, 아마도. 그게 형편이 나아질 때까지 우리가 버텨 나가는 데 힘이 되어 줄 거야. 자, 여보, 그 일감은 내려놓고 이리 와.”\n\n그 순간 그는 정말로 아내에 대해 싸늘하고 음울한 심정이었지만, 애정 없는 미래가 두려웠고, 둘 사이에 다가오는 분열에 맞서기로 마음먹고 있었다. 로저먼드는 그의 말을 따랐고, 그는 아내를 무릎에 앉혔으나, 마음속 깊은 곳에서 그녀는 그에게서 완전히 멀어져 있었다. 가엾은 그녀는 다만 세상이 자기 뜻대로 돌아가지 않는다는 것만을 보았고, 리드게이트는 그 세상의 일부였다. 그러나 그는 한 손으로 아내의 허리를 감고 다른 손은 그녀의 두 손 위에 살며시 얹었다. 다소 퉁명스러운 이 남자는 여인을 대하는 태도에 상당한 다정함을 지니고 있었으니, 여인들의 연약한 몸이며 몸과 마음 양쪽으로 위태롭게 균형 잡힌 건강을 늘 머릿속에 떠올리고 있는 듯했다. 그리고 그는 다시 설득하듯 말하기 시작했다.\n\n“이제 좀 들여다보니 알겠는데, 로지, 우리 살림에서 얼마나 많은 돈이 새어 나가는지 놀라울 정도야. 하인들이 부주의한 모양이고, 손님도 무척 많이 드나들었지. 하지만 우리와 같은 처지에서도 훨씬 적은 돈으로 꾸려 가는 사람이 분명 많을 거야. 아마 더 흔한 물건으로 살고, 남는 것을 잘 챙기겠지. 이런 일에서 돈이 얼마 가지 못하는 모양이야. 렌치만 해도 모든 걸 더할 나위 없이 수수하게 하면서도 진료는 아주 많이 하잖아.”\n\n“어머, 렌치 댁처럼 사는 걸 생각하신다면!” 로저먼드가 목을 살짝 비틀며 말했다. “하지만 당신이 그런 식의 생활에 진저리를 친다고 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있는데요.”\n\n“그래, 그 집은 모든 게 취향이 형편없지—절약을 흉하게 보이게 만든단 말이야. 우리가 그럴 필요는 없어. 나는 다만 렌치는 진료가 아주 잘되는데도 지출을 아낀다는 걸 말하려던 거야.”\n\n“당신은 왜 진료가 잘되면 안 되죠, 터셔스? 피콕 씨는 그랬잖아요. 당신은 사람들 비위를 거스르지 않도록 더 조심해야 하고, 다른 사람들처럼 약을 지어 보내 줘야 해요. 당신은 분명 출발이 좋았고, 괜찮은 집안도 여럿 잡았잖아요. 별나게 굴어서는 잘될 리가 없어요. 사람들이 대체로 좋아할 만한 게 무엇인지 생각해 봐야죠.” 로저먼드가 야무지고 단호하게 타이르는 어조로 말했다.\n\n리드게이트는 화가 치밀어 올랐다. 그는 여성적 연약함에는 너그럽게 대할 마음의 준비가 되어 있었지만, 여성적 지시질에는 그렇지 않았다. 물의 요정 같은 영혼의 얄팍함도 그녀가 훈계를 늘어놓기 전까지는 매력으로 남을 수 있는 법이다. 그러나 그는 스스로를 억누르며, 다만 다소 전제적인 단호함을 띤 어조로 말했다.\n\n“내 진료에서 내가 어떻게 해야 할지는, 로지, 내가 판단할 일이야. 그건 우리 사이의 문제가 아니야. 당신은 우리 수입이 앞으로 오랫동안 아주 빠듯할 것 같다는 것만 알면 돼—기껏해야 사백 파운드, 어쩌면 그보다 적을 거야. 그리고 우리는 그 사실에 맞춰 살림을 다시 꾸리도록 애써야 해.”\n\n로저먼드는 한두 순간 말없이 앞을 바라보다가 이윽고 말했다. “벌스트로드 외삼촌이 당신이 병원에 들이는 시간에 대해 봉급을 드려야 마땅해요. 당신이 거저 일한다는 건 옳지 않아요.”\n\n“처음부터 내 봉사는 무보수라고 양해된 일이야. 그것도 우리 논의에 끌어들일 필요가 없어. 나는 유일하게 있을 법한 가능성을 짚어 주었을 뿐이야.” 리드게이트가 짜증스럽게 말했다. 그러다 스스로를 다잡고 한결 차분하게 말을 이었다.\n\n“지금의 어려움을 상당 부분 덜어 줄 방도가 하나 보이는 것 같아. 듣자니 젊은 네드 플림데일이 소피 톨러 양과 결혼한다더군. 그 집은 부유하고, 미들마치에서 좋은 집이 비는 일은 흔치 않아. 그들이 우리 집을 가구 대부분과 함께 기꺼이 넘겨받으려 할 거라고 확신해. 임차료도 두둑이 치를 의향이 있을 거야. 내가 트럼불을 시켜 플림데일에게 이야기를 꺼내게 할 수 있어.”\n\n로저먼드는 남편의 무릎에서 일어나 방의 저쪽 끝으로 천천히 걸어갔다. 그러다 몸을 돌려 그를 향해 걸어올 때, 눈물이 차오른 것이 또렷이 보였고, 그녀가 아랫입술을 깨물며 울음을 참으려고 두 손을 맞잡고 있는 것이 보였다. 리드게이트는 비참했다—분노로 떨리면서도, 지금 그 분노를 터뜨리는 것은 사내답지 못한 일이라고 느끼고 있었다.\n\n“정말 미안해, 로저먼드. 이게 괴로운 일이라는 거 알아.”\n\n“적어도 은식기를 돌려보내고, 그 사람이 가구 목록을 작성하게 하는 걸 참고 견뎠으면—그것으로 충분할 줄 알았어요.”\n\n“그건 그때 당신한테 설명했잖아, 여보. 그건 그저 담보였을 뿐이고, 그 담보 뒤에는 빚이 있어. 그리고 그 빚은 앞으로 몇 달 안에 갚아야 해. 그러지 않으면 우리 가구가 팔려 나갈 거야. 젊은 플림데일이 우리 집과 가구 대부분을 넘겨받는다면, 우리는 그 빚을, 그리고 다른 빚도 얼마간 갚을 수 있고, 우리한테 너무 비싼 이 집에서도 벗어날 수 있어. 우리는 더 작은 집을 얻을 수 있어. 내가 알기로 트럼불한테 일 년에 삼십 파운드면 빌릴 수 있는 꽤 괜찮은 집이 있어. 그런데 이 집은 구십 파운드야.” 리드게이트는, 막연한 마음을 거역할 수 없는 사실에 못 박아 두려 할 때 우리가 흔히 쓰는 그 짧고 두드리는 듯한 어조로 이 말을 내뱉었다. 눈물이 로저먼드의 뺨을 따라 소리 없이 흘러내렸다. 그녀는 손수건을 눈물에 가만히 갖다 대고는, 벽난로 선반 위의 커다란 꽃병을 바라보며 서 있었다. 그것은 그녀가 이제껏 느껴 본 적 없는, 더욱 사무치게 쓰라린 순간이었다. 마침내 그녀는 서두르지 않고 조심스레 힘주어 말했다.\n\n“당신이 그런 식으로 행동하고 싶어 할 줄은 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어요.”\n\n“하고 싶다고?” 리드게이트가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고 벽난로에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나가며 버럭 소리쳤다. “이건 하고 싶고 말고의 문제가 아니야. 물론 나도 하고 싶지 않아. 그저 내가 할 수 있는 유일한 일일 뿐이야.” 그는 거기서 휙 돌아서서 그녀를 향했다.\n\n“그것 말고도 다른 방도가 많이 있을 줄 알았어요.” 로저먼드가 말했다. “집안 물건을 다 팔아 버리고 아예 미들마치를 떠나요.”\n\n“떠나서 뭘 하라고? 미들마치에서 하던 일을 버리고 일거리도 없는 곳으로 가는 게 무슨 소용이야? 다른 데 가도 여기 못지않게 한 푼도 없을 거야.” 리드게이트가 한층 더 노여워하며 말했다.\n\n“우리가 그런 처지에 놓인다면 그건 전적으로 당신이 자초한 일이에요, 터셔스.” 로저먼드가 돌아서서, 더없이 확신에 찬 어조로 말했다. “당신은 자기 가족에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 하지 않으려고 해요. 당신은 리드게이트 대위의 비위를 거슬렀어요. 우리가 콸링엄에 있을 때 고드윈 경께서 저한테 무척 친절하셨는데, 당신이 그분께 합당한 예를 갖추고 형편을 말씀드린다면, 분명 당신을 위해 무엇이든 해 주실 거예요. 그런데 당신은 그러기보다는 우리 집과 가구를 네드 플림데일 씨한테 넘겨주는 쪽이 좋다는 거잖아요.”\n\n리드게이트의 눈에는 사나운 기색 같은 것이 어렸고, 그는 새삼스러운 격렬함으로 대꾸했다. “좋아, 그렇다면, 정 그렇게 받아들이고 싶다면, 나도 그게 좋아. 인정하지, 아무 소용도 없는 데 구걸하러 가서 바보 꼴이 되는 것보다는 그게 더 좋아. 그러니 알아 둬, 그게 바로 내가 하고 싶은 일이야.”\n\n마지막 문장에는 그의 억센 손이 로저먼드의 가녀린 팔을 움켜쥔 것과 맞먹는 어조가 실려 있었다. 그러나 그럼에도 불구하고 그의 의지는 그녀의 의지보다 조금도 더 강하지 않았다. 그녀는 곧장 말없이 방을 나갔지만, 리드게이트가 하고 싶어 하는 일을 막겠다는 강렬한 결심을 품고 있었다.\n\n그는 집을 나섰으나, 피가 식어 가면서 그 논쟁의 가장 큰 결과란 앞으로 자기를 다시 격한 말로 몰아갈지 모를 주제를 아내에게 꺼내는 것에 대한, 마음속에 가라앉은 두려움임을 깨달았다. 그것은 마치 섬세한 수정에 금이 가기 시작한 것 같았고, 그는 그 금을 돌이킬 수 없게 만들지도 모를 어떤 움직임도 두려웠다. 두 사람이 서로 사랑을 이어 갈 수 없다면, 그의 결혼은 한낱 쓰디쓴 아이러니의 조각에 지나지 않을 터였다. 그는 오래전에 그녀의 소극적인 성격이라고 여긴 것에 대해—자기의 구체적인 바람도 전반적인 목표도 아랑곳하지 않는 데서 드러나는 그녀의 감수성 결여에 대해—이미 마음을 정해 둔 터였다. 첫 번째 큰 실망은 이미 견뎌 낸 셈이었다. 이상적인 아내가 보여 줄 다정한 헌신과 고분고분한 흠모는 단념해야 했고, 삶은, 팔다리를 잃은 사람들이 그러하듯, 더 낮은 단계의 기대 위에서 다시 떠맡아야 했다. 그러나 현실의 아내에게는 마땅한 권리만 있는 것이 아니었다. 그녀는 여전히 그의 마음을 사로잡고 있었고, 그 사로잡음이 계속 강하게 남아 있기를 그는 간절히 바랐다. 결혼에서, “그녀는 결코 나를 깊이 사랑하지 않으리라”는 확신은 “나는 더 이상 그녀를 사랑하지 않으리라”는 두려움보다 견디기가 더 쉽다. 그리하여 그렇게 폭발한 뒤, 그의 내면의 노력은 오로지 그녀를 변호하고, 일부는 자기 탓인 그 고된 상황을 탓하는 데로 향했다. 그날 저녁 그는 아내를 어르며 아침에 자기가 낸 상처를 아물게 하려 애썼고, 쌀쌀맞게 굴거나 토라지는 것은 로저먼드의 천성에 없는 일이었다. 사실 그녀는 남편이 자기를 사랑하며 자제하고 있다는 표시를 반겼다. 그러나 이것은 그를 사랑하는 것과는 전혀 별개의 일이었다. 리드게이트는 집을 내놓는 계획을 곧 다시 입에 올릴 마음은 없었다. 그는 그 일을 끝까지 밀고 나가되, 그것에 관해서는 가능한 한 더는 말을 아끼기로 작정했다. 그런데 정작 로저먼드가 아침 식사 자리에서 부드럽게 그 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n“트럼불한테 벌써 이야기했어요?”\n\n“아니.” 리드게이트가 말했다. “하지만 오늘 아침에 지나는 길에 들를 거야. 한시도 지체해선 안 돼.” 그는 로저먼드의 물음을 그녀가 마음속 반대를 거두어들이는 표시로 받아들이고는, 떠나려고 일어서면서 그녀의 머리에 다정하게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n방문하기에 충분히 늦은 시각이 되자마자, 로저먼드는 네드 씨의 어머니인 플림데일 부인을 찾아가, 다가오는 결혼에 관한 화제로 들어가 곱게 축하의 말을 건넸다. 어머니다운 플림데일 부인의 생각은, 로저먼드가 이제 어쩌면 자기 자신의 어리석음을 되돌아볼지도 모른다는 것이었다. 그리고 지금으로서는 모든 유리함이 자기 아들 쪽에 있다고 느끼면서, 그녀는 우아하게 처신하지 않을 만큼 마음씨가 박한 여인은 아니었다.\n\n“그럼요, 네드는 더없이 행복하답니다, 정말이지요. 그리고 소피 톨러는 며느릿감으로 제가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추었지요. 물론 그 애 아버지가 그 애를 위해 후하게 뭔가 해 줄 형편이고요—저런 양조장을 가진 집안이라면 그쯤은 으레 기대할 만한 일이죠. 그리고 그 혼맥은 우리가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이고요. 하지만 내가 보는 건 그게 아니에요. 그 애는 정말 참한 아가씨예요—거드름도, 허세도 없으면서도 으뜸가는 사람들과 어깨를 나란히 한다니까요. 작위를 가진 귀족 말고요. 나는 제 분수를 넘어서 위로 겨냥하는 사람들에게서 좋은 점이라곤 거의 보지 못했어요. 내 말은, 소피가 이 고장의 가장 훌륭한 사람들과 견주어 손색이 없으면서도, 그것으로 만족할 줄 안다는 거죠.”\n\n“저는 늘 그분이 무척 상냥하다고 생각했어요.” 로저먼드가 말했다.\n\n“나는 이걸 네드에게 주어진 보답이라고 봐요. 그 애는 도무지 콧대를 너무 높이 든 적이 없었으니, 가장 훌륭한 혼맥을 맺게 된 거죠.” 플림데일 부인이 말을 이었다. 자기가 올바른 견해를 취하고 있다는 뜨거운 자부심에 그녀의 타고난 날카로움이 누그러져 있었다. “게다가 톨러네처럼 까다로운 사람들이라면, 우리 친구들 중 몇몇이 자기네 친구가 아니라는 이유로 트집을 잡을 수도 있었어요. 댁의 벌스트로드 외숙모와 내가 젊을 적부터 가깝게 지내 온 건 익히 알려진 일이고, 플림데일 씨는 늘 벌스트로드 씨 편을 들어 오셨지요. 그리고 나 자신도 경건한 신앙을 더 좋아하고요. 그런데도 톨러네는 변함없이 네드를 반갑게 맞아 주었답니다.”\n\n“그분은 분명 무척 마땅한 자격을 갖춘, 행실 반듯한 청년이세요.” 로저먼드가, 플림데일 부인의 유익한 훈계에 대한 답례로 점잖게 생색내는 태도를 띠며 말했다.\n\n“오, 그 애는 육군 대위 같은 풍채라든가, 마치 세상 모두가 자기 아래인 양 거드름 피우는 태도라든가, 그런 번지르르한 말솜씨며 노래며 지적인 재주 같은 건 없어요. 하지만 나는 그 애가 그런 게 없는 게 고맙답니다. 그런 건 이승을 위해서도 저승을 위해서도 변변찮은 준비니까요.”\n\n“어머, 정말 그래요. 겉모습은 행복과 거의 관계가 없죠.” 로저먼드가 말했다. “두 분이 행복한 부부가 될 가망이 충분하다고 봐요. 두 분은 어떤 집을 얻으실까요?”\n\n“오, 그 일이라면 구할 수 있는 걸로 만족해야겠죠. 그 애들은 해크벗 씨 댁 옆, 성 베드로 광장에 있는 집을 보고 있어요. 그건 해크벗 씨 소유인데, 그가 말끔하게 손보고 있답니다. 더 나은 집 소식을 듣기는 어려울 것 같아요. 사실 네드가 오늘 그 문제를 결정할 것 같네요.”\n\n“좋은 집일 것 같아요. 저는 성 베드로 광장이 마음에 들어요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 교회와 가깝고 점잖은 자리이긴 하죠. 하지만 창문이 좁고, 온통 오르락내리락이에요. 혹시 비어 있을 만한 다른 집을 알고 있진 않나요?” 플림데일 부인이 동그란 검은 눈을 로저먼드에게 고정한 채, 그 눈에 불현듯 떠오른 생각의 생기를 담아 물었다.\n\n“아뇨, 저는 그런 일은 거의 듣지 못해서요.”\n\n로저먼드는 방문에 나설 때만 해도 이런 문답을 미리 내다보지는 못했다. 그저 자기에게 더없이 마뜩잖은 상황에서 자기 집을 넘기는 일을 막아 줄 정보라면 무엇이든 얻으려 했을 뿐이었다. 자기 대답 속의 거짓말에 대해서는, 겉모습이 행복과 거의 관계가 없다고 말한 데 들어 있는 거짓말에 대해서와 마찬가지로 조금도 곱씹지 않았다. 자기 목적은 더할 나위 없이 정당하다고 그녀는 확신했다. 용서받을 수 없는 의도를 품은 쪽은 리드게이트였다. 그리고 그녀의 마음속에는 한 가지 계획이 있었으니, 그것을 온전히 실행에 옮기고 나면, 그가 자기 입지에서 끌어내리는 것이 얼마나 그릇된 한 걸음이었을지를 증명해 줄 터였다.\n\n그녀는 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 사무소를 거쳐 집으로 돌아왔으니, 그곳에 들를 작정이었다. 로저먼드가 사무적인 형식을 띤 무언가를 해 보겠다고 생각한 것은 평생 처음이었지만, 그녀는 그 일을 능히 감당할 수 있다고 느꼈다. 자기가 몹시 싫어하는 일을 부득이 하지 않으면 안 되게 되었다는 생각은 그녀의 조용한 고집을 능동적인 궁리로 바꾸어 놓았다. 이번 경우는 단순히 거역하고 태연자약하게 천연덕스레 고집을 부리는 것만으로는 충분할 수 없는 일이었다. 그녀는 자기 판단에 따라 행동해야 했고, 자기 판단이 옳다고 스스로에게 일렀다—“하긴, 옳지 않았다면 그것에 따라 행동하고 싶지도 않았을 테니.”\n\n트럼불 씨는 사무소 안쪽 방에 있었고, 더없이 세련된 태도로 로저먼드를 맞이했다. 그녀의 매력에 무척 민감했기 때문만이 아니라, 리드게이트가 곤경에 처해 있고, 이 보기 드물게 예쁜 여인—더없이 빼어난 매력을 지닌 이 젊은 부인—이 곤궁의 쓴맛을 보게 되고, 자기 힘으로는 어쩔 수 없는 상황에 휘말리게 될 것 같다는 확신에 그의 내면에 깃든 마음씨 좋은 결이 움직였기 때문이기도 했다. 그는 부디 자리에 앉는 영광을 베풀어 달라고 청한 뒤, 그녀 앞에 서서 매무새를 다듬고 몸가짐을 가다듬으며, 주로 호의에서 우러나는 열렬한 정성을 보였다. 로저먼드의 첫 질문은, 자기 남편이 그날 아침 집을 처분하는 일에 관해 이야기하러 트럼불 씨를 찾아왔느냐는 것이었다.\n\n“예, 부인, 예, 그러셨습니다. 그러셨고말고요.” 사람 좋은 경매인이 자기 되풀이하는 말에 무언가 달래는 기색을 담으려 애쓰며 말했다. “저는 가능하면 오늘 오후에 그분의 지시를 이행하려던 참이었습니다. 그분께서 미루지 말라고 하셨으니까요.”\n\n“더 진행하지 마시라고 말씀드리러 들렀어요, 트럼불 씨. 그리고 이 일에 관해 오간 이야기는 부디 언급하지 말아 주세요. 제 부탁을 들어주시겠어요?”\n\n“물론이고말고요, 리드게이트 부인, 물론입니다. 사업에 관해서든 다른 어떤 화제에 관해서든, 신뢰는 저에게 신성한 것입니다. 그러면 그 위임은 철회된 것으로 여기면 되겠습니까?” 트럼불 씨가 두 손으로 푸른 크라바트의 긴 끝자락을 매만지며, 공손하게 로저먼드를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“네, 그래 주시면 좋겠어요. 알고 보니 네드 플림데일 씨가 집을 얻었더군요—해크벗 씨 댁 옆, 성 베드로 광장에 있는 집이요. 리드게이트 씨도 자기 지시가 쓸데없이 이행되면 언짢아할 거예요. 게다가 그 제안을 불필요하게 만드는 다른 사정도 있고요.”\n\n“아주 좋습니다, 리드게이트 부인, 아주 좋아요. 무슨 일이든 저의 도움이 필요하실 때면 언제든 분부만 내려 주십시오.” 트럼불 씨가 무언가 새로운 재원이 열린 모양이라고 짐작하며 즐거움을 느끼고 말했다. “저를 믿어 주십시오, 부디. 이 일은 더 이상 진행되지 않을 것입니다.”\n\n그날 저녁 리드게이트는 로저먼드가 요즘 들어 평소보다 더 활기차고, 시키지 않아도 자기를 기쁘게 할 일을 하는 데 관심까지 보이는 것을 알아채고 조금 위안을 얻었다. 그는 생각했다. “그녀가 행복하고 내가 그럭저럭 헤쳐 나갈 수만 있다면, 그게 다 무슨 대수겠는가? 긴 여정에서 우리가 건너야 할 좁은 늪에 지나지 않아. 머릿속을 다시 맑게 가다듬을 수만 있다면 잘 풀릴 거야.”\n\n그는 어찌나 기운이 났던지, 오래전부터 찾아보려다가 사소한 근심들에 뒤따르는 슬금슬금 기어드는 자기 절망 때문에 미뤄 두었던 어떤 실험 기록을 다시 뒤지기 시작했다. 그는 멀리까지 뻗어 나가는 탐구에 대한 옛날의 그 황홀한 몰입을 얼마간 다시 느꼈고, 그동안 로저먼드는 저녁 호숫가의 노 젓는 물소리만큼이나 그의 사색에 도움이 되는 잔잔한 음악을 연주했다. 꽤 늦은 시각이었다. 그는 책을 죄다 옆으로 밀쳐 놓고, 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 낀 채 불을 바라보며, 새로운 대조 실험을 구상하는 일 말고는 모든 것을 잊고 있었는데, 그때 피아노를 떠나 의자에 기대어 그를 지켜보고 있던 로저먼드가 말했다.\n\n“네드 플림데일 씨가 벌써 집을 얻었어요.”\n\n리드게이트는 흠칫 놀라고 신경이 거슬려, 마치 잠을 방해받은 사람처럼 잠시 말없이 고개를 들었다. 그러다 언짢은 의식에 얼굴을 붉히며 물었다.\n\n“그걸 어떻게 알아?”\n\n“오늘 아침 플림데일 부인을 찾아갔는데, 그분이 그가 해크벗 씨 댁 옆, 성 베드로 광장에 있는 집을 얻었다고 하시더군요.”\n\n리드게이트는 잠자코 있었다. 그는 머리 뒤에서 두 손을 끌어내려, 늘 그러기 십상이듯 이마 위로 한 무더기 늘어진 머리카락을 짓누르고, 두 팔꿈치를 무릎에 괴었다. 그는 마치 숨 막히는 곳에서 빠져나갈 문을 열었다가 그 문이 벽으로 막혀 있는 것을 발견한 사람처럼 쓰라린 실망을 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 동시에 로저먼드가 자기 실망의 원인을 기뻐하고 있다는 것도 분명히 느꼈다. 그는 처음의 분한 경련이 가라앉을 때까지는 그녀를 보지도 말하지도 않는 편이 낫다고 여겼다. 따지고 보면, 그가 쓰라림 속에서 중얼거리길, 여자가 집과 가구만큼 마음 쓰는 게 뭐가 있겠는가? 그것들 없는 남편이란 한낱 어이없는 존재일 뿐인 것을. 그가 고개를 들고 머리카락을 옆으로 밀어 넘겼을 때, 그의 검은 눈에는 공감을 도무지 기대하지 않는 비참한 공백이 어려 있었으나, 그는 다만 차갑게 말했다.\n\n“어쩌면 다른 누군가가 나타날지도 모르지. 플림데일과 잘 안 되면 계속 알아보라고 트럼불한테 일러 두었으니까.”\n\n로저먼드는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 어떤 결말이 자기의 개입을 정당화해 줄 때까지는 남편과 경매인 사이에 더 이상 아무 일도 오가지 않으리라는 가능성에 기대를 걸었다. 어쨌거나 그녀는 당장 두려워하던 사태를 막아 낸 셈이었다. 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“그 못마땅한 사람들이 원하는 돈이 얼마나 되는데요?”\n\n“못마땅한 사람들이라니?”\n\n“목록을 작성한 그 사람들—그리고 다른 사람들이요. 그러니까, 당신이 더 이상 시달리지 않으려면 그들을 얼마면 만족시킬 수 있느냐는 거예요.”\n\n리드게이트는 마치 증세를 살피려는 듯 잠시 그녀를 찬찬히 보다가 말했다. “오, 가구 값과 권리금으로 플림데일한테서 육백 파운드를 받을 수 있었다면 어떻게든 꾸려 갔을 거야. 도버한테 빚을 청산하고, 나머지 사람들한테도 인내심을 갖고 기다릴 만큼 얼마간 갚아 두었겠지. 우리가 지출을 줄였다면 말이야.”\n\n“하지만 우리가 이 집에 그대로 산다면 얼마나 필요하느냐는 거예요.”\n\n“내가 어디서든 마련할 수 있을 것보다 더 많이.” 리드게이트가 다소 거슬리는 빈정거림을 어조에 담아 말했다. 로저먼드의 마음이 가능한 노력을 마주하는 대신 실현 불가능한 바람 위를 떠돌고 있다는 것을 알아챈 것이 그를 화나게 했다.\n\n“금액을 말하면 안 되나요?” 로저먼드가, 그의 태도가 마음에 들지 않는다는 것을 은근히 내비치며 말했다.\n\n“글쎄,” 리드게이트가 어림짐작하는 어조로 말했다. “마음을 놓으려면 적어도 천 파운드는 있어야 해. 하지만,” 그가 날카롭게 덧붙였다. “나는 그게 있을 때가 아니라 없을 때 어떻게 할지를 궁리해야 하는 처지야.”\n\n로저먼드는 더 이상 말하지 않았다.\n\n그러나 이튿날 그녀는 고드윈 리드게이트 경에게 편지를 쓰겠다는 계획을 실행에 옮겼다. 대위가 다녀간 뒤로, 그녀는 그에게서 편지를 받았고, 또 그의 출가한 누이인 멩건 부인에게서도 한 통을 받았는데, 아기를 잃은 일을 위로하면서, 콸링엄에서 다시 그녀를 보게 되기를 바란다는 막연한 뜻을 담은 편지였다. 리드게이트는 그녀에게 그런 예의는 별 뜻이 없다고 말했지만, 그녀는 리드게이트의 가족이 그에게 보이는 미온적인 태도가 모두 그의 차갑고 멸시하는 듯한 행실 탓이라고 속으로 굳게 믿고 있었고, 그래서 그 편지들에 더없이 매력적인 태도로 답장을 보내면서, 구체적인 초대가 뒤따르리라는 어느 정도의 확신을 품고 있었다. 그러나 완전한 침묵이 이어졌다. 대위는 분명 펜을 잘 놀리는 사람이 아니었고, 로저먼드는 누이들이 외국에 나가 있었을지도 모른다고 생각했다. 어쨌거나 고향의 벗들을 생각하는 계절이 돌아왔고, 적어도 그녀의 턱을 어루만지며 그녀가 1790년에 자기를 사로잡았던 그 이름난 미인 크롤리 부인을 닮았다고 단언했던 고드윈 경이라면, 그녀의 어떤 호소에도 마음이 움직일 테고, 그녀를 위해서라도 조카에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 다하는 것을 흐뭇하게 여길 터였다. 로저먼드는 자기가 곤란을 겪지 않도록 노신사가 마땅히 해야 할 일이 무엇인지를 천진하게 확신하고 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 자기 딴에 가장 분별 있다고 여긴 편지를—고드윈 경에게 자기의 뛰어난 분별력을 증명해 줄 만한 편지를—썼으니, 터셔스가 미들마치 같은 곳을 떠나 자기 재능에 더 어울리는 곳으로 옮겨 가는 것이 얼마나 바람직한지, 그곳 주민들의 불쾌한 성정이 어떻게 그의 직업적 성공을 가로막았는지, 그리고 그 결과 그가 어떻게 금전적 곤경에 처했으며, 그를 완전히 거기서 빼내려면 천 파운드가 필요하다는 것을 짚어 보였다. 그녀는 자기가 편지를 쓰려 한다는 것을 터셔스가 모르고 있다는 말은 하지 않았다. 자기 편지를 그가 묵인했다고 가정하는 것이, 그가 늘 자기의 가장 좋은 벗이었던 친척인 고드윈 외삼촌을 크게 존경한다고 자기가 한 말과 부합하리라고 여겼기 때문이다. 가엾은 로저먼드의 술책이 이제 실무에 적용될 때 발휘되는 위력이 그러했다.\n\n이 일은 새해 첫날의 만찬 모임 이전에 있었던 것이며, 고드윈 경에게서는 아직 아무 답장도 오지 않았다. 그런데 그날 아침 리드게이트는 로저먼드가 보스럽 트럼불에게 내린 자기 지시를 취소했다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그녀가 로윅 게이트의 집을 떠나야 한다는 생각에 차츰 익숙해질 필요가 있다고 느낀 그는, 그 일에 관해 다시 그녀에게 말하기를 꺼리는 마음을 누르고, 아침 식사를 하면서 말했다.\n\n“오늘 아침에 트럼불을 만나서, 집을 ‘파이어니어’와 ‘트럼펫’에 광고로 내달라고 일러 둘 참이야. 광고를 내면, 그러지 않았더라면 이사를 생각도 안 했을 누군가가 마음이 끌릴지도 몰라. 이런 시골에서는 식구가 너무 늘어 집이 좁아져도, 다른 집을 어디서 구할 수 있는지 몰라서 옛집에 그대로 눌러 사는 사람이 많거든. 그런데 트럼불은 도무지 입질 하나 받지 못한 모양이야.”\n\n로저먼드는 피할 수 없는 순간이 왔음을 알았다. “트럼불한테 더는 알아보지 말라고 일러 두었어요.” 그녀가, 누가 봐도 방어적인, 조심스러운 침착함으로 말했다.\n\n리드게이트는 말문이 막힌 채 그녀를 멍하니 쳐다보았다. 불과 반 시간 전만 해도 그는 그녀의 땋은 머리를 매만져 주며 애정의 “어린아이 말투”를 건네던 참이었고, 로저먼드는 그것을 되돌려 주지는 않으면서도, 마치 자기가 고요하고 사랑스러운 우상이라도 되는 양, 이따금 자기를 떠받드는 신도를 향해 신비롭게 보조개를 지어 보이며 받아들이곤 했다. 그러한 애정의 결이 그의 안에서 아직 가시지 않은 채 떨고 있던 터라, 그가 받은 충격은 곧바로 또렷한 분노일 수는 없었다. 그것은 어수선한 아픔이었다. 그는 고기를 썰던 칼과 포크를 내려놓고, 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히며, 마침내 차가운 빈정거림을 어조에 담아 말했다.\n\n“언제, 왜 그랬는지 물어봐도 될까?”\n\n“플림데일네가 집을 얻은 걸 알았을 때, 그 집에 대해 그들에게 우리 집 이야기를 꺼내지 말라고 일러 두러 갔어요. 그리고 그 자리에서, 그 일을 더는 진행하지 말라고 했고요. 당신이 집과 가구를 처분하고 싶어 한다는 게 알려지면 당신한테 무척 해로울 거라는 걸 알았고, 저는 그게 몹시 못마땅했거든요. 그것으로 충분한 이유가 된다고 생각해요.”\n\n“그럼 내가 당신한테 그것과는 다른 종류의 절박한 이유를 말한 건 아무 상관도 없었다는 거군. 내가 다른 결론에 이르렀고, 그에 따라 지시를 내렸다는 것도 아무 상관 없었다는 거야?” 리드게이트가 매섭게 말했고, 천둥과 번개가 그의 이마와 눈가에 모여들고 있었다.\n\n누군가의 분노가 로저먼드에게 미치는 영향은 늘, 그녀를 싸늘한 혐오 속에 움츠러들게 하고, 남들이야 어떻게 굴든 자기는 그릇되게 행동할 사람이 아니라는 확신 속에서 한층 더 차분하게 흠잡을 데 없는 태도를 갖게 만드는 것이었다. 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n“적어도 당신만큼이나 저와 관계되는 일에 대해 말할 권리가 저한테 충분히 있다고 생각해요.”\n\n“분명하지—당신은 말할 권리가 있었어, 하지만 오직 나한테만이지. 당신한테는 내 지시를 몰래 거스를 권리도, 나를 바보 취급할 권리도 없었어.” 리드게이트가 앞서와 똑같은 어조로 말했다. 그러더니 얼마간 경멸을 더해 말했다. “그 결과가 어떻게 될지 당신한테 이해시키는 게 가능하기는 할까? 우리가 왜 이 집을 처분하려고 애써야 하는지를 다시 말해 주는 게 무슨 소용이 있기는 할까?”\n\n“다시 말해 줄 필요는 없어요.” 로저먼드가, 차가운 물방울처럼 낮게 떨어져 흘러내리는 목소리로 말했다. “당신이 한 말을 기억하고 있어요. 지금처럼 그때도 똑같이 격하게 말했죠. 하지만 그렇다고, 저한테 그토록 괴로운 그 한 걸음을 내딛기 전에 다른 온갖 방도를 다 시도해 봐야 한다는 제 생각이 바뀌지는 않아요. 그리고 집을 광고로 내는 것에 대해 말하자면, 그건 당신한테 더없이 수치스러운 일이라고 생각해요.”\n\n“그럼 당신이 내 생각을 무시하듯 내가 당신 생각을 무시한다면?”\n\n“물론 그러실 수 있죠. 하지만 자기 고집을 꺾느니 차라리 저를 최악의 처지에 빠뜨리겠다는 거라면, 결혼하기 전에 미리 말해 줬어야 한다고 생각해요.”\n\n리드게이트는 말이 없었다. 다만 고개를 한쪽으로 홱 젖히고, 절망에 입가를 씰룩였다. 로저먼드는 그가 자기를 보고 있지 않은 것을 알고 일어나 그의 앞에 커피잔을 놓아 주었다. 그러나 그는 그것에 아랑곳하지 않고, 이따금 의자에서 몸을 움직이며, 한 팔을 탁자에 괴고, 손으로 머리카락을 비비면서 내면의 극과 논쟁을 이어 갔다. 그의 안에는 갖가지 감정과 생각이 한데 모여들어, 그가 분노에 온전히 자신을 내맡기지도, 단순한 결심의 완고함을 끝까지 밀고 나가지도 못하게 했다. 로저먼드는 그의 침묵을 틈탔다.\n\n“우리가 결혼했을 때 모두가 당신 입지가 아주 높다고 여겼어요. 그때는 당신이 우리 가구를 팔고, 방들이 새장 같은 브라이드 가의 집을 얻고 싶어 하리라곤 상상도 못 했어요. 우리가 그렇게 살아야 한다면, 적어도 미들마치는 떠나요.”\n\n“그건 아주 강력한 고려 사항이겠지.” 리드게이트가 반쯤 빈정대며 말했다—그래도 커피를 바라보며 입에 대지 않는 그의 입술 언저리에는 핏기 가신 시든 빛이 감돌았다—“내가 마침 빚을 지고 있지만 않다면, 그건 아주 강력한 고려 사항이겠지.”\n\n“같은 식으로 빚을 진 사람이 분명 많았을 텐데, 그래도 그들이 점잖은 사람들이면 사람들이 믿어 주잖아요. 아빠가 토빗네도 빚을 졌었지만 아주 잘 지냈다고 말하는 걸 분명히 들었어요. 경솔하게 행동하는 건 좋을 리가 없어요.” 로저먼드가 태연자약한 지혜로 말했다.\n\n리드게이트는 서로 맞부딪치는 충동들에 마비된 채 앉아 있었다. 로저먼드에게 아무리 이치를 들이대도 그녀의 동의를 끌어낼 가망이 없어 보였으므로, 그는 적어도 어떤 자국이라도 남길 수 있는 무언가를 박살 내고 짓이기든가, 아니면 자기가 가장이고 그녀는 따라야 한다고 거칠게 쏘아붙이고 싶었다. 그러나 그는 그런 극단적인 행동이 두 사람의 부부 생활에 미칠 영향을 두려워했을 뿐 아니라—어떤 권위의 행사도 끝내 매듭짓게 두지 않는 로저먼드의 조용하고 종잡을 수 없는 고집에 대한 두려움이 점점 커져 가고 있었다. 게다가 그녀는, 그와 결혼하면서 행복에 대한 거짓 환상에 속았다는 것을 넌지시 내비침으로써, 그의 가장 예민한 곳을 건드린 터였다. 자기가 가장이라고 말하는 것으로 말하자면, 그것은 사실이 아니었다. 논리와 명예로운 자존심의 힘으로 애써 다잡아 온 바로 그 결심이, 그녀의 전기뱀장어 같은 접촉 아래 풀어지기 시작하고 있었다. 그는 커피를 반쯤 들이켜고는 일어나서 나가려 했다.\n\n“적어도, 다른 방도가 전혀 없다는 게 드러날 때까지는—당분간 트럼불한테 가지 말아 주시기를 청해도 될까요.” 로저먼드가 말했다. 그녀는 별로 겁이 많은 편은 아니었지만, 고드윈 경에게 편지를 썼다는 것을 드러내지 않는 편이 안전하다고 느꼈다. “몇 주만이라도, 아니면 저한테 말하지 않고는 그에게 가지 않겠다고 약속해 줘요.”\n\n리드게이트는 짧게 웃었다. “약속을 요구해야 할 사람은 오히려 나일 것 같은데, 당신이 나한테 말하지 않고는 아무 일도 하지 않겠다는 약속 말이야.” 그가 날카로운 눈길을 그녀에게 돌리며 말하고는, 문 쪽으로 발길을 옮겼다.\n\n“우리 아빠 댁에서 저녁 먹기로 한 거 기억하죠.” 로저먼드가, 그가 돌아서서 자기에게 좀 더 온전한 양보를 해 주기를 바라며 말했다. 그러나 그는 다만 짜증스럽게 “아, 그래.”라고만 하고는 나가 버렸다. 그녀는, 자기한테 그토록 괴로운 제안들을 해야 했던 것으로도 모자라, 그렇게 불쾌한 성미까지 부리는 것이 그에게서 참으로 가증스러운 일이라고 여겼다. 그리고 그녀가 트럼불한테 다시 가는 것을 미뤄 달라는 온건한 부탁을 했을 때, 자기가 어떻게 할 작정인지를 그녀에게 분명히 일러 주지 않은 것은 그의 잔인함이었다. 그녀는 자기가 모든 면에서 최선을 다해 행동했다고 확신했다. 그리고 리드게이트의 거슬리거나 노여운 말 한마디 한마디는 다만 그녀의 마음속 잘못의 명부에 한 항목씩 더해질 뿐이었다. 가엾은 로저먼드는 몇 달째 남편을 실망의 감정과 결부 지어 생각하기 시작했고, 결혼이라는 그 무섭도록 융통성 없는 관계는 즐거운 꿈을 북돋우는 매력을 잃어버렸다. 그것은 그녀를 친정의 갖가지 불편함에서 벗어나게 해 주었지만, 그녀가 바라고 기대했던 모든 것을 주지는 못했다. 그녀가 사랑에 빠졌던 그 리드게이트는, 그녀에게는 한 무더기의 공허한 조건들이었으니, 그 대부분은 이미 사라져 버린 반면, 그 자리는 시시각각 천천히 살아 내야 할 일상의 자질구레한 일들로 채워졌다. 그것은 유리한 면들만 재빨리 골라내 미끄러지듯 지나칠 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 리드게이트의 직업적 습관, 거의 병적인 흡혈귀의 기호처럼 그녀에게 비친, 과학적 주제에 대한 그의 집안에서의 골몰, 구애의 대화에는 한 번도 끼어든 적 없던 사물에 대한 그의 독특한 견해—이 모든 끊임없이 멀어지게 하는 영향들은, 그가 이 고장에서 스스로를 불리한 처지에 놓았다는 사실이 없었더라도, 또 도버의 빚에 관한 그 첫 폭로의 충격이 없었더라도, 그의 존재를 그녀에게 따분한 것으로 만들었을 것이다. 그녀의 결혼 초기부터 넉 달 전까지 줄곧 유쾌한 설렘이 되어 주던 또 다른 존재가 있었으나, 그것은 이제 사라지고 없었다. 로저먼드는 그로 인한 공백이 자기의 사무친 권태와 얼마나 깊이 관련되어 있는지를 스스로에게 인정하려 하지 않았다. 그리고 그녀에게는 (어쩌면 그녀가 옳았는지도 모른다) 콸링엄으로의 초대와, 리드게이트가 미들마치 아닌 다른 곳에—런던이나, 불쾌한 일에서 자유로울 만한 어딘가에 자리 잡을 길이 열린다면, 그것으로 충분히 만족스러울 것이고, 캐소본 부인을 떠받든 일로 어느 정도 원망을 품고 있던 윌 래디슬로의 부재에도 무심해질 수 있을 것 같았다.\n\n새해 첫날, 친정아버지 댁에서 저녁을 먹던 그날 리드게이트와 로저먼드의 사정이 그러했다. 그녀는 아침 식사 자리에서의 그의 고약한 처신을 떠올리며 그에게 온화하게 무관심한 태도를 보였고, 그는 그 아침의 장면이 여러 국면 가운데 하나에 지나지 않았던 그 내면의 갈등에서 훨씬 더 깊은 영향을 받은 채였다. 페어브러더 씨와 이야기하면서 그가 보인 상기된 안간힘—돈을 버는 모든 방법이 본질적으로 다 똑같으며, 우연이라는 것이 선택을 한낱 바보의 환상으로 깎아내리는 제국을 거느린다는, 그 냉소적인 가식 뒤의 안간힘—은 다만 흔들리는 결심의 한 증상이요, 열정의 옛 자극에 대한 둔감해진 반응일 뿐이었다.\n\n그는 어떻게 해야 했을까? 그녀를 브라이드 가의 그 작은 집으로 데려가는 일의 황량함을, 그는 로저먼드보다도 한층 더 절실히 보았다. 그곳에서 그녀는 빈약한 가구에 둘러싸인 채 마음속에는 불만을 품게 될 터였다. 궁핍한 삶과 로저먼드와 함께하는 삶이라는 두 이미지는, 궁핍의 위협이 모습을 드러낸 이래로 점점 더 서로 화해할 수 없는 것이 되어 갔다. 그러나 설령 그의 결심이 그 두 이미지를 억지로 결합시켰다 한들, 그 고된 변화로 가는 데 필요한 유용한 사전 절차들은 손에 잡히는 데 있어 보이지 않았다. 그리고 아내가 청한 약속을 해 주지는 않았으나, 그는 다시 트럼불에게 가지 않았다. 그는 심지어 서둘러 북쪽으로 길을 떠나 고드윈 경을 만나 볼까 하는 생각까지 하기 시작했다. 한때 그는 무슨 일이 있어도 삼촌에게 돈을 청하는 일만큼은 결코 하지 않으리라 믿었지만, 그때는 그보다 한층 더 못마땅한 대안들이 주는 압박의 무게를 온전히 알지 못했던 것이다. 그는 편지의 효과에 기댈 수가 없었다. 아무리 그 자신에게 못마땅한 일일지라도, 직접 만나는 자리에서만 철저히 사정을 설명하고 혈연의 효력을 시험해 볼 수 있었다. 그런데 이 한 걸음을 가장 손쉬운 방도로 머릿속에 그리기 시작하자마자, 곧 분노의 반작용이 일었다—오래전에 그토록 비루한 타산에서, 함께할 목표라곤 없음을 자랑스러워했던 사람들의 성향이며 호주머니에 대한 그런 이기적인 불안에서 멀찍이 떨어져 살기로 작정했던 자기가—그들의 수준으로 떨어진 것도 모자라, 그들에게 구걸하는 수준으로까지 떨어졌다는 데 대한 분노였다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXV.\n\n그대들 [남자들]은 마땅히 참아야 하리라.\n—초서: 『캔터베리 이야기』\n\n무슨 일에든 응답이 더딘 것은 인간 본성의 한 경향이어서, 그것은 만사의 속도가 전반적으로 빨라진 오늘날에도 끝내 승리를 거두는 법이다. 그러니 1832년에 늙은 고드윈 리드게이트 경이 자기 자신보다는 남에게 더 중요한 편지를 쓰는 데 느렸다 한들 무엇이 놀랍겠는가? 새해가 시작된 지 거의 삼 주가 지났고, 로저먼드는 자신의 매력적인 호소에 대한 답장을 기다리며 날마다 실망했다. 리드게이트는 아내의 기대를 전혀 알지 못한 채, 청구서들이 들어오는 것을 지켜보며 도버가 다른 채권자들보다 우위에 선 자기 권리를 곧 행사하리라는 것을 느끼고 있었다. 그는 콸링엄으로 갈 생각을 마음속에 품고 있으면서도 그것을 로저먼드에게 한 번도 말하지 않았다. 분개하며 거절한 뒤에 그녀의 바람에 양보하는 것처럼 보일 일을 마지막 순간까지는 인정하고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 그는 사실 곧 떠날 작정이었다. 철도의 한 구간을 이용하면 왕복 여행 전체를 나흘 만에 해낼 수 있을 터였다.\n\n그런데 어느 날 아침 리드게이트가 외출한 뒤에 그에게 온 편지 한 통이 도착했고, 로저먼드는 그것이 고드윈 경에게서 온 것임을 분명히 알아보았다. 그녀는 희망으로 가득 찼다. 어쩌면 그 안에 자신에게 보내는 특별한 쪽지가 동봉되어 있을지도 몰랐다. 하지만 돈이나 그 밖의 도움 문제라면 당연히 리드게이트에게 편지가 가게 마련이었고, 그에게 답장이 왔다는 사실, 아니 답장이 그토록 늦었다는 사실 자체가 그 답이 완전히 호의적이라는 것을 증명하는 듯했다. 그녀는 이런 생각에 너무 들떠서, 식당의 따뜻한 구석에서 가벼운 바느질이나 할 뿐이었고, 그 중대한 편지의 겉봉을 앞 탁자 위에 놓아두었다. 열두 시쯤 복도에서 남편의 발소리가 들리자 그녀는 종종걸음으로 문을 열러 가며 더없이 가벼운 목소리로 말했다. \"터셔스, 이리 들어와요. 당신한테 편지가 왔어요.\"\n\n\"아?\" 그가 모자도 벗지 않고 다만 그녀를 팔로 감싸 돌려세워 편지가 놓인 곳을 향해 걸으며 말했다. \"고드윈 삼촌이군!\" 그가 소리쳤고, 로저먼드는 다시 자리에 앉아 그가 편지를 뜯는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그녀는 그가 놀라리라 예상했다.\n\n리드게이트의 눈이 그 짧은 편지를 빠르게 훑는 동안, 그녀는 평소 엷은 갈색이던 그의 얼굴이 마른 듯한 흰빛으로 변하는 것을 보았다. 콧방울과 입술을 떨면서 그는 편지를 그녀 앞에 내던지고는 격렬하게 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 늘 이렇게 몰래 행동한다면, 나에게 맞서 행동하고 자기 행동을 숨긴다면, 당신과 함께 사는 삶을 견디기란 불가능할 거요.\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추고 그녀에게 등을 돌렸다. 그러다 휙 돌아서서 이리저리 거닐다가 앉았다가 다시 안절부절못하며 일어나, 주머니 깊숙이 든 물건들을 단단히 움켜쥐었다. 그는 돌이킬 수 없이 잔인한 말을 할까 봐 두려웠다.\n\n로저먼드 역시 편지를 읽으면서 안색이 변했다. 편지는 이런 내용이었다.\n\n\"친애하는 터셔스에게. 무언가 부탁할 일이 있거든 자네 아내를 시켜 나에게 편지를 쓰게 하지 말게. 그것은 빙 둘러 알랑거리는 짓으로, 자네가 그럴 사람이라고는 생각지 못했네. 나는 사업 문제로 여자에게 편지를 쓰는 일을 결코 택하지 않네. 자네에게 천 파운드를, 아니 그 절반이라도 마련해 주는 일이라면, 나로서는 도저히 할 수 없네. 내 가족만으로도 한 푼 남김없이 들어가니 말일세. 아들 둘에 딸 셋을 두었으니, 남는 돈이 있을 리 없지 않은가. 자네는 제 돈을 꽤나 빨리 다 써 버린 모양이고, 지금 있는 곳에서 엉망을 만들어 놓은 듯하네. 그러니 빨리 다른 데로 가는 편이 낫겠네. 하지만 나는 자네 직업의 사람들과는 아무 상관이 없으니, 그 일에서는 자네를 도울 수가 없네. 후견인으로서 나는 자네를 위해 최선을 다했고, 자네가 의술을 택하겠다 했을 때도 자네 뜻대로 하게 두었네. 자네는 군대나 교회로 갈 수도 있었어. 그랬다면 자네 돈으로 충분했을 것이고, 자네 앞에는 더 확실한 사다리가 놓였을 걸세. 자네 삼촌 찰스는 자네가 자기 직업을 택하지 않았다고 자네에게 앙심을 품었지만, 나는 그렇지 않네. 나는 늘 자네가 잘되기를 바랐네만, 이제 자네는 전적으로 제 발로 서야 한다고 여겨야 하네.\n\n자네를 아끼는 삼촌,\n고드윈 리드게이트.\"\n\n편지를 다 읽고 나서 로저먼드는 두 손을 앞에 모은 채 아주 가만히 앉아, 날카로운 실망의 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않고, 남편의 분노 아래 조용한 수동성으로 자신을 에워쌌다. 리드게이트는 거닐던 걸음을 멈추고 다시 그녀를 바라보며 신랄하게 매서운 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"이것으로도 당신이 몰래 참견함으로써 끼칠 수 있는 해악을 깨닫기에 충분하지 않소? 이제 당신이 나를 위해 판단하고 행동할 능력이 없다는 것을, 마땅히 내가 결정할 일에 무지한 채로 끼어든다는 것을 알아차릴 분별력은 있소?\"\n\n모진 말이었다. 하지만 리드게이트가 그녀 때문에 좌절한 것은 이번이 처음이 아니었다. 그녀는 그를 쳐다보지 않았고 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"나는 콸링엄으로 갈 결심을 거의 굳혔었소. 그렇게 하려면 나로서는 충분히 괴로운 일이었겠지만, 그래도 얼마쯤 도움이 되었을지도 모르오. 하지만 내가 무엇을 생각하든 다 소용없는 일이었군. 당신은 늘 몰래 나를 방해해 왔으니까. 당신은 거짓 동의로 나를 속이고, 그러고 나면 나는 당신의 술책에 휘둘리는 신세가 되지. 내가 표하는 모든 바람에 맞설 작정이라면, 그렇다고 말하고 나에게 대놓고 맞서시오. 그러면 적어도 내가 무엇을 하고 있는지는 알 테니.\"\n\n사랑의 끈이 맺어 준 친밀함이 이처럼 서로를 쓰라리게 하는 힘으로 변하는 것은 젊은 삶에서 끔찍한 순간이다. 로저먼드의 자제력에도 불구하고 눈물 한 방울이 소리 없이 흘러 그녀의 입술을 타고 굴러떨어졌다. 그녀는 여전히 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 그 고요함 아래에는 강렬한 영향이 숨겨져 있었다. 그녀는 남편이 너무도 역겨워서, 차라리 그를 만나지 않았더라면 좋았으리라고 생각했다. 그녀를 향한 고드윈 경의 무례함과 완전한 몰인정함은 그를 도버를 비롯한 다른 모든 채권자들과 한 부류로 만들었다. 오직 자기 생각만 하고 그녀에게 얼마나 성가신지는 아랑곳하지 않는 불쾌한 사람들 말이다. 그녀의 아버지조차 매정했고, 그들을 위해 더 해 줄 수 있었을 것이다. 사실 로저먼드의 세계에서 비난받을 만하다고 여겨지지 않는 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었으니, 바로 금발을 땋아 내리고 작은 두 손을 앞에 포갠 우아한 존재, 단 한 번도 어울리지 않게 자신을 드러낸 적 없고 늘 최선을 다해 행동해 온—물론 그 최선이란 으레 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 것이었다—그녀 자신이었다.\n\n리드게이트는 걸음을 멈추고 그녀를 바라보다가, 격정적인 사람이 자신의 격정과 마주한 것이 천진해 보이는 침묵일 때, 그 온순하게 희생당하는 듯한 태도가 도리어 자기를 잘못한 사람으로 만들고, 마침내 가장 정당한 분노마저 그 정당함을 의심하게 만들 때 밀려드는, 그 반쯤 미칠 것 같은 무력감을 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 자기 말을 누그러뜨림으로써 자신이 옳다는 온전한 감각을 되찾아야 했다.\n\n\"로저먼드,\" 그가 다시 입을 열었다. 신랄해지지 않고 그저 진지해지려 애쓰며. \"우리 사이에 솔직함과 신뢰가 없는 것만큼 치명적인 일은 없다는 걸 모르겠소? 나는 거듭거듭 분명한 바람을 표했고, 당신은 동의하는 듯 보였는데, 그러고 나서 당신은 몰래 내 바람을 어겼소. 그런 식이라면 나는 무엇에 의지해야 할지 결코 알 수 없소. 당신이 이것을 인정해 준다면 우리에게도 얼마쯤 희망이 있을 거요. 내가 그렇게도 이치에 어긋나고 사납기만 한 짐승이오? 어째서 나에게 솔직하지 못한 거요?\" 그래도 침묵뿐이었다.\n\n\"당신이 잘못 생각했다고, 그리고 앞으로는 몰래 행동하지 않으리라고 내가 믿어도 된다고, 그 말만이라도 해 주겠소?\" 리드게이트가 다급하게 말했지만, 그 어조에는 로저먼드가 재빨리 알아챈 간청 같은 것이 담겨 있었다. 그녀는 차갑게 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 나에게 쓴 그런 말들에 답하여 인정이나 약속을 할 수는 도저히 없어요. 나는 그런 종류의 말에 익숙하지 않아요. 당신은 내 '몰래 참견함'이니, '무지한 끼어듦'이니, '거짓 동의'니 하는 말을 하셨죠. 나는 당신에게 한 번도 그런 식으로 말한 적이 없으니, 당신이 사과해야 한다고 생각해요. 당신은 나와 함께 사는 게 불가능하다고 했지요. 분명 당신은 요즘 내 삶을 즐겁게 해 주지 못했어요. 우리 결혼이 나에게 가져온 고난을 내가 얼마라도 막아 보려 한 것은 당연한 일이라고 생각해요.\" 로저먼드는 말을 마치며 또 한 방울 눈물을 흘렸고, 처음 것처럼 조용히 닦아 냈다.\n\n리드게이트는 외통수에 몰린 기분으로 의자에 몸을 던졌다. 항변이 깃들 자리가 그녀의 마음 어디에 있단 말인가? 그는 모자를 내려놓고 의자 등받이에 한 팔을 걸친 채, 한참 동안 아무 말 없이 아래를 내려다보았다. 로저먼드는 그를 상대로 이중의 우위를 쥐고 있었다. 그의 책망에 담긴 정당함의 핵심에는 무감각했고, 지금 결혼 생활에 닥친 부인할 수 없는 고난에는 예민했던 것이다. 집 문제에서 그녀가 부린 표리부동함은 그가 아는 것을 넘어섰고, 실제로 플림데일 집안이 그 일을 알지 못하게 막기까지 했지만, 그녀는 자기 행동이 거짓이라 불릴 만하다는 의식이 전혀 없었다. 우리는 우리의 식료품이나 옷감을 엄격히 분류하지 않아도 되는 것처럼, 자신의 행위를 엄밀한 분류에 따라 규정해야 할 의무도 없는 법이다. 로저먼드는 자신이 부당한 일을 당했다고 느꼈고, 바로 이것을 리드게이트가 인정해야 한다고 여겼다.\n\n그로서는, 부정의 정도에 비례해 완고해지는 그녀의 본성에 자신을 맞춰야 한다는 필요가 집게로 죄듯 그를 옭아맸다. 그는 그녀가 자기를 향한 사랑을 돌이킬 수 없이 잃어 가고, 그 결과 둘의 삶이 황량해지리라는 것을 불안하게 예감하기 시작했다. 감정이 쉬이 차오르는 그의 성정 탓에, 이 두려움은 분노의 첫 격렬한 동요와 빠르게 번갈아 들었다. 자기가 그녀의 주인이라고 말한다면, 그것은 분명 그에게 헛된 호언에 지나지 않았을 것이다.\n\n\"당신은 요즘 내 삶을 즐겁게 해 주지 못했어요\"—\"우리 결혼이 나에게 가져온 고난\"—이 말들이, 고통이 꿈을 부풀리듯 그의 상상을 쏘아 댔다. 만약 자신이 가장 드높은 결심에서 가라앉기만 하는 것이 아니라, 가정 내 증오라는 그 흉측한 족쇄 속으로 가라앉아 버린다면?\n\n\"로저먼드,\" 그가 우울한 눈길을 그녀에게 돌리며 말했다. \"남자가 실망하고 화가 났을 때 하는 말은 좀 헤아려 줘야 하오. 당신과 나는 서로 반대되는 이해관계를 가질 수가 없소. 나는 내 행복을 당신의 행복과 떼어 놓을 수 없으니까. 내가 당신에게 화를 내는 건, 어떤 숨김이 우리를 갈라놓는지 당신이 보지 못하는 것 같아서요. 어떻게 내가 말이나 행동으로 당신에게 무언가를 힘들게 하고 싶겠소? 당신을 아프게 하면, 나는 내 삶의 일부를 아프게 하는 거요. 당신이 나에게 아주 솔직하기만 하다면 나는 결코 당신에게 화내지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"나는 그저 당신이 아무런 필요도 없이 우리를 비참함 속으로 몰아넣지 않게 막으려 했을 뿐이에요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. 남편이 누그러지자 마음이 풀려 다시 눈물이 솟았다. \"우리가 아는 사람들 사이에서 여기서 망신을 당하고, 이렇게 비참하게 사는 것은 너무도 견디기 힘든 일이에요. 차라리 아기와 함께 죽었더라면 좋았을 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 그렇게 부드러운 어조로 말하고 울었으니, 그런 말과 눈물은 사랑이 가득한 마음을 지닌 남자에게는 전능한 힘을 발휘하는 법이었다. 리드게이트는 의자를 그녀 가까이 끌어당겨, 강하면서도 다정한 손으로 그녀의 가냘픈 머리를 자기 뺨에 가져다 눌렀다. 그는 그저 그녀를 어루만질 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 무슨 할 말이 있었겠는가? 그는 그녀를 그 두려운 비참함으로부터 지켜 주겠다고 약속할 수 없었으니, 그렇게 할 확실한 방도가 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 다시 외출하려고 그녀를 떠날 때, 그는 이 일이 자기보다 그녀에게 열 배는 더 힘든 일이라고 스스로에게 말했다. 그에게는 집 밖의 삶이 있었고, 다른 이들을 위해 그의 활동을 끊임없이 부르는 일들이 있었으니까. 그는 할 수만 있다면 그녀의 모든 것을 너그러이 보아 주고 싶었다. 하지만 그렇게 너그러워지는 마음으로 그가 그녀를 마치 다른, 더 연약한 종에 속한 동물처럼 여기게 되는 것은 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다. 그럼에도 그녀는 그를 굴복시켰다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXVI.\n\n리드게이트가 자신의 진료가 개인적인 근심을 상쇄하는 데 도움을 준다고 곱씹은 데에는 분명 그럴 만한 이유가 있었다. 그에게는 더 이상 자발적인 연구나 사색적인 사유에 쏟을 자유로운 에너지가 남아 있지 않았으나, 환자의 머리맡에서 그의 판단과 동정심을 향해 직접 가해지는 외부의 요청은 그를 자기 자신으로부터 끌어내는 데 필요한 추가적인 자극이 되어 주었다. 그것은 단순히 어리석은 사람들로 하여금 점잖게 살게 하고 불행한 사람들로 하여금 평온하게 살게 해 주는 그 자비로운 일상의 굴레만은 아니었다. 그것은 즉각적이고 신선한 사고의 적용을, 그리고 타인의 필요와 시련에 대한 배려를 끊임없이 요구하는 것이었다. 삶을 돌아보는 우리들 중 많은 이는 우리가 알아 온 가장 친절한 사람이 어느 의사였다고, 혹은 깊은 식견에서 우러난 통찰에 의해 인도된 그 섬세한 솜씨로 우리가 곤경에 처했을 때 기적을 행하는 자들의 그것보다 더 숭고한 자애로움을 가지고 우리에게 다가와 준 어느 외과 의사였다고 말할 것이다. 그 두 배로 축복받은 자비의 얼마쯤은 언제나 리드게이트가 병원에서나 개인 가정에서 일할 때 그와 함께하며, 어떤 아편제보다도 더 잘 그를 진정시키고 그의 불안과 정신적 퇴화의 감각 아래에서 그를 지탱해 주었다.\n\n그러나 아편제에 관한 페어브러더 씨의 의심은 사실이었다. 예견된 어려움이 처음으로 그를 쓰라리게 짓누르고, 그의 결혼이 멍에 씌워진 외로움이 아니려면 사랑받는 것에 너무 마음 쓰지 않으면서 계속 사랑하려 애쓰는 상태가 되어야 한다는 것을 처음으로 깨달았을 때, 그는 한두 번 아편을 한 차례 복용해 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 그에게는 비참함의 망령으로부터 그런 일시적인 도피를 갈망하는 유전적이고 체질적인 욕구는 없었다. 그는 건강했고, 포도주를 많이 마실 수 있었으나 그것에 마음을 두지 않았다. 주위 남자들이 독주를 마실 때 그는 설탕물을 들었으니, 술기운에 흥분하는 가장 초기 단계조차 경멸 어린 연민으로 바라보았던 것이다. 도박에 대해서도 마찬가지였다. 그는 파리에서 수많은 도박을 지켜본 적이 있었는데, 마치 그것이 하나의 질병이라도 되는 양 관찰했다. 그는 술에 끌리지 않은 것과 마찬가지로 그런 승리에도 끌리지 않았다. 그는 자신이 마음을 둘 수 있는 유일한 승리란, 자애로운 결과를 지향하는 높고도 어려운 조합의 의식적인 과정을 통해서만 얻어져야 한다고 스스로에게 말해 왔다. 그가 갈망한 권능은 한 무더기의 동전을 움켜쥐는 떨리는 손가락으로, 혹은 풀이 죽은 스무 명의 동료가 건 판돈을 두 팔로 쓸어 담는 한 남자의 눈에 어린 반쯤은 야만적이고 반쯤은 백치 같은 승리감으로는 결코 표현될 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 그가 아편을 시도해 본 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그의 생각은 이제 도박 쪽으로 향하기 시작했다. 그 흥분에 대한 욕구 때문이 아니라, 어떤 청탁도 필요 없고 어떤 책임도 따르지 않는, 돈을 손쉽게 얻는 그 방법을 향한 일종의 갈망 어린 내면의 응시로 인한 것이었다. 만약 그가 그 무렵 런던이나 파리에 있었더라면, 기회까지 더해진 그런 생각들은 아마도 그를 도박장으로 이끌었을 것이다. 더 이상 도박꾼들을 구경하기 위해서가 아니라, 그들과 한 마음의 열망을 품고 함께 도박을 지켜보기 위해서였을 것이다. 만약 운이 그에게 친절을 베풀어 주기만 한다면, 이기고 싶다는 엄청난 욕구가 혐오감을 압도해 버렸을 것이다. 삼촌에게서 도움을 받겠다는 그 허황된 생각이 배제된 지 그리 오래되지 않아 일어난 한 사건은, 도박할 어떤 기회라도 있었다면 뒤따랐을 법한 결과를 강력하게 보여 주는 징표였다.\n\n그린 드래건의 당구장은 어떤 무리가 늘 모여드는 곳이었는데, 그들 대부분은 우리가 아는 뱀브리지 씨처럼 한량으로 여겨지는 사람들이었다. 가엾은 프레드 빈시가 그 잊지 못할 빚의 일부를 진 곳도 바로 여기였으니, 내기에서 돈을 잃고 그 유쾌한 동료에게 빌리지 않을 수 없었던 것이다. 이런 식으로 많은 돈을 잃고 따고 한다는 것은 미들마치에 널리 알려진 사실이었고, 그 결과 그린 드래건이 방탕한 장소로 얻은 평판은 자연히 어떤 부류 사람들에게는 그곳에 가고 싶은 유혹을 더욱 부추겼다. 아마도 그곳의 단골손님들은, 프리메이슨의 비밀 입회자들처럼, 자기들끼리만 간직할 그곳에 관한 무언가 좀 더 굉장한 것이 있었으면 하고 바랐을 것이다. 그러나 그들은 폐쇄된 집단이 아니었고, 젊은이들뿐 아니라 점잖은 연장자들도 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보려고 이따금 당구장에 들렀다. 당구에 근육적 소질이 있고 그 게임을 좋아했던 리드게이트도 미들마치에 도착한 초기 무렵에는 한두 번 그린 드래건에서 큐를 잡고 차례를 가져 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 그 후로는 게임을 할 여가도 없었고 그곳의 사교에 대한 흥미도 없었다. 그런데 어느 날 저녁 그는 그 모임 장소에서 뱀브리지 씨를 찾아야 할 일이 생겼다. 이 말 장수가 그에게 남은 좋은 말 한 필을 사 갈 손님을 구해 주기로 약속했던 것인데, 리드게이트는 그 말 대신 값싼 승용마로 바꾸기로 작정한 터였다. 이렇게 격을 낮춤으로써 어쩌면 이십 파운드쯤 얻기를 바랐던 것이다. 그리고 그는 이제 거래처 상인들의 인내심을 먹여 살리는 데 보탬이 될 만한 푼돈 하나하나에까지 마음을 쓰고 있었다. 지나가는 길에 당구장으로 잠깐 올라가면 시간을 절약할 수 있을 터였다.\n\n뱀브리지 씨는 아직 오지 않았으나 곧 틀림없이 도착할 거라고 그의 친구 호록 씨가 말했다. 그래서 리드게이트는 시간을 보낼 겸 한 게임을 하며 머물렀다. 그날 저녁 그의 눈에는 페어브러더 씨가 언젠가 그에게서 알아챈 적이 있던 그 독특한 빛과 평소답지 않은 생기가 어려 있었다. 그가 그곳에 있다는 이례적인 사실은, 미들마치 사람들이 꽤 많이 모인 그 방에서 큰 주목을 받았다. 그리고 몇몇 구경꾼들은 물론 게임을 하던 사람들 중 일부도 활기차게 내기를 걸고 있었다. 리드게이트는 게임을 잘하고 있었고 자신감을 느꼈다. 그의 주위로 내기 돈이 떨어졌고, 말을 팔아 절약하는 금액을 두 배로 불려 줄지도 모를 예상 수익을 잽싸게 떠올리며, 그는 자기 게임에 내기를 걸기 시작했고 거듭 이겼다. 뱀브리지 씨가 들어왔으나 리드게이트는 그를 알아채지 못했다. 그는 게임에 흥분해 있었을 뿐 아니라, 이튿날 더 큰 규모의 도박을 할 수 있는 브래싱으로 가는 환영이 눈앞에 어른거리고 있었다. 그곳에서라면 악마의 미끼를 한 번 강하게 낚아챔으로써 바늘은 피한 채 미끼만 가지고 달아나, 날마다 시달리는 빚 독촉으로부터의 구출을 사들일 수 있을지도 몰랐다.\n\n두 명의 새로운 방문객이 들어왔을 때에도 그는 여전히 이기고 있었다. 그중 한 사람은 시내에서 법학 공부를 막 마치고 돌아온 젊은 홀리였고, 다른 한 사람은 요즈음 자신의 옛 단골 장소에서 여러 저녁을 보내곤 했던 프레드 빈시였다. 능숙한 당구 선수인 젊은 홀리는 침착하고 산뜻한 솜씨로 큐를 잡았다. 그러나 프레드 빈시는 리드게이트를 보고 깜짝 놀랐고, 흥분한 기색으로 내기를 거는 그를 보고 어안이 벙벙해져, 한쪽으로 비켜서서 테이블 주위의 원 밖에 머물러 있었다.\n\n프레드는 요즈음 결심에 대한 보상으로 약간의 방종을 즐기고 있던 참이었다. 그는 여섯 달 동안 가스 씨 밑에서 온갖 야외 작업에 진심으로 매달려 왔고, 혹독한 연습 끝에 자신의 형편없는 글씨체의 결함을 거의 극복한 터였다. 다만 이 연습은 종종 가스 씨 댁에서 저녁에 메리의 눈길 아래 이루어졌던 만큼 어쩌면 그다지 혹독하지는 않았을지도 모른다. 그러나 지난 두 주 동안 메리는, 페어브러더 씨가 교구 사업 몇 가지를 추진하느라 미들마치에 머무는 동안, 로윅 목사관에서 그곳 부인들과 함께 지내고 있었다. 그래서 프레드는 달리 더 마음에 드는 할 일을 찾지 못한 채 그린 드래건에 들렀던 것이니, 한편으로는 당구를 치기 위해서였고 한편으로는 말과 스포츠, 그리고 그다지 엄격하게 올바르지만은 않은 관점에서 바라본 이런저런 것들에 관한 담소의 옛 맛을 보기 위해서였다. 그는 이번 시즌에 사냥을 한 번도 나가지 못했고, 탈 자기 말도 없었으며, 주로 가스 씨와 함께 그의 이륜마차를 타거나 가스 씨가 빌려줄 수 있는 그 점잖은 조랑말을 타고 이곳저곳을 다녔다. 프레드는 자신이 성직자였을 경우보다 더 엄격하게 고삐에 매여 지내는 것은 좀 너무하다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. \"이거 봐요, 메리 아가씨, 측량을 배우고 도면을 그리는 일은 설교를 쓰는 것보다 오히려 더 힘든 일일 거예요.\" 그는 그녀를 위해 자신이 어떤 고생을 겪고 있는지 그녀가 알아주기를 바라며 그렇게 말했었다. \"그리고 헤라클레스니 테세우스니 하는 자들은 나에 비하면 아무것도 아니죠. 그들은 운동이나 했지, 부기용 글씨체를 쓰는 법은 한 번도 배우지 않았으니까요.\" 그리고 이제 메리가 잠시 자리를 비우자, 프레드는 목걸이를 벗어 던질 수 없는 다른 어떤 힘센 개와 마찬가지로 사슬의 말뚝을 뽑아내어 작은 탈출을 감행했던 것이니, 물론 빨리 혹은 멀리 갈 생각은 없었다. 그가 당구를 치지 못할 이유는 없었으나, 그는 내기는 하지 않기로 굳게 마음먹고 있었다. 당장의 돈 문제로 말하자면, 프레드는 가스 씨가 그에게 준 팔십 파운드 가운데 거의 전부를 저축해 돌려주겠다는 영웅적인 계획을 마음에 품고 있었으니, 그는 옷이 남아돌 만큼 있었고 식비도 들지 않았으므로 쓸데없는 돈 낭비를 모두 그만두면 이 일을 쉽게 해낼 수 있었다. 그렇게 하면 그는 일 년 안에, 불행히도 가스 부인이 지금보다 그 돈을 더 절실히 필요로 하던 시기에 그녀에게서 빼앗았던 구십 파운드를 갚는 일에 상당히 진전을 볼 수 있을 터였다. 그럼에도 인정해야 할 것은, 최근 당구장을 다섯 번째로 찾은 이날 저녁, 프레드의 주머니가 아니라 마음속에는, 반년치 봉급에서 자기 몫으로 떼어 두려 했던 십 파운드가 있었다는 점이다(메리가 다시 집에 돌아올 무렵 가스 부인에게 삼십 파운드를 가져다드리는 기쁨을 앞두고 있었던 것이다). 그는 좋은 내기의 기회가 생긴다면 무언가를 걸어 볼 만한 밑천으로서 그 십 파운드를 마음속에 품고 있었다. 왜? 글쎄, 금화가 이리저리 날아다니는데 어찌 몇 닢쯤 잡아채지 않을 수 있겠는가? 그는 결코 다시는 그 길을 멀리 가지 않을 터였다. 그러나 사람이란, 특히 한량들이란 대개, 마음만 먹으면 자기가 못된 짓을 어디까지 할 수 있는지를 스스로 확인하고 싶어 하는 법이며, 또 자기가 병이 들도록 마시거나, 거지가 되거나, 인간의 능력이 허용하는 좁은 한계 안에서 최대한 헤프게 떠벌리는 짓을 삼간다면 그것은 자기가 얼간이여서가 아니라는 것을 확인하고 싶어 하는 법이다. 프레드는 정식 논리를 따져 본 것은 아니었으니, 그런 논리란 옛 습관이 짜릿하게 되돌아오는 것이며 젊은 혈기의 변덕을 표현하기에는 매우 인위적이고 부정확한 방식이다. 그러나 그날 저녁 그의 안에는, 게임을 시작하면 내기도 시작하게 되리라는, 펀치를 좀 마시며 즐기고, 대체로 아침이면 \"다소 찌뿌둥한\" 기분을 느낄 채비를 하게 되리라는 예언적인 예감이 도사리고 있었다. 행동이란 흔히 이처럼 규정하기 어려운 움직임 속에서 시작되는 법이다.\n\n그러나 프레드의 예상에 가장 들어올 법하지 않은 일은, 자신의 매형 리드게이트가, 그가 잘난 체하는 인간이며 자신의 우월함을 지독히 의식하고 있다는 옛 견해를 결코 완전히 버린 적 없던 그 매형이, 마치 자기가 그랬을지도 모르는 것처럼 흥분한 채 내기를 거는 모습을 보게 되는 것이었다. 프레드는, 리드게이트가 빚을 졌고 그의 아버지가 그를 돕기를 거절했다는 막연한 사실만으로는 도저히 다 설명할 수 없는 큰 충격을 느꼈다. 그리고 게임에 끼어들고 싶던 그 자신의 마음도 별안간 억제되었다. 그것은 묘하게 뒤바뀐 태도였다. 평소에는 밝고 무심하며 무엇이든 재미를 약속하는 것에는 기꺼이 주의를 기울이던 프레드의 금발 얼굴과 푸른 눈은, 무언가 어울리지 않는 광경을 본 듯이 자기도 모르게 심각하고 거의 당황한 빛을 띠고 있었다. 반면 평소에는 침착하고 강인한 태도와, 가장 주의 깊은 관찰의 이면에 깔려 있는 듯한 어떤 사색적인 면모를 지녔던 리드게이트는, 사나운 눈빛과 오므렸다 폈다 하는 발톱을 지닌 짐승을 연상시키는 그 흥분되고 좁아진 의식으로 행동하고, 지켜보고, 말하고 있었다.\n\n리드게이트는 자기가 친 공에 내기를 걸어 십육 파운드를 땄으나, 젊은 홀리의 등장이 형세의 균형을 바꿔 놓았다. 홀리 자신도 일류의 솜씨로 공을 쳤고, 리드게이트가 친 공에 반대로 내기를 걸기 시작했다. 그리하여 리드게이트의 신경에 가해지는 긴장은 자기 동작에 대한 단순한 자신감에서, 그 동작에 대한 타인의 의심에 맞서는 것으로 바뀌었다. 그 도전은 자신감보다 더 흥분되는 것이었으나 덜 확실했다. 그는 계속 자기 게임에 내기를 걸었으나, 자주 실패하기 시작했다. 그래도 그는 계속했으니, 그의 마음은 그가 그곳의 가장 무지한 빈둥거리는 자였더라도 그러했을 만큼 그 게임이라는 가파른 틈새로 완전히 좁아들어 있었기 때문이다. 프레드는 리드게이트가 빠르게 잃고 있는 것을 보고, 무례하게 굴지 않으면서 리드게이트의 주의를 돌려놓을, 그리고 어쩌면 그가 이 방을 떠날 만한 구실을 넌지시 일러 줄 어떤 방책을 짜내느라 머리를 쥐어짜는 새로운 처지에 놓이게 되었다. 그는 다른 사람들도 리드게이트가 평소의 그답지 않게 이상하다는 것을 눈여겨보고 있음을 알아챘고, 그저 그의 팔꿈치를 건드려 잠시 한쪽으로 불러내면 그를 몰입 상태에서 깨어나게 할 수 있으리라는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그가 생각해 낼 수 있는 것이라곤, 로지를 보고 싶은데 오늘 저녁 그녀가 집에 있는지 알고 싶다고 말하는, 그 대담하고 그럴듯하지 않은 핑계뿐이었다. 그가 막 자포자기하는 심정으로 이 옹색한 방책을 실행에 옮기려는 참에, 한 종업원이 그에게 다가와 페어브러더 씨가 아래층에 와 계시며 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다는 전갈을 전했다.\n\n프레드는 그리 편치 않게 놀랐으나, 곧 내려가겠다는 말을 전하고 나서, 새로운 충동에 이끌려 리드게이트에게 다가가 \"잠깐 얘기 좀 할 수 있을까요?\" 하고 말하며 그를 한쪽으로 끌어냈다.\n\n\"페어브러더 씨가 방금 저에게 할 얘기가 있다는 전갈을 보냈어요. 아래층에 와 계시고요. 그분에게 하실 말씀이 있으시면 그분이 와 계신 걸 알고 싶어 하실 것 같아서요.\"\n\n프레드는 그저 말을 꺼낼 핑계로 이것을 붙잡았던 것이니, \"형님이 지독하게 잃고 있고, 모두가 형님을 빤히 쳐다보게 만들고 있어요. 그만 자리를 뜨시는 게 좋겠어요.\" 하고 말할 수는 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 영감인들 이보다 더 그를 도울 수는 없었을 것이다. 리드게이트는 그때까지 프레드가 거기 있는 줄 몰랐던 터라, 페어브러더 씨를 알리는 그의 갑작스러운 출현은 날카로운 충격 같은 효과를 냈다.\n\n\"아니, 아니야.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"그분에게 특별히 할 얘기는 없어. 하지만, 게임은 끝났으니, 나는 가 봐야겠다. 뱀브리지를 좀 보려고 들렀던 거니까.\"\n\n\"뱀브리지는 저쪽에 있긴 한데, 소란을 피우고 있어서요. 거래할 정신은 아닌 것 같아요. 저랑 같이 내려가서 페어브러더 씨를 만나요. 그분이 저를 호되게 야단치실 것 같은데, 형님이 저를 좀 막아 주세요.\" 프레드가 약간의 재치를 부려 말했다.\n\n리드게이트는 부끄러움을 느꼈으나, 페어브러더 씨 만나기를 거절함으로써 그 부끄러움을 느끼는 듯 행동할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 그래서 그는 내려갔다. 그러나 그들은 그저 악수를 나누고 서리에 관해 이야기했을 뿐이다. 그리고 세 사람이 거리로 나서자, 목사는 기꺼이 리드게이트에게 작별을 고하고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 그의 지금 목적은 분명히 프레드와 단둘이 이야기하는 것이었으니, 그는 친절하게 말했다. \"젊은이, 자네를 방해해서 미안하네만, 자네에게 긴히 할 이야기가 있어서 말이야. 세인트 보톨프 교회까지 나와 함께 걸어 주겠나?\"\n\n화창한 밤이었고, 하늘에는 별이 빽빽했다. 그래서 페어브러더 씨는 런던 가도를 따라 그 오래된 교회까지 빙 돌아가자고 제안했다. 그가 그다음에 한 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"리드게이트는 그린 드래건에 절대 가지 않는 사람이라고 생각했는데?\"\n\n\"저도 그렇게 생각했어요.\" 프레드가 말했다. \"그런데 뱀브리지를 만나러 갔다고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"그럼 게임을 하고 있지는 않았던 거고?\"\n\n프레드는 이 얘기를 할 생각이 없었으나, 이제는 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"네, 하고 있었어요. 하지만 우연한 일이었던 것 같아요. 전에는 거기서 그분을 본 적이 한 번도 없거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 자네는 요즈음 자주 가곤 했다는 거로군?\"\n\n\"아, 다섯 번이나 여섯 번쯤이요.\"\n\n\"내 생각에 자네는 거기 다니는 버릇을 끊을 만한 어떤 그럴 만한 이유가 있었던 것 같은데?\"\n\n\"네. 그건 다 아시잖아요.\" 이런 식으로 추궁당하는 게 달갑지 않아 프레드가 말했다. \"제가 다 솔직히 털어놓았으니까요.\"\n\n\"그러니 내가 지금 이 문제에 관해 이야기할 자격이 있는 셈이지. 우리 사이엔 그렇게 통하는 거 아닌가? 우리가 터놓고 지내는 사이라는 거 말일세. 내가 자네 이야기를 들어 주었으니, 자네도 기꺼이 내 이야기를 들어 주겠지. 이번엔 내가 내 얘기를 좀 할 차례를 가져도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"저는 목사님께 더없이 깊은 은혜를 입고 있는걸요, 페어브러더 씨.\" 프레드가 불안한 추측에 사로잡힌 채 말했다.\n\n\"자네가 나에게 어느 정도 신세를 지고 있다는 걸 부인하는 척하지는 않겠네. 하지만 프레드, 자네에게 고백하건대, 방금 전에 나는 자네에게 입을 다묾으로써 그 모든 것을 뒤집어 버리고 싶은 유혹을 받았다네. 누군가가 나에게 '빈시 가의 젊은이가 또 매일 밤 당구대에 붙어살기 시작했어요. 그 친구 고삐를 오래 견디지 못할 겁니다.' 하고 말했을 때, 나는 지금 내가 하고 있는 것과 정반대의 일을 하고 싶은 유혹을 받았네. 입을 다물고, 자네가 다시 그 사다리를 내려가는 동안, 처음엔 내기를 걸고 그다음엔—\"\n\n\"저는 내기를 하나도 하지 않았어요.\" 프레드가 황급히 말했다.\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘군. 하지만 내 말은, 나의 충동은 가만히 지켜보면서 자네가 잘못된 길로 접어들어, 가스의 인내심을 닳게 하고, 자네 인생 최고의 기회를—자네가 꽤나 힘든 노력을 들여 확보한 그 기회를—잃어버리는 것을 보고 싶다는 것이었어. 자네는 내 안에 그런 유혹을 일으킨 그 감정을 짐작할 수 있을 걸세. 자네도 분명히 알 거야. 자네의 애정이 충족되는 것이 내 애정이 충족되는 길을 가로막고 있다는 것을 자네도 분명히 알 거라고 말일세.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 페어브러더 씨는 그 사실을 인정하기를 기다리는 듯했고, 그 고운 목소리의 어조에 감지되는 감정은 그의 말에 엄숙함을 더해 주었다. 그러나 어떤 감정도 프레드의 경악을 가라앉힐 수는 없었다.\n\n\"제가 그녀를 포기하리라고 기대하실 수는 없잖아요.\" 그는 잠시 머뭇거린 끝에 말했다. 이것은 너그러운 척할 만한 경우가 아니었다.\n\n\"물론 그렇지, 그녀의 애정이 자네의 애정과 만났으니 말일세. 하지만 이런 종류의 관계는, 아무리 오래 이어진 것이라도, 언제든 변할 수 있는 법이야. 나는 자네가 그녀가 자네에게 느끼는 유대를 느슨하게 만드는 식으로 행동할 수도 있다는 것을 쉽게 상상할 수 있네. 그녀가 자네에게 단지 조건부로 매여 있을 뿐이라는 것을 잊어선 안 되지. 그리고 그런 경우에, 자기가 그녀의 호의에 대해 영향력을 지녔다고 우쭐댈 만한 또 다른 남자가, 자네가 놓쳐 버린 그 존경뿐 아니라 사랑 속의 확고한 자리까지 차지하는 데 성공할 수도 있다는 것을 말일세. 나는 그런 결과를 쉽게 상상할 수 있어.\" 페어브러더 씨가 힘주어 거듭 말했다. \"기꺼이 마음을 헤아려 주는 동반자 관계라는 것이 있어서, 그것은 아무리 오래된 관계라도 능가할 수 있는 법이지.\" 프레드에게는 만약 페어브러더 씨가 그 유능한 혀 대신 부리와 발톱을 지녔더라도 그 공격 방식이 이보다 더 잔인할 수는 없을 것 같았다. 그는 이 모든 가정적인 진술의 이면에, 메리의 감정에 일어난 어떤 실제 변화에 대한 앎이 있다는 끔찍한 확신을 느꼈다.\n\n\"물론 저는 저에 관한 모든 게 쉽게 끝장날 수 있다는 걸 알아요.\" 그가 괴로운 목소리로 말했다. \"만약 그녀가 비교하기 시작했다면—\" 그는 자신이 느끼는 모든 것을 드러내고 싶지 않아 말을 끊었다가, 약간의 쓰라림에 기대어 말했다. \"하지만 저는 목사님이 저에게 우호적이신 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다네. 그래서 우리가 이렇게 함께 있는 거지. 하지만 나는 그렇지 않은 쪽으로 강하게 기울었던 적이 있어. 나는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했네. '저 젊은이가 제 신세를 망칠 가능성이 있다면, 왜 네가 끼어드느냐? 너도 그 친구만큼 가치 있는 사람 아니냐. 그리고 네가 그 친구보다 열여섯 해를 더 살았고, 그동안 꽤나 배를 곯아 가며 지냈으니, 그 세월이 그 친구보다 너에게 더 만족을 누릴 권리를 주는 것 아니냐. 그 친구가 파멸로 치달을 가능성이 있다면, 그냥 내버려 두어라. 어쩌면 네가 어떻게 해도 그것을 막을 수 없을지도 모른다. 그러니 너는 그 덕을 보아라.'\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그 사이 프레드는 더없이 불편한 한기에 사로잡혔다. 다음엔 무슨 말이 나올까? 그는 메리에게 무슨 말이 전해졌다는 것을 듣게 될까 두려웠다. 그는 마치 경고가 아니라 위협을 듣고 있는 것 같은 기분이었다. 목사가 다시 입을 열었을 때 그의 어조에는, 장조로 옮겨 가는 격려의 전환과 같은 변화가 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 한때 그보다 더 나은 뜻을 품었었고, 이제 그 옛 의도로 돌아왔다네. 프레드, 내 안에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 자네에게 그대로 말해 주는 것보다 그 의도를 더 단단히 지킬 방법은 거의 없으리라고 생각했어. 그래, 이제 내 말을 알아듣겠나? 나는 자네가 그녀의 삶과 자네 자신의 삶을 행복하게 만들기를 바라네. 그리고 나의 경고 한마디가 그에 어긋날 어떤 위험을 비껴가게 할 가능성이 조금이라도 있다면, 그래, 나는 그 말을 한 셈이지.\"\n\n목사가 그 마지막 말을 할 때 그의 목소리는 한 단계 가라앉았다. 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그들은 길이 세인트 보톨프 쪽으로 갈라지는 푸른 풀밭에 서 있었는데, 그는 마치 대화가 끝났음을 넌지시 알리려는 듯 손을 내밀었다. 프레드는 아주 새로운 감동을 받았다. 훌륭한 행위를 바라보는 데 몹시 예민한 어떤 이가 말하기를, 그런 행위는 일종의 거듭나게 하는 전율을 온몸에 일으키며, 새 삶을 시작할 채비가 된 듯한 기분을 느끼게 한다고 했다. 바로 그때 프레드 빈시에게도 그런 효과가 상당히 나타나고 있었다.\n\n\"저는 합당한 사람이 되려고 애쓰겠어요.\" 그는 \"그녀에게뿐 아니라 목사님께도\"라고 말하려다 말고 끊었다. 그러는 동안 페어브러더 씨는 무언가를 더 말하고 싶은 충동을 모으고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 지금 그녀가 자네를 선호하는 마음이 식고 있다고 믿는다고 생각해선 안 되네, 프레드. 자네가 바르게 처신하면 다른 것들도 다 바르게 될 테니, 마음을 푹 놓게나.\"\n\n\"목사님이 해 주신 일을 결코 잊지 않겠어요.\" 프레드가 대답했다. \"뭔가 할 만한 말이 떠오르지 않습니다. 다만 목사님의 선의가 헛되이 버려지지 않도록 애쓰겠어요.\"\n\n\"그거면 충분하네. 잘 가게, 그리고 신의 가호가 있기를.\"\n\n그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다. 그러나 두 사람 다 별빛 속에서 벗어나기 전에 오래도록 이리저리 거닐었다. 프레드의 골똘한 생각은 대체로 이런 말로 요약될 수 있을 것이다. \"그녀가 페어브러더와 결혼하는 게 분명 멋진 일이었겠지. 하지만 그녀가 나를 가장 사랑하고 내가 좋은 남편이 된다면야?\"\n\n어쩌면 페어브러더 씨의 생각은 한 번의 어깨짓과 한마디 짧은 말로 응축될 수 있을지도 몰랐다. \"한 자그마한 여인이 한 남자의 삶에서 어떤 역할을 할 수 있는지를 생각하면! 그녀를 단념하는 것은 영웅적 행위의 매우 그럴듯한 흉내일 수 있고, 그녀를 얻는 것은 하나의 수양일 수 있으니!\"\n\n# 제63장\n\n이제 영혼 안에서 내전이 벌어진다.\n결의는 신성한 옥좌에서 밀려나고\n\n아우성치는 욕구들에 의해, 그리고 대재상인 자존심은\n비굴한 협약을 맺으며, 굶주린 반역자들을 위해\n\n사절이자 능란한 혀를 놀리는 변호인의\n유연한 역할을 떠맡는다.\n\n다행히 리드게이트는 당구장에서 결국 잃은 것으로 끝나, 운에 기대어 한판 벌이려는 부추김을 안고 돌아오지는 않았다. 오히려 이튿날 그는 딴 돈 외에 사오 파운드를 더 물어내야 했을 때 자신에 대한 순수한 혐오를 느꼈고, 자기가 그려 낸 꼴—그린 드래건의 사내들과 팔꿈치를 부비며 어울렸을 뿐 아니라 그들과 똑같이 처신한 모습—에 대한 가장 불쾌한 영상을 떨치지 못한 채 지니고 다녔다. 도박에 빠진 철학자는 같은 처지에 놓인 속물과 거의 구별되지 않는다. 그 차이는 주로 그 뒤에 이어지는 성찰에서 발견될 터인데, 리드게이트는 바로 그런 식으로 매우 떨떠름한 되새김질을 했다. 그의 이성은 무대 배경이 조금만 달랐더라면 그 일이 어떻게 파멸로 부풀려질 수 있었을지를 일러 주었다—만일 그가 들어선 곳이, 엄지와 검지로 운을 집어 올리는 대신 두 손으로 움켜쥘 수 있는 도박장이었더라면. 그럼에도 이성이 도박하려는 욕망을 목 졸랐으나, 필요한 만큼의 운이 보장되기만 한다면, 불가피하게 다가오기 시작한 그 대안을 택하느니 차라리 도박을 하고 싶었으리라는 느낌은 여전히 남아 있었다.\n그 대안이란 불스트로드 씨에게 부탁하는 것이었다. 리드게이트는 자신이 불스트로드로부터 전적으로 독립해 있으며, 자신의 직업적 활동과 공공의 이익에 관한 자기 생각을 실현하게 해 주었기 때문에 오로지 그 이유로 그의 계획에 협력했을 뿐이라고 스스로에게도 남들에게도 숱하게 자랑해 왔고—두 사람의 개인적 교류에서도, 자기가 경멸할 만하다고 여기는 의견을 지녔으며 종종 모순된 인상이 우스꽝스럽게 뒤섞인 듯 보이는 동기를 가진 이 우세한 은행가를 사회적으로 유익하게 활용하고 있다는 의식으로 자존심을 떠받쳐 왔던지라—그리하여 자기 자신을 위해 그에게 상당한 청을 내미는 일에 대해 스스로 강력한 관념적 장벽을 쌓아 온 셈이었다.\n\n그래도 3월 초에 그의 형편은, 사람들이 자신의 맹세는 무지한 상태에서 내뱉은 것이라고 말하기 시작하고, 자신에게는 불가능하다고 일컬었던 행위가 명백히 가능해지고 있음을 깨닫게 되는 그런 막바지에 이르러 있었다. 도버의 흉한 담보가 곧 집행될 참이고, 개업 수입은 빚을 갚느라 곧장 빨려 들어가고, 최악의 사정이 알려진다면 일용품을 외상으로 받지 못할 가능성이 있고, 무엇보다 로저먼드의 가망 없는 불만에 찬 모습이 끊임없이 그를 따라다니는 가운데, 리드게이트는 자신이 불가피하게 누군가에게든 도움을 청하는 쪽으로 굽힐 수밖에 없으리라는 것을 알기 시작했다. 처음에 그는 빈시 씨에게 편지를 써야 할지 고민했다. 하지만 로저먼드에게 물어보니, 그가 짐작했던 대로 그녀는 이미 두 차례나 아버지에게 부탁했었고, 마지막은 고드윈 경으로부터 실망스러운 소식을 들은 뒤였으며, 아버지는 리드게이트가 알아서 해야 한다고 말했다는 것이었다. \"아버지 말씀이, 흉년이 해마다 이어지는 통에 점점 더 빌린 자본으로 장사를 하게 되었고, 여러 가지 즐거움을 포기해야 했다고 하셨어요. 가족을 부양하는 비용에서 백 파운드 한 장도 떼어 줄 수 없다고요. 리드게이트더러 불스트로드에게 부탁하라고 하셨어요. 둘은 늘 죽이 잘 맞는 사이였으니까.\"\n사실 리드게이트 자신도, 만일 결국 무담보 대출을 청해야 한다면, 적어도 다른 누구와의 관계보다는 불스트로드와의 관계가 순전히 개인적인 것만은 아닌 청구의 형태를 띨 수 있으리라는 결론에 이르러 있었다. 불스트로드는 간접적으로 그의 개업이 실패하는 데 원인을 보탰고, 또한 자기 계획에 의료 동업자를 얻은 것에 크게 만족했었다. 그러나 우리 가운데 누가, 지금 리드게이트가 처한 그런 종류의 의존 상태로 자신을 떨어뜨리면서도, 부탁의 굴욕을 덜어 주는 모종의 청구권을 자기가 지녔다고 믿으려 애쓰지 않았겠는가? 최근 들어 불스트로드가 병원에 대한 관심에서 새로운 무기력을 보이는 듯했던 것은 사실이었다. 하지만 그의 건강은 더 나빠졌고, 뿌리 깊은 신경성 질환의 징후를 드러내고 있었다. 다른 면에서 그는 달라진 듯 보이지 않았다. 그는 늘 대단히 정중했으나, 리드게이트는 처음부터 그에게서 자기 결혼이나 다른 사적인 사정에 관한 두드러진 냉담함을 눈여겨보았는데, 그 냉담함을 그는 지금껏 두 사람 사이의 어떤 친근한 온기보다 더 선호해 왔다. 그는 그 의도를 하루하루 미루었는데, 가능한 온갖 결론과 그에 따르는 행동에 대한 거부감 탓에 결론에 따라 행동하던 그의 습관이 흔들렸기 때문이다. 그는 불스트로드 씨를 자주 만났지만, 어떤 기회도 자신의 사적인 목적에 이용하려 들지 않았다. 어느 순간에는 \"편지를 써야지. 빙빙 둘러 가는 말보다는 그게 낫겠어\"라고 생각했고, 또 어느 순간에는 \"아니, 직접 이야기를 한다면 내키지 않아 하는 기색이 보이는 즉시 물러설 수 있을 거야\"라고 생각했다.\n\n그래도 날들은 흘러갔고 편지는 쓰이지 않았으며, 특별한 면담도 청해지지 않았다. 불스트로드에게 의존적인 태도를 취해야 하는 굴욕에서 움츠러들면서, 그는 자신이 기억하는 자기 모습과는 더더욱 동떨어진 또 다른 조치에 상상을 길들이기 시작했다. 그는 종종 자신을 화나게 했던 로저먼드의 그 유치한 발상, 곧 미들마치를 그 서막 너머의 아무것도 보지 못한 채 떠나 버리자는 생각을 실행에 옮길 수 있을지 자발적으로 헤아려 보기 시작했다. 이런 의문이 떠올랐다—\"지금이라도, 이 헐값에라도 누가 내게서 개업권을 사 줄까? 그렇다면 매각은 떠나기 위한 필수적인 준비로서 이루어질 수도 있겠지.\"\n\n하지만 이런 조치를 취하는 것에 대해—그는 여전히 그것을 현재의 일을 천하게 내팽개치는 짓이요, 가치 있는 활동을 위한 진짜 통로이자 넓어질 수도 있는 물길에서 죄스럽게 비켜서는 짓이며, 정당한 목적지도 없이 다시 시작하는 짓이라고 느꼈다—이런 장애가 있었으니, 곧 매수자를 구할 수 있다 해도 빨리 나타나지 않을지도 모른다는 것이었다. 그리고 그다음에는? 가장 큰 도시든 가장 먼 읍이든, 로저먼드가 초라한 셋방에서 산다면 그녀를 음울함에서 구해 주고 그를 그녀를 그 속에 빠뜨렸다는 비난에서 구해 줄 만한 삶을 찾지 못할 것이었다. 사람이 운수의 비탈 밑바닥에 이르면, 직업적 성취가 있더라도 거기에 오래 머물러 있을 수 있기 때문이다. 영국의 풍토에서는 과학적 통찰과 셋방살이 사이에 양립 불가능이란 없다. 양립 불가능은 주로 과학적 야망과, 그런 종류의 거처에 반대하는 아내 사이에 있는 법이다.\n\n그러나 그가 망설이는 와중에, 그를 결단하게 만들 기회가 찾아왔다. 불스트로드 씨가 보낸 쪽지가 리드게이트에게 은행으로 자기를 찾아와 달라고 청했다. 근래 그 은행가의 체질에 건강염려증 경향이 나타나고 있었다. 실은 습관적인 소화불량 증상이 조금 과장된 데 지나지 않는 불면을, 그는 광기가 닥쳐온다는 징조라고 곱씹고 있었다. 그는 전에 했던 이야기 외에 달리 할 말이 없으면서도, 바로 그날 아침에 지체 없이 리드게이트와 상의하고 싶어 했다. 그는 자신의 두려움을 떨쳐 내려는 리드게이트의 말에 열심히 귀를 기울였는데, 이 역시 되풀이에 지나지 않았다. 그리고 불스트로드가 안도감을 느끼며 의학적 소견을 받아들이고 있는 이 순간이, 리드게이트가 미리 헤아렸던 것보다 그에게 개인적 필요를 전하는 일을 더 쉽게 만들어 주는 듯했다. 그는 불스트로드 씨가 사업에 쏟는 주의를 늦추는 편이 좋겠다고 역설하던 참이었다.\n\n\"아무리 가벼운 정신적 긴장도 섬약한 체질에 어떤 영향을 미칠 수 있는지는,\" 진찰이, 개인적인 화제에서 일반적인 화제로 옮겨 가는 단계에 이르렀을 때 리드게이트가 말했다. \"젊고 정력적인 사람에게조차 불안이 한동안 깊은 자국을 새기는 것을 보면 알 수 있지요. 저는 천성이 매우 튼튼합니다. 그런데도 최근 누적된 곤경 탓에 완전히 흔들리고 말았어요.\"\n\n\"제 체질이 지금처럼 민감한 상태에 있다면, 만일 콜레라가 우리 지역을 찾아올 경우 특히나 그 희생자가 되기 쉬우리라 생각됩니다. 런던 부근에 그것이 나타난 마당이니, 우리는 마땅히 보호를 구하며 은혜의 보좌를 둘러싸고 매달려야겠지요.\" 불스트로드 씨가 말했는데, 리드게이트의 암시를 회피하려는 것이 아니라 정말로 자신에 대한 불안에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"어쨌든 선생께서는 이 읍을 위해 훌륭한 실질적 예방책을 강구하는 데 한몫하셨고, 그것이야말로 보호를 구하는 최선의 방법입니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했는데, 그 은행가가 지닌 종교의 뒤죽박죽인 비유와 어설픈 논리에 대한 강한 거부감이, 동정을 호소하는 그의 명백한 귀먹음 탓에 다소 커졌다. 하지만 그의 마음은 도움을 얻는 쪽으로 오래 준비해 온 움직임을 시작했고, 아직 멈추지 않은 상태였다. 그가 덧붙였다. \"이 읍은 청결과 설비 마련 면에서 잘해 왔고, 제 생각엔 만일 콜레라가 닥친다면 우리의 적들조차 병원의 시설이 공공의 이익이라는 점을 인정할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 불스트로드 씨가 다소 냉담하게 말했다. \"제 정신적 노고를 늦추는 일에 관해 선생께서 말씀하시는 바에 대해 말하자면, 저는 얼마 전부터 그 방향으로 작정을 품고 있었습니다—매우 단호한 성격의 작정을요. 저는 자선적인 것이든 상업적인 것이든 많은 사업의 운영에서 적어도 한동안 물러날 생각을 하고 있습니다. 또한 한동안 거처를 옮길 생각도 합니다. 아마도 '관목원'을 닫거나 세를 놓고, 해안 가까운 어딘가에 자리를 잡을 듯합니다—물론 건강에 좋은지에 관한 조언을 따라서요. 그것은 선생께서 권하실 만한 조치겠지요?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요.\" 리드게이트가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했는데, 그 은행가의 창백하고 진지한 눈길과 자기 자신에 대한 강렬한 몰두 앞에서 제대로 억누르지 못한 초조함이 배어 있었다.\n\n\"얼마 전부터 저는 우리 병원과 관련해 이 문제를 선생과 터놓고 이야기해야겠다고 느껴 왔습니다.\" 불스트로드가 말을 이었다. \"제가 말씀드린 사정 아래에서는, 당연히 저는 운영에 개인적으로 관여하는 일을 그만두어야 하고, 제가 감독하고 어느 정도 통제할 수 없는 기관에 막대한 재원을 계속 쏟아붓는 것은 책임에 관한 제 견해에 어긋납니다. 그러므로 만일 제가 끝내 미들마치를 떠나기로 결정한다면, 새 병원에 대해, 제가 건축비를 주로 댔고 그 성공적인 운영을 위해 추가로 거액을 기부했다는 사실에 깃들어 있는 지원 외의 다른 지원은 거두어들이는 것으로 여기겠습니다.\"\n\n불스트로드가 늘 하던 대로 말을 멈추자 리드게이트의 머릿속에 떠오른 생각은 이러했다. \"이 사람, 어쩌면 돈을 꽤 많이 잃은 모양이군.\" 이것이 그의 기대에 사뭇 놀라운 변화를 가져온 그 말에 대한 가장 그럴듯한 설명이었다. 그가 대답했다—\n\n\"병원이 입는 손실은 메우기 어려울 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"어렵지요.\" 불스트로드가 똑같이 신중하고 은쟁반 같은 어조로 대꾸했다. \"계획을 얼마간 바꾸지 않는 한 말입니다. 기꺼이 기부를 늘려 줄 것으로 확실히 기대할 수 있는 유일한 분은 캐소본 부인입니다. 저는 그 문제로 그분과 면담을 가졌고, 지금 선생께 말씀드리려는 것처럼, 제도를 바꾸어 새 병원에 더 폭넓은 지원을 얻는 것이 바람직하다고 그분께 일러 드렸습니다.\" 또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀으나, 리드게이트는 말이 없었다.\n\n\"제가 말하는 변화란 진료소와의 통합인데, 그리하여 새 병원이 같은 운영 이사회를 둔, 더 오래된 기관에 덧붙은 특수 부속물로 여겨지도록 하는 것입니다. 또한 두 곳의 의료 운영이 합쳐질 필요가 있겠지요. 이렇게 하면 우리 새 시설을 충분히 유지하는 데 따르는 어려움이 모두 해소되고, 이 읍의 자선적 관심사가 분열되는 일도 그치게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n불스트로드 씨는 다시 말을 멈추며 리드게이트의 얼굴에서 그의 외투 단추로 시선을 떨구었다.\n\n\"방도와 재원에 관한 한 그것이 좋은 방책이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없습니다.\" 리드게이트가 어조에 빈정거림을 실어 말했다. \"하지만 그 첫 결과 가운데 하나가, 다른 의사들이—그게 그저 제 방식이라는 이유만으로라도—제 방식을 뒤엎거나 방해하리라는 것일 텐데, 그걸 제가 대뜸 기뻐하리라고 기대하실 수는 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 선생, 아시다시피 저 자신도 선생께서 부지런히 활용해 오신 새롭고 독자적인 진료의 기회를 매우 소중히 여겼습니다. 솔직히 말씀드려, 그 본래의 계획은 제가 신의 뜻에 순종하는 가운데 마음 깊이 품어 온 것이었지요. 그러나 섭리의 징조가 제게 단념을 요구하므로, 저는 단념하렵니다.\"\n\n이 대화에서 불스트로드는 다소 사람 속을 끓게 하는 능란함을 보였다. 듣는 이의 경멸을 불러일으켰던 그 뒤죽박죽인 비유와 동기의 어설픈 논리는, 리드게이트가 자신의 분노와 실망을 토로하기 어렵게 만드는 사실 제시 방식과 완벽하게 들어맞았다. 잠시 빠르게 생각을 굴린 뒤, 그는 그저 이렇게 물었다—\n\n\"캐소본 부인은 뭐라고 하시던가요?\"\n\n\"그것이 바로 제가 선생께 덧붙여 말씀드리고 싶었던 바입니다.\" 불스트로드가 말했는데, 그는 자신의 설교조 해명을 빈틈없이 준비해 두고 있었다. \"그분은 선생도 아시다시피 더없이 후덕한 성품을 지닌 여인이며, 다행히도—대단한 부를 가졌다기보다는, 충분히 떼어 쓸 수 있는 자금을 보유하고 계십니다. 그분께서 제게 알리시기를, 그 자금의 대부분을 다른 목적에 쓰기로 정해 두긴 했으나, 병원과 관련해 제 자리를 온전히 대신할 수 있을지 고려해 보겠노라고 하셨습니다. 다만 그 문제에 관해 생각을 무르익힐 충분한 시간을 원하셔서, 저는 서두를 필요가 없다고—실은 제 계획도 아직 확정된 것은 아니라고 말씀드렸습니다.\"\n\n리드게이트는 \"캐소본 부인이 선생 자리를 대신해 주신다면 손실이 아니라 이득이 되겠지요\"라고 말할 참이었다. 하지만 이 명랑한 솔직함을 가로막는 무게가 여전히 그의 마음을 짓누르고 있었다. 그가 대답했다. \"그럼 제가 캐소본 부인과 그 문제를 의논해도 되겠군요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 그것이 바로 그분이 분명히 바라시는 바예요. 그분 말씀으로는, 결정은 선생께서 무엇을 말씀해 주실 수 있느냐에 크게 달려 있다고 합니다. 다만 지금 당장은 아니고요. 그분은 막 여행을 떠나시는 참인 듯합니다. 여기 그분의 편지가 있습니다.\" 불스트로드 씨가 편지를 꺼내 읽으며 말했다. \"'저는 당장 다른 일에 매여 있습니다'라고 쓰셨군요. '저는 제임스 경, 그리고 체텀 부인과 함께 요크셔로 갈 예정인데, 거기서 보게 될 어떤 토지에 관해 제가 내리는 결론이 병원에 기부할 수 있는 제 능력에 영향을 미칠지도 모릅니다.' 그러니 리드게이트 선생, 이 일에는 서두를 필요가 없습니다. 다만 일어날 수도 있는 일을 미리 알려 드리고 싶었던 것이지요.\"\n\n불스트로드 씨는 편지를 옆주머니에 도로 넣고는, 마치 용무가 끝났다는 듯 자세를 바꾸었다. 병원에 관해 되살아난 희망이 도리어 그 희망에 독을 풀어 넣는 사실들을 더 의식하게 만든 리드게이트는, 도움을 얻으려는 노력을 한다면 그것은 지금, 그것도 힘차게 해야만 한다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"충분히 미리 알려 주셔서 대단히 감사합니다.\" 그가 단호한 의도를 어조에 담아, 그러나 마지못해 말하고 있음을 드러내는 끊긴 어투로 말했다. \"제게 가장 높은 목표는 제 직업이고, 저는 병원을 현재 제 직업을 가장 잘 쓸 수 있는 방도와 동일시해 왔습니다. 하지만 가장 잘 쓰는 것이 늘 금전적 성공과 같은 것은 아니지요. 병원을 인기 없게 만든 모든 것이—다른 원인들과 더불어, 제 생각엔 그것들이 다 제 직업적 열의와 연결되어 있는데—저를 인기 없는 개업의로 만드는 데 한몫했습니다. 저를 찾아오는 환자는 주로 진료비를 낼 수 없는 사람들입니다. 제 쪽에서 갚아야 할 사람만 없다면, 저는 그런 환자들이 가장 좋을 텐데요.\" 리드게이트는 잠시 기다렸으나 불스트로드는 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 고개만 까딱일 뿐이었고, 그는 마치 거슬리는 부추를 깨물고 있는 듯이 여전히 끊기는 발음으로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"저는 빠져나갈 길이 도무지 보이지 않는 금전적 곤경에 빠지고 말았는데, 저와 제 장래를 믿어 주는 누군가가 다른 담보 없이 일정한 액수를 선뜻 빌려주지 않는 한 말입니다. 이곳에 왔을 때 제게 남은 재산은 거의 없었습니다. 제 집안으로부터 돈을 받을 가망도 없고요. 결혼 탓에 제 지출은 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 더 커졌습니다. 지금 이 순간의 결과는, 저를 깨끗하게 정리하는 데 천 파운드가 들리라는 것입니다. 다시 말해, 제 가장 큰 빚의 담보로 제 모든 가재도구가 팔려 나갈 위험에서 저를 벗어나게 하고—다른 빚들도 갚고—얼마 안 되는 수입으로 조금이나마 앞서 살아갈 무언가를 남기는 데 말입니다. 제 아내의 아버지가 그런 거금을 빌려준다는 것은 어림없는 일임을 알았습니다. 그래서 제 처지를—제 번영이나 파멸에 어떤 개인적 연관이 있다고 여겨질 만한, 유일하게 남은 또 한 분께 말씀드리는 것입니다.\"\n\n리드게이트는 자기 자신의 말을 듣는 것이 싫었다. 하지만 그는 이제 말을 해 버렸고, 틀림없이 단도직입적으로 말한 것이었다. 불스트로드 씨는 서두르지 않으면서도 망설임 없이 대답했다.\n\n\"솔직히 말씀드려 놀라지는 않았으나, 이 소식에 마음이 아픕니다, 리드게이트 선생. 저 개인으로서는, 선생께서 제 처남 집안과 인연을 맺으신 것을 유감스럽게 여겨 왔습니다. 그 집안은 늘 헤픈 습관을 지녀 왔고, 지금의 지위를 유지하는 데에도 이미 제게 많은 신세를 졌지요. 리드게이트 선생, 제 조언은, 선생께서 더 많은 채무에 얽혀들며 미덥지 못한 분투를 계속하느니 그냥 파산하시는 편이 낫다는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그건 제 전망을 나아지게 하지 못할 겁니다.\" 리드게이트가 일어서며 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"설령 그 자체로는 더 받아들일 만한 일이라 해도 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그것은 언제나 시련이지요.\" 불스트로드 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 시련이란, 선생, 이 세상에서 우리에게 주어진 몫이며, 꼭 필요한 교정 수단입니다. 제가 드린 조언을 잘 헤아려 보시기를 권합니다.\"\n\n\"감사합니다.\" 리드게이트가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 말했다. \"너무 오래 시간을 빼앗았군요. 안녕히 계십시오.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LXVIII.\n\n악덕이 그만큼 좋은 옷을 걸치고 그만큼 잘 처신한다면?\n그릇됨이, 간계가, 무분별이\n\n똑같이 그럴듯한 역할을 칭송받을 만한 목적으로 연기한다면?\n이 모든 사건의 거대한 책,\n\n세계, 행위의 보편적 지도가\n이 모든 것을 강력히 다스리며, 모든 내력으로부터 증명하나니,\n\n가장 곧은 길이 언제나 가장 잘 성공한다는 것을.\n세상의 모든 눈으로 바라보고,\n\n모든 시대와 더불어 슬기를 나누는\n저 진중하고 학식 있는 경험이,\n\n길잡이 없는 기만보다 더 안전히 나아가지 않을 수 있겠는가!\n—대니얼, 『무소필루스』.\n\n불스트로드가 리드게이트와 나눈 대화에서 드러냈거나 무심코 누설했던 그 계획의 변경과 관심의 이동은, 라처 씨의 경매가 있던 시기 이래로 그가 겪어 온 어떤 가혹한 경험에 의해 그의 마음속에서 결정된 것이었다. 그때 래플스가 윌 래디슬로를 알아보았고, 은행가는 고통스러운 결과를 막도록 신의 섭리를 움직일 수 있을지 모를 보상의 행위를 헛되이 시도했던 것이다.\n죽지 않았다면 래플스가 머지않아 미들마치로 돌아오리라던 그의 확신은 사실로 입증되었다. 크리스마스이브에 그는 더 슈럽스에 다시 나타났다. 불스트로드는 그를 맞이하고 그가 나머지 가족과 접촉하는 것을 막으려고 집에 있었지만, 그 방문의 정황이 자신을 위태롭게 하고 아내를 놀라게 하는 것까지 완전히 막을 수는 없었다. 래플스는 이전에 나타났을 때보다 더 다루기 힘든 모습을 보였으니, 그의 만성적인 정신적 불안정과 습관적인 무절제가 점점 더 깊이 미친 영향 탓에, 그에게 무슨 말을 하든 그 인상이 금세 그에게서 떨어져 나갔다. 그는 집에 머물겠다고 고집했고, 불스트로드는 두 가지 해악을 저울질해 보고는 이것이 적어도 그가 시내로 들어가는 것보다 더 나쁜 대안은 아니라고 느꼈다. 그는 저녁 내내 래플스를 자기 방에 붙들어 두고 잠자리에 들 때까지 지켜보았는데, 그동안 래플스는 점잖고 대단히 번창한 동료 죄인에게 폐를 끼치는 것을 즐기면서, 그 즐거움을 자기에게 도움을 주었던, 그리고 마땅히 받을 몫을 다 받지 못했던 사람을 대접하는 친구의 기쁨에 대한 공감이라며 익살스럽게 표현했다. 이 시끄러운 농담 아래에는 교활한 계산이 깔려 있었으니—이 새로운 고문에서 풀려나는 대가로 불스트로드에게서 무언가 더 두둑한 것을 뽑아내려는 냉정한 결심이었다. 그러나 그의 교활함은 그 표적을 조금 빗나가게 만들었다.\n\n불스트로드는 사실 래플스의 거친 감성이 상상할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 고통을 받고 있었다. 그는 아내에게, 자신은 그저 이 가련한 자, 악덕의 희생자를 보살피고 있을 뿐이며, 그러지 않으면 그가 스스로를 해칠 수도 있다고 말했다. 그는 직접적인 거짓말의 형태를 취하지는 않으면서, 자신을 이 보살핌으로 묶어 두는 어떤 가족적 인연이 있으며, 래플스에게 조심을 요하는 정신 착란의 징후가 보인다는 것을 넌지시 암시했다. 다음 날 아침이면 자신이 직접 그 불운한 자를 멀리 데려다줄 것이라고 했다. 이런 암시 속에서 그는 불스트로드 부인에게 딸들과 하인들을 위한 예방적 정보를 주고 있으며, 음식과 음료를 가지고 가더라도 자신 외에는 아무도 그 방에 들어가지 못하게 하는 이유를 설명하고 있다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그는 래플스가 과거의 사실을 큰 소리로 노골적으로 언급하는 것을 누가 엿들을까 봐—불스트로드 부인이 행여 문 앞에서 엿듣고 싶은 유혹에 빠질까 봐 두려움에 사로잡혀 앉아 있었다. 어떻게 그녀를 막을 수 있단 말인가, 어떻게 그녀를 적발하려고 문을 열어 자신의 공포를 드러낼 수 있단 말인가? 그녀는 정직하고 솔직한 습관을 지닌 여인이었고, 고통스러운 사실을 알아내려고 그토록 비천한 방법을 취할 가능성은 거의 없었지만, 두려움은 개연성의 계산보다 더 강했다.\n이런 식으로 래플스는 고문을 너무 멀리까지 밀어붙였고, 자기 계획에 없던 효과를 만들어 냈다. 자신이 도저히 다룰 수 없는 자임을 드러냄으로써, 그는 불스트로드로 하여금 강력한 저항만이 유일하게 남은 방책이라고 느끼게 만들었다. 그날 밤 래플스를 잠자리에 들게 한 뒤, 은행가는 다음 날 아침 일곱 시 반에 자신의 사륜마차를 준비해 두라고 지시했다. 여섯 시에 그는 이미 오래전에 옷을 다 차려입고, 비참함의 일부를 기도로 풀어내며, 신 앞에서 무엇인가에 거짓을 쓰고 사실이 아닌 것을 말했다면 그것은 최악의 해악을 막기 위함이었다고 자신의 동기를 호소했다. 불스트로드는 더 간접적인 비행의 수에 비하면 어울리지 않을 만큼 강렬하게 직접적인 거짓말 앞에서 움츠러들었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 비행들 가운데 많은 것은 의식 속에서는 헤아려지지 않으면서도 우리가 마음을 두고 바라는 목적을 이루어 내는, 저 미묘한 근육의 움직임과도 같았다. 그리고 우리가 전지자의 눈에 보인다고 생생히 상상할 수 있는 것은 오직 우리가 생생히 의식하는 것뿐이다.\n\n불스트로드는 촛불을 들고 래플스의 침대 곁으로 갔는데, 그는 분명 괴로운 꿈을 꾸고 있는 듯했다. 그는 너무 갑작스러운 깨어남의 결과로 어떤 소음이 일어날까 두려웠던 터라, 불빛의 존재가 잠든 자를 서서히 부드럽게 깨우는 데 도움이 되기를 바라며 말없이 서 있었다. 그가 깨어남으로 끝날 듯한 그 떨림과 헐떡임을 이삼 분쯤 지켜보았을 때, 래플스가 반쯤 억눌린 긴 신음과 함께 벌떡 일어나 공포에 질려 주위를 둘러보며 떨고 헐떡였다. 그러나 그는 더 이상 소리를 내지 않았고, 불스트로드는 촛불을 내려놓고 그가 진정되기를 기다렸다.\n\n십오 분쯤 지난 뒤에야 불스트로드는 전에는 보인 적 없는 차갑고 단호한 태도로 입을 열었다. \"이렇게 일찍 깨우러 온 것은, 래플스 씨, 마차를 일곱 시 반에 준비해 두라고 일러 두었기 때문이오. 내가 직접 일슬리까지 당신을 데려다줄 작정이오. 거기서 철도를 타든 역마차를 기다리든 하면 되오.\" 래플스가 막 말을 하려는데, 불스트로드가 위압적으로 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"조용히 하시오, 여보시오. 그리고 내가 할 말을 들으시오. 지금 돈을 주겠소. 그리고 당신이 편지로 내게 청한다면 때때로 적당한 액수를 보내 주겠소. 그러나 만약 당신이 다시 여기에 모습을 드러내겠다면, 만약 미들마치로 돌아온다면, 만약 나를 해치는 식으로 혀를 놀린다면, 당신은 나의 도움 없이 당신의 악의가 가져다줄 수 있는 그런 열매로 살아가야 할 것이오. 아무도 내 이름을 더럽힌 대가로 당신에게 두둑이 지불하지 않을 것이오. 당신이 나를 해코지해 할 수 있는 최악의 것을 나는 알고 있고, 당신이 감히 다시 내게 들이닥친다면 나는 그것에 맞설 것이오. 일어나시오, 여보시오. 그리고 소란 피우지 말고 내가 명하는 대로 하시오. 그러지 않으면 경관을 불러 당신을 내 집에서 끌어내게 하겠소. 그러면 당신은 시내의 모든 선술집에 당신 이야기를 떠들고 다녀도 좋지만, 그곳에서 쓸 경비로 나한테서는 단 한 푼도 받지 못할 것이오.\"\n불스트로드가 평생에 이토록 신경을 곤두세운 격렬함으로 말한 적은 거의 없었다. 그는 밤의 상당 부분을 이 연설과 그 있을 법한 효과에 대해 곰곰이 따져 보았다. 그리고 그것이 끝내 래플스의 어떤 재방문으로부터도 자신을 구해 주리라고 믿지는 않았지만, 그것이 자신이 던질 수 있는 최선의 패라고 결론지었다. 그것은 오늘 아침 지친 사내에게서 복종을 끌어내는 데 성공했다. 그 순간 독에 찌든 그의 몸은 불스트로드의 차갑고 단호한 태도 앞에서 위축되었고, 그는 가족의 아침 식사 시간 전에 조용히 마차에 실려 떠났다. 하인들은 그를 가난한 친척쯤으로 여겼고, 세상에서 고개를 높이 들고 사는 자기네 주인 같은 엄격한 사람이 그런 사촌을 부끄러워하며 떼어 버리고 싶어 하는 것을 이상하게 여기지 않았다. 그 미운 동행자와 함께한 은행가의 십 마일 마차 길은 크리스마스 날의 음울한 시작이었다. 그러나 길의 끝에 이르렀을 때 래플스는 기운을 되찾았고, 흡족한 기분으로 작별했는데, 거기에는 은행가가 그에게 백 파운드를 준 충분한 이유가 있었다. 여러 가지 동기가 불스트로드를 이런 후한 베풂으로 몰아갔으나, 그 자신은 그 모든 동기를 면밀히 캐묻지 않았다. 래플스가 불안한 잠에 빠진 모습을 지켜보고 서 있을 때, 처음 이백 파운드를 준 이후로 그 사내가 무척 망가졌다는 생각이 그의 마음에 분명 떠올랐던 것이다.\n\n그는 더 이상 농락당하지 않겠다는 자신의 결심을 날카롭게 거듭 천명하는 데 신경을 썼고, 그를 매수하는 위험이 그에게 맞서는 위험과 똑같다는 것을 자신이 보여 주었다는 사실을 래플스에게 각인시키려 했다. 그러나 그 혐오스러운 존재에게서 풀려나 조용한 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 불스트로드는 자신이 한낱 유예 이상의 무엇을 확보했다는 자신감을 가지고 오지 못했다. 마치 역겨운 꿈을 꾸고서 그 미운 감각의 무리와 함께 그 영상들을 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 것 같았고—마치 자기 삶의 그 모든 즐거운 주변에 위험한 파충류가 끈적끈적한 흔적을 남기고 간 것 같았다.\n자신의 가장 내밀한 삶이 다른 사람들이 자기에 대해 품고 있으리라 믿는 생각들로 얼마나 많이 이루어져 있는지, 그 의견의 직물이 파멸의 위협을 받기 전까지 누가 알 수 있겠는가?\n\n불스트로드는 아내의 마음속에 불안한 예감의 침전물이 있다는 것을 더욱 절실히 의식하게 되었으니, 그녀가 그것에 대한 어떤 언급도 조심스럽게 피했기 때문이다. 그는 날마다 우월함의 맛과 완전한 공경의 헌사를 음미하는 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그런데 자신이 어떤 떳떳지 못한 비밀을 가지고 있다는 숨은 의심을 품은 채 누군가에게 감시당하거나 가늠당하고 있다는 확신은, 교화의 말을 할 때 그의 목소리를 떨리게 만들었다. 불스트로드처럼 불안한 기질을 지닌 사람들에게는 예견하는 것이 흔히 직접 보는 것보다 더 나쁜 법이다. 그의 상상력은 임박한 치욕의 고뇌를 끊임없이 키웠다. 그렇다, 임박했다. 래플스에 대한 그의 저항이 그 사내를 멀리 떼어 놓지 못한다면—그는 그런 결과를 기도했지만 거의 바라지는 못했으니—치욕은 확실했다. 만약 그것이 허락된다면 그것은 신의 징벌, 응징, 준비라고 스스로에게 말해 보았으나 헛일이었다. 그는 상상 속의 그 불길로부터 움츠러들었고, 자신이 치욕을 피하는 편이 신의 영광을 위해 더 나으리라고 판단했다. 그 움츠러듦이 마침내 그로 하여금 미들마치를 떠날 준비를 하도록 몰아갔다. 만약 자신에 관한 악한 진실이 폭로되어야만 한다면, 그때 그는 오랜 이웃들의 경멸로부터 덜 살을 태우는 거리에 있게 될 것이다. 그리고 자기 삶이 같은 광범위한 민감성을 쌓지 못한 새로운 무대에서라면, 그 고문자가 자신을 쫓아온다 해도 덜 무서울 것이었다. 이곳을 영영 떠나는 것이 아내에게 극심한 고통이 되리라는 것을 그는 알았고, 다른 이유들로도 자신이 뿌리내린 이곳에 머무는 편을 더 좋아했을 것이다. 그래서 그는 처음에는 조건부의 방식으로 준비를 했으니, 섭리의 어떤 호의적인 개입이 그의 두려움을 흩어 준다면 잠시 자리를 비운 뒤 돌아올 여지를 사방에 남겨 두고자 했다. 그는 건강이 쇠약해졌다는 이유로 은행 경영을 넘기고 인근의 다른 상업적 일에 대한 어떤 적극적 통제도 포기할 준비를 했지만, 장차 그런 일을 다시 맡을 가능성을 배제하지는 않았다. 그 조치는 그에게 이미 전반적인 거래의 침체로 겪은 것을 넘어선 약간의 추가 비용과 약간의 수입 감소를 가져올 것이었다. 그리고 병원이 그가 정당하게 절약할 수 있는 주된 지출 대상으로 떠올랐다.\n\n이것이 그가 리드게이트와 나눈 대화를 결정한 경험이었다. 그러나 이 시점에 그의 준비는 대부분 불필요한 것으로 드러날 경우 철회할 수 있는 단계를 넘어서지 않았다. 그는 마지막 조치를 끊임없이 미루었다. 두려움 한가운데서 그는, 난파의 위험에 처했거나 날뛰는 말들에 의해 마차에서 내동댕이쳐질 위험에 처한 많은 사람들처럼, 무언가가 일어나 최악을 막아 주리라는, 그리고 뒤늦은 이식으로 자기 삶을 망치는 것은 지나치게 성급한 일일지 모른다는 끈질긴 인상에 매달렸다—더구나 아내가 살고 싶어 하는 유일한 곳에서 무기한 추방되는 계획을 그녀에게 만족스럽게 설명하기란 어려운 일이었으니 더욱 그러했다.\n\n불스트로드가 처리해야 할 일들 가운데에는 자신이 부재할 경우 스톤 코트 농장을 관리하는 문제가 있었다. 그리고 이 일에 관해서는 물론, 그가 미들마치 안팎에 소유한 가옥과 토지에 관련된 다른 모든 문제에 관해서도, 그는 케일럽 가스와 상의했다. 그런 종류의 일이 있는 다른 모든 사람과 마찬가지로, 그는 자기 자신의 이익보다 고용주의 이익을 더 염려하는 대리인을 얻고 싶었다. 스톤 코트에 관해서는, 불스트로드가 가축에 대한 자기 소유권을 유지하고 싶어 했고, 또 원한다면 자신이 좋아하는 감독이라는 소일거리를 다시 맡을 수 있는 방식을 마련하고 싶어 했으므로, 케일럽은 단순한 관리인에게 맡기지 말고 토지와 가축과 농기구를 연 단위로 임대하여 수익의 비례 몫을 받으라고 그에게 조언했다.\n\"이런 조건으로 소작인을 찾는 일을 당신에게 맡겨도 되겠소, 가스 씨?\" 불스트로드가 말했다. \"그리고 우리가 함께 논의한 이 일들을 관리하는 데 대한 보수로 연간 얼마를 드리면 되는지 말씀해 주시겠소?\"\n\n\"생각해 보겠습니다.\" 케일럽이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"어떻게 셈을 맞출 수 있을지 알아보지요.\"\n프레드 빈시의 장래를 고려해야 하는 처지만 아니었다면, 가스 씨는 아마 일이 더 늘어나는 것을 반기지 않았을 것이다. 그의 아내는 그가 나이를 먹어 감에 따라 일을 지나치게 떠맡지나 않을까 늘 걱정했으니 말이다. 그러나 그 대화를 마치고 불스트로드와 헤어진 뒤, 그 스톤 코트의 임대라는 것에 관해 매우 솔깃한 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다. 만약 불스트로드가 케일럽 가스 자신이 관리를 책임진다는 조건으로 프레드 빈시를 그곳에 들이는 데 동의해 준다면 어떨까? 그것은 프레드에게 훌륭한 수업이 될 터였다. 그는 거기서 변변한 수입을 올리면서도 다른 사업을 거들며 지식을 얻을 시간을 여전히 남겨 둘 수 있을 것이었다. 그가 너무도 기뻐하며 이 생각을 가스 부인에게 이야기했기에, 그녀는 그가 일을 지나치게 떠맡는 것에 대한 자신의 변함없는 두려움을 표현하여 그의 기쁨에 찬물을 끼얹을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"그 녀석은 더없이 기뻐할 거요.\" 그가 의자에 등을 기대고 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 말했다. \"다 정해졌다고 말해 줄 수만 있다면 말이오. 생각해 봐요, 수전! 페더스톤 영감이 죽기 여러 해 전부터 그 애 마음은 그 자리에 가 있었지. 그리고 결국 그 애가 사업에 뛰어들어 부지런하고 착실하게 그곳을 지킨다면, 그보다 더 멋진 일의 전환이 있을 수 없을 거요. 불스트로드가 그 애를 계속하게 두고서 점차 가축을 사들이게 할 가능성도 충분하니까. 그가 다른 어딘가에 영구히 정착할지 어떨지는 아직 마음을 정하지 못한 것 같소. 내 평생 이만큼 마음에 드는 생각은 없었소. 그러면 머지않아 아이들이 결혼할 수도 있겠지, 수전.\"\n\n\"불스트로드가 그 계획에 동의하리라는 확신이 서기 전까지는, 프레드에게 그 계획에 대해 어떤 낌새도 비치지 않으시겠지요?\" 가스 부인이 부드럽게 조심스러운 어조로 말했다. \"그리고 결혼에 관해서라면, 케일럽, 우리 늙은 사람들이 그것을 서두르도록 거들 필요는 없어요.\"\n\n\"오, 글쎄 어떨지.\" 케일럽이 고개를 옆으로 갸웃하며 말했다. \"결혼이란 사람을 길들이는 거요. 프레드는 내 재갈과 고삐가 덜 필요해질 거고. 어쨌든 내가 디디고 선 땅을 알기 전까지는 아무 말도 안 하겠소. 불스트로드와 다시 이야기해 보지.\"\n그는 그렇게 할 가장 이른 기회를 잡았다. 불스트로드는 조카 프레드 빈시에게 따뜻한 관심이라고는 도무지 없었지만, 더 양심적이지 못한 관리에 맡겼다가는 분명 상당한 손해를 볼 여러 산재한 사업 문제에서 가스 씨의 도움을 확보하고 싶은 강한 바람이 있었다. 그런 이유로 그는 가스 씨의 제안에 이의를 달지 않았다. 그리고 빈시 집안 사람 가운데 하나에게 이익이 될 동의를 해 주는 것을 그가 유감스러워하지 않은 또 다른 이유도 있었다. 그것은 불스트로드 부인이 리드게이트의 빚에 관해 듣고서 남편이 가엾은 로저먼드를 위해 무언가 해 줄 수 없는지 알고 싶어 애를 태웠고, 리드게이트의 형편이 쉽게 구제될 수 없으며 가장 현명한 방책은 일을 \"되어 가는 대로 내버려 두는\" 것이라는 말을 남편에게 듣고 무척 괴로워했기 때문이었다. 그때 불스트로드 부인은 처음으로 이렇게 말했다. \"당신은 늘 제 식구들에게 좀 모질게 구는 것 같아요, 니컬러스. 그리고 저는 제 친척 누구도 부정할 이유가 없다고 분명히 말하겠어요. 그들이 너무 세속적일지는 몰라도, 그들이 존경받을 만하지 않다고 말한 사람은 아무도 없었어요.\"\n\n\"여보, 해리엇.\" 불스트로드 씨는 눈물이 차오르는 아내의 눈길에 움찔하며 말했다. \"나는 당신 오라버니에게 막대한 자본을 대 주었소. 내가 그의 결혼한 자식들까지 돌봐 줄 거라고 기대해서는 안 되오.\"\n\n그것은 사실인 듯했고, 불스트로드 부인의 항변은 가엾은 로저먼드에 대한 연민으로 가라앉았다. 그녀는 그 애의 사치스러운 교육이 맺을 열매를 늘 예견해 왔던 터였다.\n\n그러나 그 대화를 떠올리며, 불스트로드 씨는 미들마치를 떠나려는 자신의 계획에 대해 아내와 충분히 이야기해야 할 때, 그녀의 조카 프레드에게 이로울 수도 있는 어떤 조치를 마련했다고 그녀에게 말할 수 있어 다행이라고 느꼈다. 지금으로서는 더 슈럽스를 몇 달간 닫아 두고 남부 해안에 집을 한 채 얻을까 생각 중이라고만 그녀에게 말해 둔 터였다.\n\n그리하여 가스 씨는 자신이 바라던 확약을 얻었으니, 곧 불스트로드가 무기한 미들마치를 떠날 경우 프레드 빈시가 제안된 조건으로 스톤 코트의 소작권을 가질 수 있도록 허락한다는 것이었다.\n\n케일럽은 일이 이렇게 \"깔끔한 전환\"을 맞으리라는 희망에 너무도 들떠서, 만약 애정 어린 아내의 약간의 잔소리로 자제력이 다잡아지지 않았더라면, \"그 애에게 위안을 주려고\" 메리에게 모든 것을 누설하고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 자신을 억눌렀고, 토지와 가축의 상태를 더 철저히 살펴보고 예비적인 견적을 내기 위해 스톤 코트를 몇 차례 다녀온 일을 프레드에게는 엄격히 비밀로 부쳤다. 그가 이런 방문에 들인 열의는 분명 사태가 진행될 만한 속도가 요구하는 것보다 더 컸다. 그러나 그는 프레드와 메리를 위해 감춰 둔 생일 선물처럼 간직하고 있던 이 한 조각의 있을 법한 행복으로 마음을 채우는 데서 오는 아버지다운 기쁨에 자극받았던 것이다.\n\n\"하지만 그 계획 전체가 한낱 뜬구름 잡는 일로 드러나면 어쩌지요?\" 가스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 괜찮소.\" 케일럽이 대답했다. \"그 누각이 무너진들 누구 머리 위로 떨어질 것도 아니니까.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LXIX.\n\n“네가 한 마디를 들었거든, 그것이 너와 함께 사라지게 하라.”\n— 「집회서」.\n\n벌스트로드 씨는 바로 그날 — 리드게이트를 그곳에서 맞이했던 그날 — 오후 세 시쯤, 여전히 은행의 지배인 사무실에 앉아 있었는데, 그때 서기가 들어와 말이 기다리고 있다는 것과 가스 씨가 밖에서 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다는 것을 알렸다.\n\n“얼마든지,” 벌스트로드가 말했다. 그러자 케일럽이 들어왔다. “부디 앉으시지요, 가스 씨,” 은행가는 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말을 이었다.\n\n“마침 제때 도착하셔서 저를 여기에서 만나시게 되니 다행입니다. 시간을 분 단위로 아끼시는 분이라는 걸 압니다.”\n\n“아,” 케일럽이 부드럽게 말하며 고개를 한쪽으로 천천히 기울이고는, 자리에 앉아 모자를 바닥에 내려놓았다.\n\n그는 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채 긴 손가락을 두 다리 사이로 늘어뜨리고 바닥을 바라보았는데, 손가락 하나하나가 차례로 움직이는 모습이, 마치 그의 넓고 고요한 이마를 채운 어떤 생각을 함께 나누고 있는 듯했다.\n\n케일럽을 아는 다른 모든 사람처럼, 벌스트로드 씨도 그가 중요하다고 느끼는 어떤 주제든 말을 꺼낼 때면 늘 더디다는 것을 알고 있었기에, 그가 다시 블라인드맨스 코트의 집 몇 채를 사들이는 이야기를 꺼내려는 거라고 짐작했다. 그 집들을 헐어버리려고 사들이자는 것이었으니, 그 자리에 공기와 빛이 흘러들어옴으로써 충분히 보상받을, 재산의 희생인 셈이었다. 케일럽은 때때로 이런 종류의 제안으로 고용주들을 곤란하게 했지만, 그는 대개 벌스트로드가 개선 사업이라면 기꺼이 응하는 것을 보았고, 둘은 사이좋게 일해 왔다. 그러나 그가 다시 입을 열었을 때, 그것은 다소 가라앉은 목소리로 한 말이었다.\n\n“방금 스톤 코트에서 오는 길입니다, 벌스트로드 씨.”\n\n“거기서 별일 없으셨기를 바랍니다,” 은행가가 말했다. “어제는 제가 직접 다녀왔지요. 에이블이 올해 새끼 양들을 잘 길렀더군요.”\n\n“글쎄요, 그렇긴 합니다,” 케일럽이 진지하게 고개를 들며 말했다. “별일이 좀 있습니다 — 어떤 낯선 사람이 있는데, 제 생각엔 몹시 아픈 것 같습니다. 의사가 필요해서요, 그걸 알려드리러 왔습니다. 그 사람 이름은 래플스입니다.”\n\n그는 자기 말이 벌스트로드의 온몸을 관통하는 충격을 보았다. 이 문제에 관해서라면 은행가는 자신의 두려움이 늘 빈틈없이 경계하고 있어서 불시에 당할 일은 없으리라 여겼지만, 그것은 착각이었다.\n\n“가엾은 친구!” 그가 동정 어린 어조로 말했으나, 입술은 조금 떨렸다. “그가 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지 아십니까?”\n\n“제가 직접 데려왔습니다,” 케일럽이 조용히 말했다. “제 이륜마차에 태웠지요. 그가 역마차에서 내려서 통행료 징수소 모퉁이를 지나 조금 걸어가고 있었는데, 제가 따라잡았습니다. 그가 예전에 한 번 스톤 코트에서 당신과 함께 있는 저를 본 걸 기억하고는, 태워달라고 청하더군요. 보니 아픈 사람이었습니다. 그를 지붕 아래로 데려가는 것이 옳은 일이라고 여겨졌지요. 그러니 이제 지체 없이 그를 위해 의사의 진찰을 받게 하셔야 할 것 같습니다.” 케일럽은 말을 마치며 바닥에서 모자를 집어 들고, 천천히 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n“물론이지요,” 이 순간 머리가 매우 분주하게 돌아가던 벌스트로드가 말했다. “가스 씨, 어쩌면 지나가시는 길에 리드게이트 씨 댁에 들러 주신다면 제게 큰 도움이 되겠습니다 — 아니, 잠깐만요! 이 시간이면 아마 그는 병원에 있을 겁니다. 우선 지금 즉시 말을 타고 가는 제 하인 편에 쪽지를 보내고, 그런 다음 제가 직접 스톤 코트로 가겠습니다.”\n\n벌스트로드는 재빨리 쪽지를 써서, 손수 밖으로 나가 하인에게 심부름을 시켰다. 그가 돌아왔을 때 케일럽은 여전히 한 손은 의자 등받이에, 다른 손으로는 모자를 든 채 아까처럼 서 있었다. 벌스트로드의 마음속에서 가장 큰 생각은 이러했다. “어쩌면 래플스는 가스에게 자기 병에 대해서만 말했을지도 모른다. 가스는, 분명 전에도 그랬겠지만, 이 추잡한 작자가 나와 친분이 있는 척하는 것을 의아해할 테지. 하지만 그는 아무것도 모를 것이다. 게다가 그는 내게 우호적이지 않은가 — 나는 그에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 수 있다.”\n\n그는 이 희망적인 추측을 확증해 줄 무언가를 간절히 바랐지만, 래플스가 무슨 말을 했고 무슨 짓을 했는지 묻는다면 그것은 곧 두려움을 드러내는 일이 될 터였다.\n\n“대단히 감사합니다, 가스 씨,” 그가 평소의 정중한 어조로 말했다. “제 하인이 몇 분 안에 돌아올 테고, 그러면 제가 직접 가서 이 불운한 사람을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 살펴보겠습니다. 혹시 제게 다른 볼일이 있으셨습니까? 그러시다면, 부디 앉으시지요.”\n\n“고맙습니다,” 케일럽이 오른손으로 그 권유를 사양하는 가벼운 몸짓을 하며 말했다. “벌스트로드 씨, 드릴 말씀이 있습니다. 제 일을 저 말고 다른 사람의 손에 맡기시기를 청해야겠습니다. 스톤 코트 임대 건이며 그 밖의 모든 일에서 저를 후하게 대해 주신 점은 감사하게 여깁니다. 하지만 저는 그 일을 그만두어야겠습니다.” 날카로운 확신이 칼날처럼 벌스트로드의 영혼을 찔러 들어왔다.\n\n“이거 너무 갑작스럽군요, 가스 씨,” 그가 처음에 할 수 있었던 말은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n“그렇습니다,” 케일럽이 말했다. “하지만 이미 굳게 정한 일입니다. 저는 그만두어야겠습니다.”\n\n그는 매우 온화하면서도 단호하게 말했지만, 벌스트로드가 그 온화함 앞에서 움츠러드는 듯한 모습을, 그의 얼굴이 메마르게 보이고 자신에게 머무는 시선을 피해 두 눈을 돌리는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 케일럽은 그에게 깊은 연민을 느꼈으나, 자신의 결심을 변명할 어떤 핑계도 — 설령 그것이 무슨 소용이 있다 한들 — 댈 수는 없었다.\n\n“제 짐작에는, 저 불행한 작자가 저에 관해 늘어놓은 어떤 중상모략 때문에 이런 결심을 하게 되신 듯합니다만,” 이제 가장 나쁜 사실까지 알고 싶어진 벌스트로드가 말했다.\n\n“사실입니다. 그에게서 들은 것에 따라 행동한다는 걸 부인하지 않겠습니다.”\n\n“가스 씨, 당신은 양심적인 사람입니다 — 제가 믿건대, 하느님 앞에 책임을 진다고 느끼는 사람이지요. 중상모략을 너무 쉽사리 믿어 저를 해치고 싶지는 않으실 겁니다,” 벌스트로드는 듣는 이의 마음에 맞을 만한 호소거리를 더듬어 찾으며 말했다. “그것은, 제가 감히 말하건대 서로에게 유익할 거래를 그만둘 빈약한 이유입니다.”\n\n“저는 할 수만 있다면 누구도 해치고 싶지 않습니다,” 케일럽이 말했다. “설령 하느님이 눈감아 주신다고 여겨진다 해도요. 제 동료 인간에 대한 마음은 지니고 싶습니다. 하지만, 선생 — 저는 이 래플스가 제게 진실을 말했다고 믿지 않을 수 없습니다. 그리고 당신과 함께 일하거나 당신 덕에 이득을 보면서는 마음 편할 수가 없습니다. 마음이 괴롭습니다. 부디 다른 대리인을 구하시기를 청합니다.”\n\n“좋습니다, 가스 씨. 하지만 적어도 그가 당신에게 한 가장 나쁜 말이 무엇인지는 알아야겠습니다. 제가 희생자가 될 수도 있는 그 추악한 험담이 무엇인지 알아야겠습니다,” 벌스트로드가 말했는데, 자기에게 이익을 베풀던 자리를 마다하는 이 조용한 사내 앞에서 느끼는 굴욕에 어느 정도의 분노가 섞이기 시작했다.\n\n“그러실 필요 없습니다,” 케일럽이 손을 내저으며, 고개를 살짝 숙이고는, 이 가련한 사내를 아껴 주려는 자비로운 뜻이 담긴 그 어조에서 조금도 벗어나지 않은 채 말했다. “그가 제게 한 말은, 지금 알지 못하는 어떤 일이 그것을 제 입에서 강제로 끌어내지 않는 한, 결코 제 입 밖으로 나오지 않을 겁니다. 만일 당신이 이익을 위해 남을 해치는 삶을 살았고, 더 많은 것을 차지하려고 속임수로 남의 권리를 빼앗았다면, 감히 말하건대 후회하고 계실 겁니다 — 돌이키고 싶지만 그럴 수 없으시겠지요. 그건 분명 쓰라린 일일 겁니다” — 케일럽은 잠시 말을 멈추고 고개를 가로저었다 — “제가 당신의 삶을 더 힘들게 만들 일은 아닙니다.”\n\n“하지만 당신은 그러고 있습니다 — 당신은 내 삶을 더 힘들게 만들고 있어요,” 벌스트로드가 진심 어린 애원의 외침을 어쩔 수 없이 토해내며 말했다. “당신은 내게 등을 돌림으로써 내 삶을 더 힘들게 하고 있습니다.”\n\n“그건 제가 어쩔 수 없이 하는 일입니다,” 케일럽이 손을 들어 올리며, 한층 더 부드럽게 말했다. “죄송합니다. 저는 당신을 심판하여, 저자는 악하고 나는 의롭다고 말하지 않습니다. 하느님께서 그런 일은 막아 주시기를. 저는 모든 것을 알지 못합니다. 사람이 그릇된 일을 저질러도 그 의지는 그 죄에서 맑게 솟아오를 수 있습니다, 비록 자기 삶을 깨끗이 추스를 수는 없을지라도요. 그건 가혹한 벌이지요. 만일 당신이 그런 처지라면 — 글쎄요, 저는 당신이 무척 안됐습니다. 하지만 제 안에는, 당신과 함께 일을 계속할 수 없다는 그런 느낌이 있습니다. 그게 전부입니다, 벌스트로드 씨. 그 밖의 모든 것은, 제 의지가 닿는 한 묻어 두겠습니다. 그럼 안녕히 계십시오.”\n\n“잠깐만요, 가스 씨!” 벌스트로드가 황급히 말했다. “그렇다면 당신의 엄숙한 다짐을 믿어도 되겠지요, 즉 — 설령 그 안에 어느 정도 진실이 담겨 있다 해도 — 어쨌든 악의적인 진술인 그것을, 남자든 여자든 누구에게도 되풀이하지 않으리라는 것을요?” 케일럽의 분노가 일었고, 그는 화를 내며 말했다.\n\n“제가 그럴 뜻이 없었다면 무엇 하러 그 말을 했겠습니까? 저는 당신이 두렵지 않습니다. 그런 따위의 이야기는 결코 제 혀를 유혹하지 못할 겁니다.”\n\n“미안합니다 — 제가 흥분했군요 — 저는 이 타락한 작자의 희생자입니다.”\n\n“잠깐 멈추시지요! 당신이 그의 악행으로 이득을 보았을 때, 당신이 그를 더 나쁘게 만드는 데 한몫하지 않았는지를 따져 보셔야 할 겁니다.”\n\n“당신은 그를 너무 쉽게 믿어 저를 부당하게 대하고 있습니다,” 벌스트로드가 말했는데, 래플스가 했을지도 모를 말을 단호히 부인할 수 없다는 무력감에 악몽에 짓눌리듯 짓눌리면서도, 케일럽이 그 말을 자기에게 들이대어 단호한 부인을 요구하는 식으로 진술하지는 않았다는 점에서 한 줄기 도피처를 느끼고 있었다.\n\n“아닙니다,” 케일럽이 만류하듯 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. “저는 더 나은 사실이 입증되면 기꺼이 더 좋게 믿을 사람입니다. 저는 당신에게서 그 어떤 좋은 기회도 앗아 가지 않습니다. 말을 옮기는 것에 관해서라면, 무고한 이를 구하기 위해 꼭 해야 한다는 확신이 서지 않는 한, 남의 죄를 들추는 것을 저는 죄악으로 여깁니다. 그게 제 생각입니다, 벌스트로드 씨. 그리고 제가 한 말은 굳이 맹세할 필요도 없습니다. 그럼 안녕히 계십시오.”\n\n몇 시간 뒤, 집에 돌아온 케일럽은 아내에게 지나가는 말로, 벌스트로드와 약간의 의견 차이가 있었으며, 그 결과 스톤 코트를 맡으려던 생각을 모두 접었고, 실은 그를 위해 더는 일을 하지 않기로 그만두었다고 말했다.\n\n“그가 너무 간섭하려 들던가요?” 가스 부인이 말했는데, 남편이 그의 예민한 부분을 건드려서, 일감과 작업 방식에 관해 옳다고 여기는 대로 하지 못하게 막힌 모양이라고 짐작한 것이었다.\n\n“아,” 케일럽이 고개를 숙이며 진지하게 손을 내저었다. 가스 부인은 이것이 그가 이 문제에 관해 더는 말할 뜻이 없다는 신호임을 알았다.\n\n벌스트로드로 말하자면, 그는 거의 곧바로 말에 올라 스톤 코트로 출발했으니, 리드게이트보다 먼저 그곳에 도착하고 싶어 안달이었던 것이다.\n\n그의 마음은 갖가지 형상과 추측으로 가득 차 있었는데, 그것들은 그의 희망과 두려움에게 하나의 언어와도 같았다, 마치 우리가 온몸을 뒤흔드는 진동에서 어떤 음조를 듣게 되는 것처럼. 케일럽 가스가 자기 과거를 알고 그 후원을 거절한 일 앞에서 그가 움찔하며 느꼈던 깊은 굴욕감은, 래플스가 입을 연 상대가 다른 누구도 아닌 가스였다는 사실에서 오는 안도감과 번갈아 들었고, 거의 그 안도감에 자리를 내주었다. 그에게는 그것이, 섭리가 그를 더 나쁜 결과에서 구해 내려 한다는 일종의 보증처럼 여겨졌다. 이로써 비밀이 지켜지리라는 희망의 길이 열린 셈이었다. 래플스가 병에 시달리게 된 것, 그가 다른 곳도 아닌 스톤 코트로 인도된 것 — 이러한 사건들이 불러일으킨 갖가지 개연성의 환영 앞에서 벌스트로드의 심장은 두근거렸다. 만일 그가 모든 치욕의 위험에서 풀려나는 것으로 밝혀진다면 — 만일 그가 완전한 자유 속에서 숨 쉴 수 있다면 — 그의 삶은 이제껏 어느 때보다도 더 거룩하게 바쳐지리라. 그는 마음속으로 이 맹세를 들어 올렸으니, 마치 그것이 자기가 간절히 바라는 결과를 재촉해 주기라도 할 것처럼. 그는 그 기도 어린 결심의 효력을 — 죽음을 결정짓는 그 효력을 — 믿어 보려 애썼다. 그는 자신이 “당신의 뜻이 이루어지소서” 하고 말해야 함을 알았고, 또 자주 그렇게 말했다. 그러나 하느님의 뜻이 그 미운 사내의 죽음이기를 바라는 격렬한 욕망은 여전히 남아 있었다.\n\n그러나 스톤 코트에 도착했을 때, 그는 충격 없이는 래플스의 변화를 볼 수 없었다. 만일 그 창백함과 쇠약함만 아니었다면, 벌스트로드는 그에게 일어난 변화를 전적으로 정신적인 것이라 불렀을 것이다. 시끄럽게 사람을 괴롭히던 기색 대신, 그는 격렬하고도 막연한 공포를 드러냈고, 돈이 다 사라졌다며 — 그는 강도를 당했고 — 그 절반을 빼앗겼다며, 벌스트로드의 노여움을 달래려는 듯했다. 그가 여기 온 것은 단지 아프기 때문이고 누군가 자기를 쫓고 있기 때문이라고 했다 — 누군가 자기 뒤를 밟고 있다고, 자기는 아무에게도 아무 말도 하지 않았다고, 입을 꾹 다물고 있었다고. 이런 증상들의 의미를 알지 못한 벌스트로드는, 이 새로운 신경 과민을 래플스를 겁주어 진실을 자백하게 만들 수단으로 해석하고는, 아무 말도 하지 않았다는 그의 주장이 거짓이라고 추궁했다. 방금 그를 이륜마차에 태워 스톤 코트로 데려온 사람에게 다 말해 놓고서 무슨 소리냐는 것이었다. 래플스는 엄숙히 맹세하며 이를 부인했는데, 사실인즉 그의 의식의 연결고리가 끊겨 있었던 데다, 케일럽 가스에게 한 그 자질구레하고도 공포에 질린 이야기는 어둠 속으로 도로 가라앉아 버린 일련의 환각적 충동에 휩쓸려 나온 것이었다.\n\n이 가련한 사내의 마음을 도무지 붙들 수 없으며, 그가 가장 알고 싶어 하는 사실 — 즉 그가 케일럽 가스를 제외한 인근의 모든 사람에게 정말로 침묵을 지켰는가 아닌가 — 에 관해서는 래플스의 어떤 말도 믿을 수 없다는 이 징후 앞에서, 벌스트로드의 마음은 다시 가라앉았다. 가정부는 조금도 거리낌 없는 태도로, 가스 씨가 떠난 뒤 래플스가 맥주를 청했고 그 뒤로는 말이 없으며 몹시 아파 보인다고 말했다. 그 점에서는 아무런 누설도 없었다고 결론지을 수 있었다. 에이블 부인은, 더 슈럽스의 하인들이 그러했듯, 그 낯선 사내를 부자들의 골칫거리 가운데 하나인 그 달갑잖은 “친척” 부류로 여겼다. 그녀는 처음에 그 친척 관계를 리그 씨에게 결부시켰는데, 물려받을 재산이 있는 곳이라면 그처럼 큼직한 쉬파리들이 윙윙거리며 모여드는 것이 자연스러워 보였던 것이다. 어떻게 그가 벌스트로드와도 “친척”일 수 있는지는 그리 명확하지 않았으나, 에이블 부인은 남편과 의견을 같이하여 “알 수 없는 일”이라 여겼으니, 이 명제는 그녀에게 적잖은 사색거리가 되었기에, 그녀는 더 깊이 헤아리지 않은 채 그저 고개만 갸웃거렸다.\n\n한 시간이 채 못 되어 리드게이트가 도착했다. 벌스트로드는 래플스가 있는, 벽판을 두른 응접실 밖에서 그를 맞으며 말했다.\n\n“리드게이트 씨, 여러 해 전에 한때 제 밑에서 일했던 불운한 사람 때문에 당신을 모셨습니다. 그 후 그는 미국으로 건너갔다가, 안타깝게도 게으르고 방탕한 생활로 돌아온 모양입니다. 무일푼이 된 그는 제게 도움을 청할 처지지요. 그는 이곳의 옛 주인이던 리그와 약간의 연고가 있어서, 그 때문에 여기까지 흘러들어 왔습니다. 제가 보기에 그는 심각하게 아프고, 정신도 영향을 받은 것 같습니다. 저는 그를 위해 최선을 다해야 한다고 느낍니다.”\n\n벌스트로드와 나눈 지난번 대화가 강하게 마음에 남아 있던 리드게이트는, 그에게 불필요한 말을 한마디도 하고 싶지 않아, 이 설명에 답하여 가볍게 고개만 숙였다. 그러나 방으로 들어서기 직전, 그는 무심결에 돌아서서 말했다. “이름이 무엇입니까?” — 이름을 아는 것은 실무 정치가에게나 마찬가지로 의사에게도 필수적인 소양이었던 것이다.\n\n“래플스, 존 래플스입니다,” 벌스트로드가 말했는데, 그는 래플스가 어떻게 되든 리드게이트가 그에 관해 더는 아무것도 알지 못하기를 바랐다.\n\n환자를 철저히 진찰하고 살펴본 뒤, 리드게이트는 그를 침상에 눕히고 되도록 완전한 안정 속에 두라고 지시한 다음, 벌스트로드와 함께 다른 방으로 갔다.\n\n“제 생각에는 심각한 경우인 것 같습니다만,” 리드게이트가 말을 꺼내기도 전에 은행가가 말했다.\n\n“아니요 — 그렇기도 합니다,” 리드게이트가 반쯤 미심쩍게 말했다. “오래 묵은 합병증이 어떤 영향을 미칠지는 판단하기 어렵습니다. 그렇지만 이 사람은 본래 튼튼한 체질이었습니다. 이번 발작이 치명적일 거라고는 보지 않습니다, 물론 몸 상태가 위태로운 지경이긴 하지만요. 잘 지켜보고 보살펴야 합니다.”\n\n“제가 직접 여기 남겠습니다,” 벌스트로드가 말했다. “에이블 부인과 그 남편은 경험이 없으니까요. 벌스트로드 부인에게 쪽지를 전해 주시는 수고만 해 주신다면, 저는 얼마든지 여기서 밤을 새울 수 있습니다.”\n\n“그럴 필요까진 거의 없을 듯합니다,” 리드게이트가 말했다. “그는 충분히 온순하고 겁에 질려 있어 보입니다. 더 다루기 어려워질 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 여기 일하는 사람이 있지 않습니까?”\n\n“저는 한적함을 위해 여기서 며칠 밤을 묵은 적이 한두 번이 아닙니다,” 벌스트로드가 대수롭지 않다는 듯 말했다. “지금도 얼마든지 그럴 마음이 있습니다. 필요하면 에이블 부인과 그 남편이 저를 교대해 주거나 거들어 줄 수 있고요.”\n\n“좋습니다. 그렇다면 제 지시는 당신에게만 하면 되겠군요,” 리드게이트가, 벌스트로드의 약간 별난 점에 그리 놀라지 않으며 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면, 가망이 있다고 보십니까?” 리드게이트가 지시를 마치자 벌스트로드가 말했다.\n\n“현재 제가 발견하지 못한 어떤 추가 합병증이 드러나지 않는 한 — 예, 있습니다,” 리드게이트가 말했다. “더 나쁜 단계로 넘어갈 수도 있지만, 제가 처방한 치료를 잘 지키면 며칠 안에 좋아진다 해도 놀랍지 않을 겁니다. 단호함이 있어야 합니다. 명심하십시오, 그가 어떤 종류든 술을 찾거든 절대로 주지 마십시오. 제 견해로는, 이런 상태의 사람들은 병보다 치료로 죽는 경우가 더 많습니다. 그래도 새로운 증상이 나타날 수는 있습니다. 내일 아침에 다시 오겠습니다.”\n\n쪽지가 벌스트로드 부인에게 전달되도록 기다린 뒤, 리드게이트는 말을 타고 떠났는데, 처음에는 래플스의 내력에 관해 아무런 추측도 하지 않은 채, 다만 이런 알코올 중독 사례를 다루는 올바른 방법에 관한 논쟁 전체를 머릿속으로 되짚어 보았으니, 그 논쟁은 미국에서 웨어 박사가 쌓은 풍부한 경험이 발표되면서 근래 크게 들끓던 것이었다. 리드게이트는 외국에 있을 때 이미 이 문제에 관심을 가졌었다. 그는 알코올을 허용하고 다량의 아편을 끈질기게 투여하는 통상적인 관행에 강하게 반대하는 신념을 지녔고, 이 신념에 따라 여러 차례 행동하여 좋은 결과를 얻었던 것이다.\n\n“이 사람은 병든 상태이긴 하지만,” 그는 생각했다. “아직 버틸 힘이 꽤 남아 있어. 짐작건대 벌스트로드의 자선 대상이겠지. 사람의 성품 속에 어찌 그리 모진 구석과 다정한 구석이 나란히 자리하는지 신기한 일이야. 벌스트로드는 어떤 사람들에게는 내가 본 가장 몰인정한 작자 같은데도, 자선 사업에는 한없는 수고를 들이고 막대한 돈을 써 왔으니 말이지. 짐작건대 그에게는 하늘이 누구를 보살피는지 가려내는 어떤 시금석이 있는 모양인데 — 하늘이 나는 보살피지 않는다고 그는 마음을 정해 둔 거지.”\n\n이 한 줄기 쓰라림은 풍부한 원천에서 솟아난 것으로, 그가 로윅 게이트에 가까워질수록 생각의 물줄기 속에서 점점 더 넓게 퍼져 갔다. 그는 그날 아침 벌스트로드와의 첫 면담 이후로 그곳에 가지 못했으니, 은행가의 심부름꾼이 그를 병원에서 찾아냈던 것이다. 그리고 그는 난생처음으로, 닥쳐오는 궁핍 — 결혼 생활을 견딜 만하게 해 주던 모든 것의, 그와 로저먼드를 서로에게 얼마나 보잘것없는 위안일 수밖에 없는지 깨닫지 않을 수 없는 그 헐벗은 고립으로부터 그들을 지켜 주던 모든 것의 궁핍 — 에서 자신을 구해 줄 만큼의 돈을 마련할 수 있으리라는 희망을 남겨 주는 어떤 방책의 환영도 마음 한구석에 두지 못한 채 집으로 돌아가는 길이었다. 자기 자신을 위한 다정함 없이 지내는 편이, 자신의 다정함이 그녀에게 다른 것들의 결핍을 결코 메워 줄 수 없음을 보는 것보다 차라리 견딜 만했다. 지나갔거나 앞으로 닥칠 굴욕으로 인한 그 자신의 자존심의 고통은 충분히 날카로웠으나, 그것은 그 위에 군림하는 한층 더 예리한 고통과 그 자신조차 거의 구별할 수 없었으니 — 로저먼드가 자기를 주로 자기에게 실망과 불행을 안긴 원인으로 여기게 되리라는 것을 예견하는 고통이었다. 그는 가난의 임시변통을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없었고, 그것이 자신의 앞날에 끼어든 적도 이제껏 없었다. 그러나 이제 그는, 서로 사랑하고 생각의 밑천을 공유하는 두 사람이라면 초라한 가구를 두고도, 버터와 달걀을 얼마나 살 수 있을지 셈하면서도 함께 웃을 수 있으리라는 것을 그려 보기 시작하던 참이었다. 그러나 그 시정(詩情)의 한 자락은 그에게 황금시대의 무사태평만큼이나 까마득해 보였다. 가엾은 로저먼드의 마음속에는 사치품들이 하찮게 보일 만큼의 여유 공간이 없었다. 그는 매우 침울한 기분으로 말에서 내려 집 안으로 들어갔으니, 저녁 식사 말고는 무엇으로도 기운이 나리라 기대하지 못한 채, 날이 저물기 전에 자신이 벌스트로드에게 청을 넣었다가 실패한 일을 로저먼드에게 알리는 것이 현명하리라 생각하면서였다. 최악의 상황에 대비시키는 데 지체하지 않는 편이 좋을 터였다.\n\n그러나 그의 저녁 식사는, 그가 그것을 먹을 수 있게 되기까지 오래도록 그를 기다려야 했다. 들어서면서 그는 도버의 대리인이 이미 집 안에 사람을 들여놓은 것을 발견했고, 리드게이트 부인이 어디 있느냐고 묻자, 그녀가 침실에 있다는 대답을 들었던 것이다. 그가 올라가 보니 그녀는 창백하고 말없이 침대에 뻗어 누워, 그의 어떤 말이나 눈길에도 얼굴로조차 아무 응답이 없었다. 그는 침대 곁에 앉아 그녀에게 몸을 숙이고는, 거의 기도의 외침과도 같이 말했다.\n\n“이 비참함을 용서해 주오, 가엾은 로저먼드! 우리 그저 서로 사랑하기만 합시다.”\n\n그녀는 여전히 얼굴에 공허한 절망을 띤 채 말없이 그를 바라보았다. 그러나 이윽고 그녀의 푸른 눈에 눈물이 차오르기 시작했고, 입술이 떨렸다. 그 강인한 사내는 그날 너무 많은 것을 견뎌야 했다. 그는 머리를 그녀의 머리 곁에 떨구고는 흐느꼈다.\n\n그는 그녀가 이튿날 아침 일찍 친정아버지에게 가는 것을 막지 않았으니 — 이제는 그녀가 하고 싶은 대로 하는 것을 막아서는 안 된다는 생각이 들었던 것이다. 반 시간쯤 뒤 그녀가 돌아와 말하기를, 이렇게 비참한 형편이 이어지는 동안 아빠와 엄마가 자기더러 친정에 와서 지내라고 하셨다는 것이었다. 아빠가 말하기를, 빚에 대해서는 자기로서는 어쩔 도리가 없으며 — 이것을 갚아 준다 한들 또 대여섯 건이 더 생길 거라고. 리드게이트가 그녀에게 편안한 집을 마련해 줄 때까지는 다시 친정으로 돌아오는 편이 낫겠다고. “당신 반대하나요, 터셔스?”\n\n“당신 좋을 대로 해요,” 리드게이트가 말했다. “하지만 당장 위기가 닥치는 건 아니오. 서두를 것 없소.”\n\n“저는 내일까지는 안 가겠어요,” 로저먼드가 말했다. “옷가지를 챙겨야 하니까요.”\n\n“아, 나라면 내일보다 좀 더 기다리겠소 — 무슨 일이 일어날지 알 수 없으니 말이오,” 리드게이트가 쓰라린 반어를 담아 말했다. “내가 목이라도 부러지면, 당신은 한결 수월해질지도 모르지.”\n\n그의 그녀에 대한 다정함은 감정의 충동이자 신중히 다진 결심이기도 했는데, 그것이 이런 반어적이거나 항의 어린 분노의 폭발로 어쩔 수 없이 끊기곤 한다는 것은, 리드게이트의 불행이자 로저먼드의 불행이기도 했다. 그녀는 그것을 전혀 정당하지 못하다고 여겼고, 이 유별난 가혹함이 그녀 안에 불러일으킨 반감은 그 더 끈질긴 다정함마저 받아들일 수 없게 만들 위험이 있었다.\n\n“당신은 제가 가는 걸 원치 않으시는군요,” 그녀가 쌀쌀한 부드러움으로 말했다. “그런데 어째서 그런 식의 난폭함 없이 그렇게 말씀하시지 못하나요? 당신이 다르게 하라고 청하실 때까지 저는 여기 있겠어요.”\n\n리드게이트는 더 말하지 않고 왕진을 나섰다. 그는 멍들고 부서진 듯한 느낌이었고, 두 눈 밑에는 로저먼드가 전에 본 적 없는 거뭇한 줄이 패어 있었다. 그녀는 차마 그를 바라볼 수가 없었다. 터셔스에게는 어떤 일이든 그녀에게 한층 더 나쁜 것으로 만들어 버리는 방식이 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXX.\n\n우리의 행위는 여전히 먼 곳에서 우리와 함께 여행하고, 우리가 어떠했는가가 지금의 우리를 만든다.\n\n라이드게이트가 스톤 코트를 떠난 뒤 불스트로드가 가장 먼저 한 일은 래플스의 주머니를 뒤지는 것이었다. 자신이 병들고 돈도 없어 리버풀에서 곧장 왔다는 말이 거짓이 아니라면, 그가 묵었던 곳들의 호텔 청구서 같은 흔적이 분명 주머니에서 나오리라고 생각했기 때문이다. 그의 수첩 안에는 여러 장의 청구서가 구겨진 채 쑤셔 넣어져 있었으나, 그날 아침 날짜가 찍힌 한 장을 제외하고는 어느 곳에서도 크리스마스 이후로 날짜가 더 늦은 것은 없었다. 그날 아침 날짜의 청구서는 그의 뒷주머니 한쪽에서 어느 마시(馬市)에 관한 전단과 함께 구겨져 있었는데, 그 시장이 열리는 빌클리의 한 여인숙에서 사흘간 묵은 비용을 나타내고 있었다. 빌클리는 미들마치에서 적어도 사십 마일은 떨어진 마을이었다. 청구액이 상당한 데다 래플스가 짐 하나 지니고 있지 않은 것으로 보아, 여비를 아끼기 위해 여행 가방을 숙박비 대신 맡기고 떠난 듯했다. 그의 지갑은 비어 있었고, 주머니에는 육 펜스짜리 동전 두어 개와 약간의 잔돈만 들어 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이런 정황들로부터 불스트로드는 래플스가 크리스마스의 그 잊지 못할 방문 이후 정말로 미들마치에서 멀리 떨어져 지냈다는 안도감을 얻었다. 멀리 떨어진 곳에서, 불스트로드와는 일면식도 없는 사람들 틈에 있었다면, 미들마치의 한 은행가에 관한 케케묵은 추문을 떠들어 댄다 한들 래플스의 그 괴롭히기 좋아하고 자기를 과시하려는 기질에 무슨 만족이 있겠는가? 또 그가 떠든다 해서 무슨 해가 있겠는가? 이제 중요한 점은, 케일럽 가스에게 작용한 듯 보였던 그 알아들을 만한 헛소리, 그 설명할 길 없는 폭로 충동의 위험이 있는 한 그를 지켜보는 일이었다. 그리고 불스트로드는 라이드게이트를 보면 혹 그런 충동이 그에게 일지 않을까 몹시 불안했다. 그는 밤새도록 홀로 그의 곁을 지켰고, 가정부에게는 자신이 부르면 곧장 올 수 있도록 옷을 입은 채 누워 있으라고만 일렀다. 자신이 잠을 이루지 못하는 데다 의사의 지시를 충실히 따르고 싶어서라는 핑계를 댔다. 래플스가 끊임없이 브랜디를 달라고 하며 자신이 가라앉고 있다고, 발밑에서 땅이 꺼져 내려가고 있다고 외쳐 댔지만, 그는 충실히 지시를 이행했다. 래플스는 잠도 못 자고 안절부절못했으나, 여전히 겁에 질려 다루기 쉬운 상태였다. 라이드게이트가 지시한 음식을 권하면 거절하고 자기가 요구한 다른 것들이 거부되자, 그는 자신의 모든 공포를 불스트로드에게 집중하는 듯했다. 그의 분노를, 굶겨 죽임으로써 자신에게 복수하려는 마음을 애원하듯 빌며 누그러뜨리려 했고, 누구에게도 그에 관해 한마디도 한 적이 없다고 거센 맹세로 단언했다. 이런 말조차도 불스트로드는 라이드게이트가 듣지 않기를 바랐다. 그러나 그의 섬망이 변덕스럽게 오락가락한다는 더 놀라운 징후는, 동틀 무렵의 어스름 속에서 래플스가 갑자기 의사가 곁에 있다고 상상하며, 그에게 말을 걸어 불스트로드가 자기가 발설했다는 이유로 복수하여 굶겨 죽이려 한다고, 사실은 발설한 적이 결코 없는데도 그렇게 단언하는 것이었다.\n\n불스트로드의 타고난 위압적인 기질과 강한 결단력이 그를 잘 도왔다. 이 섬세해 보이는 남자는 그 자신도 신경이 곤두서 있었으나, 절박한 상황 속에서 필요한 자극을 발견했다. 그 힘겨운 밤과 아침 내내, 온기 없이 움직임만 되찾은 살아 있는 시체 같은 모습으로, 그 차가운 무표정으로 사태를 장악하고 있는 동안, 그의 마음은 무엇을 경계해야 하며 무엇이 자신에게 안전을 가져다줄지를 골똘히 생각하느라 격렬하게 움직이고 있었다. 그가 어떤 기도를 올리든, 이 사람의 비참한 영적 상태와, 타인에게 악이 닥치기를 바라기보다는 신께서 정하신 벌을 자신이 감수해야 할 의무에 관해 마음속으로 어떤 진술을 하든 간에 — 말을 견고한 정신 상태로 응축하려는 이 모든 노력을 뚫고서, 그가 바라는 사건들의 영상이 저항할 수 없는 생생함으로 파고들어 퍼져 나갔다. 그리고 그 영상들의 행렬을 따라 그 영상들을 변호하는 논리도 따라왔다. 그는 래플스의 죽음을 보지 않을 수 없었고, 그 속에서 자신의 구원을 보지 않을 수 없었다. 이 비참한 인간이 사라진다는 것이 무엇이란 말인가? 그는 뉘우치지 않았다 — 하지만 공공의 범죄자들도 뉘우치지 않는 자들이 아니던가? 그래도 법은 그들의 운명을 결정했다. 만약 섭리가 이 경우에 죽음을 내린다면, 죽음을 바람직한 결말로 여기는 데에는 죄가 없었다 — 그가 그것을 앞당기는 데 손을 대지 않는다면, 처방된 바를 양심껏 따른다면 말이다. 여기에도 잘못이 있을 수 있었다. 인간의 처방이란 오류를 범할 수 있는 것이었다. 라이드게이트도 치료가 죽음을 앞당겼다고 하지 않았던가 — 자신의 치료법이라고 그러지 말란 법이 어디 있겠는가? 하지만 물론 옳고 그름의 문제에서는 의도가 전부였다.\n\n그리하여 불스트로드는 자신의 의도를 자신의 욕망과 분리해 두려고 애썼다. 그는 마음속으로 지시에 따를 작정이라고 선언했다. 무엇 때문에 그가 그 지시의 타당성을 두고 논쟁에 빠져야 한단 말인가? 그것은 욕망이 흔히 부리는 술책에 지나지 않았다 — 욕망은 무관한 회의(懷疑)라면 무엇이든 끌어들이며, 결과에 대한 온갖 불확실성 속에서, 법의 부재처럼 보이는 온갖 모호함 속에서 제 자리를 더 넓게 마련하는 법이다. 그래도 그는 지시에 따랐다.\n\n그의 불안은 끊임없이 라이드게이트를 향해 곁눈질했고, 전날 아침 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일에 대한 기억에는 그 실제 장면 동안에는 전혀 일지 않았던 감정들이 따라붙었다. 그때 그는 병원에 제안된 변화에 대한 라이드게이트의 괴로운 심정에 대해서도, 다소 과도한 요청이라고 여겨 거절한 것이 — 그로서는 정당한 거절이라 여겼지만 — 자신에 대해 어떤 마음을 불러일으킬지에 대해서도 거의 신경 쓰지 않았다. 그러나 이제 그는 그 장면을 되새기면서, 자신이 아마도 라이드게이트를 적으로 만들었으리라는 인식과 함께, 그를 달래고 싶은, 아니 차라리 그에게 개인적인 은혜를 입었다는 강한 느낌을 심어 주고 싶은 욕구가 깨어났다. 그는 즉시, 비록 분에 넘치는 것일지라도 금전적 희생을 치르지 않은 것을 후회했다. 불쾌한 의심이 생기거나, 심지어 래플스의 헛소리에서 사실을 알게 되는 경우라도, 불스트로드는 중대한 은혜를 베풂으로써 라이드게이트의 마음속에 자신의 방어막을 마련해 두었다고 느꼈을 터였다. 그러나 그 후회는 어쩌면 너무 늦게 찾아온 것인지도 몰랐다.\n\n이 불행한 남자의 영혼 속에서 벌어지는 기이하고도 가련한 갈등이여. 그는 여러 해 동안 지금의 자신보다 더 나은 사람이 되기를 갈망했고 — 자신의 이기적인 정념들을 훈련시켜 엄숙한 예복을 입혀 두었기에, 그것들과 함께 경건한 성가대처럼 걸어왔건만, 이제 그 가운데 공포가 일어 그들은 더 이상 성가를 부르지 못하고 안전을 구하는 공통의 비명만을 토해 내고 있었다.\n\n라이드게이트가 도착했을 때는 거의 정오 무렵이었다. 더 일찍 오려 했으나 일이 있어 붙들렸다고 그는 말했다. 그의 초췌한 모습을 불스트로드는 알아챘다. 하지만 그는 곧바로 환자를 살피는 데 몰두하여 그동안 일어난 일을 모조리 꼼꼼히 물었다. 래플스는 악화되어 음식을 거의 들지 못했고, 줄곧 잠을 못 이루며 불안하게 헛소리를 해 댔다. 그러나 여전히 난폭하지는 않았다. 불스트로드가 불안하게 예상했던 것과는 달리, 그는 라이드게이트가 있는 것을 거의 알아채지 못한 채 계속 두서없이 떠들거나 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그를 어떻게 보십니까?\" 불스트로드가 단둘이 있을 때 물었다.\n\n\"증상이 더 나빠졌습니다.\"\n\n\"희망이 줄어든 겁니까?\"\n\n\"아니요. 그래도 회복될 수 있다고 봅니다. 선생께서는 여기 머무르실 작정입니까?\" 라이드게이트가 불스트로드를 바라보며 불쑥 물었는데, 그 질문이 그를 불안하게 했다. 사실 그것은 의심 어린 추측에서 나온 것이 결코 아니었는데도 그러했다.\n\n\"네, 그럴 생각입니다.\" 불스트로드가 자신을 다스리며 신중하게 말했다. \"아내에게는 제가 붙들려 있는 사정을 알려 두었습니다. 에이블 부인과 그 남편은 완전히 맡겨 두기에는 경험이 부족하고, 이런 종류의 책임은 그들이 제게 하는 일에 포함되어 있다고 보기도 어렵습니다. 새로 지시할 것이 있으시겠지요.\"\n\n라이드게이트가 새로 일러 줄 주된 지시는, 몇 시간이 지나도 불면이 계속될 경우 극히 적은 양의 아편을 투여하라는 것이었다. 그는 만일을 대비해 아편을 주머니에 넣어 왔고, 투여량과 멈춰야 할 시점에 관해 불스트로드에게 세세히 일러 주었다. 그는 멈추지 않을 경우의 위험을 강조했고, 어떤 알코올도 주어서는 안 된다는 지시를 거듭 강조했다.\n\n\"제가 이 환자를 본 바로는,\" 그가 말을 맺었다. \"제가 가장 두려워할 만한 것은 마취 작용뿐입니다. 음식을 많이 들지 못하더라도 그는 견뎌 낼 수도 있습니다. 그에게는 상당한 체력이 있어요.\"\n\n\"라이드게이트 선생, 선생께서도 안색이 안 좋아 보입니다 — 제가 선생을 알아 온 바로는 매우 드문, 아니 전례가 없는 일이라 해야겠지요.\" 불스트로드가 전날의 무관심과는 너무도 다른 염려를 내보이며 말했다. 그것은 마치 자신의 피로에 대한 지금의 무심함이 평소의 자기 보전적인 조심성과 너무도 다른 것과 마찬가지였다. \"선생께서 시달리고 계신 듯하여 걱정입니다.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\" 라이드게이트가 모자를 들고 떠날 채비를 하며 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"무언가 새로운 일인 듯하여 걱정입니다.\" 불스트로드가 캐묻듯이 말했다. \"부디 앉으시지요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 괜찮습니다.\" 라이드게이트가 다소 거만하게 말했다. \"어제 제 형편이 어떤지 말씀드렸지요. 더 보탤 것은 없습니다. 다만 그 후로 실제로 제 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 것뿐입니다. 한 문장으로도 꽤 많은 곤경을 전할 수 있군요. 이만 가 보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만, 라이드게이트 선생, 잠깐만.\" 불스트로드가 말했다. \"이 문제를 다시 생각해 보았습니다. 어제는 갑작스러워서 피상적으로만 보았던 것이지요. 아내가 자기 조카를 걱정하고, 저 자신도 선생의 처지가 비참하게 바뀌는 것을 슬퍼할 것입니다. 제게 청구되는 것들이 많기는 하나, 다시 생각해 보니 선생을 돕지 않은 채 두기보다는 제가 작은 희생을 감수하는 편이 옳다고 여겨집니다. 천 파운드면 선생의 부담에서 완전히 벗어나 다시 굳건히 설 수 있다고 하셨던 것 같은데요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 라이드게이트가 말했는데, 그의 내면에서 솟구치는 거대한 기쁨의 도약이 다른 모든 감정을 압도했다. \"그러면 제 빚을 모두 갚고도 약간이 손에 남을 겁니다. 우리 살림을 절약하는 방향으로 꾸려 갈 수 있겠지요. 그리고 머지않아 제 진료도 나아질 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잠시 기다려 주신다면, 라이드게이트 선생, 그 금액으로 수표를 끊어 드리지요. 이런 경우의 도움이 실효를 거두려면 철저해야 한다는 것을 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n불스트로드가 수표를 쓰는 동안 라이드게이트는 창가로 돌아서서 자신의 집을 생각했다 — 좌절에서 구원받은 좋은 출발과, 아직 꺾이지 않은 선한 목적을 지닌 자신의 삶을 생각했다.\n\n\"이것에 대해 차용증서 한 장을 써 주시면 됩니다, 라이드게이트 선생.\" 은행가가 수표를 들고 그에게 다가서며 말했다. \"그리고 머지않아 선생께서 차차 형편이 되어 제게 갚을 수 있게 되기를 바랍니다. 그동안에는 선생께서 더 이상의 곤경에서 벗어나리라 생각하니 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"깊이 감사드립니다.\" 라이드게이트가 말했다. \"선생께서는 제게 얼마간의 행복과 선을 이룰 얼마간의 기회를 가지고 일할 전망을 되찾아 주셨습니다.\"\n\n불스트로드가 거절을 다시 생각한 것이 그에게는 매우 자연스러운 처사로 보였다. 그것은 그의 성품 가운데 더 너그러운 면과 일치하는 것이었다. 그러나 한시바삐 집에 돌아가 로저먼드에게 좋은 소식을 전하고, 은행에서 현금을 찾아 도버의 대리인에게 건네주려고 말을 빠른 속보로 몰면서, 마치 어두운 날개를 단 흉조의 비행이 시야를 가로지르듯, 불쾌한 인상과 함께 한 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다 — 몇 달 사이에 자신에게 일어난 그 대조에 대한 생각이었다. 강한 개인적 은혜를 입게 된 것에 자신이 더없이 기뻐하고 있다는 것 — 불스트로드에게서 자신을 위한 돈을 얻게 된 것에 자신이 더없이 기뻐하고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n은행가는 불안의 한 원인을 무효화하는 무언가를 했다고 느꼈으나, 그럼에도 마음이 그다지 편해지지는 않았다. 그는 라이드게이트의 호의를 바라게 만든 병든 동기의 양을 가늠하지는 않았으나, 그 양은 그럼에도 여전히 그의 핏속에서 자극제처럼 활발하게 작용하고 있었다. 사람은 맹세를 하면서도 그 맹세를 깨뜨릴 수단을 내던지지 않는다. 그것이 그가 명백히 그 맹세를 깨뜨릴 작정이기 때문일까? 결코 그렇지 않다. 다만 그 맹세를 깨뜨리는 쪽으로 기우는 욕망들이 그의 안에서 어렴풋이 작동하며 그의 상상 속으로 파고들어, 그가 그 맹세의 이유를 스스로에게 거듭 되뇌고 있는 바로 그 순간에 그의 근육을 풀어 놓는 것이다. 래플스가 빠르게 회복하여 그 가증스러운 능력을 다시 마음껏 휘두르게 되는 것 — 불스트로드가 어떻게 그것을 바랄 수 있겠는가? 죽은 래플스야말로 해방을 가져다주는 영상이었고, 그는 간접적으로 그 해방의 길을 위해 기도했다. 만약 가능하다면, 이 지상에 남은 자신의 여생이, 신을 섬기는 도구로서의 자신을 완전히 무너뜨릴 그 치욕의 위협에서 벗어나게 해 달라고 간청했다. 라이드게이트의 견해는 이 기도가 이루어지리라는 약속의 편에 서 있지 않았다. 그리고 하루가 저물어 가면서 불스트로드는 이 남자의 끈질긴 생명력에 점점 짜증이 나는 것을 느꼈으니, 그는 이 남자가 죽음의 침묵 속으로 가라앉는 것을 보고 싶어 했다. 위압적인 의지가 이 짐승 같은 생명에 대한 살의를 부추겼으나, 의지만으로는 그 생명에 아무런 힘을 미치지 못했다. 그는 마음속으로 자신이 너무 지쳤다고 말했다. 오늘 밤은 환자 곁을 지키지 않고, 필요하면 남편을 부를 수 있는 에이블 부인에게 맡길 작정이었다.\n\n여섯 시 무렵, 래플스가 발작적으로 어수선한 토막잠밖에 자지 못하고 그때마다 깨어나 새로운 불안과 끊임없는 비명으로 자신이 가라앉고 있다고 외쳐 댔으므로, 불스트로드는 라이드게이트의 지시에 따라 아편을 투여하기 시작했다. 삼십 분쯤 지났을 때, 그는 에이블 부인을 불러 자신이 더 이상 곁을 지킬 수 없는 상태라고 말했다. 이제 환자를 그녀의 보살핌에 맡겨야 한다며, 각 회분의 양에 관한 라이드게이트의 지시를 그녀에게 일러 주었다. 에이블 부인은 그전까지 라이드게이트의 처방에 관해 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그녀는 그저 불스트로드가 지시하는 것을 준비해 가져왔고, 그가 일러 주는 대로 했을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 이제 아편을 투여하는 것 외에 또 무엇을 해야 하는지 묻기 시작했다.\n\n\"지금으로서는 수프나 소다수를 권하는 것 말고는 없습니다. 더 지시할 것이 있으면 제게 오시오. 무언가 중대한 변화가 없는 한, 오늘 밤은 다시 이 방에 들어오지 않겠습니다. 필요하면 남편에게 도움을 청하시오. 나는 일찍 자야겠소.\"\n\n\"정말이지 그러셔야지요, 나리.\" 에이블 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 지금까지 드신 것보다 더 기운 나는 걸 좀 드셔야겠어요.\"\n\n이제 불스트로드는 래플스가 헛소리로 무슨 말을 할지 걱정하지 않고 자리를 떴다. 그의 헛소리는 위험한 믿음을 만들어 낼 것 같지 않은 두서없는 중얼거림으로 바뀌어 있었다. 어쨌든 이 정도는 감수해야 했다. 그는 먼저 판벽을 두른 응접실로 내려가, 말에 안장을 얹게 하여 달빛을 받으며 집으로 돌아가, 이 세상의 결과에 대한 근심을 내려놓아 버릴까 생각하기 시작했다. 그러다 라이드게이트에게 그날 저녁 다시 와 달라고 청했더라면 좋았을 것이라고 생각했다. 어쩌면 그가 다른 견해를 내놓아, 래플스가 희망이 덜한 상태로 접어들고 있다고 여길지도 몰랐다. 라이드게이트를 부르러 보내야 할까? 만약 래플스가 정말로 악화되어 서서히 죽어 가고 있다면, 불스트로드는 섭리에 감사하며 잠자리에 들어 잠들 수 있으리라 느꼈다. 그러나 그가 정말 더 나빠졌을까? 라이드게이트가 와서, 그저 예상대로 진행되고 있다고만 말하며, 머지않아 깊은 잠에 빠져 회복할 것이라고 예언할지도 몰랐다. 그를 부르러 보낸들 무슨 소용인가? 불스트로드는 그런 결과 앞에서 움츠러들었다. 어떤 관념이나 견해도 그가 단 하나의 개연성을 보는 것을 막을 수 없었으니, 그것은 곧 래플스가 회복되면 예전과 똑같은 사람이 되어, 괴롭히는 자로서의 힘을 새로이 갖추고서, 그로 하여금 아내를 친구들과 고향에서 떼어 내어 객지에서 세월을 보내게 하고, 그녀의 마음속에 그를 멀어지게 하는 의혹을 품게 만들리라는 것이었다.\n\n그는 오직 벽난로 불빛만 받으며 한 시간 반 동안 이 갈등에 잠겨 앉아 있다가, 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올라 자리에서 일어나 들고 내려왔던 머리맡 촛불에 불을 붙였다. 그 생각이란, 아편 투여를 언제 멈춰야 하는지 에이블 부인에게 말해 두지 않았다는 것이었다.\n\n그는 촛대를 집어 들었으나, 한참 동안 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 그녀는 이미 라이드게이트가 처방한 것보다 더 많이 그에게 주었을지도 몰랐다. 하지만 지금처럼 지친 상태에서 지시의 일부를 잊는 것은 용서받을 만한 일이었다. 그는 촛불을 손에 들고 위층으로 올라가면서도, 곧장 자기 방으로 들어가 잠자리에 들어야 할지, 아니면 환자의 방으로 가서 자신의 누락을 바로잡아야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그는 래플스의 방 쪽으로 얼굴을 향한 채 복도에서 멈춰 섰고, 그가 신음하며 중얼거리는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그렇다면 그는 잠들지 않은 것이었다. 아직도 잠이 오지 않는다면, 라이드게이트의 처방을 따르기보다 어기는 편이 더 낫지 않으리라고 누가 장담할 수 있겠는가?\n\n그는 자기 방으로 들어섰다. 옷을 채 다 벗기도 전에 에이블 부인이 문을 두드렸다. 그는 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말하는 것을 들을 수 있을 만큼만 문을 한 치쯤 열었다.\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 나리, 저 가엾은 사람에게 브랜디나 뭐라도 줄 게 없을까요? 자꾸 가라앉는다고 하면서, 다른 건 아무것도 삼키질 못해요 — 삼킨다 해도 기운이 될 만한 게 없고요 — 아편뿐인걸요. 그리고 점점 더 자기가 땅속으로 꺼져 들어간다고 하네요.\"\n\n그녀가 놀랍게도 불스트로드 씨는 대답하지 않았다. 그의 내면에서 갈등이 벌어지고 있었다.\n\n\"이대로 가다간 기운을 못 차려서 죽고 말 것 같아요. 제가 가엾은 옛 주인 로비슨 나리를 간호할 때는 포트와인이랑 브랜디를 끊임없이, 한 번에 큰 잔으로 드려야 했답니다.\" 에이블 부인이 다소 항의하는 어조로 덧붙였다.\n\n그러나 또다시 불스트로드 씨가 곧바로 대답하지 않자, 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"사람이 죽음의 문턱에 있을 때는 아낄 때가 아니지요, 나리께서도 분명 그러길 바라지 않으실 거예요. 안 그러면 제가 우리가 두고 쓰는 럼주 병이라도 그에게 줄 텐데요. 하지만 나리처럼 밤새 곁을 지키시고, 할 수 있는 일은 다 하신 분이—\"\n\n이때 문틈으로 열쇠 하나가 쑥 내밀어졌고, 불스트로드 씨가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"이것이 술 보관함 열쇠요. 거기 브랜디가 많이 있을 거요.\"\n\n이튿날 이른 아침 — 여섯 시쯤 — 불스트로드 씨는 일어나 한동안 기도하며 시간을 보냈다. 사사로운 기도가 반드시 진솔하다고, 반드시 행위의 뿌리까지 닿는다고 누가 생각하겠는가? 사사로운 기도란 소리 없는 말이며, 말이란 무언가를 대표하는 것이다. 자기 자신의 성찰 속에서조차, 누가 있는 그대로의 자신을 대표할 수 있겠는가? 불스트로드는 아직 지난 스물네 시간의 어지러운 충동들을 마음속에서 풀어내지 못한 상태였다.\n\n그는 복도에서 귀를 기울였고, 거칠게 코를 고는 듯한 숨소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그러고는 정원으로 걸어 나가, 풀잎과 싱그러운 봄 새잎에 내린 이른 서리를 바라보았다. 다시 집 안으로 들어섰을 때, 그는 에이블 부인의 모습을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"환자는 좀 어떻소 — 자고 있는 것 같은데?\" 그가 짐짓 밝은 어조를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 깊이 들었어요, 나리.\" 에이블 부인이 말했다. \"세 시에서 네 시 사이에 차츰차츰 잠들었답니다. 가서 좀 보시겠어요? 그를 두고 와도 괜찮겠다 싶어서요. 제 남편은 들에 나갔고, 어린 계집아이가 주전자를 보고 있거든요.\"\n\n불스트로드는 위층으로 올라갔다. 한눈에 그는 래플스가 소생을 가져오는 잠이 아니라, 죽음의 심연 속으로 점점 더 깊이 흘러 내려가는 잠에 빠져 있음을 알아보았다.\n\n그는 방을 둘러보다가 브랜디가 조금 남은 병과 거의 빈 아편 약병을 보았다. 그는 약병을 보이지 않는 곳에 치우고, 브랜디 병을 들고 아래층으로 내려가 다시 술 보관함에 넣고 잠갔다.\n\n아침을 먹으면서 그는 곧장 미들마치로 말을 타고 갈지, 아니면 라이드게이트가 오기를 기다릴지 곰곰이 생각했다. 그는 기다리기로 했고, 에이블 부인에게 일을 보러 가도 좋다고 말했다 — 자신이 침실에서 지켜볼 수 있다는 것이었다.\n\n그가 그곳에 앉아 자신의 평안을 위협하던 적이 돌이킬 수 없이 침묵 속으로 들어가는 것을 지켜보면서, 그는 여러 달 동안 느껴 보지 못한 평온을 느꼈다. 비밀이라는 감싸 안는 날개가 그의 양심을 달래 주었으니, 그것은 바로 그 순간 그를 구원하기 위해 내려온 천사처럼 여겨졌다. 그는 수첩을 꺼내, 미들마치를 떠날 것을 예상하고 계획하여 일부 실행에 옮겼던 여러 사항에 관한 메모들을 검토했고, 이제 자신의 부재가 잠시뿐이게 되었으니 그것들을 그대로 둘지 아니면 거둬들일지를 어디까지 정해야 할지 헤아렸다. 그가 바람직하다고 여기는 몇 가지 절약 방안은, 그가 잠시 경영에서 물러나 있는 동안에도 여전히 적당한 기회를 찾을 수 있을 터였고, 그는 여전히 캐소본 부인이 병원 비용의 상당 부분을 떠맡아 주기를 바랐다. 그렇게 시간이 흘러가다가, 코 고는 듯한 숨소리의 변화가 그의 주의를 온통 침대로 끌어당길 만큼 뚜렷해졌고, 그로 하여금 떠나가는 그 생명을 생각하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 한때 자신에게 종속되어 있던 생명 — 한때는 자신이 마음대로 이용할 수 있을 만큼 비천하다는 것을 발견하고 기뻐했던 생명이었다. 그때의 그 기쁨이야말로 이제 그로 하여금 그 생명이 끝나가는 것을 기뻐하게 만든 것이었다.\n\n그리고 래플스의 죽음이 앞당겨졌다고 누가 말할 수 있겠는가? 무엇이 그를 살릴 수 있었을지 누가 알겠는가?\n\n라이드게이트는 열 시 반에 도착하여, 마지막 숨이 멎는 순간을 목격하기에 알맞은 때였다. 그가 방에 들어섰을 때 불스트로드는 그의 얼굴에 떠오르는 갑작스러운 표정을 보았는데, 그것은 놀라움이라기보다 자신이 옳게 판단하지 못했음을 깨닫는 표정이었다. 그는 한동안 죽어 가는 사람에게 눈길을 둔 채 말없이 침대 곁에 서 있었으나, 그 표정에는 내면의 논쟁을 이어 가고 있음을 보여 주는 억눌린 활기가 어려 있었다.\n\n\"이 변화가 언제 시작되었습니까?\" 그가 불스트로드를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"어젯밤에는 제가 곁을 지키지 않았습니다.\" 불스트로드가 말했다. \"너무 지쳐서 에이블 부인에게 맡겼지요. 그녀 말로는 세 시에서 네 시 사이에 그가 잠에 빠져들었다고 합니다. 제가 여덟 시 전에 들어와 보니 거의 이런 상태였습니다.\"\n\n라이드게이트는 더 이상 묻지 않고, 말없이 지켜보다가 입을 열었다. \"다 끝났습니다.\"\n\n그날 아침 라이드게이트는 희망과 자유를 되찾은 상태였다. 그는 예전의 그 모든 활기를 띠고 자신의 일에 나섰고, 결혼 생활의 온갖 결핍을 견뎌 낼 만큼 자신이 강하다고 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 불스트로드가 자신에게 은인이 되었다는 것을 의식하고 있었다. 그러나 그는 이 환자의 일이 마음에 걸렸다. 그는 일이 이렇게 끝나리라고는 예상하지 못했다. 그러나 그는 불스트로드에게 그를 모욕하는 것처럼 보이지 않으면서 이 일에 관해 어떻게 질문을 던져야 할지 알 수 없었다. 또 가정부를 캐묻는다 한들 — 그 남자는 이미 죽었으니. 누군가의 무지나 경솔함이 그를 죽게 했다고 넌지시 비치는 것은 아무 소용이 없을 듯했다. 게다가 결국 자기 자신이 틀렸을 수도 있었다.\n\n그와 불스트로드는 함께 미들마치로 말을 타고 돌아오면서 여러 가지 이야기를 나누었다 — 주로 콜레라와 상원에서 개혁 법안이 통과될 가망성, 그리고 정치 연맹들의 굳은 결의에 관한 것이었다. 래플스에 관해서는 아무 말도 나오지 않았으나, 다만 불스트로드가 그를 로익 교회 묘지에 매장할 무덤이 필요하다는 것을 언급하며, 자신이 아는 한 그 가엾은 사람은 그에게 적대적이라고 그가 진술한 바 있는 릭을 제외하고는 친척이 없었다고 말했다.\n\n집에 돌아온 라이드게이트는 페어브러더 씨의 방문을 받았다. 목사는 전날 읍내에 나오지 않았으나, 라이드게이트의 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 소식이 저녁 무렵 로익까지 전해졌다. 구두장이이자 교구 서기인 스파이서 씨가 그 소식을 옮긴 것이었는데, 그는 로익 게이트에서 종을 다는 점잖은 직공인 자기 형에게서 그 소식을 들었다. 라이드게이트가 프레드 빈시와 함께 당구장에서 내려오던 그날 저녁 이후로, 페어브러더 씨가 그에 관해 품은 생각은 다소 어두웠다. 그린 드래건에서 한두 번 노름을 한 것은 다른 사람에게는 사소한 일일 수도 있었으나, 라이드게이트에게는 그가 예전의 자신과 달라지고 있다는 여러 징후 가운데 하나였다. 그는 예전 같았으면 지나칠 만큼 경멸했을 일들을 하기 시작하고 있었다. 어리석은 소문의 짤랑거림이 그에게 슬쩍 귀띔해 준, 결혼 생활의 어떤 불만들이 이 변화와 어떤 관련이 있든 간에, 페어브러더 씨는 그것이 주로 점점 더 분명하게 떠도는 빚과 연관되어 있음을 확신했고, 라이드게이트가 뒤에 어떤 재력이나 친구를 두고 있으리라는 어떤 생각도 전혀 허황된 것일지 모른다는 두려움이 들기 시작했다. 라이드게이트의 신뢰를 얻으려던 첫 시도에서 퇴짜를 맞은 일이 그로 하여금 두 번째 시도를 꺼리게 만들었으나, 실제로 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 이 소식이 목사로 하여금 자신의 망설임을 극복하게 했다.\n\n라이드게이트는 마침 자신이 무척 관심을 두고 있던 한 가난한 환자를 막 돌려보낸 참이었고, 페어브러더 씨를 놀라게 할 만큼 거리낌 없는 명랑함으로 손을 내밀러 나왔다. 이것 역시 동정과 도움에 대한 오만한 거절일까? 상관없었다. 동정과 도움은 베풀어져야 했다.\n\n\"잘 지내셨소, 라이드게이트? 선생에 관해 걱정스러운 이야기를 들어서 보러 왔소.\" 목사가 좋은 형 같은 어조로 말했는데, 다만 거기에는 책망이 담겨 있지 않았다. 이때쯤 두 사람은 다 자리에 앉아 있었고, 라이드게이트가 곧바로 대답했다 —\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 알 것 같습니다. 제 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 이야기를 들으셨군요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 사실이오?\"\n\n\"사실이었습니다.\" 라이드게이트가, 이제는 그 일을 이야기해도 개의치 않는다는 듯 거리낌 없는 태도로 말했다. \"하지만 위기는 지나갔습니다. 빚은 갚았어요. 이제 곤경에서 벗어났습니다. 빚에서 풀려나, 바라건대 더 나은 계획으로 새로 시작할 수 있게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 정말 다행이오.\" 목사가 의자에 등을 기대며, 짐을 내려놓은 뒤에 흔히 뒤따르는 그 낮고 빠른 어조로 말했다. \"「타임스」의 그 어떤 소식보다 그 말이 더 좋소. 솔직히 말하면 무거운 마음으로 선생을 찾아왔다오.\"\n\n\"와 주셔서 감사합니다.\" 라이드게이트가 진심으로 말했다. \"제가 더 행복해졌기에 그 친절을 더욱 만끽할 수 있습니다. 분명 저는 적잖이 짓눌렸지요. 그 멍이 머지않아 여전히 쓰라리게 느껴질까 봐 걱정입니다.\" 그가 다소 서글프게 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"하지만 지금 이 순간은 그저 고문하던 나사가 풀렸다는 것만 느껴질 뿐입니다.\"\n\n페어브러더 씨는 잠시 말이 없다가 진지하게 입을 열었다. \"이보게, 한 가지만 물어봅시다. 결례를 범하더라도 용서해 주오.\"\n\n\"제 마음을 상하게 할 만한 것은 아무것도 묻지 않으시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 — 이건 내 마음을 완전히 놓기 위해 필요한 것이오 — 선생은 혹시 — 그러지 않았소? — 빚을 갚기 위해, 나중에 더 심하게 선생을 괴롭힐지도 모를 또 다른 빚을 지지는 않았소?\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 라이드게이트가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"사실이 그러하니 말씀드리지 못할 이유가 없겠지요 — 제가 빚을 진 사람은 불스트로드입니다. 그가 제게 아주 후한 선금을 빌려주었어요 — 천 파운드를요 — 그리고 그는 상환을 느긋이 기다릴 여유가 있는 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"음, 그건 너그러운 처사로군.\" 페어브러더 씨가, 자신이 싫어하는 그 남자를 억지로 인정하며 말했다. 그의 섬세한 감정은 자신이 늘 라이드게이트에게 불스트로드와의 어떤 개인적 얽힘도 피하라고 권했다는 사실을 생각 속에서조차 곱씹기를 꺼렸다. 그는 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"그리고 불스트로드로서는 당연히 선생의 안녕에 관심을 느낄 만하지. 선생이 그와 함께 일하면서, 아마도 수입을 늘리기는커녕 오히려 줄였을 테니 말이오. 그가 그에 걸맞게 행동했다고 생각하니 기쁘오.\"\n\n라이드게이트는 이런 친절한 추측들 앞에서 불편함을 느꼈다. 그것들은 불과 몇 시간 전에야 처음으로 어렴풋이 꿈틀거리기 시작했던 그 불안한 의식을, 곧 불스트로드가 더없이 냉랭한 무관심을 보인 직후에 갑작스레 베푼 호의의 동기가 어쩌면 한낱 이기적인 것일지도 모른다는 의식을 그의 안에서 더욱 또렷하게 만들었다. 그는 그 친절한 추측들이 그냥 지나가도록 두었다. 그는 그 대출의 내력을 이야기할 수는 없었으나, 그것은 그 어느 때보다 생생하게 그의 마음에 떠올라 있었고, 목사가 짐짓 모른 체한 그 사실 — 불스트로드에게 개인적으로 빚을 지는 이 관계야말로 그가 한때 가장 단호히 피하고자 했던 것이라는 사실 또한 그러했다.\n\n그는 대답하는 대신, 자신이 계획한 절약 방안에 관해, 그리고 자신이 삶을 다른 관점에서 바라보게 되었다는 것에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"진료실을 차릴 작정입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"사실 그 점에서는 제가 잘못된 노력을 했던 것 같습니다. 그리고 로저먼드가 꺼리지 않는다면, 견습생을 하나 둘 생각입니다. 이런 일들이 마음에 들지는 않지만, 충실히 해낸다면 정말로 품위를 떨어뜨리는 것은 아니지요. 시작부터 호된 시련을 겪었으니, 그 덕에 작은 마찰들은 쉬워 보일 겁니다.\"\n\n가엾은 라이드게이트여! 그의 생각의 일부로서 무심코 흘러나온 그 \"로저먼드가 꺼리지 않는다면\"이라는 말은 그가 짊어진 멍에를 보여 주는 의미심장한 표시였다. 그러나 자신의 희망이 라이드게이트의 희망과 강하게 같은 흐름을 타고 있던 페어브러더 씨는, 이제 우울한 예감을 불러일으킬 만한 어떤 것도 그에 관해 알지 못했기에, 다정한 축하의 말을 남기고 그를 떠났다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXI.\n\n어릿광대. ……그게 포도송이 술집에서였지요, 거기 정말이지\n당신이 즐겨 앉는 자리가 있잖소, 안 그렇소?\n\n거품. 그렇소이다. 거긴 트인 방이라 겨울에 좋으니까요.\n어릿광대. 아, 그렇다면 아주 좋소. 여기서 진실이 밝혀지길 바라오.\n\n―『자에는 자로』\n* 『자에는 자로』: 셰익스피어의 희극.\n\n래플스가 죽고 닷새 뒤, 뱀브리지 씨는 그린 드래건 마구간 마당으로 통하는 커다란 아치문 아래 한가로이 서 있었다. 그는 홀로 사색에 잠기는 것을 즐기는 사람이 아니었으나, 방금 집에서 나온 참이었고, 이른 오후 아치문 아래 편안히 서 있는 사람이라면 누구든, 쪼아 먹을 만한 무언가를 발견한 비둘기처럼 어김없이 동행을 끌어모으게 마련이었다. 이 경우에는 양식이 될 만한 실체적인 대상은 없었으나, 이성의 눈은 잡담이라는 형태로 정신의 양식을 얻을 가능성을 알아보았다. 맞은편의 온순한 포목상 홉킨스 씨가 이 내적 통찰에 가장 먼저 따라 움직였으니, 그의 손님이 주로 여자들인 까닭에 사내들끼리의 대화를 더욱 갈망했던 것이다. 뱀브리지 씨는 포목상에게 다소 퉁명스러웠는데, 홉킨스가 자기와 이야기하게 되어 당연히 기뻐하리라고 느끼면서도, 자신은 홉킨스에게 말을 많이 허비할 생각이 없었던 것이다. 그러나 곧 더 중요한 청중이 작은 무리를 이루었으니, 지나가던 행인들 가운데 떨어져 나온 이들이거나, 그린 드래건에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 일부러 보러 어슬렁어슬렁 다가온 이들이었다. 그리고 뱀브리지 씨는, 자신이 보고 온 훌륭한 종마들과 방금 돌아온 북부 여행에서 사들인 말들에 관해 인상적인 이야기를 늘어놓을 만한 가치가 있음을 깨닫고 있었다. 그 자리에 있던 신사들은 만약 누군가가 마음만 먹으면 돈캐스터에서 볼 수 있는, 곧 네 살이 되는 적갈색 순종 암말을 능가할 말을 그에게 보여 줄 수 있다면 자기가 \"여기서 헤리퍼드까지\" 총에 맞아도 좋다고 그들을 흡족하게 해 주겠다는 다짐을 들었다. 또한 그가 사륜마차에 매려는 검은 말 한 쌍은, 19년에 자기가 포크너에게 백 기니를 받고 팔았던, 그리고 포크너가 두 달 뒤에 백육십 기니에 되팔았던 한 쌍을 그의 머릿속에 생생하게 떠올리게 했으니―이 말을 반증할 수 있는 신사가 있다면, 목이 마를 때까지 마음껏 뱀브리지 씨를 아주 흉한 이름으로 불러도 좋다는 특권을 주겠노라 했다.\n이야기가 이렇게 한창 무르익었을 때, 프랭크 홀리 씨가 다가왔다. 그는 그린 드래건에서 빈둥거리며 자기 품위를 떨어뜨릴 사람은 아니었으나, 마침 하이 스트리트를 지나다가 길 건너편의 뱀브리지를 보고는, 자기가 찾아봐 달라고 부탁한 일급 이륜마차용 말을 구했는지 물어보려고 성큼성큼 길을 건너왔다. 홀리 씨는 빌클리에서 골라 놓은 회색 말을 볼 때까지 기다려 달라는 청을 받았다. 만약 그 말이 그의 바람에 털끝만큼이라도 맞지 않는다면, 뱀브리지는 말을 보고도 말을 알아보지 못하는 사람이라는 것인데, 그것이야말로 상상할 수 있는 가장 있을 법하지 않은 일로 보였다. 길을 등지고 선 홀리 씨가 그 회색 말을 보고 시승해 볼 시간을 정하고 있을 때, 말을 탄 사람 하나가 천천히 지나갔다.\n\n\"불스트로드!\" 두세 사람이 동시에 나직한 목소리로 말했는데, 그중 포목상의 목소리는 공손하게 \"씨\"를 앞에 붙였다. 그러나 이렇게 외쳐 이름을 부른 데에는, 멀리서 마차가 나타났을 때 \"리버스턴 마차\"라고 말하는 것 이상의 의도는 누구에게도 없었다. 홀리 씨는 불스트로드의 등 쪽을 무심하게 한 번 둘러보았으나, 뱀브리지의 눈길이 그것을 좇으면서 그는 비꼬는 듯한 표정을 지었다.\n\"옳거니! 생각났군.\" 그가 목소리를 조금 낮추며 말을 꺼냈다. \"빌클리에서 당신 이륜마차용 말 말고도 다른 걸 하나 주워 왔지요, 홀리 씨. 불스트로드에 관한 근사한 이야기를 하나 주워 왔단 말입니다. 그가 어떻게 재산을 모았는지 아시오? 진기한 정보가 필요한 신사분께는, 내가 공짜로 알려 드리리다. 모두가 제 응보를 받는다면, 불스트로드는 보터니 만에서 기도를 올리고 있어야 할지도 모르오.\"\n\n* 보터니 만: 오스트레일리아의 유형지.\n\"무슨 말이오?\" 홀리 씨가 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 아치문 아래로 조금 다가서며 말했다. 만약 불스트로드가 악당으로 드러난다면, 프랭크 홀리는 그것을 예언한 셈이었다.\n\n\"불스트로드의 옛 친구였던 작자한테서 들은 얘깁니다. 그자를 어디서 처음 마주쳤는지 말씀드리지요.\" 뱀브리지가 갑자기 집게손가락을 까딱이며 말했다. \"그자는 라처 경매장에 있었는데, 그때는 내가 그를 전혀 몰랐소이다―손가락 사이로 빠져나갔지요―필시 불스트로드를 노리고 있었던 게요. 그자 말로는 불스트로드한테서 얼마든지 돈을 우려낼 수 있고, 그의 비밀을 죄다 안다는 거요. 어쨌든 그자가 빌클리에서 내게 죄다 털어놓았소. 술을 독하게 마시는 작자라. 빌어먹을, 그가 정말 밀고할 작정이었다고는 생각하지 않소만, 워낙 허풍 떠는 부류라, 그 허풍이 울타리고 도랑이고 넘어 다니는 통에, 마치 돈이라도 되는 양 말의 다리 종양까지 자랑할 위인이오. 사람이라면 멈출 때를 알아야 하는 법인데.\" 뱀브리지 씨는 못마땅하다는 투로 이 말을 했으니, 자기 자신의 허풍은 상품 가치를 보는 훌륭한 안목을 드러낸다고 흡족해하던 터였다.\n\n\"그자 이름이 뭐요? 어디 가면 만날 수 있소?\" 홀리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"어디 가면 만날 수 있느냐 하면, 새러슨스 헤드에 그자를 두고 왔소이다. 한데 이름은 래플스라 하오.\"\n\n\"래플스라니!\" 홉킨스 씨가 외쳤다. \"내가 어제 그의 장례 물품을 댔소이다. 그는 로윅에 묻혔지요. 불스트로드 씨가 상여를 따라갔고요. 아주 점잖은 장례였소.\" 청중들 사이에 강한 동요가 일었다. 뱀브리지 씨는 \"지옥불\"이 그중 가장 점잖은 말일 만한 욕설을 내뱉었고, 홀리 씨는 눈썹을 찌푸리고 고개를 앞으로 숙이며 외쳤다. \"뭐라고?―그자가 어디서 죽었다고?\"\n\n\"스톤 코트에서요.\" 포목상이 말했다. \"가정부 말로는 그가 주인 어른의 친척이랍디다. 금요일에 병이 들어 거기 왔다는군요.\"\n\n\"아니, 내가 그자와 술 한잔 한 게 수요일이었는데.\" 뱀브리지가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"의사가 그를 진찰했소?\" 홀리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소. 리드게이트 씨가요. 불스트로드 씨가 하룻밤 곁을 지켰고요. 그자는 사흘째 되는 날 아침에 죽었소이다.\"\n\n\"계속하시오, 뱀브리지.\" 홀리 씨가 다그치며 말했다. \"이 작자가 불스트로드에 관해 뭐라고 했소?\"\n\n무리는 어느새 더 커져 있었으니, 읍사무소 서기의 존재 자체가 거기서 들을 만한 무언가가 벌어지고 있다는 보증이었다. 그리하여 뱀브리지 씨는 일곱 사람이 듣는 가운데 자기 이야기를 풀어놓았다. 그것은 대체로 우리가 아는 내용이었으니, 윌 래디슬로에 관한 사실도 포함되어 있었고, 거기에 약간의 지방색과 정황이 덧붙여졌다. 그것은 불스트로드가 폭로될까 두려워하던 바였고―래플스의 시체와 함께 영원히 묻어 버리기를 바라던 바였다. 그것은 그의 지난 삶에 들러붙은 유령이었으니, 그가 그린 드래건의 아치문을 지나 말을 타고 가면서, 섭리가 자신을 거기서 구해 주었다고 믿고 있던 바로 그 유령이었다. 그렇다, 섭리. 그는 이 목적을 위해 자신이 무슨 책략 같은 짓을 했다고 아직 스스로 고백한 적이 없었다. 그는 제공된 듯이 보이는 것을 받아들였을 뿐이었다. 그가 그 사내의 영혼이 떠나는 것을 앞당기는 무슨 짓을 했다고 입증하기란 불가능했다.\n\n그러나 불스트로드에 관한 이 잡담은 불 냄새처럼 미들마치 전역으로 퍼져 나갔다. 프랭크 홀리 씨는 자기가 신뢰할 수 있는 서기를 건초에 관해 물어본다는 구실로 스톤 코트에 보냈으니, 실은 에이블 부인에게서 래플스와 그의 병에 관해 알아낼 수 있는 모든 것을 캐내려는 것이었다. 이런 식으로 그는 가스 씨가 그 사내를 자기 이륜마차에 태워 스톤 코트로 데려갔다는 사실을 알게 되었다. 그 결과 홀리 씨는 케일럽을 만날 기회를 잡았으니, 그의 사무실에 들러 필요하다면 중재를 맡아 줄 시간이 있는지 묻고 나서, 지나가는 말처럼 래플스에 관해 물었다. 케일럽은 지난 한 주 사이에 불스트로드를 위한 일을 그만두었다는, 인정하지 않을 수 없었던 사실 외에는 불스트로드에게 해가 될 만한 말은 한마디도 내비치지 않았다. 홀리 씨는 나름의 추론을 끌어냈고, 래플스가 가스에게 자기 이야기를 했으며 그 결과 가스가 불스트로드의 일을 그만두었다고 확신하여, 몇 시간 뒤 톨러 씨에게 그렇게 말했다. 그 진술은 이 사람 저 사람을 거치며 추론의 자취를 완전히 잃어버린 채, 가스에게서 곧장 나온 정보로 받아들여졌으니, 그리하여 부지런한 역사가라 해도 케일럽이 불스트로드의 비행을 퍼뜨린 주범이라고 결론지을 만했다.\n\n홀리 씨는 래플스가 폭로한 내용에서든 그의 죽음을 둘러싼 정황에서든 법으로 걸고넘어질 만한 구실이 없다는 것을 알아채는 데 더디지 않았다. 그는 등기부를 확인하고 이 일 전체를 페어브러더 씨와 의논하려고 직접 로윅 마을까지 말을 타고 갔으니, 페어브러더 씨는 불스트로드에 관해 흉한 비밀이 드러났다는 사실에 이 변호사 못지않게 놀라기는 했으나, 자신의 반감이 결론으로 굳어지는 것을 막을 만한 공정함은 늘 지니고 있었던 터였다. 그러나 그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안, 페어브러더 씨의 마음속에서는 또 다른 조합이 조용히 진행되고 있었으니, 그것은 머지않아 미들마치에서 필연적으로 \"둘에 둘을 더하는\" 일이라고 시끄럽게 거론될 바를 예고하고 있었다. 불스트로드가 래플스를 두려워하게 만든 이유들과 더불어, 그 두려움이 그가 주치의에게 베푼 후한 인심과 무슨 관련이 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 번쩍 스쳐 갔다. 그리고 그것이 어떤 식으로든 의식적으로 뇌물로 받아들여졌다는 추측에는 저항했지만, 이렇게 얽힌 정황이 리드게이트의 평판에 악의적인 영향을 미칠지도 모른다는 불길한 예감이 들었다. 그는 홀리 씨가 갑작스러운 빚 청산에 관해서는 현재 아무것도 모른다는 것을 알아챘고, 자신은 그 주제로 다가가는 모든 접근을 조심스럽게 비껴 갔다.\n\n\"음,\" 그는 법적으로는 아무것도 입증될 수 없으나 일어났을지도 모르는 일에 관한 끝없는 논의를 마무리 짓고 싶어서, 깊은 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"참 묘한 이야기로군. 우리의 변덕스러운 래디슬로가 그렇게 기묘한 혈통을 지녔다니! 기개 넘치는 아가씨와 음악가인 폴란드 애국지사라면 그가 나올 만한 그럴듯한 혈통이지만, 유대인 전당포 주인이 접목되어 있을 줄은 결코 의심하지 못했겠지요. 하지만 섞인 것이 어떻게 나올지는 미리 알 도리가 없는 법. 어떤 종류의 흙은 오히려 정화하는 데 쓰이기도 하지요.\"\n\n\"바로 내가 예상했던 바요.\" 홀리 씨가 말에 오르며 말했다. \"유대인이든 코르시카인이든 집시든, 빌어먹을 이방인의 피란 다 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그가 당신네 검은 양 가운데 하나인 건 알지요, 홀리. 하지만 그는 정말이지 사심 없고 세속을 모르는 친구요.\" 페어브러더 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그게 당신의 휘그당식 비틀린 생각이오.\" 홀리 씨가 말했는데, 그는 페어브러더가 어찌나 빌어먹게 유쾌하고 마음씨 좋은 친구인지 토리당원으로 착각할 지경이라고 변명하듯 말하곤 했던 터였다.\n\n홀리 씨는 리드게이트가 래플스를 돌본 일을 불스트로드에게 불리한 한 가지 증거로밖에는 달리 생각하지 않은 채 집으로 말을 몰았다. 그러나 리드게이트가 별안간 자기 집에 들어왔던 압류를 면했을 뿐 아니라 미들마치의 빚을 모두 갚을 수 있게 되었다는 소식이 빠르게 퍼져 나가면서, 그 주위로 갖가지 억측과 논평이 모여들어 새로운 살이 붙고 추진력을 얻었으니, 곧 홀리 씨 외의 다른 사람들의 귀에도 가득 차게 되었고, 그들은 이 갑작스러운 자금 동원력과 래플스에 관한 추문을 잠재우려는 불스트로드의 욕망 사이에 의미심장한 연관이 있음을 알아채는 데 더디지 않았다. 그 돈이 불스트로드에게서 나왔다는 것은, 설령 그것을 직접 증명할 증거가 없었더라도 어김없이 짐작되었을 것이다. 그의 장인도 그 자신의 가족도 그를 위해 아무것도 해 주지 않으리라는 사실이 리드게이트의 형편에 관한 잡담에 이미 들어 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 직접적인 증거가 은행 서기뿐 아니라 순진한 불스트로드 부인 본인에게서도 나왔으니, 그녀가 그 대출 이야기를 플림데일 부인에게 했고, 플림데일 부인은 그것을 톨러 가문에 시집간 며느리에게 했으며, 그 며느리는 그것을 두루 퍼뜨렸던 것이다. 이 일은 워낙 공공연하고 중대하게 여겨졌기에 그것을 떠받칠 만찬이 필요했고, 마침 그 무렵 불스트로드와 리드게이트에 관한 이 추문을 발판으로 숱한 초대장이 발송되고 수락되었다. 부인들과 과부들과 미혼 여성들이 평소보다 더 자주 일감을 들고 차를 마시러 나갔다. 그리고 그린 드래건에서 돌롭스 술집에 이르기까지 모든 공공의 흥겨운 자리는, 상원이 개혁 법안을 부결시킬 것인가 하는 문제로는 결코 얻을 수 없는 활기를 띠게 되었다.\n* 개혁 법안: 1832년 선거법 개정안.\n\n불스트로드가 리드게이트에게 베푼 후한 인심의 밑바닥에 추잡한 이유가 있다는 것을 의심하는 사람은 거의 없었으니 말이다. 실제로 홀리 씨는 우선 두 내과 의사와 톨러 씨, 렌치 씨를 포함한 엄선된 일행을 초대했으니, 래플스의 병에 관한 개연성을 면밀히 따지자는 명백한 취지에서였다. 그는 리드게이트의 진단서―사인이 진전 섬망이라는―와 관련하여 에이블 부인에게서 수집한 세부 사항을 그들에게 죄다 들려주었다. 그러나 이 병에 관해 모두 옛 방식을 흔들림 없이 고수하는 의료계 신사들은, 이 세부 사항 가운데 명확한 의심의 근거로 바뀔 만한 것은 아무것도 찾을 수 없다고 단언했다. 그러나 도덕적인 의심의 근거는 남아 있었다. 불스트로드가 래플스를 떼어 버리고 싶어 할 강력한 동기가 분명히 있었다는 점, 그리고 이 결정적인 순간에 그가 리드게이트에게, 그가 얼마간 전부터 필요로 한다는 것을 분명 알고 있었을 도움을 주었다는 사실, 게다가 불스트로드라면 양심의 거리낌이 없으리라고 믿으려는 성향, 또한 리드게이트라 해도 다른 거만한 사내들이 돈에 궁할 때 그러하듯 쉽사리 매수당할 수 있으리라고 믿는 데 아무런 거리낌이 없다는 점이 그것이었다. 설령 그 돈이 그저 불스트로드의 지난 삶에 관한 추문에 대해 입을 다물게 하려고 주어진 것이라 해도, 그 사실은 리드게이트에게 추악한 빛을 드리웠으니, 그는 스스로 우위를 차지하고 동업계의 연장자들을 깎아내리려는 속셈으로 은행가에게 굽실거린다고 오래도록 비웃음을 사 왔던 터였다. 그리하여 스톤 코트의 죽음과 관련하여 죄를 입증할 직접적인 흔적이 전혀 없다는 결론에도 불구하고, 홀리 씨가 엄선한 일행은 이 일에 \"흉한 구석\"이 있다는 느낌을 품은 채 헤어졌다.\n\n그러나 이렇게 막연하면서도 단정할 수 없는 죄의 확신은, 점잖은 직업계 원로들 사이에서조차 숱한 고갯짓과 신랄한 암시를 계속 불러일으키기에 충분했거니와, 일반 사람들의 마음에는 사실에 대한 신비의 온갖 우월한 위력을 발휘했다. 사람들은 그 일이 어떠했는지 그저 아는 것보다 추측하는 것을 누구나 더 좋아했다. 추측은 곧 지식보다 더 자신만만해졌고, 양립할 수 없는 것에 대해서도 더 너그러운 여지를 베풀었기 때문이다. 불스트로드의 지난 삶에 관한 더 분명한 추문조차도, 어떤 이들의 마음속에서는 신비의 덩어리 속으로 녹아들었으니, 마치 대화 속에 부어져 하늘이 빚고 싶은 대로 갖가지 환상적인 형상을 띠게 될, 그만큼 활기 넘치는 쇳물 같았다.\n\n이것이 슬로터 골목에 있는 탱커드 술집의 기개 넘치는 안주인 돌롭 부인이 주로 옹호하는 사고방식이었으니, 그녀는 바깥세상에서 들어온 자기들의 소식이 그녀 머릿속에 \"떠오른\" 것과 똑같은 위력을 지닌다고 여기려 드는 손님들의 얄팍한 실용주의에 자주 맞서야 했다. 그것이 어떻게 자기에게 다가왔는지 그녀는 알지 못했으나, 그것은 마치 \"굴뚝 판자에 분필로 적힌\" 것처럼 그녀 앞에 떡하니 있었다―불스트로드로 말하자면, \"그 속이 어찌나 시커먼지 제 머리카락이 제 마음속 생각을 알기라도 한다면 뿌리째 다 뽑아 버릴 위인\"이라는 것이었다.\n\n\"그것참 묘하군요.\" 약한 눈에 가느다란 목소리를 지닌, 사색적인 구두장이 림프 씨가 말했다. \"아니, 내가 『트럼펫』에서 읽기로는, 웰링턴 공작이 변절해서 로마 가톨릭 쪽으로 넘어갔을 때 한 말이 바로 그거였는데.\"\n\n\"그럴 만도 하지요.\" 돌롭 부인이 말했다. \"악당 하나가 그런 말을 했다면, 다른 악당이 그러는 게 더 당연하지 않겠소. 하지만 그자가 위선자 노릇을 하면서, 나라 안에 자기한테 어울릴 만큼 훌륭한 목사가 없다는 듯 그렇게 거만하게 굴더니, 결국 늙은 마왕을 제 모사로 끌어들일 수밖에 없었던 게요. 한데 그 늙은 마왕이 그자보다 한 수 위였던 거지.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 그놈은 이 나라 밖으로 내쫓을 수 없는 공범이지요.\" 유리장이 크래브 씨가 말했는데, 그는 온갖 소식을 그러모으되 그 속을 어렴풋이 더듬을 뿐인 사람이었다. \"하지만 내가 알아낸 바로는, 불스트로드가 진작부터 들통날까 봐 도망치려 했다고 말하는 이들이 있다더군요.\"\n\n\"내쫓기게 될 거요, 그러든 안 그러든.\" 방금 들어선 이발사 딜 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 아침에 홀리 씨네 서기인 플레처를 면도해 줬는데―손가락을 다쳤더군요―그 사람 말이, 다들 한마음으로 불스트로드를 쫓아낼 작정이랍디다. 세시저 씨도 그자에게 등을 돌려서 교구에서 내보내고 싶어 한다는 거예요. 또 이 읍내에 죄수선에서 나온 작자랑 차라리 함께 식사하겠다는 신사들이 있다더군요. '나라면 차라리 그게 훨씬 낫겠소.' 플레처가 그러더라고요. '제 종교로 사람을 불편하게 만들면서, 자기한테는 십계명만으로는 부족하다고 떠벌리는 작자보다 더 비위 상하는 게 어디 있겠소? 게다가 그자는 그러는 내내 디딜방아 죄수의 절반보다도 더 나쁜 놈인데 말이오.' 플레처가 직접 그렇게 말했소이다.\"\n\n\"그래도 불스트로드의 돈이 이 읍내를 빠져나가면, 이 동네엔 나쁜 일이 될 텐데요.\" 림프 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"아, 더 훌륭한 사람들도 제 돈을 더 나쁘게 쓰는 법이지요.\" 진홍빛 손이 사람 좋아 보이는 얼굴과 어울리지 않는 한 염색공이 단호한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 알아낸 바로는, 그자는 제 돈을 지키지 못할 거요.\" 유리장이가 말했다. \"그자한테서 돈을 다 벗겨 낼 수 있는 누군가가 있다고들 하지 않소? 내가 알기로는, 소송을 건다면 그자한테서 한 푼도 남김없이 빼앗을 수 있다던데.\"\n\n\"그런 일은 없소!\" 이발사가 말했는데, 그는 돌롭스에서 자기가 좌중보다 조금 격이 높다고 느끼면서도 그것을 싫어하지는 않았다. \"플레처 말로는 그런 일은 없답디다. 이 젊은 래디슬로가 누구 자식인지 몇 번이고 증명할 수 있다 해도, 내가 펜스 늪지대 출신이라는 걸 증명하는 것 이상은 못 한다는 거예요―한 푼도 건드릴 수 없다는 거지요.\"\n\n\"거 보시오!\" 돌롭 부인이 분개하여 말했다. \"어미 없는 자식에게 법이 해 줄 수 있는 게 고작 그거라면, 내 아이들을 하느님께서 데려가신 것을 감사할 따름이오. 그렇다면 그 말대로라면, 네 아비가 누구고 어미가 누구인지는 아무 소용이 없다는 거잖소. 한데 변호사 하나가 하는 말을 다른 변호사한테 물어보지도 않고 그냥 곧이듣다니―당신처럼 영리한 양반이 그러다니 놀랍군요, 딜 씨. 무슨 일이든 늘 양쪽이 있다는 건, 더 많지는 않더라도, 누구나 다 아는 사실이오. 그렇지 않다면 대체 누가 소송을 걸겠소, 내 묻고 싶구려? 이러쿵저러쿵 법이란 게 그렇게 많은데, 네가 누구 자식인지 증명하는 게 아무 소용이 없다면 그거야말로 한심한 노릇이지. 플레처가 그러고 싶으면 그러라지요. 하지만 나한테는 플레처고 뭐고 들먹이지 마시오!\"\n\n딜 씨는 돌롭 부인이 변호사들도 능히 당해 낼 만한 여자라며 추켜세우는 척 웃었으니, 자기에게 오래 쌓인 외상값을 가진 안주인에게서 어지간한 놀림은 감수하려는 마음에서였다.\n\n\"그자들이 소송에 들어가고, 사람들 말이 죄다 사실이라면, 돈보다 더 따져 봐야 할 게 있소.\" 유리장이가 말했다. \"이렇게 죽어 없어진 가엾은 작자 말이오. 내가 알아낸 바로는, 그 사람도 한때는 불스트로드보다 훨씬 더 훌륭한 신사였던 시절이 있었다는데.\"\n\n\"훨씬 더 훌륭한 신사였다고요! 그야 틀림없지요.\" 돌롭 부인이 말했다. \"내가 들은 바로는 풍채도 훨씬 더 그럴듯한 사람이었다는데. 세리 볼드윈 씨가 지금 당신이 앉은 자리에 떡 버티고 서서 '불스트로드는 이 읍내에 가져온 돈을 죄다 도둑질하고 사기 쳐서 모았다'고 했을 때, 내가 그랬소이다. '당신 덕에 더 알게 된 건 없소, 볼드윈 씨. 그자가 여기 슬로터 골목에 들어와 내 머리 위의 이 집을 사려고 했던 그때부터, 그자만 봤다 하면 내 피가 다 오싹해진단 말이오. 멀쩡한 사람이 빵 반죽 통 같은 낯빛을 하고서, 마치 당신 등뼈 속까지 들여다보고 싶다는 듯 공연히 빤히 쳐다보지는 않는 법이거든.' 내가 그렇게 말했고, 볼드윈 씨가 증인이 되어 줄 게요.\"\n\n\"그것도 옳은 말씀이오.\" 크래브 씨가 말했다. \"내가 알아낸 바로는, 사람들이 래플스라 부르는 이 사내는 보기에 좋을 만큼 혈색 좋고 우람한 사람이었고, 더없이 좋은 술친구였답디다―물론 지금이야 로윅 교회 묘지에 분명히 죽어 누워 있지만요. 그리고 내가 알기로는, 그자가 어떻게 거기 묻히게 됐는지를 마땅히 알아야 할 것보다 더 많이 아는 이들이 있다는 거요.\"\n\n\"누가 아니래요!\" 돌롭 부인이 크래브 씨의 빤한 둔함을 약간 비웃으며 말했다. \"사내 하나가 외딴집으로 꾀여 들어갔는데, 이 동네 절반의 병원이며 간호사 값을 다 댈 수 있는 이들이 밤낮으로 곁을 지키겠다고 나서고, 가까이 오는 이라곤 무슨 짓이든 서슴지 않기로 소문난, 게다가 빌어먹게 가난뱅이인 의사 하나뿐이더니, 그 일이 있고 나서는 그자가 어찌나 돈이 넉넉해졌는지 작년 미카엘 축일 한 해 전부터 좋은 고기 외상값이 쌓인 정육점 주인 바일스 씨한테 그걸 죄다 갚을 수 있게 됐다 하면―기도서에 그에 맞는 예식이 있는 것 이상의 무슨 일이 벌어졌다고, 나한테 누가 와서 일러 주지 않아도 다 안단 말이오―나는 눈을 끔벅끔벅하며 생각에 잠겨 있을 사람이 아니거든.\"\n\n* 미카엘 축일: 9월 29일.\n돌롭 부인은 좌중을 휘어잡는 데 익숙한 안주인다운 태도로 둘러보았다. 더 용감한 이들에게서 동조의 합창이 일었다. 그러나 림프 씨는 한 모금 들이켜고 나서, 두 손바닥을 마주 모아 무릎 사이에 꽉 끼우고는, 마치 돌롭 부인의 이글거리는 말솜씨가 그의 재치를 바싹 말려 무력하게 만들어, 더 많은 수분으로 다시 적셔질 때까지는 회복되지 못하리라는 듯, 흐릿한 눈으로 그 손을 내려다보았다.\n\n\"그자를 파내서 검시관을 부르면 안 될 게 뭐요?\" 염색공이 말했다. \"그런 일은 숱하게 있었잖소. 부정한 짓이 있었다면 밝혀낼 수도 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"못 밝혀내요, 조너스 씨!\" 돌롭 부인이 힘주어 말했다. \"내가 의사들이 어떤 작자들인지 잘 아오. 밝혀내기엔 너무도 교활한 자들이지요. 게다가 이 리드게이트 박사라는 자는 숨이 채 끊어지기도 전에 사람들을 죄다 갈라 보려고 안달이었으니―점잖은 사람들의 속을 들여다보려는 게 무슨 속셈이었는지 뻔하지 않소. 그자는 약을 잘 알지요, 틀림없이, 삼키기 전에도 후에도 냄새도 안 나고 보이지도 않는 약을 말이오. 아니, 나도 우리 공제회 의사인 갬빗 박사가―평판도 좋고 미들마치의 그 누구보다 더 많은 아이를 살려서 세상에 데려온 분이지만―처방한 물약을 직접 본 적이 있는데, 글쎄, 잔에 들었든 안 들었든 아무 차이도 없는 물약이 이튿날 사람 배를 쥐어뜯더란 말이오. 그러니 당신네 분별에 맡겨 판단하시구려. 나한테 이러쿵저러쿵 마시오! 내가 하고 싶은 말은, 이 리드게이트 박사를 우리 공제회에 들이지 않은 게 천만다행이라는 거요. 안 그랬으면 어느 어미의 자식이 후회할 뻔했지.\"\n\n\"돌롭스\"에서 이렇게 오간 논의의 골자는 읍내 모든 계층의 공통된 화제가 되어, 한쪽으로는 로윅 목사관까지, 다른 쪽으로는 팁턴 그레인지까지 실려 갔고, 빈시 가족의 귀에도 죄다 들어갔으며, 불스트로드 부인의 친구들은 모두 \"가엾은 해리엇\"을 들먹이며 슬픈 마음으로 그것을 입에 올렸으니, 리드게이트는 사람들이 왜 자기를 이상한 눈으로 쳐다보는지 분명히 알기 전이었고, 불스트로드 자신도 자기 비밀이 누설된 것을 의심하기 전이었다. 그는 이웃들과 그다지 다정한 관계를 맺어 온 적이 없었으니, 그래서 다정함의 신호가 사라진 것을 알아챌 수가 없었다. 게다가 그는 이제 미들마치를 떠날 필요가 없다고 마음을 굳혔던 터라 여러 가지 용무로 여행을 다니던 참이었고, 그리하여 전에는 미결로 남겨 두었던 문제들을 결정할 수 있다고 느끼고 있었다.\n\n\"한두 달 안으로 첼튼엄에 다녀옵시다.\" 그는 아내에게 말했다. \"그 읍내는 공기며 광천수와 더불어 영적으로도 크게 얻을 것이 있는 곳이니, 거기서 여섯 주를 지내면 우리에게 더없이 상쾌한 휴식이 될 거요.\"\n\n그는 정말로 그 영적인 이로움을 믿었고, 앞으로 자기 삶이 더욱 헌신적이어야 하리라 작정했으니, 가정형으로 그 용서를 빌며―\"만약 내가 이 일에 죄를 지었다면\"―스스로 가정적인 것이라 여기던 그 후일의 죄들 때문이었다.\n\n병원에 관해서는, 래플스가 죽자마자 너무 갑작스럽게 계획을 바꾸는 것처럼 보일까 두려워, 리드게이트에게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 마음속 깊은 곳에서, 리드게이트가 자기 지시가 고의로 지켜지지 않았음을 의심하고 있으며, 이를 의심한다면 그 동기까지도 의심하리라고 믿었다. 그러나 래플스의 내력에 관해 그에게 누설된 것은 아무것도 없었고, 불스트로드는 자기의 막연한 의심에 무게를 실어 줄 만한 일은 어떤 것도 하지 않으려고 노심초사했다. 어떤 특정한 치료법이 사람을 살리거나 죽이리라는 그 어떤 확실성에 대해서도, 리드게이트 자신이 늘 그런 독단에 반대하는 논리를 펴 왔으니, 그는 말할 자격이 없었고, 또 침묵해야 할 온갖 동기가 있었다. 그리하여 불스트로드는 자신이 섭리에 의해 보호받고 있다고 느꼈다. 그가 강하게 움찔했던 유일한 사건은 이따금 케일럽 가스와 마주친 일이었는데, 그러나 케일럽은 온화하고 진중하게 모자를 들어 올려 인사했을 뿐이었다.\n\n한편 읍내의 주요 인사들 사이에서는 그에 대한 강력한 결의가 자라나고 있었다.\n\n읍내에 콜레라 환자가 발생하여 급박하게 중요해진 위생 문제를 두고 읍사무소에서 회의가 열릴 예정이었다. 위생 조치를 위한 부과금을 승인하는 의회 법안이 서둘러 통과된 이래, 미들마치에는 그러한 조치를 감독하는 위원회가 임명되었고, 휘그당과 토리당이 의견을 모아 많은 청소와 준비 작업이 이루어졌다. 이제 문제는 읍내 외곽의 한 부지를 부과금으로 확보하여 묘지로 쓸 것인가, 아니면 개인의 기부로 할 것인가 하는 것이었다. 회의는 공개로 열릴 예정이었고, 읍내에서 중요한 인물이라면 거의 모두가 거기 참석하리라 예상되었다.\n\n불스트로드 씨는 위원회의 일원이었으니, 정오가 되기 직전에 개인 기부 방안을 강력히 주장할 작정으로 은행을 나섰다. 그동안 자기 계획이 흔들리는 통에 한동안 뒤로 물러나 있었던 그는, 자신이 여생을 마치리라 기대하는 이 읍내의 공적인 일에서, 오늘 아침에는 행동력과 영향력을 갖춘 사람으로서 옛 지위를 되찾으리라 느꼈다. 같은 방향으로 가던 여러 사람들 가운데서 그는 리드게이트를 보았다. 두 사람은 합류하여, 회의의 목적을 두고 이야기를 나누며 함께 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n내로라하는 사람들은 모두 그들보다 먼저 온 듯했다. 그러나 커다란 중앙 탁자의 상석 가까이에는 아직 빈자리가 남아 있어서, 그들은 그쪽으로 나아갔다. 페어브러더 씨는 맞은편, 홀리 씨에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 앉아 있었다. 의료계 인사들은 모두 와 있었다. 세시저 씨가 의장석에 앉았고, 팁턴의 브룩 씨가 그의 오른편에 있었다.\n\n리드게이트는 자기와 불스트로드가 자리에 앉자 묘한 눈길이 오가는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n의장이 궁극적으로 공동묘지로 쓸 만큼 넓은 부지를 기부로 매입하는 것의 이점을 지적하며 안건을 충분히 개진한 뒤, 이런 종류의 회의에서 다소 높지만 차분하고 유창한 그의 목소리에 읍내가 익숙해져 있던 불스트로드 씨가 일어나 자기 의견을 말할 수 있도록 허락을 청했다. 홀리 씨가 벌떡 일어나 단호하고 우렁찬 목소리로 말하기 전에, 리드게이트는 다시 한 번 그 묘한 눈길이 오가는 것을 볼 수 있었다. \"의장님, 이 문제에 관해 누가 의견을 말하기에 앞서, 저뿐 아니라 여기 참석한 여러 신사분들이 선결 사항으로 여기는 공적 정서의 문제에 관해 제가 말할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 청합니다.\"\n\n홀리 씨의 말투는, 공적인 예의가 그의 \"끔찍한 언사\"를 억누를 때조차도, 그 간결함과 침착함이 만만치 않았다. 세시저 씨가 그 청을 받아들였고, 불스트로드 씨는 자리에 앉았으며, 홀리 씨는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제가 드릴 말씀은, 의장님, 단지 저 자신을 위해 드리는 것이 아닙니다. 저는 바로 우리 주위에 있는, 적어도 여덟 명에 이르는 동료 읍민들의 동의를 얻어, 또 그들의 명백한 요청에 따라 말씀드리는 것입니다. 불스트로드 씨가 단지 납세자로서가 아니라 신사들 가운데 한 신사로서 맡고 있는 공직에서 물러날 것을 요구해야 한다는 것이 우리의 한결같은 정서입니다. 그리고 저는 지금 그에게 그것을 요구하는 바입니다. 정황상 법이 단죄할 수는 없으나, 법적으로 처벌 가능한 많은 일들보다 더 나쁠 수도 있는 관행과 행위가 있습니다. 정직한 사람들과 신사들은, 그런 행위를 저지르는 자들과 어울리기를 원치 않는다면, 스스로 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 자신을 지켜야 합니다. 그리고 그것이야말로 저와, 이 일에서 제가 의뢰인이라 부를 만한 친구들이 하기로 결심한 바입니다. 저는 불스트로드 씨가 수치스러운 행위를 저질렀다고 말하는 것이 아닙니다. 다만 그에게 요구하건대, 지금은 죽었고 그의 집에서 죽은 한 사내가 그에게 퍼부은 추잡한 진술―그가 여러 해 동안 사악한 짓을 저질렀으며 부정한 수단으로 재산을 모았다는 진술―을 공개적으로 부인하고 논박하든지, 아니면 신사들 가운데 한 신사로서가 아니고는 결코 허락될 수 없었던 그 지위에서 물러나든지 하라는 것입니다.\"\n\n방 안의 모든 눈길이 불스트로드 씨에게 쏠렸으니, 그는 자기 이름이 처음 거론된 순간부터, 그 가냘픈 몸으로는 거의 견디기 힘들 만큼 격렬한 감정의 위기를 겪고 있었다. 어떤 희미한 전조의 무시무시한 실제적 해석을 마주한 듯 충격을 받고 있던 리드게이트조차도, 불스트로드의 핏기 없는 얼굴에 어린 쪼그라든 비참함을 바라보았을 때, 자기 안에서 일던 분노에 찬 증오의 움직임이, 무엇보다 먼저 고통받는 자에게 구원이나 위안을 가져다줄 것을 생각하는 치유자의 본능에 의해 제지당하는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n자기 삶이 결국 실패였다는 것, 자신이 불명예스러운 사람이며, 늘 책망하는 태도를 취해 왔던 그 사람들의 눈길 앞에서 움츠러들어야 한다는 것―하느님이 사람들 앞에서 그를 부인하고, 자기들의 증오가 정당화되어 기뻐하는 자들의 의기양양한 멸시에 그를 가린 것 없이 내버려 두었다는 것―공범의 목숨을 다루면서 양심을 속였던 그 기만이 부질없었다는 자각, 이제 드러난 거짓말의 다 자란 송곳니를 드러내며 독을 품고 그에게 덤벼드는 그 기만―이 모든 것이, 죽이지는 못한 채 귀만은 열어 둔 채로 되돌아오는 저주의 물결을 듣게 하는 공포의 고통처럼 그를 휩쓸고 지나갔다. 안전하다는 감각을 다시 확립한 뒤에 찾아온 갑작스러운 노출의 감각은, 범죄자의 거친 신경이 아니라, 자기 삶의 조건이 빚어낸 그러한 지배와 우위에 가장 강렬한 존재 의의를 두었던 한 사내의 예민한 신경에 들이닥쳤다.\n\n그러나 바로 그 강렬한 존재 의의 속에 반작용의 힘이 깃들어 있었다. 그의 온갖 육체적 허약함을 관통하여 야심에 찬 자기 보존의 의지가 질긴 신경처럼 흐르고 있었으니, 그것은 온갖 교리상의 두려움을 흩어 버리며 끊임없이 불꽃처럼 솟구쳐 올랐고, 자비로운 이들에게 동정의 대상이 되어 앉아 있는 동안에도 그의 잿빛 창백함 아래서 꿈틀거리며 타오르기 시작하고 있었다. 홀리 씨의 마지막 말이 입에서 채 떨어지기도 전에, 불스트로드는 자기가 대답하리라는 것을, 그리고 그 대답이 반박이 되리라는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 감히 일어나서 \"나는 죄가 없으며, 그 이야기는 죄다 거짓이오\"라고 말하지는 못했다―설령 그렇게 할 용기가 있었다 해도, 지금처럼 배신감을 예리하게 느끼는 그에게는, 벌거벗은 몸을 가리려고, 조금만 힘을 주어도 찢어질 연약한 누더기를 끌어당기는 것만큼이나 부질없게 여겨졌을 것이다.\n\n잠시 동안 완전한 침묵이 흘렀고, 방 안의 모든 사람이 불스트로드를 바라보았다. 그는 의자 등받이에 몸을 힘껏 기댄 채 완전히 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그는 감히 일어날 엄두를 내지 못했고, 입을 열기 시작했을 때는 양옆의 의자 좌석을 두 손으로 눌렀다. 그러나 그의 목소리는 평소보다 쉬기는 했으나 또렷이 들렸고, 마치 숨이 가쁜 듯 문장 사이사이에 멈추기는 했으나 그의 말은 분명하게 발음되었다. 그는 먼저 세시저 씨 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다가 홀리 씨를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"기독교 목사이신 당신 앞에서, 저는 항의합니다. 악독한 증오에 휘둘린 저에 대한 이 절차를 용인하는 데 항의하는 바입니다. 저에게 적의를 품은 자들은 함부로 놀리는 혀가 저에 대해 내뱉은 어떤 비방이든 기꺼이 믿으려 합니다. 그리고 그들의 양심은 유독 저에게만 엄격해집니다. 제가 그 희생양이 될 그 험담이 저를 비행으로 고발한다고 칩시다―\" 여기서 불스트로드의 목소리가 높아지며 더 신랄한 어조를 띠어, 마침내 낮은 절규처럼 들렸다. \"누가 저의 고발자가 되겠습니까? 제 삶이 비기독교적이고, 아니 추문투성이인 자들은 아닐 테지요―제 목적을 이루려고 비열한 수단을 동원하는 자들도 아닐 테지요―그 직업이 협잡으로 짜인 자들도 아닐 테지요―제 수입을 제 육욕의 향락에 탕진해 온 자들도 아닐 테지요. 그러는 동안 저는 제 수입을 이승과 저승에 관한 최선의 목적을 위해 바쳐 왔습니다.\"\n\n협잡이라는 말이 나오자, 절반은 웅성거림이요 절반은 야유인 소음이 점점 커졌고, 동시에 네 사람이 벌떡 일어났다―홀리 씨, 톨러 씨, 치철리 씨, 그리고 해크벗 씨가. 그러나 홀리 씨의 폭발은 즉각적이어서, 나머지 사람들을 침묵 속에 뒤처지게 했다.\n\n\"저를 두고 하는 말이라면, 선생, 저는 당신을 비롯한 누구든 제 직업적 삶을 검증해 보라고 촉구하는 바입니다. 기독교적이냐 비기독교적이냐 하는 데 대해서는, 저는 당신의 위선적이고 입에 발린 기독교를 거부합니다. 그리고 제가 수입을 쓰는 방식으로 말하자면, 종교를 떠받치고 저 자신을 성스러운 흥취 깨는 자로 내세우려고 도둑들을 거두어 먹이고 자식들에게서 정당한 상속을 가로채는 것은 제 원칙이 아닙니다. 저는 양심의 결벽 따위는 가장하지 않습니다―저는 당신의 행위를 재기 위해 무슨 까다로운 잣대가 필요하다고 아직 여겨 본 적이 없소이다, 선생. 그리고 다시 한 번 당신에게 촉구하건대, 당신에 대한 추문에 관해 만족스러운 해명을 내놓든지, 아니면 우리가 어쨌든 동료로 받아들이기를 거부하는 그 지위에서 물러나든지 하시오. 선생, 저는 말하건대, 우리는 소문뿐 아니라 최근의 행위로도 추악한 빛이 드리워진, 그 인격이 결백하게 밝혀지지 않은 사람과는 협력하기를 거부합니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 홀리 씨.\" 의장이 말하자, 홀리 씨는 여전히 분을 삭이지 못한 채 다소 참을성 없이 고개를 숙이고는, 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣은 채 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"불스트로드 씨, 지금의 논의를 길게 끄는 것은 바람직하지 않다고 생각합니다.\" 세시저 씨가 창백하게 떨고 있는 사내를 향해 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"전반적인 정서를 표현한 홀리 씨의 말에, 저도 이만큼은 동의할 수밖에 없습니다. 즉, 당신의 기독교 신앙고백을 보아서라도, 가능하다면 이 불행한 중상으로부터 스스로를 결백하게 밝히는 것이 마땅하다고 생각합니다. 저로서는 기꺼이 당신에게 충분한 기회와 청문의 자리를 드리겠습니다. 그러나 분명히 말씀드리건대, 지금 당신의 태도는 당신이 스스로 일치시키고자 애써 온, 그리고 제가 그 명예를 마땅히 지켜야 할 그 원칙들과 고통스러울 만큼 어긋납니다. 저는 지금, 당신의 성직자로서, 또 당신이 다시 존경받는 자리로 회복되기를 바라는 한 사람으로서, 이 방을 떠나 더 이상 업무에 지장을 주지 마시기를 권합니다.\"\n\n불스트로드는 잠시 망설인 뒤, 바닥에서 모자를 집어 들고 천천히 일어났으나, 의자 모서리를 어찌나 위태롭게 부여잡았는지 리드게이트는 그에게 부축 없이 걸어 나갈 만한 힘이 남아 있지 않다고 확신했다. 그가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그는 한 사람이 도움이 없어 자기 바로 곁에서 쓰러지는 것을 보고만 있을 수는 없었다. 그는 일어나서 불스트로드에게 팔을 내주었고, 그렇게 그를 부축하여 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다. 그러나 부드러운 의무이자 순수한 연민의 행위가 될 수도 있었을 이 행동이, 이 순간 그에게는 이루 말할 수 없이 쓰라렸다. 마치 자기와 불스트로드를 한데 엮는 그 연관에 친필 서명을 하는 것만 같았으니, 그는 이제 그것이 다른 사람들의 마음에 어떻게 비쳤을지, 그 온전한 의미를 깨달았던 것이다. 그는 이제 자기 팔에 떨며 기댄 이 사내가 자기에게 천 파운드를 뇌물로 주었으며, 어떤 사악한 동기에서 래플스의 치료가 어떤 식으로든 조작되었다는 확신을 느꼈다. 그 추론들은 충분히 긴밀하게 연결되어 있었으니, 읍내는 그 대출을 알았고, 그것을 뇌물이라 믿었으며, 그가 그것을 뇌물로 받았다고 믿었던 것이다.\n\n가엾은 리드게이트는, 이 폭로의 무시무시한 손아귀 아래 마음이 몸부림치는 내내, 도덕적으로 불스트로드 씨를 은행으로 데려가, 사람을 보내 그의 마차를 부르고, 그를 집까지 바래다주려고 기다릴 수밖에 없었다.\n\n한편 회의의 업무는 처리되었고, 불스트로드와―그리고 리드게이트에 관한 이 일을 두고 여러 무리 사이에서 벌어진 열띤 논의로 가닥가닥 흩어졌다.\n\n전에는 이 일에 관해 불완전한 암시만 들었을 뿐이며, 자기가 불스트로드를 두둔하는 데 \"조금 지나쳤다\"는 것이 몹시 불안했던 브룩 씨는, 이제 자초지종을 죄다 알게 되었고, 리드게이트가 그토록 추한 시선을 받게 된 처지를 두고 페어브러더 씨와 이야기하면서 어떤 자비로운 슬픔을 느꼈다. 페어브러더 씨는 로윅까지 걸어서 돌아가려던 참이었다.\n\n\"내 마차에 타시지요.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"나는 캐소본 부인을 보러 돌아가는 길이오. 그 애가 어젯밤 요크셔에서 돌아오기로 되어 있었거든. 그 애가 나를 보면 좋아할 거요, 알다시피.\"\n\n그리하여 그들은 마차를 몰고 갔으니, 브룩 씨는 리드게이트의 행실에 정말로 시커먼 구석이 있었던 것은 아니리라는 마음씨 좋은 희망을 품고 이야기를 늘어놓았다―그가 자기 숙부 고드윈 경의 편지를 가져왔을 때, 브룩 씨는 그 젊은이가 여느 사람보다 훨씬 뛰어나다는 것을 알아보았던 터였다. 페어브러더 씨는 말을 거의 하지 않았다. 그는 깊이 침울했다. 인간의 약점을 예리하게 통찰하는 그로서는, 굴욕적인 궁핍의 압박 아래서 리드게이트가 자기 자신보다 못한 데로 떨어지지 않았으리라고 확신할 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n마차가 저택의 문 앞에 다다랐을 때, 도러시아는 자갈길에 나와 있다가 그들을 맞으러 다가왔다.\n\n\"음, 얘야,\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 방금 회의에서 오는 길이란다―위생 회의 말이다, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"리드게이트 씨도 거기 계셨나요?\" 도러시아가 말했는데, 그녀는 건강과 생기가 넘치는 모습으로, 반짝이는 4월의 햇살 아래 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 서 있었다. \"그분을 만나서 병원에 관해 크게 의논하고 싶어요. 그러기로 불스트로드 씨와 약속했거든요.\"\n\n\"오, 얘야,\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"우리가 나쁜 소식을 들었단다―나쁜 소식 말이다, 알다시피.\"\n\n그들은 정원을 가로질러 교회 묘지 문 쪽으로 걸어갔으니, 페어브러더 씨가 목사관까지 계속 가려던 참이었다. 그리고 도러시아는 그 슬픈 이야기를 죄다 들었다.\n\n그녀는 깊은 관심을 가지고 귀를 기울였고, 리드게이트에 관한 사실과 인상들을 두 번이나 들려 달라고 청했다. 잠시 침묵한 뒤, 교회 묘지 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 페어브러더 씨를 향해, 그녀는 힘차게 말했다.\n\n\"리드게이트 씨가 무슨 비열한 짓을 저질렀다고는 믿지 않으시지요? 저는 믿지 않겠어요. 진실을 밝혀내어 그분의 결백을 입증합시다!\"\n\n# BOOK VIII. SUNSET AND SUNRISE. CHAPTER LXXII.\n\n뒤로는 지나간 것들을 되비춘다.\n\n리드게이트가 뇌물로 돈을 받았다는 의혹에서 그를 변호하려 단숨에 달려들었을 도로시아의 격정적인 관대함은, 페어브러더 씨의 경험에 비추어 이 사건의 모든 정황을 헤아리게 되자 우울한 제동에 부딪혔다.\n\n\"이건 건드리기에 미묘한 문제입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"어떻게 조사를 시작할 수 있겠습니까? 공개적으로 치안 판사와 검시관을 움직이게 하든가, 아니면 사적으로 리드게이트에게 캐묻든가 둘 중 하나여야 합니다. 첫 번째 방법은 근거로 삼을 확실한 토대가 없습니다. 그게 있었다면 홀리가 진작 그렇게 했겠지요. 그리고 리드게이트에게 그 화제를 꺼내는 일이라면, 솔직히 저는 망설여집니다. 그는 아마 그것을 치명적인 모욕으로 받아들일 겁니다. 개인적인 문제로 그에게 말을 거는 어려움을 저는 한두 번 겪은 게 아닙니다. 그리고 좋은 결과를 확신하려면, 그의 행적에 대한 진실을 미리 알고 있어야 하지요.\"\n\n\"저는 그의 행동에 죄가 없다고 확신합니다. 사람은 거의 언제나 이웃이 생각하는 것보다 더 나은 존재라고 믿어요.\" 도로시아가 말했다. 지난 두 해 동안 겪은 가장 강렬한 경험들이 그녀의 마음을 타인에 대한 어떤 불리한 해석에도 강하게 맞서도록 굳혀 놓았다. 그리고 그녀는 처음으로 페어브러더 씨에게 다소 불만을 느꼈다. 그녀는 정의와 자비를 위한 노력에 대한 뜨거운 신념 대신 결과를 이렇게 신중히 저울질하는 태도가 못마땅했으니, 그 노력이야말로 감정의 힘으로 승리할 것이었기 때문이다. 이틀 뒤, 그는 영주관에서 그녀의 삼촌과 체텀 부부와 함께 식사를 하고 있었다. 디저트가 손도 대지 않은 채 놓여 있고 하인들은 방에서 나가 있었으며 브룩 씨는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있을 때, 그녀는 새로운 활기를 띠고 그 화제로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"리드게이트 씨도 자기 친구들이 자신에 관한 중상을 들으면 가장 먼저 그를 변호하고 싶어 하리라는 것을 이해할 거예요. 우리가 무엇을 위해 사나요, 서로의 삶을 덜 힘들게 해 주기 위해서가 아니라면? 제가 곤경에 처했을 때 조언해 주고 제가 병들었을 때 돌봐 준 사람의 어려움에 저는 무관심할 수 없어요.\"\n\n도로시아의 어조와 태도는 거의 삼 년 전 삼촌의 식탁 상석에 앉아 있을 때보다 더 격렬할 것도 없었으나, 그동안의 경험은 그녀에게 단호한 의견을 표명할 더 많은 자격을 주었다. 그러나 제임스 체텀 경은 더 이상 소심하고 순종적인 구혼자가 아니었다. 그는 이제 노심초사하는 처형의 매제로서, 처형을 향한 경건한 흠모를 품으면서도 그녀가 캐소본과 결혼한 것에 못지않게 나쁜 어떤 새로운 환상에 빠지지나 않을까 끊임없이 노심초사했다. 그는 훨씬 덜 웃었다. 그가 \"그렇지요\"라고 말할 때면 순종적인 독신 시절보다 반대 의견을 꺼내는 서두인 경우가 더 잦았다. 그래서 도로시아는 그를 두려워하지 않기로 마음먹어야 한다는 사실을 놀랍게도 깨달았으니, 그가 정말로 그녀의 가장 좋은 친구였기에 더욱 그러했다. 지금 그는 그녀의 의견에 반대했다.\n\n\"하지만 도로시아.\" 그가 타이르듯 말했다. \"그런 식으로 남의 인생을 대신 처리해 주겠다고 나설 수는 없어요. 리드게이트도 자기가 어떤 처지인지 알 거예요. 적어도 곧 알게 되겠지요. 자기 결백을 밝힐 수 있다면 그가 밝힐 겁니다. 그는 스스로 행동해야 해요.\"\n\n\"그의 친구들은 기회를 찾을 때까지 기다려야 한다고 봅니다.\" 페어브러더 씨가 덧붙였다. \"이런 일도 가능하지요. 저 자신에게서 그토록 많은 약점을 느껴 온 터라, 제가 늘 명예로운 기질의 사람이라 믿어 온 리드게이트 같은 사람조차도 그런 유혹에 굴복할 수 있다고 상상할 수 있습니다. 오래전에 지나간 추문에 대해 그가 침묵을 지키도록 보증하는 뇌물로, 다소 간접적으로 건네진 돈을 받는 유혹 말입니다. 다시 말하지만, 그가 가혹한 처지의 압박 아래 있었다면, 제가 분명 그러했으리라 확신하듯 그가 시달려 왔다면, 저는 이런 일을 상상할 수 있습니다. 엄격한 증거가 없는 한 저는 그에 대해 그보다 더 나쁜 어떤 것도 믿지 않겠습니다. 하지만 어떤 과오에는 무서운 응보가 따르기 마련이니, 그것을 죄로 해석하고 싶어 하는 자들에게는 언제나 그렇게 해석할 여지가 있다는 점입니다. 그 사람 자신의 양심과 주장 외에는 그에게 유리한 증거가 전혀 없는 거지요.\"\n\n\"아, 어쩌면 그렇게 잔인할까요!\" 도로시아가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"그리고 세상 모두가 그 사람을 거짓되이 헐뜯는다 해도, 그의 결백을 믿어 주는 단 한 사람이 되고 싶지 않으신가요? 게다가 그를 위해 미리 변호해 줄 그 사람의 인격이 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 친애하는 캐소본 부인.\" 페어브러더 씨가 그녀의 열정에 부드럽게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"인격이란 대리석에 새겨진 것이 아닙니다. 단단하고 변치 않는 무엇이 아니에요. 그것은 살아 움직이고 변하는 것이며, 우리 육신이 그러하듯 병들 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 구원되고 치유될 수도 있겠지요.\" 도로시아가 말했다. \"저는 리드게이트 씨에게 진실을 말해 달라고 청하는 게 두렵지 않을 거예요, 그를 도울 수 있도록 말이지요. 제가 왜 두려워해야 하나요? 이제 그 땅을 갖지 않게 되었으니, 제임스, 저는 불스트로드 씨가 제안한 대로 할 수 있고, 병원을 후원하는 일에서 그의 자리를 대신할 수도 있어요. 그러려면 리드게이트 씨와 상의해서, 현재의 계획을 유지함으로써 좋은 일을 할 가망이 어떤지 철저히 알아봐야 해요. 그러니 제가 그에게 속내를 털어놓아 달라고 청할 더없이 좋은 기회인 셈이지요. 그러면 그는 모든 정황을 분명히 밝혀 줄 만한 이야기들을 제게 해 줄 수 있을 거예요. 그러고 나면 우리 모두가 그의 편에 서서 그를 곤경에서 끌어내 줄 수 있겠지요. 사람들은 온갖 종류의 용기를 찬양하면서도, 가장 가까운 이웃을 위해 보일 수 있는 용기만은 빼놓는답니다.\" 도로시아의 눈에는 촉촉한 빛이 어렸고, 달라진 그녀의 목소리에 삼촌이 정신을 차리고 귀를 기울이기 시작했다.\n\n\"우리 남자들이 나섰다가는 좀처럼 성공하지 못할 어떤 공감의 노력을, 여자라면 감히 시도해 볼 수 있다는 것은 사실입니다.\" 도로시아의 열정에 거의 설득당한 페어브러더 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"여자라면 분명 신중해야 하고 자기보다 세상을 더 잘 아는 이들의 말에 귀를 기울여야지요.\" 제임스 경이 살짝 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"결국 무엇을 하든, 도로시아, 지금은 정말로 자제하고 이 불스트로드 사건에 자진해서 끼어들지 말아야 해요. 우리는 아직 무슨 일이 드러날지 몰라요. 제 말에 동의하시지요?\" 그가 페어브러더 씨를 바라보며 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"기다리는 편이 나으리라고 저도 생각합니다.\" 후자가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 얘야.\" 브룩 씨가 논의가 어느 지점에 이르렀는지 정확히는 모르면서도, 대체로 적절한 한마디를 보태며 끼어들었다. \"도가 지나치기는 쉬운 법이지, 알다시피. 네 생각에 끌려가서는 안 돼. 그리고 서둘러 무슨 사업에 돈을 넣는 것도 말이지, 그건 안 될 일이야, 알겠니. 가스가 수리니 배수니 그런 일로 나를 어지간히 끌어들였단다. 이것저것으로 주머니가 어지간히 비었지. 나는 고삐를 당겨야 해. 너로 말하자면, 체텀, 자네는 그 영지를 두른 떡갈나무 울타리에 거금을 쏟아붓고 있더군.\"\n\n이 만류에 불편하게 따른 도로시아는 실리아와 함께 서재로 갔는데, 그곳은 그녀가 평소 거실로 쓰는 방이었다.\n\n\"자, 도도, 제발 제임스가 하는 말을 좀 들어.\" 실리아가 말했다. \"안 그러면 곤경에 빠질 거야. 언니는 늘 그랬고, 마음 내키는 대로 하려 들면 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거야. 그래도 이젠 언니를 위해 생각해 줄 제임스가 있으니 그나마 다행이라고 난 생각해. 그이는 언니가 계획을 세우게 두면서도, 언니가 속아 넘어가는 것만은 막아 주잖아. 그게 바로 남편 대신 오라버니가 있어서 좋은 점이지. 남편이라면 언니가 계획을 세우게 두지 않을 테니까.\"\n\n\"내가 남편을 원하기라도 한다는 듯이!\" 도로시아가 말했다. \"난 그저 사사건건 내 감정이 가로막히지 않기를 바랄 뿐이야.\" 캐소본 부인은 아직도 분에 차서 눈물을 터뜨릴 만큼 다스려지지 않은 상태였다.\n\n\"정말이지, 도도.\" 실리아가 평소보다 다소 깊은 목쉰 소리로 말했다. \"언니는 모순적이야. 처음엔 이 말 했다가 다음엔 저 말 했다가. 언니는 캐소본 씨한테는 정말 창피할 만큼 순종했잖아. 그이가 부탁했다면 나를 보러 오는 것조차 그만뒀을걸.\"\n\n\"물론 나는 그분께 순종했지, 그게 내 의무였으니까. 그건 그분을 향한 내 마음이었어.\" 도로시아가 눈물의 프리즘을 통해 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 제임스가 바라는 바에 조금 순종하는 것도 언니의 의무라고 생각할 수는 없어?\" 자기 논리에 빈틈이 없다는 느낌으로 실리아가 말했다. \"그이는 오직 언니에게 이로운 것만 바라니까. 그리고 물론, 남자들은 모든 것에 대해 가장 잘 알아, 여자들이 더 잘 아는 것만 빼고는.\" 도로시아는 웃음을 터뜨리며 눈물을 잊었다.\n\n\"음, 내 말은 아기들이랑 그런 것들에 관해서야.\" 실리아가 설명했다. \"나라면 제임스가 틀렸다는 걸 알면서, 언니가 캐소본 씨한테 그러던 것처럼 그이한테 굽히지는 않을 거야.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LXXIII.\n\n그대와 나를 찾아올지도 모르니.\n\n리드게이트는 불스트로드 부인에게, 남편이 회의 도중 졸도하였으나 곧 차도가 있으리라 믿으며, 만약 그녀가 더 일찍 사람을 보내지 않는다면 이튿날 다시 들르겠다고 말하여 그녀의 불안을 가라앉힌 뒤, 곧장 집으로 가서 말에 올라타 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있기 위해 읍에서 삼 마일 밖으로 말을 달렸다.\n\n그는 마치 가시에 찔리는 고통 아래서 격분하기라도 하는 것처럼, 자신이 난폭하고 분별없어지는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 미들마치에 온 그날을 저주할 지경이었다. 그곳에서 그에게 일어난 모든 일이 이 가증스러운 운명을 위한 한낱 준비에 불과한 듯했다. 그 운명은 그의 명예로운 야망에 마름병처럼 들이닥쳐, 천박한 기준밖에 지니지 못한 사람들조차 그의 평판이 돌이킬 수 없이 손상되었다고 여기게 만들 것이었다. 그런 순간에 인간은 애정 없는 마음에서 벗어나기 어려운 법이다. 리드게이트는 스스로를 고통받는 자로, 다른 이들을 자기 운명을 해친 가해자로 생각했다. 그는 모든 것이 다르게 풀려 가기를 바랐건만, 다른 이들이 그의 삶에 비집고 들어와 그의 목적을 가로막았다. 그의 결혼은 완화될 길 없는 재앙처럼 보였다. 그래서 그는 이 고독한 분노를 다 쏟아 내기 전에는 로저먼드에게 가기가 두려웠으니, 그녀를 보는 것만으로도 자신이 격앙되어 부당하게 행동할까 봐 걱정되었기 때문이다. 대부분의 사람의 생애에는 그들의 가장 고결한 자질이 내면의 시야를 채운 대상들 위에 한낱 가로막는 그림자만을 드리우는 순간들이 있다. 리드게이트의 다정한 마음은 바로 그때 그가 그것을 거스를까 두려워하는 형태로만 존재했을 뿐, 그를 부드러움으로 이끄는 감정으로는 존재하지 않았다. 그는 무척이나 비참했기 때문이다. 지성적인 삶의 지고함을—고결한 사유와 목적의 씨앗을 품은 그 삶을—아는 사람만이, 그 평온한 활동에서 떨어져 세속적인 골칫거리와 영혼을 좀먹는 몰입의 투쟁으로 추락한 자의 비탄을 이해할 수 있다.\n\n자신을 비열하다고 의심하는 사람들 사이에서 결백을 입증하지 못한 채 어떻게 살아갈 것인가? 정당한 단죄 앞에서 물러서는 것처럼 어떻게 미들마치를 말없이 떠날 수 있겠는가? 그러나 또한 어떻게 자기 결백을 입증하는 일에 착수할 것인가?\n\n방금 그가 목격한 회의에서의 그 장면은, 비록 그에게 구체적인 내막을 일러 주지는 않았으나, 자신의 처지를 철저히 명확하게 깨닫게 하기에는 충분했기 때문이다. 불스트로드는 래플스 쪽에서 추문이 폭로될까 두려워하고 있었다. 이제 리드게이트는 이 사건의 모든 개연성을 짜 맞출 수 있었다. \"그는 내가 듣는 데서 무언가 폭로될까 두려워했어. 그가 원한 것은 오로지 강한 의무로 나를 자신에게 묶어 두는 것이었지. 그래서 그가 갑작스레 냉정함에서 관대함으로 돌아선 거야. 그리고 그는 환자를 함부로 다루었을지도 몰라. 내 지시를 어겼을지도 모르지. 그랬을 거라는 생각이 들어. 하지만 그가 그랬든 안 그랬든, 세상은 그가 어떻게든 그 사람을 독살했고 내가 범행을 거들지는 않았더라도 못 본 척 눈감아 주었다고 믿고 있어. 그렇지만—그렇지만 그가 그 마지막 죄를 짓지 않았을 수도 있어. 그리고 나를 향한 그 태도의 변화가 진정한 마음의 누그러짐이었을 가능성도 충분히 있어—그가 주장했던 것 같은 재고의 결과였을 수도 있지. 우리가 '충분히 가능한 일'이라고 부르는 것이 때로는 진실이고, 우리가 믿기 더 쉽다고 여기는 것이 터무니없이 거짓일 때가 있어. 이 사람과의 마지막 거래에서 불스트로드는, 그렇지 않으리라는 내 의심에도 불구하고, 손을 깨끗이 지켰을지도 몰라.\"\n\n그의 처지에는 사람을 마비시키는 잔혹함이 있었다. 설령 그가 자기 결백을 밝히는 것 말고 다른 모든 고려를 포기한다 하더라도—어깨를 으쓱하는 몸짓, 차가운 눈길, 그리고 회피를 하나의 고발로 받아들이고 자신이 아는 모든 사실을 공개적으로 진술한다 하더라도, 누가 납득하겠는가? 자기 자신을 위하여 스스로의 증언을 내놓으며 \"나는 그 돈을 뇌물로 받지 않았다\"라고 말하는 것은 바보 짓을 하는 격일 것이었다. 정황은 언제나 그의 주장보다 더 강력할 터였다. 게다가 나서서 자신에 관한 모든 것을 털어놓으려면 불스트로드에 관한 진술도 포함해야 했고, 그것은 그를 향한 다른 이들의 의심을 더욱 짙게 만들 것이었다. 그는 처음 불스트로드에게 절박한 돈의 필요를 언급했을 때 래플스라는 사람의 존재를 알지 못했으며, 그 이야기의 결과로 순진하게 그 돈을 받았을 뿐, 자신이 이 사람의 진료에 불려 가게 됨으로써 대출에 새로운 동기가 생겼을 수 있다는 것은 몰랐다고 말해야 했다. 그리고 결국 불스트로드의 동기에 대한 의심도 부당한 것일 수 있었다.\n\n하지만 그렇다면, 만약 그가 그 돈을 받지 않았더라면 정확히 똑같은 방식으로 행동했을 것인가 하는 물음이 떠올랐다. 분명, 만약 그가 도착했을 때 래플스가 여전히 살아 있어 더 치료할 수 있는 상태였고, 그때 불스트로드 쪽에서 자기 지시에 무언가 불복종한 정황을 알아챘더라면, 그는 엄정한 조사를 했을 것이고, 만약 그 추측이 입증되었더라면 최근의 무거운 신세에도 불구하고 그 사건을 손에서 놓아 버렸을 것이다. 그러나 만약 그가 아무 돈도 받지 않았더라면—만약 불스트로드가 파산을 권하던 냉정한 조언을 결코 철회하지 않았더라면—그, 리드게이트는 그 사람이 죽은 것을 발견하고서도 모든 조사를 삼갔을 것인가?—불스트로드를 모욕하는 것에 대한 움츠림이—모든 의학적 치료의 불확실성과, 자신의 치료법이 그 직종의 대다수 사람들에게 잘못된 것으로 통할 것이라는 논리가—그에게 똑같은 무게나 의미를 지녔을 것인가?\n\n바로 그것이, 그가 사실들을 되짚으며 모든 자책을 물리치는 동안 리드게이트의 의식 속에 자리한 불안한 구석이었다. 만약 그가 독립된 처지였더라면, 환자의 치료라는 이 문제와, 자신에게 맡겨진 생명을 위해 최선이라 믿는 바를 행하거나 행하게 해야 한다는 분명한 원칙이야말로 그가 가장 꿋꿋했을 지점이었으리라. 그러나 실상 그는, 자기 지시에 대한 불복종이 어떻게 일어났든 범죄로 간주될 수는 없다는 것, 지배적인 견해로는 자기 지시에 복종하는 것 역시 치명적이었을 가능성이 똑같이 있다는 것, 그리고 그 일이 단순히 예법의 문제일 뿐이라는 생각에 안주하고 있었다. 반면, 자유로웠던 시절에 그는 거듭거듭 병리학적 의심을 도덕적 의심으로 왜곡하는 것을 규탄하며 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"가장 순수한 치료 실험도 여전히 양심적일 수 있다. 내 일은 생명을 돌보고 그것을 위해 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 최선을 다하는 것이다. 과학은 본래 교리보다 더 세심하다. 교리는 오류에 면허를 내주지만, 과학의 숨결 그 자체는 오류와의 다툼이며, 양심을 살아 있게 지켜야 한다.\" 아아! 그 과학적 양심이 금전적 의무와 이기적인 체면이라는 타락한 무리 속으로 들어와 버린 것이다.\n\n\"미들마치의 그 모든 의료인 가운데 나처럼 스스로를 추궁할 사람이 하나라도 있을까?\" 가엾은 리드게이트는 자기 운명의 압제에 대한 반항심이 새로이 솟구치는 가운데 이렇게 말했다. \"그러면서도 그들은 모두, 내가 나병 환자라도 되는 양 나와 자기들 사이에 넓은 간격을 두는 것이 정당하다고 느낄 테지! 내 진료와 내 평판은 완전히 끝장났어—나는 그걸 알 수 있어. 설령 내가 타당한 증거로 결백을 밝힐 수 있다 해도, 이곳의 그 복된 세상에는 별 차이가 없을 거야. 나는 이미 오염된 자로 낙인찍혔으니, 어찌 됐든 그들 모두에게 값싼 존재로 떨어질 테지.\"\n\n그가 빚을 갚아 나가며 기운차게 자립해 가던 바로 그때, 읍 사람들이 그를 피하거나 이상한 눈으로 바라보았다는, 지금까지 그를 어리둥절하게 했던 징후들이 이미 숱하게 있었다. 그리고 두 번에 걸쳐 그의 환자들이 다른 의사를 불렀다는 사실을 그는 알게 되었다. 이제 그 이유는 너무도 명백했다. 모두가 그를 따돌리기 시작한 것이다.\n\n리드게이트의 활기찬 천성에서, 가망 없는 오해를 받고 있다는 의식이 쉽사리 완강한 저항으로 바뀐 것도 무리는 아니었다. 그의 각진 이마에 이따금 떠오르는 찌푸린 표정은 의미 없는 우연이 아니었다. 찌르는 듯한 고통의 첫 시간에 말을 달렸다가 읍으로 다시 들어설 때, 그는 이미 자신에게 가해질 수 있는 최악의 일에도 불구하고 미들마치에 남아 있기로 마음을 다잡고 있었다. 그는 중상모략에 굴복하는 것처럼 그 앞에서 물러서지 않을 것이었다. 그는 그것을 끝까지 마주할 것이며, 자신의 어떤 행동도 두려워한다는 것을 드러내지 않을 것이었다. 불스트로드에 대한 신세를 남김없이 드러내겠다고 결심한 것은 그의 천성에 깃든 관대함뿐 아니라 도전적인 기개에서 비롯된 것이었다. 이 사람과의 관계가 그에게 치명적이었다는 것은 사실이었고—만약 그 천 파운드가 여전히 그의 손안에 있고 빚도 모두 갚지 못한 상태였더라면 그는 그 돈을 불스트로드에게 돌려주고, 뇌물의 의심으로 더럽혀진 구원을 받느니 차라리 거지 신세를 택했으리라는 것도 사실이었다(기억하라, 그는 인간의 아들들 가운데 가장 자존심 강한 축에 드는 사람이었다)—그럼에도 그는, 자신이 그 도움을 이용했던 이 짓밟힌 동료 인간에게서 등을 돌려, 다른 사람을 향해 울부짖음으로써 자신의 무죄를 얻어 내려는 가련한 짓을 하지는 않을 것이었다. \"나는 옳다고 생각하는 대로 할 것이고, 누구에게도 해명하지 않겠어. 그들은 나를 굶겨 내쫓으려 하겠지만—\" 그는 완고한 결의로 생각을 이어 가고 있었으나, 어느새 집 가까이에 이르렀고, 로저먼드에 대한 생각이, 상처 입은 명예와 자존심의 고뇌에 찬 투쟁에 밀려나 있던 그 으뜸 자리로 다시금 비집고 들어왔다.\n\n로저먼드는 이 모든 것을 어떻게 받아들일까? 여기 끌고 가야 할 또 하나의 사슬의 무게가 있었으니, 가엾은 리드게이트는 그녀의 말 없는 지배를 견디기에 좋지 못한 기분이었다. 그는 머지않아 두 사람 모두의 것이 될 수밖에 없는 그 곤경을 그녀에게 말해 주고 싶은 충동이 일지 않았다. 그는 사태가 머지않아 불러올 우연한 폭로를 기다리는 편을 택했다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXIV.\n\n크나큰 자비로 우리가 함께 늙어 갈 수 있도록 허락하소서.\n— 토비트서: 결혼 기도\n\n미들마치에서 아내가 자기 남편에 대한 마을의 평판이 나쁘다는 사실을 오래 모르고 지낼 수는 없었다. 어떤 여자 친구도 우정을 너무 멀리 밀고 나가, 남편에 관해 알려졌거나 그렇게 믿어지는 불쾌한 사실을 아내에게 대놓고 말해 주지는 않았다. 하지만 평소 생각할 거리가 한가하던 여자가 이웃에게 몹시 불리한 일에 갑자기 생각을 골몰하게 되면, 발설을 부추기는 여러 가지 도덕적 충동이 작동하기 마련이었다. 그중 하나가 솔직함이었다. 미들마치 어법으로 솔직하다는 것은, 당신의 친구들에게 그들의 능력이나 처신이나 처지를 그다지 밝게 보지 않는다는 것을 일찌감치 기회 닿는 대로 알려 준다는 뜻이었다. 그리고 건강하고 강건한 솔직함은 의견을 청해 올 때까지 기다리는 법이 없었다. 그다음으로는 진실에 대한 사랑이 있었다. 이것은 폭넓은 표현이지만 이런 관계에서는, 한 아내가 자기 남편의 인품이 허락하는 것보다 더 행복해 보이거나 제 처지에 너무 흡족해하는 모습을 보면 활발하게 반발한다는 뜻이었다. 그 가엾은 여자는, 진실을 안다면 자기 보닛이나 야식 모임용 가벼운 요리들에 그토록 만족하지 못하리라는 것을 어느 정도 귀띔받아야 한다는 것이다. 무엇보다도 강력한 것은 친구의 도덕적 향상에 대한, 때로는 그녀의 영혼이라고도 불리는 것에 대한 관심이었다. 그것은 가구를 수심에 잠겨 바라보며, 듣는 이의 감정을 배려해서 자기 속에 있는 말을 차마 다 하지 못한다는 듯한 태도로 우울함을 자아내는 발언을 던질 때 향상될 법한 것이었다. 대체로 보아, 열렬한 자비심이 작동하여 덕망 있는 마음으로 하여금 이웃을 그녀 자신을 위한답시고 불행하게 만들도록 부추긴다고 말할 수 있을 터였다.\n\n미들마치에서 이런 도덕적 활동을 여러 방식으로 가장 많이 불러일으킬 만한 결혼 생활의 불운을 지닌 아내는, 로저먼드와 그녀의 숙모 벌스트로드 부인을 빼면 거의 없었다. 벌스트로드 부인은 미움의 대상이 아니었고, 어떤 인간도 의식적으로 해친 적이 없었다. 남자들은 늘 그녀를 잘생기고 편안한 여인이라 여겼고, 벌스트로드가 지상의 쾌락을 낮춰 보는 그의 태도에 어울릴 만한 으스스하고 우울한 사람 대신 혈색 좋은 빈시 집안 여자를 택했다는 것을 그의 위선의 징표 가운데 하나로 꼽았다. 그녀의 남편에 관한 추문이 폭로되자 사람들은 그녀를 두고 말했다. \"아, 가엾은 여자! 더없이 정직한 사람이지. 남편에게 뭔가 잘못이 있으리라고는 의심도 못 했을 거요. 틀림없어.\" 그녀와 친한 여자들은 \"가엾은 해리엇\"에 관해 많은 이야기를 나누며, 그녀가 모든 것을 알게 되었을 때 어떤 심정일지 상상했고, 이미 얼마나 알게 되었을지 추측했다. 그녀에 대한 악의는 전혀 없었다. 오히려 이런 상황에서 그녀가 무엇을 느끼고 무엇을 하는 것이 좋을지 알아내려고 안달하는 분주한 선의가 있었으니, 이는 물론 그녀가 해리엇 빈시였던 시절부터 지금까지의 성품과 내력에 상상력을 골몰하게 만들었다. 벌스트로드 부인과 그녀의 처지를 되짚다 보면, 숙모와 똑같은 먹구름 아래 놓인 앞날을 지닌 로저먼드를 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 로저먼드는 더 호되게 비판받고 덜 동정받았으나, 그녀 역시 미들마치에서 늘 알려져 온 유서 깊은 빈시 집안의 일원으로서 외부에서 끼어든 사내와의 결혼에 희생된 사람으로 여겨졌다. 빈시 집안 사람들에게도 약점은 있었지만, 그 약점은 표면에 드러나 있었다. 그들에 관해서는 \"들춰낼\" 만한 나쁜 일이 결코 없었다. 벌스트로드 부인은 자기 남편과 닮은 점이 전혀 없다는 변호를 받았다. 해리엇의 결점은 그녀 자신의 것이었다.\n\n\"그 사람은 늘 겉치레가 심했어요.\" 핵벗 부인이 작은 모임을 위해 차를 준비하며 말했다. \"물론 남편에게 맞추느라 신앙을 앞세우는 버릇이 들기는 했지만요. 리버스턴이며 그런 곳에서 성직자들이며 누군지도 모를 사람들을 초대한다는 걸 알려서 미들마치 위로 고개를 쳐들려고 했지요.\"\n\n\"그 일로 그분을 탓하기는 어려워요.\" 스프레이그 부인이 말했다. \"이 마을의 점잖은 사람들 중에 벌스트로드와 어울리려는 이가 거의 없었으니, 자기 식탁에 앉힐 누군가는 있어야 했을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"세시저 목사님은 늘 그를 두둔하셨어요.\" 핵벗 부인이 말했다. \"지금쯤은 후회하고 계실 것 같네요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 양반은 속으로는 그를 좋아한 적이 없어요. 다들 아는 사실이지요.\" 톰 톨러 부인이 말했다. \"세시저 목사님은 결코 극단으로 치우치지 않으시거든요. 복음주의에 관한 한 진실을 지키는 분이에요. 비국교도 찬송가집이며 그런 저급한 신앙을 쓰고 싶어 하는 타이크 목사님 같은 성직자들이나 벌스트로드를 취향에 맞다고 여겼지요.\"\n\n\"제가 듣기로 타이크 목사님은 그 사람 일로 크게 상심하셨다더군요.\" 핵벗 부인이 말했다. \"그럴 만도 하지요. 사람들 말로는 벌스트로드네가 타이크 집안을 절반은 먹여 살렸다니까요.\"\n\n\"그러니 당연히 그분 교리에도 먹칠이 되는 셈이지요.\" 나이가 지긋하고 견해가 구식인 스프레이그 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"앞으로 한동안 미들마치에서 자기가 감리교 신자라고 자랑하는 사람은 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"사람들의 나쁜 행실을 그들의 신앙 탓으로 돌려서는 안 된다고 봐요.\" 그때까지 잠자코 듣고 있던 매 같은 얼굴의 플림데일 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 이런, 우리가 깜빡했네요.\" 스프레이그 부인이 말했다. \"댁 앞에서 이런 이야기를 하면 안 되는 건데.\"\n\n\"제가 편을 들 이유는 전혀 없답니다.\" 플림데일 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"물론 우리 플림데일 씨가 벌스트로드 씨와 늘 사이가 좋았던 건 사실이고, 해리엇 빈시는 그 사람과 결혼하기 훨씬 전부터 제 친구였지요. 하지만 저는 늘 제 의견을 지켜 왔고, 그 가엾은 사람에게 어디가 잘못됐는지 말해 주곤 했어요. 그래도 신앙이라는 점에서 말하자면, 벌스트로드 씨가 한 짓을, 아니 그보다 더한 짓을 했다 해도 신앙이라곤 전혀 없는 사람일 수도 있는 거예요. 그게 좀 지나쳤다는 것을 부정하는 건 아니에요. 저야 절제를 좋아하니까요. 하지만 진실은 진실이지요. 순회 재판에서 재판받는 사람들이 다들 지나치게 독실하지는 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 핵벗 부인이 능란하게 화제를 돌리며 말했다. \"제가 말할 수 있는 건, 그 사람이 남편과 헤어져야 한다고 생각한다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그건 모르겠네요.\" 스프레이그 부인이 말했다. \"아시다시피 좋든 나쁘든 함께하겠다고 그를 받아들였잖아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 '나쁘든'이라는 게 남편이 뉴게이트 감옥에나 어울리는 사람임을 알게 되는 걸 뜻할 수는 없지요.\" 핵벗 부인이 말했다. \"그런 남자와 함께 산다고 생각해 봐요! 저라면 독살이라도 당할까 봐 겁날 거예요.\"\n\n* 뉴게이트: 런던의 악명 높은 감옥으로, 중범죄자가 수감되던 곳.\n\"네, 저도 그런 남자들이 착한 아내들의 보살핌과 시중을 받게 된다면 그게 범죄를 부추기는 일이라고 생각해요.\" 톰 톨러 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 가엾은 해리엇은 정말 좋은 아내였어요.\" 플림데일 부인이 말했다. \"그 사람은 자기 남편을 세상 으뜸가는 남자로 여기지요. 하기야 그가 그 사람에게 뭐든 안 된다고 한 적이 없긴 해요.\"\n\n\"음, 그 사람이 어떻게 하는지 두고 봐야겠지요.\" 핵벗 부인이 말했다. \"아직 아무것도 모르나 봐요, 가엾은 것. 정말이지 그 사람을 만나지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 행여 남편 이야기를 입에 올릴까 봐 겁이 나서 죽을 지경일 테니까요. 어떤 낌새라도 그 사람 귀에 들어갔을까요?\"\n\n\"그럴 것 같진 않아요.\" 톰 톨러 부인이 말했다. \"그 사람 남편이 아프고 목요일 모임 이후로 집 밖에 한 발짝도 안 나왔다고 들었어요. 하지만 그 사람은 어제 딸들과 함께 교회에 왔고, 다들 새 투스카니 보닛을 쓰고 있더군요. 자기 보닛에는 깃털까지 달려 있었고요. 그 사람 신앙이 옷차림에 무슨 변화를 가져온 건 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 늘 아주 단정한 무늬를 입는걸요.\" 플림데일 부인이 살짝 발끈하여 말했다. \"그리고 그 깃털은 일부러 연한 라벤더색으로 염색한 거예요. 신앙에 어울리게 하려고요. 해리엇에 대해 이 말은 해 두어야겠어요. 그 사람은 바르게 살고 싶어 한다고요.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알게 되는 거야, 오래 숨길 수는 없겠지요.\" 핵벗 부인이 말했다. \"빈시 집안은 알고 있어요. 빈시 씨가 그 모임에 있었으니까요. 그분에게 큰 타격일 거예요. 누이뿐 아니라 딸까지 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"네, 정말 그래요.\" 스프레이그 부인이 말했다. \"리드게이트 씨가 미들마치에서 계속 고개를 들고 다닐 수 있으리라고 생각하는 사람은 아무도 없어요. 그 사람이 죽은 바로 그 무렵에 천 파운드를 받았다니 모든 게 너무 수상쩍거든요. 정말이지 소름이 끼친다니까요.\"\n\n\"교만한 자는 넘어지기 마련이지요.\" 핵벗 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"저는 한때 로저먼드 빈시였던 그 사람보다 그 숙모가 더 안됐어요.\" 플림데일 부인이 말했다. \"그 애는 따끔한 교훈이 필요했어요.\"\n\n\"벌스트로드네는 어디 외국에 가서 살게 될 거예요.\" 스프레이그 부인이 말했다. \"한 집안에 망신스러운 일이 생기면 보통 그렇게들 하니까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그건 해리엇에게 더없이 치명적인 타격이 될 거예요.\" 플림데일 부인이 말했다. \"한 여자가 짓밟히는 일이 있다면, 그 사람이 그럴 거예요. 진심으로 가엾게 생각해요. 온갖 결점에도 불구하고 그 사람보다 나은 여자는 드물어요. 처녀 시절부터 더없이 깔끔했고, 늘 마음씨가 곱고 대낮처럼 환히 트인 사람이었지요. 그 사람 서랍은 언제 열어 봐도 한결같았어요. 그렇게 케이트와 엘런도 길러 냈고요. 그러니 외국인들 틈에서 살아가는 게 그 사람에게 얼마나 힘들지 짐작이 가시겠지요.\"\n\n\"의사 말로는 리드게이트네에게도 그렇게 하라고 권하겠다더군요.\" 스프레이그 부인이 말했다. \"리드게이트는 프랑스 사람들 틈에 있었어야 했다나요.\"\n\n\"그거야 그 사람한테는 충분히 어울릴 거예요.\" 플림데일 부인이 말했다. \"그 사람한테는 그런 경박한 구석이 있으니까요. 하지만 그건 친정어머니한테서 물려받은 거예요. 늘 좋은 충고를 해 주었고 내가 알기로는 차라리 다른 데로 시집보내고 싶어 했던 벌스트로드 숙모한테서 물려받은 게 아니라요.\"\n\n플림데일 부인은 다소 복잡한 심경을 일으키는 처지에 놓여 있었다. 벌스트로드 부인과의 친분뿐 아니라, 명망 높은 플림데일 염색 회사가 벌스트로드 씨와 맺어 온 짭짤한 사업 관계도 있었던 것이다. 이 때문에 한편으로는 그의 인품에 대한 가장 너그러운 해석이 진실이기를 바라는 마음으로 기울었지만, 다른 한편으로는 그의 죄과를 두둔하는 것처럼 보일까 봐 더욱 두려웠다. 또한 근래에 그녀 집안이 톨러 가문과 맺은 인척 관계 덕분에 최상류 계층과 인연이 닿게 되었으니, 이는 또 다른 의미에서 최선이라고 그녀가 믿는 그 진지한 견해들로 기우는 성향만 빼고는 모든 방면에서 그녀를 흡족하게 했다. 이 영악하고 자그마한 여인의 양심은, 서로 어긋나는 이 \"최선\"들을 조정하면서, 또한 근래의 사건들이 일으킨 자신의 슬픔과 만족을 조정하면서 다소 괴로웠다. 그 사건들은 응당 겸허해져야 할 이들을 겸허하게 만들 법했으나, 차라리 번영을 배경으로 그 결점을 보고 싶었던 그녀의 옛 친구에게도 무겁게 떨어질 것이었다.\n\n한편, 가엾은 벌스트로드 부인은 다가오는 재앙의 발소리에도, 래플스가 슈러브스에 마지막으로 다녀간 이후로 늘 그녀 안에 자리하고 있던 그 은밀한 불안이 한층 분주하게 술렁이는 것 말고는 별다른 동요를 겪지 않았다. 그 가증스러운 사내가 병들어 스톤 코트에 왔고 남편이 그곳에 머무르며 그를 돌보기로 한 일은, 래플스가 예전에 일자리를 얻고 도움을 받았던 사람이라 그가 영락하여 무력해진 상황에서 그에 대한 선의의 의리가 생긴 것이라고 설명되는 것을 그녀는 받아들였다. 그리고 그 이후로 남편이 자신의 건강과 계속 사업에 전념할 수 있는 능력에 관해 더 희망찬 말을 하는 것에 그녀는 천진하게 마음이 놓였다. 그 평온은 리드게이트가 모임에서 병든 남편을 집으로 데려왔을 때 흔들렸고, 이후 며칠간 위로가 되는 다짐들이 이어졌음에도, 그녀는 남편이 단지 육체의 병만이 아니라 마음을 괴롭히는 무언가로 고통받고 있다는 확신에 남몰래 울었다. 그는 소리와 움직임에 신경이 예민해졌다는 핑계로, 그녀가 책을 읽어 주는 것은 물론 곁에 앉아 있는 것조차 허락하지 않았다. 하지만 그녀는 그가 자기 방에 틀어박혀 서류를 들여다보며 무언가 분주히 하고 싶어 한다고 의심했다. 무슨 일이 일어난 게 틀림없다고 그녀는 확신했다. 어쩌면 큰 금전적 손실일지도 몰랐다. 그런데 그녀만 어둠 속에 갇혀 있었다. 감히 남편에게 물어보지 못한 채, 모임이 있은 지 닷새째 되던 날, 교회에 가는 것 말고는 집을 나선 적이 없던 그녀가 리드게이트에게 말했다.\n\n\"리드게이트 씨, 부디 제게 솔직히 말씀해 주세요. 저는 진실을 알고 싶어요. 벌스트로드 씨에게 무슨 일이 생긴 건가요?\"\n\n\"가벼운 신경성 충격입니다.\" 리드게이트가 얼버무리며 말했다. 그 괴로운 사실을 밝히는 것이 자기 몫은 아니라고 그는 느꼈다.\n\n\"그런데 무엇 때문에 그렇게 된 거지요?\" 벌스트로드 부인이 크고 검은 눈으로 그를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"공공장소의 공기에는 흔히 해로운 무언가가 있습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"건강한 사람은 견디지만, 신체가 섬세한 사람일수록 그 영향을 받지요. 발작이 일어나는 정확한 순간을 설명하기란 흔히 불가능합니다. 아니, 어째서 하필 그 순간에 기력이 무너지는지를 말하기란 말입니다.\"\n\n벌스트로드 부인은 이 대답에 만족하지 못했다. 그녀에게는 남편에게 어떤 재앙이 닥쳤고 자기만 그 사실을 모르도록 가려져 있다는 믿음이 남아 있었다. 그리고 그런 은폐를 강하게 거부하는 것이 그녀의 천성이었다. 그녀는 딸들에게 아버지 곁에 앉아 있으라고 청하고는, 몇 군데 방문을 하러 마차를 타고 마을로 나갔다. 벌스트로드 씨의 일에 무언가 잘못된 것이 알려져 있다면, 그 어떤 징후라도 보거나 듣게 되리라 짐작하면서였다.\n\n그녀는 세시저 부인을 찾아갔으나 부인은 집에 없었고, 그래서 교회 묘지 건너편에 있는 핵벗 부인의 집으로 마차를 몰았다. 핵벗 부인은 위층 창문에서 그녀가 오는 것을 보았고, 행여 벌스트로드 부인과 마주칠까 봐 두려워하던 예전의 마음을 떠올리며 일관성을 지키자면 집에 없다고 전갈을 보내야 마땅하다고 느꼈다. 하지만 그에 맞서, 자기 속에 있는 것은 추호도 내비치지 않겠다고 단단히 결심한 채로 면담의 흥분을 맛보고 싶은 강한 욕망이 불현듯 솟구쳤다.\n\n그리하여 벌스트로드 부인은 응접실로 안내되었고, 핵벗 부인은 평소보다 더 입술을 꼭 다물고 두 손을 비비며 그녀에게 다가갔다. 이는 말이 헤퍼지는 것을 막으려고 취한 조심성이었다. 그녀는 벌스트로드 씨가 어떠냐고 묻지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었다.\n\n\"거의 일주일 동안 교회 말고는 어디에도 가지 못했어요.\" 몇 마디 인사를 나눈 뒤 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 벌스트로드 씨가 목요일 모임에서 너무 심하게 앓아눕는 바람에 집을 비우고 싶지 않았거든요.\"\n\n핵벗 부인은 가슴에 댄 한 손의 손등을 다른 손바닥으로 문지르며, 깔개의 무늬 위로 눈길을 헤매게 두었다.\n\n\"핵벗 씨도 그 모임에 계셨나요?\" 벌스트로드 부인이 끈질기게 물었다.\n\n\"네, 계셨어요.\" 핵벗 부인이 똑같은 자세로 말했다. \"그 땅은 기부금으로 사들이게 될 거라더군요.\"\n\n\"거기 콜레라로 죽은 사람들을 더는 묻을 일이 없기를 바라야지요.\" 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다. \"무서운 재앙이에요. 하지만 저는 늘 미들마치가 아주 건강한 곳이라고 생각해요. 어릴 적부터 익숙해서 그렇겠지만, 여기보다 더 살고 싶은 마을은 본 적이 없어요. 특히 우리 동네 쪽은요.\"\n\n\"벌스트로드 부인, 부인이 늘 미들마치에 사신다면 저야 정말 기쁘지요.\" 핵벗 부인이 가벼운 한숨과 함께 말했다. \"그래도 우리의 운명이 어디로 던져지든, 우리는 체념하는 법을 배워야 하지요. 물론 이 마을에는 늘 부인이 잘되기를 바라는 사람들이 있을 테지만요.\"\n\n핵벗 부인은 \"제 충고를 받아들이신다면 남편과 헤어지셔야 해요\"라고 말하고 싶어 견딜 수 없었지만, 이 가엾은 여인이 제 머리 위로 터지려는 천둥에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 것이 분명해 보였다. 그리고 그녀 자신이 할 수 있는 일이라고는 마음의 준비를 조금 시켜 주는 것뿐이었다. 벌스트로드 부인은 갑자기 다소 오싹하고 몸이 떨렸다. 핵벗 부인의 이 말 뒤에는 분명 무언가 심상치 않은 것이 있었다. 하지만 충분히 알아내겠다는 마음으로 나섰음에도, 이제 그녀는 그 용감한 의도를 밀고 나갈 수 없음을 깨달았다. 그래서 핵벗네 아이들의 안부를 물어 화제를 돌리고는, 플림데일 부인을 만나러 가는 길이라고 말하며 곧 자리를 떴다. 그곳으로 가는 길에 그녀는 모임에서 벌스트로드 씨와 그의 잦은 적수들 가운데 누군가가, 어쩌면 핵벗 씨가 그중 하나였을지 모르나, 유난히 격렬한 언쟁을 벌인 것이 아닐까 상상해 보려 애썼다. 그렇다면 모든 것이 설명될 터였다.\n\n하지만 플림데일 부인과 이야기를 나누자, 그 위로가 되는 설명은 더 이상 지탱되지 않을 것 같았다. \"셀리나\"는 애처로운 다정함으로 그녀를 맞이하며, 더없이 평범한 화제에도 교화적인 대답을 하려는 성향을 보였다. 그것은 기껏해야 벌스트로드 씨의 건강 악화가 가장 중대한 결과인 평범한 다툼과는 도무지 관련이 없어 보였다. 미리부터 벌스트로드 부인은 다른 누구보다도 플림데일 부인에게 더 먼저 물어보리라 생각했었다. 하지만 놀랍게도 그녀는, 오랜 친구가 언제나 가장 속을 털어놓기 쉬운 사람은 아니라는 것을 깨달았다. 다른 상황에서 주고받은 이야기들이 기억의 장벽으로 남아 있었고, 오래도록 그녀에게 우위를 인정해 온 사람에게서 동정과 정보를 받기 싫은 마음이 있었던 것이다. 플림데일 부인이 결코 친구들에게 등을 돌리지 않겠다는 결심에 대해 신비롭게 들어맞는 어떤 말들을 흘렸기 때문에, 벌스트로드 부인은 일어난 일이 어떤 종류의 불행임에 틀림없다고 확신하게 되었다. 그래서 타고난 직설로 \"당신 마음속에 있는 게 뭔가요?\"라고 묻기는커녕, 더 분명한 무언가를 듣기 전에 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶어 안달이 났다. 그녀는 그 불행이 단순한 금전적 손실 이상의 무엇이라는 동요스러운 확신을 갖기 시작했다. 셀리나 역시 조금 전 핵벗 부인이 그랬던 것과 똑같이, 마치 신체의 흠을 못 본 척하듯 그녀가 남편에 관해 하는 말을 못 들은 체한다는 사실을 그녀가 예민하게 알아차렸기 때문이다.\n\n그녀는 안절부절못하며 황급히 작별 인사를 하고는, 마부에게 빈시 씨의 창고로 가자고 일렀다. 그 짧은 마차 길에, 어둠에 휩싸였다는 느낌으로 그녀의 두려움은 무척 커져서, 오빠가 책상에 앉아 있는 개인 사무실로 들어섰을 때 그녀의 무릎은 떨렸고 평소 혈색 좋던 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 창백했다. 그녀를 보는 순간 그에게도 같은 효과가 어느 정도 일어났다. 그는 그녀를 맞으러 자리에서 일어나 그녀의 손을 잡고, 충동적이고 경솔한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"하느님이 도우시기를, 해리엇! 너 다 알고 있구나.\"\n\n그 순간은 어쩌면 그 뒤에 닥친 어떤 순간보다 더 끔찍했다. 그 순간에는, 감정의 큰 위기에서 한 사람의 천성이 어느 쪽으로 기우는지를 드러내고, 중간에 벌어지는 갈등을 끝맺을 궁극의 행위를 예언하는 그 응축된 경험이 담겨 있었다. 래플스에 대한 기억이 없었다면 그녀는 여전히 금전적 파산만을 생각했을지도 모른다. 하지만 이제 오빠의 표정과 말과 더불어, 남편에게 어떤 죄가 있으리라는 생각이 그녀의 마음속으로 화살처럼 꽂혔다. 이어서 공포가 작동하는 가운데 망신을 당하는 남편의 모습이 떠올랐고, 그러고는 세상의 눈만이 느껴지던 살을 에는 수치심의 한순간이 지난 뒤, 그녀의 심장은 단번에 솟구쳐 그의 곁으로 가, 수치와 고립을 함께 나누는 슬프지만 책망하지 않는 동반자가 되었다. 이 모든 것이 그녀 안에서 한순간의 섬광처럼 지나갔다. 그녀가 의자에 주저앉으며, 자기 위로 우뚝 선 오빠에게 눈을 들어 올리는 사이에. \"저는 아무것도 몰라요, 월터. 무슨 일이지요?\" 그녀가 힘없이 말했다.\n\n그는 그녀에게 모든 것을, 아주 꾸밈없이, 더디게 토막토막 이야기해 주었다. 그리하여 그 추문이, 특히 래플스의 죽음에 관해서는, 증거를 훨씬 넘어선다는 것을 그녀가 알게 했다.\n\n\"사람들은 입방아를 찧을 거다.\" 그가 말했다. \"배심원에게 무죄 판결을 받은 사람이라 해도, 사람들은 입방아를 찧고 고개를 끄덕이며 눈짓을 할 테지. 그러니 세상이 보는 한, 사람이란 죄가 있는 거나 없는 거나 매한가지일 때가 많아. 이건 파멸의 일격이고, 벌스트로드만큼이나 리드게이트에게도 타격이다. 무엇이 진실인지 나는 함부로 말하지 않으마. 다만 우리가 벌스트로드든 리드게이트든 그 이름을 아예 들은 적이 없었더라면 싶을 뿐이야. 너는 평생 빈시 집안 사람으로 남는 게 나았을 거다. 로저먼드도 그렇고.\" 벌스트로드 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"하지만 너는 할 수 있는 한 꿋꿋이 견뎌야 한다, 해리엇. 사람들이 너를 탓하지는 않아. 그리고 네가 무엇을 하기로 마음먹든 내가 네 곁을 지키마.\" 오빠가 투박하지만 선의 어린 다정함으로 말했다.\n\n\"마차까지 팔 좀 빌려주세요, 월터.\" 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다. \"몸이 너무 약해진 것 같아요.\"\n\n그리고 집에 돌아오자 그녀는 딸에게 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"몸이 좋지 않구나, 얘야. 가서 좀 누워야겠다. 아버지를 돌봐 드려라. 나는 조용히 있게 내버려 두렴. 저녁은 들지 않으마.\"\n\n그녀는 방문을 잠갔다. 그녀에게는 절단된 의식에, 가련하게 잘려 나간 삶에 익숙해질 시간이 필요했다. 자기에게 할당된 자리로 흔들림 없이 걸어가기 전에 말이다. 새로이 파고드는 빛이 남편의 인품 위에 떨어졌고, 그녀는 그를 너그럽게 판단할 수 없었다. 그의 은폐 덕분에 그를 믿고 떠받들었던 이십 년 세월이, 그 모든 것을 가증스러운 기만으로 보이게 하는 세세한 일들과 함께 되살아났다. 그는 그 추악한 과거를 등 뒤에 감춘 채 그녀와 결혼했고, 이제 그녀에게는 그에게 씌워진 최악의 혐의에 대해 그의 결백을 항변할 믿음이 남아 있지 않았다. 정직하고 과시적인 그녀의 천성은, 당연히 받아 마땅한 불명예를 함께 짊어지는 일을 어떤 인간에게도 그러할 만큼 쓰라리게 만들었다.\n\n하지만 그녀의 어법과 습관이 기묘한 누더기 조각보 같았던, 불완전하게 교육받은 이 여인에게는 그 안에 충직한 정신이 있었다. 인생의 절반 가까이 번영을 함께 나누었고, 한결같이 그녀를 아껴 준 남자, 이제 그에게 벌이 닥쳤으니 어떤 의미에서도 그를 저버리는 일은 그녀로서는 불가능했다. 버림받은 영혼과 여전히 같은 식탁에 앉고 같은 침상에 누우면서, 애정 없는 가까움으로 그 영혼을 더욱 시들게 하는, 그런 저버림도 있는 법이다. 문을 잠그면서 그녀는 알았다. 자기가 그 문을 다시 열고, 불행한 남편에게 내려가 그의 슬픔을 함께하고, 그의 죄에 대해 책망하지 않고 슬퍼하겠노라 말할 준비를 한 채 나서리라는 것을. 하지만 그녀에게는 기운을 그러모을 시간이 필요했다. 자기 삶의 모든 기쁨과 자랑에 작별을 고하며 흐느껴 울 시간이 필요했다. 내려가기로 결심하자, 그녀는 매정한 구경꾼에게는 한낱 어리석은 짓으로 보일 법한 몇 가지 작은 행동으로 마음의 준비를 했다. 그것들은 보이든 보이지 않든 모든 구경꾼에게, 자기가 굴욕을 끌어안는 새로운 삶을 시작했음을 표현하는 그녀 나름의 방식이었다. 그녀는 장신구를 모두 떼어 내고 수수한 검은 가운을 입었으며, 잔뜩 꾸민 모자와 머리에 단 커다란 매듭 리본 대신 머리를 아래로 빗어 내리고 수수한 보닛 모자를 썼다. 그 모습은 그녀를 문득 초기 감리교 신자처럼 보이게 했다.\n\n벌스트로드는 아내가 외출했다가 몸이 좋지 않다며 돌아왔다는 것을 알고 있었고, 그동안 그녀에 못지않은 동요 속에서 시간을 보냈다. 그는 아내가 다른 사람들에게서 진실을 알게 되리라 예상해 왔고, 그것이 자신이 직접 고백하는 것보다 더 수월한 일이라며 그 가능성을 묵인했었다. 하지만 이제 그녀가 진실을 알게 되는 순간이 왔다고 상상하자, 그는 고뇌 속에서 그 결과를 기다렸다. 딸들은 어쩔 수 없이 그의 곁을 떠나는 데 동의해야 했고, 그는 음식을 가져오게는 했으나 손도 대지 않았다. 그는 아무도 동정해 주지 않는 비참함 속에서 서서히 죽어 가는 듯한 기분이었다. 어쩌면 그는 다시는 애정이 깃든 아내의 얼굴을 보지 못할지도 몰랐다. 그리고 그가 하느님께 돌아선다 해도, 응보의 압박 외에는 아무런 응답이 없는 것 같았다.\n\n문이 열리고 아내가 들어선 것은 저녁 여덟 시였다. 그는 감히 그녀를 올려다보지 못했다. 그는 눈을 내리깔고 앉아 있었고, 그녀가 그에게 다가가면서 보니 그가 더 작아 보였다. 그토록 시들고 쪼그라든 듯했다. 새로운 연민과 오래된 다정함이 큰 물결처럼 그녀를 관통했고, 의자 팔걸이에 놓인 그의 손 위에 한 손을, 그의 어깨 위에 다른 손을 얹으며 그녀는 엄숙하지만 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"고개를 드세요, 니콜라스.\"\n\n그는 흠칫 놀라며 눈을 들어 잠시 반쯤 어리둥절한 채로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀의 창백한 얼굴, 달라진 상복, 입가의 떨림, 그 모든 것이 \"나는 알아요\"라고 말하고 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 손과 눈길이 부드럽게 그에게 머물렀다. 그는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 그녀가 그의 곁에 앉아 두 사람은 함께 울었다. 그들은 그녀가 그와 함께 짊어지고 있는 수치에 대해서도, 그 수치를 그들 위로 끌어내린 행위들에 대해서도, 아직 서로에게 말할 수 없었다. 그의 고백은 침묵이었고, 그녀의 변치 않겠다는 약속도 침묵이었다. 마음이 트인 사람이었음에도 불구하고, 그녀는 그들의 공통된 의식을 표현해 줄 그 말들을 마치 불꽃을 피하듯 움츠리며 피했다. 그녀는 \"어디까지가 그저 중상과 거짓된 의심인가요?\"라고 말할 수 없었고, 그도 \"나는 결백하오\"라고 말하지 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXV.\n\n로사먼드는 위협적인 인물이 집에서 사라지고 그 모든 불쾌한 채권자들에게 빚을 갚고 나자 잠시 명랑함을 되찾는 듯한 빛을 보였다. 하지만 그녀는 즐겁지 않았다. 결혼 생활은 그녀의 어떤 희망도 채워 주지 못했고, 그녀의 상상 속에서 완전히 망쳐진 것이었다. 이 짧은 평온의 막간에, 리드게이트는 마음이 어지러울 때 자신이 종종 사나워졌던 것을 떠올리고 로사먼드가 견뎌야 했던 고통을 마음에 두며 그녀에게 조심스럽게 부드러이 대했다. 그러나 그 또한 예전의 활기를 어느 정도 잃었고, 살림살이를 절약해야 한다는 변화를 당연한 일로 그녀에게 거론할 필요를 여전히 느꼈으며, 그녀를 차츰 그 변화에 따르도록 설득하려 애쓰면서, 그녀가 차라리 런던에 가서 살았으면 좋겠다고 답할 때마다 치미는 분노를 억눌렀다. 그녀가 그런 대답을 하지 않을 때는 나른하게 귀를 기울이며, 자신에게 살아갈 가치가 있는 것이 무엇이나 있는지 의아해했다. 남편이 분노 속에서 내뱉은 모질고 경멸하는 말들은 그가 처음에는 능동적인 즐거움으로 일깨워 주었던 그 허영심을 깊이 상하게 했다. 그리고 그녀가 사물을 바라보는 그의 비뚤어진 방식이라고 여긴 것은 은밀한 반발심을 부추겨, 그가 베푸는 모든 다정함을 그가 끝내 주지 못한 행복의 초라한 대체물로 받아들이게 만들었다. 그들은 이웃들 사이에서 불리한 처지에 놓였고, 콸링엄을 향한 전망도 더 이상 없었다. 윌 래디슬로에게서 이따금 오는 편지 말고는 어디에도 전망이 없었다. 그녀는 윌이 미들마치를 떠나기로 결심한 데 마음이 찔리고 실망했으니, 그가 도로테아를 흠모한다는 것을 알고 짐작했음에도, 그가 자신을 훨씬 더 흠모하고 있거나 반드시 그렇게 되리라는 믿음을 은밀히 품고 있었기 때문이다. 로사먼드는 만나는 남자마다 자신을 더 좋아했을 텐데 다만 그 호감이 가망 없는 일이라 그러지 못했을 뿐이라는 생각 속에서 살아가는 부류의 여자였던 것이다. 캐소본 부인이야 더할 나위 없었지만, 윌이 그녀에게 품은 관심은 리드게이트 부인을 알기 전부터 시작된 것이었다. 로사먼드는 윌이 자기에게 말하는 방식을, 즉 장난스러운 흠잡기와 과장된 친절이 뒤섞인 그 방식을 더 깊은 감정의 위장으로 받아들였다. 그리고 그가 곁에 있으면 리드게이트의 존재가 더 이상 만들어 낼 마력을 잃어버린 그 허영심의 기분 좋은 간지러움과 낭만적인 연극 같은 감각을 느꼈다. 그녀는 심지어, 이런 문제에서 남녀가 무엇인들 상상하지 않겠는가만, 윌이 자기를 약 올리려고 캐소본 부인에 대한 흠모를 과장하는 것이라고까지 상상했다. 이런 식으로 가엾은 로사먼드의 머릿속은 윌이 떠나기 전부터 분주했다. 그녀가 보기에 윌은 리드게이트에게서 찾은 것보다 훨씬 더 어울리는 남편이 되었을 사람이었다. 이보다 더 그릇된 생각은 없었을 텐데, 로사먼드가 결혼 생활에서 느끼는 불만은 남편의 천성 때문이 아니라 자기 억제와 관용을 요구하는 결혼 자체의 조건 때문이었기 때문이다. 그러나 실재하지 않는 더 나은 것을 손쉽게 떠올리는 일에는 그녀의 권태를 달래 주는 감상적인 매력이 있었다. 그녀는 자기 삶의 단조로움을 변화시켜 줄 작은 연애담을 지어냈다. 윌 래디슬로는 언제까지나 독신으로 그녀 곁에 살면서 늘 그녀의 부름에 응하고, 결코 온전히 표현되지는 않으나 서로 이해되는 열정을 그녀에게 품고 있어, 이따금 흥미로운 장면들 속에서 그 열정이 가물거리는 불꽃을 내뿜으리라는 것이었다. 그가 떠난 것은 그만큼의 실망이었고, 미들마치에 대한 그녀의 권태를 슬프게도 더욱 키워 놓았다. 그러나 처음에는 콸링엄 가족과의 교류에서 누릴 즐거움이 기다리고 있으리라는 대체용 꿈이 있었다. 그 후로 결혼 생활의 시련은 깊어졌고, 다른 위안이 없다 보니 한때 마음의 양식으로 삼았던 그 빈약한 연애담을 아쉬워하며 곱씹게 되었다. 남녀는 자기 자신의 징후에 대해 슬픈 착각을 저질러, 막연하고 불안한 갈망을 어떤 때는 천재성으로, 어떤 때는 신앙심으로, 그리고 더욱 흔하게는 위대한 사랑으로 여긴다. 윌 래디슬로는 절반은 그녀에게, 절반은 리드게이트에게 보내는 식의 수다스러운 편지를 써 보냈고, 그녀는 답장을 했다. 그들의 이별이 최종적인 것은 아니리라고 그녀는 느꼈고, 이제 그녀가 가장 갈망하는 변화는 리드게이트가 런던에 가서 사는 것이었다. 런던에서라면 모든 것이 즐거우리라. 그래서 그녀가 조용한 결의로 이 결과를 얻어 내기 위해 공을 들이기 시작했을 무렵, 그녀에게 활기를 불어넣는 갑작스럽고 반가운 약속이 찾아왔다.\n\n그것은 시청에서 열린 그 잊지 못할 모임이 있기 직전에 온 것으로, 다름 아닌 윌 래디슬로가 리드게이트에게 보낸 편지였다. 편지는 사실 주로 그가 새로 관심을 갖게 된 식민 사업 계획에 관한 것이었으나, 앞으로 몇 주 안에 미들마치를 한번 찾아야 할지도 모르겠다고 지나가는 말처럼 언급하고 있었다. 그것은 학생에게 방학이나 다름없이 좋은, 아주 즐거운 일이라고 그는 말했다. 난로 앞 깔개 위의 자기 옛 자리가 그대로 있기를, 그리고 들을 음악이 잔뜩 마련되어 있기를 바란다고 했다. 다만 시기에 대해서는 도무지 확실치 않다고 했다. 리드게이트가 로사먼드에게 편지를 읽어 주는 동안, 그녀의 얼굴은 되살아나는 꽃처럼 보였다. 더 예뻐지고 더 활짝 피어났다. 이제 견딜 수 없는 일은 아무것도 없었다. 빚은 갚았고, 래디슬로 씨가 올 것이며, 리드게이트는 미들마치를 떠나 \"지방 도시와는 너무도 다른\" 런던에 정착하도록 설득될 것이었다.\n\n그것은 환한 아침의 한 조각이었다. 그러나 곧 가엾은 로사먼드의 머리 위로 하늘이 시커멓게 변했다. 남편에게 새로운 우울이 깃들었으나 그는 그것에 관해 그녀에게 완전히 입을 다물었으니, 자신의 갈가리 찢긴 감정을 그녀의 무관심과 오해에 드러내기가 두려웠던 것이다. 그 우울은 곧 고통스럽고도 기이한 설명을 얻었는데, 그것은 자신의 행복에 영향을 미칠 수 있는 것에 관한 그녀의 종래 관념과는 전혀 동떨어진 것이었다. 새로 명랑해진 기분 속에서, 그녀는 리드게이트가 그저 평소보다 더 심한 침울에 빠진 것뿐이어서 자기 말에 대꾸도 하지 않고 가능한 한 자신을 피하려는 것이 분명하다고 생각하고는, 모임이 있은 며칠 뒤에 그 일에 관해 남편에게 말하지도 않은 채 작은 저녁 모임의 초대장을 보내기로 했다. 사람들이 자기들을 멀리하는 듯하니 예전의 교류 습관을 되살릴 필요가 있다고 여겨, 이것이 분별 있는 조치라고 확신했던 것이다. 초대가 받아들여지면 리드게이트에게 알리고, 의사라면 이웃들에게 어떻게 처신해야 하는지 현명하게 타이를 작정이었다. 로사먼드는 다른 사람들의 의무에 관해서라면 더할 나위 없이 진지하고 자그마한 태도를 지니고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 초대는 모두 거절당했고, 마지막 답장이 리드게이트의 손에 들어왔다.\n\n\"이건 치철리의 갈겨쓴 글씨로군. 당신한테 무슨 일로 편지를 쓴 거지?\" 리드게이트가 의아해하며 그녀에게 쪽지를 건네면서 말했다. 그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 그에게 그것을 보여 줄 수밖에 없었고, 그는 그녀를 매섭게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"도대체 왜 나한테 말도 없이 초대장을 보낸 거요, 로사먼드? 부탁이오, 이 집에는 그 누구도 초대하지 말라고 강력히 요구하는 바이오. 다른 사람들도 초대한 모양인데, 그들도 거절했겠지.\" 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"내 말 듣고 있소?\" 리드게이트가 우레같이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"네, 물론 듣고 있어요.\" 로사먼드가 우아하게 목이 긴 새 같은 몸짓으로 고개를 돌리며 말했다.\n\n리드게이트는 조금도 우아하지 않게 머리를 홱 젖히고는 방을 나가 버렸으니, 자기 자신이 위험한 사람처럼 느껴졌던 것이다. 로사먼드의 생각은 그가 점점 더 견딜 수 없어진다는 것이었다. 이 단호함에 무슨 새로운 특별한 이유가 있어서가 아니었다. 그녀가 흥미를 가질 리 없다고 미리 확신하는 일이라면 무엇도 그녀에게 말하지 않으려는 그의 성향은 무심한 습관으로 굳어 가고 있었고, 그녀는 그 천 파운드와 관련된 모든 일에 대해, 그 돈이 외삼촌 벌스트로드에게서 빌린 것이라는 사실 말고는 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그녀에게 리드게이트의 고약한 기분과 이웃들이 자기들을 분명히 피하는 일은 돈 문제에서 벗어난 것과 더불어 도무지 설명할 수 없는 시점에 일어난 일이었다. 초대가 받아들여졌더라면 그녀는 며칠째 보지 못한 어머니와 다른 가족들을 초대하러 갔을 것이다. 그런데 이제 그녀는 모든 이를 노하게 만들기로 작정한 남편과 함께 자신을 고립 속에 가두려는 음모라도 있는 듯 갑자기 느끼면서, 그들 모두가 어찌 된 일인지 알아보러 가려고 보닛을 썼다. 만찬 시간이 지난 뒤였고, 그녀는 응접실에 아버지와 어머니만 함께 앉아 있는 것을 발견했다. 두 사람은 슬픈 표정으로 \"오냐, 얘야!\" 하고 그녀를 맞이할 뿐 더는 말이 없었다. 그녀는 아버지가 그토록 풀이 죽은 모습을 본 적이 없었다. 아버지 곁에 앉으며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있나요, 아버지?\"\n\n그는 대답하지 않았으나, 빈시 부인이 말했다. \"오, 얘야, 아무 얘기도 못 들었니? 머지않아 네 귀에도 들어갈 텐데.\"\n\n\"테르티우스에 관한 일인가요?\" 로사먼드가 창백해지며 말했다. 시련이라는 생각은 곧바로 그에게서 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었던 일과 연결되었다.\n\n\"오, 얘야, 그래. 네가 이런 시련 속으로 시집을 가다니. 빚도 충분히 나빴지만, 이건 더 나쁠 거다.\"\n\n\"가만, 가만있어요, 루시.\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. \"벌스트로드 외삼촌에 관한 얘기는 아무것도 못 들었니, 로사먼드?\"\n\n\"못 들었어요, 아버지.\" 가엾은 것이 말했으니, 시련이라는 것이 전에 겪어 본 그 무엇이 아니라 강철 같은 손아귀로 자기 영혼을 안에서 까무러치게 만드는 어떤 보이지 않는 힘처럼 느껴졌던 것이다.\n\n아버지는 그녀에게 모든 것을 이야기한 뒤 끝에 가서 말했다. \"네가 아는 편이 낫겠다, 얘야. 리드게이트는 이 마을을 떠나야 할 게다. 일이 그에게 불리하게 돌아갔어. 어쩔 수 없었으리라 싶다. 그를 무슨 잘못이 있다고 탓하는 건 아니다.\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. 그는 전에는 늘 리드게이트의 흠을 최대한 잡으려 들었던 사람이었다.\n\n로사먼드가 받은 충격은 끔찍했다. 그녀가 보기에 치욕스러운 의심의 중심이 되어 버린 남자와 결혼한 자기 처지만큼 가혹하게 모진 운명은 없을 것 같았다. 많은 경우 수치심이 범죄의 가장 나쁜 부분으로 느껴지는 것은 어쩔 수 없는 일이다. 그리고 이런 순간에 자신의 시련이 남편이 분명히 무슨 범죄를 저질렀다고 알려진 경우보다 덜하다고 느끼려면, 로사먼드의 인생에 한 번도 들어와 본 적 없는 종류의, 실타래를 풀어 가는 깊은 성찰이 무척이나 필요했을 것이다. 모든 수치가 거기 있는 것만 같았다. 그런데 그녀는 그와 그의 가문이 자신에게 영광이 되리라 믿고 순진하게도 이 남자와 결혼했던 것이다! 그녀는 부모에게 늘 그렇듯 말을 아꼈고, 다만 리드게이트가 자기 바람대로만 했더라면 진작에 미들마치를 떠났을 거라고만 말했다.\n\n\"저 애는 정말 잘도 견디는군요.\" 그녀가 떠난 뒤 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 다행이오!\" 몹시 무너져 내린 빈시 씨가 말했다.\n\n그러나 로사먼드는 남편에 대해 정당한 혐오감을 품고 집으로 돌아갔다. 그가 정말로 무슨 짓을 했는가, 정말로 어떻게 행동했는가? 그녀는 알지 못했다. 왜 그는 자기에게 모든 것을 말해 주지 않았는가? 그는 그 일에 관해 그녀에게 말하지 않았고, 물론 그녀도 그에게 말할 수가 없었다. 한번은 아버지에게 다시 친정으로 가서 살게 해 달라고 청해 볼까 하는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그 전망을 곰곰이 생각하니 그것은 더없는 황량함으로만 여겨졌다. 결혼한 여자가 부모에게로 되돌아가 사는 것이라니, 그런 처지에서는 삶에 아무런 의미도 없을 것 같았다. 그녀는 그런 처지에 놓인 자신을 도저히 그려 볼 수 없었다.\n\n그 후 이틀 동안 리드게이트는 그녀에게 일어난 변화를 알아챘고, 그녀가 그 나쁜 소식을 들었다고 믿었다. 그녀는 그 일에 관해 자기에게 말을 할 것인가, 아니면 자기를 유죄라고 믿는 듯한 그 침묵을 영원히 이어 갈 것인가? 그가 거의 모든 접촉이 고통인 병적인 마음 상태에 있었음을 우리는 기억해야 한다. 분명 이 경우 로사먼드도 그가 입을 다물고 마음을 터놓지 않는 데 대해 똑같이 불평할 이유가 있었다. 그러나 영혼의 쓰라림 속에서 그는 자신을 변명했다. 이제 그녀가 진실을 알게 되었는데도 자기에게 말을 걸 마음이 없으니, 그녀에게 알리는 일에서 물러서는 것이 정당하지 않은가? 그러나 자신에게 잘못이 있다는 더 깊이 자리 잡은 의식이 그를 불안하게 했고, 둘 사이의 침묵은 그에게 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었다. 마치 두 사람이 한 조각 난파선에 몸을 싣고 떠다니면서 서로 외면하고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n그는 생각했다. \"난 바보다. 무언가를 기대하는 걸 이미 포기하지 않았던가? 나는 도움이 아니라 근심거리와 결혼했어.\" 그날 저녁 그가 말했다.\n\n\"로사먼드, 당신을 괴롭히는 무슨 얘기를 들었소?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 평소의 자기 모습과는 전혀 다르게 나른하고 반쯤 넋이 나간 채로 해 오던 바느질감을 내려놓으며 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 얘기를 들었소?\"\n\n\"전부 다요, 아마. 아버지가 말씀해 주셨어요.\"\n\n\"사람들이 나를 망신스럽다고 여긴다는 거?\"\n\n\"네.\" 로사먼드가 희미하게 말하며 다시 기계적으로 바느질을 시작했다.\n\n침묵이 흘렀다. 리드게이트는 생각했다. \"그녀가 나를 조금이라도 믿는다면, 내가 어떤 사람인지 조금이라도 안다면, 지금 입을 열어 내가 망신을 당해 마땅하다고는 믿지 않는다고 말해야 마땅하다.\"\n\n그러나 로사먼드는 로사먼드대로 나른하게 손가락만 계속 움직였다. 이 일에 관해 할 말이 무엇이든 그것은 테르티우스에게서 나오리라고 그녀는 기대했다. 자기가 무엇을 안단 말인가? 그리고 그가 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았다면, 왜 자기 결백을 밝히기 위해 무언가를 하지 않는단 말인가?\n\n그녀의 이 침묵은 리드게이트가 아무도 자기를 믿어 주지 않는다고, 페어브러더조차 나서지 않았다고 혼잣말하던 그 쓰라린 기분에 새로운 쓸개즙을 한 줄기 끼얹었다. 그는 둘 사이에 자욱이 낀 차가운 안개를 대화로 흩어 버리려는 뜻에서 그녀에게 묻기 시작했으나, 절망 어린 원망에 자신의 결심이 가로막히는 것을 느꼈다. 다른 모든 시련과 마찬가지로 이 시련조차 그녀는 마치 자기 혼자만의 것인 양 여기는 듯했다. 그는 그녀에게 언제나 동떨어진 존재, 그녀가 반대하는 일을 하는 존재였다. 그는 분노의 충동에 사로잡혀 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었다. 그러는 내내 이 분노를 다스리고 그녀에게 모든 것을 말하여 사실을 납득시켜야 한다는 의식이 밑바닥에 깔려 있었다. 자신이 그녀의 천성에 맞추어 굽혀야 한다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 공감이 부족한 만큼 자신이 더 많이 베풀어야 한다는 것을 그는 거의 깨우친 터였기 때문이다. 곧 그는 마음을 털어놓겠다는 자신의 뜻으로 되돌아왔다. 이 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 되었다. 만약 여기 맞서 싸워야 하고 도망쳐서는 안 될 중상모략이 있다는 것을, 그리고 이 모든 시련이 절박한 돈 부족에서 비롯되었다는 것을 그녀가 어느 정도 엄숙하게 느끼게 할 수 있다면, 그때야말로 그들이 한마음이 되어 가능한 한 돈을 적게 쓰기로 결의함으로써 이 험한 시기를 헤쳐 나가고 자립을 지켜야 한다고 그녀에게 강력히 촉구할 순간이 될 것이었다. 그는 자신이 취하고자 하는 구체적인 방책들을 언급하여 그녀를 기꺼운 마음으로 이끌어 낼 작정이었다. 그는 마땅히 이를 시도해야 했다. 그것 말고 그가 달리 할 수 있는 일이 무엇이 있었겠는가?\n\n그는 자신이 얼마나 오래 불안하게 방 안을 오가고 있었는지 알지 못했으나, 로사먼드는 그것이 길다고 느껴 그가 좀 앉았으면 했다. 그녀 또한 이것을 테르티우스에게 그가 해야 할 일을 촉구할 기회라고 생각하기 시작했던 것이다. 이 모든 비참한 일에 관한 진실이 무엇이든, 떨쳐 낼 수 없이 고개를 드는 한 가지 두려움이 있었다.\n\n리드게이트는 마침내 자리에 앉았는데, 평소의 자기 의자가 아니라 로사먼드에게 더 가까운 의자였다. 그는 그쪽으로 비스듬히 몸을 기울인 채, 그 슬픈 화제를 다시 꺼내기 전에 그녀를 진지하게 바라보았다. 그는 그만큼 자신을 이겨 낸 터였고, 다시는 되풀이되지 않을 일을 대하듯 엄숙한 마음으로 입을 떼려던 참이었다. 그가 막 입술을 열었을 때, 로사먼드가 두 손을 떨어뜨리고는 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"테르티우스, 분명히—\"\n\n\"뭐요?\"\n\n\"분명히 이제는 마침내 당신도 미들마치에 머무르겠다는 생각을 버리셨겠죠. 저는 여기서 더 살 수 없어요. 우리 런던으로 가요. 아버지도, 다른 모든 사람도 당신이 떠나는 게 낫겠다고 말해요. 제가 무슨 비참한 일을 견뎌야 하든, 여기를 떠나면 한결 견디기 쉬울 거예요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 비참하게 가슴이 어긋나는 것을 느꼈다. 애써 마음의 준비를 했던 그 결정적인 토로 대신, 또다시 거쳐야 할 그 해묵은 되풀이가 거기 있었던 것이다. 그는 그것을 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 안색이 홱 변하더니 일어나서 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n어쩌면 그가 그녀가 덜한 만큼 자기가 더하리라는 결심을 끝까지 밀고 나갈 만큼 강했더라면, 그날 저녁은 더 나은 결말을 맞았을지도 모른다. 그의 기력이 그 좌절을 눌러 이겨 낼 수 있었더라면, 그는 그래도 로사먼드의 시야와 의지에 영향을 미쳤을지도 모른다. 아무리 굽힐 줄 모르고 별난 천성이라 해도 자기보다 더 묵직한 존재로부터 받는 이 영향에 저항하리라고는 우리는 장담할 수 없다. 그런 천성은 폭풍처럼 휘몰아치는 기세에 사로잡혀 한순간 돌아서서, 격렬한 움직임으로 자신을 감싸는 그 영혼의 일부가 될 수도 있다. 그러나 가엾은 리드게이트는 가슴 속에 욱신거리는 고통을 안고 있었고, 그의 기력은 그 과업에 미치지 못했다.\n\n서로를 이해하고 결의를 다지는 일의 첫걸음은 여전히 아득히 멀게만 보였다. 아니, 실패로 끝난 노력에 대한 의식에 가로막혀 버린 것만 같았다. 두 사람은 여전히 생각을 따로 둔 채 하루하루를 살아갔고, 리드게이트는 자기에게 있는 일이 무엇이든 절망적인 기분으로 처리했으며, 로사먼드는 다소 근거 있게도 그가 잔인하게 굴고 있다고 느꼈다. 테르티우스에게 무슨 말을 해 봐야 소용없었다. 그러나 윌 래디슬로가 오면, 그에게 모든 것을 이야기하리라고 그녀는 마음먹었다. 평소의 그 과묵함에도 불구하고, 그녀는 자신이 부당한 일을 당했음을 알아줄 누군가가 필요했던 것이다.\n\n# 제72장\n\n감사를 되돌려 바친다.\n. . . . . .\n\n자비는 인간의 심장을 지녔고,\n연민은 인간의 얼굴을 지녔으며,\n\n사랑은 신성한 인간의 형상이요,\n평화는 인간의 옷차림이니.\n\n―윌리엄 블레이크, 『순수의 노래』\n며칠 후 리드게이트는 도러시아의 부름을 받아 로윅 저택으로 말을 달리고 있었다. 그 부름은 뜻밖의 일이 아니었으니, 불스트로드 씨의 편지가 먼저 도착한 뒤에 이어진 것이었기 때문이다. 그 편지에서 그는 미들마치를 떠나기 위한 준비를 다시 진행하고 있으며, 병원에 관해 이전에 주고받은 이야기를 리드게이트에게 상기시켜야겠다고, 그리고 자신은 여전히 그 취지를 고수한다고 밝혔다. 더 나아가기 전에 캐소본 부인과 그 문제를 다시 의논하는 것이 자신의 의무였는데, 부인은 전과 마찬가지로 그 문제를 리드게이트와 논의하고 싶어 한다는 것이었다. \"당신의 견해가 어쩌면 다소 달라졌을지도 모르겠습니다.\" 불스트로드 씨는 이렇게 썼다. \"하지만 그렇다 하더라도 그 견해를 부인께 말씀드리는 것이 바람직할 것입니다.\"\n\n도러시아는 간절한 관심을 품고 그가 오기를 기다렸다. 남성 조언자들의 뜻을 존중하여 제임스 경이 \"이 불스트로드 일에 끼어드는 것\"이라 부른 행동은 삼갔지만, 리드게이트가 처한 곤경은 끊임없이 그녀의 마음에 걸렸다. 그리하여 불스트로드가 병원 문제로 다시 그녀에게 부탁을 해 왔을 때, 그녀는 그동안 서두르지 못하도록 막혀 있던 기회가 마침내 찾아왔다고 느꼈다. 호화로운 집에서 자기 소유의 큰 나무들이 드리운 가지 아래를 거닐 때면, 그녀의 생각은 다른 사람들의 처지로 뻗어 나갔고 그녀의 감정은 갇혀 있었다. 자신의 손이 닿는 곳에 어떤 능동적인 선행이 있다는 생각이 \"하나의 열정처럼 그녀를 사로잡았고\", 다른 사람의 절박함이 한번 또렷한 형상으로 그녀에게 다가오자, 그것은 도움을 베풀고자 하는 갈망으로 그녀의 욕망을 가득 채워 자기 자신의 안락을 무미건조하게 만들었다. 그녀는 리드게이트와의 이 면담에 대해 확신에 찬 희망으로 가득 차 있었으니, 그의 개인적인 과묵함에 관한 소문 따위는 조금도 개의치 않았고, 자신이 매우 젊은 여자라는 사실도 전혀 마음에 두지 않았다. 인간적 동료애를 보이고자 마음이 움직였을 때, 그녀의 젊음과 성별을 들먹이는 것만큼 도러시아에게 무관해 보이는 일은 없었을 것이다.\n\n서재에 앉아 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 리드게이트를 기억 속에 떠올리게 한 지난 모든 장면들을 다시 살아 보는 것밖에 할 수 없었다. 그 장면들은 모두 자신의 결혼과 그로 인한 고난에서 의미를 얻은 것이었다―아니, 그렇지 않았다. 리드게이트의 모습이 그의 아내와 또 다른 누군가와 연관되어 고통스럽게 떠오른 경우가 두 차례 있었다. 그 고통은 도러시아에게 누그러졌지만, 리드게이트의 결혼이 그에게 어떤 것일까 하는 일깨워진 추측을, 리드게이트 부인에 관한 아주 사소한 암시에도 예민해지는 감수성을 그녀 안에 남겨 두었다. 이런 생각들은 그녀에게 하나의 연극과도 같아서 두 눈을 빛나게 했고, 비록 갈색 서재에서 잔디밭과, 짙은 상록수를 배경으로 도드라진 밝은 초록빛 새순을 내다보고 있을 뿐이었지만, 그녀의 온몸에 긴장된 기대의 자세를 부여했다.\n\n리드게이트가 들어왔을 때, 그녀는 그의 얼굴이 변한 모습에 거의 충격을 받았는데, 두 달 동안 그를 보지 못한 그녀에게는 그 변화가 유난히 또렷이 느껴졌다. 그것은 수척해진 변화가 아니라, 젊은 얼굴조차도 분노와 낙담이 끈질기게 자리 잡으면 머지않아 드러내고 마는 그런 변화였다. 그녀가 손을 내밀며 보낸 다정한 눈빛은 그의 표정을 누그러뜨렸으나, 그 누그러짐은 오직 우수를 띠고 있었다.\n\n\"오래전부터 당신을 정말 뵙고 싶었어요, 리드게이트 선생님.\" 서로 마주 보고 앉았을 때 도러시아가 말했다. \"하지만 불스트로드 씨가 병원 문제로 다시 제게 부탁할 때까지 와 주십사 청하는 것을 미루었어요. 병원 운영을 진료소 운영과 분리해 두는 것의 이점이 선생님께 달려 있다는 것을, 적어도 선생님이 그것을 직접 관할함으로써 기대해도 좋다고 여기시는 그 좋은 결과에 달려 있다는 것을 저는 알아요. 그러니 선생님 생각을 정확히 말씀해 주시기를 거절하지 않으시리라 믿어요.\"\n\n\"부인께서는 병원에 후한 지원을 해야 할지 말지를 결정하고 싶으신 거지요.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"양심상 저는 제 어떤 활동을 믿고 그렇게 하시라고 권할 수가 없습니다. 저는 어쩌면 이 도시를 떠나야 할지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n그는 로저먼드가 반대하기로 마음먹은 어떤 목적도 자신이 이루어 낼 수 없으리라는 절망의 아픔을 느끼며 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"선생님을 믿어 주는 사람이 아무도 없어서 그런 건 아니지요?\" 도러시아가 가득 찬 가슴에서 명료하게 말을 쏟아내며 물었다. \"선생님에 관한 그 불행한 오해들을 저는 알아요. 처음 순간부터 그것이 오해라는 것을 알았어요. 선생님은 결코 비열한 짓을 하지 않으셨어요. 명예롭지 못한 일은 하실 분이 아니지요.\"\n그것은 리드게이트의 귀에 떨어진, 그를 믿어 준다는 첫 번째 확언이었다. 그는 깊은 숨을 들이쉬고 말했다. \"고맙습니다.\" 그는 더는 말할 수 없었다. 한 여자에게서 나온 이 몇 마디 신뢰의 말이 자신에게 이토록 큰 의미가 된다는 것은 그의 삶에서 무척 새롭고 낯선 일이었다.\n\n\"모든 일이 어떻게 된 것인지 제게 말씀해 주시기를 간청해요.\" 도러시아가 두려움 없이 말했다. \"진실이 선생님의 결백을 밝혀 주리라 확신해요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 자신이 어디에 있는지도 잊은 채 창문 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그는 어쩌면 부당하게도 불스트로드에게 불리하게 작용할 외관을 악화시키지 않으면서 모든 것을 설명할 수 있는 가능성을 마음속으로 여러 번 따져 보았고, 그때마다 그렇게 하지 않기로 결정했다. 자신이 주장한다 한들 사람들의 인상을 바꾸지는 못하리라고 여러 번 스스로에게 되뇌었기에, 도러시아의 말은 마치 냉정한 상태에서 자신이 비이성적이라고 단정해 버린 일을 하라는 유혹처럼 들렸다.\n\"제발 말씀해 주세요.\" 도러시아가 꾸밈없는 진지함으로 말했다. \"그러면 함께 의논할 수 있어요. 막을 수 있는데도 사람들이 누군가를 거짓으로 나쁘게 생각하도록 내버려 두는 것은 사악한 일이에요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 자신이 어디에 있는지를 떠올리고 몸을 돌렸다가, 다정하고 신뢰에 찬 진지함으로 자신을 올려다보는 도러시아의 얼굴을 보았다. 소망에 너그럽고 자애에 열렬한 고결한 천성이 곁에 있으면 우리에게 비치는 빛이 달라진다. 우리는 다시 사물을 더 크고 고요한 덩어리로 보기 시작하며, 우리 또한 우리 인격 전체로서 보이고 판단받을 수 있다고 믿게 된다. 그 영향력이 리드게이트에게 작용하기 시작했으니, 그는 여러 날 동안 군중 속에서 떠밀리며 발버둥 치는 사람의 눈으로 온 삶을 바라보아 왔던 터였다. 그는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 자신을 믿어 주는 사람과 함께 있다는 의식 속에서 옛날의 자신을 되찾고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"저는,\" 그가 말했다. \"불스트로드를 가혹하게 몰아붙이고 싶지 않습니다. 그는 제게 필요했던 돈을 빌려준 사람이니까요―물론 이제 와서는 차라리 그 돈 없이 지냈더라면 싶지만요. 그는 쫓기는 신세에 비참하고, 몸에는 가느다란 생명의 실 한 가닥밖에 남지 않았습니다. 하지만 부인께는 모든 것을 말씀드리고 싶습니다. 믿음이 미리 자리 잡고 있는 곳에서, 제 정직함을 스스로 주장하는 것처럼 보이지 않아도 되는 곳에서 이야기하는 것은 제게 위안이 될 겁니다. 부인께서는 제게 무엇이 공정한지를 느끼시듯, 다른 사람에게도 무엇이 공정한지를 느끼실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"부디 저를 믿어 주세요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"선생님의 허락 없이는 어떤 것도 옮기지 않겠어요. 하지만 적어도 선생님이 제게 모든 상황을 분명히 밝혀 주셨고, 선생님이 어떤 식으로도 죄가 없다는 것을 제가 안다는 말은 할 수 있어요. 페어브러더 씨도 제 말을 믿어 줄 거예요. 제 삼촌도, 제임스 체텀 경도요. 아니, 미들마치에는 제가 찾아갈 수 있는 사람들이 있어요. 그들이 저를 잘 알지는 못해도 제 말을 믿어 줄 거예요. 제게 진실과 정의 외에 다른 동기가 있을 리 없다는 것을 그들도 알 테니까요. 선생님의 결백을 밝히기 위해서라면 어떤 수고도 마다하지 않겠어요. 저는 할 일이 거의 없어요. 이 세상에서 제가 할 수 있는 일 가운데 이보다 더 나은 것은 없어요.\"\n도러시아가 자신이 하겠다는 이 아이 같은 그림을 그릴 때 그녀의 목소리는, 그녀가 그 일을 실제로 효과적으로 해낼 수 있으리라는 증거로 받아들여질 만했다. 파고드는 듯한 그녀의 여성스러운 음색의 부드러움은 성급한 고발자들에 맞서는 방패로 만들어진 듯했다. 리드게이트는 그녀가 돈키호테 같다고 생각하느라 머뭇거리지 않았다. 그는 평생 처음으로 자존심 어린 과묵함의 제어도 없이, 너그러운 공감에 온전히 기대는 그 더없이 절묘한 느낌에 자신을 내맡겼다. 그리하여 그는 그녀에게 모든 것을 이야기했다. 곤경에 짓눌려 마지못해 처음 불스트로드에게 부탁했던 때부터 시작하여, 이야기를 털어놓는 가운데 점차 마음의 안도를 얻으며 자기 마음속에서 일어났던 일들을 더 철저하게 말하게 되었다. 환자를 치료한 자신의 방식이 지배적인 관행에 어긋났다는 사실, 마지막 순간에 품었던 의심, 의술의 의무에 대한 자신의 이상, 그리고 비록 공적으로 인정된 어떤 의무를 이행하는 데는 차이가 없었지만 그 돈을 받은 것이 자신의 사적인 성향과 직업적 처신에 어떤 차이를 만들어 냈다는 불편한 자각까지 낱낱이 들어가서 이야기했다.\n\n\"그 후에 제가 알게 된 사실인데요,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"홀리가 스톤 코트의 가정부를 캐묻도록 누군가를 보냈고, 그녀는 제가 남기고 온 약병에 든 아편을 전부, 그리고 상당량의 브랜디까지 환자에게 주었다고 말했다더군요. 하지만 그것은 일류 의사들의 평범한 처방에도 어긋나는 일은 아니었을 겁니다. 저를 향한 의심은 거기서는 근거를 잃습니다. 그 의심들은 제가 돈을 받았다는 것, 불스트로드에게는 그 사람이 죽기를 바랄 강력한 동기가 있었다는 것, 그가 환자에게 행해진 이런저런 부정한 처치에 가담하라고 제게 뇌물로 그 돈을 주었다는 것―어쨌든 제가 입을 다물도록 뇌물을 받았다는 것에 근거를 두고 있습니다. 그것들은 가장 끈질기게 들러붙는 종류의 의심인데, 사람들의 성향 속에 자리 잡고 있어서 결코 반증할 수 없기 때문이지요. 제 지시가 어쩌다 어겨지게 되었는지는 제가 답을 알지 못하는 문제입니다. 불스트로드에게 어떤 범죄적 의도가 없었을 가능성도 여전히 있습니다. 그가 그 불복종과 아무 관련이 없었고 단지 그것을 언급하지 않고 넘어갔을 가능성조차 있지요. 하지만 그 모든 것은 대중의 믿음과는 아무 상관이 없습니다. 이것은 한 사람이 그 인격을 근거로 단죄당하는 그런 경우 가운데 하나입니다. 어떤 모호한 방식으로 죄를 저질렀으리라고 사람들이 믿는데, 그에게 그럴 동기가 있었기 때문이지요. 그리고 불스트로드의 인격이 저를 감싸 버린 것은, 제가 그의 돈을 받았기 때문입니다. 저는 그냥 시들어 버렸습니다―병든 옥수수 이삭처럼요―이미 끝난 일이고 되돌릴 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 가혹한 일이군요!\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"선생님이 스스로를 변호하기가 얼마나 어려운지 이해해요. 그리고 이 모든 일이, 평범한 삶보다 더 높은 삶을 살고 더 나은 길을 찾아내려 했던 선생님께 닥쳤다는 것이―저는 이것을 바꿀 수 없는 일로 받아들이고만 있을 수가 없어요. 선생님이 그런 뜻을 품었다는 것을 알아요. 처음 병원 이야기를 제게 하셨을 때 하신 말씀을 기억해요. 위대한 것을 사랑하고, 거기에 다다르려 애쓰지만 끝내 실패하는 것―이보다 더 깊이 생각해 본 슬픔은 제게 없어요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 여기서 비로소 자기 슬픔의 온전한 의미를 담을 자리를 찾은 듯 느꼈다. \"제게는 약간의 야망이 있었습니다. 저는 모든 것을 남들과 다르게 하고자 했어요. 제게는 더 큰 힘과 자제력이 있다고 생각했지요. 하지만 가장 끔찍한 장애물은 자기 자신 말고는 아무도 볼 수 없는 그런 것입니다.\"\n\n\"가령,\" 도러시아가 사색에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"가령 우리가 지금 계획대로 병원을 계속 유지하고, 선생님이 비록 몇몇 사람의 우정과 지지뿐일지라도 이곳에 머무신다면, 선생님을 향한 나쁜 감정은 차차 사라질 거예요. 사람들이 자기들이 선생님께 부당하게 굴었음을 인정할 수밖에 없는 기회가 찾아올 거예요. 선생님의 목적이 순수했음을 그들도 알게 될 테니까요. 선생님이 말씀하시던 그 루이나 라에네크처럼 여전히 큰 명성을 얻으실 수 있어요. 그러면 우리 모두 선생님을 자랑스러워할 거예요.\" 그녀는 미소 지으며 말을 맺었다.\n\n\"제가 예전처럼 저 자신에 대한 믿음이 있다면 그럴 수도 있겠지요.\" 리드게이트가 서글프게 말했다. \"이 중상모략 앞에서 등을 돌리고 도망쳐, 그것을 제지하지 않은 채 뒤에 남겨 둔다는 생각만큼 저를 괴롭히는 것은 없습니다. 그렇지만 저는 누구에게도 저 자신에게 달린 계획에 큰돈을 쏟으라고 청할 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 제가 충분히 해 볼 만한 일이에요.\" 도러시아가 꾸밈없이 말했다. \"한번 생각해 보세요. 저는 제 돈 때문에 무척 곤란해요. 사람들은 제가 가장 좋아하는 그런 종류의 큰 계획을 펼치기에는 돈이 너무 적다고 하는데, 그렇다고 또 너무 많기도 하거든요. 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요. 제 몫의 재산에서 나오는 연 칠백 파운드와, 캐소본 씨가 제게 남긴 연 천구백 파운드, 그리고 은행에 든 현금 삼사천 파운드가 있어요. 저는 돈을 마련해서, 제게 필요하지도 않은 수입으로 차차 갚아 나가며 땅을 사고, 근면의 학교가 될 마을을 세우고 싶었어요. 하지만 제임스 경과 삼촌은 위험이 너무 크다고 저를 설득했지요. 그러니 아시겠지요, 제가 가장 기뻐할 일은 제 돈으로 무언가 좋은 일을 하는 것이에요. 그 돈으로 다른 사람들의 삶이 더 나아졌으면 좋겠어요. 그게 필요하지도 않은 제게 다 굴러들어 온다는 게 저를 무척 불편하게 해요.\"\n\n리드게이트의 얼굴을 덮고 있던 음울함 사이로 미소가 비어져 나왔다. 도러시아가 이 모든 것을 말할 때의 그 아이같이 진지한 눈빛은 거역할 수 없는 것이었다―고매한 경험에 대한 그녀의 명민한 이해와 어우러져 사랑스러운 하나의 전체를 이루고 있었다. (세상에서 큰 몫을 차지하는 그런 비속한 경험에 대해서는, 가엾은 캐소본 부인은 무척 흐릿하고 근시안적인 앎밖에 없었고 상상력의 도움도 거의 받지 못했다.) 하지만 그녀는 그 미소를 자기 계획에 대한 격려로 받아들였다.\n\n\"이제 선생님도 너무 지나치게 신중하게 말씀하셨다는 것을 아시겠지요.\" 그녀가 설득하는 어조로 말했다. \"병원도 하나의 좋은 일이고, 선생님의 삶을 다시 온전하고 건강하게 만드는 것도 또 하나의 좋은 일이에요.\"\n\n리드게이트의 미소는 사라져 버렸다. \"부인께는 그 모든 것을 하실 만한 선의도, 돈도 있으십니다. 그것이 가능하다면 말이지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만―\"\n\n그는 잠시 머뭇거리며 창문 쪽을 막연히 바라보았고, 그녀는 말없이 기대하며 앉아 있었다. 마침내 그는 그녀를 향해 돌아서더니 격하게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 부인께 말씀드리지 못할 까닭이 무엇이겠습니까?―부인께서는 결혼이라는 것이 어떤 속박인지 아시지요. 모든 것을 이해하실 겁니다.\"\n\n도러시아는 가슴이 더 빠르게 뛰기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다. 이분에게도 그런 슬픔이 있었던 것일까? 하지만 그녀는 한마디라도 꺼내기가 두려웠고, 그는 곧바로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이제 저는 아내의 행복을 고려하지 않고서는 아무것도 할 수 없습니다―어떤 발걸음도 내디딜 수가 없어요. 혼자였더라면 하고 싶었을 법한 일이, 제게는 불가능한 일이 되고 맙니다. 저는 그녀가 비참해하는 모습을 볼 수가 없어요. 그녀는 자기가 무슨 길로 들어서는지도 모른 채 저와 결혼했고, 어쩌면 저와 결혼하지 않았더라면 그녀에게 더 나았을지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"알아요, 알아요―어쩔 수 없는 경우가 아니라면 선생님은 그녀에게 고통을 주지 못하실 거예요.\" 자신의 삶을 절절히 떠올리며 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그녀는 머무르지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔습니다. 떠나기를 바라지요. 여기서 겪은 시련들이 그녀를 지치게 했어요.\" 리드게이트는 너무 많은 말을 할까 봐 또 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"하지만 머무름으로써 생길 수 있는 좋은 결과를 그녀가 본다면―\" 도러시아가 항변하듯 말하며, 마치 그가 방금 함께 고려했던 이유들을 잊어버리기라도 한 것처럼 리드게이트를 바라보았다. 그는 곧바로 말하지 않았다.\n\n\"그녀는 그것을 보려 하지 않을 겁니다.\" 마침내 그가 퉁명스럽게 말했는데, 처음에는 이 말이 설명 없이도 통하리라 느꼈다. \"그리고 사실, 저 자신도 이곳에서 제 삶을 계속 꾸려 갈 의욕을 모두 잃었습니다.\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가, 도러시아가 자기 삶의 어려움을 더 깊이 들여다보게 하고 싶은 충동에 이끌려 말했다. \"사실은, 이 시련이 그녀에게 혼란스러운 형태로 닥쳤습니다. 우리는 그것에 대해 서로 이야기를 나누지 못했어요. 그것에 관해 그녀의 마음속에 무엇이 들어 있는지 저는 확신할 수 없습니다. 어쩌면 제가 정말로 무언가 비열한 짓을 저질렀을까 봐 두려워하는지도 모르지요. 제 잘못입니다. 좀 더 터놓았어야 했어요. 하지만 저는 잔인하리만치 괴로워하고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 가서 그녀를 만나 봐도 될까요?\" 도러시아가 간절하게 말했다. \"그녀가 제 공감을 받아 줄까요? 제가 그녀에게, 선생님은 선생님 자신의 판단 외에 그 누구의 판단으로도 비난받을 일이 없다고 말해 주겠어요. 공정한 마음을 가진 사람이라면 누구에게나 선생님의 결백이 밝혀질 거라고 말해 주겠어요. 그녀의 마음을 북돋아 주겠어요. 제가 그녀를 만나러 가도 되는지 그녀에게 물어봐 주시겠어요? 전에 한 번 만난 적이 있어요.\"\n\n\"물론 되고말고요.\" 리드게이트가 약간의 희망을 품고 그 제안을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"그녀는 영광스럽게 여길 겁니다―적어도 부인께서 저를 어느 정도 존중하신다는 증거에 마음이 북돋아질 것 같습니다. 부인이 오신다는 것을 제가 그녀에게 말하지는 않겠습니다―그래야 그녀가 그것을 제 바람과 전혀 연결 짓지 않을 테니까요. 다른 사람을 통해 그녀에게 전해질 일을 남겨 두지 말았어야 한다는 것을 저도 잘 압니다만, 그래도―\"\n\n그는 말을 멈추었고, 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. 도러시아는 자기 마음속에 있던 말을―남편과 아내 사이에 말로는 넘을 수 없는 보이지 않는 장벽이 있을 수 있다는 것을 자신이 얼마나 잘 아는지를 입 밖에 내지 않았다. 이것은 공감조차도 상처를 줄 수 있는 지점이었다. 그녀는 리드게이트가 처한 상황의 좀 더 겉으로 드러난 측면으로 화제를 돌려, 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 만약 리드게이트 부인이 선생님을 믿고 지지해 줄 친구들이 있다는 것을 안다면, 선생님이 자리에 머물러 희망을 되찾고―뜻하셨던 일을 하시는 것을 그분도 기뻐할지 몰라요. 그러면 어쩌면 선생님도, 병원에 계속 머무르시는 것에 관한 제 제안에 동의하는 것이 옳다고 보시게 될 거예요. 선생님이 그것을 여전히 선생님의 지식을 유용하게 쓸 수단으로 믿고 계신다면, 분명 그러시겠지요?\"\n\n리드게이트는 대답하지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 스스로와 씨름하고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"당장 결정하실 필요는 없어요.\" 그녀가 부드럽게 말했다. \"지금부터 며칠 후에 제 답을 불스트로드 씨에게 보내도 충분히 이를 거예요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 여전히 망설였으나, 마침내 더없이 단호한 어조로 말하려 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"아닙니다. 흔들릴 여지를 남겨 두지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다. 저는 더 이상 저 자신을 충분히 확신하지 못합니다―제 삶이 처한 달라진 상황 아래서 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 말입니다. 다른 사람들이 저를 믿고 무언가 중대한 일에 발을 들이게 하는 것은 명예롭지 못한 일일 겁니다. 저는 결국 떠나야 할지도 모르고요. 그 외에 다른 가능성은 거의 보이지 않습니다. 모든 것이 너무 불확실합니다. 부인의 선의가 헛되이 쓰이는 원인이 되는 것에 저는 동의할 수 없습니다. 아닙니다―새 병원을 옛 진료소와 합치고, 마치 제가 애초에 오지 않았더라면 그랬을 법한 대로 모든 것이 흘러가게 두십시오. 그곳에 있는 동안 저는 귀중한 진료 기록을 작성해 두었습니다. 그것을 활용할 사람에게 보내겠습니다.\" 그가 쓰라리게 말을 맺었다. \"한동안은 수입을 얻는 것 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"선생님이 그렇게 절망적으로 말씀하시는 것을 듣자니 무척 마음이 아파요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"선생님의 미래를, 위대한 일을 해낼 선생님의 힘을 믿는 친구들에게는, 선생님이 그들로 하여금 선생님을 그 처지에서 구하게 해 주신다면 그것이 곧 행복일 거예요. 제게 돈이 얼마나 많은지 생각해 보세요. 선생님이 이 옥죄는 수입 부족에서 벗어나실 때까지 해마다 그 돈을 조금씩 가져가신다면, 그것은 제게서 짐을 덜어 주는 것이나 마찬가지일 거예요. 어째서 사람들이 이런 일을 하면 안 되는 걸까요? 무엇이든 고르게 나누기란 정말 어려운 일이에요. 이것이 하나의 방법이에요.\"\n\n\"부인께 신의 축복이 있기를, 캐소본 부인!\" 리드게이트가, 그의 말에 힘을 실어 준 것과 같은 충동에 이끌리듯 일어서서, 자신이 앉아 있던 커다란 가죽 의자의 등받이에 팔을 기대며 말했다. \"부인께서 그런 마음을 가지셨다는 것은 좋은 일입니다. 하지만 저는 그 마음의 덕을 보도록 제 자신을 허락해서는 안 될 사람입니다. 저는 충분한 보증을 내놓지 못했어요. 적어도 저는, 제가 결코 이루지 못한 일에 대해 연금을 받는 그런 타락에 빠져들어서는 안 됩니다. 제가 할 수 있는 한 빨리 미들마치를 떠나는 것 외에는 어떤 것도 기대해서는 안 된다는 것이 제게는 아주 분명합니다. 아무리 잘되어도 한동안은 이곳에서 수입을 얻을 수 없을 테고―그리고―낯선 곳에서 필요한 변화를 만들어 가는 것이 더 쉽지요. 저도 다른 사람들이 하는 대로 해야 합니다. 세상의 비위를 맞추고 돈벌이가 될 일을 궁리하고, 런던의 인파 속에서 작은 틈새를 찾아 비집고 들어가야지요. 온천 휴양지에 개업하거나, 한가한 영국인들이 잔뜩 있는 어느 남부 도시로 가서 제 이름을 떠벌리게 만들어야 합니다―그것이 제가 기어들어 가 그 안에서 영혼을 겨우 살려 두려 애써야 할 그런 껍데기입니다.\"\n\n\"그건 용감하지 못한 일이에요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"싸움을 포기하다니요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 용감하지 못한 일입니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. \"하지만 사람이 서서히 다가오는 마비를 두려워한다면 어쩌겠습니까?\" 그러더니 다른 어조로 말했다. \"그래도 부인께서 저를 믿어 주신 덕분에 제 용기에 큰 변화가 생겼습니다. 부인과 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 모든 것이 더 견딜 만하게 느껴집니다. 그리고 부인께서 몇몇 다른 사람들의 마음속에서, 특히 페어브러더의 마음속에서 제 결백을 밝혀 주신다면 저는 깊이 감사하겠습니다. 부인께서 언급하지 않으셨으면 하는 점은, 제 지시가 어겨졌다는 사실입니다. 그것은 금세 왜곡될 테니까요. 결국, 저를 위한 증거라고는 사람들이 미리부터 저에 대해 품고 있는 평판밖에 없습니다. 부인께서는 저 자신에 대한 제 보고를 그대로 옮기실 수 있을 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"페어브러더 씨는 믿어 줄 거예요―다른 사람들도 믿어 줄 거예요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"선생님이 사악한 짓을 저지르라고 매수당하리라 짐작하는 것이 어리석은 일이 되게 만들, 그런 말을 제가 선생님에 관해 할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다.\" 리드게이트가 목소리에 신음 같은 것을 담아 말했다. \"저는 아직 뇌물을 받은 적은 없습니다. 하지만 때때로 번영이라 불리는, 뇌물의 희미한 그림자라는 것이 있지요. 그러면 부인께서 제게 또 하나의 큰 친절을 베푸셔서, 제 아내를 보러 와 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"네, 그러겠어요. 그분이 얼마나 예쁜지 기억해요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. 로저먼드에 관한 모든 인상은 그녀의 마음에 깊이 새겨져 있었다. \"그분이 저를 좋아해 주면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n리드게이트는 말을 타고 떠나며 생각했다. \"이 젊은 사람은 성모 마리아만큼이나 큰 가슴을 지녔구나. 분명 자기 자신의 미래는 조금도 생각하지 않고, 마치 자기에게는 의자 하나만 있으면 된다는 듯이―그 맑은 눈으로 자기에게 기도하는 가엾은 인간들을 내려다볼 수 있는 의자 하나만 있으면 된다는 듯이, 당장이라도 수입의 절반을 내어 줄 사람이야. 저 사람에게는 내가 여태껏 어떤 여자에게서도 본 적 없는 것이 있어―남자들을 향한 우정의 샘이 말이야―남자가 저 사람을 친구로 삼을 수 있어. 캐소본이 저 사람에게 어떤 영웅적인 환상을 불어넣었던 게 틀림없어. 저 사람이 한 남자에게 다른 종류의 열정을 품을 수도 있었을까? 래디슬로 말인가?―확실히 두 사람 사이에는 예사롭지 않은 감정이 있었지. 그리고 캐소본도 그것을 눈치챘을 게 분명해. 흠―저 사람의 사랑은 돈보다 한 남자에게 더 큰 도움이 될지도 모르지.\"\n\n도러시아는 도러시아대로, 리드게이트를 불스트로드에 대한 빚에서 벗어나게 해 줄 계획을 곧바로 세웠는데, 그 빚이 비록 작은 것이긴 해도 그가 견뎌야 하는 그 옥죄는 압박의 한 부분이라는 것을 그녀는 확신했다. 그녀는 두 사람의 면담에서 받은 영감을 안고 곧장 자리에 앉아 짧은 편지를 썼다. 그 편지에서 그녀는, 리드게이트에게 도움이 되었던 그 돈을 마련하는 만족을 누릴 권리가 불스트로드 씨보다 자신에게 더 많다고, 이 작은 일에서 자신이 그의 조력자가 되는 위치를 그가 허락하지 않는다면 그것은 리드게이트의 박정한 처사가 될 것이며, 그 호의는 전적으로 자기 같은 사람에게 베푸는 것이라고―남아도는 돈으로 할 만한, 자기에게 분명하게 정해진 일이 거의 없는 자기 같은 사람에게 베푸는 것이라고 호소했다. 그가 자신의 청을 들어준다는 뜻이기만 하다면, 그는 그녀를 채권자라 부르든 다른 어떤 이름으로 부르든 상관없다고 했다. 그녀는 천 파운드짜리 수표를 동봉했고, 이튿날 로저먼드를 만나러 갈 때 그 편지를 가지고 가기로 마음먹었다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXVII.\n\n이튿날 리드게이트는 브래싱에 가야 했으므로, 로저먼드에게 저녁까지 돌아오지 못할 것이라고 말했다. 근래 그녀는 교회에 갈 때와, 한 번 아빠를 뵈러 갈 때를 제외하고는 자기 집과 정원 밖으로 나간 적이 없었다. 그녀는 아빠에게 이렇게 말했다. \"터셔스가 떠나면 우리가 이사하는 걸 도와주실 거죠, 아빠? 우리에겐 돈이 거의 없을 것 같아요. 누군가 우리를 도와주었으면 정말 좋겠어요.\" 그러자 빈시 씨는 이렇게 대답했다. \"그럼, 얘야, 백 파운드나 이백 파운드쯤은 괜찮다. 그 정도는 감당할 수 있어.\" 이런 예외를 빼면 그녀는 집에 앉아 나른한 우울과 불안에 잠긴 채, 윌 래디슬로가 오는 것을 유일한 희망과 관심의 한 점으로 마음에 새기고 있었다. 그리고 이를 리드게이트가 미들마치를 떠나 런던으로 가는 일을 즉시 추진하도록 새롭게 다그치는 일과 결부시켰으니, 마침내 그녀는 그가 오는 것이 떠나는 일의 강력한 원인이 되리라고 확신하게 되었다. 어떻게 그렇게 될지는 전혀 알지 못한 채로. 이렇게 인과를 세우는 방식은 너무나 흔한 일이라 로저먼드만의 특이한 어리석음이라고 보기는 어렵다. 그리고 이런 종류의 인과야말로 그것이 끊어질 때 가장 큰 충격을 안겨 준다. 어떤 결과가 어떻게 생겨날 수 있는지 보는 것은 흔히 빗나갈 가능성과 가로막힘을 보는 일이지만, 바라는 원인과 그에 바짝 잇닿은 바라는 결과 외에는 아무것도 보지 않을 때 우리는 의심에서 벗어나고 우리의 마음은 강렬하게 직관적인 것이 되기 때문이다. 가엾은 로저먼드의 내면에서 진행되던 것이 바로 그런 과정이었다. 그러는 동안에도 그녀는 주위의 온갖 물건을 여전히 똑같이 꼼꼼하게 정돈했으니, 다만 좀 더 굼떴을 뿐이었다. 아니면 피아노 앞에 앉아 연주하려다가 그만두고, 그러면서도 흰 손가락을 나무로 된 앞판에 멈춘 채 피아노 의자에 머뭇거리며 꿈꾸는 듯한 권태 속에 앞을 바라보곤 했다. 그녀의 우울이 너무나 또렷해진 나머지, 리드게이트는 그것을 끊임없는 무언의 책망처럼 느끼며 그 앞에서 묘한 소심함을 느꼈다. 그리고 어쩐지 자신이 그 삶에 멍을 들인 듯한 이 곱고 연약한 존재를 향한 예리한 감수성에 사로잡힌 그 강한 남자는, 그녀의 시선 앞에서 움츠러들고 때로는 그녀가 다가오면 흠칫 놀랐으니, 그녀에 대한 두려움과 그녀를 위한 두려움이 짜증으로 잠시 쫓겨난 뒤에 오히려 더 거세게 밀려들었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 이날 아침 로저먼드는 위층 자기 방에서 — 리드게이트가 나가고 없을 때면 그녀는 가끔 하루 종일 그곳에 앉아 있었다 — 시내로 산책을 나갈 채비를 하고 내려왔다. 그녀에게는 부쳐야 할 편지가 한 통 있었는데, 래디슬로 씨 앞으로 쓴 그 편지는 자못 매력적인 신중함으로 쓰여 있었으나, 곤란한 사정을 넌지시 비쳐 그의 도착을 재촉하려는 의도가 담겨 있었다. 이제 그 집의 유일한 하인이 된 하녀는 그녀가 외출복 차림으로 계단을 내려오는 것을 보고 이렇게 생각했다. \"보닛을 쓴 모습이 저렇게 예쁜 사람은 본 적이 없어, 가엾기도 하지.\"\n\n한편 도러시아의 마음은 로저먼드를 찾아가려는 계획과, 그 방문이라는 생각을 둘러싸고 모여드는 과거와 있음직한 미래에 관한 갖가지 상념으로 가득 차 있었다. 어제 리드게이트가 자기 결혼 생활의 어떤 어려움을 그녀에게 살짝 내비치기 전까지, 그녀에게 리드게이트 부인의 모습은 늘 윌 래디슬로의 모습과 결부되어 있었다. 가장 불안했던 순간에도 — 캐드월러더 부인이 고통스러우리만치 생생하게 전한 소문 이야기에 동요했을 때조차 — 그녀의 노력은, 아니 그녀의 가장 강한 충동적 끌림은 윌을 더럽히는 어떤 억측으로부터도 그를 변호하려는 쪽이었다. 그리고 그 후 그를 만났을 때, 그녀는 처음에 그의 말을 리드게이트 부인을 향한, 그러나 결코 빠져들지 않겠다고 다짐한 어떤 감정에 대한 암시로 해석했고, 그러자 그가 그 고운 존재와 늘 함께할 기회 속에 있을지도 모르는 매력에 대해 재빠르고 슬프고 너그러운 환영을 떠올렸으니, 그녀가 분명 그의 음악적 기쁨을 나누었던 것처럼 그의 다른 취향도 함께 나누었으리라 짐작했던 것이다. 그러나 그 뒤로 그의 작별의 말이 이어졌다 — 그가 자기 사랑이 두려워하는 대상이 바로 그녀 자신이며, 자신이 결코 고백하지 않고 추방 속으로 안고 가기로 결심한 것이 오로지 그녀를 향한 사랑임을 넌지시 내비친 그 몇 마디 열정적인 말이었다. 그 이별 이후로 도러시아는 윌이 자신을 사랑한다는 것을 믿었고, 그의 섬세한 명예심과 누구도 정당하게 그를 비난하지 못하게 하리라는 그의 결심을 자랑스러운 기쁨으로 믿으면서, 그가 리드게이트 부인에게 품었을지도 모르는 마음에 대해서는 자기 가슴이 아주 평온하다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 그 마음이 흠 없는 것이라고 확신했다.\n\n어떤 천성을 지닌 사람들이 우리를 사랑하면, 우리는 일종의 세례와 봉헌을 받은 것 같은 의식을 갖게 된다. 그들은 우리에 대한 순수한 믿음으로 우리를 올곧음과 순결함에 묶어 두고, 그리하여 우리의 죄는 보이지 않는 신뢰의 제단을 허무는 가장 나쁜 종류의 신성 모독이 된다. \"당신이 선하지 않다면 누구도 선하지 않다\" — 이 짧은 말은 책임에 무시무시한 의미를 부여할 수 있고, 회한에 황산 같은 강렬함을 담을 수 있다.\n\n도러시아의 천성이 바로 그런 종류였다. 그녀 자신의 열정적인 결점들은 그녀의 열렬한 성격에서 쉽게 헤아릴 수 있는 트인 물길을 따라 놓여 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 남들의 눈에 보이는 실수에 대해서는 연민으로 가득했으나, 아직 그녀의 경험 안에는 숨겨진 잘못을 미묘하게 구성하고 의심할 만한 재료가 없었다. 그러나 그녀의 그 단순함은, 남들에 대한 자신의 믿음 어린 관념 속에서 그들에게 하나의 이상을 들어 보였으니, 그것이야말로 그녀의 여성다움의 위대한 힘 가운데 하나였다. 그리고 그것은 처음부터 윌 래디슬로에게 강하게 작용했다. 그녀와 헤어질 때 그는 느꼈다. 그녀 자신에 대한 자기 감정과 그녀의 재산이 둘 사이에 만들어 놓은 간극을 그녀에게 전하려 했던 그 짧은 말들이, 도러시아가 그것을 해석해야 할 때 오로지 그 짧음 덕분에 빛을 발하리라는 것을. 그는 그녀의 마음속에서 자신에 대한 가장 높은 평가를 발견했다고 느꼈다.\n\n그리고 그 점에서 그는 옳았다. 헤어진 이후 여러 달 동안 도러시아는 서로의 관계 속에서 슬프지만 감미로운 안식을 느껴 왔으니, 그것은 내면적으로 온전하고 흠 없는 관계였다. 그녀는 자기 안에 적극적인 대항의 힘을 지니고 있었는데, 그 대항이 자기가 믿는 계획이나 사람을 옹호하는 쪽으로 향할 때면 더욱 그러했다. 윌이 자기 남편에게서 받았다고 느낀 부당한 처사와, 다른 사람들에게는 그를 깔보는 근거가 되는 외적인 조건들은, 오히려 그녀의 애정과 찬탄 어린 판단을 더욱 끈질기게 만들 뿐이었다. 그리고 이제 불스트로드에 관한 폭로와 함께 윌의 사회적 지위에 영향을 미치는 또 하나의 사실이 드러나, 공원 울타리 안쪽에 자리한 그녀 세계의 그 부분에서 그에 대해 오가는 말에 맞서는 도러시아의 내적 저항을 새삼 일깨웠다.\n\n\"도둑질하는 유대인 전당포 주인의 손자인 젊은 래디슬로\"라는 말은 로윅과 티프턴과 프레시트에서 불스트로드 사건을 두고 오간 대화 속에 힘주어 끼어들었으니, 그것은 \"흰 생쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람\"보다 더 고약하게 가엾은 윌의 등에 붙은 표찰이었다. 올곧은 제임스 체텀 경은, 이로써 래디슬로와 도러시아 사이의 그 산처럼 까마득한 거리에 또 하나의 막대한 거리가 더해졌으며, 그 덕분에 그쪽으로의 어떤 근심도 너무 터무니없는 것으로 떨쳐 버릴 수 있게 되었다고 다소 흐뭇하게 생각하면서, 자기 만족이 정의로운 것이라고 확신했다. 그리고 어쩌면 래디슬로 가계의 이 추한 한 토막을 브룩 씨의 주의에 일깨워 주는 데에, 그가 자기 자신의 어리석음을 비추어 볼 새로운 촛불로 삼게 하는 데에 얼마간의 즐거움이 있었을지도 모른다. 도러시아는 윌이 그 고통스러운 이야기에서 맡은 역할을 한 번 이상 떠올릴 때 깃들던 적의를 알아차렸다. 그러나 그녀는 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않았으니, 예전에 윌에 관해 이야기할 때와는 달리, 이제는 두 사람 사이에 언제까지나 봉헌된 비밀로 남아야 할 더 깊은 관계가 있다는 의식이 그녀를 가로막았던 것이다. 그러나 그 침묵은 그녀의 저항하는 감정을 더욱 철저한 열기로 감싸 들였다. 그리고 남들이 치욕으로 그의 등에 내던지고 싶어 하는 듯한 윌의 운명 속 이 불행은, 그녀가 매달리는 생각에 오히려 더한 열정을 더해 줄 뿐이었다.\n\n그녀는 두 사람이 언젠가 더 가까운 결합에 이르리라는 어떤 환상도 품지 않았으나, 그렇다고 단념의 자세를 취하지도 않았다. 그녀는 윌과의 모든 관계를 아주 담담하게 자기 결혼 생활의 슬픔의 일부로 받아들였고, 완전히 행복하지 않다는 이유로 마음속 비탄을 이어 간다면 그것이야말로 매우 죄스러운 일이라고 여겼을 것이니, 오히려 자기 운명의 넘치는 것들에 마음을 두려는 편이었다. 그녀는 자기 애정의 주된 기쁨이 추억 속에 놓여 있어도 견딜 수 있었으며, 결혼이라는 생각은 오로지 지금으로서는 전혀 알지 못하지만 친구들 눈에 비친 그 장점이 그녀에게 고통의 원천이 될 어떤 구혼자가 내미는 역겨운 제안으로만 다가왔다. \"네 재산을 대신 관리해 줄 사람 말이다, 얘야\"라는 것이 어울리는 자질에 대한 브룩 씨의 매력적인 제안이었다. \"어떻게 쓸지만 안다면 제가 직접 관리하고 싶어요\"라고 도러시아는 말했다. 아니, 그녀는 다시는 결혼하지 않겠다는 자신의 선언을 고수했으며, 이정표 하나 없이 그토록 평평하고 텅 비어 보이는 자기 인생의 긴 골짜기에서, 길을 걸어가며 길동무들을 만나는 동안 인도가 찾아오리라 여겼다.\n\n윌 래디슬로에 대한 이런 습관적인 감정 상태는, 리드게이트 부인을 방문하겠다고 마음먹은 이래로 깨어 있는 모든 시간 동안 그녀 안에서 강하게 자리 잡아, 일종의 배경을 이루었으며, 그 배경을 바탕으로 그녀는 자신의 관심과 연민을 가로막는 것 없이 로저먼드의 모습을 마주 보았다. 자신의 행복을 하나의 법으로 삼아 온 남편과 이 아내 사이에는 분명 어떤 정신적인 단절이, 온전한 신뢰를 가로막는 어떤 장벽이 생겨난 것 같았다. 그것은 제삼자가 직접 건드려서는 안 되는 어려움이었다. 그러나 도러시아는 남편에게 던져진 의혹으로 인해 로저먼드에게 닥쳤을 외로움을 깊은 연민으로 생각했으니, 리드게이트에 대한 존경과 그녀에 대한 공감을 드러내는 데에는 분명 도움이 되는 무언가가 있으리라 여겼다.\n\n\"그 부인에게 남편에 관해 이야기해야겠어.\" 도러시아는 시내로 마차를 타고 가면서 그렇게 생각했다. 맑은 봄날 아침과, 촉촉한 흙냄새와, 반쯤 벌어진 깍지에서 갓 돋아 주름진 풍성한 초록빛을 내보이는 싱그러운 잎들은, 리드게이트의 처신을 변호하는 설명을 기쁘게 받아들인 페어브러더 씨와 오래 나눈 대화에서 그녀가 느끼던 명랑함의 일부인 듯했다. \"리드게이트 부인에게 좋은 소식을 가져가야지. 그러면 그분도 나와 이야기 나누고 나를 친구로 삼고 싶어 할지도 몰라.\"\n\n도러시아에게는 로윅 게이트에서 볼일이 또 하나 있었다. 학교 건물에 놓을 음색 좋은 새 종에 관한 일이었는데, 마차에서 내려야 할 곳이 리드게이트의 집 바로 근처였으므로, 그녀는 마부에게 짐 꾸러미 몇 개를 기다리라고 일러 두고 길을 건너 그쪽으로 걸어갔다. 길로 난 문은 열려 있었고, 하녀는 마침 시야 안에 멈춰 선 마차를 내다보던 참이었는데, 그 \"마차의 주인\"인 부인이 자기 쪽으로 다가오고 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"리드게이트 부인 댁에 계신가요?\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"잘 모르겠습니다, 마님. 들어와 계시면 제가 보고 오겠습니다.\" 마사는 부엌 앞치마 차림이 신경 쓰여 다소 당황했으나, 한 쌍의 말이 끄는 마차를 거느린 이 여왕 같은 젊은 미망인에게 \"사모님\"이라는 호칭은 어울리지 않는다는 것쯤은 알아챌 만큼 정신을 차리고 있었다. \"들어와 계시면 제가 가서 보고 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"캐소본 부인이 왔다고 전해 주세요.\" 마사가 그녀를 응접실로 안내한 다음 위층으로 올라가 로저먼드가 산책에서 돌아왔는지 보려고 앞으로 나아갈 때 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n그들은 현관 홀의 넓은 부분을 가로질러, 정원으로 통하는 복도 쪽으로 꺾어 들어갔다. 응접실 문은 빗장이 걸려 있지 않았고, 마사는 방 안을 들여다보지 않은 채 문을 밀고 나서 캐소본 부인이 들어가기를 기다렸다가 돌아섰는데, 문은 소리 없이 활짝 열렸다가 다시 닫혔다.\n\n이날 아침 도러시아는 지난날의 일들과 앞으로 닥칠 일들의 영상으로 가득 차 있어 평소보다 바깥을 보는 눈이 무뎠다. 그녀는 별다른 것을 보지 못한 채 문 안쪽에 들어서 있었으나, 곧 낮은 어조로 말하는 어떤 목소리가 들려와, 마치 대낮에 꿈을 꾸는 듯한 느낌으로 그녀를 흠칫 놀라게 했다. 그리고 무심코 책장의 튀어나온 판 너머로 한두 걸음 나아간 그녀는, 모든 윤곽을 또렷이 메워 버리는 어떤 확신의 무서운 조명 속에서, 그 자리에 멈춰 서게 만드는 무언가를, 입을 열 만한 침착함조차 없게 만드는 무언가를 보았다.\n\n그녀가 들어온 문과 일직선으로 벽에 붙어 놓인 소파에 등을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리고 앉은 윌 래디슬로가 보였다. 그 바로 곁에는 로저먼드가 그를 향해 돌아앉아 있었는데, 발갛게 상기된 눈물기가 그녀의 얼굴에 새로운 광채를 더했고, 보닛은 뒤로 젖혀져 있었으며, 한편 윌은 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 기울인 채 그녀가 치켜든 두 손을 자기 두 손으로 꼭 감싸 쥐고 낮은 어조의 열정으로 말하고 있었다.\n\n흥분에 사로잡혀 있던 로저먼드는 소리 없이 다가오는 모습을 알아채지 못했다. 그러나 도러시아가 이 광경의 헤아릴 수 없는 첫 순간이 지난 뒤 어쩔 줄 몰라 뒷걸음치다가 무슨 가구에 가로막히자, 로저먼드는 문득 그녀가 있음을 알아차리고는 발작하듯 두 손을 홱 빼더니 일어서서, 어쩔 수 없이 멈춰 선 도러시아를 바라보았다. 윌 래디슬로도 벌떡 일어나 주위를 둘러보았고, 새로운 번갯불이 어린 도러시아의 두 눈과 마주치자 대리석으로 변해 가는 것 같았다. 그러나 그녀는 곧장 그에게서 눈을 돌려 로저먼드를 향하고는 단호한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"실례했습니다, 리드게이트 부인. 하녀가 부인이 여기 계신 줄 몰랐던 모양이에요. 리드게이트 씨에게 전할 중요한 편지가 있어 들렀는데, 부인 손에 직접 건네고 싶었습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 자신의 뒷걸음을 막았던 작은 탁자 위에 편지를 내려놓고는, 로저먼드와 윌을 한데 묶어 멀찍이 한 번 눈길을 주고 가볍게 인사한 뒤 빠르게 방을 나섰다. 복도에서 마주친 놀란 마사가 사모님이 안 계셔서 죄송하다고 말한 다음, 신분 높은 사람들은 아마 다른 이들보다 더 참을성이 없으리라고 속으로 생각하며 그 낯선 부인을 배웅했다.\n\n도러시아는 더없이 탄력 있는 걸음으로 길을 건너 이내 다시 마차에 올랐다.\n\n\"프레시트 홀로 가요.\" 그녀가 마부에게 말했다. 그녀를 바라보는 사람이라면 누구나, 그녀의 얼굴이 평소보다 창백하긴 했어도 이토록 침착한 활력에 차 있었던 적은 없었으리라고 생각했을 것이다. 그리고 그것은 정말로 그녀가 느끼던 바였다. 마치 다른 감정에 대한 감수성을 넘어서까지 그녀를 자극하는 경멸의 술을 한 모금 크게 들이켠 것 같았다. 그녀는 자신의 믿음에 한참이나 못 미치는 무언가를 보았으니, 그녀의 감정은 거기에서 도로 밀려 나와 아무런 대상 없이 흥분한 무리를 이루었다. 그녀에게는 그 흥분을 쏟아부을 어떤 활동적인 것이 필요했다. 그녀는 음식이나 물 없이도 하루 종일 걷고 일할 힘을 느꼈다. 그래서 아침에 길을 나설 때 품었던 목적, 곧 프레시트와 티프턴에 가서 제임스 경과 삼촌에게 리드게이트에 관해 자기가 알리고 싶은 모든 것을 전하겠다는 그 목적을 실행하기로 했다. 시련 속에서 리드게이트가 겪는 결혼 생활의 외로움이 이제 그녀에게 새로운 의미로 다가왔고, 그래서 그녀는 더욱 열렬히 그의 옹호자가 될 채비가 되어 있었다. 그녀는 자기 결혼 생활의 분투 속에서 — 거기에는 늘 재빨리 가라앉는 아픔이 있었으나 — 이처럼 의기양양한 분노의 힘 같은 것을 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 그것을 새로운 힘의 표시로 받아들였다.\n\n\"도도, 눈이 어쩜 그렇게 반짝이니!\" 제임스 경이 방에서 나가자 실리아가 말했다. \"그리고 언니는 보고 있는 걸 하나도 보지 못해, 아서도 아무것도 말이야. 언니 분명 뭔가 마음 불편한 일을 벌이려는 거지. 다 리드게이트 씨 일 때문이야, 아니면 또 무슨 일이 있었던 거야?\" 실리아는 언니를 기대에 차서 지켜보는 데 익숙했다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 아주 많은 일이 있었어.\" 도도가 충만한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일인지 궁금하네.\" 실리아가 팔짱을 아늑하게 끼고 그 위에 몸을 기울이며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 이 땅 위 모든 사람의 온갖 괴로움이지.\" 도러시아가 두 팔을 들어 뒤통수에 갖다 대며 말했다.\n\n\"어머나, 도도, 언니 그걸 위해 무슨 계획이라도 세우려는 거야?\" 실리아가 이 햄릿 같은 헛소리에 다소 불안해하며 말했다.\n\n그러나 제임스 경이 도러시아를 그레인지까지 데려다줄 채비를 하고 다시 들어왔고, 그녀는 자기 집 문 앞에 내릴 때까지 결심을 굽히지 않은 채 그 여정을 무사히 마쳤다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXVIII.\n\n어제였으면, 그래서 내가 무덤 속에 있다면, 그녀의 다정한 신의가 비석처럼 그 위에 놓인다면.\n\n로저먼드와 윌은 꼼짝도 하지 않고 서 있었다. 얼마나 오래 그렇게 있었는지 그들은 알지 못했다. 그는 도러시아가 서 있던 자리를 바라보았고, 그녀는 의혹에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 로저먼드에게는 그것이 끝없는 시간처럼 느껴졌으나, 그녀의 가장 깊은 영혼 속에는 방금 일어난 일에 대한 짜증보다 만족이 더 컸다. 천박한 본성은 다른 사람의 감정을 손쉽게 휘두를 수 있으리라 꿈꾸며, 가장 깊은 물줄기조차 돌릴 수 있다는 제 보잘것없는 마술을 무턱대고 믿고, 예쁜 몸짓과 말 몇 마디로 있지 않은 것을 마치 있는 것처럼 만들 수 있다고 자신한다. 그녀는 윌이 심한 타격을 받았다는 것을 알았지만, 자기 자신의 소망에 맞추어 빚어낼 재료가 아닌 한 다른 사람의 마음 상태를 상상하는 데 익숙하지 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 자신에게 누군가를 달래거나 굴복시킬 힘이 있다고 믿었다. 그 누구보다 비뚤어진 남자인 터티어스조차 결국에는 늘 누그러지지 않았던가. 사건들은 완강했지만, 그래도 로저먼드는 결혼 전에 그랬듯이 지금도 마음먹은 일은 결코 포기하지 않는다고 말했을 것이다.\n\n그녀는 팔을 내밀어 손가락 끝을 윌의 윗옷 소매에 얹었다.\n\n\"나를 건드리지 마시오!\" 그가 채찍을 후려치듯 내뱉으며 그녀에게서 홱 물러섰다. 온몸이 독침의 고통으로 얼얼한 듯, 그의 얼굴은 붉어졌다가 하얘졌다가 다시 붉어졌다. 그는 방의 반대편으로 돌아서서 그녀와 마주 보고 섰는데, 손가락 끝을 호주머니에 꽂고 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 로저먼드가 아니라 그녀에게서 몇 인치 떨어진 한 점을 사납게 노려보고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 몹시 모욕감을 느꼈지만, 그녀가 그것을 드러내는 신호는 오직 리드게이트만이 해석하는 데 익숙한 것이었다. 그녀는 갑자기 조용해지더니 자리에 앉아, 늘어진 보닛의 끈을 풀어 숄과 함께 내려놓았다. 앞으로 모아 쥔 그녀의 작은 손은 무척 차가웠다.\n\n처음부터 윌이 모자를 집어 들고 떠나 버렸다면 더 안전했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그렇게 할 충동을 조금도 느끼지 못했다. 오히려 그는 머물러서 분노로 로저먼드를 박살 내고 싶은 끔찍한 욕구를 느꼈다. 표범이 창에 찔린 상처를 입고도 뛰어올라 물어뜯지 않고는 견딜 수 없듯이, 그녀가 자신에게 끌어내린 그 운명을 분노를 쏟아내지 않고는 견딜 수 없을 것만 같았다. 하지만, 한 여자에게 당신을 저주하고 싶다고 어떻게 말할 수 있겠는가? 그는 인정하지 않을 수 없는 억제의 법칙 아래서 부글부글 끓고 있었다. 그는 위태롭게 균형을 잡고 있었는데, 이제 로저먼드의 목소리가 결정적인 진동을 가져왔다. 그녀는 피리 같은 비아냥의 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"카소봉 부인을 쫓아가서 당신이 그분을 더 좋아한다고 설명하면 되겠네요.\"\n\n\"쫓아가라고!\" 그가 목소리에 날카로운 칼날을 세우며 터뜨렸다. \"그녀가 돌아서서 나를 봐 주기라도 할 것 같소? 내가 그녀에게 한 어떤 말이라도 더러운 깃털 한 조각 이상으로 여겨 줄 것 같소? 설명이라니! 한 남자가 어찌 한 여자를 희생시키면서 설명할 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로 말씀하시면 되죠.\" 로저먼드가 좀 더 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 희생시킨다고 그녀가 나를 더 좋아하리라 생각하오? 그녀는 내가 비열해졌다고 해서 우쭐해할 여자가 아니오. 내가 당신에게 비겁한 자였으니 그녀에게는 진실하리라고 믿을 여자가 아니란 말이오.\"\n\n그는 먹이를 보고도 잡을 수 없는 야생 동물처럼 안절부절못하며 서성이기 시작했다. 이윽고 그가 다시 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"전에도 나는 희망이 없었소. 더 나은 무언가가 오리라는 희망이 거의 없었소. 하지만 한 가지 확신은 있었지. 그녀가 나를 믿어 준다는 것 말이오. 사람들이 나에 대해 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 하든, 그녀는 나를 믿어 주었소. 그게 사라졌소! 이제 그녀는 나를 보잘것없는 위선자 이상으로는 결코 여기지 않을 거요. 아첨하는 조건이 아니고서는 천국조차 받아들이지 않을 만큼 까다로운 척하면서, 정작 슬그머니 어떤 악마의 잔돈에라도 자신을 팔아넘기는 자라고 말이오. 그녀는 나를, 우리가 처음 만난 순간부터, 자기에게 가한 살아 있는 모욕이라고 생각할 거요.\"\n\n윌은 던져서는 안 될 무언가, 던졌다가는 산산조각 날 무언가를 쥐고 있음을 깨달은 듯 말을 멈추었다. 그는 로저먼드의 말을 다시 낚아채는 데서 분노의 또 다른 배출구를 찾았다. 마치 그것이 목 졸라 내던져야 할 파충류라도 되는 듯이.\n\n\"설명이라니! 한 남자에게 자기가 어떻게 지옥에 떨어졌는지 설명하라니! 내가 그녀를 더 좋아한다는 걸 설명하라고! 나는 그녀를 좋아한 적이 없소. 숨 쉬는 것을 좋아한다고 할 수 없듯이 말이오. 그녀 곁에는 다른 어떤 여자도 존재하지 않소. 나는 다른 여자의 산 손을 만지느니 차라리 그녀의 죽은 손을 만지겠소.\"\n\n이 독 묻은 무기들이 자신에게 날아드는 동안, 로저먼드는 거의 자기 정체성의 감각을 잃어 가고 있었으며, 어떤 새롭고 끔찍한 존재로 깨어나는 것만 같았다. 리드게이트의 가장 격렬한 노여움 아래서 알았던 차갑고 단호한 반발이나 말없는 자기 정당화의 감각은 전혀 없었다. 그녀의 모든 감수성은 어리둥절한 새로운 고통으로 변해 버렸으니, 그녀는 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 채찍질 아래서 새삼 겁에 질려 움츠러드는 것을 느꼈다. 자신의 본성에 맞서는 또 다른 본성이 느끼는 바가 그녀의 의식 속으로 불태워지고 물어뜯기듯 새겨지고 있었다. 윌이 말을 멈추었을 때 그녀는 병든 비참함의 형상이 되어 있었다. 입술은 창백했고, 눈에는 눈물 없는 절망이 어려 있었다. 만약 그녀와 마주 선 사람이 터티어스였다면, 그 비참한 표정은 그에게 고통이 되었을 테고, 그는 그녀 곁에 주저앉아, 그녀가 흔히 하찮게 여기던 그 억센 팔의 위로로 그녀를 다독였을 것이다.\n\n윌에게 그런 연민의 움직임이 없었던 것은 용서받아도 좋으리라. 그는 자기 삶의 이상적인 보물을 망쳐 버린 이 여자에게 미리 어떤 유대도 느낀 적이 없었으며, 자신에게는 잘못이 없다고 여겼다. 그는 자신이 잔인하다는 것을 알았지만, 아직 마음이 누그러들지 않았다.\n\n말을 마친 뒤에도 그는 반쯤 정신이 나간 채 계속 서성거렸고, 로저먼드는 완전히 가만히 앉아 있었다. 마침내 윌은 정신을 차린 듯 모자를 집어 들었지만, 그래도 한동안 결단을 내리지 못한 채 서 있었다. 그는 그녀에게 흔한 예의의 말 한마디조차 꺼내기 어려운 방식으로 말을 해 버렸다. 그런데 막상 더 이상 아무 말 없이 그녀를 떠나는 순간에 이르자, 그는 그것이 잔인한 짓인 듯 머뭇거렸다. 그는 자신의 분노가 가로막히고 무력해진 것을 느꼈다. 그는 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 걸어가 팔을 그 위에 기댄 채, 무엇을 기다리는지도 잘 모르면서 잠자코 기다렸다. 복수심의 불길은 여전히 그의 안에서 타오르고 있어서 그는 철회의 말 한마디도 꺼낼 수 없었다. 그렇지만 그의 마음속에는 이런 생각이 자리 잡고 있었다. 다정한 우정을 누렸던 이 화롯가로 돌아와 보니 그곳에 재앙이 앉아 있었고, 집 안에서뿐만 아니라 집 밖에도 놓여 있는 고난이 그에게 불현듯 드러났다는 것이다. 그리고 어떤 예감 같은 것이 느린 집게처럼 그를 짓눌렀다. 마음의 음울한 슬픔 속에서 그에게 자신을 내던진 이 무력한 여자에게 그의 삶이 예속되어 버릴지도 모른다는 예감이었다. 그러나 그는 자신의 예민한 직감이 미리 보여 주는 그 사실에 음울하게 반항하고 있었으니, 그의 눈길이 로저먼드의 시들어 버린 얼굴에 떨어졌을 때 그에게는 자신이 둘 중 더 가련한 쪽인 것처럼 여겨졌다. 고통이 연민으로 바뀌려면 먼저 기억의 영광된 삶 속으로 들어가야 하는 법이니까.\n\n그리하여 그들은 여러 분 동안 서로 마주 본 채, 멀리 떨어져, 침묵 속에 남아 있었다. 윌의 얼굴은 여전히 말 없는 분노에 사로잡혀 있었고, 로저먼드의 얼굴은 말 없는 비참함에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그 가엾은 여인에게는 어떤 격정도 되받아 쏟아낼 힘이 없었다. 그녀의 모든 희망이 향해 팽팽히 당겨져 있던 환상의 끔찍한 붕괴는 그녀를 너무도 철저하게 뒤흔든 일격이었다. 그녀의 작은 세계는 폐허가 되었고, 그녀는 그 한가운데서 외롭고 어리둥절한 의식으로 비틀거리고 있는 자신을 느꼈다.\n\n윌은 그녀가 무슨 말이라도 해서, 되살아난 친교의 어떤 시도도 비웃듯 두 사람을 뚫어지게 노려보고 서 있는 것만 같은 자신의 잔인한 말에 누그러뜨릴 그늘을 드리워 주기를 바랐다. 그러나 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 마침내 그는 필사적으로 자신을 다잡으며 물었다. \"오늘 저녁에 들러서 리드게이트를 봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로요.\" 로저먼드가 겨우 들릴 만큼 대답했다.\n\n그러고 나서 윌은 집을 나섰는데, 마사는 그가 안에 들어와 있었던 줄도 알지 못했다.\n\n그가 가 버린 뒤, 로저먼드는 자리에서 일어서려 했으나 정신을 잃고 도로 주저앉았다. 다시 정신이 들었을 때는 너무 아파서 일어나 종을 울릴 기력도 없었고, 그녀가 오래 보이지 않는 것을 이상히 여긴 하녀가 처음으로 아래층 방들을 모두 뒤져 그녀를 찾을 생각을 할 때까지 그녀는 속수무책으로 그대로 있었다. 로저먼드는 갑자기 속이 메스껍고 어지러워졌다며 위층으로 부축해 달라고 했다. 위층에 이르자 그녀는 옷을 입은 채 침대에 몸을 던지고는, 언젠가 잊지 못할 슬픔의 날에 한 번 그랬던 것처럼 무기력에 빠진 듯 누워 있었다.\n\n리드게이트는 예상보다 일찍, 다섯 시 반쯤 집에 돌아와 그녀가 그렇게 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀가 아프다는 것을 알아차리자 다른 모든 생각이 뒤로 물러났다. 그가 그녀의 맥을 짚었을 때, 그녀의 눈은 오랫동안 보이지 않던 끈질김으로 그에게 머물렀는데, 마치 그가 거기 있다는 사실에 어떤 만족을 느끼는 것 같았다. 그는 그 차이를 단번에 알아차렸고, 그녀 곁에 앉아 팔을 그녀의 몸 아래에 살며시 넣고는 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했다. \"가엾은 내 로저먼드! 무슨 일로 마음이 흔들렸소?\" 그에게 매달린 채 그녀는 발작적인 흐느낌과 울음에 빠져들었고, 이후 한 시간 동안 그는 그녀를 달래고 보살피는 일밖에 하지 않았다. 그는 도러시아가 그녀를 보러 왔었으며, 분명 자신을 향한 어떤 새로운 마음의 변화를 담고 있는 그녀의 신경계에 미친 이 모든 영향이 그 방문이 불러일으킨 새로운 인상의 흥분에서 비롯된 것이라고 짐작했다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXIX.\n\n이제 나는 꿈속에서 보았다. 그들이 이야기를 막 끝냈을 무렵, 들판 한가운데 있는 몹시 질척한 늪에 가까이 이르렀으니, 그들은 부주의하여 둘 다 갑자기 그 수렁에 빠지고 말았다. 그 늪의 이름은 절망이었다.—번연.\n\n로저먼드가 안정을 되찾고, 진통제의 효력으로 곧 잠들 수 있기를 바라며 리드게이트가 그녀의 곁을 떠난 뒤, 그는 거실에 두고 온 책을 가지러 갔다. 저녁 시간을 작업실에서 보낼 작정이었던 것이다. 그러다 그는 탁자 위에서 자기 앞으로 온 도러시아의 편지를 보았다. 그는 캐소번 부인이 다녀갔는지 로저먼드에게 감히 묻지 못했지만, 이 편지를 읽고서 그 사실을 확인할 수 있었다. 도러시아가 자기 손으로 직접 이 편지를 전하겠노라고 적어 두었기 때문이다.\n\n조금 뒤에 윌 래디슬로가 들어왔을 때, 리드게이트는 놀란 기색으로 그를 맞이했는데, 그 모습으로 보아 그가 앞서 있었던 방문에 대해 듣지 못했음이 분명했다. 그래서 윌은 \"리드게이트 부인이 오늘 아침 제가 왔다고 말씀드리지 않던가요?\"라고 말할 수 없었다.\n\n\"가엾게도 로저먼드가 아프다네.\" 리드게이트가 인사를 건네자마자 덧붙였다.\n\n\"심각한 것은 아니겠지요.\" 윌이 말했다.\n\n\"아닐세—그저 가벼운 신경성 충격일 뿐이야—어떤 동요의 여파지. 요즘 너무 무리를 했거든. 사실대로 말하면, 래디슬로, 나는 운 없는 가련한 놈이라네. 자네가 떠난 뒤로 우리는 연옥을 몇 바퀴나 돌았고, 나는 요즘 그 어느 때보다 더 험한 벼랑에 올라서고 말았네. 자네는 이제 막 내려온 모양이군—꽤 지쳐 보이는걸—이 마을에 온 지 얼마 안 되어 아무 소식도 듣지 못했겠지?\"\n\n\"밤새 여행해서 오늘 아침 여덟 시에 화이트 하트에 닿았습니다. 그 뒤로 들어앉아 쉬고 있었지요.\" 윌이 말했다. 자신이 비겁자처럼 느껴졌지만, 이렇게 둘러대는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다.\n\n이어서 그는 로저먼드가 이미 그녀 나름의 방식으로 그에게 들려준 그 곤경에 대한 리드게이트의 설명을 들었다. 그녀는 윌의 이름이 세간의 이야기와 얽혀 있다는 사실은 언급하지 않았으니—이 세부 사항은 그녀에게 당장 영향을 미치는 것이 아니었기에—그는 이제야 처음으로 그것을 듣게 되었다.\n\n\"자네 이름이 이번에 폭로된 일들과 뒤섞여 있다는 걸 말해 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했네.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 그 폭로가 래디슬로에게 어떤 아픔을 줄 수 있을지를 대다수 사람들보다 더 잘 헤아릴 수 있었다. \"이 마을로 나가는 순간 틀림없이 듣게 될 테니까. 래플스가 자네에게 말을 걸었다는 게 사실인 모양이지.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 윌이 빈정거리듯 말했다. \"소문이 저를 이번 사건 전체에서 가장 평판 나쁜 인물로 만들지 않는다면 다행이겠지요. 아마 최신판 이야기는 제가 래플스와 공모해 불스트로드를 살해하려 했고, 그 목적으로 미들마치에서 달아났다는 식이겠지요.\"\n\n그는 이렇게 생각하고 있었다. \"그녀가 듣는 자리에서 내 이름을 추천해 줄, 또 하나의 새로운 울림이 생긴 셈이군. 하지만—이제 와서 그게 무슨 의미가 있겠는가?\"\n\n그러나 그는 불스트로드가 자신에게 제안했던 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 윌은 자기 개인적인 일에 대해서는 매우 거리낌 없고 무심한 편이었지만, 그를 빚어낸 자연의 섬세한 손길 가운데서도 더없이 절묘한 한 가지는 그가 여기서 침묵을 지키도록 일러 주는 섬세한 너그러움을 지녔다는 점이었다. 그 돈을 받아들인 것이 리드게이트의 불운이었음을 알게 된 바로 그 순간에, 자신은 불스트로드의 돈을 거절했노라고 말하기가 그는 꺼려졌다.\n\n리드게이트 역시 속을 털어놓는 와중에도 말을 아꼈다. 그는 이 곤경 앞에서 로저먼드가 느끼는 심정에 대해서는 전혀 내비치지 않았고, 도러시아에 대해서도 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"나를 향한 그 어떤 의혹도 믿지 않는다고 나서서 말해 준 단 한 사람이 바로 캐소번 부인이라네.\" 윌의 표정에 변화가 이는 것을 알아챈 그는, 두 사람의 관계를 너무도 모르는 까닭에 자기 말이 거기에 어떤 숨겨진 아픔을 건드릴까 봐 두려워, 더는 그녀를 입에 올리지 않았다. 그리고 도러시아야말로 윌이 지금 미들마치를 찾아온 진짜 이유가 아닐까 하는 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다.\n\n두 사람은 서로를 가엾이 여기고 있었지만, 상대의 곤경이 얼마나 깊은지를 짐작한 것은 윌뿐이었다. 리드게이트가 절망적인 체념에 잠겨 런던에 가서 정착하겠노라 말하며 희미하게 미소 띤 채 \"우리는 또 자넬 보게 되겠지, 이 친구야\"라고 했을 때, 윌은 이루 말할 수 없는 슬픔을 느끼며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그날 아침 로저먼드는 그에게 리드게이트에게 이 결단을 재촉해 달라고 간청했었다. 그리고 그는 마치 어떤 마법의 파노라마 속에서, 자기 자신이 상황의 사소한 유혹들에 즐거움 없이 굴복해 미끄러져 들어가는 미래를 바라보는 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그것은 어떤 단 한 번의 중대한 거래보다도 더 흔한 파멸의 역사인 것이다.\n\n우리가 우리의 미래 자아를 수동적으로 바라보기 시작할 때, 그리고 우리 자신의 모습이 무미건조한 잘못과 초라한 성취로 무딘 동의 속에 끌려가는 것을 지켜볼 때, 우리는 위태로운 벼랑 끝에 서 있는 셈이다. 가엾은 리드게이트는 그 벼랑 끝에서 속으로 신음하고 있었고, 윌은 이제 막 그곳에 다다르고 있었다. 그날 저녁 그에게는, 로저먼드에게 모질게 터뜨린 그 격정이 자신에게 하나의 의무를 지운 듯 여겨졌고, 그는 그 의무가 두려웠다. 아무것도 의심하지 않는 리드게이트의 선의가 두려웠고, 망쳐 버린 자신의 삶에 대한 자신의 혐오가 두려웠으니, 그 혐오는 그를 아무런 동기도 없는 경박함 속에 내버려 둘 것이었다.\n* 번연: 존 번연(1628~1688)의 『천로역정』에서 인용한 구절. '절망의 늪'은 작품에 등장하는 상징적 장소다.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXX.\n\n그대의 얼굴에 어린 미소만큼 아름답지 않도다.\n꽃들은 제 화단에서 그대 앞에 웃음 짓고,\n\n향기는 그대의 발걸음을 따라 퍼지노라.\n\n그대는 별들을 그릇됨으로부터 지켜 주고,\n\n가장 오래된 하늘도 그대를 통해 새롭고 굳건하도다.\n— 워즈워스, 「의무에 부치는 송시」\n\n도러시아는 아침에 페어브러더 씨를 만났을 때, 프레시트에서 돌아오는 길에 목사관에 들러 저녁을 함께하겠다고 약속했었다. 그녀와 페어브러더 가족 사이에는 잦은 왕래가 있었고, 그 덕분에 그녀는 저택에서 전혀 외롭지 않다고 말할 수 있었으며, 당분간은 말동무가 될 부인을 들이라는 엄격한 처방을 물리칠 수 있었다. 집에 도착해 약속을 떠올렸을 때, 그녀는 그 약속이 반가웠다. 그리고 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입기까지 아직 한 시간이 남았음을 알자, 곧장 학교 건물로 걸어가 교장 부부와 새 종에 관해 이야기를 나누었으며, 그들의 자질구레한 세부 사항과 거듭되는 말에 열심히 귀를 기울이면서, 자기 삶이 매우 바쁘다는 극적인 느낌을 자아냈다. 그녀는 돌아오는 길에 발걸음을 멈추고, 텃밭에 씨앗을 심고 있던 늙은 버니 영감과 이야기를 나누었으며, 그 시골의 현자와 함께 한 평의 땅에서 가장 많은 수확을 올릴 수 있는 작물에 관해, 그리고 육십 년 경험으로 얻은 토양에 관한 결론—즉 흙이 어지간히 부드러우면 괜찮지만, 비가 오고 또 오고 또 와서 온통 미라처럼 굳어 버리면, 그땐 이제—에 관해 슬기롭게 담론을 펼쳤다.\n\n사교적인 기질에 이끌려 다소 늦어졌음을 깨닫고, 그녀는 서둘러 옷을 갈아입고 필요 이상으로 일찍 목사관으로 건너갔다. 그 집은 결코 따분하지 않았으니, 페어브러더 씨는 또 한 사람의 셀번의 화이트처럼, 말 못 하는 손님들이자 자기가 돌보는 피보호자들—소년들에게 괴롭히지 말라고 가르치던 그 생물들—에 관해 늘 들려줄 새로운 이야깃거리를 가지고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 마침 마을 전체의 귀염둥이가 되어 신성한 동물처럼 마음대로 돌아다니게끔 아름다운 염소 한 쌍을 새로 길들이던 참이었다. 저녁 시간은 차를 마신 뒤까지 유쾌하게 흘러갔고, 도러시아는 평소보다 말을 많이 하며 더듬이로 간략하게 대화를 나누는, 어쩌면 우리가 모르는 사이에 개혁된 의회를 열고 있을지도 모를 생물들의 있을 법한 역사에 관해 페어브러더 씨와 더불어 이야기를 부풀려 나갔다. 그때 갑자기 모두의 주의를 끄는 알아들을 수 없는 작은 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"헨리에타 노블,\" 페어브러더 부인이, 가구 다리 사이를 안절부절 헤매고 다니는 자기 작은 여동생을 보며 말했다. \"무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n\"내 거북 등딱지 사탕갑을 잃어버렸어. 새끼 고양이가 굴려 버린 게 아닌가 싶구나.\" 그 자그마한 노부인이, 비버처럼 부산스러운 소리를 무심코 계속 내며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 그렇게 귀한 보물인가요, 이모?\" 페어브러더 씨가 안경을 추켜올리고 카펫을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"래디슬로 씨가 내게 준 거야,\" 노블 양이 말했다. \"독일제 갑인데—아주 예쁘단다. 하지만 떨어지면 늘 갈 수 있는 데까지 빙글빙글 굴러가 버리거든.\"\n\n\"아, 래디슬로의 선물이라면야,\" 페어브러더 씨가 깊이 이해한다는 어조로 말하며, 일어서서 찾기 시작했다. 마침내 그 갑은 작은 장식장 아래에서 발견되었고, 노블 양은 기쁨에 차 그것을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"지난번엔 난로 가리개 아래 있었지.\"\n\n\"이건 우리 이모님에게는 마음의 문제랍니다,\" 페어브러더 씨가 다시 자리에 앉으며 도러시아를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"헨리에타 노블이 누군가에게 애착을 품게 되면요, 커소번 부인,\" 그의 어머니가 힘주어 말했다. \"꼭 개와 같답니다. 그 사람 신발을 베개 삼아 베고 더 달게 잠을 잘 거예요.\"\n\"래디슬로 씨 신발이라면, 그럴 거예요,\" 헨리에타 노블이 말했다.\n\n도러시아는 화답하여 미소 지으려 애썼다. 그녀는 자기 심장이 격렬하게 두근거리고 있음을, 그리고 예전의 생기를 되찾으려 애써 보아야 아무 소용이 없음을 깨닫고 놀라며 당황했다. 자기 자신에게 경각심을 느끼고—그 변화가 일어난 계기가 그토록 뚜렷한 만큼 더 이상의 누설이 있을까 두려워하며, 그녀는 일어서서 낮은 목소리로 감추지 못한 불안을 담아 말했다. \"이만 가 봐야겠어요. 제가 너무 무리를 했나 봐요.\"\n지각이 빠른 페어브러더 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"그렇지요. 리드게이트 이야기를 하느라 기력을 절반은 소진하셨을 겁니다. 그런 일은 흥분이 가라앉고 나면 사람을 지치게 하지요.\"\n\n그는 팔을 내밀어 그녀를 저택까지 바래다주었지만, 도러시아는 그가 안녕히 주무시라고 인사할 때조차 말을 꺼내려 하지 않았다.\n저항의 한계에 다다랐고, 그녀는 피할 길 없는 고뇌의 손아귀에 사로잡힌 채 맥없이 주저앉았다. 몇 마디 힘없는 말로 탠트립을 물린 뒤, 그녀는 방문을 잠그고, 문에서 돌아서서 텅 빈 방 쪽을 향해 두 손으로 머리 꼭대기를 세게 누르며 신음하듯 토해 냈다—\n\n\"오, 나는 정말 그를 사랑했어!\"\n그러고 나서 고통의 물결이 그녀를 너무도 철저히 뒤흔들어 사고할 힘조차 남기지 않는 시간이 찾아왔다. 그녀는 흐느낌 사이로 큰 속삭임으로 울부짖을 수밖에 없었다. 로마 시절 이래 아주 작은 씨앗에서부터 심어 살려 온 잃어버린 믿음을 향해—남들에게 멸시받았으나 그녀의 생각으로는 가치 있던 한 사람에게 말없는 사랑과 신뢰로 매달리던, 그 잃어버린 기쁨을 향해—그의 기억 속에서 군림하던, 그 잃어버린 여인으로서의 자긍심을 향해—어느 길 위에서 그들이 변치 않은 알아봄으로 다시 만나 지나간 세월을 어제처럼 이어 가리라던, 그 잃어버린 희망의 감미롭고 아련한 전망을 향해.\n\n그 시간에 그녀는 인류의 영적 투쟁 속에서 고독의 자비로운 눈이 오랜 세월 지켜보아 온 일을 되풀이했다—그녀는 신비롭고 형체 없는 고뇌의 위력으로부터 자신을 구해 달라고, 모진 마음과 차가움과 쑤시는 듯한 피로에 간청했다. 그녀는 맨바닥에 누워 밤이 자기 주위로 차갑게 깊어 가도록 내버려 두었고, 그러는 동안 그 의젓한 여인의 몸은 절망에 빠진 어린아이라도 된 듯 흐느낌으로 떨렸다.\n거기에는 두 개의 형상—그녀의 심장을 둘로 찢어 놓는 두 개의 살아 있는 모습이 있었으니, 마치 자기 아이가 칼에 두 동강 나는 것을 보는 어머니가, 피 흐르는 한쪽을 가슴에 끌어안으면서도, 어머니의 진통을 결코 알지 못하는 거짓된 여인의 손에 실려 가는 다른 한쪽을 향해 고뇌에 차 시선을 보내는 것과도 같았다.\n\n여기, 화답하는 미소처럼 가까이, 여기, 서로 주고받는 말의 떨리는 끈 안에, 그녀가 믿었던 그 빛나는 존재가 있었다—다 닳아 버린 삶의 신부로 앉아 있던 그 어슴푸레한 지하 무덤을, 아침의 정령처럼 찾아와 준 그 존재가. 그리고 이제, 한 번도 깨어난 적 없던 충만한 자각과 더불어, 그녀는 그를 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 그들의 가까움이 한낱 이별의 환영일 뿐임을 쓰라린 울음으로 부르짖었다. 그녀는 절망의 움츠림 없는 토로 속에서 자신의 열정을 스스로에게 드러냈다.\n그리고 저편에, 멀리 떨어져 있으면서도 끈질기게 그녀와 함께, 그녀가 움직이는 곳마다 따라 움직이는, 또 한 사람의 윌 래디슬로가 있었으니, 그는 희망이 다 빠져나간 변질된 믿음이요, 들통난 환상이었다—아니, 멸시와 분노와 상처 입은 질투의 자존심 한가운데서, 아직은 그 어떤 회한 어린 연민의 통곡도 솟아날 수 없는, 살아 있는 한 남자였다. 도러시아의 분노의 불길은 쉬이 사그라지지 않아, 발작처럼 되돌아오는 멸시 어린 비난으로 타올랐다. 어째서 그는 그녀의 삶에—그가 없었어도 충분히 온전했을지 모를 그녀의 삶에—제 삶을 끼워 넣으며 비집고 들어왔던가? 어째서 그는, 대가로 내놓을 변변찮은 것이라곤 아무것도 없던 그녀에게, 자기의 값싼 호의와 입에서만 나온 말들을 가져왔던가? 그는 자기가 그녀를 속이고 있음을 알았다—바로 그 작별의 순간에, 그녀의 마음에 대한 값을 온전히 다 치르는 척하면서, 사실은 그 절반을 이미 써 버렸음을 알고 있었으면서도, 그녀가 그 말을 믿게 하고 싶어 했다. 어째서 그는, 그녀가 아무것도 바라지 않은—다만 그들이 좀 덜 경멸스러운 존재이기를 기도했을 뿐인 그 무리 사이에 그냥 머물러 있지 않았던가?\n\n그러나 마침내 그녀는 큰 속삭임의 울부짖음과 신음을 이어 갈 기력조차 잃었다. 그녀는 맥없는 흐느낌으로 가라앉았고, 차가운 바닥 위에서 흐느끼다가 잠이 들었다.\n\n새벽 여명의 쌀쌀한 시각, 주위가 온통 어슴푸레할 때, 그녀는 잠에서 깨어났다—여기가 어디인지, 무슨 일이 있었는지 어리둥절해하며 놀란 것이 아니라, 자기가 슬픔의 두 눈을 들여다보고 있다는 더없이 또렷한 자각과 더불어. 그녀는 일어나 따뜻한 것들로 몸을 감싸고, 예전에도 자주 무언가를 지켜보곤 했던 커다란 안락의자에 앉았다. 그녀는 그 모진 밤을 견디고도 몸이 아프지 않을 만큼 정정했으니, 다소의 쑤심과 피로 말고는 별다른 탈이 없었다. 하지만 그녀는 새로운 상태로 깨어나 있었다. 자기 영혼이 그 끔찍한 갈등으로부터 해방된 것 같은 느낌이었다. 더 이상 슬픔과 씨름하는 것이 아니라, 그것을 오래갈 동반자로 곁에 앉히고 자기 생각을 함께 나눌 수 있었다. 이제 생각들이 빽빽이 밀려들었기 때문이다. 발작이 지속되는 동안 말고는, 자기 재앙의 비좁은 감방에 틀어박혀, 남의 처지를 한낱 자기 처지의 부수적 사건으로만 바라보는 그 얼빠진 비참함에 잠겨 있는 것은, 도러시아의 천성이 아니었다.\n\n그녀는 이제 그 어제 아침을 다시금 신중하게 처음부터 끝까지 되살려 보기 시작하며, 모든 세부와 그것이 지닐 법한 의미 하나하나에 일부러 머물렀다. 그 장면에 있던 사람이 그녀 혼자였던가? 그것이 오로지 그녀만의 사건이었던가? 그녀는 그 일을 또 다른 여인의 삶과 얽힌 것으로—흐려진 젊음에 얼마간의 밝음과 위안을 가져다주고 싶다는 갈망을 품고 찾아갔던 그 여인의 삶과 얽힌 것으로 생각하려 애썼다. 질투 어린 분노와 혐오가 처음 솟구쳤을 때, 그 끔찍한 방을 나서며 그녀는 그 방문을 시작하게 했던 모든 자비를 던져 버렸었다. 그녀는 윌과 로저먼드 둘 다를 자신의 불타는 멸시로 휘감았고, 그녀에게는 로저먼드가 영영 시야에서 타 버린 듯이 여겨졌다. 하지만 여인으로 하여금 변심한 연인보다 연적에게 더 잔인하게 굴게 만드는 그 비열한 충동은, 그녀 안의 지배적인 정의의 정신이 일단 혼란을 이겨 내고 사물의 더 참된 척도를 그녀에게 보여 주고 나자, 도러시아 안에서 다시 되살아날 힘을 가질 수 없었다. 전에 그녀가 리드게이트의 처지의 시련을, 그리고 자기 결혼처럼 명백한 고통뿐 아니라 감추어진 고통까지 안고 있는 듯한 그 젊은 부부의 결합을 스스로에게 그려 보며 기울였던 그 모든 능동적인 사유—그 모든 생생하고 공감 어린 경험이 이제 하나의 힘이 되어 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 그것은, 한번 얻은 지식이 스스로를 주장하며 우리가 무지하던 시절에 보던 대로 다시는 보지 못하게 하듯이, 스스로를 주장했다. 그녀는 돌이킬 수 없는 자신의 슬픔에게, 그 슬픔이 자기를 노력에서 물러나게 몰아붙이는 대신 더욱 남을 도울 수 있게 만들어야 한다고 말했다.\n\n그리고 그녀와 닿은 인연이, 마치 그들이 신성한 가지를 든 탄원자들이라도 되는 양 그녀에게 의무를 지운 그 세 사람의 삶에, 이것이 어떤 위기가 되지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠는가? 그녀가 구해야 할 대상들은 그녀의 공상으로 찾아낼 것이 아니었다. 그들은 이미 그녀를 위해 선택되어 있었다. 그녀는 완전한 정의를 갈망했으니, 그것이 그녀 안에 옥좌를 세우고 그녀의 길 잃은 의지를 다스려 주기를 바랐다. \"내가 만약 나 자신의 고통을 움켜잡아 침묵하게 만들고 저 세 사람을 생각할 수 있다면, 나는 무엇을 해야 할까—오늘, 바로 이날, 어떻게 행동해야 할까?\"\n\n그 물음에 이르기까지 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 방 안으로 빛이 뚫고 들어오고 있었다. 그녀는 커튼을 열고, 시야에 들어오는 한 자락 길을, 그 너머 정문 밖의 들판을 바라보았다. 길 위에는 등에 보따리를 진 남자와 아기를 안은 여자가 있었고, 들판에서는 움직이는 형체들이 보였으니—아마도 개를 데리고 다니는 양치기였으리라. 멀리 굽은 하늘에는 진주빛 광채가 어려 있었고, 그녀는 세상의 광대함과, 노동과 인내를 향한 사람들의 갖가지 깨어남을 느꼈다. 그녀도 그 자기도 모르게 고동치는 삶의 일부였으니, 호화로운 안식처에서 한낱 구경꾼으로 그것을 내다볼 수도, 이기적인 한탄 속에 두 눈을 감출 수도 없었다.\n\n그날 무엇을 하기로 결심할지는 아직 그리 분명해 보이지 않았으나, 그녀가 이룰 수 있는 어떤 것이, 곧 또렷해질 다가오는 웅성거림처럼 그녀를 움직였다. 그녀는 모진 밤샘의 피로가 얼마간 배어든 듯한 옷을 벗고 몸단장을 시작했다. 이윽고 그녀가 탠트립을 부르는 종을 울리자, 탠트립이 실내복 차림으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"아니, 마님, 이 좋은 밤을 침대에 들지도 않으셨네요,\" 탠트립이 먼저 침대를, 그다음 도러시아의 얼굴을 바라보며 불쑥 말했다. 그 얼굴은 씻었는데도 슬픔의 성모처럼 창백한 두 뺨과 발그레한 눈꺼풀을 하고 있었다. \"이러다 몸 상하세요, 정말요. 지금쯤은 누구라도 마님께서 스스로에게 자그마한 위안 하나쯤 누리실 자격이 있다고 여길 텐데요.\"\n\n\"걱정 말아요, 탠트립,\" 도러시아가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"나 잤어요. 아프지 않아요. 되도록 빨리 커피 한 잔 마실 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 그리고 새 드레스를 가져다줘요. 오늘은 아마 새 보닛도 필요할 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"한 달 넘게 마님을 위해 마련되어 그대로 놓여 있는 것들인데요, 마님. 마님께서 두어 파운드어치 크레이프를 덜 두르신 모습을 뵙게 되다니 더없이 감사할 따름이에요,\" 탠트립이 불을 지피려 몸을 굽히며 말했다. \"상복에도 분별이 있어야 하는 법이라고 저는 늘 말해 왔지요. 치맛자락 아래 주름 세 단에 보닛에는 수수한 주름 장식이면—그리고 누가 천사처럼 보인 적이 있다면, 그건 망사 주름 장식을 두르신 마님이세요—두 해째에는 그게 격에 맞는 거예요. 적어도 제 생각엔 그래요,\" 탠트립이 불을 근심스레 바라보며 말을 맺었다. \"그리고 누가 저랑 결혼하면서 제가 그 흉측한 상복용 소맷동을 자기를 위해 두 해나 두르리라고 우쭐댄다면, 그건 제 허영에 제가 속는 거예요, 그뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"불은 그만하면 됐어요, 착한 탠,\" 도러시아가 옛 로잔 시절에 그러던 것처럼, 다만 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"커피나 갖다줘요.\"\n\n그녀는 커다란 안락의자에 몸을 파묻고, 지친 정적 속에 머리를 등받이에 기댔다. 그러는 동안 탠트립은 자기 젊은 안주인의 이 이상한 모순됨에—그 어느 때보다 더 미망인다운 얼굴을 한 바로 그날 아침에, 전에는 마다했던 더 가벼운 상복을 청하다니—의아해하며 물러갔다. 탠트립으로서는 이 수수께끼의 실마리를 결코 찾지 못했으리라. 도러시아는 은밀한 기쁨 하나를 묻었다고 해서 자기 앞에 놓인 능동적인 삶이 줄어든 것은 아님을 스스로 인정하고 싶었다. 그리고 새로운 옷이 모든 입문에 따르는 것이라는 그 전통이 그녀의 마음에 어른거렸기에, 그녀는 차분한 결심을 향한 그 사소한 외적 도움이나마 붙잡으려 했다. 그 결심은 쉽지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n그럼에도 열한 시에 그녀는 미들마치를 향해 걷고 있었으니, 되도록 조용히, 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 로저먼드를 만나 구하려는 두 번째 시도를 하기로 마음을 굳힌 뒤였다.\n* 슬픔의 성모: 원문 'mater dolorosa'. 그리스도의 수난을 슬퍼하는 성모 마리아를 가리키는 라틴어 표현.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXI.\n\n분투하려는 굳센 결의를 일깨우고 흔드는구나.\n—『파우스트』 제2부.\n\n도러시아가 다시 리드게이트의 집 문 앞에서 마사에게 말을 건네고 있을 때, 그는 문이 빠끔히 열린 바로 옆방에서 외출 채비를 하고 있었다. 그녀의 목소리를 듣자 그는 곧바로 그녀에게로 나왔다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 리드게이트 부인이 저를 만나 주실 수 있을까요?\" 그녀가 말했다. 어제 방문에 관한 언급은 일절 하지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 생각한 터였다.\n\n\"틀림없이 만날 겁니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다. 도러시아의 안색도 로저먼드만큼이나 달라졌다는 생각이 떠올랐으나 그는 그것을 입에 올리지 않았다. \"안으로 들어오셔서 제가 아내에게 부인이 오셨다는 말을 전하게 해 주신다면요. 어제 다녀가신 뒤로 아내가 그리 좋지 않았습니다만, 오늘 아침에는 한결 나아졌으니, 다시 부인을 뵈면 기운을 차릴 가능성이 아주 높을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n도러시아가 예상했던 대로 리드게이트는 어제 그녀가 방문했던 정황에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것이 분명했다. 아니, 그는 그녀가 본래 의도했던 대로 일을 마치고 갔으리라 짐작하는 듯 보였다. 그녀는 로저먼드에게 만나 달라고 청하는 짧은 쪽지를 미리 준비해 두었고, 그가 거기 없었더라면 하인에게 그것을 건넸을 터였지만, 이제는 그가 자기 방문을 알린 결과가 어찌 될지 몹시 불안했다.\n\n그녀를 응접실로 안내한 뒤 그는 잠시 멈춰 호주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"어젯밤에 이것을 썼는데, 말을 타고 나가는 길에 로윅으로 가져갈 참이었습니다. 흔한 감사의 말로는 다 갚을 수 없을 만큼 너무도 과분한 것에 고마워할 때는, 말보다 글이 덜 답답한 법이지요. 적어도 그 말이 얼마나 모자란지를 귀로 듣지는 않아도 되니까요.\"\n\n도러시아의 얼굴이 밝아졌다. \"정작 고마워해야 할 사람은 저예요. 제게 그 역할을 맡게 해 주셨으니까요. 정말 승낙하신 거지요?\" 그녀가 갑자기 의심이 들어 물었다.\n\n\"네, 오늘 수표가 불스트로드에게 갈 겁니다.\"\n\n그는 더는 말하지 않고 위층의 로저먼드에게로 올라갔다. 그녀는 막 옷차림을 끝내고 나른하게 앉아 다음으로 무엇을 할까 궁리하고 있었다. 슬픔에 잠긴 나날에도 사소한 일들에 부지런히 손을 놀리는 평소의 습성 탓에 무언가 일거리를 시작하긴 했으나, 흥미가 일지 않아 더디게 끌고 가거나 도중에 멈춰 버리곤 했다. 그녀는 아파 보였지만 평소의 차분한 태도는 되찾은 상태였고, 리드게이트는 어떤 질문으로도 그녀의 마음을 어지럽힐까 두려워했다. 그는 그녀에게 수표가 든 도러시아의 편지에 관해 이야기했고, 그러고 나서 이렇게 말했다. \"래디슬로가 돌아왔어, 로지. 어젯밤에 나와 함께 앉아 있었지. 아마 오늘 또 들를 거야. 좀 지치고 풀이 죽어 보이더군.\" 그러나 로저먼드는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n이제 위층으로 올라온 그는 그녀에게 아주 다정하게 말했다. \"로지, 캐소본 부인이 당신을 또 보러 오셨어. 만나 보고 싶지 않아?\" 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 다소 놀란 듯 움찔하는 것을 보고도 그는 놀라지 않았다. 어제의 면담이 일으킨 동요 뒤끝이려니 여긴 것이다. 그것은 이로운 동요라고, 그는 생각했다. 그 덕분에 그녀가 다시 자기에게로 마음을 돌린 듯했으니까.\n\n로저먼드는 감히 싫다고 말하지 못했다. 목소리의 한 가락으로라도 어제의 사실을 건드릴 엄두가 나지 않았다. 캐소본 부인은 어찌하여 또 온 것일까? 그 답은 텅 빈 공백이었고, 로저먼드는 그것을 오직 두려움으로만 채울 수 있었다. 윌 래디슬로의 가슴을 찢는 말들이 도러시아에 관한 온갖 생각을 새로운 아픔으로 바꿔 놓았기 때문이다. 그럼에도 굴욕에 찬 이 새로운 불확실함 속에서 그녀는 따르는 것 말고는 감히 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 그녀는 그러겠다고 말하진 않았지만 자리에서 일어나 리드게이트가 어깨에 가벼운 숄을 둘러 주도록 내버려 두었고, 그러는 동안 그는 \"난 곧 나갈 거야\"라고 말했다. 그때 무언가가 그녀의 머리를 스쳐, 그녀는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"마사에게 응접실로는 아무도 들이지 말라고 일러 줘요.\" 리드게이트는 이 바람을 충분히 이해한다고 여기며 그러마고 했다. 그는 그녀를 응접실 문까지 데려다주고는 발길을 돌리며, 아내가 자기를 신뢰하도록 만드는 일을 다른 여자의 영향력에 기대야 하는 자신은 꽤나 서툰 남편이라고 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n로저먼드는 도러시아를 향해 걸어가며 부드러운 숄을 몸에 두르는 한편, 마음속으로는 자신의 영혼을 차가운 자제로 감싸고 있었다. 캐소본 부인이 윌에 관해 무슨 말을 하러 온 것일까? 만약 그렇다면 그것은 로저먼드가 분개할 만한 무례였다. 그래서 그녀는 어떤 말에도 정중한 무감각으로 응대하리라 마음을 다잡았다. 윌이 그녀의 자존심을 너무도 모질게 짓밟아 놓아, 그녀는 그와 도러시아에게 어떤 가책도 느낄 수 없었다. 자신이 입은 상처가 훨씬 더 큰 것만 같았다. 도러시아는 단지 \"선택받은\" 여자일 뿐 아니라, 리드게이트의 은인이라는 무시무시한 이점까지 지니고 있었다. 가엾은 로저먼드의 고통스럽고 혼란스러운 눈에는, 자신과 관련된 모든 일에서 우위를 점하는 이 캐소본 부인이라는 여자가, 이제 유리한 처지에 있다는 자각과 그것을 이용하라 부추기는 적의를 품고 찾아온 것만 같았다. 사실 로저먼드뿐 아니라, 이 사건의 겉으로 드러난 사실들만 알고 도러시아가 따른 그 단순한 마음의 충동을 알지 못하는 사람이라면 누구라도, 그녀가 왜 왔는지 의아해할 만했다.\n\n마치 제 모습의 사랑스러운 유령 같은 모습으로, 우아하고 가냘픈 몸을 부드러운 흰 숄로 감싸고, 둥글고 앳된 입매와 뺨이 어쩔 수 없이 온순함과 순결함을 떠올리게 하는 채로, 로저먼드는 손님으로부터 세 야드쯤 떨어진 자리에 멈춰 서서 고개를 숙였다. 그러나 자유로움을 느끼고 싶을 때면 결코 억누를 수 없는 충동에서 장갑을 벗어 둔 도러시아는, 슬프면서도 다정한 솔직함이 가득한 얼굴로 앞으로 나서며 손을 내밀었다. 로저먼드는 그 눈길을 마주하지 않을 수 없었고, 자신의 작은 손을 도러시아의 손에 맡기지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 손은 부드러운 모성으로 그것을 감싸 쥐었다. 그러자 곧바로 자기 선입견에 대한 의구심이 그녀의 내면에서 꿈틀대기 시작했다. 로저먼드는 얼굴을 살피는 데 빠른 눈을 지녔던지라, 캐소본 부인의 얼굴이 어제보다 창백하고 달라졌으면서도 부드러운 것이, 그 손의 굳건한 온화함과 닮았다는 것을 알아보았다. 그러나 도러시아는 자신의 기력을 조금 과신했던 터였다. 그날 아침 그녀의 정신 작용이 보인 명료함과 강렬함은, 그녀의 몸을 가장 고운 베네치아 수정처럼 위태로울 만큼 예민하게 만든 신경의 고양이 이어진 것이었다. 그리하여 로저먼드를 바라보던 그녀는 갑자기 가슴이 북받쳐 오르는 것을 느끼며 말을 잇지 못했다. 눈물을 참는 데 온 힘이 필요했다. 그녀는 가까스로 그 일에 성공했고, 그 감정은 흐느낌의 혼령처럼 얼굴 위로 스쳐 갔을 뿐이었다. 하지만 그것은 캐소본 부인의 심경이 자신이 상상했던 것과는 사뭇 다른 무언가일 것이라는 로저먼드의 인상을 한층 더 굳혀 주었다.\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 한마디 서두도 없이 마침 가장 가까이에 있던, 그리고 마침 서로 바짝 붙어 있던 두 의자에 앉았다. 처음 고개를 숙였을 때 로저먼드는 캐소본 부인에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있어야겠다고 생각했었는데도 말이다. 그러나 그녀는 어떤 일이 어찌 될지 따지기를 그치고, 그저 무슨 일이 닥칠지 궁금해하기만 했다. 그리고 도러시아는 아주 담담하게 말문을 열어, 이야기를 이어 갈수록 단단해져 갔다.\n\n\"어제 다 마치지 못한 볼일이 있었어요. 그래서 이렇게 곧바로 다시 찾아온 거예요. 제가 부인을 너무 성가시게 한다고 여기지 않으시겠지요. 리드게이트 씨에게 가해진 부당한 처사에 관해 부인과 이야기하러 왔다고 말씀드리면요. 부인께 위안이 되지 않을까요? 그분에 관해 많은 것을 아시는 게요. 정작 자기 변호가 되고 자기 명예가 걸린 일이라는 바로 그 이유로 그분 자신은 입에 올리고 싶어 하지 않을 그런 것들 말이에요. 부군에게는 그분의 고결한 인품에 대한 믿음을 저버리지 않은 따뜻한 벗들이 있다는 것을 아시면 기쁘지 않으시겠어요? 제가 주제넘게 군다고 여기지 마시고 이 이야기를 하게 해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n로저먼드의 마음을, 자신과 이 여자 사이를 가로막고 미움을 부추기는 근거로 가득 채웠던 그 모든 사실들 위로, 너그러운 무심함과 함께 흘러나오는 듯한 그 다정하고 간곡한 어조는, 움츠러든 그녀의 두려움 위로 따뜻한 물줄기처럼 어루만지듯 다가왔다. 물론 캐소본 부인은 그 사실들을 마음에 두고 있었으나, 그와 관련된 어떤 것도 말하려 하지 않았다. 그 안도감이 너무 커서 로저먼드는 그 순간 다른 것을 거의 느낄 수 없었다. 그녀는 마음이 새롭게 편안해진 가운데 곱게 대답했다.\n\n\"부인이 무척 친절하셨다는 것을 알아요. 터셔스에 관해 제게 해 주실 말씀이라면 무엇이든 듣고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"그저께,\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"제가 병원 일에 관한 의견을 듣고 싶어 그분께 로윅으로 와 달라고 청했을 때, 그분은 무지한 사람들이 그분에게 의혹을 던지게 만든 이 슬픈 사건에서 자신이 어떻게 처신했고 어떤 심정이었는지를 제게 죄다 이야기해 주셨어요. 그분이 제게 말씀하신 까닭은 제가 아주 당돌하게 여쭈었기 때문이에요. 저는 그분이 결코 부정한 행동을 한 적이 없다고 믿었고, 그래서 그분께 그 자초지종을 들려 달라고 청했어요. 그분은 전에는 한 번도, 심지어 부인께도 그 이야기를 한 적이 없노라고 제게 털어놓으셨어요. 죄 지은 사람들도 그렇게 말하는 마당에, 마치 그것이 증거라도 되는 양 '나는 잘못이 없다'고 말하기가 무척 싫었기 때문이래요. 사실인즉슨, 그분은 이 래플스라는 사람에 관해 아무것도, 그에게 어떤 나쁜 비밀이 있는지도 전혀 몰랐어요. 그리고 불스트로드 씨가 자기에게 돈을 내준 것은 전에 그것을 거절했던 일을 뉘우치고 호의에서 그러는 것이라고 여겼지요. 환자에 관한 그분의 온 관심은 그를 올바르게 치료하는 데 있었고, 그 사례가 자신이 예상했던 대로 끝나지 않은 데 다소 마음이 편치 않았어요. 하지만 그분은 그때도, 그리고 지금도, 거기에 누구의 잘못도 없었을 수 있다고 생각하세요. 그리고 저는 페어브러더 씨와 브룩 씨와 제임스 체텀 경에게 이야기했어요. 그들 모두 부군을 믿고 있어요. 그러면 부인께 위안이 되지 않겠어요? 그러면 부인께 용기가 생기지 않겠어요?\"\n\n도러시아의 얼굴은 생기를 띠었고, 그것이 아주 가까이에서 로저먼드를 향해 빛나자, 그녀는 이 자기 망각의 열정 앞에서 윗사람을 대하는 듯한 수줍은 머뭇거림 비슷한 것을 느꼈다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 어색하게 말했다. \"고맙습니다. 무척 친절하시네요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그분은 이 일에 관해 모든 것을 부인께 쏟아 내지 않은 것이 너무도 잘못이었다고 느끼셨어요. 하지만 부인은 그분을 용서하실 거예요. 그것은 그분이 다른 무엇보다 부인의 행복을 훨씬 더 마음에 두기 때문이었어요. 그분은 자기 삶이 부인의 삶과 하나로 묶여 있다고 느끼시고, 자신의 불운이 부인을 아프게 할 수밖에 없다는 것을 무엇보다 가슴 아파하세요. 그분이 제게는 말씀하실 수 있었던 것은 제가 아무 상관 없는 사람이기 때문이에요. 그러고 나서 저는 그분께 부인을 뵈러 와도 되겠느냐고 여쭈었어요. 그분의 괴로움과 부인의 괴로움이 너무도 마음에 사무쳤거든요. 그래서 제가 어제 찾아온 것이고, 오늘 또 온 거예요. 괴로움이란 견디기가 참 힘든 것이지요, 그렇지 않나요? 누군가가 괴로움을, 가슴을 에는 괴로움을 겪고 있는데 우리가 도울 수 있으면서도 결코 애쓰지 않는다는 것을, 어찌 알면서 살아갈 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n자신이 토해 내는 그 감정에 온전히 사로잡힌 도러시아는, 자기 자신의 시련의 한복판에서 로저먼드의 시련을 향해 이야기하고 있다는 것 말고는 모든 것을 잊어버렸다. 그 격정이 점점 더 그녀의 말에 스며들어, 이윽고 그 어조는 어둠 속에서 고통받는 어떤 생명의 나직한 울부짖음처럼 듣는 이의 골수에까지 사무칠 만했다. 그리고 그녀는 자기도 모르게, 앞서 꼭 쥐었던 그 작은 손 위에 다시 손을 얹고 있었다.\n\n로저먼드는, 마치 내면의 상처를 후벼 판 것 같은 사무치는 아픔에 사로잡혀, 어제 남편에게 매달렸을 때와 똑같이 발작적인 울음을 터뜨렸다. 가엾은 도러시아는 자기 자신의 슬픔의 거대한 물결이 다시 자기 위로 밀려드는 것을 느끼고 있었다. 그녀의 생각이 로저먼드의 심란함에 윌 래디슬로가 어쩌면 한몫 했을지도 모른다는 데로 이끌렸던 것이다. 그녀는 이 만남이 끝날 때까지 자신을 충분히 억누를 수 없을까 봐 두려워지기 시작했고, 그녀의 손은 여전히 로저먼드의 무릎 위에 놓여 있었으나—그 밑에 있던 손은 빠져나가고 없었다—치밀어 오르는 자신의 흐느낌과 맞서 싸우고 있었다. 그녀는 이것이 세 사람의 인생에서, 자기 자신의 인생이 아니라—아니, 자기 인생에서는 이미 돌이킬 수 없는 일이 일어났으니—위험과 고통의 엄숙한 이웃으로 자기 삶을 스치고 있는 그 세 사람의 인생에서 하나의 전환점일지도 모른다는 생각으로 자신을 다스리려 애썼다. 자기 곁에서 울고 있는 이 연약한 존재를, 어긋나고 어울리지 않는 결속의 비참함에서 구해 낼 시간이 어쩌면 아직 남아 있을지도 몰랐다. 그리고 이 순간은 다른 어떤 순간과도 달랐다. 그녀와 로저먼드가 두 사람 모두 어제의 그 짜릿한 자각을 안은 채 다시 함께 있게 되는 일은 결코 없을 터였다. 그녀는 둘 사이의 관계가, 비록 자신의 감정이 어떤 식으로 얽혀 있는지를 리드게이트 부인이 죄다 알고 있다고는 짐작조차 못 했지만, 자신에게 특별한 영향력을 줄 만큼 충분히 특이하다고 느꼈다.\n\n그것은 도러시아가 상상할 수 있는 것보다도 더 새로운 위기였다. 로저먼드는 자기 자신을 손쉽게 자신하고 남들을 비판하며 지내던 자신의 꿈의 세계를 산산이 부순 첫 번째 큰 충격을 겪고 있었다. 그리고 자기에게 필연코 질투에 찬 미움을 품고 있으리라 여겨 움츠러드는 혐오와 두려움으로 다가갔던 한 여자에게서 느껴지는 이 기이하고 뜻밖의 감정의 발로는, 자신이 이제 막 들이닥친 미지의 세계를 거닐고 있었다는 자각과 더불어 그녀의 영혼을 한층 더 휘청거리게 만들었다.\n\n로저먼드의 경련하던 목이 가라앉아 잠잠해지고, 얼굴을 가리고 있던 손수건을 거두었을 때, 그녀의 눈은 마치 푸른 꽃송이라도 되는 양 속절없이 도러시아의 눈과 마주쳤다. 이렇게 울고 난 뒤에 처신을 따져 무엇하겠는가? 그리고 도러시아도 소리 없이 흘린 눈물 자국을 미처 지우지 못한 채, 거의 그만큼이나 어린아이 같아 보였다. 이 두 사람 사이에서 자존심은 허물어져 있었다.\n\n\"우리는 부군 이야기를 하고 있었지요.\" 도러시아가 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"요전 날 그분의 모습이 괴로움으로 몹시 달라져 보였어요. 그 전 여러 주 동안 그분을 뵙지 못했었거든요. 그분은 이 시련 속에서 무척 외로움을 느껴 왔다고 하셨어요. 하지만 부인께 모든 것을 활짝 털어놓을 수 있었더라면 이 모든 것을 한결 잘 견뎌 내셨으리라 생각해요.\"\n\n\"터셔스는 제가 무슨 말이라도 하면 어찌나 화를 내고 못 참아 하는지요.\" 로저먼드가, 그가 도러시아에게 자기 험담을 했으리라 짐작하며 말했다. \"제가 그이에게 괴로운 이야기를 하기 싫어하는 걸 의아해하면 안 되지요.\"\n\n\"그분은 말을 하지 않은 자기 자신을 탓하셨어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"부인에 관해 하신 말씀은, 부인을 불행하게 만드는 일은 무엇이든 자신은 그것을 하면서 행복할 수 없다는 것, 자신의 결혼은 당연히 모든 일에 관한 자기 선택에 영향을 미칠 수밖에 없는 결속이라는 것이었어요. 바로 그 이유로 그분은 병원에서 자리를 지키시라는 제 제안을 거절하셨어요. 그러면 미들마치에 머물러야 하는데, 부인께 괴로움이 될 일은 어떤 것도 하지 않으려 하셨거든요. 그분이 제게 그런 말씀을 하실 수 있었던 것은, 제가 결혼 생활에서, 남편의 병환으로 인해 많은 시련을 겪었다는 것을 아시기 때문이에요. 그 병환이 남편의 계획을 가로막고 그분을 슬프게 했지요. 그리고 우리에게 매여 있는 다른 사람을 아프게 할까 봐 늘 두려워하며 살아가는 것이 얼마나 힘든지를 제가 절감했다는 것도 아시고요.\"\n\n도러시아는 잠시 기다렸다. 로저먼드의 얼굴 위로 희미한 기쁨이 살며시 번지는 것을 알아챈 것이다. 그러나 대답이 없자, 그녀는 점점 떨려 오는 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"결혼이란 다른 무엇과도 너무 달라요. 그것이 가져다주는 가까움에는 어쩐지 두렵기까지 한 무언가가 있어요. 설령 우리가 결혼한 사람보다—그 사람보다—다른 누군가를 더 사랑한다 해도, 그것은 아무 소용이 없을 거예요.\" 가엾은 도러시아는, 가슴이 두근거리는 불안 속에서 더듬더듬 말을 붙들 수밖에 없었다. \"제 말은, 결혼은 그런 종류의 사랑 속에서 어떤 축복을 주거나 받을 수 있는 우리의 모든 힘을 빨아들여 버린다는 거예요. 그 사랑이 무척 소중할 수도 있다는 걸 알아요. 하지만 그것은 우리의 결혼을 죽여 버려요. 그러면 그 결혼은 살인처럼 우리 곁에 남고, 다른 모든 것은 사라져 버리지요. 그리고 우리 남편이—만약 그이가 우리를 사랑하고 믿었는데, 우리가 그이를 돕기는커녕 그이의 삶에 저주가 되고 말았다면—\"\n\n그녀의 목소리는 아주 낮게 가라앉았다. 너무 주제넘게 구는 것은 아닐까, 마치 자기 자신은 완전무결한데 잘못을 향해 말하는 것처럼 들리지는 않을까 하는 두려움이 그녀를 짓눌렀던 것이다. 그녀는 자신의 불안에 너무 골몰한 나머지 로저먼드도 떨고 있다는 것을 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 질책하기보다는 가엾이 여기는 동류의 정을 표하고 싶은 마음이 가득하여, 그녀는 로저먼드의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹고 한층 더 격하게 빠른 어조로 말했다. \"알아요, 그 감정이 무척 소중할 수 있다는 것을 알아요. 그것은 우리도 모르는 사이에 우리를 사로잡았지요. 그것은 너무도 힘겨워서, 그것과 헤어지는 일이 죽음처럼 느껴질지도 몰라요. 그리고 우리는 약해요. 저도 약하답니다.\"\n\n다른 사람을 구해 내려 안간힘을 쓰던 그 한복판에서 솟구친 자기 자신의 슬픔의 물결이, 도러시아를 압도하는 힘으로 덮쳐 왔다. 그녀는 말을 잃은 채 동요에 휩싸여 멈춰 섰다. 울지는 않았으나 속으로 무언가에 붙들려 씨름하는 듯한 느낌이었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 한층 죽은 듯한 창백함을 띠었고, 입술은 떨렸으며, 그녀는 자기 손 밑에 놓인 손을 속절없이 눌렀다.\n\n자기 것보다 더 강한 감정에 사로잡혀—모든 것에 새롭고 무시무시하며 형언할 수 없는 양상을 입히는 어떤 새로운 움직임에 떠밀려 가던—로저먼드는 아무 말도 찾지 못했으나, 저도 모르게 아주 가까이 있던 도러시아의 이마에 입술을 댔다. 그러고는 잠시 동안 두 여자는 마치 난파를 당한 사람들처럼 서로를 부둥켜안았다.\n\n\"부인은 사실이 아닌 것을 생각하고 계세요.\" 로저먼드가, 여전히 도러시아의 팔이 자신을 감싸고 있는 것을 느끼며 다급한 반쯤의 속삭임으로 말했다. 마치 핏빛 죄책감이라도 되는 양 자신을 짓누르는 무언가로부터 벗어나려는, 알 수 없는 절박함에 떠밀린 것이었다.\n\n두 사람은 서로를 바라보며 떨어졌다.\n\n\"어제 부인이 들어오셨을 때, 그것은 부인이 생각하신 것과 달랐어요.\" 로저먼드가 같은 어조로 말했다.\n\n도러시아에게서 놀라 귀 기울이는 움직임이 일었다. 그녀는 로저먼드 자신에 대한 변명을 예상했다.\n\n\"그이는 제게 다른 여자를 얼마나 사랑하는지를 말하고 있었어요. 자기는 결코 저를 사랑할 수 없다는 것을 제가 알게 하려고요.\" 로저먼드가, 말을 이어 갈수록 점점 더 다급해지며 말했다. \"그리고 이제 저는 그이가 저를 미워한다고 생각해요. 왜냐하면—왜냐하면 부인이 어제 그이를 오해하셨으니까요. 그이는 저 때문에 부인이 자기를 나쁘게 여기게 될 거라고, 자기가 거짓된 사람이라고 여기게 될 거라고 말해요. 하지만 저 때문에 그리되지는 않을 거예요. 그이는 저를 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없어요. 제가 알아요, 그런 적이 없다는 걸요. 그이는 늘 저를 대수롭지 않게 여겨 왔어요. 어제 그이는 부인 말고는 자기에게 다른 어떤 여자도 존재하지 않는다고 말했어요. 일어난 일의 책임은 온전히 저에게 있어요. 그이는 부인께 결코 해명할 수 없다고 말했어요. 저 때문에요. 그이는 부인이 다시는 자기를 좋게 여길 수 없을 거라고 말했어요. 하지만 이제 제가 부인께 말씀드렸으니, 그이는 더 이상 저를 책망할 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n로저먼드는 전에는 알지 못했던 충동에 떠밀려 자기 영혼을 털어놓았다. 그녀는 도러시아의 격정이 자신을 가라앉히는 영향 아래 고백을 시작했고, 말을 이어 갈수록, 여전히 내면에서 칼에 베인 상처처럼 사무치는 윌의 책망을 자신이 물리치고 있다는 자각을 얻게 되었다.\n\n도러시아 안에서 일어난 감정의 격변은 기쁨이라 부르기에는 너무도 격렬했다. 그것은 하나의 소용돌이였고, 그 속에서 밤과 아침의 끔찍한 긴장이 버티는 아픔을 빚어냈다. 그녀는 다만 자신이 그것을 느낄 힘을 되찾았을 때 이것이 기쁨이 되리라는 것만을 어렴풋이 알 수 있을 뿐이었다. 그 순간 그녀의 의식은 거침없는 무한한 연민의 의식이었다. 이제 그녀는 아무런 갈등 없이 로저먼드를 가엾이 여겼고, 그녀의 마지막 말에 진심으로 응답했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그분은 더 이상 부인을 책망할 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n남에게서 좋은 점을 과대평가하는 평소의 성향대로, 그녀는 자신을 고통에서 구해 준 그 너그러운 노력에 대해 로저먼드를 향한 큰 정이 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다. 그 노력이 실은 자기 자신의 기운이 빚어낸 반향이라는 것은 미처 헤아리지 못한 채로. 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 오늘 아침에 찾아온 게 못마땅하지 않으시죠?\"\n\n\"아니요, 부인은 제게 무척 잘해 주셨어요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"부인이 그렇게 잘해 주시리라고는 생각지 못했어요. 저는 무척 불행했어요. 지금도 행복하진 않아요. 모든 것이 너무 슬퍼요.\"\n\n\"하지만 더 나은 날들이 올 거예요. 부군은 정당하게 평가받으실 거예요. 그리고 그분은 위안을 부인께 기대고 계세요. 그분이 가장 사랑하는 사람은 부인이에요. 가장 끔찍한 상실은 그것을 잃는 것일 텐데, 부인은 그것을 잃지 않으셨어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 로저먼드의 애정이 다시 남편에게로 향해 가고 있다는 어떤 표시를 얻어 내지 못할까 봐, 자기 자신의 안도라는 너무도 압도적인 생각을 밀어내려 애썼다.\n\n\"그러면 터셔스는 저를 탓하지 않았던 거군요?\" 로저먼드가 말했다. 이제 그녀는 리드게이트가 캐소본 부인에게 무슨 말이든 했을 수 있다는 것, 그리고 이 부인이 다른 여자들과는 분명히 다르다는 것을 알아차렸다. 어쩌면 그 물음에는 희미한 질투의 맛이 배어 있었는지도 모른다. 도러시아의 얼굴 위로 미소가 번지기 시작했고, 그녀는 말했다.\n\n\"전혀요! 어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요?\" 그러나 바로 그때 문이 열리며 리드게이트가 들어왔다.\n\n\"의사 자격으로 다시 돌아왔습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"나간 뒤에 창백한 두 얼굴이 자꾸 눈앞에 어른거리더군요. 캐소본 부인도 당신만큼이나 보살핌이 필요해 보였어, 로지. 그리고 두 분을 함께 두고 나온 것이 제 도리를 다하지 못한 것 같았습니다. 그래서 콜먼네에 다녀온 뒤 다시 집으로 온 거예요. 캐소본 부인, 걸어오신 걸 봤습니다만, 하늘이 변했네요. 비가 올 것 같아요. 사람을 보내 부인을 모셔 갈 마차를 부르라고 일러도 될까요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요! 저는 튼튼해요. 걷는 게 필요하답니다.\" 도러시아가 얼굴에 생기를 띠며 일어섰다. \"리드게이트 부인과 이야기를 많이 나누었으니, 이제 갈 때가 되었네요. 저는 늘 도가 지나치고 말이 너무 많다고 핀잔을 들어 왔거든요.\"\n\n그녀는 로저먼드에게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람은 입맞춤도 다른 어떤 격정의 표시도 없이 진심 어린 조용한 작별을 나누었다. 그것을 겉으로 드러내는 표시를 피상적으로 쓰기에는, 두 사람 사이에 진지한 감정이 너무도 깊이 오갔던 것이다.\n\n리드게이트가 그녀를 문까지 배웅하는 동안 그녀는 로저먼드에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 그의 이야기를 믿음으로 귀 기울여 들어 준 페어브러더 씨와 다른 벗들에 관해 그에게 들려주었다.\n\n그가 로저먼드에게로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 이미 체념한 듯한 피로에 잠겨 소파에 몸을 던지고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 로지.\" 그가 그녀 위로 몸을 숙이고 머리카락을 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"이렇게 많이 겪어 보고 나니 이제 캐소본 부인을 어떻게 생각해?\"\n\n\"그분은 누구보다도 훌륭하신 분이 틀림없어요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"그리고 무척 아름다우시고요. 당신이 그렇게 자주 그분과 이야기하러 다니면, 저한테는 더더욱 불만이 쌓이겠지요!\"\n\n리드게이트는 \"그렇게 자주\"라는 말에 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"그런데 그분 덕분에 나에 대한 당신 불만은 좀 줄어든 거야?\"\n\n\"줄어든 것 같아요.\" 로저먼드가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다. \"당신 눈이 어쩜 그렇게 무거워 보여요, 터셔스. 그리고 머리 좀 뒤로 넘겨요.\" 그는 그녀의 말에 따르려고 크고 흰 손을 들어 올렸고, 자신에 대한 이 작은 관심의 표시에 고마움을 느꼈다. 가엾은 로저먼드의 방황하던 공상은 끔찍하게 채찍질당한 채 돌아와 있었다. 한때 업신여기던 옛 그늘 아래로 파고들 만큼 충분히 온순해진 채로. 그리고 그 그늘은 여전히 거기 있었다. 리드게이트는 좁아진 자신의 운명을 슬픈 체념으로 받아들였던 것이다. 그는 이 연약한 존재를 택했고, 그녀의 삶의 짐을 자기 팔에 떠안았다. 그는 할 수 있는 대로 걸어가야 했다. 그 짐을 가엾이 여기며 짊어진 채로.\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXII.\n\n인간의 역사를 살펴보면 추방당한 자들이 희망을 양식으로 삼는 일은 익히 알려진 사실이며, 그들은 어쩔 수 없는 처지가 아니라면 좀처럼 유배지에 머무르지 않는다. 윌 래디슬로가 스스로를 미들마치에서 추방했을 때, 그가 자신의 귀환에 세워 둔 장애물이라곤 자기 자신의 결심뿐이었으니, 그것은 결코 무쇠 빗장 같은 것이 아니라 단지 다른 마음 상태와 어우러져 미뉴에트를 추듯 녹아들고, 정중한 손쉬움으로 절하고 미소 지으며 자리를 내어 주기 일쑤인 마음의 한 상태에 지나지 않았다. 달이 거듭될수록 그는 미들마치로 잠깐 내려가서는 안 될 이유가 무엇인지를 점점 말하기 어렵게 느꼈다. 그저 도러시아에 관한 소식을 좀 듣기 위해서일 뿐이었으니까. 그리고 그렇게 잠깐 들른 길에 어떤 기이한 우연으로 그녀와 마주치게 된다 하더라도, 미리 가지 않으리라 짐작했던 결백한 여행을 했다는 것을 두고 부끄러워할 이유는 없었다. 이미 그녀와는 절망적으로 갈라선 처지였으니, 그녀가 사는 동네 근처로 가 보는 것쯤은 분명 감행해도 좋을 일이었다. 그리고 용처럼 그녀를 감시하는 의심 많은 친구들로 말하자면, 그들의 견해는 시간이 흐르고 환경이 바뀌면서 점점 덜 중요해 보였다.\n\n그런데 도러시아와는 전혀 무관한 어떤 이유 하나가 생겨났고, 그것은 미들마치로의 여행을 일종의 박애적 의무처럼 보이게 만들었다. 윌은 머나먼 서부에 새로운 방식으로 정착촌을 세우려는 계획에 사심 없는 관심을 기울여 왔는데, 훌륭한 구상을 실행하는 데 필요한 자금 문제가 그로 하여금 자문하게 만들었던 것이다. 자신에게 제안되었던 그 돈을, 널리 이로울 법한 사업을 실현하는 수단으로 쓰도록 권유하는 데 자신이 벌스트로드에게 가진 권리를 활용하는 것이야말로 칭찬받을 만한 용도가 아니겠는가. 그 문제는 윌에게 무척 미심쩍게 여겨졌고, 그 은행가와 다시 어떤 관계를 맺는다는 데 대한 혐오감 탓에 그는 그것을 어서 떨쳐 버렸을 수도 있었다. 미들마치를 한번 방문하면 자신의 판단을 더 안전하게 정할 수 있으리라는 가능성이 그의 상상 속에 떠오르지만 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n그것이 윌이 내려갈 이유랍시고 스스로에게 내세운 명분이었다. 그는 리드게이트에게 속내를 털어놓고 그와 돈 문제를 의논할 작정이었으며, 머무는 며칠 저녁을 아리따운 로저먼드와 음악을 잔뜩 즐기고 농담을 주고받으며 즐겁게 보낼 작정이었다. 물론 로윅 목사관의 친구들을 소홀히 하지 않으면서 말이다. 목사관이 영주관과 가까이 있다 한들 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니었다. 그는 떠나기 전 페어브러더 가족을 소홀히 했었는데, 도러시아와의 만남을 에둘러 꾀한다는 혐의를 받을지 모른다는 데 대한 자존심 어린 저항 때문이었다. 하지만 굶주림은 우리를 길들이는 법이라, 윌은 어떤 모습을 보고 싶고 어떤 목소리를 듣고 싶은 마음에 몹시 굶주려 있었다. 그 무엇도 그것을 대신해 주지 못했다. 오페라도, 열성적인 정치가들과의 담론도, 사설란에서 새로 발휘한 자기 솜씨에 대한 (어둑한 구석에서의) 우쭐한 환대도.\n\n그리하여 그는 내려왔으니, 자신의 익숙하고 자그마한 세계에서 거의 모든 것이 어떠하리라는 것을 자신만만하게 내다보았고, 사실 이번 방문에 아무 놀랄 일도 없으리라는 것을 오히려 두려워하기까지 했다. 그러나 그는 그 단조로운 세계가 끔찍하게 격동하는 상태에 빠져 있음을 발견했으니, 그곳에서는 농담과 서정조차 폭발성을 띠게 되어 있었다. 그리고 이 방문의 첫날은 그의 생애에서 가장 운명적인 시기가 되고 말았다. 이튿날 아침 그는 그 결과들의 악몽에 너무도 시달린 나머지, 또 자기 앞에 닥친 당장의 문제들을 너무도 두려워한 나머지, 아침을 먹다가 리버스턴행 마차가 도착하는 것을 보고는 황급히 나가 그 마차에 자리를 잡았다. 적어도 하루만이라도 미들마치에서 무언가를 하거나 말해야 할 필요에서 벗어나기 위해서였다. 윌 래디슬로는 그런 얽히고설킨 위기들 중 하나에 처해 있었는데, 그런 위기는 사람들의 판단이 지닌 천박한 단정성에서 짐작할 법한 것보다 실제 경험에서는 훨씬 흔한 것이다. 그는 진심으로 존경하던 리드게이트가, 자신의 철저하고 솔직히 표명된 동정을 마땅히 받아야 할 처지에 놓인 것을 보았다. 그런데 그러한 동정의 당위에도 불구하고 윌이 리드게이트와 더 이상의 친밀한 교류를, 아니 접촉마저도 피하는 편이 더 나았을 그 이유야말로, 그런 방도를 도무지 불가능해 보이게 만드는 종류의 것이었다. 윌처럼 민감한 기질을 지닌 존재에게는, 자기 본성 안에 무관심이라는 중립지대가 전혀 없어서 자신에게 닥치는 모든 일을 격정적인 드라마의 충돌로 바꾸어 버릴 태세가 되어 있는 존재에게는, 로저먼드가 자신의 행복을 어떤 식으로든 그에게 의존하게 만들었다는 깨달음은 하나의 곤경이었으며, 그녀를 향해 터뜨린 분노가 그 곤경을 그에게 한없이 키워 놓았던 것이다. 그는 자신의 잔인함을 미워했으나, 한편으로는 누그러진 마음을 온전히 드러내기를 두려워했다. 그는 그녀에게 다시 가야 했다. 그 우정을 느닷없이 끝낼 수는 없었으니까. 그리고 그녀의 불행은 그가 두려워하는 하나의 힘이었다. 그러는 내내 그의 앞에 놓인 삶에는 즐거움의 예감이라곤 조금도 없었으니, 마치 사지가 잘려 나간 채 목발을 짚고 새로 첫발을 내딛는 것이나 다름없었다. 밤중에 그는 리버스턴이 아니라 런던으로 가는 마차에 올라타, 리드게이트에게 자신의 퇴각에 대한 임시변통의 이유를 적은 쪽지를 남겨 두고 떠나 버릴까를 고심했다. 그러나 그 갑작스러운 떠남에서 그를 끌어당겨 막는 질긴 밧줄들이 있었다. 도러시아를 생각할 때 그의 행복에 드리워진 병충해, 단념해야 한다는 것을 인정했음에도 그래도 남아 있던 그 으뜸가는 희망이 짓밟히는 일은, 너무도 생생한 비참함이어서 그로서는 그것을 체념하고 곧장 떠나 절망이기도 한 먼 곳으로 가 버릴 수가 없었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 리버스턴행 마차를 타는 것 이상으로 결단력 있는 일은 아무것도 하지 못했다. 그는 그날 저녁 리드게이트의 집에 가야 한다고 마음을 정한 채, 아직 환할 때 그 마차로 다시 돌아왔다. 우리가 알다시피 루비콘 강은 보기에 아주 보잘것없는 시내였다. 그 의미는 전적으로 어떤 보이지 않는 조건들에 있었다. 윌은 자신의 그 자그마한 경계의 도랑을 억지로 건너야 하는 것처럼 느꼈고, 그 너머에 보이는 것은 제국이 아니라 불만에 찬 예속이었다.\n\n그러나 우리에게는 때때로 일상생활 속에서조차 고귀한 본성이 발휘하는 구원의 영향력을, 즉 자기를 억누르는 동료애의 행위 속에 깃들 수 있는 신성한 구제의 효력을 목격할 기회가 주어진다. 만약 도러시아가 그 밤의 고뇌를 겪은 뒤에 로저먼드에게로 그 발걸음을 옮기지 않았더라면, 그야 아마 그녀는 신중함으로 더 높은 평판을 얻은 여인이 되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그날 저녁 일곱 시 반에 리드게이트 집의 한 난롯가에 모여 있던 그 세 사람에게는 분명 그렇게 좋은 일이 되지 못했으리라.\n\n로저먼드는 윌의 방문에 대비하고 있었고, 나른한 냉담함으로 그를 맞이했는데, 리드게이트는 그것을 그녀의 신경 쇠약 탓으로 돌렸을 뿐, 그것이 윌과 어떤 관련이 있으리라고는 상상도 할 수 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 바느질감을 들여다보며 말없이 앉아 있자, 그는 순진하게도 그녀에게 등을 기대고 쉬라고 권하면서 에둘러 그녀를 변호했다. 윌은 처음으로 모습을 드러내며 로저먼드에게 인사를 건네는 친구의 역할을 연기해야 하는 처지가 비참했으니, 그러는 동안 그의 생각은 어제의 그 장면 이후 그녀의 심정을 두고 분주히 맴돌았던 것이다. 그 장면은 마치 이중의 광기가 빚어낸 고통스러운 환영처럼 여전히 가차 없이 두 사람을 에워싸고 있는 듯했다. 마침 리드게이트를 방 밖으로 불러내는 일은 아무것도 없었다. 그러나 로저먼드가 차를 따르고 윌이 그것을 가지러 가까이 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 그의 찻잔 받침에 자그맣게 접은 종이쪽지 하나를 놓았다. 그는 그것을 보고 재빨리 챙겼으나, 여관으로 돌아가는 길에도 그 쪽지를 펼쳐 보고 싶은 열의는 없었다. 로저먼드가 그에게 써 보낸 것은 십중팔구 그날 저녁의 고통스러운 인상을 더 깊게 만들 것이었다. 그래도 그는 침대맡 촛불 곁에서 그것을 펼쳐 읽었다. 단정하게 흘려 쓴 그녀의 글씨로 이런 몇 마디만 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"커소번 부인께 말씀드렸어요. 그분은 당신에 대해 아무 오해도 하고 계시지 않아요. 저를 보러 오셔서 무척 다정하게 대해 주셨기에 말씀드렸습니다. 이제 저를 두고 책망하실 일은 없으실 거예요. 저는 당신에게 아무런 차이도 만들지 않은 셈이 될 테니까요.\"\n\n이 말이 자아낸 효과가 온통 기쁨만은 아니었다. 윌이 흥분한 상상으로 그 말을 곱씹는 동안, 도러시아와 로저먼드 사이에 오간 일을 생각하니 그의 두 뺨과 귀가 화끈 달아올랐다. 자신의 행동에 대한 해명을 들은 일로 도러시아가 여전히 얼마만큼이나 자존심에 상처를 입었다고 느낄지 알 수 없었던 것이다. 그녀의 마음속에는 여전히 그에 대한 달라진 인상이 남아 있어, 돌이킬 수 없는 차이를, 영구한 흠집을 만들었을지도 몰랐다. 활발한 공상으로 그는 스스로를, 밤중에 난파에서 빠져나와 어둠 속 알 수 없는 땅 위에 서 있는 사람의 처지보다 별로 나을 것 없는 의심의 상태로 몰아넣었다. 그 비참한 어제까지는, 오래전 바로 그 같은 방에서 바로 그 같은 사람을 앞에 두고 느꼈던 짜증의 순간만 빼면, 두 사람의 모든 환상, 서로에 대한 모든 생각은 마치 동떨어진 세계 속에 있는 것 같았다. 거기서는 햇살이 키 큰 흰 백합 위로 쏟아졌고, 어떤 악도 도사리지 않았으며, 다른 어떤 영혼도 들어오지 않았다. 그러나 이제, 도러시아가 그 세계에서 그를 다시 만나 줄까?\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXIII.\n\n이제 우리의 깨어 있는 영혼들에게 좋은 아침을 빌어 주자\n서로를 두려움으로 살피지 않는 영혼들에게\n\n사랑은 다른 모든 풍경의 사랑을 다스려\n\n작은 방 하나를 어디에나 있는 곳으로 만드나니.\n—던 박사*\n\n* 존 던: 17세기 영국의 형이상학파 시인.\n\n도러시아가 로저먼드를 방문한 지 이틀째 되는 날 아침, 그녀는 이틀 밤을 푹 자고 나서 피로의 흔적을 모조리 떨쳐 냈을 뿐 아니라 넘쳐 나는 기운을 주체하지 못할 것 같은 느낌이 들었다. 다시 말해 어떤 일에 집중할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 기운이 남아돈다는 뜻이었다. 그 전날 그녀는 저택의 부지 밖으로 오랫동안 산책을 나갔고 목사관에도 두 번 들렀다. 하지만 자신이 왜 그토록 보람 없이 시간을 보냈는지 그 이유를 평생 누구에게도 말하지 않았으며, 이날 아침 그녀는 자신의 유치한 안절부절못함에 다소 화가 났다. 오늘은 아주 다르게 보낼 작정이었다. 마을에서 할 일이 무엇이 있을까? 아아! 아무것도 없었다. 모두 건강했고 플란넬 옷도 충분했으며, 돼지가 죽은 집도 없었다. 게다가 토요일 아침이라 집집마다 문과 댓돌을 닦아 대는 날이었으니, 학교에 가 봐야 소용없었다. 그러나 도러시아가 분명히 이해해 보려고 애쓰는 여러 가지 주제가 있었기에, 그녀는 그중에서도 가장 진지한 문제에 정력적으로 몰두해 보기로 결심했다. 그녀는 서재에서 정치 경제학과 그와 관련된 문제들을 다룬 자신만의 작은 책 더미 앞에 앉았는데, 그 책들에서 이웃에게 해를 끼치지 않으면서 돈을 쓰는 최선의 방법, 다시 말해 같은 말이지만 이웃에게 가장 큰 도움을 주는 방법에 관해 어떤 깨우침을 얻으려는 것이었다. 여기에 그녀가 붙잡기만 한다면 분명 마음을 한결같이 지켜 줄 묵직한 주제가 있었다. 불행히도 그녀의 마음은 꼬박 한 시간 동안 그 주제에서 미끄러져 나갔고, 결국 그녀는 여러 가지 일을 강렬하게 의식하면서도 정작 본문에 담긴 어느 한 가지도 의식하지 못한 채 문장을 두 번씩 거듭 읽고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 이건 가망이 없었다. 마차를 불러 팁턴으로 갈까? 아니다. 무슨 까닭에선지 그녀는 로윅에 머무는 편이 더 좋았다. 하지만 이 방황하는 마음은 질서를 되찾아야만 했다. 자기 수양에도 기술이 있는 법이었다. 그래서 그녀는 갈색 서재를 빙글빙글 돌면서 어떤 식의 책략을 써야 떠도는 생각을 붙잡을 수 있을지 궁리했다. 어쩌면 그저 어떤 과제 하나가 최선의 방편일지도 몰랐다. 끈질기게 매달려야 하는 무엇 말이다. 소아시아의 지리가 있지 않은가, 캐소본 씨가 그녀의 게으름을 종종 나무라던 그 분야가? 그녀는 지도가 든 진열장으로 가서 한 장을 펼쳤다. 오늘 아침에는 파플라고니아가 레반트 해안에 있지 않다는 것을 마침내 확실히 해 두고, 칼리베스족에 관한 자신의 완전한 무지를 흑해 연안에 단단히 고정시켜 둘 수 있으리라. 지도란 다른 무언가를 생각하고 싶을 때 공부하기에 좋은 것이었으니, 거슬러 되짚어 보면 하나의 종소리처럼 어우러지는 이름들로 이루어져 있기 때문이었다. 도러시아는 지도에 바싹 몸을 숙이고 진지하게 작업에 착수해, 들릴락 말락 한 낮은 목소리로 이름들을 외워 나갔는데, 그 소리는 종종 하나의 가락처럼 이어졌다. 그토록 깊은 경험을 다 겪고 나서도 그녀는 우습게도 소녀 같아 보였다. 고개를 끄덕이고 손가락으로 이름을 하나씩 짚어 가며, 입술을 살짝 오므렸다가, 이따금 손을 멈추고 두 손을 양 볼에 대고는 \"아아! 아아!\" 하고 탄식하곤 했다.\n\n이것이 회전목마보다 더 빨리 끝날 까닭은 없었다. 그러나 마침내 문이 열리고 노블 양이 왔다는 전갈이 들리면서 중단되었다.\n보닛을 쓴 키가 도러시아의 어깨에도 채 닿지 않는 그 자그마한 노부인은 따뜻한 환대를 받았으나, 손을 맞잡은 채로 마치 무언가 말하기 어려운 것이 있는 듯 비버 같은 소리를 잔뜩 냈다.\n\n\"앉으세요.\" 도러시아가 의자를 앞으로 밀며 말했다. \"제게 무슨 볼일이라도 있으신가요? 제가 도울 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 기쁘게 하겠어요.\"\n\"오래 있지는 않을 거예요.\" 노블 양이 작은 바구니에 손을 넣어 그 안의 무언가를 초조하게 만지작거리며 말했다. \"교회 묘지에 친구 한 사람을 두고 왔거든요.\" 그녀는 다시 알아들을 수 없는 소리로 빠져들더니, 무심결에 만지작거리던 그 물건을 꺼냈다. 그것은 거북 등딱지로 만든 마름모꼴 사탕 갑이었고, 도러시아는 얼굴로 붉은빛이 번져 오르는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"래디슬로 씨예요.\" 소심한 자그마한 부인이 말을 이었다. \"당신의 기분을 상하게 한 것은 아닐까 걱정하면서, 잠깐만이라도 만나 주실 수 있는지 여쭤 봐 달라고 제게 간청했답니다.\"\n\n도러시아는 곧바로 대답하지 못했다. 죽은 남편의 금령이 깃들어 있는 듯한 이 서재에서는 그를 맞이할 수 없다는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤기 때문이다. 그녀는 창 쪽을 바라보았다. 밖으로 나가 정원에서 그를 만날 수 있을까? 하늘은 무겁게 내려앉았고, 나무들은 다가오는 폭풍을 예감한 듯 떨기 시작했다. 게다가 그녀는 그에게로 나가는 것이 꺼려졌다.\n\"부디 만나 주세요, 캐소본 부인.\" 노블 양이 애처롭게 말했다. \"그러지 않으면 저는 돌아가서 안 된다고 말해야 하고, 그러면 그 사람이 상심할 거예요.\"\n\n\"네, 만나겠어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"부디 오시라고 전해 주세요.\"\n달리 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가? 그 순간 그녀가 간절히 바라는 것은 윌을 만나는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 그를 만날 수 있다는 가능성이 그녀와 다른 모든 대상 사이에 집요하게 끼어들었다. 그러면서도 그녀에게는 경보처럼 두근거리는 흥분이 일었다. 그를 위해 무언가 대담하게 도전하는 일을 하고 있다는 느낌이었다.\n\n자그마한 부인이 자신의 임무를 띠고 종종걸음으로 나가자, 도러시아는 서재 한가운데 서서 두 손을 앞으로 모은 채 늘어뜨리고는, 위엄 있는 무심함의 자세로 마음을 가다듬으려는 시도조차 하지 않았다. 그때 그녀가 가장 의식하지 못한 것은 바로 자기 자신의 몸이었다. 그녀는 윌의 마음속에 무엇이 있을지, 그리고 다른 사람들이 그에 대해 품었던 모진 감정들을 생각하고 있었다. 어떤 의무가 그녀를 매정함에 옭아맬 수 있겠는가? 부당한 비난에 맞서려는 마음은 처음부터 그를 향한 감정과 뒤섞여 있었고, 이제 고뇌를 겪고 난 마음의 반동 속에서 그 저항은 어느 때보다 강해졌다. \"내가 그를 너무 사랑한다면, 그건 그가 그토록 부당한 대우를 받아 왔기 때문이야.\" 서재 안의 어떤 상상 속 청중을 향해 그녀의 내면에서 이렇게 말하는 목소리가 있었는데, 그때 문이 열리고 그녀는 눈앞에 윌을 보았다.\n그녀는 움직이지 않았고, 그는 일찍이 본 적 없는 의심과 소심함이 어린 얼굴로 그녀에게 다가왔다. 그는 자신의 어떤 눈빛이나 말 한마디가 그녀로부터 새로이 멀어지는 형벌을 받게 하지나 않을까 두려워하는 불확실한 상태에 있었다. 그리고 도러시아는 자신의 감정이 두려웠다. 그녀는 마치 주문에라도 걸린 듯 미동도 없이, 모아 쥔 두 손을 풀지 못한 채, 강렬하고 진지한 어떤 갈망을 눈 속에 가둬 두고 있는 것 같았다. 그녀가 여느 때처럼 손을 내밀지 않는 것을 보고, 윌은 그녀에게서 한 발짝쯤 떨어진 곳에 멈춰 서서 당혹스럽게 말했다. \"만나 주셔서 정말 감사합니다.\"\n\n\"저도 당신을 만나고 싶었어요.\" 달리 할 말이 떠오르지 않은 도러시아가 말했다. 그녀는 앉을 생각이 미처 들지 않았고, 윌은 자신을 맞이하는 이 여왕 같은 태도를 밝게 해석하지 않았다. 그래도 그는 마음먹고 온 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"이렇게 빨리 돌아온 제가 어리석고, 어쩌면 잘못했다고 여기실까 봐 두렵습니다. 제 조급함에 대한 벌을 받았지요. 아시겠지만, 이제는 누구나 알고 있어요. 제 출생에 관한 고통스러운 이야기를요. 저는 떠나기 전에 그 일을 알고 있었고, 만약 우리가 다시 만나게 된다면 그 이야기를 꼭 당신께 하려고 늘 마음먹고 있었습니다.\"\n\n도러시아에게서 가벼운 움직임이 일더니, 그녀는 모았던 두 손을 풀었다가 곧바로 다시 포개어 쥐었다.\n\n\"하지만 이제 그 일은 사람들의 입에 오르내리는 가십거리가 되었지요.\" 윌이 말을 이었다. \"당신께 알리고 싶었던 것은, 그 일과 연관된 어떤 것, 제가 떠나기 전에 일어난 어떤 일이 저를 이곳으로 다시 끌어 내려오게 하는 데 한몫했다는 점입니다. 적어도 그것이 제가 온 것을 변명해 준다고 생각했어요. 벌스트로드에게 어떤 돈을 공적인 목적에 쓰도록 하려는 생각이었지요. 그가 제게 주려고 마음먹었던 어떤 돈을요. 어쩌면 그가 옛 잘못에 대해 제게 은밀히 보상을 제안한 것은 다소 그의 명예가 될지도 모르겠습니다. 그는 그 잘못을 갚으려고 제게 넉넉한 수입을 주겠다고 제안했어요. 하지만 그 불쾌한 이야기는 당신도 아시겠지요?\"\n\n윌은 의심스러운 눈으로 도러시아를 바라보았지만, 그의 태도에는 자신의 운명에 얽힌 이 사실을 떠올릴 때면 늘 따라붙던 반항적인 용기가 다소 깃들고 있었다. 그는 덧붙였다. \"제게는 그것이 전적으로 고통스러운 일이라는 걸 아시겠지요.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 알아요.\" 도러시아가 황급히 말했다.\n\n\"저는 그런 출처에서 나온 수입을 받아들이기로 선택하지 않았습니다. 제가 그렇게 한다면 당신이 저를 좋게 여기지 않으리라고 확신했거든요.\" 윌이 말했다. 이제 그녀에게 그런 종류의 이야기를 하는 것을 그가 왜 꺼리겠는가? 그녀는 그가 자신에 대한 사랑을 고백했다는 것을 알고 있었다. \"저는 그렇게 느꼈습니다.\" 그러나 그는 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"제가 예상한 대로 행동하셨군요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. 그녀의 얼굴이 환해지며, 아름다운 줄기 같은 목 위에서 머리가 조금 더 꼿꼿이 들렸다.\n\n\"제 출생의 어떤 정황이 다른 사람들에게는 분명히 저에 대한 편견을 심어 줄지언정, 당신만은 그것으로 인해 저에 대한 편견을 품지 않으리라 믿었습니다.\" 윌이 예전 버릇대로 고개를 뒤로 젖히며, 진지한 호소를 담아 그녀의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그것이 새로운 시련이라면, 그것은 오히려 제가 당신께 매달릴 새로운 이유가 될 거예요.\" 도러시아가 열렬히 말했다. \"저를 변하게 할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었어요. 다만……\" 그녀의 가슴이 벅차올라 말을 잇기가 어려웠다. 그녀는 안간힘을 써서 떨리는 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"다만 당신이 제가 믿었던 모습과 다르다고, 제가 믿었던 만큼 훌륭하지 않다고 생각하는 것 말고는요.\"\n\n\"당신은 한 가지만 빼면 모든 면에서 저를 실제 모습보다 더 낫게 믿어 주실 게 분명합니다.\" 윌이 그녀의 감정이 드러나는 모습에 자신의 감정도 내맡기며 말했다. \"그 한 가지란, 당신을 향한 제 진심을 두고 하는 말입니다. 당신이 그것을 의심한다고 생각했을 때, 저는 남아 있는 그 무엇에도 마음이 가지 않았어요. 제게는 모든 것이 끝났고, 이루려고 애쓸 것은 아무것도 없으며, 그저 견뎌야 할 일들만 남았다고 생각했지요.\"\n\n\"이제는 당신을 더 이상 의심하지 않아요.\" 도러시아가 손을 내밀며 말했다. 그를 향한 막연한 두려움이 형언할 수 없는 애정을 부추기고 있었다.\n\n그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 흐느낌 같은 무언가와 함께 입술로 가져갔다. 그러나 그는 다른 손에 모자와 장갑을 든 채 서 있었으니, 어느 왕당파의 초상화 속 인물로 보일 만했다. 그래도 그 손을 놓기가 어려웠고, 도러시아는 자신을 괴롭게 하는 혼란 속에서 손을 빼며, 시선을 돌리고 자리를 옮겼다.\n\n\"보세요, 구름이 얼마나 어두워졌는지, 나무들이 얼마나 휘청대는지.\" 그녀가 창 쪽으로 걸어가며 말했다. 그러나 말하고 움직이면서도 자신이 무엇을 하고 있는지는 어렴풋이만 의식할 뿐이었다.\n\n윌은 조금 떨어져 그녀를 뒤따라가, 가죽 의자의 높은 등받이에 기댔는데, 이제는 그 위에 모자와 장갑을 올려놓고서, 도러시아 앞에서 난생처음 떠안았던 그 견딜 수 없는 격식의 속박에서 자신을 풀어놓을 만큼의 여유를 부려 보았다. 고백하건대 그는 그 순간 의자에 기댄 채 무척 행복했다. 그는 이제 그녀가 무엇을 느끼든 별로 두렵지 않았다.\n\n두 사람은 말없이 서서 서로를 바라보지 않고, 휘청대는 상록수들을, 검게 물들어 가는 하늘을 배경으로 잎의 창백한 뒷면을 드러내는 그 나무들을 바라보았다. 윌은 폭풍이 다가오는 풍경을 그토록 즐겁게 여긴 적이 없었다. 그것이 그를 떠나야 하는 처지에서 풀어 주었기 때문이다. 나뭇잎과 잔가지들이 사방으로 내던져졌고, 천둥소리가 점점 가까워졌다. 빛은 점점 더 음울해졌으나, 번쩍하고 번개가 치자 두 사람은 흠칫 놀라 서로를 바라보았고, 그러고는 미소 지었다. 도러시아가 생각하고 있던 것을 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"이루려고 애쓸 것이 아무것도 없었다고 하신 건 잘못된 말이에요. 우리가 우리 자신의 가장 큰 행복을 잃는다 해도, 다른 사람들의 행복은 남아 있을 테고, 그것은 애써 볼 만한 가치가 있는 일이에요. 어떤 이들은 행복할 수 있으니까요. 저는 가장 비참했을 때 오히려 그것을 그 어느 때보다 또렷이 알아본 것 같았어요. 그런 느낌이 제게 와서 힘을 주지 않았다면, 제가 그 고난을 어떻게 견뎌 냈을지 짐작도 못 하겠어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 제가 느낀 그런 비참함을 결코 느껴 보지 못했을 겁니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"당신이 저를 경멸할 수밖에 없다는 것을 아는 비참함을요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 더한 것을 느꼈어요. 나쁘게 생각하는 게 더 괴로웠어요…….\" 도러시아가 격하게 말을 꺼냈다가 멈추었다.\n\n윌은 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그녀가 무슨 말을 하든 그것이 두 사람을 갈라놓는 어떤 숙명을 내다보며 내뱉는 말이라는 느낌이 들었다. 그는 잠시 침묵하다가, 격정적으로 말했다.\n\n\"적어도 우리는 서로에게 거짓 없이 이야기하는 위안만은 누릴 수 있겠지요. 저는 떠나야만 하니까요. 우리는 언제까지나 갈라져 있어야 하니까요. 그러니 당신은 저를 무덤가에 선 사람으로 여기셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n그가 말하는 동안 선명한 번개가 번쩍이며 두 사람을 서로에게 환히 비추었는데, 그 빛은 마치 가망 없는 사랑의 공포처럼 보였다. 도러시아가 순식간에 창가에서 물러섰고, 윌이 그녀를 따라가 발작적인 동작으로 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다. 그렇게 두 사람은 손을 맞잡고 두 아이처럼 서서 폭풍을 내다보았으며, 그동안 머리 위에서 천둥이 엄청난 굉음과 함께 우르릉 울렸고 비가 쏟아지기 시작했다. 그러더니 그들은 그의 마지막 말을 마음에 새긴 채 서로를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸고, 맞잡은 손을 놓지 않았다.\n\n\"저에게는 희망이 없습니다.\" 윌이 말했다. \"설령 당신이 제가 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 저를 사랑한다 해도, 설령 제가 당신에게 전부라 해도, 저는 십중팔구 언제까지나 무척 가난할 거예요. 냉정하게 따져 보면 비루하게 기어가는 운명 말고는 기대할 게 아무것도 없습니다. 우리가 서로의 사람이 된다는 건 결코 불가능한 일이에요. 당신에게서 한마디를 청한 것이 어쩌면 비열한 짓이었는지도 모르지요. 저는 침묵 속으로 떠나려 했는데, 마음먹은 대로 하지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"미안해하지 마세요.\" 도러시아가 맑고 다정한 목소리로 말했다. \"차라리 저는 우리 이별의 모든 괴로움을 함께 나누고 싶어요.\"\n\n그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 그의 입술도 떨렸다. 어느 쪽 입술이 먼저 다른 입술을 향해 움직였는지는 끝내 알 수 없었으나, 두 사람은 떨면서 입을 맞추었고, 그러고는 떨어졌다.\n\n빗줄기가 마치 그 안에 성난 영혼이라도 깃든 듯 창유리를 후려쳤고, 그 뒤로는 바람의 거센 휘몰아침이 있었다. 분주한 사람도 한가로운 사람도 한결같이 어떤 경외감에 사로잡혀 멈춰 서게 되는 그런 순간 가운데 하나였다.\n\n도러시아는 가장 가까운 자리, 방 한가운데 놓인 길고 낮은 오토만에 앉아, 두 손을 무릎 위에 포개어 놓고 황량한 바깥 세계를 바라보았다. 윌은 잠시 가만히 선 채 그녀를 바라보다가, 그녀 곁에 자리를 잡고 앉아 그녀의 손 위에 자신의 손을 얹었는데, 그 손은 맞잡히려는 듯 위로 돌아 펴졌다. 두 사람은 그렇게 서로를 바라보지 않은 채, 비가 잦아들어 고요히 내리기 시작할 때까지 앉아 있었다. 둘 다 어느 쪽도 차마 입 밖에 낼 수 없는 생각들로 가득 차 있었다.\n\n그러나 빗소리가 잠잠해지자 도러시아가 윌을 보려고 돌아앉았다. 마치 무슨 고문 나사가 그를 위협하기라도 하는 듯, 그는 격정에 찬 탄성을 지르며 벌떡 일어나 말했다. \"불가능해요!\"\n\n그는 다시 의자 등받이로 가서 기대고는 자신의 분노와 씨름하는 것 같았고, 그동안 그녀는 슬픈 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이건 살인이나, 사람들을 갈라놓는 그 어떤 끔찍한 일만큼이나 치명적이에요.\" 그가 다시 터뜨렸다. \"아니, 더 견딜 수 없는 일이지요. 우리의 삶이 하찮은 우연들 때문에 불구가 되어 버린다는 게.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그런 말 하지 마세요. 당신의 삶이 불구가 될 필요는 없어요.\" 도러시아가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 그럴 수밖에 없어요.\" 윌이 화가 나서 말했다. \"그런 식으로 말하다니, 마치 무슨 위안거리라도 있는 것처럼 말하다니 당신은 잔인하군요. 당신은 그 비참함 너머를 볼 수 있을지 몰라도, 저는 못 봅니다. 그건 매정한 일이에요. 이런 엄연한 사실 앞에서 그렇게 말하는 것은, 당신을 향한 제 사랑을 마치 하찮은 것인 양 도로 내던져 버리는 거예요. 우리는 결코 결혼할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"언젠가는, 우리도 할 수 있을지 몰라요.\" 도러시아가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"언제요?\" 윌이 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"제가 무슨 성공이라도 거두리라 기대하는 게 무슨 소용입니까? 제가 한낱 펜이며 대변자로 저 자신을 팔아넘기기를 택하지 않는 한, 제가 그저 그럭저럭 제 한 몸 건사하는 것 이상을 해낼 수 있을지는 순전히 운에 맡길 일이에요. 그건 제 눈에도 충분히 똑똑히 보입니다. 저는 어떤 여자에게도, 설령 그 여자가 포기할 사치라곤 하나도 없다 해도, 제 자신을 내밀 수 없어요.\"\n\n침묵이 흘렀다. 도러시아의 가슴은 말하고 싶은 무언가로 가득했지만, 그 말은 너무도 꺼내기 어려웠다. 그녀는 온통 그 말에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그 순간 그녀의 내면에서는 어떤 갈등도 입을 다물고 있었다. 그리고 하고 싶은 말을 할 수 없다는 것이 무척이나 괴로웠다. 윌은 화난 얼굴로 창밖을 내다보고 있었다. 그가 그녀를 바라보며 곁을 떠나지만 않는다면 모든 것이 더 수월하리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 마침내 그는 여전히 의자에 몸을 기댄 채 돌아서서, 무의식적으로 모자를 향해 손을 뻗으며 일종의 격앙된 어조로 말했다. \"그럼, 안녕히.\"\n\n\"오, 저는 견딜 수 없어요. 제 심장이 터질 거예요.\" 도러시아가 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. 그녀의 젊은 열정의 물결이 그녀를 침묵하게 했던 온갖 장애물을 한꺼번에 무너뜨렸고, 굵은 눈물방울이 순식간에 차올라 흘러내렸다. \"저는 가난 따위 상관없어요. 제 재산이 미워요.\"\n\n순식간에 윌이 그녀에게 바싹 다가가 두 팔로 그녀를 감쌌으나, 그녀는 말을 계속하려고 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 그의 머리를 부드럽게 밀어냈다. 눈물이 그렁그렁 고인 커다란 눈으로 너무도 천진하게 그를 바라보며, 흐느끼는 어린아이처럼 말했다. \"우리는 제 재산만으로도 아주 잘 살 수 있어요. 너무 많거든요. 연 칠백 파운드나 돼요. 저는 정말 적게 들어요. 새 옷도 필요 없고요. 그리고 모든 것이 얼마나 드는지 배울게요.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXIV.\n\n상원이 개혁 법안을 막 부결시킨 직후의 일이었다. 그것이 바로 캐드월러더 씨가 프레시트 홀의 큰 온실 근처 잔디밭 비탈을 거닐며 등 뒤로 「타임스」를 든 채, 송어 낚시꾼다운 초연함으로 제임스 체텀 경에게 나라의 앞날을 이야기하게 된 까닭이었다. 캐드월러더 부인과 노부인 체텀 부인, 그리고 실리아는 때로 정원 의자에 앉아 있기도 하고, 때로는 어린 아서를 맞으러 걸어가기도 했다. 아서는 마차에 실려 끌려오고 있었는데, 어린 부처에게 어울리게 아름다운 비단 술이 달린 신성한 양산 아래 그늘져 있었다.\n\n부인들도 정치 이야기를 했지만, 좀 더 띄엄띄엄했다. 캐드월러더 부인은 작위 수여 계획에 관해 단호했다. 그녀는 사촌에게서 확실히 들은 바, 트루베리가 그의 아내가 부추긴 탓에 완전히 반대편으로 넘어갔다는 것이었다. 그 아내는 개혁 문제가 처음 제기된 바로 그 순간부터 작위의 냄새를 맡았고, 준남작과 결혼한 동생보다 윗자리를 차지할 수만 있다면 영혼이라도 팔아넘길 여자였다. 체텀 부인은 그런 행동이 참으로 비난받아 마땅하다고 여겼으며, 트루베리 부인의 어머니가 멜스프링의 월싱엄 양이었던 것을 기억해 냈다. 실리아는 \"부인\"보다 \"귀부인\"이 더 좋다는 점은 인정하면서도, 도도는 제 뜻대로 할 수만 있다면 윗자리 따위는 전혀 신경 쓰지 않는다고 했다. 캐드월러더 부인은 주위 모든 사람이 당신의 핏줄에 좋은 피라곤 한 방울도 없음을 아는데 윗자리를 차지한들 그건 보잘것없는 만족이라고 했다. 그러자 실리아는 다시 아서를 보려고 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"그래도 이 아이가 자작이라면 참 좋을 텐데요. 자작님의 작은 이가 막 나오는데 말이에요! 제임스가 백작이었다면 이 아이도 자작이 되었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"실리아야,\" 노부인이 말했다. \"제임스의 작위는 어떤 새 백작 작위보다도 훨씬 값진 거란다. 나는 제임스의 아버지가 제임스 경 외에 다른 무엇이기를 바란 적이 한 번도 없어.\"\n\n\"아, 저는 그저 아서의 작은 이를 두고 한 말이에요.\" 실리아가 편안하게 말했다. \"그런데 보세요, 저기 외삼촌이 오시네요.\"\n\n실리아는 종종걸음으로 외삼촌을 맞으러 갔고, 제임스 경과 캐드월러더 씨는 부인들과 한데 모이려고 앞으로 나아갔다. 실리아는 외삼촌의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼웠고, 그는 다소 우울하게 \"그래, 얘야!\" 하면서 그녀의 손을 토닥였다. 그들이 다가오자 브룩 씨가 풀이 죽어 보인다는 게 분명했지만, 이는 정치 상황으로 충분히 설명될 만한 일이었다. 그가 \"음, 다들 여기 모였군그래\" 하는 인사 외엔 별다른 말도 없이 두루 악수를 나누자, 교구 목사가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"법안 부결을 그렇게 마음에 담아 두지 마시오, 브룩. 당신 편엔 이 나라의 온갖 잡것들이 다 붙어 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"법안 말이오, 응? 아!\" 브룩 씨가 온화하면서도 멍한 태도로 말했다. \"부결됐지, 응? 하지만 상원이 너무 지나치게 나가고 있어요. 결국 고삐를 죄어야 할 거요. 슬픈 소식이오. 그러니까, 여기 집안에서 말이오. 슬픈 소식이오. 하지만 나를 탓해선 안 되네, 체텀.\"\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\" 제임스 경이 말했다. \"또 사냥터지기가 총에 맞은 건 아니겠죠? 트래핑 배스 같은 놈을 그렇게 쉽게 풀어 주니, 그럴 만도 하지요.\"\n\n\"사냥터지기? 아니야. 안으로 들어가세. 집 안에서 다 말해 줄 테니, 알겠나.\" 브룩 씨가 캐드월러더 부부를 향해 고개를 끄덕여, 그들도 자신의 비밀에 끼워 준다는 뜻을 내비치며 말했다. \"트래핑 배스 같은 밀렵꾼들 말인데, 체텀,\" 그가 안으로 들어서며 말을 이었다. \"자네도 치안 판사가 되어 보면 사람을 구금하기가 그리 쉽지 않다는 걸 알게 될 거야. 엄격함도 좋지만, 그걸 대신 해 줄 누군가가 있을 때 훨씬 더 쉬운 법이지. 자네도 마음 한구석은 무른 데가 있잖나. 자네는 드라코나 제프리스, 그런 부류는 아니니까 말이야.\"\n\n브룩 씨는 분명 신경이 곤두선 상태였다. 무언가 괴로운 이야기를 전해야 할 때면, 그는 흔히 그것을 여러 두서없는 자질구레한 이야기들 사이에 끼워 넣어 꺼내는 버릇이 있었으니, 마치 다른 것과 섞으면 맛이 좀 순해지는 약이라도 되는 듯했다. 그는 모두가 자리에 앉을 때까지 제임스 경과 밀렵꾼 이야기를 계속했고, 마침내 이런 군소리를 못 견딘 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"슬픈 소식이 뭔지 궁금해 죽겠네요. 사냥터지기가 총에 맞은 건 아니다, 그건 정해졌고요. 그럼 대체 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"음, 참으로 괴로운 일이오.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"당신과 목사님이 여기 계셔서 다행이오. 집안일이지만, 당신들이 우리 모두가 이걸 견디도록 도와줄 테니까, 캐드월러더. 너에게 알려 줘야겠구나, 얘야.\" 여기서 브룩 씨는 실리아를 바라보았다. \"무슨 일인지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야. 그리고 체텀, 자네는 무척 언짢을 걸세. 하지만 알다시피 자네도 나처럼 이걸 막을 수가 없었어. 세상일엔 묘한 데가 있어. 결국 돌고 도는 법이거든.\"\n\n\"도도 언니 일이 분명해요.\" 실리아가 말했다. 그녀는 늘 언니를 집안이라는 기계 장치 가운데 위험한 부품으로 여기는 데 익숙했다. 그녀는 남편의 무릎에 기대어 낮은 발판에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"제발 무슨 일인지 들어 봅시다!\" 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n\"음, 알다시피 체텀, 나는 캐소본의 유언장을 어쩔 수가 없었네. 일을 더 나쁘게 만드는 그런 유언장이었지.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다.\" 제임스 경이 다급하게 말했다. \"그런데 무엇이 더 나빠졌단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"도러시아가 다시 결혼한다네, 알겠나.\" 브룩 씨가 실리아를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말하자, 실리아는 즉시 겁먹은 눈빛으로 남편을 올려다보며 그의 무릎에 손을 얹었다. 제임스 경은 분노로 얼굴이 거의 하얗게 질렸지만, 입을 열지는 않았다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. \"설마 그 젊은 래디슬로한테는 아니겠지요?\"\n\n브룩 씨는 고개를 끄덕이며 \"그래요, 래디슬로한테요\"라고 말하고는, 신중을 기해 침묵에 빠졌다.\n\n\"보세요, 험프리!\" 캐드월러더 부인이 남편을 향해 팔을 휘저으며 말했다. \"다음번엔 내게도 어느 정도 선견지명이 있다는 걸 인정하시겠죠. 아니면 또 반박하면서 여느 때처럼 눈먼 소리나 하시겠지요. 당신은 그 젊은이가 나라를 떠났다고 여겼잖아요.\"\n\n\"떠났을 수도 있지, 그러고도 돌아왔을 수도 있고.\" 목사가 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"이걸 언제 아셨습니까?\" 제임스 경이 말했다. 다른 누가 말하는 걸 듣기 싫었지만, 정작 자신이 말하기는 힘들었던 것이다.\n\n\"어제였네.\" 브룩 씨가 풀죽어 말했다. \"로윅에 갔었지. 도러시아가 나를 불렀거든, 알겠나. 아주 갑작스레 벌어진 일이야. 이틀 전만 해도 두 사람 다 전혀 생각이 없었어. 전혀 말이야, 알겠나. 세상일엔 묘한 데가 있어. 하지만 도러시아는 단단히 결심했어. 반대해 봐야 소용없네. 나는 강하게 말했어. 내 도리는 다했네, 체텀. 하지만 그 애는 제 좋을 대로 할 수 있는 거니까, 알겠나.\"\n\n\"일 년 전에 그자에게 결투를 신청해서 쏘아 버렸더라면 좋았을 것을.\" 제임스 경이 말했다. 잔인해서가 아니라, 무언가 강한 말이 필요했기 때문이었다.\n\n\"정말, 제임스, 그건 아주 불쾌한 일이었을 거예요.\" 실리아가 말했다.\n\n\"진정하게, 체텀. 좀 더 차분하게 이 일을 보게나.\" 캐드월러더 씨가 말했다. 그는 마음씨 좋은 친구가 분노에 그토록 휘둘리는 모습을 보는 게 안타까웠다.\n\n\"품위라곤, 옳고 그름의 분별이라곤 조금이라도 있는 사람이라면, 그것도 그 일이 자기 집안에서 일어났을 때는, 그게 그리 쉬운 일이 아닙니다.\" 제임스 경이 여전히 하얗게 질린 분노 속에서 말했다. \"그건 정말 추잡한 일입니다. 래디슬로에게 명예심이라곤 한 줌이라도 있었다면, 당장 나라를 떠나 다시는 이곳에 얼굴을 내밀지 않았을 겁니다. 하지만 나는 놀라지 않습니다. 캐소본의 장례식 다음 날 나는 어떻게 해야 하는지 말했어요. 하지만 아무도 내 말을 듣지 않았지요.\"\n\n\"자네는 불가능한 걸 원했어, 알겠나, 체텀.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다. \"그자를 배에 태워 보내길 바랐지. 나는 래디슬로를 우리 마음대로 할 수 없다고 말했네. 그자에겐 그자만의 생각이 있었어. 비범한 친구였지. 나는 늘 그가 비범한 친구라고 말했었네.\"\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 제임스 경이 받아치지 않고는 못 배기겠다는 듯 말했다. \"삼촌이 그자를 그렇게 높이 평가하신 게 다소 유감입니다. 그자가 이 동네에 자리 잡게 된 건 다 그 덕이니까요. 도러시아 같은 여인이 그자와 결혼해 스스로를 깎아내리는 꼴을 보게 된 것도 다 그 덕이고요.\" 제임스 경은 말이 쉽게 나오지 않아 구절 사이마다 조금씩 끊어 말했다. \"그 여인의 남편이 유언으로 콕 집어 둔 자라, 다시는 만나지 않는 게 도리였을 자입니다. 그 여인을 제 신분에서 끌어내려 가난 속으로 몰아넣는 자, 그런 희생을 받아들이는 비열함을 가진 자, 늘 떳떳지 못한 처지에 있었던 자, 출신이 나쁜 자, 게다가 내가 보기엔 원칙도 없고 가벼운 인간입니다. 그것이 내 생각입니다.\" 제임스 경은 단호하게 말을 맺고는 몸을 돌려 다리를 꼬았다.\n\n\"나는 그 애에게 모든 걸 짚어 줬네.\" 브룩 씨가 변명하듯 말했다. \"그러니까 가난과 신분을 버리는 일 말이야. '얘야, 너는 연 칠백 파운드로 사는 게, 마차도 없고 그런 것도 없이, 네가 누군지도 모르는 사람들 틈에서 사는 게 어떤 건지 모른다'고 말했지. 강하게 말했어. 하지만 도러시아한테 직접 이야기해 보길 권하네. 사실은, 그 애가 캐소본의 재산을 싫어해. 그 애 말을 들어 보면 알 걸세.\"\n\n\"아니요, 죄송하지만 그러지 않겠습니다.\" 제임스 경이 좀 더 차분하게 말했다. \"그녀를 다시 볼 수가 없습니다. 너무 괴로워서요. 도러시아 같은 여인이 잘못된 일을 저질렀다는 게 너무나 마음 아픕니다.\"\n\n\"공정하게 봅시다, 체텀.\" 느긋하고 입술이 두툼한 목사가 이 모든 불필요한 불편을 못마땅해하며 말했다. \"캐소본 부인이 경솔하게 행동하는 건지도 모르오. 한 남자를 위해 재산을 포기하고 있으니 말이오. 우리 사내들은 서로를 워낙 하찮게 보는지라, 그런 짓을 하는 여인을 두고 현명하다고 하기는 어렵소. 하지만 엄밀한 의미에서 그걸 잘못된 행동이라고 단죄해서는 안 될 것 같소.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇게 봅니다.\" 제임스 경이 대답했다. \"도러시아가 래디슬로와 결혼하는 건 잘못된 행동이라고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"이보게, 우리는 어떤 행동이 우리에게 불쾌하다고 해서 그걸 잘못이라 여기기 쉽다네.\" 목사가 조용히 말했다. 인생을 느긋하게 사는 많은 이들이 그렇듯, 그는 스스로 의로운 분노에 휩싸였다고 여기는 이들에게 이따금 뼈아픈 진실을 한마디 던지는 재주가 있었다. 제임스 경은 손수건을 꺼내 그 귀퉁이를 깨물기 시작했다.\n\n\"그래도 도도 언니가 너무한 거예요.\" 실리아가 남편을 두둔하고 싶어 말했다. \"언니는 절대로 다시 결혼하지 않겠다고 했어요. 그 누구와도요.\"\n\n\"나도 그 애가 똑같은 말을 하는 걸 직접 들었지.\" 체텀 부인이 마치 이것이 왕실의 증언이라도 되는 양 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그런 경우엔 으레 입 밖에 내지 않은 예외가 하나 있는 법이지요.\" 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. \"나한테 유일하게 놀라운 건, 당신들 중 누구라도 놀란다는 거예요. 당신들은 이걸 막으려고 아무것도 하지 않았어요. 트라이턴 경을 이리로 불러 그의 박애주의로 구애하게 했더라면, 그가 일 년이 가기 전에 그녀를 채 갔을 텐데요. 그 외엔 어디에도 안전이란 게 없었지요. 캐소본 씨는 이 모든 걸 더없이 아름답게 준비해 두었어요. 스스로 불쾌한 사람이 됐지요. 아니면 하느님께서 그를 그렇게 만드신 거고요. 그러고는 감히 그녀에게 거역할 테면 해 보라고 했지요. 어떤 하찮은 물건이라도 솔깃하게 만드는 비결은 바로 그렇게 비싼 값을 매겨 두는 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"잘못이라는 게 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠소, 캐드월러더.\" 제임스 경이 여전히 다소 찔린 기분으로 의자에 앉은 채 목사 쪽으로 돌아앉으며 말했다. \"그는 우리 집안에 들일 수 있는 사람이 아니오. 적어도, 나로서는 그렇다는 말이오.\" 그는 브룩 씨에게 시선을 주지 않으려 조심하며 말을 이었다. \"다른 분들은 그자와 어울리는 게 너무 즐거워서 이 일의 온당함 따위는 개의치 않으시겠지만요.\"\n\n\"음, 알다시피, 체텀,\" 브룩 씨가 다리를 어루만지며 너그럽게 말했다. \"나는 도러시아에게 등을 돌릴 수가 없어. 어느 선까지는 그 애의 아버지 노릇을 해 줘야지. 나는 '얘야, 내가 너를 넘겨주는 일을 거절하진 않으마' 하고 말했네. 그전엔 강하게 말했지만 말이야. 하지만 한정 상속을 끊어 줄 수는 있네, 알겠나. 돈도 들고 번거롭겠지만, 그렇게 할 수 있어, 알겠나.\"\n\n* 한정 상속(entail): 토지를 특정 상속인 계열에게만 물려주도록 묶어 두는 제도.\n브룩 씨는 제임스 경에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였고, 자신이 결단력을 보여 주는 동시에 준남작의 짜증 가운데 정당한 부분을 달래 주고 있다고 느꼈다. 그는 자신도 미처 깨닫지 못한 한층 교묘한 받아치기 방식을 찾아낸 것이었다. 그는 제임스 경이 부끄러워하는 어떤 동기를 건드렸다. 도러시아가 래디슬로와 결혼하는 데 대한 그의 감정은, 한편으로는 용서받을 만한 편견이나 심지어 정당한 견해에서 비롯된 것이었고, 다른 한편으로는 캐소본의 경우 못지않게 래디슬로의 경우에도 강한 질투 어린 혐오감에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그는 이 결혼이 도러시아에게 치명적인 결합이라고 확신했다. 하지만 그 감정의 덩어리 가운데에는 그가 너무 선량하고 명예로운 사람인지라 스스로에게조차 인정하기 꺼리는 한 가닥 줄기가 흐르고 있었다. 부정할 수 없는 사실은, 울타리 하나 안에 매력적으로 자리한 두 영지, 즉 티프턴과 프레시트가 하나로 합쳐지는 전망이 그의 아들이자 상속인을 위해 그의 마음을 흡족하게 했다는 것이다. 그래서 브룩 씨가 고개를 끄덕여 그 동기를 건드리자, 제임스 경은 문득 당혹스러움을 느꼈다. 목구멍이 막히는 것 같았고, 심지어 얼굴까지 붉어졌다. 분노가 처음 솟구쳤을 때는 평소보다 말이 많이 나왔지만, 브룩 씨의 달램은 캐드월러더 씨의 신랄한 암시보다 그의 혀를 더 단단히 묶어 버렸다.\n\n하지만 실리아는 외삼촌이 결혼식 이야기를 꺼낸 덕에 말할 틈이 생긴 게 반가워, 마치 저녁 식사 초대에 관한 문제라도 되는 양 별 열의 없는 태도로 말했다. \"외삼촌, 도도 언니가 곧바로 결혼한다는 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"삼 주 후래, 알겠니.\" 브룩 씨가 어쩔 수 없다는 듯 말했다. \"나는 막을 도리가 없네, 캐드월러더.\" 그는 약간의 지지를 구하듯 목사 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 덧붙였고, 목사가 말했다.\n\n\"나라면 이 일로 야단법석을 떨지 않겠소. 그녀가 가난하게 살고 싶다면, 그건 그녀의 문제요. 그녀가 그 젊은이가 부자라서 결혼한다면 아무도 한마디 안 했을 거요. 성직록을 받는 성직자들 중에도 그들이 살게 될 것보다 더 가난한 사람이 수두룩하오. 여기 엘리너만 봐도,\" 짓궂은 남편이 말을 이었다. \"이 사람은 나 때문에 친구들 속을 썩였소. 나는 연 천 파운드도 못 벌었고, 촌뜨기였고, 아무도 내게서 뭐 볼 만한 걸 찾지 못했고, 내 신발은 모양새가 그르고, 모든 사내가 어떻게 여자가 나를 좋아할 수 있는지 의아해했소. 정말이지, 래디슬로에 대해 더 나쁜 소리를 듣기 전까지는 그의 편을 들어야겠소.\"\n\n\"험프리, 그건 다 궤변이고, 당신도 알잖아요.\" 그의 아내가 말했다. \"당신에겐 모든 게 다 똑같지요. 그게 처음이자 끝이에요. 마치 당신이 캐드월러더가 아니었던 것처럼! 당신이 다른 이름이었다면 내가 당신 같은 괴물을 받아들였으리라고 누가 생각이나 하겠어요?\"\n\n\"게다가 성직자이기도 하고요.\" 체텀 부인이 흡족하게 한마디 했다. \"엘리너가 제 신분 아래로 내려갔다고는 할 수 없지요. 래디슬로 씨가 무엇이라고 말하기는 어렵겠지만 말이에요, 안 그러냐, 제임스?\"\n\n제임스 경은 짧게 끙 소리를 냈는데, 어머니에게 답하는 평소의 방식보다 덜 공손했다. 실리아는 생각에 잠긴 새끼 고양이처럼 그를 올려다보았다.\n\n\"그자의 핏줄이 끔찍한 혼합물이라는 건 인정해야겠지요!\" 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. \"우선 캐소본 집안의 오징어 먹물이 있고, 그다음엔 반항적인 폴란드 바이올린 연주자인가 무용 선생인가, 그랬던가요? 그러고는 헌 옷 장—\"\n\n\"실없는 소리 말아요, 엘리너.\" 목사가 일어서며 말했다. \"이제 우리가 갈 시간이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래도 그 젊은이는 잘생긴 도련님이지요.\" 캐드월러더 부인도 일어서며, 만회하고 싶은 마음에 말했다. \"그는 바보들이 끼어들기 전의 저 멋진 옛 크리츨리 가문 초상화들을 닮았어요.\"\n\n\"나도 함께 가겠소.\" 브룩 씨가 부랴부랴 일어서며 말했다. \"다들 내일 우리 집에 와서 식사해야 하오, 알겠소. 응, 실리아야?\"\n\n\"그렇게 할 거죠, 제임스, 그렇죠?\" 실리아가 남편의 손을 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 물론이지, 당신이 원한다면.\" 제임스 경이 조끼를 끌어내리며 말했지만, 아직 표정을 너그럽게 가다듬지는 못했다. \"그러니까, 다른 누구를 만나는 자리만 아니라면 말이오.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\" 브룩 씨가 그 조건을 알아듣고 말했다. \"도러시아는 자네가 자기를 보러 오지 않는 한 오지 않을 테니까, 알겠나.\"\n\n제임스 경과 실리아만 남게 되자, 그녀가 말했다. \"제임스, 로윅에 가게 마차를 좀 써도 괜찮을까요?\"\n\n\"뭐라고, 지금, 당장?\" 그가 다소 놀라며 대답했다.\n\n\"네, 아주 중요한 일이에요.\" 실리아가 말했다.\n\n\"기억해 둬, 실리아, 나는 그녀를 볼 수 없소.\" 제임스 경이 말했다.\n\n\"언니가 결혼을 그만둔다 해도요?\"\n\n\"그런 말을 해서 뭐 하겠소? 어쨌든 나는 마구간에 가겠소. 브리그스에게 마차를 대령하라고 이르지.\"\n\n실리아는, 그 말은 못 하더라도 적어도 도러시아의 마음을 움직이러 로윅까지 가는 것은 큰 도움이 되리라고 생각했다. 소녀 시절 내내 그녀는 적절히 놓인 한마디로, 도도가 늘 보고 살아온 이상한 색색의 등불들 사이로 자기 자신의 이해라는 햇빛이 들어갈 작은 창을 열어 줌으로써 언니에게 영향을 미칠 수 있다고 느껴 왔다. 그리고 이제 부인이 된 실리아는 자식 없는 언니에게 충고할 자격이 더 있다고 자연스레 느꼈다. 그 누가 실리아만큼 도도를 잘 이해하고, 그토록 다정하게 사랑할 수 있겠는가?\n\n화장방에서 분주하던 도러시아는, 결혼 계획을 밝힌 지 이토록 얼마 되지 않아 동생이 찾아온 모습을 보고 기쁨의 온기가 차오르는 것을 느꼈다. 그녀는 친구들의 반감을 미리, 그것도 과장되게 그려 보았고, 실리아마저 자신에게서 멀어질지도 모른다고 두려워하기까지 했던 것이다.\n\n\"오, 키티, 와 줘서 정말 기뻐!\" 도러시아가 실리아의 어깨에 두 손을 얹고 환하게 웃으며 말했다. \"나는 네가 나를 보러 오지 않을 거라고 거의 생각했었어.\"\n\n\"서두르느라 아서는 데려오지 않았어요.\" 실리아가 말했고, 두 사람은 마주 보는 작은 의자 두 개에 무릎을 맞대고 앉았다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 도도 언니, 이건 정말 나빠요.\" 실리아가 평온한 목쉰 소리로, 더없이 까탈스럽지 않은 예쁜 표정으로 말했다. \"언니는 우리 모두를 너무나 실망시켰어요. 그리고 나는 그 일이 정말로 이루어지리라곤 생각할 수가 없어요. 언니는 절대로 그런 식으로 살아갈 수 없어요. 게다가 언니의 그 모든 계획들은요! 언니는 그건 생각도 안 했을 거예요. 제임스는 언니를 위해서라면 어떤 수고도 마다하지 않았을 텐데, 언니는 평생 하고 싶은 일을 하며 살아갈 수도 있었어요.\"\n\n\"오히려 그 반대란다, 얘야.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"나는 내가 하고 싶은 일을 단 한 번도 할 수가 없었어. 아직 어떤 계획도 이루어 내지 못했지.\"\n\n\"그건 언니가 늘 될 수 없는 것들만 원했기 때문이에요. 하지만 다른 계획들이 생겼을 거예요. 그리고 우리 가운데 누구도 언니가 결혼할 수 있으리라고 생각해 본 적 없는 래디슬로 씨와 어떻게 결혼할 수 있어요? 제임스는 그 일로 너무나 충격을 받았어요. 게다가 그건 언니가 늘 살아온 것과 너무나 달라요. 언니는 캐소본 씨가 그토록 위대한 영혼을 지녔고, 또 그렇게 늙고 음울하고 학식이 많아서 그분과 결혼했잖아요. 그런데 이제 영지도 아무것도 없는 래디슬로 씨와 결혼한다니. 내 생각엔 언니가 어떻게든 스스로를 불편하게 만들고 싶어 하기 때문인 것 같아요.\"\n\n도러시아가 웃었다.\n\n\"정말, 이건 아주 심각한 일이에요, 도도 언니.\" 실리아가 더욱 진지해지며 말했다. \"어떻게 살아갈 거예요? 그리고 언니는 이상한 사람들 틈으로 떠나 버리겠죠. 나는 언니를 영영 보지 못할 거고요. 언니는 어린 아서한테도 관심을 두지 않을 거예요. 나는 늘 언니가 그럴 거라고 생각했는데—\"\n\n실리아의 좀처럼 보기 힘든 눈물이 눈에 고였고, 입가가 떨렸다.\n\n\"실리아야,\" 도러시아가 다정하면서도 진지하게 말했다. \"네가 나를 영영 보지 못한다면, 그건 내 잘못이 아닐 거야.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 언니 잘못이 될 거예요.\" 실리아가 작은 이목구비를 또 그렇게 가슴 아프게 일그러뜨리며 말했다. \"제임스가 견디지 못하는데 내가 어떻게 언니를 보러 가거나 언니를 우리 집에 둘 수 있겠어요? 그건 그이가 이 일이 옳지 않다고 여기기 때문이에요. 그이는 언니가 너무 잘못됐다고 생각해요, 도도 언니. 하지만 언니는 늘 잘못됐었어요. 그래도 나는 언니를 사랑하지 않을 수가 없어요. 그리고 언니가 어디서 살게 될지 아무도 짐작할 수 없어요. 대체 어디로 갈 거예요?\"\n\n\"나는 런던으로 갈 거야.\" 도러시아가 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 늘 길거리에서 살 수 있어요? 게다가 그렇게 가난해질 텐데. 내 물건을 절반은 줄 수도 있겠지만, 언니를 통 보지 못하는데 어떻게 그러겠어요?\"\n\n\"고맙구나, 키티.\" 도러시아가 부드러운 온정으로 말했다. \"마음 편히 가지렴. 어쩌면 제임스가 언젠가는 나를 용서해 줄지도 모르잖니.\"\n\n\"하지만 언니가 결혼만 하지 않는다면 훨씬 더 좋을 거예요.\" 실리아가 눈물을 닦으며 다시 자기 주장으로 돌아와 말했다. \"그러면 불편한 일이라곤 하나도 없을 거예요. 그리고 아무도 언니가 할 수 있으리라 생각지 않은 일을 하지도 않을 거고요. 제임스는 늘 언니가 여왕이 되어야 한다고 했는데, 이건 도무지 여왕다운 게 아니에요. 언니, 언니가 늘 어떤 실수들을 해 왔는지 알잖아요. 그리고 이건 또 하나의 실수예요. 아무도 래디슬로 씨를 언니한테 어울리는 남편이라고 생각하지 않아요. 게다가 언니는 절대 다시 결혼하지 않겠다고 말했었잖아요.\"\n\n\"내가 더 현명한 사람일 수도 있었다는 건 정말 맞는 말이야, 실리아.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"그리고 내가 더 나은 사람이었다면 더 나은 일을 할 수 있었을지도 모르지. 하지만 이게 내가 하려는 일이야. 나는 래디슬로 씨와 결혼하기로 약속했고, 그와 결혼할 거야.\"\n\n도러시아가 이 말을 하는 어조는 실리아가 오래전부터 알아차리도록 익혀 온 곡조였다. 그녀는 잠시 말이 없다가, 모든 다툼을 접은 듯이 말했다. \"그 사람이 언니를 무척 좋아해요, 도도 언니?\"\n\n\"그러길 바라. 나는 그를 무척 좋아해.\"\n\n\"그건 좋네요.\" 실리아가 편안하게 말했다. \"다만 나는 언니가 제임스 같은 남편을 두었으면 했어요. 집도 아주 가까워서 내가 마차로 갈 수 있는 그런 사람 말이에요.\"\n\n도러시아가 미소 지었고, 실리아는 다소 생각에 잠긴 표정이 되었다. 이윽고 그녀가 말했다. \"이 모든 일이 어떻게 일어났는지 도무지 모르겠어요.\" 실리아는 그 이야기를 듣는 게 즐거우리라고 생각했다.\n\n\"그렇겠지.\" 도러시아가 동생의 턱을 꼬집으며 말했다. \"그게 어떻게 일어났는지 안다면, 너한테는 그리 놀랍지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"내게 말해 줄 수 없어요?\" 실리아가 두 팔을 아늑하게 모으며 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼, 얘야, 너도 나와 함께 느껴 봐야 할 거야. 그러지 않으면 결코 알 수 없을 테니까.\"\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXV.\n\n“그러자 배심원들이 나갔으니, 그들의 이름은 눈먼 자 씨, 쓸모없는 자 씨, 악의 씨, 음욕 씨, 방탕 씨, 무모한 자 씨, 거만한 자 씨, 적의 씨, 거짓말쟁이 씨, 잔혹 씨, 빛을 미워하는 자 씨, 그리고 무자비한 자 씨였다. 이들은 저마다 자기들끼리 그에게 불리한 사사로운 평결을 내렸고, 그런 다음 만장일치로 그를 재판관 앞에서 유죄로 끌어들이기로 결론지었다. 그리하여 우선 자기들끼리, 배심장인 눈먼 자 씨가 말했다. 이 사람이 이단임을 나는 분명히 안다. 그러자 쓸모없는 자 씨가 말했다. 이런 자는 이 땅에서 없애 버려라! 옳거니, 악의 씨가 말했다. 나는 저자의 꼴조차 보기 싫으니까. 그러자 음욕 씨가 말했다. 나는 도저히 저자를 견딜 수 없었다. 나도 마찬가지요, 방탕 씨가 말했다. 저자는 늘 내 방식을 비난하려 들었으니. 목매달아라, 목매달아라, 무모한 자 씨가 말했다. 한심한 비렁뱅이로다, 거만한 자 씨가 말했다. 내 마음이 저자에게 들고일어나는구나, 적의 씨가 말했다. 저자는 악당이오, 거짓말쟁이 씨가 말했다. 목매다는 것도 저자에겐 과분하오, 잔혹 씨가 말했다. 저자를 어서 처치해 버립시다, 빛을 미워하는 자 씨가 말했다. 그러자 무자비한 자 씨가 말했다. 온 세상을 다 준다 해도 나는 저자와 화해할 수 없으니, 그러니 당장 저자를 사형에 처할 죄인으로 끌어들입시다.” — 『천로역정』.\n\n불멸의 버니언이 박해의 정념들이 유죄 평결을 내리는 장면을 그릴 때, 누가 페이스풀을 가엾게 여기겠는가? 단죄하는 군중 앞에서 자신이 무죄임을 아는 것, 우리가 비난받는 까닭이 오직 우리 안의 선함 때문임을 확신하는 것은 가장 위대한 사람들조차 이르지 못한 드물고도 복된 운명이다. 가엾은 운명이란, 자신에게 돌을 던지는 자들이 추악한 정념의 화신에 지나지 않는다고 스스로를 설득한다 해도 자신을 순교자라 부를 수 없는 사람의 운명, 자신이 정의를 표방했기 때문이 아니라 표방하던 그 사람이 되지 못했기 때문에 돌을 맞고 있음을 아는 사람의 운명이다.\n\n이것이야말로 벌스트로드가 미들마치를 떠날 채비를 하면서, 그리고 상처받은 자신의 삶을 새로운 얼굴들의 무관심이라는 그 서글픈 도피처에서 끝맺으러 가면서, 그 아래 시들어 가던 의식이었다. 아내의 의무에 충실하고 자비로운 한결같음이 그를 한 가지 공포에서 구해 주었으나, 그녀의 존재가 여전히 하나의 법정으로 남아 있는 것까지 막을 수는 없었으니, 그는 그 앞에서 고백을 움츠리면서도 변호를 갈망했다. 래플스의 죽음에 관해 자기 자신에게 둘러대 온 변명들은 그가 기도드리는 전지전능한 존재의 관념을 떠받쳐 주었지만, 그럼에도 그에게는 그 변명들을 아내에게 온전히 고백함으로써 심판대에 드러내지 못하게 막는 공포가 있었다. 내면의 논리와 동기로 씻고 희석한 행위들, 보이지 않는 용서를 비교적 쉽게 얻어 낼 수 있을 것 같던 그 행위들을 아내는 무어라 이름 붙일 것인가? 그녀가 언젠가 말없이 그의 행위를 살인이라 부른다는 것, 그것이야말로 그가 견딜 수 없는 일이었다. 그는 그녀의 의심에 둘러싸인 듯 느꼈다. 그녀가 아직은 그에게 그 최악의 단죄를 선고할 자격이 있다고 느끼지 못하리라는 데서 그는 그녀를 마주할 힘을 얻었다. 언젠가는, 어쩌면 — 죽어 갈 때에는 — 그녀에게 모든 것을 말하리라. 그 시간의 깊은 그늘 속에서, 짙어 가는 어둠 가운데 그녀가 그의 손을 잡고 있을 때면, 그녀는 그의 손길에서 움츠러들지 않고 들어 줄지도 모른다. 어쩌면. 하지만 은폐는 그의 평생의 습관이었고, 고백하려는 충동은 더 깊은 굴욕에 대한 두려움 앞에서 아무런 힘도 없었다.\n\n그는 아내를 향한 소심한 염려로 가득 차 있었으니, 그녀에게서 어떤 가혹한 판단이 나올까 두려워서만이 아니라 그녀가 고통받는 모습을 보며 깊은 비탄을 느꼈기 때문이다. 그녀는 이 위기를 가능한 한 딸들에게 숨기려고 그들을 해안가의 어느 기숙학교로 보내 버렸다. 딸들이 없는 덕분에 자신의 슬픔을 해명해야 하는 견딜 수 없는 필요나 그들의 겁먹은 놀라움을 마주해야 하는 부담에서 풀려난 그녀는, 날마다 머리카락에 흰빛을 줄긋고 눈꺼풀을 무겁게 만드는 슬픔과 거리낌 없이 함께 지낼 수 있었다.\n\n“당신이 내게 바라는 일이 있으면 무엇이든 말해 주오, 해리엇.” 벌스트로드가 그녀에게 말했다. “재산 정리에 관한 일 말이오. 나는 이 근방에 가진 땅을 팔지 않고 당신에게 안전한 생활 대책으로 남겨 줄 생각이오. 그런 문제에 무슨 바람이 있거든 내게 숨기지 마오.”\n\n며칠 뒤, 오라비를 찾아갔다가 돌아온 그녀는 한동안 마음에 품어 온 화제를 두고 남편에게 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n“나는 정말이지 오라버니 가족을 위해 무언가 해 주고 싶어요, 니컬러스. 그리고 우리는 로저먼드와 그 사람 남편에게도 얼마쯤 보상을 해야 할 의무가 있다고 생각해요. 월터 말로는 리드게이트 씨가 이 도시를 떠나야 한다는데, 그분 진료는 거의 아무 쓸모가 없게 되었고, 어디에 정착하기에도 가진 게 거의 남지 않았다고 해요. 가엾은 오라버니 가족에게 얼마쯤 보상을 해 줄 수 있다면, 차라리 우리 자신을 위한 것을 줄이는 편이 낫겠어요.”\n\n벌스트로드 부인은 “얼마쯤 보상을 해 준다”라는 말 이상으로 사실에 다가가고 싶지 않았으니, 남편이 자기 뜻을 알아들으리라 여겼기 때문이다. 그에게는 그녀가 알지 못하는 특별한 까닭이 있어 그녀의 제안에 움찔했다. 그는 머뭇거리다가 입을 열었다.\n\n“당신이 제안하는 방식으로는 당신의 바람을 이룰 수가 없소, 여보. 리드게이트 씨는 사실상 나에게서 더 이상 어떤 도움도 받기를 거절했소. 내가 빌려 준 천 파운드를 돌려보냈으니까. 캐소본 부인이 그 목적으로 그에게 그 돈을 미리 내어 주었소. 여기 그의 편지가 있소.”\n\n그 편지는 벌스트로드 부인에게 모질게 칼을 대는 듯했다. 캐소본 부인의 대부금에 관한 언급은, 누구든 자기 남편과 엮이는 일을 피하는 게 당연하다고 여기는 그 세간의 감정을 비추는 것만 같았다. 그녀는 한동안 말이 없었다. 눈물이 하나둘 떨어졌고, 그것을 닦아 내는 동안 그녀의 턱이 떨렸다. 맞은편에 앉아 있던 벌스트로드는 두 달 전만 해도 환하게 피어나 있던 그 슬픔에 지친 얼굴을 보며 마음이 아팠다. 그 얼굴은 시들어 버린 자신의 이목구비와 슬픔의 짝을 이루느라 늙어 버렸다. 그녀를 위로하려는 어떤 노력에 떠밀려 그가 말했다.\n\n“또 다른 방법이 있소, 해리엇. 당신이 거기 나서고 싶다면, 그것으로 내가 당신 오라버니 가족에게 도움을 줄 수 있을 거요. 그리고 그것은, 내 생각에, 당신에게도 이로울 것이오. 당신 것이 되도록 정해 둔 그 땅을 관리하는 데에도 유리한 방법이 될 테니 말이오.”\n\n그녀는 귀를 기울이는 표정이었다.\n\n“가스가 한때 스톤 코트의 관리를 맡아 당신 조카 프레드를 거기 앉힐 생각을 한 적이 있소. 가축은 지금 그대로 두고, 보통의 지대 대신에 수익의 일정 몫을 내는 방식이었지. 그 젊은이가 가스 밑에서 일하는 것과 더불어 그렇게 시작한다면 바람직할 거요. 당신에게 만족스러운 일이 되겠소?”\n\n“네, 그럴 거예요.” 벌스트로드 부인이 얼마간 기운을 되찾으며 말했다. “가엾은 월터가 너무 풀이 죽어 있어요. 떠나기 전에 오라버니에게 조금이라도 도움이 될 일이라면 내 힘닿는 데까지 무엇이든 해 보고 싶어요. 우리는 늘 다정한 남매였으니까요.”\n\n“그 제안은 당신이 직접 가스에게 해야 하오, 해리엇.” 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다. 해야 할 말이 마음에 들지는 않았지만, 아내를 위로하는 것 말고도 다른 이유들로 그가 마음에 둔 결말을 바랐던 것이다. “그 땅이 사실상 당신 것이며 그가 나와는 어떤 거래도 할 필요가 없다고 그에게 분명히 말해야 하오. 연락은 스탠디시를 통해 주고받을 수 있소. 이 말을 하는 까닭은 가스가 내 대리인 노릇을 그만두었기 때문이오. 그가 직접 작성해 조건을 적어 둔 서류를 당신 손에 쥐여 줄 수 있으니, 당신이 그 조건들을 그가 다시 받아들이도록 제안하면 되오. 조카를 위해서라는 명목으로 당신이 그 일을 제안한다면, 그가 받아들일 가능성이 적지 않다고 생각하오.”\n\n# CHAPTER LXXXVI.\n\n“마음은 사랑으로 포화되니, 마치 자신을 보존하는 신성한 소금에 절여지듯 하다. 그리하여 생의 새벽부터 서로를 사랑한 이들의 부패하지 않는 결속이, 그리고 오래 이어진 옛사랑의 싱그러움이 있다. 사랑의 방부(防腐)가 존재하는 것이다. 필레몬과 바우키스는 다프니스와 클로에로 빚어졌다. 그러한 노년이란, 저녁이 새벽을 닮은 모습이다.” — 빅토르 위고, 『웃는 남자』\n\n개스 부인은 차 마실 무렵 케일럽이 복도로 들어서는 소리를 듣고 거실 문을 열며 말했다. “이제 오셨군요, 케일럽. 식사는 하셨어요?” (개스 씨의 식사는 늘 “일”에 밀려 뒷전이었다.)\n\n“오, 했지, 잘 먹었어. 식은 양고기에다 뭐였는지 모를 것들. 메리는 어디 있소?”\n\n“레티하고 정원에 있을 거예요.”\n\n“프레드는 아직 안 왔소?”\n\n“네. 차도 안 드시고 또 나가시려고요, 케일럽?” 개스 부인이 말했다. 정신이 딴 데 팔린 남편이 방금 벗어 놓은 모자를 다시 쓰는 것을 보았던 것이다.\n\n“아니, 아니야. 메리에게 잠깐 다녀오려는 것뿐이오.”\n\n메리는 정원의 풀밭 한구석에 있었는데, 그곳에는 배나무 두 그루 사이에 그네가 높다랗게 매달려 있었다. 그녀는 분홍 손수건을 머리에 둘러 작은 차양처럼 만들어 비스듬히 비치는 햇살로부터 눈을 가리고 있었고, 그러면서 레티를 한껏 힘차게 밀어 주고 있었다. 레티는 마구 웃으며 소리를 질러 댔다.\n\n아버지를 보자 메리는 그네에서 떨어져 그를 맞으러 갔고, 분홍 손수건을 뒤로 젖히며 멀리서부터 사랑스러운 기쁨이 절로 배어나는 미소를 지어 보였다.\n\n“너를 찾으러 왔단다, 메리.” 개스 씨가 말했다. “잠깐 좀 걷자꾸나.”\n\n메리는 아버지가 무언가 특별히 할 말이 있다는 것을 잘 알았다. 그의 눈썹이 그 애처로운 각도를 이루고 있었고, 목소리에는 부드러운 진중함이 어려 있었다. 이런 것들은 메리가 레티만 한 나이였을 때부터 그녀에게 신호였다. 그녀는 아버지의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼었고, 두 사람은 호두나무가 늘어선 줄을 따라 돌아갔다.\n\n“네가 결혼하려면 한참을 기다려야 할 게다, 메리. 마음이 좀 안됐구나.” 아버지가 그녀를 보지 않고 다른 손에 쥔 지팡이 끝을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“안됐을 게 뭐 있어요, 아버지. 저는 즐겁게 지낼 작정인걸요.” 메리가 웃으며 말했다. “저는 스물네 해도 넘게 혼자서 즐겁게 지냈는걸요. 다음번엔 그렇게까지 오래 걸리지는 않을 거예요.” 그러고는 잠깐 말을 멈추었다가 더 진지하게, 얼굴을 아버지 쪽으로 숙이며 말했다. “아버지는 프레드가 마음에 드세요?”\n\n케일럽은 입을 오므리고 슬쩍 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n“아니, 아버지, 지난 수요일에는 그 사람을 칭찬하셨잖아요. 가축에 대한 감각이 남다르고, 보는 눈이 좋다고 하셨죠.”\n\n“내가 그랬더냐?” 케일럽이 다소 능청스럽게 말했다.\n\n“네, 제가 전부 적어 두었는걸요, 날짜에 서기 연도까지, 죄다요.” 메리가 말했다. “아버지는 뭐든 깔끔하게 장부에 적어 두는 걸 좋아하시잖아요. 그리고 아버지를 대하는 그 사람 태도도 정말 좋아요. 아버지를 깊이 존경하고요. 게다가 프레드보다 성품이 더 좋은 사람은 없을 거예요.”\n\n“그래, 그래. 그 사람이 훌륭한 신랑감이라고 나를 구슬려 믿게 하려는 게로구나.”\n\n“정말 아니에요, 아버지. 저는 그 사람이 훌륭한 신랑감이라서 사랑하는 게 아니에요.”\n\n“그럼 무엇 때문이냐?”\n\n“아유, 그야 제가 늘 그 사람을 사랑해 왔기 때문이죠. 다른 누구를 그렇게 마음껏 야단치는 건 결코 좋아하지 못할 거예요. 그건 남편감에게서 생각해 볼 점이잖아요.”\n\n“그럼 네 마음은 완전히 정해진 게냐, 메리?” 케일럽이 처음의 어조로 돌아와 말했다. “요즘처럼 일이 흘러온 동안 다른 바람이 마음에 들어선 건 아니고?” (케일럽은 그 막연한 말 속에 많은 뜻을 담았다.) “늦더라도 안 하는 것보다는 나으니 말이다. 여자는 제 마음을 억지로 다잡아선 안 돼. 그런 식으로는 남자에게 아무 도움도 못 줄 게다.”\n\n“제 마음은 변하지 않았어요, 아버지.” 메리가 차분하게 말했다. “프레드가 저에게 한결같은 한 저도 그에게 한결같을 거예요. 우리 둘 중 누구도 상대 없이는 견디지 못할 거고, 아무리 다른 사람을 흠모하게 되더라도 그 사람을 더 좋아할 수는 없을 거예요. 그렇게 되면 우리에겐 너무 큰 차이가 생기는걸요. 정든 옛 장소들이 전부 바뀌고, 모든 것의 이름이 달라지는 걸 보는 것처럼요. 우리는 오래 서로를 기다려야 해요. 하지만 프레드도 그걸 알아요.”\n\n케일럽은 곧바로 대답하지 않고 가만히 서서 풀 깔린 길에 지팡이를 비벼 댔다. 그러다 목소리에 감정을 실어 말했다. “음, 소식이 좀 있단다. 프레드가 스톤코트에 가서 살면서 그곳 땅을 관리한다면 어떻겠느냐?”\n\n“그게 어떻게 가능해요, 아버지?” 메리가 의아해하며 말했다.\n\n“그 사람 숙모인 벌스트로드 부인을 위해 관리해 주는 거지. 그 가엾은 부인이 나한테 와서 사정하며 간곡히 부탁하더구나. 그 청년에게 도움이 되어 주고 싶다는 거야. 그 사람한테는 좋은 일이 될지도 모르지. 절약하면 차차 가축을 사들일 수 있을 테고, 그 사람은 농사에 소질이 있으니까.”\n\n“오, 프레드가 정말 기뻐하겠어요! 믿어지지 않을 만큼 좋은 일이에요.”\n\n“아, 하지만 명심하거라.” 케일럽이 경고하듯 고개를 돌리며 말했다. “이 일은 내가 직접 짊어지고 책임지면서 모든 걸 살펴야 하는 거다. 그러면 네 어머니가 좀 마음 상해할 게야, 입 밖에 내진 않더라도 말이지. 프레드는 신중해야 할 거다.”\n\n“아버지, 어쩌면 너무 무리예요.” 메리가 기쁨을 누그러뜨리며 말했다. “아버지께 새로운 골칫거리를 안겨 드린다면 거기엔 아무 기쁨도 없을 거예요.”\n\n“아니다, 아니야. 일은 내 낙이란다, 얘야, 네 어머니를 속상하게만 하지 않는다면 말이지. 그리고 너랑 프레드가 결혼하면,” 여기서 케일럽의 목소리가 알아챌 듯 말 듯 떨렸다. “그 사람은 착실하고 알뜰해질 게다. 게다가 너는 여자다운 방식으로나마 네 어머니의 영민함을, 그리고 내 영민함도 물려받았으니, 그 사람을 잘 다잡아 줄 게야. 그 사람이 조금 있으면 올 테니, 너에게 먼저 말해 주고 싶었던 거란다. 네가 직접 그에게 알려 주고 싶을 것 같아서 말이지. 그다음에 내가 그 사람과 잘 의논해서, 일이며 이런저런 사정들을 짚어 볼 수 있을 게다.”\n\n“오, 다정하고 좋으신 아버지!” 메리가 외치며 두 손으로 아버지의 목을 끌어안았고, 아버지는 어루만져지는 것을 기꺼이 받아들이며 차분하게 고개를 숙였다. “다른 여자애들도 자기 아버지가 세상에서 제일 훌륭한 사람이라고 생각할까요!”\n\n“말도 안 되는 소리, 얘야. 너는 네 남편이 더 낫다고 생각하게 될 거다.”\n\n“그럴 리가 없어요.” 메리가 평소의 어조로 돌아오며 말했다. “남편들이란 다잡아 줘야 하는 열등한 부류의 인간들인걸요.”\n\n레티가 달려와 합류하여 함께 집으로 들어서던 때, 메리는 과수원 문가에 있는 프레드를 보고 그를 맞으러 갔다.\n\n“어쩜 그리 좋은 옷을 입었어요, 사치스러운 청년 같으니!” 메리가 말했다. 프레드는 가만히 서서 장난스레 격식을 차리며 그녀에게 모자를 들어 올렸다. “절약은 안 배우는군요.”\n\n“이거 너무하잖아, 메리.” 프레드가 말했다. “이 외투 소맷부리 끝을 좀 봐! 부지런히 솔질을 해서 겨우 멀쩡해 보이는 거라고. 양복 세 벌을 아껴 두고 있어. 한 벌은 결혼 예복으로 말이야.”\n\n“정말 우스워 보이겠네요! 낡은 유행 책에 나오는 신사처럼요.”\n\n“오, 아니야, 두 해는 갈 거야.”\n\n“두 해라니! 좀 현실적으로 생각해요, 프레드.” 메리가 걸음을 돌리며 말했다. “듣기 좋은 기대는 부추기지 마세요.”\n\n“왜 안 돼? 듣기 좋지 않은 기대보다는 듣기 좋은 기대로 사는 게 낫지. 우리가 두 해 안에 결혼할 수 없다면, 그 진실이 닥칠 때 충분히 나쁠 테니까.”\n\n“듣기 좋은 기대를 부추겼다가 그 때문에 해를 입은 어느 젊은 신사 이야기를 들은 적이 있어요.”\n\n“메리, 만약 나를 낙담시킬 이야기를 하려는 거라면 나는 달아날 거야. 집으로 개스 씨께 가 버릴 테야. 나는 기운이 없어. 아버지께서 너무 풀이 죽으셔서 집안이 예전 같지가 않아. 더는 나쁜 소식을 견딜 수가 없어.”\n\n“당신이 스톤코트에 살면서 농장을 관리하고, 놀라울 만큼 신중하게 굴며 해마다 돈을 모아 마침내 가축과 가구가 전부 당신 것이 되고, 보스럽 트럼불 씨 말마따나 명망 있는 농업가가 된다는 걸 나쁜 소식이라 부르겠어요? 안됐지만 좀 통통해지고, 그리스어와 라틴어는 슬프게도 비바람에 닳아 버린 채로 말이에요.”\n\n“그냥 농담하는 거지, 메리?” 그렇게 말하면서도 프레드는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n“그게 바로 방금 아버지께서 일어날 수도 있다고 말씀하신 일이에요, 아버지는 결코 허튼소리를 하시지 않잖아요.” 메리가 이제 프레드를 올려다보며 말했고, 프레드는 함께 걸으며 그녀의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐어 아플 지경이었지만, 그녀는 불평하지 않았다.\n\n“오, 그럼 나는 엄청나게 착한 사람이 될 거야, 메리, 그리고 우리는 당장 결혼할 수 있어.”\n\n“그렇게 서두르지 마세요, 신사 양반. 내가 우리 결혼을 몇 해쯤 미루는 편을 더 좋아할지도 모른다는 걸 어떻게 알아요? 그러면 당신이 못되게 굴 시간이 생길 테고, 그러다 내가 다른 누군가를 더 좋아하게 되면, 당신을 차 버릴 핑계가 생기는 거죠.”\n\n“제발 농담하지 마, 메리.” 프레드가 격한 감정에 차서 말했다. “이게 다 사실이라고, 그래서 네가 행복하다고 진지하게 말해 줘. 나를 제일 사랑하니까 말이야.”\n\n“이게 다 사실이에요, 프레드, 그래서 저는 행복해요. 당신을 제일 사랑하니까요.” 메리가 시키는 대로 외워 읊는 듯한 어조로 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 가파른 지붕의 현관 아래 문간 계단에서 머뭇거렸고, 프레드는 거의 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n“우리가 처음 약혼했을 때, 그 우산 고리로 말이야, 메리, 너는…….”\n\n기쁨의 정령이 메리의 눈 속에서 한층 또렷이 웃기 시작했지만, 그 야속한 벤이 문간으로 달려왔고 그 뒤로 브라우니가 컹컹 짖어 댔다. 벤은 두 사람에게 폴짝 부딪치며 말했다.\n\n“프레드 형, 메리 누나! 도대체 안 들어올 거예요? 아니면 누나 케이크 내가 먹어도 돼요?”\n\n피날레.\n모든 끝은 끝인 동시에 시작이기도 하다. 젊은 생애들과 오랫동안 함께한 끝에, 그들이 훗날의 세월에 어떤 일을 겪었는지 알고 싶지 않을 사람이 누가 있겠는가? 한 생의 단편이란, 아무리 전형적이라 해도, 고른 직물에서 떼어 낸 견본이 아니기 때문이다. 약속은 지켜지지 않을 수도 있고, 열렬한 출발 뒤에 쇠퇴가 따라올 수도 있으며, 잠재된 힘이 오래 기다리던 기회를 찾을 수도 있고, 지난날의 잘못이 위대한 만회를 재촉할 수도 있다.\n\n수많은 이야기의 종착지였던 결혼은, 여전히 하나의 위대한 시작이다. 에덴에서 신혼을 보냈으나 광야의 가시덤불과 엉겅퀴 사이에서 첫아이를 얻은 아담과 이브에게 그러했듯이 말이다. 결혼은 여전히 가정 서사시의 시작이다. 흘러가는 세월을 정점으로, 노년을 함께 나눈 달콤한 기억의 수확으로 만들어 주는 그 완전한 결합을 점차 쟁취하느냐, 아니면 돌이킬 수 없이 잃어버리느냐 하는 서사시의 시작인 것이다.\n\n어떤 이들은 옛 십자군처럼 희망과 열정의 찬란한 장비를 갖추고 출발하지만, 서로에게 그리고 세상에 인내심이 부족해 도중에 꺾여 버리고 만다.\n\n프레드 빈시와 메리 개스에게 마음을 써 온 모든 이들은, 이 두 사람이 그런 실패를 겪지 않고 굳건한 상호 행복을 이루었음을 알면 기뻐할 것이다. 프레드는 여러 면에서 이웃들을 놀라게 했다. 그는 자기가 사는 주(州) 일대에서 이론과 실무를 겸비한 농부로 제법 이름을 날렸고, 『풋작물 재배와 가축 사육의 경제』라는 저작을 펴내어 농업 모임에서 큰 축하를 받았다. 미들마치에서는 칭찬이 좀 더 인색했다. 그곳 사람들 대부분은 프레드 빈시가 순무나 사료용 사탕무에 관해 글을 쓰리라고는 전혀 기대하지 않았던 터라, 프레드가 거둔 저술의 공로가 그의 아내 덕분일 거라고 믿는 쪽으로 기울었다.\n\n그러나 메리가 자기 아들들을 위해 『플루타르코스에서 가려 뽑은 위인 이야기』라는 작은 책을 써서 미들마치의 그립 출판사에서 인쇄해 펴냈을 때는, 마을의 모든 사람이 이 책의 공로를 프레드에게 돌리려 했다. 프레드가 “고대 문헌을 공부하는” 대학에 다녔으며 마음만 먹었다면 성직자가 될 수도 있었으리라는 것이었다.\n\n이런 식으로, 미들마치는 결코 속은 적이 없으며, 책을 썼다고 누군가를 칭찬할 필요는 없다는 것이 분명해졌다. 책이란 늘 다른 누군가가 쓴 것이었으니 말이다.\n\n게다가 프레드는 흔들림 없이 착실했다. 결혼한 지 몇 해 뒤, 그는 메리에게 자신의 행복이 절반은 페어브러더 덕분이라고 말했다. 적절한 순간에 그를 단단히 잡아 일으켜 준 사람이 바로 그였던 것이다. 프레드가 다시는 자신의 낙천성에 현혹되지 않았다고 말할 수는 없다. 작물 수확량이나 가축 매매의 수익은 대개 그의 예상에 못 미쳤고, 그는 늘 결과가 나쁘게 드러난 말을 사들여 돈을 벌 수 있으리라 믿는 버릇이 있었다. 물론, 메리의 말에 따르면, 그건 프레드의 판단 탓이 아니라 말 탓이었지만 말이다. 그는 승마에 대한 애착을 간직했으나, 하루를 사냥에 내어 주는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 그리고 그렇게 사냥에 나설 때면, 울타리 앞에서 겁쟁이라 놀림받는 것을 순순히 받아들이는 점이 눈에 띄었다. 마치 메리와 아이들이 다섯 가로대 문에 걸터앉아 있거나, 산울타리와 도랑 사이로 곱슬머리를 내밀고 있는 모습이 보이기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n아들이 셋이었다. 메리는 사내아이만 낳은 것을 불만스러워하지 않았다. 프레드가 메리를 닮은 딸을 갖고 싶다고 하자, 그녀는 웃으며 “그러면 당신 어머니께 너무 큰 시련이 될 거예요”라고 말했다. 빈시 부인은 노년에 접어들어 살림의 광채가 빛바랜 가운데서도, 프레드의 아들들 가운데 적어도 둘은 진짜 빈시 집안 아이들이고 “개스 집안을 닮지” 않았다는 것을 알아차리고 크게 위안을 받았다. 그러나 메리는 셋 중 막내가 짧은 윗도리를 입던 시절의 자기 아버지 모습과 무척이나 닮았다는 것에, 또 구슬치기를 하거나 잘 익은 배를 떨어뜨리려 돌을 던질 때 놀라울 만큼 정확한 솜씨를 보인다는 것에 남몰래 기뻐했다.\n\n벤과 레티 개스는 제대로 십대에 접어들기도 전에 삼촌과 이모가 되었는데, 조카로는 사내아이가 나은지 계집아이가 나은지를 두고 한참 다투었다. 벤은 계집아이가 사내아이보다 쓸모가 덜한 게 분명하다고 우겼으니, 그렇지 않다면 늘 치마만 입고 있을 리가 없으며, 그건 계집아이가 별 쓸모가 없도록 만들어졌다는 증거라는 것이었다. 그러자 책에서 근거를 곧잘 끌어오는 레티가 발끈하며, 하느님은 아담과 이브 모두에게 똑같이 가죽옷을 지어 주셨다고 받아쳤다. 게다가 동방에서는 남자들도 치마를 입었다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 이 뒤엣것은 앞엣것의 위엄을 흐려 놓는, 군더더기 같은 논거였으니, 벤은 경멸하듯 “그래서 걔들이 그렇게 맹꽁이지!”라고 대꾸하고는 곧장 어머니에게 사내아이가 계집아이보다 낫지 않으냐고 호소했다. 개스 부인은 둘 다 똑같이 말썽꾸러기지만, 사내아이가 더 힘이 센 것은 의심할 여지가 없고, 더 빨리 달릴 수 있으며, 더 멀리까지 더 정확하게 던질 수 있다고 선언했다. 이 신탁 같은 판결에 벤은 말썽꾸러기라는 말은 개의치 않고 흡족해했다. 그러나 레티는 그 말을 언짢게 받아들였으니, 그녀의 우월감은 근육보다 강했던 것이다.\n\n프레드는 결코 부자가 되지 못했다. 그의 낙천성도 부자가 되리라는 기대까지 품게 하지는 않았다. 그러나 그는 차차 돈을 모아 스톤코트의 가축과 가구를 소유하게 되었고, 개스 씨가 그에게 맡긴 일 덕분에 농부들에게 늘 따라붙는 그 “어려운 시절”을 넉넉히 헤쳐 나갈 수 있었다. 메리는 한 집안의 안주인이 된 시절에 이르러 어머니만큼이나 다부진 몸매가 되었다. 그러나 어머니와 달리 아이들에게 정식 교육을 거의 시키지 않아서, 개스 부인은 아이들이 문법과 지리의 기초를 제대로 다지지 못할까 봐 걱정했다. 그럼에도 아이들은 학교에 갔을 때 충분히 앞서 있는 것으로 드러났는데, 어쩌면 그들이 어머니와 함께 있는 것만큼 좋아하는 일이 없었기 때문이었으리라. 프레드는 겨울 저녁에 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오면서, 미리부터 판벽을 두른 거실의 환한 난롯가를 즐겁게 떠올렸고, 메리를 아내로 맞지 못한 다른 남자들을 안쓰럽게 여겼다. 특히 페어브러더 씨를 말이다. “그분은 나보다 열 배는 더 너에게 어울리는 분이었어.” 이제 프레드는 너그럽게 그녀에게 그렇게 말할 수 있었다. “물론 그랬죠.” 메리가 대답했다. “바로 그 때문에 그분은 나 없이도 더 잘 지낼 수 있었던 거예요. 하지만 당신은요, 당신이 어떻게 됐을지 생각하면 몸서리가 쳐져요. 말 빌린 값과 고운 삼베 손수건 값에 빚을 진 부목사가 됐겠죠!”\n\n혹 알아본다면, 프레드와 메리가 아직도 스톤코트에 살고 있음을 발견하게 될지도 모른다. 덩굴식물이 여전히 그 거품 같은 꽃송이를 멋진 돌담 너머로, 호두나무들이 위풍당당하게 줄지어 선 들판으로 흘려보내고 있음을, 그리고 햇살 좋은 날이면 우산 고리로 처음 약혼했던 그 두 연인이, 옛 피터 페더스톤 시절에 메리 개스가 리드게이트 씨가 오는지 내다보라는 분부를 자주 받았던 그 열린 창가에서 백발의 평온함에 잠겨 앉아 있는 모습을 볼 수 있을지도 모른다.\n\n리드게이트의 머리카락은 끝내 희어지지 않았다. 그는 겨우 쉰 살에 죽으면서, 생명보험에 거액을 들어 둔 덕에 아내와 자식들의 생계를 마련해 주었다. 그는 훌륭한 개업의로 자리를 잡아 계절에 따라 런던과 대륙의 어느 온천 휴양지를 오가며 지냈고, 통풍에 관한 논문을 썼는데, 통풍이란 부유한 이들이 많이 걸리는 병이었다. 돈을 내는 많은 환자가 그의 의술에 기댔으나, 그는 늘 자신을 실패자로 여겼다. 한때 하려고 마음먹었던 일을 이루지 못했던 것이다. 지인들은 그가 그토록 매력적인 아내를 둔 것을 부러워했고, 그 생각을 흔들 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 로저먼드는 두 번 다시 명예를 더럽힐 경솔한 행동을 저지르지 않았다. 그녀는 그저 변함없이 성품이 온화하고, 판단에 있어서는 완강하며, 남편을 타이르기를 좋아하고, 술책으로 그를 좌절시킬 줄 아는 채로 지냈다. 세월이 흐르면서 그는 점점 더 그녀에게 맞서지 않게 되었는데, 그로부터 로저먼드는 그가 자기 의견의 가치를 깨달았다고 결론지었다. 한편 그녀는 그가 좋은 수입을 올리게 되자 그의 재능을 한층 철저히 확신하게 되었고, 그 위협받던 브라이드 거리의 새장 대신 온통 꽃과 금박으로 꾸민, 자신이 닮은 극락조에게 어울릴 새장을 마련했다. 요컨대 리드게이트는 이른바 성공한 남자였다. 그러나 그는 디프테리아로 일찍 세상을 떴고, 로저먼드는 그 후 나이 지긋하고 부유한 의사와 재혼했는데, 그는 그녀의 네 아이를 다정하게 대해 주었다. 그녀는 딸들을 데리고 마차를 몰고 다니며 무척 어여쁜 자태를 뽐냈고, 자신의 행복을 “보답”이라고 자주 일컬었다. 무엇에 대한 보답인지는 말하지 않았으나, 아마도 테르티우스에 대한 인내의 보답이라는 뜻이었으리라. 그의 성품은 끝내 흠 없이 다듬어지지 않아서, 죽는 순간까지도 이따금 쓰디쓴 말을 불쑥 내뱉었는데, 그것은 그가 내보인 후회의 기색보다 더 오래 기억에 남았다. 한번은 그가 그녀를 자신의 바질 화초라고 불렀고, 그녀가 무슨 뜻이냐고 묻자, 바질이란 살해된 사람의 뇌수에서 놀라울 만큼 무성하게 자란 식물이라고 대답했다. 로저먼드에게는 그런 말에 대해 평온하지만 강한 대꾸가 준비되어 있었다. 그렇다면 어째서 그는 자기를 택했단 말인가? 그가 늘 칭찬하며 자기보다 위에 두던 래디슬로 부인을 얻지 못한 것이 안타까운 일이라고. 그리하여 대화는 로저먼드에게 유리한 쪽으로 끝맺어졌다. 그러나 그녀가 도러시아를 깎아내리는 말은 한마디도 입에 올린 적이 없으며, 생애 가장 모진 위기에 자기를 도우러 와 준 그 너그러움을 경건한 마음으로 기억했다는 것을 전하지 않는다면 공정하지 못한 일이리라.\n\n도러시아 자신은 다른 여인들보다 칭송받기를 꿈꾸지 않았으니, 자신이 좀 더 나은 사람이었고 좀 더 잘 알았더라면 언제든 더 잘할 수 있는 무언가가 있었으리라고 느꼈기 때문이다. 그래도 그녀는 윌 래디슬로와 결혼하기 위해 지위와 재산을 포기한 것을 결코 후회하지 않았고, 만약 그녀가 후회했다면 윌은 그것을 자신에게 더없는 수치이자 슬픔으로 여겼을 것이다. 두 사람은 그것을 해칠 수 있는 어떤 충동보다도 강한 사랑으로 서로 묶여 있었다. 도러시아에게는 감정으로 채워지지 않은 삶이란 불가능했을 텐데, 이제 그녀의 삶은 자신을 위해 애써 찾아내고 표시해 두어야 하는 미심쩍은 수고 없이도 자비로운 활동으로도 채워졌다. 윌은 열렬한 공인(公人)이 되어, 즉각적인 선(善)에 대한 젊은 낙관과 더불어 개혁이 시작되던—우리 시대에는 그런 낙관이 크게 억눌렸지만—그런 시절에 잘 활약했고, 마침내 그의 경비를 대 준 한 선거구에 의해 의회로 진출하게 되었다. 도러시아로서는, 부조리가 존재하는 한, 남편이 그것과 맞서 싸우는 한복판에 있고 자신은 그에게 아내로서 도움을 주는 것보다 더 좋아할 일이 없었다. 그녀를 아는 많은 이들은, 그토록 실질적이고 보기 드문 존재가 다른 사람의 삶 속으로 흡수되어 어떤 한정된 무리 안에서 한낱 아내이자 어머니로만 알려진 것을 안타깝게 여겼다. 그러나 그녀가 가진 능력으로 그 대신 정확히 무엇을 했어야 했는지를 말하는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 윌 래디슬로와 결혼하지 말았어야 했다는 부정적인 처방에서 더 나아가지 못한 제임스 체텀 경조차도 그러했다.\n\n그러나 그의 이런 견해가 영구적인 소원함을 낳지는 않았다. 그리고 그 가족이 다시 온전해진 방식은 관련된 모든 이의 성격을 그대로 드러내는 것이었다. 브룩 씨는 윌과 도러시아와 편지를 주고받는 즐거움을 떨치지 못했다. 그러던 어느 날 아침, 그의 펜이 시정(市政) 개혁의 전망에 관해 유난히 막힘없이 미끄러지다가, 그랜지로 초대하는 글로 흘러가 버렸고, 일단 써 놓고 보니 그 귀중한 편지 전체를 (도무지 생각조차 할 수 없는) 희생하는 것보다 적은 대가로는 그 초대를 없던 일로 할 수가 없었다. 이 서신 왕래가 오가던 몇 달 동안, 브룩 씨는 제임스 체텀 경과 이야기를 나누면서 한정상속을 끊으려는 의도가 여전히 유지되고 있다고 줄곧 전제하거나 암시해 왔다. 그리고 그의 펜이 그 대담한 초대장을 써 버린 바로 그날, 그는 브룩 집안 상속자에게 천한 피가 섞이는 일을 막기 위한 예방책으로 그 단호한 조치를 취해야 할 이유를 어느 때보다 강하게 느낀다는 뜻을 알리려고, 일부러 프레시트로 향했다.\n\n그러나 그날 아침 홀에서는 무언가 흥분되는 일이 벌어진 참이었다. 실리아에게 편지 한 통이 와 있었는데, 그녀는 그것을 읽으며 소리 없이 눈물을 흘렸다. 그녀가 우는 모습에 익숙하지 않던 제임스 경이 무슨 일이냐고 걱정스레 묻자, 그녀는 그가 여태 들어 본 적 없는 통곡을 터뜨렸다.\n\n“도러시아가 사내아이를 낳았어요. 그런데 당신은 내가 가서 그 애를 보지도 못하게 하잖아요. 분명 그 애도 나를 보고 싶어 할 거예요. 게다가 그 애는 아기를 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 모를 거예요. 잘못된 방식으로 다룰 거라고요. 다들 그 애가 죽을 줄 알았대요. 정말 끔찍해요! 만약 그게 나하고 어린 아서였는데, 도도가 나를 보러 오는 걸 막았다고 생각해 봐요! 당신이 좀 덜 매정했으면 좋겠어요, 제임스!”\n\n“맙소사, 실리아!” 몹시 마음이 동요된 제임스 경이 말했다. “당신은 무얼 바라는 거요? 당신이 원하는 건 뭐든 하리다. 당신이 원한다면 내일 당장 시내로 데려가겠소.” 그리고 실리아는 정말로 그것을 원했다.\n\n브룩 씨가 온 것은 그다음의 일이었는데, 정원에서 준남작을 만난 그는 그 소식을 모른 채 그와 잡담을 나누기 시작했다. 무슨 까닭에서인지 제임스 경은 그 소식을 즉시 알리고 싶어 하지 않았다. 그러나 으레 그러듯 한정상속 이야기가 나오자, 그는 말했다. “이보세요, 제가 어른께 이래라저래라 할 입장은 아닙니다만, 제 생각으로는 그 일은 그냥 내버려 두겠습니다. 일이 지금 이대로 있게 두겠어요.”\n\n브룩 씨는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 자신이 특별히 무언가를 해야 하리라는 기대를 받지 않게 되었다는 사실에 얼마나 안도하고 있는지를 단번에는 깨닫지 못했다.\n\n실리아의 마음이 그러한 쪽으로 기울어 있었으니, 제임스 경이 도러시아와 그 남편과의 화해에 동의하는 것은 필연적인 일이었다. 여인들끼리 서로 사랑하면, 남자들은 서로에 대한 반감을 억누르는 법을 배우게 된다. 제임스 경은 결코 래디슬로를 좋아하지 않았고, 윌은 늘 제임스 경과 함께 있을 때면 다른 부류의 사람도 섞여 있기를 원했다. 두 사람은 서로를 너그러이 참아 주는 사이였는데, 도러시아와 실리아가 함께 있을 때에야 비로소 그 관계가 아주 수월해졌다.\n\n래디슬로 부부가 한 해에 적어도 두 번은 그랜지를 방문한다는 것이 당연한 일로 여겨지게 되었고, 프레시트에는 차차 사촌들이 한 줄로 늘어나, 그들은 팁턴을 방문하는 두 사촌과 노는 것을, 마치 그 사촌들의 피가 그리 미심쩍게 섞이지 않은 것처럼 즐거워했다.\n\n브룩 씨는 천수를 누리며 살았고, 그의 사유지는 도러시아의 아들이 물려받았다. 그 아들은 미들마치를 대표하는 의원이 될 수도 있었으나, 자신이 바깥에 남아 있어야 자기 견해가 짓눌릴 가능성이 줄어든다고 여겨 사양했다.\n\n제임스 경은 도러시아의 두 번째 결혼을 잘못된 일로 여기기를 결코 그치지 않았다. 그리고 사실 그것은 미들마치에서 그 결혼에 관한 통설로 남았으니, 그곳에서 도러시아는 다음 세대 사람들에게 이렇게 이야기되었다. 자기 아버지뻘로 늙은 병약한 성직자와 결혼했던 어여쁜 처녀, 그가 죽은 지 일 년 남짓 만에 사유지를 포기하고 그의 사촌과 결혼해 버린 처녀라고. 그 사촌이란 그의 아들뻘로 젊고, 재산도 없으며, 가문도 변변치 않은 사람이었다. 도러시아를 전혀 본 적 없는 이들은 대개, 그녀가 “괜찮은 여자”였을 리 없다고들 말했다. 그렇지 않고서야 둘 중 어느 쪽과도 결혼하지 않았으리라는 것이었다.\n\n분명 그녀 생애의 그 결정적인 행위들은 이상적으로 아름답지는 않았다. 그것들은 불완전한 사회 상태의 조건들 한가운데서 분투하던 젊고 고결한 충동이 빚어낸 혼합된 결과였으니, 그러한 사회에서는 위대한 감정이 흔히 잘못의 모습을 띠고, 위대한 신앙이 환상의 모습을 띠곤 한다. 내면의 존재가 너무도 강해서 자기 바깥에 놓인 것에 크게 좌우되지 않을 만한 피조물은 없기 때문이다. 새로운 테레사가 수도원 생활을 개혁할 기회를 얻기는 어려울 것이고, 새로운 안티고네가 오라비의 매장을 위해 모든 것을 무릅쓰며 그 영웅적 신심을 다 바칠 일도 없을 것이다. 그들의 열렬한 행위가 형태를 빚어내던 그 매질(媒質)은 영영 사라져 버렸기 때문이다. 그러나 날마다의 말과 행위를 지닌 보잘것없는 우리는 수많은 도러시아의 삶을 마련해 가고 있으니, 그 가운데 어떤 삶은 우리가 그 이야기를 아는 도러시아의 희생보다 훨씬 더 슬픈 희생을 보여 줄지도 모른다.\n\n섬세하게 다듬어진 그녀의 정신은, 비록 널리 드러나지는 않았으나, 여전히 섬세한 결실을 맺었다. 키루스가 그 위세를 꺾어 놓았던 그 강물처럼, 그녀의 충만한 본성은 이 땅에서 이렇다 할 이름을 갖지 못한 물줄기들 속으로 자신을 쏟아부었다. 그러나 그녀의 존재가 주위 사람들에게 미친 영향은 헤아릴 수 없이 널리 퍼져 나갔다. 세상이 점점 더 나아지는 것은 부분적으로 역사에 남지 않은 행위들에 달려 있으며, 당신과 내가 처할 수도 있었던 것만큼 형편이 나쁘지 않은 것은, 절반은 충실하게 숨은 삶을 살다가 찾는 이 없는 무덤에 잠든 수많은 이들의 덕분이기 때문이다.\n끝";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-145"]=[[["Who that cares much to know the history of man, and how the mysterious mixture behaves under the varying experiments of Time, has not dwelt, at least briefly, on the life of Saint Theresa, has not smiled with some gentleness at the thought of the little girl walking forth one morning hand-in-hand with her still smaller brother, to go and seek martyrdom in the country of the Moors?", "인간의 역사에, 그리고 그 신비로운 혼합물이 시간의 다양한 실험 아래서 어떻게 행동하는지에 관심을 두는 사람이라면, 적어도 잠시나마 성녀 테레사의 생애에 마음을 머문 적이 없을까, 어느 날 아침 자기보다 더 작은 남동생의 손을 잡고 무어인의 땅으로 순교를 찾아 길을 나선 어린 소녀를 떠올리며 다소 부드럽게 미소 짓지 않은 사람이 있을까?"], ["Out they toddled from rugged Avila, wide-eyed and helpless-looking as two fawns, but with human hearts, already beating to a national idea; until domestic reality met them in the shape of uncles, and turned them back from their great resolve.", "두 아이는 험준한 아빌라에서 아장아장 걸어 나왔으니, 두 마리 새끼 사슴처럼 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 어찌할 바를 모르는 듯 보였지만, 이미 하나의 국가적 이상을 향해 고동치는 인간의 심장을 지니고 있었다. 그러다 삼촌들의 모습을 한 가정의 현실이 그들을 가로막아 그 위대한 결심으로부터 돌려세웠다."], ["That child-pilgrimage was a fit beginning.", "그 어린 시절의 순례는 어울리는 시작이었다."], ["Theresa’s passionate, ideal nature demanded an epic life: what were many-volumed romances of chivalry and the social conquests of a brilliant girl to her?", "테레사의 열정적이고 이상주의적인 천성은 서사시적인 삶을 요구했으니, 여러 권에 달하는 기사도 연애담이나 한 명석한 소녀가 거두는 사교계의 정복 따위가 그녀에게 무슨 의미가 있었겠는가?"], ["Her flame quickly burned up that light fuel; and, fed from within, soared after some illimitable satisfaction, some object which would never justify weariness, which would reconcile self-despair with the rapturous consciousness of life beyond self.", "그녀의 불꽃은 그런 가벼운 연료를 순식간에 태워 버렸고, 내부로부터 양분을 받아 어떤 무한한 만족을, 결코 권태를 정당화하지 않을 어떤 대상을, 자기 자신을 넘어선 삶의 황홀한 의식과 자기 절망을 화해시켜 줄 어떤 대상을 향해 솟아올랐다."], ["She found her epos in the reform of a religious order.", "그녀는 한 종교 교단의 개혁에서 자신의 서사시를 발견했다."]], [["That Spanish woman who lived three hundred years ago, was certainly not the last of her kind.", "삼백 년 전에 살았던 그 스페인 여인은 분명 자기 부류의 마지막은 아니었다. 많은 테레사들이 태어났으나, 그들은 멀리까지 울려 퍼지는 행위가 끊임없이 펼쳐지는 서사시적인 삶을 스스로 찾아내지 못했다."], ["Many Theresas have been born who found for themselves no epic life wherein there was a constant unfolding of far-resonant action; perhaps only a life of mistakes, the offspring of a certain spiritual grandeur ill-matched with the meanness of opportunity; perhaps a tragic failure which found no sacred poet and sank unwept into oblivion.", "어쩌면 그것은 단지 실수의 연속인 삶, 어떤 정신적 위대함이 기회의 보잘것없음과 어긋나게 짝지어진 데서 태어난 자식 같은 삶이었을 것이다. 어쩌면 거룩한 시인을 만나지 못한 채 아무도 울어 주지 않는 망각 속으로 가라앉아 버린 비극적 실패였을 것이다."], ["With dim lights and tangled circumstance they tried to shape their thought and deed in noble agreement; but after all, to common eyes their struggles seemed mere inconsistency and formlessness; for these later-born Theresas were helped by no coherent social faith and order which could perform the function of knowledge for the ardently willing soul.", "그들은 어슴푸레한 빛과 뒤엉킨 환경 속에서 자신의 생각과 행위를 고결한 조화 속에 빚어내려 애썼다. 하지만 결국 평범한 눈에는 그들의 분투가 한낱 일관성 없음과 형태 없음으로 보일 따름이었다."], ["Their ardor alternated between a vague ideal and the common yearning of womanhood; so that the one was disapproved as extravagance, and the other condemned as a lapse.", "후대에 태어난 이 테레사들은, 열렬히 의지하는 영혼을 위해 지식의 기능을 수행해 줄 수 있는 일관된 사회적 신앙과 질서의 도움을 전혀 받지 못했기 때문이다. 그들의 열정은 막연한 이상과 여성다움의 평범한 갈망 사이를 오갔으니, 그리하여 전자는 지나친 일탈로 못마땅하게 여겨졌고, 후자는 타락으로 단죄되었다."]], [["Some have felt that these blundering lives are due to the inconvenient indefiniteness with which the Supreme Power has fashioned the natures of women: if there were one level of feminine incompetence as strict as the ability to count three and no more, the social lot of women might be treated with scientific certitude.", "어떤 이들은 이렇게 더듬거리는 삶이 지고한 권능이 여성의 본성을 빚어낼 때 보인, 곤혹스러울 만큼 명확하지 못한 솜씨 탓이라고 느껴 왔다. 만약 셋까지 셀 수 있되 그 이상은 셀 수 없는 능력처럼 엄격하게 정해진 단 하나의 여성적 무능의 수준이 있다면, 여성의 사회적 운명도 과학적 확실성을 가지고 다룰 수 있으리라."], ["Meanwhile the indefiniteness remains, and the limits of variation are really much wider than any one would imagine from the sameness of women’s coiffure and the favorite love-stories in prose and verse.", "하지만 그동안에도 그 불명확함은 여전히 남아 있고, 그 변이의 한계는 사실 여성들의 한결같은 머리 모양이나 산문과 운문으로 즐겨 읽히는 연애담에서 누구든 상상할 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 넓다."], ["Here and there a cygnet is reared uneasily among the ducklings in the brown pond, and never finds the living stream in fellowship with its own oary-footed kind.", "여기저기서 새끼 백조 한 마리가 흙탕물 연못의 새끼 오리들 틈에서 불안하게 길러지지만, 끝내 제 발에 물갈퀴를 단 동족과 어울려 노니는 살아 흐르는 물줄기를 찾지 못한다."], ["Here and there is born a Saint Theresa, foundress of nothing, whose loving heart-beats and sobs after an unattained goodness tremble off and are dispersed among hindrances, instead of centring in some long-recognizable deed.", "여기저기서 아무것도 세우지 못한 창립자, 한 명의 성녀 테레사가 태어나지만, 이루지 못한 선을 향한 그녀의 사랑에 찬 심장의 고동과 흐느낌은 어떤 오래 기억될 행위로 모이지 못한 채, 갖가지 장애물 사이에서 떨리며 흩어져 버린다."]], [["# BOOK I. MISS BROOKE. CHAPTER I.", "# BOOK I. MISS BROOKE. CHAPTER I."]], [["Since I can do no good because a woman, Reach constantly at something that is near it.", "그 가까이에 있는 무언가에 끊임없이 손을 뻗네."], ["—The Maid’s Tragedy: BEAUMONT AND FLETCHER.", "— 『처녀의 비극』, 보몬트와 플레처"]], [["Miss Brooke had that kind of beauty which seems to be thrown into relief by poor dress.", "브룩 양은 초라한 옷차림 덕분에 도리어 돋보이는 듯한 종류의 아름다움을 지니고 있었다."], ["Her hand and wrist were so finely formed that she could wear sleeves not less bare of style than those in which the Blessed Virgin appeared to Italian painters; and her profile as well as her stature and bearing seemed to gain the more dignity from her plain garments, which by the side of provincial fashion gave her the impressiveness of a fine quotation from the Bible,—or from one of our elder poets,—in a paragraph of to-day’s newspaper.", "그녀의 손과 손목은 어찌나 곱게 빚어졌던지, 이탈리아 화가들이 그린 성모 마리아가 입은 것 못지않게 멋이라곤 없는 소매를 걸쳐도 좋을 정도였다. 그녀의 옆모습과 키, 자태 또한 수수한 옷차림에서 한층 더 기품을 얻는 것 같았으니, 지방의 유행과 나란히 놓이면 그 옷차림은 오늘 자 신문 한 문단 속에 인용된 성경의 한 구절—아니면 우리 선대 시인 가운데 한 사람의 한 구절—같은 인상적인 느낌을 그녀에게 주었다."], ["She was usually spoken of as being remarkably clever, but with the addition that her sister Celia had more common-sense.", "사람들은 흔히 그녀가 놀랄 만큼 영리하다고 말했지만, 동생 실리아가 분별력은 더 낫다는 말을 덧붙이곤 했다."], ["Nevertheless, Celia wore scarcely more trimmings; and it was only to close observers that her dress differed from her sister’s, and had a shade of coquetry in its arrangements; for Miss Brooke’s plain dressing was due to mixed conditions, in most of which her sister shared.", "그렇지만 실리아의 옷에도 장식은 별로 더 붙어 있지 않았으니, 그녀의 옷차림이 언니의 것과 다르고 그 매무새에 약간의 교태가 어려 있다는 사실은 눈여겨보는 이들에게만 보였다. 브룩 양의 수수한 옷차림은 여러 가지 사정에서 비롯된 것이었고, 그 사정의 대부분을 동생도 함께 나누고 있었기 때문이다."], ["The pride of being ladies had something to do with it: the Brooke connections, though not exactly aristocratic, were unquestionably “good:”", "숙녀라는 자부심도 거기에 한몫했다. 브룩 가문의 인척들은 정확히 귀족이랄 수는 없어도 의심할 바 없이 \"훌륭한\""], ["if you inquired backward for a generation or two, you would not find any yard-measuring or parcel-tying forefathers—anything lower than an admiral or a clergyman; and there was even an ancestor discernible as a Puritan gentleman who served under Cromwell, but afterwards conformed, and managed to come out of all political troubles as the proprietor of a respectable family estate.", "집안이었다. 한두 세대 거슬러 올라가 보아도 자를 들고 물건을 재거나 꾸러미를 묶던 조상은 찾아볼 수 없었으니, 제독이나 성직자보다 낮은 신분은 없었다."], ["Young women of such birth, living in a quiet country-house, and attending a village church hardly larger than a parlor, naturally regarded frippery as the ambition of a huckster’s daughter.", "심지어 크롬웰 휘하에서 복무했으나 나중에 국교에 순응하여 갖가지 정치적 풍파를 헤치고 어엿한 가문 사유지의 소유주로 빠져나온 청교도 신사 한 사람이 선조로 식별되기까지 했다. 이런 가문에서 태어나 조용한 시골 저택에 살며 응접실보다 별로 크지도 않은 마을 교회에 다니는 젊은 여성들이라면, 당연히 화려한 치장을 잡화상 딸의 야심쯤으로 여겼다."], ["Then there was well-bred economy, which in those days made show in dress the first item to be deducted from, when any margin was required for expenses more distinctive of rank.", "게다가 가문 좋은 집안의 검약 정신이라는 것이 있었으니, 그 시절에는 신분에 더 어울리는 지출을 위한 여유가 필요할 때면 옷치레가 가장 먼저 줄여야 할 항목이었다. 이런 이유들만으로도 종교적 감정과는 별개로 수수한 옷차림은 충분히 설명될 수 있었다."], ["Such reasons would have been enough to account for plain dress, quite apart from religious feeling; but in Miss Brooke’s case, religion alone would have determined it; and Celia mildly acquiesced in all her sister’s sentiments, only infusing them with that common-sense which is able to accept momentous doctrines without any eccentric agitation.", "하지만 브룩 양의 경우라면 종교만으로도 그 옷차림이 결정되었을 것이다. 실리아는 언니의 모든 정서를 온순하게 따랐으나, 다만 거기에 분별력을 불어넣었으니, 그 분별력이란 별난 동요 없이도 중대한 교리를 받아들일 줄 아는 것이었다."], ["Dorothea knew many passages of Pascal’s Pensees and of Jeremy Taylor by heart; and to her the destinies of mankind, seen by the light of Christianity, made the solicitudes of feminine fashion appear an occupation for Bedlam.", "도러시아는 파스칼의 『팡세』와 제러미 테일러의 글 가운데 여러 구절을 외고 있었다. 그녀에게는 기독교의 빛으로 바라본 인류의 운명 앞에서, 여성스러운 유행에 마음 쓰는 일이란 정신병원에나 어울리는 소일거리로 보였다."], ["She could not reconcile the anxieties of a spiritual life involving eternal consequences, with a keen interest in gimp and artificial protrusions of drapery.", "그녀는 영원한 결과를 초래하는 영적 삶의 근심을, 가두리 장식과 인공적으로 부풀린 옷자락에 대한 예리한 관심과 도무지 조화시킬 수 없었다. 그녀의 정신은 이론적이어서, 본성상 어떤 고결한 세계관을 갈망했으니, 그 세계관은 팁턴 교구와 그곳에서의 자기 행동 규범까지도 거리낌 없이 품을 수 있는 것이어야 했다."], ["Her mind was theoretic, and yearned by its nature after some lofty conception of the world which might frankly include the parish of Tipton and her own rule of conduct there; she was enamoured of intensity and greatness, and rash in embracing whatever seemed to her to have those aspects; likely to seek martyrdom, to make retractations, and then to incur martyrdom after all in a quarter where she had not sought it.", "그녀는 강렬함과 위대함에 매혹되어, 그런 면모를 지닌 듯 보이는 것이라면 무엇이든 무모하게 끌어안았다. 순교를 추구하다가, 그것을 철회하고, 끝내는 자기가 찾지도 않았던 곳에서 순교를 당하기 십상인 사람이었다."], ["Certainly such elements in the character of a marriageable girl tended to interfere with her lot, and hinder it from being decided according to custom, by good looks, vanity, and merely canine affection.", "결혼 적령기 처녀의 성격에 이런 요소들이 깃들어 있으면 분명 그녀의 운명에 지장을 주어, 미모와 허영심과 한낱 개 같은 애정에 따라 관습대로 정해지는 것을 가로막기 마련이었다. 이 모든 면모를 지녔으면서도, 자매 가운데 언니인 그녀는 아직 스무 살이 채 되지 않았다."], ["With all this, she, the elder of the sisters, was not yet twenty, and they had both been educated, since they were about twelve years old and had lost their parents, on plans at once narrow and promiscuous, first in an English family and afterwards in a Swiss family at Lausanne, their bachelor uncle and guardian trying in this way to remedy the disadvantages of their orphaned condition.", "두 사람은 열두 살 무렵 부모를 여읜 뒤로, 한편으로는 편협하고 한편으로는 두서없는 방침에 따라 교육을 받았으니, 처음에는 어느 영국 가정에서, 이후에는 로잔의 어느 스위스 가정에서였다. 독신인 숙부이자 후견인이 이런 식으로 고아가 된 처지의 불리함을 메워 주려 애쓴 것이었다."]], [["It was hardly a year since they had come to live at Tipton Grange with their uncle, a man nearly sixty, of acquiescent temper, miscellaneous opinions, and uncertain vote.", "자매가 숙부와 함께 팁턴 그레인지에 와서 산 지는 일 년이 채 되지 않았다. 숙부는 예순에 가까운 사람으로, 순응적인 기질에, 잡다한 의견을 지녔으며, 표를 어디에 던질지도 종잡을 수 없는 인물이었다."], ["He had travelled in his younger years, and was held in this part of the county to have contracted a too rambling habit of mind.", "그는 젊은 시절에 여행을 다녔던 까닭에, 이 지방에서는 지나치게 산만한 사고 습관이 몸에 밴 사람으로 여겨졌다."], ["Mr. Brooke’s conclusions were as difficult to predict as the weather: it was only safe to say that he would act with benevolent intentions, and that he would spend as little money as possible in carrying them out.", "브룩 씨의 결론은 날씨만큼이나 예측하기 어려웠으니, 그가 자비로운 의도로 행동하리라는 것, 그리고 그것을 실행에 옮기는 데에는 가능한 한 돈을 적게 쓰리라는 것만은 안심하고 말할 수 있었다."], ["For the most glutinously indefinite minds enclose some hard grains of habit; and a man has been seen lax about all his own interests except the retention of his snuff-box, concerning which he was watchful, suspicious, and greedy of clutch.", "아무리 끈적끈적하니 종잡을 수 없는 정신이라도 그 안에는 단단한 습관의 알갱이가 몇 알쯤 들어 있는 법이니, 자기 코담뱃갑을 지키는 일을 제외하고는 자신의 모든 이해관계에 무심하면서도 그 갑에 대해서만은 경계하고 의심하며 움켜쥐려 탐내는 사람도 본 적이 있다."]], [["In Mr. Brooke the hereditary strain of Puritan energy was clearly in abeyance; but in his niece Dorothea it glowed alike through faults and virtues, turning sometimes into impatience of her uncle’s talk or his way of “letting things be”", "브룩 씨에게는 청교도적 정력의 유전적 기질이 분명 잠들어 있었다."], ["on his estate, and making her long all the more for the time when she would be of age and have some command of money for generous schemes.", "하지만 조카 도러시아에게서는 그 기질이 결점과 미덕 양쪽으로 똑같이 타올라, 때로는 숙부의 이야기나 사유지를 \"되는대로 내버려 두는\""], ["She was regarded as an heiress; for not only had the sisters seven hundred a-year each from their parents, but if Dorothea married and had a son, that son would inherit Mr.", "방식에 대한 조바심으로 변하기도 했고, 또 자기가 성년이 되어 돈을 어느 정도 마음대로 쓰며 너그러운 계획들을 펼칠 수 있게 될 날을 한층 더 갈망하게 만들기도 했다."], ["Brooke’s estate, presumably worth about three thousand a-year—a rental which seemed wealth to provincial families, still discussing Mr.", "그녀는 상속녀로 여겨졌으니, 자매가 각각 부모에게서 연간 칠백 파운드씩을 받을 뿐 아니라, 만약 도러시아가 결혼해 아들을 낳으면 그 아들이 브룩 씨의 사유지를 물려받을 것이었기 때문이다."], ["Peel’s late conduct on the Catholic question, innocent of future gold-fields, and of that gorgeous plutocracy which has so nobly exalted the necessities of genteel life.", "그 사유지는 연간 삼천 파운드쯤의 가치가 있는 것으로 짐작되었는데, 그런 지대 수입은 지방 가문들에게는 부유함으로 보였으니, 그들은 아직도 가톨릭 문제에 대한 필 씨의 근래 처신을 논하면서, 장차 닥칠 금광에 대해서도, 또한 점잖은 생활의 필수품을 그토록 고귀하게 드높여 놓은 저 휘황찬란한 금권 계층에 대해서도 까맣게 모르고 있었다."]], [["And how should Dorothea not marry?—a girl so handsome and with such prospects?", "그러니 도러시아가 어찌 결혼하지 않을 수 있겠는가? 그토록 아름답고 그토록 전망이 밝은 처녀가?"], ["Nothing could hinder it but her love of extremes, and her insistence on regulating life according to notions which might cause a wary man to hesitate before he made her an offer, or even might lead her at last to refuse all offers.", "그 무엇도 그것을 막을 수 없었으니, 다만 극단을 좋아하는 그녀의 성향과, 신중한 남자라면 청혼을 하기 전에 망설이게 만들 법한, 아니 끝내 그녀로 하여금 모든 청혼을 거절하게 만들지도 모를 그런 관념들에 따라 삶을 다스리겠다는 고집만은 예외였다."], ["A young lady of some birth and fortune, who knelt suddenly down on a brick floor by the side of a sick laborer and prayed fervidly as if she thought herself living in the time of the Apostles—who had strange whims of fasting like a Papist, and of sitting up at night to read old theological books!", "좋은 가문과 재산을 갖춘 젊은 숙녀가 병든 일꾼 곁의 벽돌 바닥에 느닷없이 무릎을 꿇고, 마치 자기가 사도들의 시대에 살고 있다고 여기기라도 하듯 열렬히 기도를 올린다니. 천주교도처럼 단식을 한다는 둥, 밤을 새워 낡은 신학 서적을 읽는다는 둥, 이상한 변덕을 부리는 처녀라니!"], ["Such a wife might awaken you some fine morning with a new scheme for the application of her income which would interfere with political economy and the keeping of saddle-horses: a man would naturally think twice before he risked himself in such fellowship.", "그런 아내라면 어느 화창한 아침에 당신을 깨워 자기 수입의 사용처에 관한 새로운 계획을 들이밀어, 정치경제학과 승마용 말 키우는 일에 지장을 줄지도 모른다. 그러니 남자라면 당연히 그런 동반자 관계에 자신을 내맡기기 전에 두 번은 생각해 볼 일이다."], ["Women were expected to have weak opinions; but the great safeguard of society and of domestic life was, that opinions were not acted on.", "여자란 의견이 약하리라고 으레 기대되었지만, 사회와 가정생활을 지키는 큰 안전판은 그 의견이 실행에 옮겨지지 않는다는 데 있었다."], ["Sane people did what their neighbors did, so that if any lunatics were at large, one might know and avoid them.", "제정신인 사람들은 이웃이 하는 대로 했으니, 그래야 행여 미치광이가 돌아다닌다 해도 그가 누구인지 알아보고 피할 수 있는 것이었다."]], [["The rural opinion about the new young ladies, even among the cottagers, was generally in favor of Celia, as being so amiable and innocent-looking, while Miss Brooke’s large eyes seemed, like her religion, too unusual and striking.", "새로 온 두 아가씨에 대한 시골 사람들의 의견은, 심지어 오두막에 사는 이들 사이에서도, 대체로 실리아 쪽이 우세했으니, 그녀가 무척 상냥하고 천진해 보였기 때문이다. 반면 브룩 양의 커다란 눈은 그녀의 신앙과 마찬가지로 너무 별나고 강렬해 보였다."], ["Poor Dorothea!", "가엾은 도러시아!"], ["compared with her, the innocent-looking Celia was knowing and worldly-wise; so much subtler is a human mind than the outside tissues which make a sort of blazonry or clock-face for it.", "그녀에 비하면 천진해 보이는 실리아가 오히려 영악하고 세상 물정에 밝은 셈이었다. 인간의 정신이란 그것의 일종의 문장(紋章)이나 시계 문자판 노릇을 하는 겉껍질보다 그토록 미묘한 법이다."]], [["Yet those who approached Dorothea, though prejudiced against her by this alarming hearsay, found that she had a charm unaccountably reconcilable with it.", "그렇지만 이 섬뜩한 풍문 탓에 그녀에게 편견을 품고 다가간 이들조차도, 막상 그 풍문과 어찌 된 영문인지 어우러지는 매력을 그녀에게서 발견하곤 했다."], ["Most men thought her bewitching when she was on horseback.", "대다수 남자들은 그녀가 말을 탄 모습을 보면 넋을 잃었다."], ["She loved the fresh air and the various aspects of the country, and when her eyes and cheeks glowed with mingled pleasure she looked very little like a devotee.", "그녀는 신선한 공기와 시골의 다채로운 풍경을 사랑했으니, 두 눈과 두 뺨이 뒤섞인 기쁨으로 빛날 때면 신앙에 헌신하는 사람으로는 도무지 보이지 않았다."], ["Riding was an indulgence which she allowed herself in spite of conscientious qualms; she felt that she enjoyed it in a pagan sensuous way, and always looked forward to renouncing it.", "승마는 그녀가 양심의 가책을 무릅쓰고 자신에게 허락한 즐거움이었다. 그녀는 자기가 그것을 이교도처럼 관능적인 방식으로 즐긴다고 느꼈고, 늘 그것을 단념할 날을 고대했다."]], [["She was open, ardent, and not in the least self-admiring; indeed, it was pretty to see how her imagination adorned her sister Celia with attractions altogether superior to her own, and if any gentleman appeared to come to the Grange from some other motive than that of seeing Mr.", "그녀는 솔직하고 열정적이며 조금도 자기를 우러러보지 않았다. 실제로 그녀의 상상이 동생 실리아를 자기보다 한결 뛰어난 매력으로 꾸며 놓는 모습은 보기에 사랑스러웠다."], ["Brooke, she concluded that he must be in love with Celia: Sir James Chettam, for example, whom she constantly considered from Celia’s point of view, inwardly debating whether it would be good for Celia to accept him.", "또 어느 신사가 브룩 씨를 만나는 것 말고 다른 동기로 그레인지를 찾는 듯하면, 그녀는 그가 분명 실리아를 사랑하는 것이라고 결론지었다. 이를테면 제임스 체텀 경이 그러했으니, 그녀는 늘 실리아의 관점에서 그를 헤아리며, 실리아가 그의 청혼을 받아들이는 것이 과연 좋을지 속으로 따져 보곤 했다."], ["That he should be regarded as a suitor to herself would have seemed to her a ridiculous irrelevance.", "그가 자기 자신에게 구혼하는 사람으로 여겨지리라는 생각은 그녀에게는 우스꽝스러울 만큼 엉뚱한 일로 보였을 것이다."], ["Dorothea, with all her eagerness to know the truths of life, retained very childlike ideas about marriage.", "도러시아는 삶의 진리를 알고자 그토록 열망하면서도, 결혼에 대해서는 무척이나 어린아이 같은 관념을 지니고 있었다."], ["She felt sure that she would have accepted the judicious Hooker, if she had been born in time to save him from that wretched mistake he made in matrimony; or John Milton when his blindness had come on; or any of the other great men whose odd habits it would have been glorious piety to endure; but an amiable handsome baronet, who said “Exactly”", "그녀는 만약 자기가 제때 태어나 분별 있는 후커를 그 결혼이라는 비참한 실수에서 구할 수 있었더라면 그를 받아들였으리라고 확신했다. 아니면 실명이 찾아온 존 밀턴을, 아니면 그 기이한 습관을 견뎌 내는 것이 영광스러운 신심이 되었을 다른 위인들 가운데 누구라도 받아들였으리라고."], ["to her remarks even when she expressed uncertainty,—how could he affect her as a lover?", "하지만 그녀가 불확실함을 내비칠 때조차 그녀의 말에 \"지당하신 말씀입니다\"라고 답하는 상냥하고 잘생긴 준남작이라니, 그런 사람이 어떻게 그녀에게 연인으로서 마음을 움직일 수 있겠는가?"], ["The really delightful marriage must be that where your husband was a sort of father, and could teach you even Hebrew, if you wished it.", "정말로 흐뭇한 결혼이란, 남편이 일종의 아버지 같아서, 원한다면 히브리어까지도 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 그런 결혼이어야 했다."]], [["These peculiarities of Dorothea’s character caused Mr. Brooke to be all the more blamed in neighboring families for not securing some middle-aged lady as guide and companion to his nieces.", "도러시아의 성격에 깃든 이런 별난 점들 때문에, 브룩 씨는 조카들의 길잡이이자 동무가 되어 줄 중년 부인을 들이지 않았다는 이유로 이웃 가문들로부터 한층 더 비난을 받았다."], ["But he himself dreaded so much the sort of superior woman likely to be available for such a position, that he allowed himself to be dissuaded by Dorothea’s objections, and was in this case brave enough to defy the world—that is to say, Mrs.", "하지만 정작 그 자신은 그런 자리에 들일 만한 종류의 잘난 여인을 몹시 두려워한 나머지, 도러시아의 반대에 못 이겨 단념하고 말았으니, 이 일에서만큼은 세상에 맞설 만큼 용감했다."], ["Cadwallader the Rector’s wife, and the small group of gentry with whom he visited in the northeast corner of Loamshire.", "다시 말해 교구 목사의 아내인 캐드월러더 부인과, 그가 롬셔 북동쪽 모퉁이에서 왕래하던 소수의 지주 무리에게 맞선 셈이었다."], ["So Miss Brooke presided in her uncle’s household, and did not at all dislike her new authority, with the homage that belonged to it.", "그리하여 브룩 양은 숙부의 집안을 도맡아 다스렸고, 거기에 따르는 존경과 더불어 자신의 새로운 권한을 조금도 싫어하지 않았다."]], [["Sir James Chettam was going to dine at the Grange to-day with another gentleman whom the girls had never seen, and about whom Dorothea felt some venerating expectation.", "제임스 체텀 경은 오늘 그레인지에서 만찬을 들기로 되어 있었는데, 자매가 한 번도 본 적 없는 또 다른 신사도 함께였으니, 도러시아는 그 사람에 대해 어떤 경외에 찬 기대를 품고 있었다."], ["This was the Reverend Edward Casaubon, noted in the county as a man of profound learning, understood for many years to be engaged on a great work concerning religious history; also as a man of wealth enough to give lustre to his piety, and having views of his own which were to be more clearly ascertained on the publication of his book.", "그가 바로 에드워드 캐소본 목사로, 이 지방에서 심오한 학식을 지닌 사람으로 이름이 높았으며, 여러 해 동안 종교사에 관한 위대한 저작에 매달려 있다고 알려진 인물이었다. 또한 그 신심에 광채를 더해 줄 만큼 재산이 넉넉한 사람이었으며, 자신의 견해를 지니고 있어 그 책이 출간되면 그 견해가 한층 분명히 드러나리라고 했다."], ["His very name carried an impressiveness hardly to be measured without a precise chronology of scholarship.", "그의 이름 자체가, 학문의 정확한 연대기 없이는 도무지 가늠할 수 없을 만큼 묵직한 인상을 풍겼다."]], [["Early in the day Dorothea had returned from the infant school which she had set going in the village, and was taking her usual place in the pretty sitting-room which divided the bedrooms of the sisters, bent on finishing a plan for some buildings (a kind of work which she delighted in), when Celia, who had been watching her with a hesitating desire to propose something, said—", "그날 일찍 도러시아는 자기가 마을에 세워 운영하던 유아 학교에서 돌아와, 자매의 침실 사이에 자리한 아담한 거실에서 늘 앉던 자리에 앉아, 어떤 건물의 설계도를 마무리하는 데 몰두해 있었다(그녀가 즐기는 종류의 일이었다). 그때 무언가를 제안하고 싶어 망설이며 그녀를 지켜보던 실리아가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Dorothea, dear, if you don’t mind—if you are not very busy—suppose we looked at mamma’s jewels to-day, and divided them?", "\"언니, 괜찮으면—그러니까 별로 바쁘지 않으면—오늘 엄마 보석을 꺼내 보고, 나눠 가지면 어떨까?"], ["It is exactly six months to-day since uncle gave them to you, and you have not looked at them yet.”", "숙부님이 그걸 언니한테 주신 지 오늘로 꼭 여섯 달이 됐는데, 언니는 아직 한 번도 들여다보지 않았잖아.\""]], [["Celia’s face had the shadow of a pouting expression in it, the full presence of the pout being kept back by an habitual awe of Dorothea and principle; two associated facts which might show a mysterious electricity if you touched them incautiously.", "실리아의 얼굴에는 입을 삐죽거리는 듯한 표정의 그림자가 어려 있었으나, 그것이 온전히 드러나지 못한 것은 도러시아에 대한 습관적인 경외와 신념 때문이었다. 이 둘은 함께 얽힌 사실이어서, 자칫 조심성 없이 건드리면 어떤 신비로운 전류를 내비칠지도 몰랐다."], ["To her relief, Dorothea’s eyes were full of laughter as she looked up.", "다행스럽게도 도러시아가 고개를 들었을 때 그 눈에는 웃음이 가득했다."]], [["“What a wonderful little almanac you are, Celia!", "\"넌 정말 놀라운 꼬마 달력이로구나, 실리아!"], ["Is it six calendar or six lunar months?”", "그게 양력으로 여섯 달이니, 아니면 음력으로 여섯 달이니?\""]], [["“It is the last day of September now, and it was the first of April when uncle gave them to you.", "\"지금이 9월 마지막 날이고, 숙부님이 언니한테 그걸 주신 게 4월 초하루였잖아."], ["You know, he said that he had forgotten them till then.", "그날까지 잊고 있었다고 숙부님이 말씀하신 거 기억하지."], ["I believe you have never thought of them since you locked them up in the cabinet here.”", "언니는 여기 장식장에 넣고 잠가 둔 뒤로 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없을걸.\""]], [["“Well, dear, we should never wear them, you know.”", "\"글쎄다, 얘, 우리가 그걸 차고 다닐 일이야 없잖니.\""], ["Dorothea spoke in a full cordial tone, half caressing, half explanatory.", "도러시아는 반은 어르듯, 반은 설명하듯, 다정함이 가득한 목소리로 말했다."], ["She had her pencil in her hand, and was making tiny side-plans on a margin.", "손에는 연필을 쥐고서, 여백에 자그마한 곁그림들을 그리고 있었다."]], [["Celia colored, and looked very grave.", "실리아는 얼굴을 붉히고 무척 진지한 표정을 지었다."], ["“I think, dear, we are wanting in respect to mamma’s memory, to put them by and take no notice of them.", "\"내 생각엔, 언니, 우리가 그걸 치워 두고 거들떠보지도 않는 건 엄마를 추억하는 마음에 소홀한 거야."], ["And,”", "그리고,\""], ["she added, after hesitating a little, with a rising sob of mortification, “necklaces are quite usual now; and Madame Poincon, who was stricter in some things even than you are, used to wear ornaments.", "그녀는 잠시 머뭇거리다가, 분한 마음에 울먹임이 차오르는 채로 덧붙였다. \"요즘은 목걸이를 흔히들 하잖아."], ["And Christians generally—surely there are women in heaven now who wore jewels.”", "그리고 어떤 일에서는 언니보다도 더 엄격했던 푸앵송 부인도 장신구를 차곤 했어."], ["Celia was conscious of some mental strength when she really applied herself to argument.", "게다가 기독교인들도 으레—분명 지금 천국에도 보석을 찼던 여인들이 있을 거야.\" 실리아는 정말로 작정하고 따지고 들 때면 자기에게 어떤 지적인 힘이 있음을 의식했다."]], [["“You would like to wear them?”", "\"넌 그걸 차고 싶은 거니?\""], ["exclaimed Dorothea, an air of astonished discovery animating her whole person with a dramatic action which she had caught from that very Madame Poincon who wore the ornaments.", "도러시아가 외쳤다. 놀라운 발견을 한 듯한 기색이 그녀의 온몸에 생기를 불어넣었으니, 그 극적인 몸짓은 바로 그 장신구를 차고 다녔던 푸앵송 부인에게서 배운 것이었다."], ["“Of course, then, let us have them out.", "\"그렇다면야 당연히 꺼내 봐야지."], ["Why did you not tell me before?", "왜 진작 말하지 않았어?"], ["But the keys, the keys!”", "그런데 열쇠, 열쇠가!\""], ["She pressed her hands against the sides of her head and seemed to despair of her memory.", "그녀는 두 손을 머리 양옆에 갖다 대며 자기 기억력에 절망하는 듯한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“They are here,”", "\"여기 있어.\""], ["said Celia, with whom this explanation had been long meditated and prearranged.", "실리아가 말했다. 이 설명만큼은 그녀가 오랫동안 곱씹으며 미리 준비해 둔 것이었다."]], [["“Pray open the large drawer of the cabinet and get out the jewel-box.”", "\"장식장의 큰 서랍을 열고 보석 상자를 꺼내 줘.\""]], [["The casket was soon open before them, and the various jewels spread out, making a bright parterre on the table.", "이내 작은 상자가 그들 앞에 열렸고, 갖가지 보석이 펼쳐져 식탁 위에 화사한 화단을 이루었다."], ["It was no great collection, but a few of the ornaments were really of remarkable beauty, the finest that was obvious at first being a necklace of purple amethysts set in exquisite gold work, and a pearl cross with five brilliants in it.", "그리 많은 수집품은 아니었지만, 장신구 가운데 몇몇은 정말로 빼어나게 아름다웠으니, 처음에 한눈에 드러나는 가장 훌륭한 것은 정교한 금세공에 박힌 자줏빛 자수정 목걸이와, 다섯 개의 작은 다이아몬드가 박힌 진주 십자가였다."], ["Dorothea immediately took up the necklace and fastened it round her sister’s neck, where it fitted almost as closely as a bracelet; but the circle suited the Henrietta-Maria style of Celia’s head and neck, and she could see that it did, in the pier-glass opposite.", "도러시아는 곧바로 목걸이를 집어 들어 동생의 목에 걸어 주었는데, 거의 팔찌만큼이나 꼭 맞게 둘러졌다. 그 둥근 모양은 실리아의 머리와 목이 지닌 헨리에타 마리아풍 분위기에 잘 어울렸으니, 맞은편 큰 거울로 그녀 스스로도 그것이 어울린다는 것을 알 수 있었다."]], [["“There, Celia!", "\"자, 실리아!"], ["you can wear that with your Indian muslin.", "이건 네 인도 모슬린 옷에 차면 되겠다."], ["But this cross you must wear with your dark dresses.”", "그런데 이 십자가는 너의 짙은 색 드레스에 차야 해.\""]], [["Celia was trying not to smile with pleasure.", "실리아는 기쁨에 겨워 새어 나오려는 미소를 애써 참았다."], ["“O Dodo, you must keep the cross yourself.”", "\"어머, 도도 언니, 십자가는 언니가 가져야 해.\""]], [["“No, no, dear, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야, 얘, 안 돼.\""], ["said Dorothea, putting up her hand with careless deprecation.", "도러시아가 무심하게 사양하며 손을 들어 올렸다."]], [["“Yes, indeed you must; it would suit you—in your black dress, now,”", "\"아니, 정말 언니가 가져야 해. 언니한테 어울릴 거야—이제 언니의 검은 드레스에 말이야.\""], ["said Celia, insistingly.", "실리아가 고집스레 말했다."], ["“You might wear that.”", "\"언니라면 그건 차도 되잖아.\""]], [["“Not for the world, not for the world.", "\"절대로, 절대로 안 돼."], ["A cross is the last thing I would wear as a trinket.”", "십자가야말로 내가 노리개로 차고 다닐 마지막 물건이야.\""], ["Dorothea shuddered slightly.", "도러시아는 살짝 몸서리를 쳤다."]], [["“Then you will think it wicked in me to wear it,”", "\"그럼 언니는 내가 그걸 차면 사악하다고 여기겠네.\""], ["said Celia, uneasily.", "실리아가 불안한 듯 말했다."]], [["“No, dear, no,”", "\"아니야, 얘, 그렇지 않아.\""], ["said Dorothea, stroking her sister’s cheek.", "도러시아가 동생의 뺨을 어루만지며 말했다."], ["“Souls have complexions too: what will suit one will not suit another.”", "\"영혼에도 저마다 어울리는 빛깔이 있는 법이야. 어떤 사람에게 어울리는 게 다른 사람에게는 어울리지 않거든.\""]], [["“But you might like to keep it for mamma’s sake.”", "\"하지만 언니도 엄마를 생각해서 그걸 간직하고 싶을지 모르잖아.\""]], [["“No, I have other things of mamma’s—her sandal-wood box which I am so fond of—plenty of things.", "\"아니야, 난 엄마의 다른 물건들이 있어—내가 그토록 아끼는 백단향 상자며, 그밖에도 많아."], ["In fact, they are all yours, dear.", "사실 이건 다 네 거야, 얘."], ["We need discuss them no longer.", "더 이상 이걸로 왈가왈부할 것 없어."], ["There—take away your property.”", "자, 네 것을 챙겨 가렴.\""]], [["Celia felt a little hurt.", "실리아는 마음이 조금 상했다."], ["There was a strong assumption of superiority in this Puritanic toleration, hardly less trying to the blond flesh of an unenthusiastic sister than a Puritanic persecution.", "이 청교도적인 너그러움 속에는 우월함을 강하게 내세우는 데가 있었으니, 그것은 그다지 열성적이지 못한 동생의 금빛 살결에는 청교도적인 박해 못지않게 견디기 힘든 것이었다."]], [["“But how can I wear ornaments if you, who are the elder sister, will never wear them?”", "\"하지만 언니가, 언니가 손위인데도 절대로 장신구를 차지 않으면, 내가 어떻게 그걸 차겠어?\""]], [["“Nay, Celia, that is too much to ask, that I should wear trinkets to keep you in countenance.", "\"얘, 실리아, 너를 거들어 주려고 내가 노리개를 차야 한다니, 그건 너무 무리한 부탁이야."], ["If I were to put on such a necklace as that, I should feel as if I had been pirouetting.", "만약 내가 그런 목걸이를 차고 다닌다면, 마치 발끝으로 빙글빙글 돌고 있는 것 같은 기분일 거야."], ["The world would go round with me, and I should not know how to walk.”", "세상이 빙빙 돌아서, 어떻게 걸어야 할지도 모를걸.\""]], [["Celia had unclasped the necklace and drawn it off.", "실리아는 목걸이의 잠금쇠를 풀어 벗겨 냈다."], ["“It would be a little tight for your neck; something to lie down and hang would suit you better,”", "\"이건 언니 목에는 좀 조일 거야. 언니한테는 길게 늘어뜨려 드리워지는 게 더 어울려.\""], ["she said, with some satisfaction.", "그녀가 다소 흡족한 듯 말했다."], ["The complete unfitness of the necklace from all points of view for Dorothea, made Celia happier in taking it.", "그 목걸이가 어느 모로 보나 도러시아에게는 도무지 어울리지 않는다는 사실이, 그것을 차지하는 실리아를 한결 즐겁게 했다."], ["She was opening some ring-boxes, which disclosed a fine emerald with diamonds, and just then the sun passing beyond a cloud sent a bright gleam over the table.", "그녀가 반지 상자 몇 개를 열자 다이아몬드가 박힌 고운 에메랄드가 드러났는데, 바로 그때 구름을 벗어난 해가 식탁 위로 환한 빛줄기를 보냈다."]], [["“How very beautiful these gems are!”", "\"이 보석들 정말 아름답구나!\""], ["said Dorothea, under a new current of feeling, as sudden as the gleam.", "그 빛줄기처럼 갑작스레 솟구친 새로운 감정에 휩싸여 도러시아가 말했다."], ["“It is strange how deeply colors seem to penetrate one, like scent.", "\"빛깔이라는 게 마치 향기처럼 그토록 깊이 사람한테 스며드는 것이 신기해."], ["I suppose that is the reason why gems are used as spiritual emblems in the Revelation of St. John.", "그래서 「요한 계시록」에서 보석을 영적인 상징으로 쓴 게 아닐까 싶어."], ["They look like fragments of heaven.", "보석은 천국의 조각들 같아."], ["I think that emerald is more beautiful than any of them.”", "내 생각엔 저 에메랄드가 그 가운데 어느 것보다도 아름다운 것 같아.\""]], [["“And there is a bracelet to match it,”", "\"그리고 거기 어울리는 팔찌도 있어.\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“We did not notice this at first.”", "\"처음엔 우리가 이걸 못 봤네.\""]], [["“They are lovely,”", "\"정말 사랑스러워.\""], ["said Dorothea, slipping the ring and bracelet on her finely turned finger and wrist, and holding them towards the window on a level with her eyes.", "도러시아가 반지와 팔찌를 곱게 빠진 손가락과 손목에 끼우고 차고는, 눈높이로 들어 창 쪽을 향해 비춰 보며 말했다."], ["All the while her thought was trying to justify her delight in the colors by merging them in her mystic religious joy.", "그러는 내내 그녀의 생각은, 그 빛깔에 대한 자기 기쁨을 신비로운 종교적 환희 속에 녹여 넣음으로써 정당화하려 애쓰고 있었다."]], [["“You would like those, Dorothea,”", "\"언니도 그건 마음에 드는구나.\""], ["said Celia, rather falteringly, beginning to think with wonder that her sister showed some weakness, and also that emeralds would suit her own complexion even better than purple amethysts.", "실리아가 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다. 그녀는 언니가 어떤 약점을 드러낸다는 것에 놀라워하기 시작했고, 또 에메랄드가 자줏빛 자수정보다도 자기 살결에 더 잘 어울리리라는 생각도 들었다."], ["“You must keep that ring and bracelet—if nothing else.", "\"그 반지와 팔찌는 언니가 가져—다른 건 다 그만두더라도."], ["But see, these agates are very pretty and quiet.”", "그런데 봐, 이 마노들도 참 곱고 차분하다.\""]], [["“Yes!", "\"그래!"], ["I will keep these—this ring and bracelet,”", "이건 내가 가질게—이 반지와 팔찌 말이야.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["Then, letting her hand fall on the table, she said in another tone—“Yet what miserable men find such things, and work at them, and sell them!”", "그러다 손을 식탁 위에 떨어뜨리며, 다른 어조로 말했다. \"하지만 얼마나 비참한 사람들이 이런 것을 찾아내고, 다듬고, 또 팔고 있을까!\""], ["She paused again, and Celia thought that her sister was going to renounce the ornaments, as in consistency she ought to do.", "그녀가 다시 말을 멈추자, 실리아는 언니가 일관성을 지키려면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 장신구를 단념하려는 것이라고 생각했다."]], [["“Yes, dear, I will keep these,”", "\"그래, 얘, 이건 내가 가질게.\""], ["said Dorothea, decidedly.", "도러시아가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“But take all the rest away, and the casket.”", "\"하지만 나머지는 다 가져가고, 상자도 가져가렴.\""]], [["She took up her pencil without removing the jewels, and still looking at them.", "그녀는 보석을 치우지 않은 채, 여전히 그것을 바라보며 연필을 다시 집어 들었다."], ["She thought of often having them by her, to feed her eye at these little fountains of pure color.", "그것을 자주 곁에 두고서, 이 자그마한 순수한 빛깔의 샘들로 눈을 적시는 모습을 그려 보았다."]], [["“Shall you wear them in company?”", "\"이걸 사람들 앞에서 차고 다닐 거야?\""], ["said Celia, who was watching her with real curiosity as to what she would do.", "실리아가 언니가 어떻게 할지 정말로 궁금해하며 지켜보다가 물었다."]], [["Dorothea glanced quickly at her sister.", "도러시아가 동생을 휙 돌아보았다."], ["Across all her imaginative adornment of those whom she loved, there darted now and then a keen discernment, which was not without a scorching quality.", "자기가 사랑하는 이들을 상상으로 꾸며 주는 그 모든 마음을 가로질러, 이따금 예리한 통찰이 번뜩였으니, 거기에는 사람을 그슬릴 듯한 데가 없지 않았다."], ["If Miss Brooke ever attained perfect meekness, it would not be for lack of inward fire.", "만약 브룩 양이 언젠가 완벽한 온순함에 이른다면, 그것은 내면의 불길이 모자라서는 결코 아닐 것이었다."]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면.\""], ["she said, rather haughtily.", "그녀가 다소 도도하게 말했다."], ["“I cannot tell to what level I may sink.”", "\"내가 어느 수준까지 추락할지는 나도 모르겠어.\""]], [["Celia blushed, and was unhappy: she saw that she had offended her sister, and dared not say even anything pretty about the gift of the ornaments which she put back into the box and carried away.", "실리아는 얼굴을 붉히며 마음이 언짢아졌다. 자기가 언니의 기분을 상하게 했음을 알아챈 것이다."], ["Dorothea too was unhappy, as she went on with her plan-drawing, questioning the purity of her own feeling and speech in the scene which had ended with that little explosion.", "그래서 장신구라는 선물에 대해 듣기 좋은 말 한마디조차 감히 꺼내지 못한 채, 그것을 다시 상자에 넣어 들고 나갔다. 도러시아 역시 마음이 언짢았으니, 설계도를 계속 그리면서, 그 작은 폭발로 끝나 버린 장면에서 자신의 감정과 말이 과연 순수했는지를 곱씹었다."]], [["Celia’s consciousness told her that she had not been at all in the wrong: it was quite natural and justifiable that she should have asked that question, and she repeated to herself that Dorothea was inconsistent: either she should have taken her full share of the jewels, or, after what she had said, she should have renounced them altogether.", "실리아의 양심은 자기가 조금도 잘못하지 않았다고 일러 주었다. 그런 질문을 한 것은 지극히 당연하고 정당한 일이었다고. 그러고는 도러시아야말로 일관성이 없다고 속으로 되뇌었다. 보석을 자기 몫만큼 다 가졌어야 하든지, 아니면 그렇게 말한 이상 아예 전부 단념했어야 했다고."]], [["“I am sure—at least, I trust,”", "\"분명히—적어도, 난 그렇게 믿어.\""], ["thought Celia, “that the wearing of a necklace will not interfere with my prayers.", "실리아는 생각했다. \"목걸이를 찬다고 해서 내 기도에 지장이 생기지는 않을 거야."], ["And I do not see that I should be bound by Dorothea’s opinions now we are going into society, though of course she herself ought to be bound by them.", "그리고 이제 우리가 사교계에 나가는 마당에, 내가 도러시아 언니의 의견에 매여야 할 이유는 모르겠어. 물론 언니 자신이야 당연히 그 의견에 매여야겠지만 말이야."], ["But Dorothea is not always consistent.”", "하지만 도러시아 언니는 늘 한결같은 건 아니거든.\""]], [["Thus Celia, mutely bending over her tapestry, until she heard her sister calling her.", "이렇게 실리아는 잠자코 자수에 몸을 숙이고 있었다. 언니가 부르는 소리가 들릴 때까지."]], [["“Here, Kitty, come and look at my plan; I shall think I am a great architect, if I have not got incompatible stairs and fireplaces.”", "\"이리 와, 키티, 와서 내 설계도 좀 봐 줘. 계단이랑 벽난로가 서로 안 맞게 안 그려졌으면, 난 내가 대단한 건축가라고 여기게 될 거야.\""]], [["As Celia bent over the paper, Dorothea put her cheek against her sister’s arm caressingly.", "실리아가 종이 위로 몸을 숙이자, 도러시아는 동생의 팔에 다정하게 뺨을 갖다 댔다."], ["Celia understood the action.", "실리아는 그 몸짓을 이해했다."], ["Dorothea saw that she had been in the wrong, and Celia pardoned her.", "도러시아는 자기가 잘못했음을 알았고, 실리아는 언니를 용서했다."], ["Since they could remember, there had been a mixture of criticism and awe in the attitude of Celia’s mind towards her elder sister.", "기억하는 한 언제나, 손위 언니를 향한 실리아의 마음가짐에는 비판과 경외가 뒤섞여 있었다. 손아래 동생은 늘 멍에를 짊어지고 있었던 셈이다."], ["The younger had always worn a yoke; but is there any yoked creature without its private opinions?", "하지만 멍에를 진 짐승치고 제 나름의 속생각이 없는 것이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER II.", "# CHAPTER II."]], [["“‘Dime; no ves aquel caballero que hacia nosotros viene sobre un caballo rucio rodado que trae puesto en la cabeza un yelmo de oro?’ ‘Lo que veo y columbro,’ respondio Sancho, ‘no es sino un hombre sobre un as no pardo como el mio, que trae sobre la cabeza una cosa que relumbra.’ ‘Pues ese es el yelmo de Mambrino,’ dijo Don Quijote.”—CERVANTES.", "“‘Dime; no ves aquel caballero que hacia nosotros viene sobre un caballo rucio rodado que trae puesto en la cabeza un yelmo de oro?’ ‘Lo que veo y columbro,’ respondio Sancho, ‘no es sino un hombre sobre un as no pardo como el mio, que trae sobre la cabeza una cosa que relumbra.’ ‘Pues ese es el yelmo de Mambrino,’ dijo Don Quijote.”—세르반테스."]], [["“‘Seest thou not yon cavalier who cometh toward us on a dapple-gray steed, and weareth a golden helmet?’ ‘What I see,’ answered Sancho, ‘is nothing but a man on a gray ass like my own, who carries something shiny on his head.’ ‘Just so,’ answered Don Quixote: ‘and that resplendent object is the helmet of Mambrino.’”", "“‘저기 얼룩무늬 잿빛 준마를 타고 황금 투구를 머리에 쓴 채 우리를 향해 다가오는 기사가 보이지 않느냐?’ ‘제 눈에 보이는 것은,’ 산초가 대답했다. ‘제 것과 같은 잿빛 당나귀를 탄 한 사내가 머리에 뭔가 번쩍이는 것을 얹고 있는 것뿐인뎁쇼.’ ‘바로 그것이니라,’ 돈키호테가 대답했다. ‘그 찬란하게 빛나는 물건이 바로 맘브리노의 투구이니라.’”"]], [["“Sir Humphry Davy?” said Mr.", "“험프리 데이비 경 말입니까?”"], ["Brooke, over the soup, in his easy smiling way, taking up Sir James Chettam’s remark that he was studying Davy’s Agricultural Chemistry.", "자기가 데이비의 『농경 화학』을 공부하고 있다는 제임스 체텀 경의 말을 받아, 브룩 씨가 수프를 사이에 두고 느긋하게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“Well, now, Sir Humphry Davy; I dined with him years ago at Cartwright’s, and Wordsworth was there too—the poet Wordsworth, you know.", "“자, 그런데 험프리 데이비 경이라, 나도 여러 해 전에 카트라이트 댁에서 그 사람과 식사를 한 적이 있지, 그리고 워즈워스도 거기 있었어. 시인 워즈워스 말이야, 알 테지."], ["Now there was something singular.", "그런데 거기에 묘한 점이 있단 말이지."], ["I was at Cambridge when Wordsworth was there, and I never met him—and I dined with him twenty years afterwards at Cartwright’s.", "워즈워스가 케임브리지에 다닐 때 나도 거기 있었는데, 한 번도 그를 만난 적이 없었어. 그런데 이십 년이 지나 카트라이트 댁에서 그와 함께 식사를 한 거지."], ["There’s an oddity in things, now.", "세상일에는 참 이상한 데가 있어, 정말로."], ["But Davy was there: he was a poet too.", "아무튼 데이비도 거기 있었어. 그 사람도 시인이었지."], ["Or, as I may say, Wordsworth was poet one, and Davy was poet two.", "아니, 이렇게 말할 수도 있겠군. 워즈워스가 첫째 시인이고 데이비가 둘째 시인이었다고."], ["That was true in every sense, you know.”", "그게 모든 의미에서 사실이었어, 알 테지.”"]], [["Dorothea felt a little more uneasy than usual.", "도러시아는 평소보다 조금 더 불편함을 느꼈다."], ["In the beginning of dinner, the party being small and the room still, these motes from the mass of a magistrate’s mind fell too noticeably.", "식사가 시작될 무렵, 일행이 적고 방이 고요한 탓에 한 치안 판사의 정신이라는 덩어리에서 떨어져 나오는 이런 티끌들이 지나치게 두드러지게 들렸다."], ["She wondered how a man like Mr. Casaubon would support such triviality.", "그녀는 캐소본 씨 같은 사람이 어떻게 이런 사소함을 견딜 수 있을지 의아했다."], ["His manners, she thought, were very dignified; the set of his iron-gray hair and his deep eye-sockets made him resemble the portrait of Locke.", "그의 태도는 무척 품위 있어 보였다. 철회색 머리카락의 매무새와 깊이 팬 눈자위는 그를 로크의 초상화와 닮아 보이게 했다."], ["He had the spare form and the pale complexion which became a student; as different as possible from the blooming Englishman of the red-whiskered type represented by Sir James Chettam.", "그에게는 학자에게 어울리는 마른 체구와 창백한 안색이 있었으니, 제임스 체텀 경으로 대표되는 불그스레한 구레나룻에 혈색 좋은 영국 신사와는 더없이 대조적이었다."]], [["“I am reading the Agricultural Chemistry,”", "“제가 『농경 화학』을 읽고 있는 것은,”"], ["said this excellent baronet, “because I am going to take one of the farms into my own hands, and see if something cannot be done in setting a good pattern of farming among my tenants.", "이 훌륭한 준남작이 말했다. “농장 하나를 직접 맡아 경영하면서, 소작인들 사이에 좋은 영농의 본보기를 세우는 데 무언가 할 수 있는 일이 없을지 알아보려는 겁니다."], ["Do you approve of that, Miss Brooke?”", "그 일에 찬성하십니까, 브룩 양?”"]], [["“A great mistake, Chettam,”", "“그건 큰 실수일세, 체텀.”"], ["interposed Mr.", "브룩 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Brooke, “going into electrifying your land and that kind of thing, and making a parlor of your cow-house.", "“땅에 전기를 통하게 한다느니 그런 일에 손을 대고, 외양간을 응접실로 만드는 짓 말이야. 그래 봤자 소용없어."], ["It won’t do.", "나도 한때 과학에 꽤나 깊이 들어가 봤지만, 소용없다는 걸 알았지."], ["I went into science a great deal myself at one time; but I saw it would not do.", "그게 온갖 일로 이어지거든. 그 무엇 하나 가만 둘 수가 없게 돼."], ["It leads to everything; you can let nothing alone.", "아니, 아닐세. 소작인들이 짚을 내다 팔지 않도록 단속하고, 그런 류의 일이나 챙기게."], ["No, no—see that your tenants don’t sell their straw, and that kind of thing; and give them draining-tiles, you know.", "그리고 배수용 기와나 대주게나, 알겠나. 하지만 그런 멋부리는 영농은 소용없어."], ["But your fancy farming will not do—the most expensive sort of whistle you can buy: you may as well keep a pack of hounds.”", "살 수 있는 호각 중에서도 가장 비싼 호각이지. 차라리 사냥개 떼나 기르는 게 나을 걸세.”"]], [["“Surely,”", "“하지만 분명,”"], ["said Dorothea, “it is better to spend money in finding out how men can make the most of the land which supports them all, than in keeping dogs and horses only to gallop over it.", "도러시아가 말했다. “모두를 부양하는 땅을 사람들이 어떻게 하면 최대한 활용할 수 있을지 알아내는 데 돈을 쓰는 것이, 그저 그 위를 달리려고 개와 말을 기르는 것보다 낫지 않겠어요."], ["It is not a sin to make yourself poor in performing experiments for the good of all.”", "모두의 이익을 위해 실험을 하다가 가난해지는 것은 죄가 아니에요.”"]], [["She spoke with more energy than is expected of so young a lady, but Sir James had appealed to her.", "그녀는 그토록 어린 숙녀에게 기대되는 것보다 더 힘주어 말했지만, 제임스 경이 그녀에게 물었기 때문이었다."], ["He was accustomed to do so, and she had often thought that she could urge him to many good actions when he was her brother-in-law.", "그는 으레 그렇게 묻곤 했고, 그녀는 그가 형부가 되면 여러 선한 행동을 하도록 권할 수 있으리라고 종종 생각했다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon turned his eyes very markedly on Dorothea while she was speaking, and seemed to observe her newly.", "캐소본 씨는 그녀가 말하는 동안 도러시아에게 눈길을 매우 뚜렷이 돌렸고, 그녀를 새삼스레 살펴보는 것 같았다."]], [["“Young ladies don’t understand political economy, you know,” said Mr.", "“젊은 숙녀들은 정치 경제학을 이해하지 못하지요, 알 테지요.”"], ["Brooke, smiling towards Mr. Casaubon.", "브룩 씨가 캐소본 씨를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I remember when we were all reading Adam Smith.", "“우리 모두 애덤 스미스를 읽던 때가 생각나는군요."], ["There is a book, now.", "자, 그게 바로 책이라는 거지요."], ["I took in all the new ideas at one time—human perfectibility, now.", "나도 한때 새로운 사상이란 사상은 다 받아들였어요. 인간 완전성이라는 것 말이오."], ["But some say, history moves in circles; and that may be very well argued; I have argued it myself.", "하지만 어떤 이들은 역사가 순환한다고 하지요. 그것도 충분히 논할 만한 이야기고, 나 자신도 그렇게 논해 봤어요."], ["The fact is, human reason may carry you a little too far—over the hedge, in fact.", "사실은, 인간의 이성이라는 게 사람을 좀 지나치게 멀리 데려가기도 하지요. 실은 울타리를 넘어가도록 말이오."], ["It carried me a good way at one time; but I saw it would not do.", "한때는 나도 그게 나를 꽤 멀리 데려갔지만, 소용없다는 걸 알았어요. 멈춰 섰지요."], ["I pulled up; I pulled up in time.", "제때에 멈춰 섰어요. 하지만 너무 매몰차게는 아니고."], ["But not too hard.", "나는 늘 약간의 이론에는 찬성하는 쪽이었어요."], ["I have always been in favor of a little theory: we must have Thought; else we shall be landed back in the dark ages.", "우리에게는 사상이 있어야지요. 그러지 않으면 다시 암흑시대로 떨어지고 말 테니까요."], ["But talking of books, there is Southey’s ‘Peninsular War.’ I am reading that of a morning.", "그런데 책 이야기가 나왔으니, 사우디의 『반도 전쟁』이라는 게 있지요. 나는 그걸 아침나절에 읽고 있어요."], ["You know Southey?”", "사우디를 아십니까?”"]], [["“No,” said Mr.", "“모릅니다.”"], ["Casaubon, not keeping pace with Mr. Brooke’s impetuous reason, and thinking of the book only.", "캐소본 씨가 브룩 씨의 거침없는 이성의 행보를 따라가지 못하고 그 책만을 생각하며 대답했다."], ["“I have little leisure for such literature just now.", "“요즈음은 그런 문헌을 읽을 여유가 별로 없습니다."], ["I have been using up my eyesight on old characters lately; the fact is, I want a reader for my evenings; but I am fastidious in voices, and I cannot endure listening to an imperfect reader.", "근래에는 옛 문자에 시력을 다 써 버리고 있어서요. 사실은 저녁에 책을 읽어 줄 사람이 필요합니다만, 목소리에 까다로워서 서툰 낭독자가 읽는 것을 도무지 견딜 수가 없습니다."], ["It is a misfortune, in some senses: I feed too much on the inward sources; I live too much with the dead.", "어떤 의미에서는 불운한 일이지요. 저는 내면의 원천에서 지나치게 많은 양분을 얻고, 죽은 자들과 지나치게 많이 함께 살고 있으니까요."], ["My mind is something like the ghost of an ancient, wandering about the world and trying mentally to construct it as it used to be, in spite of ruin and confusing changes.", "제 정신은 어쩐지 옛사람의 유령 같아서, 세상을 떠돌며 폐허와 어지러운 변화에도 불구하고 그것이 본래 어떠했는지를 마음속으로 다시 세워 보려고 애쓰고 있습니다."], ["But I find it necessary to use the utmost caution about my eyesight.”", "하지만 제 시력에 대해서는 더없이 조심할 필요가 있다는 걸 알게 됩니다.”"]], [["This was the first time that Mr. Casaubon had spoken at any length.", "캐소본 씨가 어느 정도 길게 말한 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["He delivered himself with precision, as if he had been called upon to make a public statement; and the balanced sing-song neatness of his speech, occasionally corresponded to by a movement of his head, was the more conspicuous from its contrast with good Mr.", "그는 마치 공적인 진술을 하라는 요청을 받기라도 한 듯 정확하게 자신을 표현했다."], ["Brooke’s scrappy slovenliness. Dorothea said to herself that Mr.", "그리고 이따금 머리의 움직임이 이를 뒷받침하는, 균형 잡힌 가락처럼 단정한 그의 말투는 선량한 브룩 씨의 토막토막 끊기는 너저분함과 대조되어 한층 두드러졌다."], ["Casaubon was the most interesting man she had ever seen, not excepting even Monsieur Liret, the Vaudois clergyman who had given conferences on the history of the Waldenses.", "도러시아는 캐소본 씨가 자신이 지금껏 본 가장 흥미로운 사람이라고 속으로 생각했는데, 발도파의 역사에 관해 강연을 했던 보드의 성직자 리레 씨조차 예외가 아니었다."], ["To reconstruct a past world, doubtless with a view to the highest purposes of truth—what a work to be in any way present at, to assist in, though only as a lamp-holder!", "과거의 세계를 다시 세우는 일, 그것도 분명 진리의 가장 드높은 목적을 위해서라니, 비록 등불을 들고 있는 사람으로서일 뿐일지라도 어떤 식으로든 그 자리에 함께하며 거들 수 있다면 얼마나 멋진 일인가!"], ["This elevating thought lifted her above her annoyance at being twitted with her ignorance of political economy, that never-explained science which was thrust as an extinguisher over all her lights.", "이 고양된 생각은 정치 경제학에 무지하다고 놀림받은 데서 온 짜증 위로 그녀를 들어 올렸다. 그 끝내 설명되지 않는 학문은 그녀의 모든 빛 위로 덮어씌워진 촛불 끄개와도 같았다."]], [["“But you are fond of riding, Miss Brooke,”", "“하지만 브룩 양은 승마를 좋아하시지요.”"], ["Sir James presently took an opportunity of saying.", "제임스 경이 곧 기회를 잡아 말했다."], ["“I should have thought you would enter a little into the pleasures of hunting.", "“사냥의 즐거움에도 조금은 빠져 보실 줄 알았는데요."], ["I wish you would let me send over a chestnut horse for you to try.", "한번 타 보시도록 밤색 말을 한 마리 보내 드리고 싶습니다."], ["It has been trained for a lady.", "숙녀용으로 길들인 놈입니다."], ["I saw you on Saturday cantering over the hill on a nag not worthy of you.", "토요일에 양에게 어울리지 않는 조랑말을 타고 언덕을 가볍게 달리시는 걸 봤어요."], ["My groom shall bring Corydon for you every day, if you will only mention the time.”", "시간만 일러 주시면 제 마부가 코리던을 날마다 끌고 오게 하겠습니다.”"]], [["“Thank you, you are very good.", "“고맙습니다, 정말 친절하시군요."], ["I mean to give up riding.", "하지만 저는 승마를 그만둘 생각이에요."], ["I shall not ride any more,”", "더는 말을 타지 않을 거예요.”"], ["said Dorothea, urged to this brusque resolution by a little annoyance that Sir James would be soliciting her attention when she wanted to give it all to Mr. Casaubon.", "도러시아가 말했는데, 캐소본 씨에게 온 주의를 기울이고 싶은 마당에 제임스 경이 자꾸 관심을 청하는 것이 조금 짜증스러워 이런 퉁명스러운 결심으로 내몰린 것이었다."]], [["“No, that is too hard,”", "“아니, 그건 너무 가혹하군요.”"], ["said Sir James, in a tone of reproach that showed strong interest.", "제임스 경이 강한 관심을 드러내는 책망의 어조로 말했다."], ["“Your sister is given to self-mortification, is she not?”", "“양의 언니께서는 자기 절제에 잘 빠지시는 편이지요?”"], ["he continued, turning to Celia, who sat at his right hand.", "그가 오른편에 앉은 실리아 쪽을 돌아보며 말을 이었다."]], [["“I think she is,”", "“그런 것 같아요.”"], ["said Celia, feeling afraid lest she should say something that would not please her sister, and blushing as prettily as possible above her necklace.", "실리아가 언니의 마음에 들지 않을 말을 할까 봐 두려워하며, 목걸이 위로 더없이 곱게 얼굴을 붉히면서 말했다."], ["“She likes giving up.”", "“언니는 무언가 포기하는 걸 좋아하거든요.”"]], [["“If that were true, Celia, my giving-up would be self-indulgence, not self-mortification.", "“그게 사실이라면, 실리아, 내가 포기하는 건 자기 절제가 아니라 자기 탐닉이겠지."], ["But there may be good reasons for choosing not to do what is very agreeable,”", "하지만 무척 즐거운 일을 하지 않기로 택하는 데에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있을 수도 있어.”"], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Brooke was speaking at the same time, but it was evident that Mr. Casaubon was observing Dorothea, and she was aware of it.", "브룩 씨도 같은 때에 말하고 있었지만, 캐소본 씨가 도러시아를 관찰하고 있는 것은 분명했고, 그녀도 그것을 알고 있었다."]], [["“Exactly,”", "“바로 그겁니다.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“You give up from some high, generous motive.”", "“양은 무언가 드높고 너그러운 동기에서 포기하시는 거지요.”"]], [["“No, indeed, not exactly.", "“아니에요, 정말이지, 꼭 그런 건 아니에요."], ["I did not say that of myself,”", "저는 제 자신에 대해 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.”"], ["answered Dorothea, reddening.", "도러시아가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["Unlike Celia, she rarely blushed, and only from high delight or anger.", "실리아와 달리 그녀는 좀처럼 얼굴을 붉히지 않았고, 오직 큰 기쁨이나 분노에서만 그랬다."], ["At this moment she felt angry with the perverse Sir James.", "이 순간 그녀는 심술궂은 제임스 경에게 화가 났다."], ["Why did he not pay attention to Celia, and leave her to listen to Mr.", "어째서 그는 실리아에게 주의를 기울이고, 그녀는 캐소본 씨의 말에 귀 기울이도록 내버려 두지 않는 것일까?"], ["Casaubon?—if that learned man would only talk, instead of allowing himself to be talked to by Mr.", "그 박식한 사람이 브룩 씨에게 말을 듣고만 있지 말고 입을 열어 주기만 한다면 좋으련만."], ["Brooke, who was just then informing him that the Reformation either meant something or it did not, that he himself was a Protestant to the core, but that Catholicism was a fact; and as to refusing an acre of your ground for a Romanist chapel, all men needed the bridle of religion, which, properly speaking, was the dread of a Hereafter.", "그때 브룩 씨는 종교 개혁이란 무언가를 의미하든가 아니든가 둘 중 하나이며, 자기 자신은 골수까지 신교도이지만 가톨릭교도 하나의 사실이라고, 그리고 가톨릭교도의 예배당을 위해 제 땅 한 에이커를 내주기를 거절하는 문제로 말하자면, 모든 사람에게는 종교라는 고삐가 필요한데 그것은 정확히 말하자면 내세에 대한 두려움이라고 그에게 일러 주고 있었다."]], [["“I made a great study of theology at one time,”", "“나도 한때 신학을 크게 연구한 적이 있어요.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke, as if to explain the insight just manifested.", "방금 드러낸 통찰을 설명이라도 하려는 듯 브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“I know something of all schools.", "“모든 학파에 대해 조금씩은 알지요."], ["I knew Wilberforce in his best days.", "윌버포스가 한창때일 적에 그를 알았어요."], ["Do you know Wilberforce?”", "윌버포스를 아십니까?”"]], [["Mr. Casaubon said, “No.”", "캐소본 씨가 “모릅니다.”라고 말했다."]], [["“Well, Wilberforce was perhaps not enough of a thinker; but if I went into Parliament, as I have been asked to do, I should sit on the independent bench, as Wilberforce did, and work at philanthropy.”", "“글쎄, 윌버포스는 어쩌면 사상가라 하기엔 좀 부족했을지도 모르지요. 하지만 내가 청을 받은 대로 의회에 들어간다면, 윌버포스가 그랬듯이 무소속 의석에 앉아 박애 사업에 힘쓸 겁니다.”"]], [["Mr. Casaubon bowed, and observed that it was a wide field.", "캐소본 씨가 고개를 숙이며, 그것은 광대한 분야라고 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” said Mr.", "“그렇지요.”"], ["Brooke, with an easy smile, “but I have documents.", "브룩 씨가 느긋하게 미소 지으며 말했다. “하지만 나에게는 자료가 있어요."], ["I began a long while ago to collect documents.", "오래전부터 자료를 모으기 시작했지요."], ["They want arranging, but when a question has struck me, I have written to somebody and got an answer.", "정리는 좀 해야 하지만, 어떤 문제가 떠오를 때마다 누군가에게 편지를 써서 답을 받아 두었거든요."], ["I have documents at my back.", "내 뒤에는 자료가 있어요."], ["But now, how do you arrange your documents?”", "그런데 선생은 자료를 어떻게 정리하십니까?”"]], [["“In pigeon-holes partly,” said Mr.", "“부분적으로는 서류함에 넣어 둡니다.”"], ["Casaubon, with rather a startled air of effort.", "캐소본 씨가 다소 놀란 듯 애쓰는 기색으로 말했다."]], [["“Ah, pigeon-holes will not do.", "“아, 서류함은 안 돼요."], ["I have tried pigeon-holes, but everything gets mixed in pigeon-holes: I never know whether a paper is in A or Z.”", "나도 서류함을 써 봤지만, 서류함에서는 모든 게 뒤섞여 버려요. 어떤 종이가 에이 칸에 있는지 제트 칸에 있는지 도무지 알 수가 없거든요.”"]], [["“I wish you would let me sort your papers for you, uncle,”", "“삼촌, 제가 서류를 정리해 드리면 좋겠어요.”"], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“I would letter them all, and then make a list of subjects under each letter.”", "“전부 글자를 매기고, 그런 다음 각 글자 아래에 주제 목록을 만들겠어요.”"]], [["Mr. Casaubon gravely smiled approval, and said to Mr.", "캐소본 씨가 엄숙하게 미소 지으며 찬성을 표하고는, 브룩 씨에게 말했다."], ["Brooke, “You have an excellent secretary at hand, you perceive.”", "“보십시오, 가까이에 훌륭한 비서를 두고 계시는군요.”"]], [["“No, no,” said Mr.", "“아니요, 아닙니다.”"], ["Brooke, shaking his head; “I cannot let young ladies meddle with my documents.", "브룩 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. “젊은 숙녀들이 내 자료에 손대게 둘 수는 없어요."], ["Young ladies are too flighty.”", "젊은 숙녀들은 너무 변덕스럽거든요.”"]], [["Dorothea felt hurt. Mr.", "도러시아는 마음이 상했다."], ["Casaubon would think that her uncle had some special reason for delivering this opinion, whereas the remark lay in his mind as lightly as the broken wing of an insect among all the other fragments there, and a chance current had sent it alighting on her.", "캐소본 씨는 삼촌이 이런 의견을 내놓은 데에 무슨 특별한 이유가 있으리라 여길 테지만, 사실 그 말은 그의 마음속에서 그곳에 흩어진 다른 온갖 조각들 사이에 끼어 있는 곤충의 부러진 날개만큼이나 가볍게 자리하고 있었고, 우연한 기류가 그것을 하필 그녀에게 내려앉게 했을 뿐이었다."]], [["When the two girls were in the drawing-room alone, Celia said—", "두 아가씨가 객실에 단둘이 있게 되자, 실리아가 말했다."]], [["“How very ugly Mr. Casaubon is!”", "“캐소본 씨는 정말 못생겼어!”"]], [["“Celia!", "“실리아!"], ["He is one of the most distinguished-looking men I ever saw.", "그분은 내가 본 사람 중에 가장 기품 있어 보이는 분이야."], ["He is remarkably like the portrait of Locke.", "로크의 초상화와 놀랄 만큼 닮았어."], ["He has the same deep eye-sockets.”", "똑같이 눈자위가 깊이 패었잖아.”"]], [["“Had Locke those two white moles with hairs on them?”", "“로크에게도 그렇게 털 난 흰 사마귀 두 개가 있었나?”"]], [["“Oh, I dare say!", "“글쎄, 그랬을지도 모르지!"], ["when people of a certain sort looked at him,”", "어떤 부류의 사람들이 그를 들여다봤다면 말이야.”"], ["said Dorothea, walking away a little.", "도러시아가 조금 멀어지며 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Casaubon is so sallow.”", "“캐소본 씨는 안색이 너무 누렇잖아.”"]], [["“All the better.", "“그게 더 나아."], ["I suppose you admire a man with the complexion of a cochon de lait.”", "너는 새끼 돼지 같은 안색을 가진 남자를 좋아하나 보구나.”"]], [["“Dodo!”", "“도도!”"], ["exclaimed Celia, looking after her in surprise.", "실리아가 놀라 언니를 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["“I never heard you make such a comparison before.”", "“언니가 그런 비유를 하는 건 전에는 한 번도 못 들었어.”"]], [["“Why should I make it before the occasion came?", "“그럴 일이 생기지도 않았는데 내가 왜 그런 비유를 했겠니?"], ["It is a good comparison: the match is perfect.”", "좋은 비유잖아. 딱 들어맞으니까.”"]], [["Miss Brooke was clearly forgetting herself, and Celia thought so.", "브룩 양은 분명 자신을 잊고 있었고, 실리아도 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["“I wonder you show temper, Dorothea.”", "“언니가 화를 내다니 의외야, 도러시아.”"]], [["“It is so painful in you, Celia, that you will look at human beings as if they were merely animals with a toilet, and never see the great soul in a man’s face.”", "“네가 그러는 게 너무 마음 아파, 실리아. 너는 인간을 마치 몸치장한 동물 정도로만 바라보고, 사람의 얼굴에서 위대한 영혼을 결코 보지 못하니 말이야.”"]], [["“Has Mr. Casaubon a great soul?”", "“캐소본 씨에게 위대한 영혼이 있어?”"], ["Celia was not without a touch of naive malice.", "실리아에게는 순진한 짓궂음이 없지 않았다."]], [["“Yes, I believe he has,”", "“그래, 나는 그렇다고 믿어.”"], ["said Dorothea, with the full voice of decision.", "도러시아가 단호한 목소리로 힘주어 말했다."], ["“Everything I see in him corresponds to his pamphlet on Biblical Cosmology.”", "“내가 그분에게서 보는 모든 것이 성서 우주론에 관한 그분의 소책자와 일치해.”"]], [["“He talks very little,”", "“그분은 말이 무척 적던데.”"], ["said Celia", "실리아가 말했다."]], [["“There is no one for him to talk to.”", "“그분이 말을 나눌 상대가 없으니까 그렇지.”"]], [["Celia thought privately, “Dorothea quite despises Sir James Chettam; I believe she would not accept him.”", "실리아는 속으로 생각했다."], ["Celia felt that this was a pity.", "‘언니는 제임스 체텀 경을 아주 경멸해."], ["She had never been deceived as to the object of the baronet’s interest.", "청혼해도 받아들이지 않을 것 같아.’ 실리아는 그것이 안타까운 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["Sometimes, indeed, she had reflected that Dodo would perhaps not make a husband happy who had not her way of looking at things; and stifled in the depths of her heart was the feeling that her sister was too religious for family comfort.", "그녀는 준남작이 누구에게 관심을 두고 있는지에 대해서는 한 번도 속은 적이 없었다. 사실 그녀는 도도가 자기처럼 사물을 보지 않는 남편이라면 어쩌면 그를 행복하게 해 주지 못할지도 모른다고 이따금 생각하기도 했다."], ["Notions and scruples were like spilt needles, making one afraid of treading, or sitting down, or even eating.", "그리고 마음 깊은 곳에는, 언니가 가정의 안락에 비해 너무 종교적이라는 느낌이 짓눌려 있었다. 관념과 양심의 가책이란 흩뿌려진 바늘 같아서, 밟지도, 앉지도, 심지어 먹지도 못하게 사람을 겁먹게 했다."]], [["When Miss Brooke was at the tea-table, Sir James came to sit down by her, not having felt her mode of answering him at all offensive.", "브룩 양이 찻상 앞에 앉자, 제임스 경이 그녀 곁에 와 앉았는데, 그녀가 자기에게 대답한 방식을 조금도 무례하다고 느끼지 않았기 때문이었다. 어째서 그가 그렇게 느끼겠는가?"], ["Why should he? He thought it probable that Miss Brooke liked him, and manners must be very marked indeed before they cease to be interpreted by preconceptions either confident or distrustful.", "그는 브룩 양이 자기를 좋아할 것이 분명하다고 생각했고, 사람의 태도라는 것은 자신만만하든 미심쩍어하든 선입견으로 해석되지 않을 만큼 정말로 두드러진 경우가 아니고서는 늘 그렇게 받아들여지기 마련이었다."], ["She was thoroughly charming to him, but of course he theorized a little about his attachment.", "그녀는 그에게 더없이 매력적인 사람이었지만, 물론 그는 자신의 연정에 대해 조금은 이론을 세워 보기도 했다."], ["He was made of excellent human dough, and had the rare merit of knowing that his talents, even if let loose, would not set the smallest stream in the county on fire: hence he liked the prospect of a wife to whom he could say, “What shall we do?”", "그는 더없이 훌륭한 인간의 반죽으로 빚어진 사람이었고, 자신의 재능이란 풀어 놓아 본들 이 고장의 가장 작은 시냇물조차 불사르지 못하리라는 것을 아는 보기 드문 미덕을 지니고 있었다. 그래서 그는 이런저런 일에 대해 “우리 어떻게 할까?”"], ["about this or that; who could help her husband out with reasons, and would also have the property qualification for doing so.", "하고 물을 수 있는 아내, 남편을 거들어 논거를 대 줄 수 있고 또 그럴 만한 재산상의 자격까지 갖춘 아내를 맞이할 전망이 마음에 들었다."], ["As to the excessive religiousness alleged against Miss Brooke, he had a very indefinite notion of what it consisted in, and thought that it would die out with marriage.", "브룩 양에게 씌워진 지나친 신앙심으로 말하자면, 그는 그것이 무엇으로 이루어진 것인지에 대해 매우 막연한 관념밖에 없었고, 그것이 결혼과 함께 사그라지리라 생각했다."], ["In short, he felt himself to be in love in the right place, and was ready to endure a great deal of predominance, which, after all, a man could always put down when he liked.", "요컨대 그는 자신이 제대로 된 상대에게 사랑에 빠졌다고 느꼈고, 적잖은 우위까지도 기꺼이 견딜 작정이었는데, 어쨌거나 남자란 마음만 먹으면 언제든 그것을 눌러 버릴 수 있는 법이었다."], ["Sir James had no idea that he should ever like to put down the predominance of this handsome girl, in whose cleverness he delighted. Why not?", "제임스 경은 자기가 그 영리함을 즐거워하는 이 아름다운 아가씨의 우위를 언젠가 눌러 버리고 싶어지리라고는 전혀 생각하지 않았다."], ["A man’s mind—what there is of it—has always the advantage of being masculine,—as the smallest birch-tree is of a higher kind than the most soaring palm,—and even his ignorance is of a sounder quality.", "어찌 그렇지 않겠는가? 남자의 정신이란, 그것이 얼마만큼 있든 간에, 늘 남성적이라는 이점을 지니고 있으니, 가장 작은 자작나무가 가장 드높이 솟은 야자수보다 더 높은 부류이듯이 말이다."], ["Sir James might not have originated this estimate; but a kind Providence furnishes the limpest personality with a little gum or starch in the form of tradition.", "게다가 남자의 무지조차 더 건실한 품질의 것이었다. 제임스 경이 이런 평가를 처음 만들어 낸 것은 아닐 테지만, 자비로운 섭리는 가장 흐물거리는 인격에도 전통이라는 형태로 약간의 풀기나 빳빳함을 보태 주는 법이다."]], [["“Let me hope that you will rescind that resolution about the horse, Miss Brooke,”", "“말에 관한 그 결심을 거두어 주시기를 바랍니다, 브룩 양.”"], ["said the persevering admirer.", "끈질긴 숭배자가 말했다."], ["“I assure you, riding is the most healthy of exercises.”", "“장담컨대 승마는 가장 건강에 좋은 운동입니다.”"]], [["“I am aware of it,”", "“알고 있어요.”"], ["said Dorothea, coldly.", "도러시아가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“I think it would do Celia good—if she would take to it.”", "“실리아에게 좋을 것 같아요. 그 애가 승마를 시작한다면 말이지요.”"]], [["“But you are such a perfect horsewoman.”", "“하지만 양은 그토록 완벽한 여기수이신데요.”"]], [["“Excuse me; I have had very little practice, and I should be easily thrown.”", "“죄송하지만, 저는 연습을 거의 한 적이 없어서 쉽게 낙마할 거예요.”"]], [["“Then that is a reason for more practice.", "“그렇다면 그건 더 많이 연습해야 할 이유가 되지요."], ["Every lady ought to be a perfect horsewoman, that she may accompany her husband.”", "숙녀라면 누구나 남편과 동행할 수 있도록 완벽한 여기수가 되어야 합니다.”"]], [["“You see how widely we differ, Sir James.", "“보세요, 우리는 이렇게 생각이 크게 다르군요, 제임스 경."], ["I have made up my mind that I ought not to be a perfect horsewoman, and so I should never correspond to your pattern of a lady.”", "저는 완벽한 여기수가 되어서는 안 된다고 마음을 정했으니, 결코 당신이 그리는 숙녀의 본보기에는 들어맞지 않을 거예요.”"], ["Dorothea looked straight before her, and spoke with cold brusquerie, very much with the air of a handsome boy, in amusing contrast with the solicitous amiability of her admirer.", "도러시아는 곧장 앞을 바라보며 차갑고 퉁명스럽게, 꼭 잘생긴 소년 같은 태도로 말했는데, 이는 그녀의 숭배자가 보이는 안달하는 다정함과 우습게도 대조를 이루었다."]], [["“I should like to know your reasons for this cruel resolution.", "“그토록 매정한 결심을 하신 이유를 알고 싶군요."], ["It is not possible that you should think horsemanship wrong.”", "양께서 승마를 그릇된 일이라 여기실 리는 없을 테니까요.”"]], [["“It is quite possible that I should think it wrong for me.”", "“저로서는 그것이 그릇된 일이라고 충분히 여길 수 있지요.”"]], [["“Oh, why?”", "“오, 어째서요?”"], ["said Sir James, in a tender tone of remonstrance.", "제임스 경이 부드럽게 항의하는 어조로 말했다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon had come up to the table, teacup in hand, and was listening.", "캐소본 씨가 찻잔을 손에 들고 찻상으로 다가와 듣고 있었다."]], [["“We must not inquire too curiously into motives,”", "“동기를 너무 꼬치꼬치 캐물어서는 안 되지요.”"], ["he interposed, in his measured way.", "그가 차분한 어조로 끼어들었다."], ["“Miss Brooke knows that they are apt to become feeble in the utterance: the aroma is mixed with the grosser air.", "“브룩 양은 동기란 입 밖으로 내면 약해지기 쉽다는 걸 압니다. 그 향기가 더 거친 공기와 뒤섞이는 거지요."], ["We must keep the germinating grain away from the light.”", "우리는 움트는 씨앗을 빛으로부터 멀리 두어야 합니다.”"]], [["Dorothea colored with pleasure, and looked up gratefully to the speaker.", "도러시아는 기쁨으로 얼굴을 붉히며 고마운 마음으로 말한 이를 올려다보았다."], ["Here was a man who could understand the higher inward life, and with whom there could be some spiritual communion; nay, who could illuminate principle with the widest knowledge: a man whose learning almost amounted to a proof of whatever he believed!", "여기, 더 높은 내면의 삶을 이해할 수 있고, 어떤 영적인 교감을 나눌 수 있는 사람이 있었다. 아니, 가장 넓은 지식으로 원칙을 밝혀 줄 수 있는 사람, 그 학식이 그가 믿는 무엇이든 거의 증명에 이르게 하는 사람이 있었다!"]], [["Dorothea’s inferences may seem large; but really life could never have gone on at any period but for this liberal allowance of conclusions, which has facilitated marriage under the difficulties of civilization.", "도러시아의 추론은 거창해 보일지 모른다. 하지만 사실 이렇게 결론을 너그럽게 허용하는 일이 없었다면 그 어느 시대에도 삶은 이어질 수 없었을 것이니, 그것이 문명의 어려움 속에서도 결혼을 수월하게 만들어 온 것이다."], ["Has any one ever pinched into its pilulous smallness the cobweb of pre-matrimonial acquaintanceship?", "결혼 전의 교제라는 거미줄을 알약만 한 작은 크기로 꼬집어 압축해 본 사람이 누가 있던가?"]], [["“Certainly,”", "“물론입니다.”"], ["said good Sir James.", "선량한 제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“Miss Brooke shall not be urged to tell reasons she would rather be silent upon.", "“브룩 양께서 침묵하고 싶어 하시는 이유를 굳이 말하시도록 재촉하지는 않겠습니다."], ["I am sure her reasons would do her honor.”", "양의 이유라면 분명 양께 명예가 될 만한 것이겠지요.”"]], [["He was not in the least jealous of the interest with which Dorothea had looked up at Mr.", "그는 도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 올려다본 그 관심에 대해 조금도 질투하지 않았다."], ["Casaubon: it never occurred to him that a girl to whom he was meditating an offer of marriage could care for a dried bookworm towards fifty, except, indeed, in a religious sort of way, as for a clergyman of some distinction.", "자기가 청혼을 생각하고 있는 아가씨가 쉰을 바라보는 메마른 책벌레에게 마음을 둘 수도 있으리라고는, 물론 어느 정도 명망 있는 성직자에게 그러듯 종교적인 의미에서가 아니라면, 그로서는 도무지 떠오르지 않는 일이었다."]], [["However, since Miss Brooke had become engaged in a conversation with Mr. Casaubon about the Vaudois clergy, Sir James betook himself to Celia, and talked to her about her sister; spoke of a house in town, and asked whether Miss Brooke disliked London.", "그렇지만 브룩 양이 캐소본 씨와 보드의 성직자에 관한 대화에 빠져들자, 제임스 경은 실리아에게로 가서 그녀의 언니에 관해 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Away from her sister, Celia talked quite easily, and Sir James said to himself that the second Miss Brooke was certainly very agreeable as well as pretty, though not, as some people pretended, more clever and sensible than the elder sister.", "그는 시내의 집 이야기를 꺼내며, 브룩 양이 런던을 싫어하는지 물었다. 언니에게서 떨어져 나오자 실리아는 아주 편안하게 이야기했고, 제임스 경은 속으로, 둘째 브룩 양은 비록 어떤 이들이 떠벌리듯 언니보다 더 영리하고 분별 있는 것은 아닐지라도 분명 예쁘기도 하거니와 무척 상냥하다고 생각했다."], ["He felt that he had chosen the one who was in all respects the superior; and a man naturally likes to look forward to having the best.", "그는 자기가 모든 면에서 더 뛰어난 쪽을 택했다고 느꼈고, 남자란 본디 최고의 것을 누릴 일을 내다보기를 좋아하는 법이었다."], ["He would be the very Mawworm of bachelors who pretended not to expect it.", "그것을 기대하지 않는 척하는 자가 있다면 그야말로 독신자들 가운데 위선의 화신일 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER III.", "# CHAPTER III."]], [["“Say, goddess, what ensued, when Raphael, The affable archangel . . . Eve The story heard attentive, and was filled With admiration, and deep muse, to hear Of things so high and strange.” —Paradise Lost, B. vii.", "“말하라, 여신이여, 그 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지를, 라파엘이,\n그 상냥한 대천사가……"]], [["If it had really occurred to Mr. Casaubon to think of Miss Brooke as a suitable wife for him, the reasons that might induce her to accept him were already planted in her mind, and by the evening of the next day the reasons had budded and bloomed. For they had had a long conversation in the morning, while Celia, who did not like the company of Mr. Casaubon’s moles and sallowness, had escaped to the vicarage to play with the curate’s ill-shod but merry children.", "이브는\n그 이야기를 주의 깊게 들으며, 가득 차올랐으니"]], [["Dorothea by this time had looked deep into the ungauged reservoir of Mr. Casaubon’s mind, seeing reflected there in vague labyrinthine extension every quality she herself brought; had opened much of her own experience to him, and had understood from him the scope of his great work, also of attractively labyrinthine extent. For he had been as instructive as Milton’s “affable archangel;” and with something of the archangelic manner he told her how he had undertaken to show (what indeed had been attempted before, but not with that thoroughness, justice of comparison, and effectiveness of arrangement at which Mr. Casaubon aimed) that all the mythical systems or erratic mythical fragments in the world were corruptions of a tradition originally revealed. Having once mastered the true position and taken a firm footing there, the vast field of mythical constructions became intelligible, nay, luminous with the reflected light of correspondences. But to gather in this great harvest of truth was no light or speedy work. His notes already made a formidable range of volumes, but the crowning task would be to condense these voluminous still-accumulating results and bring them, like the earlier vintage of Hippocratic books, to fit a little shelf. In explaining this to Dorothea, Mr. Casaubon expressed himself nearly as he would have done to a fellow-student, for he had not two styles of talking at command: it is true that when he used a Greek or Latin phrase he always gave the English with scrupulous care, but he would probably have done this in any case. A learned provincial clergyman is accustomed to think of his acquaintances as of “lords, knyghtes, and other noble and worthi men, that conne Latyn but lytille.”", "감탄으로, 그리고 깊은 명상으로, 그토록 드높고\n기이한 것들에 대해 들으면서.”"]], [["Dorothea was altogether captivated by the wide embrace of this conception.", "—『실낙원』, 제7편."], ["Here was something beyond the shallows of ladies’ school literature: here was a living Bossuet, whose work would reconcile complete knowledge with devoted piety; here was a modern Augustine who united the glories of doctor and saint.", "캐소본 씨가 정말로 브룩 양을 자신에게 어울리는 아내로 떠올려 본 적이 있었다면, 그녀로 하여금 그를 받아들이게 할 만한 이유들은 이미 그녀의 마음속에 심어져 있었고, 이튿날 저녁 무렵에는 그 이유들이 싹을 틔우고 꽃을 피웠다. 캐소본 씨의 사마귀와 누르께한 안색을 좋아하지 않던 실리아가 부목사의 신발도 변변찮으나 명랑한 아이들과 놀려고 사제관으로 도망친 사이, 아침 내내 두 사람은 긴 대화를 나누었기 때문이다."]], [["The sanctity seemed no less clearly marked than the learning, for when Dorothea was impelled to open her mind on certain themes which she could speak of to no one whom she had before seen at Tipton, especially on the secondary importance of ecclesiastical forms and articles of belief compared with that spiritual religion, that submergence of self in communion with Divine perfection which seemed to her to be expressed in the best Christian books of widely distant ages, she found in Mr. Casaubon a listener who understood her at once, who could assure her of his own agreement with that view when duly tempered with wise conformity, and could mention historical examples before unknown to her.", "이 무렵 도러시아는 캐소본 씨의 마음이라는, 가늠할 수 없는 저수지를 깊이 들여다보았고, 거기에 자신이 지닌 온갖 자질이 어렴풋이 미궁처럼 확장되어 비치는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 자기 경험의 많은 부분을 그에게 털어놓았고, 그로부터 그의 위대한 저작의 범위를, 역시 매력적이게도 미궁처럼 펼쳐진 그 규모를 이해하게 되었다. 그는 밀턴의 “상냥한 대천사”만큼이나 가르침을 주는 사람이었으니, 다소 대천사 같은 태도로 그는 그녀에게 자신이 무엇을 보여 주려 하는지를 들려주었다. 세상의 모든 신화 체계나 산만한 신화의 단편들이 본래 계시된 하나의 전통이 변질된 것임을 증명하는 일이었다(이는 사실 이전에도 시도된 바 있었으나, 캐소본 씨가 지향하는 그러한 철저함이나 비교의 공정함, 배열의 효율성을 갖추지는 못했다). 일단 참된 입지를 터득하고 거기에 확고히 발을 디디면, 신화적 구조물의 광대한 들판이 이해 가능해지고, 아니, 상응 관계가 비추는 빛으로 환히 밝아진다는 것이었다. 그러나 이 위대한 진리의 수확을 거두어들이는 일은 결코 가볍거나 빠른 작업이 아니었다. 그의 노트는 이미 어마어마한 분량의 여러 권을 이루고 있었지만, 그 절정의 과업은 이 방대하고도 여전히 쌓여 가는 결과물을 응축하여, 히포크라테스 저작들의 초기 수확처럼, 작은 선반 하나에 들어맞게 만드는 일이었다. 이것을 도러시아에게 설명하면서 캐소본 씨는 마치 동료 학자에게 말하듯 자신을 표현했으니, 그에게는 두 가지 말투가 따로 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그가 그리스어나 라틴어 구절을 쓸 때면 언제나 세심한 주의를 기울여 영어로도 옮겨 주었던 것은 사실이지만, 그것은 아마 어떤 경우에도 그렇게 했을 터였다. 학식 있는 지방 성직자란 자기 지인들을 “라틴어를 거의 알지 못하는 영주들, 기사들, 그 밖의 고귀하고 훌륭한 사람들”로 여기는 데 익숙한 법이다."]], [["“He thinks with me,”", "도러시아는 이 구상의 폭넓은 포용에 완전히 사로잡혔다."], ["said Dorothea to herself, “or rather, he thinks a whole world of which my thought is but a poor twopenny mirror.", "여기에 숙녀들의 학교 문학이라는 얕은 물웅덩이를 넘어서는 무언가가 있었다. 여기에 살아 있는 보쉬에가 있었으니, 그의 저작은 완전한 지식과 헌신적인 경건을 화해시킬 것이었다."], ["And his feelings too, his whole experience—what a lake compared with my little pool!”", "여기에 현대의 아우구스티누스가 있었으니, 그는 학자와 성인의 영광을 하나로 결합한 사람이었다."]], [["Miss Brooke argued from words and dispositions not less unhesitatingly than other young ladies of her age.", "그 거룩함은 학식 못지않게 뚜렷이 드러나 보였다."], ["Signs are small measurable things, but interpretations are illimitable, and in girls of sweet, ardent nature, every sign is apt to conjure up wonder, hope, belief, vast as a sky, and colored by a diffused thimbleful of matter in the shape of knowledge.", "도러시아가 티프턴에서 이전에 만난 어느 누구에게도 말할 수 없었던 어떤 주제들에 대해, 특히 멀리 떨어진 시대들의 가장 훌륭한 기독교 저서들에 표현되어 있는 듯한 그 영적인 신앙, 곧 신성한 완전함과의 교감 속에서 자아를 침잠시키는 일에 비하면 교회의 형식이나 신앙 조항들이 부차적 중요성밖에 지니지 못한다는 생각을 털어놓고 싶은 충동을 느꼈을 때, 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게서 자신을 단번에 이해해 주는 청자를, 현명한 순응으로 적절히 다듬어진다면 그 견해에 자신도 동의한다고 확언해 줄 수 있고, 그녀가 미처 알지 못했던 역사적 사례들을 들어 줄 수 있는 청자를 발견했다. “그분은 나와 똑같이 생각하셔.”"], ["They are not always too grossly deceived; for Sinbad himself may have fallen by good-luck on a true description, and wrong reasoning sometimes lands poor mortals in right conclusions: starting a long way off the true point, and proceeding by loops and zigzags, we now and then arrive just where we ought to be.", "도러시아는 혼잣말을 했다. “아니, 오히려 그분은 하나의 온전한 세계를 생각하시고, 내 생각은 그 세계를 비추는 한낱 보잘것없는 싸구려 거울에 지나지 않아."], ["Because Miss Brooke was hasty in her trust, it is not therefore clear that Mr. Casaubon was unworthy of it.", "그분의 감정도, 그분의 온 경험도—내 작은 물웅덩이에 비하면 얼마나 큰 호수인지!”"]], [["He stayed a little longer than he had intended, on a slight pressure of invitation from Mr.", "브룩 양은 또래의 다른 젊은 숙녀들 못지않게 거침없이 말과 성향으로부터 추론을 끌어냈다."], ["Brooke, who offered no bait except his own documents on machine-breaking and rick-burning. Mr.", "징표란 작고 측정할 수 있는 것이지만, 해석이란 한계가 없는 법이며, 다정하고 열정적인 천성을 지닌 소녀들에게는 모든 징표가 하늘만큼 광대한 경이와 희망과 믿음을 불러일으키기 쉽고, 그것은 지식이라는 형태로 흩뿌려진 골무 하나만큼의 재료로 채색되곤 한다."], ["Casaubon was called into the library to look at these in a heap, while his host picked up first one and then the other to read aloud from in a skipping and uncertain way, passing from one unfinished passage to another with a “Yes, now, but here!”", "그들이 늘 너무 심하게 속는 것은 아니다. 신드바드 자신도 운 좋게 어쩌다 참된 묘사에 도달했을 수 있고, 그릇된 추론이 때로는 가련한 인간들을 옳은 결론으로 데려다주기도 하니, 참된 지점에서 한참 벗어난 곳에서 출발하여 고리를 그리고 갈지자로 나아가다가도 우리는 이따금 마땅히 있어야 할 바로 그곳에 다다른다."], ["and finally pushing them all aside to open the journal of his youthful Continental travels.", "브룩 양이 성급하게 신뢰했다고 해서, 캐소본 씨가 그 신뢰를 받을 자격이 없다는 것이 분명해지는 것은 아니다."]], [["“Look here—here is all about Greece. Rhamnus, the ruins of Rhamnus—you are a great Grecian, now. I don’t know whether you have given much study to the topography. I spent no end of time in making out these things—Helicon, now. Here, now!—‘We started the next morning for Parnassus, the double-peaked Parnassus.’ All this volume is about Greece, you know,” Mr. Brooke wound up, rubbing his thumb transversely along the edges of the leaves as he held the book forward.", "그는 의도했던 것보다 조금 더 오래 머물렀으니, 기계 파괴와 건초더미 방화에 관한 자신의 문서밖에는 아무 미끼도 내놓지 않은 브룩 씨의 가벼운 권유 때문이었다. 캐소본 씨는 그것들을 무더기로 살펴보라며 서재로 불려 들어갔고, 그동안 주인은 이것저것 집어 들며 건너뛰는 듯 종잡을 수 없는 방식으로 소리 내어 읽다가, “그래, 자, 그런데 여기 보게나!” 하면서 끝나지 않은 한 구절에서 다른 구절로 옮겨 다니더니, 마침내 그것들을 모조리 옆으로 밀쳐 두고 자신의 젊은 시절 유럽 대륙 여행 일기를 펼쳤다."]], [["Mr.", "“여기 보게나—여기 그리스 이야기가 전부 있다네."], ["Casaubon made a dignified though somewhat sad audience; bowed in the right place, and avoided looking at anything documentary as far as possible, without showing disregard or impatience; mindful that this desultoriness was associated with the institutions of the country, and that the man who took him on this severe mental scamper was not only an amiable host, but a landholder and custos rotulorum.", "람누스, 람누스의 폐허—자네야 그리스에 정통한 사람이지. 자네가 지형학을 많이 공부했는지는 모르겠네만."], ["Was his endurance aided also by the reflection that Mr.", "나는 이런 것들을 알아내느라 한없이 시간을 보냈지—이것 보게, 헬리콘 말이야. 자, 여기!—‘우리는 이튿날 아침 파르나소스로 떠났다, 두 봉우리의 파르나소스로.’ 이 책은 죄다 그리스 이야기일세, 알겠나.”"], ["Brooke was the uncle of Dorothea?", "브룩 씨는 책을 앞으로 내밀며 엄지손가락을 책장 가장자리를 따라 가로로 문지르면서 말을 맺었다."]], [["Certainly he seemed more and more bent on making her talk to him, on drawing her out, as Celia remarked to herself; and in looking at her his face was often lit up by a smile like pale wintry sunshine.", "캐소본 씨는 위엄 있되 다소 서글픈 청중 노릇을 했으니, 적절한 대목에서 고개를 숙였고, 무시나 조바심을 드러내지 않으면서 되도록 어떤 문서에도 눈길을 주지 않으려 했다. 이 두서없음이 이 나라의 제도와 결부되어 있다는 것, 그리고 자신을 이 혹독한 정신적 질주로 끌고 다니는 이 사람이 상냥한 주인일 뿐만 아니라 지주이자 기록 보관관이라는 것을 그는 유념하고 있었다."], ["Before he left the next morning, while taking a pleasant walk with Miss Brooke along the gravelled terrace, he had mentioned to her that he felt the disadvantage of loneliness, the need of that cheerful companionship with which the presence of youth can lighten or vary the serious toils of maturity.", "브룩 씨가 도러시아의 숙부라는 생각이 그의 인내를 거들기도 했을까? 분명 그는 점점 더 그녀에게 자신과 이야기하도록 만들고, 그녀의 속내를 이끌어 내는 일에 열중하는 것 같았다고, 실리아는 속으로 생각했다."], ["And he delivered this statement with as much careful precision as if he had been a diplomatic envoy whose words would be attended with results.", "그리고 그녀를 바라볼 때면 그의 얼굴은 창백한 겨울 햇살 같은 미소로 환히 밝아지곤 했다. 이튿날 아침 떠나기 전, 브룩 양과 함께 자갈 깔린 테라스를 따라 즐거운 산책을 하면서, 그는 고독의 불리함을, 젊음의 존재가 원숙한 시절의 진지한 노고를 가볍게 하거나 변화시켜 줄 그 명랑한 동반자에 대한 필요를 느낀다고 그녀에게 언급했다."], ["Indeed, Mr. Casaubon was not used to expect that he should have to repeat or revise his communications of a practical or personal kind.", "그리고 그는 마치 그 말이 어떤 결과로 이어질 외교 사절이라도 되는 듯, 그토록 신중한 정확성을 기울여 이 진술을 전했다."], ["The inclinations which he had deliberately stated on the 2d of October he would think it enough to refer to by the mention of that date; judging by the standard of his own memory, which was a volume where a vide supra could serve instead of repetitions, and not the ordinary long-used blotting-book which only tells of forgotten writing.", "사실 캐소본 씨는 실무적이거나 개인적인 종류의 의사를 되풀이하거나 고쳐 말해야 하리라고는 예상하는 데 익숙하지 않았다. 10월 2일에 신중하게 밝힌 의향은 그 날짜를 언급하는 것만으로 충분히 가리킬 수 있다고 그는 여겼을 터였으니, 그가 판단의 기준으로 삼은 자신의 기억력이란 반복 대신 “위 참조”라는 한마디로 족한 책 같은 것이었지 잊힌 글씨에 대해서나 알려 주는, 오래 써서 닳은 흔한 압지첩 같은 것은 아니었기 때문이다."], ["But in this case Mr. Casaubon’s confidence was not likely to be falsified, for Dorothea heard and retained what he said with the eager interest of a fresh young nature to which every variety in experience is an epoch.", "그러나 이 경우 캐소본 씨의 확신은 어긋날 것 같지 않았으니, 도러시아는 그가 한 말을, 경험의 온갖 변화가 하나의 획기적 사건이 되는 풋풋하고 젊은 천성다운 열렬한 관심으로 듣고 마음에 새겼기 때문이다."]], [["It was three o’clock in the beautiful breezy autumn day when Mr.", "캐소본 씨가 티프턴에서 겨우 5마일 떨어진 로윅의 사제관으로 마차를 몰고 떠난 것은 산들바람 부는 아름다운 가을날 오후 세 시였다."], ["Casaubon drove off to his Rectory at Lowick, only five miles from Tipton; and Dorothea, who had on her bonnet and shawl, hurried along the shrubbery and across the park that she might wander through the bordering wood with no other visible companionship than that of Monk, the Great St. Bernard dog, who always took care of the young ladies in their walks.", "보닛을 쓰고 숄을 두른 도러시아는, 산책할 때 늘 젊은 숙녀들을 보살펴 주는 세인트버나드종 대형견 멍크 말고는 눈에 띄는 다른 동반자 없이 가장자리 숲을 거닐고 싶어서, 관목 숲을 따라 서둘러 걸어 정원을 가로질렀다."], ["There had risen before her the girl’s vision of a possible future for herself to which she looked forward with trembling hope, and she wanted to wander on in that visionary future without interruption.", "그녀 앞에는 떨리는 희망으로 기대하는, 자신의 가능한 미래에 대한 소녀의 환영이 떠올라 있었고, 그녀는 그 환영의 미래 속을 방해받지 않고 거닐고 싶었다."], ["She walked briskly in the brisk air, the color rose in her cheeks, and her straw bonnet (which our contemporaries might look at with conjectural curiosity as at an obsolete form of basket) fell a little backward.", "그녀는 상쾌한 공기 속을 활기차게 걸었고, 뺨에는 홍조가 떠올랐으며, 밀짚 보닛은(우리 시대 사람들이라면 한물간 바구니 모양이라도 보듯 추측 어린 호기심으로 바라볼 만한 것이었다) 조금 뒤로 젖혀졌다."], ["She would perhaps be hardly characterized enough if it were omitted that she wore her brown hair flatly braided and coiled behind so as to expose the outline of her head in a daring manner at a time when public feeling required the meagreness of nature to be dissimulated by tall barricades of frizzed curls and bows, never surpassed by any great race except the Feejeean.", "그녀가 갈색 머리카락을 납작하게 땋아 뒤로 둘둘 감아 올려 머리 윤곽을 대담하게 드러냈다는 점을 빼놓는다면, 그녀의 성격을 충분히 그려 냈다고 하기 어려울 것이다. 당시의 대중적 감정은 곱슬곱슬하게 지진 고수머리와 리본의 높다란 바리케이드로 타고난 빈약함을 감추기를 요구했으니, 피지섬 사람들 말고는 어떤 위대한 종족도 그것을 능가한 적이 없었다."], ["This was a trait of Miss Brooke’s asceticism.", "이것은 브룩 양의 금욕주의의 한 특징이었다."], ["But there was nothing of an ascetic’s expression in her bright full eyes, as she looked before her, not consciously seeing, but absorbing into the intensity of her mood, the solemn glory of the afternoon with its long swathes of light between the far-off rows of limes, whose shadows touched each other.", "그러나 멀리 늘어선 보리수 사이로 그 그림자들이 서로 맞닿은, 길게 드리운 빛줄기를 거느린 오후의 엄숙한 영광을, 의식적으로 바라보는 것이 아니라 자기 기분의 강렬함 속으로 빨아들이며 앞을 응시하는 그녀의 밝고 충만한 눈에는 금욕주의자다운 표정이라곤 없었다."]], [["All people, young or old (that is, all people in those ante-reform times), would have thought her an interesting object if they had referred the glow in her eyes and cheeks to the newly awakened ordinary images of young love: the illusions of Chloe about Strephon have been sufficiently consecrated in poetry, as the pathetic loveliness of all spontaneous trust ought to be.", "만약 사람들이 그녀의 눈과 뺨에 어린 그 광채를, 갓 눈뜬 평범한 풋사랑의 이미지로 돌려서 보았다면, 늙은이든 젊은이든 모든 사람이(다시 말해, 그 개혁 이전 시대의 모든 사람이) 그녀를 흥미로운 대상으로 여겼을 것이다. 스트레폰을 향한 클로에의 환상은, 자발적인 모든 신뢰가 지닌 애처로운 사랑스러움이 마땅히 그래야 하듯, 시 속에서 충분히 신성하게 여겨져 왔다."], ["Miss Pippin adoring young Pumpkin, and dreaming along endless vistas of unwearying companionship, was a little drama which never tired our fathers and mothers, and had been put into all costumes.", "어린 펌프킨을 흠모하며 지치지 않는 동반의 끝없는 전망을 따라 꿈꾸는 피핀 양이란, 우리 아버지 어머니들을 결코 싫증나게 하지 않았고 온갖 의상을 갈아입혀 온 작은 드라마였다."], ["Let but Pumpkin have a figure which would sustain the disadvantages of the shortwaisted swallow-tail, and everybody felt it not only natural but necessary to the perfection of womanhood, that a sweet girl should be at once convinced of his virtue, his exceptional ability, and above all, his perfect sincerity.", "짧은 허리의 연미복이라는 불리함을 견뎌 낼 만한 풍채를 펌프킨이 지니기만 하면, 어떤 사랑스러운 소녀가 그의 미덕과 비범한 능력, 무엇보다 그의 완벽한 진실함을 단번에 확신하는 것이야말로 자연스러울 뿐 아니라 여성성의 완성을 위해 필수적이라고 누구나 여겼다."], ["But perhaps no persons then living—certainly none in the neighborhood of Tipton—would have had a sympathetic understanding for the dreams of a girl whose notions about marriage took their color entirely from an exalted enthusiasm about the ends of life, an enthusiasm which was lit chiefly by its own fire, and included neither the niceties of the trousseau, the pattern of plate, nor even the honors and sweet joys of the blooming matron.", "그러나 그 시절에 살던 사람들 중에는—적어도 티프턴 근방에는 분명히 아무도—결혼에 대한 관념이 전적으로 삶의 목적에 대한 고양된 열정에서 빛깔을 끌어낸 소녀의 꿈을, 주로 자기 자신의 불꽃으로 타오르며 혼수의 자질구레함도, 식기 무늬도, 심지어 활짝 핀 주부가 누리는 영예와 달콤한 기쁨도 포함하지 않는 그런 열정을 지닌 소녀의 꿈을 공감 어린 마음으로 이해해 줄 사람이 아마 아무도 없었을 것이다."]], [["It had now entered Dorothea’s mind that Mr. Casaubon might wish to make her his wife, and the idea that he would do so touched her with a sort of reverential gratitude.", "캐소본 씨가 그녀를 자신의 아내로 삼고 싶어 할지도 모른다는 생각이 이제 도러시아의 마음속에 들어와 있었고, 그가 그렇게 하리라는 생각은 일종의 경건한 감사의 마음으로 그녀를 어루만졌다."], ["How good of him—nay, it would be almost as if a winged messenger had suddenly stood beside her path and held out his hand towards her!", "얼마나 고마운 일인가—아니, 그것은 마치 날개 달린 사자가 갑자기 그녀가 걷는 길 옆에 나타나 그녀를 향해 손을 내미는 것이나 다름없으리라!"], ["For a long while she had been oppressed by the indefiniteness which hung in her mind, like a thick summer haze, over all her desire to make her life greatly effective.", "오랫동안 그녀는 자신의 삶을 크게 보람 있게 만들고 싶은 모든 열망을 짙은 여름 안개처럼 뒤덮은 채 마음속에 걸려 있던 그 막연함에 짓눌려 있었다."], ["What could she do, what ought she to do?—she, hardly more than a budding woman, but yet with an active conscience and a great mental need, not to be satisfied by a girlish instruction comparable to the nibblings and judgments of a discursive mouse.", "그녀가 무엇을 할 수 있을까, 무엇을 해야 할까?—이제 막 피어나는 여인이나 다름없으면서도, 그래도 활동적인 양심과 큰 정신적 갈망을 지녀, 산만한 생쥐가 갉작거리고 판단을 내리는 정도에 견줄 만한 소녀다운 교육으로는 만족할 수 없는 그녀가."], ["With some endowment of stupidity and conceit, she might have thought that a Christian young lady of fortune should find her ideal of life in village charities, patronage of the humbler clergy, the perusal of “Female Scripture Characters,” unfolding the private experience of Sara under the Old Dispensation, and Dorcas under the New, and the care of her soul over her embroidery in her own boudoir—with a background of prospective marriage to a man who, if less strict than herself, as being involved in affairs religiously inexplicable, might be prayed for and seasonably exhorted.", "어리석음과 자만심을 얼마간 타고났더라면, 그녀는 재산 있는 기독교도 젊은 숙녀란 마을 자선 사업과 신분 낮은 성직자들에 대한 후원, 옛 율법 시대 사라의 사사로운 경험과 새 시대 도르가의 그것을 펼쳐 보이는 『성서 속 여성들』의 정독, 그리고 자기 방 안에서 자수를 놓으며 영혼을 돌보는 일에서 삶의 이상을 찾아야 한다고 생각했을지도 모른다—그 배경에는, 자신만큼 엄격하지는 못하더라도 종교적으로 설명할 수 없는 일들에 얽혀 있는 까닭에 그를 위해 기도하고 때맞춰 권면할 수 있는 남자와의 장래 결혼이 깔려 있을 터였다."], ["From such contentment poor Dorothea was shut out.", "가련한 도러시아는 그러한 만족으로부터 차단되어 있었다."], ["The intensity of her religious disposition, the coercion it exercised over her life, was but one aspect of a nature altogether ardent, theoretic, and intellectually consequent: and with such a nature struggling in the bands of a narrow teaching, hemmed in by a social life which seemed nothing but a labyrinth of petty courses, a walled-in maze of small paths that led no whither, the outcome was sure to strike others as at once exaggeration and inconsistency.", "그녀의 종교적 기질의 강렬함, 그것이 그녀의 삶에 행사하는 강제력이란, 전적으로 열정적이고 이론적이며 지적으로 일관된 천성의 한 단면일 뿐이었다. 그리고 그러한 천성이 편협한 가르침의 끈에 묶여 몸부림치고, 자질구레한 행로들의 미궁, 아무 데로도 이어지지 않는 작은 길들이 벽으로 둘러싸인 미로에 지나지 않는 듯한 사교 생활에 갇혀 있을 때, 그 결과는 다른 사람들에게 과장인 동시에 모순으로 비치기 마련이었다."], ["The thing which seemed to her best, she wanted to justify by the completest knowledge; and not to live in a pretended admission of rules which were never acted on.", "그녀에게 가장 좋아 보이는 것을, 그녀는 가장 완전한 지식으로 정당화하고 싶었으며, 결코 실천되지 않는 규칙들을 인정하는 척하며 살고 싶지 않았다."], ["Into this soul-hunger as yet all her youthful passion was poured; the union which attracted her was one that would deliver her from her girlish subjection to her own ignorance, and give her the freedom of voluntary submission to a guide who would take her along the grandest path.", "그녀의 젊은 열정은 아직 모두 이 영혼의 굶주림 속에 쏟아부어지고 있었다. 그녀를 끌어당기는 그 결합은 자기 자신의 무지에 대한 소녀다운 예속에서 그녀를 해방시켜 주고, 가장 웅대한 길로 그녀를 데려가 줄 안내자에게 자발적으로 복종하는 자유를 그녀에게 줄 그런 것이었다."]], [["“I should learn everything then,”", "“그러면 나는 모든 것을 배우게 되겠지.”"], ["she said to herself, still walking quickly along the bridle road through the wood.", "그녀는 숲속의 승마로를 따라 여전히 빠르게 걸으며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“It would be my duty to study that I might help him the better in his great works.", "“그분의 위대한 저작을 더 잘 도울 수 있도록 공부하는 것이 내 의무가 될 거야."], ["There would be nothing trivial about our lives.", "우리 삶에는 하찮은 것이라곤 없겠지."], ["Every-day things with us would mean the greatest things.", "우리에게는 일상적인 일들이 가장 위대한 일들을 뜻하게 될 거야."], ["It would be like marrying Pascal.", "파스칼과 결혼하는 것이나 마찬가지일 거야."], ["I should learn to see the truth by the same light as great men have seen it by.", "나는 위대한 사람들이 진리를 본 것과 똑같은 빛으로 진리를 보는 법을 배우게 되겠지."], ["And then I should know what to do, when I got older: I should see how it was possible to lead a grand life here—now—in England.", "그러면 나이가 들었을 때 무엇을 해야 할지 알게 될 거야. 어떻게 하면 여기서—지금—영국에서 웅대한 삶을 이끌어 갈 수 있는지 알게 될 거야."], ["I don’t feel sure about doing good in any way now: everything seems like going on a mission to a people whose language I don’t know;—unless it were building good cottages—there can be no doubt about that.", "지금은 내가 어떤 식으로든 선을 행할 수 있을지 확신이 서지 않아. 모든 것이 내가 모르는 언어를 쓰는 사람들에게로 선교를 떠나는 것만 같아.—좋은 오두막을 짓는 일이라면 모를까—그것만은 의심의 여지가 없지."], ["Oh, I hope I should be able to get the people well housed in Lowick!", "아, 로윅 사람들이 제대로 된 집에 살게 해 줄 수 있다면 좋겠어!"], ["I will draw plenty of plans while I have time.”", "시간이 있을 때 설계도를 잔뜩 그려 둬야지.”"]], [["Dorothea checked herself suddenly with self-rebuke for the presumptuous way in which she was reckoning on uncertain events, but she was spared any inward effort to change the direction of her thoughts by the appearance of a cantering horseman round a turning of the road.", "도러시아는 불확실한 일들을 헤아리는 자신의 주제넘은 태도를 자책하며 문득 스스로를 멈춰 세웠으나, 길모퉁이를 돌아 천천히 말을 몰고 나타난 기수의 모습 덕분에 생각의 방향을 바꾸려는 내적 노력은 덜게 되었다."], ["The well-groomed chestnut horse and two beautiful setters could leave no doubt that the rider was Sir James Chettam.", "잘 손질된 밤색 말과 두 마리의 아름다운 세터를 보니 그 기수가 제임스 체텀 경이라는 데 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["He discerned Dorothea, jumped off his horse at once, and, having delivered it to his groom, advanced towards her with something white on his arm, at which the two setters were barking in an excited manner.", "그는 도러시아를 알아보고 즉시 말에서 뛰어내렸고, 말을 마부에게 넘긴 뒤 팔에 무언가 하얀 것을 안고 그녀에게로 다가왔는데, 그것을 향해 두 마리 세터가 흥분한 듯 짖어 대고 있었다."]], [["“How delightful to meet you, Miss Brooke,”", "“브룩 양, 이렇게 만나다니 정말 반갑습니다.”"], ["he said, raising his hat and showing his sleekly waving blond hair.", "그는 모자를 들어 올리며 매끄럽게 물결치는 금발을 드러내고 말했다."], ["“It has hastened the pleasure I was looking forward to.”", "“고대하던 즐거움을 앞당겨 주셨군요.”"]], [["Miss Brooke was annoyed at the interruption.", "브룩 양은 방해받은 것이 짜증스러웠다."], ["This amiable baronet, really a suitable husband for Celia, exaggerated the necessity of making himself agreeable to the elder sister.", "정말로 실리아에게 어울리는 남편감인 이 상냥한 준남작은 손위 언니에게 자신을 호감 가게 보일 필요성을 지나치게 부풀리고 있었다."], ["Even a prospective brother-in-law may be an oppression if he will always be presupposing too good an understanding with you, and agreeing with you even when you contradict him.", "장래의 제부라 할지라도, 늘 당신과 너무 좋은 양해가 이루어진 것을 전제하고, 당신이 반박할 때조차 당신에게 동의한다면, 하나의 압박이 될 수 있는 법이다."], ["The thought that he had made the mistake of paying his addresses to herself could not take shape: all her mental activity was used up in persuasions of another kind.", "그가 자기 자신에게 구애하는 실수를 저질렀다는 생각은 도무지 형체를 갖추지 못했으니, 그녀의 정신적 활동은 온통 다른 종류의 설득에 쓰이고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But he was positively obtrusive at this moment, and his dimpled hands were quite disagreeable.", "그러나 이 순간 그는 확실히 거슬렸고, 보조개 진 그의 두 손은 영 못마땅했다."], ["Her roused temper made her color deeply, as she returned his greeting with some haughtiness.", "돋워진 성미 탓에 그녀는 다소 거만하게 그의 인사에 답하면서 얼굴을 짙게 붉혔다."]], [["Sir James interpreted the heightened color in the way most gratifying to himself, and thought he never saw Miss Brooke looking so handsome.", "제임스 경은 그 짙어진 홍조를 자신에게 가장 흡족한 방식으로 해석했고, 브룩 양이 그토록 아름다워 보인 적이 없었다고 생각했다."]], [["“I have brought a little petitioner,”", "“제가 작은 청원자를 하나 데려왔습니다.”"], ["he said, “or rather, I have brought him to see if he will be approved before his petition is offered.”", "그가 말했다. “아니, 정확히는 청원을 올리기 전에 받아들여질지를 보려고 데려왔지요.”"], ["He showed the white object under his arm, which was a tiny Maltese puppy, one of nature’s most naive toys.", "그가 팔 밑의 하얀 물체를 내보이니, 그것은 자연이 빚어낸 가장 천진난만한 장난감 중 하나인 자그마한 몰티즈 강아지였다."]], [["“It is painful to me to see these creatures that are bred merely as pets,”", "“저는 그저 애완용으로 길러진 이런 짐승들을 보면 마음이 아파요.”"], ["said Dorothea, whose opinion was forming itself that very moment (as opinions will) under the heat of irritation.", "도러시아가 말했는데, 그녀의 의견은 바로 그 순간 (의견이란 으레 그러하듯) 짜증의 열기 속에서 형성되고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, why?”", "“아니, 어째서요?”"], ["said Sir James, as they walked forward.", "두 사람이 앞으로 걸어가며 제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“I believe all the petting that is given them does not make them happy.", "“저는 이 짐승들에게 주어지는 그 모든 귀여움이 그들을 행복하게 해 주지는 못한다고 생각해요."], ["They are too helpless: their lives are too frail.", "너무 무력하잖아요. 그들의 생명은 너무 연약해요."], ["A weasel or a mouse that gets its own living is more interesting.", "스스로 살아가는 족제비나 생쥐가 더 흥미로워요."], ["I like to think that the animals about us have souls something like our own, and either carry on their own little affairs or can be companions to us, like Monk here.", "저는 우리 주위의 동물들이 우리 자신과 비슷한 영혼을 지니고서, 여기 멍크처럼 자기네 작은 일들을 꾸려 가거나 우리의 동반자가 될 수 있다고 생각하는 게 좋아요."], ["Those creatures are parasitic.”", "저런 짐승들은 기생적이에요.”"]], [["“I am so glad I know that you do not like them,”", "“당신이 그것들을 좋아하지 않는다는 걸 알게 되어 정말 기쁩니다.”"], ["said good Sir James.", "마음씨 좋은 제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“I should never keep them for myself, but ladies usually are fond of these Maltese dogs.", "“저야 결코 직접 기르지는 않겠지만, 숙녀분들은 대개 이 몰티즈 개를 좋아하시지요."], ["Here, John, take this dog, will you?”", "자, 존, 이 개를 좀 받아 주겠나?”"]], [["The objectionable puppy, whose nose and eyes were equally black and expressive, was thus got rid of, since Miss Brooke decided that it had better not have been born.", "코와 눈이 똑같이 검고 표정이 풍부한 그 못마땅한 강아지는 이렇게 치워졌으니, 브룩 양이 그것은 태어나지 않는 편이 나았으리라고 결론 내렸기 때문이다."], ["But she felt it necessary to explain.", "그러나 그녀는 설명할 필요를 느꼈다."]], [["“You must not judge of Celia’s feeling from mine.", "“제 마음으로 실리아의 마음을 판단하시면 안 돼요."], ["I think she likes these small pets.", "그 애는 이런 작은 애완동물을 좋아하는 것 같아요."], ["She had a tiny terrier once, which she was very fond of.", "한번은 아주 작은 테리어를 길렀는데, 무척 아꼈지요."], ["It made me unhappy, because I was afraid of treading on it.", "저는 그게 마음에 걸렸어요, 밟을까 봐 겁이 났거든요."], ["I am rather short-sighted.”", "제가 좀 근시라서요.”"]], [["“You have your own opinion about everything, Miss Brooke, and it is always a good opinion.”", "“브룩 양, 당신은 모든 일에 자기만의 의견을 갖고 계시고, 그건 언제나 훌륭한 의견이지요.”"]], [["What answer was possible to such stupid complimenting?", "이런 어리석은 찬사에 무슨 대꾸가 가능하겠는가?"]], [["“Do you know, I envy you that,”", "“그거 아십니까, 저는 당신의 그런 점이 부럽습니다.”"], ["Sir James said, as they continued walking at the rather brisk pace set by Dorothea.", "도러시아가 정해 놓은 다소 빠른 걸음으로 계속 걸으며 제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t quite understand what you mean.”", "“무슨 말씀이신지 잘 모르겠어요.”"]], [["“Your power of forming an opinion.", "“의견을 형성하는 능력 말입니다."], ["I can form an opinion of persons.", "저는 사람에 대해서는 의견을 세울 수 있어요."], ["I know when I like people.", "제가 누군가를 좋아하는지는 알지요."], ["But about other matters, do you know, I have often a difficulty in deciding.", "하지만 다른 문제들에 대해서는, 그거 아십니까, 결정하기가 어려울 때가 많습니다."], ["One hears very sensible things said on opposite sides.”", "양쪽에서 다 그럴듯한 말을 하는 걸 듣게 되거든요.”"]], [["“Or that seem sensible.", "“그럴듯해 보이는 말이겠지요."], ["Perhaps we don’t always discriminate between sense and nonsense.”", "어쩌면 우리가 늘 분별과 무분별을 구분하지는 못하는지도 몰라요.”"]], [["Dorothea felt that she was rather rude.", "도러시아는 자신이 다소 무례했다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Exactly,”", "“바로 그렇습니다.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“But you seem to have the power of discrimination.”", "“하지만 당신에게는 분별하는 능력이 있는 것 같군요.”"]], [["“On the contrary, I am often unable to decide.", "“오히려 저는 결정을 내리지 못할 때가 많아요."], ["But that is from ignorance.", "하지만 그건 무지에서 비롯된 거예요."], ["The right conclusion is there all the same, though I am unable to see it.”", "올바른 결론은 어김없이 거기 있는데, 제가 그것을 보지 못하는 거지요.”"]], [["“I think there are few who would see it more readily.", "“그것을 당신보다 더 선뜻 알아볼 사람은 거의 없을 겁니다."], ["Do you know, Lovegood was telling me yesterday that you had the best notion in the world of a plan for cottages—quite wonderful for a young lady, he thought.", "그거 아십니까, 어제 러브굿이 그러더군요, 당신이 오두막 설계에 관해 세상에서 가장 좋은 발상을 갖고 있다고요—젊은 숙녀로서는 참으로 놀라운 일이라고 생각하더군요."], ["You had a real genus, to use his expression. He said you wanted Mr.", "그의 표현을 빌리자면, 당신에게 진짜 ‘제누스’가 있다고요."], ["Brooke to build a new set of cottages, but he seemed to think it hardly probable that your uncle would consent.", "당신이 브룩 씨에게 새 오두막을 한 채 지어 달라고 했는데, 숙부께서 동의하실 것 같지는 않다고 여기는 듯했습니다."], ["Do you know, that is one of the things I wish to do—I mean, on my own estate.", "그거 아십니까, 그게 바로 제가 하고 싶은 일들 가운데 하나예요—제 말은, 제 영지에서 말입니다."], ["I should be so glad to carry out that plan of yours, if you would let me see it.", "당신만 보여 주신다면, 그 설계를 실행에 옮길 수 있다면 정말 기쁘겠습니다."], ["Of course, it is sinking money; that is why people object to it.", "물론 그건 돈을 까먹는 일이지요. 그래서 사람들이 반대하는 거고요."], ["Laborers can never pay rent to make it answer.", "일꾼들은 그게 수지맞을 만큼 지대를 낼 수 없으니까요."], ["But, after all, it is worth doing.”", "하지만 어쨌든 할 만한 가치가 있는 일입니다.”"]], [["“Worth doing! yes, indeed,”", "“할 만한 가치라니요!"], ["said Dorothea, energetically, forgetting her previous small vexations.", "정말, 그렇고말고요.” 앞서의 사소한 짜증을 잊은 채 도러시아가 힘차게 말했다."], ["“I think we deserve to be beaten out of our beautiful houses with a scourge of small cords—all of us who let tenants live in such sties as we see round us.", "“저는 우리가 가는 노끈 채찍으로 우리의 아름다운 집에서 쫓겨나 마땅하다고 생각해요—주위에서 보는 것 같은 그런 돼지우리에 소작인들을 살게 하는 우리 모두가요."], ["Life in cottages might be happier than ours, if they were real houses fit for human beings from whom we expect duties and affections.”", "오두막에서의 삶이 우리에게 의무와 애정을 기대받는 인간에게 어울리는 진짜 집이라면, 우리 삶보다 더 행복할지도 몰라요.”"]], [["“Will you show me your plan?”", "“당신의 설계를 제게 보여 주시겠습니까?”"]], [["“Yes, certainly.", "“네, 물론이지요."], ["I dare say it is very faulty.", "분명 결함이 많을 거예요."], ["But I have been examining all the plans for cottages in Loudon’s book, and picked out what seem the best things.", "하지만 저는 라우든의 책에 실린 오두막 설계를 죄다 살펴보고 가장 좋아 보이는 것들을 골라냈어요."], ["Oh what a happiness it would be to set the pattern about here!", "아, 이 근방에 그 본보기를 세울 수 있다면 얼마나 행복할까요!"], ["I think instead of Lazarus at the gate, we should put the pigsty cottages outside the park-gate.”", "저는 대문 앞의 나사로 대신에, 그 돼지우리 같은 오두막들을 정원 대문 밖에 두어야 한다고 생각해요.”"]], [["Dorothea was in the best temper now.", "이제 도러시아는 더없이 좋은 기분이었다."], ["Sir James, as brother in-law, building model cottages on his estate, and then, perhaps, others being built at Lowick, and more and more elsewhere in imitation—it would be as if the spirit of Oberlin had passed over the parishes to make the life of poverty beautiful!", "처남으로서 제임스 경이 자기 영지에 본보기가 될 오두막을 짓고, 그러면 어쩌면 로윅에도 다른 오두막들이 지어지고, 그것을 본떠 다른 곳곳에 점점 더 많이 지어진다면—그것은 마치 오베를랭의 정신이 여러 교구를 지나가며 가난한 이들의 삶을 아름답게 만들어 주는 것이나 다름없으리라!"]], [["Sir James saw all the plans, and took one away to consult upon with Lovegood.", "제임스 경은 설계를 모두 보았고, 러브굿과 의논하기 위해 하나를 가져갔다."], ["He also took away a complacent sense that he was making great progress in Miss Brooke’s good opinion.", "또한 그는 브룩 양의 호감 속에서 자신이 크게 진전을 이루고 있다는 흡족한 느낌도 함께 가져갔다."], ["The Maltese puppy was not offered to Celia; an omission which Dorothea afterwards thought of with surprise; but she blamed herself for it.", "그 몰티즈 강아지는 실리아에게 건네지지 않았는데, 그 누락을 도러시아는 나중에 놀라움과 함께 떠올렸으나, 그것을 자기 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["She had been engrossing Sir James.", "그녀가 제임스 경을 독차지하고 있었던 것이다."], ["After all, it was a relief that there was no puppy to tread upon.", "어쨌든 밟을 강아지가 없다는 것은 다행스러운 일이었다."]], [["Celia was present while the plans were being examined, and observed Sir James’s illusion.", "설계를 살펴보는 동안 실리아도 그 자리에 있었고, 제임스 경의 착각을 알아차렸다."], ["“He thinks that Dodo cares about him, and she only cares about her plans.", "‘저 사람은 도도가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다고 생각하지만, 도도는 오로지 자기 설계만 마음에 두고 있는걸."], ["Yet I am not certain that she would refuse him if she thought he would let her manage everything and carry out all her notions.", "그래도 만약 저 사람이 모든 걸 도도 마음대로 꾸려 나가고 도도의 온갖 생각을 실행하게 해 주리라고 도도가 여긴다면, 도도가 저 사람을 거절할지 확신이 안 서."], ["And how very uncomfortable Sir James would be!", "그렇게 되면 제임스 경은 또 얼마나 불편할까!"], ["I cannot bear notions.”", "나는 그런 생각들을 견딜 수가 없어.’"]], [["It was Celia’s private luxury to indulge in this dislike.", "이런 반감에 젖어 드는 것은 실리아의 은밀한 사치였다."], ["She dared not confess it to her sister in any direct statement, for that would be laying herself open to a demonstration that she was somehow or other at war with all goodness.", "그녀는 그것을 어떤 직접적인 진술로도 언니에게 감히 고백하지 못했으니, 그랬다가는 자신이 어떻게든 온갖 선과 맞서 싸우고 있다는 것을 입증당하는 빌미를 주는 셈이었기 때문이다."], ["But on safe opportunities, she had an indirect mode of making her negative wisdom tell upon Dorothea, and calling her down from her rhapsodic mood by reminding her that people were staring, not listening.", "그러나 안전한 기회가 있을 때면, 그녀는 자신의 소극적인 지혜를 도러시아에게 에둘러 일깨우는 방식으로, 사람들이 귀 기울여 듣는 것이 아니라 빤히 쳐다보고 있다는 사실을 상기시켜 언니를 그 황홀한 기분에서 끌어내리곤 했다."], ["Celia was not impulsive: what she had to say could wait, and came from her always with the same quiet staccato evenness.", "실리아는 충동적이지 않았다. 할 말이 있으면 기다릴 수 있었고, 그 말은 언제나 똑같이 조용하고 또박또박 끊어지는 한결같음으로 그녀에게서 나왔다."], ["When people talked with energy and emphasis she watched their faces and features merely.", "사람들이 활기차게 힘주어 이야기할 때면 그녀는 그저 그들의 얼굴과 표정을 살필 뿐이었다."], ["She never could understand how well-bred persons consented to sing and open their mouths in the ridiculous manner requisite for that vocal exercise.", "점잖은 사람들이 어떻게 그런 발성 연습에 필요한 우스꽝스러운 모양으로 입을 벌리고 노래 부르기를 마다하지 않는지 그녀는 도무지 이해할 수 없었다."]], [["It was not many days before Mr. Casaubon paid a morning visit, on which he was invited again for the following week to dine and stay the night.", "며칠 지나지 않아 캐소본 씨는 오전 방문을 했고, 그 자리에서 다음 주에 다시 와서 만찬을 들고 하룻밤 묵으라는 초대를 받았다."], ["Thus Dorothea had three more conversations with him, and was convinced that her first impressions had been just.", "그리하여 도러시아는 그와 세 번 더 대화를 나누었고, 자신의 첫인상이 옳았다고 확신하게 되었다. 그는 그녀가 처음에 상상했던 그대로의 사람이었다."], ["He was all she had at first imagined him to be: almost everything he had said seemed like a specimen from a mine, or the inscription on the door of a museum which might open on the treasures of past ages; and this trust in his mental wealth was all the deeper and more effective on her inclination because it was now obvious that his visits were made for her sake.", "그가 한 말은 거의 모두가 광맥에서 캐낸 표본 같았고, 과거 시대의 보물로 통하는 박물관 문에 새겨진 글귀 같았다. 그리고 그의 정신적 풍요로움에 대한 이 신뢰는, 그의 방문이 그녀를 위한 것임이 이제 분명해졌기에, 그녀의 마음에 더욱 깊이, 더욱 효과적으로 작용했다."], ["This accomplished man condescended to think of a young girl, and take the pains to talk to her, not with absurd compliment, but with an appeal to her understanding, and sometimes with instructive correction.", "이 학식 높은 남자가 어린 소녀를 떠올리고는 어처구니없는 찬사가 아니라 그녀의 이해력에 호소하며, 때로는 가르침을 주는 정정을 곁들여 그녀에게 말을 거는 수고를 마다하지 않은 것이다."], ["What delightful companionship!", "얼마나 즐거운 동반인가!"], ["Mr. Casaubon seemed even unconscious that trivialities existed, and never handed round that small-talk of heavy men which is as acceptable as stale bride-cake brought forth with an odor of cupboard.", "캐소본 씨는 사소한 것들이 존재한다는 것조차 의식하지 못하는 듯했고, 찬장 냄새를 풍기며 내놓는 곰팡내 나는 결혼식 케이크만큼이나 달갑잖은, 둔중한 남자들이 으레 돌리는 그런 잡담을 결코 늘어놓지 않았다."], ["He talked of what he was interested in, or else he was silent and bowed with sad civility.", "그는 자신이 관심 있는 것에 대해 이야기했고, 그렇지 않을 때는 침묵하며 서글픈 정중함으로 고개를 숙였다."], ["To Dorothea this was adorable genuineness, and religious abstinence from that artificiality which uses up the soul in the efforts of pretence.", "도러시아에게 이것은 사랑스러운 진실함이었고, 가식의 노력으로 영혼을 소진시키는 그 인위성에 대한 종교적 절제였다."], ["For she looked as reverently at Mr. Casaubon’s religious elevation above herself as she did at his intellect and learning.", "그녀는 그의 지성과 학식을 우러러보았던 것 못지않게 그가 자신보다 높이 선 종교적 고양도 경건하게 우러러보았기 때문이다."], ["He assented to her expressions of devout feeling, and usually with an appropriate quotation; he allowed himself to say that he had gone through some spiritual conflicts in his youth; in short, Dorothea saw that here she might reckon on understanding, sympathy, and guidance.", "그는 그녀의 경건한 감정 표현에 동의했는데, 대개 적절한 인용을 곁들였다. 그는 젊은 시절 얼마간 영적 갈등을 겪었노라고 스스로 말하기도 했다."], ["On one—only one—of her favorite themes she was disappointed. Mr.", "요컨대 도러시아는 여기서 이해와 공감과 인도를 기대할 수 있겠다고 보았다."], ["Casaubon apparently did not care about building cottages, and diverted the talk to the extremely narrow accommodation which was to be had in the dwellings of the ancient Egyptians, as if to check a too high standard.", "다만 그녀가 좋아하는 주제들 가운데 단 하나—오직 하나—에 대해서는 실망했다. 캐소본 씨는 오두막을 짓는 일에는 전혀 관심이 없는 듯했고, 마치 지나치게 높은 기준을 억누르려는 듯, 고대 이집트인들의 거처에서 누릴 수 있었던 극도로 협소한 주거 공간으로 이야기를 돌렸다."], ["After he was gone, Dorothea dwelt with some agitation on this indifference of his; and her mind was much exercised with arguments drawn from the varying conditions of climate which modify human needs, and from the admitted wickedness of pagan despots.", "그가 떠난 뒤 도러시아는 그의 이러한 무관심을 다소 동요하며 곱씹었고, 인간의 필요를 변화시키는 기후의 다양한 조건에서 끌어낸 논거와, 이교도 폭군들의 익히 알려진 사악함에서 끌어낸 논거로 마음을 한껏 굴렸다."], ["Should she not urge these arguments on Mr. Casaubon when he came again?", "캐소본 씨가 다시 오면 그에게 이런 논거들을 내세워야 하지 않을까?"], ["But further reflection told her that she was presumptuous in demanding his attention to such a subject; he would not disapprove of her occupying herself with it in leisure moments, as other women expected to occupy themselves with their dress and embroidery—would not forbid it when—Dorothea felt rather ashamed as she detected herself in these speculations.", "그러나 더 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 그런 주제에 그의 주의를 요구하는 것은 주제넘은 일이라는 생각이 들었다. 다른 여자들이 옷차림이나 자수로 시간을 보내려 하듯, 그녀가 한가한 틈에 그 일에 몰두하는 것을 그가 못마땅해하지는 않으리라—언젠가는 금하지 않으리라—이런 억측에 빠진 자신을 알아채고 도러시아는 다소 부끄러워졌다."], ["But her uncle had been invited to go to Lowick to stay a couple of days: was it reasonable to suppose that Mr.", "그런데 그녀의 숙부가 며칠 묵으러 로윅으로 오라는 초대를 받았다."], ["Casaubon delighted in Mr. Brooke’s society for its own sake, either with or without documents?", "캐소본 씨가 문서가 있든 없든 브룩 씨의 교제 그 자체를 즐거워한다고 추측하는 것이 과연 합리적일까?"]], [["Meanwhile that little disappointment made her delight the more in Sir James Chettam’s readiness to set on foot the desired improvements. He came much oftener than Mr.", "한편 그 작은 실망은 그녀가 바라던 개량 사업에 선뜻 착수하려는 제임스 체텀 경의 태도를 더욱 기쁘게 여기도록 만들었다."], ["Casaubon, and Dorothea ceased to find him disagreeable since he showed himself so entirely in earnest; for he had already entered with much practical ability into Lovegood’s estimates, and was charmingly docile.", "그는 캐소본 씨보다 훨씬 더 자주 찾아왔고, 그가 그토록 진심을 다하는 모습을 보이자 도러시아는 더 이상 그를 못마땅하게 여기지 않게 되었다. 그는 이미 상당한 실무 능력을 발휘해 러브굿의 견적에 파고들었고, 더없이 고분고분했다."], ["She proposed to build a couple of cottages, and transfer two families from their old cabins, which could then be pulled down, so that new ones could be built on the old sites.", "그녀는 오두막 두 채를 짓고, 두 가족을 그들의 낡은 오두막에서 옮긴 다음, 그 낡은 집은 헐어 버려 옛터에 새집을 지을 수 있게 하자고 제안했다."], ["Sir James said “Exactly,” and she bore the word remarkably well.", "제임스 경은 “바로 그렇습니다”라고 말했고, 그녀는 그 말을 놀라우리만치 잘 견뎌 냈다."]], [["Certainly these men who had so few spontaneous ideas might be very useful members of society under good feminine direction, if they were fortunate in choosing their sisters-in-law!", "확실히 스스로 떠올리는 생각이 그토록 적은 이런 남자들도, 처형이나 처제를 잘 고르는 행운만 따른다면, 훌륭한 여성의 지도 아래 사회의 매우 유용한 일원이 될 수 있으리라!"], ["It is difficult to say whether there was or was not a little wilfulness in her continuing blind to the possibility that another sort of choice was in question in relation to her.", "그녀와 관련하여 또 다른 종류의 선택이 문제되고 있을 가능성에 그녀가 계속 눈멀어 있었던 데에 약간의 고집이 있었는지 없었는지는 말하기 어렵다. 그러나 그녀의 삶은 바로 지금 희망과 활동으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["But her life was just now full of hope and action: she was not only thinking of her plans, but getting down learned books from the library and reading many things hastily (that she might be a little less ignorant in talking to Mr. Casaubon), all the while being visited with conscientious questionings whether she were not exalting these poor doings above measure and contemplating them with that self-satisfaction which was the last doom of ignorance and folly.", "그녀는 자기 설계만 생각하고 있었던 것이 아니라, 서재에서 학술서들을 꺼내 와 많은 것을 서둘러 읽고 있었으니(캐소본 씨와 이야기할 때 조금이라도 덜 무지하기 위해서였다), 그러는 내내 자신이 이 보잘것없는 행위들을 분에 넘치게 떠받들며, 무지와 어리석음에 내려지는 최후의 형벌인 그 자기만족으로 그것들을 바라보고 있는 것은 아닌지 양심의 가책 어린 물음에 시달리고 있었다."]], [["1st Gent.", "신사 1."], ["Our deeds are fetters that we forge ourselves.", "우리의 행위는 우리 스스로 벼려 낸 족쇄다."]], [["2d Gent.", "신사 2."], ["Ay, truly: but I think it is the world That brings the iron.", "그렇소, 참으로. 하지만 그 쇠붙이를 가져오는 것은 세상이라고 나는 생각하오."]], [["“Sir James seems determined to do everything you wish,”", "\"제임스 경은 언니가 바라는 일이라면 무엇이든 해 주기로 작정한 것 같아.\""], ["said Celia, as they were driving home from an inspection of the new building-site.", "새 건축 부지를 둘러보고 집으로 돌아오는 마차 안에서 실리아가 말했다."]], [["“He is a good creature, and more sensible than any one would imagine,”", "\"그분은 선량한 사람이고, 누가 짐작하는 것보다 훨씬 분별 있는 분이야.\""], ["said Dorothea, inconsiderately.", "도러시아가 무심코 말했다."]], [["“You mean that he appears silly.”", "\"그분이 어리석어 보인다는 말이지.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야.\""], ["said Dorothea, recollecting herself, and laying her hand on her sister’s a moment, “but he does not talk equally well on all subjects.”", "도러시아는 정신을 차리고 잠시 동생의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"그저 모든 주제에 똑같이 잘 말하지는 못한다는 거지.\""]], [["“I should think none but disagreeable people do,”", "\"불쾌한 사람들만 그렇게 모든 주제에 잘 말하지 않을까.\""], ["said Celia, in her usual purring way.", "실리아가 늘 그렇듯 가르랑거리는 투로 말했다."], ["“They must be very dreadful to live with.", "\"그런 사람들과 함께 사는 건 정말 끔찍할 거야."], ["Only think!", "생각해 봐!"], ["at breakfast, and always.”", "아침 식사 자리에서도, 언제나 그러는 거잖아.\""]], [["Dorothea laughed.", "도러시아는 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“O Kitty, you are a wonderful creature!”", "\"오, 키티, 너는 정말 놀라운 아이야!\""], ["She pinched Celia’s chin, being in the mood now to think her very winning and lovely—fit hereafter to be an eternal cherub, and if it were not doctrinally wrong to say so, hardly more in need of salvation than a squirrel.", "그녀는 실리아의 턱을 살짝 꼬집었다. 이제 동생이 무척 사랑스럽고 매력적으로 여겨지는 기분이었으니, 훗날 영원한 천사가 되기에 알맞고, 교리상 이렇게 말하는 것이 잘못만 아니라면 다람쥐만큼이나 구원이 필요 없는 아이로 보였다."], ["“Of course people need not be always talking well.", "\"물론 사람이 언제나 말을 잘할 필요는 없지."], ["Only one tells the quality of their minds when they try to talk well.”", "다만 말을 잘하려고 애쓸 때 그 사람 정신의 자질이 드러나는 거야.\""]], [["“You mean that Sir James tries and fails.”", "\"제임스 경이 애를 쓰지만 실패한다는 말이지.\""]], [["“I was speaking generally.", "\"나는 일반적으로 말한 거야."], ["Why do you catechise me about Sir James?", "왜 제임스 경에 대해 나를 추궁하니?"], ["It is not the object of his life to please me.”", "나를 기쁘게 하는 게 그분 인생의 목적은 아니잖아.\""]], [["“Now, Dodo, can you really believe that?”", "\"그러면, 도도, 정말로 그렇게 믿을 수 있어?\""]], [["“Certainly.", "\"물론이지."], ["He thinks of me as a future sister—that is all.”", "그분은 나를 미래의 처형으로 생각해. 그게 다야.\""], ["Dorothea had never hinted this before, waiting, from a certain shyness on such subjects which was mutual between the sisters, until it should be introduced by some decisive event.", "도러시아는 전에 이런 말을 비친 적이 없었다. 이런 주제에 대해 자매가 서로 느끼는 어떤 수줍음 때문에, 결정적인 어떤 사건으로 그 화제가 꺼내어질 때까지 기다려 온 것이었다."], ["Celia blushed, but said at once—", "실리아는 얼굴을 붉혔지만 곧바로 말했다."]], [["“Pray do not make that mistake any longer, Dodo.", "\"제발 더는 그런 착각 하지 마, 도도."], ["When Tantripp was brushing my hair the other day, she said that Sir James’s man knew from Mrs.", "며칠 전에 탠트립이 내 머리를 빗겨 주면서 그랬는데, 제임스 경의 하인이 캐드월러더 부인의 하녀한테서 들었다지 뭐야."], ["Cadwallader’s maid that Sir James was to marry the eldest Miss Brooke.”", "제임스 경이 브룩 댁의 맏딸과 결혼하려 한다고 말이야.\""]], [["“How can you let Tantripp talk such gossip to you, Celia?”", "\"실리아, 어떻게 탠트립이 그런 험담을 너한테 늘어놓도록 내버려 둘 수 있니?\""], ["said Dorothea, indignantly, not the less angry because details asleep in her memory were now awakened to confirm the unwelcome revelation.", "도러시아가 분개하며 말했다. 기억 속에 잠들어 있던 세세한 일들이 이제 깨어나 그 달갑잖은 폭로를 뒷받침해 주었기에 그녀의 화는 한층 더 컸다."], ["“You must have asked her questions.", "\"네가 그 애한테 이것저것 물어봤겠지."], ["It is degrading.”", "천박한 짓이야.\""]], [["“I see no harm at all in Tantripp’s talking to me.", "\"탠트립이 나한테 이야기하는 게 나쁠 게 하나도 없어."], ["It is better to hear what people say.", "사람들이 하는 말을 듣는 게 좋은 거야."], ["You see what mistakes you make by taking up notions.", "봐, 언니는 혼자 생각에 사로잡혀서 얼마나 착각을 많이 하는지. 나는 제임스 경이 언니한테 청혼하려 한다고 확신해."], ["I am quite sure that Sir James means to make you an offer; and he believes that you will accept him, especially since you have been so pleased with him about the plans.", "그리고 그분은 언니가 받아들일 거라고 믿고 있어. 특히 언니가 그 설계도 때문에 그분을 그렇게 마음에 들어 했으니 말이야."], ["And uncle too—I know he expects it.", "그리고 큰아버지도, 내가 알기로 그걸 기대하고 계셔."], ["Every one can see that Sir James is very much in love with you.”", "제임스 경이 언니한테 홀딱 반한 건 누구나 알 수 있다고.\""]], [["The revulsion was so strong and painful in Dorothea’s mind that the tears welled up and flowed abundantly.", "도러시아의 마음속에서 그 반발이 너무도 강하고 고통스러워 눈물이 솟아 하염없이 흘러내렸다."], ["All her dear plans were embittered, and she thought with disgust of Sir James’s conceiving that she recognized him as her lover.", "그녀의 소중한 계획들이 죄다 씁쓸해졌고, 제임스 경이 자신을 연인으로 여긴다고 받아들였다고 생각하는 것이 혐오스러웠다."], ["There was vexation too on account of Celia.", "실리아 때문에 짜증도 났다."]], [["“How could he expect it?”", "\"어떻게 그분이 그런 걸 기대할 수 있어?\""], ["she burst forth in her most impetuous manner.", "그녀가 더없이 격렬한 태도로 쏟아 냈다."], ["“I have never agreed with him about anything but the cottages: I was barely polite to him before.”", "\"나는 그분과 그 오두막들 말고는 어떤 일에도 의견이 맞은 적이 없어. 전에는 그분한테 겨우 예의나 차렸을 뿐이야.\""]], [["“But you have been so pleased with him since then; he has begun to feel quite sure that you are fond of him.”", "\"하지만 그 이후로 언니가 그분을 그렇게 마음에 들어 했잖아. 그분은 언니가 자기를 좋아한다고 꽤 확신하기 시작한 거야.\""]], [["“Fond of him, Celia!", "\"좋아한다니, 실리아!"], ["How can you choose such odious expressions?”", "어떻게 그런 끔찍한 표현을 골라 쓸 수 있니?\""], ["said Dorothea, passionately.", "도러시아가 격앙되어 말했다."]], [["“Dear me, Dorothea, I suppose it would be right for you to be fond of a man whom you accepted for a husband.”", "\"어머나, 도러시아, 남편으로 받아들이려는 남자라면 좋아하는 게 당연히 옳지 않을까.\""]], [["“It is offensive to me to say that Sir James could think I was fond of him.", "\"제임스 경이 내가 자기를 좋아한다고 생각할 수 있다는 말이 나는 모욕적이야."], ["Besides, it is not the right word for the feeling I must have towards the man I would accept as a husband.”", "게다가 그건 내가 남편으로 받아들일 남자에게 가져야 할 감정에 어울리는 말도 아니야.\""]], [["“Well, I am sorry for Sir James.", "\"그래, 나는 제임스 경이 안됐어. 언니한테 말해 주는 게 옳다고 생각했어."], ["I thought it right to tell you, because you went on as you always do, never looking just where you are, and treading in the wrong place.", "왜냐하면 언니는 늘 그러듯이, 지금 자기가 어디에 있는지는 한 번도 살피지 않고 엉뚱한 데를 밟으면서 막무가내로 나아가니까. 언니는 늘 아무도 보지 못하는 걸 보지."], ["You always see what nobody else sees; it is impossible to satisfy you; yet you never see what is quite plain.", "언니를 만족시키기란 불가능해. 그러면서도 정작 빤히 보이는 건 결코 보지 못하잖아."], ["That’s your way, Dodo.”", "그게 언니 방식이야, 도도.\""], ["Something certainly gave Celia unusual courage; and she was not sparing the sister of whom she was occasionally in awe.", "무엇인가가 실리아에게 분명 평소답지 않은 용기를 주었던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 이따금 외경심마저 품던 언니에게도 거침이 없었다."], ["Who can tell what just criticisms Murr the Cat may be passing on us beings of wider speculation?", "우리보다 더 폭넓게 사색하는 존재인 우리에게, 고양이 무르가 어떤 정당한 비판을 던지고 있을지 누가 알겠는가?"]], [["“It is very painful,”", "\"정말 괴로운 일이야.\""], ["said Dorothea, feeling scourged.", "도러시아가 채찍질을 당하는 기분으로 말했다."], ["“I can have no more to do with the cottages.", "\"이제 그 오두막들과는 더 이상 관계할 수 없어."], ["I must be uncivil to him.", "그분한테 무례하게 굴어야겠지."], ["I must tell him I will have nothing to do with them.", "그 일과는 아무것도 함께하지 않겠다고 말해야겠어."], ["It is very painful.”", "정말 괴로운 일이야.\""], ["Her eyes filled again with tears.", "그녀의 눈에 다시 눈물이 고였다."]], [["“Wait a little.", "\"조금만 기다려 봐."], ["Think about it.", "생각을 좀 해 봐."], ["You know he is going away for a day or two to see his sister.", "알다시피 그분은 누이를 만나러 하루 이틀쯤 떠나 계실 거야."], ["There will be nobody besides Lovegood.”", "러브굿 말고는 아무도 없을 거고.\""], ["Celia could not help relenting.", "실리아도 누그러지지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“Poor Dodo,”", "\"가엾은 도도.\""], ["she went on, in an amiable staccato.", "그녀가 다정한 끊긴 말투로 이어 갔다."], ["“It is very hard: it is your favorite fad to draw plans.”", "\"정말 안됐어. 설계도 그리는 건 언니가 제일 좋아하는 별난 취미잖아.\""]], [["“Fad to draw plans!", "\"별난 취미라고!"], ["Do you think I only care about my fellow-creatures’ houses in that childish way?", "너는 내가 그렇게 유치한 식으로 이웃들의 집에나 신경 쓴다고 생각하는 거니?"], ["I may well make mistakes.", "나도 충분히 실수할 수 있지."], ["How can one ever do anything nobly Christian, living among people with such petty thoughts?”", "이렇게 옹졸한 생각만 하는 사람들 틈에서 살면서 어떻게 고결하게 기독교인다운 일을 할 수 있겠어?\""]], [["No more was said; Dorothea was too much jarred to recover her temper and behave so as to show that she admitted any error in herself.", "더 이상 말은 없었다. 도러시아는 너무 격앙되어 마음을 가라앉히고 자신에게 어떤 잘못이 있음을 인정하는 태도를 보이지 못했다."], ["She was disposed rather to accuse the intolerable narrowness and the purblind conscience of the society around her: and Celia was no longer the eternal cherub, but a thorn in her spirit, a pink-and-white nullifidian, worse than any discouraging presence in the “Pilgrim’s Progress.”", "그녀는 오히려 자기를 둘러싼 사회의 견디기 힘든 편협함과 반쯤 눈먼 양심을 탓하고 싶은 심정이었다. 그리고 실리아는 더 이상 영원한 천사가 아니라 그녀의 정신을 찌르는 가시, 분홍빛과 흰빛의 신앙 없는 자, 『천로역정』에 나오는 그 어떤 낙담시키는 존재보다도 더 고약한 존재였다."], ["The fad of drawing plans!", "설계도를 그리는 별난 취미라니!"], ["What was life worth—what great faith was possible when the whole effect of one’s actions could be withered up into such parched rubbish as that?", "한 사람의 행위가 빚어내는 그 모든 효과가 그토록 메마른 잡동사니로 바싹 말라 시들어 버릴 수 있다면, 인생에 무슨 가치가 있으며, 어떤 위대한 신앙이 가능하겠는가?"], ["When she got out of the carriage, her cheeks were pale and her eyelids red.", "마차에서 내릴 때 그녀의 뺨은 창백했고 눈꺼풀은 붉었다."], ["She was an image of sorrow, and her uncle who met her in the hall would have been alarmed, if Celia had not been close to her looking so pretty and composed, that he at once concluded Dorothea’s tears to have their origin in her excessive religiousness.", "슬픔의 화신 같은 모습이었으니, 현관에서 그녀를 맞이한 큰아버지도 놀랐을 테지만, 옆에 바짝 붙어 선 실리아가 어찌나 예쁘고 차분해 보였던지 그는 곧바로 도러시아의 눈물이 그 지나친 신앙심에서 비롯된 것이라고 단정해 버렸다."], ["He had returned, during their absence, from a journey to the county town, about a petition for the pardon of some criminal.", "그는 그들이 없는 동안 어느 범죄자의 사면을 청원하는 일로 주도(州都)에 다녀온 참이었다."]], [["“Well, my dears,”", "\"그래, 얘들아.\" 그가 다정하게 말했다."], ["he said, kindly, as they went up to kiss him, “I hope nothing disagreeable has happened while I have been away.”", "두 사람이 그에게 입맞춤을 하러 다가가자 그가 말을 이었다. \"내가 없는 동안 불쾌한 일은 없었기를 바란다.\""]], [["“No, uncle,”", "\"없었어요, 큰아버지.\""], ["said Celia, “we have been to Freshitt to look at the cottages.", "실리아가 말했다. \"오두막을 보러 프레시트에 다녀왔어요."], ["We thought you would have been at home to lunch.”", "큰아버지가 점심때 댁에 계실 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["“I came by Lowick to lunch—you didn’t know I came by Lowick. And I have brought a couple of pamphlets for you, Dorothea—in the library, you know; they lie on the table in the library.”", "\"점심은 로윅에 들러서 먹었지. 너희는 내가 로윅에 들른 걸 몰랐지. 그리고 너한테 줄 소책자를 두어 권 가져왔다, 도러시아. 서재에 있단다. 서재 탁자 위에 놓여 있어.\""]], [["It seemed as if an electric stream went through Dorothea, thrilling her from despair into expectation.", "마치 전류가 도러시아를 관통하며 절망에서 기대로 그녀를 떨리게 한 것 같았다."], ["They were pamphlets about the early Church.", "그것은 초기 교회에 관한 소책자들이었다."], ["The oppression of Celia, Tantripp, and Sir James was shaken off, and she walked straight to the library.", "실리아와 탠트립과 제임스 경의 압박이 떨쳐졌고, 그녀는 곧장 서재로 걸어갔다."], ["Celia went up-stairs. Mr.", "실리아는 이층으로 올라갔다."], ["Brooke was detained by a message, but when he re-entered the library, he found Dorothea seated and already deep in one of the pamphlets which had some marginal manuscript of Mr.", "브룩 씨는 전갈 때문에 붙들렸으나, 다시 서재로 들어와 보니 도러시아가 이미 자리에 앉아 캐소본 씨의 여백 필사 메모가 적힌 소책자 한 권에 깊이 빠져 있었다."], ["Casaubon’s,—taking it in as eagerly as she might have taken in the scent of a fresh bouquet after a dry, hot, dreary walk.", "마치 메마르고 무더우며 따분한 산책 끝에 신선한 꽃다발의 향기를 들이마시듯, 그렇게 열렬히 그것을 빨아들이고 있었다."]], [["She was getting away from Tipton and Freshitt, and her own sad liability to tread in the wrong places on her way to the New Jerusalem.", "그녀는 티프턴과 프레시트에서, 그리고 새 예루살렘으로 가는 길에 엉뚱한 데를 밟고 마는 자신의 서글픈 버릇에서 벗어나고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Brooke sat down in his arm-chair, stretched his legs towards the wood-fire, which had fallen into a wondrous mass of glowing dice between the dogs, and rubbed his hands gently, looking very mildly towards Dorothea, but with a neutral leisurely air, as if he had nothing particular to say.", "브룩 씨는 안락의자에 앉아 장작불을 향해 다리를 뻗었다."], ["Dorothea closed her pamphlet, as soon as she was aware of her uncle’s presence, and rose as if to go.", "불은 장작 받침쇠 사이에서 빨갛게 달아오른 주사위 같은 놀라운 덩어리로 무너져 내려 있었다. 그는 두 손을 가만히 비비며 도러시아 쪽을 무척 온화하게 바라보았으나, 딱히 할 말이 있는 것은 아니라는 듯 무심하고 한가로운 태도였다."], ["Usually she would have been interested about her uncle’s merciful errand on behalf of the criminal, but her late agitation had made her absent-minded.", "도러시아는 큰아버지가 있다는 것을 알아채자마자 소책자를 덮고 가려는 듯 일어섰다. 평소라면 큰아버지가 범죄자를 위해 베푼 자비로운 일에 관심을 가졌을 테지만, 조금 전의 동요로 그녀는 정신이 산만했다."]], [["“I came back by Lowick, you know,” said Mr.", "\"내가 로윅을 거쳐 돌아왔단다, 알다시피.\""], ["Brooke, not as if with any intention to arrest her departure, but apparently from his usual tendency to say what he had said before.", "브룩 씨가 그녀를 붙잡으려는 의도라기보다는, 분명 앞서 한 말을 또 하는 그의 평소 버릇에서 말했다."], ["This fundamental principle of human speech was markedly exhibited in Mr. Brooke.", "인간 언어의 이 근본 원리가 브룩 씨에게서 두드러지게 드러났다."], ["“I lunched there and saw Casaubon’s library, and that kind of thing.", "\"거기서 점심을 먹고 캐소본의 서재며 뭐 그런 것들을 봤지."], ["There’s a sharp air, driving.", "마차를 모니 공기가 매섭더구나."], ["Won’t you sit down, my dear?", "앉지 그러니, 얘야?"], ["You look cold.”", "추워 보이는데.\""]], [["Dorothea felt quite inclined to accept the invitation.", "도러시아는 그 권유를 받아들이고 싶은 마음이 들었다."], ["Some times, when her uncle’s easy way of taking things did not happen to be exasperating, it was rather soothing.", "큰아버지의 느긋한 처사가 짜증스럽지 않을 때면, 그것은 오히려 마음을 달래 주기도 했다."], ["She threw off her mantle and bonnet, and sat down opposite to him, enjoying the glow, but lifting up her beautiful hands for a screen.", "그녀는 망토와 보닛을 벗고 그의 맞은편에 앉아 그 불기운을 즐기면서도 아름다운 두 손을 들어 올려 가림막으로 삼았다."], ["They were not thin hands, or small hands; but powerful, feminine, maternal hands.", "가늘거나 작은 손이 아니라, 힘이 있고 여성스러우며 모성적인 손이었다."], ["She seemed to be holding them up in propitiation for her passionate desire to know and to think, which in the unfriendly mediums of Tipton and Freshitt had issued in crying and red eyelids.", "그녀는 알고 사유하려는 자신의 열정적인 갈망을 달래 보려는 듯 그 손을 들어 올린 것 같았으니, 티프턴과 프레시트의 비우호적인 환경 속에서 그 갈망은 결국 눈물과 붉은 눈꺼풀로 터져 나왔던 것이다."]], [["She bethought herself now of the condemned criminal.", "이제 그녀는 그 사형수가 생각났다."], ["“What news have you brought about the sheep-stealer, uncle?”", "\"양 도둑에 관해서는 어떤 소식을 가져오셨어요, 큰아버지?\""]], [["“What, poor Bunch?—well, it seems we can’t get him off—he is to be hanged.”", "\"뭐, 가엾은 번치 말이냐? 글쎄, 그 사람을 살려 낼 수는 없을 것 같구나. 교수형에 처해질 거야.\""]], [["Dorothea’s brow took an expression of reprobation and pity.", "도러시아의 이마에 비난과 연민의 표정이 어렸다."]], [["“Hanged, you know,”", "\"교수형이지, 알다시피.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, with a quiet nod.", "브룩 씨가 조용히 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“Poor Romilly!", "\"가엾은 로밀리!"], ["he would have helped us.", "그 사람이라면 우리를 도와주었을 텐데."], ["I knew Romilly.", "나는 로밀리를 알았지."], ["Casaubon didn’t know Romilly.", "캐소본은 로밀리를 몰랐고."], ["He is a little buried in books, you know, Casaubon is.”", "캐소본은, 알다시피, 책 속에 조금 파묻혀 사는 사람이거든.\""]], [["“When a man has great studies and is writing a great work, he must of course give up seeing much of the world.", "\"위대한 연구를 하고 위대한 저작을 쓰는 사람이라면, 물론 세상과 많이 어울리는 일은 포기해야겠지요."], ["How can he go about making acquaintances?”", "어떻게 여기저기 다니며 사람을 사귀겠어요?\""]], [["“That’s true.", "\"그건 사실이지."], ["But a man mopes, you know.", "하지만 사람이 그러면 우울해지거든, 알다시피."], ["I have always been a bachelor too, but I have that sort of disposition that I never moped; it was my way to go about everywhere and take in everything.", "나도 줄곧 독신으로 살아왔지만, 나는 천성이 그래서 한 번도 우울해진 적이 없어. 어디든 돌아다니며 모든 걸 받아들이는 게 내 방식이었지."], ["I never moped: but I can see that Casaubon does, you know.", "나는 우울해진 적이 없어. 하지만 캐소본은 그렇다는 걸 알 수 있어, 알다시피."], ["He wants a companion—a companion, you know.”", "그 사람은 동반자가 필요해. 동반자 말이다, 알다시피.\""]], [["“It would be a great honor to any one to be his companion,”", "\"그분의 동반자가 된다는 건 누구에게나 큰 영광일 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea, energetically.", "도러시아가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“You like him, eh?” said Mr.", "\"네가 그 사람을 좋아하는 게로구나, 응?\""], ["Brooke, without showing any surprise, or other emotion.", "브룩 씨가 별다른 놀라움이나 다른 감정도 내비치지 않고 말했다."], ["“Well, now, I’ve known Casaubon ten years, ever since he came to Lowick.", "\"그래, 자, 나는 캐소본을 십 년 동안 알아 왔어. 그 사람이 로윅에 온 이후로 줄곧 말이야."], ["But I never got anything out of him—any ideas, you know.", "하지만 그 사람한테서 뭔가를 얻어 낸 적은 없었지. 무슨 생각거리 같은 것 말이다, 알다시피."], ["However, he is a tiptop man and may be a bishop—that kind of thing, you know, if Peel stays in.", "그래도 그 사람은 일류 인물이고, 주교가 될지도 몰라. 뭐 그런 거 있잖니, 알다시피, 필이 자리에 남아 있는다면 말이야."], ["And he has a very high opinion of you, my dear.”", "그리고 그 사람은 너를 아주 높이 평가한단다, 얘야.\""]], [["Dorothea could not speak.", "도러시아는 말을 할 수가 없었다."]], [["“The fact is, he has a very high opinion indeed of you.", "\"실은, 그 사람이 너를 정말이지 아주 높이 평가하거든."], ["And he speaks uncommonly well—does Casaubon.", "그리고 캐소본은 말을 유난히 잘하지."], ["He has deferred to me, you not being of age.", "그 사람이 나한테 미뤄 두었어. 네가 아직 성년이 아니니까 말이야."], ["In short, I have promised to speak to you, though I told him I thought there was not much chance.", "요컨대, 나는 너한테 말을 전하겠다고 약속했단다. 가능성이 그리 크지 않을 것 같다고 그 사람한테 말하긴 했지만 말이야."], ["I was bound to tell him that.", "나로서는 그 말을 해 주어야 했어."], ["I said, my niece is very young, and that kind of thing.", "내 조카는 아주 어리다, 뭐 그런 식으로 말했지."], ["But I didn’t think it necessary to go into everything.", "하지만 모든 걸 다 들춰낼 필요는 없다고 생각했어."], ["However, the long and the short of it is, that he has asked my permission to make you an offer of marriage—of marriage, you know,”", "어쨌든 결론을 말하자면, 그 사람이 너한테 결혼 신청을 하게 해 달라고 나한테 허락을 구했단다. 결혼 말이다, 알다시피.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, with his explanatory nod.", "브룩 씨가 설명하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“I thought it better to tell you, my dear.”", "\"너한테 말해 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었지, 얘야.\""]], [["No one could have detected any anxiety in Mr. Brooke’s manner, but he did really wish to know something of his niece’s mind, that, if there were any need for advice, he might give it in time.", "브룩 씨의 태도에서는 그 누구도 불안의 기색을 알아챌 수 없었을 테지만, 그는 정말로 조카의 마음을 좀 알고 싶었다."], ["What feeling he, as a magistrate who had taken in so many ideas, could make room for, was unmixedly kind.", "만일 조언이 필요하다면 제때 해 주려는 것이었다. 그토록 많은 생각을 받아들여 온 치안 판사인 그가 품을 수 있는 감정이라야 순전히 다정한 것뿐이었다."], ["Since Dorothea did not speak immediately, he repeated, “I thought it better to tell you, my dear.”", "도러시아가 곧바로 말을 하지 않자 그가 다시 말했다. \"너한테 말해 주는 게 낫겠다 싶었어, 얘야.\""]], [["“Thank you, uncle,”", "\"고맙습니다, 큰아버지.\""], ["said Dorothea, in a clear unwavering tone.", "도러시아가 또렷하고 흔들림 없는 어조로 말했다."], ["“I am very grateful to Mr. Casaubon.", "\"저는 캐소본 씨께 정말 감사드려요."], ["If he makes me an offer, I shall accept him.", "그분이 청혼하신다면 받아들이겠어요."], ["I admire and honor him more than any man I ever saw.”", "저는 지금껏 본 그 누구보다도 그분을 존경하고 흠모해요.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke paused a little, and then said in a lingering low tone, “Ah?", "브룩 씨는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 낮게 끄는 어조로 말했다."], ["… Well!", "\"아?"], ["He is a good match in some respects.", "…… 그래!"], ["But now, Chettam is a good match.", "그 사람은 어떤 면에서는 좋은 혼처지."], ["And our land lies together.", "하지만 말이다, 체텀도 좋은 혼처야."], ["I shall never interfere against your wishes, my dear.", "게다가 우리 땅이 서로 붙어 있잖니. 나는 결코 네 뜻을 거슬러 간섭하지 않을 거다, 얘야."], ["People should have their own way in marriage, and that sort of thing—up to a certain point, you know.", "결혼이란 자기 뜻대로 해야 하는 거지, 뭐 그런 거 말이다. 어느 정도까지는 말이야, 알다시피."], ["I have always said that, up to a certain point.", "나는 늘 그렇게 말해 왔어, 어느 정도까지는 말이지."], ["I wish you to marry well; and I have good reason to believe that Chettam wishes to marry you.", "나는 네가 좋은 결혼을 하기를 바라. 그리고 체텀이 너와 결혼하고 싶어 한다고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 나한테는 있단다."], ["I mention it, you know.”", "그냥 말해 두는 거야, 알다시피.\""]], [["“It is impossible that I should ever marry Sir James Chettam,”", "\"제가 제임스 체텀 경과 결혼하는 일은 결코 있을 수 없어요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“If he thinks of marrying me, he has made a great mistake.”", "\"만일 그분이 저와 결혼할 생각을 한다면, 그건 큰 착각을 한 거예요.\""]], [["“That is it, you see.", "\"바로 그거란다, 알다시피."], ["One never knows.", "사람 일이란 도무지 알 수가 없어."], ["I should have thought Chettam was just the sort of man a woman would like, now.”", "나는 체텀이야말로 요즘 여자가 좋아할 만한 부류의 남자라고 생각했는데 말이지.\""]], [["“Pray do not mention him in that light again, uncle,”", "\"제발 그분을 다시는 그런 식으로 언급하지 마세요, 큰아버지.\""], ["said Dorothea, feeling some of her late irritation revive.", "도러시아가 조금 전의 짜증이 되살아나는 것을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Brooke wondered, and felt that women were an inexhaustible subject of study, since even he at his age was not in a perfect state of scientific prediction about them.", "브룩 씨는 의아해하며, 여자란 무궁무진한 연구 대상이라고 느꼈다."], ["Here was a fellow like Chettam with no chance at all.", "이 나이의 자기조차도 여자에 대해서는 완벽하게 과학적으로 예측할 수 있는 상태가 아니었으니 말이다. 여기 체텀 같은 사내가 아무런 가망도 없는 것이다."]], [["“Well, but Casaubon, now.", "\"그래, 그런데 이제 캐소본 말이다."], ["There is no hurry—I mean for you.", "서두를 건 없어. 내 말은 너로서는 말이지."], ["It’s true, every year will tell upon him.", "하긴 해마다 그 사람한테는 세월이 표가 날 거야."], ["He is over five-and-forty, you know.", "그 사람은 마흔다섯이 넘었거든, 알다시피."], ["I should say a good seven-and-twenty years older than you.", "너보다 족히 스물일곱 살은 위라고 봐야지."], ["To be sure,—if you like learning and standing, and that sort of thing, we can’t have everything.", "하긴, 네가 학식이며 지위며 뭐 그런 걸 좋아한다면, 모든 걸 다 가질 수는 없는 노릇이지. 그리고 그 사람 수입도 괜찮아."], ["And his income is good—he has a handsome property independent of the Church—his income is good.", "교회 직책과는 별개로 상당한 재산을 가지고 있거든. 수입이 좋아."], ["Still he is not young, and I must not conceal from you, my dear, that I think his health is not over-strong.", "그래도 그 사람이 젊지는 않으니, 얘야, 너한테 숨길 수는 없구나. 내 생각에 그 사람 건강이 그리 튼튼한 편은 아닌 것 같아."], ["I know nothing else against him.”", "그 사람한테 흠잡을 거라곤 그것밖에 모르겠다.\""]], [["“I should not wish to have a husband very near my own age,”", "\"저는 제 또래에 아주 가까운 남편을 원하지는 않아요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with grave decision.", "도러시아가 진지하게 단언하듯 말했다."], ["“I should wish to have a husband who was above me in judgment and in all knowledge.”", "\"저는 판단력과 모든 지식에서 저보다 뛰어난 남편을 갖고 싶어요.\""]], [["Mr.", "브룩 씨가 다시 한번 나직이 되뇌었다."], ["Brooke repeated his subdued, “Ah?—I thought you had more of your own opinion than most girls.", "\"아? 나는 네가 여느 여자애들보다 자기 의견이 더 뚜렷하다고 생각했는데."], ["I thought you liked your own opinion—liked it, you know.”", "네가 자기 의견을 좋아한다고 생각했지. 좋아한다고 말이다, 알다시피.\""]], [["“I cannot imagine myself living without some opinions, but I should wish to have good reasons for them, and a wise man could help me to see which opinions had the best foundation, and would help me to live according to them.”", "\"저는 어떤 의견도 없이 사는 제 모습을 상상할 수가 없어요. 하지만 저는 그 의견에 대한 타당한 근거를 갖고 싶고, 현명한 사람이라면 어떤 의견이 가장 튼튼한 바탕을 지녔는지 제가 알아보도록 도와줄 수 있을 거예요. 그리고 그 의견에 따라 살아가도록 도와줄 거예요.\""]], [["“Very true.", "\"지당한 말이야."], ["You couldn’t put the thing better—couldn’t put it better, beforehand, you know.", "그보다 더 잘 표현할 수는 없겠구나. 미리야 그보다 더 잘 말할 수는 없지, 알다시피."], ["But there are oddities in things,”", "하지만 세상일에는 묘한 데가 있단다.\""], ["continued Mr. Brooke, whose conscience was really roused to do the best he could for his niece on this occasion.", "이번 일에서만큼은 조카를 위해 최선을 다하려는 양심이 정말로 움직인 브룩 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["“Life isn’t cast in a mould—not cut out by rule and line, and that sort of thing.", "\"인생은 틀에 부어 만들어지는 게 아니야. 자와 선을 대고 재단되는 것도 아니고, 뭐 그런 거 말이다."], ["I never married myself, and it will be the better for you and yours.", "나 자신은 결혼하지 않았는데, 그게 너랑 네 식구들한테는 더 잘된 일이지."], ["The fact is, I never loved any one well enough to put myself into a noose for them.", "실은, 나는 누구한테든 올가미에 목을 들이밀 만큼 깊이 사랑해 본 적이 없었어. 그건 올가미거든, 알다시피."], ["It is a noose, you know.", "자, 성미라는 게 있잖니."], ["Temper, now.", "성미가 있단 말이야."], ["There is temper. And a husband likes to be master.”", "게다가 남편이란 주인 노릇을 하고 싶어 하는 법이지.\""]], [["“I know that I must expect trials, uncle.", "\"저도 시련을 각오해야 한다는 건 알아요, 큰아버지."], ["Marriage is a state of higher duties.", "결혼이란 더 높은 의무를 지는 상태잖아요."], ["I never thought of it as mere personal ease,”", "저는 한 번도 그것을 단지 개인적인 안락으로 생각한 적이 없어요.\""], ["said poor Dorothea.", "가엾은 도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“Well, you are not fond of show, a great establishment, balls, dinners, that kind of thing.", "\"그래, 너는 겉치레며 큰 살림이며 무도회며 만찬회며 뭐 그런 걸 좋아하지 않지."], ["I can see that Casaubon’s ways might suit you better than Chettam’s.", "캐소본의 생활 방식이 체텀의 방식보다 너한테 더 맞을 거라는 건 알겠다. 그러니 네 마음대로 하렴, 얘야."], ["And you shall do as you like, my dear.", "나는 캐소본을 막지 않을 거야. 곧바로 그렇게 말했지."], ["I would not hinder Casaubon; I said so at once; for there is no knowing how anything may turn out.", "무슨 일이든 어떻게 풀릴지는 모르는 법이니까. 너는 여느 젊은 아가씨들과 취향이 같지 않잖니."], ["You have not the same tastes as every young lady; and a clergyman and scholar—who may be a bishop—that kind of thing—may suit you better than Chettam.", "그러니 성직자이자 학자인 데다 주교가 될지도 모르는, 뭐 그런 사람이 체텀보다 너한테 더 맞을 수도 있지. 체텀은 좋은 친구야."], ["Chettam is a good fellow, a good sound-hearted fellow, you know; but he doesn’t go much into ideas.", "마음씨 곧고 건전한 친구지, 알다시피. 하지만 생각거리 같은 데로는 그리 파고들지 않아."], ["I did, when I was his age.", "나는 그 사람 나이 때 그랬는데 말이야."], ["But Casaubon’s eyes, now.", "그런데 캐소본의 눈은, 글쎄."], ["I think he has hurt them a little with too much reading.”", "너무 많이 읽어서 눈을 좀 상한 것 같더구나.\""]], [["“I should be all the happier, uncle, the more room there was for me to help him,”", "\"저한테 그분을 도울 여지가 많을수록 저는 그만큼 더 행복할 거예요, 큰아버지.\""], ["said Dorothea, ardently.", "도러시아가 열렬히 말했다."]], [["“You have quite made up your mind, I see.", "\"네가 마음을 단단히 정한 게로구나, 알겠다."], ["Well, my dear, the fact is, I have a letter for you in my pocket.” Mr.", "그래, 얘야, 실은 내 주머니에 너한테 줄 편지가 한 통 있단다.\""], ["Brooke handed the letter to Dorothea, but as she rose to go away, he added, “There is not too much hurry, my dear.", "브룩 씨가 도러시아에게 편지를 건넸으나, 그녀가 일어나 나가려 하자 그가 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 서두를 건 없다, 얘야."], ["Think about it, you know.”", "곰곰이 생각해 보렴, 알다시피.\""]], [["When Dorothea had left him, he reflected that he had certainly spoken strongly: he had put the risks of marriage before her in a striking manner.", "도러시아가 그를 떠난 뒤, 그는 자신이 분명 강하게 말했다고 돌이켜 보았다. 결혼의 위험들을 그녀 앞에 인상 깊은 방식으로 펼쳐 보였으니 말이다."], ["It was his duty to do so.", "그렇게 하는 것이 그의 의무였다."], ["But as to pretending to be wise for young people,—no uncle, however much he had travelled in his youth, absorbed the new ideas, and dined with celebrities now deceased, could pretend to judge what sort of marriage would turn out well for a young girl who preferred Casaubon to Chettam.", "하지만 젊은이들을 위해 현명한 척하는 것으로 말하자면, 아무리 젊은 시절에 여행을 많이 다니고 새로운 사상을 흡수하고 지금은 고인이 된 명사들과 식사를 함께했던 큰아버지라 한들, 체텀보다 캐소본을 좋아하는 어린 처녀에게 어떤 결혼이 잘 풀릴지 어떻게 판단하는 척할 수 있겠는가."], ["In short, woman was a problem which, since Mr. Brooke’s mind felt blank before it, could be hardly less complicated than the revolutions of an irregular solid.", "요컨대 여자란 하나의 문제였으니, 브룩 씨의 정신이 그 앞에서 막막함을 느끼는 터라, 불규칙한 입체의 회전운동에 못지않게 복잡한 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER V.", "# CHAPTER V."]], [["“Hard students are commonly troubled with gowts, catarrhs, rheums, cachexia, bradypepsia, bad eyes, stone, and collick, crudities, oppilations, vertigo, winds, consumptions, and all such diseases as come by over-much sitting: they are most part lean, dry, ill-colored …", "\"열심히 공부하는 학생들은 흔히 통풍, 카타르, 점액병, 악액질, 소화불량, 눈병, 결석, 산통, 미숙증, 폐색증, 현기증, 위창자증, 폐결핵, 그리고 지나치게 오래 앉아 있어 생기는 온갖 질병에 시달린다. 그들은 대개 야위고 메마르며 안색이 나쁘다……"], ["and all through immoderate pains and extraordinary studies.", "모두가 도를 넘은 노고와 비상한 학구열 탓이다."], ["If you will not believe the truth of this, look upon great Tostatus and Thomas Aquinas’ works; and tell me whether those men took pains.”—BURTON’S Anatomy of Melancholy, P.", "이 말의 진실을 믿지 못하겠거든 위대한 토스타투스와 토마스 아퀴나스의 저작을 보라. 그러고 나서 그 사람들이 고생을 했는지 어쨌는지 내게 말해 보라.\""], ["I, s. 2.", "— 버턴, 『우울의 해부』, 제1부, 2절."]], [["This was Mr. Casaubon’s letter.", "이것이 캐소본 씨의 편지였다."]], [["MY DEAR MISS BROOKE,—I have your guardian’s permission to address you on a subject than which I have none more at heart.", "친애하는 브룩 양에게. 저는 무엇보다도 제 마음에 가장 가까이 두고 있는 한 가지 문제에 관해 당신께 말씀드릴 수 있는 후견인의 허락을 받았습니다."], ["I am not, I trust, mistaken in the recognition of some deeper correspondence than that of date in the fact that a consciousness of need in my own life had arisen contemporaneously with the possibility of my becoming acquainted with you.", "제 삶에서 어떤 필요에 대한 자각이 당신과 알고 지내게 될 가능성과 때를 같이하여 솟아났다는 사실 속에, 단순한 시기의 일치를 넘어서는 더 깊은 상응을 알아본 것이 잘못은 아니리라 믿습니다."], ["For in the first hour of meeting you, I had an impression of your eminent and perhaps exclusive fitness to supply that need (connected, I may say, with such activity of the affections as even the preoccupations of a work too special to be abdicated could not uninterruptedly dissimulate); and each succeeding opportunity for observation has given the impression an added depth by convincing me more emphatically of that fitness which I had preconceived, and thus evoking more decisively those affections to which I have but now referred.", "당신을 처음 만난 그 순간에, 저는 당신이 그 필요(말씀드리자면, 너무도 특수하여 결코 포기할 수 없는 작업의 몰두조차 끊임없이 감추어 둘 수는 없었던 그런 애정의 활동과 결부된 필요입니다)를 채워 줄, 더없이 뛰어나고 어쩌면 유일무이한 적임자라는 인상을 받았습니다. 그리고 관찰의 기회가 거듭될 때마다 그 인상은 한층 더 깊어졌으니, 제가 미리 그려 두었던 그 적합함을 더욱 분명하게 확신하게 되었고, 그리하여 방금 말씀드린 그 애정이 한결 결연하게 일깨워졌기 때문입니다."], ["Our conversations have, I think, made sufficiently clear to you the tenor of my life and purposes: a tenor unsuited, I am aware, to the commoner order of minds.", "우리의 대화는, 제 생각에, 제 삶과 목적의 방향을 당신께 충분히 분명하게 전했으리라 봅니다. 그것은 제가 알기로 평범한 부류의 정신에는 어울리지 않는 방향입니다."], ["But I have discerned in you an elevation of thought and a capability of devotedness, which I had hitherto not conceived to be compatible either with the early bloom of youth or with those graces of sex that may be said at once to win and to confer distinction when combined, as they notably are in you, with the mental qualities above indicated.", "하지만 저는 당신에게서 고결한 사유와 헌신의 역량을 발견했으니, 그것은 제가 여태껏 젊음의 이른 만개나, 위에서 말한 정신적 자질과 결합될 때(당신에게서 두드러지게 그러하듯) 비로소 품위를 얻는 동시에 부여하는 그 여성다운 우아함과 양립한다고는 미처 생각하지 못했던 것입니다."], ["It was, I confess, beyond my hope to meet with this rare combination of elements both solid and attractive, adapted to supply aid in graver labors and to cast a charm over vacant hours; and but for the event of my introduction to you (which, let me again say, I trust not to be superficially coincident with foreshadowing needs, but providentially related thereto as stages towards the completion of a life’s plan), I should presumably have gone on to the last without any attempt to lighten my solitariness by a matrimonial union.", "저는 고백하건대 견실하면서도 매력적인 요소들이 이토록 드물게 어우러진 사람을, 곧 더 진지한 노고를 돕고 빈 시간에 매혹을 드리울 수 있는 사람을 만나리라고는 감히 바라지도 못했습니다. 그리고 당신과 인연을 맺게 된 그 사건이 아니었다면(다시 말씀드리지만, 저는 그것이 예고된 필요와 피상적으로 우연히 겹친 것이 아니라, 한 생애의 계획이 완성되어 가는 단계로서 섭리에 따라 그것과 이어져 있다고 믿습니다), 저는 아마도 결혼이라는 결합으로 제 고독을 덜어 보려는 어떤 시도조차 없이 끝까지 살아갔을 것입니다."], ["Such, my dear Miss Brooke, is the accurate statement of my feelings; and I rely on your kind indulgence in venturing now to ask you how far your own are of a nature to confirm my happy presentiment.", "친애하는 브룩 양, 이것이 제 감정에 대한 정확한 진술입니다. 그리고 이제 감히 여쭙고자 하니, 부디 너그러이 헤아려 주시기를 믿으며, 당신 자신의 감정은 제 행복한 예감을 어느 정도까지 확증해 줄 성질의 것인지 묻습니다."], ["To be accepted by you as your husband and the earthly guardian of your welfare, I should regard as the highest of providential gifts.", "당신이 저를 남편으로, 그리고 당신의 안녕을 지키는 지상의 보호자로 받아들여 주신다면, 저는 그것을 섭리가 내려 주는 가장 높은 선물로 여길 것입니다."], ["In return I can at least offer you an affection hitherto unwasted, and the faithful consecration of a life which, however short in the sequel, has no backward pages whereon, if you choose to turn them, you will find records such as might justly cause you either bitterness or shame.", "그 대가로 저는 적어도 여태껏 헛되이 소모하지 않은 애정과, 비록 앞으로 짧을지라도 당신이 들춰 본다 해도 쓰라림이나 수치를 정당하게 안길 만한 기록이라곤 없는 한 생애의 신실한 헌신을 바칠 수 있습니다."], ["I await the expression of your sentiments with an anxiety which it would be the part of wisdom (were it possible) to divert by a more arduous labor than usual.", "저는 당신의 마음을 전해 들을 때까지 불안에 휩싸여 기다리고 있으니, 그것을 (가능하다면) 평소보다 더 고된 노고로 돌리는 것이 지혜로운 일이겠지요."], ["But in this order of experience I am still young, and in looking forward to an unfavorable possibility I cannot but feel that resignation to solitude will be more difficult after the temporary illumination of hope.", "하지만 이런 종류의 경험에서 저는 아직 미숙하며, 불리한 가능성을 내다볼 때, 희망이 잠시 비춘 빛 뒤에 다시 고독에 체념하기란 한층 더 어려우리라 느끼지 않을 수 없습니다."]], [["In any case, I shall remain, Yours with sincere devotion, EDWARD CASAUBON.", "어떤 경우에도, 저는 여전히\n진심 어린 헌신을 담아, 에드워드 캐소본 올림."]], [["Dorothea trembled while she read this letter; then she fell on her knees, buried her face, and sobbed.", "도러시아는 이 편지를 읽으며 떨었다. 그러다 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 묻은 채 흐느꼈다."], ["She could not pray: under the rush of solemn emotion in which thoughts became vague and images floated uncertainly, she could but cast herself, with a childlike sense of reclining, in the lap of a divine consciousness which sustained her own.", "기도할 수 없었다. 생각이 흐릿해지고 형상들이 정처 없이 떠도는 엄숙한 감정의 격류 속에서, 그녀는 그저 어린아이가 기대듯 자신을 지탱해 주는 신성한 의식의 품에 자신을 내맡길 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["She remained in that attitude till it was time to dress for dinner.", "그녀는 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입을 시간이 될 때까지 그 자세로 머물러 있었다."]], [["How could it occur to her to examine the letter, to look at it critically as a profession of love?", "그 편지를 따져 보거나, 사랑의 고백으로서 비판적으로 들여다볼 생각이 어떻게 그녀에게 떠오를 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Her whole soul was possessed by the fact that a fuller life was opening before her: she was a neophyte about to enter on a higher grade of initiation.", "그녀의 온 영혼은 더 충만한 삶이 자기 앞에 열리고 있다는 사실에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그녀는 더 높은 입문의 단계로 들어서려는 초심자였다."], ["She was going to have room for the energies which stirred uneasily under the dimness and pressure of her own ignorance and the petty peremptoriness of the world’s habits.", "그녀는 자신의 무지가 안기는 어둠과 압박, 그리고 세상 관습의 사소한 강제 아래 불안스레 꿈틀거리던 그 에너지를 펼칠 공간을 갖게 될 참이었다."]], [["Now she would be able to devote herself to large yet definite duties; now she would be allowed to live continually in the light of a mind that she could reverence.", "이제 그녀는 크면서도 분명한 의무에 자신을 바칠 수 있을 것이었다. 이제 그녀는 자신이 경외할 수 있는 한 정신의 빛 속에서 끊임없이 살아갈 수 있을 것이었다."], ["This hope was not unmixed with the glow of proud delight—the joyous maiden surprise that she was chosen by the man whom her admiration had chosen.", "이 희망에는 자랑스러운 기쁨의 광채가, 곧 자신이 흠모해 마지않던 그 남자에게서 선택받았다는, 처녀다운 즐거운 놀라움이 섞이지 않은 것이 아니었다."], ["All Dorothea’s passion was transfused through a mind struggling towards an ideal life; the radiance of her transfigured girlhood fell on the first object that came within its level.", "도러시아의 온 열정은 이상적인 삶을 향해 분투하는 한 정신을 거쳐 흘러나왔다. 변모한 그녀의 처녀 시절이 발하는 광휘가 그 시선 안에 들어온 첫 대상 위로 떨어졌다."], ["The impetus with which inclination became resolution was heightened by those little events of the day which had roused her discontent with the actual conditions of her life.", "마음의 기울어짐이 결심으로 바뀌는 그 추진력은, 그날 그녀의 삶의 실제 처지에 대한 불만을 일깨운 그 자질구레한 사건들 때문에 한층 강해졌다."]], [["After dinner, when Celia was playing an “air, with variations,” a small kind of tinkling which symbolized the aesthetic part of the young ladies’ education, Dorothea went up to her room to answer Mr.", "저녁 식사 후, 실리아가 \"변주가 딸린 소품\"을, 곧 젊은 숙녀들이 받는 교육의 미적인 부분을 상징하는 작은 딸랑거림을 연주하고 있을 때, 도러시아는 캐소본 씨의 편지에 답장을 쓰러 자기 방으로 올라갔다."], ["Casaubon’s letter. Why should she defer the answer?", "답장을 미룰 까닭이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["She wrote it over three times, not because she wished to change the wording, but because her hand was unusually uncertain, and she could not bear that Mr.", "그녀는 그것을 세 번이나 고쳐 썼는데, 표현을 바꾸고 싶어서가 아니라, 손이 유난히 떨려서였다. 그리고 캐소본 씨가 자기 필체를 보기 흉하고 알아보기 어렵다고 여길까 봐 견딜 수 없었던 것이다."], ["Casaubon should think her handwriting bad and illegible. She piqued herself on writing a hand in which each letter was distinguishable without any large range of conjecture, and she meant to make much use of this accomplishment, to save Mr.", "그녀는 한 글자 한 글자가 큰 추측 없이도 또렷이 구별되는 필체로 글씨 쓰는 것을 자랑으로 여겼고, 캐소본 씨의 눈을 아끼고자 이 솜씨를 한껏 활용할 작정이었다."], ["Casaubon’s eyes. Three times she wrote.", "세 번을 그녀는 다시 썼다."]], [["MY DEAR MR.", "친애하는 캐소본 씨께."], ["CASAUBON,—I am very grateful to you for loving me, and thinking me worthy to be your wife.", "저를 사랑해 주시고, 당신의 아내가 될 자격이 있다고 여겨 주셔서 진심으로 감사드립니다."], ["I can look forward to no better happiness than that which would be one with yours.", "저는 당신의 행복과 하나가 될 행복보다 더 나은 행복은 바랄 수 없습니다."], ["If I said more, it would only be the same thing written out at greater length, for I cannot now dwell on any other thought than that I may be through life", "제가 더 말씀드린다 해도, 그것은 같은 말을 더 길게 늘어놓는 것일 뿐이니, 지금 제가 평생토록 당신의 사람이 될 수 있으리라는 생각 외에 다른 어떤 생각에도 머물 수 없기 때문입니다."]], [["Yours devotedly, DOROTHEA BROOKE.", "헌신을 담아, 도러시아 브룩 올림."]], [["Later in the evening she followed her uncle into the library to give him the letter, that he might send it in the morning.", "저녁 늦게 그녀는 삼촌을 따라 서재로 들어가, 이튿날 아침에 부쳐 주십사 하고 그 편지를 건넸다."], ["He was surprised, but his surprise only issued in a few moments’ silence, during which he pushed about various objects on his writing-table, and finally stood with his back to the fire, his glasses on his nose, looking at the address of Dorothea’s letter.", "그는 놀랐지만, 그 놀라움은 잠시의 침묵으로 그쳤을 뿐이었다. 그 동안 그는 글 쓰는 탁자 위의 여러 물건을 이리저리 밀어 보다가, 마침내 안경을 코에 걸친 채 벽난로를 등지고 서서 도러시아의 편지에 적힌 수신인 주소를 바라보았다."]], [["“Have you thought enough about this, my dear?”", "\"이 일에 대해 충분히 생각해 봤느냐, 얘야?\""], ["he said at last.", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“There was no need to think long, uncle.", "\"오래 생각할 필요는 없었어요, 삼촌."], ["I know of nothing to make me vacillate.", "저를 망설이게 할 만한 게 아무것도 없으니까요."], ["If I changed my mind, it must be because of something important and entirely new to me.”", "제가 마음을 바꾼다면, 그건 분명 무언가 중대하고 제게 전혀 새로운 일 때문일 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah!—then you have accepted him?", "\"아!—그럼 그를 받아들였다는 게냐?"], ["Then Chettam has no chance?", "그럼 체텀에게는 가망이 없는 거고?"], ["Has Chettam offended you—offended you, you know?", "체텀이 너를 언짢게라도 했느냐—언짢게 말이다, 알겠지?"], ["What is it you don’t like in Chettam?”", "체텀에게서 마음에 들지 않는 게 뭐냐?\""]], [["“There is nothing that I like in him,”", "\"그 사람에게는 제 마음에 드는 게 아무것도 없어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, rather impetuously.", "도러시아가 다소 격하게 말했다."]], [["Mr. Brooke threw his head and shoulders backward as if some one had thrown a light missile at him.", "브룩 씨는 누가 가벼운 물건이라도 던진 듯 머리와 어깨를 뒤로 젖혔다."], ["Dorothea immediately felt some self-rebuke, and said—", "도러시아는 곧 얼마간 스스로를 나무라는 마음이 들어 말했다."]], [["“I mean in the light of a husband.", "\"남편으로서 말이에요."], ["He is very kind, I think—really very good about the cottages.", "그분은 무척 친절한 분이에요, 정말로. 오두막집 일에는 정말 잘해 주시고요."], ["A well-meaning man.”", "선의를 지닌 분이죠.\""]], [["“But you must have a scholar, and that sort of thing?", "\"하지만 너는 학자라든가, 그런 부류여야 한다는 거지?"], ["Well, it lies a little in our family.", "음, 그런 게 우리 집안에 좀 흐르긴 하지."], ["I had it myself—that love of knowledge, and going into everything—a little too much—it took me too far; though that sort of thing doesn’t often run in the female-line; or it runs underground like the rivers in Greece, you know—it comes out in the sons.", "나도 그랬으니까—그 지식에 대한 사랑, 모든 걸 파고드는 것 말이다—좀 지나쳤지—나를 너무 멀리 끌고 갔어. 하긴 그런 건 여자 쪽으로는 잘 흐르지 않지."], ["Clever sons, clever mothers.", "아니면 그리스의 강처럼 땅속으로 흐르다가 말이다—아들들에게서 솟아나는 거지."], ["I went a good deal into that, at one time.", "영리한 아들에 영리한 어머니."], ["However, my dear, I have always said that people should do as they like in these things, up to a certain point.", "나도 한때 그 문제를 꽤 파고들었단다. 하지만 얘야, 나는 늘 이런 일에서는 어느 정도까지는 사람이 좋을 대로 해야 한다고 말해 왔다."], ["I couldn’t, as your guardian, have consented to a bad match.", "후견인으로서 나쁜 혼처에 동의할 수는 없었을 게다."], ["But Casaubon stands well: his position is good.", "하지만 캐소본은 평판이 좋아. 처지가 괜찮지."], ["I am afraid Chettam will be hurt, though, and Mrs. Cadwallader will blame me.”", "다만 체텀이 상처를 받을까 걱정이고, 캐드월러더 부인이 나를 탓할까 걱정이로구나.\""]], [["That evening, of course, Celia knew nothing of what had happened.", "물론 그날 저녁 실리아는 무슨 일이 있었는지 전혀 알지 못했다."], ["She attributed Dorothea’s abstracted manner, and the evidence of further crying since they had got home, to the temper she had been in about Sir James Chettam and the buildings, and was careful not to give further offence: having once said what she wanted to say, Celia had no disposition to recur to disagreeable subjects.", "그녀는 도러시아의 멍한 태도와, 집에 돌아온 뒤로 다시 운 흔적을, 제임스 체텀 경과 건물 일로 언니가 빠져 있던 언짢은 기분 탓으로 돌렸고, 더 이상 기분을 상하게 하지 않으려 조심했다. 일단 하고 싶은 말을 하고 나면, 실리아는 불쾌한 화제로 되돌아가는 성미가 아니었다."], ["It had been her nature when a child never to quarrel with any one—only to observe with wonder that they quarrelled with her, and looked like turkey-cocks; whereupon she was ready to play at cat’s cradle with them whenever they recovered themselves.", "어릴 적부터 그녀의 천성은 누구와도 다투지 않는 것이었다. 그저 사람들이 자기와 다투며 칠면조 수컷처럼 구는 것을 의아하게 바라볼 뿐이었고, 그들이 마음을 가라앉히기만 하면 언제든 함께 실뜨기 놀이를 할 준비가 되어 있었다."], ["And as to Dorothea, it had always been her way to find something wrong in her sister’s words, though Celia inwardly protested that she always said just how things were, and nothing else: she never did and never could put words together out of her own head.", "그리고 도러시아로 말하자면, 동생의 말에서 늘 무언가 잘못된 점을 찾아내는 것이 그녀의 버릇이었다. 비록 실리아는 자기가 늘 있는 그대로의 사실만 말할 뿐 다른 건 말하지 않는다고 속으로 항변했지만 말이다."], ["But the best of Dodo was, that she did not keep angry for long together.", "그녀는 제 머릿속에서 지어낸 말을 엮어 본 적도 없고 엮을 줄도 몰랐다."], ["Now, though they had hardly spoken to each other all the evening, yet when Celia put by her work, intending to go to bed, a proceeding in which she was always much the earlier, Dorothea, who was seated on a low stool, unable to occupy herself except in meditation, said, with the musical intonation which in moments of deep but quiet feeling made her speech like a fine bit of recitative—", "그래도 도도의 가장 좋은 점은 오래 화를 품고 있지 않는다는 것이었다. 이제 둘은 저녁 내내 거의 말을 주고받지 않았지만, 실리아가 일감을 치우며—잠자리에 드는 일에서는 언제나 그녀가 훨씬 일렀다—자러 가려 할 때, 명상 말고는 달리 할 일이 없어 낮은 의자에 앉아 있던 도러시아가, 깊지만 잔잔한 감정의 순간이면 그녀의 말을 한 가락 아름다운 영창처럼 만드는 그 음악적인 억양으로 말했다."]], [["“Celia, dear, come and kiss me,”", "\"실리아, 얘, 이리 와서 입맞춰 주렴.\""], ["holding her arms open as she spoke.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하며 두 팔을 벌렸다."]], [["Celia knelt down to get the right level and gave her little butterfly kiss, while Dorothea encircled her with gentle arms and pressed her lips gravely on each cheek in turn.", "실리아는 알맞은 높이를 맞추려 무릎을 꿇고 나비처럼 가벼운 입맞춤을 했고, 그동안 도러시아는 부드러운 팔로 동생을 감싸 안고 양 볼에 차례로 진지하게 입술을 댔다."]], [["“Don’t sit up, Dodo, you are so pale to-night: go to bed soon,”", "\"늦게까지 앉아 있지 마, 도도, 오늘 밤은 너무 창백해 보여."], ["said Celia, in a comfortable way, without any touch of pathos.", "어서 자.\" 실리아가 애틋한 기색이라곤 없이 편안한 투로 말했다."]], [["“No, dear, I am very, very happy,”", "\"아냐, 얘, 난 아주, 아주 행복해.\""], ["said Dorothea, fervently.", "도러시아가 열렬하게 말했다."]], [["“So much the better,”", "'그렇다면 더 잘됐지.'"], ["thought Celia.", "실리아는 생각했다."], ["“But how strangely Dodo goes from one extreme to the other.”", "'하지만 도도는 어쩌면 저리도 이상하게 한 극단에서 다른 극단으로 오가는 걸까.'"]], [["The next day, at luncheon, the butler, handing something to Mr.", "이튿날 점심때, 집사가 브룩 씨에게 무언가를 건네며 말했다."], ["Brooke, said, “Jonas is come back, sir, and has brought this letter.”", "\"조나스가 돌아왔습니다, 나리, 이 편지를 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "브룩 씨는 편지를 읽고 나서 도러시아 쪽으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"캐소본이다, 얘야."], ["Brooke read the letter, and then, nodding toward Dorothea, said, “Casaubon, my dear: he will be here to dinner; he didn’t wait to write more—didn’t wait, you know.”", "저녁 식사 때 여기 온다는구나. 더 길게 쓸 겨를도 없었던 게야—겨를이 없었던 거지, 알겠지.\""]], [["It could not seem remarkable to Celia that a dinner guest should be announced to her sister beforehand, but, her eyes following the same direction as her uncle’s, she was struck with the peculiar effect of the announcement on Dorothea.", "저녁 손님이 언니에게 미리 알려진다는 것이 실리아에게 별스럽게 보일 리는 없었지만, 삼촌과 같은 방향으로 시선을 따라가던 그녀는 그 통고가 도러시아에게 미친 묘한 효과에 놀랐다."], ["It seemed as if something like the reflection of a white sunlit wing had passed across her features, ending in one of her rare blushes.", "마치 햇빛에 빛나는 하얀 날개의 반영 같은 것이 언니의 얼굴을 스쳐 지나가, 좀처럼 보기 드문 그녀의 홍조 가운데 하나로 끝맺는 것 같았다."], ["For the first time it entered into Celia’s mind that there might be something more between Mr. Casaubon and her sister than his delight in bookish talk and her delight in listening.", "캐소본 씨와 언니 사이에, 그가 책 이야기를 좋아하고 언니가 그것을 듣기 좋아하는 것 이상의 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 실리아의 마음에 처음으로 떠올랐다."], ["Hitherto she had classed the admiration for this “ugly”", "여태껏 그녀는 이 \"못생기고\""], ["and learned acquaintance with the admiration for Monsieur Liret at Lausanne, also ugly and learned.", "학식 있는 지인에 대한 흠모를, 로잔에서 만난 리레 씨, 역시 못생기고 학식 있던 그 사람에 대한 흠모와 같은 부류로 분류해 왔던 것이다."], ["Dorothea had never been tired of listening to old Monsieur Liret when Celia’s feet were as cold as possible, and when it had really become dreadful to see the skin of his bald head moving about.", "실리아의 발이 더없이 시릴 때에도, 그리고 그 대머리 살갗이 움찔거리는 꼴을 보기가 정말이지 끔찍해질 때에도, 도러시아는 늙은 리레 씨의 말을 듣는 데 결코 싫증을 내지 않았다."], ["Why then should her enthusiasm not extend to Mr. Casaubon simply in the same way as to Monsieur Liret?", "그렇다면 어째서 그녀의 열광이 리레 씨에게 향한 것과 똑같은 식으로 캐소본 씨에게도 그저 뻗어 나가지 못하겠는가?"], ["And it seemed probable that all learned men had a sort of schoolmaster’s view of young people.", "그리고 학식 있는 사람이란 다들 젊은이들을 일종의 학교 선생 같은 눈으로 본다는 것도 충분히 있을 법한 일이었다."]], [["But now Celia was really startled at the suspicion which had darted into her mind.", "하지만 이제 실리아는 마음속에 번뜩 떠오른 그 의혹에 정말로 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["She was seldom taken by surprise in this way, her marvellous quickness in observing a certain order of signs generally preparing her to expect such outward events as she had an interest in. Not that she now imagined Mr.", "그녀가 이런 식으로 불시에 놀라는 일은 드물었으니, 어떤 종류의 징후를 관찰하는 데 비상하게 빠른 덕분에 대개는 자신이 관심을 둔 외적인 일들을 미리 예상할 수 있었던 것이다."], ["Casaubon to be already an accepted lover: she had only begun to feel disgust at the possibility that anything in Dorothea’s mind could tend towards such an issue.", "그렇다고 그녀가 지금 캐소본 씨를 이미 받아들여진 구혼자라고 상상한 것은 아니었다. 다만 도러시아의 마음속 무언가가 그런 결말을 향해 기울 수도 있다는 가능성에 비로소 역겨움을 느끼기 시작했을 뿐이다."], ["Here was something really to vex her about Dodo: it was all very well not to accept Sir James Chettam, but the idea of marrying Mr.", "이거야말로 도도에 대해 정말로 속상한 일이었다. 제임스 체텀 경을 받아들이지 않는 것이야 그렇다 쳐도, 캐소본 씨와 결혼한다는 생각이라니!"], ["Casaubon! Celia felt a sort of shame mingled with a sense of the ludicrous.", "실리아는 일종의 수치심에 우스꽝스럽다는 느낌이 뒤섞인 기분이었다."], ["But perhaps Dodo, if she were really bordering on such an extravagance, might be turned away from it: experience had often shown that her impressibility might be calculated on.", "하지만 어쩌면 도도가 정말로 그런 터무니없는 일에 발을 들이려 한다 해도, 거기서 돌이켜 세울 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 언니가 곧잘 마음이 흔들린다는 것은 경험으로 자주 드러난 바였으니까."], ["The day was damp, and they were not going to walk out, so they both went up to their sitting-room; and there Celia observed that Dorothea, instead of settling down with her usual diligent interest to some occupation, simply leaned her elbow on an open book and looked out of the window at the great cedar silvered with the damp.", "날이 눅눅하여 산책을 나갈 참은 아니었으므로, 둘은 함께 거실로 올라갔다. 그리고 거기서 실리아는, 도러시아가 평소처럼 부지런한 흥미를 가지고 무슨 일에 자리를 잡는 대신, 그저 펼쳐진 책에 팔꿈치를 괴고 창밖으로 눅눅한 기운에 은빛으로 물든 커다란 삼나무를 내다보고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She herself had taken up the making of a toy for the curate’s children, and was not going to enter on any subject too precipitately.", "그녀 자신은 보좌 신부의 아이들에게 줄 장난감을 만들기 시작했고, 어떤 화제든 너무 성급하게 꺼낼 생각은 없었다."]], [["Dorothea was in fact thinking that it was desirable for Celia to know of the momentous change in Mr.", "사실 도러시아는, 캐소본 씨가 지난번 이 집에 다녀간 이후로 그의 처지에 일어난 중대한 변화를 실리아가 아는 것이 바람직하다고 생각하고 있었다."], ["Casaubon’s position since he had last been in the house: it did not seem fair to leave her in ignorance of what would necessarily affect her attitude towards him; but it was impossible not to shrink from telling her.", "동생이 그를 대하는 태도에 필연적으로 영향을 미칠 일을 모르는 채로 두는 것은 공정하지 않게 여겨졌던 것이다. 하지만 그 말을 꺼내기를 움츠리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Dorothea accused herself of some meanness in this timidity: it was always odious to her to have any small fears or contrivances about her actions, but at this moment she was seeking the highest aid possible that she might not dread the corrosiveness of Celia’s pretty carnally minded prose.", "도러시아는 이 소심함 속에서 어떤 비겁함을 들어 자신을 책망했다. 자기 행동에 대해 조그만 두려움이나 잔꾀를 품는 것은 그녀에게 늘 혐오스러운 일이었으나, 바로 이 순간 그녀는 실리아의 예쁘장하고 속되리만치 현세적인 산문이 지닌 부식성을 두려워하지 않으려, 가능한 한 가장 높은 도움을 구하고 있었다."], ["Her reverie was broken, and the difficulty of decision banished, by Celia’s small and rather guttural voice speaking in its usual tone, of a remark aside or a “by the bye.”", "그녀의 몽상은 깨어졌고, 결단을 내리기 어렵던 망설임도 사라졌으니, 실리아가 평소의 어조로, 곁들이는 말이나 \"그러고 보니\" 하고 던지는 한마디처럼, 작고 다소 목구멍에서 울리는 목소리로 입을 열었기 때문이다."]], [["“Is any one else coming to dine besides Mr. Casaubon?”", "\"캐소본 씨 말고 또 누가 저녁 먹으러 오나?\""]], [["“Not that I know of.”", "\"내가 알기로는 없어.\""]], [["“I hope there is some one else.", "\"누가 또 왔으면 좋겠는데."], ["Then I shall not hear him eat his soup so.”", "그러면 그분이 수프 드시는 소리를 그렇게 안 들어도 될 테니까.\""]], [["“What is there remarkable about his soup-eating?”", "\"그분이 수프 드시는 게 뭐가 그리 별스럽다는 거니?\""]], [["“Really, Dodo, can’t you hear how he scrapes his spoon?", "\"정말이지, 도도, 언니는 그분이 숟가락 긁는 소리가 안 들려?"], ["And he always blinks before he speaks.", "게다가 말하기 전에 늘 눈을 깜박이잖아."], ["I don’t know whether Locke blinked, but I’m sure I am sorry for those who sat opposite to him if he did.”", "로크가 눈을 깜박였는지는 모르겠지만, 만약 그랬다면 그분 맞은편에 앉았던 사람들이 참 딱했겠다 싶어.\""]], [["“Celia,”", "\"실리아.\""], ["said Dorothea, with emphatic gravity, “pray don’t make any more observations of that kind.”", "도러시아가 단호하고 엄숙하게 말했다. \"제발 그런 식의 말은 더 이상 하지 마라.\""]], [["“Why not?", "\"왜 안 돼?"], ["They are quite true,”", "다 사실인걸.\""], ["returned Celia, who had her reasons for persevering, though she was beginning to be a little afraid.", "실리아가 받아쳤다. 슬슬 좀 겁이 나기 시작했지만, 그녀에게는 굽히지 않을 나름의 이유가 있었다."]], [["“Many things are true which only the commonest minds observe.”", "\"가장 평범한 정신만이 보아 내는 것 중에도 사실인 것이 많지.\""]], [["“Then I think the commonest minds must be rather useful.", "\"그렇다면 가장 평범한 정신도 꽤 쓸모가 있나 봐."], ["I think it is a pity Mr. Casaubon’s mother had not a commoner mind: she might have taught him better.”", "캐소본 씨의 어머니가 좀 더 평범한 정신을 가지지 못했던 게 안타까워."], ["Celia was inwardly frightened, and ready to run away, now she had hurled this light javelin.", "그랬으면 아들을 좀 더 잘 가르쳤을 텐데.\" 이 가벼운 투창을 던지고 나자 실리아는 속으로 겁이 나서 달아날 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["Dorothea’s feelings had gathered to an avalanche, and there could be no further preparation.", "도러시아의 감정은 눈사태처럼 쌓여 있었고, 더 이상 준비할 여지는 없었다."]], [["“It is right to tell you, Celia, that I am engaged to marry Mr.", "\"네게 알려 주는 것이 옳겠구나, 실리아."], ["Casaubon.”", "나는 캐소본 씨와 결혼하기로 약혼했어.\""]], [["Perhaps Celia had never turned so pale before.", "아마 실리아는 그토록 창백해진 적이 없었을 것이다."], ["The paper man she was making would have had his leg injured, but for her habitual care of whatever she held in her hands.", "그녀가 만들고 있던 종이 인형은, 손에 쥔 것은 무엇이든 조심하는 그녀의 습관이 아니었다면 다리가 상했을 것이다."], ["She laid the fragile figure down at once, and sat perfectly still for a few moments.", "그녀는 그 부서지기 쉬운 인형을 곧장 내려놓고 잠시 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다."], ["When she spoke there was a tear gathering.", "다시 입을 열었을 때 눈에는 눈물이 고이고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, Dodo, I hope you will be happy.”", "\"오, 도도, 언니가 행복하길 바라.\""], ["Her sisterly tenderness could not but surmount other feelings at this moment, and her fears were the fears of affection.", "이 순간 그녀의 자매다운 다정함은 다른 감정들을 누르지 않을 수 없었고, 그녀의 두려움은 애정에서 나온 두려움이었다."]], [["Dorothea was still hurt and agitated.", "도러시아는 여전히 상처받고 동요한 상태였다."]], [["“It is quite decided, then?”", "\"그럼 완전히 정해진 거야?\""], ["said Celia, in an awed under tone.", "실리아가 외경에 찬 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“And uncle knows?”", "\"삼촌도 아셔?\""]], [["“I have accepted Mr. Casaubon’s offer.", "\"나는 캐소본 씨의 청혼을 받아들였어."], ["My uncle brought me the letter that contained it; he knew about it beforehand.”", "삼촌이 그 청혼이 담긴 편지를 가져다주셨고, 미리 알고 계셨어.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, if I have said anything to hurt you, Dodo,”", "\"내가 언니 마음을 아프게 한 말을 했다면 미안해, 도도.\""], ["said Celia, with a slight sob.", "실리아가 가볍게 흐느끼며 말했다."], ["She never could have thought that she should feel as she did.", "그녀는 자기가 이런 기분을 느끼게 되리라곤 결코 생각지 못했다."], ["There was something funereal in the whole affair, and Mr. Casaubon seemed to be the officiating clergyman, about whom it would be indecent to make remarks.", "이 모든 일에는 어딘가 장례식 같은 데가 있었고, 캐소본 씨는 그 예식을 집전하는 성직자 같아서, 그에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵 말하는 것은 무례한 일처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["“Never mind, Kitty, do not grieve.", "\"신경 쓰지 마, 키티, 슬퍼하지 마."], ["We should never admire the same people.", "우리가 같은 사람을 좋아할 수는 없는 노릇이지."], ["I often offend in something of the same way; I am apt to speak too strongly of those who don’t please me.”", "나도 곧잘 비슷한 식으로 누구의 비위를 거스르곤 해. 마음에 들지 않는 사람들에 대해서는 너무 심하게 말하는 버릇이 있거든.\""]], [["In spite of this magnanimity Dorothea was still smarting: perhaps as much from Celia’s subdued astonishment as from her small criticisms.", "이런 관대함에도 불구하고 도러시아는 여전히 쓰라렸다. 어쩌면 실리아의 자질구레한 비판 못지않게, 그녀의 억눌린 놀라움 때문이었는지도 모른다."], ["Of course all the world round Tipton would be out of sympathy with this marriage.", "물론 팁턴 일대의 온 세상이 이 결혼에 공감하지 않을 것이었다."], ["Dorothea knew of no one who thought as she did about life and its best objects.", "도러시아는 삶과 그 가장 고귀한 목적에 대해 자기처럼 생각하는 사람을 단 한 명도 알지 못했다."]], [["Nevertheless before the evening was at an end she was very happy.", "그럼에도 그날 저녁이 끝나기 전에 그녀는 무척 행복했다."], ["In an hour’s tête-à-tête with Mr. Casaubon she talked to him with more freedom than she had ever felt before, even pouring out her joy at the thought of devoting herself to him, and of learning how she might best share and further all his great ends.", "캐소본 씨와 단둘이 보낸 한 시간 동안, 그녀는 일찍이 느껴 본 적 없는 자유로움으로 그에게 이야기했고, 그에게 자신을 바치겠다는 생각에서, 그리고 그의 위대한 목적들을 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 나누고 진척시킬 수 있을지 배우겠다는 생각에서 우러나는 기쁨을 쏟아 내기까지 했다."], ["Mr. Casaubon was touched with an unknown delight (what man would not have been?) at this childlike unrestrained ardor: he was not surprised (what lover would have been?) that he should be the object of it.", "캐소본 씨는 이 어린아이같이 거리낌 없는 열정에 미지의 기쁨을 느끼며 감동했고(어느 남자인들 그러지 않았겠는가?), 자신이 그 열정의 대상이 되었다는 데에는 놀라지 않았다(어느 연인인들 놀랐겠는가?)."]], [["“My dear young lady—Miss Brooke—Dorothea!”", "\"내 사랑하는 아가씨—브룩 양—도러시아!\""], ["he said, pressing her hand between his hands, “this is a happiness greater than I had ever imagined to be in reserve for me.", "그가 그녀의 손을 두 손 사이에 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"이것은 제가 저를 위해 마련되어 있으리라고는 일찍이 상상도 못 했던 더없이 큰 행복입니다."], ["That I should ever meet with a mind and person so rich in the mingled graces which could render marriage desirable, was far indeed from my conception.", "결혼을 바람직하게 만들어 줄 그 어우러진 우아함을 정신과 인품에 이토록 풍성하게 갖춘 분을 만나리라고는, 참으로 제 머릿속에 떠올려 보지도 못한 일이었습니다."], ["You have all—nay, more than all—those qualities which I have ever regarded as the characteristic excellences of womanhood.", "당신은 제가 여태껏 여성다움의 특징적인 미덕이라 여겨 온 그 모든 자질을—아니, 그 이상을—지니고 계십니다."], ["The great charm of your sex is its capability of an ardent self-sacrificing affection, and herein we see its fitness to round and complete the existence of our own.", "여성의 위대한 매력은 열렬하고 자기희생적인 애정을 발휘할 수 있는 능력에 있으니, 바로 여기서 우리는 그것이 우리 남성의 존재를 완전하게 둥글려 채워 주기에 알맞다는 것을 봅니다."], ["Hitherto I have known few pleasures save of the severer kind: my satisfactions have been those of the solitary student.", "여태껏 저는 더 엄격한 종류의 즐거움 말고는 거의 알지 못했습니다. 제 만족이라곤 고독한 학자의 만족이었지요."], ["I have been little disposed to gather flowers that would wither in my hand, but now I shall pluck them with eagerness, to place them in your bosom.”", "손안에서 시들어 버릴 꽃을 따 모으려는 마음은 별로 없었습니다만, 이제는 그것을 당신의 가슴에 꽂아 드리려 기꺼이 따겠습니다.\""]], [["No speech could have been more thoroughly honest in its intention: the frigid rhetoric at the end was as sincere as the bark of a dog, or the cawing of an amorous rook.", "이보다 더 철저하게 그 의도에 있어 정직한 말은 없었을 것이다. 그 끝의 차가운 수사조차도 개가 짖는 소리나 사랑에 빠진 띠까마귀의 울음소리만큼이나 진실했다."], ["Would it not be rash to conclude that there was no passion behind those sonnets to Delia which strike us as the thin music of a mandolin?", "만돌린의 가냘픈 가락처럼 우리에게 들리는, 델리아에게 바친 그 소네트들 뒤에 아무런 열정도 없었다고 결론짓는 것은 경솔한 일이 아니겠는가?"]], [["Dorothea’s faith supplied all that Mr.", "도러시아의 믿음은 캐소본 씨의 말이 미처 다 하지 못한 듯한 모든 것을 채워 넣었다."], ["Casaubon’s words seemed to leave unsaid: what believer sees a disturbing omission or infelicity?", "신앙을 가진 자가 거슬리는 누락이나 어설픔을 알아채는 일이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["The text, whether of prophet or of poet, expands for whatever we can put into it, and even his bad grammar is sublime.", "예언자의 것이든 시인의 것이든, 글이란 우리가 그 안에 무엇을 담아 넣든 그만큼 넓어지는 법이며, 심지어 그의 어설픈 문법조차 숭고하게 느껴지는 것이다."]], [["“I am very ignorant—you will quite wonder at my ignorance,”", "\"저는 아주 무지해요—제 무지함에 당신은 무척 놀라실 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“I have so many thoughts that may be quite mistaken; and now I shall be able to tell them all to you, and ask you about them.", "\"온통 잘못되었을지도 모를 생각이 너무 많거든요. 하지만 이제 저는 그 모든 것을 당신께 말씀드리고, 여쭤 볼 수 있겠지요."], ["But,”", "그래도,\""], ["she added, with rapid imagination of Mr.", "그녀는 캐소본 씨의 마음을 빠르게 헤아리며 덧붙였다."], ["Casaubon’s probable feeling, “I will not trouble you too much; only when you are inclined to listen to me.", "\"당신을 너무 성가시게 하지는 않을 거예요. 당신이 제 말에 귀 기울이고 싶으실 때만요."], ["You must often be weary with the pursuit of subjects in your own track.", "당신은 자기 길에서 주제들을 좇느라 자주 지치실 테니까요."], ["I shall gain enough if you will take me with you there.”", "당신이 저를 거기 데려가 주신다면, 저는 그것으로 충분합니다.\""]], [["“How should I be able now to persevere in any path without your companionship?” said Mr.", "\"이제 당신과 함께하지 않고서야 제가 어떻게 어떤 길에서든 견뎌 나갈 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["Casaubon, kissing her candid brow, and feeling that heaven had vouchsafed him a blessing in every way suited to his peculiar wants.", "캐소본 씨가 그녀의 솔직한 이마에 입 맞추며 말했다. 하늘이 자신의 특별한 필요에 모든 면에서 들어맞는 축복을 내려 주었다고 느끼면서였다."], ["He was being unconsciously wrought upon by the charms of a nature which was entirely without hidden calculations either for immediate effects or for remoter ends.", "그는 당장의 효과를 위해서든 더 먼 목적을 위해서든 아무런 숨은 계산도 없는 한 천성의 매력에 자신도 모르게 감화되고 있었다."], ["It was this which made Dorothea so childlike, and, according to some judges, so stupid, with all her reputed cleverness; as, for example, in the present case of throwing herself, metaphorically speaking, at Mr.", "바로 이것이, 그토록 영리하다는 평판에도 불구하고 도러시아를 어린아이같이 만들고, 어떤 사람들이 보기에는 어리석게까지 보이도록 만든 것이었다."], ["Casaubon’s feet, and kissing his unfashionable shoe-ties as if he were a Protestant Pope. She was not in the least teaching Mr.", "예컨대 지금의 경우처럼, 비유적으로 말하자면 캐소본 씨의 발치에 몸을 던지고, 마치 그가 신교의 교황이라도 되는 양 그의 유행 지난 신발 끈에 입 맞추는 것이 그러했다."], ["Casaubon to ask if he were good enough for her, but merely asking herself anxiously how she could be good enough for Mr. Casaubon.", "그녀는 캐소본 씨에게 자기에게 어울릴 만큼 그가 충분히 훌륭한지 자문하도록 가르치기는커녕, 그저 자신이 어떻게 하면 캐소본 씨에게 어울릴 만큼 충분히 훌륭할 수 있을지 노심초사 자문하고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Before he left the next day it had been decided that the marriage should take place within six weeks. Why not?", "그가 이튿날 떠나기 전에 결혼식을 여섯 주 안에 올리기로 정해졌다."], ["Mr. Casaubon’s house was ready.", "안 될 이유가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["It was not a parsonage, but a considerable mansion, with much land attached to it.", "캐소본 씨의 집은 준비되어 있었다. 그것은 사제관이 아니라, 딸린 땅도 넓은 상당한 규모의 저택이었다."], ["The parsonage was inhabited by the curate, who did all the duty except preaching the morning sermon.", "사제관에는 보좌 신부가 살고 있었는데, 그는 아침 설교만 빼고 모든 직무를 맡고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI.", "# CHAPTER VI."]], [["My lady’s tongue is like the meadow blades, That cut you stroking them with idle hand.", "무심한 손으로 쓰다듬다가는 베이고 만다."], ["Nice cutting is her function: she divides With spiritual edge the millet-seed, And makes intangible savings.", "정교하게 베는 것이 그녀의 본분이라, 그녀는"]], [["As Mr. Casaubon’s carriage was passing out of the gateway, it arrested the entrance of a pony phaeton driven by a lady with a servant seated behind. It was doubtful whether the recognition had been mutual, for Mr. Casaubon was looking absently before him; but the lady was quick-eyed, and threw a nod and a “How do you do?” in the nick of time. In spite of her shabby bonnet and very old Indian shawl, it was plain that the lodge-keeper regarded her as an important personage, from the low curtsy which was dropped on the entrance of the small phaeton.", "영적인 칼날로 좁쌀 한 알을 가르고,\n만질 수 없는 절약을 이루어 낸다."]], [["“Well, Mrs.", "캐소본 씨의 마차가 정문을 빠져나오던 참에, 그것이 한 부인이 모는, 하인 하나가 뒤에 앉은 조랑말 사륜마차의 진입을 가로막았다."], ["Fitchett, how are your fowls laying now?”", "서로 알아보았는지는 확실치 않았으니, 캐소본 씨는 멍하니 앞만 바라보고 있었기 때문이다. 하지만 그 부인은 눈치가 빨라서 때를 놓치지 않고 고개를 까딱하며 \"안녕하세요?\""], ["said the high-colored, dark-eyed lady, with the clearest chiselled utterance.", "하고 던졌다. 초라한 보닛과 무척 낡은 인도산 숄을 걸쳤음에도, 문지기가 그 작은 사륜마차의 진입에 깊이 무릎을 굽혀 절하는 것으로 보아, 그녀를 중요한 인물로 여기고 있음이 분명했다."]], [["“Pretty well for laying, madam, but they’ve ta’en to eating their eggs: I’ve no peace o’ mind with ’em at all.”", "\"그래, 피칫 부인, 요즘 닭들이 알은 잘 낳나요?\" 혈색 좋고 검은 눈을 가진 부인이 더없이 또렷하게 깎아 낸 듯한 발음으로 말했다."]], [["“Oh, the cannibals!", "\"알은 그런대로 잘 낳습니다요, 마님, 한데 제 알을 쪼아 먹는 버릇이 들어서요."], ["Better sell them cheap at once.", "도무지 마음이 편치가 않아요.\" \"오, 식인종 같으니!"], ["What will you sell them a couple?", "차라리 당장 싸게 팔아 버리는 게 낫겠네요."], ["One can’t eat fowls of a bad character at a high price.”", "한 쌍에 얼마에 팔겠어요? 행실 나쁜 닭을 비싼 값에 먹을 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\""]], [["“Well, madam, half-a-crown: I couldn’t let ’em go, not under.”", "\"글쎄요, 마님, 반 크라운입죠. 그 아래로는 못 내놓습니다요.\""]], [["“Half-a-crown, these times!", "\"이런 시절에 반 크라운이라니!"], ["Come now—for the Rector’s chicken-broth on a Sunday.", "자, 이러면 어떨까요. 일요일에 목사님 닭 수프로 쓰게요."], ["He has consumed all ours that I can spare.", "우리 집 닭은 내가 내줄 만한 건 그분이 다 자셨거든요."], ["You are half paid with the sermon, Mrs. Fitchett, remember that.", "피칫 부인, 설교로 절반은 받은 셈이니, 그 점을 기억하세요."], ["Take a pair of tumbler-pigeons for them—little beauties.", "대신 공중제비비둘기 한 쌍을 받아요. 어찌나 어여쁜지."], ["You must come and see them.", "와서 꼭 봐야 해요."], ["You have no tumblers among your pigeons.”", "댁 비둘기 중에는 공중제비비둘기가 없잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, madam, Master Fitchett shall go and see ’em after work.", "\"글쎄요, 마님, 피칫이 일 끝나고 보러 가게 하겠습니다요."], ["He’s very hot on new sorts; to oblige you.”", "그이가 새로운 종류라면 사족을 못 쓰니까요. 마님 좋으시라고요.\""]], [["“Oblige me!", "\"내가 좋으라고요!"], ["It will be the best bargain he ever made.", "그이가 여태껏 한 거래 중에서 제일 남는 장사일 텐데요."], ["A pair of church pigeons for a couple of wicked Spanish fowls that eat their own eggs!", "제 알을 쪼아 먹는 못된 스페인 닭 두 마리 대신 교회 비둘기 한 쌍이라니!"], ["Don’t you and Fitchett boast too much, that is all!”", "피칫 부부, 너무 자랑하지나 말아요. 그것뿐이에요!\""]], [["The phaeton was driven onwards with the last words, leaving Mrs.", "마지막 말과 함께 사륜마차가 앞으로 나아갔고, 피칫 부인은 웃으며 천천히 고개를 가로젓고는 \"그러믄요, 그러믄요!\""], ["Fitchett laughing and shaking her head slowly, with an interjectional “Surely, surely!”—from which it might be inferred that she would have found the country-side somewhat duller if the Rector’s lady had been less free-spoken and less of a skinflint.", "하고 감탄사를 내뱉으며 남겨졌다. 이로 미루어 보아, 만약 목사님 부인이 그처럼 거리낌 없이 말하지 않고 그처럼 인색하지 않았더라면 그녀는 시골 생활이 다소 따분하게 느껴졌으리라 짐작할 만했다."], ["Indeed, both the farmers and laborers in the parishes of Freshitt and Tipton would have felt a sad lack of conversation but for the stories about what Mrs.", "사실 프레시트와 티프턴 교구의 농부들과 일꾼들도 캐드월러더 부인이 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 일을 했는지에 관한 이야기가 없었더라면 대화거리가 몹시 아쉬웠을 것이다."], ["Cadwallader said and did: a lady of immeasurably high birth, descended, as it were, from unknown earls, dim as the crowd of heroic shades—who pleaded poverty, pared down prices, and cut jokes in the most companionable manner, though with a turn of tongue that let you know who she was.", "그녀는 헤아릴 수 없이 고귀한 가문에서 태어난 부인으로, 영웅적인 망령들의 무리처럼 어렴풋한, 이름 모를 백작들의 후예라 할 만했는데, 가난을 호소하고 값을 깎고 더없이 다정스러운 태도로 농담을 던졌으되, 그 말투에는 자신이 누구인지를 알리는 무언가가 있었다."], ["Such a lady gave a neighborliness to both rank and religion, and mitigated the bitterness of uncommuted tithe.", "그런 부인은 신분에도 종교에도 친근함을 더해 주었고, 면제받지 못한 십일조의 쓰라림을 덜어 주었다."], ["A much more exemplary character with an infusion of sour dignity would not have furthered their comprehension of the Thirty-nine Articles, and would have been less socially uniting.", "시큼한 위엄이 가미된 훨씬 모범적인 인물이었다면 그들이 39개 신조를 이해하는 데 도움이 되지 못했을 것이고, 사람들을 사회적으로 결속시키는 힘도 덜했을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Brooke, seeing Mrs.", "* 39개 신조: 영국 국교회의 기본 교리를 정리한 신앙 조항."], ["Cadwallader’s merits from a different point of view, winced a little when her name was announced in the library, where he was sitting alone.", "캐드월러더 부인의 장점을 다른 관점에서 보고 있던 브룩 씨는, 혼자 앉아 있던 서재에 그녀의 이름이 전해지자 약간 움찔했다."]], [["“I see you have had our Lowick Cicero here,”", "\"여기 우리 로윅의 키케로가 다녀갔나 보군요.\""], ["she said, seating herself comfortably, throwing back her wraps, and showing a thin but well-built figure.", "그녀는 편안하게 자리를 잡고 앉아 겉옷을 뒤로 젖히며, 가냘프지만 잘 짜인 몸매를 드러내고 말했다."], ["“I suspect you and he are brewing some bad polities, else you would not be seeing so much of the lively man.", "\"당신과 그 사람이 무슨 못된 정략을 꾸미고 있는 게 아닌가 싶어요. 안 그러면 그 활달한 양반을 이렇게 자주 만날 리가 없잖아요."], ["I shall inform against you: remember you are both suspicious characters since you took Peel’s side about the Catholic Bill.", "당신을 고발해야겠어요. 가톨릭 법안 때 두 분 다 필 편을 들었으니 의심스러운 인물이라는 걸 잊지 마세요."], ["I shall tell everybody that you are going to put up for Middlemarch on the Whig side when old Pinkerton resigns, and that Casaubon is going to help you in an underhand manner: going to bribe the voters with pamphlets, and throw open the public-houses to distribute them.", "늙은 핑커턴이 사임하면 당신이 휘그당 쪽에서 미들마치 의원에 출마할 거고, 캐소본이 뒤에서 은밀히 당신을 도울 거라고 다들한테 말하고 다니겠어요. 유권자들을 소책자로 매수하고, 그걸 나눠 주려고 술집 문을 활짝 열어 줄 거라고요."], ["Come, confess!”", "자, 자백하세요!\""]], [["“Nothing of the sort,” said Mr.", "\"전혀 그런 게 아니오.\""], ["Brooke, smiling and rubbing his eye-glasses, but really blushing a little at the impeachment.", "브룩 씨가 미소 지으며 안경을 닦았으나, 사실 그 탄핵에 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“Casaubon and I don’t talk politics much.", "\"캐소본과 나는 정치 이야기는 별로 안 하오."], ["He doesn’t care much about the philanthropic side of things; punishments, and that kind of thing.", "그는 일의 자선적인 측면에는 관심이 별로 없소. 형벌이나 그런 것 말이오."], ["He only cares about Church questions.", "그는 오로지 교회 문제에만 관심이 있소."], ["That is not my line of action, you know.”", "그건 내 활동 분야가 아니라는 걸 당신도 알잖소.\""]], [["“Ra-a-ther too much, my friend.", "\"너무하시네요, 친애하는 양반."], ["I have heard of your doings.", "당신이 한 일들을 들었어요."], ["Who was it that sold his bit of land to the Papists at Middlemarch?", "미들마치에서 가톨릭교도들한테 자기 땅을 판 게 누구였더라?"], ["I believe you bought it on purpose.", "일부러 그걸 사들였다고 믿어요."], ["You are a perfect Guy Faux.", "당신은 완벽한 가이 포크스예요."], ["See if you are not burnt in effigy this 5th of November coming.", "다가오는 11월 5일에 허수아비로 화형당하지 않나 두고 보세요."], ["Humphrey would not come to quarrel with you about it, so I am come.”", "험프리는 이 일로 당신과 다투러 오지 않을 테니, 그래서 내가 온 거예요.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"아주 좋소."], ["I was prepared to be persecuted for not persecuting—not persecuting, you know.”", "박해하지 않는 죄로 박해받을 각오는 되어 있었으니까. 박해하지 않는 죄로 말이오.\""]], [["“There you go!", "\"또 시작이군요!"], ["That is a piece of clap-trap you have got ready for the hustings.", "그건 당신이 유세장에서 쓰려고 준비해 둔 입버릇이로군요."], ["Now, do not let them lure you to the hustings, my dear Mr. Brooke.", "자, 친애하는 브룩 씨, 절대로 그들이 당신을 유세장으로 꾀어내게 두지 마세요."], ["A man always makes a fool of himself, speechifying: there’s no excuse but being on the right side, so that you can ask a blessing on your humming and hawing.", "사람이 연설을 하면 늘 바보 꼴이 되고 마니까요. 옳은 편에 서서 당신의 우물쭈물 더듬거림에 축복을 빌 수 있는 경우가 아니라면 변명의 여지가 없어요."], ["You will lose yourself, I forewarn you.", "당신은 길을 잃고 말 거예요, 미리 경고하지요."], ["You will make a Saturday pie of all parties’ opinions, and be pelted by everybody.”", "온갖 정파의 의견을 잡탕 파이로 만들어서 모두에게 돌팔매질당할 거예요.\""]], [["“That is what I expect, you know,” said Mr.", "\"바로 그게 내가 기대하는 바요.\""], ["Brooke, not wishing to betray how little he enjoyed this prophetic sketch—“what I expect as an independent man.", "브룩 씨는 이 예언적 묘사가 얼마나 달갑지 않은지 드러내고 싶지 않아서 말했다. \"독립적인 인간으로서 내가 기대하는 바란 말이오."], ["As to the Whigs, a man who goes with the thinkers is not likely to be hooked on by any party.", "휘그당으로 말하자면, 사상가들과 뜻을 같이하는 사람은 어느 정파에도 낚여 들어가지 않는 법이오."], ["He may go with them up to a certain point—up to a certain point, you know.", "어느 지점까지는 그들과 함께 갈 수 있소. 어느 지점까지는 말이오."], ["But that is what you ladies never understand.”", "하지만 그게 바로 당신네 부인들은 도무지 이해하지 못하는 거요.\""]], [["“Where your certain point is? No.", "\"당신의 그 어느 지점이 어디냐고요?"], ["I should like to be told how a man can have any certain point when he belongs to no party—leading a roving life, and never letting his friends know his address.", "모르겠네요. 어느 정파에도 속하지 않고, 떠돌이 생활을 하면서 친구들한테 주소조차 알리지 않는 사람이 어떻게 어느 지점이라는 걸 가질 수 있는지 누가 좀 알려 줬으면 좋겠어요."], ["‘Nobody knows where Brooke will be—there’s no counting on Brooke’—that is what people say of you, to be quite frank.", "'브룩이 어디 있을지는 아무도 몰라. 브룩은 도무지 믿을 수가 없어.'"], ["Now, do turn respectable.", "솔직히 말하면 사람들이 당신을 두고 하는 말이 그래요."], ["How will you like going to Sessions with everybody looking shy on you, and you with a bad conscience and an empty pocket?”", "자, 이제 점잖아지세요. 다들 당신을 못마땅하게 쳐다보고, 당신은 양심에 가책을 느끼며 빈 주머니를 하고서 재판소에 가는 게 좋겠어요?\""]], [["“I don’t pretend to argue with a lady on politics,” said Mr.", "\"부인을 상대로 정치를 논할 생각은 없소.\""], ["Brooke, with an air of smiling indifference, but feeling rather unpleasantly conscious that this attack of Mrs. Cadwallader’s had opened the defensive campaign to which certain rash steps had exposed him.", "브룩 씨가 미소 띤 무관심한 태도로 말했으나, 캐드월러더 부인의 이 공격이 자신의 경솔한 몇몇 행보가 자초한 방어전의 막을 열었다는 것을 다소 불쾌하게 의식하고 있었다."], ["“Your sex are not thinkers, you know—varium et mutabile semper—that kind of thing.", "\"당신네 성은 사상가가 아니니까. 바리움 엣 무타빌레 셈페르라, 뭐 그런 거지."], ["You don’t know Virgil.", "당신은 베르길리우스를 모르잖소."], ["I knew”—Mr.", "내가 알기로는….\""], ["Brooke reflected in time that he had not had the personal acquaintance of the Augustan poet—“I was going to say, poor Stoddart, you know.", "브룩 씨는 자신이 그 아우구스투스 시대 시인과 개인적으로 친분이 있었던 게 아님을 때맞춰 깨달았다. \"내가 하려던 말은, 가엾은 스토더트 말이오."], ["That was what he said.", "그 사람이 한 말이었소. 당신네 부인들은 늘 독립적인 태도에 반대하지요."], ["You ladies are always against an independent attitude—a man’s caring for nothing but truth, and that sort of thing.", "사람이 오로지 진리만을 소중히 여기는 것, 뭐 그런 것 말이오. 그리고 이 고장만큼 여론이 편협한 곳도 없소."], ["And there is no part of the county where opinion is narrower than it is here—I don’t mean to throw stones, you know, but somebody is wanted to take the independent line; and if I don’t take it, who will?”", "돌을 던지려는 건 아니오만, 누군가는 독립적인 노선을 취해야 하고, 내가 안 하면 누가 하겠소?\" * 바리움 엣 무타빌레 셈페르: \"여자는 언제나 변덕스럽고 변하기 쉽다\"는 뜻의 라틴어로, 베르길리우스의 『아이네이스』에 나오는 구절."]], [["“Who? Why, any upstart who has got neither blood nor position.", "\"누가 하겠냐고요?"], ["People of standing should consume their independent nonsense at home, not hawk it about.", "그야 혈통도 지위도 없는 벼락출세꾼이라면 누구든 하겠지요. 신분 있는 사람들은 독립적이라는 헛소리를 집 안에서나 소비할 일이지 떠벌리고 다닐 일이 아니에요."], ["And you!", "그런데 당신은!"], ["who are going to marry your niece, as good as your daughter, to one of our best men.", "딸이나 다름없는 조카딸을 우리 고장 최고의 신사 가운데 한 사람에게 시집보내려는 사람이!"], ["Sir James would be cruelly annoyed: it will be too hard on him if you turn round now and make yourself a Whig sign-board.”", "제임스 경이 몹시 언짢아할 거예요. 당신이 이제 와서 입장을 바꿔 휘그당 간판이 된다면 그 사람에게 너무 가혹한 일이 될 거라고요.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke again winced inwardly, for Dorothea’s engagement had no sooner been decided, than he had thought of Mrs.", "브룩 씨는 다시 속으로 움찔했으니, 도러시아의 약혼이 결정되자마자 캐드월러더 부인이 앞으로 퍼부을 빈정거림을 떠올렸기 때문이었다."], ["Cadwallader’s prospective taunts. It might have been easy for ignorant observers to say, “Quarrel with Mrs.", "무지한 구경꾼이라면 \"캐드월러더 부인과 절교하시오\"라고 쉽게 말할 수 있겠지만, 가장 오랜 이웃과 다툰 시골 신사가 대체 어디로 갈 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Cadwallader;” but where is a country gentleman to go who quarrels with his oldest neighbors?", "봉인 없는 포도주처럼 무심코 전해진다면, 브룩이라는 이름의 그 그윽한 풍미를 누가 음미할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Who could taste the fine flavor in the name of Brooke if it were delivered casually, like wine without a seal? Certainly a man can only be cosmopolitan up to a certain point.", "분명 사람이란 어느 지점까지만 세계주의자가 될 수 있는 법이다."]], [["“I hope Chettam and I shall always be good friends; but I am sorry to say there is no prospect of his marrying my niece,”", "\"채텀과 나는 늘 좋은 친구로 지내기를 바라오만, 안타깝게도 그가 내 조카딸과 결혼할 전망은 없소.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, much relieved to see through the window that Celia was coming in.", "브룩 씨는 창밖으로 실리아가 들어오는 것이 보여 크게 안도하며 말했다."]], [["“Why not?” said Mrs.", "\"왜 안 되는데요?\""], ["Cadwallader, with a sharp note of surprise.", "캐드월러더 부인이 놀란 듯 날카롭게 물었다."], ["“It is hardly a fortnight since you and I were talking about it.”", "\"당신과 내가 그 일을 이야기한 게 채 보름도 안 됐는데요.\""]], [["“My niece has chosen another suitor—has chosen him, you know.", "\"내 조카딸이 다른 구혼자를 골랐소. 그를 골랐단 말이오."], ["I have had nothing to do with it.", "나는 거기 아무 상관도 없소."], ["I should have preferred Chettam; and I should have said Chettam was the man any girl would have chosen.", "나는 채텀이 더 좋았을 거요. 어떤 처녀라도 채텀을 고를 거라고 말했을 거요."], ["But there is no accounting for these things.", "하지만 이런 일은 도무지 설명할 수가 없소."], ["Your sex is capricious, you know.”", "당신네 성은 변덕스러우니까.\""]], [["“Why, whom do you mean to say that you are going to let her marry?”", "\"아니, 그럼 조카딸을 누구한테 시집보낼 작정이라는 거예요?\""], ["Mrs. Cadwallader’s mind was rapidly surveying the possibilities of choice for Dorothea.", "캐드월러더 부인의 마음은 도러시아가 고를 만한 가능성들을 재빠르게 훑고 있었다."]], [["But here Celia entered, blooming from a walk in the garden, and the greeting with her delivered Mr. Brooke from the necessity of answering immediately.", "하지만 바로 그때 실리아가 정원 산책으로 발그레해진 얼굴로 들어왔고, 그녀와 인사를 나누느라 브룩 씨는 당장 대답해야 할 처지에서 벗어났다."], ["He got up hastily, and saying, “By the way, I must speak to Wright about the horses,”", "그는 허둥지둥 일어나 \"그러고 보니 말에 관해 라이트와 얘기를 좀 해야겠군\""], ["shuffled quickly out of the room.", "하고는 잰걸음으로 방을 빠져나갔다."]], [["“My dear child, what is this?—this about your sister’s engagement?”", "\"얘야, 이게 무슨 일이니? 네 언니의 약혼 말이다.\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."]], [["“She is engaged to marry Mr. Casaubon,”", "\"언니는 캐소본 씨와 결혼하기로 약혼했어요.\""], ["said Celia, resorting, as usual, to the simplest statement of fact, and enjoying this opportunity of speaking to the Rector’s wife alone.", "실리아는 여느 때처럼 가장 단순한 사실 진술에 의지하면서, 목사님 부인과 단둘이 이야기할 이 기회를 즐기며 말했다."]], [["“This is frightful.", "\"끔찍하구나."], ["How long has it been going on?”", "얼마나 오래된 일이니?\""]], [["“I only knew of it yesterday.", "\"저도 어제야 알았어요."], ["They are to be married in six weeks.”", "6주 후에 결혼한대요.\""]], [["“Well, my dear, I wish you joy of your brother-in-law.”", "\"그래, 얘야, 형부 생기는 거 축하한다.\""]], [["“I am so sorry for Dorothea.”", "\"저는 언니가 너무 안됐어요.\""]], [["“Sorry!", "\"안됐다고!"], ["It is her doing, I suppose.”", "그 애가 한 일이잖니.\""]], [["“Yes; she says Mr.", "\"네."], ["Casaubon has a great soul.”", "언니는 캐소본 씨가 위대한 영혼을 지녔대요.\""]], [["“With all my heart.”", "\"진심으로 그러길 바란다.\""]], [["“Oh, Mrs. Cadwallader, I don’t think it can be nice to marry a man with a great soul.”", "\"오, 캐드월러더 부인, 위대한 영혼을 지닌 사람과 결혼하는 게 좋을 리 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Well, my dear, take warning.", "\"그래, 얘야, 명심하렴."], ["You know the look of one now; when the next comes and wants to marry you, don’t you accept him.”", "이제 그런 사람이 어떻게 생겼는지 알았으니, 다음에 그런 사람이 와서 너랑 결혼하자고 하면 절대 받아 주지 마라.\""]], [["“I’m sure I never should.”", "\"전 정말이지 절대 그러지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“No; one such in a family is enough.", "\"그래. 한 집안에 그런 사람 하나면 충분하지."], ["So your sister never cared about Sir James Chettam?", "그래, 네 언니는 제임스 채텀 경한테 도통 마음이 없었던 거니?"], ["What would you have said to him for a brother-in-law?”", "형부로 그분을 맞았다면 너는 뭐라고 했겠니?\""]], [["“I should have liked that very much.", "\"저는 무척 좋았을 거예요."], ["I am sure he would have been a good husband.", "분명 좋은 남편이 되셨을 거예요."], ["Only,”", "다만,\""], ["Celia added, with a slight blush (she sometimes seemed to blush as she breathed), “I don’t think he would have suited Dorothea.”", "실리아는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 덧붙였다(그녀는 때때로 숨을 쉬듯 자연스럽게 얼굴을 붉히는 듯했다). \"그분이 언니한테 어울렸을 것 같지는 않아요.\""]], [["“Not high-flown enough?”", "\"충분히 고상하지 못해서?\""]], [["“Dodo is very strict.", "\"도도는 무척 엄격해요."], ["She thinks so much about everything, and is so particular about what one says.", "모든 일을 너무 깊이 생각하고, 남이 하는 말에 무척 까다로워요."], ["Sir James never seemed to please her.”", "제임스 경은 한 번도 언니 마음에 든 적이 없는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“She must have encouraged him, I am sure.", "\"그 애가 분명 부추겼겠지."], ["That is not very creditable.”", "그건 별로 떳떳한 일이 아니구나.\""]], [["“Please don’t be angry with Dodo; she does not see things.", "\"도도한테 화내지 마세요. 언니는 그런 걸 잘 못 봐요."], ["She thought so much about the cottages, and she was rude to Sir James sometimes; but he is so kind, he never noticed it.”", "오두막집들 생각에만 너무 골몰해서 가끔 제임스 경한테 무례하게 굴기도 했지만, 그분은 워낙 친절해서 한 번도 그걸 눈치채지 못했어요.\""]], [["“Well,” said Mrs.", "\"그래.\""], ["Cadwallader, putting on her shawl, and rising, as if in haste, “I must go straight to Sir James and break this to him.", "캐드월러더 부인이 숄을 두르고 서두르듯 일어서며 말했다. \"곧장 제임스 경한테 가서 이 일을 알려야겠다."], ["He will have brought his mother back by this time, and I must call.", "지금쯤이면 어머니를 모시고 돌아왔을 테니, 찾아가야겠어."], ["Your uncle will never tell him.", "네 삼촌은 절대 그분한테 말하지 않을 테니까."], ["We are all disappointed, my dear.", "얘야, 우리 모두 실망했단다."], ["Young people should think of their families in marrying.", "젊은 사람들은 결혼할 때 가문을 생각해야 하는데. 나야 나쁜 본보기지."], ["I set a bad example—married a poor clergyman, and made myself a pitiable object among the De Bracys—obliged to get my coals by stratagem, and pray to heaven for my salad oil.", "가난한 목사와 결혼해서 드 브레이시 집안에서 가엾은 신세가 됐으니까. 석탄도 꾀를 부려 구해야 하고, 샐러드 기름은 하늘에 기도해야 할 판이지."], ["However, Casaubon has money enough; I must do him that justice.", "하지만 캐소본은 돈은 넉넉하니, 그 점만큼은 공정하게 인정해 줘야겠지."], ["As to his blood, I suppose the family quarterings are three cuttle-fish sable, and a commentator rampant.", "혈통으로 말하자면, 가문 문장이 검은 오징어 세 마리에 앞발을 든 주석가 한 마리쯤이려나."], ["By the bye, before I go, my dear, I must speak to your Mrs. Carter about pastry.", "그건 그렇고, 가기 전에 얘야, 네 집 카터 부인한테 페이스트리 만드는 얘기를 좀 해야겠다."], ["I want to send my young cook to learn of her.", "우리 집 젊은 요리사를 보내서 배우게 하고 싶거든."], ["Poor people with four children, like us, you know, can’t afford to keep a good cook.", "우리처럼 아이가 넷이나 되는 가난한 사람들은 좋은 요리사를 둘 형편이 못 되잖니."], ["I have no doubt Mrs. Carter will oblige me.", "카터 부인은 틀림없이 들어줄 거야."], ["Sir James’s cook is a perfect dragon.”", "제임스 경네 요리사는 완전히 용 같으니까.\""]], [["In less than an hour, Mrs. Cadwallader had circumvented Mrs.", "한 시간도 채 안 되어 캐드월러더 부인은 카터 부인을 구워삶고는, 자기 목사관에서 멀지 않은 프레시트 홀로 마차를 몰았다."], ["Carter and driven to Freshitt Hall, which was not far from her own parsonage, her husband being resident in Freshitt and keeping a curate in Tipton.", "그녀의 남편은 프레시트에 거주하면서 티프턴에는 부목사를 두고 있었다."]], [["Sir James Chettam had returned from the short journey which had kept him absent for a couple of days, and had changed his dress, intending to ride over to Tipton Grange. His horse was standing at the door when Mrs.", "제임스 채텀 경은 이틀간 자리를 비우게 했던 짧은 여행에서 돌아와, 티프턴 그레인지로 말을 타고 갈 작정으로 옷을 갈아입은 참이었다."], ["Cadwallader drove up, and he immediately appeared there himself, whip in hand. Lady Chettam had not yet returned, but Mrs.", "캐드월러더 부인이 마차를 몰고 왔을 때 그의 말이 문 앞에 서 있었고, 그도 곧 채찍을 손에 든 채 직접 그 앞에 나타났다."], ["Cadwallader’s errand could not be despatched in the presence of grooms, so she asked to be taken into the conservatory close by, to look at the new plants; and on coming to a contemplative stand, she said—", "채텀 부인은 아직 돌아오지 않았지만, 캐드월러더 부인의 용건은 마부들 앞에서 처리할 수 있는 것이 아니었으므로, 그녀는 새로 들여온 식물들을 구경하게 가까운 온실로 안내해 달라고 청했다. 그리고 생각에 잠긴 듯 걸음을 멈추고는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“I have a great shock for you; I hope you are not so far gone in love as you pretended to be.”", "\"당신한테 큰 충격을 줄 일이 있어요. 당신이 짐짓 그런 척했던 만큼 사랑에 깊이 빠진 건 아니길 바라요.\""]], [["It was of no use protesting against Mrs. Cadwallader’s way of putting things.", "캐드월러더 부인의 화법에 항의해 봐야 소용없는 일이었다."], ["But Sir James’s countenance changed a little.", "하지만 제임스 경의 얼굴빛이 약간 변했다."], ["He felt a vague alarm.", "그는 막연한 불안을 느꼈다."]], [["“I do believe Brooke is going to expose himself after all.", "\"아무래도 브룩이 결국 망신을 살 모양이에요."], ["I accused him of meaning to stand for Middlemarch on the Liberal side, and he looked silly and never denied it—talked about the independent line, and the usual nonsense.”", "내가 그 사람더러 자유당 쪽에서 미들마치 의원에 출마할 생각이라고 다그쳤더니, 멍청한 표정만 짓고 부인은 절대 안 하더군요. 독립적인 노선이니 늘 하던 헛소리만 늘어놓았어요.\""]], [["“Is that all?”", "\"그게 다입니까?\""], ["said Sir James, much relieved.", "제임스 경이 크게 안도하며 말했다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"아니,\""], ["rejoined Mrs.", "캐드월러더 부인이 더 날카로운 어조로 받아쳤다."], ["Cadwallader, with a sharper note, “you don’t mean to say that you would like him to turn public man in that way—making a sort of political Cheap Jack of himself?”", "\"설마 그 사람이 그런 식으로 공인이 되는 게 좋다는 말은 아니겠지요? 일종의 정치판 싸구려 장사꾼이 되는 게 말이에요.\""]], [["“He might be dissuaded, I should think.", "\"단념시킬 수 있을 것 같은데요."], ["He would not like the expense.”", "그 사람은 돈 드는 걸 싫어하니까요.\""]], [["“That is what I told him.", "\"내가 그 사람한테 한 말이 바로 그거예요."], ["He is vulnerable to reason there—always a few grains of common-sense in an ounce of miserliness.", "거기서는 이치가 통하지요. 한 온스의 인색함 속에도 늘 몇 알의 상식은 들어 있으니까요."], ["Miserliness is a capital quality to run in families; it’s the safe side for madness to dip on.", "인색함은 집안 내력으로 이어지기에 더없이 좋은 자질이지요. 광기가 빠져들 때 안전한 쪽이거든요."], ["And there must be a little crack in the Brooke family, else we should not see what we are to see.”", "그리고 브룩 집안에는 분명 조금 금이 가 있을 거예요. 안 그러면 우리가 곧 보게 될 일은 보이지 않겠지요.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["Brooke standing for Middlemarch?”", "브룩이 미들마치 의원에 출마한다고요?\""]], [["“Worse than that.", "\"그보다 더 나쁜 일이에요."], ["I really feel a little responsible.", "정말이지 제 책임도 좀 느껴요."], ["I always told you Miss Brooke would be such a fine match.", "내가 늘 당신한테 브룩 양이 그렇게 좋은 짝이 될 거라고 했잖아요."], ["I knew there was a great deal of nonsense in her—a flighty sort of Methodistical stuff.", "그 애한테 헛된 구석이 많다는 건 알고 있었어요. 변덕스러운 감리교도 같은 면 말이에요."], ["But these things wear out of girls.", "하지만 그런 건 처녀들한테서 닳아 없어지는 법이니까요."], ["However, I am taken by surprise for once.”", "그런데 이번만큼은 내가 깜짝 놀라고 말았어요.\""]], [["“What do you mean, Mrs. Cadwallader?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 캐드월러더 부인?\""], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 물었다."], ["His fear lest Miss Brooke should have run away to join the Moravian Brethren, or some preposterous sect unknown to good society, was a little allayed by the knowledge that Mrs.", "브룩 양이 모라비아 형제단이나 점잖은 사회에는 알려지지 않은 어떤 터무니없는 종파에 들어가려고 달아난 게 아닐까 하는 그의 두려움은, 캐드월러더 부인이 늘 일을 최악으로 말하곤 한다는 것을 알기에 조금 누그러졌다."], ["Cadwallader always made the worst of things.", "\"브룩 양한테 무슨 일이 생긴 겁니까?"], ["“What has happened to Miss Brooke? Pray speak out.”", "제발 분명히 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋아요. 그 애가 결혼하기로 약혼했어요.\""], ["She is engaged to be married.” Mrs.", "캐드월러더 부인은 잠시 말을 멈추고, 친구가 긴장된 미소로 감추려 애쓰며 부츠를 채찍으로 치는 동안, 그의 얼굴에 깊이 상처받은 표정이 떠오르는 것을 살펴보았다."], ["Cadwallader paused a few moments, observing the deeply hurt expression in her friend’s face, which he was trying to conceal by a nervous smile, while he whipped his boot; but she soon added, “Engaged to Casaubon.”", "그러나 곧 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"캐소본과 약혼했어요.\""]], [["Sir James let his whip fall and stooped to pick it up.", "제임스 경은 채찍을 떨어뜨리고 그것을 주우려 몸을 숙였다."], ["Perhaps his face had never before gathered so much concentrated disgust as when he turned to Mrs. Cadwallader and repeated, “Casaubon?”", "그가 캐드월러더 부인을 돌아보며 \"캐소본이라고요?\"라고 되뇌었을 때만큼 그의 얼굴에 응축된 혐오가 떠오른 적은 아마 일찍이 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Even so.", "\"바로 그래요."], ["You know my errand now.”", "이제 내가 온 용건을 알겠지요.\""]], [["“Good God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["It is horrible!", "끔찍해요!"], ["He is no better than a mummy!”", "그자는 미라나 다를 바 없는데!\""], ["(The point of view has to be allowed for, as that of a blooming and disappointed rival.)", "(이는 한창 혈기 왕성한, 실연당한 연적의 관점임을 감안해 들어야 한다.)"]], [["“She says, he is a great soul.—A great bladder for dried peas to rattle in!”", "\"그 애는 그자가 위대한 영혼을 지녔다고 하더군요. 마른 완두콩이 달그락거리는 커다란 오줌통 같은 영혼이겠지요!\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."]], [["“What business has an old bachelor like that to marry?”", "\"저런 늙은 독신자가 결혼은 무슨 결혼이람?\""], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“He has one foot in the grave.”", "\"한 발은 이미 무덤에 들여놓은 주제에.\""]], [["“He means to draw it out again, I suppose.”", "\"그 발을 다시 빼낼 작정인가 보지요.\""]], [["“Brooke ought not to allow it: he should insist on its being put off till she is of age.", "\"브룩은 이걸 허락하면 안 돼요. 그 애가 성년이 될 때까지 미루도록 우겨야 해요."], ["She would think better of it then.", "그러면 그때는 다시 생각할 거예요."], ["What is a guardian for?”", "후견인이 뭐 하러 있는 겁니까?\""]], [["“As if you could ever squeeze a resolution out of Brooke!”", "\"브룩한테서 결단이라는 걸 짜낼 수 있을 것 같아요?\""]], [["“Cadwallader might talk to him.”", "\"캐드월러더 씨가 그 사람한테 말해 볼 수 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Not he!", "\"그이는 안 돼요!"], ["Humphrey finds everybody charming.", "험프리는 누구든 다 매력적으로 본다니까요."], ["I never can get him to abuse Casaubon.", "도무지 그이가 캐소본을 욕하게 만들 수가 없어요. 심지어 주교까지도 좋게 말하는걸요."], ["He will even speak well of the bishop, though I tell him it is unnatural in a beneficed clergyman; what can one do with a husband who attends so little to the decencies?", "성직록을 받는 목사가 그러는 건 부자연스럽다고 내가 말해도요. 체면치레에 그렇게 무심한 남편을 데리고 뭘 어쩌겠어요?"], ["I hide it as well as I can by abusing everybody myself.", "그래서 내가 직접 모두를 욕하는 걸로 어떻게든 그걸 감추고 있지요."], ["Come, come, cheer up!", "자, 자, 기운 내세요!"], ["you are well rid of Miss Brooke, a girl who would have been requiring you to see the stars by daylight.", "브룩 양과 인연이 끊긴 건 잘된 일이에요. 그 애는 당신더러 대낮에 별을 보라고 요구했을 처녀니까요."], ["Between ourselves, little Celia is worth two of her, and likely after all to be the better match.", "우리끼리 얘기지만, 어린 실리아가 그 애보다 두 배는 나아요. 결국엔 더 좋은 짝이 될 것 같고요."], ["For this marriage to Casaubon is as good as going to a nunnery.”", "캐소본과의 이 결혼은 수녀원에 들어가는 거나 마찬가지니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, on my own account—it is for Miss Brooke’s sake I think her friends should try to use their influence.”", "\"오, 제 입장에서 하는 말이 아니에요. 브룩 양을 위해서 그 애 친지들이 영향력을 행사해 봐야 한다는 거지요.\""]], [["“Well, Humphrey doesn’t know yet.", "\"글쎄요, 험프리는 아직 몰라요. 하지만 내가 말하면 틀림없이 이럴 거예요."], ["But when I tell him, you may depend on it he will say, ‘Why not?", "'왜 안 돼? 캐소본은 좋은 사람이고, 젊잖아."], ["Casaubon is a good fellow—and young—young enough.’ These charitable people never know vinegar from wine till they have swallowed it and got the colic.", "충분히 젊어.' 그런 인심 좋은 사람들은 식초를 삼키고 배앓이를 해 봐야 비로소 그게 포도주가 아니란 걸 알지요."], ["However, if I were a man I should prefer Celia, especially when Dorothea was gone.", "어쨌든 내가 남자라면 실리아를 택하겠어요. 특히 도러시아가 가 버린 마당에는요."], ["The truth is, you have been courting one and have won the other.", "사실은, 당신이 한 사람한테 구애해서 다른 사람의 마음을 얻은 격이에요."], ["I can see that she admires you almost as much as a man expects to be admired.", "그 애가 남자가 받기를 기대하는 만큼이나 당신을 흠모한다는 게 내 눈엔 보여요."], ["If it were any one but me who said so, you might think it exaggeration.", "나 아닌 다른 사람이 이런 말을 했다면 과장이라고 여길지도 모르지만요."], ["Good-by!”", "그럼 안녕히!\""]], [["Sir James handed Mrs. Cadwallader to the phaeton, and then jumped on his horse.", "제임스 경은 캐드월러더 부인을 사륜마차에 태워 주고 나서 자기 말에 뛰어올랐다."], ["He was not going to renounce his ride because of his friend’s unpleasant news—only to ride the faster in some other direction than that of Tipton Grange.", "그는 친구의 달갑지 않은 소식 때문에 말 타는 것을 그만둘 생각은 없었다. 다만 티프턴 그레인지가 아닌 다른 방향으로 더 빨리 달릴 뿐이었다."]], [["Now, why on earth should Mrs. Cadwallader have been at all busy about Miss Brooke’s marriage; and why, when one match that she liked to think she had a hand in was frustrated, should she have straightway contrived the preliminaries of another?", "그런데 대체 무엇 때문에 캐드월러더 부인은 브룩 양의 결혼에 그토록 부산을 떨었을까?"], ["Was there any ingenious plot, any hide-and-seek course of action, which might be detected by a careful telescopic watch?", "그리고 자신이 거든 셈 치고 싶었던 한 혼사가 틀어지자, 왜 곧장 또 다른 혼사의 사전 작업을 꾸몄을까?"], ["Not at all: a telescope might have swept the parishes of Tipton and Freshitt, the whole area visited by Mrs.", "세심하게 망원경으로 감시하면 포착할 만한 어떤 교묘한 음모나 숨바꼭질 같은 행동이 있었을까?"], ["Cadwallader in her phaeton, without witnessing any interview that could excite suspicion, or any scene from which she did not return with the same unperturbed keenness of eye and the same high natural color.", "전혀 그렇지 않았다. 망원경으로 티프턴과 프레시트 교구, 즉 캐드월러더 부인이 사륜마차로 다니는 구역 전체를 훑어보아도 의심을 살 만한 어떤 만남도, 그녀가 한결같이 흔들림 없는 예리한 눈빛과 한결같이 높다란 천연 혈색을 잃은 채 돌아오는 어떤 장면도 목격하지 못했을 것이다."], ["In fact, if that convenient vehicle had existed in the days of the Seven Sages, one of them would doubtless have remarked, that you can know little of women by following them about in their pony-phaetons.", "사실 그런 편리한 탈것이 일곱 현인의 시대에 있었더라면, 그중 한 사람은 분명 이렇게 말했을 것이다. 여인들을 조랑말 사륜마차로 따라다녀 봐야 그들에 관해 알 수 있는 것은 거의 없다고."], ["Even with a microscope directed on a water-drop we find ourselves making interpretations which turn out to be rather coarse; for whereas under a weak lens you may seem to see a creature exhibiting an active voracity into which other smaller creatures actively play as if they were so many animated tax-pennies, a stronger lens reveals to you certain tiniest hairlets which make vortices for these victims while the swallower waits passively at his receipt of custom.", "물 한 방울에 현미경을 들이대도 우리는 다소 조잡한 것으로 드러나는 해석을 하기 마련이다. 약한 렌즈로 보면 어떤 생물이 활발한 탐식성을 발휘하고 그 속으로 더 작은 생물들이 마치 살아 움직이는 세금 동전이라도 되는 양 활발히 빨려 드는 것처럼 보일 테지만, 더 강한 렌즈를 대면 그 희생자들을 향해 소용돌이를 일으키는 아주 미세한 잔털들이 드러나고, 정작 삼키는 자는 세금 거두는 자리에서 수동적으로 기다리고 있을 뿐이다."], ["In this way, metaphorically speaking, a strong lens applied to Mrs. Cadwallader’s match-making will show a play of minute causes producing what may be called thought and speech vortices to bring her the sort of food she needed.", "비유하자면 이런 식으로, 캐드월러더 부인의 중매질에 강한 렌즈를 들이대면, 그녀가 필요로 하는 종류의 양식을 가져다주는, 이른바 사고와 언어의 소용돌이를 만들어 내는 미세한 원인들의 작용이 드러날 것이다."], ["Her life was rurally simple, quite free from secrets either foul, dangerous, or otherwise important, and not consciously affected by the great affairs of the world.", "그녀의 삶은 시골답게 단순했고, 추잡하거나 위험하거나 그 밖에 중요한 어떤 비밀도 없었으며, 세상의 큰일들에 의식적으로 영향받지도 않았다."], ["All the more did the affairs of the great world interest her, when communicated in the letters of high-born relations: the way in which fascinating younger sons had gone to the dogs by marrying their mistresses; the fine old-blooded idiocy of young Lord Tapir, and the furious gouty humors of old Lord Megatherium; the exact crossing of genealogies which had brought a coronet into a new branch and widened the relations of scandal,—these were topics of which she retained details with the utmost accuracy, and reproduced them in an excellent pickle of epigrams, which she herself enjoyed the more because she believed as unquestionably in birth and no-birth as she did in game and vermin.", "그렇기에 고귀한 친척들이 보낸 편지로 전해지는 큰 세상의 일들에 그녀는 더더욱 흥미를 느꼈다. 매력적인 차남들이 정부와 결혼해 신세를 망친 사연이며, 젊은 테이퍼 경의 고상한 명문가다운 백치 같은 짓이며, 늙은 메가테리엄 경의 통풍에 시달리는 격노한 성미며, 어느 가문의 가계도가 정확히 어떻게 교차하여 새로운 가지로 백작 작위가 넘어가고 추문의 친척 관계가 더 넓어졌는가 하는 것들이 그러한 화제였는데, 그녀는 그 세부 사항들을 더없이 정확하게 기억해 두었다가 절묘하게 절인 경구로 재현해 냈다."], ["She would never have disowned any one on the ground of poverty: a De Bracy reduced to take his dinner in a basin would have seemed to her an example of pathos worth exaggerating, and I fear his aristocratic vices would not have horrified her.", "그녀 자신은 사냥감과 해로운 짐승을 믿듯이 좋은 혈통과 미천한 혈통을 의심 없이 믿었기에 그것을 더더욱 즐겼다. 그녀는 결코 가난을 이유로 누군가를 외면하지는 않았을 것이다."], ["But her feeling towards the vulgar rich was a sort of religious hatred: they had probably made all their money out of high retail prices, and Mrs.", "대야에 밥을 받아먹을 만큼 영락한 드 브레이시 집안 사람이라면 과장할 가치가 있는 비애의 본보기로 여겼을 테고, 그의 귀족적인 악덕에도 질겁하지 않았으리라."], ["Cadwallader detested high prices for everything that was not paid in kind at the Rectory: such people were no part of God’s design in making the world; and their accent was an affliction to the ears.", "하지만 천박한 부자들을 향한 그녀의 감정은 일종의 종교적 증오였다. 그들은 아마도 높은 소매가로 그 돈을 다 벌었을 텐데, 캐드월러더 부인은 목사관에서 현물로 지불하지 않는 모든 것의 높은 가격을 혐오했던 것이다."], ["A town where such monsters abounded was hardly more than a sort of low comedy, which could not be taken account of in a well-bred scheme of the universe.", "그런 자들은 신이 세상을 만들 때 의도한 설계의 일부가 아니었고, 그들의 말투는 귀에 고통스러운 것이었다. 그런 괴물들이 우글거리는 도시란 점잖은 우주의 체계에서는 고려할 가치도 없는, 일종의 저급한 희극에 지나지 않았다."], ["Let any lady who is inclined to be hard on Mrs. Cadwallader inquire into the comprehensiveness of her own beautiful views, and be quite sure that they afford accommodation for all the lives which have the honor to coexist with hers.", "캐드월러더 부인에게 가혹하게 굴고 싶은 부인이 있다면, 자기 자신의 아름다운 견해가 얼마나 포용적인지 따져 보고, 그것이 자기와 함께 살아가는 영광을 누리는 모든 삶을 받아들일 자리를 마련해 주는지 분명히 확인해 보시라."]], [["With such a mind, active as phosphorus, biting everything that came near into the form that suited it, how could Mrs. Cadwallader feel that the Miss Brookes and their matrimonial prospects were alien to her?", "인 가까이 다가오는 것은 무엇이든 자기에게 맞는 형태로 깨물어 버리는, 인처럼 활동적인 그런 정신을 지닌 캐드월러더 부인이 어떻게 브룩 자매와 그들의 결혼 전망을 자신과 무관한 일로 느낄 수 있었겠는가?"], ["especially as it had been the habit of years for her to scold Mr. Brooke with the friendliest frankness, and let him know in confidence that she thought him a poor creature.", "더구나 그녀는 더없이 다정한 솔직함으로 브룩 씨를 꾸짖고, 은밀히 그가 변변찮은 인간이라 생각한다는 것을 알려 주는 일을 여러 해 동안 습관처럼 해 왔으니 말이다."], ["From the first arrival of the young ladies in Tipton she had prearranged Dorothea’s marriage with Sir James, and if it had taken place would have been quite sure that it was her doing: that it should not take place after she had preconceived it, caused her an irritation which every thinker will sympathize with.", "이 젊은 아가씨들이 티프턴에 처음 왔을 때부터 그녀는 도러시아와 제임스 경의 결혼을 미리 짜 두었고, 만약 그것이 성사되었더라면 그것이 자기가 한 일이라고 확신했을 것이다. 미리 구상해 둔 그 일이 성사되지 않게 되자, 그녀는 모든 사색가가 공감할 만한 짜증을 느꼈다."], ["She was the diplomatist of Tipton and Freshitt, and for anything to happen in spite of her was an offensive irregularity. As to freaks like this of Miss Brooke’s, Mrs.", "그녀는 티프턴과 프레시트의 외교관이었으니, 무슨 일이든 자기 뜻을 거슬러 일어나는 것은 불쾌한 비정상이었다."], ["Cadwallader had no patience with them, and now saw that her opinion of this girl had been infected with some of her husband’s weak charitableness: those Methodistical whims, that air of being more religious than the rector and curate together, came from a deeper and more constitutional disease than she had been willing to believe.", "브룩 양의 이런 변덕으로 말하자면, 캐드월러더 부인은 도무지 참을 수가 없었고, 이제 이 처녀에 대한 자신의 평가가 남편의 무른 자비심에 다소 물들었었다는 것을 깨달았다. 저 감리교도 같은 변덕, 목사와 부목사를 합친 것보다 더 신앙심 깊은 척하는 태도는, 그녀가 믿고 싶었던 것보다 더 깊고 더 체질적인 병에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["“However,” said Mrs.", "\"하지만,\""], ["Cadwallader, first to herself and afterwards to her husband, “I throw her over: there was a chance, if she had married Sir James, of her becoming a sane, sensible woman.", "캐드월러더 부인은 처음에는 혼잣말로, 나중에는 남편에게 말했다. \"나는 그 애를 포기하겠어요."], ["He would never have contradicted her, and when a woman is not contradicted, she has no motive for obstinacy in her absurdities.", "제임스 경과 결혼했더라면 그 애도 제정신의 분별 있는 여자가 될 가능성이 있었지요. 그분은 절대 그 애 말을 반박하지 않았을 테고, 여자란 반박당하지 않으면 자기 황당한 짓을 고집할 동기가 없는 법이니까요."], ["But now I wish her joy of her hair shirt.”", "하지만 이제는 그 애가 고행자의 거친 셔츠를 입게 된 걸 축복해 줘야겠네요.\""]], [["It followed that Mrs. Cadwallader must decide on another match for Sir James, and having made up her mind that it was to be the younger Miss Brooke, there could not have been a more skilful move towards the success of her plan than her hint to the baronet that he had made an impression on Celia’s heart.", "그리하여 캐드월러더 부인은 제임스 경을 위해 또 다른 짝을 정해야 했고, 그것이 동생 브룩 양이어야 한다고 마음을 굳힌 이상, 그가 실리아의 마음에 깊은 인상을 남겼다고 준남작에게 넌지시 일러 준 것보다 더 솜씨 좋은 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["For he was not one of those gentlemen who languish after the unattainable Sappho’s apple that laughs from the topmost bough—the charms which", "왜냐하면 그는 가장 높은 가지에서 비웃는, 손에 닿지 않는 사포의 사과를 애타게 그리워하는 그런 신사가 아니었기 때문이다. 그 매력이란"]], [["“Smile like the knot of cowslips on the cliff, Not to be come at by the willing hand.”", "\"절벽 위의 앵초 꽃송이처럼 미소 짓되,\n기꺼이 뻗은 손으로는 닿을 수 없는 것.\""]], [["He had no sonnets to write, and it could not strike him agreeably that he was not an object of preference to the woman whom he had preferred.", "그는 쓸 소네트도 없었고, 자기가 마음에 둔 여인에게 자신이 선택받지 못했다는 사실이 그에게 기분 좋게 와닿을 리도 없었다."], ["Already the knowledge that Dorothea had chosen Mr. Casaubon had bruised his attachment and relaxed its hold.", "도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 골랐다는 것을 안 것만으로도 이미 그의 애정은 멍이 들고 그 힘이 느슨해진 터였다."], ["Although Sir James was a sportsman, he had some other feelings towards women than towards grouse and foxes, and did not regard his future wife in the light of prey, valuable chiefly for the excitements of the chase.", "제임스 경은 사냥을 즐기는 사람이었으나, 여자에 대해서는 뇌조나 여우에 대한 것과는 다른 감정을 품고 있었고, 미래의 아내를 주로 추격의 흥분 때문에 가치 있는 사냥감으로 여기지도 않았다."], ["Neither was he so well acquainted with the habits of primitive races as to feel that an ideal combat for her, tomahawk in hand, so to speak, was necessary to the historical continuity of the marriage-tie.", "또한 그는 원시 부족의 풍습에 정통하여, 말하자면 도끼를 손에 들고 그녀를 두고 이상적인 결투를 벌이는 것이 결혼이라는 결속의 역사적 연속성에 필요하다고 느낄 만큼은 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, having the amiable vanity which knits us to those who are fond of us, and disinclines us to those who are indifferent, and also a good grateful nature, the mere idea that a woman had a kindness towards him spun little threads of tenderness from out his heart towards hers.", "오히려 그는 우리를 우리에게 호의를 품은 사람과 이어 주고 무심한 사람으로부터 멀어지게 하는 그 사랑스러운 허영심을 지녔고, 더불어 감사할 줄 아는 선량한 천성을 지녔기에, 한 여인이 자신에게 호감을 품고 있다는 생각만으로도 그의 마음에서 그녀의 마음을 향해 가느다란 다정함의 실들이 자아져 나왔다."]], [["Thus it happened, that after Sir James had ridden rather fast for half an hour in a direction away from Tipton Grange, he slackened his pace, and at last turned into a road which would lead him back by a shorter cut.", "그리하여 제임스 경은 티프턴 그레인지에서 멀어지는 방향으로 반 시간쯤 제법 빠르게 말을 달린 끝에 속도를 늦추었고, 마침내 지름길로 되돌아가게 해 줄 길로 접어들게 되었다. 여러 감정이 그의 안에서 작용하여, 결국 아무 새로운 일도 일어나지 않은 것처럼 오늘 그레인지를 찾아가기로 결심하게 만들었다."], ["Various feelings wrought in him the determination after all to go to the Grange to-day as if nothing new had happened.", "자신이 청혼을 했다가 거절당한 적이 없다는 것이 다행스럽게 여겨지는 마음을 그는 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["He could not help rejoicing that he had never made the offer and been rejected; mere friendly politeness required that he should call to see Dorothea about the cottages, and now happily Mrs.", "단순한 우호적 예의만으로도 오두막집들 문제로 도러시아를 만나러 가야 마땅했고, 이제 다행히도 캐드월러더 부인 덕분에 필요하다면 너무 어색해 보이지 않고 축하의 말을 건넬 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["Cadwallader had prepared him to offer his congratulations, if necessary, without showing too much awkwardness.", "사실 그는 마음이 편치 않았다. 도러시아를 단념하는 것은 그에게 무척 고통스러운 일이었다."], ["He really did not like it: giving up Dorothea was very painful to him; but there was something in the resolve to make this visit forthwith and conquer all show of feeling, which was a sort of file-biting and counter-irritant.", "하지만 이 방문을 당장 해치우고 온갖 감정의 기색을 억누르겠다는 결심에는 일종의 줄칼질 같은, 자극에는 자극으로 맞서는 무언가가 있었다."], ["And without his distinctly recognizing the impulse, there certainly was present in him the sense that Celia would be there, and that he should pay her more attention than he had done before.", "그리고 그 충동을 뚜렷이 자각하지는 못했지만, 실리아가 거기 있으리라는 것, 그리고 전보다 그녀에게 더 관심을 기울이게 되리라는 의식이 분명 그의 안에 자리 잡고 있었다."]], [["We mortals, men and women, devour many a disappointment between breakfast and dinner-time; keep back the tears and look a little pale about the lips, and in answer to inquiries say, “Oh, nothing!”", "우리 인간은, 남자든 여자든, 아침과 저녁 식사 시간 사이에 숱한 실망을 삼킨다. 눈물을 참고 입술 언저리를 약간 창백하게 한 채, 무슨 일이냐는 물음에는 \"오, 아무것도 아니에요!\"라고 대답한다."], ["Pride helps us; and pride is not a bad thing when it only urges us to hide our own hurts—not to hurt others.", "자존심이 우리를 돕는다. 그리고 자존심이란 그저 우리 자신의 상처를 감추라고만 부추길 뿐 남에게 상처 주지 말라고 한다면, 결코 나쁜 것이 아니다."]], [["“Piacer e popone Vuol la sua stagione.” —Italian Proverb.", "예상할 수 있듯이, 캐소본 씨는 이 몇 주 동안 그레인지에서 많은 시간을 보냈고, 구애가 그의 위대한 저작—모든 신화의 열쇠—의 진척에 끼치는 방해는 자연히 구애의 행복한 종결을 한층 더 간절히 고대하게 만들었다."], ["Mr. Casaubon, as might be expected, spent a great deal of his time at the Grange in these weeks, and the hindrance which courtship occasioned to the progress of his great work—the Key to all Mythologies—naturally made him look forward the more eagerly to the happy termination of courtship.", "하지만 그는 그 방해를 의도적으로 자초한 것이었으니, 이제 여성의 동반이라는 우아함으로 자신의 삶을 꾸밀 때가 되었다고, 학구적 노동의 틈새에 피로가 드리우기 쉬운 우울을 여성적 공상의 유희로 밝힐 때가 되었다고, 그리하여 이 절정의 나이에 노년을 위한 여성의 보살핌이라는 위안을 확보할 때가 되었다고 마음을 정했기 때문이다."], ["But he had deliberately incurred the hindrance, having made up his mind that it was now time for him to adorn his life with the graces of female companionship, to irradiate the gloom which fatigue was apt to hang over the intervals of studious labor with the play of female fancy, and to secure in this, his culminating age, the solace of female tendance for his declining years.", "그래서 그는 감정의 물결에 몸을 맡기기로 결심했는데, 아마도 그 물결이 얼마나 지극히 얕은 실개천인지를 발견하고는 놀랐을 것이다."], ["Hence he determined to abandon himself to the stream of feeling, and perhaps was surprised to find what an exceedingly shallow rill it was.", "가뭄이 든 지방에서는 침례를 상징적으로만 행할 수 있듯이, 캐소본 씨는 자신의 시냇물이 베풀어 줄 수 있는, 뛰어듦에 가장 근접한 행위가 물을 뿌리는 정도임을 깨달았다."], ["As in droughty regions baptism by immersion could only be performed symbolically, Mr.", "그리하여 그는 시인들이 남성적 열정의 힘을 크게 과장했다고 결론지었다."], ["Casaubon found that sprinkling was the utmost approach to a plunge which his stream would afford him; and he concluded that the poets had much exaggerated the force of masculine passion.", "그럼에도 그는 브룩 양이 결혼에 대한 자신의 가장 흡족한 예상을 채워 줄 듯한 열렬하고 순종적인 애정을 보이는 것을 흐뭇하게 지켜보았다."], ["Nevertheless, he observed with pleasure that Miss Brooke showed an ardent submissive affection which promised to fulfil his most agreeable previsions of marriage.", "그가 몸을 맡긴 정도가 그토록 절제된 까닭을 설명해 줄 어떤 결함이 도로테아에게 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 한두 번 스친 적은 있었다."], ["It had once or twice crossed his mind that possibly there was some deficiency in Dorothea to account for the moderation of his abandonment; but he was unable to discern the deficiency, or to figure to himself a woman who would have pleased him better; so that there was clearly no reason to fall back upon but the exaggerations of human tradition.", "그러나 그는 그 결함을 가려낼 수도, 자신을 더 기쁘게 해 줄 만한 여인을 머릿속에 그려 볼 수도 없었다. 그러니 인간이 물려받은 전통의 과장 말고는 의지할 만한 이유가 분명 없었다."]], [["“Could I not be preparing myself now to be more useful?”", "“이제 제가 좀 더 쓸모 있는 사람이 되도록 준비할 수는 없을까요?”"], ["said Dorothea to him, one morning, early in the time of courtship; “could I not learn to read Latin and Greek aloud to you, as Milton’s daughters did to their father, without understanding what they read?”", "구애가 시작된 초기의 어느 날 아침, 도로테아가 그에게 말했다. “밀턴의 딸들이 자기 아버지에게 그랬듯이, 읽는 내용을 이해하지 못하더라도 라틴어와 그리스어를 소리 내어 읽어 드리는 법을 배울 수는 없을까요?”"]], [["“I fear that would be wearisome to you,” said Mr.", "“그건 당신에게 지루한 일일까 봐 걱정스럽군요.”"], ["Casaubon, smiling; “and, indeed, if I remember rightly, the young women you have mentioned regarded that exercise in unknown tongues as a ground for rebellion against the poet.”", "캐소본 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다. “게다가, 내 기억이 옳다면, 당신이 언급한 그 젊은 여인들은 모르는 언어로 하는 그 작업을 시인에게 반항할 빌미로 여겼지요.”"]], [["“Yes; but in the first place they were very naughty girls, else they would have been proud to minister to such a father; and in the second place they might have studied privately and taught themselves to understand what they read, and then it would have been interesting. I hope you don’t expect me to be naughty and stupid?”", "“네, 하지만 우선 그들은 아주 못된 처녀들이었어요. 그렇지 않았다면 그런 아버지를 섬기는 것을 자랑스러워했을 테니까요. 그리고 둘째로, 그들은 따로 공부해서 읽는 내용을 이해하도록 스스로를 가르칠 수도 있었을 거예요. 그랬다면 흥미로웠겠지요. 제가 못되고 어리석은 사람이 되리라고 기대하시는 건 아니겠지요?”"]], [["“I expect you to be all that an exquisite young lady can be in every possible relation of life.", "“나는 당신이 빼어난 젊은 숙녀가 삶의 모든 가능한 관계 속에서 될 수 있는 그 모든 것이 되기를 기대합니다."], ["Certainly it might be a great advantage if you were able to copy the Greek character, and to that end it were well to begin with a little reading.”", "확실히 당신이 그리스 문자를 베껴 쓸 수 있다면 큰 도움이 되겠지요. 그러려면 약간의 읽기부터 시작하는 것이 좋겠습니다.”"]], [["Dorothea seized this as a precious permission. She would not have asked Mr.", "도로테아는 이것을 소중한 허락으로 붙들었다."], ["Casaubon at once to teach her the languages, dreading of all things to be tiresome instead of helpful; but it was not entirely out of devotion to her future husband that she wished to know Latin and Greek.", "그녀는 캐소본 씨에게 당장 그 언어들을 가르쳐 달라고 청하지는 않았을 것이다. 무엇보다 도움이 되기는커녕 성가신 존재가 될까 봐 두려웠기 때문이다."], ["Those provinces of masculine knowledge seemed to her a standing-ground from which all truth could be seen more truly.", "하지만 그녀가 라틴어와 그리스어를 알고 싶어 한 것이 전적으로 미래의 남편에 대한 헌신 때문만은 아니었다. 남성적 지식의 그 영역들은 그녀에게 모든 진리를 더 진실되게 바라볼 수 있는 발판처럼 여겨졌다."], ["As it was, she constantly doubted her own conclusions, because she felt her own ignorance: how could she be confident that one-roomed cottages were not for the glory of God, when men who knew the classics appeared to conciliate indifference to the cottages with zeal for the glory?", "사실 그녀는 자신의 무지를 느꼈기에 끊임없이 자신의 결론을 의심했다. 고전을 아는 사람들이 오두막에 대한 무관심과 신의 영광에 대한 열의를 조화시킬 줄 아는 것처럼 보이는데, 방 하나짜리 오두막이 신의 영광을 위한 것이 아니라고 어떻게 확신할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Perhaps even Hebrew might be necessary—at least the alphabet and a few roots—in order to arrive at the core of things, and judge soundly on the social duties of the Christian.", "사물의 핵심에 이르러 기독교인의 사회적 의무를 건전하게 판단하려면 어쩌면 히브리어조차 필요할지도 몰랐다—적어도 알파벳과 어근 몇 개쯤은."], ["And she had not reached that point of renunciation at which she would have been satisfied with having a wise husband: she wished, poor child, to be wise herself.", "그리고 그녀는 현명한 남편을 둔 것으로 만족할 만한 그런 체념의 경지에는 이르지 못했다. 가엾은 아이여, 그녀는 자기 자신이 현명해지기를 바랐다."], ["Miss Brooke was certainly very naive with all her alleged cleverness.", "브룩 양은 그 알려진 영민함에도 불구하고 분명 매우 순진했다."], ["Celia, whose mind had never been thought too powerful, saw the emptiness of other people’s pretensions much more readily.", "결코 너무 뛰어나다고 여겨진 적 없는 머리를 가진 실리아는 다른 사람들의 허세가 텅 비어 있음을 훨씬 더 쉽게 알아보았다."], ["To have in general but little feeling, seems to be the only security against feeling too much on any particular occasion.", "대체로 감정이 적은 것이야말로 어떤 특정한 경우에 지나치게 많이 느끼지 않도록 막아 주는 유일한 안전장치인 듯하다."]], [["However, Mr. Casaubon consented to listen and teach for an hour together, like a schoolmaster of little boys, or rather like a lover, to whom a mistress’s elementary ignorance and difficulties have a touching fitness.", "어쨌든 캐소본 씨는 한 시간씩 함께 듣고 가르치는 일에 동의했으니, 마치 어린 소년들을 가르치는 교사처럼, 아니 그보다는 연인처럼, 그에게는 사랑하는 여인의 초보적인 무지와 어려움이 가슴 뭉클하게 어울리는 것이었다."], ["Few scholars would have disliked teaching the alphabet under such circumstances.", "이런 상황에서라면 알파벳 가르치기를 싫어할 학자는 거의 없으리라."], ["But Dorothea herself was a little shocked and discouraged at her own stupidity, and the answers she got to some timid questions about the value of the Greek accents gave her a painful suspicion that here indeed there might be secrets not capable of explanation to a woman’s reason.", "하지만 도로테아 자신은 자신의 어리석음에 다소 충격을 받고 낙담했으며, 그리스어 강세의 가치에 관해 머뭇거리며 던진 몇몇 질문에 돌아온 대답은, 정말로 여기에는 여자의 이성으로는 설명할 수 없는 비밀이 있을지도 모른다는 괴로운 의혹을 그녀에게 안겨 주었다."]], [["Mr. Brooke had no doubt on that point, and expressed himself with his usual strength upon it one day that he came into the library while the reading was going forward.", "브룩 씨는 그 점에 대해서는 아무런 의심이 없었고, 읽기 수업이 진행되던 어느 날 서재에 들어와서는 평소의 단호함으로 자신의 견해를 표명했다."]], [["“Well, but now, Casaubon, such deep studies, classics, mathematics, that kind of thing, are too taxing for a woman—too taxing, you know.”", "“그런데 말이오, 캐소본, 그런 깊은 학문, 고전이니 수학이니 하는 그런 것들은 여자에게는 너무 과한 부담이라오—너무 과해요, 알다시피.”"]], [["“Dorothea is learning to read the characters simply,” said Mr.", "“도로테아는 그저 문자를 읽는 법을 배우고 있을 뿐입니다.”"], ["Casaubon, evading the question.", "캐소본 씨가 질문을 비껴가며 말했다."], ["“She had the very considerate thought of saving my eyes.”", "“내 눈을 아껴 주려는 아주 사려 깊은 생각을 해 준 거지요.”"]], [["“Ah, well, without understanding, you know—that may not be so bad.", "“아, 그래요, 이해하지는 못한 채로 말이지, 알다시피—그거라면 그리 나쁘진 않겠군."], ["But there is a lightness about the feminine mind—a touch and go—music, the fine arts, that kind of thing—they should study those up to a certain point, women should; but in a light way, you know.", "하지만 여성의 정신에는 가벼움이 있어요—툭 건드리고 지나가는 식이지—음악이니 미술이니 하는 그런 것들 말이오—그런 것들은 어느 정도까지는 공부해야지, 여자들은. 하지만 가볍게, 알다시피."], ["A woman should be able to sit down and play you or sing you a good old English tune.", "여자라면 자리에 앉아서 멋진 옛 영국 곡조 하나쯤 연주하거나 노래할 줄 알아야지. 내가 좋아하는 게 바로 그거요."], ["That is what I like; though I have heard most things—been at the opera in Vienna: Gluck, Mozart, everything of that sort.", "하긴 나도 웬만한 건 다 들어 봤소—빈에서 오페라도 봤지. 글루크, 모차르트, 뭐 그런 부류 다 말이오."], ["But I’m a conservative in music—it’s not like ideas, you know.", "하지만 음악에 관해서라면 나는 보수주의자라오—그건 사상하고는 다르거든, 알다시피."], ["I stick to the good old tunes.”", "나는 좋은 옛 곡조를 고수하지.”"]], [["“Mr. Casaubon is not fond of the piano, and I am very glad he is not,”", "“캐소본 씨는 피아노를 좋아하지 않으세요."], ["said Dorothea, whose slight regard for domestic music and feminine fine art must be forgiven her, considering the small tinkling and smearing in which they chiefly consisted at that dark period.", "그리고 저는 그분이 좋아하지 않으셔서 무척 기뻐요.” 도로테아가 말했다."], ["She smiled and looked up at her betrothed with grateful eyes. If he had always been asking her to play the “Last Rose of Summer,”", "그 음울한 시대에 가정 음악과 여성적 미술이라는 것이 주로 자잘하게 댕그랑거리고 어설프게 칠해 대는 것으로 이루어져 있었음을 감안한다면, 그녀가 그것들을 대수롭지 않게 여긴 것은 용서받아야 할 일이다."], ["she would have required much resignation.", "그녀는 미소 지으며 고마운 눈빛으로 약혼자를 올려다보았다."], ["“He says there is only an old harpsichord at Lowick, and it is covered with books.”", "만약 그가 늘 「여름의 마지막 장미」를 연주하라고 청해 왔다면, 그녀는 상당한 인내심이 필요했을 것이다. “로윅에는 낡은 하프시코드 한 대밖에 없는데, 그것도 책으로 뒤덮여 있다고 하시네요.”"]], [["“Ah, there you are behind Celia, my dear.", "“아, 거기서는 네가 실리아만 못하구나, 얘야."], ["Celia, now, plays very prettily, and is always ready to play.", "실리아는 아주 어여쁘게 연주하고, 언제든 기꺼이 연주하거든."], ["However, since Casaubon does not like it, you are all right.", "하지만 캐소본이 좋아하지 않으니, 너는 괜찮겠다."], ["But it’s a pity you should not have little recreations of that sort, Casaubon: the bow always strung—that kind of thing, you know—will not do.”", "그래도 자네가 그런 자그마한 오락을 누리지 못한다니 안타깝군, 캐소본. 활시위를 늘 팽팽하게 당겨 두는 일은—뭐 그런 거 말이오, 알다시피—좋지 않아요.”"]], [["“I never could look on it in the light of a recreation to have my ears teased with measured noises,”", "“가락 맞춰 내는 소음에 귀가 시달리는 일을 나는 도무지 오락으로 여길 수가 없습니다.”"], ["said Mr. Casaubon.", "캐소본 씨가 말했다."], ["“A tune much iterated has the ridiculous effect of making the words in my mind perform a sort of minuet to keep time—an effect hardly tolerable, I imagine, after boyhood.", "“여러 번 되풀이되는 곡조는 내 머릿속의 단어들이 박자를 맞추느라 일종의 미뉴에트를 추게 만드는 우스꽝스러운 효과를 내지요—소년기를 지난 뒤로는 견디기 힘든 효과라고 봅니다."], ["As to the grander forms of music, worthy to accompany solemn celebrations, and even to serve as an educating influence according to the ancient conception, I say nothing, for with these we are not immediately concerned.”", "엄숙한 의식을 동반할 만한, 그리고 고대의 관념에 따라 교육적 영향력으로 기능할 만한 더 웅장한 형식의 음악에 대해서라면 나는 아무 말도 하지 않겠습니다. 그것은 지금 우리가 직접 관련된 문제가 아니니까요.”"]], [["“No; but music of that sort I should enjoy,”", "“그렇지요. 하지만 그런 종류의 음악이라면 저도 즐길 것 같아요.”"], ["said Dorothea.", "도로테아가 말했다."], ["“When we were coming home from Lausanne my uncle took us to hear the great organ at Freiberg, and it made me sob.”", "“로잔에서 집으로 돌아오던 길에 삼촌이 저희를 프라이베르크의 그 거대한 오르간 소리를 들으러 데려가 주셨는데, 저는 그만 흐느껴 울었어요.”"]], [["“That kind of thing is not healthy, my dear,”", "“그런 건 건강에 좋지 않다, 얘야.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“Casaubon, she will be in your hands now: you must teach my niece to take things more quietly, eh, Dorothea?”", "“캐소본, 이 아이는 이제 자네 손에 맡겨졌네. 자네가 내 조카에게 매사를 좀 더 차분하게 받아들이도록 가르쳐 줘야 해, 안 그러냐, 도로테아?”"]], [["He ended with a smile, not wishing to hurt his niece, but really thinking that it was perhaps better for her to be early married to so sober a fellow as Casaubon, since she would not hear of Chettam.", "그는 조카의 마음을 상하게 할 생각은 없이 미소로 말을 맺었지만, 그녀가 체텀의 이야기는 들으려고도 하지 않으니, 캐소본처럼 진중한 사람과 일찌감치 결혼하는 편이 어쩌면 그녀에게 더 나을지도 모른다고 진심으로 생각하고 있었다."]], [["“It is wonderful, though,”", "“하지만 놀라운 일이야.”"], ["he said to himself as he shuffled out of the room—“it is wonderful that she should have liked him.", "그는 발을 끌며 방을 나서면서 혼잣말을 했다. “그 애가 저 사람을 좋아하게 되었다니 놀라운 일이지."], ["However, the match is good.", "그래도 좋은 짝이야."], ["I should have been travelling out of my brief to have hindered it, let Mrs. Cadwallader say what she will.", "캐드월래더 부인이 뭐라고 하든, 이 일을 막았다면 나는 분수에 넘치는 짓을 한 셈이 됐을 거야."], ["He is pretty certain to be a bishop, is Casaubon.", "캐소본은 십중팔구 주교가 될 사람이지."], ["That was a very seasonable pamphlet of his on the Catholic Question:—a deanery at least.", "가톨릭 문제에 관한 그 소책자는 아주 시의적절했어—적어도 주임 사제 자리는 받을 거야."], ["They owe him a deanery.”", "사람들이 그에게 주임 사제 자리쯤은 빚지고 있는 셈이지.”"]], [["And here I must vindicate a claim to philosophical reflectiveness, by remarking that Mr.", "여기서 나는 철학적 성찰력에 대한 권리를 입증해야겠다."], ["Brooke on this occasion little thought of the Radical speech which, at a later period, he was led to make on the incomes of the bishops.", "브룩 씨는 이 자리에서, 훗날 주교들의 수입에 관해 자신이 하게 될 급진적 연설을 거의 떠올리지 못했음을 지적함으로써 말이다."], ["What elegant historian would neglect a striking opportunity for pointing out that his heroes did not foresee the history of the world, or even their own actions?—For example, that Henry of Navarre, when a Protestant baby, little thought of being a Catholic monarch; or that Alfred the Great, when he measured his laborious nights with burning candles, had no idea of future gentlemen measuring their idle days with watches.", "어느 우아한 역사가가 자신의 영웅들이 세계의 역사를, 심지어 자기 자신의 행동조차 예견하지 못했다는 사실을 짚어 줄 인상적인 기회를 놓치겠는가?—이를테면 나바르의 앙리가 신교도 갓난아기였을 때 가톨릭 군주가 되리라고는 거의 생각지 못했다거나, 알프레드 대왕이 타오르는 촛불로 자신의 고된 밤을 측정하던 시절에 훗날의 신사들이 회중시계로 자신의 한가한 낮을 측정하리라고는 짐작도 못 했다는 사실 말이다."], ["Here is a mine of truth, which, however vigorously it may be worked, is likely to outlast our coal.", "여기 진리의 광맥이 있으니, 아무리 힘차게 캐낸들 우리의 석탄보다 더 오래 남을 법한 것이다."]], [["But of Mr. Brooke I make a further remark perhaps less warranted by precedent—namely, that if he had foreknown his speech, it might not have made any great difference.", "하지만 브룩 씨에 대해 나는 선례로 정당화되기에는 다소 미흡할지도 모를 한 가지 견해를 더 덧붙이려 한다—즉, 그가 자신의 연설을 미리 알았더라도 큰 차이는 없었으리라는 것이다."], ["To think with pleasure of his niece’s husband having a large ecclesiastical income was one thing—to make a Liberal speech was another thing; and it is a narrow mind which cannot look at a subject from various points of view.", "조카의 남편이 막대한 성직 수입을 누리게 되리라는 것을 흐뭇하게 생각하는 일은 별개의 문제였고, 자유주의적 연설을 하는 일은 또 다른 문제였다. 하나의 주제를 여러 관점에서 바라보지 못하는 것은 좁은 마음이니 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII.", "# CHAPTER VIII."]], [["“Oh, rescue her! I am her brother now, And you her father. Every gentle maid Should have a guardian in each gentleman.”", "신사들 하나하나를 후견인으로 두어야 마땅하니.」"]], [["It was wonderful to Sir James Chettam how well he continued to like going to the Grange after he had once encountered the difficulty of seeing Dorothea for the first time in the light of a woman who was engaged to another man.", "도러시아를 다른 남자와 약혼한 여인으로 처음 마주하는 곤란을 일단 한 번 겪고 난 뒤에도, 제임스 체텀 경은 자신이 여전히 그레인지를 즐겨 찾는다는 사실이 스스로도 놀라웠다."], ["Of course the forked lightning seemed to pass through him when he first approached her, and he remained conscious throughout the interview of hiding uneasiness; but, good as he was, it must be owned that his uneasiness was less than it would have been if he had thought his rival a brilliant and desirable match.", "물론 처음 그녀에게 다가갔을 때는 갈라진 번개가 온몸을 관통하는 듯했고, 면담 내내 그는 불안을 감추고 있다는 의식을 떨치지 못했다. 그러나 선량한 그였지만, 그의 불안은 만약 자신의 연적이 눈부시고 탐낼 만한 혼처라고 여겼더라면 느꼈을 것보다 덜했음을 인정해야 한다."], ["He had no sense of being eclipsed by Mr. Casaubon; he was only shocked that Dorothea was under a melancholy illusion, and his mortification lost some of its bitterness by being mingled with compassion.", "그는 캐소본 씨에게 빛이 가려진다는 느낌이 전혀 없었으니, 다만 도러시아가 우울한 환상에 사로잡혀 있다는 사실에 충격을 받았을 뿐이며, 그의 굴욕감은 연민과 뒤섞임으로써 그 쓰라림을 다소 덜어 냈다."]], [["Nevertheless, while Sir James said to himself that he had completely resigned her, since with the perversity of a Desdemona she had not affected a proposed match that was clearly suitable and according to nature; he could not yet be quite passive under the idea of her engagement to Mr.", "그렇지만 제임스 경은 스스로에게 자신이 그녀를 완전히 단념했노라고 말하면서도—명백히 적합하고 자연의 이치에 부합하는 청혼을 데스데모나 같은 심술로 받아들이지 않았으니—캐소본 씨와의 약혼이라는 생각 앞에서 아직은 도무지 무심할 수 없었다."], ["Casaubon. On the day when he first saw them together in the light of his present knowledge, it seemed to him that he had not taken the affair seriously enough.", "지금 알게 된 사실에 비추어 두 사람을 처음으로 함께 본 날, 그는 자신이 그 일을 충분히 진지하게 받아들이지 않았던 것 같다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Brooke was really culpable; he ought to have hindered it.", "브룩은 정말로 책임이 있었으니, 그가 그 일을 막았어야 했다."], ["Who could speak to him?", "누가 그에게 말할 수 있을까?"], ["Something might be done perhaps even now, at least to defer the marriage.", "어쩌면 지금이라도 무언가 할 수 있을지 몰랐으니, 적어도 결혼을 미루는 일이라도."], ["On his way home he turned into the Rectory and asked for Mr. Cadwallader.", "집으로 돌아가는 길에 그는 목사관에 들러 캐드월래더 씨를 찾았다."], ["Happily, the Rector was at home, and his visitor was shown into the study, where all the fishing tackle hung.", "다행히 목사는 집에 있었고, 손님은 온갖 낚시 도구가 걸려 있는 서재로 안내되었다."], ["But he himself was in a little room adjoining, at work with his turning apparatus, and he called to the baronet to join him there.", "하지만 목사 본인은 그 옆에 딸린 작은 방에서 선반(旋盤) 장치로 일을 하고 있었고, 그는 준남작에게 그쪽으로 오라고 불렀다."], ["The two were better friends than any other landholder and clergyman in the county—a significant fact which was in agreement with the amiable expression of their faces.", "두 사람은 그 지방의 다른 어떤 지주와 성직자보다도 사이가 좋은 친구였으니—이는 그들 얼굴에 어린 온화한 표정과 잘 어울리는 의미심장한 사실이었다."]], [["Mr. Cadwallader was a large man, with full lips and a sweet smile; very plain and rough in his exterior, but with that solid imperturbable ease and good-humor which is infectious, and like great grassy hills in the sunshine, quiets even an irritated egoism, and makes it rather ashamed of itself.", "캐드월래더 씨는 도톰한 입술에 다정한 미소를 띤 덩치 큰 남자였다."], ["“Well, how are you?” he said, showing a hand not quite fit to be grasped.", "겉모습은 무척 수수하고 거칠었으나, 전염성이 있는 그 단단하고 흔들림 없는 여유와 쾌활함을 지니고 있어서, 햇살 속의 광활한 풀 언덕처럼, 짜증 난 이기심마저 가라앉히고 오히려 스스로 부끄러워하게 만드는 데가 있었다."], ["“Sorry I missed you before.", "「자, 잘 지냈소?」 그가 차마 맞잡기에는 어울리지 않는 손을 내보이며 말했다."], ["Is there anything particular?", "「아까 못 만나서 미안하오. 무슨 특별한 일이라도 있소?"], ["You look vexed.”", "언짢아 보이는구려.」"]], [["Sir James’s brow had a little crease in it, a little depression of the eyebrow, which he seemed purposely to exaggerate as he answered.", "제임스 경의 이마에는 작은 주름이 잡혀 있었고, 눈썹이 살짝 처져 있었는데, 그는 대답하면서 그것을 일부러 과장하는 듯했다."]], [["“It is only this conduct of Brooke’s.", "「그저 브룩의 처사 때문이오."], ["I really think somebody should speak to him.”", "정말이지 누군가 그에게 한마디 해야 할 것 같소.」"]], [["“What?", "「뭐 말이오?"], ["meaning to stand?” said Mr.", "출마하겠다는 거 말이오?」"], ["Cadwallader, going on with the arrangement of the reels which he had just been turning.", "캐드월래더 씨가 방금 돌리던 낚싯줄 얼레를 정리하며 말했다."], ["“I hardly think he means it.", "「난 그가 진심이라곤 별로 생각지 않소."], ["But where’s the harm, if he likes it?", "하지만 본인이 좋다는데 무슨 해가 되겠소?"], ["Any one who objects to Whiggery should be glad when the Whigs don’t put up the strongest fellow.", "휘그당을 못마땅하게 여기는 사람이라면 휘그당이 가장 강한 자를 내세우지 않을 때 오히려 반가워해야지."], ["They won’t overturn the Constitution with our friend Brooke’s head for a battering ram.”", "우리 친구 브룩의 머리를 공성 망치 삼아서야 헌정을 뒤엎지는 못할 테니.」"]], [["“Oh, I don’t mean that,”", "「오, 그 말이 아니오.」"], ["said Sir James, who, after putting down his hat and throwing himself into a chair, had begun to nurse his leg and examine the sole of his boot with much bitterness.", "제임스 경이 모자를 내려놓고 의자에 몸을 던지더니, 다리를 끌어안고는 부츠 밑창을 무척 씁쓸한 표정으로 살피기 시작하면서 말했다. 「이 결혼 말이오."], ["“I mean this marriage. I mean his letting that blooming young girl marry Casaubon.”", "그가 그 한창 피어나는 어린 처녀를 캐소본과 결혼하게 내버려 두는 것 말이오.」"]], [["“What is the matter with Casaubon?", "「캐소본이 뭐가 어떻다는 거요?"], ["I see no harm in him—if the girl likes him.”", "난 그에게서 아무 해도 못 보겠는데—처녀가 그를 좋아한다면야.」"]], [["“She is too young to know what she likes.", "「그녀는 자기가 뭘 좋아하는지 알기엔 너무 어리오."], ["Her guardian ought to interfere.", "후견인이 나서야지."], ["He ought not to allow the thing to be done in this headlong manner.", "이런 식으로 무턱대고 일을 해치우도록 내버려 둬서는 안 되오."], ["I wonder a man like you, Cadwallader—a man with daughters, can look at the affair with indifference: and with such a heart as yours!", "당신 같은 사람이, 캐드월래더—딸을 둔 사람이, 이 일을 그토록 무심하게 바라볼 수 있다니 놀랍소. 게다가 당신처럼 마음씨 좋은 사람이!"], ["Do think seriously about it.”", "진지하게 한번 생각해 보시오.」"]], [["“I am not joking; I am as serious as possible,”", "「농담하는 게 아니오. 나는 더없이 진지하오.」"], ["said the Rector, with a provoking little inward laugh.", "목사가 사람을 약 올리는 작은 속웃음을 흘리며 말했다."], ["“You are as bad as Elinor.", "「당신은 엘리너만큼이나 못 말리겠소."], ["She has been wanting me to go and lecture Brooke; and I have reminded her that her friends had a very poor opinion of the match she made when she married me.”", "그 사람도 내게 브룩한테 가서 훈계를 좀 하라고 졸랐다오. 그래서 내가 일깨워 줬지, 그 사람 친구들은 그가 나와 결혼해서 맺은 인연을 아주 형편없는 혼처로 여겼다고 말이오.」"]], [["“But look at Casaubon,”", "「하지만 캐소본을 좀 보시오.」"], ["said Sir James, indignantly.", "제임스 경이 분개하며 말했다."], ["“He must be fifty, and I don’t believe he could ever have been much more than the shadow of a man.", "「틀림없이 쉰은 됐을 거고, 한 번이라도 사내의 그림자 이상이었던 적이 있었을지 의심스럽소."], ["Look at his legs!”", "그 다리를 좀 보시오!」"]], [["“Confound you handsome young fellows!", "「잘난 미남 청년들 같으니라고!"], ["you think of having it all your own way in the world.", "자네들은 세상만사가 제 뜻대로 되리라 생각하지."], ["You don’t understand women.", "자네들은 여자를 몰라."], ["They don’t admire you half so much as you admire yourselves.", "여자들은 자네들이 스스로를 흠모하는 것의 절반만큼도 자네들을 흠모하지 않아."], ["Elinor used to tell her sisters that she married me for my ugliness—it was so various and amusing that it had quite conquered her prudence.”", "엘리너는 제 자매들한테 늘 그러더군, 내 못생긴 얼굴 때문에 나와 결혼했다고—그게 어찌나 가지각색이고 재미있었던지 신중함이 아주 무너져 버렸다나.」"]], [["“You!", "「당신이야!"], ["it was easy enough for a woman to love you.", "여자가 당신을 사랑하기란 쉬웠지."], ["But this is no question of beauty.", "하지만 이건 미모의 문제가 아니오."], ["I don’t like Casaubon.”", "난 캐소본이 마음에 들지 않소.」"], ["This was Sir James’s strongest way of implying that he thought ill of a man’s character.", "이것이 어떤 사내의 인품을 나쁘게 본다는 뜻을 내비치는 제임스 경의 가장 강한 표현이었다."]], [["“Why?", "「왜요?"], ["what do you know against him?”", "그에 대해 뭐 안 좋은 거라도 아시오?」"], ["said the Rector laying down his reels, and putting his thumbs into his armholes with an air of attention.", "목사가 얼레를 내려놓고는, 주의를 기울이는 태도로 엄지손가락을 겨드랑이 안쪽에 끼우며 말했다."]], [["Sir James paused.", "제임스 경은 말을 멈추었다."], ["He did not usually find it easy to give his reasons: it seemed to him strange that people should not know them without being told, since he only felt what was reasonable.", "그는 평소 자기 이유를 대는 일이 쉽지 않았으니, 자신은 그저 이치에 맞는 바를 느낄 뿐인데 사람들이 굳이 말하지 않아도 그것을 알지 못한다는 게 이상하게 여겨졌다."], ["At last he said—", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“Now, Cadwallader, has he got any heart?”", "「자, 캐드월래더, 그에게 도대체 마음이란 게 있소?」"]], [["“Well, yes.", "「글쎄, 있지요."], ["I don’t mean of the melting sort, but a sound kernel, that you may be sure of.", "눈 녹듯 무르녹는 그런 종류는 아니지만, 속알맹이는 멀쩡하다오, 그건 장담해도 좋소."], ["He is very good to his poor relations: pensions several of the women, and is educating a young fellow at a good deal of expense.", "그는 가난한 친척들에게 무척 잘하오. 여자 몇몇에게는 생활비를 대 주고, 한 젊은이는 적잖은 비용을 들여 교육시키고 있소."], ["Casaubon acts up to his sense of justice.", "캐소본은 자기 정의감에 따라 행동하는 사람이오."], ["His mother’s sister made a bad match—a Pole, I think—lost herself—at any rate was disowned by her family.", "그의 이모가 잘못된 결혼을 했는데—폴란드 사람이었던 것 같소—몸을 망쳤지요, 어쨌든 집안에서 의절당했소."], ["If it had not been for that, Casaubon would not have had so much money by half.", "그 일이 아니었다면 캐소본은 지금 가진 재산의 절반도 갖지 못했을 거요."], ["I believe he went himself to find out his cousins, and see what he could do for them.", "그는 친히 사촌들을 찾아 나서서 자기가 그들을 위해 뭘 할 수 있는지 알아봤다고 하오."], ["Every man would not ring so well as that, if you tried his metal.", "쇠붙이를 두드려 보면, 누구나 그렇게 좋은 소리를 내진 못하오."], ["You would, Chettam; but not every man.”", "당신이라면 그러겠지, 체텀. 하지만 누구나 그렇지는 않다오.」"]], [["“I don’t know,”", "「모르겠소.」"], ["said Sir James, coloring.", "제임스 경이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I am not so sure of myself.”", "「나 자신도 그리 자신은 없소.」"], ["He paused a moment, and then added, “That was a right thing for Casaubon to do.", "그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. 「그건 캐소본이 한 옳은 일이오."], ["But a man may wish to do what is right, and yet be a sort of parchment code.", "하지만 사람이 옳은 일을 하고자 하면서도 일종의 양피지 법전 같은 존재일 수도 있소."], ["A woman may not be happy with him.", "여자가 그와 행복하지 못할 수도 있단 말이오."], ["And I think when a girl is so young as Miss Brooke is, her friends ought to interfere a little to hinder her from doing anything foolish.", "그리고 내 생각엔 브룩 양처럼 어린 처녀라면, 어리석은 짓을 못 하도록 친구들이 좀 나서야 마땅하오."], ["You laugh, because you fancy I have some feeling on my own account.", "당신은 내가 사사로운 감정이 있어서 그러는 줄 알고 웃는구려."], ["But upon my honor, it is not that.", "하지만 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 그게 아니오."], ["I should feel just the same if I were Miss Brooke’s brother or uncle.”", "내가 브룩 양의 오라비나 삼촌이라 해도 똑같이 느꼈을 거요.」"]], [["“Well, but what should you do?”", "「그럼, 그래서 당신이라면 어떻게 하겠소?」"]], [["“I should say that the marriage must not be decided on until she was of age.", "「그녀가 성년이 될 때까지는 결혼을 결정해서는 안 된다고 하겠소."], ["And depend upon it, in that case, it would never come off.", "그리고 분명히 말하건대, 그렇게 되면 결혼은 결코 성사되지 않을 거요."], ["I wish you saw it as I do—I wish you would talk to Brooke about it.”", "당신도 나처럼 봐 주면 좋겠소—브룩에게 이 일을 이야기해 주면 좋겠소.」"]], [["Sir James rose as he was finishing his sentence, for he saw Mrs. Cadwallader entering from the study.", "제임스 경은 문장을 마치며 자리에서 일어났으니, 캐드월래더 부인이 서재 쪽에서 들어오는 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["She held by the hand her youngest girl, about five years old, who immediately ran to papa, and was made comfortable on his knee.", "그녀는 다섯 살쯤 된 막내딸의 손을 잡고 있었는데, 아이는 곧장 아빠에게로 달려가 무릎 위에 편안히 자리를 잡았다."]], [["“I hear what you are talking about,”", "「무슨 얘기를 나누는지 들었어요.」"], ["said the wife.", "아내가 말했다."], ["“But you will make no impression on Humphrey.", "「하지만 험프리한테는 아무런 영향도 못 줄 거예요."], ["As long as the fish rise to his bait, everybody is what he ought to be.", "물고기가 미끼를 물어 주기만 하면, 그이한테는 누구나 마땅히 있어야 할 모습 그대로거든요."], ["Bless you, Casaubon has got a trout-stream, and does not care about fishing in it himself: could there be a better fellow?”", "세상에, 캐소본은 송어가 사는 개울을 가졌으면서도 거기서 손수 낚시할 생각은 안 한다지요. 이보다 더 좋은 사람이 어디 있겠어요?」"]], [["“Well, there is something in that,”", "「글쎄, 그 말에도 일리가 있소.」"], ["said the Rector, with his quiet, inward laugh.", "목사가 조용한 속웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“It is a very good quality in a man to have a trout-stream.”", "「송어 개울을 가졌다는 건 사람에게 아주 좋은 자질이지.」"]], [["“But seriously,”", "「하지만 진지하게 말해서.」"], ["said Sir James, whose vexation had not yet spent itself, “don’t you think the Rector might do some good by speaking?”", "아직 분이 다 풀리지 않은 제임스 경이 말했다. 「목사님이 한마디 하면 무슨 도움이 되지 않겠소?」"]], [["“Oh, I told you beforehand what he would say,” answered Mrs.", "「오, 그이가 무슨 말을 할지 내가 미리 일러 줬잖아요.」"], ["Cadwallader, lifting up her eyebrows.", "캐드월래더 부인이 눈썹을 치켜올리며 대꾸했다."], ["“I have done what I could: I wash my hands of the marriage.”", "「난 할 수 있는 건 다 했어요. 이 결혼에서 난 손을 떼겠어요.」"]], [["“In the first place,”", "「우선 말이오.」"], ["said the Rector, looking rather grave, “it would be nonsensical to expect that I could convince Brooke, and make him act accordingly.", "목사가 다소 진지한 표정으로 말했다. 「내가 브룩을 설득해서 그대로 움직이게 할 수 있으리라 기대하는 건 어불성설이오."], ["Brooke is a very good fellow, but pulpy; he will run into any mould, but he won’t keep shape.”", "브룩은 아주 좋은 친구지만, 물렁물렁하단 말이오. 어떤 틀에든 흘러 들어가지만, 모양을 그대로 지키지는 못하지.」"]], [["“He might keep shape long enough to defer the marriage,”", "「결혼을 미룰 만큼만은 모양을 지킬 수도 있잖소.」"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“But, my dear Chettam, why should I use my influence to Casaubon’s disadvantage, unless I were much surer than I am that I should be acting for the advantage of Miss Brooke?", "「하지만, 체텀, 내가 브룩 양에게 이로운 쪽으로 행동하는 거라고 지금보다 훨씬 더 확신하지 않는 한, 어째서 내가 내 영향력을 캐소본에게 불리하게 써야 한단 말이오?"], ["I know no harm of Casaubon.", "난 캐소본에게서 아무 잘못도 모르오."], ["I don’t care about his Xisuthrus and Fee-fo-fum and the rest; but then he doesn’t care about my fishing-tackle.", "나는 그의 크시수트로스니 피포펌이니 하는 것들에는 관심 없소. 하지만 그렇다고 그가 내 낚시 도구에 관심을 두는 것도 아니지 않소."], ["As to the line he took on the Catholic Question, that was unexpected; but he has always been civil to me, and I don’t see why I should spoil his sport.", "가톨릭 문제에서 그가 취한 입장은 뜻밖이었지만, 그는 늘 내게 예의 발랐고, 그러니 내가 어째서 그의 즐거움을 망쳐야 하는지 모르겠소."], ["For anything I can tell, Miss Brooke may be happier with him than she would be with any other man.”", "내가 알 수 있는 한, 브룩 양은 다른 어떤 남자와 사는 것보다 그와 함께 더 행복할지도 모르오.」"]], [["“Humphrey!", "「험프리!"], ["I have no patience with you.", "당신한텐 정말 인내심이 바닥나요."], ["You know you would rather dine under the hedge than with Casaubon alone.", "당신은 캐소본과 단둘이 식사하느니 차라리 울타리 밑에서 먹는 편이 낫다고 여기잖아요."], ["You have nothing to say to each other.”", "두 사람은 서로 할 말이 하나도 없으면서.」"]], [["“What has that to do with Miss Brooke’s marrying him?", "「그게 브룩 양이 그와 결혼하는 것과 무슨 상관이오?"], ["She does not do it for my amusement.”", "그녀가 내 즐거움을 위해 결혼하는 것도 아니잖소.」"]], [["“He has got no good red blood in his body,”", "「그자 몸에는 좋은 붉은 피라곤 한 방울도 없소.」"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“No.", "「없고말고요."], ["Somebody put a drop under a magnifying-glass and it was all semicolons and parentheses,”", "누가 한 방울을 확대경 아래 놓고 봤더니 온통 세미콜론하고 괄호뿐이었다지요.」"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월래더 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Why does he not bring out his book, instead of marrying,”", "「결혼할 게 아니라 어째서 자기 책을 내놓지 않는 거요?」"], ["said Sir James, with a disgust which he held warranted by the sound feeling of an English layman.", "제임스 경이, 영국 평신도다운 건전한 감정으로 정당하다 여기는 혐오감을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Oh, he dreams footnotes, and they run away with all his brains.", "「오, 그이는 각주를 꿈꾸고, 그 각주들이 그이의 머릿속을 통째로 끌고 달아나 버린답니다."], ["They say, when he was a little boy, he made an abstract of ‘Hop o’ my Thumb,’ and he has been making abstracts ever since.", "어릴 적에 「엄지 동자」 이야기의 개요를 만들었다나요, 그 뒤로 줄곧 개요만 만들고 있다지요."], ["Ugh!", "어휴!"], ["And that is the man Humphrey goes on saying that a woman may be happy with.”", "그런 사람과 여자가 행복할 수 있다고 험프리는 자꾸만 우긴다니까요.」"]], [["“Well, he is what Miss Brooke likes,”", "「뭐, 그가 바로 브룩 양이 좋아하는 사람이오.」"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“I don’t profess to understand every young lady’s taste.”", "「나는 젊은 아가씨들의 취향을 죄다 이해한다고 자처하지는 않소.」"]], [["“But if she were your own daughter?”", "「하지만 그녀가 당신 친딸이라면 어떻겠소?」"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“That would be a different affair.", "「그건 또 다른 문제겠지요."], ["She is not my daughter, and I don’t feel called upon to interfere.", "그녀는 내 딸이 아니고, 나는 나서야 할 처지라고 느끼지 않소."], ["Casaubon is as good as most of us.", "캐소본은 우리 대부분만큼은 괜찮은 사람이오."], ["He is a scholarly clergyman, and creditable to the cloth.", "학식 있는 성직자이고, 성직자 신분에 걸맞은 인물이지."], ["Some Radical fellow speechifying at Middlemarch said Casaubon was the learned straw-chopping incumbent, and Freke was the brick-and-mortar incumbent, and I was the angling incumbent.", "미들마치에서 어떤 급진파 친구가 일장 연설을 하면서, 캐소본은 학식 깊은 지푸라기나 써는 교구 목사요, 프리크는 벽돌과 회반죽이나 다루는 교구 목사요, 나는 낚시질이나 하는 교구 목사라고 했다오."], ["And upon my word, I don’t see that one is worse or better than the other.”", "그런데 정말이지, 나는 그중 누가 누구보다 더 못하거나 더 낫다고는 보지 않소.」"], ["The Rector ended with his silent laugh.", "목사는 그 소리 없는 웃음으로 말을 맺었다."], ["He always saw the joke of any satire against himself.", "그는 언제나 자신을 겨눈 풍자의 우스운 대목을 알아챘다."], ["His conscience was large and easy, like the rest of him: it did only what it could do without any trouble.", "그의 양심은 나머지 모든 면이 그러하듯 넓고 느긋했으니, 아무런 수고 없이도 할 수 있는 일만 했다."]], [["Clearly, there would be no interference with Miss Brooke’s marriage through Mr.", "분명, 캐드월래더 씨를 통해 브룩 양의 결혼에 끼어들 길은 없었다."], ["Cadwallader; and Sir James felt with some sadness that she was to have perfect liberty of misjudgment.", "제임스 경은 그녀가 잘못 판단할 완벽한 자유를 누리게 되리라는 생각에 어느 정도 슬픔을 느꼈다."], ["It was a sign of his good disposition that he did not slacken at all in his intention of carrying out Dorothea’s design of the cottages.", "그가 도러시아의 오두막 설계를 실행하려는 의도를 조금도 늦추지 않은 것은 그의 선량한 성품을 보여 주는 증표였다."], ["Doubtless this persistence was the best course for his own dignity: but pride only helps us to be generous; it never makes us so, any more than vanity makes us witty.", "분명 이런 끈기는 그 자신의 품위를 위해서도 최선의 길이었으리라. 하지만 자긍심은 우리가 너그러워지도록 도울 뿐, 결코 우리를 너그럽게 만들지는 못하니, 허영이 우리를 재치 있게 만들지 못하는 것과 마찬가지다."], ["She was now enough aware of Sir James’s position with regard to her, to appreciate the rectitude of his perseverance in a landlord’s duty, to which he had at first been urged by a lover’s complaisance, and her pleasure in it was great enough to count for something even in her present happiness.", "그녀는 이제 자신을 향한 제임스 경의 처지를 충분히 알게 되어, 처음에는 연인다운 호의에서 떠밀려 시작했으나 이제는 지주로서의 의무를 꿋꿋이 지켜 나가는 그의 올곧음을 높이 살 수 있었다. 그리고 거기서 얻는 그녀의 기쁨은 지금의 행복 속에서도 한몫을 차지할 만큼 컸다."], ["Perhaps she gave to Sir James Chettam’s cottages all the interest she could spare from Mr. Casaubon, or rather from the symphony of hopeful dreams, admiring trust, and passionate self devotion which that learned gentleman had set playing in her soul.", "어쩌면 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게서, 아니 오히려 그 학식 있는 신사가 그녀의 영혼 속에 울려 퍼지게 한 희망찬 꿈과 흠모하는 신뢰와 열정적인 자기 헌신의 교향곡에서 떼어 낼 수 있는 만큼의 관심을 제임스 체텀 경의 오두막에 쏟았는지도 모른다."], ["Hence it happened that in the good baronet’s succeeding visits, while he was beginning to pay small attentions to Celia, he found himself talking with more and more pleasure to Dorothea.", "그리하여 선량한 준남작이 잇따라 방문하는 동안, 그는 실리아에게 자잘한 관심을 기울이기 시작하면서도, 점점 더 큰 즐거움을 느끼며 도러시아와 이야기를 나누는 자신을 발견하게 되었다."], ["She was perfectly unconstrained and without irritation towards him now, and he was gradually discovering the delight there is in frank kindness and companionship between a man and a woman who have no passion to hide or confess.", "이제 그녀는 그를 향해 더없이 거리낌이 없었고 짜증도 내지 않았으니, 그는 감추거나 고백할 정념이 없는 남자와 여자 사이에 오가는 솔직한 친절과 우정에 어떤 기쁨이 깃들어 있는지를 차츰 깨달아 가고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX.", "# CHAPTER IX."]], [["1st Gent. An ancient land in ancient oracles Is called “law-thirsty”: all the struggle there Was after order and a perfect rule. Pray, where lie such lands now? . . .", "질서와 완벽한 통치를 향한 것이었다."]], [["2d Gent. Why, where they lay of old—in human souls.", "청컨대, 그런 나라들이 지금은 어디에 있는가?……"]], [["Mr.", "제2신사."], ["Casaubon’s behavior about settlements was highly satisfactory to Mr.", "어찌 묻는가, 그 나라들이 옛적에 있던 곳에—인간의 영혼 속에 있지 않은가."], ["Brooke, and the preliminaries of marriage rolled smoothly along, shortening the weeks of courtship.", "재산 분배에 관한 캐소본 씨의 처신은 브룩 씨에게 매우 흡족했고, 결혼의 사전 절차는 순조롭게 진행되어 구애의 시간을 단축시켰다."], ["The betrothed bride must see her future home, and dictate any changes that she would like to have made there.", "약혼한 신부는 자기가 살게 될 집을 둘러보고, 거기서 고치고 싶은 점이 있으면 무엇이든 지시해야 했다."], ["A woman dictates before marriage in order that she may have an appetite for submission afterwards.", "여자가 결혼 전에 이것저것 지시하는 것은, 결혼 후에 복종에 대한 입맛을 갖기 위해서이다."], ["And certainly, the mistakes that we male and female mortals make when we have our own way might fairly raise some wonder that we are so fond of it.", "그리고 분명, 우리 남녀 인간들이 제 뜻대로 할 때 저지르는 실수들을 생각하면, 우리가 그토록 제 뜻대로 하기를 좋아한다는 사실에 적이 의아함을 품을 만도 하다."]], [["On a gray but dry November morning Dorothea drove to Lowick in company with her uncle and Celia. Mr.", "잿빛이지만 건조한 어느 11월 아침, 도러시아는 삼촌과 실리아와 함께 로윅으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["Casaubon’s home was the manor-house.", "캐소본 씨의 집은 영주관이었다."], ["Close by, visible from some parts of the garden, was the little church, with the old parsonage opposite.", "바로 가까이에, 정원의 어느 지점에서는 보이는 작은 교회가 있었고, 그 맞은편에는 오래된 사제관이 있었다."], ["In the beginning of his career, Mr. Casaubon had only held the living, but the death of his brother had put him in possession of the manor also.", "캐소본 씨는 처음 성직 생활을 시작했을 무렵에는 그 성직록만을 받았으나, 형의 죽음으로 영주관까지 소유하게 되었다."], ["It had a small park, with a fine old oak here and there, and an avenue of limes towards the southwest front, with a sunk fence between park and pleasure-ground, so that from the drawing-room windows the glance swept uninterruptedly along a slope of greensward till the limes ended in a level of corn and pastures, which often seemed to melt into a lake under the setting sun.", "거기에는 작은 사슴 공원이 딸려 있어 여기저기 멋진 고목 떡갈나무가 서 있었고, 남서쪽 정면을 향해 보리수 가로수길이 나 있었으며, 공원과 정원 사이에는 우묵하게 판 울타리가 있어, 응접실 창문에서 바라보면 시선이 푸른 잔디 비탈을 따라 거침없이 미끄러져 내려가다가 보리수가 끝나는 곳에서 옥수수밭과 목초지의 평지로 이어졌고, 그곳은 지는 해 아래서 흔히 호수로 녹아드는 듯 보였다."], ["This was the happy side of the house, for the south and east looked rather melancholy even under the brightest morning.", "이쪽이 그 집의 밝은 쪽이었으니, 남쪽과 동쪽은 가장 화창한 아침에도 다소 우울해 보였기 때문이다."], ["The grounds here were more confined, the flower-beds showed no very careful tendance, and large clumps of trees, chiefly of sombre yews, had risen high, not ten yards from the windows.", "이쪽의 부지는 더 비좁았고, 화단은 그다지 정성 들여 가꾼 흔적이 없었으며, 주로 음울한 주목으로 이루어진 큰 나무 무더기가 창문에서 채 열 야드도 안 되는 곳에 높이 솟아 있었다."], ["The building, of greenish stone, was in the old English style, not ugly, but small-windowed and melancholy-looking: the sort of house that must have children, many flowers, open windows, and little vistas of bright things, to make it seem a joyous home.", "푸르스름한 돌로 지은 건물은 옛 영국식이었는데, 보기 흉하지는 않았으나 창이 작고 우울해 보였다. 즐거운 가정처럼 보이려면 아이들과 많은 꽃과 열린 창과 밝은 것들이 펼쳐지는 자그마한 전망이 반드시 있어야 할 그런 종류의 집이었다."], ["In this latter end of autumn, with a sparse remnant of yellow leaves falling slowly athwart the dark evergreens in a stillness without sunshine, the house too had an air of autumnal decline, and Mr. Casaubon, when he presented himself, had no bloom that could be thrown into relief by that background.", "햇빛 한 점 없는 고요 속에서 노랗게 남은 몇 안 되는 잎들이 짙은 상록수 사이로 천천히 비껴 떨어지는 이 늦가을 무렵, 그 집도 가을의 쇠락 같은 분위기를 띠고 있었고, 캐소본 씨가 모습을 드러냈을 때, 그 배경을 받아 도드라질 만한 생기는 그에게 조금도 없었다."]], [["“Oh dear!”", "\"어머나!\""], ["Celia said to herself, “I am sure Freshitt Hall would have been pleasanter than this.”", "실리아는 속으로 말했다. \"이보다는 프레시트 홀이 훨씬 더 기분 좋았을 텐데.\""], ["She thought of the white freestone, the pillared portico, and the terrace full of flowers, Sir James smiling above them like a prince issuing from his enchantment in a rose-bush, with a handkerchief swiftly metamorphosed from the most delicately odorous petals—Sir James, who talked so agreeably, always about things which had common-sense in them, and not about learning!", "그녀는 하얀 사암과 기둥이 늘어선 주랑 현관과 꽃이 가득한 테라스를 떠올렸고, 그 위에서 마치 장미 덤불의 마법에서 풀려나온 왕자처럼 미소 짓는 제임스 경을, 그리고 더없이 향기로운 꽃잎에서 순식간에 손수건으로 바뀌어 나오는 모습을 떠올렸다. 늘 학문 따위가 아니라 상식이 담긴 이야기를, 그토록 유쾌하게 들려주는 제임스 경을!"], ["Celia had those light young feminine tastes which grave and weatherworn gentlemen sometimes prefer in a wife; but happily Mr.", "실리아는 진지하고 풍상에 닳은 신사들이 때때로 아내에게서 선호하는, 그 가볍고 어린 여성적인 취향을 지니고 있었다."], ["Casaubon’s bias had been different, for he would have had no chance with Celia.", "하지만 다행히도 캐소본 씨의 성향은 달랐으니, 그렇지 않았다면 그는 실리아에게는 가망이 없었을 것이다."]], [["Dorothea, on the contrary, found the house and grounds all that she could wish: the dark book-shelves in the long library, the carpets and curtains with colors subdued by time, the curious old maps and bird’s-eye views on the walls of the corridor, with here and there an old vase below, had no oppression for her, and seemed more cheerful than the casts and pictures at the Grange, which her uncle had long ago brought home from his travels—they being probably among the ideas he had taken in at one time.", "도러시아는 반대로, 그 집과 부지가 자기가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이라고 여겼다. 기다란 서재의 어두운 책장들, 세월에 색이 가라앉은 양탄자와 커튼, 복도 벽에 걸린 진기한 옛 지도와 조감도들, 그리고 그 아래 여기저기 놓인 오래된 꽃병들은 그녀에게 아무런 답답함도 주지 않았고, 오래전 삼촌이 여행에서 가져온 그레인지의 석고상이며 그림들보다 더 명랑해 보였다."], ["To poor Dorothea these severe classical nudities and smirking Renaissance-Correggiosities were painfully inexplicable, staring into the midst of her Puritanic conceptions: she had never been taught how she could bring them into any sort of relevance with her life.", "그것들은 아마 삼촌이 한때 받아들였던 여러 관념들에 속한 것이리라. 가엾은 도러시아에게 그 엄숙한 고전적 나신상들과 능글맞게 웃는 르네상스풍의 코레조 같은 것들은 그녀의 청교도적 관념 한복판을 빤히 노려보는, 고통스러우리만치 이해할 수 없는 것이었으니, 그녀는 그것들을 자기 삶과 어떤 식으로든 관련지을 수 있는 방법을 한 번도 배운 적이 없었다."], ["But the owners of Lowick apparently had not been travellers, and Mr. Casaubon’s studies of the past were not carried on by means of such aids.", "그러나 로윅의 주인들은 보아하니 여행을 다닌 사람들이 아니었고, 캐소본 씨의 과거 연구도 그런 보조물의 힘을 빌려 이루어진 것이 아니었다."]], [["Dorothea walked about the house with delightful emotion.", "도러시아는 즐거운 감정에 젖어 집안을 거닐었다."], ["Everything seemed hallowed to her: this was to be the home of her wifehood, and she looked up with eyes full of confidence to Mr.", "모든 것이 그녀에게는 신성해 보였다. 이곳이 그녀의 아내로서의 보금자리가 될 것이었기에, 캐소본 씨가 어떤 실제 배치를 특별히 일러 주며 고치고 싶은지 물을 때면 그녀는 신뢰가 가득한 눈으로 그를 올려다보았다."], ["Casaubon when he drew her attention specially to some actual arrangement and asked her if she would like an alteration.", "자기 취향을 묻는 모든 청에 그녀는 고맙게 응했으나, 고칠 만한 것은 아무것도 없다고 여겼다."], ["All appeals to her taste she met gratefully, but saw nothing to alter. His efforts at exact courtesy and formal tenderness had no defect for her.", "정확한 예의와 격식 있는 다정함을 갖추려는 그의 노력은 그녀에게 아무런 결함도 없어 보였다."], ["She filled up all blanks with unmanifested perfections, interpreting him as she interpreted the works of Providence, and accounting for seeming discords by her own deafness to the higher harmonies.", "그녀는 모든 빈자리를 드러나지 않은 완전함으로 채웠으니, 섭리의 작품을 해석하듯 그를 해석했고, 어긋나 보이는 불협화음은 자신이 더 높은 화음을 듣지 못하는 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["And there are many blanks left in the weeks of courtship which a loving faith fills with happy assurance.", "그리고 구애의 시간에는 사랑에 찬 믿음이 행복한 확신으로 채워 넣는 많은 빈자리들이 남아 있는 법이다."]], [["“Now, my dear Dorothea, I wish you to favor me by pointing out which room you would like to have as your boudoir,”", "\"자, 사랑하는 도러시아, 어느 방을 당신의 규방으로 쓰고 싶은지 일러 주면 고맙겠소.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, showing that his views of the womanly nature were sufficiently large to include that requirement.", "캐소본 씨는 여성의 본성에 관한 그의 견해가 그러한 요구까지 포함할 만큼 충분히 너그럽다는 것을 내보이며 말했다."]], [["“It is very kind of you to think of that,”", "\"그렇게 생각해 주시다니 정말 친절하세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, “but I assure you I would rather have all those matters decided for me.", "도러시아가 말했다. \"하지만 정말이지 저는 그런 일들은 모두 정해 주시는 편이 더 좋아요."], ["I shall be much happier to take everything as it is—just as you have been used to have it, or as you will yourself choose it to be.", "모든 것을 있는 그대로—당신이 늘 써 오시던 그대로, 혹은 당신이 직접 정하시는 그대로 받아들이는 편이 훨씬 더 행복할 거예요."], ["I have no motive for wishing anything else.”", "달리 무엇을 바랄 까닭이 제겐 없답니다.\""]], [["“Oh, Dodo,”", "\"어머, 도도 언니,\""], ["said Celia, “will you not have the bow-windowed room up-stairs?”", "실리아가 말했다. \"위층에 그 활 모양 창이 난 방을 갖지 않을 거야?\""]], [["Mr. Casaubon led the way thither.", "캐소본 씨가 그곳으로 앞장섰다."], ["The bow-window looked down the avenue of limes; the furniture was all of a faded blue, and there were miniatures of ladies and gentlemen with powdered hair hanging in a group.", "활 모양 창은 보리수 가로수길을 내려다보고 있었고, 가구는 모두 빛바랜 푸른색이었으며, 분을 바른 머리를 한 신사 숙녀들의 세밀화가 한데 모여 걸려 있었다."], ["A piece of tapestry over a door also showed a blue-green world with a pale stag in it.", "어느 문 위에 걸린 한 폭의 태피스트리에도 창백한 수사슴 한 마리가 있는 청록색 세계가 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["The chairs and tables were thin-legged and easy to upset.", "의자와 탁자는 다리가 가늘어 넘어지기 쉬웠다."], ["It was a room where one might fancy the ghost of a tight-laced lady revisiting the scene of her embroidery.", "코르셋을 꽉 졸라맨 어느 부인의 유령이 자기가 수를 놓던 자리를 다시 찾아오는 모습을 상상해 봄 직한 방이었다."], ["A light bookcase contained duodecimo volumes of polite literature in calf, completing the furniture.", "가벼운 책장에는 송아지 가죽으로 장정한 12절판의 교양 문학책들이 꽂혀 있어, 가구를 마저 채우고 있었다."]], [["“Yes,” said Mr.", "\"그래,\""], ["Brooke, “this would be a pretty room with some new hangings, sofas, and that sort of thing.", "브룩 씨가 말했다. \"여기에 새 휘장과 소파 같은 것들을 좀 들이면 예쁜 방이 되겠구나."], ["A little bare now.”", "지금은 좀 휑하고 말이야.\""]], [["“No, uncle,”", "\"아니에요, 삼촌,\""], ["said Dorothea, eagerly.", "도러시아가 간곡히 말했다."], ["“Pray do not speak of altering anything.", "\"제발 무엇을 고친다는 말씀은 마세요."], ["There are so many other things in the world that want altering—I like to take these things as they are.", "세상에는 고쳐야 할 다른 것들이 너무도 많은걸요—저는 이런 것들을 있는 그대로 받아들이는 게 좋아요."], ["And you like them as they are, don’t you?”", "그리고 당신도 이대로가 좋으시죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["she added, looking at Mr. Casaubon.", "그녀는 캐소본 씨를 바라보며 덧붙였다."], ["“Perhaps this was your mother’s room when she was young.”", "\"어쩌면 이 방은 어머님이 젊으셨을 때 쓰시던 방이었겠네요.\""]], [["“It was,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["he said, with his slow bend of the head.", "그는 천천히 고개를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["“This is your mother,”", "\"이분이 어머님이시군요.\""], ["said Dorothea, who had turned to examine the group of miniatures.", "세밀화 무리를 살펴보려고 몸을 돌렸던 도러시아가 말했다."], ["“It is like the tiny one you brought me; only, I should think, a better portrait.", "\"당신이 제게 주신 작은 그림과 닮았어요. 다만 이게 더 잘 그린 초상화 같네요."], ["And this one opposite, who is this?”", "그리고 맞은편의 이분은, 누구신가요?\""]], [["“Her elder sister.", "\"그분의 언니요."], ["They were, like you and your sister, the only two children of their parents, who hang above them, you see.”", "두 분도 당신과 동생처럼 부모님의 자식이라곤 둘뿐이었소. 보다시피 그 위에 부모님이 걸려 계시지.\""]], [["“The sister is pretty,”", "\"언니분이 예쁘네요.\""], ["said Celia, implying that she thought less favorably of Mr. Casaubon’s mother.", "실리아가, 캐소본 씨의 어머니는 그만 못하다고 여기는 속내를 내비치며 말했다."], ["It was a new opening to Celia’s imagination, that he came of a family who had all been young in their time—the ladies wearing necklaces.", "그가 한때 모두 젊었던—그 숙녀들이 목걸이를 둘렀던—집안 출신이라는 사실은, 실리아의 상상력에 새로운 문을 열어 주었다."]], [["“It is a peculiar face,”", "\"독특한 얼굴이네요.\""], ["said Dorothea, looking closely.", "도러시아가 가까이 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“Those deep gray eyes rather near together—and the delicate irregular nose with a sort of ripple in it—and all the powdered curls hanging backward.", "\"다소 가까이 붙은 저 짙은 잿빛 눈동자며—잔물결 같은 게 진 섬세하고 고르지 않은 코며—뒤로 넘긴 분 바른 곱슬머리며."], ["Altogether it seems to me peculiar rather than pretty.", "통틀어 보면 예쁘다기보다는 독특해 보여요."], ["There is not even a family likeness between her and your mother.”", "어머님과는 가족이라 할 만한 닮은 데조차 없네요.\""]], [["“No.", "\"그렇소."], ["And they were not alike in their lot.”", "그리고 두 분은 운명도 닮지 않았소.\""]], [["“You did not mention her to me,”", "\"이분에 대해선 제게 말씀해 주신 적이 없으셨어요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“My aunt made an unfortunate marriage.", "\"내 이모는 불행한 결혼을 했소."], ["I never saw her.”", "나는 그분을 한 번도 뵌 적이 없소.\""]], [["Dorothea wondered a little, but felt that it would be indelicate just then to ask for any information which Mr. Casaubon did not proffer, and she turned to the window to admire the view.", "도러시아는 조금 의아했으나, 그 순간 캐소본 씨가 자청해서 말하지 않은 것을 더 캐묻는 것은 무례한 일이라 느꼈고, 그래서 창가로 돌아서서 전망을 감탄하며 바라보았다."], ["The sun had lately pierced the gray, and the avenue of limes cast shadows.", "어느새 햇살이 잿빛 하늘을 꿰뚫고 나와, 보리수 가로수길에 그림자를 드리우고 있었다."]], [["“Shall we not walk in the garden now?”", "\"이제 정원을 거닐까요?\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“And you would like to see the church, you know,”", "\"교회도 보고 싶을 거다, 알다시피.\" 브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "\"익살맞은 작은 교회지."], ["“It is a droll little church.", "그리고 마을도 말이야."], ["And the village. It all lies in a nut-shell.", "모든 게 호두 껍데기 안에 들어앉은 듯 자그마하단다."], ["By the way, it will suit you, Dorothea; for the cottages are like a row of alms-houses—little gardens, gilly-flowers, that sort of thing.”", "그건 그렇고, 네 마음에 들 거다, 도러시아. 오두막들이 양로원 줄지어 선 것 같거든—작은 정원이며 비단향꽃무며, 그런 것들 말이다.\""]], [["“Yes, please,”", "\"네, 부탁드려요.\""], ["said Dorothea, looking at Mr.", "도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Casaubon, “I should like to see all that.”", "\"그 모든 걸 보고 싶어요.\""], ["She had got nothing from him more graphic about the Lowick cottages than that they were “not bad.”", "그녀는 그에게서 로윅의 오두막들에 관해 \"나쁘지 않다\"는 말보다 더 생생한 이야기를 들은 적이 없었다."]], [["They were soon on a gravel walk which led chiefly between grassy borders and clumps of trees, this being the nearest way to the church, Mr. Casaubon said.", "이내 그들은 주로 풀밭 가장자리와 나무 무더기 사이로 난 자갈길에 들어섰는데, 캐소본 씨의 말로는 이것이 교회로 가는 가장 가까운 길이었다."], ["At the little gate leading into the churchyard there was a pause while Mr. Casaubon went to the parsonage close by to fetch a key.", "교회 묘지로 통하는 작은 문 앞에서, 캐소본 씨가 바로 옆 사제관으로 열쇠를 가지러 간 동안 잠시 멈춰 섰다."], ["Celia, who had been hanging a little in the rear, came up presently, when she saw that Mr. Casaubon was gone away, and said in her easy staccato, which always seemed to contradict the suspicion of any malicious intent—", "조금 뒤처져 있던 실리아가, 캐소본 씨가 자리를 비운 것을 보고는 곧 다가와, 어떤 악의의 의심도 늘 부인하는 듯한 그 편안한 또박또박한 말투로 말했다—"]], [["“Do you know, Dorothea, I saw some one quite young coming up one of the walks.”", "\"있잖아, 언니, 산책로 한쪽으로 올라오는 아주 젊은 사람을 봤어.\""]], [["“Is that astonishing, Celia?”", "\"그게 놀랄 일이니, 실리아?\""]], [["“There may be a young gardener, you know—why not?”", "\"젊은 정원사가 있을지도 모르지—안 그래?\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“I told Casaubon he should change his gardener.”", "\"내가 캐소본에게 정원사를 바꾸라고 했지.\""]], [["“No, not a gardener,”", "\"아니, 정원사가 아니었어요.\""], ["said Celia; “a gentleman with a sketch-book.", "실리아가 말했다. \"스케치북을 든 신사였어요."], ["He had light-brown curls.", "연한 갈색 곱슬머리였고요."], ["I only saw his back.", "뒷모습만 봤지만요."], ["But he was quite young.”", "그래도 꽤 젊었어요.\""]], [["“The curate’s son, perhaps,”", "\"아마 부목사의 아들이겠지.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“Ah, there is Casaubon again, and Tucker with him.", "\"아, 저기 캐소본이 다시 오는구나, 터커도 함께 오고."], ["He is going to introduce Tucker.", "터커를 소개하려나 보다."], ["You don’t know Tucker yet.”", "너희들은 아직 터커를 모르지.\""]], [["Mr. Tucker was the middle-aged curate, one of the “inferior clergy,”", "터커 씨는 중년의 부목사로, 대개 아들이 부족하지 않은 부류인 \"하급 성직자\""], ["who are usually not wanting in sons.", "가운데 하나였다."], ["But after the introduction, the conversation did not lead to any question about his family, and the startling apparition of youthfulness was forgotten by every one but Celia.", "그러나 소개가 끝난 뒤에도 대화는 그의 가족에 관한 어떤 물음으로도 이어지지 않았고, 그 깜짝 놀랄 만한 젊음의 환영은 실리아를 제외한 모두에게서 잊혔다."], ["She inwardly declined to believe that the light-brown curls and slim figure could have any relationship to Mr. Tucker, who was just as old and musty-looking as she would have expected Mr.", "그녀는 그 연한 갈색 곱슬머리와 호리호리한 모습이 터커 씨와 무슨 관계가 있을 리 없다고 속으로 부인했으니, 터커 씨는 그녀가 캐소본 씨의 부목사라면 그러려니 했던 그대로 늙고 케케묵어 보이는 사람이었다."], ["Casaubon’s curate to be; doubtless an excellent man who would go to heaven (for Celia wished not to be unprincipled), but the corners of his mouth were so unpleasant.", "분명 천국에 갈 훌륭한 분이겠지만(실리아는 신념 없는 사람이 되고 싶지 않았으므로), 그 입꼬리가 어찌나 불쾌하던지."], ["Celia thought with some dismalness of the time she should have to spend as bridesmaid at Lowick, while the curate had probably no pretty little children whom she could like, irrespective of principle.", "실리아는 로윅에서 신부 들러리로 지내야 할 시간을 다소 침울하게 떠올렸으니, 그 부목사에게는 신념과 상관없이 그녀가 좋아할 만한 예쁘고 어린 자식들도 아마 없을 터였다."]], [["Mr. Tucker was invaluable in their walk; and perhaps Mr.", "터커 씨는 그들의 산책에 더없이 요긴했다."], ["Casaubon had not been without foresight on this head, the curate being able to answer all Dorothea’s questions about the villagers and the other parishioners.", "어쩌면 캐소본 씨가 이 점에 관해 미리 내다본 바가 없지 않았을 텐데, 그 부목사는 마을 사람들과 다른 교구민들에 관한 도러시아의 모든 물음에 답할 수 있었기 때문이다. 로윅에서는 모두가 형편이 넉넉하다고 그는 장담했다."], ["Everybody, he assured her, was well off in Lowick: not a cottager in those double cottages at a low rent but kept a pig, and the strips of garden at the back were well tended.", "싼 집세로 그 연립 오두막들에 사는 이들 가운데 돼지를 치지 않는 농가가 없었고, 뒤편의 텃밭들도 잘 가꾸어져 있었다. 사내아이들은 훌륭한 코듀로이를 입었고, 계집아이들은 단정한 하녀로 나가거나 집에서 짚을 엮는 일을 조금씩 했다."], ["The small boys wore excellent corduroy, the girls went out as tidy servants, or did a little straw-plaiting at home: no looms here, no Dissent; and though the public disposition was rather towards laying by money than towards spirituality, there was not much vice.", "이곳에는 베틀도 없고 비국교도도 없었다. 그리고 사람들의 성향이 영성보다는 돈을 모으는 쪽으로 다소 기울긴 했어도, 악덕은 그리 많지 않았다."], ["The speckled fowls were so numerous that Mr.", "얼룩진 닭들이 어찌나 많은지 브룩 씨가 한마디 했다."], ["Brooke observed, “Your farmers leave some barley for the women to glean, I see.", "\"여기 농부들은 여인들이 이삭을 주울 수 있도록 보리를 좀 남겨 두는군. 보아하니 말이야."], ["The poor folks here might have a fowl in their pot, as the good French king used to wish for all his people.", "그 어진 프랑스 왕이 자기 백성 모두가 그러길 바랐듯, 여기 가난한 사람들도 냄비에 닭 한 마리쯤은 넣을 수 있겠어."], ["The French eat a good many fowls—skinny fowls, you know.”", "프랑스 사람들은 닭을 꽤 많이 먹지—비쩍 마른 닭들이긴 하지만 말이야.\""]], [["“I think it was a very cheap wish of his,”", "\"제 생각엔 그건 그 왕이 참 값싸게 한 소원이었어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, indignantly.", "도러시아가 분개하며 말했다."], ["“Are kings such monsters that a wish like that must be reckoned a royal virtue?”", "\"왕들이 그처럼 괴물 같은 존재라서, 그런 소원조차 왕다운 미덕으로 쳐 줘야 하나요?\""]], [["“And if he wished them a skinny fowl,”", "\"그리고 만약 그가 비쩍 마른 닭을 바란 거라면,\""], ["said Celia, “that would not be nice.", "실리아가 말했다. \"그건 좋지 않았을 거예요."], ["But perhaps he wished them to have fat fowls.”", "하지만 어쩌면 그들이 살진 닭을 갖길 바랐는지도 모르죠.\""]], [["“Yes, but the word has dropped out of the text, or perhaps was subauditum; that is, present in the king’s mind, but not uttered,”", "\"그래, 하지만 그 단어가 본문에서 빠졌거나, 아니면 어쩌면 함축되었던 거지."], ["said Mr. Casaubon, smiling and bending his head towards Celia, who immediately dropped backward a little, because she could not bear Mr.", "즉, 왕의 마음속에는 있었으나 입 밖에 내지 않은 것이오.\""], ["Casaubon to blink at her.", "캐소본 씨가 미소 지으며 실리아 쪽으로 고개를 숙이며 말했고, 실리아는 캐소본 씨가 자기에게 눈을 깜박이는 것을 견딜 수 없어 곧장 조금 뒤로 물러났다."]], [["Dorothea sank into silence on the way back to the house.", "집으로 돌아가는 길에 도러시아는 침묵에 잠겼다."], ["She felt some disappointment, of which she was yet ashamed, that there was nothing for her to do in Lowick; and in the next few minutes her mind had glanced over the possibility, which she would have preferred, of finding that her home would be in a parish which had a larger share of the world’s misery, so that she might have had more active duties in it.", "그녀는 로윅에서는 자기가 할 일이 아무것도 없다는 데에 어떤 실망을 느꼈고, 그러면서도 그런 실망이 부끄러웠다. 그리고 다음 몇 분 동안 그녀의 마음은, 차라리 자기가 더 좋아했을 가능성을—세상의 비참함을 더 많이 떠안은 교구에 자기 보금자리가 자리하여, 그 안에서 더 능동적인 의무를 갖게 되는 가능성을—훑어 보았다."], ["Then, recurring to the future actually before her, she made a picture of more complete devotion to Mr. Casaubon’s aims in which she would await new duties.", "그러다 실제로 자기 앞에 놓인 미래로 되돌아와, 캐소본 씨의 목표에 더욱 온전히 헌신하며 새로운 의무들을 기다리는 자기 모습을 그려 보았다."], ["Many such might reveal themselves to the higher knowledge gained by her in that companionship.", "그러한 의무들 가운데 많은 것이, 그 동반의 삶 속에서 그녀가 얻게 될 더 높은 지식에 비로소 드러날지도 몰랐다."]], [["Mr. Tucker soon left them, having some clerical work which would not allow him to lunch at the Hall; and as they were re-entering the garden through the little gate, Mr.", "터커 씨는 영주관에서 점심을 함께할 수 없게 만드는 성직 업무가 있어 이내 그들 곁을 떠났다."], ["Casaubon said—", "그리고 그들이 작은 문을 지나 다시 정원으로 들어설 때, 캐소본 씨가 말했다—"]], [["“You seem a little sad, Dorothea.", "\"좀 슬퍼 보이는구려, 도러시아."], ["I trust you are pleased with what you have seen.”", "본 것들이 마음에 들었기를 바라오.\""]], [["“I am feeling something which is perhaps foolish and wrong,”", "\"어쩌면 어리석고 잘못된 어떤 감정을 느끼고 있는 것 같아요.\""], ["answered Dorothea, with her usual openness—“almost wishing that the people wanted more to be done for them here.", "도러시아가 늘 그렇듯 솔직하게 대답했다. \"이곳 사람들이 해 줄 일을 좀 더 필요로 했으면, 하고 바랄 뻔했거든요."], ["I have known so few ways of making my life good for anything.", "저는 제 삶을 무언가에 쓸모 있게 만드는 방법을 너무도 적게밖에 알지 못해요."], ["Of course, my notions of usefulness must be narrow.", "물론 쓸모에 관한 제 생각이 좁기야 하겠죠."], ["I must learn new ways of helping people.”", "사람들을 돕는 새로운 방법들을 배워야겠어요.\""]], [["“Doubtless,”", "\"분명 그럴 거요.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon.", "캐소본 씨가 말했다."], ["“Each position has its corresponding duties.", "\"각 위치에는 그에 걸맞은 의무가 있는 법이오."], ["Yours, I trust, as the mistress of Lowick, will not leave any yearning unfulfilled.”", "로윅의 안주인으로서 당신의 의무도, 바라건대 어떤 갈망도 채워지지 않은 채 남겨 두지는 않을 거요.\""]], [["“Indeed, I believe that,”", "\"정말이지, 저도 그렇게 믿어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, earnestly.", "도러시아가 진심으로 말했다."], ["“Do not suppose that I am sad.”", "\"제가 슬퍼한다고는 여기지 마세요.\""]], [["“That is well.", "\"그렇다면 좋소."], ["But, if you are not tired, we will take another way to the house than that by which we came.”", "그런데 피곤하지 않다면, 우리가 온 길과는 다른 길로 집에 갑시다.\""]], [["Dorothea was not at all tired, and a little circuit was made towards a fine yew-tree, the chief hereditary glory of the grounds on this side of the house.", "도러시아는 조금도 피곤하지 않았고, 그래서 그들은 멋진 주목 한 그루를 향해 조금 에둘러 갔으니, 그것은 집의 이쪽 부지가 자랑하는 으뜸가는 가보였다."], ["As they approached it, a figure, conspicuous on a dark background of evergreens, was seated on a bench, sketching the old tree. Mr.", "그들이 가까이 다가가자, 상록수의 어두운 배경 속에 또렷이 드러나는 한 사람이 벤치에 앉아 그 고목을 스케치하고 있었다."], ["Brooke, who was walking in front with Celia, turned his head, and said—", "실리아와 함께 앞서 걷던 브룩 씨가 고개를 돌리며 말했다—"]], [["“Who is that youngster, Casaubon?”", "\"저 젊은이는 누구요, 캐소본?\""]], [["They had come very near when Mr. Casaubon answered—", "그들이 아주 가까이 다가갔을 때 캐소본 씨가 대답했다—"]], [["“That is a young relative of mine, a second cousin: the grandson, in fact,”", "\"저 사람은 내 젊은 친척이오, 육촌이지. 사실은 손자라오.\""], ["he added, looking at Dorothea, “of the lady whose portrait you have been noticing, my aunt Julia.”", "그가 도러시아를 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"당신이 눈여겨보던 초상화의 그 부인, 내 이모 줄리아의 손자 말이오.\""]], [["The young man had laid down his sketch-book and risen.", "그 젊은이는 스케치북을 내려놓고 일어섰다."], ["His bushy light-brown curls, as well as his youthfulness, identified him at once with Celia’s apparition.", "그의 숱 많은 연한 갈색 곱슬머리는, 젊음과 더불어 그를 단번에 실리아가 본 그 환영과 일치시켰다."]], [["“Dorothea, let me introduce to you my cousin, Mr. Ladislaw.", "\"도러시아, 내 사촌인 래디슬로 군을 소개하겠소."], ["Will, this is Miss Brooke.”", "윌, 이쪽은 브룩 양일세.\""]], [["The cousin was so close now, that, when he lifted his hat, Dorothea could see a pair of gray eyes rather near together, a delicate irregular nose with a little ripple in it, and hair falling backward; but there was a mouth and chin of a more prominent, threatening aspect than belonged to the type of the grandmother’s miniature.", "이제 그 사촌이 어찌나 가까이 있던지, 그가 모자를 들어 올렸을 때 도러시아는 다소 가까이 붙은 한 쌍의 잿빛 눈동자와, 잔물결이 살짝 진 섬세하고 고르지 않은 코와, 뒤로 넘긴 머리카락을 볼 수 있었다. 다만 그 입과 턱은 할머니의 세밀화가 보여 주는 모습보다 더 도드라지고 위협적인 인상을 풍겼다."], ["Young Ladislaw did not feel it necessary to smile, as if he were charmed with this introduction to his future second cousin and her relatives; but wore rather a pouting air of discontent.", "젊은 래디슬로는, 미래의 육촌 형수와 그 친척들에게 소개되는 것이 마치 마음에 든다는 듯 미소 지을 필요를 느끼지 않았고, 오히려 입을 삐죽 내민 불만스러운 기색을 띠고 있었다."]], [["“You are an artist, I see,”", "\"화가시군요.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, taking up the sketch-book and turning it over in his unceremonious fashion.", "브룩 씨가 스케치북을 집어 들고 거리낌 없는 그 특유의 방식으로 이리저리 넘기며 말했다."]], [["“No, I only sketch a little.", "\"아니요, 그저 조금 끄적거릴 뿐입니다."], ["There is nothing fit to be seen there,”", "거기엔 봐 줄 만한 게 아무것도 없어요.\""], ["said young Ladislaw, coloring, perhaps with temper rather than modesty.", "젊은 래디슬로가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했는데, 어쩌면 겸손이라기보다는 성미 탓이었을 것이다."]], [["“Oh, come, this is a nice bit, now.", "\"오, 무슨 말을, 이건 멋진 솜씨인데."], ["I did a little in this way myself at one time, you know.", "나도 한때 이쪽으로 좀 했지, 알다시피. 자, 여기 보게나."], ["Look here, now; this is what I call a nice thing, done with what we used to call brio.” Mr.", "이게 바로 우리가 예전에 '브리오'라고 부르던 솜씨로 그린, 내가 멋지다고 하는 것일세.\""], ["Brooke held out towards the two girls a large colored sketch of stony ground and trees, with a pool.", "브룩 씨가 두 처녀를 향해 돌투성이 땅과 나무들과 못이 그려진 커다란 채색 스케치를 내밀었다."]], [["“I am no judge of these things,”", "\"저는 그런 것들을 판단할 줄 몰라요.\""], ["said Dorothea, not coldly, but with an eager deprecation of the appeal to her.", "도러시아가, 차갑지는 않으나 자기에게 묻는 것을 간곡히 사양하며 말했다."], ["“You know, uncle, I never see the beauty of those pictures which you say are so much praised.", "\"아시잖아요, 삼촌, 저는 삼촌이 그토록 칭송받는다고 하시는 그림들의 아름다움을 도무지 알아보지 못하는걸요."], ["They are a language I do not understand.", "그건 제가 이해하지 못하는 언어예요."], ["I suppose there is some relation between pictures and nature which I am too ignorant to feel—just as you see what a Greek sentence stands for which means nothing to me.” Dorothea looked up at Mr.", "아마 그림과 자연 사이에 어떤 관계가 있을 텐데, 제가 너무 무지해서 느끼지 못하는 거겠죠—마치 삼촌은 제겐 아무 의미도 없는 그리스어 문장이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알아보시듯이요.\""], ["Casaubon, who bowed his head towards her, while Mr. Brooke said, smiling nonchalantly—", "도러시아가 캐소본 씨를 올려다보자 그는 그녀 쪽으로 고개를 숙였고, 그동안 브룩 씨가 무심히 미소 지으며 말했다—"]], [["“Bless me, now, how different people are!", "\"이런, 사람들이 어찌 이리도 다른지!"], ["But you had a bad style of teaching, you know—else this is just the thing for girls—sketching, fine art and so on.", "하지만 너는 배운 방식이 나빴던 거다, 알다시피—그렇지 않았다면 이건 딱 여자아이들에게 어울리는 것인데—스케치, 미술 같은 것 말이다."], ["But you took to drawing plans; you don’t understand morbidezza, and that kind of thing.", "그런데 너는 설계도를 그리는 쪽으로 빠졌지."], ["You will come to my house, I hope, and I will show you what I did in this way,”", "너는 '모르비데차' 같은 것을 이해하지 못해.\""], ["he continued, turning to young Ladislaw, who had to be recalled from his preoccupation in observing Dorothea.", "그가 젊은 래디슬로에게로 돌아서며 말을 이었는데, 래디슬로는 도러시아를 관찰하는 데 골몰해 있다가 불려 나와야 했다. \"우리 집에 와 주게나, 부디."], ["Ladislaw had made up his mind that she must be an unpleasant girl, since she was going to marry Casaubon, and what she said of her stupidity about pictures would have confirmed that opinion even if he had believed her.", "내가 이쪽으로 한 것들을 보여 주겠네.\" 래디슬로는 캐소본과 결혼할 여자라면 분명 불쾌한 처녀일 거라고 마음을 굳혔던 터라, 그림에 관한 자기 어리석음을 두고 그녀가 한 말은, 설령 그가 그 말을 곧이곧대로 믿었다 해도 그 견해를 더욱 굳혀 주었을 것이다."], ["As it was, he took her words for a covert judgment, and was certain that she thought his sketch detestable.", "실상 그는 그녀의 말을 은근한 평가로 받아들였고, 그녀가 자기 스케치를 형편없다고 여긴다고 확신했다."], ["There was too much cleverness in her apology: she was laughing both at her uncle and himself.", "그녀의 변명에는 영리함이 너무 많이 배어 있었으니, 그녀는 자기 삼촌과 그를 함께 비웃고 있는 것이었다."], ["But what a voice!", "하지만 그 목소리란!"], ["It was like the voice of a soul that had once lived in an Aeolian harp.", "그것은 한때 아이올로스의 하프 속에 깃들어 살았던 어느 영혼의 목소리 같았다."], ["This must be one of Nature’s inconsistencies.", "이것은 자연의 모순 가운데 하나임이 틀림없었다."], ["There could be no sort of passion in a girl who would marry Casaubon.", "캐소본과 결혼할 처녀에게 어떤 종류의 열정도 있을 리 없었다."], ["But he turned from her, and bowed his thanks for Mr. Brooke’s invitation.", "그러나 그는 그녀에게서 몸을 돌려, 브룩 씨의 초대에 고개 숙여 감사를 표했다."]], [["“We will turn over my Italian engravings together,”", "\"내 이탈리아 동판화들을 함께 넘겨 보세나.\""], ["continued that good-natured man.", "그 마음씨 좋은 사람이 말을 이었다."], ["“I have no end of those things, that I have laid by for years.", "\"그런 게 끝도 없이 많아, 몇 년이나 처박아 둔 것들이지."], ["One gets rusty in this part of the country, you know.", "이런 시골에서는 녹슬기 마련이거든, 알다시피. 자네 말고, 캐소본은 빼고 말이야."], ["Not you, Casaubon; you stick to your studies; but my best ideas get undermost—out of use, you know.", "자네야 연구를 붙들고 있으니. 하지만 내 가장 좋은 생각들은 자꾸 밑바닥으로 가라앉아—쓰이지 않고 말이야."], ["You clever young men must guard against indolence.", "자네같이 영리한 젊은이들은 게으름을 경계해야 하네."], ["I was too indolent, you know: else I might have been anywhere at one time.”", "나는 너무 게을렀거든, 알다시피. 안 그랬으면 한때 어디든 됐을 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["“That is a seasonable admonition,” said Mr.", "\"때맞은 훈계로군요.\""], ["Casaubon; “but now we will pass on to the house, lest the young ladies should be tired of standing.”", "캐소본 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 이제 집으로 갑시다, 아가씨들이 서 있느라 지치지 않도록 말이오.\""]], [["When their backs were turned, young Ladislaw sat down to go on with his sketching, and as he did so his face broke into an expression of amusement which increased as he went on drawing, till at last he threw back his head and laughed aloud.", "그들이 등을 돌리자, 젊은 래디슬로는 다시 자리에 앉아 스케치를 계속했고, 그러면서 그의 얼굴에는 재미있어하는 표정이 번지더니 그림을 그려 나갈수록 점점 짙어져, 마침내 고개를 젖히고 소리 내어 웃었다."], ["Partly it was the reception of his own artistic production that tickled him; partly the notion of his grave cousin as the lover of that girl; and partly Mr. Brooke’s definition of the place he might have held but for the impediment of indolence.", "한편으로는 자기 작품이 받은 대접이 그를 간질였고, 한편으로는 그 엄숙한 사촌이 저 처녀의 연인이라는 발상이, 그리고 한편으로는 게으름이라는 걸림돌만 없었다면 자기가 차지했을 자리에 관한 브룩 씨의 정의가 그러했다."], ["Mr. Will Ladislaw’s sense of the ludicrous lit up his features very agreeably: it was the pure enjoyment of comicality, and had no mixture of sneering and self-exaltation.", "윌 래디슬로 군의 우스꽝스러운 것을 알아보는 감각은 그의 이목구비를 매우 보기 좋게 환히 밝혔으니, 그것은 익살에 대한 순수한 즐거움일 뿐, 비웃음이나 자기 과시는 조금도 섞여 있지 않았다."]], [["“What is your nephew going to do with himself, Casaubon?”", "\"자네 조카는 뭘 하며 지내려는 건가, 캐소본?\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, as they went on.", "그들이 걸어가는 동안 브룩 씨가 말했다."]], [["“My cousin, you mean—not my nephew.”", "\"내 사촌 말이지요—조카가 아니라.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, cousin.", "\"그래, 그래, 사촌."], ["But in the way of a career, you know.”", "하지만 직업을 두고 하는 말일세, 알다시피.\""]], [["“The answer to that question is painfully doubtful.", "\"그 물음에 대한 답은 고통스러우리만치 불확실합니다."], ["On leaving Rugby he declined to go to an English university, where I would gladly have placed him, and chose what I must consider the anomalous course of studying at Heidelberg.", "럭비를 졸업하고 나서 그는 내가 기꺼이 보내 주려던 영국 대학에 가기를 거절하고, 내가 변칙적이라 여길 수밖에 없는 길, 즉 하이델베르크에서 공부하는 길을 택했습니다."], ["And now he wants to go abroad again, without any special object, save the vague purpose of what he calls culture, preparation for he knows not what.", "그리고 이제 그는 다시 외국에 나가고 싶어 하는데, 무엇을 위함인지 그 자신도 모를 준비, 이른바 교양이라는 막연한 목적 말고는 아무런 특별한 목표도 없습니다."], ["He declines to choose a profession.”", "그는 직업을 정하기를 거절합니다.\""]], [["“He has no means but what you furnish, I suppose.”", "\"자네가 대 주는 것 말고는 다른 수입이 없겠지, 아마?\""]], [["“I have always given him and his friends reason to understand that I would furnish in moderation what was necessary for providing him with a scholarly education, and launching him respectably.", "\"나는 늘 그에게나 그의 지인들에게, 그를 학자다운 교육으로 길러 내고 번듯하게 출발시키는 데 필요한 것을 적당히 대 주겠다고 알려 왔습니다."], ["I am therefore bound to fulfil the expectation so raised,” said Mr.", "그러니 그렇게 품게 한 기대를 채워 줄 의무가 내게 있는 것이지요.\""], ["Casaubon, putting his conduct in the light of mere rectitude: a trait of delicacy which Dorothea noticed with admiration.", "캐소본 씨가 자신의 처신을 한낱 올바름의 빛 아래 놓으며 말했으니, 도러시아는 그 섬세함을 감탄하며 눈여겨보았다."]], [["“He has a thirst for travelling; perhaps he may turn out a Bruce or a Mungo Park,”", "\"그 친구가 여행에 목말라 있군. 어쩌면 브루스나 멍고 파크 같은 인물이 될지도 모르지.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“I had a notion of that myself at one time.”", "\"나도 한때 그런 생각을 품었거든.\""]], [["“No, he has no bent towards exploration, or the enlargement of our geognosis: that would be a special purpose which I could recognize with some approbation, though without felicitating him on a career which so often ends in premature and violent death.", "\"아니요, 그는 탐험이나 우리의 지질 지식을 넓히는 쪽으로는 소질이 없습니다. 그거야 내가 어느 정도 인정하며 받아들일 수 있는 특별한 목적이겠지요."], ["But so far is he from having any desire for a more accurate knowledge of the earth’s surface, that he said he should prefer not to know the sources of the Nile, and that there should be some unknown regions preserved as hunting grounds for the poetic imagination.”", "비록 그런 경력이 흔히 때 이른 비명횡사로 끝나는 것을 두고 그를 축하해 줄 수는 없겠지만 말입니다. 그런데 그는 지구 표면을 더 정확히 알고자 하는 욕구가 어찌나 없는지, 차라리 나일강의 수원을 모르는 편이 좋다고, 시적 상상력의 사냥터로 보존될 미지의 영역이 좀 있어야 한다고 말하더군요.\""]], [["“Well, there is something in that, you know,”", "\"글쎄, 거기엔 일리가 있어, 알다시피.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, who had certainly an impartial mind.", "분명 공평한 마음을 지닌 브룩 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It is, I fear, nothing more than a part of his general inaccuracy and indisposition to thoroughness of all kinds, which would be a bad augury for him in any profession, civil or sacred, even were he so far submissive to ordinary rule as to choose one.”", "\"그건 유감스럽게도, 온갖 종류의 철저함을 꺼리는 그의 전반적인 부정확함과 부주의함의 한 부분에 지나지 않을 거라 봅니다. 그것은 그가 어떤 직업, 세속의 것이든 신성한 것이든, 그가 통상의 규칙에 순응하여 하나를 택할 만큼 고분고분하다 해도, 그에게 나쁜 징조가 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps he has conscientious scruples founded on his own unfitness,”", "\"어쩌면 그분은 자기가 적합하지 않다는 데서 비롯된 양심상의 거리낌을 품고 있는지도 몰라요.\""], ["said Dorothea, who was interesting herself in finding a favorable explanation.", "도러시아가, 호의적인 해석을 찾는 데 흥미를 느끼며 말했다."], ["“Because the law and medicine should be very serious professions to undertake, should they not?", "\"법률과 의학은 떠맡기에 아주 진지한 직업이어야 하니까요, 그렇지 않나요?"], ["People’s lives and fortunes depend on them.”", "사람들의 생명과 재산이 거기에 달려 있잖아요.\""]], [["“Doubtless; but I fear that my young relative Will Ladislaw is chiefly determined in his aversion to these callings by a dislike to steady application, and to that kind of acquirement which is needful instrumentally, but is not charming or immediately inviting to self-indulgent taste.", "\"분명 그렇소. 하지만 나는 내 젊은 친척 윌 래디슬로가 그런 직업을 꺼리는 까닭이, 주로 꾸준한 노력과, 도구로서는 필요하나 제멋대로인 취향에는 매력적이거나 곧장 끌리지 않는 그런 종류의 습득을 싫어하는 데 있을까 두렵소."], ["I have insisted to him on what Aristotle has stated with admirable brevity, that for the achievement of any work regarded as an end there must be a prior exercise of many energies or acquired facilities of a secondary order, demanding patience.", "나는 그에게, 아리스토텔레스가 감탄스러우리만치 간결하게 진술한 바를 누차 강조했소. 즉, 목적으로 여겨지는 어떤 일을 이루려면, 그에 앞서 인내를 요하는 이차적인 여러 능력이나 습득된 재능을 먼저 발휘해야 한다는 것이오."], ["I have pointed to my own manuscript volumes, which represent the toil of years preparatory to a work not yet accomplished.", "나는 아직 완성하지 못한 한 저작을 위해 여러 해의 노고를 담은 내 친필 원고집을 그에게 가리켜 보였소. 하지만 헛수고였소."], ["But in vain. To careful reasoning of this kind he replies by calling himself Pegasus, and every form of prescribed work ‘harness.’”", "이런 식의 신중한 논리에 그는 자신을 페가수스라 칭하고, 정해진 모든 형태의 일을 '굴레'라 부르며 응수할 뿐이오.\""]], [["Celia laughed.", "실리아가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["She was surprised to find that Mr. Casaubon could say something quite amusing.", "캐소본 씨가 꽤 재미있는 말을 할 수 있다는 것을 알고 놀랐던 것이다."]], [["“Well, you know, he may turn out a Byron, a Chatterton, a Churchill—that sort of thing—there’s no telling,”", "\"글쎄, 알다시피, 그가 바이런이나 채터턴이나 처칠 같은 인물이 될지도 모르지—그런 부류 말이야—누가 알겠나.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“Shall you let him go to Italy, or wherever else he wants to go?”", "\"그를 이탈리아든, 가고 싶어 하는 다른 어디든 보내 줄 텐가?\""]], [["“Yes; I have agreed to furnish him with moderate supplies for a year or so; he asks no more.", "\"네. 한 일 년쯤 적당한 비용을 대 주기로 했습니다."], ["I shall let him be tried by the test of freedom.”", "그도 그 이상은 청하지 않더군요. 자유라는 시험으로 그를 한번 겪어 보게 할 작정입니다.\""]], [["“That is very kind of you,”", "\"참 친절하시네요.\""], ["said Dorothea, looking up at Mr. Casaubon with delight.", "도러시아가 기쁨에 차서 캐소본 씨를 올려다보며 말했다."], ["“It is noble.", "\"고귀한 일이에요."], ["After all, people may really have in them some vocation which is not quite plain to themselves, may they not?", "결국 사람들은 정말로 자기 안에, 자신에게조차 분명히 드러나지 않은 어떤 천직을 지니고 있을 수 있잖아요, 안 그래요?"], ["They may seem idle and weak because they are growing.", "한창 자라고 있기에 게으르고 약해 보일 수도 있고요."], ["We should be very patient with each other, I think.”", "우리는 서로에게 무척 인내심을 가져야 한다고 생각해요.\""]], [["“I suppose it is being engaged to be married that has made you think patience good,”", "\"인내를 좋게 여기게 된 건 약혼을 했기 때문인가 봐.\""], ["said Celia, as soon as she and Dorothea were alone together, taking off their wrappings.", "둘만 남아 겉옷을 벗는 사이에 실리아가 곧장 말했다."]], [["“You mean that I am very impatient, Celia.”", "\"내가 무척 참을성이 없다는 뜻이구나, 실리아.\""]], [["“Yes; when people don’t do and say just what you like.”", "\"응. 사람들이 언니 마음에 꼭 드는 대로 행동하고 말하지 않을 때 말이야.\""], ["Celia had become less afraid of “saying things” to Dorothea since this engagement: cleverness seemed to her more pitiable than ever.", "이번 약혼 이후로 실리아는 도러시아에게 \"이런저런 말을 하는 것\"을 전보다 덜 두려워하게 되었으니, 영리함이라는 것이 그녀에게는 그 어느 때보다 가엾어 보였던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER X.", "# CHAPTER X."]], [["“He had catched a great cold, had he had no other clothes to wear than the skin of a bear not yet killed.”—FULLER.", "「죽이지도 않은 곰의 가죽 말고는 입을 옷이 없었더라면, 그는 심한 감기에 걸리고 말았으리라.」—풀러."]], [["Young Ladislaw did not pay that visit to which Mr. Brooke had invited him, and only six days afterwards Mr.", "젊은 래디슬로는 브룩 씨가 청한 그 방문을 끝내 하지 않았고, 불과 엿새 뒤에 캐소본 씨는 자신의 젊은 친척이 대륙으로 떠났다고 언급했는데, 그 차갑고 모호한 어조로써 더 이상의 질문을 사양하는 듯했다."], ["Casaubon mentioned that his young relative had started for the Continent, seeming by this cold vagueness to waive inquiry.", "사실 윌은 유럽 전역이라는 것 외에 더 명확한 행선지를 정하기를 거부했던 것이다."], ["Indeed, Will had declined to fix on any more precise destination than the entire area of Europe.", "그는 천재성이란 필연적으로 속박을 견디지 못한다고 여겼다."], ["Genius, he held, is necessarily intolerant of fetters: on the one hand it must have the utmost play for its spontaneity; on the other, it may confidently await those messages from the universe which summon it to its peculiar work, only placing itself in an attitude of receptivity towards all sublime chances.", "한편으로 그것은 자발성을 위해 최대한의 활동 공간을 가져야 하고, 다른 한편으로는 그것을 그 고유의 과업으로 부르는 우주로부터의 전언을, 모든 숭고한 기회에 대해 오로지 수용의 자세를 취하기만 하면, 자신만만하게 기다릴 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["The attitudes of receptivity are various, and Will had sincerely tried many of them.", "수용의 자세는 다양하며, 윌은 그중 많은 것을 진지하게 시도해 보았다."], ["He was not excessively fond of wine, but he had several times taken too much, simply as an experiment in that form of ecstasy; he had fasted till he was faint, and then supped on lobster; he had made himself ill with doses of opium.", "그는 포도주를 지나치게 좋아하지는 않았으나, 단지 그러한 형태의 황홀경을 실험해 보려고 몇 번 과음한 적이 있었다. 또 기진맥진할 때까지 단식했다가 바닷가재로 저녁을 들었고, 아편을 복용하여 몸을 병들게 한 적도 있었다."], ["Nothing greatly original had resulted from these measures; and the effects of the opium had convinced him that there was an entire dissimilarity between his constitution and De Quincey’s.", "이런 방법들로부터 그다지 독창적인 결과는 나오지 않았으며, 아편의 효과는 그의 체질과 드 퀸시의 체질이 전혀 다르다는 사실을 그에게 확신시켜 주었다."], ["The superadded circumstance which would evolve the genius had not yet come; the universe had not yet beckoned.", "천재성을 펼쳐 낼 그 부가적인 상황은 아직 찾아오지 않았고, 우주는 아직 손짓하지 않았다."], ["Even Caesar’s fortune at one time was but a grand presentiment.", "카이사르의 운명조차 한때는 한낱 거창한 예감에 불과했다."], ["We know what a masquerade all development is, and what effective shapes may be disguised in helpless embryos.", "우리는 모든 발달이 얼마나 가면극 같은 것인지, 그리고 무력한 배아 속에 얼마나 강력한 형상이 변장하고 숨어 있을 수 있는지를 안다."], ["In fact, the world is full of hopeful analogies and handsome dubious eggs called possibilities.", "사실 세상은 희망에 찬 비유들과, 가능성이라 불리는 의심스럽고 그럴싸한 알들로 가득 차 있다."], ["Will saw clearly enough the pitiable instances of long incubation producing no chick, and but for gratitude would have laughed at Casaubon, whose plodding application, rows of note-books, and small taper of learned theory exploring the tossed ruins of the world, seemed to enforce a moral entirely encouraging to Will’s generous reliance on the intentions of the universe with regard to himself.", "윌은 오랜 부화 끝에도 병아리 한 마리 나오지 않는 가련한 사례들을 충분히 똑똑히 보았으며, 만일 감사하는 마음이 아니었다면 캐소본을 비웃었을 것이다. 캐소본의 우직한 노력, 줄지어 늘어선 공책들, 그리고 세상의 흩어진 폐허를 더듬어 가는 학문적 이론의 자그마한 촛불은, 자기 자신에 관한 우주의 의도에 대한 윌의 너그러운 신뢰를 전적으로 북돋워 주는 교훈을 강요하는 듯했다."], ["He held that reliance to be a mark of genius; and certainly it is no mark to the contrary; genius consisting neither in self-conceit nor in humility, but in a power to make or do, not anything in general, but something in particular.", "그는 그러한 신뢰를 천재의 표지로 여겼으며, 분명 그것이 그 반대의 표지는 아니다. 천재성이란 자만에도 겸손에도 있지 않고, 무언가를 일반적으로가 아니라 어떤 특정한 것을 만들거나 해내는 능력에 있는 것이다."], ["Let him start for the Continent, then, without our pronouncing on his future.", "그러니 우리가 그의 미래에 대해 단언하지 않은 채로, 그를 대륙으로 떠나게 하자."], ["Among all forms of mistake, prophecy is the most gratuitous.", "온갖 형태의 오류 가운데 예언이야말로 가장 부질없는 것이다."]], [["But at present this caution against a too hasty judgment interests me more in relation to Mr. Casaubon than to his young cousin.", "그러나 지금으로서는 너무 성급한 판단에 대한 이 경계가 그의 젊은 사촌보다는 캐소본 씨와 관련하여 나의 흥미를 더 끈다."], ["If to Dorothea Mr. Casaubon had been the mere occasion which had set alight the fine inflammable material of her youthful illusions, does it follow that he was fairly represented in the minds of those less impassioned personages who have hitherto delivered their judgments concerning him?", "도러시아에게 캐소본 씨가 그저 그녀의 젊은 환상이라는 잘 타오르는 재료에 불을 댕긴 한낱 계기였다고 해서, 지금까지 그에 관한 판단을 내려 온 덜 열정적인 인물들의 마음속에 그가 공정하게 그려졌다는 결론이 따라 나오는가?"], ["I protest against any absolute conclusion, any prejudice derived from Mrs. Cadwallader’s contempt for a neighboring clergyman’s alleged greatness of soul, or Sir James Chettam’s poor opinion of his rival’s legs,—from Mr.", "나는 어떠한 절대적인 결론에도, 이웃 성직자의 위대한 영혼이라는 주장에 대한 캐드월래더 부인의 경멸이나 자기 연적의 다리에 대한 제임스 체텀 경의 형편없는 평가에서 비롯된 어떠한 편견에도, 또한 동행의 생각을 끌어내지 못한 브룩 씨의 실패나 중년 학자의 외모에 대한 실리아의 비판에서 비롯된 어떠한 편견에도 항의한다."], ["Brooke’s failure to elicit a companion’s ideas, or from Celia’s criticism of a middle-aged scholar’s personal appearance.", "만일 그러한 고독한 최상급이 존재한 적이 있다 한들, 당대의 가장 위대한 인물조차 갖가지 작은 거울들에 비친 이런 불리한 자기 모습을 피할 수 있었을지 나는 확신하지 못한다."], ["I am not sure that the greatest man of his age, if ever that solitary superlative existed, could escape these unfavorable reflections of himself in various small mirrors; and even Milton, looking for his portrait in a spoon, must submit to have the facial angle of a bumpkin. Moreover, if Mr.", "밀턴조차 숟가락에서 제 초상을 찾으려 하면 시골뜨기의 안면 각도를 감수해야 하리라."], ["Casaubon, speaking for himself, has rather a chilling rhetoric, it is not therefore certain that there is no good work or fine feeling in him.", "더욱이 캐소본 씨가 스스로를 두고 말할 때 다소 냉랭한 화법을 쓴다 해서, 그에게 훌륭한 작업이나 고운 감정이 없다고 단정할 수는 없다."], ["Did not an immortal physicist and interpreter of hieroglyphs write detestable verses?", "불멸의 어느 물리학자이자 상형문자 해독자가 가증스러운 시를 쓰지 않았던가?"], ["Has the theory of the solar system been advanced by graceful manners and conversational tact?", "태양계 이론이 우아한 태도와 능란한 화술로 진전된 적이 있는가?"], ["Suppose we turn from outside estimates of a man, to wonder, with keener interest, what is the report of his own consciousness about his doings or capacity: with what hindrances he is carrying on his daily labors; what fading of hopes, or what deeper fixity of self-delusion the years are marking off within him; and with what spirit he wrestles against universal pressure, which will one day be too heavy for him, and bring his heart to its final pause.", "한 인간에 대한 외부의 평가에서 눈을 돌려, 더 예리한 흥미로써, 그 자신의 의식이 자기 행위나 능력에 관해 보내는 보고는 어떠한지, 그가 어떤 장애 속에서 나날의 노고를 이어 가고 있는지, 세월이 그의 내부에 어떤 희망의 시듦이나 자기기만의 더 깊은 고착을 새기고 있는지, 그리고 언젠가는 그에게 너무 무거워져 그의 심장을 마지막 멈춤으로 데려갈 보편적인 압력에 맞서 그가 어떤 정신으로 분투하고 있는지를 헤아려 본다고 가정해 보자. 의심할 바 없이 그의 처지는 그 자신의 눈에는 중요한 것이다."], ["Doubtless his lot is important in his own eyes; and the chief reason that we think he asks too large a place in our consideration must be our want of room for him, since we refer him to the Divine regard with perfect confidence; nay, it is even held sublime for our neighbor to expect the utmost there, however little he may have got from us. Mr.", "그리고 우리가 그를 위한 자리가 모자라기에 그가 우리의 고려 속에서 너무 큰 자리를 요구한다고 생각하는 것이 그 주된 이유임에 틀림없으니, 우리는 그를 완전한 확신을 가지고 신의 보살핌에 맡기는 까닭이다."], ["Casaubon, too, was the centre of his own world; if he was liable to think that others were providentially made for him, and especially to consider them in the light of their fitness for the author of a “Key to all Mythologies,”", "아니, 이웃이 우리에게서 받은 것이 아무리 적다 해도, 그가 거기서 최대한의 것을 기대하는 일은 오히려 숭고하다고까지 여겨진다. 캐소본 씨 역시 자기 자신의 세계의 중심이었다."], ["this trait is not quite alien to us, and, like the other mendicant hopes of mortals, claims some of our pity.", "그가 다른 이들이 섭리에 의해 자기를 위해 만들어졌다고 생각하기 쉬웠다 한들, 그리고 특히 그들을 「모든 신화의 열쇠」의 저자에게 얼마나 알맞은가 하는 관점에서 가늠하기 쉬웠다 한들, 이 특성은 우리와 그리 동떨어진 것이 아니며, 인간이 지닌 다른 구걸하는 희망들처럼, 우리의 연민을 얼마쯤 요구한다."]], [["Certainly this affair of his marriage with Miss Brooke touched him more nearly than it did any one of the persons who have hitherto shown their disapproval of it, and in the present stage of things I feel more tenderly towards his experience of success than towards the disappointment of the amiable Sir James.", "분명 미스 브룩과의 이 결혼이라는 사건은, 지금까지 그 일에 못마땅한 기색을 보여 온 그 어느 누구보다도 그에게 더 절실하게 와닿았으며, 지금 단계에서 나는 상냥한 제임스 경의 실망보다는 그가 거둔 성공의 경험에 대해 더 따뜻한 마음이 든다."], ["For in truth, as the day fixed for his marriage came nearer, Mr. Casaubon did not find his spirits rising; nor did the contemplation of that matrimonial garden scene, where, as all experience showed, the path was to be bordered with flowers, prove persistently more enchanting to him than the accustomed vaults where he walked taper in hand.", "사실 결혼식으로 정해진 날이 가까워질수록 캐소본 씨는 자기 마음이 들뜨는 것을 느끼지 못했으니, 또한 모든 경험이 보여 주듯 길가에 꽃이 피어 있게 마련인 그 부부의 정원 풍경을 떠올리는 것이, 그가 촛불을 손에 들고 거닐던 익숙한 지하 납골당보다 그에게 한결같이 더 매혹적이지도 않았던 것이다."], ["He did not confess to himself, still less could he have breathed to another, his surprise that though he had won a lovely and noble-hearted girl he had not won delight,—which he had also regarded as an object to be found by search.", "그는 사랑스럽고 고결한 마음을 지닌 처녀를 얻었으면서도 환희는—그가 역시 찾아내야 할 대상으로 여겼던 그 환희는—얻지 못했다는 자신의 놀라움을 스스로에게 고백하지 못했으며, 하물며 남에게 입 밖에 낼 수도 없었다."], ["It is true that he knew all the classical passages implying the contrary; but knowing classical passages, we find, is a mode of motion, which explains why they leave so little extra force for their personal application.", "그가 그 반대를 함의하는 모든 고전 구절을 알고 있었던 것은 사실이다. 그러나 고전 구절을 안다는 것은, 우리가 알다시피, 일종의 운동 방식이니, 그것이 어째서 그 구절들을 개인적으로 적용할 여분의 힘을 거의 남기지 않는지를 설명해 준다."]], [["Poor Mr. Casaubon had imagined that his long studious bachelorhood had stored up for him a compound interest of enjoyment, and that large drafts on his affections would not fail to be honored; for we all of us, grave or light, get our thoughts entangled in metaphors, and act fatally on the strength of them.", "가엾은 캐소본 씨는 자신의 오랜 학구적 독신 생활이 그를 위해 향락의 복리(複利)를 쌓아 두었으며, 자기 애정에 대해 큰 액수를 인출하더라도 어김없이 지불되리라고 상상해 왔다."], ["And now he was in danger of being saddened by the very conviction that his circumstances were unusually happy: there was nothing external by which he could account for a certain blankness of sensibility which came over him just when his expectant gladness should have been most lively, just when he exchanged the accustomed dulness of his Lowick library for his visits to the Grange.", "우리는 모두, 진중한 사람이든 가벼운 사람이든, 자기 생각을 비유 속에 얽어 넣고서 그 힘에 기대어 치명적으로 행동하는 까닭이다. 그리고 이제 그는 자기 처지가 유달리 행복하다는 바로 그 확신 때문에 슬퍼질 위험에 처해 있었다."], ["Here was a weary experience in which he was as utterly condemned to loneliness as in the despair which sometimes threatened him while toiling in the morass of authorship without seeming nearer to the goal.", "기대에 찬 기쁨이 가장 생생해야 할 바로 그때, 그가 로윅 서재의 익숙한 따분함을 그레인지 방문과 맞바꾸던 바로 그때 그에게 엄습하는 어떤 감수성의 공허함을 설명해 줄 외적인 것이 아무것도 없었다. 여기에 있는 것은, 목표에 더 가까워지는 기색도 없이 저술이라는 수렁 속에서 애쓰는 동안 이따금 그를 위협하던 절망 속에서와 마찬가지로, 그가 철저히 고독에 처해진 지긋지긋한 경험이었다."], ["And his was that worst loneliness which would shrink from sympathy.", "게다가 그의 고독은 동정으로부터 움츠러드는 그 최악의 고독이었다. 그는 도러시아가 그를 세상이 그녀의 성공한 구혼자에게 기대할 만한 것에 못지않게 행복하다고 여겨 주기를 바라지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He could not but wish that Dorothea should think him not less happy than the world would expect her successful suitor to be; and in relation to his authorship he leaned on her young trust and veneration, he liked to draw forth her fresh interest in listening, as a means of encouragement to himself: in talking to her he presented all his performance and intention with the reflected confidence of the pedagogue, and rid himself for the time of that chilling ideal audience which crowded his laborious uncreative hours with the vaporous pressure of Tartarean shades.", "그리고 자신의 저술과 관련하여 그는 그녀의 젊은 신뢰와 흠모에 기댔으며, 자기 자신을 격려하는 수단으로서 그녀가 귀 기울여 듣는 신선한 관심을 끌어내기를 좋아했다. 그녀에게 이야기할 때면 그는 자신의 모든 성과와 의도를 교사다운 반사된 자신감으로 펼쳐 보였고, 그 힘겹고 비창조적인 시간들을 타르타로스의 망령들이 풍기는 안개 같은 압박으로 가득 채우던 그 냉랭한 상상 속의 청중으로부터 그 동안만은 벗어났다."]], [["For to Dorothea, after that toy-box history of the world adapted to young ladies which had made the chief part of her education, Mr.", "도러시아에게는, 그녀가 받은 교육의 주된 부분을 이루었던, 어린 숙녀들에게 맞춰진 그 장난감 상자 같은 세계사 이후로, 자신의 위대한 책에 관한 캐소본 씨의 이야기는 새로운 전망으로 가득했던 까닭이다."], ["Casaubon’s talk about his great book was full of new vistas; and this sense of revelation, this surprise of a nearer introduction to Stoics and Alexandrians, as people who had ideas not totally unlike her own, kept in abeyance for the time her usual eagerness for a binding theory which could bring her own life and doctrine into strict connection with that amazing past, and give the remotest sources of knowledge some bearing on her actions. That more complete teaching would come—Mr.", "그리고 이 계시의 감각, 스토아학파와 알렉산드리아 학자들을—자기 자신의 생각과 전혀 다르지 않은 사상을 지녔던 사람들로서—한층 가까이 소개받는 이 놀라움은, 자신의 삶과 신조를 그 경이로운 과거와 엄밀히 연결 짓고 가장 먼 지식의 원천에 자신의 행위와 관련된 의미를 부여해 줄, 어떤 구속력 있는 이론에 대한 그녀의 평소 열망을 한동안 보류시켰다."], ["Casaubon would tell her all that: she was looking forward to higher initiation in ideas, as she was looking forward to marriage, and blending her dim conceptions of both.", "그 더 완전한 가르침은 다가올 것이고, 캐소본 씨가 그녀에게 그 모든 것을 일러 주리라. 그녀는 결혼을 고대하듯 사상의 더 높은 입문을 고대했으며, 그 둘에 대한 어렴풋한 관념을 한데 뒤섞고 있었다."], ["It would be a great mistake to suppose that Dorothea would have cared about any share in Mr.", "도러시아가 캐소본 씨의 학식에 어떤 식으로든 참여하는 것을 한낱 교양으로서 마음에 두었으리라고 추측한다면 그것은 큰 잘못이리라."], ["Casaubon’s learning as mere accomplishment; for though opinion in the neighborhood of Freshitt and Tipton had pronounced her clever, that epithet would not have described her to circles in whose more precise vocabulary cleverness implies mere aptitude for knowing and doing, apart from character.", "비록 프레시트와 팁턴 인근의 여론이 그녀를 영리하다고 평했지만, 그 형용사는 영리함이 인격과는 별개로 단지 알고 행하는 적성을 뜻하는, 더 정밀한 어휘를 쓰는 부류의 사람들에게는 그녀를 제대로 묘사하지 못했을 것이다."], ["All her eagerness for acquirement lay within that full current of sympathetic motive in which her ideas and impulses were habitually swept along.", "무언가를 습득하려는 그녀의 모든 열망은, 그녀의 사상과 충동이 습관적으로 휩쓸려 흐르던 공감적 동기의 그 충만한 흐름 안에 있었다."], ["She did not want to deck herself with knowledge—to wear it loose from the nerves and blood that fed her action; and if she had written a book she must have done it as Saint Theresa did, under the command of an authority that constrained her conscience.", "그녀는 지식으로 자신을 치장하기를—자기 행위를 먹여 살리는 신경과 피로부터 떼어 낸 채 그것을 헐겁게 걸치기를—원하지 않았다. 만일 그녀가 책을 썼다면, 그녀는 성녀 테레사가 했던 것처럼, 자기 양심을 강제하는 권위의 명령 아래에서 썼을 것임에 틀림없다."], ["But something she yearned for by which her life might be filled with action at once rational and ardent; and since the time was gone by for guiding visions and spiritual directors, since prayer heightened yearning but not instruction, what lamp was there but knowledge?", "그러나 그녀가 갈망한 것은, 그것으로써 자기 삶이 이성적이면서도 동시에 열렬한 행위로 채워질 무언가였다. 그리고 인도하는 환상과 영적 지도자의 시대는 지나갔고, 기도는 갈망은 드높이되 가르침을 주지는 않았으니, 지식 말고 무슨 등불이 있었겠는가?"], ["Surely learned men kept the only oil; and who more learned than Mr. Casaubon?", "분명 학식 있는 사람들이 유일한 기름을 간직하고 있었으며, 캐소본 씨보다 더 학식 있는 자가 누구이겠는가?"]], [["Thus in these brief weeks Dorothea’s joyous grateful expectation was unbroken, and however her lover might occasionally be conscious of flatness, he could never refer it to any slackening of her affectionate interest.", "이렇듯 이 짧은 몇 주 동안 도러시아의 기쁘고 감사에 찬 기대는 흐트러지지 않았으며, 그녀의 연인이 이따금 무미건조함을 의식했을지언정, 그는 결코 그것을 그녀의 애정 어린 관심이 식은 탓으로 돌릴 수가 없었다."]], [["The season was mild enough to encourage the project of extending the wedding journey as far as Rome, and Mr. Casaubon was anxious for this because he wished to inspect some manuscripts in the Vatican.", "계절이 신혼여행을 로마까지 늘리려는 계획을 부추길 만큼 온화했으며, 캐소본 씨는 바티칸의 어떤 필사본들을 살펴보고 싶었기에 이 일을 간절히 바랐다."]], [["“I still regret that your sister is not to accompany us,”", "\"자네 동생이 우리와 동행하지 않게 된 것이 여전히 유감이오.\""], ["he said one morning, some time after it had been ascertained that Celia objected to go, and that Dorothea did not wish for her companionship.", "어느 날 아침, 실리아가 가기를 꺼린다는 것과 도러시아도 그녀의 동행을 원하지 않는다는 것이 확인되고 얼마쯤 지난 뒤에 그가 말했다."], ["“You will have many lonely hours, Dorothea, for I shall be constrained to make the utmost use of my time during our stay in Rome, and I should feel more at liberty if you had a companion.”", "\"당신은 외로운 시간을 많이 보내게 될 거요, 도러시아. 로마에 머무는 동안 나는 부득이 내 시간을 최대한 활용해야 할 테니, 당신에게 말동무가 있다면 내가 한결 홀가분할 것 같소.\""]], [["The words “I should feel more at liberty” grated on Dorothea.", "\"내가 한결 홀가분할 것 같소\"라는 말이 도러시아의 마음에 거슬렸다."], ["For the first time in speaking to Mr. Casaubon she colored from annoyance.", "캐소본 씨에게 말할 때 그녀가 언짢음으로 얼굴을 붉힌 것은 그때가 처음이었다."]], [["“You must have misunderstood me very much,”", "\"당신은 나를 몹시 오해하셨나 봐요.\""], ["she said, “if you think I should not enter into the value of your time—if you think that I should not willingly give up whatever interfered with your using it to the best purpose.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"내가 당신 시간의 가치를 헤아리지 못하리라고 생각하신다면—당신이 그 시간을 가장 알맞게 쓰는 데 방해가 되는 것이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 포기하지 않으리라고 생각하신다면 말이에요.\""]], [["“That is very amiable in you, my dear Dorothea,” said Mr.", "\"그건 참으로 갸륵한 마음이오, 사랑하는 도러시아.\""], ["Casaubon, not in the least noticing that she was hurt; “but if you had a lady as your companion, I could put you both under the care of a cicerone, and we could thus achieve two purposes in the same space of time.”", "캐소본 씨는 그녀가 상처받았다는 것을 조금도 알아차리지 못한 채 말했다. \"하지만 당신에게 숙녀 말동무가 있다면, 내가 두 사람을 안내인에게 맡길 수 있을 테고, 그러면 우리가 같은 시간 안에 두 가지 목적을 이룰 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“I beg you will not refer to this again,”", "\"제발 이 일을 다시는 입에 올리지 말아 주세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, rather haughtily.", "도러시아가 다소 도도하게 말했다."], ["But immediately she feared that she was wrong, and turning towards him she laid her hand on his, adding in a different tone, “Pray do not be anxious about me.", "그러나 곧바로 그녀는 자신이 잘못했을까 봐 두려워졌고, 그를 향해 돌아서서 그의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹으며 다른 어조로 덧붙였다. \"제발 저 때문에 염려하지 마세요."], ["I shall have so much to think of when I am alone.", "혼자 있을 때 생각할 것이 무척 많을 거예요."], ["And Tantripp will be a sufficient companion, just to take care of me.", "그리고 그저 저를 돌봐 줄 사람으로는 탠트립이면 충분한 말동무가 될 거예요."], ["I could not bear to have Celia: she would be miserable.”", "실리아를 데려가는 건 견딜 수가 없어요. 그 애는 비참해할 테니까요.\""]], [["It was time to dress.", "옷을 갈아입을 시간이었다."], ["There was to be a dinner-party that day, the last of the parties which were held at the Grange as proper preliminaries to the wedding, and Dorothea was glad of a reason for moving away at once on the sound of the bell, as if she needed more than her usual amount of preparation.", "그날은 만찬회가 열릴 예정이었는데, 그것은 결혼에 앞선 의례로서 그레인지에서 열린 만찬회들 중 마지막 것이었으며, 도러시아는 종소리가 울리자 즉시 자리를 뜰 구실이 생긴 것이, 마치 자신이 평소보다 더 많은 채비가 필요한 것처럼 보일 수 있어 다행스러웠다."], ["She was ashamed of being irritated from some cause she could not define even to herself; for though she had no intention to be untruthful, her reply had not touched the real hurt within her.", "그녀는 자기 자신에게조차 규명할 수 없는 어떤 이유로 짜증이 난 것이 부끄러웠다. 거짓말을 할 생각은 없었지만, 그녀의 대답은 자기 안의 진짜 상처를 건드리지 못했던 것이다."], ["Mr. Casaubon’s words had been quite reasonable, yet they had brought a vague instantaneous sense of aloofness on his part.", "캐소본 씨의 말은 지극히 타당했으나, 그럼에도 그것은 그의 편에서 비롯된 어떤 막연하고 순간적인 거리감을 가져다주었다."]], [["“Surely I am in a strangely selfish weak state of mind,”", "\"분명 나는 이상하리만치 이기적이고 나약한 마음 상태에 빠져 있어.\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“How can I have a husband who is so much above me without knowing that he needs me less than I need him?”", "\"나보다 그토록 뛰어난 남편을 맞이하면서, 그가 내가 그를 필요로 하는 것보다 나를 덜 필요로 한다는 사실을 모를 수가 있겠는가?\""]], [["Having convinced herself that Mr. Casaubon was altogether right, she recovered her equanimity, and was an agreeable image of serene dignity when she came into the drawing-room in her silver-gray dress—the simple lines of her dark-brown hair parted over her brow and coiled massively behind, in keeping with the entire absence from her manner and expression of all search after mere effect.", "캐소본 씨가 전적으로 옳다고 스스로 납득하고 나자, 그녀는 평정을 되찾았고, 은회색 드레스를 입고 응접실로 들어설 때 고요한 기품의 흐뭇한 모습을 띠고 있었다—이마 위로 가르마를 타고 뒤로 묵직하게 틀어 올린 짙은 갈색 머리의 단순한 선은, 한낱 효과만을 좇는 기색이 그녀의 태도와 표정에서 전혀 보이지 않는 것과 잘 어울렸다."], ["Sometimes when Dorothea was in company, there seemed to be as complete an air of repose about her as if she had been a picture of Santa Barbara looking out from her tower into the clear air; but these intervals of quietude made the energy of her speech and emotion the more remarked when some outward appeal had touched her.", "때때로 도러시아가 사람들과 함께 있을 때면, 마치 탑에서 맑은 대기를 내다보는 성녀 바르바라의 그림인 양 그녀를 감도는 평온의 기운이 완연한 것 같았다. 그러나 이런 고요의 막간들은, 어떤 외적인 호소가 그녀를 건드렸을 때 그녀의 말과 감정의 활력을 한층 더 두드러지게 했다."]], [["She was naturally the subject of many observations this evening, for the dinner-party was large and rather more miscellaneous as to the male portion than any which had been held at the Grange since Mr.", "이날 저녁 그녀는 자연히 많은 관찰의 대상이 되었으니, 만찬회가 규모도 컸을뿐더러 남성 쪽으로 보자면 브룩 씨의 조카딸들이 그와 함께 거주하게 된 이래로 그레인지에서 열린 어느 만찬회보다도 더 잡다한 편이어서, 대화가 다소 어울리지 않는 두세 사람씩의 무리로 이루어진 까닭이다."], ["Brooke’s nieces had resided with him, so that the talking was done in duos and trios more or less inharmonious.", "그곳에는 새로 선출된 미들마치 시장이 있었는데, 공교롭게도 제조업자였다."], ["There was the newly elected mayor of Middlemarch, who happened to be a manufacturer; the philanthropic banker his brother-in-law, who predominated so much in the town that some called him a Methodist, others a hypocrite, according to the resources of their vocabulary; and there were various professional men.", "그의 처남인 자선가 은행가도 있었는데, 그는 그 읍내에서 어찌나 위세를 떨쳤는지 어휘력이 닿는 대로 어떤 이들은 그를 감리교도라 불렀고 어떤 이들은 위선자라 불렀다. 또한 여러 전문직 인사들도 있었다."], ["In fact, Mrs. Cadwallader said that Brooke was beginning to treat the Middlemarchers, and that she preferred the farmers at the tithe-dinner, who drank her health unpretentiously, and were not ashamed of their grandfathers’ furniture.", "사실 캐드월래더 부인은 브룩이 미들마치 사람들을 대접하기 시작했다고 말하면서, 자기는 십일조 만찬에 오는 농부들이 더 좋다고, 그들은 가식 없이 자기 건강을 위해 축배를 들고 자기 할아버지 대의 가구를 부끄러워하지 않는다고 했다."], ["For in that part of the country, before reform had done its notable part in developing the political consciousness, there was a clearer distinction of ranks and a dimmer distinction of parties; so that Mr. Brooke’s miscellaneous invitations seemed to belong to that general laxity which came from his inordinate travel and habit of taking too much in the form of ideas.", "그 지방에서는 개혁이 정치적 의식을 발달시키는 그 주목할 만한 역할을 하기 전까지는, 신분의 구별은 더 뚜렷하고 당파의 구별은 더 흐릿했던 까닭에, 브룩 씨의 잡다한 초대는 그의 지나친 여행과 사상이라는 형태로 너무 많은 것을 받아들이는 습관에서 비롯된 그 전반적인 헐거움에 속하는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["Already, as Miss Brooke passed out of the dining-room, opportunity was found for some interjectional “asides.”", "이미, 미스 브룩이 식당을 나서자, 짤막한 \"방백(傍白)\"을 주고받을 기회가 생겼다."]], [["“A fine woman, Miss Brooke!", "\"훌륭한 여인이오, 미스 브룩은!"], ["an uncommonly fine woman, by God!” said Mr.", "보기 드물게 훌륭한 여인이지, 정말이지!\""], ["Standish, the old lawyer, who had been so long concerned with the landed gentry that he had become landed himself, and used that oath in a deep-mouthed manner as a sort of armorial bearings, stamping the speech of a man who held a good position.", "늙은 변호사 스탠디시 씨가 말했는데, 그는 워낙 오랫동안 지주 계층과 관계를 맺어 와서 자신도 지주가 되어 버린 사람이었고, 그 욕설을 묵직한 어조로 일종의 문장(紋章)처럼 내뱉어, 좋은 지위에 있는 사람의 말투에 도장을 찍었다."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode, the banker, seemed to be addressed, but that gentleman disliked coarseness and profanity, and merely bowed.", "은행가 벌스트로드 씨를 향한 말 같았으나, 그 신사는 상스러움과 불경함을 싫어하여 그저 고개만 숙였다."], ["The remark was taken up by Mr. Chichely, a middle-aged bachelor and coursing celebrity, who had a complexion something like an Easter egg, a few hairs carefully arranged, and a carriage implying the consciousness of a distinguished appearance.", "그 말을 받은 것은 치철리 씨로, 그는 중년의 독신남이자 토끼 사냥으로 이름난 인물이었으며, 부활절 달걀 같은 안색에, 몇 가닥 안 되는 머리카락을 정성스레 매만지고, 자신이 빼어난 외모를 지녔음을 의식한 태도를 풍기는 사람이었다."]], [["“Yes, but not my style of woman: I like a woman who lays herself out a little more to please us.", "\"그래요, 하지만 내 취향의 여자는 아니오. 나는 우리를 즐겁게 해 주려고 좀 더 애쓰는 여자가 좋단 말이오."], ["There should be a little filigree about a woman—something of the coquette.", "여자에게는 좀 섬세한 장식 같은 게 있어야 하지—교태 같은 거 말이오."], ["A man likes a sort of challenge.", "남자는 일종의 도전을 좋아하니까."], ["The more of a dead set she makes at you the better.”", "여자가 사내를 노골적으로 노릴수록 더 좋은 거요.\""]], [["“There’s some truth in that,”", "\"거기엔 일리가 있군.\""], ["said Mr. Standish, disposed to be genial.", "상냥하게 굴 마음이 든 스탠디시 씨가 말했다."], ["“And, by God, it’s usually the way with them.", "\"그리고, 정말이지, 여자들이란 대개 그렇지."], ["I suppose it answers some wise ends: Providence made them so, eh, Bulstrode?”", "그게 무슨 현명한 목적에 들어맞는 모양이오. 섭리가 여자들을 그렇게 만든 거지, 안 그렇소, 벌스트로드?\""]], [["“I should be disposed to refer coquetry to another source,”", "\"나라면 교태를 다른 원천으로 돌리고 싶군요.\""], ["said Mr. Bulstrode.", "벌스트로드 씨가 말했다."], ["“I should rather refer it to the devil.”", "\"차라리 그것을 악마에게로 돌리겠소.\""]], [["“Ay, to be sure, there should be a little devil in a woman,” said Mr.", "\"아, 그렇고말고, 여자에게는 약간의 악마성이 있어야지.\""], ["Chichely, whose study of the fair sex seemed to have been detrimental to his theology.", "여성에 대한 연구가 자신의 신학에 해로웠던 듯한 치철리 씨가 말했다."], ["“And I like them blond, with a certain gait, and a swan neck.", "\"그리고 나는 금발에, 어떤 걸음걸이를 갖추고, 백조 같은 목을 한 여자가 좋소."], ["Between ourselves, the mayor’s daughter is more to my taste than Miss Brooke or Miss Celia either.", "우리끼리 얘기지만, 시장의 딸이 미스 브룩이나 미스 실리아보다 내 취향에 더 맞아요."], ["If I were a marrying man I should choose Miss Vincy before either of them.”", "내가 결혼할 사람이라면, 그 둘 중 누구보다도 미스 빈시를 택하겠소.\""]], [["“Well, make up, make up,” said Mr.", "\"이런, 어서 다가서 보시오, 어서.\""], ["Standish, jocosely; “you see the middle-aged fellows carry the day.”", "스탠디시 씨가 익살스럽게 말했다. \"보다시피 중년 사내들이 이기는 법이니까.\""]], [["Mr.", "치철리 씨는 자못 의미심장하게 고개를 저었다."], ["Chichely shook his head with much meaning: he was not going to incur the certainty of being accepted by the woman he would choose.", "그는 자기가 택할 여자에게 틀림없이 받아들여질 위험을 무릅쓸 생각이 없었던 것이다."]], [["The Miss Vincy who had the honor of being Mr. Chichely’s ideal was of course not present; for Mr.", "치철리 씨의 이상이 되는 영광을 누린 그 미스 빈시는 물론 그 자리에 없었으니, 언제나 도를 넘는 것을 꺼리는 브룩 씨가, 공적인 자리가 아니라면 자기 조카딸들이 미들마치 제조업자의 딸과 만나게 하기를 택하지 않았을 것이기 때문이다."], ["Brooke, always objecting to go too far, would not have chosen that his nieces should meet the daughter of a Middlemarch manufacturer, unless it were on a public occasion. The feminine part of the company included none whom Lady Chettam or Mrs.", "모인 사람들 중 여성 쪽에는 체텀 부인이나 캐드월래더 부인이 트집 잡을 만한 이가 아무도 없었다."], ["Cadwallader could object to; for Mrs. Renfrew, the colonel’s widow, was not only unexceptionable in point of breeding, but also interesting on the ground of her complaint, which puzzled the doctors, and seemed clearly a case wherein the fulness of professional knowledge might need the supplement of quackery.", "대령의 미망인인 렌프루 부인은 가문으로 보아 나무랄 데 없을뿐더러, 의사들을 당혹케 하는 그녀의 병 때문에도 흥미로운 인물이었는데, 그 병은 분명 전문적 지식의 충만함이 돌팔이 치료의 보충을 필요로 할 법한 사례로 보였다."], ["Lady Chettam, who attributed her own remarkable health to home-made bitters united with constant medical attendance, entered with much exercise of the imagination into Mrs. Renfrew’s account of symptoms, and into the amazing futility in her case of all strengthening medicines.", "자기 자신의 놀라운 건강을 집에서 만든 쓴 약과 끊임없는 진료의 결합 덕분이라고 여기는 체텀 부인은, 렌프루 부인의 증상 설명에, 그리고 그녀의 경우 온갖 강장제가 놀랍도록 효과 없다는 이야기에 상상력을 한껏 발휘하며 빠져들었다."]], [["“Where can all the strength of those medicines go, my dear?” said the mild but stately dowager, turning to Mrs.", "\"그 약들의 그 모든 약효가 다 어디로 가는 걸까요, 부인?\""], ["Cadwallader reflectively, when Mrs. Renfrew’s attention was called away.", "온화하면서도 위엄 있는 노부인이, 렌프루 부인의 주의가 다른 데로 돌려졌을 때, 캐드월래더 부인을 향해 곰곰이 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["“It strengthens the disease,”", "\"병을 튼튼하게 만드는 거죠.\""], ["said the Rector’s wife, much too well-born not to be an amateur in medicine.", "의술의 아마추어가 되기에 충분할 만큼 좋은 집안에서 태어난 교구 목사 부인이 말했다. \"모든 것은 체질에 달려 있어요."], ["“Everything depends on the constitution: some people make fat, some blood, and some bile—that’s my view of the matter; and whatever they take is a sort of grist to the mill.”", "어떤 사람은 살을 만들고, 어떤 사람은 피를 만들고, 또 어떤 사람은 담즙을 만들지요—제 견해로는 그래요. 그러니 그들이 무엇을 먹든 그건 일종의 방앗간에 들어가는 곡물 같은 거예요.\""]], [["“Then she ought to take medicines that would reduce—reduce the disease, you know, if you are right, my dear.", "\"그렇다면 그 부인은, 부인 말씀이 옳다면, 줄여 주는—병을 줄여 주는 약을 먹어야겠군요, 안 그래요."], ["And I think what you say is reasonable.”", "그리고 부인 말씀이 일리가 있다고 생각해요.\""]], [["“Certainly it is reasonable.", "\"분명 일리가 있지요."], ["You have two sorts of potatoes, fed on the same soil.", "같은 흙에서 기른 감자가 두 종류 있다고 쳐요."], ["One of them grows more and more watery—”", "그중 하나는 점점 더 물기가 많아지고—\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["like this poor Mrs. Renfrew—that is what I think.", "이 가엾은 렌프루 부인처럼 말이지요—제 생각도 그래요."], ["Dropsy!", "수종(水腫)이에요!"], ["There is no swelling yet—it is inward.", "아직 붓지는 않았지만—속으로 그런 거지요."], ["I should say she ought to take drying medicines, shouldn’t you?—or a dry hot-air bath.", "제 생각엔 말리는 약을 먹어야 할 것 같아요, 안 그래요?—아니면 건조한 열풍 목욕이라든가."], ["Many things might be tried, of a drying nature.”", "말리는 성질의 것이라면 여러 가지를 시도해 볼 만하겠지요.\""]], [["“Let her try a certain person’s pamphlets,” said Mrs.", "\"어떤 분의 소책자들을 한번 읽어 보시라고 하지요.\""], ["Cadwallader in an undertone, seeing the gentlemen enter.", "신사들이 들어오는 것을 보며 캐드월래더 부인이 나직이 말했다."], ["“He does not want drying.”", "\"그분은 말릴 필요가 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Who, my dear?”", "\"누구 말씀이에요, 부인?\""], ["said Lady Chettam, a charming woman, not so quick as to nullify the pleasure of explanation.", "매력적인 여성이지만, 설명하는 즐거움을 앗아 갈 만큼 눈치가 빠르지는 않은 체텀 부인이 말했다."]], [["“The bridegroom—Casaubon.", "\"신랑요—캐소본 말이에요."], ["He has certainly been drying up faster since the engagement: the flame of passion, I suppose.”", "그분은 약혼 이래로 확실히 더 빨리 말라 가고 있어요. 정열의 불꽃 탓이겠지요.\""]], [["“I should think he is far from having a good constitution,”", "\"그분은 결코 좋은 체질을 가졌다고 할 수 없을 것 같아요.\""], ["said Lady Chettam, with a still deeper undertone.", "체텀 부인이 한층 더 나직한 어조로 말했다."], ["“And then his studies—so very dry, as you say.”", "\"게다가 그 연구라는 것도—부인 말씀처럼, 어찌나 메마른지.\""]], [["“Really, by the side of Sir James, he looks like a death’s head skinned over for the occasion.", "\"정말이지, 제임스 경 곁에 서 있으면 그분은 마치 이 자리를 위해 가죽을 씌워 놓은 해골 같아 보여요."], ["Mark my words: in a year from this time that girl will hate him.", "제 말을 새겨 두세요. 지금부터 일 년 뒤면 저 아가씨는 그분을 미워하게 될 거예요."], ["She looks up to him as an oracle now, and by-and-by she will be at the other extreme.", "지금은 그분을 신탁(神託)인 양 우러러보지만, 머지않아 정반대 극단으로 치닫게 될 거예요."], ["All flightiness!”", "죄다 변덕이지요!\""]], [["“How very shocking!", "\"어쩜 그렇게 충격적인 말씀을!"], ["I fear she is headstrong.", "그 애가 고집이 세지 않을까 걱정이에요."], ["But tell me—you know all about him—is there anything very bad?", "하지만 말씀해 보세요—부인은 그분에 대해 다 아시잖아요—뭔가 아주 나쁜 점이라도 있나요?"], ["What is the truth?”", "진실이 뭐예요?\""]], [["“The truth?", "\"진실이요?"], ["he is as bad as the wrong physic—nasty to take, and sure to disagree.”", "그분은 잘못 처방된 약만큼이나 나쁜 사람이에요—먹기에 역겹고, 틀림없이 몸에 안 맞지요.\""]], [["“There could not be anything worse than that,”", "\"그보다 더 나쁜 건 없겠군요.\""], ["said Lady Chettam, with so vivid a conception of the physic that she seemed to have learned something exact about Mr.", "체텀 부인이, 그 약에 대해 어찌나 생생하게 떠올렸던지 캐소본 씨의 단점에 관해 무언가 정확한 것을 알게 된 양 말했다."], ["Casaubon’s disadvantages. “However, James will hear nothing against Miss Brooke.", "\"하지만 제임스는 미스 브룩에 대해 험담은 한마디도 들으려 하지 않아요."], ["He says she is the mirror of women still.”", "여전히 그 애가 여성의 귀감이라고 한답니다.\""]], [["“That is a generous make-believe of his.", "\"그건 그 사람의 너그러운 자기기만이지요."], ["Depend upon it, he likes little Celia better, and she appreciates him.", "틀림없이 그 사람은 어린 실리아를 더 좋아하고, 그 애는 그를 알아본답니다."], ["I hope you like my little Celia?”", "제 어린 실리아를 마음에 들어 하시기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Certainly; she is fonder of geraniums, and seems more docile, though not so fine a figure.", "\"물론이지요. 그 애는 제라늄을 더 좋아하고, 더 온순해 보여요."], ["But we were talking of physic.", "비록 그만큼 빼어난 자태는 아니지만요. 그런데 우리가 약 얘기를 하고 있었지요."], ["Tell me about this new young surgeon, Mr. Lydgate.", "이 새로 온 젊은 외과의 리드게이트 씨에 대해 말씀해 주세요."], ["I am told he is wonderfully clever: he certainly looks it—a fine brow indeed.”", "놀랄 만큼 영리하다고들 하던데요. 확실히 그렇게 보여요—참으로 훌륭한 이마예요.\""]], [["“He is a gentleman.", "\"신사예요."], ["I heard him talking to Humphrey.", "그분이 험프리와 얘기하는 걸 들었어요."], ["He talks well.”", "말을 잘하더군요.\""]], [["“Yes. Mr.", "\"그래요."], ["Brooke says he is one of the Lydgates of Northumberland, really well connected.", "브룩 씨 말씀이 그분은 노섬벌랜드의 리드게이트 가문 사람으로, 정말 좋은 집안과 연결되어 있다더군요."], ["One does not expect it in a practitioner of that kind.", "그런 부류의 개업의에게서는 기대하기 어려운 일이지요."], ["For my own part, I like a medical man more on a footing with the servants; they are often all the cleverer.", "저로 말하자면, 하인들과 좀 더 비슷한 처지의 의사가 좋아요. 그런 이들이 흔히 오히려 더 영리하거든요."], ["I assure you I found poor Hicks’s judgment unfailing; I never knew him wrong.", "정말이지, 저는 가엾은 힉스의 판단이 틀림이 없다는 걸 알았어요. 그가 틀리는 걸 본 적이 없답니다."], ["He was coarse and butcher-like, but he knew my constitution.", "그는 거칠고 푸주한 같았지만, 제 체질을 잘 알았어요."], ["It was a loss to me his going off so suddenly.", "그가 그토록 갑작스레 세상을 떠난 건 제겐 손실이었지요."], ["Dear me, what a very animated conversation Miss Brooke seems to be having with this Mr. Lydgate!”", "어머나, 미스 브룩이 이 리드게이트 씨와 어찌나 활기차게 대화를 나누는지 좀 보세요!\""]], [["“She is talking cottages and hospitals with him,” said Mrs.", "\"그분과 오두막이며 병원 얘기를 하고 있는 거예요.\""], ["Cadwallader, whose ears and power of interpretation were quick.", "귀와 해석하는 능력이 빠른 캐드월래더 부인이 말했다."], ["“I believe he is a sort of philanthropist, so Brooke is sure to take him up.”", "\"내 생각엔 그분이 일종의 자선가인 모양인데, 그러니 브룩이 틀림없이 그를 후원하려 들 거예요.\""]], [["“James,”", "\"제임스.\""], ["said Lady Chettam when her son came near, “bring Mr.", "아들이 가까이 오자 체텀 부인이 말했다."], ["Lydgate and introduce him to me.", "\"리드게이트 씨를 데려와 내게 소개해 다오."], ["I want to test him.”", "그 사람을 시험해 보고 싶구나.\""]], [["The affable dowager declared herself delighted with this opportunity of making Mr. Lydgate’s acquaintance, having heard of his success in treating fever on a new plan.", "붙임성 좋은 노부인은 리드게이트 씨가 새로운 방식으로 열병을 치료해 성공을 거두었다는 이야기를 들은 터라, 그와 알게 될 이 기회가 무척 기쁘다고 밝혔다."]], [["Mr. Lydgate had the medical accomplishment of looking perfectly grave whatever nonsense was talked to him, and his dark steady eyes gave him impressiveness as a listener.", "리드게이트 씨는 자신에게 어떤 허튼소리를 늘어놓든 완벽하게 진지한 표정을 짓는 의료인다운 소양을 갖추고 있었고, 그의 검고 흔들림 없는 눈은 그를 인상적인 경청자로 만들어 주었다."], ["He was as little as possible like the lamented Hicks, especially in a certain careless refinement about his toilet and utterance.", "그는 애석한 힉스와는 가능한 한 닮은 데가 없었으니, 특히 몸단장과 말투에 어린 어떤 무심한 세련됨에서 그러했다."], ["Yet Lady Chettam gathered much confidence in him.", "그럼에도 체텀 부인은 그에게서 큰 신뢰를 느꼈다."], ["He confirmed her view of her own constitution as being peculiar, by admitting that all constitutions might be called peculiar, and he did not deny that hers might be more peculiar than others.", "그는 모든 체질이 특이하다고 불릴 수 있다고 인정함으로써 그녀 자신의 체질이 특이하다는 그녀의 견해를 확인해 주었으며, 그녀의 체질이 다른 이들보다 더 특이할지도 모른다는 것을 부인하지 않았다."], ["He did not approve of a too lowering system, including reckless cupping, nor, on the other hand, of incessant port wine and bark.", "그는 무모한 부항을 포함한 지나치게 기력을 떨어뜨리는 요법도, 다른 한편으로 끊임없는 포트와인과 기나피(幾那皮)도 찬성하지 않았다."], ["He said “I think so” with an air of so much deference accompanying the insight of agreement, that she formed the most cordial opinion of his talents.", "그는 \"저도 그렇게 생각합니다\"라고, 동의라는 통찰에 곁들여 깍듯한 공손함을 어찌나 풍기며 말했던지, 그녀는 그의 재능에 대해 더없이 호의적인 견해를 갖게 되었다."]], [["“I am quite pleased with your protege,”", "\"당신의 보호 대상에게 아주 만족하고 있어요.\""], ["she said to Mr. Brooke before going away.", "그녀가 떠나기 전에 브룩 씨에게 말했다."]], [["“My protege?—dear me!—who is that?”", "\"내 보호 대상이라니?—이런!—그게 누구요?\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."]], [["“This young Lydgate, the new doctor.", "\"이 젊은 리드게이트, 새로 온 의사 말이에요."], ["He seems to me to understand his profession admirably.”", "제가 보기에 그 사람은 자기 직업을 훌륭히 이해하고 있는 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Oh, Lydgate!", "\"오, 리드게이트!"], ["he is not my protege, you know; only I knew an uncle of his who sent me a letter about him.", "그는 내 보호 대상이 아니오, 알다시피. 다만 그에 관해 내게 편지를 보낸 그의 삼촌을 내가 알았을 뿐이지."], ["However, I think he is likely to be first-rate—has studied in Paris, knew Broussais; has ideas, you know—wants to raise the profession.”", "그래도 그는 일류가 될 가능성이 있을 것 같소—파리에서 공부했고, 브루세를 알았다지. 사상이 있단 말이오, 알다시피—이 직업의 수준을 끌어올리려 하지.\""]], [["“Lydgate has lots of ideas, quite new, about ventilation and diet, that sort of thing,”", "\"리드게이트는 환기며 식이며, 뭐 그런 것들에 관해 아주 새로운 사상을 잔뜩 갖고 있소.\""], ["resumed Mr. Brooke, after he had handed out Lady Chettam, and had returned to be civil to a group of Middlemarchers.", "브룩 씨가 체텀 부인을 배웅하고 나서 한 무리의 미들마치 사람들에게 예의를 갖추려고 돌아온 뒤에 말을 이었다."]], [["“Hang it, do you think that is quite sound?—upsetting the old treatment, which has made Englishmen what they are?”", "\"제기랄, 그게 과연 온당하다고 보시오?—잉글랜드 사람들을 지금의 그들로 만들어 준 그 옛 치료법을 뒤엎는 게 말이오?\""], ["said Mr. Standish.", "스탠디시 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Medical knowledge is at a low ebb among us,” said Mr.", "\"의학 지식은 우리 사이에서 바닥을 치고 있소이다.\""], ["Bulstrode, who spoke in a subdued tone, and had rather a sickly air.", "가라앉은 어조로 말하는, 다소 병약한 기색의 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다."], ["“I, for my part, hail the advent of Mr. Lydgate.", "\"나로 말하자면, 리드게이트 씨의 출현을 환영하오."], ["I hope to find good reason for confiding the new hospital to his management.”", "그에게 새 병원의 운영을 맡길 만한 좋은 이유를 찾게 되기를 바라오.\""]], [["“That is all very fine,”", "\"그것 참 그럴듯한 말이오만.\""], ["replied Mr. Standish, who was not fond of Mr.", "벌스트로드 씨를 좋아하지 않는 스탠디시 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Bulstrode; “if you like him to try experiments on your hospital patients, and kill a few people for charity I have no objection.", "\"당신이 그더러 당신 병원 환자들을 상대로 실험을 해 보게 하고, 자선이랍시고 몇 사람 죽이는 거야 나는 이의 없소."], ["But I am not going to hand money out of my purse to have experiments tried on me.", "하지만 나는 내 돈을 지갑에서 꺼내 나를 상대로 실험하게 할 생각은 없소이다."], ["I like treatment that has been tested a little.”", "나는 어느 정도 검증된 치료가 좋단 말이오.\""]], [["“Well, you know, Standish, every dose you take is an experiment-an experiment, you know,”", "\"이보시오, 알다시피, 스탠디시, 당신이 먹는 약 한 알 한 알이 다 실험이오—실험이란 말이오, 알다시피.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, nodding towards the lawyer.", "브룩 씨가 변호사를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, if you talk in that sense!” said Mr.", "\"오, 그런 식으로 말씀하신다면야!\""], ["Standish, with as much disgust at such non-legal quibbling as a man can well betray towards a valuable client.", "스탠디시 씨가, 소중한 의뢰인에게 사람이 드러낼 수 있는 한껏, 그런 비법률적인 궤변에 대한 역겨움을 담아 말했다."]], [["“I should be glad of any treatment that would cure me without reducing me to a skeleton, like poor Grainger,” said Mr.", "\"가엾은 그레인저처럼 나를 해골로 만들어 놓지 않고 낫게 해 주는 치료라면 무엇이든 반갑겠소.\""], ["Vincy, the mayor, a florid man, who would have served for a study of flesh in striking contrast with the Franciscan tints of Mr. Bulstrode.", "시장인 빈시 씨가 말했는데, 그는 혈색 좋은 사람으로, 벌스트로드 씨의 프란체스코회 수도사 같은 빛깔과 두드러지게 대조되는 살집의 표본으로 쓸 만한 인물이었다."], ["“It’s an uncommonly dangerous thing to be left without any padding against the shafts of disease, as somebody said,—and I think it a very good expression myself.”", "\"누군가 말했듯이, 병의 화살에 맞서 아무런 완충도 없이 남겨진다는 건 유달리 위험한 일이오—나도 그게 아주 좋은 표현이라고 생각하오만.\""]], [["Mr. Lydgate, of course, was out of hearing.", "물론 리드게이트 씨는 그 말을 듣지 못했다."], ["He had quitted the party early, and would have thought it altogether tedious but for the novelty of certain introductions, especially the introduction to Miss Brooke, whose youthful bloom, with her approaching marriage to that faded scholar, and her interest in matters socially useful, gave her the piquancy of an unusual combination.", "그는 일찍이 그 모임을 떠났고, 몇몇 사람을 새로 소개받은 신선함이 아니었다면, 특히 미스 브룩을 소개받은 것이 아니었다면, 그 자리를 완전히 따분하게 여겼을 것이다. 그녀의 젊은 꽃다움은, 저 빛바랜 학자와의 임박한 결혼과 사회적으로 유용한 일에 대한 관심과 어우러져, 그녀에게 보기 드문 조합의 묘미를 부여했다."]], [["“She is a good creature—that fine girl—but a little too earnest,”", "'착한 사람이야—저 멋진 아가씨는—하지만 좀 지나치게 진지해.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“It is troublesome to talk to such women.", "'그런 여자들과 이야기하는 건 성가신 일이야."], ["They are always wanting reasons, yet they are too ignorant to understand the merits of any question, and usually fall back on their moral sense to settle things after their own taste.”", "그들은 늘 이유를 알고 싶어 하면서도, 정작 어떤 문제의 핵심을 이해하기엔 너무 무지해서, 결국 자기 입맛대로 일을 매듭짓느라 도덕 감각에 기대고 마니까.'"]], [["Evidently Miss Brooke was not Mr. Lydgate’s style of woman any more than Mr.", "명백히 미스 브룩은 치철리 씨에게나 마찬가지로 리드게이트 씨의 취향의 여자도 아니었다."], ["Chichely’s. Considered, indeed, in relation to the latter, whose mind was matured, she was altogether a mistake, and calculated to shock his trust in final causes, including the adaptation of fine young women to purplefaced bachelors.", "사실 정신이 무르익은 후자와의 관계에서 보자면, 그녀는 전적으로 하나의 실수였으며, 자줏빛 얼굴의 독신남에게 어울리도록 빚어진 멋진 젊은 여성을 비롯한 목적인(目的因)에 대한 그의 신뢰를 뒤흔들기에 알맞았다."], ["But Lydgate was less ripe, and might possibly have experience before him which would modify his opinion as to the most excellent things in woman.", "그러나 리드게이트는 덜 무르익은 사람이었기에, 어쩌면 그의 앞에는 여성에게서 가장 빼어난 것에 관한 그의 견해를 바꿔 놓을 경험이 놓여 있을지도 몰랐다."]], [["Miss Brooke, however, was not again seen by either of these gentlemen under her maiden name.", "그러나 미스 브룩은 이 두 신사 중 누구에게도 다시는 처녀 적 이름으로 보이지 않았다."], ["Not long after that dinner-party she had become Mrs. Casaubon, and was on her way to Rome.", "그 만찬회로부터 그리 오래지 않아 그녀는 캐소본 부인이 되었고, 로마로 가는 길에 올랐던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XI.", "# CHAPTER XI."]], [["But deeds and language such as men do use, And persons such as comedy would choose, When she would show an image of the times, And sport with human follies, not with crimes.", "죄악이 아닌 인간의 어리석음과 희롱하려 할 때."], ["—BEN JONSON.", "—벤 존슨."]], [["Lydgate, in fact, was already conscious of being fascinated by a woman strikingly different from Miss Brooke: he did not in the least suppose that he had lost his balance and fallen in love, but he had said of that particular woman, “She is grace itself; she is perfectly lovely and accomplished.", "리드게이트는 사실 브룩 양과는 현저히 다른 한 여인에게 이미 매혹되었음을 의식하고 있었다. 그는 자신이 평정을 잃고 사랑에 빠졌다고는 조금도 생각하지 않았지만, 그 특정한 여인에 대해 이렇게 말했다."], ["That is what a woman ought to be: she ought to produce the effect of exquisite music.”", "\"그녀는 우아함 그 자체다. 더없이 사랑스럽고 교양 있다."], ["Plain women he regarded as he did the other severe facts of life, to be faced with philosophy and investigated by science.", "여자란 마땅히 그래야 하는 법이다. 여자는 절묘한 음악 같은 효과를 자아내야 한다.\""], ["But Rosamond Vincy seemed to have the true melodic charm; and when a man has seen the woman whom he would have chosen if he had intended to marry speedily, his remaining a bachelor will usually depend on her resolution rather than on his.", "그는 평범하게 생긴 여자들을 인생의 다른 가혹한 사실들과 마찬가지로, 철학으로 마주하고 과학으로 탐구해야 할 대상으로 여겼다. 그러나 로저먼드 빈시는 진정한 선율의 매력을 지닌 듯 보였다."], ["Lydgate believed that he should not marry for several years: not marry until he had trodden out a good clear path for himself away from the broad road which was quite ready made.", "그리고 어떤 남자가 만약 서둘러 결혼할 작정이었다면 골랐을 법한 여인을 본 경우, 그가 계속 독신으로 남느냐는 대개 그 자신의 결심보다 그녀의 결심에 달려 있는 법이다. 리드게이트는 자신이 몇 해 동안은 결혼하지 않으리라 믿었다."], ["He had seen Miss Vincy above his horizon almost as long as it had taken Mr.", "이미 닦여 있는 널찍한 큰길을 벗어나 자기만의 또렷한 길을 다져 놓기 전에는 결혼하지 않으리라고."], ["Casaubon to become engaged and married: but this learned gentleman was possessed of a fortune; he had assembled his voluminous notes, and had made that sort of reputation which precedes performance,—often the larger part of a man’s fame.", "그가 빈시 양을 자신의 지평선 위로 바라본 기간은, 캐소본이 약혼하고 결혼하기까지 걸린 시간과 거의 맞먹었다. 하지만 그 학식 있는 신사는 재산을 지니고 있었고, 방대한 노트를 모아 두었으며, 업적에 앞서는 그런 종류의 명성을 쌓아 두었다."], ["He took a wife, as we have seen, to adorn the remaining quadrant of his course, and be a little moon that would cause hardly a calculable perturbation.", "그것은 흔히 한 인간이 누리는 명성의 대부분을 차지한다. 그는 우리가 본 대로, 자기 인생행로의 남은 사분면을 장식해 줄, 거의 계산할 가치도 없을 만큼 미미한 섭동을 일으킬 작은 달 하나로서 아내를 맞이했다."], ["But Lydgate was young, poor, ambitious.", "그러나 리드게이트는 젊고 가난하며 야심에 차 있었다."], ["He had his half-century before him instead of behind him, and he had come to Middlemarch bent on doing many things that were not directly fitted to make his fortune or even secure him a good income.", "그에게는 반세기의 세월이 등 뒤가 아니라 눈앞에 놓여 있었으니, 그는 자기 재산을 늘리거나 심지어 넉넉한 수입을 확보하는 데 직접 어울리지도 않는 수많은 일들을 해내겠다는 결심을 품고 미들마치로 왔던 것이다."], ["To a man under such circumstances, taking a wife is something more than a question of adornment, however highly he may rate this; and Lydgate was disposed to give it the first place among wifely functions.", "이런 처지에 놓인 남자에게 아내를 맞이하는 일은, 그가 아무리 장식의 가치를 높이 평가한다 해도, 단순한 장식의 문제 이상이다. 그런데도 리드게이트는 아내의 역할 중에서 그것을 첫째 자리에 두려는 쪽이었다."], ["To his taste, guided by a single conversation, here was the point on which Miss Brooke would be found wanting, notwithstanding her undeniable beauty.", "단 한 번의 대화로 다듬어진 그의 취향으로 보건대, 바로 이 점에서 브룩 양은 부정할 수 없는 미모에도 불구하고 부족함이 드러날 터였다."], ["She did not look at things from the proper feminine angle.", "그녀는 여성에게 마땅한 각도에서 사물을 바라보지 않았다."], ["The society of such women was about as relaxing as going from your work to teach the second form, instead of reclining in a paradise with sweet laughs for bird-notes, and blue eyes for a heaven.", "그런 여자들과 어울리는 것은, 새소리 같은 달콤한 웃음과 천국 같은 푸른 눈에 둘러싸여 낙원에 비스듬히 누워 쉬는 것이 아니라, 일터를 떠나 이학년 학생들을 가르치러 가는 것만큼이나 마음을 편안하게 풀어 주는 일이었다."]], [["Certainly nothing at present could seem much less important to Lydgate than the turn of Miss Brooke’s mind, or to Miss Brooke than the qualities of the woman who had attracted this young surgeon.", "분명 지금 이 순간 리드게이트에게 브룩 양의 마음의 성향만큼 사소해 보이는 것도 없었고, 브룩 양에게 이 젊은 외과 의사를 사로잡은 여인의 자질만큼 하찮아 보이는 것도 없었다."], ["But any one watching keenly the stealthy convergence of human lots, sees a slow preparation of effects from one life on another, which tells like a calculated irony on the indifference or the frozen stare with which we look at our unintroduced neighbor.", "하지만 인간의 운명이 은밀히 수렴해 가는 모습을 예리하게 지켜보는 사람이라면, 한 생애가 다른 생애에 미치는 영향이 더디게 준비되는 광경을 보게 된다. 그것은 마치 우리가 미처 소개받지 못한 이웃을 무관심하게, 혹은 얼어붙은 시선으로 바라볼 때 계산된 아이러니처럼 느껴지는 것이다."], ["Destiny stands by sarcastic with our dramatis personae folded in her hand.", "운명은 우리의 등장인물들을 손안에 접어 쥔 채, 비웃듯이 곁에 서 있다."]], [["Old provincial society had its share of this subtle movement: had not only its striking downfalls, its brilliant young professional dandies who ended by living up an entry with a drab and six children for their establishment, but also those less marked vicissitudes which are constantly shifting the boundaries of social intercourse, and begetting new consciousness of interdependence.", "이 오래된 지방 사회에도 그 미묘한 움직임의 몫이 있었다. 거기에는 눈에 띄는 몰락도 있었으니, 화려하던 젊은 전문직 멋쟁이가 결국 칙칙한 여자와 여섯 아이를 살림이랍시고 거느린 채 골목 단칸방에서 연명하는 신세로 끝나기도 했다."], ["Some slipped a little downward, some got higher footing: people denied aspirates, gained wealth, and fastidious gentlemen stood for boroughs; some were caught in political currents, some in ecclesiastical, and perhaps found themselves surprisingly grouped in consequence; while a few personages or families that stood with rocky firmness amid all this fluctuation, were slowly presenting new aspects in spite of solidity, and altering with the double change of self and beholder.", "그러나 그뿐 아니라, 사교의 경계를 끊임없이 옮겨 놓으며 상호 의존이라는 새로운 의식을 낳는, 그보다 덜 두드러진 부침도 있었다. 어떤 이는 약간 아래로 미끄러졌고, 어떤 이는 더 높은 발판을 얻었다."], ["Municipal town and rural parish gradually made fresh threads of connection—gradually, as the old stocking gave way to the savings-bank, and the worship of the solar guinea became extinct; while squires and baronets, and even lords who had once lived blamelessly afar from the civic mind, gathered the faultiness of closer acquaintanceship.", "어떤 이들은 무성음을 제대로 발음하지 못하면서도 부를 거머쥐었고, 까다로운 신사들은 자치 도시의 의원으로 출마했다. 어떤 이들은 정치의 물결에, 어떤 이들은 교회의 물결에 휩쓸렸고, 그 결과 의외의 무리로 분류된 자신을 발견하기도 했다."], ["Settlers, too, came from distant counties, some with an alarming novelty of skill, others with an offensive advantage in cunning.", "한편 이 모든 변동 속에서도 바위처럼 굳건히 버티고 선 몇몇 인물이나 가문은, 그 견고함에도 불구하고 서서히 새로운 면모를 드러냈으니, 자신과 보는 이가 함께 변하는 이중의 변화 속에서 달라져 갔다. 도시의 자치구와 농촌의 교구는 차츰 새로운 연결의 실을 자아냈다."], ["In fact, much the same sort of movement and mixture went on in old England as we find in older Herodotus, who also, in telling what had been, thought it well to take a woman’s lot for his starting-point; though Io, as a maiden apparently beguiled by attractive merchandise, was the reverse of Miss Brooke, and in this respect perhaps bore more resemblance to Rosamond Vincy, who had excellent taste in costume, with that nymph-like figure and pure blondness which give the largest range to choice in the flow and color of drapery.", "그것은 더디게 이루어졌으니, 옛날의 양말 속 비상금이 저축은행에 자리를 내주고, 황금빛 기니화를 숭배하던 풍습이 사라져 갈 무렵의 일이었다. 한편 한때 도시민의 의식과는 흠 잡힐 일 없이 멀리 떨어져 살던 향사와 준남작들, 심지어 영주들까지도 가까이 교류하면서 생기는 결점을 떠안게 되었다."], ["But these things made only part of her charm.", "게다가 먼 주에서 정착민들이 찾아왔으니, 어떤 이는 놀랄 만큼 새로운 기술을 지녔고, 어떤 이는 교활함이라는 거슬리는 이점을 지녔다. 사실 옛 잉글랜드에서도 더 오래된 헤로도토스에게서 발견되는 것과 거의 같은 종류의 움직임과 뒤섞임이 일어났던 것이다."], ["She was admitted to be the flower of Mrs.", "그 또한 지난 일을 이야기하면서 한 여인의 운명을 출발점으로 삼는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다."], ["Lemon’s school, the chief school in the county, where the teaching included all that was demanded in the accomplished female—even to extras, such as the getting in and out of a carriage. Mrs.", "다만 이오는 매력적인 상품에 홀린 듯한 처녀였으니 브룩 양과는 정반대였고, 이 점에서는 오히려 로저먼드 빈시와 더 닮았는지도 모른다."], ["Lemon herself had always held up Miss Vincy as an example: no pupil, she said, exceeded that young lady for mental acquisition and propriety of speech, while her musical execution was quite exceptional.", "로저먼드는 의상에 탁월한 안목을 지녔고, 휘감기는 천의 흐름과 빛깔을 가장 폭넓게 고를 수 있게 해 주는 그 요정 같은 몸매와 순결한 금발을 지니고 있었다. 그러나 이런 것들은 그녀가 지닌 매력의 일부에 지나지 않았다."], ["We cannot help the way in which people speak of us, and probably if Mrs.", "그녀는 그 주에서 으뜸가는 학교인 레먼 부인의 학교에서도 꽃으로 인정받았으니, 그곳의 교육은 교양 있는 여성에게 요구되는 모든 것을 망라했다. 마차에 오르내리는 법 같은 부수적인 항목까지도."], ["Lemon had undertaken to describe Juliet or Imogen, these heroines would not have seemed poetical.", "레먼 부인 자신은 늘 빈시 양을 모범으로 내세웠다. 그녀의 말로는, 지식의 습득과 말씨의 단정함에서 그 아가씨를 능가하는 학생은 없었으며, 음악 연주 솜씨는 그야말로 발군이었다."], ["The first vision of Rosamond would have been enough with most judges to dispel any prejudice excited by Mrs.", "사람들이 우리를 두고 어떻게 말하는지를 우리가 어찌할 수는 없는 노릇이다. 아마 레먼 부인이 줄리엣이나 이모진을 묘사하는 일을 맡았더라면, 그 여주인공들조차 시적으로 보이지는 않았으리라."], ["Lemon’s praise.", "로저먼드를 처음 본 광경이면 대부분의 판단자에게는 레먼 부인의 칭찬이 불러일으킨 어떤 편견도 말끔히 씻어 내기에 충분했을 것이다."]], [["Lydgate could not be long in Middlemarch without having that agreeable vision, or even without making the acquaintance of the Vincy family; for though Mr.", "리드게이트가 미들마치에 머무는 동안, 그 흐뭇한 광경을 보지 않고서는, 아니 심지어 빈시 가족과 알고 지내지 않고서는 오래 지낼 수가 없었다."], ["Peacock, whose practice he had paid something to enter on, had not been their doctor (Mrs. Vincy not liking the lowering system adopted by him), he had many patients among their connections and acquaintances.", "그가 얼마간 돈을 치르고 진료를 넘겨받은 피콕 씨가 빈시 가의 주치의는 아니었지만—빈시 부인이 그의 쇠약 요법을 좋아하지 않았던 까닭이었다—그래도 빈시 가의 친척과 지인 중에는 그의 환자가 많았다."], ["For who of any consequence in Middlemarch was not connected or at least acquainted with the Vincys?", "미들마치에서 웬만큼 비중 있는 사람치고 빈시 가와 인척이거나 적어도 알고 지내지 않는 이가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["They were old manufacturers, and had kept a good house for three generations, in which there had naturally been much intermarrying with neighbors more or less decidedly genteel. Mr.", "그들은 오래된 제조업자 집안으로 삼 대에 걸쳐 번듯한 살림을 꾸려 왔고, 그 살림 안에서는 자연히 다소 분명한 양반 행세를 하는 이웃들과 통혼하는 일이 잦았다."], ["Vincy’s sister had made a wealthy match in accepting Mr. Bulstrode, who, however, as a man not born in the town, and altogether of dimly known origin, was considered to have done well in uniting himself with a real Middlemarch family; on the other hand, Mr.", "빈시 씨의 누이는 불스트로드 씨를 받아들임으로써 부유한 혼처와 맺어졌는데, 다만 불스트로드 씨는 이 도시 태생이 아니고 출신도 도무지 알 수 없는 인물이었던 터라, 진짜 미들마치 가문과 결합한 것은 그로서는 잘한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["Vincy had descended a little, having taken an innkeeper’s daughter. But on this side too there was a cheering sense of money; for Mrs.", "반면 빈시 씨는 여관 주인의 딸을 아내로 맞았으니 신분이 조금 내려간 셈이었다."], ["Vincy’s sister had been second wife to rich old Mr.", "그러나 이쪽에도 흐뭇한 재물의 기운이 감돌았다."], ["Featherstone, and had died childless years ago, so that her nephews and nieces might be supposed to touch the affections of the widower. And it happened that Mr.", "빈시 부인의 언니가 부유한 노인 페더스톤 씨의 후처였다가 여러 해 전에 자식 없이 세상을 떠난 까닭에, 그 조카들이 홀아비가 된 노인의 애정을 건드릴 수 있으리라 여겨졌던 것이다."], ["Bulstrode and Mr. Featherstone, two of Peacock’s most important patients, had, from different causes, given an especially good reception to his successor, who had raised some partisanship as well as discussion.", "그런데 마침 피콕의 가장 중요한 환자 둘인 불스트로드 씨와 페더스톤 씨가 저마다 다른 이유로 그의 후임자를 유달리 호의적으로 맞아들였으니, 그 후임자는 논쟁뿐 아니라 어느 정도 편가르기까지 불러일으킨 인물이었다."], ["Mr. Wrench, medical attendant to the Vincy family, very early had grounds for thinking lightly of Lydgate’s professional discretion, and there was no report about him which was not retailed at the Vincys’, where visitors were frequent.", "빈시 가의 주치의인 렌치 씨는 일찌감치 리드게이트의 직업적 분별을 가볍게 여길 만한 근거를 얻었고, 손님이 잦은 빈시 가에서 그에 관한 소문치고 입에 오르내리지 않는 것이 없었다."], ["Mr. Vincy was more inclined to general good-fellowship than to taking sides, but there was no need for him to be hasty in making any new man acquaintance.", "빈시 씨는 어느 편을 드는 쪽보다 두루 사람 좋게 지내는 쪽으로 기우는 사람이었지만, 그렇다고 새 인물과 굳이 서둘러 사귈 필요도 없었다."], ["Rosamond silently wished that her father would invite Mr. Lydgate.", "로저먼드는 아버지가 리드게이트 씨를 초대해 주기를 속으로 바랐다."], ["She was tired of the faces and figures she had always been used to—the various irregular profiles and gaits and turns of phrase distinguishing those Middlemarch young men whom she had known as boys.", "그녀는 늘 보아 온 얼굴과 모습들에—소년 시절부터 알고 지낸 그 미들마치 청년들의 제각각 고르지 못한 옆모습이며 걸음걸이며 말버릇에 싫증이 나 있었다."], ["She had been at school with girls of higher position, whose brothers, she felt sure, it would have been possible for her to be more interested in, than in these inevitable Middlemarch companions.", "그녀는 학교에서 더 지체 높은 집안의 아가씨들과 함께 지냈는데, 그들의 오라비라면 이 어쩔 수 없이 가까운 미들마치 친구들보다 자신이 더 관심을 가질 수 있었으리라고 확신했다."], ["But she would not have chosen to mention her wish to her father; and he, for his part, was in no hurry on the subject.", "그러나 그런 바람을 아버지에게 입 밖에 내려고는 하지 않았고, 아버지는 아버지대로 그 문제에 전혀 서두르지 않았다."], ["An alderman about to be mayor must by-and-by enlarge his dinner-parties, but at present there were plenty of guests at his well-spread table.", "곧 시장이 될 시 참사회원이라면 머지않아 만찬 모임을 늘려야 할 테지만, 당장은 그의 푸짐한 식탁에 손님이 차고 넘쳤다."]], [["That table often remained covered with the relics of the family breakfast long after Mr. Vincy had gone with his second son to the warehouse, and when Miss Morgan was already far on in morning lessons with the younger girls in the schoolroom.", "그 식탁은 흔히 빈시 씨가 둘째 아들을 데리고 창고로 떠난 뒤에도, 그리고 모건 양이 교실에서 어린 딸들과 함께 오전 수업을 한참 진행하고 있을 때에도, 가족의 아침 식사 흔적으로 덮인 채 그대로 놓여 있었다."], ["It awaited the family laggard, who found any sort of inconvenience (to others) less disagreeable than getting up when he was called. This was the case one morning of the October in which we have lately seen Mr.", "식탁은 집안의 늦잠꾸러기를 기다리고 있었으니, 그는 부르는 소리에 일어나는 것보다는 (남들에게) 온갖 불편을 끼치는 편이 덜 불쾌하다고 여겼다."], ["Casaubon visiting the Grange; and though the room was a little overheated with the fire, which had sent the spaniel panting to a remote corner, Rosamond, for some reason, continued to sit at her embroidery longer than usual, now and then giving herself a little shake, and laying her work on her knee to contemplate it with an air of hesitating weariness.", "우리가 얼마 전 캐소본 씨가 그레인지를 방문하는 모습을 보았던 바로 그 시월의 어느 아침이 그러했다. 방 안은 난롯불로 약간 후끈하여 스패니얼이 헐떡이며 멀찍한 구석으로 물러갔는데도, 로저먼드는 무슨 까닭에서인지 평소보다 오래 자수 앞에 앉아 있었다."], ["Her mamma, who had returned from an excursion to the kitchen, sat on the other side of the small work-table with an air of more entire placidity, until, the clock again giving notice that it was going to strike, she looked up from the lace-mending which was occupying her plump fingers and rang the bell.", "이따금 몸을 가볍게 떨고는, 일감을 무릎에 내려놓고 망설이는 듯한 권태로운 기색으로 그것을 바라보았다. 부엌에 잠깐 다녀온 그녀의 어머니는 한결 온전히 평온한 기색으로 작은 작업 탁자 맞은편에 앉아 있다가, 시계가 다시 종을 칠 참이라고 알리자, 통통한 손가락으로 매만지던 레이스 수선에서 눈을 들어 종을 울렸다."]], [["“Knock at Mr. Fred’s door again, Pritchard, and tell him it has struck half-past ten.”", "\"프리처드, 프레드 도련님 방문을 다시 두드려서, 벌써 열 시 반이 쳤다고 일러 주게.\""]], [["This was said without any change in the radiant good-humor of Mrs.", "이 말은 빈시 부인의 환하게 좋은 기분이 어린 얼굴에 아무런 변화도 일으키지 않은 채 흘러나왔다."], ["Vincy’s face, in which forty-five years had delved neither angles nor parallels; and pushing back her pink capstrings, she let her work rest on her lap, while she looked admiringly at her daughter.", "그 얼굴에는 사십오 년 세월이 모난 주름이나 나란한 골을 새겨 놓지 않았다. 그녀는 분홍빛 모자 끈을 뒤로 밀어 넘기고 일감을 무릎에 내려놓은 채, 흐뭇한 눈길로 딸을 바라보았다."]], [["“Mamma,”", "\"엄마,\""], ["said Rosamond, “when Fred comes down I wish you would not let him have red herrings.", "로저먼드가 말했다. \"프레드가 내려오면 청어구이는 못 먹게 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요."], ["I cannot bear the smell of them all over the house at this hour of the morning.”", "아침 이 시각에 온 집 안에 그 냄새가 진동하는 걸 견딜 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“Oh, my dear, you are so hard on your brothers!", "\"오, 얘야, 너는 오라비들한테 어쩜 그리 모질더냐!"], ["It is the only fault I have to find with you.", "너한테서 흠잡을 데라고는 그것 하나뿐이란다."], ["You are the sweetest temper in the world, but you are so tetchy with your brothers.”", "너는 세상에서 제일 다정한 성품이면서도, 오라비들한테는 어찌나 까다롭게 구는지.\""]], [["“Not tetchy, mamma: you never hear me speak in an unladylike way.”", "\"까다로운 게 아니에요, 엄마. 제가 숙녀답지 못하게 말하는 건 한 번도 들어 보신 적 없잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, but you want to deny them things.”", "\"글쎄, 하지만 너는 오라비들이 뭘 하려고 하면 안 된다고 막으려 들잖니.\""]], [["“Brothers are so unpleasant.”", "\"오라비들은 너무 거북한걸요.\""]], [["“Oh, my dear, you must allow for young men.", "\"오, 얘야, 젊은 남자들이려니 하고 봐줘야지."], ["Be thankful if they have good hearts.", "마음씨만 착하면 고맙게 여길 일이야."], ["A woman must learn to put up with little things.", "여자는 사소한 일쯤은 참아 넘기는 법을 배워야 한단다."], ["You will be married some day.”", "너도 언젠가는 결혼할 텐데.\""]], [["“Not to any one who is like Fred.”", "\"프레드 같은 사람한테는 안 해요.\""]], [["“Don’t decry your own brother, my dear.", "\"제 오라비를 깎아내리지 말려무나, 얘야."], ["Few young men have less against them, although he couldn’t take his degree—I’m sure I can’t understand why, for he seems to me most clever.", "학위를 못 딴 것만 빼면 그 애만큼 나무랄 데 적은 젊은이도 드물단다—왜 못 땄는지 나는 도무지 알 수가 없구나, 내가 보기엔 더없이 영리한 아이인데 말이야."], ["And you know yourself he was thought equal to the best society at college.", "너도 알다시피 그 애는 대학에서 최상류 사교계에도 손색없다고들 했잖니."], ["So particular as you are, my dear, I wonder you are not glad to have such a gentlemanly young man for a brother.", "너처럼 까다로운 애가, 그렇게 신사다운 청년을 오라비로 두고도 기뻐하지 않다니 의아하구나."], ["You are always finding fault with Bob because he is not Fred.”", "너는 늘 밥이 프레드가 아니라고 트집을 잡잖니.\""]], [["“Oh no, mamma, only because he is Bob.”", "\"오, 아니에요, 엄마. 그저 밥은 밥이라서 그런 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, my dear, you will not find any Middlemarch young man who has not something against him.”", "\"글쎄다, 얘야, 미들마치 청년치고 흠 한 가지 없는 사람은 못 찾을 거다.\""]], [["“But”—here Rosamond’s face broke into a smile which suddenly revealed two dimples.", "\"하지만—\" 여기서 로저먼드의 얼굴이 미소로 활짝 피어나며 보조개 두 개가 불쑥 드러났다."], ["She herself thought unfavorably of these dimples and smiled little in general society.", "그녀 자신은 이 보조개를 못마땅하게 여겨 사람들 앞에서는 좀처럼 웃지 않았다."], ["“But I shall not marry any Middlemarch young man.”", "\"하지만 저는 미들마치 청년하고는 결혼하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“So it seems, my love, for you have as good as refused the pick of them; and if there’s better to be had, I’m sure there’s no girl better deserves it.”", "\"그런 듯하구나, 얘야. 그중 으뜸가는 사람들도 사실상 마다했으니 말이다. 그리고 더 나은 사람이 있다면야, 너만큼 그걸 누릴 자격이 있는 아가씨도 없고말고.\""]], [["“Excuse me, mamma—I wish you would not say, ‘the pick of them.’”", "\"죄송하지만 엄마—'그중 으뜸'이라는 말은 안 하셨으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Why, what else are they?”", "\"아니, 그럼 달리 뭐라 하겠니?\""]], [["“I mean, mamma, it is rather a vulgar expression.”", "\"그러니까 엄마, 그건 좀 천박한 표현이에요.\""]], [["“Very likely, my dear; I never was a good speaker.", "\"그럴지도 모르지, 얘야. 나는 워낙 말솜씨가 변변치 못했으니까."], ["What should I say?”", "그럼 뭐라고 해야 하느냐?\""]], [["“The best of them.”", "\"'그중 가장 나은 사람들'이라고요.\""]], [["“Why, that seems just as plain and common.", "\"아니, 그것도 똑같이 밋밋하고 흔해 빠진 말 같은데."], ["If I had had time to think, I should have said, ‘the most superior young men.’ But with your education you must know.”", "내가 생각할 틈이 있었다면 '가장 뛰어난 청년들'이라고 했을 거다. 하지만 네가 받은 교육이 있으니 네가 더 잘 알겠지.\""]], [["“What must Rosy know, mother?” said Mr.", "\"엄마, 로지가 뭘 더 잘 안다는 거예요?\""], ["Fred, who had slid in unobserved through the half-open door while the ladies were bending over their work, and now going up to the fire stood with his back towards it, warming the soles of his slippers.", "프레드 씨가 말했다. 그는 부인들이 일감 위로 몸을 숙이고 있는 사이에 반쯤 열린 문으로 슬그머니 들어왔고, 이제 난롯가로 가서 등을 불 쪽으로 향한 채 슬리퍼 바닥을 데우며 서 있었다."]], [["“Whether it’s right to say ‘superior young men,’”", "\"'뛰어난 청년들'이라고 말하는 게 옳은지를 말이다,\""], ["said Mrs. Vincy, ringing the bell.", "빈시 부인이 종을 울리며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, there are so many superior teas and sugars now.", "\"오, 요즘은 '뛰어난' 차에 '뛰어난'"], ["Superior is getting to be shopkeepers’ slang.”", "설탕이 어찌나 많은지. 뛰어나다는 말은 이제 가게 주인들의 은어가 돼 가고 있어요.\""]], [["“Are you beginning to dislike slang, then?”", "\"그럼 너도 은어가 싫어지기 시작한 거니?\""], ["said Rosamond, with mild gravity.", "로저먼드가 온화하면서도 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“Only the wrong sort.", "\"잘못 쓰는 종류만요."], ["All choice of words is slang.", "말을 골라 쓰는 건 죄다 은어예요."], ["It marks a class.”", "그게 계층을 드러내거든요.\""]], [["“There is correct English: that is not slang.”", "\"올바른 영어라는 게 있잖아. 그건 은어가 아니야.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon: correct English is the slang of prigs who write history and essays.", "\"미안하지만, 올바른 영어란 역사책이며 수필을 쓰는 잘난 척하는 자들의 은어야."], ["And the strongest slang of all is the slang of poets.”", "그리고 그중에서도 가장 지독한 은어는 시인들의 은어고.\""]], [["“You will say anything, Fred, to gain your point.”", "\"프레드, 너는 자기 주장을 관철하려고 무슨 말이든 다 하는구나.\""]], [["“Well, tell me whether it is slang or poetry to call an ox a leg-plaiter.”", "\"그럼 황소를 두고 '다리를 엮는 짐승'이라 부르는 게 은어인지 시인지 말해 봐.\""]], [["“Of course you can call it poetry if you like.”", "\"물론 네가 시라고 부르고 싶으면 시겠지.\""]], [["“Aha, Miss Rosy, you don’t know Homer from slang.", "\"하하, 로지 양, 너는 호메로스와 은어도 구별 못 하는구나."], ["I shall invent a new game; I shall write bits of slang and poetry on slips, and give them to you to separate.”", "새 놀이를 하나 만들어야겠다. 종이쪽지에 은어 토막이랑 시 토막을 적어서 너한테 줄 테니 가려내 보라고 해야지.\""]], [["“Dear me, how amusing it is to hear young people talk!” said Mrs.", "\"어머나, 젊은 사람들 이야기를 듣고 있으면 어쩜 이리 재미있을까!\""], ["Vincy, with cheerful admiration.", "빈시 부인이 흐뭇하게 감탄하며 말했다."]], [["“Have you got nothing else for my breakfast, Pritchard?”", "\"프리처드, 내 아침으로 줄 게 이것 말고는 없나?\""], ["said Fred, to the servant who brought in coffee and buttered toast; while he walked round the table surveying the ham, potted beef, and other cold remnants, with an air of silent rejection, and polite forbearance from signs of disgust.", "프레드가 커피와 버터 바른 토스트를 가져온 하인에게 말하면서, 햄이며 항아리에 담은 쇠고기며 그 밖의 식은 음식들을 둘러보며 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌았다. 말없이 거절하는 기색이었지만, 역겨워하는 내색은 점잖게 삼갔다."]], [["“Should you like eggs, sir?”", "\"달걀은 어떠시겠어요, 도련님?\""]], [["“Eggs, no!", "\"달걀, 안 돼!"], ["Bring me a grilled bone.”", "뼈에 붙은 고기를 구워서 가져와.\""]], [["“Really, Fred,”", "\"정말이지, 프레드,\""], ["said Rosamond, when the servant had left the room, “if you must have hot things for breakfast, I wish you would come down earlier.", "하인이 방을 나가자 로저먼드가 말했다. \"기어이 아침으로 뜨거운 걸 먹어야겠으면, 좀 일찍 내려오면 좋겠어."], ["You can get up at six o’clock to go out hunting; I cannot understand why you find it so difficult to get up on other mornings.”", "사냥하러 나가려고 여섯 시에는 일어나면서, 다른 날 아침엔 왜 그렇게 일어나기를 힘들어하는지 모르겠어.\""]], [["“That is your want of understanding, Rosy.", "\"그건 네가 이해를 못 해서 그래, 로지."], ["I can get up to go hunting because I like it.”", "사냥하러 갈 때는 내가 좋아서 일어나는 거니까.\""]], [["“What would you think of me if I came down two hours after every one else and ordered grilled bone?”", "\"내가 만약 다른 사람들보다 두 시간이나 늦게 내려와서 뼈구이를 시킨다면 나를 어떻게 생각하겠어?\""]], [["“I should think you were an uncommonly fast young lady,”", "\"보기 드물게 행실이 헤픈 아가씨라고 생각하겠지,\""], ["said Fred, eating his toast with the utmost composure.", "프레드가 더없이 태연하게 토스트를 먹으며 말했다."]], [["“I cannot see why brothers are to make themselves disagreeable, any more than sisters.”", "\"오라비들이 누이들보다 더 거북하게 굴어야 할 이유를 모르겠어.\""]], [["“I don’t make myself disagreeable; it is you who find me so.", "\"내가 거북하게 구는 게 아니라, 네가 나를 거북하게 여기는 거야."], ["Disagreeable is a word that describes your feelings and not my actions.”", "거북하다는 건 내 행동이 아니라 네 기분을 설명하는 말이라고.\""]], [["“I think it describes the smell of grilled bone.”", "\"내 생각엔 그게 뼈구이 냄새를 설명하는 말 같은데.\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"천만에."], ["It describes a sensation in your little nose associated with certain finicking notions which are the classics of Mrs.", "그건 네 조그만 코의 어떤 감각을 설명하는 말이지. 레먼 부인 학교의 고전이라 할 만한 그 까탈스러운 관념들과 결부된 감각 말이야."], ["Lemon’s school.", "우리 어머니를 봐."], ["Look at my mother; you don’t see her objecting to everything except what she does herself.", "어머니는 당신이 직접 하는 일 말고는 무엇에도 트집을 잡지 않으시잖아."], ["She is my notion of a pleasant woman.”", "어머니야말로 내가 생각하는 유쾌한 여성이야.\""]], [["“Bless you both, my dears, and don’t quarrel,” said Mrs.", "\"오누이 둘 다 복 받으렴, 얘들아, 그리고 다투지들 마라,\""], ["Vincy, with motherly cordiality.", "빈시 부인이 어머니다운 다정함으로 말했다."], ["“Come, Fred, tell us all about the new doctor.", "\"자, 프레드, 새로 온 의사 이야기나 다 들려주렴."], ["How is your uncle pleased with him?”", "네 외삼촌은 그 사람을 얼마나 마음에 들어 하시던?\""]], [["“Pretty well, I think.", "\"꽤 마음에 들어 하시는 것 같아요."], ["He asks Lydgate all sorts of questions and then screws up his face while he hears the answers, as if they were pinching his toes.", "리드게이트한테 온갖 질문을 던져 놓고는, 대답을 듣는 동안 마치 누가 발가락이라도 꼬집는 것처럼 얼굴을 잔뜩 찡그리시죠."], ["That’s his way.", "그게 외삼촌 방식이에요."], ["Ah, here comes my grilled bone.”", "아, 여기 내 뼈구이가 오는군.\""]], [["“But how came you to stay out so late, my dear?", "\"그런데 얘야, 어쩌다 그리 늦게까지 밖에 있었니?"], ["You only said you were going to your uncle’s.”", "외삼촌 댁에 간다고만 했잖니.\""]], [["“Oh, I dined at Plymdale’s.", "\"오, 플림데일 댁에서 저녁을 먹었어요."], ["We had whist.", "휘스트를 했죠."], ["Lydgate was there too.”", "리드게이트도 거기 있었어요.\""]], [["“And what do you think of him?", "\"그래, 그 사람을 어떻게 생각하니?"], ["He is very gentlemanly, I suppose.", "무척 신사답겠지, 아마."], ["They say he is of excellent family—his relations quite county people.”", "사람들 말로는 훌륭한 집안 출신이라던데—친척들이 다 지체 높은 시골 양반들이라고.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."], ["“There was a Lydgate at John’s who spent no end of money.", "\"존스 칼리지에 리드게이트라는 사람이 있었는데 돈을 펑펑 썼어요."], ["I find this man is a second cousin of his.", "알고 보니 이 사람이 그 사람의 육촌이더라고요."], ["But rich men may have very poor devils for second cousins.”", "하지만 부자라고 해서 육촌까지 다 형편이 좋으란 법은 없죠.\""]], [["“It always makes a difference, though, to be of good family,”", "\"그래도 좋은 집안 출신이라는 건 늘 차이가 나는 법이지,\""], ["said Rosamond, with a tone of decision which showed that she had thought on this subject.", "로저먼드가 단호한 어조로 말했는데, 그 어조는 그녀가 이 문제를 곰곰 생각해 보았음을 드러냈다."], ["Rosamond felt that she might have been happier if she had not been the daughter of a Middlemarch manufacturer.", "로저먼드는 자신이 미들마치 제조업자의 딸이 아니었더라면 더 행복했으리라고 느꼈다."], ["She disliked anything which reminded her that her mother’s father had been an innkeeper.", "그녀는 외할아버지가 여관 주인이었다는 사실을 떠올리게 하는 것이라면 무엇이든 싫어했다."], ["Certainly any one remembering the fact might think that Mrs. Vincy had the air of a very handsome good-humored landlady, accustomed to the most capricious orders of gentlemen.", "분명 그 사실을 기억하는 사람이라면 누구든, 빈시 부인이 신사들의 변덕스럽기 짝이 없는 주문에 이골이 난, 매우 잘생기고 쾌활한 여관 안주인 같은 분위기를 풍긴다고 여길 만했다."]], [["“I thought it was odd his name was Tertius,”", "\"그 사람 이름이 터티어스라는 게 이상하다 싶었는데,\""], ["said the bright-faced matron, “but of course it’s a name in the family.", "환한 얼굴의 부인이 말했다. \"물론 집안 내림 이름이겠지."], ["But now, tell us exactly what sort of man he is.”", "그건 그렇고, 그 사람이 정확히 어떤 사람인지 말해 보렴.\""]], [["“Oh, tallish, dark, clever—talks well—rather a prig, I think.”", "\"오, 키가 큰 편이고, 거무스름하고, 영리하고—말도 잘하고—제법 잘난 척하는 친구예요.\""]], [["“I never can make out what you mean by a prig,”", "\"네가 잘난 척한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 모르겠어,\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."]], [["“A fellow who wants to show that he has opinions.”", "\"자기한테 의견이 있다는 걸 내보이고 싶어 하는 사람이지.\""]], [["“Why, my dear, doctors must have opinions,”", "\"아니, 얘야, 의사라면 의견이 있어야지,\""], ["said Mrs. Vincy.", "빈시 부인이 말했다."], ["“What are they there for else?”", "\"그게 아니면 의사가 무엇하러 있겠니?\""]], [["“Yes, mother, the opinions they are paid for.", "\"그래요, 어머니, 돈을 받고 내놓는 의견이라면 그렇죠."], ["But a prig is a fellow who is always making you a present of his opinions.”", "하지만 잘난 척하는 사람이란 늘 자기 의견을 선물처럼 안겨 주는 사람을 말해요.\""]], [["“I suppose Mary Garth admires Mr. Lydgate,”", "\"메리 가스가 리드게이트 씨를 흠모하는 모양이지,\""], ["said Rosamond, not without a touch of innuendo.", "로저먼드가 은근히 빈정대는 기색을 섞어 말했다."]], [["“Really, I can’t say.”", "\"글쎄, 그건 나도 모르겠는데.\""], ["said Fred, rather glumly, as he left the table, and taking up a novel which he had brought down with him, threw himself into an arm-chair.", "프레드가 다소 시무룩하게 말하며 식탁에서 일어나, 가지고 내려온 소설책을 집어 들고는 안락의자에 몸을 던졌다."], ["“If you are jealous of her, go oftener to Stone Court yourself and eclipse her.”", "\"그 애가 질투 나거든 네가 직접 스톤 코트에 더 자주 가서 빛이 안 나게 눌러 버리든가.\""]], [["“I wish you would not be so vulgar, Fred.", "\"제발 그렇게 천박하게 굴지 좀 마, 프레드."], ["If you have finished, pray ring the bell.”", "다 먹었으면 종이나 좀 울려 줘.\""]], [["“It is true, though—what your brother says, Rosamond,”", "\"하지만 네 오라비 말이 맞다, 로저먼드,\""], ["Mrs. Vincy began, when the servant had cleared the table.", "하인이 식탁을 치우고 나자 빈시 부인이 입을 열었다."], ["“It is a thousand pities you haven’t patience to go and see your uncle more, so proud of you as he is, and wanted you to live with him.", "\"네가 외삼촌을 더 자주 찾아뵐 인내심이 없다는 건 천번만번 아까운 일이야. 그분이 너를 그토록 자랑스러워하시고, 너더러 같이 살자고까지 하셨는데."], ["There’s no knowing what he might have done for you as well as for Fred.", "프레드뿐 아니라 너한테도 무엇을 해 주셨을지 누가 알겠니."], ["God knows, I’m fond of having you at home with me, but I can part with my children for their good.", "하느님은 아시겠지만, 나야 너를 곁에 두고 사는 게 좋단다. 그래도 자식들 잘되라고 떠나보낼 수는 있는 거야."], ["And now it stands to reason that your uncle Featherstone will do something for Mary Garth.”", "그리고 이제 외삼촌 페더스톤 씨가 메리 가스한테 무언가 해 주실 게 뻔한 노릇이고.\""]], [["“Mary Garth can bear being at Stone Court, because she likes that better than being a governess,”", "\"메리 가스는 스톤 코트에 있는 걸 견딜 수 있죠. 가정 교사 노릇 하는 것보다 그게 더 좋으니까요,\""], ["said Rosamond, folding up her work.", "로저먼드가 일감을 개키며 말했다."], ["“I would rather not have anything left to me if I must earn it by enduring much of my uncle’s cough and his ugly relations.”", "\"외삼촌의 기침이며 그 흉한 친척들을 한참 참아 가며 얻어 내야 한다면, 저는 차라리 아무것도 안 물려받는 편이 낫겠어요.\""]], [["“He can’t be long for this world, my dear; I wouldn’t hasten his end, but what with asthma and that inward complaint, let us hope there is something better for him in another.", "\"그분이 이 세상에 오래 계실 수는 없을 거다, 얘야. 내가 그분 임종을 재촉할 생각이야 없지만, 천식에다 그 속병까지 있으니, 저세상에서나마 더 나은 것이 그분을 기다리고 있기를 바라야지."], ["And I have no ill-will towards Mary Garth, but there’s justice to be thought of.", "그리고 나는 메리 가스한테 악감정은 없다만, 공평함이라는 것도 생각해야지."], ["And Mr. Featherstone’s first wife brought him no money, as my sister did.", "게다가 페더스톤 씨의 첫 부인은 우리 언니처럼 그분께 재산을 가져다주지도 않았잖니."], ["Her nieces and nephews can’t have so much claim as my sister’s.", "그 조카들이 우리 언니 조카들만큼 권리를 주장할 수는 없는 노릇이지."], ["And I must say I think Mary Garth a dreadful plain girl—more fit for a governess.”", "그리고 솔직히 말해서 나는 메리 가스가 정말 못생긴 처녀라고 생각한다—가정 교사가 차라리 더 어울려.\""]], [["“Every one would not agree with you there, mother,”", "\"누구나 그 점에서 어머니 의견에 동의하지는 않을걸요,\""], ["said Fred, who seemed to be able to read and listen too.", "책을 읽으면서도 귀를 기울일 수 있는 모양인 프레드가 말했다."]], [["“Well, my dear,” said Mrs.", "\"글쎄다, 얘야,\""], ["Vincy, wheeling skilfully, “if she had some fortune left her,—a man marries his wife’s relations, and the Garths are so poor, and live in such a small way.", "빈시 부인이 능숙하게 말머리를 돌리며 말했다. \"그 애한테 재산이라도 좀 남겨진다면야—남자란 아내의 친척들까지 맞아들이는 법이고, 가스 집안은 어찌나 가난한지, 그렇게 옹색하게 살잖니."], ["But I shall leave you to your studies, my dear; for I must go and do some shopping.”", "하지만 나는 네 공부에 방해되지 않게 자리를 비켜 주마, 얘야. 장도 좀 보러 가야 하니.\""]], [["“Fred’s studies are not very deep,”", "\"프레드의 공부는 그리 깊지 않아요, 엄마,\""], ["said Rosamond, rising with her mamma, “he is only reading a novel.”", "로저먼드가 어머니를 따라 일어서며 말했다. \"그냥 소설을 읽고 있을 뿐인걸요.\""]], [["“Well, well, by-and-by he’ll go to his Latin and things,”", "\"자, 자, 머지않아 라틴어 공부 같은 걸 할 거란다,\""], ["said Mrs. Vincy, soothingly, stroking her son’s head.", "빈시 부인이 아들의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 달래듯 말했다."], ["“There’s a fire in the smoking-room on purpose.", "\"그러라고 흡연실에 일부러 불을 지펴 놓았잖니."], ["It’s your father’s wish, you know—Fred, my dear—and I always tell him you will be good, and go to college again to take your degree.”", "너도 알다시피 그게 네 아버지 바람이란다—프레드, 얘야—나는 늘 아버지한테 네가 착하게 굴고 다시 대학에 가서 학위를 딸 거라고 말씀드린단다.\""]], [["Fred drew his mother’s hand down to his lips, but said nothing.", "프레드는 어머니의 손을 끌어다 입술에 갖다 댔지만, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“I suppose you are not going out riding to-day?”", "\"오늘은 말 타러 안 나갈 거지?\""], ["said Rosamond, lingering a little after her mamma was gone.", "어머니가 나간 뒤 로저먼드가 조금 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“No; why?”", "\"안 나가. 왜?\""]], [["“Papa says I may have the chestnut to ride now.”", "\"아빠가 이제 그 밤색 말을 내가 타도 된다고 하셨거든.\""]], [["“You can go with me to-morrow, if you like.", "\"그럼 내일 나랑 같이 가도 돼, 네가 원하면."], ["Only I am going to Stone Court, remember.”", "단, 나는 스톤 코트에 갈 거란 걸 기억해 둬.\""]], [["“I want to ride so much, it is indifferent to me where we go.”", "\"나는 말이 어찌나 타고 싶은지, 어디로 가든 상관없어.\""], ["Rosamond really wished to go to Stone Court, of all other places.", "로저먼드는 사실 다른 어떤 곳보다도 스톤 코트에 가고 싶었다."]], [["“Oh, I say, Rosy,”", "\"아, 참, 로지,\""], ["said Fred, as she was passing out of the room, “if you are going to the piano, let me come and play some airs with you.”", "그녀가 방을 나서려 할 때 프레드가 말했다. \"피아노 치러 갈 거면, 나도 가서 너랑 같이 몇 곡 연주하게 해 줘.\""]], [["“Pray do not ask me this morning.”", "\"오늘 아침엔 제발 그런 부탁 하지 마.\""]], [["“Why not this morning?”", "\"왜 오늘 아침은 안 되는데?\""]], [["“Really, Fred, I wish you would leave off playing the flute.", "\"정말이지, 프레드, 너 플루트 좀 그만 불었으면 좋겠어."], ["A man looks very silly playing the flute.", "남자가 플루트를 부는 모습은 너무 우스꽝스러워."], ["And you play so out of tune.”", "게다가 너는 음정도 그렇게 틀리잖아.\""]], [["“When next any one makes love to you, Miss Rosamond, I will tell him how obliging you are.”", "\"다음번에 누가 너한테 구애하거든, 로저먼드 양, 네가 얼마나 상냥한지 그 사람한테 일러바칠 테다.\""]], [["“Why should you expect me to oblige you by hearing you play the flute, any more than I should expect you to oblige me by not playing it?”", "\"내가 네 플루트 연주를 들어 줘서 너를 기쁘게 해 주리라고 기대할 이유가 어디 있어? 내가 너더러 플루트를 불지 말아 달라고 해서 나를 기쁘게 해 주리라고 기대하지 않는 것과 마찬가지로 말이야.\""]], [["“And why should you expect me to take you out riding?”", "\"그럼 내가 너를 말 타러 데리고 나가 주리라고 기대할 이유는 또 어디 있고?\""]], [["This question led to an adjustment, for Rosamond had set her mind on that particular ride.", "이 물음은 합의로 이어졌으니, 로저먼드가 그 특정한 승마에 마음을 단단히 두고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["So Fred was gratified with nearly an hour’s practice of “Ar hyd y nos,” “Ye banks and braes,”", "그리하여 프레드는 「밤이 새도록」과 「그대 강둑과 언덕이여」를 비롯해 자신의 『플루트 교본』에 실린 다른 애창곡들을 거의 한 시간 가까이 연습하는 흐뭇함을 누렸다."], ["and other favorite airs from his “Instructor on the Flute;” a wheezy performance, into which he threw much ambition and an irrepressible hopefulness.", "그것은 쌕쌕거리는 연주였으나, 그는 거기에 잔뜩 욕심을 부리며 억누를 수 없는 희망을 쏟아부었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII.", "# CHAPTER XII."]], [["He had more tow on his distaffe Than Gerveis knew. —CHAUCER.", "그는 거베이스가 아는 것보다\n더 많은 실을 물레에 감고 있었다."]], [["The ride to Stone Court, which Fred and Rosamond took the next morning, lay through a pretty bit of midland landscape, almost all meadows and pastures, with hedgerows still allowed to grow in bushy beauty and to spread out coral fruit for the birds.", "— 초서\n다음 날 아침 프레드와 로저먼드가 스톤 코트로 말을 달려간 길은 중부 지방의 아름다운 풍경 한 자락을 가로질렀으니, 거의 온통 풀밭과 목초지였고, 산울타리들은 여전히 무성하게 자라 그 아름다움을 뽐내며 새들에게 산호빛 열매를 펼쳐 보이도록 허락받고 있었다."], ["Little details gave each field a particular physiognomy, dear to the eyes that have looked on them from childhood: the pool in the corner where the grasses were dank and trees leaned whisperingly; the great oak shadowing a bare place in mid-pasture; the high bank where the ash-trees grew; the sudden slope of the old marl-pit making a red background for the burdock; the huddled roofs and ricks of the homestead without a traceable way of approach; the gray gate and fences against the depths of the bordering wood; and the stray hovel, its old, old thatch full of mossy hills and valleys with wondrous modulations of light and shadow such as we travel far to see in later life, and see larger, but not more beautiful.", "자잘한 세부들이 들판마다 저마다의 인상을 부여했으니, 어린 시절부터 그것들을 바라보아 온 눈에는 더없이 정겨운 것들이었다. 풀이 축축하고 나무들이 속삭이듯 기울어진 모퉁이의 웅덩이, 목초지 한가운데 휑한 곳에 그늘을 드리운 커다란 떡갈나무, 물푸레나무가 자라는 높은 둑, 우엉을 위해 붉은 배경을 만들어 주는 오래된 이회토 채굴장의 가파른 비탈, 다가갈 길조차 가늠할 수 없는 농가의 옹기종기 모인 지붕들과 낟가리들, 경계를 이루는 깊은 숲을 등진 회색 대문과 울타리, 그리고 한 채 외따로 떨어진 오두막, 그 아주아주 오래된 초가지붕은 이끼 낀 언덕과 골짜기로 가득해 훗날의 삶에서 멀리까지 찾아가 보게 되는, 더 크긴 하지만 더 아름답지는 않은 빛과 그림자의 경이로운 변주를 품고 있었다."], ["These are the things that make the gamut of joy in landscape to midland-bred souls—the things they toddled among, or perhaps learned by heart standing between their father’s knees while he drove leisurely.", "이런 것들이야말로 중부 지방에서 자란 영혼들에게 풍경 속 기쁨의 음계를 이루는 것들이다. 그들이 아장아장 그 사이를 걸었던 것들, 혹은 아버지가 한가로이 마차를 모는 동안 그 무릎 사이에 서서 마음속에 새겨 두었던 것들이다."]], [["But the road, even the byroad, was excellent; for Lowick, as we have seen, was not a parish of muddy lanes and poor tenants; and it was into Lowick parish that Fred and Rosamond entered after a couple of miles’ riding.", "그러나 길은, 심지어 샛길조차도 훌륭했다. 우리가 보았듯이 로윅은 진창 골목과 가난한 소작인의 교구가 아니었기 때문이다."], ["Another mile would bring them to Stone Court, and at the end of the first half, the house was already visible, looking as if it had been arrested in its growth toward a stone mansion by an unexpected budding of farm-buildings on its left flank, which had hindered it from becoming anything more than the substantial dwelling of a gentleman farmer.", "그리고 프레드와 로저먼드가 두 마일쯤 말을 달린 끝에 들어선 곳이 바로 로윅 교구였다. 한 마일을 더 가면 스톤 코트에 닿을 텐데, 그 첫 반 마일이 끝나는 곳에 이르자 집은 이미 눈에 들어왔다."], ["It was not the less agreeable an object in the distance for the cluster of pinnacled corn-ricks which balanced the fine row of walnuts on the right.", "그 집은 석조 저택으로 자라나려던 차에, 왼쪽 측면에서 예기치 못하게 농가 건물들이 돋아나는 바람에 그 성장이 멈춰 버린 듯, 한 신사 농부의 견실한 거처 이상으로는 더 자라나지 못한 모습이었다. 그렇다 해도 오른쪽에 늘어선 멋진 호두나무들과 균형을 이루는, 첨탑 같은 곡식 낟가리들이 무리 지어 있어, 멀리서 바라보기에 결코 덜 정겨운 풍경은 아니었다."]], [["Presently it was possible to discern something that might be a gig on the circular drive before the front door.", "이윽고 현관문 앞 둥근 진입로에 마차 같은 무언가가 서 있는 것을 분간할 수 있게 되었다."]], [["“Dear me,”", "\"어머나,\""], ["said Rosamond, “I hope none of my uncle’s horrible relations are there.”", "로저먼드가 말했다. \"외삼촌의 그 끔찍한 친척들이 와 있지 않았으면 좋겠는데.\""]], [["“They are, though. That is Mrs.", "\"하지만 와 있는걸."], ["Waule’s gig—the last yellow gig left, I should think.", "저건 월 부인의 이륜마차야. 내 생각엔 마지막으로 남은 노란 이륜마차일걸."], ["When I see Mrs. Waule in it, I understand how yellow can have been worn for mourning.", "월 부인이 저걸 타고 있는 걸 보면, 어째서 노란색이 상복으로 쓰일 수 있었는지 이해가 돼."], ["That gig seems to me more funereal than a hearse.", "저 마차는 나한테는 영구차보다 더 장례식 같아 보여."], ["But then Mrs. Waule always has black crape on.", "하긴 월 부인은 늘 검은 크레이프를 두르고 다니지."], ["How does she manage it, Rosy?", "어떻게 그게 가능한 거지, 로지?"], ["Her friends can’t always be dying.”", "그 사람 친구들이 늘 죽어 나갈 수는 없잖아.\""]], [["“I don’t know at all.", "\"나도 도무지 모르겠어."], ["And she is not in the least evangelical,”", "게다가 그 사람은 조금도 복음주의적이지도 않은데.\""], ["said Rosamond, reflectively, as if that religious point of view would have fully accounted for perpetual crape.", "로저먼드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했는데, 마치 그런 종교적 관점이라면 끊임없는 크레이프 차림을 충분히 설명해 주기라도 할 것처럼 들렸다."], ["“And, not poor,”", "\"그리고 가난하지도 않고.\""], ["she added, after a moment’s pause.", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“No, by George!", "\"그럼, 천만에!"], ["They are as rich as Jews, those Waules and Featherstones; I mean, for people like them, who don’t want to spend anything.", "그 월씨 집안이나 페더스톤 집안 사람들은 유대인처럼 부자야. 내 말은, 그 사람들처럼 아무것도 쓰려 들지 않는 부류치고는 말이지."], ["And yet they hang about my uncle like vultures, and are afraid of a farthing going away from their side of the family.", "그러면서도 그들은 외삼촌 주위를 독수리처럼 맴돌면서, 자기네 쪽 집안에서 단 한 푼이라도 새어 나갈까 봐 전전긍긍하지."], ["But I believe he hates them all.”", "하지만 내 생각엔 외삼촌이 그들 모두를 미워하는 것 같아.\""]], [["The Mrs. Waule who was so far from being admirable in the eyes of these distant connections, had happened to say this very morning (not at all with a defiant air, but in a low, muffled, neutral tone, as of a voice heard through cotton wool) that she did not wish “to enjoy their good opinion.”", "그 먼 친척들의 눈에 그토록 탐탁지 않았던 월 부인은, 바로 그날 아침에 (결코 도전적인 태도가 아니라, 솜을 통과해 들려오는 듯한 낮고 둔탁하며 무덤덤한 어조로) 자기는 \"그들의 좋은 평판을 누리고\""], ["She was seated, as she observed, on her own brother’s hearth, and had been Jane Featherstone five-and-twenty years before she had been Jane Waule, which entitled her to speak when her own brother’s name had been made free with by those who had no right to it.", "싶지 않다고 말했던 참이었다. 그녀는 자기가 직접 말했듯이, 친오빠의 화롯가에 앉아 있었고, 제인 월이 되기 스물다섯 해 전에 이미 제인 페더스톤이었으니, 그 이름에 아무 권리도 없는 자들이 친오빠의 이름을 함부로 입에 올렸을 때 한마디 할 자격이 있는 사람이었다."]], [["“What are you driving at there?” said Mr.", "\"거기서 무슨 소리를 하려는 게야?\""], ["Featherstone, holding his stick between his knees and settling his wig, while he gave her a momentary sharp glance, which seemed to react on him like a draught of cold air and set him coughing.", "페더스톤 씨가 무릎 사이에 지팡이를 끼우고 가발을 바로잡으며 말했다. 그러면서 그녀에게 잠깐 날카로운 시선을 던졌는데, 그것이 마치 찬 바람 한 줄기가 닥친 것처럼 그에게 되돌아와 기침을 터뜨리게 했다."]], [["Mrs. Waule had to defer her answer till he was quiet again, till Mary Garth had supplied him with fresh syrup, and he had begun to rub the gold knob of his stick, looking bitterly at the fire.", "월 부인은 그가 다시 잠잠해질 때까지, 메리 가스가 그에게 새 시럽을 갖다줄 때까지, 그리고 그가 불을 씁쓸하게 바라보며 지팡이의 금 손잡이를 문지르기 시작할 때까지 대답을 미뤄야 했다."], ["It was a bright fire, but it made no difference to the chill-looking purplish tint of Mrs. Waule’s face, which was as neutral as her voice; having mere chinks for eyes, and lips that hardly moved in speaking.", "불은 환하게 타올랐지만, 월 부인의 차갑고 푸르스름한 얼굴빛에는 아무 변화도 주지 못했으니, 그 얼굴은 목소리만큼이나 무덤덤했고, 눈이라고는 가느다란 틈에 지나지 않았으며, 입술은 말할 때조차 거의 움직이지 않았다."]], [["“The doctors can’t master that cough, brother.", "\"의사들도 그 기침은 못 다스리네요, 오빠."], ["It’s just like what I have; for I’m your own sister, constitution and everything.", "꼭 내 기침이랑 똑같아요. 나도 오빠의 친동생이니까 체질이고 뭐고 다 같지요."], ["But, as I was saying, it’s a pity Mrs. Vincy’s family can’t be better conducted.”", "하지만 아까도 말했다시피, 빈시 부인네 집안이 좀 더 단속이 안 되는 게 안타까운 일이에요.\""]], [["“Tchah!", "\"쳇!"], ["you said nothing o’ the sort.", "넌 그런 말 한 적 없어."], ["You said somebody had made free with my name.”", "누가 내 이름을 함부로 입에 올렸다고 했지.\""]], [["“And no more than can be proved, if what everybody says is true.", "\"누구나 하는 말이 사실이라면, 증명할 수 있는 것만 말한 거예요."], ["My brother Solomon tells me it’s the talk up and down in Middlemarch how unsteady young Vincy is, and has been forever gambling at billiards since home he came.”", "솔로몬 오빠 말로는, 빈시네 그 젊은 녀석이 얼마나 행실이 헤픈지, 집에 돌아온 뒤로 줄곧 당구로 도박을 한다고 미들마치에서 위아래로 다들 떠들썩하다네요.\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"말도 안 되는 소리!"], ["What’s a game at billiards?", "당구 한 판이 뭐가 어떻다고?"], ["It’s a good gentlemanly game; and young Vincy is not a clodhopper.", "그건 점잖은 신사다운 놀이야. 빈시네 젊은이는 촌뜨기가 아니라고."], ["If your son John took to billiards, now, he’d make a fool of himself.”", "만약 네 아들 존이 당구에 손을 댔다면, 그야말로 바보 같은 꼴이 됐겠지.\""]], [["“Your nephew John never took to billiards or any other game, brother, and is far from losing hundreds of pounds, which, if what everybody says is true, must be found somewhere else than out of Mr. Vincy the father’s pocket.", "\"오빠 조카 존은 당구든 다른 어떤 놀이든 손댄 적이 없어요, 오빠."], ["For they say he’s been losing money for years, though nobody would think so, to see him go coursing and keeping open house as they do.", "게다가 수백 파운드를 잃는 일과는 거리가 멀지요. 누구나 하는 말이 사실이라면, 그 돈은 분명 아버지 빈시 씨 호주머니가 아닌 다른 데서 나와야 할 거예요."], ["And I’ve heard say Mr. Bulstrode condemns Mrs.", "사람들 말로는 그가 몇 년째 돈을 잃고 있다는데, 그렇게 사냥개를 데리고 다니고 늘 손님을 치는 걸 보면 누구도 그렇게 생각하진 않겠지만요."], ["Vincy beyond anything for her flightiness, and spoiling her children so.”", "그리고 들리는 말로는 벌스트로드 씨가 빈시 부인을 두고, 그 들떠 있는 태도며 자식들을 그렇게 응석받이로 키우는 걸 무엇보다도 호되게 나무란다더군요.\""]], [["“What’s Bulstrode to me?", "\"벌스트로드가 나하고 무슨 상관이야?"], ["I don’t bank with him.”", "난 그자 은행에 거래도 안 해.\""]], [["“Well, Mrs. Bulstrode is Mr.", "\"그런데 벌스트로드 부인이 바로 빈시 씨의 친누이고, 사람들 말로는 빈시 씨가 거의 은행 돈으로 장사를 한다지요."], ["Vincy’s own sister, and they do say that Mr. Vincy mostly trades on the Bank money; and you may see yourself, brother, when a woman past forty has pink strings always flying, and that light way of laughing at everything, it’s very unbecoming.", "그리고 오빠도 직접 보시면 알겠지만, 마흔이 넘은 여자가 늘 분홍 리본을 나부끼고 다니면서 무슨 일에든 그렇게 가볍게 웃어 대는 건 아주 꼴사나운 일이에요."], ["But indulging your children is one thing, and finding money to pay their debts is another.", "하지만 자식들을 응석받이로 키우는 것과 그 빚을 갚을 돈을 마련하는 건 별개의 문제지요."], ["And it’s openly said that young Vincy has raised money on his expectations.", "그리고 빈시네 젊은 녀석이 자기 유산 기대를 담보로 돈을 빌렸다는 게 공공연한 소문이에요."], ["I don’t say what expectations.", "무슨 유산 기대인지는 말하지 않겠어요."], ["Miss Garth hears me, and is welcome to tell again.", "가스 양이 듣고 있으니, 얼마든지 다시 옮겨도 좋아요."], ["I know young people hang together.”", "젊은 사람들끼리는 한통속이라는 걸 잘 아니까요.\""]], [["“No, thank you, Mrs. Waule,”", "\"고맙지만 사양할게요, 월 부인.\""], ["said Mary Garth.", "메리 가스가 말했다."], ["“I dislike hearing scandal too much to wish to repeat it.”", "\"저는 험담을 듣는 것 자체를 너무 싫어해서, 그걸 옮기고 싶은 마음 같은 건 없거든요.\""]], [["Mr. Featherstone rubbed the knob of his stick and made a brief convulsive show of laughter, which had much the same genuineness as an old whist-player’s chuckle over a bad hand.", "페더스톤 씨는 지팡이 손잡이를 문지르며 발작하듯 짧게 웃어 보였는데, 그 웃음은 패가 형편없을 때 흘리는 노련한 휘스트 노름꾼의 낄낄거림과 꼭 같은 진정성을 지니고 있었다."], ["Still looking at the fire, he said—", "여전히 불을 바라보며 그가 말했다."]], [["“And who pretends to say Fred Vincy hasn’t got expectations?", "\"그래, 프레드 빈시한테 유산 기대가 없다고 감히 떠들 사람이 누구야?"], ["Such a fine, spirited fellow is like enough to have ’em.”", "그렇게 잘생기고 패기 있는 녀석이라면 그런 기대쯤 있을 만도 하지.\""]], [["There was a slight pause before Mrs. Waule replied, and when she did so, her voice seemed to be slightly moistened with tears, though her face was still dry.", "월 부인이 대답하기까지 잠깐의 사이가 있었는데, 마침내 입을 열었을 때 그녀의 목소리는 눈물에 살짝 젖은 듯했으나, 얼굴은 여전히 메말라 있었다."]], [["“Whether or no, brother, it is naturally painful to me and my brother Solomon to hear your name made free with, and your complaint being such as may carry you off sudden, and people who are no more Featherstones than the Merry-Andrew at the fair, openly reckoning on your property coming to them.", "\"그렇든 아니든, 오빠, 오빠 이름이 함부로 입에 오르내리는 걸 듣는 건 나와 솔로몬 오빠한테는 당연히 가슴 아픈 일이에요. 게다가 오빠 병이 언제 갑자기 데려갈지 모를 그런 병인데, 장터의 어릿광대만큼이나 페더스톤 집안과는 아무 상관도 없는 사람들이 오빠 재산이 자기네 차지가 될 거라고 대놓고 셈을 하고 있다니."], ["And me your own sister, and Solomon your own brother!", "나는 오빠 친동생이고, 솔로몬은 오빠 친동생인데!"], ["And if that’s to be it, what has it pleased the Almighty to make families for?”", "정말 그렇게 될 거라면, 전능하신 하느님께서 대체 무엇 하러 가족을 만드신 거예요?\""], ["Here Mrs. Waule’s tears fell, but with moderation.", "여기서 월 부인의 눈물이 떨어졌으나, 절제된 정도였다."]], [["“Come, out with it, Jane!”", "\"자, 어서 털어놓아, 제인!\""], ["said Mr. Featherstone, looking at her.", "페더스톤 씨가 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“You mean to say, Fred Vincy has been getting somebody to advance him money on what he says he knows about my will, eh?”", "\"네 말은, 프레드 빈시가 내 유언장에 대해 안다고 떠벌리면서 그걸 빌미로 누군가한테 돈을 끌어다 썼다는 거지, 응?\""]], [["“I never said so, brother”", "\"난 그런 말 한 적 없어요, 오빠.\""], ["(Mrs. Waule’s voice had again become dry and unshaken).", "(월 부인의 목소리는 다시 메마르고 흔들림 없는 것으로 돌아와 있었다.) \"어젯밤에 솔로몬 오빠가 장에서 돌아오는 길에 들러서 묵은 밀에 대해 조언을 해 주면서 말해 준 거예요."], ["“It was told me by my brother Solomon last night when he called coming from market to give me advice about the old wheat, me being a widow, and my son John only three-and-twenty, though steady beyond anything.", "내가 과부고, 아들 존은 이제 겨우 스물셋이지만, 누구보다도 착실하니까요. 그리고 오빠는 그걸 더없이 확실한 출처에서 들었대요."], ["And he had it from most undeniable authority, and not one, but many.”", "그것도 한 사람이 아니라 여러 사람한테서요.\""]], [["“Stuff and nonsense!", "\"허튼소리!"], ["I don’t believe a word of it.", "난 한마디도 안 믿어."], ["It’s all a got-up story.", "죄다 꾸며 낸 이야기야."], ["Go to the window, missy; I thought I heard a horse.", "창가로 가 보거라, 아가씨. 말 소리가 들린 것 같구나."], ["See if the doctor’s coming.”", "의사가 오는지 보거라.\""]], [["“Not got up by me, brother, nor yet by Solomon, who, whatever else he may be—and I don’t deny he has oddities—has made his will and parted his property equal between such kin as he’s friends with; though, for my part, I think there are times when some should be considered more than others. But Solomon makes it no secret what he means to do.”", "\"내가 꾸며 낸 게 아니에요, 오빠. 솔로몬 오빠도 아니고요. 솔로몬 오빠가 다른 면에서야 어떻든—별난 데가 있다는 건 나도 부인하지 않아요—이미 유언장을 작성해서, 친하게 지내는 친척들끼리 재산을 똑같이 나눠 두었어요. 하기야 내 생각으로는 어떤 사람은 다른 이들보다 더 대접받아야 할 때도 있다고 보지만요. 어쨌든 솔로몬 오빠는 자기가 뭘 할 작정인지 조금도 숨기지 않아요.\""]], [["“The more fool he!” said Mr.", "\"그러니까 더 바보 천치지!\""], ["Featherstone, with some difficulty; breaking into a severe fit of coughing that required Mary Garth to stand near him, so that she did not find out whose horses they were which presently paused stamping on the gravel before the door.", "페더스톤 씨가 간신히 말을 내뱉으며 심한 기침 발작을 일으켰는데, 그 바람에 메리 가스가 그의 곁에 가까이 서 있어야 했고, 그래서 이윽고 문 앞 자갈밭에서 발굽 소리를 내며 멈춰 선 말이 누구의 것인지 알아보지 못했다."]], [["Before Mr. Featherstone’s cough was quiet, Rosamond entered, bearing up her riding-habit with much grace.", "페더스톤 씨의 기침이 잦아들기도 전에, 로저먼드가 승마복 자락을 우아하게 추켜올리며 들어섰다."], ["She bowed ceremoniously to Mrs. Waule, who said stiffly, “How do you do, miss?”", "그녀는 월 부인에게 격식을 갖춰 인사했고, 월 부인은 뻣뻣하게 \"안녕하세요, 아가씨?\"라고 답했다."], ["smiled and nodded silently to Mary, and remained standing till the coughing should cease, and allow her uncle to notice her.", "그녀는 메리에게는 말없이 미소 지으며 고갯짓을 했고, 기침이 멎어 외삼촌이 자기를 알아볼 수 있을 때까지 선 채로 기다렸다."]], [["“Heyday, miss!”", "\"이런, 아가씨!\""], ["he said at last, “you have a fine color.", "마침내 그가 말했다. \"혈색이 참 좋구나."], ["Where’s Fred?”", "프레드는 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["“Seeing about the horses.", "\"말을 살피고 있어요."], ["He will be in presently.”", "곧 들어올 거예요.\""]], [["“Sit down, sit down.", "\"앉거라, 앉아."], ["Mrs. Waule, you’d better go.”", "월 부인, 그만 가 보는 게 좋겠어.\""]], [["Even those neighbors who had called Peter Featherstone an old fox, had never accused him of being insincerely polite, and his sister was quite used to the peculiar absence of ceremony with which he marked his sense of blood-relationship.", "피터 페더스톤을 늙은 여우라고 불렀던 이웃들조차도, 그가 마음에 없는 예의를 차린다고 비난한 적은 결코 없었다. 그의 누이도 그가 혈연관계임을 드러낼 때 보이는 그 특유의 무례함에 이미 익숙해져 있었다."], ["Indeed, she herself was accustomed to think that entire freedom from the necessity of behaving agreeably was included in the Almighty’s intentions about families.", "실제로 그녀 자신도, 가족 사이에서는 듣기 좋게 굴어야 할 의무로부터 완전히 자유로운 것이 전능하신 하느님께서 가족에 대해 품으신 뜻에 포함되어 있다고 여기는 데 익숙했다."], ["She rose slowly without any sign of resentment, and said in her usual muffled monotone, “Brother, I hope the new doctor will be able to do something for you.", "그녀는 아무런 분개의 기색도 없이 천천히 일어나, 여느 때의 둔탁하고 단조로운 목소리로 말했다. \"오빠, 새로 온 의사가 오빠한테 뭔가 도움이 될 수 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["Solomon says there’s great talk of his cleverness.", "솔로몬 오빠 말로는 그 사람이 똑똑하다고 소문이 자자하다더군요."], ["I’m sure it’s my wish you should be spared.", "나야 오빠가 오래 사시길 진심으로 바라지요."], ["And there’s none more ready to nurse you than your own sister and your own nieces, if you’d only say the word.", "그리고 한마디만 하시면, 오빠를 친동생과 친조카들만큼 정성껏 간병할 사람은 없어요."], ["There’s Rebecca, and Joanna, and Elizabeth, you know.”", "알다시피 리베카도 있고, 조애나도 있고, 엘리자베스도 있잖아요.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, I remember—you’ll see I’ve remembered ’em all—all dark and ugly.", "\"그래, 그래, 기억하지—내가 그 애들을 다 기억하고 있다는 걸 알게 될 게다—다들 거무튀튀하고 못생겼지."], ["They’d need have some money, eh?", "그 애들은 돈이라도 좀 있어야겠어, 안 그래?"], ["There never was any beauty in the women of our family; but the Featherstones have always had some money, and the Waules too.", "우리 집안 여자들한테 미모란 게 있었던 적이 없어. 하지만 페더스톤 집안은 늘 돈은 좀 있었고, 월 집안도 그랬지."], ["Waule had money too.", "월도 돈은 있었어."], ["A warm man was Waule.", "월은 잘사는 사람이었지. 그래, 그래."], ["Ay, ay; money’s a good egg; and if you’ve got money to leave behind you, lay it in a warm nest.", "돈이란 좋은 알이거든. 그리고 남길 돈이 있거든, 따뜻한 둥지에 넣어 두는 게 좋아."], ["Good-by, Mrs. Waule.”", "잘 가게, 월 부인.\""], ["Here Mr. Featherstone pulled at both sides of his wig as if he wanted to deafen himself, and his sister went away ruminating on this oracular speech of his.", "여기서 페더스톤 씨는 마치 제 귀를 먹먹하게 만들고 싶기라도 한 듯 가발을 양쪽으로 잡아당겼고, 그의 누이는 그 신탁 같은 말을 곱씹으며 떠나갔다."], ["Notwithstanding her jealousy of the Vincys and of Mary Garth, there remained as the nethermost sediment in her mental shallows a persuasion that her brother Peter Featherstone could never leave his chief property away from his blood-relations:—else, why had the Almighty carried off his two wives both childless, after he had gained so much by manganese and things, turning up when nobody expected it?—and why was there a Lowick parish church, and the Waules and Powderells all sitting in the same pew for generations, and the Featherstone pew next to them, if, the Sunday after her brother Peter’s death, everybody was to know that the property was gone out of the family?", "빈시 집안과 메리 가스에 대한 질투에도 불구하고, 그녀의 얕은 정신의 밑바닥에는 침전물처럼 한 가지 확신이 남아 있었으니, 곧 오빠 피터 페더스톤이 자기 주요 재산을 혈육에게서 멀리 떼어 놓을 리가 결코 없으리라는 것이었다. 그렇지 않다면, 그가 아무도 예상치 못했을 때 망간이며 이런저런 것들로 그토록 큰돈을 거머쥔 뒤에, 전능하신 하느님께서는 왜 그의 두 아내를 모두 자식 없이 데려가셨겠는가?"], ["The human mind has at no period accepted a moral chaos; and so preposterous a result was not strictly conceivable.", "또한 오빠 피터가 죽고 난 다음 일요일에, 그 재산이 집안 밖으로 새어 나가 버렸다는 사실을 모두가 알게 될 거라면, 로윅 교구 교회가 왜 있으며, 월 집안과 파우더렐 집안 사람들이 대대로 같은 신도석에 앉고, 그 옆에 페더스톤 집안 신도석이 왜 있겠는가? 인간의 정신은 어느 시대에도 도덕적 혼돈을 받아들인 적이 없으니, 그토록 터무니없는 결과란 도무지 상상조차 할 수 없는 일이었다."], ["But we are frightened at much that is not strictly conceivable.", "그러나 우리는 엄밀히 따지면 상상할 수도 없는 많은 것에 겁을 먹곤 한다."]], [["When Fred came in the old man eyed him with a peculiar twinkle, which the younger had often had reason to interpret as pride in the satisfactory details of his appearance.", "프레드가 들어서자 노인은 묘한 반짝임을 띤 눈으로 그를 훑어보았는데, 젊은이는 그것이 자기 외모의 흡족한 세부에 대한 자랑임을 종종 해석해 낼 수 있었다."]], [["“You two misses go away,”", "\"너희 두 아가씨는 가 있거라.\""], ["said Mr. Featherstone.", "페더스톤 씨가 말했다."], ["“I want to speak to Fred.”", "\"프레드하고 할 얘기가 있어.\""]], [["“Come into my room, Rosamond, you will not mind the cold for a little while,”", "\"내 방으로 가자, 로저먼드. 잠깐 추운 건 괜찮겠지.\""], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["The two girls had not only known each other in childhood, but had been at the same provincial school together (Mary as an articled pupil), so that they had many memories in common, and liked very well to talk in private.", "두 처녀는 어린 시절부터 알고 지냈을 뿐 아니라, 같은 지방 학교를 함께 다녔으니 (메리는 견습 학생이었다) 공유하는 추억이 많았고, 단둘이 이야기 나누기를 무척 좋아했다."], ["Indeed, this tête-à-tête was one of Rosamond’s objects in coming to Stone Court.", "실제로 이 단둘만의 대화야말로 로저먼드가 스톤 코트에 온 목적 가운데 하나였다."]], [["Old Featherstone would not begin the dialogue till the door had been closed.", "늙은 페더스톤은 문이 닫히기 전까지는 이야기를 꺼내려 하지 않았다."], ["He continued to look at Fred with the same twinkle and with one of his habitual grimaces, alternately screwing and widening his mouth; and when he spoke, it was in a low tone, which might be taken for that of an informer ready to be bought off, rather than for the tone of an offended senior.", "그는 여전히 그 반짝임을 띤 눈으로, 입을 번갈아 오므렸다 벌렸다 하는 버릇대로 얼굴을 찡그리며 프레드를 바라보았다. 그리고 입을 열었을 때, 그것은 낮은 어조였으니, 모욕당한 손윗사람의 어조라기보다는 차라리 돈을 받고 입을 다물 준비가 된 밀고자의 어조로 들릴 만했다."], ["He was not a man to feel any strong moral indignation even on account of trespasses against himself.", "그는 자기에게 가해진 잘못에 대해서조차 강한 도덕적 분노 따위는 느끼지 못하는 사람이었다."], ["It was natural that others should want to get an advantage over him, but then, he was a little too cunning for them.", "남들이 자기에게서 이득을 취하려 드는 것이야 당연한 일이고, 다만 그 자신이 그들보다 약간 더 교활할 따름이었다."]], [["“So, sir, you’ve been paying ten per cent for money which you’ve promised to pay off by mortgaging my land when I’m dead and gone, eh?", "\"그래, 이 녀석아, 내가 죽고 나면 내 땅을 저당 잡혀서 갚겠다고 약속하고 돈을 빌리면서 10퍼센트 이자를 물고 있다지, 응?"], ["You put my life at a twelvemonth, say.", "내 목숨을 일 년짜리로 쳐 놓고 말이야."], ["But I can alter my will yet.”", "하지만 난 아직 유언장을 바꿀 수 있어.\""]], [["Fred blushed.", "프레드는 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["He had not borrowed money in that way, for excellent reasons.", "그는 그런 식으로 돈을 빌린 적이 없었으니, 그럴 만한 더없이 타당한 이유가 있었다."], ["But he was conscious of having spoken with some confidence (perhaps with more than he exactly remembered) about his prospect of getting Featherstone’s land as a future means of paying present debts.", "그러나 그는 페더스톤의 땅을 손에 넣어 그것을 현재의 빚을 갚을 미래의 수단으로 삼을 전망에 대해 어느 정도 자신만만하게 (어쩌면 자기가 정확히 기억하는 것보다 더) 떠벌렸던 것을 의식하고 있었다."]], [["“I don’t know what you refer to, sir.", "\"무엇을 말씀하시는 건지 모르겠습니다, 외삼촌."], ["I have certainly never borrowed any money on such an insecurity.", "저는 분명 그런 불확실한 담보로 돈을 빌린 적이 없습니다."], ["Please do explain.”", "부디 설명해 주세요.\""]], [["“No, sir, it’s you must explain.", "\"아니지, 이놈아, 설명해야 할 사람은 너야."], ["I can alter my will yet, let me tell you.", "다시 말하지만, 난 아직 유언장을 바꿀 수 있어."], ["I’m of sound mind—can reckon compound interest in my head, and remember every fool’s name as well as I could twenty years ago.", "난 정신이 멀쩡하다고—머릿속으로 복리 계산도 할 수 있고, 어떤 바보 녀석 이름이든 이십 년 전만큼이나 또렷이 기억해."], ["What the deuce?", "대체 뭔 소리야?"], ["I’m under eighty.", "난 아직 여든도 안 됐어."], ["I say, you must contradict this story.”", "분명히 말하는데, 이 이야기를 부인해 봐.\""]], [["“I have contradicted it, sir,”", "\"이미 부인했습니다, 외삼촌.\""], ["Fred answered, with a touch of impatience, not remembering that his uncle did not verbally discriminate contradicting from disproving, though no one was further from confounding the two ideas than old Featherstone, who often wondered that so many fools took his own assertions for proofs.", "프레드가 약간 짜증 섞인 기색으로 대답했는데, 그는 외삼촌이 부인하는 것과 반증하는 것을 말로는 구별하지 않는다는 사실을 미처 떠올리지 못했다. 사실 누구도 그 두 개념을 늙은 페더스톤만큼 혼동하지 않는 사람은 없었으니, 그는 그토록 많은 바보들이 자기 주장을 증거로 받아들이는 것을 종종 의아하게 여기곤 했다."], ["“But I contradict it again.", "\"하지만 다시 한번 부인하겠습니다."], ["The story is a silly lie.”", "그 이야기는 어리석은 거짓말이에요.\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"말도 안 되는 소리!"], ["you must bring dockiments.", "증서를 가져와야지."], ["It comes from authority.”", "그건 확실한 출처에서 나온 거란 말이야.\""]], [["“Name the authority, and make him name the man of whom I borrowed the money, and then I can disprove the story.”", "\"그 출처가 누군지 대시고, 그 사람한테 제가 돈을 빌렸다는 그 상대가 누군지 대게 하세요. 그러면 제가 그 이야기를 반증할 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“It’s pretty good authority, I think—a man who knows most of what goes on in Middlemarch.", "\"꽤나 믿을 만한 출처지, 내 생각엔—미들마치에서 벌어지는 일은 거의 다 아는 사람이거든."], ["It’s that fine, religious, charitable uncle o’ yours.", "바로 그 잘나고 신앙심 깊고 자선 잘하는 네 외삼촌이야."], ["Come now!” Here Mr.", "자, 어떠냐!\""], ["Featherstone had his peculiar inward shake which signified merriment.", "여기서 페더스톤 씨는 흥겨움을 뜻하는 그 특유의 속으로 떠는 몸짓을 했다."]], [["“Mr. Bulstrode?”", "\"벌스트로드 외삼촌요?\""]], [["“Who else, eh?”", "\"그럼 누구겠어, 응?\""]], [["“Then the story has grown into this lie out of some sermonizing words he may have let fall about me.", "\"그렇다면 그 이야기는 외삼촌이 저에 대해 설교조로 흘렸을 법한 몇 마디 말에서 부풀려져 이런 거짓말이 된 거예요."], ["Do they pretend that he named the man who lent me the money?”", "그분이 저한테 돈을 빌려준 사람의 이름을 댔다고 그러기라도 하던가요?\""]], [["“If there is such a man, depend upon it Bulstrode knows him.", "\"그런 사람이 있다면, 벌스트로드가 분명히 알 거다."], ["But, supposing you only tried to get the money lent, and didn’t get it—Bulstrode ’ud know that too.", "하지만 네가 돈을 빌리려고만 했고 실제로는 못 빌렸다 쳐도—벌스트로드는 그것도 알 게야."], ["You bring me a writing from Bulstrode to say he doesn’t believe you’ve ever promised to pay your debts out o’ my land.", "벌스트로드한테서, 네가 내 땅으로 빚을 갚겠다고 약속한 적이 있다는 걸 자기는 믿지 않는다는 글을 한 장 받아 와."], ["Come now!”", "자, 어떠냐!\""]], [["Mr. Featherstone’s face required its whole scale of grimaces as a muscular outlet to his silent triumph in the soundness of his faculties.", "페더스톤 씨의 얼굴은, 자기 머리가 멀쩡하다는 데서 오는 말 없는 승리감을 근육으로 풀어내느라 온갖 찡그림을 총동원해야 했다."]], [["Fred felt himself to be in a disgusting dilemma.", "프레드는 자기가 역겨운 진퇴양난에 빠졌음을 느꼈다."]], [["“You must be joking, sir. Mr.", "\"농담하시는 거지요, 외삼촌."], ["Bulstrode, like other men, believes scores of things that are not true, and he has a prejudice against me.", "벌스트로드 씨도 다른 사람들처럼 사실이 아닌 것을 수두룩하게 믿고 있고, 게다가 저에 대해서는 편견을 갖고 있어요."], ["I could easily get him to write that he knew no facts in proof of the report you speak of, though it might lead to unpleasantness.", "외삼촌이 말씀하신 그 소문을 입증할 만한 사실을 자기는 전혀 모른다고 그분이 써 주게 하는 거야 쉽게 할 수 있겠지요. 비록 불쾌한 일로 번질 수는 있겠지만요."], ["But I could hardly ask him to write down what he believes or does not believe about me.”", "하지만 저에 대해 그분이 무엇을 믿고 안 믿는지를 적어 달라고 부탁하기는 도무지 어렵습니다.\""], ["Fred paused an instant, and then added, in politic appeal to his uncle’s vanity, “That is hardly a thing for a gentleman to ask.”", "프레드는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가, 외삼촌의 허영심에 영리하게 호소하며 덧붙였다. \"그런 건 신사가 부탁할 만한 일이 아니지요.\""], ["But he was disappointed in the result.", "그러나 그는 그 결과에 실망하고 말았다."]], [["“Ay, I know what you mean.", "\"그래, 네 속셈은 알겠다."], ["You’d sooner offend me than Bulstrode.", "너는 벌스트로드보다 나를 거스르는 게 더 낫다는 거지."], ["And what’s he?—he’s got no land hereabout that ever I heard tell of.", "그자가 뭐냐?—내가 들은 바로는 이 근방에 땅 한 뙈기도 없는 자야."], ["A speckilating fellow!", "투기꾼 같은 녀석이지!"], ["He may come down any day, when the devil leaves off backing him.", "악마가 뒤를 봐주던 걸 그만두는 날엔 언제든 거꾸러질 수 있어."], ["And that’s what his religion means: he wants God A’mighty to come in.", "그자의 신앙이란 게 바로 그런 거야. 전능하신 하느님께서 끼어들어 주시기를 바라는 거지."], ["That’s nonsense!", "그건 다 헛소리야!"], ["There’s one thing I made out pretty clear when I used to go to church—and it’s this: God A’mighty sticks to the land.", "내가 교회 다닐 적에 한 가지는 꽤 분명하게 깨달은 게 있는데, 그건 이거다. 전능하신 하느님은 땅에 매달리신다는 거야."], ["He promises land, and He gives land, and He makes chaps rich with corn and cattle.", "그분은 땅을 약속하시고, 땅을 주시며, 곡식과 가축으로 사람들을 부자로 만드시지."], ["But you take the other side.", "그런데 너는 다른 편을 택하는 거다."], ["You like Bulstrode and speckilation better than Featherstone and land.”", "너는 페더스톤과 땅보다 벌스트로드와 투기를 더 좋아하는 거지.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, sir,”", "\"죄송하지만요, 외삼촌.\""], ["said Fred, rising, standing with his back to the fire and beating his boot with his whip.", "프레드가 일어서서 불을 등지고 서서는 채찍으로 장화를 탁탁 치며 말했다."], ["“I like neither Bulstrode nor speculation.”", "\"저는 벌스트로드도 투기도 좋아하지 않습니다.\""], ["He spoke rather sulkily, feeling himself stalemated.", "그는 자기가 옴짝달싹 못하게 됐다는 느낌에 다소 뚱한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Well, well, you can do without me, that’s pretty clear,”", "\"흠, 흠, 너는 나 없이도 잘해 나갈 수 있겠지, 그건 아주 분명하구나.\""], ["said old Featherstone, secretly disliking the possibility that Fred would show himself at all independent.", "늙은 페더스톤이 말했는데, 프레드가 조금이라도 독립적인 태도를 보일 가능성이 내심 못마땅했던 것이다."], ["“You neither want a bit of land to make a squire of you instead of a starving parson, nor a lift of a hundred pound by the way.", "\"너는 굶주리는 목사 대신 시골 지주가 되게 해 줄 땅 한 뙈기도 원하지 않고, 그 김에 백 파운드쯤 거들어 주는 것도 원하지 않는다는 거지."], ["It’s all one to me.", "나야 아무래도 상관없다."], ["I can make five codicils if I like, and I shall keep my bank-notes for a nest-egg.", "내키면 유언 보충서를 다섯 장이라도 만들 수 있고, 내 은행권은 비상금으로 챙겨 둘 테니까."], ["It’s all one to me.”", "나야 아무래도 상관없어.\""]], [["Fred colored again.", "프레드는 다시 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["Featherstone had rarely given him presents of money, and at this moment it seemed almost harder to part with the immediate prospect of bank-notes than with the more distant prospect of the land.", "페더스톤은 그에게 돈을 선물로 준 적이 거의 없었는데, 바로 이 순간 그 당장의 은행권 전망을 포기하는 것이 더 먼 땅에 대한 전망을 포기하는 것보다 거의 더 힘들게 느껴졌다."]], [["“I am not ungrateful, sir.", "\"저는 은혜를 모르는 사람이 아닙니다, 외삼촌."], ["I never meant to show disregard for any kind intentions you might have towards me.", "외삼촌이 저에게 품으셨을지 모를 그 어떤 호의에도 무관심한 태도를 보일 생각은 결코 없었어요."], ["On the contrary.”", "오히려 그 반대지요.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"좋아."], ["Then prove it.", "그럼 그걸 증명해 봐."], ["You bring me a letter from Bulstrode saying he doesn’t believe you’ve been cracking and promising to pay your debts out o’ my land, and then, if there’s any scrape you’ve got into, we’ll see if I can’t back you a bit.", "벌스트로드한테서, 네가 내 땅으로 빚을 갚겠다고 떠벌리며 약속했다는 걸 자기는 믿지 않는다는 편지를 받아 와. 그러고 나서 네가 무슨 곤경에 빠진 게 있거든, 내가 너를 좀 도와줄 수 있을지 어디 보자꾸나."], ["Come now!", "자, 어떠냐!"], ["That’s a bargain.", "이건 약속이다."], ["Here, give me your arm.", "자, 내 팔을 잡거라."], ["I’ll try and walk round the room.”", "방 안을 한 바퀴 걸어 보마.\""]], [["Fred, in spite of his irritation, had kindness enough in him to be a little sorry for the unloved, unvenerated old man, who with his dropsical legs looked more than usually pitiable in walking.", "프레드는 짜증이 났음에도, 사랑받지도 존경받지도 못하는 그 노인이 부종이 든 다리로 걸을 때 여느 때보다 더 가련해 보이는 것을 안쓰러워할 만큼의 다정함은 마음속에 지니고 있었다. 그는 팔을 내주면서, 자기는 체질이 무너져 가는 늙은이가 되고 싶지는 않다고 생각했다."], ["While giving his arm, he thought that he should not himself like to be an old fellow with his constitution breaking up; and he waited good-temperedly, first before the window to hear the wonted remarks about the guinea-fowls and the weather-cock, and then before the scanty book-shelves, of which the chief glories in dark calf were Josephus, Culpepper, Klopstock’s “Messiah,” and several volumes of the “Gentleman’s Magazine.”", "그리고 그는 좋은 기분으로 기다려 주었으니, 먼저 창가에서 호로새와 풍향계에 관한 늘 듣던 말을 들어 주었고, 그다음에는 빈약한 책장 앞에 섰는데, 그 검은 송아지 가죽 표지의 주된 자랑거리는 요세푸스, 컬페퍼, 클롭슈토크의 『메시아』, 그리고 『신사의 잡지』 몇 권이었다."]], [["“Read me the names o’ the books.", "\"책 제목들을 읽어 보거라."], ["Come now!", "자, 어서!"], ["you’re a college man.”", "너야 대학 다닌 사람이니까.\""]], [["Fred gave him the titles.", "프레드는 그에게 제목들을 읽어 주었다."]], [["“What did missy want with more books?", "\"저 아가씨가 책을 더 가져다 뭣에 쓴다는 게냐?"], ["What must you be bringing her more books for?”", "무엇 하러 그 애한테 책을 더 가져다주는 거야?\""]], [["“They amuse her, sir.", "\"책이 그 애한테 즐거움을 주니까요, 외삼촌."], ["She is very fond of reading.”", "메리는 독서를 무척 좋아해요.\""]], [["“A little too fond,”", "\"좀 지나치게 좋아하지.\""], ["said Mr. Featherstone, captiously.", "페더스톤 씨가 트집을 잡듯 말했다."], ["“She was for reading when she sat with me.", "\"그 애는 나랑 같이 앉아 있을 때도 책을 읽으려 들더구나."], ["But I put a stop to that.", "하지만 내가 그걸 못 하게 했지."], ["She’s got the newspaper to read out loud.", "그 애한테는 큰 소리로 읽어 줄 신문이 있어."], ["That’s enough for one day, I should think.", "그것만 해도 하루치로는 충분하다고 본다."], ["I can’t abide to see her reading to herself.", "난 그 애가 혼자서 책 읽는 꼴은 도무지 못 봐주겠어."], ["You mind and not bring her any more books, do you hear?”", "명심하고 그 애한테 다시는 책을 가져다주지 마라, 알겠느냐?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, I hear.”", "\"네, 외삼촌, 알겠습니다.\""], ["Fred had received this order before, and had secretly disobeyed it.", "프레드는 전에도 이 명령을 받은 적이 있었고, 몰래 그것을 어겼었다."], ["He intended to disobey it again.", "그는 다시 어길 작정이었다."]], [["“Ring the bell,” said Mr.", "\"종을 울려라.\""], ["Featherstone; “I want missy to come down.”", "페더스톤 씨가 말했다. \"저 아가씨더러 내려오라고 해야겠다.\""]], [["Rosamond and Mary had been talking faster than their male friends.", "로저먼드와 메리는 그들의 남자 친구들보다 더 빠르게 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["They did not think of sitting down, but stood at the toilet-table near the window while Rosamond took off her hat, adjusted her veil, and applied little touches of her finger-tips to her hair—hair of infantine fairness, neither flaxen nor yellow.", "그들은 앉을 생각도 없이, 창가 가까이 화장대 앞에 서 있었고, 그동안 로저먼드는 모자를 벗고 베일을 매만지며 손끝으로 머리를 살짝살짝 다듬었으니—아마빛도 노란빛도 아닌, 어린아이처럼 환한 머리카락이었다."], ["Mary Garth seemed all the plainer standing at an angle between the two nymphs—the one in the glass, and the one out of it, who looked at each other with eyes of heavenly blue, deep enough to hold the most exquisite meanings an ingenious beholder could put into them, and deep enough to hide the meanings of the owner if these should happen to be less exquisite.", "메리 가스는 두 요정 사이에 비스듬히 서 있어 더욱더 평범해 보였다. 한 요정은 거울 속에, 또 한 요정은 거울 밖에 있었고, 그들은 천상의 푸른빛 눈으로 서로를 바라보았으니, 그 눈은 재간 있는 보는 이가 거기에 담을 수 있는 가장 절묘한 의미들을 다 품을 만큼 깊었고, 또한 그 주인의 의미가 혹시라도 덜 절묘한 것일 경우 그것을 감추어 줄 만큼도 깊었다."], ["Only a few children in Middlemarch looked blond by the side of Rosamond, and the slim figure displayed by her riding-habit had delicate undulations.", "미들마치에서 로저먼드 곁에서 금발로 보일 아이는 몇 안 되었고, 그녀의 승마복이 드러내는 호리호리한 자태는 섬세한 굴곡을 지니고 있었다."], ["In fact, most men in Middlemarch, except her brothers, held that Miss Vincy was the best girl in the world, and some called her an angel.", "사실 미들마치의 남자들은, 그녀의 오빠들을 빼면 대부분 빈시 양이야말로 세상에서 가장 멋진 처녀라고 여겼고, 더러는 그녀를 천사라고 부르기도 했다."], ["Mary Garth, on the contrary, had the aspect of an ordinary sinner: she was brown; her curly dark hair was rough and stubborn; her stature was low; and it would not be true to declare, in satisfactory antithesis, that she had all the virtues.", "반면에 메리 가스는 평범한 죄인의 모습을 하고 있었으니, 살결이 거무스름했고, 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리카락은 거칠고 뻣뻣했으며, 키는 작았다. 그렇다고 흡족한 대조를 이루기 위해 그녀가 온갖 미덕을 다 갖추었다고 단언하는 것도 사실은 아닐 것이다."], ["Plainness has its peculiar temptations and vices quite as much as beauty; it is apt either to feign amiability, or, not feigning it, to show all the repulsiveness of discontent: at any rate, to be called an ugly thing in contrast with that lovely creature your companion, is apt to produce some effect beyond a sense of fine veracity and fitness in the phrase.", "평범함에도 아름다움 못지않게 그 나름의 독특한 유혹과 악덕이 따르는 법이니, 평범함은 상냥함을 꾸며 내기 쉽고, 그러지 않을 경우에는 불만의 온갖 혐오스러움을 다 드러내기 쉽다. 어쨌든 곁에 있는 그 사랑스러운 존재와 대비되어 못생긴 것이라 불리는 일은, 그 표현 속의 정밀한 진실성과 적절함을 느끼는 것 이상의 어떤 영향을 낳기 쉬운 법이다."], ["At the age of two-and-twenty Mary had certainly not attained that perfect good sense and good principle which are usually recommended to the less fortunate girl, as if they were to be obtained in quantities ready mixed, with a flavor of resignation as required.", "스물두 살의 나이에 메리는 분명, 덜 운 좋은 처녀에게 흔히 권해지는 그 완벽한 분별력과 훌륭한 원칙을 갖추지는 못했으니, 마치 그것들이 미리 적당히 배합되어, 필요에 따라 체념의 풍미까지 곁들여진 채로 얻어질 수 있기라도 한 듯이 말이다."], ["Her shrewdness had a streak of satiric bitterness continually renewed and never carried utterly out of sight, except by a strong current of gratitude towards those who, instead of telling her that she ought to be contented, did something to make her so.", "그녀의 영민함에는 끊임없이 되살아나는 풍자적 신랄함의 한 줄기가 깔려 있었고, 그것은 결코 완전히 자취를 감추는 법이 없었으니, 다만 그녀에게 만족해야 마땅하다고 말하는 대신 그녀를 만족시키기 위해 무언가를 해 주는 이들을 향한 강한 감사의 물결만이 그것을 가려 줄 뿐이었다."], ["Advancing womanhood had tempered her plainness, which was of a good human sort, such as the mothers of our race have very commonly worn in all latitudes under a more or less becoming headgear.", "성숙해 가는 여성다움이 그녀의 평범함을 누그러뜨려 주었으니, 그것은 우리 인류의 어머니들이 어느 위도에서나 다소 어울리는 머릿수건 아래 흔히 지녀 온, 인간미 넘치는 종류의 평범함이었다."], ["Rembrandt would have painted her with pleasure, and would have made her broad features look out of the canvas with intelligent honesty.", "렘브란트라면 기꺼이 그녀를 그렸을 것이고, 그 넓적한 이목구비가 지적인 정직함을 띠고 화폭 밖을 내다보도록 만들었을 것이다."], ["For honesty, truth-telling fairness, was Mary’s reigning virtue: she neither tried to create illusions, nor indulged in them for her own behoof, and when she was in a good mood she had humor enough in her to laugh at herself.", "정직함, 곧 진실을 말하는 올곧음이야말로 메리를 지배하는 미덕이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 환상을 만들어 내려 하지도 않았고, 자기 잇속을 위해 환상에 빠지지도 않았으며, 기분이 좋을 때면 자기 자신을 비웃을 만큼의 유머도 충분히 지니고 있었다."], ["When she and Rosamond happened both to be reflected in the glass, she said, laughingly—", "그녀와 로저먼드가 거울에 함께 비친 순간, 그녀가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“What a brown patch I am by the side of you, Rosy!", "\"내가 네 옆에 있으니 얼마나 거무튀튀한 얼룩 같은지, 로지!"], ["You are the most unbecoming companion.”", "너야말로 가장 어울리지 않는 길동무야.\""]], [["“Oh no!", "\"어머, 아니야!"], ["No one thinks of your appearance, you are so sensible and useful, Mary.", "아무도 네 외모 같은 건 생각하지 않아. 너는 그렇게 분별 있고 쓸모 있는 사람이잖아, 메리."], ["Beauty is of very little consequence in reality,”", "미모란 사실 거의 아무 중요성도 없는걸.\""], ["said Rosamond, turning her head towards Mary, but with eyes swerving towards the new view of her neck in the glass.", "로저먼드가 메리 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 말했는데, 그 눈길은 거울 속에 새로 비친 자기 목덜미 쪽으로 슬며시 빗겨 가고 있었다."]], [["“You mean my beauty,”", "\"내 미모 말이구나.\""], ["said Mary, rather sardonically.", "메리가 다소 빈정대듯 말했다."]], [["Rosamond thought, “Poor Mary, she takes the kindest things ill.”", "로저먼드는 생각했다. '가엾은 메리, 더없이 다정한 말도 나쁘게 받아들이다니.'"], ["Aloud she said, “What have you been doing lately?”", "소리 내어서는 이렇게 말했다. \"요즘은 뭘 하면서 지내?\""]], [["“I?", "\"나?"], ["Oh, minding the house—pouring out syrup—pretending to be amiable and contented—learning to have a bad opinion of everybody.”", "아, 집안일을 돌보고—시럽을 따라 주고—상냥하고 만족스러운 척하고—모든 사람을 나쁘게 보는 법을 배우고 있지.\""]], [["“It is a wretched life for you.”", "\"너한테는 비참한 삶이구나.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["said Mary, curtly, with a little toss of her head.", "메리가 고개를 살짝 젖히며 퉁명스레 말했다."], ["“I think my life is pleasanter than your Miss Morgan’s.”", "\"내 삶은 너희 집 모건 양의 삶보다는 즐겁다고 생각해.\""]], [["“Yes; but Miss Morgan is so uninteresting, and not young.”", "\"그렇긴 해. 하지만 모건 양은 너무 재미없고, 게다가 젊지도 않잖아.\""]], [["“She is interesting to herself, I suppose; and I am not at all sure that everything gets easier as one gets older.”", "\"본인한테는 자기가 재미있겠지. 그리고 나이가 든다고 모든 게 더 쉬워지는 건지는 나도 전혀 모르겠어.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Rosamond, reflectively; “one wonders what such people do, without any prospect.", "로저먼드가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"아무 전망도 없이 그런 사람들이 뭘 하며 사는지 궁금하긴 해."], ["To be sure, there is religion as a support.", "하긴, 종교라는 버팀목이 있긴 하지."], ["But,”", "하지만,\""], ["she added, dimpling, “it is very different with you, Mary.", "그녀가 보조개를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"너는 사정이 아주 다르잖아, 메리."], ["You may have an offer.”", "너는 청혼을 받을 수도 있으니까.\""]], [["“Has any one told you he means to make me one?”", "\"누가 나한테 청혼하겠다고 너한테 말이라도 했어?\""]], [["“Of course not.", "\"그럴 리가."], ["I mean, there is a gentleman who may fall in love with you, seeing you almost every day.”", "내 말은, 너를 거의 날마다 보면서 너에게 반할지도 모르는 신사 한 분이 있다는 거지.\""]], [["A certain change in Mary’s face was chiefly determined by the resolve not to show any change.", "메리의 얼굴에 어떤 변화가 일었는데, 그것은 무엇보다도 어떤 변화도 내보이지 않겠다는 결심에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["“Does that always make people fall in love?”", "\"그렇게 본다고 사람들이 늘 사랑에 빠지나?\""], ["she answered, carelessly; “it seems to me quite as often a reason for detesting each other.”", "그녀가 아무렇지 않게 대답했다. \"내가 보기엔 오히려 그만큼 자주 서로를 싫어하게 되는 이유가 되던데.\""]], [["“Not when they are interesting and agreeable.", "\"재미있고 호감 가는 사람이라면 그렇지 않지."], ["I hear that Mr. Lydgate is both.”", "듣자 하니 리드게이트 씨가 그 두 가지를 다 갖췄다더라.\""]], [["“Oh, Mr. Lydgate!”", "\"아, 리드게이트 씨.\""], ["said Mary, with an unmistakable lapse into indifference.", "메리가 분명한 무관심으로 어조를 떨어뜨리며 말했다."], ["“You want to know something about him,”", "\"그 사람에 대해 뭔가 알고 싶은 거구나.\""], ["she added, not choosing to indulge Rosamond’s indirectness.", "그녀는 로저먼드의 에두르는 방식에 장단을 맞춰 줄 마음이 없어서 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“Merely, how you like him.”", "\"그냥, 네가 그 사람을 어떻게 생각하는지.\""]], [["“There is no question of liking at present.", "\"지금으로선 좋고 말고 할 게 없어."], ["My liking always wants some little kindness to kindle it.", "내가 누군가를 좋아하려면 늘 약간의 친절이 그 마음에 불을 붙여 줘야 하거든."], ["I am not magnanimous enough to like people who speak to me without seeming to see me.”", "나를 보는 것 같지도 않게 말을 거는 사람을 좋아할 만큼 나는 너그럽지 못해.\""]], [["“Is he so haughty?”", "\"그렇게 거만한가?\""], ["said Rosamond, with heightened satisfaction.", "로저먼드가 한층 흡족해하며 말했다."], ["“You know that he is of good family?”", "\"그 사람이 좋은 집안 출신이라는 거 알아?\""]], [["“No; he did not give that as a reason.”", "\"아니. 그 사람이 그걸 이유로 댄 건 아니야.\""]], [["“Mary!", "\"메리!"], ["you are the oddest girl.", "너는 정말 별난 애야."], ["But what sort of looking man is he?", "그런데 그 사람 생김새는 어때?"], ["Describe him to me.”", "나한테 묘사해 줘 봐.\""]], [["“How can one describe a man?", "\"사람을 어떻게 묘사해?"], ["I can give you an inventory: heavy eyebrows, dark eyes, a straight nose, thick dark hair, large solid white hands—and—let me see—oh, an exquisite cambric pocket-handkerchief.", "목록이라면 줄 수 있지. 짙은 눈썹, 검은 눈, 곧은 코, 숱 많은 검은 머리, 크고 단단한 흰 손, 그리고—어디 보자—아, 더없이 고운 무명 손수건."], ["But you will see him.", "하지만 네가 직접 보게 될 거야."], ["You know this is about the time of his visits.”", "알다시피 지금쯤이 그 사람이 방문하는 시간이거든.\""]], [["Rosamond blushed a little, but said, meditatively, “I rather like a haughty manner.", "로저먼드는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"나는 거만한 태도가 오히려 좋아."], ["I cannot endure a rattling young man.”", "시끄럽게 떠벌리는 젊은 남자는 견딜 수가 없거든.\""]], [["“I did not tell you that Mr.", "\"리드게이트 씨가 거만하다고 한 건 아니야."], ["Lydgate was haughty; but il y en a pour tous les goûts, as little Mamselle used to say, and if any girl can choose the particular sort of conceit she would like, I should think it is you, Rosy.”", "하지만 마드무아젤 선생님이 늘 말하셨듯이, 취향이란 사람마다 다 다른 법이지. 그리고 어떤 처녀든 자기가 좋아할 특정한 종류의 자만을 고를 수 있다면, 그건 바로 너일 거라고 생각해, 로지.\""]], [["“Haughtiness is not conceit; I call Fred conceited.”", "\"거만함은 자만이 아니야. 자만이라면 나는 프레드를 두고 말하겠어.\""]], [["“I wish no one said any worse of him.", "\"아무도 그 애를 그보다 더 나쁘게 말하지 않으면 좋으련만."], ["He should be more careful.", "그 앤 좀 더 조심해야 해."], ["Mrs. Waule has been telling uncle that Fred is very unsteady.”", "월 부인이 외삼촌한테, 프레드가 아주 행실이 헤프다고 말하던걸.\""], ["Mary spoke from a girlish impulse which got the better of her judgment.", "메리는 분별을 앞지른 처녀다운 충동에서 이렇게 말했다."], ["There was a vague uneasiness associated with the word “unsteady” which she hoped Rosamond might say something to dissipate.", "\"헤프다\"는 말에는 어떤 막연한 불안이 얽혀 있었고, 그녀는 로저먼드가 무슨 말이든 해서 그것을 떨쳐 주기를 바랐다."], ["But she purposely abstained from mentioning Mrs. Waule’s more special insinuation.", "그러나 그녀는 월 부인의 더 특정한 암시는 일부러 입에 올리지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, Fred is horrid!”", "\"어머, 프레드는 정말 못 말려!\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["She would not have allowed herself so unsuitable a word to any one but Mary.", "그녀는 메리가 아닌 다른 누구에게는 그토록 어울리지 않는 말을 결코 허용하지 않았을 것이다."]], [["“What do you mean by horrid?”", "\"못 말린다는 게 무슨 뜻이야?\""]], [["“He is so idle, and makes papa so angry, and says he will not take orders.”", "\"그렇게 게으르고, 아빠를 그렇게 화나게 하고, 게다가 성직도 받지 않겠다고 하니까.\""]], [["“I think Fred is quite right.”", "\"내 생각엔 프레드가 아주 옳아.\""]], [["“How can you say he is quite right, Mary?", "\"어떻게 그 애가 아주 옳다고 할 수 있어, 메리?"], ["I thought you had more sense of religion.”", "난 네가 좀 더 신앙심이 있는 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["“He is not fit to be a clergyman.”", "\"그 앤 성직자가 되기에 적합하지 않아.\""]], [["“But he ought to be fit.”—“Well, then, he is not what he ought to be.", "\"하지만 적합해야 하잖아.\"—\"그럼, 그렇다면 그 애는 마땅히 되어야 할 사람이 못 되는 거지."], ["I know some other people who are in the same case.”", "나는 그것과 똑같은 처지에 있는 다른 사람들도 좀 알고 있어.\""]], [["“But no one approves of them.", "\"하지만 아무도 그 사람들을 좋게 보지 않잖아."], ["I should not like to marry a clergyman; but there must be clergymen.”", "나라면 성직자랑 결혼하긴 싫지만, 그래도 성직자는 있어야 하는걸.\""]], [["“It does not follow that Fred must be one.”", "\"그렇다고 해서 프레드가 꼭 그중 하나여야 하는 건 아니지.\""]], [["“But when papa has been at the expense of educating him for it!", "\"하지만 아빠가 그걸 위해 돈을 들여 교육까지 시켰는데!"], ["And only suppose, if he should have no fortune left him?”", "게다가 한번 생각해 봐, 만약 그 애한테 물려받을 재산이 한 푼도 없다면?\""]], [["“I can suppose that very well,”", "\"그거야 아주 쉽게 상상이 되네.\""], ["said Mary, dryly.", "메리가 무덤덤하게 말했다."]], [["“Then I wonder you can defend Fred,”", "\"그럼 어떻게 네가 프레드를 두둔할 수 있는지 모르겠어.\""], ["said Rosamond, inclined to push this point.", "로저먼드가 이 점을 밀어붙이고 싶어 하며 말했다."]], [["“I don’t defend him,”", "\"두둔하는 게 아니야.\""], ["said Mary, laughing; “I would defend any parish from having him for a clergyman.”", "메리가 웃으며 말했다. \"어느 교구든 그 애를 성직자로 두지 않도록 지켜 주고 싶을 뿐이지.\""]], [["“But of course if he were a clergyman, he must be different.”", "\"하지만 물론 그 애가 성직자가 된다면야 달라지겠지.\""]], [["“Yes, he would be a great hypocrite; and he is not that yet.”", "\"그래, 대단한 위선자가 되겠지. 그리고 그 앤 아직 그렇지는 않으니까.\""]], [["“It is of no use saying anything to you, Mary.", "\"너한테는 무슨 말을 해도 소용없어, 메리."], ["You always take Fred’s part.”", "너는 늘 프레드 편만 들잖아.\""]], [["“Why should I not take his part?”", "\"내가 왜 그 애 편을 들면 안 되지?\""], ["said Mary, lighting up.", "메리가 발끈하며 말했다."], ["“He would take mine.", "\"그 앤 내 편을 들어 줄 테니까."], ["He is the only person who takes the least trouble to oblige me.”", "그 애야말로 나를 위해 조금이라도 수고를 해 주는 유일한 사람이거든.\""]], [["“You make me feel very uncomfortable, Mary,”", "\"너 때문에 정말 마음이 불편해, 메리.\""], ["said Rosamond, with her gravest mildness; “I would not tell mamma for the world.”", "로저먼드가 더없이 진지한 온화함으로 말했다. \"엄마한테는 절대로 말 안 할게.\""]], [["“What would you not tell her?”", "\"뭘 말 안 한다는 거야?\""], ["said Mary, angrily.", "메리가 성을 내며 말했다."]], [["“Pray do not go into a rage, Mary,”", "\"제발 화내지 마, 메리.\""], ["said Rosamond, mildly as ever.", "로저먼드가 여전히 온화하게 말했다."]], [["“If your mamma is afraid that Fred will make me an offer, tell her that I would not marry him if he asked me.", "\"네 엄마가 프레드가 나한테 청혼할까 봐 걱정하는 거라면, 설령 그 애가 청혼한다 해도 나는 그 애랑 결혼하지 않을 거라고 전해."], ["But he is not going to do so, that I am aware.", "하지만 내가 아는 한 그 앤 그러지도 않을 거야."], ["He certainly never has asked me.”", "분명 그 앤 나한테 청혼한 적도 없으니까.\""]], [["“Mary, you are always so violent.”", "\"메리, 너는 늘 그렇게 격하다니까.\""]], [["“And you are always so exasperating.”", "\"그리고 너는 늘 그렇게 사람 약을 올리고.\""]], [["“I?", "\"내가?"], ["What can you blame me for?”", "내가 뭘 잘못했다고?\""]], [["“Oh, blameless people are always the most exasperating.", "\"아, 흠잡을 데 없는 사람들이 늘 가장 약을 올리는 법이지."], ["There is the bell—I think we must go down.”", "종이 울리네—이제 내려가야 할 것 같아.\""]], [["“I did not mean to quarrel,”", "\"싸우려던 건 아니었어.\""], ["said Rosamond, putting on her hat.", "로저먼드가 모자를 쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Quarrel?", "\"싸워?"], ["Nonsense; we have not quarrelled.", "말도 안 돼. 우리는 싸운 적 없어."], ["If one is not to get into a rage sometimes, what is the good of being friends?”", "가끔 발끈하지도 못한다면, 친구인 게 무슨 소용이야?\""]], [["“Am I to repeat what you have said?”", "\"네가 한 말을 내가 옮겨도 돼?\""]], [["“Just as you please.", "\"좋을 대로 해."], ["I never say what I am afraid of having repeated.", "나는 옮겨질까 봐 두려운 말 같은 건 절대 하지 않으니까."], ["But let us go down.”", "그럼 이제 내려가자.\""]], [["Mr. Lydgate was rather late this morning, but the visitors stayed long enough to see him; for Mr.", "리드게이트 씨는 이날 아침 다소 늦었지만, 방문객들은 그를 볼 만큼 오래 머물렀다."], ["Featherstone asked Rosamond to sing to him, and she herself was so kind as to propose a second favorite song of his—“Flow on, thou shining river”—after she had sung “Home, sweet home”", "페더스톤 씨가 로저먼드에게 노래를 불러 달라고 청했고, 그녀 자신도 친절하게도 그가 좋아하는 두 번째 노래—\"흘러라, 빛나는 강물이여\"—를 부르겠다고 제안했기 때문이다."], ["(which she detested).", "그것도 (자기는 질색하는) \"즐거운 나의 집\"을 부른 뒤에 말이다."], ["This hard-headed old Overreach approved of the sentimental song, as the suitable garnish for girls, and also as fundamentally fine, sentiment being the right thing for a song.", "이 완고한 늙은 오버리치는 그 감상적인 노래를 흡족하게 여겼으니, 처녀들에게 어울리는 장식이라 여겼고, 또한 근본적으로 훌륭하다고 보았기 때문이다. 감상이란 노래에 딱 어울리는 것이니 말이다."]], [["Mr. Featherstone was still applauding the last performance, and assuring missy that her voice was as clear as a blackbird’s, when Mr. Lydgate’s horse passed the window.", "리드게이트 씨의 말이 창가를 지나갈 때, 페더스톤 씨는 여전히 마지막 곡에 박수를 보내며 아가씨의 목소리가 검은지빠귀만큼이나 맑다고 장담하고 있던 참이었다."]], [["His dull expectation of the usual disagreeable routine with an aged patient—who can hardly believe that medicine would not “set him up”", "의사가 충분히 똑똑하기만 하다면 약이 자기를 \"벌떡 일으켜 세워\""], ["if the doctor were only clever enough—added to his general disbelief in Middlemarch charms, made a doubly effective background to this vision of Rosamond, whom old Featherstone made haste ostentatiously to introduce as his niece, though he had never thought it worth while to speak of Mary Garth in that light.", "주리라는 것을 좀처럼 믿지 못하는 늙은 환자를 상대로, 늘 겪는 그 불쾌한 일과가 또 반복되리라는 따분한 예상은—미들마치의 매력 전반에 대한 그의 불신과 더해져—이 로저먼드라는 환상에 한층 효과적인 배경이 되어 주었다. 늙은 페더스톤은, 메리 가스에 대해서는 그런 식으로 소개할 가치조차 느낀 적이 없었으면서도, 부랴부랴 거창하게 로저먼드를 자기 조카딸이라고 소개했다."], ["Nothing escaped Lydgate in Rosamond’s graceful behavior: how delicately she waived the notice which the old man’s want of taste had thrust upon her by a quiet gravity, not showing her dimples on the wrong occasion, but showing them afterwards in speaking to Mary, to whom she addressed herself with so much good-natured interest, that Lydgate, after quickly examining Mary more fully than he had done before, saw an adorable kindness in Rosamond’s eyes.", "로저먼드의 우아한 처신은 무엇 하나 리드게이트의 눈을 벗어나지 못했으니, 노인의 몰취미가 그녀에게 떠안긴 그 주목을 그녀가 얼마나 섬세하게 받아넘기는지—엉뚱한 때에는 보조개를 짓지 않다가, 메리에게 말을 걸 때에야 비로소 그것을 내보이며, 그토록 따뜻한 관심을 담아 메리에게 다정히 말을 건네는 모습을, 리드게이트는 전보다 더 자세히 메리를 재빨리 살펴본 뒤에, 로저먼드의 눈에서 사랑스러운 친절함을 보아 내었다."], ["But Mary from some cause looked rather out of temper.", "그러나 메리는 무슨 까닭에선지 다소 언짢아 보였다."]], [["“Miss Rosy has been singing me a song—you’ve nothing to say against that, eh, doctor?”", "\"로지 양이 나한테 노래를 한 곡 불러 주었다네—그것까지 뭐라 할 말은 없겠지, 응, 의사 양반?\""], ["said Mr. Featherstone.", "페더스톤 씨가 말했다."], ["“I like it better than your physic.”", "\"난 자네 약보다 그게 더 좋아.\""]], [["“That has made me forget how the time was going,”", "\"그 노래 때문에 시간 가는 걸 깜빡했네요.\""], ["said Rosamond, rising to reach her hat, which she had laid aside before singing, so that her flower-like head on its white stem was seen in perfection above her riding-habit.", "로저먼드가 일어나 모자를 집으러 가며 말했다. 노래하기 전에 벗어 두었던 그 모자를 집으러 가는 동안, 흰 줄기 위에 핀 꽃 같은 그녀의 머리가 승마복 위로 완벽하게 드러나 보였다."], ["“Fred, we must really go.”", "\"프레드, 우리 정말 가야 해.\""]], [["“Very good,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["said Fred, who had his own reasons for not being in the best spirits, and wanted to get away.", "프레드가 말했는데, 그는 기분이 그리 좋지 못할 나름의 이유가 있었고, 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶었다."]], [["“Miss Vincy is a musician?”", "\"빈시 양은 음악을 하시나요?\""], ["said Lydgate, following her with his eyes.", "리드게이트가 눈으로 그녀를 좇으며 말했다."], ["(Every nerve and muscle in Rosamond was adjusted to the consciousness that she was being looked at.", "(로저먼드의 온 신경과 근육은 자기가 보여지고 있다는 의식에 맞춰 조율되어 있었다."], ["She was by nature an actress of parts that entered into her physique: she even acted her own character, and so well, that she did not know it to be precisely her own.)", "그녀는 천성적으로, 자기 육체에 배어든 배역을 연기하는 배우였다. 심지어 자기 자신의 성격마저도 연기했으니, 그것도 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 그것이 정확히 자기 본래의 성격임을 스스로도 알지 못했다.)"]], [["“The best in Middlemarch, I’ll be bound,” said Mr.", "\"미들마치에서 최고지, 내 장담하네.\""], ["Featherstone, “let the next be who she will.", "페더스톤 씨가 말했다. \"그다음이 누구든 간에 말이야."], ["Eh, Fred?", "안 그러냐, 프레드?"], ["Speak up for your sister.”", "네 누이를 두둔해 봐.\""]], [["“I’m afraid I’m out of court, sir.", "\"제 말은 인정받기 어려울 것 같은데요, 외삼촌."], ["My evidence would be good for nothing.”", "제 증언은 아무 쓸모가 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Middlemarch has not a very high standard, uncle,”", "\"미들마치는 그리 수준이 높지 않은걸요, 외삼촌.\""], ["said Rosamond, with a pretty lightness, going towards her whip, which lay at a distance.", "로저먼드가 어여쁘게 가벼운 어조로 말하며, 멀찍이 놓여 있던 자기 채찍 쪽으로 다가갔다."]], [["Lydgate was quick in anticipating her.", "리드게이트가 재빨리 그녀의 동작을 앞질렀다."], ["He reached the whip before she did, and turned to present it to her.", "그가 그녀보다 먼저 채찍에 닿아, 그것을 그녀에게 건네려고 몸을 돌렸다."], ["She bowed and looked at him: he of course was looking at her, and their eyes met with that peculiar meeting which is never arrived at by effort, but seems like a sudden divine clearance of haze.", "그녀는 고개를 숙여 인사하며 그를 바라보았다. 그도 물론 그녀를 바라보고 있었고, 그렇게 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤으니, 그것은 애써서는 결코 다다를 수 없는, 마치 안개가 별안간 신성하게 걷히는 듯한 그 묘한 마주침이었다."], ["I think Lydgate turned a little paler than usual, but Rosamond blushed deeply and felt a certain astonishment.", "내 생각에 리드게이트는 여느 때보다 조금 창백해진 것 같았으나, 로저먼드는 깊이 얼굴을 붉히며 어떤 놀라움을 느꼈다."], ["After that, she was really anxious to go, and did not know what sort of stupidity her uncle was talking of when she went to shake hands with him.", "그 일이 있고 나서 그녀는 정말로 어서 가고 싶어졌고, 외삼촌과 작별 인사를 하러 갔을 때는 그가 무슨 어리석은 소리를 하고 있는지조차 알지 못했다."]], [["Yet this result, which she took to be a mutual impression, called falling in love, was just what Rosamond had contemplated beforehand.", "그러나 그녀가 사랑에 빠지는 것이라 부르는, 서로의 인상이 통했다고 여긴 이 결과야말로, 로저먼드가 미리 마음속에 그려 두었던 바로 그것이었다."], ["Ever since that important new arrival in Middlemarch she had woven a little future, of which something like this scene was the necessary beginning.", "미들마치에 그 중요한 새 인물이 도착한 이래로, 그녀는 자그마한 미래를 짜 내려 왔고, 이런 장면 같은 것이야말로 그 미래의 필수적인 시작이었다."], ["Strangers, whether wrecked and clinging to a raft, or duly escorted and accompanied by portmanteaus, have always had a circumstantial fascination for the virgin mind, against which native merit has urged itself in vain.", "난파되어 뗏목에 매달려 있든, 정중한 수행원과 여행 가방들을 거느리고 오든, 낯선 이는 처녀의 마음에 늘 어떤 정황적인 매혹을 불러일으켜 왔으니, 그 앞에서는 토박이의 가치 따위가 아무리 애써도 헛될 뿐이었다."], ["And a stranger was absolutely necessary to Rosamond’s social romance, which had always turned on a lover and bridegroom who was not a Middlemarcher, and who had no connections at all like her own: of late, indeed, the construction seemed to demand that he should somehow be related to a baronet.", "그리고 낯선 이야말로 로저먼드의 사교적 로맨스에 절대적으로 필요한 존재였으니, 그 로맨스는 늘 미들마치 토박이가 아니면서 자기 집안 같은 연줄이라고는 전혀 없는 연인이자 신랑을 중심으로 펼쳐져 왔고, 게다가 근래에 와서는 그 구도가, 그가 어떻게든 준남작과 친척 관계여야 한다고 요구하는 듯했다."], ["Now that she and the stranger had met, reality proved much more moving than anticipation, and Rosamond could not doubt that this was the great epoch of her life.", "이제 그녀와 그 낯선 이가 만났으니, 현실은 예상보다 훨씬 더 가슴 설레는 것으로 드러났고, 로저먼드는 이것이야말로 자기 생애의 위대한 전환점임을 의심할 수가 없었다."], ["She judged of her own symptoms as those of awakening love, and she held it still more natural that Mr. Lydgate should have fallen in love at first sight of her.", "그녀는 자기 자신의 징후를 깨어나는 사랑의 징후라고 판단했고, 리드게이트 씨가 그녀를 첫눈에 보고 사랑에 빠졌으리라는 것은 한층 더 당연하게 여겼다."], ["These things happened so often at balls, and why not by the morning light, when the complexion showed all the better for it?", "그런 일이야 무도회에서 그토록 자주 일어나는데, 게다가 안색이 한결 더 돋보이는 아침 햇살 속에서라고 안 될 이유가 어디 있겠는가? 로저먼드는 메리보다 나이가 많은 것도 아니었지만, 사랑의 대상이 되는 데에는 제법 익숙했다."], ["Rosamond, though no older than Mary, was rather used to being fallen in love with; but she, for her part, had remained indifferent and fastidiously critical towards both fresh sprig and faded bachelor. And here was Mr.", "그러나 그녀로 말하자면, 풋풋한 젊은이든 한물간 노총각이든 양쪽 모두에게 무관심한 채 까다롭게 비판적인 태도를 지켜 왔다."], ["Lydgate suddenly corresponding to her ideal, being altogether foreign to Middlemarch, carrying a certain air of distinction congruous with good family, and possessing connections which offered vistas of that middle-class heaven, rank; a man of talent, also, whom it would be especially delightful to enslave: in fact, a man who had touched her nature quite newly, and brought a vivid interest into her life which was better than any fancied “might-be”", "그런데 여기 리드게이트 씨야말로 별안간 그녀의 이상에 들어맞는 사람이었으니, 미들마치와는 전적으로 무관한 사람이고, 좋은 집안에 걸맞은 어떤 기품을 풍기며, 신분이라는 그 중산층의 천국으로 통하는 전망을 열어 줄 연줄을 지니고 있었다. 게다가 재능 있는 남자이기도 했으니, 그런 사람을 노예로 삼는 것이야말로 특히나 즐거운 일일 터였다."], ["such as she was in the habit of opposing to the actual.", "요컨대 그는 그녀의 천성을 아주 새롭게 건드렸고, 그녀가 늘 현실에 맞세워 두곤 하던 어떤 가공의 \"있을 법한 일\"보다 더 나은, 생생한 흥미를 그녀의 삶에 가져다준 남자였다."]], [["Thus, in riding home, both the brother and the sister were preoccupied and inclined to be silent.", "이리하여 집으로 말을 달려 돌아가는 동안, 오빠도 누이도 저마다의 생각에 잠겨 말이 없으려 했다."], ["Rosamond, whose basis for her structure had the usual airy slightness, was of remarkably detailed and realistic imagination when the foundation had been once presupposed; and before they had ridden a mile she was far on in the costume and introductions of her wedded life, having determined on her house in Middlemarch, and foreseen the visits she would pay to her husband’s high-bred relatives at a distance, whose finished manners she could appropriate as thoroughly as she had done her school accomplishments, preparing herself thus for vaguer elevations which might ultimately come.", "로저먼드는, 자기 구조물의 토대가 여느 때처럼 허황될 만큼 빈약했음에도, 일단 그 토대가 전제되고 나면 놀랄 만큼 세세하고 사실적인 상상력을 발휘하는 사람이었다. 그래서 그들이 채 한 마일도 달리기 전에 그녀는 이미 결혼 생활의 의상과 사교 소개에 한참 빠져들어, 미들마치에 마련할 자기 집을 정해 두었고, 멀리 떨어진 남편의 지체 높은 친척들을 찾아갈 일까지 내다보았으니, 그 세련된 예법을 그녀는 학창 시절의 교양을 익혔던 것만큼이나 철저히 제 것으로 만들 수 있을 터였고, 이렇게 해서 끝내 다가올지도 모를 한층 막연한 신분 상승에 미리 대비하는 셈이었다."], ["There was nothing financial, still less sordid, in her previsions: she cared about what were considered refinements, and not about the money that was to pay for them.", "그녀의 예상에는 금전적인 것이라고는 전혀 없었고, 천박한 것은 더더욱 없었다. 그녀가 마음 쓰는 것은 세련됨이라 일컬어지는 것들이었지, 그것을 치를 돈이 아니었다."]], [["Fred’s mind, on the other hand, was busy with an anxiety which even his ready hopefulness could not immediately quell.", "반면에 프레드의 마음은, 타고난 낙천성으로도 당장은 가라앉히지 못할 어떤 근심으로 분주했다."], ["He saw no way of eluding Featherstone’s stupid demand without incurring consequences which he liked less even than the task of fulfilling it.", "그는 페더스톤의 어리석은 요구를 이행하는 일조차도 마뜩잖았지만, 그 요구를 피하려다가는 그보다 더 싫은 결과를 불러올 길밖에 보이지 않았다."], ["His father was already out of humor with him, and would be still more so if he were the occasion of any additional coolness between his own family and the Bulstrodes.", "아버지는 이미 그에게 기분이 상해 있었고, 만약 그가 자기 집안과 벌스트로드 집안 사이에 어떤 추가적인 냉랭함이라도 불러오는 빌미가 된다면 더욱더 그러할 터였다."], ["Then, he himself hated having to go and speak to his uncle Bulstrode, and perhaps after drinking wine he had said many foolish things about Featherstone’s property, and these had been magnified by report.", "게다가 그 자신도 외삼촌 벌스트로드를 찾아가 말을 거는 일이 끔찍이도 싫었고, 어쩌면 술을 마신 뒤에 페더스톤의 재산에 관해 어리석은 소리를 숱하게 늘어놓았을지도 모르며, 그것이 소문으로 부풀려졌을 수도 있었다."], ["Fred felt that he made a wretched figure as a fellow who bragged about expectations from a queer old miser like Featherstone, and went to beg for certificates at his bidding.", "프레드는 자기가 페더스톤 같은 별난 늙은 구두쇠한테서 받을 유산 기대를 떠벌리고 다니다가, 그 명령에 따라 보증서를 받으러 굽실거리러 가는 한심한 꼴이 되었다고 느꼈다."], ["But—those expectations!", "하지만—그 유산 기대라니!"], ["He really had them, and he saw no agreeable alternative if he gave them up; besides, he had lately made a debt which galled him extremely, and old Featherstone had almost bargained to pay it off.", "그는 정말로 그것을 품고 있었고, 그것을 포기한다면 마음에 드는 다른 대안이라고는 보이지 않았다. 게다가 그는 근래에 빚을 하나 졌는데, 그것이 그를 몹시 괴롭혔고, 늙은 페더스톤이 그것을 갚아 주겠다고 거의 흥정해 준 참이었다."], ["The whole affair was miserably small: his debts were small, even his expectations were not anything so very magnificent.", "이 모든 일은 비참할 만큼 사소했다. 그의 빚도 적었고, 그의 유산 기대조차도 그리 대단한 것은 아니었다."], ["Fred had known men to whom he would have been ashamed of confessing the smallness of his scrapes.", "프레드는, 자기가 처한 곤경의 그 하찮음을 고백하기조차 부끄러울 만한 사람들을 알고 있었다."], ["Such ruminations naturally produced a streak of misanthropic bitterness.", "이런 곱씹음은 자연히 한 줄기 인간 혐오의 신랄함을 자아냈다."], ["To be born the son of a Middlemarch manufacturer, and inevitable heir to nothing in particular, while such men as Mainwaring and Vyan—certainly life was a poor business, when a spirited young fellow, with a good appetite for the best of everything, had so poor an outlook.", "미들마치 제조업자의 아들로 태어나, 이렇다 할 무엇 하나 물려받지 못할 어쩔 수 없는 신세인데, 메인웨어링이나 바이언 같은 자들은 저렇건만—정녕 인생이란 보잘것없는 노릇이었다, 무엇이든 가장 좋은 것을 탐하는 왕성한 식욕을 지닌 패기 있는 젊은이에게 그토록 빈약한 앞날밖에 없다니."]], [["It had not occurred to Fred that the introduction of Bulstrode’s name in the matter was a fiction of old Featherstone’s; nor could this have made any difference to his position.", "이 일에 벌스트로드의 이름을 끌어들인 것이 늙은 페더스톤의 꾸며 낸 이야기일 수도 있다는 생각은 프레드에게 떠오르지 않았다. 설령 그랬다 한들 그의 처지에는 아무 차이도 없었을 것이다."], ["He saw plainly enough that the old man wanted to exercise his power by tormenting him a little, and also probably to get some satisfaction out of seeing him on unpleasant terms with Bulstrode.", "그는, 노인이 자기를 조금 괴롭힘으로써 그 권력을 휘두르고 싶어 한다는 것, 또한 아마도 자기가 벌스트로드와 불편한 사이가 되는 꼴을 보면서 어떤 만족을 얻으려 한다는 것쯤은 충분히 꿰뚫어 보았다."], ["Fred fancied that he saw to the bottom of his uncle Featherstone’s soul, though in reality half what he saw there was no more than the reflex of his own inclinations.", "프레드는 자기가 외삼촌 페더스톤의 영혼 밑바닥까지 들여다본다고 자부했지만, 실은 거기서 본 것의 절반은 자기 자신의 성향이 반사된 것에 지나지 않았다."], ["The difficult task of knowing another soul is not for young gentlemen whose consciousness is chiefly made up of their own wishes.", "다른 영혼을 안다는 그 어려운 일은, 의식의 대부분이 자기 욕망으로 이루어진 젊은 신사들의 몫이 아니다."]], [["Fred’s main point of debate with himself was, whether he should tell his father, or try to get through the affair without his father’s knowledge. It was probably Mrs.", "프레드가 자기 자신과 벌인 논쟁의 주된 쟁점은, 이 일을 아버지에게 말해야 할 것인지, 아니면 아버지가 모르게 일을 처리하려 해야 할 것인지였다."], ["Waule who had been talking about him; and if Mary Garth had repeated Mrs.", "자기에 대해 떠들어 댄 사람은 아마 월 부인이었을 것이다."], ["Waule’s report to Rosamond, it would be sure to reach his father, who would as surely question him about it.", "그리고 만약 메리 가스가 월 부인의 그 소문을 로저먼드에게 옮겼다면, 그것은 틀림없이 아버지의 귀에까지 들어갈 테고, 아버지는 어김없이 그에게 그 일을 캐물을 것이었다."], ["He said to Rosamond, as they slackened their pace—", "그는 말의 속도를 늦추면서 로저먼드에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Rosy, did Mary tell you that Mrs. Waule had said anything about me?”", "\"로지, 월 부인이 나에 대해 뭔가 말했다고 메리가 너한테 그러던?\""]], [["“Yes, indeed, she did.”", "\"응, 정말로 그랬어.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""]], [["“That you were very unsteady.”", "\"오빠가 아주 행실이 헤프다고.\""]], [["“Was that all?”", "\"그게 다였어?\""]], [["“I should think that was enough, Fred.”", "\"그 정도면 충분하다고 봐, 프레드.\""]], [["“You are sure she said no more?”", "\"메리가 더 말한 건 없는 게 확실해?\""]], [["“Mary mentioned nothing else.", "\"메리는 다른 건 아무 말도 안 했어."], ["But really, Fred, I think you ought to be ashamed.”", "하지만 정말이지, 프레드, 오빠는 부끄러운 줄 알아야 해.\""]], [["“Oh, fudge!", "\"아, 무슨 헛소리!"], ["Don’t lecture me.", "나한테 설교하지 마."], ["What did Mary say about it?”", "그 일에 대해 메리가 뭐래?\""]], [["“I am not obliged to tell you.", "\"꼭 말해 줄 의무는 없는데."], ["You care so very much what Mary says, and you are too rude to allow me to speak.”", "오빠는 메리가 하는 말에 그렇게 끔찍이 신경 쓰면서, 정작 내가 말하게 가만두지도 않을 만큼 무례하니까.\""]], [["“Of course I care what Mary says.", "\"당연히 난 메리가 하는 말에 신경 써."], ["She is the best girl I know.”", "그 앤 내가 아는 가장 멋진 처녀야.\""]], [["“I should never have thought she was a girl to fall in love with.”", "\"난 그 애가 사랑에 빠질 만한 처녀라고는 한 번도 생각 안 해 봤는데.\""]], [["“How do you know what men would fall in love with?", "\"남자들이 어떤 처녀한테 반할지 네가 어떻게 알아?"], ["Girls never know.”", "처녀들은 절대 몰라.\""]], [["“At least, Fred, let me advise you not to fall in love with her, for she says she would not marry you if you asked her.”", "\"적어도, 프레드, 오빠한테 충고하자면 그 애한테 반하진 마. 그 애가 그러는데, 오빠가 청혼해도 자기는 결혼 안 할 거래.\""]], [["“She might have waited till I did ask her.”", "\"그럼 내가 청혼할 때까지 기다리기라도 했어야지.\""]], [["“I knew it would nettle you, Fred.”", "\"오빠가 발끈할 줄 알았어, 프레드.\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"전혀."], ["She would not have said so if you had not provoked her.”", "네가 약을 올리지 않았으면 그 앤 그런 말 안 했을 거야.\""], ["Before reaching home, Fred concluded that he would tell the whole affair as simply as possible to his father, who might perhaps take on himself the unpleasant business of speaking to Bulstrode.", "집에 닿기 전에 프레드는, 이 일 전부를 되도록 간단하게 아버지에게 털어놓기로 마음먹었으니, 어쩌면 아버지가 벌스트로드에게 말을 거는 그 불쾌한 일을 직접 떠맡아 줄지도 몰랐기 때문이다."]], [["# BOOK II. OLD AND YOUNG. CHAPTER XIII.", "# BOOK II. OLD AND YOUNG. CHAPTER XIII."]], [["1st Gent.", "늙음과 젊음\n첫째 신사."], ["How class your man?—as better than the most, Or, seeming better, worse beneath that cloak?", "당신은 그 사람을 어떻게 분류하시오?—대다수보다 나은 자로? 아니면 나아 보이지만 그 망토 아래 더 못된 자로?"], ["As saint or knave, pilgrim or hypocrite?", "성자로, 아니면 악당으로, 순례자로, 아니면 위선자로?"]], [["2d Gent.", "둘째 신사."], ["Nay, tell me how you class your wealth of books The drifted relics of all time.", "아니, 그보다 당신의 그 책 더미는 어떻게 분류하는지부터 말해 보시오. 온 시대가 떠밀려 와 쌓인 잔해들 말이오."], ["As well sort them at once by size and livery: Vellum, tall copies, and the common calf Will hardly cover more diversity Than all your labels cunningly devised To class your unread authors.", "차라리 크기와 장정으로 단번에 가르는 편이 나을 거요. 송아지 가죽, 키 큰 판본, 흔한 송아지 가죽본인들, 당신이 그 읽지도 않은 저자들을 분류하려고 교묘하게 고안해 낸 온갖 딱지보다 더 다양함을 덮어 주지는 못할 테니."]], [["In consequence of what he had heard from Fred, Mr. Vincy determined to speak with Mr.", "프레드에게 들은 이야기 때문에, 빈시 씨는 한 시 반에 은행의 개인 사무실에서 불스트로드 씨와 이야기를 나누기로 마음먹었다."], ["Bulstrode in his private room at the Bank at half-past one, when he was usually free from other callers.", "그 시각이면 대개 다른 방문객이 없을 때였다."], ["But a visitor had come in at one o’clock, and Mr. Bulstrode had so much to say to him, that there was little chance of the interview being over in half an hour.", "그러나 한 시에 한 손님이 들어와 있었고, 불스트로드 씨가 그에게 할 말이 어찌나 많았던지 그 면담이 삼십 분 안에 끝날 가망은 거의 없어 보였다."], ["The banker’s speech was fluent, but it was also copious, and he used up an appreciable amount of time in brief meditative pauses.", "은행가의 화법은 유창했으나 또한 장황했고, 그는 짧은 명상적 침묵으로 적잖은 시간을 소모했다. 그의 병약한 외모가 누렇고 머리칼이 검은 부류였으리라고 상상하지는 마시라."], ["Do not imagine his sickly aspect to have been of the yellow, black-haired sort: he had a pale blond skin, thin gray-besprinkled brown hair, light-gray eyes, and a large forehead.", "그는 핏기 없는 금발 피부에, 회색이 듬성듬성 섞인 가는 갈색 머리, 연한 회색 눈, 그리고 넓은 이마를 지니고 있었다. 목소리 큰 사람들은 그의 낮춘 어조를 두고 속삭임이라 불렀고, 때로는 그것이 솔직함과 양립하지 않는다는 뜻을 내비치기도 했다."], ["Loud men called his subdued tone an undertone, and sometimes implied that it was inconsistent with openness; though there seems to be no reason why a loud man should not be given to concealment of anything except his own voice, unless it can be shown that Holy Writ has placed the seat of candor in the lungs.", "그러나 목소리 큰 사람이라고 해서 자기 목소리 말고 무언가를 감추지 못할 이유는 없어 보인다. 성경이 솔직함의 자리를 폐에 두었음을 증명할 수 있다면 모를까."], ["Mr. Bulstrode had also a deferential bending attitude in listening, and an apparently fixed attentiveness in his eyes which made those persons who thought themselves worth hearing infer that he was seeking the utmost improvement from their discourse.", "또한 불스트로드 씨는 남의 말을 들을 때 공손하게 몸을 숙이는 태도와, 눈에 어린 한결같이 집중하는 듯한 표정을 지니고 있었는데, 그 때문에 자신이 들을 만한 가치가 있다고 여기는 사람들은 그가 자신의 이야기에서 최대한의 유익을 구하고 있다고 짐작하게 되었다."], ["Others, who expected to make no great figure, disliked this kind of moral lantern turned on them.", "별로 대단한 인물이 못 되리라 여기는 다른 이들은 자기에게 비치는 이런 도덕적 등불을 싫어했다."], ["If you are not proud of your cellar, there is no thrill of satisfaction in seeing your guest hold up his wine-glass to the light and look judicial.", "자기 술 창고가 자랑스럽지 않다면, 손님이 술잔을 빛에 비추어 들고서 감정관 같은 표정을 짓는 것을 보아도 흡족한 전율 따위는 없는 법이다."], ["Such joys are reserved for conscious merit. Hence Mr.", "그런 기쁨은 떳떳한 자부심을 지닌 자에게나 허락되는 것이다."], ["Bulstrode’s close attention was not agreeable to the publicans and sinners in Middlemarch; it was attributed by some to his being a Pharisee, and by others to his being Evangelical.", "그리하여 불스트로드 씨의 면밀한 주의는 미들마치의 세리와 죄인들에게 달갑지 않았다. 어떤 이들은 그것을 그가 바리새인이기 때문이라 여겼고, 또 어떤 이들은 그가 복음주의자이기 때문이라 여겼다."], ["Less superficial reasoners among them wished to know who his father and grandfather were, observing that five-and-twenty years ago nobody had ever heard of a Bulstrode in Middlemarch.", "그들 가운데 좀 덜 피상적인 추론가들은 그의 아버지와 할아버지가 누구였는지 알고 싶어 했으니, 이십오 년 전만 해도 미들마치에서 불스트로드라는 이름을 들어 본 사람이 아무도 없었다는 점을 지적했다."], ["To his present visitor, Lydgate, the scrutinizing look was a matter of indifference: he simply formed an unfavorable opinion of the banker’s constitution, and concluded that he had an eager inward life with little enjoyment of tangible things.", "지금 그의 방문객인 리드게이트에게는 그 살피는 눈초리가 아무래도 상관없는 것이었다. 그는 그저 그 은행가의 체질에 대해 좋지 않은 견해를 품었고, 그가 손에 잡히는 것들을 거의 즐기지 못한 채 격렬한 내적 생활을 하고 있다고 결론지었다."]], [["“I shall be exceedingly obliged if you will look in on me here occasionally, Mr. Lydgate,”", "“가끔 이곳으로 저를 찾아와 주신다면 더없이 고맙겠습니다, 리드게이트 선생.”"], ["the banker observed, after a brief pause.", "잠시 말을 멈추었다가 은행가가 말했다."], ["“If, as I dare to hope, I have the privilege of finding you a valuable coadjutor in the interesting matter of hospital management, there will be many questions which we shall need to discuss in private.", "“감히 바라는 대로, 병원 운영이라는 흥미로운 일에서 선생을 귀중한 협력자로 모시는 영광을 누리게 된다면, 우리가 사적으로 논의해야 할 문제가 많을 겁니다."], ["As to the new hospital, which is nearly finished, I shall consider what you have said about the advantages of the special destination for fevers.", "거의 완공된 새 병원에 관해서는, 열병 환자를 위한 특수 시설의 이점에 대해 선생이 하신 말씀을 고려해 보겠습니다."], ["The decision will rest with me, for though Lord Medlicote has given the land and timber for the building, he is not disposed to give his personal attention to the object.”", "결정은 제게 달려 있습니다. 메들리코트 경이 건물을 위해 땅과 목재를 내놓기는 했으나, 그 사업에 직접 관심을 기울일 뜻은 없으니까요.”"]], [["“There are few things better worth the pains in a provincial town like this,”", "“이런 지방 도시에서 그만큼 수고를 들일 가치가 있는 일도 드물지요.”"], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“A fine fever hospital in addition to the old infirmary might be the nucleus of a medical school here, when once we get our medical reforms; and what would do more for medical education than the spread of such schools over the country?", "“기존의 진료소에 더해 훌륭한 열병 병원이 있다면, 일단 의료 개혁이 이루어지고 나서는 이곳이 의학교의 중심이 될 수도 있을 겁니다. 그런 학교들이 전국에 퍼지는 것보다 의학 교육에 더 큰 도움이 될 일이 또 있겠습니까?"], ["A born provincial man who has a grain of public spirit as well as a few ideas, should do what he can to resist the rush of everything that is a little better than common towards London.", "몇 가지 식견과 더불어 한 톨이라도 공공심을 지닌 타고난 지방 사람이라면, 평범한 것보다 조금이라도 나은 모든 것이 런던으로 몰려가는 흐름에 맞서 제가 할 수 있는 바를 해야 합니다."], ["Any valid professional aims may often find a freer, if not a richer field, in the provinces.”", "어떤 정당한 직업적 목표든, 더 풍요롭지는 않을지언정 더 자유로운 무대를 지방에서 찾는 경우가 많지요.”"]], [["One of Lydgate’s gifts was a voice habitually deep and sonorous, yet capable of becoming very low and gentle at the right moment.", "리드게이트의 재능 가운데 하나는 평소 깊고 낭랑하면서도 적절한 순간에는 매우 낮고 부드러워질 줄 아는 목소리였다."], ["About his ordinary bearing there was a certain fling, a fearless expectation of success, a confidence in his own powers and integrity much fortified by contempt for petty obstacles or seductions of which he had had no experience.", "그의 일상적인 태도에는 어떤 호기로움이, 성공에 대한 두려움 없는 기대가, 자신의 능력과 진실성에 대한 자신감이 깃들어 있었는데, 그 자신감은 경험해 본 적 없는 사소한 장애나 유혹에 대한 경멸로 한층 굳건해진 것이었다."], ["But this proud openness was made lovable by an expression of unaffected good-will. Mr.", "그러나 이 당당한 솔직함은 꾸밈없는 선의의 표정 덕분에 사랑스러운 것이 되었다."], ["Bulstrode perhaps liked him the better for the difference between them in pitch and manners; he certainly liked him the better, as Rosamond did, for being a stranger in Middlemarch.", "불스트로드 씨는 어쩌면 둘 사이의 어조와 태도의 차이 때문에 그를 더 좋아했는지도 모른다. 적어도 그가 미들마치에서 낯선 사람이라는 점 때문에, 로저먼드가 그러했듯 그를 더 좋아한 것만은 분명했다."], ["One can begin so many things with a new person!—even begin to be a better man.", "새로운 사람과는 참으로 많은 일을 시작할 수 있는 법이니!—심지어 더 나은 사람이 되기 시작할 수도 있는 것이다."]], [["“I shall rejoice to furnish your zeal with fuller opportunities,” Mr.", "“선생의 열정에 더 충분한 기회를 마련해 드리게 되어 기쁩니다.”"], ["Bulstrode answered; “I mean, by confiding to you the superintendence of my new hospital, should a maturer knowledge favor that issue, for I am determined that so great an object shall not be shackled by our two physicians.", "불스트로드 씨가 대답했다. “말하자면, 더 무르익은 식견이 그 결정을 뒷받침해 준다면 제 새 병원의 감독직을 선생께 맡기겠다는 뜻입니다."], ["Indeed, I am encouraged to consider your advent to this town as a gracious indication that a more manifest blessing is now to be awarded to my efforts, which have hitherto been much withstood.", "저는 그토록 위대한 사업이 우리의 두 내과 의사에게 발목 잡히지 않도록 하기로 결심했으니까요. 사실, 선생이 이 도시에 오신 것을 두고 저는, 지금까지 적잖이 방해받아 온 제 노력에 이제 더 분명한 축복이 내리리라는 은혜로운 징표로 여기게 됩니다."], ["With regard to the old infirmary, we have gained the initial point—I mean your election.", "기존 진료소에 관해서는 우리가 첫 관문을 통과했습니다—선생의 선출 말입니다."], ["And now I hope you will not shrink from incurring a certain amount of jealousy and dislike from your professional brethren by presenting yourself as a reformer.”", "그리고 이제 저는 선생이 개혁가로 나섬으로써 동료 의사들에게서 어느 정도의 질시와 반감을 사게 되더라도 움츠러들지 않기를 바랍니다.”"]], [["“I will not profess bravery,”", "“용기가 있는 척하지는 않겠습니다.” 리드게이트가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["said Lydgate, smiling, “but I acknowledge a good deal of pleasure in fighting, and I should not care for my profession, if I did not believe that better methods were to be found and enforced there as well as everywhere else.”", "“하지만 싸움에서 적잖은 즐거움을 느낀다는 점은 인정합니다. 그리고 다른 모든 분야에서와 마찬가지로 의료에서도 더 나은 방법이 발견되고 시행될 수 있다고 믿지 않는다면, 저는 제 직업에 미련을 두지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["“The standard of that profession is low in Middlemarch, my dear sir,”", "“미들마치에서 그 직업의 수준은 낮습니다, 선생.”"], ["said the banker.", "은행가가 말했다."], ["“I mean in knowledge and skill; not in social status, for our medical men are most of them connected with respectable townspeople here.", "“지식과 기술 면에서 그렇다는 말이지, 사회적 지위에서 그렇다는 건 아닙니다. 우리 의사들은 대부분 이곳의 점잖은 시민들과 연이 닿아 있으니까요."], ["My own imperfect health has induced me to give some attention to those palliative resources which the divine mercy has placed within our reach.", "저 자신의 부실한 건강 탓에 저는 신의 자비가 우리 손이 닿는 곳에 마련해 둔 그 완화책들에 다소 관심을 기울이게 되었습니다."], ["I have consulted eminent men in the metropolis, and I am painfully aware of the backwardness under which medical treatment labors in our provincial districts.”", "수도의 저명한 분들과 상의해 보았고, 우리 지방 지역에서 의료가 시달리고 있는 그 낙후함을 뼈저리게 깨닫고 있습니다.”"]], [["“Yes;—with our present medical rules and education, one must be satisfied now and then to meet with a fair practitioner.", "“그렇습니다—현재의 의료 규정과 교육으로는, 그저 어쩌다 한 번 괜찮은 개업의를 만나는 것에 만족해야 하지요."], ["As to all the higher questions which determine the starting-point of a diagnosis—as to the philosophy of medical evidence—any glimmering of these can only come from a scientific culture of which country practitioners have usually no more notion than the man in the moon.”", "진단의 출발점을 결정하는 온갖 고차원적 문제에 관해서는—의학적 증거의 철학에 관해서는—그것을 어렴풋이나마 이해하는 일조차 과학적 소양에서나 나올 수 있는데, 지방 개업의들은 대개 그런 것에 대해 달나라 사람이나 다름없이 아무 개념이 없습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Bulstrode, bending and looking intently, found the form which Lydgate had given to his agreement not quite suited to his comprehension.", "불스트로드 씨는 몸을 숙이고 골똘히 바라보면서, 리드게이트가 자신의 동의를 표현한 그 방식이 자기 이해력에 그리 잘 맞지 않음을 깨달았다."], ["Under such circumstances a judicious man changes the topic and enters on ground where his own gifts may be more useful.", "그런 상황에서 분별 있는 사람은 화제를 바꾸어, 자신의 재능이 더 쓸모 있을 만한 영역으로 들어서는 법이다."]], [["“I am aware,”", "“저도 압니다.”"], ["he said, “that the peculiar bias of medical ability is towards material means.", "그가 말했다. “의술의 특유한 성향이 물질적 수단 쪽으로 기운다는 것을요."], ["Nevertheless, Mr. Lydgate, I hope we shall not vary in sentiment as to a measure in which you are not likely to be actively concerned, but in which your sympathetic concurrence may be an aid to me.", "그렇지만, 리드게이트 선생, 선생이 적극적으로 관여할 일은 아니나 선생의 공감 어린 동의가 제게 도움이 될 만한 어떤 조치에 대해서는 우리의 생각이 어긋나지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["You recognize, I hope; the existence of spiritual interests in your patients?”", "선생도 환자들에게 영적 관심사가 존재한다는 것은 인정하시리라 믿습니다만?”"]], [["“Certainly I do.", "“물론 인정합니다."], ["But those words are apt to cover different meanings to different minds.”", "하지만 그 말은 사람마다 다른 의미를 담기 마련이지요.”"]], [["“Precisely.", "“바로 그렇습니다."], ["And on such subjects wrong teaching is as fatal as no teaching.", "그리고 그런 문제에서는 잘못된 가르침이 가르침이 전혀 없는 것만큼이나 치명적입니다."], ["Now a point which I have much at heart to secure is a new regulation as to clerical attendance at the old infirmary.", "그런데 제가 꼭 이루고 싶어 하는 한 가지는 기존 진료소의 성직자 봉직에 관한 새 규정입니다."], ["The building stands in Mr. Farebrother’s parish.", "그 건물은 페어브러더 씨의 교구 안에 있습니다."], ["You know Mr. Farebrother?”", "페어브러더 씨를 아십니까?”"]], [["“I have seen him.", "“뵌 적이 있습니다."], ["He gave me his vote.", "제게 표를 주셨지요."], ["I must call to thank him.", "감사 인사를 드리러 찾아뵈어야 합니다."], ["He seems a very bright pleasant little fellow.", "무척 명랑하고 유쾌한 분 같더군요."], ["And I understand he is a naturalist.”", "그리고 박물학자라고 들었습니다.”"]], [["“Mr. Farebrother, my dear sir, is a man deeply painful to contemplate.", "“페어브러더 씨는, 선생, 생각만 해도 몹시 가슴 아픈 사람입니다."], ["I suppose there is not a clergyman in this country who has greater talents.”", "이 나라에 그보다 더 큰 재능을 지닌 성직자는 없으리라 생각합니다.”"], ["Mr. Bulstrode paused and looked meditative.", "불스트로드 씨는 말을 멈추고 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“I have not yet been pained by finding any excessive talent in Middlemarch,”", "“저는 아직 미들마치에서 지나친 재능을 발견하여 가슴 아파한 적이 없습니다.”"], ["said Lydgate, bluntly.", "리드게이트가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."]], [["“What I desire,” Mr.", "“제가 바라는 것은,”"], ["Bulstrode continued, looking still more serious, “is that Mr.", "불스트로드 씨가 한층 더 진지한 표정으로 말을 이었다."], ["Farebrother’s attendance at the hospital should be superseded by the appointment of a chaplain—of Mr. Tyke, in fact—and that no other spiritual aid should be called in.”", "“페어브러더 씨의 병원 봉직을 전속 사제—실은 타이크 씨—의 임명으로 대체하는 것이며, 다른 어떤 영적 도움도 부르지 않는 것입니다.”"]], [["“As a medical man I could have no opinion on such a point unless I knew Mr. Tyke, and even then I should require to know the cases in which he was applied.”", "“의사로서 저는 타이크 씨를 알지 못하는 한 그런 문제에 대해 의견을 가질 수 없으며, 설령 안다 해도 그가 어떤 경우에 부름받는지를 알아야 할 겁니다.”"], ["Lydgate smiled, but he was bent on being circumspect.", "리드게이트는 미소 지었으나, 신중을 기하려고 작정하고 있었다."]], [["“Of course you cannot enter fully into the merits of this measure at present. But”—here Mr.", "“물론 지금 당장 이 조치의 시비를 온전히 따지실 수는 없겠지요."], ["Bulstrode began to speak with a more chiselled emphasis—“the subject is likely to be referred to the medical board of the infirmary, and what I trust I may ask of you is, that in virtue of the cooperation between us which I now look forward to, you will not, so far as you are concerned, be influenced by my opponents in this matter.”", "하지만”—여기서 불스트로드 씨는 한층 또렷하게 힘주어 말하기 시작했다—“이 문제는 진료소의 의료 위원회에 회부될 가능성이 큽니다. 그래서 제가 선생께 청하고 싶은 것은, 제가 지금 기대하고 있는 우리 사이의 협력을 생각해서라도, 적어도 선생에 관한 한 이 문제에서 제 반대자들에게 휘둘리지 않으시리라는 점입니다.”"]], [["“I hope I shall have nothing to do with clerical disputes,”", "“저는 성직자들의 분쟁에는 아무 관여도 하지 않기를 바랍니다.”"], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“The path I have chosen is to work well in my own profession.”", "“제가 택한 길은 제 직업 안에서 일을 잘 해내는 것입니다.”"]], [["“My responsibility, Mr. Lydgate, is of a broader kind.", "“제 책임은, 리드게이트 선생, 그보다 더 폭넓은 성질의 것입니다."], ["With me, indeed, this question is one of sacred accountableness; whereas with my opponents, I have good reason to say that it is an occasion for gratifying a spirit of worldly opposition.", "사실 제게 이 문제는 거룩한 책무에 관한 것입니다. 반면 제 반대자들에게는, 세속적 반목의 기질을 충족시키는 빌미라고 말할 만한 충분한 근거가 제게 있습니다."], ["But I shall not therefore drop one iota of my convictions, or cease to identify myself with that truth which an evil generation hates.", "그러나 그렇다고 해서 저는 제 신념을 티끌만큼도 버리지 않을 것이며, 사악한 세대가 미워하는 그 진리와 저 자신을 동일시하기를 그만두지도 않을 것입니다."], ["I have devoted myself to this object of hospital-improvement, but I will boldly confess to you, Mr. Lydgate, that I should have no interest in hospitals if I believed that nothing more was concerned therein than the cure of mortal diseases.", "저는 이 병원 개선 사업에 헌신해 왔습니다만, 리드게이트 선생, 솔직히 고백하건대 그 안에 죽음에 이르는 병의 치료 이상의 것이 걸려 있지 않다고 믿었다면 저는 병원에 아무 관심도 없었을 겁니다."], ["I have another ground of action, and in the face of persecution I will not conceal it.”", "제게는 또 다른 행동의 근거가 있으며, 박해에 직면하더라도 저는 그것을 감추지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["Mr. Bulstrode’s voice had become a loud and agitated whisper as he said the last words.", "불스트로드 씨의 목소리는 그 마지막 말을 할 때 크고 격앙된 속삭임이 되어 있었다."]], [["“There we certainly differ,”", "“그 점에서는 우리가 분명 다릅니다.”"], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["But he was not sorry that the door was now opened, and Mr. Vincy was announced.", "하지만 그는 이제 문이 열리고 빈시 씨가 들어왔다고 알려진 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["That florid sociable personage was become more interesting to him since he had seen Rosamond.", "혈색 좋고 사교적인 그 인물은 로저먼드를 본 뒤로 그에게 더욱 흥미로운 존재가 되어 있었다."], ["Not that, like her, he had been weaving any future in which their lots were united; but a man naturally remembers a charming girl with pleasure, and is willing to dine where he may see her again. Before he took leave, Mr.", "그녀처럼 그가 두 사람의 운명이 하나로 엮이는 어떤 미래를 짜고 있었던 것은 아니지만, 남자란 매력적인 처녀를 기분 좋게 기억하기 마련이고, 그녀를 다시 볼 수 있는 곳이라면 기꺼이 만찬에 가는 법이다."], ["Vincy had given that invitation which he had been “in no hurry about,”", "빈시 씨는 자리를 뜨기 전에, 그가 “전혀 서두르지 않았던” 그 초대를 건넸다."], ["for Rosamond at breakfast had mentioned that she thought her uncle Featherstone had taken the new doctor into great favor.", "로저먼드가 아침 식사 자리에서, 페더스톤 외삼촌이 그 새 의사를 무척 마음에 들어 하시는 것 같다고 말했기 때문이었다."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode, alone with his brother-in-law, poured himself out a glass of water, and opened a sandwich-box.", "불스트로드 씨는 처남과 단둘이 남게 되자 물 한 잔을 따르고 샌드위치 상자를 열었다."]], [["“I cannot persuade you to adopt my regimen, Vincy?”", "“자네에게 내 섭생법을 권해도 따르지 않겠지, 빈시?”"]], [["“No, no; I’ve no opinion of that system.", "“아니, 아니, 난 그런 방식엔 동의 못 하네."], ["Life wants padding,” said Mr.", "인생엔 살집이 좀 있어야지.”"], ["Vincy, unable to omit his portable theory.", "빈시 씨가 늘 지니고 다니는 지론을 빠뜨리지 못하고 말했다."], ["“However,”", "“어쨌든,”"], ["he went on, accenting the word, as if to dismiss all irrelevance, “what I came here to talk about was a little affair of my young scapegrace, Fred’s.”", "그는 무관한 이야기는 다 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 그 말에 힘을 주며 말을 이었다. “내가 여기 온 건 우리 집 망나니 녀석, 프레드에 관한 자그마한 일 때문일세.”"]], [["“That is a subject on which you and I are likely to take quite as different views as on diet, Vincy.”", "“그건 자네와 내가, 식단 문제만큼이나 사뭇 다른 견해를 가질 법한 주제로군, 빈시.”"]], [["“I hope not this time.” (Mr.", "“이번엔 아니길 바라네.”"], ["Vincy was resolved to be good-humored.) “The fact is, it’s about a whim of old Featherstone’s.", "(빈시 씨는 좋은 기분을 유지하기로 작정하고 있었다.) “실은 늙은 페더스톤의 변덕 때문일세."], ["Somebody has been cooking up a story out of spite, and telling it to the old man, to try to set him against Fred.", "누군가가 앙심을 품고 이야기를 꾸며 내서는, 프레드와 사이를 틀어 놓으려고 그 노인에게 일러바쳤네."], ["He’s very fond of Fred, and is likely to do something handsome for him; indeed he has as good as told Fred that he means to leave him his land, and that makes other people jealous.”", "영감은 프레드를 무척 아끼고, 그 애에게 무언가 후한 일을 해 줄 모양이야. 사실 자기 땅을 프레드에게 남기겠다고 그 애한테 거의 말한 거나 다름없고, 그래서 다른 사람들이 시샘하는 거지.”"]], [["“Vincy, I must repeat, that you will not get any concurrence from me as to the course you have pursued with your eldest son.", "“빈시, 다시 한번 말하지만, 자네가 맏아들을 두고 취해 온 방침에 관해서는 나로부터 어떤 동의도 얻지 못할 걸세."], ["It was entirely from worldly vanity that you destined him for the Church: with a family of three sons and four daughters, you were not warranted in devoting money to an expensive education which has succeeded in nothing but in giving him extravagant idle habits.", "자네가 그 애를 성직자로 정한 것은 전적으로 세속적 허영심에서였네. 아들 셋에 딸 넷을 둔 처지에, 결국 그 애에게 사치스럽고 게으른 습관만 길러 주고 만 값비싼 교육에 돈을 들일 명분이 자네에겐 없었어."], ["You are now reaping the consequences.”", "이제 자네는 그 결과를 거두고 있는 걸세.”"]], [["To point out other people’s errors was a duty that Mr. Bulstrode rarely shrank from, but Mr.", "남의 잘못을 지적하는 일은 불스트로드 씨가 좀처럼 회피하지 않는 의무였으나, 빈시 씨는 그만큼 참을성 있게 받아들일 준비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["Vincy was not equally prepared to be patient. When a man has the immediate prospect of being mayor, and is ready, in the interests of commerce, to take up a firm attitude on politics generally, he has naturally a sense of his importance to the framework of things which seems to throw questions of private conduct into the background.", "머지않아 시장이 될 전망을 가진 사람이, 상업의 이익을 위해 정치 전반에 단호한 태도를 취할 각오가 되어 있을 때면, 그는 자연히 자신이 세상의 틀에 중요한 존재라는 의식을 갖게 되고, 그런 의식은 개인적 처신의 문제 따위를 뒷전으로 밀어 버리는 듯하다."], ["And this particular reproof irritated him more than any other.", "그리고 이 특정한 질책은 다른 어떤 것보다도 그를 짜증나게 했다."], ["It was eminently superfluous to him to be told that he was reaping the consequences.", "그가 그 결과를 거두고 있다는 말을 듣는 것은 그에게 더없이 쓸데없는 일이었다."], ["But he felt his neck under Bulstrode’s yoke; and though he usually enjoyed kicking, he was anxious to refrain from that relief.", "하지만 그는 자기 목이 불스트로드의 멍에 아래 놓여 있음을 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 평소 발길질을 즐기는 편이었으나, 이번에는 그 후련함을 자제하려고 안간힘을 썼다."]], [["“As to that, Bulstrode, it’s no use going back.", "“그 점에 관해서는, 불스트로드, 거슬러 따져 봤자 소용없네."], ["I’m not one of your pattern men, and I don’t pretend to be.", "난 자네 같은 본보기 인간이 아니고, 그런 척하지도 않아."], ["I couldn’t foresee everything in the trade; there wasn’t a finer business in Middlemarch than ours, and the lad was clever.", "장사에서 모든 걸 미리 내다볼 수는 없었네. 미들마치에 우리 가게보다 더 번창한 사업은 없었고, 그 녀석은 영리했지."], ["My poor brother was in the Church, and would have done well—had got preferment already, but that stomach fever took him off: else he might have been a dean by this time.", "내 가엾은 형은 성직에 있었고, 잘 풀렸을 거야—이미 승진까지 했었는데, 그 위장병이 형을 데려갔지. 안 그랬으면 지금쯤 주임 사제가 됐을지도 몰라."], ["I think I was justified in what I tried to do for Fred.", "난 프레드를 위해 해 보려 한 일이 정당했다고 생각하네."], ["If you come to religion, it seems to me a man shouldn’t want to carve out his meat to an ounce beforehand:—one must trust a little to Providence and be generous.", "신앙 이야기를 꺼내자면, 사람이 제 먹을 고기를 한 온스까지 미리 잘라 두려 해서는 안 된다는 게 내 생각이야—섭리에 조금은 맡기고 너그러워야지."], ["It’s a good British feeling to try and raise your family a little: in my opinion, it’s a father’s duty to give his sons a fine chance.”", "제 가문을 조금이라도 일으켜 보려는 건 훌륭한 영국적 정서일세. 내 생각엔, 아들들에게 좋은 기회를 주는 게 아버지의 의무지.”"]], [["“I don’t wish to act otherwise than as your best friend, Vincy, when I say that what you have been uttering just now is one mass of worldliness and inconsistent folly.”", "“자네가 방금 늘어놓은 말이 온통 세속성과 모순된 어리석음 덩어리라고 내가 말하는 것은, 자네의 더없는 벗으로서 그러는 것일 뿐이네, 빈시.”"]], [["“Very well,” said Mr.", "“좋아.”"], ["Vincy, kicking in spite of resolutions, “I never professed to be anything but worldly; and, what’s more, I don’t see anybody else who is not worldly.", "빈시 씨가 결심에도 불구하고 발길질을 하며 말했다. “난 세속적인 것 말고 다른 무엇인 척한 적이 없네."], ["I suppose you don’t conduct business on what you call unworldly principles.", "게다가 세속적이지 않은 사람을 난 본 적이 없어. 자네라고 해서 이른바 비세속적인 원칙으로 사업을 하는 건 아닐 테지."], ["The only difference I see is that one worldliness is a little bit honester than another.”", "내가 보기에 유일한 차이라곤, 한쪽 세속성이 다른 쪽보다 조금 더 정직하다는 것뿐이야.”"]], [["“This kind of discussion is unfruitful, Vincy,” said Mr.", "“이런 종류의 논의는 무익하네, 빈시.”"], ["Bulstrode, who, finishing his sandwich, had thrown himself back in his chair, and shaded his eyes as if weary.", "불스트로드 씨가 말했다. 그는 샌드위치를 다 먹고 의자에 등을 기댄 채, 피곤하다는 듯 손으로 눈을 가리고 있었다."], ["“You had some more particular business.”", "“좀 더 구체적인 용건이 있었을 텐데.”"]], [["“Yes, yes.", "“그래, 그래."], ["The long and short of it is, somebody has told old Featherstone, giving you as the authority, that Fred has been borrowing or trying to borrow money on the prospect of his land.", "결론만 말하자면, 누군가가 자네를 출처로 대면서, 프레드가 자기 땅을 담보로 돈을 빌렸거나 빌리려 했다고 늙은 페더스톤에게 일러바쳤다네."], ["Of course you never said any such nonsense.", "물론 자네가 그런 허튼소리를 한 적은 없겠지."], ["But the old fellow will insist on it that Fred should bring him a denial in your handwriting; that is, just a bit of a note saying you don’t believe a word of such stuff, either of his having borrowed or tried to borrow in such a fool’s way.", "하지만 그 영감은, 프레드가 자네 필체로 된 부인서를 가져와야 한다고 우기네. 그러니까, 그 애가 그런 바보 같은 식으로 돈을 빌렸다거나 빌리려 했다거나 하는 그런 소리는 한마디도 믿지 않는다고 적은 짤막한 쪽지 말일세."], ["I suppose you can have no objection to do that.”", "그 정도는 해 주는 데 거리낄 게 없겠지.”"]], [["“Pardon me.", "“미안하네."], ["I have an objection.", "거리끼는 게 있네."], ["I am by no means sure that your son, in his recklessness and ignorance—I will use no severer word—has not tried to raise money by holding out his future prospects, or even that some one may not have been foolish enough to supply him on so vague a presumption: there is plenty of such lax money-lending as of other folly in the world.”", "나는 자네 아들이 그 무모함과 무지로—이보다 더 심한 말은 쓰지 않겠네—제 장래의 전망을 내세워 돈을 마련하려 들지 않았다고 결코 확신할 수 없고, 또 누군가가 그런 막연한 추정을 믿고 그 애에게 돈을 댈 만큼 어리석지 않았으리라고도 확신할 수 없네. 세상에는 다른 온갖 어리석음과 마찬가지로 그런 헤픈 대부 행위가 넘쳐 나니까.”"]], [["“But Fred gives me his honor that he has never borrowed money on the pretence of any understanding about his uncle’s land.", "“하지만 프레드는 외삼촌의 땅에 관한 어떤 묵계를 핑계로 돈을 빌린 적이 결단코 없다고 내게 명예를 걸고 말하네. 그 애는 거짓말쟁이가 아니야."], ["He is not a liar. I don’t want to make him better than he is.", "난 그 애를 실제보다 더 나은 사람으로 만들 생각은 없네."], ["I have blown him up well—nobody can say I wink at what he does. But he is not a liar.", "난 그 애를 호되게 꾸짖었어—내가 그 애 행실을 못 본 척한다고는 아무도 못 할 거야."], ["And I should have thought—but I may be wrong—that there was no religion to hinder a man from believing the best of a young fellow, when you don’t know worse.", "하지만 그 애는 거짓말쟁이가 아닐세. 그리고 내 생각엔—내가 틀릴 수도 있지만—더 나쁜 사정을 알지 못하는 한, 젊은이를 좋게 믿어 주는 것을 가로막는 신앙 따위는 없을 줄 알았네."], ["It seems to me it would be a poor sort of religion to put a spoke in his wheel by refusing to say you don’t believe such harm of him as you’ve got no good reason to believe.”", "믿을 만한 합당한 근거도 없는 해악을 그 애가 저질렀다고 믿지 않는다는 말조차 거부함으로써 그 애의 바퀴에 막대기를 끼우는 거라면, 그건 참 보잘것없는 신앙으로 보이는군.”"]], [["“I am not at all sure that I should be befriending your son by smoothing his way to the future possession of Featherstone’s property.", "“페더스톤의 재산을 장차 손에 넣도록 자네 아들의 앞길을 닦아 주는 것이 과연 그 애를 위하는 일인지 나는 도무지 확신할 수 없네."], ["I cannot regard wealth as a blessing to those who use it simply as a harvest for this world.", "나는 부를, 그것을 그저 이 세상의 수확으로만 쓰는 자들에게 축복이라고 여길 수 없어. 자네가 이런 말을 듣기 싫어하는 줄은 아네, 빈시."], ["You do not like to hear these things, Vincy, but on this occasion I feel called upon to tell you that I have no motive for furthering such a disposition of property as that which you refer to.", "하지만 이번만큼은 자네에게 말해야겠다는 사명감을 느끼네. 자네가 말하는 그런 식의 재산 처분을 거들 동기가 내겐 없네."], ["I do not shrink from saying that it will not tend to your son’s eternal welfare or to the glory of God.", "그것이 자네 아들의 영원한 복락에도, 신의 영광에도 이바지하지 않으리라고 나는 거리낌 없이 말하겠어."], ["Why then should you expect me to pen this kind of affidavit, which has no object but to keep up a foolish partiality and secure a foolish bequest?”", "그렇다면 자네는 어찌 내게, 어리석은 편애를 부추기고 어리석은 유증을 확보하는 것 말고는 아무 목적도 없는 그런 진술서를 쓰라고 기대하는 건가?”"]], [["“If you mean to hinder everybody from having money but saints and evangelists, you must give up some profitable partnerships, that’s all I can say,”", "“성자와 복음 전도자 말고는 아무도 돈을 갖지 못하게 막을 작정이라면, 자네는 수지맞는 동업 몇 개는 포기해야 할 걸세. 내가 할 말은 그뿐이야.”"], ["Mr. Vincy burst out very bluntly.", "빈시 씨가 매우 퉁명스럽게 내뱉었다."], ["“It may be for the glory of God, but it is not for the glory of the Middlemarch trade, that Plymdale’s house uses those blue and green dyes it gets from the Brassing manufactory; they rot the silk, that’s all I know about it.", "“신의 영광을 위한 일일지는 몰라도, 플림데일네 가게가 브래싱 공장에서 들여오는 그 파랗고 푸른 염료를 쓰는 건 미들마치 상업의 영광을 위한 일이 아닐세. 그 염료는 비단을 썩게 하지, 내가 아는 건 그게 다야."], ["Perhaps if other people knew so much of the profit went to the glory of God, they might like it better.", "어쩌면 그 이익이 그렇게나 신의 영광으로 돌아간다는 걸 사람들이 안다면, 그걸 더 좋아할지도 모르지."], ["But I don’t mind so much about that—I could get up a pretty row, if I chose.”", "하지만 난 그런 건 별로 개의치 않네—내가 마음만 먹으면 제법 한바탕 소동을 일으킬 수도 있어.”"]], [["Mr. Bulstrode paused a little before he answered.", "불스트로드 씨는 대답하기 전에 잠시 뜸을 들였다."], ["“You pain me very much by speaking in this way, Vincy.", "“자네가 그런 식으로 말하니 나는 몹시 가슴이 아프네, 빈시."], ["I do not expect you to understand my grounds of action—it is not an easy thing even to thread a path for principles in the intricacies of the world—still less to make the thread clear for the careless and the scoffing.", "자네가 내 행동의 근거를 이해하리라고는 기대하지 않아—세상의 복잡한 얽힘 속에서 원칙이 나아갈 길을 한 가닥 더듬어 내는 일조차 쉽지 않은데, 하물며 그 가닥을 무심하고 비웃는 자들에게 또렷이 보여 주는 일이야 더더욱 그렇지."], ["You must remember, if you please, that I stretch my tolerance towards you as my wife’s brother, and that it little becomes you to complain of me as withholding material help towards the worldly position of your family.", "부디 기억해 주게나, 나는 자네를 내 아내의 오라비로서 너그러이 대하고 있다는 것을, 그리고 내가 자네 가문의 세속적 지위를 위한 물질적 도움을 베풀지 않는다고 자네가 나를 탓하는 것은 자네에게 어울리지 않는다는 것을."], ["I must remind you that it is not your own prudence or judgment that has enabled you to keep your place in the trade.”", "자네가 그 장사에서 자리를 지킬 수 있었던 것이 자네 자신의 분별이나 판단 덕분이 아니었음을 내가 일깨워야겠네.”"]], [["“Very likely not; but you have been no loser by my trade yet,”", "“그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 자넨 지금껏 내 장사로 손해 본 적이 없잖나.”"], ["said Mr. Vincy, thoroughly nettled (a result which was seldom much retarded by previous resolutions).", "빈시 씨가 완전히 발끈해서 말했다."], ["“And when you married Harriet, I don’t see how you could expect that our families should not hang by the same nail.", "(그런 결과는 미리 한 결심으로도 좀처럼 오래 미뤄지지 않았다.) “그리고 자네가 해리엇과 결혼했을 때, 우리 두 집안이 같은 못에 함께 걸리지 않으리라고 어떻게 기대할 수 있었겠나?"], ["If you’ve changed your mind, and want my family to come down in the world, you’d better say so.", "자네 마음이 바뀌어서 우리 집안이 영락하기를 바란다면, 차라리 그렇게 말하게. 난 한 번도 바뀐 적이 없어."], ["I’ve never changed; I’m a plain Churchman now, just as I used to be before doctrines came up.", "난 지금도 그저 평범한 국교도일세, 교리 따위가 나오기 전에 그랬던 것처럼 말이야."], ["I take the world as I find it, in trade and everything else.", "난 장사에서든 다른 무엇에서든, 세상을 있는 그대로 받아들이네."], ["I’m contented to be no worse than my neighbors.", "난 이웃보다 나쁘지만 않으면 만족해."], ["But if you want us to come down in the world, say so.", "하지만 자네가 우리를 영락시키고 싶다면, 그렇게 말하게."], ["I shall know better what to do then.”", "그러면 난 어떻게 해야 할지 더 잘 알게 될 테니.”"]], [["“You talk unreasonably.", "“말도 안 되는 소리를 하는군."], ["Shall you come down in the world for want of this letter about your son?”", "자네 아들에 관한 이 편지가 없다고 자네 집안이 영락하기라도 한단 말인가?”"]], [["“Well, whether or not, I consider it very unhandsome of you to refuse it.", "“글쎄, 그러든 말든, 자네가 그걸 거절하는 건 매우 야박한 짓이라고 생각하네."], ["Such doings may be lined with religion, but outside they have a nasty, dog-in-the-manger look.", "그런 짓이 겉으로는 신앙으로 안감을 댔을지 몰라도, 바깥에서 보면 고약하고 심술궂기 짝이 없어 보이네."], ["You might as well slander Fred: it comes pretty near to it when you refuse to say you didn’t set a slander going.", "차라리 프레드를 중상모략하는 편이 낫겠어. 자네가 중상을 퍼뜨린 적 없다고 말하기를 거부하는 건 거의 그것과 마찬가지니까."], ["It’s this sort of thing—this tyrannical spirit, wanting to play bishop and banker everywhere—it’s this sort of thing makes a man’s name stink.”", "바로 이런 식이지—이 폭압적인 기질, 어디서나 주교 노릇에 은행가 노릇까지 하려 드는 이 기질—바로 이런 식이 사람의 이름에서 악취가 나게 만드는 걸세.”"]], [["“Vincy, if you insist on quarrelling with me, it will be exceedingly painful to Harriet as well as myself,”", "“빈시, 자네가 굳이 나와 다투려 든다면, 그건 나뿐 아니라 해리엇에게도 몹시 가슴 아픈 일이 될 걸세.”"], ["said Mr. Bulstrode, with a trifle more eagerness and paleness than usual.", "불스트로드 씨가 평소보다 조금 더 다급하고 핏기 없는 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“I don’t want to quarrel.", "“난 다투고 싶지 않네."], ["It’s for my interest—and perhaps for yours too—that we should be friends.", "우리가 친구로 지내는 게 내게 이로운 일이고—어쩌면 자네에게도 그럴 테지."], ["I bear you no grudge; I think no worse of you than I do of other people.", "난 자네에게 아무 원한도 없어. 난 자네를 다른 사람들보다 더 나쁘게 여기지 않네."], ["A man who half starves himself, and goes the length in family prayers, and so on, that you do, believes in his religion whatever it may be: you could turn over your capital just as fast with cursing and swearing:—plenty of fellows do.", "자네처럼 제 몸을 반쯤 굶기다시피 하고, 가족 기도며 뭐며 그렇게까지 하는 사람은, 그 신앙이 어떤 것이든 간에 자기 신앙을 믿는 거지. 자넨 욕설을 퍼붓고 악담을 하면서도 똑같이 빠르게 자본을 굴릴 수 있을 걸세—그렇게 하는 친구들이 수두룩하니까."], ["You like to be master, there’s no denying that; you must be first chop in heaven, else you won’t like it much.", "자네가 우두머리 노릇을 좋아한다는 건 부인할 수 없는 사실이지. 자넨 천국에서도 일등급이 되어야 할 거야, 안 그러면 별로 마음에 들지 않을 테니."], ["But you’re my sister’s husband, and we ought to stick together; and if I know Harriet, she’ll consider it your fault if we quarrel because you strain at a gnat in this way, and refuse to do Fred a good turn.", "하지만 자넨 내 누이의 남편이고, 우린 서로 뭉쳐야 하네. 그리고 내가 해리엇을 안다면, 자네가 이런 식으로 하루살이를 걸러 내듯 까다롭게 굴고 프레드에게 호의 한 번 베풀기를 거부해서 우리가 다투게 된다면, 그건 자네 잘못이라고 여길 걸세."], ["And I don’t mean to say I shall bear it well.", "그리고 난 그걸 의연하게 견디겠다고 말하진 못하겠네."], ["I consider it unhandsome.”", "난 이게 야박한 짓이라고 생각해.”"]], [["Mr. Vincy rose, began to button his great-coat, and looked steadily at his brother-in-law, meaning to imply a demand for a decisive answer.", "빈시 씨는 일어서서 외투 단추를 채우기 시작하며, 단호한 답을 요구한다는 뜻을 내비치려는 듯 처남을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["This was not the first time that Mr. Bulstrode had begun by admonishing Mr.", "불스트로드 씨가 빈시 씨를 훈계하는 것으로 시작했다가, 그 제조업자의 정신이 동료 인간들의 더 미묘한 빛과 그림자를 향해 들이대는 거칠고 아첨할 줄 모르는 거울 속에서 자기 자신의 몹시 못마땅한 모습을 보는 것으로 끝난 것이 이번이 처음은 아니었다."], ["Vincy, and had ended by seeing a very unsatisfactory reflection of himself in the coarse unflattering mirror which that manufacturer’s mind presented to the subtler lights and shadows of his fellow-men; and perhaps his experience ought to have warned him how the scene would end.", "어쩌면 그 경험이 이 장면이 어떻게 끝날지를 그에게 미리 일러 주었어야 했으리라."], ["But a full-fed fountain will be generous with its waters even in the rain, when they are worse than useless; and a fine fount of admonition is apt to be equally irrepressible.", "그러나 가득 찬 샘은 그 물이 아무 쓸모도 없는 빗속에서조차 제 물을 아낌없이 내어 주는 법이고, 훌륭한 훈계의 샘 또한 그처럼 억누를 수 없기 마련이다."]], [["It was not in Mr. Bulstrode’s nature to comply directly in consequence of uncomfortable suggestions.", "불편한 제안 때문에 곧바로 응하는 것은 불스트로드 씨의 천성에 맞지 않았다."], ["Before changing his course, he always needed to shape his motives and bring them into accordance with his habitual standard.", "진로를 바꾸기 전에 그는 언제나 자신의 동기를 빚어내어 그것을 자신의 습관적인 기준에 부합시켜야 했다."], ["He said, at last—", "마침내 그가 말했다—"]], [["“I will reflect a little, Vincy.", "“좀 생각해 보겠네, 빈시."], ["I will mention the subject to Harriet.", "해리엇에게 이 문제를 이야기해 보지."], ["I shall probably send you a letter.”", "아마 자네에게 편지를 보내게 될 걸세.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“좋아."], ["As soon as you can, please.", "되도록 빨리 부탁하네."], ["I hope it will all be settled before I see you to-morrow.”", "내일 자네를 만나기 전에 다 정리되었으면 좋겠군.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XIV.", "# CHAPTER XIV."]], [["“Follows here the strict receipt For that sauce to dainty meat, Named Idleness, which many eat By preference, and call it sweet: First watch for morsels, like a hound Mix well with buffets, stir them round With good thick oil of flatteries, And froth with mean self-lauding lies.", "여기 까다로운 입맛에 맞춘 그 소스의 정확한 비법이 있으니, 이름하여 게으름이라, 많은 이들이 즐겨 먹으며 달다고 일컫는 것. 먼저 사냥개처럼 한 입거리를 노리고, 주먹질을 잘 섞어 휘저으며, 걸쭉한 아첨의 기름을 넉넉히 두르고, 비열한 자화자찬의 거짓말로 거품을 낸다."], ["Serve warm: the vessels you must choose To keep it in are dead men’s shoes.”", "따뜻하게 내어라. 그것을 담아 둘 그릇으로는 죽은 자의 신발을 골라야 하느니."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode’s consultation of Harriet seemed to have had the effect desired by Mr.", "벌스트로드 씨가 해리엇에게 의논한 일은 빈시 씨가 바라던 효과를 거둔 듯했다."], ["Vincy, for early the next morning a letter came which Fred could carry to Mr. Featherstone as the required testimony.", "이튿날 아침 일찍 편지 한 통이 도착했고, 프레드는 그것을 페더스톤 씨에게 가져가 요구된 증거로 내놓을 수 있었던 것이다."]], [["The old gentleman was staying in bed on account of the cold weather, and as Mary Garth was not to be seen in the sitting-room, Fred went up-stairs immediately and presented the letter to his uncle, who, propped up comfortably on a bed-rest, was not less able than usual to enjoy his consciousness of wisdom in distrusting and frustrating mankind.", "노신사는 추운 날씨 탓에 침대에 누워 있었는데, 메리 가스가 거실에 보이지 않자 프레드는 곧장 위층으로 올라가 외삼촌에게 편지를 내밀었다. 그는 등받이에 편안히 기대앉아, 평소와 다름없이 인류를 불신하고 좌절시키는 자신의 지혜를 의식하며 흐뭇해할 능력을 잃지 않은 상태였다."], ["He put on his spectacles to read the letter, pursing up his lips and drawing down their corners.", "그는 편지를 읽으려고 안경을 쓰면서 입술을 오므리고 그 양 끝을 아래로 끌어내렸다."]], [["“Under the circumstances I will not decline to state my conviction—tchah!", "\"'사정이 이러하므로 저의 확신을 밝히기를 거절하지 않겠습니다'—체!"], ["what fine words the fellow puts!", "이 친구가 무슨 거창한 말을 늘어놓는 거야!"], ["He’s as fine as an auctioneer—that your son Frederic has not obtained any advance of money on bequests promised by Mr. Featherstone—promised?", "경매인만큼이나 그럴듯하게 말하는군—'귀하의 아드님 프레더릭이 페더스톤 씨께서 약속하신 유증을 담보로 어떤 금전적 선급도 받은 바 없으며'—약속?"], ["who said I had ever promised?", "누가 내가 약속했다고 했나?"], ["I promise nothing—I shall make codicils as long as I like—and that considering the nature of such a proceeding, it is unreasonable to presume that a young man of sense and character would attempt it—ah, but the gentleman doesn’t say you are a young man of sense and character, mark you that, sir!—As to my own concern with any report of such a nature, I distinctly affirm that I never made any statement to the effect that your son had borrowed money on any property that might accrue to him on Mr.", "나는 아무것도 약속한 게 없어—나는 내가 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 유언 보충서를 만들 거야—'또한 그러한 행위의 성격을 고려할 때, 분별력과 인품을 갖춘 청년이 그것을 시도하리라고 추정하는 것은 불합리한 일입니다'—아, 하지만 이 신사 양반은 네가 분별력과 인품을 갖춘 청년이라고는 말하지 않는군, 그 점을 새겨 둬라, 이놈아!—'그러한 성격의 소문에 제가 관련되어 있다는 점에 관하여는, 귀하의 아드님이 페더스톤 씨 사후에 그에게 돌아올 어떤 재산을 담보로 돈을 빌렸다는 취지의 진술을 결코 한 적이 없음을 분명히 단언하는 바입니다'—이런 세상에!"], ["Featherstone’s demise—bless my heart!", "'재산'—'돌아올'—'사후'라니!"], ["‘property’—accrue—demise! Lawyer Standish is nothing to him.", "변호사 스탠디시도 이 친구에 비하면 아무것도 아니야."], ["He couldn’t speak finer if he wanted to borrow.", "돈을 빌리려 할 때조차도 이보다 더 그럴듯하게 말하지는 못할 거다."], ["Well,” Mr.", "자,\""], ["Featherstone here looked over his spectacles at Fred, while he handed back the letter to him with a contemptuous gesture, “you don’t suppose I believe a thing because Bulstrode writes it out fine, eh?”", "페더스톤 씨는 여기서 안경 너머로 프레드를 쳐다보며 경멸하는 몸짓으로 편지를 돌려주었다. \"벌스트로드가 그럴듯하게 써 놓았다고 해서 내가 그 말을 믿을 줄 아느냐, 응?\""]], [["Fred colored.", "프레드는 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“You wished to have the letter, sir.", "\"외삼촌께서 편지를 원하셨잖아요."], ["I should think it very likely that Mr. Bulstrode’s denial is as good as the authority which told you what he denies.”", "제 생각엔 벌스트로드 씨가 부인하는 말이, 그가 부인하는 그 내용을 외삼촌께 일러바친 사람의 권위만큼은 믿을 만한 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Every bit.", "\"꼭 그만큼이지."], ["I never said I believed either one or the other.", "나는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 믿는다고 말한 적이 없다."], ["And now what d’ you expect?”", "그래, 이제 뭘 기대하느냐?\""], ["said Mr. Featherstone, curtly, keeping on his spectacles, but withdrawing his hands under his wraps.", "페더스톤 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 말하며 안경은 그대로 쓴 채 두 손을 이불 속으로 거두어들였다."]], [["“I expect nothing, sir.”", "\"아무것도 기대하지 않습니다, 외삼촌.\""], ["Fred with difficulty restrained himself from venting his irritation.", "프레드는 짜증을 터뜨리고 싶은 마음을 가까스로 억눌렀다."], ["“I came to bring you the letter.", "\"편지를 가져다 드리려고 왔습니다."], ["If you like I will bid you good morning.”", "원하신다면 이만 물러가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Not yet, not yet.", "\"아직, 아직 아니다."], ["Ring the bell; I want missy to come.”", "종을 울려라. 아가씨를 들어오게 해야겠다.\""]], [["It was a servant who came in answer to the bell.", "종소리에 응해 들어온 것은 하인이었다."]], [["“Tell missy to come!” said Mr.", "\"아가씨를 들어오라고 해!\""], ["Featherstone, impatiently.", "페더스톤 씨가 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["“What business had she to go away?”", "\"도대체 무슨 일로 나가 있는 거야?\""], ["He spoke in the same tone when Mary came.", "메리가 들어왔을 때도 그는 같은 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Why couldn’t you sit still here till I told you to go?", "\"내가 가라고 할 때까지 여기 가만히 앉아 있을 수는 없었더냐?"], ["I want my waistcoat now.", "이제 조끼를 다오."], ["I told you always to put it on the bed.”", "늘 침대 위에 올려놓으라고 일렀잖느냐.\""]], [["Mary’s eyes looked rather red, as if she had been crying.", "메리의 눈은 마치 울었던 것처럼 다소 붉어 보였다."], ["It was clear that Mr. Featherstone was in one of his most snappish humors this morning, and though Fred had now the prospect of receiving the much-needed present of money, he would have preferred being free to turn round on the old tyrant and tell him that Mary Garth was too good to be at his beck.", "페더스톤 씨가 그날 아침 가장 까칠한 기분에 빠져 있는 것이 분명했고, 프레드는 이제 그토록 절실히 필요했던 돈을 선물로 받게 될 전망이었음에도, 차라리 자유로운 몸이 되어 그 늙은 폭군을 돌아보며 메리 가스는 당신이 부른다고 굽실거릴 사람이 아니라고 말해 주고 싶었다."], ["Though Fred had risen as she entered the room, she had barely noticed him, and looked as if her nerves were quivering with the expectation that something would be thrown at her.", "그녀가 방에 들어왔을 때 프레드가 일어섰지만, 그녀는 그를 거의 알아보지도 못했고, 무언가가 자기에게 날아오리라는 예감에 신경이 떨리는 듯한 표정이었다."], ["But she never had anything worse than words to dread.", "하지만 그녀가 두려워해야 할 것이라곤 말 이상의 것은 결코 없었다."], ["When she went to reach the waistcoat from a peg, Fred went up to her and said, “Allow me.”", "그녀가 못에서 조끼를 집으려 다가가자 프레드가 그녀에게 가서 말했다. \"제가 하지요.\""]], [["“Let it alone!", "\"내버려 둬!"], ["You bring it, missy, and lay it down here,”", "네가 가져와라, 아가씨, 여기 내려놔.\""], ["said Mr. Featherstone.", "페더스톤 씨가 말했다."], ["“Now you go away again till I call you,”", "\"이제 다시 나가 있다가 내가 부르거든 오너라.\""], ["he added, when the waistcoat was laid down by him.", "조끼가 그의 옆에 놓이자 그가 덧붙였다."], ["It was usual with him to season his pleasure in showing favor to one person by being especially disagreeable to another, and Mary was always at hand to furnish the condiment.", "한 사람에게 호의를 베푸는 즐거움에다 다른 사람에게 유난히 못되게 구는 것으로 양념을 치는 것이 그의 버릇이었는데, 메리는 언제나 곁에 있어 그 양념거리를 제공해 주었다."], ["When his own relatives came she was treated better.", "그의 친척들이 올 때면 그녀는 더 나은 대접을 받았다."], ["Slowly he took out a bunch of keys from the waistcoat pocket, and slowly he drew forth a tin box which was under the bed-clothes.", "그는 천천히 조끼 주머니에서 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내고, 천천히 이불 밑에 있던 양철 상자를 끄집어냈다."]], [["“You expect I am going to give you a little fortune, eh?”", "\"내가 너한테 작은 재산이라도 줄 거라고 기대하느냐, 응?\""], ["he said, looking above his spectacles and pausing in the act of opening the lid.", "그가 안경 너머로 올려다보며 뚜껑을 여는 동작을 멈춘 채 말했다."]], [["“Not at all, sir.", "\"전혀 아닙니다, 외삼촌."], ["You were good enough to speak of making me a present the other day, else, of course, I should not have thought of the matter.”", "며칠 전에 친절하게도 제게 선물을 주겠다고 말씀하셨으니 드린 말씀이지, 그렇지 않았다면 물론 그 일을 생각조차 하지 않았을 겁니다.\""], ["But Fred was of a hopeful disposition, and a vision had presented itself of a sum just large enough to deliver him from a certain anxiety.", "하지만 프레드는 낙천적인 성품이었고, 어떤 걱정거리에서 자신을 벗어나게 해 줄 만큼 딱 충분한 액수의 환상이 그의 머릿속에 떠올라 있었다."], ["When Fred got into debt, it always seemed to him highly probable that something or other—he did not necessarily conceive what—would come to pass enabling him to pay in due time.", "프레드는 빚을 지면 언제나, 무언가가—그것이 무엇인지 굳이 떠올리지는 않았지만—일어나 제때에 갚을 수 있게 되리라는 것이 지극히 그럴듯한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["And now that the providential occurrence was apparently close at hand, it would have been sheer absurdity to think that the supply would be short of the need: as absurd as a faith that believed in half a miracle for want of strength to believe in a whole one.", "그리고 이제 그 섭리적인 사건이 분명 코앞에 닥친 듯하니, 그 공급이 필요에 못 미치리라고 생각하는 것은 순전한 어리석음일 터였다. 온전한 기적을 믿을 힘이 없어 반쪽짜리 기적을 믿는 신앙만큼이나 어리석은 일이었다."]], [["The deep-veined hands fingered many bank-notes one after the other, laying them down flat again, while Fred leaned back in his chair, scorning to look eager.", "깊은 핏줄이 도드라진 손이 지폐를 한 장 한 장 만지작거리다가 다시 평평하게 내려놓는 동안, 프레드는 안달하는 모습을 보이기를 경멸하며 의자에 등을 기대고 있었다."], ["He held himself to be a gentleman at heart, and did not like courting an old fellow for his money. At last, Mr.", "그는 마음속으로 자신을 신사라 여겼고, 돈을 노리고 노인에게 굽실거리는 것을 좋아하지 않았다."], ["Featherstone eyed him again over his spectacles and presented him with a little sheaf of notes: Fred could see distinctly that there were but five, as the less significant edges gaped towards him.", "마침내 페더스톤 씨는 안경 너머로 다시 그를 살피더니 작은 지폐 뭉치를 건넸다. 프레드는 덜 중요한 가장자리들이 그를 향해 벌어져 있어 그것이 다섯 장뿐임을 또렷이 알 수 있었다."], ["But then, each might mean fifty pounds.", "하지만 그래도 한 장이 오십 파운드를 뜻할 수도 있었다."], ["He took them, saying—", "그는 그것을 받으며 말했다."]], [["“I am very much obliged to you, sir,”", "\"정말 감사합니다, 외삼촌.\""], ["and was going to roll them up without seeming to think of their value.", "그러고는 그 가치를 생각하지 않는 척하며 돌돌 말아 넣으려 했다."], ["But this did not suit Mr. Featherstone, who was eying him intently.", "하지만 그것은 그를 유심히 지켜보던 페더스톤 씨의 마음에 들지 않았다."]], [["“Come, don’t you think it worth your while to count ’em?", "\"이봐, 그걸 세어 볼 가치도 없다고 생각하느냐?"], ["You take money like a lord; I suppose you lose it like one.”", "너는 영주처럼 돈을 받는구나. 영주처럼 잃어버리겠지, 아마.\""]], [["“I thought I was not to look a gift-horse in the mouth, sir.", "\"받은 말의 입 속을 들여다보지 말라*고 하지 않습니까, 외삼촌."], ["But I shall be very happy to count them.”", "하지만 기꺼이 세어 보겠습니다.\""]], [["Fred was not so happy, however, after he had counted them.", "* 받은 말의 입 속을 들여다보지 말라: 선물의 흠을 따지지 말라는 뜻의 서양 속담."], ["For they actually presented the absurdity of being less than his hopefulness had decided that they must be.", "말의 나이는 이빨로 가늠한다는 데서 유래했다. 하지만 다 세어 보고 나니 프레드는 그렇게 기쁘지 않았다."], ["What can the fitness of things mean, if not their fitness to a man’s expectations?", "그것은 실제로 그의 낙관이 분명 그래야 한다고 결정해 두었던 액수보다 적다는 어처구니없음을 드러냈던 것이다."], ["Failing this, absurdity and atheism gape behind him.", "사물의 합당함이란 사람의 기대에 들어맞는 합당함이 아니라면 대체 무엇을 뜻하겠는가?"], ["The collapse for Fred was severe when he found that he held no more than five twenties, and his share in the higher education of this country did not seem to help him.", "그것이 어긋날 때 어처구니없음과 무신론이 그의 등 뒤에서 입을 벌린다. 자기가 쥔 것이 이십 파운드짜리 다섯 장에 지나지 않음을 알았을 때 프레드가 받은 충격은 가혹했고, 이 나라의 고등 교육에 그가 참여한 몫도 그에게 도움이 되어 주는 것 같지 않았다."], ["Nevertheless he said, with rapid changes in his fair complexion—", "그럼에도 그는 고운 얼굴빛을 빠르게 바꾸어 가며 말했다."]], [["“It is very handsome of you, sir.”", "\"정말 후하십니다, 외삼촌.\""]], [["“I should think it is,”", "\"그렇고말고.\""], ["said Mr.", "페더스톤 씨는 상자를 잠그고 제자리에 도로 넣은 뒤, 천천히 안경을 벗었다."], ["Featherstone, locking his box and replacing it, then taking off his spectacles deliberately, and at length, as if his inward meditation had more deeply convinced him, repeating, “I should think it handsome.”", "그러고는 마침내 마음속 명상이 그를 한층 더 깊이 납득시키기라도 한 듯 되풀이했다. \"후하다고 봐야지.\""]], [["“I assure you, sir, I am very grateful,”", "\"정말이지, 외삼촌, 무척 감사드립니다.\""], ["said Fred, who had had time to recover his cheerful air.", "명랑한 태도를 되찾을 시간을 가진 프레드가 말했다."]], [["“So you ought to be.", "\"그래야 마땅하지."], ["You want to cut a figure in the world, and I reckon Peter Featherstone is the only one you’ve got to trust to.”", "너는 세상에서 한자리 차지하고 싶어 하는데, 네가 믿을 만한 사람은 이 피터 페더스톤뿐이라고 나는 본다.\""], ["Here the old man’s eyes gleamed with a curiously mingled satisfaction in the consciousness that this smart young fellow relied upon him, and that the smart young fellow was rather a fool for doing so.", "여기서 노인의 두 눈은 이 영리한 젊은이가 자기를 의지하고 있다는 의식과, 그렇게 의지하다니 이 영리한 젊은이가 어지간히 바보라는 의식이 묘하게 뒤섞인 만족감으로 번득였다."]], [["“Yes, indeed: I was not born to very splendid chances.", "\"네, 정말 그렇습니다. 저는 그리 화려한 기회를 타고나지 못했지요."], ["Few men have been more cramped than I have been,”", "저만큼 옹색하게 살아온 사람도 드물 겁니다.\""], ["said Fred, with some sense of surprise at his own virtue, considering how hardly he was dealt with.", "프레드는 자기가 얼마나 가혹한 처지에 놓여 있는지를 생각하며, 자신의 덕성에 다소 놀라는 마음으로 말했다."], ["“It really seems a little too bad to have to ride a broken-winded hunter, and see men, who, are not half such good judges as yourself, able to throw away any amount of money on buying bad bargains.”", "\"숨이 가빠 헐떡이는 사냥말을 타야 한다는 것은, 그리고 외삼촌만큼 안목도 절반밖에 안 되는 자들이 형편없는 물건을 사들이느라 돈을 얼마든지 내던지는 꼴을 보아야 한다는 것은, 정말이지 너무하다 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Well, you can buy yourself a fine hunter now.", "\"음, 이제 너도 좋은 사냥말을 한 마리 살 수 있겠구나."], ["Eighty pound is enough for that, I reckon—and you’ll have twenty pound over to get yourself out of any little scrape,”", "그러기엔 팔십 파운드면 충분하다고 본다—그리고 이십 파운드가 남으니 무슨 자잘한 곤경에서든 빠져나올 수 있겠지.\""], ["said Mr. Featherstone, chuckling slightly.", "페더스톤 씨가 살짝 킬킬거리며 말했다."]], [["“You are very good, sir,”", "\"정말 인자하십니다, 외삼촌.\""], ["said Fred, with a fine sense of contrast between the words and his feeling.", "프레드는 그 말과 자기 심정 사이의 그럴듯한 대조를 의식하며 말했다."]], [["“Ay, rather a better uncle than your fine uncle Bulstrode.", "\"그래, 네 그 잘난 외삼촌 벌스트로드보다야 좀 나은 외삼촌이지."], ["You won’t get much out of his spekilations, I think.", "그자의 투기로는 네가 별로 건질 게 없을걸."], ["He’s got a pretty strong string round your father’s leg, by what I hear, eh?”", "내가 듣기로는 그자가 네 아버지 다리에 꽤 단단한 끈을 옭아매 놓았다던데, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["“My father never tells me anything about his affairs, sir.”", "\"제 아버지는 사업 얘기를 제게 전혀 하지 않으십니다, 외삼촌.\""]], [["“Well, he shows some sense there.", "\"흠, 그 점에선 분별이 좀 있구나."], ["But other people find ’em out without his telling.", "하지만 사람들은 그 양반이 말하지 않아도 다 알아낸단다."], ["He’ll never have much to leave you: he’ll most-like die without a will—he’s the sort of man to do it—let ’em make him mayor of Middlemarch as much as they like.", "그자가 네게 남길 재산은 별로 없을 거다. 십중팔구 유언도 없이 죽을 테고—그런 짓을 할 위인이지—사람들이 그자를 미들마치 시장으로 추대하든 말든 말이야."], ["But you won’t get much by his dying without a will, though you are the eldest son.”", "하지만 그자가 유언 없이 죽는다 해도, 네가 비록 장남이긴 하다만, 별로 얻을 게 없을 거다.\""]], [["Fred thought that Mr. Featherstone had never been so disagreeable before.", "프레드는 페더스톤 씨가 이전에 이렇게까지 못마땅하게 군 적이 없었다고 생각했다."], ["True, he had never before given him quite so much money at once.", "하기야 그가 이전에 이렇게 한꺼번에 많은 돈을 준 적도 없긴 했다."]], [["“Shall I destroy this letter of Mr. Bulstrode’s, sir?”", "\"이 벌스트로드 씨의 편지는 없애 버릴까요, 외삼촌?\""], ["said Fred, rising with the letter as if he would put it in the fire.", "프레드가 그것을 불 속에 넣기라도 할 것처럼 편지를 들고 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“Ay, ay, I don’t want it.", "\"그래, 그래, 나는 필요 없다."], ["It’s worth no money to me.”", "내겐 한 푼 값어치도 없는 거야.\""]], [["Fred carried the letter to the fire, and thrust the poker through it with much zest.", "프레드는 편지를 불가로 가져가 신이 나서 부지깽이로 푹 찔러 넣었다."], ["He longed to get out of the room, but he was a little ashamed before his inner self, as well as before his uncle, to run away immediately after pocketing the money.", "그는 방에서 나가고 싶어 안달이 났지만, 돈을 주머니에 넣자마자 곧장 달아나는 것이 외삼촌 앞에서뿐 아니라 자기 양심 앞에서도 다소 부끄러웠다."], ["Presently, the farm-bailiff came up to give his master a report, and Fred, to his unspeakable relief, was dismissed with the injunction to come again soon.", "이윽고 농장 관리인이 주인에게 보고하러 올라왔고, 프레드는 곧 또 오라는 분부를 받고 물러가게 되어 이루 말할 수 없는 안도를 느꼈다."]], [["He had longed not only to be set free from his uncle, but also to find Mary Garth.", "그는 외삼촌에게서 풀려나기를 바랐을 뿐 아니라 메리 가스를 찾기를 바라기도 했다."], ["She was now in her usual place by the fire, with sewing in her hands and a book open on the little table by her side.", "그녀는 이제 손에 바느질감을 들고 곁의 작은 탁자에 책을 펼쳐 둔 채, 늘 앉던 난롯가 자리에 있었다."], ["Her eyelids had lost some of their redness now, and she had her usual air of self-command.", "이제 그녀의 눈꺼풀은 붉은 기가 좀 가셨고, 평소의 자제하는 분위기를 되찾고 있었다."]], [["“Am I wanted up-stairs?”", "\"위층에서 저를 부르시나요?\""], ["she said, half rising as Fred entered.", "프레드가 들어서자 그녀가 반쯤 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“No; I am only dismissed, because Simmons is gone up.”", "\"아니요. 시먼스가 올라가서 저는 그냥 물러나라는 거예요.\""]], [["Mary sat down again, and resumed her work.", "메리는 다시 앉아 일감을 이어 갔다."], ["She was certainly treating him with more indifference than usual: she did not know how affectionately indignant he had felt on her behalf up-stairs.", "그녀는 분명 평소보다 더 무심하게 그를 대하고 있었다. 그가 위층에서 그녀를 위해 얼마나 애정 어린 분노를 느꼈는지 그녀는 알지 못했다."]], [["“May I stay here a little, Mary, or shall I bore you?”", "\"여기 좀 있어도 될까요, 메리, 아니면 제가 지루하게 할까요?\""]], [["“Pray sit down,”", "\"앉으세요.\""], ["said Mary; “you will not be so heavy a bore as Mr.", "메리가 말했다. \"어제 여기 왔던 존 워울 씨만큼 지루하게 굴진 않으실 테니까요."], ["John Waule, who was here yesterday, and he sat down without asking my leave.”", "그 사람은 제 허락도 구하지 않고 앉아 버리던걸요.\""]], [["“Poor fellow!", "\"가엾은 친구!"], ["I think he is in love with you.”", "제 생각엔 그가 당신을 사랑하는 것 같던데요.\""]], [["“I am not aware of it.", "\"저는 모르는 일이에요."], ["And to me it is one of the most odious things in a girl’s life, that there must always be some supposition of falling in love coming between her and any man who is kind to her, and to whom she is grateful.", "그리고 제겐, 친절하게 대해 주고 제가 고마워하는 어떤 남자와 저 사이에 늘 사랑에 빠진다는 가정이 끼어들어야 한다는 것이 여자의 삶에서 가장 역겨운 일 중 하나예요."], ["I should have thought that I, at least, might have been safe from all that.", "적어도 저만은 그런 일에서 안전할 줄 알았어요."], ["I have no ground for the nonsensical vanity of fancying everybody who comes near me is in love with me.”", "제게 가까이 오는 사람마다 저를 사랑한다고 상상하는 그런 터무니없는 허영을 부릴 근거가 제겐 없으니까요.\""]], [["Mary did not mean to betray any feeling, but in spite of herself she ended in a tremulous tone of vexation.", "메리는 어떤 감정도 드러낼 생각이 없었지만, 자기도 모르게 분함이 떨리는 어조로 끝맺고 말았다."]], [["“Confound John Waule!", "\"존 워울 따위는 빌어먹을!"], ["I did not mean to make you angry.", "당신을 화나게 할 생각은 아니었어요."], ["I didn’t know you had any reason for being grateful to me.", "당신이 제게 고마워할 이유가 있는 줄도 몰랐고요."], ["I forgot what a great service you think it if any one snuffs a candle for you.”", "누가 당신을 위해 촛불 심지 하나 잘라 줘도 당신이 그걸 대단한 봉사로 여긴다는 걸 깜빡했네요.\""], ["Fred also had his pride, and was not going to show that he knew what had called forth this outburst of Mary’s.", "프레드 역시 자존심이 있었던지라, 메리의 이 격한 토로를 무엇이 불러일으켰는지 자기가 안다는 것을 드러낼 생각이 없었다."]], [["“Oh, I am not angry, except with the ways of the world.", "\"아, 화난 건 아니에요. 세상 돌아가는 방식에 화가 났을 뿐이지요."], ["I do like to be spoken to as if I had common-sense.", "저는 제게 상식이라도 있는 사람인 양 말을 걸어 주는 게 정말 좋아요."], ["I really often feel as if I could understand a little more than I ever hear even from young gentlemen who have been to college.”", "사실 저는 종종, 대학에 다녀온 젊은 신사분들에게서 듣는 것보다 조금은 더 많이 이해할 수 있을 것 같은 기분이 들거든요.\""], ["Mary had recovered, and she spoke with a suppressed rippling under-current of laughter pleasant to hear.", "메리는 평정을 되찾았고, 듣기 좋게 잔물결처럼 일렁이는 웃음을 속으로 억누르며 말했다."]], [["“I don’t care how merry you are at my expense this morning,”", "\"오늘 아침 당신이 저를 두고 아무리 즐거워해도 괜찮아요.\""], ["said Fred, “I thought you looked so sad when you came up-stairs.", "프레드가 말했다. \"당신이 위층에 올라왔을 때 무척 슬퍼 보였거든요."], ["It is a shame you should stay here to be bullied in that way.”", "당신이 여기 남아 그런 식으로 시달려야 한다는 건 정말 부끄러운 일이에요.\""]], [["“Oh, I have an easy life—by comparison.", "\"아, 저는 비교적 편한 삶을 살고 있는걸요."], ["I have tried being a teacher, and I am not fit for that: my mind is too fond of wandering on its own way.", "교사가 되어 보려고도 했지만 그 일엔 맞지 않더군요. 제 마음은 너무 제멋대로 떠돌기를 좋아해서요."], ["I think any hardship is better than pretending to do what one is paid for, and never really doing it.", "보수를 받는 일을 하는 척하면서 실제로는 결코 제대로 하지 않는 것보다는 어떤 고생이든 더 낫다고 생각해요."], ["Everything here I can do as well as any one else could; perhaps better than some—Rosy, for example.", "여기서 하는 일은 무엇이든 저도 남들만큼 잘할 수 있어요. 어쩌면 어떤 이들보다는 더 잘하겠지요—이를테면 로지보다요."], ["Though she is just the sort of beautiful creature that is imprisoned with ogres in fairy tales.”", "비록 그 애는 동화 속에서 도깨비들과 함께 갇혀 있는, 바로 그런 아름다운 존재이긴 하지만요.\""]], [["“Rosy!”", "\"로지가요!\""], ["cried Fred, in a tone of profound brotherly scepticism.", "프레드가 오빠다운 깊은 회의가 담긴 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["“Come, Fred!”", "\"이봐요, 프레드!\""], ["said Mary, emphatically; “you have no right to be so critical.”", "메리가 단호하게 말했다. \"그렇게 비판할 권리는 당신에게 없어요.\""]], [["“Do you mean anything particular—just now?”", "\"지금 무슨 특별한 걸 두고 하는 말인가요?\""]], [["“No, I mean something general—always.”", "\"아니요, 일반적인 걸 두고 하는 말이에요—늘 그렇다는 거죠.\""]], [["“Oh, that I am idle and extravagant.", "\"아, 내가 게으르고 헤프다는 거군요."], ["Well, I am not fit to be a poor man.", "음, 나는 가난한 사람이 될 자질이 못 돼요."], ["I should not have made a bad fellow if I had been rich.”", "부자였다면 나쁜 녀석은 되지 않았을 텐데.\""]], [["“You would have done your duty in that state of life to which it has not pleased God to call you,”", "\"하느님이 당신을 불러 주시지 않은 그 처지에서나마 당신은 본분을 다했겠지요.\""], ["said Mary, laughing.", "메리가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Well, I couldn’t do my duty as a clergyman, any more than you could do yours as a governess.", "\"음, 당신이 가정 교사로서 본분을 다할 수 없는 것이나 마찬가지로, 나도 성직자로서 본분을 다할 수 없어요."], ["You ought to have a little fellow-feeling there, Mary.”", "그 점에선 당신도 조금은 동병상련을 느껴야 마땅하잖아요, 메리.\""]], [["“I never said you ought to be a clergyman.", "\"당신이 성직자가 되어야 한다고는 한 번도 말한 적 없어요."], ["There are other sorts of work.", "다른 종류의 일도 있잖아요."], ["It seems to me very miserable not to resolve on some course and act accordingly.”", "어떤 길도 정하지 못하고 그에 따라 행동하지 못한다는 건 제겐 너무나 비참해 보여요.\""]], [["“So I could, if—”", "\"나도 할 수 있어요, 만약—\""], ["Fred broke off, and stood up, leaning against the mantel-piece.", "프레드는 말을 끊고 일어서서 벽난로 선반에 기댔다."]], [["“If you were sure you should not have a fortune?”", "\"재산을 물려받지 못하리라는 게 확실하다면 말이죠?\""]], [["“I did not say that.", "\"그런 말이 아니에요."], ["You want to quarrel with me.", "당신은 나와 다투고 싶어 하는군요."], ["It is too bad of you to be guided by what other people say about me.”", "나에 대해 남들이 하는 말에 휘둘리다니 당신답지 않게 너무하네요.\""]], [["“How can I want to quarrel with you?", "\"내가 어떻게 당신과 다투고 싶어 하겠어요?"], ["I should be quarrelling with all my new books,”", "그러려면 내 새 책들 전부와 다퉈야 할 텐데.\""], ["said Mary, lifting the volume on the table.", "메리가 탁자 위의 책을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["“However naughty you may be to other people, you are good to me.”", "\"당신이 다른 사람들에게 아무리 못되게 굴어도, 제겐 좋은 사람이니까요.\""]], [["“Because I like you better than any one else.", "\"누구보다도 당신을 더 좋아하니까요."], ["But I know you despise me.”", "하지만 당신이 나를 경멸한다는 건 알아요.\""]], [["“Yes, I do—a little,”", "\"네, 맞아요—조금은요.\""], ["said Mary, nodding, with a smile.", "메리가 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["“You would admire a stupendous fellow, who would have wise opinions about everything.”", "\"당신은 모든 일에 현명한 견해를 지닌 굉장한 남자라면 우러러보겠지요.\""]], [["“Yes, I should.”", "\"네, 그럴 거예요.\""], ["Mary was sewing swiftly, and seemed provokingly mistress of the situation.", "메리는 빠르게 바느질을 하고 있었는데, 얄미울 만큼 상황을 장악하고 있는 것 같았다."], ["When a conversation has taken a wrong turn for us, we only get farther and farther into the swamp of awkwardness.", "대화가 우리에게 불리한 쪽으로 흘러가면, 우리는 그저 어색함의 수렁 속으로 점점 더 깊이 빠져들 뿐이다."], ["This was what Fred Vincy felt.", "프레드 빈시가 느낀 것이 바로 그것이었다."]], [["“I suppose a woman is never in love with any one she has always known—ever since she can remember; as a man often is.", "\"여자는, 남자가 흔히 그러는 것과 달리, 늘 알고 지내 온 사람—기억할 수 있는 한 줄곧 알아 온 사람과는 결코 사랑에 빠지지 않는 모양이에요."], ["It is always some new fellow who strikes a girl.”", "여자의 마음을 사로잡는 건 언제나 새로운 사내지요.\""]], [["“Let me see,”", "\"어디 봐요.\""], ["said Mary, the corners of her mouth curling archly; “I must go back on my experience.", "메리가 입꼬리를 짓궂게 말아 올리며 말했다. \"제 경험을 되짚어 봐야겠네요."], ["There is Juliet—she seems an example of what you say.", "줄리엣이 있군요—그 애는 당신 말의 예가 될 것 같아요."], ["But then Ophelia had probably known Hamlet a long while; and Brenda Troil—she had known Mordaunt Merton ever since they were children; but then he seems to have been an estimable young man; and Minna was still more deeply in love with Cleveland, who was a stranger.", "하지만 오필리아는 아마 햄릿을 오랫동안 알았을 테고, 브렌다 트로일은 모돈트 머튼을 어릴 적부터 알았는데, 그래도 그는 훌륭한 청년이었던 것 같아요. 그리고 미나는 낯선 사람이었던 클리블런드를 더욱 깊이 사랑했지요."], ["Waverley was new to Flora MacIvor; but then she did not fall in love with him.", "웨이벌리는 플로라 매카이버에게 새로운 사람이었지만, 그래도 그녀는 그와 사랑에 빠지지 않았어요."], ["And there are Olivia and Sophia Primrose, and Corinne—they may be said to have fallen in love with new men.", "그리고 올리비아와 소피아 프림로즈가 있고, 코린이 있는데—이들은 새로운 남자들과 사랑에 빠졌다고 할 수 있겠네요."], ["Altogether, my experience is rather mixed.”", "통틀어 보면 제 경험은 꽤 뒤섞여 있군요.\""]], [["Mary looked up with some roguishness at Fred, and that look of hers was very dear to him, though the eyes were nothing more than clear windows where observation sat laughingly.", "메리는 다소 장난스럽게 프레드를 올려다보았고, 그 눈길은 그에게 무척 소중했다. 비록 그 눈이라곤 관찰이 웃으며 앉아 있는 맑은 창에 지나지 않았지만 말이다."], ["He was certainly an affectionate fellow, and as he had grown from boy to man, he had grown in love with his old playmate, notwithstanding that share in the higher education of the country which had exalted his views of rank and income.", "그는 분명 다정한 친구였고, 소년에서 어른으로 자라면서 옛 놀이 동무에 대한 사랑도 함께 자라났다. 그가 신분과 수입에 대한 안목을 한껏 높여 준, 이 나라의 고등 교육에 참여한 그 몫에도 불구하고 말이다."]], [["“When a man is not loved, it is no use for him to say that he could be a better fellow—could do anything—I mean, if he were sure of being loved in return.”", "\"사랑받지 못하는 남자가, 자기는 더 나은 사람이 될 수 있다고—무슨 일이든 할 수 있다고—그러니까 사랑이 보답으로 돌아오리라는 확신만 있다면 그럴 수 있다고 말해 봐야 아무 소용이 없겠죠.\""]], [["“Not of the least use in the world for him to say he could be better.", "\"더 나아질 수 있다고 말해 봐야 세상에 아무짝에도 쓸모없죠."], ["Might, could, would—they are contemptible auxiliaries.”", "~할지도 모른다, ~할 수 있다, ~할 텐데—그건 경멸스러운 조동사들이에요.\""]], [["“I don’t see how a man is to be good for much unless he has some one woman to love him dearly.”", "\"한 여자가 자기를 진심으로 사랑해 주지 않는 한, 남자가 어떻게 무언가에 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 수 있을지 나는 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“I think the goodness should come before he expects that.”", "\"그 선함이라는 건 그런 걸 기대하기 전에 먼저 와야 한다고 봐요.\""]], [["“You know better, Mary.", "\"당신도 잘 알잖아요, 메리."], ["Women don’t love men for their goodness.”", "여자들은 남자의 선함을 보고 사랑하지 않아요.\""]], [["“Perhaps not.", "\"그럴지도 모르죠."], ["But if they love them, they never think them bad.”", "하지만 일단 사랑하게 되면, 결코 그 남자를 나쁘다고 여기지 않아요.\""]], [["“It is hardly fair to say I am bad.”", "\"나를 나쁘다고 하는 건 좀 공정치 못하네요.\""]], [["“I said nothing at all about you.”", "\"저는 당신에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았어요.\""]], [["“I never shall be good for anything, Mary, if you will not say that you love me—if you will not promise to marry me—I mean, when I am able to marry.”", "\"나는 영영 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 사람이 될 거예요, 메리, 당신이 나를 사랑한다고 말해 주지 않는다면—나와 결혼하겠다고 약속해 주지 않는다면—그러니까, 내가 결혼할 수 있게 되었을 때 말이에요.\""]], [["“If I did love you, I would not marry you: I would certainly not promise ever to marry you.”", "\"제가 당신을 사랑한다 해도, 저는 당신과 결혼하지 않겠어요. 분명코 당신과 결혼하겠다는 약속 따위는 결코 하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“I think that is quite wicked, Mary.", "\"그건 정말 못된 거예요, 메리."], ["If you love me, you ought to promise to marry me.”", "나를 사랑한다면 나와 결혼하겠다고 약속해야 마땅하잖아요.\""]], [["“On the contrary, I think it would be wicked in me to marry you even if I did love you.”", "\"천만에요, 제가 당신을 사랑한다 해도 당신과 결혼하는 건 못된 짓일 거라고 생각해요.\""]], [["“You mean, just as I am, without any means of maintaining a wife.", "\"지금 이대로의 나, 아내를 부양할 아무런 방도도 없는 나를 두고 하는 말이군요."], ["Of course: I am but three-and-twenty.”", "물론이죠. 나는 겨우 스물셋이니까요.\""]], [["“In that last point you will alter.", "\"그 마지막 점은 당신이 달라지겠죠."], ["But I am not so sure of any other alteration.", "하지만 다른 어떤 변화에 대해서는 그리 확신할 수가 없어요."], ["My father says an idle man ought not to exist, much less, be married.”", "제 아버지는 게으른 남자란 존재해서도 안 되고, 결혼은 더더욱 해서도 안 된다고 말씀하세요.\""]], [["“Then I am to blow my brains out?”", "\"그럼 내 머리에 총이라도 쏘란 말인가요?\""]], [["“No; on the whole I should think you would do better to pass your examination.", "\"아니요. 전체적으로 보아 당신은 시험에 합격하는 편이 더 나을 것 같아요."], ["I have heard Mr. Farebrother say it is disgracefully easy.”", "페어브러더 씨 말씀이 그게 창피할 만큼 쉽다고 하던걸요.\""]], [["“That is all very fine.", "\"그거 참 듣기 좋은 말이군요."], ["Anything is easy to him.", "그분에겐 뭐든 쉽죠."], ["Not that cleverness has anything to do with it. I am ten times cleverer than many men who pass.”", "영리함과는 아무 상관도 없다는 게 아니라—나는 합격하는 많은 사람들보다 열 배는 더 영리하다고요.\""]], [["“Dear me!”", "\"어머나!\""], ["said Mary, unable to repress her sarcasm; “that accounts for the curates like Mr.", "메리가 빈정거림을 억누르지 못하고 말했다. \"그러니 크라우스 씨 같은 부목사들이 나오는 거군요."], ["Crowse. Divide your cleverness by ten, and the quotient—dear me!—is able to take a degree.", "당신의 영리함을 열로 나누면, 그 몫이—어머나!—학위를 딸 수 있다는 거네요."], ["But that only shows you are ten times more idle than the others.”", "하지만 그건 당신이 다른 사람들보다 열 배는 더 게으르다는 걸 보여 줄 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Well, if I did pass, you would not want me to go into the Church?”", "\"음, 내가 합격한다 해도, 당신은 내가 성직에 들어가길 바라지는 않겠죠?\""]], [["“That is not the question—what I want you to do.", "\"그건 문제가 아니에요—내가 당신이 무엇을 하길 바라는지는요."], ["You have a conscience of your own, I suppose.", "당신에게도 양심은 있을 거 아니에요."], ["There!", "저런!"], ["there is Mr. Lydgate.", "리드게이트 씨가 오시네요."], ["I must go and tell my uncle.”", "가서 외삼촌께 알려 드려야겠어요.\""]], [["“Mary,”", "\"메리.\""], ["said Fred, seizing her hand as she rose; “if you will not give me some encouragement, I shall get worse instead of better.”", "프레드가 그녀가 일어서는 순간 그녀의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"당신이 내게 어떤 격려도 해 주지 않는다면, 나는 나아지기는커녕 더 나빠질 거예요.\""]], [["“I will not give you any encouragement,”", "\"저는 당신에게 아무런 격려도 해 주지 않을 거예요.\""], ["said Mary, reddening.", "메리가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“Your friends would dislike it, and so would mine.", "\"당신 친구들도 그걸 못마땅해할 테고, 제 식구들도 마찬가지일 거예요."], ["My father would think it a disgrace to me if I accepted a man who got into debt, and would not work!”", "제 아버지는 빚을 지고도 일하려 하지 않는 남자를 제가 받아들인다면 그걸 제 수치로 여기실 거예요!\""]], [["Fred was stung, and released her hand.", "프레드는 마음이 찔려 그녀의 손을 놓았다."], ["She walked to the door, but there she turned and said: “Fred, you have always been so good, so generous to me.", "그녀는 문 쪽으로 걸어갔지만, 거기서 돌아서서 말했다. \"프레드, 당신은 늘 제게 무척 잘해 주고 너그러웠어요."], ["I am not ungrateful.", "저는 은혜를 모르는 사람이 아니에요."], ["But never speak to me in that way again.”", "하지만 다시는 제게 그런 식으로 말하지 말아요.\""]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아요.\""], ["said Fred, sulkily, taking up his hat and whip.", "프레드가 시무룩하게 말하며 모자와 채찍을 집어 들었다."], ["His complexion showed patches of pale pink and dead white.", "그의 얼굴에는 옅은 분홍빛과 핏기 없는 흰빛이 군데군데 얼룩져 있었다."], ["Like many a plucked idle young gentleman, he was thoroughly in love, and with a plain girl, who had no money!", "시험에 떨어진 수많은 게으른 젊은 신사들처럼, 그는 철저히 사랑에 빠져 있었다. 그것도 돈 한 푼 없는 평범한 처녀에게!"], ["But having Mr. Featherstone’s land in the background, and a persuasion that, let Mary say what she would, she really did care for him, Fred was not utterly in despair.", "하지만 페더스톤 씨의 토지가 배경에 버티고 있다는 것, 그리고 메리가 무어라 말하든 그녀가 정말로 자기를 마음에 두고 있다는 믿음이 있었기에, 프레드는 완전히 절망에 빠지지는 않았다."]], [["When he got home, he gave four of the twenties to his mother, asking her to keep them for him.", "집에 돌아온 그는 이십 파운드짜리 가운데 네 장을 어머니에게 주며 자기 대신 맡아 달라고 부탁했다."], ["“I don’t want to spend that money, mother.", "\"이 돈은 쓰고 싶지 않아요, 어머니."], ["I want it to pay a debt with.", "빚을 갚는 데 쓰려고요."], ["So keep it safe away from my fingers.”", "그러니 제 손이 닿지 않게 안전히 보관해 주세요.\""]], [["“Bless you, my dear,”", "\"착하기도 하지, 우리 아들.\""], ["said Mrs. Vincy.", "빈시 부인이 말했다."], ["She doted on her eldest son and her youngest girl (a child of six), whom others thought her two naughtiest children.", "그녀는 맏아들과 막내딸(여섯 살배기 아이)을 끔찍이 아꼈는데, 다른 사람들은 그 둘을 그녀의 가장 말썽꾸러기 자식들로 여겼다."], ["The mother’s eyes are not always deceived in their partiality: she at least can best judge who is the tender, filial-hearted child.", "어머니의 눈이 그 편애 속에서 늘 속는 것은 아니다. 적어도 누가 다정하고 효심 깊은 자식인지는 어머니가 가장 잘 판단할 수 있는 법이다."], ["And Fred was certainly very fond of his mother.", "그리고 프레드는 분명 어머니를 무척 좋아했다."], ["Perhaps it was his fondness for another person also that made him particularly anxious to take some security against his own liability to spend the hundred pounds.", "어쩌면 또 다른 한 사람을 향한 애정 때문에, 백 파운드를 써 버리고 마는 자신의 약점에 대비해 무언가 안전장치를 마련해 두려고 그가 유난히 안달했는지도 모른다."], ["For the creditor to whom he owed a hundred and sixty held a firmer security in the shape of a bill signed by Mary’s father.", "그가 백육십 파운드를 빚진 채권자는, 메리의 아버지가 서명한 어음의 형태로 더 단단한 담보를 쥐고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV.", "# 제13장"]], [["“Black eyes you have left, you say, Blue eyes fail to draw you; Yet you seem more rapt to-day, Than of old we saw you.", "검은 눈동자는 이제 놓아 버렸다 그대 말하고, 파란 눈동자는 그대를 끌지 못한다 하나, 오늘 그대는 어느 때보다 넋을 빼앗긴 듯하니, 예전에 우리가 보던 그대보다 더하구나."]], [["“Oh, I track the fairest fair Through new haunts of pleasure; Footprints here and echoes there Guide me to my treasure:", "오, 나는 가장 어여쁜 미인을 좇는다 쾌락의 새로운 은신처를 두루 누비며, 여기 발자국 저기 메아리를 따라 내 보물에게로 인도되어 가노라."]], [["“Lo!", "보라!"], ["she turns—immortal youth Wrought to mortal stature, Fresh as starlight’s aged truth— Many-namèd Nature!”", "그녀가 돌아선다—불멸의 젊음이 필멸의 몸으로 빚어졌으니, 별빛의 오래된 진리처럼 싱그럽도다— 여러 이름을 지닌 자연이여!"]], [["A great historian, as he insisted on calling himself, who had the happiness to be dead a hundred and twenty years ago, and so to take his place among the colossi whose huge legs our living pettiness is observed to walk under, glories in his copious remarks and digressions as the least imitable part of his work, and especially in those initial chapters to the successive books of his history, where he seems to bring his armchair to the proscenium and chat with us in all the lusty ease of his fine English.", "스스로를 그렇게 부르기를 고집했던 한 위대한 역사가는 백이십 년 전에 세상을 떠나는 행복을 누렸으니, 그리하여 우리네 살아 있는 보잘것없음이 그 거대한 다리 밑을 걸어 다니는 모습으로 관찰되는 거인들 사이에 자리 잡았다. 그는 자신의 풍부한 논평과 여담을 작품에서 가장 모방하기 어려운 부분으로 자랑스러워했으며, 특히 그의 역사서 각 권의 첫머리에 놓인 도입 장들을 자랑했다."], ["But Fielding lived when the days were longer (for time, like money, is measured by our needs), when summer afternoons were spacious, and the clock ticked slowly in the winter evenings.", "그곳에서 그는 마치 자기 안락의자를 무대 앞으로 끌어다 놓고, 그 훌륭한 영어의 활기차고 편안한 어조로 우리와 한담을 나누는 듯하다. 하지만 필딩은 하루가 더 길던 시절에 살았으니(시간이란 돈과 마찬가지로 우리의 필요에 따라 가늠되는 것이므로), 여름 오후가 널찍하고 겨울 저녁이면 시계가 느릿느릿 째깍이던 때였다."], ["We belated historians must not linger after his example; and if we did so, it is probable that our chat would be thin and eager, as if delivered from a campstool in a parrot-house.", "우리처럼 뒤늦게 온 역사가들은 그의 본보기를 따라 한가로이 머뭇거려서는 안 된다. 만일 우리가 그렇게 한다면, 우리의 한담은 앵무새 우리에서 접의자에 앉아 늘어놓는 듯, 빈약하고 조급한 것이 되기 십상일 터이다."], ["I at least have so much to do in unraveling certain human lots, and seeing how they were woven and interwoven, that all the light I can command must be concentrated on this particular web, and not dispersed over that tempting range of relevancies called the universe.", "적어도 나는 어떤 인간들의 운명을 풀어내고, 그것들이 어떻게 짜이고 또 서로 엮였는지 살피는 데 할 일이 너무도 많아서, 내가 부릴 수 있는 모든 빛을 이 특정한 직물 하나에 집중해야지, 우주라 불리는 그 유혹적인 연관성의 영역에 흩뿌려서는 안 된다."]], [["At present I have to make the new settler Lydgate better known to any one interested in him than he could possibly be even to those who had seen the most of him since his arrival in Middlemarch.", "지금 나는 새로 정착한 리드게이트를, 그에게 관심 있는 누구에게든, 그가 미들마치에 온 이래 그를 가장 많이 본 사람들조차 알 수 있었던 것보다 더 잘 알려야 한다."], ["For surely all must admit that a man may be puffed and belauded, envied, ridiculed, counted upon as a tool and fallen in love with, or at least selected as a future husband, and yet remain virtually unknown—known merely as a cluster of signs for his neighbors’ false suppositions.", "분명 누구나 인정할 것이다. 한 사람이 추켜세워지고 칭송받고 시기당하고 조롱당하며, 도구로 점쳐지고 사랑받거나 적어도 미래의 남편감으로 선택되면서도, 사실상 알려지지 않은 채—이웃들의 그릇된 억측을 위한 한 무리의 기호로만 알려진 채 남아 있을 수 있다는 것을."], ["There was a general impression, however, that Lydgate was not altogether a common country doctor, and in Middlemarch at that time such an impression was significant of great things being expected from him.", "그렇지만 리드게이트가 아주 평범한 시골 의사는 아니라는 일반적인 인상이 있었고, 당시 미들마치에서 그런 인상은 그에게서 대단한 무언가가 기대된다는 의미였다."], ["For everybody’s family doctor was remarkably clever, and was understood to have immeasurable skill in the management and training of the most skittish or vicious diseases.", "누구네 집 주치의든 한결같이 놀랍도록 영리했고, 가장 변덕스럽거나 고약한 질병들을 다루고 길들이는 데 헤아릴 수 없는 솜씨를 지녔다고 여겨졌기 때문이다."], ["The evidence of his cleverness was of the higher intuitive order, lying in his lady-patients’ immovable conviction, and was unassailable by any objection except that their intuitions were opposed by others equally strong; each lady who saw medical truth in Wrench and “the strengthening treatment”", "그 영리함의 증거는 보다 고차원적인 직관의 종류에 속하는 것이었으니, 그것은 그 부인 환자들의 흔들리지 않는 확신에 자리 잡고 있었고, 그들의 직관이 그에 못지않게 강한 다른 직관들과 맞부딪힌다는 반박을 제외하고는 어떤 이의도 제기될 수 없었다."], ["regarding Toller and “the lowering system” as medical perdition.", "렌치와 \"강화 치료법\"에서 의학적 진리를 본 부인은 톨러와 \"약화 요법\"을 의학적 파멸로 여겼다."], ["For the heroic times of copious bleeding and blistering had not yet departed, still less the times of thorough-going theory, when disease in general was called by some bad name, and treated accordingly without shilly-shally—as if, for example, it were to be called insurrection, which must not be fired on with blank-cartridge, but have its blood drawn at once.", "풍성한 사혈과 발포(發疱)의 영웅적 시대는 아직 가시지 않았으며, 질병 일반에 어떤 고약한 이름을 붙이고 그에 따라 우물쭈물하지 않고 처치하던 철저한 이론의 시대는 더더욱 가시지 않았기 때문이다—예컨대 질병을 반란이라 부른다면, 공포탄으로 쏘아서는 안 되고 당장 그 피를 뽑아내야 하는 것처럼 말이다."], ["The strengtheners and the lowerers were all “clever”", "강화론자들과 약화론자들은 누군가의 의견 속에서는 모두 \"영리한\""], ["men in somebody’s opinion, which is really as much as can be said for any living talents.", "이들이었으니, 사실 이것은 살아 있는 어떤 재능에 대해서도 할 수 있는 말의 전부다."], ["Nobody’s imagination had gone so far as to conjecture that Mr. Lydgate could know as much as Dr.", "리드게이트 씨가 두 내과 의사인 스프레이그 박사와 민친 박사만큼 많이 알 수 있으리라고 짐작하는 데까지 상상력을 뻗친 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["Sprague and Dr. Minchin, the two physicians, who alone could offer any hope when danger was extreme, and when the smallest hope was worth a guinea.", "위험이 극에 달하고 아주 작은 희망이라도 1기니의 값어치가 있을 때, 오직 그 두 사람만이 어떤 희망이라도 줄 수 있었다."], ["Still, I repeat, there was a general impression that Lydgate was something rather more uncommon than any general practitioner in Middlemarch.", "그래도 거듭 말하건대, 리드게이트가 미들마치의 어떤 일반 개업의보다는 다소 더 흔치 않은 인물이라는 일반적인 인상이 있었다."], ["And this was true.", "그리고 그것은 사실이었다."], ["He was but seven-and-twenty, an age at which many men are not quite common—at which they are hopeful of achievement, resolute in avoidance, thinking that Mammon shall never put a bit in their mouths and get astride their backs, but rather that Mammon, if they have anything to do with him, shall draw their chariot.", "그는 겨우 스물일곱이었으니, 많은 남자들이 아직 그리 평범하지 않은 나이—성취를 희망하고 회피에 단호하며, 마몬이 결코 자기 입에 재갈을 물리고 등에 올라타지 못하리라, 오히려 마몬과 무슨 관계를 맺는다면 그가 자기 마차를 끌게 하리라 생각하는 나이였다."]], [["He had been left an orphan when he was fresh from a public school.", "그는 퍼블릭 스쿨을 갓 나왔을 때 고아가 되었다."], ["His father, a military man, had made but little provision for three children, and when the boy Tertius asked to have a medical education, it seemed easier to his guardians to grant his request by apprenticing him to a country practitioner than to make any objections on the score of family dignity.", "군인이었던 그의 아버지는 세 자녀에게 거의 아무 대비도 마련해 두지 않았고, 소년 터티어스가 의학 교육을 받게 해 달라고 청했을 때, 그의 후견인들에게는 가문의 품위를 들어 반대하기보다 시골 개업의에게 도제로 보내 그 청을 들어주는 편이 더 쉬워 보였다."], ["He was one of the rarer lads who early get a decided bent and make up their minds that there is something particular in life which they would like to do for its own sake, and not because their fathers did it.", "그는 일찍이 뚜렷한 성향을 지니고, 인생에는 아버지가 했기 때문이 아니라 그 자체를 위해 자신이 하고 싶은 어떤 특별한 무언가가 있다고 마음을 정하는, 보기 드문 소년들 가운데 하나였다."], ["Most of us who turn to any subject with love remember some morning or evening hour when we got on a high stool to reach down an untried volume, or sat with parted lips listening to a new talker, or for very lack of books began to listen to the voices within, as the first traceable beginning of our love.", "어떤 주제든 사랑으로 향하는 우리 대부분은, 시도해 본 적 없는 책을 꺼내려 높은 걸상에 올라섰던 어느 아침이나 저녁의 한때를, 혹은 입을 벌린 채 새로운 이야기꾼의 말에 귀 기울이며 앉아 있던 때를, 또는 책이 없어서 안에서 들려오는 목소리에 귀 기울이기 시작했던 때를, 우리 사랑의 추적할 수 있는 첫 시작으로 기억한다."], ["Something of that sort happened to Lydgate.", "리드게이트에게도 그런 비슷한 일이 일어났다."], ["He was a quick fellow, and when hot from play, would toss himself in a corner, and in five minutes be deep in any sort of book that he could lay his hands on: if it were Rasselas or Gulliver, so much the better, but Bailey’s Dictionary would do, or the Bible with the Apocrypha in it.", "그는 영민한 녀석이었고, 놀다가 달아올랐을 때면 한구석에 몸을 던지고는 오 분 만에 손에 잡히는 어떤 책에든 깊이 빠져들곤 했다. 그것이 『라셀라스』나 『걸리버 여행기』라면 더할 나위 없었지만, 베일리의 사전이라도 좋았고, 외경이 들어 있는 성경이라도 괜찮았다."], ["Something he must read, when he was not riding the pony, or running and hunting, or listening to the talk of men.", "조랑말을 타거나 뛰어다니며 사냥을 하거나 어른들의 이야기를 듣고 있지 않을 때면, 그는 무언가를 꼭 읽어야 했다."], ["All this was true of him at ten years of age; he had then read through “Chrysal, or the Adventures of a Guinea,” which was neither milk for babes, nor any chalky mixture meant to pass for milk, and it had already occurred to him that books were stuff, and that life was stupid.", "이 모든 것이 그가 열 살 때 사실이었으니, 그는 그때 이미 『크리설, 혹은 어느 기니화의 모험』을 다 읽었는데, 그것은 갓난아기를 위한 젖도, 젖인 척 통하려는 백악(白堊) 섞인 혼합물도 아니었고, 책이란 쓸데없는 것이며 인생이란 어리석은 것이라는 생각이 그에게는 이미 떠올라 있었다."], ["His school studies had not much modified that opinion, for though he “did”", "학교 공부도 그 생각을 그리 바꾸어 놓지는 못했으니, 그는 고전과 수학을 \"해내기는\""], ["his classics and mathematics, he was not pre-eminent in them.", "했어도 거기서 두각을 나타내지는 못했기 때문이다."], ["It was said of him, that Lydgate could do anything he liked, but he had certainly not yet liked to do anything remarkable.", "사람들은 그를 두고 리드게이트는 마음만 먹으면 무엇이든 할 수 있다고 말했지만, 그가 무언가 비범한 일을 하고 싶어 한 적은 분명 아직 없었다."], ["He was a vigorous animal with a ready understanding, but no spark had yet kindled in him an intellectual passion; knowledge seemed to him a very superficial affair, easily mastered: judging from the conversation of his elders, he had apparently got already more than was necessary for mature life.", "그는 이해가 빠른 활기찬 동물이었으나, 아직 어떤 불꽃도 그의 안에 지적 열정을 지피지 못했다. 그에게 지식이란 매우 피상적인 일이라 쉽게 통달할 수 있는 것으로 보였다."], ["Probably this was not an exceptional result of expensive teaching at that period of short-waisted coats, and other fashions which have not yet recurred.", "손윗사람들의 대화로 판단하건대, 그는 이미 성숙한 인생에 필요한 것 이상을 분명 갖춘 듯했다. 짧은 허리의 코트와, 아직 다시 유행하지 않은 다른 풍속들의 그 시절에, 값비싼 교육이 빚어낸 이런 결과는 아마 예외적인 것이 아니었으리라."], ["But, one vacation, a wet day sent him to the small home library to hunt once more for a book which might have some freshness for him: in vain!", "하지만 어느 방학 때, 비 오는 날이 그를 자그마한 집 안 서재로 보내, 그에게 어떤 신선함이라도 줄 만한 책을 다시 한 번 찾게 했다."], ["unless, indeed, he took down a dusty row of volumes with gray-paper backs and dingy labels—the volumes of an old Cyclopaedia which he had never disturbed.", "헛수고였다! 정말로 그가 회색 종이 등표지에 칙칙한 라벨이 붙은 먼지투성이 책들의 줄을—한 번도 건드린 적 없던 낡은 백과사전 여러 권을—꺼내지 않는 한 말이다."], ["It would at least be a novelty to disturb them.", "적어도 그것들을 건드리는 일은 새로운 시도이긴 할 터였다."], ["They were on the highest shelf, and he stood on a chair to get them down.", "그것들은 맨 위 선반에 있어서, 그는 의자에 올라서서 내렸다."], ["But he opened the volume which he first took from the shelf: somehow, one is apt to read in a makeshift attitude, just where it might seem inconvenient to do so.", "그러나 그는 선반에서 처음 집어 든 권을 펼쳐 보았다. 어쩐 일인지 사람은 불편할 법한 바로 그 자리에서, 임시변통의 자세로 책을 읽기 마련이다."], ["The page he opened on was under the head of Anatomy, and the first passage that drew his eyes was on the valves of the heart.", "그가 펼친 페이지는 해부학 항목 아래 있었고, 처음 그의 눈을 끈 대목은 심장의 판막에 관한 것이었다."], ["He was not much acquainted with valves of any sort, but he knew that valvae were folding-doors, and through this crevice came a sudden light startling him with his first vivid notion of finely adjusted mechanism in the human frame.", "그는 어떤 종류의 판막에도 그리 익숙지 않았지만, 발바이(valvae)가 접이문이라는 것은 알고 있었고, 이 틈새로 갑작스러운 빛이 들어와 인체라는 구조물 속에 정교하게 조정된 기계 장치가 있다는 생생한 첫 관념으로 그를 깜짝 놀라게 했다."], ["A liberal education had of course left him free to read the indecent passages in the school classics, but beyond a general sense of secrecy and obscenity in connection with his internal structure, had left his imagination quite unbiassed, so that for anything he knew his brains lay in small bags at his temples, and he had no more thought of representing to himself how his blood circulated than how paper served instead of gold.", "자유로운 교양 교육은 물론 그가 학교 고전 속 음란한 대목들을 마음껏 읽도록 내버려 두었지만, 자기 신체 내부 구조와 결부된 비밀스러움과 외설스러움이라는 막연한 느낌 너머로는, 그의 상상력을 전혀 한쪽으로 치우치게 하지 않았다. 그래서 그가 아는 한 자기 뇌는 관자놀이께의 작은 주머니들 속에 들어 있는 셈이었고, 종이가 금화 대신 쓰인다는 것을 떠올리지 않는 것과 마찬가지로 자기 피가 어떻게 순환하는지 스스로에게 그려 본 적이 없었다."], ["But the moment of vocation had come, and before he got down from his chair, the world was made new to him by a presentiment of endless processes filling the vast spaces planked out of his sight by that wordy ignorance which he had supposed to be knowledge.", "그러나 소명의 순간이 찾아왔으니, 그가 의자에서 내려오기도 전에, 자신이 지식이라 여겨 왔던 그 말뿐인 무지가 가려 놓았던 광대한 공간들을 채우는 끝없는 과정에 대한 예감으로, 세상은 그에게 새롭게 만들어졌다."], ["From that hour Lydgate felt the growth of an intellectual passion.", "그 시간 이후로 리드게이트는 지적 열정이 자라나는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["We are not afraid of telling over and over again how a man comes to fall in love with a woman and be wedded to her, or else be fatally parted from her.", "우리는 한 남자가 어떻게 한 여자와 사랑에 빠져 그녀와 맺어지거나, 그렇지 않으면 그녀와 운명적으로 갈라서게 되는지를, 거듭거듭 이야기하기를 두려워하지 않는다."], ["Is it due to excess of poetry or of stupidity that we are never weary of describing what King James called a woman’s “makdom and her fairnesse,” never weary of listening to the twanging of the old Troubadour strings, and are comparatively uninterested in that other kind of “makdom and fairnesse”", "제임스 왕이 여인의 \"맵시와 어여쁨\"이라 불렀던 것을 묘사하는 데 결코 싫증 내지 않고, 옛 음유시인의 현이 울리는 소리를 듣는 데 결코 싫증 내지 않으면서, 부지런한 사색과 자잘한 욕망에 대한 끈기 있는 단념으로 구애해야 하는 저 다른 종류의 \"맵시와 어여쁨\"에는 비교적 무관심한 것은, 시정(詩情)이 지나친 탓인가 아니면 어리석음이 지나친 탓인가?"], ["which must be wooed with industrious thought and patient renunciation of small desires?", "이 열정의 이야기에서도 그 전개는 다양하다."], ["In the story of this passion, too, the development varies: sometimes it is the glorious marriage, sometimes frustration and final parting.", "어떤 때는 영광스러운 결혼이고, 어떤 때는 좌절과 끝내 갈라섬이다."], ["And not seldom the catastrophe is bound up with the other passion, sung by the Troubadours.", "그리고 적지 않게 그 파국은 음유시인들이 노래한 저 다른 열정과 얽혀 있다."], ["For in the multitude of middle-aged men who go about their vocations in a daily course determined for them much in the same way as the tie of their cravats, there is always a good number who once meant to shape their own deeds and alter the world a little.", "자기 넥타이를 매는 방식과 거의 같은 방식으로 정해진 일상의 행로 속에서 자기 직업에 종사하는 수많은 중년 남자들 가운데에는, 한때 자기 행위를 스스로 빚어내고 세상을 조금쯤 바꾸어 보려 했던 이들이 언제나 상당수 있기 마련이다."], ["The story of their coming to be shapen after the average and fit to be packed by the gross, is hardly ever told even in their consciousness; for perhaps their ardor in generous unpaid toil cooled as imperceptibly as the ardor of other youthful loves, till one day their earlier self walked like a ghost in its old home and made the new furniture ghastly.", "그들이 평균에 맞추어 빚어지고 다발로 묶여 포장되기에 알맞게 되어 가는 이야기는, 그들 자신의 의식 속에서조차 좀처럼 이야기되지 않는다. 아마도 너그러운 무보수 노고를 향한 그들의 열정은 다른 젊은 날 사랑의 열정만큼이나 알아채지 못할 만큼 서서히 식어 갔을 것이고, 그러다 어느 날 예전의 자아가 옛집을 거니는 유령처럼 나타나 새 가구를 으스스하게 만들었을 것이다."], ["Nothing in the world more subtle than the process of their gradual change!", "세상에 그들의 점진적인 변화 과정보다 더 미묘한 것은 없다! 처음에 그들은 자기도 모르게 그것을 들이마셨다."], ["In the beginning they inhaled it unknowingly: you and I may have sent some of our breath towards infecting them, when we uttered our conforming falsities or drew our silly conclusions: or perhaps it came with the vibrations from a woman’s glance.", "당신과 나도, 우리가 순응의 거짓말을 내뱉거나 어리석은 결론을 끌어냈을 때, 그들을 물들이는 데 우리 입김을 얼마쯤 보탰을지 모른다. 혹은 어쩌면 그것은 한 여자의 눈길에서 오는 떨림과 함께 왔을지도 모른다."]], [["Lydgate did not mean to be one of those failures, and there was the better hope of him because his scientific interest soon took the form of a professional enthusiasm: he had a youthful belief in his bread-winning work, not to be stifled by that initiation in makeshift called his ’prentice days; and he carried to his studies in London, Edinburgh, and Paris, the conviction that the medical profession as it might be was the finest in the world; presenting the most perfect interchange between science and art; offering the most direct alliance between intellectual conquest and the social good.", "리드게이트는 그런 실패자들 가운데 하나가 될 생각이 없었으며, 그에 대한 더 나은 희망이 있었던 것은 그의 과학적 관심이 곧 직업적 열정의 형태를 띠었기 때문이다. 그는 자신의 밥벌이 일에 대한 젊은이다운 믿음을 지녔으니, 도제 시절이라 불리던 그 임시변통의 입문기에도 질식되지 않은 믿음이었다."], ["Lydgate’s nature demanded this combination: he was an emotional creature, with a flesh-and-blood sense of fellowship which withstood all the abstractions of special study.", "그리고 그는 런던과 에든버러와 파리에서의 학업으로, 의술이란 본래의 모습대로라면 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 직업이라는 확신을 가져갔다. 그것은 과학과 기술 사이의 가장 완벽한 교류를 보여 주며, 지적 정복과 사회적 선 사이의 가장 직접적인 동맹을 제공한다는 것이었다."], ["He cared not only for “cases,”", "리드게이트의 천성은 이 결합을 요구했다."], ["but for John and Elizabeth, especially Elizabeth.", "그는 정서가 풍부한 존재였으니, 특수한 연구의 온갖 추상에도 굴하지 않는, 피와 살로 이루어진 동료애의 감각을 지니고 있었다. 그는 \"증례\"뿐 아니라 존과 엘리자베스에게도, 특히 엘리자베스에게 마음을 썼다."]], [["There was another attraction in his profession: it wanted reform, and gave a man an opportunity for some indignant resolve to reject its venal decorations and other humbug, and to be the possessor of genuine though undemanded qualifications.", "그의 직업에는 또 다른 매력이 있었다. 그것은 개혁을 필요로 했으며, 한 사람에게 그 직업의 매수된 장식들과 다른 허튼소리를 분연히 거부하고, 요구되지 않을지언정 진정한 자격을 갖춘 자가 되겠노라 결의할 기회를 주었다."], ["He went to study in Paris with the determination that when he came home again he would settle in some provincial town as a general practitioner, and resist the irrational severance between medical and surgical knowledge in the interest of his own scientific pursuits, as well as of the general advance: he would keep away from the range of London intrigues, jealousies, and social truckling, and win celebrity, however slowly, as Jenner had done, by the independent value of his work.", "그는 다시 고향에 돌아오면 어느 지방 도시에 일반 개업의로 정착하여, 자신의 과학적 탐구를 위해서나 전반적인 진보를 위해서나 내과적 지식과 외과적 지식 사이의 비합리적인 분리에 맞서겠다는 결심을 품고 파리로 공부하러 갔다. 그는 런던의 음모와 질시와 사교적 아첨의 영역에서 멀리 떨어져, 제너가 그랬듯이 아무리 더디더라도 자기 일의 독자적인 가치로 명성을 얻으려 했다."], ["For it must be remembered that this was a dark period; and in spite of venerable colleges which used great efforts to secure purity of knowledge by making it scarce, and to exclude error by a rigid exclusiveness in relation to fees and appointments, it happened that very ignorant young gentlemen were promoted in town, and many more got a legal right to practise over large areas in the country.", "이 시절이 어두운 시기였음을 기억해야 하기 때문이다. 지식을 희소하게 만들어 그 순수성을 지키고, 진료비와 임명에 관한 엄격한 배타성으로 오류를 배제하려 큰 노력을 기울인 유서 깊은 단과 대학들이 있었음에도, 매우 무지한 젊은 신사들이 도시에서 출세하고, 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 이들이 시골의 넓은 지역에서 개업할 법적 권리를 얻는 일이 벌어졌다."], ["Also, the high standard held up to the public mind by the College of Physicians, which gave its peculiar sanction to the expensive and highly rarefied medical instruction obtained by graduates of Oxford and Cambridge, did not hinder quackery from having an excellent time of it; for since professional practice chiefly consisted in giving a great many drugs, the public inferred that it might be better off with more drugs still, if they could only be got cheaply, and hence swallowed large cubic measures of physic prescribed by unscrupulous ignorance which had taken no degrees.", "또한 옥스퍼드와 케임브리지 졸업생들이 얻는 값비싸고 극도로 정제된 의학 교육에 특유의 인가를 부여하며 내과 의사 협회가 대중의 마음에 떠받쳐 놓은 그 높은 기준도, 돌팔이 의술이 호시절을 누리는 것을 막지는 못했다. 직업적 진료란 주로 수많은 약을 처방하는 데 있었으므로, 대중은 값만 싸게 구할 수 있다면 약이 더 많을수록 더 나으리라 추론했고, 그리하여 아무런 학위도 받지 않은 파렴치한 무지가 처방한 약물을 어마어마한 양으로 들이켰던 것이다."], ["Considering that statistics had not yet embraced a calculation as to the number of ignorant or canting doctors which absolutely must exist in the teeth of all changes, it seemed to Lydgate that a change in the units was the most direct mode of changing the numbers.", "어떤 변화에도 불구하고 반드시 존재할 수밖에 없는 무지하거나 위선적인 의사들의 수에 관한 계산을 통계학이 아직 포함하지 못했음을 생각하면, 리드게이트에게는 단위들을 바꾸는 것이 그 수를 바꾸는 가장 직접적인 방법으로 여겨졌다."], ["He meant to be a unit who would make a certain amount of difference towards that spreading change which would one day tell appreciably upon the averages, and in the mean time have the pleasure of making an advantageous difference to the viscera of his own patients.", "그는 언젠가 평균에 뚜렷이 영향을 미칠 그 확산되는 변화를 향해 일정한 차이를 만들어 낼 하나의 단위가 되고자 했고, 그러는 동안 자기 환자들의 내장에 유익한 차이를 만들어 내는 즐거움을 누리고자 했다."], ["But he did not simply aim at a more genuine kind of practice than was common.", "그러나 그는 단지 흔한 진료보다 더 진정한 종류의 진료를 목표로 한 것만은 아니었다."], ["He was ambitious of a wider effect: he was fired with the possibility that he might work out the proof of an anatomical conception and make a link in the chain of discovery.", "그는 더 폭넓은 영향력을 야심껏 바랐으니, 어떤 해부학적 착상의 증명을 이루어 내어 발견의 사슬에 한 고리를 잇게 될지도 모른다는 가능성에 불타올랐다."]], [["Does it seem incongruous to you that a Middlemarch surgeon should dream of himself as a discoverer?", "미들마치의 외과 의사가 자기 자신을 발견자로 꿈꾼다는 것이 당신에게는 어울리지 않게 보이는가?"], ["Most of us, indeed, know little of the great originators until they have been lifted up among the constellations and already rule our fates.", "우리 대부분은 실로 위대한 창시자들이 별자리들 사이로 들어 올려져 이미 우리 운명을 다스리게 되기 전까지는 그들에 대해 거의 알지 못한다."], ["But that Herschel, for example, who “broke the barriers of the heavens”—did he not once play a provincial church-organ, and give music-lessons to stumbling pianists?", "그러나 예를 들어 \"하늘의 장벽을 깨뜨린\" 저 허셜은, 한때 지방 교회의 오르간을 연주하고 더듬거리는 피아노 연주자들에게 음악 교습을 하지 않았던가?"], ["Each of those Shining Ones had to walk on the earth among neighbors who perhaps thought much more of his gait and his garments than of anything which was to give him a title to everlasting fame: each of them had his little local personal history sprinkled with small temptations and sordid cares, which made the retarding friction of his course towards final companionship with the immortals.", "그 빛나는 이들은 저마다 이웃들 사이에서 땅 위를 걸어야 했으니, 그 이웃들은 어쩌면 그에게 영원한 명성의 자격을 부여할 무언가보다 그의 걸음걸이와 옷차림을 훨씬 더 마음에 두었을 것이다. 그들은 저마다 자잘한 유혹과 누추한 근심이 뿌려진 작은 지역적·개인적 내력을 지녔으며, 그것이 불멸의 존재들과 끝내 동무가 되는 길로 나아가는 그의 행로를 지체시키는 마찰이 되었다."], ["Lydgate was not blind to the dangers of such friction, but he had plenty of confidence in his resolution to avoid it as far as possible: being seven-and-twenty, he felt himself experienced.", "리드게이트는 그런 마찰의 위험에 눈멀어 있지 않았으나, 가능한 한 그것을 피하겠다는 자신의 결심에 충분한 자신이 있었다. 스물일곱이었으므로, 그는 스스로 경험이 많다고 느꼈다."], ["And he was not going to have his vanities provoked by contact with the showy worldly successes of the capital, but to live among people who could hold no rivalry with that pursuit of a great idea which was to be a twin object with the assiduous practice of his profession.", "그리고 그는 수도의 화려한 세속적 성공과 접촉하여 자신의 허영심을 자극받지 않고, 자기 직업의 부지런한 실천과 더불어 쌍둥이 목표가 될 위대한 착상의 추구와 결코 경쟁할 수 없는 사람들 사이에서 살아가려 했다."], ["There was fascination in the hope that the two purposes would illuminate each other: the careful observation and inference which was his daily work, the use of the lens to further his judgment in special cases, would further his thought as an instrument of larger inquiry.", "그 두 목표가 서로를 밝혀 주리라는 희망에는 매혹이 깃들어 있었다. 그의 일상 작업인 세심한 관찰과 추론, 특수한 증례들에서 판단을 돕기 위한 렌즈의 사용은, 더 큰 탐구의 도구로서 그의 사색을 진전시킬 터였다."], ["Was not this the typical pre-eminence of his profession?", "이것이야말로 그의 직업이 지닌 전형적인 탁월함이 아니던가?"], ["He would be a good Middlemarch doctor, and by that very means keep himself in the track of far-reaching investigation.", "그는 훌륭한 미들마치 의사가 될 것이고, 바로 그 수단으로 멀리까지 미치는 연구의 궤도에 자신을 머무르게 할 것이었다."], ["On one point he may fairly claim approval at this particular stage of his career: he did not mean to imitate those philanthropic models who make a profit out of poisonous pickles to support themselves while they are exposing adulteration, or hold shares in a gambling-hell that they may have leisure to represent the cause of public morality.", "한 가지 점에서 그는 자기 경력의 바로 이 단계에서 정당히 인정을 주장할 만하다. 그는 불순물 섞기를 폭로하는 동안 제 생계를 꾸리려고 유독한 절임 식품으로 이익을 챙기거나, 공중도덕의 대의를 대변할 여유를 얻으려고 도박장 주식을 가지고 있는 저 박애주의적 본보기들을 흉내 낼 생각이 없었다."], ["He intended to begin in his own case some particular reforms which were quite certainly within his reach, and much less of a problem than the demonstrating of an anatomical conception.", "그는 자기 경우에 한해서, 확실히 자신의 손이 닿는 범위 안에 있고 해부학적 착상을 입증하는 일보다 훨씬 덜 어려운, 몇 가지 특정한 개혁을 시작하려 했다."], ["One of these reforms was to act stoutly on the strength of a recent legal decision, and simply prescribe, without dispensing drugs or taking percentage from druggists.", "그 개혁 가운데 하나는 최근의 한 법적 판결의 힘을 빌려 단호히 행동하는 것, 곧 약을 조제하거나 약제사에게서 수수료를 받지 않고 단지 처방만 하는 것이었다."], ["This was an innovation for one who had chosen to adopt the style of general practitioner in a country town, and would be felt as offensive criticism by his professional brethren.", "이것은 시골 도시에서 일반 개업의의 방식을 택하기로 한 사람에게는 하나의 혁신이었고, 동업하는 의사 형제들에게는 모욕적인 비판으로 받아들여질 터였다."], ["But Lydgate meant to innovate in his treatment also, and he was wise enough to see that the best security for his practising honestly according to his belief was to get rid of systematic temptations to the contrary.", "그러나 리드게이트는 자기 치료법에서도 혁신을 꾀할 작정이었고, 자신의 신념에 따라 정직하게 진료하는 데 가장 확실한 보장은 그에 어긋나는 체계적인 유혹들을 없애 버리는 것임을 알 만큼 현명했다."]], [["Perhaps that was a more cheerful time for observers and theorizers than the present; we are apt to think it the finest era of the world when America was beginning to be discovered, when a bold sailor, even if he were wrecked, might alight on a new kingdom; and about 1829 the dark territories of Pathology were a fine America for a spirited young adventurer.", "어쩌면 그때가 관찰자와 이론가에게는 지금보다 더 신나는 시절이었으리라. 우리는 아메리카가 처음 발견되기 시작했을 때, 대담한 뱃사람이 설령 난파했더라도 새로운 왕국에 발을 디딜 수 있었을 그때를 세상에서 가장 멋진 시대라고 생각하기 쉽다."], ["Lydgate was ambitious above all to contribute towards enlarging the scientific, rational basis of his profession.", "그리고 1829년 무렵, 병리학의 어두운 영역들은 기개 있는 젊은 모험가에게 멋진 아메리카였다."], ["The more he became interested in special questions of disease, such as the nature of fever or fevers, the more keenly he felt the need for that fundamental knowledge of structure which just at the beginning of the century had been illuminated by the brief and glorious career of Bichat, who died when he was only one-and-thirty, but, like another Alexander, left a realm large enough for many heirs.", "리드게이트는 무엇보다 자기 직업의 과학적이고 합리적인 토대를 넓히는 데 이바지하려는 야심을 품었다. 열병 혹은 여러 열병들의 본질 같은 질병의 특수한 문제들에 관심을 가지면 가질수록, 그는 19세기 초에 비샤의 짧고도 영광스러운 생애로 환히 밝혀졌던 그 근본적인 구조 지식의 필요성을 더욱 절실히 느꼈다."], ["That great Frenchman first carried out the conception that living bodies, fundamentally considered, are not associations of organs which can be understood by studying them first apart, and then as it were federally; but must be regarded as consisting of certain primary webs or tissues, out of which the various organs—brain, heart, lungs, and so on—are compacted, as the various accommodations of a house are built up in various proportions of wood, iron, stone, brick, zinc, and the rest, each material having its peculiar composition and proportions.", "비샤는 겨우 서른한 살에 죽었지만, 또 한 명의 알렉산드로스처럼 수많은 후계자에게 충분할 만큼 넓은 영토를 남겼다. 그 위대한 프랑스인은, 살아 있는 신체란 근본적으로 보아 먼저 따로따로 연구한 다음 이를테면 연방처럼 합쳐서 이해할 수 있는 기관들의 집합이 아니라, 어떤 일차적인 망(網) 혹은 조직으로 이루어진 것으로 보아야 하며, 그것들로부터 뇌, 심장, 폐 따위의 여러 기관이—집의 여러 시설이 목재, 철, 돌, 벽돌, 아연 따위가 저마다 다른 비율로 짜여 세워지듯, 그리고 각 재료가 저마다 고유한 구성과 비율을 지니듯—압축되어 만들어진다는 착상을 처음으로 펼쳐 보였다."], ["No man, one sees, can understand and estimate the entire structure or its parts—what are its frailties and what its repairs, without knowing the nature of the materials.", "보다시피, 어느 누구도 재료의 본질을 알지 못한 채로는 전체 구조나 그 부분들을—그 약한 곳이 무엇이고 그 보수할 곳이 무엇인지를—이해하고 가늠할 수 없다."], ["And the conception wrought out by Bichat, with his detailed study of the different tissues, acted necessarily on medical questions as the turning of gas-light would act on a dim, oil-lit street, showing new connections and hitherto hidden facts of structure which must be taken into account in considering the symptoms of maladies and the action of medicaments.", "그리고 여러 조직에 대한 세밀한 연구로 비샤가 이루어 낸 그 착상은, 흐릿한 기름 등불의 거리에 가스등을 켜는 것이 그러하듯, 새로운 연관들과 그때까지 숨겨져 있던 구조의 사실들—질병의 증상과 약물의 작용을 고찰할 때 반드시 고려해야 할 사실들—을 드러내며, 필연적으로 의학적 문제들에 작용했다."], ["But results which depend on human conscience and intelligence work slowly, and now at the end of 1829, most medical practice was still strutting or shambling along the old paths, and there was still scientific work to be done which might have seemed to be a direct sequence of Bichat’s.", "그러나 인간의 양심과 지성에 달린 결과들은 더디게 작동하는 법이어서, 이제 1829년이 끝나갈 무렵에도 대부분의 의료 행위는 여전히 옛 길을 으스대거나 비틀거리며 걷고 있었고, 비샤의 작업의 직접적인 후속으로 보였을 법한 과학적 작업이 여전히 행해져야 했다. 이 위대한 선견자는 살아 있는 유기체의 궁극적 사실로서 조직들을 고찰하는 것을 넘어서지는 않았으니, 해부학적 분석의 한계를 그어 놓은 셈이었다."], ["This great seer did not go beyond the consideration of the tissues as ultimate facts in the living organism, marking the limit of anatomical analysis; but it was open to another mind to say, have not these structures some common basis from which they have all started, as your sarsnet, gauze, net, satin, and velvet from the raw cocoon?", "그러나 또 다른 정신이 이렇게 물을 여지가 있었다. 당신의 명주천, 거즈, 망사, 새틴, 벨벳이 모두 날것의 고치에서 비롯되듯, 이 구조들도 모두 거기서 출발한 어떤 공통의 토대를 지니지 않았는가?"], ["Here would be another light, as of oxy-hydrogen, showing the very grain of things, and revising all former explanations.", "여기에 또 다른 빛이, 산수소(酸水素) 불빛 같은 빛이 있어, 사물의 바로 그 결을 보여 주며 이전의 모든 설명을 수정할 터였다."], ["Of this sequence to Bichat’s work, already vibrating along many currents of the European mind, Lydgate was enamoured; he longed to demonstrate the more intimate relations of living structure, and help to define men’s thought more accurately after the true order.", "이미 유럽 정신의 수많은 흐름을 따라 떨려 오던 비샤의 작업에 대한 이 후속을 리드게이트는 사모했다. 그는 살아 있는 구조의 보다 내밀한 관계들을 입증하고, 참된 질서에 따라 인간의 사유를 더 정확하게 규정하는 데 보태고자 갈망했다."], ["The work had not yet been done, but only prepared for those who knew how to use the preparation.", "그 작업은 아직 이루어지지 않았고, 다만 그 준비를 어떻게 쓸지 아는 이들을 위해 준비되어 있을 뿐이었다."], ["What was the primitive tissue?", "원초적 조직이란 무엇인가?"], ["In that way Lydgate put the question—not quite in the way required by the awaiting answer; but such missing of the right word befalls many seekers.", "리드게이트는 그런 식으로 질문을 던졌으니—기다리는 답이 요구하는 방식과 꼭 들어맞지는 않았으나, 그렇게 옳은 말을 놓치는 일은 많은 탐구자에게 일어나는 법이다."], ["And he counted on quiet intervals to be watchfully seized, for taking up the threads of investigation—on many hints to be won from diligent application, not only of the scalpel, but of the microscope, which research had begun to use again with new enthusiasm of reliance.", "그리고 그는 조용한 틈들을 빈틈없이 붙잡아 탐구의 실마리를 다시 잡으리라 기대했고—메스뿐 아니라 현미경의 부지런한 사용에서 얻을 수많은 단서를 기대했으니, 그 현미경은 새로운 신뢰의 열의로 연구에 다시 쓰이기 시작하던 참이었다. 이것이 자기 미래에 대한 리드게이트의 계획이었다."], ["Such was Lydgate’s plan of his future: to do good small work for Middlemarch, and great work for the world.", "미들마치를 위해서는 훌륭한 작은 일을 하고, 세상을 위해서는 위대한 일을 하는 것이었다."]], [["He was certainly a happy fellow at this time: to be seven-and-twenty, without any fixed vices, with a generous resolution that his action should be beneficent, and with ideas in his brain that made life interesting quite apart from the cultus of horseflesh and other mystic rites of costly observance, which the eight hundred pounds left him after buying his practice would certainly not have gone far in paying for.", "이 무렵 그는 분명 행복한 녀석이었다. 정해진 악덕이라곤 없이 스물일곱이라는 나이로, 자기 행동이 이로운 것이어야 한다는 너그러운 결심을 품고, 말 다루는 일의 숭배나 값비싼 격식을 차리는 다른 신비로운 의례들—개업 비용을 치르고 그에게 남은 팔백 파운드로는 분명 그 비용을 대는 데 얼마 가지도 못했을 의례들—과는 전혀 별개로 인생을 흥미롭게 만드는 착상들을 머릿속에 지니고 있었기 때문이다."], ["He was at a starting-point which makes many a man’s career a fine subject for betting, if there were any gentlemen given to that amusement who could appreciate the complicated probabilities of an arduous purpose, with all the possible thwartings and furtherings of circumstance, all the niceties of inward balance, by which a man swims and makes his point or else is carried headlong.", "그는 많은 남자들의 경력을 내기 좋은 멋진 대상으로 만들 만한 출발점에 서 있었다—만일 그런 오락을 즐기는 신사들 가운데, 온갖 상황의 방해와 도움, 그리고 한 사람이 헤엄쳐 목적지에 닿거나 아니면 곤두박질쳐 떠내려가게 되는 내적 균형의 온갖 미묘함과 더불어, 고된 목적이 지닌 복잡한 개연성을 음미할 줄 아는 이가 있다면 말이다."], ["The risk would remain even with close knowledge of Lydgate’s character; for character too is a process and an unfolding.", "그 위험은 리드게이트의 성품을 속속들이 안다 해도 여전히 남을 것이니, 성품 또한 하나의 과정이요 펼쳐짐이기 때문이다."], ["The man was still in the making, as much as the Middlemarch doctor and immortal discoverer, and there were both virtues and faults capable of shrinking or expanding.", "그 사람은 미들마치의 의사이자 불멸의 발견자만큼이나 아직 만들어지는 중이었고, 움츠러들 수도 부풀어 오를 수도 있는 미덕과 결점이 모두 있었다."], ["The faults will not, I hope, be a reason for the withdrawal of your interest in him.", "바라건대 그 결점들이 그에 대한 당신의 관심을 거두는 이유가 되지는 않기를."], ["Among our valued friends is there not some one or other who is a little too self-confident and disdainful; whose distinguished mind is a little spotted with commonness; who is a little pinched here and protuberant there with native prejudices; or whose better energies are liable to lapse down the wrong channel under the influence of transient solicitations?", "우리가 아끼는 벗들 가운데, 좀 지나치게 자신만만하고 거만한 이가, 그 뛰어난 정신이 다소 평범함으로 얼룩진 이가, 타고난 편견으로 여기는 조금 옹색하고 저기는 불룩 튀어나온 이가, 혹은 그 더 나은 기력이 일시적인 유혹의 영향 아래 엉뚱한 물길로 흘러내리기 쉬운 이가 한둘쯤 있지 않은가?"], ["All these things might be alleged against Lydgate, but then, they are the periphrases of a polite preacher, who talks of Adam, and would not like to mention anything painful to the pew-renters.", "이 모든 것이 리드게이트에게 들이댈 만한 것이었지만, 그런데 이것들은 아담을 입에 올리면서도 신도석을 빌린 이들에게 거북한 것은 무엇 하나 언급하고 싶어 하지 않는 점잖은 설교자의 에두른 표현일 뿐이다."], ["The particular faults from which these delicate generalities are distilled have distinguishable physiognomies, diction, accent, and grimaces; filling up parts in very various dramas.", "이 섬세한 일반론들이 증류되어 나온 그 특정한 결점들에는 분간할 수 있는 인상과 어법과 말투와 찡그린 표정이 있어, 매우 다양한 연극에서 여러 역을 맡는다."], ["Our vanities differ as our noses do: all conceit is not the same conceit, but varies in correspondence with the minutiae of mental make in which one of us differs from another.", "우리의 허영은 우리의 코가 다르듯 저마다 다르다. 모든 자만이 같은 자만은 아니며, 우리 가운데 한 사람이 다른 사람과 구별되는 그 정신적 구성의 미세한 차이에 따라 달라진다."], ["Lydgate’s conceit was of the arrogant sort, never simpering, never impertinent, but massive in its claims and benevolently contemptuous.", "리드게이트의 자만은 거만한 종류로, 결코 실실 웃지 않고 결코 무례하지 않으나, 그 주장에 있어 묵직하고 자비롭게 깔보는 것이었다."], ["He would do a great deal for noodles, being sorry for them, and feeling quite sure that they could have no power over him: he had thought of joining the Saint Simonians when he was in Paris, in order to turn them against some of their own doctrines.", "그는 멍청이들을 딱하게 여기며 그들을 위해 많은 일을 해 줄 만했으니, 그들이 자신에게는 아무런 힘도 미치지 못하리라 꽤나 확신했기 때문이다. 그는 파리에 있을 때 생시몽주의자들에게 가담할 생각을 했었는데, 그들의 교리 가운데 일부에 그들 자신을 맞서게 하려는 의도에서였다."], ["All his faults were marked by kindred traits, and were those of a man who had a fine baritone, whose clothes hung well upon him, and who even in his ordinary gestures had an air of inbred distinction.", "그의 모든 결점에는 비슷한 특질이 새겨져 있었으니, 그것은 멋진 바리톤 음성을 지니고, 옷이 몸에 잘 어울리며, 평범한 몸짓에서조차 타고난 기품의 분위기를 풍기는 남자의 결점이었다."], ["Where then lay the spots of commonness?", "그렇다면 그 평범함의 얼룩은 어디에 있었던가?"], ["says a young lady enamoured of that careless grace.", "그 무심한 우아함에 반한 어느 젊은 아가씨는 이렇게 묻는다."], ["How could there be any commonness in a man so well-bred, so ambitious of social distinction, so generous and unusual in his views of social duty?", "그토록 가문이 좋고, 사회적 명성을 그토록 야심 차게 바라며, 사회적 의무에 대한 견해가 그토록 너그럽고 남다른 남자에게 어떻게 평범함이라는 것이 있을 수 있겠는가?"], ["As easily as there may be stupidity in a man of genius if you take him unawares on the wrong subject, or as many a man who has the best will to advance the social millennium might be ill-inspired in imagining its lighter pleasures; unable to go beyond Offenbach’s music, or the brilliant punning in the last burlesque.", "천재인 사람이라도 그를 엉뚱한 주제로 불시에 붙잡으면 어리석을 수 있는 것만큼이나 쉽사리, 혹은 사회적 천년왕국을 진전시키려는 더없이 선한 의지를 지닌 많은 이가 그 가벼운 즐거움을 그려 내는 데서는 형편없는 착상에 빠질 수 있는 것만큼이나 쉽사리 그러하다—그들은 오펜바흐의 음악이나 최신 익살극의 현란한 말장난을 넘어서지 못하는 것이다."], ["Lydgate’s spots of commonness lay in the complexion of his prejudices, which, in spite of noble intention and sympathy, were half of them such as are found in ordinary men of the world: that distinction of mind which belonged to his intellectual ardor, did not penetrate his feeling and judgment about furniture, or women, or the desirability of its being known (without his telling) that he was better born than other country surgeons.", "리드게이트의 평범함의 얼룩은 그의 편견의 빛깔에 있었으니, 그 편견은 고결한 의도와 공감에도 불구하고 절반쯤은 세상 보통 사람들에게서 발견되는 것들이었다. 그의 지적 열의에 속했던 그 정신의 탁월함은, 가구나 여자에 대한, 혹은 그가 다른 시골 외과 의사들보다 태생이 더 낫다는 것이 (그가 말하지 않아도) 알려지면 좋겠다는 바람에 대한 그의 감정과 판단에는 스며들지 못했다."], ["He did not mean to think of furniture at present; but whenever he did so it was to be feared that neither biology nor schemes of reform would lift him above the vulgarity of feeling that there would be an incompatibility in his furniture not being of the best.", "그는 당장은 가구에 대해 생각할 마음이 없었지만, 언제든 그가 그렇게 할 때면, 생물학도 개혁의 구상도 그를 자기 가구가 최상의 것이 아니면 어딘가 어울리지 않으리라는 천박한 감정 위로 끌어올려 주지 못하리라는 것이 우려되는 일이었다."]], [["As to women, he had once already been drawn headlong by impetuous folly, which he meant to be final, since marriage at some distant period would of course not be impetuous.", "여자들에 관해 말하자면, 그는 이미 한 번 충동적인 어리석음에 휩쓸려 곤두박질친 적이 있었는데, 그것을 마지막으로 삼을 작정이었다. 먼 훗날 어느 때의 결혼이라면 물론 충동적이지 않을 터였으니 말이다."], ["For those who want to be acquainted with Lydgate it will be good to know what was that case of impetuous folly, for it may stand as an example of the fitful swerving of passion to which he was prone, together with the chivalrous kindness which helped to make him morally lovable.", "리드게이트와 친해지고 싶은 이들에게는 그 충동적인 어리석음의 사례가 무엇이었는지 아는 것이 좋을 것이다. 그것은 그가 빠지기 쉬웠던 열정의 변덕스러운 일탈의, 그리고 그를 도덕적으로 사랑스럽게 만드는 데 한몫한 기사도적 다정함의 한 예가 될 수 있기 때문이다."], ["The story can be told without many words.", "그 이야기는 많은 말 없이도 할 수 있다."], ["It happened when he was studying in Paris, and just at the time when, over and above his other work, he was occupied with some galvanic experiments.", "그것은 그가 파리에서 공부하던 때, 바로 다른 일에 더하여 어떤 갈바니 전기 실험에 몰두해 있던 무렵에 일어났다."], ["One evening, tired with his experimenting, and not being able to elicit the facts he needed, he left his frogs and rabbits to some repose under their trying and mysterious dispensation of unexplained shocks, and went to finish his evening at the theatre of the Porte Saint Martin, where there was a melodrama which he had already seen several times; attracted, not by the ingenious work of the collaborating authors, but by an actress whose part it was to stab her lover, mistaking him for the evil-designing duke of the piece.", "어느 저녁, 실험에 지치고 자신이 필요로 하는 사실을 끌어내지 못한 채, 그는 개구리와 토끼들을 설명되지 않는 충격이라는 그 가혹하고 신비로운 처분 아래 잠시 쉬게 두고, 포르트 생마르탱 극장에서 저녁 시간을 마저 보내러 갔다. 그곳에서는 그가 이미 여러 번 본 멜로드라마가 상연되고 있었는데, 그를 끌어당긴 것은 공동 집필한 작가들의 기발한 작품이 아니라, 작품 속의 악의에 찬 공작으로 착각하여 자기 연인을 칼로 찌르는 역을 맡은 한 여배우였다."], ["Lydgate was in love with this actress, as a man is in love with a woman whom he never expects to speak to.", "리드게이트는 결코 말을 걸어 볼 일이 없으리라 여기는 여자에게 남자가 사랑에 빠지듯, 이 여배우를 사랑했다."], ["She was a Provencale, with dark eyes, a Greek profile, and rounded majestic form, having that sort of beauty which carries a sweet matronliness even in youth, and her voice was a soft cooing.", "그녀는 프로방스 여인으로, 검은 눈동자와 그리스풍의 옆얼굴, 둥그스름하고 위엄 있는 몸매를 지녔으며, 젊어서도 달콤한 주부다움을 풍기는 그런 종류의 아름다움을 지녔고, 그 목소리는 부드러운 비둘기 울음 같았다."], ["She had but lately come to Paris, and bore a virtuous reputation, her husband acting with her as the unfortunate lover.", "그녀는 파리에 온 지 얼마 되지 않았고 정숙하다는 평판을 지녔으며, 그녀의 남편이 불운한 연인 역으로 그녀와 함께 연기했다."], ["It was her acting which was “no better than it should be,”", "\"딱히 나무랄 데 없다고는 할 수 없는\""], ["but the public was satisfied.", "것은 그녀의 연기였으나, 관객은 만족했다."], ["Lydgate’s only relaxation now was to go and look at this woman, just as he might have thrown himself under the breath of the sweet south on a bank of violets for a while, without prejudice to his galvanism, to which he would presently return.", "이제 리드게이트의 유일한 휴식은 이 여자를 보러 가는 것이었으니, 마치 잠시 제비꽃 핀 둑에서 달콤한 남풍의 숨결 아래 몸을 던지면서도, 곧 돌아갈 자신의 갈바니 전기에는 아무런 손해를 끼치지 않는 것과 같았다."], ["But this evening the old drama had a new catastrophe.", "그러나 이날 저녁, 그 낡은 극에는 새로운 파국이 있었다."], ["At the moment when the heroine was to act the stabbing of her lover, and he was to fall gracefully, the wife veritably stabbed her husband, who fell as death willed.", "여주인공이 자기 연인을 칼로 찌르는 연기를 하고 그가 우아하게 쓰러지기로 한 순간, 아내가 정말로 남편을 칼로 찔렀고, 그는 죽음이 명한 대로 쓰러졌다."], ["A wild shriek pierced the house, and the Provencale fell swooning: a shriek and a swoon were demanded by the play, but the swooning too was real this time.", "거친 비명이 극장을 꿰뚫었고, 그 프로방스 여인은 졸도하여 쓰러졌다. 비명과 졸도는 극이 요구하는 것이었지만, 이번에는 졸도 또한 진짜였다."], ["Lydgate leaped and climbed, he hardly knew how, on to the stage, and was active in help, making the acquaintance of his heroine by finding a contusion on her head and lifting her gently in his arms.", "리드게이트는 어찌했는지도 모르게 펄쩍 뛰어 무대 위로 기어올라가 분주히 거들었고, 그녀의 머리에서 타박상을 발견하고 두 팔로 그녀를 살며시 들어 올리며 자신의 여주인공과 알게 되었다."], ["Paris rang with the story of this death:—was it a murder?", "파리는 이 죽음의 이야기로 떠들썩했다. 살인이었던가?"], ["Some of the actress’s warmest admirers were inclined to believe in her guilt, and liked her the better for it (such was the taste of those times); but Lydgate was not one of these.", "그 여배우의 가장 열렬한 숭배자들 가운데 일부는 그녀의 유죄를 믿고 싶어 했고, 그 때문에 그녀를 더욱 좋아했다(그 시절의 취향이 그러했다). 그러나 리드게이트는 그런 이들 가운데 하나가 아니었다."], ["He vehemently contended for her innocence, and the remote impersonal passion for her beauty which he had felt before, had passed now into personal devotion, and tender thought of her lot.", "그는 그녀의 무죄를 격렬히 변호했고, 전에 그가 느꼈던 그녀의 아름다움에 대한 멀고 비개인적인 열정은 이제 개인적인 헌신으로, 그녀의 처지에 대한 애틋한 생각으로 옮아갔다."], ["The notion of murder was absurd: no motive was discoverable, the young couple being understood to dote on each other; and it was not unprecedented that an accidental slip of the foot should have brought these grave consequences.", "살인이라는 발상은 터무니없었다. 어떤 동기도 찾아낼 수 없었으니, 그 젊은 부부는 서로 끔찍이 아끼는 것으로 알려져 있었고, 우연히 발을 헛디딘 것이 이런 중대한 결과를 불러온 일은 전례가 없지도 않았다."], ["The legal investigation ended in Madame Laure’s release.", "법적 조사는 로르 부인의 석방으로 끝났다."], ["Lydgate by this time had had many interviews with her, and found her more and more adorable.", "리드게이트는 이 무렵 이미 그녀와 여러 차례 만나 이야기를 나누었고, 그녀가 점점 더 흠모할 만하다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["She talked little; but that was an additional charm.", "그녀는 말이 적었지만, 그것이 오히려 또 하나의 매력이었다."], ["She was melancholy, and seemed grateful; her presence was enough, like that of the evening light.", "그녀는 우울해 보였고 고마워하는 것 같았으며, 그녀의 존재만으로 충분했으니, 마치 저녁 빛처럼 그러했다."], ["Lydgate was madly anxious about her affection, and jealous lest any other man than himself should win it and ask her to marry him.", "리드게이트는 그녀의 애정에 대해 미칠 듯이 노심초사했고, 자기 아닌 다른 어떤 남자가 그것을 얻어 그녀에게 청혼할까 봐 질투했다."], ["But instead of reopening her engagement at the Porte Saint Martin, where she would have been all the more popular for the fatal episode, she left Paris without warning, forsaking her little court of admirers.", "그러나 그녀는 그 치명적인 사건 덕분에 더욱 인기를 끌었을 포르트 생마르탱에서의 계약을 다시 트는 대신, 예고도 없이 파리를 떠나 자신의 작은 숭배자 무리를 저버렸다."], ["Perhaps no one carried inquiry far except Lydgate, who felt that all science had come to a stand-still while he imagined the unhappy Laure, stricken by ever-wandering sorrow, herself wandering, and finding no faithful comforter.", "아마도 리드게이트 말고는 아무도 멀리까지 수소문하지 않았을 것이니, 그는 끊임없이 떠도는 슬픔에 짓눌린 불행한 로르가 스스로도 떠돌며 충실한 위로자를 찾지 못하는 모습을 상상하는 동안, 모든 과학이 멈춰 서 버린 듯 느꼈다."], ["Hidden actresses, however, are not so difficult to find as some other hidden facts, and it was not long before Lydgate gathered indications that Laure had taken the route to Lyons.", "그러나 자취를 감춘 여배우란 다른 숨은 사실들만큼 찾기 어렵지는 않은 법이어서, 리드게이트가 로르가 리옹으로 가는 길을 택했다는 단서들을 모으기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다."], ["He found her at last acting with great success at Avignon under the same name, looking more majestic than ever as a forsaken wife carrying her child in her arms.", "그는 마침내 같은 이름으로 아비뇽에서 큰 성공을 거두며 연기하고 있는 그녀를 찾아냈으니, 그녀는 아이를 품에 안은 버림받은 아내로서 어느 때보다 위엄 있어 보였다."], ["He spoke to her after the play, was received with the usual quietude which seemed to him beautiful as clear depths of water, and obtained leave to visit her the next day; when he was bent on telling her that he adored her, and on asking her to marry him.", "그는 공연이 끝난 뒤 그녀에게 말을 걸었고, 그에게는 맑은 물의 깊이처럼 아름답게 느껴지는 그 여느 때의 고요함으로 맞아들여졌으며, 이튿날 그녀를 방문해도 좋다는 허락을 얻었다. 그는 그녀를 흠모한다고 말하고 청혼할 작정이었다."], ["He knew that this was like the sudden impulse of a madman—incongruous even with his habitual foibles.", "그는 이것이 미친 사람의 갑작스러운 충동과도 같다는 것을—그의 평소의 약점들과도 어울리지 않는다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["No matter!", "상관없었다!"], ["It was the one thing which he was resolved to do.", "그것은 그가 하기로 결심한 단 하나의 일이었다."], ["He had two selves within him apparently, and they must learn to accommodate each other and bear reciprocal impediments.", "그의 안에는 두 자아가 있는 것이 분명했고, 그것들은 서로 맞춰 가며 서로의 방해를 견디는 법을 배워야 했다."], ["Strange, that some of us, with quick alternate vision, see beyond our infatuations, and even while we rave on the heights, behold the wide plain where our persistent self pauses and awaits us.", "기이하게도, 우리 가운데 어떤 이들은 번갈아 빠르게 바라보는 시야로 자신의 미혹 너머를 보며, 우리가 높은 곳에서 열에 들떠 떠들어 대는 동안에도, 우리의 끈질긴 자아가 멈춰 서서 우리를 기다리는 그 너른 평원을 바라본다."]], [["To have approached Laure with any suit that was not reverentially tender would have been simply a contradiction of his whole feeling towards her.", "로르에게 경건하리만치 다정하지 않은 어떤 구애를 가지고 다가갔다면, 그것은 그녀를 향한 그의 온 감정에 대한 명백한 모순이었을 것이다."]], [["“You have come all the way from Paris to find me?”", "\"저를 찾으러 파리에서 그 먼 길을 오셨군요?\""], ["she said to him the next day, sitting before him with folded arms, and looking at him with eyes that seemed to wonder as an untamed ruminating animal wonders.", "이튿날 그녀는 팔짱을 끼고 그의 앞에 앉아, 길들지 않은 채 되새김질하는 짐승이 의아해하듯 의아해하는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Are all Englishmen like that?”", "\"영국 남자들은 다 그런가요?\""]], [["“I came because I could not live without trying to see you. You are lonely; I love you; I want you to consent to be my wife; I will wait, but I want you to promise that you will marry me—no one else.”", "\"당신을 만나 보려 애쓰지 않고는 살 수가 없어서 왔습니다. 당신은 외롭고, 나는 당신을 사랑합니다. 당신이 내 아내가 되기로 승낙해 주기를 바랍니다. 나는 기다리겠습니다. 다만 당신이 나와 결혼하겠다고—다른 누구도 아닌 나와—약속해 주기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Laure looked at him in silence with a melancholy radiance from under her grand eyelids, until he was full of rapturous certainty, and knelt close to her knees.", "로르는 그 큰 눈꺼풀 아래로 우울한 광채를 띤 채 말없이 그를 바라보았고, 마침내 그가 황홀한 확신에 가득 차 그녀의 무릎 가까이 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["“I will tell you something,”", "\"당신에게 한 가지 말씀드릴 게 있어요.\""], ["she said, in her cooing way, keeping her arms folded.", "그녀는 팔짱을 낀 채 그 비둘기 울음 같은 어조로 말했다."], ["“My foot really slipped.”", "\"제 발이 정말로 미끄러진 거였어요.\""]], [["“I know, I know,”", "\"압니다, 알아요.\""], ["said Lydgate, deprecatingly.", "리드게이트가 만류하듯 말했다."], ["“It was a fatal accident—a dreadful stroke of calamity that bound me to you the more.”", "\"그건 치명적인 사고였어요—나를 당신에게 더욱 묶어 놓은 끔찍한 재앙의 일격이었지요.\""]], [["Again Laure paused a little and then said, slowly, “I meant to do it.”", "로르는 다시 잠시 멈추었다가 천천히 말했다. \"나는 그걸 할 작정이었어요.\""]], [["Lydgate, strong man as he was, turned pale and trembled: moments seemed to pass before he rose and stood at a distance from her.", "강인한 사내였던 리드게이트는 창백해지며 몸을 떨었다. 그가 일어나 그녀에게서 떨어져 설 때까지 여러 순간이 지나가는 것 같았다."]], [["“There was a secret, then,”", "\"그렇다면 비밀이 있었군요.\""], ["he said at last, even vehemently.", "마침내 그가, 사뭇 격렬하게 말했다."], ["“He was brutal to you: you hated him.”", "\"그가 당신에게 잔인하게 굴었군요. 당신은 그를 미워했고요.\""]], [["“No! he wearied me; he was too fond: he would live in Paris, and not in my country; that was not agreeable to me.”", "\"아니에요! 그가 나를 지치게 했어요. 그는 나를 너무 좋아했지요. 그는 내 고향이 아니라 파리에서 살려고 했어요. 그게 나는 못마땅했어요.\""]], [["“Great God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["said Lydgate, in a groan of horror.", "리드게이트가 공포에 찬 신음으로 말했다."], ["“And you planned to murder him?”", "\"그래서 당신은 그를 죽이려고 계획했단 말입니까?\""]], [["“I did not plan: it came to me in the play—I meant to do it.”", "\"계획한 게 아니에요. 연극 도중에 떠올랐던 거예요—나는 그걸 할 작정이었어요.\""]], [["Lydgate stood mute, and unconsciously pressed his hat on while he looked at her.", "리드게이트는 말없이 서서, 그녀를 바라보며 자기도 모르게 모자를 꾹 눌러썼다."], ["He saw this woman—the first to whom he had given his young adoration—amid the throng of stupid criminals.", "그는 이 여자를—자신이 젊은 흠모를 바친 첫 번째 여자를—어리석은 범죄자들의 무리 속에서 보았다."]], [["“You are a good young man,”", "\"당신은 착한 청년이에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“But I do not like husbands.", "\"하지만 나는 남편이라는 게 싫어요."], ["I will never have another.”", "두 번 다시 남편을 두지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["Three days afterwards Lydgate was at his galvanism again in his Paris chambers, believing that illusions were at an end for him.", "사흘 뒤 리드게이트는 파리의 자기 방에서 다시 갈바니 전기 실험에 매달렸으니, 자신에게 환상이란 이제 끝났다고 믿었다."], ["He was saved from hardening effects by the abundant kindness of his heart and his belief that human life might be made better.", "그는 풍성한 마음의 친절함과, 인간의 삶이 더 나아질 수 있으리라는 믿음 덕분에 냉혹해지는 영향에서 구원받았다."], ["But he had more reason than ever for trusting his judgment, now that it was so experienced; and henceforth he would take a strictly scientific view of woman, entertaining no expectations but such as were justified beforehand.", "그러나 그는 이제 자신의 판단이 그토록 경험을 쌓았으니 그 어느 때보다 그것을 신뢰할 이유가 있었고, 이후로 여자에 대해서는 엄격히 과학적인 관점을 취하여, 미리 정당화된 것 외에는 어떤 기대도 품지 않으리라 다짐했다."]], [["No one in Middlemarch was likely to have such a notion of Lydgate’s past as has here been faintly shadowed, and indeed the respectable townsfolk there were not more given than mortals generally to any eager attempt at exactness in the representation to themselves of what did not come under their own senses.", "미들마치의 누구도 여기서 어렴풋이 그림자처럼 그려진 것과 같은 리드게이트의 과거에 대한 관념을 지닐 법하지 않았고, 실로 그곳의 점잖은 읍민들은 자기네 감각에 들어오지 않는 것을 스스로에게 정확히 그려 보려는 열성적인 시도에서 보통 사람들보다 더할 것이 없었다."], ["Not only young virgins of that town, but gray-bearded men also, were often in haste to conjecture how a new acquaintance might be wrought into their purposes, contented with very vague knowledge as to the way in which life had been shaping him for that instrumentality.", "그 읍의 젊은 처녀들뿐 아니라 잿빛 수염의 남자들조차도, 한 인생이 그 도구적 쓸모를 위해 그를 어떻게 빚어 왔는가에 관해서는 매우 막연한 지식에 만족한 채, 새로 알게 된 사람이 어떻게 자기네 목적에 맞춰질 수 있을지를 추측하느라 흔히 서두르곤 했다."], ["Middlemarch, in fact, counted on swallowing Lydgate and assimilating him very comfortably.", "사실 미들마치는 리드게이트를 삼켜 매우 편안하게 자기 것으로 동화시키리라 셈하고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI.", "# 제14장"]], [["“All that in woman is adored In thy fair self I find— For the whole sex can but afford The handsome and the kind.”", "오직 아리따움과 다정함뿐이니."], ["—SIR CHARLES SEDLEY.", "ㅡ찰스 세들리 경"]], [["The question whether Mr.", "타이크 씨를 병원의 봉급받는 원목으로 임명할 것인가 하는 문제는 미들마치 사람들에게 흥미진진한 화젯거리였다."], ["Tyke should be appointed as salaried chaplain to the hospital was an exciting topic to the Middlemarchers; and Lydgate heard it discussed in a way that threw much light on the power exercised in the town by Mr.", "리드게이트는 그 논의가 오가는 방식을 들으면서 불스트로드 씨가 이 도시에서 행사하는 권력의 실체를 적잖이 깨달았다."], ["Bulstrode. The banker was evidently a ruler, but there was an opposition party, and even among his supporters there were some who allowed it to be seen that their support was a compromise, and who frankly stated their impression that the general scheme of things, and especially the casualties of trade, required you to hold a candle to the devil.", "그 은행가는 분명 지배자였으나, 반대파가 있었고, 심지어 그의 지지자들 가운데서도 자신들의 지지가 하나의 타협임을 내비치는 이들이 있었으니, 이들은 세상사의 일반적인 형편, 특히 장사에서 닥치는 뜻밖의 사고들 때문에 악마에게도 촛불을 들어 줘야 한다는 인상을 솔직히 털어놓았다."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode’s power was not due simply to his being a country banker, who knew the financial secrets of most traders in the town and could touch the springs of their credit; it was fortified by a beneficence that was at once ready and severe—ready to confer obligations, and severe in watching the result.", "불스트로드 씨의 권력은 단지 그가 시골 은행가로서 도시의 거의 모든 상인들의 재정적 비밀을 알고 그들의 신용의 발판을 건드릴 수 있다는 데서만 비롯된 것이 아니었다."], ["He had gathered, as an industrious man always at his post, a chief share in administering the town charities, and his private charities were both minute and abundant.", "그것은 즉각적이면서도 준엄한 자선, 곧 기꺼이 은혜를 베풀되 그 결과를 준엄하게 감시하는 자선으로 한층 강화되었다. 그는 늘 제자리를 지키는 부지런한 사람으로서 도시의 자선 사업을 운영하는 데 주된 몫을 차지했으며, 사적인 자선도 세심하고 풍성했다."], ["He would take a great deal of pains about apprenticing Tegg the shoemaker’s son, and he would watch over Tegg’s church-going; he would defend Mrs.", "그는 구두장이 테그의 아들을 도제로 들여보내는 데 큰 수고를 마다하지 않았고, 테그가 교회에 나가는지도 지켜보았다."], ["Strype the washerwoman against Stubbs’s unjust exaction on the score of her drying-ground, and he would himself scrutinize a calumny against Mrs. Strype.", "그는 세탁부 스트라이프 부인을 빨래 너는 땅을 두고 부당하게 돈을 뜯어내려는 스텁스로부터 보호해 주었고, 스트라이프 부인에 관한 중상에 대해서는 몸소 자세히 따져 보았다."], ["His private minor loans were numerous, but he would inquire strictly into the circumstances both before and after.", "그의 사적인 소액 대출은 수없이 많았지만, 그는 대출 전후의 사정을 엄격하게 캐물었다."], ["In this way a man gathers a domain in his neighbors’ hope and fear as well as gratitude; and power, when once it has got into that subtle region, propagates itself, spreading out of all proportion to its external means.", "이런 식으로 한 사람은 이웃들의 감사뿐 아니라 희망과 두려움 속에서도 자신의 영역을 넓혀 가는 법이다. 그리고 권력이란 일단 그런 미묘한 영역에 발을 들이면 스스로 번식하여, 외적인 수단에 전혀 걸맞지 않을 만큼 멀리 퍼져 나간다."], ["It was a principle with Mr. Bulstrode to gain as much power as possible, that he might use it for the glory of God.", "가능한 한 많은 권력을 얻어 그것을 하느님의 영광을 위해 쓰겠다는 것이 불스트로드 씨의 원칙이었다."], ["He went through a great deal of spiritual conflict and inward argument in order to adjust his motives, and make clear to himself what God’s glory required.", "그는 자신의 동기를 조율하고 하느님의 영광이 무엇을 요구하는지 스스로 분명히 하기 위해 숱한 영적 갈등과 내면의 논쟁을 거쳤다."], ["But, as we have seen, his motives were not always rightly appreciated.", "그러나 우리가 보았듯이 그의 동기는 늘 올바르게 평가받지는 못했다."], ["There were many crass minds in Middlemarch whose reflective scales could only weigh things in the lump; and they had a strong suspicion that since Mr.", "미들마치에는 사물을 뭉뚱그려서만 저울질할 수 있는 무딘 사람들이 많았다."], ["Bulstrode could not enjoy life in their fashion, eating and drinking so little as he did, and worreting himself about everything, he must have a sort of vampire’s feast in the sense of mastery.", "그들은 불스트로드 씨가 자기들처럼 인생을 즐기지 못하고 그토록 적게 먹고 마시며 모든 일에 안달복달하니, 분명 지배감이라는 의미에서 일종의 흡혈귀 같은 향연을 누리고 있으리라고 강하게 의심했다."]], [["The subject of the chaplaincy came up at Mr. Vincy’s table when Lydgate was dining there, and the family connection with Mr.", "원목 임명 문제는 리드게이트가 빈시 씨 댁에서 저녁을 먹을 때 식탁에 올랐는데, 불스트로드 씨와의 인척 관계가 주인장 자신조차 어느 정도 자유로운 발언을 하는 데 걸림돌이 되지 않는다는 것을 그는 알아차렸다."], ["Bulstrode did not, he observed, prevent some freedom of remark even on the part of the host himself, though his reasons against the proposed arrangement turned entirely on his objection to Mr.", "다만 빈시 씨가 그 제안에 반대하는 이유는 전적으로 온통 교리뿐인 타이크 씨의 설교를 못마땅하게 여기는 데, 그리고 그런 흠이 없는 페어브러더 씨를 선호하는 데 있었다."], ["Tyke’s sermons, which were all doctrine, and his preference for Mr. Farebrother, whose sermons were free from that taint.", "빈시 씨는 원목에게 봉급을 준다는 발상 자체는 흡족해했으나, 그것이 페어브러더에게 돌아간다는 전제하에서였다."], ["Mr. Vincy liked well enough the notion of the chaplain’s having a salary, supposing it were given to Farebrother, who was as good a little fellow as ever breathed, and the best preacher anywhere, and companionable too.", "페어브러더로 말하자면 둘도 없이 착한 친구이자 어디 내놔도 가장 뛰어난 설교자였고, 더구나 어울리기에도 좋은 사람이었다."]], [["“What line shall you take, then?” said Mr.", "\"그럼 자네는 어느 편에 서겠나?\""], ["Chichely, the coroner, a great coursing comrade of Mr. Vincy’s.", "빈시 씨와 사냥을 함께 즐기는 절친한 동무이자 검시관인 치철리 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, I’m precious glad I’m not one of the Directors now.", "\"아, 지금 내가 이사진의 한 사람이 아니라서 천만다행이지."], ["I shall vote for referring the matter to the Directors and the Medical Board together.", "나는 그 문제를 이사진과 의무 위원회에 함께 회부하자는 데 표를 던질 걸세."], ["I shall roll some of my responsibility on your shoulders, Doctor,” said Mr.", "내 책임의 얼마쯤은 자네 어깨에 떠넘기겠네, 박사 양반.\""], ["Vincy, glancing first at Dr. Sprague, the senior physician of the town, and then at Lydgate who sat opposite.", "빈시 씨는 먼저 도시의 선임 내과 의사인 스프레이그 박사를, 이어 맞은편에 앉은 리드게이트를 흘긋 보며 말했다."], ["“You medical gentlemen must consult which sort of black draught you will prescribe, eh, Mr. Lydgate?”", "\"의사 양반들이 어떤 종류의 검은 물약을 처방할지 의논해 보셔야겠소, 안 그렇소, 리드게이트 씨?\""]], [["“I know little of either,”", "\"저는 그 어느 쪽에 대해서도 아는 게 별로 없습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate; “but in general, appointments are apt to be made too much a question of personal liking.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"하지만 일반적으로 임명이라는 게 너무 개인적인 호감의 문제가 되기 십상이지요."], ["The fittest man for a particular post is not always the best fellow or the most agreeable.", "특정한 자리에 가장 적합한 사람이 늘 가장 착한 친구거나 가장 사람 좋은 인물은 아니니까요."], ["Sometimes, if you wanted to get a reform, your only way would be to pension off the good fellows whom everybody is fond of, and put them out of the question.”", "때로는 개혁을 이루고 싶으면, 누구나 좋아하는 그 착한 친구들에게 연금을 주어 물러나게 하고 아예 고려 대상에서 빼는 것이 유일한 길일 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["Dr. Sprague, who was considered the physician of most “weight,”", "스프레이그 박사는, 비록 민친 박사가 흔히 더 \"통찰력\"이 있다고 일컬어지긴 했어도 가장 \"비중\""], ["though Dr. Minchin was usually said to have more “penetration,”", "있는 의사로 여겨졌는데, 리드게이트가 말하는 동안 그 크고 묵직한 얼굴에서 일체의 표정을 거두고 자기 포도주 잔만 바라보았다."], ["divested his large heavy face of all expression, and looked at his wine-glass while Lydgate was speaking. Whatever was not problematical and suspected about this young man—for example, a certain showiness as to foreign ideas, and a disposition to unsettle what had been settled and forgotten by his elders—was positively unwelcome to a physician whose standing had been fixed thirty years before by a treatise on Meningitis, of which at least one copy marked “own”", "이 젊은이에 관해 문젯거리도 의심스러운 것도 아닌 점들ㅡ이를테면 외국 사상에 대한 어떤 과시벽, 그리고 손위 사람들이 이미 정해 두고 잊어버린 것을 다시 들쑤시려는 성향ㅡ은 삼십 년 전에 뇌막염에 관한 논문으로 자기 입지를 굳힌 의사에게는 단연코 달갑지 않은 것이었다."], ["was bound in calf. For my part I have some fellow-feeling with Dr.", "그 논문은 적어도 \"본인 소장\"이라고 표시된 한 부가 송아지 가죽으로 장정되어 있었다."], ["Sprague: one’s self-satisfaction is an untaxed kind of property which it is very unpleasant to find deprecated.", "나로서는 스프레이그 박사에게 어느 정도 동병상련을 느낀다. 자기만족이란 세금도 물지 않는 일종의 재산인데, 그것이 폄하당하는 것을 보는 일은 여간 불쾌하지 않으니까."]], [["Lydgate’s remark, however, did not meet the sense of the company.", "그러나 리드게이트의 발언은 좌중의 정서와 들어맞지 않았다."], ["Mr. Vincy said, that if he could have his way, he would not put disagreeable fellows anywhere.", "빈시 씨는 자기 뜻대로 할 수 있다면 못마땅한 친구들은 어디에도 앉히지 않을 거라고 말했다."]], [["“Hang your reforms!”", "\"개혁 따위는 집어치우게!\""], ["said Mr. Chichely.", "치철리 씨가 말했다."], ["“There’s no greater humbug in the world.", "\"세상에 그보다 더한 사기는 없어."], ["You never hear of a reform, but it means some trick to put in new men. I hope you are not one of the ‘Lancet’s’ men, Mr.", "개혁이라는 말이 나오면 그건 으레 새 사람을 들이려는 무슨 술책이라는 뜻이지."], ["Lydgate—wanting to take the coronership out of the hands of the legal profession: your words appear to point that way.”", "자네가 그 『랜싯』 패거리의 한 사람은 아니길 바라네, 리드게이트 씨ㅡ검시관 자리를 법조계의 손에서 빼앗으려는 자들 말일세. 자네 말이 그쪽을 가리키는 것 같으니 하는 소릴세.\""]], [["“I disapprove of Wakley,”", "\"나는 웨이클리를 인정하지 않소.\""], ["interposed Dr.", "스프레이그 박사가 끼어들었다."], ["Sprague, “no man more: he is an ill-intentioned fellow, who would sacrifice the respectability of the profession, which everybody knows depends on the London Colleges, for the sake of getting some notoriety for himself.", "\"그 누구보다도 말이오. 그자는 심보가 고약한 작자라, 누구나 알다시피 런던의 의학원들에 달려 있는 우리 직업의 품위를, 그저 제 한 몸 악명이나 떨치려고 희생시킬 인간이오."], ["There are men who don’t mind about being kicked blue if they can only get talked about.", "입에만 오를 수 있다면 흠씬 두들겨 맞아 멍이 들어도 개의치 않는 자들이 있지."], ["But Wakley is right sometimes,”", "하지만 웨이클리가 옳을 때도 있긴 하오.\""], ["the Doctor added, judicially.", "박사는 판관처럼 신중하게 덧붙였다."], ["“I could mention one or two points in which Wakley is in the right.”", "\"웨이클리가 옳은 점을 한두 가지 들 수도 있소.\""]], [["“Oh, well,”", "\"아, 뭐,\""], ["said Mr.", "치철리 씨가 말했다."], ["Chichely, “I blame no man for standing up in favor of his own cloth; but, coming to argument, I should like to know how a coroner is to judge of evidence if he has not had a legal training?”", "\"자기 직업 편을 들고 나서는 거야 누구도 탓할 수 없지. 하지만 따지고 들자면, 법률 교육을 받지 못한 검시관이 대체 어떻게 증거를 판단한단 말인가, 그게 알고 싶군.\""]], [["“In my opinion,”", "\"제 생각으로는,\""], ["said Lydgate, “legal training only makes a man more incompetent in questions that require knowledge of another kind.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"법률 교육은 다른 종류의 지식을 요하는 문제에서 오히려 사람을 더 무능하게 만들 뿐입니다."], ["People talk about evidence as if it could really be weighed in scales by a blind Justice.", "사람들은 마치 눈먼 정의의 여신이 저울로 정말 달아 볼 수 있기라도 한 듯 증거를 입에 올리지요."], ["No man can judge what is good evidence on any particular subject, unless he knows that subject well.", "어떤 주제든 그것을 잘 알지 못하는 한 무엇이 좋은 증거인지 누구도 판단할 수 없습니다."], ["A lawyer is no better than an old woman at a post-mortem examination.", "부검에 있어서는 변호사라도 노파보다 나을 게 없어요."], ["How is he to know the action of a poison?", "변호사가 독약의 작용을 어찌 알겠습니까?"], ["You might as well say that scanning verse will teach you to scan the potato crops.”", "그건 운율을 따질 줄 알면 감자 농사도 따질 수 있다고 말하는 거나 마찬가지지요.\""]], [["“You are aware, I suppose, that it is not the coroner’s business to conduct the post-mortem, but only to take the evidence of the medical witness?”", "\"검시관의 일이란 부검을 직접 하는 게 아니라 그저 의학 증인의 증언을 받는 것뿐이라는 점은, 자네도 알 텐데?\""], ["said Mr. Chichely, with some scorn.", "치철리 씨가 다소 비웃듯이 말했다."]], [["“Who is often almost as ignorant as the coroner himself,”", "\"그 의학 증인이라는 게 검시관 자신만큼이나 무지한 경우가 허다하지요.\""], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“Questions of medical jurisprudence ought not to be left to the chance of decent knowledge in a medical witness, and the coroner ought not to be a man who will believe that strychnine will destroy the coats of the stomach if an ignorant practitioner happens to tell him so.”", "\"법의학 문제를 의학 증인이 어쩌다 가진 알량한 지식에 맡겨서는 안 됩니다. 그리고 무지한 개업의가 어쩌다 일러 준 말이라고 해서 스트리크닌이 위벽을 파괴한다고 곧이곧대로 믿을 사람을 검시관으로 앉혀서도 안 되고요.\""]], [["Lydgate had really lost sight of the fact that Mr.", "리드게이트는 치철리 씨가 다름 아닌 국왕 폐하의 검시관이라는 사실을 정작 까맣게 잊고서, 천연덕스럽게 이런 질문으로 말을 맺었다."], ["Chichely was his Majesty’s coroner, and ended innocently with the question, “Don’t you agree with me, Dr. Sprague?”", "\"박사님도 제 의견에 동의하시지요, 스프레이그 박사님?\""]], [["“To a certain extent—with regard to populous districts, and in the metropolis,”", "\"어느 정도까지는요ㅡ인구가 많은 지역이나 수도의 경우에는 말입니다.\""], ["said the Doctor.", "박사가 말했다."], ["“But I hope it will be long before this part of the country loses the services of my friend Chichely, even though it might get the best man in our profession to succeed him.", "\"하지만 우리 직업에서 가장 뛰어난 사람을 후임으로 들인다 할지라도, 이 고장이 내 친구 치철리의 봉사를 잃게 되는 일은 한참 뒤의 일이기를 바라오."], ["I am sure Vincy will agree with me.”", "빈시도 분명 내 말에 동의할 거요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, give me a coroner who is a good coursing man,”", "\"그럼, 그렇고말고, 나는 사냥 잘하는 검시관이 좋소.\""], ["said Mr. Vincy, jovially.", "빈시 씨가 유쾌하게 말했다."], ["“And in my opinion, you’re safest with a lawyer.", "\"내 생각에는 변호사를 두는 게 제일 안전하지."], ["Nobody can know everything.", "아무도 모든 걸 다 알 수는 없는 법이오."], ["Most things are ‘visitation of God.’ And as to poisoning, why, what you want to know is the law.", "대개의 일들은 '하느님의 부르심'이니까. 그리고 독살로 말하자면, 결국 알아야 할 건 법이지."], ["Come, shall we join the ladies?”", "자, 이제 부인들에게로 갈까요?\""]], [["Lydgate’s private opinion was that Mr. Chichely might be the very coroner without bias as to the coats of the stomach, but he had not meant to be personal.", "리드게이트는 속으로 치철리 씨야말로 위벽에 관해 아무런 편견도 없는 바로 그런 검시관일지 모른다고 생각했으나, 그를 빗대어 한 말은 아니었다."], ["This was one of the difficulties of moving in good Middlemarch society: it was dangerous to insist on knowledge as a qualification for any salaried office.", "이것이 미들마치의 점잖은 사교계에서 처신하는 데 따르는 어려움 가운데 하나였다. 봉급받는 어떤 자리든 그 자격으로 지식을 고집하는 것은 위험한 일이었다."], ["Fred Vincy had called Lydgate a prig, and now Mr.", "프레드 빈시는 리드게이트를 잘난 척하는 샌님이라 불렀고, 이제 치철리 씨는 그를 건방진 풋내기라 부르고 싶어 했다."], ["Chichely was inclined to call him prick-eared; especially when, in the drawing-room, he seemed to be making himself eminently agreeable to Rosamond, whom he had easily monopolized in a tête-à-tête, since Mrs.", "특히 응접실에서 그가 로저먼드의 비위를 더없이 잘 맞추는 것처럼 보일 때 그러했다. 빈시 부인이 직접 차를 따르는 탁자에 앉아 있었던 터라, 그는 단둘이 마주 앉아 어렵잖게 로저먼드를 독차지하고 있었다."], ["Vincy herself sat at the tea-table.", "빈시 부인은 어떤 살림의 소임도 딸에게 넘기지 않았다."], ["She resigned no domestic function to her daughter; and the matron’s blooming good-natured face, with the two volatile pink strings floating from her fine throat, and her cheery manners to husband and children, was certainly among the great attractions of the Vincy house—attractions which made it all the easier to fall in love with the daughter.", "곱고 환한 목덜미에서 휘날리는 두 가닥 분홍빛 끈, 남편과 아이들을 대하는 쾌활한 태도, 그리고 그 활짝 핀 듯 인심 좋아 보이는 안주인의 얼굴은 분명 빈시가의 큰 매력 가운데 하나였으니ㅡ이런 매력은 그 집 딸과 사랑에 빠지기를 한결 쉽게 만들었다."], ["The tinge of unpretentious, inoffensive vulgarity in Mrs. Vincy gave more effect to Rosamond’s refinement, which was beyond what Lydgate had expected.", "빈시 부인에게서 풍기는, 잘난 체하지 않고 거슬리지도 않는 약간의 천박함은 로저먼드의 세련됨을 더욱 돋보이게 했으며, 그 세련됨은 리드게이트가 기대했던 것 이상이었다."]], [["Certainly, small feet and perfectly turned shoulders aid the impression of refined manners, and the right thing said seems quite astonishingly right when it is accompanied with exquisite curves of lip and eyelid.", "분명 작은 발과 완벽하게 빠진 어깨선은 세련된 몸가짐이라는 인상을 거들기 마련이고, 입술과 눈꺼풀의 더없이 아름다운 곡선이 함께할 때면 적절한 말 한마디가 놀랍도록 적절하게 들리는 법이다."], ["And Rosamond could say the right thing; for she was clever with that sort of cleverness which catches every tone except the humorous.", "그런데 로저먼드는 적절한 말을 할 줄 알았다. 익살스러운 가락만 빼고 온갖 가락을 다 포착하는 그런 종류의 영민함을 지녔기 때문이다."], ["Happily she never attempted to joke, and this perhaps was the most decisive mark of her cleverness.", "다행히도 그녀는 농담을 시도한 적이 한 번도 없었으니, 어쩌면 이것이야말로 그녀의 영민함을 가장 결정적으로 드러내는 표시였는지도 모른다."]], [["She and Lydgate readily got into conversation.", "그녀와 리드게이트는 어렵잖게 대화에 빠져들었다."], ["He regretted that he had not heard her sing the other day at Stone Court.", "그는 며칠 전 스톤 코트에서 그녀의 노래를 듣지 못한 것이 아쉽다고 했다."], ["The only pleasure he allowed himself during the latter part of his stay in Paris was to go and hear music.", "그가 파리 체류 후반에 스스로에게 허락한 유일한 즐거움이 음악을 들으러 가는 것이었다."]], [["“You have studied music, probably?”", "\"음악을 공부하셨나 보군요?\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."]], [["“No, I know the notes of many birds, and I know many melodies by ear; but the music that I don’t know at all, and have no notion about, delights me—affects me.", "\"아니요, 저는 새들의 지저귐을 많이 알고, 귀로 익힌 가락도 많지요. 하지만 제가 전혀 알지 못하고 아무 짐작도 없는 음악이 오히려 저를 즐겁게 합니다ㅡ저를 감동시키지요."], ["How stupid the world is that it does not make more use of such a pleasure within its reach!”", "손 닿는 곳에 그런 즐거움이 있는데도 더 많이 누리지 못하다니, 세상이란 얼마나 어리석은지요!\""]], [["“Yes, and you will find Middlemarch very tuneless.", "\"네, 그리고 미들마치가 무척 음악과 거리가 멀다는 걸 알게 되실 거예요."], ["There are hardly any good musicians.", "변변한 음악가가 거의 없거든요."], ["I only know two gentlemen who sing at all well.”", "제법 노래를 잘하는 신사라고는 두 분밖에 몰라요.\""]], [["“I suppose it is the fashion to sing comic songs in a rhythmic way, leaving you to fancy the tune—very much as if it were tapped on a drum?”", "\"아마 우스꽝스러운 노래를 마치 북을 두드리듯 박자만 맞춰 부르면서, 가락은 듣는 사람이 알아서 상상하라고 내버려 두는 것이 유행인가 보지요?\""]], [["“Ah, you have heard Mr. Bowyer,”", "\"아, 바우어 씨의 노래를 들으셨군요.\""], ["said Rosamond, with one of her rare smiles.", "로저먼드가 좀체 보기 드문 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["“But we are speaking very ill of our neighbors.”", "\"그런데 우리가 이웃들 흉을 너무 보고 있네요.\""]], [["Lydgate was almost forgetting that he must carry on the conversation, in thinking how lovely this creature was, her garment seeming to be made out of the faintest blue sky, herself so immaculately blond, as if the petals of some gigantic flower had just opened and disclosed her; and yet with this infantine blondness showing so much ready, self-possessed grace.", "리드게이트는 이 가녀린 존재가 얼마나 사랑스러운지 헤아리느라 대화를 이어 가야 한다는 것마저 거의 잊을 지경이었다. 그녀의 옷은 더없이 옅은 푸른 하늘로 지은 듯했고, 그녀 자신은 어떤 거대한 꽃잎이 막 벌어지며 그 속에서 드러난 듯 티 없이 금빛으로 빛났다."], ["Since he had had the memory of Laure, Lydgate had lost all taste for large-eyed silence: the divine cow no longer attracted him, and Rosamond was her very opposite.", "그러면서도 그 어린아이 같은 금빛에는 그토록 자연스럽고 침착한 우아함이 어려 있었다. 로르의 기억을 품게 된 뒤로 리드게이트는 눈만 큰 침묵에 대한 모든 취향을 잃어버렸다."], ["But he recalled himself.", "그 성스러운 암소는 더 이상 그를 끌어당기지 못했고, 로저먼드는 그와 정반대였다. 하지만 그는 정신을 차렸다."]], [["“You will let me hear some music to-night, I hope.”", "\"오늘 밤 음악을 좀 들려주시겠지요.\""]], [["“I will let you hear my attempts, if you like,”", "\"원하신다면 제 서툰 솜씨를 들려 드리지요.\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“Papa is sure to insist on my singing.", "\"아버지가 분명 제게 노래를 시키실 테니까요."], ["But I shall tremble before you, who have heard the best singers in Paris.", "하지만 파리에서 최고의 가수들을 들으신 당신 앞에서는 떨릴 거예요."], ["I have heard very little: I have only once been to London.", "저는 들은 게 거의 없거든요. 런던에도 딱 한 번 가 봤을 뿐이고요."], ["But our organist at St. Peter’s is a good musician, and I go on studying with him.”", "그래도 성 베드로 성당의 오르간 연주자가 훌륭한 음악가여서, 저는 그분께 계속 배우고 있어요.\""]], [["“Tell me what you saw in London.”", "\"런던에서 무엇을 보셨는지 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“Very little.”", "\"별것 없었어요.\""], ["(A more naive girl would have said, “Oh, everything!”", "(좀 더 순진한 처녀였다면 \"아, 모든 걸요!\"라고 했으리라."], ["But Rosamond knew better.) “A few of the ordinary sights, such as raw country girls are always taken to.”", "하지만 로저먼드는 그보다 영리했다.) \"촌스러운 시골 처녀들이 으레 구경하는 평범한 명소 몇 군데뿐이었지요.\""]], [["“Do you call yourself a raw country girl?”", "\"스스로를 촌스러운 시골 처녀라고 부르시는 겁니까?\""], ["said Lydgate, looking at her with an involuntary emphasis of admiration, which made Rosamond blush with pleasure.", "리드게이트는 저도 모르게 감탄을 실어 그녀를 바라보며 말했고, 그 바람에 로저먼드는 기쁨에 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["But she remained simply serious, turned her long neck a little, and put up her hand to touch her wondrous hair-plaits—an habitual gesture with her as pretty as any movements of a kitten’s paw.", "하지만 그녀는 그저 진지한 표정을 유지한 채 긴 목을 살짝 돌리고는 손을 들어 그 경탄스러운 땋은 머리채를 매만졌다ㅡ그녀의 버릇 같은 몸짓으로, 새끼 고양이의 앞발 놀림 못지않게 어여뻤다. 그렇다고 로저먼드가 조금이라도 새끼 고양이를 닮았다는 말은 아니다."], ["Not that Rosamond was in the least like a kitten: she was a sylph caught young and educated at Mrs. Lemon’s.", "그녀는 어린 나이에 붙잡혀 레먼 부인의 학교에서 교육받은 한 마리 요정이었다."]], [["“I assure you my mind is raw,”", "\"제 머릿속이 정말로 설익었다는 걸 장담해요.\""], ["she said immediately; “I pass at Middlemarch.", "그녀가 곧바로 말했다. \"미들마치에서야 그럭저럭 통하지요."], ["I am not afraid of talking to our old neighbors.", "우리 오랜 이웃들과 이야기하는 건 두렵지 않아요."], ["But I am really afraid of you.”", "하지만 당신은 정말로 두려워요.\""]], [["“An accomplished woman almost always knows more than we men, though her knowledge is of a different sort.", "\"교양 있는 여인은 거의 언제나 우리 남자들보다 더 많이 압니다. 비록 그 지식의 종류가 다르긴 하지만요."], ["I am sure you could teach me a thousand things—as an exquisite bird could teach a bear if there were any common language between them.", "당신이라면 제게 수천 가지를 가르쳐 주실 수 있을 겁니다ㅡ아리따운 새가 곰에게 가르쳐 줄 수 있듯이요, 둘 사이에 공통의 언어만 있다면 말이지요."], ["Happily, there is a common language between women and men, and so the bears can get taught.”", "다행히 여자와 남자 사이에는 공통의 언어가 있으니, 곰도 배울 수 있는 셈이지요.\""]], [["“Ah, there is Fred beginning to strum!", "\"아, 저기 프레드가 또 건반을 두드리기 시작하네요!"], ["I must go and hinder him from jarring all your nerves,”", "가서 당신의 신경을 온통 거스르지 못하게 말려야겠어요.\""], ["said Rosamond, moving to the other side of the room, where Fred having opened the piano, at his father’s desire, that Rosamond might give them some music, was parenthetically performing “Cherry Ripe!”", "로저먼드는 이렇게 말하며 방 저편으로 옮겨 갔다. 그곳에서는 프레드가, 로저먼드가 음악을 들려주도록 아버지가 청한 대로 피아노 뚜껑을 열어 놓고는, 곁다리 삼아 한 손으로 「체리 라이프」를 치고 있었다."], ["with one hand. Able men who have passed their examinations will do these things sometimes, not less than the plucked Fred.", "시험을 통과한 유능한 사람들도 이따금 이런 짓을 하니, 낙방한 프레드만 그러는 것은 아니다."]], [["“Fred, pray defer your practising till to-morrow; you will make Mr.", "\"프레드, 연습은 내일로 미뤄 줘. 리드게이트 씨를 병나게 만들겠어.\""], ["Lydgate ill,”", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["said Rosamond. “He has an ear.”", "\"이분은 귀가 예민하시거든.\""]], [["Fred laughed, and went on with his tune to the end.", "프레드는 웃으며 곡을 끝까지 마저 쳤다."]], [["Rosamond turned to Lydgate, smiling gently, and said, “You perceive, the bears will not always be taught.”", "로저먼드는 리드게이트를 돌아보며 부드럽게 미소 짓고는 말했다. \"보시다시피 곰이라고 늘 배워지는 건 아니랍니다.\""]], [["“Now then, Rosy!”", "\"자, 그럼 로지!\""], ["said Fred, springing from the stool and twisting it upward for her, with a hearty expectation of enjoyment.", "프레드가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 누나를 위해 그것을 위로 돌려 높이며, 한껏 즐길 기대에 부풀어 말했다."], ["“Some good rousing tunes first.”", "\"먼저 신나는 곡 몇 개부터.\""]], [["Rosamond played admirably.", "로저먼드의 연주는 훌륭했다."], ["Her master at Mrs. Lemon’s school (close to a county town with a memorable history that had its relics in church and castle) was one of those excellent musicians here and there to be found in our provinces, worthy to compare with many a noted Kapellmeister in a country which offers more plentiful conditions of musical celebrity.", "(교회와 성에 그 유물이 남아 있는, 기억할 만한 역사를 지닌 어느 주도 가까이에 있던) 레먼 부인 학교에서 그녀를 가르친 선생은, 우리 지방에서 여기저기 더러 만날 수 있는 그런 빼어난 음악가 가운데 하나로, 음악으로 이름을 떨칠 조건이 한결 풍부한 어느 나라의 이름난 악장 못지않게 견줄 만한 인물이었다."], ["Rosamond, with the executant’s instinct, had seized his manner of playing, and gave forth his large rendering of noble music with the precision of an echo.", "연주자의 본능을 지닌 로저먼드는 그의 연주법을 그대로 붙잡아, 고결한 음악에 대한 그의 폭넓은 해석을 메아리처럼 정확하게 풀어냈다. 처음 들으면 거의 깜짝 놀랄 만했다."], ["It was almost startling, heard for the first time.", "로저먼드의 손가락에서 숨은 영혼이 흘러나오는 듯했다."], ["A hidden soul seemed to be flowing forth from Rosamond’s fingers; and so indeed it was, since souls live on in perpetual echoes, and to all fine expression there goes somewhere an originating activity, if it be only that of an interpreter.", "그리고 사실 그러했으니, 영혼이란 끊임없는 메아리 속에 살아남는 법이고, 모든 빼어난 표현에는 그것이 비록 해석자의 활동에 지나지 않을지라도 어딘가에 그것을 낳는 활동이 깃들어 있기 때문이다."], ["Lydgate was taken possession of, and began to believe in her as something exceptional.", "리드게이트는 사로잡혔고, 그녀를 무언가 예외적인 존재로 믿기 시작했다."], ["After all, he thought, one need not be surprised to find the rare conjunctions of nature under circumstances apparently unfavorable: come where they may, they always depend on conditions that are not obvious.", "결국, 하고 그는 생각했다, 겉보기에 불리해 보이는 환경 아래에서 자연의 진귀한 조합을 발견한다 해도 놀랄 일은 아니다. 그것들은 어디에서 나타나든 늘 겉으로 드러나지 않는 조건에 달려 있는 법이니까."], ["He sat looking at her, and did not rise to pay her any compliments, leaving that to others, now that his admiration was deepened.", "그는 앉아서 그녀를 바라보았고, 일어나 찬사를 건네지도 않은 채 그것을 다른 이들에게 맡겨 두었으니, 이제 그의 감탄이 한층 깊어진 까닭이었다."]], [["Her singing was less remarkable, but also well trained, and sweet to hear as a chime perfectly in tune.", "그녀의 노래는 그만큼 빼어나지는 않았으나 역시 잘 다듬어져 있었고, 완벽하게 가락이 맞는 종소리처럼 듣기에 감미로웠다."], ["It is true she sang “Meet me by moonlight,”", "사실 그녀는 「달빛 속에서 나를 만나요」와 「나는 떠돌아다녔네」를 불렀다."], ["and “I’ve been roaming”; for mortals must share the fashions of their time, and none but the ancients can be always classical. But Rosamond could also sing “Black-eyed Susan”", "인간이란 자기 시대의 유행을 함께 나누기 마련이고, 오로지 옛사람만이 늘 고전적일 수 있기 때문이다."], ["with effect, or Haydn’s canzonets, or “Voi, che sapete,” or “Batti, batti”—she only wanted to know what her audience liked.", "하지만 로저먼드는 「검은 눈의 수전」도 멋지게 부를 수 있었고, 하이든의 칸초네타도, 「당신은 아시지요」도, 「때려요, 때려요」도 부를 수 있었으니ㅡ그저 청중이 무엇을 좋아하는지 알고 싶을 따름이었다."]], [["Her father looked round at the company, delighting in their admiration.", "그녀의 아버지는 좌중을 둘러보며 그들의 감탄에 흐뭇해했다."], ["Her mother sat, like a Niobe before her troubles, with her youngest little girl on her lap, softly beating the child’s hand up and down in time to the music.", "그녀의 어머니는 시련을 겪기 전의 니오베처럼 막내딸을 무릎에 앉히고서, 음악 박자에 맞춰 아이의 손을 부드럽게 위아래로 토닥이고 있었다."], ["And Fred, notwithstanding his general scepticism about Rosy, listened to her music with perfect allegiance, wishing he could do the same thing on his flute.", "그리고 프레드는, 로지에 대한 평소의 회의에도 불구하고 더없이 충성스럽게 그녀의 음악에 귀 기울이며, 자기도 플루트로 같은 것을 할 수 있다면 싶어 했다."], ["It was the pleasantest family party that Lydgate had seen since he came to Middlemarch.", "리드게이트가 미들마치에 온 이래로 본 가장 즐거운 가족 모임이었다."], ["The Vincys had the readiness to enjoy, the rejection of all anxiety, and the belief in life as a merry lot, which made a house exceptional in most county towns at that time, when Evangelicalism had cast a certain suspicion as of plague-infection over the few amusements which survived in the provinces.", "빈시가 사람들에게는 즐길 줄 아는 천성, 일체의 근심을 떨쳐 내는 태도, 그리고 인생을 즐거운 운명으로 믿는 믿음이 있었으니, 이것이 그 무렵 대부분의 주도에서 그 집을 별난 집으로 만들었다. 그 시절은 복음주의가, 지방에 살아남은 몇 안 되는 오락거리에 마치 역병이 옮은 듯한 어떤 의혹의 그림자를 드리운 때였다."], ["At the Vincys’ there was always whist, and the card-tables stood ready now, making some of the company secretly impatient of the music.", "빈시가에서는 늘 휘스트 놀이가 있었고, 지금도 카드 탁자가 준비되어 있어서 좌중의 몇몇은 속으로 음악이 어서 끝나기를 안달했다."], ["Before it ceased Mr. Farebrother came in—a handsome, broad-chested but otherwise small man, about forty, whose black was very threadbare: the brilliancy was all in his quick gray eyes.", "음악이 끝나기 전에 페어브러더 씨가 들어왔으니ㅡ잘생기고 가슴이 떡 벌어졌으되 그밖에는 자그마한, 마흔쯤 된 사내로, 검은 옷은 몹시 해져 있었다."], ["He came like a pleasant change in the light, arresting little Louisa with fatherly nonsense as she was being led out of the room by Miss Morgan, greeting everybody with some special word, and seeming to condense more talk into ten minutes than had been held all through the evening.", "그 광채라는 광채는 죄다 그 재빠른 잿빛 눈에 깃들어 있었다. 그는 빛 속의 유쾌한 변화처럼 들어와, 모건 양에게 이끌려 방을 나서던 어린 루이자를 아버지 같은 너스레로 붙잡고, 모두에게 저마다 특별한 말을 건네며 인사했으니, 마치 그날 저녁 내내 오간 것보다 더 많은 이야기를 십 분 안에 압축해 넣는 듯했다."], ["He claimed from Lydgate the fulfilment of a promise to come and see him.", "그는 리드게이트에게 자신을 만나러 오겠다던 약속을 지키라고 졸랐다."], ["“I can’t let you off, you know, because I have some beetles to show you.", "\"놓아줄 수 없습니다, 알다시피, 보여 드릴 딱정벌레가 좀 있거든요."], ["We collectors feel an interest in every new man till he has seen all we have to show him.”", "우리 수집가들은 자기가 보여 줄 것을 다 보여 줄 때까지는 새로 온 사람마다 흥미를 느낀답니다.\""]], [["But soon he swerved to the whist-table, rubbing his hands and saying, “Come now, let us be serious!", "그러나 그는 이내 휘스트 탁자로 발길을 돌려, 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"자, 이제 진지해집시다!"], ["Mr. Lydgate?", "리드게이트 씨?"], ["not play? Ah!", "안 하신다고요?"], ["you are too young and light for this kind of thing.”", "아! 당신은 이런 일을 하기엔 너무 젊고 가볍군요.\""]], [["Lydgate said to himself that the clergyman whose abilities were so painful to Mr. Bulstrode, appeared to have found an agreeable resort in this certainly not erudite household.", "리드게이트는 속으로, 불스트로드 씨에게 그토록 거슬리는 재능을 지닌 이 성직자가 학식이라고는 분명 없는 이 가정에서 마음에 드는 안식처를 찾은 모양이라고 생각했다."], ["He could half understand it: the good-humor, the good looks of elder and younger, and the provision for passing the time without any labor of intelligence, might make the house beguiling to people who had no particular use for their odd hours.", "그도 어느 정도는 이해할 수 있었다. 노소를 막론한 사람들의 좋은 기분과 빼어난 용모, 그리고 지성을 수고롭게 쓰지 않고도 시간을 보낼 수 있다는 점은, 남는 시간을 특별히 쓸 데 없는 이들에게 이 집을 매혹적인 곳으로 만들었으리라."]], [["Everything looked blooming and joyous except Miss Morgan, who was brown, dull, and resigned, and altogether, as Mrs.", "모건 양만 빼고는 모든 것이 활짝 피어나고 즐거워 보였다. 모건 양은 거무튀튀하고 따분하고 체념한 듯한 사람으로, 빈시 부인이 곧잘 말하듯 한마디로 가정교사에 딱 어울리는 부류였다."], ["Vincy often said, just the sort of person for a governess.", "리드게이트로서는 이런 방문을 자주 할 생각이 없었다."], ["Lydgate did not mean to pay many such visits himself.", "저녁 시간을 형편없이 낭비하는 일이었다."], ["They were a wretched waste of the evenings; and now, when he had talked a little more to Rosamond, he meant to excuse himself and go.", "그래서 그는 이제 로저먼드와 조금만 더 이야기를 나눈 다음, 양해를 구하고 자리를 뜰 작정이었다."]], [["“You will not like us at Middlemarch, I feel sure,”", "\"미들마치에 사는 우리가 마음에 안 드시리라는 걸, 저는 분명히 느껴요.\""], ["she said, when the whist-players were settled.", "휘스트 놀이꾼들이 자리를 잡고 앉자 그녀가 말했다."], ["“We are very stupid, and you have been used to something quite different.”", "\"우리는 무척 따분한데, 당신은 아주 다른 것에 익숙하셨을 테니까요.\""]], [["“I suppose all country towns are pretty much alike,”", "\"주도라는 게 다 거기서 거기겠지요.\" 리드게이트가 말했다."], ["said Lydgate. “But I have noticed that one always believes one’s own town to be more stupid than any other.", "\"하지만 사람은 누구나 자기가 사는 도시가 다른 어떤 곳보다 더 따분하다고 믿는다는 걸 저는 알아차렸습니다."], ["I have made up my mind to take Middlemarch as it comes, and shall be much obliged if the town will take me in the same way.", "저는 미들마치를 있는 그대로 받아들이기로 마음먹었으니, 이 도시도 저를 똑같은 식으로 받아들여 준다면 무척 고맙겠습니다."], ["I have certainly found some charms in it which are much greater than I had expected.”", "분명 저는 이곳에서 제가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 큰 매력을 몇 가지 발견했거든요.\""]], [["“You mean the rides towards Tipton and Lowick; every one is pleased with those,”", "\"팁턴과 로윅 쪽으로 나가는 승마길을 말씀하시는 거군요. 누구나 그걸 좋아하지요.\""], ["said Rosamond, with simplicity.", "로저먼드가 천연덕스럽게 말했다."]], [["“No, I mean something much nearer to me.”", "\"아니요, 제게 훨씬 더 가까운 무언가를 말하는 겁니다.\""]], [["Rosamond rose and reached her netting, and then said, “Do you care about dancing at all?", "로저먼드는 일어나 뜨개질감을 집어 들고는 말했다. \"춤에는 좀 관심이 있으세요?"], ["I am not quite sure whether clever men ever dance.”", "영리한 남자들이 춤을 추기나 하는지 잘 모르겠어서요.\""]], [["“I would dance with you if you would allow me.”", "\"허락만 해 주신다면 당신과 춤을 추겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"어머!\""], ["said Rosamond, with a slight deprecatory laugh.", "로저먼드가 살짝 사양하는 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“I was only going to say that we sometimes have dancing, and I wanted to know whether you would feel insulted if you were asked to come.”", "\"저는 그저 우리가 가끔 춤추는 모임을 연다는 말씀을 드리려던 거예요. 그러니까 와 달라는 청을 받으면 모욕으로 여기실지 어떨지 알고 싶었던 거고요.\""]], [["“Not on the condition I mentioned.”", "\"제가 말씀드린 조건이라면 그럴 리 없지요.\""]], [["After this chat Lydgate thought that he was going, but on moving towards the whist-tables, he got interested in watching Mr. Farebrother’s play, which was masterly, and also his face, which was a striking mixture of the shrewd and the mild.", "이런 대화를 나눈 뒤 리드게이트는 자리를 뜨려고 했으나, 휘스트 탁자 쪽으로 다가가다가 페어브러더 씨의 능란한 솜씨에, 그리고 빈틈없음과 온화함이 묘하게 어우러진 그의 얼굴에 흥미를 느껴 구경에 빠져들었다."], ["At ten o’clock supper was brought in (such were the customs of Middlemarch) and there was punch-drinking; but Mr. Farebrother had only a glass of water.", "열 시에 (미들마치의 풍습이 그러했다) 야식이 들어왔고 펀치 술을 돌렸으나, 페어브러더 씨는 물 한 잔만 들었다."], ["He was winning, but there seemed to be no reason why the renewal of rubbers should end, and Lydgate at last took his leave.", "그가 따고 있었지만 게임이 거듭되는 데 끝날 이유가 없어 보였으므로, 리드게이트는 마침내 작별을 고했다."]], [["But as it was not eleven o’clock, he chose to walk in the brisk air towards the tower of St. Botolph’s, Mr.", "하지만 아직 열한 시가 안 된 터라, 그는 상쾌한 공기를 가르며 페어브러더 씨의 교회인 성 보톨프 교회의 탑 쪽으로 걷기로 했다."], ["Farebrother’s church, which stood out dark, square, and massive against the starlight.", "그 탑은 별빛을 배경으로 어둑하고 네모지고 육중하게 솟아 있었다. 미들마치에서 가장 오래된 교회였다."], ["It was the oldest church in Middlemarch; the living, however, was but a vicarage worth barely four hundred a-year. Lydgate had heard that, and he wondered now whether Mr.", "그러나 그 성직록은 한 해에 사백 파운드도 채 안 되는 보좌신부직에 지나지 않았다."], ["Farebrother cared about the money he won at cards; thinking, “He seems a very pleasant fellow, but Bulstrode may have his good reasons.”", "리드게이트는 그 이야기를 들은 적이 있던지라, 이제 페어브러더 씨가 카드로 딴 돈을 마음에 두는 것은 아닐까 궁금해하며 생각했다. \"무척 유쾌한 친구 같긴 한데, 불스트로드에게도 그럴 만한 이유가 있을지 모르지.\""], ["Many things would be easier to Lydgate if it should turn out that Mr. Bulstrode was generally justifiable.", "불스트로드 씨가 대체로 정당하다는 것이 드러난다면 리드게이트에게는 많은 일이 한결 수월해질 터였다."], ["“What is his religious doctrine to me, if he carries some good notions along with it?", "\"그가 좋은 생각 몇 가지를 함께 지니고 있다면, 그의 종교적 교리 따위가 내게 무슨 상관이란 말인가?"], ["One must use such brains as are to be found.”", "사람은 구할 수 있는 만큼의 두뇌를 써야 하는 법이지.\""]], [["These were actually Lydgate’s first meditations as he walked away from Mr.", "이것이 실은 리드게이트가 빈시 씨 댁을 나와 걸으며 가장 먼저 한 사색이었으니, 이런 점 때문에 많은 부인들은 그가 자기네 관심을 받을 자격이 거의 없다고 여길까 두렵다."], ["Vincy’s, and on this ground I fear that many ladies will consider him hardly worthy of their attention.", "그는 로저먼드와 그녀의 음악을 두 번째로나 떠올렸다."], ["He thought of Rosamond and her music only in the second place; and though, when her turn came, he dwelt on the image of her for the rest of his walk, he felt no agitation, and had no sense that any new current had set into his life.", "그리고 그녀의 차례가 돌아왔을 때는 남은 길을 걷는 내내 그녀의 모습에 마음을 머물렀으나, 아무런 동요도 느끼지 않았고, 자기 삶에 어떤 새로운 물결이 들이쳤다는 느낌도 없었다. 그는 아직 결혼할 수 없었다."], ["He could not marry yet; he wished not to marry for several years; and therefore he was not ready to entertain the notion of being in love with a girl whom he happened to admire.", "몇 해 동안은 결혼할 마음이 없었다. 그러니 어쩌다 감탄하게 된 처녀와 사랑에 빠진다는 생각을 받아들일 준비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["He did admire Rosamond exceedingly; but that madness which had once beset him about Laure was not, he thought, likely to recur in relation to any other woman.", "그는 로저먼드에게 더없이 감탄했다. 하지만 한때 로르를 두고 그를 사로잡았던 그 광기가 다른 어떤 여자에게 되살아날 성싶지는 않다고 그는 생각했다."], ["Certainly, if falling in love had been at all in question, it would have been quite safe with a creature like this Miss Vincy, who had just the kind of intelligence one would desire in a woman—polished, refined, docile, lending itself to finish in all the delicacies of life, and enshrined in a body which expressed this with a force of demonstration that excluded the need for other evidence.", "분명, 사랑에 빠지는 일이 조금이라도 문제가 된다면, 이 빈시 양 같은 존재라면 더없이 안전할 터였다. 그녀에게는 남자가 여자에게 바랄 법한 바로 그런 종류의 지성ㅡ세련되고 정제되었으며 고분고분하고, 인생의 온갖 섬세한 일에 마무리를 더할 줄 알며, 그것을 더 이상의 증거가 필요 없을 만큼 분명하게 드러내는 몸에 깃든 지성이 있었기 때문이다."], ["Lydgate felt sure that if ever he married, his wife would have that feminine radiance, that distinctive womanhood which must be classed with flowers and music, that sort of beauty which by its very nature was virtuous, being moulded only for pure and delicate joys.", "리드게이트는 확신했다. 자기가 언젠가 결혼한다면 그의 아내는 그 여성다운 광채를, 꽃과 음악에 견주어야 할 그 독특한 여성성을, 본성상 순수하고 섬세한 기쁨만을 위해 빚어진 까닭에 그 자체로 미덕인 그런 종류의 아름다움을 지녔으리라고."]], [["But since he did not mean to marry for the next five years—his more pressing business was to look into Louis’ new book on Fever, which he was specially interested in, because he had known Louis in Paris, and had followed many anatomical demonstrations in order to ascertain the specific differences of typhus and typhoid.", "그러나 그는 앞으로 다섯 해 동안은 결혼할 생각이 없었으므로ㅡ그에게 더 시급한 일은 루이의 새로운 열병 책을 살펴보는 것이었다. 그는 그 책에 특별히 관심이 있었으니, 파리에서 루이를 알고 지냈고, 발진티푸스와 장티푸스의 구체적인 차이를 규명하기 위해 숱한 해부 시연을 따라다녔기 때문이다."], ["He went home and read far into the smallest hour, bringing a much more testing vision of details and relations into this pathological study than he had ever thought it necessary to apply to the complexities of love and marriage, these being subjects on which he felt himself amply informed by literature, and that traditional wisdom which is handed down in the genial conversation of men.", "그는 집으로 돌아가 한밤중이 깊도록 책을 읽으면서, 이 병리학 연구에는 사랑과 결혼의 복잡함에 적용해야 한다고 여겼던 것보다 훨씬 더 까다롭게 세부와 관계를 따져 보는 안목을 들이댔다. 사랑과 결혼이라는 주제에 관해서는, 문학과 남자들의 화기애애한 대화 속에 전해 내려오는 그 전통적 지혜로 이미 넉넉히 알고 있다고 그는 느꼈던 까닭이다."], ["Whereas Fever had obscure conditions, and gave him that delightful labor of the imagination which is not mere arbitrariness, but the exercise of disciplined power—combining and constructing with the clearest eye for probabilities and the fullest obedience to knowledge; and then, in yet more energetic alliance with impartial Nature, standing aloof to invent tests by which to try its own work.", "반면 열병에는 모호한 조건들이 있었고, 그것은 그에게 한낱 자의가 아니라 단련된 능력의 발휘인 그 즐거운 상상의 노동을 안겨 주었다ㅡ개연성에 대한 더없이 또렷한 안목과 지식에 대한 더없이 완전한 복종으로 결합하고 구성하는 일, 그리고 다시 공정한 자연과 한층 힘차게 동맹하여, 한 발 물러선 채 제 작업을 시험할 검증법을 고안해 내는 일이었다."]], [["Many men have been praised as vividly imaginative on the strength of their profuseness in indifferent drawing or cheap narration:—reports of very poor talk going on in distant orbs; or portraits of Lucifer coming down on his bad errands as a large ugly man with bat’s wings and spurts of phosphorescence; or exaggerations of wantonness that seem to reflect life in a diseased dream. But these kinds of inspiration Lydgate regarded as rather vulgar and vinous compared with the imagination that reveals subtle actions inaccessible by any sort of lens, but tracked in that outer darkness through long pathways of necessary sequence by the inward light which is the last refinement of Energy, capable of bathing even the ethereal atoms in its ideally illuminated space. He for his part had tossed away all cheap inventions where ignorance finds itself able and at ease: he was enamoured of that arduous invention which is the very eye of research, provisionally framing its object and correcting it to more and more exactness of relation; he wanted to pierce the obscurity of those minute processes which prepare human misery and joy, those invisible thoroughfares which are the first lurking-places of anguish, mania, and crime, that delicate poise and transition which determine the growth of happy or unhappy consciousness.", "많은 사람들이, 그저 그런 그림이나 값싼 서술을 잔뜩 늘어놓은 것만으로 생생한 상상력을 지녔다고 칭송받아 왔다. 이를테면 먼 천체에서 오가는 형편없는 잡담을 전한다거나, 박쥐 날개를 달고 인광을 뿜으며 못된 심부름을 하러 내려오는 크고 흉측한 사내로 루시퍼를 그려 낸다거나, 마치 병든 꿈속의 삶을 비추는 듯한 음탕함을 과장한다거나 하는 것들 말이다. 하지만 리드게이트는 이런 종류의 영감을, 어떤 렌즈로도 닿을 수 없으나 그 바깥의 어둠 속에서 필연적 인과의 긴 행로를 따라 추적되는 미묘한 작용들을 드러내는 상상력에 견주면 다소 천박하고 술기운에 젖은 것으로 여겼다. 그 상상력은 에너지의 마지막 정수인 내면의 빛으로 추적하는 것이었으니, 그 빛은 이상적으로 밝혀진 공간 속에서 영묘한 원자들까지도 적실 수 있는 것이었다. 그로 말하자면, 무지가 스스로 능하고 편안하다고 여기는 그 모든 값싼 발상을 내던져 버렸다. 그는 연구의 바로 그 눈인 저 고된 발상에 매혹되어 있었으니, 그것은 잠정적으로 대상의 틀을 잡고는 점점 더 정확한 관계로 바로잡아 가는 것이었다. 그는 인간의 비참과 기쁨을 빚어내는 저 미세한 과정들의 모호함을, 고통과 광기와 범죄가 처음 숨어드는 잠복처인 저 보이지 않는 통로들을, 행복하거나 불행한 의식의 성장을 결정짓는 저 섬세한 균형과 전이를 꿰뚫고 싶었다."]], [["As he threw down his book, stretched his legs towards the embers in the grate, and clasped his hands at the back of his head, in that agreeable afterglow of excitement when thought lapses from examination of a specific object into a suffusive sense of its connections with all the rest of our existence—seems, as it were, to throw itself on its back after vigorous swimming and float with the repose of unexhausted strength—Lydgate felt a triumphant delight in his studies, and something like pity for those less lucky men who were not of his profession.", "그는 책을 내려놓고 벽난로 쇠살대의 잉걸불 쪽으로 다리를 뻗은 채 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 끼었다. 사고가 어떤 특정한 대상에 대한 검토에서 벗어나 그것이 우리 존재의 나머지 전부와 맺는 관계에 대한 충만한 감각 속으로 풀려나는 그 흐뭇한 흥분의 여운 속에서ㅡ말하자면 힘차게 헤엄친 뒤 벌렁 드러누워, 아직 다 쓰지 않은 힘의 여유로움으로 둥둥 떠 있는 듯한 그 순간에ㅡ리드게이트는 자신의 연구에 의기양양한 기쁨을 느꼈고, 자기 직업에 속하지 못한 운 나쁜 사람들에게 일종의 연민 같은 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“If I had not taken that turn when I was a lad,”", "\"소년 시절에 그 길로 접어들지 않았더라면,\""], ["he thought, “I might have got into some stupid draught-horse work or other, and lived always in blinkers.", "하고 그는 생각했다. \"나는 무슨 멍청한 짐말 같은 일에나 빠져들어 늘 눈가리개를 한 채 살았을지도 몰라."], ["I should never have been happy in any profession that did not call forth the highest intellectual strain, and yet keep me in good warm contact with my neighbors.", "최고의 지적 긴장을 불러내면서도 이웃들과 따뜻하게 어울리게 해 주지 않는 직업에서는 결코 행복하지 못했을 거야."], ["There is nothing like the medical profession for that: one can have the exclusive scientific life that touches the distance and befriend the old fogies in the parish too.", "그 점에서는 의학만 한 게 없지. 머나먼 곳에 닿는 그 순수한 과학적 삶을 누리면서도 교구의 케케묵은 노인네들과 벗할 수 있으니까."], ["It is rather harder for a clergyman: Farebrother seems to be an anomaly.”", "성직자에게는 그게 좀 더 어렵지. 페어브러더는 별종인 모양이야.\""]], [["This last thought brought back the Vincys and all the pictures of the evening.", "이 마지막 생각은 빈시가 사람들과 그날 저녁의 온갖 장면을 다시 떠올리게 했다."], ["They floated in his mind agreeably enough, and as he took up his bed-candle his lips were curled with that incipient smile which is apt to accompany agreeable recollections.", "그것들은 그의 마음속을 제법 흐뭇하게 떠다녔고, 그가 잠자리 촛대를 집어 들 때 그의 입술은 흐뭇한 회상에 으레 따르기 마련인 그 막 피어나려는 미소로 살짝 굽어 있었다."], ["He was an ardent fellow, but at present his ardor was absorbed in love of his work and in the ambition of making his life recognized as a factor in the better life of mankind—like other heroes of science who had nothing but an obscure country practice to begin with.", "그는 열정적인 사내였으나, 지금 그의 열정은 자기 일에 대한 사랑과, 변변찮은 시골 개업으로 시작할 수밖에 없었던 다른 과학의 영웅들처럼, 자기 삶을 인류의 더 나은 삶을 이루는 한 요소로 인정받게 하려는 야망에 온통 빨려들어 있었다."]], [["Poor Lydgate!", "가엾은 리드게이트!"], ["or shall I say, Poor Rosamond!", "아니, 가엾은 로저먼드라고 해야 할까?"], ["Each lived in a world of which the other knew nothing.", "두 사람은 저마다 상대가 전혀 알지 못하는 세계 속에 살고 있었다. 리드게이트는 자신이 로저먼드의 열렬한 사색의 대상이 되어 왔다는 것을 떠올린 적이 없었다."], ["It had not occurred to Lydgate that he had been a subject of eager meditation to Rosamond, who had neither any reason for throwing her marriage into distant perspective, nor any pathological studies to divert her mind from that ruminating habit, that inward repetition of looks, words, and phrases, which makes a large part in the lives of most girls.", "그녀로 말하자면 자신의 결혼을 먼 훗날로 미뤄 둘 까닭도 없었고, 대다수 처녀들의 삶에서 큰 몫을 차지하는 그 곱씹는 버릇ㅡ눈길과 말과 문구를 마음속으로 거듭 되뇌는 일에서 마음을 돌려놓을 병리학 연구 따위도 없었다. 그는 아름다운 처녀에게 남자가 으레 바쳐야 할 어쩔 수 없는 만큼의 감탄과 찬사 이상으로 그녀를 바라보거나 그녀에게 말을 건넬 생각이 없었다."], ["He had not meant to look at her or speak to her with more than the inevitable amount of admiration and compliment which a man must give to a beautiful girl; indeed, it seemed to him that his enjoyment of her music had remained almost silent, for he feared falling into the rudeness of telling her his great surprise at her possession of such accomplishment.", "사실 그가 보기에는, 그녀의 음악을 즐긴 일조차 거의 입 밖에 내지 못한 채로 남아 있었다. 그녀가 그런 재능을 지닌 데 대한 자신의 큰 놀라움을 그녀에게 털어놓는 무례를 범할까 두려웠던 까닭이다."], ["But Rosamond had registered every look and word, and estimated them as the opening incidents of a preconceived romance—incidents which gather value from the foreseen development and climax.", "하지만 로저먼드는 모든 눈길과 말을 빠짐없이 기록해 두고는, 그것들을 미리 그려 둔 어떤 연애담의 첫 장면들로ㅡ앞으로 펼쳐질 전개와 절정에서 가치를 얻게 될 장면들로 가늠했다. 로저먼드의 연애담에서는 주인공의 내면생활이나 그가 세상에서 하는 진지한 일에 관해 굳이 많이 상상할 필요가 없었다."], ["In Rosamond’s romance it was not necessary to imagine much about the inward life of the hero, or of his serious business in the world: of course, he had a profession and was clever, as well as sufficiently handsome; but the piquant fact about Lydgate was his good birth, which distinguished him from all Middlemarch admirers, and presented marriage as a prospect of rising in rank and getting a little nearer to that celestial condition on earth in which she would have nothing to do with vulgar people, and perhaps at last associate with relatives quite equal to the county people who looked down on the Middlemarchers.", "물론 그에게는 직업이 있었고 영리했으며 충분히 잘생기기도 했다. 하지만 리드게이트에 관한 짜릿한 사실은 그의 좋은 가문이었으니, 그것이 그를 미들마치의 모든 숭배자들과 구별 짓고, 결혼을 신분 상승의 전망으로ㅡ그녀가 천박한 사람들과는 아무 상관도 없게 될, 그리고 어쩌면 마침내 미들마치 사람들을 깔보는 그 군 유지들과 어깨를 나란히 할 만한 친척들과 어울리게 될, 지상에서의 그 천상 같은 경지에 조금이나마 더 가까이 다가서는 일로 제시했기 때문이다."], ["It was part of Rosamond’s cleverness to discern very subtly the faintest aroma of rank, and once when she had seen the Miss Brookes accompanying their uncle at the county assizes, and seated among the aristocracy, she had envied them, notwithstanding their plain dress.", "신분의 가장 희미한 향기까지도 무척 미묘하게 분별해 내는 것이 로저먼드의 영민함의 한 부분이었으니, 언젠가 브룩가 자매가 삼촌을 따라 순회 재판에 와서 귀족들 틈에 앉아 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀는 그들의 수수한 옷차림에도 불구하고 그들을 부러워했다."]], [["If you think it incredible that to imagine Lydgate as a man of family could cause thrills of satisfaction which had anything to do with the sense that she was in love with him, I will ask you to use your power of comparison a little more effectively, and consider whether red cloth and epaulets have never had an influence of that sort.", "리드게이트를 가문 좋은 사내로 상상하는 일이 그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 느낌과 조금이라도 관련된 만족의 전율을 불러일으킬 수 있다는 것이 믿기지 않는다면, 나는 당신에게 비교의 능력을 좀 더 효과적으로 발휘해 보기를, 그리하여 붉은 군복과 견장이 그런 종류의 영향을 끼친 적이 정녕 없었는지 헤아려 보기를 청하겠다."], ["Our passions do not live apart in locked chambers, but, dressed in their small wardrobe of notions, bring their provisions to a common table and mess together, feeding out of the common store according to their appetite.", "우리의 열정이란 잠긴 방 안에 따로따로 사는 것이 아니라, 저마다 관념이라는 자그마한 옷장의 옷을 차려입고 공동의 식탁으로 양식을 가져와 함께 어울려 식사하며, 제 식욕에 따라 공동의 곳간에서 양분을 먹는 법이다."]], [["Rosamond, in fact, was entirely occupied not exactly with Tertius Lydgate as he was in himself, but with his relation to her; and it was excusable in a girl who was accustomed to hear that all young men might, could, would be, or actually were in love with her, to believe at once that Lydgate could be no exception.", "사실 로저먼드는 테르티우스 리드게이트라는 사람 그 자체보다는, 정확히 말해 그와 자기 사이의 관계에 온통 마음을 빼앗기고 있었다. 모든 젊은 남자들이 자기에게 반할 수 있고, 반할 만하고, 반할 것이며, 실제로 반해 있다는 말을 늘 들으며 자란 처녀가 리드게이트도 예외일 수 없다고 대뜸 믿어 버린 것은 너그러이 봐줄 만한 일이었다."], ["His looks and words meant more to her than other men’s, because she cared more for them: she thought of them diligently, and diligently attended to that perfection of appearance, behavior, sentiments, and all other elegancies, which would find in Lydgate a more adequate admirer than she had yet been conscious of.", "그의 눈길과 말은 그녀에게 다른 남자들의 것보다 더 큰 의미가 있었으니, 그녀가 그것들을 더 마음에 두었기 때문이다. 그녀는 부지런히 그것들을 떠올렸고, 자신이 여태껏 의식했던 것보다 더 합당한 찬미자를 리드게이트에게서 찾아낼 그 완벽한 용모와 거동과 감정과 온갖 우아함을 부지런히 가다듬었다."]], [["For Rosamond, though she would never do anything that was disagreeable to her, was industrious; and now more than ever she was active in sketching her landscapes and market-carts and portraits of friends, in practising her music, and in being from morning till night her own standard of a perfect lady, having always an audience in her own consciousness, with sometimes the not unwelcome addition of a more variable external audience in the numerous visitors of the house.", "로저먼드로 말하자면, 자기에게 거슬리는 일은 결코 하지 않으려 했으되 부지런한 처녀였다. 그리고 이제 그 어느 때보다도 그녀는 풍경화와 장터 수레와 친구들의 초상을 그리고, 음악을 연습하고, 아침부터 밤까지 완벽한 숙녀라는 자신의 본보기가 되기에 열심이었다."], ["She found time also to read the best novels, and even the second best, and she knew much poetry by heart.", "그녀에게는 늘 자신의 의식이라는 청중이 있었고, 거기에 더러는 그 집을 드나드는 숱한 손님들이라는, 한결 변덕스러우나 결코 달갑지 않지는 않은 외부의 청중까지 더해졌다. 그녀는 또한 가장 좋은 소설, 심지어 둘째로 좋은 소설까지 읽을 짬도 냈고, 시도 많이 외우고 있었다."], ["Her favorite poem was “Lalla Rookh.”", "그녀가 가장 좋아하는 시는 「랄라 루크」였다."]], [["“The best girl in the world!", "\"세상에서 가장 좋은 처녀라니까!"], ["He will be a happy fellow who gets her!”", "저 애를 얻는 사내는 행복한 친구가 될 거야!\""], ["was the sentiment of the elderly gentlemen who visited the Vincys; and the rejected young men thought of trying again, as is the fashion in country towns where the horizon is not thick with coming rivals.", "이것이 빈시가를 드나드는 노신사들의 마음이었다. 그리고 퇴짜 맞은 젊은이들은 다시 한번 도전해 볼까 생각했으니, 다가올 경쟁자들로 지평선이 빽빽하지 않은 주도에서는 으레 그러는 법이었다."], ["But Mrs. Plymdale thought that Rosamond had been educated to a ridiculous pitch, for what was the use of accomplishments which would be all laid aside as soon as she was married?", "하지만 플림데일 부인은 로저먼드가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 과한 교육을 받았다고 여겼다."], ["While her aunt Bulstrode, who had a sisterly faithfulness towards her brother’s family, had two sincere wishes for Rosamond—that she might show a more serious turn of mind, and that she might meet with a husband whose wealth corresponded to her habits.", "결혼하고 나면 죄다 내팽개치게 될 재주가 무슨 소용이란 말인가? 한편 오라비의 가족에게 누이로서의 정성을 다하던 이모 불스트로드 부인은 로저먼드에 대해 두 가지 진심 어린 소망을 품고 있었으니ㅡ그 애가 좀 더 진지한 마음가짐을 보여 주는 것, 그리고 그 애의 습성에 걸맞은 재산을 지닌 남편을 만나는 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVII.", "# CHAPTER XVII."]], [["“The clerkly person smiled and said Promise was a pretty maid, But being poor she died unwed.” The Rev.", "친애하는 카멘 페어브라더 목사를 리드게이트는 이튿날 저녁에 찾아갔는데, 그는 돌로 지은 오래된 목사관에 살고 있었으니, 그 건물은 마주 보이는 교회와 어울릴 만큼 위엄 있게 낡아 있었다."], ["Camden Farebrother, whom Lydgate went to see the next evening, lived in an old parsonage, built of stone, venerable enough to match the church which it looked out upon.", "집 안의 가구들도 모두 오래된 것이었지만, 그 낡음의 결은 또 달랐다."], ["All the furniture too in the house was old, but with another grade of age—that of Mr.", "페어브라더 씨의 부친과 조부 대의 것이었던 것이다."], ["Farebrother’s father and grandfather. There were painted white chairs, with gilding and wreaths on them, and some lingering red silk damask with slits in it.", "도금과 화환 무늬가 새겨진, 하얗게 칠한 의자들이 있었고, 군데군데 째진 붉은 비단 다마스크 천이 아직 남아 있었다."], ["There were engraved portraits of Lord Chancellors and other celebrated lawyers of the last century; and there were old pier-glasses to reflect them, as well as the little satin-wood tables and the sofas resembling a prolongation of uneasy chairs, all standing in relief against the dark wainscot.", "지난 세기의 대법관들과 그 밖에 유명한 법률가들의 동판 초상화가 걸려 있었고, 그것들을 비추는 오래된 벽거울들도 있었으며, 새틴우드로 만든 작은 탁자들과 불편한 의자를 길게 늘인 듯한 소파도 있었으니, 모두 짙은 빛깔의 벽널을 배경으로 도드라져 보였다. 이것이 리드게이트가 안내받아 들어선 응접실의 면모였다."], ["This was the physiognomy of the drawing-room into which Lydgate was shown; and there were three ladies to receive him, who were also old-fashioned, and of a faded but genuine respectability: Mrs.", "그를 맞이한 세 부인 역시 구식이었고, 빛바랬으되 진정한 품위를 지니고 있었다. 페어브라더 부인은 목사의 백발 모친으로, 정갈하고 깔끔하게 주름 장식과 머릿수건을 두른 채 꼿꼿한 자세에 눈빛이 또렷했으며 아직 칠십이 되지 않았다."], ["Farebrother, the Vicar’s white-haired mother, befrilled and kerchiefed with dainty cleanliness, upright, quick-eyed, and still under seventy; Miss Noble, her sister, a tiny old lady of meeker aspect, with frills and kerchief decidedly more worn and mended; and Miss Winifred Farebrother, the Vicar’s elder sister, well-looking like himself, but nipped and subdued as single women are apt to be who spend their lives in uninterrupted subjection to their elders.", "그녀의 여동생인 노블 양은 한결 온순해 보이는 자그마한 노부인으로, 주름 장식과 머릿수건은 확실히 더 닳고 기운 것이었다. 그리고 목사의 누이인 위니프레드 페어브라더 양은 동생과 마찬가지로 인상이 좋았으나, 평생 윗사람들에게 끊임없이 순종하며 살아온 독신 여성들이 흔히 그렇듯 위축되고 기가 꺾여 있었다."], ["Lydgate had not expected to see so quaint a group: knowing simply that Mr.", "리드게이트는 이토록 예스러운 무리를 만나리라고는 예상하지 못했다."], ["Farebrother was a bachelor, he had thought of being ushered into a snuggery where the chief furniture would probably be books and collections of natural objects.", "그저 페어브라더 씨가 독신이라는 것만 알고 있었던 터라, 그는 십중팔구 주된 가구라고는 책과 자연물 수집품일 아늑한 서재로 안내되리라 생각했던 것이다."], ["The Vicar himself seemed to wear rather a changed aspect, as most men do when acquaintances made elsewhere see them for the first time in their own homes; some indeed showing like an actor of genial parts disadvantageously cast for the curmudgeon in a new piece.", "목사 자신도 오히려 다소 달라진 모습으로 보였으니, 다른 데서 사귄 지인이 자기 집에서 그를 처음 보는 경우 대부분의 남자가 그러하듯이 말이다. 사실 어떤 이들은 새 작품에서 심술쟁이 역할을 어울리지 않게 맡은, 본디 호인 역의 배우처럼 보이기도 한다."], ["This was not the case with Mr.", "페어브라더 씨는 그런 경우는 아니었다."], ["Farebrother: he seemed a trifle milder and more silent, the chief talker being his mother, while he only put in a good-humored moderating remark here and there.", "그는 조금 더 부드럽고 더 말이 없어 보였으며, 주로 이야기하는 사람은 그의 모친이었고, 그는 이따금 좋은 기분으로 누그러뜨리는 한마디를 끼워 넣을 뿐이었다."], ["The old lady was evidently accustomed to tell her company what they ought to think, and to regard no subject as quite safe without her steering.", "노부인은 함께한 사람들에게 마땅히 무엇을 생각해야 하는지 일러 주는 데 익숙한 것이 분명했고, 자신의 키질 없이는 어떤 화제도 영 안전하지 않다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["She was afforded leisure for this function by having all her little wants attended to by Miss Winifred.", "그녀가 이런 소임을 다할 여유를 누릴 수 있었던 것은 자질구레한 시중을 모두 위니프레드 양이 들어 주었기 때문이다."], ["Meanwhile tiny Miss Noble carried on her arm a small basket, into which she diverted a bit of sugar, which she had first dropped in her saucer as if by mistake; looking round furtively afterwards, and reverting to her teacup with a small innocent noise as of a tiny timid quadruped.", "그러는 동안 자그마한 노블 양은 팔에 작은 바구니를 걸고 있었는데, 마치 실수인 양 먼저 받침 접시에 떨어뜨린 설탕 조각을 그 안으로 슬쩍 옮겨 넣고는, 그 뒤에 은밀히 주위를 둘러보고 나서 자그맣고 겁 많은 네발짐승처럼 작고 천진한 소리를 내며 다시 찻잔으로 돌아왔다."], ["Pray think no ill of Miss Noble.", "부디 노블 양을 나쁘게 여기지 마시기를."], ["That basket held small savings from her more portable food, destined for the children of her poor friends among whom she trotted on fine mornings; fostering and petting all needy creatures being so spontaneous a delight to her, that she regarded it much as if it had been a pleasant vice that she was addicted to.", "그 바구니에는 그녀가 비교적 옮기기 쉬운 음식에서 아껴 둔 것들이 담겨 있었으니, 화창한 아침이면 종종걸음으로 찾아가는 가난한 친구들의 아이들에게 줄 것이었다. 궁핍한 모든 것을 보살피고 어루만지는 일이 그녀에게는 너무도 자연스러운 기쁨이어서, 그녀는 그것을 마치 자기가 빠져 있는 어떤 즐거운 악덕쯤으로 여겼다."], ["Perhaps she was conscious of being tempted to steal from those who had much that she might give to those who had nothing, and carried in her conscience the guilt of that repressed desire.", "어쩌면 그녀는 아무것도 가진 게 없는 이들에게 줄 것을 많이 가진 이들에게서 훔치고 싶은 유혹에 빠져 있음을 의식하고 있었는지도 모르고, 그 억눌린 욕망의 죄책감을 양심 속에 짊어지고 있었는지도 모른다."], ["One must be poor to know the luxury of giving!", "베푸는 사치를 알려면 가난해야 하는 법이다!"]], [["Mrs. Farebrother welcomed the guest with a lively formality and precision.", "페어브라더 부인은 활기차면서도 격식 있고 또박또박한 태도로 손님을 맞았다."], ["She presently informed him that they were not often in want of medical aid in that house.", "그녀는 곧 이 집에서는 의술의 도움이 필요한 일이 흔치 않다고 그에게 알렸다."], ["She had brought up her children to wear flannel and not to over-eat themselves, which last habit she considered the chief reason why people needed doctors.", "그녀는 자식들을 플란넬을 입히고 과식하지 않도록 키웠는데, 그 마지막 습관이야말로 사람들이 의사를 필요로 하는 주된 이유라고 여겼다."], ["Lydgate pleaded for those whose fathers and mothers had over-eaten themselves, but Mrs.", "리드게이트가 부모가 과식한 탓에 고생하는 이들을 두고 변호하자, 페어브라더 부인은 그런 관점은 위험하다고 했다."], ["Farebrother held that view of things dangerous: Nature was more just than that; it would be easy for any felon to say that his ancestors ought to have been hanged instead of him.", "자연은 그보다 더 공정하다는 것이었다. 어떤 흉악범이든 자기 대신 조상이 교수형을 당했어야 한다고 말하기는 쉬운 일이 아니냐고 했다."], ["If those who had bad fathers and mothers were bad themselves, they were hanged for that.", "못된 부모를 둔 이들이 스스로도 못됐다면, 그것은 그 못됨 때문에 교수형을 당하는 것이라고 했다."], ["There was no need to go back on what you couldn’t see.", "눈에 보이지도 않는 것까지 거슬러 올라갈 필요는 없다는 것이었다."]], [["“My mother is like old George the Third,”", "\"우리 어머니는 늙은 조지 3세 같으시지요.\""], ["said the Vicar, “she objects to metaphysics.”", "목사가 말했다. \"형이상학을 반대하시거든요.\""]], [["“I object to what is wrong, Camden.", "\"나는 그른 것을 반대하는 거다, 카멘."], ["I say, keep hold of a few plain truths, and make everything square with them.", "내 말은, 몇 가지 명백한 진리를 붙들고서 모든 것을 거기에 맞춰 가라는 거야."], ["When I was young, Mr. Lydgate, there never was any question about right and wrong.", "내가 젊었을 적엔, 리드게이트 선생, 옳고 그름에 대해 의문을 품는 일이라곤 없었어요."], ["We knew our catechism, and that was enough; we learned our creed and our duty.", "우리는 교리문답을 알았고, 그것으로 충분했지요. 우리는 신조와 의무를 배웠어요."], ["Every respectable Church person had the same opinions.", "점잖은 교회 신자라면 누구나 똑같은 의견을 가졌고요."], ["But now, if you speak out of the Prayer-book itself, you are liable to be contradicted.”", "그런데 지금은 기도서 그 자체에서 인용해 말해도 반박당하기 십상이에요.\""]], [["“That makes rather a pleasant time of it for those who like to maintain their own point,”", "\"그렇다면 자기 견해를 지키기 좋아하는 사람들에게는 오히려 즐거운 시절이겠군요.\""], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."]], [["“But my mother always gives way,”", "\"하지만 우리 어머니는 늘 양보하시지요.\""], ["said the Vicar, slyly.", "목사가 짓궂게 말했다."]], [["“No, no, Camden, you must not lead Mr. Lydgate into a mistake about me.", "\"아니, 아니다, 카멘, 리드게이트 선생이 나에 대해 오해하게 만들면 못 써."], ["I shall never show that disrespect to my parents, to give up what they taught me.", "나는 부모님이 가르쳐 주신 것을 저버리는 그런 불경을 절대 저지르지 않을 게다."], ["Any one may see what comes of turning.", "마음을 바꾸면 어찌 되는지는 누구나 알 수 있는 일이야."], ["If you change once, why not twenty times?”", "한 번 바꾸면, 어찌 스무 번인들 못 바꾸겠니?\""]], [["“A man might see good arguments for changing once, and not see them for changing again,”", "\"한 번 바꾸는 데는 좋은 논거를 찾으면서도, 다시 바꾸는 데는 그 논거를 찾지 못할 수도 있지요.\""], ["said Lydgate, amused with the decisive old lady.", "리드게이트가 단호한 노부인에게 흥미를 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“Excuse me there.", "\"그 점은 실례지만 동의할 수 없어요."], ["If you go upon arguments, they are never wanting, when a man has no constancy of mind.", "논거로만 따진다면, 마음에 한결같음이 없는 사람에게는 논거란 결코 모자랄 일이 없거든."], ["My father never changed, and he preached plain moral sermons without arguments, and was a good man—few better.", "우리 아버지는 절대 바꾸지 않으셨고, 논거 없이 평이한 도덕적 설교를 하셨으며, 훌륭한 분이셨어요."], ["When you get me a good man made out of arguments, I will get you a good dinner with reading you the cookery-book.", "그보다 나은 사람이 드물 정도로요. 논거로 빚어낸 훌륭한 사람을 데려오신다면, 나는 요리책을 읽어 드리고서 좋은 만찬을 차려 드리지요."], ["That’s my opinion, and I think anybody’s stomach will bear me out.”", "그게 내 의견이고, 누구의 위장이든 내 말이 옳다고 입증해 줄 거예요.\""]], [["“About the dinner certainly, mother,”", "\"만찬에 관한 한은 분명 그렇겠지요, 어머니.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother.", "페어브라더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It is the same thing, the dinner or the man.", "\"만찬이든 사람이든 매한가지야."], ["I am nearly seventy, Mr. Lydgate, and I go upon experience.", "나는 칠십이 다 됐어요, 리드게이트 선생, 그래서 경험으로 판단하지요."], ["I am not likely to follow new lights, though there are plenty of them here as elsewhere.", "여기에도 다른 데처럼 새로운 깨달음을 외치는 자들이 수두룩하지만, 나는 그런 걸 따를 사람이 아니에요."], ["I say, they came in with the mixed stuffs that will neither wash nor wear.", "내 말로는, 그런 것들은 빨아도 못 견디고 입어도 못 견디는 혼방 천과 함께 들어온 거예요. 내 젊을 적엔 그렇지 않았어요."], ["It was not so in my youth: a Churchman was a Churchman, and a clergyman, you might be pretty sure, was a gentleman, if nothing else.", "교회 신자는 교회 신자였고, 성직자라면 다른 건 몰라도 신사임은 꽤 확신할 수 있었지요. 그런데 지금은 비국교도보다 나을 것도 없는 자가 교리를 빙자해 내 아들을 밀어내려 들어요."], ["But now he may be no better than a Dissenter, and want to push aside my son on pretence of doctrine.", "하지만 누가 그 애를 밀어내려 하든, 자랑스럽게 말하건대, 리드게이트 선생, 내 아들은 이 왕국의 어떤 설교자와 견주어도 손색이 없어요."], ["But whoever may wish to push him aside, I am proud to say, Mr.", "이 도시 안에서야 말할 것도 없지만, 이 도시는 견줄 기준으로는 낮은 편이지요."], ["Lydgate, that he will compare with any preacher in this kingdom, not to speak of this town, which is but a low standard to go by; at least, to my thinking, for I was born and bred at Exeter.”", "적어도 내 생각엔 그래요. 나는 엑서터에서 나고 자랐으니까요.\""]], [["“A mother is never partial,” said Mr.", "\"어머니란 결코 공평하지 않은 법이지요.\""], ["Farebrother, smiling.", "페어브라더 씨가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“What do you think Tyke’s mother says about him?”", "\"타이크의 어머니는 그분에 대해 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\""]], [["“Ah, poor creature!", "\"아, 가엾은 사람!"], ["what indeed?” said Mrs.", "정말이지 뭐라고 하겠니?\""], ["Farebrother, her sharpness blunted for the moment by her confidence in maternal judgments.", "페어브라더 부인이 말했는데, 모성의 판단에 대한 그녀의 믿음 때문에 그 날카로움이 잠시 무뎌진 것이었다."], ["“She says the truth to herself, depend upon it.”", "\"분명 마음속으론 진실을 말할 게다.\""]], [["“And what is the truth?”", "\"그런데 그 진실이란 게 뭡니까?\""], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“I am curious to know.”", "\"궁금하군요.\""]], [["“Oh, nothing bad at all,”", "\"오, 나쁜 거라곤 전혀 없어요.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother.", "페어브라더 씨가 말했다."], ["“He is a zealous fellow: not very learned, and not very wise, I think—because I don’t agree with him.”", "\"그는 열성적인 사람이지요. 그리 박식하지도, 그리 지혜롭지도 않다고 나는 생각하는데, 그건 내가 그와 의견이 맞지 않기 때문일 거고요.\""]], [["“Why, Camden!”", "\"어머나, 카멘!\""], ["said Miss Winifred, “Griffin and his wife told me only to-day, that Mr.", "위니프레드 양이 말했다."], ["Tyke said they should have no more coals if they came to hear you preach.”", "\"오늘에야 그리핀과 그 아내가 나한테 말해 줬는데, 타이크 씨가 그러더래요, 그 부부가 오빠 설교를 들으러 가면 석탄을 더는 안 주겠다고요.\""]], [["Mrs. Farebrother laid down her knitting, which she had resumed after her small allowance of tea and toast, and looked at her son as if to say “You hear that?”", "페어브라더 부인은 차와 토스트를 조금 든 뒤에 다시 시작했던 뜨개질을 내려놓고, \"들었지?\"라고 말하려는 듯 아들을 바라보았다."], ["Miss Noble said, “Oh poor things!", "노블 양은 \"오, 가엾어라!"], ["poor things!”", "가엾기도 하지!\""], ["in reference, probably, to the double loss of preaching and coal.", "하고 말했는데, 아마도 설교와 석탄을 둘 다 잃는 것을 두고 한 말이었으리라."], ["But the Vicar answered quietly—", "그러나 목사는 차분하게 대답했다."]], [["“That is because they are not my parishioners.", "\"그건 그 사람들이 내 교구민이 아니어서 그런 거예요."], ["And I don’t think my sermons are worth a load of coals to them.”", "그리고 내 설교가 그 사람들한테 석탄 한 짐만 한 값어치가 있다고도 생각지 않고요.\""]], [["“Mr. Lydgate,”", "\"리드게이트 선생,\""], ["said Mrs.", "이 말을 그냥 넘길 수 없었던 페어브라더 부인이 말했다."], ["Farebrother, who could not let this pass, “you don’t know my son: he always undervalues himself.", "\"당신은 내 아들을 몰라요. 저 애는 늘 자기를 낮춘다니까요."], ["I tell him he is undervaluing the God who made him, and made him a most excellent preacher.”", "나는 저 애한테 그러지요, 너를 만드시고 더없이 훌륭한 설교자로 만드신 하느님을 낮추는 거라고요.\""]], [["“That must be a hint for me to take Mr. Lydgate away to my study, mother,”", "\"그건 제가 리드게이트 선생을 모시고 서재로 가라는 암시로군요, 어머니.\""], ["said the Vicar, laughing.", "목사가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I promised to show you my collection,”", "\"수집품을 보여 드리겠다고 약속했지요.\""], ["he added, turning to Lydgate; “shall we go?”", "그가 리드게이트를 향해 돌아서며 덧붙였다. \"가 볼까요?\""]], [["All three ladies remonstrated. Mr.", "세 부인 모두 만류했다."], ["Lydgate ought not to be hurried away without being allowed to accept another cup of tea: Miss Winifred had abundance of good tea in the pot.", "리드게이트 씨가 차 한 잔 더 들 겨를도 없이 그렇게 서둘러 끌려가서는 안 된다는 것이었다. 위니프레드 양의 주전자에는 좋은 차가 넉넉히 있었다."], ["Why was Camden in such haste to take a visitor to his den?", "카멘은 무슨 까닭으로 손님을 자기 굴로 데려가지 못해 안달인가?"], ["There was nothing but pickled vermin, and drawers full of blue-bottles and moths, with no carpet on the floor.", "그곳에는 절여 둔 벌레들이며 청파리와 나방으로 가득 찬 서랍들밖에 없고, 바닥엔 양탄자도 깔려 있지 않았다."], ["Mr. Lydgate must excuse it.", "리드게이트 씨가 양해해 줘야 할 형편이었다."], ["A game at cribbage would be far better.", "크리비지 한 판이 훨씬 나을 것이었다."], ["In short, it was plain that a vicar might be adored by his womankind as the king of men and preachers, and yet be held by them to stand in much need of their direction.", "요컨대, 목사가 자기 집 여인들로부터 남자 중의 남자, 설교자 중의 으뜸으로 추앙받으면서도, 동시에 그들의 지도가 몹시 필요한 사람으로 여겨질 수 있다는 것이 분명했다."], ["Lydgate, with the usual shallowness of a young bachelor, wondered that Mr. Farebrother had not taught them better.", "리드게이트는 젊은 독신 남자 특유의 천박한 안목으로, 페어브라더 씨가 어찌 그들을 더 잘 가르쳐 두지 못했을까 의아해했다."]], [["“My mother is not used to my having visitors who can take any interest in my hobbies,”", "\"우리 어머니는 내 취미에 조금이라도 관심을 가질 만한 손님을 맞는 데 익숙하지 않으시지요.\""], ["said the Vicar, as he opened the door of his study, which was indeed as bare of luxuries for the body as the ladies had implied, unless a short porcelain pipe and a tobacco-box were to be excepted.", "목사가 서재 문을 열며 말했는데, 그곳은 과연 부인들이 넌지시 일러 준 대로 짧은 자기 파이프와 담배통을 빼면 몸을 위한 사치라곤 찾아볼 수 없을 만큼 휑했다."]], [["“Men of your profession don’t generally smoke,”", "\"선생 같은 직업을 가진 분들은 보통 담배를 안 피우지요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["Lydgate smiled and shook his head.", "리드게이트는 미소를 지으며 고개를 가로저었다."], ["“Nor of mine either, properly, I suppose.", "\"엄밀히 말하면 내 직업도 그렇겠지요."], ["You will hear that pipe alleged against me by Bulstrode and Company.", "벌스트로드 일당이 이 파이프를 두고 나를 헐뜯는 소리를 듣게 될 거예요."], ["They don’t know how pleased the devil would be if I gave it up.”", "그들은 내가 이걸 끊으면 악마가 얼마나 좋아할지 모르거든요.\""]], [["“I understand.", "\"이해합니다."], ["You are of an excitable temper and want a sedative.", "선생은 흥분하기 쉬운 기질이라 진정제가 필요한 거지요."], ["I am heavier, and should get idle with it.", "저는 더 둔중한 편이어서, 그걸 하면 게을러질 겁니다."], ["I should rush into idleness, and stagnate there with all my might.”", "나태함 속으로 곤두박질쳐서, 거기 고인 채 온 힘을 다해 정체해 버리겠지요.\""]], [["“And you mean to give it all to your work.", "\"그래서 선생은 그 모든 걸 일에 쏟으려는 거군요."], ["I am some ten or twelve years older than you, and have come to a compromise.", "나는 선생보다 한 열두어 살 위인데, 타협하기에 이르렀어요."], ["I feed a weakness or two lest they should get clamorous.", "한두 가지 약점에 먹이를 주지요, 안 그러면 그것들이 아우성을 칠 테니까요."], ["See,”", "보세요.\""], ["continued the Vicar, opening several small drawers, “I fancy I have made an exhaustive study of the entomology of this district.", "목사가 작은 서랍 여럿을 열며 말을 이었다. \"내가 이 지역의 곤충학을 철저히 연구했다고 자부합니다."], ["I am going on both with the fauna and flora; but I have at least done my insects well.", "동물상과 식물상도 함께 다루고 있긴 하지만, 적어도 곤충만큼은 제대로 해 두었어요."], ["We are singularly rich in orthoptera: I don’t know whether—Ah!", "우리 지역은 직시류가 유난히 풍부하지요. 글쎄, 혹시 — 아!"], ["you have got hold of that glass jar—you are looking into that instead of my drawers.", "그 유리병을 집어 드셨군요. 내 서랍 대신 그걸 들여다보고 계시는군."], ["You don’t really care about these things?”", "이런 것들엔 정말로 관심이 없으신 거지요?\""]], [["“Not by the side of this lovely anencephalous monster.", "\"이 사랑스러운 무뇌 기형 표본 옆에 두고 보면 그렇지요."], ["I have never had time to give myself much to natural history.", "저는 박물학에 제 자신을 많이 쏟을 시간이 없었습니다."], ["I was early bitten with an interest in structure, and it is what lies most directly in my profession.", "일찍부터 구조에 흥미가 동했고, 그것이야말로 제 직업에 가장 직결되는 것이지요."], ["I have no hobby besides.", "그 밖에 다른 취미는 없습니다."], ["I have the sea to swim in there.”", "저한테는 거기에 헤엄칠 바다가 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you are a happy fellow,”", "선생은 행복한 사람이군요.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother, turning on his heel and beginning to fill his pipe.", "페어브라더 씨가 발꿈치로 홱 돌아서며 파이프에 담배를 채우기 시작했다."], ["“You don’t know what it is to want spiritual tobacco—bad emendations of old texts, or small items about a variety of Aphis Brassicae, with the well-known signature of Philomicron, for the ‘Twaddler’s Magazine;’ or a learned treatise on the entomology of the Pentateuch, including all the insects not mentioned, but probably met with by the Israelites in their passage through the desert; with a monograph on the Ant, as treated by Solomon, showing the harmony of the Book of Proverbs with the results of modern research.", "\"선생은 영적인 담배를 갈망한다는 게 어떤 건지 모르지요. 옛 문헌의 서툰 교정이라든가, 『잡담쟁이 잡지』에 실으려고 필로마이크론이라는 잘 알려진 서명을 달아 양배추진딧물의 여러 변종에 관한 자질구레한 항목을 쓴다든가, 아니면 모세오경의 곤충학에 관한 학술 논문 — 거기 언급되지는 않았으되 이스라엘 백성이 광야를 지날 때 분명 마주쳤을 모든 곤충을 다루고, 솔로몬이 다룬 개미에 관한 단행본을 곁들여, 잠언서가 현대 연구 성과와 조화를 이룸을 보여 주는 식이지요."], ["You don’t mind my fumigating you?”", "내가 선생한테 연기를 뿜어 대도 괜찮겠어요?\""]], [["Lydgate was more surprised at the openness of this talk than at its implied meaning—that the Vicar felt himself not altogether in the right vocation.", "리드게이트는 이 이야기에 담긴 함의 — 목사가 스스로 썩 어울리는 소명을 얻지 못했다고 느끼고 있다는 것 — 보다도 그 이야기의 솔직함에 더 놀랐다."], ["The neat fitting-up of drawers and shelves, and the bookcase filled with expensive illustrated books on Natural History, made him think again of the winnings at cards and their destination.", "깔끔하게 갖춰진 서랍과 선반, 그리고 값비싼 박물학 도판 서적으로 가득 찬 책장을 보자, 그는 다시금 카드놀이로 딴 돈과 그 돈의 쓰임새를 떠올렸다."], ["But he was beginning to wish that the very best construction of everything that Mr. Farebrother did should be the true one.", "그러나 그는 페어브라더 씨가 하는 모든 일에 대한 가장 호의적인 해석이 진실이기를 바라기 시작하고 있었다."], ["The Vicar’s frankness seemed not of the repulsive sort that comes from an uneasy consciousness seeking to forestall the judgment of others, but simply the relief of a desire to do with as little pretence as possible.", "목사의 솔직함은 남의 판단을 앞질러 막으려는 불안한 의식에서 나오는 그런 거슬리는 종류가 아니라, 그저 되도록 가식 없이 처신하고 싶은 욕구가 풀려 나온 것으로 보였다."], ["Apparently he was not without a sense that his freedom of speech might seem premature, for he presently said—", "보아하니 그는 자기 발언의 거리낌 없음이 성급해 보일 수도 있다는 자각이 없지 않았으니, 곧 이렇게 말했던 것이다."]], [["“I have not yet told you that I have the advantage of you, Mr. Lydgate, and know you better than you know me.", "\"내가 선생보다 유리한 점이 있고 선생이 나를 아는 것보다 내가 선생을 더 잘 안다는 것을, 아직 말씀드리지 않았군요, 리드게이트 선생."], ["You remember Trawley who shared your apartment at Paris for some time?", "파리에서 한동안 선생과 같은 방을 썼던 트롤리를 기억하시지요?"], ["I was a correspondent of his, and he told me a good deal about you.", "나는 그와 편지를 주고받던 사이였는데, 그가 선생에 대해 꽤 많은 이야기를 해 주었어요."], ["I was not quite sure when you first came that you were the same man.", "선생이 처음 왔을 때는 같은 사람인지 확신이 서지 않았어요."], ["I was very glad when I found that you were.", "같은 분임을 알고서 무척 반가웠지요."], ["Only I don’t forget that you have not had the like prologue about me.”", "다만 나는 선생에게는 나에 대한 그런 사전 설명이 없었다는 걸 잊지 않고 있습니다.\""]], [["Lydgate divined some delicacy of feeling here, but did not half understand it.", "리드게이트는 여기에 어떤 섬세한 감정이 있음을 알아차렸으나, 절반도 이해하지 못했다."], ["“By the way,”", "\"그런데 말입니다.\""], ["he said, “what has become of Trawley?", "그가 말했다. \"트롤리는 어찌 됐습니까?"], ["I have quite lost sight of him.", "저는 그 친구 소식을 영 모르게 됐어요."], ["He was hot on the French social systems, and talked of going to the Backwoods to found a sort of Pythagorean community.", "그는 프랑스 사회 체제에 열을 올렸고, 일종의 피타고라스식 공동체를 세우러 오지로 가겠다고 했지요."], ["Is he gone?”", "떠났나요?\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"전혀요."], ["He is practising at a German bath, and has married a rich patient.”", "그는 독일의 온천에서 개업하고 있고, 부유한 환자와 결혼했어요.\""]], [["“Then my notions wear the best, so far,”", "\"그렇다면 지금까지는 제 생각이 더 잘 견디는 셈이군요.\""], ["said Lydgate, with a short scornful laugh.", "리드게이트가 짧고 경멸 어린 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“He would have it, the medical profession was an inevitable system of humbug.", "\"그는 의료라는 직업이 불가피한 사기 체계라고 우겼지요."], ["I said, the fault was in the men—men who truckle to lies and folly.", "저는 잘못이 사람들에게 있다고 했고요. 거짓과 어리석음에 굴종하는 사람들 말입니다."], ["Instead of preaching against humbug outside the walls, it might be better to set up a disinfecting apparatus within.", "담장 밖에서 사기를 향해 설교할 게 아니라, 안에다 소독 장치를 설치하는 편이 나을지도 모른다고요."], ["In short—I am reporting my own conversation—you may be sure I had all the good sense on my side.”", "요컨대 — 제가 제 말을 옮기고 있는 거지만 — 분명 옳은 분별은 다 제 쪽에 있었지요.\""]], [["“Your scheme is a good deal more difficult to carry out than the Pythagorean community, though.", "\"하지만 선생의 계획은 피타고라스식 공동체보다 훨씬 더 실행하기 어렵지요."], ["You have not only got the old Adam in yourself against you, but you have got all those descendants of the original Adam who form the society around you.", "선생은 자기 안의 옛 아담만 상대로 두고 있는 게 아니라, 선생 주위에서 사회를 이루고 있는 그 태초 아담의 후손들 전부를 상대로 두고 있으니까요."], ["You see, I have paid twelve or thirteen years more than you for my knowledge of difficulties.", "보세요, 나는 어려움에 대한 지식을 위해 선생보다 열두세 해를 더 치렀어요."], ["But”—Mr.", "한데\""], ["Farebrother broke off a moment, and then added, “you are eying that glass vase again.", "— 페어브라더 씨가 잠시 말을 끊었다가 덧붙였다 — \"선생, 또 그 유리병을 노려보고 계시는군요."], ["Do you want to make an exchange?", "맞바꾸고 싶으세요?"], ["You shall not have it without a fair barter.”", "공정한 거래 없이는 못 드립니다.\""]], [["“I have some sea-mice—fine specimens—in spirits.", "\"제게 알코올에 담근 바다쥐 표본이 — 훌륭한 표본들이 — 좀 있습니다."], ["And I will throw in Robert Brown’s new thing—‘Microscopic Observations on the Pollen of Plants’—if you don’t happen to have it already.”", "그리고 로버트 브라운의 새 저작 『식물 화분에 관한 현미경 관찰』도 끼워 드리지요. 혹시 아직 안 가지고 계시다면 말입니다.\""]], [["“Why, seeing how you long for the monster, I might ask a higher price.", "\"이런, 선생이 그 표본을 그토록 탐내는 걸 보니, 더 높은 값을 불러도 되겠는데요."], ["Suppose I ask you to look through my drawers and agree with me about all my new species?”", "내 서랍을 다 살펴보고서 내 새로운 종들에 대해 모두 나와 의견을 같이해 달라고 하면 어떨까요?\""], ["The Vicar, while he talked in this way, alternately moved about with his pipe in his mouth, and returned to hang rather fondly over his drawers.", "목사는 이렇게 이야기하면서 파이프를 입에 문 채 이리저리 거닐었다가, 다시 돌아와 서랍 위로 자못 애틋하게 몸을 기울이곤 했다."], ["“That would be good discipline, you know, for a young doctor who has to please his patients in Middlemarch.", "\"미들마치에서 환자들을 만족시켜야 하는 젊은 의사한테는 그게 좋은 수련이 될 거예요."], ["You must learn to be bored, remember.", "지루함을 견디는 법을 배워 둬야지요, 명심하세요."], ["However, you shall have the monster on your own terms.”", "어쨌든 그 표본은 선생이 부른 조건대로 가지세요.\""]], [["“Don’t you think men overrate the necessity for humoring everybody’s nonsense, till they get despised by the very fools they humor?” said Lydgate, moving to Mr.", "\"사람들이 누구든의 허튼소리를 받아 주어야 할 필요를 지나치게 과대평가하다가, 결국 자기가 받아 준 바로 그 바보들한테 멸시당하게 되는 건 아닐까요?\""], ["Farebrother’s side, and looking rather absently at the insects ranged in fine gradation, with names subscribed in exquisite writing.", "리드게이트가 페어브라더 씨 곁으로 다가가며, 정교한 필체로 이름이 적힌 채 고운 단계에 따라 늘어선 곤충들을 다소 멍하니 바라보면서 말했다."], ["“The shortest way is to make your value felt, so that people must put up with you whether you flatter them or not.”", "\"가장 빠른 길은 자기 값어치를 느끼게 만드는 거지요. 그러면 사람들은 선생이 자기들한테 아첨을 하든 안 하든 선생을 받아들일 수밖에 없게 되니까요.\""]], [["“With all my heart.", "\"전적으로 동감입니다."], ["But then you must be sure of having the value, and you must keep yourself independent.", "하지만 그러려면 그 값어치를 확실히 갖추고 있어야 하고, 또 독립을 지켜야 해요."], ["Very few men can do that.", "그럴 수 있는 사람은 극히 드물지요."], ["Either you slip out of service altogether, and become good for nothing, or you wear the harness and draw a good deal where your yoke-fellows pull you.", "아예 직무에서 미끄러져 나가 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 사람이 되거나, 아니면 멍에를 쓰고서 같이 멍에를 진 동료들이 끄는 대로 따라 끌려가거나 둘 중 하나예요."], ["But do look at these delicate orthoptera!”", "한데 이 섬세한 직시류들 좀 보세요!\""]], [["Lydgate had after all to give some scrutiny to each drawer, the Vicar laughing at himself, and yet persisting in the exhibition.", "리드게이트는 결국 서랍을 하나하나 어느 정도 들여다봐 줘야 했고, 목사는 스스로를 비웃으면서도 끈질기게 그것들을 펼쳐 보였다."]], [["“Apropos of what you said about wearing harness,”", "\"멍에를 쓰는 일에 대해 선생이 하신 말씀과 관련해서요,\""], ["Lydgate began, after they had sat down, “I made up my mind some time ago to do with as little of it as possible.", "그들이 자리에 앉은 뒤에 리드게이트가 입을 열었다. \"저는 얼마 전에 그걸 되도록 적게 쓰고 살기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["That was why I determined not to try anything in London, for a good many years at least.", "그래서 적어도 앞으로 여러 해 동안은 런던에서 무엇이든 시도하지 않기로 결심했던 거고요."], ["I didn’t like what I saw when I was studying there—so much empty bigwiggism, and obstructive trickery.", "거기서 공부할 때 본 것이 마음에 들지 않았거든요. 공허한 거드름이라든가, 앞길을 막는 술수가 너무 많았어요."], ["In the country, people have less pretension to knowledge, and are less of companions, but for that reason they affect one’s amour-propre less: one makes less bad blood, and can follow one’s own course more quietly.”", "시골에서는 사람들이 지식을 내세우는 일도 덜하고, 좋은 벗이 되어 주는 일도 덜하지만, 바로 그 때문에 사람의 자존심을 덜 건드리지요. 나쁜 감정도 덜 생기고, 자기 길을 더 조용히 좇아갈 수 있고요.\""]], [["“Yes—well—you have got a good start; you are in the right profession, the work you feel yourself most fit for.", "\"그렇지요, 음, 선생은 출발이 좋아요. 옳은 직업을 택했고, 스스로 가장 잘 맞는다고 느끼는 일을 하고 있으니까요."], ["Some people miss that, and repent too late.", "어떤 사람들은 그걸 놓쳐 버리고는 너무 늦게야 후회하지요."], ["But you must not be too sure of keeping your independence.”", "하지만 독립을 지킬 거라고 너무 확신하지는 마세요.\""]], [["“You mean of family ties?”", "\"가족이라는 굴레를 말씀하시는 거지요?\""], ["said Lydgate, conceiving that these might press rather tightly on Mr. Farebrother.", "리드게이트가 말했는데, 그런 것들이 페어브라더 씨를 다소 옥죄고 있으리라 짐작하면서였다."]], [["“Not altogether.", "\"꼭 그것만은 아니에요."], ["Of course they make many things more difficult.", "물론 그런 것들이 많은 일을 더 어렵게 만들긴 하지요."], ["But a good wife—a good unworldly woman—may really help a man, and keep him more independent.", "하지만 좋은 아내 — 세속에 물들지 않은 좋은 여인은 — 정말로 남자를 도와줄 수 있고, 그를 더 독립적으로 지켜 줄 수 있어요."], ["There’s a parishioner of mine—a fine fellow, but who would hardly have pulled through as he has done without his wife.", "내 교구민 중에 한 사람 — 훌륭한 친구지만 아내가 없었더라면 지금처럼 잘 헤쳐 나오지 못했을 사람이 있지요."], ["Do you know the Garths?", "가스 일가를 아세요?"], ["I think they were not Peacock’s patients.”", "피콕의 환자는 아니었던 것 같은데.\""]], [["“No; but there is a Miss Garth at old Featherstone’s, at Lowick.”", "\"모릅니다. 하지만 로윅의 페더스톤 노인 댁에 가스 양이라는 분이 있더군요.\""]], [["“Their daughter: an excellent girl.”", "\"그 집 딸이지요. 더없이 훌륭한 처녀예요.\""]], [["“She is very quiet—I have hardly noticed her.”", "\"아주 조용한 분이라, 저는 거의 눈여겨보지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“She has taken notice of you, though, depend upon it.”", "\"하지만 그 처녀는 선생을 눈여겨봤을 거예요, 틀림없이.\""]], [["“I don’t understand,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠군요.\""], ["said Lydgate; he could hardly say “Of course.”", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"당연하지요\"라고는 차마 말할 수 없었다."]], [["“Oh, she gauges everybody.", "\"오, 그 처녀는 누구든 가늠한다니까요."], ["I prepared her for confirmation—she is a favorite of mine.”", "내가 그 애한테 견진성사 준비를 시켰지요. 내가 아끼는 아이예요.\""]], [["Mr. Farebrother puffed a few moments in silence, Lydgate not caring to know more about the Garths.", "페어브라더 씨는 잠시 말없이 몇 모금 빨았고, 리드게이트는 가스 일가에 대해 더 알고 싶은 마음이 없었다."], ["At last the Vicar laid down his pipe, stretched out his legs, and turned his bright eyes with a smile towards Lydgate, saying—", "마침내 목사는 파이프를 내려놓고 다리를 쭉 뻗더니, 빛나는 눈을 미소와 함께 리드게이트 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“But we Middlemarchers are not so tame as you take us to be.", "\"하지만 우리 미들마치 사람들은 선생이 생각하시는 만큼 그렇게 순하지가 않아요."], ["We have our intrigues and our parties.", "우리에게도 우리 나름의 암투가 있고 파벌이 있지요."], ["I am a party man, for example, and Bulstrode is another.", "가령 나도 파벌에 속한 사람이고, 벌스트로드도 다른 파벌 사람이에요."], ["If you vote for me you will offend Bulstrode.”", "선생이 나한테 표를 주면 벌스트로드의 비위를 거스르게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“What is there against Bulstrode?”", "\"벌스트로드에게 무슨 문제가 있나요?\""], ["said Lydgate, emphatically.", "리드게이트가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“I did not say there was anything against him except that.", "\"그거 말고 그에게 무슨 문제가 있다고 한 건 아니에요."], ["If you vote against him you will make him your enemy.”", "그에게 반대표를 던지면 그를 적으로 만들게 된다는 거지요.\""]], [["“I don’t know that I need mind about that,”", "\"그런 거라면 제가 굳이 신경 쓸 필요는 없을 듯합니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, rather proudly; “but he seems to have good ideas about hospitals, and he spends large sums on useful public objects.", "리드게이트가 다소 자존심 있게 말했다. \"하지만 그는 병원에 대해 좋은 생각을 가진 듯하고, 유용한 공공사업에 큰돈을 쓰지요."], ["He might help me a good deal in carrying out my ideas.", "제 구상을 실현하는 데 그가 저를 꽤 도와줄 수 있을 겁니다."], ["As to his religious notions—why, as Voltaire said, incantations will destroy a flock of sheep if administered with a certain quantity of arsenic.", "그의 종교적 신념으로 말하자면 — 그야, 볼테르가 말했듯이, 일정량의 비소와 함께 쓴다면 주문(呪文)도 양 떼를 죽일 수 있는 법이지요."], ["I look for the man who will bring the arsenic, and don’t mind about his incantations.”", "저는 비소를 가져올 사람을 찾는 것이고, 그의 주문 따위는 개의치 않습니다.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"아주 좋아요."], ["But then you must not offend your arsenic-man.", "하지만 그렇다면 선생의 비소 담당자를 거스르면 안 되겠지요."], ["You will not offend me, you know,”", "나야 거슬러도 괜찮아요, 아시다시피.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother, quite unaffectedly.", "페어브라더 씨가 아주 꾸밈없이 말했다."], ["“I don’t translate my own convenience into other people’s duties.", "\"나는 내 편의를 남의 의무로 둔갑시키지 않으니까요. 나는 여러모로 벌스트로드와 반대편에 서 있어요."], ["I am opposed to Bulstrode in many ways.", "그가 속한 무리를 좋아하지 않거든요."], ["I don’t like the set he belongs to: they are a narrow ignorant set, and do more to make their neighbors uncomfortable than to make them better.", "그들은 편협하고 무지한 무리인 데다, 이웃을 더 낫게 만드는 일보다 불편하게 만드는 일을 더 많이 하지요. 그들의 방식이란 일종의 세속적이면서 영적인 파벌주의예요."], ["Their system is a sort of worldly-spiritual cliqueism: they really look on the rest of mankind as a doomed carcass which is to nourish them for heaven.", "그들은 정말이지 나머지 인류를 자기들이 천국에 가도록 자양분이 되어 줄, 죽을 운명의 송장쯤으로 여긴다니까요. 하지만,\""], ["But,”", "그가 미소 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["he added, smilingly, “I don’t say that Bulstrode’s new hospital is a bad thing; and as to his wanting to oust me from the old one—why, if he thinks me a mischievous fellow, he is only returning a compliment.", "\"벌스트로드의 새 병원이 나쁜 거라고 말하는 건 아니에요. 그리고 그가 나를 옛 병원에서 몰아내려 한다 해도 — 그야, 그가 나를 해로운 인간으로 여긴다면, 그저 인사치레를 되돌려 주는 것뿐이지요."], ["And I am not a model clergyman—only a decent makeshift.”", "게다가 나는 모범적인 성직자가 아니에요. 그저 그럭저럭한 임시변통일 뿐이지요.\""]], [["Lydgate was not at all sure that the Vicar maligned himself.", "리드게이트는 목사가 자신을 깎아내리고 있는 것인지 도무지 확신할 수 없었다."], ["A model clergyman, like a model doctor, ought to think his own profession the finest in the world, and take all knowledge as mere nourishment to his moral pathology and therapeutics.", "모범적인 성직자란 모범적인 의사와 마찬가지로, 자기 직업을 세상에서 가장 훌륭한 것으로 여기고, 모든 지식을 자기 도덕의 병리학과 치료학에 바칠 단순한 자양분으로 받아들여야 마땅했다."], ["He only said, “What reason does Bulstrode give for superseding you?”", "그는 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"벌스트로드가 선생을 갈아 치우려는 이유로 무엇을 댑니까?\""]], [["“That I don’t teach his opinions—which he calls spiritual religion; and that I have no time to spare.", "\"내가 그의 견해를 — 그가 영적 신앙이라 부르는 것을 — 가르치지 않는다는 것, 그리고 내게는 시간 여유가 없다는 것이지요."], ["Both statements are true.", "두 가지 다 사실이에요."], ["But then I could make time, and I should be glad of the forty pounds.", "하지만 그렇다 해도 나는 시간을 낼 수 있을 테고, 그 사십 파운드는 반가울 거예요."], ["That is the plain fact of the case.", "그게 이 일의 명백한 실상이지요."], ["But let us dismiss it.", "하지만 이 이야기는 그만둡시다."], ["I only wanted to tell you that if you vote for your arsenic-man, you are not to cut me in consequence.", "나는 다만, 선생이 비소 담당자에게 표를 준다 해도 그 때문에 나와 인연을 끊어선 안 된다는 걸 말하고 싶었을 뿐이에요."], ["I can’t spare you.", "나는 선생을 놓칠 수가 없거든요."], ["You are a sort of circumnavigator come to settle among us, and will keep up my belief in the antipodes.", "선생은 우리 가운데 정착하러 온 일종의 세계 일주 항해자라, 내가 지구 반대편의 존재를 믿게끔 지켜 줄 사람이지요."], ["Now tell me all about them in Paris.”", "자, 이제 파리에 있는 그 사람들에 대해 다 말해 보세요.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII.", "# CHAPTER XVIII."]], [["“Oh, sir, the loftiest hopes on earth Draw lots with meaner hopes: heroic breasts, Breathing bad air, run risk of pestilence; Or, lacking lime-juice when they cross the Line, May languish with the scurvy.”", "더 비천한 희망들과 함께 제비를 뽑나니, 영웅적인 가슴도\n탁한 공기를 들이마시면 역병에 걸릴 위험을 무릅쓰고,"]], [["Some weeks passed after this conversation before the question of the chaplaincy gathered any practical import for Lydgate, and without telling himself the reason, he deferred the predetermination on which side he should give his vote. It would really have been a matter of total indifference to him—that is to say, he would have taken the more convenient side, and given his vote for the appointment of Tyke without any hesitation—if he had not cared personally for Mr. Farebrother.", "적도를 건널 때 라임즙이 없으면\n괴혈병으로 시들 수도 있으니.」"]], [["But his liking for the Vicar of St. Botolph’s grew with growing acquaintanceship.", "이 대화가 있고 나서 몇 주가 지나서야 비로소 예배당 목사직 문제가 리드게이트에게 실질적인 의미를 띠게 되었고, 그는 스스로 그 까닭을 인정하지는 않으면서도 어느 편에 표를 던질지 미리 정하는 일을 미루었다."], ["That, entering into Lydgate’s position as a new-comer who had his own professional objects to secure, Mr.", "만약 그가 페어브라더 씨를 개인적으로 좋아하지 않았더라면, 그것은 정말이지 그에게 아무래도 좋은 문제였을 것이다."], ["Farebrother should have taken pains rather to warn off than to obtain his interest, showed an unusual delicacy and generosity, which Lydgate’s nature was keenly alive to.", "다시 말해 그는 더 편한 쪽을 택해 망설임 없이 타이크의 임명에 표를 던졌을 것이다. 그러나 세인트 보톨프의 교구 목사에 대한 그의 호감은 친분이 깊어 갈수록 커졌다."], ["It went along with other points of conduct in Mr. Farebrother which were exceptionally fine, and made his character resemble those southern landscapes which seem divided between natural grandeur and social slovenliness.", "자기 나름의 직업적 목표를 확보해야 하는 신참내기로서의 리드게이트의 처지를 헤아려, 페어브라더 씨가 그의 관심을 얻으려 하기보다는 오히려 멀리하도록 애써 주었다는 사실은, 리드게이트의 천성이 예민하게 감응하는 보기 드문 섬세함과 너그러움을 보여 주었다."], ["Very few men could have been as filial and chivalrous as he was to the mother, aunt, and sister, whose dependence on him had in many ways shaped his life rather uneasily for himself; few men who feel the pressure of small needs are so nobly resolute not to dress up their inevitably self-interested desires in a pretext of better motives.", "그것은 페어브라더 씨가 보이는 다른 면모들, 즉 유달리 훌륭하여 그의 성품을 자연의 웅장함과 사회적 너저분함으로 나뉜 듯한 저 남방의 풍경과 닮게 만드는 그런 행실들과 한데 어울렸다. 그처럼 자식 된 도리를 다하고 기사도적으로 어머니와 고모와 누이를 대할 수 있는 사람은 거의 없었으니, 그들이 그에게 의지한 탓에 그의 삶은 여러모로 그 자신에게는 다소 거북하게 빚어졌던 것이다."], ["In these matters he was conscious that his life would bear the closest scrutiny; and perhaps the consciousness encouraged a little defiance towards the critical strictness of persons whose celestial intimacies seemed not to improve their domestic manners, and whose lofty aims were not needed to account for their actions.", "자질구레한 필요에서 오는 압박을 느끼는 사람들 가운데 불가피하게 사사로운 욕망을 더 나은 동기라는 핑계로 치장하지 않으리라 그토록 고결하게 결심하는 이는 드물다. 이런 문제들에서 그는 자신의 삶이 어떤 면밀한 검증도 견뎌 내리라는 것을 의식하고 있었다."], ["Then, his preaching was ingenious and pithy, like the preaching of the English Church in its robust age, and his sermons were delivered without book.", "그리고 어쩌면 그 의식이, 천상과의 친교가 가정에서의 행실은 조금도 나아지게 하지 못하는 듯하고 드높은 목표가 없어도 그들의 행동을 충분히 설명할 수 있는 그런 사람들의 까다로운 엄격함에 대해 약간의 반항심을 부추겼을지도 모른다. 게다가 그의 설교는 영국 교회가 건강하던 시절의 설교처럼 기발하고 함축적이었으며, 책 없이 전해졌다."], ["People outside his parish went to hear him; and, since to fill the church was always the most difficult part of a clergyman’s function, here was another ground for a careless sense of superiority.", "그의 교구 밖 사람들도 그의 설교를 들으러 왔다. 그리고 교회를 가득 채우는 일이 언제나 성직자의 직무 가운데 가장 어려운 부분이었으므로, 여기에 그가 무심한 우월감을 느낄 또 하나의 근거가 있었다."], ["Besides, he was a likable man: sweet-tempered, ready-witted, frank, without grins of suppressed bitterness or other conversational flavors which make half of us an affliction to our friends.", "게다가 그는 호감 가는 사람이었다. 성품이 온화하고 재치가 빠르며 솔직했고, 억눌린 쓰라림이 묻어나는 능글맞은 웃음이나 우리 가운데 절반을 친구들에게 골칫거리로 만드는 그 밖의 대화상의 버릇도 없었다."], ["Lydgate liked him heartily, and wished for his friendship.", "리드게이트는 진심으로 그를 좋아했고, 그와 벗이 되기를 바랐다."]], [["With this feeling uppermost, he continued to waive the question of the chaplaincy, and to persuade himself that it was not only no proper business of his, but likely enough never to vex him with a demand for his vote. Lydgate, at Mr.", "이런 감정이 마음을 지배하는 가운데, 그는 계속해서 예배당 목사직 문제를 미뤄 두었고, 그것이 자기가 마땅히 관여할 일이 아닐뿐더러 자기에게 표를 요구하며 성가시게 하는 일도 결코 없으리라고 스스로를 설득했다."], ["Bulstrode’s request, was laying down plans for the internal arrangements of the new hospital, and the two were often in consultation.", "리드게이트는 벌스트로드 씨의 요청으로 새 병원의 내부 배치 계획을 세우고 있었고, 두 사람은 자주 의논을 나누었다."], ["The banker was always presupposing that he could count in general on Lydgate as a coadjutor, but made no special recurrence to the coming decision between Tyke and Farebrother.", "은행가는 늘 대체로 리드게이트를 협력자로 믿을 수 있으리라 전제했지만, 타이크와 페어브라더 사이에 다가올 결정에 대해서는 따로 언급하지 않았다."], ["When the General Board of the Infirmary had met, however, and Lydgate had notice that the question of the chaplaincy was thrown on a council of the directors and medical men, to meet on the following Friday, he had a vexed sense that he must make up his mind on this trivial Middlemarch business.", "그러나 병원 이사회 총회가 열리고, 예배당 목사직 문제가 다음 금요일에 모일 이사들과 의료진의 협의에 부쳐졌다는 통지를 받았을 때, 그는 이 사소한 미들마치의 일에 대해 마음을 정해야만 한다는 짜증스러운 기분이 들었다."], ["He could not help hearing within him the distinct declaration that Bulstrode was prime minister, and that the Tyke affair was a question of office or no office; and he could not help an equally pronounced dislike to giving up the prospect of office.", "그는 마음속에서 벌스트로드가 수상이고 타이크 사건이 자리를 얻느냐 마느냐의 문제라는 뚜렷한 선언을 듣지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 자리에 대한 전망을 포기하기 싫다는 그에 못지않게 분명한 거부감도 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["For his observation was constantly confirming Mr. Farebrother’s assurance that the banker would not overlook opposition.", "그의 관찰이 은행가가 반대를 그냥 넘기지 않으리라는 페어브라더 씨의 장담을 끊임없이 확인해 주었기 때문이다."], ["“Confound their petty politics!”", "\"그들의 옹졸한 정치 따위, 빌어먹을!\""], ["was one of his thoughts for three mornings in the meditative process of shaving, when he had begun to feel that he must really hold a court of conscience on this matter.", "이것이 그가 이 문제에 대해 정말로 양심의 법정을 열어야겠다고 느끼기 시작했을 무렵, 면도하며 사색에 잠겨 보낸 사흘 아침 동안 떠올린 생각 가운데 하나였다."], ["Certainly there were valid things to be said against the election of Mr.", "분명 페어브라더 씨의 선출에 반대해 할 수 있는 타당한 말들이 있었다."], ["Farebrother: he had too much on his hands already, especially considering how much time he spent on non-clerical occupations.", "그는 이미 너무 많은 일을 떠맡고 있었으니, 특히 성직과 무관한 일들에 얼마나 많은 시간을 쓰는지를 생각하면 그러했다."], ["Then again it was a continually repeated shock, disturbing Lydgate’s esteem, that the Vicar should obviously play for the sake of money, liking the play indeed, but evidently liking some end which it served. Mr.", "게다가 그 목사가 명백히 돈을 위해 노름을 한다는 사실은, 정말로 노름 자체를 즐기기는 하지만 분명히 그것이 가져다주는 어떤 목적을 좋아한다는 사실은, 리드게이트의 존경심을 흔들며 거듭 충격을 주었다."], ["Farebrother contended on theory for the desirability of all games, and said that Englishmen’s wit was stagnant for want of them; but Lydgate felt certain that he would have played very much less but for the money.", "페어브라더 씨는 이론적으로 모든 놀이가 바람직하다고 주장했고, 영국인의 재치가 그런 것이 없어 정체되어 있다고 말했다. 그러나 리드게이트는 그가 돈이 아니었다면 훨씬 덜 노름을 했으리라 확신했다."], ["There was a billiard-room at the Green Dragon, which some anxious mothers and wives regarded as the chief temptation in Middlemarch.", "그린 드래건에는 당구장이 있었는데, 일부 노심초사하는 어머니들과 아내들은 그곳을 미들마치 최대의 유혹으로 여겼다."], ["The Vicar was a first-rate billiard-player, and though he did not frequent the Green Dragon, there were reports that he had sometimes been there in the daytime and had won money.", "그 목사는 일급 당구 선수였고, 그린 드래건에 자주 드나들지는 않았지만, 이따금 대낮에 그곳에 가서 돈을 땄다는 소문이 있었다."], ["And as to the chaplaincy, he did not pretend that he cared for it, except for the sake of the forty pounds.", "그리고 예배당 목사직에 관해서는, 그 사십 파운드를 위해서가 아니라면 자기는 그 자리에 관심이 없다고 그는 굳이 꾸미려 들지 않았다."], ["Lydgate was no Puritan, but he did not care for play, and winning money at it had always seemed a meanness to him; besides, he had an ideal of life which made this subservience of conduct to the gaining of small sums thoroughly hateful to him.", "리드게이트는 청교도는 아니었지만 노름을 좋아하지 않았고, 노름으로 돈을 따는 것은 늘 그에게 비열한 짓으로 여겨졌다. 게다가 그에게는 삶에 대한 어떤 이상이 있었으니, 행실을 푼돈벌이에 종속시키는 이런 일이 그에게는 더없이 혐오스럽게 느껴졌다."], ["Hitherto in his own life his wants had been supplied without any trouble to himself, and his first impulse was always to be liberal with half-crowns as matters of no importance to a gentleman; it had never occurred to him to devise a plan for getting half-crowns.", "지금껏 그 자신의 삶에서 그의 필요는 그 자신은 아무런 수고도 하지 않은 채 채워졌고, 그의 첫 충동은 언제나 신사에게는 대수롭지 않은 것이라며 반 크라운짜리 돈을 후하게 쓰는 것이었다. 반 크라운을 벌어들일 궁리를 짜내야겠다는 생각은 그에게 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["He had always known in a general way that he was not rich, but he had never felt poor, and he had no power of imagining the part which the want of money plays in determining the actions of men.", "그는 자신이 부자가 아니라는 것을 늘 대강은 알고 있었지만, 가난하다고 느낀 적은 없었고, 돈의 부족이 사람들의 행동을 결정하는 데 어떤 역할을 하는지 상상할 능력이 없었다."], ["Money had never been a motive to him.", "돈은 그에게 결코 동기가 된 적이 없었다."], ["Hence he was not ready to frame excuses for this deliberate pursuit of small gains.", "그래서 그는 이런 의도적인 푼돈 추구에 대해 변명을 마련해 줄 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["It was altogether repulsive to him, and he never entered into any calculation of the ratio between the Vicar’s income and his more or less necessary expenditure.", "그것은 그에게 그야말로 역겨운 일이었고, 그는 그 목사의 수입과 그의 다소간 필수적인 지출 사이의 비율을 따져 본 적도 없었다."], ["It was possible that he would not have made such a calculation in his own case.", "어쩌면 자기 자신의 경우라면 그런 계산을 하지 않았을 것이다."]], [["And now, when the question of voting had come, this repulsive fact told more strongly against Mr. Farebrother than it had done before.", "그런데 이제 투표 문제가 다가오자, 이 역겨운 사실은 전보다 더 강하게 페어브라더 씨에게 불리하게 작용했다."], ["One would know much better what to do if men’s characters were more consistent, and especially if one’s friends were invariably fit for any function they desired to undertake! Lydgate was convinced that if there had been no valid objection to Mr.", "사람들의 성품이 좀 더 한결같다면, 특히 친구들이 자기가 맡고자 하는 어떤 직무에든 어김없이 적합하다면, 우리는 어떻게 해야 할지 훨씬 더 잘 알 수 있으련만!"], ["Farebrother, he would have voted for him, whatever Bulstrode might have felt on the subject: he did not intend to be a vassal of Bulstrode’s.", "리드게이트는 페어브라더 씨에게 아무런 타당한 반대 사유가 없었다면, 벌스트로드가 그 문제에 대해 어떻게 느끼든 상관없이 그에게 표를 던졌으리라 확신했다. 그는 벌스트로드의 종복이 될 생각은 없었다."], ["On the other hand, there was Tyke, a man entirely given to his clerical office, who was simply curate at a chapel of ease in St. Peter’s parish, and had time for extra duty.", "다른 한편으로 타이크가 있었으니, 그는 전적으로 성직에 헌신하는 사람으로, 세인트 피터 교구의 한 부속 예배당의 보좌 사제에 지나지 않아 부수적인 직무를 맡을 시간이 있었다."], ["Nobody had anything to say against Mr. Tyke, except that they could not bear him, and suspected him of cant.", "타이크 씨에 대해서는, 그를 도무지 견딜 수 없고 위선적인 말투를 쓴다고 의심한다는 것 말고는 누구도 흠잡을 말이 없었다."], ["Really, from his point of view, Bulstrode was thoroughly justified.", "정말이지, 그의 관점에서 보면 벌스트로드는 더없이 정당했다."]], [["But whichever way Lydgate began to incline, there was something to make him wince; and being a proud man, he was a little exasperated at being obliged to wince.", "그러나 리드게이트가 어느 쪽으로 기울기 시작하든, 그를 움찔하게 만드는 무언가가 있었다. 그리고 그는 자존심이 강한 사람이었던지라, 움찔할 수밖에 없는 처지가 다소 화가 났다."], ["He did not like frustrating his own best purposes by getting on bad terms with Bulstrode; he did not like voting against Farebrother, and helping to deprive him of function and salary; and the question occurred whether the additional forty pounds might not leave the Vicar free from that ignoble care about winning at cards.", "그는 벌스트로드와 사이가 틀어져 자신의 가장 훌륭한 목표를 좌절시키고 싶지 않았다. 페어브라더에게 불리하게 표를 던져 그에게서 직무와 봉급을 빼앗는 데 거드는 것도 싫었다."], ["Moreover, Lydgate did not like the consciousness that in voting for Tyke he should be voting on the side obviously convenient for himself.", "그리고 그 사십 파운드가 더 생긴다면 그 목사가 카드놀이에서 따려는 저 천박한 근심에서 벗어날 수 있지 않을까 하는 의문이 떠올랐다. 더욱이 리드게이트는 타이크에게 표를 던짐으로써 명백히 자기에게 편한 쪽에 표를 던지는 셈이 된다는 의식이 싫었다."], ["But would the end really be his own convenience?", "그러나 그 결과가 정말로 자기 편의를 위한 것일까?"], ["Other people would say so, and would allege that he was currying favor with Bulstrode for the sake of making himself important and getting on in the world.", "다른 사람들은 그렇다고 말할 테고, 그가 자신을 중요한 인물로 만들어 출세하려고 벌스트로드의 비위를 맞춘다고 주장하리라. 그러면 또 어떤가?"], ["What then? He for his own part knew that if his personal prospects simply had been concerned, he would not have cared a rotten nut for the banker’s friendship or enmity.", "그로서는 단지 자신의 개인적인 전망만이 걸린 문제였다면 은행가의 우정이든 적의든 썩은 호두 한 알만큼도 개의치 않았으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["What he really cared for was a medium for his work, a vehicle for his ideas; and after all, was he not bound to prefer the object of getting a good hospital, where he could demonstrate the specific distinctions of fever and test therapeutic results, before anything else connected with this chaplaincy?", "그가 정말로 마음을 쓴 것은 자기 일을 펼칠 매개이자 자기 생각을 실어 나를 수단이었다. 그러니 따지고 보면, 열병의 구체적인 구별을 입증하고 치료 결과를 시험할 수 있는 좋은 병원을 얻겠다는 목표를, 이 예배당 목사직과 관련된 그 무엇보다도 우선해야 마땅하지 않은가?"], ["For the first time Lydgate was feeling the hampering threadlike pressure of small social conditions, and their frustrating complexity.", "리드게이트는 처음으로 자질구레한 사회적 조건들이 실오라기처럼 옭아매는 압박과 그 좌절스러운 복잡함을 느끼고 있었다."], ["At the end of his inward debate, when he set out for the hospital, his hope was really in the chance that discussion might somehow give a new aspect to the question, and make the scale dip so as to exclude the necessity for voting.", "내적 토론 끝에 병원으로 출발할 때, 그의 희망은 사실 토론이 어떻게든 문제에 새로운 양상을 부여하여, 투표할 필요가 없도록 저울추를 기울게 해 주리라는 가능성에 걸려 있었다. 내 생각에 그는 또한 상황이 낳는 힘에도 약간 기대를 걸었던 것 같다."], ["I think he trusted a little also to the energy which is begotten by circumstances—some feeling rushing warmly and making resolve easy, while debate in cool blood had only made it more difficult.", "어떤 감정이 따뜻하게 솟구쳐 결심을 쉽게 만들어 주리라는 기대 말이다. 냉정하게 따지는 토론은 그것을 오히려 더 어렵게 만들 뿐이었으니까."], ["However it was, he did not distinctly say to himself on which side he would vote; and all the while he was inwardly resenting the subjection which had been forced upon him.", "어쨌든 그는 어느 편에 표를 던질지 스스로에게 분명히 말하지 않았다. 그리고 그러는 내내 그는 자기에게 강요된 굴종에 속으로 분개하고 있었다."], ["It would have seemed beforehand like a ridiculous piece of bad logic that he, with his unmixed resolutions of independence and his select purposes, would find himself at the very outset in the grasp of petty alternatives, each of which was repugnant to him.", "독립에 대한 순수한 결의와 선택받은 목표를 지닌 그가, 출발선에서부터 저마다 그에게 혐오스러운 옹졸한 양자택일의 손아귀에 붙들리게 되리라는 것은, 미리 보았다면 가소로울 만큼 어처구니없는 잘못된 논리처럼 여겨졌을 것이다."], ["In his student’s chambers, he had prearranged his social action quite differently.", "학생 시절의 하숙방에서 그는 자신의 사회적 행보를 이와는 사뭇 다르게 미리 그려 보았던 것이다."]], [["Lydgate was late in setting out, but Dr. Sprague, the two other surgeons, and several of the directors had arrived early; Mr.", "리드게이트는 늦게 출발했지만, 스프레이그 박사와 다른 두 외과 의사, 그리고 몇몇 이사들은 일찍 도착해 있었다."], ["Bulstrode, treasurer and chairman, being among those who were still absent.", "회계 담당이자 의장인 벌스트로드 씨는 아직 오지 않은 사람들 가운데 하나였다."], ["The conversation seemed to imply that the issue was problematical, and that a majority for Tyke was not so certain as had been generally supposed.", "오가는 대화로 미루어 보아 결과가 불확실하고, 타이크 쪽의 다수가 일반적으로 짐작되던 것만큼 확실하지는 않은 듯했다."], ["The two physicians, for a wonder, turned out to be unanimous, or rather, though of different minds, they concurred in action. Dr.", "놀랍게도 두 내과 의사는 의견이 일치한 것으로, 아니 오히려 생각은 달랐으나 행동에서는 뜻을 같이한 것으로 드러났다."], ["Sprague, the rugged and weighty, was, as every one had foreseen, an adherent of Mr. Farebrother.", "투박하고 묵직한 스프레이그 박사는 모두가 예견했던 대로 페어브라더 씨의 지지자였다."], ["The Doctor was more than suspected of having no religion, but somehow Middlemarch tolerated this deficiency in him as if he had been a Lord Chancellor; indeed it is probable that his professional weight was the more believed in, the world-old association of cleverness with the evil principle being still potent in the minds even of lady-patients who had the strictest ideas of frilling and sentiment.", "박사는 종교가 없다는 의심을 단단히 받고 있었지만, 어찌 된 영문인지 미들마치는 마치 그가 대법관이라도 되는 양 그의 이 결함을 너그러이 보아 넘겼다. 실제로 영리함을 악의 원리와 결부 짓는 케케묵은 관념이, 주름 장식과 감상에 대해 가장 엄격한 관념을 지닌 부인 환자들의 마음속에서조차 여전히 힘을 떨치고 있었으니, 그의 직업적 무게는 오히려 그 때문에 더 믿어졌을 가능성이 크다."], ["It was perhaps this negation in the Doctor which made his neighbors call him hard-headed and dry-witted; conditions of texture which were also held favorable to the storing of judgments connected with drugs.", "어쩌면 박사의 이런 부정적인 면이 이웃들로 하여금 그를 냉철하고 무뚝뚝하게 재치 있는 사람이라 부르게 한 것인지도 모른다. 그런 기질의 결은 약물과 관련된 판단을 쌓아 두기에도 유리하다고 여겨졌다."], ["At all events, it is certain that if any medical man had come to Middlemarch with the reputation of having very definite religious views, of being given to prayer, and of otherwise showing an active piety, there would have been a general presumption against his medical skill.", "어쨌든 분명한 것은, 어떤 의사가 매우 확고한 종교적 견해를 지녔고 기도에 열심이며 그 밖에도 적극적인 신앙심을 드러낸다는 평판을 안고 미들마치에 왔더라면, 그의 의술에 대한 전반적인 의구심이 일었으리라는 점이다."]], [["On this ground it was (professionally speaking) fortunate for Dr. Minchin that his religious sympathies were of a general kind, and such as gave a distant medical sanction to all serious sentiment, whether of Church or Dissent, rather than any adhesion to particular tenets.", "이런 까닭에 민친 박사로서는 (직업적으로 말하자면) 그의 종교적 공감이 일반적인 종류여서, 특정 교리에 집착하기보다는 교회파든 비국교파든 모든 진지한 감정에 대해 멀찍이서 의학적 승인을 보내는 정도였다는 점이 다행스러웠다."], ["If Mr. Bulstrode insisted, as he was apt to do, on the Lutheran doctrine of justification, as that by which a Church must stand or fall, Dr.", "벌스트로드 씨가 곧잘 그러듯 교회의 존립이 걸린 것이라며 루터파의 칭의 교리를 고집하면, 민친 박사는 그에 맞서 인간이 한낱 기계나 우연히 모인 원자 덩어리가 아님을 자못 확신했다."], ["Minchin in return was quite sure that man was not a mere machine or a fortuitous conjunction of atoms; if Mrs. Wimple insisted on a particular providence in relation to her stomach complaint, Dr.", "윔플 부인이 자신의 위장병과 관련해 특별한 섭리를 고집하면, 민친 박사는 자기로서는 정신의 창문을 열어 두기를 좋아하며 고정된 한계에는 반대한다고 했다."], ["Minchin for his part liked to keep the mental windows open and objected to fixed limits; if the Unitarian brewer jested about the Athanasian Creed, Dr.", "유니테리언파 양조업자가 아타나시우스 신경을 두고 농을 던지면, 민친 박사는 포프의 「인간론」을 인용했다."], ["Minchin quoted Pope’s “Essay on Man.” He objected to the rather free style of anecdote in which Dr.", "그는 스프레이그 박사가 즐기는 다소 거리낌 없는 일화 투의 화법에 반대했고, 권위 있는 인용구를 선호했으며 온갖 종류의 세련됨을 좋아했다."], ["Sprague indulged, preferring well-sanctioned quotations, and liking refinement of all kinds: it was generally known that he had some kinship to a bishop, and sometimes spent his holidays at “the palace.”", "그가 어느 주교와 친척 관계라는 것은 널리 알려진 사실이었고, 그는 이따금 \"주교관\"에서 휴일을 보내곤 했다."]], [["Dr. Minchin was soft-handed, pale-complexioned, and of rounded outline, not to be distinguished from a mild clergyman in appearance: whereas Dr.", "민친 박사는 손이 부드럽고 안색이 창백하며 윤곽이 둥글어서, 겉모습만으로는 온화한 성직자와 구별되지 않았다."], ["Sprague was superfluously tall; his trousers got creased at the knees, and showed an excess of boot at a time when straps seemed necessary to any dignity of bearing; you heard him go in and out, and up and down, as if he had come to see after the roofing.", "반면에 스프레이그 박사는 쓸데없이 키가 컸다. 그의 바지는 무릎이 구겨졌고, 단정한 차림새에 바짓단 끈이 필수처럼 여겨지던 시절에 부츠가 지나치게 드러나 보였다."], ["In short, he had weight, and might be expected to grapple with a disease and throw it; while Dr.", "그가 드나들고 오르내리는 소리를 듣고 있노라면, 마치 지붕 잇는 일을 살피러 온 사람 같았다."], ["Minchin might be better able to detect it lurking and to circumvent it. They enjoyed about equally the mysterious privilege of medical reputation, and concealed with much etiquette their contempt for each other’s skill.", "요컨대 그는 무게가 있어, 질병과 맞붙어 그것을 메다꽂으리라 기대할 만했고, 한편 민친 박사는 숨어 있는 질병을 찾아내 그것을 따돌리는 데 더 능할 법했다."], ["Regarding themselves as Middlemarch institutions, they were ready to combine against all innovators, and against non-professionals given to interference.", "두 사람은 의학적 명성이라는 신비로운 특권을 거의 똑같이 누렸고, 서로의 의술에 대한 경멸을 깍듯한 예의로 감추었다."], ["On this ground they were both in their hearts equally averse to Mr. Bulstrode, though Dr.", "자신들을 미들마치의 제도적 기둥으로 여기는 그들은, 모든 혁신가들에 맞서, 그리고 간섭하기 좋아하는 비전문가들에 맞서 손잡을 태세가 되어 있었다."], ["Minchin had never been in open hostility with him, and never differed from him without elaborate explanation to Mrs. Bulstrode, who had found that Dr.", "이런 점에서 두 사람은 마음속으로 똑같이 벌스트로드 씨를 꺼렸으니, 다만 민친 박사는 그와 공공연히 적대한 적이 없었고, 그와 의견이 다를 때면 언제나 벌스트로드 부인에게 정성껏 해명을 했는데, 그녀는 오직 민친 박사만이 자신의 체질을 이해한다고 여겼던 것이다."], ["Minchin alone understood her constitution. A layman who pried into the professional conduct of medical men, and was always obtruding his reforms,—though he was less directly embarrassing to the two physicians than to the surgeon-apothecaries who attended paupers by contract, was nevertheless offensive to the professional nostril as such; and Dr.", "의사들의 직업적 처신을 캐묻고 늘 자신의 개혁안을 들이미는 한 평신도는 — 계약을 맺고 극빈자들을 돌보는 외과 겸 약제사들에게보다는 이 두 내과 의사에게 직접적인 곤란을 덜 끼쳤지만 — 그럼에도 직업적 후각으로 보아 그 자체로 불쾌한 존재였다."], ["Minchin shared fully in the new pique against Bulstrode, excited by his apparent determination to patronize Lydgate. The long-established practitioners, Mr.", "그리고 민친 박사는 벌스트로드가 리드게이트를 후원하기로 작심한 듯한 데서 비롯된, 그에 대한 새로운 반감에 충분히 동참하고 있었다."], ["Wrench and Mr. Toller; were just now standing apart and having a friendly colloquy, in which they agreed that Lydgate was a jackanapes, just made to serve Bulstrode’s purpose.", "오랜 세월 자리 잡은 개업의들인 렌치 씨와 톨러 씨는 마침 한쪽에 떨어져 다정하게 담소를 나누고 있었는데, 그 자리에서 그들은 리드게이트가 그야말로 벌스트로드의 속셈에 봉사하기 딱 알맞게 생겨먹은 건방진 애송이라는 데 의견을 모았다."], ["To non-medical friends they had already concurred in praising the other young practitioner, who had come into the town on Mr. Peacock’s retirement without further recommendation than his own merits and such argument for solid professional acquirement as might be gathered from his having apparently wasted no time on other branches of knowledge.", "의료계 밖의 친구들에게 그들은 이미 입을 모아, 피콕 씨가 은퇴하면서 자기 자신의 자질과 다른 분야의 지식에 시간을 허비하지 않은 듯하다는 데서 끌어낼 수 있는 견실한 직업적 소양에 대한 논거 말고는 별다른 추천도 없이 이 도시에 들어온 그 다른 젊은 개업의를 칭찬했던 터였다."], ["It was clear that Lydgate, by not dispensing drugs, intended to cast imputations on his equals, and also to obscure the limit between his own rank as a general practitioner and that of the physicians, who, in the interest of the profession, felt bound to maintain its various grades,—especially against a man who had not been to either of the English universities and enjoyed the absence of anatomical and bedside study there, but came with a libellous pretension to experience in Edinburgh and Paris, where observation might be abundant indeed, but hardly sound.", "리드게이트가 약을 조제하지 않음으로써 동료들에게 혐의를 뒤집어씌우려는 동시에, 일반 개업의로서의 자기 등급과 내과 의사들의 등급 사이의 경계를 흐리려 한다는 것은 분명했다. 내과 의사들은 직업의 이익을 위해 그 갖가지 등급을 지켜야 한다고 느꼈으니 — 특히 영국의 두 대학 어느 쪽에도 가 본 적 없어 그곳의 해부학 및 임상 수련의 결여라는 이점을 누리지 못한 채, 관찰이야 실로 풍부할지언정 도무지 건실하다고는 할 수 없는 에든버러와 파리에서 쌓았다는 비방거리가 될 만한 경험을 내세우며 온 사람에 대해서는 더욱 그러했다."]], [["Thus it happened that on this occasion Bulstrode became identified with Lydgate, and Lydgate with Tyke; and owing to this variety of interchangeable names for the chaplaincy question, diverse minds were enabled to form the same judgment concerning it.", "이리하여 이번 일에서 벌스트로드는 리드게이트와 동일시되고, 리드게이트는 타이크와 동일시되었다. 그리고 예배당 목사직 문제를 두고 이렇게 서로 바꿔 쓸 수 있는 이름들이 다양했던 덕분에, 서로 다른 머리들이 그 문제에 대해 똑같은 판단에 이를 수 있었다."]], [["Dr. Sprague said at once bluntly to the group assembled when he entered, “I go for Farebrother.", "스프레이그 박사는 들어서면서 모여 있는 사람들에게 대뜸 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["A salary, with all my heart.", "\"나는 페어브라더에게 찬성이오."], ["But why take it from the Vicar?", "봉급이야 진심으로 찬성하지요."], ["He has none too much—has to insure his life, besides keeping house, and doing a vicar’s charities.", "한데 어째서 그걸 목사한테서 빼앗는단 말이오? 그 사람은 가진 게 넉넉하지도 않은데 — 살림을 꾸리고 목사로서 자선을 베푸는 것 말고도 생명 보험까지 들어야 하지 않소."], ["Put forty pounds in his pocket and you’ll do no harm.", "그 주머니에 사십 파운드를 넣어 준대도 해 될 건 없소."], ["He’s a good fellow, is Farebrother, with as little of the parson about him as will serve to carry orders.”", "페어브라더는 좋은 친구요, 성직을 수행할 만큼만 목사티가 날 뿐 그 이상은 아니지요.\""]], [["“Ho, ho! Doctor,”", "\"허, 허!"], ["said old Mr.", "박사 양반,\""], ["Powderell, a retired iron-monger of some standing—his interjection being something between a laugh and a Parliamentary disapproval; “we must let you have your say.", "어느 정도 명망 있는 은퇴한 철물상 노인 파우더렐 씨가 말했는데, 그 감탄사는 웃음과 의회식 반대 표명의 중간쯤 되는 것이었다. \"그 말씀이야 하시게 둡시다."], ["But what we have to consider is not anybody’s income—it’s the souls of the poor sick people”—here Mr.", "하지만 우리가 고려해야 할 것은 누구의 수입이 아니라 — 가엾은 병든 사람들의 영혼이오.\""], ["Powderell’s voice and face had a sincere pathos in them.", "— 이 대목에서 파우더렐 씨의 목소리와 얼굴에는 진실한 비애가 어려 있었다."], ["“He is a real Gospel preacher, is Mr. Tyke.", "\"타이크 씨는 진정한 복음의 설교자요."], ["I should vote against my conscience if I voted against Mr. Tyke—I should indeed.”", "내가 타이크 씨를 반대하는 표를 던진다면 양심을 거스르는 게 될 거요 — 정말 그럴 거요.\""]], [["“Mr. Tyke’s opponents have not asked any one to vote against his conscience, I believe,”", "\"타이크 씨를 반대하는 쪽은 누구에게도 양심을 거슬러 표를 던지라고 한 적이 없을 텐데요.\""], ["said Mr. Hackbutt, a rich tanner of fluent speech, whose glittering spectacles and erect hair were turned with some severity towards innocent Mr.", "유창한 화술의 부유한 무두장이 해크버트 씨가 말했는데, 번쩍이는 안경과 곧추선 머리카락이 다소 준엄하게 순진한 파우더렐 씨를 향하고 있었다."], ["Powderell. “But in my judgment it behoves us, as Directors, to consider whether we will regard it as our whole business to carry out propositions emanating from a single quarter.", "\"하지만 내 판단으로는, 우리는 이사로서 한쪽 진영에서만 나온 제안을 실행하는 것을 우리의 본분 전부로 여길지 말지를 숙고해야 마땅하오."], ["Will any member of the committee aver that he would have entertained the idea of displacing the gentleman who has always discharged the function of chaplain here, if it had not been suggested to him by parties whose disposition it is to regard every institution of this town as a machinery for carrying out their own views?", "위원회의 어느 분이든, 이 도시의 모든 기관을 자기네 견해를 관철하기 위한 기계 장치쯤으로 여기는 성향을 지닌 자들이 부추기지 않았더라도, 여기서 늘 예배당 목사 직무를 수행해 온 신사분을 몰아낼 생각을 품었으리라고 단언할 수 있겠소? 나는 누구의 동기도 따지지 않겠소."], ["I tax no man’s motives: let them lie between himself and a higher Power; but I do say, that there are influences at work here which are incompatible with genuine independence, and that a crawling servility is usually dictated by circumstances which gentlemen so conducting themselves could not afford either morally or financially to avow.", "그건 그 사람 자신과 더 높은 권능 사이에 두라지요. 그러나 분명히 말하건대, 여기에는 진정한 독립과 양립할 수 없는 영향력이 작용하고 있으며, 비굴하게 굽실거리는 짓이란 으레, 그렇게 처신하는 신사들이 도덕적으로도 재정적으로도 차마 인정할 수 없는 사정에 따라 행해지는 법이오."], ["I myself am a layman, but I have given no inconsiderable attention to the divisions in the Church and—”", "나 자신은 평신도이긴 하지만, 교회 내 분파에 대해서는 적잖이 관심을 기울여 왔고 —\""]], [["“Oh, damn the divisions!” burst in Mr.", "\"오, 그 분파들이고 뭐고 집어치우시오!\""], ["Frank Hawley, lawyer and town-clerk, who rarely presented himself at the board, but now looked in hurriedly, whip in hand.", "변호사 겸 읍 서기인 프랭크 홀리 씨가 불쑥 끼어들었는데, 그는 이사회에 좀처럼 모습을 드러내지 않다가 이제 채찍을 손에 든 채 바삐 들여다본 참이었다."], ["“We have nothing to do with them here.", "\"여기서는 그런 것과 아무 상관 없소."], ["Farebrother has been doing the work—what there was—without pay, and if pay is to be given, it should be given to him.", "페어브라더가 — 있던 일이나마 — 보수도 없이 그 일을 해 왔고, 보수를 줄 거라면 마땅히 그에게 줘야 하오."], ["I call it a confounded job to take the thing away from Farebrother.”", "페어브라더에게서 그 일을 빼앗는 것은 그야말로 괘씸한 농간이라고 봅니다.\""]], [["“I think it would be as well for gentlemen not to give their remarks a personal bearing,” said Mr.", "\"신사 여러분께서 자신의 발언에 개인적인 빛깔을 입히지 않는 게 좋을 듯합니다.\""], ["Plymdale. “I shall vote for the appointment of Mr.", "플림데일 씨가 말했다."], ["Tyke, but I should not have known, if Mr. Hackbutt hadn’t hinted it, that I was a Servile Crawler.”", "\"나는 타이크 씨의 임명에 찬성표를 던지겠소만, 해크버트 씨가 넌지시 일러 주지 않았더라면 내가 비굴하게 굽실거리는 자라는 걸 몰랐을 거요.\""]], [["“I disclaim any personalities.", "\"나는 어떤 인신공격도 하지 않았소."], ["I expressly said, if I may be allowed to repeat, or even to conclude what I was about to say—”", "분명히 말했고, 다시 말하거나 하다 만 말을 마저 끝내도 된다면 —\""]], [["“Ah, here’s Minchin!” said Mr.", "\"아, 여기 민친이 오는군요!\""], ["Frank Hawley; at which everybody turned away from Mr.", "프랭크 홀리 씨가 말했다."], ["Hackbutt, leaving him to feel the uselessness of superior gifts in Middlemarch.", "이에 모두가 해크버트 씨에게서 등을 돌렸고, 그는 미들마치에서 뛰어난 재능이란 부질없다는 것을 절감하게 되었다."], ["“Come, Doctor, I must have you on the right side, eh?”", "\"어서 오시오, 박사. 당신은 꼭 옳은 편에 서 줘야겠소, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“I hope so,” said Dr.", "\"그러기를 바라오.\""], ["Minchin, nodding and shaking hands here and there; “at whatever cost to my feelings.”", "민친 박사가 이쪽저쪽으로 고개를 끄덕이고 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"내 감정이 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 말이오.\""]], [["“If there’s any feeling here, it should be feeling for the man who is turned out, I think,”", "\"여기에 감정이란 게 있다면, 그건 쫓겨나는 사람을 위한 감정이어야 한다고 보오만.\""], ["said Mr. Frank Hawley.", "프랭크 홀리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I confess I have feelings on the other side also.", "\"솔직히 나는 다른 쪽에도 감정이 있소."], ["I have a divided esteem,” said Dr.", "내 존경심은 둘로 나뉘어 있지요.\""], ["Minchin, rubbing his hands.", "민친 박사가 두 손을 비비며 말했다."], ["“I consider Mr. Tyke an exemplary man—none more so—and I believe him to be proposed from unimpeachable motives.", "\"나는 타이크 씨를 모범적인 사람이라 여기오 — 그보다 더한 사람도 없지요 — 그리고 그가 흠잡을 데 없는 동기에서 추천되었다고 믿소."], ["I, for my part, wish that I could give him my vote.", "나로서는 그에게 표를 줄 수 있다면 좋겠소."], ["But I am constrained to take a view of the case which gives the preponderance to Mr.", "하지만 나는 페어브라더 씨의 자격에 더 무게가 실리는 견해를 취하지 않을 수 없소."], ["Farebrother’s claims. He is an amiable man, an able preacher, and has been longer among us.”", "그는 상냥한 사람이고 유능한 설교자이며 우리와 더 오래 함께해 왔으니 말이오.\""]], [["Old Mr. Powderell looked on, sad and silent.", "파우더렐 씨 노인은 슬프고 말없이 바라보았다."], ["Mr. Plymdale settled his cravat, uneasily.", "플림데일 씨는 거북한 듯 자기 넥타이를 매만졌다."]], [["“You don’t set up Farebrother as a pattern of what a clergyman ought to be, I hope,” said Mr.", "\"설마 페어브라더를 성직자의 본보기로 내세우시는 건 아니겠지요.\""], ["Larcher, the eminent carrier, who had just come in.", "방금 들어온 저명한 운송업자 라처 씨가 말했다."], ["“I have no ill-will towards him, but I think we owe something to the public, not to speak of anything higher, in these appointments.", "\"나는 그에게 악감정은 없소만, 이런 임명에서는 더 높은 무엇은 차치하고라도 대중에 대해 우리가 빚진 바가 있다고 보오."], ["In my opinion Farebrother is too lax for a clergyman.", "내 의견으로는 페어브라더는 성직자치고 너무 해이하오."], ["I don’t wish to bring up particulars against him; but he will make a little attendance here go as far as he can.”", "그를 두고 시시콜콜 들춰내고 싶지는 않소만, 그는 여기서도 출석이라는 걸 할 수 있는 한 적게 하려 들 거요.\""]], [["“And a devilish deal better than too much,”", "\"그게 지나친 것보다야 훨씬 낫지요.\""], ["said Mr. Hawley, whose bad language was notorious in that part of the county.", "그 일대에서 험한 말로 악명 높은 홀리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Sick people can’t bear so much praying and preaching.", "\"병든 사람들은 그렇게 많은 기도와 설교를 견뎌 내지 못하오."], ["And that methodistical sort of religion is bad for the spirits—bad for the inside, eh?”", "게다가 그 감리교식 종교란 게 기분에 안 좋소 — 속에도 안 좋지요, 안 그렇소?\""], ["he added, turning quickly round to the four medical men who were assembled.", "그는 모여 있는 네 명의 의사를 향해 휙 돌아서며 덧붙였다."]], [["But any answer was dispensed with by the entrance of three gentlemen, with whom there were greetings more or less cordial. These were the Reverend Edward Thesiger, Rector of St. Peter’s, Mr. Bulstrode, and our friend Mr. Brooke of Tipton, who had lately allowed himself to be put on the board of directors in his turn, but had never before attended, his attendance now being due to Mr. Bulstrode’s exertions. Lydgate was the only person still expected.", "그러나 세 신사가 들어서는 바람에 어떤 대답도 필요 없게 되었고, 그들과는 다소간 정중한 인사가 오갔다. 세 사람은 세인트 피터 교구의 주임 사제인 에드워드 데시저 목사와 벌스트로드 씨, 그리고 우리의 벗 티프턴의 브룩 씨였는데, 브룩 씨는 근래 자기 차례가 되어 이사진에 이름을 올리는 것을 허락한 터였으나 전에는 한 번도 출석한 적이 없었고, 이번 출석은 벌스트로드 씨의 노력 덕분이었다. 이제 기다리는 사람은 리드게이트뿐이었다."]], [["Every one now sat down, Mr. Bulstrode presiding, pale and self-restrained as usual.", "이제 모두 자리에 앉았고, 벌스트로드 씨가 여느 때처럼 창백하고 자제하는 모습으로 의장석에 앉았다."], ["Mr. Thesiger, a moderate evangelical, wished for the appointment of his friend Mr.", "온건한 복음주의자인 데시저 씨는 자기 벗인 타이크 씨의 임명을 바랐는데, 타이크 씨는 열성적이고 유능한 사람으로 부속 예배당에서 성직을 맡고 있어 돌볼 영혼이 그리 많지 않은 까닭에 새 직무에 쓸 시간이 넉넉했다."], ["Tyke, a zealous able man, who, officiating at a chapel of ease, had not a cure of souls too extensive to leave him ample time for the new duty.", "이런 종류의 예배당 목사직은 열렬한 의도를 품고 맡아야 바람직했다. 그것은 영적 감화를 끼칠 특별한 기회였으니까."], ["It was desirable that chaplaincies of this kind should be entered on with a fervent intention: they were peculiar opportunities for spiritual influence; and while it was good that a salary should be allotted, there was the more need for scrupulous watching lest the office should be perverted into a mere question of salary.", "그리고 봉급이 책정되는 것은 좋은 일이지만, 그만큼 이 직책이 한낱 봉급 문제로 변질되지 않도록 세심히 지켜보아야 할 필요가 있었다."], ["Mr. Thesiger’s manner had so much quiet propriety that objectors could only simmer in silence.", "데시저 씨의 태도가 어찌나 조용한 예법을 갖추었던지 반대자들은 그저 잠자코 속으로 부글거릴 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Mr. Brooke believed that everybody meant well in the matter.", "브룩 씨는 이 문제에서 모두가 좋은 뜻을 품고 있다고 믿었다."], ["He had not himself attended to the affairs of the Infirmary, though he had a strong interest in whatever was for the benefit of Middlemarch, and was most happy to meet the gentlemen present on any public question—“any public question, you know,”", "그 자신은 병원 일에 관여해 본 적이 없었지만, 미들마치에 이로운 것이라면 무엇이든 깊은 관심을 두었고, 어떤 공적 문제에 관해서든 자리에 모인 신사들을 만나게 되어 더없이 기뻤다 — \"어떤 공적 문제에 관해서든 말이오, 아시겠소.\""], ["Mr. Brooke repeated, with his nod of perfect understanding.", "브룩 씨는 완벽히 이해했다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이며 거듭 말했다."], ["“I am a good deal occupied as a magistrate, and in the collection of documentary evidence, but I regard my time as being at the disposal of the public—and, in short, my friends have convinced me that a chaplain with a salary—a salary, you know—is a very good thing, and I am happy to be able to come here and vote for the appointment of Mr.", "\"나는 치안 판사로서, 또 문서 증거를 수집하느라 꽤나 바쁘오만, 내 시간은 대중을 위해 쓸 수 있는 것이라 여기오 — 그리고 요컨대, 내 벗들이 봉급을 받는 예배당 목사 — 봉급 말이오, 아시겠소 — 가 아주 좋은 것이라고 나를 설득했고, 그래서 나는 이 자리에 와서 타이크 씨의 임명에 찬성표를 던질 수 있어 기쁘오."], ["Tyke, who, I understand, is an unexceptionable man, apostolic and eloquent and everything of that kind—and I am the last man to withhold my vote—under the circumstances, you know.”", "내가 듣기로 그는 나무랄 데 없는 사람으로, 사도다운 데다 언변이 좋고 그 밖에도 그런 온갖 미덕을 갖춘 사람이라 하니 — 그러니 나는 결코 표를 아끼지 않을 사람이오 — 이런 상황에서는 말이오, 아시겠소.\""]], [["“It seems to me that you have been crammed with one side of the question, Mr. Brooke,”", "\"제가 보기엔 브룩 씨께서 이 문제의 한쪽 이야기만 잔뜩 주입받으신 것 같소이다.\""], ["said Mr. Frank Hawley, who was afraid of nobody, and was a Tory suspicious of electioneering intentions.", "누구도 두려워하지 않으며 선거 운동의 속셈을 의심하는 토리당원인 프랭크 홀리 씨가 말했다."], ["“You don’t seem to know that one of the worthiest men we have has been doing duty as chaplain here for years without pay, and that Mr. Tyke is proposed to supersede him.”", "\"여기 우리 중에 가장 훌륭한 사람 하나가 여러 해 동안 보수도 없이 예배당 목사 직무를 해 왔는데, 그를 밀어내려고 타이크 씨가 추천되었다는 걸 모르시는 모양이군요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, Mr. Hawley,” said Mr. Bulstrode. “Mr. Brooke has been fully informed of Mr. Farebrother’s character and position.”", "\"실례지만, 홀리 씨.\" 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다. \"브룩 씨는 페어브라더 씨의 성품과 처지에 대해 충분히 설명을 들으셨소.\""]], [["“By his enemies,”", "\"그의 적들한테서 말이지요.\""], ["flashed out Mr. Hawley.", "홀리 씨가 발끈 쏘아붙였다."]], [["“I trust there is no personal hostility concerned here,”", "\"여기에 개인적인 적대감이 끼어 있지 않기를 바라오.\""], ["said Mr. Thesiger.", "데시저 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I’ll swear there is, though,”", "\"끼어 있다는 데 맹세라도 하겠소이다.\""], ["retorted Mr. Hawley.", "홀리 씨가 되받아쳤다."]], [["“Gentlemen,” said Mr.", "\"신사 여러분,\""], ["Bulstrode, in a subdued tone, “the merits of the question may be very briefly stated, and if any one present doubts that every gentleman who is about to give his vote has not been fully informed, I can now recapitulate the considerations that should weigh on either side.”", "벌스트로드 씨가 가라앉은 어조로 말했다. \"이 문제의 핵심은 아주 간략히 말씀드릴 수 있으며, 만약 표를 던지려는 신사 여러분 누구라도 충분한 설명을 듣지 못했다고 여기는 분이 있다면, 나는 지금이라도 양편에 두루 고려해야 할 점들을 다시 요약해 드릴 수 있소.\""]], [["“I don’t see the good of that,”", "\"그게 무슨 소용 있는지 모르겠소.\""], ["said Mr. Hawley.", "홀리 씨가 말했다."], ["“I suppose we all know whom we mean to vote for.", "\"우리 모두 누구에게 표를 던질지 알고 있을 텐데 말이오."], ["Any man who wants to do justice does not wait till the last minute to hear both sides of the question.", "정의를 행하고자 하는 사람이라면 마지막 순간까지 기다렸다가 양쪽 이야기를 듣는 법이 없소."], ["I have no time to lose, and I propose that the matter be put to the vote at once.”", "나는 허비할 시간이 없으니, 이 문제를 당장 표결에 부치자고 제안하오.\""]], [["A brief but still hot discussion followed before each person wrote “Tyke” or “Farebrother”", "각자가 종이쪽지에 \"타이크\"나 \"페어브라더\"라고 적어 유리잔에 밀어 넣기까지, 짧지만 그래도 후끈 달아오른 논의가 이어졌다."], ["on a piece of paper and slipped it into a glass tumbler; and in the mean time Mr. Bulstrode saw Lydgate enter.", "그러는 사이 벌스트로드 씨는 리드게이트가 들어서는 것을 보았다."]], [["“I perceive that the votes are equally divided at present,”", "\"현재로서는 표가 똑같이 갈린 것으로 보입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Bulstrode, in a clear biting voice.", "벌스트로드 씨가 또렷하고 쏘는 듯한 목소리로 말했다."], ["Then, looking up at Lydgate—", "그러고는 리드게이트를 올려다보며 —"]], [["“There is a casting-vote still to be given. It is yours, Mr.", "\"아직 던질 결정표가 하나 남아 있습니다."], ["Lydgate: will you be good enough to write?”", "그건 당신 것이오, 리드게이트 씨. 부디 적어 주시겠소?\""]], [["“The thing is settled now,”", "\"이제 결판이 났군요.\""], ["said Mr. Wrench, rising.", "렌치 씨가 자리에서 일어나며 말했다."], ["“We all know how Mr. Lydgate will vote.”", "\"리드게이트 씨가 어떻게 표를 던질지야 우리 모두 알고 있으니.\""]], [["“You seem to speak with some peculiar meaning, sir,”", "\"어딘가 묘한 뜻을 담아 말씀하시는 것 같소이다.\""], ["said Lydgate, rather defiantly, and keeping his pencil suspended.", "리드게이트가 다소 도전적으로 말하며 연필을 든 채 멈추었다."]], [["“I merely mean that you are expected to vote with Mr. Bulstrode.", "\"단지 당신이 벌스트로드 씨와 같은 편에 표를 던지리라 예상된다는 뜻이오."], ["Do you regard that meaning as offensive?”", "그 말이 모욕적으로 들리시오?\""]], [["“It may be offensive to others.", "\"다른 사람들에게는 모욕적일지도 모르지요."], ["But I shall not desist from voting with him on that account.”", "하지만 그렇다고 해서 내가 그와 같은 편에 표를 던지기를 그만두지는 않을 거요.\""], ["Lydgate immediately wrote down “Tyke.”", "리드게이트는 즉시 \"타이크\"라고 적어 넣었다."]], [["So the Rev. Walter Tyke became chaplain to the Infirmary, and Lydgate continued to work with Mr.", "이리하여 월터 타이크 목사가 병원의 예배당 목사가 되었고, 리드게이트는 계속 벌스트로드 씨와 함께 일하게 되었다."], ["Bulstrode. He was really uncertain whether Tyke were not the more suitable candidate, and yet his consciousness told him that if he had been quite free from indirect bias he should have voted for Mr.", "그는 타이크가 더 적합한 후보가 아닌지 정말로 확신이 서지 않았지만, 그러면서도 만약 자신이 간접적인 치우침에서 완전히 자유로웠다면 페어브라더 씨에게 표를 던졌으리라고 그의 양심은 일러 주었다."], ["Farebrother. The affair of the chaplaincy remained a sore point in his memory as a case in which this petty medium of Middlemarch had been too strong for him.", "예배당 목사직 일은, 미들마치의 이 옹졸한 매개가 그에게 너무도 버거웠던 사례로서 그의 기억 속에 쓰라린 응어리로 남았다."], ["How could a man be satisfied with a decision between such alternatives and under such circumstances?", "어떻게 사람이 그런 양자택일 사이에서, 그런 상황 아래 내린 결정에 만족할 수 있겠는가?"], ["No more than he can be satisfied with his hat, which he has chosen from among such shapes as the resources of the age offer him, wearing it at best with a resignation which is chiefly supported by comparison.", "그것은 시대의 형편이 그에게 내놓은 갖가지 모양 가운데서 골라 쓰는 모자에 만족할 수 없는 것이나 마찬가지였으니, 그 모자야 기껏해야 비교를 통해 겨우 지탱되는 체념으로나 쓰고 다닐 따름인 것이다."]], [["But Mr. Farebrother met him with the same friendliness as before.", "그러나 페어브라더 씨는 전과 다름없는 다정함으로 그를 대했다."], ["The character of the publican and sinner is not always practically incompatible with that of the modern Pharisee, for the majority of us scarcely see more distinctly the faultiness of our own conduct than the faultiness of our own arguments, or the dulness of our own jokes.", "세리이자 죄인의 성품이 늘 현대판 바리새인의 성품과 실제로 양립할 수 없는 것은 아니니, 우리 대다수는 우리 자신의 논리의 허점이나 우리 자신의 농담의 시시함을 보지 못하는 것 못지않게 우리 자신의 행실의 흠도 좀처럼 또렷이 보지 못하기 때문이다."], ["But the Vicar of St. Botolph’s had certainly escaped the slightest tincture of the Pharisee, and by dint of admitting to himself that he was too much as other men were, he had become remarkably unlike them in this—that he could excuse others for thinking slightly of him, and could judge impartially of their conduct even when it told against him.", "그러나 세인트 보톨프의 교구 목사는 분명 바리새인의 기미를 털끝만큼도 띠지 않았으니, 자신도 다른 사람들과 너무나 다를 바 없다고 스스로 인정함으로써, 오히려 이 점에서 그들과 놀랍도록 달라진 것이다 — 즉, 그는 남들이 자기를 대수롭지 않게 여기는 것을 용서할 수 있었고, 자기에게 불리하게 작용하는 경우에조차 그들의 행실을 공정하게 판단할 수 있었다."]], [["“The world has been too strong for me, I know,”", "\"세상이 나한테는 너무 강했지, 나도 알아.\""], ["he said one day to Lydgate.", "어느 날 그가 리드게이트에게 말했다."], ["“But then I am not a mighty man—I shall never be a man of renown.", "\"하지만 따지고 보면 나는 대단한 인물이 아닐세 — 나는 결코 이름을 떨칠 사람이 못 돼."], ["The choice of Hercules is a pretty fable; but Prodicus makes it easy work for the hero, as if the first resolves were enough.", "헤라클레스의 선택이란 그럴듯한 우화지만, 프로디코스는 첫 결심만으로 충분하다는 듯 그 영웅에게 일을 쉽게 만들어 주지."], ["Another story says that he came to hold the distaff, and at last wore the Nessus shirt.", "또 다른 이야기에서는 그가 실 잣는 물레를 잡게 되고, 끝내는 네소스의 옷을 입고 만다네."], ["I suppose one good resolve might keep a man right if everybody else’s resolve helped him.”", "모두의 결심이 한 사람을 도와준다면, 단 한 번의 굳은 결심이 그를 바른길에 붙들어 둘 수 있을지도 모르지.\""]], [["The Vicar’s talk was not always inspiriting: he had escaped being a Pharisee, but he had not escaped that low estimate of possibilities which we rather hastily arrive at as an inference from our own failure.", "목사의 이야기가 늘 기운을 북돋아 주는 것은 아니었다. 그는 바리새인이 되는 것은 면했지만, 우리 자신의 실패에서 다소 성급하게 추론으로 끌어내는 저 가능성에 대한 낮은 평가만큼은 면하지 못했다."], ["Lydgate thought that there was a pitiable infirmity of will in Mr. Farebrother.", "리드게이트는 페어브라더 씨에게 가엾을 만큼 의지가 박약한 데가 있다고 생각했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX.", "# CHAPTER XIX."]], [["“L’ altra vedete ch’ha fatto alla guancia Della sua palma, sospirando, letto.” —Purgatorio, vii.", "조지 4세가 아직 윈저의 사사로운 영역을 다스리고 있었고, 웰링턴 공작이 수상이었으며, 빈시 씨가 미들마치 옛 자치 단체의 시장이던 시절, 도러시아 브룩으로 태어난 캐소본 부인은 신혼여행으로 로마를 찾았다."], ["When George the Fourth was still reigning over the privacies of Windsor, when the Duke of Wellington was Prime Minister, and Mr. Vincy was mayor of the old corporation in Middlemarch, Mrs.", "그 시절 세상은 대체로 지금보다 사십 년만큼 선과 악에 더 무지했다."], ["Casaubon, born Dorothea Brooke, had taken her wedding journey to Rome. In those days the world in general was more ignorant of good and evil by forty years than it is at present.", "여행자들은 머릿속에도 호주머니 속에도 기독교 미술에 관한 충분한 정보를 지니고 다니는 일이 드물었으며, 당대 가장 명석한 영국 비평가조차 승천하는 성모의 꽃으로 붉게 물든 무덤을 화가의 상상에서 비롯된 장식용 꽃병으로 오인할 정도였다."], ["Travellers did not often carry full information on Christian art either in their heads or their pockets; and even the most brilliant English critic of the day mistook the flower-flushed tomb of the ascended Virgin for an ornamental vase due to the painter’s fancy. Romanticism, which has helped to fill some dull blanks with love and knowledge, had not yet penetrated the times with its leaven and entered into everybody’s food; it was fermenting still as a distinguishable vigorous enthusiasm in certain long-haired German artists at Rome, and the youth of other nations who worked or idled near them were sometimes caught in the spreading movement.", "어떤 무미건조한 공백을 사랑과 지식으로 채우는 데 일조한 낭만주의도 아직은 그 누룩으로 시대에 스며들어 모든 이의 양식 속으로 들어가지는 못했으니, 그것은 여전히 로마에 머무는 어떤 장발의 독일 화가들 사이에서 뚜렷이 구별되는 활기찬 열정으로 발효되고 있었고, 그들 곁에서 일하거나 빈둥거리던 다른 나라의 젊은이들이 이따금 그 번져 가는 운동에 휩쓸리곤 했다."]], [["One fine morning a young man whose hair was not immoderately long, but abundant and curly, and who was otherwise English in his equipment, had just turned his back on the Belvedere Torso in the Vatican and was looking out on the magnificent view of the mountains from the adjoining round vestibule.", "어느 화창한 아침, 머리카락이 지나치게 길지는 않으나 숱이 많고 곱슬곱슬했으며 그 밖의 차림새는 영국식이던 한 젊은이가 바티칸의 벨베데레 토르소에서 막 등을 돌려, 인접한 둥근 현관에서 펼쳐지는 장엄한 산의 풍경을 내다보고 있었다."], ["He was sufficiently absorbed not to notice the approach of a dark-eyed, animated German who came up to him and placing a hand on his shoulder, said with a strong accent, “Come here, quick!", "그는 충분히 몰입해 있어서, 검은 눈에 활기 넘치는 한 독일인이 다가와 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 강한 억양으로 이렇게 말하는 것도 알아차리지 못했다. \"이리 와요, 빨리!"], ["else she will have changed her pose.”", "안 그러면 저 여자가 자세를 바꿔 버릴 테니.\""]], [["Quickness was ready at the call, and the two figures passed lightly along by the Meleager, towards the hall where the reclining Ariadne, then called the Cleopatra, lies in the marble voluptuousness of her beauty, the drapery folding around her with a petal-like ease and tenderness.", "그 부름에 곧장 민첩함이 응했고, 두 사람은 멜레아그로스 상을 지나 가볍게 걸어, 그 무렵 클레오파트라라 불리던, 비스듬히 누운 아리아드네가 대리석의 관능적인 아름다움 속에 누워 있는 홀로 향했다. 옷자락이 꽃잎처럼 편안하고 부드럽게 그녀를 감싸고 있었다."], ["They were just in time to see another figure standing against a pedestal near the reclining marble: a breathing blooming girl, whose form, not shamed by the Ariadne, was clad in Quakerish gray drapery; her long cloak, fastened at the neck, was thrown backward from her arms, and one beautiful ungloved hand pillowed her cheek, pushing somewhat backward the white beaver bonnet which made a sort of halo to her face around the simply braided dark-brown hair.", "그들은 마침 그 누운 대리석상 가까이 받침대에 기대어 선 또 다른 형상을 볼 수 있었다. 숨 쉬며 피어나는 한 소녀, 그 자태는 아리아드네에 견주어도 부끄럽지 않았으며, 퀘이커교도 같은 회색 옷을 두르고 있었다."], ["She was not looking at the sculpture, probably not thinking of it: her large eyes were fixed dreamily on a streak of sunlight which fell across the floor.", "목에서 여민 긴 망토는 양팔에서 뒤로 젖혀져 있었고, 장갑을 벗은 아름다운 한 손이 뺨을 받친 채, 소박하게 땋은 짙은 갈색 머리 둘레로 얼굴에 일종의 후광을 이루던 흰 비버 모자를 다소 뒤로 밀어 올리고 있었다. 그녀는 조각상을 바라보고 있지 않았고, 아마 그것을 생각하지도 않는 듯했다."], ["But she became conscious of the two strangers who suddenly paused as if to contemplate the Cleopatra, and, without looking at them, immediately turned away to join a maid-servant and courier who were loitering along the hall at a little distance off.", "그녀의 커다란 눈은 바닥을 가로질러 비치는 한 줄기 햇살에 꿈꾸듯 고정되어 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 마치 클레오파트라를 응시하려는 듯 돌연 걸음을 멈춘 두 낯선 이를 의식하게 되었고, 그들을 바라보지도 않은 채 곧바로 몸을 돌려, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 홀을 따라 어슬렁거리던 하녀와 안내원에게로 향했다."]], [["“What do you think of that for a fine bit of antithesis?”", "\"저거야말로 멋진 대비라고 생각하지 않소?\" 독일인이 친구의 얼굴에서 공감 어린 감탄을 찾으면서도 다른 대답을 기다리지 않고 청산유수로 말을 이었다."], ["said the German, searching in his friend’s face for responding admiration, but going on volubly without waiting for any other answer.", "\"저기 고대의 아름다움이 누워 있소. 죽어서도 시체 같지 않고, 관능적 완벽함의 충만한 만족 속에 멈춰 있지."], ["“There lies antique beauty, not corpse-like even in death, but arrested in the complete contentment of its sensuous perfection: and here stands beauty in its breathing life, with the consciousness of Christian centuries in its bosom.", "그리고 여기에는 숨 쉬는 생명 속의 아름다움이, 기독교 수 세기의 의식을 가슴에 품은 채 서 있소. 하지만 저 여자는 수녀처럼 차려입어야 해."], ["But she should be dressed as a nun; I think she looks almost what you call a Quaker; I would dress her as a nun in my picture.", "내가 보기엔 당신들이 퀘이커교도라 부르는 모습에 거의 가깝군. 내 그림에서는 그녀를 수녀로 입히겠소."], ["However, she is married; I saw her wedding-ring on that wonderful left hand, otherwise I should have thought the sallow Geistlicher was her father.", "그렇지만 그녀는 결혼했어. 저 놀라운 왼손에 낀 결혼반지를 봤거든."], ["I saw him parting from her a good while ago, and just now I found her in that magnificent pose.", "아니었으면 저 누르께한 성직자가 그녀의 아버지인 줄 알았을 거요. 한참 전에 그가 그녀와 헤어지는 걸 봤고, 방금 저 장엄한 자세로 있는 그녀를 발견했지."], ["Only think!", "생각해 보시오!"], ["he is perhaps rich, and would like to have her portrait taken.", "어쩌면 그는 부자이고, 그녀의 초상을 그리게 하고 싶어 할지도 모르오."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["it is no use looking after her—there she goes!", "그녀를 좇아 봐야 소용없군 — 저기 가 버리잖소!"], ["Let us follow her home!”", "그녀를 따라 집까지 갑시다!\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 안 돼요.\""], ["said his companion, with a little frown.", "그의 동행이 살짝 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["“You are singular, Ladislaw.", "\"당신 별나군, 래디슬로."], ["You look struck together.", "충격이라도 받은 표정이야."], ["Do you know her?”", "저 여자를 아시오?\""]], [["“I know that she is married to my cousin,”", "\"내 사촌과 결혼한 여자라는 건 압니다.\""], ["said Will Ladislaw, sauntering down the hall with a preoccupied air, while his German friend kept at his side and watched him eagerly.", "윌 래디슬로가 무언가에 골몰한 듯한 기색으로 홀을 따라 천천히 걸으며 말했고, 독일인 친구는 그의 곁에 붙어 열렬히 그를 살폈다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["the Geistlicher?", "그 성직자가?"], ["He looks more like an uncle—a more useful sort of relation.”", "그 사람은 외삼촌처럼 보이던데 — 좀 더 쓸모 있는 부류의 친척 말이오.\""]], [["“He is not my uncle.", "\"그는 내 외삼촌이 아닙니다."], ["I tell you he is my second cousin,”", "그는 내 육촌이라고 말했잖아요.\""], ["said Ladislaw, with some irritation.", "래디슬로가 다소 짜증스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Schön, schön.", "\"쇤, 쇤."], ["Don’t be snappish.", "톡 쏘지 마시오."], ["You are not angry with me for thinking Mrs. Second-Cousin the most perfect young Madonna I ever saw?”", "내가 그 육촌 부인을 여태껏 본 가장 완벽한 젊은 마돈나라고 생각한다고 해서 화내는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["“Angry?", "\"화내냐고요?"], ["nonsense.", "말도 안 돼요."], ["I have only seen her once before, for a couple of minutes, when my cousin introduced her to me, just before I left England.", "그녀를 본 건 전에 딱 한 번, 그것도 이삼 분뿐이었습니다. 내가 영국을 떠나기 직전에 사촌이 그녀를 소개해 줬을 때였죠."], ["They were not married then.", "그때는 결혼한 사이가 아니었어요."], ["I didn’t know they were coming to Rome.”", "그들이 로마에 오는 줄도 몰랐습니다.\""]], [["“But you will go to see them now—you will find out what they have for an address—since you know the name.", "\"하지만 이제 그들을 보러 가야지 — 이름을 아니까 주소가 뭔지 알아낼 수 있지 않소."], ["Shall we go to the post?", "우체국에 가 볼까?"], ["And you could speak about the portrait.”", "그러면 초상화 이야기를 꺼낼 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“Confound you, Naumann!", "\"빌어먹을, 나우만!"], ["I don’t know what I shall do.", "내가 어떻게 할지 나도 모르겠소."], ["I am not so brazen as you.”", "난 당신만큼 뻔뻔하지 못하니까.\""]], [["“Bah!", "\"흥!"], ["that is because you are dilettantish and amateurish.", "그건 당신이 어설픈 호사가에 풋내기이기 때문이오."], ["If you were an artist, you would think of Mistress Second-Cousin as antique form animated by Christian sentiment—a sort of Christian Antigone—sensuous force controlled by spiritual passion.”", "당신이 화가였다면, 육촌 부인을 기독교적 정서로 생기를 얻은 고대의 형상으로 — 일종의 기독교적 안티고네로, 정신적 열정에 통제된 관능적 힘으로 여겼을 거요.\""]], [["“Yes, and that your painting her was the chief outcome of her existence—the divinity passing into higher completeness and all but exhausted in the act of covering your bit of canvas.", "\"그래요, 그리고 당신이 그녀를 그리는 것이 그녀가 존재하는 주된 결실이라고도 여겼겠지 — 신성이 더 높은 완성으로 옮겨 가, 당신의 그 캔버스 조각을 덮는 행위에서 거의 소진되어 버린다고 말이오."], ["I am amateurish if you like: I do not think that all the universe is straining towards the obscure significance of your pictures.”", "정 그렇다면 나는 풋내기로 두시오. 나는 온 우주가 당신 그림들의 모호한 의미를 향해 안간힘을 쓰고 있다고는 생각하지 않으니까.\""]], [["“But it is, my dear!—so far as it is straining through me, Adolf Naumann: that stands firm,”", "\"하지만 그렇다오, 이 친구야! — 그것이 나 아돌프 나우만을 통해 안간힘을 쓰는 한에서는 말이지."], ["said the good-natured painter, putting a hand on Ladislaw’s shoulder, and not in the least disturbed by the unaccountable touch of ill-humor in his tone.", "그건 확고하오.\" 사람 좋은 화가가 래디슬로의 어깨에 손을 얹으며, 상대의 말투에 깃든 까닭 모를 언짢음에는 조금도 동요하지 않고 말했다."], ["“See now!", "\"자 보시오!"], ["My existence presupposes the existence of the whole universe—does it not?", "나의 존재는 온 우주의 존재를 전제로 하오 — 그렇지 않소?"], ["and my function is to paint—and as a painter I have a conception which is altogether genialisch, of your great-aunt or second grandmother as a subject for a picture; therefore, the universe is straining towards that picture through that particular hook or claw which it puts forth in the shape of me—not true?”", "그리고 내 본분은 그림을 그리는 것이고 — 화가로서 나는 당신의 큰이모님인지 둘째 할머니인지를 그림의 소재로 삼는, 전적으로 천재적인 착상을 품고 있소. 따라서 우주는 나라는 모양으로 내미는 바로 그 갈고리 혹은 발톱을 통해 그 그림을 향해 안간힘을 쓰고 있는 것이오 — 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“But how if another claw in the shape of me is straining to thwart it?—the case is a little less simple then.”", "\"그런데 나라는 모양을 한 또 다른 발톱이 그것을 방해하려고 안간힘을 쓴다면 어쩌겠소? — 그러면 문제가 좀 덜 단순해지지 않소.\""]], [["“Not at all: the result of the struggle is the same thing—picture or no picture—logically.”", "\"전혀 아니오. 그 다툼의 결과는 같은 것이오 — 그림이 되든 안 되든 — 논리적으로 말이지.\""]], [["Will could not resist this imperturbable temper, and the cloud in his face broke into sunshiny laughter.", "윌은 이 흔들림 없는 기질에 당해 낼 수가 없었고, 얼굴에 드리웠던 먹구름이 환한 웃음으로 터져 나왔다."]], [["“Come now, my friend—you will help?”", "\"자 어서, 이 친구야 — 도와줄 거지?\""], ["said Naumann, in a hopeful tone.", "나우만이 기대에 찬 어조로 말했다."]], [["“No; nonsense, Naumann!", "\"아니요. 말도 안 돼요, 나우만!"], ["English ladies are not at everybody’s service as models.", "영국 숙녀들이 누구나 부른다고 모델이 되어 주는 건 아니오."], ["And you want to express too much with your painting.", "게다가 당신은 그림으로 너무 많은 걸 표현하려 들어요."], ["You would only have made a better or worse portrait with a background which every connoisseur would give a different reason for or against.", "당신은 그저 어떤 감식가든 찬반의 이유를 제각기 댈 만한 배경을 깔아, 더 낫거나 못한 초상화 하나를 만들어 냈을 뿐일 거요."], ["And what is a portrait of a woman?", "게다가 한 여인의 초상이 다 무엇이란 말이오?"], ["Your painting and Plastik are poor stuff after all.", "당신의 회화니 조형이니 하는 것은 결국 변변찮은 것들이오."], ["They perturb and dull conceptions instead of raising them.", "그것들은 관념을 고양하기는커녕 어지럽히고 무디게 만드니까."], ["Language is a finer medium.”", "언어가 더 섬세한 매체요.\""]], [["“Yes, for those who can’t paint,”", "\"그렇겠지, 그림을 못 그리는 사람들에게는 말이오.\""], ["said Naumann.", "나우만이 말했다."], ["“There you have perfect right.", "\"그 점에선 당신이 전적으로 옳소."], ["I did not recommend you to paint, my friend.”", "나는 당신에게 그림을 그리라고 권하지는 않았소, 친구.\""]], [["The amiable artist carried his sting, but Ladislaw did not choose to appear stung.", "상냥한 화가는 가시를 품고 있었으나, 래디슬로는 찔린 티를 내려 하지 않았다."], ["He went on as if he had not heard.", "그는 마치 듣지 못한 듯이 말을 이었다."]], [["“Language gives a fuller image, which is all the better for being vague.", "\"언어는 더 충만한 이미지를 주는데, 모호한 까닭에 오히려 더 좋소."], ["After all, the true seeing is within; and painting stares at you with an insistent imperfection.", "결국 참된 봄이란 내면에 있고, 그림은 집요한 불완전함을 지닌 채 당신을 빤히 쳐다보지요."], ["I feel that especially about representations of women.", "나는 여인을 그린 것들에서 특히 그것을 느낍니다."], ["As if a woman were a mere colored superficies!", "마치 여인이 한낱 색칠된 표면에 불과한 것처럼 말이오!"], ["You must wait for movement and tone.", "움직임과 음색을 기다려야 해요."], ["There is a difference in their very breathing: they change from moment to moment.—This woman whom you have just seen, for example: how would you paint her voice, pray?", "바로 그 숨결에도 차이가 있으니, 그들은 순간순간 달라지지요. 가령 방금 당신이 본 저 여인 말이오."], ["But her voice is much diviner than anything you have seen of her.”", "그녀의 목소리를 대체 어떻게 그리겠소? 한데 그녀의 목소리는 당신이 본 그녀의 그 무엇보다도 훨씬 더 신성하다오.\""]], [["“I see, I see.", "\"알겠소, 알겠어."], ["You are jealous.", "당신은 질투하는 거요."], ["No man must presume to think that he can paint your ideal.", "어느 누구도 당신의 이상을 그릴 수 있다고 감히 생각해선 안 되는 거지."], ["This is serious, my friend!", "이거 심각하군, 친구!"], ["Your great-aunt!", "당신의 큰이모라니!"], ["‘Der Neffe als Onkel’ in a tragic sense—ungeheuer!”", "비극적인 의미에서 '삼촌 노릇 하는 조카'라, 엄청나군!\""]], [["“You and I shall quarrel, Naumann, if you call that lady my aunt again.”", "\"또 한 번 그 부인을 내 이모라고 부르면, 나우만, 우리 사이가 틀어질 거요.\""]], [["“How is she to be called then?”", "\"그럼 뭐라고 불러야 하오?\""]], [["“Mrs. Casaubon.”", "\"캐소본 부인이오.\""]], [["“Good.", "\"좋소."], ["Suppose I get acquainted with her in spite of you, and find that she very much wishes to be painted?”", "당신이 반대하든 말든 내가 그녀와 알게 되어, 그녀가 자기를 그려 주기를 몹시 바란다는 걸 알아낸다면 어쩌겠소?\""]], [["“Yes, suppose!”", "\"그래, 그렇다면 어디 해 보시오!\""], ["said Will Ladislaw, in a contemptuous undertone, intended to dismiss the subject.", "윌 래디슬로가 그 화제를 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 경멸 어린 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["He was conscious of being irritated by ridiculously small causes, which were half of his own creation.", "그는 우스울 만큼 사소한 일들에, 그것도 절반은 스스로 만들어 낸 일들에 짜증이 나 있음을 의식했다."], ["Why was he making any fuss about Mrs. Casaubon?", "어째서 그는 캐소본 부인을 두고 이런 야단법석을 떨고 있단 말인가?"], ["And yet he felt as if something had happened to him with regard to her.", "그러면서도 그는 그녀와 관련해 자신에게 무언가 일이 일어난 것 같은 느낌이 들었다."], ["There are characters which are continually creating collisions and nodes for themselves in dramas which nobody is prepared to act with them.", "누구도 함께 연기할 준비가 되어 있지 않은 극 속에서, 끊임없이 스스로를 위한 충돌과 매듭을 만들어 내는 성격들이 있다."], ["Their susceptibilities will clash against objects that remain innocently quiet.", "그들의 예민함은 천진하게 가만히 있을 뿐인 대상들에 부딪쳐 부서지는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XX.", "# CHAPTER XX."]], [["“A child forsaken, waking suddenly, Whose gaze afeard on all things round doth rove, And seeth only that it cannot see The meeting eyes of love.”", "버림받은 아이가 문득 깨어나 무서움에 찬 눈길로 주위 만물을 헤매다가 오직 보지 못함만을 볼 뿐, 사랑의 마주치는 눈길을."]], [["Two hours later, Dorothea was seated in an inner room or boudoir of a handsome apartment in the Via Sistina.", "두 시간 뒤, 도러시아는 비아 시스티나에 있는 어느 멋진 거처의 안쪽 방, 곧 규방에 앉아 있었다."]], [["I am sorry to add that she was sobbing bitterly, with such abandonment to this relief of an oppressed heart as a woman habitually controlled by pride on her own account and thoughtfulness for others will sometimes allow herself when she feels securely alone.", "유감스럽게도 그녀는 비통하게 흐느끼고 있었다고 덧붙여야겠다. 자존심 때문에 자신을 늘 억제하고 남들을 배려하는 데 마음 쓰는 여인이, 안전하게 혼자라고 느낄 때 이따금 스스로에게 허락하는, 짓눌린 가슴을 풀어내는 그런 내맡김으로 말이다."], ["And Mr. Casaubon was certain to remain away for some time at the Vatican.", "그리고 캐소본 씨는 한동안 바티칸에서 돌아오지 않을 것이 분명했다."]], [["Yet Dorothea had no distinctly shapen grievance that she could state even to herself; and in the midst of her confused thought and passion, the mental act that was struggling forth into clearness was a self-accusing cry that her feeling of desolation was the fault of her own spiritual poverty.", "하지만 도러시아에게는 스스로에게조차 분명히 말할 수 있는 또렷한 형태의 불만이 없었다. 혼란스러운 생각과 격정 한가운데서, 또렷함을 향해 솟아 나오려 애쓰던 정신적 행위는, 자신이 느끼는 황량함이 자신의 영적 빈곤 탓이라는 자책의 외침이었다."], ["She had married the man of her choice, and with the advantage over most girls that she had contemplated her marriage chiefly as the beginning of new duties: from the very first she had thought of Mr.", "그녀는 자신이 선택한 남자와 결혼했고, 대부분의 소녀들보다 유리한 점은 자신의 결혼을 무엇보다 새로운 의무의 시작으로 바라보았다는 데 있었다."], ["Casaubon as having a mind so much above her own, that he must often be claimed by studies which she could not entirely share; moreover, after the brief narrow experience of her girlhood she was beholding Rome, the city of visible history, where the past of a whole hemisphere seems moving in funeral procession with strange ancestral images and trophies gathered from afar.", "처음부터 그녀는 캐소본 씨가 자신보다 훨씬 높은 정신의 소유자라서, 자신이 온전히 함께할 수 없는 연구에 자주 매여 있을 수밖에 없으리라 여겼다. 게다가 짧고도 협소했던 처녀 시절의 경험을 지나, 이제 그녀는 눈에 보이는 역사의 도시 로마를 바라보고 있었으니, 그곳에서는 온 반구의 과거가 먼 곳에서 모아 온 기이한 조상들의 형상과 전리품을 거느리고 장송 행렬처럼 움직이는 듯했다."]], [["But this stupendous fragmentariness heightened the dreamlike strangeness of her bridal life.", "하지만 이 어마어마한 파편성은 그녀의 신혼 생활의 꿈결 같은 낯섦을 한층 더 짙게 만들었다."], ["Dorothea had now been five weeks in Rome, and in the kindly mornings when autumn and winter seemed to go hand in hand like a happy aged couple one of whom would presently survive in chiller loneliness, she had driven about at first with Mr. Casaubon, but of late chiefly with Tantripp and their experienced courier.", "도러시아는 이제 로마에 온 지 다섯 주가 되었고, 가을과 겨울이 행복한 노부부처럼 손을 맞잡고 함께 걷는 듯한—머지않아 그중 하나는 더 쌀쌀한 외로움 속에 홀로 남게 될—온화한 아침이면, 처음에는 캐소본 씨와 함께, 그러나 근래에는 주로 탠트립과 노련한 안내인과 함께 마차를 타고 돌아다녔다."], ["She had been led through the best galleries, had been taken to the chief points of view, had been shown the grandest ruins and the most glorious churches, and she had ended by oftenest choosing to drive out to the Campagna where she could feel alone with the earth and sky, away-from the oppressive masquerade of ages, in which her own life too seemed to become a masque with enigmatical costumes.", "그녀는 최고의 미술관들을 두루 안내받았고, 주요한 전망 지점들로 인도되었으며, 가장 웅장한 폐허와 가장 영광스러운 교회들을 구경했지만, 결국 가장 자주 택하게 된 것은 마차를 몰아 캄파냐로 나가는 일이었다. 그곳에서는 여러 시대가 빚어내는 답답한 가장무도회에서 벗어나 대지와 하늘과 더불어 홀로 있는 듯한 느낌을 받을 수 있었으니, 그 가장무도회 속에서는 그녀 자신의 삶마저도 수수께끼 같은 의상을 걸친 가면극이 되어 버리는 것 같았다."]], [["To those who have looked at Rome with the quickening power of a knowledge which breathes a growing soul into all historic shapes, and traces out the suppressed transitions which unite all contrasts, Rome may still be the spiritual centre and interpreter of the world.", "모든 역사적 형상에 자라나는 영혼을 불어넣고, 모든 대조를 잇는 억눌린 이행의 자취를 더듬어 내는 지식의 생기 어린 힘으로 로마를 바라본 이들에게는, 로마가 여전히 세계의 영적 중심이자 해석자일 수 있으리라. 하지만 그들로 하여금 한 가지 역사적 대조를 더 떠올리게 하라."], ["But let them conceive one more historical contrast: the gigantic broken revelations of that Imperial and Papal city thrust abruptly on the notions of a girl who had been brought up in English and Swiss Puritanism, fed on meagre Protestant histories and on art chiefly of the hand-screen sort; a girl whose ardent nature turned all her small allowance of knowledge into principles, fusing her actions into their mould, and whose quick emotions gave the most abstract things the quality of a pleasure or a pain; a girl who had lately become a wife, and from the enthusiastic acceptance of untried duty found herself plunged in tumultuous preoccupation with her personal lot.", "그 제국과 교황의 도시가 보여 주는 거대하고 부서진 계시들이, 영국과 스위스의 청교도주의 속에서 자라났고, 빈약한 신교도 역사서와 주로 부채에 그려진 그림 같은 미술로 양육된 한 소녀의 관념 위로 느닷없이 들이닥치는 것을. 그 소녀는 열렬한 천성으로 자신이 가진 보잘것없는 지식의 몫을 모두 원칙으로 바꾸고, 자신의 행동을 그 틀 속으로 녹여 부었으며, 빠르게 솟는 감정으로 가장 추상적인 것들에까지 기쁨이나 고통의 성질을 부여하던 소녀였다."], ["The weight of unintelligible Rome might lie easily on bright nymphs to whom it formed a background for the brilliant picnic of Anglo-foreign society; but Dorothea had no such defence against deep impressions.", "또한 최근에 아내가 되어, 시험해 보지 않은 의무를 열정적으로 받아들였다가 어느새 자신의 개인적 운명에 대한 격동하는 사로잡힘 속으로 빠져든 소녀였다. 알 수 없는 로마의 무게는, 그곳을 영국과 외국 사교계가 펼치는 화려한 소풍의 배경쯤으로 여기는 명랑한 요정들에게라면 가볍게 얹혔을지도 모른다."], ["Ruins and basilicas, palaces and colossi, set in the midst of a sordid present, where all that was living and warm-blooded seemed sunk in the deep degeneracy of a superstition divorced from reverence; the dimmer but yet eager Titanic life gazing and struggling on walls and ceilings; the long vistas of white forms whose marble eyes seemed to hold the monotonous light of an alien world: all this vast wreck of ambitious ideals, sensuous and spiritual, mixed confusedly with the signs of breathing forgetfulness and degradation, at first jarred her as with an electric shock, and then urged themselves on her with that ache belonging to a glut of confused ideas which check the flow of emotion.", "하지만 도러시아에게는 깊은 인상에 맞설 그런 방어막이 없었다. 폐허와 바실리카, 궁전과 거상들이, 살아 있고 따뜻한 피가 도는 모든 것이 경외심과 결별한 미신의 깊은 타락 속에 가라앉은 듯한 누추한 현재 한복판에 놓여 있었다."], ["Forms both pale and glowing took possession of her young sense, and fixed themselves in her memory even when she was not thinking of them, preparing strange associations which remained through her after-years.", "벽과 천장 위에서는 한결 흐릿하지만 여전히 열렬한 타이탄적 생명이 응시하며 분투하고 있었고, 길게 늘어선 하얀 형상들의 대리석 눈은 낯선 세계의 단조로운 빛을 머금은 듯했다. 육체적이고 영적인 야심 찬 이상들이 빚어낸 이 거대한 잔해 전부가, 숨 쉬는 망각과 타락의 징표들과 어지러이 뒤섞여, 처음에는 전기 충격을 받은 듯 그녀를 거슬리게 했고, 이윽고 감정의 흐름을 막아 버리는 혼란스러운 관념의 과잉에 따르는 그 통증으로 그녀를 짓눌렀다."], ["Our moods are apt to bring with them images which succeed each other like the magic-lantern pictures of a doze; and in certain states of dull forlornness Dorothea all her life continued to see the vastness of St.", "창백하면서도 빛나는 형상들이 그녀의 젊은 감각을 사로잡았고, 그녀가 그것들을 생각하지 않을 때조차 기억 속에 박혀, 그녀의 훗날 여러 해 동안 남게 될 기이한 연상들을 마련하고 있었다."], ["Peter’s, the huge bronze canopy, the excited intention in the attitudes and garments of the prophets and evangelists in the mosaics above, and the red drapery which was being hung for Christmas spreading itself everywhere like a disease of the retina.", "우리의 기분은 선잠 속 환등기 그림처럼 연이어 떠오르는 심상들을 데려오기 마련이다. 도러시아는 평생토록, 어떤 둔탁하고 쓸쓸한 상태에 빠질 때면 성 베드로 대성당의 광대함과, 거대한 청동 천개와, 위쪽 모자이크 속 예언자들과 복음서 저자들의 자세와 의복에 깃든 흥분한 의도, 그리고 성탄절을 위해 걸리던 붉은 휘장이 망막의 질병처럼 사방에 번지던 모습을 계속 보게 되었다."]], [["Not that this inward amazement of Dorothea’s was anything very exceptional: many souls in their young nudity are tumbled out among incongruities and left to “find their feet”", "도러시아의 이 내면적 경악이 그리 유별난 것은 아니었다."], ["among them, while their elders go about their business. Nor can I suppose that when Mrs.", "많은 영혼들이 젊은 알몸 그대로 온갖 부조화 속으로 내던져진 채, 어른들이 제 일을 보러 다니는 동안 그것들 사이에서 \"제 발로 서는 법\"을 알아내도록 내버려진다."], ["Casaubon is discovered in a fit of weeping six weeks after her wedding, the situation will be regarded as tragic.", "또 캐소본 부인이 결혼 육 주 만에 울음을 터뜨린 모습으로 발견된다고 해서, 그 상황이 비극으로 여겨지리라고 나는 생각하지 않는다."], ["Some discouragement, some faintness of heart at the new real future which replaces the imaginary, is not unusual, and we do not expect people to be deeply moved by what is not unusual.", "상상했던 미래를 대신하는 새로운 현실의 미래 앞에서 느끼는 약간의 낙담, 약간의 가슴 내려앉음은 드문 일이 아니며, 우리는 사람들이 드물지 않은 일에 깊이 동요하리라고 기대하지 않는다."], ["That element of tragedy which lies in the very fact of frequency, has not yet wrought itself into the coarse emotion of mankind; and perhaps our frames could hardly bear much of it.", "바로 그 빈번함이라는 사실 자체에 깃든 비극의 요소는, 아직 인류의 거친 감정 속으로 스며들지 못했다. 그리고 어쩌면 우리의 몸은 그것을 많이 견뎌 내기 어려울 것이다."], ["If we had a keen vision and feeling of all ordinary human life, it would be like hearing the grass grow and the squirrel’s heart beat, and we should die of that roar which lies on the other side of silence.", "만약 우리가 모든 평범한 인간의 삶을 예리하게 보고 느낄 수 있다면, 그것은 풀이 자라는 소리와 다람쥐의 심장이 뛰는 소리를 듣는 것과 같으리니, 우리는 침묵의 저편에 도사린 그 굉음에 그만 죽고 말 것이다."], ["As it is, the quickest of us walk about well wadded with stupidity.", "사정이 이러하므로, 우리 가운데 가장 영민한 자조차 어리석음으로 두툼하게 둘러싸인 채 살아간다."]], [["However, Dorothea was crying, and if she had been required to state the cause, she could only have done so in some such general words as I have already used: to have been driven to be more particular would have been like trying to give a history of the lights and shadows, for that new real future which was replacing the imaginary drew its material from the endless minutiae by which her view of Mr.", "어쨌든 도러시아는 울고 있었다. 만약 그 이유를 대라고 했다면, 그녀는 내가 이미 사용한 것과 같은 두루뭉술한 말로밖에 대답할 수 없었으리라."], ["Casaubon and her wifely relation, now that she was married to him, was gradually changing with the secret motion of a watch-hand from what it had been in her maiden dream.", "더 구체적으로 말하도록 떠밀렸다면 그것은 빛과 그림자의 내력을 들려주려 애쓰는 일과 같았을 터인데, 상상을 대신해 가던 그 새로운 현실의 미래는, 이제 그와 결혼한 처지에서 캐소본 씨와 아내로서의 관계를 바라보는 그녀의 시각이 처녀 시절의 꿈속에서와는 다른 것으로 시곗바늘의 은밀한 움직임처럼 차츰 변해 가게 만드는 끝없는 사소함들에서 그 재료를 끌어왔기 때문이다."], ["It was too early yet for her fully to recognize or at least admit the change, still more for her to have readjusted that devotedness which was so necessary a part of her mental life that she was almost sure sooner or later to recover it.", "그 변화를 온전히 인식하거나 적어도 인정하기에는 아직 너무 일렀고, 그녀의 정신적 삶에 너무도 필수적인 부분이어서 조만간 분명 되찾으리라 거의 확신하던 그 헌신을 다시 가다듬기에는 더더욱 일렀다."], ["Permanent rebellion, the disorder of a life without some loving reverent resolve, was not possible to her; but she was now in an interval when the very force of her nature heightened its confusion.", "영속적인 반항, 사랑으로 가득한 경건한 결심이 없는 삶의 무질서란 그녀에게 가능한 일이 아니었다. 하지만 그녀는 지금 자기 천성의 바로 그 힘이 혼란을 한층 키우는 그런 간극에 놓여 있었다."], ["In this way, the early months of marriage often are times of critical tumult—whether that of a shrimp-pool or of deeper waters—which afterwards subsides into cheerful peace.", "이런 식으로 결혼 초기의 몇 달은 흔히 위기의 격동기가 되는데—그것이 새우 웅덩이의 동요든 더 깊은 물의 동요든—이윽고 그것은 가라앉아 명랑한 평온으로 잦아든다."]], [["But was not Mr. Casaubon just as learned as before?", "하지만 캐소본 씨가 예전만큼 박식하지 않았던가?"], ["Had his forms of expression changed, or his sentiments become less laudable?", "그의 표현 방식이 달라졌던가, 아니면 그의 감정이 덜 갸륵해졌던가?"], ["Oh waywardness of womanhood!", "오, 여인됨의 변덕이여!"], ["did his chronology fail him, or his ability to state not only a theory but the names of those who held it; or his provision for giving the heads of any subject on demand?", "그의 연대기 지식이 그를 저버렸던가, 아니면 어떤 이론뿐 아니라 그 이론을 주창한 이들의 이름까지 댈 줄 아는 능력이 사라졌던가, 아니면 요청만 하면 어떤 주제든 그 요점들을 짚어 줄 채비가 모자랐던가?"], ["And was not Rome the place in all the world to give free play to such accomplishments?", "그리고 로마야말로 그러한 재능을 마음껏 펼칠 만한, 세상에 둘도 없는 곳이 아니었던가?"], ["Besides, had not Dorothea’s enthusiasm especially dwelt on the prospect of relieving the weight and perhaps the sadness with which great tasks lie on him who has to achieve them?— And that such weight pressed on Mr.", "게다가 도러시아의 열정은, 위대한 과업이 그것을 이루어야 할 자에게 얹는 무게와 어쩌면 슬픔을 덜어 주리라는 전망에 특히 머물러 있지 않았던가?"], ["Casaubon was only plainer than before.", "그리고 그러한 무게가 캐소본 씨를 짓누르고 있다는 사실은 전보다 더욱 분명할 뿐이었다."]], [["All these are crushing questions; but whatever else remained the same, the light had changed, and you cannot find the pearly dawn at noonday.", "이 모든 것은 사람을 짓누르는 물음들이다. 하지만 다른 무엇이 그대로 남아 있든, 빛이 달라져 버렸으니, 정오에 진주빛 새벽을 찾을 수는 없는 법이다."], ["The fact is unalterable, that a fellow-mortal with whose nature you are acquainted solely through the brief entrances and exits of a few imaginative weeks called courtship, may, when seen in the continuity of married companionship, be disclosed as something better or worse than what you have preconceived, but will certainly not appear altogether the same.", "변할 수 없는 사실인즉, 구애라 불리는 몇 주간의 상상에 찬 등장과 퇴장을 통해서만 그 천성을 알게 된 동료 인간은, 결혼 생활의 연속성 속에서 보았을 때 그대가 미리 그려 두었던 것보다 더 나은 무엇으로, 혹은 더 못한 무엇으로 드러날 수는 있어도, 결코 완전히 똑같이 보이지는 않으리라는 것이다."], ["And it would be astonishing to find how soon the change is felt if we had no kindred changes to compare with it.", "그리고 만약 그것에 견줄 만한 비슷한 변화들을 우리가 겪어 보지 못했다면, 그 변화가 얼마나 빨리 느껴지는지를 알고 깜짝 놀랄 것이다."], ["To share lodgings with a brilliant dinner-companion, or to see your favorite politician in the Ministry, may bring about changes quite as rapid: in these cases too we begin by knowing little and believing much, and we sometimes end by inverting the quantities.", "명석한 만찬 동석자와 한 거처를 나누어 쓰거나, 좋아하던 정치가가 내각에 들어간 모습을 보는 일도 꼭 그만큼이나 빠른 변화를 가져올 수 있다. 이런 경우들에서도 우리는 거의 알지 못하면서 많이 믿는 데서 시작했다가, 때로는 그 양을 뒤집어 놓으며 끝나기도 한다."]], [["Still, such comparisons might mislead, for no man was more incapable of flashy make-believe than Mr.", "그렇더라도 그런 비교는 사람을 오도할 수 있으니, 캐소본 씨만큼 번지르르한 허울을 꾸며 낼 줄 모르는 사람도 없었기 때문이다."], ["Casaubon: he was as genuine a character as any ruminant animal, and he had not actively assisted in creating any illusions about himself.", "그는 어떤 되새김질하는 짐승 못지않게 진실한 인물이었고, 자신에 관한 어떤 환상도 적극적으로 빚어내는 데 가담하지 않았다."], ["How was it that in the weeks since her marriage, Dorothea had not distinctly observed but felt with a stifling depression, that the large vistas and wide fresh air which she had dreamed of finding in her husband’s mind were replaced by anterooms and winding passages which seemed to lead nowhither?", "그렇다면 결혼 이후 몇 주 동안 도러시아가, 남편의 정신 속에서 발견하리라 꿈꾸었던 드넓은 전망과 광활하고 신선한 공기가, 어디로도 통하지 않는 듯한 대기실들과 구불구불한 통로들로 대체되었음을, 또렷이 관찰하지는 못했으되 숨 막히는 우울 속에서 느끼게 된 것은 어찌 된 일이었을까?"], ["I suppose it was that in courtship everything is regarded as provisional and preliminary, and the smallest sample of virtue or accomplishment is taken to guarantee delightful stores which the broad leisure of marriage will reveal.", "짐작건대 구애 기간에는 모든 것이 잠정적이고 예비적인 것으로 여겨지고, 미덕이나 재능의 가장 작은 표본조차 결혼이 주는 넉넉한 여유가 드러내 줄 황홀한 보고를 보증하는 것으로 받아들여지기 때문이리라."], ["But the door-sill of marriage once crossed, expectation is concentrated on the present.", "하지만 결혼의 문지방을 일단 넘고 나면, 기대는 현재에 집중된다."], ["Having once embarked on your marital voyage, it is impossible not to be aware that you make no way and that the sea is not within sight—that, in fact, you are exploring an enclosed basin.", "일단 결혼이라는 항해에 올라타고 나면, 자신이 한 발짝도 나아가지 못하고 바다는 시야에 들어오지도 않는다는 것을—사실은 자신이 닫힌 못 안을 탐사하고 있다는 것을—깨닫지 않을 도리가 없다."]], [["In their conversation before marriage, Mr. Casaubon had often dwelt on some explanation or questionable detail of which Dorothea did not see the bearing; but such imperfect coherence seemed due to the brokenness of their intercourse, and, supported by her faith in their future, she had listened with fervid patience to a recitation of possible arguments to be brought against Mr.", "결혼 전 대화에서 캐소본 씨는 종종 도러시아가 그 의미를 알 수 없는 어떤 설명이나 의문스러운 세부에 머물러 있곤 했다."], ["Casaubon’s entirely new view of the Philistine god Dagon and other fish-deities, thinking that hereafter she should see this subject which touched him so nearly from the same high ground whence doubtless it had become so important to him.", "하지만 그처럼 불완전한 일관성은 그들 교제의 단편적인 성격 탓인 듯했고, 그들의 미래에 대한 믿음에 기대어, 그녀는 캐소본 씨의 전적으로 새로운 견해—블레셋의 신 다곤과 그 밖의 물고기 신들에 관한 견해—에 맞서 제기될 수 있는 반론들을 늘어놓는 이야기를 열렬한 인내심으로 경청했다."], ["Again, the matter-of-course statement and tone of dismissal with which he treated what to her were the most stirring thoughts, was easily accounted for as belonging to the sense of haste and preoccupation in which she herself shared during their engagement.", "그를 그토록 절실히 사로잡는 이 주제를, 훗날 자신도 그것이 그에게 그토록 중요해진 그 높은 자리에서 똑같이 바라보게 되리라 생각하면서 말이다. 또한 그녀에게는 가장 가슴 떨리는 생각들을 그가 당연하다는 듯한 진술과 일축하는 어조로 다루는 것도, 약혼 기간에 그녀 자신도 함께 나누었던 서두름과 골몰의 감각에 속한 것으로 쉽게 설명되었다."], ["But now, since they had been in Rome, with all the depths of her emotion roused to tumultuous activity, and with life made a new problem by new elements, she had been becoming more and more aware, with a certain terror, that her mind was continually sliding into inward fits of anger and repulsion, or else into forlorn weariness.", "하지만 이제, 로마에 온 뒤로 그녀의 모든 감정의 심연이 격동하는 활동으로 일깨워지고, 새로운 요소들로 인해 삶이 새로운 문제로 변하면서, 그녀는 어떤 두려움과 더불어 점점 더 또렷이 깨닫게 되었다. 자기 마음이 끊임없이 내면적인 분노와 반감의 발작으로, 그렇지 않으면 쓸쓸한 권태로 미끄러져 들어가고 있다는 것을."], ["How far the judicious Hooker or any other hero of erudition would have been the same at Mr.", "분별 있던 후커나 다른 어떤 박학의 영웅이라 해도 캐소본 씨 나이에 이르러서는 마찬가지였을지 그녀로서는 알 길이 없었으니, 그는 비교의 덕을 볼 수도 없었다."], ["Casaubon’s time of life, she had no means of knowing, so that he could not have the advantage of comparison; but her husband’s way of commenting on the strangely impressive objects around them had begun to affect her with a sort of mental shiver: he had perhaps the best intention of acquitting himself worthily, but only of acquitting himself.", "다만 그들 주위의 기이하리만치 인상적인 대상들에 대해 남편이 논평하는 방식은, 그녀에게 일종의 정신적 전율을 일으키기 시작했다. 그는 어쩌면 자신의 소임을 훌륭히 해내려는 더없이 좋은 의도를 품었겠지만, 그것은 오직 제 소임만을 해내려는 것이었다."], ["What was fresh to her mind was worn out to his; and such capacity of thought and feeling as had ever been stimulated in him by the general life of mankind had long shrunk to a sort of dried preparation, a lifeless embalmment of knowledge.", "그녀의 마음에는 신선한 것이 그에게는 닳아 버린 것이었다. 인류의 보편적 삶에 의해 그의 내면에서 한때 자극받았던 사유와 감정의 역량은, 이미 오래전에 일종의 메마른 표본으로, 생기 없이 박제된 지식으로 쪼그라들어 있었다."]], [["When he said, “Does this interest you, Dorothea?", "그가 \"이게 재미있소, 도러시아? 좀 더 머물까요?"], ["Shall we stay a little longer? I am ready to stay if you wish it,”—it seemed to her as if going or staying were alike dreary.", "당신이 원한다면 나는 머물 준비가 되어 있소\"라고 말했을 때, 그녀에게는 가는 것이나 머무는 것이나 똑같이 음울하게 느껴졌다."], ["Or, “Should you like to go to the Farnesina, Dorothea?", "혹은 \"파르네시나에 가 보겠소, 도러시아?"], ["It contains celebrated frescos designed or painted by Raphael, which most persons think it worth while to visit.”", "거기에는 라파엘로가 도안했거나 그렸다는 유명한 프레스코화가 있는데, 대다수 사람들이 방문할 만한 가치가 있다고 여기지요.\""]], [["“But do you care about them?” was always Dorothea’s question.", "\"하지만 당신은 그것들에 관심이 있나요?\"라는 것이 도러시아의 한결같은 물음이었다."]], [["“They are, I believe, highly esteemed.", "\"내 생각에 그것들은 매우 높이 평가받고 있소."], ["Some of them represent the fable of Cupid and Psyche, which is probably the romantic invention of a literary period, and cannot, I think, be reckoned as a genuine mythical product.", "그중 일부는 큐피드와 프시케의 우화를 묘사한 것인데, 그것은 아마 어느 문학 시대의 낭만적 창작일 뿐, 내 생각에는 진정한 신화적 산물로 칠 수는 없소."], ["But if you like these wall-paintings we can easily drive thither; and you will then, I think, have seen the chief works of Raphael, any of which it were a pity to omit in a visit to Rome.", "하지만 당신이 이 벽화들을 좋아한다면 손쉽게 그리로 마차를 몰 수 있소. 그러면 당신은, 내 생각에, 라파엘로의 주요 작품들을 다 본 셈이 될 텐데, 로마를 방문하면서 그 어느 하나라도 빠뜨린다면 애석한 일이지요."], ["He is the painter who has been held to combine the most complete grace of form with sublimity of expression.", "그는 가장 완벽한 형태의 우아함과 표현의 숭고함을 결합한 화가로 여겨져 왔소."], ["Such at least I have gathered to be the opinion of cognoscenti.”", "적어도 그것이 감식가들의 견해라고 나는 들었소.\""]], [["This kind of answer given in a measured official tone, as of a clergyman reading according to the rubric, did not help to justify the glories of the Eternal City, or to give her the hope that if she knew more about them the world would be joyously illuminated for her.", "성직자가 전례서에 따라 낭독하듯 절도 있고 공적인 어조로 주어진 이런 종류의 대답은, 영원의 도시가 지닌 영광을 정당화하는 데에도, 또 그녀가 그것들에 대해 더 알게 되면 세상이 그녀를 위해 기쁘게 빛나리라는 희망을 주는 데에도 도움이 되지 못했다."], ["There is hardly any contact more depressing to a young ardent creature than that of a mind in which years full of knowledge seem to have issued in a blank absence of interest or sympathy.", "젊고 열정적인 존재에게 있어, 지식으로 가득 찬 세월이 흥미나 공감의 텅 빈 부재로 귀결된 듯한 정신과 접하는 것보다 더 의기소침하게 하는 일은 거의 없다."]], [["On other subjects indeed Mr. Casaubon showed a tenacity of occupation and an eagerness which are usually regarded as the effect of enthusiasm, and Dorothea was anxious to follow this spontaneous direction of his thoughts, instead of being made to feel that she dragged him away from it.", "물론 다른 주제들에 대해서는 캐소본 씨도 끈질긴 몰두와, 흔히 열정의 효과로 여겨지는 열의를 보였고, 도러시아는 자신이 그를 그것에서 끌어낸다는 느낌을 받기보다는, 그의 생각이 저절로 향하는 그 방향을 따르고 싶어 안달했다."], ["But she was gradually ceasing to expect with her former delightful confidence that she should see any wide opening where she followed him.", "하지만 그녀는 그를 따라가는 곳에서 어떤 드넓은 전망이 열리리라는, 예전의 즐거운 확신을 차츰 잃어 가고 있었다."], ["Poor Mr. Casaubon himself was lost among small closets and winding stairs, and in an agitated dimness about the Cabeiri, or in an exposure of other mythologists’ ill-considered parallels, easily lost sight of any purpose which had prompted him to these labors.", "가엾은 캐소본 씨 자신도 작은 골방들과 구불구불한 계단 사이에서, 그리고 카비리에 관한 어수선한 모호함 속에서, 혹은 다른 신화학자들의 사려 깊지 못한 유비를 폭로하는 일에 빠져, 자신을 이런 노고로 몰아넣었던 목적을 손쉽게 놓쳐 버렸다."], ["With his taper stuck before him he forgot the absence of windows, and in bitter manuscript remarks on other men’s notions about the solar deities, he had become indifferent to the sunlight.", "가느다란 초를 앞에 켜 둔 채 그는 창문이 없다는 것을 잊었고, 태양신들에 관한 다른 이들의 견해에 신랄한 필사본 주석을 다는 동안, 그는 햇빛에 무관심해져 버렸다."]], [["These characteristics, fixed and unchangeable as bone in Mr. Casaubon, might have remained longer unfelt by Dorothea if she had been encouraged to pour forth her girlish and womanly feeling—if he would have held her hands between his and listened with the delight of tenderness and understanding to all the little histories which made up her experience, and would have given her the same sort of intimacy in return, so that the past life of each could be included in their mutual knowledge and affection—or if she could have fed her affection with those childlike caresses which are the bent of every sweet woman, who has begun by showering kisses on the hard pate of her bald doll, creating a happy soul within that woodenness from the wealth of her own love.", "캐소본 씨에게 뼈처럼 고착되어 변할 수 없는 이러한 특성들은, 만약 그가 도러시아로 하여금 처녀다운, 여인다운 감정을 쏟아 내도록 북돋아 주었더라면—만약 그가 그녀의 두 손을 자기 손 사이에 쥐고서, 그녀의 경험을 이루는 모든 자질구레한 사연들을 다정함과 이해의 기쁨으로 귀 기울여 들어 주고, 자기도 그녀에게 똑같은 종류의 친밀함을 되돌려 주어, 두 사람 각자의 지난 삶이 서로의 앎과 애정 속에 포함될 수 있게 해 주었더라면—혹은 그녀가, 대머리 인형의 딱딱한 머리통에 입맞춤을 퍼붓고 자신의 풍부한 사랑으로 그 나무토막 속에 행복한 영혼을 빚어내는 일로 시작하는 모든 다정한 여인의 천성인, 그러한 어린아이 같은 애무로 자신의 애정을 길러 낼 수 있었더라면, 도러시아에게 더 오래 느껴지지 않은 채 남아 있었을지도 모른다."], ["That was Dorothea’s bent.", "그것이 도러시아의 천성이었다."], ["With all her yearning to know what was afar from her and to be widely benignant, she had ardor enough for what was near, to have kissed Mr.", "멀리 있는 것을 알고 싶고 널리 자애로워지고 싶은 온갖 갈망에도 불구하고, 그녀에게는 가까이 있는 것을 향한 열정 또한 충분해서, 캐소본 씨가 받아들인다는 어떤 다른 표시만 보여 주었더라면 그의 외투 소매에 입을 맞추거나 그의 신발 끈을 어루만졌을 것이다."], ["Casaubon’s coat-sleeve, or to have caressed his shoe-latchet, if he would have made any other sign of acceptance than pronouncing her, with his unfailing propriety, to be of a most affectionate and truly feminine nature, indicating at the same time by politely reaching a chair for her that he regarded these manifestations as rather crude and startling.", "하지만 그는 변함없는 예법으로, 그녀가 더없이 다정하고 진실로 여성적인 천성을 지녔노라고 선언하는 것 외에는 다른 표시를 하지 않았고, 동시에 그녀에게 정중히 의자를 끌어다 주는 것으로 자신이 그러한 표현들을 다소 거칠고 당혹스러운 것으로 여긴다는 것을 드러냈다."], ["Having made his clerical toilet with due care in the morning, he was prepared only for those amenities of life which were suited to the well-adjusted stiff cravat of the period, and to a mind weighted with unpublished matter.", "아침에 성직자다운 몸단장을 빈틈없이 마친 그는, 그 시대의 잘 맞춰진 빳빳한 크라바트와 출판되지 않은 사안들로 무거운 정신에 어울리는 그런 생활의 예의범절에만 대비하고 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["And by a sad contradiction Dorothea’s ideas and resolves seemed like melting ice floating and lost in the warm flood of which they had been but another form.", "그리고 슬픈 모순에 의해, 도러시아의 생각과 결심은 따뜻한 물결—본디 그것들도 그 물결의 또 다른 형태일 뿐이었던—속에 떠다니다 사라지는 녹아내리는 얼음 같았다. 그녀는 마치 그 매개를 통하지 않고는 아무것도 알 수 없다는 듯, 자신이 한낱 감정의 희생자임을 깨닫고 굴욕을 느꼈다."], ["She was humiliated to find herself a mere victim of feeling, as if she could know nothing except through that medium: all her strength was scattered in fits of agitation, of struggle, of despondency, and then again in visions of more complete renunciation, transforming all hard conditions into duty.", "그녀의 모든 힘은 동요와 분투와 낙담의 발작으로, 그러다가 또다시 더 완전한 체념의 환영—모든 가혹한 조건을 의무로 탈바꿈시키는—으로 흩어져 버렸다. 가엾은 도러시아!"], ["Poor Dorothea!", "그녀는 분명 성가신 존재였으니—주로 자기 자신에게 그러했다."], ["she was certainly troublesome—to herself chiefly; but this morning for the first time she had been troublesome to Mr. Casaubon.", "하지만 이날 아침 처음으로 그녀는 캐소본 씨에게 성가신 존재가 되었다."]], [["She had begun, while they were taking coffee, with a determination to shake off what she inwardly called her selfishness, and turned a face all cheerful attention to her husband when he said, “My dear Dorothea, we must now think of all that is yet left undone, as a preliminary to our departure.", "그녀는 커피를 마시는 동안, 스스로 마음속으로 이기심이라 부르던 것을 떨쳐 버리겠다는 결심으로 시작하여, 남편이 다음과 같이 말했을 때 온통 명랑한 주의로 가득 찬 얼굴을 그에게 돌렸다. \"사랑하는 도러시아, 이제 우리는 떠날 준비로서 아직 마치지 못한 모든 일을 생각해야 하오."], ["I would fain have returned home earlier that we might have been at Lowick for the Christmas; but my inquiries here have been protracted beyond their anticipated period.", "더 일찍 집으로 돌아가 성탄절을 로윅에서 보낼 수 있었으면 했지만, 이곳에서의 조사가 예상했던 기간을 넘겨 길어졌소."], ["I trust, however, that the time here has not been passed unpleasantly to you.", "그러나 이곳에서 보낸 시간이 당신에게 불쾌하지 않았기를 바라오."], ["Among the sights of Europe, that of Rome has ever been held one of the most striking and in some respects edifying.", "유럽의 명소들 가운데 로마는 늘 가장 인상적이며 어떤 점에서는 교화적인 곳 가운데 하나로 여겨져 왔소."], ["I well remember that I considered it an epoch in my life when I visited it for the first time; after the fall of Napoleon, an event which opened the Continent to travellers.", "나는 처음 이곳을 방문했을 때 그것을 내 생애의 한 획으로 여겼던 것을 잘 기억하고 있소. 나폴레옹의 몰락 이후였는데, 그것은 대륙을 여행자들에게 열어 준 사건이었지요."], ["Indeed I think it is one among several cities to which an extreme hyperbole has been applied—‘See Rome and die:’ but in your case I would propose an emendation and say, See Rome as a bride, and live henceforth as a happy wife.”", "사실 로마는 극단적인 과장이 적용된 몇몇 도시 가운데 하나라고 생각하오—'로마를 보고 죽으라'는 말 말이오. 하지만 당신의 경우에는 내가 수정을 제안하여 이렇게 말하고 싶소, 신부로서 로마를 보고, 이후로는 행복한 아내로 살라고.\""]], [["Mr. Casaubon pronounced this little speech with the most conscientious intention, blinking a little and swaying his head up and down, and concluding with a smile.", "캐소본 씨는 이 짧은 연설을 더없이 양심적인 의도로 발음하면서, 눈을 조금 깜박이고 고개를 위아래로 끄덕이다가 미소로 마무리했다."], ["He had not found marriage a rapturous state, but he had no idea of being anything else than an irreproachable husband, who would make a charming young woman as happy as she deserved to be.", "그는 결혼이 황홀한 상태임을 발견하지는 못했으나, 매력적인 젊은 여인을 그녀가 마땅히 누려야 할 만큼 행복하게 해 줄, 나무랄 데 없는 남편 외의 다른 무엇이 될 생각은 전혀 없었다."]], [["“I hope you are thoroughly satisfied with our stay—I mean, with the result so far as your studies are concerned,”", "\"당신이 우리의 체류에 진정으로 만족하시길 바라요—제 말은, 당신의 연구에 관한 한 그동안의 성과에 말이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, trying to keep her mind fixed on what most affected her husband.", "도러시아는 남편에게 가장 절실한 것에 마음을 붙들어 두려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Yes,” said Mr.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["Casaubon, with that peculiar pitch of voice which makes the word half a negative.", "캐소본 씨는 그 단어를 절반쯤 부정으로 만드는 그 독특한 음조로 말했다."], ["“I have been led farther than I had foreseen, and various subjects for annotation have presented themselves which, though I have no direct need of them, I could not pretermit.", "\"예상했던 것보다 더 멀리까지 이끌려 갔고, 주석을 달 만한 갖가지 주제들이 떠올랐는데, 직접적으로 필요하지는 않았으나 그냥 지나칠 수가 없었소."], ["The task, notwithstanding the assistance of my amanuensis, has been a somewhat laborious one, but your society has happily prevented me from that too continuous prosecution of thought beyond the hours of study which has been the snare of my solitary life.”", "이 작업은 필사 조수의 도움에도 불구하고 다소 고된 일이었지만, 당신과 함께한 덕분에 다행히도 연구 시간을 넘겨서까지 너무 끊임없이 생각을 밀어붙이는 일은 막을 수 있었소. 그것은 내 고독한 삶의 덫이었지요.\""]], [["“I am very glad that my presence has made any difference to you,”", "\"제가 곁에 있어서 당신에게 조금이나마 변화가 생겼다니 무척 기뻐요.\" 도러시아가 말했다."], ["said Dorothea, who had a vivid memory of evenings in which she had supposed that Mr. Casaubon’s mind had gone too deep during the day to be able to get to the surface again.", "그녀는 캐소본 씨의 정신이 낮 동안 너무 깊이 가라앉아 다시는 수면으로 떠오르지 못하리라 여겨졌던 여러 저녁들을 생생히 기억하고 있었다."], ["I fear there was a little temper in her reply.", "그녀의 대답에는 약간의 노여움이 섞여 있었던 듯하다."], ["“I hope when we get to Lowick, I shall be more useful to you, and be able to enter a little more into what interests you.”", "\"로윅에 가게 되면, 제가 당신에게 더 도움이 되고, 당신이 관심을 두는 일에 조금 더 들어갈 수 있게 되기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Doubtless, my dear,”", "\"틀림없이 그럴 거요, 여보.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, with a slight bow.", "캐소본 씨가 가볍게 고개를 숙이며 말했다."], ["“The notes I have here made will want sifting, and you can, if you please, extract them under my direction.”", "\"여기서 내가 적어 둔 노트들은 추려 낼 필요가 있을 텐데, 원한다면 내 지시에 따라 그것들을 발췌해도 좋소.\""]], [["“And all your notes,”", "\"그리고 당신의 그 모든 노트들 말이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, whose heart had already burned within her on this subject, so that now she could not help speaking with her tongue.", "도러시아가 말했다. 이 주제에 대해 이미 그녀의 마음이 안에서 뜨겁게 타올랐던 터라, 이제는 입 밖으로 말하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“All those rows of volumes—will you not now do what you used to speak of?—will you not make up your mind what part of them you will use, and begin to write the book which will make your vast knowledge useful to the world?", "\"그 모든 책더미들—이제 당신이 늘 말씀하시던 그 일을 하지 않으시겠어요?—그것들 중 어느 부분을 쓸지 마음을 정하고, 당신의 방대한 지식을 세상에 유용하게 만들어 줄 그 책을 쓰기 시작하지 않으시겠어요?"], ["I will write to your dictation, or I will copy and extract what you tell me: I can be of no other use.”", "제가 당신의 구술을 받아 적을게요, 아니면 당신이 일러 주시는 것을 옮겨 적고 발췌할게요. 저는 그것 말고는 아무 쓸모가 없으니까요.\""], ["Dorothea, in a most unaccountable, darkly feminine manner, ended with a slight sob and eyes full of tears.", "도러시아는 도무지 설명할 길 없는, 어둑하게 여성적인 태도로, 가벼운 흐느낌과 눈물 가득한 눈으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["The excessive feeling manifested would alone have been highly disturbing to Mr. Casaubon, but there were other reasons why Dorothea’s words were among the most cutting and irritating to him that she could have been impelled to use.", "이렇게 드러난 지나친 감정만으로도 캐소본 씨에게는 몹시 거슬렸을 테지만, 도러시아의 말이 그가 들을 수 있었을 가장 날카롭고 짜증스러운 말 가운데 하나였던 데에는 다른 이유들도 있었다."], ["She was as blind to his inward troubles as he to hers: she had not yet learned those hidden conflicts in her husband which claim our pity.", "그가 그녀의 내면적 고뇌에 눈멀어 있듯, 그녀도 그의 고뇌에 눈멀어 있었다. 그녀는 우리의 연민을 요하는 남편 내면의 그 숨은 갈등들을 아직 알아채지 못했다."], ["She had not yet listened patiently to his heartbeats, but only felt that her own was beating violently. In Mr.", "그녀는 아직 그의 심장 박동에 인내심 있게 귀 기울인 적이 없었고, 그저 자기 심장이 격렬히 뛰는 것만을 느낄 뿐이었다."], ["Casaubon’s ear, Dorothea’s voice gave loud emphatic iteration to those muffled suggestions of consciousness which it was possible to explain as mere fancy, the illusion of exaggerated sensitiveness: always when such suggestions are unmistakably repeated from without, they are resisted as cruel and unjust.", "캐소본 씨의 귀에는, 도러시아의 목소리가 의식의 그 어렴풋한 암시들—한낱 망상이라고, 과장된 예민함이 빚어낸 환각이라고 설명할 수도 있었던—을 큰 소리로 강조하며 되풀이하고 있었다. 그러한 암시들이 외부로부터 분명하게 되풀이될 때면, 우리는 언제나 그것들을 잔인하고 부당하다며 물리친다."], ["We are angered even by the full acceptance of our humiliating confessions—how much more by hearing in hard distinct syllables from the lips of a near observer, those confused murmurs which we try to call morbid, and strive against as if they were the oncoming of numbness!", "우리는 자신의 굴욕스러운 고백이 온전히 받아들여지는 것에도 화가 나는 법인데—하물며 가까이서 지켜보는 자의 입술로부터, 우리가 병적인 것이라 부르려 애쓰고 마비의 엄습인 양 맞서 싸우는 그 어수선한 웅얼거림을, 단단하고 또렷한 음절로 듣는 것은 얼마나 더 화가 나겠는가!"], ["And this cruel outward accuser was there in the shape of a wife—nay, of a young bride, who, instead of observing his abundant pen-scratches and amplitude of paper with the uncritical awe of an elegant-minded canary-bird, seemed to present herself as a spy watching everything with a malign power of inference.", "그런데 이 잔인한 외부의 고발자가 아내의 모습으로—아니, 젊은 신부의 모습으로 거기 있었다. 그 신부는 그의 무수한 펜 자국과 넉넉한 종이 더미를, 고상한 마음씨를 지닌 카나리아처럼 비판 없는 경외로 바라보기는커녕, 음험한 추론의 힘으로 모든 것을 감시하는 첩자로 제 모습을 드러내는 듯했다."], ["Here, towards this particular point of the compass, Mr. Casaubon had a sensitiveness to match Dorothea’s, and an equal quickness to imagine more than the fact.", "바로 이 특정한 방위를 향해서, 캐소본 씨는 도러시아에 못지않은 예민함과, 사실 이상을 상상해 내는 그녀와 똑같은 민첩함을 지니고 있었다."], ["He had formerly observed with approbation her capacity for worshipping the right object; he now foresaw with sudden terror that this capacity might be replaced by presumption, this worship by the most exasperating of all criticism,—that which sees vaguely a great many fine ends, and has not the least notion what it costs to reach them.", "그는 예전에 올바른 대상을 숭배할 줄 아는 그녀의 능력을 흡족하게 지켜보았으나, 이제 갑작스러운 두려움과 함께 예감했다. 그 능력이 주제넘음으로 대체되고, 그 숭배가 모든 비판 가운데 가장 부아를 돋우는 것—훌륭한 목표들을 막연하게 잔뜩 보면서도 그것에 이르는 데 무엇이 드는지는 조금도 알지 못하는 비판—으로 바뀔지도 모른다고."]], [["For the first time since Dorothea had known him, Mr. Casaubon’s face had a quick angry flush upon it.", "도러시아가 그를 알게 된 이래 처음으로, 캐소본 씨의 얼굴에 성난 홍조가 빠르게 번졌다."]], [["“My love,”", "\"여보.\""], ["he said, with irritation reined in by propriety, “you may rely upon me for knowing the times and the seasons, adapted to the different stages of a work which is not to be measured by the facile conjectures of ignorant onlookers.", "그가 예법으로 짜증을 억누른 채 말했다. \"무지한 구경꾼들의 섣부른 억측으로 가늠할 수 없는 작업의 여러 단계에 맞춰, 그 시기와 적기를 아는 일은 나를 믿고 맡겨도 좋소."], ["It had been easy for me to gain a temporary effect by a mirage of baseless opinion; but it is ever the trial of the scrupulous explorer to be saluted with the impatient scorn of chatterers who attempt only the smallest achievements, being indeed equipped for no other.", "근거 없는 의견의 신기루로 일시적인 효과를 거두는 것쯤은 나로서도 쉬운 일이었소. 하지만 가장 작은 성취만을 시도하며—실로 그 외의 것에는 채비도 되어 있지 않은—수다쟁이들의 조급한 경멸을 받는 것은, 양심적인 탐구자가 늘 겪는 시련이오."], ["And it were well if all such could be admonished to discriminate judgments of which the true subject-matter lies entirely beyond their reach, from those of which the elements may be compassed by a narrow and superficial survey.”", "그리고 그러한 자들 모두가, 그 진정한 본질이 자기들 손이 닿는 범위를 완전히 벗어난 판단과, 그 요소들을 좁고 피상적인 일별로 파악할 수 있는 판단을 구별하도록 깨우침을 받는다면 좋으련만.\""]], [["This speech was delivered with an energy and readiness quite unusual with Mr.", "이 연설은 캐소본 씨로서는 자못 이례적인 활기와 능란함으로 전달되었다."], ["Casaubon. It was not indeed entirely an improvisation, but had taken shape in inward colloquy, and rushed out like the round grains from a fruit when sudden heat cracks it.", "사실 그것은 전적인 즉흥은 아니었고, 내면의 독백 속에서 형태를 갖추었다가, 갑작스러운 열기가 과일을 터뜨릴 때 둥근 씨앗들이 쏟아져 나오듯 튀어나온 것이었다."], ["Dorothea was not only his wife: she was a personification of that shallow world which surrounds the appreciated or desponding author.", "도러시아는 그의 아내일 뿐만 아니라, 인정받는 작가나 의기소침한 작가를 에워싼 그 천박한 세상의 화신이었다."]], [["Dorothea was indignant in her turn.", "이번에는 도러시아가 분개했다."], ["Had she not been repressing everything in herself except the desire to enter into some fellowship with her husband’s chief interests?", "그녀는 남편의 주된 관심사와 어떤 동지애로 결합되고 싶다는 소망 외에는 자기 안의 모든 것을 억눌러 오지 않았던가?"]], [["“My judgment was a very superficial one—such as I am capable of forming,”", "\"제 판단은 정말이지 매우 피상적인 것이었어요—제가 형성할 수 있는 정도의 것이지요.\""], ["she answered, with a prompt resentment, that needed no rehearsal.", "그녀는 미리 연습할 필요도 없었던 즉각적인 분노로 대답했다."], ["“You showed me the rows of notebooks—you have often spoken of them—you have often said that they wanted digesting.", "\"당신은 제게 그 노트들의 줄지어 선 모습을 보여 주셨고—그것들에 대해 자주 말씀하셨고—그것들이 정리될 필요가 있다고 자주 말씀하셨어요."], ["But I never heard you speak of the writing that is to be published.", "하지만 저는 출판될 그 저술에 대해 당신이 말씀하시는 것은 한 번도 들은 적이 없어요."], ["Those were very simple facts, and my judgment went no farther.", "그것들은 매우 단순한 사실들이었고, 제 판단은 그 이상으로 나아가지 않았어요."], ["I only begged you to let me be of some good to you.”", "저는 그저 제가 당신에게 조금이나마 도움이 되게 해 달라고 청했을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["Dorothea rose to leave the table and Mr. Casaubon made no reply, taking up a letter which lay beside him as if to reperuse it.", "도러시아가 식탁을 떠나려 일어섰고, 캐소본 씨는 아무 대답도 하지 않은 채, 곁에 놓여 있던 편지를 다시 읽기라도 하려는 듯 집어 들었다."], ["Both were shocked at their mutual situation—that each should have betrayed anger towards the other.", "두 사람 모두 서로 처한 상황에—각자가 상대에게 노여움을 드러냈다는 사실에—충격을 받았다."], ["If they had been at home, settled at Lowick in ordinary life among their neighbors, the clash would have been less embarrassing: but on a wedding journey, the express object of which is to isolate two people on the ground that they are all the world to each other, the sense of disagreement is, to say the least, confounding and stultifying.", "만약 그들이 집에, 로윅에 자리 잡고 이웃들 사이에서 평범한 일상을 살고 있었다면, 그 충돌은 덜 거북했을 것이다. 하지만 신혼여행에서, 그 명백한 목적이 두 사람을 서로에게 온 세상이라는 이유로 따로 떼어 놓는 데 있는 여행에서, 의견이 어긋난다는 감각은 적어도 당혹스럽고 김빠지는 일이었다."], ["To have changed your longitude extensively and placed yourselves in a moral solitude in order to have small explosions, to find conversation difficult and to hand a glass of water without looking, can hardly be regarded as satisfactory fulfilment even to the toughest minds.", "경도를 멀리까지 바꾸어 도덕적 고독 속에 자리 잡은 끝에, 작은 폭발들을 겪고, 대화가 어려워지고, 쳐다보지도 않은 채 물잔을 건네게 되는 것을, 아무리 강인한 정신의 소유자라 한들 만족스러운 성취라고 보기는 어렵다."], ["To Dorothea’s inexperienced sensitiveness, it seemed like a catastrophe, changing all prospects; and to Mr.", "도러시아의 경험 없는 예민함에는 그것이 모든 전망을 뒤바꾸는 하나의 파국처럼 보였다."], ["Casaubon it was a new pain, he never having been on a wedding journey before, or found himself in that close union which was more of a subjection than he had been able to imagine, since this charming young bride not only obliged him to much consideration on her behalf (which he had sedulously given), but turned out to be capable of agitating him cruelly just where he most needed soothing.", "캐소본 씨에게는 그것이 새로운 고통이었으니, 그는 전에 신혼여행을 한 적도, 그가 상상할 수 있었던 것보다 더 종속에 가까운 그 긴밀한 결합 속에 자신을 둔 적도 없었기 때문이다. 이 매력적인 젊은 신부는 그에게 자기를 위한 많은 배려를 요구할 뿐 아니라(그는 이를 부지런히 베풀어 왔다), 정작 그가 가장 위로를 필요로 하는 바로 그 지점에서 그를 잔인하게 동요시킬 줄 아는 존재로 드러났다."], ["Instead of getting a soft fence against the cold, shadowy, unapplausive audience of his life, had he only given it a more substantial presence?", "자기 삶을 둘러싼 차갑고 그림자 같으며 갈채 없는 청중에 맞설 부드러운 울타리를 얻기는커녕, 그는 그저 그 청중에게 더 실질적인 존재감을 부여한 것에 지나지 않았던가?"]], [["Neither of them felt it possible to speak again at present.", "두 사람 중 누구도 지금 다시 입을 열 수 있다고 느끼지 못했다."], ["To have reversed a previous arrangement and declined to go out would have been a show of persistent anger which Dorothea’s conscience shrank from, seeing that she already began to feel herself guilty.", "이미 정해 둔 일정을 뒤집어 외출하지 않겠다고 하는 것은, 끈질긴 노여움을 드러내 보이는 일이 되었을 텐데, 이미 자신이 죄책감을 느끼기 시작한 터라 도러시아의 양심은 그것을 꺼렸다."], ["However just her indignation might be, her ideal was not to claim justice, but to give tenderness.", "자신의 분노가 아무리 정당하다 해도, 그녀의 이상은 정의를 주장하는 것이 아니라 다정함을 베푸는 것이었다."], ["So when the carriage came to the door, she drove with Mr. Casaubon to the Vatican, walked with him through the stony avenue of inscriptions, and when she parted with him at the entrance to the Library, went on through the Museum out of mere listlessness as to what was around her.", "그래서 마차가 문 앞에 오자, 그녀는 캐소본 씨와 함께 바티칸으로 갔고, 비문이 새겨진 돌투성이 통로를 그와 더불어 걸었으며, 도서관 입구에서 그와 헤어진 뒤에는, 주위에 무엇이 있든 그저 무기력하게 박물관을 가로질러 나아갔다."], ["She had not spirit to turn round and say that she would drive anywhere. It was when Mr.", "그녀에게는 돌아서서 어디든 가고 싶다고 말할 기운조차 없었다."], ["Casaubon was quitting her that Naumann had first seen her, and he had entered the long gallery of sculpture at the same time with her; but here Naumann had to await Ladislaw with whom he was to settle a bet of champagne about an enigmatical mediaeval-looking figure there.", "캐소본 씨가 그녀와 헤어지던 바로 그때 나우만이 처음으로 그녀를 보았고, 그는 그녀와 동시에 긴 조각 회랑으로 들어섰다. 하지만 거기서 나우만은 라디슬로를 기다려야 했는데, 그곳에 있는 수수께끼 같은 중세풍 인물상을 두고 그와 샴페인 내기를 정리하기로 했기 때문이다."], ["After they had examined the figure, and had walked on finishing their dispute, they had parted, Ladislaw lingering behind while Naumann had gone into the Hall of Statues where he again saw Dorothea, and saw her in that brooding abstraction which made her pose remarkable.", "그 인물상을 살펴보고, 논쟁을 마무리하며 걸어간 뒤 두 사람은 헤어졌고, 라디슬로는 뒤에 남아 머뭇거렸으며 나우만은 조각상의 전당으로 들어가 거기서 다시 도러시아를 보았다. 그녀의 자세를 두드러지게 만드는 그 골똘한 상념에 잠긴 모습으로 그녀를 본 것이다."], ["She did not really see the streak of sunlight on the floor more than she saw the statues: she was inwardly seeing the light of years to come in her own home and over the English fields and elms and hedge-bordered highroads; and feeling that the way in which they might be filled with joyful devotedness was not so clear to her as it had been.", "그녀는 조각상들을 보고 있지 않았던 만큼이나 바닥에 비친 한 줄기 햇빛도 실은 보고 있지 않았다. 그녀는 자기 집에서, 영국의 들판과 느릅나무들과 산울타리가 늘어선 큰길 위로 펼쳐질 다가올 세월의 빛을 내면으로 보고 있었고, 그 세월을 기쁨에 찬 헌신으로 채울 방도가 예전만큼 또렷하지 않다는 것을 느끼고 있었다."], ["But in Dorothea’s mind there was a current into which all thought and feeling were apt sooner or later to flow—the reaching forward of the whole consciousness towards the fullest truth, the least partial good.", "하지만 도러시아의 마음속에는, 모든 생각과 감정이 조만간 흘러들기 마련인 하나의 흐름이 있었으니—그것은 가장 온전한 진리, 가장 덜 편파적인 선을 향해 의식 전체가 앞으로 내뻗는 움직임이었다."], ["There was clearly something better than anger and despondency.", "분노와 낙담보다 더 나은 무언가가 분명히 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI.", "# CHAPTER XXI."]], [["“Hire facounde eke full womanly and plain, No contrefeted termes had she To semen wise.”", "그녀의 언변 또한 매우 여성스럽고 꾸밈이 없으며, 현명해 보이려고 지어낸 말을 쓰지 않았다."], ["—CHAUCER.", "— 초서"]], [["It was in that way Dorothea came to be sobbing as soon as she was securely alone.", "도러시아가 안전하게 혼자가 되자마자 흐느끼게 된 것은 그런 까닭에서였다."], ["But she was presently roused by a knock at the door, which made her hastily dry her eyes before saying, “Come in.”", "그러나 그녀는 곧 문을 두드리는 소리에 정신이 들었고, 그 바람에 \"들어오세요\"라고 말하기 전에 황급히 눈물을 닦았다."], ["Tantripp had brought a card, and said that there was a gentleman waiting in the lobby. The courier had told him that only Mrs.", "탠트립이 명함을 가지고 와서, 어떤 신사가 현관에서 기다리고 있다고 말했다."], ["Casaubon was at home, but he said he was a relation of Mr. Casaubon’s: would she see him?", "안내인이 그에게 캐소본 부인만 댁에 계신다고 했는데도, 그는 자신이 캐소본 씨의 친척이라며 부인을 뵐 수 있겠느냐고 했다는 것이다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Dorothea, without pause; “show him into the salon.”", "도러시아가 망설임 없이 말했다. \"응접실로 모시세요.\""], ["Her chief impressions about young Ladislaw were that when she had seen him at Lowick she had been made aware of Mr. Casaubon’s generosity towards him, and also that she had been interested in his own hesitation about his career.", "젊은 래디슬로에 대한 그녀의 주된 인상이라면, 로윅에서 그를 보았을 때 캐소본 씨가 그에게 베푼 관대함을 알게 되었다는 것, 그리고 그가 자신의 진로를 두고 망설이던 모습에 흥미를 느꼈다는 것이었다."], ["She was alive to anything that gave her an opportunity for active sympathy, and at this moment it seemed as if the visit had come to shake her out of her self-absorbed discontent—to remind her of her husband’s goodness, and make her feel that she had now the right to be his helpmate in all kind deeds.", "그녀는 적극적인 공감을 베풀 기회를 주는 것이라면 무엇에든 마음이 살아 움직였는데, 지금 이 순간에는 그 방문이 마치 자기 안에만 갇혀 있던 불만에서 그녀를 흔들어 깨우려 온 것처럼 — 남편의 선량함을 일깨워 주고, 이제 모든 선한 행위에서 그의 조력자가 될 권리가 자신에게 있음을 느끼게 해 주려고 온 것처럼 보였다."], ["She waited a minute or two, but when she passed into the next room there were just signs enough that she had been crying to make her open face look more youthful and appealing than usual.", "그녀는 잠시 한두 순간 기다렸지만, 옆방으로 들어설 때 그녀의 얼굴에는 울었던 흔적이 꼭 그만큼만 남아 있어, 그 솔직한 얼굴이 평소보다 더 앳되고 호소력 있게 보였다."], ["She met Ladislaw with that exquisite smile of good-will which is unmixed with vanity, and held out her hand to him.", "그녀는 허영이 섞이지 않은 그 절묘한 선의의 미소로 래디슬로를 맞이하며 손을 내밀었다. 그는 그녀보다 몇 살 위였지만, 그 순간에는 훨씬 더 젊어 보였다."], ["He was the elder by several years, but at that moment he looked much the younger, for his transparent complexion flushed suddenly, and he spoke with a shyness extremely unlike the ready indifference of his manner with his male companion, while Dorothea became all the calmer with a wondering desire to put him at ease.", "투명한 얼굴빛이 갑자기 붉어졌고, 남자 동행과 있을 때의 그 거침없는 무심함과는 전혀 다른 수줍음을 띠며 말했기 때문이다. 그동안 도러시아는 그를 편안하게 해 주고 싶다는 의아한 마음에 점점 더 차분해졌다."]], [["“I was not aware that you and Mr. Casaubon were in Rome, until this morning, when I saw you in the Vatican Museum,”", "\"오늘 아침 바티칸 박물관에서 뵙기 전까지는 당신과 캐소본 씨가 로마에 계신 줄 몰랐습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I knew you at once—but—I mean, that I concluded Mr. Casaubon’s address would be found at the Poste Restante, and I was anxious to pay my respects to him and you as early as possible.”", "\"저는 곧바로 당신을 알아보았지만 — 하지만 — 제 말은, 캐소본 씨의 주소가 우편국 유치 창구에 있으리라 짐작했고, 가능한 한 빨리 그분과 당신께 인사를 드리고 싶었다는 뜻입니다.\""]], [["“Pray sit down.", "\"앉으세요."], ["He is not here now, but he will be glad to hear of you, I am sure,”", "그이는 지금 여기 없지만, 분명 당신 소식을 들으면 기뻐할 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea, seating herself unthinkingly between the fire and the light of the tall window, and pointing to a chair opposite, with the quietude of a benignant matron.", "도러시아는 무심코 벽난로와 높다란 창의 빛 사이에 앉으며, 자비로운 부인의 차분함으로 맞은편 의자를 가리키며 말했다. 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 소녀다운 슬픔의 흔적이 오히려 더 두드러져 보였다."], ["The signs of girlish sorrow in her face were only the more striking.", "\"캐소본 씨는 무척 바쁘답니다. 하지만 주소를 남겨 주시겠지요?"], ["“Mr. Casaubon is much engaged; but you will leave your address—will you not?—and he will write to you.”", "그러면 그이가 당신께 편지를 쓸 거예요.\""]], [["“You are very good,”", "\"정말 친절하시군요.\""], ["said Ladislaw, beginning to lose his diffidence in the interest with which he was observing the signs of weeping which had altered her face.", "래디슬로가 말했다. 그녀의 얼굴을 달라지게 한 울음의 흔적을 흥미롭게 관찰하면서 차츰 소심함을 잃어 갔다."], ["“My address is on my card.", "\"제 주소는 명함에 있습니다."], ["But if you will allow me I will call again to-morrow at an hour when Mr. Casaubon is likely to be at home.”", "하지만 허락해 주신다면, 내일 캐소본 씨가 댁에 계실 만한 시간에 다시 찾아뵙겠습니다.\""]], [["“He goes to read in the Library of the Vatican every day, and you can hardly see him except by an appointment.", "\"그이는 매일 바티칸 도서관에 가서 글을 읽기 때문에, 약속을 하지 않고는 좀처럼 만나기 어려워요."], ["Especially now.", "특히 지금은요."], ["We are about to leave Rome, and he is very busy.", "우리는 곧 로마를 떠날 예정이라 그이가 무척 바쁘답니다."], ["He is usually away almost from breakfast till dinner.", "보통 아침 식사 후부터 저녁 식사 때까지 거의 자리를 비워요."], ["But I am sure he will wish you to dine with us.”", "하지만 그이도 분명 당신이 우리와 함께 저녁 식사를 하기를 바랄 거예요.\""]], [["Will Ladislaw was struck mute for a few moments.", "윌 래디슬로는 몇 순간 말문이 막혔다."], ["He had never been fond of Mr. Casaubon, and if it had not been for the sense of obligation, would have laughed at him as a Bat of erudition.", "그는 캐소본 씨를 좋아한 적이 없었고, 신세를 졌다는 의식이 없었더라면 박학다식한 박쥐 같은 인간이라고 비웃었을 것이다."], ["But the idea of this dried-up pedant, this elaborator of small explanations about as important as the surplus stock of false antiquities kept in a vendor’s back chamber, having first got this adorable young creature to marry him, and then passing his honeymoon away from her, groping after his mouldy futilities (Will was given to hyperbole)—this sudden picture stirred him with a sort of comic disgust: he was divided between the impulse to laugh aloud and the equally unseasonable impulse to burst into scornful invective.", "하지만 이 메마른 현학자, 골동품상이 뒷방에 쌓아 둔 가짜 골동품 재고만큼이나 하찮은 자질구레한 설명들을 공들여 다듬는 이 사내가, 우선 이 사랑스러운 젊은 여인을 자신과 결혼하게 만들어 놓고는, 신혼여행 기간을 그녀에게서 떨어져 곰팡내 나는 무익한 일들을 더듬으며 보내고 있다니 (윌은 과장에 능했다) — 이 돌연한 광경에 그는 일종의 우스꽝스러운 혐오감에 사로잡혔다. 큰 소리로 웃고 싶은 충동과, 마찬가지로 때에 맞지 않게 경멸 어린 욕설을 터뜨리고 싶은 충동 사이에서 그는 갈팡질팡했다."]], [["For an instant he felt that the struggle was causing a queer contortion of his mobile features, but with a good effort he resolved it into nothing more offensive than a merry smile.", "한순간 그는 그 갈등이 자신의 변화무쌍한 이목구비를 기묘하게 일그러뜨리고 있음을 느꼈지만, 애써 그것을 유쾌한 미소 정도로, 그 이상 거슬리지 않게 풀어냈다."]], [["Dorothea wondered; but the smile was irresistible, and shone back from her face too.", "도러시아는 의아했지만, 그 미소는 거부할 수 없는 것이어서 그녀의 얼굴에서도 되비쳐 빛났다."], ["Will Ladislaw’s smile was delightful, unless you were angry with him beforehand: it was a gush of inward light illuminating the transparent skin as well as the eyes, and playing about every curve and line as if some Ariel were touching them with a new charm, and banishing forever the traces of moodiness.", "윌 래디슬로의 미소는, 미리부터 그에게 화가 나 있지 않은 한, 사람을 즐겁게 했다. 그것은 내면의 빛이 솟구쳐 두 눈은 물론 투명한 살갗까지 환히 밝히며, 마치 어떤 아리엘이 새로운 매력으로 어루만지듯 모든 곡선과 선들 위에서 노닐어, 침울함의 자취를 영원히 쫓아내는 것이었다."], ["The reflection of that smile could not but have a little merriment in it too, even under dark eyelashes still moist, as Dorothea said inquiringly, “Something amuses you?”", "그 미소의 반영은, 아직 촉촉한 검은 속눈썹 아래에서일지라도, 조금의 유쾌함을 띠지 않을 수 없었으니, 도러시아는 묻듯이 말했다. \"무언가 재미있으신가요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Will, quick in finding resources.", "윌이 재빨리 둘러댈 거리를 찾아내며 말했다."], ["“I am thinking of the sort of figure I cut the first time I saw you, when you annihilated my poor sketch with your criticism.”", "\"처음 당신을 뵈었을 때 제가 했던 우스운 꼴을 생각하던 참입니다. 당신의 비평으로 제 보잘것없는 스케치를 단번에 무너뜨리셨을 때 말입니다.\""]], [["“My criticism?”", "\"제 비평이라뇨?\""], ["said Dorothea, wondering still more.", "도러시아가 더욱 의아해하며 말했다."], ["“Surely not.", "\"그럴 리가요."], ["I always feel particularly ignorant about painting.”", "저는 그림에 관해서는 늘 유난히 무지하다고 느끼는걸요.\""]], [["“I suspected you of knowing so much, that you knew how to say just what was most cutting.", "\"저는 당신이 너무 많이 알아서, 가장 신랄한 말이 무엇인지 정확히 짚어 낼 줄 안다고 의심했습니다."], ["You said—I dare say you don’t remember it as I do—that the relation of my sketch to nature was quite hidden from you.", "당신은 말씀하셨지요 — 저만큼 기억하지는 못하시겠지만 — 제 스케치가 자연과 어떤 관계를 맺는지 당신께는 전혀 보이지 않는다고요."], ["At least, you implied that.”", "적어도 그런 뜻을 내비치셨습니다.\""], ["Will could laugh now as well as smile.", "이제 윌은 미소뿐 아니라 웃을 수도 있었다."]], [["“That was really my ignorance,”", "\"그건 정말 제 무지 탓이었어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, admiring Will’s good-humor.", "도러시아가 윌의 호탕함에 감탄하며 말했다."], ["“I must have said so only because I never could see any beauty in the pictures which my uncle told me all judges thought very fine.", "\"제 삼촌이 모든 감식가들이 아주 훌륭하다고 평한다던 그림들에서 저는 도무지 아름다움을 볼 수 없었기에 그렇게 말한 게 분명해요."], ["And I have gone about with just the same ignorance in Rome.", "그리고 로마에서도 똑같은 무지를 안고 다녔답니다."], ["There are comparatively few paintings that I can really enjoy.", "제가 정말로 즐길 수 있는 그림은 비교적 적어요."], ["At first when I enter a room where the walls are covered with frescos, or with rare pictures, I feel a kind of awe—like a child present at great ceremonies where there are grand robes and processions; I feel myself in the presence of some higher life than my own.", "처음에 벽이 프레스코화나 진귀한 그림들로 뒤덮인 방에 들어서면, 저는 일종의 경외감을 느껴요 — 마치 화려한 예복과 행렬이 펼쳐지는 대단한 의식에 참석한 아이처럼요. 저 자신보다 더 높은 어떤 삶 앞에 서 있는 듯한 느낌이 들지요."], ["But when I begin to examine the pictures one by one the life goes out of them, or else is something violent and strange to me.", "하지만 그림을 하나씩 들여다보기 시작하면 그 생명이 빠져나가 버리거나, 아니면 제게는 무언가 격렬하고 낯선 것이 되고 말아요."], ["It must be my own dulness.", "분명 제 둔함 탓이겠지요."], ["I am seeing so much all at once, and not understanding half of it.", "한꺼번에 너무 많은 것을 보면서 절반도 이해하지 못하니까요."], ["That always makes one feel stupid.", "그러면 늘 자신이 어리석게 느껴져요."], ["It is painful to be told that anything is very fine and not be able to feel that it is fine—something like being blind, while people talk of the sky.”", "무언가가 매우 훌륭하다는 말을 들으면서도 그것이 훌륭하다고 느끼지 못하는 건 괴로운 일이에요 — 사람들이 하늘에 관해 이야기하는데 자기는 눈이 먼 것과도 같지요.\""]], [["“Oh, there is a great deal in the feeling for art which must be acquired,”", "\"오, 예술을 느끼는 감각에는 후천적으로 익혀야 하는 부분이 많습니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["(It was impossible now to doubt the directness of Dorothea’s confession.) “Art is an old language with a great many artificial affected styles, and sometimes the chief pleasure one gets out of knowing them is the mere sense of knowing.", "(이제 도러시아의 고백이 진솔하다는 것을 의심할 수 없었다.) \"예술은 인위적이고 꾸민 양식이 아주 많은 오래된 언어여서, 때로는 그것들을 알게 됨으로써 얻는 주된 즐거움이 그저 안다는 느낌일 뿐이지요."], ["I enjoy the art of all sorts here immensely; but I suppose if I could pick my enjoyment to pieces I should find it made up of many different threads.", "저는 여기서 온갖 종류의 예술을 더없이 즐기고 있습니다. 하지만 제 즐거움을 낱낱이 분해해 본다면, 그것이 여러 가닥의 다른 실들로 짜여 있음을 알게 될 것 같아요."], ["There is something in daubing a little one’s self, and having an idea of the process.”", "자기도 조금쯤 서툴게나마 그려 보고 그 과정을 알고 있다는 데서 오는 무언가가 있거든요.\""]], [["“You mean perhaps to be a painter?”", "\"그럼 어쩌면 화가가 되실 작정인가요?\""], ["said Dorothea, with a new direction of interest.", "도러시아가 새롭게 관심을 돌리며 말했다."], ["“You mean to make painting your profession?", "\"그림을 직업으로 삼으실 생각이세요?"], ["Mr. Casaubon will like to hear that you have chosen a profession.”", "캐소본 씨도 당신이 직업을 정했다는 말을 들으면 좋아할 거예요.\""]], [["“No, oh no,” said Will, with some coldness.", "\"아니요, 오, 아닙니다.\""], ["“I have quite made up my mind against it.", "윌이 다소 냉랭하게 말했다. \"그쪽은 완전히 마음을 접었습니다."], ["It is too one-sided a life.", "너무 한쪽으로 치우친 삶이거든요."], ["I have been seeing a great deal of the German artists here: I travelled from Frankfort with one of them.", "저는 여기서 독일 화가들을 많이 보아 왔습니다. 프랑크푸르트에서 그들 중 한 명과 함께 여행을 했지요."], ["Some are fine, even brilliant fellows—but I should not like to get into their way of looking at the world entirely from the studio point of view.”", "더러는 훌륭하고, 심지어 뛰어난 친구들도 있어요 — 하지만 세상을 온전히 화실의 관점에서만 바라보는 그들의 방식에 빠져들고 싶지는 않습니다.\""]], [["“That I can understand,”", "\"그 점은 이해할 수 있어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, cordially.", "도러시아가 다정하게 말했다."], ["“And in Rome it seems as if there were so many things which are more wanted in the world than pictures.", "\"그리고 로마에서는 그림보다 이 세상에 더 필요한 것들이 너무도 많은 것처럼 보여요."], ["But if you have a genius for painting, would it not be right to take that as a guide?", "하지만 당신께 그림에 대한 천부적 재능이 있다면, 그것을 길잡이로 삼는 것이 옳지 않을까요?"], ["Perhaps you might do better things than these—or different, so that there might not be so many pictures almost all alike in the same place.”", "어쩌면 당신은 이런 것들보다 더 나은 것을 — 아니면 다른 것을 그려, 한 장소에 거의 다 비슷비슷한 그림들이 그렇게 많지 않도록 할 수도 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["There was no mistaking this simplicity, and Will was won by it into frankness.", "이 순박함은 오해의 여지가 없었고, 윌은 그것에 마음이 끌려 솔직해졌다."], ["“A man must have a very rare genius to make changes of that sort.", "\"그런 종류의 변화를 일으키려면 아주 드문 천재성이 있어야 합니다."], ["I am afraid mine would not carry me even to the pitch of doing well what has been done already, at least not so well as to make it worth while.", "저의 재능은 이미 이루어진 것을 잘 해내는 수준에도, 적어도 해 볼 만한 가치가 있을 만큼 잘 해내는 수준에도 이르지 못할까 봐 걱정입니다."], ["And I should never succeed in anything by dint of drudgery.", "그리고 저는 고된 노력으로는 어떤 것도 결코 이루지 못할 거예요."], ["If things don’t come easily to me I never get them.”", "무언가가 쉽게 다가오지 않으면 저는 절대 손에 넣지 못하거든요.\""]], [["“I have heard Mr. Casaubon say that he regrets your want of patience,”", "\"캐소본 씨가 당신의 인내심 부족을 안타까워한다는 말을 들은 적이 있어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, gently.", "도러시아가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["She was rather shocked at this mode of taking all life as a holiday.", "그녀는 모든 삶을 휴일처럼 받아들이는 이런 태도에 다소 충격을 받았다."]], [["“Yes, I know Mr. Casaubon’s opinion.", "\"네, 캐소본 씨의 견해는 압니다."], ["He and I differ.”", "그분과 저는 생각이 다릅니다.\""]], [["The slight streak of contempt in this hasty reply offended Dorothea.", "이 성급한 대답에 살짝 배어 있는 경멸의 기색이 도러시아의 비위를 거슬렀다."], ["She was all the more susceptible about Mr. Casaubon because of her morning’s trouble.", "아침의 괴로움 때문에 그녀는 캐소본 씨에 관한 일에 더욱 예민해져 있었다."]], [["“Certainly you differ,”", "\"확실히 두 분은 다르지요.\""], ["she said, rather proudly.", "그녀가 다소 자랑스럽게 말했다."], ["“I did not think of comparing you: such power of persevering devoted labor as Mr.", "\"저는 당신을 그분과 비교할 생각은 없었어요."], ["Casaubon’s is not common.”", "캐소본 씨처럼 끈기 있고 헌신적으로 노력하는 힘은 흔한 게 아니거든요.\""]], [["Will saw that she was offended, but this only gave an additional impulse to the new irritation of his latent dislike towards Mr. Casaubon.", "윌은 그녀가 기분이 상한 것을 알아챘지만, 이는 오히려 캐소본 씨를 향한 그의 잠재된 반감이 새롭게 일으킨 짜증에 한층 더 박차를 가했을 뿐이었다."], ["It was too intolerable that Dorothea should be worshipping this husband: such weakness in a woman is pleasant to no man but the husband in question.", "도러시아가 이런 남편을 숭배하다니 참을 수 없는 노릇이었다. 여자의 그런 나약함은 다른 어떤 남자도 아닌 바로 그 남편에게만 흐뭇한 법이다."], ["Mortals are easily tempted to pinch the life out of their neighbor’s buzzing glory, and think that such killing is no murder.", "인간은 이웃의 윙윙대는 영광에서 생명을 짓눌러 없애 버리고도, 그런 살해를 살인이 아니라고 여기는 유혹에 쉽게 빠진다."]], [["“No, indeed,”", "\"네, 정말 그렇습니다.\""], ["he answered, promptly.", "그가 즉각 대답했다."], ["“And therefore it is a pity that it should be thrown away, as so much English scholarship is, for want of knowing what is being done by the rest of the world.", "\"그래서 더욱 안타까운 일이지요. 세상의 다른 사람들이 무엇을 하고 있는지 모르는 탓에, 그토록 많은 영국 학문이 그러하듯이 그 노력이 헛되이 버려진다면 말입니다."], ["If Mr. Casaubon read German he would save himself a great deal of trouble.”", "캐소본 씨가 독일어를 읽으신다면 많은 수고를 덜 수 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["“I do not understand you,”", "\"무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, startled and anxious.", "도러시아가 깜짝 놀라 불안해하며 말했다."]], [["“I merely mean,”", "\"제 말은 그저,\" 윌이 무심하게 말했다."], ["said Will, in an offhand way, “that the Germans have taken the lead in historical inquiries, and they laugh at results which are got by groping about in woods with a pocket-compass while they have made good roads. When I was with Mr.", "\"역사 연구에서는 독일인들이 앞서가고 있고, 그들은 자기들이 잘 닦인 길을 만들어 놓은 마당에 누군가가 주머니 나침반을 들고 숲속을 더듬어 얻어 낸 결과를 비웃는다는 겁니다."], ["Casaubon I saw that he deafened himself in that direction: it was almost against his will that he read a Latin treatise written by a German.", "제가 캐소본 씨와 함께 있을 때 보니, 그분은 그쪽 방면에는 귀를 닫고 계셨습니다. 독일인이 쓴 라틴어 논문 한 편을 읽는 것조차 거의 마지못해 하셨지요."], ["I was very sorry.”", "저는 무척 안타까웠습니다.\""]], [["Will only thought of giving a good pinch that would annihilate that vaunted laboriousness, and was unable to imagine the mode in which Dorothea would be wounded. Young Mr.", "윌은 그저 그 자랑스러워하는 근면함을 무너뜨릴 통렬한 한 방을 먹일 생각만 했을 뿐, 도러시아가 어떤 식으로 상처받을지는 상상하지 못했다."], ["Ladislaw was not at all deep himself in German writers; but very little achievement is required in order to pity another man’s shortcomings.", "젊은 래디슬로 본인도 독일 저술가들에 그다지 정통한 것은 아니었다. 하지만 남의 결점을 가엾이 여기는 데는 아주 작은 성취만 있으면 족한 법이다."]], [["Poor Dorothea felt a pang at the thought that the labor of her husband’s life might be void, which left her no energy to spare for the question whether this young relative who was so much obliged to him ought not to have repressed his observation.", "가엾은 도러시아는 남편이 평생 바쳐 온 노력이 헛수고일지도 모른다는 생각에 가슴이 아렸고, 그 통증에 짓눌려, 그토록 신세를 진 이 젊은 친척이 그런 말을 삼갔어야 하지 않았는가 하는 물음에 마음 쓸 여력이 없었다."], ["She did not even speak, but sat looking at her hands, absorbed in the piteousness of that thought.", "그녀는 말조차 하지 못하고, 그 처량한 생각에 사로잡힌 채 자기 두 손만 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["Will, however, having given that annihilating pinch, was rather ashamed, imagining from Dorothea’s silence that he had offended her still more; and having also a conscience about plucking the tail-feathers from a benefactor.", "그러나 그 무너뜨리는 한 방을 먹인 윌은 자못 부끄러워졌으니, 도러시아의 침묵을 보고 그녀를 더욱 화나게 한 것이라 짐작한 데다, 은인의 꽁지깃을 뽑아 버린 데 대한 양심의 가책도 있었던 것이다."]], [["“I regretted it especially,”", "\"제가 특히 안타까워한 까닭은,\""], ["he resumed, taking the usual course from detraction to insincere eulogy, “because of my gratitude and respect towards my cousin.", "그가 헐뜯기에서 진심 아닌 찬사로 으레 옮겨 가며 말을 이었다. \"제 사촌에 대한 감사와 존경 때문입니다."], ["It would not signify so much in a man whose talents and character were less distinguished.”", "재능과 인품이 그만큼 빼어나지 않은 사람이라면 그리 큰 문제도 아닐 테니까요.\""]], [["Dorothea raised her eyes, brighter than usual with excited feeling, and said in her saddest recitative, “How I wish I had learned German when I was at Lausanne!", "도러시아는 격앙된 감정으로 평소보다 더 빛나는 눈을 들어, 더없이 서글픈 음조로 말했다. \"로잔에 있을 때 독일어를 배워 두었더라면 얼마나 좋았을까요!"], ["There were plenty of German teachers.", "독일어 선생이 얼마든지 있었는데요."], ["But now I can be of no use.”", "하지만 이제 저는 아무 도움도 될 수 없네요.\""]], [["There was a new light, but still a mysterious light, for Will in Dorothea’s last words.", "윌에게 도러시아의 마지막 말은 새로운 빛, 그러나 여전히 알 수 없는 빛이었다."], ["The question how she had come to accept Mr. Casaubon—which he had dismissed when he first saw her by saying that she must be disagreeable in spite of appearances—was not now to be answered on any such short and easy method.", "그녀가 어떻게 캐소본 씨를 받아들이게 되었는가 하는 의문은 — 처음 그녀를 보았을 때는 겉모습과 달리 까다로운 사람일 게 분명하다고 일축해 버렸던 의문이었지만 — 이제 그렇게 간단하고 손쉬운 방법으로는 답할 수 없는 것이 되었다."], ["Whatever else she might be, she was not disagreeable.", "그녀가 다른 무엇이든 간에, 까다로운 사람은 아니었다."], ["She was not coldly clever and indirectly satirical, but adorably simple and full of feeling.", "그녀는 차갑게 영리하고 에둘러 풍자하는 사람이 아니라, 사랑스러우리만치 순박하고 감정이 충만한 사람이었다."], ["She was an angel beguiled.", "그녀는 속아 넘어간 천사였다."], ["It would be a unique delight to wait and watch for the melodious fragments in which her heart and soul came forth so directly and ingenuously.", "그녀의 마음과 영혼이 그토록 직접적이고 천진하게 흘러나오는 그 가락 같은 단편들을 기다리며 지켜보는 것은 더없이 특별한 기쁨일 터였다."], ["The Aeolian harp again came into his mind.", "다시금 그의 마음속에 아이올로스의 하프가 떠올랐다."]], [["She must have made some original romance for herself in this marriage. And if Mr.", "그녀는 이 결혼에서 자기 나름의 독창적인 낭만을 지어냈음에 틀림없었다."], ["Casaubon had been a dragon who had carried her off to his lair with his talons simply and without legal forms, it would have been an unavoidable feat of heroism to release her and fall at her feet.", "만약 캐소본 씨가 법적 절차도 없이 그저 발톱으로 그녀를 낚아채 자기 굴로 끌고 간 용이었다면, 그녀를 풀어 주고 그녀의 발치에 엎드리는 것이야말로 피할 수 없는 영웅적 위업이었으리라. 하지만 그는 용보다 더 다루기 힘든 존재였다."], ["But he was something more unmanageable than a dragon: he was a benefactor with collective society at his back, and he was at that moment entering the room in all the unimpeachable correctness of his demeanor, while Dorothea was looking animated with a newly roused alarm and regret, and Will was looking animated with his admiring speculation about her feelings.", "그는 사회 집단 전체를 등에 업은 은인이었고, 바로 그 순간 흠잡을 데 없이 단정한 거동으로 방에 들어서고 있었다. 한편 도러시아는 새로 깨어난 불안과 회한으로 생기를 띠고 있었고, 윌은 그녀의 감정에 대한 흠모 어린 억측으로 생기를 띠고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon felt a surprise which was quite unmixed with pleasure, but he did not swerve from his usual politeness of greeting, when Will rose and explained his presence.", "캐소본 씨는 기쁨이라고는 전혀 섞이지 않은 놀라움을 느꼈지만, 윌이 일어나 자신이 찾아온 까닭을 설명하자 평소의 정중한 인사에서 벗어나지 않았다."], ["Mr. Casaubon was less happy than usual, and this perhaps made him look all the dimmer and more faded; else, the effect might easily have been produced by the contrast of his young cousin’s appearance.", "캐소본 씨는 평소보다 덜 행복했고, 어쩌면 그 때문에 한층 더 흐릿하고 빛바래 보였는지도 모른다."], ["The first impression on seeing Will was one of sunny brightness, which added to the uncertainty of his changing expression.", "그게 아니라면, 그런 인상은 젊은 사촌의 모습과 대비되어 손쉽게 빚어졌을 수도 있다. 윌을 처음 보았을 때 받는 인상은 햇살처럼 환한 밝음이었고, 이는 변화무쌍한 그의 표정이 주는 종잡을 수 없는 느낌을 더했다."], ["Surely, his very features changed their form, his jaw looked sometimes large and sometimes small; and the little ripple in his nose was a preparation for metamorphosis.", "정말이지 그의 이목구비 자체가 모양을 바꾸는 듯했다. 그의 턱은 때로 커 보이다가 때로 작아 보였고, 콧등의 자그마한 잔주름은 무슨 변신을 준비하는 것 같았다."], ["When he turned his head quickly his hair seemed to shake out light, and some persons thought they saw decided genius in this coruscation.", "그가 고개를 휙 돌리면 머리카락에서 빛이 흩날리는 듯했고, 어떤 사람들은 그 반짝임에서 분명한 천재성을 보았다고 여겼다."], ["Mr. Casaubon, on the contrary, stood rayless.", "이와 반대로 캐소본 씨는 광채라고는 없이 서 있었다."]], [["As Dorothea’s eyes were turned anxiously on her husband she was perhaps not insensible to the contrast, but it was only mingled with other causes in making her more conscious of that new alarm on his behalf which was the first stirring of a pitying tenderness fed by the realities of his lot and not by her own dreams.", "도러시아의 두 눈이 남편에게 불안하게 쏠려 있었으니, 어쩌면 그녀도 그 대비를 느끼지 못한 것은 아니었으리라. 하지만 그것은 그저 다른 여러 원인과 뒤섞여, 남편을 위한 그 새로운 불안을 그녀가 더욱 의식하게 만들었을 뿐이다."], ["Yet it was a source of greater freedom to her that Will was there; his young equality was agreeable, and also perhaps his openness to conviction.", "그 불안은 자기 자신의 꿈이 아니라 남편이 처한 현실에서 자라난, 연민 어린 다정함의 첫 움직임이었다. 그렇지만 윌이 거기 있다는 것은 그녀에게 한결 더 큰 자유의 원천이었다."], ["She felt an immense need of some one to speak to, and she had never before seen any one who seemed so quick and pliable, so likely to understand everything.", "그의 젊은 대등함이 유쾌했고, 또한 어쩌면 그가 설득에 열려 있다는 점도 그러했다. 그녀는 누군가에게 이야기하고 싶은 절실한 욕구를 느꼈는데, 이전까지 그토록 명민하고 유연한, 무엇이든 다 이해할 것 같은 사람은 본 적이 없었다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon gravely hoped that Will was passing his time profitably as well as pleasantly in Rome—had thought his intention was to remain in South Germany—but begged him to come and dine to-morrow, when he could converse more at large: at present he was somewhat weary.", "캐소본 씨는 윌이 로마에서 유익하고도 즐겁게 시간을 보내고 있기를 바란다고 점잖게 말하고는 — 윌의 의도가 남부 독일에 머무는 것인 줄 알았다고 하면서 — 내일 저녁 식사를 하러 와서 좀 더 폭넓게 이야기를 나누자고 청했다."], ["Ladislaw understood, and accepting the invitation immediately took his leave.", "지금은 다소 피곤하다는 것이었다. 래디슬로는 알아차렸고, 초대를 받아들이고는 곧바로 작별을 고했다."]], [["Dorothea’s eyes followed her husband anxiously, while he sank down wearily at the end of a sofa, and resting his elbow supported his head and looked on the floor.", "캐소본 씨가 소파 끝에 지친 듯 주저앉아 팔꿈치를 괴고 머리를 받친 채 바닥을 내려다보는 동안, 도러시아의 눈은 불안하게 남편을 좇았다."], ["A little flushed, and with bright eyes, she seated herself beside him, and said—", "얼굴이 살짝 붉어지고 눈을 반짝이며 그녀는 그의 곁에 앉아 말했다."]], [["“Forgive me for speaking so hastily to you this morning.", "\"오늘 아침 그렇게 성급하게 말씀드려서 용서해 주세요."], ["I was wrong.", "제가 잘못했어요."], ["I fear I hurt you and made the day more burdensome.”", "당신께 상처를 드리고 하루를 더 무겁게 만든 것 같아 걱정이에요.\""]], [["“I am glad that you feel that, my dear,”", "\"당신이 그렇게 느낀다니 기쁘구려, 여보.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon.", "캐소본 씨가 말했다."], ["He spoke quietly and bowed his head a little, but there was still an uneasy feeling in his eyes as he looked at her.", "그는 조용히 말하며 고개를 약간 숙였지만, 그녀를 바라보는 그의 눈에는 여전히 불편한 기색이 남아 있었다."]], [["“But you do forgive me?”", "\"하지만 저를 용서하시는 거지요?\""], ["said Dorothea, with a quick sob.", "도러시아가 흐느낌을 삼키며 재빨리 말했다."], ["In her need for some manifestation of feeling she was ready to exaggerate her own fault.", "어떤 감정의 표현을 갈구하던 그녀는 자기 잘못을 과장할 준비가 되어 있었다."], ["Would not love see returning penitence afar off, and fall on its neck and kiss it?", "사랑이라면 멀리서 돌아오는 뉘우침을 알아보고, 그 목을 끌어안고 입맞추지 않겠는가?"]], [["“My dear Dorothea—‘who with repentance is not satisfied, is not of heaven nor earth:’—you do not think me worthy to be banished by that severe sentence,”", "\"내 사랑 도러시아 — '뉘우침으로도 흡족하지 않은 자는 하늘에도 땅에도 속하지 못하나니' — 당신은 내가 그토록 엄한 선고로 추방당할 만한 사람이라고는 생각하지 않겠지.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, exerting himself to make a strong statement, and also to smile faintly.", "캐소본 씨는 단호한 말을 하려고, 또한 희미하게나마 미소를 지으려고 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["Dorothea was silent, but a tear which had come up with the sob would insist on falling.", "도러시아는 잠자코 있었지만, 흐느낌과 함께 차올랐던 눈물 한 방울이 기어이 떨어지고야 말았다."]], [["“You are excited, my dear.", "\"당신은 흥분했구려, 여보."], ["And I also am feeling some unpleasant consequences of too much mental disturbance,”", "나 또한 너무 큰 마음의 동요로 인해 좀 좋지 않은 결과를 느끼고 있다오.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon.", "캐소본 씨가 말했다."], ["In fact, he had it in his thought to tell her that she ought not to have received young Ladislaw in his absence: but he abstained, partly from the sense that it would be ungracious to bring a new complaint in the moment of her penitent acknowledgment, partly because he wanted to avoid further agitation of himself by speech, and partly because he was too proud to betray that jealousy of disposition which was not so exhausted on his scholarly compeers that there was none to spare in other directions.", "사실 그는 자신이 없는 사이에 젊은 래디슬로를 맞아들이지 말았어야 했다고 그녀에게 말할 생각이 있었다. 하지만 그는 입을 다물었으니, 한편으로는 그녀가 뉘우치며 잘못을 시인하는 마당에 새로운 불평을 꺼내는 것은 무례한 일이라는 의식 때문이었고, 한편으로는 말로 자신을 더욱 동요시키는 것을 피하고 싶었기 때문이며, 또 한편으로는 학자 동료들에게 다 소진되지 않고 다른 방면에 쓸 만큼 남아 있던 그 시샘하는 기질을 드러내기에는 너무 자존심이 강했기 때문이었다."], ["There is a sort of jealousy which needs very little fire: it is hardly a passion, but a blight bred in the cloudy, damp despondency of uneasy egoism.", "아주 작은 불씨만으로도 충분한 질투가 있다. 그것은 정념이라기보다는, 불안한 자기중심주의의 흐리고 눅눅한 낙담 속에서 자라난 마름병에 가깝다."]], [["“I think it is time for us to dress,”", "\"이제 옷을 갈아입을 시간인 것 같소.\""], ["he added, looking at his watch.", "그가 시계를 보며 덧붙였다."], ["They both rose, and there was never any further allusion between them to what had passed on this day.", "두 사람은 모두 일어섰고, 그날 있었던 일에 관해서는 그 후로 결코 다시 언급하지 않았다."]], [["But Dorothea remembered it to the last with the vividness with which we all remember epochs in our experience when some dear expectation dies, or some new motive is born.", "그러나 도러시아는 그 일을, 어떤 소중한 기대가 죽거나 어떤 새로운 동기가 태어나는, 우리 경험 속의 분기점을 누구나 기억하는 그 생생함으로 끝끝내 기억했다."], ["Today she had begun to see that she had been under a wild illusion in expecting a response to her feeling from Mr. Casaubon, and she had felt the waking of a presentiment that there might be a sad consciousness in his life which made as great a need on his side as on her own.", "오늘 그녀는 자신이 캐소본 씨에게서 자기 감정에 대한 응답을 기대했던 것이 한낱 터무니없는 환상이었음을 깨닫기 시작했고, 그의 삶에도 어떤 슬픈 의식이 있어 그녀 못지않게 그에게도 절실한 갈망이 있을지 모른다는 예감이 깨어나는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["We are all of us born in moral stupidity, taking the world as an udder to feed our supreme selves: Dorothea had early begun to emerge from that stupidity, but yet it had been easier to her to imagine how she would devote herself to Mr.", "우리는 모두 도덕적 우둔함 속에서 태어나, 세상을 우리의 지고한 자아를 먹여 살리는 젖통쯤으로 여긴다."], ["Casaubon, and become wise and strong in his strength and wisdom, than to conceive with that distinctness which is no longer reflection but feeling—an idea wrought back to the directness of sense, like the solidity of objects—that he had an equivalent centre of self, whence the lights and shadows must always fall with a certain difference.", "도러시아는 일찍이 그 우둔함에서 벗어나기 시작했지만, 그럼에도 그녀에게는 자신이 어떻게 캐소본 씨에게 헌신하여 그의 힘과 지혜 안에서 현명하고 강해질 것인가를 상상하는 편이, 그에게도 자기 나름의 대등한 자아의 중심이 있어 거기서부터 빛과 그늘이 언제나 어떤 차이를 두고 비쳐 들 수밖에 없다는 사실을 — 더는 사색이 아니라 감정으로, 사물의 견고함처럼 감각의 직접성으로 되살아난 관념으로 — 그토록 또렷하게 인식하기보다 더 쉬웠던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII.", "# CHAPTER XXII."]], [["“Nous câusames longtemps; elle était simple et bonne.", "우리는 오랫동안 이야기를 나누었네. 그녀는 단순하고 선했지."], ["Ne sachant pas le mal, elle faisait le bien; Des richesses du coeur elle me fit l’aumône, Et tout en écoutant comme le coeur se donne, Sans oser y penser je lui donnai le mien; Elle emporta ma vie, et n’en sut jamais rien.” —ALFRED DE MUSSET.", "악을 알지 못했기에, 그녀는 선을 행했네."]], [["Will Ladislaw was delightfully agreeable at dinner the next day, and gave no opportunity for Mr. Casaubon to show disapprobation. On the contrary it seemed to Dorothea that Will had a happier way of drawing her husband into conversation and of deferentially listening to him than she had ever observed in any one before. To be sure, the listeners about Tipton were not highly gifted! Will talked a good deal himself, but what he said was thrown in with such rapidity, and with such an unimportant air of saying something by the way, that it seemed a gay little chime after the great bell. If Will was not always perfect, this was certainly one of his good days. He described touches of incident among the poor people in Rome, only to be seen by one who could move about freely; he found himself in agreement with Mr. Casaubon as to the unsound opinions of Middleton concerning the relations of Judaism and Catholicism; and passed easily to a half-enthusiastic half-playful picture of the enjoyment he got out of the very miscellaneousness of Rome, which made the mind flexible with constant comparison, and saved you from seeing the world’s ages as a set of box-like partitions without vital connection. Mr. Casaubon’s studies, Will observed, had always been of too broad a kind for that, and he had perhaps never felt any such sudden effect, but for himself he confessed that Rome had given him quite a new sense of history as a whole: the fragments stimulated his imagination and made him constructive. Then occasionally, but not too often, he appealed to Dorothea, and discussed what she said, as if her sentiment were an item to be considered in the final judgment even of the Madonna di Foligno or the Laocoon. A sense of contributing to form the world’s opinion makes conversation particularly cheerful; and Mr. Casaubon too was not without his pride in his young wife, who spoke better than most women, as indeed he had perceived in choosing her.", "마음의 풍요로움으로 그녀는 내게 자비를 베풀었고,"]], [["Since things were going on so pleasantly, Mr. Casaubon’s statement that his labors in the Library would be suspended for a couple of days, and that after a brief renewal he should have no further reason for staying in Rome, encouraged Will to urge that Mrs. Casaubon should not go away without seeing a studio or two. Would not Mr. Casaubon take her? That sort of thing ought not to be missed: it was quite special: it was a form of life that grew like a small fresh vegetation with its population of insects on huge fossils. Will would be happy to conduct them—not to anything wearisome, only to a few examples.", "마음이 어떻게 주어지는지 귀 기울이는 동안,\n감히 생각할 겨를도 없이 나는 그녀에게 내 마음을 주었네."]], [["Mr.", "그녀는 내 삶을 가져갔으나, 끝내 그것을 알지 못했네."], ["Casaubon, seeing Dorothea look earnestly towards him, could not but ask her if she would be interested in such visits: he was now at her service during the whole day; and it was agreed that Will should come on the morrow and drive with them.", "— 알프레드 드 뮈세"]], [["Will could not omit Thorwaldsen, a living celebrity about whom even Mr. Casaubon inquired, but before the day was far advanced he led the way to the studio of his friend Adolf Naumann, whom he mentioned as one of the chief renovators of Christian art, one of those who had not only revived but expanded that grand conception of supreme events as mysteries at which the successive ages were spectators, and in relation to which the great souls of all periods became as it were contemporaries. Will added that he had made himself Naumann’s pupil for the nonce.", "윌 래디슬로는 다음 날 만찬 자리에서 더없이 유쾌하고 상냥하게 굴어서, 캐소본 씨가 못마땅함을 드러낼 만한 기회를 조금도 주지 않았다. 오히려 도러시아가 보기에 윌은 자기 남편을 대화로 끌어들이고 공손하게 그의 말에 귀 기울이는 데 있어, 지금껏 그녀가 누구에게서도 본 적 없는 더 행복한 솜씨를 지닌 것 같았다. 하긴 티프턴 근방의 청자들이라야 그리 뛰어난 사람들은 아니었다! 윌도 적잖이 말을 했지만, 그가 하는 말은 어찌나 빠르게, 그리고 무언가를 지나가는 길에 슬쩍 던진다는 듯 대수롭지 않은 태도로 끼어드는지, 마치 거대한 종소리 뒤에 울리는 경쾌하고 자그마한 종소리 같았다. 윌이 늘 완벽한 것은 아니었지만, 이날은 분명 그의 좋은 날들 가운데 하나였다. 그는 로마의 가난한 사람들 사이에서 일어나는 소소한 일들, 자유롭게 돌아다닐 수 있는 사람만이 볼 수 있는 장면들을 묘사했다. 그는 유대교와 가톨릭교의 관계에 관한 미들턴의 부당한 견해에 대해 캐소본 씨와 의견이 일치함을 알게 되었다. 그리고 자연스럽게, 로마의 바로 그 잡다함에서 얻는 즐거움을 절반은 열광적으로 절반은 장난스럽게 그려 보이는 데로 옮겨 갔는데, 그 잡다함이야말로 끊임없는 비교로 정신을 유연하게 만들고, 세계의 여러 시대를 생생한 연관 없이 칸칸이 나뉜 상자 같은 것으로 보지 않게 해 준다는 것이었다. 캐소본 씨의 연구는 언제나 그렇게 좁은 종류와는 거리가 멀 만큼 폭넓었으니, 어쩌면 그런 갑작스러운 효과를 한 번도 느껴 본 적이 없었을 거라고 윌은 말했다. 그러나 자기 자신으로 말하자면, 로마가 역사를 하나의 전체로 보는 아주 새로운 감각을 안겨 주었음을 고백한다고 했다. 그 단편들이 자기 상상력을 자극하여 건설적으로 만들었다는 것이다. 그러다 이따금, 그러나 지나치게 자주는 아니게, 그는 도러시아에게 의견을 구하고 그녀가 한 말을 논의했는데, 마치 그녀의 감상이 폴리뇨의 마돈나나 라오콘 군상에 대한 최종 판단에서조차 고려해야 할 한 항목이라도 되는 듯했다. 세계의 의견을 형성하는 데 기여한다는 느낌은 대화를 유난히 활기차게 만드는 법이다. 그리고 캐소본 씨 역시 자기 젊은 아내에 대한 자부심이 없지 않았으니, 그녀는 대부분의 여성보다 말을 잘했고, 그가 그녀를 택할 때 실제로 알아본 바이기도 했다. 일이 이토록 즐겁게 풀려 가던 차에, 도서관에서의 작업을 이틀쯤 중단하겠으며 짧은 재개 후에는 더 이상 로마에 머물 이유가 없으리라는 캐소본 씨의 말은, 캐소본 부인이 화실 한두 군데는 보지 않고 떠나서는 안 된다고 권할 용기를 윌에게 주었다. 캐소본 씨가 그녀를 데려가지 않으시겠는가? 그런 것은 놓쳐서는 안 될 일이었다. 아주 특별한 것이었으니, 거대한 화석 위에 곤충 떼와 더불어 자라는 작고 싱싱한 식물 같은 삶의 한 형태였다. 윌이 기꺼이 안내하겠다고 했다. 따분한 것은 빼고, 그저 몇몇 본보기만 보여 주겠다는 것이었다."]], [["“I have been making some oil-sketches under him,” said Will.", "도러시아가 자기 쪽을 간절히 바라보는 것을 본 캐소본 씨는, 그런 방문에 관심이 있겠느냐고 그녀에게 묻지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“I hate copying. I must put something of my own in.", "이제 그는 하루 종일 그녀를 위해 시간을 낼 수 있었다."], ["Naumann has been painting the Saints drawing the Car of the Church, and I have been making a sketch of Marlowe’s Tamburlaine Driving the Conquered Kings in his Chariot.", "그리하여 윌이 이튿날 와서 함께 마차를 타기로 합의되었다."], ["I am not so ecclesiastical as Naumann, and I sometimes twit him with his excess of meaning.", "윌은 살아 있는 명사인 토르발센을 빼놓을 수 없었으니, 캐소본 씨조차 그에 대해 물어볼 정도였다."], ["But this time I mean to outdo him in breadth of intention.", "그러나 날이 그리 깊어지기 전에 그는 친구 아돌프 나우만의 화실로 길을 이끌었다."], ["I take Tamburlaine in his chariot for the tremendous course of the world’s physical history lashing on the harnessed dynasties. In my opinion, that is a good mythical interpretation.”", "그를 가리켜 윌은 기독교 미술을 되살린 주요 인물 가운데 하나, 지고한 사건들을 신비로 보는 저 웅대한 관념—거기서 잇따른 시대들이 관객이 되고, 그와 관련하여 모든 시대의 위대한 영혼들이 말하자면 동시대인이 되는—을 되살렸을 뿐 아니라 확장한 이들 중 하나라고 소개했다."], ["Will here looked at Mr. Casaubon, who received this offhand treatment of symbolism very uneasily, and bowed with a neutral air.", "윌은 자기가 당분간 나우만의 제자로 들어가 있다고 덧붙였다."]], [["“The sketch must be very grand, if it conveys so much,”", "\"그 사람 밑에서 유화 습작을 몇 점 그려 봤어요.\" 윌이 말했다."], ["said Dorothea.", "\"저는 모사가 싫습니다."], ["“I should need some explanation even of the meaning you give.", "제 것을 뭔가 집어넣어야 직성이 풀려요. 나우만은 성인들이 교회의 수레를 끄는 그림을 그려 왔는데, 저는 말로의 탬벌레인이 정복당한 왕들을 자기 전차에 매어 몰고 가는 장면을 습작으로 그려 봤습니다."], ["Do you intend Tamburlaine to represent earthquakes and volcanoes?”", "저는 나우만만큼 교회적이지 않아서, 가끔은 의미가 너무 넘친다고 그를 놀리곤 하지요. 하지만 이번에는 의도의 폭에서 그를 능가할 작정입니다."], ["“Oh yes,” said Will, laughing, “and migrations of races and clearings of forests—and America and the steam-engine.", "저는 전차에 탄 탬벌레인을, 멍에 메인 왕조들을 채찍질해 내모는 세계의 물리적 역사의 어마어마한 흐름으로 삼은 거예요."], ["Everything you can imagine!”", "제 생각에 그건 훌륭한 신화적 해석이죠.\" 윌은 여기서 캐소본 씨를 바라보았는데, 그는 상징을 이렇게 즉흥적으로 다루는 것을 몹시 불편하게 받아들이며 무덤덤한 태도로 고개를 까딱했다."]], [["“What a difficult kind of shorthand!”", "\"그렇게나 많은 것을 담아낸다면 그 습작은 무척 웅대하겠어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, smiling towards her husband.", "도러시아가 말했다. \"저로서는 당신이 부여한 의미조차 약간의 설명이 필요할 것 같아요."], ["“It would require all your knowledge to be able to read it.”", "탬벌레인이 지진과 화산을 나타내게 하실 생각인가요?\""]], [["Mr. Casaubon blinked furtively at Will.", "\"오, 그렇습니다.\""], ["He had a suspicion that he was being laughed at.", "윌이 웃으며 말했다. \"그리고 종족의 이동과 숲의 개간도요."], ["But it was not possible to include Dorothea in the suspicion.", "또 아메리카와 증기 기관도. 당신이 상상할 수 있는 모든 것이죠!\""]], [["They found Naumann painting industriously, but no model was present; his pictures were advantageously arranged, and his own plain vivacious person set off by a dove-colored blouse and a maroon velvet cap, so that everything was as fortunate as if he had expected the beautiful young English lady exactly at that time.", "\"참으로 어려운 종류의 속기로군요!\" 도러시아가 남편 쪽을 보며 미소 지었다. \"그걸 읽어 내려면 당신의 온갖 지식이 다 필요하겠어요.\" 캐소본 씨는 윌을 슬쩍 곁눈질했다. 그는 자기가 조롱당하고 있는 게 아닌가 의심했다. 그러나 그 의심에 도러시아까지 포함시키는 것은 가능한 일이 아니었다."]], [["The painter in his confident English gave little dissertations on his finished and unfinished subjects, seeming to observe Mr.", "그들이 들어섰을 때 나우만은 부지런히 그림을 그리고 있었으나, 모델은 한 사람도 와 있지 않았다. 그의 그림들은 보기 좋게 배치되어 있었고, 수수하나 생기 있는 그 자신의 풍채는 비둘기빛 작업복과 적갈색 벨벳 모자로 한층 돋보였으니, 마치 바로 그 시각에 아름다운 영국 젊은 부인이 오리라 예상이라도 한 듯 모든 것이 더없이 운 좋게 갖추어져 있었다."], ["Casaubon as much as he did Dorothea.", "화가는 자신만만한 영어로 완성작과 미완성작에 관해 자그마한 강론을 늘어놓았는데, 도러시아 못지않게 캐소본 씨도 유심히 관찰하는 듯했다."], ["Will burst in here and there with ardent words of praise, marking out particular merits in his friend’s work; and Dorothea felt that she was getting quite new notions as to the significance of Madonnas seated under inexplicable canopied thrones with the simple country as a background, and of saints with architectural models in their hands, or knives accidentally wedged in their skulls.", "윌은 여기저기서 친구의 작품에서 두드러진 장점들을 짚어 가며 열렬한 찬사의 말을 불쑥불쑥 끼워 넣었다. 그러는 동안 도러시아는, 까닭 모를 닫집 씌운 옥좌 아래 소박한 시골을 배경 삼아 앉은 마돈나들의 의미에 대해, 그리고 손에 건축 모형을 든 성인들이나 두개골에 우연히 칼이 박힌 성인들의 의미에 대해 아주 새로운 관념을 얻고 있다는 느낌이 들었다."], ["Some things which had seemed monstrous to her were gathering intelligibility and even a natural meaning: but all this was apparently a branch of knowledge in which Mr.", "그녀에게 괴이하게만 보였던 어떤 것들이 이해할 만한 것이 되어 가고, 심지어 자연스러운 의미마저 띠어 갔다."], ["Casaubon had not interested himself.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 분명 캐소본 씨가 관심을 두지 않았던 지식의 한 갈래였다."]], [["“I think I would rather feel that painting is beautiful than have to read it as an enigma; but I should learn to understand these pictures sooner than yours with the very wide meaning,”", "\"저는 그림을 수수께끼처럼 읽어 내야 하기보다는 차라리 아름답다고 느끼는 편이 좋아요. 하지만 매우 폭넓은 의미를 지닌 당신 그림보다는 이런 그림들을 더 빨리 이해하게 될 것 같아요.\""], ["said Dorothea, speaking to Will.", "도러시아가 윌에게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t speak of my painting before Naumann,”", "\"나우만 앞에서 제 그림 얘기는 하지 마세요.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“He will tell you, it is all pfuscherei, which is his most opprobrious word!”", "\"그 친구가 그러는데, 그건 전부 푸셔라이라더군요. 그가 쓰는 가장 모욕적인 말이지요!\""]], [["“Is that true?”", "\"그게 정말인가요?\""], ["said Dorothea, turning her sincere eyes on Naumann, who made a slight grimace and said—", "도러시아가 진실한 눈길을 나우만에게로 돌리며 묻자, 그는 살짝 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, he does not mean it seriously with painting.", "\"오, 그림에 대해서 진지하게 하는 말은 아닙니다."], ["His walk must be belles-lettres.", "저 사람이 갈 길은 문—학이지요."], ["That is wi-ide.”", "그건 넓—습니다.\""]], [["Naumann’s pronunciation of the vowel seemed to stretch the word satirically.", "나우만의 모음 발음이 그 말을 빈정대듯 늘이는 듯했다."], ["Will did not half like it, but managed to laugh: and Mr.", "윌은 그것이 영 마음에 들지 않았으나 가까스로 웃어넘겼다."], ["Casaubon, while he felt some disgust at the artist’s German accent, began to entertain a little respect for his judicious severity.", "그리고 캐소본 씨는 그 화가의 독일식 억양에 다소 거부감을 느끼면서도, 그의 사려 깊은 엄격함에 약간의 존경심을 품기 시작했다."]], [["The respect was not diminished when Naumann, after drawing Will aside for a moment and looking, first at a large canvas, then at Mr. Casaubon, came forward again and said—", "나우만이 잠시 윌을 한쪽으로 끌고 가 먼저 커다란 화폭을, 그다음 캐소본 씨를 번갈아 보고 나서 다시 앞으로 나와 이렇게 말했을 때, 그 존경심은 줄어들지 않았다."]], [["“My friend Ladislaw thinks you will pardon me, sir, if I say that a sketch of your head would be invaluable to me for the St.", "\"제 친구 래디슬로 말로는, 제가 이런 말씀을 드려도 용서해 주시리라더군요. 선생님 두상을 습작으로 그릴 수 있다면 저기 제 그림 속 성 토마스 아퀴나스를 위해 더없이 귀중한 자료가 될 것입니다."], ["Thomas Aquinas in my picture there.", "지나친 부탁이지요."], ["It is too much to ask; but I so seldom see just what I want—the idealistic in the real.”", "하지만 제가 원하는 바로 그것—실재 속의 이상적인 것을 이렇게 좀처럼 만나기 어려워서요.\""]], [["“You astonish me greatly, sir,” said Mr.", "\"정말 놀라운 말씀이군요, 선생.\""], ["Casaubon, his looks improved with a glow of delight; “but if my poor physiognomy, which I have been accustomed to regard as of the commonest order, can be of any use to you in furnishing some traits for the angelical doctor, I shall feel honored.", "캐소본 씨가 기쁨의 홍조로 한결 나아진 얼굴로 말했다. \"하나 가장 평범한 부류라고 여겨 온 제 보잘것없는 용모가, 그 천사적 박사의 특징 몇 가지를 갖추는 데 조금이라도 쓸모가 있다면 영광으로 알겠습니다."], ["That is to say, if the operation will not be a lengthy one; and if Mrs. Casaubon will not object to the delay.”", "다만 그 작업이 오래 걸리지 않는다면, 그리고 캐소본 부인이 그 지체를 마다하지 않는다면 말이오.\""]], [["As for Dorothea, nothing could have pleased her more, unless it had been a miraculous voice pronouncing Mr. Casaubon the wisest and worthiest among the sons of men.", "도러시아로 말하자면, 캐소본 씨가 인간의 아들들 가운데 가장 현명하고 가장 훌륭하다고 선포하는 기적의 음성이 아니고서야, 이보다 더 그녀를 기쁘게 할 수 있는 것은 없었다."], ["In that case her tottering faith would have become firm again.", "그런 일이 있었더라면 흔들리던 그녀의 믿음은 다시 굳건해졌으리라."]], [["Naumann’s apparatus was at hand in wonderful completeness, and the sketch went on at once as well as the conversation.", "나우만의 도구는 놀랄 만큼 완벽하게 가까이 갖추어져 있어서, 습작은 대화와 더불어 곧장 진행되었다."], ["Dorothea sat down and subsided into calm silence, feeling happier than she had done for a long while before.", "도러시아는 앉아서 고요한 침묵 속으로 잦아들었으니, 오래도록 느껴 보지 못한 행복을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Every one about her seemed good, and she said to herself that Rome, if she had only been less ignorant, would have been full of beauty: its sadness would have been winged with hope.", "자기 주위의 모든 사람이 선해 보였고, 그녀는 속으로 자기가 그토록 무지하지만 않았던들 로마는 아름다움으로 가득했으리라고, 그 슬픔조차 희망의 날개를 달았으리라고 되뇌었다."], ["No nature could be less suspicious than hers: when she was a child she believed in the gratitude of wasps and the honorable susceptibility of sparrows, and was proportionately indignant when their baseness was made manifest.", "그녀만큼 의심을 모르는 천성도 없었을 것이다. 어린아이였을 때 그녀는 말벌의 고마움과 참새의 고결한 감수성을 믿었으며, 그것들의 비열함이 드러났을 때 그만큼 분개했다."]], [["The adroit artist was asking Mr.", "그 능란한 화가는 캐소본 씨에게 영국 정치에 관한 질문들을 던졌고, 그것은 기나긴 답변을 끌어냈다."], ["Casaubon questions about English polities, which brought long answers, and, Will meanwhile had perched himself on some steps in the background overlooking all.", "그러는 동안 윌은 모든 것을 내려다볼 수 있는 뒤편의 어떤 계단 위에 걸터앉아 있었다."]], [["Presently Naumann said—“Now if I could lay this by for half an hour and take it up again—come and look, Ladislaw—I think it is perfect so far.”", "이윽고 나우만이 말했다. \"자, 이걸 반 시간쯤 미뤄 두었다가 다시 손댈 수 있다면—와서 보게, 래디슬로—여기까지는 완벽한 것 같은데.\""]], [["Will vented those adjuring interjections which imply that admiration is too strong for syntax; and Naumann said in a tone of piteous regret—", "윌은 감탄이 너무 강렬해 문법을 갖출 수 없을 때 나오는 그런 애원조의 감탄사를 터뜨렸다. 그러자 나우만이 가련하게 안타까워하는 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Ah—now—if I could but have had more—but you have other engagements—I could not ask it—or even to come again to-morrow.”", "\"아—이제—조금만 더 시간이 있었더라면—하지만 다른 약속이 있으시니—그걸 청할 수도 없고—내일 다시 와 주십사 할 수도 없겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh, let us stay!”", "\"오, 그냥 더 있어요!\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“We have nothing to do to-day except go about, have we?”", "\"오늘은 여기저기 돌아다니는 것 말고는 할 일이 없잖아요, 그렇지요?\""], ["she added, looking entreatingly at Mr. Casaubon.", "그녀가 캐소본 씨를 간청하듯 바라보며 덧붙였다."], ["“It would be a pity not to make the head as good as possible.”", "\"두상을 되도록 좋게 만들지 못한다면 안타까운 일이에요.\""]], [["“I am at your service, sir, in the matter,”", "\"그 일이라면 제가 시간을 내겠소, 선생.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, with polite condescension.", "캐소본 씨가 정중하게 겸양을 보이며 말했다."], ["“Having given up the interior of my head to idleness, it is as well that the exterior should work in this way.”", "\"내 머릿속은 게으름에 내맡겨 두었으니, 그 바깥쪽이라도 이런 식으로 일하는 편이 좋겠지요.\""]], [["“You are unspeakably good—now I am happy!”", "\"이루 말할 수 없이 친절하십니다—이제 저는 행복합니다!\""], ["said Naumann, and then went on in German to Will, pointing here and there to the sketch as if he were considering that.", "나우만이 말하고는 독일어로 윌에게 무어라 말을 이어 가며, 마치 습작을 살펴보는 양 여기저기를 가리켰다."], ["Putting it aside for a moment, he looked round vaguely, as if seeking some occupation for his visitors, and afterwards turning to Mr. Casaubon, said—", "잠시 그것을 옆으로 치워 두고 그는 손님들에게 무슨 소일거리라도 찾아 주려는 듯 막연히 둘러보더니, 이윽고 캐소본 씨를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps the beautiful bride, the gracious lady, would not be unwilling to let me fill up the time by trying to make a slight sketch of her—not, of course, as you see, for that picture—only as a single study.”", "\"아마도 이 아름다운 신부님께서, 이 우아한 부인께서, 제가 그 시간을 채울 수 있도록 당신의 가벼운 습작 하나를 그려 보게 허락해 주실는지요—물론, 보시다시피, 저 그림을 위한 것은 아니고요—그저 단독 습작으로 말입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Casaubon, bowing, doubted not that Mrs.", "캐소본 씨는 고개를 숙이며 캐소본 부인이 호의를 베풀어 주리라 의심치 않는다고 했고, 도러시아는 곧바로 \"어디에 서면 될까요?\""], ["Casaubon would oblige him, and Dorothea said, at once, “Where shall I put myself?”", "하고 물었다."]], [["Naumann was all apologies in asking her to stand, and allow him to adjust her attitude, to which she submitted without any of the affected airs and laughs frequently thought necessary on such occasions, when the painter said, “It is as Santa Clara that I want you to stand—leaning so, with your cheek against your hand—so—looking at that stool, please, so!”", "나우만은 그녀에게 서 달라고 부탁하며, 자세를 잡아 줄 수 있게 해 달라고 연신 사과를 했다. 그녀는 그런 자리에서 흔히들 필요하다고 여기는 꾸민 태도나 웃음 따위는 전혀 없이 그에게 순순히 따랐고, 화가가 말했다. \"산타 클라라처럼 서 주셨으면 합니다—그렇게 기대어—손에 뺨을 괴고—그렇게—저 의자를 바라봐 주세요, 그렇게요!\""]], [["Will was divided between the inclination to fall at the Saint’s feet and kiss her robe, and the temptation to knock Naumann down while he was adjusting her arm.", "윌은 그 성녀의 발치에 엎드려 그 옷자락에 입 맞추고 싶은 마음과, 나우만이 그녀의 팔을 매만지는 동안 그를 때려눕히고 싶은 충동 사이에서 갈라져 있었다."], ["All this was impudence and desecration, and he repented that he had brought her.", "이 모든 것이 무엄하고 신성을 더럽히는 짓이었으니, 그는 그녀를 데려온 것을 후회했다."]], [["The artist was diligent, and Will recovering himself moved about and occupied Mr.", "화가는 부지런했고, 윌은 마음을 추슬러 이리저리 움직이며 할 수 있는 한 솜씨껏 캐소본 씨를 상대해 주었다."], ["Casaubon as ingeniously as he could; but he did not in the end prevent the time from seeming long to that gentleman, as was clear from his expressing a fear that Mrs. Casaubon would be tired.", "그러나 결국 그 신사에게 시간이 길게 느껴지는 것을 막지는 못했으니, 캐소본 부인이 피곤하지 않을까 염려된다고 그가 말한 데서 그 점이 분명히 드러났다."], ["Naumann took the hint and said—", "나우만은 그 암시를 알아채고 말했다."]], [["“Now, sir, if you can oblige me again; I will release the lady-wife.”", "\"자, 선생님, 다시 한 번 호의를 베풀어 주신다면, 사모님은 그만 놓아 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["So Mr. Casaubon’s patience held out further, and when after all it turned out that the head of Saint Thomas Aquinas would be more perfect if another sitting could be had, it was granted for the morrow.", "그리하여 캐소본 씨의 인내심은 좀 더 버텨 주었고, 결국 한 번 더 앉아 줄 수 있다면 성 토마스 아퀴나스의 두상이 한결 완벽해지리라는 결론이 나자, 이튿날로 그것이 허락되었다."], ["On the morrow Santa Clara too was retouched more than once.", "이튿날 산타 클라라 역시 한 번 이상 수정되었다."], ["The result of all was so far from displeasing to Mr. Casaubon, that he arranged for the purchase of the picture in which Saint Thomas Aquinas sat among the doctors of the Church in a disputation too abstract to be represented, but listened to with more or less attention by an audience above.", "이 모든 것의 결과가 캐소본 씨에게는 그리 언짢지 않아서, 그는 성 토마스 아퀴나스가 교회의 박사들 사이에 앉아—그림으로 나타내기에는 너무 추상적인 논쟁을 벌이지만 위쪽의 청중이 다소간 주의를 기울여 경청하는—그 그림을 사들이기로 했다."], ["The Santa Clara, which was spoken of in the second place, Naumann declared himself to be dissatisfied with—he could not, in conscience, engage to make a worthy picture of it; so about the Santa Clara the arrangement was conditional.", "두 번째로 거론된 산타 클라라에 대해서는, 나우만이 스스로 만족스럽지 못하다고 밝혔다—양심상 그것으로 그럴듯한 그림을 만들겠다고 장담할 수가 없다는 것이었다. 그래서 산타 클라라에 관한 약정은 조건부로 남았다."]], [["I will not dwell on Naumann’s jokes at the expense of Mr.", "그날 저녁 캐소본 씨를 안주 삼은 나우만의 농담들이나, 도러시아의 매력에 관한 그의 찬가에 대해서는 굳이 길게 늘어놓지 않겠다."], ["Casaubon that evening, or on his dithyrambs about Dorothea’s charm, in all which Will joined, but with a difference.", "그 모든 데에 윌도 끼어들었으되, 결은 달랐다."], ["No sooner did Naumann mention any detail of Dorothea’s beauty, than Will got exasperated at his presumption: there was grossness in his choice of the most ordinary words, and what business had he to talk of her lips?", "나우만이 도러시아의 아름다움을 한 가지라도 세세히 언급할라치면, 윌은 그 주제넘음에 분통이 치밀었다. 가장 평범한 말을 골라 쓰는 데에 천박함이 있었으니, 대체 그가 무슨 자격으로 그녀의 입술 따위를 입에 올린단 말인가?"], ["She was not a woman to be spoken of as other women were.", "그녀는 다른 여자들처럼 운위될 여자가 아니었다."], ["Will could not say just what he thought, but he became irritable.", "윌은 자기가 생각하는 바를 콕 집어 말할 수는 없었으나, 점점 신경이 곤두섰다."], ["And yet, when after some resistance he had consented to take the Casaubons to his friend’s studio, he had been allured by the gratification of his pride in being the person who could grant Naumann such an opportunity of studying her loveliness—or rather her divineness, for the ordinary phrases which might apply to mere bodily prettiness were not applicable to her.", "그러면서도, 얼마간 버티다가 캐소본 부부를 친구의 화실로 데려가는 데 동의했을 때, 그는 나우만에게 그녀의 사랑스러움을—아니, 차라리 그녀의 신성함을, 한낱 육체의 어여쁨에나 어울릴 흔한 표현들은 그녀에게 들어맞지 않았으니—연구할 그런 기회를 베풀어 줄 수 있는 사람이라는 자부심의 충족에 끌렸던 것이었다."], ["(Certainly all Tipton and its neighborhood, as well as Dorothea herself, would have been surprised at her beauty being made so much of.", "(티프턴과 그 인근 전체는 물론이고 도러시아 자신조차도, 그녀의 아름다움이 이토록 떠받들어지는 데 분명 놀랐을 것이다."], ["In that part of the world Miss Brooke had been only a “fine young woman.”)", "그 지방에서 브룩 양은 그저 \"잘난 젊은 여자\"였을 뿐이니까.)"]], [["“Oblige me by letting the subject drop, Naumann.", "\"제발 그 얘기는 그만하게, 나우만."], ["Mrs. Casaubon is not to be talked of as if she were a model,”", "캐소본 부인은 마치 모델이라도 되는 양 입에 올릴 분이 아니야.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["Naumann stared at him.", "나우만은 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“Schön!", "\"쇤!"], ["I will talk of my Aquinas.", "그럼 내 아퀴나스 얘기나 하지."], ["The head is not a bad type, after all.", "그 두상은 결국 나쁘지 않은 유형이야."], ["I dare say the great scholastic himself would have been flattered to have his portrait asked for.", "위대한 스콜라 학자 본인도 제 초상을 그려 달라는 청을 받으면 우쭐했을 게 분명해."], ["Nothing like these starchy doctors for vanity!", "허영으로 치면 그 빳빳한 박사님들만 한 자들이 없거든!"], ["It was as I thought: he cared much less for her portrait than his own.”", "내 생각대로였어. 그 사람은 제 초상보다 아내 초상에 훨씬 마음을 덜 쓰더군.\""]], [["“He’s a cursed white-blooded pedantic coxcomb,”", "\"그자는 빌어먹을, 피가 허옇게 식은 현학적인 멋쟁이 바보야.\""], ["said Will, with gnashing impetuosity.", "윌이 이를 갈듯 격하게 말했다."], ["His obligations to Mr. Casaubon were not known to his hearer, but Will himself was thinking of them, and wishing that he could discharge them all by a check.", "캐소본 씨에 대한 그의 빚을 그 청자는 알지 못했으나, 윌 자신은 그것을 떠올리며, 수표 한 장으로 그 모든 것을 청산할 수 있다면 좋겠다고 바라고 있었다."]], [["Naumann gave a shrug and said, “It is good they go away soon, my dear.", "나우만은 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"저 사람들이 곧 떠나는 건 다행이야, 이 친구야."], ["They are spoiling your fine temper.”", "자네의 그 좋은 성미를 망쳐 놓고 있으니 말이지.\""]], [["All Will’s hope and contrivance were now concentrated on seeing Dorothea when she was alone.", "이제 윌의 온갖 희망과 궁리는 도러시아가 혼자 있을 때 그녀를 만나는 데 집중되었다."], ["He only wanted her to take more emphatic notice of him; he only wanted to be something more special in her remembrance than he could yet believe himself likely to be.", "그는 다만 그녀가 자기를 좀 더 또렷이 마음에 새겨 주기를 바랐다. 그는 다만 아직은 자기가 그러리라 믿기 어려운 무엇, 그녀의 기억 속에서 좀 더 특별한 무엇이 되기를 바랐다."], ["He was rather impatient under that open ardent good-will, which he saw was her usual state of feeling.", "그녀의 그 솔직하고 열렬한 호의가—그것이 그녀의 평소 감정 상태임을 그는 알았다—그는 다소 견디기 어려웠다."], ["The remote worship of a woman throned out of their reach plays a great part in men’s lives, but in most cases the worshipper longs for some queenly recognition, some approving sign by which his soul’s sovereign may cheer him without descending from her high place.", "손이 닿지 않는 옥좌에 앉은 여인을 멀리서 흠모하는 일은 남자들의 삶에서 큰 몫을 차지하지만, 대개의 경우 흠모하는 자는 어떤 여왕다운 인정을, 자기 영혼의 군주가 그 높은 자리에서 내려오지 않은 채 자기를 격려해 줄 어떤 승인의 표시를 갈망한다. 윌이 바란 것이 바로 그것이었다."], ["That was precisely what Will wanted.", "그러나 그의 상상적 요구에는 모순이 가득했다."], ["But there were plenty of contradictions in his imaginative demands.", "도러시아의 두 눈이 아내다운 근심과 간청을 담아 캐소본 씨에게로 향하는 것을 보는 일은 아름다웠다."], ["It was beautiful to see how Dorothea’s eyes turned with wifely anxiety and beseeching to Mr.", "그 의무에 찬 골몰함이 없었더라면 그녀는 후광의 일부를 잃었으리라."], ["Casaubon: she would have lost some of her halo if she had been without that duteous preoccupation; and yet at the next moment the husband’s sandy absorption of such nectar was too intolerable; and Will’s longing to say damaging things about him was perhaps not the less tormenting because he felt the strongest reasons for restraining it.", "그러면서도 바로 다음 순간, 그런 감로를 모래처럼 빨아들이는 남편의 모습은 도무지 참을 수가 없었다. 그래서 윌이 그를 헐뜯는 말을 하고 싶어 안달하는 마음은, 그것을 억눌러야 할 더없이 강력한 이유들을 느끼고 있었기에 어쩌면 그만큼 더 괴로운 것이었는지도 모른다."]], [["Will had not been invited to dine the next day.", "윌은 이튿날 만찬에 초대받지 못했다."], ["Hence he persuaded himself that he was bound to call, and that the only eligible time was the middle of the day, when Mr. Casaubon would not be at home.", "그래서 그는 자기가 마땅히 방문해야 한다고, 그리고 알맞은 시간은 캐소본 씨가 집에 없을 한낮뿐이라고 스스로를 설득했다."]], [["Dorothea, who had not been made aware that her former reception of Will had displeased her husband, had no hesitation about seeing him, especially as he might be come to pay a farewell visit.", "전에 윌을 맞이한 일이 남편의 심기를 거슬렀다는 것을 알지 못한 도러시아는, 그를 만나는 데 아무 망설임이 없었으니, 특히나 그가 작별 인사를 하러 온 것일 수도 있었기 때문이다."], ["When he entered she was looking at some cameos which she had been buying for Celia.", "그가 들어섰을 때 그녀는 실리아에게 주려고 사 둔 카메오 몇 점을 들여다보고 있었다."], ["She greeted Will as if his visit were quite a matter of course, and said at once, having a cameo bracelet in her hand—", "그녀는 그의 방문이 지극히 당연한 일이라는 듯 윌을 맞이했고, 손에 카메오 팔찌를 든 채 곧바로 말했다."]], [["“I am so glad you are come.", "\"와 주셔서 정말 기뻐요."], ["Perhaps you understand all about cameos, and can tell me if these are really good.", "어쩌면 카메오에 대해 잘 아실 테니, 이것들이 정말 좋은 건지 말씀해 주실 수 있겠네요."], ["I wished to have you with us in choosing them, but Mr.", "고를 때 당신이 우리와 함께 있어 주기를 바랐는데, 캐소본 씨가 반대하셨어요."], ["Casaubon objected: he thought there was not time.", "시간이 없다고 생각하셨거든요."], ["He will finish his work to-morrow, and we shall go away in three days.", "그이는 내일 일을 끝낼 테고, 우리는 사흘 뒤에 떠나요."], ["I have been uneasy about these cameos.", "이 카메오들 때문에 마음이 편치 않았어요."], ["Pray sit down and look at them.”", "부디 앉아서 좀 봐 주세요.\""]], [["“I am not particularly knowing, but there can be no great mistake about these little Homeric bits: they are exquisitely neat.", "\"제가 특별히 잘 아는 건 아니지만, 이 자그마한 호메로스풍 조각들에 대해서는 큰 실수가 있을 수 없어요. 더없이 정교하고 깔끔하니까요."], ["And the color is fine: it will just suit you.”", "색도 좋고요. 부인께 꼭 어울릴 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh, they are for my sister, who has quite a different complexion.", "\"오, 이것들은 제 동생 거예요. 그 애는 안색이 아주 다르거든요."], ["You saw her with me at Lowick: she is light-haired and very pretty—at least I think so.", "로윅에서 저와 함께 있는 걸 보셨지요. 머리가 밝은 빛이고 무척 예뻐요—적어도 제 생각엔 그래요."], ["We were never so long away from each other in our lives before.", "우리는 평생 이렇게 오래 떨어져 지낸 적이 없었어요."], ["She is a great pet and never was naughty in her life.", "그 애는 정말 귀여운 아이고 한 번도 못된 짓을 한 적이 없답니다."], ["I found out before I came away that she wanted me to buy her some cameos, and I should be sorry for them not to be good—after their kind.”", "떠나오기 전에 그 애가 카메오를 좀 사다 주길 바란다는 걸 알게 됐는데, 이게—그 나름으로—좋은 것이 아니면 안타까울 것 같아요.\""], ["Dorothea added the last words with a smile.", "도러시아가 마지막 말을 미소와 함께 덧붙였다."]], [["“You seem not to care about cameos,”", "\"카메오에는 별 관심이 없으신 모양이군요.\""], ["said Will, seating himself at some distance from her, and observing her while she closed the cases.", "윌이 그녀에게서 얼마간 떨어진 자리에 앉아, 그녀가 갑들을 닫는 동안 그녀를 지켜보며 말했다."]], [["“No, frankly, I don’t think them a great object in life,”", "\"네, 솔직히 그게 인생에서 대단한 목표라고는 생각하지 않아요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“I fear you are a heretic about art generally.", "\"미술 전반에 대해 이단자이신 게 아닌가 싶군요."], ["How is that?", "어째서지요?"], ["I should have expected you to be very sensitive to the beautiful everywhere.”", "저는 부인이 어디서든 아름다운 것에 무척 민감하시리라 짐작했는데요.\""]], [["“I suppose I am dull about many things,”", "\"많은 것에 무딘가 봐요.\""], ["said Dorothea, simply.", "도러시아가 솔직하게 말했다."], ["“I should like to make life beautiful—I mean everybody’s life.", "\"저는 삶을 아름답게 만들고 싶어요—모두의 삶을 말이에요."], ["And then all this immense expense of art, that seems somehow to lie outside life and make it no better for the world, pains one.", "그런데 미술에 들이는 이 엄청난 비용은, 어쩐지 삶의 바깥에 놓여 세상을 조금도 더 낫게 해 주지 못하는 것 같아서 마음이 아파요."], ["It spoils my enjoyment of anything when I am made to think that most people are shut out from it.”", "대다수의 사람들이 그것에서 배제되어 있다는 생각이 들면, 무엇을 즐기든 그 즐거움이 망가져 버려요.\""]], [["“I call that the fanaticism of sympathy,”", "\"저는 그걸 동정의 광신이라고 부릅니다.\""], ["said Will, impetuously.", "윌이 격하게 말했다."], ["“You might say the same of landscape, of poetry, of all refinement.", "\"그런 식이면 풍경에 대해서도, 시에 대해서도, 온갖 세련된 것 전부에 대해서도 똑같이 말할 수 있겠지요."], ["If you carried it out you ought to be miserable in your own goodness, and turn evil that you might have no advantage over others.", "그걸 끝까지 밀고 나간다면, 부인은 자기 자신의 선함 속에서 비참해야 하고, 남들보다 나은 점을 갖지 않으려고 스스로 악해져야 할 겁니다."], ["The best piety is to enjoy—when you can.", "최선의 경건함은 즐기는 것입니다—즐길 수 있을 때 말이지요."], ["You are doing the most then to save the earth’s character as an agreeable planet.", "그래야 이 지구를 살 만한 행성으로 지키는 데 가장 크게 이바지하는 것이고요."], ["And enjoyment radiates.", "그리고 즐거움은 사방으로 퍼져 나갑니다."], ["It is of no use to try and take care of all the world; that is being taken care of when you feel delight—in art or in anything else.", "온 세상을 다 돌보려 애써 봐야 소용없어요. 당신이—미술에서든 다른 무엇에서든—기쁨을 느낄 때, 그것이 곧 세상을 돌보는 일이니까요."], ["Would you turn all the youth of the world into a tragic chorus, wailing and moralizing over misery?", "부인은 세상의 모든 젊은이를 비참함을 두고 통곡하며 설교하는 비극의 합창단으로 만들고 싶으신가요?"], ["I suspect that you have some false belief in the virtues of misery, and want to make your life a martyrdom.”", "제 생각에 부인은 비참함의 미덕에 대해 무언가 그릇된 믿음을 품고 있고, 자기 삶을 순교로 만들고 싶어 하는 것 같습니다.\""], ["Will had gone further than he intended, and checked himself.", "윌은 작정했던 것보다 더 멀리 나가 버려 말을 멈추었다."], ["But Dorothea’s thought was not taking just the same direction as his own, and she answered without any special emotion—", "그러나 도러시아의 생각은 그의 것과 똑같은 방향으로 흐르고 있지는 않았으므로, 그녀는 별다른 감정 없이 대답했다."]], [["“Indeed you mistake me. I am not a sad, melancholy creature.", "\"정말이지 당신은 저를 오해하고 있어요."], ["I am never unhappy long together.", "저는 슬프고 우울한 사람이 아니에요."], ["I am angry and naughty—not like Celia: I have a great outburst, and then all seems glorious again.", "오래도록 불행한 적은 한 번도 없어요. 저는 화도 잘 내고 못되게도 굴어요—실리아와는 다르죠."], ["I cannot help believing in glorious things in a blind sort of way.", "한바탕 크게 터뜨리고 나면, 다시 모든 것이 찬란해 보여요."], ["I should be quite willing to enjoy the art here, but there is so much that I don’t know the reason of—so much that seems to me a consecration of ugliness rather than beauty.", "저는 어쩐지 맹목적으로나마 찬란한 것들을 믿지 않을 수가 없어요. 이곳의 미술도 기꺼이 즐기고 싶지만, 까닭을 모를 것이 너무 많아요—아름다움이라기보다 추함을 봉헌한 것처럼 보이는 게 너무 많고요."], ["The painting and sculpture may be wonderful, but the feeling is often low and brutal, and sometimes even ridiculous.", "회화와 조각은 경이로울지 몰라도, 그 정서는 흔히 저열하고 잔혹하며, 때로는 우스꽝스럽기까지 해요."], ["Here and there I see what takes me at once as noble—something that I might compare with the Alban Mountains or the sunset from the Pincian Hill; but that makes it the greater pity that there is so little of the best kind among all that mass of things over which men have toiled so.”", "여기저기서 단번에 저를 사로잡는 고귀한 것을—알반 산맥이나 핀치오 언덕에서 보는 석양에 견줄 만한 무언가를—보기도 해요. 하지만 그렇기에, 사람들이 그토록 애써 만들어 낸 그 숱한 것들 가운데 최상의 것이 그토록 적다는 게 더욱 안타까운 거예요.\""]], [["“Of course there is always a great deal of poor work: the rarer things want that soil to grow in.”", "\"당연히 형편없는 작품은 늘 많기 마련이지요. 더 드문 것들은 그런 토양이 있어야 자라니까요.\""]], [["“Oh dear,”", "\"오, 저런.\""], ["said Dorothea, taking up that thought into the chief current of her anxiety; “I see it must be very difficult to do anything good.", "도러시아가 그 생각을 자기 근심의 주된 흐름 속으로 끌어들이며 말했다. \"무언가 좋은 것을 해낸다는 게 무척 어려운 일이라는 걸 알겠어요."], ["I have often felt since I have been in Rome that most of our lives would look much uglier and more bungling than the pictures, if they could be put on the wall.”", "로마에 와 있는 동안 저는, 우리 삶의 대부분이 벽에 걸어 놓을 수 있다면 그림보다 훨씬 더 추하고 서투르게 보이리라는 생각을 자주 했어요.\""]], [["Dorothea parted her lips again as if she were going to say more, but changed her mind and paused.", "도러시아는 무언가 더 말하려는 듯 다시 입술을 떼었으나, 마음을 바꾸어 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“You are too young—it is an anachronism for you to have such thoughts,”", "\"부인은 너무 젊으세요—그런 생각을 품다니 시대착오예요.\""], ["said Will, energetically, with a quick shake of the head habitual to him.", "윌이 그 특유의 빠르게 머리를 가로젓는 동작과 함께 힘주어 말했다."], ["“You talk as if you had never known any youth.", "\"마치 젊음이라곤 한 번도 알아 본 적 없는 사람처럼 말씀하시는군요."], ["It is monstrous—as if you had had a vision of Hades in your childhood, like the boy in the legend.", "괴이한 일이에요—마치 전설 속 그 소년처럼 어린 시절에 하데스의 환영을 본 사람처럼 말이에요."], ["You have been brought up in some of those horrible notions that choose the sweetest women to devour—like Minotaurs.", "부인은 미노타우로스처럼 가장 사랑스러운 여인들을 골라 집어삼키는 그런 끔찍한 관념들 속에서 자라셨군요."], ["And now you will go and be shut up in that stone prison at Lowick: you will be buried alive.", "그런데 이제 로윅의 그 돌 감옥에 갇히러 가시겠다니. 산 채로 묻히는 거예요."], ["It makes me savage to think of it!", "그 생각만 하면 사나워져요!"], ["I would rather never have seen you than think of you with such a prospect.”", "그런 앞날을 그리느니 차라리 부인을 만나지 않았던 편이 나았을 겁니다.\""]], [["Will again feared that he had gone too far; but the meaning we attach to words depends on our feeling, and his tone of angry regret had so much kindness in it for Dorothea’s heart, which had always been giving out ardor and had never been fed with much from the living beings around her, that she felt a new sense of gratitude and answered with a gentle smile—", "윌은 또다시 너무 멀리 나갔다고 두려워했다. 그러나 말에 우리가 부여하는 의미는 우리의 감정에 달려 있는 법이라, 그 화난 듯 안타까워하는 어조에는 도러시아의 마음을 향한 다정함이 너무도 많이 담겨 있었으니—언제나 열정을 내어 주기만 했을 뿐 주위의 살아 있는 존재들로부터 그리 많이 받아 본 적 없는 그 마음에—그녀는 새삼스러운 고마움을 느끼며 부드러운 미소로 대답했다."]], [["“It is very good of you to be anxious about me.", "\"저를 그토록 걱정해 주시다니 참 고마워요."], ["It is because you did not like Lowick yourself: you had set your heart on another kind of life.", "당신 자신이 로윅을 좋아하지 않았기 때문이겠지요. 당신은 다른 종류의 삶에 마음을 두었으니까요."], ["But Lowick is my chosen home.”", "하지만 로윅은 제가 택한 보금자리예요.\""]], [["The last sentence was spoken with an almost solemn cadence, and Will did not know what to say, since it would not be useful for him to embrace her slippers, and tell her that he would die for her: it was clear that she required nothing of the sort; and they were both silent for a moment or two, when Dorothea began again with an air of saying at last what had been in her mind beforehand.", "마지막 문장은 거의 엄숙한 가락으로 말해졌고, 윌은 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그녀의 신발에 입 맞추며 그녀를 위해 죽겠노라 말해 봐야 그에게 아무 소용이 없을 테니까. 그녀가 그런 것을 조금도 요구하지 않는다는 것은 분명했다. 두 사람은 한두 순간 잠자코 있었는데, 이윽고 도러시아가 미리 마음에 두고 있던 것을 마침내 꺼내려는 기색으로 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“I wanted to ask you again about something you said the other day.", "\"요전 날 당신이 하신 말씀에 대해 한 번 더 여쭤보고 싶었어요. 어쩌면 그건 절반쯤은 당신의 발랄한 말버릇이었겠지요."], ["Perhaps it was half of it your lively way of speaking: I notice that you like to put things strongly; I myself often exaggerate when I speak hastily.”", "당신이 무엇이든 강하게 표현하기를 좋아하신다는 걸 저도 알아요. 저 역시 급하게 말할 때면 곧잘 과장하니까요.\""]], [["“What was it?”", "\"무엇이었지요?\""], ["said Will, observing that she spoke with a timidity quite new in her.", "윌이, 그녀에게서 전에 없던 머뭇거림을 알아채며 말했다."], ["“I have a hyperbolical tongue: it catches fire as it goes.", "\"제 혀가 과장이 심해서요. 굴러가면서 불이 붙거든요."], ["I dare say I shall have to retract.”", "어쩌면 철회해야 할지도 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["“I mean what you said about the necessity of knowing German—I mean, for the subjects that Mr. Casaubon is engaged in.", "\"독일어를 알아야 할 필요가 있다고 하신 말씀 말이에요—제 말은, 캐소본 씨가 몰두하고 계신 주제들에 대해서요."], ["I have been thinking about it; and it seems to me that with Mr. Casaubon’s learning he must have before him the same materials as German scholars—has he not?”", "그걸 곰곰이 생각해 봤는데, 캐소본 씨만한 학식이라면 독일 학자들과 같은 자료를 앞에 두고 계시지 않을까 싶어요—그렇지 않나요?\""], ["Dorothea’s timidity was due to an indistinct consciousness that she was in the strange situation of consulting a third person about the adequacy of Mr. Casaubon’s learning.", "도러시아의 머뭇거림은, 자기가 제삼자에게 캐소본 씨의 학식이 충분한지를 의논하는 묘한 처지에 놓여 있다는 어렴풋한 자각에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["“Not exactly the same materials,”", "\"꼭 같은 자료는 아니지요.\""], ["said Will, thinking that he would be duly reserved.", "윌이 마땅히 신중해야겠다고 생각하며 말했다."], ["“He is not an Orientalist, you know.", "\"그분은 동양학자가 아니시잖아요."], ["He does not profess to have more than second-hand knowledge there.”", "그 분야에 대해서는 간접적인 지식 이상을 가졌다고 자처하지 않으시고요.\""]], [["“But there are very valuable books about antiquities which were written a long while ago by scholars who knew nothing about these modern things; and they are still used.", "\"하지만 고대에 관한 매우 귀중한 책들 중에는 이런 근래의 것들에 대해 아무것도 몰랐던 학자들이 아주 오래전에 쓴 것들도 있고, 그것들은 여전히 쓰이고 있잖아요."], ["Why should Mr. Casaubon’s not be valuable, like theirs?”", "어째서 캐소본 씨의 것은 그들의 것처럼 귀중하지 않다는 거죠?\""], ["said Dorothea, with more remonstrant energy.", "도러시아가 한층 항변하는 기세로 말했다."], ["She was impelled to have the argument aloud, which she had been having in her own mind.", "그녀는 마음속에서 벌여 온 그 논쟁을 소리 내어 펼치지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“That depends on the line of study taken,”", "\"그건 어떤 연구 노선을 택하느냐에 달려 있어요.\""], ["said Will, also getting a tone of rejoinder. “The subject Mr.", "윌도 받아치는 어조를 띠며 말했다."], ["Casaubon has chosen is as changing as chemistry: new discoveries are constantly making new points of view.", "\"캐소본 씨가 택하신 주제는 화학만큼이나 변화무쌍합니다. 새로운 발견이 끊임없이 새로운 관점을 만들어 내니까요."], ["Who wants a system on the basis of the four elements, or a book to refute Paracelsus?", "누가 이제 와서 사원소설에 기초한 체계를 원하겠어요, 아니면 파라켈수스를 반박하는 책을 원하겠습니까?"], ["Do you not see that it is no use now to be crawling a little way after men of the last century—men like Bryant—and correcting their mistakes?—living in a lumber-room and furbishing up broken-legged theories about Chus and Mizraim?”", "모르시겠어요? 브라이언트 같은 지난 세기 사람들의 뒤를 조금 기어가며 그들의 오류를 바로잡아 봐야 이제는 아무 소용이 없다는 걸요—잡동사니 방에 틀어박혀 쿠스니 미스라임이니 하는 다리 부러진 이론들을 손질하는 게 말이에요.\""]], [["“How can you bear to speak so lightly?”", "\"어떻게 그렇게 가볍게 말씀하실 수 있어요?\""], ["said Dorothea, with a look between sorrow and anger.", "도러시아가 슬픔과 분노 사이의 표정으로 말했다."], ["“If it were as you say, what could be sadder than so much ardent labor all in vain?", "\"만약 당신 말대로라면, 그토록 열렬한 노고가 죄다 헛수고였다는 것보다 더 슬픈 일이 어디 있겠어요?"], ["I wonder it does not affect you more painfully, if you really think that a man like Mr. Casaubon, of so much goodness, power, and learning, should in any way fail in what has been the labor of his best years.”", "캐소본 씨처럼 선함과 능력과 학식을 그토록 많이 갖춘 분이, 평생 가장 좋은 시절을 바친 일에서 어떤 식으로든 실패하리라고 정말로 생각하신다면, 그게 당신에게 더 가슴 아프게 다가오지 않는다는 게 의아하군요.\""], ["She was beginning to be shocked that she had got to such a point of supposition, and indignant with Will for having led her to it.", "그녀는 자기가 그런 가정의 지점에까지 이르렀다는 데 충격을 받기 시작했고, 자기를 거기까지 이끈 윌에게 분개했다."]], [["“You questioned me about the matter of fact, not of feeling,”", "\"부인은 제게 사실에 관해 물으셨지, 감정에 관해 물으신 게 아니지요.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“But if you wish to punish me for the fact, I submit.", "\"하지만 그 사실 때문에 저를 벌하고 싶으시다면, 받아들이겠습니다."], ["I am not in a position to express my feeling toward Mr.", "저는 캐소본 씨에 대한 제 감정을 표현할 처지가 못 됩니다."], ["Casaubon: it would be at best a pensioner’s eulogy.”", "기껏해야 식객의 찬사가 될 테니까요.\""]], [["“Pray excuse me,”", "\"부디 용서하세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, coloring deeply.", "도러시아가 얼굴을 짙게 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I am aware, as you say, that I am in fault in having introduced the subject.", "\"말씀대로, 이 주제를 꺼낸 제게 잘못이 있다는 걸 알아요."], ["Indeed, I am wrong altogether.", "정말이지 제가 통째로 잘못했어요."], ["Failure after long perseverance is much grander than never to have a striving good enough to be called a failure.”", "오랜 끈기 끝의 실패가, 실패라 부를 만큼도 되지 못하는 미약한 분투보다 훨씬 더 위대한 것인데요.\""]], [["“I quite agree with you,”", "\"전적으로 동감입니다.\""], ["said Will, determined to change the situation—“so much so that I have made up my mind not to run that risk of never attaining a failure.", "윌이 상황을 바꾸기로 마음먹고 말했다. \"어찌나 동감인지, 저는 실패에조차 결코 이르지 못할 그 위험을 무릅쓰지 않기로 작정했답니다."], ["Mr. Casaubon’s generosity has perhaps been dangerous to me, and I mean to renounce the liberty it has given me.", "캐소본 씨의 관대함이 어쩌면 제게 위험한 것이었는지도 모르니, 그것이 제게 준 자유를 저는 단념하려 합니다."], ["I mean to go back to England shortly and work my own way—depend on nobody else than myself.”", "머지않아 영국으로 돌아가 제 힘으로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 생각이에요—저 자신 말고는 누구에게도 기대지 않고요.\""]], [["“That is fine—I respect that feeling,”", "\"훌륭해요—그런 마음 존경해요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with returning kindness.", "도러시아가 다시금 다정함을 되찾으며 말했다."], ["“But Mr. Casaubon, I am sure, has never thought of anything in the matter except what was most for your welfare.”", "\"하지만 캐소본 씨는, 분명히, 이 일에서 당신의 안녕에 가장 도움이 되는 것 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않으셨을 거예요.\""]], [["“She has obstinacy and pride enough to serve instead of love, now she has married him,”", "'이제 그자와 결혼했으니, 저 여자에겐 사랑 대신 노릇을 해 줄 만한 고집과 자존심이 넘치는군.' 윌은 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["said Will to himself. Aloud he said, rising—", "소리 내어서는, 자리에서 일어나며 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“I shall not see you again.”", "\"다시는 부인을 뵙지 못하겠군요.\""]], [["“Oh, stay till Mr. Casaubon comes,”", "\"오, 캐소본 씨가 오실 때까지 계세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, earnestly.", "도러시아가 간곡히 말했다."], ["“I am so glad we met in Rome.", "\"우리가 로마에서 만나게 되어 정말 기뻐요."], ["I wanted to know you.”", "저는 당신을 알고 싶었어요.\""]], [["“And I have made you angry,”", "\"그런데 제가 부인을 화나게 만들었군요.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“I have made you think ill of me.”", "\"제가 부인이 저를 나쁘게 생각하시도록 만들었어요.\""]], [["“Oh no.", "\"오, 아니에요."], ["My sister tells me I am always angry with people who do not say just what I like.", "동생 말로는 제가 제 마음에 꼭 드는 말을 하지 않는 사람들에게 늘 화를 낸대요."], ["But I hope I am not given to think ill of them.", "하지만 그 사람들을 나쁘게 생각하는 버릇은 없기를 바라요."], ["In the end I am usually obliged to think ill of myself for being so impatient.”", "결국에는 늘, 제가 그토록 성급했던 걸 두고 제 자신을 나쁘게 생각하게 되거든요.\""]], [["“Still, you don’t like me; I have made myself an unpleasant thought to you.”", "\"그래도, 부인은 저를 좋아하지 않으시는군요. 제가 부인께 언짢은 생각거리가 되어 버렸어요.\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with the most open kindness.", "도러시아가 더없이 솔직한 다정함으로 말했다."], ["“I like you very much.”", "\"저는 당신이 무척 좋아요.\""]], [["Will was not quite contented, thinking that he would apparently have been of more importance if he had been disliked.", "윌은 썩 만족스럽지 않았으니, 차라리 미움을 받았더라면 자기가 더 중요한 사람이었을 텐데 하는 생각이 들었기 때문이다."], ["He said nothing, but looked dull, not to say sulky.", "그는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 시무룩하다고까지는 않더라도 멍한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“And I am quite interested to see what you will do,”", "\"그리고 저는 당신이 무슨 일을 하게 될지 무척 궁금해요.\""], ["Dorothea went on cheerfully.", "도러시아가 명랑하게 말을 이었다."], ["“I believe devoutly in a natural difference of vocation.", "\"저는 타고난 천직의 차이를 독실하게 믿어요."], ["If it were not for that belief, I suppose I should be very narrow—there are so many things, besides painting, that I am quite ignorant of.", "그런 믿음이 없다면 저는 아마 무척 편협한 사람이 되었을 거예요—그림 말고도 제가 도무지 모르는 것이 너무 많거든요."], ["You would hardly believe how little I have taken in of music and literature, which you know so much of.", "당신이 그토록 잘 아는 음악과 문학을 제가 얼마나 조금밖에 받아들이지 못했는지 들으면 좀처럼 믿기지 않으실 거예요."], ["I wonder what your vocation will turn out to be: perhaps you will be a poet?”", "당신의 천직이 무엇으로 드러날지 궁금해요. 어쩌면 시인이 되실까요?\""]], [["“That depends.", "\"그건 경우에 따라 다르지요."], ["To be a poet is to have a soul so quick to discern that no shade of quality escapes it, and so quick to feel, that discernment is but a hand playing with finely ordered variety on the chords of emotion—a soul in which knowledge passes instantaneously into feeling, and feeling flashes back as a new organ of knowledge.", "시인이 된다는 것은, 어떤 질의 미묘한 차이도 놓치지 않을 만큼 분별이 빠르고, 또 느낌이 빨라서, 그 분별이 곧 정교하게 배열된 다채로움을 감정의 현 위에서 켜는 손이 되는 그런 영혼을 갖는 것입니다—지식이 순식간에 느낌으로 옮겨 가고, 느낌이 다시 새로운 지식의 기관이 되어 번뜩이는 그런 영혼을요."], ["One may have that condition by fits only.”", "그런 상태는 발작적으로만 찾아올 수도 있고요.\""]], [["“But you leave out the poems,”", "\"하지만 당신은 시는 빼놓으시는군요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“I think they are wanted to complete the poet.", "\"시인을 완성하려면 시가 필요할 것 같아요."], ["I understand what you mean about knowledge passing into feeling, for that seems to be just what I experience.", "지식이 느낌으로 옮겨 간다는 그 말씀은 이해해요, 바로 그게 제가 경험하는 것 같으니까요."], ["But I am sure I could never produce a poem.”", "하지만 저는 분명 시 한 편도 결코 지어내지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“You are a poem—and that is to be the best part of a poet—what makes up the poet’s consciousness in his best moods,”", "\"부인 자신이 한 편의 시예요—그리고 그것이야말로 시인의 가장 좋은 부분, 최상의 순간에 시인의 의식을 이루는 것이지요.\""], ["said Will, showing such originality as we all share with the morning and the spring-time and other endless renewals.", "윌은, 우리 모두가 아침이며 봄철이며 그 밖의 끝없는 갱신들과 더불어 나누어 갖는 그런 독창성을 보이며 말했다."]], [["“I am very glad to hear it,”", "\"그 말을 들으니 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["said Dorothea, laughing out her words in a bird-like modulation, and looking at Will with playful gratitude in her eyes.", "도러시아가 새처럼 가락 있는 어조로 웃음 섞인 말을 내뱉으며, 장난스러운 고마움을 눈에 담아 윌을 바라보았다."], ["“What very kind things you say to me!”", "\"저에게 어쩜 그렇게 다정한 말씀을 하세요!\""]], [["“I wish I could ever do anything that would be what you call kind—that I could ever be of the slightest service to you.", "\"제가 부인이 다정하다 부르실 만한 일을 한 번이라도 할 수 있다면—부인께 조금이라도 도움이 될 수 있다면 좋겠습니다."], ["I fear I shall never have the opportunity.”", "그런 기회는 결코 오지 않을 것 같아 두렵지만요.\""], ["Will spoke with fervor.", "윌이 열정을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Oh yes,”", "\"오, 그래요.\""], ["said Dorothea, cordially.", "도러시아가 진심으로 말했다."], ["“It will come; and I shall remember how well you wish me.", "\"기회는 올 거예요. 그러면 저는 당신이 저를 얼마나 잘 되기를 바랐는지 떠올릴 거예요."], ["I quite hoped that we should be friends when I first saw you—because of your relationship to Mr.", "당신을 처음 봤을 때 저는 우리가 친구가 되기를 무척 바랐어요—당신이 캐소본 씨의 친척이니까요.\""], ["Casaubon.” There was a certain liquid brightness in her eyes, and Will was conscious that his own were obeying a law of nature and filling too.", "그녀의 두 눈에는 어떤 촉촉한 빛이 어렸고, 윌은 자기 눈도 자연의 법칙을 따라 그렁그렁 차오르는 것을 의식했다."], ["The allusion to Mr. Casaubon would have spoiled all if anything at that moment could have spoiled the subduing power, the sweet dignity, of her noble unsuspicious inexperience.", "만약 그 순간 무언가가 그녀의 그 고결하고 의심을 모르는 순진함이 지닌 압도하는 힘과 감미로운 기품을 망쳐 놓을 수 있었다면, 캐소본 씨에 대한 그 언급이 모든 것을 망쳐 놓았으리라."]], [["“And there is one thing even now that you can do,”", "\"그리고 지금 당장이라도 당신이 해 주실 수 있는 일이 하나 있어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, rising and walking a little way under the strength of a recurring impulse.", "도러시아가, 되살아나는 충동에 떠밀려 일어서서 몇 걸음 거닐며 말했다."], ["“Promise me that you will not again, to any one, speak of that subject—I mean about Mr. Casaubon’s writings—I mean in that kind of way.", "\"다시는, 그 누구에게도, 그 주제에 대해—제 말은 캐소본 씨의 저작에 대해—그런 식으로 말하지 않겠다고 약속해 주세요."], ["It was I who led to it.", "그 얘기를 꺼낸 건 저였어요."], ["It was my fault.", "제 잘못이에요."], ["But promise me.”", "그래도 약속해 주세요.\""]], [["She had returned from her brief pacing and stood opposite Will, looking gravely at him.", "그녀는 짧은 거닐기를 마치고 돌아와 윌의 맞은편에 서서, 그를 진지하게 바라보았다."]], [["“Certainly, I will promise you,”", "\"물론, 약속하지요.\""], ["said Will, reddening however.", "윌이 그러면서도 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["If he never said a cutting word about Mr. Casaubon again and left off receiving favors from him, it would clearly be permissible to hate him the more.", "다시는 캐소본 씨에 대해 모진 말을 하지 않고 그에게서 호의를 받는 일도 그만둔다면, 그를 더욱더 미워하는 것쯤은 분명 허용될 만한 일이었다."], ["The poet must know how to hate, says Goethe; and Will was at least ready with that accomplishment.", "시인은 미워할 줄 알아야 한다고 괴테는 말했으니, 윌은 적어도 그 재주만큼은 갖추고 있었다."], ["He said that he must go now without waiting for Mr. Casaubon, whom he would come to take leave of at the last moment.", "그는 캐소본 씨를 기다리지 않고 이제 가 봐야겠다고, 그분께는 떠나기 직전 마지막 순간에 작별 인사를 하러 오겠다고 말했다."], ["Dorothea gave him her hand, and they exchanged a simple “Good-by.”", "도러시아가 그에게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람은 간소한 \"안녕히\"를 주고받았다."]], [["But going out of the porte cochere he met Mr. Casaubon, and that gentleman, expressing the best wishes for his cousin, politely waived the pleasure of any further leave-taking on the morrow, which would be sufficiently crowded with the preparations for departure.", "그러나 정문을 나서다가 그는 캐소본 씨와 마주쳤고, 그 신사는 자기 사촌에게 더없는 행운을 빈다고 하면서, 출발 준비로 충분히 분주할 이튿날에 다시 작별 인사를 나누는 수고는 정중히 사양했다."]], [["“I have something to tell you about our cousin Mr.", "\"우리 사촌 래디슬로 씨에 대해 당신에게 들려줄 이야기가 있어요."], ["Ladislaw, which I think will heighten your opinion of him,”", "그에 대한 당신의 평가가 한층 높아질 것 같은데요.\""], ["said Dorothea to her husband in the course of the evening.", "그날 저녁 도러시아가 남편에게 말했다."], ["She had mentioned immediately on his entering that Will had just gone away, and would come again, but Mr. Casaubon had said, “I met him outside, and we made our final adieux, I believe,”", "그녀는 그가 들어서자마자 윌이 막 다녀갔으며 다시 오리라고 일러두었으나, 캐소본 씨는 \"밖에서 그를 만났소, 그리고 우리는 마지막 작별을 나눈 것 같소\""], ["saying this with the air and tone by which we imply that any subject, whether private or public, does not interest us enough to wish for a further remark upon it.", "하고 말했었다. 사적인 것이든 공적인 것이든 어떤 주제도 더 언급하고 싶을 만큼 흥미롭지는 않다는 뜻을 담은 그런 태도와 어조로 말이다."], ["So Dorothea had waited.", "그래서 도러시아는 기다렸던 것이다."]], [["“What is that, my love?”", "\"무엇이오, 여보?\""], ["said Mr Casaubon (he always said “my love” when his manner was the coldest).", "캐소본 씨가 말했다(그는 태도가 가장 차가울 때면 늘 \"여보\"라고 했다)."]], [["“He has made up his mind to leave off wandering at once, and to give up his dependence on your generosity.", "\"그이는 이제 곧 떠돌이 생활을 그만두고, 당신의 관대함에 기대는 일도 그만두기로 마음먹었대요."], ["He means soon to go back to England, and work his own way.", "머지않아 영국으로 돌아가 제 힘으로 길을 헤쳐 나갈 생각이래요."], ["I thought you would consider that a good sign,”", "당신도 그걸 좋은 징조로 여기시리라 생각했어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with an appealing look into her husband’s neutral face.", "도러시아가 남편의 무덤덤한 얼굴을 호소하듯 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“Did he mention the precise order of occupation to which he would addict himself?”", "\"그가 어떤 일에 몸 바칠지 그 정확한 종류를 언급하던가?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["But he said that he felt the danger which lay for him in your generosity.", "하지만 당신의 관대함 속에 자기에게 도사린 위험을 느낀다고 했어요."], ["Of course he will write to you about it.", "물론 그 일에 대해 당신에게 편지를 쓰겠지요."], ["Do you not think better of him for his resolve?”", "그의 결심을 보고 그를 더 좋게 생각하지 않으세요?\""]], [["“I shall await his communication on the subject,”", "\"그 문제에 관한 그의 연락을 기다려 보겠소.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon.", "캐소본 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I told him I was sure that the thing you considered in all you did for him was his own welfare.", "\"당신이 그를 위해 하신 모든 일에서 염두에 두신 것은 그 자신의 안녕이었으리라 확신한다고 그에게 말했어요."], ["I remembered your goodness in what you said about him when I first saw him at Lowick,”", "로윅에서 그를 처음 봤을 때 당신이 그에 대해 하신 말씀의 그 너그러움을 떠올렸거든요.\""], ["said Dorothea, putting her hand on her husband’s.", "도러시아가 남편의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“I had a duty towards him,” said Mr.", "\"나는 그에게 의무가 있었소.\""], ["Casaubon, laying his other hand on Dorothea’s in conscientious acceptance of her caress, but with a glance which he could not hinder from being uneasy.", "캐소본 씨가 양심적으로 그녀의 애정 표시를 받아들이며 다른 한 손을 도러시아의 손 위에 얹었으나, 그 눈길만은 불안한 빛을 띠는 것을 어쩌지 못했다. \"그 젊은이가, 고백하건대, 그 밖의 점에서 내게 흥미를 끄는 대상은 아니오."], ["“The young man, I confess, is not otherwise an object of interest to me, nor need we, I think, discuss his future course, which it is not ours to determine beyond the limits which I have sufficiently indicated.”", "또 그의 앞날을 우리가 논할 필요도 없을 것 같소. 그것을 정하는 일은 내가 충분히 그어 놓은 한계를 넘어서서는 우리 몫이 아니니 말이오.\""], ["Dorothea did not mention Will again.", "도러시아는 다시는 윌을 입에 올리지 않았다."]], [["# BOOK III. WAITING FOR DEATH. CHAPTER XXIII.", "# BOOK III. WAITING FOR DEATH. CHAPTER XXIII."]], [["“Your horses of the Sun,” he said, “And first-rate whip Apollo!", "“그리고 일류 채찍잡이 아폴로여!"], ["Whate’er they be, I’ll eat my head, But I will beat them hollow.”", "저들이 무엇이든, 내 목을 걸겠노라,"]], [["Fred Vincy, we have seen, had a debt on his mind, and though no such immaterial burthen could depress that buoyant-hearted young gentleman for many hours together, there were circumstances connected with this debt which made the thought of it unusually importunate.", "내 저들을 완패시키고 말 테니.” 앞서 보았듯이 프레드 빈시는 빚을 마음에 품고 있었다."], ["The creditor was Mr. Bambridge, a horse-dealer of the neighborhood, whose company was much sought in Middlemarch by young men understood to be “addicted to pleasure.”", "비록 그런 비물질적인 짐 따위가 이 들뜬 마음의 젊은 신사를 여러 시간 내내 짓누를 수는 없었지만, 이 빚에는 그 생각을 유난히 집요하게 만드는 사정들이 얽혀 있었다."], ["During the vacations Fred had naturally required more amusements than he had ready money for, and Mr.", "채권자는 인근의 말 거래상인 뱀브리지였는데, 미들마치에서 “쾌락에 빠졌다”고 알려진 젊은이들이 그의 교제를 무척 탐냈다."], ["Bambridge had been accommodating enough not only to trust him for the hire of horses and the accidental expense of ruining a fine hunter, but also to make a small advance by which he might be able to meet some losses at billiards.", "방학 동안 프레드는 자연히 수중의 현금보다 더 많은 오락을 필요로 했고, 뱀브리지는 마음씨가 후하여 말을 빌려주거나 멋진 사냥말을 망가뜨려 생긴 우발적 비용을 외상으로 봐주었을 뿐 아니라, 당구에서 본 약간의 손실을 메울 수 있도록 소액을 미리 빌려주기까지 했다."], ["The total debt was a hundred and sixty pounds.", "빚의 총액은 백육십 파운드였다."], ["Bambridge was in no alarm about his money, being sure that young Vincy had backers; but he had required something to show for it, and Fred had at first given a bill with his own signature.", "뱀브리지는 자기 돈에 대해 전혀 걱정하지 않았으니, 빈시 청년에게 뒤를 봐줄 사람들이 있다고 확신했기 때문이다. 다만 그는 증서를 요구했고, 프레드는 처음에 자기 서명으로 어음을 끊어 주었다."], ["Three months later he had renewed this bill with the signature of Caleb Garth.", "석 달 뒤 그는 케일럽 가스의 서명을 받아 이 어음을 갱신했다."], ["On both occasions Fred had felt confident that he should meet the bill himself, having ample funds at disposal in his own hopefulness.", "두 번 다 프레드는 자기가 직접 어음을 갚으리라 확신했으니, 자기 자신의 낙관 속에 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 넉넉한 자금을 두고 있었던 것이다. 그의 확신이 외적인 사실에 근거를 두기를 요구하지는 않으리라."], ["You will hardly demand that his confidence should have a basis in external facts; such confidence, we know, is something less coarse and materialistic: it is a comfortable disposition leading us to expect that the wisdom of providence or the folly of our friends, the mysteries of luck or the still greater mystery of our high individual value in the universe, will bring about agreeable issues, such as are consistent with our good taste in costume, and our general preference for the best style of thing.", "우리가 알다시피 그런 확신은 그보다 덜 거칠고 덜 물질적인 무엇이다. 그것은 섭리의 지혜나 우리 친구들의 어리석음, 행운의 신비나 우주 안에서 우리 개인의 높은 가치라는 더욱 큰 신비가, 우리의 의상 취향이나 가장 좋은 것을 선호하는 우리의 일반적 성향에 어울리는 흐뭇한 결말을 가져다주리라 기대하게 만드는, 편안한 기질인 것이다."], ["Fred felt sure that he should have a present from his uncle, that he should have a run of luck, that by dint of “swapping” he should gradually metamorphose a horse worth forty pounds into a horse that would fetch a hundred at any moment—“judgment”", "프레드는 자기가 삼촌에게서 선물을 받으리라고, 운이 트이리라고, “바꿔치기”를 거듭하다 보면 사십 파운드짜리 말이 언제든 백 파운드를 부를 만한 말로 점차 변신하리라고 확신했다 — “안목”이란 늘 액수를 정하지 않은 현금과 맞먹는 것이었으니까."], ["being always equivalent to an unspecified sum in hard cash. And in any case, even supposing negations which only a morbid distrust could imagine, Fred had always (at that time) his father’s pocket as a last resource, so that his assets of hopefulness had a sort of gorgeous superfluity about them.", "그리고 어떤 경우에도, 심지어 병적인 불신만이 상상할 법한 부정적 상황을 가정한다 해도, 프레드에게는 (그 무렵) 늘 최후의 수단으로 아버지의 지갑이 있었으니, 그의 낙관이라는 자산에는 일종의 호화로운 과잉이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Of what might be the capacity of his father’s pocket, Fred had only a vague notion: was not trade elastic?", "아버지의 지갑이 어느 정도 능력을 지녔는지 프레드는 막연한 생각만 가졌을 뿐이었다."], ["And would not the deficiencies of one year be made up for by the surplus of another?", "장사란 탄력적인 것이 아니던가? 한 해의 부족분은 다른 해의 잉여로 메워지지 않겠는가?"], ["The Vincys lived in an easy profuse way, not with any new ostentation, but according to the family habits and traditions, so that the children had no standard of economy, and the elder ones retained some of their infantine notion that their father might pay for anything if he would. Mr.", "빈시 집안은 여유롭고 풍족하게 살았는데, 무슨 새삼스러운 과시에서가 아니라 집안의 습관과 전통에 따른 것이었으므로, 아이들에게는 절약의 기준이라는 것이 없었고, 손위 아이들은 마음만 먹으면 아버지가 무엇이든 치러 줄 수 있다는 어린 시절의 관념을 어느 정도 간직하고 있었다."], ["Vincy himself had expensive Middlemarch habits—spent money on coursing, on his cellar, and on dinner-giving, while mamma had those running accounts with tradespeople, which give a cheerful sense of getting everything one wants without any question of payment.", "빈시 씨 자신도 미들마치의 돈 드는 습관을 지니고 있었다 — 사냥개 경주에, 술 창고에, 만찬 베푸는 데 돈을 썼고, 어머니는 상인들과 외상 거래를 텄으니, 이는 지불 문제는 따지지 않고 원하는 것을 모두 손에 넣는다는 유쾌한 기분을 안겨 주었다. 그러나 비용 문제로 사람을 윽박지르는 것이 아버지들의 본성임을 프레드는 알고 있었다."], ["But it was in the nature of fathers, Fred knew, to bully one about expenses: there was always a little storm over his extravagance if he had to disclose a debt, and Fred disliked bad weather within doors.", "빚을 털어놓아야 할 때면 늘 그의 낭비를 두고 작은 폭풍이 일었고, 프레드는 집 안의 궂은 날씨를 싫어했다. 그는 너무도 효성스러워 아버지에게 불경하게 굴지 못했고, 그것이 일시적임을 확신하며 천둥을 견뎌 냈다."], ["He was too filial to be disrespectful to his father, and he bore the thunder with the certainty that it was transient; but in the mean time it was disagreeable to see his mother cry, and also to be obliged to look sulky instead of having fun; for Fred was so good-tempered that if he looked glum under scolding, it was chiefly for propriety’s sake.", "그러나 그동안 어머니가 우는 것을 보거나 재미있게 놀지 못한 채 부루퉁한 표정을 지어야 하는 것은 영 달갑지 않았다. 프레드는 천성이 워낙 온순해서 꾸지람을 들으며 시무룩한 표정을 짓는다 해도 그것은 주로 체면을 차리기 위해서였다."], ["The easier course plainly, was to renew the bill with a friend’s signature.", "분명 더 쉬운 길은 친구의 서명을 받아 어음을 갱신하는 것이었다."], ["Why not?", "왜 안 되겠는가?"], ["With the superfluous securities of hope at his command, there was no reason why he should not have increased other people’s liabilities to any extent, but for the fact that men whose names were good for anything were usually pessimists, indisposed to believe that the universal order of things would necessarily be agreeable to an agreeable young gentleman.", "마음대로 쓸 수 있는 희망의 과잉 담보를 손에 쥔 그로서는, 남들의 채무를 얼마든지 늘리지 못할 이유가 없었다. 다만 이름값이 좀 나가는 사람들이 대개 비관주의자라서, 만물의 보편적 질서가 어느 호감 가는 젊은 신사에게 반드시 호의적이리라고는 믿으려 들지 않는다는 사실만 빼면 말이다."]], [["With a favor to ask we review our list of friends, do justice to their more amiable qualities, forgive their little offenses, and concerning each in turn, try to arrive at the conclusion that he will be eager to oblige us, our own eagerness to be obliged being as communicable as other warmth.", "부탁할 일이 생기면 우리는 친구 목록을 점검하고, 그들의 더 사랑스러운 자질을 공정하게 평가하며, 그들의 사소한 잘못을 용서하고, 차례차례 한 사람씩 두고 그가 기꺼이 우리 청을 들어주리라는 결론에 이르려 애쓴다. 신세 지고 싶어 하는 우리 자신의 열의가 다른 온기와 마찬가지로 전염성을 지닌 까닭이다."], ["Still there is always a certain number who are dismissed as but moderately eager until the others have refused; and it happened that Fred checked off all his friends but one, on the ground that applying to them would be disagreeable; being implicitly convinced that he at least (whatever might be maintained about mankind generally) had a right to be free from anything disagreeable.", "그래도 늘 일정한 수의 사람들은 다른 이들이 거절할 때까지는 기껏해야 미적지근하다고 제쳐 두기 마련이다. 그리하여 프레드는 한 사람만 빼고 모든 친구를 지워 버렸는데, 그들에게 부탁하는 것은 달갑지 않으리라는 이유에서였다."], ["That he should ever fall into a thoroughly unpleasant position—wear trousers shrunk with washing, eat cold mutton, have to walk for want of a horse, or to “duck under”", "그는 적어도 자기만큼은 (인류 일반에 대해서는 무어라 주장하든 간에) 달갑지 않은 일에서 면제받을 권리가 있다고 은연중에 확신하고 있었던 것이다. 자기가 정녕 철저히 불쾌한 처지에 빠진다는 것 — 빨아서 줄어든 바지를 입고, 식은 양고기를 먹으며, 말이 없어 걸어 다니거나 어떤 식으로든 “고개를 숙이고 들어가야”"], ["in any sort of way—was an absurdity irreconcilable with those cheerful intuitions implanted in him by nature.", "한다는 것 — 은 천성이 그에게 심어 준 그 명랑한 직관과는 도무지 화해할 수 없는 부조리였다."], ["And Fred winced under the idea of being looked down upon as wanting funds for small debts.", "그리고 프레드는 사소한 빚을 갚을 자금조차 없는 자로 멸시당한다는 생각에 움찔했다."], ["Thus it came to pass that the friend whom he chose to apply to was at once the poorest and the kindest—namely, Caleb Garth.", "이리하여 그가 부탁하기로 고른 친구는 가장 가난하면서 동시에 가장 친절한 사람, 곧 케일럽 가스가 되었다."]], [["The Garths were very fond of Fred, as he was of them; for when he and Rosamond were little ones, and the Garths were better off, the slight connection between the two families through Mr.", "가스 집안은 프레드를 무척 좋아했고, 프레드도 그들을 좋아했다. 그와 로저먼드가 어렸을 적, 가스 집안 형편이 더 나았을 때, 페더스톤 씨의 두 번 결혼(첫 번째는 가스 씨의 누이와, 두 번째는 빈시 부인과)을 통해 맺어진 두 집안의 희미한 인연이 아이들 사이에서 — 부모들보다는 — 이어지는 교분을 낳았던 것이다."], ["Featherstone’s double marriage (the first to Mr. Garth’s sister, and the second to Mrs.", "아이들은 장난감 찻잔으로 함께 차를 마셨고, 온종일 함께 놀며 지냈다."], ["Vincy’s) had led to an acquaintance which was carried on between the children rather than the parents: the children drank tea together out of their toy teacups, and spent whole days together in play.", "메리는 자그마한 말괄량이였고, 여섯 살의 프레드는 그녀가 세상에서 가장 멋진 여자아이라고 여겨, 우산에서 잘라 낸 놋쇠 고리로 그녀를 아내로 삼았다."], ["Mary was a little hoyden, and Fred at six years old thought her the nicest girl in the world, making her his wife with a brass ring which he had cut from an umbrella.", "교육의 온갖 단계를 거치는 내내 그는 가스 집안에 대한 애정을 간직했고, 그 집을 제2의 고향처럼 드나드는 버릇을 지켰으니, 그들과 그의 집안 어른들 사이의 왕래는 오래전에 끊겼는데도 그러했다."], ["Through all the stages of his education he had kept his affection for the Garths, and his habit of going to their house as a second home, though any intercourse between them and the elders of his family had long ceased.", "케일럽 가스가 형편이 넉넉했던 시절에도 빈시 집안은 그와 그의 아내를 한 수 아래로 대했으니, 미들마치에는 신분의 미묘한 구별이 있었기 때문이다."], ["Even when Caleb Garth was prosperous, the Vincys were on condescending terms with him and his wife, for there were nice distinctions of rank in Middlemarch; and though old manufacturers could not any more than dukes be connected with none but equals, they were conscious of an inherent social superiority which was defined with great nicety in practice, though hardly expressible theoretically. Since then Mr.", "옛 제조업자들이 공작들과 마찬가지로 동등한 자들하고만 어울릴 수는 없었지만, 그들은 이론적으로는 거의 표현할 수 없되 실제로는 대단히 정교하게 구분되는, 타고난 사회적 우월감을 의식하고 있었다."], ["Garth had failed in the building business, which he had unfortunately added to his other avocations of surveyor, valuer, and agent, had conducted that business for a time entirely for the benefit of his assignees, and had been living narrowly, exerting himself to the utmost that he might after all pay twenty shillings in the pound.", "그 후 가스 씨는 건축업에서 실패했으니, 그는 불행히도 측량사, 감정사, 대리인이라는 다른 본업에 그것을 더해 두었던 것이다. 한동안은 오로지 채권 양수인들의 이익을 위해 그 사업을 꾸려야 했고, 결국 1파운드당 20실링을 다 갚으려고 안간힘을 쓰며 빠듯하게 살아왔다."], ["He had now achieved this, and from all who did not think it a bad precedent, his honorable exertions had won him due esteem; but in no part of the world is genteel visiting founded on esteem, in the absence of suitable furniture and complete dinner-service. Mrs.", "이제 그는 그것을 이루어 냈고, 그것을 나쁜 선례로 여기지 않는 모든 이들로부터 그의 명예로운 노력은 마땅한 존경을 얻었다."], ["Vincy had never been at her ease with Mrs. Garth, and frequently spoke of her as a woman who had had to work for her bread—meaning that Mrs.", "그러나 세상 어디에서도 점잖은 방문 교제가, 알맞은 가구와 완비된 만찬 식기 없이 존경만으로 이루어지지는 않는다."], ["Garth had been a teacher before her marriage; in which case an intimacy with Lindley Murray and Mangnall’s Questions was something like a draper’s discrimination of calico trademarks, or a courier’s acquaintance with foreign countries: no woman who was better off needed that sort of thing.", "빈시 부인은 가스 부인 앞에서 한 번도 편안한 적이 없었고, 그녀를 두고 흔히 빵을 벌기 위해 일해야 했던 여자라고 말했다 — 가스 부인이 결혼 전에 교사였다는 뜻이었다. 그렇다면 린들리 머리와 맹널의 문제집에 정통한 것이란, 포목상이 옥양목 상표를 분간하는 것이나 안내인이 외국에 대해 아는 것과 비슷한 셈이었다."], ["And since Mary had been keeping Mr. Featherstone’s house, Mrs.", "형편이 더 나은 여자라면 그런 것이 필요할 리 없었으니까."], ["Vincy’s want of liking for the Garths had been converted into something more positive, by alarm lest Fred should engage himself to this plain girl, whose parents “lived in such a small way.”", "그리고 메리가 페더스톤 씨의 살림을 맡게 된 뒤로, 가스 집안에 대한 빈시 부인의 호감 부족은 더 적극적인 무언가로 바뀌었으니, 부모가 “그토록 가난하게 사는”"], ["Fred, being aware of this, never spoke at home of his visits to Mrs.", "이 못생긴 여자아이에게 프레드가 약혼이라도 할까 봐 불안했던 것이다."], ["Garth, which had of late become more frequent, the increasing ardor of his affection for Mary inclining him the more towards those who belonged to her.", "이를 알고 있던 프레드는 집에서 가스 부인을 찾아간 일을 결코 입에 올리지 않았는데, 그 방문은 근래 들어 더 잦아졌다. 메리에 대한 사랑이 점점 더 뜨거워지면서 그녀에게 속한 이들에게 그가 더욱 끌렸던 까닭이다."]], [["Mr. Garth had a small office in the town, and to this Fred went with his request.", "가스 씨는 읍내에 작은 사무실을 두고 있었고, 프레드는 부탁을 가지고 그곳을 찾아갔다."], ["He obtained it without much difficulty, for a large amount of painful experience had not sufficed to make Caleb Garth cautious about his own affairs, or distrustful of his fellow-men when they had not proved themselves untrustworthy; and he had the highest opinion of Fred, was “sure the lad would turn out well—an open affectionate fellow, with a good bottom to his character—you might trust him for anything.”", "그는 별 어려움 없이 부탁을 들어주었으니, 숱한 쓰라린 경험도 케일럽 가스로 하여금 제 일에 신중을 기하게 하거나, 아직 미덥지 못함이 드러나지 않은 동료들을 불신하게 하지는 못했기 때문이다. 게다가 그는 프레드를 더없이 높이 평가하여, “그 녀석은 분명 잘될 거야 — 솔직하고 다정한 친구인 데다, 사람 됨됨이의 바탕이 좋거든 — 무슨 일이든 믿고 맡길 만하지”라고 했다."], ["Such was Caleb’s psychological argument.", "이것이 케일럽의 심리학적 논거였다."], ["He was one of those rare men who are rigid to themselves and indulgent to others.", "그는 자기 자신에게는 엄격하면서 남에게는 너그러운 보기 드문 사람 가운데 하나였다."], ["He had a certain shame about his neighbors’ errors, and never spoke of them willingly; hence he was not likely to divert his mind from the best mode of hardening timber and other ingenious devices in order to preconceive those errors.", "그는 이웃의 잘못에 대해 일종의 부끄러움을 느꼈고, 그것을 기꺼이 입에 올리는 법이 없었다. 그러니 그는 목재를 가장 잘 단단하게 만드는 방법이나 다른 기발한 장치들에 쏠린 마음을 돌려, 그런 잘못을 미리 헤아릴 사람이 아니었다."], ["If he had to blame any one, it was necessary for him to move all the papers within his reach, or describe various diagrams with his stick, or make calculations with the odd money in his pocket, before he could begin; and he would rather do other men’s work than find fault with their doing.", "누군가를 책망해야 할 때면, 그는 시작도 하기 전에 손이 닿는 종이를 모조리 옮기거나, 지팡이로 갖가지 도면을 그리거나, 주머니 속 잔돈으로 계산을 해 봐야만 했다. 그리고 그는 남이 한 일을 흠잡기보다 차라리 남의 일을 대신 해 주려는 사람이었다."], ["I fear he was a bad disciplinarian.", "안타깝게도 그는 서투른 훈육자였던 듯하다."]], [["When Fred stated the circumstances of his debt, his wish to meet it without troubling his father, and the certainty that the money would be forthcoming so as to cause no one any inconvenience, Caleb pushed his spectacles upward, listened, looked into his favorite’s clear young eyes, and believed him, not distinguishing confidence about the future from veracity about the past; but he felt that it was an occasion for a friendly hint as to conduct, and that before giving his signature he must give a rather strong admonition.", "프레드가 자기 빚의 사정과, 아버지를 번거롭게 하지 않고 그것을 갚고 싶다는 바람과, 누구에게도 불편을 끼치지 않을 만큼 돈이 마련되리라는 확신을 늘어놓자, 케일럽은 안경을 위로 밀어 올리고 귀를 기울이며, 자기가 아끼는 이 청년의 맑고 젊은 눈을 들여다보고는 그를 믿었으니, 미래에 대한 확신과 과거에 대한 진실성을 구별하지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 그는 이것이 처신에 관해 다정한 충고를 건넬 기회라고, 서명을 해 주기 전에 다소 강한 훈계를 해야겠다고 느꼈다."], ["Accordingly, he took the paper and lowered his spectacles, measured the space at his command, reached his pen and examined it, dipped it in the ink and examined it again, then pushed the paper a little way from him, lifted up his spectacles again, showed a deepened depression in the outer angle of his bushy eyebrows, which gave his face a peculiar mildness (pardon these details for once—you would have learned to love them if you had known Caleb Garth), and said in a comfortable tone,—", "그리하여 그는 종이를 집어 들고 안경을 내려 쓰고는, 자기가 쓸 자리를 가늠하고, 펜을 집어 살펴보고, 잉크에 적신 뒤 다시 살펴보고, 그런 다음 종이를 살짝 밀어 두고 다시 안경을 위로 올렸다. 그의 덥수룩한 눈썹 바깥쪽 끝이 한층 깊이 처지면서 그의 얼굴에 독특한 온화함을 자아냈으니(이 한 번만은 이런 자질구레함을 너그러이 봐주시기를 — 케일럽 가스를 알았더라면 당신도 그런 점을 사랑하게 되었으리라), 그는 편안한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“It was a misfortune, eh, that breaking the horse’s knees?", "“말 무릎을 부러뜨린 건, 음, 불운이었지?"], ["And then, these exchanges, they don’t answer when you have ’cute jockeys to deal with.", "그리고 이런 맞바꾸기란, 약삭빠른 말 장수들을 상대할 때는 영 수지가 안 맞아."], ["You’ll be wiser another time, my boy.”", "다음번엔 더 영리해질 게다, 얘야.”"]], [["Whereupon Caleb drew down his spectacles, and proceeded to write his signature with the care which he always gave to that performance; for whatever he did in the way of business he did well.", "그러고 나서 케일럽은 안경을 내려 쓰고, 늘 그 일에 기울이던 정성을 다해 자기 서명을 써 내려갔다. 무릇 그는 일이라면 무엇이든 잘 해냈으니까."], ["He contemplated the large well-proportioned letters and final flourish, with his head a trifle on one side for an instant, then handed it to Fred, said “Good-by,” and returned forthwith to his absorption in a plan for Sir James Chettam’s new farm-buildings.", "그는 큼직하고 균형 잡힌 글자들과 마지막 장식 획을 잠시 고개를 살짝 한쪽으로 기울인 채 들여다보더니, 그것을 프레드에게 건네고 “잘 가게”라고 말한 뒤, 곧장 제임스 체텀 경의 새 농장 건물 설계에 다시 몰두했다."]], [["Either because his interest in this work thrust the incident of the signature from his memory, or for some reason of which Caleb was more conscious, Mrs. Garth remained ignorant of the affair.", "이 일에 대한 관심이 서명이라는 사건을 그의 기억에서 밀어내 버렸기 때문인지, 아니면 케일럽 자신이 더 의식하고 있던 어떤 이유에서인지, 가스 부인은 그 일을 까맣게 모른 채 지냈다."]], [["Since it occurred, a change had come over Fred’s sky, which altered his view of the distance, and was the reason why his uncle Featherstone’s present of money was of importance enough to make his color come and go, first with a too definite expectation, and afterwards with a proportionate disappointment.", "그 일이 있은 뒤로 프레드의 하늘에 변화가 찾아왔으니, 그것은 그가 앞날을 바라보는 시야를 바꾸어 놓았고, 페더스톤 삼촌이 준 돈이 그의 얼굴빛을 변하게 할 만큼 — 처음엔 지나치게 분명한 기대로, 그다음엔 그에 걸맞은 실망으로 — 중요해진 까닭이었다."], ["His failure in passing his examination, had made his accumulation of college debts the more unpardonable by his father, and there had been an unprecedented storm at home. Mr.", "시험에 통과하지 못한 일 탓에 그가 쌓은 대학 빚은 아버지로서 더욱 용서할 수 없는 것이 되었고, 집에서는 전에 없던 폭풍이 일었다."], ["Vincy had sworn that if he had anything more of that sort to put up with, Fred should turn out and get his living how he could; and he had never yet quite recovered his good-humored tone to his son, who had especially enraged him by saying at this stage of things that he did not want to be a clergyman, and would rather not “go on with that.”", "빈시 씨는 그런 일을 한 번만 더 참아야 한다면 프레드를 내쫓아 알아서 먹고살게 하겠노라 호언했고, 아들에게 너그럽던 어조를 아직 온전히 되찾지 못했다. 더구나 프레드는 이 마당에 자기는 성직자가 되고 싶지 않으며 “그 일을 계속하지”"], ["Fred was conscious that he would have been yet more severely dealt with if his family as well as himself had not secretly regarded him as Mr.", "않겠다고 말해 그를 특히 격노케 했던 것이다. 프레드는 자기 자신뿐 아니라 가족까지도 그를 페더스톤 씨의 상속인으로 은근히 여기지 않았더라면 더욱 호되게 다루어졌으리라는 것을 의식하고 있었다."], ["Featherstone’s heir; that old gentleman’s pride in him, and apparent fondness for him, serving in the stead of more exemplary conduct—just as when a youthful nobleman steals jewellery we call the act kleptomania, speak of it with a philosophical smile, and never think of his being sent to the house of correction as if he were a ragged boy who had stolen turnips.", "그 노인이 그를 자랑스러워하고 그를 아끼는 듯 보이는 것이, 더 모범적인 처신을 대신해 준 셈이었으니 — 마치 젊은 귀족이 보석을 훔치면 우리는 그 행위를 도벽이라 부르며 철학적인 미소로 이야기할 뿐, 순무를 훔친 누더기 차림의 소년처럼 그를 교화원에 보낼 생각은 결코 하지 않는 것과 같았다."], ["In fact, tacit expectations of what would be done for him by uncle Featherstone determined the angle at which most people viewed Fred Vincy in Middlemarch; and in his own consciousness, what uncle Featherstone would do for him in an emergency, or what he would do simply as an incorporated luck, formed always an immeasurable depth of aerial perspective.", "사실 페더스톤 삼촌이 그에게 무엇을 해 줄 것인가에 대한 무언의 기대가, 미들마치에서 대부분의 사람들이 프레드 빈시를 바라보는 각도를 결정했다. 그리고 그 자신의 의식 속에서도, 위급할 때 페더스톤 삼촌이 무엇을 해 줄 것인가, 혹은 단지 하나의 화신화된 행운으로서 무엇을 해 줄 것인가 하는 것은, 늘 가늠할 수 없이 깊은 공중 원근법을 이루고 있었다."], ["But that present of bank-notes, once made, was measurable, and being applied to the amount of the debt, showed a deficit which had still to be filled up either by Fred’s “judgment” or by luck in some other shape.", "그러나 일단 건네진 그 지폐 선물은 가늠할 수 있는 것이었고, 그것을 빚의 액수에 적용해 보니 여전히 메워야 할 부족분이 드러났는데, 그것은 프레드의 “안목”이나 다른 형태의 행운으로 채워져야 했다."], ["For that little episode of the alleged borrowing, in which he had made his father the agent in getting the Bulstrode certificate, was a new reason against going to his father for money towards meeting his actual debt.", "빌렸다고 하는 그 작은 일화 — 그가 아버지를 시켜 불스트로드의 증명서를 받게 했던 그 일 — 때문에, 실제 빚을 갚으려고 아버지에게 돈을 청하는 것이 새삼 꺼려졌던 것이다."], ["Fred was keen enough to foresee that anger would confuse distinctions, and that his denial of having borrowed expressly on the strength of his uncle’s will would be taken as a falsehood.", "프레드는 분노가 구별을 흐려 놓으리라는 것, 그리고 자기가 삼촌의 유언을 명백한 담보로 삼아 돈을 빌린 적이 없다고 부인해 봐야 거짓말로 받아들여지리라는 것을 충분히 예견할 만큼 명민했다."], ["He had gone to his father and told him one vexatious affair, and he had left another untold: in such cases the complete revelation always produces the impression of a previous duplicity.", "그는 아버지에게 가서 한 가지 골치 아픈 일은 털어놓고 다른 한 가지는 말하지 않은 채 두었던 것이다. 이런 경우 완전한 폭로란 늘 앞선 이중성이 있었다는 인상을 남기는 법이다."], ["Now Fred piqued himself on keeping clear of lies, and even fibs; he often shrugged his shoulders and made a significant grimace at what he called Rosamond’s fibs (it is only brothers who can associate such ideas with a lovely girl); and rather than incur the accusation of falsehood he would even incur some trouble and self-restraint.", "그런데 프레드는 거짓말은 물론 사소한 허튼소리조차 피하는 것을 자랑으로 삼았다. 그는 로저먼드의 허튼소리라고 부르는 것을 두고 종종 어깨를 으쓱하며 의미심장하게 얼굴을 찌푸리곤 했다(이런 생각을 사랑스러운 처녀와 연결 지을 수 있는 것은 오직 오라비뿐이다)."], ["It was under strong inward pressure of this kind that Fred had taken the wise step of depositing the eighty pounds with his mother.", "그리고 거짓말쟁이라는 비난을 받느니 차라리 얼마간의 수고와 자제를 감수하려 했다. 프레드가 그 팔십 파운드를 어머니에게 맡기는 현명한 조치를 취한 것도 이런 강한 내적 압박 아래서였다."], ["It was a pity that he had not at once given them to Mr.", "그것을 곧장 가스 씨에게 건네지 않은 것은 아쉬운 일이었다."], ["Garth; but he meant to make the sum complete with another sixty, and with a view to this, he had kept twenty pounds in his own pocket as a sort of seed-corn, which, planted by judgment, and watered by luck, might yield more than threefold—a very poor rate of multiplication when the field is a young gentleman’s infinite soul, with all the numerals at command.", "그러나 그는 거기에 육십 파운드를 더해 액수를 채울 작정이었고, 이를 위해 이십 파운드를 일종의 씨앗 곡식처럼 제 주머니에 넣어 두었으니, 그것은 안목으로 심고 행운으로 물을 준다면 세 곱절 넘게 거둘 수 있을 터였다 — 밭이 온갖 숫자를 마음대로 부리는 젊은 신사의 무한한 영혼일 때, 그것은 참으로 보잘것없는 증식률이었지만."]], [["Fred was not a gambler: he had not that specific disease in which the suspension of the whole nervous energy on a chance or risk becomes as necessary as the dram to the drunkard; he had only the tendency to that diffusive form of gambling which has no alcoholic intensity, but is carried on with the healthiest chyle-fed blood, keeping up a joyous imaginative activity which fashions events according to desire, and having no fears about its own weather, only sees the advantage there must be to others in going aboard with it.", "프레드는 도박꾼이 아니었다. 그는 우연이나 위험에 온 신경의 기운을 거는 일이 술꾼에게 한 모금 술과 같이 필요해지는, 그런 특정한 병을 앓지는 않았다."], ["Hopefulness has a pleasure in making a throw of any kind, because the prospect of success is certain; and only a more generous pleasure in offering as many as possible a share in the stake.", "그에게는 다만 그 산만한 형태의 도박 성향이 있을 뿐이었으니, 그것은 알코올 같은 격렬함은 없이 더없이 건강하고 영양을 잘 섭취한 피로 이루어지며, 욕망에 따라 사건을 빚어내는 즐겁고 상상력 넘치는 활동을 유지하고, 제 앞날의 날씨를 두려워하지 않은 채 함께 배에 올라타는 것이 남들에게 분명 이득이 되리라고만 여기는 것이었다. 낙관은 어떤 종류의 던지기든 즐거움을 느끼게 하니, 성공의 전망이 확실하기 때문이다."], ["Fred liked play, especially billiards, as he liked hunting or riding a steeple-chase; and he only liked it the better because he wanted money and hoped to win.", "그리고 되도록 많은 이에게 그 판돈의 몫을 나눠 주는 데서 한층 너그러운 즐거움을 느낄 뿐이다. 프레드는 놀이를, 특히 당구를 좋아했으니, 사냥이나 장애물 경마를 좋아하는 것과 같았다."], ["But the twenty pounds’ worth of seed-corn had been planted in vain in the seductive green plot—all of it at least which had not been dispersed by the roadside—and Fred found himself close upon the term of payment with no money at command beyond the eighty pounds which he had deposited with his mother.", "그리고 돈이 필요하고 따기를 바랐기에 그것을 더욱 좋아할 뿐이었다. 그러나 이십 파운드어치 씨앗 곡식은 그 유혹적인 초록 풀밭에 헛되이 뿌려졌으니 — 적어도 길가에 흩어지지 않은 부분은 모두 그러했고 — 프레드는 어머니에게 맡긴 팔십 파운드 말고는 마음대로 쓸 돈 한 푼 없이 지불 기한이 코앞에 닥친 처지가 되었다."], ["The broken-winded horse which he rode represented a present which had been made to him a long while ago by his uncle Featherstone: his father always allowed him to keep a horse, Mr.", "그가 타고 다니는 숨찬 말은 오래전 페더스톤 삼촌이 그에게 준 선물이었다. 아버지는 늘 그가 말 한 필을 두는 것을 허락했으니, 빈시 씨 자신의 습관 탓에 다소 사람 속을 뒤집는 아들에게도 그것만은 합당한 요구로 여겼던 것이다."], ["Vincy’s own habits making him regard this as a reasonable demand even for a son who was rather exasperating.", "그러니 이 말은 프레드의 재산이었고, 임박한 어음을 갚아야 한다는 불안 속에서 그는 그것 없이는 삶이 정녕 무가치해질 소유물을 희생하기로 마음먹었다."], ["This horse, then, was Fred’s property, and in his anxiety to meet the imminent bill he determined to sacrifice a possession without which life would certainly be worth little.", "그는 영웅적인 기분으로 그 결심을 했다 — 가스 씨에게 한 약속을 어길까 하는 두려움과 메리에 대한 사랑, 그리고 그녀의 평가에 대한 경외감이 강요한 영웅심이었다."], ["He made the resolution with a sense of heroism—heroism forced on him by the dread of breaking his word to Mr. Garth, by his love for Mary and awe of her opinion.", "그는 이튿날 아침 열리는 하운즐리 말 시장으로 출발해서 — 그저 말을 팔고 그 돈을 마차로 가져오면 되지 않을까?"], ["He would start for Houndsley horse-fair which was to be held the next morning, and—simply sell his horse, bringing back the money by coach?—Well, the horse would hardly fetch more than thirty pounds, and there was no knowing what might happen; it would be folly to balk himself of luck beforehand.", "— 글쎄, 그 말은 삼십 파운드 이상 받기 어려울 테고, 무슨 일이 일어날지 알 수 없었다. 미리 행운을 가로막는 것은 어리석은 짓이리라."], ["It was a hundred to one that some good chance would fall in his way; the longer he thought of it, the less possible it seemed that he should not have a good chance, and the less reasonable that he should not equip himself with the powder and shot for bringing it down.", "좋은 기회 하나가 그의 앞에 떨어질 가능성은 백에 아흔아홉이었다. 생각하면 할수록 좋은 기회가 오지 않는다는 것은 더욱 있을 수 없는 일로 여겨졌고, 그것을 떨어뜨릴 화약과 산탄으로 무장하지 않는 것이 더욱 이치에 맞지 않게 느껴졌다."], ["He would ride to Houndsley with Bambridge and with Horrock “the vet,”", "그는 뱀브리지와 “수의사”"], ["and without asking them anything expressly, he should virtually get the benefit of their opinion.", "호럭과 함께 하운즐리로 말을 몰고 가서, 무엇 하나 대놓고 묻지 않더라도 사실상 그들의 견해라는 이득을 챙기게 될 것이었다."], ["Before he set out, Fred got the eighty pounds from his mother.", "길을 떠나기 전에 프레드는 어머니에게서 팔십 파운드를 받아 냈다."]], [["Most of those who saw Fred riding out of Middlemarch in company with Bambridge and Horrock, on his way of course to Houndsley horse-fair, thought that young Vincy was pleasure-seeking as usual; and but for an unwonted consciousness of grave matters on hand, he himself would have had a sense of dissipation, and of doing what might be expected of a gay young fellow.", "프레드가 뱀브리지와 호럭과 한 무리가 되어 — 물론 하운즐리 말 시장으로 가는 길에 — 미들마치를 빠져나가는 것을 본 대부분의 사람들은, 빈시 청년이 여느 때처럼 쾌락을 좇고 있다고 여겼다. 그리고 중대한 일이 걸려 있다는 낯선 의식만 없었더라면, 그 자신도 방탕에 빠진 듯한 기분, 명랑한 젊은이에게 기대됨 직한 일을 하고 있다는 기분을 느꼈을 것이다."], ["Considering that Fred was not at all coarse, that he rather looked down on the manners and speech of young men who had not been to the university, and that he had written stanzas as pastoral and unvoluptuous as his flute-playing, his attraction towards Bambridge and Horrock was an interesting fact which even the love of horse-flesh would not wholly account for without that mysterious influence of Naming which determinates so much of mortal choice.", "프레드가 결코 천박하지 않았다는 것, 대학에 다니지 않은 젊은이들의 몸가짐과 말씨를 다소 깔보았다는 것, 그리고 그의 플루트 연주만큼이나 목가적이고 관능과는 거리가 먼 시구를 지었다는 것을 생각하면, 뱀브리지와 호럭에게 끌렸다는 것은 흥미로운 사실이었으니, 인간의 선택을 그토록 많이 좌우하는 그 신비로운 명명(命名)의 영향 없이는 말에 대한 애착만으로 온전히 설명될 수 없는 일이었다. “쾌락”"], ["Under any other name than “pleasure”", "말고 다른 어떤 이름이 붙었더라면 뱀브리지 씨와 호럭 씨의 교제는 분명 단조롭다고 여겨졌으리라."], ["the society of Messieurs Bambridge and Horrock must certainly have been regarded as monotonous; and to arrive with them at Houndsley on a drizzling afternoon, to get down at the Red Lion in a street shaded with coal-dust, and dine in a room furnished with a dirt-enamelled map of the county, a bad portrait of an anonymous horse in a stable, His Majesty George the Fourth with legs and cravat, and various leaden spittoons, might have seemed a hard business, but for the sustaining power of nomenclature which determined that the pursuit of these things was “gay.”", "그리고 보슬비 내리는 오후에 그들과 함께 하운즐리에 도착하여, 석탄 먼지에 그늘진 거리의 레드 라이언 여관에서 내려, 때에 절어 윤이 나는 그 지방 지도와, 마구간에 선 이름 모를 말의 형편없는 초상화와, 다리와 크라바트를 갖춘 국왕 조지 4세의 그림과, 갖가지 납 타구가 비치된 방에서 식사를 하는 것은, 그런 일을 좇는 것이 “명랑한” 일이라고 못 박은 명명법의 떠받치는 힘이 없었더라면 고된 일로 보였을 것이다."]], [["In Mr. Horrock there was certainly an apparent unfathomableness which offered play to the imagination.", "호럭 씨에게는 분명 상상력에 여지를 주는 헤아릴 수 없는 무언가가 있어 보였다."], ["Costume, at a glance, gave him a thrilling association with horses (enough to specify the hat-brim which took the slightest upward angle just to escape the suspicion of bending downwards), and nature had given him a face which by dint of Mongolian eyes, and a nose, mouth, and chin seeming to follow his hat-brim in a moderate inclination upwards, gave the effect of a subdued unchangeable sceptical smile, of all expressions the most tyrannous over a susceptible mind, and, when accompanied by adequate silence, likely to create the reputation of an invincible understanding, an infinite fund of humor—too dry to flow, and probably in a state of immovable crust,—and a critical judgment which, if you could ever be fortunate enough to know it, would be the thing and no other.", "차림새는 한눈에 그를 말과 짜릿하게 결부시켰고(아래로 굽었다는 의심을 가까스로 면할 만큼만 살짝 위로 각이 진 모자챙을 짚는 것으로 충분하리라), 또 자연은 그에게 몽골인 같은 눈과, 모자챙을 따라 적당히 위로 기우는 듯한 코와 입과 턱을 지닌 얼굴을 주어, 변치 않는 은근한 회의적 미소의 인상을 자아냈다. 모든 표정 가운데서도 그것은 감수성 예민한 마음을 가장 폭압적으로 지배하는 것이었고, 충분한 침묵이 따를 때면 천하무적의 분별력과, 흐르기엔 너무 메말라 아마도 꿈쩍 않는 딱지 상태에 있을 무진장한 유머와, 만에 하나 당신이 그것을 알 수 있는 행운을 누린다면 다른 어떤 것도 아닌 바로 그것이라 할 비평적 판단력을 갖췄다는 평판을 낳기 십상이었다."], ["It is a physiognomy seen in all vocations, but perhaps it has never been more powerful over the youth of England than in a judge of horses.", "그것은 온갖 직업에서 볼 수 있는 인상이지만, 어쩌면 말 감정가에게서만큼 잉글랜드 젊은이들을 강하게 사로잡은 적은 없었으리라."]], [["Mr. Horrock, at a question from Fred about his horse’s fetlock, turned sideways in his saddle, and watched the horse’s action for the space of three minutes, then turned forward, twitched his own bridle, and remained silent with a profile neither more nor less sceptical than it had been.", "프레드가 자기 말의 발목 털에 대해 묻자, 호럭 씨는 안장에서 옆으로 몸을 틀어 삼 분 동안 말의 움직임을 지켜보더니, 다시 앞을 향하고 자기 고삐를 한 번 당긴 뒤, 조금도 더하지도 덜하지도 않게 여전히 회의적인 옆모습으로 침묵을 지켰다."]], [["The part thus played in dialogue by Mr. Horrock was terribly effective.", "이렇게 호럭 씨가 대화에서 맡은 역할은 무시무시하게 효과적이었다."], ["A mixture of passions was excited in Fred—a mad desire to thrash Horrock’s opinion into utterance, restrained by anxiety to retain the advantage of his friendship.", "프레드의 마음속에는 갖가지 감정이 뒤섞여 일었다 — 호럭의 견해를 후려쳐서라도 입 밖에 내게 하고 싶은 미친 듯한 욕망과, 그 우정의 이점을 잃지 않으려는 불안이 그것을 억눌렀던 것이다."], ["There was always the chance that Horrock might say something quite invaluable at the right moment.", "호럭이 결정적인 순간에 더없이 값진 무언가를 말할 가능성은 늘 있었다."]], [["Mr. Bambridge had more open manners, and appeared to give forth his ideas without economy.", "뱀브리지 씨는 더 솔직한 태도를 지녀, 자기 생각을 아낌없이 쏟아 내는 듯했다."], ["He was loud, robust, and was sometimes spoken of as being “given to indulgence”—chiefly in swearing, drinking, and beating his wife.", "그는 목소리가 크고 다부졌으며, 때로는 “방종에 빠졌다”고 일컬어졌으니 — 주로 욕설과 술, 그리고 아내를 때리는 일에서였다. 그에게 손해를 본 어떤 사람들은 그를 악당이라 불렀다."], ["Some people who had lost by him called him a vicious man; but he regarded horse-dealing as the finest of the arts, and might have argued plausibly that it had nothing to do with morality.", "그러나 그는 말 거래를 가장 훌륭한 기예로 여겼고, 그것이 도덕과는 아무 상관이 없다고 그럴듯하게 주장할 법도 했다. 그가 번창하는 사람임은 부인할 수 없었으니, 남들이 절제를 견디는 것보다 그가 술을 견디는 솜씨가 나았고, 대체로 푸른 월계수처럼 무성하게 번성했다."], ["He was undeniably a prosperous man, bore his drinking better than others bore their moderation, and, on the whole, flourished like the green bay-tree.", "그러나 그의 화제의 폭은 한정되어 있어서, “브랜디 방울”이라는 그 오래되고 멋진 곡조처럼, 얼마 지나면 약한 머리는 어지러워질 만큼 같은 자리를 맴도는 느낌을 주었다."], ["But his range of conversation was limited, and like the fine old tune, “Drops of brandy,”", "그래도 뱀브리지 씨가 살짝 섞여 들면 미들마치의 여러 모임에 색조와 개성이 더해진다고들 여겼다."], ["gave you after a while a sense of returning upon itself in a way that might make weak heads dizzy.", "그는 그린 드래건의 술집과 당구장에서 두드러진 인물이었다."], ["But a slight infusion of Mr. Bambridge was felt to give tone and character to several circles in Middlemarch; and he was a distinguished figure in the bar and billiard-room at the Green Dragon.", "그는 경마계 영웅들에 관한 일화 몇 가지와, 후작과 자작들의 갖가지 교묘한 술책을 알고 있었으니, 그것은 사기 도박꾼들 사이에서도 혈통이 그 우월함을 주장한다는 것을 증명하는 듯했다."], ["He knew some anecdotes about the heroes of the turf, and various clever tricks of Marquesses and Viscounts which seemed to prove that blood asserted its pre-eminence even among black-legs; but the minute retentiveness of his memory was chiefly shown about the horses he had himself bought and sold; the number of miles they would trot you in no time without turning a hair being, after the lapse of years, still a subject of passionate asseveration, in which he would assist the imagination of his hearers by solemnly swearing that they never saw anything like it.", "그러나 그의 기억의 세세한 보존력이 주로 발휘되는 것은 자기가 직접 사고팔았던 말들에 관해서였다. 그 말들이 털끝 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 눈 깜짝할 새 몇 마일을 달리는지는, 여러 해가 지난 뒤에도 여전히 열렬한 단언의 주제였고, 그럴 때면 그는 그런 광경을 본 적이 없으리라고 엄숙히 맹세하여 듣는 이의 상상을 거들곤 했다."], ["In short, Mr. Bambridge was a man of pleasure and a gay companion.", "요컨대 뱀브리지 씨는 쾌락을 아는 사내이자 명랑한 동행이었다."]], [["Fred was subtle, and did not tell his friends that he was going to Houndsley bent on selling his horse: he wished to get indirectly at their genuine opinion of its value, not being aware that a genuine opinion was the last thing likely to be extracted from such eminent critics.", "프레드는 약삭빨라서, 자기가 말을 팔 작정으로 하운즐리에 간다는 것을 친구들에게 말하지 않았다. 그는 말 값에 대한 그들의 진짜 견해를 에둘러 얻어 내고 싶었던 것이다."], ["It was not Mr. Bambridge’s weakness to be a gratuitous flatterer.", "그런 저명한 비평가들에게서 진짜 견해를 끌어낼 가능성이란 가장 희박하다는 것을 모른 채 말이다."], ["He had never before been so much struck with the fact that this unfortunate bay was a roarer to a degree which required the roundest word for perdition to give you any idea of it.", "공짜로 아첨하는 것은 뱀브리지 씨의 약점이 아니었다. 그는 이 불행한 밤색 말이 천벌이라는 말의 가장 둥글둥글한 표현을 써야만 짐작이라도 갈 만큼 숨이 차는 말이라는 사실에, 그토록 강한 인상을 받은 적이 일찍이 없었다."]], [["“You made a bad hand at swapping when you went to anybody but me, Vincy!", "“자네, 나 말고 딴 사람한테 가서 바꿔치기를 해서 헛수고를 했구먼, 빈시!"], ["Why, you never threw your leg across a finer horse than that chestnut, and you gave him for this brute.", "아니, 자네가 그 밤색 말보다 더 좋은 말에 다리를 걸쳐 본 적이 없었는데, 그걸 주고 이 짐승을 받았다니. 저놈을 가볍게 달리게 해 보면, 톱질꾼 스무 명이 한꺼번에 톱질하는 소리를 낸다고."], ["If you set him cantering, he goes on like twenty sawyers.", "내 평생 저것보다 더 심하게 숨찬 말은 딱 한 마리 들어 봤는데, 그건 적갈색 말이었어. 곡물상 페그웰 거였지."], ["I never heard but one worse roarer in my life, and that was a roan: it belonged to Pegwell, the corn-factor; he used to drive him in his gig seven years ago, and he wanted me to take him, but I said, ‘Thank you, Peg, I don’t deal in wind-instruments.’ That was what I said.", "그 사람이 칠 년 전에 그놈을 이륜마차에 메고 다녔는데, 나더러 받으라고 하더라고. 그래서 내가 그랬지, ‘고맙네, 페그, 난 관악기는 안 다루네.’ 그렇게 말했지."], ["It went the round of the country, that joke did.", "그 농담이 온 고을을 돌았어."], ["But, what the hell!", "하지만 빌어먹을!"], ["the horse was a penny trumpet to that roarer of yours.”", "그 말은 자네 그 숨찬 놈에 대면 한 푼짜리 나팔에 불과했다고.”"]], [["“Why, you said just now his was worse than mine,”", "“아니, 방금은 그 말이 내 것보다 더 심하다고 했잖소.”"], ["said Fred, more irritable than usual.", "프레드가 평소보다 더 짜증스럽게 말했다."]], [["“I said a lie, then,” said Mr.", "“그럼 내가 거짓말한 거지.”"], ["Bambridge, emphatically.", "뱀브리지 씨가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“There wasn’t a penny to choose between ’em.”", "“둘 사이엔 한 푼어치 차이도 없었으니까.”"]], [["Fred spurred his horse, and they trotted on a little way.", "프레드가 말에 박차를 가하자 그들은 잠시 빠르게 나아갔다."], ["When they slackened again, Mr. Bambridge said—", "다시 속도를 늦추었을 때 뱀브리지 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Not but what the roan was a better trotter than yours.”", "“그렇다고 그 적갈색 말이 자네 것보다 더 잘 달리는 놈이 아니었던 건 아니지만.”"]], [["“I’m quite satisfied with his paces, I know,”", "“난 이놈 걸음에 아주 만족하오, 분명히 말하건대.”"], ["said Fred, who required all the consciousness of being in gay company to support him; “I say his trot is an uncommonly clean one, eh, Horrock?”", "명랑한 무리와 함께 있다는 의식을 죄다 끌어모아야 버틸 수 있었던 프레드가 말했다. “이놈 걸음새는 유달리 깔끔하다니까, 안 그렇소, 호럭?”"]], [["Mr. Horrock looked before him with as complete a neutrality as if he had been a portrait by a great master.", "호럭 씨는 대가의 손으로 그린 초상화라도 되는 양 완벽한 중립을 지키며 앞만 바라보았다."]], [["Fred gave up the fallacious hope of getting a genuine opinion; but on reflection he saw that Bambridge’s depreciation and Horrock’s silence were both virtually encouraging, and indicated that they thought better of the horse than they chose to say.", "프레드는 진짜 견해를 얻으려는 헛된 희망을 포기했다. 그러나 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 뱀브리지의 깎아내림과 호럭의 침묵이 둘 다 사실상 고무적이며, 그들이 입 밖에 내려는 것보다 그 말을 더 좋게 여기고 있음을 가리킨다는 것을 알 수 있었다."]], [["That very evening, indeed, before the fair had set in, Fred thought he saw a favorable opening for disposing advantageously of his horse, but an opening which made him congratulate himself on his foresight in bringing with him his eighty pounds.", "실로 바로 그날 저녁, 시장이 채 열리기도 전에, 프레드는 자기 말을 유리하게 처분할 좋은 기회가 보인다고 생각했다. 다만 그것은 팔십 파운드를 챙겨 온 자기 선견지명을 스스로 대견해할 만한 기회였다."], ["A young farmer, acquainted with Mr. Bambridge, came into the Red Lion, and entered into conversation about parting with a hunter, which he introduced at once as Diamond, implying that it was a public character.", "뱀브리지 씨와 아는 사이인 젊은 농부 하나가 레드 라이언에 들어와, 사냥말 한 마리를 처분하는 이야기를 꺼냈는데, 대뜸 그것을 다이아몬드라고 소개하여 마치 널리 알려진 명물인 양 암시했다."], ["For himself he only wanted a useful hack, which would draw upon occasion; being about to marry and to give up hunting.", "자기로 말하자면 가끔 짐을 끌 수 있는 쓸 만한 보통 말이 한 마리 필요할 뿐이었으니, 곧 결혼을 앞두고 사냥을 그만둘 참이었던 것이다."], ["The hunter was in a friend’s stable at some little distance; there was still time for gentlemen to see it before dark.", "그 사냥말은 조금 떨어진 친구의 마구간에 있었다. 어두워지기 전에 신사분들이 보러 갈 시간은 아직 있었다."], ["The friend’s stable had to be reached through a back street where you might as easily have been poisoned without expense of drugs as in any grim street of that unsanitary period.", "친구의 마구간은 뒷골목을 지나야 닿을 수 있었는데, 그 비위생적인 시절의 어느 음산한 거리에서나 그렇듯, 약값 한 푼 들이지 않고도 손쉽게 독에 중독될 만한 곳이었다."], ["Fred was not fortified against disgust by brandy, as his companions were, but the hope of having at last seen the horse that would enable him to make money was exhilarating enough to lead him over the same ground again the first thing in the morning.", "프레드는 동행들처럼 브랜디로 역겨움에 무장하지 못했지만, 마침내 돈을 벌게 해 줄 말을 보았다는 희망에 한껏 들떠, 이튿날 아침 제일 먼저 같은 길을 다시 밟게 되었다."], ["He felt sure that if he did not come to a bargain with the farmer, Bambridge would; for the stress of circumstances, Fred felt, was sharpening his acuteness and endowing him with all the constructive power of suspicion.", "만약 자기가 그 농부와 흥정을 매듭짓지 못하면 뱀브리지가 매듭지으리라고 그는 확신했다. 상황의 압박이 자기의 명민함을 날카롭게 갈고, 의심이 지닌 온갖 구성력을 자기에게 부여하고 있다고 프레드는 느꼈던 것이다."], ["Bambridge had run down Diamond in a way that he never would have done (the horse being a friend’s) if he had not thought of buying it; every one who looked at the animal—even Horrock—was evidently impressed with its merit.", "뱀브리지가 다이아몬드를 깎아내린 것은, 사려는 생각이 없었더라면 (그 말이 친구의 것이니) 결코 하지 않았을 짓이었다. 그 짐승을 본 사람이라면 누구나 — 심지어 호럭조차 — 분명 그 가치에 깊은 인상을 받았다."], ["To get all the advantage of being with men of this sort, you must know how to draw your inferences, and not be a spoon who takes things literally.", "이런 부류의 사람들과 어울리는 이점을 모조리 챙기려면, 추론하는 법을 알아야 하고, 무엇이든 곧이곧대로 받아들이는 얼간이가 되어서는 안 된다."], ["The color of the horse was a dappled gray, and Fred happened to know that Lord Medlicote’s man was on the look-out for just such a horse.", "그 말의 빛깔은 얼룩진 회색이었는데, 마침 프레드는 메들리코트 경의 하인이 바로 그런 말을 찾고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["After all his running down, Bambridge let it out in the course of the evening, when the farmer was absent, that he had seen worse horses go for eighty pounds.", "그 모든 깎아내림 끝에, 농부가 자리를 비운 저녁나절에 뱀브리지는 그보다 못한 말도 팔십 파운드에 팔리는 것을 본 적이 있다고 무심코 흘렸다."], ["Of course he contradicted himself twenty times over, but when you know what is likely to be true you can test a man’s admissions.", "물론 그는 스무 번도 넘게 자기 말을 뒤집었지만, 무엇이 사실일 법한지를 알면 사람의 시인을 시험해 볼 수 있는 법이다."], ["And Fred could not but reckon his own judgment of a horse as worth something.", "그리고 프레드도 말에 대한 자기 안목이 어느 정도 값어치가 있다고 여기지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The farmer had paused over Fred’s respectable though broken-winded steed long enough to show that he thought it worth consideration, and it seemed probable that he would take it, with five-and-twenty pounds in addition, as the equivalent of Diamond.", "농부는 프레드의 점잖되 숨찬 말 앞에서 한참 머뭇거려, 그것을 고려할 만하다고 여기는 기색을 보였고, 거기에 이십오 파운드를 더 얹어 받고 다이아몬드의 대가로 그것을 가져갈 듯했다."], ["In that case Fred, when he had parted with his new horse for at least eighty pounds, would be fifty-five pounds in pocket by the transaction, and would have a hundred and thirty-five pounds towards meeting the bill; so that the deficit temporarily thrown on Mr. Garth would at the utmost be twenty-five pounds.", "그렇게 되면 프레드는 새 말을 적어도 팔십 파운드에 처분했을 때, 이 거래로 오십오 파운드를 손에 쥐게 되고, 어음을 갚는 데 백삼십오 파운드를 보태게 되니, 가스 씨에게 임시로 떠넘겨질 부족분은 기껏해야 이십오 파운드일 터였다."], ["By the time he was hurrying on his clothes in the morning, he saw so clearly the importance of not losing this rare chance, that if Bambridge and Horrock had both dissuaded him, he would not have been deluded into a direct interpretation of their purpose: he would have been aware that those deep hands held something else than a young fellow’s interest.", "아침에 옷을 서둘러 챙겨 입을 무렵, 그는 이 드문 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 된다는 것을 너무도 분명히 깨달은 나머지, 설령 뱀브리지와 호럭이 둘 다 만류했더라도 그들의 의도를 곧이곧대로 해석하는 미혹에 빠지지는 않았을 것이다. 그 음흉한 자들이 젊은이의 이익 말고 딴것을 손에 쥐고 있다는 것을 알아챘으리라."], ["With regard to horses, distrust was your only clew.", "말에 관해서라면 불신이야말로 유일한 실마리였다."], ["But scepticism, as we know, can never be thoroughly applied, else life would come to a standstill: something we must believe in and do, and whatever that something may be called, it is virtually our own judgment, even when it seems like the most slavish reliance on another.", "그러나 우리가 알다시피 회의란 결코 철저히 적용될 수 없으니, 그렇지 않다면 삶은 멈춰 버릴 것이다. 무언가 우리는 믿고 행해야 하며, 그 무언가가 무어라 불리든 그것은 사실상 우리 자신의 판단이니, 남에게 가장 노예처럼 기대는 것처럼 보일 때조차 그러하다."], ["Fred believed in the excellence of his bargain, and even before the fair had well set in, had got possession of the dappled gray, at the price of his old horse and thirty pounds in addition—only five pounds more than he had expected to give.", "프레드는 자기 거래가 훌륭하다고 믿었고, 시장이 채 제대로 열리기도 전에 그 얼룩진 회색 말을, 옛 말에 삼십 파운드를 더 얹은 값에 손에 넣었으니 — 그가 치르리라 예상했던 것보다 고작 오 파운드 더 든 셈이었다."]], [["But he felt a little worried and wearied, perhaps with mental debate, and without waiting for the further gayeties of the horse-fair, he set out alone on his fourteen miles’ journey, meaning to take it very quietly and keep his horse fresh.", "그러나 그는 아마도 마음속 논쟁 탓이었는지 약간 근심스럽고 지친 기분이 들어, 말 시장의 더한 흥겨움을 기다리지 않고 홀로 십사 마일의 여정에 올랐으니, 아주 느긋하게 가서 말을 싱싱하게 유지할 작정이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV.", "# CHAPTER XXIV."]], [["“The offender’s sorrow brings but small relief To him who wears the strong offence’s cross.”", "사랑하는 이가 저지른 죄의 슬픔은 그 죄의 무거운 십자가를 지고 있는 자에게 거의 위안이 되지 못한다."], ["—SHAKESPEARE: Sonnets.", "— 셰익스피어, 『소네트』"]], [["I am sorry to say that only the third day after the propitious events at Houndsley Fred Vincy had fallen into worse spirits than he had known in his life before.", "유감스럽게도, 하운즐리에서 그 길조의 사건이 있은 지 사흘째 되던 날, 프레드 빈시는 생애 그 어느 때보다 더 침울한 기분에 빠지고 말았다."], ["Not that he had been disappointed as to the possible market for his horse, but that before the bargain could be concluded with Lord Medlicote’s man, this Diamond, in which hope to the amount of eighty pounds had been invested, had without the slightest warning exhibited in the stable a most vicious energy in kicking, had just missed killing the groom, and had ended in laming himself severely by catching his leg in a rope that overhung the stable-board.", "자기 말을 팔 시장이 마땅치 않아 실망한 것은 아니었다. 다만 메들리코트 경의 사람과 흥정이 매듭지어지기 전에, 무려 팔십 파운드의 희망을 걸어 둔 그 다이아몬드라는 말이 아무런 예고도 없이 마구간에서 발길질을 해 대는 지독한 악성을 드러냈고, 하마터면 마부를 죽일 뻔하더니, 마침내 마구간 칸막이 위로 늘어진 밧줄에 다리가 걸려 스스로 심하게 절게 되고 만 것이다."], ["There was no more redress for this than for the discovery of bad temper after marriage—which of course old companions were aware of before the ceremony.", "이것은 결혼 후에야 드러나는 못된 성미만큼이나 어찌해 볼 도리가 없는 일이었다. 물론 그 못된 성미라는 것은 식을 올리기 전부터 오랜 친구들은 이미 알고 있었겠지만 말이다."], ["For some reason or other, Fred had none of his usual elasticity under this stroke of ill-fortune: he was simply aware that he had only fifty pounds, that there was no chance of his getting any more at present, and that the bill for a hundred and sixty would be presented in five days.", "무슨 까닭에선지 프레드는 이번 불운의 일격 앞에서 평소의 탄력성을 조금도 발휘하지 못했다. 그는 그저 자기에게 오십 파운드밖에 없고, 당장 더 마련할 가망이 없으며, 백육십 파운드짜리 어음이 닷새 후면 제시되리라는 것을 알 뿐이었다."], ["Even if he had applied to his father on the plea that Mr. Garth should be saved from loss, Fred felt smartingly that his father would angrily refuse to rescue Mr.", "설령 개스 씨가 손해를 보지 않게 해 달라는 명목으로 아버지에게 부탁한다 해도, 아버지는 그것을 사치와 기만을 부추기는 짓이라 부르며 그 결과로부터 개스 씨를 구해 주는 것을 화를 내며 거절하리라는 것을 프레드는 쓰라리게 느꼈다."], ["Garth from the consequence of what he would call encouraging extravagance and deceit.", "그는 너무도 풀이 죽은 나머지, 곧장 개스 씨에게 가서 슬픈 진실을 털어놓는 것 외에는 다른 계획을 세울 수가 없었다."], ["He was so utterly downcast that he could frame no other project than to go straight to Mr.", "오십 파운드를 들고 가서, 적어도 그 돈만큼은 자기 손에서 안전하게 떼어 놓겠다는 것이었다."], ["Garth and tell him the sad truth, carrying with him the fifty pounds, and getting that sum at least safely out of his own hands.", "아버지는 창고에 가 있어서 아직 그 사고를 모르고 있었다. 알게 되면 그 못된 짐승을 마구간에 들였다고 노발대발할 터였다."], ["His father, being at the warehouse, did not yet know of the accident: when he did, he would storm about the vicious brute being brought into his stable; and before meeting that lesser annoyance Fred wanted to get away with all his courage to face the greater.", "그 작은 골칫거리를 마주하기 전에 프레드는 온 용기를 끌어모아 더 큰일을 마주하러 떠나고 싶었다."], ["He took his father’s nag, for he had made up his mind that when he had told Mr. Garth, he would ride to Stone Court and confess all to Mary.", "그는 아버지의 말을 탔는데, 개스 씨에게 이야기를 마치고 나면 스톤 코트로 말을 달려 메리에게 모든 것을 고백하기로 마음먹었기 때문이다."], ["In fact, it is probable that but for Mary’s existence and Fred’s love for her, his conscience would have been much less active both in previously urging the debt on his thought and impelling him not to spare himself after his usual fashion by deferring an unpleasant task, but to act as directly and simply as he could.", "사실 메리라는 존재와 그녀를 향한 프레드의 사랑이 없었더라면, 그의 양심은 미리 그 빚을 자꾸 떠올리게 하는 데서도, 또 불쾌한 일을 미루는 평소 버릇대로 자신을 봐주지 않고 되도록 곧장 단순하게 행동하도록 그를 떠미는 데서도 훨씬 덜 활발했을 것이다."], ["Even much stronger mortals than Fred Vincy hold half their rectitude in the mind of the being they love best.", "프레드 빈시보다 훨씬 강인한 사람들조차도 자신의 올바름 가운데 절반은 가장 사랑하는 사람의 마음속에 두고 있는 법이다."], ["“The theatre of all my actions is fallen,”", "\"내 모든 행동의 무대가 무너졌다.\""], ["said an antique personage when his chief friend was dead; and they are fortunate who get a theatre where the audience demands their best.", "어느 고대인은 가장 가까운 벗이 죽었을 때 이렇게 말했다. 관객이 최선을 요구하는 무대를 얻은 사람은 행운이다."], ["Certainly it would have made a considerable difference to Fred at that time if Mary Garth had had no decided notions as to what was admirable in character.", "만일 메리 개스가 인격에서 무엇이 훌륭한지에 관해 뚜렷한 생각이 없었더라면, 그 무렵 프레드에게는 분명 적잖은 차이가 생겼을 것이다."]], [["Mr. Garth was not at the office, and Fred rode on to his house, which was a little way outside the town—a homely place with an orchard in front of it, a rambling, old-fashioned, half-timbered building, which before the town had spread had been a farm-house, but was now surrounded with the private gardens of the townsmen.", "개스 씨는 사무소에 없었고, 프레드는 그의 집으로 말을 몰았다."], ["We get the fonder of our houses if they have a physiognomy of their own, as our friends have.", "그 집은 마을에서 조금 벗어난 곳에 있었다."], ["The Garth family, which was rather a large one, for Mary had four brothers and one sister, were very fond of their old house, from which all the best furniture had long been sold.", "앞쪽에 과수원이 딸린 소박한 곳으로, 두서없이 뻗은 구식의 반목조 건물이었는데, 마을이 퍼져 나가기 전에는 농가였으나 지금은 읍내 사람들의 개인 정원에 둘러싸여 있었다."], ["Fred liked it too, knowing it by heart even to the attic which smelt deliciously of apples and quinces, and until to-day he had never come to it without pleasant expectations; but his heart beat uneasily now with the sense that he should probably have to make his confession before Mrs.", "우리는 친구들이 그렇듯 집도 저마다 제 얼굴을 지니고 있으면 더욱 정이 가는 법이다. 메리에게는 남동생이 넷, 여동생이 하나 있어서 식구가 꽤 많은 편이었는데, 개스 가족은 그 낡은 집을 무척 사랑했다."], ["Garth, of whom he was rather more in awe than of her husband.", "좋은 가구는 이미 오래전에 다 팔아 버린 집이었는데도 그러했다."], ["Not that she was inclined to sarcasm and to impulsive sallies, as Mary was. In her present matronly age at least, Mrs.", "프레드도 그 집을 좋아했고, 사과와 모과 냄새가 향긋하게 풍기는 다락방까지 속속들이 알고 있었다."], ["Garth never committed herself by over-hasty speech; having, as she said, borne the yoke in her youth, and learned self-control.", "오늘 전까지 그는 즐거운 기대 없이 그 집을 찾은 적이 없었다."], ["She had that rare sense which discerns what is unalterable, and submits to it without murmuring.", "그러나 지금 그의 심장은 불안하게 뛰었으니, 아마도 개스 부인 앞에서 고백을 해야 할 것이라는 느낌 때문이었다."], ["Adoring her husband’s virtues, she had very early made up her mind to his incapacity of minding his own interests, and had met the consequences cheerfully.", "그는 그녀의 남편보다 그녀를 다소 더 어렵게 여겼다. 그렇다고 그녀가 메리처럼 빈정거림이나 충동적인 말투에 빠지는 사람이라서는 아니었다."], ["She had been magnanimous enough to renounce all pride in teapots or children’s frilling, and had never poured any pathetic confidences into the ears of her feminine neighbors concerning Mr.", "적어도 지금의 부인다운 나이에 이르러서 개스 부인은 결코 성급한 말로 자신을 그르치는 일이 없었다. 그녀의 말마따나 젊은 시절에 멍에를 짊어지며 자제력을 익혔기 때문이다."], ["Garth’s want of prudence and the sums he might have had if he had been like other men.", "그녀에게는 바꿀 수 없는 것을 분별하여 불평 없이 받아들이는 드문 감각이 있었다."], ["Hence these fair neighbors thought her either proud or eccentric, and sometimes spoke of her to their husbands as “your fine Mrs. Garth.”", "남편의 미덕을 흠모하면서도, 그녀는 일찍이 남편이 자기 잇속을 챙기는 데는 무능하다고 마음을 정해 놓고, 그 결과를 쾌활하게 맞아들였다."], ["She was not without her criticism of them in return, being more accurately instructed than most matrons in Middlemarch, and—where is the blameless woman?—apt to be a little severe towards her own sex, which in her opinion was framed to be entirely subordinate.", "그녀는 찻주전자나 아이들 옷의 주름 장식에 대한 자부심을 모두 단념할 만큼 도량이 넓었고, 개스 씨가 분별이 없다거나, 다른 남자들처럼 했더라면 벌었을 돈이 얼마라거나 하는 따위의 애처로운 하소연을 이웃 여인네들의 귀에 쏟아 놓은 적도 결코 없었다."], ["On the other hand, she was disproportionately indulgent towards the failings of men, and was often heard to say that these were natural. Also, it must be admitted that Mrs.", "그래서 이 점잖은 이웃들은 그녀를 거만하다거나 별나다고 여겼고, 때로는 자기 남편들에게 그녀를 두고 \"당신네 잘난 개스 부인\"이라고 말하곤 했다."], ["Garth was a trifle too emphatic in her resistance to what she held to be follies: the passage from governess into housewife had wrought itself a little too strongly into her consciousness, and she rarely forgot that while her grammar and accent were above the town standard, she wore a plain cap, cooked the family dinner, and darned all the stockings.", "그녀 또한 그들에 대해 나름의 비판을 품지 않은 것은 아니었으니, 미들마치의 여느 부인들보다 더 정확히 배운 사람이었고, 또한—흠 없는 여자가 어디 있겠는가?—제 성(性)에 대해 조금은 엄격해지기 쉬웠다. 그녀가 보기에 여성이란 전적으로 종속되도록 빚어진 존재였던 것이다."], ["She had sometimes taken pupils in a peripatetic fashion, making them follow her about in the kitchen with their book or slate.", "반면에 남자들의 결점에 대해서는 균형이 맞지 않을 만큼 너그러웠고, 그런 것은 자연스러운 일이라고 말하는 것을 사람들은 자주 들었다. 또한 인정해야 할 것은, 개스 부인이 어리석은 짓이라 여기는 것에 맞설 때면 다소 지나치게 단호했다는 점이다."], ["She thought it good for them to see that she could make an excellent lather while she corrected their blunders “without looking,”—that a woman with her sleeves tucked up above her elbows might know all about the Subjunctive Mood or the Torrid Zone—that, in short, she might possess “education”", "가정교사에서 주부로 넘어온 과정이 그녀의 의식 속에 조금은 너무 강하게 새겨졌던 터라, 그녀는 자기 문법과 발음이 읍내 수준을 넘어서면서도 수수한 모자를 쓰고 식구들 저녁을 짓고 양말을 모두 깁는다는 사실을 좀처럼 잊지 않았다. 그녀는 이따금 떠돌이식으로 학생들을 가르쳐서, 책이나 석판을 든 아이들을 부엌까지 졸졸 따라다니게 했다."], ["and other good things ending in “tion,” and worthy to be pronounced emphatically, without being a useless doll.", "그녀는 아이들이, 자기가 \"쳐다보지 않고도\""], ["When she made remarks to this edifying effect, she had a firm little frown on her brow, which yet did not hinder her face from looking benevolent, and her words which came forth like a procession were uttered in a fervid agreeable contralto.", "그들의 실수를 바로잡으면서 훌륭하게 비누 거품을 낼 수 있다는 것을, 다시 말해 소매를 팔꿈치 위로 걷어붙인 여자도 가정법이나 열대 지방에 관해 모든 것을 알 수 있다는 것을, 요컨대 쓸모없는 인형이 되지 않고서도 \"교육\"이라든가 힘주어 발음할 만한 다른 좋은 것들을 지닐 수 있다는 것을 보는 것이 아이들에게 유익하다고 생각했다. 이런 교훈적인 취지의 말을 할 때면 그녀의 이마에는 야무진 작은 주름이 잡혔지만, 그렇다고 해서 그녀의 얼굴이 인자해 보이는 것을 막지는 못했고, 행렬처럼 줄지어 나오는 그녀의 말들은 열정적이고 듣기 좋은 콘트랄토 음성으로 발화되었다."], ["Certainly, the exemplary Mrs. Garth had her droll aspects, but her character sustained her oddities, as a very fine wine sustains a flavor of skin.", "분명 이 모범적인 개스 부인에게도 우스꽝스러운 구석이 있었지만, 그녀의 인격은 그 별난 점들을 떠받쳐 주었으니, 마치 아주 좋은 포도주가 껍질의 풍미마저 살려 내는 것과 같았다."]], [["Towards Fred Vincy she had a motherly feeling, and had always been disposed to excuse his errors, though she would probably not have excused Mary for engaging herself to him, her daughter being included in that more rigorous judgment which she applied to her own sex.", "프레드 빈시에 대해 그녀는 어머니 같은 감정을 품고 있어서, 늘 그의 잘못을 너그러이 봐주는 편이었다. 다만 메리가 그와 약혼하는 일만큼은 아마 용서하지 않았을 것이다."], ["But this very fact of her exceptional indulgence towards him made it the harder to Fred that he must now inevitably sink in her opinion.", "자기 딸은 자기 성에 적용하는 더 엄격한 판단 속에 포함되어 있었으니까. 그러나 바로 이렇게 그를 예외적으로 너그럽게 대해 왔다는 사실 때문에, 이제 그녀의 평가 속에서 자신이 불가피하게 추락하리라는 것이 프레드에게는 더욱 견디기 힘들었다."], ["And the circumstances of his visit turned out to be still more unpleasant than he had expected; for Caleb Garth had gone out early to look at some repairs not far off. Mrs.", "게다가 방문의 상황은 그가 예상했던 것보다 한층 더 불쾌하게 돌아갔으니, 케일럽 개스가 멀지 않은 곳의 수리 작업을 보러 일찍 나가 버렸기 때문이다."], ["Garth at certain hours was always in the kitchen, and this morning she was carrying on several occupations at once there—making her pies at the well-scoured deal table on one side of that airy room, observing Sally’s movements at the oven and dough-tub through an open door, and giving lessons to her youngest boy and girl, who were standing opposite to her at the table with their books and slates before them.", "개스 부인은 정해진 시간이면 늘 부엌에 있었고, 그날 아침 그녀는 그곳에서 여러 일을 한꺼번에 해 나가고 있었다. 그 환한 방 한쪽의 깨끗이 닦은 전나무 식탁에서 파이를 만들고, 열린 문 너머로 화덕과 반죽통 앞에서 움직이는 샐리를 살피며, 동시에 막내아들과 막내딸에게 수업을 하고 있었는데, 두 아이는 책과 석판을 앞에 놓고 식탁 맞은편에 서 있었다."], ["A tub and a clothes-horse at the other end of the kitchen indicated an intermittent wash of small things also going on.", "부엌 다른 쪽 끝의 빨래통과 빨래걸이는 자잘한 것들을 틈틈이 빨고 있다는 것을 말해 주었다."]], [["Mrs. Garth, with her sleeves turned above her elbows, deftly handling her pastry—applying her rolling-pin and giving ornamental pinches, while she expounded with grammatical fervor what were the right views about the concord of verbs and pronouns with “nouns of multitude or signifying many,”", "소매를 팔꿈치 위로 걷어붙이고 능숙하게 반죽을 다루며—밀방망이를 굴리고 장식 무늬를 집어 가며—\"집합 명사, 곧 다수를 뜻하는 명사\"와 동사·대명사의 일치에 관해 무엇이 옳은 견해인지를 문법적 열의를 가지고 설명하는 개스 부인의 모습은 보기에 즐거운 광경이었다."], ["was a sight agreeably amusing. She was of the same curly-haired, square-faced type as Mary, but handsomer, with more delicacy of feature, a pale skin, a solid matronly figure, and a remarkable firmness of glance.", "그녀는 메리와 같은, 곱슬머리에 각진 얼굴형이었으나 더 잘생겼고, 이목구비가 더 섬세하며, 살결이 희고, 부인다운 단단한 체격에 눈빛이 놀랍도록 야무졌다."], ["In her snowy-frilled cap she reminded one of that delightful Frenchwoman whom we have all seen marketing, basket on arm.", "눈처럼 흰 주름 장식 모자를 쓴 그녀의 모습은 팔에 바구니를 걸고 장을 보는, 우리 모두 한 번쯤 본 적 있는 그 사랑스러운 프랑스 여인을 떠올리게 했다."], ["Looking at the mother, you might hope that the daughter would become like her, which is a prospective advantage equal to a dowry—the mother too often standing behind the daughter like a malignant prophecy—“Such as I am, she will shortly be.”", "그 어머니를 보면 딸도 그렇게 되리라는 희망을 품게 되는데, 이는 지참금에 맞먹는 장래의 이점이다. 너무도 자주 어머니는 딸 뒤에 악의에 찬 예언처럼 서 있곤 한다—\"지금의 나처럼, 머지않아 너도 이렇게 될 것이다.\"라고."]], [["“Now let us go through that once more,” said Mrs.", "\"자, 그걸 한 번 더 짚어 보자.\""], ["Garth, pinching an apple-puff which seemed to distract Ben, an energetic young male with a heavy brow, from due attention to the lesson.", "개스 부인이 사과 퍼프 하나를 집으며 말했다. 그 퍼프가 진중한 이마를 가진 혈기 왕성한 사내아이 벤의 주의를 수업에서 흐트러뜨리고 있는 듯 보였다."], ["“‘Not without regard to the import of the word as conveying unity or plurality of idea’—tell me again what that means, Ben.”", "\"'관념의 단일성이나 복수성을 전달하는 그 단어의 의미를 고려하지 않고서는 안 된다'—그게 무슨 뜻인지 다시 말해 보렴, 벤.\""]], [["(Mrs. Garth, like more celebrated educators, had her favorite ancient paths, and in a general wreck of society would have tried to hold her “Lindley Murray” above the waves.)", "(개스 부인은 더 유명한 교육자들이 그러하듯 즐겨 다니는 옛길이 있었고, 사회가 송두리째 난파한다 해도 자신의 \"린들리 머리\"를 물결 위로 떠받치려 애썼을 것이다.)"]], [["“Oh—it means—you must think what you mean,”", "\"어—그건—제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 생각해야 한다는 거예요.\""], ["said Ben, rather peevishly.", "벤이 다소 짜증스레 말했다."], ["“I hate grammar.", "\"전 문법이 싫어요."], ["What’s the use of it?”", "그게 무슨 소용이에요?\""]], [["“To teach you to speak and write correctly, so that you can be understood,”", "\"네 말과 글을 바르게 해서 남들이 알아듣게 하려는 거란다.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth, with severe precision.", "개스 부인이 엄격하고 정확하게 말했다."], ["“Should you like to speak as old Job does?”", "\"넌 늙은 욥처럼 말하고 싶니?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Ben, stoutly; “it’s funnier.", "벤이 당차게 말했다. \"그게 더 재밌는걸요."], ["He says, ‘Yo goo’—that’s just as good as ‘You go.’”", "그 사람은 '너 가'라고 하잖아요—그게 '너 가거라'만큼이나 좋은데요.\""]], [["“But he says, ‘A ship’s in the garden,’ instead of ‘a sheep,’”", "\"하지만 그 사람은 '양'이라고 해야 할 걸 '배가 정원에 있다'고 하잖아.\""], ["said Letty, with an air of superiority.", "레티가 우쭐대는 투로 말했다."], ["“You might think he meant a ship off the sea.”", "\"바다에서 온 배를 말하는 줄 알겠어.\""]], [["“No, you mightn’t, if you weren’t silly,”", "\"아니, 안 그래. 네가 멍청이만 아니면.\""], ["said Ben.", "벤이 말했다."], ["“How could a ship off the sea come there?”", "\"어떻게 바다에서 온 배가 거기 들어와?\""]], [["“These things belong only to pronunciation, which is the least part of grammar,”", "\"이런 것들은 발음에만 관계되는 거란다. 발음은 문법에서 가장 사소한 부분이지.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth.", "개스 부인이 말했다."], ["“That apple-peel is to be eaten by the pigs, Ben; if you eat it, I must give them your piece of pasty.", "\"그 사과 껍질은 돼지가 먹을 거야, 벤. 네가 먹으면 네 파이 조각을 돼지한테 줘야겠다."], ["Job has only to speak about very plain things.", "욥은 아주 평범한 것들에 관해서만 말하면 돼."], ["How do you think you would write or speak about anything more difficult, if you knew no more of grammar than he does?", "그런데 네가 문법을 그 사람만큼밖에 모른다면, 그보다 어려운 것에 관해 어떻게 쓰거나 말할 수 있겠니?"], ["You would use wrong words, and put words in the wrong places, and instead of making people understand you, they would turn away from you as a tiresome person.", "잘못된 단어를 쓰고, 단어를 엉뚱한 자리에 놓아서, 사람들이 너를 이해하기는커녕 지긋지긋한 사람이라며 등을 돌리겠지."], ["What would you do then?”", "그러면 넌 어떡할래?\""]], [["“I shouldn’t care, I should leave off,”", "\"전 상관없어요. 그만두면 되죠.\""], ["said Ben, with a sense that this was an agreeable issue where grammar was concerned.", "벤이, 문법에 관한 한 그것이야말로 반가운 결말이라는 듯이 말했다."]], [["“I see you are getting tired and stupid, Ben,” said Mrs.", "\"네가 지치고 둔해지는 게 보이는구나, 벤.\""], ["Garth, accustomed to these obstructive arguments from her male offspring.", "사내아이들의 이런 방해성 논리에 익숙한 개스 부인이 말했다."], ["Having finished her pies, she moved towards the clothes-horse, and said, “Come here and tell me the story I told you on Wednesday, about Cincinnatus.”", "파이를 다 만든 그녀는 빨래걸이 쪽으로 가며 말했다. \"이리 와서, 수요일에 내가 들려준 신키나투스 이야기를 해 보렴.\""]], [["“I know!", "\"알아요!"], ["he was a farmer,”", "그 사람 농부였어요.\""], ["said Ben.", "벤이 말했다."]], [["“Now, Ben, he was a Roman—let me tell,”", "\"아냐, 벤. 그 사람은 로마인이야—내가 말할래.\""], ["said Letty, using her elbow contentiously.", "레티가 다투듯 팔꿈치를 들이밀며 말했다."]], [["“You silly thing, he was a Roman farmer, and he was ploughing.”", "\"바보 같긴, 그 사람은 로마의 농부였고, 밭을 갈고 있었어.\""]], [["“Yes, but before that—that didn’t come first—people wanted him,”", "\"맞아, 그런데 그 전에—그게 먼저가 아니야—사람들이 그 사람을 원했어.\""], ["said Letty.", "레티가 말했다."]], [["“Well, but you must say what sort of a man he was first,”", "\"아니, 그래도 그 사람이 어떤 사람이었는지부터 말해야지.\""], ["insisted Ben.", "벤이 우겼다."], ["“He was a wise man, like my father, and that made the people want his advice.", "\"그 사람은 우리 아빠처럼 현명한 사람이어서, 사람들이 그 사람한테 조언을 구한 거야."], ["And he was a brave man, and could fight.", "그리고 용감한 사람이라 싸울 줄도 알았어."], ["And so could my father—couldn’t he, mother?”", "우리 아빠도 그렇지—그렇죠, 엄마?\""]], [["“Now, Ben, let me tell the story straight on, as mother told it us,”", "\"자, 벤, 엄마가 우리한테 들려준 대로 내가 이야기를 처음부터 끝까지 할래.\""], ["said Letty, frowning.", "레티가 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“Please, mother, tell Ben not to speak.”", "\"엄마, 제발 벤더러 말하지 말라고 해 주세요.\""]], [["“Letty, I am ashamed of you,”", "\"레티, 엄마는 네가 부끄럽구나.\""], ["said her mother, wringing out the caps from the tub.", "어머니가 통에서 모자들을 비틀어 짜며 말했다."], ["“When your brother began, you ought to have waited to see if he could not tell the story.", "\"오빠가 시작했으면 오빠가 이야기를 할 수 있는지 기다려 봤어야지."], ["How rude you look, pushing and frowning, as if you wanted to conquer with your elbows!", "팔꿈치로 밀치고 인상을 쓰면서, 마치 팔꿈치로 이기려는 것처럼 보이니 얼마나 무례하니!"], ["Cincinnatus, I am sure, would have been sorry to see his daughter behave so.” (Mrs.", "신키나투스도 분명 자기 딸이 그렇게 행동하는 걸 보면 안타까워했을 거야.\""], ["Garth delivered this awful sentence with much majesty of enunciation, and Letty felt that between repressed volubility and general disesteem, that of the Romans inclusive, life was already a painful affair.) “Now, Ben.”", "(개스 부인은 이 무시무시한 문장을 매우 위엄 있게 발음했고, 레티는 억눌린 말솜씨와, 로마인까지 포함한 전반적인 멸시 사이에서 인생이 벌써부터 괴로운 일이라고 느꼈다.) \"자, 벤.\""]], [["“Well—oh—well—why, there was a great deal of fighting, and they were all blockheads, and—I can’t tell it just how you told it—but they wanted a man to be captain and king and everything—”", "\"음—어—저—그러니까, 싸움이 엄청 많이 났고, 다들 멍청이였고, 그리고—엄마가 말한 그대로는 못 하겠지만—사람들이 어떤 사람을 대장이자 왕이자 뭐든 다 시키고 싶어 했는데—\""]], [["“Dictator, now,”", "\"독재관, 이라니까.\""], ["said Letty, with injured looks, and not without a wish to make her mother repent.", "레티가 상처받은 표정으로, 엄마가 후회하게 만들고 싶은 마음이 없지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["“Very well, dictator!”", "\"좋아, 독재관!\""], ["said Ben, contemptuously.", "벤이 경멸하듯 말했다."], ["“But that isn’t a good word: he didn’t tell them to write on slates.”", "\"그런데 그건 좋은 단어가 아니야. 그 사람은 사람들한테 석판에 받아쓰라고 시킨 게 아니거든.\""]], [["“Come, come, Ben, you are not so ignorant as that,”", "\"이런, 이런, 벤, 넌 그렇게까지 무지하진 않잖니.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth, carefully serious.", "개스 부인이 애써 진지하게 말했다."], ["“Hark, there is a knock at the door!", "\"들어 봐, 문에 누가 노크를 하는구나!"], ["Run, Letty, and open it.”", "뛰어가서 열어 드려라, 레티.\""]], [["The knock was Fred’s; and when Letty said that her father was not in yet, but that her mother was in the kitchen, Fred had no alternative.", "노크한 사람은 프레드였다. 레티가 아버지는 아직 안 들어오셨지만 어머니는 부엌에 계신다고 말하자, 프레드에게는 달리 선택의 여지가 없었다."], ["He could not depart from his usual practice of going to see Mrs. Garth in the kitchen if she happened to be at work there.", "그는 개스 부인이 부엌에서 일하고 있을 때면 으레 그리로 찾아가 인사하던 평소의 습관을 저버릴 수 없었다."], ["He put his arm round Letty’s neck silently, and led her into the kitchen without his usual jokes and caresses.", "그는 말없이 레티의 목에 팔을 두르고, 평소의 농담이나 다정한 손짓도 없이 그 애를 부엌으로 데리고 들어갔다."]], [["Mrs. Garth was surprised to see Fred at this hour, but surprise was not a feeling that she was given to express, and she only said, quietly continuing her work—", "개스 부인은 이런 시각에 프레드를 보고 놀랐지만, 놀라움이란 그녀가 곧잘 드러내는 감정은 아니어서, 그저 조용히 일을 계속하며 말할 뿐이었다."]], [["“You, Fred, so early in the day?", "\"프레드, 이렇게 이른 시간에?"], ["You look quite pale.", "안색이 아주 창백하구나."], ["Has anything happened?”", "무슨 일이라도 있었니?\""]], [["“I want to speak to Mr. Garth,”", "\"개스 씨와 이야기를 좀 하고 싶어서요.\""], ["said Fred, not yet ready to say more—“and to you also,”", "프레드가 아직 더 말할 준비가 되지 않은 채 말했다. \"그리고 부인과도요.\""], ["he added, after a little pause, for he had no doubt that Mrs.", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그가 덧붙였다."], ["Garth knew everything about the bill, and he must in the end speak of it before her, if not to her solely.", "개스 부인이 어음에 관해 모든 것을 알고 있으리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없었고, 설령 그녀에게만 따로 말하는 것이 아니더라도 결국에는 그녀 앞에서 그 이야기를 해야 하리라 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["“Caleb will be in again in a few minutes,”", "\"케일럽이 몇 분 안에 돌아올 거야.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth, who imagined some trouble between Fred and his father.", "프레드와 그의 아버지 사이에 무슨 곤란한 일이 생겼다고 짐작한 개스 부인이 말했다."], ["“He is sure not to be long, because he has some work at his desk that must be done this morning.", "\"오래 걸리진 않을 거야. 오늘 아침까지 책상에서 끝내야 할 일이 있거든."], ["Do you mind staying with me, while I finish my matters here?”", "내가 여기 일을 마치는 동안 나랑 같이 있어도 괜찮겠니?\""]], [["“But we needn’t go on about Cincinnatus, need we?”", "\"그런데 우리, 신키나투스 이야기는 계속 안 해도 되죠?\""], ["said Ben, who had taken Fred’s whip out of his hand, and was trying its efficiency on the cat.", "벤이 프레드의 손에서 채찍을 빼앗아 그것의 위력을 고양이한테 시험해 보며 말했다."]], [["“No, go out now.", "\"안 해도 돼. 이제 나가렴."], ["But put that whip down.", "그런데 그 채찍은 내려놓아라."], ["How very mean of you to whip poor old Tortoise!", "불쌍한 늙은 거북이를 채찍질하다니 어쩜 그렇게 짓궂니!"], ["Pray take the whip from him, Fred.”", "프레드, 미안하지만 그 애한테서 채찍을 좀 거둬 주렴.\""]], [["“Come, old boy, give it me,”", "\"자, 꼬마야, 이리 줘.\""], ["said Fred, putting out his hand.", "프레드가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Will you let me ride on your horse to-day?”", "\"오늘 형 말 타게 해 줄 거예요?\""], ["said Ben, rendering up the whip, with an air of not being obliged to do it.", "벤이 채찍을 건네면서도 마지못해서가 아니라는 듯한 태도로 말했다."]], [["“Not to-day—another time.", "\"오늘은 안 돼—다음에."], ["I am not riding my own horse.”", "오늘은 내 말이 아니거든.\""]], [["“Shall you see Mary to-day?”", "\"오늘 메리 누나 봐요?\""]], [["“Yes, I think so,”", "\"응, 그럴 것 같다.\""], ["said Fred, with an unpleasant twinge.", "프레드가 불쾌한 통증을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“Tell her to come home soon, and play at forfeits, and make fun.”", "\"누나한테 얼른 집에 와서 벌금 놀이 하고 재밌게 놀자고 전해 줘요.\""]], [["“Enough, enough, Ben!", "\"그만, 그만하렴, 벤!"], ["run away,”", "어서 가.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth, seeing that Fred was teased.", "프레드가 시달리고 있는 것을 본 개스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Are Letty and Ben your only pupils now, Mrs. Garth?”", "\"이제 부인의 학생은 레티와 벤뿐인가요?\""], ["said Fred, when the children were gone and it was needful to say something that would pass the time. He was not yet sure whether he should wait for Mr.", "아이들이 나가고, 시간을 보낼 만한 무슨 말이라도 해야 할 필요가 생기자 프레드가 말했다."], ["Garth, or use any good opportunity in conversation to confess to Mrs. Garth herself, give her the money and ride away.", "그는 아직 개스 씨를 기다려야 할지, 아니면 대화 중에 좋은 기회를 잡아 개스 부인에게 직접 고백하고 돈을 건넨 뒤 말을 타고 떠나야 할지 마음을 정하지 못하고 있었다."]], [["“One—only one.", "\"한 명—딱 한 명."], ["Fanny Hackbutt comes at half past eleven.", "패니 핵벗이 열한 시 반에 온단다."], ["I am not getting a great income now,”", "요즘은 수입이 변변치 않아.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth, smiling.", "개스 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I am at a low ebb with pupils.", "\"학생이 거의 없는 썰물 때거든."], ["But I have saved my little purse for Alfred’s premium: I have ninety-two pounds.", "그래도 앨프리드의 도제 수업료로 내 작은 지갑을 모아 두었지."], ["He can go to Mr.", "구십이 파운드란다."], ["Hanmer’s now; he is just at the right age.”", "이제 그 애는 한머 씨한테 갈 수 있어. 딱 알맞은 나이거든.\""]], [["This did not lead well towards the news that Mr. Garth was on the brink of losing ninety-two pounds and more.", "이 말은 개스 씨가 구십이 파운드도 넘는 돈을 잃을 처지에 놓였다는 소식으로 이어지기에 좋은 길이 아니었다."], ["Fred was silent.", "프레드는 입을 다물었다."], ["“Young gentlemen who go to college are rather more costly than that,”", "\"대학에 가는 도련님들은 그보다 비용이 좀 더 들지.\""], ["Mrs. Garth innocently continued, pulling out the edging on a cap-border.", "개스 부인이 모자 가장자리의 장식을 잡아당기며 천연덕스럽게 이어 갔다."], ["“And Caleb thinks that Alfred will turn out a distinguished engineer: he wants to give the boy a good chance.", "\"그리고 케일럽은 앨프리드가 뛰어난 기술자가 될 거라고 생각해. 그 애한테 좋은 기회를 주고 싶어 하지."], ["There he is!", "저기 오는구나!"], ["I hear him coming in.", "들어오는 소리가 들려."], ["We will go to him in the parlor, shall we?”", "우리, 거실로 가서 보자, 그럴까?\""]], [["When they entered the parlor Caleb had thrown down his hat and was seated at his desk.", "그들이 거실로 들어갔을 때 케일럽은 모자를 던져 놓고 책상 앞에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["Fred, my boy!”", "프레드, 얘야!\""], ["he said, in a tone of mild surprise, holding his pen still undipped; “you are here betimes.”", "그가 펜에 아직 잉크도 찍지 않은 채 들고서, 부드러운 놀라움이 담긴 어조로 말했다. \"이렇게 일찍 왔구나.\""], ["But missing the usual expression of cheerful greeting in Fred’s face, he immediately added, “Is there anything up at home?—anything the matter?”", "그러나 프레드의 얼굴에서 늘 보이던 명랑한 인사의 표정이 보이지 않자, 그는 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"집에 무슨 일이 있는 게냐?—뭐가 잘못됐니?\""]], [["“Yes, Mr. Garth, I am come to tell something that I am afraid will give you a bad opinion of me.", "\"네, 개스 씨, 아저씨께서 저를 나쁘게 생각하시게 될까 봐 걱정스러운 일을 말씀드리러 왔어요."], ["I am come to tell you and Mrs. Garth that I can’t keep my word.", "아저씨와 부인께, 제가 약속을 지킬 수 없게 됐다는 걸 말씀드리러 왔어요."], ["I can’t find the money to meet the bill after all.", "결국 어음을 막을 돈을 마련할 수가 없게 됐어요."], ["I have been unfortunate; I have only got these fifty pounds towards the hundred and sixty.”", "운이 나빴거든요. 백육십 파운드 중에 이 오십 파운드밖에 마련하지 못했어요.\""]], [["While Fred was speaking, he had taken out the notes and laid them on the desk before Mr.", "프레드는 말하는 동안 어음을 꺼내 개스 씨 앞 책상 위에 올려놓았다."], ["Garth. He had burst forth at once with the plain fact, feeling boyishly miserable and without verbal resources.", "그는 소년처럼 비참한 심정에다 말주변도 없어서, 단번에 그 적나라한 사실을 쏟아 내고 말았다."], ["Mrs. Garth was mutely astonished, and looked at her husband for an explanation.", "개스 부인은 말없이 놀라며 설명을 구하듯 남편을 바라보았다."], ["Caleb blushed, and after a little pause said—", "케일럽은 얼굴을 붉히더니, 잠시 뜸을 들인 후 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I didn’t tell you, Susan: I put my name to a bill for Fred; it was for a hundred and sixty pounds.", "\"아, 당신한테 말 안 했지, 수전. 내가 프레드의 어음에 보증을 섰어."], ["He made sure he could meet it himself.”", "백육십 파운드짜리였어. 이 애는 자기가 직접 막을 수 있다고 확신했고.\""]], [["There was an evident change in Mrs. Garth’s face, but it was like a change below the surface of water which remains smooth.", "개스 부인의 얼굴에 분명한 변화가 일었지만, 그것은 표면은 여전히 잔잔한 채 수면 아래에서 일어나는 변화 같았다."], ["She fixed her eyes on Fred, saying—", "그녀는 프레드에게 시선을 고정하고 말했다."]], [["“I suppose you have asked your father for the rest of the money and he has refused you.”", "\"네 아버지께 나머지 돈을 부탁드렸는데 거절하신 거겠지.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["said Fred, biting his lip, and speaking with more difficulty; “but I know it will be of no use to ask him; and unless it were of use, I should not like to mention Mr.", "프레드가 입술을 깨물며, 더 힘겹게 말했다. \"하지만 부탁드려 봐야 소용없을 거예요."], ["Garth’s name in the matter.”", "소용이 없을 바에는 이 일에 개스 아저씨의 이름을 꺼내고 싶지 않았고요.\""]], [["“It has come at an unfortunate time,”", "\"때가 안 좋게 닥쳤구나.\""], ["said Caleb, in his hesitating way, looking down at the notes and nervously fingering the paper, “Christmas upon us—I’m rather hard up just now.", "케일럽이 머뭇거리는 투로, 어음을 내려다보며 초조하게 종이를 만지작거리며 말했다. \"크리스마스가 코앞인데—요즘 형편이 좀 빠듯하단다."], ["You see, I have to cut out everything like a tailor with short measure.", "알다시피 난 옷감이 모자라는 재단사처럼 모든 걸 잘라 맞춰야 해."], ["What can we do, Susan?", "어쩌면 좋겠소, 수전?"], ["I shall want every farthing we have in the bank.", "은행에 있는 돈을 한 푼도 빠짐없이 써야겠어."], ["It’s a hundred and ten pounds, the deuce take it!”", "백십 파운드인데, 빌어먹을!\""]], [["“I must give you the ninety-two pounds that I have put by for Alfred’s premium,”", "\"앨프리드의 도제 수업료로 모아 둔 구십이 파운드를 당신한테 줘야겠어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth, gravely and decisively, though a nice ear might have discerned a slight tremor in some of the words.", "개스 부인이 진지하고 단호하게 말했지만, 예민한 귀라면 몇몇 말끝에서 미세한 떨림을 알아챘을지도 모른다."], ["“And I have no doubt that Mary has twenty pounds saved from her salary by this time.", "\"그리고 메리도 이맘때면 봉급에서 이십 파운드쯤은 모아 두었을 거예요."], ["She will advance it.”", "그 애가 보태 줄 거고요.\""]], [["Mrs. Garth had not again looked at Fred, and was not in the least calculating what words she should use to cut him the most effectively.", "개스 부인은 다시 프레드를 쳐다보지 않았고, 그에게 가장 효과적으로 상처를 입힐 말이 무엇일까 따위는 조금도 궁리하지 않았다."], ["Like the eccentric woman she was, she was at present absorbed in considering what was to be done, and did not fancy that the end could be better achieved by bitter remarks or explosions.", "별난 여자답게 그녀는 지금 무엇을 해야 할지를 골똘히 생각하는 데 몰두해 있었고, 쓰디쓴 말이나 폭발로 일이 더 잘 마무리될 수 있으리라고는 여기지 않았다."], ["But she had made Fred feel for the first time something like the tooth of remorse.", "그러나 그녀는 프레드에게 난생처음으로 후회의 이빨 같은 것을 느끼게 했다."], ["Curiously enough, his pain in the affair beforehand had consisted almost entirely in the sense that he must seem dishonorable, and sink in the opinion of the Garths: he had not occupied himself with the inconvenience and possible injury that his breach might occasion them, for this exercise of the imagination on other people’s needs is not common with hopeful young gentlemen.", "묘하게도, 그 일에 대해 미리 그가 느꼈던 고통은 거의 전적으로 자신이 명예롭지 못해 보이고 개스 가족의 평가 속에서 추락하리라는 의식에 있었다. 자기의 약속 위반이 그들에게 끼칠 불편이나 있을 법한 손해에는 마음을 쓰지 않았으니, 다른 사람의 곤궁에 이런 상상력을 발휘하는 것은 희망에 찬 젊은 도련님들에게 흔한 일이 아니다."], ["Indeed we are most of us brought up in the notion that the highest motive for not doing a wrong is something irrespective of the beings who would suffer the wrong.", "사실 우리 대부분은 잘못을 저지르지 않는 가장 높은 동기가 그 잘못으로 고통받을 존재와는 무관한 어떤 것이라는 생각 속에 자라난다."], ["But at this moment he suddenly saw himself as a pitiful rascal who was robbing two women of their savings.", "그러나 이 순간 그는 문득 두 여인의 저축을 빼앗고 있는 가련한 악당으로서의 자기 모습을 보았다."]], [["“I shall certainly pay it all, Mrs. Garth—ultimately,”", "\"틀림없이 다 갚을게요, 개스 부인—결국에는요.\""], ["he stammered out.", "그가 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, ultimately,” said Mrs.", "\"그래, 결국에는.\""], ["Garth, who having a special dislike to fine words on ugly occasions, could not now repress an epigram.", "개스 부인이 말했다. 추한 자리에 번지르르한 말이 나오는 것을 유난히 싫어하는 그녀는 이번에는 경구 한마디를 억누를 수 없었다."], ["“But boys cannot well be apprenticed ultimately: they should be apprenticed at fifteen.”", "\"하지만 소년들을 결국에 가서 도제로 보낼 수는 없는 노릇이지. 열다섯에 도제로 들어가야 하니까.\""], ["She had never been so little inclined to make excuses for Fred.", "그녀가 프레드를 봐줄 마음이 이때만큼 적었던 적은 없었다."]], [["“I was the most in the wrong, Susan,”", "\"내 잘못이 제일 컸어, 수전.\""], ["said Caleb.", "케일럽이 말했다."], ["“Fred made sure of finding the money.", "\"프레드는 돈을 마련할 수 있다고 확신했고."], ["But I’d no business to be fingering bills.", "어음에 손을 댄 게 내 일이 아니었는데."], ["I suppose you have looked all round and tried all honest means?”", "사방을 다 알아보고 정직한 수단은 다 써 봤겠지?\""], ["he added, fixing his merciful gray eyes on Fred.", "그가 자비로운 잿빛 눈을 프레드에게 고정하며 덧붙였다."], ["Caleb was too delicate to specify Mr. Featherstone.", "케일럽은 페더스톤 씨를 콕 집어 말하기에는 너무 섬세했다."]], [["“Yes, I have tried everything—I really have.", "\"네, 다 해 봤어요—정말로요."], ["I should have had a hundred and thirty pounds ready but for a misfortune with a horse which I was about to sell.", "막 팔려던 말에 불운이 닥치지만 않았어도 백삼십 파운드는 준비할 수 있었을 거예요."], ["My uncle had given me eighty pounds, and I paid away thirty with my old horse in order to get another which I was going to sell for eighty or more—I meant to go without a horse—but now it has turned out vicious and lamed itself.", "삼촌이 저한테 팔십 파운드를 주셨고, 제 헌 말에 삼십 파운드를 보태서 다른 말을 한 마리 샀거든요. 그걸 팔십 파운드나 그 이상에 팔 작정이었고요—저는 말 없이 지낼 생각이었어요—그런데 그 말이 그만 사나운 놈으로 드러나서 스스로 다리를 절게 됐어요."], ["I wish I and the horses too had been at the devil, before I had brought this on you.", "이 일로 아저씨께 짐을 지우느니, 저도 말들도 차라리 악마한테나 끌려갔으면 좋았을 텐데요."], ["There’s no one else I care so much for: you and Mrs.", "제가 이만큼 아끼는 사람은 달리 없어요."], ["Garth have always been so kind to me.", "아저씨와 부인은 늘 저한테 그렇게 잘해 주셨잖아요."], ["However, it’s no use saying that.", "하지만 그런 말을 해 봐야 소용없죠."], ["You will always think me a rascal now.”", "이제 두 분은 늘 저를 악당으로 여기실 테니까요.\""]], [["Fred turned round and hurried out of the room, conscious that he was getting rather womanish, and feeling confusedly that his being sorry was not of much use to the Garths.", "프레드는 돌아서서 방을 황급히 빠져나갔다. 자기가 다소 계집애처럼 굴고 있다는 것을 의식했고, 자기가 미안해한들 개스 가족에게 별 소용이 없다는 것을 어렴풋이 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["They could see him mount, and quickly pass through the gate.", "그들은 그가 말에 올라 재빨리 문을 빠져나가는 것을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“I am disappointed in Fred Vincy,”", "\"프레드 빈시한테 실망했어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth.", "개스 부인이 말했다."], ["“I would not have believed beforehand that he would have drawn you into his debts.", "\"그 애가 당신을 자기 빚에 끌어들이리라고는 미리 알았어도 믿지 않았을 거예요."], ["I knew he was extravagant, but I did not think that he would be so mean as to hang his risks on his oldest friend, who could the least afford to lose.”", "그 애가 헤픈 건 알았지만, 가장 손해를 감당하기 어려운 가장 오랜 친구한테 자기 위험을 떠넘길 만큼 비열할 줄은 몰랐어요.\""]], [["“I was a fool, Susan.”", "\"내가 바보였어, 수전.\""]], [["“That you were,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said the wife, nodding and smiling.", "아내가 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었다."], ["“But I should not have gone to publish it in the market-place.", "\"하지만 나라면 그걸 장터에 광고하러 다니진 않았을 거예요."], ["Why should you keep such things from me?", "왜 그런 일들을 나한테 숨겨요?"], ["It is just so with your buttons: you let them burst off without telling me, and go out with your wristband hanging.", "당신 단추도 꼭 그래요. 떨어지게 두고도 나한테 말 안 하고, 소맷동을 늘어뜨린 채 나가잖아요."], ["If I had only known I might have been ready with some better plan.”", "진작 알았으면 더 나은 방도를 마련해 두었을 텐데.\""]], [["“You are sadly cut up, I know, Susan,”", "\"당신이 무척 속상해하는 거 알아, 수전.\""], ["said Caleb, looking feelingly at her.", "케일럽이 정겹게 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I can’t abide your losing the money you’ve scraped together for Alfred.”", "\"앨프리드를 위해 긁어모은 돈을 당신이 잃는 건 도저히 못 견디겠어.\""]], [["“It is very well that I had scraped it together; and it is you who will have to suffer, for you must teach the boy yourself.", "\"그걸 긁어모아 둔 게 천만다행이죠. 그리고 고생할 사람은 당신이에요."], ["You must give up your bad habits.", "그 애를 당신이 직접 가르쳐야 할 테니까요."], ["Some men take to drinking, and you have taken to working without pay.", "당신은 그 나쁜 버릇을 고쳐야 해요. 어떤 남자들은 술에 빠지는데, 당신은 보수 없이 일하는 데 빠졌어요."], ["You must indulge yourself a little less in that.", "그것 좀 덜 즐겨야 해요."], ["And you must ride over to Mary, and ask the child what money she has.”", "그리고 메리한테 말을 타고 가서, 그 애가 가진 돈이 얼마인지 물어봐야 하고요.\""]], [["Caleb had pushed his chair back, and was leaning forward, shaking his head slowly, and fitting his finger-tips together with much nicety.", "케일럽은 의자를 뒤로 밀고 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채, 천천히 고개를 가로저으며 손끝을 아주 정성스럽게 맞붙이고 있었다."]], [["“Poor Mary!”", "\"가엾은 메리!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Susan,”", "\"수전.\""], ["he went on in a lowered tone, “I’m afraid she may be fond of Fred.”", "그가 목소리를 낮추며 이어 갔다. \"그 애가 프레드를 좋아하는 게 아닌가 걱정이오.\""]], [["“Oh no!", "\"아, 아니에요!"], ["She always laughs at him; and he is not likely to think of her in any other than a brotherly way.”", "그 애는 늘 그 애를 비웃잖아요. 그리고 그 애도 메리를 오누이 사이가 아닌 다른 식으로 생각할 리 없고요.\""]], [["Caleb made no rejoinder, but presently lowered his spectacles, drew up his chair to the desk, and said, “Deuce take the bill—I wish it was at Hanover!", "케일럽은 대꾸하지 않았으나, 이내 안경을 내려 쓰고 책상 쪽으로 의자를 당기며 말했다. \"빌어먹을 어음—하노버에나 가 있었으면!"], ["These things are a sad interruption to business!”", "이런 일들은 업무를 끔찍하게 방해한다니까!\""]], [["The first part of this speech comprised his whole store of maledictory expression, and was uttered with a slight snarl easy to imagine.", "이 말의 앞부분은 그가 지닌 저주의 표현을 통틀어 망라한 것으로, 능히 상상할 만한 가벼운 으르렁거림과 함께 발화되었다."], ["But it would be difficult to convey to those who never heard him utter the word “business,”", "그러나 그가 \"업무\"라는 말을 발음하는 것을 들어 본 적 없는 사람에게는, 그가 그 말을 감싸는 그 독특한 어조, 곧 뜨거운 숭배와 종교적 경의의 어조를 전하기란 어려운 일일 것이다."], ["the peculiar tone of fervid veneration, of religious regard, in which he wrapped it, as a consecrated symbol is wrapped in its gold-fringed linen.", "마치 신성한 상징물을 금실로 가장자리를 두른 아마포로 감싸듯 그는 그 말을 감쌌다."]], [["Caleb Garth often shook his head in meditation on the value, the indispensable might of that myriad-headed, myriad-handed labor by which the social body is fed, clothed, and housed.", "케일럽 개스는 사회라는 몸을 먹이고 입히고 거처를 마련해 주는, 그 머리가 수만이요 손이 수만인 노동의 가치와 없어서는 안 될 위력을 곰곰이 생각하며 종종 고개를 가로젓곤 했다."], ["It had laid hold of his imagination in boyhood.", "그것은 소년 시절부터 그의 상상력을 사로잡았다."], ["The echoes of the great hammer where roof or keel were a-making, the signal-shouts of the workmen, the roar of the furnace, the thunder and plash of the engine, were a sublime music to him; the felling and lading of timber, and the huge trunk vibrating star-like in the distance along the highway, the crane at work on the wharf, the piled-up produce in warehouses, the precision and variety of muscular effort wherever exact work had to be turned out,—all these sights of his youth had acted on him as poetry without the aid of the poets, had made a philosophy for him without the aid of philosophers, a religion without the aid of theology.", "지붕이나 용골을 만드는 곳에서 울리는 큰 망치 소리의 메아리, 일꾼들이 신호로 외치는 함성, 용광로의 굉음, 기관의 우레 같은 소리와 철썩이는 물소리는 그에게 숭고한 음악이었다. 목재를 베어 싣는 일, 큰길을 따라 멀리서 별처럼 흔들리며 가는 거대한 통나무, 부두에서 일하는 기중기, 창고에 쌓인 산물들, 정확한 작업이 산출되어야 하는 곳이라면 어디서나 발휘되는 근육의 정밀하고 다채로운 노력—이 모든 어린 시절의 광경은 시인들의 도움 없이도 그에게 시로서 작용했고, 철학자들의 도움 없이도 그를 위한 철학을 빚어냈으며, 신학의 도움 없이도 하나의 종교가 되어 주었다."], ["His early ambition had been to have as effective a share as possible in this sublime labor, which was peculiarly dignified by him with the name of “business;”", "그의 어릴 적 야심은 이 숭고한 노동에 되도록 효과적으로 한몫을 차지하는 것이었으니, 그는 그 노동을 유난히 \"업무\"라는 이름으로 품위 있게 불렀다."], ["and though he had only been a short time under a surveyor, and had been chiefly his own teacher, he knew more of land, building, and mining than most of the special men in the county.", "비록 측량 기사 밑에서 짧은 기간 일했을 뿐이고 대개 독학으로 익혔지만, 그는 토지와 건축과 광업에 관해서라면 그 지방의 어지간한 전문가들보다 더 많이 알고 있었다."]], [["His classification of human employments was rather crude, and, like the categories of more celebrated men, would not be acceptable in these advanced times.", "인간의 직업에 대한 그의 분류는 다소 거칠어서, 더 유명한 사람들의 범주가 그러하듯, 이 진보된 시대에는 받아들여지지 않을 것이었다."], ["He divided them into “business, politics, preaching, learning, and amusement.”", "그는 직업을 \"업무, 정치, 설교, 학문, 그리고 오락\"으로 나누었다."], ["He had nothing to say against the last four; but he regarded them as a reverential pagan regarded other gods than his own.", "뒤의 네 가지에 대해서는 그가 할 말이 없었다. 다만 그것들을, 경건한 이교도가 자기 신이 아닌 다른 신들을 대하듯 바라보았다."], ["In the same way, he thought very well of all ranks, but he would not himself have liked to be of any rank in which he had not such close contact with “business” as to get often honorably decorated with marks of dust and mortar, the damp of the engine, or the sweet soil of the woods and fields.", "같은 식으로, 그는 모든 신분을 매우 좋게 여겼지만, 자기 자신은 \"업무\"와 긴밀히 맞닿아 종종 먼지와 회반죽 자국이나 기관의 습기, 혹은 숲과 들의 향긋한 흙으로 명예롭게 치장되지 못할 신분에는 들고 싶어 하지 않았을 것이다."], ["Though he had never regarded himself as other than an orthodox Christian, and would argue on prevenient grace if the subject were proposed to him, I think his virtual divinities were good practical schemes, accurate work, and the faithful completion of undertakings: his prince of darkness was a slack workman.", "그는 자신을 정통 기독교인이 아닌 다른 무엇으로 여긴 적이 없었고, 누가 그 주제를 꺼내면 선행 은총에 관해 논하기도 했겠지만, 내 생각에 그가 실질적으로 섬긴 신들은 훌륭한 실용적 계획, 정확한 작업, 그리고 맡은 일의 충실한 완수였다. 그의 암흑의 군주는 게으른 일꾼이었다."], ["But there was no spirit of denial in Caleb, and the world seemed so wondrous to him that he was ready to accept any number of systems, like any number of firmaments, if they did not obviously interfere with the best land-drainage, solid building, correct measuring, and judicious boring (for coal).", "그러나 케일럽에게는 부정의 정신이 없었고, 세상이 그에게는 너무도 경이로워 보였으므로, 그것들이 최선의 배수 작업이나 견고한 건축, 정확한 측량, 그리고 (석탄을 위한) 분별 있는 시추를 명백히 방해하지만 않는다면, 그는 얼마든지 많은 창공처럼 얼마든지 많은 체계라도 기꺼이 받아들일 사람이었다. 사실 그는 강한 실무적 지성을 갖춘 경건한 영혼의 소유자였다."], ["In fact, he had a reverential soul with a strong practical intelligence.", "그러나 그는 재정만큼은 다룰 줄 몰랐다. 가치는 잘 알았지만, 손익이라는 형태의 금전적 결과를 그려 내는 예리한 상상력이 그에게는 없었다."], ["But he could not manage finance: he knew values well, but he had no keenness of imagination for monetary results in the shape of profit and loss: and having ascertained this to his cost, he determined to give up all forms of his beloved “business” which required that talent.", "그리고 이 점을 비싼 대가를 치르며 깨달은 뒤로, 그는 그런 재능을 요하는 자신이 사랑하는 \"업무\"의 모든 형태를 단념하기로 마음먹었다."], ["He gave himself up entirely to the many kinds of work which he could do without handling capital, and was one of those precious men within his own district whom everybody would choose to work for them, because he did his work well, charged very little, and often declined to charge at all.", "그는 자본을 다루지 않고도 할 수 있는 온갖 종류의 일에 온전히 자신을 바쳤고, 자기 구역 안에서는 누구나 자기 일을 맡기고 싶어 하는 그 귀한 사람들 중 하나였다. 일을 잘하고, 비용을 아주 적게 청구하며, 때로는 아예 청구하기를 마다했기 때문이다."], ["It is no wonder, then, that the Garths were poor, and “lived in a small way.”", "그러니 개스 가족이 가난하고 \"옹색하게 살았던\" 것도 놀랄 일은 아니다."], ["However, they did not mind it.", "하지만 그들은 그것을 개의치 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXV.", "# 제23장"]], [["“Love seeketh not itself to please, Nor for itself hath any care But for another gives its ease And builds a heaven in hell’s despair.", "사랑은 자기를 기쁘게 하려 하지 않고, 자기를 위해 마음 쓰지도 않으며, 오직 남을 위해 제 안식을 내주고, 지옥의 절망 속에 천국을 세운다. ."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [".", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], ["Love seeketh only self to please, To bind another to its delight, Joys in another’s loss of ease, And builds a hell in heaven’s despite.”", ". 사랑은 오직 자기만을 기쁘게 하려 하고, 남을 제 즐거움에 옭아매며, 남이 안식을 잃는 것을 기뻐하고, 천국을 거슬러 지옥을 세운다."], ["—W.", "─W."], ["BLAKE: Songs of Experience.", "블레이크, 『경험의 노래』"]], [["Fred Vincy wanted to arrive at Stone Court when Mary could not expect him, and when his uncle was not downstairs: in that case she might be sitting alone in the wainscoted parlor.", "프레드 빈시는 메리가 그를 기다리지 않을 때, 그리고 삼촌이 아래층에 없을 때 스톤 코트에 닿고 싶었다. 그래야 메리가 징두리널을 댄 거실에 홀로 앉아 있을 테니까."], ["He left his horse in the yard to avoid making a noise on the gravel in front, and entered the parlor without other notice than the noise of the door-handle.", "그는 앞마당의 자갈 위에서 소리를 내지 않으려고 말을 뜰에 매어 두고, 문손잡이 소리 말고는 아무 기별 없이 거실로 들어섰다."], ["Mary was in her usual corner, laughing over Mrs. Piozzi’s recollections of Johnson, and looked up with the fun still in her face.", "메리는 늘 앉던 구석에서 존슨에 관한 피오치 부인의 회상록을 읽으며 웃고 있다가, 여전히 얼굴에 즐거움을 띤 채 고개를 들었다."], ["It gradually faded as she saw Fred approach her without speaking, and stand before her with his elbow on the mantel-piece, looking ill.", "그 즐거움은 프레드가 말없이 다가와 벽난로 선반에 팔꿈치를 괴고 아파 보이는 얼굴로 자기 앞에 서는 것을 보면서 차츰 사라졌다."], ["She too was silent, only raising her eyes to him inquiringly.", "그녀 역시 아무 말 없이 묻는 듯한 눈길로 그를 올려다보기만 했다."]], [["“Mary,”", "\"메리.\""], ["he began, “I am a good-for-nothing blackguard.”", "그가 입을 열었다. \"난 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 불한당이야.\""]], [["“I should think one of those epithets would do at a time,”", "\"그런 호칭은 한 번에 하나씩이면 충분할 것 같은데.\""], ["said Mary, trying to smile, but feeling alarmed.", "메리는 미소를 지으려 애썼지만 불안한 마음이 들었다."]], [["“I know you will never think well of me any more.", "\"넌 이제 다시는 날 좋게 생각하지 않겠지."], ["You will think me a liar.", "날 거짓말쟁이라고 여기겠지."], ["You will think me dishonest.", "부정직한 인간이라고 생각할 거야."], ["You will think I didn’t care for you, or your father and mother.", "내가 너나 네 아버지 어머니를 조금도 아끼지 않았다고 여기겠지."], ["You always do make the worst of me, I know.”", "넌 늘 날 최악으로 생각하니까, 알아.\""]], [["“I cannot deny that I shall think all that of you, Fred, if you give me good reasons.", "\"그럴 만한 이유를 댄다면 너에 대해 그 모든 걸 생각하게 되겠지, 부정할 수 없어, 프레드."], ["But please to tell me at once what you have done.", "하지만 네가 무슨 일을 저질렀는지 어서 말해 줘."], ["I would rather know the painful truth than imagine it.”", "괴로운 진실을 상상하기보다는 차라리 아는 편이 나아.\""]], [["“I owed money—a hundred and sixty pounds.", "\"빚을 졌어. 백육십 파운드를."], ["I asked your father to put his name to a bill.", "네 아버지께 어음에 이름을 적어 달라고 부탁드렸어."], ["I thought it would not signify to him.", "아버님께는 별일 아닐 거라고 생각했지."], ["I made sure of paying the money myself, and I have tried as hard as I could.", "내가 직접 갚을 거라고 확신했고, 정말 할 수 있는 한 애썼어."], ["And now, I have been so unlucky—a horse has turned out badly—I can only pay fifty pounds.", "그런데 이제 와서 운이 너무 나빠서, 말 한 마리가 잘못돼서, 겨우 오십 파운드밖에 갚지 못해."], ["And I can’t ask my father for the money: he would not give me a farthing.", "우리 아버지께는 돈을 청할 수도 없어. 한 푼도 주지 않으실 테니까."], ["And my uncle gave me a hundred a little while ago.", "삼촌은 얼마 전에 백 파운드를 주셨고."], ["So what can I do?", "그러니 내가 어쩌겠어?"], ["And now your father has no ready money to spare, and your mother will have to pay away her ninety-two pounds that she has saved, and she says your savings must go too.", "그런데 이제 네 아버지께는 융통할 현금이 없고, 네 어머니는 모아 두신 구십이 파운드를 내놓으셔야 하고, 게다가 네 저금까지 보태야 한다고 하셔."], ["You see what a—”", "봐, 내가 얼마나─\""]], [["“Oh, poor mother, poor father!”", "\"아, 가엾은 어머니, 가엾은 아버지!\""], ["said Mary, her eyes filling with tears, and a little sob rising which she tried to repress.", "메리가 말했다. 두 눈에 눈물이 차오르고, 억누르려 애쓰는 작은 흐느낌이 솟았다."], ["She looked straight before her and took no notice of Fred, all the consequences at home becoming present to her.", "그녀는 똑바로 앞을 바라보며 프레드는 거들떠보지도 않았다. 집에서 벌어질 온갖 결과가 눈앞에 떠올랐던 것이다."], ["He too remained silent for some moments, feeling more miserable than ever.", "그도 한동안 말없이 그 어느 때보다 비참한 심정으로 있었다."], ["“I wouldn’t have hurt you for the world, Mary,”", "\"절대로 너에게 상처 주고 싶지 않았어, 메리.\""], ["he said at last.", "마침내 그가 말했다."], ["“You can never forgive me.”", "\"넌 결코 날 용서할 수 없겠지.\""]], [["“What does it matter whether I forgive you?”", "\"내가 널 용서하는 게 무슨 상관이야?\""], ["said Mary, passionately.", "메리가 격하게 말했다."], ["“Would that make it any better for my mother to lose the money she has been earning by lessons for four years, that she might send Alfred to Mr.", "\"그렇다고 어머니가 사 년 동안 알프레드를 한머 씨에게 보내려고 과외 수업으로 벌어 모으신 돈을 잃는 게 조금이라도 나아져?"], ["Hanmer’s? Should you think all that pleasant enough if I forgave you?”", "내가 널 용서하면 그 모든 게 충분히 흐뭇해질 것 같아?\""]], [["“Say what you like, Mary.", "\"무슨 말이든 해, 메리."], ["I deserve it all.”", "다 들어 마땅하니까.\""]], [["“I don’t want to say anything,”", "\"난 아무 말도 하고 싶지 않아.\""], ["said Mary, more quietly, “and my anger is of no use.”", "메리가 한결 차분하게 말했다. \"내가 화내 봤자 소용없으니까.\""], ["She dried her eyes, threw aside her book, rose and fetched her sewing.", "그녀는 눈물을 닦고 책을 옆으로 밀어 두고 일어나 바느질감을 가져왔다."]], [["Fred followed her with his eyes, hoping that they would meet hers, and in that way find access for his imploring penitence.", "프레드는 자기 눈이 그녀의 눈과 마주치기를, 그리하여 애원하는 참회가 들어설 길을 찾기를 바라며 눈으로 그녀를 좇았다. 하지만 헛일이었다!"], ["But no! Mary could easily avoid looking upward.", "메리는 쉽사리 위를 올려다보지 않을 수 있었다."]], [["“I do care about your mother’s money going,”", "\"네 어머니 돈이 나가는 건 나도 정말 마음 쓰여.\""], ["he said, when she was seated again and sewing quickly.", "그녀가 다시 앉아 빠르게 바느질을 시작했을 때 그가 말했다."], ["“I wanted to ask you, Mary—don’t you think that Mr.", "\"물어보고 싶은 게 있어, 메리."], ["Featherstone—if you were to tell him—tell him, I mean, about apprenticing Alfred—would advance the money?”", "페더스톤 씨가, 네가 그분께 말씀드리면, 그러니까 알프레드를 도제로 보내는 일을 말씀드리면, 그 돈을 미리 내주시지 않을까?\""]], [["“My family is not fond of begging, Fred.", "\"우리 식구는 구걸하는 걸 좋아하지 않아, 프레드."], ["We would rather work for our money.", "차라리 일해서 돈을 버는 쪽이야."], ["Besides, you say that Mr. Featherstone has lately given you a hundred pounds.", "게다가 너도 말했잖아, 페더스톤 씨가 최근에 너한테 백 파운드를 주셨다고."], ["He rarely makes presents; he has never made presents to us.", "그분은 좀처럼 선물을 하지 않으셔. 우리한테는 한 번도 선물을 주신 적이 없고."], ["I am sure my father will not ask him for anything; and even if I chose to beg of him, it would be of no use.”", "우리 아버지는 분명 그분께 아무것도 청하지 않으실 거야. 설령 내가 그분께 구걸하려 든대도 소용없을 거고.\""]], [["“I am so miserable, Mary—if you knew how miserable I am, you would be sorry for me.”", "\"난 너무 비참해, 메리. 내가 얼마나 비참한지 네가 안다면 날 가엾게 여길 텐데.\""]], [["“There are other things to be more sorry for than that.", "\"그것보다 더 가여운 일들이 있어."], ["But selfish people always think their own discomfort of more importance than anything else in the world.", "하지만 이기적인 사람은 늘 제 불편함을 세상 그 무엇보다 더 중요하게 여기지."], ["I see enough of that every day.”", "난 그런 걸 매일 충분히 보고 있어.\""]], [["“It is hardly fair to call me selfish.", "\"날 이기적이라고 부르는 건 좀 부당해."], ["If you knew what things other young men do, you would think me a good way off the worst.”", "다른 젊은이들이 무슨 짓을 하는지 네가 안다면, 날 최악과는 한참 거리가 멀다고 생각할걸.\""]], [["“I know that people who spend a great deal of money on themselves without knowing how they shall pay, must be selfish.", "\"갚을 방도도 모르면서 자기한테 큰돈을 쓰는 사람은 이기적일 수밖에 없다는 건 알아."], ["They are always thinking of what they can get for themselves, and not of what other people may lose.”", "그런 사람은 늘 자기가 무엇을 얻을 수 있는지만 생각하지, 다른 사람이 무엇을 잃을지는 생각하지 않으니까.\""]], [["“Any man may be unfortunate, Mary, and find himself unable to pay when he meant it.", "\"누구든 불운할 수 있어, 메리. 갚을 생각이었는데도 갚지 못하게 될 수 있다고."], ["There is not a better man in the world than your father, and yet he got into trouble.”", "세상에 네 아버지보다 더 훌륭한 분은 없지만, 그분도 곤경에 빠지셨잖아.\""]], [["“How dare you make any comparison between my father and you, Fred?”", "\"감히 어떻게 우리 아버지와 너를 비교하는 거야, 프레드?\""], ["said Mary, in a deep tone of indignation.", "메리가 깊은 분노에 찬 목소리로 말했다."], ["“He never got into trouble by thinking of his own idle pleasures, but because he was always thinking of the work he was doing for other people.", "\"아버지는 한가한 쾌락을 생각하다가 곤경에 빠지신 게 아니라, 늘 남을 위해 하시던 일을 생각하셨기 때문에 그렇게 되신 거야."], ["And he has fared hard, and worked hard to make good everybody’s loss.”", "그리고 모두의 손실을 메우려고 고생도 하셨고 열심히 일도 하셨어.\""]], [["“And you think that I shall never try to make good anything, Mary.", "\"그래, 넌 내가 결코 아무것도 메우려고 애쓰지 않을 거라고 생각하는구나, 메리."], ["It is not generous to believe the worst of a man.", "한 사람을 두고 최악을 믿는 건 너그러운 일이 아니야."], ["When you have got any power over him, I think you might try and use it to make him better; but that is what you never do.", "그 사람에게 어떤 영향력을 가지게 됐다면, 그걸 써서 그를 더 나은 사람으로 만들어 보려고 할 수도 있을 텐데, 넌 절대 그러지 않아."], ["However, I’m going,”", "어쨌든 난 갈게.\""], ["Fred ended, languidly.", "프레드가 맥없이 말을 맺었다."], ["“I shall never speak to you about anything again.", "\"다시는 너한테 어떤 일에 대해서도 말하지 않을 거야."], ["I’m very sorry for all the trouble I’ve caused—that’s all.”", "내가 일으킨 온갖 말썽이 정말 미안해. 그게 다야.\""]], [["Mary had dropped her work out of her hand and looked up.", "메리는 손에서 바느질감을 떨어뜨리고 고개를 들었다."], ["There is often something maternal even in a girlish love, and Mary’s hard experience had wrought her nature to an impressibility very different from that hard slight thing which we call girlishness.", "소녀다운 사랑에도 모성적인 무언가가 깃들어 있을 때가 많은데, 메리의 고된 경험은 그녀의 천성을, 우리가 소녀다움이라 부르는 딱딱하고 가벼운 것과는 사뭇 다른 감수성으로 빚어 놓았다."], ["At Fred’s last words she felt an instantaneous pang, something like what a mother feels at the imagined sobs or cries of her naughty truant child, which may lose itself and get harm.", "프레드의 마지막 말에 그녀는 순간적인 아픔을 느꼈으니, 길을 잃고 해를 입을지도 모를 말썽꾸러기 가출한 아이의 상상 속 흐느낌이나 울음소리에 어머니가 느끼는 것과 비슷한 무엇이었다."], ["And when, looking up, her eyes met his dull despairing glance, her pity for him surmounted her anger and all her other anxieties.", "그리고 고개를 들었을 때 그녀의 눈이 그의 흐릿하게 절망한 눈길과 마주치자, 그에 대한 연민이 그녀의 분노와 다른 모든 근심을 넘어섰다."]], [["“Oh, Fred, how ill you look!", "\"아, 프레드, 너 정말 아파 보여!"], ["Sit down a moment.", "잠깐 앉아."], ["Don’t go yet.", "아직 가지 마."], ["Let me tell uncle that you are here.", "네가 왔다고 삼촌께 말씀드릴게."], ["He has been wondering that he has not seen you for a whole week.”", "한 주 내내 널 못 봤다고 의아해하고 계셨거든.\""], ["Mary spoke hurriedly, saying the words that came first without knowing very well what they were, but saying them in a half-soothing half-beseeching tone, and rising as if to go away to Mr. Featherstone.", "메리는 무슨 말인지 잘 알지도 못한 채 먼저 떠오르는 말을 서둘러 내뱉었지만, 절반은 달래는 듯, 절반은 간청하는 듯한 어조로 말하며 페더스톤 씨에게 가려는 듯 일어섰다."], ["Of course Fred felt as if the clouds had parted and a gleam had come: he moved and stood in her way.", "프레드는 당연히 구름이 갈라지고 한 줄기 빛이 비치는 것 같은 기분이 들었다. 그는 움직여 그녀의 길을 막아섰다."]], [["“Say one word, Mary, and I will do anything.", "\"한마디만 해 줘, 메리. 그러면 뭐든 할게."], ["Say you will not think the worst of me—will not give me up altogether.”", "날 최악으로 생각하지는 않겠다고, 날 아주 단념하지는 않겠다고 말해 줘.\""]], [["“As if it were any pleasure to me to think ill of you,”", "\"널 나쁘게 생각하는 게 나한테 무슨 즐거움이라도 되는 것처럼.\""], ["said Mary, in a mournful tone.", "메리가 서글픈 어조로 말했다."], ["“As if it were not very painful to me to see you an idle frivolous creature.", "\"네가 게으르고 경박한 인간으로 지내는 걸 보는 게 나한테 몹시 괴롭지 않기라도 한 것처럼."], ["How can you bear to be so contemptible, when others are working and striving, and there are so many things to be done—how can you bear to be fit for nothing in the world that is useful?", "남들은 일하고 분투하고, 해야 할 일도 그토록 많은데, 어떻게 그렇게 한심하게 지낼 수 있어? 어떻게 세상의 쓸모 있는 일 가운데 아무것에도 어울리지 못하는 사람으로 견딜 수 있어?"], ["And with so much good in your disposition, Fred,—you might be worth a great deal.”", "게다가 넌 천성에 그토록 좋은 면이 많은데, 프레드, 넌 무척 가치 있는 사람이 될 수도 있을 텐데.\""]], [["“I will try to be anything you like, Mary, if you will say that you love me.”", "\"네가 날 사랑한다고 말해 준다면, 메리, 네가 바라는 무엇이든 되려고 애쓸게.\""]], [["“I should be ashamed to say that I loved a man who must always be hanging on others, and reckoning on what they would do for him.", "\"늘 남에게 매달리고, 남이 자기를 위해 무얼 해 줄지나 셈하는 남자를 사랑한다고 말하기는 부끄러울 거야."], ["What will you be when you are forty? Like Mr.", "마흔이 되면 넌 어떤 사람이 되어 있을까?"], ["Bowyer, I suppose—just as idle, living in Mrs.", "보이어 씨처럼 되겠지, 아마."], ["Beck’s front parlor—fat and shabby, hoping somebody will invite you to dinner—spending your morning in learning a comic song—oh no! learning a tune on the flute.”", "꼭 그렇게 게으르게, 벡 부인의 앞쪽 거실에 얹혀살면서, 뚱뚱하고 추레한 모습으로 누군가 저녁 식사에 초대해 주기를 바라며, 오전 내내 우스꽝스러운 노래나 익히면서, 아니, 플루트로 곡조나 익히면서.\""]], [["Mary’s lips had begun to curl with a smile as soon as she had asked that question about Fred’s future (young souls are mobile), and before she ended, her face had its full illumination of fun.", "메리의 입술은 프레드의 미래에 관한 그 질문을 던지기 시작하자마자 미소로 말려 올라갔고(젊은 영혼은 변덕스러우니), 말을 끝맺기도 전에 그녀의 얼굴은 즐거움으로 환히 빛났다."], ["To him it was like the cessation of an ache that Mary could laugh at him, and with a passive sort of smile he tried to reach her hand; but she slipped away quickly towards the door and said, “I shall tell uncle.", "그에게는 메리가 자기를 보며 웃을 수 있다는 것이 마치 아픔이 멎는 것 같았고, 수동적인 미소를 띤 채 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다. 하지만 그녀는 재빨리 문 쪽으로 미끄러져 가며 말했다."], ["You must see him for a moment or two.”", "\"삼촌께 말씀드릴게. 잠깐이라도 꼭 뵙고 가야 해.\""]], [["Fred secretly felt that his future was guaranteed against the fulfilment of Mary’s sarcastic prophecies, apart from that “anything” which he was ready to do if she would define it.", "프레드는 자기가 정의해 주기만 하면 기꺼이 하겠다는 그 \"무엇이든\"과는 별개로, 자신의 미래가 메리의 빈정대는 예언이 실현되는 것에 맞서 보장되어 있다고 속으로 느꼈다."], ["He never dared in Mary’s presence to approach the subject of his expectations from Mr. Featherstone, and she always ignored them, as if everything depended on himself.", "그는 메리 앞에서는 감히 페더스톤 씨에게서 받을 유산에 대한 기대를 입에 올린 적이 없었고, 그녀는 마치 모든 것이 그 자신에게 달려 있다는 듯 늘 그 문제를 모른 체했다."], ["But if ever he actually came into the property, she must recognize the change in his position.", "그러나 만일 그가 실제로 그 재산을 물려받는다면, 그녀도 그의 처지가 달라졌음을 인정해야 할 터였다."], ["All this passed through his mind somewhat languidly, before he went up to see his uncle.", "이 모든 생각이 삼촌을 만나러 올라가기 전에 다소 나른하게 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["He stayed but a little while, excusing himself on the ground that he had a cold; and Mary did not reappear before he left the house.", "그는 감기에 걸렸다는 핑계를 대며 잠깐만 머물렀고, 그가 집을 나서기 전에 메리는 다시 나타나지 않았다."], ["But as he rode home, he began to be more conscious of being ill, than of being melancholy.", "그러나 말을 타고 집으로 가면서 그는 우울하다기보다 몸이 아프다는 것을 더 의식하기 시작했다."]], [["When Caleb Garth arrived at Stone Court soon after dusk, Mary was not surprised, although he seldom had leisure for paying her a visit, and was not at all fond of having to talk with Mr.", "땅거미가 진 직후에 케일럽 가스가 스톤 코트에 도착했을 때, 메리는 놀라지 않았다. 그가 그녀를 찾아올 짬을 내는 일이 드물고, 페더스톤 씨와 이야기를 나눠야 하는 것을 전혀 좋아하지 않는데도 그러했다."], ["Featherstone. The old man, on the other hand, felt himself ill at ease with a brother-in-law whom he could not annoy, who did not mind about being considered poor, had nothing to ask of him, and understood all kinds of farming and mining business better than he did.", "한편 노인은 처남이 영 거북했으니, 그는 약 올릴 수도 없고, 가난하게 여겨지는 것을 개의치 않으며, 청할 것도 없고, 게다가 온갖 농사일과 광산일을 자기보다 더 잘 알았기 때문이다."], ["But Mary had felt sure that her parents would want to see her, and if her father had not come, she would have obtained leave to go home for an hour or two the next day. After discussing prices during tea with Mr.", "그러나 메리는 부모가 자기를 보고 싶어 하리라는 것을 확신하고 있었고, 아버지가 오지 않았다면 이튿날 한두 시간쯤 집에 다녀올 허락을 받았을 터였다."], ["Featherstone, Caleb rose to bid him good-by, and said, “I want to speak to you, Mary.”", "차를 마시며 페더스톤 씨와 시세를 논한 뒤, 케일럽은 일어나 작별 인사를 하고 말했다. \"너한테 할 말이 있다, 메리.\""]], [["She took a candle into another large parlor, where there was no fire, and setting down the feeble light on the dark mahogany table, turned round to her father, and putting her arms round his neck kissed him with childish kisses which he delighted in,—the expression of his large brows softening as the expression of a great beautiful dog softens when it is caressed.", "그녀는 촛불을 들고 불기 없는 또 다른 큰 거실로 들어가, 거뭇한 마호가니 탁자 위에 그 희미한 빛을 내려놓고는, 아버지를 향해 돌아서서 두 팔로 그의 목을 감싸고 어린아이처럼 입을 맞췄다. 그가 그토록 좋아하는 입맞춤이었다."], ["Mary was his favorite child, and whatever Susan might say, and right as she was on all other subjects, Caleb thought it natural that Fred or any one else should think Mary more lovable than other girls.", "그의 넓은 이마의 표정이, 쓰다듬어 줄 때 크고 아름다운 개의 표정이 누그러지듯 부드러워졌다. 메리는 그가 가장 아끼는 자식이었고, 수전이 무어라 말하든, 다른 모든 일에서는 그녀가 옳다 해도, 케일럽은 프레드든 누구든 메리를 다른 여자아이들보다 더 사랑스럽게 여기는 것을 당연하게 생각했다."]], [["“I’ve got something to tell you, my dear,”", "\"너한테 할 얘기가 좀 있단다, 얘야.\""], ["said Caleb in his hesitating way.", "케일럽이 머뭇거리는 투로 말했다."], ["“No very good news; but then it might be worse.”", "\"그리 좋은 소식은 아니지만, 그래도 더 나쁠 수도 있었지.\""]], [["“About money, father?", "\"돈 얘기예요, 아버지?"], ["I think I know what it is.”", "무슨 일인지 알 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Ay?", "\"그래?"], ["how can that be?", "어떻게 알지? 그게 말이다, 내가 또 좀 어리석은 짓을 해서 어음에 이름을 적었는데, 이제 갚을 때가 됐단다."], ["You see, I’ve been a bit of a fool again, and put my name to a bill, and now it comes to paying; and your mother has got to part with her savings, that’s the worst of it, and even they won’t quite make things even.", "그래서 네 어머니가 모아 둔 돈을 내놓아야 하게 됐지, 그게 제일 안된 일이야. 그런데도 그것으로 셈이 딱 맞아떨어지질 않는구나."], ["We wanted a hundred and ten pounds: your mother has ninety-two, and I have none to spare in the bank; and she thinks that you have some savings.”", "백십 파운드가 필요한데, 네 어머니한테 구십이 파운드가 있고, 내 은행에는 여유 돈이 없어. 그래서 네 어머니가 너한테도 모아 둔 게 좀 있을 거라고 하더구나.\""]], [["“Oh yes; I have more than four-and-twenty pounds.", "\"네, 그럼요. 이십사 파운드 넘게 있어요."], ["I thought you would come, father, so I put it in my bag.", "아버지가 오실 것 같아서 가방에 넣어 뒀어요. 보세요!"], ["See! beautiful white notes and gold.”", "아름다운 흰 지폐랑 금화예요.\""]], [["Mary took out the folded money from her reticule and put it into her father’s hand.", "메리는 손가방에서 접어 둔 돈을 꺼내 아버지의 손에 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“Well, but how—we only want eighteen—here, put the rest back, child,—but how did you know about it?” said Caleb, who, in his unconquerable indifference to money, was beginning to be chiefly concerned about the relation the affair might have to Mary’s affections.", "\"음, 하지만 어떻게, 우리한테 필요한 건 십팔 파운드뿐이란다. 자, 나머지는 도로 넣어 두렴, 얘야. 그런데 넌 이걸 어떻게 알았니?\" 케일럽이 물었다. 돈에는 도무지 어찌할 수 없이 무심한 그가, 이제 이 일이 메리의 마음과 어떤 관계가 있을지를 주로 염려하기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["“Fred told me this morning.”", "\"오늘 아침에 프레드가 말해 줬어요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Did he come on purpose?”", "일부러 온 거니?\""]], [["“Yes, I think so.", "\"네, 그런 것 같아요."], ["He was a good deal distressed.”", "무척 괴로워하더군요.\""]], [["“I’m afraid Fred is not to be trusted, Mary,”", "\"프레드는 믿을 만한 사람이 못 되는 것 같아 걱정이구나, 메리.\""], ["said the father, with hesitating tenderness.", "아버지가 머뭇거리는 다정함으로 말했다."], ["“He means better than he acts, perhaps.", "\"행동보다 마음은 더 나은 사람일지도 모르지."], ["But I should think it a pity for any body’s happiness to be wrapped up in him, and so would your mother.”", "하지만 누군가의 행복이 그 애한테 매여 있는 건 안타까운 일이라고 생각한다. 네 어머니도 그렇게 생각할 거고.\""]], [["“And so should I, father,”", "\"저도 그렇게 생각해요, 아버지.\""], ["said Mary, not looking up, but putting the back of her father’s hand against her cheek.", "메리가 고개를 들지 않은 채 아버지의 손등을 자기 뺨에 갖다 대며 말했다."]], [["“I don’t want to pry, my dear.", "\"꼬치꼬치 캐묻고 싶지는 않다, 얘야."], ["But I was afraid there might be something between you and Fred, and I wanted to caution you.", "다만 너와 프레드 사이에 무슨 일이 있을까 봐 염려스러워서 너한테 일러 두고 싶었단다. 그게 말이다, 메리.\""], ["You see, Mary”—here Caleb’s voice became more tender; he had been pushing his hat about on the table and looking at it, but finally he turned his eyes on his daughter—“a woman, let her be as good as she may, has got to put up with the life her husband makes for her.", "여기서 케일럽의 목소리가 한결 다정해졌다. 그는 탁자 위에서 모자를 이리저리 밀며 그것을 바라보고 있다가, 마침내 눈을 들어 딸을 바라보았다."], ["Your mother has had to put up with a good deal because of me.”", "\"여자란, 아무리 착하다 해도, 남편이 만들어 주는 삶을 견디며 살아야 한단다. 네 어머니도 나 때문에 많은 걸 견뎌야 했지.\""]], [["Mary turned the back of her father’s hand to her lips and smiled at him.", "메리는 아버지의 손등을 입술에 가져다 대고 그에게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Well, well, nobody’s perfect, but”—here Mr.", "\"그래, 그래, 완벽한 사람은 없지, 하지만.\" 여기서 가스 씨는 말로 다 못 한 것을 거들려는 듯 고개를 저었다."], ["Garth shook his head to help out the inadequacy of words—“what I am thinking of is—what it must be for a wife when she’s never sure of her husband, when he hasn’t got a principle in him to make him more afraid of doing the wrong thing by others than of getting his own toes pinched.", "\"내가 생각하는 건 이런 거란다. 아내가 남편을 한 번도 믿을 수 없을 때, 남편 마음속에 남에게 잘못하는 것을 제 발가락 짓밟히는 것보다 더 두려워하게 만들 원칙이라곤 없을 때, 그게 아내한테 어떤 일일까."], ["That’s the long and the short of it, Mary.", "그게 결국 핵심이란다, 메리."], ["Young folks may get fond of each other before they know what life is, and they may think it all holiday if they can only get together; but it soon turns into working day, my dear.", "젊은 사람들은 삶이 뭔지 알기도 전에 서로 좋아하게 될 수 있고, 그저 함께만 있을 수 있으면 다 휴일 같을 거라고 여길지도 모르지. 하지만 그건 머잖아 일하는 날로 변하는 법이야, 얘야."], ["However, you have more sense than most, and you haven’t been kept in cotton-wool: there may be no occasion for me to say this, but a father trembles for his daughter, and you are all by yourself here.”", "그래도 넌 누구보다 분별이 있고, 솜에 싸여 자라지도 않았으니, 내가 이런 말을 할 필요가 없을지도 모르겠다. 하지만 아버지란 딸을 위해 떨기 마련이고, 넌 여기 혼자 있으니 말이다.\""]], [["“Don’t fear for me, father,”", "\"제 걱정은 마세요, 아버지.\""], ["said Mary, gravely meeting her father’s eyes; “Fred has always been very good to me; he is kind-hearted and affectionate, and not false, I think, with all his self-indulgence.", "메리가 진지하게 아버지의 눈을 마주 보며 말했다. \"프레드는 늘 저한테 무척 잘해 줬어요."], ["But I will never engage myself to one who has no manly independence, and who goes on loitering away his time on the chance that others will provide for him.", "마음씨가 곱고 다정하고, 그 모든 방종에도 불구하고 거짓된 사람은 아니라고 생각해요. 하지만 저는 남자다운 자립심이라곤 없고, 남이 자기를 거둬 주리라는 요행만 바라며 시간을 허비하는 사람과는 절대로 약혼하지 않을 거예요."], ["You and my mother have taught me too much pride for that.”", "아버지와 어머니가 그러기에는 제게 너무 큰 자긍심을 가르쳐 주셨거든요.\""]], [["“That’s right—that’s right.", "\"그래, 그렇지, 그래."], ["Then I am easy,” said Mr.", "그럼 난 마음이 놓이는구나.\""], ["Garth, taking up his hat.", "가스 씨가 모자를 집어 들며 말했다."], ["“But it’s hard to run away with your earnings, eh child.”", "\"하지만 네가 번 돈을 가지고 도망쳐야 하다니 마음이 아프구나, 얘야.\""]], [["“Father!”", "\"아버지!\""], ["said Mary, in her deepest tone of remonstrance.", "메리가 더없이 깊은 만류의 어조로 말했다."], ["“Take pocketfuls of love besides to them all at home,”", "\"집에 있는 모두에게 호주머니 가득 사랑도 함께 전해 주세요.\""], ["was her last word before he closed the outer door on himself.", "그가 바깥문을 닫고 나서기 전, 그녀의 마지막 말이었다."]], [["“I suppose your father wanted your earnings,”", "\"네 아버지가 네 돈을 원하셨던 모양이구나.\""], ["said old Mr. Featherstone, with his usual power of unpleasant surmise, when Mary returned to him.", "메리가 돌아오자 노인 페더스톤 씨가 늘 그렇듯 못마땅한 억측을 부리는 솜씨로 말했다."], ["“He makes but a tight fit, I reckon.", "\"살림이 빠듯한 게지, 내 짐작에는."], ["You’re of age now; you ought to be saving for yourself.”", "넌 이제 성년이니, 네 자신을 위해 모아 둬야 할 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["“I consider my father and mother the best part of myself, sir,”", "\"저는 제 아버지와 어머니를 제 자신의 가장 좋은 부분으로 여깁니다, 어르신.\""], ["said Mary, coldly.", "메리가 차갑게 말했다."]], [["Mr. Featherstone grunted: he could not deny that an ordinary sort of girl like her might be expected to be useful, so he thought of another rejoinder, disagreeable enough to be always apropos.", "페더스톤 씨는 끙 하고 앓는 소리를 냈다."], ["“If Fred Vincy comes to-morrow, now, don’t you keep him chattering: let him come up to me.”", "그녀 같은 평범한 부류의 여자아이가 쓸모 있기를 기대받는 것이야 부정할 수 없었으므로, 그는 언제 꺼내도 어울릴 만큼 충분히 고약한 다른 대꾸를 생각해 냈다. \"프레드 빈시가 내일 오거든, 그 애를 붙들고 수다나 떨게 하지 말고, 나한테로 올려 보내거라.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XXVI.", "# CHAPTER XXVI."]], [["He beats me and I rail at him: O worthy satisfaction!", "그는 나를 때리고 나는 그에게 욕설을 퍼붓는다. 오, 가치 있는 만족이여!"], ["would it were otherwise—that I could beat him while he railed at me.—Troilus and Cressida.", "차라리 반대였다면—그가 내게 욕하는 동안 내가 그를 때릴 수 있다면.—『트로일러스와 크레시다』"]], [["But Fred did not go to Stone Court the next day, for reasons that were quite peremptory. From those visits to unsanitary Houndsley streets in search of Diamond, he had brought back not only a bad bargain in horse-flesh, but the further misfortune of some ailment which for a day or two had deemed mere depression and headache, but which got so much worse when he returned from his visit to Stone Court that, going into the dining-room, he threw himself on the sofa, and in answer to his mother’s anxious question, said, “I feel very ill: I think you must send for Wrench.”", "그러나 프레드는 다음 날 스톤 코트에 가지 않았으니, 그럴 만한 분명한 이유가 있었다. 다이아몬드를 찾아 위생 상태가 나쁜 하운즐리의 거리를 돌아다닌 그 방문에서 그는 마필 거래의 손해뿐만 아니라, 하루 이틀 동안은 단순한 우울과 두통이라 여겼던 어떤 병까지 함께 얻어 돌아왔다. 그런데 스톤 코트 방문에서 돌아오자 그 증세가 너무도 심해져서, 식당으로 들어가 소파에 몸을 던지고는 어머니의 근심 어린 물음에 이렇게 답했다. \"몹시 아파요. 렌치 선생을 불러 주셔야겠어요.\""]], [["Wrench came, but did not apprehend anything serious, spoke of a “slight derangement,” and did not speak of coming again on the morrow.", "렌치가 왔지만 심각한 것은 없다고 보았으며, \"가벼운 이상\"이라고 말했을 뿐, 다음 날 다시 오겠다는 말은 하지 않았다."], ["He had a due value for the Vincys’ house, but the wariest men are apt to be dulled by routine, and on worried mornings will sometimes go through their business with the zest of the daily bell-ringer. Mr.", "그는 빈시 집안을 응당 소중하게 여겼으나, 아무리 신중한 사람도 일상에 무뎌지기 쉬워서, 분주한 아침이면 종지기가 매일 종을 치듯 무심한 열의로 일을 해치우는 법이다."], ["Wrench was a small, neat, bilious man, with a well-dressed wig: he had a laborious practice, an irascible temper, a lymphatic wife and seven children; and he was already rather late before setting out on a four-miles drive to meet Dr.", "렌치는 자그마하고 단정하며 신경질적인 사내로, 잘 손질한 가발을 쓰고 있었다. 그는 고된 진료에, 성마른 기질에, 림프질의 아내와 일곱 명의 아이를 두었다."], ["Minchin on the other side of Tipton, the decease of Hicks, a rural practitioner, having increased Middlemarch practice in that direction. Great statesmen err, and why not small medical men?", "그리고 그날도 이미 다소 늦은 시각에야, 티프턴 건너편에서 민친 박사를 만나러 사 마일 떨어진 곳까지 마차를 몰고 떠나야 했으니, 시골 개업의 힉스가 사망한 탓에 그쪽 방면의 미들마치 진료가 늘어났던 것이다."], ["Mr. Wrench did not neglect sending the usual white parcels, which this time had black and drastic contents.", "위대한 정치가도 그르치는데, 하물며 보잘것없는 의사라고 어찌 그러지 않겠는가?"], ["Their effect was not alleviating to poor Fred, who, however, unwilling as he said to believe that he was “in for an illness,” rose at his usual easy hour the next morning and went down-stairs meaning to breakfast, but succeeded in nothing but in sitting and shivering by the fire.", "렌치는 평소대로 흰 약 꾸러미를 보내는 일을 빠뜨리지 않았는데, 이번에는 그 안에 검고 독한 내용물이 들어 있었다."], ["Mr. Wrench was again sent for, but was gone on his rounds, and Mrs.", "그 효과는 가엾은 프레드를 누그러뜨리기는커녕 더 괴롭혔으나, 자신이 \"병에 걸렸다\"고는 믿고 싶지 않다던 그는 이튿날 아침 평소의 느긋한 시각에 일어나 아침을 먹으려고 아래층으로 내려갔지만, 난롯가에 앉아 떨고 있는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 못했다."], ["Vincy seeing her darling’s changed looks and general misery, began to cry and said she would send for Dr. Sprague.", "다시 렌치를 부르러 사람을 보냈으나 그는 왕진을 나간 뒤였고, 빈시 부인은 사랑하는 아들의 변한 안색과 전반적인 비참한 모습을 보고 울음을 터뜨리며 스프래그 박사를 부르겠다고 했다."]], [["“Oh, nonsense, mother!", "\"오, 말도 안 돼요, 어머니!"], ["It’s nothing,”", "아무것도 아니에요.\""], ["said Fred, putting out his hot dry hand to her, “I shall soon be all right.", "프레드가 뜨겁고 메마른 손을 어머니에게 내밀며 말했다. \"금방 괜찮아질 거예요."], ["I must have taken cold in that nasty damp ride.”", "그 고약하게 축축한 길을 달리다 감기에 걸렸나 봐요.\""]], [["“Mamma!”", "\"엄마!\""], ["said Rosamond, who was seated near the window (the dining-room windows looked on that highly respectable street called Lowick Gate), “there is Mr.", "창가에 앉아 있던 로저먼드가 말했다(식당 창문은 로윅 게이트라 불리는 그 매우 점잖은 거리를 향해 있었다). \"리드게이트 선생님이 누군가와 이야기하려고 멈춰 서 계세요."], ["Lydgate, stopping to speak to some one.", "저라면 안으로 불러들이겠어요."], ["If I were you I would call him in. He has cured Ellen Bulstrode.", "그분이 엘렌 불스트로드를 낫게 해 주셨대요."], ["They say he cures every one.”", "누구든 다 고친다고들 해요.\""]], [["Mrs. Vincy sprang to the window and opened it in an instant, thinking only of Fred and not of medical etiquette.", "빈시 부인은 의료상의 예법 따위는 생각지도 않고 오직 프레드만을 떠올리며, 창가로 뛰어가 순식간에 창문을 열었다."], ["Lydgate was only two yards off on the other side of some iron palisading, and turned round at the sudden sound of the sash, before she called to him.", "리드게이트는 철책 너머 불과 두 야드 떨어진 곳에 있었고, 그녀가 부르기도 전에 갑작스러운 창틀 소리에 돌아보았다."], ["In two minutes he was in the room, and Rosamond went out, after waiting just long enough to show a pretty anxiety conflicting with her sense of what was becoming.", "이 분도 채 못 되어 그는 방 안에 들어와 있었고, 로저먼드는 적당한 예의에 대한 의식과 충돌하는 어여쁜 근심을 드러낼 만큼만 머물렀다가 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["Lydgate had to hear a narrative in which Mrs. Vincy’s mind insisted with remarkable instinct on every point of minor importance, especially on what Mr.", "리드게이트는 빈시 부인의 정신이 놀라운 본능으로 사소한 사항 하나하나에, 특히 렌치가 다시 오겠다고 했는지 안 했는지에 관해 고집스럽게 매달리는 이야기를 들어야 했다."], ["Wrench had said and had not said about coming again. That there might be an awkward affair with Wrench, Lydgate saw at once; but the case was serious enough to make him dismiss that consideration: he was convinced that Fred was in the pink-skinned stage of typhoid fever, and that he had taken just the wrong medicines.", "렌치와의 사이에 어색한 일이 생길 수도 있겠다는 것을 리드게이트는 단번에 알아차렸으나, 사태가 워낙 심각해서 그런 고려는 접어 둘 수밖에 없었다."], ["He must go to bed immediately, must have a regular nurse, and various appliances and precautions must be used, about which Lydgate was particular.", "그는 프레드가 장티푸스의 발진기에 있으며, 바로 그 잘못된 약을 먹었다고 확신했다. 그는 즉시 침대에 누워야 하고, 전문 간호인을 두어야 하며, 여러 가지 기구와 예방 조치를 써야 했는데, 리드게이트는 이에 관해 까다로웠다."], ["Poor Mrs. Vincy’s terror at these indications of danger found vent in such words as came most easily.", "가엾은 빈시 부인은 이러한 위험의 징후에 대한 공포를 가장 쉽게 떠오르는 말들로 쏟아냈다."], ["She thought it “very ill usage on the part of Mr. Wrench, who had attended their house so many years in preference to Mr.", "그녀는 그것이 \"렌치 선생의 매우 못된 처사\"라고 생각했다."], ["Peacock, though Mr. Peacock was equally a friend.", "피콕 선생도 똑같이 친구였건만, 그는 여러 해 동안 피콕 선생보다 자기 집안을 더 챙겨 주지 않았던가."], ["Why Mr. Wrench should neglect her children more than others, she could not for the life of her understand.", "어째서 렌치 선생이 남의 아이들보다 자기 아이들을 더 소홀히 대하는지 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["He had not neglected Mrs. Larcher’s when they had the measles, nor indeed would Mrs.", "그는 라처 부인의 아이들이 홍역에 걸렸을 때 소홀히 하지 않았고, 사실 빈시 부인도 그가 그러기를 바라지는 않았다."], ["Vincy have wished that he should. And if anything should happen—”", "그런데 만약 무슨 일이라도 생긴다면—"]], [["Here poor Mrs. Vincy’s spirit quite broke down, and her Niobe throat and good-humored face were sadly convulsed.", "여기서 가엾은 빈시 부인의 기력이 완전히 꺾였고, 니오베 같은 목과 선량한 얼굴이 슬프게 일그러졌다."], ["This was in the hall out of Fred’s hearing, but Rosamond had opened the drawing-room door, and now came forward anxiously.", "이것은 프레드에게 들리지 않는 현관에서 일어난 일이었으나, 로저먼드가 응접실 문을 열고 근심스럽게 다가왔다."], ["Lydgate apologized for Mr. Wrench, said that the symptoms yesterday might have been disguising, and that this form of fever was very equivocal in its beginnings: he would go immediately to the druggist’s and have a prescription made up in order to lose no time, but he would write to Mr.", "리드게이트는 렌치를 변호하며, 어제의 증상은 분간하기 어려웠을 수 있고 이런 형태의 열병은 그 초기 단계에서 매우 모호하다고 말했다."], ["Wrench and tell him what had been done.", "그는 지체 없이 즉시 약방으로 가서 처방을 조제하게 하겠지만, 렌치 선생에게 편지를 써서 어떤 조치를 했는지 알리겠다고 했다."]], [["“But you must come again—you must go on attending Fred.", "\"하지만 선생님은 다시 와 주셔야 해요—계속 프레드를 봐 주셔야 해요."], ["I can’t have my boy left to anybody who may come or not.", "제 아이를 올지 안 올지도 모르는 사람에게 맡길 수는 없어요."], ["I bear nobody ill-will, thank God, and Mr.", "저는 누구에게도 악감정을 품지 않아요, 정말이지요."], ["Wrench saved me in the pleurisy, but he’d better have let me die—if—if—”", "렌치 선생이 제 늑막염을 낫게 해 주긴 했지만, 차라리 저를 죽게 두는 편이 나았을 거예요—만약—만약—\""]], [["“I will meet Mr. Wrench here, then, shall I?”", "\"그렇다면 제가 여기서 렌치 선생을 만나겠습니다, 그래도 될까요?\""], ["said Lydgate, really believing that Wrench was not well prepared to deal wisely with a case of this kind.", "리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 렌치가 이런 종류의 사례를 현명하게 다룰 준비가 제대로 되어 있지 않다고 진심으로 믿고 있었다."]], [["“Pray make that arrangement, Mr. Lydgate,”", "\"부디 그렇게 해 주세요, 리드게이트 선생님.\""], ["said Rosamond, coming to her mother’s aid, and supporting her arm to lead her away.", "로저먼드가 어머니를 거들며 말했고, 어머니의 팔을 부축해 자리를 떠나도록 이끌었다."]], [["When Mr. Vincy came home he was very angry with Wrench, and did not care if he never came into his house again.", "빈시 씨는 집에 돌아오자 렌치에게 몹시 화가 나서, 그가 다시는 자기 집에 발을 들이지 않는다 해도 상관없다고 했다."], ["Lydgate should go on now, whether Wrench liked it or not.", "이제는 렌치가 좋아하든 말든 리드게이트가 계속 봐야 했다."], ["It was no joke to have fever in the house.", "집안에 열병이 든 것은 결코 농담거리가 아니었다."], ["Everybody must be sent to now, not to come to dinner on Thursday.", "이제 모두에게 사람을 보내 목요일 만찬에 오지 말라고 알려야 했다."], ["And Pritchard needn’t get up any wine: brandy was the best thing against infection.", "그리고 프리처드는 포도주를 준비할 필요가 없었으니, 전염병에는 브랜디가 최고였다."], ["“I shall drink brandy,” added Mr.", "\"나는 브랜디를 마시겠어.\""], ["Vincy, emphatically—as much as to say, this was not an occasion for firing with blank-cartridges.", "빈시 씨가 강조하며 덧붙였다—마치 지금은 빈 탄약으로 허세를 부릴 때가 아니라는 듯이."], ["“He’s an uncommonly unfortunate lad, is Fred.", "\"프레드 녀석은 유난히 운이 없는 놈이야."], ["He’d need have some luck by and by to make up for all this—else I don’t know who’d have an eldest son.”", "이 모든 걸 메우려면 앞으로 운이 좀 따라 줘야 할 텐데—안 그러면 누가 장남을 두려고 하겠나.\""]], [["“Don’t say so, Vincy,”", "\"그런 말 마세요, 빈시.\""], ["said the mother, with a quivering lip, “if you don’t want him to be taken from me.”", "어머니가 입술을 떨며 말했다. \"그 애를 제게서 빼앗기게 하고 싶지 않다면요.\""]], [["“It will worret you to death, Lucy; that I can see,”", "\"그러다 당신은 걱정으로 죽겠소, 루시. 그게 내 눈에 다 보인다오.\""], ["said Mr. Vincy, more mildly.", "빈시 씨가 좀 더 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“However, Wrench shall know what I think of the matter.”", "\"어쨌든 렌치에게는 이 일에 대한 내 생각을 알려야겠어.\""], ["(What Mr. Vincy thought confusedly was, that the fever might somehow have been hindered if Wrench had shown the proper solicitude about his—the Mayor’s—family.) “I’m the last man to give in to the cry about new doctors, or new parsons either—whether they’re Bulstrode’s men or not.", "(빈시 씨가 어렴풋이 생각한 바는, 렌치가 자기—시장의—집안에 대해 마땅한 정성을 보였더라면 어떻게든 이 열병을 막을 수 있었으리라는 것이었다.) \"나는 새 의사니 새 목사니 하는 떠들썩한 소리에 동조하는 마지막 사람일세—그들이 불스트로드의 사람이든 아니든 말이야."], ["But Wrench shall know what I think, take it as he will.”", "하지만 렌치에게는 내 생각을 알릴 테니, 좋을 대로 받아들이라지.\""]], [["Wrench did not take it at all well.", "렌치는 그것을 전혀 좋게 받아들이지 않았다."], ["Lydgate was as polite as he could be in his offhand way, but politeness in a man who has placed you at a disadvantage is only an additional exasperation, especially if he happens to have been an object of dislike beforehand.", "리드게이트는 무뚝뚝한 방식 안에서나마 최대한 정중했지만, 자신을 곤경에 빠뜨린 사람의 정중함이란 한층 더한 분노를 돋울 뿐이고, 더구나 미리부터 미움의 대상이었다면 더욱 그러했다."], ["Country practitioners used to be an irritable species, susceptible on the point of honor; and Mr. Wrench was one of the most irritable among them.", "시골 개업의들은 으레 성마른 부류라서 명예 문제에 예민했는데, 렌치는 그중에서도 가장 성마른 축에 들었다."], ["He did not refuse to meet Lydgate in the evening, but his temper was somewhat tried on the occasion.", "그는 저녁에 리드게이트를 만나는 것을 거절하지는 않았으나, 그 자리에서 그의 성미는 다소 시험에 들었다."], ["He had to hear Mrs. Vincy say—", "그는 빈시 부인이 이렇게 말하는 것을 들어야 했다—"]], [["“Oh, Mr. Wrench, what have I ever done that you should use me so?— To go away, and never to come again!", "\"오, 렌치 선생, 제가 도대체 무슨 짓을 했기에 저를 이렇게 대하시나요?—가 버린 뒤에 다시는 오지 않으시다니!"], ["And my boy might have been stretched a corpse!”", "제 아들이 시신이 되어 누워 있을 수도 있었어요!\""]], [["Mr. Vincy, who had been keeping up a sharp fire on the enemy Infection, and was a good deal heated in consequence, started up when he heard Wrench come in, and went into the hall to let him know what he thought.", "적군인 전염병을 향해 맹렬한 포화를 퍼붓느라 결과적으로 잔뜩 달아올라 있던 빈시 씨는, 렌치가 들어오는 소리를 듣자 벌떡 일어나 자기 생각을 알리려고 현관으로 나갔다."]], [["“I’ll tell you what, Wrench, this is beyond a joke,”", "\"이보게, 렌치, 이건 농담을 넘어선 일일세.\""], ["said the Mayor, who of late had had to rebuke offenders with an official air, and now broadened himself by putting his thumbs in his armholes.", "시장이 말했다. 그는 요즘 들어 공직자다운 위엄으로 위반자들을 꾸짖어야 했던 터라, 이제 엄지손가락을 겨드랑이에 끼워 넣으며 몸을 한껏 부풀렸다."], ["“To let fever get unawares into a house like this.", "\"이런 집안에 열병이 모르는 새 들어오게 만들다니."], ["There are some things that ought to be actionable, and are not so— that’s my opinion.”", "마땅히 고소할 만한 일인데도 그렇게 안 되는 일들이 있단 말이지—그게 내 생각일세.\""]], [["But irrational reproaches were easier to bear than the sense of being instructed, or rather the sense that a younger man, like Lydgate, inwardly considered him in need of instruction, for “in point of fact,”", "그러나 불합리한 비난은, 가르침을 받는다는 느낌, 아니 차라리 리드게이트 같은 젊은 자가 속으로 자기에게 가르침이 필요하다고 여긴다는 느낌보다는 견디기가 쉬웠다. \"사실인즉슨\""], ["Mr. Wrench afterwards said, Lydgate paraded flighty, foreign notions, which would not wear.", "나중에 렌치가 말한 바로는, 리드게이트가 변덕스럽고 외래적인 관념을 떠벌렸는데 그런 것은 오래갈 리 없다는 것이었다."], ["He swallowed his ire for the moment, but he afterwards wrote to decline further attendance in the case.", "그는 순간의 노여움을 삼켰으나, 나중에 편지를 써서 이 사례를 더 이상 맡지 않겠다고 통보했다."], ["The house might be a good one, but Mr. Wrench was not going to truckle to anybody on a professional matter.", "좋은 집안일지는 몰라도, 렌치는 전문적인 문제에서 누구에게도 굽실거릴 생각이 없었다."], ["He reflected, with much probability on his side, that Lydgate would by-and-by be caught tripping too, and that his ungentlemanly attempts to discredit the sale of drugs by his professional brethren, would by-and-by recoil on himself.", "그는 상당한 개연성을 자기편에 두고서, 리드게이트도 머지않아 실수를 들킬 것이며, 동업자들의 약품 판매를 깎아내리려는 그 신사답지 못한 시도가 머지않아 자신에게 되돌아오리라고 짚었다."], ["He threw out biting remarks on Lydgate’s tricks, worthy only of a quack, to get himself a factitious reputation with credulous people.", "그는 리드게이트의 술수에 대해 신랄한 말을 던졌으니, 잘 속는 사람들 사이에서 거짓 평판을 얻으려는, 돌팔이에게나 어울리는 짓이라는 것이었다."], ["That cant about cures was never got up by sound practitioners.", "치료법에 관한 그런 허튼소리는 건실한 개업의가 결코 꾸며 내는 법이 없었다."]], [["This was a point on which Lydgate smarted as much as Wrench could desire.", "이것은 렌치가 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 리드게이트를 아프게 찌르는 대목이었다."], ["To be puffed by ignorance was not only humiliating, but perilous, and not more enviable than the reputation of the weather-prophet.", "무지에 의해 추켜세워지는 것은 굴욕적일 뿐만 아니라 위험하기까지 했고, 날씨 예언자의 평판보다 나을 게 없었다."], ["He was impatient of the foolish expectations amidst which all work must be carried on, and likely enough to damage himself as much as Mr. Wrench could wish, by an unprofessional openness.", "그는 모든 일이 진행되어야 하는 그 어리석은 기대들에 진절머리가 났고, 전문가답지 못한 솔직함으로 인해 렌치가 바랄 만큼이나 자기 자신을 해칠 가능성이 다분했다."]], [["However, Lydgate was installed as medical attendant on the Vincys, and the event was a subject of general conversation in Middlemarch. Some said, that the Vincys had behaved scandalously, that Mr.", "그러나 리드게이트는 빈시 집안의 진료를 맡게 되었고, 그 일은 미들마치에서 널리 회자되는 화제가 되었다."], ["Vincy had threatened Wrench, and that Mrs. Vincy had accused him of poisoning her son.", "어떤 이들은 빈시 집안이 망신스럽게 처신했다고, 빈시 씨가 렌치를 협박했으며 빈시 부인은 그가 아들을 독살하려 했다고 비난했다고들 말했다."], ["Others were of opinion that Mr. Lydgate’s passing by was providential, that he was wonderfully clever in fevers, and that Bulstrode was in the right to bring him forward.", "또 어떤 이들은 리드게이트가 마침 그곳을 지나간 것이 하늘의 섭리였으며, 그가 열병에 놀랍도록 능하고, 불스트로드가 그를 내세운 것은 옳았다는 의견이었다."], ["Many people believed that Lydgate’s coming to the town at all was really due to Bulstrode; and Mrs.", "많은 사람들은 리드게이트가 애초에 이 도시로 온 것 자체가 실은 불스트로드 덕분이라고 믿었다."], ["Taft, who was always counting stitches and gathered her information in misleading fragments caught between the rows of her knitting, had got it into her head that Mr. Lydgate was a natural son of Bulstrode’s, a fact which seemed to justify her suspicions of evangelical laymen.", "그리고 늘 뜨개질 코를 세느라 바빠서 뜨개질 줄 사이에서 주워들은 오해의 단편들로 정보를 그러모으던 태프트 부인은, 리드게이트가 불스트로드의 사생아라는 생각을 머릿속에 굳혀 버렸으니, 그것은 복음주의 평신도들에 대한 그녀의 의심을 정당화해 주는 듯한 사실이었다."]], [["She one day communicated this piece of knowledge to Mrs. Farebrother, who did not fail to tell her son of it, observing—", "그녀는 어느 날 페어브러더 부인에게 이 지식의 조각을 전했고, 부인은 빠짐없이 아들에게 그것을 일러 주며 한마디 덧붙였다—"]], [["“I should not be surprised at anything in Bulstrode, but I should be sorry to think it of Mr. Lydgate.”", "\"불스트로드에 관한 거라면 나는 무슨 일에도 놀라지 않겠다만, 리드게이트 선생에 대해 그런 생각을 한다는 건 안타까운 일이구나.\""]], [["“Why, mother,” said Mr.", "\"아니, 어머니.\""], ["Farebrother, after an explosive laugh, “you know very well that Lydgate is of a good family in the North.", "페어브러더가 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨린 뒤에 말했다. \"리드게이트가 북부의 훌륭한 집안 출신이라는 걸 잘 아시잖아요."], ["He never heard of Bulstrode before he came here.”", "그는 여기 오기 전까지 불스트로드라는 이름을 들어 본 적도 없어요.\""]], [["“That is satisfactory so far as Mr.", "\"리드게이트 선생에 관한 한 그것 참 다행이구나, 캠던.\""], ["Lydgate is concerned, Camden,”", "노부인이 단호한 태도로 말했다."], ["said the old lady, with an air of precision.—“But as to Bulstrode—the report may be true of some other son.”", "\"하지만 불스트로드로 말할 것 같으면—그 소문은 다른 어떤 아들에게는 사실일지도 모르지.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XXVII.", "# 제25장"]], [["Let the high Muse chant loves Olympian: We are but mortals, and must sing of man.", "높은 뮤즈로 하여금 올림포스의 사랑을 노래하게 하라. 우리는 한낱 인간일 뿐이니, 인간을 노래할 수밖에 없도다."]], [["An eminent philosopher among my friends, who can dignify even your ugly furniture by lifting it into the serene light of science, has shown me this pregnant little fact.", "내 친구들 가운데 한 저명한 철학자가 있는데, 그는 당신의 보잘것없는 가구조차도 과학의 청명한 빛 속으로 들어 올림으로써 품위를 부여할 줄 안다. 그가 내게 의미심장한 작은 사실 하나를 보여 주었다."], ["Your pier-glass or extensive surface of polished steel made to be rubbed by a housemaid, will be minutely and multitudinously scratched in all directions; but place now against it a lighted candle as a centre of illumination, and lo!", "당신의 거울이나, 가정부가 닦도록 만들어진 광이 나는 강철의 널찍한 표면은, 사방으로 미세하고도 무수하게 긁혀 있을 것이다. 그러나 이제 그 앞에 불 켜진 양초 하나를 빛의 중심으로 놓아 보라, 그러면 보라!"], ["the scratches will seem to arrange themselves in a fine series of concentric circles round that little sun.", "그 긁힌 자국들은 그 작은 태양을 중심으로 동심원의 아름다운 무리를 이루며 스스로 정렬하는 듯 보일 것이다."], ["It is demonstrable that the scratches are going everywhere impartially and it is only your candle which produces the flattering illusion of a concentric arrangement, its light falling with an exclusive optical selection.", "그 긁힌 자국들은 어디로든 공평하게 뻗어 있다는 사실이 입증될 수 있으며, 동심원 배열이라는 그 그럴듯한 환영을 만들어 내는 것은 오직 당신의 양초일 뿐, 그 빛이 배타적인 시각적 선택을 하며 떨어지는 것이다."], ["These things are a parable.", "이런 것들은 하나의 우화다."], ["The scratches are events, and the candle is the egoism of any person now absent—of Miss Vincy, for example.", "긁힌 자국들은 사건들이고, 양초는 지금 여기 없는 어떤 사람의, 이를테면 빈시 양의 이기심이다."], ["Rosamond had a Providence of her own who had kindly made her more charming than other girls, and who seemed to have arranged Fred’s illness and Mr. Wrench’s mistake in order to bring her and Lydgate within effective proximity.", "로저먼드에게는 자기만의 섭리가 있었으니, 그것은 친절하게도 그녀를 다른 처녀들보다 더 매력적으로 만들어 주었고, 그녀와 리드게이트를 효과적으로 가까운 거리에 데려다 놓으려고 프레드의 병과 렌치 씨의 실수까지도 마련해 놓은 듯 보였다."], ["It would have been to contravene these arrangements if Rosamond had consented to go away to Stone Court or elsewhere, as her parents wished her to do, especially since Mr.", "부모가 바란 대로 로저먼드가 스톤 코트나 다른 곳으로 떠나는 데 동의했다면, 그것은 이러한 섭리에 어긋나는 일이 되었을 것이다. 더구나 리드게이트 씨가 그런 예방책은 불필요하다고 여겼으니 말이다."], ["Lydgate thought the precaution needless. Therefore, while Miss Morgan and the children were sent away to a farmhouse the morning after Fred’s illness had declared itself, Rosamond refused to leave papa and mamma.", "그리하여 프레드의 병이 드러난 다음 날 아침에 모건 양과 아이들은 어느 농가로 보내졌지만, 로저먼드는 아빠와 엄마 곁을 떠나지 않겠다고 했다."]], [["Poor mamma indeed was an object to touch any creature born of woman; and Mr. Vincy, who doted on his wife, was more alarmed on her account than on Fred’s.", "가엾은 엄마는 참으로 여자에게서 태어난 그 누구의 마음이라도 움직일 만한 모습이었고, 아내를 끔찍이 사랑하는 빈시 씨는 프레드보다도 아내가 더 걱정스러웠다."], ["But for his insistence she would have taken no rest: her brightness was all bedimmed; unconscious of her costume which had always been so fresh and gay, she was like a sick bird with languid eye and plumage ruffled, her senses dulled to the sights and sounds that used most to interest her.", "그가 고집하지 않았더라면 그녀는 한순간도 쉬지 않았을 것이다. 그녀의 환한 빛은 온통 흐려졌고, 늘 그토록 산뜻하고 화사하던 옷차림도 의식하지 못한 채, 그녀는 눈빛이 흐릿하고 깃털이 헝클어진 병든 새처럼 보였으며, 예전에 가장 흥미를 끌던 광경과 소리에도 감각이 무뎌져 있었다."], ["Fred’s delirium, in which he seemed to be wandering out of her reach, tore her heart.", "자기 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 헤매어 가는 듯한 프레드의 헛소리는 그녀의 가슴을 찢어 놓았다."], ["After her first outburst against Mr. Wrench she went about very quietly: her one low cry was to Lydgate.", "렌치 씨를 향한 첫 분노가 터져 나온 뒤로 그녀는 아주 조용히 지냈으니, 그녀가 낮게 부르짖는 단 하나의 호소는 리드게이트를 향한 것이었다."], ["She would follow him out of the room and put her hand on his arm moaning out, “Save my boy.”", "그녀는 그를 방 밖으로 따라 나가 그의 팔에 손을 얹고 신음하듯 말하곤 했다. \"내 아들을 살려 주세요.\""], ["Once she pleaded, “He has always been good to me, Mr.", "한번은 이렇게 간청했다. \"그 애는 늘 제게 잘했어요, 리드게이트 씨."], ["Lydgate: he never had a hard word for his mother,”—as if poor Fred’s suffering were an accusation against him.", "한 번도 제 어미한테 모진 말을 한 적이 없답니다.\" 마치 가엾은 프레드의 고통이 그를 향한 고발이라도 되는 듯이."], ["All the deepest fibres of the mother’s memory were stirred, and the young man whose voice took a gentler tone when he spoke to her, was one with the babe whom she had loved, with a love new to her, before he was born.", "어머니의 기억 속 가장 깊은 가닥들이 모두 흔들렸고, 그녀에게 말을 건넬 때 한층 부드러운 어조를 띠던 그 젊은이는, 그녀가 그 아이를 낳기도 전에 새롭게 알게 된 사랑으로 사랑했던 그 갓난아기와 하나가 되었다."]], [["“I have good hope, Mrs.", "\"좋은 희망이 있습니다, 빈시 부인.\""], ["Vincy,” Lydgate would say.", "리드게이트는 말하곤 했다."], ["“Come down with me and let us talk about the food.”", "\"저와 함께 아래층으로 내려가서 음식 이야기를 나눕시다.\""], ["In that way he led her to the parlor where Rosamond was, and made a change for her, surprising her into taking some tea or broth which had been prepared for her.", "그렇게 그는 로저먼드가 있는 거실로 그녀를 데려갔고, 그녀에게 변화를 주어, 그녀를 위해 마련해 둔 차나 죽을 뜻밖에도 들게 만들었다."], ["There was a constant understanding between him and Rosamond on these matters.", "이런 일들에 관해서 그와 로저먼드 사이에는 한결같은 양해가 있었다."], ["He almost always saw her before going to the sickroom, and she appealed to him as to what she could do for mamma.", "그는 병실로 가기 전에 거의 언제나 그녀를 만났고, 그녀는 엄마를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있는지 그에게 도움을 청했다."], ["Her presence of mind and adroitness in carrying out his hints were admirable, and it is not wonderful that the idea of seeing Rosamond began to mingle itself with his interest in the case.", "그의 지시를 실행하는 그녀의 침착함과 능란함은 감탄할 만한 것이었으니, 로저먼드를 본다는 생각이 그 병에 대한 그의 관심과 뒤섞이기 시작한 것도 이상한 일은 아니었다."], ["Especially when the critical stage was passed, and he began to feel confident of Fred’s recovery.", "특히 위태로운 고비를 넘기고 프레드의 회복을 확신하기 시작했을 때는 더욱 그러했다."], ["In the more doubtful time, he had advised calling in Dr. Sprague (who, if he could, would rather have remained neutral on Wrench’s account); but after two consultations, the conduct of the case was left to Lydgate, and there was every reason to make him assiduous.", "더 불확실하던 시기에는 그가 스프레이그 박사를 부르자고 권한 적이 있었지만(그 박사는 가능하다면 렌치를 생각해서 차라리 중립을 지키고 싶어 했을 것이다), 두 번의 진찰 끝에 그 병의 처치는 리드게이트에게 맡겨졌으며, 그가 부지런히 매달릴 만한 이유는 충분했다."], ["Morning and evening he was at Mr. Vincy’s, and gradually the visits became cheerful as Fred became simply feeble, and lay not only in need of the utmost petting but conscious of it, so that Mrs.", "아침저녁으로 그는 빈시 씨 댁에 있었고, 프레드가 그저 기력이 없을 뿐인 상태가 되어, 더할 나위 없는 보살핌을 필요로 할 뿐 아니라 그것을 의식하며 누워 있게 되자, 그 방문은 차츰 즐거워졌다."], ["Vincy felt as if, after all, the illness had made a festival for her tenderness.", "그리하여 빈시 부인은 결국 그 병이 자기 애정을 위한 축제를 마련해 준 것 같은 기분이 들었다."]], [["Both father and mother held it an added reason for good spirits, when old Mr. Featherstone sent messages by Lydgate, saying that Fred must make haste and get well, as he, Peter Featherstone, could not do without him, and missed his visits sadly.", "늙은 페더스톤 씨가 리드게이트를 통해, 자기 피터 페더스톤은 프레드 없이는 지낼 수 없고 그의 방문이 무척 그립다며 프레드더러 어서 나으라는 전갈을 보냈을 때, 아버지와 어머니는 모두 그것을 기운을 낼 또 하나의 이유로 받아들였다."], ["The old man himself was getting bedridden. Mrs.", "그 노인 자신도 점점 자리보전을 하게 되고 있었다."], ["Vincy told these messages to Fred when he could listen, and he turned towards her his delicate, pinched face, from which all the thick blond hair had been cut away, and in which the eyes seemed to have got larger, yearning for some word about Mary—wondering what she felt about his illness.", "빈시 부인은 프레드가 들을 수 있게 되었을 때 이런 전갈들을 그에게 전했고, 그는 숱 많은 금발이 모조리 깎여 나간 섬세하고 야윈 얼굴을 그녀에게로 돌렸는데, 그 얼굴에서 두 눈은 한층 커진 듯 보였고, 메리에 관한 한마디 말을 갈망하며 그녀가 자기 병에 대해 어떻게 느끼는지 궁금해하는 듯했다. 그의 입술에서는 한마디도 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["No word passed his lips; but “to hear with eyes belongs to love’s rare wit,” and the mother in the fulness of her heart not only divined Fred’s longing, but felt ready for any sacrifice in order to satisfy him.", "그러나 \"눈으로 듣는 것은 사랑의 진귀한 재치에 속하나니\", 마음이 가득 차오른 어머니는 프레드의 갈망을 알아차렸을 뿐 아니라, 그를 만족시키기 위해서라면 어떤 희생이라도 치를 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["“If I can only see my boy strong again,”", "\"내 아들이 다시 튼튼해지는 것만 볼 수 있다면,\""], ["she said, in her loving folly; “and who knows?—perhaps master of Stone Court!", "그녀는 사랑에 겨운 어리석음으로 말했다. \"또 누가 알겠니?"], ["and he can marry anybody he likes then.”", "어쩌면 스톤 코트의 주인이 될지도! 그러면 그 애는 마음에 드는 누구하고든 결혼할 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“Not if they won’t have me, mother,”", "\"그쪽에서 저를 마다하면 안 되지요, 어머니.\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."], ["The illness had made him childish, and tears came as he spoke.", "병이 그를 어린애처럼 만들어, 그가 말하는 동안 눈물이 솟았다."]], [["“Oh, take a bit of jelly, my dear,”", "\"오, 젤리 좀 들렴, 얘야.\""], ["said Mrs. Vincy, secretly incredulous of any such refusal.", "빈시 부인이 말했는데, 속으로는 그런 거절이 있으리라고는 도무지 믿지 않았다."]], [["She never left Fred’s side when her husband was not in the house, and thus Rosamond was in the unusual position of being much alone.", "남편이 집에 없을 때면 그녀는 결코 프레드의 곁을 떠나지 않았고, 그래서 로저먼드는 혼자 있는 일이 많은 이례적인 처지에 놓이게 되었다."], ["Lydgate, naturally, never thought of staying long with her, yet it seemed that the brief impersonal conversations they had together were creating that peculiar intimacy which consists in shyness.", "리드게이트는 당연히 그녀와 오래 머물 생각은 전혀 하지 않았지만, 두 사람이 나누는 짧고 사사로움 없는 대화가 수줍음으로 이루어진 그 독특한 친밀감을 빚어내고 있는 듯했다."], ["They were obliged to look at each other in speaking, and somehow the looking could not be carried through as the matter of course which it really was.", "그들은 말을 할 때 서로 마주 보지 않을 수 없었는데, 어쩐 일인지 그 마주 봄은 실제로 그러했던 것처럼 당연한 일로 끝까지 이어지지 못했다."], ["Lydgate began to feel this sort of consciousness unpleasant and one day looked down, or anywhere, like an ill-worked puppet.", "리드게이트는 이런 종류의 의식이 불쾌하게 느껴지기 시작해서, 어느 날은 잘못 다룬 꼭두각시처럼 아래쪽이나 아무 데나 내려다보았다."], ["But this turned out badly: the next day, Rosamond looked down, and the consequence was that when their eyes met again, both were more conscious than before.", "그러나 이것은 좋지 않게 끝났다. 다음 날에는 로저먼드가 아래를 내려다보았고, 그 결과 두 사람의 눈이 다시 마주쳤을 때 둘 다 전보다 더 의식하게 되었던 것이다."], ["There was no help for this in science, and as Lydgate did not want to flirt, there seemed to be no help for it in folly.", "이 일에 과학으로는 도움 될 것이 없었고, 리드게이트는 시시덕거리고 싶지 않았으니 어리석음으로도 도움 될 것이 없어 보였다."], ["It was therefore a relief when neighbors no longer considered the house in quarantine, and when the chances of seeing Rosamond alone were very much reduced.", "그러므로 이웃 사람들이 더 이상 그 집을 격리 상태로 여기지 않게 되고, 로저먼드를 혼자 만날 기회가 매우 줄어들었을 때는 오히려 마음이 놓였다."]], [["But that intimacy of mutual embarrassment, in which each feels that the other is feeling something, having once existed, its effect is not to be done away with.", "그러나 서로가 상대방이 무언가를 느끼고 있다고 느끼는 그 어색함의 친밀감은, 일단 생겨난 이상 그 효과를 없앨 수 없는 법이다."], ["Talk about the weather and other well-bred topics is apt to seem a hollow device, and behavior can hardly become easy unless it frankly recognizes a mutual fascination—which of course need not mean anything deep or serious.", "날씨에 관한 이야기나 그 밖의 교양 있는 화제는 공허한 방편처럼 보이기 십상이고, 서로에게 끌린다는 사실을 솔직히 인정하지 않는 한 행동은 좀처럼 편안해지지 못하는데, 물론 그것이 무언가 깊거나 진지한 것을 뜻할 필요는 없었다."], ["This was the way in which Rosamond and Lydgate slid gracefully into ease, and made their intercourse lively again.", "로저먼드와 리드게이트가 우아하게 편안함으로 미끄러져 들어가, 그들의 교제를 다시 활기차게 만든 것도 바로 이런 식이었다."], ["Visitors came and went as usual, there was once more music in the drawing-room, and all the extra hospitality of Mr.", "손님들이 여느 때처럼 오가고, 응접실에서는 다시 음악이 흘렀으며, 빈시 씨의 시장직에 따르는 온갖 별스러운 환대가 되돌아왔다."], ["Vincy’s mayoralty returned. Lydgate, whenever he could, took his seat by Rosamond’s side, and lingered to hear her music, calling himself her captive—meaning, all the while, not to be her captive.", "리드게이트는 기회가 닿을 때마다 로저먼드의 옆자리에 앉아, 그녀의 연주를 들으려고 머무르며 자신을 그녀의 포로라고 칭했지만, 정작 속으로는 줄곧 그녀의 포로가 되지 않을 작정이었다."], ["The preposterousness of the notion that he could at once set up a satisfactory establishment as a married man was a sufficient guarantee against danger.", "그가 당장 결혼한 남자로서 만족스러운 살림을 차릴 수 있다는 생각이 터무니없다는 사실 자체가 위험에 대한 충분한 보증이었다."], ["This play at being a little in love was agreeable, and did not interfere with graver pursuits.", "조금 사랑에 빠진 척하는 이 놀이는 즐거웠고, 더 진지한 일을 방해하지도 않았다."], ["Flirtation, after all, was not necessarily a singeing process.", "어쨌거나 시시덕거림이 반드시 불에 데는 과정은 아니었다."], ["Rosamond, for her part, had never enjoyed the days so much in her life before: she was sure of being admired by some one worth captivating, and she did not distinguish flirtation from love, either in herself or in another.", "한편 로저먼드로 말하자면, 평생 그토록 즐거운 나날을 누려 본 적이 없었다. 매혹할 만한 가치가 있는 누군가에게 자신이 흠모받고 있다는 것이 확실했고, 자신에게서든 남에게서든 시시덕거림과 사랑을 구별하지 못했다."], ["She seemed to be sailing with a fair wind just whither she would go, and her thoughts were much occupied with a handsome house in Lowick Gate which she hoped would by-and-by be vacant.", "그녀는 순풍을 받으며 가고 싶은 곳으로 곧장 항해하는 듯했고, 그녀의 생각은 로윅 게이트에 있는, 머지않아 비기를 바라는 어느 멋진 집에 온통 쏠려 있었다."], ["She was quite determined, when she was married, to rid herself adroitly of all the visitors who were not agreeable to her at her father’s; and she imagined the drawing-room in her favorite house with various styles of furniture.", "그녀는 결혼하면 아버지 집에서 마음에 들지 않던 모든 손님들을 능란하게 떨쳐 버리겠다고 단단히 작정했고, 자기가 좋아하는 그 집의 응접실을 갖가지 양식의 가구로 꾸민 모습을 상상했다."]], [["Certainly her thoughts were much occupied with Lydgate himself; he seemed to her almost perfect: if he had known his notes so that his enchantment under her music had been less like an emotional elephant’s, and if he had been able to discriminate better the refinements of her taste in dress, she could hardly have mentioned a deficiency in him.", "물론 그녀의 생각은 리드게이트 자신에게도 온통 쏠려 있었다."], ["How different he was from young Plymdale or Mr. Caius Larcher!", "그는 그녀에게 거의 완벽해 보였다."], ["Those young men had not a notion of French, and could speak on no subject with striking knowledge, except perhaps the dyeing and carrying trades, which of course they were ashamed to mention; they were Middlemarch gentry, elated with their silver-headed whips and satin stocks, but embarrassed in their manners, and timidly jocose: even Fred was above them, having at least the accent and manner of a university man.", "그가 음표를 제대로 알아서 그녀의 음악에 빠져드는 그의 황홀함이 감정에 휩싸인 코끼리처럼 보이지만 않았더라면, 그리고 그가 옷차림에 관한 그녀의 세련된 취향을 좀 더 잘 분간할 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 그에게서 결점을 거의 짚어 낼 수 없었을 것이다. 그는 젊은 플림데일이나 케이어스 라처 씨와 얼마나 다른가!"], ["Whereas Lydgate was always listened to, bore himself with the careless politeness of conscious superiority, and seemed to have the right clothes on by a certain natural affinity, without ever having to think about them.", "그 젊은이들은 프랑스어라고는 한마디도 몰랐고, 어떤 주제에 관해서도 놀랄 만한 지식으로 이야기하지 못했는데, 다만 염색업이나 운송업에 관해서라면 모를까, 그런 것은 물론 입에 올리기 부끄러워했다."], ["Rosamond was proud when he entered the room, and when he approached her with a distinguishing smile, she had a delicious sense that she was the object of enviable homage.", "그들은 미들마치의 신사 계층으로서, 은제 손잡이가 달린 채찍과 새틴 넥타이에 우쭐했지만 태도는 어색했고, 머뭇거리며 농담을 했다. 프레드조차도 그들보다는 나았으니, 적어도 대학 출신의 말투와 태도는 지니고 있었던 것이다."], ["If Lydgate had been aware of all the pride he excited in that delicate bosom, he might have been just as well pleased as any other man, even the most densely ignorant of humoral pathology or fibrous tissue: he held it one of the prettiest attitudes of the feminine mind to adore a man’s pre-eminence without too precise a knowledge of what it consisted in.", "반면에 리드게이트는 언제나 사람들이 귀를 기울였고, 우월함을 의식한 자의 무심한 정중함으로 처신했으며, 옷에 대해 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없으면서도 어떤 타고난 친화력으로 알맞은 옷을 걸친 듯 보였다. 그가 방에 들어서면 로저먼드는 자랑스러웠고, 그가 그녀를 알아보는 미소를 띠며 다가오면, 그녀는 자신이 부러움을 살 만한 경의의 대상이라는 감미로운 느낌에 젖었다."], ["But Rosamond was not one of those helpless girls who betray themselves unawares, and whose behavior is awkwardly driven by their impulses, instead of being steered by wary grace and propriety.", "만약 리드게이트가 자신이 그 섬세한 가슴속에 불러일으킨 그 모든 자부심을 알았더라면, 그는 다른 어떤 남자와도, 심지어 체액 병리학이나 섬유 조직에 대해 더없이 무지한 남자와도 마찬가지로 흡족해했을지 모른다. 그는 한 남자의 탁월함을 그것이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 너무 정확히 알지 못한 채 흠모하는 것을 여성 정신의 가장 어여쁜 자태 가운데 하나로 여겼다."], ["Do you imagine that her rapid forecast and rumination concerning house-furniture and society were ever discernible in her conversation, even with her mamma?", "그러나 로저먼드는 자기도 모르게 속내를 드러내고, 신중한 우아함과 예법으로 키를 잡는 대신 충동에 떠밀려 어색하게 행동하는 그런 속수무책의 처녀들 가운데 하나가 아니었다."], ["On the contrary, she would have expressed the prettiest surprise and disapprobation if she had heard that another young lady had been detected in that immodest prematureness—indeed, would probably have disbelieved in its possibility.", "집안 가구와 사교계에 관한 그녀의 재빠른 예측과 궁리가, 자기 엄마와 나누는 대화에서조차 드러난 적이 있었으리라고 상상하는가? 오히려 그녀는 다른 젊은 숙녀가 그런 점잖지 못한 조급함을 들켰다는 말을 들었다면 더없이 어여쁜 놀라움과 못마땅함을 드러냈을 것이고, 사실 그런 일이 있을 수 있다고는 아마 믿지 않았을 것이다."], ["For Rosamond never showed any unbecoming knowledge, and was always that combination of correct sentiments, music, dancing, drawing, elegant note-writing, private album for extracted verse, and perfect blond loveliness, which made the irresistible woman for the doomed man of that date.", "로저먼드는 결코 어울리지 않는 지식을 내비치는 법이 없었고, 언제나 올바른 정서와 음악, 춤, 그림, 우아한 편지 쓰기, 시구를 가려 적어 두는 개인 앨범, 그리고 완벽한 금발의 사랑스러움이 어우러진 그런 여인이었으니, 그것은 그 시절 운명 지어진 남자에게 거역할 수 없는 여인을 만들어 내는 것이었다. 부디 그녀를 부당하게 나쁘게 여기지 말아 달라."], ["Think no unfair evil of her, pray: she had no wicked plots, nothing sordid or mercenary; in fact, she never thought of money except as something necessary which other people would always provide.", "그녀에게는 사악한 음모도, 더럽거나 돈을 노리는 마음도 없었다. 사실 그녀는 돈이란 다른 사람들이 언제나 마련해 줄 어떤 필요한 것 정도로밖에는 생각하지 않았다."], ["She was not in the habit of devising falsehoods, and if her statements were no direct clew to fact, why, they were not intended in that light—they were among her elegant accomplishments, intended to please.", "그녀는 거짓을 꾸며 내는 버릇도 없었고, 설령 그녀의 말이 사실에 대한 직접적인 단서가 되지 못했다 한들, 그것은 본디 그런 의도로 한 말이 아니었으니, 그것은 그녀의 우아한 재주들 가운데 하나로서 남을 즐겁게 하려는 것이었다."], ["Nature had inspired many arts in finishing Mrs. Lemon’s favorite pupil, who by general consent (Fred’s excepted) was a rare compound of beauty, cleverness, and amiability.", "자연은 레먼 부인이 아끼던 제자를 완성하는 데 수많은 기예를 불어넣었으니, 그녀는 (프레드의 의견만 빼면) 모두가 인정하듯 아름다움과 영리함과 상냥함이 어우러진 보기 드문 결합체였다."]], [["Lydgate found it more and more agreeable to be with her, and there was no constraint now, there was a delightful interchange of influence in their eyes, and what they said had that superfluity of meaning for them, which is observable with some sense of flatness by a third person; still they had no interviews or asides from which a third person need have been excluded.", "리드게이트는 그녀와 함께 있는 것이 점점 더 흐뭇해졌고, 이제는 아무런 거북함도 없이 그들의 눈빛 속에서 영향을 주고받는 즐거운 교환이 있었으며, 그들이 나누는 말에는 제삼자가 다소 맥없게 느낄 만한 그런 넘쳐 나는 의미가 담겨 있었다."], ["In fact, they flirted; and Lydgate was secure in the belief that they did nothing else.", "그래도 제삼자를 굳이 배제해야 할 만한 만남이나 귓속말 같은 것은 없었다."], ["If a man could not love and be wise, surely he could flirt and be wise at the same time? Really, the men in Middlemarch, except Mr.", "사실 그들은 시시덕거렸을 뿐이고, 리드게이트는 자기들이 그 이상 아무것도 하지 않는다는 믿음 속에서 안심하고 있었다."], ["Farebrother, were great bores, and Lydgate did not care about commercial politics or cards: what was he to do for relaxation?", "한 남자가 사랑을 하면서 동시에 지혜로울 수 없다면, 적어도 시시덕거리면서 동시에 지혜로울 수는 있지 않겠는가?"], ["He was often invited to the Bulstrodes’; but the girls there were hardly out of the schoolroom; and Mrs.", "정말이지 미들마치의 남자들은 페어브러더 씨를 빼면 지독히 따분한 자들이었고, 리드게이트는 상업 정치나 카드놀이에는 관심이 없었으니, 휴식을 위해 무엇을 한단 말인가?"], ["Bulstrode’s naive way of conciliating piety and worldliness, the nothingness of this life and the desirability of cut glass, the consciousness at once of filthy rags and the best damask, was not a sufficient relief from the weight of her husband’s invariable seriousness.", "그는 종종 불스트로드 댁에 초대받았다. 그러나 그 집 처녀들은 아직 교실을 갓 벗어났을 뿐이었고, 신앙과 세속, 이 삶의 덧없음과 컷글라스의 탐스러움, 더러운 누더기와 최고급 다마스크천을 동시에 의식하는 불스트로드 부인의 순진한 화해 방식은, 한결같이 엄숙한 그녀 남편의 무게로부터 충분한 위안이 되지 못했다."], ["The Vincys’ house, with all its faults, was the pleasanter by contrast; besides, it nourished Rosamond—sweet to look at as a half-opened blush-rose, and adorned with accomplishments for the refined amusement of man.", "온갖 결점에도 불구하고 빈시 씨네 집은 그에 비하면 더 유쾌했다. 게다가 그 집은 로저먼드를 길러 내고 있었으니, 그녀는 반쯤 핀 발그레한 장미처럼 바라보기에 사랑스러웠고, 남자의 세련된 즐거움을 위한 갖가지 재주로 단장되어 있었다."]], [["But he made some enemies, other than medical, by his success with Miss Vincy.", "그러나 그는 빈시 양에게 거둔 성공으로 의료계 외에도 몇몇 적을 만들었다."], ["One evening he came into the drawing-room rather late, when several other visitors were there.", "어느 날 저녁 그는 다른 손님 몇 명이 와 있을 때 다소 늦게 응접실로 들어섰다."], ["The card-table had drawn off the elders, and Mr. Ned Plymdale (one of the good matches in Middlemarch, though not one of its leading minds) was in tête-à-tête with Rosamond.", "카드 탁자가 어른들을 끌어가 버려서, 네드 플림데일 씨(미들마치에서 손꼽히는 좋은 신랑감 가운데 하나였으나 손꼽히는 지성은 아니었다)가 로저먼드와 단둘이 마주 앉아 있었다."], ["He had brought the last “Keepsake,” the gorgeous watered-silk publication which marked modern progress at that time; and he considered himself very fortunate that he could be the first to look over it with her, dwelling on the ladies and gentlemen with shiny copper-plate cheeks and copper-plate smiles, and pointing to comic verses as capital and sentimental stories as interesting.", "그는 그 무렵 현대적 진보를 보여 주던 호화로운 물결무늬 비단 장정의 간행물인 최신호 「키프세이크」를 가져왔는데, 자기가 그것을 그녀와 함께 처음으로 들여다볼 수 있게 된 것을 무척 다행으로 여기며, 윤기 나는 동판화 같은 뺨과 동판화 같은 미소를 띤 신사 숙녀들에 대해 이런저런 말을 늘어놓고, 익살스러운 시구를 일품이라 가리키며 감상적인 이야기들을 흥미롭다고 가리켰다."], ["Rosamond was gracious, and Mr.", "로저먼드는 상냥했고, 네드 씨는 자기가 \"구애를 하는\""], ["Ned was satisfied that he had the very best thing in art and literature as a medium for “paying addresses”—the very thing to please a nice girl.", "수단으로서 예술과 문학에서 최고의 것을, 그러니까 멋진 처녀를 즐겁게 할 바로 그것을 손에 쥐고 있다고 흡족해했다."], ["He had also reasons, deep rather than ostensible, for being satisfied with his own appearance.", "그는 또 자신의 외모에 만족할 만한, 겉으로 드러난다기보다는 깊은 이유들도 가지고 있었다."], ["To superficial observers his chin had too vanishing an aspect, looking as if it were being gradually reabsorbed.", "겉만 보는 사람들에게는 그의 턱이 너무 희미한 데가 있어서, 마치 차츰 도로 빨려 들어가는 듯이 보였다."], ["And it did indeed cause him some difficulty about the fit of his satin stocks, for which chins were at that time useful.", "그리고 실제로 그것 때문에 그는 새틴 넥타이를 매는 데 다소 애를 먹었으니, 그 시절에는 넥타이를 매는 데 턱이 요긴했던 것이다."]], [["“I think the Honorable Mrs.", "\"제 생각엔 S."], ["S. is something like you,”", "부인 각하가 당신과 좀 닮은 것 같아요.\""], ["said Mr. Ned.", "네드 씨가 말했다."], ["He kept the book open at the bewitching portrait, and looked at it rather languishingly.", "그는 그 매혹적인 초상화가 펼쳐진 채로 책을 들고서, 다소 애틋하게 그것을 바라보았다."]], [["“Her back is very large; she seems to have sat for that,”", "\"등이 아주 넓은걸요. 저 자세로 앉아 있었나 봐요.\""], ["said Rosamond, not meaning any satire, but thinking how red young Plymdale’s hands were, and wondering why Lydgate did not come.", "로저먼드는 무슨 풍자를 의도한 것은 아니었으나, 젊은 플림데일의 손이 어쩌면 저렇게 붉은가 생각하고 리드게이트는 왜 오지 않을까 궁금해하며 그렇게 말했다."], ["She went on with her tatting all the while.", "그녀는 그러는 내내 태팅 뜨개질을 계속하고 있었다."]], [["“I did not say she was as beautiful as you are,”", "\"그분이 당신만큼 아름답다고 말한 건 아니에요.\""], ["said Mr. Ned, venturing to look from the portrait to its rival.", "네드 씨는 그 초상화에서 그 경쟁자에게로 감히 눈길을 옮기며 말했다."]], [["“I suspect you of being an adroit flatterer,”", "\"제 보기엔 능란한 아첨꾼이신 것 같은데요.\""], ["said Rosamond, feeling sure that she should have to reject this young gentleman a second time.", "로저먼드는 이 젊은 신사를 두 번째로 거절해야 하리라는 것을 확신하며 말했다."]], [["But now Lydgate came in; the book was closed before he reached Rosamond’s corner, and as he took his seat with easy confidence on the other side of her, young Plymdale’s jaw fell like a barometer towards the cheerless side of change.", "그러나 이제 리드게이트가 들어왔다. 그가 로저먼드의 자리에 이르기 전에 책은 덮였고, 그가 편안한 자신감으로 그녀의 반대편에 자리를 잡자, 젊은 플림데일의 턱은 흐린 날을 향해 떨어지는 기압계처럼 내려앉았다."], ["Rosamond enjoyed not only Lydgate’s presence but its effect: she liked to excite jealousy.", "로저먼드는 리드게이트가 와 있다는 사실뿐 아니라 그 효과까지도 즐겼다. 그녀는 질투를 불러일으키는 것을 좋아했다."]], [["“What a late comer you are!”", "\"정말 늦게도 오셨네요!\""], ["she said, as they shook hands.", "두 사람이 악수하는 동안 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Mamma had given you up a little while ago.", "\"엄마는 조금 전에 당신이 안 오는 줄 알고 단념하셨어요."], ["How do you find Fred?”", "프레드는 어떤가요?\""]], [["“As usual; going on well, but slowly.", "\"여전합니다. 잘 회복되고 있지만 더디지요."], ["I want him to go away—to Stone Court, for example.", "그를 떠나보내고 싶은데, 이를테면 스톤 코트로요."], ["But your mamma seems to have some objection.”", "그런데 어머님께서 좀 반대하시는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Poor fellow!”", "\"가엾은 오빠!\""], ["said Rosamond, prettily.", "로저먼드가 어여쁘게 말했다."], ["“You will see Fred so changed,”", "\"프레드가 무척 달라진 걸 보시게 될 거예요.\""], ["she added, turning to the other suitor; “we have looked to Mr.", "그녀는 다른 구혼자 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다."], ["Lydgate as our guardian angel during this illness.”", "\"이번에 병을 앓는 동안 저희는 리드게이트 씨를 수호천사로 여겨 왔답니다.\""]], [["Mr. Ned smiled nervously, while Lydgate, drawing the “Keepsake” towards him and opening it, gave a short scornful laugh and tossed up his chin, as if in wonderment at human folly.", "네드 씨는 초조하게 미소 지었고, 그동안 리드게이트는 「키프세이크」를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 펼치고는 짧고 경멸 어린 웃음을 지으며, 인간의 어리석음에 놀라기라도 한 듯 턱을 치켜올렸다."]], [["“What are you laughing at so profanely?”", "\"뭘 그렇게 불경스럽게 비웃으시는 거예요?\""], ["said Rosamond, with bland neutrality.", "로저먼드가 부드러운 중립의 태도로 말했다."]], [["“I wonder which would turn out to be the silliest—the engravings or the writing here,”", "\"여기 있는 판화하고 글 중에 어느 쪽이 더 어리석은지 모르겠군요.\""], ["said Lydgate, in his most convinced tone, while he turned over the pages quickly, seeming to see all through the book in no time, and showing his large white hands to much advantage, as Rosamond thought.", "리드게이트는 더없이 확신에 찬 어조로 말하며, 책장을 빠르게 넘겨 순식간에 책 전체를 다 들여다보는 듯하면서, 로저먼드가 보기에 자기의 크고 흰 손을 한껏 돋보이게 내보였다."], ["“Do look at this bridegroom coming out of church: did you ever see such a ‘sugared invention’—as the Elizabethans used to say?", "\"교회에서 나오는 이 신랑 좀 보세요. 엘리자베스 시대 사람들 말마따나 이렇게 '설탕을 입힌 고안물'을 본 적 있으세요?"], ["Did any haberdasher ever look so smirking?", "잡화상이라도 이렇게 능글맞게 웃었을까요?"], ["Yet I will answer for it the story makes him one of the first gentlemen in the land.”", "그런데도 장담컨대 이 이야기는 그를 이 나라 으뜸가는 신사들 가운데 하나로 만들어 놓았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“You are so severe, I am frightened at you,”", "\"어쩜 그렇게 매정하세요, 무서울 지경이에요.\""], ["said Rosamond, keeping her amusement duly moderate.", "로저먼드는 자기의 재미를 알맞게 누그러뜨리며 말했다."], ["Poor young Plymdale had lingered with admiration over this very engraving, and his spirit was stirred.", "가엾은 젊은 플림데일은 바로 이 판화를 감탄하며 들여다보고 있던 참이라, 그의 심기가 발끈했다."]], [["“There are a great many celebrated people writing in the ‘Keepsake,’ at all events,”", "\"어쨌든 「키프세이크」에는 유명한 사람들이 많이 글을 쓰는데요.\""], ["he said, in a tone at once piqued and timid.", "그는 발끈하면서도 머뭇거리는 어조로 말했다."], ["“This is the first time I have heard it called silly.”", "\"이게 어리석다는 말은 처음 듣습니다.\""]], [["“I think I shall turn round on you and accuse you of being a Goth,”", "\"저는 거꾸로 당신한테 따져서 야만인이라고 몰아세울까 봐요.\""], ["said Rosamond, looking at Lydgate with a smile.", "로저먼드가 미소를 띠고 리드게이트를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I suspect you know nothing about Lady Blessington and L. E.", "\"당신은 블레싱턴 부인이나 L."], ["L.”", "E."], ["Rosamond herself was not without relish for these writers, but she did not readily commit herself by admiration, and was alive to the slightest hint that anything was not, according to Lydgate, in the very highest taste.", "L.에 대해 아무것도 모르실 것 같은데요.\" 로저먼드 자신도 이런 작가들을 즐기지 않는 것은 아니었지만, 쉽사리 감탄을 드러내어 스스로를 드러내지 않았고, 무언가가 리드게이트의 기준에서 최고의 취향이 아니라는 아주 작은 기미에도 민감했다."]], [["“But Sir Walter Scott—I suppose Mr. Lydgate knows him,”", "\"하지만 월터 스콧 경이라면, 리드게이트 씨도 알고 계시겠지요.\""], ["said young Plymdale, a little cheered by this advantage.", "젊은 플림데일이 이 우세에 다소 기운을 차리며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I read no literature now,”", "\"오, 저는 이제 문학이라곤 읽지 않습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, shutting the book, and pushing it away.", "리드게이트가 책을 덮어 밀쳐 놓으며 말했다."], ["“I read so much when I was a lad, that I suppose it will last me all my life.", "\"소년 시절에 어찌나 많이 읽었던지, 그것으로 평생 우려먹을 것 같군요."], ["I used to know Scott’s poems by heart.”", "한때는 스콧의 시를 줄줄 외웠지요.\""]], [["“I should like to know when you left off,”", "\"언제 그만두셨는지 알고 싶네요.\""], ["said Rosamond, “because then I might be sure that I knew something which you did not know.”", "로저먼드가 말했다. \"그래야 당신이 모르는 무언가를 제가 안다고 확신할 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Mr. Lydgate would say that was not worth knowing,”", "\"리드게이트 씨라면 그런 건 알 가치도 없다고 하실걸요.\""], ["said Mr. Ned, purposely caustic.", "네드 씨가 일부러 가시 돋친 말을 했다."]], [["“On the contrary,”", "\"천만에요.\""], ["said Lydgate, showing no smart; but smiling with exasperating confidence at Rosamond.", "리드게이트는 아무런 자극도 받지 않은 채, 그러나 로저먼드를 향해 약 오를 만큼 자신만만하게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“It would be worth knowing by the fact that Miss Vincy could tell it me.”", "\"빈시 양이 제게 알려 줄 수 있다는 사실만으로도 알 가치가 있겠지요.\""]], [["Young Plymdale soon went to look at the whist-playing, thinking that Lydgate was one of the most conceited, unpleasant fellows it had ever been his ill-fortune to meet.", "젊은 플림데일은 이내 휘스트 판을 구경하러 갔는데, 리드게이트야말로 자기가 운수 사납게 만나 본 가장 자만심 강하고 불쾌한 작자들 가운데 하나라고 생각하면서였다."]], [["“How rash you are!”", "\"어쩜 그렇게 경솔하세요!\""], ["said Rosamond, inwardly delighted.", "로저먼드가 속으로는 기뻐하며 말했다."], ["“Do you see that you have given offence?”", "\"당신이 기분을 상하게 한 거 아세요?\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["is it Mr. Plymdale’s book?", "그게 플림데일 씨 책이었나요?"], ["I am sorry.", "미안하군요."], ["I didn’t think about it.”", "미처 생각을 못 했습니다.\""]], [["“I shall begin to admit what you said of yourself when you first came here—that you are a bear, and want teaching by the birds.”", "\"당신이 여기 처음 왔을 때 자신에 대해 한 말을 이제 인정하기 시작해야겠어요. 당신은 곰이라서 새들한테 가르침을 받아야 한다고 했잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, there is a bird who can teach me what she will.", "\"글쎄요, 마음대로 절 가르칠 수 있는 새가 한 마리 있긴 하지요."], ["Don’t I listen to her willingly?”", "제가 그 새의 말을 기꺼이 듣고 있지 않습니까?\""]], [["To Rosamond it seemed as if she and Lydgate were as good as engaged.", "로저먼드에게는 자기와 리드게이트가 거의 약혼한 사이나 다름없는 듯이 여겨졌다."], ["That they were some time to be engaged had long been an idea in her mind; and ideas, we know, tend to a more solid kind of existence, the necessary materials being at hand.", "언젠가 두 사람이 약혼하리라는 것은 오래전부터 그녀의 마음속에 자리 잡은 생각이었고, 우리도 알다시피, 필요한 재료가 갖춰져 있으면 생각이란 더 단단한 종류의 존재로 나아가는 법이다."], ["It is true, Lydgate had the counter-idea of remaining unengaged; but this was a mere negative, a shadow cast by other resolves which themselves were capable of shrinking.", "사실 리드게이트에게는 약혼하지 않은 채로 있겠다는 반대되는 생각이 있었다. 그러나 이것은 한낱 부정적인 것, 그 자체로 줄어들 수 있는 다른 결심들이 드리운 그림자에 불과했다."], ["Circumstance was almost sure to be on the side of Rosamond’s idea, which had a shaping activity and looked through watchful blue eyes, whereas Lydgate’s lay blind and unconcerned as a jelly-fish which gets melted without knowing it.", "정황은 거의 틀림없이 로저먼드의 생각 편에 서 있었으니, 그것은 형성하는 활동을 지녔고 주의 깊은 푸른 눈으로 내다보고 있었던 반면, 리드게이트의 생각은 자기도 모르는 사이에 녹아 버리는 해파리처럼 눈멀고 무심하게 놓여 있었다."]], [["That evening when he went home, he looked at his phials to see how a process of maceration was going on, with undisturbed interest; and he wrote out his daily notes with as much precision as usual.", "그날 저녁 집으로 돌아갔을 때, 그는 침연 과정이 어떻게 진행되고 있는지 자기의 약병들을 들여다보며 흔들림 없는 흥미를 느꼈고, 여느 때와 다름없이 정확하게 그날의 기록을 적어 내려갔다."], ["The reveries from which it was difficult for him to detach himself were ideal constructions of something else than Rosamond’s virtues, and the primitive tissue was still his fair unknown.", "그가 좀처럼 떨쳐 내기 어려웠던 몽상은 로저먼드의 미덕이 아닌 다른 무언가에 대한 이상적인 구상이었고, 원시 조직이 여전히 그의 아리따운 미지의 대상이었다."], ["Moreover, he was beginning to feel some zest for the growing though half-suppressed feud between him and the other medical men, which was likely to become more manifest, now that Bulstrode’s method of managing the new hospital was about to be declared; and there were various inspiriting signs that his non-acceptance by some of Peacock’s patients might be counterbalanced by the impression he had produced in other quarters.", "더구나 그는 자기와 다른 의료인들 사이에서 자라나는, 반쯤 억눌린 그 반목에도 어떤 흥미를 느끼기 시작하고 있었는데, 그 반목은 불스트로드가 새 병원을 운영하는 방식이 곧 발표되려는 마당이라 한층 분명해질 듯했다. 그리고 피콕의 환자들 가운데 일부가 그를 받아들이지 않는 것이, 다른 곳에서 그가 남긴 인상으로 상쇄될지 모른다는 여러 고무적인 조짐도 있었다."], ["Only a few days later, when he had happened to overtake Rosamond on the Lowick road and had got down from his horse to walk by her side until he had quite protected her from a passing drove, he had been stopped by a servant on horseback with a message calling him in to a house of some importance where Peacock had never attended; and it was the second instance of this kind.", "불과 며칠 뒤, 로윅 길에서 우연히 로저먼드를 따라잡아 말에서 내려, 지나가는 가축 떼로부터 그녀를 완전히 보호할 때까지 그녀의 곁에서 함께 걷고 있었는데, 말을 탄 어느 하인이 그를 불러 세우더니 피콕이 한 번도 진료한 적 없는 어느 지체 높은 집으로 와 달라는 전갈을 전했다. 그리고 그것은 이런 일이 두 번째였다."], ["The servant was Sir James Chettam’s, and the house was Lowick Manor.", "그 하인은 제임스 체텀 경의 하인이었고, 그 집은 로윅 저택이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXVIII.", "# CHAPTER XXVIII."]], [["1st Gent.", "첫 번째 신사."], ["All times are good to seek your wedded home Bringing a mutual delight.", "결혼한 보금자리를 찾아가는 데에 좋지 않은 때란 없으니\n서로에게 기쁨을 가져다주는 일이로다."]], [["2d Gent. Why, true.", "두 번째 신사."], ["The calendar hath not an evil day For souls made one by love, and even death Were sweetness, if it came like rolling waves While they two clasped each other, and foresaw No life apart.", "옳거니, 참으로 그러하오. 사랑으로 하나 된 영혼들에게"]], [["Mr. and Mrs. Casaubon, returning from their wedding journey, arrived at Lowick Manor in the middle of January. A light snow was falling as they descended at the door, and in the morning, when Dorothea passed from her dressing-room into the blue-green boudoir that we know of, she saw the long avenue of limes lifting their trunks from a white earth, and spreading white branches against the dun and motionless sky. The distant flat shrank in uniform whiteness and low-hanging uniformity of cloud. The very furniture in the room seemed to have shrunk since she saw it before: the stag in the tapestry looked more like a ghost in his ghostly blue-green world; the volumes of polite literature in the bookcase looked more like immovable imitations of books. The bright fire of dry oak-boughs burning on the logs seemed an incongruous renewal of life and glow—like the figure of Dorothea herself as she entered carrying the red-leather cases containing the cameos for Celia.", "달력에 흉한 날이란 없으며, 죽음조차도\n둘이 서로를 끌어안은 채"]], [["She was glowing from her morning toilet as only healthful youth can glow: there was gem-like brightness on her coiled hair and in her hazel eyes; there was warm red life in her lips; her throat had a breathing whiteness above the differing white of the fur which itself seemed to wind about her neck and cling down her blue-gray pelisse with a tenderness gathered from her own, a sentient commingled innocence which kept its loveliness against the crystalline purity of the outdoor snow. As she laid the cameo-cases on the table in the bow-window, she unconsciously kept her hands on them, immediately absorbed in looking out on the still, white enclosure which made her visible world.", "헤어진 삶이란 없으리라 내다보는 동안\n굽이치는 파도처럼 다가온다면, 한낱 달콤함이리라."]], [["Mr. Casaubon, who had risen early complaining of palpitation, was in the library giving audience to his curate Mr.", "캐소본 부부는 신혼여행에서 돌아와 1월 중순에 로윅 영지에 다다랐다."], ["Tucker. By-and-by Celia would come in her quality of bridesmaid as well as sister, and through the next weeks there would be wedding visits received and given; all in continuance of that transitional life understood to correspond with the excitement of bridal felicity, and keeping up the sense of busy ineffectiveness, as of a dream which the dreamer begins to suspect.", "그들이 문 앞에서 마차에서 내릴 때 가벼운 눈발이 흩날리고 있었고, 아침에 도로시아가 화장실에서 우리가 익히 아는 그 청록색 내실로 건너갔을 때, 그녀는 보리수의 긴 가로수길이 하얀 대지에서 줄기를 일으켜 세우고 거무스름하니 미동도 없는 하늘을 향해 하얀 가지를 펼치고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The duties of her married life, contemplated as so great beforehand, seemed to be shrinking with the furniture and the white vapor-walled landscape.", "멀리 펼쳐진 평지는 한결같은 흰빛과 낮게 드리운 한결같은 구름 속으로 움츠러들어 있었다. 방 안의 가구마저 전에 보았을 때보다 줄어든 것 같았다."], ["The clear heights where she expected to walk in full communion had become difficult to see even in her imagination; the delicious repose of the soul on a complete superior had been shaken into uneasy effort and alarmed with dim presentiment.", "태피스트리 속의 수사슴은 그 으스스한 청록색 세계 속에서 한층 더 유령처럼 보였고, 책장에 꽂힌 우아한 문학 전집들은 한층 더 옮길 수 없는 책의 모조품처럼 보였다."], ["When would the days begin of that active wifely devotion which was to strengthen her husband’s life and exalt her own? Never perhaps, as she had preconceived them; but somehow—still somehow.", "장작 위에서 타오르는 마른 떡갈나무 가지의 환한 불은 어울리지 않게 되살아난 생명과 광채처럼 보였으니, 그것은 마치 실리아에게 줄 카메오가 든 붉은 가죽 상자들을 들고 들어서는 도로시아 자신의 모습과도 같았다."], ["In this solemnly pledged union of her life, duty would present itself in some new form of inspiration and give a new meaning to wifely love.", "* 카메오: 마노·조개껍데기 따위에 양각으로 형상을 새긴 장식용 보석."]], [["Meanwhile there was the snow and the low arch of dun vapor—there was the stifling oppression of that gentlewoman’s world, where everything was done for her and none asked for her aid—where the sense of connection with a manifold pregnant existence had to be kept up painfully as an inward vision, instead of coming from without in claims that would have shaped her energies.— “What shall I do?” “Whatever you please, my dear:” that had been her brief history since she had left off learning morning lessons and practising silly rhythms on the hated piano. Marriage, which was to bring guidance into worthy and imperative occupation, had not yet freed her from the gentlewoman’s oppressive liberty: it had not even filled her leisure with the ruminant joy of unchecked tenderness. Her blooming full-pulsed youth stood there in a moral imprisonment which made itself one with the chill, colorless, narrowed landscape, with the shrunken furniture, the never-read books, and the ghostly stag in a pale fantastic world that seemed to be vanishing from the daylight.", "그녀는 건강한 젊음만이 빛낼 수 있는 그런 빛으로 아침 단장에서 빛나고 있었다. 틀어 올린 머리칼과 담갈색 눈동자에는 보석 같은 광채가 있었고, 입술에는 따뜻하고 붉은 생기가 감돌았다. 모피의 흰빛과는 또 다른 흰빛 위로 그녀의 목은 숨 쉬듯 희게 드러나 있었는데, 그 모피 자체가 그녀의 목을 휘감고 청회색 외투를 따라 흘러내리며, 그녀에게서 모아 온 듯한 부드러움을, 바깥 눈의 수정 같은 순결함에 맞서 그 사랑스러움을 지키는 감성 어린 순진함을 한데 어우러뜨리고 있는 것 같았다. 그녀는 활 모양 창가의 탁자에 카메오 상자들을 내려놓으면서, 무심코 그 위에 손을 얹은 채, 곧장 자신의 눈에 보이는 세계를 이루는 고요하고 하얀 울안을 내다보는 데 몰입했다. 가슴이 두근거린다고 호소하며 일찍 일어난 캐소본 씨는 서재에서 부목사 터커 씨를 맞이하고 있었다. 곧 실리아가 누이로서뿐 아니라 신부 들러리의 자격으로 찾아올 터였고, 다음 몇 주 동안에는 결혼 인사 방문을 주고받게 될 터였다. 이 모든 것이 신혼의 행복이 주는 흥분에 어울린다고 여겨지는 그 과도기적 삶의 연속이었으며, 꿈꾸는 자가 차츰 의심을 품기 시작하는 꿈처럼, 분주하지만 헛된 느낌을 이어 가는 것이었다. 미리 그토록 위대하게 그려 보았던 결혼 생활의 의무들은 가구며 흰 안개의 벽으로 둘러싸인 풍경과 더불어 움츠러드는 것 같았다. 그녀가 온전한 교감 속에 거닐기를 기대했던 그 맑은 높은 곳은 이제 상상 속에서조차 보기가 어려워졌고, 우월한 존재에게 온전히 기대어 누리리라던 영혼의 감미로운 안식은 불안한 노력으로 흔들리고 어렴풋한 예감으로 인해 놀라움에 사로잡혀 있었다. 남편의 삶을 굳건하게 하고 자기 자신의 삶을 드높여 줄 그 적극적인 아내로서의 헌신, 그 나날은 언제 시작될 것인가? 어쩌면 그녀가 미리 그려 본 모습 그대로는 결코 시작되지 않을지도 모른다. 하지만 어떻게든, 그래도 어떻게든. 엄숙하게 맹세한 이 삶의 결합 속에서, 의무는 어떤 새로운 영감의 형태로 모습을 드러내며 아내의 사랑에 새로운 의미를 부여할 것이었다."]], [["In the first minutes when Dorothea looked out she felt nothing but the dreary oppression; then came a keen remembrance, and turning away from the window she walked round the room.", "그러는 동안에도 눈이 있었고 거무스름한 안개의 나지막한 둥근 천장이 있었으며, 그 귀부인의 세계가 주는 숨 막히는 압박이 있었으니, 그곳에서는 모든 일이 그녀를 위해 처리되었고 누구도 그녀의 도움을 청하지 않았다. 그곳에서는 다채롭고 의미로 가득 찬 존재와의 연결감을, 그녀의 기운을 빚어 주었을 외부로부터의 요구로서 받는 대신, 내면의 환영으로서 애써 고통스럽게 지켜 가야 했다."], ["The ideas and hopes which were living in her mind when she first saw this room nearly three months before were present now only as memories: she judged them as we judge transient and departed things.", "\"내가 무엇을 하면 될까요?\" \"무엇이든 그대 좋을 대로 하시오, 여보.\""], ["All existence seemed to beat with a lower pulse than her own, and her religious faith was a solitary cry, the struggle out of a nightmare in which every object was withering and shrinking away from her.", "이것이 그녀가 아침 공부를 그만두고 그 지긋지긋한 피아노로 어리석은 리듬을 연습하는 일을 그만둔 이래의 짧은 내력이었다. 가치 있고 절실한 일거리로 인도해 주리라던 결혼은 아직 그녀를 귀부인의 답답한 자유로부터 풀어 주지 못했다."], ["Each remembered thing in the room was disenchanted, was deadened as an unlit transparency, till her wandering gaze came to the group of miniatures, and there at last she saw something which had gathered new breath and meaning: it was the miniature of Mr.", "결혼은 그녀의 한가로움을 거리낌 없는 다정함이 주는 되새김의 기쁨으로 채워 주지조차 못했다. 활짝 피어나 힘차게 고동치는 그녀의 젊음은, 차갑고 빛깔 없이 좁아진 풍경과, 움츠러든 가구와, 한 번도 읽히지 않은 책들과, 환한 대낮으로부터 사라져 가는 듯한 창백하고 기괴한 세계 속의 유령 같은 수사슴과 하나가 되어 버린, 그러한 도덕적 감금 속에 서 있었다."], ["Casaubon’s aunt Julia, who had made the unfortunate marriage—of Will Ladislaw’s grandmother.", "도로시아가 바깥을 내다본 처음 몇 분 동안 그녀는 음울한 압박감만을 느꼈다. 그러다 날카로운 기억이 떠올랐고, 그녀는 창에서 몸을 돌려 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌았다."], ["Dorothea could fancy that it was alive now—the delicate woman’s face which yet had a headstrong look, a peculiarity difficult to interpret.", "거의 석 달 전에 이 방을 처음 보았을 때 그녀의 마음속에 살아 있던 생각과 희망들은 이제 한낱 기억으로서만 남아 있을 따름이었다. 그녀는 그것들을, 우리가 덧없이 지나가 버린 것들을 평가하듯이 판단했다."], ["Was it only her friends who thought her marriage unfortunate?", "모든 존재가 그녀 자신보다 더 낮은 맥박으로 고동치는 것 같았고, 그녀의 종교적 신앙은 고독한 외침이었으며, 모든 사물이 시들어 그녀에게서 움츠러들며 떨어져 나가는 악몽으로부터 벗어나려는 몸부림이었다."], ["or did she herself find it out to be a mistake, and taste the salt bitterness of her tears in the merciful silence of the night?", "방 안에서 기억나는 사물 하나하나가 마법이 풀린 채, 불 꺼진 투명체처럼 생기를 잃어 갔는데, 마침내 헤매던 그녀의 시선이 작은 초상화들이 모인 곳에 이르자, 거기서 비로소 새로운 숨결과 의미를 머금은 무언가를 보았다. 그것은 불행한 결혼을 했던 캐소본 씨의 고모 줄리아의 세밀화, 윌 래디슬로의 외할머니의 초상화였다."], ["What breadths of experience Dorothea seemed to have passed over since she first looked at this miniature!", "도로시아는 그것이 지금 살아 있다고 상상할 수 있을 것 같았다. 그 섬세한 여인의 얼굴은 그러면서도 고집스러운 데가 있었는데, 그것은 해석하기 어려운 특이함이었다."], ["She felt a new companionship with it, as if it had an ear for her and could see how she was looking at it.", "그녀의 결혼을 불행하다고 여긴 것은 다만 그녀의 친구들뿐이었을까? 아니면 그녀 자신도 그것이 잘못임을 깨닫고, 자비로운 밤의 침묵 속에서 짠 눈물의 쓰디씀을 맛보았을까?"], ["Here was a woman who had known some difficulty about marriage.", "도로시아가 이 세밀화를 처음 본 이래 얼마나 넓은 경험을 지나온 것 같았던가!"], ["Nay, the colors deepened, the lips and chin seemed to get larger, the hair and eyes seemed to be sending out light, the face was masculine and beamed on her with that full gaze which tells her on whom it falls that she is too interesting for the slightest movement of her eyelid to pass unnoticed and uninterpreted.", "그녀는 그것에 대해 새로운 동반자 의식을 느꼈으니, 마치 그것이 그녀의 말에 귀 기울일 수 있고 그녀가 그것을 어떻게 바라보고 있는지 볼 수 있기라도 한 듯했다. 여기 결혼에 대해 어떤 어려움을 겪어 본 여인이 있었다."], ["The vivid presentation came like a pleasant glow to Dorothea: she felt herself smiling, and turning from the miniature sat down and looked up as if she were again talking to a figure in front of her.", "아니, 빛깔이 짙어지고, 입술과 턱이 더 커지는 것 같았으며, 머리칼과 눈은 빛을 내뿜는 것 같았고, 얼굴은 남성적이었으며, 시선이 닿는 이로 하여금 자신이 너무도 흥미로운 존재여서 눈꺼풀의 아주 미세한 움직임 하나도 알아채지 못한 채 해석되지 않고 지나칠 리 없음을 일러 주는 그런 충만한 응시로 그녀를 비추었다. 그 생생한 현현은 도로시아에게 기분 좋은 온기처럼 다가왔다."], ["But the smile disappeared as she went on meditating, and at last she said aloud—", "그녀는 자신이 미소 짓고 있음을 느꼈고, 세밀화에서 몸을 돌려 자리에 앉아, 마치 자기 앞에 있는 사람과 다시 이야기를 나누기라도 하듯 위를 올려다보았다. 하지만 골똘히 생각을 이어 가면서 미소는 사라졌고, 마침내 그녀는 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it was cruel to speak so!", "\"오, 그렇게 말하다니 잔인했어!"], ["How sad—how dreadful!”", "어쩌면 그리도 슬픈가, 그리도 끔찍한가!\""]], [["She rose quickly and went out of the room, hurrying along the corridor, with the irresistible impulse to go and see her husband and inquire if she could do anything for him. Perhaps Mr.", "그녀는 재빨리 일어나 방에서 나가, 남편에게 가서 그를 위해 무언가 할 수 있는 일이 없는지 묻고 싶은 억누를 수 없는 충동에 사로잡혀 복도를 서둘러 걸어갔다."], ["Tucker was gone and Mr. Casaubon was alone in the library.", "어쩌면 터커 씨는 떠났고 캐소본 씨가 서재에 홀로 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["She felt as if all her morning’s gloom would vanish if she could see her husband glad because of her presence.", "자신의 존재로 인해 남편이 기뻐하는 모습을 볼 수만 있다면 아침의 우울이 모두 사라져 버릴 것 같았다."]], [["But when she reached the head of the dark oak there was Celia coming up, and below there was Mr. Brooke, exchanging welcomes and congratulations with Mr. Casaubon.", "하지만 그녀가 검은 떡갈나무 계단 위에 이르렀을 때, 실리아가 올라오고 있었고, 아래쪽에서는 브룩 씨가 캐소본 씨와 환영과 축하의 인사를 주고받고 있었다."]], [["“Dodo!”", "\"도도!\""], ["said Celia, in her quiet staccato; then kissed her sister, whose arms encircled her, and said no more.", "실리아가 특유의 조용하고 또박또박한 말투로 말했다. 그러고는 두 팔로 자신을 감싸 안는 언니에게 입을 맞추고는, 더는 말이 없었다."], ["I think they both cried a little in a furtive manner, while Dorothea ran down-stairs to greet her uncle.", "내 생각엔 도로시아가 삼촌을 맞으러 아래층으로 달려가는 동안, 두 사람 다 남몰래 조금씩 운 것 같았다."]], [["“I need not ask how you are, my dear,”", "\"어떻게 지냈는지 물을 필요도 없겠구나, 얘야.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, after kissing her forehead.", "브룩 씨가 그녀의 이마에 입을 맞추고 나서 말했다."], ["“Rome has agreed with you, I see—happiness, frescos, the antique—that sort of thing.", "\"로마가 네게 잘 맞은 모양이야. 보아하니, 행복이며, 프레스코화며, 고대 유물이며, 그런 것들 말이다."], ["Well, it’s very pleasant to have you back again, and you understand all about art now, eh?", "그래, 네가 다시 돌아오니 참으로 반갑구나, 그래 이제 예술에 대해 죄다 알게 됐겠지, 응?"], ["But Casaubon is a little pale, I tell him—a little pale, you know.", "그런데 캐소본은 좀 창백해, 내 그에게도 말했다만, 좀 창백해 보여, 알겠지."], ["Studying hard in his holidays is carrying it rather too far.", "휴가 중에 그토록 열심히 공부하는 건 좀 지나친 일이야."], ["I overdid it at one time”—Mr.", "나도 한때 무리한 적이 있었지.\""], ["Brooke still held Dorothea’s hand, but had turned his face to Mr.", "브룩 씨는 여전히 도로시아의 손을 잡은 채로, 얼굴은 캐소본 씨 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["Casaubon—“about topography, ruins, temples—I thought I had a clew, but I saw it would carry me too far, and nothing might come of it.", "\"지형이며, 유적이며, 신전이며, 실마리를 잡았다 싶었는데, 그게 나를 너무 멀리까지 끌고 갈 거고, 아무 결실도 없으리란 걸 알았지."], ["You may go any length in that sort of thing, and nothing may come of it, you know.”", "그런 일은 한정 없이 파고들 수 있지만, 아무 결실도 없을 수가 있어, 알겠나.\""]], [["Dorothea’s eyes also were turned up to her husband’s face with some anxiety at the idea that those who saw him afresh after absence might be aware of signs which she had not noticed.", "도로시아의 눈길 또한 남편의 얼굴을 향해 올라가 있었으니, 한동안 떨어져 있다가 새로이 그를 보는 사람들이 그녀가 알아채지 못한 어떤 징후를 알아챘을지도 모른다는 생각에 다소 불안했던 것이다."]], [["“Nothing to alarm you, my dear,” said Mr.", "\"놀랄 일은 없단다, 얘야.\""], ["Brooke, observing her expression.", "브룩 씨가 그녀의 표정을 살피며 말했다."], ["“A little English beef and mutton will soon make a difference.", "\"영국식 쇠고기와 양고기를 조금 먹으면 금세 달라질 게야."], ["It was all very well to look pale, sitting for the portrait of Aquinas, you know—we got your letter just in time.", "아퀴나스의 초상화를 위해 모델로 앉아 있으려면 창백한 게 다 좋았겠지만 말이다, 알겠지, 네 편지를 마침맞게 받았단다."], ["But Aquinas, now—he was a little too subtle, wasn’t he?", "그런데 아퀴나스라, 그자는 좀 지나치게 난해하지 않은가, 그렇지? 대체 누가 아퀴나스를 읽는단 말인가?\""], ["Does anybody read Aquinas?”", "* 아퀴나스: 13세기의 스콜라 철학자이자 신학자 토마스 아퀴나스."]], [["“He is not indeed an author adapted to superficial minds,”", "\"그는 참으로 피상적인 정신에 맞는 저술가는 아니지요.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, meeting these timely questions with dignified patience.", "캐소본 씨가 때마침 던져진 이 질문들을 위엄 있는 인내로 받아넘기며 말했다."]], [["“You would like coffee in your own room, uncle?”", "\"삼촌, 방으로 커피를 가져다 드리는 게 좋겠지요?\""], ["said Dorothea, coming to the rescue.", "도로시아가 거들고 나서며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; and you must go to Celia: she has great news to tell you, you know.", "\"그래. 너는 실리아에게 가 보아라."], ["I leave it all to her.”", "그 애가 네게 전할 굉장한 소식이 있단다, 알겠지. 다 그 애한테 맡기마.\""]], [["The blue-green boudoir looked much more cheerful when Celia was seated there in a pelisse exactly like her sister’s, surveying the cameos with a placid satisfaction, while the conversation passed on to other topics.", "실리아가 언니의 것과 똑같은 외투를 입고 그곳에 앉아, 차분한 만족감으로 카메오를 살펴보고 있고, 대화가 다른 화제로 옮겨 가니, 청록색 내실은 한결 더 밝아 보였다."]], [["“Do you think it nice to go to Rome on a wedding journey?”", "\"신혼여행으로 로마에 가는 게 좋다고 생각해, 언니?\""], ["said Celia, with her ready delicate blush which Dorothea was used to on the smallest occasions.", "실리아가 도로시아가 아주 사소한 일에도 익히 보아 온 그 곱고 발그레한 홍조를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“It would not suit all—not you, dear, for example,”", "\"모두에게 다 맞는 건 아니겠지, 가령 너한테는 안 맞을 거야, 얘야.\""], ["said Dorothea, quietly.", "도로시아가 조용히 말했다."], ["No one would ever know what she thought of a wedding journey to Rome.", "로마로의 신혼여행을 그녀가 어떻게 생각하는지는 그 누구도 결코 알지 못할 것이었다."]], [["“Mrs. Cadwallader says it is nonsense, people going a long journey when they are married.", "\"캐드월래더 부인은 그게 다 말도 안 된다고 해, 결혼한 사람들이 먼 길을 떠난다는 게."], ["She says they get tired to death of each other, and can’t quarrel comfortably, as they would at home.", "서로한테 진절머리가 나도록 질려 버려서, 집에서처럼 마음 편히 다툴 수도 없다는 거야."], ["And Lady Chettam says she went to Bath.”", "그리고 체텀 부인은 배스로 다녀왔대.\""], ["Celia’s color changed again and again—seemed", "실리아의 얼굴빛은 또다시 변하고 또 변했으니,"]], [["“To come and go with tidings from the heart, As it a running messenger had been.”", "\"마치 달음박질하는 전령이라도 된 듯이\n마음의 소식을 안고 오갔다.\""]], [["It must mean more than Celia’s blushing usually did.", "이는 실리아의 평소 홍조가 뜻하는 것보다 더한 무언가를 의미하는 게 틀림없었다."]], [["“Celia!", "\"실리아!"], ["has something happened?”", "무슨 일이 있었니?\""], ["said Dorothea, in a tone full of sisterly feeling.", "도로시아가 누이다운 정이 가득한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Have you really any great news to tell me?”", "\"정말 내게 전할 굉장한 소식이라도 있는 거야?\""]], [["“It was because you went away, Dodo.", "\"언니가 떠나 버려서 그랬어, 도도."], ["Then there was nobody but me for Sir James to talk to,”", "그러고 나니 제임스 경이 이야기를 나눌 사람이라곤 나밖에 없었거든.\""], ["said Celia, with a certain roguishness in her eyes.", "실리아가 두 눈에 다소 장난기를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“I understand.", "\"알겠다."], ["It is as I used to hope and believe,”", "내가 바라고 믿어 왔던 그대로구나.\""], ["said Dorothea, taking her sister’s face between her hands, and looking at her half anxiously.", "도로시아가 두 손으로 누이의 얼굴을 감싸며, 반쯤은 걱정스럽게 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Celia’s marriage seemed more serious than it used to do.", "실리아의 결혼은 예전보다 더 진지하게 느껴졌다."]], [["“It was only three days ago,”", "\"바로 사흘 전 일이야.\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“And Lady Chettam is very kind.”", "\"그리고 체텀 부인은 무척 다정하셔.\""]], [["“And you are very happy?”", "\"그래서 너는 아주 행복하니?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"응."], ["We are not going to be married yet.", "우린 아직 결혼하지 않을 거야."], ["Because every thing is to be got ready.", "모든 걸 다 준비해야 하거든."], ["And I don’t want to be married so very soon, because I think it is nice to be engaged.", "그리고 난 그렇게 빨리 결혼하고 싶지 않아, 약혼해 있는 게 좋은 것 같으니까."], ["And we shall be married all our lives after.”", "그러고 나서 우린 남은 평생 부부로 지낼 거잖아.\""]], [["“I do believe you could not marry better, Kitty.", "\"네가 이보다 더 좋은 사람과 결혼할 수는 없을 거라고 정말 믿어, 키티."], ["Sir James is a good, honorable man,”", "제임스 경은 선량하고 명예로운 분이셔.\""], ["said Dorothea, warmly.", "도로시아가 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“He has gone on with the cottages, Dodo.", "\"그분은 오두막 짓는 일을 계속하고 계셔, 도도."], ["He will tell you about them when he comes.", "오시면 그것에 대해 언니한테 말씀하실 거야."], ["Shall you be glad to see him?”", "언니, 그분을 만나면 기쁘겠어?\""]], [["“Of course I shall.", "\"물론 기쁘지."], ["How can you ask me?”", "그걸 어떻게 묻니?\""]], [["“Only I was afraid you would be getting so learned,”", "\"다만 언니가 너무 박식해지지나 않을까 걱정됐을 뿐이야.\""], ["said Celia, regarding Mr. Casaubon’s learning as a kind of damp which might in due time saturate a neighboring body.", "실리아가 캐소본 씨의 학식을, 머지않아 이웃한 몸뚱이를 흠뻑 적셔 버릴지도 모를 일종의 습기처럼 여기며 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIX.", "# CHAPTER XXIX."]], [["I found that no genius in another could please me.", "또 다른 사람에게 깃든 천재성이 나를 즐겁게 할 수 없음을 나는 알았다."], ["My unfortunate paradoxes had entirely dried up that source of comfort.—GOLDSMITH.", "나의 불행한 역설들이 그 위안의 샘을 완전히 말려 버린 것이다.—골드스미스."]], [["One morning, some weeks after her arrival at Lowick, Dorothea—but why always Dorothea?", "로윅에 도착한 지 몇 주가 지난 어느 날 아침, 도러시아는—그런데 왜 늘 도러시아인가?"], ["Was her point of view the only possible one with regard to this marriage?", "이 결혼에 관해서 그녀의 관점만이 유일하게 가능한 것일까?"], ["I protest against all our interest, all our effort at understanding being given to the young skins that look blooming in spite of trouble; for these too will get faded, and will know the older and more eating griefs which we are helping to neglect.", "나는 우리의 모든 관심과 이해하려는 모든 노력이 곤경에도 불구하고 활짝 피어 보이는 젊은 살결에만 쏟아지는 것에 항의한다. 그들 역시 시들어 갈 것이고, 우리가 등한시하도록 거드는 더 오래되고 더 갉아먹는 슬픔을 알게 될 테니 말이다."], ["In spite of the blinking eyes and white moles objectionable to Celia, and the want of muscular curve which was morally painful to Sir James, Mr. Casaubon had an intense consciousness within him, and was spiritually a-hungered like the rest of us.", "실리아에게 거슬렸던 깜박이는 눈과 흰 사마귀, 그리고 제임스 경에게 도덕적 고통을 안긴 근육의 곡선이 없음에도 불구하고, 캐소본 씨는 자기 안에 강렬한 의식을 지니고 있었고, 우리 모두와 마찬가지로 영적으로 굶주려 있었다."], ["He had done nothing exceptional in marrying—nothing but what society sanctions, and considers an occasion for wreaths and bouquets.", "그는 결혼함에 있어 별난 일을 한 것이 아니었다—사회가 승인하고, 화환과 꽃다발의 계기로 여기는 일을 했을 뿐이었다."], ["It had occurred to him that he must not any longer defer his intention of matrimony, and he had reflected that in taking a wife, a man of good position should expect and carefully choose a blooming young lady—the younger the better, because more educable and submissive—of a rank equal to his own, of religious principles, virtuous disposition, and good understanding.", "그는 결혼의 뜻을 더 이상 미뤄서는 안 되겠다는 생각이 들었고, 좋은 지위를 가진 남자라면 아내를 맞을 때 활짝 핀 젊은 숙녀를—어릴수록 좋은데, 그래야 더 교육하기 쉽고 순종적이기 때문이다—자신과 동등한 신분에, 종교적 원칙을 지니고, 덕성스러운 기질에, 분별력이 좋은 여성을 기대하고 신중히 골라야 한다고 생각했다."], ["On such a young lady he would make handsome settlements, and he would neglect no arrangement for her happiness: in return, he should receive family pleasures and leave behind him that copy of himself which seemed so urgently required of a man—to the sonneteers of the sixteenth century.", "그런 젊은 숙녀에게라면 그는 후한 재산을 정해 주고, 그녀의 행복을 위한 어떤 마련도 소홀히 하지 않을 터였다. 그 대가로 그는 가정의 즐거움을 누리고, 자신을 복제한 자식을 남길 것이었으니—16세기 소네트 시인들에게는 남자에게 그토록 절실히 요구되는 것으로 보였던 그것을 말이다."], ["Times had altered since then, and no sonneteer had insisted on Mr.", "그때 이후로 시대는 변했고, 어떤 소네트 시인도 캐소본 씨에게 자신의 복제본을 남기라고 강요하지 않았다."], ["Casaubon’s leaving a copy of himself; moreover, he had not yet succeeded in issuing copies of his mythological key; but he had always intended to acquit himself by marriage, and the sense that he was fast leaving the years behind him, that the world was getting dimmer and that he felt lonely, was a reason to him for losing no more time in overtaking domestic delights before they too were left behind by the years.", "게다가 그는 아직 자신의 신화 풀이 열쇠의 사본을 발행하는 데도 성공하지 못한 처지였다. 하지만 그는 늘 결혼으로 자기 의무를 다하리라 마음먹고 있었고, 자신이 빠르게 세월을 뒤로하고 있다는 것, 세상이 점점 흐려지고 외로움을 느낀다는 의식은, 가정의 즐거움마저 세월에 뒤처지기 전에 더 이상 시간을 허비하지 말고 그것을 따라잡아야 할 이유가 되었다."]], [["And when he had seen Dorothea he believed that he had found even more than he demanded: she might really be such a helpmate to him as would enable him to dispense with a hired secretary, an aid which Mr. Casaubon had never yet employed and had a suspicious dread of.", "그리고 도러시아를 보았을 때 그는 자기가 요구한 것보다 더 많은 것을 발견했다고 믿었다."], ["(Mr. Casaubon was nervously conscious that he was expected to manifest a powerful mind.) Providence, in its kindness, had supplied him with the wife he needed.", "그녀는 정말로 고용한 비서를 둘 필요가 없을 만큼 자신에게 알맞은 조력자가 되어 줄 듯했는데, 그 도움이란 캐소본 씨가 아직껏 한 번도 쓴 적이 없고 의심스럽게 두려워하던 것이었다."], ["A wife, a modest young lady, with the purely appreciative, unambitious abilities of her sex, is sure to think her husband’s mind powerful.", "(캐소본 씨는 자신이 강력한 지성을 드러내 보여야 한다는 기대를 받고 있다는 것을 신경질적으로 의식하고 있었다.) 섭리는 그 자비로움 속에서 그에게 필요한 아내를 마련해 주었다."], ["Whether Providence had taken equal care of Miss Brooke in presenting her with Mr. Casaubon was an idea which could hardly occur to him.", "아내, 즉 자기 성별 특유의 순전히 감상하기만 하고 야심 없는 능력을 지닌 정숙한 젊은 숙녀라면, 남편의 지성이 강력하다고 으레 생각하게 마련이다."], ["Society never made the preposterous demand that a man should think as much about his own qualifications for making a charming girl happy as he thinks of hers for making himself happy.", "섭리가 브룩 양에게 캐소본 씨를 보내 줌으로써 그녀에게도 동등한 배려를 했는지 어떤지는, 그로서는 좀처럼 떠오를 수 없는 생각이었다. 사회는 한 남자가 매력적인 처녀를 행복하게 해 줄 자기 자질에 대해, 그녀가 자신을 행복하게 해 줄 자질을 헤아리는 만큼 헤아려야 한다는 터무니없는 요구를 결코 하지 않았다."], ["As if a man could choose not only his wife but his wife’s husband!", "마치 남자가 자기 아내뿐 아니라 아내의 남편까지 고를 수 있기라도 한 듯이!"], ["Or as if he were bound to provide charms for his posterity in his own person!— When Dorothea accepted him with effusion, that was only natural; and Mr.", "또는 마치 자신의 몸으로 후손을 위한 매력을 마련해 줄 의무라도 있는 듯이!— 도러시아가 감격에 차서 그를 받아들였을 때, 그것은 지극히 당연한 일이었다."], ["Casaubon believed that his happiness was going to begin.", "그리고 캐소본 씨는 자신의 행복이 이제 막 시작되려 한다고 믿었다."]], [["He had not had much foretaste of happiness in his previous life.", "그는 이전의 삶에서 행복을 별로 맛보지 못했다. 튼튼한 육체 없이 강렬한 기쁨을 알려면 열정적인 영혼을 지녀야 한다."], ["To know intense joy without a strong bodily frame, one must have an enthusiastic soul. Mr.", "캐소본 씨는 한 번도 튼튼한 육체를 가져 본 적이 없었고, 그의 영혼은 예민했으나 열정적이지는 않았다."], ["Casaubon had never had a strong bodily frame, and his soul was sensitive without being enthusiastic: it was too languid to thrill out of self-consciousness into passionate delight; it went on fluttering in the swampy ground where it was hatched, thinking of its wings and never flying.", "그것은 자의식에서 떨치고 나와 정열적인 환희로 전율하기에는 너무 무기력했다. 그것은 자기가 부화된 늪지에서 날개를 생각하면서도 결코 날지는 못한 채 계속 파닥거리기만 했다."], ["His experience was of that pitiable kind which shrinks from pity, and fears most of all that it should be known: it was that proud narrow sensitiveness which has not mass enough to spare for transformation into sympathy, and quivers thread-like in small currents of self-preoccupation or at best of an egoistic scrupulosity.", "그의 경험은 연민에서 움츠러들고 무엇보다 알려지는 것을 두려워하는 가련한 종류의 것이었다. 그것은 동정으로 변모할 만큼의 충분한 무게를 지니지 못한 채, 자기 몰두의, 기껏해야 이기적인 결벽의 작은 물결 속에서 실처럼 떨리는, 자존심 강하고 옹졸한 예민함이었다."], ["And Mr. Casaubon had many scruples: he was capable of a severe self-restraint; he was resolute in being a man of honor according to the code; he would be unimpeachable by any recognized opinion.", "그리고 캐소본 씨는 결벽이 많았다."], ["In conduct these ends had been attained; but the difficulty of making his Key to all Mythologies unimpeachable weighed like lead upon his mind; and the pamphlets—or “Parerga”", "그는 엄격한 자기 절제를 할 수 있었고, 규범에 따른 명예로운 사람이 되기로 단호했으며, 어떤 공인된 견해로도 흠잡을 수 없는 사람이고자 했다. 행실에서 이러한 목표들은 달성되었다."], ["as he called them—by which he tested his public and deposited small monumental records of his march, were far from having been seen in all their significance.", "하지만 자신의 신화 풀이 열쇠를 흠잡을 수 없게 만드는 일의 어려움은 그의 마음을 납덩이처럼 짓눌렀다. 그리고 그가 대중을 시험하고 자신의 행진의 자그마한 기념비적 기록을 남겨 둔 소책자들—그가 부르는 이름으로는 '부록 논문들'—은 그 온전한 의미가 인정받기에는 한참 멀었다."], ["He suspected the Archdeacon of not having read them; he was in painful doubt as to what was really thought of them by the leading minds of Brasenose, and bitterly convinced that his old acquaintance Carp had been the writer of that depreciatory recension which was kept locked in a small drawer of Mr.", "그는 부주교가 그것들을 읽지 않았으리라고 의심했다. 브레이즈노즈의 일류 지성들이 그것들을 진정 어떻게 생각하는지에 대해 고통스러운 의구심에 빠져 있었고, 캐소본 씨의 책상 작은 서랍 속에, 또한 언어 기억의 어두운 골방 속에 잠가 둔 그 깎아내리는 비평문이 옛 지인 카프의 손에서 나왔으리라고 쓰라리게 확신하고 있었다."], ["Casaubon’s desk, and also in a dark closet of his verbal memory.", "이것들은 맞서 싸우기에 무거운 인상들이었고, 모든 지나친 자부심의 결과인 그 우울한 비탄을 가져왔다."], ["These were heavy impressions to struggle against, and brought that melancholy embitterment which is the consequence of all excessive claim: even his religious faith wavered with his wavering trust in his own authorship, and the consolations of the Christian hope in immortality seemed to lean on the immortality of the still unwritten Key to all Mythologies.", "자신의 저술에 대한 흔들리는 신뢰와 더불어 그의 종교적 신앙마저 흔들렸고, 불멸에 대한 기독교적 희망의 위안은 아직 쓰이지 않은 신화 풀이 열쇠의 불멸에 기대고 있는 듯 보였다."], ["For my part I am very sorry for him.", "나로서는 그가 무척 안쓰럽다."], ["It is an uneasy lot at best, to be what we call highly taught and yet not to enjoy: to be present at this great spectacle of life and never to be liberated from a small hungry shivering self—never to be fully possessed by the glory we behold, never to have our consciousness rapturously transformed into the vividness of a thought, the ardor of a passion, the energy of an action, but always to be scholarly and uninspired, ambitious and timid, scrupulous and dim-sighted.", "우리가 이른바 고도로 교육받았다 하면서도 그것을 누리지 못하는 것은 기껏해야 불편한 운명이다. 삶이라는 이 거대한 장관에 참석하면서도 작고 굶주리고 떨리는 자아로부터 결코 해방되지 못하는 것, 우리가 바라보는 영광에 결코 온전히 사로잡히지 못하는 것, 우리의 의식이 한 사상의 생생함으로, 한 정열의 열기로, 한 행동의 활력으로 환희에 차 변모하지 못한 채, 늘 학구적이되 영감이 없고, 야심차되 소심하며, 결벽적이되 시야가 흐린 채로 있는 것 말이다."], ["Becoming a dean or even a bishop would make little difference, I fear, to Mr. Casaubon’s uneasiness.", "학장이 되거나 심지어 주교가 된다 해도 캐소본 씨의 불편함에는 별 차이가 없으리라고, 나는 우려한다."], ["Doubtless some ancient Greek has observed that behind the big mask and the speaking-trumpet, there must always be our poor little eyes peeping as usual and our timorous lips more or less under anxious control.", "분명 어느 고대 그리스인이 일찍이 관찰했듯이, 그 커다란 가면과 확성용 나팔 뒤에는 언제나 평소처럼 빼꼼히 내다보는 우리의 가련하고 작은 눈과, 불안한 통제 아래 어느 정도 놓인 우리의 겁먹은 입술이 있게 마련이다."]], [["To this mental estate mapped out a quarter of a century before, to sensibilities thus fenced in, Mr.", "사반세기 전에 구획해 둔 이 정신적 영토에, 이렇게 울타리 쳐진 감수성에, 캐소본 씨는 사랑스러운 젊은 신부를 통해 행복을 보태리라 생각했다."], ["Casaubon had thought of annexing happiness with a lovely young bride; but even before marriage, as we have seen, he found himself under a new depression in the consciousness that the new bliss was not blissful to him.", "하지만 결혼하기도 전에, 우리가 이미 본 대로, 그는 그 새로운 지복이 자신에게는 지복답지 못하다는 의식 속에서 새로운 우울에 빠진 자신을 발견했다."], ["Inclination yearned back to its old, easier custom.", "마음은 옛날의 더 편안한 습관으로 되돌아가기를 갈망했다."], ["And the deeper he went in domesticity the more did the sense of acquitting himself and acting with propriety predominate over any other satisfaction.", "그리고 그가 가정생활로 깊이 들어갈수록, 그 어떤 다른 만족보다도 자기 의무를 다하고 합당하게 처신한다는 의식이 우위를 점했다."], ["Marriage, like religion and erudition, nay, like authorship itself, was fated to become an outward requirement, and Edward Casaubon was bent on fulfilling unimpeachably all requirements.", "결혼은, 종교와 학식처럼, 아니, 저술 자체와 마찬가지로, 외적인 요구 사항이 될 운명이었고, 에드워드 캐소본은 모든 요구 사항을 흠잡을 데 없이 이행하기로 작정한 사람이었다."], ["Even drawing Dorothea into use in his study, according to his own intention before marriage, was an effort which he was always tempted to defer, and but for her pleading insistence it might never have begun.", "결혼 전 자신의 의도대로 도러시아를 서재 일에 끌어들이는 것조차 그는 늘 미루고 싶은 유혹을 느끼는 노력이었으니, 그녀가 간청하며 고집하지 않았더라면 결코 시작되지 못했을지도 모른다."], ["But she had succeeded in making it a matter of course that she should take her place at an early hour in the library and have work either of reading aloud or copying assigned her.", "하지만 그녀는 이른 시각에 서재에 자리를 잡고서, 소리 내어 읽거나 베껴 쓰는 일을 맡는 것을 당연한 일로 만드는 데 성공했다."], ["The work had been easier to define because Mr.", "캐소본 씨가 당장의 의도 하나를 채택했기 때문에 일을 규정하기는 더 수월했다."], ["Casaubon had adopted an immediate intention: there was to be a new Parergon, a small monograph on some lately traced indications concerning the Egyptian mysteries whereby certain assertions of Warburton’s could be corrected.", "새로운 부록 논문이 나올 예정이었는데, 워버턴의 어떤 주장들을 바로잡을 수 있는, 이집트 신비 의식에 관해 최근 추적된 몇몇 단서들을 다룬 작은 단행본이었다. 여기서도 참고 문헌은 방대했으나, 아주 끝이 없을 정도는 아니었다."], ["References were extensive even here, but not altogether shoreless; and sentences were actually to be written in the shape wherein they would be scanned by Brasenose and a less formidable posterity.", "그리고 브레이즈노즈와 그보다 덜 두려운 후세 사람들이 면밀히 살필 형태 그대로 실제 문장들이 쓰일 참이었다. 이러한 자잘한 기념비적 산물들은 늘 캐소본 씨를 흥분시켰다."], ["These minor monumental productions were always exciting to Mr.", "인용문의 간섭이나, 머릿속에서 서로 부딪쳐 울리는 변증법적 어구들의 경합 때문에 소화가 어려워지곤 했다."], ["Casaubon; digestion was made difficult by the interference of citations, or by the rivalry of dialectical phrases ringing against each other in his brain.", "그리고 처음부터 라틴어 헌사가 들어갈 예정이었는데, 그것이 카프에게 바쳐지지 않으리라는 점만 빼고는 모든 것이 불확실했다. 캐소본 씨에게는, 일찍이 카프에게 헌사를 바치면서 그 동물계의 일원을 가리켜 '어느 시대에도 사라지지 않을 사람들'"], ["And from the first there was to be a Latin dedication about which everything was uncertain except that it was not to be addressed to Carp: it was a poisonous regret to Mr.", "가운데 헤아렸던 일이 독을 머금은 후회로 남아 있었으니, 그것은 다음 시대에 헌사를 바친 자를 영락없이 조롱거리로 만들 실수였고, 어쩌면 현 시대에 파이크와 텐치가 키득거릴 거리가 될 수도 있었다."], ["Casaubon that he had once addressed a dedication to Carp in which he had numbered that member of the animal kingdom among the viros nullo ævo perituros, a mistake which would infallibly lay the dedicator open to ridicule in the next age, and might even be chuckled over by Pike and Tench in the present.", "* '어느 시대에도 사라지지 않을 사람들'(viros nullo ævo perituros): \"결코 멸하지 않을 인물들\"이라는 라틴어 찬사. 카프(Carp)는 잉어, 파이크(Pike)는 강꼬치고기, 텐치(Tench)는 잉어과 민물고기로, 캐소본의 학계 지인들 이름이 모두 물고기여서 '동물계의 일원'이라는 빈정거림이 성립한다."]], [["Thus Mr. Casaubon was in one of his busiest epochs, and as I began to say a little while ago, Dorothea joined him early in the library where he had breakfasted alone.", "이렇듯 캐소본 씨는 가장 바쁜 시기 가운데 하나를 보내고 있었는데, 조금 전에 내가 말하기 시작한 대로, 도러시아는 그가 홀로 아침을 든 서재로 일찍 그를 찾아왔다."], ["Celia at this time was on a second visit to Lowick, probably the last before her marriage, and was in the drawing-room expecting Sir James.", "이 무렵 실리아는 두 번째로 로윅을 방문 중이었는데, 결혼 전으로는 아마 마지막 방문일 터였고, 응접실에서 제임스 경을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Dorothea had learned to read the signs of her husband’s mood, and she saw that the morning had become more foggy there during the last hour.", "도러시아는 남편의 기분을 나타내는 신호를 읽는 법을 익혀 두었기에, 지난 한 시간 사이 그곳의 아침 공기가 한층 더 안개 낀 듯 무거워졌음을 알아챘다."], ["She was going silently to her desk when he said, in that distant tone which implied that he was discharging a disagreeable duty—", "그녀가 말없이 자기 책상으로 가려는데 그가 말했는데, 그것은 그가 불쾌한 의무를 이행하고 있음을 암시하는 그 멀찍한 어조였다—"]], [["“Dorothea, here is a letter for you, which was enclosed in one addressed to me.”", "\"도러시아, 여기 당신에게 온 편지가 있소. 내게 온 편지 안에 동봉되어 있었소.\""]], [["It was a letter of two pages, and she immediately looked at the signature.", "두 장짜리 편지였고, 그녀는 곧바로 서명을 보았다."]], [["“Mr. Ladislaw!", "\"래디슬로 씨로군요!"], ["What can he have to say to me?”", "그분이 내게 무슨 할 말이 있을까요?\""], ["she exclaimed, in a tone of pleased surprise.", "그녀는 반가운 놀라움의 어조로 외쳤다. \"하지만,\""], ["“But,” she added, looking at Mr.", "그녀는 캐소본 씨를 바라보며 덧붙였다."], ["Casaubon, “I can imagine what he has written to you about.”", "\"그분이 당신께 무슨 일로 편지를 보냈는지는 짐작할 수 있어요.\""]], [["“You can, if you please, read the letter,”", "\"원한다면 그 편지를 읽어 봐도 좋소.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, severely pointing to it with his pen, and not looking at her.", "캐소본 씨가 펜으로 그것을 엄하게 가리키면서, 그녀를 보지는 않은 채 말했다."], ["“But I may as well say beforehand, that I must decline the proposal it contains to pay a visit here.", "\"하지만 미리 말해 두는 편이 낫겠소. 그 편지에 담긴, 이곳을 방문하겠다는 제안은 내가 거절해야겠소."], ["I trust I may be excused for desiring an interval of complete freedom from such distractions as have been hitherto inevitable, and especially from guests whose desultory vivacity makes their presence a fatigue.”", "지금껏 불가피했던 그런 방해들로부터, 특히 종잡을 수 없는 활기로 그 존재만으로도 피로를 안기는 손님들로부터 완전히 자유로운 기간을 갖고 싶다는 내 바람이 양해되기를 바라오.\""]], [["There had been no clashing of temper between Dorothea and her husband since that little explosion in Rome, which had left such strong traces in her mind that it had been easier ever since to quell emotion than to incur the consequence of venting it.", "로마에서의 그 작은 폭발 이후로 도러시아와 남편 사이에 기질의 충돌은 없었는데, 그 일은 그녀의 마음에 워낙 강한 자취를 남겨서 그 이후로는 감정을 터뜨려 그 결과를 떠안기보다는 감정을 억누르는 편이 더 쉬워졌던 것이다. 하지만 그녀가 남편에게 불쾌할 수도 있는 방문을 원할 거라는 이 심술궂은 넘겨짚음, 그녀 쪽의 이기적인 불평에 맞선 이 쓸데없는 자기 방어는, 분개하고 난 뒤에야 곱씹을 수 있을 만큼 너무 날카로운 가시였다."], ["But this ill-tempered anticipation that she could desire visits which might be disagreeable to her husband, this gratuitous defence of himself against selfish complaint on her part, was too sharp a sting to be meditated on until after it had been resented.", "도러시아는 자기가 존 밀턴에게라면 참을성을 보일 수 있었으리라 생각해 왔지만, 그가 이런 식으로 처신하는 모습은 한 번도 상상해 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 한순간 캐소본 씨는 어리석을 만큼 분별이 없고 가증스러울 만큼 부당해 보였다."], ["Dorothea had thought that she could have been patient with John Milton, but she had never imagined him behaving in this way; and for a moment Mr.", "머지않아 그녀 안의 숱한 폭풍을 다스리게 될 그 \"갓 태어난 아기\"인 연민은, 이번만큼은 \"돌풍을 타고 넘지\" 않았다."], ["Casaubon seemed to be stupidly undiscerning and odiously unjust. Pity, that “new-born babe”", "그를 떨게 만든 어조로 내뱉은 첫마디에서, 그녀는 캐소본 씨를 깜짝 놀라게 하여 그를 돌아보게 만들었고, 번득이는 그녀의 두 눈과 마주치게 했다."], ["which was by-and-by to rule many a storm within her, did not “stride the blast” on this occasion.", "* \"갓 태어난 아기\"와 \"돌풍을 타고 넘지\": 셰익스피어 「맥베스」"], ["With her first words, uttered in a tone that shook him, she startled Mr.", "1막 7장에서 연민(pity)을 \"갓 태어난 발가벗은 아기가 돌풍을 타고 넘는\""], ["Casaubon into looking at her, and meeting the flash of her eyes.", "모습에 비유한 구절을 인유한 것이다."]], [["“Why do you attribute to me a wish for anything that would annoy you?", "\"어째서 당신을 짜증나게 할 일을 제가 바라리라고 단정하시나요?"], ["You speak to me as if I were something you had to contend against.", "마치 제가 당신이 맞서 싸워야 할 무엇이라도 되는 양 말씀하시는군요."], ["Wait at least till I appear to consult my own pleasure apart from yours.”", "적어도 제가 당신과 별개로 제 즐거움만 챙기는 듯 보일 때까지는 기다려 주세요.\""]], [["“Dorothea, you are hasty,”", "\"도러시아, 당신은 성급하구려.\""], ["answered Mr. Casaubon, nervously.", "캐소본 씨가 신경질적으로 대답했다."]], [["Decidedly, this woman was too young to be on the formidable level of wifehood—unless she had been pale and featureless and taken everything for granted.", "확실히 이 여자는 결혼이라는 만만찮은 차원에 오르기에는 너무 젊었다—그녀가 창백하고 특색 없으며 모든 것을 당연하게 받아들이는 사람이 아니라면 말이다."]], [["“I think it was you who were first hasty in your false suppositions about my feeling,”", "\"제 감정에 대해 잘못된 억측을 하시며 먼저 성급하셨던 건 당신이라고 생각해요.\""], ["said Dorothea, in the same tone.", "도러시아가 똑같은 어조로 말했다."], ["The fire was not dissipated yet, and she thought it was ignoble in her husband not to apologize to her.", "불길은 아직 가라앉지 않았고, 그녀는 남편이 자기에게 사과하지 않는 것이 비열하다고 생각했다."]], [["“We will, if you please, say no more on this subject, Dorothea.", "\"괜찮다면 이 문제에 대해서는 더 말하지 맙시다, 도러시아."], ["I have neither leisure nor energy for this kind of debate.”", "나는 이런 종류의 논쟁을 할 여유도 기운도 없소.\""]], [["Here Mr. Casaubon dipped his pen and made as if he would return to his writing, though his hand trembled so much that the words seemed to be written in an unknown character.", "여기서 캐소본 씨는 펜을 잉크에 적셔 글쓰기로 돌아가려는 듯한 시늉을 했지만, 그의 손이 어찌나 떨리는지 마치 알 수 없는 문자로 글이 쓰이는 것만 같았다."], ["There are answers which, in turning away wrath, only send it to the other end of the room, and to have a discussion coolly waived when you feel that justice is all on your own side is even more exasperating in marriage than in philosophy.", "분노를 돌려세우면서도 그저 그것을 방 저편 끝으로 보내 버릴 뿐인 대답들이 있는 법인데, 정의가 온전히 자기편에 있다고 느낄 때 토론이 냉랭하게 회피당하는 것은, 철학에서보다 결혼에서 한층 더 사람을 격분케 한다."]], [["Dorothea left Ladislaw’s two letters unread on her husband’s writing-table and went to her own place, the scorn and indignation within her rejecting the reading of these letters, just as we hurl away any trash towards which we seem to have been suspected of mean cupidity.", "도러시아는 래디슬로의 편지 두 장을 읽지 않은 채 남편의 책상 위에 남겨 두고 자기 자리로 갔는데, 그녀 안의 경멸과 분개는 그 편지들을 읽기를 거부했으니, 마치 우리가 비열한 탐욕을 품었다고 의심받은 듯한 어떤 쓰레기를 멀리 내던져 버리듯이 그러했다. 그녀는 이 편지들을 둘러싼 남편의 언짢은 기분의 미묘한 근원을 조금도 알아채지 못했다."], ["She did not in the least divine the subtle sources of her husband’s bad temper about these letters: she only knew that they had caused him to offend her.", "다만 그 편지들이 그로 하여금 자신을 불쾌하게 만들도록 했다는 것만 알았다. 그녀는 곧바로 일을 시작했고, 그녀의 손은 떨리지 않았다."], ["She began to work at once, and her hand did not tremble; on the contrary, in writing out the quotations which had been given to her the day before, she felt that she was forming her letters beautifully, and it seemed to her that she saw the construction of the Latin she was copying, and which she was beginning to understand, more clearly than usual.", "오히려 전날 받은 인용문들을 옮겨 적으면서 그녀는 자기가 글자를 아름답게 만들어 가고 있다고 느꼈고, 베끼고 있는, 그리고 이제 막 이해하기 시작한 라틴어의 구조가 평소보다 더 또렷이 보이는 것 같았다. 그녀의 분개 속에는 우월감이 깃들어 있었지만, 그것은 당장은 단호한 필치로 흘러나갔을 뿐, 한때 \"상냥한 대천사\"였던 이를 형편없는 인간이라 선언하는 또렷한 내면의 목소리로 응축되지는 않았다."], ["In her indignation there was a sense of superiority, but it went out for the present in firmness of stroke, and did not compress itself into an inward articulate voice pronouncing the once “affable archangel” a poor creature.", "* \"상냥한 대천사\"(affable archangel): 밀턴 「실낙원」에서 아담에게 친절히 가르침을 베푸는 대천사 라파엘을 가리키는 표현으로, 도러시아가 결혼 전 캐소본에게 품었던 이상을 빗댄다."]], [["There had been this apparent quiet for half an hour, and Dorothea had not looked away from her own table, when she heard the loud bang of a book on the floor, and turning quickly saw Mr.", "이렇게 겉으로 고요한 상태가 반 시간쯤 이어졌고, 도러시아가 자기 책상에서 눈을 떼지 않고 있던 차에, 그녀는 책 한 권이 바닥에 쾅 하고 떨어지는 소리를 들었다."], ["Casaubon on the library steps clinging forward as if he were in some bodily distress.", "얼른 돌아보니 캐소본 씨가 서재 사다리 위에서 무슨 육체적 고통에 시달리는 듯 앞으로 매달려 있는 것이 보였다."], ["She started up and bounded towards him in an instant: he was evidently in great straits for breath.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 순식간에 그에게로 달려갔다. 그는 분명 숨을 쉬느라 몹시 애를 먹고 있었다."], ["Jumping on a stool she got close to his elbow and said with her whole soul melted into tender alarm—", "의자 위로 뛰어올라 그의 팔꿈치 가까이로 다가간 그녀는 온 영혼이 다정한 놀라움으로 녹아든 채 말했다—"]], [["“Can you lean on me, dear?”", "\"제게 기대실 수 있겠어요, 여보?\""]], [["He was still for two or three minutes, which seemed endless to her, unable to speak or move, gasping for breath.", "그는 그녀에게는 끝없이 길게 느껴지는 이삼 분 동안 가만히 있었는데, 말도 못 하고 움직이지도 못한 채 숨을 헐떡거렸다."], ["When at last he descended the three steps and fell backward in the large chair which Dorothea had drawn close to the foot of the ladder, he no longer gasped but seemed helpless and about to faint.", "마침내 그가 세 계단을 내려와, 도러시아가 사다리 발치 가까이 끌어다 놓은 큰 의자에 뒤로 쓰러지듯 앉았을 때, 그는 더는 헐떡이지 않았으나 무력하게 막 기절할 것처럼 보였다."], ["Dorothea rang the bell violently, and presently Mr.", "도러시아가 사납게 벨을 울리자 이내 캐소본 씨는 부축을 받아 긴 의자로 옮겨졌다."], ["Casaubon was helped to the couch: he did not faint, and was gradually reviving, when Sir James Chettam came in, having been met in the hall with the news that Mr. Casaubon had “had a fit in the library.”", "그는 기절하지 않고 점차 되살아나고 있었는데, 바로 그때 캐소본 씨가 \"서재에서 발작을 일으켰다\"는 소식을 현관에서 전해 들은 제임스 체텀 경이 들어왔다."]], [["“Good God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["this is just what might have been expected,”", "이건 충분히 예상할 수 있었던 일이잖아.\""], ["was his immediate thought.", "그것이 그의 즉각적인 생각이었다."], ["If his prophetic soul had been urged to particularize, it seemed to him that “fits” would have been the definite expression alighted upon.", "그의 예언자적 영혼이 좀 더 구체적으로 짚어 보라고 다그쳐졌다면, 그가 떠올렸을 명확한 표현은 \"발작\"이었으리라 여겨졌다."], ["He asked his informant, the butler, whether the doctor had been sent for.", "그는 소식을 전한 집사에게 의사를 부르러 보냈는지 물었다."], ["The butler never knew his master to want the doctor before; but would it not be right to send for a physician?", "집사는 주인이 전에 의사를 찾는 것을 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 하지만 의사를 부르러 보내는 것이 옳지 않겠는가?"]], [["When Sir James entered the library, however, Mr. Casaubon could make some signs of his usual politeness, and Dorothea, who in the reaction from her first terror had been kneeling and sobbing by his side now rose and herself proposed that some one should ride off for a medical man.", "그러나 제임스 경이 서재로 들어섰을 때, 캐소본 씨는 평소의 예의를 나타내는 몇 가지 몸짓을 할 수 있었고, 처음의 공포에서 벗어난 반동으로 그의 곁에 무릎 꿇고 흐느끼던 도러시아는 이제 몸을 일으켜 직접, 누군가 말을 타고 의사를 부르러 가야 한다고 제안했다."]], [["“I recommend you to send for Lydgate,”", "\"리드게이트를 부르시기를 권하오.\""], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“My mother has called him in, and she has found him uncommonly clever.", "\"내 어머니가 그를 모셔 왔는데, 보기 드물게 영리하다고 보시더군요."], ["She has had a poor opinion of the physicians since my father’s death.”", "어머니는 아버지가 돌아가신 뒤로 의사들을 탐탁잖게 여기셨거든요.\""]], [["Dorothea appealed to her husband, and he made a silent sign of approval.", "도러시아가 남편에게 의견을 구하자 그는 말없이 동의의 표시를 했다."], ["So Mr. Lydgate was sent for and he came wonderfully soon, for the messenger, who was Sir James Chettam’s man and knew Mr.", "그리하여 리드게이트 씨를 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 그는 놀랄 만큼 금세 도착했다."], ["Lydgate, met him leading his horse along the Lowick road and giving his arm to Miss Vincy.", "심부름꾼은 제임스 체텀 경의 하인으로 리드게이트 씨를 알고 있었는데, 마침 로윅 가도를 따라 말을 끌면서 빈시 양에게 팔을 내어 주고 있던 그를 만났던 것이다."]], [["Celia, in the drawing-room, had known nothing of the trouble till Sir James told her of it.", "응접실에 있던 실리아는 제임스 경이 알려 줄 때까지 그 소동을 전혀 모르고 있었다."], ["After Dorothea’s account, he no longer considered the illness a fit, but still something “of that nature.”", "도러시아의 설명을 듣고 나서 그는 더 이상 그 병을 발작으로 여기지는 않았으나, 그래도 \"그런 종류의\" 무언가라고 여겼다."]], [["“Poor dear Dodo—how dreadful!”", "\"가엾은 도도—정말 끔찍해라!\""], ["said Celia, feeling as much grieved as her own perfect happiness would allow.", "실리아가, 자신의 더없는 행복이 허락하는 만큼 슬퍼하며 말했다."], ["Her little hands were clasped, and enclosed by Sir James’s as a bud is enfolded by a liberal calyx.", "그녀의 작은 두 손은 마주 잡혀 있었고, 봉오리가 넉넉한 꽃받침에 감싸이듯 제임스 경의 손에 감싸여 있었다. \"캐소본 씨가 편찮으시다니 정말 충격적이에요."], ["“It is very shocking that Mr.", "하지만 저는 그분을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없어요."], ["Casaubon should be ill; but I never did like him.", "게다가 그분은 도러시아를 반만큼도 충분히 아끼지 않는 것 같아요."], ["And I think he is not half fond enough of Dorothea; and he ought to be, for I am sure no one else would have had him—do you think they would?”", "마땅히 그래야 하는데도요. 그분과 결혼할 사람은 분명 언니 말고는 아무도 없었을 테니까요—그렇게 생각하지 않으세요?\""]], [["“I always thought it a horrible sacrifice of your sister,”", "\"나는 늘 그것이 처형의 끔찍한 희생이라고 생각했소.\""], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"맞아요."], ["But poor Dodo never did do what other people do, and I think she never will.”", "하지만 가엾은 도도는 한 번도 남들 하는 대로 한 적이 없고, 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“She is a noble creature,”", "\"처형은 고귀한 사람이오.\""], ["said the loyal-hearted Sir James.", "충직한 마음의 제임스 경이 말했다."], ["He had just had a fresh impression of this kind, as he had seen Dorothea stretching her tender arm under her husband’s neck and looking at him with unspeakable sorrow.", "그는 방금 이런 종류의 인상을 새로이 받은 참이었는데, 도러시아가 다정한 팔을 남편의 목 아래로 뻗으며 형언할 수 없는 슬픔으로 그를 바라보는 모습을 보았기 때문이다."], ["He did not know how much penitence there was in the sorrow.", "그는 그 슬픔 속에 얼마나 많은 뉘우침이 깃들어 있는지는 알지 못했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["said Celia, thinking it was very well for Sir James to say so, but he would not have been comfortable with Dodo.", "실리아가 말했는데, 제임스 경이 그렇게 말하는 거야 좋은 일이지만 그라면 도도와 함께 살면서 편안하지 못했을 거라고 생각하면서였다."], ["“Shall I go to her?", "\"제가 언니에게 가 볼까요?"], ["Could I help her, do you think?”", "제가 언니를 도울 수 있을까요, 그렇게 생각하세요?\""]], [["“I think it would be well for you just to go and see her before Lydgate comes,”", "\"리드게이트가 오기 전에 가서 처형을 잠깐 보고 오는 것이 좋을 것 같소.\""], ["said Sir James, magnanimously.", "제임스 경이 너그럽게 말했다."], ["“Only don’t stay long.”", "\"다만 오래 머물지는 마시오.\""]], [["While Celia was gone he walked up and down remembering what he had originally felt about Dorothea’s engagement, and feeling a revival of his disgust at Mr.", "실리아가 간 동안 그는 도러시아의 약혼에 대해 처음에 자기가 느꼈던 바를 떠올리며 이리저리 거닐었고, 브룩 씨의 무관심에 대한 혐오가 되살아나는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Brooke’s indifference. If Cadwallader—if every one else had regarded the affair as he, Sir James, had done, the marriage might have been hindered.", "만약 캐드월래더가—만약 다른 모든 사람이 그 일을 자신, 곧 제임스 경이 보았던 대로 보았더라면, 그 결혼은 막을 수 있었을지도 모른다."], ["It was wicked to let a young girl blindly decide her fate in that way, without any effort to save her.", "어린 처녀가 그런 식으로, 그녀를 구하려는 어떤 노력도 없이 눈먼 채 자기 운명을 결정하도록 내버려 둔 것은 사악한 일이었다."], ["Sir James had long ceased to have any regrets on his own account: his heart was satisfied with his engagement to Celia.", "제임스 경은 자기 자신을 위해서는 이미 오래전에 아무런 미련도 갖지 않게 되었다. 그의 마음은 실리아와의 약혼으로 흡족했다."], ["But he had a chivalrous nature (was not the disinterested service of woman among the ideal glories of old chivalry?): his disregarded love had not turned to bitterness; its death had made sweet odors—floating memories that clung with a consecrating effect to Dorothea.", "하지만 그는 기사도적인 천성을 지녔으니(여성에 대한 사심 없는 헌신이야말로 옛 기사도의 이상적인 영광 가운데 하나가 아니었던가?), 외면당한 그의 사랑은 쓰라림으로 변하지 않았다. 그 사랑의 죽음은 달콤한 향기를 빚어냈으니—도러시아에게 신성하게 어리는 효과로 들러붙어 떠도는 추억들이었다."], ["He could remain her brotherly friend, interpreting her actions with generous trustfulness.", "그는 그녀의 오라비 같은 벗으로 남아, 너그러운 믿음으로 그녀의 행동을 해석할 수 있었다."]], [["Qui veut délasser hors de propos, lasse.—PASCAL. Mr.", "카소본 씨는 첫 번째 발작만큼 심한 두 번째 발작을 겪지 않았고, 며칠 만에 평소의 상태를 회복하기 시작했다."], ["Casaubon had no second attack of equal severity with the first, and in a few days began to recover his usual condition. But Lydgate seemed to think the case worth a great deal of attention.", "그러나 리드게이트는 이 병을 상당히 주의 깊게 살펴야 한다고 여기는 것 같았다."], ["He not only used his stethoscope (which had not become a matter of course in practice at that time), but sat quietly by his patient and watched him. To Mr.", "그는 청진기를 사용했을 뿐만 아니라(당시만 해도 진료에서 청진기가 당연한 일이 아니었다) 환자 곁에 조용히 앉아 그를 지켜보았다."], ["Casaubon’s questions about himself, he replied that the source of the illness was the common error of intellectual men—a too eager and monotonous application: the remedy was, to be satisfied with moderate work, and to seek variety of relaxation.", "자신에 관한 카소본 씨의 질문에 그는, 병의 근원이 지적인 사람들의 흔한 잘못, 곧 너무 열렬하고 단조로운 정신 집중에 있다고 대답했다."], ["Mr. Brooke, who sat by on one occasion, suggested that Mr.", "처방은 적당한 양의 일에 만족하고 다양한 휴식을 구하는 것이었다."], ["Casaubon should go fishing, as Cadwallader did, and have a turning-room, make toys, table-legs, and that kind of thing.", "한번은 그 자리에 앉아 있던 브룩 씨가, 카소본 씨도 캐드월래더처럼 낚시를 하러 가고, 선반 작업실을 두어 장난감이며 식탁 다리며 그런 것들을 만들어 보면 어떻겠느냐고 권했다."]], [["“In short, you recommend me to anticipate the arrival of my second childhood,”", "\"요컨대 제 두 번째 어린 시절이 다가오기를 미리 맞이하라고 권하시는 거군요.\""], ["said poor Mr. Casaubon, with some bitterness.", "가엾은 카소본 씨가 다소 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["“These things,”", "\"그런 것들은,\""], ["he added, looking at Lydgate, “would be to me such relaxation as tow-picking is to prisoners in a house of correction.”", "그는 리드게이트를 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"교화소의 죄수들이 노끈을 푸는 것이나 마찬가지로 제게 휴식이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“I confess,”", "\"솔직히,\""], ["said Lydgate, smiling, “amusement is rather an unsatisfactory prescription.", "리드게이트가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"오락이란 다소 만족스럽지 못한 처방입니다."], ["It is something like telling people to keep up their spirits.", "사람들에게 기운을 내라고 말하는 것과 비슷하지요."], ["Perhaps I had better say, that you must submit to be mildly bored rather than to go on working.”", "어쩌면 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠군요. 계속 일하시기보다는 가벼운 권태를 견디는 데 순응하셔야 한다고요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그렇고말고요.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“Get Dorothea to play backgammon with you in the evenings.", "\"저녁이면 도러시아에게 백개먼을 같이 하자고 하시오."], ["And shuttlecock, now—I don’t know a finer game than shuttlecock for the daytime.", "그리고 셔틀콕도 말이지, 나는 낮 시간에 셔틀콕만큼 좋은 놀이를 모른다오."], ["I remember it all the fashion.", "한창 유행하던 시절이 기억나는군."], ["To be sure, your eyes might not stand that, Casaubon.", "하기야 자네 눈이 그걸 견디지 못할지도 모르지, 카소본. 하지만 알다시피 좀 느긋해져야 하네."], ["But you must unbend, you know.", "아니면 뭔가 가벼운 공부를 해 보면 어떻겠나."], ["Why, you might take to some light study: conchology, now: I always think that must be a light study.", "패류학(貝類學) 같은 것 말이지. 나는 늘 그게 가벼운 공부라고 생각해 왔다네."], ["Or get Dorothea to read you light things, Smollett—‘Roderick Random,’ ‘Humphry Clinker:’ they are a little broad, but she may read anything now she’s married, you know.", "아니면 도러시아한테 가벼운 책을 읽어 달라고 하든가, 스몰릿 말일세, '로더릭 랜덤'이나 '험프리 클링커' 같은 것."], ["I remember they made me laugh uncommonly—there’s a droll bit about a postilion’s breeches.", "좀 야하긴 하지만, 알다시피 이제 결혼했으니 무엇이든 읽어도 되지 않겠나. 그게 나를 유난히 웃겼던 게 기억나는군."], ["We have no such humor now.", "마부 바지에 관한 우스운 대목이 있지."], ["I have gone through all these things, but they might be rather new to you.”", "요즘은 그런 유머가 없어. 나는 그런 것들을 다 거쳐 왔지만, 자네에게는 좀 새로울지도 모르겠군.\""]], [["“As new as eating thistles,” would have been an answer to represent Mr.", "\"엉겅퀴를 먹는 것만큼이나 새롭겠지요\"가 카소본 씨의 심정을 대변할 만한 대답이었을 것이다."], ["Casaubon’s feelings. But he only bowed resignedly, with due respect to his wife’s uncle, and observed that doubtless the works he mentioned had “served as a resource to a certain order of minds.”", "그러나 그는 그저 아내의 삼촌에 대한 마땅한 예의를 갖추어 체념한 듯 고개를 숙이고는, 그분이 언급한 작품들이 의심할 바 없이 \"어떤 부류의 사람들에게는 위안거리가 되어 왔겠지요\"라고만 말했다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"보시다시피,\""], ["said the able magistrate to Lydgate, when they were outside the door, “Casaubon has been a little narrow: it leaves him rather at a loss when you forbid him his particular work, which I believe is something very deep indeed—in the line of research, you know.", "그 유능한 치안 판사는 문밖으로 나서자 리드게이트에게 말했다. \"카소본은 좀 편협한 데가 있었어요."], ["I would never give way to that; I was always versatile.", "그래서 그 특유의 일을 못 하게 막으면 어찌할 바를 모르고 막막해하지요. 그 일이라는 게 참으로 심오한 것이긴 한 모양입니다만, 알다시피 연구 분야의 일 말이오."], ["But a clergyman is tied a little tight.", "나라면 결코 거기에 그렇게 빠지지 않았을 텐데. 나는 늘 다재다능했거든요."], ["If they would make him a bishop, now!—he did a very good pamphlet for Peel.", "하지만 성직자란 좀 옴짝달싹 못 하지요. 그를 주교로라도 만들어 준다면 좋으련만!"], ["He would have more movement then, more show; he might get a little flesh.", "그는 필을 위해 아주 훌륭한 소책자를 써냈지요. 그러면 활동도 더 많아지고, 드러날 자리도 더 생기고, 살도 좀 붙을 텐데."], ["But I recommend you to talk to Mrs. Casaubon.", "어쨌든 카소본 부인과 이야기해 보시길 권하오."], ["She is clever enough for anything, is my niece.", "내 조카는 뭐든 해낼 만큼 영리한 아이라오."], ["Tell her, her husband wants liveliness, diversion: put her on amusing tactics.”", "남편에게는 활기와 기분 전환이 필요하다고 일러 주시오. 재미있는 방법을 한번 써 보라고 하시오.\""]], [["Without Mr. Brooke’s advice, Lydgate had determined on speaking to Dorothea.", "브룩 씨의 조언이 없었더라도 리드게이트는 도러시아에게 말하기로 작정한 터였다."], ["She had not been present while her uncle was throwing out his pleasant suggestions as to the mode in which life at Lowick might be enlivened, but she was usually by her husband’s side, and the unaffected signs of intense anxiety in her face and voice about whatever touched his mind or health, made a drama which Lydgate was inclined to watch.", "로윅의 생활을 어떻게 활기차게 할 수 있을지에 관해 삼촌이 유쾌한 제안들을 늘어놓는 동안 그녀는 그 자리에 없었지만, 평소에는 늘 남편 곁에 있었다. 남편의 정신이나 건강에 관계된 것이라면 무엇에든 그녀의 얼굴과 목소리에 드러나는 강렬한 불안의 꾸밈없는 기색은, 리드게이트가 지켜보고 싶어지는 하나의 드라마를 이루었다."], ["He said to himself that he was only doing right in telling her the truth about her husband’s probable future, but he certainly thought also that it would be interesting to talk confidentially with her.", "그는 남편의 앞날에 대한 진실을 그녀에게 말하는 것은 그저 옳은 일을 하는 것뿐이라고 스스로에게 말했지만, 한편으로 그녀와 은밀하게 이야기를 나누는 것이 흥미로우리라고도 분명히 생각했다."], ["A medical man likes to make psychological observations, and sometimes in the pursuit of such studies is too easily tempted into momentous prophecy which life and death easily set at nought.", "의사란 심리적 관찰을 즐기는 법이고, 때로 그런 연구에 몰두하다 보면 삶과 죽음이 손쉽게 무위로 돌려 버리는 중대한 예언에 너무 쉽게 빠져들곤 한다."], ["Lydgate had often been satirical on this gratuitous prediction, and he meant now to be guarded.", "리드게이트는 그런 근거 없는 예측을 두고 자주 빈정거려 왔던 터라, 이제는 신중하려고 마음먹었다."]], [["He asked for Mrs. Casaubon, but being told that she was out walking, he was going away, when Dorothea and Celia appeared, both glowing from their struggle with the March wind.", "그는 카소본 부인을 찾았으나 그녀가 산책을 나갔다는 말에 막 돌아가려던 참에, 도러시아와 실리아가 나타났다."], ["When Lydgate begged to speak with her alone, Dorothea opened the library door which happened to be the nearest, thinking of nothing at the moment but what he might have to say about Mr.", "두 사람 다 삼월의 바람과 씨름하느라 발갛게 상기되어 있었다. 리드게이트가 그녀와 단둘이 이야기하기를 청하자, 도러시아는 마침 가장 가까이 있던 서재 문을 열었다."], ["Casaubon. It was the first time she had entered this room since her husband had been taken ill, and the servant had chosen not to open the shutters.", "그 순간 그녀의 머릿속에는 그가 카소본 씨에 관해 무슨 말을 하려는 것일까 하는 생각뿐이었다."], ["But there was light enough to read by from the narrow upper panes of the windows.", "남편이 병을 앓은 이래 그녀가 이 방에 들어온 것은 처음이었고, 하인은 덧창을 열지 않은 채로 두기로 한 모양이었다. 그래도 창문 위쪽의 좁은 유리창으로 들어오는 빛이 책을 읽을 만큼은 되었다."]], [["“You will not mind this sombre light,”", "\"이 어슴푸레한 빛을 개의치 않으시겠지요.\""], ["said Dorothea, standing in the middle of the room.", "도러시아가 방 한가운데 서서 말했다."], ["“Since you forbade books, the library has been out of the question.", "\"선생님이 책을 금하신 뒤로는 서재를 쓸 일이 없었어요."], ["But Mr. Casaubon will soon be here again, I hope.", "하지만 곧 카소본 씨가 다시 이곳에 오실 수 있겠지요."], ["Is he not making progress?”", "차도가 있지 않나요?\""]], [["“Yes, much more rapid progress than I at first expected.", "\"네, 처음 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 빠르게 나아지고 계십니다."], ["Indeed, he is already nearly in his usual state of health.”", "사실 이미 평소의 건강 상태에 거의 가까워지셨습니다.\""]], [["“You do not fear that the illness will return?”", "\"병이 재발할까 봐 염려하시는 건 아니지요?\""], ["said Dorothea, whose quick ear had detected some significance in Lydgate’s tone.", "도러시아가 말했다. 그녀의 예민한 귀는 리드게이트의 어조에서 어떤 의미를 알아챘던 것이다."]], [["“Such cases are peculiarly difficult to pronounce upon,”", "\"이런 경우는 단정해 말씀드리기가 유난히 어렵습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“The only point on which I can be confident is that it will be desirable to be very watchful on Mr. Casaubon’s account, lest he should in any way strain his nervous power.”", "\"제가 확신할 수 있는 유일한 점은, 카소본 씨가 어떤 식으로든 신경의 기력을 무리하게 쓰지 않도록 매우 세심하게 살피는 것이 바람직하다는 것입니다.\""]], [["“I beseech you to speak quite plainly,”", "\"제발 아주 분명하게 말씀해 주세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, in an imploring tone.", "도러시아가 애원하는 어조로 말했다."], ["“I cannot bear to think that there might be something which I did not know, and which, if I had known it, would have made me act differently.”", "\"제가 알지 못하는 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 것, 그리고 만약 제가 그것을 알았더라면 다르게 행동했을 무언가가 있을지도 모른다는 것을 견딜 수가 없어요.\""], ["The words came out like a cry: it was evident that they were the voice of some mental experience which lay not very far off.", "그 말은 외침처럼 터져 나왔다. 그것이 멀지 않은 곳에 자리한 어떤 마음의 체험에서 우러난 목소리임이 분명했다."]], [["“Sit down,”", "\"앉으세요.\""], ["she added, placing herself on the nearest chair, and throwing off her bonnet and gloves, with an instinctive discarding of formality where a great question of destiny was concerned.", "그녀는 가장 가까운 의자에 앉으며 덧붙였다. 운명에 관한 중대한 문제가 걸려 있을 때 본능적으로 격식을 벗어 던지듯, 그녀는 보닛과 장갑을 벗어 던졌다."]], [["“What you say now justifies my own view,” said Lydgate.", "\"지금 하신 말씀이 제 생각이 옳았음을 입증해 줍니다.\""], ["“I think it is one’s function as a medical man to hinder regrets of that sort as far as possible.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"의사로서 그런 후회를 되도록 막아 드리는 것이 제 역할이라고 생각합니다."], ["But I beg you to observe that Mr. Casaubon’s case is precisely of the kind in which the issue is most difficult to pronounce upon.", "하지만 카소본 씨의 경우는 바로 그 결과를 단정하기가 가장 어려운 부류에 속한다는 점을 유념해 주시기 바랍니다."], ["He may possibly live for fifteen years or more, without much worse health than he has had hitherto.”", "어쩌면 지금까지보다 그리 나빠지지 않은 건강으로 십오 년, 혹은 그 이상을 사실 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["Dorothea had turned very pale, and when Lydgate paused she said in a low voice, “You mean if we are very careful.”", "도러시아는 몹시 창백해졌고, 리드게이트가 말을 멈추자 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"아주 조심한다면 그렇다는 말씀이군요.\""]], [["“Yes—careful against mental agitation of all kinds, and against excessive application.”", "\"네, 온갖 종류의 정신적 동요를 막고, 지나친 몰두를 막는다면요.\""]], [["“He would be miserable, if he had to give up his work,”", "\"일을 포기해야 한다면 그분은 비참해하실 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with a quick prevision of that wretchedness.", "도러시아가 그 비참함을 재빨리 내다보며 말했다."]], [["“I am aware of that.", "\"저도 알고 있습니다."], ["The only course is to try by all means, direct and indirect, to moderate and vary his occupations.", "유일한 방법은 직접적이든 간접적이든 모든 수단을 다해 그분의 일을 절제하게 하고 다양하게 하는 것입니다."], ["With a happy concurrence of circumstances, there is, as I said, no immediate danger from that affection of the heart, which I believe to have been the cause of his late attack.", "상황이 순조롭게 맞아떨어진다면, 앞서 말씀드린 대로 지난번 발작의 원인이라고 제가 여기는 그 심장의 질환으로부터 당장의 위험은 없습니다."], ["On the other hand, it is possible that the disease may develop itself more rapidly: it is one of those cases in which death is sometimes sudden.", "한편으로는 병이 더 빠르게 진행될 가능성도 있습니다. 때로 갑작스러운 죽음이 찾아오는 그런 부류의 경우이지요."], ["Nothing should be neglected which might be affected by such an issue.”", "그런 결말에 영향을 받을 만한 것은 무엇 하나 소홀히 해서는 안 됩니다.\""]], [["There was silence for a few moments, while Dorothea sat as if she had been turned to marble, though the life within her was so intense that her mind had never before swept in brief time over an equal range of scenes and motives.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그동안 도러시아는 대리석으로 변해 버린 듯 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그녀 안의 생명은 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그녀의 마음은 그 짧은 시간 동안 일찍이 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 폭넓은 장면과 동기들을 휩쓸어 지나갔다."]], [["“Help me, pray,”", "\"제발 저를 도와주세요.\""], ["she said, at last, in the same low voice as before.", "그녀가 마침내, 아까와 같은 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Tell me what I can do.”", "\"제가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“What do you think of foreign travel?", "\"외국 여행은 어떻게 생각하십니까?"], ["You have been lately in Rome, I think.”", "최근에 로마에 다녀오셨지요?\""]], [["The memories which made this resource utterly hopeless were a new current that shook Dorothea out of her pallid immobility.", "이 방책을 도무지 가망 없게 만드는 기억들이 새로운 물결처럼 밀려와, 도러시아를 그 창백한 부동의 상태에서 흔들어 깨웠다."]], [["“Oh, that would not do—that would be worse than anything,”", "\"아, 그건 안 돼요. 그건 무엇보다 더 나쁠 거예요.\""], ["she said with a more childlike despondency, while the tears rolled down.", "그녀가 한층 어린아이 같은 낙담에 잠겨 말하는 동안 눈물이 흘러내렸다."], ["“Nothing will be of any use that he does not enjoy.”", "\"그분이 즐기지 못하는 것은 아무 소용이 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“I wish that I could have spared you this pain,”", "\"이 고통을 덜어 드릴 수 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\""], ["said Lydgate, deeply touched, yet wondering about her marriage.", "리드게이트가 깊이 마음이 움직인 채 말했다. 그러면서도 그녀의 결혼이 의아하게 여겨졌다."], ["Women just like Dorothea had not entered into his traditions.", "도러시아 같은 여인은 그가 지닌 관념의 틀에 들어 있지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“It was right of you to tell me.", "\"말씀해 주신 것은 옳은 일이었어요."], ["I thank you for telling me the truth.”", "진실을 말씀해 주셔서 감사해요.\""]], [["“I wish you to understand that I shall not say anything to enlighten Mr. Casaubon himself.", "\"카소본 씨 본인에게는 무엇도 일러 드리지 않을 것임을 이해해 주시기 바랍니다."], ["I think it desirable for him to know nothing more than that he must not overwork himself, and must observe certain rules.", "그분은 무리하게 일해서는 안 되며 몇 가지 규칙을 지켜야 한다는 것 외에는 더 아무것도 모르시는 편이 바람직하다고 생각합니다."], ["Anxiety of any kind would be precisely the most unfavorable condition for him.”", "어떤 종류의 불안이든 그분에게는 바로 가장 좋지 않은 상태일 테니까요.\""]], [["Lydgate rose, and Dorothea mechanically rose at the same time, unclasping her cloak and throwing it off as if it stifled her.", "리드게이트가 일어서자 도러시아도 무심결에 동시에 일어나, 숨이 막힌다는 듯 망토를 풀어 벗어 던졌다."], ["He was bowing and quitting her, when an impulse which if she had been alone would have turned into a prayer, made her say with a sob in her voice—", "그가 인사를 하고 자리를 뜨려는데, 만약 그녀가 혼자였더라면 기도로 바뀌었을 어떤 충동이 그녀로 하여금 흐느낌 섞인 목소리로 이렇게 말하게 했다."]], [["“Oh, you are a wise man, are you not?", "\"아, 선생님은 현명한 분이시지요?"], ["You know all about life and death.", "삶과 죽음에 관해 다 아시잖아요. 제게 조언해 주세요."], ["Advise me. Think what I can do.", "제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 생각해 주세요."], ["He has been laboring all his life and looking forward.", "그분은 평생을 애써 일하며 앞날을 바라보아 왔어요."], ["He minds about nothing else.— And I mind about nothing else—”", "그분은 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 마음을 두지 않으세요. 그리고 저도 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 마음을 두지 않아요.\""]], [["For years after Lydgate remembered the impression produced in him by this involuntary appeal—this cry from soul to soul, without other consciousness than their moving with kindred natures in the same embroiled medium, the same troublous fitfully illuminated life.", "그 후로 오랜 세월 동안 리드게이트는 이 무의식적인 호소가, 영혼에서 영혼으로 건너간 이 외침이 자기 안에 남긴 인상을 기억했다. 같은 뒤엉킨 매질 속에서, 같은 어수선하고 간헐적으로만 밝혀지는 삶 속에서 비슷한 천성을 지닌 채 함께 움직이고 있다는 의식 외에 다른 의식이라곤 없는 외침이었다."], ["But what could he say now except that he should see Mr. Casaubon again to-morrow?", "그러나 지금 그가 내일 다시 카소본 씨를 보러 오겠다는 말 외에 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["When he was gone, Dorothea’s tears gushed forth, and relieved her stifling oppression.", "그가 떠나자 도러시아의 눈물이 쏟아져 나왔고, 그녀의 숨 막히는 답답함을 덜어 주었다. 이윽고 그녀는 자신의 괴로움을 남편에게 들켜서는 안 된다는 것을 떠올리며 눈물을 닦았다."], ["Then she dried her eyes, reminded that her distress must not be betrayed to her husband; and looked round the room thinking that she must order the servant to attend to it as usual, since Mr. Casaubon might now at any moment wish to enter.", "그리고 카소본 씨가 이제 언제든 들어오고 싶어 할지도 모르니 하인에게 평소처럼 방을 정돈하라고 일러야겠다고 생각하며 방 안을 둘러보았다."], ["On his writing-table there were letters which had lain untouched since the morning when he was taken ill, and among them, as Dorothea well remembered, there were young Ladislaw’s letters, the one addressed to her still unopened.", "그의 책상 위에는 병으로 쓰러지던 날 아침 이래 손대지 않은 채로 놓여 있는 편지들이 있었고, 도러시아가 잘 기억하고 있듯이 그 가운데에는 젊은 래디슬로의 편지들이 있었으며, 그녀 앞으로 온 편지는 아직 봉투조차 뜯지 않은 채였다. 이 편지들과 얽힌 일들은, 자신의 분노가 일으킨 동요가 어느 정도 부추겼을지도 모른다고 그녀가 느끼는 그 갑작스러운 발작 때문에 한층 더 고통스러워졌다."], ["The associations of these letters had been made the more painful by that sudden attack of illness which she felt that the agitation caused by her anger might have helped to bring on: it would be time enough to read them when they were again thrust upon her, and she had had no inclination to fetch them from the library.", "그것들이 다시 그녀 앞에 들이밀어질 때 읽어도 충분하리라 여겨, 그녀는 서재에서 그것들을 가져올 마음이 도무지 들지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 그 편지들을 남편의 눈에 띄지 않게 치워야겠다는 생각이 들었다."], ["But now it occurred to her that they should be put out of her husband’s sight: whatever might have been the sources of his annoyance about them, he must, if possible, not be annoyed again; and she ran her eyes first over the letter addressed to him to assure herself whether or not it would be necessary to write in order to hinder the offensive visit.", "그것들에 관한 그의 언짢음이 무엇에서 비롯되었든, 그가 다시는 언짢아지지 않게 해야 했다. 그래서 그녀는 그 불쾌한 방문을 막기 위해 편지를 써야 할지 어떨지 확인하려고, 먼저 그에게 온 편지를 훑어보았다."]], [["Will wrote from Rome, and began by saying that his obligations to Mr. Casaubon were too deep for all thanks not to seem impertinent.", "윌은 로마에서 편지를 썼는데, 카소본 씨에게 진 신세가 너무도 깊어서 어떤 감사의 말도 무례하게 보이지 않을 도리가 없다는 말로 시작했다."], ["It was plain that if he were not grateful, he must be the poorest-spirited rascal who had ever found a generous friend.", "만약 그가 감사할 줄 모른다면, 너그러운 벗을 만난 자 가운데 가장 비루한 정신의 악당일 것이 분명했다."], ["To expand in wordy thanks would be like saying, “I am honest.”", "장황한 감사의 말을 늘어놓는 것은 \"나는 정직합니다\"라고 말하는 것과 같았다."], ["But Will had come to perceive that his defects—defects which Mr. Casaubon had himself often pointed to—needed for their correction that more strenuous position which his relative’s generosity had hitherto prevented from being inevitable.", "그러나 윌은, 자신의 결점들, 카소본 씨 자신도 자주 지적했던 그 결점들을 고치려면 더 분발하는 처지가 필요한데, 지금까지는 친척의 너그러움이 그것을 불가피하게 만들지 않았음을 깨닫게 되었다."], ["He trusted that he should make the best return, if return were possible, by showing the effectiveness of the education for which he was indebted, and by ceasing in future to need any diversion towards himself of funds on which others might have a better claim.", "그는, 만약 보답이 가능하다면, 신세를 진 그 교육의 효과를 보여 주고, 앞으로는 다른 이들이 더 마땅한 권리를 가졌을지 모를 자금을 자기에게 돌리지 않도록 함으로써 최선의 보답을 하리라 믿었다."], ["He was coming to England, to try his fortune, as many other young men were obliged to do whose only capital was in their brains.", "그는 두뇌만이 유일한 밑천인 다른 많은 젊은이들이 그래야 하듯이, 자신의 운을 시험하러 영국으로 갈 참이었다."], ["His friend Naumann had desired him to take charge of the “Dispute”—the picture painted for Mr. Casaubon, with whose permission, and Mrs.", "그의 벗 나우만이 그에게 '논쟁'을, 곧 카소본 씨를 위해 그린 그림을 맡아 달라고 부탁했기에, 그분과 카소본 부인의 허락을 받아 윌이 직접 그것을 로윅으로 가져갈 것이었다."], ["Casaubon’s, Will would convey it to Lowick in person. A letter addressed to the Poste Restante in Paris within the fortnight would hinder him, if necessary, from arriving at an inconvenient moment.", "보름 안에 파리의 유치(留置) 우편으로 편지를 보내면, 필요할 경우 그가 부적절한 시기에 도착하는 것을 막을 수 있을 터였다."], ["He enclosed a letter to Mrs. Casaubon in which he continued a discussion about art, begun with her in Rome.", "그는 카소본 부인 앞으로 보내는 편지를 동봉했는데, 그 안에서 로마에서 그녀와 시작했던 예술에 관한 논의를 이어 가고 있었다."]], [["Opening her own letter Dorothea saw that it was a lively continuation of his remonstrance with her fanatical sympathy and her want of sturdy neutral delight in things as they were—an outpouring of his young vivacity which it was impossible to read just now.", "자신에게 온 편지를 펴 보고 도러시아는, 그것이 그녀의 광적인 동정심과, 사물을 있는 그대로 받아들이며 누리는 굳건하고 중립적인 즐거움의 결여에 대해 그가 항변하던 것을 활기차게 이어 가고 있음을 알았다. 그것은 그의 젊은 발랄함이 쏟아져 나온 것이어서, 지금 당장은 도무지 읽을 수가 없었다."], ["She had immediately to consider what was to be done about the other letter: there was still time perhaps to prevent Will from coming to Lowick.", "그녀는 곧바로 다른 편지에 대해 어떻게 해야 할지 궁리해야 했다. 어쩌면 윌이 로윅에 오는 것을 막을 시간이 아직 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["Dorothea ended by giving the letter to her uncle, who was still in the house, and begging him to let Will know that Mr. Casaubon had been ill, and that his health would not allow the reception of any visitors.", "도러시아는 결국 아직 집에 있던 삼촌에게 그 편지를 건네며, 카소본 씨가 병을 앓았고 건강 때문에 어떤 손님도 맞을 수 없게 되었다는 사실을 윌에게 알려 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["No one more ready than Mr.", "편지 쓰는 일이라면 브룩 씨만큼 기꺼운 사람도 없었다."], ["Brooke to write a letter: his only difficulty was to write a short one, and his ideas in this case expanded over the three large pages and the inward foldings.", "그의 유일한 어려움은 짧게 쓰는 것이었으니, 이 경우 그의 생각은 큼직한 종이 석 장과 안으로 접힌 부분까지 가득 펼쳐졌다."], ["He had simply said to Dorothea—", "그는 도러시아에게는 그저 이렇게만 말했다."]], [["“To be sure, I will write, my dear.", "\"물론 쓰고말고, 얘야."], ["He’s a very clever young fellow—this young Ladislaw—I dare say will be a rising young man.", "아주 영리한 청년이지, 이 젊은 래디슬로 말이다. 장차 두각을 나타낼 젊은이일 게야."], ["It’s a good letter—marks his sense of things, you know.", "잘 쓴 편지야, 알다시피 사물을 보는 분별이 드러나 있어."], ["However, I will tell him about Casaubon.”", "어쨌든 카소본 일은 내가 알려 주마.\""]], [["But the end of Mr. Brooke’s pen was a thinking organ, evolving sentences, especially of a benevolent kind, before the rest of his mind could well overtake them.", "그러나 브룩 씨의 펜 끝은 하나의 사고 기관이어서, 특히 자비로운 종류의 문장이라면 그의 나머지 정신이 미처 따라잡기도 전에 줄줄 빚어냈다."], ["It expressed regrets and proposed remedies, which, when Mr. Brooke read them, seemed felicitously worded—surprisingly the right thing, and determined a sequel which he had never before thought of.", "그것은 유감을 표하고 해결책을 제안했는데, 브룩 씨가 읽어 보면 그 글들은 절묘하게 표현된 것처럼, 놀랍도록 적절한 것처럼 보였고, 그가 전에는 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없는 결말로 이어졌다."], ["In this case, his pen found it such a pity young Ladislaw should not have come into the neighborhood just at that time, in order that Mr.", "이 경우 그의 펜은, 자신이 젊은 래디슬로와 더 충분히 사귀고, 오래 방치되어 온 이탈리아 그림들을 함께 살펴볼 수 있도록, 바로 그 시기에 그가 이 근방으로 오지 못하는 것이 참으로 안타까운 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["Brooke might make his acquaintance more fully, and that they might go over the long-neglected Italian drawings together—it also felt such an interest in a young man who was starting in life with a stock of ideas—that by the end of the second page it had persuaded Mr.", "또한 한 보따리의 사상을 품고 인생을 시작하려는 젊은이에게 어찌나 흥미를 느꼈던지, 둘째 장의 끝에 이르렀을 무렵 그것은 브룩 씨를 설득하여, 로윅에서 맞이할 수 없다면 젊은 래디슬로를 티프턴 그레인지로 초대하게 만들었다."], ["Brooke to invite young Ladislaw, since he could not be received at Lowick, to come to Tipton Grange.", "안 될 게 무어란 말인가? 둘이서 함께 할 만한 일이 아주 많을 테고, 지금은 유난히 성장하는 시기였다."], ["Why not? They could find a great many things to do together, and this was a period of peculiar growth—the political horizon was expanding, and—in short, Mr.", "정치적 지평이 넓어지고 있었으며, 요컨대 브룩 씨의 펜은 최근 그가 그 미흡하게 편집된 기관지 '미들마치 파이어니어'에 실으려고 기고했던 짤막한 연설로 흘러 들어갔다."], ["Brooke’s pen went off into a little speech which it had lately reported for that imperfectly edited organ the “Middlemarch Pioneer.”", "이 편지를 봉하면서 브룩 씨는 어렴풋한 계획들이 밀려드는 데 들떴다."], ["While Mr. Brooke was sealing this letter, he felt elated with an influx of dim projects:—a young man capable of putting ideas into form, the “Pioneer”", "사상을 형태로 빚어낼 줄 아는 젊은이, 새 후보를 위해 길을 닦으려고 사들인 '파이어니어', 활용될 문서들, 이 모든 것에서 무슨 일이 생겨날지 누가 알겠는가?"], ["purchased to clear the pathway for a new candidate, documents utilized—who knew what might come of it all?", "실리아가 곧 결혼할 참이니, 적어도 한동안은 식탁에 젊은이 하나를 함께 두는 것이 무척 즐거운 일일 터였다."], ["Since Celia was going to marry immediately, it would be very pleasant to have a young fellow at table with him, at least for a time.", "그러나 그는 도러시아에게 편지에 무엇을 써 넣었는지 말하지 않은 채 떠났다. 그녀가 남편을 돌보느라 바빴기 때문이기도 했고, 사실 이런 일들은 그녀에게 아무런 중요성도 없었기 때문이다."]], [["But he went away without telling Dorothea what he had put into the letter, for she was engaged with her husband, and—in fact, these things were of no importance to her.", "* 패류학: 조개류를 연구하는 학문. * 유치 우편: 수신인이 우체국에 직접 가서 받을 수 있도록 보관해 두는 우편물."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXI.", "# 제28장"]], [["How will you know the pitch of that great bell Too large for you to stir?", "정교하게 섞인 금속 아래서 울리게 하라."], ["Let but a flute Play ’neath the fine-mixed metal: listen close Till the right note flows forth, a silvery rill: Then shall the huge bell tremble—then the mass With myriad waves concurrent shall respond In low soft unison.", "가만히 귀 기울여\n맞는 음이 흘러나오기를 기다리라, 은빛 시냇물처럼."]], [["Lydgate that evening spoke to Miss Vincy of Mrs. Casaubon, and laid some emphasis on the strong feeling she appeared to have for that formal studious man thirty years older than herself.", "그러면 그 거대한 종이 떨리리니—그러면 그 덩어리가\n함께 일렁이는 무수한 파동으로"]], [["“Of course she is devoted to her husband,”", "낮고 부드러운 화음으로 응답하리라."], ["said Rosamond, implying a notion of necessary sequence which the scientific man regarded as the prettiest possible for a woman; but she was thinking at the same time that it was not so very melancholy to be mistress of Lowick Manor with a husband likely to die soon. “Do you think her very handsome?”", "그날 저녁 리드게이트는 빈시 양에게 캐소본 부인 이야기를 했는데, 자기보다 서른 살이나 많은 그 격식 차린 학구적인 남자에게 그녀가 품은 듯한 강렬한 감정을 다소 강조했다."]], [["“She certainly is handsome, but I have not thought about it,”", "\"물론 그분은 남편에게 헌신적이지요.\""], ["said Lydgate. “I suppose it would be unprofessional,”", "로저먼드가 말했는데, 거기에는 어떤 필연적인 귀결의 관념이 담겨 있었으니, 그 과학자는 그것을 여자에게 더없이 어여쁜 태도로 여겼다."], ["said Rosamond, dimpling. “But how your practice is spreading!", "그러나 그녀는 동시에, 머지않아 죽을 듯한 남편을 두고 로윅 영지의 안주인이 된다는 것이 그리 서글픈 일만은 아니라고 생각하고 있었다."], ["You were called in before to the Chettams, I think; and now, the Casaubons.”", "\"그분이 아주 아름답다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"분명 아름다운 분이지요."], ["said Lydgate, in a tone of compulsory admission.", "하지만 그 점을 생각해 본 적은 없습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“But I don’t really like attending such people so well as the poor.", "\"그건 직업상 어울리지 않는 일이겠지요.\" 로저먼드가 보조개를 지으며 말했다."], ["The cases are more monotonous, and one has to go through more fuss and listen more deferentially to nonsense.”", "\"하지만 진료 범위가 정말 넓어지고 계시네요! 전에는 체텀 가에 불려 가셨던 것 같고, 이제는 캐소본 가까지.\""]], [["“Not more than in Middlemarch,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Rosamond.", "리드게이트가 마지못해 인정하는 어조로 말했다."], ["“And at least you go through wide corridors and have the scent of rose-leaves everywhere.”", "\"하지만 사실 저는 그런 사람들을 진료하는 것이 가난한 이들을 돌보는 것만큼 좋지가 않습니다. 증상은 더 단조롭고, 더 많은 야단법석을 거쳐야 하고, 터무니없는 말에 더 공손히 귀를 기울여야 하니까요.\""]], [["“That is true, Mademoiselle de Montmorenci,” said Lydgate, just bending his head to the table and lifting with his fourth finger her delicate handkerchief which lay at the mouth of her reticule, as if to enjoy its scent, while he looked at her with a smile.", "\"미들마치에서보다 더하지는 않겠지요.\" 로저먼드가 말했다. \"그래도 적어도 넓은 복도를 지나다니시고, 가는 곳마다 장미 잎 향기가 나잖아요.\" \"그건 사실입니다, 몽모랑시 양.\" 리드게이트가 탁자 쪽으로 살짝 머리를 숙이며, 마치 향기를 즐기려는 듯 넷째 손가락으로 그녀의 손가방 입구에 놓여 있던 섬세한 손수건을 들어 올리고는, 미소 띤 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["But this agreeable holiday freedom with which Lydgate hovered about the flower of Middlemarch, could not continue indefinitely.", "그러나 리드게이트가 미들마치의 꽃 주위를 맴돌며 누리던 이 유쾌한 휴일 같은 자유가 언제까지나 이어질 수는 없었다."], ["It was not more possible to find social isolation in that town than elsewhere, and two people persistently flirting could by no means escape from “the various entanglements, weights, blows, clashings, motions, by which things severally go on.”", "그 도시에서 사회적 고립을 찾기란 다른 어디서나 마찬가지로 가능하지 않은 일이었고, 끈질기게 시시덕거리는 두 사람이 \"여러 가지가 제각기 굴러가게 하는 갖가지 얽힘과 무게와 타격과 충돌과 운동\"에서 결코 벗어날 수는 없었다."], ["Whatever Miss Vincy did must be remarked, and she was perhaps the more conspicuous to admirers and critics because just now Mrs.", "빈시 양이 무엇을 하든 다 사람들의 입에 오르내렸으니, 어쩌면 그녀는 지금 더더욱 찬미자들과 비평가들의 눈에 띄었을 것이다."], ["Vincy, after some struggle, had gone with Fred to stay a little while at Stone Court, there being no other way of at once gratifying old Featherstone and keeping watch against Mary Garth, who appeared a less tolerable daughter-in-law in proportion as Fred’s illness disappeared.", "마침 이즈음 빈시 부인이 얼마간의 갈등 끝에 프레드를 데리고 스톤 코트에 잠시 머물러 있었기 때문이다. 늙은 페더스톤을 흡족하게 하는 동시에 메리 가스를 경계할 다른 방도가 없었으니, 메리 가스는 프레드의 병이 나아 갈수록 점점 더 며느릿감으로 탐탁지 않아 보였다."]], [["Aunt Bulstrode, for example, came a little oftener into Lowick Gate to see Rosamond, now she was alone. For Mrs.", "이를테면 벌스트로드 이모는 로저먼드가 이제 혼자 지내게 되자 그녀를 보러 로윅 게이트에 조금 더 자주 들렀다."], ["Bulstrode had a true sisterly feeling for her brother; always thinking that he might have married better, but wishing well to the children. Now Mrs.", "벌스트로드 부인은 오빠에 대해 진정 누이다운 애정을 품고 있었으니, 늘 오빠가 더 나은 결혼을 할 수도 있었으리라 생각하면서도 그 아이들에게는 잘되기를 바랐다."], ["Bulstrode had a long-standing intimacy with Mrs. Plymdale.", "그런데 벌스트로드 부인은 플림데일 부인과 오랜 친분이 있었다."], ["They had nearly the same preferences in silks, patterns for underclothing, china-ware, and clergymen; they confided their little troubles of health and household management to each other, and various little points of superiority on Mrs.", "두 사람은 비단이며 속옷의 무늬며 도자기며 성직자에 대해 거의 같은 취향을 지녔고, 건강과 살림살이의 자잘한 고민을 서로 털어놓았다."], ["Bulstrode’s side, namely, more decided seriousness, more admiration for mind, and a house outside the town, sometimes served to give color to their conversation without dividing them—well-meaning women both, knowing very little of their own motives.", "벌스트로드 부인 쪽의 몇몇 자잘한 우월함, 이를테면 더 확고한 진지함, 정신적인 것에 대한 더 깊은 경탄, 그리고 도시 밖에 있는 저택 같은 것은 이따금 두 사람의 대화에 활기를 더해 주면서도 둘 사이를 갈라놓지는 않았다. 둘 다 선의를 지닌 여자들이었고, 자기 자신의 동기에 대해서는 거의 알지 못했다."]], [["Mrs. Bulstrode, paying a morning visit to Mrs. Plymdale, happened to say that she could not stay longer, because she was going to see poor Rosamond.", "벌스트로드 부인은 플림데일 부인을 오전에 방문했다가, 가엾은 로저먼드를 보러 가야 하니 더 오래 머물 수 없다는 말을 우연히 꺼냈다."]], [["“Why do you say ‘poor Rosamond’?”", "\"왜 '가엾은 로저먼드'라고 하세요?\""], ["said Mrs. Plymdale, a round-eyed sharp little woman, like a tamed falcon.", "길든 매처럼 동그란 눈을 한, 야무지고 자그마한 여인 플림데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["“She is so pretty, and has been brought up in such thoughtlessness.", "\"그 아이가 워낙 예쁜 데다 너무 분별없이 자랐거든요."], ["The mother, you know, had always that levity about her, which makes me anxious for the children.”", "알다시피 그 어머니가 늘 그런 경박함이 있어서, 그게 아이들 일로 저를 걱정하게 만든답니다.\""]], [["“Well, Harriet, if I am to speak my mind,”", "\"글쎄요, 해리엇, 제 마음을 솔직히 말하라면,\""], ["said Mrs.", "플림데일 부인이 힘주어 말했다."], ["Plymdale, with emphasis, “I must say, anybody would suppose you and Mr.", "\"이렇게 말할 수밖에 없네요. 누가 봐도 댁이며 벌스트로드 씨가 이번 일을 기뻐하실 거라고 생각할 거예요."], ["Bulstrode would be delighted with what has happened, for you have done everything to put Mr. Lydgate forward.”", "댁이야말로 리드게이트 씨를 내세우려고 온갖 일을 다 하셨으니까요.\""]], [["“Selina, what do you mean?”", "\"실리나, 무슨 말씀이세요?\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, in genuine surprise.", "벌스트로드 부인이 정말로 놀라서 말했다."]], [["“Not but what I am truly thankful for Ned’s sake,”", "\"네드를 생각하면 진심으로 고맙기는 하지만요.\""], ["said Mrs. Plymdale.", "플림데일 부인이 말했다."], ["“He could certainly better afford to keep such a wife than some people can; but I should wish him to look elsewhere.", "\"그 애야 분명 여느 사람들보다 그런 아내를 거뜬히 부양할 수 있겠지요. 그래도 저는 그 애가 다른 데를 알아보길 바라요."], ["Still a mother has anxieties, and some young men would take to a bad life in consequence.", "그래도 어미한테는 걱정이 있는 법이고, 어떤 젊은이들은 그 일로 못된 길로 빠지기도 하니까요."], ["Besides, if I was obliged to speak, I should say I was not fond of strangers coming into a town.”", "게다가 굳이 말하라면, 저는 외지 사람이 도시로 들어오는 게 마뜩잖다고 말하겠어요.\""]], [["“I don’t know, Selina,”", "\"글쎄요, 실리나.\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, with a little emphasis in her turn.", "이번에는 벌스트로드 부인이 조금 힘주어 말했다."], ["“Mr. Bulstrode was a stranger here at one time.", "\"벌스트로드 씨도 한때는 이곳에 외지 사람이었어요."], ["Abraham and Moses were strangers in the land, and we are told to entertain strangers.", "아브라함과 모세도 그 땅에서 나그네였고, 우리는 나그네를 후히 대접하라는 가르침을 받았잖아요."], ["And especially,”", "더욱이,\""], ["she added, after a slight pause, “when they are unexceptionable.”", "그녀는 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"흠잡을 데 없는 사람이라면 말이지요.\""]], [["“I was not speaking in a religious sense, Harriet.", "\"저는 종교적인 뜻으로 말한 게 아니에요, 해리엇."], ["I spoke as a mother.”", "어미로서 말한 거예요.\""]], [["“Selina, I am sure you have never heard me say anything against a niece of mine marrying your son.”", "\"실리나, 제가 제 조카가 댁의 아들과 결혼하는 것에 대해 무슨 반대하는 말을 한 적은 결코 없을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Oh, it is pride in Miss Vincy—I am sure it is nothing else,”", "\"아, 그건 빈시 양의 자존심이에요. 분명히 다른 게 아니라고요.\""], ["said Mrs. Plymdale, who had never before given all her confidence to “Harriet”", "이 일에 대해 한 번도 '해리엇'에게 속내를 다 털어놓은 적이 없던 플림데일 부인이 말했다."], ["on this subject. “No young man in Middlemarch was good enough for her: I have heard her mother say as much.", "\"미들마치의 어떤 젊은이도 그 애한테는 성에 차지 않았어요."], ["That is not a Christian spirit, I think.", "그 어미가 그렇게 말하는 걸 제가 들었거든요."], ["But now, from all I hear, she has found a man as proud as herself.”", "그건 그리스도교 정신이 아니라고 생각해요. 그런데 이제 제가 듣자 하니, 그 애가 저 못지않게 자존심 센 남자를 만났더군요.\""]], [["“You don’t mean that there is anything between Rosamond and Mr. Lydgate?”", "\"로저먼드와 리드게이트 씨 사이에 뭔가 있다는 말씀은 아니겠지요?\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, rather mortified at finding out her own ignorance.", "자기 자신의 무지를 깨닫고 다소 무안해진 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Is it possible you don’t know, Harriet?”", "\"설마 모르고 계셨던 거예요, 해리엇?\""]], [["“Oh, I go about so little; and I am not fond of gossip; I really never hear any.", "\"아, 제가 워낙 바깥출입을 거의 안 하잖아요. 게다가 저는 남 얘기 하는 걸 좋아하지 않아서 정말 아무것도 못 들어요."], ["You see so many people that I don’t see.", "댁은 제가 못 보는 사람들을 무척 많이 만나시잖아요."], ["Your circle is rather different from ours.”", "댁의 교제 범위는 우리와 좀 다르니까요.\""]], [["“Well, but your own niece and Mr. Bulstrode’s great favorite—and yours too, I am sure, Harriet!", "\"하지만 댁의 친조카이자 벌스트로드 씨가 그렇게 아끼는 사람인데, 그리고 댁도 분명 아끼시고요, 해리엇!"], ["I thought, at one time, you meant him for Kate, when she is a little older.”", "저는 한때 케이트가 좀 더 크면 그 사람을 케이트한테 점찍어 두신 줄 알았다니까요.\""]], [["“I don’t believe there can be anything serious at present,”", "\"지금으로서는 무슨 진지한 일이 있을 리 없다고 봐요.\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode.", "벌스트로드 부인이 말했다."], ["“My brother would certainly have told me.”", "\"그렇다면 오라버니가 분명 제게 말했을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Well, people have different ways, but I understand that nobody can see Miss Vincy and Mr. Lydgate together without taking them to be engaged.", "\"뭐, 사람마다 방식이 다르긴 하지만, 제가 알기로는 빈시 양과 리드게이트 씨가 함께 있는 걸 보고 약혼한 사이라고 여기지 않을 사람은 아무도 없어요."], ["However, it is not my business.", "하지만 제가 상관할 바는 아니지요."], ["Shall I put up the pattern of mittens?”", "이 손모아장갑 본 좀 챙겨 둘까요?\""]], [["After this Mrs. Bulstrode drove to her niece with a mind newly weighted.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 벌스트로드 부인은 새삼 무거워진 마음으로 마차를 몰아 조카에게 갔다."], ["She was herself handsomely dressed, but she noticed with a little more regret than usual that Rosamond, who was just come in and met her in walking-dress, was almost as expensively equipped.", "그녀 자신도 곱게 차려입었지만, 마침 들어와서 외출복 차림으로 자신을 맞이한 로저먼드가 거의 자기만큼이나 값비싸게 차려입은 것을 평소보다 조금 더 안타까운 마음으로 알아보았다."], ["Mrs. Bulstrode was a feminine smaller edition of her brother, and had none of her husband’s low-toned pallor.", "벌스트로드 부인은 오빠를 여성스럽게 축소해 놓은 판본 같았고, 남편의 그 나직한 창백함은 조금도 지니고 있지 않았다."], ["She had a good honest glance and used no circumlocution.", "그녀의 눈길은 정직하고 선했으며, 그녀는 에두르는 법이 없었다."]], [["“You are alone, I see, my dear,”", "\"보니 너 혼자 있구나, 얘야.\""], ["she said, as they entered the drawing-room together, looking round gravely.", "그들이 함께 응접실로 들어서면서 그녀가 진지하게 둘러보며 말했다."], ["Rosamond felt sure that her aunt had something particular to say, and they sat down near each other.", "로저먼드는 이모가 무언가 특별히 할 말이 있음을 직감했고, 두 사람은 가까이 마주 앉았다."], ["Nevertheless, the quilling inside Rosamond’s bonnet was so charming that it was impossible not to desire the same kind of thing for Kate, and Mrs.", "그럼에도 로저먼드의 보닛 안쪽 주름 장식이 어찌나 매력적이던지, 케이트에게도 똑같은 것을 마련해 주고 싶다는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었다."], ["Bulstrode’s eyes, which were rather fine, rolled round that ample quilled circuit, while she spoke.", "그래서 벌스트로드 부인은 꽤 고운 편인 눈으로 그 넓은 주름 장식의 둘레를 굴리며 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have just heard something about you that has surprised me very much, Rosamond.”", "\"방금 너에 관해 아주 놀라운 이야기를 들었단다, 로저먼드.\""]], [["“What is that, aunt?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 이모?\""], ["Rosamond’s eyes also were roaming over her aunt’s large embroidered collar.", "로저먼드의 눈길 역시 이모의 큼직한 자수 옷깃 위를 돌아다니고 있었다."]], [["“I can hardly believe it—that you should be engaged without my knowing it—without your father’s telling me.”", "\"도무지 믿을 수가 없구나. 네가 약혼을 했는데 내가 모르고 있다니, 네 아버지가 내게 말도 안 해 주다니.\""], ["Here Mrs. Bulstrode’s eyes finally rested on Rosamond’s, who blushed deeply, and said—", "여기서 벌스트로드 부인의 눈길이 마침내 로저먼드의 눈에 가 닿았고, 로저먼드는 얼굴이 빨개지며 말했다."]], [["“I am not engaged, aunt.”", "\"저는 약혼하지 않았어요, 이모.\""]], [["“How is it that every one says so, then—that it is the town’s talk?”", "\"그럼 어째서 다들 그렇게 말하지? 온 도시의 이야깃거리던데?\""]], [["“The town’s talk is of very little consequence, I think,”", "\"온 도시의 이야깃거리야 별 대수롭지 않다고 생각해요.\""], ["said Rosamond, inwardly gratified.", "로저먼드가 속으로는 흐뭇해하며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, my dear, be more thoughtful; don’t despise your neighbors so.", "\"아, 얘야, 좀 더 분별 있게 굴어라. 이웃을 그렇게 깔보지 말고."], ["Remember you are turned twenty-two now, and you will have no fortune: your father, I am sure, will not be able to spare you anything.", "너도 이제 스물둘이 넘었고 재산도 없을 거란 걸 기억해라. 네 아버지가 너한테 무얼 떼어 줄 형편이 못 된다는 건 분명하니까."], ["Mr. Lydgate is very intellectual and clever; I know there is an attraction in that.", "리드게이트 씨는 무척 지적이고 영리하지."], ["I like talking to such men myself; and your uncle finds him very useful.", "거기에 끌리는 게 있다는 건 나도 알아. 나 자신도 그런 남자들과 이야기하는 걸 좋아하고, 네 이모부도 그 사람을 아주 요긴하게 여기니까."], ["But the profession is a poor one here.", "하지만 여기서는 그 직업이 변변치 못해."], ["To be sure, this life is not everything; but it is seldom a medical man has true religious views—there is too much pride of intellect.", "물론 이 세상살이가 전부는 아니지. 하지만 의사가 참된 신앙심을 지닌 경우는 드물단다."], ["And you are not fit to marry a poor man.", "지성에 대한 자만이 지나치거든. 그리고 너는 가난한 남자와 결혼하기에는 어울리지 않는 아이야.\""]], [["“Mr. Lydgate is not a poor man, aunt.", "\"리드게이트 씨는 가난한 사람이 아니에요, 이모."], ["He has very high connections.”", "그분은 아주 훌륭한 집안과 연줄이 있어요.\""]], [["“He told me himself he was poor.”", "\"그 사람이 직접 자기가 가난하다고 내게 말했단다.\""]], [["“That is because he is used to people who have a high style of living.”", "\"그건 그분이 호화롭게 사는 사람들에게 익숙해서 그런 거예요.\""]], [["“My dear Rosamond, you must not think of living in high style.”", "\"얘, 로저먼드, 너는 호화롭게 살 생각을 해선 안 된다.\""]], [["Rosamond looked down and played with her reticule.", "로저먼드는 눈을 내리깔고 손가방을 만지작거렸다."], ["She was not a fiery young lady and had no sharp answers, but she meant to live as she pleased.", "그녀는 불같은 아가씨도 아니었고 날카롭게 대꾸할 줄도 몰랐지만, 자기 마음 내키는 대로 살 작정이었다."]], [["“Then it is really true?”", "\"그럼 정말이로구나?\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, looking very earnestly at her niece.", "벌스트로드 부인이 조카를 매우 진지하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["“You are thinking of Mr. Lydgate—there is some understanding between you, though your father doesn’t know.", "\"너는 리드게이트 씨를 마음에 두고 있고, 네 아버지는 모르지만 너희 사이에 무슨 약조가 있는 거야."], ["Be open, my dear Rosamond: Mr.", "솔직히 말해 보렴, 얘 로저먼드."], ["Lydgate has really made you an offer?”", "리드게이트 씨가 정말로 너에게 청혼을 한 거니?\""]], [["Poor Rosamond’s feelings were very unpleasant.", "가엾은 로저먼드의 심정은 몹시 언짢았다."], ["She had been quite easy as to Lydgate’s feeling and intention, but now when her aunt put this question she did not like being unable to say Yes.", "그녀는 리드게이트의 감정과 의향에 대해 마음을 아주 놓고 있었지만, 막상 이모가 이런 질문을 하자 그렇다고 대답할 수 없는 것이 싫었다."], ["Her pride was hurt, but her habitual control of manner helped her.", "자존심이 상했으나, 몸에 밴 태도의 절제가 그녀를 도왔다."]], [["“Pray excuse me, aunt.", "\"용서하세요, 이모."], ["I would rather not speak on the subject.”", "그 이야기는 하지 않는 편이 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“You would not give your heart to a man without a decided prospect, I trust, my dear.", "\"확실한 전망도 없는 남자에게 네 마음을 줄 리는 없겠지, 얘야."], ["And think of the two excellent offers I know of that you have refused!—and one still within your reach, if you will not throw it away.", "그리고 내가 아는, 네가 거절한 그 훌륭한 청혼 두 건을 생각해 보렴! 그중 하나는 네가 차 버리지만 않는다면 아직 손이 닿는 곳에 있단다."], ["I knew a very great beauty who married badly at last, by doing so. Mr.", "나는 그런 식으로 굴다가 끝내 결혼을 잘못한 굉장한 미인을 알고 있어."], ["Ned Plymdale is a nice young man—some might think good-looking; and an only son; and a large business of that kind is better than a profession.", "네드 플림데일 씨는 좋은 청년이야. 어떤 이들은 잘생겼다고 여길 만하고, 외아들인 데다, 그런 큰 사업이 직업보다 낫지."], ["Not that marrying is everything.", "결혼이 전부라는 건 아니다."], ["I would have you seek first the kingdom of God.", "나야 네가 무엇보다 먼저 하느님의 나라를 구하길 바라지."], ["But a girl should keep her heart within her own power.”", "하지만 처녀라면 제 마음을 제 손아귀에 두어야 하는 법이란다.\""]], [["“I should never give it to Mr. Ned Plymdale, if it were.", "\"설령 그렇대도 저는 네드 플림데일 씨에게는 결코 제 마음을 주지 않겠어요."], ["I have already refused him.", "이미 거절했는걸요."], ["If I loved, I should love at once and without change,”", "제가 사랑한다면, 단번에 변함없이 사랑할 거예요.\""], ["said Rosamond, with a great sense of being a romantic heroine, and playing the part prettily.", "로저먼드가 자신이 낭만적인 여주인공이 된 듯한 기분에 흠뻑 젖어, 그 역할을 어여쁘게 연기하며 말했다."]], [["“I see how it is, my dear,” said Mrs.", "\"어떻게 된 일인지 알겠구나, 얘야.\""], ["Bulstrode, in a melancholy voice, rising to go.", "벌스트로드 부인이 자리에서 일어서며 서글픈 목소리로 말했다."], ["“You have allowed your affections to be engaged without return.”", "\"보답도 받지 못한 채 네 마음을 내주어 버린 게로구나.\""]], [["“No, indeed, aunt,”", "\"아니에요, 정말로요, 이모.\""], ["said Rosamond, with emphasis.", "로저먼드가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“Then you are quite confident that Mr. Lydgate has a serious attachment to you?”", "\"그럼 리드게이트 씨가 너에게 진지한 애정을 품고 있다고 정말로 자신하는 거니?\""]], [["Rosamond’s cheeks by this time were persistently burning, and she felt much mortification.", "이때쯤 로저먼드의 두 뺨은 끈질기게 달아올라 있었고, 그녀는 깊은 무안함을 느꼈다."], ["She chose to be silent, and her aunt went away all the more convinced.", "그녀는 입을 다물기로 했고, 이모는 한결 더 확신한 채 자리를 떴다."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode in things worldly and indifferent was disposed to do what his wife bade him, and she now, without telling her reasons, desired him on the next opportunity to find out in conversation with Mr.", "벌스트로드 씨는 세속적이고 사소한 일에서는 아내가 시키는 대로 하는 편이었으므로, 아내는 이제 이유는 말하지 않은 채 다음 기회에 리드게이트 씨와 대화하면서 그가 곧 결혼할 의향이 있는지 알아봐 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Lydgate whether he had any intention of marrying soon. The result was a decided negative.", "그 결과는 분명한 부정이었다."], ["Mr. Bulstrode, on being cross-questioned, showed that Lydgate had spoken as no man would who had any attachment that could issue in matrimony.", "벌스트로드 씨가 꼬치꼬치 캐물어 보니, 리드게이트는 결혼으로 이어질 만한 애정을 품은 남자라면 결코 하지 않았을 법한 말투로 이야기했다."], ["Mrs. Bulstrode now felt that she had a serious duty before her, and she soon managed to arrange a tête-à-tête with Lydgate, in which she passed from inquiries about Fred Vincy’s health, and expressions of her sincere anxiety for her brother’s large family, to general remarks on the dangers which lay before young people with regard to their settlement in life.", "이제 벌스트로드 부인은 자신 앞에 중대한 의무가 놓여 있다고 느꼈고, 곧 리드게이트와 단둘이 마주 앉을 자리를 마련했다."], ["Young men were often wild and disappointing, making little return for the money spent on them, and a girl was exposed to many circumstances which might interfere with her prospects.", "그 자리에서 그녀는 프레드 빈시의 건강을 묻고 오빠의 대가족에 대한 진심 어린 염려를 표하는 데서 시작해, 젊은 사람들이 인생에서 자리를 잡는 일과 관련해 그들 앞에 놓인 위험에 관한 일반적인 이야기로 옮겨 갔다. 젊은 남자들은 흔히 방탕하고 사람을 실망시켜서, 자기에게 들인 돈에 비해 보답이 적은 법이며, 처녀는 자신의 앞날을 가로막을 수 있는 갖가지 상황에 노출되어 있다는 것이었다."]], [["“Especially when she has great attractions, and her parents see much company,”", "\"특히 매력이 뛰어나고 부모가 손님을 많이 맞이하는 경우에는 더욱 그렇지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode.", "벌스트로드 부인이 말했다."], ["“Gentlemen pay her attention, and engross her all to themselves, for the mere pleasure of the moment, and that drives off others.", "\"신사들이 그저 한순간의 즐거움을 위해 그 처녀에게 관심을 쏟고 그녀를 독차지하면, 그게 다른 이들을 쫓아 버리거든요."], ["I think it is a heavy responsibility, Mr. Lydgate, to interfere with the prospects of any girl.”", "어떤 처녀의 앞날에 끼어든다는 것은 무거운 책임이라고 생각해요, 리드게이트 씨.\""], ["Here Mrs. Bulstrode fixed her eyes on him, with an unmistakable purpose of warning, if not of rebuke.", "여기서 벌스트로드 부인은, 질책까지는 아니더라도 경고하려는 의도를 분명히 드러내며 그에게 눈길을 고정했다."]], [["“Clearly,”", "\"그렇겠지요.\""], ["said Lydgate, looking at her—perhaps even staring a little in return.", "리드게이트가 그녀를 바라보며—아마 마주 보고 약간 빤히 응시하기까지 하며 말했다."], ["“On the other hand, a man must be a great coxcomb to go about with a notion that he must not pay attention to a young lady lest she should fall in love with him, or lest others should think she must.”", "\"하지만 한편으로, 어떤 처녀가 자기에게 반할까 봐, 또는 남들이 그 처녀가 그러리라 생각할까 봐 처녀에게 관심을 주어선 안 된다는 생각을 품고 다니는 남자라면 어지간한 멋쟁이 바보일 겁니다.\""]], [["“Oh, Mr. Lydgate, you know well what your advantages are.", "\"아, 리드게이트 씨, 당신은 자기 강점이 무엇인지 잘 알잖아요."], ["You know that our young men here cannot cope with you.", "여기 우리 젊은이들이 당신과 겨룰 수 없다는 것도 알고요."], ["Where you frequent a house it may militate very much against a girl’s making a desirable settlement in life, and prevent her from accepting offers even if they are made.”", "당신이 어느 집을 드나들면, 그게 한 처녀가 바람직한 혼처를 잡는 데 크게 방해가 되어, 청혼이 들어와도 받아들이지 못하게 만들 수 있어요.\""]], [["Lydgate was less flattered by his advantage over the Middlemarch Orlandos than he was annoyed by the perception of Mrs. Bulstrode’s meaning.", "리드게이트는 미들마치의 올랜도들에 대해 자신이 지닌 우위에 으쓱하기보다는, 벌스트로드 부인의 속뜻을 알아챈 것이 더 거슬렸다."], ["She felt that she had spoken as impressively as it was necessary to do, and that in using the superior word “militate” she had thrown a noble drapery over a mass of particulars which were still evident enough.", "그녀는 자신이 필요한 만큼 충분히 강한 인상을 주며 말했다고 느꼈고, '방해가 되다'라는 점잖은 단어를 씀으로써 여전히 충분히 빤히 드러나는 자질구레한 사정 덩어리에 고상한 휘장을 둘렀다고 느꼈다."]], [["Lydgate was fuming a little, pushed his hair back with one hand, felt curiously in his waistcoat-pocket with the other, and then stooped to beckon the tiny black spaniel, which had the insight to decline his hollow caresses.", "리드게이트는 살짝 화가 치밀어, 한 손으로 머리카락을 뒤로 쓸어 넘기고 다른 손으로는 조끼 주머니를 괜히 더듬다가, 몸을 굽혀 자그마한 검은 스패니얼을 불렀는데, 그 개는 그의 건성인 쓰다듬음을 마다할 만큼 눈치가 있었다."], ["It would not have been decent to go away, because he had been dining with other guests, and had just taken tea. But Mrs.", "그는 다른 손님들과 함께 만찬을 들고 막 차를 마신 참이었으니, 자리를 뜨는 것은 점잖지 못한 일이었을 것이다."], ["Bulstrode, having no doubt that she had been understood, turned the conversation.", "그러나 벌스트로드 부인은 자기 말이 이해되었음을 의심치 않고 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["Solomon’s Proverbs, I think, have omitted to say, that as the sore palate findeth grit, so an uneasy consciousness heareth innuendoes.", "솔로몬의 잠언은, 헌 입천장이 모래알을 잘 느끼듯 켕기는 의식은 빗댄 말을 잘 알아듣는다는 것을 말하는 걸 빠뜨린 듯하다."], ["The next day Mr.", "이튿날 페어브러더 씨가 거리에서 리드게이트와 헤어지면서, 저녁에 빈시 가에서 만나겠거니 했다."], ["Farebrother, parting from Lydgate in the street, supposed that they should meet at Vincy’s in the evening.", "리드게이트는 무뚝뚝하게 아니라고, 할 일이 있다고, 저녁 외출을 끊어야 한다고 대답했다. \"아니, 자네는 돛대에 몸을 묶고 귀를 막을 작정인가?\""], ["Lydgate answered curtly, no—he had work to do—he must give up going out in the evening.", "부목사가 말했다. \"글쎄, 세이렌에게 홀리지 않을 작정이라면, 제때에 대비책을 세우는 게 옳지.\""]], [["“What! you are going to get lashed to the mast, eh, and are stopping your ears?”", "* 세이렌: 그리스 신화에서 아름다운 노래로 뱃사람을 홀려 죽음에 이르게 한 바다의 요정."], ["said the Vicar. “Well, if you don’t mean to be won by the sirens, you are right to take precautions in time.”", "오디세우스는 부하들에게 자신을 돛대에 묶게 하고 부하들의 귀는 막아 그 유혹을 피했다."]], [["A few days before, Lydgate would have taken no notice of these words as anything more than the Vicar’s usual way of putting things.", "며칠 전이었다면 리드게이트는 이 말을, 부목사가 늘 말하는 방식 이상으로는 전혀 마음에 두지 않았을 것이다."], ["They seemed now to convey an innuendo which confirmed the impression that he had been making a fool of himself and behaving so as to be misunderstood: not, he believed, by Rosamond herself; she, he felt sure, took everything as lightly as he intended it.", "그런데 이제 그 말은 어떤 빗댐을 담은 듯했고, 그가 바보짓을 하며 오해를 살 만하게 처신해 왔다는 인상을 굳혀 주는 것 같았다. 다만 그가 믿기로는 로저먼드 자신에게 오해받은 것은 아니었다."], ["She had an exquisite tact and insight in relation to all points of manners; but the people she lived among were blunderers and busybodies.", "그녀는 분명 모든 것을 그가 의도한 만큼이나 가볍게 받아들였으리라 확신했다. 그녀는 예절의 모든 점에 관해 절묘한 재치와 통찰을 지니고 있었지만, 그녀가 어울려 사는 사람들은 멍청이들이고 참견꾼들이었다."], ["However, the mistake should go no farther.", "어쨌든 이 오해를 더 키워서는 안 되었다."], ["He resolved—and kept his resolution—that he would not go to Mr. Vincy’s except on business.", "그는 용무가 아니고서는 빈시 씨 댁에 가지 않겠다고 결심했고, 그 결심을 지켰다."]], [["Rosamond became very unhappy.", "로저먼드는 무척 불행해졌다."], ["The uneasiness first stirred by her aunt’s questions grew and grew till at the end of ten days that she had not seen Lydgate, it grew into terror at the blank that might possibly come—into foreboding of that ready, fatal sponge which so cheaply wipes out the hopes of mortals.", "이모의 질문에 처음 일었던 불안은 점점 커지고 또 커져서, 리드게이트를 보지 못한 지 열흘이 다 되어 갈 무렵에는 어쩌면 닥칠지도 모를 그 공허함에 대한 공포로, 인간의 희망을 그토록 손쉽게 지워 버리는 그 날렵하고 치명적인 해면에 대한 불길한 예감으로 자라났다."], ["The world would have a new dreariness for her, as a wilderness that a magician’s spells had turned for a little while into a garden.", "마법사의 주문이 잠시 정원으로 바꾸어 놓았던 황무지처럼, 세상은 그녀에게 새로운 황량함을 띠게 될 터였다."], ["She felt that she was beginning to know the pang of disappointed love, and that no other man could be the occasion of such delightful aerial building as she had been enjoying for the last six months.", "그녀는 자신이 사랑에 좌절하는 아픔을 알기 시작했음을, 그리고 지난 여섯 달 동안 누려 온 것 같은 그토록 즐거운 공중누각을 어떤 다른 남자도 일으켜 줄 수 없으리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Poor Rosamond lost her appetite and felt as forlorn as Ariadne—as a charming stage Ariadne left behind with all her boxes full of costumes and no hope of a coach.", "가엾은 로저먼드는 입맛을 잃었고, 의상이 가득 든 상자들만 남겨진 채 마차를 기대할 길 없는, 무대 위의 어여쁜 아리아드네처럼 버림받은 기분이 들었다."]], [["There are many wonderful mixtures in the world which are all alike called love, and claim the privileges of a sublime rage which is an apology for everything (in literature and the drama).", "* 아리아드네: 그리스 신화에서 크레타의 공주. 테세우스를 도와 미궁을 빠져나오게 했으나, 함께 떠난 뒤 낙소스섬에 홀로 버려졌다."], ["Happily Rosamond did not think of committing any desperate act: she plaited her fair hair as beautifully as usual, and kept herself proudly calm.", "세상에는 한결같이 사랑이라 불리며, (문학과 연극에서) 모든 것을 변명해 주는 숭고한 격정의 특권을 주장하는 온갖 신기한 혼합물이 있다. 다행히 로저먼드는 무슨 절망적인 행동을 저지를 생각은 하지 않았다."], ["Her most cheerful supposition was that her aunt Bulstrode had interfered in some way to hinder Lydgate’s visits: everything was better than a spontaneous indifference in him.", "그녀는 여느 때처럼 아름답게 고운 머리를 땋았고, 의젓하게 평정을 유지했다. 그녀가 떠올린 가장 기운 나는 추측은, 벌스트로드 이모가 어떤 식으로든 끼어들어 리드게이트의 방문을 막았으리라는 것이었다."], ["Any one who imagines ten days too short a time—not for falling into leanness, lightness, or other measurable effects of passion, but—for the whole spiritual circuit of alarmed conjecture and disappointment, is ignorant of what can go on in the elegant leisure of a young lady’s mind.", "무엇이든 그 사람의 자발적인 무관심보다는 나았으니까. 열흘이 너무 짧다고 여기는 사람은—여위거나 가벼워지거나 하는, 측정 가능한 격정의 효과를 위해서가 아니라, 놀란 억측과 실망이 한 바퀴 도는 정신의 온전한 회로를 위해서—한 젊은 아가씨의 우아한 한가로움 속에서 무슨 일이 벌어질 수 있는지를 모르는 사람이다."]], [["On the eleventh day, however, Lydgate when leaving Stone Court was requested by Mrs. Vincy to let her husband know that there was a marked change in Mr.", "그러나 열하루째 되는 날, 리드게이트가 스톤 코트를 떠나려 할 때 빈시 부인이, 페더스톤 씨의 건강에 뚜렷한 변화가 생겼으니 그날 안으로 스톤 코트로 와 달라는 뜻을 남편에게 전해 달라고 그에게 부탁했다."], ["Featherstone’s health, and that she wished him to come to Stone Court on that day. Now Lydgate might have called at the warehouse, or might have written a message on a leaf of his pocket-book and left it at the door.", "그런데 리드게이트는 창고로 찾아갈 수도 있었고, 수첩 한 장에 전갈을 적어 문간에 남겨 둘 수도 있었다."], ["Yet these simple devices apparently did not occur to him, from which we may conclude that he had no strong objection to calling at the house at an hour when Mr. Vincy was not at home, and leaving the message with Miss Vincy.", "그러나 이런 간단한 방편들이 분명 그에게는 떠오르지 않았으니, 이로 미루어 우리는 그가 빈시 씨가 집에 없는 시각에 그 집에 찾아가 빈시 양에게 전갈을 남기는 데에 별다른 거부감이 없었으리라고 짐작할 수 있다."], ["A man may, from various motives, decline to give his company, but perhaps not even a sage would be gratified that nobody missed him.", "사람은 여러 가지 동기에서 누군가와 어울리기를 마다할 수 있지만, 아무도 자신을 그리워하지 않는다면 아마 현자라도 흐뭇해하지는 않으리라."], ["It would be a graceful, easy way of piecing on the new habits to the old, to have a few playful words with Rosamond about his resistance to dissipation, and his firm resolve to take long fasts even from sweet sounds.", "새 습관을 옛 습관에 이어 붙이는 우아하고 손쉬운 방법은, 방탕에 맞선 자신의 저항이며 달콤한 소리마저도 오래 끊는 단식을 하겠다는 자신의 굳은 결심에 대해 로저먼드와 몇 마디 장난스러운 말을 나누는 것이었다."], ["It must be confessed, also, that momentary speculations as to all the possible grounds for Mrs. Bulstrode’s hints had managed to get woven like slight clinging hairs into the more substantial web of his thoughts.", "또한 고백하건대, 벌스트로드 부인의 암시가 어디에 근거를 두고 있을지에 대한 온갖 순간적인 억측이, 가느다란 잔털처럼 그의 더 단단한 생각의 직물에 어느새 짜여 들어 엉겨 붙어 있었다."]], [["Miss Vincy was alone, and blushed so deeply when Lydgate came in that he felt a corresponding embarrassment, and instead of any playfulness, he began at once to speak of his reason for calling, and to beg her, almost formally, to deliver the message to her father.", "빈시 양은 혼자 있었고, 리드게이트가 들어서자 어찌나 얼굴이 빨개지던지 그도 덩달아 당혹스러워져서, 무슨 장난기는커녕 곧장 자신이 찾아온 이유를 이야기하기 시작했고, 거의 격식을 차려 가며 그 전갈을 아버지에게 전해 달라고 부탁했다. 처음 순간에는 자신의 행복이 되돌아오는 듯 느꼈던 로저먼드는 리드게이트의 태도에 몹시 상처받았다."], ["Rosamond, who at the first moment felt as if her happiness were returning, was keenly hurt by Lydgate’s manner; her blush had departed, and she assented coldly, without adding an unnecessary word, some trivial chain-work which she had in her hands enabling her to avoid looking at Lydgate higher than his chin.", "그녀의 홍조는 가셨고, 그녀는 불필요한 말 한마디 덧붙이지 않은 채 차갑게 응했다. 손에 들고 있던 자질구레한 사슬 세공 덕분에 그녀는 리드게이트를 턱 위로는 올려다보지 않을 수 있었다."], ["In all failures, the beginning is certainly the half of the whole.", "모든 실패에서 시작은 분명 그 전체의 절반이다."], ["After sitting two long moments while he moved his whip and could say nothing, Lydgate rose to go, and Rosamond, made nervous by her struggle between mortification and the wish not to betray it, dropped her chain as if startled, and rose too, mechanically.", "그가 채찍을 만지작거리며 아무 말도 못 한 채 두 번의 긴 순간을 앉아 있다가 리드게이트가 일어서서 가려 하자, 무안함과 그것을 드러내지 않으려는 마음 사이에서 갈등하느라 안절부절못하던 로저먼드는 놀란 듯 사슬을 떨어뜨리고는 자기도 기계적으로 일어섰다."], ["Lydgate instantaneously stooped to pick up the chain.", "리드게이트가 즉각 몸을 굽혀 사슬을 집어 들었다."], ["When he rose he was very near to a lovely little face set on a fair long neck which he had been used to see turning about under the most perfect management of self-contented grace.", "그가 몸을 일으키자, 그는 곱고 긴 목 위에 얹힌 사랑스러운 작은 얼굴에 아주 가까이 다가가 있었으니, 그 얼굴은 그가 늘 더없이 완벽하게 다듬어진 자족적인 우아함으로 돌아가는 것을 보아 오던 것이었다."], ["But as he raised his eyes now he saw a certain helpless quivering which touched him quite newly, and made him look at Rosamond with a questioning flash.", "그러나 이제 그가 눈을 들어 보니, 어떤 어찌할 바 모르는 떨림이 보였고, 그것이 아주 새삼스럽게 그의 마음을 건드려 그는 의아한 듯 번뜩이는 눈으로 로저먼드를 바라보게 되었다."], ["At this moment she was as natural as she had ever been when she was five years old: she felt that her tears had risen, and it was no use to try to do anything else than let them stay like water on a blue flower or let them fall over her cheeks, even as they would.", "이 순간 그녀는 다섯 살 적의 그 어느 때만큼이나 꾸밈없었다. 그녀는 눈물이 차오른 것을 느꼈고, 그것을 파란 꽃 위에 맺힌 물방울처럼 그대로 두거나, 흐르고 싶은 대로 두 뺨 위로 흘러내리게 하는 것 외에 달리 어찌해 볼 도리가 없음을 느꼈다."]], [["That moment of naturalness was the crystallizing feather-touch: it shook flirtation into love.", "그 꾸밈없는 순간이 결정을 굳히는 깃털의 한 스침이 되었으니, 그것이 시시덕거림을 흔들어 사랑으로 만들었다."], ["Remember that the ambitious man who was looking at those Forget-me-nots under the water was very warm-hearted and rash.", "물 아래 그 물망초들을 들여다보던 그 야심 찬 남자가 매우 따뜻한 마음을 지녔고 또 무모했다는 것을 기억하라."], ["He did not know where the chain went; an idea had thrilled through the recesses within him which had a miraculous effect in raising the power of passionate love lying buried there in no sealed sepulchre, but under the lightest, easily pierced mould.", "그는 사슬이 어디로 가는지도 몰랐다. 어떤 생각 하나가 그의 내면 깊은 곳을 꿰뚫고 전율처럼 지나갔는데, 그것은 봉인된 무덤 속이 아니라 더없이 가볍고 쉽게 뚫리는 흙더미 아래 묻혀 있던 열정적인 사랑의 힘을 일으켜 세우는 데에 기적 같은 효과를 발휘했다."], ["His words were quite abrupt and awkward; but the tone made them sound like an ardent, appealing avowal.", "그의 말은 자못 불쑥 튀어나온 어색한 것이었지만, 그 어조는 그 말을 간절히 호소하는 열렬한 고백처럼 들리게 했다."]], [["“What is the matter?", "\"무슨 일이지요?"], ["you are distressed.", "괴로워하고 있군요."], ["Tell me, pray.”", "부디 말해 주세요.\""]], [["Rosamond had never been spoken to in such tones before.", "로저먼드는 그런 어조로 누가 자신에게 말을 건넨 적이 한 번도 없었다."], ["I am not sure that she knew what the words were: but she looked at Lydgate and the tears fell over her cheeks.", "그녀가 그 말이 무엇이었는지 알아들었는지조차 나는 잘 모르겠다. 그러나 그녀는 리드게이트를 바라보았고 눈물이 두 뺨 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["There could have been no more complete answer than that silence, and Lydgate, forgetting everything else, completely mastered by the outrush of tenderness at the sudden belief that this sweet young creature depended on him for her joy, actually put his arms round her, folding her gently and protectingly—he was used to being gentle with the weak and suffering—and kissed each of the two large tears.", "그 침묵보다 더 완전한 대답은 없었을 것이고, 리드게이트는 다른 모든 것을 잊은 채, 이 사랑스러운 젊은 아가씨가 자신의 기쁨을 자기에게 기대고 있다는 갑작스러운 믿음에서 솟구쳐 오른 애틋함에 완전히 사로잡혀, 정말로 그녀를 두 팔로 감싸 안고는—그는 약하고 고통받는 이들에게 다정하게 대하는 데 익숙했다—부드럽게 지켜 주듯 그녀를 안았고, 그 큼직한 두 방울의 눈물에 하나씩 입을 맞추었다."], ["This was a strange way of arriving at an understanding, but it was a short way.", "이것은 서로의 마음을 확인하는 묘한 방식이었으나, 그것은 빠른 방식이었다."], ["Rosamond was not angry, but she moved backward a little in timid happiness, and Lydgate could now sit near her and speak less incompletely.", "로저먼드는 화내지 않았으나, 수줍은 행복에 잠겨 조금 뒤로 물러섰고, 이제 리드게이트는 그녀 곁에 앉아 좀 더 온전하게 이야기할 수 있었다."], ["Rosamond had to make her little confession, and he poured out words of gratitude and tenderness with impulsive lavishment.", "로저먼드는 자신의 자그마한 고백을 해야 했고, 그는 충동적으로 흘러넘치게 감사와 애정의 말들을 쏟아 냈다."], ["In half an hour he left the house an engaged man, whose soul was not his own, but the woman’s to whom he had bound himself.", "반 시간 뒤 그는 약혼한 남자가 되어 그 집을 나섰으니, 그의 영혼은 더 이상 자신의 것이 아니라 자신이 매여 버린 여자의 것이었다."]], [["He came again in the evening to speak with Mr. Vincy, who, just returned from Stone Court, was feeling sure that it would not be long before he heard of Mr.", "그는 저녁에 다시 와서 빈시 씨와 이야기를 나누었는데, 빈시 씨는 막 스톤 코트에서 돌아온 참으로, 머지않아 페더스톤 씨의 '서거'"], ["Featherstone’s demise. The felicitous word “demise,”", "소식을 듣게 되리라 확신하고 있었다."], ["which had seasonably occurred to him, had raised his spirits even above their usual evening pitch.", "때맞춰 떠오른 '서거'라는 그 절묘한 단어는 그의 기분을 평소 저녁의 들뜬 수준보다도 더 높여 놓았다."], ["The right word is always a power, and communicates its definiteness to our action.", "적절한 단어는 언제나 하나의 힘이어서, 그 명료함을 우리의 행동에 옮겨 준다."], ["Considered as a demise, old Featherstone’s death assumed a merely legal aspect, so that Mr.", "서거로 여기고 보니 늙은 페더스톤의 죽음은 한낱 법적인 양상을 띠게 되어, 빈시 씨는 그것을 두고 코담뱃갑을 톡톡 두드리며 명랑하게 굴 수 있었고, 잠깐씩이나마 엄숙한 척조차 할 필요가 없었다."], ["Vincy could tap his snuff-box over it and be jovial, without even an intermittent affectation of solemnity; and Mr.", "게다가 빈시 씨는 엄숙함도 가식도 다 싫어했다. 누가 유언자를 두고 경외감에 사로잡힌 적이 있으며, 부동산 권리증서를 두고 찬송가를 부른 적이 있던가?"], ["Vincy hated both solemnity and affectation.", "빈시 씨는 그날 저녁 만사를 유쾌하게 보려는 기분이었다."], ["Who was ever awe struck about a testator, or sang a hymn on the title to real property? Mr.", "그는 리드게이트에게, 프레드가 결국 집안의 튼튼한 체질을 물려받았으니 곧 다시 더없이 건장한 청년이 될 거라고 말하기까지 했다."], ["Vincy was inclined to take a jovial view of all things that evening: he even observed to Lydgate that Fred had got the family constitution after all, and would soon be as fine a fellow as ever again; and when his approbation of Rosamond’s engagement was asked for, he gave it with astonishing facility, passing at once to general remarks on the desirableness of matrimony for young men and maidens, and apparently deducing from the whole the appropriateness of a little more punch.", "그리고 로저먼드의 약혼에 대한 동의를 청하자 그는 놀라우리만치 선뜻 그것을 허락하고는, 곧장 젊은 남녀에게 결혼이란 바람직한 것이라는 일반적인 이야기로 넘어갔으며, 그 모든 것에서 펀치를 조금 더 마시는 것이 적절하다는 결론을 끌어내는 듯 보였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXII.", "# CHAPTER XXXII."]], [["They’ll take suggestion as a cat laps milk.", "그들은 고양이가 우유를 핥듯 암시를 받아들이리라."], ["—SHAKESPEARE: Tempest.", "— 셰익스피어, 「폭풍우」"]], [["The triumphant confidence of the Mayor founded on Mr. Featherstone’s insistent demand that Fred and his mother should not leave him, was a feeble emotion compared with all that was agitating the breasts of the old man’s blood-relations, who naturally manifested more their sense of the family tie and were more visibly numerous now that he had become bedridden.", "페더스톤 씨가 프레드와 그의 어머니에게 곁을 떠나지 말라고 한사코 요구한 데 근거를 둔 시장의 의기양양한 자신감은, 그 노인의 혈족들 가슴속에서 들끓고 있던 온갖 감정에 비하면 미약한 정서에 지나지 않았다."], ["Naturally: for when “poor Peter”", "노인이 자리에 눕게 되자 그 혈족들은 자연히 가족의 유대 의식을 더 드러냈고 눈에 띄게 그 수가 늘어났다."], ["had occupied his arm-chair in the wainscoted parlor, no assiduous beetles for whom the cook prepares boiling water could have been less welcome on a hearth which they had reasons for preferring, than those persons whose Featherstone blood was ill-nourished, not from penuriousness on their part, but from poverty.", "자연히 그럴 수밖에 없었다. \"불쌍한 피터\"가 징두리널을 댄 거실의 안락의자를 차지하고 있을 적에는, 요리사가 끓는 물을 준비해 두고 기다리는 부지런한 딱정벌레들조차도, 그들이 선호할 만한 이유가 있는 난롯가에서 그만큼 환영받지 못한 자들만큼 달갑지 않았으니, 페더스톤가의 피가 빈약하게 흐르던 그 사람들이 바로 그러했다."], ["Brother Solomon and Sister Jane were rich, and the family candor and total abstinence from false politeness with which they were always received seemed to them no argument that their brother in the solemn act of making his will would overlook the superior claims of wealth.", "그것은 그들 자신의 인색함 때문이 아니라 가난 때문이었다. 솔로몬 형제와 제인 자매는 부유했고, 그들을 맞이할 때마다 보이는 가족 특유의 솔직함과 거짓 예의의 완전한 결여를, 그들은 자기 형제가 유언을 작성하는 엄숙한 행위에서 재력의 우월한 자격을 무시하리라는 논거로 여기지 않았다."], ["Themselves at least he had never been unnatural enough to banish from his house, and it seemed hardly eccentric that he should have kept away Brother Jonah, Sister Martha, and the rest, who had no shadow of such claims.", "적어도 그들 자신만큼은, 형제가 집에서 내쫓을 만큼 매정하게 군 적이 없었으니, 그런 자격의 그림자조차 없는 요나 형제, 마사 자매와 나머지 사람들을 멀리해 온 것이 별스러운 일로 보이지 않았다."], ["They knew Peter’s maxim, that money was a good egg, and should be laid in a warm nest.", "그들은 돈이란 좋은 알이어서 따뜻한 둥지에 품어 두어야 한다는 피터의 격언을 알고 있었다."]], [["But Brother Jonah, Sister Martha, and all the needy exiles, held a different point of view.", "그러나 요나 형제, 마사 자매와 궁핍한 추방자들 전부는 다른 관점을 지니고 있었다."], ["Probabilities are as various as the faces to be seen at will in fretwork or paper-hangings: every form is there, from Jupiter to Judy, if you only look with creative inclination.", "개연성이란 투조 세공이나 벽지 무늬에서 마음대로 보이는 얼굴들만큼이나 다양하다. 창조적인 성향을 가지고 보기만 하면, 거기에는 주피터부터 주디까지 온갖 형상이 다 들어 있다."], ["To the poorer and least favored it seemed likely that since Peter had done nothing for them in his life, he would remember them at the last.", "더 가난하고 덜 총애받는 자들에게는, 피터가 살아생전 그들을 위해 아무것도 해 주지 않았으니 마지막에 가서는 그들을 기억해 주리라는 것이 그럴듯해 보였다."], ["Jonah argued that men liked to make a surprise of their wills, while Martha said that nobody need be surprised if he left the best part of his money to those who least expected it.", "요나는 사람들이란 유언을 깜짝 놀랄 만한 것으로 만들기를 좋아한다고 주장했고, 마사는 가장 기대하지 않은 자들에게 재산의 대부분을 남긴다 해도 아무도 놀랄 것이 없다고 말했다."], ["Also it was not to be thought but that an own brother “lying there”", "게다가 다리에 수종을 앓으며 \"저기 누워 있는\""], ["with dropsy in his legs must come to feel that blood was thicker than water, and if he didn’t alter his will, he might have money by him.", "친형제라면 피가 물보다 진하다는 것을 절감하게 마련이며, 유언을 바꾸지 않았다 해도 손에 쥔 돈이 있을지 모를 일이었다."], ["At any rate some blood-relations should be on the premises and on the watch against those who were hardly relations at all.", "어쨌거나 혈족이라고 할 수도 없는 자들을 경계하기 위해서라도 혈족 몇은 그 집에 들어앉아 망을 보아야 했다."], ["Such things had been known as forged wills and disputed wills, which seemed to have the golden-hazy advantage of somehow enabling non-legatees to live out of them.", "위조된 유언이나 다툼이 벌어진 유언 같은 일이 세상에 알려진 바 있었으니, 그런 것에는 유산 상속인이 아닌 자들도 어떻게든 그것으로 먹고살 수 있게 해 주는 황금빛 아련한 이점이 있는 듯했다."], ["Again, those who were no blood-relations might be caught making away with things—and poor Peter “lying there”", "또한 혈족이 아닌 자들이 물건을 빼돌리다 붙잡힐 수도 있었다. 게다가 불쌍한 피터는 \"저기 누워\""], ["helpless!", "속수무책이 아닌가!"], ["Somebody should be on the watch.", "누군가는 망을 보아야 했다."], ["But in this conclusion they were at one with Solomon and Jane; also, some nephews, nieces, and cousins, arguing with still greater subtilty as to what might be done by a man able to “will away”", "그러나 이 결론에서는 그들도 솔로몬, 제인과 한마음이었다. 더욱이 몇몇 조카와 조카딸, 사촌들은, 제 재산을 \"마음대로 물려줄\""], ["his property and give himself large treats of oddity, felt in a handsome sort of way that there was a family interest to be attended to, and thought of Stone Court as a place which it would be nothing but right for them to visit.", "수 있고 스스로 별스러운 짓을 큼직하게 즐길 수 있는 사람이 어떤 일을 저지를지에 관해 한층 더 미묘하게 따져 가며, 챙겨야 할 가족의 이해관계가 있다고 멋들어지게 느꼈고, 스톤 코트야말로 자신들이 찾아가는 것이 당연한 곳이라고 여겼다."], ["Sister Martha, otherwise Mrs. Cranch, living with some wheeziness in the Chalky Flats, could not undertake the journey; but her son, as being poor Peter’s own nephew, could represent her advantageously, and watch lest his uncle Jonah should make an unfair use of the improbable things which seemed likely to happen.", "마사 자매, 다른 이름으로는 크랜치 부인은 초크 플랫에서 색색거리는 숨을 쉬며 살고 있어 그 여행을 감당할 수 없었지만, 그녀의 아들은 불쌍한 피터의 친조카로서 그녀를 유리하게 대리할 수 있었고, 일어날 법해 보이는 그 있을 법하지 않은 일들을 외삼촌 요나가 부당하게 이용하지 못하도록 망을 볼 수 있었다."], ["In fact there was a general sense running in the Featherstone blood that everybody must watch everybody else, and that it would be well for everybody else to reflect that the Almighty was watching him.", "사실 페더스톤가의 피에는 누구나 서로서로 감시해야 하며, 또한 다른 모든 사람은 전능하신 분이 자신을 지켜보고 계심을 명심하는 것이 좋으리라는 보편적인 의식이 흐르고 있었다."]], [["Thus Stone Court continually saw one or other blood-relation alighting or departing, and Mary Garth had the unpleasant task of carrying their messages to Mr. Featherstone, who would see none of them, and sent her down with the still more unpleasant task of telling them so. As manager of the household she felt bound to ask them in good provincial fashion to stay and eat; but she chose to consult Mrs. Vincy on the point of extra down-stairs consumption now that Mr. Featherstone was laid up.", "이리하여 스톤 코트에서는 끊임없이 어느 혈족인가가 마차에서 내리거나 떠나는 광경이 보였고, 메리 가스는 그들의 전갈을 페더스톤 씨에게 전해야 하는 달갑잖은 임무를 떠맡았는데, 노인은 그들 중 아무도 만나려 하지 않았고, 그녀를 아래층으로 내려보내 그 사실을 알리는 한층 더 달갑잖은 임무를 맡겼다. 그녀는 살림을 맡은 사람으로서 시골의 좋은 관습에 따라 그들에게 머물러 식사하기를 권해야 한다고 느꼈지만, 페더스톤 씨가 자리에 누운 지금 아래층에서 늘어날 식비 문제에 관해서는 빈시 부인과 상의하기로 했다."]], [["“Oh, my dear, you must do things handsomely where there’s last illness and a property.", "\"오, 얘야, 임종을 앞둔 병환에 재산까지 걸린 곳에서는 일을 후하게 해야 한단다."], ["God knows, I don’t grudge them every ham in the house—only, save the best for the funeral.", "하느님께 맹세코, 집 안의 햄을 죄다 내놓는 것도 아깝지 않아. 다만 제일 좋은 건 장례식을 위해 남겨 둬라."], ["Have some stuffed veal always, and a fine cheese in cut.", "송아지 고기 속을 채운 요리는 늘 준비해 두고, 좋은 치즈도 잘라서 내놓고."], ["You must expect to keep open house in these last illnesses,”", "이런 임종 병환 때에는 손님이 끊이지 않으리라 각오해야 한다.\""], ["said liberal Mrs. Vincy, once more of cheerful note and bright plumage.", "후한 빈시 부인은 다시금 명랑한 어조에 화사한 깃털을 되찾은 채 말했다."]], [["But some of the visitors alighted and did not depart after the handsome treating to veal and ham.", "그러나 일부 방문객들은 마차에서 내린 뒤 송아지 고기와 햄을 후하게 대접받고서도 떠나지 않았다."], ["Brother Jonah, for example (there are such unpleasant people in most families; perhaps even in the highest aristocracy there are Brobdingnag specimens, gigantically in debt and bloated at greater expense)—Brother Jonah, I say, having come down in the world, was mainly supported by a calling which he was modest enough not to boast of, though it was much better than swindling either on exchange or turf, but which did not require his presence at Brassing so long as he had a good corner to sit in and a supply of food.", "예컨대 요나 형제가 그러했는데(대부분의 가문에는 그런 달갑잖은 사람들이 있게 마련이고, 어쩌면 최고의 귀족 가문에도 거대하게 빚을 지고 더 큰 비용을 들여 부풀어 오른 브롭딩낵의 표본들이 있을 것이다)—그러니까 내가 말하건대, 요나 형제는 영락한 처지가 되어, 주로 어떤 직업으로 생계를 꾸렸는데, 그것은 증권 거래나 경마장에서 사기를 치는 것보다는 훨씬 나았지만 그가 떠벌리지 않을 만큼은 겸손한 일이었고, 다만 앉아 있기 좋은 구석과 먹을 것만 넉넉하면 브래싱에 그가 있을 필요는 없는 일이었다."], ["He chose the kitchen-corner, partly because he liked it best, and partly because he did not want to sit with Solomon, concerning whom he had a strong brotherly opinion.", "그는 부엌 구석을 골랐는데, 한편으로는 그곳이 제일 마음에 들어서였고, 한편으로는 솔로몬과 함께 앉고 싶지 않아서였으니, 형제로서 그에 대해 강경한 견해를 품고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Seated in a famous arm-chair and in his best suit, constantly within sight of good cheer, he had a comfortable consciousness of being on the premises, mingled with fleeting suggestions of Sunday and the bar at the Green Man; and he informed Mary Garth that he should not go out of reach of his brother Peter while that poor fellow was above ground.", "그 유명한 안락의자에 가장 좋은 양복을 입고 앉아, 좋은 음식이 늘 눈에 들어오는 곳에서, 그는 이 집에 들어앉아 있다는 편안한 의식을 품었고, 거기에는 일요일과 그린 맨 주점의 술집에 대한 덧없는 연상이 뒤섞여 있었다. 그는 메리 가스에게 그 불쌍한 친구가 땅 위에 있는 한 자신은 피터 형제의 손이 닿는 곳을 벗어나지 않겠노라고 알렸다."], ["The troublesome ones in a family are usually either the wits or the idiots.", "가문에서 골칫거리인 자들은 대개 재담꾼 아니면 멍청이다."], ["Jonah was the wit among the Featherstones, and joked with the maid-servants when they came about the hearth, but seemed to consider Miss Garth a suspicious character, and followed her with cold eyes.", "요나는 페더스톤가에서 재담꾼이어서, 하녀들이 난롯가로 다가오면 농을 주고받았지만, 가스 양만큼은 수상쩍은 인물로 여기는 듯 차가운 눈초리로 그녀를 좇았다."]], [["Mary would have borne this one pair of eyes with comparative ease, but unfortunately there was young Cranch, who, having come all the way from the Chalky Flats to represent his mother and watch his uncle Jonah, also felt it his duty to stay and to sit chiefly in the kitchen to give his uncle company.", "메리로서는 이 한 쌍의 눈만이라면 비교적 수월하게 견뎌 냈을 테지만, 불행히도 젊은 크랜치가 있었으니, 그는 어머니를 대리하고 외삼촌 요나를 감시하려고 초크 플랫에서 머나먼 길을 와서는, 머물면서 주로 부엌에 앉아 삼촌의 말동무가 되어 주는 것을 의무로 느꼈다."], ["Young Cranch was not exactly the balancing point between the wit and the idiot,—verging slightly towards the latter type, and squinting so as to leave everything in doubt about his sentiments except that they were not of a forcible character.", "젊은 크랜치는 정확히 재담꾼과 멍청이의 중간점은 아니었다—약간 후자 쪽으로 기울어 있었고, 사팔뜨기여서 그의 심정이라고는, 강경한 성질의 것이 아니라는 점 말고는 모든 게 의심스럽게 남았다."], ["When Mary Garth entered the kitchen and Mr. Jonah Featherstone began to follow her with his cold detective eyes, young Cranch turning his head in the same direction seemed to insist on it that she should remark how he was squinting, as if he did it with design, like the gypsies when Borrow read the New Testament to them.", "메리 가스가 부엌에 들어와 요나 페더스톤 씨가 차가운 형사 같은 눈으로 그녀를 좇기 시작하면, 젊은 크랜치는 같은 방향으로 고개를 돌리며, 마치 일부러 그러는 것처럼, 보로가 집시들에게 신약 성서를 읽어 줄 때의 그들처럼, 자신이 어떻게 사팔눈을 뜨는지 그녀가 주목하라고 우기는 듯했다."], ["This was rather too much for poor Mary; sometimes it made her bilious, sometimes it upset her gravity.", "이는 불쌍한 메리에게는 좀 지나친 일이었다. 어떤 때는 그 때문에 속이 메슥거렸고, 어떤 때는 진지함을 잃고 말았다."], ["One day that she had an opportunity she could not resist describing the kitchen scene to Fred, who would not be hindered from immediately going to see it, affecting simply to pass through.", "어느 날 기회가 생기자 그녀는 부엌 광경을 프레드에게 묘사하지 않을 수 없었는데, 프레드는 당장 그것을 보러 가는 것을 막을 수 없어 그저 지나가는 척하려 했다."], ["But no sooner did he face the four eyes than he had to rush through the nearest door which happened to lead to the dairy, and there under the high roof and among the pans he gave way to laughter which made a hollow resonance perfectly audible in the kitchen. He fled by another doorway, but Mr.", "그러나 그 네 개의 눈과 마주하자마자 그는 가장 가까운 문으로 황급히 달려 나가야 했고, 그 문은 마침 우유 저장실로 통해 있었으니, 그곳 높은 지붕 아래 우유통들 사이에서 그는 웃음을 터뜨리고 말았고, 그 웃음은 부엌에까지 또렷이 들릴 만큼 텅 빈 울림을 만들어 냈다."], ["Jonah, who had not before seen Fred’s white complexion, long legs, and pinched delicacy of face, prepared many sarcasms in which these points of appearance were wittily combined with the lowest moral attributes.", "그는 다른 문으로 달아났지만, 그전까지 프레드의 흰 안색과 긴 다리, 야무지게 가늘어진 얼굴을 본 적이 없던 요나 씨는, 이런 외모의 특징들을 가장 저열한 도덕적 속성들과 재치 있게 결합한 숱한 비아냥을 준비해 두었다."]], [["“Why, Tom, you don’t wear such gentlemanly trousers—you haven’t got half such fine long legs,”", "\"이봐 톰, 너는 그렇게 신사다운 바지를 입지 못하잖니—너는 그만큼 곱고 긴 다리를 절반도 갖지 못했고.\""], ["said Jonah to his nephew, winking at the same time, to imply that there was something more in these statements than their undeniableness.", "요나가 조카에게 말하며, 동시에 한쪽 눈을 찡긋했으니, 이 말들에는 부인할 수 없다는 점 말고도 무언가 더 있다는 뜻이었다."], ["Tom looked at his legs, but left it uncertain whether he preferred his moral advantages to a more vicious length of limb and reprehensible gentility of trouser.", "톰은 제 다리를 내려다보았으나, 자신의 도덕적 우월함을 더 사악하게 긴 다리와 비난받아 마땅한 바지의 우아함보다 더 좋아하는지는 분명히 하지 않았다."]], [["In the large wainscoted parlor too there were constantly pairs of eyes on the watch, and own relatives eager to be “sitters-up.”", "징두리널을 댄 그 큰 거실에서도 늘 한 쌍씩의 눈이 망을 보고 있었으며, 친척들은 \"밤샘하는 사람\"이 되고 싶어 안달했다."], ["Many came, lunched, and departed, but Brother Solomon and the lady who had been Jane Featherstone for twenty-five years before she was Mrs. Waule found it good to be there every day for hours, without other calculable occupation than that of observing the cunning Mary Garth (who was so deep that she could be found out in nothing) and giving occasional dry wrinkly indications of crying—as if capable of torrents in a wetter season—at the thought that they were not allowed to go into Mr.", "많은 이들이 와서 점심을 먹고 떠났지만, 솔로몬 형제와 와울 부인이 되기 전 스물다섯 해 동안 제인 페더스톤이었던 부인은 날마다 몇 시간씩 그곳에 머무는 것이 좋다고 여겼으니, 교활한 메리 가스를 관찰하는 일(그녀는 어찌나 속이 깊은지 무슨 일에서도 들통날 데가 없었다) 말고는 헤아릴 만한 다른 소일거리도 없이, 페더스톤 씨의 방에 들어가는 것이 허락되지 않는다는 생각에 이따금 메마르게 주름진 울음의 기색을 내비치곤 했다—더 축축한 계절이었더라면 눈물을 폭포처럼 쏟아 낼 능력이 있는 양."], ["Featherstone’s room. For the old man’s dislike of his own family seemed to get stronger as he got less able to amuse himself by saying biting things to them.", "노인이 제 가족을 싫어하는 마음은, 그들에게 모진 말을 던지며 스스로를 즐겁게 할 기력이 줄어들수록 도리어 강해지는 듯했다."], ["Too languid to sting, he had the more venom refluent in his blood.", "너무 무기력해 쏘아붙이지 못하니, 그 독은 그만큼 더 그의 핏속으로 역류했다."]], [["Not fully believing the message sent through Mary Garth, they had presented themselves together within the door of the bedroom, both in black—Mrs. Waule having a white handkerchief partially unfolded in her hand—and both with faces in a sort of half-mourning purple; while Mrs.", "메리 가스를 통해 전해진 전갈을 온전히 믿지 못한 그들은, 둘 다 검은 옷을 입고—와울 부인은 흰 손수건을 반쯤 펼쳐 손에 들고서—둘 다 일종의 반쯤 상중인 듯한 자줏빛 얼굴을 한 채, 함께 침실 문 안에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Vincy with her pink cheeks and pink ribbons flying was actually administering a cordial to their own brother, and the light-complexioned Fred, his short hair curling as might be expected in a gambler’s, was lolling at his ease in a large chair.", "한편 빈시 부인은 발그레한 뺨에 분홍 리본을 나부끼며, 다름 아닌 그들의 친형제에게 강심제를 먹이고 있었고, 안색이 환한 프레드는 노름꾼답게 짧은 머리칼을 곱슬거리며 큰 의자에 느긋하게 기대어 있었다."]], [["Old Featherstone no sooner caught sight of these funereal figures appearing in spite of his orders than rage came to strengthen him more successfully than the cordial.", "페더스톤 노인은 자기 명령을 어기고 나타난 이 장례식 같은 형상들을 보자마자, 강심제보다 훨씬 효과적으로 그를 일으켜 세우는 분노가 솟구쳤다."], ["He was propped up on a bed-rest, and always had his gold-headed stick lying by him.", "그는 등받이에 기대어 몸을 받치고 있었고, 늘 금 손잡이 지팡이를 곁에 두고 있었다."], ["He seized it now and swept it backwards and forwards in as large an area as he could, apparently to ban these ugly spectres, crying in a hoarse sort of screech—", "이제 그는 그것을 움켜쥐고, 이 흉측한 망령들을 쫓아내려는 듯 할 수 있는 한 넓은 범위로 앞뒤로 휘두르며, 쉰 듯한 비명을 질러 댔다—"]], [["“Back, back, Mrs. Waule!", "\"물러가, 물러가, 와울 부인!"], ["Back, Solomon!”", "물러가, 솔로몬!\""]], [["“Oh, Brother. Peter,”", "\"오, 피터 형제.\""], ["Mrs. Waule began—but Solomon put his hand before her repressingly.", "와울 부인이 입을 떼었으나—솔로몬이 그녀 앞으로 손을 들어 제지했다."], ["He was a large-cheeked man, nearly seventy, with small furtive eyes, and was not only of much blander temper but thought himself much deeper than his brother Peter; indeed not likely to be deceived in any of his fellow-men, inasmuch as they could not well be more greedy and deceitful than he suspected them of being.", "그는 뺨이 큰 사내로, 일흔 가까운 나이에 작고 음흉한 눈을 지녔고, 형제 피터보다 훨씬 온화한 성정일 뿐 아니라 스스로를 훨씬 더 속이 깊다고 여겼으니, 실로 어떤 동료에게도 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니었다. 왜냐하면 그들이 그가 의심하는 것보다 더 탐욕스럽고 기만적일 수는 없을 터였기 때문이다."], ["Even the invisible powers, he thought, were likely to be soothed by a bland parenthesis here and there—coming from a man of property, who might have been as impious as others.", "그는 눈에 보이지 않는 권능들조차도, 남들 못지않게 불경할 수 있었던 재산가가 여기저기 온화한 말 한마디씩을 끼워 넣으면 누그러질 법하다고 생각했다."]], [["“Brother Peter,”", "\"피터 형제.\""], ["he said, in a wheedling yet gravely official tone, “It’s nothing but right I should speak to you about the Three Crofts and the Manganese.", "그는 비위를 맞추면서도 진지하게 격식을 차린 어조로 말했다. \"스리 크로프츠와 망간 건에 관해 형제께 말씀드리는 게 마땅한 일입니다."], ["The Almighty knows what I’ve got on my mind—”", "제 마음에 무엇이 걸려 있는지는 전능하신 분께서 아십니다—\""]], [["“Then he knows more than I want to know,”", "\"그럼 내가 알고 싶지 않은 것까지 그분은 아시겠구먼.\""], ["said Peter, laying down his stick with a show of truce which had a threat in it too, for he reversed the stick so as to make the gold handle a club in case of closer fighting, and looked hard at Solomon’s bald head.", "피터가 휴전을 내보이듯 지팡이를 내려놓으며 말했는데, 거기에는 위협도 깃들어 있었으니, 그는 더 가까운 싸움이 벌어질 경우 금 손잡이를 곤봉 삼으려고 지팡이를 거꾸로 돌려 쥔 채 솔로몬의 대머리를 매섭게 노려보았다."]], [["“There’s things you might repent of, Brother, for want of speaking to me,”", "\"형제께서는 제게 말씀하지 않으셨다가 후회하실 일들이 있을 겁니다, 형제.\""], ["said Solomon, not advancing, however.", "솔로몬이 말했으나, 그러면서도 앞으로 다가가지는 않았다."], ["“I could sit up with you to-night, and Jane with me, willingly, and you might take your own time to speak, or let me speak.”", "\"오늘 밤 제가 형제 곁을 지키고, 제인도 저와 함께 기꺼이 지키겠습니다. 그러면 형제께서 편하실 때 말씀하시거나, 제가 말하게 두셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["“Yes, I shall take my own time—you needn’t offer me yours,”", "\"그래, 나는 내가 편할 때 하겠다—네 시간을 내게 내줄 것 없어.\""], ["said Peter.", "피터가 말했다."]], [["“But you can’t take your own time to die in, Brother,”", "\"하지만 형제께서 죽으실 때는 형제 편하실 때로 잡을 수가 없잖습니까, 형제.\""], ["began Mrs. Waule, with her usual woolly tone.", "와울 부인이 평소의 흐리멍덩한 어조로 입을 떼었다."], ["“And when you lie speechless you may be tired of having strangers about you, and you may think of me and my children”—but here her voice broke under the touching thought which she was attributing to her speechless brother; the mention of ourselves being naturally affecting.", "\"말씀도 못 하시고 누워 계실 때면 낯선 사람들이 곁에 있는 게 지겨워지실 테고, 그러면 저와 제 자식들 생각이 나실 거예요\"—그러나 여기서 그녀의 목소리는, 말 못 하는 형제에게 자기가 갖다 붙인 그 감동적인 생각에 겨워 잠겼으니, 우리 자신을 언급하는 일이란 자연히 마음을 울리게 마련이었다."]], [["“No, I shan’t,”", "\"아니, 안 날 거다.\""], ["said old Featherstone, contradictiously.", "페더스톤 노인이 어깃장을 놓듯 말했다."], ["“I shan’t think of any of you.", "\"너희 중 누구 생각도 안 할 거야."], ["I’ve made my will, I tell you, I’ve made my will.”", "유언장은 만들어 놨다고, 말했잖아, 유언장은 다 만들어 놨다고.\""], ["Here he turned his head towards Mrs. Vincy, and swallowed some more of his cordial.", "여기서 그는 빈시 부인 쪽으로 고개를 돌리고 강심제를 좀 더 들이켰다."]], [["“Some people would be ashamed to fill up a place belonging by rights to others,” said Mrs.", "\"어떤 사람들은 마땅히 남의 것인 자리를 차지하고 들어앉은 걸 부끄러워할 텐데요.\""], ["Waule, turning her narrow eyes in the same direction.", "와울 부인이 가느다란 눈을 같은 방향으로 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, sister,”", "\"오, 누이.\" 솔로몬이 비꼬듯 부드럽게 말했다."], ["said Solomon, with ironical softness, “you and me are not fine, and handsome, and clever enough: we must be humble and let smart people push themselves before us.”", "\"너랑 나는 곱지도, 잘나지도, 똑똑하지도 못하잖아. 우린 겸손하게 굴며 똑똑한 사람들이 우리 앞에 끼어들도록 내버려 둬야지.\""]], [["Fred’s spirit could not bear this: rising and looking at Mr.", "프레드는 이를 참을 수 없었다. 그는 일어나 페더스톤 씨를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Featherstone, he said, “Shall my mother and I leave the room, sir, that you may be alone with your friends?”", "\"어르신, 친지분들과 단둘이 계시도록 저와 제 어머니가 자리를 비울까요?\""]], [["“Sit down, I tell you,”", "\"앉아 있으래도.\""], ["said old Featherstone, snappishly.", "페더스톤 노인이 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["“Stop where you are.", "\"있는 자리에 그대로 있어."], ["Good-by, Solomon,”", "잘 가게, 솔로몬.\""], ["he added, trying to wield his stick again, but failing now that he had reversed the handle.", "그는 다시 지팡이를 휘두르려 했으나, 손잡이를 거꾸로 돌려 쥔 터라 이번에는 실패하며 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["“Good-by, Mrs. Waule.", "\"잘 가게, 와울 부인."], ["Don’t you come again.”", "다시는 오지 마.\""]], [["“I shall be down-stairs, Brother, whether or no,”", "\"형제, 그래도 저는 아래층에 있겠습니다.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“I shall do my duty, and it remains to be seen what the Almighty will allow.”", "\"제 도리를 다할 것이고, 전능하신 분께서 무엇을 허락하실지는 두고 볼 일이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, in property going out of families,” said Mrs.", "\"그렇고말고요, 재산이 집안 밖으로 나가는 일에 대해서 말이지요.\""], ["Waule, in continuation,—“and where there’s steady young men to carry on.", "와울 부인이 이어서 말했다. \"그리고 가업을 이어 갈 견실한 젊은이들이 있는 마당에 말이에요."], ["But I pity them who are not such, and I pity their mothers.", "하지만 저는 그렇지 못한 자들이 가엾고, 그 어미들이 가엾어요."], ["Good-by, Brother Peter.”", "잘 계세요, 피터 형제.\""]], [["“Remember, I’m the eldest after you, Brother, and prospered from the first, just as you did, and have got land already by the name of Featherstone,”", "\"명심하세요, 형제, 저는 형제 다음으로 맏이이고, 형제와 똑같이 처음부터 번창했으며, 페더스톤이라는 이름으로 이미 땅을 가지고 있다는 걸요.\""], ["said Solomon, relying much on that reflection, as one which might be suggested in the watches of the night.", "솔로몬은 한밤중 잠 못 이루는 시각에 떠오를 법한 생각이라며 그 점에 크게 기대를 걸고 말했다."], ["“But I bid you good-by for the present.”", "\"그러나 우선은 작별 인사를 드리지요.\""]], [["Their exit was hastened by their seeing old Mr. Featherstone pull his wig on each side and shut his eyes with his mouth-widening grimace, as if he were determined to be deaf and blind.", "페더스톤 노인이 가발을 양옆으로 잡아당기고 입을 한껏 벌려 찡그리며 눈을 감는 것을, 마치 귀먹고 눈먼 사람이 되기로 작정한 듯한 그 모습을 보고서 그들은 서둘러 나갔다."]], [["None the less they came to Stone Court daily and sat below at the post of duty, sometimes carrying on a slow dialogue in an undertone in which the observation and response were so far apart, that any one hearing them might have imagined himself listening to speaking automata, in some doubt whether the ingenious mechanism would really work, or wind itself up for a long time in order to stick and be silent.", "그럼에도 그들은 날마다 스톤 코트에 와서 아래층 의무의 초소에 앉아, 때로는 낮은 목소리로 느릿느릿 대화를 이어 갔는데, 그 말과 응답이 어찌나 멀리 떨어져 있던지, 누군가 그것을 들었다면 자신이 말하는 자동인형의 소리에 귀 기울이고 있다고 여기며, 그 정교한 기계 장치가 정말로 작동할지, 아니면 한참 동안 태엽을 감다가 멈춰 버리고 잠잠해질지 미심쩍어했을 것이다."], ["Solomon and Jane would have been sorry to be quick: what that led to might be seen on the other side of the wall in the person of Brother Jonah.", "솔로몬과 제인은 빨라지는 것을 한사코 꺼렸을 것이다. 그것이 무엇으로 이어지는지는 담장 너머 요나 형제라는 인물에게서 볼 수 있었으니까."]], [["But their watch in the wainscoted parlor was sometimes varied by the presence of other guests from far or near.", "그러나 징두리널을 댄 거실에서 그들이 망보는 일은 때때로 멀고 가까운 곳에서 온 다른 손님들의 존재로 변화를 맞았다."], ["Now that Peter Featherstone was up-stairs, his property could be discussed with all that local enlightenment to be found on the spot: some rural and Middlemarch neighbors expressed much agreement with the family and sympathy with their interest against the Vincys, and feminine visitors were even moved to tears, in conversation with Mrs.", "피터 페더스톤이 위층에 있게 된 지금, 그의 재산은 현장에서 얻을 수 있는 온갖 지역적 식견을 동원하여 논의될 수 있었다."], ["Waule, when they recalled the fact that they themselves had been disappointed in times past by codicils and marriages for spite on the part of ungrateful elderly gentlemen, who, it might have been supposed, had been spared for something better.", "시골과 미들마치의 이웃들 가운데 더러는 가족의 의견에 크게 동의하며 빈시가에 맞선 그들의 이해에 공감을 표했고, 여성 방문객들은 와울 부인과 이야기를 나누다, 마땅히 더 나은 일을 위해 명을 부지하고 있으리라 여겨질 법한 은혜를 모르는 노신사들이 심술궂게 유언 보충서를 만들거나 결혼을 해 버리는 통에 자기들도 지난날 실망했던 사실을 떠올리며 눈물을 흘리기까지 했다."], ["Such conversation paused suddenly, like an organ when the bellows are let drop, if Mary Garth came into the room; and all eyes were turned on her as a possible legatee, or one who might get access to iron chests.", "그런 대화는 메리 가스가 방에 들어오면, 풀무에서 손을 놓아 버린 오르간처럼 뚝 멎었고, 모든 눈이 잠재적 유산 상속인일지도 모를, 아니면 무쇠 궤짝에 접근할 수 있을지도 모를 사람으로서 그녀에게 쏠렸다."]], [["But the younger men who were relatives or connections of the family, were disposed to admire her in this problematic light, as a girl who showed much conduct, and who among all the chances that were flying might turn out to be at least a moderate prize.", "그러나 가족의 친척이거나 인척인 젊은 남자들은, 이런 미심쩍은 처지에 놓인 그녀를, 처신이 반듯하고, 이리저리 날아다니는 온갖 기회 가운데 적어도 적당한 상품쯤은 될지도 모를 처녀로 보아 흠모하는 쪽이었다."], ["Hence she had her share of compliments and polite attentions.", "그리하여 그녀는 그녀 몫의 찬사와 정중한 관심을 받았다."]], [["Especially from Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, a distinguished bachelor and auctioneer of those parts, much concerned in the sale of land and cattle: a public character, indeed, whose name was seen on widely distributed placards, and who might reasonably be sorry for those who did not know of him.", "특히 그 지방의 저명한 독신남이자 경매인인 보스럽 트럼불 씨가 그러했는데, 그는 토지와 가축의 매매에 깊이 관여한 인물이었다—실로 널리 뿌려진 벽보에서 그 이름을 볼 수 있는 공인이었으니, 자기를 모르는 자들을 딱하게 여길 만도 했다."], ["He was second cousin to Peter Featherstone, and had been treated by him with more amenity than any other relative, being useful in matters of business; and in that programme of his funeral which the old man had himself dictated, he had been named as a Bearer. There was no odious cupidity in Mr.", "그는 피터 페더스톤의 육촌으로, 사업적인 일에 유용했던 까닭에 다른 어느 친척보다도 호의적인 대접을 받아 왔고, 노인이 직접 지시해 둔 장례식 절차에서 운구인으로 지명되어 있었다."], ["Borthrop Trumbull—nothing more than a sincere sense of his own merit, which, he was aware, in case of rivalry might tell against competitors; so that if Peter Featherstone, who so far as he, Trumbull, was concerned, had behaved like as good a soul as ever breathed, should have done anything handsome by him, all he could say was, that he had never fished and fawned, but had advised him to the best of his experience, which now extended over twenty years from the time of his apprenticeship at fifteen, and was likely to yield a knowledge of no surreptitious kind.", "보스럽 트럼불 씨에게는 가증스러운 탐욕 따위는 없었다—자기 자신의 가치에 대한 진실한 의식 말고는 아무것도 없었는데, 그는 경쟁이 붙을 경우 그 가치가 경쟁자들에게 불리하게 작용하리라는 것도 알고 있었다. 그러니 자기, 트럼불에 관한 한 더없이 선량한 사람처럼 행동해 온 피터 페더스톤이 그에게 무언가 후한 처사를 베푼다 해도, 자기로서는 결코 알랑거리거나 아첨한 적 없이 그저 자신의 경험을 다해 그에게 조언했을 뿐이라고밖에 할 말이 없을 터였다."], ["His admiration was far from being confined to himself, but was accustomed professionally as well as privately to delight in estimating things at a high rate.", "그 경험은 이제 열다섯 살에 도제 생활을 시작한 때로부터 이십 년에 걸쳐 있었으니, 떳떳하지 못한 종류의 지식을 내놓을 리 없는 경험이었다."], ["He was an amateur of superior phrases, and never used poor language without immediately correcting himself—which was fortunate, as he was rather loud, and given to predominate, standing or walking about frequently, pulling down his waistcoat with the air of a man who is very much of his own opinion, trimming himself rapidly with his fore-finger, and marking each new series in these movements by a busy play with his large seals.", "그의 흠모는 결코 자기 자신에게만 국한되지 않았으니, 직업적으로나 사적으로나 무엇이든 높이 평가하기를 즐기는 데 익숙했다. 그는 고상한 표현의 애호가여서, 변변찮은 말을 쓸 때면 곧바로 스스로 고치곤 했다—이는 다행한 일이었으니, 그는 다소 목소리가 크고 좌중을 압도하는 경향이 있어, 자주 서거나 걸어 다니고, 자기 의견이 확고한 사람다운 태도로 조끼를 끌어내리며, 집게손가락으로 재빨리 옷매무새를 가다듬고, 이런 동작들의 새로운 단계마다 큼직한 인장들을 분주히 만지작거려 표시하곤 했다."], ["There was occasionally a little fierceness in his demeanor, but it was directed chiefly against false opinion, of which there is so much to correct in the world that a man of some reading and experience necessarily has his patience tried.", "이따금 그의 태도에는 약간의 사나움이 깃들기도 했지만, 그것은 주로 그릇된 견해를 겨눈 것이었으니, 세상에는 바로잡아야 할 그릇된 견해가 어찌나 많은지 어지간히 책을 읽고 경험을 쌓은 사람이라면 필연코 그 인내심을 시험당하게 마련이었다."], ["He felt that the Featherstone family generally was of limited understanding, but being a man of the world and a public character, took everything as a matter of course, and even went to converse with Mr. Jonah and young Cranch in the kitchen, not doubting that he had impressed the latter greatly by his leading questions concerning the Chalky Flats.", "그는 페더스톤 가문이 대체로 이해력이 한정된 사람들이라고 느꼈지만, 세상 물정에 밝은 공인답게 모든 것을 당연한 일로 받아들였고, 심지어 부엌으로 가서 요나 씨와 젊은 크랜치와 담소를 나누기까지 했는데, 초크 플랫에 관한 유도 질문들로 후자에게 크게 깊은 인상을 남겼다고 믿어 의심치 않았다."], ["If anybody had observed that Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, being an auctioneer, was bound to know the nature of everything, he would have smiled and trimmed himself silently with the sense that he came pretty near that.", "만일 누군가가 보스럽 트럼불 씨는 경매인이니 무엇이든 그 본질을 알아야 마땅하다고 한다면, 그는 미소를 지으며 자기가 그에 꽤 가깝다는 의식과 함께 말없이 옷매무새를 가다듬었을 것이다."], ["On the whole, in an auctioneering way, he was an honorable man, not ashamed of his business, and feeling that “the celebrated Peel, now Sir Robert,”", "대체로 그는 경매인다운 방식으로 명예로운 사람이었고, 자기 직업을 부끄러워하지 않았으며, \"이제는 로버트 경이 된 저 유명한 필\""], ["if introduced to him, would not fail to recognize his importance.", "씨가 자신에게 소개된다면 틀림없이 자신의 중요함을 알아보리라고 여겼다."]], [["“I don’t mind if I have a slice of that ham, and a glass of that ale, Miss Garth, if you will allow me,”", "\"저 햄을 한 조각 먹고 저 에일을 한 잔 마셔도 괜찮다면, 가스 양, 그렇게 하겠소.\""], ["he said, coming into the parlor at half-past eleven, after having had the exceptional privilege of seeing old Featherstone, and standing with his back to the fire between Mrs. Waule and Solomon.", "그는 예외적으로 페더스톤 노인을 보는 특권을 누린 뒤 열한 시 반에 거실로 들어와, 와울 부인과 솔로몬 사이에서 난롯불을 등지고 서서 말했다."]], [["“It’s not necessary for you to go out;—let me ring the bell.”", "\"나가실 필요 없습니다—제가 종을 울리지요.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙소.\""], ["said Mary, “I have an errand.”", "메리가 말했다. \"심부름 갈 일이 있어서요.\""]], [["“Well, Mr. Trumbull, you’re highly favored,”", "\"흠, 트럼불 씨, 큰 은총을 입으셨군요.\""], ["said Mrs. Waule.", "와울 부인이 말했다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["seeing the old man?”", "노인을 만난 것 말이오?\""], ["said the auctioneer, playing with his seals dispassionately.", "경매인이 무심하게 인장을 만지작거리며 말했다."], ["“Ah, you see he has relied on me considerably.”", "\"아, 보시다시피 그분은 저를 상당히 의지하셨지요.\""], ["Here he pressed his lips together, and frowned meditatively.", "여기서 그는 입술을 꾹 다물고 생각에 잠긴 듯 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Might anybody ask what their brother has been saying?”", "\"형제분께서 무슨 말씀을 하셨는지 누구라도 여쭤봐도 될까요?\""], ["said Solomon, in a soft tone of humility, in which he had a sense of luxurious cunning, he being a rich man and not in need of it.", "솔로몬이 부드럽고 겸손한 어조로 말했는데, 그 안에는 부유해서 그럴 필요도 없는 사람이 누리는 호사스러운 교활함의 의식이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["“Oh yes, anybody may ask,” said Mr.", "\"오, 그렇소, 누구든 물어도 좋소.\""], ["Trumbull, with loud and good-humored though cutting sarcasm.", "트럼불 씨가 크고 호탕하면서도 날카로운 비아냥을 담아 말했다. \"누구든 캐물어도 좋고."], ["“Anybody may interrogate.", "누구든 자기 말을 의문형으로 돌려도 좋지요.\""], ["Any one may give their remarks an interrogative turn,”", "그는 어조에 따라 점점 더 낭랑해지며 말을 이었다."], ["he continued, his sonorousness rising with his style.", "\"이는 훌륭한 연설가들이 답을 기대하지 않을 때에도 늘 하는 일이지요."], ["“This is constantly done by good speakers, even when they anticipate no answer.", "우리가 수사적 표현이라 부르는 겁니다—말하자면, 비싼 값으로 하는 표현 말이오.\" 능변의 경매인은 자기 재치에 흐뭇이 미소를 지었다."], ["It is what we call a figure of speech—speech at a high figure, as one may say.” The eloquent auctioneer smiled at his own ingenuity.", "* 수사적 표현: 원문은 'figure of speech'를 'speech at a high figure'(높은 값으로 하는 말)로 비튼 말장난이다."]], [["“I shouldn’t be sorry to hear he’d remembered you, Mr. Trumbull,”", "\"형제분께서 당신을 기억해 주셨다는 말을 들어도 저는 섭섭지 않겠습니다, 트럼불 씨.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“I never was against the deserving.", "\"저는 받을 만한 사람을 마다한 적이 없습니다."], ["It’s the undeserving I’m against.”", "제가 마다하는 건 받을 만하지 못한 자들이지요.\""]], [["“Ah, there it is, you see, there it is,”", "\"아, 바로 그겁니다, 보세요, 바로 그거예요.\""], ["said Mr. Trumbull, significantly.", "트럼불 씨가 의미심장하게 말했다."], ["“It can’t be denied that undeserving people have been legatees, and even residuary legatees.", "\"받을 만하지 못한 사람들이 유산 상속인이, 심지어 잔여 재산 상속인이 되어 온 것은 부인할 수 없는 사실이지요."], ["It is so, with testamentary dispositions.”", "유언에 의한 처분이란 게 그런 겁니다.\""], ["Again he pursed up his lips and frowned a little.", "그는 다시금 입술을 오므리고 살짝 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Do you mean to say for certain, Mr. Trumbull, that my brother has left his land away from our family?”", "\"트럼불 씨, 제 형제가 자기 땅을 우리 집안에서 떼어 내 남에게 남겼다는 걸 확실히 말씀하시는 건가요?\""], ["said Mrs. Waule, on whom, as an unhopeful woman, those long words had a depressing effect.", "와울 부인이 말했는데, 희망 없는 여인인 그녀에게 그 긴 단어들은 의기소침하게 만드는 효과가 있었다."]], [["“A man might as well turn his land into charity land at once as leave it to some people,”", "\"어떤 사람들에게 땅을 남기느니, 차라리 단번에 자선용 토지로 바꿔 버리는 편이 나을 거요.\""], ["observed Solomon, his sister’s question having drawn no answer.", "솔로몬이, 누이의 물음에 아무 대답이 나오지 않자 한마디 했다."]], [["“What, Blue-Coat land?” said Mrs.", "\"뭐라고요, 자선 학교 땅으로 말인가요?\""], ["Waule, again. “Oh, Mr.", "와울 부인이 다시 말했다."], ["Trumbull, you never can mean to say that.", "\"오, 트럼불 씨, 설마 그런 말씀을 하시는 건 아니겠지요."], ["It would be flying in the face of the Almighty that’s prospered him.”", "그건 그분을 번창케 하신 전능하신 분의 뜻을 거스르는 일이 될 거예요.\""]], [["While Mrs. Waule was speaking, Mr.", "와울 부인이 말하는 동안, 보스럽 트럼불 씨는 난롯가에서 창가 쪽으로 걸어가며, 집게손가락으로 옷깃 안쪽을 한 바퀴 돌리고, 이어 구레나룻과 곱슬머리의 굴곡을 따라 쓸어내렸다."], ["Borthrop Trumbull walked away from the fireplace towards the window, patrolling with his fore-finger round the inside of his stock, then along his whiskers and the curves of his hair. He now walked to Miss Garth’s work-table, opened a book which lay there and read the title aloud with pompous emphasis as if he were offering it for sale:", "이제 그는 가스 양의 반짇고리 탁자로 걸어가, 거기 놓인 책 한 권을 펴서, 마치 그것을 경매에 내놓기라도 하는 듯 거드름을 피우며 제목을 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["“‘Anne of Geierstein’ (pronounced Jeersteen) or the ‘Maiden of the Mist, by the author of Waverley.’”", "\"'가이어슈타인의 안네'(지어스틴이라 발음하오) 혹은 '안개의 처녀, 「웨이벌리」의 저자 지음.'\""], ["Then turning the page, he began sonorously—“The course of four centuries has well-nigh elapsed since the series of events which are related in the following chapters took place on the Continent.”", "그러고는 책장을 넘기며 낭랑하게 시작했다—\"다음 장들에서 서술되는 일련의 사건들이 대륙에서 일어난 이래 사백 년의 세월이 흐른 셈이다.\""], ["He pronounced the last truly admirable word with the accent on the last syllable, not as unaware of vulgar usage, but feeling that this novel delivery enhanced the sonorous beauty which his reading had given to the whole.", "그는 마지막의 참으로 멋진 단어를 마지막 음절에 강세를 주어 발음했는데, 통속적인 발음을 모르는 게 아니라, 이 색다른 발음이 자기 낭독이 전체에 부여한 낭랑한 아름다움을 한층 돋우어 준다고 느꼈기 때문이다. * 대륙: 원문 'Continent'를 트럼불은 마지막 음절에 엉뚱하게 강세를 두어 'Continént'로 발음한다."]], [["And now the servant came in with the tray, so that the moments for answering Mrs. Waule’s question had gone by safely, while she and Solomon, watching Mr.", "마침 그때 하인이 쟁반을 들고 들어왔고, 그리하여 와울 부인의 물음에 답할 순간들은 무사히 지나가 버렸으며, 그녀와 솔로몬은 트럼불 씨의 동작을 지켜보며, 높은 학식이란 진지한 일에 딱하게도 방해가 되는구나 하고 생각하고 있었다."], ["Trumbull’s movements, were thinking that high learning interfered sadly with serious affairs.", "보스럽 트럼불 씨는 페더스톤 노인의 유언에 관해 실은 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["Mr. Borthrop Trumbull really knew nothing about old Featherstone’s will; but he could hardly have been brought to declare any ignorance unless he had been arrested for misprision of treason.", "그러나 반역 은닉죄로 체포되지 않는 한, 그가 무지를 고백하게 만들기란 도무지 불가능했을 것이다."]], [["“I shall take a mere mouthful of ham and a glass of ale,”", "\"햄은 그저 한 입, 에일은 한 잔만 들겠소.\""], ["he said, reassuringly.", "그는 안심시키듯 말했다."], ["“As a man with public business, I take a snack when I can.", "\"공무를 보는 사람이라 틈날 때 요기를 해 두지요."], ["I will back this ham,”", "이 햄으로 말하자면,\""], ["he added, after swallowing some morsels with alarming haste, “against any ham in the three kingdoms.", "그는 위태로울 만큼 황급히 몇 점을 삼키고 나서 덧붙였다, \"세 왕국의 어떤 햄과 견주어도 이 햄에 걸겠소."], ["In my opinion it is better than the hams at Freshitt Hall—and I think I am a tolerable judge.”", "내 보기엔 프레시트 홀의 햄보다도 낫소—그리고 내가 웬만큼은 감식가라고 생각하오만.\""]], [["“Some don’t like so much sugar in their hams,”", "\"어떤 사람들은 햄에 설탕을 그렇게 많이 넣는 걸 안 좋아하지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Waule.", "와울 부인이 말했다."], ["“But my poor brother would always have sugar.”", "\"하지만 제 가엾은 형제는 늘 설탕을 넣곤 했어요.\""]], [["“If any person demands better, he is at liberty to do so; but, God bless me, what an aroma!", "\"더 나은 걸 요구하는 사람이 있다면 마음대로 하라지요. 하지만 맙소사, 이 향이라니!"], ["I should be glad to buy in that quality, I know.", "정말이지 나라면 이런 품질을 사들이고 싶을 거요."], ["There is some gratification to a gentleman”—here Mr. Trumbull’s voice conveyed an emotional remonstrance—“in having this kind of ham set on his table.”", "신사에게는 어떤 흡족함이 있단 말이오\"—여기서 트럼불 씨의 목소리는 감정이 북받친 항변을 담았다—\"이런 햄을 식탁에 차려 놓는 데에 말이오.\""]], [["He pushed aside his plate, poured out his glass of ale and drew his chair a little forward, profiting by the occasion to look at the inner side of his legs, which he stroked approvingly—Mr. Trumbull having all those less frivolous airs and gestures which distinguish the predominant races of the north.", "그는 접시를 옆으로 밀어 놓고, 에일을 잔에 따른 뒤, 의자를 약간 앞으로 끌어당기며, 그 기회를 빌려 제 다리 안쪽을 들여다보고는 흡족하게 쓰다듬었다—트럼불 씨에게는 북방의 우월한 종족들을 구별 짓는, 그 모든 덜 경박한 태도와 몸짓이 두루 갖추어져 있었다."]], [["“You have an interesting work there, I see, Miss Garth,”", "\"흥미로운 책을 가지고 계시는군요, 가스 양.\""], ["he observed, when Mary re-entered.", "메리가 다시 들어오자 그가 말했다."], ["“It is by the author of ‘Waverley’: that is Sir Walter Scott.", "\"이건 「웨이벌리」의 저자가 쓴 것이오. 다름 아닌 월터 스콧 경이지."], ["I have bought one of his works myself—a very nice thing, a very superior publication, entitled ‘Ivanhoe.’ You will not get any writer to beat him in a hurry, I think—he will not, in my opinion, be speedily surpassed.", "나도 그의 작품 하나를 사 둔 게 있소—아주 근사한 것으로, 매우 뛰어난 출판물인데, 제목이 「아이반호」요. 내 생각엔 당분간 그를 능가할 작가는 쉬 나오지 않을 거요—내 보기에 그는 머잖아 추월당하지는 않을 거란 말이오."], ["I have just been reading a portion at the commencement of ‘Anne of Jeersteen.’ It commences well.”", "방금 막 「지어스틴의 안네」의 첫머리 한 대목을 읽던 참인데."], ["(Things never began with Mr.", "시작이 좋소.\""], ["Borthrop Trumbull: they always commenced, both in private life and on his handbills.) “You are a reader, I see.", "(보스럽 트럼불 씨에게는 무엇도 그저 '시작'되는 법이 없었다. 그것은 사생활에서나 그의 전단지에서나 늘 '개시'되었다.) \"보아하니 독서가시구려."], ["Do you subscribe to our Middlemarch library?”", "우리 미들마치 도서관에 가입하셨소?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["“Mr. Fred Vincy brought this book.”", "\"이 책은 프레드 빈시 씨가 가져온 거예요.\""]], [["“I am a great bookman myself,”", "\"나로 말하면 굉장한 장서가요.\""], ["returned Mr. Trumbull.", "트럼불 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“I have no less than two hundred volumes in calf, and I flatter myself they are well selected.", "\"송아지 가죽으로 장정한 책이 자그마치 이백 권은 되고, 자부하건대 잘 골라 모은 것들이지."], ["Also pictures by Murillo, Rubens, Teniers, Titian, Vandyck, and others.", "게다가 무리요, 루벤스, 테니르스, 티치아노, 반다이크 등의 그림도 있소."], ["I shall be happy to lend you any work you like to mention, Miss Garth.”", "가스 양, 말씀만 하시면 어떤 작품이든 기꺼이 빌려 드리리다.\""]], [["“I am much obliged,”", "\"대단히 감사합니다.\""], ["said Mary, hastening away again, “but I have little time for reading.”", "메리가 다시 서둘러 자리를 뜨며 말했다. \"하지만 저는 책 읽을 시간이 거의 없어서요.\""]], [["“I should say my brother has done something for her in his will,”", "\"제 형제가 유언으로 저 여자에게 뭔가 해 줬을 것 같소만.\""], ["said Mr. Solomon, in a very low undertone, when she had shut the door behind her, pointing with his head towards the absent Mary.", "메리가 등 뒤로 문을 닫고 나가자, 솔로몬 씨가 자리에 없는 메리 쪽으로 고개를 까딱이며 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“His first wife was a poor match for him, though,”", "\"그래도 형제의 첫 부인은 그분에게 어울리지 않는 짝이었어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Waule.", "와울 부인이 말했다."], ["“She brought him nothing: and this young woman is only her niece,—and very proud.", "\"아무것도 가져온 게 없었지요. 게다가 이 젊은 여자는 그 부인의 조카에 불과한데다—몹시 도도하고요."], ["And my brother has always paid her wage.”", "그리고 제 형제는 늘 저 여자에게 품삯을 줘 왔어요.\""]], [["“A sensible girl though, in my opinion,”", "\"하지만 내 생각엔 분별 있는 처녀요.\""], ["said Mr. Trumbull, finishing his ale and starting up with an emphatic adjustment of his waistcoat.", "트럼불 씨가 에일을 다 마시고는 조끼를 힘주어 매만지며 벌떡 일어나 말했다."], ["“I have observed her when she has been mixing medicine in drops.", "\"약을 방울로 섞는 걸 내가 지켜본 적이 있소."], ["She minds what she is doing, sir.", "자기가 하는 일에 마음을 쓰더이다."], ["That is a great point in a woman, and a great point for our friend up-stairs, poor dear old soul.", "그건 여자에게 큰 장점이고, 위층의 우리 친구, 가엾고 다정한 노인분께도 큰 다행이지."], ["A man whose life is of any value should think of his wife as a nurse: that is what I should do, if I married; and I believe I have lived single long enough not to make a mistake in that line.", "목숨에 조금이라도 가치가 있는 사내라면 아내를 간호사로 생각해야 하는 법이오. 나라면, 결혼한다면 그렇게 하겠소."], ["Some men must marry to elevate themselves a little, but when I am in need of that, I hope some one will tell me so—I hope some individual will apprise me of the fact.", "그리고 내 믿건대 나는 이 방면에서 실수하지 않을 만큼은 충분히 오래 독신으로 살아왔소. 어떤 사내들은 제 처지를 좀 끌어올리려고 결혼해야 하지만, 내가 그게 필요할 때면 누군가 내게 그렇다고 말해 주기를 바라오—누군가 그 사실을 내게 일러 주기를 바란단 말이오."], ["I wish you good morning, Mrs. Waule.", "그럼 안녕히 계시오, 와울 부인."], ["Good morning, Mr. Solomon.", "안녕히 계시오, 솔로몬 씨."], ["I trust we shall meet under less melancholy auspices.”", "다음번엔 덜 우울한 자리에서 뵙기를 바라오.\""]], [["When Mr.", "트럼불 씨가 멋들어진 인사를 남기고 떠나자, 솔로몬은 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 누이에게 말했다."], ["Trumbull had departed with a fine bow, Solomon, leaning forward, observed to his sister, “You may depend, Jane, my brother has left that girl a lumping sum.”", "\"제인, 분명히 말하건대, 내 형제가 저 처녀한테 큰돈을 남겼어.\""]], [["“Anybody would think so, from the way Mr. Trumbull talks,”", "\"트럼불 씨가 말하는 걸 보면 누구라도 그렇게 생각하겠지요.\""], ["said Jane.", "제인이 말했다."], ["Then, after a pause, “He talks as if my daughters wasn’t to be trusted to give drops.”", "그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가, \"마치 내 딸들은 약방울 하나 맡길 수 없다는 식으로 말하던데요.\""]], [["“Auctioneers talk wild,”", "\"경매인들은 말을 함부로 하지.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“Not but what Trumbull has made money.”", "\"하긴 트럼불이 돈을 벌긴 했다만.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIII.", "# CHAPTER XXXIII."]], [["“Close up his eyes and draw the curtain close; And let us all to meditation.”", "“그의 두 눈을 감기고 휘장을 단단히 드리워라."], ["—2 Henry VI.", "그리고 우리 모두 묵상에 잠기자.”"]], [["That night after twelve o’clock Mary Garth relieved the watch in Mr. Featherstone’s room, and sat there alone through the small hours.", "—『헨리 6세』 제2부."], ["She often chose this task, in which she found some pleasure, notwithstanding the old man’s testiness whenever he demanded her attentions.", "그날 밤 자정이 지난 뒤 메리 가스는 페더스톤 씨의 방에서 간병을 교대하고는, 새벽의 그 적막한 시간 내내 홀로 그곳에 앉아 있었다."], ["There were intervals in which she could sit perfectly still, enjoying the outer stillness and the subdued light.", "그녀는 종종 이 일을 자청했는데, 노인이 시중을 요구할 때마다 부려 대는 짜증에도 불구하고 거기서 어떤 즐거움을 찾았기 때문이다."], ["The red fire with its gently audible movement seemed like a solemn existence calmly independent of the petty passions, the imbecile desires, the straining after worthless uncertainties, which were daily moving her contempt.", "그녀가 완전히 가만히 앉아 바깥의 고요와 은은한 불빛을 만끽할 수 있는 짬도 있었다. 부드럽게 사각거리는 소리를 내며 타오르는 붉은 불은 그녀가 날마다 경멸하던 시시한 격정, 어리석은 욕망, 하잘것없는 불확실함을 향한 안간힘 따위로부터 침착하게 독립한, 어떤 엄숙한 존재처럼 보였다."], ["Mary was fond of her own thoughts, and could amuse herself well sitting in twilight with her hands in her lap; for, having early had strong reason to believe that things were not likely to be arranged for her peculiar satisfaction, she wasted no time in astonishment and annoyance at that fact.", "메리는 자기만의 생각을 즐겼고, 무릎에 손을 얹은 채 어스름 속에 앉아서도 능히 스스로를 즐겁게 할 수 있었다. 일찍이 세상사가 자신의 별난 만족에 맞게 배열되지는 않으리라고 믿을 만한 강력한 이유를 알게 된 터라, 그녀는 그 사실에 놀라거나 짜증을 내며 시간을 허비하지 않았다."], ["And she had already come to take life very much as a comedy in which she had a proud, nay, a generous resolution not to act the mean or treacherous part.", "그리고 그녀는 이미 인생을 하나의 희극으로 받아들이게 되었으니, 그 안에서 자신은 비열하거나 배신적인 역할을 맡지 않겠다는 당당한, 아니 관대한 결심을 품고 있었다."], ["Mary might have become cynical if she had not had parents whom she honored, and a well of affectionate gratitude within her, which was all the fuller because she had learned to make no unreasonable claims.", "메리는 자신이 존경하는 부모와, 내면에 솟아나는 애정 어린 감사의 샘이 없었다면 냉소적인 사람이 되었을지도 모른다. 그 샘은 그녀가 부당한 요구를 하지 않는 법을 배웠기에 더욱 가득 차 있었다."]], [["She sat to-night revolving, as she was wont, the scenes of the day, her lips often curling with amusement at the oddities to which her fancy added fresh drollery: people were so ridiculous with their illusions, carrying their fool’s caps unawares, thinking their own lies opaque while everybody else’s were transparent, making themselves exceptions to everything, as if when all the world looked yellow under a lamp they alone were rosy.", "오늘 밤 그녀는 늘 그러하듯 그날의 장면들을 곱씹으며 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 상상이 새로운 익살을 보태는 기묘한 일들에 종종 입가에 즐거운 미소를 띠었다. 사람들은 그 환상으로 어찌나 우스꽝스러운지, 자기도 모르게 바보의 고깔모자를 쓰고 다니며, 남의 거짓말은 다 들여다보이는데 제 거짓말만은 불투명하다고 여기고, 매사에 자신만은 예외인 것처럼 굴었다."], ["Yet there were some illusions under Mary’s eyes which were not quite comic to her.", "마치 등불 아래 온 세상이 노랗게 보일 때 저 혼자만 장밋빛이라는 듯이."], ["She was secretly convinced, though she had no other grounds than her close observation of old Featherstone’s nature, that in spite of his fondness for having the Vincys about him, they were as likely to be disappointed as any of the relations whom he kept at a distance.", "그러나 메리의 눈앞에는 그녀에게 그다지 우습지만은 않은 환상들도 있었다. 그녀는 늙은 페더스톤의 성정을 가까이서 관찰한 것 말고는 다른 근거가 없었음에도, 그가 빈시 식구들을 곁에 두기를 좋아하긴 해도 그들 또한 그가 멀리하는 다른 친척들과 다름없이 실망할 공산이 크다고 남몰래 확신하고 있었다."], ["She had a good deal of disdain for Mrs. Vincy’s evident alarm lest she and Fred should be alone together, but it did not hinder her from thinking anxiously of the way in which Fred would be affected, if it should turn out that his uncle had left him as poor as ever.", "그녀는 자신과 프레드가 단둘이 있을까 봐 빈시 부인이 노골적으로 경계하는 것을 상당히 경멸했지만, 그렇다고 해서 만일 삼촌이 프레드를 여전히 빈털터리로 남겨 둔 것으로 판명될 경우 그가 어떻게 영향을 받을지 근심스레 생각하는 것을 막지는 못했다."], ["She could make a butt of Fred when he was present, but she did not enjoy his follies when he was absent.", "그녀는 프레드가 곁에 있을 때면 그를 놀림감으로 삼을 수 있었지만, 그가 없을 때는 그의 어리석음을 즐기지 못했다."]], [["Yet she liked her thoughts: a vigorous young mind not overbalanced by passion, finds a good in making acquaintance with life, and watches its own powers with interest.", "그래도 그녀는 자신의 생각을 좋아했다. 격정에 지나치게 휘둘리지 않는 활기찬 젊은 정신은 인생과 친분을 맺는 데서 보람을 찾고, 자기 자신의 역량을 흥미롭게 지켜보는 법이다."], ["Mary had plenty of merriment within.", "메리의 내면에는 유쾌함이 가득했다."]], [["Her thought was not veined by any solemnity or pathos about the old man on the bed: such sentiments are easier to affect than to feel about an aged creature whose life is not visibly anything but a remnant of vices.", "침대에 누운 노인에 대한 그녀의 생각에는 어떤 엄숙함이나 비애도 서려 있지 않았다. 그런 감정이란 삶이 보아하니 악덕의 잔재일 뿐인 늙은 존재에 대해 실제로 느끼기보다 꾸며 내기가 더 쉬운 법이다."], ["She had always seen the most disagreeable side of Mr.", "그녀는 늘 페더스톤 씨의 가장 못마땅한 면만을 보아 왔다."], ["Featherstone: he was not proud of her, and she was only useful to him.", "그는 그녀를 자랑스러워하지 않았고, 그녀는 그에게 그저 쓸모 있는 존재일 뿐이었다."], ["To be anxious about a soul that is always snapping at you must be left to the saints of the earth; and Mary was not one of them.", "언제나 당신에게 으르렁대는 영혼을 두고 근심하는 일은 이 세상의 성인들에게나 맡겨 둘 일이다. 그리고 메리는 그런 성인이 아니었다."], ["She had never returned him a harsh word, and had waited on him faithfully: that was her utmost.", "그녀는 그에게 한 번도 모진 말을 돌려준 적이 없었고, 충실히 시중을 들었다. 그것이 그녀가 할 수 있는 최선이었다."], ["Old Featherstone himself was not in the least anxious about his soul, and had declined to see Mr. Tucker on the subject.", "늙은 페더스톤 자신은 제 영혼에 대해 조금도 근심하지 않았고, 그 문제로 터커 씨를 만나는 것도 거절했다."]], [["To-night he had not snapped, and for the first hour or two he lay remarkably still, until at last Mary heard him rattling his bunch of keys against the tin box which he always kept in the bed beside him.", "오늘 밤 그는 으르렁대지 않았고, 처음 한두 시간 동안은 놀라울 만큼 가만히 누워 있었다. 그러다 마침내 메리는 그가 늘 침대 곁에 두는 양철 상자에 열쇠 꾸러미를 절그럭거리며 부딪치는 소리를 들었다."], ["About three o’clock he said, with remarkable distinctness, “Missy, come here!”", "세 시쯤 그가 놀랄 만큼 또렷한 목소리로 말했다. “아가씨, 이리 와!”"]], [["Mary obeyed, and found that he had already drawn the tin box from under the clothes, though he usually asked to have this done for him; and he had selected the key.", "메리는 시키는 대로 했고, 그가 평소에는 누군가에게 시켜 꺼내 달라고 하던 양철 상자를 벌써 이불 밑에서 끌어내 놓았으며, 게다가 열쇠까지 골라 둔 것을 보았다."], ["He now unlocked the box, and, drawing from it another key, looked straight at her with eyes that seemed to have recovered all their sharpness and said, “How many of ’em are in the house?”", "그는 이제 상자를 열고 그 안에서 또 다른 열쇠를 꺼내더니, 본래의 날카로움을 모두 되찾은 듯한 눈으로 그녀를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. “이 집에 그것들이 몇이나 있느냐?”"]], [["“You mean of your own relations, sir,”", "“어르신의 친척분들을 말씀하시는 거지요.”"], ["said Mary, well used to the old man’s way of speech.", "노인의 말투에 익숙한 메리가 말했다."], ["He nodded slightly and she went on.", "그가 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이자 그녀는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Mr. Jonah Featherstone and young Cranch are sleeping here.”", "“조나 페더스톤 씨와 젊은 크랜치가 여기서 자고 있어요.”"]], [["“Oh ay, they stick, do they?", "“오 그래, 그것들이 들러붙어 있단 말이지?"], ["and the rest—they come every day, I’ll warrant—Solomon and Jane, and all the young uns?", "그리고 나머지는—날마다 오겠지, 내 장담하건대—솔로몬과 제인, 그리고 어린것들까지 죄다?"], ["They come peeping, and counting and casting up?”", "기웃거리며 와서 세어 보고 따져 보겠지?”"]], [["“Not all of them every day. Mr.", "“다들 매일 오는 건 아니에요."], ["Solomon and Mrs. Waule are here every day, and the others come often.”", "솔로몬 씨와 와울 부인은 매일 오시고, 다른 분들은 자주 오세요.”"]], [["The old man listened with a grimace while she spoke, and then said, relaxing his face, “The more fools they.", "노인은 그녀가 말하는 동안 얼굴을 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 듣더니, 이윽고 표정을 누그러뜨리며 말했다. “그것들 멍청하기도 하지."], ["You hearken, missy.", "잘 들어라, 아가씨."], ["It’s three o’clock in the morning, and I’ve got all my faculties as well as ever I had in my life.", "지금 새벽 세 시인데, 나는 평생 어느 때 못지않게 정신이 또렷하다."], ["I know all my property, and where the money’s put out, and everything.", "내 재산이 전부 어디에 있는지, 돈을 어디에 빌려줬는지, 그 모든 걸 다 알고 있어."], ["And I’ve made everything ready to change my mind, and do as I like at the last.", "그리고 나는 마음을 바꿔서 마지막에 내 뜻대로 할 수 있도록 만반의 준비를 해 놓았다."], ["Do you hear, missy?", "알아듣겠느냐, 아가씨?"], ["I’ve got my faculties.”", "나는 정신이 멀쩡해.”"]], [["“Well, sir?”", "“네, 어르신?”"], ["said Mary, quietly.", "메리가 조용히 말했다."]], [["He now lowered his tone with an air of deeper cunning.", "그는 이제 한층 음흉한 기색을 띠며 목소리를 낮추었다."], ["“I’ve made two wills, and I’m going to burn one.", "“나는 유언장을 두 개 만들어 놓았고, 그중 하나를 태워 버릴 참이다."], ["Now you do as I tell you.", "자, 내가 시키는 대로 해라."], ["This is the key of my iron chest, in the closet there.", "이건 저 벽장 안에 있는 내 쇠궤의 열쇠다."], ["You push well at the side of the brass plate at the top, till it goes like a bolt: then you can put the key in the front lock and turn it.", "맨 위에 있는 놋쇠 판의 옆쪽을 빗장처럼 미끄러질 때까지 잘 밀어라. 그러면 정면 자물쇠에 열쇠를 꽂아 돌릴 수 있다."], ["See and do that; and take out the topmost paper—Last Will and Testament—big printed.”", "잘 보고 그대로 해라. 그리고 맨 위에 있는 서류를 꺼내라—유언장이라고, 큼직하게 인쇄된 거다.”"]], [["“No, sir,”", "“아니요, 어르신.”"], ["said Mary, in a firm voice, “I cannot do that.”", "메리가 단호한 목소리로 말했다. “저는 그 일을 할 수 없어요.”"]], [["“Not do it? I tell you, you must,”", "“못 하겠다고?"], ["said the old man, his voice beginning to shake under the shock of this resistance.", "내가 말하잖느냐, 너는 해야 한다.” 노인의 목소리가 이 저항의 충격으로 떨리기 시작했다."]], [["“I cannot touch your iron chest or your will.", "“저는 어르신의 쇠궤도 유언장도 손댈 수 없어요."], ["I must refuse to do anything that might lay me open to suspicion.”", "저를 의심받게 할 만한 일은 무엇이든 거절하겠어요.”"]], [["“I tell you, I’m in my right mind.", "“내가 말하잖느냐, 나는 정신이 멀쩡하다."], ["Shan’t I do as I like at the last?", "마지막에 내 뜻대로 하지도 못한단 말이냐?"], ["I made two wills on purpose.", "일부러 유언장을 두 개나 만들었어."], ["Take the key, I say.”", "열쇠를 받아라, 어서.”"]], [["“No, sir, I will not,”", "“아니요, 어르신, 그러지 않겠어요.”"], ["said Mary, more resolutely still.", "메리가 더욱 단호하게 말했다."], ["Her repulsion was getting stronger.", "그녀의 거부감은 점점 강해지고 있었다."]], [["“I tell you, there’s no time to lose.”", "“말하잖느냐, 지체할 시간이 없다.”"]], [["“I cannot help that, sir.", "“그건 제가 어쩔 수 없어요, 어르신."], ["I will not let the close of your life soil the beginning of mine.", "어르신 인생의 끝자락이 제 인생의 시작을 더럽히게 두지 않겠어요."], ["I will not touch your iron chest or your will.”", "저는 어르신의 쇠궤도 유언장도 손대지 않겠어요.”"], ["She moved to a little distance from the bedside.", "그녀는 침대 곁에서 조금 떨어진 곳으로 물러섰다."]], [["The old man paused with a blank stare for a little while, holding the one key erect on the ring; then with an agitated jerk he began to work with his bony left hand at emptying the tin box before him.", "노인은 한동안 멍한 눈으로 말을 멈춘 채, 고리에 꿰인 열쇠 하나를 곧추세워 들고 있었다. 그러다 격하게 몸을 홱 움직이더니 앙상한 왼손으로 자기 앞의 양철 상자를 비우기 시작했다."]], [["“Missy,”", "“아가씨,”"], ["he began to say, hurriedly, “look here!", "그가 다급하게 말을 꺼냈다. “여기 봐라!"], ["take the money—the notes and gold—look here—take it—you shall have it all—do as I tell you.”", "이 돈을 가져라—지폐와 금화를—여기 봐라—가지라니까—전부 다 네 것으로 해 주마—내가 시키는 대로 해라.”"]], [["He made an effort to stretch out the key towards her as far as possible, and Mary again retreated.", "그는 가능한 한 멀리 그녀를 향해 열쇠를 내밀려고 안간힘을 썼고, 메리는 다시 물러섰다."]], [["“I will not touch your key or your money, sir.", "“저는 어르신의 열쇠도 돈도 손대지 않겠어요, 어르신."], ["Pray don’t ask me to do it again.", "제발 다시는 그런 부탁 하지 마세요."], ["If you do, I must go and call your brother.”", "그러시면 저는 가서 동생분을 불러야 해요.”"]], [["He let his hand fall, and for the first time in her life Mary saw old Peter Featherstone begin to cry childishly.", "그가 손을 떨어뜨렸고, 메리는 난생처음으로 늙은 피터 페더스톤이 어린아이처럼 울기 시작하는 것을 보았다."], ["She said, in as gentle a tone as she could command, “Pray put up your money, sir;”", "그녀는 자신이 낼 수 있는 한 부드러운 어조로 말했다. “제발 돈을 도로 넣어 두세요, 어르신.”"], ["and then went away to her seat by the fire, hoping this would help to convince him that it was useless to say more.", "그러고는 더 말해 봐야 소용없다는 것을 그가 납득하는 데 도움이 되기를 바라며 난롯가 자기 자리로 돌아갔다."], ["Presently he rallied and said eagerly—", "이윽고 그가 기운을 차리더니 간절하게 말했다."]], [["“Look here, then.", "“그럼 이거 봐라."], ["Call the young chap.", "그 젊은 녀석을 불러라."], ["Call Fred Vincy.”", "프레드 빈시를 불러.”"]], [["Mary’s heart began to beat more quickly.", "메리의 심장이 더 빠르게 뛰기 시작했다."], ["Various ideas rushed through her mind as to what the burning of a second will might imply.", "두 번째 유언장을 태운다는 것이 무엇을 의미할지 갖가지 생각이 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."], ["She had to make a difficult decision in a hurry.", "그녀는 급히 어려운 결정을 내려야 했다."]], [["“I will call him, if you will let me call Mr. Jonah and others with him.”", "“그 사람을 부르되, 조나 씨와 다른 분들도 함께 부르게 해 주신다면 부르겠어요.”"]], [["“Nobody else, I say.", "“다른 누구도 안 돼, 내 말했다."], ["The young chap.", "그 젊은 녀석만이다."], ["I shall do as I like.”", "나는 내 뜻대로 할 거야.”"]], [["“Wait till broad daylight, sir, when every one is stirring.", "“환한 대낮이 될 때까지, 다들 일어나 움직일 때까지 기다리세요, 어르신."], ["Or let me call Simmons now, to go and fetch the lawyer?", "아니면 지금 시먼스를 불러 변호사를 데려오게 할까요?"], ["He can be here in less than two hours.”", "두 시간도 안 되어 여기 올 수 있어요.”"]], [["“Lawyer?", "“변호사?"], ["What do I want with the lawyer?", "변호사가 내게 무슨 소용이냐?"], ["Nobody shall know—I say, nobody shall know.", "아무도 알아선 안 돼—말하잖느냐, 아무도 몰라야 한다."], ["I shall do as I like.”", "나는 내 뜻대로 할 거야.”"]], [["“Let me call some one else, sir,”", "“다른 누구라도 부르게 해 주세요, 어르신.” 메리가 달래듯 말했다."], ["said Mary, persuasively.", "그녀는 자신의 처지가 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["She did not like her position—alone with the old man, who seemed to show a strange flaring of nervous energy which enabled him to speak again and again without falling into his usual cough; yet she desired not to push unnecessarily the contradiction which agitated him.", "노인과 단둘이 있는데, 그는 평소의 기침에 빠지지 않고 거듭거듭 말을 할 수 있게 하는 어떤 기이한 신경의 불꽃을 내보이는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 동요시키는 반발을 공연히 부추기고 싶지 않았다."], ["“Let me, pray, call some one else.”", "“제발, 다른 누구라도 부르게 해 주세요.”"]], [["“You let me alone, I say.", "“나를 가만히 내버려 두래도."], ["Look here, missy.", "이거 봐라, 아가씨."], ["Take the money.", "이 돈을 가져라."], ["You’ll never have the chance again.", "다시는 이런 기회가 없을 거다."], ["It’s pretty nigh two hundred—there’s more in the box, and nobody knows how much there was.", "거의 이백 파운드는 될 거야—상자 안에 더 있고, 얼마나 들었는지는 아무도 몰라."], ["Take it and do as I tell you.”", "이걸 가지고 내가 시키는 대로 해라.”"]], [["Mary, standing by the fire, saw its red light falling on the old man, propped up on his pillows and bed-rest, with his bony hand holding out the key, and the money lying on the quilt before him.", "난롯가에 서 있던 메리는 붉은 불빛이, 베개와 등받이에 몸을 기댄 채 앙상한 손으로 열쇠를 내밀고 있는 노인과 그의 앞 누비이불 위에 놓인 돈 위로 떨어지는 것을 보았다."], ["She never forgot that vision of a man wanting to do as he liked at the last.", "마지막에 제 뜻대로 하고 싶어 하는 한 인간의 그 광경을 그녀는 결코 잊지 못했다."], ["But the way in which he had put the offer of the money urged her to speak with harder resolution than ever.", "그러나 그가 돈을 내미는 그 방식이 도리어 그녀를 그 어느 때보다 단호하게 말하도록 몰아붙였다."]], [["“It is of no use, sir.", "“소용없어요, 어르신."], ["I will not do it.", "저는 하지 않겠어요."], ["Put up your money.", "돈을 도로 넣으세요."], ["I will not touch your money.", "저는 어르신 돈에 손대지 않겠어요."], ["I will do anything else I can to comfort you; but I will not touch your keys or your money.”", "어르신을 위로하기 위해서라면 제가 할 수 있는 다른 일은 무엇이든 하겠어요. 하지만 어르신의 열쇠도 돈도 손대지 않겠어요.”"]], [["“Anything else—anything else!”", "“다른 일이라—다른 일이라니!”"], ["said old Featherstone, with hoarse rage, which, as if in a nightmare, tried to be loud, and yet was only just audible.", "늙은 페더스톤이 쉰 목소리로 격노하여 외쳤는데, 마치 악몽 속에서처럼 크게 소리치려 했으나 가까스로 들릴 정도에 그쳤다."], ["“I want nothing else.", "“나는 다른 건 아무것도 바라지 않아."], ["You come here—you come here.”", "이리 와—이리 오래도.”"]], [["Mary approached him cautiously, knowing him too well.", "메리는 그를 너무도 잘 알기에 조심스럽게 다가갔다."], ["She saw him dropping his keys and trying to grasp his stick, while he looked at her like an aged hyena, the muscles of his face getting distorted with the effort of his hand.", "그가 열쇠를 떨어뜨리고 지팡이를 움켜쥐려 애쓰는 것이 보였고, 손에 힘을 주느라 얼굴 근육이 일그러진 채 늙은 하이에나처럼 그녀를 노려보았다."], ["She paused at a safe distance.", "그녀는 안전한 거리에서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Let me give you some cordial,”", "“강심제를 좀 드릴게요.”"], ["she said, quietly, “and try to compose yourself.", "그녀가 조용히 말했다. “그리고 마음을 진정시키려 해 보세요."], ["You will perhaps go to sleep.", "그러면 잠이 드실지도 몰라요."], ["And to-morrow by daylight you can do as you like.”", "그리고 내일 날이 밝으면 뜻대로 하시면 돼요.”"]], [["He lifted the stick, in spite of her being beyond his reach, and threw it with a hard effort which was but impotence.", "그는 그녀가 자기 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있는데도 지팡이를 들어, 무력하기 짝이 없는 안간힘으로 그것을 던졌다."], ["It fell, slipping over the foot of the bed.", "지팡이는 침대 발치를 넘어 미끄러지며 떨어졌다."], ["Mary let it lie, and retreated to her chair by the fire.", "메리는 그것을 그대로 둔 채 난롯가 의자로 물러났다."], ["By-and-by she would go to him with the cordial.", "잠시 후에 강심제를 가지고 그에게 갈 작정이었다."], ["Fatigue would make him passive.", "피로가 그를 잠잠하게 만들 터였다."], ["It was getting towards the chillest moment of the morning, the fire had got low, and she could see through the chink between the moreen window-curtains the light whitened by the blind.", "아침 중 가장 쌀쌀한 순간이 다가오고 있었고, 불은 사그라들었으며, 모린 천 창문 휘장 사이의 틈으로 블라인드에 의해 희뿌예진 빛이 보였다."], ["Having put some wood on the fire and thrown a shawl over her, she sat down, hoping that Mr. Featherstone might now fall asleep.", "불에 장작을 좀 얹고 어깨에 숄을 두른 다음, 그녀는 이제 페더스톤 씨가 잠들기를 바라며 자리에 앉았다."], ["If she went near him the irritation might be kept up.", "그에게 가까이 가면 짜증이 계속될지도 몰랐다."], ["He had said nothing after throwing the stick, but she had seen him taking his keys again and laying his right hand on the money.", "지팡이를 던진 후로 그는 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 그녀는 그가 다시 열쇠를 집어 들고 오른손을 돈 위에 얹는 것을 보았다."], ["He did not put it up, however, and she thought that he was dropping off to sleep.", "그러나 그는 돈을 도로 넣지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 잠에 빠져들고 있다고 생각했다."]], [["But Mary herself began to be more agitated by the remembrance of what she had gone through, than she had been by the reality—questioning those acts of hers which had come imperatively and excluded all question in the critical moment.", "하지만 메리 자신은, 실제 겪을 때보다도 자신이 겪어 낸 일을 떠올리며 더 동요하기 시작했다. 그 위급한 순간에 어쩔 수 없이 닥쳐와 일체의 망설임을 배제했던 자신의 그 행동들을 이제 와 곱씹는 것이었다."]], [["Presently the dry wood sent out a flame which illuminated every crevice, and Mary saw that the old man was lying quietly with his head turned a little on one side.", "이윽고 마른 장작이 불길을 피워 올려 온갖 틈새를 환히 비추었고, 메리는 노인이 고개를 한쪽으로 약간 돌린 채 조용히 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She went towards him with inaudible steps, and thought that his face looked strangely motionless; but the next moment the movement of the flame communicating itself to all objects made her uncertain.", "그녀는 발소리를 죽이며 그에게 다가갔고, 그의 얼굴이 기이할 만큼 미동도 없어 보인다고 생각했다. 그러나 다음 순간 모든 사물에 옮겨 붙은 불길의 일렁임 때문에 확신할 수 없었다."], ["The violent beating of her heart rendered her perceptions so doubtful that even when she touched him and listened for his breathing, she could not trust her conclusions.", "심장이 격렬하게 뛰는 탓에 지각이 어찌나 미덥지 못했던지, 그를 만지고 숨소리에 귀를 기울일 때조차 그녀는 자신의 판단을 믿을 수 없었다."], ["She went to the window and gently propped aside the curtain and blind, so that the still light of the sky fell on the bed.", "그녀는 창가로 가서 휘장과 블라인드를 살며시 밀어젖혀, 고요한 하늘빛이 침대 위로 떨어지게 했다."]], [["The next moment she ran to the bell and rang it energetically.", "다음 순간 그녀는 종으로 달려가 힘차게 울렸다."], ["In a very little while there was no longer any doubt that Peter Featherstone was dead, with his right hand clasping the keys, and his left hand lying on the heap of notes and gold.", "아주 잠깐 사이에 피터 페더스톤이 죽었다는 사실에는 더 이상 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그의 오른손은 열쇠를 움켜쥐고 있었고, 왼손은 지폐와 금화 무더기 위에 놓여 있었다."]], [["# BOOK IV. THREE LOVE PROBLEMS. CHAPTER XXXIV.", "# BOOK IV. THREE LOVE PROBLEMS. CHAPTER XXXIV."]], [["“1st Gent.", "“첫째 신사."], ["Such men as this are feathers, chips, and straws, Carry no weight, no force.", "이런 자들은 깃털이요, 나뭇조각이며, 지푸라기라,\n무게도 지니지 않고, 힘도 없다네."]], [["2d Gent. But levity Is causal too, and makes the sum of weight.", "둘째 신사."], ["For power finds its place in lack of power; Advance is cession, and the driven ship May run aground because the helmsman’s thought Lacked force to balance opposites.”", "허나 가벼움 또한\n하나의 원인이 되어, 무게의 총합을 이루나니."]], [["It was on a morning of May that Peter Featherstone was buried. In the prosaic neighborhood of Middlemarch, May was not always warm and sunny, and on this particular morning a chill wind was blowing the blossoms from the surrounding gardens on to the green mounds of Lowick churchyard. Swiftly moving clouds only now and then allowed a gleam to light up any object, whether ugly or beautiful, that happened to stand within its golden shower. In the churchyard the objects were remarkably various, for there was a little country crowd waiting to see the funeral. The news had spread that it was to be a “big burying;” the old gentleman had left written directions about everything and meant to have a funeral “beyond his betters.” This was true; for old Featherstone had not been a Harpagon whose passions had all been devoured by the ever-lean and ever-hungry passion of saving, and who would drive a bargain with his undertaker beforehand. He loved money, but he also loved to spend it in gratifying his peculiar tastes, and perhaps he loved it best of all as a means of making others feel his power more or less uncomfortably. If any one will here contend that there must have been traits of goodness in old Featherstone, I will not presume to deny this; but I must observe that goodness is of a modest nature, easily discouraged, and when much privacy, elbowed in early life by unabashed vices, is apt to retire into extreme privacy, so that it is more easily believed in by those who construct a selfish old gentleman theoretically, than by those who form the narrower judgments based on his personal acquaintance. In any case, he had been bent on having a handsome funeral, and on having persons “bid” to it who would rather have stayed at home. He had even desired that female relatives should follow him to the grave, and poor sister Martha had taken a difficult journey for this purpose from the Chalky Flats. She and Jane would have been altogether cheered (in a tearful manner) by this sign that a brother who disliked seeing them while he was living had been prospectively fond of their presence when he should have become a testator, if the sign had not been made equivocal by being extended to Mrs. Vincy, whose expense in handsome crape seemed to imply the most presumptuous hopes, aggravated by a bloom of complexion which told pretty plainly that she was not a blood-relation, but of that generally objectionable class called wife’s kin.", "권능은 권능의 결핍 속에서 제자리를 찾고,\n나아감이란 곧 물러섬이라, 떠밀려 가는 배도"]], [["We are all of us imaginative in some form or other, for images are the brood of desire; and poor old Featherstone, who laughed much at the way in which others cajoled themselves, did not escape the fellowship of illusion. In writing the programme for his burial he certainly did not make clear to himself that his pleasure in the little drama of which it formed a part was confined to anticipation. In chuckling over the vexations he could inflict by the rigid clutch of his dead hand, he inevitably mingled his consciousness with that livid stagnant presence, and so far as he was preoccupied with a future life, it was with one of gratification inside his coffin. Thus old Featherstone was imaginative, after his fashion.", "좌초할 수 있으니, 키잡이의 생각이\n상반된 것을 저울질할 힘이 모자란 까닭이라.”"]], [["However, the three mourning-coaches were filled according to the written orders of the deceased.", "피터 페더스톤이 묻힌 것은 어느 오월의 아침이었다. 따분한 미들마치 인근에서 오월이 늘 따뜻하고 화창한 것은 아니어서, 바로 그날 아침에는 차가운 바람이 둘레의 정원에서 꽃잎을 불어와 로윅 교회 묘지의 푸른 봉분 위로 흩뿌렸다."], ["There were pall-bearers on horseback, with the richest scarfs and hatbands, and even the under-bearers had trappings of woe which were of a good well-priced quality.", "빠르게 흘러가는 구름은 이따금씩만 한 줄기 빛을 허락하여, 그 황금빛 소나기 속에 우연히 서 있게 된 것이라면 추하든 아름답든 가리지 않고 비추었다. 교회 묘지에 서 있는 것들은 유난히 다채로웠으니, 장례식을 구경하려고 기다리는 시골 사람들이 자그마하게 무리를 이루고 있었기 때문이다."], ["The black procession, when dismounted, looked the larger for the smallness of the churchyard; the heavy human faces and the black draperies shivering in the wind seemed to tell of a world strangely incongruous with the lightly dropping blossoms and the gleams of sunshine on the daisies.", "그것이 “성대한 장례식”이 될 것이라는 소문이 퍼져 있었다. 노신사가 모든 일에 대해 글로 지시를 남겨 놓았고, “제 윗사람들을 능가하는”"], ["The clergyman who met the procession was Mr.", "장례식을 치를 작정이었던 것이다. 사실이 그러했다."], ["Cadwallader—also according to the request of Peter Featherstone, prompted as usual by peculiar reasons.", "늙은 페더스톤은 저축이라는 늘 야위고 늘 굶주린 열정에 다른 모든 열정을 잡아먹힌 인색한 수전노 같은 자가 아니었고, 미리 장의사와 흥정을 벌일 위인도 아니었다."], ["Having a contempt for curates, whom he always called understrappers, he was resolved to be buried by a beneficed clergyman. Mr.", "그는 돈을 사랑했으나, 자신의 별난 취향을 만족시키는 데 돈을 쓰는 것도 사랑했고, 어쩌면 무엇보다도 남들이 자신의 권력을 다소 불편하게 느끼도록 만드는 수단으로서 돈을 가장 사랑했는지도 모른다."], ["Casaubon was out of the question, not merely because he declined duty of this sort, but because Featherstone had an especial dislike to him as the rector of his own parish, who had a lien on the land in the shape of tithe, also as the deliverer of morning sermons, which the old man, being in his pew and not at all sleepy, had been obliged to sit through with an inward snarl.", "여기서 누군가가 늙은 페더스톤에게도 선한 면모가 분명히 있었을 것이라고 주장한다면, 나는 감히 그것을 부정하지는 않겠다. 다만 선이란 본성이 겸손하여 쉽게 기가 꺾이는 것이고, 삶의 초기에 뻔뻔한 악덕들에게 밀려나 많은 은밀함 속에 갇히면 극단적인 은밀함 속으로 물러나 버리기 쉽다는 점은 짚어 두어야겠다."], ["He had an objection to a parson stuck up above his head preaching to him. But his relations with Mr.", "그리하여 그 선은 이론적으로 이기적인 노신사를 구성해 보는 이들에게는 더 쉽게 믿어지지만, 그와 개인적으로 알고 지내며 더 좁은 판단을 내리는 이들에게는 그렇지 못하다."], ["Cadwallader had been of a different kind: the trout-stream which ran through Mr.", "어쨌거나 그는 번듯한 장례식을 치르겠다고, 그리고 차라리 집에 있고 싶어 할 사람들을 그 자리에 “초대해” 부르겠다고 마음먹고 있었다."], ["Casaubon’s land took its course through Featherstone’s also, so that Mr.", "그는 심지어 여자 친척들이 자기를 무덤까지 따라오기를 바랐으니, 가엾은 누이 마사는 이 목적을 위해 초크 플랫에서 힘든 여정을 거쳐 왔다."], ["Cadwallader was a parson who had had to ask a favor instead of preaching.", "그녀와 제인은, 살아 있는 동안에는 그들을 보기 싫어하던 오라비가 유언자가 되었을 때를 미리 내다보며 그들의 참석을 흐뭇하게 여겼다는 이 표시에 (눈물을 머금은 채) 적잖이 위안을 받았을 것이다. 다만 그 표시가 빈시 부인에게까지 확대됨으로써 애매하게 흐려지지 않았더라면 말이다."], ["Moreover, he was one of the high gentry living four miles away from Lowick, and was thus exalted to an equal sky with the sheriff of the county and other dignities vaguely regarded as necessary to the system of things.", "빈시 부인이 번듯한 검정 크레이프에 들인 비용은 더없이 주제넘은 기대를 암시하는 듯했고, 게다가 그 화사한 혈색은 그녀가 혈연이 아니라 흔히 못마땅하게 여겨지는 처가붙이 부류임을 꽤 분명하게 말해 주어 그 인상을 한층 더 짙게 했다. 우리는 누구나 어떤 형태로든 상상력을 지니고 있으니, 이미지란 욕망이 낳은 새끼이기 때문이다."], ["There would be a satisfaction in being buried by Mr.", "그리고 남들이 스스로를 속이는 방식을 두고 그토록 비웃던 가엾은 늙은 페더스톤도 환상의 동지 관계에서 벗어나지는 못했다. 자기 장례식의 식순을 적으면서, 그는 그것이 일부를 이루는 그 작은 연극에서 느끼는 즐거움이 오로지 예상에만 국한된다는 사실을 분명히 깨닫지 못한 것이 확실하다."], ["Cadwallader, whose very name offered a fine opportunity for pronouncing wrongly if you liked.", "죽은 손의 뻣뻣한 움켜쥠으로 가할 수 있는 괴로움들을 떠올리며 낄낄대는 동안, 그는 어쩔 수 없이 자신의 의식을 그 시퍼렇고 정체된 존재와 뒤섞었고, 그가 내세에 마음을 쓰는 한 그것은 자기 관 속에서 느끼는 만족의 내세였다. 이렇게 늙은 페더스톤은 그 나름의 방식으로 상상력이 풍부했다."]], [["This distinction conferred on the Rector of Tipton and Freshitt was the reason why Mrs. Cadwallader made one of the group that watched old Featherstone’s funeral from an upper window of the manor. She was not fond of visiting that house, but she liked, as she said, to see collections of strange animals such as there would be at this funeral; and she had persuaded Sir James and the young Lady Chettam to drive the Rector and herself to Lowick in order that the visit might be altogether pleasant. “I will go anywhere with you, Mrs. Cadwallader,” Celia had said; “but I don’t like funerals.”", "어쨌거나 세 대의 상여 마차는 고인이 글로 남긴 지시에 따라 채워졌다. 말을 탄 운구자들은 더없이 화려한 띠와 모자 띠를 둘렀고, 아래쪽 운구꾼들조차 값나가는 좋은 품질의 애도용 장식을 갖추고 있었다. 그 검은 행렬은 말에서 내리자 교회 묘지가 작은 탓에 한층 커 보였다. 바람 속에 떨리는 묵직한 사람들의 얼굴과 검은 휘장들은, 가볍게 떨어지는 꽃잎이며 데이지 위에 반짝이는 햇살과는 묘하게 어울리지 않는 어떤 세계를 일러 주는 듯했다. 행렬을 맞이한 성직자는 캐드월러더 씨였는데, 이 역시 늘 그렇듯 별난 이유에서 비롯된 피터 페더스톤의 요청에 따른 것이었다. 그는 보좌 신부를 경멸하여 늘 “심부름꾼”이라 불렀던 터라, 성직록을 받는 정식 성직자의 손에 묻히기로 작정했던 것이다. 캐소본 씨는 애초에 고려 대상이 아니었으니, 그가 이런 종류의 직무를 거절했기 때문만이 아니라, 페더스톤이 자기 교구의 교구장으로서 십일조라는 형태로 토지에 대한 권리를 쥐고 있는 그를 특별히 싫어했기 때문이다. 또한 캐소본 씨는 아침 설교를 하는 사람이기도 했으니, 노인은 자기 자리에 앉아 조금도 졸리지 않은 채로 속으로 으르렁대며 그 설교를 끝까지 들어야만 했던 것이다. 그는 목사가 자기 머리 위에 우뚝 서서 설교하는 것을 못마땅하게 여겼다. 그러나 캐드월러더 씨와의 관계는 다른 종류였다. 캐소본 씨의 땅을 가로지르는 송어 개울이 페더스톤의 땅으로도 흘러갔으니, 그리하여 캐드월러더 씨는 설교를 하기는커녕 부탁을 해야 했던 목사였던 것이다. 게다가 그는 로윅에서 사 마일 떨어진 곳에 사는 상류 신사 중 한 사람이었으므로, 카운티의 주장관이나 사물의 질서에 막연히 필요하다고 여겨지는 다른 고관들과 동등한 하늘로 떠받들어졌다. 캐드월러더 씨의 손에 묻히는 데에는 만족스러운 구석이 있었으니, 그 이름 자체가 마음만 먹으면 틀리게 발음하기 좋은 멋진 기회를 제공했기 때문이다."]], [["“Oh, my dear, when you have a clergyman in your family you must accommodate your tastes: I did that very early.", "팁턴과 프레시트의 교구장에게 부여된 이 영예야말로 캐드월러더 부인이 영지 저택의 위층 창가에서 늙은 페더스톤의 장례식을 지켜보는 무리에 끼게 된 이유였다."], ["When I married Humphrey I made up my mind to like sermons, and I set out by liking the end very much.", "그녀는 그 집을 방문하는 것을 좋아하지 않았으나, 본인 말마따나 이번 장례식에 모일 법한 별난 동물들의 진열을 구경하는 것은 즐겨 했다."], ["That soon spread to the middle and the beginning, because I couldn’t have the end without them.”", "그래서 그녀는 제임스 경과 젊은 채텀 부인을 설득해, 목사와 자신을 로윅까지 마차로 태워다 달라고 하여 그 방문이 전적으로 즐거운 나들이가 되게 했다."]], [["“No, to be sure not,”", "“부인이 가시는 곳이라면 어디든 따라가겠어요, 캐드월러더 부인.”"], ["said the Dowager Lady Chettam, with stately emphasis.", "실리아가 말했다. “하지만 저는 장례식이 싫어요.”"]], [["The upper window from which the funeral could be well seen was in the room occupied by Mr. Casaubon when he had been forbidden to work; but he had resumed nearly his habitual style of life now in spite of warnings and prescriptions, and after politely welcoming Mrs. Cadwallader had slipped again into the library to chew a cud of erudite mistake about Cush and Mizraim.", "“오, 얘야, 집안에 성직자가 있으면 취향을 맞춰 가야 한단다. 나는 아주 일찍부터 그렇게 했지. 험프리와 결혼했을 때 나는 설교를 좋아하기로 마음먹었고, 우선 끝부분을 무척 좋아하는 데서 출발했어. 그러다 보니 그게 곧 중간으로, 또 처음으로 번져 갔단다. 처음과 중간이 없으면 끝도 있을 수 없으니까 말이야.” “그렇고말고요, 정말 그렇지요.” 노부인 채텀이 위엄 있게 힘주어 말했다."]], [["But for her visitors Dorothea too might have been shut up in the library, and would not have witnessed this scene of old Featherstone’s funeral, which, aloof as it seemed to be from the tenor of her life, always afterwards came back to her at the touch of certain sensitive points in memory, just as the vision of St.", "장례식을 잘 볼 수 있는 그 위층 창문은 캐소본 씨가 일을 금지당했을 때 머물던 방에 있었다. 그러나 그는 이제 경고와 처방에도 아랑곳없이 거의 평소의 생활 방식을 다시 되찾았던 터라, 캐드월러더 부인을 정중히 맞이한 뒤 다시 서재로 슬그머니 빠져나가 구시와 미스라임에 관한 박학한 오류라는 새김질거리를 되씹고 있었다."], ["Peter’s at Rome was inwoven with moods of despondency.", "손님들이 오지 않았더라면 도러시아 역시 서재에 갇혀 있었을 테고, 늙은 페더스톤의 장례식이라는 이 광경을 목격하지 못했을 것이다. 그 광경은 그녀의 삶의 흐름과는 동떨어진 것처럼 보였지만, 훗날 기억의 어떤 예민한 지점이 건드려질 때면 늘 그녀에게 되돌아왔으니, 마치 로마의 성 베드로 대성당의 환영이 낙담의 기분과 한데 짜여 있었던 것과도 같았다."], ["Scenes which make vital changes in our neighbors’ lot are but the background of our own, yet, like a particular aspect of the fields and trees, they become associated for us with the epochs of our own history, and make a part of that unity which lies in the selection of our keenest consciousness.", "우리 이웃들의 운명에 결정적인 변화를 일으키는 장면들은 우리 자신에게는 한낱 배경에 지나지 않는다. 그러나 들판과 나무들의 어떤 특정한 모습처럼, 그것들은 우리에게 우리 자신의 역사의 한 시기와 결부되어, 가장 예리한 의식의 선택 속에 깃든 그 통일성의 일부를 이루게 된다."]], [["The dream-like association of something alien and ill-understood with the deepest secrets of her experience seemed to mirror that sense of loneliness which was due to the very ardor of Dorothea’s nature.", "낯설고 잘 이해되지 않는 무언가가 그녀 경험의 가장 깊은 비밀과 꿈결처럼 결부되는 그 느낌은, 도러시아의 천성이 지닌 바로 그 열정에서 비롯된 고독감을 비추는 듯했다."], ["The country gentry of old time lived in a rarefied social air: dotted apart on their stations up the mountain they looked down with imperfect discrimination on the belts of thicker life below.", "옛 시절의 시골 신사 계층은 희박한 사교의 공기 속에 살았으니, 산자락 위 저마다의 위치에 점점이 떨어져 자리 잡은 채, 아래쪽의 더 빽빽한 삶의 무리들을 불완전한 분별력으로 내려다보았다."], ["And Dorothea was not at ease in the perspective and chilliness of that height.", "그리고 도러시아는 그 높이의 조망과 냉기 속에서 마음이 편치 않았다."]], [["“I shall not look any more,”", "“이제 더는 안 볼래요.”"], ["said Celia, after the train had entered the church, placing herself a little behind her husband’s elbow so that she could slyly touch his coat with her cheek.", "행렬이 교회 안으로 들어간 뒤 실리아가 말하며, 남편의 팔꿈치 뒤쪽으로 조금 물러서서 그의 외투에 뺨을 슬쩍 댈 수 있게 자리를 잡았다."], ["“I dare say Dodo likes it: she is fond of melancholy things and ugly people.”", "“도도는 좋아하겠죠. 언니는 우울한 것이랑 못생긴 사람들을 좋아하니까요.”"]], [["“I am fond of knowing something about the people I live among,”", "“나는 내가 더불어 사는 사람들에 대해 뭔가 알게 되는 걸 좋아해.”"], ["said Dorothea, who had been watching everything with the interest of a monk on his holiday tour.", "휴일을 맞은 수도사 같은 흥미로 모든 것을 지켜보던 도러시아가 말했다."], ["“It seems to me we know nothing of our neighbors, unless they are cottagers.", "“우리는 이웃이 오두막에 사는 소작인이 아니고서는 그들에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것 같아."], ["One is constantly wondering what sort of lives other people lead, and how they take things.", "다른 사람들이 어떤 삶을 살고, 일을 어떻게 받아들이는지 끊임없이 궁금해지잖니."], ["I am quite obliged to Mrs. Cadwallader for coming and calling me out of the library.”", "캐드월러더 부인이 오셔서 나를 서재 밖으로 불러내 주셔서 정말 감사해요.”"]], [["“Quite right to feel obliged to me,”", "“나에게 감사하는 게 당연하지요.” 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs.", "“댁의 부유한 로윅 농부들은 물소나 들소만큼이나 신기한 구경거리예요."], ["Cadwallader.", "게다가 교회에서는 그들을 절반도 못 볼 게 분명하고요."], ["“Your rich Lowick farmers are as curious as any buffaloes or bisons, and I dare say you don’t half see them at church.", "그들은 댁의 삼촌이나 제임스 경의 소작인들과는 아주 달라요. 괴물들이지요."], ["They are quite different from your uncle’s tenants or Sir James’s—monsters—farmers without landlords—one can’t tell how to class them.”", "지주가 없는 농부들이라니. 어떻게 분류해야 할지 알 수가 없어요.”"]], [["“Most of these followers are not Lowick people,”", "“이 조문객들 대부분은 로윅 사람이 아닙니다.”"], ["said Sir James; “I suppose they are legatees from a distance, or from Middlemarch.", "제임스 경이 말했다. “먼 곳에서 왔거나, 미들마치에서 온 유산 상속인들이겠지요."], ["Lovegood tells me the old fellow has left a good deal of money as well as land.”", "러브굿 말로는 그 노인이 토지뿐 아니라 상당한 돈도 남겼다더군요.”"]], [["“Think of that now!", "“생각 좀 해 보세요!"], ["when so many younger sons can’t dine at their own expense,”", "차남 이하의 아들들 가운데 제 돈으로 끼니도 못 챙기는 이가 그토록 많은데 말이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“Ah,” turning round at the sound of the opening door, “here is Mr.", "그러더니 문이 열리는 소리에 돌아서며, “아, 브룩 씨가 오셨네요."], ["Brooke. I felt that we were incomplete before, and here is the explanation.", "아까 우리 모임이 어딘가 미흡하다 싶더니, 여기 그 까닭이 있었군요."], ["You are come to see this odd funeral, of course?”", "물론 이 별난 장례식을 구경하러 오신 거겠지요?”"]], [["“No, I came to look after Casaubon—to see how he goes on, you know.", "“아니요, 캐소본을 살피러 왔습니다. 그 사람이 어떻게 지내는지 보려고요."], ["And to bring a little news—a little news, my dear,”", "그리고 자그마한 소식도 좀 전하려고요."], ["said Mr.", "자그마한 소식 말이오, 얘야.”"], ["Brooke, nodding at Dorothea as she came towards him.", "브룩 씨가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 도러시아에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “서재를 들여다보니, 캐소본이 책에 파묻혀 있더구나."], ["“I looked into the library, and I saw Casaubon over his books.", "그래서 그러면 안 된다고 했지. ‘이래선 절대 안 돼요."], ["I told him it wouldn’t do: I said, ‘This will never do, you know: think of your wife, Casaubon.’ And he promised me to come up.", "아내를 생각하시오, 캐소본.’ 이렇게 말했지. 그러니까 그가 올라오겠다고 약속하더구나."], ["I didn’t tell him my news: I said, he must come up.”", "소식은 말하지 않았어. 그저 올라오셔야 한다고만 했지.”"]], [["“Ah, now they are coming out of church,”", "“아, 이제 사람들이 교회에서 나오는군요.”"], ["Mrs. Cadwallader exclaimed.", "캐드월러더 부인이 소리쳤다."], ["“Dear me, what a wonderfully mixed set!", "“어머나, 정말 기막히게 뒤섞인 무리네요!"], ["Mr. Lydgate as doctor, I suppose.", "저 사람이 의사 리드게이트 씨겠지요."], ["But that is really a good looking woman, and the fair young man must be her son.", "그런데 저 여자는 정말 인물이 곱고, 저 잘생긴 젊은이는 그 여자의 아들이 분명해요."], ["Who are they, Sir James, do you know?”", "저들은 누구인가요, 제임스 경, 아세요?”"]], [["“I see Vincy, the Mayor of Middlemarch; they are probably his wife and son,”", "“미들마치 시장인 빈시가 보이는군요. 아마 그의 아내와 아들일 겁니다.”"], ["said Sir James, looking interrogatively at Mr. Brooke, who nodded and said—", "제임스 경이 브룩 씨를 미심쩍은 듯 바라보며 말하자, 브룩 씨가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, a very decent family—a very good fellow is Vincy; a credit to the manufacturing interest.", "“그렇지요, 아주 점잖은 집안이에요. 빈시는 아주 좋은 사람이고요."], ["You have seen him at my house, you know.”", "제조업계의 자랑이지요. 우리 집에서 보신 적 있을 거요.”"]], [["“Ah, yes: one of your secret committee,”", "“아, 그럼요. 댁의 비밀 위원회 사람 중 하나죠.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader, provokingly.", "캐드월러더 부인이 약을 올리듯 말했다."]], [["“A coursing fellow, though,”", "“하긴 사냥개로 토끼를 쫓아다니는 작자이긴 합니다만.”"], ["said Sir James, with a fox-hunter’s disgust.", "제임스 경이 여우 사냥꾼다운 혐오감을 담아 말했다."]], [["“And one of those who suck the life out of the wretched handloom weavers in Tipton and Freshitt.", "“게다가 팁턴과 프레시트의 가엾은 수직기 직조공들의 생기를 쪽쪽 빨아먹는 자들 중 하나죠."], ["That is how his family look so fair and sleek,”", "그 집안 식구들이 저렇게 곱고 윤기 나 보이는 게 다 그 덕분이고요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“Those dark, purple-faced people are an excellent foil.", "“저 거무튀튀하고 검붉은 얼굴의 사람들이 아주 훌륭한 들러리 노릇을 해 주네요."], ["Dear me, they are like a set of jugs!", "어머나, 저들은 무슨 물주전자들 같아요!"], ["Do look at Humphrey: one might fancy him an ugly archangel towering above them in his white surplice.”", "험프리 좀 보세요. 흰 중백의를 입고 그들 위로 우뚝 솟은 모습이 못생긴 대천사라 해도 믿겠어요.”"]], [["“It’s a solemn thing, though, a funeral,” said Mr.", "“그래도 장례식은 엄숙한 일이잖소.”"], ["Brooke, “if you take it in that light, you know.”", "브룩 씨가 말했다. “그런 관점에서 보면 말이오.”"]], [["“But I am not taking it in that light.", "“하지만 나는 그런 관점으로 보고 있지 않은걸요."], ["I can’t wear my solemnity too often, else it will go to rags.", "나는 내 엄숙함을 너무 자주 걸칠 수가 없어요. 안 그러면 다 해져 버릴 테니까요."], ["It was time the old man died, and none of these people are sorry.”", "그 노인은 죽을 때가 됐고, 이 사람들 중 슬퍼하는 이는 하나도 없으니까요.”"]], [["“How piteous!” said Dorothea.", "“어쩌면 그리 가엾을까요!”"], ["“This funeral seems to me the most dismal thing I ever saw.", "도러시아가 말했다. “이 장례식은 제가 본 것 중 가장 음울한 것 같아요."], ["It is a blot on the morning.", "아침에 드리운 얼룩 같아요."], ["I cannot bear to think that any one should die and leave no love behind.”", "누군가 죽으면서 아무 사랑도 남기지 못한다는 걸 차마 생각할 수가 없어요.”"]], [["She was going to say more, but she saw her husband enter and seat himself a little in the background.", "그녀는 더 말하려 했으나, 남편이 들어와 조금 뒤편에 자리를 잡고 앉는 것을 보았다."], ["The difference his presence made to her was not always a happy one: she felt that he often inwardly objected to her speech.", "그의 존재가 그녀에게 가져오는 차이는 늘 행복한 것만은 아니었다. 그녀는 그가 자기 말에 속으로 못마땅해할 때가 많다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Positively,”", "“어머, 정말이지,”"], ["exclaimed Mrs.", "캐드월러더 부인이 외쳤다."], ["Cadwallader, “there is a new face come out from behind that broad man queerer than any of them: a little round head with bulging eyes—a sort of frog-face—do look.", "“저 떡 벌어진 남자 뒤에서 그들 누구보다도 더 괴상한 새 얼굴이 나왔어요. 자그맣고 둥근 머리에 눈이 불룩 튀어나온, 일종의 개구리 얼굴 같은."], ["He must be of another blood, I think.”", "좀 보세요. 저 사람은 다른 핏줄인 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Let me see!”", "“어디 봐요!”"], ["said Celia, with awakened curiosity, standing behind Mrs.", "호기심이 일어난 실리아가 캐드월러더 부인 뒤에 서서 그녀의 머리 너머로 몸을 내밀며 말했다. “어머, 정말 별난 얼굴이네요!”"], ["Cadwallader and leaning forward over her head. “Oh, what an odd face!”", "그러더니 또 다른 종류의 놀란 표정으로 재빨리 바꾸며 덧붙였다."], ["Then with a quick change to another sort of surprised expression, she added, “Why, Dodo, you never told me that Mr. Ladislaw was come again!”", "“어머, 도도, 래디슬로 씨가 다시 오셨다는 말은 한 번도 안 했잖아요!”"]], [["Dorothea felt a shock of alarm: every one noticed her sudden paleness as she looked up immediately at her uncle, while Mr.", "도러시아는 깜짝 놀라 충격을 받았다."], ["Casaubon looked at her.", "그녀가 곧장 삼촌을 올려다보는 사이 캐소본 씨는 그녀를 바라보았고, 모두가 그녀의 갑작스러운 창백함을 알아차렸다."]], [["“He came with me, you know; he is my guest—puts up with me at the Grange,”", "“그 청년은 나와 함께 왔단다."], ["said Mr.", "내 손님이지."], ["Brooke, in his easiest tone, nodding at Dorothea, as if the announcement were just what she might have expected.", "그레인지에 묵고 있어.” 브룩 씨가 더없이 편안한 어조로, 마치 그 소식이 그녀가 충분히 예상할 만한 것이라는 듯 도러시아에게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“And we have brought the picture at the top of the carriage.", "“게다가 그림을 마차 지붕에 싣고 왔다네. 자네가 이 깜짝 선물을 좋아할 줄 알았지, 캐소본."], ["I knew you would be pleased with the surprise, Casaubon.", "거기 자네가 꼭 살아 있는 모습 그대로 그려졌어. 아퀴나스로 말이지."], ["There you are to the very life—as Aquinas, you know.", "더없이 어울리는 소재야. 그리고 젊은 래디슬로가 그것에 대해 이야기하는 걸 듣게 될 거요."], ["Quite the right sort of thing.", "그 친구는 놀랄 만큼 말을 잘하거든. 이것저것 짚어 가면서 말이오."], ["And you will hear young Ladislaw talk about it.", "미술이며 그런 종류의 것을 죄다 알고 있어. 좋은 말동무이고 말이야."], ["He talks uncommonly well—points out this, that, and the other—knows art and everything of that kind—companionable, you know—is up with you in any track—what I’ve been wanting a long while.”", "어떤 화제로든 자네와 척척 통하지. 내가 오래전부터 바라던 그런 사람이라네.”"]], [["Mr. Casaubon bowed with cold politeness, mastering his irritation, but only so far as to be silent.", "캐소본 씨는 짜증을 다스리며 차가운 정중함으로 고개를 숙였으나, 그 다스림은 침묵을 지키는 정도에 그쳤다."], ["He remembered Will’s letter quite as well as Dorothea did; he had noticed that it was not among the letters which had been reserved for him on his recovery, and secretly concluding that Dorothea had sent word to Will not to come to Lowick, he had shrunk with proud sensitiveness from ever recurring to the subject.", "그는 도러시아 못지않게 윌의 편지를 또렷이 기억하고 있었다. 그 편지가 회복 후 자신에게 따로 챙겨진 편지들 가운데 없다는 것을 그는 알아차렸고, 도러시아가 윌에게 로윅으로 오지 말라는 전갈을 보냈으리라고 속으로 단정한 터라, 자존심 강한 예민함으로 그 화제를 다시 꺼내기를 줄곧 꺼려 왔다."], ["He now inferred that she had asked her uncle to invite Will to the Grange; and she felt it impossible at that moment to enter into any explanation.", "이제 그는 그녀가 삼촌에게 부탁해 윌을 그레인지로 초대하게 한 것이라고 추측했다. 그리고 그녀는 그 순간 어떤 설명도 시작하기가 불가능하다고 느꼈다."]], [["Mrs. Cadwallader’s eyes, diverted from the churchyard, saw a good deal of dumb show which was not so intelligible to her as she could have desired, and could not repress the question, “Who is Mr.", "교회 묘지에서 시선을 돌린 캐드월러더 부인의 눈에는 적잖은 무언극이 들어왔으나, 그녀가 바라는 만큼 알아볼 만한 것은 아니었다."], ["Ladislaw?”", "그녀는 “래디슬로 씨가 누구죠?”라는 질문을 억누를 수 없었다."]], [["“A young relative of Mr. Casaubon’s,”", "“캐소본 씨의 젊은 친척입니다.”"], ["said Sir James, promptly.", "제임스 경이 얼른 대답했다."], ["His good-nature often made him quick and clear-seeing in personal matters, and he had divined from Dorothea’s glance at her husband that there was some alarm in her mind.", "그는 사람 좋은 성품 덕분에 종종 개인사에 관해서는 재빠르고 명민하게 알아차렸으니, 도러시아가 남편을 흘낏 바라본 데서 그녀의 마음에 어떤 불안이 있음을 간파한 것이다."]], [["“A very nice young fellow—Casaubon has done everything for him,”", "“아주 괜찮은 청년이지요. 캐소본이 그를 위해 온갖 일을 다 해 주었답니다.”"], ["explained Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 설명했다."], ["“He repays your expense in him, Casaubon,”", "“그가 자네가 그에게 들인 비용을 갚아 주고 있다네, 캐소본.”"], ["he went on, nodding encouragingly.", "그가 격려하듯 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 이었다."], ["“I hope he will stay with me a long while and we shall make something of my documents.", "“그가 나와 함께 오래 머물러서 내 자료들로 뭔가를 만들어 냈으면 좋겠어. 나한테는 아이디어며 사실이 넘쳐 나는데 말이오, 보아하니 그가 바로 그것들을 꼴을 갖춰 정리해 줄 사람이라네."], ["I have plenty of ideas and facts, you know, and I can see he is just the man to put them into shape—remembers what the right quotations are, omne tulit punctum, and that sort of thing—gives subjects a kind of turn.", "알맞은 인용구가 뭔지 기억해 내고, 라틴어로 ‘만장일치를 얻었도다’ 운운하는 식이지. 화제에 일종의 묘미를 더해 준다네."], ["I invited him some time ago when you were ill, Casaubon; Dorothea said you couldn’t have anybody in the house, you know, and she asked me to write.”", "자네가 아플 때 얼마 전에 그를 초대했어, 캐소본. 도러시아가 자네 집에는 아무도 들일 수 없다고 해서, 나더러 편지를 쓰라고 부탁했거든.”"]], [["Poor Dorothea felt that every word of her uncle’s was about as pleasant as a grain of sand in the eye to Mr.", "* 만장일치를 얻었도다: 라틴어 omne tulit punctum."], ["Casaubon.", "호라티우스의 시구로, ‘유익함과 즐거움을 두루 갖춰 모든 표를 얻었다’는 뜻."], ["It would be altogether unfitting now to explain that she had not wished her uncle to invite Will Ladislaw.", "가엾은 도러시아는 삼촌의 말 한마디 한마디가 캐소본 씨에게는 눈에 들어간 모래알만큼이나 거슬릴 것이라고 느꼈다. 자신이 삼촌에게 윌 래디슬로를 초대해 달라고 바란 적이 없다고 지금 해명하는 것은 도무지 어울리지 않을 일이었다."], ["She could not in the least make clear to herself the reasons for her husband’s dislike to his presence—a dislike painfully impressed on her by the scene in the library; but she felt the unbecomingness of saying anything that might convey a notion of it to others. Mr.", "남편이 그의 존재를 싫어하는 까닭을 그녀는 스스로도 조금도 분명히 알 수 없었다."], ["Casaubon, indeed, had not thoroughly represented those mixed reasons to himself; irritated feeling with him, as with all of us, seeking rather for justification than for self-knowledge.", "서재에서의 그 장면을 통해 그 혐오감이 그녀에게 고통스럽게 각인되긴 했지만 말이다. 그러나 그녀는 그 혐오감을 다른 이들에게 짐작하게 할 만한 말을 하는 것이 온당치 못하다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["But he wished to repress outward signs, and only Dorothea could discern the changes in her husband’s face before he observed with more of dignified bending and sing-song than usual—", "사실 캐소본 씨 자신도 그 뒤섞인 이유들을 스스로에게 철저히 표명해 본 적이 없었으니, 그에게도 우리 모두와 마찬가지로 짜증 난 감정이란 자기 인식보다는 자기 정당화를 구하는 법이었다. 하지만 그는 겉으로 드러나는 기색을 억누르고자 했고, 평소보다 더 위엄 있게 몸을 굽히고 가락 섞인 어조로 다음과 같이 말하기 전에, 남편 얼굴의 변화를 알아챌 수 있는 사람은 오직 도러시아뿐이었다."]], [["“You are exceedingly hospitable, my dear sir; and I owe you acknowledgments for exercising your hospitality towards a relative of mine.”", "“참으로 후한 대접이시오, 친애하는 어른. 제 친척에게 환대를 베풀어 주신 데 대해 감사의 말씀을 드려야겠습니다.”"]], [["The funeral was ended now, and the churchyard was being cleared.", "장례식은 이제 끝났고, 교회 묘지에서는 사람들이 흩어지고 있었다."]], [["“Now you can see him, Mrs. Cadwallader,”", "“이제 그분을 볼 수 있어요, 캐드월러더 부인.”"], ["said Celia. “He is just like a miniature of Mr.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["Casaubon’s aunt that hangs in Dorothea’s boudoir—quite nice-looking.”", "“도러시아의 규방에 걸린 캐소본 씨의 고모 세밀화하고 꼭 닮았어요. 아주 잘생겼고요.”"]], [["“A very pretty sprig,”", "“참 어여쁜 잔가지로군요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader, dryly.", "캐드월러더 부인이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["“What is your nephew to be, Mr. Casaubon?”", "“댁의 조카는 장차 무엇이 될 사람인가요, 캐소본 씨?”"]], [["“Pardon me, he is not my nephew.", "“실례지만, 그는 제 조카가 아닙니다."], ["He is my cousin.”", "제 사촌이지요.”"]], [["“Well, you know,”", "“음, 그러니까 말입니다.”"], ["interposed Mr.", "브룩 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Brooke, “he is trying his wings.", "“그는 지금 날개를 펼쳐 보는 중이에요."], ["He is just the sort of young fellow to rise.", "출세할 만한 그런 청년이지요."], ["I should be glad to give him an opportunity.", "나는 기꺼이 그에게 기회를 주고 싶어요."], ["He would make a good secretary, now, like Hobbes, Milton, Swift—that sort of man.”", "이를테면 홉스나 밀턴, 스위프트 같은, 그런 부류의 사람처럼 훌륭한 비서가 될 거요.”"]], [["“I understand,”", "“알겠어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“One who can write speeches.”", "“연설문을 써 줄 수 있는 사람 말이지요.”"]], [["“I’ll fetch him in now, eh, Casaubon?”", "“이제 그를 데려올까, 응, 캐소본?”"], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“He wouldn’t come in till I had announced him, you know.", "“내가 알리기 전에는 들어오려 하지 않더군. 그러고 나서 우리 다 같이 내려가서 그림을 보세."], ["And we’ll go down and look at the picture.", "거기 자네가 살아 있는 듯 그려져 있어. 손가락을 책장에 얹은, 깊고 미묘한 사색가의 모습이지."], ["There you are to the life: a deep subtle sort of thinker with his fore-finger on the page, while Saint Bonaventure or somebody else, rather fat and florid, is looking up at the Trinity.", "그동안 성 보나벤투라인지 누군지가, 다소 살집 있고 혈색 좋은 모습으로 삼위일체를 올려다보고 있고 말이야. 모든 것이 상징적이라네."], ["Everything is symbolical, you know—the higher style of art: I like that up to a certain point, but not too far—it’s rather straining to keep up with, you know.", "더 높은 양식의 미술이지. 나도 어느 정도까지는 그런 걸 좋아하지만, 너무 지나치면 안 돼."], ["But you are at home in that, Casaubon.", "따라가기가 다소 버겁거든, 알다시피."], ["And your painter’s flesh is good—solidity, transparency, everything of that sort.", "하지만 자네는 그 분야에 정통하지 않은가, 캐소본. 그리고 자네 화가의 살결 표현이 훌륭해."], ["I went into that a great deal at one time.", "견고함이며 투명함이며, 그런 종류의 것이 다 있어. 한때 나도 그 분야를 꽤 깊이 파고들었지."], ["However, I’ll go and fetch Ladislaw.”", "어쨌든, 내가 가서 래디슬로를 데려오겠네.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXV.", "# CHAPTER XXXV."]], [["“Non, je ne comprends pas de plus charmant plaisir Que de voir d’héritiers une troupe affligée Le maintien interdit, et la mine allongée, Lire un long testament où pales, étonnés On leur laisse un bonsoir avec un pied de nez.", "아니, 슬픔에 잠긴 상속자 무리가 망연자실한 자세로 얼굴을 길게 늘어뜨린 채 긴 유언장을 읽으며, 창백하고 놀란 얼굴로 조롱과 함께 작별 인사나 받는 꼴을 보는 것보다 더 매력적인 즐거움을 나는 알지 못하노라."], ["Pour voir au naturel leur tristesse profonde Je reviendrais, je crois, exprès de l’autre monde.”", "그들의 깊은 슬픔을 있는 그대로 보기 위해서라면 나는 일부러 저세상에서라도 돌아오리라."], ["—REGNARD: Le Légataire Universel.", "—레냐르, 『만물의 상속자』"]], [["When the animals entered the Ark in pairs, one may imagine that allied species made much private remark on each other, and were tempted to think that so many forms feeding on the same store of fodder were eminently superfluous, as tending to diminish the rations.", "짐승들이 짝을 지어 방주로 들어갔을 때, 가까운 종끼리는 서로를 두고 속으로 이런저런 품평을 하면서, 같은 사료 더미를 먹어 치우는 형태가 그토록 많다는 것은 결국 각자의 몫을 줄이는 데나 기여할 뿐이니 지극히 군더더기라고 여기고 싶은 유혹에 빠졌으리라고 상상해 볼 수 있다."], ["(I fear the part played by the vultures on that occasion would be too painful for art to represent, those birds being disadvantageously naked about the gullet, and apparently without rites and ceremonies.)", "(그때 독수리들이 맡았던 역할은 예술로 표현하기에는 너무 고통스러우리라 싶다. 그 새들은 모이주머니 근처가 보기 흉하게 벌거벗었고, 또 보아하니 아무런 격식이나 의식도 갖추지 않은 듯하니 말이다.)"]], [["The same sort of temptation befell the Christian Carnivora who formed Peter Featherstone’s funeral procession; most of them having their minds bent on a limited store which each would have liked to get the most of.", "피터 페더스톤의 장례 행렬을 이룬 기독교도 육식 동물들에게도 똑같은 종류의 유혹이 닥쳤다. 그들 대부분은 한정된 재산에 마음이 쏠려 있었고, 저마다 그 가운데 가장 큰 몫을 차지하고 싶어 했다."], ["The long-recognized blood-relations and connections by marriage made already a goodly number, which, multiplied by possibilities, presented a fine range for jealous conjecture and pathetic hopefulness.", "오래전부터 인정받아 온 혈족과 혼인으로 맺어진 인척만 해도 이미 상당한 수였고, 거기에 갖가지 가능성을 곱하니 시샘 어린 추측과 애처로운 기대가 펼쳐질 멋진 무대가 마련되었다."], ["Jealousy of the Vincys had created a fellowship in hostility among all persons of the Featherstone blood, so that in the absence of any decided indication that one of themselves was to have more than the rest, the dread lest that long-legged Fred Vincy should have the land was necessarily dominant, though it left abundant feeling and leisure for vaguer jealousies, such as were entertained towards Mary Garth.", "빈시 일가에 대한 시샘은 페더스톤 가문의 피를 이은 모든 사람들 사이에 적개심의 동지애를 만들어 냈으므로, 그들 가운데 누군가가 나머지보다 더 많이 받으리라는 확실한 조짐이 없는 한, 그 다리 긴 프레드 빈시가 땅을 차지할지 모른다는 두려움이 자연히 지배적이었다. 그러면서도 메리 가스에게 품은 것 같은 막연한 시샘에 쏟을 감정과 여유는 넉넉히 남아 있었다."], ["Solomon found time to reflect that Jonah was undeserving, and Jonah to abuse Solomon as greedy; Jane, the elder sister, held that Martha’s children ought not to expect so much as the young Waules; and Martha, more lax on the subject of primogeniture, was sorry to think that Jane was so “having.”", "솔로몬은 조나가 자격이 없다고 곱씹을 짬을 냈고, 조나는 솔로몬을 탐욕스럽다고 헐뜯었다. 큰누이 제인은 마사의 자식들이 어린 와울 가의 아이들만큼 기대해서는 안 된다고 여겼고, 장자 상속 문제에 좀 더 너그러운 마사는 제인이 그토록 \"욕심 사납다\"는 사실에 마음이 쓰라렸다."], ["These nearest of kin were naturally impressed with the unreasonableness of expectations in cousins and second cousins, and used their arithmetic in reckoning the large sums that small legacies might mount to, if there were too many of them.", "이 가장 가까운 친족들은 당연히 사촌이며 육촌이 품는 기대가 터무니없다고 느꼈고, 작은 유산도 너무 여럿이면 큰 금액으로 불어날 수 있다는 셈을 셈하는 데 산수를 동원했다."], ["Two cousins were present to hear the will, and a second cousin besides Mr. Trumbull.", "유언장 낭독을 들으러 온 사촌은 둘이었고, 트럼불 씨 말고도 육촌이 하나 있었다."], ["This second cousin was a Middlemarch mercer of polite manners and superfluous aspirates.", "이 육촌은 미들마치의 포목상으로, 예의 바른 태도와 필요 이상으로 자주 새어 나오는 발음을 지닌 사람이었다."], ["The two cousins were elderly men from Brassing, one of them conscious of claims on the score of inconvenient expense sustained by him in presents of oysters and other eatables to his rich cousin Peter; the other entirely saturnine, leaning his hands and chin on a stick, and conscious of claims based on no narrow performance but on merit generally: both blameless citizens of Brassing, who wished that Jonah Featherstone did not live there.", "두 사촌은 브래싱에서 온 노인들이었는데, 한 사람은 부유한 사촌 피터에게 굴이며 그 밖의 먹을거리를 선물하느라 적잖은 비용을 치렀다는 명목으로 권리를 주장할 만하다고 여겼고, 다른 한 사람은 더없이 음울한 표정으로 두 손과 턱을 지팡이에 괸 채, 어떤 좁은 공적이 아니라 전반적인 인덕에 근거한 권리를 주장할 만하다고 여겼다. 둘 다 흠잡을 데 없는 브래싱 시민으로서, 조나 페더스톤이 그곳에 살지 않았으면 좋겠다고 바라는 사람들이었다."], ["The wit of a family is usually best received among strangers.", "한 집안에서 익살이란 대개 낯선 사람들 사이에서 가장 환대받는 법이다."]], [["“Why, Trumbull himself is pretty sure of five hundred—that you may depend,—I shouldn’t wonder if my brother promised him,”", "\"아니, 트럼불 본인도 오백 파운드는 거의 확실해. 그건 믿어도 돼."], ["said Solomon, musing aloud with his sisters, the evening before the funeral.", "형님이 약속했다 해도 난 놀라지 않을 거야.\" 장례식 전날 저녁, 솔로몬이 누이들과 함께 소리 내어 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."]], [["“Dear, dear!”", "\"저런, 저런!\""], ["said poor sister Martha, whose imagination of hundreds had been habitually narrowed to the amount of her unpaid rent.", "가엾은 누이 마사가 말했다. 그녀의 수백 파운드에 대한 상상은 늘 밀린 집세 액수에 맞춰 좁아져 있었다."]], [["But in the morning all the ordinary currents of conjecture were disturbed by the presence of a strange mourner who had plashed among them as if from the moon. This was the stranger described by Mrs.", "그러나 아침이 되자 추측의 모든 통상적인 흐름이, 마치 달에서 떨어진 듯 그들 사이에 첨벙 끼어든 낯선 조문객의 출현으로 어지러워졌다."], ["Cadwallader as frog-faced: a man perhaps about two or three and thirty, whose prominent eyes, thin-lipped, downward-curved mouth, and hair sleekly brushed away from a forehead that sank suddenly above the ridge of the eyebrows, certainly gave his face a batrachian unchangeableness of expression.", "이 사람이 바로 캐드월러더 부인이 개구리 얼굴이라고 묘사했던 그 낯선 이였다. 서른두셋쯤 되어 보이는 남자로, 툭 튀어나온 눈, 입술이 얇고 아래로 처진 입, 눈썹 능선 위에서 갑자기 꺼져 들어간 이마에서 매끈하게 빗어 넘긴 머리카락이, 그의 얼굴에 분명 양서류처럼 변치 않는 표정을 부여하고 있었다."], ["Here, clearly, was a new legatee; else why was he bidden as a mourner?", "여기 분명히 새로운 수유자가 있었다. 그렇지 않다면 어째서 그가 조문객으로 초대되었겠는가?"], ["Here were new possibilities, raising a new uncertainty, which almost checked remark in the mourning-coaches.", "여기 새로운 가능성이 있어 새로운 불확실성을 불러일으켰고, 그 탓에 장례 마차 안에서는 말이 거의 끊겼다."], ["We are all humiliated by the sudden discovery of a fact which has existed very comfortably and perhaps been staring at us in private while we have been making up our world entirely without it.", "우리는 어떤 사실이 — 아주 편안하게 존재해 왔고 어쩌면 우리가 그것을 완전히 빼놓은 채 세계를 꾸려 가는 동안 은밀히 우리를 빤히 쳐다보고 있었을지도 모를 사실이 — 느닷없이 발견되면 누구나 굴욕을 느낀다."], ["No one had seen this questionable stranger before except Mary Garth, and she knew nothing more of him than that he had twice been to Stone Court when Mr. Featherstone was down-stairs, and had sat alone with him for several hours.", "메리 가스를 제외하고는 아무도 이 수상쩍은 낯선 이를 전에 본 적이 없었는데, 그녀가 그에 대해 아는 것이라곤 페더스톤 씨가 아래층에 내려와 있을 때 그가 스톤 코트에 두 번 찾아와 몇 시간씩 단둘이 앉아 있었다는 사실뿐이었다."], ["She had found an opportunity of mentioning this to her father, and perhaps Caleb’s were the only eyes, except the lawyer’s, which examined the stranger with more of inquiry than of disgust or suspicion.", "그녀는 이 일을 아버지에게 말할 기회를 얻었고, 변호사를 빼면 그 낯선 이를 혐오나 의심보다 호기심에 가까운 눈으로 살피는 눈은 어쩌면 케일럽의 눈뿐이었을 것이다. 케일럽 가스는 기대가 적었고 욕심은 더 적었으므로, 자기 짐작이 맞는지 확인하는 데 흥미가 있었다."], ["Caleb Garth, having little expectation and less cupidity, was interested in the verification of his own guesses, and the calmness with which he half smilingly rubbed his chin and shot intelligent glances much as if he were valuing a tree, made a fine contrast with the alarm or scorn visible in other faces when the unknown mourner, whose name was understood to be Rigg, entered the wainscoted parlor and took his seat near the door to make part of the audience when the will should be read. Just then Mr.", "그가 차분하게 반쯤 미소 지으며 턱을 문지르고는, 마치 나무 한 그루의 값을 매기듯 영민한 눈길을 던지는 모습은, 리그라는 이름으로 알려진 그 낯선 조문객이 판벽을 두른 응접실로 들어와 유언장이 낭독될 때 청중의 한 사람이 되려고 문가에 자리를 잡았을 때 다른 얼굴들에 드러난 경악이나 경멸과 멋진 대조를 이루었다."], ["Solomon and Mr. Jonah were gone up-stairs with the lawyer to search for the will; and Mrs.", "바로 그때 솔로몬 씨와 조나 씨는 변호사와 함께 유언장을 찾으러 위층으로 올라가 있었다."], ["Waule, seeing two vacant seats between herself and Mr.", "와울 부인은 자신과 보스럽 트럼불 씨 사이에 빈자리가 둘 있는 것을 보고, 그 대단한 권위자 옆으로 옮겨 앉을 만한 배짱을 발휘했다."], ["Borthrop Trumbull, had the spirit to move next to that great authority, who was handling his watch-seals and trimming his outlines with a determination not to show anything so compromising to a man of ability as wonder or surprise.", "트럼불 씨는 시곗줄 장식을 만지작거리며 옷매무새를 가다듬고 있었는데, 능력 있는 사람에게 경이나 놀라움처럼 체면을 손상시키는 것은 결코 드러내지 않겠다는 결의가 엿보였다."]], [["“I suppose you know everything about what my poor brother’s done, Mr. Trumbull,” said Mrs. Waule, in the lowest of her woolly tones, while she turned her crape-shadowed bonnet towards Mr. Trumbull’s ear.", "\"제 가엾은 오라버니가 무슨 일을 해 놓으셨는지 트럼불 씨는 다 아실 것 같은데요.\" 와울 부인이 가장 솜털 같은 낮은 목소리로 말하며, 검은 상장(喪章)으로 그늘진 보닛을 트럼불 씨의 귀 쪽으로 돌렸다."]], [["“My good lady, whatever was told me was told in confidence,”", "\"부인, 제가 들은 것이 무엇이든 그것은 비밀로 들은 것입니다.\""], ["said the auctioneer, putting his hand up to screen that secret.", "경매인은 그 비밀을 가리려는 듯 손을 들어 올리며 말했다."]], [["“Them who’ve made sure of their good-luck may be disappointed yet,”", "\"제 행운을 확신했던 사람들이 끝내 실망하게 될지도 모르지요.\""], ["Mrs. Waule continued, finding some relief in this communication.", "와울 부인은 이 말 속에서 얼마간 위안을 찾으며 계속했다."]], [["“Hopes are often delusive,”", "\"기대란 종종 헛된 것이지요.\""], ["said Mr. Trumbull, still in confidence.", "트럼불 씨는 여전히 은밀한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Ah!” said Mrs.", "\"아!\""], ["Waule, looking across at the Vincys, and then moving back to the side of her sister Martha.", "와울 부인은 맞은편의 빈시 일가를 건너다보고는, 다시 누이 마사의 곁으로 돌아가며 말했다."]], [["“It’s wonderful how close poor Peter was,”", "\"가엾은 피터 오라버니가 얼마나 속을 안 보였는지, 참 놀라울 정도야.\""], ["she said, in the same undertones.", "그녀는 같은 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“We none of us know what he might have had on his mind.", "\"그이가 마음속에 무얼 품고 있었는지 우리는 아무도 몰라."], ["I only hope and trust he wasn’t a worse liver than we think of, Martha.”", "그저 오라버니가 우리 생각보다 더 못된 사람은 아니었기만을 바라고 또 빌 뿐이야, 마사.\""]], [["Poor Mrs.", "가엾은 크랜치 부인은 몸집이 우람했고, 천식 환자처럼 숨을 쉬었기에, 자기 말이 흠잡을 데 없고 일반적인 취지를 띠도록 신경 써야 할 까닭이 하나 더 있었다."], ["Cranch was bulky, and, breathing asthmatically, had the additional motive for making her remarks unexceptionable and giving them a general bearing, that even her whispers were loud and liable to sudden bursts like those of a deranged barrel-organ.", "그녀는 속삭임조차 컸으며, 고장 난 손풍금처럼 별안간 터져 나오기 십상이었기 때문이다."]], [["“I never was covetous, Jane,”", "\"난 한 번도 욕심을 부린 적이 없어, 제인.\""], ["she replied; “but I have six children and have buried three, and I didn’t marry into money.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 난 자식이 여섯이고 셋을 묻었으며, 돈 있는 집에 시집간 것도 아니야."], ["The eldest, that sits there, is but nineteen—so I leave you to guess.", "저기 앉은 맏이가 이제 겨우 열아홉이니, 나머지는 알아서 짐작해 봐."], ["And stock always short, and land most awkward.", "게다가 가축은 늘 모자라고 땅은 더없이 다루기 까다롭고."], ["But if ever I’ve begged and prayed; it’s been to God above; though where there’s one brother a bachelor and the other childless after twice marrying—anybody might think!”", "하지만 내가 무언가를 빌고 또 빌었다면 그건 위에 계신 하느님께였어. 그래도 한 형제는 독신이고 다른 형제는 두 번 결혼하고도 자식이 없으니, 누구라도 그런 생각을 할 거 아냐!\""]], [["Meanwhile, Mr. Vincy had glanced at the passive face of Mr.", "그동안 빈시 씨는 무표정한 리그 씨의 얼굴을 슬쩍 보고는 코담뱃갑을 꺼내 톡톡 두드렸지만, 판단을 아무리 또렷하게 해 준다 한들 이런 자리에는 어울리지 않는 호사라 여겨 열지 않은 채 도로 집어넣었다."], ["Rigg, and had taken out his snuff-box and tapped it, but had put it again unopened as an indulgence which, however clarifying to the judgment, was unsuited to the occasion. “I shouldn’t wonder if Featherstone had better feelings than any of us gave him credit for,”", "\"페더스톤이 우리 누구도 인정해 주지 않았던 좋은 감정을 품고 있었다 해도 난 놀라지 않겠어.\""], ["he observed, in the ear of his wife.", "그는 아내의 귀에 대고 말했다."], ["“This funeral shows a thought about everybody: it looks well when a man wants to be followed by his friends, and if they are humble, not to be ashamed of them.", "\"이 장례식을 보면 모든 사람을 두루 생각했다는 걸 알 수 있잖아. 사람이 죽어서 친구들의 배웅을 받고 싶어 하고, 그들이 비천하더라도 부끄러워하지 않는 건 보기 좋은 일이지."], ["I should be all the better pleased if he’d left lots of small legacies.", "그 사람이 자잘한 유산을 잔뜩 남겨 줬으면 난 더없이 기뻤을 거야."], ["They may be uncommonly useful to fellows in a small way.”", "형편이 변변찮은 친구들한테는 그런 게 무척 요긴할 수 있으니까.\""]], [["“Everything is as handsome as could be, crape and silk and everything,”", "\"모든 게 더할 나위 없이 훌륭해요. 상장이며 비단이며 죄다요.\""], ["said Mrs. Vincy, contentedly.", "빈시 부인이 흡족하게 말했다."]], [["But I am sorry to say that Fred was under some difficulty in repressing a laugh, which would have been more unsuitable than his father’s snuff-box.", "그러나 안타깝게도 프레드는 웃음을 억누르는 데 적잖이 애를 먹고 있었는데, 그 웃음은 아버지의 코담뱃갑보다 더 어울리지 않는 것이었으리라. 프레드는 조나 씨가 \"사생아\""], ["Fred had overheard Mr. Jonah suggesting something about a “love-child,”", "어쩌고 하는 말을 우연히 엿들었고, 그런 생각이 머릿속에 박힌 채 마침 맞은편에 있는 낯선 이의 얼굴을 보니 너무도 우스꽝스럽게 느껴졌다."], ["and with this thought in his mind, the stranger’s face, which happened to be opposite him, affected him too ludicrously.", "메리 가스는 그의 입술이 씰룩거리고 헛기침으로 얼버무리는 것을 보고 그의 곤경을 알아챘고, 자리를 바꿔 앉자고 청하여 슬기롭게 그를 구해 주었다."], ["Mary Garth, discerning his distress in the twitchings of his mouth, and his recourse to a cough, came cleverly to his rescue by asking him to change seats with her, so that he got into a shadowy corner.", "그리하여 그는 그늘진 구석으로 들어갈 수 있었다. 프레드는 리그를 비롯한 모든 사람에게 더없이 너그러운 심정이었다."], ["Fred was feeling as good-naturedly as possible towards everybody, including Rigg; and having some relenting towards all these people who were less lucky than he was aware of being himself, he would not for the world have behaved amiss; still, it was particularly easy to laugh.", "자신이 운 좋다는 것을 스스로 깨닫는 만큼 운이 덜한 이 모든 사람들에게 얼마간 동정을 품고 있었기에, 무슨 일이 있어도 무례하게 굴고 싶지 않았다. 그런데도 웃기는 유난히 쉬웠다."]], [["But the entrance of the lawyer and the two brothers drew every one’s attention. The lawyer was Mr.", "그러나 변호사와 두 형제가 들어서자 모두의 시선이 그쪽으로 쏠렸다."], ["Standish, and he had come to Stone Court this morning believing that he knew thoroughly well who would be pleased and who disappointed before the day was over.", "변호사는 스탠디시 씨였는데, 그는 그날이 저물기 전에 누가 기뻐하고 누가 실망할지 자기가 속속들이 알고 있다고 믿으며 이날 아침 스톤 코트에 왔다. 그가 낭독하리라 예상한 유언장은 페더스톤 씨를 위해 작성한 세 통 가운데 마지막 것이었다."], ["The will he expected to read was the last of three which he had drawn up for Mr. Featherstone.", "스탠디시 씨는 태도를 바꾸는 사람이 아니었다."], ["Mr. Standish was not a man who varied his manners: he behaved with the same deep-voiced, off-hand civility to everybody, as if he saw no difference in them, and talked chiefly of the hay-crop, which would be “very fine, by God!”", "그는 모든 사람에게 똑같이 굵은 목소리로 무뚝뚝하면서도 정중하게 대했는데, 마치 그들 사이에 아무런 차이도 보이지 않는다는 투였고, 주로 건초 작황 이야기를 했다."], ["of the last bulletins concerning the King, and of the Duke of Clarence, who was a sailor every inch of him, and just the man to rule over an island like Britain.", "올 작황은 \"맙소사, 아주 좋을 거\"라느니, 국왕에 관한 최근 소식이라느니, 클래런스 공작은 뼛속까지 뱃사람이라 영국 같은 섬나라를 다스리기에 꼭 알맞은 인물이라느니 하는 식이었다."]], [["Old Featherstone had often reflected as he sat looking at the fire that Standish would be surprised some day: it is true that if he had done as he liked at the last, and burnt the will drawn up by another lawyer, he would not have secured that minor end; still he had had his pleasure in ruminating on it.", "늙은 페더스톤은 불을 바라보고 앉아 언젠가 스탠디시가 깜짝 놀라게 되리라고 종종 생각하곤 했다. 사실 그가 마지막에 자기 뜻대로 하여 다른 변호사가 작성한 유언장을 태워 버렸더라면 그 사소한 목적은 이루지 못했을 테지만, 그래도 그는 그 일을 곱씹으며 즐거움을 누렸다."], ["And certainly Mr.", "그리고 과연 스탠디시 씨는 놀랐으나 조금도 언짢지는 않았다."], ["Standish was surprised, but not at all sorry; on the contrary, he rather enjoyed the zest of a little curiosity in his own mind, which the discovery of a second will added to the prospective amazement on the part of the Featherstone family.", "오히려 그는 두 번째 유언장의 발견이 페더스톤 가문이 앞으로 받을 충격에 더해 준, 자기 마음속 작은 호기심의 묘미를 꽤 즐기는 편이었다."]], [["As to the sentiments of Solomon and Jonah, they were held in utter suspense: it seemed to them that the old will would have a certain validity, and that there might be such an interlacement of poor Peter’s former and latter intentions as to create endless “lawing”", "솔로몬과 조나의 심정으로 말하자면, 그들은 완전한 불안 속에 매여 있었다. 그들에게는 옛 유언장이 일정한 효력을 지닐 것 같았고, 가엾은 피터의 예전 의도와 나중 의도가 뒤엉켜, 아무도 자기 몫을 차지하기 전에 끝없는 \"소송\"이 벌어질지도 모른다고 여겨졌다."], ["before anybody came by their own—an inconvenience which would have at least the advantage of going all round.", "그것은 적어도 모두에게 두루 돌아간다는 이점은 있는 골칫거리였다."], ["Hence the brothers showed a thoroughly neutral gravity as they re-entered with Mr.", "그래서 두 형제는 스탠디시 씨와 함께 다시 들어올 때 철저히 중립적인 근엄함을 보였다."], ["Standish; but Solomon took out his white handkerchief again with a sense that in any case there would be affecting passages, and crying at funerals, however dry, was customarily served up in lawn.", "그러나 솔로몬은 어쨌든 사람의 마음을 흔드는 대목이 있으리라는 생각에 다시 흰 손수건을 꺼냈으니, 장례식에서 우는 일은 아무리 눈물이 메말랐다 한들 으레 고운 아마포에 받쳐 차려 내는 법이었기 때문이다."]], [["Perhaps the person who felt the most throbbing excitement at this moment was Mary Garth, in the consciousness that it was she who had virtually determined the production of this second will, which might have momentous effects on the lot of some persons present.", "아마도 이 순간 가장 두근거리는 흥분을 느낀 사람은 메리 가스였을 것이다. 이 두 번째 유언장의 작성을 사실상 결정지은 사람이 바로 자신이라는 것을, 그리고 그것이 여기 모인 몇몇 사람의 운명에 중대한 영향을 미칠지도 모른다는 것을 의식하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["No soul except herself knew what had passed on that final night.", "그 마지막 밤에 무슨 일이 있었는지 그녀 말고는 어느 누구도 알지 못했다."]], [["“The will I hold in my hand,” said Mr.", "\"제 손에 들린 이 유언장은,\""], ["Standish, who, seated at the table in the middle of the room, took his time about everything, including the coughs with which he showed a disposition to clear his voice, “was drawn up by myself and executed by our deceased friend on the 9th of August, 1825.", "방 한가운데 탁자 앞에 앉은 스탠디시 씨가 말했다. 그는 목소리를 가다듬으려는 듯한 기침을 비롯하여 모든 일에 충분히 시간을 들이는 사람이었다."], ["But I find that there is a subsequent instrument hitherto unknown to me, bearing date the 20th of July, 1826, hardly a year later than the previous one.", "\"제가 직접 작성했고 우리의 고인 친구께서 1825년 8월 9일에 서명하신 것입니다. 그런데 보니 지금까지 제가 알지 못했던 그 후의 문서가 하나 더 있군요."], ["And there is farther, I see”—Mr.", "1826년 7월 20일 자로, 앞의 것보다 채 일 년도 지나지 않은 때입니다."], ["Standish was cautiously travelling over the document with his spectacles—“a codicil to this latter will, bearing date March 1, 1828.”", "그리고 더 보니\" — 스탠디시 씨는 안경을 쓰고 문서를 조심스럽게 훑어 나갔다 — \"이 나중 유언장에는 1828년 3월 1일 자 추가 조항이 붙어 있습니다.\""]], [["“Dear, dear!”", "\"저런, 저런!\""], ["said sister Martha, not meaning to be audible, but driven to some articulation under this pressure of dates.", "누이 마사가 말했다. 소리 내려던 것은 아니었으나, 날짜가 쏟아지는 압박에 무어라도 입 밖으로 내고 만 것이었다."]], [["“I shall begin by reading the earlier will,” continued Mr.", "\"먼저 앞의 유언장부터 읽겠습니다.\""], ["Standish, “since such, as appears by his not having destroyed the document, was the intention of the deceased.”", "스탠디시 씨가 계속했다. \"고인이 이 문서를 파기하지 않으신 것으로 보아 그것이 고인의 뜻인 듯하니 말입니다.\""]], [["The preamble was felt to be rather long, and several besides Solomon shook their heads pathetically, looking on the ground: all eyes avoided meeting other eyes, and were chiefly fixed either on the spots in the table-cloth or on Mr.", "서두는 꽤 길게 느껴졌고, 솔로몬 말고도 여럿이 땅을 내려다보며 애처롭게 고개를 가로저었다. 모든 눈이 다른 눈과 마주치기를 피했고, 주로 식탁보의 얼룩이나 스탠디시 씨의 벗어진 머리에 고정되었다."], ["Standish’s bald head; excepting Mary Garth’s.", "메리 가스의 눈만은 예외였다."], ["When all the rest were trying to look nowhere in particular, it was safe for her to look at them.", "다른 모든 이들이 어디에도 시선을 두지 않으려 애쓰는 동안, 그녀는 그들을 바라보아도 안전했다."], ["And at the sound of the first “give and bequeath” she could see all complexions changing subtly, as if some faint vibration were passing through them, save that of Mr.", "그리고 첫 번째 \"준다, 그리고 유증한다\"라는 소리가 들리자, 그녀는 리그 씨를 제외한 모두의 안색이, 마치 희미한 떨림이 그들을 관통하는 듯 미묘하게 변하는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Rigg. He sat in unaltered calm, and, in fact, the company, preoccupied with more important problems, and with the complication of listening to bequests which might or might not be revoked, had ceased to think of him.", "그는 변함없이 평온하게 앉아 있었고, 사실 더 중요한 문제와 철회될 수도 철회되지 않을 수도 있는 유증을 듣느라 복잡한 마음에 사로잡힌 일동은 그를 더 이상 생각하지 않게 되었다."], ["Fred blushed, and Mr. Vincy found it impossible to do without his snuff-box in his hand, though he kept it closed.", "프레드는 얼굴을 붉혔고, 빈시 씨는 닫아 둔 채로나마 코담뱃갑을 손에 쥐고 있지 않으면 견딜 수 없었다."]], [["The small bequests came first, and even the recollection that there was another will and that poor Peter might have thought better of it, could not quell the rising disgust and indignation.", "자잘한 유증이 먼저 나왔는데, 또 다른 유언장이 있고 가엾은 피터가 마음을 고쳐먹었을지도 모른다는 생각을 떠올린들 솟구치는 혐오와 분노를 가라앉힐 수는 없었다."], ["One likes to be done well by in every tense, past, present, and future.", "사람이란 과거든 현재든 미래든 모든 시제에서 후하게 대접받기를 바라는 법이다."], ["And here was Peter capable five years ago of leaving only two hundred apiece to his own brothers and sisters, and only a hundred apiece to his own nephews and nieces: the Garths were not mentioned, but Mrs.", "그런데 여기 피터는 오 년 전에 자기 친형제자매에게 고작 이백 파운드씩, 친조카들에게는 고작 백 파운드씩만 남길 수 있었던 사람이었다."], ["Vincy and Rosamond were each to have a hundred. Mr.", "가스 가는 언급조차 되지 않았으나, 빈시 부인과 로저먼드는 각각 백 파운드씩 받게 되어 있었다."], ["Trumbull was to have the gold-headed cane and fifty pounds; the other second cousins and the cousins present were each to have the like handsome sum, which, as the saturnine cousin observed, was a sort of legacy that left a man nowhere; and there was much more of such offensive dribbling in favor of persons not present—problematical, and, it was to be feared, low connections.", "트럼불 씨에게는 금장 손잡이 지팡이와 오십 파운드가 돌아갔고, 다른 육촌들과 자리에 함께한 사촌들에게도 저마다 그와 같은 후한 액수가 돌아갔는데, 음울한 사촌이 한마디 했듯이 그것은 사람을 이도 저도 아닌 처지에 빠뜨리는 종류의 유산이었다. 게다가 그 자리에 없는 사람들 — 정체가 모호한, 게다가 유감스럽게도 천한 인척일 게 분명한 사람들 — 에게 그런 식으로 찔끔찔끔 베푸는 대목이 훨씬 더 많았다."], ["Altogether, reckoning hastily, here were about three thousand disposed of.", "어림잡아 다 합치니 여기서 약 삼천 파운드가 처분되었다."], ["Where then had Peter meant the rest of the money to go—and where the land?", "그렇다면 피터는 나머지 돈을 어디로 보내려 했단 말인가 — 그리고 땅은 어디로?"], ["and what was revoked and what not revoked—and was the revocation for better or for worse?", "무엇이 철회되었고 무엇이 철회되지 않았는가 — 그리고 그 철회는 더 나은 쪽인가 더 나쁜 쪽인가?"], ["All emotion must be conditional, and might turn out to be the wrong thing.", "모든 감정은 조건부일 수밖에 없었고, 엉뚱한 방향으로 드러날 수도 있었다."], ["The men were strong enough to bear up and keep quiet under this confused suspense; some letting their lower lip fall, others pursing it up, according to the habit of their muscles.", "남자들은 이 어수선한 불안 속에서도 버티며 잠자코 있을 만큼 강했다. 어떤 이는 아랫입술을 늘어뜨리고, 어떤 이는 그것을 오므렸는데, 저마다 제 근육의 습관에 따른 것이었다."], ["But Jane and Martha sank under the rush of questions, and began to cry; poor Mrs.", "그러나 제인과 마사는 쏟아지는 의문의 격류에 무너져 울기 시작했다."], ["Cranch being half moved with the consolation of getting any hundreds at all without working for them, and half aware that her share was scanty; whereas Mrs.", "가엾은 크랜치 부인은 일하지 않고도 어쨌든 몇백 파운드를 얻는다는 위안에 반쯤 마음이 움직이면서도, 자기 몫이 변변찮다는 것을 반쯤 알아채고 있었다."], ["Waule’s mind was entirely flooded with the sense of being an own sister and getting little, while somebody else was to have much.", "반면 와울 부인의 마음은 자신이 친누이인데도 적게 받고 다른 누군가는 많이 받게 된다는 생각으로 온통 넘쳐흘렀다."], ["The general expectation now was that the “much”", "이제 모두는 그 \"많은\""], ["would fall to Fred Vincy, but the Vincys themselves were surprised when ten thousand pounds in specified investments were declared to be bequeathed to him:—was the land coming too?", "몫이 프레드 빈시에게 돌아가리라 예상했으나, 정작 명시된 투자 자산으로 만 파운드가 그에게 유증된다고 선언되자 빈시 일가 자신들조차 놀랐다."], ["Fred bit his lips: it was difficult to help smiling, and Mrs.", "땅도 함께 오는 것일까? 프레드는 입술을 깨물었다."], ["Vincy felt herself the happiest of women—possible revocation shrinking out of sight in this dazzling vision.", "미소를 참기가 어려웠고, 빈시 부인은 자신이 세상에서 가장 행복한 여인이라고 느꼈다. 이 눈부신 환상 속에서 철회의 가능성 따위는 시야에서 쪼그라들어 버렸다."]], [["There was still a residue of personal property as well as the land, but the whole was left to one person, and that person was—O possibilities!", "땅 말고도 동산이 아직 얼마간 남아 있었지만, 그 전부가 한 사람에게 남겨졌으니, 그 사람은 — 오, 가능성이여! 오, \"속을 안 보이던\""], ["O expectations founded on the favor of “close”", "노인의 호의에 기댄 기대여! 오, 죽어야 할 인간의 어리석음을 재는 잣대로는 표현이 속절없이 미끄러질 뿐인, 그 끝없는 호격이여!"], ["old gentlemen!", "— 그 잔여 재산의 수유자는 조슈아 리그였다."], ["O endless vocatives that would still leave expression slipping helpless from the measurement of mortal folly!—that residuary legatee was Joshua Rigg, who was also sole executor, and who was to take thenceforth the name of Featherstone.", "그는 단독 유언 집행인이기도 했으며, 그때부터 페더스톤이라는 이름을 쓰게 되어 있었다."]], [["There was a rustling which seemed like a shudder running round the room.", "방 안을 한 바퀴 도는 전율처럼 보이는 술렁임이 일었다."], ["Every one stared afresh at Mr. Rigg, who apparently experienced no surprise.", "모두가 리그 씨를 다시 빤히 쳐다보았으나, 그는 도무지 놀라는 기색이 없었다."]], [["“A most singular testamentary disposition!”", "\"참으로 기이하기 짝이 없는 유산 처분입니다!\""], ["exclaimed Mr.", "트럼불 씨가 외쳤다."], ["Trumbull, preferring for once that he should be considered ignorant in the past.", "그는 이번만큼은 자기가 사정을 미리 몰랐던 사람으로 여겨지기를 바랐다."], ["“But there is a second will—there is a further document.", "\"그러나 두 번째 유언장이 있습니다 — 또 다른 문서가 있어요."], ["We have not yet heard the final wishes of the deceased.”", "우리는 아직 고인의 마지막 뜻을 듣지 못했습니다.\""]], [["Mary Garth was feeling that what they had yet to hear were not the final wishes.", "메리 가스는 그들이 아직 들어야 할 것이 마지막 뜻은 아니라고 느끼고 있었다."], ["The second will revoked everything except the legacies to the low persons before mentioned (some alterations in these being the occasion of the codicil), and the bequest of all the land lying in Lowick parish with all the stock and household furniture, to Joshua Rigg.", "두 번째 유언장은 앞서 언급된 천한 사람들에게 주는 유산(이에 대한 몇 가지 변경이 추가 조항의 사유였다)과 로윅 교구에 자리한 모든 땅을 가축과 가재도구 전부와 함께 조슈아 리그에게 주는 유증을 제외하고는 모든 것을 철회했다."], ["The residue of the property was to be devoted to the erection and endowment of almshouses for old men, to be called Featherstone’s Alms-Houses, and to be built on a piece of land near Middlemarch already bought for the purpose by the testator, he wishing—so the document declared—to please God Almighty.", "나머지 재산은 노인들을 위한 자선 양로원을 짓고 기금을 대는 데 바쳐지게 되어 있었으며, 그 양로원은 페더스톤 양로원이라 불리고, 유언자가 이미 그 목적으로 사들인 미들마치 부근의 땅에 세워질 것이었다. 문서에 적힌 바에 따르면 그는 전능하신 하느님을 기쁘게 해 드리고자 그리한 것이었다."], ["Nobody present had a farthing; but Mr.", "자리에 함께한 누구도 한 푼 받지 못했다."], ["Trumbull had the gold-headed cane.", "다만 트럼불 씨에게는 금장 손잡이 지팡이가 있었다."], ["It took some time for the company to recover the power of expression.", "일동이 말할 기운을 되찾기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸다."], ["Mary dared not look at Fred.", "메리는 차마 프레드를 쳐다볼 수 없었다."]], [["Mr. Vincy was the first to speak—after using his snuff-box energetically—and he spoke with loud indignation.", "가장 먼저 입을 연 사람은 빈시 씨였다."], ["“The most unaccountable will I ever heard!", "코담뱃갑을 힘차게 사용한 뒤, 그는 큰 소리로 분개하며 말했다."], ["I should say he was not in his right mind when he made it.", "\"이렇게 어처구니없는 유언장은 난생처음 들어 봅니다!"], ["I should say this last will was void,” added Mr.", "이걸 만들 때 제정신이 아니었을 거라고 봐요."], ["Vincy, feeling that this expression put the thing in the true light.", "이 마지막 유언장은 무효라고 봅니다.\" 빈시 씨는 이 표현이 사태를 제대로 짚어 준다는 느낌에 덧붙였다."], ["“Eh Standish?”", "\"안 그렇소, 스탠디시?\""]], [["“Our deceased friend always knew what he was about, I think,”", "\"우리 고인 친구께서는 늘 자기가 무얼 하는지 아셨던 분이라고 봅니다.\""], ["said Mr. Standish.", "스탠디시 씨가 말했다."], ["“Everything is quite regular.", "\"모든 것이 아주 적법합니다."], ["Here is a letter from Clemmens of Brassing tied with the will.", "여기 유언장에 묶인, 브래싱의 클레먼스가 보낸 편지가 있군요."], ["He drew it up.", "그가 작성했습니다."], ["A very respectable solicitor.”", "아주 훌륭한 사무 변호사지요.\""]], [["“I never noticed any alienation of mind—any aberration of intellect in the late Mr. Featherstone,”", "\"저는 고(故) 페더스톤 씨에게서 정신의 이상이라든가 지성의 착란 같은 것을 전혀 알아채지 못했습니다.\""], ["said Borthrop Trumbull, “but I call this will eccentric.", "보스럽 트럼불이 말했다. \"하지만 이 유언장은 별나다고 부르겠습니다."], ["I was always willingly of service to the old soul; and he intimated pretty plainly a sense of obligation which would show itself in his will.", "저는 그 노인장께 늘 기꺼이 도움을 드렸고, 그분도 유언장에 드러날 만한 신세 진 마음을 꽤 분명하게 내비치셨지요."], ["The gold-headed cane is farcical considered as an acknowledgment to me; but happily I am above mercenary considerations.”", "금장 손잡이 지팡이라니, 제게 보답이랍시고 준 것치고는 가소롭습니다. 그러나 다행히도 저는 돈 따위에 연연하는 사람은 아니지요.\""]], [["“There’s nothing very surprising in the matter that I can see,”", "\"제가 보기엔 이 일에 그다지 놀랄 만한 건 없습니다.\""], ["said Caleb Garth.", "케일럽 가스가 말했다."], ["“Anybody might have had more reason for wondering if the will had been what you might expect from an open-minded straightforward man.", "\"오히려 이 유언장이 트인 마음을 지닌 정직한 사람한테 기대할 법한 것이었다면, 누구든 더 의아해할 까닭이 있었겠지요."], ["For my part, I wish there was no such thing as a will.”", "저로서는 유언장이라는 게 아예 없었으면 합니다.\""]], [["“That’s a strange sentiment to come from a Christian man, by God!”", "\"맙소사, 기독교도 입에서 나올 말로는 참 별난 소감이로군!\""], ["said the lawyer.", "변호사가 말했다."], ["“I should like to know how you will back that up, Garth!”", "\"그걸 어떻게 뒷받침할 셈인지 들어 보고 싶구먼, 가스!\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아.\""], ["said Caleb, leaning forward, adjusting his finger-tips with nicety and looking meditatively on the ground.", "케일럽이 몸을 앞으로 숙이고 손가락 끝을 정성스레 매만지며 골똘히 땅을 바라보며 말했다."], ["It always seemed to him that words were the hardest part of “business.”", "그에게는 늘 말이야말로 \"일\"에서 가장 어려운 부분으로 여겨졌다."]], [["But here Mr. Jonah Featherstone made himself heard.", "그러나 여기서 조나 페더스톤 씨가 목소리를 냈다."], ["“Well, he always was a fine hypocrite, was my brother Peter.", "\"흥, 우리 형 피터는 늘 대단한 위선자였지."], ["But this will cuts out everything.", "하지만 이 유언장은 모든 걸 잘라 버리는군."], ["If I’d known, a wagon and six horses shouldn’t have drawn me from Brassing.", "진작 알았더라면 짐마차에 말 여섯 마리를 매도 날 브래싱에서 끌어내지 못했을 거야."], ["I’ll put a white hat and drab coat on to-morrow.”", "내일은 흰 모자에 누런 코트를 걸쳐야겠어.\""]], [["“Dear, dear,” wept Mrs.", "\"저런, 저런.\""], ["Cranch, “and we’ve been at the expense of travelling, and that poor lad sitting idle here so long!", "크랜치 부인이 울먹였다. \"우리는 여비를 들여 왔고, 저 가엾은 애는 여기서 그토록 오래 빈둥대며 앉아 있었는데!"], ["It’s the first time I ever heard my brother Peter was so wishful to please God Almighty; but if I was to be struck helpless I must say it’s hard—I can think no other.”", "피터 오라버니가 전능하신 하느님을 그토록 기쁘게 해 드리고 싶어 했다는 말은 난생처음 들어 봐. 하지만 내가 그 자리에서 못 박힌 듯 굳어 버린다 해도, 이건 너무하다고 말할 수밖에 없어 — 달리는 생각할 수가 없어.\""]], [["“It’ll do him no good where he’s gone, that’s my belief,”", "\"그래 봤자 그가 간 곳에서는 아무 소용도 없을 거야, 내 장담하지.\""], ["said Solomon, with a bitterness which was remarkably genuine, though his tone could not help being sly.", "솔로몬이 말했다. 그 쓰라림은 놀랄 만큼 진심이었으나, 그 어조는 어쩔 수 없이 음흉했다."], ["“Peter was a bad liver, and almshouses won’t cover it, when he’s had the impudence to show it at the last.”", "\"피터는 못된 인생을 산 사람이었고, 마지막에 그걸 뻔뻔스럽게 드러낸 마당에 양로원 따위로 덮을 수 있는 게 아니야.\""]], [["“And all the while had got his own lawful family—brothers and sisters and nephews and nieces—and has sat in church with ’em whenever he thought well to come,”", "\"게다가 그동안 줄곧 제 적법한 가족 — 형제자매며 조카들이 있었고, 그러고 싶을 때면 언제든 교회에서 우리와 함께 앉기도 했으면서.\""], ["said Mrs. Waule.", "와울 부인이 말했다."], ["“And might have left his property so respectable, to them that’s never been used to extravagance or unsteadiness in no manner of way—and not so poor but what they could have saved every penny and made more of it.", "\"그리고 어떤 식으로도 사치나 방탕에 길든 적 없는 사람들에게 그토록 번듯하게 재산을 남길 수도 있었는데 — 게다가 그 사람들은 한 푼도 허투루 쓰지 않고 모아서 더 불릴 수 있을 만큼 변변치 못한 처지도 아니었는데 말이야."], ["And me—the trouble I’ve been at, times and times, to come here and be sisterly—and him with things on his mind all the while that might make anybody’s flesh creep.", "그리고 나는 — 여기 와서 누이답게 굴려고 그 숱한 수고를 마다하지 않았건만 — 그동안 줄곧 누구든 소름 끼칠 만한 것을 마음에 품고 있었던 거야."], ["But if the Almighty’s allowed it, he means to punish him for it.", "하지만 전능하신 분이 그걸 허락하셨다면, 그건 그 일로 그를 벌하시려는 게야."], ["Brother Solomon, I shall be going, if you’ll drive me.”", "솔로몬 오라버니, 태워다 주시면 저는 이만 가겠어요.\""]], [["“I’ve no desire to put my foot on the premises again,”", "\"나도 이 집에 다시는 발을 들여놓고 싶지 않아.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“I’ve got land of my own and property of my own to will away.”", "\"나야 내 땅이 있고 물려줄 내 재산도 있으니까.\""]], [["“It’s a poor tale how luck goes in the world,”", "\"세상에서 운이라는 게 어찌 돌아가는지 참 한심한 얘기야.\""], ["said Jonah.", "조나가 말했다."], ["“It never answers to have a bit of spirit in you.", "\"근성이라는 게 조금이라도 있으면 절대 보답을 못 받지."], ["You’d better be a dog in the manger.", "차라리 구유 속의 개*가 되는 편이 나아."], ["But those above ground might learn a lesson.", "하지만 아직 땅 위에 있는 사람들은 교훈을 얻을 수 있겠지."], ["One fool’s will is enough in a family.”", "한 집안에 바보 같은 유언장 하나면 충분하다는 거.\""]], [["“There’s more ways than one of being a fool,”", "\"바보가 되는 길도 한 가지만 있는 게 아니지.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“I shan’t leave my money to be poured down the sink, and I shan’t leave it to foundlings from Africay.", "\"나는 내 돈을 하수구에 쏟아붓도록 남기지 않을 거고, 아프리카에서 온 업둥이한테 남기지도 않을 거야."], ["I like Featherstones that were brewed such, and not turned Featherstones with sticking the name on ’em.”", "나는 그렇게 빚어진 페더스톤들이 좋지, 이름만 갖다 붙여 페더스톤이 된 자가 아니라.\""]], [["Solomon addressed these remarks in a loud aside to Mrs. Waule as he rose to accompany her.", "솔로몬은 와울 부인을 배웅하러 일어서면서 이 말들을 그녀에게 큰 소리로 곁말처럼 건넸다."], ["Brother Jonah felt himself capable of much more stinging wit than this, but he reflected that there was no use in offending the new proprietor of Stone Court, until you were certain that he was quite without intentions of hospitality towards witty men whose name he was about to bear.", "조나 형은 자신이 이보다 훨씬 더 쏘는 듯한 익살을 부릴 수 있다고 느꼈지만, 자신이 곧 이어받을 이름을 쓰게 될 익살꾼들에게 그 새 주인이 환대할 뜻이 전혀 없다고 확실히 알기 전까지는, 스톤 코트의 새 소유주의 비위를 거스를 까닭이 없다고 생각했다."]], [["Mr. Joshua Rigg, in fact, appeared to trouble himself little about any innuendoes, but showed a notable change of manner, walking coolly up to Mr.", "사실 조슈아 리그 씨는 어떤 빈정거림에도 거의 신경 쓰지 않는 듯했으나, 눈에 띄게 태도를 바꾸어 스탠디시 씨에게 태연히 걸어가서는 사무적인 질문을 매우 침착하게 던졌다."], ["Standish and putting business questions with much coolness.", "그는 높고 짹짹거리는 목소리에 천박한 말씨를 지니고 있었다."], ["He had a high chirping voice and a vile accent. Fred, whom he no longer moved to laughter, thought him the lowest monster he had ever seen.", "더는 그를 보고 웃을 마음이 들지 않는 프레드는 그가 자기가 본 가장 천박한 괴물이라고 생각했다."], ["But Fred was feeling rather sick.", "그러나 프레드는 속이 좀 메스꺼웠다."], ["The Middlemarch mercer waited for an opportunity of engaging Mr.", "미들마치의 포목상은 리그 씨와 대화를 나눌 기회를 노리고 있었다."], ["Rigg in conversation: there was no knowing how many pairs of legs the new proprietor might require hose for, and profits were more to be relied on than legacies.", "그 새 소유주가 몇 켤레나 되는 다리에 양말을 필요로 할지 알 수 없는 노릇이었고, 이문은 유산보다 더 믿을 만한 것이었으니 말이다."], ["Also, the mercer, as a second cousin, was dispassionate enough to feel curiosity.", "게다가 포목상은 육촌으로서 호기심을 느낄 만큼 이해관계에서 벗어나 있기도 했다."]], [["Mr.", "빈시 씨는 한 차례 터뜨린 뒤로 거만하게 침묵을 지키고 있었다. 불쾌한 감정에 너무 사로잡혀 자리를 뜰 생각조차 못 하고 있다가, 아내가 프레드 곁으로 가서 사랑하는 아들의 손을 잡고 소리 없이 울고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Vincy, after his one outburst, had remained proudly silent, though too much preoccupied with unpleasant feelings to think of moving, till he observed that his wife had gone to Fred’s side and was crying silently while she held her darling’s hand.", "그는 곧바로 일어나 일동에게 등을 돌린 채 그녀에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"기죽지 마, 루시."], ["He rose immediately, and turning his back on the company while he said to her in an undertone,—“Don’t give way, Lucy; don’t make a fool of yourself, my dear, before these people,”", "이 사람들 앞에서 바보처럼 굴지 마, 여보.\" 그러고는 평소의 큰 목소리로 덧붙였다."], ["he added in his usual loud voice—“Go and order the phaeton, Fred; I have no time to waste.”", "\"가서 사두마차를 대령하라 일러라, 프레드. 나는 허비할 시간이 없다.\""]], [["Mary Garth had before this been getting ready to go home with her father.", "메리 가스는 그보다 앞서 아버지와 함께 집으로 갈 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["She met Fred in the hall, and now for the first time had the courage to look at him.", "그녀는 현관에서 프레드와 마주쳤고, 이제야 비로소 그를 바라볼 용기가 났다."], ["He had that withered sort of paleness which will sometimes come on young faces, and his hand was very cold when she shook it.", "그의 얼굴에는 젊은 얼굴에 이따금 떠오르곤 하는, 시든 듯한 창백함이 어려 있었고, 그녀가 그의 손을 잡았을 때 그 손은 몹시 차가웠다."], ["Mary too was agitated; she was conscious that fatally, without will of her own, she had perhaps made a great difference to Fred’s lot.", "메리도 동요하고 있었다. 자기 뜻과는 무관하게, 운명적으로, 어쩌면 자신이 프레드의 운명에 커다란 차이를 빚어냈을지도 모른다는 것을 의식하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“Good-by,”", "\"잘 가요.\""], ["she said, with affectionate sadness.", "그녀가 애정 어린 슬픔을 담아 말했다."], ["“Be brave, Fred.", "\"용기를 내요, 프레드."], ["I do believe you are better without the money.", "난 정말로 당신이 그 돈이 없는 편이 더 낫다고 믿어요."], ["What was the good of it to Mr. Featherstone?”", "그 돈이 페더스톤 씨에게 무슨 소용이었어요?\""]], [["“That’s all very fine,”", "\"그거야 다 듣기 좋은 소리지.\""], ["said Fred, pettishly.", "프레드가 토라져 말했다."], ["“What is a fellow to do?", "\"사내가 어떻게 해야 한단 말이오?"], ["I must go into the Church now.”", "난 이제 성직자가 될 수밖에 없어.\""], ["(He knew that this would vex Mary: very well; then she must tell him what else he could do.) “And I thought I should be able to pay your father at once and make everything right.", "(그는 이 말이 메리의 비위를 거스르리라는 것을 알았다. 좋아, 그렇다면 그녀가 다른 무엇을 할 수 있는지 말해 보라지.) \"게다가 난 당신 아버지께 곧장 돈을 갚고 모든 걸 바로잡을 수 있으리라 생각했는데."], ["And you have not even a hundred pounds left you.", "그런데 당신은 백 파운드조차 남겨 받지 못했잖아."], ["What shall you do now, Mary?”", "이제 어쩔 거요, 메리?\""]], [["“Take another situation, of course, as soon as I can get one.", "\"물론 일자리를 구하는 대로 또 다른 자리로 가야지요."], ["My father has enough to do to keep the rest, without me.", "우리 아버지는 저 없이도 식구들을 건사하느라 벅찬걸요."], ["Good-by.”", "잘 가요.\""]], [["In a very short time Stone Court was cleared of well-brewed Featherstones and other long-accustomed visitors.", "아주 짧은 시간 만에 스톤 코트에서는 제대로 빚어진 페더스톤들과 그 밖에 오랫동안 드나들던 방문객들이 모두 빠져나갔다."], ["Another stranger had been brought to settle in the neighborhood of Middlemarch, but in the case of Mr. Rigg Featherstone there was more discontent with immediate visible consequences than speculation as to the effect which his presence might have in the future.", "또 한 사람의 낯선 이가 미들마치 인근에 정착하게 되었으나, 리그 페더스톤 씨의 경우에는 그의 존재가 장차 미칠 영향에 대한 추측보다 당장 눈앞에 드러난 결과에 대한 불만이 더 컸다."], ["No soul was prophetic enough to have any foreboding as to what might appear on the trial of Joshua Rigg.", "조슈아 리그가 시험대에 올랐을 때 무엇이 드러날지를 미리 점칠 만큼 선견지명을 지닌 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["And here I am naturally led to reflect on the means of elevating a low subject.", "그리고 여기서 나는 자연히 비천한 소재를 격상시키는 방법에 대해 생각하게 된다."], ["Historical parallels are remarkably efficient in this way.", "역사적 유추는 이 점에서 놀랄 만큼 효과적이다."], ["The chief objection to them is, that the diligent narrator may lack space, or (what is often the same thing) may not be able to think of them with any degree of particularity, though he may have a philosophical confidence that if known they would be illustrative.", "거기에 대한 주된 이견이라면, 부지런한 서술자가 지면이 부족하거나 (흔히 같은 말이지만) 그 유추를 충분히 구체적으로 떠올리지 못할 수 있다는 점이다. 비록 그것이 알려지기만 한다면 좋은 예증이 되리라는 철학적 확신은 품고 있을지라도 말이다."], ["It seems an easier and shorter way to dignity, to observe that—since there never was a true story which could not be told in parables, where you might put a monkey for a margrave, and vice versa—whatever has been or is to be narrated by me about low people, may be ennobled by being considered a parable; so that if any bad habits and ugly consequences are brought into view, the reader may have the relief of regarding them as not more than figuratively ungenteel, and may feel himself virtually in company with persons of some style.", "좀 더 쉽고 빠른 품위의 길은 이렇게 말하는 것 같다. 즉, 후작 자리에 원숭이를 앉히고 그 반대로도 할 수 있는 우화로 풀어내지 못할 참된 이야기란 결코 없었으니, 내가 비천한 사람들에 관해 이야기했거나 앞으로 이야기할 무엇이든 우화로 여겨짐으로써 고상해질 수 있다는 것이다."], ["Thus while I tell the truth about loobies, my reader’s imagination need not be entirely excluded from an occupation with lords; and the petty sums which any bankrupt of high standing would be sorry to retire upon, may be lifted to the level of high commercial transactions by the inexpensive addition of proportional ciphers.", "그리하여 어떤 나쁜 습관과 추한 결과가 눈앞에 펼쳐진다 하더라도, 독자는 그것을 비유적으로나 천박할 뿐이라고 여기는 위안을 얻을 수 있고, 자신이 사실상 얼마간 품격 있는 사람들과 어울리고 있다고 느낄 수 있다. 이렇게 하여 내가 멍청이들에 관한 진실을 이야기하는 동안에도, 독자의 상상력은 영주들과 함께하는 일에서 완전히 배제될 필요가 없으며, 지위 높은 어떤 파산자라도 그것만 쥐고 물러나기는 서글퍼할 자잘한 금액도, 비례하는 영(零)들을 비용 한 푼 들이지 않고 덧붙임으로써 거대한 상업 거래의 수준으로 끌어올릴 수 있다."]], [["As to any provincial history in which the agents are all of high moral rank, that must be of a date long posterior to the first Reform Bill, and Peter Featherstone, you perceive, was dead and buried some months before Lord Grey came into office.", "* 구유 속의 개: 이솝 우화에서, 제 먹지도 못할 건초를 깔고 누워 소가 먹지도 못하게 하는 개. 자기에게는 쓸모없으면서 남도 누리지 못하게 하는 심술을 가리킨다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVI.", "# 제33장"]], [["’T is strange to see the humors of these men, These great aspiring spirits, that should be wise: .", "마땅히 현명해야 할, 저 높이 솟구치는 위대한 영혼들이여."], [".", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], ["For being the nature of great spirits to love To be where they may be most eminent; They, rating of themselves so farre above Us in conceit, with whom they do frequent, Imagine how we wonder and esteeme All that they do or say; which makes them strive To make our admiration more extreme, Which they suppose they cannot, ’less they give Notice of their extreme and highest thoughts.", ". ."], ["—DANIEL: Tragedy of Philotas.", ". ."]], [["Mr. Vincy went home from the reading of the will with his point of view considerably changed in relation to many subjects. He was an open-minded man, but given to indirect modes of expressing himself: when he was disappointed in a market for his silk braids, he swore at the groom; when his brother-in-law Bulstrode had vexed him, he made cutting remarks on Methodism; and it was now apparent that he regarded Fred’s idleness with a sudden increase of severity, by his throwing an embroidered cap out of the smoking-room on to the hall-floor.", "위대한 영혼들의 본성이란 자신이 가장\n빼어나 보일 수 있는 곳에 있기를 사랑하는 법,"]], [["“Well, sir,” he observed, when that young gentleman was moving off to bed, “I hope you’ve made up your mind now to go up next term and pass your examination. I’ve taken my resolution, so I advise you to lose no time in taking yours.”", "그들은 어울려 지내는 우리보다 자신을\n한껏 드높이 헤아리며,"]], [["Fred made no answer: he was too utterly depressed. Twenty-four hours ago he had thought that instead of needing to know what he should do, he should by this time know that he needed to do nothing: that he should hunt in pink, have a first-rate hunter, ride to cover on a fine hack, and be generally respected for doing so; moreover, that he should be able at once to pay Mr. Garth, and that Mary could no longer have any reason for not marrying him. And all this was to have come without study or other inconvenience, purely by the favor of providence in the shape of an old gentleman’s caprice. But now, at the end of the twenty-four hours, all those firm expectations were upset. It was “rather hard lines” that while he was smarting under this disappointment he should be treated as if he could have helped it. But he went away silently and his mother pleaded for him.", "우리가 그들의 모든 언행을 얼마나 경탄하고\n우러르는가를 상상하나니, 그리하여 그들은"]], [["“Don’t be hard on the poor boy, Vincy. He’ll turn out well yet, though that wicked man has deceived him. I feel as sure as I sit here, Fred will turn out well—else why was he brought back from the brink of the grave? And I call it a robbery: it was like giving him the land, to promise it; and what is promising, if making everybody believe is not promising? And you see he did leave him ten thousand pounds, and then took it away again.”", "우리의 찬탄을 더욱 극진히 만들고자 애쓰며,\n자신의 극단적이고 드높은 사상을 알리지 않고서는"]], [["“Took it away again!” said Mr. Vincy, pettishly. “I tell you the lad’s an unlucky lad, Lucy. And you’ve always spoiled him.”", "그리할 수 없다고 여기는 것이다. —대니얼, 『필로타스의 비극』"]], [["“Well, Vincy, he was my first, and you made a fine fuss with him when he came.", "빈시 씨는 유언장을 다 듣고 여러 문제에 대한 자신의 견해가 상당히 바뀐 채 집으로 돌아갔다. 그는 너그러운 사람이었으나 자기 생각을 에둘러 표현하는 버릇이 있었다."], ["You were as proud as proud,” said Mrs.", "비단 끈 시장에서 실망스러운 일이 생기면 마부에게 욕을 퍼부었고, 처남인 불스트로드가 그를 화나게 하면 감리교에 대해 신랄한 말을 늘어놓았다."], ["Vincy, easily recovering her cheerful smile.", "그리고 그가 자수 놓은 모자를 흡연실에서 현관 바닥으로 내던지는 것을 보면, 이제 프레드의 게으름을 갑작스레 한층 엄하게 여기고 있음이 분명했다."]], [["“Who knows what babies will turn to?", "\"그래, 얘야.\" 그 젊은이가 자러 가려고 일어서자 그가 말했다."], ["I was fool enough, I dare say,”", "\"이제 다음 학기에 올라가서 시험을 통과하기로 마음을 굳혔기를 바란다."], ["said the husband—more mildly, however.", "나는 결심을 했으니, 너도 지체 없이 결심하기를 권한다.\""]], [["“But who has handsomer, better children than ours?", "프레드는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 너무도 완전히 풀이 죽어 있었다."], ["Fred is far beyond other people’s sons: you may hear it in his speech, that he has kept college company.", "스물네 시간 전만 해도 그는 자신이 무엇을 해야 할지 알아야 할 필요가 없을 뿐 아니라, 이제쯤이면 아무것도 할 필요가 없다는 것을 알게 되리라고 생각했었다. 분홍빛 사냥복을 입고 사냥을 다니고, 일류 사냥말을 가지고, 멋진 승용마를 타고 사냥터로 나가며, 그렇게 하는 것으로 두루 존경을 받으리라고."], ["And Rosamond—where is there a girl like her?", "게다가 곧바로 가스 씨에게 빚을 갚을 수 있을 테고, 그러면 메리도 더 이상 그와 결혼하지 않을 이유가 없으리라고."], ["She might stand beside any lady in the land, and only look the better for it.", "그리고 이 모든 것이 공부나 다른 수고 없이, 순전히 한 노신사의 변덕이라는 형태로 찾아온 섭리의 은총에 의해 이루어질 터였다. 그런데 이제 스물네 시간이 지나자 그 확고했던 기대가 모조리 뒤집혀 버렸다."], ["You see—Mr.", "이 실망으로 쓰라려하는 와중에, 마치 그것이 자기 탓이라도 되는 양 취급받는 것은 \"다소 가혹한 처사\"였다."], ["Lydgate has kept the highest company and been everywhere, and he fell in love with her at once.", "하지만 그는 말없이 자리를 떠났고, 그의 어머니가 그를 위해 변호하고 나섰다. \"불쌍한 아이에게 모질게 굴지 말아요, 여보."], ["Not but what I could have wished Rosamond had not engaged herself.", "저 사악한 자가 그 애를 속였다고는 해도, 그 애는 아직 잘될 거예요. 내가 여기 앉아 있는 것만큼이나 확실히 느껴져요, 프레드는 잘될 거라고요."], ["She might have met somebody on a visit who would have been a far better match; I mean at her schoolfellow Miss Willoughby’s.", "그렇지 않다면 왜 그 애가 무덤 가장자리에서 다시 살아 돌아왔겠어요? 그리고 나는 이걸 도둑질이라고 부르겠어요."], ["There are relations in that family quite as high as Mr.", "약속을 했으니 땅을 준 거나 마찬가지죠. 모두가 믿게 만드는 것이 약속이 아니라면 약속이 대체 뭐겠어요?"], ["Lydgate’s.”", "그리고 보세요, 그 사람은 그 애에게 만 파운드를 남기더니 다시 빼앗아 갔잖아요.\""]], [["“Damn relations!” said Mr.", "\"다시 빼앗아 갔다고!\""], ["Vincy; “I’ve had enough of them.", "빈시 씨가 짜증스레 말했다."], ["I don’t want a son-in-law who has got nothing but his relations to recommend him.”", "\"그 녀석은 운이 없는 녀석이오, 루시. 그리고 당신은 늘 그 애를 버릇없이 키웠지.\""]], [["“Why, my dear,” said Mrs.", "\"하지만 여보, 그 애는 내 첫아이였고, 그 애가 태어났을 때 당신도 야단스럽게 좋아했잖아요."], ["Vincy, “you seemed as pleased as could be about it.", "어찌나 자랑스러워했는지.\""], ["It’s true, I wasn’t at home; but Rosamond told me you hadn’t a word to say against the engagement.", "빈시 부인이 손쉽게 명랑한 미소를 되찾으며 말했다. \"갓난아기가 어떻게 될지 누가 알겠소?"], ["And she has begun to buy in the best linen and cambric for her underclothing.”", "내가 어리석었던 게지.\" 남편이 말했으나, 그래도 한결 누그러진 어조였다."]], [["“Not by my will,”", "\"하지만 우리 애들보다 더 잘생기고 더 훌륭한 자식을 둔 사람이 어디 있어요?"], ["said Mr. Vincy.", "프레드는 다른 집 아들들보다 훨씬 뛰어나요."], ["“I shall have enough to do this year, with an idle scamp of a son, without paying for wedding-clothes.", "말씨를 들어 보면 대학에서 좋은 사람들과 어울렸다는 걸 알 수 있죠. 그리고 로저먼드는요, 그 애 같은 처녀가 어디 있겠어요?"], ["The times are as tight as can be; everybody is being ruined; and I don’t believe Lydgate has got a farthing.", "이 나라 어느 귀부인 옆에 서더라도 오히려 더 돋보일 거예요. 보세요, 리드게이트 씨는 최상류층과 어울리며 안 가 본 데가 없는 사람인데도 그 애에게 단번에 반했잖아요."], ["I shan’t give my consent to their marrying.", "하긴 로저먼드가 약혼하지 않았더라면 하는 마음도 있긴 해요. 손님으로 갔다가 훨씬 나은 혼처를 만났을지도 모르니까요."], ["Let ’em wait, as their elders have done before ’em.”", "그 애 학교 친구 윌러비 양 댁 말이에요. 그 집안에는 리드게이트 씨 못지않게 지체 높은 친척들이 있거든요.\""]], [["“Rosamond will take it hard, Vincy, and you know you never could bear to cross her.”", "\"친척이고 뭐고 빌어먹을!\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. \"친척이라면 이제 지긋지긋하오. 나는 친척 말고는 내세울 게 없는 사위는 바라지 않소.\""]], [["“Yes, I could. The sooner the engagement’s off, the better. I don’t believe he’ll ever make an income, the way he goes on. He makes enemies; that’s all I hear of his making.”", "\"어머나 여보, 당신은 그 일을 더없이 기뻐하는 것 같았는데요.\" 빈시 부인이 말했다. \"사실 그때 나는 집에 없었지만, 당신이 약혼에 대해 한마디도 반대하지 않았다고 로저먼드가 그러던걸요. 그리고 그 애는 속옷감으로 최고급 아마포와 고급 무명을 사들이기 시작했어요.\" \"내 뜻은 아니오.\" 빈시 씨가 말했다. \"올해는 게으른 망나니 아들놈 때문에라도 할 일이 태산인데, 혼수 옷감 값까지 치를 여유는 없소. 시절은 더없이 빡빡하고, 다들 망해 가고 있소. 그리고 나는 리드게이트가 한 푼이라도 가지고 있다고 믿지 않소. 나는 그들의 결혼에 동의해 주지 않겠소. 어른들이 전에 그랬던 것처럼 그들도 기다리게 합시다.\""]], [["“But he stands very high with Mr. Bulstrode, my dear.", "\"로저먼드가 가슴 아파할 거예요, 여보."], ["The marriage would please him, I should think.”", "당신은 그 애 뜻을 거스르는 걸 견디지 못하잖아요.\""]], [["“Please the deuce!”", "\"아니, 견딜 수 있소."], ["said Mr. Vincy.", "약혼은 깨질수록 좋소."], ["“Bulstrode won’t pay for their keep.", "저렇게 처신하는 걸 보면 평생 변변한 수입을 올릴 것 같지 않소."], ["And if Lydgate thinks I’m going to give money for them to set up housekeeping, he’s mistaken, that’s all.", "적만 만들고 다닐 뿐이지. 내가 그 사람에 대해 듣는 거라곤 그게 전부요.\""], ["I expect I shall have to put down my horses soon.", "\"하지만 그 사람은 불스트로드 씨에게 신망이 아주 두텁잖아요, 여보."], ["You’d better tell Rosy what I say.”", "그 결혼은 그분도 좋아하실 텐데요.\""]], [["This was a not infrequent procedure with Mr.", "\"좋아하긴 무슨!\" 빈시 씨가 말했다."], ["Vincy—to be rash in jovial assent, and on becoming subsequently conscious that he had been rash, to employ others in making the offensive retractation.", "\"불스트로드가 그들 생활비를 대 주진 않을 거요. 그리고 리드게이트가 내가 그들 살림 차리는 데 돈을 내줄 거라고 생각한다면 오산이오, 그뿐이오."], ["However, Mrs.", "나는 곧 말들을 처분해야 할 판이오. 로지에게 내 말을 전하는 게 좋겠소.\""], ["Vincy, who never willingly opposed her husband, lost no time the next morning in letting Rosamond know what he had said.", "이것은 빈시 씨에게 드물지 않은 행동 방식이었다. 유쾌하게 덜컥 동의해 놓고는, 나중에 자신이 경솔했음을 깨닫고는 그 무례한 철회를 다른 사람에게 떠맡기는 것이다."], ["Rosamond, examining some muslin-work, listened in silence, and at the end gave a certain turn of her graceful neck, of which only long experience could teach you that it meant perfect obstinacy.", "어쨌거나 결코 남편에게 기꺼이 맞서는 법이 없는 빈시 부인은 이튿날 아침 지체 없이 로저먼드에게 그가 한 말을 전했다. 모슬린 자수 작업을 살펴보고 있던 로저먼드는 말없이 듣더니, 다 듣고 나서 우아한 목을 어떤 식으로 갸웃했는데, 그것이 완벽한 고집을 뜻한다는 것은 오랜 경험을 통해서만 알 수 있는 일이었다."]], [["“What do you say, my dear?”", "\"넌 어떻게 생각하니, 얘야?\""], ["said her mother, with affectionate deference.", "어머니가 애정 어린 조심스러움으로 물었다."]], [["“Papa does not mean anything of the kind,”", "\"아빠는 전혀 그런 뜻이 아니에요.\""], ["said Rosamond, quite calmly.", "로저먼드가 아주 차분하게 말했다."], ["“He has always said that he wished me to marry the man I loved.", "\"아빠는 늘 제가 사랑하는 사람과 결혼하기를 바란다고 말씀하셨어요."], ["And I shall marry Mr. Lydgate.", "그리고 저는 리드게이트 씨와 결혼할 거예요."], ["It is seven weeks now since papa gave his consent.", "아빠가 동의하신 지 벌써 칠 주가 지났어요."], ["And I hope we shall have Mrs. Bretton’s house.”", "그리고 저는 우리가 브레턴 부인 댁을 얻을 수 있기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Well, my dear, I shall leave you to manage your papa.", "\"그래, 얘야, 네 아빠 문제는 네가 알아서 하렴. 너는 늘 모두를 잘 다루니까."], ["You always do manage everybody.", "하지만 우리가 정말 다마스크 천을 사러 가게 된다면, 새들러네가 좋단다. 홉킨스네보다 훨씬 낫지."], ["But if we ever do go and get damask, Sadler’s is the place—far better than Hopkins’s. Mrs.", "그런데 브레턴 부인 댁은 아주 크던데."], ["Bretton’s is very large, though: I should love you to have such a house; but it will take a great deal of furniture—carpeting and everything, besides plate and glass.", "네가 그런 집을 갖는다면야 나도 정말 좋겠지만, 가구가 엄청나게 들 거야. 양탄자에 온갖 것들, 게다가 은식기와 유리그릇까지."], ["And you hear, your papa says he will give no money.", "그리고 너도 들었다시피 네 아빠는 돈을 한 푼도 안 주겠다고 하시잖니."], ["Do you think Mr. Lydgate expects it?”", "리드게이트 씨가 그걸 기대하고 있을 것 같니?\""]], [["“You cannot imagine that I should ask him, mamma.", "\"제가 그분께 그런 걸 부탁하리라고는 상상도 못 하시겠죠, 엄마."], ["Of course he understands his own affairs.”", "물론 그분은 자기 일을 알아서 하실 거예요.\""]], [["“But he may have been looking for money, my dear, and we all thought of your having a pretty legacy as well as Fred;—and now everything is so dreadful—there’s no pleasure in thinking of anything, with that poor boy disappointed as he is.”", "\"하지만 그분도 돈을 기대하고 있었을지 모르잖니, 얘야. 우리도 다들 네가 프레드처럼 두둑한 유산을 받으리라 생각했고. 그런데 이제 모든 게 이렇게 끔찍하게 됐으니, 저 불쌍한 애가 저렇게 실망한 마당에 뭘 생각해도 기쁘지가 않구나.\""]], [["“That has nothing to do with my marriage, mamma.", "\"그건 제 결혼과는 아무 상관 없어요, 엄마."], ["Fred must leave off being idle.", "프레드는 게으름을 그만둬야 해요."], ["I am going up-stairs to take this work to Miss Morgan: she does the open hemming very well.", "저는 이 작업을 모건 양에게 가져다주러 위층에 올라갈 거예요. 그분은 오픈 헤밍을 아주 잘하시거든요."], ["Mary Garth might do some work for me now, I should think.", "메리 가스도 이제 제 일을 좀 해 줄 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["Her sewing is exquisite; it is the nicest thing I know about Mary.", "그 애 바느질 솜씨는 일품이죠. 메리에 대해 내가 아는 것 중 가장 좋은 점이에요."], ["I should so like to have all my cambric frilling double-hemmed.", "제 고급 무명 주름 장식을 전부 이중으로 감치고 싶거든요."], ["And it takes a long time.”", "그건 시간이 오래 걸려요.\""]], [["Mrs. Vincy’s belief that Rosamond could manage her papa was well founded.", "로저먼드가 아빠를 다룰 수 있다는 빈시 부인의 믿음은 근거가 충분했다."], ["Apart from his dinners and his coursing, Mr.", "만찬과 사냥을 빼면, 빈시 씨는 큰소리는 쳤어도 마치 총리대신이라도 된 양 제 마음대로 하는 일이 거의 없었다."], ["Vincy, blustering as he was, had as little of his own way as if he had been a prime minister: the force of circumstances was easily too much for him, as it is for most pleasure-loving florid men; and the circumstance called Rosamond was particularly forcible by means of that mild persistence which, as we know, enables a white soft living substance to make its way in spite of opposing rock.", "쾌락을 좋아하는 혈색 좋은 사내들 대부분이 그러하듯, 상황의 힘이 그에게는 쉽사리 감당하기 벅찼던 것이다. 그리고 로저먼드라는 이름의 상황은, 우리가 알다시피 부드러운 살아 있는 물질이 가로막는 바위를 뚫고 제 길을 내게 하는 그 온화한 끈기를 통해 특히나 강력했다."], ["Papa was not a rock: he had no other fixity than that fixity of alternating impulses sometimes called habit, and this was altogether unfavorable to his taking the only decisive line of conduct in relation to his daughter’s engagement—namely, to inquire thoroughly into Lydgate’s circumstances, declare his own inability to furnish money, and forbid alike either a speedy marriage or an engagement which must be too lengthy.", "아빠는 바위가 아니었다. 그에게는 때로 습관이라 불리는, 번갈아 이는 충동의 고정성 말고는 다른 고정성이 없었으니, 이는 딸의 약혼과 관련해 유일하게 단호한 행동 노선을 취하는 데 전혀 도움이 되지 않았다."], ["That seems very simple and easy in the statement; but a disagreeable resolve formed in the chill hours of the morning had as many conditions against it as the early frost, and rarely persisted under the warming influences of the day.", "그 노선이란 곧 리드게이트의 형편을 철저히 알아보고, 자신은 돈을 댈 능력이 없음을 선언하며, 성급한 결혼이든 지나치게 길어질 약혼이든 똑같이 금지하는 것이었다. 말로 하면 아주 간단하고 쉬워 보인다."], ["The indirect though emphatic expression of opinion to which Mr.", "하지만 아침의 쌀쌀한 시간에 세운 언짢은 결심은 이른 서리만큼이나 그것을 가로막는 조건들이 많아, 낮의 따스한 기운 아래서는 좀처럼 지속되지 못했다."], ["Vincy was prone suffered much restraint in this case: Lydgate was a proud man towards whom innuendoes were obviously unsafe, and throwing his hat on the floor was out of the question.", "빈시 씨가 즐겨 쓰는, 에두르되 강조하는 의견 표현 방식도 이번 경우에는 크게 억제될 수밖에 없었다. 리드게이트는 자존심 강한 사내라 그에게 넌지시 빈정대는 것은 분명 안전하지 못했고, 모자를 바닥에 내던지는 것 따위는 어림도 없었다."], ["Mr. Vincy was a little in awe of him, a little vain that he wanted to marry Rosamond, a little indisposed to raise a question of money in which his own position was not advantageous, a little afraid of being worsted in dialogue with a man better educated and more highly bred than himself, and a little afraid of doing what his daughter would not like.", "빈시 씨는 그를 조금 두려워했고, 그가 로저먼드와 결혼하고 싶어 한다는 데 조금 우쭐했으며, 자기 처지가 유리하지 못한 돈 문제를 꺼내기를 조금 꺼렸고, 자기보다 교육을 잘 받고 가문이 좋은 사내와 대화에서 밀릴까 조금 두려웠으며, 딸이 싫어할 일을 하는 것이 조금 무서웠다."], ["The part Mr. Vincy preferred playing was that of the generous host whom nobody criticises.", "빈시 씨가 즐겨 맡고 싶어 한 역할은 누구도 흉보지 않는 너그러운 주인이라는 역할이었다."], ["In the earlier half of the day there was business to hinder any formal communication of an adverse resolve; in the later there was dinner, wine, whist, and general satisfaction.", "하루의 전반부에는 반대 결심을 정식으로 전달하는 것을 가로막는 일거리가 있었고, 후반부에는 만찬과 술과 휘스트와 두루두루 흡족한 기분이 있었다."], ["And in the mean while the hours were each leaving their little deposit and gradually forming the final reason for inaction, namely, that action was too late.", "그리고 그러는 사이에 시간은 저마다 작은 침전물을 남기며 점차 무위(無爲)의 최종적인 이유를 빚어냈으니, 곧 행동하기에는 이미 너무 늦었다는 것이었다."], ["The accepted lover spent most of his evenings in Lowick Gate, and a love-making not at all dependent on money-advances from fathers-in-law, or prospective income from a profession, went on flourishingly under Mr.", "약혼을 받아들여진 그 연인은 저녁 대부분을 로윅 게이트에서 보냈고, 장인의 자금 지원이나 직업에서 나올 장래 수입 따위에는 전혀 기대지 않는 사랑놀이가 빈시 씨의 두 눈앞에서 무성하게 진행되고 있었다."], ["Vincy’s own eyes. Young love-making—that gossamer web!", "젊은 날의 사랑놀이, 그 거미줄 같은 것이여!"], ["Even the points it clings to—the things whence its subtle interlacings are swung—are scarcely perceptible: momentary touches of fingertips, meetings of rays from blue and dark orbs, unfinished phrases, lightest changes of cheek and lip, faintest tremors.", "그것이 매달리는 점들마저, 그 미묘한 얽힘이 걸려 흔들리는 그 지점들마저 거의 알아챌 수 없을 정도다. 손끝의 순간적인 스침, 푸른 눈동자와 검은 눈동자에서 나온 빛줄기들의 마주침, 끝맺지 못한 말들, 뺨과 입술의 더없이 가벼운 변화, 가장 희미한 떨림."], ["The web itself is made of spontaneous beliefs and indefinable joys, yearnings of one life towards another, visions of completeness, indefinite trust.", "그 거미줄 자체는 자연스러운 믿음과 형언할 수 없는 기쁨, 한 생명이 다른 생명을 향해 품는 갈망, 온전함의 환상, 막연한 신뢰로 이루어진다."], ["And Lydgate fell to spinning that web from his inward self with wonderful rapidity, in spite of experience supposed to be finished off with the drama of Laure—in spite too of medicine and biology; for the inspection of macerated muscle or of eyes presented in a dish (like Santa Lucia’s), and other incidents of scientific inquiry, are observed to be less incompatible with poetic love than a native dulness or a lively addiction to the lowest prose.", "그리고 리드게이트는 로르의 비극으로 끝났으리라 여겨지는 경험에도 불구하고, 또 의학과 생물학에도 불구하고, 자신의 내면에서 그 거미줄을 놀라운 속도로 자아내기 시작했다. 물에 불린 근육이나 접시에 담긴 눈알(성녀 루치아의 것처럼)을 살펴보는 일, 그 밖의 과학적 탐구의 사례들은, 타고난 둔감함이나 가장 저속한 산문에 대한 활기찬 탐닉보다는 시적인 사랑과 덜 양립 불가능한 것으로 관찰되기 때문이다."], ["As for Rosamond, she was in the water-lily’s expanding wonderment at its own fuller life, and she too was spinning industriously at the mutual web.", "로저먼드로 말하자면, 그녀는 제 충만해진 생명에 대한 수련의 활짝 피어나는 경이로움에 잠겨 있었고, 그녀 역시 부지런히 그 공동의 거미줄을 자아내고 있었다."], ["All this went on in the corner of the drawing-room where the piano stood, and subtle as it was, the light made it a sort of rainbow visible to many observers besides Mr. Farebrother.", "이 모든 일은 피아노가 놓인 응접실 구석에서 벌어졌고, 그것이 아무리 미묘했다 한들, 그 빛은 그것을 페어브러더 씨 말고도 많은 사람들의 눈에 보이는 일종의 무지개로 만들어 놓았다."], ["The certainty that Miss Vincy and Mr. Lydgate were engaged became general in Middlemarch without the aid of formal announcement.", "빈시 양과 리드게이트 씨가 약혼했다는 확실한 사실은 정식 발표의 도움 없이도 미들마치에 두루 퍼졌다."]], [["Aunt Bulstrode was again stirred to anxiety; but this time she addressed herself to her brother, going to the warehouse expressly to avoid Mrs.", "불스트로드 이모는 다시금 마음이 불안해졌다. 하지만 이번에는 빈시 부인의 변덕스러움을 일부러 피하려고 창고로 직접 찾아가 오빠에게 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["Vincy’s volatility. His replies were not satisfactory.", "그의 대답은 만족스럽지 못했다."]], [["“Walter, you never mean to tell me that you have allowed all this to go on without inquiry into Mr. Lydgate’s prospects?”", "\"월터, 설마 리드게이트 씨의 장래에 대해 알아보지도 않고 이 모든 일이 진행되도록 내버려 뒀다는 건 아니겠죠?\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, opening her eyes with wider gravity at her brother, who was in his peevish warehouse humor.", "불스트로드 부인이 창고 일로 짜증이 나 있는 오빠를 향해 더욱 큰 진지함으로 눈을 크게 뜨며 말했다."], ["“Think of this girl brought up in luxury—in too worldly a way, I am sorry to say—what will she do on a small income?”", "\"사치스럽게, 미안한 말이지만 너무 세속적으로 자란 그 애를 생각해 봐요. 적은 수입으로 그 애가 어떻게 살아가겠어요?\""]], [["“Oh, confound it, Harriet!", "\"아, 빌어먹을, 해리엇!"], ["What can I do when men come into the town without any asking of mine?", "내가 청하지도 않았는데 사내들이 이 동네로 들어오는데 나더러 어쩌란 말이냐?"], ["Did you shut your house up against Lydgate?", "너는 리드게이트한테 네 집 문을 닫아걸기라도 했더냐?"], ["Bulstrode has pushed him forward more than anybody.", "불스트로드가 누구보다도 그를 밀어 줬어."], ["I never made any fuss about the young fellow.", "나는 그 젊은이에 대해 야단법석을 떤 적이 없다."], ["You should go and talk to your husband about it, not me.”", "그 문제는 나한테 올 게 아니라 네 남편한테 가서 이야기해야지.\""]], [["“Well, really, Walter, how can Mr. Bulstrode be to blame?", "\"하지만 정말, 월터, 불스트로드 씨가 어떻게 잘못이 있겠어요?"], ["I am sure he did not wish for the engagement.”", "그분은 분명히 이 약혼을 바라지 않으셨는데요.\""]], [["“Oh, if Bulstrode had not taken him by the hand, I should never have invited him.”", "\"아, 불스트로드가 그를 거두지 않았더라면 나는 그를 초대하는 일도 결코 없었을 거다.\""]], [["“But you called him in to attend on Fred, and I am sure that was a mercy,” said Mrs.", "\"하지만 오빠가 그분을 불러 프레드를 봐 달라고 했고, 저는 그게 다행한 일이었다고 생각해요.\""], ["Bulstrode, losing her clew in the intricacies of the subject.", "불스트로드 부인이 복잡한 화제 속에서 갈피를 놓치며 말했다."]], [["“I don’t know about mercy,”", "\"다행인지 어떤지는 모르겠다.\""], ["said Mr. Vincy, testily.", "빈시 씨가 신경질적으로 말했다."], ["“I know I am worried more than I like with my family.", "\"나는 내 식구들 때문에 내가 바라는 것보다 더 골치를 썩고 있다는 건 안다."], ["I was a good brother to you, Harriet, before you married Bulstrode, and I must say he doesn’t always show that friendly spirit towards your family that might have been expected of him.”", "해리엇, 나는 네가 불스트로드와 결혼하기 전에 너에게 좋은 오빠였어. 그런데 그자는 네 식구한테 마땅히 기대할 법한 그런 우애 어린 마음을 늘 보여 주는 건 아니라고 말할 수밖에 없구나.\""], ["Mr. Vincy was very little like a Jesuit, but no accomplished Jesuit could have turned a question more adroitly.", "빈시 씨는 예수회 수도사와는 거리가 멀었으나, 아무리 노련한 예수회 수도사라도 화제를 그보다 더 능란하게 돌려놓지는 못했을 것이다."], ["Harriet had to defend her husband instead of blaming her brother, and the conversation ended at a point as far from the beginning as some recent sparring between the brothers-in-law at a vestry meeting.", "해리엇은 오빠를 탓하는 대신 남편을 변호해야 했고, 대화는 시작점으로부터 멀찍이 떨어진 지점에서 끝나고 말았으니, 마치 최근에 교구위원회에서 두 사람이 벌인 옥신각신만큼이나 동떨어진 지점이었다."]], [["Mrs. Bulstrode did not repeat her brother’s complaints to her husband, but in the evening she spoke to him of Lydgate and Rosamond.", "불스트로드 부인은 오빠의 불평을 남편에게 되풀이하지 않았으나, 저녁에 리드게이트와 로저먼드 이야기를 그에게 꺼냈다."], ["He did not share her warm interest, however; and only spoke with resignation of the risks attendant on the beginning of medical practice and the desirability of prudence.", "하지만 그는 그녀의 뜨거운 관심을 함께 나누지 않았고, 다만 의료 개업 초기에 따르는 위험과 신중함의 필요성에 대해 체념한 듯 말할 따름이었다."]], [["“I am sure we are bound to pray for that thoughtless girl—brought up as she has been,”", "\"그렇게 자란 그 철없는 애를 위해 우리가 기도해야 한다는 건 분명해요.\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, wishing to rouse her husband’s feelings.", "불스트로드 부인이 남편의 감정을 일깨우고 싶어 하며 말했다."]], [["“Truly, my dear,” said Mr.", "\"참으로 그렇소, 여보.\""], ["Bulstrode, assentingly.", "불스트로드 씨가 동의하며 말했다."], ["“Those who are not of this world can do little else to arrest the errors of the obstinately worldly.", "\"이 세상에 속하지 않은 자들은 고집스레 세속적인 자들의 잘못을 막기 위해 달리 할 수 있는 일이 거의 없소."], ["That is what we must accustom ourselves to recognize with regard to your brother’s family. I could have wished that Mr.", "당신 오빠 식구에 관해서는 그 점을 인정하는 데 우리 스스로 익숙해져야 하오."], ["Lydgate had not entered into such a union; but my relations with him are limited to that use of his gifts for God’s purposes which is taught us by the divine government under each dispensation.”", "나는 리드게이트 씨가 그런 혼인을 맺지 않았더라면 했소만, 그와 나의 관계는 그가 지닌 재능을 하느님의 목적을 위해 쓰는 일에 한정되어 있소. 그것은 각 시대의 섭리 아래 신의 통치가 우리에게 가르치는 바요.\""]], [["Mrs. Bulstrode said no more, attributing some dissatisfaction which she felt to her own want of spirituality.", "불스트로드 부인은 더 이상 말하지 않았고, 자신이 느낀 어떤 불만을 자기 자신의 영성이 부족한 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["She believed that her husband was one of those men whose memoirs should be written when they died.", "그녀는 남편이 죽으면 그 회고록을 마땅히 써야 할 그런 사람들 중 하나라고 믿었다."]], [["As to Lydgate himself, having been accepted, he was prepared to accept all the consequences which he believed himself to foresee with perfect clearness.", "리드게이트 자신으로 말하자면, 약혼이 받아들여진 이상, 그는 자신이 완벽하게 명료히 내다본다고 믿는 모든 결과를 받아들일 각오가 되어 있었다."], ["Of course he must be married in a year—perhaps even in half a year.", "물론 그는 일 년 안에, 어쩌면 반년 안에라도 결혼해야 했다."], ["This was not what he had intended; but other schemes would not be hindered: they would simply adjust themselves anew.", "이것은 그가 의도했던 바가 아니었으나, 다른 계획들이 방해받지는 않을 터였다. 그저 새로이 조정되기만 하면 되었다."], ["Marriage, of course, must be prepared for in the usual way.", "결혼은 물론 통상적인 방식으로 준비해야 했다."], ["A house must be taken instead of the rooms he at present occupied; and Lydgate, having heard Rosamond speak with admiration of old Mrs. Bretton’s house (situated in Lowick Gate), took notice when it fell vacant after the old lady’s death, and immediately entered into treaty for it.", "지금 쓰고 있는 셋방 대신 집 한 채를 얻어야 했고, 리드게이트는 로저먼드가 (로윅 게이트에 자리한) 늙은 브레턴 부인의 집을 감탄하며 말하는 것을 들었던 터라, 그 노부인이 죽고 집이 비자 그것을 눈여겨보았다가 즉시 매입 협상에 들어갔다."]], [["He did this in an episodic way, very much as he gave orders to his tailor for every requisite of perfect dress, without any notion of being extravagant.", "그는 이 일을 마치 곁다리로 처리하듯, 완벽한 의복에 필요한 일체를 재단사에게 주문할 때와 똑같이 했으니, 사치를 부린다는 생각 따위는 전혀 없었다."], ["On the contrary, he would have despised any ostentation of expense; his profession had familiarized him with all grades of poverty, and he cared much for those who suffered hardships.", "오히려 그는 비용을 과시하는 짓은 경멸했을 것이다. 그의 직업은 그를 온갖 등급의 가난에 익숙해지게 했고, 그는 곤궁을 겪는 이들을 깊이 염려했다."], ["He would have behaved perfectly at a table where the sauce was served in a jug with the handle off, and he would have remembered nothing about a grand dinner except that a man was there who talked well.", "그는 손잡이가 떨어진 항아리에 소스가 담겨 나오는 식탁에서도 흠잡을 데 없이 처신했을 것이고, 성대한 만찬에 대해서도 말솜씨 좋은 사내가 한 사람 있었다는 것 말고는 아무것도 기억하지 못했을 것이다."], ["But it had never occurred to him that he should live in any other than what he would have called an ordinary way, with green glasses for hock, and excellent waiting at table.", "하지만 그는 자신이 이른바 평범한 방식 외에 다른 방식으로 살게 되리라고는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었으니, 그 평범한 방식이란 호크 포도주를 담는 초록빛 유리잔과 식탁에서의 훌륭한 시중을 갖춘 것이었다."], ["In warming himself at French social theories he had brought away no smell of scorching.", "프랑스 사회 이론으로 몸을 녹이면서도 그는 그을린 냄새는 조금도 묻혀 오지 않았다."], ["We may handle even extreme opinions with impunity while our furniture, our dinner-giving, and preference for armorial bearings in our own case, link us indissolubly with the established order.", "우리의 가구와 우리가 베푸는 만찬, 그리고 자기 경우에는 문장(紋章)을 선호하는 취향이 우리를 기성 질서에 풀 수 없이 묶어 두는 한, 우리는 극단적인 의견조차 탈 없이 다룰 수 있는 법이다. 그리고 리드게이트의 성향은 극단적인 의견 쪽으로 기울지 않았다."], ["And Lydgate’s tendency was not towards extreme opinions: he would have liked no barefooted doctrines, being particular about his boots: he was no radical in relation to anything but medical reform and the prosecution of discovery.", "그는 자기 부츠에 까다로운 사람이었던 만큼 맨발의 교리 따위는 좋아하지 않았을 것이다. 그는 의학 개혁과 발견의 추구를 제외하고는 무엇에 관해서도 급진주의자가 아니었다."], ["In the rest of practical life he walked by hereditary habit; half from that personal pride and unreflecting egoism which I have already called commonness, and half from that naivete which belonged to preoccupation with favorite ideas.", "나머지 실생활에서 그는 물려받은 습관에 따라 걸었으니, 그 절반은 내가 이미 통속성이라 일컬은 그 개인적 자존심과 무반성적인 이기심에서 비롯되었고, 나머지 절반은 좋아하는 생각에 골몰한 데서 나온 그 순진함에서 비롯되었다."]], [["Any inward debate Lydgate had as to the consequences of this engagement which had stolen upon him, turned on the paucity of time rather than of money.", "자기도 모르게 슬그머니 다가든 이 약혼의 결과에 대해 리드게이트가 마음속으로 벌인 논쟁이 있었다면, 그것은 돈의 부족보다는 시간의 부족을 둘러싼 것이었다."], ["Certainly, being in love and being expected continually by some one who always turned out to be prettier than memory could represent her to be, did interfere with the diligent use of spare hours which might serve some “plodding fellow of a German” to make the great, imminent discovery.", "분명, 사랑에 빠져 있고, 기억이 그려 내는 것보다 늘 더 예뻐 보이는 어떤 사람이 끊임없이 자기를 기다리고 있다는 사실은, 임박한 위대한 발견을 어느 \"꾸준한 독일인 친구\"가 이뤄 내는 데 쓰일 법한 여가 시간을 부지런히 활용하는 일을 방해했다."], ["This was really an argument for not deferring the marriage too long, as he implied to Mr. Farebrother, one day that the Vicar came to his room with some pond-products which he wanted to examine under a better microscope than his own, and, finding Lydgate’s tableful of apparatus and specimens in confusion, said sarcastically—", "이것은 결혼을 너무 오래 미루지 말아야 할 진짜 이유였으니, 어느 날 목사가 자기 것보다 더 좋은 현미경으로 들여다보고 싶은 연못 생물을 가지고 그의 방에 들렀다가, 리드게이트의 탁자가 기구와 표본으로 어지러운 것을 보고는 비꼬듯 말했을 때 그는 그런 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["“Eros has degenerated; he began by introducing order and harmony, and now he brings back chaos.”", "\"에로스가 타락했군. 처음에는 질서와 조화를 들여오더니, 이제는 혼돈을 도로 끌어오는구먼.\""]], [["“Yes, at some stages,”", "\"네, 어떤 단계에서는요.\""], ["said Lydgate, lifting his brows and smiling, while he began to arrange his microscope.", "리드게이트가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 미소 짓고는 현미경을 정돈하기 시작하며 말했다."], ["“But a better order will begin after.”", "\"하지만 그 후에는 더 나은 질서가 시작될 겁니다.\""]], [["“Soon?”", "\"곧?\""], ["said the Vicar.", "목사가 말했다."]], [["“I hope so, really.", "\"정말 그러기를 바랍니다."], ["This unsettled state of affairs uses up the time, and when one has notions in science, every moment is an opportunity.", "이 어수선한 상태가 시간을 잡아먹는데, 과학에 관해 어떤 착상을 품고 있을 때는 매 순간이 기회거든요."], ["I feel sure that marriage must be the best thing for a man who wants to work steadily.", "저는 꾸준히 일하고 싶은 사람에게 결혼이 분명 가장 좋은 것이라고 확신합니다."], ["He has everything at home then—no teasing with personal speculations—he can get calmness and freedom.”", "그러면 모든 것이 집 안에 갖춰져 있고, 개인적인 궁리로 시달릴 일도 없고, 평온과 자유를 얻을 수 있죠.\""]], [["“You are an enviable dog,”", "\"자네는 부러운 친구로군.\" 목사가 말했다."], ["said the Vicar, “to have such a prospect—Rosamond, calmness and freedom, all to your share.", "\"그런 전망을 가졌으니 말이야. 로저먼드, 평온, 자유, 죄다 자네 몫이지 않은가."], ["Here am I with nothing but my pipe and pond-animalcules.", "그런데 나는 내 파이프와 연못의 미생물밖에 없으니."], ["Now, are you ready?”", "자, 이제 준비됐나?\""]], [["Lydgate did not mention to the Vicar another reason he had for wishing to shorten the period of courtship.", "리드게이트는 구애 기간을 단축하고 싶은 또 다른 이유를 목사에게 말하지 않았다."], ["It was rather irritating to him, even with the wine of love in his veins, to be obliged to mingle so often with the family party at the Vincys’, and to enter so much into Middlemarch gossip, protracted good cheer, whist-playing, and general futility. He had to be deferential when Mr.", "사랑의 술이 핏줄을 타고 흐르는 와중에도, 빈시 가의 가족 모임에 그토록 자주 끼어들어, 미들마치의 잡담과 한없이 이어지는 환담과 휘스트 놀이와 두루 쓸데없는 짓에 그토록 깊이 빠져들어야 하는 것은 그에게 다소 짜증스러운 일이었다."], ["Vincy decided questions with trenchant ignorance, especially as to those liquors which were the best inward pickle, preserving you from the effects of bad air. Mrs.", "빈시 씨가 무지를 단칼에 휘두르며 문제들을 단정 지을 때, 특히 어떤 술이 나쁜 공기의 영향으로부터 사람을 지켜 주는 최고의 속 절임이 되느냐 하는 문제에 대해 그럴 때면, 그는 공손한 태도를 보여야 했다."], ["Vincy’s openness and simplicity were quite unstreaked with suspicion as to the subtle offence she might give to the taste of her intended son-in-law; and altogether Lydgate had to confess to himself that he was descending a little in relation to Rosamond’s family.", "빈시 부인의 솔직함과 단순함은 자신이 사위 될 사람의 취향에 끼칠지도 모를 미묘한 거슬림에 대한 의심이라곤 한 가닥도 섞여 있지 않았다. 그래서 대체로 리드게이트는 로저먼드의 집안과 관련해서는 자신이 조금 격이 떨어진다는 것을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["But that exquisite creature herself suffered in the same sort of way:—it was at least one delightful thought that in marrying her, he could give her a much-needed transplantation.", "하지만 그 빼어난 여인 자신도 같은 식으로 괴로움을 겪고 있었다. 그녀와 결혼함으로써 그녀에게 절실히 필요한 옮겨심기를 해 줄 수 있다는 것은 적어도 하나의 즐거운 생각이었다."]], [["“Dear!”", "\"여보!\""], ["he said to her one evening, in his gentlest tone, as he sat down by her and looked closely at her face—", "어느 날 저녁, 그가 그녀 곁에 앉아 그녀의 얼굴을 가까이 들여다보며 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말했다."]], [["But I must first say that he had found her alone in the drawing-room, where the great old-fashioned window, almost as large as the side of the room, was opened to the summer scents of the garden at the back of the house.", "하지만 먼저 말해 두어야겠다. 그는 그녀가 응접실에 혼자 있는 것을 보았는데, 거의 방 한쪽 벽만큼이나 큰 그 구식의 커다란 창문은 집 뒤편 정원의 여름 향기를 향해 열려 있었다."], ["Her father and mother were gone to a party, and the rest were all out with the butterflies.", "그녀의 아버지와 어머니는 파티에 가고 없었고, 나머지 식구들은 모두 나비를 쫓아 밖에 나가 있었다."]], [["“Dear!", "\"여보!"], ["your eyelids are red.”", "눈꺼풀이 빨갛군요.\""]], [["“Are they?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“I wonder why.”", "\"왜 그럴까요.\""], ["It was not in her nature to pour forth wishes or grievances.", "소망이나 불평을 쏟아 내는 것은 그녀의 천성이 아니었다."], ["They only came forth gracefully on solicitation.", "그것들은 청을 받을 때라야 우아하게 흘러나왔다."]], [["“As if you could hide it from me!”", "\"나한테 그걸 숨길 수 있다는 듯이!\""], ["said Lydgate, laying his hand tenderly on both of hers.", "리드게이트가 그녀의 두 손 위에 다정하게 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다."], ["“Don’t I see a tiny drop on one of the lashes?", "\"속눈썹 하나에 작은 물방울이 맺힌 게 안 보일 것 같소?"], ["Things trouble you, and you don’t tell me.", "무언가 당신을 괴롭히는데도 나한테 말하지 않다니."], ["That is unloving.”", "그건 사랑이 없는 거요.\""]], [["“Why should I tell you what you cannot alter?", "\"당신이 바꿀 수도 없는 걸 무엇하러 말하겠어요?"], ["They are every-day things:—perhaps they have been a little worse lately.”", "그건 날마다 있는 일들인걸요. 요즘 들어 좀 더 심해졌을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Family annoyances.", "\"집안의 골칫거리로군."], ["Don’t fear speaking.", "말하기를 두려워하지 마요."], ["I guess them.”", "짐작이 가니까.\""]], [["“Papa has been more irritable lately.", "\"아빠가 요즘 더 신경질적이에요."], ["Fred makes him angry, and this morning there was a fresh quarrel because Fred threatens to throw his whole education away, and do something quite beneath him.", "프레드가 아빠를 화나게 하고, 오늘 아침에도 또 새로 다툼이 있었어요. 프레드가 받은 교육을 전부 내팽개치고 자기 격에 한참 못 미치는 일을 하겠다고 으름장을 놓았거든요."], ["And besides—”", "그리고 또—\""]], [["Rosamond hesitated, and her cheeks were gathering a slight flush.", "로저먼드가 머뭇거렸고, 그녀의 뺨에는 옅은 홍조가 번지고 있었다."], ["Lydgate had never seen her in trouble since the morning of their engagement, and he had never felt so passionately towards her as at this moment.", "리드게이트는 약혼한 날 아침 이후로 그녀가 괴로워하는 모습을 본 적이 없었고, 이 순간만큼 그녀에게 열정적인 감정을 느껴 본 적도 없었다."], ["He kissed the hesitating lips gently, as if to encourage them.", "그는 마치 그 입술을 격려하기라도 하듯, 머뭇거리는 그 입술에 부드럽게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“I feel that papa is not quite pleased about our engagement,”", "\"아빠가 우리 약혼을 그다지 좋아하지 않으시는 것 같아요.\""], ["Rosamond continued, almost in a whisper; “and he said last night that he should certainly speak to you and say it must be given up.”", "로저먼드가 거의 속삭이듯 말을 이었다. \"그리고 어젯밤에는 당신에게 꼭 말씀하셔서, 약혼을 깨야 한다고 하시겠다고 했어요.\""]], [["“Will you give it up?”", "\"당신은 그걸 깰 거요?\""], ["said Lydgate, with quick energy—almost angrily.", "리드게이트가 빠르고 격하게, 거의 화난 듯이 말했다."]], [["“I never give up anything that I choose to do,”", "\"저는 제가 하기로 마음먹은 일은 절대 포기하지 않아요.\""], ["said Rosamond, recovering her calmness at the touching of this chord.", "로저먼드가 이 심금을 건드리는 말에 차분함을 되찾으며 말했다."]], [["“God bless you!”", "\"신의 축복을 받기를!\""], ["said Lydgate, kissing her again.", "리드게이트가 그녀에게 다시 입맞추며 말했다."], ["This constancy of purpose in the right place was adorable.", "올바른 자리에서 발휘되는 이 한결같은 의지는 사랑스러웠다."], ["He went on:—", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“It is too late now for your father to say that our engagement must be given up.", "\"이제 당신 아버지가 우리 약혼을 깨야 한다고 말하기에는 너무 늦었소."], ["You are of age, and I claim you as mine.", "당신은 성년이고, 나는 당신이 내 사람이라고 주장하오."], ["If anything is done to make you unhappy,—that is a reason for hastening our marriage.”", "무엇이든 당신을 불행하게 만드는 일이 생긴다면, 그건 우리 결혼을 서둘러야 할 이유요.\""]], [["An unmistakable delight shone forth from the blue eyes that met his, and the radiance seemed to light up all his future with mild sunshine.", "그의 눈과 마주친 푸른 눈동자에서 틀림없는 기쁨이 빛났고, 그 광채는 그의 모든 앞날을 온화한 햇살로 환히 비추는 듯했다."], ["Ideal happiness (of the kind known in the Arabian Nights, in which you are invited to step from the labor and discord of the street into a paradise where everything is given to you and nothing claimed) seemed to be an affair of a few weeks’ waiting, more or less.", "(『아라비안나이트』에 나오는 그런 종류의) 이상적인 행복, 곧 거리의 노동과 불화에서 벗어나 모든 것이 주어지되 아무것도 요구되지 않는 낙원으로 발을 들이라는 초대를 받는 그런 행복이, 길어야 몇 주를 기다리면 될 일처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["“Why should we defer it?”", "\"우리가 왜 그것을 미뤄야 하오?\""], ["he said, with ardent insistence.", "그가 열렬히 우기며 말했다."], ["“I have taken the house now: everything else can soon be got ready—can it not?", "\"이제 집도 얻었소. 나머지는 모두 곧 마련할 수 있을 거요."], ["You will not mind about new clothes.", "그렇지 않소? 당신은 새 옷에 연연하지 않겠지."], ["Those can be bought afterwards.”", "그런 건 나중에 사면 되니까.\""]], [["“What original notions you clever men have!”", "\"똑똑한 분들은 어쩌면 그렇게 독창적인 생각을 하시는지!\""], ["said Rosamond, dimpling with more thorough laughter than usual at this humorous incongruity.", "로저먼드가 이 우스꽝스러운 엇박자에 평소보다 한결 흐드러진 웃음으로 보조개를 지으며 말했다."], ["“This is the first time I ever heard of wedding-clothes being bought after marriage.”", "\"혼수 옷을 결혼한 뒤에 산다는 말은 난생처음 들어 봐요.\""]], [["“But you don’t mean to say you would insist on my waiting months for the sake of clothes?”", "\"그렇다고 설마 옷 때문에 나더러 몇 달이나 기다리라고 고집부리려는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["said Lydgate, half thinking that Rosamond was tormenting him prettily, and half fearing that she really shrank from speedy marriage.", "리드게이트가 로저먼드가 자기를 어여쁘게 애태우고 있는 거라고 반쯤 생각하면서도, 그녀가 정말로 성급한 결혼을 꺼리는 것은 아닐까 반쯤 두려워하며 말했다."], ["“Remember, we are looking forward to a better sort of happiness even than this—being continually together, independent of others, and ordering our lives as we will.", "\"기억해요, 우리는 이보다 더 나은 종류의 행복을 고대하고 있다는 걸. 늘 함께 있고, 남들에게 얽매이지 않고, 우리 뜻대로 삶을 꾸려 가는 행복 말이오."], ["Come, dear, tell me how soon you can be altogether mine.”", "자, 여보, 얼마나 빨리 온전히 내 사람이 될 수 있는지 말해 줘요.\""]], [["There was a serious pleading in Lydgate’s tone, as if he felt that she would be injuring him by any fantastic delays.", "리드게이트의 어조에는 진지한 호소가 담겨 있었으니, 마치 그녀가 변덕스러운 지연으로 자기에게 해를 입히리라 느끼는 듯했다."], ["Rosamond became serious too, and slightly meditative; in fact, she was going through many intricacies of lace-edging and hosiery and petticoat-tucking, in order to give an answer that would at least be approximative.", "로저먼드도 진지해졌고, 약간 생각에 잠겼다. 사실 그녀는 적어도 어림셈이라도 될 만한 대답을 내놓기 위해, 레이스 가장자리며 양말이며 페티코트 주름잡기의 온갖 복잡한 사정을 머릿속으로 헤아리고 있었다."]], [["“Six weeks would be ample—say so, Rosamond,”", "\"육 주면 넉넉할 거요. 그렇다고 말해 줘요, 로저먼드.\""], ["insisted Lydgate, releasing her hands to put his arm gently round her.", "리드게이트가 그녀의 손을 놓고 그녀의 허리에 가만히 팔을 두르며 우겼다."]], [["One little hand immediately went to pat her hair, while she gave her neck a meditative turn, and then said seriously—", "작은 한 손이 곧바로 머리를 매만지러 올라갔고, 그녀는 생각에 잠겨 목을 갸웃하더니 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“There would be the house-linen and the furniture to be prepared.", "\"집안 리넨이랑 가구를 마련해야 해요."], ["Still, mamma could see to those while we were away.”", "그래도 우리가 떠나 있는 동안 엄마가 그것들을 챙겨 주실 수 있겠죠.\""]], [["“Yes, to be sure.", "\"그럼, 물론이지."], ["We must be away a week or so.”", "우리는 일주일쯤 떠나 있어야 해요.\""]], [["“Oh, more than that!”", "\"어머, 그보다는 더요!\""], ["said Rosamond, earnestly.", "로저먼드가 간곡하게 말했다."], ["She was thinking of her evening dresses for the visit to Sir Godwin Lydgate’s, which she had long been secretly hoping for as a delightful employment of at least one quarter of the honeymoon, even if she deferred her introduction to the uncle who was a doctor of divinity (also a pleasing though sober kind of rank, when sustained by blood).", "그녀는 신혼여행의 적어도 사 분의 일은 즐거운 일로 채워 줄 것이라며 오랫동안 속으로 바라 온, 고드윈 리드게이트 경 댁을 방문할 때 입을 야회복을 생각하고 있었다. 신학 박사이기도 한 그 백부에게 인사드리는 일은 미뤄 두더라도 말이다(혈통으로 뒷받침될 때, 그 또한 차분하긴 해도 흐뭇한 종류의 지위였다)."], ["She looked at her lover with some wondering remonstrance as she spoke, and he readily understood that she might wish to lengthen the sweet time of double solitude.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하며 다소 의아한 항의의 눈길로 연인을 바라보았고, 그는 그녀가 둘만의 그 달콤한 고독의 시간을 늘리고 싶어 하는 것이리라고 선뜻 이해했다."]], [["“Whatever you wish, my darling, when the day is fixed.", "\"날짜만 정해지면 당신이 바라는 대로 하리다, 내 사랑."], ["But let us take a decided course, and put an end to any discomfort you may be suffering.", "하지만 단호한 방침을 정해서, 당신이 겪고 있을지 모를 불편을 끝내도록 합시다."], ["Six weeks!—I am sure they would be ample.”", "육 주면! 정말 넉넉할 거요.\""]], [["“I could certainly hasten the work,”", "\"준비를 서두를 수는 분명히 있을 거예요.\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“Will you, then, mention it to papa?—I think it would be better to write to him.”", "\"그럼 아빠에게 그 말씀을 드리시겠어요? 편지를 쓰시는 게 더 나을 것 같아요.\""], ["She blushed and looked at him as the garden flowers look at us when we walk forth happily among them in the transcendent evening light: is there not a soul beyond utterance, half nymph, half child, in those delicate petals which glow and breathe about the centres of deep color?", "그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 그를 바라보았는데, 그 모습은 우리가 더없이 찬란한 저녁 빛 속을 행복하게 거닐 때 정원의 꽃들이 우리를 바라보는 것 같았다. 깊은 빛깔의 중심부를 둘러싸고 환히 빛나며 숨 쉬는 그 가녀린 꽃잎들 속에는, 반은 요정이요 반은 어린아이인, 말로 다 할 수 없는 영혼이 깃들어 있지 않은가?"]], [["He touched her ear and a little bit of neck under it with his lips, and they sat quite still for many minutes which flowed by them like a small gurgling brook with the kisses of the sun upon it.", "그는 입술로 그녀의 귀와 그 아래 목의 작은 자리에 입을 맞추었고, 두 사람은 여러 분 동안 아주 가만히 앉아 있었으니, 그 시간은 햇살의 입맞춤을 받은 작은 졸졸 흐르는 시냇물처럼 그들 곁을 흘러갔다. 로저먼드는 자기만큼 사랑에 빠진 사람은 없으리라 생각했고, 리드게이트는 그 온갖 무모한 실수와 터무니없는 맹신 끝에 마침내 완벽한 여성성을 찾았노라고 생각했다."], ["Rosamond thought that no one could be more in love than she was; and Lydgate thought that after all his wild mistakes and absurd credulity, he had found perfect womanhood—felt as if already breathed upon by exquisite wedded affection such as would be bestowed by an accomplished creature who venerated his high musings and momentous labors and would never interfere with them; who would create order in the home and accounts with still magic, yet keep her fingers ready to touch the lute and transform life into romance at any moment; who was instructed to the true womanly limit and not a hair’s-breadth beyond—docile, therefore, and ready to carry out behests which came from that limit.", "자기의 드높은 사색과 중대한 노고를 우러르며 결코 그것을 방해하지 않을, 교양 있는 여인이 베풀어 줄 더없이 빼어난 부부의 애정이 벌써 자기에게 숨결처럼 와 닿는 것만 같았다. 그런 여인이라면 고요한 마법으로 집안과 가계부에 질서를 세우면서도, 류트를 어루만질 손가락은 언제든 준비해 두어 어느 순간에든 삶을 로맨스로 바꿔 놓을 것이고, 참된 여성의 한계까지, 그러나 터럭 한 올만큼도 그 너머로는 가지 않게끔 교육받았기에, 따라서 고분고분하고, 그 한계에서 비롯된 분부라면 기꺼이 받들 터였다."], ["It was plainer now than ever that his notion of remaining much longer a bachelor had been a mistake: marriage would not be an obstruction but a furtherance.", "자기가 훨씬 더 오래 독신으로 지내겠다던 생각이 잘못이었음이 이제 그 어느 때보다 분명해졌다. 결혼은 방해물이 아니라 진척을 돕는 것일 터였다."], ["And happening the next day to accompany a patient to Brassing, he saw a dinner-service there which struck him as so exactly the right thing that he bought it at once.", "그리고 이튿날 마침 환자 한 사람을 따라 브래싱에 갔다가, 그는 거기서 정말 안성맞춤이라 여겨지는 만찬용 식기 세트를 보고는 그 자리에서 사 버렸다."], ["It saved time to do these things just when you thought of them, and Lydgate hated ugly crockery.", "생각났을 때 바로 이런 일들을 해 치우는 편이 시간을 아꼈고, 리드게이트는 보기 흉한 그릇을 싫어했다."], ["The dinner-service in question was expensive, but that might be in the nature of dinner-services.", "문제의 그 식기 세트는 값이 비쌌으나, 그것은 만찬용 식기 세트의 본성상 그런 것일 수도 있었다."], ["Furnishing was necessarily expensive; but then it had to be done only once.", "살림살이를 갖추는 데는 으레 돈이 들기 마련이었다. 하지만 그것은 단 한 번만 하면 되는 일이었다."]], [["“It must be lovely,” said Mrs.", "\"분명 사랑스러울 거예요.\""], ["Vincy, when Lydgate mentioned his purchase with some descriptive touches.", "리드게이트가 자신이 산 물건을 몇 마디 묘사를 곁들여 이야기하자 빈시 부인이 말했다."], ["“Just what Rosy ought to have.", "\"우리 로지에게 꼭 어울리는 거죠."], ["I trust in heaven it won’t be broken!”", "부디 깨지지 않기를 빌어요!\""]], [["“One must hire servants who will not break things,”", "\"물건을 깨뜨리지 않을 하인을 들이면 됩니다.\""], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["(Certainly, this was reasoning with an imperfect vision of sequences.", "(분명 이것은 인과의 연쇄를 불완전하게 내다본 추론이었다."], ["But at that period there was no sort of reasoning which was not more or less sanctioned by men of science.)", "하지만 그 시절에는 과학자들에게 다소간 용인되지 않는 추론이란 없었다.)"]], [["Of course it was unnecessary to defer the mention of anything to mamma, who did not readily take views that were not cheerful, and being a happy wife herself, had hardly any feeling but pride in her daughter’s marriage.", "물론 어머니에게는 무엇이든 알리기를 미룰 필요가 없었으니, 그녀는 명랑하지 않은 견해는 좀처럼 받아들이지 않았고, 자신이 행복한 아내였던 만큼 딸의 결혼에 대해서도 자랑스러운 마음 외에는 거의 다른 감정이 없었다."], ["But Rosamond had good reasons for suggesting to Lydgate that papa should be appealed to in writing.", "하지만 로저먼드에게는 아빠에게 편지로 청해야 한다고 리드게이트에게 제안할 만한 그럴듯한 이유가 있었다."], ["She prepared for the arrival of the letter by walking with her papa to the warehouse the next morning, and telling him on the way that Mr. Lydgate wished to be married soon.", "그녀는 이튿날 아침 아빠와 함께 창고까지 걸어가면서, 가는 길에 리드게이트 씨가 곧 결혼하고 싶어 한다고 그에게 말함으로써 편지의 도착에 대비했다."]], [["“Nonsense, my dear!” said Mr.", "\"말도 안 되는 소리, 얘야!\""], ["Vincy. “What has he got to marry on?", "빈시 씨가 말했다."], ["You’d much better give up the engagement.", "\"그 사람이 무슨 밑천으로 결혼을 하겠다는 거냐?"], ["I’ve told you so pretty plainly before this.", "너는 그 약혼을 그만두는 편이 훨씬 나아."], ["What have you had such an education for, if you are to go and marry a poor man?", "전에도 꽤 분명하게 말했잖니. 가난한 사람한테 시집가려고 한다면, 그런 교육은 무엇하러 받았단 말이냐?"], ["It’s a cruel thing for a father to see.”", "아비로서 보기에 참 매정한 일이구나.\""]], [["“Mr. Lydgate is not poor, papa.", "\"리드게이트 씨는 가난하지 않아요, 아빠."], ["He bought Mr. Peacock’s practice, which, they say, is worth eight or nine hundred a-year.”", "그분은 피콕 씨의 개업권을 사들였는데, 그게 연 팔구백 파운드의 값어치가 있다고들 해요.\""]], [["“Stuff and nonsense!", "\"허튼소리 마라!"], ["What’s buying a practice?", "개업권을 사는 게 뭐 대수냐?"], ["He might as well buy next year’s swallows.", "내년에 올 제비를 사는 거나 다름없다."], ["It’ll all slip through his fingers.”", "죄다 손가락 사이로 빠져나갈 게다.\""]], [["“On the contrary, papa, he will increase the practice.", "\"그 반대예요, 아빠. 그분은 개업을 키워 갈 거예요."], ["See how he has been called in by the Chettams and Casaubons.”", "보세요, 체텀 가와 캐소본 가에서도 그분을 불렀잖아요.\""]], [["“I hope he knows I shan’t give anything—with this disappointment about Fred, and Parliament going to be dissolved, and machine-breaking everywhere, and an election coming on—”", "\"프레드 일로 이렇게 실망스러운 데다, 의회는 해산될 참이고, 사방에서 기계를 부수고 있고, 선거까지 다가오는 마당에, 내가 한 푼도 안 줄 거라는 걸 그 사람이 알고 있기를 바란다.\""]], [["“Dear papa!", "\"아빠도 참!"], ["what can that have to do with my marriage?”", "그게 제 결혼과 무슨 상관이 있어요?\""]], [["“A pretty deal to do with it!", "\"상관이 아주 많지!"], ["We may all be ruined for what I know—the country’s in that state!", "내가 알기로 우리 모두 거덜 날지도 몰라. 나라가 그 지경이라니까!"], ["Some say it’s the end of the world, and be hanged if I don’t think it looks like it!", "어떤 이들은 세상의 종말이라고도 하는데, 정말 그렇게 보이지 않으면 내 목을 내놓겠다!"], ["Anyhow, it’s not a time for me to be drawing money out of my business, and I should wish Lydgate to know that.”", "어쨌든 지금은 내가 사업에서 돈을 빼낼 때가 아니고, 나는 리드게이트가 그걸 알았으면 한다.\""]], [["“I am sure he expects nothing, papa.", "\"그분은 아무것도 기대하지 않으세요, 아빠."], ["And he has such very high connections: he is sure to rise in one way or another.", "그리고 그분은 인맥이 아주 좋아서, 어떻게든 출세하실 게 분명해요."], ["He is engaged in making scientific discoveries.”", "그분은 과학적인 발견에 매진하고 계세요.\""]], [["Mr. Vincy was silent.", "빈시 씨는 입을 다물었다."]], [["“I cannot give up my only prospect of happiness, papa. Mr.", "\"저는 제 유일한 행복의 전망을 포기할 수 없어요, 아빠."], ["Lydgate is a gentleman.", "리드게이트 씨는 신사예요."], ["I could never love any one who was not a perfect gentleman.", "저는 완벽한 신사가 아닌 사람은 결코 사랑할 수 없을 거예요."], ["You would not like me to go into a consumption, as Arabella Hawley did.", "제가 아라벨라 홀리처럼 폐병에 걸리는 걸 바라지는 않으시겠죠."], ["And you know that I never change my mind.”", "그리고 제가 한번 먹은 마음을 바꾸는 법이 없다는 걸 아시잖아요.\""]], [["Again papa was silent.", "다시 아빠는 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Promise me, papa, that you will consent to what we wish.", "\"약속해 주세요, 아빠, 우리가 바라는 대로 동의해 주시겠다고요."], ["We shall never give each other up; and you know that you have always objected to long courtships and late marriages.”", "우리는 결코 서로를 포기하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 아빠는 늘 긴 구애와 늦은 결혼에 반대하셨잖아요.\""]], [["There was a little more urgency of this kind, till Mr.", "이런 식의 채근이 조금 더 이어지다가, 빈시 씨가 \"그래, 그래, 얘야, 내가 답하기 전에 그 사람이 먼저 나한테 편지를 써야지\""], ["Vincy said, “Well, well, child, he must write to me first before I can answer him,”—and Rosamond was certain that she had gained her point.", "하고 말했고, 로저먼드는 자기가 뜻을 이루었음을 확신했다."]], [["Mr. Vincy’s answer consisted chiefly in a demand that Lydgate should insure his life—a demand immediately conceded.", "빈시 씨의 답장은 주로 리드게이트가 생명보험에 가입해야 한다는 요구로 이루어졌고, 그 요구는 즉각 받아들여졌다."], ["This was a delightfully reassuring idea supposing that Lydgate died, but in the mean time not a self-supporting idea.", "이는 리드게이트가 죽는 경우를 상정한다면 더없이 안심되는 발상이었으나, 당장에는 스스로를 부양해 줄 발상은 아니었다."], ["However, it seemed to make everything comfortable about Rosamond’s marriage; and the necessary purchases went on with much spirit.", "그래도 그것은 로저먼드의 결혼에 관한 모든 일을 편안하게 만들어 주는 듯했고, 필요한 구매들은 한껏 활기차게 진행되었다."], ["Not without prudential considerations, however.", "그렇다고 신중한 고려가 없지는 않았다."], ["A bride (who is going to visit at a baronet’s) must have a few first-rate pocket-handkerchiefs; but beyond the absolutely necessary half-dozen, Rosamond contented herself without the very highest style of embroidery and Valenciennes.", "신부는 (준남작 댁을 방문하러 가는 신부는) 일류 손수건을 몇 장 갖추어야 한다. 하지만 반드시 필요한 여섯 장 외에는, 로저먼드도 최상급의 자수와 발랑시엔 레이스까지는 갖추지 않는 것으로 만족했다."], ["Lydgate also, finding that his sum of eight hundred pounds had been considerably reduced since he had come to Middlemarch, restrained his inclination for some plate of an old pattern which was shown to him when he went into Kibble’s establishment at Brassing to buy forks and spoons. He was too proud to act as if he presupposed that Mr.", "리드게이트 역시, 미들마치에 온 이후로 자신의 팔백 파운드가 상당히 줄어든 것을 알고는, 포크와 숟가락을 사러 브래싱의 키블 상점에 들어갔을 때 그에게 보여 준 고풍스러운 무늬의 은식기에 대한 욕심을 억눌렀다."], ["Vincy would advance money to provide furniture; and though, since it would not be necessary to pay for everything at once, some bills would be left standing over, he did not waste time in conjecturing how much his father-in-law would give in the form of dowry, to make payment easy.", "그는 자존심이 강해서, 빈시 씨가 가구를 마련하라고 돈을 대 주리라 미리 짐작하는 듯 행동할 수 없었다. 그리고 모든 것을 한꺼번에 치를 필요는 없을 것이므로 미결제로 남을 청구서도 좀 있겠지만, 그는 장인이 지불을 수월하게 해 주려고 지참금 형태로 얼마나 내놓을지 따위를 짐작하느라 시간을 허비하지 않았다."], ["He was not going to do anything extravagant, but the requisite things must be bought, and it would be bad economy to buy them of a poor quality.", "그는 사치를 부릴 생각은 없었으나, 필요한 물건들은 사야 했고, 그것들을 질 낮은 것으로 사는 것은 잘못된 절약일 터였다."], ["All these matters were by the bye.", "이 모든 일은 곁가지에 불과했다."], ["Lydgate foresaw that science and his profession were the objects he should alone pursue enthusiastically; but he could not imagine himself pursuing them in such a home as Wrench had—the doors all open, the oil-cloth worn, the children in soiled pinafores, and lunch lingering in the form of bones, black-handled knives, and willow-pattern.", "리드게이트는 과학과 자신의 직업만이 자기가 열정적으로 추구해야 할 대상임을 내다보았다. 하지만 그는 렌치 같은 가정에서 그것들을 추구하는 자신을 도무지 상상할 수 없었으니, 그 집은 문이라는 문은 죄다 열려 있고, 방수포는 닳아 해졌으며, 아이들은 더러운 앞치마를 두르고, 점심은 뼈다귀며 검은 손잡이 칼이며 버드나무 무늬 그릇의 형태로 어수선하게 남아 있었다."], ["But Wrench had a wretched lymphatic wife who made a mummy of herself indoors in a large shawl; and he must have altogether begun with an ill-chosen domestic apparatus.", "하지만 렌치에게는 큼지막한 숄을 두른 채 집 안에서 미라처럼 지내는 가련하고 림프질의 아내가 있었다. 그러니 그는 애초에 잘못 고른 살림 도구로 시작했음이 틀림없었다."]], [["Rosamond, however, was on her side much occupied with conjectures, though her quick imitative perception warned her against betraying them too crudely.", "그렇지만 로저먼드도 그녀대로 갖가지 추측에 골몰해 있었으니, 다만 흉내 내는 데 빠른 그녀의 감각은 그것들을 너무 노골적으로 드러내지 않도록 그녀에게 경계심을 일러 주었다."]], [["“I shall like so much to know your family,”", "\"당신 가족을 알게 되면 정말 좋겠어요.\""], ["she said one day, when the wedding journey was being discussed.", "어느 날 신혼여행 이야기가 나왔을 때 그녀가 말했다."], ["“We might perhaps take a direction that would allow us to see them as we returned.", "\"어쩌면 돌아오는 길에 그분들을 뵐 수 있는 쪽으로 방향을 잡을 수도 있겠죠."], ["Which of your uncles do you like best?”", "백부님들 중에 누구를 가장 좋아하세요?\""]], [["“Oh,—my uncle Godwin, I think.", "\"아, 고드윈 백부님이지 싶소."], ["He is a good-natured old fellow.”", "마음씨 좋은 노인이거든.\""]], [["“You were constantly at his house at Quallingham, when you were a boy, were you not?", "\"어렸을 때 콸링엄의 그분 댁에 늘 가 계셨다면서요, 그렇죠?"], ["I should so like to see the old spot and everything you were used to.", "그 옛 장소며 당신이 익숙하게 지내던 모든 걸 정말 보고 싶어요."], ["Does he know you are going to be married?”", "당신이 결혼한다는 걸 그분이 아시나요?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["said Lydgate, carelessly, turning in his chair and rubbing his hair up.", "리드게이트가 무심하게 말하며, 의자에서 몸을 돌려 머리를 위로 쓸어 넘겼다."]], [["“Do send him word of it, you naughty undutiful nephew.", "\"꼭 알려 드리세요, 당신 이 못된 불효 조카님."], ["He will perhaps ask you to take me to Quallingham; and then you could show me about the grounds, and I could imagine you there when you were a boy.", "그분이 어쩌면 저를 콸링엄으로 데려오라고 하실지도 모르잖아요. 그러면 당신이 저에게 그 영지를 보여 주실 수 있고, 저는 거기 있던 어린 시절의 당신을 그려 볼 수 있겠죠."], ["Remember, you see me in my home, just as it has been since I was a child.", "기억하세요, 당신은 어릴 적부터 한결같은 모습 그대로의 제 집에서 저를 보고 있다는 걸요."], ["It is not fair that I should be so ignorant of yours.", "제가 당신의 집을 이토록 모르고 지내는 건 공평하지 않아요."], ["But perhaps you would be a little ashamed of me.", "하지만 어쩌면 당신은 저를 좀 부끄러워하실지도 모르겠네요."], ["I forgot that.”", "그걸 깜빡했어요.\""]], [["Lydgate smiled at her tenderly, and really accepted the suggestion that the proud pleasure of showing so charming a bride was worth some trouble.", "리드게이트는 그녀에게 다정하게 미소 지었고, 이토록 매혹적인 신부를 보여 주는 뿌듯한 즐거움이 약간의 수고를 들일 만한 가치가 있다는 그 제안을 정말로 받아들였다."], ["And now he came to think of it, he would like to see the old spots with Rosamond.", "그리고 막상 그렇게 생각하고 보니, 그도 로저먼드와 함께 그 옛 장소들을 보고 싶었다."]], [["“I will write to him, then.", "\"그럼 그분께 편지를 쓰겠소."], ["But my cousins are bores.”", "하지만 내 사촌들은 따분한 작자들이오.\""]], [["It seemed magnificent to Rosamond to be able to speak so slightingly of a baronet’s family, and she felt much contentment in the prospect of being able to estimate them contemptuously on her own account.", "준남작의 가족을 그토록 깔보듯 말할 수 있다는 것이 로저먼드에게는 근사하게 여겨졌고, 자기 나름으로 그들을 경멸하듯 가늠할 수 있으리라는 전망에 그녀는 큰 만족을 느꼈다."]], [["But mamma was near spoiling all, a day or two later, by saying—", "하지만 하루 이틀 뒤에 엄마가 모든 것을 거의 망쳐 놓을 뻔했으니, 이렇게 말했던 것이다."]], [["“I hope your uncle Sir Godwin will not look down on Rosy, Mr. Lydgate.", "\"백부님이신 고드윈 경께서 우리 로지를 깔보지 않으시기를 바라요, 리드게이트 씨."], ["I should think he would do something handsome.", "그분이라면 뭔가 후하게 해 주실 것 같은데요."], ["A thousand or two can be nothing to a baronet.”", "천 파운드나 이천 파운드쯤이야 준남작에게는 아무것도 아닐 테니까요.\""]], [["“Mamma!”", "\"엄마!\""], ["said Rosamond, blushing deeply; and Lydgate pitied her so much that he remained silent and went to the other end of the room to examine a print curiously, as if he had been absent-minded.", "로저먼드가 얼굴을 새빨갛게 붉히며 말했다. 그리고 리드게이트는 그녀가 너무도 가여워서, 아무 말 없이 방 저쪽 끝으로 가서 마치 정신이 딴 데 팔린 양 판화 한 점을 신기한 듯 들여다보았다."], ["Mamma had a little filial lecture afterwards, and was docile as usual.", "엄마는 그 후에 딸에게 약간의 잔소리를 들었고, 여느 때처럼 고분고분했다."], ["But Rosamond reflected that if any of those high-bred cousins who were bores, should be induced to visit Middlemarch, they would see many things in her own family which might shock them.", "하지만 로저먼드는 따분한 작자들인 그 지체 높은 사촌들 가운데 누구라도 미들마치를 방문하게 된다면, 자기 집안에서 그들에게 충격을 줄 만한 것들을 많이 보게 되리라고 생각했다."], ["Hence it seemed desirable that Lydgate should by-and-by get some first-rate position elsewhere than in Middlemarch; and this could hardly be difficult in the case of a man who had a titled uncle and could make discoveries.", "그러니 리드게이트가 머지않아 미들마치가 아닌 다른 곳에서 일류의 지위를 얻는 것이 바람직해 보였다. 그리고 작위를 가진 백부가 있고 발견을 해낼 수 있는 사람의 경우라면, 이는 그리 어려운 일이 아닐 터였다."], ["Lydgate, you perceive, had talked fervidly to Rosamond of his hopes as to the highest uses of his life, and had found it delightful to be listened to by a creature who would bring him the sweet furtherance of satisfying affection—beauty—repose—such help as our thoughts get from the summer sky and the flower-fringed meadows.", "알다시피 리드게이트는 자기 삶의 가장 드높은 쓰임에 관한 희망을 로저먼드에게 열렬히 이야기했고, 흡족한 애정과 아름다움과 안식이라는 달콤한 진척을, 곧 여름 하늘과 꽃 두른 초원에서 우리의 생각이 얻는 그런 도움을 가져다줄 여인이 자기 말에 귀 기울여 주는 것을 즐거워했다."]], [["Lydgate relied much on the psychological difference between what for the sake of variety I will call goose and gander: especially on the innate submissiveness of the goose as beautifully corresponding to the strength of the gander.", "리드게이트는 변화를 위해 내가 암컷 거위와 수컷 거위라 부르겠다는 양자 사이의 심리적 차이에 크게 기대고 있었다. 특히 암컷 거위의 타고난 순종성이 수컷 거위의 힘에 더없이 아름답게 호응한다는 점에 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVII.", "# 제34장"]], [["Thrice happy she that is so well assured Unto herself and settled so in heart That neither will for better be allured Ne fears to worse with any chance to start, But like a steddy ship doth strongly part The raging waves and keeps her course aright; Ne aught for tempest doth from it depart, Ne aught for fairer weather’s false delight. Such self-assurance need not fear the spight Of grudging foes; ne favour seek of friends; But in the stay of her own stedfast might Neither to one herself nor other bends. Most happy she that most assured doth rest, But he most happy who such one loves best. —SPENSER. The doubt hinted by Mr. Vincy whether it were only the general election or the end of the world that was coming on, now that George the Fourth was dead, Parliament dissolved, Wellington and Peel generally depreciated and the new King apologetic, was a feeble type of the uncertainties in provincial opinion at that time. With the glow-worm lights of country places, how could men see which were their own thoughts in the confusion of a Tory Ministry passing Liberal measures, of Tory nobles and electors being anxious to return Liberals rather than friends of the recreant Ministers, and of outcries for remedies which seemed to have a mysteriously remote bearing on private interest, and were made suspicious by the advocacy of disagreeable neighbors? Buyers of the Middlemarch newspapers found themselves in an anomalous position: during the agitation on the Catholic Question many had given up the “Pioneer”—which had a motto from Charles James Fox and was in the van of progress—because it had taken Peel’s side about the Papists, and had thus blotted its Liberalism with a toleration of Jesuitry and Baal; but they were ill-satisfied with the “Trumpet,” which—since its blasts against Rome, and in the general flaccidity of the public mind (nobody knowing who would support whom)—had become feeble in its blowing.", "어떤 운명으로도 더 나쁜 데로 끌려갈까 두려워하지 않으며"]], [["It was a time, according to a noticeable article in the “Pioneer,” when the crying needs of the country might well counteract a reluctance to public action on the part of men whose minds had from long experience acquired breadth as well as concentration, decision of judgment as well as tolerance, dispassionateness as well as energy—in fact, all those qualities which in the melancholy experience of mankind have been the least disposed to share lodgings.", "든든한 배처럼 굳세게 사나운 물결을\n가르고 제 항로를 곧게 지키는 그녀여."]], [["Mr. Hackbutt, whose fluent speech was at that time floating more widely than usual, and leaving much uncertainty as to its ultimate channel, was heard to say in Mr. Hawley’s office that the article in question “emanated” from Brooke of Tipton, and that Brooke had secretly bought the “Pioneer” some months ago.", "폭풍우에도 그 길에서 벗어나지 않고\n더 맑은 날씨의 거짓 기쁨에도 흔들리지 않으니."]], [["“That means mischief, eh?” said Mr. Hawley. “He’s got the freak of being a popular man now, after dangling about like a stray tortoise. So much the worse for him. I’ve had my eye on him for some time. He shall be prettily pumped upon. He’s a damned bad landlord. What business has an old county man to come currying favor with a low set of dark-blue freemen? As to his paper, I only hope he may do the writing himself. It would be worth our paying for.”", "그런 자기 확신은 시샘하는 적들의 악의를"]], [["“I understand he has got a very brilliant young fellow to edit it, who can write the highest style of leading article, quite equal to anything in the London papers.", "두려워할 필요도, 벗들의 호의를 구할 필요도 없도다."], ["And he means to take very high ground on Reform.”", "오직 제 한결같은 힘에 의지하여"]], [["“Let Brooke reform his rent-roll. He’s a cursed old screw, and the buildings all over his estate are going to rack.", "누구에게도 자신을 굽히지 않으니."], ["I suppose this young fellow is some loose fish from London.”", "가장 든든히 쉬는 그녀가 가장 행복하나,"]], [["“His name is Ladislaw.", "그런 이를 가장 사랑하는 그가 가장 행복하리라."], ["He is said to be of foreign extraction.”", "—스펜서."]], [["“I know the sort,” said Mr.", "조지 4세가 죽고 의회가 해산되고 웰링턴과 필이 대체로 평가절하되고 새 왕이 사죄의 태도를 보이는 지금, 다가오는 것이 단지 총선거인지 아니면 세상의 종말인지 빈시 씨가 넌지시 던진 그 의문은 당시 지방 여론의 불확실성을 보여 주는 미약한 한 예에 지나지 않았다."], ["Hawley; “some emissary. He’ll begin with flourishing about the Rights of Man and end with murdering a wench.", "시골의 반딧불 같은 희미한 빛 속에서, 자유주의적 법안을 통과시키는 토리당 내각, 변절한 장관들의 친구보다 차라리 자유주의자를 의회에 보내고 싶어 안달하는 토리당 귀족과 유권자들, 그리고 사적인 이해와는 신비로울 만큼 동떨어진 듯하면서도 마뜩잖은 이웃이 옹호하는 까닭에 의심스러워진 개혁 요구의 외침이 뒤엉킨 혼란 속에서, 사람들이 어찌 어느 것이 자기 자신의 생각인지 분간할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["That’s the style.” “You must concede that there are abuses, Hawley,”", "미들마치의 신문을 사는 이들은 기묘한 처지에 놓여 있었다."], ["said Mr. Hackbutt, foreseeing some political disagreement with his family lawyer.", "가톨릭 문제로 동요가 일던 동안 많은 이들이 「개척자」를 끊어 버렸는데—이 신문은 찰스 제임스 폭스의 경구를 표어로 내걸고 진보의 선봉에 서 있었으나—교황주의자들에 관해 필의 편을 들어 예수회주의와 바알에 대한 관용으로 자기 자유주의에 오점을 남겼기 때문이었다."], ["“I myself should never favor immoderate views—in fact I take my stand with Huskisson—but I cannot blind myself to the consideration that the non-representation of large towns—”", "하지만 그들은 「나팔」에도 만족하지 못했으니, 로마를 향해 나팔을 불어 댄 이래로, 그리고 누가 누구를 지지할지 아무도 모르는 일반적인 여론의 무기력함 속에서 그 나팔 소리가 미약해져 버린 까닭이었다."]], [["“Large towns be damned!” said Mr.", "「개척자」의 한 주목할 만한 기사에 따르면, 그것은 나라의 절박한 필요가, 오랜 경험을 통해 집중력뿐 아니라 폭넓음을, 관용뿐 아니라 판단의 결단력을, 정력뿐 아니라 냉정함을—사실상 인류의 우울한 경험 속에서 한 거처에 좀처럼 함께 머물려 하지 않았던 그 모든 자질을—갖춘 사람들이 공적 행동을 꺼리는 마음을 능히 상쇄할 법한 시기였다."], ["Hawley, impatient of exposition. “I know a little too much about Middlemarch elections.", "당시 평소보다 더 널리 떠돌면서 그 최종 물길이 어디인지 매우 불확실하게 만들던 유창한 언변의 소유자 핵벗 씨는, 홀리 씨의 사무실에서 문제의 그 기사가 티프턴의 브룩에게서 \"흘러나온\""], ["Let ’em quash every pocket borough to-morrow, and bring in every mushroom town in the kingdom—they’ll only increase the expense of getting into Parliament. I go upon facts.”", "것이며 브룩이 몇 달 전에 「개척자」를 은밀히 사들였다고 말하는 것이 들렸다."]], [["Mr. Hawley’s disgust at the notion of the “Pioneer”", "\"그건 말썽을 부리겠다는 거지, 응?\""], ["being edited by an emissary, and of Brooke becoming actively political—as if a tortoise of desultory pursuits should protrude its small head ambitiously and become rampant—was hardly equal to the annoyance felt by some members of Mr.", "홀리 씨가 말했다. \"길 잃은 거북처럼 어슬렁거리더니 이제 인기 있는 사람이 되어 보겠다는 변덕이 든 거요."], ["Brooke’s own family.", "그자한테는 그만큼 더 나쁜 일이지. 한동안 그자를 지켜봐 왔소."], ["The result had oozed forth gradually, like the discovery that your neighbor has set up an unpleasant kind of manufacture which will be permanently under your nostrils without legal remedy.", "톡톡히 망신을 당하게 될 거요. 그자는 지독하게 형편없는 지주거든."], ["The “Pioneer”", "오래된 시골 양반이 무슨 까닭으로 비천한 무리의 짙푸른 자유민들에게 알랑거리러 온단 말이오?"], ["had been secretly bought even before Will Ladislaw’s arrival, the expected opportunity having offered itself in the readiness of the proprietor to part with a valuable property which did not pay; and in the interval since Mr.", "그 신문으로 말하자면, 그저 글을 직접 쓰기나 바랄 뿐이오. 그렇다면 우리가 돈을 낼 만한 가치가 있을 테니.\""], ["Brooke had written his invitation, those germinal ideas of making his mind tell upon the world at large which had been present in him from his younger years, but had hitherto lain in some obstruction, had been sprouting under cover.", "\"듣자 하니 아주 영리한 젊은이를 편집자로 들였다는데, 런던 신문에 실린 어떤 것에도 뒤지지 않을 최고 수준의 사설을 쓸 수 있다는군요. 그리고 개혁에 관해 아주 높은 입장을 취할 작정이라고 하오.\""]], [["The development was much furthered by a delight in his guest which proved greater even than he had anticipated.", "\"브룩더러 자기 소작료 장부나 개혁하라고 하시오. 그자는 빌어먹을 늙은 구두쇠고, 사유지 곳곳의 건물이 죄다 허물어져 가고 있소."], ["For it seemed that Will was not only at home in all those artistic and literary subjects which Mr.", "그 젊은이라는 작자도 런던에서 굴러온 어중이떠중이 한 명이겠지.\""], ["Brooke had gone into at one time, but that he was strikingly ready at seizing the points of the political situation, and dealing with them in that large spirit which, aided by adequate memory, lends itself to quotation and general effectiveness of treatment.", "\"이름이 래디슬로라고 합니다. 외국 혈통이라는 말이 있더군요.\""]], [["“He seems to me a kind of Shelley, you know,” Mr.", "\"그런 부류는 내가 알지.\""], ["Brooke took an opportunity of saying, for the gratification of Mr.", "홀리 씨가 말했다. \"어떤 밀정 같은 자 말이오."], ["Casaubon. “I don’t mean as to anything objectionable—laxities or atheism, or anything of that kind, you know—Ladislaw’s sentiments in every way I am sure are good—indeed, we were talking a great deal together last night.", "인권을 떠벌리며 시작했다가 끝에 가선 계집을 하나 죽이겠지."], ["But he has the same sort of enthusiasm for liberty, freedom, emancipation—a fine thing under guidance—under guidance, you know.", "그게 그 부류의 방식이오.\" \"폐단이 있다는 건 인정해야 하오, 홀리.\""], ["I think I shall be able to put him on the right tack; and I am the more pleased because he is a relation of yours, Casaubon.”", "핵벗 씨가, 자기 집안의 변호사와 정치적 의견 충돌이 생기리라 예감하며 말했다. \"나 자신도 과격한 견해를 결코 옹호하지 않을 것이오—사실 나는 허스키슨과 입장을 같이하지만—큰 도시들이 대표되지 못한다는 점은 못 본 척할 수가 없단 말이오—\""]], [["If the right tack implied anything more precise than the rest of Mr.", "\"큰 도시고 나발이고!\" 설명을 못 참는 홀리 씨가 말했다."], ["Brooke’s speech, Mr. Casaubon silently hoped that it referred to some occupation at a great distance from Lowick.", "\"나는 미들마치 선거에 대해 좀 지나치게 잘 알고 있소."], ["He had disliked Will while he helped him, but he had begun to dislike him still more now that Will had declined his help.", "내일 당장 부패 선거구를 죄다 없애 버리고 왕국의 우후죽순 같은 신흥 도시를 죄다 끌어들여 보시오—의회에 들어가는 비용만 늘어날 뿐이오."], ["That is the way with us when we have any uneasy jealousy in our disposition: if our talents are chiefly of the burrowing kind, our honey-sipping cousin (whom we have grave reasons for objecting to) is likely to have a secret contempt for us, and any one who admires him passes an oblique criticism on ourselves.", "나는 사실에 근거해 말하는 거요.\" 「개척자」가 밀정의 손에 편집된다는 생각과 브룩이 적극적으로 정치에 나선다는 생각에—마치 종잡을 수 없이 무얼 좇던 거북이 야심차게 작은 머리를 내밀고 날뛰기라도 하듯—홀리 씨가 느낀 혐오감은, 브룩 씨 자신의 집안 일부 구성원들이 느낀 짜증에는 거의 미치지 못했다."], ["Having the scruples of rectitude in our souls, we are above the meanness of injuring him—rather we meet all his claims on us by active benefits; and the drawing of cheques for him, being a superiority which he must recognize, gives our bitterness a milder infusion.", "그 결과는, 마치 이웃이 법적 구제 수단도 없이 영영 당신의 콧구멍 아래 자리할 불쾌한 종류의 공장을 차렸다는 사실을 알아차렸을 때처럼, 서서히 새어 나왔다."], ["Now Mr. Casaubon had been deprived of that superiority (as anything more than a remembrance) in a sudden, capricious manner.", "「개척자」는 윌 래디슬로가 도착하기도 전에 이미 은밀히 사들여진 터였는데, 수익을 내지 못하는 값진 자산을 기꺼이 처분하려던 소유주의 의향 속에 기대하던 기회가 마련된 셈이었다."], ["His antipathy to Will did not spring from the common jealousy of a winter-worn husband: it was something deeper, bred by his lifelong claims and discontents; but Dorothea, now that she was present—Dorothea, as a young wife who herself had shown an offensive capability of criticism, necessarily gave concentration to the uneasiness which had before been vague.", "그리고 브룩 씨가 초대 편지를 쓴 이후의 기간 동안, 젊은 시절부터 그의 안에 있었으나 지금껏 어떤 장애물에 가로막혀 있던, 세상 전반에 자기 정신을 떨치게 하려는 그 싹트는 생각들이 은밀히 움터 오르고 있었다."]], [["Will Ladislaw on his side felt that his dislike was flourishing at the expense of his gratitude, and spent much inward discourse in justifying the dislike.", "그 전개는, 그가 예상했던 것보다도 더 컸던 손님에 대한 기쁨으로 한껏 촉진되었다."], ["Casaubon hated him—he knew that very well; on his first entrance he could discern a bitterness in the mouth and a venom in the glance which would almost justify declaring war in spite of past benefits. He was much obliged to Casaubon in the past, but really the act of marrying this wife was a set-off against the obligation.", "윌은 한때 브룩 씨가 파고들었던 그 모든 예술적·문학적 주제에 정통할 뿐 아니라, 정치적 정세의 요점을 놀랍도록 재빨리 포착하여, 충분한 기억력의 도움을 받아 인용에 능하고 다루는 솜씨가 전반적으로 효과적인, 그 폭넓은 정신으로 그것들을 다루는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["It was a question whether gratitude which refers to what is done for one’s self ought not to give way to indignation at what is done against another.", "\"내가 보기에 그는 일종의 셸리 같은 사람이오.\""], ["And Casaubon had done a wrong to Dorothea in marrying her.", "브룩 씨가 캐소본 씨를 흡족하게 해 주려고 기회를 잡아 말했다."], ["A man was bound to know himself better than that, and if he chose to grow gray crunching bones in a cavern, he had no business to be luring a girl into his companionship. “It is the most horrible of virgin-sacrifices,”", "\"못마땅한 점이 있다는 뜻은 아니오—방종이라든가 무신론이라든가 그런 것 말이오—래디슬로의 정서는 모든 면에서 분명 훌륭하오—실은 간밤에 우리가 함께 이야기를 많이 나눴소."], ["said Will; and he painted to himself what were Dorothea’s inward sorrows as if he had been writing a choric wail. But he would never lose sight of her: he would watch over her—if he gave up everything else in life he would watch over her, and she should know that she had one slave in the world.", "하지만 그는 자유, 해방, 속박에서 벗어남에 대해 같은 종류의 열정을 지녔는데—지도만 받는다면 훌륭한 것이지요—지도를 받는다면 말이오."], ["Will had—to use Sir Thomas Browne’s phrase—a “passionate prodigality” of statement both to himself and others.", "내가 그를 올바른 항로에 올려놓을 수 있을 것 같소."], ["The simple truth was that nothing then invited him so strongly as the presence of Dorothea.", "게다가 그가 당신 친척이라 더욱 기쁘오, 캐소본.\""]], [["Invitations of the formal kind had been wanting, however, for Will had never been asked to go to Lowick. Mr. Brooke, indeed, confident of doing everything agreeable which Casaubon, poor fellow, was too much absorbed to think of, had arranged to bring Ladislaw to Lowick several times (not neglecting meanwhile to introduce him elsewhere on every opportunity as “a young relative of Casaubon’s”). And though Will had not seen Dorothea alone, their interviews had been enough to restore her former sense of young companionship with one who was cleverer than herself, yet seemed ready to be swayed by her. Poor Dorothea before her marriage had never found much room in other minds for what she cared most to say; and she had not, as we know, enjoyed her husband’s superior instruction so much as she had expected. If she spoke with any keenness of interest to Mr. Casaubon, he heard her with an air of patience as if she had given a quotation from the Delectus familiar to him from his tender years, and sometimes mentioned curtly what ancient sects or personages had held similar ideas, as if there were too much of that sort in stock already; at other times he would inform her that she was mistaken, and reassert what her remark had questioned.", "올바른 항로라는 것이 브룩 씨의 나머지 말보다 조금이라도 더 분명한 무언가를 뜻한다면, 캐소본 씨는 그것이 로윅에서 멀리 떨어진 어떤 일자리를 가리키기를 속으로 바랐다. 그는 윌을 도와주는 동안에도 그를 싫어했지만, 윌이 자신의 도움을 거절한 지금 그를 더욱 싫어하기 시작한 터였다. 우리 기질 속에 어떤 불안한 질투심이 있을 때 우리는 다 그렇게 되는 법이다. 우리의 재능이 주로 땅속을 파헤치는 종류라면, 우리의 꿀을 빨아 먹는 사촌(우리가 못마땅해할 중대한 이유를 지닌 그 사촌)은 우리를 남몰래 경멸하기 마련이고, 그를 흠모하는 자는 누구든 우리에게 비스듬한 비판을 던지는 셈이 된다. 우리 영혼에 올바름의 양심이 있는 까닭에, 우리는 그를 해치는 비열함보다는 높은 자리에 서서—오히려 우리에 대한 그의 모든 요구를 적극적인 혜택으로 갚는다. 그리고 그를 위해 수표를 끊어 주는 일은 그가 인정할 수밖에 없는 우월함인 만큼, 우리의 쓰라림에 한결 부드러운 약을 타 준다. 그런데 캐소본 씨는 그 우월함을(추억 이상의 무언가로서의 그것을) 별안간 변덕스러운 방식으로 빼앗긴 터였다. 윌에 대한 그의 반감은 겨울에 시든 남편의 흔한 질투에서 비롯된 것이 아니었다. 그것은 더 깊은 무엇으로, 평생에 걸친 그의 요구와 불만이 길러 낸 것이었다. 그러나 이제 그 자리에 있는 도러시아는—그녀 자신이 비판하는 능력을 보란 듯이 드러낸 적이 있는 젊은 아내로서—전에는 막연했던 그 불안에 필연적으로 초점을 맞춰 주었다. 한편 윌 래디슬로는 자기 혐오가 감사의 마음을 희생시키며 무성해지고 있음을 느꼈고, 그 혐오를 정당화하느라 많은 내면의 담론에 빠져들었다. 캐소본은 자기를 미워한다—그는 그것을 아주 잘 알았다. 처음 들어섰을 때 그는 그 입가의 쓰라림과 눈빛 속의 독기를 알아차릴 수 있었는데, 그것은 과거의 은혜에도 불구하고 거의 전쟁 선포를 정당화할 만한 것이었다. 그는 과거에 캐소본에게 많은 신세를 졌지만, 사실 이 아내와 결혼한 행위는 그 빚에 대한 상쇄였다. 자기 자신을 위해 베풀어진 것에 대한 감사가, 다른 이에게 저질러진 것에 대한 분노에 마땅히 자리를 내주어야 하지 않을까 하는 것은 하나의 물음이었다. 그리고 캐소본은 도러시아와 결혼함으로써 그녀에게 잘못을 저질렀다. 사내라면 마땅히 자기 자신을 그보다는 더 잘 알아야 하는 법이고, 동굴 속에서 뼈다귀나 갉아 먹으며 백발이 되기를 택했다면, 한 처녀를 자기 동무로 꾀어들일 일은 아니었다. \"처녀를 제물로 바치는 가장 끔찍한 짓이야.\" 윌은 말했고, 마치 합창대의 통곡을 쓰기라도 하듯 도러시아의 내면의 슬픔이 어떠할지 스스로 그려 보았다. 하지만 그는 결코 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않으리라. 그녀를 지켜보리라—삶의 다른 모든 것을 포기한다 해도 그녀를 지켜보리라, 그리고 그녀는 이 세상에 자기를 위한 노예가 하나 있음을 알게 되리라. 윌은—토머스 브라운 경의 표현을 빌리자면—자기 자신과 남들 모두에게 진술의 \"열정적인 낭비벽\"을 지니고 있었다. 단순한 진실은, 그때 그를 그토록 강하게 끌어당기는 것은 도러시아의 존재만 한 게 없었다는 것이다."]], [["But Will Ladislaw always seemed to see more in what she said than she herself saw.", "하지만 정식 초대 같은 것은 없었으니, 윌은 한 번도 로윅에 오라는 청을 받은 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["Dorothea had little vanity, but she had the ardent woman’s need to rule beneficently by making the joy of another soul.", "사실 브룩 씨는, 가엾은 캐소본이 너무 몰두한 나머지 생각하지 못하는 유쾌한 일이라면 무엇이든 해내겠다는 자신감으로, 래디슬로를 여러 차례 로윅에 데려가기로 일을 꾸며 두었다(그러는 동안에도 기회 있을 때마다 그를 다른 곳에 \"캐소본의 젊은 친척\"이라고 소개하는 일을 게을리하지 않았다). 그리고 윌이 도러시아를 단둘이 만난 적은 없었지만, 그들의 만남은 자기보다 더 영리하면서도 기꺼이 자기에게 좌우되려는 듯 보이는 한 사람과 나누던 젊은 동무 의식을 그녀에게 되살려 주기에 충분했다."], ["Hence the mere chance of seeing Will occasionally was like a lunette opened in the wall of her prison, giving her a glimpse of the sunny air; and this pleasure began to nullify her original alarm at what her husband might think about the introduction of Will as her uncle’s guest.", "가엾은 도러시아는 결혼 전에 자기가 가장 하고 싶었던 말을 받아 줄 자리를 다른 이들의 마음속에서 좀처럼 찾지 못했었다. 그리고 우리가 알다시피, 그녀는 남편의 우월한 가르침을 기대했던 만큼 누리지 못했다."], ["On this subject Mr. Casaubon had remained dumb.", "그녀가 조금이라도 예리한 관심을 담아 캐소본 씨에게 말하면, 그는 그녀가 자기에게는 어린 시절부터 익숙한 라틴어 독본에서 한 구절을 인용하기라도 한 듯 참는 기색으로 들었고, 때로는 이미 그런 부류는 충분히 비축되어 있다는 양 어떤 고대 종파나 인물이 비슷한 생각을 품었었는지 퉁명스레 언급했으며, 또 어떤 때는 그녀가 틀렸다고 알려 주고 그녀의 말이 의문시한 바를 다시 단언하곤 했다."]], [["But Will wanted to talk with Dorothea alone, and was impatient of slow circumstance.", "하지만 윌 래디슬로는 언제나 그녀가 한 말 속에서 그녀 자신이 본 것보다 더 많은 것을 보는 듯했다. 도러시아에게는 허영심이 거의 없었지만, 다른 영혼의 기쁨을 만들어 냄으로써 자애롭게 다스리고자 하는 열렬한 여인의 욕구가 있었다."], ["However slight the terrestrial intercourse between Dante and Beatrice or Petrarch and Laura, time changes the proportion of things, and in later days it is preferable to have fewer sonnets and more conversation.", "그리하여 이따금 윌을 볼 수 있다는 단순한 기회만으로도 그것은 그녀의 감옥 벽에 뚫린 작은 채광창 같았으니, 햇살 가득한 공기를 잠깐 엿보게 해 주는 것이었다. 그리고 이 기쁨은 윌을 삼촌의 손님으로 소개하는 일에 대해 남편이 어떻게 생각할지를 두고 처음에 품었던 불안을 무효로 만들기 시작했다."], ["Necessity excused stratagem, but stratagem was limited by the dread of offending Dorothea.", "이 문제에 관해 캐소본 씨는 말이 없었다. 하지만 윌은 도러시아와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶었고, 더디게 흐르는 정황을 참지 못했다."], ["He found out at last that he wanted to take a particular sketch at Lowick; and one morning when Mr.", "단테와 베아트리체 사이의, 혹은 페트라르카와 라우라 사이의 현세적 교류가 아무리 미미했다 한들, 시간은 사물의 비율을 바꾸어 놓는 법이고, 훗날에는 소네트는 더 적고 대화는 더 많은 편이 나은 것이다. 필요가 책략을 변명해 주었지만, 책략은 도러시아의 기분을 상하게 할까 하는 두려움에 제한받았다."], ["Brooke had to drive along the Lowick road on his way to the county town, Will asked to be set down with his sketch-book and camp-stool at Lowick, and without announcing himself at the Manor settled himself to sketch in a position where he must see Dorothea if she came out to walk—and he knew that she usually walked an hour in the morning.", "마침내 그는 로윅에서 어떤 특정한 풍경을 스케치하고 싶다는 사실을 알아냈다. 그리고 어느 날 아침 브룩 씨가 군청 소재지로 가는 길에 로윅 가도를 따라 마차를 몰아야 했을 때, 윌은 스케치북과 접이의자를 들고 로윅에 내려 달라고 청했고, 저택에 자기 도착을 알리지도 않은 채, 도러시아가 산책하러 나온다면 반드시 그녀를 보게 될 자리를 잡고 스케치할 채비를 했다—그리고 그는 그녀가 보통 아침에 한 시간씩 산책한다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["But the stratagem was defeated by the weather.", "하지만 그 책략은 날씨에 무산되었다."], ["Clouds gathered with treacherous quickness, the rain came down, and Will was obliged to take shelter in the house.", "구름이 의뭉스러울 만큼 빠르게 몰려들었고, 비가 내렸으며, 윌은 어쩔 수 없이 집 안으로 피신해야 했다. 그는 친척이라는 명분에 기대어, 응접실로 들어가 알리지도 않은 채 그곳에서 기다릴 작정이었다."], ["He intended, on the strength of relationship, to go into the drawing-room and wait there without being announced; and seeing his old acquaintance the butler in the hall, he said, “Don’t mention that I am here, Pratt; I will wait till luncheon; I know Mr.", "그리고 현관에서 오랜 지인인 집사를 보고는 이렇게 말했다. \"내가 여기 있다는 말은 하지 마시오, 프랫."], ["Casaubon does not like to be disturbed when he is in the library.”", "점심때까지 기다릴 테니. 캐소본 씨가 서재에 계실 때는 방해받기 싫어하시는 걸 알고 있소.\""]], [["“Master is out, sir; there’s only Mrs.", "\"주인님은 출타 중이십니다, 나리."], ["Casaubon in the library.", "서재에는 캐소본 부인만 계십니다."], ["I’d better tell her you’re here, sir,”", "나리께서 여기 계신다고 부인께 말씀드리는 편이 낫겠습니다, 나리.\""], ["said Pratt, a red-cheeked man given to lively converse with Tantripp, and often agreeing with her that it must be dull for Madam.", "프랫이 말했다. 그는 볼이 붉은 사내로, 탠트립과 활기차게 수다 떠는 것을 좋아했고, 마님이 따분하시겠다는 데 그녀와 자주 의견을 같이하곤 했다."]], [["“Oh, very well; this confounded rain has hindered me from sketching,”", "\"오, 그럼 좋소. 이 망할 비 때문에 스케치를 못 하게 됐단 말이오.\""], ["said Will, feeling so happy that he affected indifference with delightful ease.", "윌이 말했는데, 어찌나 행복했던지 더없이 손쉽게 무심한 척했다."]], [["In another minute he was in the library, and Dorothea was meeting him with her sweet unconstrained smile.", "다음 순간 그는 서재에 있었고, 도러시아가 그 다정하고 거리낌 없는 미소로 그를 맞고 있었다."]], [["“Mr. Casaubon has gone to the Archdeacon’s,”", "\"캐소본 씨는 부주교님 댁에 가셨어요.\""], ["she said, at once.", "그녀가 곧바로 말했다."], ["“I don’t know whether he will be at home again long before dinner.", "\"저녁 식사 한참 전에 돌아오실지 어떨지 모르겠네요."], ["He was uncertain how long he should be.", "얼마나 걸릴지 분명치 않다고 하셨거든요."], ["Did you want to say anything particular to him?”", "그분께 무슨 특별히 하실 말씀이 있으셨나요?\""]], [["“No; I came to sketch, but the rain drove me in.", "\"아니요. 스케치하러 왔다가 비 때문에 안으로 떠밀려 들어왔습니다."], ["Else I would not have disturbed you yet.", "아니었다면 아직 부인을 방해하지 않았을 겁니다."], ["I supposed that Mr. Casaubon was here, and I know he dislikes interruption at this hour.”", "캐소본 씨가 계실 줄 알았는데, 이 시간에 방해받는 걸 싫어하시는 걸 알거든요.\""]], [["“I am indebted to the rain, then.", "\"그렇다면 비에 신세를 졌네요."], ["I am so glad to see you.”", "뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["Dorothea uttered these common words with the simple sincerity of an unhappy child, visited at school.", "도러시아는 학교로 면회 온 불행한 아이의 소박한 진심으로 이 평범한 말들을 했다."]], [["“I really came for the chance of seeing you alone,”", "\"실은 부인을 단둘이 뵐 기회를 노리고 왔습니다.\""], ["said Will, mysteriously forced to be just as simple as she was.", "윌이 말했는데, 알 수 없는 까닭에 그녀만큼이나 소박해질 수밖에 없었다."], ["He could not stay to ask himself, why not?", "그는 어째서 안 될 것이 있느냐고 자문할 겨를도 없었다."], ["“I wanted to talk about things, as we did in Rome.", "\"로마에서 그랬듯이 이런저런 것들에 관해 이야기하고 싶었어요."], ["It always makes a difference when other people are present.”", "다른 사람들이 있으면 늘 다르게 마련이니까요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Dorothea, in her clear full tone of assent.", "도러시아가 맑고 충만한 어조로 동의하며 말했다."], ["“Sit down.”", "\"앉으세요.\""], ["She seated herself on a dark ottoman with the brown books behind her, looking in her plain dress of some thin woollen-white material, without a single ornament on her besides her wedding-ring, as if she were under a vow to be different from all other women; and Will sat down opposite her at two yards’ distance, the light falling on his bright curls and delicate but rather petulant profile, with its defiant curves of lip and chin.", "그녀는 갈색 책들을 등진 어두운 색 오토만에 앉았는데, 결혼반지 말고는 몸에 장신구 하나 없이 어떤 얇은 모직 흰색 천으로 지은 수수한 옷을 입은 그 모습은, 마치 다른 모든 여자와 달라야 한다는 서원이라도 한 사람 같았다. 윌은 두 야드쯤 떨어진 맞은편에 앉았고, 빛이 그의 빛나는 곱슬머리와, 입술과 턱의 도전적인 곡선이 어려 섬세하면서도 다소 토라진 듯한 옆얼굴에 떨어졌다."], ["Each looked at the other as if they had been two flowers which had opened then and there.", "두 사람은 마치 바로 그 자리에서 피어난 두 송이 꽃이라도 되는 양 서로를 바라보았다. 도러시아는 그 순간 윌에 대한 남편의 알 수 없는 노여움을 잊었다."], ["Dorothea for the moment forgot her husband’s mysterious irritation against Will: it seemed fresh water at her thirsty lips to speak without fear to the one person whom she had found receptive; for in looking backward through sadness she exaggerated a past solace.", "자기가 받아들여 준다고 여겼던 단 한 사람에게 두려움 없이 말할 수 있다는 것은 목마른 입술에 닿는 신선한 물 같았다. 슬픔을 통해 과거를 돌아보면서 그녀는 지난날의 위안을 과장했던 것이다."]], [["“I have often thought that I should like to talk to you again,”", "\"당신과 다시 이야기를 나눴으면 좋겠다고 자주 생각했어요.\""], ["she said, immediately.", "그녀가 곧바로 말했다."], ["“It seems strange to me how many things I said to you.”", "\"제가 당신께 얼마나 많은 것들을 말했었는지 생각하면 이상해요.\""]], [["“I remember them all,”", "\"저는 그것들을 다 기억합니다.\""], ["said Will, with the unspeakable content in his soul of feeling that he was in the presence of a creature worthy to be perfectly loved.", "윌이 말했는데, 완전히 사랑받을 만한 존재 앞에 있다는 느낌에 그의 영혼은 형언할 수 없는 만족으로 차 있었다."], ["I think his own feelings at that moment were perfect, for we mortals have our divine moments, when love is satisfied in the completeness of the beloved object.", "내 생각에 그 순간 그 자신의 감정은 완전한 것이었으니, 우리 같은 한낱 인간에게도 사랑이 사랑하는 대상의 완전함 속에서 충족되는 신성한 순간이 있는 법이다."]], [["“I have tried to learn a great deal since we were in Rome,”", "\"로마에 있던 이후로 저는 아주 많은 것을 배우려고 애써 왔어요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“I can read Latin a little, and I am beginning to understand just a little Greek.", "\"이젠 라틴어를 조금 읽을 수 있고, 그리스어도 아주 조금 이해하기 시작했어요."], ["I can help Mr. Casaubon better now.", "이제 캐소본 씨를 더 잘 도와드릴 수 있어요."], ["I can find out references for him and save his eyes in many ways.", "그분을 위해 참고 문헌을 찾아드리고 여러 방법으로 눈을 아껴드릴 수 있거든요."], ["But it is very difficult to be learned; it seems as if people were worn out on the way to great thoughts, and can never enjoy them because they are too tired.”", "하지만 학식을 갖춘다는 건 무척 어려운 일이에요. 사람들이 위대한 사상에 이르는 길에서 지쳐 버려서, 너무 피곤한 나머지 결코 그것을 누리지 못하는 것만 같아요.\""]], [["“If a man has a capacity for great thoughts, he is likely to overtake them before he is decrepit,”", "\"위대한 사상을 품을 능력이 있는 사람이라면, 노쇠해지기 전에 그것을 따라잡을 가능성이 높지요.\""], ["said Will, with irrepressible quickness.", "윌이 억누를 수 없는 민첩함으로 말했다."], ["But through certain sensibilities Dorothea was as quick as he, and seeing her face change, he added, immediately, “But it is quite true that the best minds have been sometimes overstrained in working out their ideas.”", "하지만 어떤 감수성에서는 도러시아도 그만큼 민첩했기에, 그녀의 얼굴빛이 변하는 것을 보고 그는 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"하지만 가장 뛰어난 정신도 자기 생각을 풀어내느라 때로 과로했다는 건 분명한 사실입니다.\""]], [["“You correct me,” said Dorothea.", "\"제 말을 바로잡아 주시는군요.\""], ["“I expressed myself ill.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["I should have said that those who have great thoughts get too much worn in working them out.", "\"제가 표현을 잘못했어요. 위대한 사상을 품은 이들이 그것을 풀어내느라 너무 지쳐 버린다고 말했어야 했어요."], ["I used to feel about that, even when I was a little girl; and it always seemed to me that the use I should like to make of my life would be to help some one who did great works, so that his burthen might be lighter.”", "저는 어린 소녀였을 때도 그것에 대해 늘 느끼곤 했어요. 그리고 제 삶을 쓸 수 있다면, 위대한 일을 하는 누군가를 도와 그의 짐을 좀 더 가볍게 해 주는 데 쓰고 싶다는 생각이 언제나 들었어요.\""]], [["Dorothea was led on to this bit of autobiography without any sense of making a revelation.", "도러시아는 무언가를 털어놓는다는 의식도 없이 이 짤막한 자기 고백으로 이끌려 갔다."], ["But she had never before said anything to Will which threw so strong a light on her marriage.", "하지만 그녀가 윌에게 자기 결혼에 그토록 강한 빛을 던지는 말을 한 적은 전에 한 번도 없었다."], ["He did not shrug his shoulders; and for want of that muscular outlet he thought the more irritably of beautiful lips kissing holy skulls and other emptinesses ecclesiastically enshrined.", "그는 어깨를 으쓱하지 않았다. 그리고 그 근육의 분출구가 없는 탓에, 아름다운 입술이 거룩한 해골이며 교회의 신전에 모셔진 다른 공허들에 입 맞추는 광경을 더욱 짜증스럽게 떠올렸다."], ["Also he had to take care that his speech should not betray that thought.", "또한 그는 자기 말이 그 생각을 드러내지 않도록 조심해야 했다."]], [["“But you may easily carry the help too far,” he said, “and get over-wrought yourself.", "\"하지만 그 도움을 너무 멀리까지 밀고 나가다가는 부인 자신이 과로하게 되기 쉽습니다.\""], ["Are you not too much shut up?", "그가 말했다."], ["You already look paler.", "\"너무 갇혀 지내시는 것 아닙니까?"], ["It would be better for Mr.", "벌써 안색이 더 창백해 보여요."], ["Casaubon to have a secretary; he could easily get a man who would do half his work for him.", "캐소본 씨께 비서를 두는 편이 나을 겁니다. 그분 일의 절반을 대신해 줄 사람을 쉽게 구하실 수 있을 거예요."], ["It would save him more effectually, and you need only help him in lighter ways.”", "그게 그분을 더 효과적으로 아껴 드리는 길이고, 부인은 더 가벼운 일로만 도와드리면 될 테니까요.\""]], [["“How can you think of that?”", "\"어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요?\""], ["said Dorothea, in a tone of earnest remonstrance.", "도러시아가 진지하게 항의하는 어조로 말했다."], ["“I should have no happiness if I did not help him in his work.", "\"그분의 일을 돕지 못한다면 제겐 아무 행복도 없을 거예요."], ["What could I do?", "제가 무얼 할 수 있겠어요?"], ["There is no good to be done in Lowick.", "로윅에는 할 만한 좋은 일이 없는걸요."], ["The only thing I desire is to help him more.", "제가 바라는 단 하나는 그분을 더 많이 돕는 거예요."], ["And he objects to a secretary: please not to mention that again.”", "그리고 그분은 비서를 두는 걸 반대하세요. 부디 그 말씀은 다시 꺼내지 말아 주세요.\""]], [["“Certainly not, now I know your feeling.", "\"물론입니다, 이제 부인의 마음을 알았으니까요."], ["But I have heard both Mr. Brooke and Sir James Chettam express the same wish.”", "하지만 브룩 씨와 제임스 체텀 경도 같은 바람을 말하는 것을 들었습니다.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Dorothea, “but they don’t understand—they want me to be a great deal on horseback, and have the garden altered and new conservatories, to fill up my days.", "도러시아가 말했다. \"하지만 그분들은 이해하지 못하세요—제가 말을 많이 타기를, 정원을 고치고 새 온실을 짓기를, 그렇게 하루하루를 채우기를 바라시거든요."], ["I thought you could understand that one’s mind has other wants,”", "저는 당신이라면 사람의 마음에 다른 욕구가 있다는 걸 이해하실 줄 알았어요.\""], ["she added, rather impatiently—“besides, Mr.", "그녀가 다소 안달하며 덧붙였다."], ["Casaubon cannot bear to hear of a secretary.”", "\"게다가 캐소본 씨는 비서라는 말은 듣고도 못 견디세요.\""]], [["“My mistake is excusable,”", "\"제 착오는 용서받을 만합니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“In old days I used to hear Mr. Casaubon speak as if he looked forward to having a secretary.", "\"예전에는 캐소본 씨가 마치 비서를 두기를 고대하시는 듯 말씀하시는 걸 듣곤 했거든요."], ["Indeed he held out the prospect of that office to me.", "사실 제게 그 자리를 맡을 가능성을 내비치셨고요."], ["But I turned out to be—not good enough for it.”", "하지만 알고 보니 저는—그 일에 적합할 만큼 훌륭하지 못했지요.\""]], [["Dorothea was trying to extract out of this an excuse for her husband’s evident repulsion, as she said, with a playful smile, “You were not a steady worker enough.”", "도러시아는 이 말에서 남편의 명백한 거부감에 대한 변명을 끌어내려 애쓰며, 장난스러운 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"당신이 충분히 꾸준한 일꾼이 아니었던 거군요.\""]], [["“No,” said Will, shaking his head backward somewhat after the manner of a spirited horse.", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["And then, the old irritable demon prompting him to give another good pinch at the moth-wings of poor Mr.", "윌이 기운찬 말처럼 머리를 뒤로 흔들며 말했다. 그러고는 가엾은 캐소본 씨의 영광이라는 나방 날개를 한 번 더 톡 꼬집어 주라고 부추기는 그 옛 짜증의 악마에 이끌려 말을 이었다."], ["Casaubon’s glory, he went on, “And I have seen since that Mr. Casaubon does not like any one to overlook his work and know thoroughly what he is doing.", "\"그리고 그 후로 보니, 캐소본 씨는 누가 자기 일을 들여다보고 자기가 하는 일을 속속들이 아는 걸 좋아하지 않으시더군요."], ["He is too doubtful—too uncertain of himself.", "그분은 너무 의심이 많아요—자기 자신을 너무 확신하지 못하세요."], ["I may not be good for much, but he dislikes me because I disagree with him.”", "제가 별 쓸모는 없을지 몰라도, 그분이 저를 싫어하는 건 제가 그분 의견에 동의하지 않기 때문이에요.\""]], [["Will was not without his intentions to be always generous, but our tongues are little triggers which have usually been pulled before general intentions can be brought to bear.", "윌에게 늘 너그럽고자 하는 의도가 없었던 건 아니지만, 우리의 혀란 보통 전반적인 의도가 작동되기도 전에 당겨지고 마는 작은 방아쇠인 것이다."], ["And it was too intolerable that Casaubon’s dislike of him should not be fairly accounted for to Dorothea.", "그리고 캐소본이 자기를 싫어하는 까닭이 도러시아에게 제대로 설명되지 않는다는 것은 너무 참을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Yet when he had spoken he was rather uneasy as to the effect on her.", "그러나 말을 하고 나자 그는 그것이 그녀에게 미칠 영향에 대해 다소 불안해졌다."]], [["But Dorothea was strangely quiet—not immediately indignant, as she had been on a like occasion in Rome.", "하지만 도러시아는 이상하리만치 조용했다—로마에서 비슷한 일이 있었을 때처럼 즉각 분개하지 않았다."], ["And the cause lay deep.", "그리고 그 까닭은 깊은 데 있었다."], ["She was no longer struggling against the perception of facts, but adjusting herself to their clearest perception; and now when she looked steadily at her husband’s failure, still more at his possible consciousness of failure, she seemed to be looking along the one track where duty became tenderness.", "그녀는 이제 더 이상 사실에 대한 인식과 싸우지 않고, 그것을 더없이 또렷하게 인식하는 쪽으로 자신을 맞춰 가고 있었다. 그리하여 이제 남편의 실패를, 더욱이 그가 그 실패를 의식하고 있을지도 모른다는 사실을 똑바로 바라보았을 때, 그녀는 의무가 다정함이 되는 그 하나의 길을 따라가고 있는 듯했다."], ["Will’s want of reticence might have been met with more severity, if he had not already been recommended to her mercy by her husband’s dislike, which must seem hard to her till she saw better reason for it.", "윌의 신중하지 못함은 좀 더 엄하게 받아들여졌을지도 모른다, 만약 남편의 그 못마땅함이—그녀가 더 나은 이유를 알기 전까지는 가혹하게 느껴질 수밖에 없는 그 못마땅함이—이미 그를 그녀의 자비에 맡기지 않았더라면."]], [["She did not answer at once, but after looking down ruminatingly she said, with some earnestness, “Mr.", "그녀는 곧바로 대답하지 않고, 골똘히 생각하며 시선을 내리깐 뒤 다소 진지하게 말했다."], ["Casaubon must have overcome his dislike of you so far as his actions were concerned: and that is admirable.”", "\"캐소본 씨는 적어도 행동에 관한 한 당신에 대한 못마땅함을 극복하셨던 게 분명해요. 그리고 그건 훌륭한 일이에요.\""]], [["“Yes; he has shown a sense of justice in family matters.", "\"그렇습니다. 그분은 집안일에서 정의감을 보여 주셨지요."], ["It was an abominable thing that my grandmother should have been disinherited because she made what they called a mesalliance, though there was nothing to be said against her husband except that he was a Polish refugee who gave lessons for his bread.”", "제 할머니가 이른바 신분이 안 맞는 결혼을 했다는 이유로 상속에서 제외되었다는 건 가증스러운 일이었어요. 할머니의 남편으로 말하자면, 빵을 벌기 위해 수업을 하던 폴란드 망명객이라는 점 말고는 흠잡을 데가 없었는데도 말이지요.\""]], [["“I wish I knew all about her!”", "\"그분에 대해 다 알았으면 좋겠어요!\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“I wonder how she bore the change from wealth to poverty: I wonder whether she was happy with her husband!", "\"그분이 부유함에서 가난으로의 변화를 어떻게 견디셨을지 궁금해요. 남편과 행복하셨을지 궁금하고요!"], ["Do you know much about them?”", "그분들에 대해 많이 아세요?\""]], [["“No; only that my grandfather was a patriot—a bright fellow—could speak many languages—musical—got his bread by teaching all sorts of things.", "\"아니요. 다만 제 할아버지가 애국자였다는 것—명석한 분이었고—여러 언어를 할 줄 알았고—음악에도 재능이 있었고—온갖 것을 가르쳐 빵을 벌었다는 것 정도예요."], ["They both died rather early.", "두 분 다 비교적 일찍 돌아가셨어요."], ["And I never knew much of my father, beyond what my mother told me; but he inherited the musical talents.", "그리고 저는 아버지에 대해서도 어머니가 들려주신 것 말고는 잘 몰라요. 다만 아버지는 그 음악적 재능을 물려받으셨지요."], ["I remember his slow walk and his long thin hands; and one day remains with me when he was lying ill, and I was very hungry, and had only a little bit of bread.”", "저는 아버지의 느린 걸음걸이와 길고 가느다란 손을 기억해요. 그리고 아버지가 병석에 누워 계시고, 제가 무척 배가 고픈데도 빵 한 조각밖에 없던 어느 날이 제게 남아 있어요.\""]], [["“Ah, what a different life from mine!”", "\"아, 제 삶과는 어쩌면 그리 다른 삶일까요!\""], ["said Dorothea, with keen interest, clasping her hands on her lap.", "도러시아가 깊은 관심을 담아 무릎 위에 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."], ["“I have always had too much of everything.", "\"저는 늘 모든 게 너무 넘쳤어요."], ["But tell me how it was—Mr. Casaubon could not have known about you then.”", "하지만 어떻게 된 일인지 말해 주세요—그때는 캐소본 씨가 당신에 대해 아실 수 없었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“No; but my father had made himself known to Mr. Casaubon, and that was my last hungry day.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["My father died soon after, and my mother and I were well taken care of. Mr.", "하지만 아버지가 캐소본 씨께 자기 존재를 알리셨고, 그날이 제가 굶주린 마지막 날이었어요."], ["Casaubon always expressly recognized it as his duty to take care of us because of the harsh injustice which had been shown to his mother’s sister.", "아버지는 곧 돌아가셨고, 어머니와 저는 잘 보살핌을 받았지요. 캐소본 씨는 자기 어머니의 누이에게 가해진 모진 불의 때문에 우리를 보살피는 것이 자기 의무라고 늘 분명히 인정하셨어요."], ["But now I am telling you what is not new to you.”", "하지만 지금 저는 부인께 새로울 것 없는 이야기를 하고 있군요.\""]], [["In his inmost soul Will was conscious of wishing to tell Dorothea what was rather new even in his own construction of things—namely, that Mr. Casaubon had never done more than pay a debt towards him.", "속마음으로 윌은 도러시아에게, 자기 자신의 해석에서조차 다소 새로운 무언가를 말하고 싶어 했다—다시 말해 캐소본 씨는 자기에게 빚을 갚은 것 이상의 일은 결코 한 적이 없다는 것을."], ["Will was much too good a fellow to be easy under the sense of being ungrateful.", "윌은 배은망덕하다는 느낌 아래 편할 수 있기에는 너무도 좋은 사내였다."], ["And when gratitude has become a matter of reasoning there are many ways of escaping from its bonds.", "그리고 감사가 따지고 드는 문제가 되어 버리면, 그 속박에서 빠져나갈 길은 많은 법이다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["answered Dorothea; “Mr.", "도러시아가 대답했다."], ["Casaubon has always avoided dwelling on his own honorable actions.”", "\"캐소본 씨는 늘 자신의 명예로운 행동을 두고 길게 늘어놓는 걸 삼가셨어요.\""], ["She did not feel that her husband’s conduct was depreciated; but this notion of what justice had required in his relations with Will Ladislaw took strong hold on her mind.", "그녀는 남편의 행실이 깎아내려졌다고 느끼지는 않았다. 하지만 윌 래디슬로와의 관계에서 정의가 무엇을 요구했었는가 하는 이 생각이 그녀의 마음을 강하게 사로잡았다."], ["After a moment’s pause, she added, “He had never told me that he supported your mother.", "잠시 멈춘 뒤 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그분이 당신 어머니를 부양하셨다는 건 제게 말씀하신 적이 없어요."], ["Is she still living?”", "그분은 아직 살아 계신가요?\""]], [["“No; she died by an accident—a fall—four years ago.", "\"아니요. 사 년 전에 사고로—넘어지셔서—돌아가셨어요."], ["It is curious that my mother, too, ran away from her family, but not for the sake of her husband.", "묘한 건 제 어머니도 가족에게서 도망치셨다는 거예요. 다만 남편 때문은 아니었지요."], ["She never would tell me anything about her family, except that she forsook them to get her own living—went on the stage, in fact.", "어머니는 가족에 대해 제게 아무 말도 하려 하지 않으셨어요. 다만 스스로 벌어먹기 위해 그들을 버렸다는 것—실은 무대에 서셨다는 것만 빼고요."], ["She was a dark-eyed creature, with crisp ringlets, and never seemed to be getting old.", "어머니는 검은 눈에 곱슬곱슬한 머리를 한 분이었고, 결코 늙어 가는 것 같지 않았어요."], ["You see I come of rebellious blood on both sides,”", "보시다시피 저는 양쪽 다 반항적인 피를 타고났지요.\""], ["Will ended, smiling brightly at Dorothea, while she was still looking with serious intentness before her, like a child seeing a drama for the first time.", "윌은 도러시아를 향해 환하게 미소 지으며 말을 맺었고, 그동안에도 그녀는 마치 처음으로 연극을 보는 아이처럼 진지한 골똘함으로 앞을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["But her face, too, broke into a smile as she said, “That is your apology, I suppose, for having yourself been rather rebellious; I mean, to Mr.", "하지만 그녀의 얼굴 역시 미소로 피어나며 말했다. \"그게 당신 자신이 다소 반항적이었던 것에 대한—제 말은, 캐소본 씨의 바람에 반항한 것에 대한 변명이로군요."], ["Casaubon’s wishes. You must remember that you have not done what he thought best for you.", "그분이 당신을 위해 가장 좋다고 여기신 일을 당신이 하지 않았다는 걸 잊으시면 안 돼요."], ["And if he dislikes you—you were speaking of dislike a little while ago—but I should rather say, if he has shown any painful feelings towards you, you must consider how sensitive he has become from the wearing effect of study.", "그리고 만약 그분이 당신을 싫어하신다면—조금 전에 당신이 싫어함을 말씀하셨지만—차라리 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠네요, 만약 그분이 당신에게 어떤 괴로운 감정을 내비치셨다면, 그분이 학문에 시달린 끝에 얼마나 예민해지셨는지를 헤아려 주셔야 해요."], ["Perhaps,”", "어쩌면,\""], ["she continued, getting into a pleading tone, “my uncle has not told you how serious Mr.", "그녀가 호소하는 어조에 들어서며 말을 이었다. \"제 삼촌이 캐소본 씨의 병환이 얼마나 심각했는지 당신께 말씀하지 않으셨을지도 몰라요."], ["Casaubon’s illness was. It would be very petty of us who are well and can bear things, to think much of small offences from those who carry a weight of trial.”", "건강해서 여러 일을 견딜 수 있는 우리가, 시련의 무게를 짊어진 분들이 저지르는 사소한 잘못을 크게 여긴다면 무척 옹졸한 일일 거예요.\""]], [["“You teach me better,”", "\"부인 덕에 더 잘 배웁니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“I will never grumble on that subject again.”", "\"다시는 그 일로 투덜대지 않겠습니다.\""], ["There was a gentleness in his tone which came from the unutterable contentment of perceiving—what Dorothea was hardly conscious of—that she was travelling into the remoteness of pure pity and loyalty towards her husband.", "그의 어조에는 부드러움이 깃들어 있었는데, 그것은 도러시아 자신은 거의 의식하지 못하는 사실—그녀가 남편을 향한 순수한 연민과 충정의 아득한 곳으로 나아가고 있다는 사실—을 알아차린 데서 오는 말할 수 없는 만족에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["Will was ready to adore her pity and loyalty, if she would associate himself with her in manifesting them.", "윌은, 그녀가 그것을 드러내는 일에 자기를 함께해 준다면, 그녀의 연민과 충정을 흠모할 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["“I have really sometimes been a perverse fellow,”", "\"저는 정말 가끔 비뚤어진 사람이었어요.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he went on, “but I will never again, if I can help it, do or say what you would disapprove.”", "\"하지만 다시는, 제가 어쩔 수만 있다면, 부인이 못마땅해하실 일을 하거나 말하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“That is very good of you,”", "\"참 고마운 말씀이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with another open smile.", "도러시아가 또 한 번 환한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“I shall have a little kingdom then, where I shall give laws.", "\"그렇다면 제겐 작은 왕국이 하나 생기는 셈이네요. 거기서 제가 법을 내리겠지요."], ["But you will soon go away, out of my rule, I imagine.", "하지만 당신은 곧 떠나, 제 통치 밖으로 나가시겠지요, 제 짐작에."], ["You will soon be tired of staying at the Grange.”", "곧 그레인지에 머무는 게 싫증나실 테니까요.\""]], [["“That is a point I wanted to mention to you—one of the reasons why I wished to speak to you alone.", "\"그게 바로 부인께 말씀드리고 싶었던 점이에요—부인을 단둘이 뵙고 싶었던 이유 중 하나지요."], ["Mr. Brooke proposes that I should stay in this neighborhood.", "브룩 씨가 제게 이 근방에 머물러 달라고 하셨어요."], ["He has bought one of the Middlemarch newspapers, and he wishes me to conduct that, and also to help him in other ways.”", "미들마치 신문 중 하나를 사들이셨는데, 제가 그걸 운영하고 다른 일로도 도와주기를 바라세요.\""]], [["“Would not that be a sacrifice of higher prospects for you?”", "\"그건 당신에게 더 높은 전망을 희생하는 일이 되지 않을까요?\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps; but I have always been blamed for thinking of prospects, and not settling to anything.", "\"어쩌면요. 하지만 저는 늘 전망만 생각하고 무엇 하나에도 정착하지 않는다고 비난받아 왔어요."], ["And here is something offered to me.", "그런데 여기 제게 제안된 일이 있고요."], ["If you would not like me to accept it, I will give it up.", "만약 부인이 제가 그 일을 받아들이는 걸 원치 않으신다면, 그만두겠습니다."], ["Otherwise I would rather stay in this part of the country than go away.", "그게 아니라면 저는 떠나기보다는 이 고장에 머무는 편이 좋아요."], ["I belong to nobody anywhere else.”", "다른 어디에도 저는 누구의 사람도 아니거든요.\""]], [["“I should like you to stay very much,”", "\"저는 당신이 머무시기를 정말 바라요.\""], ["said Dorothea, at once, as simply and readily as she had spoken at Rome.", "도러시아가, 로마에서 말했던 것처럼이나 소박하고 선뜻 곧바로 말했다."], ["There was not the shadow of a reason in her mind at the moment why she should not say so.", "그 순간 그녀의 마음속에는 그렇게 말해서는 안 될 이유가 그림자조차 없었다."]], [["“Then I will stay,”", "\"그렇다면 머물겠습니다.\""], ["said Ladislaw, shaking his head backward, rising and going towards the window, as if to see whether the rain had ceased.", "래디슬로가 머리를 뒤로 흔들며 말했고, 일어나 마치 비가 그쳤는지 보려는 듯 창가로 걸어갔다."]], [["But the next moment, Dorothea, according to a habit which was getting continually stronger, began to reflect that her husband felt differently from herself, and she colored deeply under the double embarrassment of having expressed what might be in opposition to her husband’s feeling, and of having to suggest this opposition to Will.", "하지만 다음 순간 도러시아는, 점점 더 강해지는 어떤 버릇에 따라, 남편이 자기와는 다르게 느낀다는 사실을 곱씹기 시작했고, 남편의 감정에 어긋날지도 모르는 것을 입 밖에 냈다는 것과 그 어긋남을 윌에게 넌지시 알려야 한다는 것, 그 이중의 당혹감에 얼굴이 깊이 붉어졌다."], ["His face was not turned towards her, and this made it easier to say—", "그의 얼굴이 그녀 쪽을 향해 있지 않았고, 그래서 이렇게 말하기가 한결 쉬웠다—"]], [["“But my opinion is of little consequence on such a subject.", "\"하지만 그런 문제에 제 의견은 별 중요하지 않아요."], ["I think you should be guided by Mr. Casaubon.", "당신은 캐소본 씨의 인도를 받으셔야 한다고 생각해요."], ["I spoke without thinking of anything else than my own feeling, which has nothing to do with the real question.", "저는 제 자신의 감정 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않고 말했는데, 그건 정작 문제와는 아무 상관이 없어요."], ["But it now occurs to me—perhaps Mr. Casaubon might see that the proposal was not wise.", "하지만 이제 생각해 보니—어쩌면 캐소본 씨는 그 제안이 현명하지 못하다고 보실지도 몰라요."], ["Can you not wait now and mention it to him?”", "지금은 기다렸다가 그분께 그 얘기를 꺼내면 안 될까요?\""]], [["“I can’t wait to-day,”", "\"오늘은 기다릴 수 없어요.\""], ["said Will, inwardly seared by the possibility that Mr.", "윌이, 캐소본 씨가 들어올지도 모른다는 가능성에 속으로 데인 채 말했다."], ["Casaubon would enter.", "\"비는 이제 완전히 그쳤어요."], ["“The rain is quite over now. I told Mr.", "브룩 씨께 저를 데리러 오시지 마시라고 일러두었거든요."], ["Brooke not to call for me: I would rather walk the five miles.", "차라리 오 마일을 걷는 편이 좋아요."], ["I shall strike across Halsell Common, and see the gleams on the wet grass.", "핼셀 공유지를 가로질러 가면서 젖은 풀밭에 어른거리는 빛을 보겠습니다."], ["I like that.”", "저는 그게 좋아요.\""]], [["He approached her to shake hands quite hurriedly, longing but not daring to say, “Don’t mention the subject to Mr.", "그는 작별 악수를 하려고 꽤 서둘러 그녀에게 다가가면서, \"캐소본 씨께는 그 얘기를 꺼내지 마세요\"라고 말하고 싶었으나 감히 그러지 못했다."], ["Casaubon.” No, he dared not, could not say it.", "아니, 그는 감히 그 말을 할 수 없었고, 할 수도 없었다."], ["To ask her to be less simple and direct would be like breathing on the crystal that you want to see the light through.", "그녀에게 덜 소박하고 덜 솔직해지라고 청하는 것은, 그 너머로 빛을 보고 싶은 수정에 입김을 부는 것과 같으리라."], ["And there was always the other great dread—of himself becoming dimmed and forever ray-shorn in her eyes.", "그리고 거기엔 늘 또 다른 커다란 두려움이 있었으니—그녀의 눈에 비친 자기 자신이 흐려지고 영영 광채를 잃을지도 모른다는 두려움이었다."]], [["“I wish you could have stayed,”", "\"머무실 수 있었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with a touch of mournfulness, as she rose and put out her hand.", "도러시아가 일어나 손을 내밀며 다소 애잔한 기색으로 말했다."], ["She also had her thought which she did not like to express:—Will certainly ought to lose no time in consulting Mr. Casaubon’s wishes, but for her to urge this might seem an undue dictation.", "그녀에게도 입 밖에 내고 싶지 않은 생각이 있었으니—윌이 어김없이 지체 없이 캐소본 씨의 뜻을 여쭈어야 하지만, 그녀가 그것을 다그치는 것은 지나친 지시처럼 보일지도 모른다는 생각이었다."]], [["So they only said “Good-by,” and Will quitted the house, striking across the fields so as not to run any risk of encountering Mr.", "그리하여 두 사람은 그저 \"안녕히 가세요\"라고만 말했고, 윌은 집을 나서서 캐소본 씨의 마차와 마주칠 위험을 조금도 무릅쓰지 않으려고 들판을 가로질러 갔다."], ["Casaubon’s carriage, which, however, did not appear at the gate until four o’clock.", "그러나 그 마차는 네 시가 되어서야 대문에 나타났다."], ["That was an unpropitious hour for coming home: it was too early to gain the moral support under ennui of dressing his person for dinner, and too late to undress his mind of the day’s frivolous ceremony and affairs, so as to be prepared for a good plunge into the serious business of study.", "그것은 귀가하기에 상서롭지 못한 시각이었다. 저녁 식사를 위해 몸단장을 함으로써 권태로부터 정신적 지지를 얻기에는 너무 일렀고, 하루의 하찮은 격식과 용무로부터 마음을 벗겨 내어 학문이라는 진지한 일에 제대로 뛰어들 채비를 하기에는 너무 늦은 때였다."], ["On such occasions he usually threw into an easy-chair in the library, and allowed Dorothea to read the London papers to him, closing his eyes the while.", "그런 때면 그는 보통 서재의 안락의자에 몸을 던지고, 그동안 눈을 감은 채 도러시아가 런던 신문을 읽어 주도록 하곤 했다."], ["To-day, however, he declined that relief, observing that he had already had too many public details urged upon him; but he spoke more cheerfully than usual, when Dorothea asked about his fatigue, and added with that air of formal effort which never forsook him even when he spoke without his waistcoat and cravat—", "그러나 오늘은 그 위안을 마다하며, 이미 공적인 자질구레한 이야기를 너무 많이 들었노라고 말했다. 하지만 도러시아가 피곤하냐고 묻자 평소보다 한결 명랑하게 말했고, 조끼와 크라바트를 벗고 말할 때조차 결코 그를 떠나지 않는 그 격식 차린 노력의 태도로 덧붙였다—"]], [["“I have had the gratification of meeting my former acquaintance, Dr. Spanning, to-day, and of being praised by one who is himself a worthy recipient of praise.", "\"오늘은 옛 지인인 스패닝 박사를 만나고, 그 자신이 칭찬받기에 합당한 사람에게서 칭찬을 받는 기쁨을 누렸소."], ["He spoke very handsomely of my late tractate on the Egyptian Mysteries,—using, in fact, terms which it would not become me to repeat.” In uttering the last clause, Mr.", "그가 이집트 신비 의식에 관한 내 근래의 소론을 무척 훌륭하게 평하더군—사실 내가 입에 담기에는 어울리지 않을 표현들을 써 가면서 말이오.\""], ["Casaubon leaned over the elbow of his chair, and swayed his head up and down, apparently as a muscular outlet instead of that recapitulation which would not have been becoming.", "마지막 구절을 말하면서 캐소본 씨는 의자 팔걸이에 몸을 기대고 머리를 위아래로 끄덕였는데, 어울리지 않을 그 말의 되풀이 대신 분명 근육의 분출구를 삼는 듯했다."]], [["“I am very glad you have had that pleasure,”", "\"그런 기쁨을 누리셨다니 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["said Dorothea, delighted to see her husband less weary than usual at this hour.", "도러시아가, 이 시간에 남편이 평소보다 덜 지쳐 보이는 것을 보고 기뻐하며 말했다."], ["“Before you came I had been regretting that you happened to be out to-day.”", "\"당신이 오시기 전에 저는 마침 오늘 출타하셨던 게 아쉽다고 생각하고 있었거든요.\""]], [["“Why so, my dear?”", "\"어째서요, 여보?\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, throwing himself backward again.", "캐소본 씨가 다시 뒤로 몸을 젖히며 말했다."]], [["“Because Mr. Ladislaw has been here; and he has mentioned a proposal of my uncle’s which I should like to know your opinion of.”", "\"래디슬로 씨가 여기 다녀가셨거든요."], ["Her husband she felt was really concerned in this question.", "그분이 제 삼촌의 제안을 말씀하셨는데, 그것에 대한 당신 의견을 알고 싶어요.\""], ["Even with her ignorance of the world she had a vague impression that the position offered to Will was out of keeping with his family connections, and certainly Mr.", "그녀는 남편이 이 문제에 정말로 관계되어 있다고 느꼈다. 세상 물정에 어두운 그녀로서도, 윌에게 제안된 자리가 그의 집안 연줄과 어울리지 않는다는 막연한 인상은 있었고, 캐소본 씨에게는 분명 의견을 구할 만한 권리가 있었다."], ["Casaubon had a claim to be consulted. He did not speak, but merely bowed.", "그는 말하지 않고 그저 고개만 숙였다."]], [["“Dear uncle, you know, has many projects.", "\"아시다시피 삼촌은 여러 계획을 갖고 계세요."], ["It appears that he has bought one of the Middlemarch newspapers, and he has asked Mr. Ladislaw to stay in this neighborhood and conduct the paper for him, besides helping him in other ways.”", "보아하니 미들마치 신문 중 하나를 사들이셨는데, 래디슬로 씨에게 이 근방에 머무르며 그 신문을 운영해 주고, 그 밖의 다른 일로도 도와 달라고 청하셨대요.\""]], [["Dorothea looked at her husband while she spoke, but he had at first blinked and finally closed his eyes, as if to save them; while his lips became more tense.", "도러시아는 말하면서 남편을 바라보았으나, 그는 처음에 눈을 깜박이더니 마침내 마치 눈을 아끼려는 듯 감아 버렸다. 그러는 동안 입술은 더욱 굳어졌다."], ["“What is your opinion?”", "\"당신 의견은 어떠세요?\""], ["she added, rather timidly, after a slight pause.", "그녀가 잠시 멈춘 뒤 다소 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Did Mr. Ladislaw come on purpose to ask my opinion?”", "\"래디슬로 씨가 일부러 내 의견을 물으러 왔소?\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, opening his eyes narrowly with a knife-edged look at Dorothea.", "캐소본 씨가 도러시아를 칼날같이 날카로운 눈빛으로 보며 가늘게 눈을 뜨고 말했다."], ["She was really uncomfortable on the point he inquired about, but she only became a little more serious, and her eyes did not swerve.", "그녀는 그가 물어본 그 점에 대해 정말로 마음이 불편했지만, 다만 조금 더 진지해졌을 뿐 눈길을 돌리지는 않았다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요.\""], ["she answered immediately, “he did not say that he came to ask your opinion.", "그녀가 곧바로 대답했다. \"당신 의견을 물으러 왔다고는 하지 않으셨어요."], ["But when he mentioned the proposal, he of course expected me to tell you of it.”", "하지만 그 제안을 말씀하셨을 때, 당연히 제가 당신께 그것을 전하리라 기대하셨겠지요.\""]], [["Mr. Casaubon was silent.", "캐소본 씨는 말이 없었다."]], [["“I feared that you might feel some objection.", "\"당신이 어떤 반대를 느끼실까 봐 걱정스러웠어요."], ["But certainly a young man with so much talent might be very useful to my uncle—might help him to do good in a better way.", "하지만 그렇게 재능 많은 젊은이라면 분명 삼촌께 큰 도움이 될 수 있을 거예요—삼촌이 더 나은 방식으로 좋은 일을 하도록 도울 수 있을 테고요."], ["And Mr. Ladislaw wishes to have some fixed occupation.", "그리고 래디슬로 씨는 정해진 일자리를 갖고 싶어 해요."], ["He has been blamed, he says, for not seeking something of that kind, and he would like to stay in this neighborhood because no one cares for him elsewhere.”", "그런 종류의 무언가를 찾지 않는다고 비난받아 왔다고 하더군요. 그리고 다른 곳에서는 아무도 자기를 마음에 두지 않으니 이 근방에 머물고 싶어 해요.\""]], [["Dorothea felt that this was a consideration to soften her husband.", "도러시아는 이것이 남편의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 만한 고려 사항이라고 느꼈다."], ["However, he did not speak, and she presently recurred to Dr. Spanning and the Archdeacon’s breakfast.", "하지만 그는 말이 없었고, 그녀는 이내 스패닝 박사와 부주교의 조찬 이야기로 돌아갔다."], ["But there was no longer sunshine on these subjects.", "그러나 이런 화제에는 더 이상 햇살이 비치지 않았다."]], [["The next morning, without Dorothea’s knowledge, Mr. Casaubon despatched the following letter, beginning “Dear Mr. Ladislaw” (he had always before addressed him as “Will”):—", "이튿날 아침, 도러시아가 모르는 사이에 캐소본 씨는 다음과 같은 편지를 발송했는데, 첫머리를 \"친애하는 래디슬로 씨\"라고 시작했다(그는 전에는 늘 그를 \"윌\"이라고 불렀다)—"]], [["“Mrs. Casaubon informs me that a proposal has been made to you, and (according to an inference by no means stretched) has on your part been in some degree entertained, which involves your residence in this neighborhood in a capacity which I am justified in saying touches my own position in such a way as renders it not only natural and warrantable in me when that effect is viewed under the influence of legitimate feeling, but incumbent on me when the same effect is considered in the light of my responsibilities, to state at once that your acceptance of the proposal above indicated would be highly offensive to me.", "\"캐소본 부인에게 들으니, 자네에게 어떤 제안이 들어왔고, (결코 무리하지 않은 추론에 따르건대) 자네 쪽에서도 어느 정도 그것을 마음에 두고 있는 듯하네."], ["That I have some claim to the exercise of a veto here, would not, I believe, be denied by any reasonable person cognizant of the relations between us: relations which, though thrown into the past by your recent procedure, are not thereby annulled in their character of determining antecedents.", "그것은 자네가 이 근방에 거주하는 일을 수반하는데, 그 처지는 내 자신의 입장을 건드리는 것이라 내가 단언해도 무방한바, 그 영향을 정당한 감정의 견지에서 본다면 이렇게 말하는 것이 나로서 자연스럽고 정당할 뿐 아니라, 같은 영향을 내 책임의 견지에서 헤아린다면 즉시 다음을 밝히는 것이 내 의무이기도 하네. 즉, 위에서 언급한 제안을 자네가 수락한다면 내게 심히 불쾌할 것이라는 점일세."], ["I will not here make reflections on any person’s judgment.", "이 일에 내가 거부권을 행사할 만한 약간의 권리가 있다는 점은, 우리 사이의 관계를 아는 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구도 부정하지 않으리라 믿네. 그 관계는 비록 자네의 최근 처신으로 과거의 일이 되었다 해도, 그렇다고 해서 결정적 선행 사실로서의 그 성격이 무효화되는 것은 아닐세."], ["It is enough for me to point out to yourself that there are certain social fitnesses and proprieties which should hinder a somewhat near relative of mine from becoming any wise conspicuous in this vicinity in a status not only much beneath my own, but associated at best with the sciolism of literary or political adventurers.", "나는 여기서 어떤 사람의 판단에 대해서도 논평하지 않겠네. 다만, 어떤 사회적 적절함과 예법이 있어서, 나의 다소 가까운 친척이 이 인근에서 나 자신의 지위보다 훨씬 낮을 뿐 아니라, 기껏해야 문필적이거나 정치적인 모험가들의 어설픈 지식과 결부된 신분으로 어떤 식으로든 두드러지는 것을 마땅히 막아야 한다는 점을 자네에게 지적하는 것으로 충분하네."], ["At any rate, the contrary issue must exclude you from further reception at my house.", "어쨌든 그와 반대되는 결말은 자네가 앞으로 내 집에서 환대받는 것을 배제할 수밖에 없을 것일세."]], [["Yours faithfully, “EDWARD CASAUBON.”", "이만 줄이네,\n에드워드 캐소본.\""]], [["Meanwhile Dorothea’s mind was innocently at work towards the further embitterment of her husband; dwelling, with a sympathy that grew to agitation, on what Will had told her about his parents and grandparents.", "한편 도러시아의 마음은 천진하게도 남편을 한층 더 쓰라리게 만드는 쪽으로 움직이고 있었다. 윌이 자기 부모와 조부모에 대해 들려준 이야기를 동요에 이를 만큼 깊어진 공감으로 곱씹고 있었던 것이다."], ["Any private hours in her day were usually spent in her blue-green boudoir, and she had come to be very fond of its pallid quaintness.", "하루 중 사적인 시간이면 그녀는 보통 청록색 규방에서 보냈는데, 그 창백한 고풍스러움을 무척 좋아하게 된 터였다."], ["Nothing had been outwardly altered there; but while the summer had gradually advanced over the western fields beyond the avenue of elms, the bare room had gathered within it those memories of an inward life which fill the air as with a cloud of good or bad angels, the invisible yet active forms of our spiritual triumphs or our spiritual falls.", "그곳에서 겉으로 달라진 것은 아무것도 없었다. 하지만 여름이 느릅나무 가로수 길 너머 서쪽 들판으로 서서히 번져 가는 동안, 그 휑한 방은 마치 선하거나 악한 천사들의 구름처럼 공기를 채우는 그 내면의 삶의 기억들, 우리 영혼의 승리나 영혼의 추락의 보이지 않으나 살아 움직이는 형상들을 그 안에 모아 들였다."], ["She had been so used to struggle for and to find resolve in looking along the avenue towards the arch of western light that the vision itself had gained a communicating power.", "그녀는 가로수 길을 따라 서쪽 빛의 아치를 바라보며 결심을 짜내고 또 찾는 데 너무도 익숙해진 나머지, 그 광경 자체가 무언가를 전해 주는 힘을 얻게 되었던 것이다."], ["Even the pale stag seemed to have reminding glances and to mean mutely, “Yes, we know.”", "창백한 수사슴조차 일깨우는 듯한 눈길을 보내며 말없이 \"그래, 우리도 알아\"라고 말하는 것 같았다."], ["And the group of delicately touched miniatures had made an audience as of beings no longer disturbed about their own earthly lot, but still humanly interested.", "그리고 섬세하게 그려진 세밀화들의 무리는, 이제 더 이상 자기 자신의 세속적 운명에 대해 동요하지 않으면서도 여전히 인간적인 관심을 지닌 존재들의 청중 같은 것을 이루었다."], ["Especially the mysterious “Aunt Julia” about whom Dorothea had never found it easy to question her husband.", "특히 도러시아가 남편에게 좀처럼 캐묻기 어려웠던 그 신비로운 \"줄리아 고모\"가 그러했다."]], [["And now, since her conversation with Will, many fresh images had gathered round that Aunt Julia who was Will’s grandmother; the presence of that delicate miniature, so like a living face that she knew, helping to concentrate her feelings.", "그리고 이제, 윌과 대화를 나눈 이래로, 윌의 할머니인 그 줄리아 고모를 둘러싸고 많은 새로운 이미지가 모여들었다. 그녀가 아는 어느 살아 있는 얼굴과 그토록 닮은 그 섬세한 세밀화의 존재가, 그녀의 감정을 한데 모으는 데 도움을 주었다."], ["What a wrong, to cut off the girl from the family protection and inheritance only because she had chosen a man who was poor!", "가난한 남자를 택했다는 이유만으로 그 처녀를 가족의 보호와 상속에서 끊어 낸다는 것은 얼마나 큰 잘못인가!"], ["Dorothea, early troubling her elders with questions about the facts around her, had wrought herself into some independent clearness as to the historical, political reasons why eldest sons had superior rights, and why land should be entailed: those reasons, impressing her with a certain awe, might be weightier than she knew, but here was a question of ties which left them uninfringed.", "어린 시절부터 자기 주변의 사실들에 대해 질문을 던져 어른들을 곤란하게 했던 도러시아는, 어째서 장남이 우월한 권리를 갖는지, 어째서 토지가 한정 상속되어야 하는지에 대한 역사적·정치적 이유들을 어느 정도 독자적으로 명료하게 정리해 두고 있었다. 어떤 경외감으로 그녀에게 새겨진 그 이유들은 어쩌면 그녀가 아는 것보다 더 무게가 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["Here was a daughter whose child—even according to the ordinary aping of aristocratic institutions by people who are no more aristocratic than retired grocers, and who have no more land to “keep together”", "하지만 여기 있는 것은 그 이유들을 침해하지 않은 채로 남겨 두는 인연의 문제였다. 여기 있는 것은, 그 딸의 자식이—은퇴한 식료품상보다 더 귀족적일 것도 없고 잔디밭과 작은 목초지 말고는 \"한데 지킬\""], ["than a lawn and a paddock—would have a prior claim. Was inheritance a question of liking or of responsibility?", "땅이라곤 없는 사람들이 귀족 제도를 천박하게 흉내 내는 통상적 관행에 따르더라도—우선권을 가질, 그런 딸의 경우였다."], ["All the energy of Dorothea’s nature went on the side of responsibility—the fulfilment of claims founded on our own deeds, such as marriage and parentage.", "상속이란 호불호의 문제인가, 아니면 책임의 문제인가? 도러시아의 천성이 지닌 온 힘은 책임의 편에 섰다—결혼이나 부모 됨처럼, 우리 자신의 행위에 근거한 요구들을 이행하는 편에."]], [["It was true, she said to herself, that Mr. Casaubon had a debt to the Ladislaws—that he had to pay back what the Ladislaws had been wronged of.", "캐소본 씨가 래디슬로 가문에 빚을 지고 있다는 것, 래디슬로 가문이 부당하게 빼앗긴 것을 그가 갚아야 한다는 것은 사실이라고 그녀는 스스로 되뇌었다."], ["And now she began to think of her husband’s will, which had been made at the time of their marriage, leaving the bulk of his property to her, with proviso in case of her having children.", "그리고 이제 그녀는, 결혼할 무렵에 작성되어 자기 재산의 대부분을 자녀가 생길 경우를 단서로 도러시아에게 남기도록 한 남편의 유언을 떠올리기 시작했다. 그것은 고쳐져야 마땅했다."], ["That ought to be altered; and no time ought to be lost.", "그리고 한시도 지체해서는 안 되었다."], ["This very question which had just arisen about Will Ladislaw’s occupation, was the occasion for placing things on a new, right footing.", "방금 윌 래디슬로의 일자리를 두고 생긴 바로 이 문제야말로, 모든 것을 새롭고 올바른 토대 위에 놓을 계기였다."], ["Her husband, she felt sure, according to all his previous conduct, would be ready to take the just view, if she proposed it—she, in whose interest an unfair concentration of the property had been urged.", "남편은, 지금까지의 모든 처신으로 미루어 보건대, 그녀가 제안하기만 한다면 분명 정당한 견해를 기꺼이 받아들일 것이라고 그녀는 확신했다—재산의 부당한 집중이 바로 그녀의 이익을 위해 추진되었던 만큼."], ["His sense of right had surmounted and would continue to surmount anything that might be called antipathy. She suspected that her uncle’s scheme was disapproved by Mr.", "그의 올바름에 대한 감각은 반감이라 부를 만한 어떤 것도 이겨 냈고 앞으로도 계속 이겨 낼 터였다."], ["Casaubon, and this made it seem all the more opportune that a fresh understanding should be begun, so that instead of Will’s starting penniless and accepting the first function that offered itself, he should find himself in possession of a rightful income which should be paid by her husband during his life, and, by an immediate alteration of the will, should be secured at his death.", "그녀는 삼촌의 계획을 캐소본 씨가 못마땅해한다고 짐작했고, 그래서 새로운 양해가 시작되는 것이 더욱 시의적절해 보였다. 그리하여 윌이 무일푼으로 출발해 처음 제안된 일자리를 받아들이는 대신, 남편이 살아 있는 동안 지급하고 유언을 즉시 고쳐 그가 죽은 뒤에도 보장될, 정당한 수입을 손에 넣게 되도록 말이다."], ["The vision of all this as what ought to be done seemed to Dorothea like a sudden letting in of daylight, waking her from her previous stupidity and incurious self-absorbed ignorance about her husband’s relation to others.", "마땅히 해야 할 일로서 이 모든 것을 떠올리는 것은 도러시아에게 마치 별안간 햇빛이 들이치는 것 같았고, 남편이 다른 이들과 맺은 관계에 대한 이전의 어리석음과, 호기심 없이 자기에게만 몰입했던 무지에서 그녀를 깨워 주었다."], ["Will Ladislaw had refused Mr. Casaubon’s future aid on a ground that no longer appeared right to her; and Mr.", "윌 래디슬로는 이제 그녀에게는 더 이상 옳아 보이지 않는 근거에서 캐소본 씨의 장래의 도움을 거절했었다."], ["Casaubon had never himself seen fully what was the claim upon him.", "그리고 캐소본 씨 자신은 자기에게 지워진 그 요구가 무엇인지 결코 온전히 보지 못했었다."], ["“But he will!” said Dorothea.", "\"하지만 그분은 보실 거야!\""], ["“The great strength of his character lies here.", "도러시아는 말했다. \"그분 성품의 큰 힘이 바로 거기 있어."], ["And what are we doing with our money?", "그런데 우리는 우리 돈으로 무얼 하고 있지?"], ["We make no use of half of our income.", "우리 수입의 절반도 쓰지 않잖아."], ["My own money buys me nothing but an uneasy conscience.”", "내 돈은 내게 불편한 양심 말고는 아무것도 사 주지 않아.\""]], [["There was a peculiar fascination for Dorothea in this division of property intended for herself, and always regarded by her as excessive.", "도러시아에게는 자기 몫으로 정해진, 그리고 늘 지나치다고 여겨 왔던 이 재산 분배에 묘한 매혹이 있었다."], ["She was blind, you see, to many things obvious to others—likely to tread in the wrong places, as Celia had warned her; yet her blindness to whatever did not lie in her own pure purpose carried her safely by the side of precipices where vision would have been perilous with fear.", "보다시피 그녀는 남들에게는 빤한 많은 것들에 눈멀어 있었다—실리아가 경고했듯이 엉뚱한 곳에 발을 디디기 십상이었다. 하지만 자기 자신의 순수한 목적 안에 있지 않은 것에 대한 그 눈멂이, 시야가 있었다면 두려움으로 위태로웠을 벼랑 가를 그녀가 무사히 지나가게 해 주었다."]], [["The thoughts which had gathered vividness in the solitude of her boudoir occupied her incessantly through the day on which Mr. Casaubon had sent his letter to Will.", "규방의 고독 속에서 생생함을 더해 갔던 그 생각들은, 캐소본 씨가 윌에게 편지를 보낸 그날 내내 그녀를 끊임없이 사로잡았다."], ["Everything seemed hindrance to her till she could find an opportunity of opening her heart to her husband.", "남편에게 마음을 열 기회를 찾기 전까지는 모든 것이 그녀에게 방해물처럼 여겨졌다."], ["To his preoccupied mind all subjects were to be approached gently, and she had never since his illness lost from her consciousness the dread of agitating him.", "그의 골몰한 마음에는 어떤 화제든 부드럽게 다가가야 했고, 그녀는 그의 병환 이래로 그를 동요시킬까 하는 두려움을 의식에서 한순간도 떨치지 못했다."], ["But when young ardor is set brooding over the conception of a prompt deed, the deed itself seems to start forth with independent life, mastering ideal obstacles.", "하지만 젊은 열정이 신속한 행위의 구상에 골몰하게 되면, 그 행위 자체가 독자적인 생명을 띠고 솟아나 관념적인 장애물들을 제압하는 듯하다."], ["The day passed in a sombre fashion, not unusual, though Mr.", "그날은 드물지 않은 음울한 방식으로 지나갔는데, 캐소본 씨가 어쩌면 평소보다 더 말이 없긴 했다."], ["Casaubon was perhaps unusually silent; but there were hours of the night which might be counted on as opportunities of conversation; for Dorothea, when aware of her husband’s sleeplessness, had established a habit of rising, lighting a candle, and reading him to sleep again.", "하지만 밤 시간에는 대화의 기회로 기대할 만한 때가 있었다. 도러시아는 남편이 잠 못 드는 것을 알아차렸을 때, 일어나 촛불을 켜고 그가 다시 잠들 때까지 책을 읽어 주는 버릇을 들여 두었기 때문이다."], ["And this night she was from the beginning sleepless, excited by resolves.", "그리고 이날 밤 그녀는 결심에 들떠 처음부터 잠을 이루지 못했다."], ["He slept as usual for a few hours, but she had risen softly and had sat in the darkness for nearly an hour before he said—", "그는 평소처럼 몇 시간 잠들었지만, 그녀는 가만히 일어나 거의 한 시간 가까이 어둠 속에 앉아 있었고, 그제야 그가 입을 열었다—"]], [["“Dorothea, since you are up, will you light a candle?”", "\"도러시아, 일어나 있으니, 촛불을 켜 주겠소?\""]], [["“Do you feel ill, dear?”", "\"어디 편찮으세요, 여보?\""], ["was her first question, as she obeyed him.", "그녀는 그의 말에 따르며 첫 물음을 던졌다."]], [["“No, not at all; but I shall be obliged, since you are up, if you will read me a few pages of Lowth.”", "\"아니, 전혀 아니오. 다만 당신이 일어나 있으니, 라우스의 글을 몇 쪽 읽어 준다면 고맙겠소.\""]], [["“May I talk to you a little instead?”", "\"대신 잠깐 이야기를 나눠도 될까요?\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론이오.\""]], [["“I have been thinking about money all day—that I have always had too much, and especially the prospect of too much.”", "\"저는 하루 종일 돈에 대해 생각하고 있었어요—제가 늘 너무 많이 가졌다는 것, 특히 앞으로 너무 많이 갖게 되리라는 전망에 대해서요.\""]], [["“These, my dear Dorothea, are providential arrangements.”", "\"여보 도러시아, 그런 것들은 신의 섭리에 따른 배치라오.\""]], [["“But if one has too much in consequence of others being wronged, it seems to me that the divine voice which tells us to set that wrong right must be obeyed.”", "\"하지만 다른 이들이 부당한 일을 당한 탓에 누군가 너무 많이 갖게 된 거라면, 그 잘못을 바로잡으라고 우리에게 이르는 신의 음성에 따라야 한다고 저는 생각해요.\""]], [["“What, my love, is the bearing of your remark?”", "\"여보, 그 말의 요지가 무엇이오?\""]], [["“That you have been too liberal in arrangements for me—I mean, with regard to property; and that makes me unhappy.”", "\"당신이 저를 위해—제 말은, 재산에 관해서요—너무 후하게 마련해 두셨다는 거예요. 그게 저를 불행하게 만들어요.\""]], [["“How so?", "\"어째서요?"], ["I have none but comparatively distant connections.”", "내게는 비교적 먼 친척 말고는 없는데.\""]], [["“I have been led to think about your aunt Julia, and how she was left in poverty only because she married a poor man, an act which was not disgraceful, since he was not unworthy.", "\"저는 당신의 줄리아 고모님에 대해, 그리고 그분이 가난한 남자와 결혼했다는 이유만으로 가난 속에 버려졌다는 것에 대해 생각하게 됐어요. 그 남편이 부족한 사람이 아니었으니, 그건 수치스러운 행위가 아니었지요."], ["It was on that ground, I know, that you educated Mr. Ladislaw and provided for his mother.”", "제가 알기로 바로 그 때문에 당신이 래디슬로 씨를 교육시키고 그의 어머니를 부양하셨던 거고요.\""]], [["Dorothea waited a few moments for some answer that would help her onward.", "도러시아는 자기를 앞으로 이끌어 줄 어떤 대답을 잠시 기다렸다."], ["None came, and her next words seemed the more forcible to her, falling clear upon the dark silence.", "아무 대답도 없었고, 어두운 침묵 위로 또렷이 떨어지는 그녀의 다음 말은 그녀 자신에게 한층 더 힘 있게 느껴졌다."]], [["“But surely we should regard his claim as a much greater one, even to the half of that property which I know that you have destined for me.", "\"하지만 분명 우리는 그분의 권리를, 당신이 제게 정해 두셨다고 제가 알고 있는 그 재산의 절반에 대해서도, 훨씬 더 큰 것으로 여겨야 해요."], ["And I think he ought at once to be provided for on that understanding.", "그리고 그런 양해 아래 그분에게 즉시 마련해 드려야 한다고 생각해요."], ["It is not right that he should be in the dependence of poverty while we are rich.", "우리는 부유한데 그분이 가난에 매여 의존하며 사는 건 옳지 않아요."], ["And if there is any objection to the proposal he mentioned, the giving him his true place and his true share would set aside any motive for his accepting it.”", "그리고 그분이 말씀하신 그 제안에 무슨 반대가 있으시다면, 그분에게 정당한 자리와 정당한 몫을 드리는 것이 그분이 그 제안을 받아들일 어떤 동기도 없애 줄 거예요.\""]], [["“Mr. Ladislaw has probably been speaking to you on this subject?”", "\"래디슬로 씨가 이 문제에 대해 당신에게 말한 모양이군?\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, with a certain biting quickness not habitual to him.", "캐소본 씨가, 그답지 않게 어떤 물어뜯는 듯한 신속함으로 말했다."]], [["“Indeed, no!”", "\"전혀 아니에요!\""], ["said Dorothea, earnestly.", "도러시아가 진지하게 말했다."], ["“How can you imagine it, since he has so lately declined everything from you?", "\"그분이 그토록 최근에 당신에게서 모든 걸 거절했는데 어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요?"], ["I fear you think too hardly of him, dear.", "저는 당신이 그분을 너무 모질게 여기시는 게 아닐까 걱정스러워요, 여보."], ["He only told me a little about his parents and grandparents, and almost all in answer to my questions.", "그분은 그저 자기 부모와 조부모에 대해 조금 들려주셨을 뿐이고, 그것도 거의 다 제 물음에 답하느라 그러신 거예요."], ["You are so good, so just—you have done everything you thought to be right.", "당신은 그토록 선하고, 그토록 정의로우셔서—옳다고 여기신 일은 다 하셨어요."], ["But it seems to me clear that more than that is right; and I must speak about it, since I am the person who would get what is called benefit by that ‘more’ not being done.”", "하지만 제겐 그 이상의 것이 옳다는 게 분명해 보여요. 그리고 저는 그것에 대해 말씀드려야 해요, 그 '이상의 것'이 행해지지 않음으로써 이른바 덕을 보게 될 사람이 바로 저니까요.\""]], [["There was a perceptible pause before Mr. Casaubon replied, not quickly as before, but with a still more biting emphasis.", "캐소본 씨가 대답하기까지 눈에 띄는 침묵이 있었는데, 이번에는 앞서처럼 신속하지 않고, 한층 더 물어뜯는 듯한 강조를 담고 있었다."]], [["“Dorothea, my love, this is not the first occasion, but it were well that it should be the last, on which you have assumed a judgment on subjects beyond your scope.", "\"도러시아, 여보, 당신이 당신 능력 밖의 문제에 대해 판단을 내린 것이 이번이 처음은 아니지만, 이번이 마지막이기를 바라는 바이오."], ["Into the question how far conduct, especially in the matter of alliances, constitutes a forfeiture of family claims, I do not now enter.", "처신이, 특히 혼인 관계의 문제에서, 어느 정도까지 가문에 대한 권리의 박탈을 구성하는가 하는 물음으로 나는 지금 들어가지 않겠소."], ["Suffice it, that you are not here qualified to discriminate.", "다만 당신이 그것을 분별할 자격이 여기서 없다는 것으로 충분하오."], ["What I now wish you to understand is, that I accept no revision, still less dictation within that range of affairs which I have deliberated upon as distinctly and properly mine.", "지금 내가 당신에게 깨닫게 하고 싶은 것은, 내가 분명하고도 정당하게 내 것이라고 숙고해 온 그 영역의 일에 대해서는 어떤 수정도, 더더욱 지시 따위는 받아들이지 않겠다는 것이오."], ["It is not for you to interfere between me and Mr. Ladislaw, and still less to encourage communications from him to you which constitute a criticism on my procedure.”", "나와 래디슬로 씨 사이에 끼어드는 것은 당신이 할 일이 아니며, 더욱이 내 처신에 대한 비판을 이루는 그의 전언이 당신에게 오도록 부추기는 것은 더더욱 할 일이 아니오.\""]], [["Poor Dorothea, shrouded in the darkness, was in a tumult of conflicting emotions.", "가엾은 도러시아는 어둠에 싸인 채 상충하는 감정들로 들끓고 있었다."], ["Alarm at the possible effect on himself of her husband’s strongly manifested anger, would have checked any expression of her own resentment, even if she had been quite free from doubt and compunction under the consciousness that there might be some justice in his last insinuation.", "남편이 그토록 강하게 드러낸 분노가 그 자신에게 미칠 영향에 대한 불안은, 설령 그녀가 그의 마지막 빈정거림에 어떤 정당함이 있을지도 모른다는 의식 아래 의심과 가책에서 완전히 자유로웠다 해도, 자기 자신의 분개를 입 밖에 내는 것을 억눌렀을 것이다."], ["Hearing him breathe quickly after he had spoken, she sat listening, frightened, wretched—with a dumb inward cry for help to bear this nightmare of a life in which every energy was arrested by dread.", "그가 말을 마친 뒤 가쁘게 숨 쉬는 소리를 들으며, 그녀는 겁에 질려 비참한 심정으로 앉아 귀를 기울였다—온갖 기운이 두려움에 가로막히는 이 악몽 같은 삶을 견디게 해 달라는, 도움을 구하는 말 없는 내면의 외침과 함께."], ["But nothing else happened, except that they both remained a long while sleepless, without speaking again.", "하지만 두 사람이 다시는 말을 나누지 않은 채 오랫동안 잠들지 못한 채로 있었다는 것 말고는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."]], [["The next day, Mr. Casaubon received the following answer from Will Ladislaw:—", "이튿날 캐소본 씨는 윌 래디슬로에게서 다음과 같은 답장을 받았다—"]], [["“DEAR MR. CASAUBON,—I have given all due consideration to your letter of yesterday, but I am unable to take precisely your view of our mutual position.", "\"친애하는 캐소본 씨, 어제 보내 주신 편지를 충분히 숙고했습니다만, 저는 우리 상호 간의 입장에 대해 선생님의 견해를 그대로 받아들일 수가 없습니다."], ["With the fullest acknowledgment of your generous conduct to me in the past, I must still maintain that an obligation of this kind cannot fairly fetter me as you appear to expect that it should.", "과거에 제게 베풀어 주신 너그러운 처신을 더없이 충분히 인정하면서도, 저는 여전히, 이런 종류의 의무가 선생님께서 기대하시는 듯한 방식으로 저를 부당하게 옭아맬 수는 없다고 주장해야겠습니다."], ["Granted that a benefactor’s wishes may constitute a claim; there must always be a reservation as to the quality of those wishes.", "은인의 바람이 하나의 권리를 구성할 수 있다 치더라도, 그 바람의 성질에 관해서는 언제나 유보가 있어야 합니다."], ["They may possibly clash with more imperative considerations.", "그것은 더 절박한 고려 사항들과 충돌할 수도 있습니다."], ["Or a benefactor’s veto might impose such a negation on a man’s life that the consequent blank might be more cruel than the benefaction was generous.", "혹은 은인의 거부권이 한 사람의 삶에 그러한 부정을 가하여, 그로 인한 공백이 그 은혜가 너그러웠던 것보다 더 잔인할 수도 있습니다."], ["I am merely using strong illustrations.", "저는 단지 강한 예시를 들고 있을 뿐입니다."], ["In the present case I am unable to take your view of the bearing which my acceptance of occupation—not enriching certainly, but not dishonorable—will have on your own position which seems to me too substantial to be affected in that shadowy manner.", "현재의 경우, 제가 어떤 일자리를—분명 부를 가져다주지는 않으나 불명예스럽지도 않은 일자리를—받아들이는 것이 선생님 자신의 입장에 미칠 영향에 대해, 저는 선생님의 견해를 받아들일 수가 없습니다. 그 입장은 제게는 너무도 견고해 보여서 그런 그림자 같은 방식으로 영향받을 것 같지 않기 때문입니다."], ["And though I do not believe that any change in our relations will occur (certainly none has yet occurred) which can nullify the obligations imposed on me by the past, pardon me for not seeing that those obligations should restrain me from using the ordinary freedom of living where I choose, and maintaining myself by any lawful occupation I may choose.", "그리고 우리 관계에 과거가 제게 지운 의무를 무효화할 어떤 변화가 일어나리라고는 믿지 않습니다만(분명 아직 아무 변화도 일어나지 않았고요), 그 의무가 제가 원하는 곳에서 살고 제가 택할 수 있는 어떤 합법적인 일로 저 자신을 부양하는 통상적인 자유를 누리는 것을 막아야 한다고는 저로서는 보이지 않으니, 부디 용서해 주십시오."], ["Regretting that there exists this difference between us as to a relation in which the conferring of benefits has been entirely on your side—", "혜택을 베푸는 일이 전적으로 선생님 쪽에만 있었던 그런 관계에 관해 우리 사이에 이런 견해차가 존재하게 된 것을 유감으로 여기며—"]], [["I remain, yours with persistent obligation, WILL LADISLAW.”", "변함없는 의무를 지닌 채 이만 줄입니다,\n윌 래디슬로.\""]], [["Poor Mr. Casaubon felt (and must not we, being impartial, feel with him a little?) that no man had juster cause for disgust and suspicion than he.", "가엾은 캐소본 씨는 자기보다 더 정당한 혐오와 의심의 근거를 가진 사람은 없다고 느꼈다(공정하다면 우리도 그와 더불어 조금은 그렇게 느껴야 하지 않을까?)."], ["Young Ladislaw, he was sure, meant to defy and annoy him, meant to win Dorothea’s confidence and sow her mind with disrespect, and perhaps aversion, towards her husband.", "그는 젊은 래디슬로가 자기에게 맞서고 성가시게 굴 작정이며, 도러시아의 신뢰를 얻어 그녀의 마음에 남편에 대한 무례함을, 어쩌면 혐오까지 뿌리려 한다고 확신했다."], ["Some motive beneath the surface had been needed to account for Will’s sudden change of course in rejecting Mr.", "윌이 캐소본 씨의 도움을 거부하고 여행을 그만둠으로써 별안간 진로를 바꾼 것을 설명하려면 표면 아래의 어떤 동기가 필요했다."], ["Casaubon’s aid and quitting his travels; and this defiant determination to fix himself in the neighborhood by taking up something so much at variance with his former choice as Mr.", "그리고 자신의 이전 선택과 그토록 어긋나는 브룩 씨의 미들마치 계획 같은 것을 떠맡으면서까지 이 근방에 자리 잡으려는 이 도전적인 결의는, 그 밝히지 않은 동기가 도러시아와 관련되어 있음을 충분히 분명하게 드러냈다."], ["Brooke’s Middlemarch projects, revealed clearly enough that the undeclared motive had relation to Dorothea.", "캐소본 씨는 한순간도 도러시아를 어떤 이중성으로 의심하지 않았다."], ["Not for one moment did Mr. Casaubon suspect Dorothea of any doubleness: he had no suspicions of her, but he had (what was little less uncomfortable) the positive knowledge that her tendency to form opinions about her husband’s conduct was accompanied with a disposition to regard Will Ladislaw favorably and be influenced by what he said.", "그녀에 대한 의심은 없었으나, 그에게는 (그에 못지않게 불편한) 확실한 앎이 있었으니, 남편의 처신에 대해 의견을 형성하는 그녀의 성향이 윌 래디슬로를 호의적으로 여기고 그가 하는 말에 영향받는 기질을 동반하고 있다는 것이었다."], ["His own proud reticence had prevented him from ever being undeceived in the supposition that Dorothea had originally asked her uncle to invite Will to his house.", "자존심에서 비롯된 그의 과묵함은, 애초에 도러시아가 삼촌에게 윌을 집에 초대해 달라고 청했다는 추측에서 그가 결코 벗어나지 못하게 만들었다."]], [["And now, on receiving Will’s letter, Mr. Casaubon had to consider his duty.", "그리고 이제 윌의 편지를 받고서, 캐소본 씨는 자신의 의무를 고려해야 했다."], ["He would never have been easy to call his action anything else than duty; but in this case, contending motives thrust him back into negations.", "그는 자기 행동을 의무 말고 다른 무엇으로 부르는 것을 결코 마음 편히 여기지 못할 사람이었다. 하지만 이 경우에는 상충하는 동기들이 그를 부정의 상태로 밀어 넣었다."]], [["Should he apply directly to Mr. Brooke, and demand of that troublesome gentleman to revoke his proposal?", "브룩 씨에게 직접 청하여, 그 성가신 신사에게 제안을 철회하라고 요구해야 할까?"], ["Or should he consult Sir James Chettam, and get him to concur in remonstrance against a step which touched the whole family?", "아니면 제임스 체텀 경에게 상의하여, 온 집안과 관련된 그런 조치에 반대하는 항의에 동조하도록 해야 할까?"], ["In either case Mr. Casaubon was aware that failure was just as probable as success.", "어느 경우든 캐소본 씨는 실패가 성공만큼이나 있을 법하다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["It was impossible for him to mention Dorothea’s name in the matter, and without some alarming urgency Mr. Brooke was as likely as not, after meeting all representations with apparent assent, to wind up by saying, “Never fear, Casaubon!", "그가 이 문제에서 도러시아의 이름을 입에 올리는 것은 불가능했고, 무언가 위급함을 알리지 않는 한 브룩 씨는 모든 진술에 겉으로는 동의하다가도, 마지막에 가서 \"걱정 마시오, 캐소본!"], ["Depend upon it, young Ladislaw will do you credit.", "내 장담하건대 젊은 래디슬로는 당신 체면을 세워 줄 거요."], ["Depend upon it, I have put my finger on the right thing.” And Mr.", "내 장담하건대 나는 딱 맞는 일을 짚어 냈소\"라고 말할 공산이 적지 않았다."], ["Casaubon shrank nervously from communicating on the subject with Sir James Chettam, between whom and himself there had never been any cordiality, and who would immediately think of Dorothea without any mention of her.", "그리고 캐소본 씨는 제임스 체텀 경과 이 문제로 의논하는 것을 신경질적으로 꺼렸으니, 둘 사이에는 한 번도 살가움이 없었던 데다, 그는 도러시아의 이름을 입에 올리지 않아도 즉각 도러시아를 떠올릴 사람이었기 때문이다."]], [["Poor Mr. Casaubon was distrustful of everybody’s feeling towards him, especially as a husband.", "가엾은 캐소본 씨는 자기를 향한 모든 이의 감정을, 특히 남편으로서의 자기를 향한 감정을 미덥지 않게 여겼다."], ["To let any one suppose that he was jealous would be to admit their (suspected) view of his disadvantages: to let them know that he did not find marriage particularly blissful would imply his conversion to their (probably) earlier disapproval.", "자기가 질투한다고 누군가 짐작하게 두는 것은, 자기 약점에 대한 그들의 (의심되는) 견해를 인정하는 셈이었다. 자기가 결혼을 그리 더없이 행복하다고 여기지 않는다는 것을 그들이 알게 두는 것은, 자기가 그들의 (아마도) 이전의 반대로 돌아섰음을 함축하는 셈이었다."], ["It would be as bad as letting Carp, and Brasenose generally, know how backward he was in organizing the matter for his “Key to all Mythologies.” All through his life Mr.", "그것은 자기 「모든 신화의 열쇠」를 짜내는 일에서 자기가 얼마나 뒤처져 있는지를 카프와 브레이즈노스 사람들 전반에게 알게 두는 것만큼이나 나쁜 일이었다."], ["Casaubon had been trying not to admit even to himself the inward sores of self-doubt and jealousy.", "평생토록 캐소본 씨는 자기 의심과 질투라는 내면의 상처를 자기 자신에게조차 인정하지 않으려 애써 왔다."], ["And on the most delicate of all personal subjects, the habit of proud suspicious reticence told doubly.", "그리고 모든 개인적 문제 가운데 가장 미묘한 이 문제에서, 자존심 강하고 의심 많은 과묵함의 습관은 이중으로 작용했다."]], [["Thus Mr. Casaubon remained proudly, bitterly silent.", "이리하여 캐소본 씨는 자존심에 차서, 쓰라리게 침묵을 지켰다."], ["But he had forbidden Will to come to Lowick Manor, and he was mentally preparing other measures of frustration.", "하지만 그는 윌이 로윅 저택에 오는 것을 금했고, 마음속으로는 다른 좌절의 방책들을 마련하고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXXVIII.", "# CHAPTER XXXVIII."]], [["“C’est beaucoup que le jugement des hommes sur les actions humaines; tôt ou tard il devient efficace.”—GUIZOT.", "“인간의 행위에 대한 사람들의 판단은 대단한 것이니, 늦든 빠르든 그것은 힘을 발휘하게 된다.” —기조"]], [["Sir James Chettam could not look with any satisfaction on Mr.", "* 기조: 프랑수아 기조(1787~1874), 프랑스의 역사가이자 정치가."], ["Brooke’s new courses; but it was easier to object than to hinder.", "제임스 체텀 경은 브룩 씨의 새로운 행보를 아무리 보아도 흡족할 수 없었다."], ["Sir James accounted for his having come in alone one day to lunch with the Cadwalladers by saying—", "그러나 반대하기는 쉬워도 막기는 어려운 일이었다. 제임스 경은 어느 날 혼자서 캐드월러더 부부와 점심을 함께하러 온 까닭을 이렇게 설명했다."]], [["“I can’t talk to you as I want, before Celia: it might hurt her.", "“실리아 앞에서는 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 수가 없습니다. 그 애가 상처받을지도 모르니까요."], ["Indeed, it would not be right.”", "사실, 그건 옳지 않은 일이지요.”"]], [["“I know what you mean—the ‘Pioneer’ at the Grange!”", "“무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요. 그레인지의 그 『파이어니어』 말이지요!”"], ["darted in Mrs. Cadwallader, almost before the last word was off her friend’s tongue.", "캐드월러더 부인이 친구의 입에서 마지막 단어가 떨어지기도 전에 끼어들었다."], ["“It is frightful—this taking to buying whistles and blowing them in everybody’s hearing.", "“정말 끔찍한 일이에요. 호루라기를 사들여서 아무나 다 듣게 불어대다니 말이에요."], ["Lying in bed all day and playing at dominoes, like poor Lord Plessy, would be more private and bearable.”", "가엾은 플레시 경처럼 온종일 침대에 누워 도미노 놀이나 하는 편이 그래도 더 은밀하고 견딜 만하겠어요.”"]], [["“I see they are beginning to attack our friend Brooke in the ‘Trumpet,’”", "“『트럼펫』에서 우리 친구 브룩을 공격하기 시작한 것 같던데요.”"], ["said the Rector, lounging back and smiling easily, as he would have done if he had been attacked himself.", "목사가 의자에 느긋하게 몸을 기대며, 자신이 공격받기라도 한 듯 편안한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“There are tremendous sarcasms against a landlord not a hundred miles from Middlemarch, who receives his own rents, and makes no returns.”", "“미들마치에서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사는 어떤 지주를 두고 굉장한 풍자를 퍼붓고 있어요. 자기 지대는 꼬박꼬박 받으면서 환원은 전혀 하지 않는 지주라고 말이지요.”"]], [["“I do wish Brooke would leave that off,”", "“브룩이 정말 그 짓을 그만두면 좋겠습니다.”"], ["said Sir James, with his little frown of annoyance.", "제임스 경이 짜증스러운 듯 살짝 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["“Is he really going to be put in nomination, though?”", "“그런데 그 사람, 정말로 후보로 지명될 모양인가요?”"], ["said Mr. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 씨가 물었다."], ["“I saw Farebrother yesterday—he’s Whiggish himself, hoists Brougham and Useful Knowledge; that’s the worst I know of him;—and he says that Brooke is getting up a pretty strong party.", "“어제 페어브러더를 만났는데—그 사람도 휘그파라서 브로엄이며 ‘유익한 지식’ 따위를 떠받들지요. 내가 그에게서 알아낸 최악의 점이 그거예요—그가 말하길 브룩이 제법 강한 세력을 모으고 있다더군요."], ["Bulstrode, the banker, is his foremost man.", "은행가 벌스트로드가 그의 최측근이라고요."], ["But he thinks Brooke would come off badly at a nomination.”", "하지만 그는 브룩이 지명에서 형편없이 될 거라고 보더군요.”"]], [["“Exactly,”", "“바로 그렇습니다.”"], ["said Sir James, with earnestness.", "제임스 경이 진지하게 말했다."], ["“I have been inquiring into the thing, for I’ve never known anything about Middlemarch politics before—the county being my business.", "“나도 그 일을 알아보고 있었습니다. 전에는 미들마치 정치에 대해 아무것도 몰랐거든요—주(州) 일이 내 관할이니까요."], ["What Brooke trusts to, is that they are going to turn out Oliver because he is a Peelite.", "브룩이 믿고 있는 건, 올리버가 필파라서 사람들이 그를 끌어내리려 한다는 점입니다."], ["But Hawley tells me that if they send up a Whig at all it is sure to be Bagster, one of those candidates who come from heaven knows where, but dead against Ministers, and an experienced Parliamentary man.", "하지만 홀리가 내게 말하길, 만약 그들이 휘그 후보를 내세운다면 틀림없이 배그스터일 거라더군요. 어디서 굴러왔는지 알 수 없는 후보 중 하나지만, 내각에 결사반대하는 노련한 의회 인사라고요."], ["Hawley’s rather rough: he forgot that he was speaking to me.", "홀리는 좀 거친 사람이라, 자기가 누구에게 말하고 있는지 잊더군요."], ["He said if Brooke wanted a pelting, he could get it cheaper than by going to the hustings.”", "브룩이 매질을 당하고 싶다면, 선거 연단에 나갈 것 없이 더 싸게 당할 수 있다고 하더이다.”"]], [["“I warned you all of it,” said Mrs.", "“내가 그 모든 걸 다 경고했잖아요.”"], ["Cadwallader, waving her hands outward.", "캐드월러더 부인이 두 손을 바깥으로 내저으며 말했다."], ["“I said to Humphrey long ago, Mr.", "“오래전에 험프리에게 말했어요."], ["Brooke is going to make a splash in the mud.", "브룩 씨가 진창에서 첨벙대는 꼴을 보일 거라고요."], ["And now he has done it.”", "그리고 지금 정말 그렇게 됐잖아요.”"]], [["“Well, he might have taken it into his head to marry,”", "“글쎄요, 차라리 결혼이나 할 생각을 했더라면 좋았을 텐데요.”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“That would have been a graver mess than a little flirtation with politics.”", "“그게 정치를 가지고 잠깐 추파를 던지는 것보다는 더 심각한 난장판이었겠지만요.”"]], [["“He may do that afterwards,” said Mrs.", "“그건 나중에 할 수도 있겠죠.”"], ["Cadwallader—“when he has come out on the other side of the mud with an ague.”", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다. “학질에 걸린 채 진창 반대편으로 빠져나온 다음에 말이에요.”"]], [["“What I care for most is his own dignity,”", "“내가 무엇보다 신경 쓰이는 건 그 사람 자신의 품위입니다.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“Of course I care the more because of the family.", "“물론 가족 때문에 더 마음이 쓰이는 거지요."], ["But he’s getting on in life now, and I don’t like to think of his exposing himself.", "하지만 그분은 이제 나이도 들어가는데, 스스로를 웃음거리로 만드는 걸 생각하면 마음이 좋지 않습니다."], ["They will be raking up everything against him.”", "사람들이 그분에 대한 온갖 일을 들춰낼 테니까요.”"]], [["“I suppose it’s no use trying any persuasion,”", "“설득을 해봐야 소용없겠지요.”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“There’s such an odd mixture of obstinacy and changeableness in Brooke.", "“브룩에게는 고집과 변덕이 어찌나 묘하게 뒤섞여 있는지요."], ["Have you tried him on the subject?”", "그 문제로 그분께 말씀해보셨나요?”"]], [["“Well, no,”", "“글쎄요, 아니요.”"], ["said Sir James; “I feel a delicacy in appearing to dictate.", "제임스 경이 말했다. “지시하는 것처럼 보이는 게 좀 조심스러워서요."], ["But I have been talking to this young Ladislaw that Brooke is making a factotum of.", "하지만 브룩이 만능 심부름꾼처럼 부리는 그 젊은 래디슬로와는 얘기를 나눠봤습니다."], ["Ladislaw seems clever enough for anything.", "래디슬로는 무슨 일이든 해낼 만큼 영리해 보이더군요."], ["I thought it as well to hear what he had to say; and he is against Brooke’s standing this time.", "그가 뭐라고 하는지 들어보는 게 좋겠다 싶었어요. 그런데 그는 이번에 브룩이 출마하는 것에 반대합니다."], ["I think he’ll turn him round: I think the nomination may be staved off.”", "내 생각에 그가 그분 마음을 돌려놓을 것 같아요. 지명을 막을 수 있을 것 같습니다.”"]], [["“I know,”", "“그렇겠죠.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader, nodding.", "캐드월러더 부인이 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["“The independent member hasn’t got his speeches well enough by heart.”", "“그 무소속 의원 양반이 아직 연설을 충분히 외우지 못한 모양이니까요.”"]], [["“But this Ladislaw—there again is a vexatious business,”", "“그런데 이 래디슬로라는 자—그게 또 골치 아픈 문제예요.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“We have had him two or three times to dine at the Hall (you have met him, by the bye) as Brooke’s guest and a relation of Casaubon’s, thinking he was only on a flying visit.", "“브룩의 손님이자 캐소본의 친척이라고 해서, 두세 번 저택 만찬에 그를 불렀거든요(그러고 보니 부인께서도 그를 만나셨지요). 그저 잠깐 들른 거라고만 생각했어요."], ["And now I find he’s in everybody’s mouth in Middlemarch as the editor of the ‘Pioneer.’ There are stories going about him as a quill-driving alien, a foreign emissary, and what not.”", "그런데 지금 보니 그가 『파이어니어』의 편집장이라고 미들마치 사람들 입에 다 오르내리고 있더군요. 펜대나 굴리는 외지인이니, 외국에서 온 밀정이니, 별의별 소문이 다 돌고 있습니다.”"]], [["“Casaubon won’t like that,”", "“캐소본이 그걸 달가워하지 않겠군요.”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."]], [["“There is some foreign blood in Ladislaw,”", "“래디슬로에게는 분명 외국 피가 좀 섞여 있어요.”"], ["returned Sir James.", "제임스 경이 대답했다."], ["“I hope he won’t go into extreme opinions and carry Brooke on.”", "“극단적인 견해에 빠져들어 브룩까지 끌고 가는 일이 없으면 좋겠는데요.”"]], [["“Oh, he’s a dangerous young sprig, that Mr.", "“오, 그 래디슬로 씨는 위험한 애송이예요.”"], ["Ladislaw,” said Mrs.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["Cadwallader, “with his opera songs and his ready tongue.", "“오페라 곡조나 흥얼대고 입담도 청산유수지요."], ["A sort of Byronic hero—an amorous conspirator, it strikes me.", "일종의 바이런풍 영웅이랄까—내가 보기엔 호색적인 음모가예요."], ["And Thomas Aquinas is not fond of him.", "게다가 토마스 아퀴나스도 그를 좋아하지 않아요."], ["I could see that, the day the picture was brought.”", "그림이 도착하던 날 그걸 알아챘지요.”"]], [["“I don’t like to begin on the subject with Casaubon,”", "“캐소본과 이 문제를 꺼내기는 좀 꺼려집니다.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“He has more right to interfere than I.", "“나보다는 그가 끼어들 권리가 더 많으니까요."], ["But it’s a disagreeable affair all round.", "하지만 어느 모로 보나 거북한 일이에요."], ["What a character for anybody with decent connections to show himself in!—one of those newspaper fellows!", "변변한 연고가 있는 사람이 그런 식으로 나서다니 무슨 꼴입니까!—그 신문쟁이들 중 하나라니요!"], ["You have only to look at Keck, who manages the ‘Trumpet.’ I saw him the other day with Hawley.", "『트럼펫』을 운영하는 켁만 봐도 알 만하지요. 며칠 전에 홀리와 함께 있는 걸 봤어요."], ["His writing is sound enough, I believe, but he’s such a low fellow, that I wished he had been on the wrong side.”", "글솜씨야 제법 탄탄한 것 같지만, 어찌나 천박한 작자인지, 차라리 반대편에 있었으면 싶더군요.”"]], [["“What can you expect with these peddling Middlemarch papers?”", "“이런 시시한 미들마치 신문들에서 뭘 기대하겠습니까?”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“I don’t suppose you could get a high style of man anywhere to be writing up interests he doesn’t really care about, and for pay that hardly keeps him in at elbows.”", "“정말로는 관심도 없는 이익을 떠받들고, 입에 풀칠하기도 빠듯한 보수를 받으면서 글을 쓸 고상한 인물을 어디서 구하겠어요.”"]], [["“Exactly: that makes it so annoying that Brooke should have put a man who has a sort of connection with the family in a position of that kind.", "“바로 그겁니다. 그래서 브룩이 가족과 어느 정도 연이 있는 사람을 그런 자리에 앉혔다는 게 그토록 짜증스러운 거지요."], ["For my part, I think Ladislaw is rather a fool for accepting.”", "내 생각엔 래디슬로도 그걸 받아들이다니 어리석은 사람이에요.”"]], [["“It is Aquinas’s fault,”", "“그건 아퀴나스 잘못이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“Why didn’t he use his interest to get Ladislaw made an attache or sent to India?", "“자기 연줄을 써서 래디슬로를 외교관 수행원으로 만들거나 인도로 보내버리면 됐잖아요."], ["That is how families get rid of troublesome sprigs.”", "집안에서 골칫덩어리 애송이를 처리하는 방법이 그거 아니겠어요.”"]], [["“There is no knowing to what lengths the mischief may go,”", "“이 말썽이 어디까지 번질지 알 수가 없어요.”"], ["said Sir James, anxiously.", "제임스 경이 근심스럽게 말했다."], ["“But if Casaubon says nothing, what can I do?”", "“하지만 캐소본이 아무 말도 하지 않는다면, 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?”"]], [["“Oh my dear Sir James,”", "“오, 제임스 경.”"], ["said the Rector, “don’t let us make too much of all this.", "목사가 말했다. “이 일을 너무 대단하게 여기지는 맙시다."], ["It is likely enough to end in mere smoke.", "그저 연기처럼 흩어져 끝날 공산이 큽니다."], ["After a month or two Brooke and this Master Ladislaw will get tired of each other; Ladislaw will take wing; Brooke will sell the ‘Pioneer,’ and everything will settle down again as usual.”", "한두 달 지나면 브룩과 이 래디슬로 군은 서로 싫증이 날 테고, 래디슬로는 날아가 버릴 거예요. 브룩은 『파이어니어』를 팔아치울 거고, 모든 게 다시 평소대로 가라앉겠지요.”"]], [["“There is one good chance—that he will not like to feel his money oozing away,”", "“좋은 가능성이 하나 있어요. 그가 자기 돈이 줄줄 새어 나가는 걸 느끼고 싶어 하지 않으리란 거죠.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“If I knew the items of election expenses I could scare him.", "“내가 선거 비용 항목들을 알기만 한다면 그를 겁줄 수 있을 텐데요."], ["It’s no use plying him with wide words like Expenditure: I wouldn’t talk of phlebotomy, I would empty a pot of leeches upon him.", "‘지출’ 같은 두루뭉술한 말로 다그쳐봐야 소용없어요. 나라면 사혈(瀉血) 같은 말은 꺼내지도 않고, 거머리가 든 단지를 통째로 그에게 쏟아붓겠어요."], ["What we good stingy people don’t like, is having our sixpences sucked away from us.”", "우리처럼 알뜰한 사람들이 가장 싫어하는 건, 6펜스짜리 동전을 쪽쪽 빨려 나가는 거니까요.”"]], [["“And he will not like having things raked up against him,”", "“그리고 그는 자기에 대한 일들이 들춰지는 걸 싫어할 거예요.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“There is the management of his estate.", "“그분 사유지 관리만 봐도 그렇지요."], ["They have begun upon that already.", "벌써 그걸 물고 늘어지기 시작했어요."], ["And it really is painful for me to see.", "그리고 나로서는 정말 보기 괴롭습니다."], ["It is a nuisance under one’s very nose.", "바로 코앞에서 벌어지는 골칫거리니까요."], ["I do think one is bound to do the best for one’s land and tenants, especially in these hard times.”", "사람은 누구나 자기 땅과 소작인들을 위해 최선을 다해야 한다고 봐요. 특히 이런 어려운 시절에는 말이지요.”"]], [["“Perhaps the ‘Trumpet’ may rouse him to make a change, and some good may come of it all,”", "“어쩌면 『트럼펫』이 그를 자극해서 변화를 일으키게 할지도 모르지요. 그러면 이 모든 일에서 무언가 좋은 게 나올 수도 있고요.”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“I know I should be glad.", "“나야 그러면 기쁘겠지요."], ["I should hear less grumbling when my tithe is paid.", "십일조를 낼 때 불평을 덜 듣게 될 테니까요."], ["I don’t know what I should do if there were not a modus in Tipton.”", "팁턴에 정액 대납 관행이 없었다면 어쩔 뻔했나 싶어요.”"]], [["“I want him to have a proper man to look after things—I want him to take on Garth again,”", "* 정액 대납: 십일조를 현물 대신 고정된 금액으로 대신 내는 관행. “나는 그분이 제대로 된 사람을 들여서 일을 맡기길 바랍니다—다시 가스를 고용하길 바라요.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“He got rid of Garth twelve years ago, and everything has been going wrong since.", "“십이 년 전에 가스를 내보낸 뒤로 모든 게 엉망이 되어왔으니까요."], ["I think of getting Garth to manage for me—he has made such a capital plan for my buildings; and Lovegood is hardly up to the mark.", "나는 가스에게 내 일을 맡길까 생각 중이에요—내 건물 일에 정말 훌륭한 설계를 해주었거든요. 그리고 러브굿은 기대에 좀 못 미쳐요."], ["But Garth would not undertake the Tipton estate again unless Brooke left it entirely to him.”", "하지만 가스는 브룩이 전권을 완전히 넘겨주지 않는 한 다시는 팁턴 사유지를 맡지 않을 겁니다.”"]], [["“In the right of it too,”", "“그것도 당연한 일이지요.”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“Garth is an independent fellow: an original, simple-minded fellow.", "“가스는 독립적인 사람이에요. 독창적이고 소박한 사람이죠."], ["One day, when he was doing some valuation for me, he told me point-blank that clergymen seldom understood anything about business, and did mischief when they meddled; but he said it as quietly and respectfully as if he had been talking to me about sailors.", "언젠가 그가 내 일로 평가를 해주던 날, 성직자들은 사업에 대해 아는 게 거의 없으면서 끼어들어 일을 망친다고 대놓고 말하더군요. 하지만 마치 뱃사람 얘기라도 하듯 차분하고 정중하게 말했어요."], ["He would make a different parish of Tipton, if Brooke would let him manage.", "브룩이 그에게 관리를 맡기기만 한다면 팁턴을 전혀 다른 교구로 만들어놓을 겁니다."], ["I wish, by the help of the ‘Trumpet,’ you could bring that round.”", "『트럼펫』의 도움을 받아 그렇게 되도록 해주시면 좋겠어요.”"]], [["“If Dorothea had kept near her uncle, there would have been some chance,”", "“도러시아가 삼촌 곁에 머물렀다면 가능성이 좀 있었을 텐데요.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“She might have got some power over him in time, and she was always uneasy about the estate.", "“시간이 지나면서 그분께 어느 정도 영향력을 가졌을지도 몰라요. 그 애는 늘 사유지를 걱정했으니까요."], ["She had wonderfully good notions about such things.", "그런 일에 놀랄 만큼 좋은 식견이 있었어요."], ["But now Casaubon takes her up entirely.", "하지만 지금은 캐소본이 그 애를 온통 차지하고 있지요."], ["Celia complains a good deal.", "실리아도 불평이 이만저만이 아닙니다."], ["We can hardly get her to dine with us, since he had that fit.”", "그분이 그 발작을 일으킨 뒤로는 그 애를 우리 집 만찬에 부르기도 어려워졌어요.”"], ["Sir James ended with a look of pitying disgust, and Mrs. Cadwallader shrugged her shoulders as much as to say that she was not likely to see anything new in that direction.", "제임스 경은 측은함과 역겨움이 뒤섞인 표정으로 말을 맺었고, 캐드월러더 부인은 어깨를 으쓱하며 자기는 그쪽에서 새로운 일이 생기리라고 보지 않는다는 뜻을 내비쳤다."]], [["“Poor Casaubon!”", "“가엾은 캐소본!”"], ["the Rector said.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“That was a nasty attack.", "“고약한 발작이었지요."], ["I thought he looked shattered the other day at the Archdeacon’s.”", "며칠 전 부주교 댁에서 봤을 때 무척 쇠약해 보이더군요.”"]], [["“In point of fact,”", "“사실은 말입니다.”"], ["resumed Sir James, not choosing to dwell on “fits,”", "제임스 경이 ‘발작’ 이야기는 길게 끌고 싶지 않은 듯 말을 이었다."], ["“Brooke doesn’t mean badly by his tenants or any one else, but he has got that way of paring and clipping at expenses.”", "“브룩이 소작인들이나 다른 누구에게 악의가 있는 건 아니에요. 다만 비용을 깎고 잘라내는 그 버릇이 문제지요.”"]], [["“Come, that’s a blessing,”", "“어머, 그거야말로 다행이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“That helps him to find himself in a morning.", "“그 덕분에 아침마다 제정신을 차리는 거니까요."], ["He may not know his own opinions, but he does know his own pocket.”", "자기 의견은 잘 모를지 몰라도 자기 주머니 사정만은 잘 아는 사람이거든요.”"]], [["“I don’t believe a man is in pocket by stinginess on his land,”", "“땅에 인색하게 굴어서 돈을 버는 사람이 있다고는 생각하지 않습니다.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, stinginess may be abused like other virtues: it will not do to keep one’s own pigs lean,”", "“오, 인색함도 다른 미덕처럼 남용될 수 있지요. 자기 돼지를 비쩍 마르게 키우면 안 되는 법이니까요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader, who had risen to look out of the window.", "창밖을 내다보려고 일어선 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“But talk of an independent politician and he will appear.”", "“그런데 무소속 정치인 얘기를 하니 바로 그가 나타나는군요.”"]], [["“What!", "“뭐라고요!"], ["Brooke?”", "브룩이?”"], ["said her husband.", "남편이 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "“네."], ["Now, you ply him with the ‘Trumpet,’ Humphrey; and I will put the leeches on him.", "자, 험프리, 당신은 『트럼펫』으로 그를 다그치세요. 나는 그에게 거머리를 붙이겠어요."], ["What will you do, Sir James?”", "제임스 경은 어떻게 하시겠어요?”"]], [["“The fact is, I don’t like to begin about it with Brooke, in our mutual position; the whole thing is so unpleasant.", "“사실은, 서로 처지가 처지인지라 브룩과 그 얘기를 꺼내기가 영 내키지 않습니다. 모든 게 너무나 불쾌한 일이라서요."], ["I do wish people would behave like gentlemen,”", "사람들이 신사답게 처신해주면 좋으련만.”"], ["said the good baronet, feeling that this was a simple and comprehensive programme for social well-being.", "마음씨 좋은 준남작이 말했다. 그는 이것이 사회의 안녕을 위한 간단하고도 포괄적인 강령이라고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["“Here you all are, eh?”", "“다들 여기 계셨군요, 응?”"], ["said Mr. Brooke, shuffling round and shaking hands.", "브룩 씨가 발을 끌며 둘러보고 악수를 청하며 말했다."], ["“I was going up to the Hall by-and-by, Chettam.", "“이따가 저택으로 갈 참이었네, 체텀."], ["But it’s pleasant to find everybody, you know.", "그래도 다들 모인 걸 보니 반갑구먼, 알다시피."], ["Well, what do you think of things?—going on a little fast!", "자, 요즘 돌아가는 일들을 어떻게들 보시오?—좀 빠르게 굴러가고 있지!"], ["It was true enough, what Lafitte said—‘Since yesterday, a century has passed away:’—they’re in the next century, you know, on the other side of the water.", "라피트가 한 말이 딱 맞았어—‘어제 이후로 한 세기가 지나갔다’고 말이야. 알다시피 바다 건너 저쪽 사람들은 벌써 다음 세기에 살고 있지."], ["Going on faster than we are.”", "우리보다 빠르게 나아가고 있어.”"]], [["“Why, yes,”", "“그렇고말고요.”"], ["said the Rector, taking up the newspaper.", "목사가 신문을 집어 들며 말했다."], ["“Here is the ‘Trumpet’ accusing you of lagging behind—did you see?”", "“여기 『트럼펫』에서 당신이 뒤처져 있다고 비난하는군요—보셨소?”"]], [["“Eh? no,”", "“응?"], ["said Mr.", "아니.”"], ["Brooke, dropping his gloves into his hat and hastily adjusting his eye-glass.", "브룩 씨가 모자 안에 장갑을 떨어뜨리고 황급히 안경을 고쳐 쓰며 말했다."], ["But Mr. Cadwallader kept the paper in his hand, saying, with a smile in his eyes—", "그러나 캐드월러더 씨는 눈에 미소를 띤 채 신문을 손에 든 채로 말했다."]], [["“Look here!", "“여기 보세요!"], ["all this is about a landlord not a hundred miles from Middlemarch, who receives his own rents.", "이게 전부 미들마치에서 백 마일도 떨어지지 않은 곳에 사는, 자기 지대를 꼬박꼬박 받는 어떤 지주에 관한 얘기예요."], ["They say he is the most retrogressive man in the county.", "그를 두고 주 안에서 가장 퇴행적인 사람이라고 하는군요."], ["I think you must have taught them that word in the ‘Pioneer.’”", "아마 당신이 『파이어니어』에서 그들에게 그 단어를 가르쳐준 모양이지요.”"]], [["“Oh, that is Keck—an illiterate fellow, you know.", "“오, 그건 켁이야—알다시피 무식한 작자지."], ["Retrogressive, now!", "퇴행적이라니, 원!"], ["Come, that’s capital.", "거참, 걸작이로군."], ["He thinks it means destructive: they want to make me out a destructive, you know,”", "그자는 그게 파괴적이란 뜻인 줄 아는 거야. 알다시피 나를 파괴분자로 몰아세우려는 거지.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke, with that cheerfulness which is usually sustained by an adversary’s ignorance.", "보통 적수의 무지에서 비롯되곤 하는 그 쾌활함을 띠고 브룩 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I think he knows the meaning of the word.", "“내 생각엔 그 사람도 그 단어 뜻을 알고 있어요."], ["Here is a sharp stroke or two.", "여기 날카로운 일격이 한두 번 나오는군요."], ["If we had to describe a man who is retrogressive in the most evil sense of the word—we should say, he is one who would dub himself a reformer of our constitution, while every interest for which he is immediately responsible is going to decay: a philanthropist who cannot bear one rogue to be hanged, but does not mind five honest tenants being half-starved: a man who shrieks at corruption, and keeps his farms at rack-rent: who roars himself red at rotten boroughs, and does not mind if every field on his farms has a rotten gate: a man very open-hearted to Leeds and Manchester, no doubt; he would give any number of representatives who will pay for their seats out of their own pockets: what he objects to giving, is a little return on rent-days to help a tenant to buy stock, or an outlay on repairs to keep the weather out at a tenant’s barn-door or make his house look a little less like an Irish cottier’s.", "‘우리가 가장 사악한 의미에서 퇴행적인 사람을 묘사해야 한다면, 그는 자신이 직접 책임지고 있는 모든 이익이 썩어가는 동안에도 스스로 우리 헌정의 개혁가를 자처하는 자라고 하겠다. 악당 하나가 교수형 당하는 것은 차마 못 보면서, 정직한 소작인 다섯이 반쯤 굶주리는 것은 개의치 않는 박애주의자."], ["But we all know the wag’s definition of a philanthropist: a man whose charity increases directly as the square of the distance.", "부패를 두고 비명을 지르면서 자기 농장은 터무니없이 비싼 소작료로 묶어두는 자. 부패 선거구를 두고 얼굴이 벌게지도록 고함치면서, 정작 자기 농장의 들판마다 문짝이 썩어 있어도 아랑곳하지 않는 자."], ["And so on.", "그가 리즈와 맨체스터에는 더없이 너그러운 게 분명하다."], ["All the rest is to show what sort of legislator a philanthropist is likely to make,”", "제 주머니를 털어 의석을 사들일 의원이라면 얼마든지 내어줄 테니까. 그가 내어주기 싫어하는 것은, 소작료 납부일에 소작인이 가축을 사는 데 보태라고 환원해주는 약간의 돈, 혹은 소작인의 헛간 문으로 비바람이 새어 들지 않게 하거나 그 집이 아일랜드 빈농의 오두막보다 조금이라도 덜 초라해 보이게 할 수리 비용이다."], ["ended the Rector, throwing down the paper, and clasping his hands at the back of his head, while he looked at Mr.", "하지만 박애주의자에 대한 익살꾼의 정의를 우리 모두 알지 않는가. 그 자선이 거리의 제곱에 정비례해 커지는 사람이라고.’ 이런 식이에요."], ["Brooke with an air of amused neutrality.", "나머지는 전부 박애주의자가 어떤 입법자가 되기 십상인지를 보여주는 내용이고요.” 목사가 신문을 내던지고 두 손을 뒤통수에 깍지 낀 채, 재미있어하는 중립적인 태도로 브룩 씨를 바라보며 말을 맺었다."]], [["“Come, that’s rather good, you know,” said Mr.", "“거참, 제법 잘 썼군, 알다시피.”"], ["Brooke, taking up the paper and trying to bear the attack as easily as his neighbor did, but coloring and smiling rather nervously; “that about roaring himself red at rotten boroughs—I never made a speech about rotten boroughs in my life.", "브룩 씨가 신문을 집어 들며, 이웃이 그러했듯 그 공격을 태연히 받아넘기려 애썼으나 얼굴을 붉히고 다소 초조하게 미소 지으며 말했다. “부패 선거구를 두고 얼굴이 벌게지도록 고함친다는 그 대목—나는 평생 부패 선거구에 관해 연설한 적이 한 번도 없네."], ["And as to roaring myself red and that kind of thing—these men never understand what is good satire.", "얼굴이 벌게지도록 고함치니 뭐니 하는 것도 그래—이런 자들은 좋은 풍자가 뭔지 도무지 몰라."], ["Satire, you know, should be true up to a certain point.", "알다시피 풍자란 어느 정도까지는 사실이어야 하는 거야."], ["I recollect they said that in ‘The Edinburgh’ somewhere—it must be true up to a certain point.”", "『에든버러』 어딘가에서 그렇게 말한 걸 기억해—어느 정도까지는 사실이어야 한다고 말이야.”"]], [["“Well, that is really a hit about the gates,”", "“글쎄요, 문짝에 관한 건 정말 정곡을 찌른 얘기예요.”"], ["said Sir James, anxious to tread carefully.", "제임스 경이 조심스럽게 디디려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“Dagley complained to me the other day that he hadn’t got a decent gate on his farm.", "“요전 날 대글리가 내게 불평하기를, 자기 농장에 변변한 문짝 하나 없다더군요."], ["Garth has invented a new pattern of gate—I wish you would try it.", "가스가 새로운 문짝 양식을 고안했는데—한번 써보시면 좋겠습니다."], ["One ought to use some of one’s timber in that way.”", "목재는 마땅히 그런 데 좀 써야지요.”"]], [["“You go in for fancy farming, you know, Chettam,” said Mr.", "“자네는 별난 영농에 빠져 있잖나, 체텀, 알다시피.”"], ["Brooke, appearing to glance over the columns of the “Trumpet.”", "브룩 씨가 『트럼펫』의 지면을 훑어보는 척하며 말했다."], ["“That’s your hobby, and you don’t mind the expense.”", "“그게 자네 취미니까 비용은 개의치 않는 거지.”"]], [["“I thought the most expensive hobby in the world was standing for Parliament,”", "“세상에서 가장 돈이 많이 드는 취미는 의회 출마인 줄 알았는데요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“They said the last unsuccessful candidate at Middlemarch—Giles, wasn’t his name?—spent ten thousand pounds and failed because he did not bribe enough.", "“미들마치에서 지난번에 낙선한 후보가—이름이 자일스였나요?—만 파운드를 쓰고도 뇌물을 충분히 안 써서 떨어졌다더군요."], ["What a bitter reflection for a man!”", "그 사람으로서는 얼마나 쓰라린 일이겠어요!”"]], [["“Somebody was saying,”", "“누군가가 그러는데,”"], ["said the Rector, laughingly, “that East Retford was nothing to Middlemarch, for bribery.”", "목사가 웃으며 말했다. “뇌물 쓰는 걸로 치면 이스트 레트퍼드도 미들마치에 비하면 아무것도 아니라더군요.”"]], [["“Nothing of the kind,”", "“천만에.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“The Tories bribe, you know: Hawley and his set bribe with treating, hot codlings, and that sort of thing; and they bring the voters drunk to the poll.", "“알다시피 뇌물은 토리당이 쓰지. 홀리와 그 패거리는 향응이며 따끈한 군밤이며 그런 걸로 매수하고, 유권자들을 술에 취하게 해서 투표소로 끌고 간다네."], ["But they are not going to have it their own way in future—not in future, you know.", "하지만 앞으로는 자기들 멋대로 하지 못할 거야—알다시피 앞으로는 말이지."], ["Middlemarch is a little backward, I admit—the freemen are a little backward.", "미들마치가 좀 뒤처져 있다는 건 인정해—자유민들이 좀 뒤처져 있지."], ["But we shall educate them—we shall bring them on, you know.", "하지만 우리가 그들을 교육할 거야—알다시피 우리가 그들을 끌어올릴 거라고."], ["The best people there are on our side.”", "그곳의 가장 훌륭한 사람들은 우리 편이니까.”"]], [["“Hawley says you have men on your side who will do you harm,”", "“홀리 말로는 당신 편에 당신에게 해를 끼칠 사람들이 있다더군요.”"], ["remarked Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“He says Bulstrode the banker will do you harm.”", "“은행가 벌스트로드가 당신에게 해가 될 거라고요.”"]], [["“And that if you got pelted,” interposed Mrs.", "“그리고 만약 당신이 매질을 당하게 되면,”"], ["Cadwallader, “half the rotten eggs would mean hatred of your committee-man.", "캐드월러더 부인이 끼어들었다. “던져지는 썩은 달걀의 절반은 당신 위원회 사람에 대한 증오를 뜻할 거예요."], ["Good heavens!", "세상에!"], ["Think what it must be to be pelted for wrong opinions.", "잘못된 견해 때문에 매질당하는 게 어떤 건지 생각해보세요."], ["And I seem to remember a story of a man they pretended to chair and let him fall into a dust-heap on purpose!”", "그리고 사람들이 어떤 사람을 헹가래 치는 척하다가 일부러 쓰레기 더미에 떨어뜨렸다는 얘기를 들은 기억이 나네요!”"]], [["“Pelting is nothing to their finding holes in one’s coat,”", "“던져 맞는 거야 사람들이 외투에서 구멍을 찾아내는 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니지요.”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“I confess that’s what I should be afraid of, if we parsons had to stand at the hustings for preferment.", "“솔직히 우리 목사들이 성직록을 얻으려고 선거 연단에 서야 한다면, 내가 두려워할 게 바로 그겁니다."], ["I should be afraid of their reckoning up all my fishing days.", "내가 낚시하러 다닌 날들을 죄다 헤아릴까 봐 겁이 나겠지요."], ["Upon my word, I think the truth is the hardest missile one can be pelted with.”", "정말이지, 진실이야말로 사람이 던져 맞을 수 있는 가장 견디기 힘든 무기라고 생각해요.”"]], [["“The fact is,”", "“사실은 말입니다,”"], ["said Sir James, “if a man goes into public life he must be prepared for the consequences.", "제임스 경이 말했다. “공적인 삶에 발을 들이는 사람이라면 그 결과를 각오해야지요."], ["He must make himself proof against calumny.”", "중상모략에 끄떡없도록 스스로를 단련해야 하고요.”"]], [["“My dear Chettam, that is all very fine, you know,”", "“여보게 체텀, 그거야 다 듣기 좋은 말이지, 알다시피.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“But how will you make yourself proof against calumny?", "“하지만 어떻게 중상모략에 끄떡없도록 자기를 단련하겠나?"], ["You should read history—look at ostracism, persecution, martyrdom, and that kind of thing.", "역사를 읽어봐야 해—도편추방이며, 박해며, 순교며, 그런 걸 보라고. 알다시피 그런 일은 늘 가장 훌륭한 사람들에게 일어나는 법이야."], ["They always happen to the best men, you know.", "그런데 호라티우스에 나오는 그 말이 뭐였더라?—‘피아트 유스티티아, 루아트…’ 뭐라뭐라 하는 거 말일세.”"], ["But what is that in Horace?—fiat justitia, ruat … something or other.”", "* 피아트 유스티티아, 루아트 카일룸: ‘하늘이 무너져도 정의를 행하라’라는 뜻의 라틴어 격언."]], [["“Exactly,”", "“바로 그겁니다.”"], ["said Sir James, with a little more heat than usual.", "제임스 경이 평소보다 좀 더 열을 띠며 말했다."], ["“What I mean by being proof against calumny is being able to point to the fact as a contradiction.”", "“내가 중상모략에 끄떡없다고 하는 건, 사실을 들어 그것이 거짓임을 입증할 수 있다는 뜻입니다.”"]], [["“And it is not martyrdom to pay bills that one has run into one’s self,”", "“그리고 자기가 직접 진 빚을 갚는 건 순교가 아니지요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."]], [["But it was Sir James’s evident annoyance that most stirred Mr. Brooke.", "그러나 브룩 씨의 마음을 가장 크게 흔든 것은 제임스 경의 역력한 짜증이었다."], ["“Well, you know, Chettam,”", "“글쎄, 알다시피 체텀,”"], ["he said, rising, taking up his hat and leaning on his stick, “you and I have a different system.", "그가 일어서서 모자를 집어 들고 지팡이에 몸을 기대며 말했다. “자네와 나는 방식이 달라."], ["You are all for outlay with your farms.", "자네는 농장에 죄다 비용을 들이려는 쪽이지."], ["I don’t want to make out that my system is good under all circumstances—under all circumstances, you know.”", "내 방식이 어떤 상황에서나 좋다고 우길 생각은 없네—어떤 상황에서나 말일세, 알다시피.”"]], [["“There ought to be a new valuation made from time to time,”", "“이따금 새로 평가를 해야 합니다.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“Returns are very well occasionally, but I like a fair valuation.", "“환원도 가끔은 좋지만, 나는 공정한 평가가 좋습니다."], ["What do you say, Cadwallader?”", "어떻게 생각하시오, 캐드월러더?”"]], [["“I agree with you.", "“동감입니다. 내가 브룩이라면 당장 『트럼펫』의 입을 막아버리겠어요."], ["If I were Brooke, I would choke the ‘Trumpet’ at once by getting Garth to make a new valuation of the farms, and giving him carte blanche about gates and repairs: that’s my view of the political situation,”", "가스에게 농장을 새로 평가하게 하고, 문짝이며 수리에 대해 전권을 주는 거지요. 그게 정치 상황에 대한 내 견해예요.”"], ["said the Rector, broadening himself by sticking his thumbs in his armholes, and laughing towards Mr. Brooke.", "목사가 양 겨드랑이에 엄지손가락을 찔러 넣고 몸을 떡 벌리며, 브룩 씨를 향해 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“That’s a showy sort of thing to do, you know,”", "“그거야 알다시피 겉만 그럴듯한 짓이지.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“But I should like you to tell me of another landlord who has distressed his tenants for arrears as little as I have.", "“하지만 나만큼 연체된 소작료로 소작인들을 닦달하지 않은 지주가 또 있으면 말해보게. 나는 오래된 소작인들을 그대로 두고 있어."], ["I let the old tenants stay on. I’m uncommonly easy, let me tell you, uncommonly easy.", "내가 보기 드물게 너그럽다는 걸 말해두지, 보기 드물게 너그럽다고."], ["I have my own ideas, and I take my stand on them, you know.", "나는 내 나름의 생각이 있고, 알다시피 그 생각을 고수하는 거야."], ["A man who does that is always charged with eccentricity, inconsistency, and that kind of thing.", "그런 사람은 늘 괴짜니, 일관성이 없느니 하는 소리를 듣게 마련이지."], ["When I change my line of action, I shall follow my own ideas.”", "내가 방침을 바꾼다면, 그건 내 나름의 생각을 따르는 게 될 걸세.”"]], [["After that, Mr. Brooke remembered that there was a packet which he had omitted to send off from the Grange, and he bade everybody hurriedly good-by.", "그러고 나서 브룩 씨는 그레인지에서 부치는 걸 깜빡한 꾸러미가 있다는 게 생각난 듯, 모두에게 황급히 작별 인사를 했다."]], [["“I didn’t want to take a liberty with Brooke,”", "“브룩에게 주제넘게 굴고 싶지 않았어요.”"], ["said Sir James; “I see he is nettled.", "제임스 경이 말했다. “그분이 발끈한 게 보이더군요."], ["But as to what he says about old tenants, in point of fact no new tenant would take the farms on the present terms.”", "하지만 오래된 소작인들에 관해 그분이 한 말로 말하자면, 사실 지금 같은 조건으로는 새 소작인이 그 농장들을 맡으려 하지 않을 거예요.”"]], [["“I have a notion that he will be brought round in time,”", "“내 생각엔 그분도 때가 되면 마음을 돌리게 될 것 같아요.”"], ["said the Rector.", "목사가 말했다."], ["“But you were pulling one way, Elinor, and we were pulling another.", "“하지만 엘리너, 당신은 한쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었고, 우리는 다른 쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었던 거요."], ["You wanted to frighten him away from expense, and we want to frighten him into it.", "당신은 그를 겁줘서 비용에서 멀어지게 하려 했고, 우리는 겁줘서 비용을 쓰게 하려는 거니까."], ["Better let him try to be popular and see that his character as a landlord stands in his way.", "차라리 그가 인기를 얻으려 애쓰다가, 지주로서의 평판이 발목을 잡는다는 걸 깨닫게 두는 편이 나아요."], ["I don’t think it signifies two straws about the ‘Pioneer,’ or Ladislaw, or Brooke’s speechifying to the Middlemarchers.", "『파이어니어』니, 래디슬로니, 브룩이 미들마치 사람들에게 연설하는 거니 하는 건 지푸라기 두 개만큼도 중요하지 않다고 봐요."], ["But it does signify about the parishioners in Tipton being comfortable.”", "하지만 팁턴의 교구민들이 편안하게 사는가 하는 건 정말 중요한 문제지요.”"]], [["“Excuse me, it is you two who are on the wrong tack,”", "“미안하지만, 잘못된 방향으로 가고 있는 건 두 분이에요.”"], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“You should have proved to him that he loses money by bad management, and then we should all have pulled together.", "“관리를 잘못해서 돈을 잃는다는 걸 그에게 입증했어야 해요. 그랬다면 우리 모두 한 방향으로 끌어당겼겠지요."], ["If you put him a-horseback on politics, I warn you of the consequences.", "그를 정치라는 말에 태워놓으면, 그 결과가 어찌 될지 내가 경고하는 바예요."], ["It was all very well to ride on sticks at home and call them ideas.”", "집에서 막대기를 타고 다니며 그걸 사상이라 부르는 거야 무방했지만 말이에요.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XXXIX.", "# CHAPTER XXXIX."]], [["“If, as I have, you also doe, Vertue attired in woman see, And dare love that, and say so too, And forget the He and She;", "그가 남자인지 여자인지 잊는다면. 또 이 사랑이, 그렇게 자리 잡았어도,"]], [["And if this love, though placed so, From prophane men you hide, Which will no faith on this bestow, Or, if they doe, deride:", "그것에 아무 믿음도 두지 않거나,\n설령 믿더라도 비웃을 뿐인"]], [["Then you have done a braver thing Than all the Worthies did, And a braver thence will spring, Which is, to keep that hid.”", "속된 사람들로부터 그대가 감춘다면."], ["—DR. DONNE.", "그렇다면 그대는 모든 위인들이 한 일보다"]], [["Sir James Chettam’s mind was not fruitful in devices, but his growing anxiety to “act on Brooke,” once brought close to his constant belief in Dorothea’s capacity for influence, became formative, and issued in a little plan; namely, to plead Celia’s indisposition as a reason for fetching Dorothea by herself to the Hall, and to leave her at the Grange with the carriage on the way, after making her fully aware of the situation concerning the management of the estate.", "더 용감한 일을 해낸 것이며,\n거기서 더 용감한 일이 솟아나리니,"]], [["In this way it happened that one day near four o’clock, when Mr. Brooke and Ladislaw were seated in the library, the door opened and Mrs.", "그것은 곧 그것을 감추는 일이라."], ["Casaubon was announced.", "—던 박사"]], [["Will, the moment before, had been low in the depths of boredom, and, obliged to help Mr. Brooke in arranging “documents” about hanging sheep-stealers, was exemplifying the power our minds have of riding several horses at once by inwardly arranging measures towards getting a lodging for himself in Middlemarch and cutting short his constant residence at the Grange; while there flitted through all these steadier images a tickling vision of a sheep-stealing epic written with Homeric particularity. When Mrs. Casaubon was announced he started up as from an electric shock, and felt a tingling at his finger-ends. Any one observing him would have seen a change in his complexion, in the adjustment of his facial muscles, in the vividness of his glance, which might have made them imagine that every molecule in his body had passed the message of a magic touch. And so it had. For effective magic is transcendent nature; and who shall measure the subtlety of those touches which convey the quality of soul as well as body, and make a man’s passion for one woman differ from his passion for another as joy in the morning light over valley and river and white mountain-top differs from joy among Chinese lanterns and glass panels? Will, too, was made of very impressible stuff. The bow of a violin drawn near him cleverly, would at one stroke change the aspect of the world for him, and his point of view shifted as easily as his mood. Dorothea’s entrance was the freshness of morning.", "제임스 체텀 경의 머리는 책략을 풍부하게 짜내는 편이 아니었으나, \"브룩 씨에게 영향을 미치고\" 싶은 점점 커지는 그의 조바심이, 도러시아의 설득력에 대한 그의 변함없는 믿음과 한번 가까이 맞닿자 어떤 형태를 갖추기 시작하더니 마침내 작은 계획으로 결실을 맺었다. 즉 실리아가 몸이 좋지 않다는 것을 구실 삼아 도러시아만 따로 홀로 데려오되, 오는 길에 영지 관리에 관한 상황을 그녀에게 충분히 알린 뒤, 마차와 함께 그레인지에 그녀를 내려 준다는 것이었다. 그리하여 어느 날 네 시 무렵, 브룩 씨와 래디슬로가 서재에 앉아 있을 때, 문이 열리고 캐소본 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 들어오는 일이 일어났다."]], [["“Well, my dear, this is pleasant, now,” said Mr.", "윌은 바로 그 직전까지 권태의 밑바닥에 가라앉아 있었고, 양 도둑을 교수형에 처하는 일에 관한 \"서류\"를 정리하는 브룩 씨를 도와야 하는 처지에서, 미들마치에 자기 거처를 마련해 그레인지에 늘 머무는 것을 끝낼 방안을 속으로 궁리하며, 우리 마음이 한꺼번에 여러 마리 말을 몰 수 있는 힘을 몸소 보여 주고 있었다."], ["Brooke, meeting and kissing her.", "그러면서도 이 한층 진지한 심상들 사이로, 호메로스적인 세밀함으로 쓴 양 도둑 서사시라는 간질간질한 환상이 스쳐 지나가고 있었다."], ["“You have left Casaubon with his books, I suppose.", "캐소본 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 들어오자 그는 전기 충격이라도 받은 듯 벌떡 일어났고, 손끝이 짜릿해지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["That’s right. We must not have you getting too learned for a woman, you know.”", "누가 그를 지켜보았더라면 그의 안색이, 얼굴 근육의 자리잡음이, 눈빛의 생생함이 변하는 것을 보았을 테고, 그의 몸 안 모든 분자가 어떤 마법의 손길이 전하는 전갈을 받았으리라 상상했을 것이다."], ["“There is no fear of that, uncle,”", "실제로 그러했다."], ["said Dorothea, turning to Will and shaking hands with open cheerfulness, while she made no other form of greeting, but went on answering her uncle.", "효과적인 마법이란 초월적인 자연이니, 영혼의 자질을 육체의 자질과 더불어 전달하고, 한 남자가 한 여인에게 품는 열정을 다른 여인에게 품는 열정과 다르게 만드는—마치 골짜기와 강과 흰 산봉우리 위로 쏟아지는 아침 햇살 속의 기쁨이 중국식 등롱과 유리 칸막이 사이의 기쁨과 다르듯이—그런 손길의 섬세함을 누가 가늠할 수 있겠는가?"], ["“I am very slow.", "윌 또한 매우 예민한 재질로 빚어진 사람이었다."], ["When I want to be busy with books, I am often playing truant among my thoughts.", "가까이서 솜씨 좋게 그은 바이올린의 활은 단 한 번의 긋기로 그에게 세상의 양상을 바꿔 놓을 수 있었고, 그의 관점은 기분만큼이나 쉽게 옮겨 갔다."], ["I find it is not so easy to be learned as to plan cottages.”", "도러시아의 등장은 아침의 신선함이었다."]], [["She seated herself beside her uncle opposite to Will, and was evidently preoccupied with something that made her almost unmindful of him. He was ridiculously disappointed, as if he had imagined that her coming had anything to do with him.", "\"그래, 얘야, 이거 참 반갑구나.\" 브룩 씨가 그녀를 맞아 입맞춤하며 말했다. \"캐소본은 책과 함께 두고 왔겠지. 잘했다. 너를 여자치고 너무 박식하게 만들면 안 되니까 말이다.\""]], [["“Why, yes, my dear, it was quite your hobby to draw plans.", "\"그럴 염려는 없어요, 외삼촌.\""], ["But it was good to break that off a little. Hobbies are apt to run away with us, you know; it doesn’t do to be run away with.", "도러시아는 윌 쪽으로 돌아서서 거리낌 없이 명랑하게 악수를 하며 말했는데, 다른 인사말은 하지 않고 곧장 외삼촌에게 대답을 이어 갔다."], ["We must keep the reins.", "\"저는 아주 느려요."], ["I have never let myself be run away with; I always pulled up.", "책에 열중하고 싶을 때면 자꾸만 제 생각들 사이로 딴청을 부리는걸요."], ["That is what I tell Ladislaw.", "박식해지는 것이 오두막 설계도를 그리는 것만큼 쉽지가 않네요.\""], ["He and I are alike, you know: he likes to go into everything.", "그녀는 윌의 맞은편에 외삼촌 곁에 앉았는데, 무언가에 마음을 빼앗긴 듯 그를 거의 의식하지 않는 게 분명했다."], ["We are working at capital punishment. We shall do a great deal together, Ladislaw and I.”", "그는 마치 그녀가 온 것이 자기와 무슨 관계라도 있다고 상상했던 것처럼, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 실망했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Dorothea, with characteristic directness, “Sir James has been telling me that he is in hope of seeing a great change made soon in your management of the estate—that you are thinking of having the farms valued, and repairs made, and the cottages improved, so that Tipton may look quite another place.", "도러시아가 특유의 직설적인 태도로 말했다. \"제임스 경이 말씀하시기를, 외삼촌의 영지 관리에 곧 큰 변화가 생기기를 바라고 계신대요."], ["Oh, how happy!”—she went on, clasping her hands, with a return to that more childlike impetuous manner, which had been subdued since her marriage.", "농장을 평가하고, 수리를 하고, 오두막을 개선해서 팁턴이 아주 다른 곳처럼 보이도록 하실 생각이라고요. 아, 정말 기뻐요!\""], ["“If I were at home still, I should take to riding again, that I might go about with you and see all that!", "그녀는 두 손을 맞잡으며, 결혼 후로 가라앉아 있던 그 한층 어린아이 같고 격정적인 태도로 돌아가 말을 이었다. \"제가 아직 집에 있었더라면 다시 말을 타기 시작했을 거예요."], ["And you are going to engage Mr.", "그래서 외삼촌과 함께 돌아다니며 그 모든 걸 보았을 텐데요!"], ["Garth, who praised my cottages, Sir James says.”", "그리고 제 오두막을 칭찬했던 가스 씨를 고용하실 거라고 제임스 경이 말씀하셨어요.\""]], [["“Chettam is a little hasty, my dear,” said Mr.", "\"체텀은 좀 성급하구나, 얘야.\""], ["Brooke, coloring slightly; “a little hasty, you know.", "브룩 씨가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"좀 성급해, 알다시피."], ["I never said I should do anything of the kind.", "나는 그런 일을 하겠다고 말한 적이 없어."], ["I never said I should not do it, you know.”", "그렇다고 안 하겠다고 말한 적도 없지만 말이다, 알다시피.\""]], [["“He only feels confident that you will do it,”", "\"그분은 외삼촌이 그렇게 하시리라 확신할 뿐이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, in a voice as clear and unhesitating as that of a young chorister chanting a credo, “because you mean to enter Parliament as a member who cares for the improvement of the people, and one of the first things to be made better is the state of the land and the laborers.", "도러시아가 신경을 외는 어린 성가대원처럼 맑고 거침없는 목소리로 말했다. \"왜냐하면 외삼촌은 백성들의 향상을 염려하는 의원으로 의회에 들어가실 작정이시고, 가장 먼저 개선되어야 할 것 중 하나가 바로 땅과 노동자들의 처지니까요."], ["Think of Kit Downes, uncle, who lives with his wife and seven children in a house with one sitting room and one bedroom hardly larger than this table!—and those poor Dagleys, in their tumble-down farmhouse, where they live in the back kitchen and leave the other rooms to the rats!", "키트 다운스를 생각해 보세요, 외삼촌. 그는 아내와 일곱 아이와 함께 거실 하나, 그리고 이 탁자보다 별로 크지도 않은 침실 하나뿐인 집에서 살고 있어요!"], ["That is one reason why I did not like the pictures here, dear uncle—which you think me stupid about.", "그리고 저 가엾은 데글리네는 다 쓰러져 가는 농가에서, 뒷부엌에서 살며 다른 방들은 쥐들에게 내준 채 지내고 있고요! 그것이 바로 제가 이곳의 그림들을 좋아하지 않았던 한 가지 이유예요, 외삼촌."], ["I used to come from the village with all that dirt and coarse ugliness like a pain within me, and the simpering pictures in the drawing-room seemed to me like a wicked attempt to find delight in what is false, while we don’t mind how hard the truth is for the neighbors outside our walls.", "외삼촌은 제가 그 일에 어리석다고 여기시지만요. 저는 그 온갖 더러움과 거친 추함을 가슴속 아픔처럼 안고 마을에서 돌아오곤 했는데, 그러면 응접실의 그 간드러진 그림들이 거짓된 것에서 즐거움을 찾으려는 사악한 시도처럼 보였어요."], ["I think we have no right to come forward and urge wider changes for good, until we have tried to alter the evils which lie under our own hands.”", "우리 담장 밖 이웃들에게 진실이 얼마나 가혹한지는 아랑곳하지 않으면서 말이에요. 우리 손이 닿는 곳에 놓인 악들을 바꾸려고 애써 보지도 않은 채 앞에 나서서 더 넓은 선의 변화를 부르짖을 권리는 우리에게 없다고 생각해요.\""]], [["Dorothea had gathered emotion as she went on, and had forgotten everything except the relief of pouring forth her feelings, unchecked: an experience once habitual with her, but hardly ever present since her marriage, which had been a perpetual struggle of energy with fear.", "도러시아는 말을 이어 가면서 점점 감정에 사로잡혔고, 제지받지 않고 마음을 쏟아 내는 안도감 외에는 모든 것을 잊고 있었다. 그것은 한때 그녀에게 익숙했으나 결혼 후로는 거의 찾아오지 않은 경험이었으니, 그녀의 결혼 생활은 활력과 두려움 사이의 끊임없는 투쟁이었던 것이다."], ["For the moment, Will’s admiration was accompanied with a chilling sense of remoteness.", "그 순간 윌의 감탄에는 서늘한 거리감이 함께 깃들었다."], ["A man is seldom ashamed of feeling that he cannot love a woman so well when he sees a certain greatness in her: nature having intended greatness for men.", "한 여인에게서 어떤 위대함을 볼 때, 그녀를 그만큼 잘 사랑할 수 없다고 느끼는 것을 부끄러워하는 남자는 드물다. 자연은 위대함을 남자의 몫으로 정해 두었으니까."], ["But nature has sometimes made sad oversights in carrying out her intention; as in the case of good Mr.", "하지만 자연은 때때로 그 의도를 실행하면서 서글픈 실수를 저질러 왔다."], ["Brooke, whose masculine consciousness was at this moment in rather a stammering condition under the eloquence of his niece.", "선량한 브룩 씨의 경우가 그러했으니, 그의 남성적 의식은 이 순간 조카딸의 웅변 앞에서 다소 더듬거리는 상태에 빠져 있었다."], ["He could not immediately find any other mode of expressing himself than that of rising, fixing his eye-glass, and fingering the papers before him.", "그는 자리에서 일어나 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 앞에 놓인 서류들을 만지작거리는 것 말고는 당장 자기를 표현할 다른 방도를 찾지 못했다."], ["At last he said—", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“There is something in what you say, my dear, something in what you say—but not everything—eh, Ladislaw?", "\"네 말에 일리가 있다, 얘야, 네 말에 일리가 있어. 하지만 전부 옳은 건 아니지."], ["You and I don’t like our pictures and statues being found fault with.", "안 그런가, 래디슬로? 자네와 나는 우리 그림이며 조각이 흠잡히는 걸 좋아하지 않아."], ["Young ladies are a little ardent, you know—a little one-sided, my dear.", "젊은 아가씨들은 좀 열정적이거든, 알다시피. 좀 한쪽으로 치우치고 말이야, 얘야."], ["Fine art, poetry, that kind of thing, elevates a nation—emollit mores—you understand a little Latin now.", "미술이라든가 시 같은 것은 한 나라를 고양시켜 주거든. 에몰리트 모레스(품성을 부드럽게 한다)지."], ["But—eh?", "너도 이제 라틴어를 좀 알지. 그런데, 어?"], ["what?”", "뭐냐?\""]], [["These interrogatives were addressed to the footman who had come in to say that the keeper had found one of Dagley’s boys with a leveret in his hand just killed.", "이 물음들은 사냥터지기가 데글리네 아들 하나가 방금 잡은 새끼 산토끼를 손에 든 채 붙잡혔다고 전하러 들어온 하인을 향한 것이었다."]], [["“I’ll come, I’ll come.", "\"가지, 가지."], ["I shall let him off easily, you know,”", "가볍게 풀어 줄 거야, 알다시피.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke aside to Dorothea, shuffling away very cheerfully.", "브룩 씨가 도러시아에게 슬쩍 말하고는 아주 흔쾌하게 발을 끌며 나갔다."]], [["“I hope you feel how right this change is that I—that Sir James wishes for,”", "\"제가, 아니 제임스 경이 바라는 이 변화가 얼마나 옳은 것인지 당신도 느끼시기를 바라요.\""], ["said Dorothea to Will, as soon as her uncle was gone.", "외삼촌이 나가자마자 도러시아가 윌에게 말했다."]], [["“I do, now I have heard you speak about it.", "\"느낍니다, 이제 당신이 그것에 관해 말하는 걸 들었으니까요."], ["I shall not forget what you have said.", "당신이 한 말을 잊지 않겠습니다."], ["But can you think of something else at this moment?", "그런데 지금 이 순간 다른 일을 좀 생각해 주실 수 있겠습니까?"], ["I may not have another opportunity of speaking to you about what has occurred,”", "일어난 일에 관해 당신과 이야기할 기회가 다시 없을지도 모르니까요.\""], ["said Will, rising with a movement of impatience, and holding the back of his chair with both hands.", "윌이 조바심이 묻어나는 동작으로 일어나 두 손으로 의자 등받이를 잡으며 말했다."]], [["“Pray tell me what it is,”", "\"그게 무엇인지 말씀해 주세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, anxiously, also rising and going to the open window, where Monk was looking in, panting and wagging his tail.", "도러시아가 걱정스레 말하며, 그녀 역시 일어나 열린 창가로 갔다. 거기서 멍크가 헐떡이며 꼬리를 흔들며 안을 들여다보고 있었다."], ["She leaned her back against the window-frame, and laid her hand on the dog’s head; for though, as we know, she was not fond of pets that must be held in the hands or trodden on, she was always attentive to the feelings of dogs, and very polite if she had to decline their advances.", "그녀는 창틀에 등을 기댄 채 개의 머리에 손을 얹었다. 우리도 알다시피 그녀는 손에 안고 다니거나 발치에서 밟히는 애완동물을 좋아하지 않았지만, 개들의 감정에는 늘 마음을 썼고, 그들의 접근을 거절해야 할 때면 무척 정중했던 것이다."]], [["Will followed her only with his eyes and said, “I presume you know that Mr.", "윌은 눈으로만 그녀를 좇으며 말했다."], ["Casaubon has forbidden me to go to his house.”", "\"캐소본 씨가 제게 자기 집에 오는 것을 금했다는 걸 당신도 아시리라 짐작합니다.\""]], [["“No, I did not,”", "\"아니요, 몰랐어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, after a moment’s pause.", "도러시아가 잠시 멈췄다가 말했다. 그녀는 분명 무척 동요한 듯했다."], ["She was evidently much moved.", "\"정말, 정말 안타까워요.\""], ["“I am very, very sorry,”", "그녀가 슬프게 덧붙였다."], ["she added, mournfully.", "그녀는 윌이 알지 못하는 것을 떠올리고 있었다."], ["She was thinking of what Will had no knowledge of—the conversation between her and her husband in the darkness; and she was anew smitten with hopelessness that she could influence Mr.", "어둠 속에서 그녀와 남편 사이에 오갔던 대화를. 그리고 자신이 캐소본 씨의 행동에 영향을 미칠 수 있으리라는 희망 없음에 새삼 마음이 찔렸다."], ["Casaubon’s action. But the marked expression of her sorrow convinced Will that it was not all given to him personally, and that Dorothea had not been visited by the idea that Mr.", "그러나 그녀의 슬픔이 뚜렷이 드러난 표정을 보고 윌은, 그것이 모두 자기 개인을 향한 것이 아니며, 캐소본 씨가 그를 싫어하고 질투하는 것이 그녀 자신에게로 향한 것이라는 생각이 도러시아에게는 떠오른 적조차 없음을 확신하게 되었다."], ["Casaubon’s dislike and jealousy of him turned upon herself.", "그는 기쁨과 짜증이 묘하게 뒤섞인 감정을 느꼈다."], ["He felt an odd mixture of delight and vexation: of delight that he could dwell and be cherished in her thought as in a pure home, without suspicion and without stint—of vexation because he was of too little account with her, was not formidable enough, was treated with an unhesitating benevolence which did not flatter him.", "의심도 인색함도 없이, 순수한 보금자리에서처럼 그녀의 생각 속에 머물며 소중히 여겨질 수 있다는 기쁨, 그리고 그녀에게 자기가 너무도 하찮은 존재이며, 두려워할 만한 상대가 못 되고, 그를 우쭐하게 하지도 않는 거침없는 호의로 대접받고 있다는 짜증이었다."], ["But his dread of any change in Dorothea was stronger than his discontent, and he began to speak again in a tone of mere explanation.", "그러나 도러시아에게 어떤 변화가 생길까 봐 두려운 마음이 불만보다 강했으므로, 그는 그저 설명하는 어조로 다시 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“Mr. Casaubon’s reason is, his displeasure at my taking a position here which he considers unsuited to my rank as his cousin.", "\"캐소본 씨의 이유인즉, 제가 여기서 그의 사촌이라는 신분에 어울리지 않는다고 그가 여기는 자리를 맡은 것에 대한 불쾌함입니다."], ["I have told him that I cannot give way on this point.", "저는 이 점에 관해서는 양보할 수 없다고 그에게 말했습니다."], ["It is a little too hard on me to expect that my course in life is to be hampered by prejudices which I think ridiculous.", "제가 우스꽝스럽다고 여기는 편견 때문에 제 삶의 진로가 방해받기를 기대하는 것은 제게 좀 너무 가혹한 일이지요."], ["Obligation may be stretched till it is no better than a brand of slavery stamped on us when we were too young to know its meaning.", "의무라는 것도 잡아 늘이다 보면, 우리가 그 의미를 알기에는 너무 어렸을 때 우리에게 찍힌 노예의 낙인이나 다름없는 것이 되어 버릴 수 있습니다."], ["I would not have accepted the position if I had not meant to make it useful and honorable.", "저는 이 자리를 유용하고 명예롭게 만들 생각이 없었다면 받아들이지도 않았을 겁니다."], ["I am not bound to regard family dignity in any other light.”", "저는 가문의 위엄을 그 외의 다른 관점으로 존중할 의무가 없습니다.\""]], [["Dorothea felt wretched.", "도러시아는 비참한 기분이 들었다."], ["She thought her husband altogether in the wrong, on more grounds than Will had mentioned.", "그녀는 남편이 윌이 말한 것보다 더 여러 가지 점에서 완전히 잘못되었다고 생각했다."]], [["“It is better for us not to speak on the subject,”", "\"우리는 이 문제에 관해 이야기하지 않는 편이 좋겠어요.\""], ["she said, with a tremulousness not common in her voice, “since you and Mr.", "그녀가 평소 그녀의 목소리에서는 보기 드문 떨림을 띠고 말했다."], ["Casaubon disagree. You intend to remain?”", "\"당신과 캐소본 씨가 의견이 다르니까요."], ["She was looking out on the lawn, with melancholy meditation.", "계속 여기 머무실 작정이세요?\" 그녀는 우울한 명상에 잠겨 잔디밭을 내다보고 있었다."]], [["“Yes; but I shall hardly ever see you now,”", "\"네, 하지만 이제 당신을 거의 뵙지 못하겠지요.\""], ["said Will, in a tone of almost boyish complaint.", "윌이 거의 소년 같은 푸념조로 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"그렇겠죠.\""], ["said Dorothea, turning her eyes full upon him, “hardly ever.", "도러시아가 그를 향해 눈을 가득 돌리며 말했다. \"거의 못 보겠죠."], ["But I shall hear of you.", "하지만 당신 소식은 듣겠지요."], ["I shall know what you are doing for my uncle.”", "당신이 외삼촌을 위해 무얼 하는지 알게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“I shall know hardly anything about you,”", "\"저는 당신에 관해선 거의 아무것도 모를 텐데요.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“No one will tell me anything.”", "\"아무도 제게 아무 말도 해 주지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, my life is very simple,”", "\"오, 제 삶은 아주 단순해요.\""], ["said Dorothea, her lips curling with an exquisite smile, which irradiated her melancholy.", "도러시아가 입가에 더없이 고운 미소를 머금자, 그 미소가 그녀의 우울을 환히 밝혔다."], ["“I am always at Lowick.”", "\"저는 늘 로윅에 있어요.\""]], [["“That is a dreadful imprisonment,”", "\"그건 끔찍한 감금이군요.\""], ["said Will, impetuously.", "윌이 격하게 말했다."]], [["“No, don’t think that,”", "\"아니요, 그렇게 생각하지 마세요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“I have no longings.”", "\"저는 갈망하는 게 없어요.\""]], [["He did not speak, but she replied to some change in his expression.", "그는 말이 없었지만, 그녀는 그의 표정에 떠오른 어떤 변화에 답하듯 말했다."], ["“I mean, for myself.", "\"제 말은, 제 자신을 위해서는요."], ["Except that I should like not to have so much more than my share without doing anything for others.", "다만 남들을 위해 아무것도 하지 않으면서 제 몫보다 그렇게 많이 갖지는 않았으면 좋겠다는 것뿐이에요."], ["But I have a belief of my own, and it comforts me.”", "하지만 저에게는 저 나름의 믿음이 있고, 그것이 저를 위로해 줘요.\""]], [["“What is that?”", "\"그게 무엇입니까?\""], ["said Will, rather jealous of the belief.", "윌이 그 믿음을 다소 질투하듯 물었다."]], [["“That by desiring what is perfectly good, even when we don’t quite know what it is and cannot do what we would, we are part of the divine power against evil—widening the skirts of light and making the struggle with darkness narrower.”", "\"완전히 선한 것을 갈망함으로써, 비록 그것이 무엇인지 잘 모르고 우리가 바라는 바를 행할 수 없을 때조차, 우리는 악에 맞서는 신성한 권능의 일부가 된다는 거예요. 빛의 자락을 넓히고, 어둠과의 투쟁을 좁혀 가는 거죠.\""]], [["“That is a beautiful mysticism—it is a—”", "\"그건 아름다운 신비주의로군요. 그건 일종의—\""]], [["“Please not to call it by any name,”", "\"제발 그것에 무슨 이름을 붙이지 말아 주세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, putting out her hands entreatingly.", "도러시아가 간청하듯 두 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“You will say it is Persian, or something else geographical.", "\"당신은 그게 페르시아 식이라느니, 뭔가 지리적인 무엇이라느니 하시겠죠."], ["It is my life.", "그건 제 삶이에요."], ["I have found it out, and cannot part with it.", "제가 찾아낸 것이고, 떼어 놓을 수 없는 것이에요."], ["I have always been finding out my religion since I was a little girl.", "저는 어린 소녀 때부터 늘 제 신앙을 찾아오고 있었어요."], ["I used to pray so much—now I hardly ever pray.", "예전엔 기도를 그렇게 많이 했는데, 지금은 거의 기도하지 않아요."], ["I try not to have desires merely for myself, because they may not be good for others, and I have too much already.", "저는 그저 제 자신만을 위한 욕망을 갖지 않으려고 애써요. 그건 남들에게는 좋지 않을 수도 있고, 저는 이미 너무 많이 가졌으니까요."], ["I only told you, that you might know quite well how my days go at Lowick.”", "제가 로윅에서 어떻게 나날을 보내는지 당신이 잘 알 수 있도록 말씀드린 것뿐이에요.\""]], [["“God bless you for telling me!”", "\"말해 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다, 신의 축복이 있기를!\""], ["said Will, ardently, and rather wondering at himself.", "윌이 열렬하게, 그리고 스스로도 다소 놀라며 말했다."], ["They were looking at each other like two fond children who were talking confidentially of birds.", "두 사람은 새 이야기를 은밀히 주고받는 다정한 두 아이처럼 서로를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“What is your religion?”", "\"당신의 신앙은 무엇인가요?\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 물었다."], ["“I mean—not what you know about religion, but the belief that helps you most?”", "\"제 말은, 당신이 종교에 관해 무엇을 아는가가 아니라, 당신을 가장 많이 도와주는 믿음 말이에요.\""]], [["“To love what is good and beautiful when I see it,”", "\"제 눈에 선하고 아름다운 것이 보일 때 그것을 사랑하는 것입니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“But I am a rebel: I don’t feel bound, as you do, to submit to what I don’t like.”", "\"하지만 저는 반항아예요. 당신처럼 마음에 들지 않는 것에 복종해야 한다고 느끼지 않거든요.\""]], [["“But if you like what is good, that comes to the same thing,”", "\"하지만 당신이 선한 것을 좋아한다면, 그건 결국 같은 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea, smiling.", "도러시아가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Now you are subtle,”", "\"이제 당신이 미묘해지는군요.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."]], [["“Yes; Mr. Casaubon often says I am too subtle.", "\"네, 캐소본 씨도 제가 너무 미묘하다고 자주 말해요."], ["I don’t feel as if I were subtle,”", "저는 제가 미묘하다고는 느껴지지 않는데요.\""], ["said Dorothea, playfully.", "도러시아가 장난스럽게 말했다."], ["“But how long my uncle is! I must go and look for him.", "\"그런데 외삼촌이 어쩜 이리 오래 걸리실까!"], ["I must really go on to the Hall.", "가서 찾아봐야겠어요. 정말 홀로 가야 해요."], ["Celia is expecting me.”", "실리아가 저를 기다리고 있거든요.\""]], [["Will offered to tell Mr. Brooke, who presently came and said that he would step into the carriage and go with Dorothea as far as Dagley’s, to speak about the small delinquent who had been caught with the leveret.", "윌이 브룩 씨에게 알려 드리겠다고 나섰는데, 브룩 씨는 곧 와서는 자기도 마차에 올라타 새끼 산토끼를 잡다 걸린 그 어린 범법자에 관해 이야기하러 데글리네까지 도러시아와 함께 가겠다고 말했다."], ["Dorothea renewed the subject of the estate as they drove along, but Mr. Brooke, not being taken unawares, got the talk under his own control.", "도러시아는 마차를 타고 가는 동안 영지 문제를 다시 꺼냈으나, 이번에는 허를 찔리지 않은 브룩 씨가 대화를 자기 뜻대로 끌고 갔다."]], [["“Chettam, now,”", "\"체텀으로 말하자면 말이다.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied; “he finds fault with me, my dear; but I should not preserve my game if it were not for Chettam, and he can’t say that that expense is for the sake of the tenants, you know.", "\"그는 나를 흠잡는단다, 얘야. 하지만 체텀이 아니었더라면 나는 사냥감을 보존하지도 않았을 게다."], ["It’s a little against my feeling:—poaching, now, if you come to look into it—I have often thought of getting up the subject.", "그런데 그 비용이 소작인들을 위한 것이라고는 말할 수 없거든, 알다시피. 그게 내 마음에는 좀 걸린단다."], ["Not long ago, Flavell, the Methodist preacher, was brought up for knocking down a hare that came across his path when he and his wife were walking out together.", "밀렵 말이야, 따지고 보면 말이지, 나는 이 문제를 한번 다뤄 봐야겠다고 자주 생각했어. 얼마 전에 감리교 설교자인 플라벨이 아내와 함께 산책하던 중에 길을 가로질러 온 산토끼를 때려잡은 일로 끌려왔지."], ["He was pretty quick, and knocked it on the neck.”", "그가 꽤 날쌔서 산토끼 목을 후려쳤거든.\""]], [["“That was very brutal, I think,”", "\"제 생각엔 그건 아주 잔인한 짓이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“Well, now, it seemed rather black to me, I confess, in a Methodist preacher, you know.", "\"음, 그래, 솔직히 내게도 그게 좀 떳떳지 못해 보이더구나, 감리교 설교자라는 사람이 말이야, 알다시피. 그래서 존슨이 그러더군."], ["And Johnson said, ‘You may judge what a hypocrite he is.’ And upon my word, I thought Flavell looked very little like ‘the highest style of man’—as somebody calls the Christian—Young, the poet Young, I think—you know Young?", "'저자가 얼마나 위선자인지 알 만하지 않습니까.' 정말이지 플라벨은 어떤 이가 그리스도인을 두고 일컬은 '인간의 가장 높은 경지'와는 영 거리가 멀어 보였어."], ["Well, now, Flavell in his shabby black gaiters, pleading that he thought the Lord had sent him and his wife a good dinner, and he had a right to knock it down, though not a mighty hunter before the Lord, as Nimrod was—I assure you it was rather comic: Fielding would have made something of it—or Scott, now—Scott might have worked it up.", "그렇게 일컬은 게 누구더라, 영, 시인 영이었던 것 같은데. 너 영을 아니?"], ["But really, when I came to think of it, I couldn’t help liking that the fellow should have a bit of hare to say grace over.", "아무튼, 플라벨이 그 다 해진 검은 각반 차림으로, 주님이 자기와 아내에게 좋은 저녁거리를 보내 주셨다고 생각했으니 그걸 때려잡을 권리가 있었다고 변명을 하더란 말이지, 비록 그가 니므롯처럼 주님 앞의 위대한 사냥꾼은 아니었지만 말이야. 정말이지 좀 우스꽝스러웠어."], ["It’s all a matter of prejudice—prejudice with the law on its side, you know—about the stick and the gaiters, and so on.", "필딩이라면 그걸 가지고 뭔가 만들어 냈을 테고, 아니면 스콧이라면 말이야, 스콧이라면 그걸 잘 다듬어 냈을 거야. 그런데 정말이지, 곰곰 생각해 보니 그 친구가 식전 기도를 올릴 산토끼 한 조각이라도 갖게 된 게 싫지가 않더구나."], ["However, it doesn’t do to reason about things; and law is law.", "그게 다 편견의 문제거든. 법이 제 편에 선 편견 말이야, 알다시피."], ["But I got Johnson to be quiet, and I hushed the matter up.", "그 막대기니 각반이니 하는 것들에 관한 편견 말이다. 하지만 무슨 일이든 이치를 따져 봐야 소용없는 법이고, 법은 법이지."], ["I doubt whether Chettam would not have been more severe, and yet he comes down on me as if I were the hardest man in the county.", "어쨌든 나는 존슨을 조용히 시키고 그 일을 덮어 버렸어. 체텀이라면 더 엄하게 굴지 않았을까 싶기도 한데, 그런데도 그는 내가 이 지방에서 가장 모진 사람이라도 되는 양 몰아붙이거든."], ["But here we are at Dagley’s.”", "자, 데글리네에 다 왔구나.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke got down at a farmyard-gate, and Dorothea drove on.", "브룩 씨가 농가 마당 문 앞에서 내리자 도러시아는 마차를 타고 계속 갔다."], ["It is wonderful how much uglier things will look when we only suspect that we are blamed for them.", "어떤 일로 우리가 비난받고 있다고 의심만 하게 되어도 그 일이 얼마나 더 흉하게 보이는지 놀라울 정도다."], ["Even our own persons in the glass are apt to change their aspect for us after we have heard some frank remark on their less admirable points; and on the other hand it is astonishing how pleasantly conscience takes our encroachments on those who never complain or have nobody to complain for them.", "우리 자신의 모습조차도, 그 덜 훌륭한 점에 관해 누군가의 솔직한 말을 듣고 나면 거울 속에서 그 양상이 달라져 보이기 십상이다."], ["Dagley’s homestead never before looked so dismal to Mr.", "반면, 결코 불평하지 않거나 대신 불평해 줄 이가 없는 사람들에게 우리가 끼친 침해를 양심이 얼마나 편안하게 받아들이는지는 놀라운 일이다."], ["Brooke as it did today, with his mind thus sore about the fault-finding of the “Trumpet,” echoed by Sir James.", "데글리네 농가가 오늘처럼 브룩 씨에게 음울하게 보인 적은 일찍이 없었으니, 그의 마음이 「트럼펫」지의 흠잡기로, 그리고 제임스 경이 되풀이한 그 흠잡기로 이렇게 쓰라렸던 것이다."]], [["It is true that an observer, under that softening influence of the fine arts which makes other people’s hardships picturesque, might have been delighted with this homestead called Freeman’s End: the old house had dormer-windows in the dark red roof, two of the chimneys were choked with ivy, the large porch was blocked up with bundles of sticks, and half the windows were closed with gray worm-eaten shutters about which the jasmine-boughs grew in wild luxuriance; the mouldering garden wall with hollyhocks peeping over it was a perfect study of highly mingled subdued color, and there was an aged goat (kept doubtless on interesting superstitious grounds) lying against the open back-kitchen door.", "물론, 남들의 고난을 그림처럼 보이게 하는 그 미술의 부드러운 영향 아래 있는 관찰자라면 프리먼스 엔드라 불리는 이 농가를 보고 흐뭇해했을지도 모른다. 그 오래된 집은 검붉은 지붕에 지붕창들이 나 있었고, 두 굴뚝은 담쟁이덩굴로 막혀 있었으며, 커다란 현관은 잔가지 다발들로 가로막혀 있었고, 창문의 절반은 벌레 먹은 회색 덧문으로 닫혀 있었는데 그 주위로 재스민 가지들이 거칠게 무성히 자라 있었다."], ["The mossy thatch of the cow-shed, the broken gray barn-doors, the pauper laborers in ragged breeches who had nearly finished unloading a wagon of corn into the barn ready for early thrashing; the scanty dairy of cows being tethered for milking and leaving one half of the shed in brown emptiness; the very pigs and white ducks seeming to wander about the uneven neglected yard as if in low spirits from feeding on a too meagre quality of rinsings,—all these objects under the quiet light of a sky marbled with high clouds would have made a sort of picture which we have all paused over as a “charming bit,”", "접시꽃이 너머로 빠끔히 내다보는 허물어져 가는 정원 담장은 한껏 어우러진 은은한 색채의 완벽한 습작이었고, (틀림없이 흥미로운 미신적 이유로 키우는) 늙은 염소 한 마리가 열린 뒷부엌 문에 기대 누워 있었다. 이끼 낀 소외양간의 초가지붕, 부서진 회색 헛간 문, 이른 타작을 위해 헛간에 옥수수 한 수레를 거의 다 부려 놓은, 누더기 반바지 차림의 가난한 일꾼들, 젖을 짜려고 매어 둔 빈약한 젖소 떼와 그 탓에 헛간 한쪽이 갈색의 텅 빈 공간으로 남은 모습, 너무 형편없는 구정물을 먹어 풀이 죽은 듯 울퉁불퉁한 버려진 마당을 어슬렁거리는 듯한 돼지들과 흰 오리들까지, 높은 구름이 대리석 무늬를 이룬 하늘의 고요한 빛 아래 이 모든 것은 우리 모두가 한 번쯤 \"근사한 한 폭\"이라 일컬으며 발길을 멈춘 그런 그림을 이루었을 테고, 그 시절 신문에 끊임없이 실리던 대로 농업 자본의 서글픈 부족과 더불어 농업의 침체가 불러일으키는 감정과는 다른 감수성을 건드렸을 것이다."], ["touching other sensibilities than those which are stirred by the depression of the agricultural interest, with the sad lack of farming capital, as seen constantly in the newspapers of that time.", "그러나 이 골치 아픈 연상들이 지금 브룩 씨의 마음에 강하게 떠올라 그에게 그 광경을 망쳐 놓았다."], ["But these troublesome associations were just now strongly present to Mr. Brooke, and spoiled the scene for him.", "데글리 씨 자신도 그 풍경의 한 인물을 이루었으니, 그는 쇠스랑을 든 채 젖 짤 때 쓰는 모자—앞부분이 납작해진 아주 낡은 비버 가죽 모자를 쓰고 있었다."], ["Mr. Dagley himself made a figure in the landscape, carrying a pitchfork and wearing his milking-hat—a very old beaver flattened in front.", "그가 입은 외투와 반바지는 가진 것 중 가장 좋은 것이어서, 이런 평일에는 입지 않았을 텐데, 장에 나갔다가 블루 불 식당의 공동 식탁에서 점심을 먹는 드문 호사를 누리느라 평소보다 늦게 돌아온 탓이었다."], ["His coat and breeches were the best he had, and he would not have been wearing them on this weekday occasion if he had not been to market and returned later than usual, having given himself the rare treat of dining at the public table of the Blue Bull.", "그가 어쩌다 이런 사치에 빠졌는지는 어쩌면 그 자신도 이튿날 의아해할 일이었다."], ["How he came to fall into this extravagance would perhaps be matter of wonderment to himself on the morrow; but before dinner something in the state of the country, a slight pause in the harvest before the Far Dips were cut, the stories about the new King and the numerous handbills on the walls, had seemed to warrant a little recklessness.", "하지만 점심을 들기 전에 나라 사정이라든가, 파 딥스 밭을 베기 전 추수의 짧은 휴지기라든가, 새 임금에 관한 이야기들과 벽에 잔뜩 붙은 전단들 같은 것들이 약간의 무모함을 정당화해 주는 듯 보였던 것이다."], ["It was a maxim about Middlemarch, and regarded as self-evident, that good meat should have good drink, which last Dagley interpreted as plenty of table ale well followed up by rum-and-water.", "좋은 고기에는 좋은 술이 따라야 한다는 것이 미들마치의 격언이자 자명한 이치로 통했는데, 데글리는 그것을 식탁용 에일을 넉넉히 마시고 럼주를 탄 물로 든든히 뒤를 받치는 것으로 해석했다."], ["These liquors have so far truth in them that they were not false enough to make poor Dagley seem merry: they only made his discontent less tongue-tied than usual.", "이 술들에는 그나마 진실이 담겨 있었으니, 가엾은 데글리를 흥겨워 보이게 할 만큼 거짓되지는 않아, 다만 평소보다 그의 불만을 덜 굳게 만들었을 뿐이다."], ["He had also taken too much in the shape of muddy political talk, a stimulant dangerously disturbing to his farming conservatism, which consisted in holding that whatever is, is bad, and any change is likely to be worse.", "그는 또한 흙탕물 같은 정치 이야기도 너무 많이 들이켰는데, 그것은 그의 농사꾼다운 보수성을 위험하게 흔드는 자극제였다. 그 보수성이란 무엇이 됐든 현재 있는 것은 나쁘고, 어떤 변화든 더 나빠지기 쉽다고 여기는 데에 있었다."], ["He was flushed, and his eyes had a decidedly quarrelsome stare as he stood still grasping his pitchfork, while the landlord approached with his easy shuffling walk, one hand in his trouser-pocket and the other swinging round a thin walking-stick.", "그는 얼굴이 벌게져 있었고, 쇠스랑을 꼭 쥔 채 가만히 서 있을 때 그의 눈에는 단연 싸움을 거는 듯한 눈빛이 서려 있었다. 그러는 사이 지주가 한 손은 바지 주머니에 찌르고 다른 손으로는 가느다란 지팡이를 빙빙 돌리며 느긋하게 발을 끌며 다가왔다."]], [["“Dagley, my good fellow,”", "\"데글리, 이 사람아.\""], ["began Mr. Brooke, conscious that he was going to be very friendly about the boy.", "브룩 씨가 그 아이 일에 관해 아주 다정하게 굴리라 마음먹은 채 입을 열었다."]], [["“Oh, ay, I’m a good feller, am I?", "\"오, 그렇죠, 제가 좋은 사람이라고요?"], ["Thank ye, sir, thank ye,”", "고맙수다, 나리, 고마워요.\""], ["said Dagley, with a loud snarling irony which made Fag the sheep-dog stir from his seat and prick his ears; but seeing Monk enter the yard after some outside loitering, Fag seated himself again in an attitude of observation.", "데글리가 으르렁대는 듯한 비아냥을 큰 소리로 내뱉자, 양치기 개 패그가 앉은 자리에서 몸을 들썩이며 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다. 하지만 멍크가 바깥에서 어슬렁거리다가 마당으로 들어오는 것을 보고는 패그는 다시 자리에 앉아 살피는 자세를 취했다."], ["“I’m glad to hear I’m a good feller.”", "\"내가 좋은 사람이란 말을 들으니 기쁘구먼요.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke reflected that it was market-day, and that his worthy tenant had probably been dining, but saw no reason why he should not go on, since he could take the precaution of repeating what he had to say to Mrs. Dagley.", "브룩 씨는 장날인 데다 자기의 점잖은 소작인이 아마 점심을 든 모양이라고 헤아렸지만, 데글리 부인에게 해야 할 말을 되풀이하는 조처를 취하면 되니 말을 계속하지 못할 이유가 없다고 보았다."]], [["“Your little lad Jacob has been caught killing a leveret, Dagley: I have told Johnson to lock him up in the empty stable an hour or two, just to frighten him, you know.", "\"자네 아들 녀석 제이컵이 새끼 산토끼를 잡다가 붙잡혔다네, 데글리. 내가 존슨에게 일러서 그 애를 한두 시간 빈 마구간에 가둬 두라고 했어, 그저 겁을 좀 주려고 말이야, 알다시피."], ["But he will be brought home by-and-by, before night: and you’ll just look after him, will you, and give him a reprimand, you know?”", "하지만 이따가, 날 저물기 전에 집으로 데려다줄 걸세. 그러니 자네가 그 애를 잘 살펴서 따끔하게 야단을 좀 쳐 주게나, 알겠지?\""]], [["“No, I woon’t: I’ll be dee’d if I’ll leather my boy to please you or anybody else, not if you was twenty landlords istid o’ one, and that a bad un.”", "\"싫소. 나리든 그 누구든 비위 맞추자고 내 자식을 두들겨 패느니 차라리 뒈지고 말겠소. 나리가 한 사람이 아니라 스무 명 지주라 해도, 그것도 나쁜 지주라면 말이오.\""]], [["Dagley’s words were loud enough to summon his wife to the back-kitchen door—the only entrance ever used, and one always open except in bad weather—and Mr.", "데글리의 말은 그의 아내를 뒷부엌 문으로 불러낼 만큼 큰 소리였으니, 그 문은 늘 쓰는 유일한 출입구로 궂은 날씨가 아니면 언제나 열려 있었다."], ["Brooke, saying soothingly, “Well, well, I’ll speak to your wife—I didn’t mean beating, you know,”", "브룩 씨는 \"그래, 그래, 자네 아내에게 말하겠네."], ["turned to walk to the house.", "때리라는 뜻은 아니었어, 알다시피.\""], ["But Dagley, only the more inclined to “have his say”", "하고 달래듯 말하며 집 쪽으로 걸어가려고 돌아섰다."], ["with a gentleman who walked away from him, followed at once, with Fag slouching at his heels and sullenly evading some small and probably charitable advances on the part of Monk.", "하지만 데글리는 자기를 두고 걸어가 버리는 신사에게 \"할 말을 다 하고\" 싶은 마음이 더욱 동했으므로 곧장 뒤따랐고, 패그는 그 발치에서 어슬렁거리며 멍크가 베푸는 작고 아마도 호의적인 접근들을 시무룩하게 피했다."]], [["“How do you do, Mrs. Dagley?”", "\"안녕하시오, 데글리 부인?\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, making some haste.", "브룩 씨가 다소 서둘러 말했다."], ["“I came to tell you about your boy: I don’t want you to give him the stick, you know.”", "\"댁의 아들 일로 말하러 왔소. 그 애에게 매를 들기를 바라지 않소, 알겠지.\""], ["He was careful to speak quite plainly this time.", "그는 이번에는 아주 분명하게 말하려고 신경을 썼다."]], [["Overworked Mrs. Dagley—a thin, worn woman, from whose life pleasure had so entirely vanished that she had not even any Sunday clothes which could give her satisfaction in preparing for church—had already had a misunderstanding with her husband since he had come home, and was in low spirits, expecting the worst.", "혹사당한 데글리 부인은 여위고 지친 여인으로, 그 삶에서 즐거움이 너무도 완전히 사라져 버려 교회에 갈 채비를 하며 만족감을 느낄 만한 나들이옷 한 벌조차 없는 처지였는데, 남편이 돌아온 뒤로 이미 그와 한바탕 말다툼을 벌인 터라 풀이 죽어 최악의 사태를 예상하고 있었다."], ["But her husband was beforehand in answering.", "그러나 남편이 먼저 나서서 대답했다."]], [["“No, nor he woon’t hev the stick, whether you want it or no,”", "\"안 되오, 그 애는 매를 안 맞을 거요, 나리가 원하든 말든.\""], ["pursued Dagley, throwing out his voice, as if he wanted it to hit hard.", "데글리가 멀리까지 들리라는 듯 목소리를 내지르며 말을 이었다."], ["“You’ve got no call to come an’ talk about sticks o’ these primises, as you woon’t give a stick tow’rt mending.", "\"나리는 이 집에 와서 매가 어떠니 말할 자격이 없소. 고치라고 막대기 하나 안 대 줄 거면서 말이오."], ["Go to Middlemarch to ax for your charrickter.”", "나리 평판이나 알아보러 미들마치에 가 보슈.\""]], [["“You’d far better hold your tongue, Dagley,”", "\"입 좀 다무는 게 훨씬 낫겠수, 데글리.\""], ["said the wife, “and not kick your own trough over.", "아내가 말했다. \"제 밥그릇 제가 차지 말고요."], ["When a man as is father of a family has been an’ spent money at market and made himself the worse for liquor, he’s done enough mischief for one day.", "한 집안의 가장이란 사람이 장에 가서 돈을 펑펑 쓰고 술에 절어 망가져서 돌아왔으면, 하루치 못된 짓은 충분히 한 거예요."], ["But I should like to know what my boy’s done, sir.”", "하지만 우리 애가 무슨 짓을 했는지 저는 알고 싶네요, 나리.\""]], [["“Niver do you mind what he’s done,”", "\"그 애가 뭘 했는지 당신은 신경 쓰지 마.\""], ["said Dagley, more fiercely, “it’s my business to speak, an’ not yourn.", "데글리가 더 사납게 말했다. \"말하는 건 내 일이지 당신 일이 아니야."], ["An’ I wull speak, too.", "그리고 난 말할 거야."], ["I’ll hev my say—supper or no.", "할 말은 하고 말 거라고, 저녁이고 뭐고. 내가 하려는 말은 이거요."], ["An’ what I say is, as I’ve lived upo’ your ground from my father and grandfather afore me, an’ hev dropped our money into’t, an’ me an’ my children might lie an’ rot on the ground for top-dressin’ as we can’t find the money to buy, if the King wasn’t to put a stop.”", "나는 우리 아버지, 그 위로 할아버지 적부터 나리네 땅에 살면서 우리 돈을 그 땅에 떨어뜨려 왔단 말이오. 그런데 임금님이 막아 주지 않으면, 나하고 내 자식들은 거름 살 돈도 없어서 땅바닥에 누워 썩어 갈 판이오.\""]], [["“My good fellow, you’re drunk, you know,” said Mr.", "\"이 사람아, 자네 취했구먼, 알다시피.\""], ["Brooke, confidentially but not judiciously.", "브룩 씨가 은밀히, 그러나 분별없이 말했다."], ["“Another day, another day,”", "\"다음에, 다음에 하지.\""], ["he added, turning as if to go.", "그가 가려는 듯 돌아서며 덧붙였다."]], [["But Dagley immediately fronted him, and Fag at his heels growled low, as his master’s voice grew louder and more insulting, while Monk also drew close in silent dignified watch.", "하지만 데글리가 즉시 그의 앞을 가로막았고, 주인의 목소리가 점점 커지고 더 모욕적으로 변하자 패그도 그 발치에서 낮게 으르렁거렸으며, 멍크 역시 가까이 다가와 조용히 위엄 있게 지켜보았다."], ["The laborers on the wagon were pausing to listen, and it seemed wiser to be quite passive than to attempt a ridiculous flight pursued by a bawling man.", "수레의 일꾼들도 귀를 기울이려 손을 멈추었으니, 고래고래 소리치는 사내에게 쫓기며 우스꽝스럽게 달아나려 하기보다 완전히 잠자코 있는 편이 현명해 보였다."]], [["“I’m no more drunk nor you are, nor so much,”", "\"나는 나리보다 더 취하지도 않았고, 그만큼도 안 취했소.\""], ["said Dagley.", "데글리가 말했다."], ["“I can carry my liquor, an’ I know what I meean.", "\"나는 술을 견딜 줄 알고, 내가 무슨 말을 하는지도 안단 말이오. 내 말은 임금님이 그걸 막아 주실 거란 거요."], ["An’ I meean as the King ’ull put a stop to ’t, for them say it as knows it, as there’s to be a Rinform, and them landlords as never done the right thing by their tenants ’ull be treated i’ that way as they’ll hev to scuttle off.", "아는 사람들이 그러는데, 개혁이란 게 있을 거고, 소작인들한테 옳은 일을 한 번도 안 한 지주들은 줄행랑을 쳐야 할 만큼 혼이 날 거라고 말이오. 미들마치에는 개혁이 뭔지 아는 사람들이 있단 말이오."], ["An’ there’s them i’ Middlemarch knows what the Rinform is—an’ as knows who’ll hev to scuttle.", "누가 줄행랑을 쳐야 할지도 아는 사람들이. 그자들이 그래요."], ["Says they, ‘I know who your landlord is.’ An’ says I, ‘I hope you’re the better for knowin’ him, I arn’t.’ Says they, ‘He’s a close-fisted un.’ ‘Ay ay,’ says I.", "'난 네 지주가 누군지 알지.' 그래서 내가 그랬소."], ["‘He’s a man for the Rinform,’ says they.", "'그 사람을 안다고 당신들이 더 나아지길 바라오, 난 그렇질 못하니.' 그자들이 그래요."], ["That’s what they says.", "'그 사람 손이 야무진 구두쇠지.' '그렇소, 그렇고말고.'"], ["An’ I made out what the Rinform were—an’ it were to send you an’ your likes a-scuttlin’ an’ wi’ pretty strong-smellin’ things too.", "하고 내가 그랬소. '그 사람은 개혁을 지지하는 사람이오.'"], ["An’ you may do as you like now, for I’m none afeard on you.", "하고 그자들이 그래요. 그게 그자들이 한 말이오."], ["An’ you’d better let my boy aloan, an’ look to yoursen, afore the Rinform has got upo’ your back.", "그래서 내가 개혁이 뭔지 알아냈는데, 그건 나리하고 나리 같은 사람들을 줄행랑치게 만드는 거였소. 그것도 아주 고약한 냄새 풍기는 것들로 말이오."], ["That’s what I’n got to say,”", "이제 나리 멋대로 하슈, 난 나리가 하나도 안 무서우니까. 그리고 내 자식은 내버려 두고, 개혁이 나리 등짝을 덮치기 전에 나리 앞가림이나 잘하슈."], ["concluded Mr.", "내가 할 말은 이게 다요.\""], ["Dagley, striking his fork into the ground with a firmness which proved inconvenient as he tried to draw it up again.", "데글리 씨는 이렇게 말을 맺으며 쇠스랑을 단호하게 땅에 꽂았는데, 그 단호함은 다시 뽑아 올리려 할 때 불편하게 작용했다."]], [["At this last action Monk began to bark loudly, and it was a moment for Mr. Brooke to escape.", "이 마지막 동작에 멍크가 요란하게 짖어 대기 시작했고, 브룩 씨로서는 빠져나갈 순간이었다."], ["He walked out of the yard as quickly as he could, in some amazement at the novelty of his situation.", "그는 자기 처지의 생소함에 다소 어리둥절해하며 되도록 빨리 마당에서 걸어 나왔다."], ["He had never been insulted on his own land before, and had been inclined to regard himself as a general favorite (we are all apt to do so, when we think of our own amiability more than of what other people are likely to want of us).", "그는 자기 땅에서 모욕을 당해 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 스스로를 두루 사랑받는 사람으로 여기는 쪽이었다(우리는 남들이 우리에게서 바랄 법한 것보다 우리 자신의 상냥함을 더 떠올릴 때면 누구나 그렇게 여기기 마련이다)."], ["When he had quarrelled with Caleb Garth twelve years before he had thought that the tenants would be pleased at the landlord’s taking everything into his own hands.", "십이 년 전 케일럽 가스와 다투었을 때, 그는 지주가 모든 것을 자기 손에 직접 거머쥐는 것을 소작인들이 좋아하리라 생각했었다."]], [["Some who follow the narrative of his experience may wonder at the midnight darkness of Mr.", "그의 경험담을 따라온 이들 중 누군가는 데글리 씨의 한밤중 같은 무지에 놀랄지도 모른다."], ["Dagley; but nothing was easier in those times than for an hereditary farmer of his grade to be ignorant, in spite somehow of having a rector in the twin parish who was a gentleman to the backbone, a curate nearer at hand who preached more learnedly than the rector, a landlord who had gone into everything, especially fine art and social improvement, and all the lights of Middlemarch only three miles off.", "하지만 그 시절에는 그만한 처지의 세습 농사꾼이 무지한 것보다 더 쉬운 일은 없었다. 어찌 된 일인지 쌍둥이 교구에는 뼛속까지 신사인 교구 목사가 있었고, 더 가까이에는 그 목사보다 더 박식하게 설교하는 부목사가 있었으며, 무엇이든, 특히 미술과 사회 개선에 파고든 지주가 있었고, 미들마치의 온갖 빛이 불과 삼 마일 떨어진 곳에 있었는데도 그러했다."], ["As to the facility with which mortals escape knowledge, try an average acquaintance in the intellectual blaze of London, and consider what that eligible person for a dinner-party would have been if he had learned scant skill in “summing”", "인간이 얼마나 쉽게 지식에서 벗어나는지로 말하자면, 런던의 지적인 광채 속에 있는 평범한 지인 하나를 시험 삼아 떠올려 보고, 그 만찬에 어울릴 법한 사람이 만약 팁턴의 교구 서기에게서 \"셈하기\"를 어설프게밖에 못 배웠고, 이사야니 아폴로니 하는 이름들이 두 번을 떠듬떠듬 읽고 나서도 도무지 다룰 수 없는 탓에 성경 한 장을 엄청나게 애를 써서 읽었다면 어떠했을지 생각해 보라."], ["from the parish-clerk of Tipton, and read a chapter in the Bible with immense difficulty, because such names as Isaiah or Apollos remained unmanageable after twice spelling.", "가엾은 데글리는 일요일 저녁이면 가끔 몇 구절을 읽었고, 그에게 세상은 적어도 예전보다 더 어둡지는 않았다."], ["Poor Dagley read a few verses sometimes on a Sunday evening, and the world was at least not darker to him than it had been before.", "그가 속속들이 아는 것이 몇 가지 있었으니, 곧 엉성한 농사 습관이며, 프리먼스 엔드의 날씨와 가축과 작물의 까다로움이었다."], ["Some things he knew thoroughly, namely, the slovenly habits of farming, and the awkwardness of weather, stock and crops, at Freeman’s End—so called apparently by way of sarcasm, to imply that a man was free to quit it if he chose, but that there was no earthly “beyond” open to him.", "프리먼스 엔드라는 이름은 아무래도 빈정대는 뜻으로 붙인 듯했으니, 마음만 먹으면 이곳을 떠날 자유는 있으나 그에게 열려 있는 이 세상의 \"그 너머\"란 어디에도 없음을 빗댄 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XL.", "# CHAPTER XL."]], [["Wise in his daily work was he: To fruits of diligence, And not to faiths or polity, He plied his utmost sense.", "그는 매일의 일에 있어 현명했네."], ["These perfect in their little parts, Whose work is all their prize— Without them how could laws, or arts, Or towered cities rise?", "부지런함의 결실을 위해,"]], [["In watching effects, if only of an electric battery, it is often necessary to change our place and examine a particular mixture or group at some distance from the point where the movement we are interested in was set up. The group I am moving towards is at Caleb Garth’s breakfast-table in the large parlor where the maps and desk were: father, mother, and five of the children. Mary was just now at home waiting for a situation, while Christy, the boy next to her, was getting cheap learning and cheap fare in Scotland, having to his father’s disappointment taken to books instead of that sacred calling “business.”", "신앙이나 정치를 위해서가 아니라,\n온 힘을 다해 분별을 기울였네."]], [["The letters had come—nine costly letters, for which the postman had been paid three and twopence, and Mr. Garth was forgetting his tea and toast while he read his letters and laid them open one above the other, sometimes swaying his head slowly, sometimes screwing up his mouth in inward debate, but not forgetting to cut off a large red seal unbroken, which Letty snatched up like an eager terrier.", "저마다 작은 몫에서 완벽한 이들,\n그 일이 곧 그들의 유일한 보상이니—"]], [["The talk among the rest went on unrestrainedly, for nothing disturbed Caleb’s absorption except shaking the table when he was writing.", "그들이 없다면 어찌 법이나 예술이,\n혹은 탑 솟은 도시가 일어설 수 있으랴?"]], [["Two letters of the nine had been for Mary. After reading them, she had passed them to her mother, and sat playing with her tea-spoon absently, till with a sudden recollection she returned to her sewing, which she had kept on her lap during breakfast.", "어떤 작용을 관찰할 때면, 그것이 비록 전지(電池) 하나의 작용에 불과하더라도, 우리가 관심을 둔 운동이 시작된 지점에서 다소 떨어진 곳에 자리한 특정한 혼합물이나 무리를 살펴보기 위해 자리를 옮길 필요가 종종 있는 법이다. 내가 지금 옮겨 가려는 무리는 케일럽 가스의 아침 식탁, 곧 지도와 책상이 놓인 널찍한 거실에 있다. 아버지와 어머니, 그리고 다섯 아이들. 메리는 지금 일자리를 기다리며 집에 머물고 있었고, 그녀 바로 아래 사내아이인 크리스티는 스코틀랜드에서 값싼 학문과 값싼 끼니를 얻고 있었으니, 아버지를 실망시키게도 “사업”이라는 그 신성한 소명 대신 책에 빠져 버린 탓이었다. 편지가 도착했다. 무려 아홉 통이나 되는 비싼 편지였고, 우편배달부에게 삼 실링 이 펜스를 치러야 했다. 가스 씨는 차와 토스트를 잊은 채 편지를 읽으며 한 통씩 위로 겹쳐 펼쳐 놓았는데, 때로는 천천히 고개를 갸웃하기도 하고, 때로는 속으로 따져 보느라 입을 오므리기도 했지만, 깨지지 않은 커다란 붉은 봉인을 잘라 내는 일만은 잊지 않았으니, 레티가 그것을 마치 열성적인 테리어처럼 낚아챘다."]], [["“Oh, don’t sew, Mary!” said Ben, pulling her arm down.", "나머지 식구들 사이의 이야기는 거침없이 이어졌다."], ["“Make me a peacock with this bread-crumb.” He had been kneading a small mass for the purpose.", "케일럽이 글을 쓸 때 식탁을 흔드는 것 말고는 무엇도 그의 몰두를 방해하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["“No, no, Mischief!”", "아홉 통 중 두 통은 메리에게 온 것이었다."], ["said Mary, good-humoredly, while she pricked his hand lightly with her needle. “Try and mould it yourself: you have seen me do it often enough.", "그것을 읽고 나서 그녀는 어머니에게 건넨 뒤, 멍하니 찻숟가락을 만지작거리며 앉아 있다가, 문득 생각이 나서 아침 식사 내내 무릎에 올려 두었던 바느질감으로 돌아갔다."], ["I must get this sewing done.", "“아, 메리, 바느질하지 마!”"], ["It is for Rosamond Vincy: she is to be married next week, and she can’t be married without this handkerchief.”", "벤이 그녀의 팔을 끌어내리며 말했다. “이 빵 부스러기로 공작새 좀 만들어 줘.”"], ["Mary ended merrily, amused with the last notion.", "그러려고 그는 작은 덩어리를 조몰락거리고 있던 참이었다."]], [["“Why can’t she, Mary?” said Letty, seriously interested in this mystery, and pushing her head so close to her sister that Mary now turned the threatening needle towards Letty’s nose.", "“안 돼, 안 돼, 이 장난꾸러기!” 메리가 명랑하게 말하며 바늘로 그의 손을 살짝 찔렀다. “네가 직접 빚어 봐. 내가 만드는 걸 충분히 봤잖니. 난 이 바느질을 끝내야 해. 로저먼드 빈시한테 줄 거야. 다음 주에 결혼하는데, 이 손수건이 없으면 결혼을 못 한대.” 메리는 마지막 말을 떠올리며 재미있어하면서 즐겁게 말을 맺었다."]], [["“Because this is one of a dozen, and without it there would only be eleven,” said Mary, with a grave air of explanation, so that Letty sank back with a sense of knowledge.", "“왜 못 해, 메리?” 레티가 이 수수께끼에 진지하게 흥미를 느껴, 언니에게 바짝 머리를 들이밀자, 메리는 이제 위협적인 바늘을 레티의 코 쪽으로 돌렸다. “이게 열두 장 중 하나거든. 이게 없으면 열한 장밖에 안 되니까.” 메리가 자못 진지하게 설명하는 투로 말하자, 레티는 무언가 알게 되었다는 듯한 기분으로 물러앉았다."]], [["“Have you made up your mind, my dear?”", "“마음을 정했니, 얘야?”"], ["said Mrs. Garth, laying the letters down.", "가스 부인이 편지를 내려놓으며 물었다."]], [["“I shall go to the school at York,”", "“요크에 있는 학교로 가겠어요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["“I am less unfit to teach in a school than in a family.", "“저는 가정에서보다 학교에서 가르치는 편이 덜 부적합해요."], ["I like to teach classes best.", "반을 맡아 가르치는 게 제일 좋고요."], ["And, you see, I must teach: there is nothing else to be done.”", "그리고, 보세요, 저는 가르쳐야만 해요. 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없으니까요.”"]], [["“Teaching seems to me the most delightful work in the world,” said Mrs.", "“내가 보기엔 가르치는 일이야말로 세상에서 가장 즐거운 일이란다.”"], ["Garth, with a touch of rebuke in her tone.", "가스 부인이 약간 나무라는 어조로 말했다."], ["“I could understand your objection to it if you had not knowledge enough, Mary, or if you disliked children.”", "“네게 지식이 부족하거나, 네가 아이들을 싫어한다면 네가 꺼리는 걸 이해할 수 있겠지만 말이야, 메리.”"]], [["“I suppose we never quite understand why another dislikes what we like, mother,”", "“우리는 남이 왜 우리가 좋아하는 걸 싫어하는지 결코 온전히 이해하지 못하는 것 같아요, 어머니.”"], ["said Mary, rather curtly.", "메리가 다소 퉁명스럽게 말했다."], ["“I am not fond of a schoolroom: I like the outside world better.", "“저는 교실이 좋지 않아요. 바깥세상이 더 좋아요."], ["It is a very inconvenient fault of mine.”", "그게 제 아주 곤란한 결점이죠.”"]], [["“It must be very stupid to be always in a girls’ school,”", "“늘 여학교에만 틀어박혀 있으면 무척 답답할 거예요.”"], ["said Alfred.", "앨프리드가 말했다."], ["“Such a set of nincompoops, like Mrs. Ballard’s pupils walking two and two.”", "“꼭 밸러드 부인네 학생들이 둘씩 짝지어 걸어 다니는 것처럼, 그런 멍청이들이라니.”"]], [["“And they have no games worth playing at,”", "“게다가 걔들은 할 만한 놀이도 없어요.”"], ["said Jim.", "짐이 말했다."], ["“They can neither throw nor leap.", "“던지지도, 뛰지도 못하니까요."], ["I don’t wonder at Mary’s not liking it.”", "메리가 그걸 싫어하는 게 이상하지 않아요.”"]], [["“What is that Mary doesn’t like, eh?”", "“메리가 싫어하는 게 뭐라고, 응?”"], ["said the father, looking over his spectacles and pausing before he opened his next letter.", "아버지가 안경 너머로 바라보며, 다음 편지를 열기 전에 잠시 멈추고 물었다."]], [["“Being among a lot of nincompoop girls,”", "“멍청이 여자애들 사이에 끼어 있는 거요.”"], ["said Alfred.", "앨프리드가 말했다."]], [["“Is it the situation you had heard of, Mary?”", "“네가 들었다던 그 일자리니, 메리?”"], ["said Caleb, gently, looking at his daughter.", "케일럽이 딸을 바라보며 부드럽게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, father: the school at York.", "“네, 아버지. 요크의 학교예요."], ["I have determined to take it.", "그걸 맡기로 정했어요."], ["It is quite the best.", "가장 좋은 자리예요."], ["Thirty-five pounds a-year, and extra pay for teaching the smallest strummers at the piano.”", "연봉이 삼십오 파운드인 데다, 가장 어린 피아노 초보들을 가르치면 수당도 따로 받아요.”"]], [["“Poor child!", "“가엾은 것!"], ["I wish she could stay at home with us, Susan,”", "우리와 함께 집에 있을 수 있다면 좋으련만, 수전.”"], ["said Caleb, looking plaintively at his wife.", "케일럽이 아내를 애처롭게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Mary would not be happy without doing her duty,”", "“메리는 자기 의무를 다하지 않으면 행복하지 못할 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Garth, magisterially, conscious of having done her own.", "자신의 의무를 다했다는 의식을 품고서 가스 부인이 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["“It wouldn’t make me happy to do such a nasty duty as that,”", "“그런 끔찍한 의무를 다한다고 제가 행복해질 것 같진 않은데요.”"], ["said Alfred—at which Mary and her father laughed silently, but Mrs. Garth said, gravely—", "앨프리드가 말하자, 메리와 아버지는 소리 없이 웃었으나, 가스 부인은 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Do find a fitter word than nasty, my dear Alfred, for everything that you think disagreeable.", "“네가 못마땅하게 여기는 모든 것에 끔찍하다는 말보다 더 어울리는 단어를 좀 찾아보렴, 앨프리드."], ["And suppose that Mary could help you to go to Mr. Hanmer’s with the money she gets?”", "그리고 메리가 버는 돈으로 너를 해머 씨네로 보내는 데 보태 줄 수도 있다고 생각해 보면 어떻겠니?”"]], [["“That seems to me a great shame.", "“그건 정말 부끄러운 일 같아요."], ["But she’s an old brick,”", "그래도 누난 정말 멋진 사람이에요.”"], ["said Alfred, rising from his chair, and pulling Mary’s head backward to kiss her.", "앨프리드가 의자에서 일어나 메리의 머리를 뒤로 젖혀 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["Mary colored and laughed, but could not conceal that the tears were coming.", "메리는 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었지만, 눈물이 차오르는 것을 감추지는 못했다."], ["Caleb, looking on over his spectacles, with the angles of his eyebrows falling, had an expression of mingled delight and sorrow as he returned to the opening of his letter; and even Mrs.", "안경 너머로 지켜보던 케일럽은 눈썹 끝이 처진 채, 편지를 마저 펼치며 기쁨과 슬픔이 뒤섞인 표정을 지었다."], ["Garth, her lips curling with a calm contentment, allowed that inappropriate language to pass without correction, although Ben immediately took it up, and sang, “She’s an old brick, old brick, old brick!”", "가스 부인조차도, 차분한 만족감에 입술을 살짝 말아 올린 채 그 부적절한 말을 바로잡지 않고 넘겼다. 다만 벤이 곧바로 그 말을 받아, “누난 멋진 사람, 멋진 사람, 멋진 사람!”"], ["to a cantering measure, which he beat out with his fist on Mary’s arm.", "하고 경쾌한 박자에 맞춰 노래하며 주먹으로 메리의 팔을 두드려 박자를 맞추었다."]], [["But Mrs.", "그러나 이제 가스 부인의 시선은 남편에게로 향했다."], ["Garth’s eyes were now drawn towards her husband, who was already deep in the letter he was reading.", "남편은 벌써 읽고 있던 편지에 깊이 빠져 있었다. 그의 얼굴에는 자못 놀란 표정이 떠올라 그녀를 조금 불안하게 했지만, 그는 읽는 도중에 질문받는 것을 좋아하지 않았으므로, 그녀는 초조하게 지켜보며 기다렸다."], ["His face had an expression of grave surprise, which alarmed her a little, but he did not like to be questioned while he was reading, and she remained anxiously watching till she saw him suddenly shaken by a little joyous laugh as he turned back to the beginning of the letter, and looking at her above his spectacles, said, in a low tone, “What do you think, Susan?”", "그러다 그가 편지의 첫머리로 되돌아가며 작은 환희의 웃음에 문득 몸을 떨더니, 안경 너머로 그녀를 보며 낮은 목소리로 말하는 것을 보았다. “어떻게 생각하오, 수전?”"]], [["She went and stood behind him, putting her hand on his shoulder, while they read the letter together. It was from Sir James Chettam, offering to Mr. Garth the management of the family estates at Freshitt and elsewhere, and adding that Sir James had been requested by Mr. Brooke of Tipton to ascertain whether Mr. Garth would be disposed at the same time to resume the agency of the Tipton property. The Baronet added in very obliging words that he himself was particularly desirous of seeing the Freshitt and Tipton estates under the same management, and he hoped to be able to show that the double agency might be held on terms agreeable to Mr. Garth, whom he would be glad to see at the Hall at twelve o’clock on the following day.", "그녀는 다가가 그의 뒤에 서서 어깨에 손을 얹었고, 두 사람은 함께 편지를 읽었다. 그것은 제임스 체텀 경이 가스 씨에게 프레시트와 그 밖의 곳에 있는 가문 사유지의 관리를 맡아 달라고 청하는 편지였고, 티프턴의 브룩 씨가 제임스 경에게 부탁해, 가스 씨가 동시에 티프턴 영지의 관리직도 다시 맡을 의향이 있는지 알아보려 한다는 말이 덧붙어 있었다. 준남작은 자신이 특히 프레시트와 티프턴 사유지가 같은 사람의 관리 아래 놓이기를 바란다고 매우 정중한 말로 적었고, 그 두 곳의 관리직이 가스 씨에게 흡족한 조건으로 유지될 수 있음을 보여 드리기를 바란다며, 이튿날 정오에 저택에서 그를 뵙기를 기쁘게 여기겠다고 했다."]], [["“He writes handsomely, doesn’t he, Susan?”", "“근사하게 썼지 않소, 수전?”"], ["said Caleb, turning his eyes upward to his wife, who raised her hand from his shoulder to his ear, while she rested her chin on his head.", "케일럽이 아내를 올려다보며 말하자, 아내는 그의 머리에 턱을 괴며 어깨에 얹었던 손을 그의 귀로 올렸다."], ["“Brooke didn’t like to ask me himself, I can see,”", "“브룩은 내게 직접 부탁하기가 싫었던 게로군, 알 만하지.”"], ["he continued, laughing silently.", "그가 소리 없이 웃으며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Here is an honor to your father, children,” said Mrs.", "“얘들아, 이건 너희 아버지에게 큰 영광이란다.”"], ["Garth, looking round at the five pair of eyes, all fixed on the parents.", "가스 부인이 부모에게 모두 고정된 다섯 쌍의 눈을 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“He is asked to take a post again by those who dismissed him long ago.", "“오래전에 너희 아버지를 내보냈던 사람들이 다시 자리를 맡아 달라고 청하는 거야."], ["That shows that he did his work well, so that they feel the want of him.”", "그건 아버지가 일을 잘 해내서, 그들이 아버지가 없는 걸 아쉬워한다는 뜻이지.”"]], [["“Like Cincinnatus—hooray!”", "“킨킨나투스처럼요. 만세!”"], ["said Ben, riding on his chair, with a pleasant confidence that discipline was relaxed.", "벤이 규율이 느슨해졌다는 유쾌한 확신에 차서 의자를 말 타듯 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“Will they come to fetch him, mother?” said Letty, thinking of the Mayor and Corporation in their robes.", "* 킨킨나투스: 고대 로마의 정치가로, 위기 때 부름을 받아 나라를 구하고 다시 농사일로 돌아간 인물."]], [["Mrs. Garth patted Letty’s head and smiled, but seeing that her husband was gathering up his letters and likely soon to be out of reach in that sanctuary “business,”", "“그분들이 아버지를 모시러 와요, 어머니?”"], ["she pressed his shoulder and said emphatically—", "레티가 예복을 차려입은 시장과 시 참사회를 떠올리며 물었다. 가스 부인은 레티의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 미소 지었지만, 남편이 편지를 챙기며 곧 “사업”이라는 그 성소(聖所) 안으로 손이 닿지 않게 들어가 버릴 것 같자, 그의 어깨를 누르며 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“Now, mind you ask fair pay, Caleb.”", "“이봐요, 정당한 보수를 꼭 청하세요, 케일럽.”"]], [["“Oh yes,”", "“오, 그럼.”"], ["said Caleb, in a deep voice of assent, as if it would be unreasonable to suppose anything else of him.", "케일럽이, 자신에게 그 외의 다른 것을 기대하는 게 가당치 않다는 듯, 낮고 깊은 동의의 목소리로 말했다."], ["“It’ll come to between four and five hundred, the two together.”", "“둘을 합치면 사백에서 오백 사이가 될 거요.”"], ["Then with a little start of remembrance he said, “Mary, write and give up that school.", "그러더니 문득 무언가 떠오른 듯 흠칫하며 말했다. “메리, 편지를 써서 그 학교는 그만두겠다고 하렴."], ["Stay and help your mother.", "집에 남아서 어머니를 도와다오."], ["I’m as pleased as Punch, now I’ve thought of that.”", "그 생각을 하니, 나는 더없이 기쁘구나.”"]], [["No manner could have been less like that of Punch triumphant than Caleb’s, but his talents did not lie in finding phrases, though he was very particular about his letter-writing, and regarded his wife as a treasury of correct language.", "* 펀치: 영국 인형극의 주인공으로, 의기양양하게 떠벌리는 모습으로 유명하다. 의기양양한 펀치의 모습과 케일럽만큼 동떨어진 것도 없었지만, 그의 재능은 표현을 찾아내는 데 있지 않았다. 비록 그는 편지 쓰는 일에 무척 까다로웠고, 아내를 올바른 언어의 보고(寶庫)로 여겼지만 말이다."]], [["There was almost an uproar among the children now, and Mary held up the cambric embroidery towards her mother entreatingly, that it might be put out of reach while the boys dragged her into a dance. Mrs.", "이제 아이들 사이에서는 거의 한바탕 소동이 일었고, 메리는 사내아이들이 자기를 끌어다 춤을 추는 동안 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 치워 두려고 어머니에게 그 고운 무명 자수감을 애원하듯 내밀었다."], ["Garth, in placid joy, began to put the cups and plates together, while Caleb pushing his chair from the table, as if he were going to move to the desk, still sat holding his letters in his hand and looking on the ground meditatively, stretching out the fingers of his left hand, according to a mute language of his own.", "가스 부인은 잔잔한 기쁨에 잠겨 잔과 접시를 한데 모으기 시작했고, 케일럽은 책상으로 옮겨 가려는 듯 의자를 식탁에서 밀어냈으나, 여전히 손에 편지를 든 채 앉아서 생각에 잠겨 바닥을 바라보며, 자기만의 무언의 언어에 따라 왼손 손가락을 쭉 펴 보였다."], ["At last he said—", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["“It’s a thousand pities Christy didn’t take to business, Susan.", "“크리스티가 사업에 뜻을 두지 않은 게 천추의 한이오, 수전."], ["I shall want help by-and-by.", "머지않아 나는 일손이 필요할 거요."], ["And Alfred must go off to the engineering—I’ve made up my mind to that.”", "그리고 앨프리드는 공학 쪽으로 보내야 해. 그건 이미 마음을 정했소.”"], ["He fell into meditation and finger-rhetoric again for a little while, and then continued: “I shall make Brooke have new agreements with the tenants, and I shall draw up a rotation of crops.", "그는 잠시 다시 명상과 손가락 웅변에 빠져들었다가 말을 이었다. “브룩에게 소작인들과 새 계약을 맺게 할 거요."], ["And I’ll lay a wager we can get fine bricks out of the clay at Bott’s corner.", "그리고 윤작(輪作) 계획도 짤 거고. 보츠 모퉁이의 점토로 좋은 벽돌을 뽑아낼 수 있다는 데 내기를 걸지."], ["I must look into that: it would cheapen the repairs.", "그건 내가 꼭 살펴봐야겠소. 수리비를 줄여 줄 테니까."], ["It’s a fine bit of work, Susan!", "정말 훌륭한 일거리요, 수전!"], ["A man without a family would be glad to do it for nothing.”", "식솔 없는 사람이라면 거저라도 기꺼이 할 일이지.”"]], [["“Mind you don’t, though,”", "“그래도 당신은 그러지 마세요.”"], ["said his wife, lifting up her finger.", "아내가 손가락을 들어 보이며 말했다."]], [["“No, no; but it’s a fine thing to come to a man when he’s seen into the nature of business: to have the chance of getting a bit of the country into good fettle, as they say, and putting men into the right way with their farming, and getting a bit of good contriving and solid building done—that those who are living and those who come after will be the better for.", "“아니, 아니지. 하지만 사업의 본질을 꿰뚫어 본 사람에게 이런 일이 찾아온다는 건 멋진 일이오."], ["I’d sooner have it than a fortune.", "말하자면, 시골 한구석을 제대로 가꿀 기회를 얻고, 사람들이 농사를 옳은 방향으로 짓도록 이끌고, 알찬 설계와 견실한 건축을 해내서, 지금 사는 이들과 후세에 올 이들이 모두 더 나아지게 하는 일 말이오."], ["I hold it the most honorable work that is.”", "나는 그것을 한 재산보다 더 갖고 싶소. 나는 그것이 세상에서 가장 명예로운 일이라고 믿어요.”"], ["Here Caleb laid down his letters, thrust his fingers between the buttons of his waistcoat, and sat upright, but presently proceeded with some awe in his voice and moving his head slowly aside—“It’s a great gift of God, Susan.”", "여기서 케일럽은 편지를 내려놓고, 손가락을 조끼 단추 사이에 찔러 넣고 똑바로 앉았다가, 이내 목소리에 어떤 경외를 담아 천천히 고개를 옆으로 기울이며 말을 이었다. “이건 하느님의 크나큰 선물이오, 수전.”"]], [["“That it is, Caleb,”", "“정말 그래요, 케일럽.”"], ["said his wife, with answering fervor.", "아내가 화답하는 열정으로 말했다."], ["“And it will be a blessing to your children to have had a father who did such work: a father whose good work remains though his name may be forgotten.”", "“그리고 이런 일을 해낸 아버지를 둔 것이 우리 아이들에게 축복이 될 거예요. 이름은 잊힐지언정 그 훌륭한 일은 남는, 그런 아버지를 둔 것 말이에요.”"], ["She could not say any more to him then about the pay.", "그녀는 그때 그에게 보수에 관해서는 더 말할 수 없었다."]], [["In the evening, when Caleb, rather tired with his day’s work, was seated in silence with his pocket-book open on his knee, while Mrs. Garth and Mary were at their sewing, and Letty in a corner was whispering a dialogue with her doll, Mr.", "저녁이 되어, 케일럽이 하루의 일로 다소 지친 채 무릎에 수첩을 펼쳐 놓고 말없이 앉아 있고, 가스 부인과 메리가 바느질을 하고, 레티가 한구석에서 인형과 속삭이는 대화를 나누고 있을 때, 페어브러더 씨가 무성한 풀숲과 사과나무 가지 사이로 팔월의 밝은 빛과 그늘을 가르며 과수원 길을 따라 올라왔다."], ["Farebrother came up the orchard walk, dividing the bright August lights and shadows with the tufted grass and the apple-tree boughs.", "우리는 그가 자신의 교구민인 가스 가족을 좋아했고, 리드게이트에게 메리를 언급할 만하다고 여겼다는 것을 안다."], ["We know that he was fond of his parishioners the Garths, and had thought Mary worth mentioning to Lydgate. He used to the full the clergyman’s privilege of disregarding the Middlemarch discrimination of ranks, and always told his mother that Mrs.", "그는 미들마치의 신분 차별을 무시하는 성직자의 특권을 한껏 누렸으며, 자기 어머니에게 늘 가스 부인이 읍내의 어떤 부인보다도 더 숙녀답다고 말하곤 했다."], ["Garth was more of a lady than any matron in the town. Still, you see, he spent his evenings at the Vincys’, where the matron, though less of a lady, presided over a well-lit drawing-room and whist.", "그렇지만, 보다시피, 그는 저녁이면 빈시네에서 시간을 보냈으니, 그 집 안주인은 숙녀다움은 덜할지언정 환하게 불 밝힌 응접실과 휘스트 놀이를 주관했다."], ["In those days human intercourse was not determined solely by respect.", "그 시절 사람들의 교제가 오로지 존경으로만 결정되던 것은 아니었다."], ["But the Vicar did heartily respect the Garths, and a visit from him was no surprise to that family.", "하지만 부목사는 진심으로 가스 가족을 존경했고, 그가 찾아온 것이 그 가족에게 놀랄 일은 아니었다."], ["Nevertheless he accounted for it even while he was shaking hands, by saying, “I come as an envoy, Mrs.", "그럼에도 그는 악수를 나누면서까지 그 방문의 까닭을 밝혔다. “저는 사절(使節)로 왔습니다, 가스 부인."], ["Garth: I have something to say to you and Garth on behalf of Fred Vincy.", "프레드 빈시를 대신해서 당신과 가스 씨에게 드릴 말씀이 있어서요."], ["The fact is, poor fellow,”", "실은, 가엾은 친구가,”"], ["he continued, as he seated himself and looked round with his bright glance at the three who were listening to him, “he has taken me into his confidence.”", "그가 자리에 앉아 자신의 말에 귀 기울이는 세 사람을 밝은 눈빛으로 둘러보며 말을 이었다. “저를 믿고 속내를 털어놓았답니다.”"]], [["Mary’s heart beat rather quickly: she wondered how far Fred’s confidence had gone.", "메리의 심장이 다소 빠르게 뛰었다. 그녀는 프레드의 고백이 어디까지 갔을까 궁금했다."]], [["“We haven’t seen the lad for months,”", "“그 녀석을 몇 달째 못 봤소.”"], ["said Caleb.", "케일럽이 말했다."], ["“I couldn’t think what was become of him.”", "“대체 어찌 된 영문인지 알 수가 없었지.”"]], [["“He has been away on a visit,”", "“다른 데 다녀왔습니다.”"], ["said the Vicar, “because home was a little too hot for him, and Lydgate told his mother that the poor fellow must not begin to study yet.", "부목사가 말했다. “집이 좀 견디기 힘들었던 데다, 리드게이트가 그 가엾은 친구는 아직 공부를 시작해서는 안 된다고 그 어머니에게 일렀거든요."], ["But yesterday he came and poured himself out to me.", "그런데 어제 그가 와서 제게 속을 다 쏟아 놓았습니다."], ["I am very glad he did, because I have seen him grow up from a youngster of fourteen, and I am so much at home in the house that the children are like nephews and nieces to me.", "저는 그 일이 무척 다행스럽습니다. 그가 열네 살 풋내기일 때부터 자라는 걸 봐 왔고, 그 집에 워낙 편하게 드나들어서 아이들이 제겐 조카들이나 다름없으니까요."], ["But it is a difficult case to advise upon.", "하지만 조언하기 어려운 경우입니다."], ["However, he has asked me to come and tell you that he is going away, and that he is so miserable about his debt to you, and his inability to pay, that he can’t bear to come himself even to bid you good by.”", "어쨌든 그가 제게 부탁하기를, 와서 전해 달라더군요. 자기가 떠날 거라고, 그리고 당신에게 진 빚과 그걸 갚지 못하는 자신의 처지가 너무도 비참해서, 작별 인사를 하러 직접 찾아오는 것조차 견딜 수가 없다고요.”"]], [["“Tell him it doesn’t signify a farthing,”", "“그에게 한 푼어치도 문제될 게 없다고 전해 주시오.”"], ["said Caleb, waving his hand.", "케일럽이 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["“We’ve had the pinch and have got over it.", "“우리도 쪼들렸지만 다 넘겼소."], ["And now I’m going to be as rich as a Jew.”", "게다가 이제 나는 유대인처럼 부자가 될 참이라오.”"]], [["“Which means,” said Mrs.", "“그 말씀인즉,”"], ["Garth, smiling at the Vicar, “that we are going to have enough to bring up the boys well and to keep Mary at home.”", "가스 부인이 부목사를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다. “아이들을 잘 키우고 메리를 집에 둘 만큼은 살림이 넉넉해진다는 거예요.”"]], [["“What is the treasure-trove?”", "“그 횡재가 무엇입니까?”"], ["said Mr. Farebrother.", "페어브러더 씨가 물었다."]], [["“I’m going to be agent for two estates, Freshitt and Tipton; and perhaps for a pretty little bit of land in Lowick besides: it’s all the same family connection, and employment spreads like water if it’s once set going.", "“두 사유지의 관리인이 될 거요. 프레시트와 티프턴 말이오."], ["It makes me very happy, Mr.", "거기다 어쩌면 로윅에 있는 자그마하니 괜찮은 땅도요. 다 같은 집안 연줄이라, 일이란 한번 굴러가기 시작하면 물처럼 번지는 법이지요."], ["Farebrother”—here Caleb threw back his head a little, and spread his arms on the elbows of his chair—“that I’ve got an opportunity again with the letting of the land, and carrying out a notion or two with improvements.", "정말 기쁩니다, 페어브러더 씨.” 여기서 케일럽은 고개를 살짝 젖히고 의자 팔걸이에 두 팔을 벌려 놓았다."], ["It’s a most uncommonly cramping thing, as I’ve often told Susan, to sit on horseback and look over the hedges at the wrong thing, and not be able to put your hand to it to make it right.", "“땅을 임대하고, 개량에 대한 한두 가지 구상을 실행할 기회를 다시 얻게 되어서 말이오. 수전에게 자주 말했지만, 말 위에 앉아 울타리 너머로 잘못된 것을 보면서도 손을 대 바로잡지 못하는 건 여간 답답한 일이 아니거든요."], ["What people do who go into politics I can’t think: it drives me almost mad to see mismanagement over only a few hundred acres.”", "정치에 발을 들이는 사람들은 대체 무슨 마음인지 모르겠소. 나는 고작 몇백 에이커의 잘못된 관리만 봐도 거의 미칠 지경이니 말이오.”"]], [["It was seldom that Caleb volunteered so long a speech, but his happiness had the effect of mountain air: his eyes were bright, and the words came without effort.", "케일럽이 이토록 긴 말을 자청하는 일은 드물었지만, 그의 행복은 산속 공기 같은 효과를 냈다. 그의 눈은 빛났고, 말은 힘들이지 않고 흘러나왔다."]], [["“I congratulate you heartily, Garth,”", "“진심으로 축하드립니다, 가스.”"], ["said the Vicar.", "부목사가 말했다."], ["“This is the best sort of news I could have had to carry to Fred Vincy, for he dwelt a good deal on the injury he had done you in causing you to part with money—robbing you of it, he said—which you wanted for other purposes.", "“이건 제가 프레드 빈시에게 전할 수 있는 가장 좋은 소식이로군요. 그가 당신에게 끼친 피해를, 그러니까 다른 데 쓰려던 돈을 내놓게 한 일을, 그의 표현으로는 그 돈을 강탈한 일을 무척이나 마음에 두고 있었으니까요."], ["I wish Fred were not such an idle dog; he has some very good points, and his father is a little hard upon him.”", "프레드가 저런 게으름뱅이만 아니라면 좋으련만. 그에게는 아주 좋은 점도 몇 가지 있는데, 그의 아버지는 그에게 좀 가혹하지요.”"]], [["“Where is he going?”", "“그 애는 어디로 갑니까?”"], ["said Mrs. Garth, rather coldly.", "가스 부인이 다소 차갑게 물었다."]], [["“He means to try again for his degree, and he is going up to study before term.", "“다시 학위에 도전해 볼 생각이라, 학기 전에 공부하러 올라간답니다."], ["I have advised him to do that.", "제가 그렇게 하라고 권했지요."], ["I don’t urge him to enter the Church—on the contrary.", "저는 그에게 성직에 들어서라고 다그치지는 않습니다. 오히려 그 반대지요."], ["But if he will go and work so as to pass, that will be some guarantee that he has energy and a will; and he is quite at sea; he doesn’t know what else to do.", "하지만 그가 가서 시험에 통과할 만큼 노력한다면, 그건 그에게 기력과 의지가 있다는 어느 정도의 보증이 될 겁니다. 게다가 그는 완전히 막막한 처지라, 달리 무엇을 해야 할지 모르고 있어요."], ["So far he will please his father, and I have promised in the mean time to try and reconcile Vincy to his son’s adopting some other line of life.", "그렇게라도 하면 그 아버지의 뜻에 어느 정도 맞을 테고, 저는 그동안 빈시 씨가 아들이 다른 길을 택하는 데 마음을 누그러뜨리도록 애써 보겠다고 약속했습니다."], ["Fred says frankly he is not fit for a clergyman, and I would do anything I could to hinder a man from the fatal step of choosing the wrong profession.", "프레드는 자기가 성직자에 맞지 않는다고 솔직히 말합니다. 저로서는 한 사람이 그릇된 직업을 택하는 치명적인 발걸음을 내딛는 걸 막기 위해서라면 무엇이든 하고 싶습니다."], ["He quoted to me what you said, Miss Garth—do you remember it?” (Mr.", "그가 제게 당신이 했던 말을 인용하더군요, 가스 양."], ["Farebrother used to say “Mary”", "기억하십니까?”"], ["instead of “Miss Garth,”", "(페어브러더 씨는 “가스 양”"], ["but it was part of his delicacy to treat her with the more deference because, according to Mrs. Vincy’s phrase, she worked for her bread.)", "대신 “메리”라고 부르곤 했지만, 빈시 부인의 표현대로 그녀가 빵벌이를 한다는 이유로 오히려 더 깍듯이 대하는 것이 그의 섬세함의 일면이었다.)"]], [["Mary felt uncomfortable, but, determined to take the matter lightly, answered at once, “I have said so many impertinent things to Fred—we are such old playfellows.”", "메리는 불편했지만, 이 일을 가볍게 받아넘기기로 마음먹고 곧바로 대답했다. “저는 프레드에게 무례한 말을 어찌나 많이 했는지 몰라요. 우리는 워낙 오랜 소꿉동무라서요.”"]], [["“You said, according to him, that he would be one of those ridiculous clergymen who help to make the whole clergy ridiculous.", "“그의 말로는, 당신이 그가 성직자 전체를 우스꽝스럽게 만드는 데 일조하는 그런 우스꽝스러운 성직자 중 하나가 될 거라고 했다더군요."], ["Really, that was so cutting that I felt a little cut myself.”", "정말이지 그 말이 어찌나 날카로운지, 저까지도 좀 베인 기분이 들었습니다.”"]], [["Caleb laughed.", "케일럽이 웃었다."], ["“She gets her tongue from you, Susan,”", "“이 애는 말솜씨를 당신한테서 물려받았구려, 수전.”"], ["he said, with some enjoyment.", "그가 다소 흐뭇해하며 말했다."]], [["“Not its flippancy, father,”", "“그 입바른 가벼움은 아니에요, 아버지.”"], ["said Mary, quickly, fearing that her mother would be displeased.", "메리가 어머니가 언짢아할까 봐 얼른 말했다."], ["“It is rather too bad of Fred to repeat my flippant speeches to Mr. Farebrother.”", "“제 경솔한 말을 페어브러더 씨께 옮기다니, 프레드도 참 너무했어요.”"]], [["“It was certainly a hasty speech, my dear,”", "“확실히 성급한 말이었어, 얘야.” 가스 부인이 말했다."], ["said Mrs. Garth, with whom speaking evil of dignities was a high misdemeanor.", "그녀에게는 윗사람을 험담하는 것이 중대한 비행(非行)이었다."], ["“We should not value our Vicar the less because there was a ridiculous curate in the next parish.”", "“옆 교구에 우스꽝스러운 부목사가 있다고 해서 우리 부목사님을 덜 귀하게 여길 일은 아니지 않니.”"]], [["“There’s something in what she says, though,”", "“그래도 그 애 말에 일리가 있소.”"], ["said Caleb, not disposed to have Mary’s sharpness undervalued.", "케일럽이 메리의 날카로움이 깎아내려지는 것을 원치 않아 말했다."], ["“A bad workman of any sort makes his fellows mistrusted. Things hang together,”", "“어떤 일이든 솜씨 나쁜 일꾼 하나가 제 동료들을 의심받게 만드는 법이거든."], ["he added, looking on the floor and moving his feet uneasily with a sense that words were scantier than thoughts.", "만사가 서로 얽혀 있으니까.” 그는 말이 생각만큼 넉넉지 못하다는 느낌에, 바닥을 내려다보며 발을 불편하게 움직이고는 덧붙였다."]], [["“Clearly,”", "“분명히 그렇습니다.”"], ["said the Vicar, amused.", "부목사가 재미있어하며 말했다."], ["“By being contemptible we set men’s minds to the tune of contempt.", "“경멸받을 만하게 굴어서 우리는 사람들의 마음을 경멸의 가락에 맞춰 놓는 셈이지요."], ["I certainly agree with Miss Garth’s view of the matter, whether I am condemned by it or not.", "비록 제가 그로 인해 단죄될지라도, 저는 분명 가스 양의 견해에 동의합니다."], ["But as to Fred Vincy, it is only fair he should be excused a little: old Featherstone’s delusive behavior did help to spoil him.", "다만 프레드 빈시로 말하자면, 그를 조금은 너그러이 봐주는 것이 마땅합니다. 늙은 페더스톤의 사람을 속이는 행태가 그를 망쳐 놓는 데 한몫했으니까요."], ["There was something quite diabolical in not leaving him a farthing after all.", "결국 그에게 한 푼도 남기지 않은 것은 자못 악마 같은 짓이었지요."], ["But Fred has the good taste not to dwell on that.", "하지만 프레드는 그 일을 곱씹지 않을 만큼은 분별이 있더군요."], ["And what he cares most about is having offended you, Mrs.", "그가 가장 마음 쓰는 건 당신의 노여움을 산 일입니다, 가스 부인."], ["Garth; he supposes you will never think well of him again.”", "당신이 다시는 자기를 좋게 보지 않으리라 여기더군요.”"]], [["“I have been disappointed in Fred,”", "“저는 프레드에게 실망했어요.”"], ["said Mrs. Garth, with decision.", "가스 부인이 단호하게 말했다."], ["“But I shall be ready to think well of him again when he gives me good reason to do so.”", "“하지만 그 애가 다시 좋게 볼 만한 분명한 이유를 보여 준다면, 저는 기꺼이 그렇게 할 거예요.”"]], [["At this point Mary went out of the room, taking Letty with her.", "이 대목에서 메리는 레티를 데리고 방을 나갔다."]], [["“Oh, we must forgive young people when they’re sorry,”", "“오, 젊은 사람들이 뉘우치면 용서해 줘야지요.”"], ["said Caleb, watching Mary close the door.", "케일럽이 메리가 문을 닫는 것을 지켜보며 말했다."], ["“And as you say, Mr. Farebrother, there was the very devil in that old man.", "“그리고 당신 말마따나, 페어브러더 씨, 그 늙은이한테는 정말 악마가 들어 있었소."], ["Now Mary’s gone out, I must tell you a thing—it’s only known to Susan and me, and you’ll not tell it again.", "이제 메리가 나갔으니 한 가지 이야기를 해 드려야겠소. 이건 수전과 나만 아는 일이니, 다시는 입 밖에 내지 마시오."], ["The old scoundrel wanted Mary to burn one of the wills the very night he died, when she was sitting up with him by herself, and he offered her a sum of money that he had in the box by him if she would do it.", "그 늙은 악당이 죽던 바로 그날 밤, 메리가 혼자 곁을 지키고 있을 때, 유언장 하나를 태워 달라고 메리한테 청했답니다. 그러면 곁에 둔 상자 속 돈을 주겠다고 했지요."], ["But Mary, you understand, could do no such thing—would not be handling his iron chest, and so on.", "하지만 메리는, 아시다시피, 그런 짓을 할 아이가 아니오. 그의 철궤에 손을 대거나 하지 않으려 했지요."], ["Now, you see, the will he wanted burnt was this last, so that if Mary had done what he wanted, Fred Vincy would have had ten thousand pounds.", "그런데, 보시오, 그가 태우려던 게 바로 그 마지막 유언장이었소. 그래서 메리가 그가 시키는 대로 했더라면 프레드 빈시가 만 파운드를 받았을 거란 말이오."], ["The old man did turn to him at the last.", "그 늙은이가 마지막엔 그 애한테 마음을 돌렸던 게지요."], ["That touches poor Mary close; she couldn’t help it—she was in the right to do what she did, but she feels, as she says, much as if she had knocked down somebody’s property and broken it against her will, when she was rightfully defending herself.", "그게 가엾은 메리의 마음을 깊이 찌르는 거요. 어쩔 수 없는 일이었고, 그 애가 한 일은 옳았지만, 그 애 말로는, 정당하게 자기를 지키다가 본의 아니게 남의 재산을 쓰러뜨려 부순 것 같은 기분이 든다는 거요."], ["I feel with her, somehow, and if I could make any amends to the poor lad, instead of bearing him a grudge for the harm he did us, I should be glad to do it.", "나도 어쩐지 그 애와 같은 심정이라, 그 가엾은 녀석이 우리에게 끼친 해를 두고 원망하기보다, 만일 그 애한테 어떻게든 보상해 줄 수 있다면 기꺼이 그러고 싶소."], ["Now, what is your opinion, sir?", "자, 당신 생각은 어떻소, 선생?"], ["Susan doesn’t agree with me; she says—tell what you say, Susan.”", "수전은 내 말에 동의하지 않는다오. 수전 말로는, 당신이 직접 말해 보구려, 수전.”"]], [["“Mary could not have acted otherwise, even if she had known what would be the effect on Fred,” said Mrs.", "“프레드에게 어떤 결과가 미칠지 알았더라도, 메리는 달리 행동할 수 없었을 거예요.”"], ["Garth, pausing from her work, and looking at Mr. Farebrother.", "가스 부인이 하던 일을 멈추고 페어브러더 씨를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“And she was quite ignorant of it.", "“게다가 그 애는 그 일을 전혀 몰랐어요."], ["It seems to me, a loss which falls on another because we have done right is not to lie upon our conscience.”", "제 생각엔, 우리가 옳은 일을 했기에 남에게 손실이 돌아간 것이라면, 그것이 우리 양심에 걸려서는 안 되는 거예요.”"]], [["The Vicar did not answer immediately, and Caleb said, “It’s the feeling.", "부목사가 곧바로 대답하지 않자, 케일럽이 말했다. “그건 감정의 문제요."], ["The child feels in that way, and I feel with her.", "그 애가 그런 식으로 느끼고, 나도 그 애와 같이 느끼니까."], ["You don’t mean your horse to tread on a dog when you’re backing out of the way; but it goes through you, when it’s done.”", "길을 비키려고 말을 뒤로 물릴 때 개를 밟을 작정은 아니지만, 일단 그리 되고 나면 마음이 사무치는 법이지요.”"]], [["“I am sure Mrs. Garth would agree with you there,”", "“가스 부인께서도 그 점은 동의하시리라 확신합니다.”"], ["said Mr. Farebrother, who for some reason seemed more inclined to ruminate than to speak.", "무슨 까닭에선지 말하기보다 곱씹는 쪽으로 더 기우는 듯한 페어브러더 씨가 말했다."], ["“One could hardly say that the feeling you mention about Fred is wrong—or rather, mistaken—though no man ought to make a claim on such feeling.”", "“프레드에 관해 당신이 말씀하신 그 감정이 그릇되었다고, 아니 차라리 잘못되었다고는 말하기 어렵겠지요. 비록 누구도 그런 감정에 기대어 권리를 주장해서는 안 되겠지만요.”"]], [["“Well, well,”", "“뭐, 그래요.”"], ["said Caleb, “it’s a secret.", "케일럽이 말했다. “이건 비밀이오."], ["You will not tell Fred.”", "프레드에게는 말하지 마시오.”"]], [["“Certainly not.", "“물론입니다."], ["But I shall carry the other good news—that you can afford the loss he caused you.”", "하지만 다른 좋은 소식은 전하겠습니다. 그가 끼친 손실을 당신이 감당할 수 있게 되었다는 것 말이지요.”"]], [["Mr. Farebrother left the house soon after, and seeing Mary in the orchard with Letty, went to say good-by to her.", "페어브러더 씨는 곧이어 그 집을 나섰고, 과수원에서 레티와 함께 있는 메리를 보고는 작별 인사를 하러 다가갔다."], ["They made a pretty picture in the western light which brought out the brightness of the apples on the old scant-leaved boughs—Mary in her lavender gingham and black ribbons holding a basket, while Letty in her well-worn nankin picked up the fallen apples.", "서쪽 햇살이 잎이 성긴 늙은 가지에 매달린 사과들의 윤기를 도드라지게 하는 가운데, 두 사람은 한 폭의 예쁜 그림을 이루었다. 라벤더빛 깅엄 옷에 검은 리본을 단 메리는 바구니를 들고 있었고, 닳고 닳은 무명옷을 입은 레티는 떨어진 사과를 줍고 있었다."], ["If you want to know more particularly how Mary looked, ten to one you will see a face like hers in the crowded street to-morrow, if you are there on the watch: she will not be among those daughters of Zion who are haughty, and walk with stretched-out necks and wanton eyes, mincing as they go: let all those pass, and fix your eyes on some small plump brownish person of firm but quiet carriage, who looks about her, but does not suppose that anybody is looking at her.", "메리가 어떻게 생겼는지 더 자세히 알고 싶다면, 십중팔구 내일 붐비는 거리에서 그녀 같은 얼굴을 보게 될 것이다. 당신이 거기서 눈여겨본다면 말이다."], ["If she has a broad face and square brow, well-marked eyebrows and curly dark hair, a certain expression of amusement in her glance which her mouth keeps the secret of, and for the rest features entirely insignificant—take that ordinary but not disagreeable person for a portrait of Mary Garth.", "그녀는 거만하게 목을 빼고 음탕한 눈으로 아장아장 걸어 다니는, 시온의 그 오만한 딸들 축에는 들지 않을 것이다. 그런 이들은 다 지나치게 두고, 당당하되 차분한 몸가짐으로 주위를 둘러보면서도 아무도 자기를 보지 않으리라 여기는, 작고 통통하며 거무스름한 사람에게 눈길을 멈춰 보라."], ["If you made her smile, she would show you perfect little teeth; if you made her angry, she would not raise her voice, but would probably say one of the bitterest things you have ever tasted the flavor of; if you did her a kindness, she would never forget it.", "그녀가 넓적한 얼굴에 네모진 이마, 또렷한 눈썹과 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 지녔고, 입은 비밀을 지키는데도 눈빛에는 어떤 유쾌함의 기색이 어리며, 그 밖의 이목구비는 도무지 대수롭지 않다면, 그 평범하되 밉지 않은 사람을 메리 가스의 초상이라 여겨도 좋다."], ["Mary admired the keen-faced handsome little Vicar in his well-brushed threadbare clothes more than any man she had had the opportunity of knowing.", "그녀를 웃게 만들면, 가지런한 작은 이를 보여 줄 것이다. 그녀를 화나게 만들면, 목소리를 높이지는 않되 십중팔구 당신이 일찍이 맛본 가장 쓰디쓴 말 한마디를 던질 것이다."], ["She had never heard him say a foolish thing, though she knew that he did unwise ones; and perhaps foolish sayings were more objectionable to her than any of Mr.", "그녀에게 친절을 베풀면, 결코 잊지 않을 것이다. 메리는 솔질이 잘 된 낡은 옷을 걸친, 날카로운 얼굴의 잘생긴 그 자그마한 부목사를, 자신이 알게 된 그 어떤 남자보다도 더 존경했다."], ["Farebrother’s unwise doings.", "그녀는 그가 어리석은 짓을 한다는 건 알았지만, 어리석은 말을 하는 것은 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없었다."], ["At least, it was remarkable that the actual imperfections of the Vicar’s clerical character never seemed to call forth the same scorn and dislike which she showed beforehand for the predicted imperfections of the clerical character sustained by Fred Vincy.", "그리고 어쩌면 어리석은 말이, 페어브러더 씨의 그 어떤 분별없는 행동보다도 그녀에게 더 못마땅한 것이었는지도 모른다. 적어도, 주목할 만한 점은, 부목사의 성직자적 인품에 실제로 있는 결함들이, 프레드 빈시가 떠맡게 될 성직자적 인품의 예견된 결함들에 대해 그녀가 미리 드러냈던 것과 같은 경멸과 혐오를 결코 불러일으키지 않았다는 사실이다."], ["These irregularities of judgment, I imagine, are found even in riper minds than Mary Garth’s: our impartiality is kept for abstract merit and demerit, which none of us ever saw.", "이런 판단의 편차는, 짐작건대, 메리 가스보다 더 원숙한 정신에서도 발견되는 법이다. 우리의 공정함이란 우리 가운데 누구도 본 적 없는 추상적 미덕과 결함을 위해서만 간직되는 것이니까."], ["Will any one guess towards which of those widely different men Mary had the peculiar woman’s tenderness?—the one she was most inclined to be severe on, or the contrary?", "이 판이하게 다른 두 남자 가운데 메리가 그 특유의 여성적 다정함을 품은 쪽이 누구일지, 누가 알아맞힐 수 있을까? 그녀가 가장 모질게 대하려 드는 쪽일까, 아니면 그 반대쪽일까?"]], [["“Have you any message for your old playfellow, Miss Garth?”", "“옛 소꿉동무에게 전할 말이라도 있습니까, 가스 양?”"], ["said the Vicar, as he took a fragrant apple from the basket which she held towards him, and put it in his pocket.", "부목사가 그녀가 내미는 바구니에서 향긋한 사과 하나를 집어 주머니에 넣으며 말했다."], ["“Something to soften down that harsh judgment?", "“그 모진 판단을 좀 누그러뜨릴 말이라도?"], ["I am going straight to see him.”", "저는 곧장 그를 만나러 가는 길입니다.”"]], [["“No,”", "“없어요.”"], ["said Mary, shaking her head, and smiling.", "메리가 고개를 저으며 미소 지었다."], ["“If I were to say that he would not be ridiculous as a clergyman, I must say that he would be something worse than ridiculous.", "“그가 성직자로서 우스꽝스럽지 않을 거라고 말하려면, 우스꽝스러운 것보다 더 못한 무언가가 될 거라고 말해야 하니까요."], ["But I am very glad to hear that he is going away to work.”", "하지만 그가 일하러 떠난다니 정말 기뻐요.”"]], [["“On the other hand, I am very glad to hear that you are not going away to work.", "“반면에, 저는 당신이 일하러 떠나지 않는다는 게 정말 기쁩니다."], ["My mother, I am sure, will be all the happier if you will come to see her at the vicarage: you know she is fond of having young people to talk to, and she has a great deal to tell about old times.", "우리 어머니께서도, 분명, 당신이 사제관으로 찾아와 주신다면 한결 더 행복해하실 거예요. 아시다시피 어머니는 젊은 사람들과 이야기 나누는 걸 좋아하시고, 옛날이야기도 들려주실 게 많거든요."], ["You will really be doing a kindness.”", "정말 친절을 베푸시는 셈이 될 겁니다.”"]], [["“I should like it very much, if I may,”", "“그래도 된다면, 정말 그러고 싶어요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["“Everything seems too happy for me all at once.", "“갑자기 모든 게 제겐 너무 행복해 보여요."], ["I thought it would always be part of my life to long for home, and losing that grievance makes me feel rather empty: I suppose it served instead of sense to fill up my mind?”", "집을 그리워하는 게 늘 제 삶의 일부일 거라고 생각했는데, 그 불만이 사라지니 어쩐지 좀 허전해요. 그게 분별을 대신해서 제 마음을 채워 주었던 모양이지요?”"]], [["“May I go with you, Mary?”", "“저도 같이 가도 돼요, 메리?”"], ["whispered Letty—a most inconvenient child, who listened to everything.", "레티가 속삭였다. 무엇이든 다 엿듣는, 여간 성가신 아이가 아니었다."], ["But she was made exultant by having her chin pinched and her cheek kissed by Mr. Farebrother—an incident which she narrated to her mother and father.", "그러나 페어브러더 씨가 턱을 살짝 꼬집고 뺨에 입을 맞춰 주자 그녀는 의기양양해졌고, 그 일을 어머니와 아버지에게 떠들어 댔다."]], [["As the Vicar walked to Lowick, any one watching him closely might have seen him twice shrug his shoulders.", "부목사가 로윅으로 걸어가는 동안, 그를 가까이서 지켜본 사람이라면 그가 두 번 어깨를 으쓱하는 것을 보았을지도 모른다."], ["I think that the rare Englishmen who have this gesture are never of the heavy type—for fear of any lumbering instance to the contrary, I will say, hardly ever; they have usually a fine temperament and much tolerance towards the smaller errors of men (themselves inclusive).", "내 생각에, 이런 몸짓을 하는 보기 드문 영국인들은 결코 둔중한 부류가 아니다. 혹시라도 굼뜬 반례가 있을까 저어되니, 거의 그렇지 않다고 해 두자."], ["The Vicar was holding an inward dialogue in which he told himself that there was probably something more between Fred and Mary Garth than the regard of old playfellows, and replied with a question whether that bit of womanhood were not a great deal too choice for that crude young gentleman.", "그들은 대개 섬세한 기질을 지녔고, 사람들의 (자기 자신을 포함한) 사소한 과오에 너그러운 편이다. 부목사는 마음속으로 대화를 나누고 있었으니, 프레드와 메리 가스 사이에는 아마 옛 소꿉동무의 정 이상의 무언가가 있을 거라고 스스로에게 말하고는, 그 한 조각 여인다움이 저 풋내기 청년에게는 지나치게 과분한 것이 아니겠느냐고 자문하며 답했다."], ["The rejoinder to this was the first shrug.", "이에 대한 응답이 바로 첫 번째 어깨 으쓱임이었다."], ["Then he laughed at himself for being likely to have felt jealous, as if he had been a man able to marry, which, added he, it is as clear as any balance-sheet that I am not.", "그러고는 자신이 질투를 느꼈을 법한 것이, 마치 결혼할 수 있는 남자라도 되는 양 굴었다며 스스로를 비웃었으니, 그가 덧붙이기를, 내가 그럴 처지가 못 된다는 건 어떤 대차대조표만큼이나 명백하지 않은가, 했다."], ["Whereupon followed the second shrug.", "이에 두 번째 어깨 으쓱임이 뒤따랐다."]], [["What could two men, so different from each other, see in this “brown patch,” as Mary called herself?", "이토록 서로 다른 두 남자가, 메리 스스로 일컫듯 이 “거무튀튀한 점박이”에게서 대체 무엇을 본 것일까?"], ["It was certainly not her plainness that attracted them (and let all plain young ladies be warned against the dangerous encouragement given them by Society to confide in their want of beauty).", "그들을 끌어당긴 것이 그녀의 평범한 외모가 아닌 것만은 분명했다. (그러니 모든 평범한 처녀들이여, 미모가 없음을 믿어 보라며 사교계가 건네는 그 위험한 부추김을 경계할지어다.) 이 유서 깊은 우리 나라의 한 인간이란, 오랜 세월 서로 주고받은 영향들이 더디게 빚어낸, 참으로 경이로운 하나의 전체다."], ["A human being in this aged nation of ours is a very wonderful whole, the slow creation of long interchanging influences: and charm is a result of two such wholes, the one loving and the one loved.", "그리고 매력이란 그러한 두 전체, 곧 사랑하는 자와 사랑받는 자가 어우러진 결과인 것이다."]], [["When Mr. and Mrs.", "가스 부부가 단둘이 앉아 있을 때, 케일럽이 말했다."], ["Garth were sitting alone, Caleb said, “Susan, guess what I’m thinking of.”", "“수전, 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 맞혀 보오.”"]], [["“The rotation of crops,” said Mrs.", "“윤작 계획이겠죠.”"], ["Garth, smiling at him, above her knitting, “or else the back-doors of the Tipton cottages.”", "가스 부인이 뜨개질감 너머로 그를 보며 미소 지었다. “아니면 티프턴 오두막들의 뒷문이거나요.”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니오.”"], ["said Caleb, gravely; “I am thinking that I could do a great turn for Fred Vincy.", "케일럽이 진지하게 말했다. “나는 프레드 빈시를 위해 큰일을 해 줄 수 있겠다는 생각을 하고 있소."], ["Christy’s gone, Alfred will be gone soon, and it will be five years before Jim is ready to take to business.", "크리스티는 떠났고, 앨프리드도 곧 떠날 테고, 짐이 사업을 맡을 만큼 자라려면 오 년은 걸릴 거요."], ["I shall want help, and Fred might come in and learn the nature of things and act under me, and it might be the making of him into a useful man, if he gives up being a parson.", "나는 일손이 필요할 테고, 프레드가 들어와서 일의 본질을 배우고 내 밑에서 일한다면, 만일 그가 목사 되기를 포기한다면, 그를 쓸모 있는 사람으로 만들어 줄 수도 있을 거요."], ["What do you think?”", "당신 생각은 어떻소?”"]], [["“I think, there is hardly anything honest that his family would object to more,”", "“제 생각엔, 그 애 집안이 그보다 더 못마땅해할 정직한 일은 거의 없을 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Garth, decidedly.", "가스 부인이 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“What care I about their objecting?”", "“그들이 못마땅해한들 내가 무슨 상관이오?”"], ["said Caleb, with a sturdiness which he was apt to show when he had an opinion.", "케일럽이, 자신의 견해가 섰을 때면 곧잘 드러내는 그 완강함으로 말했다."], ["“The lad is of age and must get his bread.", "“그 녀석은 성년이니 제 밥벌이를 해야 하오."], ["He has sense enough and quickness enough; he likes being on the land, and it’s my belief that he could learn business well if he gave his mind to it.”", "분별도 충분하고 머리 회전도 빠른 데다, 땅에서 일하는 걸 좋아하지요. 내가 보기엔 마음만 쏟으면 사업을 충분히 익힐 수 있을 거요.”"]], [["“But would he?", "“하지만 그 애가 그러려 들까요?"], ["His father and mother wanted him to be a fine gentleman, and I think he has the same sort of feeling himself.", "그 애 부모는 그가 번듯한 신사가 되기를 바랐고, 제 생각엔 그 애 자신도 같은 마음이에요."], ["They all think us beneath them.", "그들은 다들 우리를 자기네보다 아래로 여기거든요."], ["And if the proposal came from you, I am sure Mrs. Vincy would say that we wanted Fred for Mary.”", "게다가 그 제안이 당신한테서 나온다면, 빈시 부인은 분명 우리가 메리를 위해 프레드를 탐낸다고 말할 거예요.”"]], [["“Life is a poor tale, if it is to be settled by nonsense of that sort,”", "“그런 따위 헛소리로 좌우될 거라면, 인생이란 참으로 하찮은 이야기겠구려.”"], ["said Caleb, with disgust.", "케일럽이 못마땅한 듯 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but there is a certain pride which is proper, Caleb.”", "“그렇지만 마땅히 지녀야 할 자존심이라는 것도 있는 법이에요, 케일럽.”"]], [["“I call it improper pride to let fools’ notions hinder you from doing a good action.", "“어리석은 자들의 생각 때문에 좋은 일을 못 하게 막는 거야말로 부당한 자존심이라 부르겠소."], ["There’s no sort of work,”", "어떤 종류의 일이든,”"], ["said Caleb, with fervor, putting out his hand and moving it up and down to mark his emphasis, “that could ever be done well, if you minded what fools say.", "케일럽이 손을 내밀어 위아래로 흔들며 강조하면서 열정적으로 말했다. “어리석은 자들의 말에 신경 쓴다면 결코 제대로 해낼 수 없는 법이오."], ["You must have it inside you that your plan is right, and that plan you must follow.”", "자기 계획이 옳다는 확신을 가슴속에 품어야 하고, 그 계획을 따라야만 하는 거지요.”"]], [["“I will not oppose any plan you have set your mind on, Caleb,” said Mrs.", "“당신이 마음을 굳힌 계획에는 반대하지 않겠어요, 케일럽.”"], ["Garth, who was a firm woman, but knew that there were some points on which her mild husband was yet firmer.", "가스 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 굳센 여인이었지만, 자기의 온순한 남편이 오히려 더 굳건한 몇몇 지점이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["“Still, it seems to be fixed that Fred is to go back to college: will it not be better to wait and see what he will choose to do after that?", "“하지만 프레드가 대학으로 돌아가는 건 정해진 일 같으니, 그 후에 그가 무엇을 택할지 기다려 보는 게 낫지 않겠어요?"], ["It is not easy to keep people against their will.", "사람을 제 뜻에 거슬러 붙잡아 두기란 쉽지 않으니까요."], ["And you are not yet quite sure enough of your own position, or what you will want.”", "게다가 당신도 아직 자기 처지가, 또 무엇이 필요할지가 그리 확실치 않잖아요.”"]], [["“Well, it may be better to wait a bit.", "“그래, 좀 기다리는 게 나을지도 모르겠소."], ["But as to my getting plenty of work for two, I’m pretty sure of that.", "하지만 두 사람 몫의 일거리를 넉넉히 구하는 거라면, 그건 꽤 자신 있소."], ["I’ve always had my hands full with scattered things, and there’s always something fresh turning up.", "나는 늘 여기저기 흩어진 일들로 손이 가득 찼고, 언제나 새로운 일이 생기곤 하니까."], ["Why, only yesterday—bless me, I don’t think I told you!—it was rather odd that two men should have been at me on different sides to do the same bit of valuing.", "아니, 바로 어제만 해도, 저런, 당신한테 말 안 한 것 같은데! 두 사람이 서로 다른 쪽에서 같은 평가 일을 맡기겠다고 나를 찾아온 게 좀 묘한 일이었소."], ["And who do you think they were?”", "그 둘이 누구였는지 알겠소?”"], ["said Caleb, taking a pinch of snuff and holding it up between his fingers, as if it were a part of his exposition.", "케일럽이 코담배 한 줌을 집어 마치 자기 설명의 일부라도 되는 양 손가락 사이에 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["He was fond of a pinch when it occurred to him, but he usually forgot that this indulgence was at his command.", "그는 생각날 때면 코담배 한 줌을 즐겼지만, 대개 그 즐거움이 제 손안에 있다는 것을 잊곤 했다."]], [["His wife held down her knitting and looked attentive.", "아내는 뜨개질감을 내려놓고 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Why, that Rigg, or Rigg Featherstone, was one.", "“글쎄, 그 리그, 아니 리그 페더스톤이 하나였소."], ["But Bulstrode was before him, so I’m going to do it for Bulstrode.", "하지만 벌스트로드가 먼저였으니, 나는 벌스트로드 쪽 일을 해 줄 참이오."], ["Whether it’s mortgage or purchase they’re going for, I can’t tell yet.”", "그들이 노리는 게 저당인지 매입인지는 아직 모르겠소.”"]], [["“Can that man be going to sell the land just left him—which he has taken the name for?”", "“그 사람, 방금 물려받은 땅을, 그 이름까지 따른 그 땅을 팔려는 걸까요?”"], ["said Mrs. Garth.", "가스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Deuce knows,”", "“귀신이나 알겠지.”"], ["said Caleb, who never referred the knowledge of discreditable doings to any higher power than the deuce.", "케일럽이 말했다. 그는 떳떳지 못한 행실의 내막을 두고 ‘귀신’보다 더 높은 권능을 들먹이는 법이 없었다."], ["“But Bulstrode has long been wanting to get a handsome bit of land under his fingers—that I know.", "“하지만 벌스트로드가 오래전부터 번듯한 땅을 손아귀에 넣고 싶어 했다는 건 내가 아오."], ["And it’s a difficult matter to get, in this part of the country.”", "그리고 이 고장에서 그런 땅을 구하기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니지.”"]], [["Caleb scattered his snuff carefully instead of taking it, and then added, “The ins and outs of things are curious.", "케일럽은 코담배를 들이마시는 대신 조심스레 흩뜨리고는 덧붙였다. “일이란 그 속내가 묘한 거요."], ["Here is the land they’ve been all along expecting for Fred, which it seems the old man never meant to leave him a foot of, but left it to this side-slip of a son that he kept in the dark, and thought of his sticking there and vexing everybody as well as he could have vexed ’em himself if he could have kept alive.", "여기 다들 줄곧 프레드 몫이라 여겨 왔던 땅이 있는데, 정작 그 늙은이는 그에게 한 뼘도 남길 생각이 없었던 모양이오. 대신 남몰래 숨겨 두었던 곁가지 아들에게 물려주었으니, 그 아들이 거기 눌러앉아 자기가 살아생전 그러했을 만큼 모두를 약 올리길 바란 게지."], ["I say, it would be curious if it got into Bulstrode’s hands after all.", "내 말하건대, 결국 그게 벌스트로드의 손에 들어간다면 묘한 일이 될 거요."], ["The old man hated him, and never would bank with him.”", "그 늙은이는 벌스트로드를 미워해서, 결코 그와 거래하려 들지 않았거든.”"]], [["“What reason could the miserable creature have for hating a man whom he had nothing to do with?”", "“그 가련한 작자가 아무 상관도 없는 사람을 미워할 무슨 까닭이 있었을까요?”"], ["said Mrs. Garth.", "가스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Pooh!", "“쳇!"], ["where’s the use of asking for such fellows’ reasons?", "그런 작자들의 까닭을 물어 무엇 하겠소? 인간의 영혼이란,”"], ["The soul of man,”", "케일럽이 이 표현을 쓸 때면 늘 나오는 그 깊은 어조와 진중하게 고개를 젓는 몸짓으로 말했다."], ["said Caleb, with the deep tone and grave shake of the head which always came when he used this phrase—“The soul of man, when it gets fairly rotten, will bear you all sorts of poisonous toad-stools, and no eye can see whence came the seed thereof.”", "“인간의 영혼이란, 일단 제대로 썩고 나면, 온갖 독버섯을 자네에게 피워 올리는 법이지. 그 씨앗이 어디서 왔는지는 어떤 눈으로도 볼 수 없거든.”"]], [["It was one of Caleb’s quaintnesses, that in his difficulty of finding speech for his thought, he caught, as it were, snatches of diction which he associated with various points of view or states of mind; and whenever he had a feeling of awe, he was haunted by a sense of Biblical phraseology, though he could hardly have given a strict quotation.", "케일럽의 별난 구석 가운데 하나는, 자기 생각을 말로 옮기기가 어려울 때면, 말하자면 갖가지 관점이나 마음 상태와 결부 지어 둔 표현의 토막들을 끌어다 쓴다는 것이었다. 그리고 경외감이 일 때면 언제나 성경 어투의 기억에 사로잡히곤 했는데, 정작 정확한 인용을 댈 수 있었던 적은 거의 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLI.", "# 제38장"]], [["By swaggering could I never thrive, For the rain it raineth every day.", "비는 날마다 내리는 것을."], ["—Twelfth Night.", "— 『십이야』."]], [["The transactions referred to by Caleb Garth as having gone forward between Mr. Bulstrode and Mr.", "케일럽 가스가 스톤 코트에 딸린 토지와 관련해 불스트로드 씨와 조슈아 리그 페더스톤 씨 사이에 오갔다고 언급한 거래는, 이 두 사람 사이에 한두 통의 편지가 오가는 계기가 되었다."], ["Joshua Rigg Featherstone concerning the land attached to Stone Court, had occasioned the interchange of a letter or two between these personages.", "* 십이야: 셰익스피어의 희극."]], [["Who shall tell what may be the effect of writing?", "글로 쓴 것의 영향이 어떠할지 누가 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["If it happens to have been cut in stone, though it lie face down-most for ages on a forsaken beach, or “rest quietly under the drums and tramplings of many conquests,”", "그것이 돌에 새겨졌다면, 비록 버림받은 해변에서 몇 세대에 걸쳐 글자가 새겨진 면을 아래로 향한 채 누워 있거나, \"수많은 정복의 북소리와 짓밟힘 아래 고요히 쉰다\""], ["it may end by letting us into the secret of usurpations and other scandals gossiped about long empires ago:—this world being apparently a huge whispering-gallery.", "하더라도, 끝내는 오래전 제국들 시절에 입에 오르내리던 찬탈과 그 밖의 추문의 비밀을 우리에게 알려 줄지도 모른다. 이 세상은 분명 거대한 속삭임의 회랑인 듯하다."], ["Such conditions are often minutely represented in our petty lifetimes.", "그런 사정은 우리의 자질구레한 일생에서도 종종 세밀하게 재현된다."], ["As the stone which has been kicked by generations of clowns may come by curious little links of effect under the eyes of a scholar, through whose labors it may at last fix the date of invasions and unlock religions, so a bit of ink and paper which has long been an innocent wrapping or stop-gap may at last be laid open under the one pair of eyes which have knowledge enough to turn it into the opening of a catastrophe.", "여러 세대의 촌뜨기들에게 차이던 돌멩이가 기묘하게 이어지는 작은 인과의 고리들을 거쳐 한 학자의 눈앞에 놓이고, 그의 노고를 통해 마침내 침략의 연대를 확정하고 종교의 비밀을 열어 줄 수 있듯이, 오랫동안 무해한 포장지나 빈틈을 메우는 종잇조각에 불과했던 잉크와 종이 한 조각이 마침내, 그것을 재앙의 서막으로 바꿔 놓을 만한 지식을 갖춘 단 한 쌍의 눈 아래 펼쳐질 수 있다."], ["To Uriel watching the progress of planetary history from the sun, the one result would be just as much of a coincidence as the other.", "태양에서 행성의 역사가 흘러가는 것을 지켜보는 우리엘에게는, 그 어느 결과든 똑같이 우연일 뿐일 것이다. * 우리엘: 밀턴의 『실낙원』에서 태양을 다스리는 천사."]], [["Having made this rather lofty comparison I am less uneasy in calling attention to the existence of low people by whose interference, however little we may like it, the course of the world is very much determined.", "이렇게 다소 거창한 비유를 늘어놓고 나니, 우리가 아무리 그것을 달가워하지 않더라도 그들의 간섭으로 세상의 흐름이 크게 좌우되는 천한 사람들의 존재로 관심을 돌리기가 한결 덜 거북하다."], ["It would be well, certainly, if we could help to reduce their number, and something might perhaps be done by not lightly giving occasion to their existence.", "물론 우리가 그들의 수를 줄이는 데 일조할 수 있다면 좋을 것이고, 어쩌면 그들의 존재에 빌미를 가벼이 주지 않음으로써 무언가 이룰 수도 있을 것이다."], ["Socially speaking, Joshua Rigg would have been generally pronounced a superfluity.", "사회적으로 말하자면, 조슈아 리그는 대체로 군더더기로 평가받았을 것이다."], ["But those who like Peter Featherstone never had a copy of themselves demanded, are the very last to wait for such a request either in prose or verse.", "그러나 피터 페더스톤처럼 자기 자신의 복제본을 만들어 달라는 요청을 한 번도 받아 본 적 없는 이들이야말로, 산문으로든 운문으로든 그런 요청을 가장 기다리지 않을 자들이다."], ["The copy in this case bore more of outside resemblance to the mother, in whose sex frog-features, accompanied with fresh-colored cheeks and a well-rounded figure, are compatible with much charm for a certain order of admirers.", "이 경우의 복제본은 외양으로는 어머니를 더 닮았는데, 여성의 경우 개구리 같은 이목구비가 싱그러운 혈색의 뺨과 둥글둥글한 몸매를 동반하면 어떤 부류의 숭배자들에게는 적잖은 매력으로 통할 수 있다."], ["The result is sometimes a frog-faced male, desirable, surely, to no order of intelligent beings.", "그 결과는 때로 개구리 얼굴을 한 사내로 나타나는데, 이는 분명 어떤 지각 있는 존재에게도 탐탁할 리 없다."], ["Especially when he is suddenly brought into evidence to frustrate other people’s expectations—the very lowest aspect in which a social superfluity can present himself.", "더구나 그가 갑작스레 나타나 다른 사람들의 기대를 좌절시킬 때는 — 사회적 군더더기가 자신을 드러낼 수 있는 가장 천한 모습이다."]], [["But Mr. Rigg Featherstone’s low characteristics were all of the sober, water-drinking kind.", "그러나 리그 페더스톤 씨의 천한 특성들은 모두 술을 멀리하고 절제하는 종류의 것이었다."], ["From the earliest to the latest hour of the day he was always as sleek, neat, and cool as the frog he resembled, and old Peter had secretly chuckled over an offshoot almost more calculating, and far more imperturbable, than himself.", "하루 중 가장 이른 시각부터 가장 늦은 시각까지 그는 자신이 닮은 개구리처럼 늘 매끄럽고 단정하고 냉정했으며, 늙은 피터는 자기보다 거의 더 타산적이고 훨씬 더 흔들림 없는 자손을 두었다며 은근히 흐뭇해했다."], ["I will add that his finger-nails were scrupulously attended to, and that he meant to marry a well-educated young lady (as yet unspecified) whose person was good, and whose connections, in a solid middle-class way, were undeniable.", "덧붙이자면 그는 손톱을 빈틈없이 손질했고, 인물이 반반하고 견실한 중산층의 방식으로 연줄이 흠잡을 데 없는, 교양 있는 젊은 숙녀(아직 누구인지 정해지지 않은)와 결혼할 작정이었다."], ["Thus his nails and modesty were comparable to those of most gentlemen; though his ambition had been educated only by the opportunities of a clerk and accountant in the smaller commercial houses of a seaport.", "따라서 그의 손톱과 단정함은 여느 신사들의 그것에 견줄 만했다. 비록 그의 야망은 항구 도시의 작은 상점들에서 서기 겸 회계원으로 일하며 얻은 기회에 의해서만 길러진 것이었지만 말이다."], ["He thought the rural Featherstones very simple absurd people, and they in their turn regarded his “bringing up” in a seaport town as an exaggeration of the monstrosity that their brother Peter, and still more Peter’s property, should have had such belongings.", "그는 시골의 페더스톤 일가가 매우 단순하고 우스꽝스러운 사람들이라 여겼고, 그들은 그들대로 그가 항구 도시에서 받은 \"양육\"을, 오라비 피터가, 더구나 피터의 재산이 그런 친족을 두었다는 괴이함을 더욱 과장한 것이라 여겼다."]], [["The garden and gravel approach, as seen from the two windows of the wainscoted parlor at Stone Court, were never in better trim than now, when Mr. Rigg Featherstone stood, with his hands behind him, looking out on these grounds as their master.", "스톤 코트의 판벽을 두른 응접실 두 창문에서 내다보이는 정원과 자갈길은, 리그 페더스톤 씨가 뒷짐을 진 채 이 땅의 주인으로서 그 부지를 내다보며 서 있는 지금보다 더 잘 손질된 적이 없었다."], ["But it seemed doubtful whether he looked out for the sake of contemplation or of turning his back to a person who stood in the middle of the room, with his legs considerably apart and his hands in his trouser-pockets: a person in all respects a contrast to the sleek and cool Rigg.", "하지만 그가 사색을 위해 밖을 내다보는 것인지, 아니면 방 한가운데 두 다리를 상당히 벌리고 두 손을 바지 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 서 있는 사람에게 등을 돌리려고 그러는 것인지는 의심스러워 보였다. 그 사람은 모든 면에서 매끄럽고 냉정한 리그와 대조를 이루었다."], ["He was a man obviously on the way towards sixty, very florid and hairy, with much gray in his bushy whiskers and thick curly hair, a stoutish body which showed to disadvantage the somewhat worn joinings of his clothes, and the air of a swaggerer, who would aim at being noticeable even at a show of fireworks, regarding his own remarks on any other person’s performance as likely to be more interesting than the performance itself.", "그는 분명 예순을 향해 가는 남자로, 얼굴이 몹시 불그레하고 털이 많았으며, 덥수룩한 구레나룻과 두툼하고 곱슬곱슬한 머리칼에 흰 빛이 많이 섞여 있었고, 약간 비대한 몸집은 다소 닳은 옷의 이음새를 불리하게 드러냈으며, 불꽃놀이 자리에서조차 눈에 띄려 하고 남이 하는 어떤 일에 대한 자신의 논평이 그 일 자체보다 더 흥미로우리라 여기는 허풍쟁이의 분위기를 풍겼다."]], [["His name was John Raffles, and he sometimes wrote jocosely W.A.G. after his signature, observing when he did so, that he was once taught by Leonard Lamb of Finsbury who wrote B.A.", "그의 이름은 존 래플스였고, 때로 서명 뒤에 익살스럽게 W.A.G.라고 적었는데, 그럴 때면 자기가 한때 이름 뒤에 B.A.라고 적던 핀스베리의 레너드 램에게 배웠으며, 그 유명한 교장을 바 램이라 부르는 익살을 자기가, 곧 래플스가 처음 만들어 냈노라고 말하곤 했다."], ["after his name, and that he, Raffles, originated the witticism of calling that celebrated principal Ba-Lamb.", "래플스 씨의 외양과 정신적 풍취는 이러했으며, 그 둘 다 그 시절 상업 호텔의 여행객 휴게실 같은 케케묵은 냄새를 풍기는 듯했다."], ["Such were the appearance and mental flavor of Mr.", "* W.A.G.: '익살꾼(wag)'을 뜻하는 말장난."], ["Raffles, both of which seemed to have a stale odor of travellers’ rooms in the commercial hotels of that period.", "B.A.는 학사 학위. 'Ba-Lamb'은 '아기 양(baa-lamb)'을 빗댄 익살."]], [["“Come, now, Josh,”", "\"이봐, 조시,\" 그가 우렁우렁한 목소리로 말하고 있었다."], ["he was saying, in a full rumbling tone, “look at it in this light: here is your poor mother going into the vale of years, and you could afford something handsome now to make her comfortable.”", "\"이렇게 한번 생각해 봐. 자네 가엾은 어머니가 노년의 골짜기로 접어들고 있는데, 자네는 이제 어머니를 편히 모실 만큼 넉넉히 베풀 수 있잖아.\""]], [["“Not while you live.", "\"당신이 살아 있는 한은 안 됩니다."], ["Nothing would make her comfortable while you live,”", "당신이 살아 있는 한 어머니는 결코 편할 수 없을 테니까요.\""], ["returned Rigg, in his cool high voice.", "리그가 냉정하고 높은 목소리로 대꾸했다."], ["“What I give her, you’ll take.”", "\"내가 어머니께 드리는 걸 당신이 가로챌 테죠.\""]], [["“You bear me a grudge, Josh, that I know.", "\"자네가 날 원망하는 거 알아, 조시."], ["But come, now—as between man and man—without humbug—a little capital might enable me to make a first-rate thing of the shop.", "하지만 이봐, 이제 — 남자 대 남자로서 — 허튼소리 빼고 — 약간의 밑천만 있으면 그 가게로 제대로 한몫 잡을 수 있단 말이야."], ["The tobacco trade is growing.", "담배 장사가 커지고 있어."], ["I should cut my own nose off in not doing the best I could at it.", "거기에 최선을 다하지 않으면 내 코를 베어 내는 격이지."], ["I should stick to it like a flea to a fleece for my own sake.", "나를 위해서라도 양털에 붙은 벼룩처럼 거기 딱 붙어 있을 거야."], ["I should always be on the spot.", "늘 그 자리를 지킬 거고."], ["And nothing would make your poor mother so happy.", "그러면 자네 가엾은 어머니가 그보다 더 기뻐할 일이 없을 거야."], ["I’ve pretty well done with my wild oats—turned fifty-five.", "나도 젊은 날 객기는 거의 다 부렸어 — 쉰다섯을 넘겼으니까."], ["I want to settle down in my chimney-corner.", "이제 난롯가에 자리 잡고 싶다고."], ["And if I once buckled to the tobacco trade, I could bring an amount of brains and experience to bear on it that would not be found elsewhere in a hurry.", "일단 담배 장사에 매달리기만 하면, 어디서도 쉽게 찾을 수 없는 두뇌와 경험을 거기에 쏟아부을 수 있어."], ["I don’t want to be bothering you one time after another, but to get things once for all into the right channel.", "자네를 한 번 또 한 번 귀찮게 하고 싶진 않아, 단번에 일을 제대로 된 궤도에 올려놓고 싶은 거지."], ["Consider that, Josh—as between man and man—and with your poor mother to be made easy for her life.", "그걸 생각해 봐, 조시 — 남자 대 남자로서 — 게다가 자네 가엾은 어머니가 평생 편히 지내게 되는 일이잖아."], ["I was always fond of the old woman, by Jove!”", "나야 늘 그 노인네를 아꼈지, 정말이야!\""]], [["“Have you done?”", "\"다 했소?\""], ["said Mr. Rigg, quietly, without looking away from the window.", "리그 씨가 창에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I’ve done,”", "\"그래, 나야 다 했지.\""], ["said Raffles, taking hold of his hat which stood before him on the table, and giving it a sort of oratorical push.", "래플스가 자기 앞 탁자 위에 놓인 모자를 집어 들며, 연설하듯 한번 쓱 밀어 올리며 말했다."]], [["“Then just listen to me.", "\"그럼 내 말을 들으시오."], ["The more you say anything, the less I shall believe it.", "당신이 어떤 말을 하면 할수록, 난 그걸 더 안 믿을 거요."], ["The more you want me to do a thing, the more reason I shall have for never doing it.", "당신이 내게 어떤 일을 시키고 싶어 하면 할수록, 난 그 일을 절대 하지 않을 이유가 더 늘어날 거고."], ["Do you think I mean to forget your kicking me when I was a lad, and eating all the best victual away from me and my mother?", "내가 어릴 때 당신이 날 발로 차고, 나하고 어머니 입에서 제일 좋은 음식을 죄다 빼앗아 먹은 걸 내가 잊을 성싶소?"], ["Do you think I forget your always coming home to sell and pocket everything, and going off again leaving us in the lurch?", "당신이 늘 집에 와서는 죄다 팔아 챙겨 주머니에 넣고는, 우릴 곤경에 내버려 둔 채 다시 떠나 버린 걸 내가 잊을 성싶소?"], ["I should be glad to see you whipped at the cart-tail.", "난 당신이 짐수레 꽁무니에 묶여 채찍질당하는 꼴을 보면 좋겠소."], ["My mother was a fool to you: she’d no right to give me a father-in-law, and she’s been punished for it.", "내 어머니는 당신한테 바보처럼 굴었지. 어머닌 내게 새아버지를 안겨 줄 권리가 없었고, 그 벌을 받아 온 거요."], ["She shall have her weekly allowance paid and no more: and that shall be stopped if you dare to come on to these premises again, or to come into this country after me again.", "어머닌 매주 받는 생활비를 받을 거고, 그 이상은 없소. 그리고 당신이 다시 이 집에 발을 들이거나, 다시 이 고장으로 날 쫓아온다면 그것마저 끊길 거요."], ["The next time you show yourself inside the gates here, you shall be driven off with the dogs and the wagoner’s whip.”", "다음번에 당신이 이 문 안에 모습을 드러내면, 개들과 마부의 채찍에 쫓겨날 줄 아시오.\""]], [["As Rigg pronounced the last words he turned round and looked at Raffles with his prominent frozen eyes.", "리그는 마지막 말을 내뱉으며 돌아서서 툭 불거진 얼음장 같은 눈으로 래플스를 바라보았다."], ["The contrast was as striking as it could have been eighteen years before, when Rigg was a most unengaging kickable boy, and Raffles was the rather thick-set Adonis of bar-rooms and back-parlors.", "그 대조는 십팔 년 전, 리그가 발로 차이기 딱 좋은 더없이 밉살스러운 소년이었고 래플스가 술집과 뒷방을 누비던 다소 다부진 미남이었을 때와 다름없이 뚜렷했다."], ["But the advantage now was on the side of Rigg, and auditors of this conversation might probably have expected that Raffles would retire with the air of a defeated dog.", "하지만 이제 우위는 리그 쪽에 있었으니, 이 대화를 듣는 이들이라면 아마 래플스가 패배한 개꼴로 물러나리라 예상했을 것이다."], ["Not at all.", "천만에."], ["He made a grimace which was habitual with him whenever he was “out”", "그는 노름판에서 \"졌을\""], ["in a game; then subsided into a laugh, and drew a brandy-flask from his pocket.", "때면 으레 짓던 찡그린 표정을 짓더니, 이내 웃음으로 누그러지며 주머니에서 브랜디 병을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Come, Josh,”", "\"이봐, 조시,\""], ["he said, in a cajoling tone, “give us a spoonful of brandy, and a sovereign to pay the way back, and I’ll go.", "그가 구슬리는 어조로 말했다. \"브랜디 한 모금하고 돌아갈 노자로 1소버린만 주면 갈게."], ["Honor bright!", "명예를 걸고!"], ["I’ll go like a bullet, by Jove!”", "총알처럼 사라질게, 정말이야!\""]], [["“Mind,”", "\"명심하시오,\""], ["said Rigg, drawing out a bunch of keys, “if I ever see you again, I shan’t speak to you.", "리그가 열쇠 꾸러미를 꺼내며 말했다. \"또다시 당신을 보게 되더라도 난 당신한테 말을 걸지 않을 거요."], ["I don’t own you any more than if I saw a crow; and if you want to own me you’ll get nothing by it but a character for being what you are—a spiteful, brassy, bullying rogue.”", "난 까마귀를 본 것이나 다름없이 당신을 내 사람으로 여기지 않으니까. 그리고 당신이 날 자기 사람이라 주장하려 든다면, 얻을 거라곤 당신이 본디 어떤 자인지에 대한 평판뿐일 거요 — 심술궂고 뻔뻔하고 으름장이나 놓는 악당이라는 평판 말이오.\""]], [["“That’s a pity, now, Josh,”", "\"그것참 안타까운 일이군, 조시.\""], ["said Raffles, affecting to scratch his head and wrinkle his brows upward as if he were nonplussed.", "래플스가 어쩔 줄 모르겠다는 듯 머리를 긁적이고 눈썹을 위로 찌푸리는 시늉을 하며 말했다."], ["“I’m very fond of you; by Jove, I am!", "\"난 자네가 정말 좋아. 맹세코, 정말이야!"], ["There’s nothing I like better than plaguing you—you’re so like your mother, and I must do without it.", "자네를 골탕 먹이는 것보다 더 좋은 게 없는데 말이야 — 자네가 어머니를 어찌나 닮았는지 — 이제 그것 없이 지내야 하다니."], ["But the brandy and the sovereign’s a bargain.”", "하지만 브랜디하고 소버린은 약속한 거다.\""]], [["He jerked forward the flask and Rigg went to a fine old oaken bureau with his keys.", "그가 병을 앞으로 쑥 내밀자 리그는 열쇠를 들고 멋들어진 낡은 떡갈나무 책상으로 갔다."], ["But Raffles had reminded himself by his movement with the flask that it had become dangerously loose from its leather covering, and catching sight of a folded paper which had fallen within the fender, he took it up and shoved it under the leather so as to make the glass firm.", "그런데 래플스는 병을 다루다가, 그 병이 가죽 덮개에서 위험할 만큼 헐거워졌다는 걸 떠올렸고, 난로 가리개 안으로 떨어져 있던 접힌 종이 한 장을 발견하고는 그것을 집어 가죽 밑으로 밀어 넣어 유리병을 단단히 고정시켰다."]], [["By that time Rigg came forward with a brandy-bottle, filled the flask, and handed Raffles a sovereign, neither looking at him nor speaking to him.", "그때쯤 리그가 브랜디 병을 들고 와서 병을 채워 주고 래플스에게 1소버린을 건넸는데, 그를 쳐다보지도 말을 걸지도 않았다."], ["After locking up the bureau again, he walked to the window and gazed out as impassibly as he had done at the beginning of the interview, while Raffles took a small allowance from the flask, screwed it up, and deposited it in his side-pocket, with provoking slowness, making a grimace at his stepson’s back.", "다시 책상을 잠근 뒤 그는 창가로 걸어가 면담 처음과 똑같이 무심하게 밖을 응시했고, 그동안 래플스는 병에서 조금 따라 마시고는 마개를 돌려 닫아 옆 주머니에 챙겨 넣으며, 약을 올리듯 느릿느릿 의붓아들의 등을 향해 얼굴을 찡그렸다."]], [["“Farewell, Josh—and if forever!”", "\"잘 있게, 조시 — 영영 헤어지는 거라 해도!\""], ["said Raffles, turning back his head as he opened the door.", "래플스가 문을 열며 고개를 돌려 말했다."]], [["Rigg saw him leave the grounds and enter the lane.", "리그는 그가 부지를 떠나 오솔길로 들어서는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["The gray day had turned to a light drizzling rain, which freshened the hedgerows and the grassy borders of the by-roads, and hastened the laborers who were loading the last shocks of corn.", "잿빛이던 하루는 가볍게 부슬부슬 내리는 비로 바뀌어 산울타리와 샛길의 풀 돋은 가장자리를 싱그럽게 적시고, 마지막 곡식 단을 거두어 싣던 일꾼들의 손길을 재촉했다."], ["Raffles, walking with the uneasy gait of a town loiterer obliged to do a bit of country journeying on foot, looked as incongruous amid this moist rural quiet and industry as if he had been a baboon escaped from a menagerie.", "도시의 한량이 시골길을 걸어가야 할 때 보일 법한 불편한 걸음걸이로 걷는 래플스는, 이 촉촉한 시골의 고요와 부지런함 한가운데서 마치 동물원에서 도망친 비비처럼 어울리지 않아 보였다."], ["But there were none to stare at him except the long-weaned calves, and none to show dislike of his appearance except the little water-rats which rustled away at his approach.", "하지만 그를 빤히 쳐다보는 것이라곤 젖 뗀 지 오래된 송아지들뿐이었고, 그의 모습을 싫어하는 기색을 보이는 것이라곤 그가 다가오자 바스락거리며 달아나는 작은 물쥐들뿐이었다."]], [["He was fortunate enough when he got on to the highroad to be overtaken by the stage-coach, which carried him to Brassing; and there he took the new-made railway, observing to his fellow-passengers that he considered it pretty well seasoned now it had done for Huskisson. Mr. Raffles on most occasions kept up the sense of having been educated at an academy, and being able, if he chose, to pass well everywhere; indeed, there was not one of his fellow-men whom he did not feel himself in a position to ridicule and torment, confident of the entertainment which he thus gave to all the rest of the company.", "그는 큰길에 들어섰을 때 운 좋게도 역마차를 만나 그것을 타고 브래싱까지 갔다. 거기서 그는 새로 놓인 철도를 탔는데, 동승객들에게 이제 이 철도가 허스키슨까지 해치웠으니 꽤 길이 들었다 싶다고 말했다. 래플스 씨는 대개의 경우 자신이 학교에서 교육받았으며, 마음만 먹으면 어디서든 무난히 처신할 수 있다는 느낌을 유지했다. 실제로 그는 동료 인간들 가운데 자기가 조롱하고 괴롭힐 처지가 못 된다고 여기는 자가 하나도 없었으며, 그렇게 함으로써 나머지 일행 모두에게 즐거움을 준다고 자신했다. * 허스키슨: 윌리엄 허스키슨. 1830년 리버풀-맨체스터 철도 개통식에서 기차에 치여 숨진 정치가. 철도 사고 사망자의 대명사처럼 쓰인다."]], [["He played this part now with as much spirit as if his journey had been entirely successful, resorting at frequent intervals to his flask.", "그는 지금도 마치 자기 여행이 완전히 성공한 양 그 역할을 한껏 신명 나게 해냈으며, 틈틈이 병에 손을 댔다."], ["The paper with which he had wedged it was a letter signed Nicholas Bulstrode, but Raffles was not likely to disturb it from its present useful position.", "그가 병을 고정시키는 데 끼워 둔 종이는 니컬러스 불스트로드라는 서명이 적힌 편지였지만, 래플스가 그것을 지금의 유용한 자리에서 끄집어낼 일은 없을 듯했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLII.", "# CHAPTER XLII."]], [["How much, methinks, I could despise this man Were I not bound in charity against it!", "생각해 보건대, 자비심에 매여 있지 않다면 내가 이 사람을 얼마나 경멸할 수 있으랴!"], ["—SHAKESPEARE: Henry VIII.", "— 셰익스피어, 『헨리 8세』*"]], [["One of the professional calls made by Lydgate soon after his return from his wedding-journey was to Lowick Manor, in consequence of a letter which had requested him to fix a time for his visit.", "* 셰익스피어의 사극 『헨리 8세』 2막 1장에 나오는 대사. 리드게이트가 신혼여행에서 돌아온 직후 직업상 방문한 곳 가운데 하나는 로윅 영주관이었는데, 이는 방문 시간을 정해 달라고 청하는 편지를 받았기 때문이었다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon had never put any question concerning the nature of his illness to Lydgate, nor had he even to Dorothea betrayed any anxiety as to how far it might be likely to cut short his labors or his life.", "캐소본 씨는 리드게이트에게 자기 병의 성질에 관해 어떤 질문도 한 적이 없었고, 도러시아에게조차 그 병이 자신의 노작이나 생명을 얼마나 단축시킬지에 대한 어떤 불안도 내비친 적이 없었다."], ["On this point, as on all others, he shrank from pity; and if the suspicion of being pitied for anything in his lot surmised or known in spite of himself was embittering, the idea of calling forth a show of compassion by frankly admitting an alarm or a sorrow was necessarily intolerable to him.", "이 점에서도, 다른 모든 점에서와 마찬가지로, 그는 동정을 받는 것을 꺼렸다. 자기 처지의 무언가가 본의 아니게 짐작되거나 알려져 동정의 대상이 되리라는 의심이 쓰라린 것이라면, 두려움이나 슬픔을 솔직히 인정함으로써 연민의 표시를 불러일으킨다는 생각은 그에게 도저히 견딜 수 없는 것일 수밖에 없었다."], ["Every proud mind knows something of this experience, and perhaps it is only to be overcome by a sense of fellowship deep enough to make all efforts at isolation seem mean and petty instead of exalting.", "자존심 강한 정신이라면 누구나 이런 경험을 어느 정도 알고 있으니, 어쩌면 그것은 고립을 위한 온갖 노력을 고결한 것이 아니라 비열하고 하찮은 것으로 보이게 할 만큼 깊은 동류의식에 의해서만 극복될 수 있는 것인지도 모른다."]], [["But Mr. Casaubon was now brooding over something through which the question of his health and life haunted his silence with a more harassing importunity even than through the autumnal unripeness of his authorship.", "그러나 캐소본 씨는 이제 무언가를 곱씹고 있었으니, 그것을 통해 그의 건강과 생명의 문제가 그의 침묵을 괴롭혔던바, 그의 저술이 가을이 되어도 익지 못한 채라는 사실을 통한 것보다 한층 더 성가신 집요함으로 그를 따라다녔다."], ["It is true that this last might be called his central ambition; but there are some kinds of authorship in which by far the largest result is the uneasy susceptibility accumulated in the consciousness of the author—one knows of the river by a few streaks amid a long-gathered deposit of uncomfortable mud.", "사실 이 마지막 것은 그의 핵심적인 야심이라 불릴 만했다. 그러나 어떤 종류의 저술에서는 가장 큰 결과가 저자의 의식 속에 쌓인 불안정한 예민함뿐인 경우가 있으니 — 누군가는 오래 가라앉은 불쾌한 진흙 퇴적층 사이로 드러난 몇 줄기 흔적으로 강이 있음을 알아챈다."], ["That was the way with Mr. Casaubon’s hard intellectual labors.", "캐소본 씨의 고된 지적 노작이 바로 그러했다."], ["Their most characteristic result was not the “Key to all Mythologies,” but a morbid consciousness that others did not give him the place which he had not demonstrably merited—a perpetual suspicious conjecture that the views entertained of him were not to his advantage—a melancholy absence of passion in his efforts at achievement, and a passionate resistance to the confession that he had achieved nothing.", "그것의 가장 특징적인 결과는 『모든 신화의 열쇠』가 아니라, 다른 이들이 자기에게 합당한 자리를 — 입증할 수 없게도 받을 자격이 없는 자리를 — 주지 않는다는 병적인 의식이었고, 자기를 향한 견해가 자기에게 유리하지 않으리라는 끊임없는 의심에 찬 억측이었으며, 업적을 이루려는 노력에 열정이 우울하게 결여되어 있다는 사실과, 아무것도 이루지 못했다는 고백에 대한 열정적인 저항이었다."]], [["Thus his intellectual ambition which seemed to others to have absorbed and dried him, was really no security against wounds, least of all against those which came from Dorothea.", "이리하여 다른 이들에게는 그를 송두리째 흡수해 메마르게 한 것처럼 보였던 그의 지적 야심은, 실은 상처를 막아 줄 아무런 방패가 되지 못했으니, 무엇보다도 도러시아에게서 오는 상처에 대해서는 더욱 그러했다."], ["And he had begun now to frame possibilities for the future which were somehow more embittering to him than anything his mind had dwelt on before.", "그리고 그는 이제 미래에 대한 여러 가능성을 그려 보기 시작했는데, 그것은 어쩐지 그의 정신이 이전에 머물렀던 그 무엇보다도 그를 더욱 쓰라리게 했다."]], [["Against certain facts he was helpless: against Will Ladislaw’s existence, his defiant stay in the neighborhood of Lowick, and his flippant state of mind with regard to the possessors of authentic, well-stamped erudition: against Dorothea’s nature, always taking on some new shape of ardent activity, and even in submission and silence covering fervid reasons which it was an irritation to think of: against certain notions and likings which had taken possession of her mind in relation to subjects that he could not possibly discuss with her. There was no denying that Dorothea was as virtuous and lovely a young lady as he could have obtained for a wife; but a young lady turned out to be something more troublesome than he had conceived. She nursed him, she read to him, she anticipated his wants, and was solicitous about his feelings; but there had entered into the husband’s mind the certainty that she judged him, and that her wifely devotedness was like a penitential expiation of unbelieving thoughts—was accompanied with a power of comparison by which himself and his doings were seen too luminously as a part of things in general. His discontent passed vapor-like through all her gentle loving manifestations, and clung to that inappreciative world which she had only brought nearer to him.", "어떤 사실들 앞에서 그는 무력했다. 윌 래디슬로의 존재, 로윅 근방에 도전적으로 머무르는 그의 태도, 진정하고 확실하게 검증된 박식을 지닌 이들에 대한 그의 경박한 마음가짐 앞에서 그러했다. 끊임없이 열렬한 활동의 새로운 형태를 띠는, 그리고 순종과 침묵 속에서조차 생각만 해도 짜증스러운 열정적인 이유를 감추고 있는 도러시아의 천성 앞에서도 그러했다. 그가 그녀와 도저히 논할 수 없는 주제들과 관련하여 그녀의 마음을 사로잡은 어떤 관념과 호감 앞에서도 그러했다. 도러시아가 그가 아내로 얻을 수 있었던 만큼 정숙하고 사랑스러운 젊은 숙녀라는 것은 부인할 수 없었다. 그러나 한 젊은 숙녀란 그가 생각했던 것보다 더 성가신 존재로 드러났다. 그녀는 그를 간호했고, 그에게 책을 읽어 주었으며, 그의 필요를 미리 헤아렸고, 그의 감정에 마음을 썼다. 그러나 남편의 마음속에는 그녀가 자기를 판단하고 있다는 확신과, 그녀의 아내다운 헌신이 마치 믿음 없는 생각에 대한 참회의 속죄와도 같다는 확신 — 자기 자신과 자기가 하는 일이 전체 사물의 일부로서 너무나 환히 드러나 보이는 비교의 능력을 동반하고 있다는 확신이 들어와 있었다. 그의 불만은 그녀의 온갖 다정하고 사랑스러운 표현을 안개처럼 뚫고 지나가, 그녀가 그에게 더 가까이 끌어다 놓았을 뿐인 그 몰이해한 세계에 들러붙었다."]], [["Poor Mr. Casaubon!", "가엾은 캐소본 씨!"], ["This suffering was the harder to bear because it seemed like a betrayal: the young creature who had worshipped him with perfect trust had quickly turned into the critical wife; and early instances of criticism and resentment had made an impression which no tenderness and submission afterwards could remove.", "이 고통은 배신처럼 느껴졌기에 견디기가 더욱 힘들었다. 완전한 신뢰로 그를 숭배하던 어린 존재가 어느새 비판적인 아내로 변해 버렸고, 비판과 분개의 초기 사례들이 새겨 놓은 인상은 그 후의 어떤 다정함과 순종으로도 지울 수 없었다."], ["To his suspicious interpretation Dorothea’s silence now was a suppressed rebellion; a remark from her which he had not in any way anticipated was an assertion of conscious superiority; her gentle answers had an irritating cautiousness in them; and when she acquiesced it was a self-approved effort of forbearance.", "그의 의심에 찬 해석으로는, 이제 도러시아의 침묵은 억눌린 반항이었고, 그가 전혀 예상치 못한 그녀의 말 한마디는 의식적인 우월감의 표명이었으며, 그녀의 부드러운 대답에는 짜증스러운 신중함이 깃들어 있었고, 그녀가 묵묵히 따를 때면 그것은 스스로 흡족해하는 인내의 노력이었다."], ["The tenacity with which he strove to hide this inward drama made it the more vivid for him; as we hear with the more keenness what we wish others not to hear.", "이 내면의 드라마를 감추려는 그의 끈질긴 애씀이 오히려 그것을 그에게 더욱 생생하게 만들었으니, 우리가 남들이 듣지 않기를 바라는 것일수록 더 예민하게 들리는 법이다."]], [["Instead of wondering at this result of misery in Mr. Casaubon, I think it quite ordinary.", "캐소본 씨에게서 비참함이 빚어낸 이런 결과를 의아하게 여기기보다, 나는 그것이 지극히 평범한 일이라고 생각한다."], ["Will not a tiny speck very close to our vision blot out the glory of the world, and leave only a margin by which we see the blot?", "우리 눈에 아주 가까이 붙은 작은 티끌 하나가 세계의 영광을 가려 버리고, 그 티끌을 보게 하는 가장자리만 남기지 않던가?"], ["I know no speck so troublesome as self.", "나는 자아만큼 성가신 티끌을 알지 못한다."], ["And who, if Mr. Casaubon had chosen to expound his discontents—his suspicions that he was not any longer adored without criticism—could have denied that they were founded on good reasons?", "그리고 만약 캐소본 씨가 자기 불만을 — 자기가 더 이상 비판 없이 흠모받지 못한다는 의심을 — 펼쳐 보이기로 했다면, 그 의심이 그럴 만한 이유에 근거한 것임을 누가 부정할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["On the contrary, there was a strong reason to be added, which he had not himself taken explicitly into account—namely, that he was not unmixedly adorable.", "오히려 그가 스스로 명확히 고려하지 못한 강력한 이유 하나를 덧붙일 수 있었으니 — 바로 그가 순전하게 흠모할 만한 사람은 아니었다는 점이다."], ["He suspected this, however, as he suspected other things, without confessing it, and like the rest of us, felt how soothing it would have been to have a companion who would never find it out.", "그러나 그는 다른 일들을 의심하듯 이 점도 고백하지 않은 채 의심하면서, 우리 나머지 사람들과 마찬가지로, 그것을 결코 알아채지 못할 동반자가 있었다면 얼마나 위안이 되었을까 하고 느꼈다."]], [["This sore susceptibility in relation to Dorothea was thoroughly prepared before Will Ladislaw had returned to Lowick, and what had occurred since then had brought Mr. Casaubon’s power of suspicious construction into exasperated activity.", "도러시아와 관련된 이 쓰라린 예민함은 윌 래디슬로가 로윅으로 돌아오기 전에 이미 충분히 준비되어 있었으니, 그 이후에 일어난 일들이 캐소본 씨의 의심에 찬 해석의 능력을 격앙된 활동으로 몰아넣었다."], ["To all the facts which he knew, he added imaginary facts both present and future which became more real to him than those because they called up a stronger dislike, a more predominating bitterness.", "그는 자기가 아는 모든 사실에다 현재와 미래의 상상의 사실들을 보태었는데, 이것들이 더 강한 혐오와 더 압도적인 쓰라림을 불러일으켰기에 실제 사실들보다 그에게는 더 현실적인 것이 되었다."], ["Suspicion and jealousy of Will Ladislaw’s intentions, suspicion and jealousy of Dorothea’s impressions, were constantly at their weaving work.", "윌 래디슬로의 의도에 대한 의심과 질투, 도러시아의 인상에 대한 의심과 질투가 끊임없이 베틀을 짜고 있었다."], ["It would be quite unjust to him to suppose that he could have entered into any coarse misinterpretation of Dorothea: his own habits of mind and conduct, quite as much as the open elevation of her nature, saved him from any such mistake.", "그가 도러시아에 대해 어떤 천박한 오해에 빠질 수 있었으리라고 가정하는 것은 그에게 매우 부당한 일이리라. 그녀의 천성이 지닌 공공연한 고결함 못지않게 그 자신의 정신과 행동의 습관이 그를 그런 잘못으로부터 지켜 주었다."], ["What he was jealous of was her opinion, the sway that might be given to her ardent mind in its judgments, and the future possibilities to which these might lead her.", "그가 질투한 것은 그녀의 견해였고, 판단에 있어 그녀의 열렬한 정신에 주어질지도 모를 영향력이었으며, 그것이 그녀를 이끌어 갈 미래의 가능성들이었다."], ["As to Will, though until his last defiant letter he had nothing definite which he would choose formally to allege against him, he felt himself warranted in believing that he was capable of any design which could fascinate a rebellious temper and an undisciplined impulsiveness.", "윌에 대해서는, 그의 마지막 도전적인 편지가 오기 전까지는 정식으로 그에게 들이댈 만한 명확한 것이 아무것도 없었지만, 그는 윌이 반항적인 기질과 절제되지 않은 충동성을 매혹할 수 있는 어떤 계략이든 꾸밀 수 있는 자라고 믿을 만한 근거가 자기에게 있다고 느꼈다."], ["He was quite sure that Dorothea was the cause of Will’s return from Rome, and his determination to settle in the neighborhood; and he was penetrating enough to imagine that Dorothea had innocently encouraged this course.", "그는 도러시아가 윌이 로마에서 돌아온 원인이며 근방에 정착하기로 한 결심의 원인이라고 확신했다. 그리고 그는 도러시아가 순진하게 이 행보를 부추겼으리라고 상상할 만큼 통찰력이 있었다."], ["It was as clear as possible that she was ready to be attached to Will and to be pliant to his suggestions: they had never had a tête-à-tête without her bringing away from it some new troublesome impression, and the last interview that Mr.", "그녀가 윌에게 마음을 주고 그의 제안에 유순하게 따를 준비가 되어 있다는 것은 더할 나위 없이 분명해 보였다."], ["Casaubon was aware of (Dorothea, on returning from Freshitt Hall, had for the first time been silent about having seen Will) had led to a scene which roused an angrier feeling against them both than he had ever known before.", "두 사람이 단둘이 마주한 자리에서 그녀가 새로운 성가신 인상을 가지고 돌아오지 않은 적이 없었고, 캐소본 씨가 아는 마지막 만남은(도러시아는 프레시트 홀에서 돌아왔을 때 처음으로 윌을 만났다는 사실을 침묵했다) 그가 일찍이 알았던 것보다 더 분노에 찬 감정을 두 사람 모두에 대해 불러일으킨 장면으로 이어졌다."], ["Dorothea’s outpouring of her notions about money, in the darkness of the night, had done nothing but bring a mixture of more odious foreboding into her husband’s mind.", "한밤의 어둠 속에서 돈에 관한 자기 생각을 쏟아낸 도러시아의 말은 남편의 마음에 더욱 가증스러운 예감의 혼합물을 가져다주었을 뿐이었다."]], [["And there was the shock lately given to his health always sadly present with him.", "게다가 최근 건강에 받은 충격이 늘 슬프게도 그와 함께했다."], ["He was certainly much revived; he had recovered all his usual power of work: the illness might have been mere fatigue, and there might still be twenty years of achievement before him, which would justify the thirty years of preparation.", "그는 분명 많이 회복되었다. 평소의 작업 능력을 모두 되찾았으니, 그 병은 단순한 피로였을지도 모르고, 그 앞에는 아직 이십 년의 업적이 남아 있어 삼십 년의 준비를 정당화해 줄지도 몰랐다."], ["That prospect was made the sweeter by a flavor of vengeance against the hasty sneers of Carp & Company; for even when Mr. Casaubon was carrying his taper among the tombs of the past, those modern figures came athwart the dim light, and interrupted his diligent exploration.", "그 전망은 카프 일당의 성급한 비웃음에 대한 복수의 맛으로 더욱 달콤해졌으니, 캐소본 씨가 과거의 무덤들 사이로 촛불을 들고 다닐 때조차도 그 현대의 인물들이 희미한 불빛을 가로질러 다가와 그의 부지런한 탐구를 방해했기 때문이다."], ["To convince Carp of his mistake, so that he would have to eat his own words with a good deal of indigestion, would be an agreeable accident of triumphant authorship, which the prospect of living to future ages on earth and to all eternity in heaven could not exclude from contemplation.", "카프에게 그의 잘못을 깨닫게 하여 그가 적잖은 소화불량을 겪으며 자기 말을 도로 삼키게 만드는 것이야말로 의기양양한 저술이 가져다줄 유쾌한 부수입이 되리라. 지상에서 후대까지, 그리고 천국에서 영원토록 살리라는 전망조차도 그 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수는 없었다."], ["Since, thus, the prevision of his own unending bliss could not nullify the bitter savors of irritated jealousy and vindictiveness, it is the less surprising that the probability of a transient earthly bliss for other persons, when he himself should have entered into glory, had not a potently sweetening effect.", "이렇듯 자기 자신의 끝없는 지복에 대한 예견조차 격앙된 질투와 복수심의 쓴맛을 없애지 못했으니, 그 자신이 영광에 들어선 뒤에 다른 사람들이 잠시 누릴 지상의 행복의 가능성이 강력하게 달콤한 효과를 발휘하지 못했다는 것은 그리 놀라운 일도 아니다."], ["If the truth should be that some undermining disease was at work within him, there might be large opportunity for some people to be the happier when he was gone; and if one of those people should be Will Ladislaw, Mr.", "만약 어떤 좀먹는 질병이 그의 몸속에서 작용하고 있다는 것이 사실이라면, 그가 떠난 뒤에 어떤 이들이 더 행복해질 큰 기회가 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["Casaubon objected so strongly that it seemed as if the annoyance would make part of his disembodied existence.", "그리고 만약 그 이들 가운데 하나가 윌 래디슬로라면, 캐소본 씨가 어찌나 강하게 반대했던지 그 짜증이 그의 육신을 벗어난 존재의 일부가 되기라도 할 것 같았다."]], [["This is a very bare and therefore a very incomplete way of putting the case. The human soul moves in many channels, and Mr.", "이것은 사정을 매우 앙상하게, 따라서 매우 불완전하게 진술하는 방식이다."], ["Casaubon, we know, had a sense of rectitude and an honorable pride in satisfying the requirements of honor, which compelled him to find other reasons for his conduct than those of jealousy and vindictiveness. The way in which Mr.", "인간의 영혼은 수많은 물길로 움직이는바, 우리가 알다시피 캐소본 씨에게는 올바름에 대한 감각과 명예의 요구를 충족시키는 데서 오는 떳떳한 자존심이 있었고, 이것이 그로 하여금 자기 행동에 대해 질투와 복수심 이외의 다른 이유를 찾도록 강요했다."], ["Casaubon put the case was this:—“In marrying Dorothea Brooke I had to care for her well-being in case of my death.", "캐소본 씨가 사정을 진술한 방식은 이러했다. \"나는 도러시아 브룩과 결혼하면서 내가 죽을 경우에 대비해 그녀의 안녕을 돌봐야 했다."], ["But well-being is not to be secured by ample, independent possession of property; on the contrary, occasions might arise in which such possession might expose her to the more danger.", "그러나 안녕이란 넉넉하고 독립적인 재산의 소유로 보장되는 것이 아니다. 오히려 그런 소유가 그녀를 더 큰 위험에 노출시킬 경우가 생길지도 모른다."], ["She is ready prey to any man who knows how to play adroitly either on her affectionate ardor or her Quixotic enthusiasm; and a man stands by with that very intention in his mind—a man with no other principle than transient caprice, and who has a personal animosity towards me—I am sure of it—an animosity which is fed by the consciousness of his ingratitude, and which he has constantly vented in ridicule of which I am as well assured as if I had heard it.", "그녀는 그녀의 다정한 열정이나 돈키호테 같은 열의를 교묘하게 다룰 줄 아는 어떤 남자에게든 쉽사리 먹이가 되고 만다. 그런데 바로 그런 의도를 마음에 품고 곁에 버티고 선 남자가 있으니 — 일시적인 변덕 말고는 아무런 원칙도 없고, 나에게 개인적인 적의를 품은 남자 — 나는 확신한다 — 자기 배은망덕에 대한 의식으로 길러진 적의, 그가 끊임없이 조롱으로 터뜨려 온, 마치 내가 직접 들은 것처럼 확신하는 그 적의를."], ["Even if I live I shall not be without uneasiness as to what he may attempt through indirect influence.", "설령 내가 산다 해도, 그가 간접적인 영향력을 통해 무엇을 꾀할지에 대한 불안에서 벗어나지 못하리라."], ["This man has gained Dorothea’s ear: he has fascinated her attention; he has evidently tried to impress her mind with the notion that he has claims beyond anything I have done for him.", "이 남자는 도러시아의 귀를 사로잡았고, 그녀의 주의를 매혹했으며, 내가 그를 위해 해 준 그 무엇보다도 더 큰 권리를 자기가 지녔다는 관념을 그녀의 마음에 심으려고 분명히 애썼다. 내가 죽으면 — 그리고 그는 여기서 그것을 노리며 기다리고 있다 — 그는 그녀를 설득해 자기와 결혼하게 할 것이다."], ["If I die—and he is waiting here on the watch for that—he will persuade her to marry him.", "그것은 그녀에게는 재앙이요 그에게는 성공이리라. 그녀는 그것을 재앙으로 여기지 않을 것이다."], ["That would be calamity for her and success for him.", "그는 그녀로 하여금 무엇이든 믿게 만들 테니까."], ["She would not think it calamity: he would make her believe anything; she has a tendency to immoderate attachment which she inwardly reproaches me for not responding to, and already her mind is occupied with his fortunes.", "그녀에게는 내가 응답해 주지 않는다고 속으로 나를 책망하는 지나친 애착의 경향이 있고, 이미 그녀의 마음은 그의 처지로 가득 차 있다."], ["He thinks of an easy conquest and of entering into my nest.", "그는 손쉬운 정복과 내 둥지로 들어오는 것을 꿈꾼다."], ["That I will hinder!", "그것을 나는 막으리라!"], ["Such a marriage would be fatal to Dorothea.", "그런 결혼은 도러시아에게 치명적이리라."], ["Has he ever persisted in anything except from contradiction?", "그가 반발심에서가 아니고서 무언가를 끝까지 밀고 나간 적이 있던가?"], ["In knowledge he has always tried to be showy at small cost.", "지식에 있어 그는 늘 적은 밑천으로 화려하게 보이려 애써 왔다."], ["In religion he could be, as long as it suited him, the facile echo of Dorothea’s vagaries.", "종교에 있어 그는, 자기에게 유리한 동안에는, 도러시아의 변덕에 대한 손쉬운 메아리가 될 수 있었다."], ["When was sciolism ever dissociated from laxity?", "어쭙잖은 지식이 방종과 떨어진 적이 언제 있던가?"], ["I utterly distrust his morals, and it is my duty to hinder to the utmost the fulfilment of his designs.”", "나는 그의 도덕성을 전혀 믿지 못하며, 그의 계략의 실현을 온 힘을 다해 막는 것이 나의 의무다.\""]], [["The arrangements made by Mr. Casaubon on his marriage left strong measures open to him, but in ruminating on them his mind inevitably dwelt so much on the probabilities of his own life that the longing to get the nearest possible calculation had at last overcome his proud reticence, and had determined him to ask Lydgate’s opinion as to the nature of his illness.", "캐소본 씨가 결혼할 때 마련해 둔 조치들은 그에게 강력한 수단을 열어 두고 있었지만, 그것들을 곱씹는 동안 그의 마음은 어쩔 수 없이 자기 수명의 개연성에 너무나 많이 머물게 되어, 가능한 한 가장 정확한 계산을 얻고자 하는 갈망이 마침내 그의 자존심 어린 과묵함을 이기고 말았고, 리드게이트에게 자기 병의 성질에 대한 견해를 묻기로 결심하게 만들었다."]], [["He had mentioned to Dorothea that Lydgate was coming by appointment at half-past three, and in answer to her anxious question, whether he had felt ill, replied,—“No, I merely wish to have his opinion concerning some habitual symptoms.", "그는 도러시아에게 리드게이트가 약속대로 세 시 반에 올 것이라고 말했고, 어디 편찮으신 거냐고 묻는 그녀의 불안한 질문에 이렇게 대답했다. \"아니오."], ["You need not see him, my dear.", "그저 몇 가지 습관적인 증상에 대해 그의 의견을 듣고 싶을 뿐이오."], ["I shall give orders that he may be sent to me in the Yew-tree Walk, where I shall be taking my usual exercise.”", "당신은 그를 만날 필요가 없소, 여보. 내가 평소처럼 운동을 하고 있을 주목 산책로로 그를 보내라고 일러 두겠소.\""]], [["When Lydgate entered the Yew-tree Walk he saw Mr. Casaubon slowly receding with his hands behind him according to his habit, and his head bent forward.", "리드게이트가 주목 산책로에 들어섰을 때, 그는 캐소본 씨가 습관대로 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채 고개를 앞으로 숙이고 천천히 멀어져 가는 것을 보았다."], ["It was a lovely afternoon; the leaves from the lofty limes were falling silently across the sombre evergreens, while the lights and shadows slept side by side: there was no sound but the cawing of the rooks, which to the accustomed ear is a lullaby, or that last solemn lullaby, a dirge.", "사랑스러운 오후였다. 우뚝 솟은 보리수에서 잎들이 음울한 상록수를 가로질러 소리 없이 떨어지고 있었고, 빛과 그림자가 나란히 잠들어 있었다."], ["Lydgate, conscious of an energetic frame in its prime, felt some compassion when the figure which he was likely soon to overtake turned round, and in advancing towards him showed more markedly than ever the signs of premature age—the student’s bent shoulders, the emaciated limbs, and the melancholy lines of the mouth.", "떼까마귀의 울음소리 말고는 아무 소리도 없었으니, 익숙한 귀에는 그것이 자장가, 혹은 저 마지막 엄숙한 자장가인 만가와도 같았다. 한창때의 기운찬 신체를 의식하고 있던 리드게이트는 곧 따라잡을 듯한 그 인물이 돌아서서 자기 쪽으로 다가오며 그 어느 때보다도 뚜렷하게 노쇠의 징후를 — 학자의 굽은 어깨, 수척한 사지, 입가의 우울한 주름을 — 드러내는 것을 보고 다소간 연민을 느꼈다."], ["“Poor fellow,”", "\"가엾은 사람,\""], ["he thought, “some men with his years are like lions; one can tell nothing of their age except that they are full grown.”", "그는 생각했다. \"저 나이에 어떤 사람들은 사자 같아서, 다 자랐다는 것 말고는 나이를 짐작할 수 없는데.\""]], [["“Mr. Lydgate,”", "\"리드게이트 선생,\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, with his invariably polite air, “I am exceedingly obliged to you for your punctuality.", "캐소본 씨가 변함없이 정중한 태도로 말했다."], ["We will, if you please, carry on our conversation in walking to and fro.”", "\"시간을 정확히 지켜 주셔서 대단히 고맙습니다. 괜찮으시다면 거닐면서 이야기를 나누도록 합시다.\""]], [["“I hope your wish to see me is not due to the return of unpleasant symptoms,”", "\"저를 만나시려는 것이 불쾌한 증상이 다시 나타난 탓은 아니길 바랍니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, filling up a pause.", "리드게이트가 침묵을 메우며 말했다."]], [["“Not immediately—no.", "\"당장은 아니오 — 그렇소."], ["In order to account for that wish I must mention—what it were otherwise needless to refer to—that my life, on all collateral accounts insignificant, derives a possible importance from the incompleteness of labors which have extended through all its best years.", "그 바람을 설명하자면, 그렇지 않으면 굳이 언급할 필요가 없을 무언가를 말해야겠소 — 즉, 모든 부수적인 면에서는 하찮은 내 삶이, 그 가장 좋은 시절 전체에 걸쳐 이어 온 노작이 미완으로 남았기에 어쩌면 중요성을 띨 수도 있다는 것 말이오."], ["In short, I have long had on hand a work which I would fain leave behind me in such a state, at least, that it might be committed to the press by—others.", "요컨대 나는 오랫동안 어떤 저작에 매달려 왔는데, 적어도 다른 이들의 손으로 인쇄에 부쳐질 수 있을 만한 상태로는 남겨 두고 싶소."], ["Were I assured that this is the utmost I can reasonably expect, that assurance would be a useful circumscription of my attempts, and a guide in both the positive and negative determination of my course.”", "만약 이것이 내가 합리적으로 기대할 수 있는 최대치라는 확신이 든다면, 그 확신은 내 시도를 유용하게 한정해 줄 것이고, 내 진로의 적극적, 소극적 결정 모두에서 길잡이가 되어 줄 것이오.\""]], [["Here Mr. Casaubon paused, removed one hand from his back and thrust it between the buttons of his single-breasted coat.", "여기서 캐소본 씨는 말을 멈추고, 한 손을 등에서 떼어 외짝 단추가 달린 외투의 단추 사이에 찔러 넣었다."], ["To a mind largely instructed in the human destiny hardly anything could be more interesting than the inward conflict implied in his formal measured address, delivered with the usual sing-song and motion of the head.", "인간의 운명에 대해 풍부히 배운 정신에게는, 평소의 단조로운 어조와 고갯짓으로 전달된 그의 격식 차린 절제된 발언에 담긴 내면의 갈등만큼 흥미로운 것이 거의 없으리라."], ["Nay, are there many situations more sublimely tragic than the struggle of the soul with the demand to renounce a work which has been all the significance of its life—a significance which is to vanish as the waters which come and go where no man has need of them?", "아니, 그 영혼의 의의 전부였던 노작을 — 아무도 필요로 하지 않는 곳에서 오갔다 사라지는 물처럼 사라져 버릴 의의를 — 단념하라는 요구와 맞서는 영혼의 투쟁보다 더 숭고하게 비극적인 상황이 과연 많을까?"], ["But there was nothing to strike others as sublime about Mr. Casaubon, and Lydgate, who had some contempt at hand for futile scholarship, felt a little amusement mingling with his pity.", "그러나 캐소본 씨에게는 남들에게 숭고하게 비칠 만한 것이 전혀 없었고, 헛된 학식에 대해 어느 정도 경멸감을 품고 있던 리드게이트는 연민에 약간의 재미가 뒤섞이는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He was at present too ill acquainted with disaster to enter into the pathos of a lot where everything is below the level of tragedy except the passionate egoism of the sufferer.", "그는 당시 재난에 너무나 어두웠기에, 고통받는 자의 열정적인 자기중심성만 제외하고는 모든 것이 비극의 수준에 못 미치는 그런 처지의 비애 속으로 들어갈 수 없었다."]], [["“You refer to the possible hindrances from want of health?”", "\"건강 부족에서 비롯될 수 있는 지장을 말씀하시는 것입니까?\""], ["he said, wishing to help forward Mr. Casaubon’s purpose, which seemed to be clogged by some hesitation.", "그가 말했으니, 어떤 망설임에 막혀 있는 듯한 캐소본 씨의 목적을 거들고자 함이었다."]], [["“I do.", "\"그렇소."], ["You have not implied to me that the symptoms which—I am bound to testify—you watched with scrupulous care, were those of a fatal disease. But were it so, Mr.", "당신은 — 내가 증언하건대 — 세심한 주의를 기울여 관찰한 그 증상들이 치명적인 병의 증상이라고 내게 시사한 적이 없소."], ["Lydgate, I should desire to know the truth without reservation, and I appeal to you for an exact statement of your conclusions: I request it as a friendly service.", "그러나 만약 그렇다면, 리드게이트 선생, 나는 가감 없이 진실을 알고 싶고, 당신의 결론을 정확히 진술해 주기를 청하오. 우정 어린 봉사로서 그것을 요청하는 바요."], ["If you can tell me that my life is not threatened by anything else than ordinary casualties, I shall rejoice, on grounds which I have already indicated.", "만약 내 생명이 평범한 우연 외의 다른 무엇으로도 위협받지 않는다고 말해 줄 수 있다면, 나는 이미 밝힌 이유들로 기뻐할 것이오."], ["If not, knowledge of the truth is even more important to me.”", "만약 그렇지 않다면, 진실을 아는 것이 내게는 더욱 중요하오.\""]], [["“Then I can no longer hesitate as to my course,”", "\"그러시다면 저도 제가 취할 길을 더는 망설이지 않겠습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate; “but the first thing I must impress on you is that my conclusions are doubly uncertain—uncertain not only because of my fallibility, but because diseases of the heart are eminently difficult to found predictions on.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"그러나 제가 먼저 강조해 드려야 할 것은 제 결론이 이중으로 불확실하다는 점입니다 — 제가 오류를 범할 수 있다는 점 때문만이 아니라, 심장 질환이란 예측의 근거로 삼기가 지극히 어렵기 때문에도 불확실합니다."], ["In any case, one can hardly increase appreciably the tremendous uncertainty of life.”", "어쨌든 누구도 생명의 그 엄청난 불확실성을 눈에 띄게 더 키울 수는 없는 법입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Casaubon winced perceptibly, but bowed.", "캐소본 씨는 눈에 띄게 움찔했으나, 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“I believe that you are suffering from what is called fatty degeneration of the heart, a disease which was first divined and explored by Laennec, the man who gave us the stethoscope, not so very many years ago.", "\"제 생각에 선생께서는 이른바 심장의 지방변성을 앓고 계십니다. 이는 그리 오래되지 않은 옛날에 청진기를 우리에게 준 사람인 라에네크가 처음으로 알아내고 탐구한 질병입니다."], ["A good deal of experience—a more lengthened observation—is wanting on the subject.", "이 주제에 관해서는 상당한 경험이 — 더 오랜 관찰이 — 아직 부족합니다."], ["But after what you have said, it is my duty to tell you that death from this disease is often sudden.", "그러나 선생께서 말씀하신 바에 비추어 보면, 이 병으로 인한 죽음이 흔히 갑작스럽다는 것을 말씀드리는 것이 제 의무입니다."], ["At the same time, no such result can be predicted.", "동시에 그런 결과를 예측할 수는 없습니다."], ["Your condition may be consistent with a tolerably comfortable life for another fifteen years, or even more.", "선생의 상태로 앞으로 십오 년, 혹은 그 이상도 그런대로 편안한 삶을 사실 수 있을 것입니다."], ["I could add no information to this beyond anatomical or medical details, which would leave expectation at precisely the same point.”", "이에 덧붙일 수 있는 것은 해부학적, 의학적 세부 사항뿐인데, 그것은 예측을 정확히 같은 지점에 남겨 둘 따름입니다.\""], ["Lydgate’s instinct was fine enough to tell him that plain speech, quite free from ostentatious caution, would be felt by Mr. Casaubon as a tribute of respect.", "리드게이트의 본능은 충분히 섬세하여, 과시적인 신중함이 전혀 없는 솔직한 말이 캐소본 씨에게 경의의 표시로 느껴지리라는 것을 그에게 일러 주었다."]], [["“I thank you, Mr.", "\"고맙소, 리드게이트 선생.\""], ["Lydgate,” said Mr.", "캐소본 씨가 잠시 멈추었다가 말했다."], ["Casaubon, after a moment’s pause.", "\"한 가지 더 물어볼 것이 있소."], ["“One thing more I have still to ask: did you communicate what you have now told me to Mrs. Casaubon?”", "지금 내게 말한 것을 캐소본 부인에게 알렸소?\""]], [["“Partly—I mean, as to the possible issues.” Lydgate was going to explain why he had told Dorothea, but Mr.", "\"부분적으로 — 그러니까, 일어날 수 있는 결과에 대해서 말입니다.\""], ["Casaubon, with an unmistakable desire to end the conversation, waved his hand slightly, and said again, “I thank you,” proceeding to remark on the rare beauty of the day.", "리드게이트는 자기가 왜 도러시아에게 말했는지 설명하려 했으나, 캐소본 씨는 대화를 끝내고 싶다는 분명한 욕구로 가볍게 손을 내저으며 \"고맙소\"라고 다시 말하고는, 그날의 보기 드문 아름다움에 대해 한마디 하기 시작했다."]], [["Lydgate, certain that his patient wished to be alone, soon left him; and the black figure with hands behind and head bent forward continued to pace the walk where the dark yew-trees gave him a mute companionship in melancholy, and the little shadows of bird or leaf that fleeted across the isles of sunlight, stole along in silence as in the presence of a sorrow.", "자기 환자가 혼자 있기를 바란다는 것을 확신한 리드게이트는 곧 그를 떠났다. 그리고 두 손을 등 뒤로 하고 고개를 앞으로 숙인 그 검은 인물은 어두운 주목들이 우울 속에서 말없는 벗이 되어 주는 산책로를 계속 거닐었고, 햇빛의 섬들을 가로질러 스쳐 가는 새나 잎의 작은 그림자들이 마치 슬픔 앞에 놓인 듯 소리 없이 살그머니 지나갔다."], ["Here was a man who now for the first time found himself looking into the eyes of death—who was passing through one of those rare moments of experience when we feel the truth of a commonplace, which is as different from what we call knowing it, as the vision of waters upon the earth is different from the delirious vision of the water which cannot be had to cool the burning tongue.", "여기, 이제 처음으로 죽음의 눈을 들여다보게 된 한 사람이 있었다 — 우리가 어떤 진부한 진리를 그저 안다고 부르는 것과는 전혀 다르게, 그 진리를 느끼게 되는 드문 경험의 순간 가운데 하나를 지나가는 한 사람이 있었으니, 그것은 땅 위의 물을 바라보는 것이 타들어 가는 혀를 식힐 수 없는 물을 향한 헛된 환영과 다른 것만큼이나 다르다."], ["When the commonplace “We must all die” transforms itself suddenly into the acute consciousness “I must die—and soon,”", "\"우리는 모두 죽는다\"라는 진부한 말이 별안간 \"나는 죽는다 — 그것도 곧\"이라는 날카로운 의식으로 변할 때, 그제야 죽음이 우리를 움켜쥐고, 그 손가락은 잔인하다."], ["then death grapples us, and his fingers are cruel; afterwards, he may come to fold us in his arms as our mother did, and our last moment of dim earthly discerning may be like the first. To Mr.", "그 뒤에 죽음은 우리 어머니가 그러했듯 우리를 품에 안아 줄지도 모르고, 지상의 것을 어렴풋이 분별하는 우리의 마지막 순간은 그 첫 순간과도 같을지 모른다."], ["Casaubon now, it was as if he suddenly found himself on the dark river-brink and heard the plash of the oncoming oar, not discerning the forms, but expecting the summons.", "이제 캐소본 씨에게는, 자기가 별안간 어두운 강가에 서서 다가오는 노 젓는 소리를 듣되 그 형체는 분간하지 못한 채 부름을 기다리는 것만 같았다."], ["In such an hour the mind does not change its lifelong bias, but carries it onward in imagination to the other side of death, gazing backward—perhaps with the divine calm of beneficence, perhaps with the petty anxieties of self-assertion.", "그런 시간에 정신은 평생의 성향을 바꾸지 않고, 그것을 상상 속에서 죽음의 저편으로까지 이어 가, 뒤를 돌아본다 — 어쩌면 자비로움의 거룩한 평온함으로, 어쩌면 자기주장의 하찮은 불안으로."], ["What was Mr. Casaubon’s bias his acts will give us a clew to.", "캐소본 씨의 성향이 무엇이었는지는 그의 행위가 우리에게 실마리를 줄 것이다."], ["He held himself to be, with some private scholarly reservations, a believing Christian, as to estimates of the present and hopes of the future.", "그는 현재에 대한 평가와 미래에 대한 희망에 있어, 약간의 사사로운 학자적 유보를 두면서, 자기가 신앙심 깊은 기독교인이라고 자처했다."], ["But what we strive to gratify, though we may call it a distant hope, is an immediate desire: the future estate for which men drudge up city alleys exists already in their imagination and love.", "그러나 우리가 충족시키려 애쓰는 것은, 비록 그것을 먼 희망이라 부를지라도, 당장의 욕망이다. 사람들이 도시의 뒷골목을 헤매며 애써 마련하려는 미래의 재산은 이미 그들의 상상과 사랑 속에 존재한다."], ["And Mr. Casaubon’s immediate desire was not for divine communion and light divested of earthly conditions; his passionate longings, poor man, clung low and mist-like in very shady places.", "그리고 캐소본 씨의 당장의 욕망은 지상의 조건을 벗어난 신과의 교감과 빛이 아니었으니, 그 가엾은 사람의 열정적인 갈망은 매우 그늘진 곳에 안개처럼 낮게 들러붙어 있었다."]], [["Dorothea had been aware when Lydgate had ridden away, and she had stepped into the garden, with the impulse to go at once to her husband.", "도러시아는 리드게이트가 말을 타고 떠났을 때 그것을 알아채고, 곧장 남편에게 가려는 충동에 정원으로 나섰다. 그러나 그녀는 자기를 들이밀어 그를 언짢게 할까 두려워 망설였다."], ["But she hesitated, fearing to offend him by obtruding herself; for her ardor, continually repulsed, served, with her intense memory, to heighten her dread, as thwarted energy subsides into a shudder; and she wandered slowly round the nearer clumps of trees until she saw him advancing.", "끊임없이 거부당한 그녀의 열정이, 그녀의 강렬한 기억과 더불어, 마치 좌절된 기운이 전율로 가라앉듯 그녀의 두려움을 한층 높였기 때문이다. 그래서 그녀는 가까운 나무 무리 사이를 천천히 거닐다가, 마침내 그가 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["Then she went towards him, and might have represented a heaven-sent angel coming with a promise that the short hours remaining should yet be filled with that faithful love which clings the closer to a comprehended grief.", "그러자 그녀는 그를 향해 나아갔으니, 그 모습은 남은 짧은 시간이나마 이해받은 슬픔에 더욱 가까이 매달리는 그 충실한 사랑으로 채워지리라는 약속을 들고 오는, 하늘이 보낸 천사를 떠올리게 했을지도 모른다."], ["His glance in reply to hers was so chill that she felt her timidity increased; yet she turned and passed her hand through his arm.", "그녀의 시선에 답하는 그의 눈길이 너무나 차가워서 그녀는 소심함이 더해지는 것을 느꼈다. 그래도 그녀는 몸을 돌려 그의 팔에 손을 끼웠다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon kept his hands behind him and allowed her pliant arm to cling with difficulty against his rigid arm.", "캐소본 씨는 두 손을 등 뒤로 한 채로, 그녀의 유순한 팔이 자기의 뻣뻣한 팔에 힘겹게 매달리도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["There was something horrible to Dorothea in the sensation which this unresponsive hardness inflicted on her.", "이 무반응한 단단함이 도러시아에게 안긴 그 느낌에는 무언가 끔찍한 데가 있었다."], ["That is a strong word, but not too strong: it is in these acts called trivialities that the seeds of joy are forever wasted, until men and women look round with haggard faces at the devastation their own waste has made, and say, the earth bears no harvest of sweetness—calling their denial knowledge.", "강한 말이지만, 지나친 말은 아니다. 사람들이 사소하다고 부르는 바로 이런 행위들 속에서 기쁨의 씨앗이 영영 허비되고 마니, 마침내 남녀는 자기들이 빚어낸 황폐를 초췌한 얼굴로 둘러보며 대지가 달콤함의 수확을 맺지 못한다고 말하면서 — 그 부정을 두고 깨달음이라 부르는 것이다."], ["You may ask why, in the name of manliness, Mr. Casaubon should have behaved in that way.", "어째서 사내답게 굴려면, 캐소본 씨가 그런 식으로 행동했어야 하느냐고 물을지도 모른다."], ["Consider that his was a mind which shrank from pity: have you ever watched in such a mind the effect of a suspicion that what is pressing it as a grief may be really a source of contentment, either actual or future, to the being who already offends by pitying?", "그의 정신이 동정을 꺼리는 정신이었음을 생각해 보라. 그런 정신 속에서, 자기를 슬픔으로 짓누르는 것이 사실은 이미 동정함으로써 거슬리게 하는 그 존재에게 현재든 미래든 만족의 원천일지도 모른다는 의심이 미치는 영향을 지켜본 적이 있는가?"], ["Besides, he knew little of Dorothea’s sensations, and had not reflected that on such an occasion as the present they were comparable in strength to his own sensibilities about Carp’s criticisms.", "게다가 그는 도러시아의 감정에 대해 거의 알지 못했고, 지금 같은 경우에 그 감정이 카프의 비판에 대한 자기 자신의 예민함과 맞먹는 강도라는 것을 헤아려 보지도 못했다."]], [["Dorothea did not withdraw her arm, but she could not venture to speak. Mr.", "도러시아는 팔을 빼지 않았지만, 감히 말을 꺼낼 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["Casaubon did not say, “I wish to be alone,” but he directed his steps in silence towards the house, and as they entered by the glass door on this eastern side, Dorothea withdrew her arm and lingered on the matting, that she might leave her husband quite free.", "캐소본 씨는 \"혼자 있고 싶소\"라고 말하지는 않았으나, 말없이 집을 향해 걸음을 옮겼고, 두 사람이 이 동쪽 면의 유리문으로 들어설 때, 도러시아는 남편을 완전히 자유롭게 해 주려고 팔을 빼고 깔개 위에서 머뭇거렸다."], ["He entered the library and shut himself in, alone with his sorrow.", "그는 서재로 들어가 문을 닫고, 자기 슬픔과 더불어 홀로 있었다."]], [["She went up to her boudoir.", "그녀는 자기 규방으로 올라갔다."], ["The open bow-window let in the serene glory of the afternoon lying in the avenue, where the lime-trees cast long shadows.", "활짝 열린 곡선형 창으로 오후의 고요한 영광이 들어왔으니, 보리수가 긴 그림자를 드리운 진입로 위에 그 영광이 깃들어 있었다."], ["But Dorothea knew nothing of the scene.", "그러나 도러시아는 그 풍경을 전혀 알지 못했다."], ["She threw herself on a chair, not heeding that she was in the dazzling sun-rays: if there were discomfort in that, how could she tell that it was not part of her inward misery?", "그녀는 자기가 눈부신 햇살 속에 있다는 것도 개의치 않고 의자에 몸을 던졌다. 거기에 불편함이 있다 한들, 그것이 자기 내면의 비참함의 일부가 아니라고 어떻게 알 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["She was in the reaction of a rebellious anger stronger than any she had felt since her marriage.", "그녀는 결혼한 이래 느꼈던 그 어떤 분노보다도 강한, 반항적인 분노의 반작용에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Instead of tears there came words:—", "눈물 대신 말이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“What have I done—what am I—that he should treat me so?", "\"내가 무엇을 했기에 — 내가 어떤 사람이기에 — 그가 나를 이렇게 대한단 말인가?"], ["He never knows what is in my mind—he never cares.", "그는 내 마음속에 무엇이 있는지 결코 모르고 — 결코 신경 쓰지 않아."], ["What is the use of anything I do?", "내가 하는 일이 다 무슨 소용이람?"], ["He wishes he had never married me.”", "그는 나와 결혼하지 않았기를 바라고 있어.\""]], [["She began to hear herself, and was checked into stillness.", "그녀는 자기 말을 듣기 시작했고, 그 소리에 제지당해 잠잠해졌다."], ["Like one who has lost his way and is weary, she sat and saw as in one glance all the paths of her young hope which she should never find again.", "길을 잃고 지친 사람처럼, 그녀는 앉아서 다시는 찾지 못할 자기 젊은 희망의 온갖 길들을 단번에 보는 듯했다."], ["And just as clearly in the miserable light she saw her own and her husband’s solitude—how they walked apart so that she was obliged to survey him.", "그리고 그 비참한 빛 속에서, 그녀는 자기 자신과 남편의 고독을 — 두 사람이 따로 떨어져 걸었기에 그녀가 어쩔 수 없이 그를 살펴봐야 했음을 — 마찬가지로 똑똑히 보았다."], ["If he had drawn her towards him, she would never have surveyed him—never have said, “Is he worth living for?”", "만약 그가 그녀를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겼더라면, 그녀는 결코 그를 살펴보지도 — 결코 \"그가 위해서 살 만한 가치가 있는 사람인가?\"라고 묻지도 않았을 것이고, 그저 그를 자기 삶의 일부로 느꼈으리라."], ["but would have felt him simply a part of her own life.", "이제 그녀는 쓰라리게 말했다. \"이건 내 잘못이 아니라 그의 잘못이야.\""], ["Now she said bitterly, “It is his fault, not mine.”", "그녀의 온 존재가 진동하는 가운데 연민이 무너져 내렸다."], ["In the jar of her whole being, Pity was overthrown.", "그를 믿었던 것이 — 그의 가치를 믿었던 것이 그녀의 잘못이었던가?"], ["Was it her fault that she had believed in him—had believed in his worthiness?—And what, exactly, was he?— She was able enough to estimate him—she who waited on his glances with trembling, and shut her best soul in prison, paying it only hidden visits, that she might be petty enough to please him.", "그리고 그는 정확히 어떤 사람인가? 그녀는 그를 평가할 만한 능력이 충분히 있었으니 — 떨면서 그의 눈길을 기다리고, 그를 기쁘게 하기에 충분히 하찮아지려고 자기의 가장 좋은 영혼을 감옥에 가두어 둔 채 그곳에 은밀한 방문만을 하던 그녀가 말이다."], ["In such a crisis as this, some women begin to hate.", "이런 위기에서 어떤 여자들은 미워하기 시작한다."]], [["The sun was low when Dorothea was thinking that she would not go down again, but would send a message to her husband saying that she was not well and preferred remaining up-stairs.", "도러시아가 다시 내려가지 않고 남편에게 몸이 좋지 않아 위층에 머물고 싶다는 전갈을 보내야겠다고 생각했을 때, 해는 낮게 기울어 있었다."], ["She had never deliberately allowed her resentment to govern her in this way before, but she believed now that she could not see him again without telling him the truth about her feeling, and she must wait till she could do it without interruption.", "그녀는 이전에는 결코 자기 원한이 이런 식으로 자기를 지배하도록 일부러 내버려 둔 적이 없었으나, 이제 자기 감정에 대한 진실을 그에게 말하지 않고서는 그를 다시 볼 수 없으리라고 믿었고, 방해받지 않고 그렇게 할 수 있을 때까지 기다려야 했다."], ["He might wonder and be hurt at her message.", "그가 자기 전갈에 의아해하고 상처받을지도 몰랐다."], ["It was good that he should wonder and be hurt.", "그가 의아해하고 상처받는 것은 잘된 일이었다."], ["Her anger said, as anger is apt to say, that God was with her—that all heaven, though it were crowded with spirits watching them, must be on her side.", "그녀의 분노는, 분노가 곧잘 그러하듯, 신이 그녀의 편이라고 — 비록 두 사람을 지켜보는 영들로 가득 차 있을지언정 온 천국이 그녀 편일 수밖에 없다고 말했다."], ["She had determined to ring her bell, when there came a rap at the door.", "그녀가 종을 울리기로 마음먹었을 때, 문에서 노크 소리가 났다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon had sent to say that he would have his dinner in the library.", "캐소본 씨가 사람을 보내어, 저녁은 서재에서 들겠다고 전했다."], ["He wished to be quite alone this evening, being much occupied.", "그는 할 일이 많아 오늘 저녁은 아주 혼자 있고 싶다는 것이었다."]], [["“I shall not dine, then, Tantripp.”", "\"그러면 나는 저녁을 들지 않겠다, 탠트립.\""]], [["“Oh, madam, let me bring you a little something?”", "\"오, 마님, 조금이라도 뭘 좀 가져다드릴까요?\""]], [["“No; I am not well.", "\"아니야. 몸이 좋지 않구나."], ["Get everything ready in my dressing room, but pray do not disturb me again.”", "화장실에 모든 것을 준비해 두되, 다시는 나를 방해하지 말아 다오.\""]], [["Dorothea sat almost motionless in her meditative struggle, while the evening slowly deepened into night.", "도러시아는 명상에 잠긴 채 거의 미동도 없이 앉아 있었고, 그동안 저녁은 천천히 짙어져 밤이 되었다."], ["But the struggle changed continually, as that of a man who begins with a movement towards striking and ends with conquering his desire to strike.", "그러나 그 투쟁은 끊임없이 변해 갔으니, 마치 처음에는 내리치려는 움직임으로 시작했다가 결국 내리치고 싶은 욕망을 이겨 내고 끝맺는 사람의 투쟁과도 같았다."], ["The energy that would animate a crime is not more than is wanted to inspire a resolved submission, when the noble habit of the soul reasserts itself.", "범죄에 생기를 불어넣을 기운은, 영혼의 고결한 습성이 다시 제 모습을 되찾을 때 결연한 순종을 불어넣는 데 필요한 기운보다 더 큰 것이 아니다."], ["That thought with which Dorothea had gone out to meet her husband—her conviction that he had been asking about the possible arrest of all his work, and that the answer must have wrung his heart, could not be long without rising beside the image of him, like a shadowy monitor looking at her anger with sad remonstrance.", "도러시아가 남편을 만나러 나갈 때 품었던 그 생각 — 그가 자기 모든 노작이 중단될 가능성에 대해 물었으리라는 확신, 그리고 그 대답이 그의 가슴을 쥐어짰으리라는 확신은, 마치 슬픈 책망의 눈길로 그녀의 분노를 바라보는 어렴풋한 감독자처럼, 그의 모습 곁에 떠오르지 않을 수 없었다."], ["It cost her a litany of pictured sorrows and of silent cries that she might be the mercy for those sorrows—but the resolved submission did come; and when the house was still, and she knew that it was near the time when Mr.", "그녀가 그 슬픔들을 위한 자비가 될 수 있도록, 그것은 그녀에게 그려진 슬픔들과 소리 없는 부르짖음의 연속된 기도를 치르게 했다 — 그러나 결연한 순종은 끝내 찾아왔다."], ["Casaubon habitually went to rest, she opened her door gently and stood outside in the darkness waiting for his coming up-stairs with a light in his hand.", "그리고 집이 고요해지고, 캐소본 씨가 습관적으로 잠자리에 드는 시각이 가까워졌음을 알았을 때, 그녀는 가만히 문을 열고 어둠 속에서 밖에 서서, 손에 등불을 들고 위층으로 올라올 그를 기다렸다."], ["If he did not come soon she thought that she would go down and even risk incurring another pang.", "그가 곧 오지 않으면 그녀는 아래로 내려가, 또 한 번의 아픔을 무릅쓰는 한이 있더라도 그렇게 하리라 생각했다."], ["She would never again expect anything else.", "그녀는 다시는 다른 무엇도 기대하지 않으리라."], ["But she did hear the library door open, and slowly the light advanced up the staircase without noise from the footsteps on the carpet.", "그러나 그녀는 정말로 서재 문이 열리는 소리를 들었고, 카펫 위의 발소리도 없이 등불이 천천히 계단을 올라왔다."], ["When her husband stood opposite to her, she saw that his face was more haggard.", "남편이 그녀의 맞은편에 섰을 때, 그녀는 그의 얼굴이 더욱 초췌해진 것을 보았다."], ["He started slightly on seeing her, and she looked up at him beseechingly, without speaking.", "그는 그녀를 보고 살짝 움찔했고, 그녀는 말없이 애원하듯 그를 올려다보았다."]], [["“Dorothea!”", "\"도러시아!\""], ["he said, with a gentle surprise in his tone.", "그가 다정하게 놀란 어조로 말했다."], ["“Were you waiting for me?”", "\"나를 기다리고 있었소?\""]], [["“Yes, I did not like to disturb you.”", "\"네, 당신을 방해하고 싶지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Come, my dear, come.", "\"이리 와요, 여보, 이리 와요."], ["You are young, and need not to extend your life by watching.”", "당신은 젊으니, 깨어 지켜보느라 수명을 늘릴 필요는 없소.\""]], [["When the kind quiet melancholy of that speech fell on Dorothea’s ears, she felt something like the thankfulness that might well up in us if we had narrowly escaped hurting a lamed creature.", "그 다정하고 차분한 우수가 깃든 말이 도러시아의 귀에 와닿았을 때, 그녀는 절름거리는 짐승을 다치게 할 뻔하다가 간신히 모면했을 때 우리 안에서 솟구칠 법한 그런 감사와도 같은 무언가를 느꼈다."], ["She put her hand into her husband’s, and they went along the broad corridor together.", "그녀는 남편의 손에 자기 손을 얹었고, 두 사람은 함께 넓은 복도를 따라 걸어갔다."]], [["# BOOK V. THE DEAD HAND. CHAPTER XLIII.", "# BOOK V. THE DEAD HAND. CHAPTER XLIII."]], [["“This figure hath high price: ’t was wrought with love Ages ago in finest ivory; Nought modish in it, pure and noble lines Of generous womanhood that fits all time That too is costly ware; majolica Of deft design, to please a lordly eye: The smile, you see, is perfect—wonderful As mere Faience!", "너그러운 여성성의 순수하고 고귀한 선이라."], ["a table ornament To suit the richest mounting.”", "저것 또한 값진 물건, 솜씨 좋게 빚은"]], [["Dorothea seldom left home without her husband, but she did occasionally drive into Middlemarch alone, on little errands of shopping or charity such as occur to every lady of any wealth when she lives within three miles of a town. Two days after that scene in the Yew-tree Walk, she determined to use such an opportunity in order if possible to see Lydgate, and learn from him whether her husband had really felt any depressing change of symptoms which he was concealing from her, and whether he had insisted on knowing the utmost about himself. She felt almost guilty in asking for knowledge about him from another, but the dread of being without it—the dread of that ignorance which would make her unjust or hard—overcame every scruple. That there had been some crisis in her husband’s mind she was certain: he had the very next day begun a new method of arranging his notes, and had associated her quite newly in carrying out his plan. Poor Dorothea needed to lay up stores of patience.", "마욜리카 도자기, 고귀한 눈을 즐겁게 하려는 것. 보다시피 그 미소는 완벽하다, 한낱"]], [["It was about four o’clock when she drove to Lydgate’s house in Lowick Gate, wishing, in her immediate doubt of finding him at home, that she had written beforehand.", "채색 도기의 솜씨로는 경이로울 만큼!"], ["And he was not at home.", "가장 호화로운\n장식대에 어울릴 식탁의 장식품이라.”"]], [["“Is Mrs.", "도러시아는 좀처럼 남편 없이 집을 나서지 않았지만, 작은 쇼핑이나 자선 같은 용무로 이따금 혼자 미들마치까지 마차를 타고 가곤 했다."], ["Lydgate at home?”", "도시에서 삼 마일 이내에 사는, 다소의 재산을 지닌 부인이라면 누구에게나 으레 생기는 일이었다."], ["said Dorothea, who had never, that she knew of, seen Rosamond, but now remembered the fact of the marriage.", "주목 산책로에서의 그 장면이 있은 지 이틀 후, 그녀는 그런 기회를 이용해 가능하다면 리드게이트를 만나, 남편이 정말로 자신에게 숨기고 있는 증상의 어떤 우울한 변화를 느낀 적이 있는지, 그리고 자기 자신에 관해 최악의 것까지 알기를 고집했는지 그에게서 알아내기로 결심했다. 다른 사람에게 남편에 대한 정보를 구하는 것이 거의 죄스럽게 느껴졌지만, 그것을 모른 채 지내는 데 대한 두려움—자신을 부당하거나 모질게 만들지도 모를 그 무지에 대한 두려움이 모든 거리낌을 이겨 냈다."], ["Yes, Mrs.", "남편의 마음속에 어떤 위기가 있었다는 것만은 확신했다."], ["Lydgate was at home.", "바로 그 이튿날 그는 노트를 정리하는 새로운 방식을 시작했고, 자신의 계획을 수행하는 데 그녀를 아주 새롭게 끌어들였던 것이다. 가엾은 도러시아는 인내심을 비축해 둘 필요가 있었다."]], [["“I will go in and speak to her, if she will allow me.", "그녀가 로익 게이트에 있는 리드게이트의 집으로 마차를 몰고 간 것은 네 시쯤이었다."], ["Will you ask her if she can see me—see Mrs.", "그가 집에 있을지 당장 의심스러워, 미리 편지를 써 둘 걸 그랬다고 생각했다."], ["Casaubon, for a few minutes?”", "그리고 그는 집에 없었다."]], [["When the servant had gone to deliver that message, Dorothea could hear sounds of music through an open window—a few notes from a man’s voice and then a piano bursting into roulades.", "“리드게이트 부인은 계신가요?” 도러시아가 물었다."], ["But the roulades broke off suddenly, and then the servant came back saying that Mrs.", "그녀는 자신이 아는 한 로저먼드를 본 적이 없었으나, 이제야 그 결혼 사실이 떠올랐다. 그렇다고, 리드게이트 부인은 집에 있었다."], ["Lydgate would be happy to see Mrs.", "“허락해 주신다면 들어가 말씀을 나누고 싶군요."], ["Casaubon.", "잠시 저를—케소번 부인을 만나 주실 수 있는지 여쭤봐 주시겠어요?”"]], [["When the drawing-room door opened and Dorothea entered, there was a sort of contrast not infrequent in country life when the habits of the different ranks were less blent than now.", "하인이 그 전갈을 전하러 가자, 도러시아는 열린 창문을 통해 음악 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 남자 목소리로 부른 몇 소절에 이어 피아노가 빠른 장식음을 쏟아 냈다."], ["Let those who know, tell us exactly what stuff it was that Dorothea wore in those days of mild autumn—that thin white woollen stuff soft to the touch and soft to the eye.", "그러나 그 장식음은 돌연 멈췄고, 곧 하인이 돌아와 리드게이트 부인이 케소번 부인을 기꺼이 만나겠다고 한다고 전했다. 응접실 문이 열리고 도러시아가 들어서자, 신분이 다른 계층의 습관이 지금처럼 섞이지 않았던 시골 생활에서 드물지 않게 볼 수 있는 일종의 대조가 빚어졌다."], ["It always seemed to have been lately washed, and to smell of the sweet hedges—was always in the shape of a pelisse with sleeves hanging all out of the fashion.", "그 온화한 가을날에 도러시아가 입었던 옷이 정확히 어떤 천이었는지는, 아는 사람이 있다면 우리에게 말해 주기를 바란다—촉감도 부드럽고 눈에도 부드러운 그 얇은 흰 모직 천 말이다."], ["Yet if she had entered before a still audience as Imogene or Cato’s daughter, the dress might have seemed right enough: the grace and dignity were in her limbs and neck; and about her simply parted hair and candid eyes the large round poke which was then in the fate of women, seemed no more odd as a head-dress than the gold trencher we call a halo.", "그 옷은 늘 막 세탁한 것처럼 보였고 향긋한 산울타리 냄새를 풍기는 듯했으며, 언제나 유행에서 완전히 벗어난 소매가 늘어진 외투 모양이었다. 그러나 만약 그녀가 이모진이나 카토의 딸로 분장하여 조용히 지켜보는 관객 앞에 등장했더라면, 그 옷차림이 충분히 어울려 보였을 것이다."], ["By the present audience of two persons, no dramatic heroine could have been expected with more interest than Mrs. Casaubon.", "우아함과 기품이 그녀의 사지와 목에 깃들어 있었기 때문이다."], ["To Rosamond she was one of those county divinities not mixing with Middlemarch mortality, whose slightest marks of manner or appearance were worthy of her study; moreover, Rosamond was not without satisfaction that Mrs.", "그리고 단정하게 가른 머리와 솔직한 눈 둘레에, 당시 여성들의 운명이었던 커다랗고 둥근 모자 챙은, 우리가 후광이라 부르는 황금빛 쟁반보다 더 이상해 보이지 않는 머리 장식이었다. 지금 두 사람으로 이루어진 관객에게, 케소번 부인보다 더 큰 관심을 기울일 만한 극중 여주인공은 없었을 것이다."], ["Casaubon should have an opportunity of studying her. What is the use of being exquisite if you are not seen by the best judges?", "로저먼드에게 그녀는 미들마치의 속세와 어울리지 않는 그 시골의 여신 같은 존재 중 하나였으니, 그 태도나 외모의 사소한 특징조차 연구할 가치가 있는 대상이었다."], ["and since Rosamond had received the highest compliments at Sir Godwin Lydgate’s, she felt quite confident of the impression she must make on people of good birth.", "게다가 로저먼드는 케소번 부인이 자기를 연구할 기회를 갖게 된 것에 만족스럽지 않은 것도 아니었다. 가장 안목 있는 사람에게 보여지지 못한다면 우아하게 가꾼들 무슨 소용이겠는가?"], ["Dorothea put out her hand with her usual simple kindness, and looked admiringly at Lydgate’s lovely bride—aware that there was a gentleman standing at a distance, but seeing him merely as a coated figure at a wide angle.", "또 로저먼드는 고드윈 리드게이트 경의 댁에서 최고의 찬사를 받았던 터라, 좋은 가문의 사람들에게 자신이 줄 인상에 대해 자못 자신만만했다. 도러시아는 늘 그렇듯 소박한 친절을 담아 손을 내밀며, 리드게이트의 사랑스러운 신부를 감탄하듯 바라보았다—멀리 신사 한 사람이 서 있다는 것은 알아챘으나, 그저 넓은 각도로 떨어진 외투 입은 형상으로만 보았다."], ["The gentleman was too much occupied with the presence of the one woman to reflect on the contrast between the two—a contrast that would certainly have been striking to a calm observer.", "그 신사는 한 여인의 존재에 너무도 사로잡혀 두 여인 사이의 대조—차분한 관찰자라면 분명 인상적으로 느꼈을 그 대조—를 헤아릴 겨를이 없었다. 두 사람 다 키가 컸고 눈높이가 같았다."], ["They were both tall, and their eyes were on a level; but imagine Rosamond’s infantine blondness and wondrous crown of hair-plaits, with her pale-blue dress of a fit and fashion so perfect that no dressmaker could look at it without emotion, a large embroidered collar which it was to be hoped all beholders would know the price of, her small hands duly set off with rings, and that controlled self-consciousness of manner which is the expensive substitute for simplicity.", "그러나 로저먼드의 아기 같은 금발과 경탄스러운 머리 땋은 관을 상상해 보라. 어떤 양재사라도 감동 없이는 볼 수 없을 만큼 완벽하게 재단되고 멋스러운 연푸른 드레스를 입었고, 모든 구경꾼이 그 값을 알아주기를 바랄 만한 커다란 자수 깃을 둘렀으며, 작은 손은 반지로 한껏 돋보이게 했고, 소박함을 대신하는 값비싼 대용품인 그 절제된 자의식이 몸에 배어 있었다."]], [["“Thank you very much for allowing me to interrupt you,”", "“방해해도 좋다고 허락해 주셔서 정말 감사합니다.”"], ["said Dorothea, immediately.", "도러시아가 곧바로 말했다."], ["“I am anxious to see Mr. Lydgate, if possible, before I go home, and I hoped that you might possibly tell me where I could find him, or even allow me to wait for him, if you expect him soon.”", "“집에 돌아가기 전에 가능하면 리드게이트 씨를 꼭 만나 뵙고 싶은데, 혹시 어디서 그분을 찾을 수 있을지 알려 주시거나, 곧 오실 것 같으면 기다려도 좋을지 여쭤보고 싶었어요.”"]], [["“He is at the New Hospital,”", "“그이는 새 병원에 있어요.”"], ["said Rosamond; “I am not sure how soon he will come home.", "로저먼드가 말했다. “언제 돌아올지는 잘 모르겠어요."], ["But I can send for him.”", "하지만 사람을 보내 부를 수 있어요.”"]], [["“Will you let me go and fetch him?”", "“제가 가서 모셔 올까요?”"], ["said Will Ladislaw, coming forward.", "윌 라디슬로가 앞으로 나서며 말했다."], ["He had already taken up his hat before Dorothea entered.", "그는 도러시아가 들어오기 전에 이미 모자를 집어 든 상태였다."], ["She colored with surprise, but put out her hand with a smile of unmistakable pleasure, saying—", "그녀는 놀라서 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 틀림없는 기쁨의 미소를 띠며 손을 내밀고 말했다—"]], [["“I did not know it was you: I had no thought of seeing you here.”", "“당신인 줄 몰랐어요. 여기서 만나리라고는 생각도 못 했어요.”"]], [["“May I go to the Hospital and tell Mr. Lydgate that you wish to see him?”", "“병원에 가서 리드게이트 씨에게 당신이 만나고 싶어 한다고 전할까요?”"], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."]], [["“It would be quicker to send the carriage for him,”", "“마차를 보내 그분을 모셔 오는 게 더 빠를 거예요.”"], ["said Dorothea, “if you will be kind enough to give the message to the coachman.”", "도러시아가 말했다. “수고스럽겠지만 마부에게 전갈을 전해 주신다면요.”"]], [["Will was moving to the door when Dorothea, whose mind had flashed in an instant over many connected memories, turned quickly and said, “I will go myself, thank you.", "윌이 문 쪽으로 움직일 때, 순간적으로 여러 연결된 기억들이 머릿속을 스쳐 간 도러시아가 재빨리 돌아서며 말했다. “제가 직접 가겠어요, 고마워요."], ["I wish to lose no time before getting home again.", "다시 집에 돌아가기 전에 시간을 허비하고 싶지 않아요."], ["I will drive to the Hospital and see Mr. Lydgate there.", "병원으로 마차를 몰고 가서 거기서 리드게이트 씨를 뵙겠어요."], ["Pray excuse me, Mrs. Lydgate.", "죄송해요, 리드게이트 부인."], ["I am very much obliged to you.”", "정말 감사합니다.”"]], [["Her mind was evidently arrested by some sudden thought, and she left the room hardly conscious of what was immediately around her—hardly conscious that Will opened the door for her and offered her his arm to lead her to the carriage.", "그녀의 마음은 어떤 갑작스러운 생각에 분명히 사로잡혀 있었고, 그녀는 바로 주위에서 일어나는 일을 거의 의식하지 못한 채 방을 나섰다—윌이 그녀를 위해 문을 열어 주고 마차까지 이끌려고 팔을 내밀었다는 것조차 거의 의식하지 못했다."], ["She took the arm but said nothing.", "그녀는 팔을 잡았으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Will was feeling rather vexed and miserable, and found nothing to say on his side.", "윌은 다소 언짢고 비참한 기분이 들어, 자신도 할 말을 찾지 못했다."], ["He handed her into the carriage in silence, they said good-by, and Dorothea drove away.", "그는 말없이 그녀를 마차에 태웠고, 두 사람은 작별 인사를 했으며, 도러시아는 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["In the five minutes’ drive to the Hospital she had time for some reflections that were quite new to her.", "병원까지 가는 오 분의 마차 길에서 그녀는 자신에게 아주 새로운 몇 가지 생각을 할 시간이 있었다."], ["Her decision to go, and her preoccupation in leaving the room, had come from the sudden sense that there would be a sort of deception in her voluntarily allowing any further intercourse between herself and Will which she was unable to mention to her husband, and already her errand in seeking Lydgate was a matter of concealment.", "가겠다는 결심과 방을 나서며 골몰했던 마음은, 남편에게 말할 수 없는 자신과 윌 사이의 어떤 추가적인 교류를 스스로 허용하는 데 일종의 기만이 있으리라는 갑작스러운 깨달음에서 비롯된 것이었고, 이미 리드게이트를 찾아 나선 그녀의 용무 자체가 숨겨야 할 일이었다."], ["That was all that had been explicitly in her mind; but she had been urged also by a vague discomfort.", "마음속에 분명하게 떠오른 것은 그것뿐이었다. 그러나 어떤 막연한 불편함도 그녀를 부추겼다."], ["Now that she was alone in her drive, she heard the notes of the man’s voice and the accompanying piano, which she had not noted much at the time, returning on her inward sense; and she found herself thinking with some wonder that Will Ladislaw was passing his time with Mrs.", "이제 마차 안에 홀로 있게 되자, 그때는 별로 주의를 기울이지 않았던 그 남자의 목소리와 함께한 피아노 소리가 내면의 감각으로 되살아났다. 그리고 그녀는 남편이 없는 사이에 윌 라디슬로가 리드게이트 부인과 함께 시간을 보내고 있었다는 사실에 다소 의아한 마음으로 생각에 잠긴 자신을 발견했다."], ["Lydgate in her husband’s absence. And then she could not help remembering that he had passed some time with her under like circumstances, so why should there be any unfitness in the fact?", "그러다 그가 비슷한 상황에서 자신과도 얼마간 시간을 보냈던 일을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으니, 그렇다면 그 사실에 무슨 부적절함이 있겠는가?"], ["But Will was Mr. Casaubon’s relative, and one towards whom she was bound to show kindness.", "그러나 윌은 케소번 씨의 친척이었고, 그녀가 친절을 베풀어야 할 사람이었다."], ["Still there had been signs which perhaps she ought to have understood as implying that Mr. Casaubon did not like his cousin’s visits during his own absence.", "그래도 어쩌면 케소번 씨가 자신이 없는 동안 사촌이 찾아오는 것을 좋아하지 않는다는 뜻으로 이해했어야 할 징후들이 있었다."], ["“Perhaps I have been mistaken in many things,”", "“어쩌면 나는 여러 가지를 잘못 생각해 왔는지도 몰라.”"], ["said poor Dorothea to herself, while the tears came rolling and she had to dry them quickly.", "가엾은 도러시아는 속으로 중얼거렸고, 눈물이 흘러내려 얼른 닦아 내야 했다."], ["She felt confusedly unhappy, and the image of Will which had been so clear to her before was mysteriously spoiled.", "그녀는 혼란스럽게 불행한 기분이었고, 전에는 그토록 또렷했던 윌의 모습이 알 수 없이 흐려졌다."], ["But the carriage stopped at the gate of the Hospital.", "그러나 마차가 병원 정문에 멈춰 섰다."], ["She was soon walking round the grass plots with Lydgate, and her feelings recovered the strong bent which had made her seek for this interview.", "그녀는 곧 리드게이트와 함께 잔디밭을 거닐었고, 그녀의 감정은 이 만남을 청하게 만들었던 강한 의지를 되찾았다."]], [["Will Ladislaw, meanwhile, was mortified, and knew the reason of it clearly enough.", "한편 윌 라디슬로는 굴욕감을 느꼈고, 그 이유를 충분히 명확하게 알고 있었다."], ["His chances of meeting Dorothea were rare; and here for the first time there had come a chance which had set him at a disadvantage.", "도러시아를 만날 기회는 드물었는데, 여기서 처음으로 자신을 불리한 처지에 놓이게 한 기회가 찾아온 것이었다."], ["It was not only, as it had been hitherto, that she was not supremely occupied with him, but that she had seen him under circumstances in which he might appear not to be supremely occupied with her.", "그것은 지금까지처럼 그녀가 그에게 온전히 마음을 쏟지 않고 있다는 것만이 아니라, 그가 그녀에게 온전히 마음을 쏟고 있지 않은 것처럼 보일 만한 상황에서 그녀가 그를 보았다는 것이었다."], ["He felt thrust to a new distance from her, amongst the circles of Middlemarchers who made no part of her life.", "그는 그녀의 삶의 일부를 이루지 못하는 미들마치 사람들의 무리 가운데서, 그녀로부터 새로운 거리만큼 떠밀려난 느낌이 들었다."], ["But that was not his fault: of course, since he had taken his lodgings in the town, he had been making as many acquaintances as he could, his position requiring that he should know everybody and everything.", "그러나 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니었다. 당연히 그는 이 도시에 거처를 정한 이상 가능한 한 많은 사람과 알고 지내 왔으니, 그의 지위가 누구든 무엇이든 다 알아야 할 것을 요구했기 때문이다."], ["Lydgate was really better worth knowing than any one else in the neighborhood, and he happened to have a wife who was musical and altogether worth calling upon.", "리드게이트는 이 인근의 그 누구보다도 정말 알고 지낼 만한 사람이었고, 마침 음악에 조예가 있고 여러모로 방문할 만한 아내를 두고 있었다."], ["Here was the whole history of the situation in which Diana had descended too unexpectedly on her worshipper.", "디아나가 자신을 숭배하는 자에게 너무도 예기치 않게 강림한 그 상황의 전모는 이러했다."], ["It was mortifying.", "굴욕스러운 일이었다."], ["Will was conscious that he should not have been at Middlemarch but for Dorothea; and yet his position there was threatening to divide him from her with those barriers of habitual sentiment which are more fatal to the persistence of mutual interest than all the distance between Rome and Britain.", "윌은 도러시아가 아니었다면 자신이 미들마치에 와 있지도 않았으리라는 것을 의식하고 있었다. 그런데도 그곳에서의 그의 처지는, 로마와 브리타니아 사이의 온갖 거리보다도 상호 관심의 지속에 더 치명적인, 그 습관적 감정의 장벽으로 그를 그녀와 갈라놓으려 하고 있었다."], ["Prejudices about rank and status were easy enough to defy in the form of a tyrannical letter from Mr.", "신분과 지위에 관한 편견은 케소번 씨의 횡포한 편지 같은 형태로는 충분히 쉽게 거역할 수 있었다."], ["Casaubon; but prejudices, like odorous bodies, have a double existence both solid and subtle—solid as the pyramids, subtle as the twentieth echo of an echo, or as the memory of hyacinths which once scented the darkness.", "그러나 편견이란, 향기를 내뿜는 물체처럼, 견고하면서도 미묘한 이중의 존재를 지니는 법이다—피라미드처럼 견고하고, 메아리의 메아리가 스무 번째로 울리는 것처럼, 혹은 한때 어둠에 향기를 더했던 히아신스의 기억처럼 미묘하다."], ["And Will was of a temperament to feel keenly the presence of subtleties: a man of clumsier perceptions would not have felt, as he did, that for the first time some sense of unfitness in perfect freedom with him had sprung up in Dorothea’s mind, and that their silence, as he conducted her to the carriage, had had a chill in it.", "그리고 윌은 그 미묘함의 존재를 예민하게 느끼는 기질이었다. 더 둔한 감각을 지닌 사람이라면, 도러시아의 마음속에 그와의 완전한 자유로움 속에서 처음으로 어떤 부적절함의 감각이 싹텄다는 것을, 그리고 그가 그녀를 마차로 이끌 때의 그 침묵에 냉기가 서려 있었다는 것을 그처럼 느끼지 못했을 것이다."], ["Perhaps Casaubon, in his hatred and jealousy, had been insisting to Dorothea that Will had slid below her socially.", "어쩌면 케소번이 증오와 질투에 사로잡혀, 윌이 사회적으로 그녀보다 낮은 곳으로 굴러떨어졌다고 도러시아에게 우겨 댔는지도 모른다."], ["Confound Casaubon!", "케소번, 빌어먹을!"]], [["Will re-entered the drawing-room, took up his hat, and looking irritated as he advanced towards Mrs. Lydgate, who had seated herself at her work-table, said—", "윌은 응접실로 다시 들어가 모자를 집어 들고, 작업대 앞에 앉은 리드게이트 부인에게 다가가며 짜증스러운 기색으로 말했다—"]], [["“It is always fatal to have music or poetry interrupted.", "“음악이나 시가 중단되는 건 언제나 치명적이지요."], ["May I come another day and just finish about the rendering of ‘Lungi dal caro bene’?”", "다른 날 다시 와서 ‘사랑하는 임에게서 멀리’의 해석을 마저 끝내도 될까요?”"]], [["“I shall be happy to be taught,”", "“가르침을 받을 수 있다면 기쁘겠어요.”"], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“But I am sure you admit that the interruption was a very beautiful one.", "“하지만 그 방해가 무척 아름다운 것이었다는 건 인정하시겠지요."], ["I quite envy your acquaintance with Mrs.", "케소번 부인과 아는 사이라니 정말 부러워요."], ["Casaubon. Is she very clever?", "그분은 무척 영리한가요?"], ["She looks as if she were.”", "그래 보이던데요.”"]], [["“Really, I never thought about it,”", "“글쎄요, 그런 건 생각해 본 적이 없습니다.”"], ["said Will, sulkily.", "윌이 뚱하게 말했다."]], [["“That is just the answer Tertius gave me, when I first asked him if she were handsome.", "“그분이 미인이냐고 테르티우스에게 처음 물었을 때 그이가 한 대답이 꼭 그랬어요."], ["What is it that you gentlemen are thinking of when you are with Mrs. Casaubon?”", "당신네 신사분들은 케소번 부인과 함께 있을 때 대체 무슨 생각을 하시나요?”"]], [["“Herself,”", "“그분 자신이지요.”"], ["said Will, not indisposed to provoke the charming Mrs. Lydgate.", "윌이 매력적인 리드게이트 부인을 약 올리고 싶은 마음이 없지 않아 말했다."], ["“When one sees a perfect woman, one never thinks of her attributes—one is conscious of her presence.”", "“완벽한 여인을 볼 때면 그 속성 따위는 결코 생각하지 않습니다—그저 그 존재를 의식할 뿐이지요.”"]], [["“I shall be jealous when Tertius goes to Lowick,”", "“테르티우스가 로익에 가면 질투가 날 거예요.”"], ["said Rosamond, dimpling, and speaking with aery lightness.", "로저먼드가 보조개를 지으며 사뿐한 경쾌함으로 말했다."], ["“He will come back and think nothing of me.”", "“그이는 돌아와서 저 따위는 거들떠보지도 않을걸요.”"]], [["“That does not seem to have been the effect on Lydgate hitherto.", "“지금껏 리드게이트에게 그런 영향을 미친 것 같지는 않은데요."], ["Mrs. Casaubon is too unlike other women for them to be compared with her.”", "케소번 부인은 다른 여성들과는 너무 달라서 그들과 비교될 수가 없어요.”"]], [["“You are a devout worshipper, I perceive.", "“보아하니 당신은 독실한 숭배자시군요."], ["You often see her, I suppose.”", "그분을 자주 뵙겠지요.”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니요.”"], ["said Will, almost pettishly.", "윌이 거의 토라진 듯 말했다."], ["“Worship is usually a matter of theory rather than of practice.", "“숭배란 대개 실천이라기보다 이론의 문제니까요."], ["But I am practising it to excess just at this moment—I must really tear myself away.”", "하지만 지금 이 순간 저는 그것을 지나치게 실천하고 있군요—정말로 떨쳐 일어나 가 봐야겠습니다.”"]], [["“Pray come again some evening: Mr.", "“언제 저녁에 또 들러 주세요."], ["Lydgate will like to hear the music, and I cannot enjoy it so well without him.”", "리드게이트 씨도 음악을 듣고 싶어 할 테고, 저도 그이 없이는 그만큼 즐길 수가 없으니까요.”"]], [["When her husband was at home again, Rosamond said, standing in front of him and holding his coat-collar with both her hands, “Mr.", "남편이 다시 집에 돌아왔을 때, 로저먼드는 그의 앞에 서서 두 손으로 그의 외투 깃을 잡고 말했다."], ["Ladislaw was here singing with me when Mrs. Casaubon came in.", "“케소번 부인이 들어왔을 때 라디슬로 씨가 여기서 저와 노래를 부르고 있었어요."], ["He seemed vexed.", "그분이 언짢아 보이더군요."], ["Do you think he disliked her seeing him at our house?", "그분이 우리 집에서 자기 모습을 그 부인에게 보인 게 싫었던 걸까요?"], ["Surely your position is more than equal to his—whatever may be his relation to the Casaubons.”", "케소번가와의 관계가 어떻든 간에, 당신 지위가 분명 그분 못지않을 텐데요.”"]], [["“No, no; it must be something else if he were really vexed.", "“아니, 아니야. 정말로 언짢았다면 다른 이유 때문일 거야."], ["Ladislaw is a sort of gypsy; he thinks nothing of leather and prunella.”", "라디슬로는 일종의 집시 같은 친구야. 가죽이니 모직이니 하는 신분 따위는 아랑곳하지 않거든.”"]], [["“Music apart, he is not always very agreeable.", "“음악을 빼면 그분이 늘 그렇게 유쾌한 건 아니에요."], ["Do you like him?”", "당신은 그분이 좋아요?”"]], [["“Yes: I think he is a good fellow: rather miscellaneous and bric-a-brac, but likable.”", "“그럼. 괜찮은 친구라고 생각해. 좀 잡다하고 골동품 같은 데가 있긴 해도, 호감 가는 친구야.”"]], [["“Do you know, I think he adores Mrs. Casaubon.”", "“있잖아요, 그분이 케소번 부인을 흠모하는 것 같아요.”"]], [["“Poor devil!”", "“가엾은 친구!”"], ["said Lydgate, smiling and pinching his wife’s ears.", "리드게이트가 미소 지으며 아내의 귀를 살짝 꼬집고 말했다."]], [["Rosamond felt herself beginning to know a great deal of the world, especially in discovering what when she was in her unmarried girlhood had been inconceivable to her except as a dim tragedy in by-gone costumes—that women, even after marriage, might make conquests and enslave men.", "로저먼드는 자신이 세상사를 많이 알게 되었다고 느끼기 시작했는데, 특히 처녀 시절에는 지나간 시대의 의상을 걸친 어슴푸레한 비극으로밖에는 상상할 수 없었던 것을 깨닫게 되면서 그러했다—여자란 결혼한 뒤에도 남자를 정복하고 노예로 삼을 수 있다는 사실 말이다."], ["At that time young ladies in the country, even when educated at Mrs. Lemon’s, read little French literature later than Racine, and public prints had not cast their present magnificent illumination over the scandals of life.", "그 시절 시골의 젊은 숙녀들은, 레먼 부인의 학교에서 교육받은 경우라 해도 라신보다 후대의 프랑스 문학은 거의 읽지 않았고, 출판물도 인생의 추문 위에 오늘날과 같은 찬란한 조명을 비추지 않았다."], ["Still, vanity, with a woman’s whole mind and day to work in, can construct abundantly on slight hints, especially on such a hint as the possibility of indefinite conquests.", "그래도 허영심이란, 여자의 온 정신과 하루를 부려 쓸 수만 있다면, 사소한 암시만으로도, 특히 무한한 정복의 가능성 같은 암시만으로도 풍성하게 무언가를 지어낼 수 있는 법이다."], ["How delightful to make captives from the throne of marriage with a husband as crown-prince by your side—himself in fact a subject—while the captives look up forever hopeless, losing their rest probably, and if their appetite too, so much the better!", "황태자 같은 남편을 곁에 두고—사실은 그 남편 자신이 신하인 셈인데—결혼의 옥좌에서 포로들을 사로잡는다는 것은 얼마나 즐거운 일인가. 그러는 동안 포로들은 영원히 가망 없이 우러러보며, 아마도 안식을 잃고, 입맛까지 잃는다면 더더욱 좋을 테고!"], ["But Rosamond’s romance turned at present chiefly on her crown-prince, and it was enough to enjoy his assured subjection.", "그러나 로저먼드의 낭만은 지금으로서는 주로 자신의 황태자에게 쏠려 있었고, 그가 확실하게 복종하고 있음을 누리는 것으로 충분했다."], ["When he said, “Poor devil!” she asked, with playful curiosity—", "그가 “가엾은 친구!”라고 말했을 때, 그녀는 장난스러운 호기심으로 물었다—"]], [["“Why so?”", "“어째서요?”"]], [["“Why, what can a man do when he takes to adoring one of you mermaids?", "“글쎄, 당신 같은 인어 하나를 숭배하기 시작하면 남자가 뭘 할 수 있겠어?"], ["He only neglects his work and runs up bills.”", "그저 일을 소홀히 하고 빚이나 쌓아 올릴 뿐이지.”"]], [["“I am sure you do not neglect your work.", "“당신은 분명 일을 소홀히 하지 않으시잖아요."], ["You are always at the Hospital, or seeing poor patients, or thinking about some doctor’s quarrel; and then at home you always want to pore over your microscope and phials.", "늘 병원에 가 있거나, 가난한 환자들을 보거나, 무슨 의사들 싸움을 생각하고 있고요. 그러다 집에 와서는 늘 현미경이랑 약병만 들여다보고 싶어 하시죠."], ["Confess you like those things better than me.”", "솔직히 그런 것들을 저보다 더 좋아한다고 인정하세요.”"]], [["“Haven’t you ambition enough to wish that your husband should be something better than a Middlemarch doctor?”", "“당신은 남편이 한낱 미들마치의 의사보다 더 나은 사람이 되기를 바랄 만한 야심도 없어?”"], ["said Lydgate, letting his hands fall on to his wife’s shoulders, and looking at her with affectionate gravity.", "리드게이트가 두 손을 아내의 어깨에 떨어뜨리며 애정 어린 진지함으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I shall make you learn my favorite bit from an old poet—", "“당신에게 옛 시인의 내가 좋아하는 구절을 외우게 해야겠군—\n‘어찌하여 우리의 자긍심은 그토록 부산을 떨며"]], [["‘Why should our pride make such a stir to be And be forgot?", "존재했다가 잊히려 하는가?"], ["What good is like to this, To do worthy the writing, and to write Worthy the reading and the worlds delight?’", "이만한 선이 또 어디 있으랴,\n기록할 가치가 있는 일을 행하고, 또 그것을"]], [["What I want, Rosy, is to do worthy the writing,—and to write out myself what I have done.", "읽을 가치가, 세상이 기뻐할 가치가 있게 적어 내는 것 말고는?’\n내가 바라는 건, 로지, 기록할 가치가 있는 일을 해내는 거야—그리고 내가 해낸 것을 내 손으로 직접 적어 내는 거지."], ["A man must work, to do that, my pet.”", "그러려면 사람은 일을 해야 해, 내 사랑.”"]], [["“Of course, I wish you to make discoveries: no one could more wish you to attain a high position in some better place than Middlemarch.", "“물론 당신이 발견을 이뤄 내기를 바라요. 미들마치보다 더 나은 곳에서 당신이 높은 지위에 오르기를 저보다 더 바랄 사람은 없을 거예요."], ["You cannot say that I have ever tried to hinder you from working.", "제가 당신의 일을 방해하려 한 적이 있다고는 말 못 하실 거예요."], ["But we cannot live like hermits.", "하지만 우리가 은둔자처럼 살 수는 없잖아요."], ["You are not discontented with me, Tertius?”", "저한테 불만이 있는 건 아니죠, 테르티우스?”"]], [["“No, dear, no.", "“아니야, 여보, 아니야."], ["I am too entirely contented.”", "난 더할 나위 없이 만족하고 있어.”"]], [["“But what did Mrs. Casaubon want to say to you?”", "“그런데 케소번 부인이 당신에게 무슨 말을 하려던 거예요?”"]], [["“Merely to ask about her husband’s health.", "“그저 남편의 건강에 대해 묻고 싶었던 거야."], ["But I think she is going to be splendid to our New Hospital: I think she will give us two hundred a-year.”", "하지만 그 부인이 우리 새 병원에 훌륭한 도움을 줄 것 같아. 연 이백 파운드를 기부해 줄 것 같거든.”"]], [["# CHAPTER XLIV.", "# CHAPTER XLIV."]], [["I would not creep along the coast but steer Out in mid-sea, by guidance of the stars.", "해안을 따라 살금살금 나아가지 않고, 별빛을 길잡이 삼아 바다 한가운데로 키를 잡으리라."]], [["When Dorothea, walking round the laurel-planted plots of the New Hospital with Lydgate, had learned from him that there were no signs of change in Mr. Casaubon’s bodily condition beyond the mental sign of anxiety to know the truth about his illness, she was silent for a few moments, wondering whether she had said or done anything to rouse this new anxiety.", "도러시아가 리드게이트와 함께 새 병원의 월계수가 심긴 화단 주위를 거닐다가, 캐소본 씨의 병세에는 자신의 병에 관한 진실을 알고 싶어 하는 불안이라는 정신적 징후 외에 신체적 변화의 조짐이 없다는 말을 그에게서 들었을 때, 그녀는 몇 순간 잠자코, 혹시 자기가 이 새로운 불안을 불러일으킬 만한 말이나 행동을 하지는 않았을까 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["Lydgate, not willing to let slip an opportunity of furthering a favorite purpose, ventured to say—", "리드게이트는 평소 마음에 둔 목적을 진척시킬 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않아 용기를 내어 말했다."]], [["“I don’t know whether your or Mr. Casaubon’s attention has been drawn to the needs of our New Hospital.", "\"부인이나 캐소본 씨께서 우리 새 병원이 처한 어려움에 관심을 가지신 적이 있는지 모르겠습니다."], ["Circumstances have made it seem rather egotistic in me to urge the subject; but that is not my fault: it is because there is a fight being made against it by the other medical men.", "사정이 이렇다 보니 제가 이 문제를 거론하는 게 다소 이기적으로 비칠 것 같습니다만, 그건 제 잘못이 아닙니다. 다른 의료인들이 병원을 반대하며 싸움을 벌이고 있기 때문이지요."], ["I think you are generally interested in such things, for I remember that when I first had the pleasure of seeing you at Tipton Grange before your marriage, you were asking me some questions about the way in which the health of the poor was affected by their miserable housing.”", "부인께서는 대체로 그런 일에 관심을 두시는 것 같습니다. 결혼하시기 전 티프턴 그레인지에서 처음 뵙는 즐거움을 누렸을 때, 부인께서 빈민들의 비참한 주거가 그들의 건강에 어떤 영향을 미치는지 제게 몇 가지 질문하셨던 일이 기억나니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, indeed,”", "\"네, 정말 그랬지요.\""], ["said Dorothea, brightening.", "도러시아가 환해진 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“I shall be quite grateful to you if you will tell me how I can help to make things a little better.", "\"상황을 조금이라도 나아지게 하려면 제가 어떻게 도울 수 있는지 말씀해 주신다면 정말 감사하겠어요."], ["Everything of that sort has slipped away from me since I have been married.", "결혼한 뒤로는 그런 일이 모두 제게서 멀어져 버렸어요."], ["I mean,”", "제 말은,\""], ["she said, after a moment’s hesitation, “that the people in our village are tolerably comfortable, and my mind has been too much taken up for me to inquire further.", "그녀는 잠시 망설이다가 덧붙였다. \"우리 마을 사람들은 그런대로 편안하게 지내고, 또 제 마음이 너무 다른 데 쏠려 있어서 더는 알아볼 여유가 없었다는 뜻이에요."], ["But here—in such a place as Middlemarch—there must be a great deal to be done.”", "하지만 이곳에서는, 미들마치 같은 곳에서는 할 일이 무척 많을 게 분명해요.\""]], [["“There is everything to be done,”", "\"해야 할 일은 전부라고 할 수 있습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, with abrupt energy.", "리드게이트가 대뜸 힘주어 말했다."], ["“And this Hospital is a capital piece of work, due entirely to Mr.", "\"그리고 이 병원은 훌륭한 사업이지요."], ["Bulstrode’s exertions, and in a great degree to his money.", "전적으로 불스트로드 씨의 노력 덕분이고, 상당 부분 그분의 돈으로 이루어진 일입니다."], ["But one man can’t do everything in a scheme of this sort.", "하지만 이런 계획에서는 한 사람이 모든 일을 다 할 수는 없습니다."], ["Of course he looked forward to help.", "물론 그분도 도움을 기대했지요."], ["And now there’s a mean, petty feud set up against the thing in the town, by certain persons who want to make it a failure.”", "그런데 이제 이 사업을 실패로 만들려는 몇몇 사람들이 마을에서 치사하고 옹졸한 반목을 일으키고 있습니다.\""]], [["“What can be their reasons?”", "\"무슨 이유로 그러는 걸까요?\""], ["said Dorothea, with naive surprise.", "도러시아가 순진하게 놀라며 말했다."]], [["“Chiefly Mr. Bulstrode’s unpopularity, to begin with.", "\"우선 무엇보다 불스트로드 씨의 인기 없음이 가장 큰 이유지요."], ["Half the town would almost take trouble for the sake of thwarting him.", "마을 사람 절반은 그분을 방해하기 위해서라면 거의 수고도 마다하지 않을 겁니다."], ["In this stupid world most people never consider that a thing is good to be done unless it is done by their own set.", "이 어리석은 세상에서 대부분의 사람들은 어떤 일이 자기 무리가 하는 일이 아니라면 좋은 일이라고 여기지 않거든요."], ["I had no connection with Bulstrode before I came here.", "저는 이곳에 오기 전에 불스트로드와 아무런 인연이 없었습니다."], ["I look at him quite impartially, and I see that he has some notions—that he has set things on foot—which I can turn to good public purpose.", "저는 그분을 아주 공정하게 봅니다. 그래서 그분에게 어떤 구상들이 있다는 걸, 그분이 어떤 일들을 시작해 놓았다는 걸 압니다."], ["If a fair number of the better educated men went to work with the belief that their observations might contribute to the reform of medical doctrine and practice, we should soon see a change for the better.", "저는 그것을 훌륭한 공공의 목적으로 돌릴 수 있고요. 만약 교육받은 사람들 가운데 상당수가 자신들의 관찰이 의학 이론과 진료의 개혁에 이바지할 수 있으리라는 믿음을 가지고 일에 나선다면, 우리는 머지않아 더 나은 변화를 보게 될 것입니다."], ["That’s my point of view.", "그게 제 관점입니다."], ["I hold that by refusing to work with Mr. Bulstrode I should be turning my back on an opportunity of making my profession more generally serviceable.”", "불스트로드 씨와 함께 일하기를 거부한다면, 저는 제 직업을 더 폭넓게 쓸모 있게 만들 기회에 등을 돌리는 셈이 되리라고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“I quite agree with you,”", "\"전적으로 동감이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, at once fascinated by the situation sketched in Lydgate’s words.", "리드게이트의 말이 그려 낸 상황에 단번에 매료된 도러시아가 말했다."], ["“But what is there against Mr. Bulstrode?", "\"그런데 불스트로드 씨에게 무슨 흠이 있다는 건가요?"], ["I know that my uncle is friendly with him.”", "저희 삼촌은 그분과 친하게 지내시는 걸로 아는데요.\""]], [["“People don’t like his religious tone,”", "\"사람들은 그분의 종교적인 어조를 좋아하지 않습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, breaking off there.", "리드게이트가 거기서 말을 끊으며 대답했다."]], [["“That is all the stronger reason for despising such an opposition,”", "\"그렇다면 그런 반대를 경멸할 이유가 더욱 분명해지는군요.\""], ["said Dorothea, looking at the affairs of Middlemarch by the light of the great persecutions.", "도러시아가 위대한 박해의 빛에 비추어 미들마치의 일을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“To put the matter quite fairly, they have other objections to him:—he is masterful and rather unsociable, and he is concerned with trade, which has complaints of its own that I know nothing about.", "\"공정하게 말씀드리자면, 사람들이 그분에게 다른 불만도 갖고 있긴 합니다. 그분은 위압적이고 다소 비사교적이며, 또 상업에 관여하고 있는데, 거기에는 제가 전혀 모르는 그 나름의 말썽들이 있지요."], ["But what has that to do with the question whether it would not be a fine thing to establish here a more valuable hospital than any they have in the county?", "하지만 그것이 이 고을에 있는 어떤 병원보다 더 가치 있는 병원을 여기에 세우는 것이 좋은 일인지 아닌지 하는 문제와 무슨 상관이 있겠습니까?"], ["The immediate motive to the opposition, however, is the fact that Bulstrode has put the medical direction into my hands.", "하지만 반대의 직접적인 동기는, 불스트로드가 의료 책임을 제 손에 맡겼다는 사실입니다."], ["Of course I am glad of that.", "물론 저는 그 점이 기쁩니다."], ["It gives me an opportunity of doing some good work,—and I am aware that I have to justify his choice of me.", "그것은 제게 훌륭한 일을 할 기회를 주니까요. 그리고 그분이 저를 택한 일이 옳았음을 제가 증명해야 한다는 것도 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["But the consequence is, that the whole profession in Middlemarch have set themselves tooth and nail against the Hospital, and not only refuse to cooperate themselves, but try to blacken the whole affair and hinder subscriptions.”", "그런데 그 결과로 미들마치의 의료계 전체가 이 병원에 맹렬히 맞서고 나서서, 자기들이 협력하기를 거부할 뿐 아니라 이 일 전체를 깎아내리고 기부를 방해하려 들고 있습니다.\""]], [["“How very petty!”", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 옹졸할까요!\""], ["exclaimed Dorothea, indignantly.", "도러시아가 분개하여 외쳤다."]], [["“I suppose one must expect to fight one’s way: there is hardly anything to be done without it.", "\"제 길은 싸워서 헤쳐 나가야 한다고 각오해야겠지요. 그런 싸움 없이 이룰 수 있는 일은 거의 없으니까요."], ["And the ignorance of people about here is stupendous.", "게다가 이 근방 사람들의 무지는 엄청납니다."], ["I don’t lay claim to anything else than having used some opportunities which have not come within everybody’s reach; but there is no stifling the offence of being young, and a new-comer, and happening to know something more than the old inhabitants.", "저는 그저 누구에게나 주어지지는 않았던 몇 가지 기회를 활용했다는 것 말고는 아무것도 내세우지 않습니다. 하지만 젊다는 것, 외지에서 온 사람이라는 것, 그리고 우연히 오래 산 주민들보다 무언가를 조금 더 안다는 것은 도무지 잠재울 수 없는 괘씸한 죄가 되지요."], ["Still, if I believe that I can set going a better method of treatment—if I believe that I can pursue certain observations and inquiries which may be a lasting benefit to medical practice, I should be a base truckler if I allowed any consideration of personal comfort to hinder me.", "그래도 제가 더 나은 치료법을 시작할 수 있다고 믿는다면, 의료 진료에 오래도록 도움이 될 어떤 관찰과 탐구를 이어 갈 수 있다고 믿는다면, 개인적인 안락을 고려해 그것을 가로막도록 내버려둔다면 저는 비열한 아첨꾼이 되고 말 겁니다."], ["And the course is all the clearer from there being no salary in question to put my persistence in an equivocal light.”", "더구나 이 일에는 봉급이 걸려 있지 않아 제 고집을 의심스러운 빛으로 비추지 않으니, 제가 가야 할 길은 한층 더 분명합니다.\""]], [["“I am glad you have told me this, Mr. Lydgate,”", "\"이 이야기를 해 주셔서 기뻐요, 리드게이트 선생님.\""], ["said Dorothea, cordially.", "도러시아가 진심을 담아 말했다."], ["“I feel sure I can help a little.", "\"제가 조금은 도울 수 있을 거라고 확신해요."], ["I have some money, and don’t know what to do with it—that is often an uncomfortable thought to me.", "제게는 돈이 좀 있는데 그걸 어떻게 써야 할지 모르겠어요. 그게 종종 마음을 불편하게 하는 생각이지요."], ["I am sure I can spare two hundred a-year for a grand purpose like this.", "이렇게 훌륭한 목적이라면 일 년에 이백 파운드쯤은 분명히 내놓을 수 있어요."], ["How happy you must be, to know things that you feel sure will do great good!", "큰 도움이 될 거라고 확신하는 일을 알고 계시다니, 얼마나 행복하시겠어요!"], ["I wish I could awake with that knowledge every morning.", "저도 매일 아침 그런 확신을 가지고 깨어날 수 있다면 좋겠어요."], ["There seems to be so much trouble taken that one can hardly see the good of!”", "정작 그 좋은 점은 좀처럼 보이지 않는데도 들이는 수고는 너무나 많은 것 같으니까요!\""]], [["There was a melancholy cadence in Dorothea’s voice as she spoke these last words.", "이 마지막 말을 할 때 도러시아의 목소리에는 우울한 가락이 묻어 있었다."], ["But she presently added, more cheerfully, “Pray come to Lowick and tell us more of this.", "하지만 그녀는 곧 한결 밝은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"부디 로윅에 오셔서 이 일에 관해 더 자세히 이야기해 주세요."], ["I will mention the subject to Mr.", "제가 캐소본 씨께 이 문제를 말씀드릴게요."], ["Casaubon. I must hasten home now.”", "이제 서둘러 집으로 돌아가야겠어요.\""]], [["She did mention it that evening, and said that she should like to subscribe two hundred a-year—she had seven hundred a-year as the equivalent of her own fortune, settled on her at her marriage.", "그녀는 그날 저녁 정말로 그 이야기를 꺼내며, 일 년에 이백 파운드를 기부하고 싶다고 말했다. 그녀는 결혼할 때 자기 몫의 재산에 해당하는 일 년 칠백 파운드를 받기로 정해져 있었다."], ["Mr. Casaubon made no objection beyond a passing remark that the sum might be disproportionate in relation to other good objects, but when Dorothea in her ignorance resisted that suggestion, he acquiesced.", "캐소본 씨는 그 액수가 다른 좋은 목적들에 비추어 균형이 맞지 않을지도 모른다고 지나가듯 한마디 했을 뿐 별다른 반대는 하지 않았는데, 도러시아가 그런 사정을 모른 채 그 제안에 맞서자 그는 묵묵히 동의했다."], ["He did not care himself about spending money, and was not reluctant to give it.", "그 자신은 돈 쓰는 일에 개의치 않았고, 돈을 내놓기를 꺼리지도 않았다."], ["If he ever felt keenly any question of money it was through the medium of another passion than the love of material property.", "만약 그가 돈에 관한 어떤 문제로 절실하게 마음을 쓴 적이 있다면, 그것은 물질적 재산에 대한 사랑이 아닌 다른 열정을 매개로 한 것이었다."]], [["Dorothea told him that she had seen Lydgate, and recited the gist of her conversation with him about the Hospital.", "도러시아는 그에게 리드게이트를 만났다고 말하고, 병원에 관해 그와 나눈 대화의 요지를 들려주었다."], ["Mr. Casaubon did not question her further, but he felt sure that she had wished to know what had passed between Lydgate and himself.", "캐소본 씨는 더 캐묻지 않았으나, 그녀가 리드게이트와 자기 사이에 오간 이야기를 알고 싶어 했으리라고 확신했다."], ["“She knows that I know,”", "\"내가 안다는 걸 그녀는 알고 있어.\""], ["said the ever-restless voice within; but that increase of tacit knowledge only thrust further off any confidence between them.", "늘 안절부절못하는 내면의 목소리가 말했다. 하지만 그렇게 말없는 앎이 늘어날수록 둘 사이의 어떤 신뢰도 점점 더 멀어질 뿐이었다."], ["He distrusted her affection; and what loneliness is more lonely than distrust?", "그는 그녀의 애정을 의심했다. 그런데 의심보다 더 외로운 고독이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["# CHAPTER XLV.", "# CHAPTER XLV."]], [["It is the humor of many heads to extol the days of their forefathers, and declaim against the wickedness of times present.", "많은 사람들의 머릿속에는 선조들의 시대를 찬양하고 지금 시대의 사악함을 규탄하는 변덕이 들어 있다."], ["Which notwithstanding they cannot handsomely do, without the borrowed help and satire of times past; condemning the vices of their own times, by the expressions of vices in times which they commend, which cannot but argue the community of vice in both.", "그럼에도 그들은 지나간 시대의 빌려 온 도움과 풍자가 없이는 그 일을 그럴듯하게 해내지 못한다. 그들은 자기 시대의 악덕을 단죄하되 자신들이 찬양하는 시대의 악덕을 표현한 말을 빌려 쓰는데, 이는 두 시대 모두에 악덕이 공통으로 존재함을 입증할 따름이다."], ["Horace, therefore, Juvenal, and Persius, were no prophets, although their lines did seem to indigitate and point at our times.—SIR THOMAS BROWNE: Pseudodoxia Epidemica.", "그러므로 호라티우스도, 유베날리스도, 페르시우스도 예언자가 아니었으니, 그들의 시구가 우리 시대를 가리키고 짚어 내는 듯 보였을지라도 그러하다.—토머스 브라운 경: 『유행하는 거짓 통념들』"]], [["That opposition to the New Fever Hospital which Lydgate had sketched to Dorothea was, like other oppositions, to be viewed in many different lights.", "리드게이트가 도러시아에게 대략 설명했던 그 새 열병 병원에 대한 반대는, 다른 반대들과 마찬가지로 여러 다른 각도에서 바라볼 수 있는 것이었다."], ["He regarded it as a mixture of jealousy and dunderheaded prejudice. Mr.", "그는 그것을 질투와 우둔한 편견의 혼합물로 여겼다."], ["Bulstrode saw in it not only medical jealousy but a determination to thwart himself, prompted mainly by a hatred of that vital religion of which he had striven to be an effectual lay representative—a hatred which certainly found pretexts apart from religion such as were only too easy to find in the entanglements of human action.", "불스트로드 씨는 그 안에서 의학적 질투뿐만 아니라 자신을 방해하려는 결의를 보았는데, 그것은 주로 자신이 유능한 평신도 대표가 되고자 애써 온 그 생생한 신앙에 대한 증오에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그 증오는 분명 종교와 무관한 구실들도 찾아냈으니, 인간 행위의 얽힘 속에서는 그런 구실을 찾기가 너무도 쉬웠다."], ["These might be called the ministerial views.", "이런 것들은 성직자적 견해라 부를 만했다."], ["But oppositions have the illimitable range of objections at command, which need never stop short at the boundary of knowledge, but can draw forever on the vasts of ignorance.", "그러나 반대라는 것은 마음대로 동원할 수 있는 무한한 범위의 이의들을 거느리고 있어서, 지식의 경계에서 멈출 필요가 결코 없으며 무지의 광대함에서 영원히 끌어다 쓸 수 있다."], ["What the opposition in Middlemarch said about the New Hospital and its administration had certainly a great deal of echo in it, for heaven has taken care that everybody shall not be an originator; but there were differences which represented every social shade between the polished moderation of Dr.", "미들마치의 반대 세력이 새 병원과 그 운영에 대해 한 말에는 분명 메아리가 많이 섞여 있었으니, 하늘은 모든 사람이 창안자가 되지는 못하도록 마련해 두었기 때문이다."], ["Minchin and the trenchant assertion of Mrs. Dollop, the landlady of the Tankard in Slaughter Lane.", "하지만 그 안에는 민친 박사의 세련된 절제로부터 슬로터 레인의 술집 탱커드의 여주인 돌롭 부인의 신랄한 단언에 이르기까지, 온갖 사회적 색조를 대표하는 차이들이 있었다."]], [["Mrs. Dollop became more and more convinced by her own asseveration, that Dr.", "돌롭 부인은 자신의 거듭된 주장을 통해 점점 더 확신하게 되었으니, 리드게이트 박사가 사람들을 독살하지는 않더라도 병원에서 죽게 내버려 둘 작정인데, 그래야 양해를 구하거나 구하지 않고서 그들을 해부할 수 있기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["Lydgate meant to let the people die in the Hospital, if not to poison them, for the sake of cutting them up without saying by your leave or with your leave; for it was a known “fac”", "그가 팔리 스트리트의 어떤 여인 못지않게 점잖은, 결혼 전 신탁된 재산을 가지고 있던 고비 부인을 해부하려 했다는 것은 누구나 아는 \"사실\"이었으니까."], ["that he had wanted to cut up Mrs.", "의사라는 자가 그 모양이라니 한심한 노릇이었다."], ["Goby, as respectable a woman as any in Parley Street, who had money in trust before her marriage—a poor tale for a doctor, who if he was good for anything should know what was the matter with you before you died, and not want to pry into your inside after you were gone.", "의사가 쓸모 있는 자라면 환자가 죽기 전에 무엇이 문제인지 알아야지, 죽은 다음에 속을 들여다보려 들어서는 안 되는 법이었다."], ["If that was not reason, Mrs.", "그게 이유가 아니라면 도대체 무엇이 이유냐고 돌롭 부인은 따지고 싶어 했다."], ["Dollop wished to know what was; but there was a prevalent feeling in her audience that her opinion was a bulwark, and that if it were overthrown there would be no limits to the cutting-up of bodies, as had been well seen in Burke and Hare with their pitch-plaisters—such a hanging business as that was not wanted in Middlemarch!", "그러나 그녀의 청중들 사이에는 그녀의 의견이 하나의 방벽이며, 그것이 무너진다면 시신 해부에 한계가 없어지리라는 정서가 널리 퍼져 있었으니, 버크와 헤어가 그들의 송진 고약을 가지고 한 짓에서 똑똑히 보지 않았던가. 그런 교수형 감의 일은 미들마치에서는 결코 바라지 않는 바였다!"]], [["And let it not be supposed that opinion at the Tankard in Slaughter Lane was unimportant to the medical profession: that old authentic public-house—the original Tankard, known by the name of Dollop’s—was the resort of a great Benefit Club, which had some months before put to the vote whether its long-standing medical man, “Doctor Gambit,”", "그리고 슬로터 레인의 탱커드에서의 여론이 의료계에 중요하지 않으리라고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 돌롭이라는 이름으로 알려진 그 유서 깊고 정통적인 술집, 원조 탱커드는 한 거대한 공제 조합의 단골 모임 장소였는데, 그 조합은 몇 달 전에 오랜 기간 그들의 주치의였던 \"갬빗 박사\"를 면직하고 그 대신 \"이 리드게이트 박사\"를 앉혀야 하지 않겠느냐를 두고 표결에 부친 적이 있었다."], ["should not be cashiered in favor of “this Doctor Lydgate,” who was capable of performing the most astonishing cures, and rescuing people altogether given up by other practitioners.", "리드게이트 박사는 가장 놀라운 치료를 해내고 다른 의사들이 완전히 포기한 사람들을 구해 낼 능력이 있었기 때문이다."], ["But the balance had been turned against Lydgate by two members, who for some private reasons held that this power of resuscitating persons as good as dead was an equivocal recommendation, and might interfere with providential favors.", "그러나 두 회원에 의해 저울추가 리드게이트에게 불리하게 기울었으니, 그들은 사적인 이유로 죽은 거나 다름없는 사람을 되살리는 그 능력이 모호한 추천 사유라고 여겼고, 섭리에 따른 은총을 방해할지도 모른다고 보았기 때문이다."], ["In the course of the year, however, there had been a change in the public sentiment, of which the unanimity at Dollop’s was an index.", "그러나 한 해가 지나는 동안 여론에 변화가 생겼고, 돌롭의 술집에서의 만장일치가 그 지표였다."]], [["A good deal more than a year ago, before anything was known of Lydgate’s skill, the judgments on it had naturally been divided, depending on a sense of likelihood, situated perhaps in the pit of the stomach or in the pineal gland, and differing in its verdicts, but not the less valuable as a guide in the total deficit of evidence.", "한 해도 더 전, 리드게이트의 솜씨에 대해 아무것도 알려지지 않았을 때 그에 대한 판단은 자연히 갈렸는데, 그것은 어쩌면 명치께나 송과선에 자리 잡고 있을 개연성의 감각에 좌우되어 그 판결이 제각각이었으나, 증거가 전적으로 결핍된 상황에서는 그래도 길잡이로서 못지않은 가치가 있었다. 만성 질환을 앓거나 늙은 페더스톤처럼 오래도록 생명이 닳아 너덜너덜해진 환자들은 곧장 그를 시험해 보고 싶어 했다."], ["Patients who had chronic diseases or whose lives had long been worn threadbare, like old Featherstone’s, had been at once inclined to try him; also, many who did not like paying their doctor’s bills, thought agreeably of opening an account with a new doctor and sending for him without stint if the children’s temper wanted a dose, occasions when the old practitioners were often crusty; and all persons thus inclined to employ Lydgate held it likely that he was clever.", "또한 의사 청구서 내는 것을 싫어하는 많은 이들은 새 의사와 거래를 트는 것을 기껍게 여겼는데, 아이들 성미가 약 한 첩을 필요로 할 때면—그런 경우 옛 의사들은 흔히 퉁명스러웠으므로—마음 놓고 그를 부를 수 있겠다 싶어서였다. 이처럼 리드게이트를 쓰고 싶어 한 사람들은 모두 그가 영리하리라고 여겼다."], ["Some considered that he might do more than others “where there was liver;”—at least there would be no harm in getting a few bottles of “stuff”", "어떤 이들은 \"간이 문제일 때\" 그가 남들보다 더 잘할지도 모른다고 생각했다."], ["from him, since if these proved useless it would still be possible to return to the Purifying Pills, which kept you alive if they did not remove the yellowness.", "적어도 그에게서 \"약\" 몇 병 얻어 둔다고 해될 것은 없었으니, 그것이 쓸모없다고 판명되더라도 정화 환약으로 돌아갈 수 있을 테고, 그 약은 누런 기를 없애 주지는 못해도 목숨은 부지하게 해 주었으니까."], ["But these were people of minor importance.", "그러나 이들은 별로 중요하지 않은 사람들이었다."], ["Good Middlemarch families were of course not going to change their doctor without reason shown; and everybody who had employed Mr. Peacock did not feel obliged to accept a new man merely in the character of his successor, objecting that he was “not likely to be equal to Peacock.”", "미들마치의 좋은 가문들은 물론 합당한 이유가 드러나지 않는 한 의사를 바꾸려 하지 않았으며, 피콕 씨를 써 온 사람은 누구든 그저 후계자라는 이유만으로 새 사람을 받아들여야 한다고 느끼지 않았으니, 그가 \"피콕에 필적할 것 같지 않다\"고 트집을 잡았다."]], [["But Lydgate had not been long in the town before there were particulars enough reported of him to breed much more specific expectations and to intensify differences into partisanship; some of the particulars being of that impressive order of which the significance is entirely hidden, like a statistical amount without a standard of comparison, but with a note of exclamation at the end.", "그러나 리드게이트가 마을에 온 지 오래지 않아 그에 관한 시시콜콜한 소문이 충분히 퍼져 훨씬 더 구체적인 기대를 낳고 차이를 당파적 대립으로까지 격화시켰으니, 그 시시콜콜한 것들 중에는 비교 기준 없는 통계 수치처럼 의미가 완전히 감춰져 있되 끝에 느낌표가 붙은, 그런 깊은 인상을 주는 부류가 있었다."], ["The cubic feet of oxygen yearly swallowed by a full-grown man—what a shudder they might have created in some Middlemarch circles!", "다 자란 남자가 한 해에 들이마시는 산소의 세제곱피트 양이라니—그것이 미들마치의 어떤 모임에 얼마나 큰 전율을 불러일으켰을까!"], ["“Oxygen!", "\"산소라!"], ["nobody knows what that may be—is it any wonder the cholera has got to Dantzic?", "그게 무엇일지 아무도 모르지—콜레라가 단치히까지 퍼진 게 뭐 놀랄 일인가?"], ["And yet there are people who say quarantine is no good!”", "그런데도 검역이 아무 소용없다는 사람들이 있다니!\""]], [["One of the facts quickly rumored was that Lydgate did not dispense drugs.", "빠르게 퍼진 사실들 중 하나는 리드게이트가 약을 조제해 주지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["This was offensive both to the physicians whose exclusive distinction seemed infringed on, and to the surgeon-apothecaries with whom he ranged himself; and only a little while before, they might have counted on having the law on their side against a man who without calling himself a London-made M.D.", "이는 자신들만의 고유한 특권이 침해당하는 듯 느낀 내과 의사들에게도, 그리고 리드게이트가 자신을 그 부류에 넣은 외과 겸 약제사들에게도 거슬리는 일이었다. 그리고 얼마 전까지만 해도 그들은, 런던에서 학위를 딴 의학 박사라고 자칭하지도 않으면서 감히 약값 외의 명목으로 보수를 요구하는 자에 맞서 법이 자기들 편이라고 기댈 수 있었다."], ["dared to ask for pay except as a charge on drugs. But Lydgate had not been experienced enough to foresee that his new course would be even more offensive to the laity; and to Mr.", "그러나 리드게이트는 자신의 새로운 방침이 일반 사람들에게는 더더욱 거슬리리라는 것을 내다볼 만큼 경험이 많지 않았다."], ["Mawmsey, an important grocer in the Top Market, who, though not one of his patients, questioned him in an affable manner on the subject, he was injudicious enough to give a hasty popular explanation of his reasons, pointing out to Mr.", "그래서 그의 환자도 아니면서 그 문제에 대해 상냥하게 그에게 물어 온, 윗 시장의 유력한 식료품상 마음지 씨에게, 그는 경솔하게도 자신의 이유를 성급하고 속된 방식으로 설명해 버렸다."], ["Mawmsey that it must lower the character of practitioners, and be a constant injury to the public, if their only mode of getting paid for their work was by their making out long bills for draughts, boluses, and mixtures.", "그는 마음지 씨에게, 의사들이 일한 대가를 받는 유일한 방법이 물약과 환약과 혼합제에 대해 긴 청구서를 작성하는 것뿐이라면, 그것은 틀림없이 의사들의 품격을 떨어뜨리고 대중에게 끊임없는 해가 되리라고 지적했다."]], [["“It is in that way that hard-working medical men may come to be almost as mischievous as quacks,”", "\"바로 그런 식으로 부지런히 일하는 의사들이 돌팔이만큼이나 해로운 존재가 될 수 있는 겁니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, rather thoughtlessly.", "리드게이트는 다소 생각 없이 말했다."], ["“To get their own bread they must overdose the king’s lieges; and that’s a bad sort of treason, Mr.", "\"제 밥벌이를 위해 그들은 임금님의 백성들에게 약을 과하게 먹여야 하지요."], ["Mawmsey—undermines the constitution in a fatal way.”", "그건 못된 종류의 반역입니다, 마음지 씨—치명적인 방식으로 국체를 좀먹는 짓이지요.\""]], [["Mr. Mawmsey was not only an overseer (it was about a question of outdoor pay that he was having an interview with Lydgate), he was also asthmatic and had an increasing family: thus, from a medical point of view, as well as from his own, he was an important man; indeed, an exceptional grocer, whose hair was arranged in a flame-like pyramid, and whose retail deference was of the cordial, encouraging kind—jocosely complimentary, and with a certain considerate abstinence from letting out the full force of his mind.", "마음지 씨는 빈민 구호 담당관이었을 뿐 아니라(그가 리드게이트와 면담하고 있던 것은 옥외 구호금에 관한 문제 때문이었다), 천식을 앓고 있었고 식구도 늘어 가고 있었다."], ["It was Mr.", "그리하여 의학적 관점에서나 그 자신의 관점에서나 그는 중요한 인물이었다."], ["Mawmsey’s friendly jocoseness in questioning him which had set the tone of Lydgate’s reply.", "실로 예외적인 식료품상이었으니, 머리카락을 불꽃 모양의 피라미드처럼 정돈했고, 소매상 특유의 깍듯함은 다정하고 격려하는 종류여서—농담조로 칭찬을 건네되, 자기 생각의 온전한 힘을 다 드러내지 않는 어떤 사려 깊은 절제가 있었다. 리드게이트의 대답에 그런 어조를 갖게 한 것은 그에게 질문을 던진 마음지 씨의 친근한 농담조였다."], ["But let the wise be warned against too great readiness at explanation: it multiplies the sources of mistake, lengthening the sum for reckoners sure to go wrong.", "그러나 현명한 자들은 너무 선뜻 설명하려 드는 일을 경계하라. 그것은 오해의 원천을 늘리고, 틀림없이 잘못 셈할 자들을 위해 셈해야 할 액수만 길게 늘려 놓을 뿐이니."]], [["Lydgate smiled as he ended his speech, putting his foot into the stirrup, and Mr. Mawmsey laughed more than he would have done if he had known who the king’s lieges were, giving his “Good morning, sir, good-morning, sir,”", "리드게이트는 말을 마치며 등자에 발을 걸고 미소 지었고, 마음지 씨는 임금님의 백성이 누구인지 알았더라면 그랬을 것보다 더 크게 웃으며 \"안녕히 가세요, 선생님, 안녕히 가세요, 선생님\""], ["with the air of one who saw everything clearly enough.", "하고 모든 것을 명확히 다 이해했다는 듯한 태도로 인사했다."], ["But in truth his views were perturbed.", "그러나 사실 그의 생각은 혼란스러웠다."], ["For years he had been paying bills with strictly made items, so that for every half-crown and eighteen-pence he was certain something measurable had been delivered.", "그는 여러 해 동안 항목이 엄밀히 적힌 청구서로 값을 치러 왔고, 그래서 반 크라운이나 일 실링 육 펜스마다 측정 가능한 무언가가 분명히 제공되었다고 확신해 왔다."], ["He had done this with satisfaction, including it among his responsibilities as a husband and father, and regarding a longer bill than usual as a dignity worth mentioning.", "그는 이 일을 만족스럽게 해 왔으며, 그것을 남편이자 아버지로서의 책무에 포함시켰고, 평소보다 긴 청구서를 언급할 만한 위엄으로 여겼다."], ["Moreover, in addition to the massive benefit of the drugs to “self and family,” he had enjoyed the pleasure of forming an acute judgment as to their immediate effects, so as to give an intelligent statement for the guidance of Mr.", "게다가 \"본인과 가족\"에게 약이 주는 막대한 혜택에 더하여, 그는 그 즉각적인 효과에 대해 예리한 판단을 내리는 즐거움을 누려 왔으니, 그리하여 갬빗 씨의 지침이 되도록 지적인 진술을 해 줄 수 있었다."], ["Gambit—a practitioner just a little lower in status than Wrench or Toller, and especially esteemed as an accoucheur, of whose ability Mr. Mawmsey had the poorest opinion on all other points, but in doctoring, he was wont to say in an undertone, he placed Gambit above any of them.", "갬빗 씨는 렌치나 톨러보다 지위가 조금 낮은 의사로, 특히 산파술 의사로 존중받았는데, 마음지 씨는 다른 모든 점에서는 그의 능력을 가장 낮게 평가했으나, 진료에 있어서는 그가 누구보다 갬빗을 위에 둔다고 낮은 목소리로 말하곤 했다."]], [["Here were deeper reasons than the superficial talk of a new man, which appeared still flimsier in the drawing-room over the shop, when they were recited to Mrs. Mawmsey, a woman accustomed to be made much of as a fertile mother,—generally under attendance more or less frequent from Mr.", "여기에는 새 의사의 피상적인 말보다 더 깊은 이유들이 있었으니, 그 말은 가게 위층 응접실에서 마음지 부인에게 되풀이될 때 한층 더 빈약하게 들렸다."], ["Gambit, and occasionally having attacks which required Dr. Minchin.", "마음지 부인은 다산의 어머니로서 떠받들어지는 데 익숙한 여인으로—대개 갬빗 씨의 잦은 진료를 받았고, 가끔은 민친 박사가 필요한 발작을 일으키곤 했다."]], [["“Does this Mr. Lydgate mean to say there is no use in taking medicine?”", "\"이 리드게이트 씨라는 사람은 약을 먹는 게 아무 소용없다고 말하려는 건가요?\""], ["said Mrs. Mawmsey, who was slightly given to drawling.", "약간 느릿느릿 말하는 버릇이 있는 마음지 부인이 말했다."], ["“I should like him to tell me how I could bear up at Fair time, if I didn’t take strengthening medicine for a month beforehand.", "\"장날에 한 달 전부터 미리 강장제를 먹지 않는다면 내가 어떻게 버틸 수 있는지 그 사람이 한번 말해 줬으면 좋겠네요."], ["Think of what I have to provide for calling customers, my dear!”—here Mrs. Mawmsey turned to an intimate female friend who sat by—“a large veal pie—a stuffed fillet—a round of beef—ham, tongue, et cetera, et cetera!", "손님들을 부르려면 내가 무엇을 마련해야 하는지 생각해 보세요, 여보!\"—여기서 마음지 부인은 곁에 앉은 친한 여자 친구에게로 몸을 돌렸다—\"커다란 송아지 고기 파이며, 속 채운 살코기며, 소고기 등심 한 덩이며, 햄, 혓바닥 고기, 기타 등등, 기타 등등을요!"], ["But what keeps me up best is the pink mixture, not the brown.", "하지만 나를 가장 잘 버티게 해 주는 건 분홍색 혼합제예요, 갈색이 아니라."], ["I wonder, Mr. Mawmsey, with your experience, you could have patience to listen.", "마음지 씨, 당신쯤 되는 경험을 가진 분이 그런 말을 참고 들어 줬다니 이상하네요."], ["I should have told him at once that I knew a little better than that.”", "나라면 그 자리에서 내가 그보다는 좀 더 잘 안다고 말해 줬을 거예요.\""]], [["“No, no, no,” said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\""], ["Mawmsey; “I was not going to tell him my opinion.", "마음지 씨가 말했다. \"나는 그에게 내 의견을 말할 생각이 없었어."], ["Hear everything and judge for yourself is my motto.", "모든 걸 듣고 스스로 판단하라, 그게 내 좌우명이지."], ["But he didn’t know who he was talking to.", "하지만 그는 자기가 누구한테 말하고 있는지 몰랐어."], ["I was not to be turned on his finger.", "나는 그자의 손가락에 놀아날 사람이 아니거든."], ["People often pretend to tell me things, when they might as well say, ‘Mawmsey, you’re a fool.’ But I smile at it: I humor everybody’s weak place.", "사람들은 흔히 나한테 뭔가 가르쳐 주는 척하는데, 그럴 바엔 차라리 '마음지, 당신은 바보야'라고 말하는 게 나을 거야. 하지만 나는 그저 빙그레 웃고 말지."], ["If physic had done harm to self and family, I should have found it out by this time.”", "나는 누구나의 약점을 받아 주거든. 약이 나 자신과 가족에게 해를 끼쳤다면 지금쯤은 알아챘을 거야.\""]], [["The next day Mr. Gambit was told that Lydgate went about saying physic was of no use.", "이튿날 갬빗 씨는 리드게이트가 약이 아무 소용없다고 떠들고 다닌다는 말을 들었다."]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"그래요!\""], ["said he, lifting his eyebrows with cautious surprise.", "그가 조심스러운 놀라움으로 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다."], ["(He was a stout husky man with a large ring on his fourth finger.) “How will he cure his patients, then?”", "(그는 넷째 손가락에 커다란 반지를 낀, 다부지고 쉰 목소리의 사내였다.) \"그럼 그자는 환자를 어떻게 고친답니까?\""]], [["“That is what I say,” returned Mrs.", "\"제 말이 바로 그거예요.\""], ["Mawmsey, who habitually gave weight to her speech by loading her pronouns.", "습관적으로 대명사에 힘을 실어 자기 말에 무게를 더하는 마음지 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["“Does he suppose that people will pay him only to come and sit with them and go away again?”", "\"그자는 사람들이 그저 곁에 와서 앉았다가 도로 가 버리는 값으로 돈을 낼 거라고 생각하는 걸까요?\""]], [["Mrs. Mawmsey had had a great deal of sitting from Mr.", "마음지 부인은 갬빗 씨에게서 곁에 앉아 있는 진료를 많이 받았는데, 거기에는 그 자신의 신체 습관이나 기타 일들에 대한 아주 상세한 이야기도 포함되어 있었다."], ["Gambit, including very full accounts of his own habits of body and other affairs; but of course he knew there was no innuendo in her remark, since his spare time and personal narrative had never been charged for.", "그러나 물론 그는 그녀의 말에 비꼬는 뜻이 없음을 알았으니, 그의 한가한 시간이나 신상 이야기에는 값이 매겨진 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["So he replied, humorously—", "그래서 그는 익살스럽게 대답했다."]], [["“Well, Lydgate is a good-looking young fellow, you know.”", "\"음, 리드게이트는 잘생긴 젊은이지요, 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["“Not one that I would employ,”", "\"내가 쓸 사람은 아니에요.\""], ["said Mrs. Mawmsey.", "마음지 부인이 말했다."], ["“Others may do as they please.”", "\"다른 사람들이야 좋을 대로 하라죠.\""]], [["Hence Mr. Gambit could go away from the chief grocer’s without fear of rivalry, but not without a sense that Lydgate was one of those hypocrites who try to discredit others by advertising their own honesty, and that it might be worth some people’s while to show him up.", "이리하여 갬빗 씨는 경쟁에 대한 두려움 없이 그 으뜸가는 식료품상의 집을 떠날 수 있었으나, 리드게이트가 자기 정직함을 광고함으로써 남들의 신용을 떨어뜨리려 드는 그런 위선자들 가운데 하나이며, 어떤 사람들에게는 그의 정체를 폭로하는 것이 수고를 들일 만한 일이 될지도 모른다는 느낌이 없지 않았다."], ["Mr. Gambit, however, had a satisfactory practice, much pervaded by the smells of retail trading which suggested the reduction of cash payments to a balance.", "그러나 갬빗 씨는 만족스러운 진료 일감을 가지고 있었는데, 거기에는 현금 지불을 차감 계산으로 줄이는 일을 떠올리게 하는 소매업의 냄새가 짙게 배어 있었다."], ["And he did not think it worth his while to show Lydgate up until he knew how.", "그리고 그는 어떻게 폭로할지를 알기 전까지는 리드게이트를 폭로하는 것이 수고를 들일 만한 일이라고 생각하지 않았다."], ["He had not indeed great resources of education, and had had to work his own way against a good deal of professional contempt; but he made none the worse accoucheur for calling the breathing apparatus “longs.”", "사실 그는 대단한 교육 밑천이 없어 적잖은 직업적 멸시에 맞서 제 힘으로 길을 헤쳐 와야 했지만, 호흡 기관을 \"허파\"라고 부른다고 해서 산파술 의사로서 못한 것은 결코 아니었다."]], [["Other medical men felt themselves more capable. Mr.", "다른 의사들은 자신이 더 유능하다고 느꼈다."], ["Toller shared the highest practice in the town and belonged to an old Middlemarch family: there were Tollers in the law and everything else above the line of retail trade.", "톨러 씨는 마을에서 가장 큰 진료 일감을 나눠 가졌으며 오래된 미들마치 가문 출신이었으니, 법조계를 비롯해 소매업 위쪽의 모든 분야에 톨러 집안 사람들이 있었다. 성마른 우리의 친구 렌치와는 달리, 그는 자신을 짜증나게 할 법한 일들을 세상없이 편안하게 받아들이는 사람이었다."], ["Unlike our irascible friend Wrench, he had the easiest way in the world of taking things which might be supposed to annoy him, being a well-bred, quietly facetious man, who kept a good house, was very fond of a little sporting when he could get it, very friendly with Mr. Hawley, and hostile to Mr.", "교양 있고 조용히 익살스러운 사내로, 좋은 집을 건사했고, 기회가 닿을 때면 소소한 사냥을 무척 즐겼으며, 홀리 씨와는 아주 친했고 불스트로드 씨에게는 적대적이었다."], ["Bulstrode. It may seem odd that with such pleasant habits he should have been given to the heroic treatment, bleeding and blistering and starving his patients, with a dispassionate disregard to his personal example; but the incongruity favored the opinion of his ability among his patients, who commonly observed that Mr.", "그토록 유쾌한 습성을 가진 사람이 영웅적 치료법, 곧 환자에게 피를 뽑고 물집을 잡고 굶기는 치료를 즐겼다는 것이, 그것도 자신의 본보기에는 냉정하게 아랑곳하지 않으면서 그랬다는 것이 이상해 보일지도 모른다."], ["Toller had lazy manners, but his treatment was as active as you could desire: no man, said they, carried more seriousness into his profession: he was a little slow in coming, but when he came, he did something.", "그러나 그 부조화가 환자들 사이에서 그의 능력에 대한 평판에 유리하게 작용했으니, 환자들은 흔히 톨러 씨가 태도는 게을러도 치료만큼은 누구라도 바랄 만큼 적극적이라고 말했다. 그만큼 자기 직업에 진지함을 쏟아붓는 사람이 없다고들 했다."], ["He was a great favorite in his own circle, and whatever he implied to any one’s disadvantage told doubly from his careless ironical tone.", "오는 것은 좀 굼떴어도 일단 오면 그는 정말 무언가를 했다는 것이다. 그는 자기 모임에서 대단한 인기인이었고, 누군가에게 불리한 암시를 흘릴 때면 그의 무심하고 반어적인 어조 탓에 그 효과가 두 배로 컸다."]], [["He naturally got tired of smiling and saying, “Ah!” when he was told that Mr.", "피콕 씨의 후계자가 약을 조제할 생각이 없다는 말을 들을 때마다 미소 지으며 \"허!\""], ["Peacock’s successor did not mean to dispense medicines; and Mr.", "하고 받아넘기는 데 그가 자연히 싫증을 내게 된 것은 당연했다."], ["Hackbutt one day mentioning it over the wine at a dinner-party, Mr.", "그래서 어느 날 만찬 자리에서 햄벗 씨가 포도주를 들며 그 얘기를 꺼냈을 때, 톨러 씨는 웃으며 말했다."], ["Toller said, laughingly, “Dibbitts will get rid of his stale drugs, then.", "\"그럼 디빗츠는 케케묵은 약을 처분하게 되겠군."], ["I’m fond of little Dibbitts—I’m glad he’s in luck.”", "나는 그 작은 디빗츠를 좋아하지—그 친구가 운이 트여서 기쁘네.\""]], [["“I see your meaning, Toller,” said Mr.", "\"무슨 뜻인지 알겠소, 톨러.\""], ["Hackbutt, “and I am entirely of your opinion.", "햄벗 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 나도 전적으로 같은 의견이오."], ["I shall take an opportunity of expressing myself to that effect.", "기회가 되는 대로 그 취지로 내 생각을 밝히겠소."], ["A medical man should be responsible for the quality of the drugs consumed by his patients.", "의사는 환자가 복용하는 약의 품질에 대해 책임을 져야 하오."], ["That is the rationale of the system of charging which has hitherto obtained; and nothing is more offensive than this ostentation of reform, where there is no real amelioration.”", "그것이 지금껏 통용되어 온 청구 제도의 근거지요. 그리고 실질적인 개선이라곤 없으면서 개혁이랍시고 과시하는 것보다 더 거슬리는 일은 없소.\""]], [["“Ostentation, Hackbutt?”", "\"과시라고요, 햄벗?\""], ["said Mr. Toller, ironically.", "톨러 씨가 반어적으로 말했다."], ["“I don’t see that.", "\"난 그렇게 보지 않소."], ["A man can’t very well be ostentatious of what nobody believes in.", "아무도 믿지 않는 것을 두고 과시할 수는 없는 노릇이니까."], ["There’s no reform in the matter: the question is, whether the profit on the drugs is paid to the medical man by the druggist or by the patient, and whether there shall be extra pay under the name of attendance.”", "그 문제에 개혁이란 건 없소. 문제는 약에서 나는 이익을 약제사가 의사에게 지불하느냐 환자가 지불하느냐, 그리고 진료라는 이름으로 별도의 보수가 있어야 하느냐 하는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["“Ah, to be sure; one of your damned new versions of old humbug,”", "\"아, 그렇고말고."], ["said Mr. Hawley, passing the decanter to Mr.", "케케묵은 헛소리를 새 판으로 짜낸 망할 놈의 변종 중 하나지.\""], ["Wrench.", "홀리 씨가 렌치 씨에게 술병을 건네며 말했다."]], [["Mr. Wrench, generally abstemious, often drank wine rather freely at a party, getting the more irritable in consequence.", "렌치 씨는 평소 술을 절제하는 편이었으나 모임에서는 곧잘 포도주를 꽤 자유롭게 마셨고, 그 결과 더욱 신경질적이 되었다."]], [["“As to humbug, Hawley,”", "\"헛소리라니, 홀리.\""], ["he said, “that’s a word easy to fling about.", "그가 말했다. \"그건 함부로 내던지기 쉬운 말이오."], ["But what I contend against is the way medical men are fouling their own nest, and setting up a cry about the country as if a general practitioner who dispenses drugs couldn’t be a gentleman.", "하지만 내가 맞서 싸우는 건, 의사들이 제 둥지를 더럽히고 온 고장에 떠들어 대는 그 방식이오. 마치 약을 조제하는 일반의는 신사가 될 수 없다는 듯이 말이오."], ["I throw back the imputation with scorn.", "나는 그런 누명을 경멸하며 되받아치겠소."], ["I say, the most ungentlemanly trick a man can be guilty of is to come among the members of his profession with innovations which are a libel on their time-honored procedure.", "한 사람이 저지를 수 있는 가장 신사답지 못한 짓은, 자기 직업 동료들 사이에 끼어들어 그들의 유서 깊은 관행을 모욕하는 혁신을 들고 나오는 것이라고 나는 말하겠소."], ["That is my opinion, and I am ready to maintain it against any one who contradicts me.” Mr.", "그게 내 의견이며, 나는 나에게 반박하는 그 누구에 맞서서라도 이를 지킬 각오가 되어 있소.\""], ["Wrench’s voice had become exceedingly sharp.", "렌치 씨의 목소리는 몹시 날카로워져 있었다."]], [["“I can’t oblige you there, Wrench,” said Mr.", "\"그건 내가 들어줄 수 없겠는걸, 렌치.\""], ["Hawley, thrusting his hands into his trouser-pockets.", "홀리 씨가 바지 주머니에 두 손을 찔러 넣으며 말했다."]], [["“My dear fellow,”", "\"여보게.\""], ["said Mr. Toller, striking in pacifically, and looking at Mr.", "톨러 씨가 화해시키려는 듯 끼어들며 렌치 씨를 바라보고 말했다."], ["Wrench, “the physicians have their toes trodden on more than we have.", "\"우리보다 발등을 더 밟히는 건 내과 의사들이지."], ["If you come to dignity it is a question for Minchin and Sprague.”", "위엄 문제를 따지자면 그건 민친과 스프레이그의 일일세.\""]], [["“Does medical jurisprudence provide nothing against these infringements?”", "\"이런 침해에 맞서 의료 법학이 마련해 둔 게 아무것도 없소?\""], ["said Mr. Hackbutt, with a disinterested desire to offer his lights.", "햄벗 씨가 자기 식견을 보태려는 사심 없는 욕심에서 말했다."], ["“How does the law stand, eh, Hawley?”", "\"법은 어떻게 되어 있소, 응, 홀리?\""]], [["“Nothing to be done there,”", "\"거기서는 할 수 있는 게 없소.\""], ["said Mr. Hawley.", "홀리 씨가 말했다."], ["“I looked into it for Sprague.", "\"스프레이그를 위해 알아봤었지."], ["You’d only break your nose against a damned judge’s decision.”", "망할 놈의 판사 판결에 코나 깨질 뿐이오.\""]], [["“Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["no need of law,”", "법은 필요 없소.\""], ["said Mr. Toller.", "톨러 씨가 말했다."], ["“So far as practice is concerned the attempt is an absurdity.", "\"진료에 관한 한 그런 시도는 어불성설이오."], ["No patient will like it—certainly not Peacock’s, who have been used to depletion.", "어떤 환자도 그걸 좋아하지 않을 테니까—사혈에 익숙해진 피콕의 환자들이야 더 말할 것도 없고."], ["Pass the wine.”", "술이나 돌리시오.\""]], [["Mr. Toller’s prediction was partly verified.", "톨러 씨의 예언은 부분적으로 들어맞았다."], ["If Mr. and Mrs.", "리드게이트를 쓸 생각이 전혀 없던 마음지 씨 부부조차 약에 반대한다는 그의 추정된 선언에 불안해졌으니, 그를 불러 쓰는 사람들이 그가 그 환자의 경우에 \"쓸 수 있는 모든 수단을 다 쓰는지\""], ["Mawmsey, who had no idea of employing Lydgate, were made uneasy by his supposed declaration against drugs, it was inevitable that those who called him in should watch a little anxiously to see whether he did “use all the means he might use” in the case.", "다소 노심초사하며 지켜보는 것은 불가피한 일이었다."], ["Even good Mr. Powderell, who in his constant charity of interpretation was inclined to esteem Lydgate the more for what seemed a conscientious pursuit of a better plan, had his mind disturbed with doubts during his wife’s attack of erysipelas, and could not abstain from mentioning to Lydgate that Mr.", "매사를 너그럽게 해석하는 한결같은 자비심에서 더 나은 방침을 양심적으로 추구하는 듯한 점 때문에 리드게이트를 더욱 높이 평가하는 쪽이었던 선량한 파우더렐 씨조차도, 아내가 단독에 걸렸을 때 의혹으로 마음이 어지러웠다."], ["Peacock on a similar occasion had administered a series of boluses which were not otherwise definable than by their remarkable effect in bringing Mrs.", "그래서 그는 리드게이트에게, 피콕 씨가 비슷한 경우에 환약을 연이어 처방했었는데, 그 환약이란 유난히 무더웠던 팔월에 시작된 병에서 파우더렐 부인을 성 미카엘 축일 전에 일으켜 세운 그 놀라운 효과 말고는 달리 설명할 길이 없는 것이었다고 말하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Powderell round before Michaelmas from an illness which had begun in a remarkably hot August. At last, indeed, in the conflict between his desire not to hurt Lydgate and his anxiety that no “means”", "마침내, 리드게이트를 상하게 하고 싶지 않은 마음과 어떤 \"수단\"도 빠뜨리지 말아야 한다는 불안 사이의 갈등 속에서, 그는 아내를 몰래 설득해 위전의 정화 환약을 먹게 했다."], ["should be lacking, he induced his wife privately to take Widgeon’s Purifying Pills, an esteemed Middlemarch medicine, which arrested every disease at the fountain by setting to work at once upon the blood.", "미들마치에서 높이 평가받는 약으로, 곧장 피에 작용함으로써 모든 병을 근원에서 막아 준다는 것이었다."], ["This co-operative measure was not to be mentioned to Lydgate, and Mr. Powderell himself had no certain reliance on it, only hoping that it might be attended with a blessing.", "이 협력적 조치는 리드게이트에게 알려서는 안 되는 것이었고, 파우더렐 씨 자신도 그것을 확실히 믿지는 못했으며, 그저 거기에 축복이 따르기를 바랄 뿐이었다."]], [["But in this doubtful stage of Lydgate’s introduction he was helped by what we mortals rashly call good fortune.", "그러나 리드게이트가 자리를 잡아 가는 이 미심쩍은 단계에서, 그는 우리 인간이 경솔하게도 행운이라 부르는 것의 도움을 받았다."], ["I suppose no doctor ever came newly to a place without making cures that surprised somebody—cures which may be called fortune’s testimonials, and deserve as much credit as the written or printed kind.", "짐작건대 어떤 의사도 새로 어딘가에 와서 누군가를 놀라게 하는 치료를 해내지 않고서 자리 잡은 적은 없을 것이다—그런 치료는 행운의 추천서라 부를 만하며, 글로 쓰거나 인쇄된 추천서 못지않은 신뢰를 받을 만하다."], ["Various patients got well while Lydgate was attending them, some even of dangerous illnesses; and it was remarked that the new doctor with his new ways had at least the merit of bringing people back from the brink of death.", "여러 환자가 리드게이트의 진료를 받는 동안 나았으니, 그중에는 위험한 병을 앓던 이들도 있었다. 그래서 새로운 방식을 쓰는 새 의사가 적어도 사람들을 죽음의 문턱에서 되돌려 놓는 공만큼은 있다는 평이 돌았다."], ["The trash talked on such occasions was the more vexatious to Lydgate, because it gave precisely the sort of prestige which an incompetent and unscrupulous man would desire, and was sure to be imputed to him by the simmering dislike of the other medical men as an encouragement on his own part of ignorant puffing.", "그런 경우에 오가는 허튼소리는 리드게이트에게 더욱 짜증스러웠으니, 그것은 무능하고 양심 없는 자가 바랄 법한 바로 그런 종류의 명성을 안겨 주었고, 또 다른 의사들의 부글거리는 반감 탓에 그가 무지한 허풍을 스스로 부추긴 것으로 틀림없이 그에게 덮어씌워질 것이었기 때문이다."], ["But even his proud outspokenness was checked by the discernment that it was as useless to fight against the interpretations of ignorance as to whip the fog; and “good fortune”", "그러나 그의 자존심 강한 직설조차도, 무지의 해석과 싸우는 것은 안개를 채찍질하는 것만큼이나 부질없다는 통찰 앞에서 멈칫했다."], ["insisted on using those interpretations.", "게다가 \"행운\"은 한사코 그 해석들을 써먹으려 들었다."]], [["Mrs. Larcher having just become charitably concerned about alarming symptoms in her charwoman, when Dr.", "라처 부인이 마침 자기네 잡역부에게 나타난 심상찮은 증상을 자비롭게 걱정하던 차에 민친 박사가 들렀고, 그녀는 그 자리에서 바로 그 여자를 봐 달라고, 그리고 진료소에 보낼 진단서를 써 달라고 청했다."], ["Minchin called, asked him to see her then and there, and to give her a certificate for the Infirmary; whereupon after examination he wrote a statement of the case as one of tumor, and recommended the bearer Nancy Nash as an out-patient.", "그리하여 진찰을 마친 뒤 그는 그 병을 종양으로 진단하는 소견서를 작성하고, 그것을 지닌 낸시 내시를 외래 환자로 추천했다. 낸시는 진료소로 가는 길에 집에 들러, 자기가 다락방을 세 들어 사는 코르셋 제조공과 그 아내에게 민친 박사의 소견서를 읽게 했고, 그리하여 처치야드 레인의 인근 가게들에서 동정 어린 화젯거리가 되었다."], ["Nancy, calling at home on her way to the Infirmary, allowed the stay maker and his wife, in whose attic she lodged, to read Dr.", "처음에는 오리 알만큼 크고 단단한 종양이라더니, 그날 하루가 저물어 갈 무렵에는 \"주먹\"만 한 크기라고들 했다."], ["Minchin’s paper, and by this means became a subject of compassionate conversation in the neighboring shops of Churchyard Lane as being afflicted with a tumor at first declared to be as large and hard as a duck’s egg, but later in the day to be about the size of “your fist.” Most hearers agreed that it would have to be cut out, but one had known of oil and another of “squitchineal”", "듣는 이들 대부분은 그것을 잘라 내야 하리라는 데 동의했으나, 한 사람은 기름이, 또 한 사람은 \"코치닐\"이 몸속의 어떤 멍울이든 충분히 안에 넣고 먹으면 무르게 하고 줄어들게 하는 데 효험이 있다고 알고 있었다—기름은 차츰 \"물러지게\""], ["as adequate to soften and reduce any lump in the body when taken enough of into the inside—the oil by gradually “soopling,” the squitchineal by eating away.", "하고, 코치닐은 갉아먹어 들어가는 식으로 말이다."]], [["Meanwhile when Nancy presented herself at the Infirmary, it happened to be one of Lydgate’s days there.", "한편 낸시가 진료소에 모습을 드러낸 날은 마침 리드게이트가 그곳에 나오는 날 중 하루였다. 그녀에게 이것저것 묻고 진찰한 뒤, 리드게이트는 입주 외과의에게 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["After questioning and examining her, Lydgate said to the house-surgeon in an undertone, “It’s not tumor: it’s cramp.”", "\"종양이 아니야. 경련이야.\""], ["He ordered her a blister and some steel mixture, and told her to go home and rest, giving her at the same time a note to Mrs. Larcher, who, she said, was her best employer, to testify that she was in need of good food.", "그는 그녀에게 물집 고약과 철분 혼합제를 처방하고, 집에 가서 쉬라고 일렀으며, 동시에 자기가 최고의 고용주라고 한 라처 부인에게 그녀가 좋은 음식이 필요한 처지임을 증명하는 쪽지를 써 주었다."]], [["But by-and-by Nancy, in her attic, became portentously worse, the supposed tumor having indeed given way to the blister, but only wandered to another region with angrier pain.", "그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 다락방의 낸시는 심상찮게 더 나빠졌다. 종양으로 추정되던 것이 과연 물집 고약 앞에서 물러가기는 했으나, 그저 더 사나운 통증과 함께 다른 부위로 옮겨 갔을 뿐이었다."], ["The staymaker’s wife went to fetch Lydgate, and he continued for a fortnight to attend Nancy in her own home, until under his treatment she got quite well and went to work again.", "코르셋 제조공의 아내가 리드게이트를 데리러 갔고, 그는 보름 동안 낸시의 집을 다니며 그녀를 돌보았으니, 마침내 그의 치료 아래 그녀는 완전히 나아 다시 일하러 나갔다."], ["But the case continued to be described as one of tumor in Churchyard Lane and other streets—nay, by Mrs.", "그러나 그 병은 처치야드 레인과 다른 거리들에서 여전히 종양 사례로 이야기되었으며—아니, 라처 부인까지도 그렇게 말했다."], ["Larcher also; for when Lydgate’s remarkable cure was mentioned to Dr. Minchin, he naturally did not like to say, “The case was not one of tumor, and I was mistaken in describing it as such,”", "리드게이트의 놀라운 치료가 민친 박사에게 언급되었을 때, 그는 당연히 \"그건 종양이 아니었고, 내가 그렇게 진단한 것은 잘못이었소\"라고 말하고 싶지 않았으므로 이렇게 대답했다."], ["but answered, “Indeed!", "\"그래요!"], ["ah!", "아!"], ["I saw it was a surgical case, not of a fatal kind.”", "나는 그게 외과적 사례지 치명적인 종류는 아니라고 봤지요.\""], ["He had been inwardly annoyed, however, when he had asked at the Infirmary about the woman he had recommended two days before, to hear from the house-surgeon, a youngster who was not sorry to vex Minchin with impunity, exactly what had occurred: he privately pronounced that it was indecent in a general practitioner to contradict a physician’s diagnosis in that open manner, and afterwards agreed with Wrench that Lydgate was disagreeably inattentive to etiquette.", "그러나 그는 이틀 전에 자기가 추천했던 그 여자에 대해 진료소에 물어보았을 때, 거리낌 없이 민친을 골탕 먹이는 것을 마다하지 않는 젊은 입주 외과의로부터 일어난 일을 그대로 전해 듣고는 속으로 언짢았다. 그는 일반의가 그런 식으로 대놓고 내과 의사의 진단을 반박하는 것은 점잖지 못한 짓이라고 사사로이 단정했고, 나중에 렌치와 함께 리드게이트가 예법에 거슬릴 만큼 무신경하다는 데 의견을 같이했다."], ["Lydgate did not make the affair a ground for valuing himself or (very particularly) despising Minchin, such rectification of misjudgments often happening among men of equal qualifications.", "리드게이트는 이 일을 가지고 자만하거나 (특별히) 민친을 멸시할 거리로 삼지 않았으니, 이런 오진의 정정은 동등한 자격을 가진 사람들 사이에서도 흔히 일어나는 일이었기 때문이다."], ["But report took up this amazing case of tumor, not clearly distinguished from cancer, and considered the more awful for being of the wandering sort; till much prejudice against Lydgate’s method as to drugs was overcome by the proof of his marvellous skill in the speedy restoration of Nancy Nash after she had been rolling and rolling in agonies from the presence of a tumor both hard and obstinate, but nevertheless compelled to yield.", "그러나 소문은 암과 명확히 구별되지 않는 이 놀라운 종양 사례를 물고 늘어졌고, 그것이 옮겨 다니는 종류라는 점 때문에 한층 더 무서운 것으로 여겨졌다. 그리하여 단단하고 고집스러운 종양 때문에 고통 속에서 데굴데굴 구르던 낸시 내시를, 그래도 끝내 굴복할 수밖에 없게 만들어 신속히 회복시킨 그의 경이로운 솜씨가 입증되자, 약에 관한 리드게이트의 방식에 대한 많은 편견이 극복되었다."]], [["How could Lydgate help himself?", "리드게이트인들 어쩌겠는가?"], ["It is offensive to tell a lady when she is expressing her amazement at your skill, that she is altogether mistaken and rather foolish in her amazement.", "한 부인이 당신의 솜씨에 놀라움을 표하고 있을 때, 그녀가 완전히 잘못 알고 있으며 그 놀라움이 다소 어리석다고 말하는 것은 무례한 일이다."], ["And to have entered into the nature of diseases would only have added to his breaches of medical propriety.", "그리고 병의 본질에 대해 설명에 들어가는 것은 그의 의료적 예법 위반을 더할 뿐이었으리라."], ["Thus he had to wince under a promise of success given by that ignorant praise which misses every valid quality.", "그리하여 그는 온갖 타당한 자질을 죄다 놓쳐 버리는 그 무지한 칭찬이 안겨 준 성공의 약속 아래서 움찔거려야 했다."]], [["In the case of a more conspicuous patient, Mr. Borthrop Trumbull, Lydgate was conscious of having shown himself something better than an every-day doctor, though here too it was an equivocal advantage that he won.", "좀 더 눈에 띄는 환자인 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 경우, 리드게이트는 자신이 그저 그런 보통 의사보다 나은 무언가를 보여 주었다는 자각이 있었으나, 여기서도 그가 얻은 것은 모호한 이점이었다."], ["The eloquent auctioneer was seized with pneumonia, and having been a patient of Mr. Peacock’s, sent for Lydgate, whom he had expressed his intention to patronize.", "그 달변의 경매인은 폐렴에 걸렸는데, 피콕 씨의 환자였던지라 리드게이트를 불렀으니, 그는 진작 리드게이트를 후원하겠다는 뜻을 밝혀 둔 터였다."], ["Mr Trumbull was a robust man, a good subject for trying the expectant theory upon—watching the course of an interesting disease when left as much as possible to itself, so that the stages might be noted for future guidance; and from the air with which he described his sensations Lydgate surmised that he would like to be taken into his medical man’s confidence, and be represented as a partner in his own cure.", "트럼불 씨는 건장한 사람이라, 흥미로운 병의 경과를 되도록 자연에 맡겨 두고 지켜보면서 앞으로의 지침을 위해 그 단계들을 기록하는 대기 요법을 시험해 보기에 좋은 대상이었다. 그리고 자기 증상을 묘사하는 태도로 보아, 리드게이트는 그가 자신의 의사로부터 신뢰를 받고 자기 자신의 치료에 동업자로 내세워지기를 바라리라고 짐작했다."], ["The auctioneer heard, without much surprise, that his was a constitution which (always with due watching) might be left to itself, so as to offer a beautiful example of a disease with all its phases seen in clear delineation, and that he probably had the rare strength of mind voluntarily to become the test of a rational procedure, and thus make the disorder of his pulmonary functions a general benefit to society.", "경매인은 자신의 체질이 (언제나 적절히 지켜본다는 조건 아래) 자연에 맡겨 두어도 좋을 만한 것이라, 병의 모든 국면이 명확한 윤곽으로 드러나는 아름다운 본보기를 제공할 수 있다는 말을, 또 자신이 자발적으로 합리적 치료의 시험 대상이 되어 자기 폐 기능의 장애를 사회 전체의 이익으로 삼을 만한 보기 드문 정신력을 아마도 지니고 있으리라는 말을 그다지 놀라지 않고 들었다."]], [["Mr. Trumbull acquiesced at once, and entered strongly into the view that an illness of his was no ordinary occasion for medical science.", "트럼불 씨는 즉시 수긍했으며, 자신의 병은 의학에 보통의 기회가 아니라는 견해에 열렬히 찬동했다."]], [["“Never fear, sir; you are not speaking to one who is altogether ignorant of the vis medicatrix,”", "\"염려 마십시오, 선생. 당신은 자연 치유력에 대해 전혀 무지한 사람과 이야기하고 있는 게 아니니까요.\""], ["said he, with his usual superiority of expression, made rather pathetic by difficulty of breathing.", "그가 늘 그렇듯 격조 있는 말투로 말했는데, 숨쉬기가 힘들어 그 말투가 다소 애처롭게 느껴졌다."], ["And he went without shrinking through his abstinence from drugs, much sustained by application of the thermometer which implied the importance of his temperature, by the sense that he furnished objects for the microscope, and by learning many new words which seemed suited to the dignity of his secretions.", "그리고 그는 약을 끊는 것을 움츠림 없이 견뎌 냈으니, 자기 체온의 중요성을 암시해 주는 체온계의 사용과, 자신이 현미경에 들여다볼 대상을 제공한다는 의식, 그리고 자기 분비물의 위엄에 어울려 보이는 많은 새 단어들을 배우는 데서 크게 힘을 얻었다."], ["For Lydgate was acute enough to indulge him with a little technical talk.", "리드게이트가 그를 약간의 전문적인 이야기로 즐겁게 해 줄 만큼 약삭빨랐기 때문이다."]], [["It may be imagined that Mr. Trumbull rose from his couch with a disposition to speak of an illness in which he had manifested the strength of his mind as well as constitution; and he was not backward in awarding credit to the medical man who had discerned the quality of patient he had to deal with.", "트럼불 씨가 침상에서 일어났을 때, 자신이 체질뿐 아니라 정신력까지 발휘한 병에 대해 이야기하고 싶어 했으리라는 것은 능히 짐작할 만하다."], ["The auctioneer was not an ungenerous man, and liked to give others their due, feeling that he could afford it. He had caught the words “expectant method,”", "그리고 그는 자기가 상대해야 할 환자의 자질을 알아본 그 의사에게 공을 돌리는 데 인색하지 않았다."], ["and rang chimes on this and other learned phrases to accompany the assurance that Lydgate “knew a thing or two more than the rest of the doctors—was far better versed in the secrets of his profession than the majority of his compeers.”", "경매인은 인색한 사람이 아니었고, 남에게 마땅한 몫을 주기를 좋아했으니, 자기에게 그럴 여유가 있다고 느꼈기 때문이다. 그는 \"대기 요법\"이라는 말을 주워들어, 리드게이트가 \"다른 의사들보다 한두 가지는 더 안다—직업의 비밀에 있어서 동료 대다수보다 훨씬 더 정통하다\"는 보증과 더불어, 이 말과 다른 유식한 어구들을 종처럼 울려 댔다."]], [["This had happened before the affair of Fred Vincy’s illness had given to Mr. Wrench’s enmity towards Lydgate more definite personal ground.", "이 일은 프레드 빈시의 병이 리드게이트에 대한 렌치 씨의 적의에 더 분명한 개인적 근거를 주기 전에 일어났다."], ["The new-comer already threatened to be a nuisance in the shape of rivalry, and was certainly a nuisance in the shape of practical criticism or reflections on his hard-driven elders, who had had something else to do than to busy themselves with untried notions.", "이 새내기는 이미 경쟁의 형태로 골칫거리가 될 조짐을 보였고, 검증되지 않은 관념에 매달리느니 다른 할 일이 있던 고생에 찌든 선배들에 대한 실질적 비판이나 논평의 형태로는 분명 골칫거리였다."], ["His practice had spread in one or two quarters, and from the first the report of his high family had led to his being pretty generally invited, so that the other medical men had to meet him at dinner in the best houses; and having to meet a man whom you dislike is not observed always to end in a mutual attachment.", "그의 진료는 한두 군데에서 퍼져 나갔고, 처음부터 그의 명문가 출신이라는 소문 덕에 그는 두루 초대를 받았으니, 그리하여 다른 의사들도 좋은 집안의 만찬 자리에서 그를 마주쳐야 했다. 그런데 싫어하는 사람을 마주쳐야 하는 일이 언제나 서로의 애착으로 끝나는 것은 아니라고 흔히들 본다."], ["There was hardly ever so much unanimity among them as in the opinion that Lydgate was an arrogant young fellow, and yet ready for the sake of ultimately predominating to show a crawling subservience to Bulstrode.", "리드게이트가 오만한 젊은이라는 의견만큼 그들 사이에 의견 일치를 본 적이 거의 없었으니, 그러면서도 그가 결국 우위를 점하기 위해 불스트로드에게 비굴할 만큼 알랑거릴 준비가 되어 있다는 점에서도 그러했다."], ["That Mr. Farebrother, whose name was a chief flag of the anti-Bulstrode party, always defended Lydgate and made a friend of him, was referred to Farebrother’s unaccountable way of fighting on both sides.", "반反불스트로드 파의 주된 깃발과도 같은 이름을 가진 페어브라더 씨가 늘 리드게이트를 변호하고 그를 벗으로 삼은 것은, 양편에서 싸우는 페어브라더 특유의 알 수 없는 방식 탓으로 돌려졌다."]], [["Here was plenty of preparation for the outburst of professional disgust at the announcement of the laws Mr.", "여기에는 불스트로드 씨가 새 병원의 운영을 위해 정해 둔 규칙들이 발표되었을 때 직업적 반감이 폭발할 만한 준비가 충분히 갖춰져 있었다. 그 규칙들은 더욱 격분을 자아냈으니, 그의 의지와 뜻에 당장 간섭할 길이 없었기 때문이다."], ["Bulstrode was laying down for the direction of the New Hospital, which were the more exasperating because there was no present possibility of interfering with his will and pleasure, everybody except Lord Medlicote having refused help towards the building, on the ground that they preferred giving to the Old Infirmary. Mr.", "메들리코트 경을 제외한 모두가 건물 건립에 도움을 거절했는데, 옛 진료소에 기부하는 쪽을 택했다는 이유에서였다."], ["Bulstrode met all the expenses, and had ceased to be sorry that he was purchasing the right to carry out his notions of improvement without hindrance from prejudiced coadjutors; but he had had to spend large sums, and the building had lingered.", "불스트로드 씨는 모든 비용을 댔으며, 편견에 사로잡힌 협력자들의 방해 없이 자기 개량 구상을 실행할 권리를 사들이는 셈인 것을 더는 유감으로 여기지 않게 되었다. 그러나 그는 거액을 써야 했고, 건물은 더디게 지어졌다."], ["Caleb Garth had undertaken it, had failed during its progress, and before the interior fittings were begun had retired from the management of the business; and when referring to the Hospital he often said that however Bulstrode might ring if you tried him, he liked good solid carpentry and masonry, and had a notion both of drains and chimneys.", "케일럽 가스가 그 일을 맡았다가 진행 도중에 실패했고, 내부 마감 공사가 시작되기 전에 사업 관리에서 물러났다. 그리고 병원 이야기가 나오면 그는 흔히, 불스트로드가 시험해 보면 어떻게 쩌렁쩌렁 울려 댈지는 몰라도, 자기는 견실하고 튼튼한 목공과 석공 일을 좋아하며 배수로와 굴뚝에 대해서도 일가견이 있다고 말하곤 했다."], ["In fact, the Hospital had become an object of intense interest to Bulstrode, and he would willingly have continued to spare a large yearly sum that he might rule it dictatorially without any Board; but he had another favorite object which also required money for its accomplishment: he wished to buy some land in the neighborhood of Middlemarch, and therefore he wished to get considerable contributions towards maintaining the Hospital.", "사실 병원은 불스트로드에게 지대한 관심의 대상이 되어 있었고, 그는 어떤 이사회도 없이 독단적으로 그것을 다스리기 위해서라면 기꺼이 매년 거액을 계속 내놓을 용의가 있었다. 그러나 그에게는 그 성취를 위해 역시 돈이 필요한 또 다른 애착의 대상이 있었으니, 미들마치 근방의 땅을 좀 사들이고 싶었던 것이다."], ["Meanwhile he framed his plan of management.", "그래서 그는 병원 유지를 위한 상당한 기부금을 얻어 내고자 했다. 한편 그는 운영 계획을 짰다."], ["The Hospital was to be reserved for fever in all its forms; Lydgate was to be chief medical superintendent, that he might have free authority to pursue all comparative investigations which his studies, particularly in Paris, had shown him the importance of, the other medical visitors having a consultative influence, but no power to contravene Lydgate’s ultimate decisions; and the general management was to be lodged exclusively in the hands of five directors associated with Mr.", "병원은 모든 형태의 열병을 위해 전용될 것이었다. 리드게이트가 의무 총책임자가 되어, 그의 연구가, 특히 파리에서의 연구가 그 중요성을 일깨워 준 온갖 비교 조사를 자유로이 추진할 권한을 갖게 하고, 다른 방문 의사들은 자문의 영향력은 가지되 리드게이트의 최종 결정을 뒤집을 권한은 없도록 했다."], ["Bulstrode, who were to have votes in the ratio of their contributions, the Board itself filling up any vacancy in its numbers, and no mob of small contributors being admitted to a share of government.", "그리고 전반적 관리는 불스트로드 씨와 결합된 다섯 이사의 손에 전적으로 맡겨졌으니, 그들은 기부액에 비례하여 표결권을 가졌고, 이사회 스스로가 결원을 채우며, 소액 기부자 무리는 운영에 참여하지 못하도록 했다."]], [["There was an immediate refusal on the part of every medical man in the town to become a visitor at the Fever Hospital.", "마을의 모든 의사가 즉각 열병 병원의 방문 의사가 되기를 거부했다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate to Mr.", "리드게이트가 불스트로드 씨에게 말했다."], ["Bulstrode, “we have a capital house-surgeon and dispenser, a clear-headed, neat-handed fellow; we’ll get Webbe from Crabsley, as good a country practitioner as any of them, to come over twice a-week, and in case of any exceptional operation, Protheroe will come from Brassing.", "\"우리에게는 명민하고 손놀림이 야무진 훌륭한 입주 외과의 겸 조제사가 있습니다. 크랩슬리에서 웨브를 데려오면 되겠지요."], ["I must work the harder, that’s all, and I have given up my post at the Infirmary.", "그들 중 누구 못지않게 유능한 시골 의사니, 일주일에 두 번 건너오게 하고, 무슨 예외적인 수술이 있을 경우엔 브래싱에서 프로서로를 부르면 됩니다. 저야 더 열심히 일하면 되는 거지요, 그뿐입니다."], ["The plan will flourish in spite of them, and then they’ll be glad to come in.", "진료소 자리는 그만두었습니다. 그들이 어쩌든 이 계획은 번창할 것이고, 그러면 그들도 기꺼이 들어오려 할 겁니다."], ["Things can’t last as they are: there must be all sorts of reform soon, and then young fellows may be glad to come and study here.”", "일이 지금 이대로 갈 수는 없습니다. 머지않아 온갖 개혁이 일어날 테고, 그러면 젊은이들이 여기 와서 기꺼이 공부하려 들 겁니다.\""], ["Lydgate was in high spirits.", "리드게이트는 의기양양했다."]], [["“I shall not flinch, you may depend upon it, Mr. Lydgate,”", "\"내가 물러서는 일은 결코 없을 거요, 그것은 믿어도 좋소, 리드게이트 씨.\""], ["said Mr. Bulstrode.", "불스트로드 씨가 말했다."], ["“While I see you carrying out high intentions with vigor, you shall have my unfailing support.", "\"당신이 높은 뜻을 힘차게 펴 나가는 것을 보는 한, 당신은 나의 변함없는 지지를 받을 거요."], ["And I have humble confidence that the blessing which has hitherto attended my efforts against the spirit of evil in this town will not be withdrawn.", "그리고 나는 이 마을의 악의 정신에 맞선 내 노력에 지금껏 함께해 온 축복이 거두어지지 않으리라고 겸허히 확신하오."], ["Suitable directors to assist me I have no doubt of securing. Mr.", "나를 도와줄 적절한 이사들을 확보하는 것은 의심치 않소."], ["Brooke of Tipton has already given me his concurrence, and a pledge to contribute yearly: he has not specified the sum—probably not a great one.", "팁턴의 브룩 씨가 이미 동의해 주었고, 매년 기부하겠다는 약속도 했소. 액수를 명시하지는 않았는데—아마 큰 액수는 아닐 거요."], ["But he will be a useful member of the board.”", "하지만 그는 이사회의 쓸모 있는 일원이 될 거요.\""]], [["A useful member was perhaps to be defined as one who would originate nothing, and always vote with Mr. Bulstrode.", "쓸모 있는 일원이란 어쩌면 아무것도 발의하지 않으면서 늘 불스트로드 씨에게 표를 던지는 사람이라고 정의할 수 있으리라."]], [["The medical aversion to Lydgate was hardly disguised now. Neither Dr.", "이제 리드게이트에 대한 의료계의 반감은 거의 감춰지지 않았다."], ["Sprague nor Dr. Minchin said that he disliked Lydgate’s knowledge, or his disposition to improve treatment: what they disliked was his arrogance, which nobody felt to be altogether deniable.", "스프레이그 박사도 민친 박사도 리드게이트의 지식이나 치료를 개선하려는 그의 성향을 싫어한다고는 말하지 않았다."], ["They implied that he was insolent, pretentious, and given to that reckless innovation for the sake of noise and show which was the essence of the charlatan.", "그들이 싫어한 것은 그의 오만이었으니, 그것은 아무도 전적으로 부인할 수 없다고 느끼는 바였다. 그들은 그가 무례하고 잘난 체하며, 돌팔이의 본질인 그 소란과 과시를 위한 무모한 혁신에 빠져 있다고 넌지시 일렀다."]], [["The word charlatan once thrown on the air could not be let drop. In those days the world was agitated about the wondrous doings of Mr. St. John Long, “noblemen and gentlemen” attesting his extraction of a fluid like mercury from the temples of a patient.", "돌팔이라는 말이 일단 허공에 던져지자 그것은 떨어진 채로 둘 수가 없었다. 그 무렵 세상은 세인트 존 롱 씨의 경이로운 행적으로 떠들썩했으니, \"귀족과 신사들\"이 그가 환자의 관자놀이에서 수은 같은 액체를 뽑아냈다고 증언하고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Toller remarked one day, smilingly, to Mrs.", "톨러 씨는 어느 날 태프트 부인에게 웃으며 말했다."], ["Taft, that “Bulstrode had found a man to suit him in Lydgate; a charlatan in religion is sure to like other sorts of charlatans.”", "\"불스트로드가 리드게이트라는, 자기한테 딱 맞는 사람을 찾아낸 거지요. 종교의 돌팔이는 다른 종류의 돌팔이도 으레 좋아하게 마련이니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, indeed, I can imagine,” said Mrs.", "\"네, 정말, 그럴 만하지요.\""], ["Taft, keeping the number of thirty stitches carefully in her mind all the while; “there are so many of that sort.", "그러는 내내 서른 코의 뜨개질 수를 머릿속에 조심스레 세고 있던 태프트 부인이 말했다. \"그런 부류가 어찌나 많은지요."], ["I remember Mr. Cheshire, with his irons, trying to make people straight when the Almighty had made them crooked.”", "전능하신 하느님이 굽게 만드신 사람들을, 쇠붙이로 곧게 펴 보겠다던 체셔 씨가 기억나네요.\""]], [["“No, no,” said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니에요.\""], ["Toller, “Cheshire was all right—all fair and above board.", "톨러 씨가 말했다. \"체셔는 멀쩡했어요—다 정당하고 떳떳했지요."], ["But there’s St. John Long—that’s the kind of fellow we call a charlatan, advertising cures in ways nobody knows anything about: a fellow who wants to make a noise by pretending to go deeper than other people.", "하지만 세인트 존 롱 같은 자—그게 바로 우리가 돌팔이라고 부르는 부류예요."], ["The other day he was pretending to tap a man’s brain and get quicksilver out of it.”", "아무도 영문을 모르는 방식으로 치료를 광고하고, 남들보다 더 깊이 파고드는 척해서 소란을 피우려는 자 말입니다. 며칠 전에는 사람 뇌를 두드려서 수은을 뽑아낸다고 우겼다지 뭡니까.\""]], [["“Good gracious!", "\"세상에!"], ["what dreadful trifling with people’s constitutions!”", "사람의 몸을 가지고 무슨 그런 끔찍한 장난을!\""], ["said Mrs. Taft.", "태프트 부인이 말했다."]], [["After this, it came to be held in various quarters that Lydgate played even with respectable constitutions for his own purposes, and how much more likely that in his flighty experimenting he should make sixes and sevens of hospital patients.", "이 일이 있은 뒤로, 리드게이트가 점잖은 사람들의 몸까지 제 목적을 위해 가지고 논다는 생각이 여기저기서 굳어졌고, 그렇다면 그가 변덕스러운 실험을 하면서 병원 환자들을 엉망진창으로 만들 가능성은 또 얼마나 크겠는가."], ["Especially it was to be expected, as the landlady of the Tankard had said, that he would recklessly cut up their dead bodies. For Lydgate having attended Mrs.", "특히 탱커드의 여주인이 말했듯이, 그가 무모하게 그들의 시신을 해부하리라는 것은 충분히 예상할 만한 일이었다."], ["Goby, who died apparently of a heart-disease not very clearly expressed in the symptoms, too daringly asked leave of her relatives to open the body, and thus gave an offence quickly spreading beyond Parley Street, where that lady had long resided on an income such as made this association of her body with the victims of Burke and Hare a flagrant insult to her memory.", "리드게이트가 고비 부인을 돌보았는데, 그녀는 증상에 그리 명확히 드러나지 않은 심장병으로 죽은 듯했고, 그가 너무 대담하게도 유족에게 시신 해부를 허락해 달라고 청한 탓에, 그 일은 팔리 스트리트 너머로 빠르게 번지는 반감을 자아냈다. 그 부인은 오래도록 그곳에서 상당한 수입으로 살아왔으므로, 그녀의 시신을 버크와 헤어의 희생자들과 한데 엮는 것은 그녀의 명예에 대한 노골적인 모욕이었다."]], [["Affairs were in this stage when Lydgate opened the subject of the Hospital to Dorothea.", "리드게이트가 도러시아에게 병원 문제를 꺼냈을 때 사정은 이런 단계에 있었다."], ["We see that he was bearing enmity and silly misconception with much spirit, aware that they were partly created by his good share of success.", "우리는 그가 적의와 어리석은 오해를 상당한 기개로 견뎌 내고 있었음을, 그리고 그것들이 어느 정도는 자신의 큰 성공에서 비롯되었음을 알고 있었음을 본다."]], [["“They will not drive me away,”", "\"그들이 나를 쫓아내지는 못할 겁니다.\""], ["he said, talking confidentially in Mr. Farebrother’s study.", "그가 페어브라더 씨의 서재에서 흉금을 터놓고 이야기하며 말했다."], ["“I have got a good opportunity here, for the ends I care most about; and I am pretty sure to get income enough for our wants.", "\"여기서 나는 내가 가장 마음 쓰는 목적을 위해 좋은 기회를 잡았습니다. 그리고 우리에게 필요한 만큼의 수입은 거의 틀림없이 얻을 수 있을 겁니다."], ["By-and-by I shall go on as quietly as possible: I have no seductions now away from home and work.", "머지않아 나는 되도록 조용히 일을 이어 갈 겁니다. 지금은 집과 일에서 나를 떼어 놓을 유혹이 없으니까요."], ["And I am more and more convinced that it will be possible to demonstrate the homogeneous origin of all the tissues.", "그리고 모든 조직의 기원이 동일하다는 것을 입증하는 일이 가능하리라는 확신이 점점 더 굳어집니다."], ["Raspail and others are on the same track, and I have been losing time.”", "라스파이와 다른 이들도 같은 길을 가고 있는데, 나는 시간을 허비해 왔어요.\""]], [["“I have no power of prophecy there,” said Mr.", "\"그 점에 대해서는 내게 예언할 능력이 없네.\""], ["Farebrother, who had been puffing at his pipe thoughtfully while Lydgate talked; “but as to the hostility in the town, you’ll weather it if you are prudent.”", "리드게이트가 말하는 동안 생각에 잠겨 파이프를 빨고 있던 페어브라더 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 마을의 적의에 관해서라면, 자네가 신중하기만 하다면 그건 견뎌 낼 걸세.\""]], [["“How am I to be prudent?”", "\"제가 어떻게 신중해야 합니까?\""], ["said Lydgate, “I just do what comes before me to do.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"저는 그저 제 앞에 닥친 일을 할 뿐입니다."], ["I can’t help people’s ignorance and spite, any more than Vesalius could.", "베살리우스가 그랬듯이, 저로서도 사람들의 무지와 악의를 어쩔 도리가 없습니다."], ["It isn’t possible to square one’s conduct to silly conclusions which nobody can foresee.”", "아무도 내다볼 수 없는 어리석은 결론에 자기 행동을 맞춘다는 건 불가능한 일이에요.\""]], [["“Quite true; I didn’t mean that.", "\"맞는 말일세. 그런 뜻이 아니었네."], ["I meant only two things.", "내 말은 단지 두 가지였네."], ["One is, keep yourself as separable from Bulstrode as you can: of course, you can go on doing good work of your own by his help; but don’t get tied.", "하나는, 불스트로드와는 되도록 떼어 놓을 수 있는 채로 자네 자신을 지키게. 물론 그의 도움으로 자네만의 훌륭한 일을 계속해 나갈 수야 있겠지."], ["Perhaps it seems like personal feeling in me to say so—and there’s a good deal of that, I own—but personal feeling is not always in the wrong if you boil it down to the impressions which make it simply an opinion.”", "하지만 묶이지는 말게. 어쩌면 이렇게 말하는 것이 내 사사로운 감정처럼 보일지도 모르고—사실 그런 게 상당히 있다는 건 인정하네—하지만 사사로운 감정도 그것을 그저 하나의 의견으로 만들어 주는 인상들로 끓여서 졸여 내면 늘 틀린 것만은 아니라네.\""]], [["“Bulstrode is nothing to me,”", "\"불스트로드는 저에게 아무것도 아닙니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, carelessly, “except on public grounds.", "리드게이트가 무심하게 말했다. \"공적인 근거에서가 아니라면요."], ["As to getting very closely united to him, I am not fond enough of him for that.", "그와 아주 긴밀히 결합되는 것으로 말하자면, 저는 그러기에는 그를 그리 좋아하지 않습니다."], ["But what was the other thing you meant?”", "그런데 선생님이 말씀하신 다른 한 가지는 무엇이었습니까?\""], ["said Lydgate, who was nursing his leg as comfortably as possible, and feeling in no great need of advice.", "다리를 되도록 편안하게 감싸 안고서 별로 충고가 필요하다고 느끼지 않던 리드게이트가 말했다."]], [["“Why, this.", "\"음, 이걸세."], ["Take care—experto crede—take care not to get hampered about money matters.", "조심하게—경험자의 말을 믿게—돈 문제로 발이 묶이지 않도록 조심하게."], ["I know, by a word you let fall one day, that you don’t like my playing at cards so much for money.", "언젠가 자네가 흘린 한마디로, 자네가 내가 돈을 걸고 카드놀이를 그렇게 많이 하는 걸 못마땅해한다는 걸 안다네."], ["You are right enough there.", "그 점에선 자네가 옳네."], ["But try and keep clear of wanting small sums that you haven’t got.", "하지만 자네가 가지지 못한 적은 돈을 아쉬워하는 일이 없도록 애써 보게."], ["I am perhaps talking rather superfluously; but a man likes to assume superiority over himself, by holding up his bad example and sermonizing on it.”", "어쩌면 내가 쓸데없는 말을 하고 있는지도 모르지. 하지만 사람이란 제 나쁜 본보기를 치켜들고 그것을 가지고 설교함으로써 자기 자신에 대해 우월감을 누리고 싶어 하는 법이거든.\""]], [["Lydgate took Mr. Farebrother’s hints very cordially, though he would hardly have borne them from another man.", "리드게이트는 페어브라더 씨의 충고를 아주 정답게 받아들였으니, 다른 사람에게서였다면 좀처럼 견디지 못했을 것이다."], ["He could not help remembering that he had lately made some debts, but these had seemed inevitable, and he had no intention now to do more than keep house in a simple way.", "그는 자기가 근래에 빚을 좀 졌다는 것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었으나, 그 빚은 불가피해 보였고, 이제 그는 검소하게 살림을 꾸려 가는 것 이상은 할 생각이 없었다."], ["The furniture for which he owed would not want renewing; nor even the stock of wine for a long while.", "빚을 진 가구는 새로 장만할 필요가 없을 것이고, 포도주 재고도 한참 동안은 그러할 것이었다."]], [["Many thoughts cheered him at that time—and justly.", "그 무렵 많은 생각들이 그에게 기운을 북돋아 주었으니—마땅한 일이었다."], ["A man conscious of enthusiasm for worthy aims is sustained under petty hostilities by the memory of great workers who had to fight their way not without wounds, and who hover in his mind as patron saints, invisibly helping. At home, that same evening when he had been chatting with Mr.", "가치 있는 목표에 대한 열정을 자각하는 사람은, 상처를 입어 가며 길을 헤쳐 나가야 했던, 그리고 그의 마음속에서 수호성인처럼 떠돌며 보이지 않게 도와주는 위대한 일꾼들의 기억에 힘입어 사소한 적의들을 견뎌 낸다."], ["Farebrother, he had his long legs stretched on the sofa, his head thrown back, and his hands clasped behind it according to his favorite ruminating attitude, while Rosamond sat at the piano, and played one tune after another, of which her husband only knew (like the emotional elephant he was!) that they fell in with his mood as if they had been melodious sea-breezes.", "페어브라더 씨와 담소를 나눈 바로 그날 저녁, 집에서 그는 즐겨 하는 사색의 자세대로 긴 다리를 소파에 뻗고 머리를 뒤로 젖힌 채 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 끼고 있었다. 그동안 로저먼드는 피아노 앞에 앉아 이런저런 곡을 잇따라 연주했는데, 그녀의 남편은 (그가 다정다감한 코끼리답게!) 그저 그 곡들이 마치 가락 고운 바닷바람처럼 자기 기분에 맞아떨어진다는 것만 알 뿐이었다."]], [["There was something very fine in Lydgate’s look just then, and any one might have been encouraged to bet on his achievement.", "바로 그때 리드게이트의 모습에는 무언가 매우 멋진 데가 있어서, 누구라도 그의 성취에 기꺼이 돈을 걸 만했다."], ["In his dark eyes and on his mouth and brow there was that placidity which comes from the fulness of contemplative thought—the mind not searching, but beholding, and the glance seeming to be filled with what is behind it.", "그의 검은 눈과 입가와 이마에는 사색적 사유의 충만함에서 오는 그 평온함이 어려 있었으니—탐색하지 않고 바라보는 정신, 그리고 그 뒤에 있는 것으로 가득 찬 듯한 눈길이었다."]], [["Presently Rosamond left the piano and seated herself on a chair close to the sofa and opposite her husband’s face.", "이윽고 로저먼드는 피아노를 떠나 소파 가까이, 남편의 얼굴 맞은편 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“Is that enough music for you, my lord?”", "\"이제 음악은 충분하신가요, 나리?\""], ["she said, folding her hands before her and putting on a little air of meekness.", "그녀가 두 손을 앞으로 모으고 약간 순종하는 척하는 태도를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, dear, if you are tired,”", "\"그래, 여보, 당신이 피곤하다면.\""], ["said Lydgate, gently, turning his eyes and resting them on her, but not otherwise moving.", "리드게이트가 다정하게 말하며, 다른 데는 꼼짝 않고 눈길만 돌려 그녀에게 머물렀다."], ["Rosamond’s presence at that moment was perhaps no more than a spoonful brought to the lake, and her woman’s instinct in this matter was not dull.", "그 순간 로저먼드의 존재는 어쩌면 호수에 떠 온 한 숟갈의 물에 지나지 않았는데, 이 일에 관한 그녀의 여자다운 직감은 둔하지 않았다."]], [["“What is absorbing you?”", "\"무엇에 그리 빠져 계세요?\""], ["she said, leaning forward and bringing her face nearer to his.", "그녀가 몸을 앞으로 기울여 얼굴을 그의 얼굴 가까이 가져가며 말했다."]], [["He moved his hands and placed them gently behind her shoulders.", "그는 두 손을 풀어 그녀의 어깨 뒤에 가만히 갖다 대었다."]], [["“I am thinking of a great fellow, who was about as old as I am three hundred years ago, and had already begun a new era in anatomy.”", "\"삼백 년 전, 지금의 나만 한 나이였을 때 이미 해부학에 새 시대를 연 한 위대한 사람을 생각하고 있었소.\""]], [["“I can’t guess,”", "\"못 맞히겠어요.\""], ["said Rosamond, shaking her head.", "로저먼드가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["“We used to play at guessing historical characters at Mrs. Lemon’s, but not anatomists.”", "\"레먼 부인 댁에서 역사적 인물 알아맞히기 놀이는 했지만, 해부학자는 안 했거든요.\""]], [["“I’ll tell you. His name was Vesalius.", "\"내가 말해 주지."], ["And the only way he could get to know anatomy as he did, was by going to snatch bodies at night, from graveyards and places of execution.”", "그의 이름은 베살리우스였소. 그가 그렇게 해부학을 알게 된 유일한 길은, 밤에 묘지와 처형장에서 시신을 훔쳐 오는 것이었지.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"어머!\""], ["said Rosamond, with a look of disgust on her pretty face, “I am very glad you are not Vesalius.", "로저먼드가 예쁜 얼굴에 역겨운 표정을 지으며 말했다. \"당신이 베살리우스가 아니라서 정말 다행이에요."], ["I should have thought he might find some less horrible way than that.”", "그보다는 덜 끔찍한 방법을 찾아낼 수도 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“No, he couldn’t,”", "\"아니, 그럴 수 없었소.\""], ["said Lydgate, going on too earnestly to take much notice of her answer.", "리드게이트가 너무 열중한 나머지 그녀의 대답에는 별로 주의를 기울이지 않은 채 말을 이었다."], ["“He could only get a complete skeleton by snatching the whitened bones of a criminal from the gallows, and burying them, and fetching them away by bits secretly, in the dead of night.”", "\"완전한 골격을 얻을 수 있는 유일한 방법은 교수대에서 죄인의 허옇게 바랜 뼈를 훔쳐다 묻어 두고, 깊은 밤에 몰래 조금씩 가져오는 것뿐이었지.\""]], [["“I hope he is not one of your great heroes,”", "\"그 사람이 당신의 위대한 영웅들 가운데 하나가 아니길 바라요.\""], ["said Rosamond, half playfully, half anxiously, “else I shall have you getting up in the night to go to St.", "로저먼드가 반은 장난스럽게, 반은 걱정스럽게 말했다. \"안 그러면 당신이 한밤중에 일어나 성 베드로 묘지로 가게 될 테니까요."], ["Peter’s churchyard. You know how angry you told me the people were about Mrs.", "사람들이 고비 부인 일로 얼마나 화가 났었는지 당신이 말해 줬잖아요."], ["Goby. You have enemies enough already.”", "당신은 이미 적이 충분히 많아요.\""]], [["“So had Vesalius, Rosy.", "\"베살리우스도 그랬소, 로지."], ["No wonder the medical fogies in Middlemarch are jealous, when some of the greatest doctors living were fierce upon Vesalius because they had believed in Galen, and he showed that Galen was wrong.", "살아 있는 가장 위대한 의사들 가운데 일부가 베살리우스에게 사납게 굴었던 걸 생각하면, 미들마치의 케케묵은 의사 영감들이 질투하는 것도 놀랄 일이 아니지. 그들은 갈레노스를 믿고 있었는데, 베살리우스가 갈레노스가 틀렸다는 걸 보여 줬거든."], ["They called him a liar and a poisonous monster.", "그들은 그를 거짓말쟁이요 독을 품은 괴물이라고 불렀지."], ["But the facts of the human frame were on his side; and so he got the better of them.”", "하지만 인체의 사실들이 그의 편이었고, 그래서 그는 그들을 이겨 냈소.\""]], [["“And what happened to him afterwards?”", "\"그 뒤에 그는 어떻게 됐어요?\""], ["said Rosamond, with some interest.", "로저먼드가 약간 흥미를 보이며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, he had a good deal of fighting to the last.", "\"오, 그는 끝까지 숱한 싸움을 치렀지."], ["And they did exasperate him enough at one time to make him burn a good deal of his work.", "그리고 한때는 그들이 그를 어찌나 격분시켰던지 그가 자기 저작의 상당 부분을 불태워 버렸을 정도였소."], ["Then he got shipwrecked just as he was coming from Jerusalem to take a great chair at Padua.", "그러고는 예루살렘에서 파도바의 큰 교수직을 맡으러 오던 길에 그만 배가 난파되었지."], ["He died rather miserably.”", "그는 다소 비참하게 죽었소.\""]], [["There was a moment’s pause before Rosamond said, “Do you know, Tertius, I often wish you had not been a medical man.”", "로저먼드가 입을 열기까지 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다. \"있잖아요, 터티어스, 나는 당신이 의사가 아니었더라면 하고 자주 바라요.\""]], [["“Nay, Rosy, don’t say that,”", "\"아니, 로지, 그런 말 말아요.\""], ["said Lydgate, drawing her closer to him.", "리드게이트가 그녀를 더 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["“That is like saying you wish you had married another man.”", "\"그건 당신이 다른 남자와 결혼했더라면 좋았겠다고 말하는 거나 마찬가지요.\""]], [["“Not at all; you are clever enough for anything: you might easily have been something else.", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아요. 당신은 무엇이든 할 만큼 영리하니까, 얼마든지 다른 게 될 수도 있었을 거예요."], ["And your cousins at Quallingham all think that you have sunk below them in your choice of a profession.”", "그리고 콸링엄에 있는 당신 사촌들도 모두, 당신이 그런 직업을 택해서 그들보다 아래로 떨어졌다고 생각하잖아요.\""]], [["“The cousins at Quallingham may go to the devil!”", "\"콸링엄의 사촌들이야 악마한테나 가라지!\""], ["said Lydgate, with scorn.", "리드게이트가 경멸조로 말했다."], ["“It was like their impudence if they said anything of the sort to you.”", "\"그자들이 당신에게 그런 말을 했다면 그건 뻔뻔스러운 짓이오.\""]], [["“Still,”", "\"그래도,\""], ["said Rosamond, “I do not think it is a nice profession, dear.”", "로저먼드가 말했다. \"나는 그게 멋진 직업이라고는 생각하지 않아요, 여보.\""], ["We know that she had much quiet perseverance in her opinion.", "우리는 그녀가 자기 의견에 조용한 끈기를 상당히 지니고 있었음을 안다."]], [["“It is the grandest profession in the world, Rosamond,”", "\"그건 세상에서 가장 위대한 직업이오, 로저먼드.\""], ["said Lydgate, gravely.", "리드게이트가 진지하게 말했다."], ["“And to say that you love me without loving the medical man in me, is the same sort of thing as to say that you like eating a peach but don’t like its flavor.", "\"그리고 내 안에 있는 의사는 사랑하지 않으면서 나를 사랑한다고 말하는 건, 복숭아 먹는 건 좋아하지만 그 맛은 싫다고 말하는 거나 마찬가지요. 다시는 그런 말 말아요, 여보."], ["Don’t say that again, dear, it pains me.”", "그건 나를 아프게 하니까.\""]], [["“Very well, Doctor Grave-face,”", "\"좋아요, 근엄한 얼굴의 박사님.\""], ["said Rosy, dimpling, “I will declare in future that I dote on skeletons, and body-snatchers, and bits of things in phials, and quarrels with everybody, that end in your dying miserably.”", "로지가 보조개를 지으며 말했다. \"앞으로는 내가 해골이며, 시체 도둑이며, 약병에 든 것 쪼가리며, 비참하게 죽는 걸로 끝나는 모든 사람과의 다툼을 끔찍이 사랑한다고 선언할게요.\""]], [["“No, no, not so bad as that,”", "\"아니, 아니, 그렇게까지는 아니오.\""], ["said Lydgate, giving up remonstrance and petting her resignedly.", "리드게이트가 항변을 그만두고 체념한 듯 그녀를 어르며 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVI.", "# 제43장"]], [["Pues no podemos haber aquello que queremos, queramos aquello que podremos.", "우리는 좋아하는 것을 얻을 수 없으니, 얻을 수 있는 것을 좋아하기로 하자.—스페인 속담*"]], [["Since we cannot get what we like, let us like what we can get.—Spanish Proverb.", "* 본문 첫머리의 스페인어와 영어는 같은 속담의 원어와 번역으로, \"우리는 우리가 원하는 것을 가질 수 없으니, 우리가 가질 수 있는 것을 원하자\"라는 뜻이다."]], [["While Lydgate, safely married and with the Hospital under his command, felt himself struggling for Medical Reform against Middlemarch, Middlemarch was becoming more and more conscious of the national struggle for another kind of Reform.", "리드게이트가 안전하게 결혼하고 병원을 자기 지휘 아래 둔 채 미들마치를 상대로 의료 개혁을 위해 분투하고 있다고 느끼는 동안, 미들마치는 또 다른 종류의 개혁을 위한 전국적 투쟁을 점점 더 의식하게 되었다."]], [["By the time that Lord John Russell’s measure was being debated in the House of Commons, there was a new political animation in Middlemarch, and a new definition of parties which might show a decided change of balance if a new election came.", "존 러셀 경의 법안이 하원에서 토의되고 있을 무렵, 미들마치에는 새로운 정치적 활기가 일었고, 새로운 선거가 치러진다면 세력의 뚜렷한 변화를 보여 줄 만한 새로운 정당 구분이 생겨났다."], ["And there were some who already predicted this event, declaring that a Reform Bill would never be carried by the actual Parliament.", "그리고 이미 이런 일을 예언하며, 개혁 법안이 현 의회에서는 결코 통과되지 못할 것이라고 단언하는 이들도 있었다."], ["This was what Will Ladislaw dwelt on to Mr. Brooke as a reason for congratulation that he had not yet tried his strength at the hustings.", "윌 래디슬로가 브룩 씨에게, 그가 아직 유세장에서 자기 힘을 시험해 보지 않은 것이 다행스러운 일이라며 강조한 것도 바로 이 점이었다."]], [["“Things will grow and ripen as if it were a comet year,”", "\"마치 혜성이 나타나는 해처럼 사태가 자라나고 무르익을 겁니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“The public temper will soon get to a cometary heat, now the question of Reform has set in.", "\"개혁 문제가 본격화되었으니, 대중의 정서가 곧 혜성처럼 뜨겁게 달아오를 거예요."], ["There is likely to be another election before long, and by that time Middlemarch will have got more ideas into its head.", "머지않아 또 한 차례 선거가 있을 가능성이 높고, 그때쯤이면 미들마치도 머릿속에 더 많은 생각을 담게 될 겁니다."], ["What we have to work at now is the ‘Pioneer’ and political meetings.”", "지금 우리가 매달려야 할 일은 『파이어니어』와 정치 집회예요.\""]], [["“Quite right, Ladislaw; we shall make a new thing of opinion here,”", "\"옳은 말이네, 래디슬로. 우리는 여기서 여론을 새로운 것으로 만들어 낼 걸세.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“Only I want to keep myself independent about Reform, you know; I don’t want to go too far.", "\"다만 나는 개혁에 관해서는 독자적인 입장을 지키고 싶네, 알겠나."], ["I want to take up Wilberforce’s and Romilly’s line, you know, and work at Negro Emancipation, Criminal Law—that kind of thing.", "너무 멀리 나가고 싶지는 않아. 윌버포스와 로밀리의 노선을 따라, 흑인 해방이라든가 형법 같은 것에 힘을 쏟고 싶단 말일세."], ["But of course I should support Grey.”", "하지만 물론 그레이는 지지해야겠지.\""]], [["“If you go in for the principle of Reform, you must be prepared to take what the situation offers,”", "\"개혁의 원칙을 지지하신다면, 상황이 내놓는 것을 받아들일 준비도 하셔야 합니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“If everybody pulled for his own bit against everybody else, the whole question would go to tatters.”", "\"모두가 저마다 제 몫을 챙기려고 서로 잡아당긴다면, 문제 전체가 갈가리 찢어지고 말 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, I agree with you—I quite take that point of view.", "\"그래, 그래, 자네 말에 동의하네. 나도 그 관점을 충분히 받아들여."], ["I should put it in that light.", "나라면 그런 식으로 표현하겠지."], ["I should support Grey, you know.", "그레이를 지지하겠네, 알다시피."], ["But I don’t want to change the balance of the constitution, and I don’t think Grey would.”", "하지만 헌정의 균형을 바꾸고 싶지는 않고, 그레이도 그러지는 않으리라고 보네.\""]], [["“But that is what the country wants,”", "\"하지만 나라가 원하는 것이 바로 그것입니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“Else there would be no meaning in political unions or any other movement that knows what it’s about.", "\"그렇지 않다면 정치 동맹이나 자기가 무엇을 하는지 아는 다른 어떤 운동도 무의미할 거예요."], ["It wants to have a House of Commons which is not weighted with nominees of the landed class, but with representatives of the other interests.", "나라는 지주 계급의 지명자들로 채워진 하원이 아니라, 다른 이해 집단의 대표들로 이루어진 하원을 원합니다."], ["And as to contending for a reform short of that, it is like asking for a bit of an avalanche which has already begun to thunder.”", "그리고 거기에 못 미치는 개혁을 두고 다투는 것은, 이미 천둥처럼 굉음을 내며 흘러내리기 시작한 눈사태에서 한 조각만 떼어 달라고 요구하는 것과 같아요.\""]], [["“That is fine, Ladislaw: that is the way to put it.", "\"훌륭하군, 래디슬로. 바로 그렇게 표현해야지."], ["Write that down, now.", "자, 그걸 적어 두게."], ["We must begin to get documents about the feeling of the country, as well as the machine-breaking and general distress.”", "우리는 기계 파괴나 전반적인 궁핍에 관한 것뿐 아니라 나라의 정서에 관한 자료도 모으기 시작해야 하네.\""]], [["“As to documents,”", "\"자료라면,\" 윌이 말했다."], ["said Will, “a two-inch card will hold plenty.", "\"가로세로 2인치짜리 카드 한 장이면 충분히 담을 수 있습니다."], ["A few rows of figures are enough to deduce misery from, and a few more will show the rate at which the political determination of the people is growing.”", "몇 줄의 숫자만 있으면 거기서 빈곤을 추론하기에 족하고, 몇 줄을 더하면 국민의 정치적 결의가 자라나는 속도를 보여 줄 수 있어요.\""]], [["“Good: draw that out a little more at length, Ladislaw.", "\"좋아. 그걸 좀 더 길게 풀어내 보게, 래디슬로."], ["That is an idea, now: write it out in the ‘Pioneer.’ Put the figures and deduce the misery, you know; and put the other figures and deduce—and so on.", "자, 그게 바로 착상이야."], ["You have a way of putting things.", "『파이어니어』에 써 보게."], ["Burke, now:—when I think of Burke, I can’t help wishing somebody had a pocket-borough to give you, Ladislaw.", "숫자를 제시하고 빈곤을 추론하고, 알겠나. 또 다른 숫자를 제시하고 추론하고, 그런 식으로 말일세."], ["You’d never get elected, you know.", "자네는 사물을 표현하는 재주가 있어."], ["And we shall always want talent in the House: reform as we will, we shall always want talent.", "자, 버크를 생각하면—버크를 떠올릴 때면, 누군가 자네에게 줄 수 있는 매수 선거구라도 하나 있었으면 하는 바람을 떨칠 수가 없네, 래디슬로. 자네는 도무지 당선될 사람이 아니거든, 알다시피."], ["That avalanche and the thunder, now, was really a little like Burke.", "그런데 우리는 의회에 늘 인재를 필요로 할 걸세. 아무리 개혁을 한다 해도, 우리는 늘 인재가 필요하단 말이지."], ["I want that sort of thing—not ideas, you know, but a way of putting them.”", "자, 그 눈사태와 천둥, 그건 정말이지 버크와 좀 닮았어. 나는 그런 것을 원해—착상이 아니라, 알겠나, 그걸 표현하는 방식 말일세.\""]], [["“Pocket-boroughs would be a fine thing,”", "\"매수 선거구도 좋은 것이겠지요.\""], ["said Ladislaw, “if they were always in the right pocket, and there were always a Burke at hand.”", "래디슬로가 말했다. \"그것이 언제나 올바른 주머니 속에 들어 있고, 언제나 가까이에 버크가 있다면 말입니다.\"*"]], [["Will was not displeased with that complimentary comparison, even from Mr.", "* 매수 선거구(pocket-borough): 선거구가 사실상 한 지주의 '주머니' 안에 있어 그의 뜻대로 의원을 앉힐 수 있던 부패한 선거구."], ["Brooke; for it is a little too trying to human flesh to be conscious of expressing one’s self better than others and never to have it noticed, and in the general dearth of admiration for the right thing, even a chance bray of applause falling exactly in time is rather fortifying.", "버크(Edmund Burke, 1729~1797)는 이런 선거구를 통해 의회에 진출한 대정치가다. 윌은 비록 브룩 씨에게서 나온 것일지언정 그런 찬사 어린 비교가 싫지 않았다."], ["Will felt that his literary refinements were usually beyond the limits of Middlemarch perception; nevertheless, he was beginning thoroughly to like the work of which when he began he had said to himself rather languidly, “Why not?”—and he studied the political situation with as ardent an interest as he had ever given to poetic metres or mediaevalism.", "자기가 남들보다 더 잘 표현한다는 것을 스스로 의식하면서도 아무도 그것을 알아주지 않는다는 것은 인간의 본성으로서는 견디기에 다소 가혹한 일이고, 올바른 것에 대한 감탄이 전반적으로 메말라 있는 가운데, 우연히 박자에 딱 맞아떨어지는 한 가닥의 칭찬 소리는 오히려 사람을 다소 든든하게 해 주기 때문이다. 윌은 자기의 문학적 세련됨이 대개 미들마치의 인식 범위를 넘어선다고 느꼈다."], ["It is undeniable that but for the desire to be where Dorothea was, and perhaps the want of knowing what else to do, Will would not at this time have been meditating on the needs of the English people or criticising English statesmanship: he would probably have been rambling in Italy sketching plans for several dramas, trying prose and finding it too jejune, trying verse and finding it too artificial, beginning to copy “bits”", "그럼에도 그는, 처음 시작할 때 다소 나른하게 \"안 될 것도 없지?\"라고 스스로에게 말했던 그 일을 점차 진심으로 좋아하게 되었고, 일찍이 시의 운율이나 중세 연구에 쏟았던 것 못지않게 열렬한 관심을 가지고 정치 상황을 연구했다. 도러시아가 있는 곳에 있고 싶다는 욕망이 없었다면, 그리고 어쩌면 달리 무엇을 해야 할지 몰랐던 탓이 아니었다면, 윌이 이 무렵 영국 국민의 필요에 대해 사색하거나 영국의 정치력을 비판하고 있지는 않았으리라는 것은 부인할 수 없다."], ["from old pictures, leaving off because they were “no good,” and observing that, after all, self-culture was the principal point; while in politics he would have been sympathizing warmly with liberty and progress in general.", "그랬다면 그는 아마도 이탈리아를 떠돌면서 여러 편의 희곡 구상을 스케치하고, 산문을 시도하다가 너무 무미건조하다고 여기고, 운문을 시도하다가 너무 인위적이라고 여기고, 옛 그림에서 \"부분\"을 베끼기 시작하다가 \"별 볼 일 없다\"고 그만두고, 결국 무엇보다 중요한 것은 자기 수양이라고 생각하고 있었을 것이다."], ["Our sense of duty must often wait for some work which shall take the place of dilettanteism and make us feel that the quality of our action is not a matter of indifference.", "한편 정치에 있어서는 자유와 진보 일반에 대해 따뜻하게 공감하고 있었으리라. 우리의 의무감은 흔히 호사가적 취미를 대신할 어떤 일을, 우리의 행위의 질이 아무래도 좋은 문제가 아님을 느끼게 해 줄 어떤 일을 기다려야만 한다."]], [["Ladislaw had now accepted his bit of work, though it was not that indeterminate loftiest thing which he had once dreamed of as alone worthy of continuous effort.", "래디슬로는 이제 자기 몫의 일을 받아들였다. 비록 그것이 한때 그가 지속적인 노력을 바칠 가치가 있는 유일한 것이라고 꿈꾸었던, 그 막연하고 더없이 고결한 무엇은 아니었지만 말이다."], ["His nature warmed easily in the presence of subjects which were visibly mixed with life and action, and the easily stirred rebellion in him helped the glow of public spirit.", "그의 천성은 삶과 행동에 눈에 띄게 뒤섞인 주제 앞에서 쉽게 달아올랐고, 그의 안에 쉽사리 일어나는 반항심은 공공 정신의 열기를 북돋웠다. 캐소본 씨와 로윅으로부터의 추방에도 불구하고, 그는 오히려 행복한 편이었다."], ["In spite of Mr. Casaubon and the banishment from Lowick, he was rather happy; getting a great deal of fresh knowledge in a vivid way and for practical purposes, and making the “Pioneer”", "새로운 지식을 생생하게, 그리고 실용적인 목적에 맞게 잔뜩 얻고 있었고, 『파이어니어』를 브래싱에 이르기까지 유명하게 만들고 있었으니 말이다(그 범위가 좁다고 해서 개의치 말 것."], ["celebrated as far as Brassing (never mind the smallness of the area; the writing was not worse than much that reaches the four corners of the earth).", "그 글은 세상의 사방 구석구석까지 닿는 많은 글들보다 못하지 않았다)."]], [["Mr.", "브룩 씨는 이따금 사람을 짜증나게 했다."], ["Brooke was occasionally irritating; but Will’s impatience was relieved by the division of his time between visits to the Grange and retreats to his Middlemarch lodgings, which gave variety to his life.", "하지만 윌의 조바심은, 그가 그레인지를 방문하는 시간과 미들마치의 자기 하숙으로 물러나는 시간으로 나뉘어 있던 덕분에 누그러졌으니, 이것이 그의 생활에 변화를 주었다."]], [["“Shift the pegs a little,”", "\"못을 조금만 옮겨 박으면,\""], ["he said to himself, “and Mr.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["Brooke might be in the Cabinet, while I was Under-Secretary.", "\"브룩 씨가 내각에 들어가고 내가 차관이 될 수도 있겠지."], ["That is the common order of things: the little waves make the large ones and are of the same pattern.", "그게 흔히 돌아가는 이치야. 작은 물결이 큰 물결을 만들고, 둘은 같은 무늬를 지녔으니까."], ["I am better here than in the sort of life Mr.", "캐소본 씨가 나를 길들이려 했던 그런 삶보다는 여기가 낫다."], ["Casaubon would have trained me for, where the doing would be all laid down by a precedent too rigid for me to react upon.", "거기서라면 모든 일이 너무 경직된 전례에 따라 정해져 있어서 내가 거기에 반응할 여지조차 없었을 테니."], ["I don’t care for prestige or high pay.”", "나는 명망이나 높은 보수 따위에는 관심 없어.\""]], [["As Lydgate had said of him, he was a sort of gypsy, rather enjoying the sense of belonging to no class; he had a feeling of romance in his position, and a pleasant consciousness of creating a little surprise wherever he went.", "리드게이트가 그에 대해 말했듯이, 그는 일종의 집시 같았고, 어느 계급에도 속하지 않는다는 느낌을 오히려 즐겼다. 그는 자기 처지에 낭만의 정취를 느꼈고, 가는 곳마다 약간의 놀라움을 일으킨다는 것을 유쾌하게 의식했다."], ["That sort of enjoyment had been disturbed when he had felt some new distance between himself and Dorothea in their accidental meeting at Lydgate’s, and his irritation had gone out towards Mr.", "그런 즐거움은, 리드게이트의 집에서 우연히 도러시아를 만났을 때 그녀와 자기 사이에 어떤 새로운 거리감을 느끼면서 흐트러졌고, 그의 짜증은 캐소본 씨를 향했다."], ["Casaubon, who had declared beforehand that Will would lose caste.", "윌이 신분을 잃게 될 것이라고 미리 단언했던 사람 말이다."], ["“I never had any caste,”", "\"나는 애초에 신분이라는 게 없었어.\""], ["he would have said, if that prophecy had been uttered to him, and the quick blood would have come and gone like breath in his transparent skin.", "만약 그 예언이 그에게 직접 발설되었더라면 그는 이렇게 말했을 것이고, 빠른 핏기가 그의 투명한 피부 위로 숨결처럼 오갔을 것이다."], ["But it is one thing to like defiance, and another thing to like its consequences.", "그러나 반항을 좋아하는 것과 그 결과를 좋아하는 것은 별개의 일이다."]], [["Meanwhile, the town opinion about the new editor of the “Pioneer” was tending to confirm Mr.", "한편 『파이어니어』의 새 편집인에 대한 읍내의 여론은 캐소본 씨의 견해를 굳히는 쪽으로 기울고 있었다."], ["Casaubon’s view. Will’s relationship in that distinguished quarter did not, like Lydgate’s high connections, serve as an advantageous introduction: if it was rumored that young Ladislaw was Mr.", "그 명망 높은 집안과의 윌의 인척 관계는, 리드게이트의 높은 연줄과 달리 유리한 소개장 구실을 하지 못했다."], ["Casaubon’s nephew or cousin, it was also rumored that “Mr. Casaubon would have nothing to do with him.”", "젊은 래디슬로가 캐소본 씨의 조카니 사촌이니 하는 소문이 돌면, \"캐소본 씨가 그와는 상종하려 하지 않는다\"는 소문도 함께 돌았던 것이다."]], [["“Brooke has taken him up,” said Mr.", "\"브룩이 그자를 거둔 거지.\""], ["Hawley, “because that is what no man in his senses could have expected.", "홀리 씨가 말했다. \"그게 바로 제정신인 사람이라면 아무도 예상치 못할 일이니까."], ["Casaubon has devilish good reasons, you may be sure, for turning the cold shoulder on a young fellow whose bringing-up he paid for.", "캐소본이 저가 양육비를 대 준 젊은이를 냉대하는 데는 분명 지독히도 그럴 만한 이유가 있을 거요."], ["Just like Brooke—one of those fellows who would praise a cat to sell a horse.”", "딱 브룩다운 짓이지—말 한 마리 팔아먹으려고 고양이를 칭찬할 그런 작자들 중 하나란 말이오.\""]], [["And some oddities of Will’s, more or less poetical, appeared to support Mr. Keck, the editor of the “Trumpet,”", "그리고 다소간 시적인 윌의 몇몇 기벽은, 『트럼펫』의 편집인 케크 씨가 래디슬로에 대해 단언한 바를 뒷받침해 주는 듯했다."], ["in asserting that Ladislaw, if the truth were known, was not only a Polish emissary but crack-brained, which accounted for the preternatural quickness and glibness of his speech when he got on to a platform—as he did whenever he had an opportunity, speaking with a facility which cast reflections on solid Englishmen generally.", "즉 진실을 알고 보면 래디슬로는 폴란드의 밀정일 뿐 아니라 머리가 돈 자라는 것, 그래서 그가 연단에 오르기만 하면—기회가 있을 때마다 그는 그렇게 했는데—말이 그토록 초자연적으로 빠르고 유창하게 쏟아져 나오고, 그 능변이 견실한 영국인 전반에 그늘을 드리운다는 것이었다."], ["It was disgusting to Keck to see a strip of a fellow, with light curls round his head, get up and speechify by the hour against institutions “which had existed when he was in his cradle.”", "케크로서는, 머리에 옅은 곱슬머리가 둘린 풋내기 한 명이 일어서서 \"제가 요람에 있을 때 이미 존재하던\" 제도들을 향해 몇 시간씩 일장연설을 늘어놓는 꼴을 보는 것이 역겨웠다."], ["And in a leading article of the “Trumpet,” Keck characterized Ladislaw’s speech at a Reform meeting as “the violence of an energumen—a miserable effort to shroud in the brilliancy of fireworks the daring of irresponsible statements and the poverty of a knowledge which was of the cheapest and most recent description.”", "그리고 케크는 『트럼펫』의 한 사설에서, 어느 개혁 집회에서 행한 래디슬로의 연설을 두고 \"악령 들린 자의 광란—불꽃놀이의 현란함 속에 무책임한 주장의 만용과, 가장 값싸고 가장 최근의 부류에 속하는 지식의 빈곤을 가려 보려는 가련한 시도\"라고 규정했다."]], [["“That was a rattling article yesterday, Keck,”", "\"어제 그 사설은 아주 신나는 글이었소, 케크.\""], ["said Dr. Sprague, with sarcastic intentions.", "빈정대려는 의도로 스프레이그 박사가 말했다."], ["“But what is an energumen?”", "\"그런데 '악령 들린 자'라는 게 뭐요?\""]], [["“Oh, a term that came up in the French Revolution,”", "\"아, 프랑스 혁명 때 나온 용어요.\""], ["said Keck.", "케크가 말했다."]], [["This dangerous aspect of Ladislaw was strangely contrasted with other habits which became matter of remark.", "래디슬로의 이런 위험한 면모는, 사람들 입에 오르내리게 된 다른 습관들과 묘하게 대조를 이루었다."], ["He had a fondness, half artistic, half affectionate, for little children—the smaller they were on tolerably active legs, and the funnier their clothing, the better Will liked to surprise and please them.", "그에게는 반은 예술적이고 반은 다정한, 어린아이들을 향한 애정이 있었다. 아이가 어지간히 활발히 걸어 다닐 만한 다리를 지니고서도 몸집이 작을수록, 그리고 옷차림이 우스꽝스러울수록, 윌은 그 아이를 놀래 주고 기쁘게 해 주는 것을 더 좋아했다."], ["We know that in Rome he was given to ramble about among the poor people, and the taste did not quit him in Middlemarch.", "로마에서 그가 가난한 사람들 사이를 곧잘 어슬렁거렸다는 것을 우리는 알고 있거니와, 그 취향은 미들마치에서도 그를 떠나지 않았다."]], [["He had somehow picked up a troop of droll children, little hatless boys with their galligaskins much worn and scant shirting to hang out, little girls who tossed their hair out of their eyes to look at him, and guardian brothers at the mature age of seven.", "그는 어찌어찌하여 우스꽝스러운 아이들 한 무리를 거두게 되었다. 모자도 안 쓰고 무릎바지가 닳을 대로 닳은, 셔츠 자락도 변변히 빼낼 게 없는 사내아이들, 그를 보려고 눈가의 머리카락을 휙 넘기는 계집아이들, 그리고 일곱 살이라는 성숙한 나이에 보호자 노릇을 하는 오빠들이었다."], ["This troop he had led out on gypsy excursions to Halsell Wood at nutting-time, and since the cold weather had set in he had taken them on a clear day to gather sticks for a bonfire in the hollow of a hillside, where he drew out a small feast of gingerbread for them, and improvised a Punch-and-Judy drama with some private home-made puppets. Here was one oddity.", "그는 이 무리를 이끌고 도토리철이면 핼셀 숲으로 집시처럼 소풍을 나갔고, 추운 날씨가 시작된 뒤로는 맑은 날을 골라 산비탈 우묵한 곳에서 모닥불을 피울 나뭇가지를 모으러 데리고 갔는데, 거기서 아이들에게 생강 과자로 조촐한 잔치를 베풀고, 손수 만든 인형 몇 개로 즉석에서 펀치와 주디 인형극을 펼쳐 보였다.* 이것이 하나의 기벽이었다."], ["Another was, that in houses where he got friendly, he was given to stretch himself at full length on the rug while he talked, and was apt to be discovered in this attitude by occasional callers for whom such an irregularity was likely to confirm the notions of his dangerously mixed blood and general laxity.", "또 하나는, 그가 친해진 집에서는 이야기를 나누는 동안 난롯가 깔개 위에 길게 드러눕기를 즐겨서, 이따금 찾아오는 손님들에게 그런 자세로 발견되곤 했다는 것인데, 그런 손님들로서는 이런 무례함이 그의 위험하게 뒤섞인 핏줄과 전반적인 방종에 대한 관념을 굳히기 십상이었다."]], [["But Will’s articles and speeches naturally recommended him in families which the new strictness of party division had marked off on the side of Reform.", "* 펀치와 주디(Punch-and-Judy): 영국의 전통 손인형 희극. 하지만 윌의 기고문과 연설은, 새로운 당파 구분의 엄정함이 개혁 쪽으로 갈라놓은 가문들에서 그를 자연스럽게 환영받게 했다."], ["He was invited to Mr.", "그는 벌스트로드 씨 댁에 초대받았다."], ["Bulstrode’s; but here he could not lie down on the rug, and Mrs. Bulstrode felt that his mode of talking about Catholic countries, as if there were any truce with Antichrist, illustrated the usual tendency to unsoundness in intellectual men.", "하지만 거기서는 깔개 위에 드러누울 수 없었고, 벌스트로드 부인은 그가 가톨릭 국가들에 대해 마치 적그리스도와 무슨 휴전이라도 있을 수 있다는 듯이 이야기하는 방식이, 지식인들에게 흔한 그 불건전한 경향을 잘 보여 준다고 느꼈다."]], [["At Mr.", "그러나 사태의 아이러니로 인해 이 전국적 운동에서 벌스트로드와 같은 편에 서게 된 페어브러더 씨 댁에서는, 윌이 부인들의 총애를 받게 되었다."], ["Farebrother’s, however, whom the irony of events had brought on the same side with Bulstrode in the national movement, Will became a favorite with the ladies; especially with little Miss Noble, whom it was one of his oddities to escort when he met her in the street with her little basket, giving her his arm in the eyes of the town, and insisting on going with her to pay some call where she distributed her small filchings from her own share of sweet things.", "특히 자그마한 노블 양에게 그러했는데, 그녀가 작은 바구니를 들고 거리를 지날 때 마주치면 그녀를 호위하는 것이 그의 기벽 중 하나였다. 그는 온 읍내가 보는 앞에서 그녀에게 팔을 내밀고, 그녀가 자기 몫의 단것에서 슬쩍 챙겨 둔 자그마한 것들을 나누어 주러 어딘가로 방문 가는 길에 굳이 따라나서곤 했다."]], [["But the house where he visited oftenest and lay most on the rug was Lydgate’s.", "하지만 그가 가장 자주 방문하고 깔개 위에 가장 많이 드러누운 집은 리드게이트의 집이었다."], ["The two men were not at all alike, but they agreed none the worse.", "두 사람은 조금도 닮지 않았지만, 그렇다고 사이가 나쁘지도 않았다."], ["Lydgate was abrupt but not irritable, taking little notice of megrims in healthy people; and Ladislaw did not usually throw away his susceptibilities on those who took no notice of them.", "리드게이트는 무뚝뚝하긴 해도 신경질적이지는 않아서, 건강한 사람들의 변덕에는 별로 신경 쓰지 않았다. 그리고 래디슬로는 보통 자기의 예민함을 그것에 무심한 사람들에게 함부로 낭비하지 않았다."], ["With Rosamond, on the other hand, he pouted and was wayward—nay, often uncomplimentary, much to her inward surprise; nevertheless he was gradually becoming necessary to her entertainment by his companionship in her music, his varied talk, and his freedom from the grave preoccupation which, with all her husband’s tenderness and indulgence, often made his manners unsatisfactory to her, and confirmed her dislike of the medical profession.", "반면 로저먼드에게는 입을 삐죽이며 변덕을 부렸고—아니, 그녀가 속으로 적잖이 놀랄 만큼 종종 무례하기까지 했다. 그럼에도 그는 점차 그녀의 소일거리에 없어서는 안 될 존재가 되어 갔으니, 그녀의 음악에 동무가 되어 주고, 다채로운 이야기를 들려주고, 또 그 진지한 골몰함—남편이 아무리 다정하고 너그럽게 대해 주어도 그 골몰함이 종종 그의 태도를 그녀에게 불만스럽게 만들고 의사라는 직업에 대한 그녀의 혐오를 굳혀 놓았던 그것—에서 자유로웠기 때문이다."]], [["Lydgate, inclined to be sarcastic on the superstitious faith of the people in the efficacy of “the bill,” while nobody cared about the low state of pathology, sometimes assailed Will with troublesome questions.", "리드게이트는, 병리학의 낙후된 상태에는 아무도 관심을 두지 않으면서 \"그 법안\"의 효험에 대한 사람들의 미신적인 믿음을 비꼬는 쪽이었던 터라, 때로 윌에게 곤란한 질문을 던져 그를 공격하곤 했다."], ["One evening in March, Rosamond in her cherry-colored dress with swansdown trimming about the throat sat at the tea-table; Lydgate, lately come in tired from his outdoor work, was seated sideways on an easy-chair by the fire with one leg over the elbow, his brow looking a little troubled as his eyes rambled over the columns of the “Pioneer,”", "3월의 어느 저녁, 로저먼드는 목둘레에 백조 깃털 장식을 두른 체리 빛 드레스를 입고 찻상 앞에 앉아 있었다. 바깥일을 하다가 막 지쳐서 들어온 리드게이트는 난롯가 안락의자에 비스듬히 한쪽 다리를 팔걸이에 걸치고 앉아, 눈으로 『파이어니어』의 지면을 훑으며 이마를 다소 찌푸리고 있었고, 한편 로저먼드는 그가 심란해하는 것을 눈치채고서 그를 쳐다보지 않으려 했으며, 속으로는 자기 자신이 침울한 성정이 아닌 것을 하늘에 감사했다."], ["while Rosamond, having noticed that he was perturbed, avoided looking at him, and inwardly thanked heaven that she herself had not a moody disposition.", "윌 래디슬로는 깔개 위에 길게 드러누워 멍하니 커튼 봉을 바라보며 \"그대의 얼굴을 처음 보았을 때\"의 가락을 아주 낮게 흥얼거리고 있었다."], ["Will Ladislaw was stretched on the rug contemplating the curtain-pole abstractedly, and humming very low the notes of “When first I saw thy face;” while the house spaniel, also stretched out with small choice of room, looked from between his paws at the usurper of the rug with silent but strong objection.", "한편 집에서 기르는 스패니얼도 변변한 자리도 못 차지한 채 길게 늘어져서는, 앞발 사이로 깔개를 빼앗은 그 침입자를 말없이, 그러나 강하게 못마땅해하며 바라보고 있었다."]], [["Rosamond bringing Lydgate his cup of tea, he threw down the paper, and said to Will, who had started up and gone to the table—", "로저먼드가 리드게이트에게 찻잔을 가져다주자, 그는 신문을 내던지고는, 벌떡 일어나 찻상으로 다가온 윌에게 말했다."]], [["“It’s no use your puffing Brooke as a reforming landlord, Ladislaw: they only pick the more holes in his coat in the ‘Trumpet.’”", "\"브룩을 개혁적인 지주라고 추켜세워 봐야 소용없네, 래디슬로. 『트럼펫』에서 그자의 옷에 흠집만 더 후벼낼 뿐이니까.\""]], [["“No matter; those who read the ‘Pioneer’ don’t read the ‘Trumpet,’”", "\"상관없어요. 『파이어니어』를 읽는 사람들은 『트럼펫』을 안 읽으니까요.\""], ["said Will, swallowing his tea and walking about.", "윌이 차를 들이켜고 이리저리 거닐며 말했다."], ["“Do you suppose the public reads with a view to its own conversion?", "\"대중이 자기를 회심시키려는 마음으로 읽는다고 생각하세요?"], ["We should have a witches’ brewing with a vengeance then—‘Mingle, mingle, mingle, mingle, You that mingle may’—and nobody would know which side he was going to take.”", "그렇다면 우리는 정말이지 마녀들이 끓이는 가마솥 꼴이 될 겁니다—'섞어라, 섞어라, 섞어라, 섞어라, 섞을 수 있는 너희들이여'—그리고 아무도 자기가 어느 편을 들지 모르게 되겠지요.\"*\n* \"섞어라, 섞어라…\": 셰익스피어의 『맥베스』에서 마녀들이 가마솥 주위를 돌며 주문을 외는 장면을 빗댄 표현."]], [["“Farebrother says, he doesn’t believe Brooke would get elected if the opportunity came: the very men who profess to be for him would bring another member out of the bag at the right moment.”", "\"페어브러더 말로는, 기회가 와도 브룩이 당선될 것 같지는 않다더군. 그를 지지한다고 공언하는 바로 그 사람들이 결정적인 순간에 다른 후보를 자루에서 꺼내 들 거라고 말이야.\""]], [["“There’s no harm in trying.", "\"시도해 봐서 나쁠 건 없죠."], ["It’s good to have resident members.”", "지역에 거주하는 의원을 두는 건 좋은 일이고요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""], ["said Lydgate, who was much given to use that inconvenient word in a curt tone.", "리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 그 거북한 말을 퉁명스러운 어조로 곧잘 쓰는 사람이었다."]], [["“They represent the local stupidity better,”", "\"지역의 어리석음을 더 잘 대변하니까요.\""], ["said Will, laughing, and shaking his curls; “and they are kept on their best behavior in the neighborhood.", "윌이 곱슬머리를 흔들며 웃으면서 말했다. \"그리고 그런 의원들은 이웃 사람들 앞에서 행실을 가장 바르게 유지하게 되거든요."], ["Brooke is not a bad fellow, but he has done some good things on his estate that he never would have done but for this Parliamentary bite.”", "브룩이 나쁜 사람은 아니에요. 다만 이 의회 욕심이 아니었다면 결코 하지 않았을 좋은 일들을 자기 사유지에서 몇 가지 해 놓았지요.\""]], [["“He’s not fitted to be a public man,”", "\"그는 공인이 될 만한 사람이 아니야.\""], ["said Lydgate, with contemptuous decision.", "리드게이트가 경멸 섞인 단호함으로 말했다."], ["“He would disappoint everybody who counted on him: I can see that at the Hospital.", "\"자기를 믿었던 모든 사람을 실망시킬 거야. 나는 병원에서 그걸 알 수 있어."], ["Only, there Bulstrode holds the reins and drives him.”", "다만 거기서는 벌스트로드가 고삐를 쥐고 그를 몰고 가지.\""]], [["“That depends on how you fix your standard of public men,”", "\"그건 공인의 기준을 어디에 두느냐에 달렸죠.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“He’s good enough for the occasion: when the people have made up their mind as they are making it up now, they don’t want a man—they only want a vote.”", "\"이번 경우에는 그 정도면 충분합니다. 사람들이 지금처럼 마음을 굳히고 나면, 그들은 사람을 원하는 게 아니에요—그저 한 표를 원할 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“That is the way with you political writers, Ladislaw—crying up a measure as if it were a universal cure, and crying up men who are a part of the very disease that wants curing.”", "\"바로 그게 자네들 정치 평론가들의 방식이지, 래디슬로—어떤 법안을 만병통치약이라도 되는 양 떠받들고, 치유해야 할 바로 그 질병의 일부인 사람들을 추켜세우는 것 말일세.\""]], [["“Why not?", "\"안 될 거 있나요?"], ["Men may help to cure themselves off the face of the land without knowing it,”", "사람들은 자기도 모르는 사이에 자신들을 이 땅에서 치유해 없앨 수도 있는 거예요.\""], ["said Will, who could find reasons impromptu, when he had not thought of a question beforehand.", "윌이 말했다. 그는 미리 어떤 문제를 생각해 두지 않았을 때면 즉석에서 이유를 찾아낼 줄 아는 사람이었다."]], [["“That is no excuse for encouraging the superstitious exaggeration of hopes about this particular measure, helping the cry to swallow it whole and to send up voting popinjays who are good for nothing but to carry it.", "\"그렇다고 이 특정 법안에 대한 미신적이고 과장된 희망을 부추기는 것, 그것을 통째로 삼키라고 외치는 데 가세하고, 그걸 통과시키는 것 말고는 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 투표하는 앵무새들을 의회로 올려 보내는 것이 정당화되는 건 아니야."], ["You go against rottenness, and there is nothing more thoroughly rotten than making people believe that society can be cured by a political hocus-pocus.”", "자네는 부패에 맞선다고 하지만, 정치적 요술로 사회를 치유할 수 있다고 사람들을 믿게 만드는 것보다 더 철저히 썩은 건 없다네.\""]], [["“That’s very fine, my dear fellow.", "\"아주 훌륭한 말씀이군요, 선생님."], ["But your cure must begin somewhere, and put it that a thousand things which debase a population can never be reformed without this particular reform to begin with.", "하지만 선생님의 치료도 어딘가에서는 시작되어야 하고, 한 집단을 타락시키는 천 가지 것들이 우선 이 특정한 개혁 없이는 결코 바로잡힐 수 없다고 한번 가정해 보세요."], ["Look what Stanley said the other day—that the House had been tinkering long enough at small questions of bribery, inquiring whether this or that voter has had a guinea when everybody knows that the seats have been sold wholesale.", "얼마 전 스탠리가 한 말을 보세요—의석이 통째로 팔려 나갔다는 걸 누구나 아는 마당에, 의회가 이런저런 유권자가 1기니를 받았느냐 아니냐를 따지면서, 매수라는 자질구레한 문제나 너무 오래 만지작거려 왔다고 말이지요."], ["Wait for wisdom and conscience in public agents—fiddlestick!", "공직자들에게서 지혜와 양심이 나오기를 기다린다고요—말도 안 되는 소리!"], ["The only conscience we can trust to is the massive sense of wrong in a class, and the best wisdom that will work is the wisdom of balancing claims.", "우리가 믿을 수 있는 유일한 양심은 한 계급의 거대한 부당함의 감각이고, 실제로 작동할 최선의 지혜는 여러 요구를 견주어 저울질하는 지혜입니다."], ["That’s my text—which side is injured?", "그게 제 주장의 요지예요—어느 쪽이 피해를 보는가?"], ["I support the man who supports their claims; not the virtuous upholder of the wrong.”", "저는 그들의 요구를 지지하는 사람을 지지합니다. 부당함을 떠받드는 점잖은 옹호자가 아니라 말이지요.\""]], [["“That general talk about a particular case is mere question begging, Ladislaw.", "\"특정한 사안을 두고 그런 일반론을 펴는 건 그저 논점을 슬쩍 회피하는 거야, 래디슬로."], ["When I say, I go in for the dose that cures, it doesn’t follow that I go in for opium in a given case of gout.”", "내가 치유하는 약을 지지한다고 해서, 통풍이라는 특정한 경우에 아편을 지지한다는 결론이 나오는 건 아니란 말일세.\""]], [["“I am not begging the question we are upon—whether we are to try for nothing till we find immaculate men to work with.", "\"저는 우리가 논하는 논점을 회피하는 게 아닙니다—즉, 함께 일할 흠 하나 없는 사람들을 찾을 때까지 아무것도 시도하지 말아야 하느냐 하는 점 말이지요."], ["Should you go on that plan?", "선생님이라면 그런 방침대로 하시겠어요?"], ["If there were one man who would carry you a medical reform and another who would oppose it, should you inquire which had the better motives or even the better brains?”", "만약 선생님께 의료 개혁을 추진해 줄 사람 하나와 그것을 반대할 사람 하나가 있다면, 어느 쪽이 더 나은 동기를, 아니 더 나은 머리를 가졌는지 따지시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, of course,”", "\"아, 물론 아니지.\""], ["said Lydgate, seeing himself checkmated by a move which he had often used himself, “if one did not work with such men as are at hand, things must come to a dead-lock.", "리드게이트가, 자기도 종종 써먹던 수에 자기가 외통수에 걸린 것을 깨닫고 말했다. \"지금 곁에 있는 그런 사람들과 일하지 않는다면, 만사가 꽉 막혀 버리고 말 테니까."], ["Suppose the worst opinion in the town about Bulstrode were a true one, that would not make it less true that he has the sense and the resolution to do what I think ought to be done in the matters I know and care most about; but that is the only ground on which I go with him,”", "벌스트로드에 대한 읍내의 가장 나쁜 평판이 사실이라고 한들, 내가 가장 잘 알고 가장 마음 쓰는 일들에서 마땅히 해야 한다고 생각하는 바를 행할 분별력과 결단력이 그에게 있다는 사실이 덜 진실해지지는 않아. 하지만 내가 그와 함께하는 건 오직 그 점 때문일세.\""], ["Lydgate added rather proudly, bearing in mind Mr. Farebrother’s remarks.", "리드게이트는 페어브러더 씨의 말을 떠올리며 다소 자랑스럽게 덧붙였다."], ["“He is nothing to me otherwise; I would not cry him up on any personal ground—I would keep clear of that.”", "\"그 밖에 그는 내게 아무것도 아니야. 어떤 개인적인 이유로도 그를 추켜세우진 않을 거고—그런 건 멀리할 걸세.\""]], [["“Do you mean that I cry up Brooke on any personal ground?”", "\"그럼 제가 어떤 개인적인 이유로 브룩을 추켜세운다는 말씀입니까?\" 윌 래디슬로가 발끈하며 홱 돌아서서 말했다."], ["said Will Ladislaw, nettled, and turning sharp round.", "그가 리드게이트에게 기분이 상한 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["For the first time he felt offended with Lydgate; not the less so, perhaps, because he would have declined any close inquiry into the growth of his relation to Mr. Brooke.", "어쩌면 그가 브룩 씨와의 관계가 어떻게 자라 왔는지 면밀히 캐묻는 것을 사양했으리라는 점 때문에 더더욱 그러했는지도 모른다."]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"전혀 아닐세.\""], ["said Lydgate, “I was simply explaining my own action.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"나는 그저 내 행동을 설명하고 있었네."], ["I meant that a man may work for a special end with others whose motives and general course are equivocal, if he is quite sure of his personal independence, and that he is not working for his private interest—either place or money.”", "내 말은, 자기의 개인적 독립을 확신하고, 또 자기가 사사로운 이익—지위든 돈이든—을 위해 일하는 게 아니라는 것을 확신한다면, 동기와 전반적 행로가 미심쩍은 사람들과도 어떤 특정한 목적을 위해 함께 일할 수 있다는 것이었네.\""]], [["“Then, why don’t you extend your liberality to others?”", "\"그럼, 그 너그러움을 왜 남들에게는 베풀지 않으십니까?\""], ["said Will, still nettled.", "여전히 발끈한 채 윌이 말했다."], ["“My personal independence is as important to me as yours is to you.", "\"제 개인적 독립은 선생님의 것이 선생님께 중요한 만큼이나 제게 중요합니다."], ["You have no more reason to imagine that I have personal expectations from Brooke, than I have to imagine that you have personal expectations from Bulstrode.", "선생님께서 제가 브룩에게서 개인적인 기대를 품고 있다고 상상하실 이유는, 제가 선생님께서 벌스트로드에게서 개인적인 기대를 품고 있다고 상상할 이유보다 조금도 더 많지 않아요."], ["Motives are points of honor, I suppose—nobody can prove them.", "동기란 명예에 관한 문제겠지요—아무도 그것을 입증할 수는 없으니까요."], ["But as to money and place in the world,”", "하지만 세상의 돈이나 지위로 말하자면,\""], ["Will ended, tossing back his head, “I think it is pretty clear that I am not determined by considerations of that sort.”", "윌이 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 끝맺었다. \"제가 그런 종류의 고려에 좌우되는 사람이 아니라는 건 꽤 분명하다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“You quite mistake me, Ladislaw,”", "\"자네는 나를 완전히 오해했네, 래디슬로.\""], ["said Lydgate, surprised.", "리드게이트가 놀라서 말했다."], ["He had been preoccupied with his own vindication, and had been blind to what Ladislaw might infer on his own account.", "그는 자기 자신을 변호하는 데 골몰해 있었던 터라, 래디슬로가 자기 처지에 비추어 무엇을 추론할지 미처 보지 못했던 것이다."], ["“I beg your pardon for unintentionally annoying you.", "\"본의 아니게 자네를 화나게 해서 미안하네."], ["In fact, I should rather attribute to you a romantic disregard of your own worldly interests.", "사실 나는 오히려 자네가 자기 자신의 세속적 이익을 낭만적으로 무시한다고 여기고 있어."], ["On the political question, I referred simply to intellectual bias.”", "정치 문제에 관해서는, 나는 그저 지적인 편향을 두고 한 말이었네.\""]], [["“How very unpleasant you both are this evening!”", "\"두 분 다 오늘 저녁엔 어쩜 그렇게 불쾌하게 구세요!\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“I cannot conceive why money should have been referred to.", "\"왜 돈 이야기까지 나와야 하는지 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["Politics and Medicine are sufficiently disagreeable to quarrel upon.", "정치와 의학만으로도 다투기엔 충분히 거북한 화제잖아요."], ["You can both of you go on quarrelling with all the world and with each other on those two topics.”", "두 분 다 그 두 가지 주제를 가지고 온 세상과, 또 서로와 얼마든지 계속 다투세요.\""]], [["Rosamond looked mildly neutral as she said this, rising to ring the bell, and then crossing to her work-table.", "로저먼드는 이렇게 말하며 온화하게 중립을 지키는 표정으로 일어나 종을 울리고는, 자기 반짇고리 탁자 쪽으로 건너갔다."]], [["“Poor Rosy!”", "\"가엾은 로지!\""], ["said Lydgate, putting out his hand to her as she was passing him.", "리드게이트가, 그녀가 자기 곁을 지날 때 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“Disputation is not amusing to cherubs.", "\"논쟁은 천사들에게는 재미없는 거지."], ["Have some music.", "음악이나 좀 들려주구려."], ["Ask Ladislaw to sing with you.”", "래디슬로한테 같이 노래하자고 청해 봐요.\""]], [["When Will was gone Rosamond said to her husband, “What put you out of temper this evening, Tertius?”", "윌이 가고 나자 로저먼드가 남편에게 말했다. \"오늘 저녁엔 무슨 일로 그렇게 언짢으셨어요, 터셔스?\""]], [["“Me?", "\"내가?"], ["It was Ladislaw who was out of temper.", "언짢았던 건 래디슬로였소."], ["He is like a bit of tinder.”", "그 친구는 부싯깃 같으니까.\""]], [["“But I mean, before that.", "\"하지만 제 말은, 그 전에요."], ["Something had vexed you before you came in, you looked cross.", "들어오시기 전에 뭔가 당신을 화나게 한 일이 있었던 거예요. 못마땅한 표정이셨거든요."], ["And that made you begin to dispute with Mr.", "그래서 래디슬로 씨와 언쟁을 시작하신 거고요."], ["Ladislaw. You hurt me very much when you look so, Tertius.”", "당신이 그런 표정을 지으시면 저는 무척 마음이 아파요, 터셔스.\""]], [["“Do I?", "\"그랬소?"], ["Then I am a brute,”", "그렇다면 내가 짐승 같은 놈이군.\""], ["said Lydgate, caressing her penitently.", "리드게이트가 뉘우치듯 그녀를 어루만지며 말했다."]], [["“What vexed you?”", "\"무슨 일로 화나셨어요?\""]], [["“Oh, outdoor things—business.”", "\"아, 바깥일이오—사업 일.\""], ["It was really a letter insisting on the payment of a bill for furniture.", "사실은 가구값 청구서의 지불을 독촉하는 편지였다."], ["But Rosamond was expecting to have a baby, and Lydgate wished to save her from any perturbation.", "하지만 로저먼드는 아이를 가질 예정이었고, 리드게이트는 그녀가 어떤 동요도 겪지 않도록 지켜 주고 싶었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVII.", "# CHAPTER XLVII."]], [["Was never true love loved in vain, For truest love is highest gain. No art can make it: it must spring Where elements are fostering.", "가장 진실한 사랑이 가장 높은 보람이라."], ["So in heaven’s spot and hour Springs the little native flower, Downward root and upward eye, Shapen by the earth and sky.", "어떤 기교도 그것을 빚어내지 못하니, 반드시 솟아나야 하리,"]], [["It happened to be on a Saturday evening that Will Ladislaw had that little discussion with Lydgate. Its effect when he went to his own rooms was to make him sit up half the night, thinking over again, under a new irritation, all that he had before thought of his having settled in Middlemarch and harnessed himself with Mr. Brooke. Hesitations before he had taken the step had since turned into susceptibility to every hint that he would have been wiser not to take it; and hence came his heat towards Lydgate—a heat which still kept him restless. Was he not making a fool of himself?—and at a time when he was more than ever conscious of being something better than a fool? And for what end?", "온갖 요소들이 길러 주는 그곳에서. 그리하여 하늘이 정한 자리와 시각에"]], [["Well, for no definite end. True, he had dreamy visions of possibilities: there is no human being who having both passions and thoughts does not think in consequence of his passions—does not find images rising in his mind which soothe the passion with hope or sting it with dread. But this, which happens to us all, happens to some with a wide difference; and Will was not one of those whose wit “keeps the roadway:” he had his bypaths where there were little joys of his own choosing, such as gentlemen cantering on the highroad might have thought rather idiotic. The way in which he made a sort of happiness for himself out of his feeling for Dorothea was an example of this. It may seem strange, but it is the fact, that the ordinary vulgar vision of which Mr. Casaubon suspected him—namely, that Dorothea might become a widow, and that the interest he had established in her mind might turn into acceptance of him as a husband—had no tempting, arresting power over him; he did not live in the scenery of such an event, and follow it out, as we all do with that imagined “otherwise” which is our practical heaven. It was not only that he was unwilling to entertain thoughts which could be accused of baseness, and was already uneasy in the sense that he had to justify himself from the charge of ingratitude—the latent consciousness of many other barriers between himself and Dorothea besides the existence of her husband, had helped to turn away his imagination from speculating on what might befall Mr. Casaubon. And there were yet other reasons. Will, we know, could not bear the thought of any flaw appearing in his crystal: he was at once exasperated and delighted by the calm freedom with which Dorothea looked at him and spoke to him, and there was something so exquisite in thinking of her just as she was, that he could not long for a change which must somehow change her. Do we not shun the street version of a fine melody?—or shrink from the news that the rarity—some bit of chiselling or engraving perhaps—which we have dwelt on even with exultation in the trouble it has cost us to snatch glimpses of it, is really not an uncommon thing, and may be obtained as an every-day possession? Our good depends on the quality and breadth of our emotion; and to Will, a creature who cared little for what are called the solid things of life and greatly for its subtler influences, to have within him such a feeling as he had towards Dorothea, was like the inheritance of a fortune. What others might have called the futility of his passion, made an additional delight for his imagination: he was conscious of a generous movement, and of verifying in his own experience that higher love-poetry which had charmed his fancy. Dorothea, he said to himself, was forever enthroned in his soul: no other woman could sit higher than her footstool; and if he could have written out in immortal syllables the effect she wrought within him, he might have boasted after the example of old Drayton, that,—", "작은 토종 꽃 한 송이 피어나니,"]], [["“Queens hereafter might be glad to live Upon the alms of her superfluous praise.”", "뿌리는 아래로, 눈길은 위로 향한 채,\n땅과 하늘이 그 모양을 빚어내네.」"]], [["But this result was questionable.", "윌 라디슬로가 리드게이트와 그 짧은 언쟁을 나눈 것은 어느 토요일 저녁의 일이었다."], ["And what else could he do for Dorothea? What was his devotion worth to her?", "자기 방으로 돌아갔을 때 그 언쟁의 여파로 그는 밤의 절반을 자지 않고 앉아, 새로운 짜증에 사로잡힌 채 미들마치에 정착하여 브룩 씨와 한 멍에를 진 일에 관해 전에 생각했던 모든 것을 다시금 곱씹었다."], ["It was impossible to tell. He would not go out of her reach.", "그 발걸음을 내딛기 전의 망설임은 그 후로 차라리 그러지 않는 편이 현명했으리라는 온갖 암시에 예민하게 반응하는 마음으로 바뀌었고, 그래서 리드게이트를 향한 그의 격앙이 생겨난 것이었으니, 그 격앙은 여전히 그를 안절부절못하게 했다."], ["He saw no creature among her friends to whom he could believe that she spoke with the same simple confidence as to him.", "그는 자기 자신을 바보로 만들고 있는 것은 아닐까? 그것도 자신이 바보보다는 더 나은 존재라는 것을 그 어느 때보다 절실히 의식하던 때에?"], ["She had once said that she would like him to stay; and stay he would, whatever fire-breathing dragons might hiss around her.", "게다가 무슨 목적을 위해서?"]], [["This had always been the conclusion of Will’s hesitations. But he was not without contradictoriness and rebellion even towards his own resolve. He had often got irritated, as he was on this particular night, by some outside demonstration that his public exertions with Mr. Brooke as a chief could not seem as heroic as he would like them to be, and this was always associated with the other ground of irritation—that notwithstanding his sacrifice of dignity for Dorothea’s sake, he could hardly ever see her. Whereupon, not being able to contradict these unpleasant facts, he contradicted his own strongest bias and said, “I am a fool.”", "그렇다, 어떤 명확한 목적도 없었다. 사실 그에게는 가능성에 대한 몽롱한 환상이 있었다. 열정과 사유를 함께 지닌 인간치고 자신의 열정의 결과로 사유하지 않는 자는 없으며, 그 열정을 희망으로 달래거나 두려움으로 찌르는 심상들이 마음속에 떠오르는 것을 경험하지 않는 자는 없다. 하지만 우리 모두에게 일어나는 이 일이 어떤 이들에게는 사뭇 다른 양상으로 일어나는 법이니, 윌은 그 기지가 \"큰길을 벗어나지 않는\" 부류의 사람이 아니었다. 그에게는 제 나름대로 고른 자그마한 기쁨이 깃든 샛길들이 있었는데, 큰길에서 말을 달리는 신사들이라면 다소 어리석다 여겼을 법한 것들이었다. 도러시아에 대한 감정으로부터 그가 일종의 행복을 빚어낸 방식이 바로 그런 예였다. 이상하게 들릴지 모르나 사실인즉, 캐소본 씨가 그에게 품었던 의심, 다시 말해 도러시아가 과부가 될 수도 있고 그녀의 마음속에 그가 심어 둔 관심이 그를 남편으로 받아들이는 데까지 이를 수도 있다는 그 흔하고 속된 상상은 그에게 아무런 유혹의 힘도, 사로잡는 힘도 없었다. 그는 우리 모두가 우리의 실질적인 천국인 상상 속의 \"만약 달리 되었더라면\"을 두고 그러하듯, 그러한 사건의 정경 속에 머무르며 그것을 끝까지 그려 나가지 않았다. 비난받을 만한 비열함이 깃든 생각을 품기를 꺼렸고, 배은망덕하다는 비난으로부터 스스로를 변호해야 한다는 의식에 이미 불편했기 때문만은 아니었다. 그녀의 남편이 존재한다는 것 말고도 자신과 도러시아 사이에 가로놓인 여러 다른 장벽에 대한 잠재된 의식이, 캐소본 씨에게 무슨 일이 일어날지 추측하는 데서 그의 상상력을 돌려세우는 데 일조했던 것이다. 게다가 또 다른 이유들도 있었다. 우리가 알다시피 윌은 자신의 수정에 어떤 흠이 나타난다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었다. 도러시아가 그를 바라보고 그에게 말을 거는 그 차분한 자유로움에 그는 격분하면서도 동시에 기뻐했으며, 그녀를 있는 그대로 떠올리는 데 너무도 그윽한 무언가가 있었기에, 어떻게든 그녀를 변하게 할 변화를 갈망할 수가 없었다. 우리는 아름다운 선율의 거리 버전을 피하지 않는가? 혹은 우리가 어쩌다 한 번 엿보려고 애쓴 그 수고를 도리어 기뻐하며 마음에 두고 음미해 온 진귀한 것, 어쩌면 조각이나 판화 한 점이 실은 흔한 것이며 누구나 일상적으로 소유할 수 있다는 소식 앞에서 움츠러들지 않는가? 우리의 행복은 우리 감정의 질과 폭에 달려 있는 법이니, 흔히 인생의 견실한 것들이라 불리는 것에는 거의 관심이 없고 그 미묘한 영향력에 크게 마음을 쓰는 윌 같은 사람에게, 도러시아를 향한 것과 같은 감정을 마음에 품는 것은 한 재산을 물려받는 것이나 다름없었다. 다른 사람들이라면 그의 열정의 부질없음이라 불렀을 것이, 그의 상상력에는 또 다른 기쁨을 더해 주었다. 그는 자신이 너그러운 마음의 움직임을 지녔음을 의식했고, 자신의 공상을 매혹했던 저 드높은 사랑의 시를 제 경험 속에서 확인하고 있음을 의식했다. 도러시아는, 그는 혼잣말을 했다, 그의 영혼 속에 영원히 군림하고 있다고. 다른 어떤 여인도 그녀의 발판보다 더 높이 앉을 수 없으리라고. 그리고 만약 그녀가 그의 내면에 일으킨 효과를 불멸의 음절로 적어 낼 수 있었다면, 그는 옛 드레이턴을 본받아 이렇게 자랑했을지도 모른다. 「훗날의 여왕들이 기꺼이 살아가리라,"]], [["Nevertheless, since the inward debate necessarily turned on Dorothea, he ended, as he had done before, only by getting a livelier sense of what her presence would be to him; and suddenly reflecting that the morrow would be Sunday, he determined to go to Lowick Church and see her. He slept upon that idea, but when he was dressing in the rational morning light, Objection said—", "그녀가 넘치도록 베푼 찬미의 적선에 기대어.」 하지만 이런 결과는 의심스러웠다. 그렇다면 그가 도러시아를 위해 그 밖에 무엇을 할 수 있단 말인가? 그의 헌신이 그녀에게 무슨 가치가 있단 말인가? 알 수 없는 일이었다. 그는 그녀의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 떠나지 않으리라. 그녀의 벗들 가운데 그녀가 자기에게 하듯 그토록 소박한 신뢰로 말을 건넬 만한 사람은 하나도 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 언젠가 그가 머물러 주었으면 좋겠다고 말한 적이 있었으니, 그는 머물 것이었다. 어떤 불을 뿜는 용들이 그녀의 주위에서 쉭쉭거린다 해도."]], [["“That will be a virtual defiance of Mr. Casaubon’s prohibition to visit Lowick, and Dorothea will be displeased.”", "이것이 언제나 윌의 망설임이 다다른 결론이었다. 하지만 그는 자신의 결심에 대해서조차 모순과 반항이 없지 않았다. 그날 밤 그러했듯이, 그는 종종 짜증이 나곤 했다. 브룩 씨를 우두머리로 삼은 자신의 공적인 노력이 그가 바라는 만큼 영웅적으로 보일 수 없다는 어떤 외부의 표시 때문에. 그리고 이것은 늘 또 다른 짜증의 근원과 결부되어 있었으니, 도러시아를 위해 품위를 희생했음에도 그녀를 좀처럼 볼 수 없다는 것이었다. 그리하여 이 불쾌한 사실들을 반박할 수 없게 되자, 그는 자기 자신의 가장 강한 성향을 반박하며 이렇게 말했다. \"나는 바보다.\""]], [["“Nonsense!”", "그럼에도 불구하고, 그 내면의 논쟁이 필연적으로 도러시아를 중심으로 돌았기에, 그는 전에 그랬던 것처럼 그녀의 존재가 자신에게 어떤 의미일지 더욱 생생하게 느끼게 되는 것으로 끝맺을 뿐이었다."], ["argued Inclination, “it would be too monstrous for him to hinder me from going out to a pretty country church on a spring morning.", "그러다 문득 내일이 일요일이라는 생각이 떠오르자, 그는 로윅 교회에 가서 그녀를 보기로 마음먹었다. 그는 그 생각을 품은 채 잠들었으나, 이성적인 아침 햇살 속에서 옷을 입을 때 반대의 목소리가 말했다."], ["And Dorothea will be glad.”", "\"그건 로윅을 방문하지 말라는 캐소본 씨의 금지에 대한 사실상의 도전이 될 테고, 도러시아도 언짢아할 거야.\""]], [["“It will be clear to Mr.", "\"말도 안 돼!\" 욕망이 항변했다."], ["Casaubon that you have come either to annoy him or to see Dorothea.”", "\"봄날 아침에 어여쁜 시골 교회에 나가는 것조차 그가 막는다면 그건 너무 터무니없는 일이지. 그리고 도러시아도 기뻐할 거야.\""]], [["“It is not true that I go to annoy him, and why should I not go to see Dorothea?", "\"네가 그를 성가시게 하려고 왔거나 아니면 도러시아를 보려고 왔다는 게 캐소본 씨에게는 빤히 보일 거야.\""], ["Is he to have everything to himself and be always comfortable?", "\"내가 그를 성가시게 하려고 간다는 건 사실이 아니고, 도러시아를 보러 가면 안 될 이유가 어디 있어? 그는 모든 걸 혼자 독차지하고 늘 편안해야만 하나?"], ["Let him smart a little, as other people are obliged to do.", "다른 사람들이 그래야만 하듯, 그도 좀 따끔한 맛을 봐도 되지. 나는 늘 그 교회와 신도들의 예스러운 정취를 좋아했어."], ["I have always liked the quaintness of the church and congregation; besides, I know the Tuckers: I shall go into their pew.”", "게다가 나는 터커 일가를 알지. 그들의 자리에 들어가 앉으면 돼.\""]], [["Having silenced Objection by force of unreason, Will walked to Lowick as if he had been on the way to Paradise, crossing Halsell Common and skirting the wood, where the sunlight fell broadly under the budding boughs, bringing out the beauties of moss and lichen, and fresh green growths piercing the brown.", "비합리의 힘으로 반대의 목소리를 잠재운 뒤, 윌은 마치 천국으로 향하는 길에 오른 것처럼 로윅을 향해 걸어갔다. 그는 핼셀 공유지를 가로지르고 숲가를 따라 걸었는데, 그곳에는 움트는 가지들 아래로 햇빛이 넓게 쏟아져 이끼와 지의의 아름다움을, 그리고 갈색 흙을 뚫고 나온 싱그러운 초록의 새싹들을 도드라지게 드러내고 있었다."], ["Everything seemed to know that it was Sunday, and to approve of his going to Lowick Church.", "만물이 그날이 일요일임을 아는 듯했고, 그가 로윅 교회에 가는 것을 흡족해하는 듯했다."], ["Will easily felt happy when nothing crossed his humor, and by this time the thought of vexing Mr.", "윌은 자신의 기분을 거스르는 것이 아무것도 없을 때면 쉽사리 행복해졌으니, 이즈음에는 캐소본 씨를 약 올린다는 생각이 도리어 꽤 즐거워져서, 그의 얼굴에 명랑한 미소가 번지게 했다."], ["Casaubon had become rather amusing to him, making his face break into its merry smile, pleasant to see as the breaking of sunshine on the water—though the occasion was not exemplary.", "물밑에서 햇살이 부서지는 것처럼 보기 좋은 미소였다. 비록 그 동기는 본받을 만한 것이 못 되었지만 말이다."], ["But most of us are apt to settle within ourselves that the man who blocks our way is odious, and not to mind causing him a little of the disgust which his personality excites in ourselves.", "하지만 우리 대부분은 우리의 길을 가로막는 사람을 가증스럽다고 마음속으로 단정해 버리고, 그의 인품이 우리 안에 불러일으키는 그 혐오를 그에게 조금 안겨 주는 것을 개의치 않는 경향이 있다."], ["Will went along with a small book under his arm and a hand in each side-pocket, never reading, but chanting a little, as he made scenes of what would happen in church and coming out.", "윌은 작은 책 한 권을 옆구리에 끼고 양손을 옆주머니에 찔러 넣은 채 걸어갔는데, 책을 읽기는커녕 교회 안에서 그리고 나올 때 벌어질 장면을 머릿속에 그리며 흥얼흥얼 작은 노래를 불렀다."], ["He was experimenting in tunes to suit some words of his own, sometimes trying a ready-made melody, sometimes improvising.", "그는 자기가 지은 가사에 맞을 곡조를 이리저리 시험하고 있었으니, 때로는 기성의 선율을 붙여 보고 때로는 즉흥적으로 지어냈다."], ["The words were not exactly a hymn, but they certainly fitted his Sunday experience:—", "그 가사는 정확히 찬송가는 아니었으나, 분명 그의 일요일 경험에는 들어맞았다. 「오 나여, 오 나여, 어찌 이리 보잘것없는 양식을"]], [["“O me, O me, what frugal cheer My love doth feed upon!", "내 사랑은 먹고 사는가!"], ["A touch, a ray, that is not here, A shadow that is gone:", "여기에 없는 손길 하나, 빛줄기 하나,"]], [["“A dream of breath that might be near, An inly-echoed tone, The thought that one may think me dear, The place where one was known,", "이미 사라진 그림자 하나. 여기 있을지도 모를 숨결의 꿈 하나,"]], [["“The tremor of a banished fear, An ill that was not done— O me, O me, what frugal cheer My love doth feed upon!”", "마음속에 울리는 가락 하나,\n누군가 나를 소중히 여길지도 모른다는 생각,"]], [["Sometimes, when he took off his hat, shaking his head backward, and showing his delicate throat as he sang, he looked like an incarnation of the spring whose spirit filled the air—a bright creature, abundant in uncertain promises.", "누군가에게 알려졌던 그 자리,\n물러간 두려움의 떨림 하나,"]], [["The bells were still ringing when he got to Lowick, and he went into the curate’s pew before any one else arrived there. But he was still left alone in it when the congregation had assembled. The curate’s pew was opposite the rector’s at the entrance of the small chancel, and Will had time to fear that Dorothea might not come while he looked round at the group of rural faces which made the congregation from year to year within the white-washed walls and dark old pews, hardly with more change than we see in the boughs of a tree which breaks here and there with age, but yet has young shoots. Mr. Rigg’s frog-face was something alien and unaccountable, but notwithstanding this shock to the order of things, there were still the Waules and the rural stock of the Powderells in their pews side by side; brother Samuel’s cheek had the same purple round as ever, and the three generations of decent cottagers came as of old with a sense of duty to their betters generally—the smaller children regarding Mr. Casaubon, who wore the black gown and mounted to the highest box, as probably the chief of all betters, and the one most awful if offended. Even in 1831 Lowick was at peace, not more agitated by Reform than by the solemn tenor of the Sunday sermon. The congregation had been used to seeing Will at church in former days, and no one took much note of him except the choir, who expected him to make a figure in the singing.", "저질러지지 않은 잘못 하나. 오 나여, 오 나여, 어찌 이리 보잘것없는 양식을"]], [["Dorothea did at last appear on this quaint background, walking up the short aisle in her white beaver bonnet and gray cloak—the same she had worn in the Vatican. Her face being, from her entrance, towards the chancel, even her shortsighted eyes soon discerned Will, but there was no outward show of her feeling except a slight paleness and a grave bow as she passed him. To his own surprise Will felt suddenly uncomfortable, and dared not look at her after they had bowed to each other. Two minutes later, when Mr. Casaubon came out of the vestry, and, entering the pew, seated himself in face of Dorothea, Will felt his paralysis more complete. He could look nowhere except at the choir in the little gallery over the vestry-door: Dorothea was perhaps pained, and he had made a wretched blunder. It was no longer amusing to vex Mr. Casaubon, who had the advantage probably of watching him and seeing that he dared not turn his head. Why had he not imagined this beforehand?—but he could not expect that he should sit in that square pew alone, unrelieved by any Tuckers, who had apparently departed from Lowick altogether, for a new clergyman was in the desk. Still he called himself stupid now for not foreseeing that it would be impossible for him to look towards Dorothea—nay, that she might feel his coming an impertinence. There was no delivering himself from his cage, however; and Will found his places and looked at his book as if he had been a school-mistress, feeling that the morning service had never been so immeasurably long before, that he was utterly ridiculous, out of temper, and miserable. This was what a man got by worshipping the sight of a woman! The clerk observed with surprise that Mr. Ladislaw did not join in the tune of Hanover, and reflected that he might have a cold.", "내 사랑은 먹고 사는가!」 이따금 그가 모자를 벗고 고개를 뒤로 젖히며, 노래를 부르느라 가냘픈 목을 드러낼 때면, 그는 공기를 가득 채운 봄의 정령이 육화한 모습 같았으니, 불확실한 약속으로 충만한 빛나는 존재였다."]], [["Mr. Casaubon did not preach that morning, and there was no change in Will’s situation until the blessing had been pronounced and every one rose. It was the fashion at Lowick for “the betters” to go out first. With a sudden determination to break the spell that was upon him, Will looked straight at Mr. Casaubon. But that gentleman’s eyes were on the button of the pew-door, which he opened, allowing Dorothea to pass, and following her immediately without raising his eyelids. Will’s glance had caught Dorothea’s as she turned out of the pew, and again she bowed, but this time with a look of agitation, as if she were repressing tears. Will walked out after them, but they went on towards the little gate leading out of the churchyard into the shrubbery, never looking round.", "그가 로윅에 이르렀을 때 종은 아직 울리고 있었고, 그는 다른 누구도 도착하기 전에 부목사석으로 들어갔다. 하지만 신도들이 다 모인 뒤에도 그는 여전히 그 자리에 홀로 남겨졌다. 부목사석은 작은 성단소 입구에서 교구 목사석과 마주 보고 있었으니, 윌은 도러시아가 오지 않을지도 모른다고 두려워할 겨를이 있었다. 그러는 동안 그는 회칠한 벽과 어두운 옛 신도석 안에서 해마다 회중을 이루는 시골 사람들의 얼굴 무리를 둘러보았는데, 그 변화란 늙어 여기저기 부러지면서도 여전히 어린 가지를 틔우는 나뭇가지에서 우리가 보는 것보다 그리 크지 않았다. 리그 씨의 개구리 같은 얼굴은 무언가 낯설고 영문 모를 것이었으나, 이렇게 사물의 질서에 가해진 충격에도 불구하고, 거기에는 여전히 월 일가와 파우더렐 집안의 시골 혈통이 나란히 자기 자리에 앉아 있었다. 동생 새뮤얼의 뺨에는 여전히 예전과 똑같은 자줏빛 둘레가 있었고, 점잖은 소작농의 삼 대가 옛날과 다름없이 자기네 윗사람들에 대한 전반적인 의무감을 품고 왔다. 어린아이들은 검은 가운을 입고 가장 높은 칸에 오르는 캐소본 씨를 아마도 모든 윗사람들 중의 으뜸이요, 노여움을 사면 가장 무시무시한 분이라 여겼다. 1831년에도 로윅은 평온했으니, 개혁이라는 것에도 일요일 설교의 엄숙한 가락에 흔들리는 것 이상으로 동요하지 않았다. 신도들은 예전에 윌이 교회에 오는 것을 보는 데 익숙했으므로, 그가 노래에서 한몫할 것을 기대한 성가대 말고는 아무도 그를 별로 눈여겨보지 않았다. 도러시아가 마침내 이 예스러운 배경 속에 나타나, 흰 비버 가죽 보닛과 회색 망토 차림으로 짧은 통로를 걸어 올라왔으니, 바티칸에서 입었던 바로 그 차림이었다. 들어서는 순간부터 그녀의 얼굴이 성단소 쪽을 향하고 있었기에, 근시인 그녀의 눈도 이내 윌을 알아보았으나, 그녀의 감정이 겉으로 드러난 것이라곤 그를 지나칠 때의 가벼운 창백함과 진중한 목례뿐이었다. 윌은 자기 자신도 놀랍게 갑자기 거북해져서, 서로 목례를 나눈 뒤에는 감히 그녀를 쳐다보지 못했다. 이 분 후, 캐소본 씨가 제의실에서 나와 신도석으로 들어가 도러시아를 마주 보고 앉자, 윌은 자신의 마비가 한층 완전해지는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 제의실 문 위 작은 회랑의 성가대 말고는 어디도 쳐다볼 수 없었다. 도러시아는 어쩌면 마음 아파하고 있을지도 모르고, 그는 비참한 실수를 저지른 것이었다. 캐소본 씨를 약 올리는 것은 이제 더는 즐겁지 않았으니, 그 사람은 아마도 윌을 지켜보며 그가 감히 고개를 돌리지 못한다는 것을 알아챈 유리한 입장에 있었다. 그는 어째서 이것을 미리 상상하지 못했던가? 하지만 그가 그 네모난 신도석에 홀로 앉아 있게 되리라고는, 어떤 터커 일가의 사람도 곁에 없어 거북함을 덜어 주지 못하리라고는 예상할 수 없었다. 그들은 분명 로윅을 아주 떠난 모양이었으니, 새 성직자가 강독대에 서 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 그는 도러시아 쪽을 바라보는 일이 자신에게 불가능하리라는 것을, 아니 그녀가 자신이 온 것을 무례하다고 느낄지도 모른다는 것을 예견하지 못한 일로 이제 스스로를 어리석다 책망했다. 그러나 자신의 새장에서 빠져나올 길은 없었다. 윌은 마치 자기가 여교사라도 된 양 자기 차례의 구절들을 찾아 책을 들여다보면서, 아침 예배가 전에는 한 번도 이토록 한없이 길었던 적이 없었다고, 자기가 더없이 우스꽝스럽고, 기분이 상했으며, 비참하다고 느꼈다. 한 여인의 모습을 흠모하여 한 남자가 얻은 것이 고작 이것이었다! 교회지기는 라디슬로 씨가 하노버 곡조에 함께 부르지 않는 것을 놀랍게 여기고는, 그가 감기에 걸린 모양이라고 생각했다."]], [["It was impossible for him to follow them, and he could only walk back sadly at mid-day along the same road which he had trodden hopefully in the morning. The lights were all changed for him both without and within.", "캐소본 씨는 그날 아침 설교를 하지 않았으며, 축도가 끝나고 모두가 자리에서 일어날 때까지 윌의 처지에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 로윅에서는 \"윗사람들\"이 먼저 나가는 것이 관례였다. 자신을 짓누르는 주문을 깨뜨리겠다는 갑작스러운 결심으로, 윌은 캐소본 씨를 똑바로 쳐다보았다. 하지만 그 신사의 눈길은 신도석 문의 단추에 가 있었으니, 그는 그 문을 열어 도러시아를 먼저 지나가게 한 다음, 눈꺼풀을 들지도 않은 채 곧바로 그녀를 뒤따라갔다. 윌의 눈길은 도러시아가 신도석에서 나설 때 그녀의 눈길과 마주쳤고, 그녀는 다시 한번 목례했으나 이번에는 동요한 기색이었으니, 마치 눈물을 억누르고 있는 듯했다. 윌은 그들을 따라 걸어 나갔으나, 그들은 교회 묘지에서 관목 숲으로 통하는 작은 문 쪽으로 한 번도 뒤돌아보지 않고 가 버렸다. 그가 그들을 뒤따르기란 불가능했으니, 그는 그저 한낮에, 아침에 희망에 차서 밟았던 바로 그 길을 따라 쓸쓸히 걸어 돌아올 수 있을 뿐이었다. 밖에서도 안에서도 그의 빛은 모두 바뀌어 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLVIII.", "# CHAPTER XLVIII."]], [["Surely the golden hours are turning gray And dance no more, and vainly strive to run: I see their white locks streaming in the wind— Each face is haggard as it looks at me, Slow turning in the constant clasping round Storm-driven.", "분명 황금빛 시간들은 잿빛으로 바뀌어 가고 더는 춤추지 않으며, 달려 보려 헛되이 애쓴다. 나는 그들의 흰 머리채가 바람에 흩날리는 것을 본다— 저마다의 얼굴은 나를 바라보며 초췌하고, 폭풍에 떠밀려 쉼 없이 맞잡고 도는 그 회전 속에서 천천히 돌아선다."]], [["Dorothea’s distress when she was leaving the church came chiefly from the perception that Mr. Casaubon was determined not to speak to his cousin, and that Will’s presence at church had served to mark more strongly the alienation between them.", "도러시아가 교회를 나서며 느낀 괴로움은 무엇보다도 캐소본 씨가 사촌과 말을 섞지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었다는 것, 그리고 윌이 교회에 나타난 일이 두 사람 사이의 소원함을 한층 더 또렷하게 드러냈다는 것을 깨달은 데서 비롯되었다."], ["Will’s coming seemed to her quite excusable, nay, she thought it an amiable movement in him towards a reconciliation which she herself had been constantly wishing for.", "도러시아가 보기에 윌이 온 것은 충분히 변명될 만한 일이었고, 아니 오히려 그녀 자신이 줄곧 바라 왔던 화해를 향한 그의 다정한 몸짓이라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["He had probably imagined, as she had, that if Mr. Casaubon and he could meet easily, they would shake hands and friendly intercourse might return.", "그도 아마 그녀처럼, 캐소본 씨와 자기가 무람없이 마주칠 수만 있다면 악수를 나누고 다정한 교류가 되살아나리라 상상했을 것이다."], ["But now Dorothea felt quite robbed of that hope.", "하지만 이제 도러시아는 그 희망마저 송두리째 빼앗긴 기분이었다."], ["Will was banished further than ever, for Mr. Casaubon must have been newly embittered by this thrusting upon him of a presence which he refused to recognize.", "윌은 그 어느 때보다 멀리 추방당했으니, 캐소본 씨는 자신이 인정하기를 거부하는 존재가 이렇게 들이밀어진 일로 새로이 쓰라림을 느꼈을 것이 분명했다."]], [["He had not been very well that morning, suffering from some difficulty in breathing, and had not preached in consequence; she was not surprised, therefore, that he was nearly silent at luncheon, still less that he made no allusion to Will Ladislaw.", "그날 아침 그는 몸이 그리 좋지 못해 숨쉬기가 다소 힘들어 설교도 하지 못했다. 그러니 점심 식사 자리에서 그가 거의 말이 없었던 것도 그녀는 놀랍지 않았고, 윌 래디슬로에 관해 아무 언급도 하지 않은 것은 더더욱 그러했다."], ["For her own part she felt that she could never again introduce that subject.", "그녀 자신으로서도 다시는 그 화제를 꺼낼 수 없으리라 느꼈다."], ["They usually spent apart the hours between luncheon and dinner on a Sunday; Mr.", "일요일이면 두 사람은 보통 점심과 저녁 사이의 시간을 따로 보냈다."], ["Casaubon in the library dozing chiefly, and Dorothea in her boudoir, where she was wont to occupy herself with some of her favorite books.", "캐소본 씨는 서재에서 주로 졸았고, 도러시아는 자신의 거실에서 좋아하는 책 몇 권을 펼쳐 시간을 보내곤 했다."], ["There was a little heap of them on the table in the bow-window—of various sorts, from Herodotus, which she was learning to read with Mr.", "활 모양 창가의 탁자 위에는 책이 자그마하게 쌓여 있었으니, 캐소본 씨와 함께 읽기 시작한 헤로도토스부터, 그녀의 오랜 벗인 파스칼, 그리고 키블의 『기독교의 한 해』에 이르기까지 종류가 다양했다."], ["Casaubon, to her old companion Pascal, and Keble’s “Christian Year.”", "하지만 오늘은 한 권 한 권 펼쳐 보아도 어느 것도 읽을 수가 없었다."], ["But to-day she opened one after another, and could read none of them.", "모든 것이 음울하게만 느껴졌다."], ["Everything seemed dreary: the portents before the birth of Cyrus—Jewish antiquities—oh dear!—devout epigrams—the sacred chime of favorite hymns—all alike were as flat as tunes beaten on wood: even the spring flowers and the grass had a dull shiver in them under the afternoon clouds that hid the sun fitfully; even the sustaining thoughts which had become habits seemed to have in them the weariness of long future days in which she would still live with them for her sole companions.", "키루스의 탄생을 예고하는 전조들도, 유대의 고대 유물들도—아, 정말!—경건한 경구들도, 즐겨 부르던 찬송가의 거룩한 가락도, 모두 한결같이 나무를 두드리는 곡조처럼 밋밋하게만 들렸다. 봄꽃과 풀잎마저도 이따금 해를 가리는 오후의 구름 아래에서 칙칙하게 떨고 있었다."], ["It was another or rather a fuller sort of companionship that poor Dorothea was hungering for, and the hunger had grown from the perpetual effort demanded by her married life.", "습관이 되어 버린, 그녀를 지탱해 주던 생각들조차도, 앞으로 오랜 세월 그것들만을 유일한 벗으로 삼아 살아가야 한다는 권태를 머금고 있는 것 같았다."], ["She was always trying to be what her husband wished, and never able to repose on his delight in what she was.", "가엾은 도러시아가 굶주리듯 갈망하던 것은 또 다른, 아니 좀 더 충만한 종류의 교제였고, 그 굶주림은 결혼 생활이 끊임없이 요구하는 노력에서 자라난 것이었다."], ["The thing that she liked, that she spontaneously cared to have, seemed to be always excluded from her life; for if it was only granted and not shared by her husband it might as well have been denied.", "그녀는 늘 남편이 바라는 사람이 되려고 애썼으나, 있는 그대로의 자신을 그가 기뻐하는 것에 기댈 수는 한 번도 없었다. 자기가 좋아하는 것, 자발적으로 마음이 가는 것은 언제나 그녀의 삶에서 배제되는 것 같았다."], ["About Will Ladislaw there had been a difference between them from the first, and it had ended, since Mr.", "남편이 그저 허락만 하고 함께 나누지 않는다면, 그것은 거절당한 것이나 다름없었기 때문이다."], ["Casaubon had so severely repulsed Dorothea’s strong feeling about his claims on the family property, by her being convinced that she was in the right and her husband in the wrong, but that she was helpless.", "윌 래디슬로에 관해서는 처음부터 두 사람 사이에 견해 차이가 있었고, 캐소본 씨가 가문의 재산에 대한 윌의 권리를 둘러싼 도러시아의 강한 감정을 그토록 모질게 물리쳤기에, 그것은 결국 자기가 옳고 남편이 그르되 자신은 어쩔 도리가 없다는 확신으로 끝을 맺었다."], ["This afternoon the helplessness was more wretchedly benumbing than ever: she longed for objects who could be dear to her, and to whom she could be dear.", "그날 오후, 그 무력감은 그 어느 때보다도 비참하게 그녀를 마비시켰다. 그녀는 자신에게 소중할 수 있는, 그리고 자신이 소중할 수 있는 대상들을 갈망했다."], ["She longed for work which would be directly beneficent like the sunshine and the rain, and now it appeared that she was to live more and more in a virtual tomb, where there was the apparatus of a ghastly labor producing what would never see the light.", "햇빛과 비처럼 곧바로 은혜로운 일을 갈망했으나, 이제 보니 그녀는 점점 더 살아 있는 무덤 속에서 살아가야 할 형편이었다. 그 무덤에는 결코 빛을 보지 못할 것을 만들어 내는 음산한 노역의 장치가 갖추어져 있었다."], ["Today she had stood at the door of the tomb and seen Will Ladislaw receding into the distant world of warm activity and fellowship—turning his face towards her as he went.", "오늘 그녀는 그 무덤의 문턱에 서서, 윌 래디슬로가 따뜻한 활동과 사귐의 머나먼 세계로 물러가는 모습을, 떠나가면서도 그녀에게로 얼굴을 돌리는 모습을 지켜본 것이다."]], [["Books were of no use.", "책은 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["Thinking was of no use.", "생각도 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["It was Sunday, and she could not have the carriage to go to Celia, who had lately had a baby.", "일요일이었으니, 얼마 전 아기를 낳은 실리아에게 갈 마차를 부릴 수도 없었다."], ["There was no refuge now from spiritual emptiness and discontent, and Dorothea had to bear her bad mood, as she would have borne a headache.", "이제 정신적 공허와 불만으로부터 피할 곳은 어디에도 없었고, 도러시아는 두통을 견디듯 자신의 침울한 기분을 견뎌야 했다."]], [["After dinner, at the hour when she usually began to read aloud, Mr. Casaubon proposed that they should go into the library, where, he said, he had ordered a fire and lights.", "저녁 식사 후, 평소 그녀가 소리 내어 책을 읽기 시작하던 시각에, 캐소본 씨는 서재로 가자고 제안하며, 거기에 불과 등불을 준비해 두라고 일러 두었다고 말했다."], ["He seemed to have revived, and to be thinking intently.", "그는 기운을 되찾은 듯, 무언가를 골똘히 생각하는 것 같았다."]], [["In the library Dorothea observed that he had newly arranged a row of his note-books on a table, and now he took up and put into her hand a well-known volume, which was a table of contents to all the others.", "서재에서 도러시아는 그가 탁자 위에 자신의 공책들을 한 줄로 새로 정리해 둔 것을 보았는데, 그는 이제 익히 알려진 한 권을 집어 들어 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주었으니, 그것은 다른 모든 공책의 목차에 해당하는 것이었다."]], [["“You will oblige me, my dear,”", "\"여보, 한 가지 청을 들어주면 고맙겠소.\""], ["he said, seating himself, “if instead of other reading this evening, you will go through this aloud, pencil in hand, and at each point where I say ‘mark,’ will make a cross with your pencil.", "그가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"오늘 저녁에는 다른 책을 읽는 대신, 연필을 손에 들고 이것을 소리 내어 끝까지 읽으면서, 내가 '표시'라고 말하는 대목마다 연필로 십자 표시를 해 주오."], ["This is the first step in a sifting process which I have long had in view, and as we go on I shall be able to indicate to you certain principles of selection whereby you will, I trust, have an intelligent participation in my purpose.”", "이것은 내가 오래전부터 마음에 두어 온 선별 작업의 첫 단계요. 우리가 진행해 나가다 보면 내가 그대에게 선택의 어떤 원칙들을 일러 줄 수 있을 테고, 그러면 그대도 내 목적에 지적으로 참여하게 되리라 믿소.\""]], [["This proposal was only one more sign added to many since his memorable interview with Lydgate, that Mr. Casaubon’s original reluctance to let Dorothea work with him had given place to the contrary disposition, namely, to demand much interest and labor from her.", "이 제안은 리드게이트와의 잊지 못할 면담 이후로 여러 차례 나타난 징표에 하나가 더 보태진 것에 불과했으니, 곧 도러시아가 자신과 함께 일하는 것을 마뜩잖아 하던 캐소본 씨의 본래 태도가 그 반대로, 다시 말해 그녀에게 많은 관심과 노고를 요구하는 쪽으로 바뀌었다는 표지였다."]], [["After she had read and marked for two hours, he said, “We will take the volume up-stairs—and the pencil, if you please—and in case of reading in the night, we can pursue this task.", "두 시간 동안 그녀가 읽으며 표시를 한 뒤, 그가 말했다. \"이 책을 위층으로 가져갑시다—연필도, 괜찮다면—그래야 밤중에 책을 읽게 되더라도 이 일을 이어 갈 수 있으니."], ["It is not wearisome to you, I trust, Dorothea?”", "그대에게 고되지는 않겠지, 도러시아?\""]], [["“I prefer always reading what you like best to hear,”", "\"저는 언제나 당신이 가장 듣고 싶어 하시는 것을 읽는 편이 좋아요.\""], ["said Dorothea, who told the simple truth; for what she dreaded was to exert herself in reading or anything else which left him as joyless as ever.", "도러시아가 말했으니, 그것은 꾸밈없는 진실이었다. 그녀가 두려워하는 것은 책을 읽거나 그 무엇을 하느라 애쓰고도 그를 여전히 기쁨 없는 상태로 남겨 두는 일이었기 때문이다."]], [["It was a proof of the force with which certain characteristics in Dorothea impressed those around her, that her husband, with all his jealousy and suspicion, had gathered implicit trust in the integrity of her promises, and her power of devoting herself to her idea of the right and best.", "도러시아의 어떤 특성들이 주위 사람들에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다는 것은, 온갖 질투와 의심을 품은 그녀의 남편마저도 그녀가 한 약속의 신실함과, 자기가 생각하는 옳고 가장 좋은 일에 헌신하는 그녀의 능력에 대해 절대적인 신뢰를 품게 되었다는 사실로 입증되었다."], ["Of late he had begun to feel that these qualities were a peculiar possession for himself, and he wanted to engross them.", "근래에 그는 이런 자질들이 자기에게 속한 특별한 소유물이라고 느끼기 시작했고, 그것들을 독차지하고 싶어 했다."]], [["The reading in the night did come.", "밤중의 독서는 정말로 찾아왔다."], ["Dorothea in her young weariness had slept soon and fast: she was awakened by a sense of light, which seemed to her at first like a sudden vision of sunset after she had climbed a steep hill: she opened her eyes and saw her husband wrapped in his warm gown seating himself in the arm-chair near the fire-place where the embers were still glowing.", "젊은 몸의 피로에 도러시아는 일찍 깊이 잠들었다가, 어떤 빛의 감각에 깨어났으니, 처음에는 그것이 가파른 언덕을 오른 뒤에 문득 마주한 노을의 환영처럼 느껴졌다. 그녀가 눈을 뜨니, 따뜻한 가운을 두른 남편이 아직 잉걸불이 빨갛게 빛나는 벽난로 가까이의 안락의자에 앉으려는 것이 보였다."], ["He had lit two candles, expecting that Dorothea would awake, but not liking to rouse her by more direct means.", "그는 도러시아가 깰 것을 예상하면서도, 더 직접적인 방법으로 그녀를 깨우기는 싫어서 촛불 두 개를 켜 둔 것이었다."]], [["“Are you ill, Edward?”", "\"편찮으세요, 에드워드?\""], ["she said, rising immediately.", "그녀가 곧바로 일어나며 말했다."]], [["“I felt some uneasiness in a reclining posture.", "\"누운 자세로 있으니 좀 불편하더군."], ["I will sit here for a time.”", "한동안 여기 앉아 있겠소.\""], ["She threw wood on the fire, wrapped herself up, and said, “You would like me to read to you?”", "그녀는 불에 장작을 던져 넣고, 몸을 감싼 뒤 말했다. \"제가 책을 읽어 드릴까요?\""]], [["“You would oblige me greatly by doing so, Dorothea,”", "\"그래 주면 정말 고맙겠소, 도러시아.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, with a shade more meekness than usual in his polite manner.", "캐소본 씨가 평소의 정중한 태도에 평소보다 한층 부드러운 기색을 더해 말했다."], ["“I am wakeful: my mind is remarkably lucid.”", "\"잠이 오지 않는구려. 머리가 유난히 맑소.\""]], [["“I fear that the excitement may be too great for you,”", "\"그 흥분이 당신께 너무 큰 부담이 될까 두려워요.\""], ["said Dorothea, remembering Lydgate’s cautions.", "도러시아가 리드게이트의 당부를 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“No, I am not conscious of undue excitement.", "\"아니, 나는 지나친 흥분을 느끼지 않소."], ["Thought is easy.”", "생각이 수월하게 흘러가오.\""], ["Dorothea dared not insist, and she read for an hour or more on the same plan as she had done in the evening, but getting over the pages with more quickness.", "도러시아는 감히 고집을 부리지 못하고, 저녁에 했던 것과 똑같은 방식으로 한 시간 남짓 책을 읽었으나, 페이지를 더 빠르게 넘겨 나갔다."], ["Mr. Casaubon’s mind was more alert, and he seemed to anticipate what was coming after a very slight verbal indication, saying, “That will do—mark that”—or “Pass on to the next head—I omit the second excursus on Crete.”", "캐소본 씨의 정신은 한결 또렷해서, 아주 짧은 말의 신호만으로도 다음에 올 내용을 예측하는 듯이 \"됐소—거기 표시하오\"라거나 \"다음 항목으로 넘어가오—크레타에 관한 두 번째 부론은 생략하겠소\"라고 말했다."], ["Dorothea was amazed to think of the bird-like speed with which his mind was surveying the ground where it had been creeping for years.", "도러시아는 여러 해 동안 기어 다니듯 더듬어 온 영역을 그의 정신이 새처럼 빠른 속도로 훑어 나가는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["At last he said—", "마침내 그가 말했다—"]], [["“Close the book now, my dear.", "\"이제 책을 덮어요, 여보."], ["We will resume our work to-morrow.", "우리 작업은 내일 다시 이어 갑시다."], ["I have deferred it too long, and would gladly see it completed.", "너무 오래 미루어 왔으니, 마무리되는 것을 기쁘게 보고 싶구려."], ["But you observe that the principle on which my selection is made, is to give adequate, and not disproportionate illustration to each of the theses enumerated in my introduction, as at present sketched.", "하지만 알다시피, 내 선별의 원칙은 지금 잡아 둔 대로, 서론에 열거한 각 명제에 대해 적절하되 한쪽으로 치우치지 않는 예증을 제시하는 데 있소."], ["You have perceived that distinctly, Dorothea?”", "그것을 분명히 헤아렸지, 도러시아?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Dorothea, rather tremulously.", "도러시아가 다소 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["She felt sick at heart.", "그녀는 가슴이 메스꺼울 만큼 괴로웠다."]], [["“And now I think that I can take some repose,”", "\"그러면 이제 좀 쉴 수 있을 것 같소.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon.", "캐소본 씨가 말했다."], ["He laid down again and begged her to put out the lights.", "그는 다시 자리에 누우며 등불을 꺼 달라고 청했다."], ["When she had lain down too, and there was a darkness only broken by a dull glow on the hearth, he said—", "그녀도 자리에 눕고, 난로 위의 희미한 불빛만이 깨뜨리는 어둠이 깔리자, 그가 말했다—"]], [["“Before I sleep, I have a request to make, Dorothea.”", "\"잠들기 전에, 그대에게 한 가지 청할 것이 있소, 도러시아.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무엇인가요?\""], ["said Dorothea, with dread in her mind.", "도러시아가 마음속에 두려움을 품은 채 말했다."]], [["“It is that you will let me know, deliberately, whether, in case of my death, you will carry out my wishes: whether you will avoid doing what I should deprecate, and apply yourself to do what I should desire.”", "\"내가 만일 죽게 되면 그대가 내 뜻을 받들어 줄지를, 곧 내가 못마땅해할 일은 하지 않고 내가 바라는 일에 전념해 줄지를, 신중히 생각하여 내게 알려 달라는 것이오.\""]], [["Dorothea was not taken by surprise: many incidents had been leading her to the conjecture of some intention on her husband’s part which might make a new yoke for her.", "도러시아는 놀라지 않았다. 여러 가지 일이 있어 그녀에게 새로운 멍에가 될지도 모를 남편의 어떤 의도를 짐작하게 만들었던 것이다."], ["She did not answer immediately.", "그녀는 곧바로 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“You refuse?” said Mr.", "\"거절하는 거요?\""], ["Casaubon, with more edge in his tone.", "캐소본 씨가 한층 날 선 어조로 말했다."]], [["“No, I do not yet refuse,”", "\"아니요, 아직 거절하는 건 아니에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, in a clear voice, the need of freedom asserting itself within her; “but it is too solemn—I think it is not right—to make a promise when I am ignorant what it will bind me to.", "도러시아가 또렷한 목소리로 말했으니, 자유에 대한 갈망이 그녀의 내부에서 고개를 든 것이었다. \"하지만 그것이 무엇에 저를 매어 놓을지도 모르는 채로 약속을 한다는 것은 너무나 엄숙한 일이에요—옳지 않다고 생각해요."], ["Whatever affection prompted I would do without promising.”", "어떤 애정이 시키는 일이라면 저는 약속하지 않고도 할 거예요.\""]], [["“But you would use your own judgment: I ask you to obey mine; you refuse.”", "\"하지만 그대는 그대 나름의 판단을 따르겠다는 것이지. 나는 그대가 내 판단에 따르기를 청하는 거요. 그대는 거절하는 거요.\""]], [["“No, dear, no!”", "\"아니에요, 여보, 아니에요!\""], ["said Dorothea, beseechingly, crushed by opposing fears.", "도러시아가 서로 맞서는 두려움에 짓눌려 애원하듯 말했다."], ["“But may I wait and reflect a little while?", "\"하지만 잠시 기다리며 조금만 더 생각해 봐도 될까요?"], ["I desire with my whole soul to do what will comfort you; but I cannot give any pledge suddenly—still less a pledge to do I know not what.”", "저는 온 영혼을 다해 당신을 위로할 일을 하고 싶어요. 하지만 이렇게 갑작스레 어떤 서약도 드릴 수가 없어요—무엇을 하게 될지조차 모르는 일에 대한 서약은 더더욱요.\""]], [["“You cannot then confide in the nature of my wishes?”", "\"그러면 그대는 내 뜻의 본질을 믿을 수 없다는 거요?\""]], [["“Grant me till to-morrow,”", "\"내일까지만 시간을 주세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, beseechingly.", "도러시아가 애원하듯 말했다."]], [["“Till to-morrow then,”", "\"그럼 내일까지.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon.", "캐소본 씨가 말했다."]], [["Soon she could hear that he was sleeping, but there was no more sleep for her.", "이윽고 그녀는 그가 잠든 소리를 들을 수 있었으나, 그녀에게는 더 이상 잠이 오지 않았다."], ["While she constrained herself to lie still lest she should disturb him, her mind was carrying on a conflict in which imagination ranged its forces first on one side and then on the other.", "그를 방해할까 봐 가만히 누워 있으려 애쓰는 동안, 그녀의 마음속에서는 상상력이 그 군대를 처음에는 한쪽에, 다음에는 다른 쪽에 배치하는 갈등이 벌어지고 있었다."], ["She had no presentiment that the power which her husband wished to establish over her future action had relation to anything else than his work.", "남편이 자신의 앞으로의 행동에 대해 확립하려는 그 권한이 그의 작업 말고 다른 무언가와 관련이 있으리라는 예감은 그녀에게 전혀 없었다."], ["But it was clear enough to her that he would expect her to devote herself to sifting those mixed heaps of material, which were to be the doubtful illustration of principles still more doubtful.", "하지만 그가, 더더욱 미덥지 못한 원칙들의 미심쩍은 예증이 될 그 뒤섞인 자료 더미를 추려 내는 일에 자신이 헌신하기를 기대하리라는 것만은 충분히 분명했다."], ["The poor child had become altogether unbelieving as to the trustworthiness of that Key which had made the ambition and the labor of her husband’s life.", "가엾은 아이는 남편의 일생의 야망이자 노고였던 그 '열쇠'의 신뢰성에 대해 이미 완전히 믿음을 잃은 터였다."], ["It was not wonderful that, in spite of her small instruction, her judgment in this matter was truer than his: for she looked with unbiassed comparison and healthy sense at probabilities on which he had risked all his egoism.", "변변치 못한 배움에도 불구하고 이 문제에 관한 그녀의 판단이 그의 것보다 더 진실했던 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그녀는 그가 자신의 모든 자아를 걸었던 개연성들을, 편견 없는 비교와 건강한 분별로 바라보았기 때문이다."], ["And now she pictured to herself the days, and months, and years which she must spend in sorting what might be called shattered mummies, and fragments of a tradition which was itself a mosaic wrought from crushed ruins—sorting them as food for a theory which was already withered in the birth like an elfin child.", "그리고 이제 그녀는 산산이 부서진 미라라 부를 만한 것들, 그리고 그 자체가 부서진 폐허에서 짜낸 모자이크인 전통의 파편들을 분류하며 보내야 할 나날과 달과 해를 머릿속에 그려 보았으니—그것들을, 마치 요정의 아이처럼 태어나면서부터 이미 시들어 버린 어떤 이론의 양식으로 분류하는 일이었다. 의심할 바 없이, 활기차게 추구된 활기찬 오류가 진리의 배아를 살아 숨 쉬게 한 적은 있었다."], ["Doubtless a vigorous error vigorously pursued has kept the embryos of truth a-breathing: the quest of gold being at the same time a questioning of substances, the body of chemistry is prepared for its soul, and Lavoisier is born. But Mr.", "황금을 찾는 일이 동시에 물질을 탐구하는 일이 되어, 화학이라는 육체가 그 영혼을 맞이할 채비를 갖추고 라부아지에가 태어났듯이."], ["Casaubon’s theory of the elements which made the seed of all tradition was not likely to bruise itself unawares against discoveries: it floated among flexible conjectures no more solid than those etymologies which seemed strong because of likeness in sound until it was shown that likeness in sound made them impossible: it was a method of interpretation which was not tested by the necessity of forming anything which had sharper collisions than an elaborate notion of Gog and Magog: it was as free from interruption as a plan for threading the stars together.", "하지만 모든 전통의 씨앗을 이룬 요소들에 관한 캐소본 씨의 이론은 미처 알지 못한 채로 발견들과 부딪쳐 멍들 일이 없을 것 같았다. 그것은 소리의 유사함 때문에 강해 보이다가 끝내 소리의 유사함이 도리어 그것들을 불가능하게 만든다는 사실이 밝혀지는 어원풀이들만큼이나, 견고함이라곤 없는 유연한 추측들 사이를 떠다닐 뿐이었다."], ["And Dorothea had so often had to check her weariness and impatience over this questionable riddle-guessing, as it revealed itself to her instead of the fellowship in high knowledge which was to make life worthier!", "그것은 곡과 마곡에 관한 정교한 관념보다 더 날카로운 충돌을 일으키는 무언가를 형성해야 할 필요에 의해 검증되지 않는 해석의 방법이었다. 그것은 별들을 한데 꿰어 보겠다는 계획만큼이나 아무런 방해도 받지 않았다."], ["She could understand well enough now why her husband had come to cling to her, as possibly the only hope left that his labors would ever take a shape in which they could be given to the world.", "그리고 도러시아는 삶을 더 가치 있게 만들어 줄 고매한 지식 속의 동반 관계 대신에 그녀에게 모습을 드러낸 이 미심쩍은 수수께끼 풀이에 대해, 얼마나 자주 자신의 권태와 조바심을 억눌러야 했던가! 이제 그녀는 남편이 왜 자신에게 매달리게 되었는지를 충분히 이해할 수 있었으니, 그것은 어쩌면 자신의 노고가 언젠가 세상에 내놓을 수 있는 형태를 갖추리라는 마지막 남은 희망이었기 때문이다."], ["At first it had seemed that he wished to keep even her aloof from any close knowledge of what he was doing; but gradually the terrible stringency of human need—the prospect of a too speedy death—", "처음에 그는 자신이 하는 일에 대한 어떤 깊은 앎으로부터 그녀마저 멀리 떼어 놓고 싶어 하는 듯 보였다. 하지만 차츰 인간적 필요의 무서운 절박함이—너무 이른 죽음의 전망이—"]], [["And here Dorothea’s pity turned from her own future to her husband’s past—nay, to his present hard struggle with a lot which had grown out of that past: the lonely labor, the ambition breathing hardly under the pressure of self-distrust; the goal receding, and the heavier limbs; and now at last the sword visibly trembling above him!", "그리고 여기에서 도러시아의 연민은 자신의 미래로부터 남편의 과거로—아니, 그 과거에서 자라난 운명과 벌이는 그의 현재의 고된 분투로 옮겨 갔다. 외로운 노고, 자기 불신의 압박 아래 가까스로 숨 쉬는 야망, 멀어져 가는 목표와 점점 무거워지는 사지, 그리고 마침내 그의 머리 위에서 눈에 보이게 떨고 있는 칼!"], ["And had she not wished to marry him that she might help him in his life’s labor?—But she had thought the work was to be something greater, which she could serve in devoutly for its own sake.", "그녀가 그와 결혼하려 했던 것은 그의 일생의 노고를 돕기 위해서가 아니었던가?—하지만 그녀는 그 일이 무언가 더 위대한 것, 그 자체를 위해 경건하게 봉사할 수 있는 것이리라 여겼었다."], ["Was it right, even to soothe his grief—would it be possible, even if she promised—to work as in a treadmill fruitlessly?", "그의 슬픔을 달래기 위해서라 한들, 결실 없이 쳇바퀴 돌듯 일하는 것이—설령 약속을 한다 해도—과연 옳을까, 아니 가능하기나 할까?"]], [["And yet, could she deny him?", "그렇다 한들, 그녀가 그를 거절할 수 있을까?"], ["Could she say, “I refuse to content this pining hunger?”", "\"이 애타는 굶주림을 채워 드리기를 거절하겠어요\"라고 말할 수 있을까?"], ["It would be refusing to do for him dead, what she was almost sure to do for him living.", "그것은 그가 살아 있을 때 거의 틀림없이 해 줄 일을, 그가 죽었을 때 해 주기를 거절하는 셈이었다."], ["If he lived as Lydgate had said he might, for fifteen years or more, her life would certainly be spent in helping him and obeying him.", "리드게이트가 말한 대로 그가 십오 년 혹은 그 이상을 산다면, 그녀의 삶은 분명 그를 돕고 그에게 순종하는 데 바쳐질 것이었다."]], [["Still, there was a deep difference between that devotion to the living and that indefinite promise of devotion to the dead.", "그럼에도 살아 있는 이에게 바치는 헌신과 죽은 이에게 바치겠다는 막연한 약속 사이에는 깊은 차이가 있었다."], ["While he lived, he could claim nothing that she would not still be free to remonstrate against, and even to refuse.", "그가 살아 있는 동안에는, 그녀가 여전히 자유로이 항변하고 심지어 거절할 수 있는 것이 아닌 어떤 것도 그는 요구할 수 없었다."], ["But—the thought passed through her mind more than once, though she could not believe in it—might he not mean to demand something more from her than she had been able to imagine, since he wanted her pledge to carry out his wishes without telling her exactly what they were?", "하지만—그 생각이 그녀의 마음을 한 번 이상 스쳐 갔으나 그녀는 그것을 믿을 수가 없었다—그가 자기 뜻이 정확히 무엇인지 말해 주지도 않으면서 그것을 받들겠다는 서약을 원하는 것을 보면, 혹시 그녀가 상상할 수 있었던 것보다 더한 무언가를 그녀에게 요구하려는 것은 아닐까? 아니다."], ["No; his heart was bound up in his work only: that was the end for which his failing life was to be eked out by hers.", "그의 마음은 오직 자신의 작업에만 묶여 있었다. 그것이야말로 그의 꺼져 가는 생명을 그녀의 생명으로 이어 붙여 이루려는 목적이었다."]], [["And now, if she were to say, “No!", "그리고 이제 만일 그녀가 \"안 돼요!"], ["if you die, I will put no finger to your work”—it seemed as if she would be crushing that bruised heart.", "당신이 죽으면 저는 당신의 작업에 손가락 하나 대지 않겠어요\"라고 말한다면—그것은 마치 그 멍든 가슴을 짓밟아 부수는 것만 같았다."]], [["For four hours Dorothea lay in this conflict, till she felt ill and bewildered, unable to resolve, praying mutely.", "네 시간 동안 도러시아는 이 갈등 속에 누워 있다가, 끝내 속이 메스껍고 정신이 어지러워, 아무것도 결정하지 못한 채 말없이 기도했다."], ["Helpless as a child which has sobbed and sought too long, she fell into a late morning sleep, and when she waked Mr. Casaubon was already up.", "너무 오래 흐느끼며 무언가를 구하다 지친 아이처럼 어찌할 바를 몰라, 그녀는 아침 늦게야 잠이 들었고, 깨어났을 때 캐소본 씨는 이미 일어나 있었다."], ["Tantripp told her that he had read prayers, breakfasted, and was in the library.", "탠트립이 그녀에게 그가 기도를 올리고 아침을 들고는 서재에 있다고 일러 주었다."]], [["“I never saw you look so pale, madam,”", "\"마님께서 그렇게 창백하신 건 처음 봬요.\""], ["said Tantripp, a solid-figured woman who had been with the sisters at Lausanne.", "로잔에서부터 자매를 모셔 온, 다부진 몸집의 탠트립이 말했다."]], [["“Was I ever high-colored, Tantripp?”", "\"내가 혈색이 좋았던 적이 있던가, 탠트립?\""], ["said Dorothea, smiling faintly.", "도러시아가 희미하게 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Well, not to say high-colored, but with a bloom like a Chiny rose.", "\"글쎄요, 혈색이 좋다고까지야 할 수 없겠지만, 차이나 장미 같은 발그레함은 도시던데요."], ["But always smelling those leather books, what can be expected?", "그렇지만 늘 그 가죽 책들 냄새만 맡고 계시니, 무얼 더 바라겠어요?"], ["Do rest a little this morning, madam.", "오늘 아침은 조금 쉬세요, 마님."], ["Let me say you are ill and not able to go into that close library.”", "제가 마님께서 편찮으셔서 그 답답한 서재엔 못 들어가신다고 여쭐게요.\""]], [["“Oh no, no!", "\"아니, 아니야!"], ["let me make haste,”", "어서 가야지.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“Mr. Casaubon wants me particularly.”", "\"캐소본 씨가 나를 특별히 찾으시잖니.\""]], [["When she went down she felt sure that she should promise to fulfil his wishes; but that would be later in the day—not yet.", "아래층으로 내려가면서 그녀는 그의 뜻을 받들겠다고 약속하게 되리라 확신했다. 하지만 그것은 그날 늦게의 일이 될 테고—아직은 아니었다."]], [["As Dorothea entered the library, Mr. Casaubon turned round from the table where he had been placing some books, and said—", "도러시아가 서재로 들어서자, 책 몇 권을 정리하고 있던 탁자에서 캐소본 씨가 몸을 돌리며 말했다—"]], [["“I was waiting for your appearance, my dear.", "\"그대가 오기를 기다리고 있었소, 여보."], ["I had hoped to set to work at once this morning, but I find myself under some indisposition, probably from too much excitement yesterday.", "오늘 아침은 곧바로 일을 시작하려 했는데, 아마도 어제 너무 흥분한 탓인지 몸이 좀 좋지 않구려."], ["I am going now to take a turn in the shrubbery, since the air is milder.”", "공기가 좀 누그러졌으니, 이제 관목 정원이나 한 바퀴 거닐고 오겠소.\""]], [["“I am glad to hear that,”", "\"잘됐네요.\" 도러시아가 말했다."], ["said Dorothea. “Your mind, I feared, was too active last night.”", "\"어젯밤 당신의 정신이 너무 활발한 것 같아 걱정스러웠어요.\""]], [["“I would fain have it set at rest on the point I last spoke of, Dorothea.", "\"내가 지난번에 말한 그 일을 마음 놓고 정리하고 싶구려, 도러시아."], ["You can now, I hope, give me an answer.”", "이제는 그대가 내게 답을 줄 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“May I come out to you in the garden presently?”", "\"제가 잠시 후에 정원으로 나가도 될까요?\""], ["said Dorothea, winning a little breathing space in that way.", "도러시아가 그런 식으로 잠시 숨 돌릴 틈을 얻으며 말했다."]], [["“I shall be in the Yew-tree Walk for the next half-hour,”", "\"앞으로 반 시간 동안은 주목 산책로에 있겠소.\""], ["said Mr. Casaubon, and then he left her.", "캐소본 씨가 말하고는 그녀를 떠났다."]], [["Dorothea, feeling very weary, rang and asked Tantripp to bring her some wraps.", "몹시 지친 도러시아는 종을 울려 탠트립에게 걸칠 것을 가져다 달라고 청했다."], ["She had been sitting still for a few minutes, but not in any renewal of the former conflict: she simply felt that she was going to say “Yes”", "그녀는 몇 분간 가만히 앉아 있었으나, 앞서의 갈등이 다시 일어난 것은 아니었다. 다만 자신이 스스로의 운명에 \"네\"라고 말하게 되리라는 것을 느낄 뿐이었다."], ["to her own doom: she was too weak, too full of dread at the thought of inflicting a keen-edged blow on her husband, to do anything but submit completely.", "그녀는 너무도 약했고, 남편에게 날카로운 일격을 가한다는 생각에 너무도 두려움이 가득 차서, 온전히 굴복하는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수가 없었다."], ["She sat still and let Tantripp put on her bonnet and shawl, a passivity which was unusual with her, for she liked to wait on herself.", "그녀는 가만히 앉아 탠트립이 모자를 씌우고 숄을 둘러 주도록 내버려 두었으니, 이는 자기 일은 스스로 하기를 좋아하는 그녀에게는 흔치 않은 수동적인 태도였다."]], [["“God bless you, madam!”", "\"하느님께서 마님을 축복하시길!\""], ["said Tantripp, with an irrepressible movement of love towards the beautiful, gentle creature for whom she felt unable to do anything more, now that she had finished tying the bonnet.", "탠트립이 말했으니, 이제 모자 끈을 다 매어 주고 나니 더는 해 드릴 것이 없다고 느낀 그 아름답고 온순한 사람을 향한, 억누를 수 없는 사랑의 움직임이었다."]], [["This was too much for Dorothea’s highly-strung feeling, and she burst into tears, sobbing against Tantripp’s arm.", "이 한마디가 도러시아의 팽팽히 곤두선 감정에는 너무 벅찬 것이어서, 그녀는 와락 눈물을 터뜨리며 탠트립의 팔에 기대어 흐느꼈다."], ["But soon she checked herself, dried her eyes, and went out at the glass door into the shrubbery.", "하지만 곧 자신을 다잡아 눈물을 닦고는 유리문을 지나 관목 정원으로 나갔다."]], [["“I wish every book in that library was built into a caticom for your master,”", "\"저 서재의 책이란 책은 모조리 주인 어른의 지하 묘소로 쌓아 올려졌으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["said Tantripp to Pratt, the butler, finding him in the breakfast-room.", "탠트립이 아침 식사실에서 마주친 집사 프랫에게 말했다."], ["She had been at Rome, and visited the antiquities, as we know; and she always declined to call Mr.", "우리가 알다시피 그녀는 로마에 가 본 적이 있어 고대 유적들을 둘러보았던 것이다."], ["Casaubon anything but “your master,”", "그리고 그녀는 다른 하인들에게 말할 때면 언제나 캐소본 씨를 \"주인 어른\""], ["when speaking to the other servants.", "말고 달리 부르기를 한사코 마다했다."]], [["Pratt laughed.", "프랫은 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["He liked his master very well, but he liked Tantripp better.", "그는 주인을 무척 좋아했으나, 탠트립을 더 좋아했다."]], [["When Dorothea was out on the gravel walks, she lingered among the nearer clumps of trees, hesitating, as she had done once before, though from a different cause.", "자갈길로 나선 도러시아는, 전에 한 번 그랬던 것처럼, 다만 이번에는 다른 까닭으로 망설이며 가까이 있는 나무 무리 사이에서 발걸음을 늦추었다."], ["Then she had feared lest her effort at fellowship should be unwelcome; now she dreaded going to the spot where she foresaw that she must bind herself to a fellowship from which she shrank.", "그때 그녀는 동반의 노력이 환영받지 못할까 두려워했었다. 이제 그녀는 자신이 움츠러드는 어떤 동반 관계에 스스로를 매어야 하리라는 것을 예감하는 그 장소로 가는 일이 두려웠다."], ["Neither law nor the world’s opinion compelled her to this—only her husband’s nature and her own compassion, only the ideal and not the real yoke of marriage.", "법도, 세상의 평판도 그녀에게 이를 강요하지 않았으니—오직 남편의 천성과 그녀 자신의 연민만이, 오직 결혼의 실제 멍에가 아닌 이상적인 멍에만이 그러했다."], ["She saw clearly enough the whole situation, yet she was fettered: she could not smite the stricken soul that entreated hers.", "그녀는 상황 전체를 충분히 또렷이 보면서도 결박되어 있었다. 자기를 애원하는 그 상처 입은 영혼을 그녀는 내려칠 수가 없었다."], ["If that were weakness, Dorothea was weak.", "그것이 나약함이라면, 도러시아는 나약했다."], ["But the half-hour was passing, and she must not delay longer.", "하지만 반 시간이 흘러가고 있었으니, 더는 지체해서는 안 되었다."], ["When she entered the Yew-tree Walk she could not see her husband; but the walk had bends, and she went, expecting to catch sight of his figure wrapped in a blue cloak, which, with a warm velvet cap, was his outer garment on chill days for the garden.", "주목 산책로에 들어섰을 때 그녀는 남편을 볼 수 없었으나, 그 길에는 굽이가 있었으므로, 그녀는 푸른 망토를 두른 그의 모습을 곧 발견하리라 기대하며 걸음을 옮겼다. 그 망토는 따뜻한 벨벳 모자와 함께 쌀쌀한 날 정원에 나설 때 그가 걸치는 겉옷이었다."], ["It occurred to her that he might be resting in the summer-house, towards which the path diverged a little.", "길이 조금 갈라져 나간 정자 쪽에서 그가 쉬고 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Turning the angle, she could see him seated on the bench, close to a stone table.", "모퉁이를 돌자, 돌 탁자 가까이 의자에 앉아 있는 그가 보였다."], ["His arms were resting on the table, and his brow was bowed down on them, the blue cloak being dragged forward and screening his face on each side.", "그의 두 팔은 탁자 위에 놓여 있었고, 이마는 그 위로 수그러져 있었으며, 푸른 망토는 앞으로 끌려 와 그의 얼굴을 양옆에서 가리고 있었다."]], [["“He exhausted himself last night,”", "\"어젯밤에 기진하셨구나.\""], ["Dorothea said to herself, thinking at first that he was asleep, and that the summer-house was too damp a place to rest in.", "도러시아는 처음에 그가 잠든 것이고, 정자란 곳이 쉬기에는 너무 눅눅하다고 여기며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["But then she remembered that of late she had seen him take that attitude when she was reading to him, as if he found it easier than any other; and that he would sometimes speak, as well as listen, with his face down in that way.", "하지만 곧 그녀는 근래에 자기가 책을 읽어 줄 때 그가 그 자세를 취하던 것을 떠올렸으니, 마치 다른 어떤 자세보다 그것이 더 편하다는 듯했고, 더러는 그렇게 얼굴을 숙인 채 듣기도 하고 말하기도 했던 것이다. 그녀는 정자 안으로 들어가 말했다."], ["She went into the summerhouse and said, “I am come, Edward; I am ready.”", "\"왔어요, 에드워드. 저는 준비됐어요.\""]], [["He took no notice, and she thought that he must be fast asleep.", "그는 아무런 기척이 없었고, 그녀는 그가 깊이 잠든 것이 분명하다고 생각했다."], ["She laid her hand on his shoulder, and repeated, “I am ready!”", "그녀는 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 다시 말했다. \"준비됐어요!\""], ["Still he was motionless; and with a sudden confused fear, she leaned down to him, took off his velvet cap, and leaned her cheek close to his head, crying in a distressed tone—", "그래도 그는 꼼짝하지 않았다. 문득 일렁이는 혼란스러운 두려움에 그녀는 그에게로 몸을 숙여 벨벳 모자를 벗기고는 그의 머리에 뺨을 가까이 댄 채, 괴로운 어조로 외쳤다—"]], [["“Wake, dear, wake!", "\"깨세요, 여보, 깨세요!"], ["Listen to me.", "제 말을 들어 주세요."], ["I am come to answer.”", "답을 드리러 왔어요.\""], ["But Dorothea never gave her answer.", "하지만 도러시아는 끝내 그 답을 주지 못했다."]], [["Later in the day, Lydgate was seated by her bedside, and she was talking deliriously, thinking aloud, and recalling what had gone through her mind the night before.", "그날 늦게, 리드게이트는 그녀의 침상 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그녀는 헛소리하듯 생각을 입 밖으로 내며, 전날 밤 자신의 마음을 스쳐 간 것들을 되새기고 있었다."], ["She knew him, and called him by his name, but appeared to think it right that she should explain everything to him; and again, and again, begged him to explain everything to her husband.", "그녀는 그를 알아보고 이름을 부르면서도, 자기가 그에게 모든 것을 설명해야 옳다고 여기는 듯했고, 거듭거듭 그에게 남편에게 모든 것을 설명해 달라고 애원했다."]], [["“Tell him I shall go to him soon: I am ready to promise.", "\"제가 곧 그분께 가겠다고 전해 주세요. 저는 약속할 준비가 됐어요."], ["Only, thinking about it was so dreadful—it has made me ill.", "다만, 그걸 생각하는 것이 너무도 끔찍해서—그 때문에 제가 병이 났어요."], ["Not very ill.", "아주 아픈 건 아니에요."], ["I shall soon be better.", "저는 곧 나을 거예요."], ["Go and tell him.”", "가서 그분께 전해 주세요.\""]], [["But the silence in her husband’s ear was never more to be broken.", "하지만 그녀 남편의 귓가에 깃든 침묵은 다시는 깨어지지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XLIX.", "# CHAPTER XLIX."]], [["“A task too strong for wizard spells This squire had brought about; ’T is easy dropping stones in wells, But who shall get them out?”", "이 시골 양반이 저질러 놓았구나. 우물에 돌을 던져 넣기는 쉬워도,"]], [["“I wish to God we could hinder Dorothea from knowing this,”", "대체 누가 그것을 끄집어낼 것인가?”"], ["said Sir James Chettam, with a little frown on his brow, and an expression of intense disgust about his mouth.", "“정말이지 도러시아가 이 일을 알지 못하게 막을 수만 있다면 좋겠소.” 제임스 체텀 경은 이마를 살짝 찌푸리고 입가에 극도의 혐오를 드러내며 말했다."]], [["He was standing on the hearth-rug in the library at Lowick Grange, and speaking to Mr. Brooke.", "그는 로윅 그레인지의 서재에서 벽난로 깔개 위에 서서 브룩 씨에게 말하고 있었다."], ["It was the day after Mr. Casaubon had been buried, and Dorothea was not yet able to leave her room.", "캐소본 씨를 매장한 다음 날이었고, 도러시아는 아직 방을 나설 수 없는 상태였다."]], [["“That would be difficult, you know, Chettam, as she is an executrix, and she likes to go into these things—property, land, that kind of thing.", "“그게 어렵다는 걸 알지 않소, 체텀. 그 애는 유언 집행인인 데다, 그런 일들—재산이며 토지며 그런 종류의 일에 직접 관여하고 싶어 하니 말이오."], ["She has her notions, you know,” said Mr.", "그 애에겐 나름의 생각이 있어요.”"], ["Brooke, sticking his eye-glasses on nervously, and exploring the edges of a folded paper which he held in his hand; “and she would like to act—depend upon it, as an executrix Dorothea would want to act.", "브룩 씨는 안경을 신경질적으로 코에 걸치며, 손에 든 접힌 서류의 가장자리를 더듬으며 말했다. “게다가 그 애는 직접 처리하고 싶어 할 거요—분명히 말하지만, 유언 집행인으로서 도러시아는 나서고 싶어 할 거란 말이오."], ["And she was twenty-one last December, you know.", "그리고 지난 십이월에 스물한 살이 되었지 않소."], ["I can hinder nothing.”", "나로서는 아무것도 막을 수 없어요.”"]], [["Sir James looked at the carpet for a minute in silence, and then lifting his eyes suddenly fixed them on Mr.", "제임스 경은 잠시 말없이 양탄자를 바라보다가, 별안간 눈을 들어 브룩 씨를 똑바로 응시하며 말했다."], ["Brooke, saying, “I will tell you what we can do.", "“우리가 할 수 있는 일을 말씀드리지요."], ["Until Dorothea is well, all business must be kept from her, and as soon as she is able to be moved she must come to us.", "도러시아가 회복될 때까지 모든 일을 그녀에게 알려서는 안 됩니다. 그리고 움직일 수 있게 되는 즉시 우리 집으로 와야 합니다."], ["Being with Celia and the baby will be the best thing in the world for her, and will pass away the time.", "실리아와 아기와 함께 지내는 것이 그녀에게 세상에서 가장 좋은 일일 테고, 그러면서 시간도 흘러갈 겁니다."], ["And meanwhile you must get rid of Ladislaw: you must send him out of the country.”", "그리고 그동안 어르신은 래디슬로를 처리하셔야 합니다. 그를 이 고장에서 내보내셔야 해요.”"], ["Here Sir James’s look of disgust returned in all its intensity.", "여기서 제임스 경의 혐오에 찬 표정이 다시금 온전한 강도로 되살아났다."]], [["Mr. Brooke put his hands behind him, walked to the window and straightened his back with a little shake before he replied.", "브룩 씨는 두 손을 등 뒤로 모으고 창가로 걸어가서, 대답하기 전에 가볍게 몸을 한 번 흔들며 허리를 곧게 폈다."]], [["“That is easily said, Chettam, easily said, you know.”", "“그거야 말이야 쉽지, 체텀, 말이야 쉬운 일이오.”"]], [["“My dear sir,”", "“이보세요, 어르신.”"], ["persisted Sir James, restraining his indignation within respectful forms, “it was you who brought him here, and you who keep him here—I mean by the occupation you give him.”", "제임스 경은 분노를 정중한 형식 안에 억누르며 끈질기게 말했다. “그를 이곳으로 데려온 것도 어르신이고, 그를 이곳에 붙잡아 두고 계신 것도 어르신입니다—제 말은, 그에게 일거리를 주심으로써 말입니다.”"]], [["“Yes, but I can’t dismiss him in an instant without assigning reasons, my dear Chettam.", "“그렇지, 하지만 이유도 대지 않고 단번에 그를 해고할 수는 없잖소, 이보게 체텀."], ["Ladislaw has been invaluable, most satisfactory.", "래디슬로는 더없이 소중한 존재였소, 아주 만족스러웠지."], ["I consider that I have done this part of the country a service by bringing him—by bringing him, you know.”", "나는 그를 데려온 것—그를 데려온 것으로 이 지방에 봉사했다고 생각하오, 알겠소.”"], ["Mr. Brooke ended with a nod, turning round to give it.", "브룩 씨는 고개를 끄덕이며 말을 맺었는데, 일부러 몸을 돌려 그 끄덕임을 보여 주었다."]], [["“It’s a pity this part of the country didn’t do without him, that’s all I have to say about it.", "“이 지방이 그 사람 없이 지냈더라면 좋았을 것을, 그것이 제가 드릴 말씀의 전부입니다."], ["At any rate, as Dorothea’s brother-in-law, I feel warranted in objecting strongly to his being kept here by any action on the part of her friends.", "어쨌든 저는 도러시아의 처남으로서, 그녀의 친지들의 어떤 조처로 그가 이곳에 붙잡혀 있는 데 강력히 반대할 자격이 있다고 느낍니다."], ["You admit, I hope, that I have a right to speak about what concerns the dignity of my wife’s sister?”", "제 아내의 언니의 품위에 관한 일을 두고 제가 말할 권리가 있다는 것은, 부디 인정하시겠지요?”"]], [["Sir James was getting warm.", "제임스 경은 점점 격해지고 있었다."]], [["“Of course, my dear Chettam, of course.", "“물론이오, 이보게 체텀, 물론이지."], ["But you and I have different ideas—different—”", "하지만 자네와 나는 생각이 다르오—다르단 말이지—”"]], [["“Not about this action of Casaubon’s, I should hope,”", "“캐소본의 이 처사에 관해서는 다르지 않기를 바랍니다.”"], ["interrupted Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말을 가로막았다."], ["“I say that he has most unfairly compromised Dorothea.", "“그는 도러시아를 더없이 부당하게 욕보였습니다."], ["I say that there never was a meaner, more ungentlemanly action than this—a codicil of this sort to a will which he made at the time of his marriage with the knowledge and reliance of her family—a positive insult to Dorothea!”", "이보다 더 비열하고 더 신사답지 못한 행위는 일찍이 없었다고 봅니다—그녀의 가족이 알고 신뢰한 가운데 결혼할 때 작성한 유언장에 이런 종류의 추가 조항을 붙이다니—이는 도러시아에 대한 명백한 모욕입니다!” * 추가 조항: 유언장에 덧붙이는 부가 조항(codicil)으로, 원래 유언의 일부를 변경하거나 보충하는 법적 문서."]], [["“Well, you know, Casaubon was a little twisted about Ladislaw.", "“글쎄, 알다시피 캐소본은 래디슬로에 관해 조금 비틀려 있었소."], ["Ladislaw has told me the reason—dislike of the bent he took, you know—Ladislaw didn’t think much of Casaubon’s notions, Thoth and Dagon—that sort of thing: and I fancy that Casaubon didn’t like the independent position Ladislaw had taken up.", "래디슬로가 그 이유를 내게 말해 주었지—그가 택한 성향이 마음에 들지 않았던 거요—래디슬로는 캐소본의 생각들을, 토트니 다곤이니 하는 그런 것들을 대단치 않게 여겼거든. 그리고 내 생각엔 캐소본도 래디슬로가 취한 독립적인 처지가 마음에 들지 않았던 것 같소."], ["I saw the letters between them, you know.", "나는 둘 사이에 오간 편지들을 봤다오. 가엾은 캐소본은 책 속에 좀 파묻혀 있었어—세상 물정을 몰랐던 거지.”"], ["Poor Casaubon was a little buried in books—he didn’t know the world.”", "* 토트, 다곤: 토트는 고대 이집트의 지혜의 신, 다곤은 고대 셈족의 신. 캐소본의 신화 연구 대상을 가리킨다."]], [["“It’s all very well for Ladislaw to put that color on it,”", "“래디슬로가 그렇게 채색해서 말하는 거야 좋습니다.” 제임스 경이 말했다."], ["said Sir James.", "“하지만 저는 캐소본이 오로지 도러시아 때문에 그를 질투한 것이라고 믿습니다."], ["“But I believe Casaubon was only jealous of him on Dorothea’s account, and the world will suppose that she gave him some reason; and that is what makes it so abominable—coupling her name with this young fellow’s.”", "그리고 세상 사람들은 그녀가 그에게 무슨 빌미를 주었으리라 짐작할 겁니다. 바로 그 점이 이 일을 이토록 가증스럽게 만드는 거지요—그녀의 이름을 이 젊은이의 이름과 엮어 놓다니 말입니다.”"]], [["“My dear Chettam, it won’t lead to anything, you know,”", "“이보게 체텀, 이건 아무 결과도 낳지 않을 거요.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke, seating himself and sticking on his eye-glass again.", "브룩 씨는 자리에 앉아 다시 안경을 걸치며 말했다."], ["“It’s all of a piece with Casaubon’s oddity.", "“이건 다 캐소본의 별난 성미와 한통속인 일이오."], ["This paper, now, ‘Synoptical Tabulation’ and so on, ‘for the use of Mrs. Casaubon,’ it was locked up in the desk with the will.", "자, 이 서류만 해도 그렇소, ‘개요 도표’니 뭐니 하고, ‘캐소본 부인의 사용을 위하여’라고 되어 있는데, 유언장과 함께 책상에 잠겨 있었다오."], ["I suppose he meant Dorothea to publish his researches, eh?", "내 짐작에 그는 도러시아가 자기 연구를 출판해 주기를 바란 게야, 안 그렇소?"], ["and she’ll do it, you know; she has gone into his studies uncommonly.”", "그리고 그 애는 그렇게 할 거요, 알다시피. 그 애는 그의 연구에 유난히 깊이 파고들었으니까.”"]], [["“My dear sir,”", "“이보세요, 어르신.”"], ["said Sir James, impatiently, “that is neither here nor there.", "제임스 경이 초조하게 말했다. “그건 이 일과 아무 상관도 없습니다."], ["The question is, whether you don’t see with me the propriety of sending young Ladislaw away?”", "문제는, 젊은 래디슬로를 멀리 보내는 것이 마땅하다는 데 어르신께서 저와 견해를 같이하시느냐는 겁니다.”"]], [["“Well, no, not the urgency of the thing.", "“글쎄, 아니, 그렇게 급한 일은 아니오."], ["By-and-by, perhaps, it may come round.", "머지않아 어쩌면 일이 저절로 풀릴 수도 있고."], ["As to gossip, you know, sending him away won’t hinder gossip.", "소문으로 말하자면, 알다시피 그를 멀리 보낸다고 소문이 막히는 것도 아니오."], ["People say what they like to say, not what they have chapter and verse for,”", "사람들은 근거가 있는 말이 아니라 자기들이 하고 싶은 말을 하는 법이니까.” 브룩 씨는 자기 바람 쪽에 놓인 진실들에 관해서는 예리해지면서 말했다."], ["said Mr Brooke, becoming acute about the truths that lay on the side of his own wishes.", "“나는 어느 정도까지는 래디슬로를 떼어 놓을 수 있소—그에게서 ‘파이어니어’를 빼앗는다든가 그런 식으로 말이오."], ["“I might get rid of Ladislaw up to a certain point—take away the ‘Pioneer’ from him, and that sort of thing; but I couldn’t send him out of the country if he didn’t choose to go—didn’t choose, you know.”", "하지만 그가 가지 않겠다고 한다면 그를 이 고장 밖으로 내보낼 수는 없어요—가지 않겠다고 하면 말이오.” * 파이어니어: 브룩 씨가 후원하는 지역 신문의 이름."]], [["Mr. Brooke, persisting as quietly as if he were only discussing the nature of last year’s weather, and nodding at the end with his usual amenity, was an exasperating form of obstinacy.", "작년 날씨가 어떠했는지나 논하는 듯이 차분하게 고집을 부리며, 끝에 가서는 늘 그렇듯 상냥하게 고개를 끄덕이는 브룩 씨의 모습은 사람의 속을 뒤집어 놓는 완고함의 한 형태였다."]], [["“Good God!”", "“맙소사!”"], ["said Sir James, with as much passion as he ever showed, “let us get him a post; let us spend money on him.", "제임스 경은 평소 좀처럼 드러내지 않던 격정을 다해 말했다. “그에게 자리를 마련해 줍시다."], ["If he could go in the suite of some Colonial Governor!", "그를 위해 돈을 씁시다. 어느 식민지 총독의 수행원으로 갈 수만 있다면!"], ["Grampus might take him—and I could write to Fulke about it.”", "그램퍼스가 그를 받아 줄지도 모르고—제가 그 일로 풀크에게 편지를 쓸 수도 있습니다.”"]], [["“But Ladislaw won’t be shipped off like a head of cattle, my dear fellow; Ladislaw has his ideas.", "“하지만 래디슬로는 소 떼처럼 배에 실려 보내질 사람이 아니오, 이보게. 래디슬로에겐 나름의 생각이 있어."], ["It’s my opinion that if he were to part from me to-morrow, you’d only hear the more of him in the country.", "내 생각엔 그가 내일 당장 나와 헤어진다 해도, 자네는 이 고장에서 그에 관한 소문을 더 많이 듣게 될 거요."], ["With his talent for speaking and drawing up documents, there are few men who could come up to him as an agitator—an agitator, you know.”", "연설하고 문서를 작성하는 그 재능으로 말하자면, 선동가로서 그를 따라올 사람이 거의 없을 테니까—선동가로서 말이오, 알겠소.”"]], [["“Agitator!”", "“선동가라뇨!”"], ["said Sir James, with bitter emphasis, feeling that the syllables of this word properly repeated were a sufficient exposure of its hatefulness.", "제임스 경이 쓰디쓴 어조로 강조하며 말했는데, 이 단어를 제대로 한 음절 한 음절 되뇌는 것만으로도 그 가증스러움을 충분히 드러낼 수 있다고 느끼는 듯했다."]], [["“But be reasonable, Chettam.", "“하지만 사리를 따져 보세요, 체텀."], ["Dorothea, now.", "자, 도러시아 말이오."], ["As you say, she had better go to Celia as soon as possible.", "자네 말대로 그 애는 가능한 한 빨리 실리아에게 가는 게 좋겠지."], ["She can stay under your roof, and in the mean time things may come round quietly.", "자네 집 지붕 아래 머물 수 있을 테고, 그러는 사이에 일이 조용히 풀릴 수도 있을 거요."], ["Don’t let us be firing off our guns in a hurry, you know.", "알다시피 우리가 서둘러 대포를 쏘아 댈 건 없소."], ["Standish will keep our counsel, and the news will be old before it’s known.", "스탠디시가 비밀을 지켜 줄 테고, 소문은 알려지기도 전에 낡은 이야기가 될 거요."], ["Twenty things may happen to carry off Ladislaw—without my doing anything, you know.”", "스무 가지나 되는 일이 일어나 래디슬로를 데려가 버릴 수도 있어—내가 아무것도 하지 않아도 말이오, 알겠소.”"]], [["“Then I am to conclude that you decline to do anything?”", "“그렇다면 어르신께서는 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 말씀으로 결론을 내려도 되겠습니까?”"]], [["“Decline, Chettam?—no—I didn’t say decline.", "“거절이라니, 체텀?—아니—거절한다고 한 적은 없소."], ["But I really don’t see what I could do.", "하지만 정말이지 내가 무얼 할 수 있을지 모르겠단 말이오."], ["Ladislaw is a gentleman.”", "래디슬로는 신사잖소.”"]], [["“I am glad to hear it!”", "“그 말씀을 들으니 기쁘군요!”"], ["said Sir James, his irritation making him forget himself a little.", "제임스 경은 짜증이 치밀어 잠시 자제력을 잃고 말했다."], ["“I am sure Casaubon was not.”", "“캐소본은 분명 신사가 아니었습니다.”"]], [["“Well, it would have been worse if he had made the codicil to hinder her from marrying again at all, you know.”", "“글쎄, 만약 그가 그 애가 아예 재혼하지 못하도록 추가 조항을 달았더라면 더 나빴을 거요, 알다시피.”"]], [["“I don’t know that,”", "“그건 모를 일입니다.”"], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“It would have been less indelicate.”", "“그게 차라리 덜 무례했겠지요.”"]], [["“One of poor Casaubon’s freaks!", "“가엾은 캐소본의 변덕 가운데 하나요!"], ["That attack upset his brain a little.", "그 발작이 그의 머리를 좀 흐트러뜨린 거지."], ["It all goes for nothing.", "다 부질없는 일이오."], ["She doesn’t want to marry Ladislaw.”", "그 애는 래디슬로와 결혼하고 싶어 하지 않으니까.”"]], [["“But this codicil is framed so as to make everybody believe that she did.", "“하지만 이 추가 조항은 모든 사람이 그녀가 그러고 싶어 했다고 믿게끔 짜여 있습니다."], ["I don’t believe anything of the sort about Dorothea,”", "저는 도러시아에 관해서는 그런 종류의 것을 조금도 믿지 않습니다.”"], ["said Sir James—then frowningly, “but I suspect Ladislaw.", "제임스 경은 이렇게 말하고 나서 이맛살을 찌푸리며 덧붙였다. “하지만 래디슬로는 의심스럽습니다."], ["I tell you frankly, I suspect Ladislaw.”", "솔직히 말씀드리지요, 저는 래디슬로를 의심합니다.”"]], [["“I couldn’t take any immediate action on that ground, Chettam.", "“그런 근거로는 내가 당장 어떤 조처도 취할 수 없소, 체텀."], ["In fact, if it were possible to pack him off—send him to Norfolk Island—that sort of thing—it would look all the worse for Dorothea to those who knew about it.", "사실, 만에 하나 그를 짐짝처럼 부쳐 보내는 게—그를 노퍽섬으로 보낸다든가 하는 그런 일이 가능하다 해도—그 내막을 아는 사람들에게는 도러시아에게 도리어 더 나쁘게 보일 거요."], ["It would seem as if we distrusted her—distrusted her, you know.”", "마치 우리가 그 애를 못 믿는 것처럼—그 애를 의심하는 것처럼 보일 테니까, 알겠소.”"]], [["That Mr. Brooke had hit on an undeniable argument, did not tend to soothe Sir James.", "* 노퍽섬: 당시 영국의 유배지로 쓰이던 남태평양의 섬."], ["He put out his hand to reach his hat, implying that he did not mean to contend further, and said, still with some heat—", "브룩 씨가 부인할 수 없는 논거를 짚어 냈다는 사실이 제임스 경의 마음을 누그러뜨리지는 못했다. 그는 더 이상 다툴 뜻이 없음을 내비치며 모자를 집으려 손을 뻗었고, 여전히 다소 열기를 띤 채 말했다—"]], [["“Well, I can only say that I think Dorothea was sacrificed once, because her friends were too careless.", "“그렇다면 저로서는 이 말밖에 드릴 게 없군요. 도러시아는 한 번 희생되었다고 생각합니다."], ["I shall do what I can, as her brother, to protect her now.”", "그녀의 친지들이 너무 무심했던 탓이지요. 이제 저는 그녀의 오빠로서 그녀를 지키기 위해 할 수 있는 일을 하겠습니다.”"]], [["“You can’t do better than get her to Freshitt as soon as possible, Chettam.", "“그 애를 가능한 한 빨리 프레시트로 데려가는 것보다 더 나은 일은 없을 거요, 체텀."], ["I approve that plan altogether,” said Mr.", "그 계획에 나는 전적으로 찬성하오.”"], ["Brooke, well pleased that he had won the argument.", "브룩 씨는 논쟁에서 이긴 것이 흡족하여 말했다."], ["It would have been highly inconvenient to him to part with Ladislaw at that time, when a dissolution might happen any day, and electors were to be convinced of the course by which the interests of the country would be best served. Mr.", "의회 해산이 언제 닥칠지 모르고, 나라의 이익에 가장 잘 부합하는 노선이 무엇인지 유권자들을 설득해야 하는 이 시기에 래디슬로와 헤어진다는 것은 그에게 몹시 곤란한 일이었을 터였다."], ["Brooke sincerely believed that this end could be secured by his own return to Parliament: he offered the forces of his mind honestly to the nation.", "브룩 씨는 자신이 의회로 복귀함으로써 그 목적을 이룰 수 있다고 진심으로 믿었다. 그는 자기 정신의 힘을 나라에 정직하게 바쳤던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER L.", "# CHAPTER L."]], [["“This Loller here wol precilen us somewhat.” “Nay by my father’s soule!", "“아니, 내 아버지의 영혼을 두고 맹세컨대!"], ["that schal he nat,” Sayde the Schipman, ‘here schal he not preche, We schal no gospel glosen here ne teche.", "그럴 수는 없지,”"], ["We leven all in the gret God,’ quod he.", "선장이 말했다."], ["He wolden sowen some diffcultee.”—Canterbury Tales.", "“여기서 설교 따위는 못 하리라,"]], [["Dorothea had been safe at Freshitt Hall nearly a week before she had asked any dangerous questions. Every morning now she sat with Celia in the prettiest of up-stairs sitting-rooms, opening into a small conservatory—Celia all in white and lavender like a bunch of mixed violets, watching the remarkable acts of the baby, which were so dubious to her inexperienced mind that all conversation was interrupted by appeals for their interpretation made to the oracular nurse.", "우리는 여기서 복음을 해석하거나 가르치지 않으리니,\n우리 모두는 위대하신 하느님을 믿노라.”"], ["Dorothea sat by in her widow’s dress, with an expression which rather provoked Celia, as being much too sad; for not only was baby quite well, but really when a husband had been so dull and troublesome while he lived, and besides that had—well, well! Sir James, of course, had told Celia everything, with a strong representation how important it was that Dorothea should not know it sooner than was inevitable.", "그가 말했다."]], [["But Mr. Brooke had been right in predicting that Dorothea would not long remain passive where action had been assigned to her; she knew the purport of her husband’s will made at the time of their marriage, and her mind, as soon as she was clearly conscious of her position, was silently occupied with what she ought to do as the owner of Lowick Manor with the patronage of the living attached to it.", "“그자는 무언가 골치 아픈 문제를 뿌리려 하는 것이로다.” — 「캔터베리 이야기」 도로시아는 프레시트 저택에서 거의 일주일을 무사히 지낸 뒤에야 위험한 질문들을 던지기 시작했다. 이제 그녀는 아침마다 작은 온실로 통하는 위층의 가장 예쁜 거실에서 실리아와 함께 앉아 있었다. 흰빛과 연보라빛으로 차려입은 실리아는 마치 여러 빛깔이 뒤섞인 제비꽃 한 다발 같았고, 아기의 놀라운 행동들을 지켜보고 있었는데, 그 행동들이 미숙한 그녀의 마음에는 너무도 모호해서 모든 대화가 신탁을 내리는 유모에게 그 해석을 청하는 일로 자꾸만 끊기곤 했다. 도로시아는 과부의 상복 차림으로 그 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그 표정이 실리아를 다소 언짢게 했으니, 지나치게 슬퍼 보였던 까닭이다. 아기는 더없이 건강했고, 게다가 살아 있는 동안 그토록 따분하고 성가셨던 남편이었으며, 또 그 위에 — 아무튼, 아무튼! 물론 제임스 경은 실리아에게 모든 것을 이야기해 주면서, 도로시아가 어쩔 수 없이 알게 되기 전에 미리 알아서는 안 된다고 강하게 당부했던 터였다."]], [["One morning when her uncle paid his usual visit, though with an unusual alacrity in his manner which he accounted for by saying that it was now pretty certain Parliament would be dissolved forthwith, Dorothea said—", "하지만 브룩 씨는 도로시아가 자기에게 행동이 맡겨진 자리에서 오래도록 수동적으로 머물러 있지 않으리라고 정확히 내다보았다. 그녀는 결혼 당시에 작성된 남편의 유언 내용을 알고 있었으니, 자신의 처지를 분명히 의식하게 되자마자 그녀의 마음은 로윅 영지의 소유주이자 거기 딸린 성직 추천권을 지닌 사람으로서 마땅히 해야 할 일을 두고 조용히 골몰하기 시작했다."]], [["“Uncle, it is right now that I should consider who is to have the living at Lowick.", "어느 날 아침 삼촌이 평소처럼 찾아왔는데, 그날따라 태도에 유난히 활기가 넘쳤다. 그는 이제 의회가 곧 해산되리라는 것이 꽤 확실해졌기 때문이라고 그 까닭을 설명했다."], ["After Mr.", "도로시아가 말했다."], ["Tucker had been provided for, I never heard my husband say that he had any clergyman in his mind as a successor to himself.", "“삼촌, 이제 로윅의 성직을 누구에게 맡길지 제가 생각해 보아야 마땅하겠어요. 터커 씨에게 자리가 마련된 뒤로, 남편이 자기 후임으로 어떤 성직자를 염두에 두고 있다고 말하는 걸 한 번도 들은 적이 없거든요."], ["I think I ought to have the keys now and go to Lowick to examine all my husband’s papers.", "이제 제가 열쇠를 받아 로윅으로 가서 남편의 서류를 모두 살펴보아야 할 것 같아요."], ["There may be something that would throw light on his wishes.”", "그분의 뜻을 밝혀 줄 무언가가 있을지도 모르니까요.”"]], [["“No hurry, my dear,”", "“서두를 것 없다, 얘야.”"], ["said Mr. Brooke, quietly.", "브룩 씨가 조용히 말했다."], ["“By-and-by, you know, you can go, if you like.", "“이따가, 알다시피, 네가 원하면 가도 좋아."], ["But I cast my eyes over things in the desks and drawers—there was nothing—nothing but deep subjects, you know—besides the will.", "하지만 내가 책상이며 서랍 속을 죽 훑어보았는데 — 아무것도 없더구나 — 알다시피 깊이 있는 주제들뿐, 그 밖에는 아무것도 없었어 — 유언장 말고는 말이지."], ["Everything can be done by-and-by.", "모든 건 이따가 하면 돼."], ["As to the living, I have had an application for interest already—I should say rather good.", "성직 자리로 말하자면, 이미 청탁을 하나 받았단다 — 꽤 괜찮다고 해야겠지."], ["Mr. Tyke has been strongly recommended to me—I had something to do with getting him an appointment before.", "타이크 씨를 강력히 추천받았어 — 전에 그자가 한 자리를 얻는 데 내가 좀 거든 적이 있지."], ["An apostolic man, I believe—the sort of thing that would suit you, my dear.”", "사도 같은 사람이라더구나 — 알다시피 네게 딱 어울릴 만한 부류 말이다, 얘야.”"]], [["“I should like to have fuller knowledge about him, uncle, and judge for myself, if Mr. Casaubon has not left any expression of his wishes.", "“캐소본 씨가 자기 뜻을 적어 남긴 게 없다면, 삼촌, 저는 그분에 대해 더 자세히 알아보고 스스로 판단하고 싶어요."], ["He has perhaps made some addition to his will—there may be some instructions for me,”", "어쩌면 유언장에 무언가를 덧붙였을지도 모르고요 — 저를 위한 어떤 지시가 있을지도 모르잖아요.”"], ["said Dorothea, who had all the while had this conjecture in her mind with relation to her husband’s work.", "도로시아가 말했다. 그녀는 줄곧 남편의 저작과 관련해 이런 추측을 마음에 품고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Nothing about the rectory, my dear—nothing,” said Mr.", "“목사관에 대해서는 아무것도 없다, 얘야 — 아무것도.”"], ["Brooke, rising to go away, and putting out his hand to his nieces: “nor about his researches, you know.", "브룩 씨가 자리에서 일어나 떠나려고 조카딸들에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. “알다시피 그의 연구에 대해서도 마찬가지야."], ["Nothing in the will.”", "유언장에는 아무것도 없어.”"]], [["Dorothea’s lip quivered.", "도로시아의 입술이 떨렸다."]], [["“Come, you must not think of these things yet, my dear.", "“자, 아직은 이런 일들을 생각해서는 안 된다, 얘야."], ["By-and-by, you know.”", "이따가, 알다시피.”"]], [["“I am quite well now, uncle; I wish to exert myself.”", "“저는 이제 아주 멀쩡해요, 삼촌. 저 자신을 좀 부지런히 움직이고 싶어요.”"]], [["“Well, well, we shall see.", "“그래, 그래, 두고 보자꾸나."], ["But I must run away now—I have no end of work now—it’s a crisis—a political crisis, you know.", "하지만 난 지금 서둘러 가야겠다 — 지금 할 일이 끝도 없어 — 위기야 — 정치적 위기란다, 알다시피."], ["And here is Celia and her little man—you are an aunt, you know, now, and I am a sort of grandfather,” said Mr.", "그리고 여기 실리아와 이 작은 사내가 있구나 — 너는 이제 이모가 되었고, 알다시피, 나는 일종의 할아버지가 된 셈이지.”"], ["Brooke, with placid hurry, anxious to get away and tell Chettam that it would not be his (Mr. Brooke’s) fault if Dorothea insisted on looking into everything.", "브룩 씨는 차분한 다급함 속에서 말했으니, 어서 빠져나가 채텀에게, 도로시아가 모든 일을 들여다보겠다고 고집한다면 그것은 자기(브룩 씨)의 잘못이 아니라고 말하고 싶어 안달이 났던 것이다."]], [["Dorothea sank back in her chair when her uncle had left the room, and cast her eyes down meditatively on her crossed hands.", "삼촌이 방을 나가자 도로시아는 의자에 깊이 몸을 묻고, 깍지 낀 두 손으로 시선을 떨군 채 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Look, Dodo!", "“봐, 도도!"], ["look at him!", "얘 좀 봐!"], ["Did you ever see anything like that?”", "이런 거 본 적 있어?”"], ["said Celia, in her comfortable staccato.", "실리아가 편안하고 또박또박한 말투로 말했다."]], [["“What, Kitty?”", "“뭘, 키티?”"], ["said Dorothea, lifting her eyes rather absently.", "도로시아가 다소 멍하니 눈을 들며 말했다."]], [["“What?", "“뭐긴?"], ["why, his upper lip; see how he is drawing it down, as if he meant to make a face.", "아니, 얘 윗입술 말이야. 어떻게 끌어내리는지 봐, 마치 얼굴을 찡그리려는 것처럼."], ["Isn’t it wonderful!", "신기하지 않아!"], ["He may have his little thoughts.", "얘도 나름대로 생각이 있나 봐."], ["I wish nurse were here.", "유모가 여기 있으면 좋을 텐데."], ["Do look at him.”", "얘 좀 봐.”"]], [["A large tear which had been for some time gathering, rolled down Dorothea’s cheek as she looked up and tried to smile.", "한동안 고여 있던 커다란 눈물 한 방울이 도로시아의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸고, 그녀는 고개를 들어 미소 지으려 애썼다."]], [["“Don’t be sad, Dodo; kiss baby.", "“슬퍼하지 마, 도도. 아기에게 입 맞춰 줘."], ["What are you brooding over so?", "뭘 그렇게 골똘히 생각해?"], ["I am sure you did everything, and a great deal too much.", "언니는 분명 할 수 있는 건 다 했어, 그것도 너무 지나치게 많이."], ["You should be happy now.”", "이제는 행복해야 해.”"]], [["“I wonder if Sir James would drive me to Lowick.", "“제임스 경이 나를 로윅까지 태워다 줄 수 있을지 모르겠구나."], ["I want to look over everything—to see if there were any words written for me.”", "모든 걸 살펴보고 싶어 — 나를 위해 적어 둔 말이 있었는지 보고 싶어.”"]], [["“You are not to go till Mr. Lydgate says you may go.", "“리드게이트 씨가 가도 좋다고 할 때까지는 가면 안 돼."], ["And he has not said so yet (here you are, nurse; take baby and walk up and down the gallery).", "아직 그렇게 말하지 않았잖아 (여기 왔구나, 유모. 아기를 받아 회랑을 오르내리며 걸어 줘요)."], ["Besides, you have got a wrong notion in your head as usual, Dodo—I can see that: it vexes me.”", "게다가 언니는 늘 그렇듯이 머릿속에 잘못된 생각을 품고 있어, 도도 — 난 알 수 있어. 그게 나를 속상하게 해.”"]], [["“Where am I wrong, Kitty?”", "“내가 어디가 잘못됐다는 거니, 키티?”"], ["said Dorothea, quite meekly.", "도로시아가 자못 온순하게 말했다."], ["She was almost ready now to think Celia wiser than herself, and was really wondering with some fear what her wrong notion was.", "그녀는 이제 거의 실리아가 자기보다 현명하다고 여길 지경이 되어, 자신의 잘못된 생각이 무엇일까 약간의 두려움을 품고 진심으로 궁금해하고 있었다."], ["Celia felt her advantage, and was determined to use it.", "실리아는 자기가 유리한 처지에 있음을 느꼈고, 그것을 활용하기로 마음먹었다."], ["None of them knew Dodo as well as she did, or knew how to manage her.", "식구들 중 누구도 자기만큼 도도를 잘 알거나 그녀를 잘 다룰 줄 모른다고 여겼다."], ["Since Celia’s baby was born, she had had a new sense of her mental solidity and calm wisdom.", "아기를 낳은 뒤로 실리아는 자신의 정신적 견고함과 차분한 지혜에 대한 새로운 자각을 갖게 되었다."], ["It seemed clear that where there was a baby, things were right enough, and that error, in general, was a mere lack of that central poising force.", "아기가 있는 곳에서는 만사가 충분히 제대로 돌아가고, 일반적으로 오류란 바로 그 중심을 잡아 주는 균형의 힘이 결여된 데서 비롯된다는 것이 분명해 보였다."]], [["“I can see what you are thinking of as well as can be, Dodo,”", "“언니가 무슨 생각을 하는지 나는 더없이 잘 알겠어, 도도.”"], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“You are wanting to find out if there is anything uncomfortable for you to do now, only because Mr. Casaubon wished it.", "“언니는 지금 캐소본 씨가 바랐다는 이유만으로, 자기한테 거북한 일이 또 없는지 찾아내려는 거잖아."], ["As if you had not been uncomfortable enough before.", "마치 이제껏 충분히 거북하지 않았다는 듯이 말이야."], ["And he doesn’t deserve it, and you will find that out.", "그런데 그분은 그럴 자격이 없어, 언니도 곧 알게 될 거야."], ["He has behaved very badly.", "그분은 아주 못되게 굴었어."], ["James is as angry with him as can be.", "제임스는 그분한테 이루 말할 수 없이 화가 나 있어."], ["And I had better tell you, to prepare you.”", "그러니 마음의 준비를 하도록 차라리 내가 말해 주는 게 낫겠어.”"]], [["“Celia,”", "“실리아,”"], ["said Dorothea, entreatingly, “you distress me.", "도로시아가 애원하듯 말했다. “네 말이 나를 괴롭게 하는구나."], ["Tell me at once what you mean.”", "무슨 뜻인지 당장 말해 줘.”"], ["It glanced through her mind that Mr. Casaubon had left the property away from her—which would not be so very distressing.", "캐소본 씨가 재산을 자기에게서 거두어 갔을지도 모른다는 생각이 그녀의 머리를 스쳤지만 — 그것은 그리 괴로운 일은 아닐 터였다."]], [["“Why, he has made a codicil to his will, to say the property was all to go away from you if you married—I mean—”", "“그게 말이야, 그분이 유언장에 추가 조항을 만들어서, 언니가 결혼하면 재산이 모두 언니에게서 거두어진다고 해 놨어 — 그러니까 내 말은 —”"]], [["“That is of no consequence,”", "“그건 아무래도 상관없어.”"], ["said Dorothea, breaking in impetuously.", "도로시아가 격하게 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“But if you married Mr. Ladislaw, not anybody else,”", "“하지만 언니가 래디슬로 씨와 결혼할 경우에 그렇다는 거야, 다른 누구도 아니고.”"], ["Celia went on with persevering quietude.", "실리아가 끈질긴 침착함으로 말을 이었다."], ["“Of course that is of no consequence in one way—you never would marry Mr.", "“물론 한편으로는 아무 상관없는 일이지 — 언니는 절대로 래디슬로 씨와 결혼하지 않을 테니까."], ["Ladislaw; but that only makes it worse of Mr. Casaubon.”", "하지만 그래서 캐소본 씨가 더더욱 못된 거야.”"]], [["The blood rushed to Dorothea’s face and neck painfully.", "피가 도로시아의 얼굴과 목으로 고통스럽게 솟구쳤다."], ["But Celia was administering what she thought a sobering dose of fact.", "하지만 실리아는 자기가 정신을 차리게 해 줄 사실의 처방이라고 여기는 것을 베풀고 있는 중이었다."], ["It was taking up notions that had done Dodo’s health so much harm.", "도도의 건강을 그토록 망쳐 놓은 것은 바로 그런 잡념을 떠안는 짓이었다."], ["So she went on in her neutral tone, as if she had been remarking on baby’s robes.", "그래서 실리아는 마치 아기의 배냇저고리에 대해 이야기하기라도 하듯 무심한 어조로 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“James says so.", "“제임스가 그렇게 말해."], ["He says it is abominable, and not like a gentleman.", "그건 가증스러운 짓이고, 신사답지 못한 짓이라고 했어."], ["And there never was a better judge than James. It is as if Mr.", "그리고 제임스보다 더 나은 판단을 할 사람은 없어."], ["Casaubon wanted to make people believe that you would wish to marry Mr. Ladislaw—which is ridiculous.", "마치 캐소본 씨는 언니가 래디슬로 씨와 결혼하고 싶어 할 거라고 사람들이 믿게 만들려는 것 같아 — 우스꽝스러운 일이지."], ["Only James says it was to hinder Mr. Ladislaw from wanting to marry you for your money—just as if he ever would think of making you an offer.", "다만 제임스 말로는, 래디슬로 씨가 언니 돈을 노리고 결혼하려는 걸 막으려는 거였대 — 마치 그 사람이 언니한테 청혼할 생각이라도 할 것처럼 말이야."], ["Mrs. Cadwallader said you might as well marry an Italian with white mice!", "캐드월래더 부인은 차라리 흰쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람하고 결혼하는 게 낫겠다고 했어!"], ["But I must just go and look at baby,”", "그건 그렇고 나는 아기를 좀 보러 가야겠어.”"], ["Celia added, without the least change of tone, throwing a light shawl over her, and tripping away.", "실리아는 조금도 어조를 바꾸지 않은 채 이렇게 덧붙이고는, 가벼운 숄을 어깨에 두르고 종종걸음으로 사라졌다."]], [["Dorothea by this time had turned cold again, and now threw herself back helplessly in her chair.", "이때쯤 도로시아는 다시 온몸이 싸늘해져 있었고, 이제는 무력하게 의자에 몸을 던지듯 기댔다."], ["She might have compared her experience at that moment to the vague, alarmed consciousness that her life was taking on a new form, that she was undergoing a metamorphosis in which memory would not adjust itself to the stirring of new organs.", "그 순간의 자신의 경험을, 그녀는 자기 삶이 새로운 형태를 띠어 가고 있다는, 기억이 새로 꿈틀거리는 기관들의 움직임에 미처 적응하지 못하는 어떤 변태를 겪고 있다는, 막연하고 불안한 의식에 견줄 수도 있었으리라. 모든 것이 그 양상을 바꾸고 있었다."], ["Everything was changing its aspect: her husband’s conduct, her own duteous feeling towards him, every struggle between them—and yet more, her whole relation to Will Ladislaw.", "남편의 행동, 그를 향한 자신의 의무감에 찬 마음, 두 사람 사이의 모든 갈등 — 그리고 그보다 더, 윌 래디슬로와의 관계 전부가. 그녀의 세계는 발작적인 변화의 와중에 있었다."], ["Her world was in a state of convulsive change; the only thing she could say distinctly to herself was, that she must wait and think anew.", "그녀가 자기 자신에게 분명히 말할 수 있는 단 한 가지는, 기다리며 새롭게 생각해 보아야 한다는 것뿐이었다. 한 가지 변화는 마치 그것이 죄이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 두렵게 했다."], ["One change terrified her as if it had been a sin; it was a violent shock of repulsion from her departed husband, who had had hidden thoughts, perhaps perverting everything she said and did.", "그것은 세상을 떠난 남편으로부터의 격렬한 혐오의 충격이었으니, 남편은 숨겨진 생각들을 품고 있었고, 어쩌면 그녀의 말과 행동 모두를 왜곡해 받아들였을지도 몰랐다. 그러다가 그녀는 자신을 또한 떨리게 만드는 또 다른 변화를 의식했다."], ["Then again she was conscious of another change which also made her tremulous; it was a sudden strange yearning of heart towards Will Ladislaw.", "그것은 윌 래디슬로를 향한 갑작스럽고 낯선 마음의 갈망이었다. 그가 어떤 상황에서든 자신의 연인이 될 수 있으리라는 생각은 이제껏 한 번도 그녀의 마음에 떠오른 적이 없었다."], ["It had never before entered her mind that he could, under any circumstances, be her lover: conceive the effect of the sudden revelation that another had thought of him in that light—that perhaps he himself had been conscious of such a possibility,—and this with the hurrying, crowding vision of unfitting conditions, and questions not soon to be solved.", "그런데 다른 누군가가 그를 그런 시선으로 생각해 왔다는 — 어쩌면 그 자신도 그런 가능성을 의식하고 있었을지 모른다는 — 갑작스러운 폭로가 어떤 영향을 미쳤을지 헤아려 보라. 게다가 어울리지 않는 여러 조건들과 쉽사리 풀리지 않을 의문들이 황급히 한꺼번에 밀려드는 환영까지 더해진 채로 말이다."]], [["It seemed a long while—she did not know how long—before she heard Celia saying, “That will do, nurse; he will be quiet on my lap now.", "오랜 시간이 지난 것 같았다 — 얼마나 오래인지 그녀는 알 수 없었다 — 실리아가 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들려올 때까지는. “이제 됐어요, 유모."], ["You can go to lunch, and let Garratt stay in the next room.", "얘는 내 무릎에서 얌전히 있을 거예요. 점심 들러 가요, 그리고 개럿더러 옆방에 있게 해 줘요."], ["What I think, Dodo,”", "내 생각은 말이야, 도도,”"], ["Celia went on, observing nothing more than that Dorothea was leaning back in her chair, and likely to be passive, “is that Mr.", "실리아는 도로시아가 의자에 기대 앉아 잠자코 있을 듯하다는 것 말고는 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 채 말을 이었다. “캐소본 씨가 심술궂었다는 거야."], ["Casaubon was spiteful. I never did like him, and James never did.", "나는 그분을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없고, 제임스도 마찬가지였어."], ["I think the corners of his mouth were dreadfully spiteful.", "내 생각엔 그분 입가가 끔찍하게 심술궂었던 것 같아."], ["And now he has behaved in this way, I am sure religion does not require you to make yourself uncomfortable about him.", "그리고 이제 그분이 이런 식으로 굴었으니, 종교가 언니더러 그분 때문에 자기 마음을 괴롭히라고 요구하지는 않을 거야, 분명히."], ["If he has been taken away, that is a mercy, and you ought to be grateful.", "그분이 데려가졌다면, 그건 자비고, 언니는 감사해야 마땅해."], ["We should not grieve, should we, baby?”", "우리는 슬퍼해서는 안 돼, 그렇지, 아가?”"], ["said Celia confidentially to that unconscious centre and poise of the world, who had the most remarkable fists all complete even to the nails, and hair enough, really, when you took his cap off, to make—you didn’t know what:—in short, he was Bouddha in a Western form.", "실리아는 세상의 무의식적인 중심이자 균형추인 그 존재에게 은밀히 속삭이듯 말했으니, 그 존재는 손톱까지 완전히 갖춘 더없이 놀라운 두 주먹을 지녔고, 모자를 벗기면 정말이지 무언가를 — 무엇이라 할 수 없는 무언가를 만들기에 충분할 만큼의 머리카락이 나 있었다. 한마디로, 그는 서양식 형상을 한 부처였다."]], [["At this crisis Lydgate was announced, and one of the first things he said was, “I fear you are not so well as you were, Mrs.", "이 위기의 순간에 리드게이트가 왔다는 전갈이 들어왔고, 그가 처음 꺼낸 말 가운데 하나는 이러했다. “전보다 안색이 좋지 않으신 것 같군요, 캐소본 부인."], ["Casaubon; have you been agitated?", "마음이 동요하셨습니까?"], ["allow me to feel your pulse.”", "맥을 좀 짚어 보게 해 주십시오.”"], ["Dorothea’s hand was of a marble coldness.", "도로시아의 손은 대리석처럼 차가웠다."]], [["“She wants to go to Lowick, to look over papers,”", "“언니는 서류를 살펴보러 로윅에 가고 싶어 해요.”"], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“She ought not, ought she?”", "“가면 안 되겠죠, 그렇죠?”"]], [["Lydgate did not speak for a few moments.", "리드게이트는 잠시 말이 없었다."], ["Then he said, looking at Dorothea.", "그러다가 도로시아를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I hardly know.", "“저도 잘 모르겠습니다."], ["In my opinion Mrs. Casaubon should do what would give her the most repose of mind.", "제 생각에는 캐소본 부인께서 마음에 가장 큰 평온을 주는 일을 하셔야 할 것 같습니다."], ["That repose will not always come from being forbidden to act.”", "그 평온이 행동을 금지당함으로써 늘 찾아오는 것은 아니니까요.”"]], [["“Thank you,”", "“고맙습니다.”"], ["said Dorothea, exerting herself, “I am sure that is wise.", "도로시아가 기운을 내며 말했다. “그게 현명한 말씀이라는 걸 잘 알겠어요."], ["There are so many things which I ought to attend to.", "제가 처리해야 할 일이 너무 많아요."], ["Why should I sit here idle?”", "어째서 제가 여기 빈둥거리며 앉아 있어야 하나요?”"], ["Then, with an effort to recall subjects not connected with her agitation, she added, abruptly, “You know every one in Middlemarch, I think, Mr.", "그러고 나서 자신의 동요와 무관한 화제를 떠올리려 애쓰며 그녀는 불쑥 덧붙였다. “리드게이트 씨, 당신은 미들마치 사람들을 모두 아실 것 같은데요."], ["Lydgate. I shall ask you to tell me a great deal.", "제가 여쭤볼 게 많을 거예요."], ["I have serious things to do now.", "이제 저는 진지하게 해야 할 일들이 있어요."], ["I have a living to give away.", "제게 맡겨야 할 성직 자리가 하나 있거든요."], ["You know Mr. Tyke and all the—”", "타이크 씨도 아실 테고, 또 그 모든 —”"], ["But Dorothea’s effort was too much for her; she broke off and burst into sobs.", "하지만 도로시아의 노력은 그녀에게 너무 벅찼다. 그녀는 말을 멈추고 흐느껴 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Lydgate made her drink a dose of sal volatile.", "리드게이트는 그녀에게 각성제 한 모금을 마시게 했다."]], [["“Let Mrs. Casaubon do as she likes,”", "“캐소본 부인이 하고 싶은 대로 하시게 두십시오.”"], ["he said to Sir James, whom he asked to see before quitting the house.", "그는 집을 나서기 전에 만나기를 청했던 제임스 경에게 말했다."], ["“She wants perfect freedom, I think, more than any other prescription.”", "“제 생각에 그분께는 다른 어떤 처방보다도 완전한 자유가 필요합니다.”"]], [["His attendance on Dorothea while her brain was excited, had enabled him to form some true conclusions concerning the trials of her life.", "그녀의 머릿속이 흥분 상태에 있는 동안 그녀를 돌본 덕분에, 그는 그녀의 삶의 시련에 관해 몇 가지 참된 결론을 내릴 수 있었다."], ["He felt sure that she had been suffering from the strain and conflict of self-repression; and that she was likely now to feel herself only in another sort of pinfold than that from which she had been released.", "그는 그녀가 자기 억제의 긴장과 갈등으로 고통받아 왔으며, 이제는 자신이 풀려나온 우리와는 또 다른 종류의 우리 안에 갇혀 있을 뿐이라고 느끼게 될 공산이 크다고 확신했다."]], [["Lydgate’s advice was all the easier for Sir James to follow when he found that Celia had already told Dorothea the unpleasant fact about the will.", "실리아가 이미 도로시아에게 유언장에 관한 불쾌한 사실을 말해 버렸음을 알게 되자, 리드게이트의 충고는 제임스 경이 따르기에 한결 수월해졌다."], ["There was no help for it now—no reason for any further delay in the execution of necessary business.", "이제는 어쩔 도리가 없었고 — 필요한 일을 처리하는 것을 더 미룰 이유도 없었다."], ["And the next day Sir James complied at once with her request that he would drive her to Lowick.", "그래서 이튿날 제임스 경은 자신을 로윅까지 태워다 달라는 그녀의 청에 즉시 응했다."]], [["“I have no wish to stay there at present,”", "“지금 당장은 거기 머무르고 싶지 않아요.”"], ["said Dorothea; “I could hardly bear it.", "도로시아가 말했다. “도저히 견디기 힘들 것 같아요."], ["I am much happier at Freshitt with Celia.", "저는 실리아와 함께 프레시트에 있는 게 훨씬 행복해요."], ["I shall be able to think better about what should be done at Lowick by looking at it from a distance.", "멀찍이서 바라보는 편이 로윅에서 무엇을 해야 할지 더 잘 생각할 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["And I should like to be at the Grange a little while with my uncle, and go about in all the old walks and among the people in the village.”", "그리고 그레인지에서 삼촌과 함께 얼마간 지내면서, 예전에 거닐던 길들과 마을 사람들 사이를 두루 돌아다니고 싶기도 해요.”"]], [["“Not yet, I think.", "“아직은 아닌 것 같습니다."], ["Your uncle is having political company, and you are better out of the way of such doings,”", "삼촌께서 정치적인 손님들을 맞이하고 계시니, 그런 일들에서 비켜나 계시는 편이 낫습니다.”"], ["said Sir James, who at that moment thought of the Grange chiefly as a haunt of young Ladislaw’s.", "제임스 경이 말했으니, 그 순간 그는 그레인지를 주로 젊은 래디슬로가 드나드는 곳으로 떠올리고 있었다."], ["But no word passed between him and Dorothea about the objectionable part of the will; indeed, both of them felt that the mention of it between them would be impossible.", "하지만 유언장의 거슬리는 부분에 대해서는 그와 도로시아 사이에 단 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 실로 두 사람 모두 그것을 서로 입에 올리는 일은 불가능하다고 느꼈다."], ["Sir James was shy, even with men, about disagreeable subjects; and the one thing that Dorothea would have chosen to say, if she had spoken on the matter at all, was forbidden to her at present because it seemed to be a further exposure of her husband’s injustice.", "제임스 경은 남자들과 있을 때조차 불쾌한 화제에 대해서는 수줍어했고, 도로시아가 만약 그 일을 두고 입을 연다면 가장 하고 싶었을 그 한마디는, 지금으로서는 남편의 부당함을 한층 더 드러내는 일처럼 여겨졌기에 그녀에게 금지되어 있었다. 그렇지만 그녀는 윌 래디슬로의 재산에 대한 도의적 권리를 두고 자신과 남편 사이에 오갔던 일을 제임스 경이 알아주기를 바라기는 했다."], ["Yet she did wish that Sir James could know what had passed between her and her husband about Will Ladislaw’s moral claim on the property: it would then, she thought, be apparent to him as it was to her, that her husband’s strange indelicate proviso had been chiefly urged by his bitter resistance to that idea of claim, and not merely by personal feelings more difficult to talk about.", "그리하면, 자신에게 그러하듯 그에게도 분명해지리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 즉, 남편의 그 기이하고 무례한 단서 조항은 주로 그 권리라는 생각에 대한 남편의 쓰라린 저항에서 비롯된 것이지, 단지 말로 꺼내기 더 어려운 사사로운 감정 때문만은 아니라는 것이."], ["Also, it must be admitted, Dorothea wished that this could be known for Will’s sake, since her friends seemed to think of him as simply an object of Mr.", "또한, 인정하건대, 도로시아는 윌을 위해서도 이 사실이 알려지기를 바랐으니, 친구들이 그를 그저 캐소본 씨의 자선의 대상쯤으로만 여기는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["Casaubon’s charity. Why should he be compared with an Italian carrying white mice?", "어째서 그가 흰쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람에 견주어져야 한단 말인가?"], ["That word quoted from Mrs. Cadwallader seemed like a mocking travesty wrought in the dark by an impish finger.", "캐드월래더 부인에게서 인용된 그 말은, 마치 어둠 속에서 장난꾸러기 손가락이 빚어낸 조롱 섞인 우스꽝스러운 모사처럼 느껴졌다."]], [["At Lowick Dorothea searched desk and drawer—searched all her husband’s places of deposit for private writing, but found no paper addressed especially to her, except that “Synoptical Tabulation,”", "로윅에서 도로시아는 책상과 서랍을 뒤졌다 — 남편이 사적인 글을 보관해 두던 모든 곳을 뒤졌지만, 자신에게 특별히 부친 종이라곤 그 “개요표” 말고는 찾지 못했는데, 그것은 아마도 그녀를 인도하기 위해 의도된 수많은 지시의 시작에 불과했을 것이다."], ["which was probably only the beginning of many intended directions for her guidance. In carrying out this bequest of labor to Dorothea, as in all else, Mr.", "도로시아에게 노고를 유산으로 물려주는 이 일에서도, 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로, 캐소본 씨는 더디고 머뭇거렸으니, 자기 저작을 실행할 때 그랬듯이 그것을 물려주는 계획에서도 어둑하고 발목을 붙드는 매질 속을 무겁게 헤쳐 나가는 듯한 느낌에 짓눌렸던 것이다."], ["Casaubon had been slow and hesitating, oppressed in the plan of transmitting his work, as he had been in executing it, by the sense of moving heavily in a dim and clogging medium: distrust of Dorothea’s competence to arrange what he had prepared was subdued only by distrust of any other redactor.", "자기가 준비해 둔 것을 정리할 도로시아의 능력에 대한 불신은, 다른 어떤 편집자에 대한 불신에 의해서만 겨우 가라앉았다. 하지만 그는 마침내 도로시아의 천성에서 스스로 의지할 신뢰를 빚어내기에 이르렀으니, 그녀라면 자기가 하기로 결심한 일은 해낼 수 있으리라는 것이었다."], ["But he had come at last to create a trust for himself out of Dorothea’s nature: she could do what she resolved to do: and he willingly imagined her toiling under the fetters of a promise to erect a tomb with his name upon it.", "그리고 그는 그녀가 자기 이름이 새겨진 무덤을 세우겠다는 약속의 족쇄에 묶인 채 고되게 일하는 모습을 기꺼이 상상했다. (캐소본 씨가 그 앞으로 나올 권들을 무덤이라 불렀다는 말은 아니다."], ["(Not that Mr. Casaubon called the future volumes a tomb; he called them the Key to all Mythologies.) But the months gained on him and left his plans belated: he had only had time to ask for that promise by which he sought to keep his cold grasp on Dorothea’s life.", "그는 그것들을 모든 신화의 열쇠라 불렀다.) 하지만 세월은 그를 앞질러 가며 그의 계획을 뒤처지게 만들었으니, 그는 도로시아의 삶을 자신의 차가운 손아귀에 붙들어 두려고 그 약속만을 청할 시간밖에 없었던 것이다."]], [["The grasp had slipped away.", "그 손아귀는 미끄러져 빠져나갔다."], ["Bound by a pledge given from the depths of her pity, she would have been capable of undertaking a toil which her judgment whispered was vain for all uses except that consecration of faithfulness which is a supreme use.", "연민의 밑바닥에서 우러나온 맹세에 묶였더라면, 그녀는 자신의 판단이 충실함의 봉헌이라는 — 그 자체로 지고한 쓸모인 — 그것 말고는 어떤 쓸모도 없는 헛된 일이라고 속삭이는 그 노고를 떠맡을 수도 있었으리라."], ["But now her judgment, instead of being controlled by duteous devotion, was made active by the imbittering discovery that in her past union there had lurked the hidden alienation of secrecy and suspicion.", "하지만 이제 그녀의 판단은, 의무에 찬 헌신에 통제되는 대신, 지난날의 결합 속에 비밀과 의심이라는 숨겨진 소외가 도사리고 있었다는 쓰라린 발견에 의해 활동을 시작했다."], ["The living, suffering man was no longer before her to awaken her pity: there remained only the retrospect of painful subjection to a husband whose thoughts had been lower than she had believed, whose exorbitant claims for himself had even blinded his scrupulous care for his own character, and made him defeat his own pride by shocking men of ordinary honor.", "살아서 고통받는 남자는 더 이상 그녀 앞에 있지 않아 연민을 일깨우지 못했다. 남은 것은 오직, 그녀가 믿었던 것보다 생각이 더 낮았던, 자신을 위한 터무니없는 권리 주장이 자기 인격에 대한 그 세심한 배려마저 눈멀게 하여, 평범한 명예심을 지닌 사람들에게 충격을 줌으로써 스스로 자기 자존심을 꺾어 버리게 만든 한 남편에게 고통스럽게 복종했던 일에 대한 회고뿐이었다."], ["As for the property which was the sign of that broken tie, she would have been glad to be free from it and have nothing more than her original fortune which had been settled on her, if there had not been duties attached to ownership, which she ought not to flinch from.", "그 끊어진 인연의 표징인 재산으로 말하자면, 소유에 따르는 의무들만 없었다면 — 그녀가 마땅히 외면해서는 안 되는 그 의무들만 없었다면 — 그녀는 기꺼이 그것에서 벗어나, 자신에게 본래 정해져 있던 원래의 재산 말고는 아무것도 갖지 않기를 바랐을 것이다. 이 재산을 둘러싸고 골치 아픈 의문들이 한사코 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["About this property many troublous questions insisted on rising: had she not been right in thinking that the half of it ought to go to Will Ladislaw?—but was it not impossible now for her to do that act of justice?", "그것의 절반은 윌 래디슬로에게 돌아가야 마땅하다고 생각한 것이 옳지 않았던가? — 하지만 이제 그녀가 그 정의로운 행동을 하기란 불가능해지지 않았던가?"], ["Mr. Casaubon had taken a cruelly effective means of hindering her: even with indignation against him in her heart, any act that seemed a triumphant eluding of his purpose revolted her.", "캐소본 씨는 그녀를 막을 잔인하리만치 효과적인 수단을 취해 놓았으니, 그를 향한 분노를 가슴에 품고 있으면서도, 그의 의도를 보란 듯이 따돌리는 것처럼 보이는 어떤 행동도 그녀에게는 역겹게 느껴졌다."]], [["After collecting papers of business which she wished to examine, she locked up again the desks and drawers—all empty of personal words for her—empty of any sign that in her husband’s lonely brooding his heart had gone out to her in excuse or explanation; and she went back to Freshitt with the sense that around his last hard demand and his last injurious assertion of his power, the silence was unbroken.", "살펴보고 싶은 사무 관련 서류들을 챙긴 뒤, 그녀는 다시 책상과 서랍을 잠갔다 — 그녀를 위한 사사로운 말이라곤 한 마디도 없이 텅 빈 채로 — 남편이 홀로 골똘히 생각에 잠겼을 때 그의 마음이 변명이나 해명으로 그녀에게 향한 적이 있었다는 어떤 흔적도 없이 텅 빈 채로. 그리고 그녀는 그의 마지막 모진 요구와 자기 권력에 대한 그의 마지막 해로운 주장 둘레에 침묵이 깨지지 않은 채 남아 있다는 느낌을 안고 프레시트로 돌아갔다."]], [["Dorothea tried now to turn her thoughts towards immediate duties, and one of these was of a kind which others were determined to remind her of.", "이제 도로시아는 당장의 의무들로 생각을 돌리려 애썼고, 그중 하나는 다른 이들이 기어이 그녀에게 일깨워 주려 작정한 종류의 것이었다."], ["Lydgate’s ear had caught eagerly her mention of the living, and as soon as he could, he reopened the subject, seeing here a possibility of making amends for the casting-vote he had once given with an ill-satisfied conscience.", "리드게이트의 귀는 그녀가 성직 자리를 언급한 것을 놓치지 않고 열심히 붙들었으며, 그는 되도록 빨리 그 화제를 다시 꺼냈으니, 여기서 한때 만족스럽지 못한 양심으로 던졌던 그 결정표를 만회할 가능성을 보았던 것이다."], ["“Instead of telling you anything about Mr.", "“타이크 씨에 대해 무언가 말씀드리는 대신,”"], ["Tyke,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “I should like to speak of another man—Mr. Farebrother, the Vicar of St.", "“저는 다른 사람에 대해 이야기하고 싶습니다 — 세인트 보톨프 교회의 교구 목사인 페어브라더 씨 말입니다."], ["Botolph’s. His living is a poor one, and gives him a stinted provision for himself and his family.", "그의 성직록은 보잘것없어서, 자신과 가족을 위한 옹색한 생계만을 마련해 줍니다."], ["His mother, aunt, and sister all live with him, and depend upon him.", "그의 어머니와 이모, 누이가 모두 그와 함께 살며 그에게 기대고 있지요."], ["I believe he has never married because of them.", "제 생각엔 그들 때문에 그가 결혼을 못 한 것 같습니다."], ["I never heard such good preaching as his—such plain, easy eloquence.", "저는 그분의 설교만큼 훌륭한 설교를 들어 본 적이 없습니다 — 그토록 소박하고 거침없는 웅변을요."], ["He would have done to preach at St. Paul’s Cross after old Latimer.", "그분이라면 옛 래티머의 뒤를 이어 세인트 폴 십자가에서 설교했어도 손색이 없었을 겁니다."], ["His talk is just as good about all subjects: original, simple, clear.", "그의 이야기는 어떤 주제에 대해서든 꼭 그만큼 훌륭합니다 — 독창적이고, 단순하고, 명료하지요."], ["I think him a remarkable fellow: he ought to have done more than he has done.”", "저는 그를 비범한 사람이라고 생각합니다. 그는 지금까지 해 온 것보다 더 많은 일을 했어야 마땅한 사람입니다.”"]], [["“Why has he not done more?”", "“어째서 그분이 더 많은 일을 하지 못했나요?”"], ["said Dorothea, interested now in all who had slipped below their own intention.", "도로시아가 물었으니, 이제 그녀는 자신이 본래 품었던 뜻 아래로 미끄러져 내린 모든 이에게 관심이 있었다."]], [["“That’s a hard question,”", "“그건 어려운 질문입니다.”"], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“I find myself that it’s uncommonly difficult to make the right thing work: there are so many strings pulling at once.", "“저 자신도 옳은 일을 제대로 풀어 나가기란 유난히 어렵다는 걸 깨닫고 있습니다. 한꺼번에 잡아당기는 끈이 너무 많거든요."], ["Farebrother often hints that he has got into the wrong profession; he wants a wider range than that of a poor clergyman, and I suppose he has no interest to help him on.", "페어브라더는 자기가 잘못된 직업을 택했다고 종종 넌지시 말합니다. 그는 가난한 성직자의 범위보다 더 넓은 영역을 원하는데, 짐작건대 그를 밀어 줄 연줄이 없는 것 같습니다."], ["He is very fond of Natural History and various scientific matters, and he is hampered in reconciling these tastes with his position.", "그는 박물학과 여러 과학적 사안을 무척 좋아하는데, 이런 취향을 자기 처지와 조화시키는 데 애를 먹습니다."], ["He has no money to spare—hardly enough to use; and that has led him into card-playing—Middlemarch is a great place for whist.", "그에게는 여유 자금이 없습니다 — 쓸 돈조차 빠듯하지요. 그리고 그것이 그를 카드놀이로 이끌었습니다 — 미들마치는 휘스트가 성행하는 곳이거든요."], ["He does play for money, and he wins a good deal.", "그는 정말로 돈을 걸고 놀고, 꽤 많이 땁니다."], ["Of course that takes him into company a little beneath him, and makes him slack about some things; and yet, with all that, looking at him as a whole, I think he is one of the most blameless men I ever knew.", "물론 그 때문에 자기보다 좀 못한 사람들과 어울리게 되고, 어떤 일들에는 소홀해지지요. 그럼에도 그 모든 것을 다 감안해 그를 전체로서 바라보면, 저는 그가 제가 알던 가장 흠 없는 사람들 중 하나라고 생각합니다."], ["He has neither venom nor doubleness in him, and those often go with a more correct outside.”", "그에게는 독기도 이중성도 없는데, 그런 것들은 흔히 더 반듯한 겉모습과 함께 가곤 하지요.”"]], [["“I wonder whether he suffers in his conscience because of that habit,”", "“그분이 그 습관 때문에 양심의 가책을 느끼는지 궁금하네요.”"], ["said Dorothea; “I wonder whether he wishes he could leave it off.”", "도로시아가 말했다. “그것을 그만두기를 바라는지 궁금해요.”"]], [["“I have no doubt he would leave it off, if he were transplanted into plenty: he would be glad of the time for other things.”", "“풍족한 처지로 옮겨진다면 분명 그만둘 겁니다. 다른 일에 쓸 시간이 생겨서 기뻐하겠지요.”"]], [["“My uncle says that Mr. Tyke is spoken of as an apostolic man,”", "“삼촌은 타이크 씨가 사도 같은 사람으로 일컬어진다고 하셨어요.”"], ["said Dorothea, meditatively.", "도로시아가 생각에 잠겨 말했다."], ["She was wishing it were possible to restore the times of primitive zeal, and yet thinking of Mr. Farebrother with a strong desire to rescue him from his chance-gotten money.", "그녀는 원시적인 열정의 시대를 되살릴 수 있기를 바라면서도, 한편으로는 우연히 얻은 돈에서 페어브라더 씨를 구해 내고픈 강한 바람으로 그를 떠올리고 있었다."]], [["“I don’t pretend to say that Farebrother is apostolic,”", "“페어브라더가 사도 같은 사람이라고는 감히 말하지 않겠습니다.”"], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“His position is not quite like that of the Apostles: he is only a parson among parishioners whose lives he has to try and make better.", "“그의 처지는 사도들의 처지와는 사뭇 다르니까요. 그는 그저 자신이 그 삶을 더 낫게 만들어 보려 애써야 하는 교구민들 가운데 있는 한 명의 목사일 뿐입니다."], ["Practically I find that what is called being apostolic now, is an impatience of everything in which the parson doesn’t cut the principal figure.", "실제로 제가 보기에 요즘 사도 같다고 일컬어지는 것은, 목사가 주역으로 나서지 않는 모든 일에 대한 조바심에 지나지 않습니다."], ["I see something of that in Mr.", "저는 병원에서 타이크 씨에게서 그런 모습을 좀 봅니다."], ["Tyke at the Hospital: a good deal of his doctrine is a sort of pinching hard to make people uncomfortably aware of him.", "그의 교리 가운데 상당 부분은 사람들로 하여금 거북하게나마 자신을 의식하도록 몰아붙이는 일종의 옥죄기 같은 것이지요."], ["Besides, an apostolic man at Lowick!—he ought to think, as St.", "게다가 로윅에 사도 같은 사람이라니!"], ["Francis did, that it is needful to preach to the birds.”", "— 그라면 성 프란체스코가 그랬듯이, 새들에게 설교해야 한다고 생각해야 할 겁니다.”"]], [["“True,” said Dorothea.", "“맞는 말씀이에요.”"], ["“It is hard to imagine what sort of notions our farmers and laborers get from their teaching.", "도로시아가 말했다. “우리 농부들과 일꾼들이 그런 가르침에서 어떤 관념을 얻게 될지 상상하기 어렵네요."], ["I have been looking into a volume of sermons by Mr.", "저는 타이크 씨의 설교집 한 권을 들여다보고 있었어요."], ["Tyke: such sermons would be of no use at Lowick—I mean, about imputed righteousness and the prophecies in the Apocalypse.", "그런 설교는 로윅에서는 아무 쓸모가 없을 거예요 — 그러니까 전가된 의나 요한 묵시록의 예언 같은 것 말이에요."], ["I have always been thinking of the different ways in which Christianity is taught, and whenever I find one way that makes it a wider blessing than any other, I cling to that as the truest—I mean that which takes in the most good of all kinds, and brings in the most people as sharers in it.", "저는 늘 기독교가 가르쳐지는 여러 방식에 대해 생각해 왔는데, 어떤 한 방식이 다른 어떤 방식보다 더 넓은 축복이 되는 것을 발견할 때면, 저는 그것을 가장 참된 것으로 붙듭니다 — 그러니까 온갖 종류의 선을 가장 많이 품어 안고, 가장 많은 사람을 그 나눔에 끌어들이는 방식 말이에요."], ["It is surely better to pardon too much, than to condemn too much.", "너무 많이 단죄하기보다는 너무 많이 용서하는 편이 분명 더 낫지요."], ["But I should like to see Mr. Farebrother and hear him preach.”", "하지만 저는 페어브라더 씨를 만나고 그분의 설교를 들어 보고 싶어요.”"]], [["“Do,” said Lydgate; “I trust to the effect of that.", "“그러십시오.”"], ["He is very much beloved, but he has his enemies too: there are always people who can’t forgive an able man for differing from them.", "리드게이트가 말했다. “저는 그 효과를 믿습니다."], ["And that money-winning business is really a blot.", "그는 많은 사랑을 받지만, 그에게도 적이 있긴 합니다."], ["You don’t, of course, see many Middlemarch people: but Mr.", "유능한 사람이 자기와 의견을 달리하는 것을 용서하지 못하는 사람들은 언제나 있는 법이니까요."], ["Ladislaw, who is constantly seeing Mr.", "그리고 그 돈 따는 일은 정말이지 하나의 오점이지요."], ["Brooke, is a great friend of Mr. Farebrother’s old ladies, and would be glad to sing the Vicar’s praises.", "물론 부인께서는 미들마치 사람들을 많이 만나지 않으시겠지만, 브룩 씨를 늘 만나는 래디슬로 씨는 페어브라더 씨네 노부인들과 절친한 사이여서, 그 목사를 기꺼이 칭송할 겁니다."], ["One of the old ladies—Miss Noble, the aunt—is a wonderfully quaint picture of self-forgetful goodness, and Ladislaw gallants her about sometimes.", "노부인들 중 한 분 — 노블 양이라는 이모 되시는 분은 — 자기를 잊은 선량함의 놀랍도록 진기한 한 폭의 그림 같은데, 래디슬로가 가끔 그분을 모시고 다니지요. 어느 날 뒷골목에서 그들을 마주친 적이 있습니다."], ["I met them one day in a back street: you know Ladislaw’s look—a sort of Daphnis in coat and waistcoat; and this little old maid reaching up to his arm—they looked like a couple dropped out of a romantic comedy.", "부인께서도 래디슬로의 모습을 아시잖습니까 — 코트와 조끼를 걸친 일종의 다프니스 같은 모습 말입니다. 그리고 이 자그마한 노처녀가 그의 팔에 닿으려 손을 뻗고 있었는데 — 두 사람은 마치 낭만 희극에서 빠져나온 한 쌍처럼 보였습니다."], ["But the best evidence about Farebrother is to see him and hear him.”", "하지만 페어브라더에 관한 가장 좋은 증거는 그를 만나 그의 말을 들어 보는 것이지요.”"]], [["Happily Dorothea was in her private sitting-room when this conversation occurred, and there was no one present to make Lydgate’s innocent introduction of Ladislaw painful to her.", "다행히도 이 대화가 오갈 때 도로시아는 자신의 사적인 거실에 있었고, 리드게이트가 별 뜻 없이 래디슬로를 입에 올린 것이 그녀를 괴롭게 하는 모습을 지켜볼 사람이 곁에 아무도 없었다."], ["As was usual with him in matters of personal gossip, Lydgate had quite forgotten Rosamond’s remark that she thought Will adored Mrs. Casaubon.", "사사로운 잡담에 관한 한 늘 그러하듯, 리드게이트는 윌이 캐소본 부인을 흠모하는 것 같다던 로저먼드의 말을 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["At that moment he was only caring for what would recommend the Farebrother family; and he had purposely given emphasis to the worst that could be said about the Vicar, in order to forestall objections. In the weeks since Mr.", "그 순간 그는 오로지 페어브라더 가족을 천거하는 데에만 마음을 쓰고 있었고, 미리 반대를 막아 두려고 그 목사에 대해 할 수 있는 가장 나쁜 말을 일부러 강조했던 것이다."], ["Casaubon’s death he had hardly seen Ladislaw, and he had heard no rumor to warn him that Mr. Brooke’s confidential secretary was a dangerous subject with Mrs.", "캐소본 씨가 죽은 뒤 몇 주 동안 그는 래디슬로를 거의 만나지 못했고, 브룩 씨의 비밀 비서가 캐소본 부인에게 위험한 화제라는 것을 경고해 줄 어떤 소문도 듣지 못했다."], ["Casaubon. When he was gone, his picture of Ladislaw lingered in her mind and disputed the ground with that question of the Lowick living.", "그가 떠난 뒤, 래디슬로에 대한 그의 묘사는 그녀의 마음속에 오래 머무르며 로윅 성직 문제와 그 자리를 다투었다."], ["What was Will Ladislaw thinking about her?", "윌 래디슬로는 자신에 대해 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까?"], ["Would he hear of that fact which made her cheeks burn as they never used to do?", "예전에는 결코 그러지 않던 식으로 자신의 두 뺨을 화끈거리게 만든 그 사실을 그가 듣게 될까?"], ["And how would he feel when he heard it?—But she could see as well as possible how he smiled down at the little old maid.", "그리고 그것을 들으면 그는 어떤 기분이 들까? — 하지만 그녀는 그가 그 자그마한 노처녀를 내려다보며 미소 짓는 모습을 더없이 또렷하게 그려 볼 수 있었다."], ["An Italian with white mice!—on the contrary, he was a creature who entered into every one’s feelings, and could take the pressure of their thought instead of urging his own with iron resistance.", "흰쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람이라니! — 오히려 그는 모든 사람의 감정 속으로 들어가, 무쇠 같은 저항으로 자기 생각을 밀어붙이는 대신 다른 이들의 생각의 무게를 함께 받아 줄 수 있는 사람이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LI.", "# CHAPTER LI."]], [["Party is Nature too, and you shall see By force of Logic how they both agree: The Many in the One, the One in Many; All is not Some, nor Some the same as Any: Genus holds species, both are great or small; One genus highest, one not high at all; Each species has its differentia too, This is not That, and He was never You, Though this and that are AYES, and you and he Are like as one to one, or three to three.", "당파도 자연의 일부이니, 그대는 보리라\n논리의 힘으로 둘이 어떻게 일치하는지를."]], [["No gossip about Mr. Casaubon’s will had yet reached Ladislaw: the air seemed to be filled with the dissolution of Parliament and the coming election, as the old wakes and fairs were filled with the rival clatter of itinerant shows; and more private noises were taken little notice of. The famous “dry election” was at hand, in which the depths of public feeling might be measured by the low flood-mark of drink. Will Ladislaw was one of the busiest at this time; and though Dorothea’s widowhood was continually in his thought, he was so far from wishing to be spoken to on the subject, that when Lydgate sought him out to tell him what had passed about the Lowick living, he answered rather waspishly—", "다수가 하나 안에, 하나가 다수 안에 있고,\n전체는 일부가 아니며, 일부는 임의의 것과 같지 않다."]], [["“Why should you bring me into the matter? I never see Mrs. Casaubon, and am not likely to see her, since she is at Freshitt. I never go there. It is Tory ground, where I and the ‘Pioneer’ are no more welcome than a poacher and his gun.”", "유는 종을 거느리니, 둘 다 크거나 작고,\n한 유는 가장 높되, 한 유는 전혀 높지 않다."]], [["The fact was that Will had been made the more susceptible by observing that Mr. Brooke, instead of wishing him, as before, to come to the Grange oftener than was quite agreeable to himself, seemed now to contrive that he should go there as little as possible. This was a shuffling concession of Mr. Brooke’s to Sir James Chettam’s indignant remonstrance; and Will, awake to the slightest hint in this direction, concluded that he was to be kept away from the Grange on Dorothea’s account. Her friends, then, regarded him with some suspicion? Their fears were quite superfluous: they were very much mistaken if they imagined that he would put himself forward as a needy adventurer trying to win the favor of a rich woman.", "각 종에는 또한 그 종차가 있으니,\n이것은 저것이 아니고, 그는 결코 그대가 아니었다."]], [["Until now Will had never fully seen the chasm between himself and Dorothea—until now that he was come to the brink of it, and saw her on the other side. He began, not without some inward rage, to think of going away from the neighborhood: it would be impossible for him to show any further interest in Dorothea without subjecting himself to disagreeable imputations—perhaps even in her mind, which others might try to poison.", "비록 이것과 저것이 모두 긍정이고, 그대와 그가\n하나 대 하나처럼, 혹은 셋 대 셋처럼 닮았을지라도."]], [["“We are forever divided,” said Will. “I might as well be at Rome; she would be no farther from me.” But what we call our despair is often only the painful eagerness of unfed hope. There were plenty of reasons why he should not go—public reasons why he should not quit his post at this crisis, leaving Mr. Brooke in the lurch when he needed “coaching” for the election, and when there was so much canvassing, direct and indirect, to be carried on. Will could not like to leave his own chessmen in the heat of a game; and any candidate on the right side, even if his brain and marrow had been as soft as was consistent with a gentlemanly bearing, might help to turn a majority. To coach Mr. Brooke and keep him steadily to the idea that he must pledge himself to vote for the actual Reform Bill, instead of insisting on his independence and power of pulling up in time, was not an easy task. Mr. Farebrother’s prophecy of a fourth candidate “in the bag” had not yet been fulfilled, neither the Parliamentary Candidate Society nor any other power on the watch to secure a reforming majority seeing a worthy nodus for interference while there was a second reforming candidate like Mr. Brooke, who might be returned at his own expense; and the fight lay entirely between Pinkerton the old Tory member, Bagster the new Whig member returned at the last election, and Brooke the future independent member, who was to fetter himself for this occasion only. Mr. Hawley and his party would bend all their forces to the return of Pinkerton, and Mr. Brooke’s success must depend either on plumpers which would leave Bagster in the rear, or on the new minting of Tory votes into reforming votes. The latter means, of course, would be preferable.", "캐소본 씨의 유언에 관한 소문은 아직 래디슬로의 귀에 들어오지 않았다. 옛날 마을 축제와 장터가 떠돌이 공연단들이 다투어 떠드는 소리로 가득 찼듯이, 이제 대기는 의회 해산과 다가오는 선거로 가득 찬 듯했다. 그래서 좀 더 사사로운 소음들은 거의 주목을 받지 못했다. 그 유명한 \"건조한 선거\"가 코앞에 닥쳐 있었으니, 그 선거에서는 술의 낮은 수위로 대중 감정의 깊이를 가늠할 수 있었다. 윌 래디슬로는 이 무렵 가장 분주한 사람 중 하나였다. 그리고 도러시아의 과부살이가 끊임없이 그의 머릿속을 맴돌았지만, 그 일에 관해 누군가가 말을 거는 것을 너무도 원치 않았던 터라, 리드게이트가 그를 찾아와 로윅 성직록에 관해 있었던 일을 전하려 하자 그는 다소 쏘아붙이듯 대답했다."]], [["This prospect of converting votes was a dangerous distraction to Mr. Brooke: his impression that waverers were likely to be allured by wavering statements, and also the liability of his mind to stick afresh at opposing arguments as they turned up in his memory, gave Will Ladislaw much trouble.", "\"왜 나를 그 일에 끌어들이는 겁니까? 나는 캐소본 부인을 본 적도 없고, 앞으로 볼 일도 없을 것 같습니다. 그분은 프레시트에 계시니까요. 나는 거기에 가지 않습니다. 거기는 토리당의 땅이라, 나와 '파이어니어'는 밀렵꾼과 그 총만큼이나 환영받지 못하니까요.\""]], [["“You know there are tactics in these things,” said Mr.", "사실 윌은 브룩 씨가 예전처럼 자신에게 그가 편히 여기는 것 이상으로 그레인지에 자주 와 달라고 청하기는커녕, 이제는 가능한 한 그가 그곳에 적게 가도록 궁리하는 듯한 것을 알아채고 더욱 민감해진 상태였다."], ["Brooke; “meeting people half-way—tempering your ideas—saying, ‘Well now, there’s something in that,’ and so on.", "이는 제임스 체텀 경의 분노에 찬 항의에 대한 브룩 씨의 어정쩡한 양보였다."], ["I agree with you that this is a peculiar occasion—the country with a will of its own—political unions—that sort of thing—but we sometimes cut with rather too sharp a knife, Ladislaw.", "그리고 이런 방향의 아주 미세한 기미에도 깨어 있던 윌은, 자신이 도러시아 때문에 그레인지에서 멀어지게 되었다고 결론지었다. 그렇다면 그녀의 친지들이 자신을 다소 의심의 눈으로 본다는 말인가?"], ["These ten-pound householders, now: why ten?", "그들의 두려움은 전혀 쓸데없는 것이었다."], ["Draw the line somewhere—yes: but why just at ten? That’s a difficult question, now, if you go into it.”", "그가 부유한 여인의 호의를 얻으려는 궁색한 모험가로 나서리라고 상상한다면, 그들은 크게 잘못 짚은 것이었다."]], [["“Of course it is,” said Will, impatiently.", "지금까지 윌은 자신과 도러시아 사이에 가로놓인 심연을 온전히 본 적이 없었다."], ["“But if you are to wait till we get a logical Bill, you must put yourself forward as a revolutionist, and then Middlemarch would not elect you, I fancy.", "이제 그 가장자리에 다다라 건너편에 있는 그녀를 보고서야 비로소 보게 된 것이다. 그는 어느 정도 속에서 끓어오르는 분노와 함께 이 근방을 떠날 생각을 하기 시작했다."], ["As for trimming, this is not a time for trimming.”", "불쾌한 누명을 스스로 뒤집어쓰지 않고서는 도러시아에게 더 이상의 관심을 보이는 일이 불가능할 터였다. 어쩌면 다른 이들이 독을 풀려 애쓸 그녀의 마음속에서조차 그러할지 몰랐다."]], [["Mr. Brooke always ended by agreeing with Ladislaw, who still appeared to him a sort of Burke with a leaven of Shelley; but after an interval the wisdom of his own methods reasserted itself, and he was again drawn into using them with much hopefulness.", "\"우리는 영원히 갈라져 있다.\""], ["At this stage of affairs he was in excellent spirits, which even supported him under large advances of money; for his powers of convincing and persuading had not yet been tested by anything more difficult than a chairman’s speech introducing other orators, or a dialogue with a Middlemarch voter, from which he came away with a sense that he was a tactician by nature, and that it was a pity he had not gone earlier into this kind of thing.", "윌은 말했다. \"차라리 로마에 가 있어도 마찬가지일 것이다."], ["He was a little conscious of defeat, however, with Mr.", "그래도 그녀가 지금보다 더 멀어지지는 않을 테니.\" 그러나 우리가 절망이라 부르는 것은 흔히 먹이를 얻지 못한 희망의 고통스러운 갈망에 지나지 않는다."], ["Mawmsey, a chief representative in Middlemarch of that great social power, the retail trader, and naturally one of the most doubtful voters in the borough—willing for his own part to supply an equal quality of teas and sugars to reformer and anti-reformer, as well as to agree impartially with both, and feeling like the burgesses of old that this necessity of electing members was a great burthen to a town; for even if there were no danger in holding out hopes to all parties beforehand, there would be the painful necessity at last of disappointing respectable people whose names were on his books.", "그가 떠나서는 안 될 이유는 충분했다. 이 위기의 순간에 자기 자리를 떠나서는 안 될 공적인 이유들이 있었으니, 브룩 씨가 선거를 위해 \"지도\"를 받아야 하고 직간접적인 유세를 그토록 많이 벌여야 하는 마당에 그를 곤경에 빠뜨려 둘 수는 없었다."], ["He was accustomed to receive large orders from Mr.", "윌은 한창 열기가 오른 게임판에서 자기 장기 말을 두고 떠나기가 싫었다. 그리고 옳은 편에 선 후보라면, 비록 그 두뇌와 골수가 신사다운 풍모와 양립할 수 있는 한도까지 무르다 하더라도, 과반수를 뒤집는 데 한몫할 수 있었다."], ["Brooke of Tipton; but then, there were many of Pinkerton’s committee whose opinions had a great weight of grocery on their side.", "브룩 씨를 지도하여, 자신의 독립성과 적절한 때 멈출 수 있는 능력을 고집하는 대신 실제 개혁 법안에 찬성표를 던지겠다고 서약해야 한다는 생각을 그가 꾸준히 견지하게 하는 것은 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 페어브러더 씨가 예언한 네 번째 후보가 \"주머니 속에\""], ["Mr. Mawmsey thinking that Mr.", "들어 있다는 말은 아직 실현되지 않았다."], ["Brooke, as not too “clever in his intellects,”", "의회 후보 협회도, 개혁파 과반수를 확보하려고 지켜보는 그 어떤 세력도, 자기 비용으로 출마할 수 있는 브룩 씨 같은 두 번째 개혁파 후보가 있는 한, 개입할 만한 가치 있는 매듭을 보지 못했기 때문이다. 그리하여 싸움은 전적으로 옛 토리당 의원인 핑커턴, 지난 선거에서 당선된 새 휘그당 의원 백스터, 그리고 이번 경우에만 자신을 옭아매기로 한 미래의 무소속 의원 브룩, 이 셋 사이에 놓여 있었다."], ["was the more likely to forgive a grocer who gave a hostile vote under pressure, had become confidential in his back parlor.", "홀리 씨와 그 일파는 핑커턴의 당선에 온 힘을 쏟을 터였고, 브룩 씨의 성공은 백스터를 뒤로 처지게 할 단독 투표에 의존하거나, 토리당 표를 개혁파 표로 새로이 주조해 내는 데 달려 있었다. 물론 후자의 방법이 더 바람직할 터였다."]], [["“As to Reform, sir, put it in a family light,”", "이렇게 표를 전향시킬 수 있다는 전망은 브룩 씨에게 위험한 곁눈질거리였다."], ["he said, rattling the small silver in his pocket, and smiling affably.", "흔들리는 자들은 흔들리는 발언에 끌려들기 쉽다는 그의 인상, 그리고 기억 속에서 반대 논거가 떠오를 때마다 그것에 다시 발목 잡히는 그의 정신적 성향이 윌 래디슬로에게 큰 골칫거리였다. \"이런 일에는 전술이라는 게 있는 걸 자네도 알잖나.\""], ["“Will it support Mrs.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["Mawmsey, and enable her to bring up six children when I am no more?", "\"사람들과 절반쯤 만나 주고, 자기 생각을 누그러뜨리고, '음, 그 말에도 일리가 있군' 하는 식으로 말하는 것 말일세."], ["I put the question fictiously, knowing what must be the answer.", "이번이 특별한 경우라는 데는 나도 동의하네. 나라가 제 의지를 지니고 있고, 정치 결사며, 그런 종류의 것들이 있으니 말이야."], ["Very well, sir.", "하지만 우리가 때로는 칼날을 너무 날카롭게 들이대기도 한단 말이지, 래디슬로."], ["I ask you what, as a husband and a father, I am to do when gentlemen come to me and say, ‘Do as you like, Mawmsey; but if you vote against us, I shall get my groceries elsewhere: when I sugar my liquor I like to feel that I am benefiting the country by maintaining tradesmen of the right color.’ Those very words have been spoken to me, sir, in the very chair where you are now sitting.", "이 십 파운드 가구주들 말인데, 왜 하필 십 파운드인가? 어딘가에 선을 긋긴 해야지."], ["I don’t mean by your honorable self, Mr.", "그래. 하지만 왜 꼭 십에서 긋는가?"], ["Brooke.”", "이건 파고들면 어려운 문제란 말일세.\""]], [["“No, no, no—that’s narrow, you know. Until my butler complains to me of your goods, Mr.", "\"물론 그렇지요.\""], ["Mawmsey,” said Mr.", "윌이 조바심을 내며 말했다."], ["Brooke, soothingly, “until I hear that you send bad sugars, spices—that sort of thing—I shall never order him to go elsewhere.”", "\"하지만 논리적으로 완벽한 법안이 나올 때까지 기다리시려면, 스스로 혁명가로 나서셔야 할 테고, 그러면 미들마치는 어르신을 뽑지 않을 겁니다. 그리고 어정쩡한 절충에 관해서라면, 지금은 절충할 때가 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Sir, I am your humble servant, and greatly obliged,” said Mr.", "브룩 씨는 언제나 결국에는 래디슬로의 말에 동의하고 말았으니, 그에게는 여전히 윌이 셸리의 효모가 섞인 일종의 버크처럼 보였던 것이다."], ["Mawmsey, feeling that politics were clearing up a little.", "그러나 얼마간 시간이 지나면 자기 방식의 지혜가 다시 고개를 들었고, 그는 또다시 큰 기대를 품고 그 방식을 쓰는 쪽으로 끌려들었다."], ["“There would be some pleasure in voting for a gentleman who speaks in that honorable manner.”", "일이 이쯤 진행되던 무렵 그는 더없이 좋은 기분이었고, 그 기분은 거액의 자금을 선뜻 내놓는 와중에도 그를 떠받쳐 주었다. 그의 설득력과 설복하는 능력은 아직 다른 연사들을 소개하는 의장의 연설이나 미들마치 유권자와의 대화보다 더 어려운 무엇으로도 시험받은 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["“Well, you know, Mr. Mawmsey, you would find it the right thing to put yourself on our side.", "그런 대화를 마치고 나면 그는 자신이 천성적으로 전술가이며, 이런 종류의 일에 좀 더 일찍 뛰어들지 않은 것이 안타깝다는 느낌을 안고 돌아오곤 했다."], ["This Reform will touch everybody by-and-by—a thoroughly popular measure—a sort of A, B, C, you know, that must come first before the rest can follow.", "그렇지만 그는 마음지 씨에게는 다소 패배를 의식하고 있었다. 마음지 씨는 그 위대한 사회적 권력인 소매상인을 대표하는 미들마치의 유력 인사였고, 당연히 이 자치구에서 가장 향방을 알 수 없는 유권자 중 하나였다."], ["I quite agree with you that you’ve got to look at the thing in a family light: but public spirit, now.", "그는 자기 입장에서는 개혁파에게나 반개혁파에게나 똑같은 품질의 차와 설탕을 기꺼이 공급하려 했고, 또한 양쪽 모두에 공평하게 동의하려 했으며, 옛 자치 시민들처럼 이렇게 의원을 뽑아야 하는 일이 도시에 큰 짐이라고 느꼈다."], ["We’re all one family, you know—it’s all one cupboard.", "미리 모든 당파에게 희망을 품게 하는 데 위험이 없다 하더라도, 결국에는 자기 장부에 이름이 올라 있는 점잖은 사람들을 실망시켜야 하는 괴로운 일이 남을 터였기 때문이다."], ["Such a thing as a vote, now: why, it may help to make men’s fortunes at the Cape—there’s no knowing what may be the effect of a vote,” Mr.", "그는 티프턴의 브룩 씨에게서 큰 주문을 받는 데 익숙했다."], ["Brooke ended, with a sense of being a little out at sea, though finding it still enjoyable.", "하지만 핑커턴의 위원회에도 그 의견에 식료품의 무게가 상당히 실린 사람들이 많았다. 마음지 씨는 브룩 씨가 \"지적으로 그리 영리하지\""], ["But Mr. Mawmsey answered in a tone of decisive check.", "못한 만큼, 압력에 못 이겨 적대적인 표를 던지는 식료품상을 더 너그러이 용서해 줄 사람이라고 여기고는, 자기 뒷방에서 흉금을 털어놓았다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, sir, but I can’t afford that. When I give a vote I must know what I am doing; I must look to what will be the effects on my till and ledger, speaking respectfully. Prices, I’ll admit, are what nobody can know the merits of; and the sudden falls after you’ve bought in currants, which are a goods that will not keep—I’ve never; myself seen into the ins and outs there; which is a rebuke to human pride. But as to one family, there’s debtor and creditor, I hope; they’re not going to reform that away; else I should vote for things staying as they are. Few men have less need to cry for change than I have, personally speaking—that is, for self and family. I am not one of those who have nothing to lose: I mean as to respectability both in parish and private business, and noways in respect of your honorable self and custom, which you was good enough to say you would not withdraw from me, vote or no vote, while the article sent in was satisfactory.”", "\"개혁에 관해서라면 말입니다, 나리, 그걸 가정의 관점에서 봐 주십시오.\" 그는 주머니 속 잔돈을 짤랑거리며 상냥하게 웃으면서 말했다. \"그게 우리 마음지 부인을 부양해 주고, 제가 죽고 난 뒤 그 사람이 여섯 아이를 길러 낼 수 있게 해 줄까요? 답이 무엇일지 뻔히 알면서도 가정삼아 여쭙는 겁니다. 좋습니다, 나리. 그럼 여쭙겠습니다. 남편이자 아버지로서 제가 어떻게 해야겠습니까? 신사분들이 제게 와서 이렇게 말합니다. '마음대로 하시오, 마음지. 하지만 우리에게 반대표를 던진다면 식료품은 다른 데서 사겠소. 술에 설탕을 탈 때면 올바른 색깔의 상인을 떠받쳐 나라에 이바지하고 있다고 느끼고 싶으니까.' 바로 그 말을 들었습니다, 나리, 지금 나리께서 앉아 계신 바로 그 의자에서요. 존경하는 나리께서 그러셨다는 말씀은 아닙니다, 브룩 씨.\" \"아니, 아니, 아닐세. 그건 옹졸한 짓이지, 안 그런가. 내 집사가 자네 물건에 대해 불평할 때까지는 말일세, 마음지 씨.\" 브룩 씨가 달래듯 말했다. \"자네가 나쁜 설탕이며 향신료며 그런 것들을 보낸다는 말을 듣기 전까지는, 나는 결코 그에게 다른 데서 사라고 시키지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["After this conversation Mr.", "\"나리, 저는 나리의 변변찮은 종이며, 깊이 감사드립니다.\""], ["Mawmsey went up and boasted to his wife that he had been rather too many for Brooke of Tipton, and that he didn’t mind so much now about going to the poll.", "마음지 씨는 정치 형편이 조금 맑아지는 듯한 느낌을 받으며 말했다. \"그렇게 존경스러운 태도로 말씀하시는 신사분께 표를 드린다면 다소나마 기쁨이 있겠지요.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke on this occasion abstained from boasting of his tactics to Ladislaw, who for his part was glad enough to persuade himself that he had no concern with any canvassing except the purely argumentative sort, and that he worked no meaner engine than knowledge. Mr. Brooke, necessarily, had his agents, who understood the nature of the Middlemarch voter and the means of enlisting his ignorance on the side of the Bill—which were remarkably similar to the means of enlisting it on the side against the Bill. Will stopped his ears. Occasionally Parliament, like the rest of our lives, even to our eating and apparel, could hardly go on if our imaginations were too active about processes. There were plenty of dirty-handed men in the world to do dirty business; and Will protested to himself that his share in bringing Mr. Brooke through would be quite innocent.", "\"음, 그러니까 말일세, 마음지 씨, 자네는 우리 편에 서는 것이 옳은 일이라는 걸 알게 될 걸세. 이 개혁은 머지않아 모든 사람에게 영향을 미칠 거야. 철저히 대중적인 조치지. 말하자면 알파벳의 가, 나, 다 같은 것이라서, 나머지가 따라오려면 그것이 먼저 와야 한단 말일세. 자네가 그 일을 가정의 관점에서 봐야 한다는 데는 나도 전적으로 동의하네. 하지만 공공심이라는 게 있잖나. 우리는 다 한 가족이야, 알잖나. 다 한 찬장 속에 있는 거라고. 이를테면 표라는 것 말인데, 글쎄, 그게 케이프에서 사람들의 재산을 일구는 데 도움이 될지도 모르지. 표 한 장이 무슨 결과를 낳을지 누가 알겠나.\" 브룩 씨는 다소 망망대해에 떠 있는 듯한 느낌을 받으면서도 여전히 그것이 즐겁다고 여기며 말을 맺었다. 그러나 마음지 씨는 단호히 제동을 거는 어조로 대답했다. \"송구합니다만, 나리, 저는 그런 건 감당할 수 없습니다. 표를 던질 때는 제가 무얼 하는지 알아야 합니다. 외람된 말씀이오나, 그게 제 돈궤와 장부에 어떤 영향을 미칠지 살펴야 한단 말씀입니다. 가격이야, 인정하건대, 그 시비를 아무도 알 수 없는 것이지요. 그리고 보관이 안 되는 물건인 건포도를 사들이고 나면 값이 갑자기 떨어지곤 하는데, 저 자신도 그 속사정을 도무지 헤아려 본 적이 없습니다. 그건 인간의 자만심에 대한 질책이지요. 하지만 한 가족이라는 것에 관해서라면, 그래도 채무자와 채권자는 있겠지요. 설마 그것까지 개혁으로 없애지는 않을 테고요. 안 그렇다면 저는 만사가 지금 그대로 있는 쪽에 표를 던지겠습니다. 개인적으로 말하자면, 즉 저 자신과 가족을 두고 말하자면, 저만큼 변화를 부르짖을 필요가 적은 사람도 드뭅니다. 저는 잃을 게 없는 부류가 아니란 말씀입니다. 교구에서나 사적인 사업에서나 누리는 신망을 두고 하는 말이지, 결코 존경하는 나리와 그 단골 거래를 두고 하는 말은 아닙니다. 나리께서는 친절하게도, 보내 드린 물건이 만족스럽기만 하면 표를 주든 안 주든 그 거래를 끊지 않으시겠다고 말씀해 주셨으니까요.\""]], [["But whether he should succeed in that mode of contributing to the majority on the right side was very doubtful to him.", "이 대화를 마친 뒤 마음지 씨는 위층으로 올라가 아내에게, 자기가 티프턴의 브룩한테 한 수 위였노라고, 이제는 투표하러 가는 일에 별로 개의치 않는다고 자랑했다."], ["He had written out various speeches and memoranda for speeches, but he had begun to perceive that Mr. Brooke’s mind, if it had the burthen of remembering any train of thought, would let it drop, run away in search of it, and not easily come back again.", "브룩 씨는 이번에는 자기 전술을 래디슬로에게 자랑하기를 삼갔고, 윌은 윌대로 자신이 순전히 논쟁적인 종류의 유세 말고는 어떤 유세와도 무관하며, 지식보다 더 비천한 도구는 부리지 않는다고 스스로를 설득할 수 있어 다행이라 여겼다."], ["To collect documents is one mode of serving your country, and to remember the contents of a document is another. No!", "브룩 씨에게는 필연적으로 자기 사람들이 있어서, 그들은 미들마치 유권자의 본성과 그 무지를 법안 편으로 끌어들이는 수단을 잘 알고 있었으니, 그 수단은 무지를 법안 반대편으로 끌어들이는 수단과 놀랍도록 비슷했다."], ["the only way in which Mr.", "윌은 귀를 막았다."], ["Brooke could be coerced into thinking of the right arguments at the right time was to be well plied with them till they took up all the room in his brain.", "때로는 의회도, 우리 삶의 나머지 부분이, 심지어 우리가 먹는 것과 입는 것까지도 그러하듯이, 우리의 상상이 그 과정에 너무 활발히 작동한다면 도무지 굴러가지 못할 지경이었다."], ["But here there was the difficulty of finding room, so many things having been taken in beforehand.", "세상에는 더러운 일을 맡아 할 손이 더러운 자들이 얼마든지 있었다."], ["Mr. Brooke himself observed that his ideas stood rather in his way when he was speaking.", "그리고 윌은 브룩 씨를 당선시키는 데서 자기가 맡은 몫만큼은 전적으로 결백하다고 스스로에게 항변했다."]], [["However, Ladislaw’s coaching was forthwith to be put to the test, for before the day of nomination Mr.", "그러나 그가 그런 방식으로 옳은 편의 과반수에 기여하는 데 성공할지는 그 자신도 매우 의심스러웠다."], ["Brooke was to explain himself to the worthy electors of Middlemarch from the balcony of the White Hart, which looked out advantageously at an angle of the market-place, commanding a large area in front and two converging streets.", "그는 갖가지 연설문과 연설용 메모를 작성해 두었지만, 브룩 씨의 정신이란 일단 어떤 사고의 흐름을 기억해야 하는 짐을 떠안으면 그것을 떨어뜨리고, 그것을 찾으러 달아났다가도 좀처럼 다시 돌아오지 못한다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다. 문서를 모으는 것은 나라에 봉사하는 한 가지 방법이고, 문서의 내용을 기억하는 것은 또 다른 방법이다."], ["It was a fine May morning, and everything seemed hopeful: there was some prospect of an understanding between Bagster’s committee and Brooke’s, to which Mr.", "아니! 브룩 씨가 적절한 때에 적절한 논거를 떠올리도록 강제할 유일한 방법은, 그 논거들이 그의 머릿속 공간을 전부 차지할 때까지 부지런히 들이붓는 것이었다."], ["Bulstrode, Mr. Standish as a Liberal lawyer, and such manufacturers as Mr.", "그러나 여기에는 공간을 찾는 어려움이 있었으니, 미리 너무 많은 것이 들어차 있었기 때문이다."], ["Plymdale and Mr. Vincy, gave a solidity which almost counterbalanced Mr.", "브룩 씨 자신도 연설할 때면 자기 생각들이 다소 거치적거린다고 말했다."], ["Hawley and his associates who sat for Pinkerton at the Green Dragon.", "그렇지만 래디슬로의 지도는 곧 시험대에 오를 참이었다. 후보 지명일에 앞서 브룩 씨가 화이트 하트 여관의 발코니에서 미들마치의 존경할 만한 유권자들에게 자신의 입장을 밝히기로 되어 있었기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Brooke, conscious of having weakened the blasts of the “Trumpet”", "그 발코니는 장터 한 모퉁이에서 유리한 각도로 내다보여, 앞쪽의 너른 공간과 모여드는 두 갈래 거리를 한눈에 굽어볼 수 있었다."], ["against him, by his reforms as a landlord in the last half year, and hearing himself cheered a little as he drove into the town, felt his heart tolerably light under his buff-colored waistcoat.", "화창한 5월의 아침이었고, 모든 것이 희망차 보였다. 백스터의 위원회와 브룩의 위원회 사이에 합의가 이루어질 가망이 좀 있었으니, 거기에는 불스트로드 씨와 자유주의 변호사인 스탠디시 씨, 그리고 플림데일 씨와 빈시 씨 같은 제조업자들이 견실함을 더해 주어, 그린 드래건에서 핑커턴을 위해 진을 친 홀리 씨와 그 일당과 거의 맞먹을 정도였다."], ["But with regard to critical occasions, it often happens that all moments seem comfortably remote until the last.", "브룩 씨는 지난 반년간 지주로서 단행한 개혁으로 자신을 향한 \"트럼펫\"의 나팔 소리를 누그러뜨렸음을 의식하고 있었고, 시내로 마차를 몰고 들어오면서 자신에게 보내는 약간의 환호를 듣자, 황갈색 조끼 아래 마음이 제법 가벼워지는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 결정적인 순간이란, 흔히 마지막에 이르기 전까지는 모든 순간이 편안하게 멀리 있는 것처럼 여겨지는 법이다."]], [["“This looks well, eh?” said Mr.", "\"이거 괜찮아 보이는데, 안 그런가?\""], ["Brooke as the crowd gathered.", "군중이 모여들자 브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“I shall have a good audience, at any rate.", "\"어쨌든 청중은 잘 모이겠어."], ["I like this, now—this kind of public made up of one’s own neighbors, you know.”", "난 이런 게 좋단 말이지. 이렇게 자기 이웃들로 이루어진 청중 말일세, 알잖나.\""]], [["The weavers and tanners of Middlemarch, unlike Mr. Mawmsey, had never thought of Mr.", "미들마치의 직조공들과 무두장이들은 마음지 씨와 달리 브룩 씨를 이웃으로 여겨 본 적이 한 번도 없었고, 그가 런던에서 상자에 담겨 보내져 온 사람이라 한들 더 정 붙을 것도 없는 형편이었다."], ["Brooke as a neighbor, and were not more attached to him than if he had been sent in a box from London.", "그러나 그들은 후보를 소개하는 연사들의 말을 별다른 소란 없이 들었다."], ["But they listened without much disturbance to the speakers who introduced the candidate, one of them—a political personage from Brassing, who came to tell Middlemarch its duty—spoke so fully, that it was alarming to think what the candidate could find to say after him.", "그중 하나인 브래싱에서 온 어떤 정치 인사는 미들마치에 그 의무를 일러 주려고 왔는데, 어찌나 빠짐없이 말을 늘어놓는지, 그 뒤에 후보가 무슨 할 말을 찾아낼 수 있을지 생각하면 아찔할 지경이었다."], ["Meanwhile the crowd became denser, and as the political personage neared the end of his speech, Mr.", "그사이 군중은 더욱 빽빽해졌고, 그 정치 인사가 연설의 끝에 가까워질수록 브룩 씨는 자기 감각에 놀라운 변화가 이는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Brooke felt a remarkable change in his sensations while he still handled his eye-glass, trifled with documents before him, and exchanged remarks with his committee, as a man to whom the moment of summons was indifferent.", "그러면서도 그는 호출의 순간 따위는 아무렇지도 않다는 듯한 사람처럼, 여전히 안경을 만지작거리고 앞에 놓인 문서들을 매만지며 자기 위원회 사람들과 이런저런 말을 주고받았다."]], [["“I’ll take another glass of sherry, Ladislaw,”", "\"셰리주 한 잔 더 주게, 래디슬로.\""], ["he said, with an easy air, to Will, who was close behind him, and presently handed him the supposed fortifier.", "그는 바로 뒤에 있던 윌에게 태연한 태도로 말했고, 윌은 곧 그 기운을 북돋워 주리라 여겨지는 술을 건넸다."], ["It was ill-chosen; for Mr.", "그것은 잘못된 선택이었다."], ["Brooke was an abstemious man, and to drink a second glass of sherry quickly at no great interval from the first was a surprise to his system which tended to scatter his energies instead of collecting them.", "브룩 씨는 절제하는 사람이라, 첫 잔과 그리 멀지 않은 간격을 두고 셰리주 둘째 잔을 급히 마시는 것은 그의 신체에 충격을 주어, 기력을 모으기는커녕 오히려 흩어 놓는 경향이 있었기 때문이다. 부디 그를 가엾이 여기시라."], ["Pray pity him: so many English gentlemen make themselves miserable by speechifying on entirely private grounds! whereas Mr.", "그토록 많은 영국 신사들이 순전히 사사로운 자리에서 연설을 하느라 스스로를 비참하게 만드는데!"], ["Brooke wished to serve his country by standing for Parliament—which, indeed, may also be done on private grounds, but being once undertaken does absolutely demand some speechifying.", "그에 비하면 브룩 씨는 의회에 출마하여 나라에 봉사하고자 했던 것이다. 하기야 그 또한 사사로운 동기에서 할 수 있는 일이긴 하나, 일단 떠맡고 나면 어쨌든 얼마간의 연설을 절대적으로 요구하는 법이다."]], [["It was not about the beginning of his speech that Mr.", "브룩 씨가 조금이라도 불안해한 것은 연설의 첫머리에 관해서가 아니었다."], ["Brooke was at all anxious; this, he felt sure, would be all right; he should have it quite pat, cut out as neatly as a set of couplets from Pope.", "그 부분이라면 틀림없이 잘되리라고, 포프의 대구 한 묶음처럼 말끔히 잘라 내어 척척 외울 수 있으리라고 확신했다."], ["Embarking would be easy, but the vision of open sea that might come after was alarming.", "출항은 쉬울 터였다. 그러나 그 뒤에 펼쳐질지 모를 망망대해의 환영이 두려웠다."], ["“And questions, now,”", "\"그리고 질문 말인데,\""], ["hinted the demon just waking up in his stomach, “somebody may put questions about the schedules.—Ladislaw,”", "그의 뱃속에서 막 깨어나는 악마가 속삭였다. \"누군가 부칙에 관해 질문할지도 모르지.—래디슬로,\""], ["he continued, aloud, “just hand me the memorandum of the schedules.”", "그가 소리 내어 말을 이었다. \"부칙 메모 좀 건네주게.\""]], [["When Mr. Brooke presented himself on the balcony, the cheers were quite loud enough to counterbalance the yells, groans, brayings, and other expressions of adverse theory, which were so moderate that Mr.", "브룩 씨가 발코니에 모습을 드러내자 환호 소리가 제법 우렁차서, 야유며 신음 소리며 당나귀 울음 흉내며 그 밖의 반대 견해 표현을 충분히 상쇄하고도 남았다."], ["Standish (decidedly an old bird) observed in the ear next to him, “This looks dangerous, by God! Hawley has got some deeper plan than this.”", "그 반대 소리들이 어찌나 미미했던지, 스탠디시 씨는 (확실히 노련한 사람이었다) 옆 사람의 귀에 대고 말했다."], ["Still, the cheers were exhilarating, and no candidate could look more amiable than Mr.", "\"이거 위험해 보이는군, 정말이지! 홀리가 이보다 더 깊은 수를 숨겨 두고 있어.\""], ["Brooke, with the memorandum in his breast-pocket, his left hand on the rail of the balcony, and his right trifling with his eye-glass.", "그래도 환호는 신바람을 돋우었고, 가슴 주머니에 메모를 넣고 왼손을 발코니 난간에 올린 채 오른손으로 안경을 만지작거리는 브룩 씨보다 더 상냥해 보이는 후보는 없었다."], ["The striking points in his appearance were his buff waistcoat, short-clipped blond hair, and neutral physiognomy.", "그의 외모에서 두드러진 점은 황갈색 조끼와 짧게 깎은 금발, 그리고 어느 쪽으로도 기울지 않은 인상이었다."], ["He began with some confidence.", "그는 어느 정도 자신감을 가지고 입을 열었다."]], [["“Gentlemen—Electors of Middlemarch!”", "\"신사 여러분—미들마치의 유권자 여러분!\""]], [["This was so much the right thing that a little pause after it seemed natural.", "이것이야말로 더할 나위 없이 옳은 말이었기에, 그 뒤에 잠깐 사이를 두는 것이 자연스러워 보였다."]], [["“I’m uncommonly glad to be here—I was never so proud and happy in my life—never so happy, you know.”", "\"이 자리에 서게 되어 더없이 기쁩니다. 제 평생 이렇게 자랑스럽고 행복한 적이 없었습니다. 이토록 행복한 적이 없어요, 알다시피.\""]], [["This was a bold figure of speech, but not exactly the right thing; for, unhappily, the pat opening had slipped away—even couplets from Pope may be but “fallings from us, vanishings,”", "이것은 대담한 수사였으나 딱히 옳은 말은 아니었다. 불행히도 척척 외우려던 첫머리가 미끄러져 달아나 버렸기 때문이다."], ["when fear clutches us, and a glass of sherry is hurrying like smoke among our ideas.", "두려움이 우리를 움켜쥐고 셰리주 한 잔이 우리 생각들 사이를 연기처럼 헤집고 다닐 때면, 포프의 대구마저도 한낱 \"우리에게서 떨어져 나가는 것, 사라져 가는 것\"에 지나지 않을 수 있다."], ["Ladislaw, who stood at the window behind the speaker, thought, “it’s all up now.", "연사 뒤편 창가에 서 있던 래디슬로는 생각했다. \"이제 다 끝났군."], ["The only chance is that, since the best thing won’t always do, floundering may answer for once.” Mr.", "가장 좋은 방법이 늘 통하는 것은 아니니, 허우적거리는 게 이번 한 번은 먹힐지도 모른다는 것이 유일한 희망이야.\""], ["Brooke, meanwhile, having lost other clews, fell back on himself and his qualifications—always an appropriate graceful subject for a candidate.", "그사이 브룩 씨는 다른 실마리를 다 놓치고서 자기 자신과 자기 자격으로 되돌아왔으니, 후보에게는 언제나 적절하고도 우아한 화제였다."]], [["“I am a close neighbor of yours, my good friends—you’ve known me on the bench a good while—I’ve always gone a good deal into public questions—machinery, now, and machine-breaking—you’re many of you concerned with machinery, and I’ve been going into that lately. It won’t do, you know, breaking machines: everything must go on—trade, manufactures, commerce, interchange of staples—that kind of thing—since Adam Smith, that must go on. We must look all over the globe:—‘Observation with extensive view,’ must look everywhere, ‘from China to Peru,’ as somebody says—Johnson, I think, ‘The Rambler,’ you know. That is what I have done up to a certain point—not as far as Peru; but I’ve not always stayed at home—I saw it wouldn’t do. I’ve been in the Levant, where some of your Middlemarch goods go—and then, again, in the Baltic. The Baltic, now.”", "\"여러분, 저는 여러분의 가까운 이웃입니다. 좋은 친구 여러분, 여러분은 오랫동안 치안 판사석에 앉은 저를 보아 오셨지요. 저는 늘 공공의 문제에 깊이 관여해 왔습니다. 이를테면 기계며, 기계 파괴며 말입니다. 여러분 중 많은 분이 기계와 관련이 있으시고, 저도 요즘 그 문제를 파고들고 있습니다. 기계를 부수는 건 안 될 일이지요, 알다시피. 모든 게 굴러가야 하니까요. 무역이며, 제조며, 상업이며, 주요 산물의 교환이며, 그런 것들 말입니다. 애덤 스미스 이래로 그건 계속 굴러가야 합니다. 우리는 온 지구를 두루 살펴야 합니다. '드넓은 시야로 살피는 관찰' 말이지요, 어디든 두루 살펴야 합니다, '중국에서 페루까지' 말입니다, 누군가의 말마따나요. 존슨이었던 것 같군요, '램블러' 말입니다, 알다시피. 그게 바로 제가 어느 정도까지 해 온 일입니다. 페루까지는 아니지만 말이지요. 하지만 저는 늘 집에만 머물러 있지는 않았습니다. 그래선 안 된다는 걸 알았으니까요. 저는 레반트에 가 보았는데, 여러분 미들마치의 물건 일부가 거기로 가지요. 그리고 또, 발트해에도 가 보았습니다. 발트해 말입니다.\""]], [["Plying among his recollections in this way, Mr. Brooke might have got along, easily to himself, and would have come back from the remotest seas without trouble; but a diabolical procedure had been set up by the enemy.", "이런 식으로 자기 추억들 사이를 헤집고 다니다 보면, 브룩 씨는 스스로는 편안히 잘 헤쳐 나가, 가장 먼 바다에서도 별 탈 없이 돌아왔을지 모른다."], ["At one and the same moment there had risen above the shoulders of the crowd, nearly opposite Mr.", "그러나 적이 악마 같은 술책을 꾸며 놓았다. 바로 그 순간, 브룩 씨와 거의 마주 보는 자리, 그에게서 십 야드도 안 되는 곳에서 군중의 어깨 위로 그 자신의 형상이 솟아올랐다."], ["Brooke, and within ten yards of him, the effigy of himself: buff-colored waistcoat, eye-glass, and neutral physiognomy, painted on rag; and there had arisen, apparently in the air, like the note of the cuckoo, a parrot-like, Punch-voiced echo of his words.", "황갈색 조끼에 안경에 어느 쪽으로도 기울지 않은 인상이 헝겊에 그려져 있었다. 그리고 마치 뻐꾸기 울음소리처럼, 허공 어디선가 앵무새 같고 펀치 인형 같은 목소리로 그의 말을 흉내 내는 메아리가 일었다."], ["Everybody looked up at the open windows in the houses at the opposite angles of the converging streets; but they were either blank, or filled by laughing listeners.", "모두가 모여드는 두 거리의 맞은편 모퉁이 집들의 열린 창문을 올려다보았다. 그러나 그 창들은 텅 비어 있거나, 웃어 대는 구경꾼들로 차 있었다."], ["The most innocent echo has an impish mockery in it when it follows a gravely persistent speaker, and this echo was not at all innocent; if it did not follow with the precision of a natural echo, it had a wicked choice of the words it overtook.", "아무리 천진한 메아리라도 진지하게 끈질긴 연사를 뒤따를 때면 짓궂은 조롱이 깃들기 마련인데, 이 메아리는 도무지 천진하지가 않았다. 자연의 메아리처럼 정확하게 뒤따르지는 않았지만, 따라잡을 말을 사악하게 골라내는 솜씨가 있었다."], ["By the time it said, “The Baltic, now,”", "그것이 \"발트해 말입니다\"라고 했을 때쯤, 청중 사이를 훑고 다니던 웃음은 전체의 함성으로 번졌다."], ["the laugh which had been running through the audience became a general shout, and but for the sobering effects of party and that great public cause which the entanglement of things had identified with “Brooke of Tipton,” the laugh might have caught his committee.", "그리고 당파와, 일의 얽힘으로 \"티프턴의 브룩\"과 동일시되어 버린 그 위대한 공적 대의가 분위기를 가라앉히지 않았더라면, 그 웃음은 그의 위원회마저 사로잡았을 것이다."], ["Mr. Bulstrode asked, reprehensively, what the new police was doing; but a voice could not well be collared, and an attack on the effigy of the candidate would have been too equivocal, since Hawley probably meant it to be pelted.", "불스트로드 씨가 신설된 경찰이 뭘 하고 있느냐고 나무라듯 물었지만, 목소리를 멱살 잡을 수는 없는 노릇이었고, 후보의 형상을 공격하는 것도 너무 애매한 일이었으니, 홀리가 아마도 거기에 물건을 던지게 할 작정이었을 터이기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Brooke himself was not in a position to be quickly conscious of anything except a general slipping away of ideas within himself: he had even a little singing in the ears, and he was the only person who had not yet taken distinct account of the echo or discerned the image of himself.", "브룩 씨 자신은 자기 안에서 생각들이 죄다 미끄러져 빠져나가는 것 말고는 무엇도 재빨리 의식할 수 있는 처지가 아니었다."], ["Few things hold the perceptions more thoroughly captive than anxiety about what we have got to say.", "그는 귀에서 약간 윙윙거리는 소리까지 들렸고, 메아리를 똑똑히 알아차리지도, 자기 형상을 분간하지도 못한 유일한 사람이었다. 우리가 해야 할 말에 대한 불안만큼 우리의 지각을 철저히 사로잡는 것도 드물다."], ["Mr.", "브룩 씨는 웃음소리를 듣기는 했다."], ["Brooke heard the laughter; but he had expected some Tory efforts at disturbance, and he was at this moment additionally excited by the tickling, stinging sense that his lost exordium was coming back to fetch him from the Baltic.", "그러나 그는 토리당 쪽에서 얼마간 훼방을 놓으리라 예상하고 있었고, 게다가 잃어버린 서두가 발트해에서 자기를 데리러 돌아오고 있다는 간질간질하고 따끔따끔한 느낌에 이 순간 한층 더 흥분해 있었다."]], [["“That reminds me,” he went on, thrusting a hand into his side-pocket, with an easy air, “if I wanted a precedent, you know—but we never want a precedent for the right thing—but there is Chatham, now; I can’t say I should have supported Chatham, or Pitt, the younger Pitt—he was not a man of ideas, and we want ideas, you know.”", "\"그러고 보니 생각나는군요.\" 그는 태연한 태도로 한 손을 옆 주머니에 찔러 넣으며 말을 이었다. \"제가 선례가 필요하다면 말이지요, 알다시피. 하지만 우리는 옳은 일을 하는 데 선례가 필요한 적이 없습니다. 그래도 채텀이 있긴 하지요. 제가 채텀을 지지했으리라고는 말 못 하겠습니다. 피트, 그러니까 소小 피트도 마찬가지고요. 그는 이념을 지닌 사람이 아니었는데, 우리는 이념을 원하니까요, 알다시피.\""]], [["“Blast your ideas!", "\"이념이고 나발이고!"], ["we want the Bill,”", "우리가 원하는 건 법안이야!\""], ["said a loud rough voice from the crowd below.", "아래쪽 군중에서 거칠고 큰 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["Immediately the invisible Punch, who had hitherto followed Mr.", "곧바로, 그때까지 브룩 씨를 뒤따르던 보이지 않는 펀치가 되받았다."], ["Brooke, repeated, “Blast your ideas!", "\"이념이고 나발이고!"], ["we want the Bill.”", "우리가 원하는 건 법안이야!\""], ["The laugh was louder than ever, and for the first time Mr. Brooke being himself silent, heard distinctly the mocking echo.", "웃음소리는 그 어느 때보다 컸고, 브룩 씨는 자신이 입을 다물고 있던 터라 처음으로 그 조롱하는 메아리를 똑똑히 들었다."], ["But it seemed to ridicule his interrupter, and in that light was encouraging; so he replied with amenity—", "그러나 그것은 자신의 말을 가로막은 자를 비웃는 것처럼 보였고, 그런 점에서는 오히려 고무적이었다. 그래서 그는 상냥하게 대꾸했다."]], [["“There is something in what you say, my good friend, and what do we meet for but to speak our minds—freedom of opinion, freedom of the press, liberty—that kind of thing?", "\"좋은 친구여, 당신 말에도 일리가 있습니다. 그리고 우리가 모인 것이 다 무엇 때문입니까, 우리 생각을 말하기 위해서가 아니겠습니까."], ["The Bill, now—you shall have the Bill”—here Mr.", "의견의 자유, 출판의 자유, 자유 말입니다, 그런 종류의 것 말이지요."], ["Brooke paused a moment to fix on his eye-glass and take the paper from his breast-pocket, with a sense of being practical and coming to particulars.", "법안이라, 그래요, 여러분은 법안을 가지게 될 겁니다.\" 여기서 브룩 씨는 잠시 멈추어 안경을 고쳐 쓰고 가슴 주머니에서 종이를 꺼냈는데, 자신이 실무적이며 구체적인 사항으로 들어서고 있다는 느낌이 들었다."], ["The invisible Punch followed:—", "보이지 않는 펀치가 뒤따랐다."]], [["“You shall have the Bill, Mr. Brooke, per electioneering contest, and a seat outside Parliament as delivered, five thousand pounds, seven shillings, and fourpence.”", "\"여러분은 법안을 가지게 될 겁니다, 브룩 씨, 선거전 한 차례에, 의회 바깥의 의석 하나를 배달해 드리니, 도합 오천 파운드 칠 실링 사 펜스올시다.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke, amid the roars of laughter, turned red, let his eye-glass fall, and looking about him confusedly, saw the image of himself, which had come nearer.", "웃음이 와르르 터지는 가운데 브룩 씨는 얼굴이 붉어져 안경을 떨어뜨렸고, 어리둥절하여 주위를 둘러보다가 어느새 더 가까이 다가와 있는 자기 형상을 보았다."], ["The next moment he saw it dolorously bespattered with eggs.", "다음 순간 그는 그것이 처량하게 달걀 범벅이 된 것을 보았다."], ["His spirit rose a little, and his voice too.", "그의 기운이 조금 살아났고, 목소리도 그러했다."]], [["“Buffoonery, tricks, ridicule the test of truth—all that is very well”—here an unpleasant egg broke on Mr.", "\"익살이며, 술책이며, 조롱이 진리의 시금석이라니, 그것도 다 좋습니다.\""], ["Brooke’s shoulder, as the echo said, “All that is very well;”", "여기서 고약한 달걀 하나가 브룩 씨의 어깨에 깨어지는 가운데, 메아리가 말했다."], ["then came a hail of eggs, chiefly aimed at the image, but occasionally hitting the original, as if by chance.", "\"그것도 다 좋습니다.\" 이어서 달걀이 우박처럼 쏟아졌는데, 주로 형상을 겨냥했으나 이따금 마치 우연인 양 원본을 맞히기도 했다."], ["There was a stream of new men pushing among the crowd; whistles, yells, bellowings, and fifes made all the greater hubbub because there was shouting and struggling to put them down.", "새로운 사내들의 무리가 군중 사이를 밀치며 밀려들었다. 호루라기며 야유며 고함이며 피리 소리가 더욱 큰 소동을 일으켰으니, 그것들을 잠재우려고 또 외쳐 대고 몸싸움하는 소리가 더해진 탓이었다."], ["No voice would have had wing enough to rise above the uproar, and Mr. Brooke, disagreeably anointed, stood his ground no longer.", "그 어떤 목소리도 그 소란을 뚫고 솟아오를 만큼의 날개를 갖지 못했을 터였고, 불쾌하게 기름칠당한 브룩 씨는 더는 자리를 지키지 못했다."], ["The frustration would have been less exasperating if it had been less gamesome and boyish: a serious assault of which the newspaper reporter “can aver that it endangered the learned gentleman’s ribs,”", "그 좌절은, 좀 덜 장난스럽고 덜 어린애 같았더라면 그토록 약 오르지는 않았을 것이다."], ["or can respectfully bear witness to “the soles of that gentleman’s boots having been visible above the railing,” has perhaps more consolations attached to it.", "신문 기자가 \"그 일이 학식 높은 신사분의 갈비뼈를 위태롭게 했음을 단언할 수 있다\"고 쓰거나, \"그 신사분의 구두 밑창이 난간 위로 보였음\"을 정중히 증언할 수 있는 진지한 습격이라면, 어쩌면 거기에 딸린 위안이 더 클 법도 하다."]], [["Mr. Brooke re-entered the committee-room, saying, as carelessly as he could, “This is a little too bad, you know.", "브룩 씨는 위원회실로 돌아 들어오면서 되도록 아무렇지 않은 듯 말했다."], ["I should have got the ear of the people by-and-by—but they didn’t give me time.", "\"이거 좀 너무하는군, 정말이지. 조금만 있으면 사람들의 귀를 사로잡을 수 있었을 텐데, 시간을 안 주잖나."], ["I should have gone into the Bill by-and-by, you know,”", "조금만 있었으면 법안으로 들어갔을 텐데 말이야, 알다시피.\""], ["he added, glancing at Ladislaw.", "그는 래디슬로를 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다."], ["“However, things will come all right at the nomination.”", "\"그렇지만, 후보 지명일에는 다 잘될 거야.\""]], [["But it was not resolved unanimously that things would come right; on the contrary, the committee looked rather grim, and the political personage from Brassing was writing busily, as if he were brewing new devices.", "그러나 일이 잘되리라는 데 만장일치로 합의가 이루어진 것은 아니었다. 오히려 위원회는 다소 침울한 표정이었고, 브래싱에서 온 그 정치 인사는 마치 새로운 술책이라도 빚어내는 듯 부지런히 무언가를 쓰고 있었다."]], [["“It was Bowyer who did it,”", "\"그건 보이어의 짓입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Standish, evasively.", "스탠디시 씨가 말끝을 흐리며 말했다."], ["“I know it as well as if he had been advertised.", "\"마치 광고라도 난 것처럼 분명히 압니다."], ["He’s uncommonly good at ventriloquism, and he did it uncommonly well, by God!", "그자는 복화술에 유난히 능하고, 이번에도 유난히 잘해냈지요, 정말이지!"], ["Hawley has been having him to dinner lately: there’s a fund of talent in Bowyer.”", "홀리가 요즘 그자를 만찬에 부르곤 했답니다. 보이어한테는 재주가 넘치거든요.\""]], [["“Well, you know, you never mentioned him to me, Standish, else I would have invited him to dine,”", "\"음, 그러니까 말일세, 자네가 그자 얘기를 내게 한 적이 없잖나, 스탠디시. 안 그랬으면 그자를 만찬에 초대했을 텐데.\""], ["said poor Mr. Brooke, who had gone through a great deal of inviting for the good of his country.", "가엾은 브룩 씨가 말했으니, 그는 나라를 위해 그토록 많은 초대를 해 온 터였다."]], [["“There’s not a more paltry fellow in Middlemarch than Bowyer,”", "\"미들마치에 보이어보다 더 하찮은 자도 없습니다.\""], ["said Ladislaw, indignantly, “but it seems as if the paltry fellows were always to turn the scale.”", "래디슬로가 분개하여 말했다. \"그런데도 늘 하찮은 자들이 저울추를 기울이는 것 같단 말이지요.\""]], [["Will was thoroughly out of temper with himself as well as with his “principal,”", "윌은 자기 \"주군\"에 대해서뿐 아니라 자기 자신에 대해서도 단단히 부아가 치밀어 있었고, \"파이어니어\"와 브룩 씨를 함께 내던져 버릴까 하는 어렴풋한 결심을 품고서 자기 방에 틀어박히러 갔다. 무엇 때문에 그가 여기 머물러 있겠는가?"], ["and he went to shut himself in his rooms with a half-formed resolve to throw up the “Pioneer” and Mr.", "자신과 도러시아 사이에 건널 수 없이 가로놓인 심연을 언젠가 메울 수 있다면, 그것은 여기 머물러 브룩의 하수인으로서 마땅한 경멸 속으로 미끄러져 드는 것보다는, 차라리 떠나가 전혀 다른 위치에 올라서는 편이 나을 터였다."], ["Brooke together. Why should he stay?", "그러자 자기가 이룰 수 있을 경이로운 일들에 대한 젊은이의 꿈이 찾아왔다."], ["If the impassable gulf between himself and Dorothea were ever to be filled up, it must rather be by his going away and getting into a thoroughly different position than by staying here and slipping into deserved contempt as an understrapper of Brooke’s.", "이를테면 오 년 안에 말이다. 정치적 저술과 정치적 연설은, 이제 공적 생활이 더 넓어지고 더 전국적으로 되어 가는 만큼 더 높은 가치를 지니게 될 터였고, 그것들이 그에게 그런 명성을 안겨 주어, 그가 도러시아더러 자기에게 격을 낮추어 내려오라고 청하는 것처럼 보이지 않게 될지도 몰랐다."], ["Then came the young dream of wonders that he might do—in five years, for example: political writing, political speaking, would get a higher value now public life was going to be wider and more national, and they might give him such distinction that he would not seem to be asking Dorothea to step down to him.", "오 년이라. 그녀가 다른 누구보다 자기를 더 마음에 두고 있다고 확신만 할 수 있다면, 자신이 스스로를 낮추지 않고 사랑을 고백할 수 있게 될 때까지는 거리를 두고 있는 것이라고 그녀에게 알릴 수만 있다면, 그러면 그는 수월히 떠나가, 스물다섯 살의 그에게는 사물의 내적 질서 속에서 충분히 있을 법해 보이던 그런 출세길에 오를 수 있을 터였다."], ["Five years:—if he could only be sure that she cared for him more than for others; if he could only make her aware that he stood aloof until he could tell his love without lowering himself—then he could go away easily, and begin a career which at five-and-twenty seemed probable enough in the inward order of things, where talent brings fame, and fame everything else which is delightful.", "그 질서 속에서는 재능이 명성을 가져오고, 명성이 그 밖에 즐거운 모든 것을 가져다주니 말이다."], ["He could speak and he could write; he could master any subject if he chose, and he meant always to take the side of reason and justice, on which he would carry all his ardor.", "그는 말할 줄도 알았고 글 쓸 줄도 알았다. 마음만 먹으면 어떤 주제든 통달할 수 있었고, 언제나 이성과 정의의 편에 서서, 그쪽으로 자신의 온 열정을 쏟아부을 작정이었다."], ["Why should he not one day be lifted above the shoulders of the crowd, and feel that he had won that eminence well?", "어찌 언젠가 그가 군중의 어깨 위로 들어 올려져, 그 높은 자리를 떳떳이 얻었다고 느끼지 못하겠는가?"], ["Without doubt he would leave Middlemarch, go to town, and make himself fit for celebrity by “eating his dinners.”", "의심할 바 없이 그는 미들마치를 떠나 도시로 가서, \"법조인의 회식에 참석하며\" 명성에 어울리는 사람이 될 터였다."]], [["But not immediately: not until some kind of sign had passed between him and Dorothea.", "그러나 당장은 아니었다. 자신과 도러시아 사이에 어떤 종류든 신호가 오가기 전까지는 아니었다."], ["He could not be satisfied until she knew why, even if he were the man she would choose to marry, he would not marry her.", "설령 자신이 그녀가 남편으로 택할 만한 사람이라 하더라도 왜 그녀와 결혼하지 않으려는지 그녀가 알기 전까지는 그는 만족할 수 없었다."], ["Hence he must keep his post and bear with Mr. Brooke a little longer.", "그러니 그는 자기 자리를 지키며 조금 더 브룩 씨를 견뎌 내야 했다."]], [["But he soon had reason to suspect that Mr. Brooke had anticipated him in the wish to break up their connection.", "그러나 그는 곧, 브룩 씨가 둘의 관계를 끝내고 싶어 하는 마음에서 자기보다 한발 앞섰음을 의심할 만한 까닭을 얻게 되었다."], ["Deputations without and voices within had concurred in inducing that philanthropist to take a stronger measure than usual for the good of mankind; namely, to withdraw in favor of another candidate, to whom he left the advantages of his canvassing machinery.", "밖에서의 대표단들과 안에서의 목소리들이 합세하여 그 박애주의자로 하여금 인류의 안녕을 위해 평소보다 더 강력한 조치를 취하도록 부추긴 것이다. 다름 아니라, 다른 후보를 위해 사퇴하여 그에게 자기 유세 기구의 이점을 넘겨주는 일이었다."], ["He himself called this a strong measure, but observed that his health was less capable of sustaining excitement than he had imagined.", "그 자신은 이를 강력한 조치라 일컬었으나, 자기 건강이 생각했던 것만큼 흥분을 견뎌 낼 능력이 못 된다고 말했다."]], [["“I have felt uneasy about the chest—it won’t do to carry that too far,”", "\"가슴이 좀 불편하단 말이야. 그걸 너무 무리하면 안 되지.\""], ["he said to Ladislaw in explaining the affair.", "그는 그 일을 설명하면서 래디슬로에게 말했다."], ["“I must pull up.", "\"나는 멈춰야 해."], ["Poor Casaubon was a warning, you know.", "가엾은 캐소본이 경고가 됐잖나, 알다시피."], ["I’ve made some heavy advances, but I’ve dug a channel.", "내가 적잖은 자금을 댔지만, 물길은 하나 파 놓은 셈이지."], ["It’s rather coarse work—this electioneering, eh, Ladislaw?", "이 선거 운동이라는 게 좀 거친 일이야, 안 그런가, 래디슬로?"], ["dare say you are tired of it.", "자네도 진력이 났을 테지."], ["However, we have dug a channel with the ‘Pioneer’—put things in a track, and so on.", "그래도 우리는 '파이어니어'로 물길을 하나 파 놓았어. 일을 궤도에 올려놓았다, 그런 거지."], ["A more ordinary man than you might carry it on now—more ordinary, you know.”", "이제는 자네보다 더 평범한 사람도 이걸 이어 갈 수 있을 거야. 더 평범한 사람 말일세, 알다시피.\""]], [["“Do you wish me to give it up?”", "\"제가 그만두기를 바라십니까?\""], ["said Will, the quick color coming in his face, as he rose from the writing-table, and took a turn of three steps with his hands in his pockets.", "윌이 글 쓰던 탁자에서 일어서며 말했는데, 얼굴에 핏기가 확 올랐다. 그는 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채 세 걸음을 떼며 돌아섰다."], ["“I am ready to do so whenever you wish it.”", "\"원하신다면 언제든 그만둘 준비가 되어 있습니다.\""]], [["“As to wishing, my dear Ladislaw, I have the highest opinion of your powers, you know.", "\"바라느냐고 하면, 이보게 래디슬로, 나는 자네 능력을 더없이 높이 평가하네, 알잖나. 하지만 '파이어니어'"], ["But about the ‘Pioneer,’ I have been consulting a little with some of the men on our side, and they are inclined to take it into their hands—indemnify me to a certain extent—carry it on, in fact.", "일은, 내가 우리 편 사람들 몇과 좀 상의해 봤는데, 그들이 그걸 자기들 손에 넘겨받으려는 눈치야. 어느 정도까지 내게 손실을 보전해 주고, 실은 그걸 이어 가겠다는 거지."], ["And under the circumstances, you might like to give up—might find a better field.", "그런 사정이니, 자네는 그만두고 싶을지도 몰라. 더 나은 무대를 찾을 수도 있고."], ["These people might not take that high view of you which I have always taken, as an alter ego, a right hand—though I always looked forward to your doing something else.", "그 사람들은 내가 늘 자네를 보아 온 것만큼 자네를 높이 평가하지 않을지도 모르네. 나는 자네를 또 다른 나, 오른팔로 여겨 왔는데 말이야."], ["I think of having a run into France.", "하기야 나는 늘 자네가 다른 무언가를 하리라 내다보긴 했지. 나는 프랑스에 한번 다녀올까 하네."], ["But I’ll write you any letters, you know—to Althorpe and people of that kind.", "하지만 자네에게 추천서야 얼마든지 써 주겠네, 알잖나. 올소프며 그런 부류의 사람들에게 말이야."], ["I’ve met Althorpe.”", "나는 올소프를 만난 적이 있거든.\""]], [["“I am exceedingly obliged to you,”", "\"대단히 감사합니다.\""], ["said Ladislaw, proudly.", "래디슬로가 자존심을 세우며 말했다."], ["“Since you are going to part with the ‘Pioneer,’ I need not trouble you about the steps I shall take.", "\"어르신께서 '파이어니어'를 손에서 놓으실 거라면, 제가 앞으로 취할 행보에 관해서는 어르신을 번거롭게 해 드릴 필요가 없겠지요."], ["I may choose to continue here for the present.”", "저는 당분간 여기 계속 머무는 쪽을 택할지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["After Mr.", "브룩 씨가 떠난 뒤 윌은 혼잣말을 했다."], ["Brooke had left him Will said to himself, “The rest of the family have been urging him to get rid of me, and he doesn’t care now about my going.", "\"집안 식구들이 나를 떼어 내라고 그를 졸라 댔는데, 이제 그는 내가 떠나든 말든 개의치 않는군."], ["I shall stay as long as I like.", "나는 내가 있고 싶은 만큼 있겠어."], ["I shall go of my own movements and not because they are afraid of me.”", "떠난다면 그건 내 뜻으로 떠나는 거지, 그들이 나를 두려워해서가 아니야.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LII.", "# CHAPTER LII."]], [["“His heart The lowliest duties on itself did lay.”", "가장 미천한 의무를 스스로 떠맡았네."], ["—WORDSWORTH.", "—워즈워스"]], [["On that June evening when Mr.", "페어브러더 씨가 로윅의 성직록을 받게 되리라는 것을 알게 된 그 유월 저녁, 고풍스러운 응접실에는 기쁨이 가득했고, 위대한 법률가들의 초상화마저 흡족하게 내려다보는 듯했다."], ["Farebrother knew that he was to have the Lowick living, there was joy in the old fashioned parlor, and even the portraits of the great lawyers seemed to look on with satisfaction.", "그의 어머니는 차와 토스트에 손도 대지 않은 채, 여느 때처럼 곱고 단정한 자세로 앉아 있었는데, 오직 두 뺨에 떠오른 홍조와 두 눈에 어린 광채로만 감정을 드러냈을 뿐이었다."], ["His mother left her tea and toast untouched, but sat with her usual pretty primness, only showing her emotion by that flush in the cheeks and brightness in the eyes which give an old woman a touching momentary identity with her far-off youthful self, and saying decisively—", "그것은 노부인에게 아득히 멀어진 젊은 날의 자신과 잠시나마 가슴 뭉클하게 겹치는 모습을 부여하는 것이었다. 그러더니 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“The greatest comfort, Camden, is that you have deserved it.”", "\"가장 큰 위안은, 캠던, 네가 그것을 받을 만한 자격이 있다는 거란다.\""]], [["“When a man gets a good berth, mother, half the deserving must come after,”", "\"어머니, 사람이 좋은 자리를 얻으면, 자격의 절반은 그 뒤에 갖춰지는 법이지요.\""], ["said the son, brimful of pleasure, and not trying to conceal it.", "아들이 기쁨에 넘쳐 그것을 감추려 하지도 않고 말했다."], ["The gladness in his face was of that active kind which seems to have energy enough not only to flash outwardly, but to light up busy vision within: one seemed to see thoughts, as well as delight, in his glances.", "그의 얼굴에 어린 환희는 능동적인 종류의 것이어서, 밖으로 번뜩일 뿐만 아니라 안으로도 분주한 상상을 환히 밝힐 만한 에너지가 있는 듯했다. 그의 시선에는 기쁨뿐 아니라 갖가지 생각도 깃들어 있는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["“Now, aunt,”", "\"자, 고모.\" 그는 두 손을 비비며 새끼 비버 같은 가냘프고 정겨운 소리를 내고 있는 노블 양을 바라보며 말을 이었다."], ["he went on, rubbing his hands and looking at Miss Noble, who was making tender little beaver-like noises, “There shall be sugar-candy always on the table for you to steal and give to the children, and you shall have a great many new stockings to make presents of, and you shall darn your own more than ever!”", "\"이제 식탁에는 늘 사탕이 놓여 있을 테니, 마음껏 슬쩍해서 아이들에게 나눠 주세요. 그리고 선물로 줄 새 양말도 많이 갖게 되실 테고, 고모 양말도 어느 때보다 더 많이 기우실 거예요!\""]], [["Miss Noble nodded at her nephew with a subdued half-frightened laugh, conscious of having already dropped an additional lump of sugar into her basket on the strength of the new preferment.", "노블 양은 조카를 향해 다소 겁먹은 듯 나직이 웃으며 고개를 끄덕였는데, 이미 그 새로운 성직 임명을 빌미로 설탕 한 덩이를 더 자기 바구니에 떨어뜨려 넣었다는 것을 의식하고 있었다."]], [["“As for you, Winny”—the Vicar went on—“I shall make no difficulty about your marrying any Lowick bachelor—Mr. Solomon Featherstone, for example, as soon as I find you are in love with him.”", "\"그리고 위니, 너로 말할 것 같으면\" 하고 사제는 말을 이었다. \"네가 로윅의 어느 독신 남자와 결혼한다 해도 나는 조금도 반대하지 않을 거다. 이를테면 솔로몬 페더스톤 씨 같은 사람 말이지. 네가 그 사람을 사랑한다는 걸 알게 되는 즉시 말이야.\""]], [["Miss Winifred, who had been looking at her brother all the while and crying heartily, which was her way of rejoicing, smiled through her tears and said, “You must set me the example, Cam: you must marry now.”", "내내 오빠를 바라보며 진심으로 울고 있던 위니프레드 양은 — 그것이 그녀가 기쁨을 표현하는 방식이었다 — 눈물 사이로 미소 지으며 말했다. \"오빠가 먼저 본을 보여 줘야지, 캠. 이제 오빠가 결혼해야 해.\""]], [["“With all my heart.", "\"기꺼이 그러마."], ["But who is in love with me?", "그런데 누가 나를 사랑하겠니?"], ["I am a seedy old fellow,”", "나는 초라한 늙은이인걸.\""], ["said the Vicar, rising, pushing his chair away and looking down at himself.", "사제는 일어서서 의자를 밀어내고 자기 모습을 내려다보며 말했다."], ["“What do you say, mother?”", "\"어머니는 어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["“You are a handsome man, Camden: though not so fine a figure of a man as your father,”", "\"너는 잘생긴 남자란다, 캠던. 비록 네 아버지만큼 풍채가 훌륭하진 않지만 말이다.\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."]], [["“I wish you would marry Miss Garth, brother,”", "\"오빠가 가스 양과 결혼하면 좋겠어.\""], ["said Miss Winifred.", "위니프레드 양이 말했다."], ["“She would make us so lively at Lowick.”", "\"그분이라면 로윅 생활을 아주 활기차게 만들어 줄 텐데.\""]], [["“Very fine!", "\"거참 멋진 말이로구나!"], ["You talk as if young women were tied up to be chosen, like poultry at market; as if I had only to ask and everybody would have me,”", "너는 마치 젊은 여자들이 시장의 가금처럼 골라 가라고 묶여 있는 것처럼, 내가 청하기만 하면 누구든 나를 받아 줄 것처럼 말하는구나.\""], ["said the Vicar, not caring to specify.", "사제는 누구라고 콕 집어 말할 마음이 없는 채로 말했다."]], [["“We don’t want everybody,”", "\"우리가 아무나 원하는 건 아니야.\""], ["said Miss Winifred.", "위니프레드 양이 말했다."], ["“But you would like Miss Garth, mother, shouldn’t you?”", "\"하지만 어머니, 어머니도 가스 양이 마음에 드시지요?\""]], [["“My son’s choice shall be mine,” said Mrs.", "\"내 아들의 선택이 곧 나의 선택이지.\""], ["Farebrother, with majestic discretion, “and a wife would be most welcome, Camden.", "페어브러더 부인이 위엄 있는 신중함으로 말했다. \"그리고 며느리는 더없이 환영이란다, 캠던."], ["You will want your whist at home when we go to Lowick, and Henrietta Noble never was a whist-player.” (Mrs.", "우리가 로윅으로 가면 너는 집에서 휘스트를 하고 싶을 텐데, 헨리에타 노블은 한 번도 휘스트를 한 적이 없으니 말이다.\""], ["Farebrother always called her tiny old sister by that magnificent name.)", "(페어브러더 부인은 자그마한 노처녀 여동생을 늘 그 거창한 이름으로 불렀다.)"]], [["“I shall do without whist now, mother.”", "\"이제 휘스트는 안 할 작정이에요, 어머니.\""]], [["“Why so, Camden?", "\"어째서, 캠던?"], ["In my time whist was thought an undeniable amusement for a good churchman,” said Mrs.", "내 시절에는 휘스트가 신앙심 깊은 교인에게도 나무랄 데 없는 오락으로 여겨졌단다.\""], ["Farebrother, innocent of the meaning that whist had for her son, and speaking rather sharply, as at some dangerous countenancing of new doctrine.", "페어브러더 부인이 휘스트가 아들에게 어떤 의미였는지는 전혀 모른 채, 무언가 새로운 교리를 위태롭게 두둔하는 데 대해서인 양 다소 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["“I shall be too busy for whist; I shall have two parishes,”", "\"휘스트를 할 만큼 한가하지 않을 거예요. 교구를 둘이나 맡게 될 테니까요.\""], ["said the Vicar, preferring not to discuss the virtues of that game.", "사제는 그 노름의 미덕을 논하고 싶지 않아 그렇게 말했다."]], [["He had already said to Dorothea, “I don’t feel bound to give up St.", "그는 이미 도러시아에게 이렇게 말한 바 있었다. \"성 보톨프 교회를 포기해야 한다고는 생각하지 않습니다."], ["Botolph’s. It is protest enough against the pluralism they want to reform if I give somebody else most of the money.", "제가 다른 누군가에게 그 수입의 대부분을 넘겨준다면, 그것만으로도 사람들이 개혁하려는 성직 겸임에 대한 충분한 항의가 되겠지요."], ["The stronger thing is not to give up power, but to use it well.”", "더 강한 일은 권한을 포기하는 것이 아니라 그것을 잘 쓰는 것입니다.\""]], [["“I have thought of that,”", "\"저도 그 점을 생각했어요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“So far as self is concerned, I think it would be easier to give up power and money than to keep them.", "\"저 자신에 관한 한, 권한과 돈은 그것을 지키는 것보다 포기하는 편이 더 쉬울 것 같아요."], ["It seems very unfitting that I should have this patronage, yet I felt that I ought not to let it be used by some one else instead of me.”", "제가 이런 성직 임명권을 갖는 것이 무척 어울리지 않는 것 같지만, 그래도 그것을 저 대신 다른 누군가가 쓰도록 내버려 두어서는 안 된다고 느꼈답니다.\""]], [["“It is I who am bound to act so that you will not regret your power,”", "\"당신이 그 권한을 후회하지 않도록 처신해야 할 사람은 바로 저입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother.", "페어브러더 씨가 말했다."]], [["His was one of the natures in which conscience gets the more active when the yoke of life ceases to gall them.", "그는 삶의 멍에가 더 이상 자신을 짓누르지 않게 되자 양심이 오히려 더 활발해지는 부류의 사람이었다."], ["He made no display of humility on the subject, but in his heart he felt rather ashamed that his conduct had shown laches which others who did not get benefices were free from.", "그는 그 문제에 관해 겸손을 드러내 보이지는 않았으나, 마음속으로는 성직록을 얻지 못한 다른 이들에게는 없는 태만함을 자신의 행실이 드러내 보였다는 것이 다소 부끄러웠다."]], [["“I used often to wish I had been something else than a clergyman,”", "\"성직자가 아닌 다른 무언가가 되었더라면 하고 자주 바라곤 했지요.\""], ["he said to Lydgate, “but perhaps it will be better to try and make as good a clergyman out of myself as I can.", "그는 리드게이트에게 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌면 나 자신을 최대한 훌륭한 성직자로 만들어 보는 편이 더 나을지도 모르겠습니다."], ["That is the well-beneficed point of view, you perceive, from which difficulties are much simplified,”", "보시다시피, 이것은 좋은 성직록을 가진 자의 관점이라, 거기서는 온갖 어려움이 한결 단순해진답니다.\""], ["he ended, smiling.", "그는 미소 지으며 말을 맺었다."]], [["The Vicar did feel then as if his share of duties would be easy.", "그때 사제는 정말이지 자기 몫의 의무가 수월하리라 느꼈다."], ["But Duty has a trick of behaving unexpectedly—something like a heavy friend whom we have amiably asked to visit us, and who breaks his leg within our gates.", "하지만 의무란 뜻밖의 방식으로 행동하는 버릇이 있다 — 우리가 다정하게 방문을 청해 놓았더니 우리 집 문 안에서 다리가 부러져 버리는 거추장스러운 친구처럼."]], [["Hardly a week later, Duty presented itself in his study under the disguise of Fred Vincy, now returned from Omnibus College with his bachelor’s degree.", "채 일주일도 지나지 않아, 의무는 옴니버스 칼리지에서 학사 학위를 받고 막 돌아온 프레드 빈시로 변장한 채 그의 서재에 나타났다."]], [["“I am ashamed to trouble you, Mr. Farebrother,”", "\"이런 일로 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다, 페어브러더 씨.\""], ["said Fred, whose fair open face was propitiating, “but you are the only friend I can consult.", "프레드가 말했는데, 그 환하고 솔직한 얼굴이 호감을 자아냈다. \"하지만 제가 상의할 수 있는 친구는 선생님뿐입니다."], ["I told you everything once before, and you were so good that I can’t help coming to you again.”", "전에 한 번 모든 걸 다 말씀드렸을 때 선생님이 너무나 친절하게 대해 주셔서, 다시 찾아오지 않을 수가 없었어요.\""]], [["“Sit down, Fred, I’m ready to hear and do anything I can,”", "\"앉게, 프레드. 무슨 이야기든 듣고, 내가 할 수 있는 일이라면 뭐든 하겠네.\""], ["said the Vicar, who was busy packing some small objects for removal, and went on with his work.", "사제는 옮길 자잘한 물건들을 챙기느라 분주했고, 그러면서도 하던 일을 계속했다."]], [["“I wanted to tell you—”", "\"말씀드리고 싶은 건—\" 프레드는 한순간 머뭇거리다가 단숨에 털어놓듯 말을 이었다."], ["Fred hesitated an instant and then went on plungingly, “I might go into the Church now; and really, look where I may, I can’t see anything else to do.", "\"이제 성직에 들어갈 수도 있겠습니다. 그리고 솔직히 아무리 둘러봐도 달리 할 일이 보이지 않아요."], ["I don’t like it, but I know it’s uncommonly hard on my father to say so, after he has spent a good deal of money in educating me for it.”", "마음에 들진 않지만, 이런 말을 하는 게 아버지께 몹시 모진 일이라는 건 알아요. 아버지가 저를 그 길로 키우느라 적잖은 돈을 쓰셨으니까요.\""], ["Fred paused again an instant, and then repeated, “and I can’t see anything else to do.”", "프레드는 다시 한순간 멈췄다가 되풀이했다. \"그리고 달리 할 일이 보이질 않습니다.\""]], [["“I did talk to your father about it, Fred, but I made little way with him.", "\"내가 자네 아버지께 그 일을 말씀드린 적이 있네, 프레드. 하지만 별 진전이 없었어."], ["He said it was too late.", "너무 늦었다고 하시더군."], ["But you have got over one bridge now: what are your other difficulties?”", "하지만 자네는 이제 다리 하나를 건넌 셈이야. 나머지 어려움이라면 무엇인가?\""]], [["“Merely that I don’t like it.", "\"그저 마음에 들지 않는다는 것뿐이에요."], ["I don’t like divinity, and preaching, and feeling obliged to look serious.", "신학도, 설교도, 진지한 표정을 지어야 한다는 것도 싫어요."], ["I like riding across country, and doing as other men do.", "저는 들판을 가로질러 말을 달리고, 다른 사내들처럼 사는 게 좋습니다."], ["I don’t mean that I want to be a bad fellow in any way; but I’ve no taste for the sort of thing people expect of a clergyman.", "무슨 나쁜 사람이 되고 싶다는 뜻은 아니에요. 다만 사람들이 성직자에게 기대하는 그런 것에는 취미가 없다는 거죠."], ["And yet what else am I to do?", "그렇지만 달리 무엇을 하겠어요?"], ["My father can’t spare me any capital, else I might go into farming.", "아버지가 제게 밑천을 떼어 주실 수 없으니, 안 그러면 농사라도 지어 볼 텐데요."], ["And he has no room for me in his trade.", "또 가게에는 제가 들어갈 자리도 없어요."], ["And of course I can’t begin to study for law or physic now, when my father wants me to earn something.", "게다가 아버지가 제가 뭐라도 벌기를 바라시는 지금에 와서 법학이나 의학 공부를 새로 시작할 수도 없는 노릇이고요."], ["It’s all very well to say I’m wrong to go into the Church; but those who say so might as well tell me to go into the backwoods.”", "제가 성직에 들어가는 게 잘못이라고 말하기야 쉽지요. 하지만 그렇게 말하는 사람들은 차라리 저더러 오지로 떠나라고 하는 편이 나을 거예요.\""]], [["Fred’s voice had taken a tone of grumbling remonstrance, and Mr. Farebrother might have been inclined to smile if his mind had not been too busy in imagining more than Fred told him.", "프레드의 목소리에는 투덜대는 항변의 어조가 배어 있었고, 페어브러더 씨는 만약 프레드가 말한 것 이상을 머릿속으로 그려 보느라 바쁘지만 않았더라면 빙그레 웃었을지도 모른다."]], [["“Have you any difficulties about doctrines—about the Articles?”", "\"교리에 관해서는 어려움이 없는가? 신앙 조항 말일세.\""], ["he said, trying hard to think of the question simply for Fred’s sake.", "그는 오로지 프레드를 위해 그 물음을 단순하게 생각하려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“No; I suppose the Articles are right.", "\"없습니다. 조항들은 옳다고 생각해요."], ["I am not prepared with any arguments to disprove them, and much better, cleverer fellows than I am go in for them entirely.", "그것을 반박할 논거 같은 건 제겐 없고, 저보다 훨씬 훌륭하고 영리한 사람들이 전적으로 그것을 받아들이지 않습니까."], ["I think it would be rather ridiculous in me to urge scruples of that sort, as if I were a judge,”", "제가 마치 무슨 심판관이라도 되는 양 그런 거리낌을 들먹이는 건 오히려 우스운 일일 거예요.\""], ["said Fred, quite simply.", "프레드가 아주 솔직하게 말했다."]], [["“I suppose, then, it has occurred to you that you might be a fair parish priest without being much of a divine?”", "\"그렇다면 자네가 대단한 신학자가 아니더라도 그럭저럭 괜찮은 교구 사제는 될 수 있겠다고 생각해 본 적은 있겠군?\""]], [["“Of course, if I am obliged to be a clergyman, I shall try and do my duty, though I mayn’t like it.", "\"물론, 어쩔 수 없이 성직자가 되어야 한다면, 마음에 들지 않더라도 제 의무를 다하려고 애쓸 겁니다."], ["Do you think any body ought to blame me?”", "누가 저를 탓할 거라고 생각하세요?\""]], [["“For going into the Church under the circumstances?", "\"이런 형편에 성직에 들어가는 일을 두고 말인가?"], ["That depends on your conscience, Fred—how far you have counted the cost, and seen what your position will require of you.", "그건 자네 양심에 달린 문제일세, 프레드. 자네가 그 대가를 얼마나 헤아렸는지, 자네의 자리가 자네에게 무엇을 요구할지 얼마나 내다봤는지에 달린 거지."], ["I can only tell you about myself, that I have always been too lax, and have been uneasy in consequence.”", "나로 말하자면, 나는 늘 너무 안일했고 그 결과로 마음이 편치 못했다는 것밖에는 자네에게 해 줄 말이 없네.\""]], [["“But there is another hindrance,”", "\"하지만 또 다른 걸림돌이 있어요.\""], ["said Fred, coloring.", "프레드가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I did not tell you before, though perhaps I may have said things that made you guess it.", "\"전에는 말씀드리지 않았지만, 어쩌면 선생님이 짐작하실 만한 말들을 흘렸을지도 모르겠습니다."], ["There is somebody I am very fond of: I have loved her ever since we were children.”", "제가 무척 좋아하는 사람이 있어요. 어릴 때부터 줄곧 그녀를 사랑해 왔습니다.\""]], [["“Miss Garth, I suppose?”", "\"가스 양 말이겠지?\""], ["said the Vicar, examining some labels very closely.", "사제는 어떤 꼬리표들을 아주 유심히 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["I shouldn’t mind anything if she would have me.", "그녀가 저를 받아 주기만 한다면 다른 건 아무래도 좋아요."], ["And I know I could be a good fellow then.”", "그러면 저도 좋은 사람이 될 수 있을 거라고 확신합니다.\""]], [["“And you think she returns the feeling?”", "\"그래, 그녀도 그 마음에 화답한다고 생각하나?\""]], [["“She never will say so; and a good while ago she made me promise not to speak to her about it again.", "\"그녀는 절대 그렇다고 말하지 않을 거예요. 그리고 한참 전에 다시는 그 얘길 꺼내지 않겠다고 그녀에게 약속하게 됐어요."], ["And she has set her mind especially against my being a clergyman; I know that.", "게다가 제가 성직자가 되는 것을 특히나 단호하게 반대하고 있고요. 그건 저도 압니다."], ["But I can’t give her up.", "하지만 그녀를 포기할 수가 없어요."], ["I do think she cares about me. I saw Mrs.", "그녀가 저를 마음에 두고 있다고 정말로 믿어요."], ["Garth last night, and she said that Mary was staying at Lowick Rectory with Miss Farebrother.”", "어젯밤 가스 부인을 뵈었는데, 메리가 페어브러더 양과 함께 로윅 사제관에 머물고 있다고 하시더군요.\""]], [["“Yes, she is very kindly helping my sister.", "\"그래, 그녀가 내 누이를 친절히 도와주고 있다네."], ["Do you wish to go there?”", "자네는 그곳에 가 보고 싶은가?\""]], [["“No, I want to ask a great favor of you.", "\"아니요, 선생님께 큰 부탁을 드리고 싶어요."], ["I am ashamed to bother you in this way; but Mary might listen to what you said, if you mentioned the subject to her—I mean about my going into the Church.”", "이렇게 폐를 끼치는 게 면목 없지만, 선생님이 메리에게 그 이야기를 꺼내 주시면 그녀가 선생님 말씀에는 귀를 기울일 것 같아서요. 그러니까 제가 성직에 들어가는 일 말입니다.\""]], [["“That is rather a delicate task, my dear Fred.", "\"그건 꽤 미묘한 임무로구나, 프레드."], ["I shall have to presuppose your attachment to her; and to enter on the subject as you wish me to do, will be asking her to tell me whether she returns it.”", "자네가 그녀를 사모한다는 것을 전제로 삼아야 할 테고, 자네가 바라는 대로 그 이야기를 꺼낸다는 건 곧 그녀에게 그 마음에 화답하는지 어떤지를 말해 달라고 청하는 셈이 될 테니 말이야.\""]], [["“That is what I want her to tell you,”", "\"바로 그걸 그녀가 선생님께 말해 주기를 바라는 거예요.\""], ["said Fred, bluntly.", "프레드가 단도직입적으로 말했다."], ["“I don’t know what to do, unless I can get at her feeling.”", "\"그녀의 마음을 알아내지 못하면 저는 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“You mean that you would be guided by that as to your going into the Church?”", "\"성직에 들어가는 일을 두고 그것에 따라 결정하겠다는 말인가?\""]], [["“If Mary said she would never have me I might as well go wrong in one way as another.”", "\"메리가 절대 저를 받아 주지 않겠다고 하면, 어느 쪽으로 잘못되든 매한가지일 테니까요.\""]], [["“That is nonsense, Fred.", "\"그건 말도 안 되는 소리야, 프레드."], ["Men outlive their love, but they don’t outlive the consequences of their recklessness.”", "사람은 사랑은 견뎌 내고 살아남지만, 자기 무모함의 결과까지 견뎌 내며 살아남지는 못한다네.\""]], [["“Not my sort of love: I have never been without loving Mary.", "\"제 사랑은 그렇지 않아요. 저는 한 번도 메리를 사랑하지 않은 적이 없어요."], ["If I had to give her up, it would be like beginning to live on wooden legs.”", "만약 그녀를 포기해야 한다면, 그건 나무 의족을 짚고 살아가기 시작하는 것과 같을 거예요.\""]], [["“Will she not be hurt at my intrusion?”", "\"내가 끼어드는 것을 그녀가 언짢아하지는 않을까?\""]], [["“No, I feel sure she will not.", "\"아뇨, 그러지 않을 거라고 확신해요."], ["She respects you more than any one, and she would not put you off with fun as she does me.", "그녀는 누구보다 선생님을 존경하니, 저를 대할 때처럼 농담으로 얼버무리지는 않을 거예요."], ["Of course I could not have told any one else, or asked any one else to speak to her, but you.", "물론 선생님 말고 다른 누구에게도 이런 이야기를 할 수도, 그녀에게 말해 달라고 부탁할 수도 없었어요."], ["There is no one else who could be such a friend to both of us.”", "우리 두 사람 모두에게 이만한 친구가 되어 줄 사람은 달리 없으니까요.\""], ["Fred paused a moment, and then said, rather complainingly, “And she ought to acknowledge that I have worked in order to pass.", "프레드는 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 다소 푸념하듯 덧붙였다. \"그리고 그녀도 제가 시험에 통과하려고 애썼다는 걸 인정해 줘야 해요."], ["She ought to believe that I would exert myself for her sake.”", "제가 그녀를 위해서라면 스스로 노력하리라는 걸 믿어 줘야 한다고요.\""]], [["There was a moment’s silence before Mr. Farebrother laid down his work, and putting out his hand to Fred said—", "페어브러더 씨가 하던 일을 내려놓고 프레드에게 손을 내밀며 말하기까지 한순간의 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“Very well, my boy.", "\"좋아, 이 사람아."], ["I will do what you wish.”", "자네가 바라는 대로 해 주지.\""]], [["That very day Mr. Farebrother went to Lowick parsonage on the nag which he had just set up.", "바로 그날 페어브러더 씨는 막 새로 마련한 조랑말을 타고 로윅 사제관으로 향했다."], ["“Decidedly I am an old stalk,”", "\"확실히 나는 늙은 줄기로구나.\""], ["he thought, “the young growths are pushing me aside.”", "그는 생각했다. \"젊은 싹들이 나를 옆으로 밀어내고 있어.\""]], [["He found Mary in the garden gathering roses and sprinkling the petals on a sheet.", "그는 메리가 정원에서 장미를 따서 천 위에 꽃잎을 뿌리고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["The sun was low, and tall trees sent their shadows across the grassy walks where Mary was moving without bonnet or parasol. She did not observe Mr.", "해는 낮게 기울었고, 키 큰 나무들이 잔디 길 위로 그림자를 드리웠는데, 메리는 모자도 양산도 없이 그 길을 거닐고 있었다."], ["Farebrother’s approach along the grass, and had just stooped down to lecture a small black-and-tan terrier, which would persist in walking on the sheet and smelling at the rose-leaves as Mary sprinkled them.", "그녀는 잔디를 가로질러 다가오는 페어브러더 씨를 알아채지 못했고, 마침 몸을 숙여 작은 검정 갈색 테리어를 타이르던 참이었다. 메리가 꽃잎을 뿌리는 동안 그 개가 한사코 천 위를 걸어 다니며 장미 잎의 냄새를 맡으려 들었기 때문이다."], ["She took his fore-paws in one hand, and lifted up the forefinger of the other, while the dog wrinkled his brows and looked embarrassed.", "그녀는 한 손으로 개의 앞발을 잡고 다른 손의 집게손가락을 치켜들었고, 개는 이마를 찌푸린 채 난처한 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["“Fly, Fly, I am ashamed of you,”", "\"플라이, 플라이, 부끄러운 줄 알아.\""], ["Mary was saying in a grave contralto.", "메리가 묵직한 콘트랄토 음성으로 말하고 있었다."], ["“This is not becoming in a sensible dog; anybody would think you were a silly young gentleman.”", "\"분별 있는 개라면 이래선 못쓰지. 누가 보면 너를 어리석은 젊은 신사로 여기겠다.\""]], [["“You are unmerciful to young gentlemen, Miss Garth,”", "\"가스 양은 젊은 신사들에게 자비가 없으시군요.\""], ["said the Vicar, within two yards of her.", "사제가 그녀에게서 두 야드도 안 되는 곳에서 말했다."]], [["Mary started up and blushed.", "메리는 화들짝 일어서며 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“It always answers to reason with Fly,”", "\"플라이한테는 늘 타이르는 게 효과가 있어요.\""], ["she said, laughingly.", "그녀가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“But not with young gentlemen?”", "\"하지만 젊은 신사들에게는 안 그렇고요?\""]], [["“Oh, with some, I suppose; since some of them turn into excellent men.”", "\"오, 더러는 효과가 있겠지요. 개중에는 훌륭한 사람이 되는 이도 있으니까요.\""]], [["“I am glad of that admission, because I want at this very moment to interest you in a young gentleman.”", "\"그렇게 인정해 주시니 다행입니다. 다름 아니라 지금 이 순간 어떤 젊은 신사에게 관심을 가져 주십사 하고 부탁드리려던 참이거든요.\""]], [["“Not a silly one, I hope,”", "\"어리석은 사람은 아니길 바라요.\""], ["said Mary, beginning to pluck the roses again, and feeling her heart beat uncomfortably.", "메리는 다시 장미를 따기 시작하며 가슴이 거북할 만큼 두근거리는 것을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“No; though perhaps wisdom is not his strong point, but rather affection and sincerity.", "\"아닙니다. 다만 지혜가 그의 강점은 아닐지 모르지요."], ["However, wisdom lies more in those two qualities than people are apt to imagine.", "오히려 애정과 진실함이 그렇답니다. 하지만 사람들이 흔히 생각하는 것보다 지혜는 그 두 가지 자질 속에 더 많이 깃들어 있지요."], ["I hope you know by those marks what young gentleman I mean.”", "그 특징만으로도 내가 누구를 말하는지 아실 줄 압니다.\""]], [["“Yes, I think I do,”", "\"네, 알 것 같아요.\""], ["said Mary, bravely, her face getting more serious, and her hands cold; “it must be Fred Vincy.”", "메리가 용감하게 말했지만, 얼굴은 한층 진지해지고 두 손은 차가워졌다. \"프레드 빈시일 거예요.\""]], [["“He has asked me to consult you about his going into the Church.", "\"그가 자기 성직 진로에 관해 양에게 의논해 달라고 부탁했답니다."], ["I hope you will not think that I consented to take a liberty in promising to do so.”", "내가 그러겠다고 약속한 것이 주제넘은 짓이라고 여기지는 않으시길 바랍니다.\""]], [["“On the contrary, Mr. Farebrother,”", "\"오히려 그 반대예요, 페어브러더 씨.\""], ["said Mary, giving up the roses, and folding her arms, but unable to look up, “whenever you have anything to say to me I feel honored.”", "메리가 장미를 내려놓고 두 팔을 모아 잡았으나 차마 고개를 들지는 못한 채 말했다. \"선생님이 제게 하실 말씀이 있으시다면, 저는 언제나 영광으로 여겨요.\""]], [["“But before I enter on that question, let me just touch a point on which your father took me into confidence; by the way, it was that very evening on which I once before fulfilled a mission from Fred, just after he had gone to college. Mr. Garth told me what happened on the night of Featherstone’s death—how you refused to burn the will; and he said that you had some heart-prickings on that subject, because you had been the innocent means of hindering Fred from getting his ten thousand pounds. I have kept that in mind, and I have heard something that may relieve you on that score—may show you that no sin-offering is demanded from you there.”", "\"하지만 그 문제로 들어가기 전에, 양의 아버님께서 내게 털어놓으셨던 한 가지를 잠깐 짚고 넘어가겠습니다. 그러고 보니 그것은 바로 전에 한 번 프레드의 부탁을 전했던 그 저녁이었지요. 그가 막 대학으로 떠난 직후 말입니다. 가스 씨가 페더스톤이 죽던 날 밤에 일어난 일을 내게 말해 주셨어요. 양이 유언장 태우기를 거절한 일 말입니다. 그리고 그 일로 양이 다소 마음의 가책을 느꼈다고 하시더군요. 죄 없이 프레드가 만 파운드를 받지 못하게 막은 셈이 되었으니까요. 나는 그 일을 마음에 새겨 두고 있었는데, 그 점에서 양의 마음을 덜어 줄 만한 이야기를 들었습니다. 거기에는 양이 치러야 할 어떤 속죄의 제물도 요구되지 않는다는 것을 알려 드릴 수 있겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Farebrother paused a moment and looked at Mary.", "페어브러더 씨는 한순간 멈추고 메리를 바라보았다."], ["He meant to give Fred his full advantage, but it would be well, he thought, to clear her mind of any superstitions, such as women sometimes follow when they do a man the wrong of marrying him as an act of atonement.", "그는 프레드에게 충분히 유리하도록 해 줄 작정이었으나, 여자들이 이따금 빠지곤 하는 어떤 미신적인 생각에서 그녀의 마음을 풀어 주는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 여자가 속죄의 행위로 어떤 남자와 결혼하여 그에게 잘못을 저지를 때 따르는 그런 생각 말이다."], ["Mary’s cheeks had begun to burn a little, and she was mute.", "메리의 두 뺨이 약간 달아오르기 시작했고, 그녀는 말이 없었다."]], [["“I mean, that your action made no real difference to Fred’s lot.", "\"내 말은, 양의 행동이 프레드의 운명에는 실제로 아무런 차이도 만들지 않았다는 겁니다."], ["I find that the first will would not have been legally good after the burning of the last; it would not have stood if it had been disputed, and you may be sure it would have been disputed.", "알아보니 맨 마지막 유언장을 태웠다 해도 첫 번째 유언장은 법적으로 효력이 없었을 거예요. 이의가 제기되었더라면 그것은 인정받지 못했을 텐데, 분명 이의가 제기되었으리라는 점은 양도 확신해도 좋습니다."], ["So, on that score, you may feel your mind free.”", "그러니 그 점에서는 마음을 자유로이 가지셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, Mr. Farebrother,”", "\"고맙습니다, 페어브러더 씨.\""], ["said Mary, earnestly.", "메리가 진심으로 말했다."], ["“I am grateful to you for remembering my feelings.”", "\"제 마음을 헤아려 주셔서 감사드려요.\""]], [["“Well, now I may go on.", "\"자, 이제 본론을 계속하지요."], ["Fred, you know, has taken his degree.", "알다시피 프레드는 학위를 받았습니다."], ["He has worked his way so far, and now the question is, what is he to do?", "여기까지 스스로 헤쳐 왔는데, 이제 문제는 그가 무엇을 할 것인가입니다."], ["That question is so difficult that he is inclined to follow his father’s wishes and enter the Church, though you know better than I do that he was quite set against that formerly.", "그 문제가 워낙 어려운 터라, 그는 아버지의 뜻을 따라 성직에 들어가는 쪽으로 기울고 있어요. 양은 나보다 더 잘 알겠지만 그가 예전에는 그 길을 한사코 반대했었지요."], ["I have questioned him on the subject, and I confess I see no insuperable objection to his being a clergyman, as things go.", "내가 그에게 그 문제를 캐물어 보았는데, 솔직히 요즘 형편으로 보면 그가 성직자가 되는 데 넘을 수 없는 반대 사유는 보이지 않습니다."], ["He says that he could turn his mind to doing his best in that vocation, on one condition.", "그는 한 가지 조건만 갖춰진다면 그 천직에서 최선을 다하는 데 마음을 쏟을 수 있다고 합니다."], ["If that condition were fulfilled I would do my utmost in helping Fred on.", "만약 그 조건이 채워진다면, 나는 프레드를 돕는 데 온 힘을 기울일 거예요."], ["After a time—not, of course, at first—he might be with me as my curate, and he would have so much to do that his stipend would be nearly what I used to get as vicar.", "얼마간 시간이 지나면 — 물론 처음부터는 아니지요 — 그가 내 부사제로 함께 일할 수 있을 테고, 할 일이 워낙 많아서 그의 봉급이 내가 사제로 받던 것과 거의 맞먹게 될 겁니다."], ["But I repeat that there is a condition without which all this good cannot come to pass.", "하지만 거듭 말하지만, 이 모든 좋은 일이 이루어지려면 반드시 갖춰져야 할 한 가지 조건이 있어요."], ["He has opened his heart to me, Miss Garth, and asked me to plead for him.", "그가 내게 마음을 열어 보였습니다, 가스 양, 그러고는 자기를 위해 변호해 달라고 부탁했지요."], ["The condition lies entirely in your feeling.”", "그 조건은 전적으로 양의 마음에 달려 있습니다.\""]], [["Mary looked so much moved, that he said after a moment, “Let us walk a little;”", "메리가 무척 마음이 흔들린 듯 보이자, 그는 한순간 뒤에 \"조금 걸읍시다\" 하고 말했고, 둘이 걷기 시작하자 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["and when they were walking he added, “To speak quite plainly, Fred will not take any course which would lessen the chance that you would consent to be his wife; but with that prospect, he will try his best at anything you approve.”", "\"아주 솔직히 말하자면, 프레드는 양이 자기 아내가 되어 줄 가망을 줄일 만한 길은 결코 택하지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 그런 가망만 있다면, 그는 양이 인정하는 일이라면 무엇이든 최선을 다하려 할 거예요.\""]], [["“I cannot possibly say that I will ever be his wife, Mr.", "\"제가 언젠가 그분의 아내가 되겠다고는 도저히 말할 수 없어요, 페어브러더 씨."], ["Farebrother: but I certainly never will be his wife if he becomes a clergyman.", "하지만 그분이 성직자가 된다면 결코 그분의 아내가 되지 않을 거예요."], ["What you say is most generous and kind; I don’t mean for a moment to correct your judgment.", "선생님 말씀은 더없이 너그럽고 친절하세요. 저는 한순간도 선생님의 판단을 바로잡으려는 게 아니에요."], ["It is only that I have my girlish, mocking way of looking at things,”", "다만 제게는 소녀답게 비꼬는 식으로 사물을 바라보는 버릇이 있어서요.\""], ["said Mary, with a returning sparkle of playfulness in her answer which only made its modesty more charming.", "메리가 대답에 다시 장난기 어린 반짝임을 띠고 말했는데, 그것이 오히려 그 겸손함을 한층 사랑스럽게 만들었다."]], [["“He wishes me to report exactly what you think,”", "\"그가 바라기로는, 양이 어떻게 생각하는지 그대로 전해 달라더군요.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother.", "페어브러더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I could not love a man who is ridiculous,”", "\"저는 우스꽝스러운 남자는 사랑할 수 없어요.\""], ["said Mary, not choosing to go deeper.", "메리는 더 깊이 들어가지 않기로 하고 말했다."], ["“Fred has sense and knowledge enough to make him respectable, if he likes, in some good worldly business, but I can never imagine him preaching and exhorting, and pronouncing blessings, and praying by the sick, without feeling as if I were looking at a caricature.", "\"프레드는 마음만 먹으면 어떤 번듯한 세속적인 일에서 존경받을 만한 사람이 될 분별과 지식을 갖추고 있어요. 하지만 그분이 설교를 하고, 권면을 하고, 축복을 선언하고, 병자 곁에서 기도하는 모습은, 무슨 풍자화를 보고 있는 듯한 기분이 들지 않고서는 도무지 상상이 안 돼요."], ["His being a clergyman would be only for gentility’s sake, and I think there is nothing more contemptible than such imbecile gentility.", "그분이 성직자가 되는 건 오직 체면 때문일 텐데, 저는 그런 얼빠진 체면치레만큼 경멸스러운 게 없다고 생각해요."], ["I used to think that of Mr.", "크라우스 씨를 보면서 늘 그런 생각을 했어요."], ["Crowse, with his empty face and neat umbrella, and mincing little speeches.", "멍한 얼굴에 말끔한 우산을 들고, 점잔 빼며 짤막한 말을 늘어놓는 그 사람 말이에요."], ["What right have such men to represent Christianity—as if it were an institution for getting up idiots genteelly—as if—”", "그런 사람들에게 무슨 자격이 있어서 기독교를 대표한단 말인가요. 마치 기독교가 멍청이들을 점잖게 길러 내는 기관이라도 되는 양, 마치—\""], ["Mary checked herself.", "메리는 말을 멈췄다."], ["She had been carried along as if she had been speaking to Fred instead of Mr. Farebrother.", "그녀는 마치 페어브러더 씨가 아니라 프레드에게 말하고 있는 듯 격해져 버렸던 것이다."]], [["“Young women are severe: they don’t feel the stress of action as men do, though perhaps I ought to make you an exception there.", "\"젊은 여인들은 가혹하군요. 남자들처럼 행동의 압박을 느끼지 않으니까요."], ["But you don’t put Fred Vincy on so low a level as that?”", "하기야 그 점에서는 양을 예외로 두어야 할지도 모르겠습니다. 하지만 프레드 빈시를 그토록 낮은 수준에 놓는 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“No, indeed, he has plenty of sense, but I think he would not show it as a clergyman.", "\"네, 정말 그렇지 않아요. 그분은 분별이 넘치는 사람이에요."], ["He would be a piece of professional affectation.”", "다만 성직자로서는 그것을 드러내지 못할 것 같아요. 직업적인 가식 덩어리가 되고 말 거예요.\""]], [["“Then the answer is quite decided.", "\"그렇다면 대답은 아주 분명하군요."], ["As a clergyman he could have no hope?”", "성직자로서는 아무런 희망이 없다는 거지요?\""]], [["Mary shook her head.", "메리는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“But if he braved all the difficulties of getting his bread in some other way—will you give him the support of hope?", "\"하지만 그가 다른 길로 생계를 꾸리는 온갖 어려움을 무릅쓴다면 — 그에게 희망이라는 힘을 주시겠습니까?"], ["May he count on winning you?”", "그가 양의 마음을 얻으리라 기대해도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“I think Fred ought not to need telling again what I have already said to him,”", "\"제가 이미 그분에게 했던 말을 프레드에게 또 일러 줄 필요는 없다고 생각해요.\""], ["Mary answered, with a slight resentment in her manner.", "메리가 다소 언짢은 기색으로 대답했다."], ["“I mean that he ought not to put such questions until he has done something worthy, instead of saying that he could do it.”", "\"제 말은, 그분이 무언가 할 수 있다고 말만 할 게 아니라 무언가 가치 있는 일을 해낼 때까지는 그런 질문을 해서는 안 된다는 거예요.\""]], [["Mr.", "페어브러더 씨는 일 분 남짓 말이 없었고, 이윽고 두 사람이 잔디 길 끝의 단풍나무 그늘 아래에서 발걸음을 돌려 멈춰 섰을 때 이렇게 말했다."], ["Farebrother was silent for a minute or more, and then, as they turned and paused under the shadow of a maple at the end of a grassy walk, said, “I understand that you resist any attempt to fetter you, but either your feeling for Fred Vincy excludes your entertaining another attachment, or it does not: either he may count on your remaining single until he shall have earned your hand, or he may in any case be disappointed.", "\"양이 어떤 식으로든 속박당하는 데 저항한다는 건 이해합니다. 하지만 양이 프레드 빈시에게 품은 감정이 다른 사람에게 마음을 두는 것을 가로막든가, 아니면 그렇지 않든가 둘 중 하나겠지요."], ["Pardon me, Mary—you know I used to catechise you under that name—but when the state of a woman’s affections touches the happiness of another life—of more lives than one—I think it would be the nobler course for her to be perfectly direct and open.”", "그가 양의 손을 얻을 자격을 갖출 때까지 양이 독신으로 지내리라 기대해도 되든가, 아니면 어떤 경우에도 그가 실망하게 되든가 말입니다. 용서하세요, 메리 — 알다시피 나는 예전에 그 이름으로 양에게 교리 문답을 가르쳤지요 — 하지만 한 여인의 마음 상태가 다른 한 생명의 행복에, 그것도 하나 이상의 생명의 행복에 닿아 있을 때는, 그 여인이 완전히 솔직하고 숨김없이 처신하는 것이 더 고결한 길이라고 생각합니다.\""]], [["Mary in her turn was silent, wondering not at Mr.", "이번에는 메리가 침묵했는데, 페어브러더 씨의 태도가 아니라 그 어조에 의아함을 느꼈다."], ["Farebrother’s manner but at his tone, which had a grave restrained emotion in it.", "거기에는 진지하게 억눌린 감정이 담겨 있었기 때문이다."], ["When the strange idea flashed across her that his words had reference to himself, she was incredulous, and ashamed of entertaining it.", "그의 말이 그 자신을 가리키는 것일지도 모른다는 야릇한 생각이 문득 머리를 스쳤을 때, 그녀는 믿기지 않았고 그런 생각을 품은 것이 부끄러웠다. 그녀는 프레드 말고는 어떤 남자가 자기를 사랑할 수 있으리라고 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["She had never thought that any man could love her except Fred, who had espoused her with the umbrella ring, when she wore socks and little strapped shoes; still less that she could be of any importance to Mr.", "프레드는 그녀가 양말에 끈 달린 작은 신을 신던 시절에 우산 고리로 그녀와 약혼한 사이였다. 더구나 자기가 좁은 교제 범위 안에서 가장 영리한 남자인 페어브러더 씨에게 무슨 중요한 존재일 수 있으리라고는 더더욱 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["Farebrother, the cleverest man in her narrow circle. She had only time to feel that all this was hazy and perhaps illusory; but one thing was clear and determined—her answer.", "이 모든 것이 어렴풋하고 어쩌면 헛된 것이라고 느낄 겨를밖에 없었으나, 한 가지만은 분명하고 확고했다 — 바로 그녀의 대답이었다."]], [["“Since you think it my duty, Mr.", "\"선생님이 그것을 제 도리라고 여기시니, 말씀드리겠어요, 페어브러더 씨."], ["Farebrother, I will tell you that I have too strong a feeling for Fred to give him up for any one else.", "저는 프레드에게 너무나 강한 감정을 품고 있어서 그분을 다른 누구를 위해서도 포기할 수 없어요."], ["I should never be quite happy if I thought he was unhappy for the loss of me.", "그분이 저를 잃어 불행하다고 생각하면 저는 결코 온전히 행복할 수 없을 거예요."], ["It has taken such deep root in me—my gratitude to him for always loving me best, and minding so much if I hurt myself, from the time when we were very little.", "그 감정은 제 안에 너무도 깊이 뿌리내렸어요 — 아주 어렸을 적부터 그분이 늘 저를 가장 사랑해 주고, 제가 다칠까 봐 그토록 마음 써 준 것에 대한 고마움 말이에요."], ["I cannot imagine any new feeling coming to make that weaker.", "그 마음을 약하게 만들 새로운 감정이 찾아오리라고는 상상조차 할 수 없어요."], ["I should like better than anything to see him worthy of every one’s respect.", "저는 무엇보다도 그분이 모두에게 존경받을 만한 사람이 되는 것을 보고 싶어요."], ["But please tell him I will not promise to marry him till then: I should shame and grieve my father and mother.", "하지만 부디 그분에게 전해 주세요, 그때까지는 그분과 결혼하겠다고 약속하지 않겠다고요. 그러면 저는 아버지와 어머니를 부끄럽게 하고 슬프게 할 거예요."], ["He is free to choose some one else.”", "그분은 자유롭게 다른 사람을 택해도 좋아요.\""]], [["“Then I have fulfilled my commission thoroughly,” said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 나는 내 임무를 빈틈없이 완수한 셈이군요.\""], ["Farebrother, putting out his hand to Mary, “and I shall ride back to Middlemarch forthwith.", "페어브러더 씨가 메리에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"그러니 곧장 미들마치로 말을 달려 돌아가겠습니다."], ["With this prospect before him, we shall get Fred into the right niche somehow, and I hope I shall live to join your hands.", "그에게 이런 가망이 놓여 있으니, 우리는 어떻게든 프레드를 알맞은 자리에 앉히게 될 거고, 나는 두 사람의 손을 맺어 주는 자리에 함께할 만큼 오래 살았으면 합니다."], ["God bless you!”", "신의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["“Oh, please stay, and let me give you some tea,”", "\"오, 부디 더 계세요. 차라도 한잔 드리게 해 주세요.\""], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["Her eyes filled with tears, for something indefinable, something like the resolute suppression of a pain in Mr.", "그녀의 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐는데, 페어브러더 씨의 태도에 깃든 무어라 형언할 수 없는 것, 마치 고통을 단호히 억누르는 듯한 무언가가 그녀를 문득 비참하게 만들었던 것이다."], ["Farebrother’s manner, made her feel suddenly miserable, as she had once felt when she saw her father’s hands trembling in a moment of trouble.", "언젠가 곤경에 처한 순간 아버지의 손이 떨리는 것을 보았을 때 느꼈던 그런 비참함이었다."]], [["“No, my dear, no.", "\"아니요, 아가씨, 됐습니다."], ["I must get back.”", "돌아가야 해요.\""]], [["In three minutes the Vicar was on horseback again, having gone magnanimously through a duty much harder than the renunciation of whist, or even than the writing of penitential meditations.", "삼 분 뒤 사제는 다시 말 위에 올라 있었다. 휘스트를 단념하는 것보다, 아니 참회의 명상록을 써 내려가는 것보다 훨씬 더 힘든 의무를 너그러이 치러 낸 채로."]], [["# CHAPTER LIII.", "# CHAPTER LIII."]], [["It is but a shallow haste which concludeth insincerity from what outsiders call inconsistency—putting a dead mechanism of “ifs”", "외부인들이 일관성 없음이라 부르는 것에서 곧장 진실하지 못함을 끌어내는 것은 한낱 얄팍한 성급함일 뿐이다."], ["and “therefores” for the living myriad of hidden suckers whereby the belief and the conduct are wrought into mutual sustainment.", "신앙과 행위가 서로를 떠받치도록 엮어 내는, 살아 있는 무수한 숨은 빨판들을, \"만일\"과 \"그러므로\"라는 죽은 기계 장치로 대체해 버리는 짓이니까."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode, when he was hoping to acquire a new interest in Lowick, had naturally had an especial wish that the new clergyman should be one whom he thoroughly approved; and he believed it to be a chastisement and admonition directed to his own shortcomings and those of the nation at large, that just about the time when he came in possession of the deeds which made him the proprietor of Stone Court, Mr.", "벌스트로드 씨는 로윅에 새로운 이권을 얻기를 바라던 무렵, 새로 부임할 성직자가 자신이 전적으로 인정할 수 있는 인물이기를 각별히 소망한 것이 당연했다."], ["Farebrother “read himself” into the quaint little church and preached his first sermon to the congregation of farmers, laborers, and village artisans.", "그리고 그는 자신을 스톤 코트의 소유주로 만들어 줄 권리 증서를 손에 넣은 바로 그 시기에, 페어브러더 씨가 그 고풍스러운 작은 교회에 \"취임 선서를 읽고\""], ["It was not that Mr. Bulstrode intended to frequent Lowick Church or to reside at Stone Court for a good while to come: he had bought the excellent farm and fine homestead simply as a retreat which he might gradually enlarge as to the land and beautify as to the dwelling, until it should be conducive to the divine glory that he should enter on it as a residence, partially withdrawing from his present exertions in the administration of business, and throwing more conspicuously on the side of Gospel truth the weight of local landed proprietorship, which Providence might increase by unforeseen occasions of purchase.", "농부와 일꾼, 마을 장인 들로 이루어진 신도들 앞에서 첫 설교를 한 것이, 자신의 결점과 나라 전체의 결점을 향한 징계이자 훈계라고 믿었다."], ["A strong leading in this direction seemed to have been given in the surprising facility of getting Stone Court, when every one had expected that Mr.", "벌스트로드 씨가 로윅 교회에 자주 다니거나 한동안 스톤 코트에 거주할 의도가 있었던 것은 아니다. 그가 그 훌륭한 농장과 멋진 저택을 사들인 것은 그저 은거지로 삼아, 토지는 차츰 넓히고 거처는 아름답게 가꾸어, 마침내 그곳을 거처로 삼는 것이 신의 영광에 이바지하게 되었을 때, 현재의 사업 운영에 들이는 노고에서 어느 정도 물러나, 섭리가 예기치 못한 매입의 기회로 늘려 줄지도 모를 지역 토지 소유권의 무게를 복음의 진리 쪽에 더 두드러지게 실어 놓으려는 뜻에서였다."], ["Rigg Featherstone would have clung to it as the Garden of Eden.", "모두가 리그 페더스톤 씨라면 에덴동산처럼 그곳에 매달리리라 예상했건만, 뜻밖에도 손쉽게 스톤 코트를 손에 넣게 된 데서 이 방향으로 강한 인도가 있었던 것처럼 보였다."], ["That was what poor old Peter himself had expected; having often, in imagination, looked up through the sods above him, and, unobstructed by perspective, seen his frog-faced legatee enjoying the fine old place to the perpetual surprise and disappointment of other survivors.", "그것은 가엾은 노인 피터 자신이 예상했던 바이기도 했다. 그는 상상 속에서 자기 위에 덮인 잔디를 올려다보며, 원근에 가로막히지 않고서, 개구리 상판을 한 그 수유자가 다른 생존자들의 끝없는 놀라움과 실망 속에서 그 멋진 옛집을 즐기는 모습을 자주 그려 보았던 것이다."]], [["But how little we know what would make paradise for our neighbors!", "하지만 우리는 무엇이 이웃에게 낙원이 될지 얼마나 모르는가!"], ["We judge from our own desires, and our neighbors themselves are not always open enough even to throw out a hint of theirs.", "우리는 자신의 욕망으로 판단할 뿐이며, 이웃들 자신도 자기 욕망의 기미조차 드러낼 만큼 늘 솔직하지는 않다."], ["The cool and judicious Joshua Rigg had not allowed his parent to perceive that Stone Court was anything less than the chief good in his estimation, and he had certainly wished to call it his own.", "냉정하고 분별 있는 조슈아 리그는 부친에게 스톤 코트가 자기 평가에서 최고선보다 못한 무엇이라는 낌새를 결코 알아채게 하지 않았고, 분명 그곳을 제 것이라 부르고 싶어 했다. 그러나 워런 헤이스팅스가 금을 보며 데일스퍼드를 사들일 생각을 했듯, 조슈아 리그는 스톤 코트를 보며 금을 사들일 생각을 했다."], ["But as Warren Hastings looked at gold and thought of buying Daylesford, so Joshua Rigg looked at Stone Court and thought of buying gold.", "그는 제 최고선에 대해 매우 또렷하고 강렬한 환상을 품고 있었으니, 물려받은 왕성한 탐욕이 환경의 힘으로 특별한 형태를 띠게 된 것이었다. 그의 최고선이란 환전상이 되는 것이었다."], ["He had a very distinct and intense vision of his chief good, the vigorous greed which he had inherited having taken a special form by dint of circumstance: and his chief good was to be a moneychanger.", "항구 도시에서 심부름꾼 소년으로 일하던 가장 어린 시절부터, 그는 다른 소년들이 제과점 진열창을 들여다보듯 환전상의 창문을 들여다보았다. 그 매혹은 차츰 깊고 특별한 열정으로 자라났다."], ["From his earliest employment as an errand-boy in a seaport, he had looked through the windows of the moneychangers as other boys look through the windows of the pastry-cooks; the fascination had wrought itself gradually into a deep special passion; he meant, when he had property, to do many things, one of them being to marry a genteel young person; but these were all accidents and joys that imagination could dispense with.", "그는 재산이 생기면 여러 일을 할 작정이었고, 그중 하나는 점잖은 젊은 여자와 결혼하는 것이었다. 그러나 이런 것들은 모두 상상이 없이도 견딜 수 있는 우연이자 즐거움일 뿐이었다."], ["The one joy after which his soul thirsted was to have a money-changer’s shop on a much-frequented quay, to have locks all round him of which he held the keys, and to look sublimely cool as he handled the breeding coins of all nations, while helpless Cupidity looked at him enviously from the other side of an iron lattice.", "그의 영혼이 갈망하는 단 하나의 기쁨은, 사람들이 붐비는 부두에 환전상 가게를 차려, 자기가 열쇠를 쥔 자물쇠들로 둘러싸인 채, 만국의 새끼 치는 동전들을 다루며 숭고할 만큼 냉정한 표정을 짓고, 그동안 무력한 탐욕이 철 격자 너머에서 부러운 눈으로 자기를 바라보게 하는 것이었다."], ["The strength of that passion had been a power enabling him to master all the knowledge necessary to gratify it.", "그 열정의 힘은 그것을 충족시키는 데 필요한 모든 지식을 익히게 해 주는 동력이었다."], ["And when others were thinking that he had settled at Stone Court for life, Joshua himself was thinking that the moment now was not far off when he should settle on the North Quay with the best appointments in safes and locks.", "그래서 남들이 그가 스톤 코트에 평생 자리 잡았다고 여길 때, 조슈아 자신은 이제 머지않아 최고급 금고와 자물쇠를 갖추고 노스 키에 자리 잡을 순간이 오리라 생각하고 있었다."]], [["Enough. We are concerned with looking at Joshua Rigg’s sale of his land from Mr.", "이만하면 됐다."], ["Bulstrode’s point of view, and he interpreted it as a cheering dispensation conveying perhaps a sanction to a purpose which he had for some time entertained without external encouragement; he interpreted it thus, but not too confidently, offering up his thanksgiving in guarded phraseology.", "우리의 관심사는 조슈아 리그의 토지 매각을 벌스트로드 씨의 관점에서 바라보는 것이며, 그는 이를 한동안 외부의 격려 없이 품어 온 어떤 목적에 어쩌면 승인을 전하는, 고무적인 섭리의 처분으로 해석했다."], ["His doubts did not arise from the possible relations of the event to Joshua Rigg’s destiny, which belonged to the unmapped regions not taken under the providential government, except perhaps in an imperfect colonial way; but they arose from reflecting that this dispensation too might be a chastisement for himself, as Mr.", "그는 그렇게 해석했으나 지나치게 자신만만하지는 않았고, 조심스러운 어구로 감사를 올렸다. 그의 의심은 이 사건이 조슈아 리그의 운명과 맺을지도 모를 관계에서 비롯된 것은 아니었으니, 그 운명은 어쩌면 불완전한 식민지적 방식으로나 모를까, 섭리의 통치 아래 들지 않은 미답의 영역에 속한 것이었다."], ["Farebrother’s induction to the living clearly was.", "그의 의심은 페어브러더 씨가 성직록에 임명된 일이 분명 그러했듯, 이 처분 역시 자신에 대한 징계일지도 모른다는 생각에서 비롯되었다."]], [["This was not what Mr.", "이것은 벌스트로드 씨가 누군가를 속이려고 한 말이 아니었다."], ["Bulstrode said to any man for the sake of deceiving him: it was what he said to himself—it was as genuinely his mode of explaining events as any theory of yours may be, if you happen to disagree with him.", "그것은 그가 스스로에게 한 말이었다. 그것은, 당신이 그와 의견을 달리한다 해도, 당신의 어떤 이론 못지않게 진정으로 그가 사건을 설명하는 방식이었다."], ["For the egoism which enters into our theories does not affect their sincerity; rather, the more our egoism is satisfied, the more robust is our belief.", "우리의 이론에 끼어드는 이기심이 그 이론의 진실성을 해치는 것은 아니기 때문이다. 오히려 우리의 이기심이 충족될수록 우리의 신념은 더욱 굳건해진다."]], [["However, whether for sanction or for chastisement, Mr. Bulstrode, hardly fifteen months after the death of Peter Featherstone, had become the proprietor of Stone Court, and what Peter would say “if he were worthy to know,”", "어쨌거나, 승인을 위해서든 징계를 위해서든, 벌스트로드 씨는 피터 페더스톤이 죽은 지 채 열다섯 달이 지나지 않아 스톤 코트의 소유주가 되었고, 피터가 \"알 자격이 있다면\""], ["had become an inexhaustible and consolatory subject of conversation to his disappointed relatives.", "무슨 말을 할까 하는 것은 실망한 친척들에게 끝없는 위안의 화젯거리가 되었다."], ["The tables were now turned on that dear brother departed, and to contemplate the frustration of his cunning by the superior cunning of things in general was a cud of delight to Solomon. Mrs.", "이제 그 사랑하는 고인이 된 형제에게 형세가 뒤바뀐 셈이었고, 그의 교활함이 만물 전반의 더 우월한 교활함에 좌절되는 모습을 곱씹는 것은 솔로몬에게 되새김질의 즐거움이었다."], ["Waule had a melancholy triumph in the proof that it did not answer to make false Featherstones and cut off the genuine; and Sister Martha receiving the news in the Chalky Flats said, “Dear, dear!", "월 부인은 가짜 페더스톤을 만들고 진짜를 잘라 낸 짓이 헛수고였다는 증거에서 우울한 승리감을 맛보았다. 그리고 초크 플래츠에서 그 소식을 받은 마사 누이는 이렇게 말했다."], ["then the Almighty could have been none so pleased with the almshouses after all.”", "\"저런, 저런! 그러니 전능하신 하느님께서도 결국 그 양로원이 그리 마음에 드시지는 않았던 게로구나.\""]], [["Affectionate Mrs. Bulstrode was particularly glad of the advantage which her husband’s health was likely to get from the purchase of Stone Court.", "다정한 벌스트로드 부인은 스톤 코트 매입으로 남편의 건강이 얻게 될 이점이 특히나 기뻤다."], ["Few days passed without his riding thither and looking over some part of the farm with the bailiff, and the evenings were delicious in that quiet spot, when the new hay-ricks lately set up were sending forth odors to mingle with the breath of the rich old garden.", "그가 그곳까지 말을 타고 가서 관리인과 함께 농장 한구석을 둘러보지 않는 날이 드물었고, 새로 쌓아 올린 건초 더미가 풍요로운 옛 정원의 숨결과 어우러질 향기를 내뿜는 그 고요한 곳의 저녁은 감미로웠다."], ["One evening, while the sun was still above the horizon and burning in golden lamps among the great walnut boughs, Mr. Bulstrode was pausing on horseback outside the front gate waiting for Caleb Garth, who had met him by appointment to give an opinion on a question of stable drainage, and was now advising the bailiff in the rick-yard.", "어느 날 저녁, 해가 아직 지평선 위에서 커다란 호두나무 가지들 사이로 황금 등불처럼 타오르고 있을 때, 벌스트로드 씨는 정문 밖에서 말을 탄 채 멈춰, 약속에 따라 마구간 배수 문제에 대한 의견을 주러 와서는 지금 건초 마당에서 관리인에게 조언을 하고 있는 케일럽 가스를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode was conscious of being in a good spiritual frame and more than usually serene, under the influence of his innocent recreation.", "벌스트로드 씨는 자신이 좋은 영적 상태에 있으며, 그 무해한 휴양의 영향으로 평소보다 한결 평온하다고 의식하고 있었다."], ["He was doctrinally convinced that there was a total absence of merit in himself; but that doctrinal conviction may be held without pain when the sense of demerit does not take a distinct shape in memory and revive the tingling of shame or the pang of remorse.", "그는 교리상 자기 안에 공덕이 전혀 없다고 확신하고 있었다. 그러나 그 교리상의 확신은, 죄의식이 기억 속에서 뚜렷한 형태를 띠며 수치의 따가움이나 회한의 아픔을 되살리지 않는 한, 고통 없이 품을 수 있는 것이다."], ["Nay, it may be held with intense satisfaction when the depth of our sinning is but a measure for the depth of forgiveness, and a clenching proof that we are peculiar instruments of the divine intention.", "아니, 우리가 지은 죄의 깊이가 다만 용서의 깊이를 재는 척도가 되고, 우리가 신의 의도가 쓰는 특별한 도구임을 못 박는 증거가 될 때, 그 확신은 강렬한 만족과 함께 품을 수도 있는 것이다."], ["The memory has as many moods as the temper, and shifts its scenery like a diorama.", "기억은 기질만큼이나 많은 분위기를 지니며, 디오라마처럼 그 배경을 바꾼다."], ["At this moment Mr. Bulstrode felt as if the sunshine were all one with that of far-off evenings when he was a very young man and used to go out preaching beyond Highbury.", "이 순간 벌스트로드 씨는 이 햇살이 자신이 아주 젊은 시절 하이버리 너머로 설교하러 나가곤 하던 머나먼 옛 저녁의 햇살과 온전히 하나인 것처럼 느꼈다."], ["And he would willingly have had that service of exhortation in prospect now.", "그래서 그는 지금 그 권면의 봉사를 기꺼이 눈앞에 두고 싶었다."], ["The texts were there still, and so was his own facility in expounding them.", "성경 구절들은 여전히 거기 있었고, 그것을 풀어내는 그 자신의 능숙함도 여전했다."], ["His brief reverie was interrupted by the return of Caleb Garth, who also was on horseback, and was just shaking his bridle before starting, when he exclaimed—", "그 짧은 몽상은 역시 말을 타고 막 떠나려고 고삐를 흔들던 케일럽 가스가 돌아오면서 깨졌으니, 그가 이렇게 외쳤던 것이다."]], [["“Bless my heart!", "\"이런, 맙소사!"], ["what’s this fellow in black coming along the lane?", "저 길로 오는 검은 옷 입은 사내는 누구지?"], ["He’s like one of those men one sees about after the races.”", "경마 끝나고 어슬렁거리는 그런 부류 같은데.\""]], [["Mr. Bulstrode turned his horse and looked along the lane, but made no reply.", "벌스트로드 씨는 말을 돌려 길을 따라 바라보았으나 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["The comer was our slight acquaintance Mr. Raffles, whose appearance presented no other change than such as was due to a suit of black and a crape hat-band.", "다가오는 사내는 우리가 살짝 아는 래플스 씨였는데, 그 모습은 검은 정장과 검은 상장 모자 띠 말고는 달라진 데가 없었다."], ["He was within three yards of the horseman now, and they could see the flash of recognition in his face as he whirled his stick upward, looking all the while at Mr. Bulstrode, and at last exclaiming:—", "이제 그는 말 탄 사람들로부터 삼 야드 안에 들어와 있었고, 그들은 그가 지팡이를 위로 빙 돌리면서 줄곧 벌스트로드 씨를 바라보다가 마침내 이렇게 외칠 때, 그의 얼굴에 떠오르는 알아봤다는 빛을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“By Jove, Nick, it’s you!", "\"이런 세상에, 닉, 너로구나!"], ["I couldn’t be mistaken, though the five-and-twenty years have played old Boguy with us both!", "잘못 봤을 리가 없지, 이십오 년 세월이 우리 둘 다 늙은 마귀처럼 갉아먹어 놨지만 말이야!"], ["How are you, eh?", "어떻게 지냈나, 응?"], ["you didn’t expect to see me here.", "여기서 나를 보게 될 줄은 몰랐겠지."], ["Come, shake us by the hand.”", "자, 악수나 한번 하세.\""], ["To say that Mr. Raffles’ manner was rather excited would be only one mode of saying that it was evening.", "래플스 씨의 태도가 다소 들떠 있었다고 말하는 것은, 그저 때가 저녁이었다고 말하는 한 가지 방식일 뿐이리라."], ["Caleb Garth could see that there was a moment of struggle and hesitation in Mr. Bulstrode, but it ended in his putting out his hand coldly to Raffles and saying—", "케일럽 가스는 벌스트로드 씨 안에 한순간의 갈등과 망설임이 있는 것을 볼 수 있었으나, 그것은 결국 그가 래플스에게 차갑게 손을 내밀며 이렇게 말하는 것으로 끝났다."]], [["“I did not indeed expect to see you in this remote country place.”", "\"이 외진 시골에서 자네를 만나리라곤 정말 예상치 못했네.\""]], [["“Well, it belongs to a stepson of mine,”", "\"뭐, 이곳은 내 의붓아들 소유라네.\""], ["said Raffles, adjusting himself in a swaggering attitude.", "래플스가 거들먹거리는 자세를 취하며 말했다. \"전에 그 애를 보러 여기 온 적이 있지."], ["“I came to see him here before.", "자네를 보고는 그리 놀라지 않았네, 이 친구야, 편지를 한 장 주웠거든."], ["I’m not so surprised at seeing you, old fellow, because I picked up a letter—what you may call a providential thing.", "말하자면 섭리라고 할 만한 일이지. 그래도 자네를 만나다니 어지간히 운이 좋군."], ["It’s uncommonly fortunate I met you, though; for I don’t care about seeing my stepson: he’s not affectionate, and his poor mother’s gone now.", "사실 난 의붓아들 보는 데는 관심도 없으니까. 그 애는 다정하지가 않고, 이제 그 가엾은 어미도 세상을 떠났으니."], ["To tell the truth, I came out of love to you, Nick: I came to get your address, for—look here!”", "솔직히 말하면 난 자네가 그리워서 온 걸세, 닉. 자네 주소를 알아보려고 왔지."], ["Raffles drew a crumpled paper from his pocket.", "이것 좀 보게!\" 래플스가 주머니에서 구겨진 종이를 꺼냈다."]], [["Almost any other man than Caleb Garth might have been tempted to linger on the spot for the sake of hearing all he could about a man whose acquaintance with Bulstrode seemed to imply passages in the banker’s life so unlike anything that was known of him in Middlemarch that they must have the nature of a secret to pique curiosity.", "케일럽 가스가 아닌 다른 사람이었다면 거의 누구라도 그 자리에 머물며, 벌스트로드와의 교분이 미들마치에서 알려진 어떤 것과도 너무나 다른, 호기심을 자극하는 비밀의 성격을 띨 수밖에 없는, 은행가 인생의 어떤 일들을 암시하는 듯한 그 사내에 관해 들을 수 있는 모든 것을 듣고 싶은 유혹에 빠졌을지도 모른다."], ["But Caleb was peculiar: certain human tendencies which are commonly strong were almost absent from his mind; and one of these was curiosity about personal affairs.", "그러나 케일럽은 별난 사람이었다. 흔히 강한 어떤 인간적 성향들이 그의 마음에는 거의 없었는데, 그중 하나가 남의 사사로운 일에 대한 호기심이었다."], ["Especially if there was anything discreditable to be found out concerning another man, Caleb preferred not to know it; and if he had to tell anybody under him that his evil doings were discovered, he was more embarrassed than the culprit.", "특히 다른 사람에 관해 드러날 떳떳치 못한 무언가가 있다면, 케일럽은 차라리 모르는 편을 택했다. 그리고 아랫사람에게 그의 나쁜 짓이 발각되었음을 알려야 할 때면, 그는 죄지은 당사자보다도 더 난처해했다."], ["He now spurred his horse, and saying, “I wish you good evening, Mr.", "이제 그는 말에 박차를 가하며 \"그럼 안녕히 가십시오, 벌스트로드 씨."], ["Bulstrode; I must be getting home,” set off at a trot.", "저는 집에 가 봐야겠습니다.\"라고 말하고는 빠른 걸음으로 떠났다."]], [["“You didn’t put your full address to this letter,”", "\"이 편지에 자네 주소를 다 적지 않았더군.\""], ["Raffles continued.", "래플스가 말을 이었다."], ["“That was not like the first-rate man of business you used to be.", "\"예전에 자네가 보여 주던 일류 사업가다운 면모가 아니야. '슈러브스'라니, 그건 어디든 있을 수 있잖나."], ["‘The Shrubs,’—they may be anywhere: you live near at hand, eh?—have cut the London concern altogether—perhaps turned country squire—have a rural mansion to invite me to.", "자넨 근처에 사는 게로군, 응? 런던 사업은 통째로 접고, 아마 시골 지주가 된 게로군."], ["Lord, how many years it is ago!", "나를 초대할 시골 저택도 있고. 세상에, 얼마나 오랜 세월이 흘렀는지!"], ["The old lady must have been dead a pretty long while—gone to glory without the pain of knowing how poor her daughter was, eh?", "그 노부인은 진작에 돌아가신 지 꽤 됐겠군. 자기 딸이 얼마나 가난하게 사는지 알게 되는 고통도 없이 영광 속으로 가셨겠어, 응?"], ["But, by Jove! you’re very pale and pasty, Nick.", "그런데 이런, 닉, 자네 안색이 아주 창백하고 누렇구먼."], ["Come, if you’re going home, I’ll walk by your side.”", "자, 집에 가는 길이면 내가 옆에서 같이 걸어 주지.\""]], [["Mr. Bulstrode’s usual paleness had in fact taken an almost deathly hue.", "벌스트로드 씨의 평소 창백한 안색은 실로 거의 죽은 사람 같은 빛을 띠고 있었다."], ["Five minutes before, the expanse of his life had been submerged in its evening sunshine which shone backward to its remembered morning: sin seemed to be a question of doctrine and inward penitence, humiliation an exercise of the closet, the bearing of his deeds a matter of private vision adjusted solely by spiritual relations and conceptions of the divine purposes.", "오 분 전만 해도 그의 인생의 드넓은 폭은, 기억 속의 아침을 향해 거슬러 비치는 저녁 햇살에 잠겨 있었다. 죄란 교리와 내면의 참회의 문제로, 굴욕이란 골방에서 행하는 수련으로, 자기 행위의 무게란 오로지 영적 관계와 신의 목적에 대한 관념으로만 조율되는 사사로운 환영의 문제로 여겨졌다."], ["And now, as if by some hideous magic, this loud red figure had risen before him in unmanageable solidity—an incorporate past which had not entered into his imagination of chastisements.", "그런데 이제, 무슨 흉측한 마법에라도 걸린 듯, 이 떠들썩한 붉은 형상이 다루기 힘든 견고함으로 그의 앞에 솟아오른 것이다. 그가 상상하던 징계의 범주에는 들어 있지 않던, 육신을 입은 과거였다."], ["But Mr. Bulstrode’s thought was busy, and he was not a man to act or speak rashly.", "그러나 벌스트로드 씨의 생각은 분주히 움직였고, 그는 경솔하게 행동하거나 말할 사람이 아니었다."]], [["“I was going home,” he said, “but I can defer my ride a little.", "\"집에 가는 길이었네만, 좀 미뤄도 되겠지."], ["And you can, if you please, rest here.”", "그리고 원한다면 여기서 좀 쉬어도 좋네.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙군.\""], ["said Raffles, making a grimace.", "래플스가 얼굴을 찡그리며 말했다."], ["“I don’t care now about seeing my stepson.", "\"이제 의붓아들 보는 데는 관심 없어."], ["I’d rather go home with you.”", "차라리 자네와 함께 집으로 가는 편이 낫지.\""]], [["“Your stepson, if Mr. Rigg Featherstone was he, is here no longer.", "\"자네 의붓아들이 리그 페더스톤 씨라면, 그는 이제 여기 없네."], ["I am master here now.”", "이제 이곳의 주인은 나야.\""]], [["Raffles opened wide eyes, and gave a long whistle of surprise, before he said, “Well then, I’ve no objection.", "래플스는 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고 길게 휘파람을 불며 놀라움을 드러낸 뒤 이렇게 말했다. \"그렇다면야, 나도 이의 없네."], ["I’ve had enough walking from the coach-road.", "큰길에서부터 걸어왔더니 이제 걷는 건 충분해."], ["I never was much of a walker, or rider either.", "난 원래 걷는 것도, 말 타는 것도 그리 즐기는 편이 아니었지."], ["What I like is a smart vehicle and a spirited cob.", "내가 좋아하는 건 멋진 마차에 기운찬 조랑말이야. 난 늘 안장에 앉으면 좀 무거웠거든."], ["I was always a little heavy in the saddle.", "나를 보다니 자네한텐 얼마나 즐거운 뜻밖의 일이겠나, 이 친구야!\""], ["What a pleasant surprise it must be to you to see me, old fellow!”", "그들이 집 쪽으로 돌아서며 그가 말을 이었다. \"자넨 그렇다고 말은 안 하지만 말이야."], ["he continued, as they turned towards the house.", "하지만 자넨 한 번도 행운을 마음껏 누린 적이 없었어."], ["“You don’t say so; but you never took your luck heartily—you were always thinking of improving the occasion—you’d such a gift for improving your luck.”", "늘 기회를 잘 써먹을 궁리만 했지. 자넨 행운을 잘 써먹는 데 그런 재주가 있었으니까.\""]], [["Mr. Raffles seemed greatly to enjoy his own wit, and swung his leg in a swaggering manner which was rather too much for his companion’s judicious patience.", "래플스 씨는 제 익살을 무척이나 즐기는 듯했고, 거들먹거리는 태도로 다리를 흔들어 댔으니, 그것은 그의 동행이 분별 있게 인내하기에는 다소 지나친 것이었다."]], [["“If I remember rightly,” Mr.", "\"내 기억이 옳다면, 래플스 씨.\""], ["Bulstrode observed, with chill anger, “our acquaintance many years ago had not the sort of intimacy which you are now assuming, Mr.", "벌스트로드 씨가 싸늘한 분노를 담아 말했다. \"여러 해 전 우리의 교분은 자네가 지금 가장하는 그런 종류의 친밀함은 아니었네."], ["Raffles. Any services you desire of me will be the more readily rendered if you will avoid a tone of familiarity which did not lie in our former intercourse, and can hardly be warranted by more than twenty years of separation.”", "자네가 내게 바라는 어떤 도움이든, 우리의 옛 교제에는 없었고 이십 년이 넘는 단절로 도무지 정당화될 수 없는 그런 허물없는 말투를 삼간다면 한결 기꺼이 베풀어질 걸세.\""]], [["“You don’t like being called Nick?", "\"닉이라고 불리는 게 싫은가?"], ["Why, I always called you Nick in my heart, and though lost to sight, to memory dear. By Jove!", "아니, 난 마음속으로는 늘 자넬 닉이라 불렀어, 눈에서 멀어졌어도 기억엔 정겨운 사람이었지."], ["my feelings have ripened for you like fine old cognac.", "이런, 자네에 대한 내 감정은 잘 묵은 코냑처럼 농익었다네."], ["I hope you’ve got some in the house now.", "지금 집에 코냑이 좀 있으면 좋겠군."], ["Josh filled my flask well the last time.”", "지난번엔 조시가 내 술병을 가득 채워 줬는데 말이야.\""]], [["Mr. Bulstrode had not yet fully learned that even the desire for cognac was not stronger in Raffles than the desire to torment, and that a hint of annoyance always served him as a fresh cue.", "벌스트로드 씨는 래플스에게 코냑을 향한 욕망조차 남을 괴롭히려는 욕망보다 더 강하지는 않다는 것을, 그리고 짜증의 기미가 늘 그에게 새로운 신호 노릇을 한다는 것을 아직 충분히 깨닫지 못하고 있었다."], ["But it was at least clear that further objection was useless, and Mr. Bulstrode, in giving orders to the housekeeper for the accommodation of the guest, had a resolute air of quietude.", "그러나 더 이상의 반대가 소용없다는 것만은 적어도 분명했고, 벌스트로드 씨는 손님을 묵게 하라고 가정부에게 지시하면서도 단호한 평정의 기색을 보였다."]], [["There was the comfort of thinking that this housekeeper had been in the service of Rigg also, and might accept the idea that Mr. Bulstrode entertained Raffles merely as a friend of her former master.", "이 가정부가 리그를 모셨던 사람이기도 하니, 벌스트로드 씨가 단지 옛 주인의 친구로서 래플스를 대접하는 것이라고 여겨 줄지 모른다는 데에 위안이 있었다."]], [["When there was food and drink spread before his visitor in the wainscoted parlor, and no witness in the room, Mr. Bulstrode said—", "징두리 판자를 댄 거실에 손님 앞으로 음식과 술이 차려지고 방 안에 다른 목격자가 없게 되자, 벌스트로드 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Your habits and mine are so different, Mr. Raffles, that we can hardly enjoy each other’s society.", "\"자네와 나의 습성은 너무나 다르니, 래플스 씨, 우리는 서로의 교제를 즐기기 어렵네."], ["The wisest plan for both of us will therefore be to part as soon as possible.", "그러니 우리 둘 모두에게 가장 현명한 방책은 되도록 빨리 헤어지는 것일세."], ["Since you say that you wished to meet me, you probably considered that you had some business to transact with me.", "자네가 나를 만나고 싶었다고 하니, 아마 나와 처리할 무슨 용무가 있다고 여긴 모양이지."], ["But under the circumstances I will invite you to remain here for the night, and I will myself ride over here early to-morrow morning—before breakfast, in fact—when I can receive any communication you have to make to me.”", "하지만 이런 사정이니, 오늘 밤은 여기 묵으라고 청하겠네. 그리고 내가 내일 아침 일찍, 사실은 조반 전에 직접 이곳으로 말을 타고 와서, 자네가 내게 할 어떤 이야기든 듣겠네.\""]], [["“With all my heart,” said Raffles; “this is a comfortable place—a little dull for a continuance; but I can put up with it for a night, with this good liquor and the prospect of seeing you again in the morning. You’re a much better host than my stepson was; but Josh owed me a bit of a grudge for marrying his mother; and between you and me there was never anything but kindness.”", "\"좋고말고.\" 래플스가 말했다. \"여긴 편안한 곳이군. 오래 있기엔 좀 따분하지만, 이 좋은 술이 있고 아침에 자넬 다시 볼 기약이 있으니 하룻밤쯤은 견딜 만하지. 자넨 내 의붓아들보다 훨씬 나은 주인이야. 하긴 조시는 내가 제 어미와 결혼한 것에 좀 앙심을 품었지. 그래도 자네와 나 사이엔 친절 말고는 아무것도 없었으니까.\""]], [["Mr. Bulstrode, hoping that the peculiar mixture of joviality and sneering in Raffles’ manner was a good deal the effect of drink, had determined to wait till he was quite sober before he spent more words upon him.", "벌스트로드 씨는 래플스의 태도에 깃든 그 묘한 쾌활함과 냉소의 혼합이 상당 부분 술의 작용이기를 바라며, 그가 완전히 술이 깰 때까지 기다렸다가 더 말을 섞기로 작정했다."], ["But he rode home with a terribly lucid vision of the difficulty there would be in arranging any result that could be permanently counted on with this man.", "그러나 집으로 말을 타고 가는 내내, 이 사내와는 영구히 믿고 의지할 수 있는 어떤 결말도 마련하기가 얼마나 어려울지 무섭도록 또렷한 환영에 시달렸다."], ["It was inevitable that he should wish to get rid of John Raffles, though his reappearance could not be regarded as lying outside the divine plan. The spirit of evil might have sent him to threaten Mr.", "존 래플스의 재출현을 신의 계획 밖에 놓인 일로 볼 수는 없었지만, 그를 떼어 내고 싶은 마음만은 피할 수 없었다."], ["Bulstrode’s subversion as an instrument of good; but the threat must have been permitted, and was a chastisement of a new kind.", "악의 영이 선의 도구인 벌스트로드 씨를 무너뜨리려고 위협하려 그를 보냈는지도 모른다. 그러나 그 위협은 허락된 것이 분명했고, 새로운 종류의 징계였다."], ["It was an hour of anguish for him very different from the hours in which his struggle had been securely private, and which had ended with a sense that his secret misdeeds were pardoned and his services accepted.", "그것은 그에게 고뇌의 시간이었으니, 그의 갈등이 안전하게 사사로웠고 자신의 은밀한 비행이 용서받고 봉사가 받아들여졌다는 느낌으로 끝나곤 하던 그 시간들과는 사뭇 달랐다."], ["Those misdeeds even when committed—had they not been half sanctified by the singleness of his desire to devote himself and all he possessed to the furtherance of the divine scheme?", "그 비행들조차도, 저질러질 당시에, 자기 자신과 자기가 가진 모든 것을 신의 계획을 진척시키는 데 바치려는 한결같은 열망으로 반쯤 성스러워진 것이 아니었던가?"], ["And was he after all to become a mere stone of stumbling and a rock of offence?", "그런데 결국 그가 한낱 걸림돌이요 거치는 바위가 되고 만단 말인가?"], ["For who would understand the work within him?", "누가 그의 내면의 역사를 이해해 주겠는가?"], ["Who would not, when there was the pretext of casting disgrace upon him, confound his whole life and the truths he had espoused, in one heap of obloquy?", "그에게 치욕을 씌울 구실이 생긴다면, 누군들 그의 평생과 그가 신봉해 온 진리를 한 무더기의 오욕 속에 한데 뒤섞어 버리지 않겠는가?"]], [["In his closest meditations the life-long habit of Mr. Bulstrode’s mind clad his most egoistic terrors in doctrinal references to superhuman ends.", "가장 은밀한 명상 속에서, 벌스트로드 씨의 마음이 평생 들인 습성은 그의 가장 이기적인 공포들을 초인간적 목적에 대한 교리적 언급으로 감쌌다."], ["But even while we are talking and meditating about the earth’s orbit and the solar system, what we feel and adjust our movements to is the stable earth and the changing day.", "그러나 우리가 지구의 궤도와 태양계에 관해 이야기하고 사색하는 동안에도, 우리가 실제로 느끼고 그에 맞추어 움직이는 것은 안정된 대지와 변해 가는 하루다."], ["And now within all the automatic succession of theoretic phrases—distinct and inmost as the shiver and the ache of oncoming fever when we are discussing abstract pain, was the forecast of disgrace in the presence of his neighbors and of his own wife.", "그리고 이제, 그 모든 자동으로 이어지는 이론적 어구들 안쪽에는, 우리가 추상적 고통을 논할 때 닥쳐오는 열병의 오한과 통증처럼 또렷하고 깊숙한 것으로서, 이웃들과 제 아내 앞에서 치욕을 당하리라는 예감이 자리하고 있었다."], ["For the pain, as well as the public estimate of disgrace, depends on the amount of previous profession.", "그 고통이란, 그리고 치욕에 대한 세간의 평가란, 앞서 내세운 표방의 양에 달려 있기 때문이다."], ["To men who only aim at escaping felony, nothing short of the prisoner’s dock is disgrace.", "중죄를 면하는 것만을 목표로 삼는 사람들에게는 피고석에 서는 것 말고는 무엇도 치욕이 아니다."], ["But Mr. Bulstrode had aimed at being an eminent Christian.", "그러나 벌스트로드 씨는 저명한 기독교인이 되는 것을 목표로 삼아 왔던 것이다."]], [["It was not more than half-past seven in the morning when he again reached Stone Court.", "그가 다시 스톤 코트에 이른 것은 아침 일곱 시 반이 채 되지 않은 때였다. 그 멋진 옛집이 그 순간만큼 기쁨에 찬 보금자리처럼 보인 적이 없었다."], ["The fine old place never looked more like a delightful home than at that moment; the great white lilies were in flower, the nasturtiums, their pretty leaves all silvered with dew, were running away over the low stone wall; the very noises all around had a heart of peace within them.", "커다란 흰 백합이 꽃을 피웠고, 이슬에 온통 잎이 은빛으로 물든 어여쁜 한련화가 낮은 돌담 위로 뻗어 나가고 있었다. 사방의 온갖 소리마저 그 안에 평화의 심장을 품고 있었다."], ["But everything was spoiled for the owner as he walked on the gravel in front and awaited the descent of Mr. Raffles, with whom he was condemned to breakfast.", "그러나 앞뜰 자갈길을 거닐며, 함께 조반을 들도록 운명 지어진 래플스 씨가 내려오기를 기다리는 주인에게는 모든 것이 망가져 버렸다."]], [["It was not long before they were seated together in the wainscoted parlor over their tea and toast, which was as much as Raffles cared to take at that early hour.", "오래지 않아 그들은 징두리 판자를 댄 거실에 함께 앉아 차와 토스트를 들었는데, 그것이 그 이른 시각에 래플스가 들고 싶어 한 전부였다."], ["The difference between his morning and evening self was not so great as his companion had imagined that it might be; the delight in tormenting was perhaps even the stronger because his spirits were rather less highly pitched.", "그의 아침의 자아와 저녁의 자아의 차이는 그의 동행이 짐작했을 만큼 크지는 않았다. 어쩌면 기분이 다소 덜 들떠 있었던 탓에 괴롭히는 즐거움은 오히려 더 강했는지도 모른다."], ["Certainly his manners seemed more disagreeable by the morning light.", "확실히 그의 태도는 아침 햇살 아래서 더 불쾌해 보였다."]], [["“As I have little time to spare, Mr. Raffles,”", "\"내겐 시간이 별로 없으니, 래플스 씨.\""], ["said the banker, who could hardly do more than sip his tea and break his toast without eating it, “I shall be obliged if you will mention at once the ground on which you wished to meet with me.", "차를 홀짝이고 토스트를 부수기만 할 뿐 먹지는 못하는 은행가가 말했다. \"자네가 나를 만나고 싶어 한 까닭을 곧바로 말해 준다면 고맙겠네."], ["I presume that you have a home elsewhere and will be glad to return to it.”", "자네에게 다른 곳에 거처가 있어 그리로 기꺼이 돌아가리라 짐작하네만.\""]], [["“Why, if a man has got any heart, doesn’t he want to see an old friend, Nick?—I must call you Nick—we always did call you young Nick when we knew you meant to marry the old widow.", "\"아니, 사람에게 마음이라는 게 있다면 옛 친구를 보고 싶지 않겠나, 닉? 자넬 닉이라고 불러야겠어."], ["Some said you had a handsome family likeness to old Nick, but that was your mother’s fault, calling you Nicholas.", "자네가 그 늙은 과부와 결혼할 작정인 걸 알았을 때 우린 늘 자넬 젊은 닉이라고 불렀잖나. 어떤 이들은 자네가 늙은 닉 영감, 그러니까 악마와 잘생긴 집안 내력처럼 닮았다고들 했지만, 그건 자네 어머니 탓이지, 자넬 니콜라스라 이름 지으셨으니."], ["Aren’t you glad to see me again?", "날 다시 만나서 기쁘지 않은가?"], ["I expected an invite to stay with you at some pretty place.", "난 자네가 어느 근사한 곳에 와서 묵으라고 초대해 줄 줄 알았네."], ["My own establishment is broken up now my wife’s dead.", "내 살림은 이제 아내가 죽어서 다 흩어졌어."], ["I’ve no particular attachment to any spot; I would as soon settle hereabout as anywhere.”", "난 어느 한 곳에 딱히 정붙인 데가 없으니, 어디든 마찬가지로 이 근방에 자리 잡아도 좋겠어.\""]], [["“May I ask why you returned from America?", "\"자네가 미국에서 돌아온 까닭을 물어도 되겠나?"], ["I considered that the strong wish you expressed to go there, when an adequate sum was furnished, was tantamount to an engagement that you would remain there for life.”", "자네가 충분한 돈을 받고 그리로 가겠다고 강하게 바랐을 때, 나는 그것이 거기서 평생 머물겠다는 약속과 다름없다고 여겼네.\""]], [["“Never knew that a wish to go to a place was the same thing as a wish to stay.", "\"어느 곳에 가고 싶다는 게 거기 머물고 싶다는 것과 같은 말이라고는 들어 본 적이 없는데."], ["But I did stay a matter of ten years; it didn’t suit me to stay any longer.", "그래도 한 십 년쯤은 머물렀어. 더 오래 있는 건 내게 맞지 않더군."], ["And I’m not going again, Nick.” Here Mr.", "그리고 다시는 안 갈 걸세, 닉.\""], ["Raffles winked slowly as he looked at Mr. Bulstrode.", "여기서 래플스 씨는 벌스트로드 씨를 바라보며 천천히 윙크를 했다."]], [["“Do you wish to be settled in any business?", "\"무슨 사업에든 자리를 잡고 싶은 건가?"], ["What is your calling now?”", "지금 자네 생업은 무엇인가?\""]], [["“Thank you, my calling is to enjoy myself as much as I can.", "\"고맙네만, 내 생업은 되도록 즐기는 거라네."], ["I don’t care about working any more.", "이제 일하는 데는 관심 없어."], ["If I did anything it would be a little travelling in the tobacco line—or something of that sort, which takes a man into agreeable company.", "뭔가를 한다면 담배 거래로 좀 돌아다니는 정도, 아니면 그런 비슷한, 사람을 유쾌한 무리 속에 끼게 해 주는 일이겠지. 하지만 기댈 만한 자립 밑천 없이는 안 되지."], ["But not without an independence to fall back upon.", "내가 원하는 게 바로 그걸세."], ["That’s what I want: I’m not so strong as I was, Nick, though I’ve got more color than you.", "난 예전만큼 튼튼하지 않아, 닉. 자네보다 혈색은 더 좋지만 말이야."], ["I want an independence.”", "난 자립 밑천이 필요하네.\""]], [["“That could be supplied to you, if you would engage to keep at a distance,”", "\"자네가 멀리 떨어져 있겠다고 약속한다면, 그것은 마련해 줄 수 있네.\""], ["said Mr. Bulstrode, perhaps with a little too much eagerness in his undertone.", "벌스트로드 씨가, 낮은 목소리에 어쩌면 다소 지나친 열의를 담아 말했다."]], [["“That must be as it suits my convenience,”", "\"그건 내 편의에 맞아야지.\""], ["said Raffles coolly.", "래플스가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“I see no reason why I shouldn’t make a few acquaintances hereabout.", "\"내가 이 근방에서 아는 사람 몇쯤 못 사귈 이유가 없잖나."], ["I’m not ashamed of myself as company for anybody.", "난 누구에게든 동행으로서 나 자신이 부끄럽지 않아."], ["I dropped my portmanteau at the turnpike when I got down—change of linen—genuine—honor bright—more than fronts and wristbands; and with this suit of mourning, straps and everything, I should do you credit among the nobs here.”", "내려서는 여행 가방을 통행료 징수소에 맡겨 뒀지. 갈아입을 속옷도 있어, 진짜야, 정말로 명예를 걸고, 셔츠 앞자락이며 소맷동 정도가 아니라네."], ["Mr. Raffles had pushed away his chair and looked down at himself, particularly at his straps.", "게다가 이 상복 차림에 끈이며 뭐며 다 갖췄으니, 여기 양반네들 사이에서도 자네 체면을 세워 주겠는걸.\""], ["His chief intention was to annoy Bulstrode, but he really thought that his appearance now would produce a good effect, and that he was not only handsome and witty, but clad in a mourning style which implied solid connections.", "래플스 씨는 의자를 뒤로 밀고 자기 몸을, 특히 바지의 끈을 내려다보았다. 그의 주된 의도는 벌스트로드를 괴롭히는 것이었지만, 그는 진심으로 자신의 지금 모습이 좋은 인상을 주리라고, 자신이 잘생기고 재치 있을 뿐 아니라 탄탄한 연줄을 암시하는 상복 차림을 하고 있다고 생각했다."]], [["“If you intend to rely on me in any way, Mr. Raffles,”", "\"자네가 어떤 식으로든 나를 의지할 작정이라면, 래플스 씨.\""], ["said Bulstrode, after a moment’s pause, “you will expect to meet my wishes.”", "벌스트로드가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다. \"자넨 내 뜻에 맞추기를 기대받을 걸세.\""]], [["“Ah, to be sure,”", "\"아, 그야 물론이지.\""], ["said Raffles, with a mocking cordiality.", "래플스가 비웃는 듯한 다정함을 담아 말했다."], ["“Didn’t I always do it?", "\"내가 늘 그러지 않았나?"], ["Lord, you made a pretty thing out of me, and I got but little.", "세상에, 자넨 날 가지고 짭짤하게 재미를 봤지만 난 받은 게 별로 없었지."], ["I’ve often thought since, I might have done better by telling the old woman that I’d found her daughter and her grandchild: it would have suited my feelings better; I’ve got a soft place in my heart.", "그 뒤로 종종 생각했네, 그 노파에게 딸과 손주를 찾았다고 일러 주는 편이 나았을 거라고. 그게 내 심정에는 더 맞았을 텐데."], ["But you’ve buried the old lady by this time, I suppose—it’s all one to her now.", "난 마음 한구석이 무른 데가 있거든. 하지만 그 노부인은 지금쯤 자네가 묻어 드렸겠지."], ["And you’ve got your fortune out of that profitable business which had such a blessing on it.", "이제 그분에겐 다 매한가지지만. 그리고 자넨 그토록 큰 축복을 받은 그 잘나가는 사업에서 재산을 일궜고."], ["You’ve taken to being a nob, buying land, being a country bashaw.", "양반 행세를 하며 땅을 사들이고 시골 영주가 됐구먼."], ["Still in the Dissenting line, eh?", "아직도 비국교도 노선인가, 응?"], ["Still godly?", "여전히 신앙심이 깊은가?"], ["Or taken to the Church as more genteel?”", "아니면 더 점잖다고 국교회로 갈아탔나?\""]], [["This time Mr. Raffles’ slow wink and slight protrusion of his tongue was worse than a nightmare, because it held the certitude that it was not a nightmare, but a waking misery.", "이번에 래플스 씨의 느린 윙크와 살짝 내민 혀는 악몽보다도 더 끔찍했으니, 그것이 악몽이 아니라 깨어 있는 채로 겪는 비참함이라는 확신을 담고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Bulstrode felt a shuddering nausea, and did not speak, but was considering diligently whether he should not leave Raffles to do as he would, and simply defy him as a slanderer.", "벌스트로드 씨는 진저리 치는 메스꺼움을 느꼈고, 말은 하지 않은 채, 차라리 래플스가 제멋대로 하게 내버려 두고 그를 한낱 비방꾼으로 몰아 맞서야 하지 않을까 부지런히 궁리하고 있었다."], ["The man would soon show himself disreputable enough to make people disbelieve him.", "그 사내는 머지않아 사람들이 그의 말을 믿지 않을 만큼 추잡한 자임을 스스로 드러낼 터였다."], ["“But not when he tells any ugly-looking truth about you,”", "\"하지만 그자가 자네에 관한, 보기 흉한 진실을 말할 때는 그렇지 않지.\""], ["said discerning consciousness.", "분별 있는 의식이 말했다. 그리고 또 이렇게도."], ["And again: it seemed no wrong to keep Raffles at a distance, but Mr. Bulstrode shrank from the direct falsehood of denying true statements.", "래플스를 멀리 떼어 두는 것은 잘못이 아닌 듯했으나, 벌스트로드 씨는 참된 진술을 부인하는 노골적인 거짓에는 움츠러들었다."], ["It was one thing to look back on forgiven sins, nay, to explain questionable conformity to lax customs, and another to enter deliberately on the necessity of falsehood.", "용서받은 죄들을 되돌아보는 것, 아니, 느슨한 관습에 마지못해 따른 미심쩍은 동조를 해명하는 것은 하나의 일이요, 거짓을 말해야 할 필요 속으로 짐짓 발을 들이는 것은 또 다른 일이었다."]], [["But since Bulstrode did not speak, Raffles ran on, by way of using time to the utmost.", "그러나 벌스트로드가 말을 하지 않자, 래플스는 시간을 최대한 써먹으려는 양 줄곧 떠들어 댔다."]], [["“I’ve not had such fine luck as you, by Jove!", "\"난 자네만큼 좋은 운을 누리진 못했어, 정말이지! 뉴욕에서 일이 지독하게 꼬였거든."], ["Things went confoundedly with me in New York; those Yankees are cool hands, and a man of gentlemanly feelings has no chance with them.", "그 양키들은 보통내기가 아니라서, 신사다운 감정을 지닌 사람은 도무지 그들을 당해 낼 재간이 없어. 돌아와서 결혼했지."], ["I married when I came back—a nice woman in the tobacco trade—very fond of me—but the trade was restricted, as we say.", "담배 장사를 하는 참한 여자였는데, 날 무척 좋아했어. 그런데 그 장사가, 우리 말로 하자면, 제약이 있었지."], ["She had been settled there a good many years by a friend; but there was a son too much in the case.", "그 여잔 어떤 친구가 거기 오래전에 자리를 잡아 줬는데, 군더더기 같은 아들이 하나 딸려 있었거든."], ["Josh and I never hit it off.", "조시와 나는 도무지 마음이 맞질 않았어."], ["However, I made the most of the position, and I’ve always taken my glass in good company.", "그래도 난 그 처지에서 최대한 잘 지냈고, 늘 좋은 사람들과 어울려 한잔씩 걸쳤지."], ["It’s been all on the square with me; I’m as open as the day.", "난 매사에 떳떳했어. 대낮처럼 솔직한 사람이지."], ["You won’t take it ill of me that I didn’t look you up before.", "진작 자넬 찾아오지 않았다고 언짢게 여기진 말게."], ["I’ve got a complaint that makes me a little dilatory.", "난 사람을 좀 굼뜨게 만드는 지병이 있거든."], ["I thought you were trading and praying away in London still, and didn’t find you there.", "자네가 아직 런던에서 장사하고 기도하며 지내는 줄 알았는데, 거기 없더군."], ["But you see I was sent to you, Nick—perhaps for a blessing to both of us.”", "하지만 보다시피 난 자네에게로 보내진 걸세, 닉. 어쩌면 우리 둘 모두에게 축복이 되라고 말이야.\""]], [["Mr. Raffles ended with a jocose snuffle: no man felt his intellect more superior to religious cant.", "래플스 씨는 우스꽝스러운 콧소리로 말을 맺었다."], ["And if the cunning which calculates on the meanest feelings in men could be called intellect, he had his share, for under the blurting rallying tone with which he spoke to Bulstrode, there was an evident selection of statements, as if they had been so many moves at chess.", "종교적 위선보다 제 지성이 우월하다고 느끼는 데서 그를 따를 자가 없었다. 그리고 사람들의 가장 비루한 감정을 셈하는 교활함을 지성이라 부를 수 있다면, 그에게도 제 몫의 지성이 있었으니, 벌스트로드에게 불쑥불쑥 놀려 대는 말투 아래에는 마치 그 말들이 체스의 수인 양 분명한 진술의 선별이 깔려 있었기 때문이다."], ["Meanwhile Bulstrode had determined on his move, and he said, with gathered resolution—", "그동안 벌스트로드는 자신의 수를 정한 터라, 가다듬은 결의를 담아 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“You will do well to reflect, Mr.", "\"자네는 잘 헤아려 보는 게 좋을 걸세, 래플스 씨."], ["Raffles, that it is possible for a man to overreach himself in the effort to secure undue advantage.", "부당한 이득을 챙기려는 노력으로 사람이 도리어 제 발등을 찍을 수도 있는 법이니."], ["Although I am not in any way bound to you, I am willing to supply you with a regular annuity—in quarterly payments—so long as you fulfil a promise to remain at a distance from this neighborhood.", "나는 자네에게 어떤 식으로도 매여 있지 않네만, 자네가 이 근방에서 멀리 떨어져 있겠다는 약속을 지키는 한, 분기마다 지급되는 정기 연금을 기꺼이 대 주겠네."], ["It is in your power to choose.", "선택은 자네 손에 달렸어."], ["If you insist on remaining here, even for a short time, you will get nothing from me.", "자네가 단 한동안이라도 여기 머물기를 고집한다면, 자넨 내게서 아무것도 얻지 못할 걸세."], ["I shall decline to know you.”", "나는 자네를 모르는 척하겠네.\""]], [["“Ha, ha!”", "\"하, 하!\""], ["said Raffles, with an affected explosion, “that reminds me of a droll dog of a thief who declined to know the constable.”", "래플스가 짐짓 폭소를 터뜨리며 말했다. \"그 말을 들으니 순경 모르는 척한 우스운 도둑놈이 생각나는군.\""]], [["“Your allusions are lost on me sir,”", "\"자네 빗댄 말은 내게 통하지 않네, 이 사람아.\""], ["said Bulstrode, with white heat; “the law has no hold on me either through your agency or any other.”", "벌스트로드가 새하얗게 달아오른 노기로 말했다. \"법은 자네를 통해서든 다른 무엇을 통해서든 나를 옭아맬 게 없어.\""]], [["“You can’t understand a joke, my good fellow.", "\"농담도 못 알아듣는구먼, 이 친구야."], ["I only meant that I should never decline to know you.", "난 그저 절대로 자넬 모르는 척하지 않겠다고 말하려던 거야."], ["But let us be serious.", "하지만 진지하게 얘기해 보세."], ["Your quarterly payment won’t quite suit me.", "자네 분기 지급은 내게 영 맞질 않아."], ["I like my freedom.”", "난 내 자유가 좋거든.\""]], [["Here Raffles rose and stalked once or twice up and down the room, swinging his leg, and assuming an air of masterly meditation.", "여기서 래플스는 일어나 다리를 흔들며 한두 차례 방 안을 거들먹거리며 거닐었고, 무언가 골똘히 사색하는 듯한 태도를 취했다."], ["At last he stopped opposite Bulstrode, and said, “I’ll tell you what!", "마침내 그는 벌스트로드 맞은편에 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"이렇게 하지!"], ["Give us a couple of hundreds—come, that’s modest—and I’ll go away—honor bright!—pick up my portmanteau and go away.", "이백 파운드만 내놓게. 자, 이만하면 양심적이지."], ["But I shall not give up my liberty for a dirty annuity.", "그럼 떠나겠네, 명예를 걸고! 여행 가방을 챙겨서 떠나지."], ["I shall come and go where I like.", "하지만 더러운 연금 따위에 내 자유를 내주지는 않겠어."], ["Perhaps it may suit me to stay away, and correspond with a friend; perhaps not.", "난 내키는 데로 오가겠네. 어쩌면 멀리 떨어져 지내면서 친구와 편지나 주고받는 게 내게 맞을지도 모르고, 아닐지도 모르지."], ["Have you the money with you?”", "그 돈, 지금 가지고 있나?\""]], [["“No, I have one hundred,”", "\"아니, 백 파운드가 있네.\""], ["said Bulstrode, feeling the immediate riddance too great a relief to be rejected on the ground of future uncertainties.", "벌스트로드가 말했으니, 당장의 해방이 미래의 불확실함을 빌미로 마다하기엔 너무나 큰 안도였던 것이다."], ["“I will forward you the other if you will mention an address.”", "\"주소를 알려 주면 나머지는 부쳐 주겠네.\""]], [["“No, I’ll wait here till you bring it,”", "\"아니, 자네가 가져올 때까지 여기서 기다리겠네.\""], ["said Raffles.", "래플스가 말했다."], ["“I’ll take a stroll and have a snack, and you’ll be back by that time.”", "\"한 바퀴 거닐고 요기나 좀 하고 있으면 그때쯤 자네가 돌아오겠지.\""]], [["Mr. Bulstrode’s sickly body, shattered by the agitations he had gone through since the last evening, made him feel abjectly in the power of this loud invulnerable man.", "벌스트로드 씨의 병약한 몸은, 전날 저녁 이후 겪은 동요로 산산이 부서져, 이 떠들썩하고 끄떡없는 사내의 손아귀에 비굴하게 잡혀 있는 듯한 느낌을 그에게 안겼다."], ["At that moment he snatched at a temporary repose to be won on any terms.", "그 순간 그는 어떤 조건으로든 얻어 낼 수 있는 한때의 휴식을 덥석 붙잡았다."], ["He was rising to do what Raffles suggested, when the latter said, lifting up his finger as if with a sudden recollection—", "그가 래플스가 제안한 대로 하려고 일어서는데, 래플스가 무언가 문득 떠오른 듯 손가락을 치켜들며 말했다."]], [["“I did have another look after Sarah again, though I didn’t tell you; I’d a tender conscience about that pretty young woman.", "\"내가 세라를 다시 한번 수소문해 봤어, 자네한텐 말 안 했지만. 그 어여쁜 젊은 여자에 대해선 나도 양심이 좀 찔렸거든."], ["I didn’t find her, but I found out her husband’s name, and I made a note of it.", "그 여잘 찾진 못했지만, 남편 이름은 알아내서 적어 뒀지."], ["But hang it, I lost my pocketbook.", "그런데 빌어먹을, 수첩을 잃어버렸어."], ["However, if I heard it, I should know it again.", "그래도 다시 들으면 알아볼 걸세."], ["I’ve got my faculties as if I was in my prime, but names wear out, by Jove!", "난 정신만은 한창때처럼 멀쩡하지만, 이름이란 게 닳아 없어진단 말이야, 정말이지!"], ["Sometimes I’m no better than a confounded tax-paper before the names are filled in.", "가끔은 이름이 채워지기 전의 그 빌어먹을 세금 고지서나 다름없다니까."], ["However, if I hear of her and her family, you shall know, Nick.", "어쨌든 그 여자와 그 가족 소식을 들으면 자네한테 알려 줌세, 닉."], ["You’d like to do something for her, now she’s your step-daughter.”", "이제 그 여자가 자네 의붓딸이 됐으니, 뭔가 해 주고 싶겠지.\""]], [["“Doubtless,” said Mr.", "\"틀림없지.\""], ["Bulstrode, with the usual steady look of his light-gray eyes; “though that might reduce my power of assisting you.”", "벌스트로드 씨가 옅은 잿빛 눈으로 평소의 흔들림 없는 시선을 보내며 말했다. \"그러면 자넬 도울 내 여력이 줄어들지도 모르네만.\""]], [["As he walked out of the room, Raffles winked slowly at his back, and then turned towards the window to watch the banker riding away—virtually at his command.", "그가 방을 나서자, 래플스는 그의 등 뒤로 천천히 윙크를 했고, 이어 창문 쪽으로 돌아서서 사실상 제 명령에 따라 말을 타고 떠나가는 은행가를 지켜보았다."], ["His lips first curled with a smile and then opened with a short triumphant laugh.", "그의 입술은 처음엔 미소로 비틀렸다가, 이내 벌어지며 짤막한 의기양양한 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“But what the deuce was the name?”", "\"그런데 그 이름이 도대체 뭐였더라?\""], ["he presently said, half aloud, scratching his head, and wrinkling his brows horizontally.", "이윽고 그가 머리를 긁적이고 이마를 가로로 찌푸리며 반쯤 소리 내어 말했다."], ["He had not really cared or thought about this point of forgetfulness until it occurred to him in his invention of annoyances for Bulstrode.", "그는 벌스트로드를 괴롭힐 거리를 궁리하다 떠올리기 전까지는 이 깜빡한 대목을 사실 마음에 두거나 생각해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“It began with L; it was almost all l’s I fancy,”", "\"엘 자로 시작했는데. 거의 죄다 엘 자였던 것 같아.\""], ["he went on, with a sense that he was getting hold of the slippery name.", "그는 그 미끄러운 이름을 붙잡아 가는 듯한 느낌으로 말을 이었다. 그러나 잡은 것이 너무 어설펐고, 그는 곧 이 머릿속 추적에 싫증이 났다."], ["But the hold was too slight, and he soon got tired of this mental chase; for few men were more impatient of private occupation or more in need of making themselves continually heard than Mr. Raffles.", "래플스 씨만큼 혼자 하는 일을 못 견뎌 하거나, 끊임없이 제 목소리를 남들에게 들려주어야 하는 사람도 드물었기 때문이다."], ["He preferred using his time in pleasant conversation with the bailiff and the housekeeper, from whom he gathered as much as he wanted to know about Mr. Bulstrode’s position in Middlemarch.", "그는 자기 시간을 관리인과 가정부와의 유쾌한 대화에 쓰는 편을 택했고, 그들에게서 미들마치에서의 벌스트로드 씨의 처지에 관해 알고 싶은 만큼 알아냈다."]], [["After all, however, there was a dull space of time which needed relieving with bread and cheese and ale, and when he was seated alone with these resources in the wainscoted parlor, he suddenly slapped his knee, and exclaimed, “Ladislaw!”", "그래도 결국엔 빵과 치즈와 에일로 달래야 할 따분한 시간이 있었고, 징두리 판자를 댄 거실에 이 양식들을 곁에 두고 홀로 앉아 있던 그는 별안간 제 무릎을 탁 치며 외쳤다. \"래디슬로!\""], ["That action of memory which he had tried to set going, and had abandoned in despair, had suddenly completed itself without conscious effort—a common experience, agreeable as a completed sneeze, even if the name remembered is of no value.", "그가 굴러가게 하려 애쓰다 절망 속에 내버려 둔 그 기억의 작용이, 의식적인 노력 없이 별안간 저절로 완성된 것이었다. 떠올린 이름이 아무런 값어치가 없다 해도, 다 끝낸 재채기처럼 흐뭇한, 흔한 경험이었다."], ["Raffles immediately took out his pocket-book, and wrote down the name, not because he expected to use it, but merely for the sake of not being at a loss if he ever did happen to want it.", "래플스는 곧장 수첩을 꺼내 그 이름을 적었으니, 그것을 쓸 작정이 있어서가 아니라 그저 언젠가 정말 필요해질 경우에 난감해지지 않으려는 셈이었다. 그는 벌스트로드에게 말할 생각이 없었다."], ["He was not going to tell Bulstrode: there was no actual good in telling, and to a mind like that of Mr. Raffles there is always probable good in a secret.", "말해 봐야 실속이 없었고, 래플스 씨 같은 사람의 마음에는 비밀 속에 늘 그럴싸한 실속이 있는 법이었다."]], [["He was satisfied with his present success, and by three o’clock that day he had taken up his portmanteau at the turnpike and mounted the coach, relieving Mr.", "그는 지금의 성과에 만족했고, 그날 세 시 무렵 통행료 징수소에서 여행 가방을 챙겨 마차에 올랐으니, 스톤 코트의 풍경에 박힌 보기 흉한 검은 점을 벌스트로드 씨의 눈에서 거두어 갔다."], ["Bulstrode’s eyes of an ugly black spot on the landscape at Stone Court, but not relieving him of the dread that the black spot might reappear and become inseparable even from the vision of his hearth.", "그러나 그 검은 점이 다시 나타나 제 화롯가의 환영에서마저 떼어 낼 수 없게 될지도 모른다는 두려움까지 거두어 가지는 못했다."]], [["# BOOK VI. THE WIDOW AND THE WIFE. CHAPTER LIV.", "# BOOK VI. THE WIDOW AND THE WIFE. CHAPTER LIV."]], [["“Negli occhi porta la mia donna Amore; Per che si fa gentil ciò ch’ella mira: Ov’ella passa, ogni uom ver lei si gira, E cui saluta fa tremar lo core.", "내 여인은 그 두 눈에 사랑을 담고 다니니 그녀가 바라보는 것은 무엇이든 고결해지네. 그녀가 지나가는 곳마다 모든 이가 그녀를 향해 돌아서고, 그녀가 인사하는 이의 심장은 떨리네."]], [["Sicchè, bassando il viso, tutto smore, E d’ogni suo difetto allor sospira: Fuggon dinanzi a lei Superbia ed Ira: Aiutatemi, donne, a farle onore.", "그리하여 그는 얼굴을 숙인 채 온통 창백해지고, 자기의 온갖 결점을 그제야 한숨지으며 뉘우치네. 그녀 앞에서는 오만과 분노가 달아나니, 숙녀들이여, 나를 도와 그녀를 기리게 하소서."]], [["Ogni dolcezza, ogni pensiero umile Nasce nel core a chi parlar la sente; Ond’è beato chi prima la vide.", "온갖 달콤함과 온갖 겸허한 생각이 그녀의 말을 듣는 이의 가슴속에서 피어나니, 그녀를 가장 먼저 본 이는 복되도다."], ["Quel ch’ella par quand’ un poco sorride, Non si può dicer, nè tener a mente, Si è nuovo miracolo gentile.”", "그녀가 살며시 미소 지을 때의 그 모습은 말로 할 수도, 마음에 담아 둘 수도 없으니, 그토록 새롭고 고결한 기적이라네."], ["—DANTE: La Vita Nuova.", "― 단테, 『신생』"]], [["By that delightful morning when the hay-ricks at Stone Court were scenting the air quite impartially, as if Mr. Raffles had been a guest worthy of finest incense, Dorothea had again taken up her abode at Lowick Manor.", "스톤 코트의 건초 더미가 마치 래플스 씨가 가장 고운 향을 받을 만한 손님이기라도 한 듯 공기 중에 아무런 차별 없이 향기를 뿜어내던 그 상쾌한 아침 무렵, 도로테아는 다시 로윅 저택에 거처를 정한 터였다."], ["After three months Freshitt had become rather oppressive: to sit like a model for Saint Catherine looking rapturously at Celia’s baby would not do for many hours in the day, and to remain in that momentous babe’s presence with persistent disregard was a course that could not have been tolerated in a childless sister.", "석 달이 지나니 프레시트는 다소 답답하게 느껴졌다. 실리아의 아기를 황홀하게 바라보는 성 카타리나의 모델처럼 앉아 있는 일을 하루에 여러 시간씩 할 수는 없었고, 그 중대한 아기의 면전에서 한결같이 무관심한 태도로 머무는 것은 아이 없는 이모로서 용납될 수 없는 처사였다."], ["Dorothea would have been capable of carrying baby joyfully for a mile if there had been need, and of loving it the more tenderly for that labor; but to an aunt who does not recognize her infant nephew as Bouddha, and has nothing to do for him but to admire, his behavior is apt to appear monotonous, and the interest of watching him exhaustible.", "도로테아는 필요하다면 아기를 안고 1마일이라도 기쁘게 걸어갈 수 있었고, 그 수고 때문에 아기를 더욱 다정하게 사랑할 수도 있었을 것이다. 하지만 어린 조카를 부처로 받아들이지 않고, 그저 감탄하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없는 이모에게는, 아기의 행동이 단조롭게 보이고 그를 바라보는 흥미도 쉬이 바닥나기 마련이다."], ["This possibility was quite hidden from Celia, who felt that Dorothea’s childless widowhood fell in quite prettily with the birth of little Arthur (baby was named after Mr. Brooke).", "이런 가능성은 실리아에게는 전혀 보이지 않았으니, 그녀는 도로테아의 아이 없는 과부 신세가 어린 아서의 탄생(아기는 브룩 씨의 이름을 따서 지어졌다)과 아주 그럴싸하게 맞아떨어진다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Dodo is just the creature not to mind about having anything of her own—children or anything!”", "\"도도는 자기 것을 가지는 일에는 도무지 마음 쓰지 않는 사람이야. 아이든 뭐든 말이야!\""], ["said Celia to her husband.", "실리아가 남편에게 말했다."], ["“And if she had had a baby, it never could have been such a dear as Arthur.", "\"게다가 언니가 아기를 낳았다 해도 결코 아서만큼 사랑스럽지는 못했을 거예요."], ["Could it, James?", "안 그래요, 제임스?\""]], [["“Not if it had been like Casaubon,”", "\"캐소본을 닮았다면 그랬겠지.\""], ["said Sir James, conscious of some indirectness in his answer, and of holding a strictly private opinion as to the perfections of his first-born.", "제임스 경은 자기 대답에 어딘가 에두른 데가 있음을, 그리고 자기 맏아들의 완벽함에 관해서는 엄격히 사적인 견해를 품고 있음을 의식하며 말했다."]], [["“No!", "\"맞아요!"], ["just imagine!", "상상해 봐요!"], ["Really it was a mercy,”", "정말이지 다행이었어요.\""], ["said Celia; “and I think it is very nice for Dodo to be a widow.", "실리아가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 도도가 과부인 게 아주 좋다고 생각해요."], ["She can be just as fond of our baby as if it were her own, and she can have as many notions of her own as she likes.”", "언니는 우리 아기를 자기 아이처럼 사랑할 수 있고, 자기가 원하는 만큼 얼마든지 자기만의 생각을 품을 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["“It is a pity she was not a queen,”", "\"언니가 여왕이 아니라는 게 안타깝군.\""], ["said the devout Sir James.", "경건한 제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“But what should we have been then?", "\"하지만 그러면 우리는 뭐가 됐을까요?"], ["We must have been something else,”", "우리는 다른 무엇이 되어야 했을 거예요.\""], ["said Celia, objecting to so laborious a flight of imagination.", "실리아가 그토록 수고로운 상상의 비약에 반대하며 말했다."], ["“I like her better as she is.”", "\"나는 지금 그대로의 언니가 더 좋아요.\""]], [["Hence, when she found that Dorothea was making arrangements for her final departure to Lowick, Celia raised her eyebrows with disappointment, and in her quiet unemphatic way shot a needle-arrow of sarcasm.", "그리하여 도로테아가 로윅으로 영영 떠날 채비를 하고 있음을 알았을 때, 실리아는 실망하여 눈썹을 치켜올리고는, 특유의 조용하고 강조 없는 투로 빈정거림의 바늘 화살을 쏘았다."]], [["“What will you do at Lowick, Dodo?", "\"로윅에서 뭘 하려는 거야, 도도?"], ["You say yourself there is nothing to be done there: everybody is so clean and well off, it makes you quite melancholy.", "언니 입으로도 거기서는 할 일이 없다고 했잖아. 다들 너무 깨끗하고 잘살아서 오히려 우울해진다고 말이야."], ["And here you have been so happy going all about Tipton with Mr.", "그런데 여기서는 가스 씨와 함께 팁턴을 두루 돌아다니며, 가장 형편없는 뒷마당까지 다니면서 그토록 행복해했으면서."], ["Garth into the worst backyards. And now uncle is abroad, you and Mr.", "게다가 이제 삼촌도 외국에 가셨으니 언니랑 가스 씨가 마음대로 할 수 있잖아."], ["Garth can have it all your own way; and I am sure James does everything you tell him.”", "그리고 제임스는 언니가 시키는 건 뭐든 다 한다니까.\""]], [["“I shall often come here, and I shall see how baby grows all the better,”", "\"여기에 자주 올 거야. 그러면 아기가 자라는 모습도 오히려 더 잘 볼 수 있을 거고.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도로테아가 말했다."]], [["“But you will never see him washed,”", "\"하지만 아기 목욕시키는 건 절대 못 볼 거야.\""], ["said Celia; “and that is quite the best part of the day.”", "실리아가 말했다. \"그게 하루 중에 제일 좋은 부분인데.\""], ["She was almost pouting: it did seem to her very hard in Dodo to go away from the baby when she might stay.", "그녀는 거의 입을 삐죽이고 있었다. 도도가 머물 수 있는데도 아기 곁을 떠나는 것이 정말이지 너무하다고 여겨졌던 것이다."]], [["“Dear Kitty, I will come and stay all night on purpose,”", "\"키티야, 일부러라도 와서 하룻밤 묵을게.\""], ["said Dorothea; “but I want to be alone now, and in my own home.", "도로테아가 말했다. \"하지만 지금은 혼자, 내 집에 있고 싶어."], ["I wish to know the Farebrothers better, and to talk to Mr. Farebrother about what there is to be done in Middlemarch.”", "페어브러더 댁 사람들을 더 잘 알고 싶고, 미들마치에서 해야 할 일에 관해 페어브러더 씨와 이야기하고 싶어.\""]], [["Dorothea’s native strength of will was no longer all converted into resolute submission.", "도로테아의 타고난 강한 의지력은 더 이상 온전히 단호한 복종으로만 전환되지 않았다."], ["She had a great yearning to be at Lowick, and was simply determined to go, not feeling bound to tell all her reasons.", "그녀는 로윅에 있고 싶은 간절한 바람이 있었고, 그저 가기로 마음먹었으며, 자기 이유를 모두 밝혀야 한다고 느끼지도 않았다."], ["But every one around her disapproved.", "하지만 주위 사람은 모두 반대했다."], ["Sir James was much pained, and offered that they should all migrate to Cheltenham for a few months with the sacred ark, otherwise called a cradle: at that period a man could hardly know what to propose if Cheltenham were rejected.", "제임스 경은 몹시 마음 아파하며, 다들 그 신성한 궤짝, 달리 말하면 요람을 들고 몇 달간 첼튼엄으로 옮겨 가자고 제안했다. 그 시절에는 첼튼엄이 거절당하면 남자로서 달리 무엇을 제안해야 할지 알기 어려웠다."]], [["The Dowager Lady Chettam, just returned from a visit to her daughter in town, wished, at least, that Mrs. Vigo should be written to, and invited to accept the office of companion to Mrs.", "도시에 사는 딸을 방문하고 막 돌아온 노부인 체텀 부인은, 적어도 비고 부인에게 편지를 써서 캐소본 부인의 말동무 자리를 맡아 달라고 청해야 한다고 했다."], ["Casaubon: it was not credible that Dorothea as a young widow would think of living alone in the house at Lowick.", "젊은 과부인 도로테아가 로윅의 집에서 혼자 살 생각을 하다니 믿을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Mrs. Vigo had been reader and secretary to royal personages, and in point of knowledge and sentiments even Dorothea could have nothing to object to her.", "비고 부인은 왕족의 낭독자이자 비서였던 사람으로, 지식과 정서 면에서는 도로테아조차 그녀에게 흠잡을 데가 없을 정도였다."]], [["Mrs. Cadwallader said, privately, “You will certainly go mad in that house alone, my dear.", "캐드월래더 부인은 은밀히 말했다."], ["You will see visions. We have all got to exert ourselves a little to keep sane, and call things by the same names as other people call them by.", "\"그 집에서 혼자 지내면 자네는 분명 미쳐 버릴 거야, 그러면 헛것을 보게 될 테고."], ["To be sure, for younger sons and women who have no money, it is a sort of provision to go mad: they are taken care of then.", "우리는 모두 제정신을 유지하려고, 또 다른 사람들이 부르는 이름과 똑같이 사물을 부르려고 조금은 애써야 한다네. 물론 돈 없는 차남들이나 여자들에게는 미치는 게 일종의 살길이긴 하지."], ["But you must not run into that.", "그러면 보살핌을 받게 되니까. 하지만 자네는 그렇게 빠져들면 안 돼."], ["I dare say you are a little bored here with our good dowager; but think what a bore you might become yourself to your fellow-creatures if you were always playing tragedy queen and taking things sublimely.", "우리 마음씨 좋은 노부인 때문에 여기서 좀 지루할 거라는 건 인정하네. 하지만 자네가 늘 비극의 여왕을 연기하며 만사를 숭고하게 받아들인다면, 동료 인간들에게 자네 자신이 얼마나 지루한 존재가 될지 생각해 보게."], ["Sitting alone in that library at Lowick you may fancy yourself ruling the weather; you must get a few people round you who wouldn’t believe you if you told them.", "로윅의 그 서재에 홀로 앉아 있으면 자네가 날씨를 다스린다고 착각하게 될지도 몰라. 그러니 자네가 무슨 말을 해도 믿지 않을 사람들을 몇 명 곁에 두어야 해."], ["That is a good lowering medicine.”", "그게 좋은 진정제거든.\""]], [["“I never called everything by the same name that all the people about me did,”", "\"저는 제 주위 사람들이 모든 것을 부르는 이름과 똑같이 부른 적이 한 번도 없는걸요.\""], ["said Dorothea, stoutly.", "도로테아가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“But I suppose you have found out your mistake, my dear,” said Mrs.", "\"하지만 이제 자네의 실수를 깨달았겠지, 그렇지?\""], ["Cadwallader, “and that is a proof of sanity.”", "캐드월래더 부인이 말했다. \"그게 바로 제정신이라는 증거라네.\""]], [["Dorothea was aware of the sting, but it did not hurt her.", "도로테아는 그 가시를 의식했지만, 마음이 상하지는 않았다."], ["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["she said, “I still think that the greater part of the world is mistaken about many things.", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 여전히 세상 대부분이 많은 것에 대해 잘못 생각하고 있다고 봐요."], ["Surely one may be sane and yet think so, since the greater part of the world has often had to come round from its opinion.”", "분명 제정신이면서도 그렇게 생각할 수 있어요. 세상 대부분이 종종 자기 의견을 바꾸어야 했으니까요.\""]], [["Mrs. Cadwallader said no more on that point to Dorothea, but to her husband she remarked, “It will be well for her to marry again as soon as it is proper, if one could get her among the right people.", "캐드월래더 부인은 그 점에 대해 도로테아에게 더 말하지 않았으나, 남편에게는 이렇게 언급했다."], ["Of course the Chettams would not wish it.", "\"적당한 때가 되면 가능한 한 빨리 다시 결혼하는 게 저 사람에게 좋을 거예요."], ["But I see clearly a husband is the best thing to keep her in order.", "제대로 된 사람들 틈에 넣어 줄 수만 있다면 말이죠. 물론 체텀가에서는 원치 않겠지만."], ["If we were not so poor I would invite Lord Triton.", "하지만 저 사람을 다잡는 데는 남편이 제일이라는 게 내 눈에는 훤히 보여요. 우리가 이렇게 가난하지만 않다면 트라이턴 경을 초대하겠어요."], ["He will be marquis some day, and there is no denying that she would make a good marchioness: she looks handsomer than ever in her mourning.”", "그는 언젠가 후작이 될 사람이고, 저 여자가 훌륭한 후작 부인이 될 거라는 건 부인할 수 없죠. 상복을 입으니 그 어느 때보다 아름다워 보이잖아요.\""]], [["“My dear Elinor, do let the poor woman alone.", "\"여보 엘리너, 제발 그 가엾은 여자를 가만 좀 내버려 두구려."], ["Such contrivances are of no use,”", "그런 꾀를 부려 봐야 아무 소용 없소.\""], ["said the easy Rector.", "느긋한 교구 목사가 말했다."]], [["“No use?", "\"소용없다고요?"], ["How are matches made, except by bringing men and women together?", "남자와 여자를 한자리에 모으는 것 말고 어떻게 혼사가 이루어지겠어요?"], ["And it is a shame that her uncle should have run away and shut up the Grange just now.", "게다가 하필 지금 저 여자 삼촌이 달아나 그레인지를 닫아 버린 게 너무하지 뭐예요."], ["There ought to be plenty of eligible matches invited to Freshitt and the Grange.", "프레시트와 그레인지에 마땅한 혼처들을 잔뜩 초대해야 마땅한데."], ["Lord Triton is precisely the man: full of plans for making the people happy in a soft-headed sort of way.", "트라이턴 경이 딱 알맞은 사람이에요. 좀 무른 데가 있긴 해도 사람들을 행복하게 해 줄 계획으로 가득한 사람이거든요."], ["That would just suit Mrs. Casaubon.”", "그게 캐소본 부인에게 딱 어울려요.\""]], [["“Let Mrs. Casaubon choose for herself, Elinor.”", "\"캐소본 부인이 스스로 고르도록 두구려, 엘리너.\""]], [["“That is the nonsense you wise men talk!", "\"그게 바로 당신네 똑똑한 양반들이 늘어놓는 헛소리예요!"], ["How can she choose if she has no variety to choose from?", "고를 만한 변변한 선택지가 없는데 어떻게 고른단 말예요?"], ["A woman’s choice usually means taking the only man she can get.", "여자의 선택이란 대개 손에 넣을 수 있는 단 하나의 남자를 받아들이는 거라고요."], ["Mark my words, Humphrey.", "내 말 명심해요, 험프리."], ["If her friends don’t exert themselves, there will be a worse business than the Casaubon business yet.”", "저 여자 친구들이 애쓰지 않으면 캐소본 사건보다 더 고약한 일이 또 벌어질 거예요.\""]], [["“For heaven’s sake don’t touch on that topic, Elinor!", "\"제발 그 이야기는 꺼내지 마시오, 엘리너!"], ["It is a very sore point with Sir James.", "제임스 경에게는 아주 아픈 대목이라오."], ["He would be deeply offended if you entered on it to him unnecessarily.”", "당신이 괜히 그 양반 앞에서 그 일을 들먹였다간 크게 노여워할 거요.\""]], [["“I have never entered on it,”", "\"나는 그 이야기를 꺼낸 적이 한 번도 없어요.\""], ["said Mrs Cadwallader, opening her hands.", "캐드월래더 부인이 두 손을 펼치며 말했다."], ["“Celia told me all about the will at the beginning, without any asking of mine.”", "\"실리아가 처음부터 유언장에 관해 다 이야기해 준 거예요. 내가 묻지도 않았는데 말이죠.\""]], [["“Yes, yes; but they want the thing hushed up, and I understand that the young fellow is going out of the neighborhood.”", "\"그래, 그래. 하지만 그쪽에서는 그 일을 덮어 두고 싶어 하고, 내가 듣기로는 그 젊은 친구가 이 동네를 떠난다더군.\""]], [["Mrs. Cadwallader said nothing, but gave her husband three significant nods, with a very sarcastic expression in her dark eyes.", "캐드월래더 부인은 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 검은 두 눈에 몹시 빈정대는 표정을 띤 채 남편을 향해 의미심장하게 세 번 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["Dorothea quietly persisted in spite of remonstrance and persuasion.", "도로테아는 만류와 설득에도 불구하고 조용히 고집을 부렸다."], ["So by the end of June the shutters were all opened at Lowick Manor, and the morning gazed calmly into the library, shining on the rows of note-books as it shines on the weary waste planted with huge stones, the mute memorial of a forgotten faith; and the evening laden with roses entered silently into the blue-green boudoir where Dorothea chose oftenest to sit.", "그리하여 6월 말이 되자 로윅 저택의 덧창이 모두 열렸고, 아침 햇살은 잊힌 신앙의 말 없는 기념물인 거대한 돌들이 심긴 황량하고 쓸쓸한 들판을 비추듯 차분하게 서재를 들여다보며 줄지어 늘어선 노트들 위를 비추었다. 그리고 장미꽃을 가득 실은 저녁은 도로테아가 가장 즐겨 앉던 청록색 내실로 소리 없이 스며들었다."], ["At first she walked into every room, questioning the eighteen months of her married life, and carrying on her thoughts as if they were a speech to be heard by her husband.", "처음에 그녀는 방마다 들어가 보며 자신의 결혼 생활 열여덟 달을 되짚었고, 남편이 들어야 할 이야기인 양 생각을 이어 갔다."], ["Then, she lingered in the library and could not be at rest till she had carefully ranged all the note-books as she imagined that he would wish to see them, in orderly sequence.", "그러다 서재에 머물렀으며, 남편이 보고 싶어 했으리라 짐작되는 대로 노트들을 모두 정연한 순서로 조심스레 정리해 놓고서야 마음이 놓였다."], ["The pity which had been the restraining compelling motive in her life with him still clung about his image, even while she remonstrated with him in indignant thought and told him that he was unjust.", "그와 함께한 삶에서 억제하고 강제하는 동력이 되었던 그 연민은, 분개한 마음으로 그에게 항변하며 당신은 부당하다고 따지는 와중에도 여전히 그의 모습에 들러붙어 있었다."], ["One little act of hers may perhaps be smiled at as superstitious. The Synoptical Tabulation for the use of Mrs.", "그녀의 어떤 사소한 행동 하나는 어쩌면 미신적이라며 미소를 자아낼지도 모른다."], ["Casaubon, she carefully enclosed and sealed, writing within the envelope, “I could not use it.", "캐소본 부인이 쓰도록 마련된 그 개요표를, 그녀는 조심스럽게 봉투에 넣어 봉인하고는 봉투 안에 이렇게 적었다. \"저는 이것을 쓸 수 없었어요."], ["Do you not see now that I could not submit my soul to yours, by working hopelessly at what I have no belief in—Dorothea?”", "제가 믿지 않는 일에 가망 없이 매달림으로써 제 영혼을 당신의 영혼에 복종시킬 수 없었다는 걸 이제 아시겠지요? ― 도로테아.\""], ["Then she deposited the paper in her own desk.", "그러고는 그 종이를 자기 책상에 넣어 두었다."]], [["That silent colloquy was perhaps only the more earnest because underneath and through it all there was always the deep longing which had really determined her to come to Lowick.", "그 말 없는 대화는 어쩌면 그 모든 것 아래로, 그리고 그것을 관통해 늘 흐르던 깊은 갈망, 진정으로 그녀를 로윅으로 오게 만든 그 갈망 때문에 한층 더 간절했는지도 모른다. 그 갈망은 윌 래디슬로를 보고 싶다는 것이었다."], ["The longing was to see Will Ladislaw.", "그녀는 두 사람의 만남에서 무슨 좋은 일이 생길 수 있을지 알지 못했다."], ["She did not know any good that could come of their meeting: she was helpless; her hands had been tied from making up to him for any unfairness in his lot.", "그녀는 속수무책이었다. 그의 처지에 깃든 온갖 부당함을 보상해 줄 길은 손이 묶인 듯 막혀 있었다."], ["But her soul thirsted to see him. How could it be otherwise?", "하지만 그녀의 영혼은 그를 보고 싶어 목말라했다."], ["If a princess in the days of enchantment had seen a four-footed creature from among those which live in herds come to her once and again with a human gaze which rested upon her with choice and beseeching, what would she think of in her journeying, what would she look for when the herds passed her?", "어찌 그러지 않을 수 있겠는가? 마법의 시대에 어느 공주가, 무리 지어 사는 짐승들 가운데 한 마리가 자신을 택하여 간청하듯 머무는 인간의 눈빛을 띠고 거듭 자기에게 다가오는 것을 보았다면, 길을 가는 동안 무엇을 생각하고, 그 무리가 곁을 지날 때 무엇을 찾으려 하겠는가?"], ["Surely for the gaze which had found her, and which she would know again.", "분명 자신을 알아본 그 눈빛, 다시 보면 알아볼 그 눈빛을 찾으려 할 것이다."], ["Life would be no better than candle-light tinsel and daylight rubbish if our spirits were not touched by what has been, to issues of longing and constancy.", "우리의 영혼이 지나간 것에 의해 갈망과 변함없음의 결말로 이끌리지 않는다면, 인생은 촛불 빛 장식이나 대낮의 쓰레기만도 못할 것이다."], ["It was true that Dorothea wanted to know the Farebrothers better, and especially to talk to the new rector, but also true that remembering what Lydgate had told her about Will Ladislaw and little Miss Noble, she counted on Will’s coming to Lowick to see the Farebrother family.", "도로테아가 페어브러더 댁 사람들을 더 잘 알고 싶어 했고 특히 새 교구 목사와 이야기하고 싶어 한 것은 사실이었다. 하지만 윌 래디슬로와 어린 노블 양에 관해 리드게이트가 들려준 이야기를 떠올리며, 윌이 페어브러더 가족을 보러 로윅에 오리라고 기대한 것 또한 사실이었다."], ["The very first Sunday, before she entered the church, she saw him as she had seen him the last time she was there, alone in the clergyman’s pew; but when she entered his figure was gone.", "바로 첫 번째 일요일, 교회에 들어서기 전에 그녀는 지난번 그곳에 갔을 때 보았던 그 모습 그대로, 성직자석에 홀로 앉은 그를 보았다. 하지만 막상 안으로 들어서자 그의 모습은 사라지고 없었다."]], [["In the week-days when she went to see the ladies at the Rectory, she listened in vain for some word that they might let fall about Will; but it seemed to her that Mrs.", "평일에 교구 목사관의 부인들을 만나러 갔을 때, 그녀는 그들이 윌에 관해 흘릴지도 모를 한마디를 헛되이 기다렸다."], ["Farebrother talked of every one else in the neighborhood and out of it.", "하지만 그녀가 보기에 페어브러더 부인은 그 동네 안팎의 다른 사람들 이야기는 다 하면서도 정작 그 이야기는 하지 않는 듯했다."]], [["“Probably some of Mr. Farebrother’s Middlemarch hearers may follow him to Lowick sometimes.", "\"아마 페어브러더 씨의 미들마치 신도들 가운데 더러는 가끔 로윅까지 그분을 따라오기도 하겠지요."], ["Do you not think so?”", "그렇게 생각지 않으세요?\""], ["said Dorothea, rather despising herself for having a secret motive in asking the question.", "도로테아가 물으면서, 그런 질문에 은밀한 속셈을 품은 자신을 다소 경멸하며 말했다."]], [["“If they are wise they will, Mrs. Casaubon,”", "\"현명한 사람들이라면 그러겠지요, 캐소본 부인.\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."], ["“I see that you set a right value on my son’s preaching.", "\"부인께서 제 아들의 설교에 마땅한 가치를 두어 주시니 알겠어요."], ["His grandfather on my side was an excellent clergyman, but his father was in the law:—most exemplary and honest nevertheless, which is a reason for our never being rich.", "제 친정 쪽 할아버지는 훌륭한 성직자셨지만, 아들 아비는 법률 쪽이었답니다. 그럼에도 더없이 모범적이고 정직한 사람이었는데, 그게 바로 우리가 결코 부유해질 수 없었던 까닭이지요."], ["They say Fortune is a woman and capricious.", "운명의 여신은 여자라서 변덕스럽다고들 하잖아요."], ["But sometimes she is a good woman and gives to those who merit, which has been the case with you, Mrs. Casaubon, who have given a living to my son.”", "하지만 때로는 그분도 선량한 여인이 되어 마땅한 이들에게 베푸시는데, 제 아들에게 성직록을 주신 부인이 바로 그런 경우랍니다, 캐소본 부인.\""]], [["Mrs. Farebrother recurred to her knitting with a dignified satisfaction in her neat little effort at oratory, but this was not what Dorothea wanted to hear.", "페어브러더 부인은 자신의 단정하고 자그마한 웅변 솜씨에 점잖은 만족을 느끼며 다시 뜨개질로 돌아갔으나, 이것은 도로테아가 듣고 싶어 한 말이 아니었다."], ["Poor thing!", "가엾은 여인!"], ["she did not even know whether Will Ladislaw was still at Middlemarch, and there was no one whom she dared to ask, unless it were Lydgate.", "그녀는 윌 래디슬로가 아직 미들마치에 있는지조차 알지 못했고, 리드게이트가 아니라면 감히 물어볼 사람이 아무도 없었다."], ["But just now she could not see Lydgate without sending for him or going to seek him.", "하지만 지금으로서는 그를 부르러 보내거나 직접 찾아가지 않고서는 리드게이트를 만날 수 없었다."], ["Perhaps Will Ladislaw, having heard of that strange ban against him left by Mr.", "어쩌면 윌 래디슬로는 캐소본 씨가 자신에게 남긴 그 기이한 금지 조항을 전해 듣고서, 자기와 그녀가 다시 만나지 않는 편이 낫다고 느꼈는지도 몰랐다."], ["Casaubon, had felt it better that he and she should not meet again, and perhaps she was wrong to wish for a meeting that others might find many good reasons against.", "또 어쩌면 다른 이들이 반대할 그럴듯한 이유를 잔뜩 댈 만한 만남을 바라는 것은 그녀가 잘못한 일인지도 몰랐다."], ["Still “I do wish it” came at the end of those wise reflections as naturally as a sob after holding the breath.", "그래도 그 모든 현명한 성찰의 끝에는 \"그래도 나는 그걸 바라\"라는 말이, 숨을 참다가 터져 나오는 흐느낌처럼 자연스레 따라 나왔다."], ["And the meeting did happen, but in a formal way quite unexpected by her.", "그리고 그 만남은 정말로 이루어졌으나, 그녀가 전혀 예상치 못한 격식 차린 방식으로였다."]], [["One morning, about eleven, Dorothea was seated in her boudoir with a map of the land attached to the manor and other papers before her, which were to help her in making an exact statement for herself of her income and affairs.", "어느 날 아침 열한 시쯤, 도로테아는 자기 내실에 앉아 저택에 딸린 토지의 지도와 다른 서류들을 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 있었는데, 그것들은 자신의 수입과 재정 상태를 스스로 정확히 파악하는 데 도움이 될 것들이었다."], ["She had not yet applied herself to her work, but was seated with her hands folded on her lap, looking out along the avenue of limes to the distant fields.", "하지만 그녀는 아직 일에 손을 대지 못한 채, 두 손을 무릎에 포갠 채로 앉아, 보리수 가로수 길 너머 멀리 들판을 바라보고 있었다."], ["Every leaf was at rest in the sunshine, the familiar scene was changeless, and seemed to represent the prospect of her life, full of motiveless ease—motiveless, if her own energy could not seek out reasons for ardent action.", "햇살 속에서 잎사귀 하나하나가 고요히 쉬고 있었고, 그 낯익은 풍경은 변함이 없었으며, 동기 없는 안락으로 가득한 그녀의 인생 전망을 나타내는 듯했다. 그녀 자신의 활력이 열렬한 행동의 이유를 찾아내지 못한다면, 동기 없는 안락 말이다."], ["The widow’s cap of those times made an oval frame for the face, and had a crown standing up; the dress was an experiment in the utmost laying on of crape; but this heavy solemnity of clothing made her face look all the younger, with its recovered bloom, and the sweet, inquiring candor of her eyes.", "그 시절의 과부 모자는 얼굴을 감싸는 타원형 테두리를 이루며 위로 솟은 관 모양을 하고 있었고, 드레스는 크레이프 천을 최대한 겹쳐 두른 시도와도 같았다. 하지만 이 묵직하고 엄숙한 의복은 오히려 그녀의 얼굴을, 되살아난 생기와 다정하고 묻는 듯한 솔직한 눈동자와 더불어 한층 더 앳되어 보이게 했다."]], [["Her reverie was broken by Tantripp, who came to say that Mr. Ladislaw was below, and begged permission to see Madam if it were not too early.", "그녀의 몽상은 탠트립이 들어와, 래디슬로 씨가 아래층에 와 계시는데 너무 이르지 않다면 마님을 뵙고 싶다고 청한다는 말을 전하면서 깨어졌다."]], [["“I will see him,”", "\"만나뵐게요.\""], ["said Dorothea, rising immediately.", "도로테아가 즉시 일어서며 말했다."], ["“Let him be shown into the drawing-room.”", "\"응접실로 안내해 드려요.\""]], [["The drawing-room was the most neutral room in the house to her—the one least associated with the trials of her married life: the damask matched the wood-work, which was all white and gold; there were two tall mirrors and tables with nothing on them—in brief, it was a room where you had no reason for sitting in one place rather than in another.", "응접실은 그녀에게 집 안에서 가장 무난한 방, 결혼 생활의 시련과 가장 적게 결부된 방이었다. 다마스크 천은 온통 흰색과 금색으로 된 목조 부분과 잘 어우러졌고, 키 큰 거울이 두 개 있었으며, 아무것도 올려놓지 않은 탁자들이 있었다."], ["It was below the boudoir, and had also a bow-window looking out on the avenue.", "한마디로 어느 한자리에 앉을 까닭이 다른 자리보다 더 있는 것도 아닌 방이었다."], ["But when Pratt showed Will Ladislaw into it the window was open; and a winged visitor, buzzing in and out now and then without minding the furniture, made the room look less formal and uninhabited.", "그 방은 내실 아래층에 있었고, 역시 가로수 길이 내다보이는 활 모양 내닫이창이 있었다. 하지만 프랫이 윌 래디슬로를 그 방으로 안내했을 때 창문은 열려 있었고, 날개 달린 손님 하나가 가구에는 아랑곳없이 이따금 윙윙대며 들락거려, 그 방을 덜 격식 차리고 사람이 살지 않는 듯한 느낌을 덜어 주었다."]], [["“Glad to see you here again, sir,”", "\"다시 뵙게 되어 반갑습니다, 나리.\""], ["said Pratt, lingering to adjust a blind.", "블라인드를 매만지느라 머뭇거리며 프랫이 말했다."]], [["“I am only come to say good-by, Pratt,”", "\"작별 인사를 하러 왔을 뿐이네, 프랫.\" 윌이 말했다."], ["said Will, who wished even the butler to know that he was too proud to hang about Mrs. Casaubon now she was a rich widow.", "그는 캐소본 부인이 부유한 과부가 된 지금, 자기가 그녀 주위를 맴돌기에는 너무 자존심이 강하다는 것을 집사에게까지 알리고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["“Very sorry to hear it, sir,”", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 참으로 섭섭합니다, 나리.\""], ["said Pratt, retiring.", "프랫이 물러가며 말했다."], ["Of course, as a servant who was to be told nothing, he knew the fact of which Ladislaw was still ignorant, and had drawn his inferences; indeed, had not differed from his betrothed Tantripp when she said, “Your master was as jealous as a fiend—and no reason.", "물론 아무것도 들어서는 안 될 하인으로서, 그는 래디슬로가 아직 모르는 사실을 알고 있었고 나름의 추론을 내려 둔 터였다. 실제로 그는 약혼녀 탠트립이 이렇게 말했을 때 이의를 달지 않았다."], ["Madam would look higher than Mr.", "\"주인어른이 악마처럼 질투를 하셨다니까."], ["Ladislaw, else I don’t know her. Mrs.", "그것도 아무 이유 없이 말이야."], ["Cadwallader’s maid says there’s a lord coming who is to marry her when the mourning’s over.”", "마님이라면 래디슬로 씨보다는 더 높은 데를 보실 분이지, 안 그러면 내가 마님을 모르는 거고. 캐드월래더 부인 댁 하녀 말로는, 상이 끝나면 마님과 결혼할 어떤 귀족 나리가 오신다더라고.\""]], [["There were not many moments for Will to walk about with his hat in his hand before Dorothea entered.", "도로테아가 들어오기 전, 윌이 손에 모자를 든 채 서성일 시간은 그리 많지 않았다."], ["The meeting was very different from that first meeting in Rome when Will had been embarrassed and Dorothea calm.", "그 만남은 윌이 당황했고 도로테아가 침착했던 로마에서의 첫 만남과는 사뭇 달랐다. 이번에는 그가 비참하면서도 단호했던 반면, 그녀는 감출 수 없는 동요의 상태에 있었다."], ["This time he felt miserable but determined, while she was in a state of agitation which could not be hidden.", "문밖에서 그녀는 그토록 고대하던 이 만남이 결국 너무나 어려운 일이라고 느꼈고, 윌이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀에게는 좀처럼 없던 깊은 홍조가 고통스러울 만큼 갑작스레 떠올랐다."], ["Just outside the door she had felt that this longed-for meeting was after all too difficult, and when she saw Will advancing towards her, the deep blush which was rare in her came with painful suddenness.", "두 사람 다 어찌 된 영문인지 알지 못했으나, 둘 다 입을 열지 못했다. 그녀는 잠시 손을 내밀었고, 이윽고 두 사람은 창가로 가서 앉았는데, 그녀는 한쪽 긴 의자에, 그는 맞은편 다른 의자에 앉았다."], ["Neither of them knew how it was, but neither of them spoke.", "윌은 유난히 불안했다."], ["She gave her hand for a moment, and then they went to sit down near the window, she on one settee and he on another opposite.", "그저 그녀가 과부가 되었다는 사실만으로 자기를 맞이하는 태도가 이렇게 달라지다니, 그에게는 도로테아답지 않게 여겨졌다."], ["Will was peculiarly uneasy: it seemed to him not like Dorothea that the mere fact of her being a widow should cause such a change in her manner of receiving him; and he knew of no other condition which could have affected their previous relation to each other—except that, as his imagination at once told him, her friends might have been poisoning her mind with their suspicions of him.", "그리고 두 사람의 이전 관계에 영향을 줄 만한 다른 사정이라고는 알지 못했다. 다만 그의 상상력이 곧바로 일러 준 대로, 그녀의 친구들이 자신에 대한 의혹으로 그녀의 마음을 물들였을 가능성을 빼고는 말이다."]], [["“I hope I have not presumed too much in calling,”", "\"제가 찾아온 것이 너무 주제넘은 일이 아니길 바랍니다.\""], ["said Will; “I could not bear to leave the neighborhood and begin a new life without seeing you to say good-by.”", "윌이 말했다. \"이 동네를 떠나 새 삶을 시작하면서, 부인을 뵙고 작별 인사를 드리지 못하는 것은 견딜 수가 없었습니다.\""]], [["“Presumed?", "\"주제넘다니요?"], ["Surely not.", "결코 아니에요."], ["I should have thought it unkind if you had not wished to see me,”", "만일 저를 보고 싶어 하지 않으셨다면 오히려 야박하다고 여겼을 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea, her habit of speaking with perfect genuineness asserting itself through all her uncertainty and agitation.", "도로테아가 말했다. 더없이 진솔하게 말하는 그녀의 습관이 온갖 망설임과 동요를 뚫고 드러난 것이다."], ["“Are you going away immediately?”", "\"곧 떠나시나요?\""]], [["“Very soon, I think.", "\"꽤 곧 떠날 것 같습니다."], ["I intend to go to town and eat my dinners as a barrister, since, they say, that is the preparation for all public business.", "도시로 가서 변호사로서 회식에 참석할 생각입니다. 그게 모든 공적인 일을 위한 준비라고들 하니까요."], ["There will be a great deal of political work to be done by-and-by, and I mean to try and do some of it.", "머지않아 정치적인 일이 산더미처럼 쌓일 텐데, 그중 일부를 해 보려 합니다."], ["Other men have managed to win an honorable position for themselves without family or money.”", "가문도 돈도 없이 스스로 명예로운 지위를 일군 사람들이 더러 있으니까요.\""]], [["“And that will make it all the more honorable,”", "\"그러면 그건 더욱더 명예로운 일이 되겠지요.\""], ["said Dorothea, ardently.", "도로테아가 열렬하게 말했다."], ["“Besides, you have so many talents.", "\"게다가 당신은 재능이 그토록 많으시잖아요. 삼촌께 들었어요."], ["I have heard from my uncle how well you speak in public, so that every one is sorry when you leave off, and how clearly you can explain things.", "당신이 사람들 앞에서 어찌나 말을 잘하는지, 당신이 말을 그치면 모두가 아쉬워한다고요. 그리고 얼마나 명료하게 설명을 잘하는지도요."], ["And you care that justice should be done to every one.", "그리고 당신은 모든 사람에게 정의가 실현되기를 바라시잖아요."], ["I am so glad.", "저는 정말 기뻐요."], ["When we were in Rome, I thought you only cared for poetry and art, and the things that adorn life for us who are well off.", "로마에 있을 때 저는 당신이 그저 시와 예술, 그리고 형편이 넉넉한 우리네 삶을 꾸며 주는 것들에만 관심이 있는 줄 알았어요."], ["But now I know you think about the rest of the world.”", "하지만 이제 당신이 나머지 세상에 대해서도 생각한다는 걸 알겠어요.\""]], [["While she was speaking Dorothea had lost her personal embarrassment, and had become like her former self.", "말하는 동안 도로테아는 개인적인 당혹감을 잊고 예전의 자신으로 되돌아가 있었다."], ["She looked at Will with a direct glance, full of delighted confidence.", "그녀는 기쁜 신뢰로 가득한 시선으로 윌을 똑바로 바라보았다."]], [["“You approve of my going away for years, then, and never coming here again till I have made myself of some mark in the world?”", "\"그러면 부인께서는, 제가 몇 년간 떠나 있다가 세상에서 어느 정도 인정받기 전까지는 이곳에 다시 오지 않는 것을 찬성하시는 거군요?\""], ["said Will, trying hard to reconcile the utmost pride with the utmost effort to get an expression of strong feeling from Dorothea.", "윌이 더없는 자존심과, 도로테아에게서 강한 감정의 표현을 끌어내려는 더없는 노력을 어떻게든 양립시키려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["She was not aware how long it was before she answered.", "그녀는 대답하기까지 얼마나 오랜 시간이 흘렀는지 의식하지 못했다."], ["She had turned her head and was looking out of the window on the rose-bushes, which seemed to have in them the summers of all the years when Will would be away.", "그녀는 고개를 돌려 창밖의 장미 덤불을 바라보고 있었는데, 그 덤불 속에는 윌이 떠나 있을 그 모든 세월의 여름이 담겨 있는 듯했다."], ["This was not judicious behavior.", "이것은 분별 있는 처신이 아니었다."], ["But Dorothea never thought of studying her manners: she thought only of bowing to a sad necessity which divided her from Will.", "하지만 도로테아는 자기 태도를 따져 볼 생각 따위는 결코 하지 않았다. 그녀는 다만 자신을 윌과 갈라놓는 그 슬픈 필연에 고개 숙일 생각만 했다."], ["Those first words of his about his intentions had seemed to make everything clear to her: he knew, she supposed, all about Mr.", "자기 의도에 관한 그의 첫마디는 그녀에게 모든 것을 분명히 해 주는 듯했다."], ["Casaubon’s final conduct in relation to him, and it had come to him with the same sort of shock as to herself.", "그녀가 짐작하기로, 그는 자신과 관련된 캐소본 씨의 마지막 처사를 모두 알고 있었고, 그것이 그녀에게 그랬듯 그에게도 똑같은 충격으로 다가왔던 것이다."], ["He had never felt more than friendship for her—had never had anything in his mind to justify what she felt to be her husband’s outrage on the feelings of both: and that friendship he still felt.", "그는 그녀에게 우정 이상의 감정을 결코 품은 적이 없었고, 두 사람의 감정에 대한 남편의 모욕이라고 그녀가 느낀 그 일을 정당화할 만한 것을 마음에 둔 적도 없었다. 그리고 그 우정만큼은 그가 여전히 느끼고 있었다."], ["Something which may be called an inward silent sob had gone on in Dorothea before she said with a pure voice, just trembling in the last words as if only from its liquid flexibility—", "도로테아의 내면에서는 내적이고 소리 없는 흐느낌이라 할 만한 무언가가 흘렀고, 이윽고 그녀는 맑은 목소리로, 그저 그 유려한 떨림 때문인 양 마지막 말들이 살짝 떨리는 채로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it must be right for you to do as you say.", "\"네, 당신 말씀대로 하는 것이 분명 옳을 거예요."], ["I shall be very happy when I hear that you have made your value felt.", "당신이 자기 가치를 인정받게 되었다는 소식을 들으면 저는 무척 행복할 거예요."], ["But you must have patience.", "하지만 인내심을 가지셔야 해요."], ["It will perhaps be a long while.”", "어쩌면 아주 오랜 세월이 걸릴 테니까요.\""]], [["Will never quite knew how it was that he saved himself from falling down at her feet, when the “long while” came forth with its gentle tremor.", "윌은 그 \"오랜 세월\"이라는 말이 부드러운 떨림과 함께 흘러나왔을 때, 자기가 그녀의 발치에 무너져 내리지 않은 것이 어떻게 가능했는지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["He used to say that the horrible hue and surface of her crape dress was most likely the sufficient controlling force.", "그는 훗날, 아마도 그녀의 크레이프 드레스의 그 끔찍한 빛깔과 촉감이야말로 충분한 억제력이었으리라고 말하곤 했다."], ["He sat still, however, and only said—", "그러나 그는 가만히 앉은 채 다만 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“I shall never hear from you.", "\"저는 결코 부인의 소식을 듣지 못할 겁니다."], ["And you will forget all about me.”", "그리고 부인은 저에 관한 것을 모조리 잊으시겠지요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, “I shall never forget you.", "도로테아가 말했다. \"저는 결코 당신을 잊지 않을 거예요."], ["I have never forgotten any one whom I once knew.", "저는 한번 알게 된 사람을 잊은 적이 없어요."], ["My life has never been crowded, and seems not likely to be so.", "제 삶은 한 번도 북적인 적이 없었고, 앞으로도 그럴 것 같지 않으니까요."], ["And I have a great deal of space for memory at Lowick, haven’t I?”", "게다가 로윅에는 기억을 위한 자리가 아주 넉넉하잖아요, 그렇지 않아요?\""], ["She smiled.", "그녀가 미소 지었다."]], [["“Good God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["Will burst out passionately, rising, with his hat still in his hand, and walking away to a marble table, where he suddenly turned and leaned his back against it.", "윌이 격정에 차서 외치며, 여전히 손에 모자를 든 채 일어나 대리석 탁자 쪽으로 걸어가더니, 별안간 돌아서서 거기에 등을 기댔다."], ["The blood had mounted to his face and neck, and he looked almost angry.", "피가 그의 얼굴과 목까지 솟구쳐 올라, 그는 거의 화가 난 듯 보였다."], ["It had seemed to him as if they were like two creatures slowly turning to marble in each other’s presence, while their hearts were conscious and their eyes were yearning.", "그에게는 두 사람이 마치, 심장은 의식하고 두 눈은 갈망하면서도 서로의 면전에서 천천히 대리석으로 굳어 가는 두 존재처럼 여겨졌다."], ["But there was no help for it.", "하지만 어쩔 도리가 없었다."], ["It should never be true of him that in this meeting to which he had come with bitter resolution he had ended by a confession which might be interpreted into asking for her fortune.", "쓰라린 결심을 품고 찾아온 이 만남에서, 그가 결국 그녀의 재산을 달라고 청하는 것으로 해석될 수 있는 고백으로 끝맺었다는 말이, 자신에게는 결코 진실이 되어서는 안 되었다."], ["Moreover, it was actually true that he was fearful of the effect which such confessions might have on Dorothea herself.", "게다가 그런 고백이 도로테아 자신에게 미칠 영향이 두렵다는 것 또한 사실이었다."]], [["She looked at him from that distance in some trouble, imagining that there might have been an offence in her words.", "그녀는 그 거리에서 다소 곤혹스러워하며 그를 바라보았다. 자기 말에 무언가 거슬리는 데가 있었던 것은 아닐까 싶었다."], ["But all the while there was a current of thought in her about his probable want of money, and the impossibility of her helping him.", "하지만 그 와중에도 그녀의 마음속에는 그가 돈이 궁할 법하다는 것, 그리고 자신이 그를 도울 길이 없다는 생각의 물줄기가 줄곧 흐르고 있었다."], ["If her uncle had been at home, something might have been done through him!", "삼촌이 집에 계셨다면 그분을 통해 무언가 할 수 있었을 텐데!"], ["It was this preoccupation with the hardship of Will’s wanting money, while she had what ought to have been his share, which led her to say, seeing that he remained silent and looked away from her—", "윌이 돈이 궁한 처지인데 정작 그의 몫이 되었어야 할 것을 자신이 가지고 있다는 그 안타까움에 사로잡혀 있던 까닭에, 그녀는 그가 여전히 입을 다문 채 시선을 돌리고 있는 것을 보며 이렇게 말하게 되었다."]], [["“I wonder whether you would like to have that miniature which hangs up-stairs—I mean that beautiful miniature of your grandmother.", "\"위층에 걸려 있는 그 세밀화 초상 말인데요. 당신 할머니의 그 아름다운 세밀화 초상을 가지고 싶지 않으세요?"], ["I think it is not right for me to keep it, if you would wish to have it.", "당신이 그것을 갖기를 바라신다면, 제가 간직하고 있는 것은 옳지 않은 듯해요."], ["It is wonderfully like you.”", "그것은 당신과 놀랍도록 닮았거든요.\""]], [["“You are very good,”", "\"참 친절하시군요.\""], ["said Will, irritably.", "윌이 짜증스레 말했다."], ["“No; I don’t mind about it.", "\"아니요, 저는 그것에 마음 두지 않습니다."], ["It is not very consoling to have one’s own likeness.", "제 자신의 초상을 갖는 것은 그리 위안이 되지 않거든요."], ["It would be more consoling if others wanted to have it.”", "다른 사람들이 그걸 갖고 싶어 한다면 더 큰 위안이 되겠지만요.\""]], [["“I thought you would like to cherish her memory—I thought—” Dorothea broke off an instant, her imagination suddenly warning her away from Aunt Julia’s history—“you would surely like to have the miniature as a family memorial.”", "\"당신이 할머니의 기억을 소중히 간직하고 싶어 하실 줄 알았어요. 저는 그저ㅡ\" 도로테아는 잠시 말을 멈추었다. 줄리아 고모의 내력 쪽으로는 가지 말라고 그녀의 상상력이 별안간 경고했던 것이다. \"그 세밀화를 가문의 기념물로 분명 갖고 싶어 하시리라 생각했어요.\""]], [["“Why should I have that, when I have nothing else!", "\"제가 그걸 왜 갖겠습니까, 다른 건 아무것도 없는 마당에!"], ["A man with only a portmanteau for his stowage must keep his memorials in his head.”", "짐이라곤 여행 가방 하나뿐인 사람은 기념물을 머릿속에 간직하는 수밖에요.\""]], [["Will spoke at random: he was merely venting his petulance; it was a little too exasperating to have his grandmother’s portrait offered him at that moment.", "윌은 닥치는 대로 말했다. 그는 그저 짜증을 토해 내고 있었을 뿐이다."], ["But to Dorothea’s feeling his words had a peculiar sting.", "바로 그 순간에 할머니의 초상화를 받으라는 권유를 받는 것은 다소 지나치게 부아가 치미는 일이었다."], ["She rose and said with a touch of indignation as well as hauteur—", "하지만 도로테아의 감정에 그의 말은 독특한 가시처럼 박혔다. 그녀는 일어서서 분개와 함께 거만함이 살짝 깃든 어조로 말했다."]], [["“You are much the happier of us two, Mr. Ladislaw, to have nothing.”", "\"우리 둘 중에는 당신이 훨씬 더 행복한 사람이네요, 래디슬로 씨, 아무것도 없으니 말이에요.\""]], [["Will was startled.", "윌은 흠칫했다."], ["Whatever the words might be, the tone seemed like a dismissal; and quitting his leaning posture, he walked a little way towards her.", "말이야 어떻든, 그 어조는 마치 내쫓는 것처럼 들렸다. 그는 기대고 있던 자세를 풀고 그녀 쪽으로 조금 다가갔다."], ["Their eyes met, but with a strange questioning gravity.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤으나, 기이하게 묻는 듯한 진지함을 띤 채였다."], ["Something was keeping their minds aloof, and each was left to conjecture what was in the other.", "무언가가 두 사람의 마음을 서로 멀리 떼어 놓고 있었고, 각자는 상대의 마음속에 무엇이 있는지 추측하는 데에 내맡겨져 있었다."], ["Will had really never thought of himself as having a claim of inheritance on the property which was held by Dorothea, and would have required a narrative to make him understand her present feeling.", "윌은 도로테아가 보유한 재산에 대해 자신에게 상속권 같은 것이 있다고는 정말이지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었으므로, 그녀의 지금 심정을 이해하려면 사연을 처음부터 들어야 했을 것이다."]], [["“I never felt it a misfortune to have nothing till now,”", "\"지금까지는 아무것도 없는 것을 불행이라 느낀 적이 없었습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But poverty may be as bad as leprosy, if it divides us from what we most care for.”", "\"하지만 가난이, 우리가 가장 아끼는 것으로부터 우리를 갈라놓는다면, 그것은 나병만큼이나 고약한 것일 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["The words cut Dorothea to the heart, and made her relent.", "그 말은 도로테아의 가슴을 찔렀고, 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨렸다."], ["She answered in a tone of sad fellowship.", "그녀는 슬픈 동류의식이 깃든 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Sorrow comes in so many ways.", "\"슬픔은 참으로 여러 모습으로 찾아오지요."], ["Two years ago I had no notion of that—I mean of the unexpected way in which trouble comes, and ties our hands, and makes us silent when we long to speak.", "이 년 전만 해도 저는 그런 것을 몰랐어요. 그러니까, 시련이 뜻밖의 방식으로 찾아와서 우리 손을 묶어 버리고, 말하고 싶을 때 우리를 침묵하게 만든다는 것을요."], ["I used to despise women a little for not shaping their lives more, and doing better things.", "저는 여자들이 자기 삶을 더 잘 빚어내지 못하고 더 나은 일을 하지 못한다고 조금은 경멸하곤 했어요."], ["I was very fond of doing as I liked, but I have almost given it up,”", "저는 제 멋대로 하는 것을 무척 좋아했지만, 이제는 거의 그만두었답니다.\""], ["she ended, smiling playfully.", "그녀가 장난스레 미소 지으며 끝맺었다."]], [["“I have not given up doing as I like, but I can very seldom do it,”", "\"저는 제 멋대로 하는 것을 그만두지는 못했지만, 좀처럼 그렇게 할 수가 없습니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["He was standing two yards from her with his mind full of contradictory desires and resolves—desiring some unmistakable proof that she loved him, and yet dreading the position into which such a proof might bring him.", "그는 그녀에게서 두 야드쯤 떨어져 서서, 서로 모순되는 욕망과 결심으로 마음이 가득 차 있었다. 그녀가 자신을 사랑한다는 틀림없는 증거를 바라면서도, 그런 증거가 자신을 몰아넣을 처지를 두려워하는 마음이었다."], ["“The thing one most longs for may be surrounded with conditions that would be intolerable.”", "\"가장 간절히 바라는 것이, 견딜 수 없는 조건들에 둘러싸여 있을 수도 있는 법이니까요.\""]], [["At this moment Pratt entered and said, “Sir James Chettam is in the library, madam.”", "바로 그 순간 프랫이 들어와 말했다. \"제임스 체텀 경께서 서재에 와 계십니다, 마님.\""]], [["“Ask Sir James to come in here,”", "\"제임스 경께 이리로 들어오시라고 해요.\""], ["said Dorothea, immediately.", "도로테아가 즉시 말했다."], ["It was as if the same electric shock had passed through her and Will.", "마치 똑같은 전기 충격이 그녀와 윌을 동시에 관통한 것 같았다."], ["Each of them felt proudly resistant, and neither looked at the other, while they awaited Sir James’s entrance.", "두 사람은 각자 자존심에 차서 저항감을 느꼈고, 제임스 경이 들어오기를 기다리는 동안 누구도 상대를 쳐다보지 않았다."]], [["After shaking hands with Dorothea, he bowed as slightly as possible to Ladislaw, who repaid the slightness exactly, and then going towards Dorothea, said—", "도로테아와 악수를 나눈 뒤, 그는 래디슬로에게는 가능한 한 가볍게 고개를 숙였고, 래디슬로도 그 가벼움을 똑같이 되갚았다. 그러고는 도로테아 쪽으로 가며 말했다."]], [["“I must say good-by, Mrs.", "\"작별 인사를 드려야겠습니다, 캐소본 부인."], ["Casaubon; and probably for a long while.”", "아마도 꽤 오랫동안 못 뵐 것 같습니다.\""]], [["Dorothea put out her hand and said her good-by cordially.", "도로테아는 손을 내밀며 진심 어린 작별 인사를 건넸다."], ["The sense that Sir James was depreciating Will, and behaving rudely to him, roused her resolution and dignity: there was no touch of confusion in her manner.", "제임스 경이 윌을 깎아내리고 그에게 무례하게 굴고 있다는 느낌은 그녀의 결의와 품위를 불러일으켰다. 그녀의 태도에는 조금의 혼란도 없었다."], ["And when Will had left the room, she looked with such calm self-possession at Sir James, saying, “How is Celia?” that he was obliged to behave as if nothing had annoyed him.", "그리고 윌이 방을 나가자, 그녀는 더없이 침착하게 자제력을 발휘하며 제임스 경을 바라보고 \"실리아는 어때요?\"라고 물었기에, 그는 마치 자기를 언짢게 한 일이라곤 아무것도 없었던 것처럼 처신할 수밖에 없었다."], ["And what would be the use of behaving otherwise?", "게다가 달리 처신해 본들 무슨 소용이 있겠는가?"], ["Indeed, Sir James shrank with so much dislike from the association even in thought of Dorothea with Ladislaw as her possible lover, that he would himself have wished to avoid an outward show of displeasure which would have recognized the disagreeable possibility.", "실로 제임스 경은 도로테아를 래디슬로와 연인 사이가 될 수 있는 관계로 결부시키는 것을 생각만으로도 너무나 싫어하여 몸서리쳤기에, 그 불쾌한 가능성을 인정하는 꼴이 될 불쾌감의 외적 표출은 스스로도 피하고 싶었던 것이다."], ["If any one had asked him why he shrank in that way, I am not sure that he would at first have said anything fuller or more precise than “That Ladislaw!”—though on reflection he might have urged that Mr.", "누군가 그에게 왜 그렇게 몸서리치느냐고 물었다면, 그가 처음에는 \"저 래디슬로 녀석이라니!\" 이상으로 더 충실하거나 정확한 말을 했을지 나로서는 장담할 수 없다."], ["Casaubon’s codicil, barring Dorothea’s marriage with Will, except under a penalty, was enough to cast unfitness over any relation at all between them.", "다만 곰곰이 생각해 보면, 도로테아가 윌과 결혼하면 벌칙을 받도록 막아 놓은 캐소본 씨의 유언 추가 조항만으로도, 두 사람 사이의 그 어떤 관계에도 부적절함의 그늘을 드리우기에 충분하다고 그는 주장했을지도 모른다."], ["His aversion was all the stronger because he felt himself unable to interfere.", "그의 혐오감은, 자기가 끼어들 수 없다는 것을 느꼈기에 더욱 강했다."]], [["But Sir James was a power in a way unguessed by himself.", "하지만 제임스 경은 스스로도 짐작 못 한 방식으로 하나의 힘이었다."], ["Entering at that moment, he was an incorporation of the strongest reasons through which Will’s pride became a repellent force, keeping him asunder from Dorothea.", "바로 그 순간에 들어섬으로써, 그는 윌의 자존심을 도로테아에게서 그를 떼어 놓는 밀어내는 힘으로 만든 가장 강력한 이유들의 화신이 되었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER LV.", "# CHAPTER LV."]], [["Hath she her faults?", "그녀에게 결점이 있는가?"], ["I would you had them too.", "그대도 그런 결점을 지녔으면 좋으련만."], ["They are the fruity must of soundest wine; Or say, they are regenerating fire Such as hath turned the dense black element Into a crystal pathway for the sun.", "그것들은 더없이 순수한 포도주의 향기로운 발효즙이요, 아니, 거듭나게 하는 불길이라 할까, 짙고 검은 원소를 태워 태양을 위한 수정의 길로 바꾸어 놓은 그런 불길."]], [["If youth is the season of hope, it is often so only in the sense that our elders are hopeful about us; for no age is so apt as youth to think its emotions, partings, and resolves are the last of their kind.", "젊음이 희망의 계절이라면, 그것은 흔히 우리의 윗사람들이 우리에게 희망을 거는 한에서만 그러하다. 자신의 감정과 이별, 결심이 같은 종류의 마지막 것이라고 여기기에 젊음만큼 쉬운 시기는 없으니 말이다."], ["Each crisis seems final, simply because it is new.", "모든 위기는 단지 새롭다는 이유만으로 마지막인 듯 보인다."], ["We are told that the oldest inhabitants in Peru do not cease to be agitated by the earthquakes, but they probably see beyond each shock, and reflect that there are plenty more to come.", "페루에서 가장 오래 산 주민들도 지진에 동요하기를 멈추지 않는다고들 하지만, 그들은 아마도 각각의 충격 너머를 내다보며 앞으로도 그런 일이 얼마든지 더 일어나리라 헤아릴 것이다."]], [["To Dorothea, still in that time of youth when the eyes with their long full lashes look out after their rain of tears unsoiled and unwearied as a freshly opened passion-flower, that morning’s parting with Will Ladislaw seemed to be the close of their personal relations.", "도러시아는 긴 속눈썹이 가득한 두 눈이 눈물의 비를 쏟은 뒤에도 갓 피어난 시계꽃처럼 더럽혀지지 않고 지치지 않은 채 세상을 바라보는, 여전히 그런 젊음의 시기에 있었으니, 그날 아침 윌 래디슬로와의 이별이 두 사람의 사적인 관계의 끝인 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["He was going away into the distance of unknown years, and if ever he came back he would be another man.", "그는 알 수 없는 세월의 먼 곳으로 떠나가고 있었고, 혹여 돌아온다 해도 다른 사람이 되어 있을 터였다."], ["The actual state of his mind—his proud resolve to give the lie beforehand to any suspicion that he would play the needy adventurer seeking a rich woman—lay quite out of her imagination, and she had interpreted all his behavior easily enough by her supposition that Mr. Casaubon’s codicil seemed to him, as it did to her, a gross and cruel interdict on any active friendship between them.", "그의 마음의 실제 상태는—부유한 여인을 좇는 궁색한 모험가 노릇을 하리라는 어떤 의심에도 미리 거짓말을 하리라는 그 도도한 결심은—그녀의 상상 밖에 완전히 놓여 있었고, 그녀는 그의 모든 행동을, 캐소본 씨의 추가 유언이 자신에게 그렇듯 그에게도 두 사람 사이의 그 어떤 적극적인 우정에 대한 거칠고 잔인한 금령으로 여겨지는 듯하다는 가정으로 충분히 쉽게 해석해 버렸다."], ["Their young delight in speaking to each other, and saying what no one else would care to hear, was forever ended, and become a treasure of the past.", "서로 이야기하며 다른 누구도 들으려 하지 않을 말을 나누던 그들의 젊은 기쁨은 영원히 끝나 버렸고, 과거의 보물이 되어 버렸다."], ["For this very reason she dwelt on it without inward check.", "바로 그런 이유로 그녀는 내적인 거리낌 없이 그 기억에 머물렀다."], ["That unique happiness too was dead, and in its shadowed silent chamber she might vent the passionate grief which she herself wondered at.", "그 둘도 없는 행복마저 죽어 버렸고, 그 그늘진 고요한 방에서 그녀는 스스로도 놀랄 만큼 격렬한 슬픔을 토해 낼 수 있었다."], ["For the first time she took down the miniature from the wall and kept it before her, liking to blend the woman who had been too hardly judged with the grandson whom her own heart and judgment defended.", "그녀는 처음으로 벽에서 그 세밀화를 떼어 내 자기 앞에 두었으니, 너무 가혹하게 판단되었던 여인과 자신의 마음과 판단이 옹호하는 그 손자를 한데 섞어 그려 보는 것이 좋았다."], ["Can any one who has rejoiced in woman’s tenderness think it a reproach to her that she took the little oval picture in her palm and made a bed for it there, and leaned her cheek upon it, as if that would soothe the creatures who had suffered unjust condemnation?", "여인의 다정함에서 기쁨을 누려 본 사람이라면, 그녀가 그 작은 타원형 그림을 손바닥에 받쳐 들고 거기에 잠자리를 만들어 주듯 뺨을 기댄 것을, 마치 그렇게 하면 부당한 단죄를 받은 이들을 달랠 수 있기라도 하다는 듯한 그 행동을 어찌 비난거리라 여길 수 있겠는가?"], ["She did not know then that it was Love who had come to her briefly, as in a dream before awaking, with the hues of morning on his wings—that it was Love to whom she was sobbing her farewell as his image was banished by the blameless rigor of irresistible day.", "그녀는 그때 몰랐다. 그것이 잠에서 깨어나기 전의 꿈처럼, 날개에 아침의 빛깔을 띠고 잠시 자신을 찾아왔던 사랑이었음을—거역할 수 없는 낮의 흠 없는 엄격함이 그 형상을 내쫓을 때 그녀가 흐느끼며 작별을 고하던 상대가 바로 사랑이었음을."], ["She only felt that there was something irrevocably amiss and lost in her lot, and her thoughts about the future were the more readily shapen into resolve.", "그녀는 다만 자신의 운명에 무언가 돌이킬 수 없이 어긋나고 사라져 버린 것이 있다고 느꼈을 뿐이고, 그래서 미래에 대한 그녀의 생각은 더 쉽사리 결심의 형태로 빚어졌다."], ["Ardent souls, ready to construct their coming lives, are apt to commit themselves to the fulfilment of their own visions.", "다가올 삶을 짓고자 하는 열렬한 영혼은 자기 자신의 환상을 실현하는 일에 스스로를 내맡기기 쉬운 법이다."]], [["One day that she went to Freshitt to fulfil her promise of staying all night and seeing baby washed, Mrs. Cadwallader came to dine, the Rector being gone on a fishing excursion.", "어느 날 그녀가 하룻밤 묵으며 아기 목욕시키는 것을 보겠다는 약속을 지키러 프레시트로 갔을 때, 마침 목사가 낚시 여행을 떠나고 없던 터라 커들래더 부인이 만찬에 왔다."], ["It was a warm evening, and even in the delightful drawing-room, where the fine old turf sloped from the open window towards a lilied pool and well-planted mounds, the heat was enough to make Celia in her white muslin and light curls reflect with pity on what Dodo must feel in her black dress and close cap.", "따뜻한 저녁이었고, 열린 창문에서 고운 옛 잔디밭이 백합이 핀 연못과 나무가 잘 심긴 둔덕을 향해 비탈져 내려가는 그 쾌적한 응접실에서조차 더위가 충분히 심해서, 흰 모슬린 옷에 가벼운 곱슬머리를 한 실리아는 검은 옷에 꼭 맞는 모자를 쓴 도도가 얼마나 답답할까 측은하게 생각할 정도였다."], ["But this was not until some episodes with baby were over, and had left her mind at leisure.", "하지만 그것은 아기를 둘러싼 몇몇 일들이 끝나고 그녀의 마음에 여유가 생긴 뒤의 일이었다."], ["She had seated herself and taken up a fan for some time before she said, in her quiet guttural—", "그녀는 자리에 앉아 한동안 부채를 들고 있다가, 그 조용하고 낮은 목소리로 말했다—"]], [["“Dear Dodo, do throw off that cap.", "\"도도, 그 모자 좀 벗어요."], ["I am sure your dress must make you feel ill.”", "그 옷 때문에 분명 몸이 안 좋을 거예요.\""]], [["“I am so used to the cap—it has become a sort of shell,”", "\"이 모자에 너무 익숙해져서—일종의 껍데기처럼 되어 버렸어.\""], ["said Dorothea, smiling.", "도러시아가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I feel rather bare and exposed when it is off.”", "\"벗으면 오히려 헐벗고 드러난 것 같은 느낌이 들어.\""]], [["“I must see you without it; it makes us all warm,”", "\"모자 벗은 모습을 봐야겠어요. 그것 때문에 우리 모두 덥잖아요.\""], ["said Celia, throwing down her fan, and going to Dorothea.", "실리아는 부채를 내려놓고 도러시아에게 다가가며 말했다."], ["It was a pretty picture to see this little lady in white muslin unfastening the widow’s cap from her more majestic sister, and tossing it on to a chair.", "흰 모슬린을 입은 이 자그마한 부인이 한층 위엄 있는 언니에게서 미망인의 모자를 풀어 의자 위로 던지는 모습은 예쁜 그림이었다."], ["Just as the coils and braids of dark-brown hair had been set free, Sir James entered the room.", "짙은 갈색 머리의 타래와 땋은 머리가 막 풀려난 순간, 제임스 경이 방으로 들어왔다."], ["He looked at the released head, and said, “Ah!”", "그는 풀려난 머리를 보더니 흡족한 어조로 \"아!\""], ["in a tone of satisfaction.", "하고 말했다."]], [["“It was I who did it, James,”", "\"내가 한 거예요, 제임스.\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“Dodo need not make such a slavery of her mourning; she need not wear that cap any more among her friends.”", "\"도도가 상복을 그렇게 노예처럼 입을 필요는 없잖아요. 친구들 사이에서까지 저 모자를 쓰고 있을 필요는 없어요.\""]], [["“My dear Celia,”", "\"실리아야,\""], ["said Lady Chettam, “a widow must wear her mourning at least a year.”", "채텀 부인이 말했다. \"미망인은 적어도 일 년은 상복을 입어야 한단다.\""]], [["“Not if she marries again before the end of it,”", "\"일 년이 끝나기 전에 재혼한다면 그럴 필요 없지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader, who had some pleasure in startling her good friend the Dowager.", "커들래더 부인이 말했는데, 그녀는 자신의 좋은 벗인 미망인 부인을 놀라게 하는 데 다소간 즐거움을 느꼈다."], ["Sir James was annoyed, and leaned forward to play with Celia’s Maltese dog.", "제임스 경은 언짢아져서 몸을 앞으로 숙여 실리아의 몰타산 강아지와 장난을 쳤다."]], [["“That is very rare, I hope,”", "\"그런 일은 아주 드물기를 바라요.\""], ["said Lady Chettam, in a tone intended to guard against such events.", "채텀 부인이 그런 일을 막으려는 듯한 어조로 말했다."], ["“No friend of ours ever committed herself in that way except Mrs. Beevor, and it was very painful to Lord Grinsell when she did so.", "\"우리 친구 중에 그런 식으로 처신한 사람은 비버 부인뿐이었는데, 그녀가 그러자 그린셀 경이 무척 괴로워했지요."], ["Her first husband was objectionable, which made it the greater wonder.", "그 부인의 첫 남편은 흠잡을 데가 많았던 사람이라 더욱 놀라운 일이었어요."], ["And severely she was punished for it.", "그리고 그녀는 그 일로 호되게 벌을 받았지요."], ["They said Captain Beevor dragged her about by the hair, and held up loaded pistols at her.”", "비버 대위가 그녀의 머리채를 잡고 끌고 다니며 장전한 권총을 들이댔다더군요.\""]], [["“Oh, if she took the wrong man!” said Mrs.", "\"오, 그야 잘못된 남자를 골랐다면 그렇지요!\""], ["Cadwallader, who was in a decidedly wicked mood.", "단연 심술궂은 기분에 빠져 있던 커들래더 부인이 말했다."], ["“Marriage is always bad then, first or second.", "\"그러면 첫 번째든 두 번째든 결혼은 언제나 나쁜 거예요."], ["Priority is a poor recommendation in a husband if he has got no other.", "다른 장점이 없는 남편이라면 먼저였다는 건 보잘것없는 추천장이지요."], ["I would rather have a good second husband than an indifferent first.”", "저는 시원찮은 첫 남편보다는 좋은 두 번째 남편을 택하겠어요.\""]], [["“My dear, your clever tongue runs away with you,”", "\"당신은 그 영리한 혀에 끌려다니는군요.\""], ["said Lady Chettam.", "채텀 부인이 말했다."], ["“I am sure you would be the last woman to marry again prematurely, if our dear Rector were taken away.”", "\"우리 목사님께 무슨 일이 생긴다 해도, 당신이야말로 성급하게 재혼할 마지막 여인일 텐데요.\""]], [["“Oh, I make no vows; it might be a necessary economy.", "\"오, 저는 맹세 같은 건 안 해요. 어쩌면 꼭 필요한 절약일 수도 있으니까요."], ["It is lawful to marry again, I suppose; else we might as well be Hindoos instead of Christians.", "재혼하는 건 율법에 어긋나지 않는 거겠지요. 그렇지 않다면 우리가 기독교인이 아니라 차라리 힌두교도여도 마찬가지일 테고요."], ["Of course if a woman accepts the wrong man, she must take the consequences, and one who does it twice over deserves her fate.", "물론 여자가 잘못된 남자를 받아들이면 그 결과를 감수해야 하고, 그런 짓을 두 번이나 하는 사람은 그런 운명을 당해도 싸지요."], ["But if she can marry blood, beauty, and bravery—the sooner the better.”", "하지만 가문과 미모와 용기를 다 갖춘 남자와 결혼할 수 있다면—빠를수록 좋고말고요.\""]], [["“I think the subject of our conversation is very ill-chosen,”", "\"우리 대화의 주제가 아주 잘못 골라진 것 같군요.\""], ["said Sir James, with a look of disgust.", "제임스 경이 역겨운 표정으로 말했다."], ["“Suppose we change it.”", "\"주제를 바꾸는 게 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["“Not on my account, Sir James,”", "\"저를 위해서라면 그러지 마세요, 제임스 경.\""], ["said Dorothea, determined not to lose the opportunity of freeing herself from certain oblique references to excellent matches.", "도러시아가 훌륭한 혼처에 관한 어떤 에두른 언급들로부터 자신을 해방시킬 기회를 놓치지 않겠다고 결심하며 말했다."], ["“If you are speaking on my behalf, I can assure you that no question can be more indifferent and impersonal to me than second marriage.", "\"저를 두고 하시는 말씀이라면, 분명히 말씀드리는데 제게 재혼만큼 무관심하고 남의 일 같은 문제는 없어요."], ["It is no more to me than if you talked of women going fox-hunting: whether it is admirable in them or not, I shall not follow them.", "여자들이 여우 사냥을 다닌다는 이야기와 다를 바 없어요. 그게 그들에게 훌륭한 일인지 아닌지는 몰라도, 저는 그들을 따라가지 않을 테니까요."], ["Pray let Mrs. Cadwallader amuse herself on that subject as much as on any other.”", "커들래더 부인이 다른 어떤 주제에서나 마찬가지로 그 주제로도 마음껏 즐기시게 두세요.\""]], [["“My dear Mrs. Casaubon,”", "\"캐소본 부인,\""], ["said Lady Chettam, in her stateliest way, “you do not, I hope, think there was any allusion to you in my mentioning Mrs.", "채텀 부인이 더없이 위엄 있는 태도로 말했다. \"제가 비버 부인 이야기를 했다고 해서 거기에 당신을 빗댄 것이 있었다고는 생각지 않으시길 바라요."], ["Beevor. It was only an instance that occurred to me.", "그저 제게 떠오른 한 가지 예였을 뿐이에요."], ["She was step-daughter to Lord Grinsell: he married Mrs.", "그녀는 그린셀 경의 의붓딸이었지요."], ["Teveroy for his second wife.", "경은 두 번째 부인으로 테버로이 부인과 결혼했고요."], ["There could be no possible allusion to you.”", "당신을 빗댈 만한 것은 전혀 없었어요.\""]], [["“Oh no,”", "\"오, 그럼요.\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“Nobody chose the subject; it all came out of Dodo’s cap.", "\"아무도 그 주제를 고른 게 아니에요. 다 도도의 모자에서 나온 거지요."], ["Mrs. Cadwallader only said what was quite true.", "커들래더 부인은 그저 아주 옳은 말씀을 하셨을 뿐이에요."], ["A woman could not be married in a widow’s cap, James.”", "여자가 미망인의 모자를 쓴 채로 결혼할 수는 없잖아요, 제임스.\""]], [["“Hush, my dear!”", "\"쉿, 얘야!\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "커들래더 부인이 말했다."], ["“I will not offend again.", "\"다시는 거슬리게 하지 않을게요."], ["I will not even refer to Dido or Zenobia.", "디도나 제노비아 얘기도 꺼내지 않겠어요."], ["Only what are we to talk about?", "그런데 우리 대체 무슨 얘기를 해야 할까요?"], ["I, for my part, object to the discussion of Human Nature, because that is the nature of rectors’ wives.”", "저로 말하자면 인간 본성에 관한 논의는 사양하겠어요. 그건 목사 아내들의 본성이니까요.\""]], [["Later in the evening, after Mrs. Cadwallader was gone, Celia said privately to Dorothea, “Really, Dodo, taking your cap off made you like yourself again in more ways than one.", "저녁 늦게 커들래더 부인이 돌아간 뒤, 실리아가 도러시아에게 조용히 말했다."], ["You spoke up just as you used to do, when anything was said to displease you.", "\"정말이지, 도도, 모자를 벗으니 여러모로 다시 언니다워졌어요. 마음에 거슬리는 말이 나오면 예전에 그러던 것처럼 똑 부러지게 말하더군요."], ["But I could hardly make out whether it was James that you thought wrong, or Mrs. Cadwallader.”", "그런데 언니가 잘못이라고 생각한 게 제임스인지 커들래더 부인인지 잘 모르겠어.\""]], [["“Neither,”", "\"둘 다 아니야.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“James spoke out of delicacy to me, but he was mistaken in supposing that I minded what Mrs. Cadwallader said.", "\"제임스는 나를 배려해서 한 말이지만, 내가 커들래더 부인의 말을 마음에 둔다고 생각한 건 잘못이었지."], ["I should only mind if there were a law obliging me to take any piece of blood and beauty that she or anybody else recommended.”", "그 부인이든 다른 누구든 추천하는 가문 좋고 잘생긴 남자를 받아들이라고 강요하는 법이 있다면, 그때나 신경 쓰일 거야.\""]], [["“But you know, Dodo, if you ever did marry, it would be all the better to have blood and beauty,”", "\"그렇지만 언니, 언니가 언젠가 결혼한다면 가문과 미모를 갖춘 게 훨씬 나을 거예요.\""], ["said Celia, reflecting that Mr. Casaubon had not been richly endowed with those gifts, and that it would be well to caution Dorothea in time.", "실리아는 캐소본 씨가 그런 자질을 풍부히 타고나지 못했다는 것을, 그리고 도러시아에게 미리 주의를 주는 게 좋겠다는 것을 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t be anxious, Kitty; I have quite other thoughts about my life.", "\"걱정 마, 키티. 난 내 삶에 대해 전혀 다른 생각을 품고 있어."], ["I shall never marry again,”", "다시는 결혼하지 않을 거야.\""], ["said Dorothea, touching her sister’s chin, and looking at her with indulgent affection.", "도러시아가 동생의 턱을 만지며 너그러운 애정으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["Celia was nursing her baby, and Dorothea had come to say good-night to her.", "실리아는 아기에게 젖을 물리고 있었고, 도러시아는 작별 인사를 하러 온 참이었다."]], [["“Really—quite?”", "\"정말—정말로요?\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“Not anybody at all—if he were very wonderful indeed?”", "\"아무도—그가 정말로 더없이 훌륭한 사람이라 해도요?\""]], [["Dorothea shook her head slowly.", "도러시아가 천천히 고개를 저었다."], ["“Not anybody at all.", "\"아무하고도."], ["I have delightful plans.", "난 멋진 계획들이 있어."], ["I should like to take a great deal of land, and drain it, and make a little colony, where everybody should work, and all the work should be done well.", "넓은 땅을 얻어 물을 빼내고, 작은 마을을 하나 만들어서, 거기서는 모두가 일하고 모든 일이 훌륭하게 이루어지면 좋겠어."], ["I should know every one of the people and be their friend.", "거기 사람들을 한 명 한 명 다 알고 그들의 친구가 되고 싶어."], ["I am going to have great consultations with Mr.", "가스 씨와 큰 의논을 할 작정이야."], ["Garth: he can tell me almost everything I want to know.”", "그분은 내가 알고 싶은 걸 거의 다 알려 줄 수 있거든.\""]], [["“Then you will be happy, if you have a plan, Dodo?”", "\"그럼 계획이 있으면 언니는 행복해질 거예요, 도도?\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."], ["“Perhaps little Arthur will like plans when he grows up, and then he can help you.”", "\"어쩌면 우리 아서도 자라면 계획을 좋아하게 될지 몰라요. 그러면 그 애가 언니를 도울 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["Sir James was informed that same night that Dorothea was really quite set against marrying anybody at all, and was going to take to “all sorts of plans,”", "그날 밤 제임스 경은 도러시아가 정말로 그 누구와도 결혼하지 않겠다고 단단히 마음먹었으며, 예전에 늘 그러던 것처럼 \"온갖 계획들\"에 몰두하려 한다는 이야기를 전해 들었다."], ["just like what she used to have.", "제임스 경은 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Sir James made no remark. To his secret feeling there was something repulsive in a woman’s second marriage, and no match would prevent him from feeling it a sort of desecration for Dorothea.", "그의 은밀한 감정으로는 여자의 재혼에는 무언가 혐오스러운 데가 있었고, 그 어떤 혼처라도 도러시아에게는 그것을 일종의 신성모독처럼 느끼는 마음을 막지 못할 것이었다."], ["He was aware that the world would regard such a sentiment as preposterous, especially in relation to a woman of one-and-twenty; the practice of “the world”", "그는 세상이 그런 감정을, 특히 스물한 살 여인에 관해서는 터무니없다고 여길 것임을 알고 있었다."], ["being to treat of a young widow’s second marriage as certain and probably near, and to smile with meaning if the widow acts accordingly.", "\"세상\"의 관행이란 젊은 미망인의 재혼을 기정사실이며 아마도 임박한 일로 다루는 것이고, 미망인이 그에 맞게 처신하면 의미심장하게 미소 짓는 것이었으니 말이다."], ["But if Dorothea did choose to espouse her solitude, he felt that the resolution would well become her.", "하지만 도러시아가 정녕 자신의 고독을 택하기로 한다면, 그는 그 결심이 그녀에게 잘 어울리리라 느꼈다."]], [["# CHAPTER LVI.", "# CHAPTER LVI."]], [["“How happy is he born and taught That serveth not another’s will; Whose armor is his honest thought, And simple truth his only skill! .", "태어나고 길러진 자, 그 얼마나 행복한가!"], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], ["This man is freed from servile bands Of hope to rise or fear to fall; Lord of himself though not of lands; And having nothing yet hath all.”", ". ."], ["—SIR HENRY WOTTON.", ". ."]], [["Dorothea’s confidence in Caleb Garth’s knowledge, which had begun on her hearing that he approved of her cottages, had grown fast during her stay at Freshitt, Sir James having induced her to take rides over the two estates in company with himself and Caleb, who quite returned her admiration, and told his wife that Mrs. Casaubon had a head for business most uncommon in a woman. It must be remembered that by “business” Caleb never meant money transactions, but the skilful application of labor.", "이 사람은 출세하려는 희망의 노예 같은 속박에서도"]], [["“Most uncommon!” repeated Caleb. “She said a thing I often used to think myself when I was a lad:—‘Mr. Garth, I should like to feel, if I lived to be old, that I had improved a great piece of land and built a great many good cottages, because the work is of a healthy kind while it is being done, and after it is done, men are the better for it.’ Those were the very words: she sees into things in that way.”", "추락을 두려워하는 마음에서도 자유로우니\n땅의 주인은 아닐지언정 제 자신의 주인이요"]], [["“But womanly, I hope,” said Mrs.", "아무것도 가진 것 없으나 모든 것을 가졌도다.”"], ["Garth, half suspecting that Mrs. Casaubon might not hold the true principle of subordination.", "— 헨리 워튼 경"]], [["“Oh, you can’t think!”", "캘럽 가스의 식견에 대한 도로테아의 신뢰는 그가 자신의 오두막들을 인정한다는 말을 들으면서 시작되었는데, 프레시트에 머무는 동안 빠르게 자라났다."], ["said Caleb, shaking his head.", "제임스 경이 그녀를 권하여 자신과 캘럽과 함께 두 사유지를 둘러보는 승마에 나서게 했기 때문이었다. 캘럽 또한 그녀의 찬탄에 충분히 화답하여, 캐소본 부인은 여자로서는 보기 드문 사업적 두뇌를 지녔다고 아내에게 말했다."], ["“You would like to hear her speak, Susan.", "다만 캘럽이 말하는 \"사업\"이란 돈을 주고받는 거래가 아니라 노동을 능숙하게 적용하는 일을 뜻했다는 점을 기억해 두어야 한다. \"정말 보기 드물지!\""], ["She speaks in such plain words, and a voice like music.", "캘럽이 거듭 말했다. \"내가 어렸을 때 곧잘 혼자 생각하던 바로 그런 말을 그분이 하더란 말이오."], ["Bless me!", "‘가스 씨, 나는 늙도록 산다면 큰 땅 한 뙈기를 개량하고 좋은 오두막을 많이 지었다는 느낌을 가지고 싶어요."], ["it reminds me of bits in the ‘Messiah’—‘and straightway there appeared a multitude of the heavenly host, praising God and saying;’ it has a tone with it that satisfies your ear.”", "그런 일은 하는 동안에도 건강한 종류의 일이고, 다 하고 나면 사람들이 그 덕을 보게 되니까요.’ 바로 그 말 그대로였소. 그분은 그런 식으로 사물의 속을 들여다본단 말이지.\""]], [["Caleb was very fond of music, and when he could afford it went to hear an oratorio that came within his reach, returning from it with a profound reverence for this mighty structure of tones, which made him sit meditatively, looking on the floor and throwing much unutterable language into his outstretched hands.", "\"하지만 여자다웠으면 좋겠어요.\" 가스 부인이 말했다. 캐소본 부인이 종속의 참된 원칙을 지키지 않을지도 모른다고 반쯤 의심하면서였다. \"오, 당신은 짐작도 못 할 거요!\" 캘럽이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"당신도 그분이 말하는 걸 듣고 싶어 할 거요, 수전. 그토록 쉬운 말로 이야기하는데, 목소리는 음악 같다오. 세상에! 「메시아」의 한 구절이 떠오를 정도지. ‘그러자 곧 수많은 하늘의 무리가 나타나 하느님을 찬미하며 이르되.’ 귀를 만족시키는 가락이 깃들어 있단 말이오.\""]], [["With this good understanding between them, it was natural that Dorothea asked Mr.", "캘럽은 음악을 무척 좋아해서, 형편이 닿을 때면 손이 미치는 곳에서 열리는 오라토리오를 들으러 갔다."], ["Garth to undertake any business connected with the three farms and the numerous tenements attached to Lowick Manor; indeed, his expectation of getting work for two was being fast fulfilled.", "그러고 돌아오면 이 장엄한 가락의 구조물에 깊은 경외심을 품고서, 바닥을 바라보며 명상에 잠겨 앉아 내뻗은 두 손에 말로 다 할 수 없는 갖가지 언어를 쏟아붓곤 했다."], ["As he said, “Business breeds.” And one form of business which was beginning to breed just then was the construction of railways.", "이렇게 두 사람 사이에 좋은 이해가 자리 잡았으니, 도로테아가 가스 씨에게 로윅 저택에 딸린 세 농장과 수많은 소작지에 관련된 일을 맡아 달라고 부탁한 것은 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["A projected line was to run through Lowick parish where the cattle had hitherto grazed in a peace unbroken by astonishment; and thus it happened that the infant struggles of the railway system entered into the affairs of Caleb Garth, and determined the course of this history with regard to two persons who were dear to him.", "실제로 두 사람 몫의 일거리를 얻게 되리라던 그의 기대는 빠르게 채워지고 있었다. 그의 말마따나, \"일은 일을 낳는다.\""], ["The submarine railway may have its difficulties; but the bed of the sea is not divided among various landed proprietors with claims for damages not only measurable but sentimental.", "그리고 바로 그 무렵 새끼를 치기 시작한 사업의 한 형태가 철도 건설이었다. 한 노선이 로윅 교구를 가로질러 놓일 예정이었는데, 그곳에서는 그때까지 소들이 놀라움에 깨질 일 없는 평화 속에서 풀을 뜯고 있었다."], ["In the hundred to which Middlemarch belonged railways were as exciting a topic as the Reform Bill or the imminent horrors of Cholera, and those who held the most decided views on the subject were women and landholders.", "그리하여 철도 체제의 갓난아기 같은 분투가 캘럽 가스의 일에 끼어들었고, 그에게 소중한 두 사람과 관련하여 이 이야기의 흐름을 결정짓게 되었다. 해저 철도에는 나름의 어려움이 있을 것이다."], ["Women both old and young regarded travelling by steam as presumptuous and dangerous, and argued against it by saying that nothing should induce them to get into a railway carriage; while proprietors, differing from each other in their arguments as much as Mr.", "하지만 바다 밑바닥은 측정 가능할 뿐 아니라 감정적이기까지 한 손해 배상 청구를 내거는 갖가지 지주들 사이에서 나뉘어 있지는 않다. 미들마치가 속한 백호구에서 철도는 개혁 법안이나 임박한 콜레라의 공포만큼이나 흥미진진한 화제였고, 이 문제에 가장 단호한 견해를 지닌 이들은 여자들과 지주들이었다."], ["Solomon Featherstone differed from Lord Medlicote, were yet unanimous in the opinion that in selling land, whether to the Enemy of mankind or to a company obliged to purchase, these pernicious agencies must be made to pay a very high price to landowners for permission to injure mankind.", "늙은 여자든 젊은 여자든 증기로 여행하는 것을 외람되고 위험한 짓이라 여겼고, 무슨 일이 있어도 철도 객차에 올라타지 않겠다고 말하며 그에 반대했다. 한편 지주들은 솔로몬 페더스톤 씨가 메들리코트 경과 다르듯 저마다 논리는 천차만별이었으나, 땅을 팔 때에는—그것이 인류의 적에게든 매입할 의무를 진 회사에게든—이 해로운 매개체들로 하여금 인류에게 해를 끼칠 허가의 대가로 지주들에게 아주 높은 값을 치르게 해야 한다는 의견에는 한결같이 일치했다."]], [["But the slower wits, such as Mr. Solomon and Mrs.", "하지만 둘 다 제 땅을 가지고 있던 솔로몬 씨나 월 부인처럼 머리가 더딘 이들은 이 결론에 다다르는 데 오랜 시간이 걸렸다."], ["Waule, who both occupied land of their own, took a long time to arrive at this conclusion, their minds halting at the vivid conception of what it would be to cut the Big Pasture in two, and turn it into three-cornered bits, which would be “nohow;”", "그들의 마음은 큰 목초지를 둘로 갈라 세모꼴 조각들로 만들어 놓는다는 것이 \"말도 안 되는\""], ["while accommodation-bridges and high payments were remote and incredible.", "일이라는 생생한 상념에 멈춰 있었고, 건널목 다리니 높은 보상금이니 하는 것은 까마득하고 믿기 어려운 일이었다."]], [["“The cows will all cast their calves, brother,” said Mrs.", "\"근목초지를 철도가 가로지르면 암소들이 죄다 새끼를 유산할 거예요, 오라버니.\""], ["Waule, in a tone of deep melancholy, “if the railway comes across the Near Close; and I shouldn’t wonder at the mare too, if she was in foal.", "월 부인이 깊은 우수에 잠긴 어조로 말했다. \"그 암말도, 새끼를 뱄다면 그러지 않을까 싶고요."], ["It’s a poor tale if a widow’s property is to be spaded away, and the law say nothing to it.", "과부의 재산이 삽질에 깎여 나가는데 법이 아무 말도 안 한다면, 그건 딱한 노릇이지요."], ["What’s to hinder ’em from cutting right and left if they begin?", "시작했다 하면 좌우로 함부로 갈라 대는 걸 누가 막겠어요?"], ["It’s well known, I can’t fight.”", "다들 알다시피 나는 싸울 줄을 모르니까요.\""]], [["“The best way would be to say nothing, and set somebody on to send ’em away with a flea in their ear, when they came spying and measuring,”", "\"제일 좋은 방법은 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 그자들이 염탐하고 측량하러 오면 누구 하나를 붙여서 귀에 따끔하게 한마디 해 쫓아 보내는 거지.\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“Folks did that about Brassing, by what I can understand.", "\"브래싱 쪽에서는 사람들이 그랬다더군, 내가 알기로는."], ["It’s all a pretence, if the truth was known, about their being forced to take one way.", "진실을 알고 보면, 한 길로만 가야 한다는 건 다 핑계라고."], ["Let ’em go cutting in another parish.", "다른 교구로 가서 갈라 대라고 해."], ["And I don’t believe in any pay to make amends for bringing a lot of ruffians to trample your crops.", "그리고 한 무리 깡패들을 데려와 작물을 짓밟게 해 놓고 그 보상이랍시고 무슨 돈을 준다는 것도 나는 못 믿겠어."], ["Where’s a company’s pocket?”", "회사 호주머니란 게 어디 있냐고?\""]], [["“Brother Peter, God forgive him, got money out of a company,”", "\"피터 오라버니는, 하느님이 용서해 주시기를, 회사에서 돈을 받아 냈지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Waule.", "월 부인이 말했다."], ["“But that was for the manganese.", "\"하지만 그건 망간 때문이었잖아요."], ["That wasn’t for railways to blow you to pieces right and left.”", "좌우로 사람을 산산조각 낼 철도 때문이 아니라.\""]], [["“Well, there’s this to be said, Jane,” Mr.", "\"하긴, 이런 말은 할 수 있지, 제인.\""], ["Solomon concluded, lowering his voice in a cautious manner—“the more spokes we put in their wheel, the more they’ll pay us to let ’em go on, if they must come whether or not.”", "솔로몬 씨가 조심스러운 태도로 목소리를 낮추며 결론을 내렸다. \"이러나저러나 그자들이 올 수밖에 없다면, 우리가 그 바퀴에 살을 더 많이 끼워 넣을수록 계속 가게 해 달라고 우리한테 더 많은 값을 치를 거란 말이야.\""]], [["This reasoning of Mr. Solomon’s was perhaps less thorough than he imagined, his cunning bearing about the same relation to the course of railways as the cunning of a diplomatist bears to the general chill or catarrh of the solar system.", "솔로몬 씨의 이런 추론은 어쩌면 그가 생각한 것만큼 철저하지는 못했으니, 그의 잔꾀가 철도의 진로에 미치는 영향이란 외교관의 잔꾀가 태양계 전체의 한기나 카타르에 미치는 영향과 엇비슷한 것이었다."], ["But he set about acting on his views in a thoroughly diplomatic manner, by stimulating suspicion.", "그러나 그는 의심을 부추기는, 지극히 외교적인 방식으로 자신의 견해를 실행에 옮기기 시작했다."], ["His side of Lowick was the most remote from the village, and the houses of the laboring people were either lone cottages or were collected in a hamlet called Frick, where a water-mill and some stone-pits made a little centre of slow, heavy-shouldered industry.", "그가 가진 로윅 쪽 땅은 마을에서 가장 멀리 떨어진 곳이었고, 일꾼들의 집은 외딴 오두막이거나, 아니면 프릭이라 불리는 작은 마을에 모여 있었다. 그곳에서는 물레방앗간과 몇몇 채석장이 느릿느릿하고 어깨 무거운 산업의 작은 중심을 이루고 있었다."]], [["In the absence of any precise idea as to what railways were, public opinion in Frick was against them; for the human mind in that grassy corner had not the proverbial tendency to admire the unknown, holding rather that it was likely to be against the poor man, and that suspicion was the only wise attitude with regard to it.", "철도가 무엇인지 정확한 관념이 전혀 없는 까닭에, 프릭의 여론은 철도에 반대했다. 그 풀밭 한구석의 인간 정신에는 미지의 것을 찬탄하는 흔한 경향이 없었고, 오히려 그것이 가난한 사람에게 불리할 공산이 크다고 여겨, 의심만이 그에 대한 유일하게 현명한 태도라고 보았기 때문이다."], ["Even the rumor of Reform had not yet excited any millennial expectations in Frick, there being no definite promise in it, as of gratuitous grains to fatten Hiram Ford’s pig, or of a publican at the “Weights and Scales”", "개혁의 소문조차 프릭에서는 아직 어떤 천년왕국의 기대도 불러일으키지 못했다. 거기에는 하이럼 포드의 돼지를 살찌울 공짜 곡물이라든가, \"저울대 주막\"에 공짜로 맥주를 빚어 줄 술집 주인이 생긴다든가, 이웃한 세 농부가 겨울 동안 품삯을 올려 주겠다고 제안한다든가 하는 명확한 약속이 전혀 없었기 때문이다."], ["who would brew beer for nothing, or of an offer on the part of the three neighboring farmers to raise wages during winter. And without distinct good of this kind in its promises, Reform seemed on a footing with the bragging of pedlers, which was a hint for distrust to every knowing person.", "이런 종류의 뚜렷한 이득이 약속에 담겨 있지 않으니, 개혁은 행상꾼의 허풍과 다를 바 없어 보였고, 그것은 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구나 불신해야 한다는 신호였다."], ["The men of Frick were not ill-fed, and were less given to fanaticism than to a strong muscular suspicion; less inclined to believe that they were peculiarly cared for by heaven, than to regard heaven itself as rather disposed to take them in—a disposition observable in the weather.", "프릭의 남자들은 못 먹고 사는 처지는 아니었고, 광신에 빠지기보다는 근육질의 강인한 의심에 기우는 편이었다. 자신들이 하늘로부터 특별히 보살핌을 받는다고 믿기보다는, 하늘 자체가 오히려 자신들을 속여 넘기려는 쪽이라고 여겼으니—이는 날씨에서 능히 관찰할 수 있는 성향이었다."]], [["Thus the mind of Frick was exactly of the sort for Mr.", "이렇듯 프릭의 정신은 솔로몬 페더스톤 씨가 작업을 걸기에 꼭 알맞은 종류였다."], ["Solomon Featherstone to work upon, he having more plenteous ideas of the same order, with a suspicion of heaven and earth which was better fed and more entirely at leisure.", "그는 같은 종류의 관념을 더 풍성하게 지니고 있었으며, 더 잘 먹고 더 한가로이 무르익은, 하늘과 땅에 대한 의심을 품고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Solomon was overseer of the roads at that time, and on his slow-paced cob often took his rounds by Frick to look at the workmen getting the stones there, pausing with a mysterious deliberation, which might have misled you into supposing that he had some other reason for staying than the mere want of impulse to move.", "솔로몬은 당시 도로 감독관이었는데, 발걸음 느린 조랑말을 타고 곧잘 프릭 쪽으로 순찰을 돌며 그곳에서 돌을 캐는 일꾼들을 들여다보았다. 그러다 알 수 없는 신중함을 띠고 멈추어 서곤 했는데, 그 모습을 보면 단지 움직일 마음이 없어서가 아니라 머무를 다른 이유라도 있는 줄로 오해할 만했다."], ["After looking for a long while at any work that was going on, he would raise his eyes a little and look at the horizon; finally he would shake his bridle, touch his horse with the whip, and get it to move slowly onward.", "진행 중인 어떤 일이든 한참을 들여다본 뒤, 그는 눈을 약간 들어 지평선을 바라보았다. 마침내 고삐를 흔들고 채찍으로 말을 건드려 천천히 앞으로 나아가게 했다."], ["The hour-hand of a clock was quick by comparison with Mr. Solomon, who had an agreeable sense that he could afford to be slow.", "솔로몬 씨에 비하면 시계의 시침마저 빠른 편이었으니, 그는 느릿느릿해도 괜찮다는 흐뭇한 여유를 누리고 있었다."], ["He was in the habit of pausing for a cautious, vaguely designing chat with every hedger or ditcher on his way, and was especially willing to listen even to news which he had heard before, feeling himself at an advantage over all narrators in partially disbelieving them.", "그는 길에서 마주치는 울타리꾼이나 도랑 파는 일꾼마다 붙잡고, 조심스럽고 어렴풋이 속셈을 품은 잡담을 나누는 버릇이 있었으며, 전에 들은 적 있는 소식이라도 기꺼이 귀 기울여 들었다. 그것을 절반쯤 믿지 않는다는 점에서 모든 이야기꾼보다 자신이 유리하다고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["One day, however, he got into a dialogue with Hiram Ford, a wagoner, in which he himself contributed information.", "그러나 어느 날 그는 짐마차꾼 하이럼 포드와 대화를 나누게 되었는데, 이번에는 자신이 직접 정보를 제공했다."], ["He wished to know whether Hiram had seen fellows with staves and instruments spying about: they called themselves railroad people, but there was no telling what they were or what they meant to do.", "그는 하이럼이 막대기며 기구를 들고 여기저기 염탐하는 자들을 보았는지 알고 싶어 했다. 그자들은 자기들을 철도 사람들이라 부르지만, 그게 뭔지, 무슨 짓을 하려는 건지 알 길이 없었다."], ["The least they pretended was that they were going to cut Lowick Parish into sixes and sevens.", "적어도 그자들이 내세우는 명목은 로윅 교구를 갈가리 토막 내겠다는 것이었다."]], [["“Why, there’ll be no stirrin’ from one pla-ace to another,”", "\"아니, 그럼 한 군데서 다른 데로 옮길 수가 없잖여.\""], ["said Hiram, thinking of his wagon and horses.", "하이럼이 제 짐마차와 말들을 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“Not a bit,”", "\"전혀 그렇지.\""], ["said Mr. Solomon.", "솔로몬 씨가 말했다."], ["“And cutting up fine land such as this parish!", "\"더구나 이 교구 같은 좋은 땅을 토막 낸다니!"], ["Let ’em go into Tipton, say I.", "티프턴으로나 가라지, 내 말은."], ["But there’s no knowing what there is at the bottom of it.", "하지만 그 속에 뭐가 있는지는 알 수 없어."], ["Traffic is what they put for’ard; but it’s to do harm to the land and the poor man in the long-run.”", "교통이니 뭐니 앞세우지만, 결국 길게 보면 땅과 가난한 사람한테 해를 끼치자는 거지.\""]], [["“Why, they’re Lunnon chaps, I reckon,”", "\"아니, 그놈들은 런던 놈들이렷다.\""], ["said Hiram, who had a dim notion of London as a centre of hostility to the country.", "하이럼이 말했다. 그는 런던을 시골에 적대적인 중심지쯤으로 어렴풋이 여기고 있었다."]], [["“Ay, to be sure.", "\"암, 그렇고말고."], ["And in some parts against Brassing, by what I’ve heard say, the folks fell on ’em when they were spying, and broke their peep-holes as they carry, and drove ’em away, so as they knew better than come again.”", "그리고 내가 들은 바로는 브래싱 쪽 어디서는, 그놈들이 염탐하고 있을 때 사람들이 달려들어, 들고 다니는 들여다보는 기구를 부숴 버리고 쫓아내서, 다시는 올 엄두를 못 내게 만들었다더군.\""]], [["“It war good foon, I’d be bound,”", "\"거 참 재밌었겄네, 틀림없이.\""], ["said Hiram, whose fun was much restricted by circumstances.", "하이럼이 말했다. 형편상 그가 누릴 재미란 무척 제한된 것이었다."]], [["“Well, I wouldn’t meddle with ’em myself,”", "\"글쎄, 나 같으면 그놈들한테 손대진 않겠어.\" 솔로몬이 말했다."], ["said Solomon.", "\"하지만 누구는 이 나라가 좋은 시절은 다 보냈다고 하지."], ["“But some say this country’s seen its best days, and the sign is, as it’s being overrun with these fellows trampling right and left, and wanting to cut it up into railways; and all for the big traffic to swallow up the little, so as there shan’t be a team left on the land, nor a whip to crack.”", "그 징조가 바로, 이런 놈들이 좌우로 짓밟고 다니며 땅을 철도로 토막 내려고 온통 들끓는 거라는 거야. 그것도 다 큰 교통이 작은 교통을 집어삼키게 해서, 땅에 짐말 한 마리 남지 않고, 채찍 휘두를 일도 안 남게 하려는 수작이지.\""]], [["“I’ll crack my whip about their ear’n, afore they bring it to that, though,”", "\"그 지경 만들기 전에, 그놈들 귓전에다 내 채찍을 한번 휘둘러 줄 테여.\""], ["said Hiram, while Mr. Solomon, shaking his bridle, moved onward.", "하이럼이 말하는 동안, 솔로몬 씨는 고삐를 흔들며 앞으로 나아갔다."]], [["Nettle-seed needs no digging.", "쐐기풀 씨앗은 땅 파 묻을 것도 없다."], ["The ruin of this countryside by railroads was discussed, not only at the “Weights and Scales,”", "철도가 이 시골을 망쳐 놓으리라는 이야기는 \"저울대 주막\"에서만이 아니라 건초밭에서도 오갔다."], ["but in the hay-field, where the muster of working hands gave opportunities for talk such as were rarely had through the rural year.", "그곳에서는 일손들이 한데 모이는 까닭에, 농촌의 한 해 동안 좀처럼 갖기 힘든 이야기 나눌 기회가 생기곤 했다."]], [["One morning, not long after that interview between Mr. Farebrother and Mary Garth, in which she confessed to him her feeling for Fred Vincy, it happened that her father had some business which took him to Yoddrell’s farm in the direction of Frick: it was to measure and value an outlying piece of land belonging to Lowick Manor, which Caleb expected to dispose of advantageously for Dorothea (it must be confessed that his bias was towards getting the best possible terms from railroad companies).", "페어브라더 씨와 메리 가스가 만나, 그녀가 그에게 프레드 빈시를 향한 자신의 마음을 고백한 그 만남이 있은 지 얼마 지나지 않은 어느 아침, 그녀의 아버지는 일이 생겨 프릭 방향에 있는 요드렐의 농장으로 가게 되었다."], ["He put up his gig at Yoddrell’s, and in walking with his assistant and measuring-chain to the scene of his work, he encountered the party of the company’s agents, who were adjusting their spirit-level.", "로윅 저택에 딸린 외곽의 한 땅뙈기를 측량하고 값을 매기는 일이었는데, 캘럽은 그것을 도로테아에게 유리하게 처분할 수 있으리라 내다보고 있었다.(고백하건대 그의 편향은 철도 회사들로부터 가능한 한 좋은 조건을 얻어 내자는 쪽이었다.) 그는 요드렐의 농장에 이륜마차를 세워 두고, 조수와 측량용 사슬을 들고 일할 곳으로 걸어가다가, 수준기를 맞추고 있던 회사 측 직원 일행과 마주쳤다."], ["After a little chat he left them, observing that by-and-by they would reach him again where he was going to measure.", "잠시 잡담을 나눈 뒤 그는 그들을 떠나며, 이따가 자신이 측량하려는 곳에서 다시 마주치게 될 거라고 일러두었다."], ["It was one of those gray mornings after light rains, which become delicious about twelve o’clock, when the clouds part a little, and the scent of the earth is sweet along the lanes and by the hedgerows.", "가벼운 비가 지나간 뒤 잿빛으로 흐린, 그런 아침들 가운데 하나였다. 정오 무렵이 되면 구름이 조금 갈라지고 오솔길과 산울타리를 따라 흙 내음이 향긋해져 황홀해지는 그런 아침이었다."]], [["The scent would have been sweeter to Fred Vincy, who was coming along the lanes on horseback, if his mind had not been worried by unsuccessful efforts to imagine what he was to do, with his father on one side expecting him straightway to enter the Church, with Mary on the other threatening to forsake him if he did enter it, and with the working-day world showing no eager need whatever of a young gentleman without capital and generally unskilled.", "말을 타고 오솔길을 따라 오던 프레드 빈시에게도 그 향기는 더 향긋했을 것이다. 다만 그가 무엇을 해야 할지 그려 보려다 번번이 실패하는 헛된 노력에 마음이 시달리고 있지만 않았다면 말이다."], ["It was the harder to Fred’s disposition because his father, satisfied that he was no longer rebellious, was in good humor with him, and had sent him on this pleasant ride to see after some greyhounds.", "한쪽에서는 아버지가 곧장 성직에 들어서기를 바라고 있었고, 다른 한쪽에서는 메리가 그가 성직에 들어선다면 그를 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓고 있었으며, 일상의 노동 세계는 자본도 없고 대체로 기술도 없는 젊은 신사를 조금도 절실히 필요로 하지 않았다. 프레드의 기질에는 이것이 한층 더 견디기 힘든 일이었으니, 아버지는 그가 더는 반항하지 않는다는 데 만족하여 그에게 기분 좋게 대했고, 그레이하운드 몇 마리를 보러 가라며 이 즐거운 승마 길에 그를 내보냈기 때문이다."], ["Even when he had fixed on what he should do, there would be the task of telling his father.", "무엇을 할지 정한다 해도, 그것을 아버지에게 알려야 하는 과제가 남아 있었다."], ["But it must be admitted that the fixing, which had to come first, was the more difficult task:—what secular avocation on earth was there for a young man (whose friends could not get him an “appointment”) which was at once gentlemanly, lucrative, and to be followed without special knowledge?", "하지만 먼저 와야 할 그 정하는 일이야말로 더 어려운 과제였음을 인정해야 한다. 신사다우면서 동시에 돈벌이가 되고, 게다가 특별한 지식 없이도 따를 수 있는, 그런 세속의 생업이 이 세상에(\"임명직\"을 얻어 줄 만한 친구도 없는) 젊은이에게 도대체 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["Riding along the lanes by Frick in this mood, and slackening his pace while he reflected whether he should venture to go round by Lowick Parsonage to call on Mary, he could see over the hedges from one field to another.", "이런 심정으로 프릭 곁의 오솔길을 따라 말을 몰며, 로윅 목사관 쪽으로 돌아가 메리를 찾아볼 엄두를 내야 할지 곰곰이 생각하느라 걸음을 늦추던 그는, 산울타리 너머로 이 들판에서 저 들판까지 내다볼 수 있었다. 갑자기 어떤 소리가 그의 주의를 끌었다."], ["Suddenly a noise roused his attention, and on the far side of a field on his left hand he could see six or seven men in smock-frocks with hay-forks in their hands making an offensive approach towards the four railway agents who were facing them, while Caleb Garth and his assistant were hastening across the field to join the threatened group.", "왼편 들판 저쪽 끝에서, 작업복 차림의 남자 예닐곱이 손에 건초용 갈퀴를 들고, 자기들을 마주하고 선 네 명의 철도 직원을 향해 공세적으로 다가가는 것이 보였고, 그동안 캘럽 가스와 그의 조수는 위협받는 무리에 합류하려고 들판을 가로질러 서둘러 달려가고 있었다. 프레드는 출입문을 찾느라 몇 순간 지체하는 바람에, 작업복 무리가 미처 그 자리에 다다르기 전에 말을 달려가지는 못했다."], ["Fred, delayed a few moments by having to find the gate, could not gallop up to the spot before the party in smock-frocks, whose work of turning the hay had not been too pressing after swallowing their mid-day beer, were driving the men in coats before them with their hay-forks; while Caleb Garth’s assistant, a lad of seventeen, who had snatched up the spirit-level at Caleb’s order, had been knocked down and seemed to be lying helpless.", "정오의 맥주를 들이켜고 나서 건초 뒤집는 일이 그리 다급하지 않았던 그 작업복 무리는, 갈퀴를 휘둘러 외투 입은 사람들을 앞으로 몰아대고 있었다. 한편 캘럽 가스의 조수인 열일곱 살 소년은 캘럽의 지시로 수준기를 집어 든 참이었는데, 얻어맞고 쓰러져 속수무책으로 누워 있는 듯 보였다."], ["The coated men had the advantage as runners, and Fred covered their retreat by getting in front of the smock-frocks and charging them suddenly enough to throw their chase into confusion.", "외투 입은 사람들은 달리기에 유리했고, 프레드는 작업복 무리 앞으로 끼어들어 느닷없이 돌격함으로써 그들의 추격을 혼란에 빠뜨려 후퇴를 엄호했다."], ["“What do you confounded fools mean?”", "\"이 빌어먹을 멍청이들아, 무슨 짓들이냐?\""], ["shouted Fred, pursuing the divided group in a zigzag, and cutting right and left with his whip.", "프레드가 갈라진 무리를 지그재그로 뒤쫓으며 채찍을 좌우로 휘두르며 소리쳤다."], ["“I’ll swear to every one of you before the magistrate.", "\"치안판사 앞에서 너희 하나하나를 다 고발해 버리겠다."], ["You’ve knocked the lad down and killed him, for what I know.", "너희가 그 애를 때려눕혀서, 내가 알기로는 죽여 버린 거야."], ["You’ll every one of you be hanged at the next assizes, if you don’t mind,”", "조심하지 않으면 너희 모두 다음 순회재판 때 교수형을 당하게 될 거다.\""], ["said Fred, who afterwards laughed heartily as he remembered his own phrases.", "프레드는 이렇게 말했는데, 나중에 자신이 내뱉은 표현들을 떠올리며 한바탕 유쾌하게 웃었다."]], [["The laborers had been driven through the gate-way into their hay-field, and Fred had checked his horse, when Hiram Ford, observing himself at a safe challenging distance, turned back and shouted a defiance which he did not know to be Homeric.", "일꾼들은 출입문을 통해 자기네 건초밭으로 쫓겨 들어갔고, 프레드는 말을 멈춰 세웠다. 그때 하이럼 포드가 안전하게 시비를 걸 만한 거리에 자신이 있음을 알아채고는 돌아서서, 자신은 호메로스풍인 줄 모르는 도전의 말을 외쳤다."]], [["“Yo’re a coward, yo are.", "\"넌 겁쟁이여, 그려."], ["Yo git off your horse, young measter, and I’ll have a round wi’ ye, I wull.", "말에서 내려와 봐, 젊은 도련님, 그럼 내 한판 붙어 주지, 암."], ["Yo daredn’t come on wi’out your hoss an’ whip.", "말이랑 채찍 없이는 덤빌 엄두도 못 내잖여."], ["I’d soon knock the breath out on ye, I would.”", "단숨에 숨통을 끊어 놔 줄 텐디, 내가.\""]], [["“Wait a minute, and I’ll come back presently, and have a round with you all in turn, if you like,”", "\"잠깐만 기다려라. 곧 돌아와서 원한다면 너희 모두와 차례차례 한판씩 붙어 주마.\""], ["said Fred, who felt confidence in his power of boxing with his dearly beloved brethren.", "프레드가 말했다. 그는 자신이 끔찍이도 아끼는 형제들과 권투로 맞붙을 자신이 있었다."], ["But just now he wanted to hasten back to Caleb and the prostrate youth.", "하지만 지금 당장은 캘럽과 쓰러진 소년에게로 서둘러 돌아가고 싶었다."]], [["The lad’s ankle was strained, and he was in much pain from it, but he was no further hurt, and Fred placed him on the horse that he might ride to Yoddrell’s and be taken care of there.", "소년은 발목을 삐어 무척 아파했으나, 그 이상 다친 데는 없었다. 프레드는 소년을 말에 태워 요드렐의 농장으로 가서 거기서 돌봄을 받게 했다."]], [["“Let them put the horse in the stable, and tell the surveyors they can come back for their traps,”", "\"말은 마구간에 들여놓게 하고, 측량 기사들한테는 와서 도구를 가져가도 된다고 전하렴.\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."], ["“The ground is clear now.”", "\"이제 땅은 비었으니까.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야.\""], ["said Caleb, “here’s a breakage.", "캘럽이 말했다. \"여기 부서진 게 있어."], ["They’ll have to give up for to-day, and it will be as well.", "오늘은 그만둬야 할 테고, 그게 차라리 낫지."], ["Here, take the things before you on the horse, Tom.", "자, 톰, 그 물건들은 말 위 네 앞에 얹어 가져가거라."], ["They’ll see you coming, and they’ll turn back.”", "네가 오는 걸 보면 그 사람들도 돌아갈 게다.\""]], [["“I’m glad I happened to be here at the right moment, Mr.", "\"제가 마침 알맞은 때에 여기 있게 되어 다행입니다, 가스 씨.\""], ["Garth,” said Fred, as Tom rode away.", "톰이 말을 타고 떠나자 프레드가 말했다."], ["“No knowing what might have happened if the cavalry had not come up in time.”", "\"기병대가 제때 나타나지 않았으면 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 모르죠.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, it was lucky,”", "\"그래, 그래, 운이 좋았어.\""], ["said Caleb, speaking rather absently, and looking towards the spot where he had been at work at the moment of interruption.", "캘럽이 다소 멍하니 말했다. 그러면서 일이 중단된 순간에 자신이 작업하던 자리 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["“But—deuce take it—this is what comes of men being fools—I’m hindered of my day’s work.", "\"하지만—제기랄—사람들이 어리석으니 이런 일이 생기는 거지—덕분에 오늘 일을 못 하게 됐어."], ["I can’t get along without somebody to help me with the measuring-chain.", "측량용 사슬을 도와줄 누군가가 없으면 일을 못 한단 말이야."], ["However!”", "하지만 뭐!\""], ["He was beginning to move towards the spot with a look of vexation, as if he had forgotten Fred’s presence, but suddenly he turned round and said quickly, “What have you got to do to-day, young fellow?”", "그는 프레드가 있다는 것도 잊은 듯 짜증스러운 표정으로 그 자리를 향해 움직이려다가, 문득 돌아서서 빠르게 말했다. \"오늘 자네는 할 일이 있나, 젊은이?\""]], [["“Nothing, Mr. Garth.", "\"없습니다, 가스 씨."], ["I’ll help you with pleasure—can I?”", "기꺼이 도와 드리겠어요—그래도 될까요?\""], ["said Fred, with a sense that he should be courting Mary when he was helping her father.", "프레드가 말했다. 메리의 아버지를 돕는 것이 곧 메리에게 구애하는 일이라는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“Well, you mustn’t mind stooping and getting hot.”", "\"그럼, 허리 굽히고 땀 흘리는 걸 마다하지 않아야 하네.\""]], [["“I don’t mind anything.", "\"저는 아무것도 마다하지 않습니다."], ["Only I want to go first and have a round with that hulky fellow who turned to challenge me.", "다만 먼저 가서, 돌아서서 저한테 도전한 그 덩치 큰 녀석과 한판 붙고 싶군요."], ["It would be a good lesson for him.", "그놈한테 좋은 교훈이 될 겁니다."], ["I shall not be five minutes.”", "오 분도 안 걸릴 거예요.\""]], [["“Nonsense!”", "\"무슨 소리!\""], ["said Caleb, with his most peremptory intonation.", "캘럽이 더없이 단호한 어조로 말했다."], ["“I shall go and speak to the men myself.", "\"그 사람들한테는 내가 직접 가서 이야기하겠네."], ["It’s all ignorance.", "다 무지에서 나온 짓이야."], ["Somebody has been telling them lies.", "누가 거짓말을 한 거지."], ["The poor fools don’t know any better.”", "그 가엾은 멍청이들은 더 잘 알지를 못해.\""]], [["“I shall go with you, then,”", "\"그럼 저도 함께 가겠습니다.\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."]], [["“No, no; stay where you are.", "\"아니, 아니, 자네는 여기 있게."], ["I don’t want your young blood.", "자네의 젊은 혈기는 필요 없어."], ["I can take care of myself.”", "나 혼자 알아서 할 수 있네.\""]], [["Caleb was a powerful man and knew little of any fear except the fear of hurting others and the fear of having to speechify.", "캘럽은 힘센 사람이었고, 남에게 상처 주는 것에 대한 두려움과 일장 연설을 해야 하는 것에 대한 두려움 말고는 두려움을 거의 몰랐다."], ["But he felt it his duty at this moment to try and give a little harangue.", "하지만 이 순간 그는 짤막한 일장 연설을 해 보는 것이 자신의 의무라고 느꼈다."], ["There was a striking mixture in him—which came from his having always been a hard-working man himself—of rigorous notions about workmen and practical indulgence towards them.", "그에게는 두드러진 혼합이 있었으니—언제나 고된 노동을 해 온 사람이었던 데서 비롯된—일꾼들에 대한 엄격한 관념과 그들을 향한 현실적인 관용이 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["To do a good day’s work and to do it well, he held to be part of their welfare, as it was the chief part of his own happiness; but he had a strong sense of fellowship with them.", "좋은 하루치 일을 해내는 것, 그것도 잘 해내는 것을 그는 그들의 복지의 일부라고 여겼으니, 그것이 자신의 행복에서도 가장 큰 부분을 차지하는 까닭이었다."], ["When he advanced towards the laborers they had not gone to work again, but were standing in that form of rural grouping which consists in each turning a shoulder towards the other, at a distance of two or three yards.", "동시에 그는 그들과의 동류 의식도 강하게 지니고 있었다. 그가 일꾼들에게 다가갔을 때 그들은 다시 일하러 가지 않고, 서로에게 어깨를 돌린 채 두세 야드씩 떨어져 서 있는, 그 농촌 특유의 무리 짓는 모양새를 하고 있었다."], ["They looked rather sulkily at Caleb, who walked quickly with one hand in his pocket and the other thrust between the buttons of his waistcoat, and had his every-day mild air when he paused among them.", "그들은 다소 뚱하니 캘럽을 바라보았다. 캘럽은 한 손은 주머니에 넣고 다른 손은 조끼 단추 사이에 찔러 넣은 채 빠르게 걸어와, 그들 사이에 멈춰 서며 여느 때처럼 온화한 기색을 띠었다."]], [["“Why, my lads, how’s this?”", "\"아니, 이보게들, 이게 어찌 된 일인가?\""], ["he began, taking as usual to brief phrases, which seemed pregnant to himself, because he had many thoughts lying under them, like the abundant roots of a plant that just manages to peep above the water.", "그는 여느 때처럼 짤막한 어구로 입을 떼었다. 그 어구들은 그 자신에게는 깊은 뜻을 품은 것으로 여겨졌으니, 그 아래에는 마치 겨우 수면 위로 머리를 내미는 식물의 무성한 뿌리처럼 많은 생각이 깔려 있었기 때문이다."], ["“How came you to make such a mistake as this?", "\"어쩌다 이런 실수를 저질렀나?"], ["Somebody has been telling you lies.", "누가 자네들한테 거짓말을 한 게야."], ["You thought those men up there wanted to do mischief.”", "자네들은 저 위에 있는 저 사람들이 못된 짓을 하려는 줄로 알았겠지.\""]], [["“Aw!”", "\"어이!\""], ["was the answer, dropped at intervals by each according to his degree of unreadiness.", "저마다 굼뜬 정도에 따라 간격을 두고 던지는 대답이었다."]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"무슨 소리!"], ["No such thing!", "그런 거 아닐세!"], ["They’re looking out to see which way the railroad is to take.", "저 사람들은 철도가 어느 쪽으로 날지 살펴보는 거라네."], ["Now, my lads, you can’t hinder the railroad: it will be made whether you like it or not.", "자, 이보게들, 자네들이 철도를 막을 수는 없어. 좋든 싫든 철도는 놓일 걸세."], ["And if you go fighting against it, you’ll get yourselves into trouble.", "그리고 자네들이 거기 맞서 싸우면, 자네들만 곤경에 빠질 게야."], ["The law gives those men leave to come here on the land.", "법이 저 사람들한테 이 땅에 들어올 허가를 준 거란 말이야."], ["The owner has nothing to say against it, and if you meddle with them you’ll have to do with the constable and Justice Blakesley, and with the handcuffs and Middlemarch jail.", "땅 주인도 거기 대해 할 말이 없고, 자네들이 저 사람들한테 손댔다간 순경하고 블레익슬리 치안판사, 게다가 수갑과 미들마치 감옥하고 상대해야 할 걸세."], ["And you might be in for it now, if anybody informed against you.”", "그리고 누가 자네들을 고발하기라도 하면 지금 당장에라도 그렇게 될 수 있어.\""]], [["Caleb paused here, and perhaps the greatest orator could not have chosen either his pause or his images better for the occasion.", "캘럽은 여기서 말을 멈추었다. 어쩌면 제아무리 위대한 웅변가라도 이 상황에 이보다 더 나은 멈춤이나 더 나은 이미지를 고를 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["“But come, you didn’t mean any harm.", "\"하지만 이보게, 자네들이 나쁜 뜻이 있었던 건 아니야."], ["Somebody told you the railroad was a bad thing.", "누가 자네들한테 철도가 나쁜 거라고 했겠지."], ["That was a lie.", "그건 거짓말이야."], ["It may do a bit of harm here and there, to this and to that; and so does the sun in heaven.", "여기저기, 이것저것에 조금 해를 끼칠 수도 있지. 하늘의 태양도 그러니까."], ["But the railway’s a good thing.”", "하지만 철도는 좋은 거라네.\""]], [["“Aw!", "\"어이!"], ["good for the big folks to make money out on,”", "큰사람들이 돈 벌어 먹기에나 좋은 거지.\" 늙은 티머시 쿠퍼가 말했다."], ["said old Timothy Cooper, who had stayed behind turning his hay while the others had been gone on their spree;—“I’n seen lots o’ things turn up sin’ I war a young un—the war an’ the peace, and the canells, an’ the oald King George, an’ the Regen’, an’ the new King George, an’ the new un as has got a new ne-ame—an’ it’s been all aloike to the poor mon.", "그는 다른 사람들이 한바탕 소동을 벌이러 가 있는 동안 뒤에 남아 건초를 뒤집고 있던 참이었다. \"내 젊었을 적부텀 별 게 다 일어나는 걸 봤지—전쟁이며 평화며, 운하며, 옛 조지 왕이며, 섭정이며, 새 조지 왕이며, 새 이름 단 새 양반이며—그래도 가난한 사람한테는 죄다 매한가지였어."], ["What’s the canells been t’ him?", "운하가 가난한 사람한테 뭘 해 줬간디?"], ["They’n brought him neyther me-at nor be-acon, nor wage to lay by, if he didn’t save it wi’ clemmin’ his own inside.", "운하가 그자한테 고기 한 점, 베이컨 한 점 갖다준 적 없고, 제 뱃속 곯아 가며 아껴 모으지 않는 한 따로 떼어 둘 품삯도 못 줬어."], ["Times ha’ got wusser for him sin’ I war a young un.", "내 젊었을 적부텀 가난한 사람한테는 형편이 점점 더 나빠지기만 했어."], ["An’ so it’ll be wi’ the railroads.", "철도도 똑같을 거여."], ["They’ll on’y leave the poor mon furder behind.", "가난한 사람을 더 뒤처지게 둘 뿐이지."], ["But them are fools as meddle, and so I told the chaps here.", "하지만 거기 끼어드는 놈들은 바보여, 그래서 여기 이 사람들한테도 그리 말했고."], ["This is the big folks’s world, this is.", "이건 큰사람들의 세상이여, 이건."], ["But yo’re for the big folks, Muster Garth, yo are.”", "그런디 당신은 큰사람들 편이여, 가스 나리, 당신은.\""]], [["Timothy was a wiry old laborer, of a type lingering in those times—who had his savings in a stocking-foot, lived in a lone cottage, and was not to be wrought on by any oratory, having as little of the feudal spirit, and believing as little, as if he had not been totally unacquainted with the Age of Reason and the Rights of Man.", "티머시는 그 시절에도 드물게 남아 있던 부류에 속하는, 깡마르고 억센 늙은 일꾼이었다. 양말 발치에 저축을 모아 두고, 외딴 오두막에 살며, 그 어떤 웅변에도 흔들리지 않는 사람이었으니, 그에게는 봉건적 정신이라곤 거의 없고, 마치 이성의 시대니 인권이니 하는 것을 전혀 모르고 살았던 것이나 다름없을 만큼 믿는 것도 거의 없었다."], ["Caleb was in a difficulty known to any person attempting in dark times and unassisted by miracle to reason with rustics who are in possession of an undeniable truth which they know through a hard process of feeling, and can let it fall like a giant’s club on your neatly carved argument for a social benefit which they do not feel.", "캘럽은, 어둠의 시대에 기적의 도움도 없이, 부인할 수 없는 진실을 — 고된 감정의 과정을 거쳐 알아낸 진실을 — 거머쥐고 있는 시골뜨기들과 따져 보려는 사람이라면 누구나 아는 곤경에 빠져 있었다. 그들은 자신들이 느끼지도 못하는 사회적 이익에 관한 당신의 깔끔하게 다듬어진 논거 위로, 그 진실을 거인의 몽둥이처럼 내리꽂을 수 있는 것이다."], ["Caleb had no cant at command, even if he could have chosen to use it; and he had been accustomed to meet all such difficulties in no other way than by doing his “business”", "캘럽에게는 입에 발린 상투어가 없었고, 설령 그것을 쓰기로 마음먹을 수 있었다 해도 마찬가지였다. 그는 그런 온갖 곤경을, 자기 \"일\"을 충실히 해내는 것 말고 다른 어떤 방식으로도 마주해 본 적이 없었다."], ["faithfully. He answered—", "그는 대답했다—"]], [["“If you don’t think well of me, Tim, never mind; that’s neither here nor there now.", "\"자네가 나를 좋게 여기지 않는다면, 팀, 괜찮아. 그건 지금 중요한 게 아니니까."], ["Things may be bad for the poor man—bad they are; but I want the lads here not to do what will make things worse for themselves.", "가난한 사람한테 형편이 나쁠 수도 있지—실제로 나쁘고. 하지만 나는 여기 이 사람들이 자기들 형편을 더 나쁘게 만들 짓은 안 했으면 하는 거야."], ["The cattle may have a heavy load, but it won’t help ’em to throw it over into the roadside pit, when it’s partly their own fodder.”", "짐말이 무거운 짐을 졌다 해도, 그 짐을 길가 구덩이에 내던져 버려서야 도움이 안 되지. 그 짐엔 그놈들 먹이도 얼마쯤 들어 있는데 말이야.\""]], [["“We war on’y for a bit o’ foon,”", "\"우린 그저 좀 재미 삼아 그런 거여.\""], ["said Hiram, who was beginning to see consequences.", "하이럼이 말했다. 그는 결과를 깨닫기 시작하고 있었다."], ["“That war all we war arter.”", "\"그게 다였어유.\""]], [["“Well, promise me not to meddle again, and I’ll see that nobody informs against you.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 다시는 끼어들지 않겠다고 약속하게. 그럼 아무도 자네들을 고발하지 못하게 내가 살펴봄세.\""]], [["“I’n ne’er meddled, an’ I’n no call to promise,”", "\"난 끼어든 적도 없은께, 약속할 까닭도 없어.\""], ["said Timothy.", "티머시가 말했다."]], [["“No, but the rest.", "\"아니, 하지만 나머지는 하게."], ["Come, I’m as hard at work as any of you to-day, and I can’t spare much time.", "자, 나도 오늘 자네들 누구 못지않게 고되게 일하는 중이라 시간을 많이 낼 수가 없어."], ["Say you’ll be quiet without the constable.”", "순경 부르는 일 없이 얌전히 있겠다고 말하게.\""]], [["“Aw, we wooant meddle—they may do as they loike for oos”—were the forms in which Caleb got his pledges; and then he hastened back to Fred, who had followed him, and watched him in the gateway.", "\"어이, 우린 안 끼어들겄어—그자들이야 우리 알 바 아니니께 멋대로 하라지\"—이것이 캘럽이 받아 낸 약속의 형태였다. 그러고 나서 그는 프레드에게로 서둘러 돌아갔다. 프레드는 그를 따라와 출입문에서 그를 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["They went to work, and Fred helped vigorously.", "두 사람은 일에 착수했고, 프레드는 힘차게 도왔다."], ["His spirits had risen, and he heartily enjoyed a good slip in the moist earth under the hedgerow, which soiled his perfect summer trousers.", "그의 기분은 한껏 들떠 있었으니, 산울타리 아래 축축한 흙에서 한바탕 미끄러져 완벽하던 여름 바지가 더럽혀지는 것마저 진심으로 즐거웠다."], ["Was it his successful onset which had elated him, or the satisfaction of helping Mary’s father?", "그를 들뜨게 한 것은 성공적인 돌격이었을까, 아니면 메리의 아버지를 돕는다는 만족감이었을까?"], ["Something more.", "그 이상의 무언가였다."], ["The accidents of the morning had helped his frustrated imagination to shape an employment for himself which had several attractions.", "그날 아침의 우연한 사건들은 좌절되어 있던 그의 상상력이, 여러모로 매력적인 일거리 하나를 자신을 위해 그려 내도록 거들어 주었다."], ["I am not sure that certain fibres in Mr. Garth’s mind had not resumed their old vibration towards the very end which now revealed itself to Fred.", "가스 씨의 마음속 어떤 섬유들도, 이제 프레드 앞에 모습을 드러낸 바로 그 목표를 향해 옛 진동을 되살린 것은 아닐까 싶다."], ["For the effective accident is but the touch of fire where there is oil and tow; and it always appeared to Fred that the railway brought the needed touch.", "효과를 내는 우연이란 기름과 부싯깃이 있는 곳에 닿는 불씨에 지나지 않으니, 프레드에게는 언제나 철도가 바로 그 필요한 불씨를 가져다준 것으로 보였다."], ["But they went on in silence except when their business demanded speech.", "하지만 두 사람은 일에 말이 필요할 때 말고는 잠자코 일을 계속했다."], ["At last, when they had finished and were walking away, Mr. Garth said—", "마침내 일을 끝마치고 걸어 나오면서, 가스 씨가 말했다—"]], [["“A young fellow needn’t be a B. A. to do this sort of work, eh, Fred?”", "\"이런 일 하는 데 젊은이가 학사일 필요는 없지, 안 그런가, 프레드?\""]], [["“I wish I had taken to it before I had thought of being a B.", "\"학사가 될 생각을 하기 전에 이 일에 손댔더라면 좋았을걸요.\" 프레드가 말했다."], ["A.,” said Fred.", "그는 잠시 멈추었다가, 좀 더 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다."], ["He paused a moment, and then added, more hesitatingly, “Do you think I am too old to learn your business, Mr. Garth?”", "\"제가 가스 씨 일을 배우기에는 너무 나이가 많을까요?\""]], [["“My business is of many sorts, my boy,”", "\"내 일은 여러 가지란다, 얘야.\""], ["said Mr. Garth, smiling.", "가스 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“A good deal of what I know can only come from experience: you can’t learn it off as you learn things out of a book.", "\"내가 아는 것 가운데 상당수는 경험으로만 얻을 수 있는 거지. 책에서 배우듯 외워서 익힐 수 있는 게 아니야."], ["But you are young enough to lay a foundation yet.”", "하지만 자네는 아직 기초를 닦을 만큼 젊어.\""], ["Caleb pronounced the last sentence emphatically, but paused in some uncertainty.", "캘럽은 마지막 문장을 힘주어 말했으나, 어떤 망설임 속에서 멈추었다."], ["He had been under the impression lately that Fred had made up his mind to enter the Church.", "그는 요사이 프레드가 성직에 들어서기로 마음을 굳혔다는 인상을 받고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“You do think I could do some good at it, if I were to try?”", "\"제가 한번 해 본다면 이 일에서 뭔가 잘할 수 있으리라고 보시는 거죠?\""], ["said Fred, more eagerly.", "프레드가 좀 더 간절하게 말했다."]], [["“That depends,”", "\"그건 경우에 따라 다르지.\""], ["said Caleb, turning his head on one side and lowering his voice, with the air of a man who felt himself to be saying something deeply religious.", "캘럽이 고개를 한쪽으로 돌리고 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. 무언가 깊이 종교적인 말을 하고 있다고 스스로 느끼는 사람의 태도였다."], ["“You must be sure of two things: you must love your work, and not be always looking over the edge of it, wanting your play to begin.", "\"자네는 두 가지를 확실히 해야 하네. 첫째, 자네 일을 사랑해야 하고, 늘 그 가장자리 너머만 바라보며 놀 시간이 오기만 기다려서는 안 돼."], ["And the other is, you must not be ashamed of your work, and think it would be more honorable to you to be doing something else.", "그리고 다른 하나는, 자네 일을 부끄러워해서 다른 일을 하는 게 더 명예로울 거라고 여겨서는 안 된다는 거야."], ["You must have a pride in your own work and in learning to do it well, and not be always saying, There’s this and there’s that—if I had this or that to do, I might make something of it.", "자기 일에, 그리고 그것을 잘하는 법을 배우는 데 자부심을 가져야 하고, 늘 이렇다 저렇다—이런저런 일이 내게 주어졌더라면 뭔가 해냈을 텐데, 하고 말해서는 안 돼."], ["No matter what a man is—I wouldn’t give twopence for him”—here Caleb’s mouth looked bitter, and he snapped his fingers—“whether he was the prime minister or the rick-thatcher, if he didn’t do well what he undertook to do.”", "사람이 무엇이든 간에—나는 그런 사람한테는 두 푼도 안 주겠어\"—여기서 캘럽의 입가가 쓰디쓴 빛을 띠었고, 그는 손가락을 탁 튕겼다—\"그가 총리든 짚 이엉장이든, 자기가 맡은 일을 잘 해내지 못한다면 말이야.\""]], [["“I can never feel that I should do that in being a clergyman,”", "\"성직자가 되어서는 제가 그렇게 할 수 있으리라는 느낌이 도무지 들지 않아요.\""], ["said Fred, meaning to take a step in argument.", "프레드가 말했다. 논의를 한 걸음 진전시킬 작정이었다."]], [["“Then let it alone, my boy,”", "\"그럼 그건 그만두게, 얘야.\""], ["said Caleb, abruptly, “else you’ll never be easy.", "캘럽이 불쑥 말했다. \"안 그러면 자네는 결코 마음 편할 수 없을 게야."], ["Or, if you are easy, you’ll be a poor stick.”", "아니면, 설령 마음이 편하다 해도, 자네는 형편없는 인간이 되고 말 테지.\""]], [["“That is very nearly what Mary thinks about it,”", "\"메리가 그 일에 대해 생각하는 것도 거의 그와 같아요.\""], ["said Fred, coloring.", "프레드가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“I think you must know what I feel for Mary, Mr.", "\"제가 메리에게 어떤 마음인지 가스 씨도 아셔야 할 것 같아요."], ["Garth: I hope it does not displease you that I have always loved her better than any one else, and that I shall never love any one as I love her.”", "제가 늘 그 누구보다 메리를 더 사랑해 왔고, 또 메리를 사랑하는 것만큼 누군가를 사랑하는 일은 결코 없으리라는 게 가스 씨를 불쾌하게 하지 않기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["The expression of Caleb’s face was visibly softening while Fred spoke.", "프레드가 말하는 동안 캘럽의 얼굴 표정이 눈에 띄게 부드러워지고 있었다."], ["But he swung his head with a solemn slowness, and said—", "하지만 그는 엄숙하고 느릿하게 고개를 가로저으며 말했다—"]], [["“That makes things more serious, Fred, if you want to take Mary’s happiness into your keeping.”", "\"자네가 메리의 행복을 자네 손에 맡아 두려 한다면, 일이 더 심각해지는 거야, 프레드.\""]], [["“I know that, Mr. Garth,”", "\"저도 압니다, 가스 씨.\""], ["said Fred, eagerly, “and I would do anything for her.", "프레드가 간절히 말했다. \"그리고 저는 메리를 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠어요."], ["She says she will never have me if I go into the Church; and I shall be the most miserable devil in the world if I lose all hope of Mary.", "메리는 제가 성직에 들어가면 결코 저와 결혼하지 않겠다고 합니다. 그리고 메리에 대한 모든 희망을 잃는다면 저는 세상에서 가장 비참한 인간이 될 거예요."], ["Really, if I could get some other profession, business—anything that I am at all fit for, I would work hard, I would deserve your good opinion.", "정말이지, 제가 조금이라도 적성에 맞는 다른 직업이나 일을—무엇이든 얻을 수만 있다면, 저는 열심히 일해서 가스 씨의 좋은 평가를 받을 만한 사람이 되겠어요."], ["I should like to have to do with outdoor things.", "저는 야외에서 하는 일을 하고 싶어요."], ["I know a good deal about land and cattle already.", "땅과 가축에 대해서는 이미 꽤 많이 알고요."], ["I used to believe, you know—though you will think me rather foolish for it—that I should have land of my own.", "아시다시피 저는 한때, 이런 걸로 저를 좀 어리석다 여기시겠지만, 제 땅을 가지게 될 거라고 믿었거든요."], ["I am sure knowledge of that sort would come easily to me, especially if I could be under you in any way.”", "분명 그런 종류의 지식이라면 저한테 쉽게 익혀질 거예요. 특히 어떤 식으로든 가스 씨 밑에서 일할 수 있다면요.\""]], [["“Softly, my boy,”", "\"천천히, 얘야.\""], ["said Caleb, having the image of “Susan”", "캘럽이 말했다. 눈앞에 \"수전\"의 모습이 떠올랐던 것이다."], ["before his eyes. “What have you said to your father about all this?”", "\"이 모든 걸 자네 아버님께는 뭐라고 말씀드렸나?\""]], [["“Nothing, yet; but I must tell him.", "\"아직 아무 말도 못 했습니다. 하지만 말씀드려야겠죠."], ["I am only waiting to know what I can do instead of entering the Church.", "다만 성직에 들어서는 대신 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 알고 나서 말씀드리려고 기다리는 중이에요."], ["I am very sorry to disappoint him, but a man ought to be allowed to judge for himself when he is four-and-twenty.", "아버지를 실망시켜 드려 정말 죄송하지만, 사람이 스물네 살이 되었으면 자기 일은 스스로 판단하도록 허락받아야 하지 않겠어요?"], ["How could I know when I was fifteen, what it would be right for me to do now?", "열다섯 살에 제가 어떻게 지금 무엇을 하는 게 옳을지 알 수 있었겠습니까?"], ["My education was a mistake.”", "제 교육이 잘못된 거였어요.\""]], [["“But hearken to this, Fred,”", "\"하지만 이걸 들어 보게, 프레드.\""], ["said Caleb.", "캘럽이 말했다."], ["“Are you sure Mary is fond of you, or would ever have you?”", "\"자네 메리가 자네를 좋아한다고, 또 자네를 받아들이리라고 확신하나?\""]], [["“I asked Mr. Farebrother to talk to her, because she had forbidden me—I didn’t know what else to do,”", "\"메리가 직접 말하는 걸 금지했기 때문에, 페어브라더 씨께 메리와 이야기를 좀 해 달라고 부탁드렸어요—달리 어쩔 도리를 몰랐거든요.\""], ["said Fred, apologetically.", "프레드가 변명하듯 말했다."], ["“And he says that I have every reason to hope, if I can put myself in an honorable position—I mean, out of the Church.", "\"그리고 그분 말씀이, 제가 명예로운 자리에—그러니까 성직 밖에서요—설 수만 있다면 충분히 희망을 가질 만하다고 하셨어요."], ["I dare say you think it unwarrantable in me, Mr. Garth, to be troubling you and obtruding my own wishes about Mary, before I have done anything at all for myself.", "제가 저 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 해 놓은 게 없으면서 가스 씨를 귀찮게 하고 메리에 대한 제 바람을 들이미는 게 주제넘은 짓이라 여기시겠죠."], ["Of course I have not the least claim—indeed, I have already a debt to you which will never be discharged, even when I have been able to pay it in the shape of money.”", "물론 저는 무슨 권리도 없습니다—사실, 이미 가스 씨께 진 빚이 있는데, 그건 제가 돈으로 갚을 수 있게 된 뒤라 해도 결코 다 갚지 못할 빚이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, my boy, you have a claim,”", "\"아니, 얘야, 자네한테는 권리가 있어.\""], ["said Caleb, with much feeling in his voice.", "캘럽이 목소리에 깊은 감정을 담아 말했다."], ["“The young ones have always a claim on the old to help them forward.", "\"젊은이들한테는 자기를 앞으로 이끌어 달라고 늙은이들한테 청할 권리가 언제나 있는 법이지."], ["I was young myself once and had to do without much help; but help would have been welcome to me, if it had been only for the fellow-feeling’s sake.", "나도 한때는 젊었고, 변변한 도움 없이 헤쳐 나가야 했어. 하지만 그저 동류 의식에서 나온 것이라도, 도움이 있었더라면 반가웠을 거야."], ["But I must consider.", "하지만 좀 생각을 해 봐야겠네."], ["Come to me to-morrow at the office, at nine o’clock.", "내일 사무소로 아홉 시에 오게."], ["At the office, mind.”", "사무소로, 알겠지.\""]], [["Mr. Garth would take no important step without consulting Susan, but it must be confessed that before he reached home he had taken his resolution.", "가스 씨는 수전과 의논하지 않고는 중대한 결정을 내리는 법이 없었지만, 집에 다다르기도 전에 이미 결심을 굳혔음을 인정해야겠다."], ["With regard to a large number of matters about which other men are decided or obstinate, he was the most easily manageable man in the world.", "다른 사람들이라면 단호하거나 고집스러울 수많은 문제에 대해, 그는 세상에서 가장 다루기 쉬운 사람이었다."], ["He never knew what meat he would choose, and if Susan had said that they ought to live in a four-roomed cottage, in order to save, he would have said, “Let us go,”", "그는 어떤 고기를 고를지조차 모르는 사람이라, 만약 수전이 절약을 위해 방 네 칸짜리 오두막에서 살아야 한다고 말했다면, 그는 자초지종도 캐묻지 않고 \"그럼 가지\""], ["without inquiring into details.", "하고 말했을 것이다."], ["But where Caleb’s feeling and judgment strongly pronounced, he was a ruler; and in spite of his mildness and timidity in reproving, every one about him knew that on the exceptional occasions when he chose, he was absolute.", "하지만 캘럽의 감정과 판단이 강하게 발언하는 문제에서는, 그가 지배자였다. 나무라는 데서는 온화하고 소심했음에도, 그가 마음먹는 예외적인 경우에는 절대적이라는 것을 주위 사람들 모두가 알았다."], ["He never, indeed, chose to be absolute except on some one else’s behalf.", "사실 그는 다른 누군가를 위해서가 아니고는 결코 절대적이 되기를 택하지 않았다."], ["On ninety-nine points Mrs. Garth decided, but on the hundredth she was often aware that she would have to perform the singularly difficult task of carrying out her own principle, and to make herself subordinate.", "백 가지 가운데 아흔아홉 가지는 가스 부인이 결정했으나, 백 번째에 이르러서는, 자기 자신의 원칙을 실행하여 스스로를 종속시켜야 하는 유난히 어려운 과제를 떠맡아야 하리라는 것을 부인은 종종 깨닫곤 했다."]], [["“It is come round as I thought, Susan,”", "\"내 생각대로 일이 돌아갔구려, 수전.\""], ["said Caleb, when they were seated alone in the evening.", "그날 저녁 둘만 마주 앉았을 때 캘럽이 말했다."], ["He had already narrated the adventure which had brought about Fred’s sharing in his work, but had kept back the further result.", "그는 프레드가 자기 일을 함께하게 된 계기가 된 모험은 이미 이야기했으나, 그 뒤에 이어진 결과는 말하지 않고 있었다."], ["“The children are fond of each other—I mean, Fred and Mary.”", "\"아이들이 정말로 서로 좋아한다오—프레드와 메리 말이오.\""]], [["Mrs. Garth laid her work on her knee, and fixed her penetrating eyes anxiously on her husband.", "가스 부인은 일감을 무릎에 내려놓고, 꿰뚫어 보는 눈으로 남편을 불안하게 응시했다."]], [["“After we’d done our work, Fred poured it all out to me.", "\"일을 다 마치고 나서, 프레드가 나한테 죄다 털어놓더군."], ["He can’t bear to be a clergyman, and Mary says she won’t have him if he is one; and the lad would like to be under me and give his mind to business.", "그 애는 성직자가 되는 걸 견딜 수 없어 하고, 메리는 그 애가 성직자가 되면 받아들이지 않겠다고 하고. 그 애는 내 밑에서 일하며 사업에 마음을 쏟고 싶어 해."], ["And I’ve determined to take him and make a man of him.”", "그래서 나는 그 애를 받아들여 어엿한 사람으로 만들어 주기로 결심했소.\""]], [["“Caleb!”", "\"캘럽!\""], ["said Mrs. Garth, in a deep contralto, expressive of resigned astonishment.", "가스 부인이 체념 섞인 놀라움이 묻어나는 깊은 콘트랄토 음성으로 말했다."]], [["“It’s a fine thing to do,”", "\"좋은 일이오.\""], ["said Mr. Garth, settling himself firmly against the back of his chair, and grasping the elbows.", "가스 씨가 의자 등받이에 단단히 몸을 기대고 팔걸이를 거머쥐며 말했다."], ["“I shall have trouble with him, but I think I shall carry it through.", "\"그 애 때문에 애를 먹기야 하겠지만, 그래도 끝까지 해낼 수 있을 것 같소."], ["The lad loves Mary, and a true love for a good woman is a great thing, Susan.", "그 애는 메리를 사랑하고, 좋은 여자를 향한 참된 사랑은 대단한 거라오, 수전."], ["It shapes many a rough fellow.”", "그건 거친 녀석도 여럿 빚어 놓는 법이지.\""]], [["“Has Mary spoken to you on the subject?”", "\"메리가 이 일에 대해 당신한테 말한 적이 있나요?\""], ["said Mrs Garth, secretly a little hurt that she had to be informed on it herself.", "가스 부인이 말했다. 자기가 이 일을 남에게서 전해 들어야 한다는 데 속으로 조금 서운한 마음이 들었다."]], [["“Not a word.", "\"한마디도 없었소."], ["I asked her about Fred once; I gave her a bit of a warning.", "언젠가 프레드에 대해 그 애한테 물어본 적은 있지. 약간 주의를 주었고."], ["But she assured me she would never marry an idle self-indulgent man—nothing since. But it seems Fred set on Mr.", "하지만 그 애는 게으르고 제멋대로인 남자와는 절대 결혼하지 않겠다고 다짐하더군—그 뒤로는 아무 말도."], ["Farebrother to talk to her, because she had forbidden him to speak himself, and Mr. Farebrother has found out that she is fond of Fred, but says he must not be a clergyman.", "그런데 듣자 하니, 메리가 직접 말하는 걸 금지하니까 프레드가 페어브라더 씨를 시켜서 메리한테 이야기하게 했고, 페어브라더 씨가 알아낸 바로는, 메리가 프레드를 좋아하긴 하는데 그 애가 성직자가 되어서는 안 된다고 한다는 거요."], ["Fred’s heart is fixed on Mary, that I can see: it gives me a good opinion of the lad—and we always liked him, Susan.”", "프레드의 마음은 메리한테 단단히 가 있어, 그건 내 눈에 보여. 그래서 그 애를 좋게 보게 되었다오—우리는 늘 그 애를 좋아했잖소, 수전.\""]], [["“It is a pity for Mary, I think,”", "\"제 생각엔 메리한테는 안된 일이에요.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth.", "가스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Why—a pity?”", "\"왜—안된 일이라니?\""]], [["“Because, Caleb, she might have had a man who is worth twenty Fred Vincy’s.”", "\"왜냐하면, 캘럽, 메리는 프레드 빈시 같은 사람 스무 명의 값어치가 있는 남자를 얻을 수도 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Ah?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said Caleb, with surprise.", "캘럽이 놀라며 말했다."]], [["“I firmly believe that Mr. Farebrother is attached to her, and meant to make her an offer; but of course, now that Fred has used him as an envoy, there is an end to that better prospect.”", "\"저는 페어브라더 씨가 메리한테 마음을 두고 있고 청혼할 작정이었다고 굳게 믿어요."], ["There was a severe precision in Mrs. Garth’s utterance.", "하지만 물론, 이제 프레드가 그분을 사자로 부려 먹었으니, 그 더 나은 가능성은 끝이 난 거죠.\""], ["She was vexed and disappointed, but she was bent on abstaining from useless words.", "가스 부인의 말투에는 준엄한 정확함이 깃들어 있었다. 그녀는 속상하고 실망스러웠으나, 쓸데없는 말은 삼가기로 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["Caleb was silent a few moments under a conflict of feelings.", "캘럽은 갖가지 감정이 뒤얽힌 채 잠시 말이 없었다."], ["He looked at the floor and moved his head and hands in accompaniment to some inward argumentation.", "그는 바닥을 바라보며, 마음속 어떤 논쟁에 맞춰 고개와 손을 움직였다."], ["At last he said—", "마침내 그가 말했다—"]], [["“That would have made me very proud and happy, Susan, and I should have been glad for your sake.", "\"그렇게 됐더라면 나도 무척 자랑스럽고 행복했을 거요, 수전. 당신을 위해서도 기뻤을 테고."], ["I’ve always felt that your belongings have never been on a level with you.", "나는 늘 당신 집안 식구들이 당신과 어울리는 수준에 미치지 못했다고 느껴 왔소."], ["But you took me, though I was a plain man.”", "그런데도 당신은, 내가 보잘것없는 사람인데도 나를 택해 주었지.\""]], [["“I took the best and cleverest man I had ever known,” said Mrs.", "\"저는 제가 아는 가장 훌륭하고 가장 영리한 남자를 택한 거예요.\""], ["Garth, convinced that she would never have loved any one who came short of that mark.", "가스 부인이 말했다. 그 기준에 못 미치는 사람이라면 자기는 결코 사랑하지 않았으리라고 확신하며 한 말이었다."]], [["“Well, perhaps others thought you might have done better.", "\"글쎄, 다른 사람들은 당신이 더 나은 선택을 할 수도 있었다고 여겼을지 모르지."], ["But it would have been worse for me.", "하지만 그랬다면 나한테는 더 못한 일이었을 거요."], ["And that is what touches me close about Fred.", "그리고 바로 그 점이 프레드 일을 두고 내 마음에 사무친다오."], ["The lad is good at bottom, and clever enough to do, if he’s put in the right way; and he loves and honors my daughter beyond anything, and she has given him a sort of promise according to what he turns out.", "그 애는 바탕이 착하고, 제대로 길을 잡아 주면 일을 해낼 만큼 영리해. 게다가 내 딸을 무엇보다 사랑하고 떠받들고, 메리도 그 애가 어떤 사람이 되느냐에 따라 일종의 약속을 해 준 거지."], ["I say, that young man’s soul is in my hand; and I’ll do the best I can for him, so help me God!", "내 말하건대, 그 젊은이의 영혼이 내 손안에 있소. 그러니 나는 그 애를 위해 할 수 있는 최선을 다할 거요, 하느님 도와주소서!"], ["It’s my duty, Susan.”", "그게 내 의무라오, 수전.\""]], [["Mrs. Garth was not given to tears, but there was a large one rolling down her face before her husband had finished.", "가스 부인은 눈물을 잘 흘리는 사람이 아니었으나, 남편이 말을 마치기도 전에 굵은 눈물 한 방울이 그녀의 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["It came from the pressure of various feelings, in which there was much affection and some vexation.", "그것은 갖가지 감정의 압박에서 비롯된 것이었으니, 그 안에는 깊은 애정과 약간의 속상함이 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["She wiped it away quickly, saying—", "그녀는 얼른 눈물을 닦으며 말했다—"]], [["“Few men besides you would think it a duty to add to their anxieties in that way, Caleb.”", "\"당신 말고는, 그런 식으로 일부러 제 걱정거리를 늘리는 걸 의무라고 여길 사람은 거의 없을 거예요, 캘럽.\""]], [["“That signifies nothing—what other men would think.", "\"다른 사람들이 어떻게 여길지는 아무 상관 없소. 내 안에는 분명한 느낌이 있고, 나는 그걸 따를 거요."], ["I’ve got a clear feeling inside me, and that I shall follow; and I hope your heart will go with me, Susan, in making everything as light as can be to Mary, poor child.”", "그리고 당신 마음도 나와 함께해 주기를 바라오, 수전. 가엾은 메리한테 모든 일을 되도록 가볍게 해 주는 데 말이오.\""]], [["Caleb, leaning back in his chair, looked with anxious appeal towards his wife.", "캘럽은 의자에 등을 기대고, 불안한 호소를 담아 아내 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["She rose and kissed him, saying, “God bless you, Caleb!", "그녀는 일어나 그에게 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"하느님이 당신을 축복하시기를, 캘럽!"], ["Our children have a good father.”", "우리 아이들은 좋은 아버지를 두었어요.\""]], [["But she went out and had a hearty cry to make up for the suppression of her words.", "하지만 그녀는 방을 나가, 억눌렀던 말들을 만회하기라도 하듯 한바탕 실컷 울었다."], ["She felt sure that her husband’s conduct would be misunderstood, and about Fred she was rational and unhopeful.", "그녀는 남편의 행동이 오해받으리라고 확신했고, 프레드에 대해서는 이성적이고 희망을 품지 않았다."], ["Which would turn out to have the more foresight in it—her rationality or Caleb’s ardent generosity?", "어느 쪽에 더 멀리 내다보는 안목이 있었던 것으로 판명될까—그녀의 이성일까, 캘럽의 뜨거운 너그러움일까?"]], [["When Fred went to the office the next morning, there was a test to be gone through which he was not prepared for.", "이튿날 아침 프레드가 사무소로 갔을 때, 그가 미처 예상하지 못한 시험을 거쳐야 했다."]], [["“Now Fred,”", "\"자, 프레드.\""], ["said Caleb, “you will have some desk-work.", "캘럽이 말했다. \"자네는 책상에서 하는 일을 좀 맡게 될 거야."], ["I have always done a good deal of writing myself, but I can’t do without help, and as I want you to understand the accounts and get the values into your head, I mean to do without another clerk.", "나도 늘 글 쓰는 일을 꽤 많이 해 왔지만, 도와줄 사람 없이는 안 되거든. 그리고 나는 자네가 회계를 이해하고 가치 평가를 머릿속에 익혔으면 하니까, 다른 사무원은 두지 않을 작정이야."], ["So you must buckle to.", "그러니 자네가 단단히 매달려야 해."], ["How are you at writing and arithmetic?”", "글씨와 셈은 어떤가?\""]], [["Fred felt an awkward movement of the heart; he had not thought of desk-work; but he was in a resolute mood, and not going to shrink.", "프레드는 어색하게 가슴이 철렁하는 것을 느꼈다. 책상 일은 생각도 못 했던 것이다."], ["“I’m not afraid of arithmetic, Mr.", "하지만 그는 단단히 마음먹은 상태였고, 물러설 생각이 없었다."], ["Garth: it always came easily to me.", "\"셈은 두렵지 않습니다, 가스 씨. 셈은 늘 쉽게 익혀졌어요."], ["I think you know my writing.”", "제 글씨는 가스 씨도 아실 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Let us see,”", "\"어디 보세.\""], ["said Caleb, taking up a pen, examining it carefully and handing it, well dipped, to Fred with a sheet of ruled paper.", "캘럽이 펜을 집어 들고 꼼꼼히 살펴본 뒤, 잉크를 잘 묻혀 줄 그은 종이 한 장과 함께 프레드에게 건네며 말했다."], ["“Copy me a line or two of that valuation, with the figures at the end.”", "\"그 가치 평가서를, 끝에 숫자까지 넣어서 한두 줄 베껴 보게.\""]], [["At that time the opinion existed that it was beneath a gentleman to write legibly, or with a hand in the least suitable to a clerk.", "그 시절에는 신사라면 알아보기 쉽게, 혹은 조금이라도 사무원에게 어울릴 만한 필체로 글씨를 쓰는 것이 품위를 떨어뜨리는 일이라는 견해가 있었다."], ["Fred wrote the lines demanded in a hand as gentlemanly as that of any viscount or bishop of the day: the vowels were all alike and the consonants only distinguishable as turning up or down, the strokes had a blotted solidity and the letters disdained to keep the line—in short, it was a manuscript of that venerable kind easy to interpret when you know beforehand what the writer means.", "프레드는 요구받은 줄들을, 당대의 어느 자작이나 주교 못지않게 신사다운 필체로 써 내려갔다. 모음들은 죄다 똑같이 생겼고, 자음들은 위로 올라갔느냐 아래로 내려갔느냐로나 겨우 구별되었으며, 획들은 번진 듯 뭉툭했고 글자들은 줄을 지키기를 경멸했다—한마디로, 글쓴이가 무슨 뜻으로 썼는지 미리 알고 있어야만 풀어낼 수 있는, 그런 유서 깊은 종류의 필적이었다."]], [["As Caleb looked on, his visage showed a growing depression, but when Fred handed him the paper he gave something like a snarl, and rapped the paper passionately with the back of his hand.", "캘럽이 들여다보는 사이, 그의 얼굴에는 점점 짙어지는 침울함이 드러났다. 그러나 프레드가 종이를 건네자, 그는 으르렁거리는 듯한 소리를 내며 손등으로 종이를 격하게 탁 쳤다."], ["Bad work like this dispelled all Caleb’s mildness.", "이런 형편없는 솜씨는 캘럽의 온화함을 죄다 흩어 버렸다."]], [["“The deuce!”", "\"제기랄!\""], ["he exclaimed, snarlingly.", "그가 으르렁거리듯 외쳤다."], ["“To think that this is a country where a man’s education may cost hundreds and hundreds, and it turns you out this!”", "\"한 사람의 교육에 몇백 파운드, 몇백 파운드씩 들 수도 있는 나라에서, 그래 봐야 자네 같은 사람을 내놓는다니!\""], ["Then in a more pathetic tone, pushing up his spectacles and looking at the unfortunate scribe, “The Lord have mercy on us, Fred, I can’t put up with this!”", "그러더니 좀 더 애처로운 어조로, 안경을 밀어 올리고 그 가엾은 필경사를 바라보며 말했다. \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서, 프레드, 나는 이건 참아 줄 수가 없어!\""]], [["“What can I do, Mr. Garth?”", "\"제가 어떻게 하면 될까요, 가스 씨?\""], ["said Fred, whose spirits had sunk very low, not only at the estimate of his handwriting, but at the vision of himself as liable to be ranked with office clerks.", "프레드가 말했다. 그의 기분은 바닥까지 가라앉아 있었으니, 자기 글씨에 대한 평가 때문만이 아니라, 자신이 사무소 사무원과 동급으로 매겨질 처지라는 상상 때문이기도 했다."]], [["“Do? Why, you must learn to form your letters and keep the line.", "\"어떻게 하다니?"], ["What’s the use of writing at all if nobody can understand it?”", "아니, 글자를 제대로 만들고 줄을 지키는 법을 배워야지."], ["asked Caleb, energetically, quite preoccupied with the bad quality of the work.", "아무도 알아볼 수 없는데 글을 쓴들 무슨 소용인가?\" 캘럽이 그 형편없는 솜씨에 온통 정신이 팔린 채 기운차게 물었다."], ["“Is there so little business in the world that you must be sending puzzles over the country?", "\"세상에 일거리가 그렇게도 없어서 온 나라에 수수께끼나 보내야 한단 말인가?"], ["But that’s the way people are brought up.", "하지만 이게 다 사람들 키워진 방식이지."], ["I should lose no end of time with the letters some people send me, if Susan did not make them out for me.", "어떤 이들이 나한테 보내는 편지는, 수전이 풀어 주지 않았다면 끝없이 시간을 잡아먹었을 거야."], ["It’s disgusting.”", "정말 역겨운 일이지.\""], ["Here Caleb tossed the paper from him.", "여기서 캘럽은 종이를 멀찍이 내던졌다."]], [["Any stranger peeping into the office at that moment might have wondered what was the drama between the indignant man of business, and the fine-looking young fellow whose blond complexion was getting rather patchy as he bit his lip with mortification.", "그 순간 사무소를 들여다본 낯선 이가 있었다면, 분개한 사업가와, 분해서 입술을 깨무느라 그 금발의 얼굴빛이 군데군데 얼룩져 가는 잘생긴 젊은이 사이에 어떤 연극이 벌어지고 있는지 궁금해했을 것이다."], ["Fred was struggling with many thoughts. Mr.", "프레드는 여러 생각과 씨름하고 있었다."], ["Garth had been so kind and encouraging at the beginning of their interview, that gratitude and hopefulness had been at a high pitch, and the downfall was proportionate.", "가스 씨가 면담 초반에는 어찌나 친절하고 격려해 주었던지, 감사와 희망이 한껏 드높았던 만큼 그 추락도 그에 비례했다."], ["He had not thought of desk-work—in fact, like the majority of young gentlemen, he wanted an occupation which should be free from disagreeables.", "그는 책상 일은 생각해 본 적이 없었다—사실, 대다수 젊은 신사들이 그렇듯, 그는 불쾌한 구석이 없는 일거리를 원했던 것이다."], ["I cannot tell what might have been the consequences if he had not distinctly promised himself that he would go to Lowick to see Mary and tell her that he was engaged to work under her father.", "만약 그가 로윅으로 메리를 만나러 가서, 그녀의 아버지 밑에서 일하기로 했다고 말하리라 스스로에게 분명히 약속해 두지 않았다면 어떤 결과가 빚어졌을지 나는 알 수 없다."], ["He did not like to disappoint himself there.", "그 일에서만큼은 자신을 실망시키고 싶지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“I am very sorry,”", "\"정말 죄송합니다.\""], ["were all the words that he could muster.", "그가 그러모을 수 있는 말이라곤 그것뿐이었다."], ["But Mr. Garth was already relenting.", "하지만 가스 씨는 이미 누그러지고 있었다."]], [["“We must make the best of it, Fred,”", "\"되는대로 최선을 다해 보세, 프레드.\""], ["he began, with a return to his usual quiet tone.", "그가 평소의 차분한 어조로 돌아와 입을 열었다."], ["“Every man can learn to write.", "\"누구나 글씨는 배울 수 있어."], ["I taught myself.", "나도 혼자 익혔으니까."], ["Go at it with a will, and sit up at night if the day-time isn’t enough.", "의지를 가지고 매달려서, 낮 동안만으로 모자라면 밤에도 앉아서 하게."], ["We’ll be patient, my boy.", "우리 참고 해 보세, 얘야."], ["Callum shall go on with the books for a bit, while you are learning.", "자네가 배우는 동안은 칼럼이 한동안 장부를 계속 맡으면 돼."], ["But now I must be off,”", "하지만 이제 나는 가 봐야겠네.\""], ["said Caleb, rising.", "캘럽이 일어서며 말했다."], ["“You must let your father know our agreement.", "\"우리 합의는 자네 아버님께 알려 드리게."], ["You’ll save me Callum’s salary, you know, when you can write; and I can afford to give you eighty pounds for the first year, and more after.”", "자네가 글씨를 쓸 수 있게 되면, 알다시피, 칼럼한테 줄 봉급을 내가 아끼게 되는 거야. 그래서 첫해에는 자네한테 80파운드를 줄 수 있고, 그 뒤로는 더 줄 수 있어.\""]], [["When Fred made the necessary disclosure to his parents, the relative effect on the two was a surprise which entered very deeply into his memory. He went straight from Mr.", "프레드가 부모에게 그 불가피한 고백을 했을 때, 두 사람에게 그것이 미친 상대적인 효과는 그의 기억 속에 아주 깊이 박힌 하나의 놀라움이었다."], ["Garth’s office to the warehouse, rightly feeling that the most respectful way in which he could behave to his father was to make the painful communication as gravely and formally as possible.", "그는 가스 씨의 사무소에서 곧장 창고로 갔는데, 아버지에게 표할 수 있는 가장 정중한 태도는 이 고통스러운 통보를 되도록 진중하고 격식 있게 하는 것이라고 옳게 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Moreover, the decision would be more certainly understood to be final, if the interview took place in his father’s gravest hours, which were always those spent in his private room at the warehouse.", "게다가, 그 면담이 아버지의 가장 진중한 시간—언제나 창고의 개인 방에서 보내는 그 시간—에 이루어진다면, 그 결정이 최종적인 것으로 더 확실히 이해될 터였다."]], [["Fred entered on the subject directly, and declared briefly what he had done and was resolved to do, expressing at the end his regret that he should be the cause of disappointment to his father, and taking the blame on his own deficiencies.", "프레드는 곧장 본론으로 들어가, 자신이 한 일과 하기로 결심한 일을 간략히 밝히고, 마지막에는 자신이 아버지에게 실망을 안겨 드리는 원인이 된 것을 유감스러워하며, 자기 자신의 부족함에 그 탓을 돌렸다."], ["The regret was genuine, and inspired Fred with strong, simple words.", "그 유감은 진심에서 우러난 것이었고, 프레드에게 강하고 소박한 말들을 불어넣어 주었다."]], [["Mr. Vincy listened in profound surprise without uttering even an exclamation, a silence which in his impatient temperament was a sign of unusual emotion.", "빈시 씨는 깊은 놀라움 속에서, 탄성 한 번 내뱉지 않고 들었다."], ["He had not been in good spirits about trade that morning, and the slight bitterness in his lips grew intense as he listened.", "성마른 그의 기질에서 그 침묵은 예사롭지 않은 격정의 표시였다. 그날 아침 그는 장사 일로 기분이 좋지 않았던 터라, 듣는 동안 그의 입가에 어린 옅은 쓴 빛이 점점 짙어졌다."], ["When Fred had ended, there was a pause of nearly a minute, during which Mr. Vincy replaced a book in his desk and turned the key emphatically.", "프레드가 말을 마치자, 거의 일 분 가까이 멈춤이 이어졌고, 그동안 빈시 씨는 책 한 권을 책상에 도로 넣고 단호하게 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔다."], ["Then he looked at his son steadily, and said—", "그러고는 아들을 물끄러미 바라보며 말했다—"]], [["“So you’ve made up your mind at last, sir?”", "\"그래, 마침내 마음을 굳혔단 말이지, 이놈?\""]], [["“Yes, father.”", "\"네, 아버지.\""]], [["“Very well; stick to it.", "\"좋아. 그럼 거기 매달려라."], ["I’ve no more to say.", "더는 할 말 없다."], ["You’ve thrown away your education, and gone down a step in life, when I had given you the means of rising, that’s all.”", "내가 너한테 출세할 수단을 마련해 줬건만, 너는 네 교육을 내팽개치고 한 단계 내려간 거다, 그게 다야.\""]], [["“I am very sorry that we differ, father.", "\"아버지와 생각이 다른 게 정말 죄송합니다."], ["I think I can be quite as much of a gentleman at the work I have undertaken, as if I had been a curate.", "제가 맡은 일을 하면서도 부목사가 되었을 때 못지않게 충분히 신사다울 수 있다고 생각해요."], ["But I am grateful to you for wishing to do the best for me.”", "하지만 저를 위해 최선을 다해 주려 하신 데 대해서는 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“Very well; I have no more to say.", "\"좋아. 더는 할 말 없다."], ["I wash my hands of you.", "너한테서 손 떼마."], ["I only hope, when you have a son of your own he will make a better return for the pains you spend on him.”", "다만, 네가 네 아들을 두게 되거든, 그 아이는 네가 쏟는 수고에 대해 너보다 더 나은 보답을 해 주기를 바랄 뿐이다.\""]], [["This was very cutting to Fred.", "이 말은 프레드에게 무척 아프게 와닿았다."], ["His father was using that unfair advantage possessed by us all when we are in a pathetic situation and see our own past as if it were simply part of the pathos. In reality, Mr.", "그의 아버지는, 우리 모두가 애처로운 처지에 놓여 자신의 지난날을 마치 단순히 그 애처로움의 일부인 양 바라볼 때 누리게 되는, 그 부당한 우위를 써먹고 있었다."], ["Vincy’s wishes about his son had had a great deal of pride, inconsiderateness, and egoistic folly in them.", "실상 빈시 씨가 아들에 대해 품었던 바람에는 적잖은 자만과 무분별과 이기적인 어리석음이 담겨 있었다."], ["But still the disappointed father held a strong lever; and Fred felt as if he were being banished with a malediction.", "하지만 그래도 실망한 아버지는 강한 지렛대를 쥐고 있었으니, 프레드는 마치 저주와 함께 추방당하는 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["“I hope you will not object to my remaining at home, sir?”", "\"제가 집에 계속 머무는 걸 반대하지 않으시겠죠, 아버지?\""], ["he said, after rising to go; “I shall have a sufficient salary to pay for my board, as of course I should wish to do.”", "일어나 나가려다가 그가 말했다. \"물론 마땅히 그러고 싶은 만큼, 식비를 낼 만큼의 봉급은 받게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Board be hanged!” said Mr.", "\"식비는 무슨!\""], ["Vincy, recovering himself in his disgust at the notion that Fred’s keep would be missed at his table.", "빈시 씨가 말했다. 프레드의 밥값이 자기 식탁에서 아쉬울 것이라는 생각에 역겨워하며 마음을 추스른 것이었다."], ["“Of course your mother will want you to stay.", "\"물론 네 어머니는 네가 머물기를 바랄 거다."], ["But I shall keep no horse for you, you understand; and you will pay your own tailor.", "하지만 너한테 말 한 마리도 두지 않겠으니, 알아 두어라. 그리고 양복점 값은 네가 직접 치러야 해."], ["You will do with a suit or two less, I fancy, when you have to pay for ’em.”", "옷값을 직접 내야 할 처지가 되면, 양복 한두 벌쯤은 줄여서 지내게 되겠지, 아마.\""]], [["Fred lingered; there was still something to be said.", "프레드는 머뭇거렸다. 아직 할 말이 남아 있었다."], ["At last it came.", "마침내 그것이 나왔다."]], [["“I hope you will shake hands with me, father, and forgive me the vexation I have caused you.”", "\"아버지, 저와 악수해 주시고, 제가 끼쳐 드린 속상함을 용서해 주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "빈시 씨는 의자에 앉은 채, 가까이 다가온 아들을 향해 재빨리 눈길을 위로 던지더니, 손을 내밀며 서둘러 말했다."], ["Vincy from his chair threw a quick glance upward at his son, who had advanced near to him, and then gave his hand, saying hurriedly, “Yes, yes, let us say no more.”", "\"그래, 그래, 더는 말하지 말자.\""]], [["Fred went through much more narrative and explanation with his mother, but she was inconsolable, having before her eyes what perhaps her husband had never thought of, the certainty that Fred would marry Mary Garth, that her life would henceforth be spoiled by a perpetual infusion of Garths and their ways, and that her darling boy, with his beautiful face and stylish air “beyond anybody else’s son in Middlemarch,”", "프레드는 어머니에게는 훨씬 더 많은 이야기와 설명을 늘어놓았으나, 어머니는 도무지 위로받지 못했다. 어쩌면 남편은 한 번도 떠올리지 못했을 일들이 그녀의 눈앞에 어른거렸기 때문이다."], ["would be sure to get like that family in plainness of appearance and carelessness about his clothes.", "즉 프레드가 메리 가스와 결혼하리라는 것, 그리하여 자기 삶이 앞으로는 가스 집안 사람들과 그들의 방식이 끊임없이 흘러들어 망쳐지리라는 것, 그리고 그 아름다운 얼굴과 \"미들마치의 그 누구 아들보다도\""], ["To her it seemed that there was a Garth conspiracy to get possession of the desirable Fred, but she dared not enlarge on this opinion, because a slight hint of it had made him “fly out”", "멋스러운 풍모를 지닌 자신의 사랑하는 아들이, 그 집안처럼 외모는 수수해지고 옷차림에는 무심해지고 말리라는 확신이었다. 그녀에게는 마치 그 탐나는 프레드를 손에 넣으려는 가스 집안의 음모가 있는 듯 여겨졌으나, 그녀는 감히 이 견해를 늘어놓지 못했다."], ["at her as he had never done before.", "그것을 살짝 비친 것만으로도 프레드가 전에 없이 그녀에게 \"발끈\""], ["Her temper was too sweet for her to show any anger, but she felt that her happiness had received a bruise, and for several days merely to look at Fred made her cry a little as if he were the subject of some baleful prophecy.", "했던 것이다. 그녀의 성정은 너무도 다정해서 어떤 노여움도 드러내지 못했으나, 그녀는 자신의 행복이 멍든 것을 느꼈고, 며칠 동안은 그저 프레드를 바라보기만 해도, 마치 그가 무슨 불길한 예언의 대상이라도 되는 양 조금씩 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["Perhaps she was the slower to recover her usual cheerfulness because Fred had warned her that she must not reopen the sore question with his father, who had accepted his decision and forgiven him.", "그녀가 평소의 명랑함을 되찾는 데 더 더뎠던 것은, 어쩌면 프레드가 그 아픈 문제를 아버지에게 다시 꺼내서는 안 된다고 그녀에게 미리 일러두었기 때문인지도 모른다. 아버지는 그의 결정을 받아들였고 그를 용서한 터였다."], ["If her husband had been vehement against Fred, she would have been urged into defence of her darling.", "만약 남편이 프레드에게 격하게 굴었다면, 그녀는 사랑하는 아들을 변호하느라 떠밀려 나섰을 것이다."], ["It was the end of the fourth day when Mr. Vincy said to her—", "빈시 씨가 그녀에게 이렇게 말한 것은 나흘째 되던 날 저물녘이었다—"]], [["“Come, Lucy, my dear, don’t be so down-hearted.", "\"자, 루시, 여보, 그렇게 기죽어 있지 말구려."], ["You always have spoiled the boy, and you must go on spoiling him.”", "당신은 늘 그 애를 응석받이로 키웠으니, 앞으로도 계속 응석을 받아 줘야지.\""]], [["“Nothing ever did cut me so before, Vincy,”", "\"전에는 무슨 일도 이렇게까지 제 마음을 후벼 판 적이 없었어요, 빈시.\" 아내가 말했다."], ["said the wife, her fair throat and chin beginning to tremble again, “only his illness.”", "그 고운 목과 턱이 다시 떨리기 시작했다. \"그 애가 아팠을 때 말고는요.\""]], [["“Pooh, pooh, never mind!", "\"쯧, 쯧, 신경 쓰지 마!"], ["We must expect to have trouble with our children.", "자식들 때문에 속 썩는 거야 으레 있는 일이려니 해야지."], ["Don’t make it worse by letting me see you out of spirits.”", "당신이 기죽은 모습을 나한테 보여서 일을 더 나쁘게 만들지 말아요.\""]], [["“Well, I won’t,” said Mrs.", "\"네, 그러지 않을게요.\""], ["Vincy, roused by this appeal and adjusting herself with a little shake as of a bird which lays down its ruffled plumage.", "빈시 부인이 말했다. 이 호소에 마음을 추스르고, 헝클어진 깃털을 가다듬는 새처럼 살짝 몸을 떨며 자세를 바로잡았다."]], [["“It won’t do to begin making a fuss about one,” said Mr.", "\"하나 가지고 호들갑을 떨기 시작하면 안 되지.\""], ["Vincy, wishing to combine a little grumbling with domestic cheerfulness.", "빈시 씨가 약간의 투덜거림에 가정적인 명랑함을 섞고 싶어 하며 말했다."], ["“There’s Rosamond as well as Fred.”", "\"프레드뿐 아니라 로저먼드도 있잖소.\""]], [["“Yes, poor thing.", "\"네, 가엾은 것."], ["I’m sure I felt for her being disappointed of her baby; but she got over it nicely.”", "그 애가 아기를 잃고 실망한 게 저도 정말 가슴 아팠어요. 그래도 잘 추스르긴 했지요.\""]], [["“Baby, pooh!", "\"아기는 무슨!"], ["I can see Lydgate is making a mess of his practice, and getting into debt too, by what I hear.", "내가 듣자 하니 리드게이트가 진료를 엉망으로 망쳐 가고 빚까지 지고 있는 모양이야."], ["I shall have Rosamond coming to me with a pretty tale one of these days.", "머지않아 로저먼드가 그럴듯한 사정을 들고 나한테 찾아오겠지."], ["But they’ll get no money from me, I know.", "하지만 나한테서는 한 푼도 못 받을 거야, 분명히."], ["Let his family help him.", "그 사람 집안더러 도우라 해."], ["I never did like that marriage.", "나는 그 결혼이 도무지 마음에 든 적이 없었어."], ["But it’s no use talking.", "하지만 말해 봐야 소용없지."], ["Ring the bell for lemons, and don’t look dull any more, Lucy.", "종을 울려 레몬을 가져오라 하고, 더는 시무룩한 얼굴 하지 말아요, 루시."], ["I’ll drive you and Louisa to Riverston to-morrow.”", "내일 당신하고 루이자를 데리고 리버스턴으로 마차나 몰고 갑시다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LVII.", "# CHAPTER LVII."]], [["They numbered scarce eight summers when a name Rose on their souls and stirred such motions there As thrill the buds and shape their hidden frame At penetration of the quickening air: His name who told of loyal Evan Dhu, Of quaint Bradwardine, and Vich Ian Vor, Making the little world their childhood knew Large with a land of mountain lake and scaur, And larger yet with wonder, love, belief Toward Walter Scott who living far away Sent them this wealth of joy and noble grief. The book and they must part, but day by day, In lines that thwart like portly spiders ran They wrote the tale, from Tully Veolan.", "한 이름이 그들 영혼 위로 떠올라 그 안에서 그런 움직임을 일으켰으니\n생기를 불어넣는 공기가 스며들 때"]], [["The evening that Fred Vincy walked to Lowick parsonage (he had begun to see that this was a world in which even a spirited young man must sometimes walk for want of a horse to carry him) he set out at five o’clock and called on Mrs.", "봉오리를 떨게 하고 그 숨은 형태를 빚는 것과 같았다."], ["Garth by the way, wishing to assure himself that she accepted their new relations willingly.", "충직한 에번 두를, 기이한 브래드워딘과"]], [["He found the family group, dogs and cats included, under the great apple-tree in the orchard. It was a festival with Mrs. Garth, for her eldest son, Christy, her peculiar joy and pride, had come home for a short holiday—Christy, who held it the most desirable thing in the world to be a tutor, to study all literatures and be a regenerate Porson, and who was an incorporate criticism on poor Fred, a sort of object-lesson given to him by the educational mother. Christy himself, a square-browed, broad-shouldered masculine edition of his mother not much higher than Fred’s shoulder—which made it the harder that he should be held superior—was always as simple as possible, and thought no more of Fred’s disinclination to scholarship than of a giraffe’s, wishing that he himself were more of the same height. He was lying on the ground now by his mother’s chair, with his straw hat laid flat over his eyes, while Jim on the other side was reading aloud from that beloved writer who has made a chief part in the happiness of many young lives. The volume was “Ivanhoe,” and Jim was in the great archery scene at the tournament, but suffered much interruption from Ben, who had fetched his own old bow and arrows, and was making himself dreadfully disagreeable, Letty thought, by begging all present to observe his random shots, which no one wished to do except Brownie, the active-minded but probably shallow mongrel, while the grizzled Newfoundland lying in the sun looked on with the dull-eyed neutrality of extreme old age. Letty herself, showing as to her mouth and pinafore some slight signs that she had been assisting at the gathering of the cherries which stood in a coral-heap on the tea-table, was now seated on the grass, listening open-eyed to the reading.", "비치 이언 보어를 들려준 그 이의 이름,\n어린 시절 알던 작은 세계를"]], [["But the centre of interest was changed for all by the arrival of Fred Vincy. When, seating himself on a garden-stool, he said that he was on his way to Lowick Parsonage, Ben, who had thrown down his bow, and snatched up a reluctant half-grown kitten instead, strode across Fred’s outstretched leg, and said “Take me!”", "산과 호수와 벼랑의 땅으로 넓혀 주고,\n경이와 사랑과 믿음으로 더욱 넓혀 준"]], [["“Oh, and me too,” said Letty.", "멀리 떨어져 살던 월터 스콧을 향한 믿음으로."]], [["“You can’t keep up with Fred and me,”", "그가 보낸 이 기쁨의 풍요와 고귀한 슬픔."], ["said Ben.", "책과 그들은 헤어져야 했지만, 날마다,"]], [["“Yes, I can. Mother, please say that I am to go,” urged Letty, whose life was much checkered by resistance to her depreciation as a girl.", "뚱뚱한 거미처럼 비뚤비뚤 가로지르는 줄로\n그들은 그 이야기를 썼다, 털리 비올란에서부터."]], [["“I shall stay with Christy,”", "* 에번 두, 브래드워딘, 비치 이언 보어, 털리 비올란: 모두 월터 스콧의 소설 『웨이벌리』에 나오는 인물과 장소 이름."], ["observed Jim; as much as to say that he had the advantage of those simpletons; whereupon Letty put her hand up to her head and looked with jealous indecision from the one to the other.", "프레드 빈시가 로윅 목사관까지 걸어갔던 날 저녁(그는 이제 기개 있는 젊은이라 해도 자신을 태워 줄 말이 없으면 이따금 걸어야만 하는 세상에 살고 있음을 깨닫기 시작하던 참이었다) 그는 다섯 시에 길을 나섰고, 가스 부인이 두 사람의 새로운 관계를 기꺼이 받아들이는지 확인하고 싶은 마음에 도중에 그녀를 찾아갔다."]], [["“Let us all go and see Mary,”", "그는 과수원의 커다란 사과나무 아래에서 개와 고양이까지 포함된 가족 무리를 발견했다."], ["said Christy, opening his arms.", "가스 부인에게는 축제와도 같은 날이었으니, 그녀의 각별한 기쁨이자 자랑인 맏아들 크리스티가 짧은 휴가를 맞아 집에 돌아왔기 때문이었다."], ["“No, my dear child, we must not go in a swarm to the parsonage.", "크리스티는 가정교사가 되어 온갖 문학을 연구하고 다시 태어난 포슨이 되는 것이야말로 세상에서 가장 바람직한 일이라 여기는 청년이자, 가엾은 프레드에 대한 살아 있는 비판이요, 교육열에 찬 어머니가 프레드에게 들이미는 일종의 본보기였다."], ["And that old Glasgow suit of yours would never do.", "정작 크리스티 자신은 어머니를 남성판으로 옮겨 놓은 듯 네모진 이마에 떡 벌어진 어깨를 가졌으되 키는 프레드의 어깨에 겨우 닿을 정도였는데—그래서 그가 더 우월하게 여겨진다는 사실이 한층 견디기 어려웠다—언제나 더할 나위 없이 소박했고, 프레드가 학문에 마음이 없다는 것을 기린이 학문에 마음이 없는 것 이상으로 대수롭게 여기지 않았으며, 다만 자신이 좀 더 그만큼 키가 컸으면 하고 바랄 뿐이었다."], ["Besides, your father will come home.", "지금 그는 어머니의 의자 곁 땅바닥에 누워 밀짚모자를 눈 위에 납작하게 얹고 있었고, 그 맞은편에서는 짐이 많은 젊은이의 행복에 큰 몫을 차지해 온 그 사랑받는 작가의 글을 소리 내어 읽고 있었다."], ["We must let Fred go alone.", "그 책은 『아이반호』였고, 짐은 마상 시합의 대단한 활쏘기 장면을 읽던 중이었으나, 자기 낡은 활과 화살을 가져온 벤 때문에 자주 방해를 받았다."], ["He can tell Mary that you are here, and she will come back to-morrow.”", "레티가 보기에 벤은 자기가 마구잡이로 쏘는 화살을 보아 달라고 거기 있는 모든 이에게 졸라 대며 지독히도 성가시게 굴고 있었는데, 활기는 넘치나 아마도 머리는 얕은 잡종견 브라우니 말고는 아무도 그러고 싶어 하지 않았고, 햇볕 아래 누운 잿빛 늙은 뉴펀들랜드는 극도의 노령다운 흐릿한 눈의 무관심으로 그것을 바라보고 있었다. 레티 자신은 입가와 앞치마에, 찻상 위 산호 더미처럼 쌓인 버찌를 따는 일을 거들었다는 약간의 흔적을 남긴 채, 지금은 풀밭에 앉아 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 책 읽는 소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."]], [["Christy glanced at his own threadbare knees, and then at Fred’s beautiful white trousers.", "그러나 프레드 빈시가 도착하자 모두에게 관심의 중심이 바뀌었다. 그가 정원 의자에 앉으며 로윅 목사관으로 가는 길이라고 말하자, 활을 내던지고 대신 마지못해하는 반쯤 자란 새끼 고양이를 움켜쥐고 있던 벤이 프레드가 뻗은 다리를 성큼 넘어와 \"나 데려가!\"라고 말했다."], ["Certainly Fred’s tailoring suggested the advantages of an English university, and he had a graceful way even of looking warm and of pushing his hair back with his handkerchief.", "\"아, 나도요.\" 레티가 말했다."]], [["“Children, run away,”", "\"넌 나하고 프레드를 못 따라와.\""], ["said Mrs.", "벤이 말했다."], ["Garth; “it is too warm to hang about your friends.", "\"아냐, 따라갈 수 있어. 엄마, 제발 나 가도 된다고 말해 줘요.\""], ["Take your brother and show him the rabbits.”", "여자아이라고 깔보는 데 맞서느라 인생이 자못 얼룩져 온 레티가 졸랐다."]], [["The eldest understood, and led off the children immediately.", "\"난 크리스티 형하고 있을래.\""], ["Fred felt that Mrs.", "짐이 말했다."], ["Garth wished to give him an opportunity of saying anything he had to say, but he could only begin by observing—", "마치 자기가 저 어수룩한 아이들보다 유리하다는 듯한 말투였다. 그러자 레티는 손을 머리로 가져가며 질투 어린 망설임으로 이쪽저쪽을 번갈아 바라보았다."]], [["“How glad you must be to have Christy here!”", "\"우리 다 같이 메리를 보러 가자.\" 크리스티가 두 팔을 벌리며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; he has come sooner than I expected.", "\"아니, 얘야, 우리가 떼를 지어 목사관에 몰려가서는 안 된다."], ["He got down from the coach at nine o’clock, just after his father went out.", "게다가 네 그 낡은 글래스고 양복으로는 도무지 안 되지. 그리고 네 아버지가 집에 오실 거야."], ["I am longing for Caleb to come and hear what wonderful progress Christy is making.", "프레드 혼자 가게 두자. 프레드가 네가 여기 있다고 메리에게 전해 줄 테고, 메리는 내일 돌아올 게다.\""], ["He has paid his expenses for the last year by giving lessons, carrying on hard study at the same time.", "크리스티는 자기 닳아빠진 무릎을 흘긋 보았다가 프레드의 멋진 흰 바지를 보았다."], ["He hopes soon to get a private tutorship and go abroad.”", "분명 프레드의 옷차림은 영국 대학의 이점을 떠올리게 했고, 그는 더워 보이는 것조차도, 손수건으로 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기는 것조차도 우아한 데가 있었다."]], [["“He is a great fellow,”", "\"얘들아, 저리 가렴.\""], ["said Fred, to whom these cheerful truths had a medicinal taste, “and no trouble to anybody.”", "가스 부인이 말했다. \"이렇게 더운데 친구 곁에 매달려 있으면 못쓴다."], ["After a slight pause, he added, “But I fear you will think that I am going to be a great deal of trouble to Mr.", "형을 데리고 가서 토끼를 보여 드려라.\" 맏이가 알아듣고 곧장 아이들을 이끌고 갔다."], ["Garth.”", "프레드는 가스 부인이 자기가 할 말이 있으면 할 기회를 주려 한다는 것을 느꼈으나, 이렇게밖에 말을 꺼낼 수 없었다."]], [["“Caleb likes taking trouble: he is one of those men who always do more than any one would have thought of asking them to do,”", "\"크리스티가 와서 정말 기쁘시겠어요!\""], ["answered Mrs. Garth.", "\"그럼, 생각보다 일찍 왔지."], ["She was knitting, and could either look at Fred or not, as she chose—always an advantage when one is bent on loading speech with salutary meaning; and though Mrs.", "아홉 시에 마차에서 내렸는데, 마침 제 아버지가 막 나가신 뒤였어. 어서 케일럽이 와서 크리스티가 얼마나 놀랍게 발전했는지 들었으면 좋겠구나."], ["Garth intended to be duly reserved, she did wish to say something that Fred might be the better for.", "그 애는 지난 한 해 동안 수업을 해 가며 학비를 댔고, 동시에 힘든 공부도 계속했어. 곧 개인 가정교사 자리를 얻어 외국에 나가기를 바라고 있단다.\""]], [["“I know you think me very undeserving, Mrs.", "\"훌륭한 친구죠.\" 프레드가 말했는데, 이런 명랑한 진실들이 그에게는 약처럼 쓴맛이 났다."], ["Garth, and with good reason,”", "\"누구에게도 폐를 끼치는 법이 없고요.\""], ["said Fred, his spirit rising a little at the perception of something like a disposition to lecture him.", "그는 잠시 뜸을 들이다 덧붙였다. \"하지만 제가 가스 씨께 무척 폐를 끼치게 될 거라고 생각하실까 걱정입니다.\""], ["“I happen to have behaved just the worst to the people I can’t help wishing for the most from.", "\"케일럽은 수고를 마다하지 않아. 누가 부탁할 생각조차 못 한 일까지 늘 더 해내는 그런 사람이지.\""], ["But while two men like Mr.", "가스 부인이 대답했다."], ["Garth and Mr. Farebrother have not given me up, I don’t see why I should give myself up.”", "그녀는 뜨개질을 하고 있어서, 마음 내키는 대로 프레드를 보기도 하고 보지 않기도 할 수 있었다—말에 유익한 의미를 담으려 작정한 사람에게는 언제나 유리한 일이었다."], ["Fred thought it might be well to suggest these masculine examples to Mrs. Garth.", "가스 부인은 마땅히 삼가려는 마음이었으나, 프레드에게 도움이 될 만한 말을 하고 싶은 것도 사실이었다."]], [["“Assuredly,”", "\"제가 자격이 없다고 여기시는 걸 알아요, 가스 부인, 그럴 만한 이유도 있으시고요.\""], ["said she, with gathering emphasis.", "프레드가 자기를 훈계하려는 듯한 기색을 알아채자 약간 기운이 솟아 말했다. \"공교롭게도 저는 가장 많은 것을 바라지 않을 수 없는 사람들에게 하필 가장 못되게 굴어 왔지요."], ["“A young man for whom two such elders had devoted themselves would indeed be culpable if he threw himself away and made their sacrifices vain.”", "하지만 가스 씨나 페어브러더 씨 같은 두 분이 저를 포기하지 않으셨는데, 제가 어찌 저 자신을 포기해야 하겠습니까.\" 프레드는 이런 남자다운 본보기를 가스 부인에게 들이미는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했다."]], [["Fred wondered a little at this strong language, but only said, “I hope it will not be so with me, Mrs.", "\"물론이지.\" 그녀가 점점 힘주어 말했다."], ["Garth, since I have some encouragement to believe that I may win Mary.", "\"그렇게 어른 두 분이 헌신해 주신 젊은이가 자기 자신을 내던져 그분들의 희생을 헛되게 만든다면 정말이지 죄가 될 게야.\" 프레드는 이 강한 말투에 약간 의아해했으나, \"저는 그렇게 되지 않기를 바랍니다, 가스 부인, 메리의 마음을 얻을 수 있으리라 믿을 만한 격려를 좀 받았으니까요."], ["Mr. Garth has told you about that?", "가스 씨가 그 이야기를 해 드렸지요?"], ["You were not surprised, I dare say?”", "놀라지 않으셨겠지요?\"라고만 말했다."], ["Fred ended, innocently referring only to his own love as probably evident enough.", "프레드는 순진하게도 충분히 분명할 자기 사랑만을 두고 한 말이었다."]], [["“Not surprised that Mary has given you encouragement?” returned Mrs.", "\"메리가 너에게 격려를 보냈다는 게 놀랍지 않으냐고?\""], ["Garth, who thought it would be well for Fred to be more alive to the fact that Mary’s friends could not possibly have wished this beforehand, whatever the Vincys might suppose.", "가스 부인이 되받았다. 그녀는 빈시 집안 사람들이 무어라 짐작하든, 메리의 가족이 이런 일을 미리 바랐을 리 만무하다는 사실을 프레드가 좀 더 의식하는 것이 좋겠다고 생각했다."], ["“Yes, I confess I was surprised.”", "\"그래, 솔직히 놀랐다.\""]], [["“She never did give me any—not the least in the world, when I talked to her myself,”", "\"제가 직접 말했을 때 메리는 결코 제게 아무 격려도 하지 않았어요—조금도요.\""], ["said Fred, eager to vindicate Mary.", "메리를 변호하고 싶은 마음에 프레드가 말했다."], ["“But when I asked Mr. Farebrother to speak for me, she allowed him to tell me there was a hope.”", "\"하지만 제가 페어브러더 씨에게 저를 위해 말해 달라고 부탁했더니, 메리가 그분을 통해 제게 희망이 있다고 전하도록 허락했지요.\""]], [["The power of admonition which had begun to stir in Mrs. Garth had not yet discharged itself.", "가스 부인의 마음속에서 꿈틀거리기 시작한 훈계의 힘은 아직 다 풀려나오지 않은 터였다."], ["It was a little too provoking even for her self-control that this blooming youngster should flourish on the disappointments of sadder and wiser people—making a meal of a nightingale and never knowing it—and that all the while his family should suppose that hers was in eager need of this sprig; and her vexation had fermented the more actively because of its total repression towards her husband.", "이 활짝 핀 풋내기가 더 슬프고 더 현명한 사람들의 실망을 양분 삼아 번성한다는 것은—나이팅게일을 한 끼 식사로 먹으면서도 그런 줄 전혀 모른다는 것은—그리고 그러는 내내 그 집안 사람들은 이쪽이 이 잔가지를 간절히 필요로 하리라 짐작한다는 것은, 그녀의 자제력으로도 좀 지나치게 부아가 치미는 노릇이었다. 남편을 향해서는 그 분함을 완전히 억눌렀던 탓에 그것이 한층 더 활발히 발효되었다."], ["Exemplary wives will sometimes find scapegoats in this way.", "모범적인 아내들은 이따금 이런 식으로 희생양을 찾는 법이다. 그녀는 이제 단호하고 힘 있게 말했다."], ["She now said with energetic decision, “You made a great mistake, Fred, in asking Mr. Farebrother to speak for you.”", "\"큰 실수를 한 게야, 프레드, 페어브러더 씨에게 너를 위해 말해 달라고 부탁한 것은.\""]], [["“Did I?”", "\"제가요?\""], ["said Fred, reddening instantaneously.", "프레드가 즉각 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["He was alarmed, but at a loss to know what Mrs.", "그는 깜짝 놀랐으나, 가스 부인이 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 종잡을 수 없어, 변명하는 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["Garth meant, and added, in an apologetic tone, “Mr.", "\"페어브러더 씨는 늘 우리 집과 그렇게나 친한 분이셨고요."], ["Farebrother has always been such a friend of ours; and Mary, I knew, would listen to him gravely; and he took it on himself quite readily.”", "그리고 저는 메리가 그분 말씀은 진지하게 들으리라는 걸 알았어요. 게다가 그분이 아주 선뜻 그 일을 맡아 주셨고요.\""]], [["“Yes, young people are usually blind to everything but their own wishes, and seldom imagine how much those wishes cost others,”", "\"그래, 젊은이들은 보통 자기 바람 말고는 아무것도 보지 못하고, 그 바람이 남에게 얼마나 큰 대가를 치르게 하는지는 좀처럼 상상하지 못하지.\""], ["said Mrs. Garth.", "가스 부인이 말했다."], ["She did not mean to go beyond this salutary general doctrine, and threw her indignation into a needless unwinding of her worsted, knitting her brow at it with a grand air.", "그녀는 이 유익한 일반론 이상으로 나아갈 생각은 없었고, 분함을 공연히 털실을 풀어내는 데로 던지며 거창한 태도로 그것에 미간을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“I cannot conceive how it could be any pain to Mr. Farebrother,”", "\"페어브러더 씨에게 그게 어떻게 괴로움이 될 수 있는지 저는 짐작도 못 하겠습니다.\""], ["said Fred, who nevertheless felt that surprising conceptions were beginning to form themselves.", "프레드가 말했으나, 그럼에도 놀라운 짐작들이 마음속에 형체를 잡아 가기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Precisely; you cannot conceive,” said Mrs.", "\"바로 그거다, 너는 짐작도 못 하지.\""], ["Garth, cutting her words as neatly as possible.", "가스 부인이 되도록 또박또박 말을 끊으며 말했다."]], [["For a moment Fred looked at the horizon with a dismayed anxiety, and then turning with a quick movement said almost sharply—", "잠시 프레드는 당혹스러운 불안에 휩싸여 지평선을 바라보다가, 이내 빠른 동작으로 돌아서며 거의 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["“Do you mean to say, Mrs. Garth, that Mr. Farebrother is in love with Mary?”", "\"그러니까 가스 부인, 페어브러더 씨가 메리를 사랑한다는 말씀이십니까?\""]], [["“And if it were so, Fred, I think you are the last person who ought to be surprised,” returned Mrs.", "\"설령 그렇다 한들, 프레드, 너야말로 놀라서는 안 될 마지막 사람이라고 나는 생각한다.\""], ["Garth, laying her knitting down beside her and folding her arms.", "가스 부인이 뜨개질감을 곁에 내려놓고 팔짱을 끼며 대꾸했다."], ["It was an unwonted sign of emotion in her that she should put her work out of her hands.", "그녀가 손에서 일감을 놓는다는 것은 그녀로서는 보기 드문 감정의 표시였다."], ["In fact her feelings were divided between the satisfaction of giving Fred his discipline and the sense of having gone a little too far.", "사실 그녀의 마음은 프레드를 훈계했다는 만족감과 좀 지나쳤다는 느낌 사이에서 갈라져 있었다."], ["Fred took his hat and stick and rose quickly.", "프레드는 모자와 지팡이를 집어 들고 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Then you think I am standing in his way, and in Mary’s too?”", "\"그럼 부인은 제가 그분의 앞길을, 그리고 메리의 앞길까지 가로막고 있다고 생각하시는군요?\""], ["he said, in a tone which seemed to demand an answer.", "그가 대답을 요구하는 듯한 어조로 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Garth could not speak immediately.", "가스 부인은 곧바로 말할 수 없었다."], ["She had brought herself into the unpleasant position of being called on to say what she really felt, yet what she knew there were strong reasons for concealing.", "그녀는 자기가 정말로 느끼는 바를, 그러나 감추어야 할 강력한 이유가 있다고 아는 바를 말하라고 요구받는 곤란한 처지에 스스로를 몰아넣은 것이었다."], ["And to her the consciousness of having exceeded in words was peculiarly mortifying.", "그리고 그녀에게는 말이 지나쳤다는 의식이 유난히 굴욕적이었다."], ["Besides, Fred had given out unexpected electricity, and he now added, “Mr.", "게다가 프레드가 뜻밖의 전기를 내뿜었고, 그는 이제 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["Garth seemed pleased that Mary should be attached to me.", "\"가스 씨는 메리가 저를 좋아하는 걸 기뻐하시는 듯했어요."], ["He could not have known anything of this.”", "그분은 이런 일을 전혀 모르셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Garth felt a severe twinge at this mention of her husband, the fear that Caleb might think her in the wrong not being easily endurable.", "남편이 거론되자 가스 부인은 심한 통증을 느꼈으니, 케일럽이 자기가 잘못했다고 여길지 모른다는 두려움은 쉬이 견딜 수 있는 것이 아니었다."], ["She answered, wanting to check unintended consequences—", "그녀는 의도치 않은 결과를 막고 싶은 마음에 대답했다."]], [["“I spoke from inference only.", "\"나는 그저 추측으로 말한 거다."], ["I am not aware that Mary knows anything of the matter.”", "메리가 그 일에 대해 무언가 안다고는 생각지 않아.\""]], [["But she hesitated to beg that he would keep entire silence on a subject which she had herself unnecessarily mentioned, not being used to stoop in that way; and while she was hesitating there was already a rush of unintended consequences under the apple-tree where the tea-things stood.", "그러나 그녀는 정작 자기가 불필요하게 꺼낸 화제를 두고 프레드에게 완전히 침묵해 달라고 부탁하기를 망설였으니, 그런 식으로 자세를 낮추는 데 익숙지 않았던 것이다. 그리고 그녀가 망설이는 사이, 찻상이 놓인 사과나무 아래에서는 이미 의도치 않은 결과가 한바탕 쏟아져 나오고 있었다."], ["Ben, bouncing across the grass with Brownie at his heels, and seeing the kitten dragging the knitting by a lengthening line of wool, shouted and clapped his hands; Brownie barked, the kitten, desperate, jumped on the tea-table and upset the milk, then jumped down again and swept half the cherries with it; and Ben, snatching up the half-knitted sock-top, fitted it over the kitten’s head as a new source of madness, while Letty arriving cried out to her mother against this cruelty—it was a history as full of sensation as “This is the house that Jack built.”", "벤이 브라우니를 뒤에 달고 풀밭을 가로질러 깡충거리다가, 새끼 고양이가 점점 길어지는 털실 줄로 뜨개질감을 끌고 가는 것을 보고는 소리치며 손뼉을 쳤다. 브라우니가 짖었고, 절박해진 새끼 고양이는 찻상으로 뛰어올라 우유를 엎질렀다가 다시 뛰어내리며 버찌를 절반이나 휩쓸어 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Mrs. Garth was obliged to interfere, the other young ones came up and the tête-à-tête with Fred was ended.", "그러자 벤이 반쯤 뜬 양말목을 낚아채어 새로운 광란의 원천으로 삼고자 새끼 고양이 머리에 씌웠고, 마침 도착한 레티가 이런 잔인한 짓을 두고 어머니에게 울며 항의했다—그것은 \"이것은 잭이 지은 집이라\"만큼이나 사건으로 가득 찬 이야기였다."], ["He got away as soon as he could, and Mrs.", "가스 부인은 끼어들 수밖에 없었고, 다른 아이들도 다가왔으며, 프레드와의 단둘이 나누던 이야기는 끝나고 말았다."], ["Garth could only imply some retractation of her severity by saying “God bless you”", "그는 되도록 빨리 그곳을 떠났고, 가스 부인은 그와 악수하며 \"신의 가호가 있기를\"이라고 말하는 것으로 자신의 가혹함을 다소 거두어들였음을 내비칠 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["when she shook hands with him.", "* \"이것은 잭이 지은 집이라\": 영국 전래 누적 동요로, 한 사건이 꼬리에 꼬리를 물고 이어지는 형식."]], [["She was unpleasantly conscious that she had been on the verge of speaking as “one of the foolish women speaketh”—telling first and entreating silence after.", "그녀는 자기가 \"어리석은 여인의 말처럼\"—먼저 일러바치고 나서 침묵을 간청하는 식으로—말할 뻔했다는 것을 불쾌하게 의식했다."], ["But she had not entreated silence, and to prevent Caleb’s blame she determined to blame herself and confess all to him that very night.", "그러나 그녀는 침묵을 간청하지 않았고, 케일럽의 비난을 막기 위해 스스로를 탓하고 바로 그날 밤 그에게 모든 것을 고백하기로 결심했다."], ["It was curious what an awful tribunal the mild Caleb’s was to her, whenever he set it up.", "온화한 케일럽이 일단 재판정을 차리고 나면 그녀에게는 그것이 얼마나 무시무시한 법정이 되는지 참으로 묘한 일이었다."], ["But she meant to point out to him that the revelation might do Fred Vincy a great deal of good.", "그러나 그녀는 이 폭로가 프레드 빈시에게 큰 도움이 될 수도 있음을 그에게 일러 줄 작정이었다."]], [["No doubt it was having a strong effect on him as he walked to Lowick.", "의심할 바 없이 그것은 로윅으로 걸어가는 프레드에게 강한 영향을 미치고 있었다."], ["Fred’s light hopeful nature had perhaps never had so much of a bruise as from this suggestion that if he had been out of the way Mary might have made a thoroughly good match.", "프레드의 가볍고 희망찬 천성이, 자기가 끼어들지 않았더라면 메리가 더할 나위 없이 좋은 혼처를 만났을지 모른다는 그 암시만큼 큰 멍이 든 적은 아마 없었을 것이다."], ["Also he was piqued that he had been what he called such a stupid lout as to ask that intervention from Mr. Farebrother.", "또한 그는 자신이 페어브러더 씨에게 그런 중재를 부탁한, 스스로 말하는 그런 멍청한 얼간이였다는 데 자존심이 상했다."], ["But it was not in a lover’s nature—it was not in Fred’s, that the new anxiety raised about Mary’s feeling should not surmount every other. Notwithstanding his trust in Mr.", "그러나 메리의 마음을 두고 새로 일어난 불안이 다른 모든 것을 압도하지 않는다는 것은 연인의 본성에—프레드의 본성에 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Farebrother’s generosity, notwithstanding what Mary had said to him, Fred could not help feeling that he had a rival: it was a new consciousness, and he objected to it extremely, not being in the least ready to give up Mary for her good, being ready rather to fight for her with any man whatsoever.", "페어브러더 씨의 너그러움을 믿었음에도, 메리가 자기에게 한 말이 있었음에도, 프레드는 자기에게 연적이 있다는 느낌을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그것은 새로운 의식이었고, 그는 그것이 몹시 못마땅했으니, 메리를 위해 메리를 단념할 마음은 조금도 없었고, 오히려 그 누구와도 그녀를 두고 싸울 각오가 되어 있었던 것이다."], ["But the fighting with Mr. Farebrother must be of a metaphorical kind, which was much more difficult to Fred than the muscular.", "그러나 페어브러더 씨와의 싸움은 비유적인 것일 수밖에 없었는데, 그것은 프레드에게 근육을 쓰는 싸움보다 훨씬 더 어려운 일이었다."], ["Certainly this experience was a discipline for Fred hardly less sharp than his disappointment about his uncle’s will.", "분명 이 경험은 프레드에게 큰아버지의 유언을 둘러싼 실망 못지않게 날카로운 단련이었다."], ["The iron had not entered into his soul, but he had begun to imagine what the sharp edge would be. It did not once occur to Fred that Mrs.", "쇠가 아직 그의 영혼을 꿰뚫지는 않았으나, 그는 그 예리한 날이 어떨지를 상상하기 시작했다."], ["Garth might be mistaken about Mr. Farebrother, but he suspected that she might be wrong about Mary.", "가스 부인이 페어브러더 씨에 관해 잘못 알고 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 프레드에게 한 번도 떠오르지 않았으나, 그녀가 메리에 관해서는 틀렸을지도 모른다고 의심했다."], ["Mary had been staying at the parsonage lately, and her mother might know very little of what had been passing in her mind.", "메리는 요즘 목사관에 머물고 있었으니, 그녀의 어머니는 메리의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는지 거의 모를 수도 있었다."]], [["He did not feel easier when he found her looking cheerful with the three ladies in the drawing-room.", "객실에서 세 부인과 함께 명랑해 보이는 그녀를 발견했을 때도 그는 마음이 한결 편해지지 않았다."], ["They were in animated discussion on some subject which was dropped when he entered, and Mary was copying the labels from a heap of shallow cabinet drawers, in a minute handwriting which she was skilled in. Mr.", "그가 들어서자 뚝 그쳐 버린 어떤 화제를 두고 그들은 활기차게 토론하던 중이었고, 메리는 얕은 진열장 서랍 한 무더기에서 꼬리표를 베껴 적고 있었는데, 그녀가 능숙한 그 자잘한 필체였다."], ["Farebrother was somewhere in the village, and the three ladies knew nothing of Fred’s peculiar relation to Mary: it was impossible for either of them to propose that they should walk round the garden, and Fred predicted to himself that he should have to go away without saying a word to her in private.", "페어브러더 씨는 마을 어딘가에 있었고, 세 부인은 프레드와 메리의 특별한 관계에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다. 둘 중 누구도 정원을 한 바퀴 돌자고 제안할 수 없었으니, 프레드는 그녀에게 단둘이 한마디도 하지 못한 채 떠나야 하리라고 스스로 예언했다."], ["He told her first of Christy’s arrival and then of his own engagement with her father; and he was comforted by seeing that this latter news touched her keenly.", "그는 먼저 크리스티가 도착했다는 소식을, 그다음에는 자기가 그녀의 아버지와 일하기로 했다는 소식을 전했다. 그리고 이 뒤의 소식이 그녀의 마음을 절절히 건드리는 것을 보고 위안을 얻었다."], ["She said hurriedly, “I am so glad,” and then bent over her writing to hinder any one from noticing her face.", "그녀는 황급히 \"정말 기뻐요\"라고 말하고는, 누구도 자기 얼굴을 알아채지 못하도록 글씨 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["But here was a subject which Mrs. Farebrother could not let pass.", "그러나 이것은 페어브러더 부인이 그냥 넘길 수 없는 화제였다."]], [["“You don’t mean, my dear Miss Garth, that you are glad to hear of a young man giving up the Church for which he was educated: you only mean that things being so, you are glad that he should be under an excellent man like your father.”", "\"가스 양, 설마 한 젊은이가 자기가 교육받은 성직을 포기한다는 소식이 기쁘다는 뜻은 아니겠지요. 사정이 그러하니 그가 가스 양 아버님 같은 훌륭한 분 밑에 있게 되어 기쁘다는 뜻일 뿐이겠지요.\""]], [["“No, really, Mrs. Farebrother, I am glad of both, I fear,”", "\"아니에요, 정말로, 페어브러더 부인, 저는 두 가지 다 기뻐요, 유감스럽게도요.\""], ["said Mary, cleverly getting rid of one rebellious tear.", "메리가 솟구치는 눈물 한 방울을 영리하게 떨쳐 내며 말했다."], ["“I have a dreadfully secular mind.", "\"저는 속물스럽기 짝이 없는 사람이거든요."], ["I never liked any clergyman except the Vicar of Wakefield and Mr. Farebrother.”", "저는 『웨이크필드의 목사』와 페어브러더 씨 말고는 어떤 성직자도 좋아한 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Now why, my dear?” said Mrs.", "* 『웨이크필드의 목사』: 올리버 골드스미스의 소설로, 그 주인공인 시골 목사를 가리킨다."], ["Farebrother, pausing on her large wooden knitting-needles and looking at Mary.", "\"아니 왜 그러니, 얘야?\" 페어브러더 부인이 커다란 나무 뜨개바늘을 멈추고 메리를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“You have always a good reason for your opinions, but this astonishes me.", "\"넌 늘 네 의견에 그럴듯한 이유가 있더니, 이건 정말 놀랍구나."], ["Of course I put out of the question those who preach new doctrine.", "물론 새로운 교리를 설교하는 자들은 논외로 친다만."], ["But why should you dislike clergymen?”", "그런데 어째서 성직자를 싫어하는 게냐?\""]], [["“Oh dear,”", "\"어머나,\""], ["said Mary, her face breaking into merriment as she seemed to consider a moment, “I don’t like their neckcloths.”", "메리가 잠시 생각하는 듯하더니 얼굴에 명랑한 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"저는 그분들의 목깃이 마음에 안 들어요.\""]], [["“Why, you don’t like Camden’s, then,”", "\"아니, 그럼 캠던의 것도 마음에 안 든다는 게냐.\""], ["said Miss Winifred, in some anxiety.", "위니프레드 양이 다소 걱정스레 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I do,” said Mary.", "\"아뇨, 그건 마음에 들어요.\""], ["“I don’t like the other clergymen’s neckcloths, because it is they who wear them.”", "메리가 말했다. \"다른 성직자들의 목깃이 마음에 안 드는 건, 그걸 두른 사람이 그분들이기 때문이에요.\""]], [["“How very puzzling!”", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 알쏭달쏭하니!\""], ["said Miss Noble, feeling that her own intellect was probably deficient.", "노블 양이 자기 머리가 아마 모자란 모양이라고 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“My dear, you are joking.", "\"얘야, 농담이로구나."], ["You would have better reasons than these for slighting so respectable a class of men,”", "그렇게 훌륭한 부류의 사람들을 깔보는 데는 이보다 나은 이유가 있어야지.\""], ["said Mrs. Farebrother, majestically.", "페어브러더 부인이 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["“Miss Garth has such severe notions of what people should be that it is difficult to satisfy her,”", "\"가스 양은 사람이 어떠해야 하는지에 대해 너무 엄격한 관념을 가지고 있어서 만족시켜 드리기가 어렵답니다.\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."]], [["“Well, I am glad at least that she makes an exception in favor of my son,”", "\"음, 그래도 적어도 내 아들은 예외로 쳐 준다니 기쁘구나.\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."]], [["Mary was wondering at Fred’s piqued tone, when Mr. Farebrother came in and had to hear the news about the engagement under Mr.", "메리가 프레드의 부아 난 어조에 의아해하고 있는데, 페어브러더 씨가 들어와 메리가 가스 씨 밑에서 일하게 된다는 소식을 들어야 했다."], ["Garth. At the end he said with quiet satisfaction, “That is right;”", "다 듣고 나서 그는 조용한 만족으로 말했다."], ["and then bent to look at Mary’s labels and praise her handwriting.", "\"그것 잘됐구나.\" 그러고는 몸을 숙여 메리의 꼬리표를 들여다보며 그녀의 필체를 칭찬했다."], ["Fred felt horribly jealous—was glad, of course, that Mr. Farebrother was so estimable, but wished that he had been ugly and fat as men at forty sometimes are.", "프레드는 끔찍하게 질투가 났다—물론 페어브러더 씨가 그토록 존경할 만한 사람이라 기뻤으나, 마흔 살 남자들이 더러 그렇듯 그가 못생기고 뚱뚱했더라면 싶었다."], ["It was clear what the end would be, since Mary openly placed Farebrother above everybody, and these women were all evidently encouraging the affair.", "메리가 페어브러더를 누구보다 위에 두는 것을 공공연히 드러내고, 이 여인들이 하나같이 분명 그 일을 부추기는 터라, 결말이 어떻게 될지는 자명했다."], ["He was feeling sure that he should have no chance of speaking to Mary, when Mr. Farebrother said—", "메리와 말할 기회는 없으리라 그가 확신하고 있는데, 페어브러더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Fred, help me to carry these drawers back into my study—you have never seen my fine new study.", "\"프레드, 이 서랍들을 내 서재로 도로 옮기는 걸 좀 도와주게—내 멋진 새 서재를 아직 못 봤지."], ["Pray come too, Miss Garth.", "가스 양도 부디 같이 와요."], ["I want you to see a stupendous spider I found this morning.”", "오늘 아침에 찾아낸 굉장한 거미를 보여 주고 싶군요.\""]], [["Mary at once saw the Vicar’s intention.", "메리는 곧 목사의 의도를 알아챘다."], ["He had never since the memorable evening deviated from his old pastoral kindness towards her, and her momentary wonder and doubt had quite gone to sleep.", "그는 그 잊을 수 없는 저녁 이후로 그녀를 향한 옛 목회자다운 친절에서 한 번도 벗어난 적이 없었고, 그녀의 순간적인 의아함과 의심은 완전히 잠들어 버린 터였다."], ["Mary was accustomed to think rather rigorously of what was probable, and if a belief flattered her vanity she felt warned to dismiss it as ridiculous, having early had much exercise in such dismissals.", "메리는 무엇이 그럴듯한지를 꽤나 엄정하게 생각하는 데 익숙했고, 어떤 믿음이 자신의 허영심을 추켜세우면 그것을 우스꽝스럽다며 떨쳐 내야 한다고 경계하는 마음이 들었으니, 그런 떨쳐 냄을 일찍부터 많이 연습해 온 까닭이었다. 그것은 그녀가 내다본 대로였다."], ["It was as she had foreseen: when Fred had been asked to admire the fittings of the study, and she had been asked to admire the spider, Mr. Farebrother said—", "프레드가 서재의 비품들을 감탄해 보라는 청을 받고, 그녀가 거미를 감탄해 보라는 청을 받고 나자, 페어브러더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Wait here a minute or two.", "\"여기서 잠깐만 기다려요."], ["I am going to look out an engraving which Fred is tall enough to hang for me.", "프레드가 키가 충분히 크니 나 대신 걸어 줄 판화를 하나 찾아오겠소."], ["I shall be back in a few minutes.”", "곧 돌아오리다.\""], ["And then he went out.", "그러고는 그가 나갔다."], ["Nevertheless, the first word Fred said to Mary was—", "그럼에도 프레드가 메리에게 처음으로 한 말은 이러했다."]], [["“It is of no use, whatever I do, Mary.", "\"내가 무얼 하든 소용없어, 메리."], ["You are sure to marry Farebrother at last.”", "너는 결국 틀림없이 페어브러더와 결혼하게 될 거야.\""], ["There was some rage in his tone.", "그의 어조에는 다소 분노가 서려 있었다."]], [["“What do you mean, Fred?”", "\"무슨 말이야, 프레드?\""], ["Mary exclaimed indignantly, blushing deeply, and surprised out of all her readiness in reply.", "메리가 발끈하여 외쳤고, 얼굴이 새빨개졌으며, 평소의 재치 있는 대꾸도 잊은 채 놀라 버렸다."]], [["“It is impossible that you should not see it all clearly enough—you who see everything.”", "\"네가 모든 걸 환히 보면서—모든 걸 다 보는 네가—이걸 못 본다는 건 있을 수 없는 일이야.\""]], [["“I only see that you are behaving very ill, Fred, in speaking so of Mr. Farebrother after he has pleaded your cause in every way.", "\"내가 보기엔, 프레드, 페어브러더 씨가 온갖 방법으로 너를 위해 변호해 주신 마당에 그분에 대해 그렇게 말하는 네가 아주 못되게 굴고 있을 뿐이야."], ["How can you have taken up such an idea?”", "어떻게 그런 생각을 다 했어?\""]], [["Fred was rather deep, in spite of his irritation.", "프레드는 짜증이 났음에도 자못 신중했다."], ["If Mary had really been unsuspicious, there was no good in telling her what Mrs. Garth had said.", "메리가 정말로 아무것도 의심하지 않았다면, 가스 부인이 한 말을 그녀에게 알려서 좋을 게 없었다."]], [["“It follows as a matter of course,”", "\"당연한 귀결이지.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied. “When you are continually seeing a man who beats me in everything, and whom you set up above everybody, I can have no fair chance.”", "\"매사에 나를 능가하고, 네가 누구보다 위에 모시는 사람을 네가 줄곧 만나고 있으니, 나에게는 공정한 기회가 있을 수 없어.\""]], [["“You are very ungrateful, Fred,”", "\"넌 정말 배은망덕하구나, 프레드.\""], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["“I wish I had never told Mr. Farebrother that I cared for you in the least.”", "\"페어브러더 씨에게 내가 너를 조금이라도 마음에 둔다고 말한 걸 후회하게 되네.\""]], [["“No, I am not ungrateful; I should be the happiest fellow in the world if it were not for this.", "\"아니, 난 배은망덕한 게 아니야. 이 일만 아니라면 난 세상에서 가장 행복한 녀석일 거야."], ["I told your father everything, and he was very kind; he treated me as if I were his son.", "난 네 아버지께 모든 걸 말씀드렸고, 그분은 무척 다정하셨어. 나를 마치 당신 아들처럼 대해 주셨지."], ["I could go at the work with a will, writing and everything, if it were not for this.”", "이 일만 아니라면 나는 글 쓰는 일이며 뭐며 의욕을 가지고 매달릴 수 있어.\""]], [["“For this?", "\"이 일?"], ["for what?”", "무슨 일?\""], ["said Mary, imagining now that something specific must have been said or done.", "메리가 이제는 무언가 구체적인 말이나 행동이 있었던 게 틀림없다고 짐작하며 말했다."]], [["“This dreadful certainty that I shall be bowled out by Farebrother.”", "\"내가 페어브러더에게 밀려나고 말 거라는 이 끔찍한 확신 말이야.\""], ["Mary was appeased by her inclination to laugh.", "메리는 웃음이 나오려는 마음에 누그러졌다."]], [["“Fred,”", "\"프레드,\""], ["she said, peeping round to catch his eyes, which were sulkily turned away from her, “you are too delightfully ridiculous.", "그녀가 자기에게서 뾰로통하게 돌려진 그의 눈을 마주치려고 빙 돌아 들여다보며 말했다. \"너는 너무 사랑스럽게 우스꽝스러워."], ["If you were not such a charming simpleton, what a temptation this would be to play the wicked coquette, and let you suppose that somebody besides you has made love to me.”", "네가 이렇게 매력적인 얼간이만 아니라면, 못된 요부 노릇을 하면서 너 말고도 누군가가 내게 구애했다고 믿게 만들고 싶은 유혹이 얼마나 컸겠니.\""]], [["“Do you really like me best, Mary?”", "\"정말 나를 제일 좋아하는 거야, 메리?\""], ["said Fred, turning eyes full of affection on her, and trying to take her hand.", "프레드가 애정 가득한 눈을 그녀에게 돌리며, 그녀의 손을 잡으려 하면서 말했다."]], [["“I don’t like you at all at this moment,”", "\"지금 이 순간엔 너를 조금도 좋아하지 않아.\""], ["said Mary, retreating, and putting her hands behind her.", "메리가 물러서며 두 손을 등 뒤로 감추고 말했다."], ["“I only said that no mortal ever made love to me besides you.", "\"나는 그저 너 말고는 살아 있는 그 누구도 내게 구애한 적이 없다고 말했을 뿐이야."], ["And that is no argument that a very wise man ever will,”", "그렇다고 어떤 아주 현명한 사람이 언젠가 그러리라는 근거가 되는 건 아니지.\""], ["she ended, merrily.", "그녀가 명랑하게 말을 맺었다."]], [["“I wish you would tell me that you could not possibly ever think of him,”", "\"네가 그 사람을 절대로 생각할 수 없다고 말해 주면 좋겠어.\""], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."]], [["“Never dare to mention this any more to me, Fred,”", "\"두 번 다시 이런 이야기를 내게 꺼낼 생각 마, 프레드.\""], ["said Mary, getting serious again.", "메리가 다시 진지해지며 말했다."], ["“I don’t know whether it is more stupid or ungenerous in you not to see that Mr. Farebrother has left us together on purpose that we might speak freely.", "\"페어브러더 씨가 우리 둘이 자유롭게 이야기하라고 일부러 자리를 비켜 주신 걸 네가 못 보는 게 더 어리석은 건지 더 야박한 건지 모르겠어."], ["I am disappointed that you should be so blind to his delicate feeling.”", "네가 그분의 섬세한 마음에 그렇게 눈멀어 있다니 실망이야.\""]], [["There was no time to say any more before Mr.", "페어브러더 씨가 판화를 가지고 돌아오기 전에 더 말할 시간은 없었다."], ["Farebrother came back with the engraving; and Fred had to return to the drawing-room still with a jealous dread in his heart, but yet with comforting arguments from Mary’s words and manner.", "프레드는 마음속에 여전히 질투 어린 두려움을 품은 채, 그러나 메리의 말과 태도에서 위안이 되는 근거도 얻은 채 객실로 돌아가야 했다."], ["The result of the conversation was on the whole more painful to Mary: inevitably her attention had taken a new attitude, and she saw the possibility of new interpretations.", "그 대화의 결과는 전체적으로 메리에게 더 괴로운 것이었다. 어쩔 수 없이 그녀의 관심은 새로운 태도를 취하게 되었고, 그녀는 새로운 해석의 가능성을 보게 되었던 것이다."], ["She was in a position in which she seemed to herself to be slighting Mr. Farebrother, and this, in relation to a man who is much honored, is always dangerous to the firmness of a grateful woman.", "그녀는 스스로 페어브러더 씨를 홀대하는 듯이 여겨지는 처지에 놓였는데, 이는 많은 존경을 받는 남자와 관련될 때 늘 고마움을 아는 여자의 굳건함에 위험한 일이었다."], ["To have a reason for going home the next day was a relief, for Mary earnestly desired to be always clear that she loved Fred best.", "다음 날 집에 갈 이유가 있다는 것이 안도가 되었으니, 메리는 자신이 프레드를 제일 사랑한다는 것을 늘 분명히 하고 싶은 마음이 간절했기 때문이다."], ["When a tender affection has been storing itself in us through many of our years, the idea that we could accept any exchange for it seems to be a cheapening of our lives.", "다정한 애정이 여러 해에 걸쳐 우리 안에 쌓여 왔을 때, 그것을 무언가와 맞바꿀 수 있다는 생각은 우리 삶을 헐값으로 만드는 일처럼 보인다."], ["And we can set a watch over our affections and our constancy as we can over other treasures.", "그리고 우리는 다른 보물들을 지키듯이 우리의 애정과 변함없음에도 파수꾼을 세워 둘 수 있다."]], [["“Fred has lost all his other expectations; he must keep this,”", "\"프레드는 다른 기대를 모두 잃었어. 이것만은 지켜야 해.\""], ["Mary said to herself, with a smile curling her lips.", "메리가 입가에 미소를 머금으며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["It was impossible to help fleeting visions of another kind—new dignities and an acknowledged value of which she had often felt the absence.", "또 다른 종류의 덧없는 환상들이—그녀가 그 부재를 자주 느껴 온 새로운 품위와, 인정받는 가치가—떠오르는 것을 막을 도리는 없었다."], ["But these things with Fred outside them, Fred forsaken and looking sad for the want of her, could never tempt her deliberate thought.", "그러나 이런 것들이 프레드를 빼놓은 채라면, 프레드가 버림받아 자기를 그리워하며 슬퍼 보인다면, 그것은 결코 그녀의 깊은 생각을 유혹할 수 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LVIII.", "# CHAPTER LVIII."]], [["“For there can live no hatred in thine eye, Therefore in that I cannot know thy change: In many’s looks the false heart’s history Is writ in moods and frowns and wrinkles strange: But Heaven in thy creation did decree That in thy face sweet love should ever dwell: Whate’er thy thoughts or thy heart’s workings be Thy looks should nothing thence but sweetness tell.”", "그러므로 나는 그대의 변심을 알아챌 길 없네."], ["—SHAKESPEARE: Sonnets.", "많은 이들의 표정에는 거짓된 마음의 내력이"]], [["At the time when Mr. Vincy uttered that presentiment about Rosamond, she herself had never had the idea that she should be driven to make the sort of appeal which he foresaw. She had not yet had any anxiety about ways and means, although her domestic life had been expensive as well as eventful. Her baby had been born prematurely, and all the embroidered robes and caps had to be laid by in darkness. This misfortune was attributed entirely to her having persisted in going out on horseback one day when her husband had desired her not to do so; but it must not be supposed that she had shown temper on the occasion, or rudely told him that she would do as she liked.", "기이한 변덕과 찡그림과 주름으로 새겨지건만,\n하늘은 그대를 빚을 때 이렇게 정하셨으니"]], [["What led her particularly to desire horse-exercise was a visit from Captain Lydgate, the baronet’s third son, who, I am sorry to say, was detested by our Tertius of that name as a vapid fop “parting his hair from brow to nape in a despicable fashion” (not followed by Tertius himself), and showing an ignorant security that he knew the proper thing to say on every topic. Lydgate inwardly cursed his own folly that he had drawn down this visit by consenting to go to his uncle’s on the wedding-tour, and he made himself rather disagreeable to Rosamond by saying so in private. For to Rosamond this visit was a source of unprecedented but gracefully concealed exultation. She was so intensely conscious of having a cousin who was a baronet’s son staying in the house, that she imagined the knowledge of what was implied by his presence to be diffused through all other minds; and when she introduced Captain Lydgate to her guests, she had a placid sense that his rank penetrated them as if it had been an odor. The satisfaction was enough for the time to melt away some disappointment in the conditions of marriage with a medical man even of good birth: it seemed now that her marriage was visibly as well as ideally floating her above the Middlemarch level, and the future looked bright with letters and visits to and from Quallingham, and vague advancement in consequence for Tertius. Especially as, probably at the Captain’s suggestion, his married sister, Mrs. Mengan, had come with her maid, and stayed two nights on her way from town. Hence it was clearly worth while for Rosamond to take pains with her music and the careful selection of her lace.", "그대 얼굴에는 언제나 달콤한 사랑이 머물러야 한다고. 그대의 생각이 무엇이든, 그대 마음의 움직임이 어떠하든"]], [["As to Captain Lydgate himself, his low brow, his aquiline nose bent on one side, and his rather heavy utterance, might have been disadvantageous in any young gentleman who had not a military bearing and mustache to give him what is doted on by some flower-like blond heads as “style.” He had, moreover, that sort of high-breeding which consists in being free from the petty solicitudes of middle-class gentility, and he was a great critic of feminine charms. Rosamond delighted in his admiration now even more than she had done at Quallingham, and he found it easy to spend several hours of the day in flirting with her. The visit altogether was one of the pleasantest larks he had ever had, not the less so perhaps because he suspected that his queer cousin Tertius wished him away: though Lydgate, who would rather (hyperbolically speaking) have died than have failed in polite hospitality, suppressed his dislike, and only pretended generally not to hear what the gallant officer said, consigning the task of answering him to Rosamond. For he was not at all a jealous husband, and preferred leaving a feather-headed young gentleman alone with his wife to bearing him company.", "그대의 표정은 오직 달콤함 외에 아무것도 말하지 않으리. — 셰익스피어, 『소네트』"]], [["“I wish you would talk more to the Captain at dinner, Tertius,” said Rosamond, one evening when the important guest was gone to Loamford to see some brother officers stationed there. “You really look so absent sometimes—you seem to be seeing through his head into something behind it, instead of looking at him.”", "빈시 씨가 로저먼드를 두고 그런 예감을 말했을 무렵, 정작 그녀 자신은 아버지가 예견한 그런 종류의 부탁을 하게 되리라고는 생각조차 해 본 적이 없었다. 그녀의 가정생활은 다사다난한 동시에 사치스럽기도 했지만, 그녀는 아직 돈을 마련하는 방도에 대해 아무런 불안도 느끼지 않았다. 아기는 달을 채우지 못하고 일찍 태어났고, 수놓인 배냇저고리와 모자는 모두 어둠 속에 치워 두어야 했다. 이 불운은 전적으로, 남편이 하지 말라고 당부했는데도 그녀가 어느 날 굳이 말을 타고 외출한 탓으로 돌려졌다. 그러나 그녀가 그 일로 성을 냈다거나, 자기 마음대로 하겠다고 무례하게 쏘아붙였다고 짐작해서는 안 된다. 그녀가 특별히 승마를 하고 싶어 하게 된 것은 준남작의 셋째 아들인 리드게이트 대위의 방문 때문이었는데, 안타깝게도 같은 성을 지닌 우리의 터셔스는 그를 시시한 멋쟁이라며 몹시 싫어했다. \"이마에서 목덜미까지 가르마를 천박하게 타고는\"(터셔스 자신은 따르지 않는 방식이었다) 어떤 주제에 대해서든 적절히 말할 줄 안다는 무지한 자신감을 드러내는 자라는 것이었다. 리드게이트는 신혼여행 중에 숙부 댁에 가기로 응낙하는 바람에 이 방문을 끌어들였다고 자기 어리석음을 속으로 저주했고, 그 말을 사석에서 털어놓아 로저먼드에게 다소 거슬리는 사람이 되었다. 로저먼드에게는 이 방문이 전에 없던, 그러나 우아하게 감춰진 의기양양함의 원천이었기 때문이다. 그녀는 준남작의 아들인 사촌이 집에 머물고 있다는 사실을 너무도 강렬하게 의식한 나머지, 그의 존재가 함축하는 바를 다른 모든 사람의 마음도 알고 있으리라 상상했다. 그래서 리드게이트 대위를 손님들에게 소개할 때 그의 신분이 마치 향기처럼 그들에게 스며든다는 평온한 느낌을 받았다. 그 만족감은 한동안, 좋은 가문 출신이라 해도 의사와 결혼한 처지에 대한 약간의 실망을 녹여 버리기에 충분했다. 이제 그녀의 결혼은 관념적으로뿐 아니라 눈에 보이게도 그녀를 미들마치의 수준 위로 떠받쳐 주는 것 같았고, 퀄링엄과 주고받는 편지와 방문으로, 그리고 그에 따른 터셔스의 막연한 출세로 미래가 밝아 보였다. 더구나 아마도 대위의 권유였겠지만, 그의 출가한 누이인 멘건 부인이 하녀를 데리고 와서, 런던에서 돌아가는 길에 이틀 밤을 묵었다. 그러니 로저먼드가 음악에 공을 들이고 레이스를 신중히 고르는 데 정성을 쏟을 만한 가치가 분명히 있었다."]], [["“My dear Rosy, you don’t expect me to talk much to such a conceited ass as that, I hope,”", "리드게이트 대위로 말하자면, 낮은 이마와 한쪽으로 휜 매부리코와 다소 굼뜬 말투는, 군인다운 풍채와 콧수염이 없었더라면 어느 젊은 신사에게든 불리하게 작용했을 법한 것들이었다."], ["said Lydgate, brusquely.", "그 풍채와 콧수염이, 꽃처럼 어여쁜 금발 머리를 가진 어떤 여자들이 떠받드는 이른바 \"스타일\"을 그에게 부여해 주었던 것이다."], ["“If he got his head broken, I might look at it with interest, not before.”", "게다가 그는 중산층 신사 계층의 자질구레한 걱정에서 자유롭다는 점에서 고상한 가문 태생다운 면모를 지녔고, 여성의 매력에 대한 대단한 비평가였다. 로저먼드는 퀄링엄에서보다 지금 그의 찬탄을 더욱 기뻐했고, 그는 하루에 몇 시간씩 그녀와 시시덕거리며 보내기를 쉬운 일로 여겼다."], ["“I cannot conceive why you should speak of your cousin so contemptuously,”", "그 방문은 전체적으로 그가 누려 본 가장 즐거운 장난 가운데 하나였는데, 어쩌면 그 괴짜 사촌 터셔스가 자기를 쫓아내고 싶어 한다는 것을 눈치챘기 때문에 그 즐거움이 덜하지 않았을 것이다."], ["said Rosamond, her fingers moving at her work while she spoke with a mild gravity which had a touch of disdain in it.", "다만 (과장해서 말하자면) 정중한 환대를 그르치느니 차라리 죽고 싶어 했을 리드게이트는 그 혐오감을 억누른 채, 대체로 그 멋진 장교가 하는 말을 못 들은 척하면서 그에게 응답하는 일을 로저먼드에게 떠맡길 뿐이었다. 왜냐하면 그는 결코 질투하는 남편이 아니어서, 머리가 텅 빈 젊은 신사와 자리를 함께하느니 차라리 그를 아내와 단둘이 내버려 두는 편을 택했기 때문이다."]], [["“Ask Ladislaw if he doesn’t think your Captain the greatest bore he ever met with.", "\"터셔스, 저녁 식사 때 대위와 좀 더 이야기를 나누셨으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["Ladislaw has almost forsaken the house since he came.”", "어느 날 저녁, 그 중요한 손님이 그곳에 주둔한 동료 장교들을 만나러 로엄퍼드에 간 사이에 로저먼드가 말했다."], ["Rosamond thought she knew perfectly well why Mr.", "\"당신은 정말이지 가끔 너무 멍해 보여요."], ["Ladislaw disliked the Captain: he was jealous, and she liked his being jealous.", "그를 바라보는 게 아니라 그의 머리를 꿰뚫고 그 뒤에 있는 무언가를 보고 있는 것 같다니까요.\""]], [["“It is impossible to say what will suit eccentric persons,”", "\"여보 로지, 설마 내가 저런 잘난 척하는 멍청이와 많은 이야기를 나누기를 바라는 건 아니겠지.\""], ["she answered, “but in my opinion Captain Lydgate is a thorough gentleman, and I think you ought not, out of respect to Sir Godwin, to treat him with neglect.”", "리드게이트가 퉁명스럽게 말했다. \"저 친구 머리가 깨지기라도 하면 흥미롭게 들여다볼지 몰라도, 그전에는 어림없어.\""]], [["“No, dear; but we have had dinners for him.", "\"왜 당신 사촌을 그토록 경멸하듯 말하는지 이해할 수가 없네요.\""], ["And he comes in and goes out as he likes. He doesn’t want me.”", "로저먼드가 말했는데, 그녀는 말하는 동안 손으로 바느질감을 놀리면서 약간의 멸시가 담긴 온화한 진지함을 보였다."]], [["“Still, when he is in the room, you might show him more attention.", "\"래디슬로한테 당신 대위가 여태껏 만나 본 가장 지독한 따분쟁이가 아닌지 물어보구려."], ["He may not be a phoenix of cleverness in your sense; his profession is different; but it would be all the better for you to talk a little on his subjects.", "그가 온 뒤로 래디슬로는 거의 이 집 발길을 끊다시피 했으니까.\" 로저먼드는 래디슬로 씨가 왜 대위를 싫어하는지 자기가 완벽하게 알고 있다고 생각했다."], ["I think his conversation is quite agreeable. And he is anything but an unprincipled man.”", "그는 질투하고 있었고, 그녀는 그가 질투하는 것이 마음에 들었다."]], [["“The fact is, you would wish me to be a little more like him, Rosy,” said Lydgate, in a sort of resigned murmur, with a smile which was not exactly tender, and certainly not merry. Rosamond was silent and did not smile again; but the lovely curves of her face looked good-tempered enough without smiling.", "\"별난 사람들에게 뭐가 맞는지는 알 수 없는 노릇이죠.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"하지만 제 생각에 리드게이트 대위는 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 신사예요. 그리고 당신은 고드윈 경에 대한 예의로라도 그를 소홀히 대해서는 안 된다고 봐요.\" \"안 그래, 여보. 하지만 우리는 그를 위해 만찬을 여러 번 베풀었잖소. 게다가 그 친구는 제 마음대로 드나든다고. 나를 필요로 하지도 않고.\""]], [["Those words of Lydgate’s were like a sad milestone marking how far he had travelled from his old dreamland, in which Rosamond Vincy appeared to be that perfect piece of womanhood who would reverence her husband’s mind after the fashion of an accomplished mermaid, using her comb and looking-glass and singing her song for the relaxation of his adored wisdom alone. He had begun to distinguish between that imagined adoration and the attraction towards a man’s talent because it gives him prestige, and is like an order in his button-hole or an Honorable before his name.", "\"그래도 그가 방에 있을 때는 좀 더 관심을 보일 수 있잖아요. 당신이 말하는 의미에서 그가 비범하게 영리한 사람은 아닐지 몰라요. 직업이 다르니까요. 하지만 당신이 그의 관심사에 대해 조금이라도 이야기를 나눈다면 당신에게도 더 좋을 거예요. 저는 그의 대화가 꽤 유쾌하다고 생각해요. 그리고 그는 결코 원칙 없는 사람이 아니에요.\" \"사실은 당신이 나도 그를 조금 닮기를 바라는 거로군, 로지.\" 리드게이트가 일종의 체념 어린 중얼거림으로, 그다지 다정하지도 않고 분명 유쾌하지도 않은 미소를 지으며 말했다. 로저먼드는 잠자코 다시 미소 짓지 않았다. 하지만 미소가 없어도 그녀의 얼굴에 흐르는 사랑스러운 곡선은 충분히 온화해 보였다."]], [["It might have been supposed that Rosamond had travelled too, since she had found the pointless conversation of Mr.", "리드게이트의 그 말은, 그가 예전의 꿈나라에서 얼마나 멀리 떠나왔는지를 표시하는 서글픈 이정표 같은 것이었다."], ["Ned Plymdale perfectly wearisome; but to most mortals there is a stupidity which is unendurable and a stupidity which is altogether acceptable—else, indeed, what would become of social bonds? Captain Lydgate’s stupidity was delicately scented, carried itself with “style,”", "그 꿈나라에서 로저먼드 빈시는, 자기 빗과 거울을 사용하고 오로지 남편의 흠모받는 지혜를 쉬게 하기 위해 노래를 부르는, 재주 많은 인어처럼 남편의 정신을 경배할 완벽한 여성의 표본으로 보였다."], ["talked with a good accent, and was closely related to Sir Godwin. Rosamond found it quite agreeable and caught many of its phrases.", "그는 그 상상 속의 경배와, 한 남자의 재능이 그에게 명성을 가져다주기에—단춧구멍의 훈장이나 이름 앞에 붙는 경칭과 같기에—그 재능에 이끌리는 것을 구별하기 시작했다."]], [["Therefore since Rosamond, as we know, was fond of horseback, there were plenty of reasons why she should be tempted to resume her riding when Captain Lydgate, who had ordered his man with two horses to follow him and put up at the “Green Dragon,” begged her to go out on the gray which he warranted to be gentle and trained to carry a lady—indeed, he had bought it for his sister, and was taking it to Quallingham. Rosamond went out the first time without telling her husband, and came back before his return; but the ride had been so thorough a success, and she declared herself so much the better in consequence, that he was informed of it with full reliance on his consent that she should go riding again.", "로저먼드 역시 길을 떠나왔다고 짐작할 수도 있었을 것이다. 그녀는 네드 플림데일 씨의 핵심 없는 대화를 완전히 지겹게 여겼으니 말이다. 하지만 대다수 인간에게는 견딜 수 없는 어리석음과 전적으로 받아들일 만한 어리석음이 있는 법이다. 그렇지 않다면 사회적 유대가 도대체 어떻게 되겠는가? 리드게이트 대위의 어리석음은 은은한 향기를 풍겼고, \"스타일\"을 갖추고 있었으며, 좋은 억양으로 이야기했고, 고드윈 경과 가까운 친척이었다. 로저먼드는 그것을 꽤 유쾌하게 여겼고, 그 말투를 많이 받아들였다. 그러므로 우리가 아는 대로 로저먼드는 승마를 좋아했으니, 리드게이트 대위가 다시 말을 타도록 그녀를 유혹할 만한 이유는 충분했다. 대위는 하인에게 말 두 필을 끌고 자기를 뒤따라와 \"그린 드래건\"에 묵으라고 일러 두었고, 그녀에게 그 회색 말을 타고 나가자고 청했다. 그는 그 말이 온순하며 숙녀를 태우도록 훈련되었음을 보장했다. 사실 그것은 누이를 위해 사들인 말로, 퀄링엄으로 데려가는 중이었다. 로저먼드는 처음에는 남편에게 알리지 않고 외출했다가 그가 돌아오기 전에 돌아왔다. 하지만 그 승마가 너무도 완벽한 성공이었고, 그 덕분에 자기 몸 상태가 훨씬 좋아졌다고 그녀가 단언했으므로, 그녀는 남편이 자기가 다시 말을 타러 가는 것에 동의해 주리라 전적으로 믿으며 그에게 그 사실을 알렸다."]], [["On the contrary Lydgate was more than hurt—he was utterly confounded that she had risked herself on a strange horse without referring the matter to his wish.", "그러나 리드게이트는 단지 상처받은 정도가 아니었다. 그는 그녀가 자기 뜻을 물어보지도 않고 낯선 말에 위험을 무릅썼다는 사실에 완전히 어안이 벙벙했다."], ["After the first almost thundering exclamations of astonishment, which sufficiently warned Rosamond of what was coming, he was silent for some moments.", "다가올 일을 충분히 예고하는, 거의 천둥 같은 첫 경악의 외침을 쏟아 낸 뒤, 그는 잠시 말이 없었다."]], [["“However, you have come back safely,”", "\"하지만 어쨌든 무사히 돌아왔으니.\""], ["he said, at last, in a decisive tone.", "마침내 그가 단호한 어조로 말했다."], ["“You will not go again, Rosy; that is understood.", "\"다시는 가지 않을 거요, 로지. 그렇게 알아 둬요."], ["If it were the quietest, most familiar horse in the world, there would always be the chance of accident.", "세상에서 가장 얌전하고 가장 익숙한 말이라 해도 사고의 위험은 언제나 있는 법이오."], ["And you know very well that I wished you to give up riding the roan on that account.”", "그래서 내가 당신더러 그 적갈색 말을 그만 타라고 한 걸 당신도 잘 알잖소.\""]], [["“But there is the chance of accident indoors, Tertius.”", "\"하지만 집 안에서도 사고의 위험은 있어요, 터셔스.\""]], [["“My darling, don’t talk nonsense,”", "\"여보, 말도 안 되는 소리 하지 마.\""], ["said Lydgate, in an imploring tone; “surely I am the person to judge for you.", "리드게이트가 애원하는 어조로 말했다. \"당신을 위해 판단해 줄 사람은 분명 나잖소."], ["I think it is enough that I say you are not to go again.”", "다시는 가지 말라고 내가 말하는 것으로 충분하다고 보오.\""]], [["Rosamond was arranging her hair before dinner, and the reflection of her head in the glass showed no change in its loveliness except a little turning aside of the long neck.", "로저먼드는 저녁 식사 전에 머리를 매만지고 있었는데, 거울에 비친 그녀의 머리는 긴 목을 약간 옆으로 돌린 것 외에는 그 사랑스러움에 아무런 변화가 없었다."], ["Lydgate had been moving about with his hands in his pockets, and now paused near her, as if he awaited some assurance.", "리드게이트는 두 손을 주머니에 넣은 채 서성이다가 이제 그녀 가까이에서 멈춰 섰는데, 마치 어떤 다짐을 기다리는 것 같았다."]], [["“I wish you would fasten up my plaits, dear,”", "\"여보, 땋은 머리를 좀 고정해 줘요.\""], ["said Rosamond, letting her arms fall with a little sigh, so as to make a husband ashamed of standing there like a brute.", "로저먼드가 작게 한숨을 쉬며 두 팔을 떨어뜨리고 말했는데, 짐승처럼 우두커니 서 있는 남편이 부끄러워지게 만들 만한 모양새였다."], ["Lydgate had often fastened the plaits before, being among the deftest of men with his large finely formed fingers.", "리드게이트는 크지만 곱게 빚어진 손가락을 지닌, 사내 중에서도 가장 솜씨 좋은 축에 들었기에 전에도 종종 그 땋은 머리를 고정해 주곤 했다."], ["He swept up the soft festoons of plaits and fastened in the tall comb (to such uses do men come!); and what could he do then but kiss the exquisite nape which was shown in all its delicate curves?", "그는 부드러운 머리 타래를 쓸어 올려 큼직한 빗으로 고정했다(사내가 이런 일에까지 이르다니!). 그러고 나서 섬세한 곡선을 그대로 드러낸 그 우아한 목덜미에 입 맞추는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["But when we do what we have done before, it is often with a difference.", "하지만 우리가 전에 했던 일을 다시 할 때는, 흔히 약간의 차이가 있는 법이다."], ["Lydgate was still angry, and had not forgotten his point.", "리드게이트는 여전히 화가 나 있었고, 자기 주장을 잊지 않고 있었다."]], [["“I shall tell the Captain that he ought to have known better than offer you his horse,”", "\"대위한테 당신에게 말을 권하다니 분별이 없었다고 말해야겠소.\""], ["he said, as he moved away.", "그가 물러나며 말했다."]], [["“I beg you will not do anything of the kind, Tertius,”", "\"제발 그런 일은 하지 마세요, 터셔스.\""], ["said Rosamond, looking at him with something more marked than usual in her speech.", "로저먼드가 평소보다 한층 또렷한 말투로 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“It will be treating me as if I were a child.", "\"그건 저를 어린애 취급하는 거예요."], ["Promise that you will leave the subject to me.”", "이 문제는 제게 맡기겠다고 약속해 주세요.\""]], [["There did seem to be some truth in her objection.", "그녀의 항변에는 어느 정도 진실이 있는 듯 보였다."], ["Lydgate said, “Very well,” with a surly obedience, and thus the discussion ended with his promising Rosamond, and not with her promising him.", "리드게이트는 무뚝뚝하게 복종하며 \"좋아\"라고 말했고, 그리하여 그 논쟁은 그녀가 그에게 약속하는 것이 아니라 그가 로저먼드에게 약속하는 것으로 끝났다."]], [["In fact, she had been determined not to promise.", "사실 그녀는 약속하지 않기로 작정하고 있었다."], ["Rosamond had that victorious obstinacy which never wastes its energy in impetuous resistance.", "로저먼드에게는 결코 충동적인 저항에 기운을 허비하지 않는 승리하는 고집이 있었다."], ["What she liked to do was to her the right thing, and all her cleverness was directed to getting the means of doing it.", "그녀가 하고 싶은 일은 그녀에게 곧 옳은 일이었고, 그녀의 모든 영리함은 그것을 할 수단을 손에 넣는 데로 향했다. 그녀는 다시 그 회색 말을 타고 나갈 작정이었고, 남편이 자리를 비운 다음 기회에 정말로 그렇게 했다."], ["She meant to go out riding again on the gray, and she did go on the next opportunity of her husband’s absence, not intending that he should know until it was late enough not to signify to her.", "자기에게 문제가 되지 않을 만큼 늦은 시점이 되기 전에는 남편이 알지 못하게 할 셈이었다. 유혹은 분명 컸다."], ["The temptation was certainly great: she was very fond of the exercise, and the gratification of riding on a fine horse, with Captain Lydgate, Sir Godwin’s son, on another fine horse by her side, and of being met in this position by any one but her husband, was something as good as her dreams before marriage: moreover she was riveting the connection with the family at Quallingham, which must be a wise thing to do.", "그녀는 그 운동을 무척 좋아했고, 멋진 말을 타고 그 옆에서 고드윈 경의 아들인 리드게이트 대위가 또 다른 멋진 말을 타고 가는 가운데, 남편이 아닌 누군가에게 그런 모습을 들킨다는 만족감은 결혼 전 그녀의 꿈만큼이나 좋은 것이었다. 게다가 그녀는 퀄링엄 가문과의 인연을 단단히 다지고 있었으니, 이는 분명 현명한 일임에 틀림없었다."]], [["But the gentle gray, unprepared for the crash of a tree that was being felled on the edge of Halsell wood, took fright, and caused a worse fright to Rosamond, leading finally to the loss of her baby.", "하지만 그 온순한 회색 말은 핼셀 숲 가장자리에서 베어 넘어지는 나무가 쾅 하고 쓰러지는 소리에 미처 대비하지 못해 놀랐고, 그 바람에 로저먼드에게 더 큰 놀라움을 안겨, 결국 그녀가 아기를 잃는 결과로 이어졌다."], ["Lydgate could not show his anger towards her, but he was rather bearish to the Captain, whose visit naturally soon came to an end.", "리드게이트는 그녀에게 화를 낼 수 없었지만, 대위에게는 다소 무뚝뚝하게 굴었고, 대위의 방문은 자연히 곧 끝났다."]], [["In all future conversations on the subject, Rosamond was mildly certain that the ride had made no difference, and that if she had stayed at home the same symptoms would have come on and would have ended in the same way, because she had felt something like them before.", "이후 그 일에 관한 모든 대화에서 로저먼드는, 그 승마가 아무런 영향을 끼치지 않았으며, 만약 자기가 집에 있었더라도 똑같은 증상이 나타나 똑같은 식으로 끝났을 거라고 온화하게 확신했다. 전에도 그와 비슷한 무언가를 느낀 적이 있었기 때문이라는 것이었다."]], [["Lydgate could only say, “Poor, poor darling!”—but he secretly wondered over the terrible tenacity of this mild creature.", "리드게이트는 \"가엾고도 가엾은 내 사랑!\"이라고밖에 말할 수 없었다. 하지만 그는 이 온순한 사람의 무서운 끈질김을 속으로 의아해했다."], ["There was gathering within him an amazed sense of his powerlessness over Rosamond.", "그의 안에서는 로저먼드에 대한 자신의 무력함을 깨닫는 놀라운 감각이 자라나고 있었다."], ["His superior knowledge and mental force, instead of being, as he had imagined, a shrine to consult on all occasions, was simply set aside on every practical question.", "그의 뛰어난 지식과 정신력은, 그가 상상했던 것처럼 모든 일에서 상의할 성소가 되기는커녕, 실제적인 모든 문제에서 그저 한쪽으로 밀쳐졌다."], ["He had regarded Rosamond’s cleverness as precisely of the receptive kind which became a woman.", "그는 로저먼드의 영리함을 바로 여자에게 어울리는 수용적인 종류의 것으로 여겨 왔다."], ["He was now beginning to find out what that cleverness was—what was the shape into which it had run as into a close network aloof and independent.", "그는 이제 그 영리함이 어떤 것인지—그것이 어떤 형태로, 마치 동떨어지고 독립된 촘촘한 그물망처럼 굳어졌는지 알아 가기 시작했다. 자기 취향과 관심의 궤도 안에 놓인 원인과 결과를 보는 데 로저먼드보다 빠른 사람은 없었다."], ["No one quicker than Rosamond to see causes and effects which lay within the track of her own tastes and interests: she had seen clearly Lydgate’s preeminence in Middlemarch society, and could go on imaginatively tracing still more agreeable social effects when his talent should have advanced him; but for her, his professional and scientific ambition had no other relation to these desirable effects than if they had been the fortunate discovery of an ill-smelling oil.", "그녀는 미들마치 사교계에서 리드게이트가 두드러진 존재임을 분명히 보았고, 그의 재능이 그를 출세시켰을 때 더욱 유쾌해질 사교적 결과들을 상상으로 계속 그려 나갈 수 있었다. 하지만 그녀에게 그의 직업적이고 과학적인 야망은, 마치 그것이 고약한 냄새가 나는 기름을 운 좋게 발견한 것에 지나지 않는 듯, 그 바람직한 결과들과 그 외의 다른 관계가 없었다."], ["And that oil apart, with which she had nothing to do, of course she believed in her own opinion more than she did in his.", "그리고 자기와는 아무 상관도 없는 그 기름을 제쳐 둔다면, 그녀는 당연히 그의 의견보다 자기 의견을 더 믿었다."], ["Lydgate was astounded to find in numberless trifling matters, as well as in this last serious case of the riding, that affection did not make her compliant.", "리드게이트는 이번의 심각한 승마 사건뿐 아니라 수없이 사소한 문제에서도 애정이 그녀를 순순하게 만들지 못한다는 것을 알고 경악했다."], ["He had no doubt that the affection was there, and had no presentiment that he had done anything to repel it.", "그는 애정이 거기 있다는 것을 의심하지 않았고, 자기가 그 애정을 밀어낼 어떤 일을 했으리라는 예감도 없었다."], ["For his own part he said to himself that he loved her as tenderly as ever, and could make up his mind to her negations; but—well!", "그 자신으로 말하자면, 여전히 변함없이 다정하게 그녀를 사랑하며 그녀의 거부도 받아들이기로 마음먹을 수 있다고 스스로에게 말했다. 하지만—아아!"], ["Lydgate was much worried, and conscious of new elements in his life as noxious to him as an inlet of mud to a creature that has been used to breathe and bathe and dart after its illuminated prey in the clearest of waters.", "리드게이트는 무척 시달렸고, 자기 삶에 새로 들어온 요소들을 의식했다. 그것들은, 가장 맑은 물속에서 숨 쉬고 멱 감으며 햇빛에 반짝이는 먹이를 향해 쏜살같이 달려들곤 하던 생물에게 진흙탕이 흘러드는 것만큼이나 그에게 해로운 것이었다."]], [["Rosamond was soon looking lovelier than ever at her worktable, enjoying drives in her father’s phaeton and thinking it likely that she might be invited to Quallingham.", "로저먼드는 곧 자기 작업대 앞에서 그 어느 때보다 사랑스러워 보였고, 아버지의 사륜마차로 드라이브를 즐기며 퀄링엄에 초대받을지도 모른다고 생각했다."], ["She knew that she was a much more exquisite ornament to the drawing-room there than any daughter of the family, and in reflecting that the gentlemen were aware of that, did not perhaps sufficiently consider whether the ladies would be eager to see themselves surpassed.", "그녀는 자기가 그곳 응접실에서 그 집안의 어떤 딸보다도 훨씬 더 정교한 장식물이라는 것을 알았고, 신사들이 그 점을 알고 있다고 생각하면서도, 부인들이 자기들이 능가당하는 꼴을 기꺼이 보려 할지는 아마 충분히 헤아리지 못했다."]], [["Lydgate, relieved from anxiety about her, relapsed into what she inwardly called his moodiness—a name which to her covered his thoughtful preoccupation with other subjects than herself, as well as that uneasy look of the brow and distaste for all ordinary things as if they were mixed with bitter herbs, which really made a sort of weather-glass to his vexation and foreboding.", "그녀에 대한 불안에서 벗어난 리드게이트는, 그녀가 속으로 그의 침울함이라 부르는 상태로 다시 빠져들었다. 그녀에게 그 이름은, 그가 자기 아닌 다른 주제에 사려 깊게 골몰하는 것뿐 아니라, 마치 평범한 모든 것에 쓴 약초가 섞이기라도 한 듯 그 모든 것을 못마땅해하는 불안한 이맛살을 가리키는 것이었다."], ["These latter states of mind had one cause amongst others, which he had generously but mistakenly avoided mentioning to Rosamond, lest it should affect her health and spirits.", "사실 그것은 그의 근심과 불길한 예감을 알려 주는 일종의 청우계 같은 것이었다. 이런 후자의 심적 상태에는 다른 여러 원인 가운데 하나가 있었는데, 그는 그녀의 건강과 기분에 영향을 줄까 봐 너그러우면서도 잘못된 마음으로 그것을 로저먼드에게 말하지 않고 있었다."], ["Between him and her indeed there was that total missing of each other’s mental track, which is too evidently possible even between persons who are continually thinking of each other.", "사실 그와 그녀 사이에는 서로의 정신적 궤도를 완전히 어긋나게 비껴가는 일이 있었으니, 이는 끊임없이 서로를 생각하는 사람들 사이에서조차 너무도 분명하게 가능한 일이다."], ["To Lydgate it seemed that he had been spending month after month in sacrificing more than half of his best intent and best power to his tenderness for Rosamond; bearing her little claims and interruptions without impatience, and, above all, bearing without betrayal of bitterness to look through less and less of interfering illusion at the blank unreflecting surface her mind presented to his ardor for the more impersonal ends of his profession and his scientific study, an ardor which he had fancied that the ideal wife must somehow worship as sublime, though not in the least knowing why.", "리드게이트에게는, 자기가 여러 달에 걸쳐 자기의 가장 좋은 의도와 가장 좋은 능력의 절반 이상을 로저먼드에 대한 다정함에 희생하며 보낸 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그녀의 자질구레한 요구와 방해를 짜증 내지 않고 견뎌 냈고, 무엇보다도 쓴 기색 한번 내비치지 않은 채, 점점 줄어드는 환상의 장막 너머로, 그의 직업과 과학 연구라는 한층 비개인적인 목적을 향한 열정에 그녀의 마음이 내미는 그 텅 비고 아무것도 비추지 않는 표면을 바라보며 견뎠다."], ["But his endurance was mingled with a self-discontent which, if we know how to be candid, we shall confess to make more than half our bitterness under grievances, wife or husband included.", "그는 이상적인 아내라면 그 열정을 어찌하든 숭고한 것으로 경배해야 한다고 상상했지만, 정작 그 이유는 조금도 알지 못했다. 그러나 그의 인내에는 자기 자신에 대한 불만이 뒤섞여 있었으니, 우리가 솔직해질 줄 안다면, 아내든 남편이든 불만스러운 처지에서 느끼는 쓰라림의 절반 이상을 만들어 내는 것이 바로 그 자기 불만임을 고백하게 될 것이다."], ["It always remains true that if we had been greater, circumstance would have been less strong against us.", "우리가 더 위대했더라면 환경이 우리에게 그토록 강하게 맞서지는 않았으리라는 것은 언제나 진실로 남는다."], ["Lydgate was aware that his concessions to Rosamond were often little more than the lapse of slackening resolution, the creeping paralysis apt to seize an enthusiasm which is out of adjustment to a constant portion of our lives.", "리드게이트는 자기가 로저먼드에게 양보하는 것이 흔히, 느슨해진 결심이 무너지는 것에 지나지 않음을, 우리 삶의 일정 부분과 조화를 이루지 못하는 열정을 곧잘 사로잡는 슬금슬금 다가오는 마비 증세에 지나지 않음을 알고 있었다."], ["And on Lydgate’s enthusiasm there was constantly pressing not a simple weight of sorrow, but the biting presence of a petty degrading care, such as casts the blight of irony over all higher effort.", "그리고 리드게이트의 열정 위에는 단순한 슬픔의 무게가 아니라, 모든 고상한 노력 위로 아이러니의 먹구름을 드리우는, 자질구레하고 비천한 근심거리가 따끔하게 짓누르고 있었다."]], [["This was the care which he had hitherto abstained from mentioning to Rosamond; and he believed, with some wonder, that it had never entered her mind, though certainly no difficulty could be less mysterious. It was an inference with a conspicuous handle to it, and had been easily drawn by indifferent observers, that Lydgate was in debt; and he could not succeed in keeping out of his mind for long together that he was every day getting deeper into that swamp, which tempts men towards it with such a pretty covering of flowers and verdure. It is wonderful how soon a man gets up to his chin there—in a condition in which, in spite of himself, he is forced to think chiefly of release, though he had a scheme of the universe in his soul.", "이것이 바로 그가 지금껏 로저먼드에게 말하기를 삼가 온 근심이었다. 그리고 그는, 분명 이보다 덜 알기 어려운 곤경도 없을 텐데 그것이 그녀의 마음에는 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없는 듯하다는 사실을 다소 의아해하며 믿고 있었다. 그것은 손잡이가 빤히 드러난 추론이어서, 무심한 관찰자들조차 쉽게 끌어낼 수 있는 것, 곧 리드게이트가 빚을 지고 있다는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 자기가 날마다 그 늪 속으로 점점 더 깊이 빠져들고 있다는 생각을 오래도록 머릿속에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 그 늪은 꽃과 푸른 풀로 어찌나 예쁘게 덮여 있는지 사람들을 그쪽으로 유혹하는 법이다. 사람이 그 속에서 턱밑까지 빠져드는 것은 놀랄 만큼 순식간의 일이다. 영혼 속에 우주의 구상을 품고 있던 사람이라 해도, 결국 어쩔 수 없이 빠져나올 궁리만을 주로 하게 되는 처지에 이르고 마는 것이다."]], [["Eighteen months ago Lydgate was poor, but had never known the eager want of small sums, and felt rather a burning contempt for any one who descended a step in order to gain them.", "열여덟 달 전만 해도 리드게이트는 가난했지만, 적은 액수의 돈을 애타게 아쉬워해 본 적이 없었고, 그런 돈을 얻으려 한 단계 몸을 낮추는 사람을 오히려 불같이 경멸했다."], ["He was now experiencing something worse than a simple deficit: he was assailed by the vulgar hateful trials of a man who has bought and used a great many things which might have been done without, and which he is unable to pay for, though the demand for payment has become pressing.", "그런데 이제 그는 단순한 부족보다 더 나쁜 무언가를 겪고 있었다. 없어도 되었을 물건을 잔뜩 사서 써 버리고는 값을 치를 수 없는데도 그 지불 요구가 절박해진 사람이 겪는, 천박하고 가증스러운 시련에 시달리고 있었던 것이다."]], [["How this came about may be easily seen without much arithmetic or knowledge of prices.", "이런 일이 어떻게 일어났는지는 많은 계산이나 물가에 관한 지식 없이도 쉽게 알 수 있다."], ["When a man in setting up a house and preparing for marriage finds that his furniture and other initial expenses come to between four and five hundred pounds more than he has capital to pay for; when at the end of a year it appears that his household expenses, horses and et caeteras, amount to nearly a thousand, while the proceeds of the practice reckoned from the old books to be worth eight hundred per annum have sunk like a summer pond and make hardly five hundred, chiefly in unpaid entries, the plain inference is that, whether he minds it or not, he is in debt.", "한 남자가 집을 마련하고 결혼을 준비하면서, 가구와 그 밖의 초기 비용이 자기가 지불할 자본보다 사오백 파운드나 더 든다는 것을 알게 되고, 한 해가 지난 끝에 가계 지출과 말과 그 밖의 잡다한 것들이 거의 천 파운드에 이르는데, 한편 옛 장부로 따져 연간 팔백 파운드의 가치가 있다던 개업의 수입은 여름 연못처럼 말라붙어 겨우 오백 파운드, 그것도 대부분 미수금으로 잡힌다면, 그가 그것을 마음에 두든 두지 않든 빚을 지고 있다는 것이 자명한 결론이다. 그때는 우리 시대보다 물가가 덜 비쌌고 지방 생활은 비교적 검소했다."], ["Those were less expensive times than our own, and provincial life was comparatively modest; but the ease with which a medical man who had lately bought a practice, who thought that he was obliged to keep two horses, whose table was supplied without stint, and who paid an insurance on his life and a high rent for house and garden, might find his expenses doubling his receipts, can be conceived by any one who does not think these details beneath his consideration.", "하지만 최근에 개업권을 사들였고, 말 두 필을 둬야 한다고 생각했으며, 아낌없이 차려진 식탁에, 생명 보험료를 내고 집과 정원에 비싼 집세를 내는 의사가 자기 지출이 수입의 두 배에 이르는 것을 얼마나 쉽게 발견할 수 있는지는, 이런 세부 사항을 자기가 헤아릴 일이 아니라고 여기지 않는 사람이라면 누구나 짐작할 수 있다. 어릴 적부터 사치스러운 가정에 익숙했던 로저먼드는, 살림을 잘하는 것이 그저 모든 것의 가장 좋은 것을 주문하는 데 있다고 생각했다."], ["Rosamond, accustomed from her childhood to an extravagant household, thought that good housekeeping consisted simply in ordering the best of everything—nothing else “answered;”", "그 밖의 다른 것은 \"성에 차지\" 않았던 것이다."], ["and Lydgate supposed that “if things were done at all, they must be done properly”—he did not see how they were to live otherwise.", "그리고 리드게이트는 \"어차피 할 바에는 제대로 해야 한다\"고 여겼다. 그는 그렇지 않고서 어떻게 살아갈 수 있는지 알지 못했다."], ["If each head of household expenditure had been mentioned to him beforehand, he would have probably observed that “it could hardly come to much,” and if any one had suggested a saving on a particular article—for example, the substitution of cheap fish for dear—it would have appeared to him simply a penny-wise, mean notion.", "만약 가계 지출의 각 항목을 미리 그에게 일러 주었더라면, 그는 아마 \"그래 봐야 얼마 안 될 텐데\"라고 했을 것이고, 누군가가 어떤 특정 항목에서 절약하라고—이를테면 비싼 생선 대신 싼 생선을 쓰라고—권했더라면, 그것은 그에게 그저 푼돈을 아끼려는 인색한 발상으로만 보였을 것이다."], ["Rosamond, even without such an occasion as Captain Lydgate’s visit, was fond of giving invitations, and Lydgate, though he often thought the guests tiresome, did not interfere.", "로저먼드는 리드게이트 대위의 방문 같은 계기가 없어도 손님을 초대하기를 좋아했고, 리드게이트는 그 손님들이 따분하다고 종종 생각하면서도 간섭하지 않았다."], ["This sociability seemed a necessary part of professional prudence, and the entertainment must be suitable.", "이런 사교성은 직업적 신중함의 필수적인 일부로 여겨졌고, 또 그 접대는 마땅히 격에 맞아야 했다."], ["It is true Lydgate was constantly visiting the homes of the poor and adjusting his prescriptions of diet to their small means; but, dear me!", "사실 리드게이트는 끊임없이 가난한 이들의 집을 찾아가 그들의 적은 형편에 맞게 식이 처방을 조정해 주었다. 하지만 저런!"], ["has it not by this time ceased to be remarkable—is it not rather that we expect in men, that they should have numerous strands of experience lying side by side and never compare them with each other?", "사람이란 나란히 놓인 수많은 경험의 가닥들을 지니고 있으면서도 그것들을 서로 견주어 보지 않는다는 사실은, 이제는 더 이상 놀랄 일이 아니지 않은가—오히려 우리가 사람들에게 으레 기대하는 바가 아닌가?"], ["Expenditure—like ugliness and errors—becomes a totally new thing when we attach our own personality to it, and measure it by that wide difference which is manifest (in our own sensations) between ourselves and others.", "지출이란—추함이나 잘못이 그렇듯이—우리가 거기에 우리 자신의 인격을 결부시키고, (우리 자신의 감각 안에서) 우리와 남들 사이에 뚜렷이 드러나는 그 커다란 차이로 그것을 가늠할 때, 전혀 새로운 것이 되어 버린다."], ["Lydgate believed himself to be careless about his dress, and he despised a man who calculated the effects of his costume; it seemed to him only a matter of course that he had abundance of fresh garments—such things were naturally ordered in sheaves.", "리드게이트는 자기가 옷차림에 무심하다고 믿었고, 옷의 효과를 계산하는 남자를 경멸했다. 자기에게 새 옷이 넉넉히 있는 것은 그에게 그저 당연한 일로만 여겨졌다."], ["It must be remembered that he had never hitherto felt the check of importunate debt, and he walked by habit, not by self-criticism.", "그런 것들은 자연히 한 묶음씩 주문되는 법이었으니까. 그가 지금껏 빚의 성가신 독촉에 제동이 걸려 본 적이 없으며, 자기비판이 아니라 습관에 따라 걸어왔다는 것을 기억해 둬야 한다."], ["But the check had come.", "그러나 이제 그 제동이 걸린 것이다."]], [["Its novelty made it the more irritating.", "그것이 새로웠던 까닭에 더욱 짜증스러웠다."], ["He was amazed, disgusted that conditions so foreign to all his purposes, so hatefully disconnected with the objects he cared to occupy himself with, should have lain in ambush and clutched him when he was unaware.", "그는 자기의 모든 목적과 그토록 동떨어진, 자기가 몰두하고 싶어 하던 대상들과 그토록 가증스럽게 무관한 처지가 매복해 있다가 방심한 사이에 자기를 덮쳤다는 사실에 놀랐고, 또 진저리가 났다."], ["And there was not only the actual debt; there was the certainty that in his present position he must go on deepening it.", "게다가 실제로 진 빚만 있는 것이 아니었다. 지금 처지에서는 그 빚을 점점 더 깊게 만들어 갈 수밖에 없다는 확실함도 있었다."], ["Two furnishing tradesmen at Brassing, whose bills had been incurred before his marriage, and whom uncalculated current expenses had ever since prevented him from paying, had repeatedly sent him unpleasant letters which had forced themselves on his attention.", "결혼 전에 빚진 청구서를 두고, 그 뒤로 줄곧 예상치 못한 일상 경비 때문에 갚지 못해 온 브래싱의 가구상 두 곳은, 그의 주의를 끌지 않을 수 없는 불쾌한 편지를 거듭 보내왔다. 강렬한 자존심을 지닌—호의를 청하거나 누군가에게 신세 지기를 싫어하는—리드게이트의 기질만큼 이런 일에 더 분통이 터지는 기질도 없었을 것이다."], ["This could hardly have been more galling to any disposition than to Lydgate’s, with his intense pride—his dislike of asking a favor or being under an obligation to any one. He had scorned even to form conjectures about Mr.", "그는 돈 문제에 관한 빈시 씨의 의향을 추측해 보는 것조차 경멸했고, 극단적인 상황이 아니고서는 결코 장인에게 부탁할 마음이 들지 않았을 것이다."], ["Vincy’s intentions on money matters, and nothing but extremity could have induced him to apply to his father-in-law, even if he had not been made aware in various indirect ways since his marriage that Mr.", "더구나 결혼 후로 그는 여러 간접적인 경로를 통해, 빈시 씨 자신의 형편도 번창하지 못하고 있으며 그에게서 도움을 기대하는 것이 못마땅하게 여겨지리라는 것을 이미 알게 되었던 터였다. 어떤 사람들은 친구들의 선뜻함을 쉽게 믿는다."], ["Vincy’s own affairs were not flourishing, and that the expectation of help from him would be resented.", "리드게이트는 자기 생애의 이전 시절에 그래야 할 필요가 있으리라고는 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["Some men easily trust in the readiness of friends; it had never in the former part of his life occurred to Lydgate that he should need to do so: he had never thought what borrowing would be to him; but now that the idea had entered his mind, he felt that he would rather incur any other hardship.", "돈을 빌리는 것이 자기에게 어떤 일일지 결코 생각해 보지 않았던 것이다. 하지만 이제 그 생각이 머릿속에 들어오자, 그는 다른 어떤 고생이라도 차라리 떠안는 편이 낫겠다고 느꼈다."], ["In the mean time he had no money or prospects of money; and his practice was not getting more lucrative.", "한편 그에게는 돈도, 돈이 들어올 가망도 없었고, 그의 개업은 더 수지맞게 되어 가지도 않았다."]], [["No wonder that Lydgate had been unable to suppress all signs of inward trouble during the last few months, and now that Rosamond was regaining brilliant health, he meditated taking her entirely into confidence on his difficulties.", "리드게이트가 지난 몇 달 동안 속내의 괴로움을 드러내는 온갖 기색을 다 억누를 수 없었던 것도 당연했다. 그리고 이제 로저먼드가 화사한 건강을 되찾고 있었으므로, 그는 자기 어려움을 그녀에게 전적으로 털어놓을까 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["New conversance with tradesmen’s bills had forced his reasoning into a new channel of comparison: he had begun to consider from a new point of view what was necessary and unnecessary in goods ordered, and to see that there must be some change of habits.", "상인들의 청구서를 새로이 접하게 되면서 그의 사고는 새로운 비교의 물길로 떠밀려 갔다. 그는 주문한 물건들 가운데 무엇이 필요하고 무엇이 불필요한지를 새로운 관점에서 따져 보기 시작했고, 생활 습관을 어느 정도 바꿔야만 한다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["How could such a change be made without Rosamond’s concurrence?", "그런 변화를 로저먼드의 동의 없이 어떻게 이룰 수 있겠는가?"], ["The immediate occasion of opening the disagreeable fact to her was forced upon him.", "그 불쾌한 사실을 그녀에게 털어놓을 직접적인 계기가 그에게 닥쳐왔다."]], [["Having no money, and having privately sought advice as to what security could possibly be given by a man in his position, Lydgate had offered the one good security in his power to the less peremptory creditor, who was a silversmith and jeweller, and who consented to take on himself the upholsterer’s credit also, accepting interest for a given term.", "돈이 없었던 데다, 자기 처지의 사람이 도대체 무슨 담보를 제공할 수 있을지 은밀히 조언을 구한 끝에, 리드게이트는 자기 능력 안에서 댈 수 있는 단 하나의 확실한 담보를, 덜 강압적인 채권자에게 제시했다. 그 채권자는 은세공인이자 보석상이었는데, 가구상의 외상까지 자기가 떠맡되 일정 기간의 이자를 받기로 하는 데 동의했다."], ["The security necessary was a bill of sale on the furniture of his house, which might make a creditor easy for a reasonable time about a debt amounting to less than four hundred pounds; and the silversmith, Mr.", "필요한 담보는 그의 집 가구에 대한 매도 증서였고, 이는 사백 파운드에 못 미치는 빚에 대해 적당한 기간 동안 채권자를 안심시킬 만한 것이었다."], ["Dover, was willing to reduce it by taking back a portion of the plate and any other article which was as good as new. “Any other article”", "그리고 그 은세공인 도버 씨는 은제 식기 일부와 새것이나 다름없는 다른 물건들을 되받음으로써 빚을 줄여 줄 용의가 있었다."], ["was a phrase delicately implying jewellery, and more particularly some purple amethysts costing thirty pounds, which Lydgate had bought as a bridal present.", "\"다른 물건들\"이란 보석류를, 더 구체적으로는 리드게이트가 신부 선물로 사 두었던 서른 파운드짜리 자수정 몇 개를 은근히 가리키는 표현이었다."]], [["Opinions may be divided as to his wisdom in making this present: some may think that it was a graceful attention to be expected from a man like Lydgate, and that the fault of any troublesome consequences lay in the pinched narrowness of provincial life at that time, which offered no conveniences for professional people whose fortune was not proportioned to their tastes; also, in Lydgate’s ridiculous fastidiousness about asking his friends for money.", "그가 이 선물을 한 것이 현명했는지를 두고는 의견이 갈릴 수 있다. 어떤 이들은 그것이 리드게이트 같은 남자에게 기대할 만한 우아한 배려였으며, 골치 아픈 결과가 빚어진 잘못은 당시 지방 생활의 옹색한 궁색함에 있다고 생각할지 모른다. 그 생활은 자기 재산이 취향에 걸맞지 않은 전문직 사람들에게 아무런 편의도 제공하지 못했으니 말이다. 또 친구들에게 돈을 청하기를 꺼린 리드게이트의 우스꽝스러운 까다로움에 잘못이 있다고 여길 수도 있다."]], [["However, it had seemed a question of no moment to him on that fine morning when he went to give a final order for plate: in the presence of other jewels enormously expensive, and as an addition to orders of which the amount had not been exactly calculated, thirty pounds for ornaments so exquisitely suited to Rosamond’s neck and arms could hardly appear excessive when there was no ready cash for it to exceed.", "그러나 그가 식기를 마지막으로 주문하러 가던 그 화창한 아침에는, 그것이 그에게 조금도 대수롭지 않은 문제로 여겨졌다. 엄청나게 값비싼 다른 보석들 앞에서, 그것도 정확히 액수를 계산해 두지 않은 주문에 덧붙이는 것이었으니, 로저먼드의 목과 팔에 그토록 절묘하게 어울리는 장신구에 서른 파운드를 쓴다는 것이, 그것을 치를 현금이 애초에 없는 마당에 과하게 보일 리 없었던 것이다."], ["But at this crisis Lydgate’s imagination could not help dwelling on the possibility of letting the amethysts take their place again among Mr.", "하지만 이 위기에 이르러 리드게이트의 상상은, 그 자수정을 다시 도버 씨의 재고로 되돌릴 수도 있다는 가능성에 머무르지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Dover’s stock, though he shrank from the idea of proposing this to Rosamond.", "비록 그것을 로저먼드에게 제안한다는 생각만으로도 그는 움츠러들었지만 말이다."], ["Having been roused to discern consequences which he had never been in the habit of tracing, he was preparing to act on this discernment with some of the rigor (by no means all) that he would have applied in pursuing experiment.", "지금껏 한 번도 더듬어 본 적 없는 결과들을 분별하도록 일깨워진 그는, 실험을 수행할 때라면 적용했을 그 엄격함의 일부(전부는 결코 아니었다)를 가지고 이 분별에 따라 행동하려 준비하고 있었다."], ["He was nerving himself to this rigor as he rode from Brassing, and meditated on the representations he must make to Rosamond.", "그는 브래싱에서 말을 타고 돌아오며 그 엄격함을 향해 마음을 다잡았고, 로저먼드에게 해야 할 이야기를 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["It was evening when he got home.", "그가 집에 도착했을 때는 저녁이었다."], ["He was intensely miserable, this strong man of nine-and-twenty and of many gifts.", "스물아홉 살에 많은 재능을 지닌 이 건장한 사내는 몹시 비참했다."], ["He was not saying angrily within himself that he had made a profound mistake; but the mistake was at work in him like a recognized chronic disease, mingling its uneasy importunities with every prospect, and enfeebling every thought.", "그는 자기가 심각한 실수를 저질렀다고 속으로 성내며 말하고 있지는 않았다. 하지만 그 실수는 인정된 만성 질환처럼 그의 안에서 작동하며, 그 불안한 독촉을 온갖 전망에 뒤섞고 모든 생각을 약하게 만들었다."], ["As he went along the passage to the drawing-room, he heard the piano and singing.", "응접실로 가는 복도를 지나는데 피아노 소리와 노랫소리가 들렸다."], ["Of course, Ladislaw was there.", "물론 래디슬로가 와 있었다."], ["It was some weeks since Will had parted from Dorothea, yet he was still at the old post in Middlemarch.", "윌이 도러시아와 헤어진 지 몇 주가 지났는데도, 그는 여전히 미들마치의 옛 자리를 지키고 있었다."], ["Lydgate had no objection in general to Ladislaw’s coming, but just now he was annoyed that he could not find his hearth free.", "리드게이트는 대체로 래디슬로가 오는 것에 반대하지 않았지만, 바로 지금은 자기 집 난롯가를 비워 둔 채로 찾을 수 없다는 것이 못마땅했다."], ["When he opened the door the two singers went on towards the key-note, raising their eyes and looking at him indeed, but not regarding his entrance as an interruption.", "그가 문을 열자 두 가수는 으뜸음을 향해 노래를 이어 가며, 눈을 들어 그를 바라보기는 했으나 그의 등장을 방해로 여기지는 않았다."], ["To a man galled with his harness as poor Lydgate was, it is not soothing to see two people warbling at him, as he comes in with the sense that the painful day has still pains in store.", "가엾은 리드게이트처럼 멍에에 쓸린 사내에게는, 고통스러운 하루가 아직 더 많은 고통을 예비하고 있다는 느낌으로 들어서는 마당에 두 사람이 자기를 향해 지저귀는 모습을 보는 것이 결코 마음을 달래 주는 일이 아니었다."], ["His face, already paler than usual, took on a scowl as he walked across the room and flung himself into a chair.", "가뜩이나 평소보다 창백하던 그의 얼굴은 방을 가로질러 의자에 털썩 몸을 던지면서 잔뜩 찌푸려졌다."]], [["The singers feeling themselves excused by the fact that they had only three bars to sing, now turned round.", "부를 소절이 세 마디밖에 남지 않았으니 양해되리라 여긴 두 가수는 이제 돌아섰다."]], [["“How are you, Lydgate?”", "\"잘 지냈나, 리드게이트?\""], ["said Will, coming forward to shake hands.", "윌이 악수하러 다가오며 말했다."]], [["Lydgate took his hand, but did not think it necessary to speak.", "리드게이트는 그의 손을 잡았지만 굳이 말을 할 필요는 느끼지 않았다."]], [["“Have you dined, Tertius?", "\"식사는 하셨어요, 터셔스?"], ["I expected you much earlier,”", "훨씬 일찍 오실 줄 알았는데.\""], ["said Rosamond, who had already seen that her husband was in a “horrible humor.”", "로저먼드가 말했는데, 그녀는 이미 남편이 \"끔찍한 기분\"임을 알아챈 터였다."], ["She seated herself in her usual place as she spoke.", "그녀는 말하면서 늘 앉던 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“I have dined.", "\"식사는 했소."], ["I should like some tea, please,”", "차나 좀 마셨으면 좋겠군.\""], ["said Lydgate, curtly, still scowling and looking markedly at his legs stretched out before him.", "리드게이트가 무뚝뚝하게 말했는데, 여전히 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 앞으로 뻗은 자기 다리를 빤히 내려다보고 있었다."]], [["Will was too quick to need more.", "윌은 더 말할 필요도 없을 만큼 눈치가 빨랐다."], ["“I shall be off,”", "\"전 가 보겠습니다.\""], ["he said, reaching his hat.", "그가 모자를 집으며 말했다."]], [["“Tea is coming,”", "\"차가 오고 있어요.\""], ["said Rosamond; “pray don’t go.”", "로저먼드가 말했다. \"제발 가지 마세요.\""]], [["“Yes, Lydgate is bored,”", "\"아뇨, 리드게이트가 따분해하고 있어요.\""], ["said Will, who had more comprehension of Lydgate than Rosamond had, and was not offended by his manner, easily imagining outdoor causes of annoyance.", "윌이 말했다. 그는 로저먼드보다 리드게이트를 더 잘 이해했기에, 바깥일에서 비롯된 짜증의 원인을 쉽게 상상하며 그의 태도에 기분 상해 하지 않았다."]], [["“There is the more need for you to stay,”", "\"그러니 더더욱 당신이 계셔야죠.\""], ["said Rosamond, playfully, and in her lightest accent; “he will not speak to me all the evening.”", "로저먼드가 장난스럽게, 더없이 가벼운 어조로 말했다. \"이이는 저녁 내내 저한테 한마디도 안 할 테니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, Rosamond, I shall,”", "\"아니, 로저먼드, 할 거요.\""], ["said Lydgate, in his strong baritone.", "리드게이트가 그 힘찬 바리톤으로 말했다."], ["“I have some serious business to speak to you about.”", "\"당신과 의논할 진지한 일이 좀 있소.\""]], [["No introduction of the business could have been less like that which Lydgate had intended; but her indifferent manner had been too provoking.", "리드게이트가 의도했던 것과 이보다 덜 닮은 화제의 도입은 없었을 것이다. 하지만 그녀의 무심한 태도가 너무도 화를 돋우었던 것이다."]], [["“There! you see,”", "\"거 보세요!\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“I’m going to the meeting about the Mechanics’ Institute.", "\"전 직공 학교 회의에 갑니다. 안녕히 계세요.\""], ["Good-by;” and he went quickly out of the room.", "그러고는 재빨리 방을 나갔다."]], [["Rosamond did not look at her husband, but presently rose and took her place before the tea-tray.", "로저먼드는 남편을 바라보지 않았으나, 이윽고 일어나 찻쟁반 앞 자기 자리에 앉았다."], ["She was thinking that she had never seen him so disagreeable.", "그녀는 남편이 이토록 무뚝뚝한 모습은 처음 본다고 생각하고 있었다."], ["Lydgate turned his dark eyes on her and watched her as she delicately handled the tea-service with her taper fingers, and looked at the objects immediately before her with no curve in her face disturbed, and yet with an ineffable protest in her air against all people with unpleasant manners.", "리드게이트는 검은 눈을 그녀에게 돌려, 그녀가 가느다란 손가락으로 차 도구를 섬세하게 다루는 모습을, 얼굴의 곡선 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않고 바로 앞의 물건들을 바라보면서도 불쾌한 태도를 지닌 모든 사람을 향한 형언할 수 없는 항의를 그 분위기에 담고 있는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["For the moment he lost the sense of his wound in a sudden speculation about this new form of feminine impassibility revealing itself in the sylph-like frame which he had once interpreted as the sign of a ready intelligent sensitiveness.", "한순간 그는, 한때 민첩하고 영민한 감수성의 표시로 해석했던 그 요정 같은 몸매에서 새로이 드러나는 이 여성적 무감각의 형태에 대한 불현듯한 사색에 잠겨 자기 상처의 느낌을 잊었다."], ["His mind glancing back to Laure while he looked at Rosamond, he said inwardly, “Would she kill me because I wearied her?”", "로저먼드를 바라보는 동안 그의 마음은 로르에게로 흘끗 되돌아갔고, 그는 속으로 말했다. \"그녀라면 내가 자기를 싫증 나게 했다고 나를 죽이려 들었을까?\""], ["and then, “It is the way with all women.”", "그리고 이어서, \"여자들은 다 그런 식이지.\""], ["But this power of generalizing which gives men so much the superiority in mistake over the dumb animals, was immediately thwarted by Lydgate’s memory of wondering impressions from the behavior of another woman—from Dorothea’s looks and tones of emotion about her husband when Lydgate began to attend him—from her passionate cry to be taught what would best comfort that man for whose sake it seemed as if she must quell every impulse in her except the yearnings of faithfulness and compassion.", "그러나 인간에게 실수에 있어서만큼은 말 못 하는 짐승보다 그토록 우월한 지위를 안겨 주는 이 일반화의 능력은, 또 다른 여인의 행동에서 받은 경이로운 인상에 대한 리드게이트의 기억에 곧바로 가로막혔다. 그것은 리드게이트가 남편을 진료하기 시작했을 때 도러시아가 남편을 두고 보인 표정과 감정 어린 어조에서, 그리고 충실함과 연민의 갈망 외에 자기 안의 모든 충동을 억눌러야만 할 것 같던 그 남자를 가장 잘 위로할 방도를 가르쳐 달라던 그녀의 열정적인 외침에서 받은 인상이었다."], ["These revived impressions succeeded each other quickly and dreamily in Lydgate’s mind while the tea was being brewed.", "차가 우러나는 동안 이 되살아난 인상들이 리드게이트의 마음속에서 빠르게, 또 꿈결처럼 잇따라 떠올랐다. 그는 도러시아가 \"조언해 주세요—제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 생각해 주세요—그이는 평생을 애쓰며 앞날을 바라보아 왔어요."], ["He had shut his eyes in the last instant of reverie while he heard Dorothea saying, “Advise me—think what I can do—he has been all his life laboring and looking forward. He minds about nothing else—and I mind about nothing else.”", "그이는 다른 무엇에도 마음을 쓰지 않아요—저도 다른 무엇에도 마음을 쓰지 않고요\"라고 말하는 소리를 들으며, 그 공상의 마지막 순간에 눈을 감고 있었다."]], [["That voice of deep-souled womanhood had remained within him as the enkindling conceptions of dead and sceptred genius had remained within him (is there not a genius for feeling nobly which also reigns over human spirits and their conclusions?); the tones were a music from which he was falling away—he had really fallen into a momentary doze, when Rosamond said in her silvery neutral way, “Here is your tea, Tertius,”", "깊은 영혼을 지닌 여성성의 그 목소리는, 죽어 왕홀을 든 천재들의 불을 지피는 착상들이 그의 안에 남아 있었듯 그의 안에 남아 있었다(인간의 정신과 그 결론을 다스리는, 고귀하게 느끼는 천재성 또한 있지 않은가?). 그 음색은 그가 멀어져 가고 있던 음악이었다."], ["setting it on the small table by his side, and then moved back to her place without looking at him.", "로저먼드가 그 은빛의 무심한 어조로 \"차 여기 있어요, 터셔스\"라고 말하며 그 곁의 작은 탁자에 찻잔을 내려놓고 그를 보지도 않은 채 제자리로 물러났을 때, 그는 실은 잠깐 졸음에 빠져 있었다."], ["Lydgate was too hasty in attributing insensibility to her; after her own fashion, she was sensitive enough, and took lasting impressions.", "리드게이트는 그녀에게 무감각함을 돌리는 데 너무 성급했다. 그녀는 그녀 나름대로 충분히 예민했고 오래가는 인상을 받았다."], ["Her impression now was one of offence and repulsion.", "지금 그녀가 받은 인상은 모욕감과 반감의 인상이었다."], ["But then, Rosamond had no scowls and had never raised her voice: she was quite sure that no one could justly find fault with her.", "그러나 로저먼드는 얼굴을 찌푸리지도 않았고 목소리를 높인 적도 없었다. 그녀는 누구도 자기에게서 정당하게 흠잡을 수 없으리라고 꽤 확신하고 있었다."]], [["Perhaps Lydgate and she had never felt so far off each other before; but there were strong reasons for not deferring his revelation, even if he had not already begun it by that abrupt announcement; indeed some of the angry desire to rouse her into more sensibility on his account which had prompted him to speak prematurely, still mingled with his pain in the prospect of her pain.", "어쩌면 리드게이트와 그녀가 서로를 이토록 멀게 느낀 적은 전에 없었을 것이다. 하지만 그가 비록 그 불쑥 던진 말로 이미 시작하지 않았더라도, 자기 고백을 미룰 수 없는 강력한 이유가 있었다."], ["But he waited till the tray was gone, the candles were lit, and the evening quiet might be counted on: the interval had left time for repelled tenderness to return into the old course.", "실제로 자기를 위해 그녀를 좀 더 민감하게 일깨우고 싶은 성난 욕망—그 욕망이 그로 하여금 성급히 입을 열게 했던 것인데—이 일부, 그녀가 겪을 고통을 내다보는 그 자신의 고통에 여전히 뒤섞여 있었다. 하지만 그는 쟁반이 치워지고 촛불이 밝혀지며 저녁의 고요가 기대할 만해질 때까지 기다렸다."], ["He spoke kindly.", "그 간격은 밀려났던 다정함이 다시 옛 물길로 돌아올 시간을 남겨 주었다. 그는 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“Dear Rosy, lay down your work and come to sit by me,”", "\"여보 로지, 일감은 내려놓고 이리 와서 내 옆에 앉아요.\""], ["he said, gently, pushing away the table, and stretching out his arm to draw a chair near his own.", "그가 탁자를 밀어내고 팔을 뻗어 의자를 자기 옆으로 끌어당기며 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["Rosamond obeyed.", "로저먼드는 그 말에 따랐다."], ["As she came towards him in her drapery of transparent faintly tinted muslin, her slim yet round figure never looked more graceful; as she sat down by him and laid one hand on the elbow of his chair, at last looking at him and meeting his eyes, her delicate neck and cheek and purely cut lips never had more of that untarnished beauty which touches as in spring-time and infancy and all sweet freshness.", "투명하고 옅게 물든 모슬린 옷을 두른 채 그에게 다가오는 그녀의 날씬하면서도 둥근 몸매는 그 어느 때보다 우아해 보였다. 그의 옆에 앉아 한 손을 그의 의자 팔걸이에 얹고는 마침내 그를 바라보며 그의 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그녀의 섬세한 목과 뺨과 또렷이 빚어진 입술은, 봄철과 유년기와 온갖 달콤한 싱그러움에서처럼 마음을 울리는 그 티 없는 아름다움을 그 어느 때보다 가득 머금고 있었다."], ["It touched Lydgate now, and mingled the early moments of his love for her with all the other memories which were stirred in this crisis of deep trouble.", "그것은 지금 리드게이트의 마음을 울렸고, 그녀를 향한 사랑의 초기 순간들을 이 깊은 곤경의 위기에 들끓는 다른 모든 기억과 뒤섞었다."], ["He laid his ample hand softly on hers, saying—", "그는 자기의 큼직한 손을 그녀의 손 위에 부드럽게 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“Dear!”", "\"여보!\""], ["with the lingering utterance which affection gives to the word.", "애정이 그 말에 실어 주는, 여운을 끄는 어조였다."], ["Rosamond too was still under the power of that same past, and her husband was still in part the Lydgate whose approval had stirred delight.", "로저먼드 역시 여전히 그 똑같은 과거의 힘 아래 있었고, 그녀의 남편은 여전히 일부, 그의 칭찬이 기쁨을 일으키곤 했던 그 리드게이트였다."], ["She put his hair lightly away from his forehead, then laid her other hand on his, and was conscious of forgiving him.", "그녀는 그의 이마에서 머리카락을 가볍게 쓸어 넘기고는 다른 한 손을 그의 손 위에 얹었고, 그를 용서하고 있음을 의식했다."]], [["“I am obliged to tell you what will hurt you, Rosy.", "\"당신을 아프게 할 이야기를 해야겠소, 로지."], ["But there are things which husband and wife must think of together.", "하지만 부부가 함께 생각해야만 하는 일들이 있는 법이오."], ["I dare say it has occurred to you already that I am short of money.”", "내가 돈이 궁하다는 걸 당신도 이미 알아챘으리라고 보오만.\""]], [["Lydgate paused; but Rosamond turned her neck and looked at a vase on the mantel-piece.", "리드게이트는 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 로저먼드는 목을 돌려 벽난로 위 선반의 꽃병을 바라보았다."]], [["“I was not able to pay for all the things we had to get before we were married, and there have been expenses since which I have been obliged to meet.", "\"우리가 결혼하기 전에 마련해야 했던 모든 물건의 값을 다 치를 수가 없었고, 그 뒤로도 부득이 떠안아야 했던 지출이 있었소. 그 결과 브래싱에 큰 빚이 생겼는데—삼백팔십 파운드라오—한동안 나를 짓눌러 왔지."], ["The consequence is, there is a large debt at Brassing—three hundred and eighty pounds—which has been pressing on me a good while, and in fact we are getting deeper every day, for people don’t pay me the faster because others want the money.", "게다가 사실 우리는 날마다 더 깊이 빠져들고 있소. 다른 이들이 돈을 필요로 한다고 해서 사람들이 내게 더 빨리 돈을 갚는 건 아니니까."], ["I took pains to keep it from you while you were not well; but now we must think together about it, and you must help me.”", "당신이 몸이 좋지 않은 동안에는 이걸 당신에게 숨기려 애썼소. 하지만 이제 우리는 함께 이 일을 생각해야 하고, 당신이 나를 도와줘야 하오.\""]], [["“What can I do, Tertius?”", "\"제가 뭘 할 수 있겠어요, 터셔스?\""], ["said Rosamond, turning her eyes on him again.", "로저먼드가 다시 그에게 눈을 돌리며 말했다."], ["That little speech of four words, like so many others in all languages, is capable by varied vocal inflections of expressing all states of mind from helpless dimness to exhaustive argumentative perception, from the completest self-devoting fellowship to the most neutral aloofness.", "그 네 마디의 짧은 말은, 모든 언어의 수많은 다른 말들과 마찬가지로, 다양한 음성의 굴절을 통해 속수무책의 막막함에서부터 빈틈없이 따지는 논리적 통찰에 이르기까지, 자기를 온전히 바치는 동지애에서부터 더없이 무심한 초연함에 이르기까지, 모든 마음 상태를 표현할 수 있는 말이다."], ["Rosamond’s thin utterance threw into the words “What can—I—do!” as much neutrality as they could hold.", "로저먼드의 가느다란 발화는 \"제가—뭘—할 수 있겠어요!\"라는 말에, 그 말이 담을 수 있는 한껏의 무심함을 실었다."], ["They fell like a mortal chill on Lydgate’s roused tenderness.", "그것은 들끓던 리드게이트의 다정함 위로 치명적인 한기처럼 떨어졌다."], ["He did not storm in indignation—he felt too sad a sinking of the heart.", "그는 분개하여 노발대발하지 않았다. 너무도 서글픈 마음의 가라앉음을 느꼈던 것이다."], ["And when he spoke again it was more in the tone of a man who forces himself to fulfil a task.", "그리고 그가 다시 입을 열었을 때는, 억지로 임무를 다하려는 사람의 어조에 더 가까웠다."]], [["“It is necessary for you to know, because I have to give security for a time, and a man must come to make an inventory of the furniture.”", "\"당신이 알아야만 하는 까닭은, 내가 한동안 담보를 제공해야 하고, 누군가 와서 가구 목록을 작성해야 하기 때문이오.\""]], [["Rosamond colored deeply.", "로저먼드의 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."], ["“Have you not asked papa for money?”", "\"아버지께 돈을 부탁하지 않으셨어요?\""], ["she said, as soon as she could speak.", "그녀가 입을 떼자마자 말했다. \"안 했소.\""]], [["“No.” “Then I must ask him!”", "\"그럼 제가 부탁해야겠네요!\""], ["she said, releasing her hands from Lydgate’s, and rising to stand at two yards’ distance from him.", "그녀가 자기 손을 리드게이트의 손에서 빼내며 말했고, 일어나서 그에게서 두 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에 섰다."]], [["“No, Rosy,”", "\"안 돼, 로지.\""], ["said Lydgate, decisively.", "리드게이트가 단호하게 말했다."], ["“It is too late to do that.", "\"그러기엔 너무 늦었소."], ["The inventory will be begun to-morrow.", "목록 작성은 내일 시작될 거요."], ["Remember it is a mere security: it will make no difference: it is a temporary affair.", "그저 담보라는 걸 기억하구려. 그건 아무것도 달라지게 하지 않소."], ["I insist upon it that your father shall not know, unless I choose to tell him,”", "일시적인 일이오. 내가 직접 말하기로 정하지 않는 한, 당신 아버지가 이 일을 알아서는 안 된다고 나는 고집하겠소.\""], ["added Lydgate, with a more peremptory emphasis.", "리드게이트가 한층 단호한 강조로 덧붙였다."]], [["This certainly was unkind, but Rosamond had thrown him back on evil expectation as to what she would do in the way of quiet steady disobedience.", "이것은 분명 야박한 말이었다. 하지만 로저먼드는 그녀가 조용하고 한결같은 불복종으로 무슨 짓을 할지에 관한 좋지 않은 예상으로 그를 도로 내몰았던 것이다."], ["The unkindness seemed unpardonable to her: she was not given to weeping and disliked it, but now her chin and lips began to tremble and the tears welled up.", "그녀에게는 그 야박함이 용서할 수 없는 것으로 여겨졌다. 그녀는 우는 버릇이 없었고 우는 것을 싫어했지만, 이제 그녀의 턱과 입술이 떨리기 시작하더니 눈물이 솟아올랐다."], ["Perhaps it was not possible for Lydgate, under the double stress of outward material difficulty and of his own proud resistance to humiliating consequences, to imagine fully what this sudden trial was to a young creature who had known nothing but indulgence, and whose dreams had all been of new indulgence, more exactly to her taste.", "어쩌면 리드게이트로서는, 외적인 물질적 어려움과 굴욕적인 결과에 대한 자기 자신의 자존심 어린 저항이라는 이중의 압박 아래서, 이 갑작스러운 시련이 응석 외에는 아무것도 몰랐고 그 꿈이라곤 자기 취향에 한층 꼭 맞는 새로운 응석뿐이었던 한 어린 사람에게 어떤 것일지를 온전히 상상하기란 불가능했을 것이다. 하지만 그는 그녀를 되도록 아껴 주고 싶었고, 그녀의 눈물은 그의 가슴을 후벼 팠다."], ["But he did wish to spare her as much as he could, and her tears cut him to the heart.", "그는 곧바로 다시 입을 열 수 없었다. 그러나 로저먼드는 계속 흐느끼지 않았다."], ["He could not speak again immediately; but Rosamond did not go on sobbing: she tried to conquer her agitation and wiped away her tears, continuing to look before her at the mantel-piece.", "그녀는 자기 동요를 가라앉히려 애쓰며 눈물을 닦았고, 계속해서 앞에 있는 벽난로 선반을 바라보았다."]], [["“Try not to grieve, darling,”", "\"슬퍼하지 마요, 여보.\""], ["said Lydgate, turning his eyes up towards her.", "리드게이트가 눈을 들어 그녀를 올려다보며 말했다."], ["That she had chosen to move away from him in this moment of her trouble made everything harder to say, but he must absolutely go on.", "그녀가 이 곤경의 순간에 자기에게서 떨어져 가기를 택했다는 사실이 모든 말을 더 하기 어렵게 만들었지만, 그는 기어이 말을 이어 가야 했다."], ["“We must brace ourselves to do what is necessary.", "\"우리는 필요한 일을 할 수 있도록 마음을 다잡아야 하오."], ["It is I who have been in fault: I ought to have seen that I could not afford to live in this way.", "잘못한 사람은 나요. 이런 식으로 살 형편이 못 된다는 걸 진작 봤어야 했소."], ["But many things have told against me in my practice, and it really just now has ebbed to a low point.", "하지만 내 개업에는 여러 가지가 불리하게 작용했고, 정말이지 지금은 그게 바닥까지 내려앉았소."], ["I may recover it, but in the mean time we must pull up—we must change our way of living.", "다시 회복할 수도 있겠지만, 그동안 우리는 졸라매야 하오—생활 방식을 바꿔야 하오."], ["We shall weather it.", "우리는 이 일을 헤쳐 나갈 거요."], ["When I have given this security I shall have time to look about me; and you are so clever that if you turn your mind to managing you will school me into carefulness.", "이 담보를 제공하고 나면 주위를 둘러볼 시간이 생길 거고, 당신은 워낙 영리하니 마음먹고 살림을 꾸려 나간다면 나를 조심성 있게 길들여 줄 거요."], ["I have been a thoughtless rascal about squaring prices—but come, dear, sit down and forgive me.”", "나는 값을 맞추는 데 생각 없는 못난이였소—하지만 자, 여보, 앉아서 나를 용서해 줘요.\""]], [["Lydgate was bowing his neck under the yoke like a creature who had talons, but who had Reason too, which often reduces us to meekness.", "리드게이트는 발톱을 지녔으면서도 이성 또한 지닌 짐승처럼 멍에 아래 목을 숙이고 있었으니, 그 이성이란 흔히 우리를 온순함으로 굴복시키는 법이다."], ["When he had spoken the last words in an imploring tone, Rosamond returned to the chair by his side.", "그가 애원하는 어조로 마지막 말을 했을 때, 로저먼드는 그의 곁 의자로 돌아왔다."], ["His self-blame gave her some hope that he would attend to her opinion, and she said—", "그의 자책은 그가 자기 의견에 귀 기울이리라는 약간의 희망을 그녀에게 안겨 주었고, 그녀는 말했다."]], [["“Why can you not put off having the inventory made?", "\"목록 작성하는 걸 미룰 수는 없나요?"], ["You can send the men away to-morrow when they come.”", "내일 사람들이 오면 돌려보낼 수 있잖아요.\""]], [["“I shall not send them away,”", "\"돌려보내지 않을 거요.\""], ["said Lydgate, the peremptoriness rising again.", "리드게이트가 단호함을 다시 드러내며 말했다."], ["Was it of any use to explain?", "설명한들 무슨 소용이 있을까?"]], [["“If we left Middlemarch?", "\"우리가 미들마치를 떠난다면요?"], ["there would of course be a sale, and that would do as well.”", "그러면 당연히 경매가 있을 테고, 그것도 마찬가지로 효과가 있겠죠.\""]], [["“But we are not going to leave Middlemarch.”", "\"하지만 우린 미들마치를 떠나지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“I am sure, Tertius, it would be much better to do so.", "\"분명히 말하지만, 터셔스, 그러는 편이 훨씬 나을 거예요."], ["Why can we not go to London?", "우리가 런던으로 가면 안 되나요?"], ["Or near Durham, where your family is known?”", "아니면 당신 가족이 알려진 더럼 근처라든가요?\""]], [["“We can go nowhere without money, Rosamond.”", "\"돈 없이는 우린 아무 데도 갈 수 없소, 로저먼드.\""]], [["“Your friends would not wish you to be without money.", "\"당신 친구들은 당신이 돈 없이 지내기를 바라지 않을 거예요."], ["And surely these odious tradesmen might be made to understand that, and to wait, if you would make proper representations to them.”", "그리고 이 가증스러운 상인들도, 당신이 제대로 사정을 설명한다면 분명 그걸 이해하고 기다려 줄 수 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["“This is idle Rosamond,”", "\"그건 부질없는 소리요, 로저먼드.\""], ["said Lydgate, angrily.", "리드게이트가 성내며 말했다."], ["“You must learn to take my judgment on questions you don’t understand.", "\"당신은 당신이 이해하지 못하는 문제에서 내 판단을 받아들이는 법을 배워야 하오."], ["I have made necessary arrangements, and they must be carried out.", "나는 필요한 조처를 해 두었고, 그것은 실행되어야만 하오."], ["As to friends, I have no expectations whatever from them, and shall not ask them for anything.”", "친구들로 말하자면, 나는 그들에게 아무런 기대도 없으며, 그들에게 무엇도 청하지 않을 거요.\""]], [["Rosamond sat perfectly still.", "로저먼드는 미동도 없이 앉아 있었다."], ["The thought in her mind was that if she had known how Lydgate would behave, she would never have married him.", "그녀 마음속의 생각은, 리드게이트가 이렇게 행동할 줄 알았더라면 결코 그와 결혼하지 않았으리라는 것이었다."]], [["“We have no time to waste now on unnecessary words, dear,”", "\"이제 우리는 불필요한 말에 시간을 허비할 여유가 없소, 여보.\""], ["said Lydgate, trying to be gentle again.", "리드게이트가 다시 다정해지려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“There are some details that I want to consider with you.", "\"당신과 함께 고려하고 싶은 몇 가지 세부 사항이 있소."], ["Dover says he will take a good deal of the plate back again, and any of the jewellery we like.", "도버는 은제 식기를 상당 부분 되받겠다고 하고, 보석류도 우리가 원하는 건 무엇이든 받겠다고 하오."], ["He really behaves very well.”", "정말 무척 합당하게 처신하는 거지.\""]], [["“Are we to go without spoons and forks then?”", "\"그럼 우리는 숟가락이랑 포크도 없이 지내야 하는 건가요?\""], ["said Rosamond, whose very lips seemed to get thinner with the thinness of her utterance.", "로저먼드가 말했는데, 그 가느다란 발화만큼이나 입술 자체도 얇아지는 것 같았다."], ["She was determined to make no further resistance or suggestions.", "그녀는 더 이상 저항하거나 제안하지 않기로 작정한 터였다."]], [["“Oh no, dear!” said Lydgate.", "\"오, 아니오, 여보!\""], ["“But look here,”", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["he continued, drawing a paper from his pocket and opening it; “here is Dover’s account.", "\"하지만 여길 봐요.\" 그가 주머니에서 종이를 꺼내 펼치며 말을 이었다."], ["See, I have marked a number of articles, which if we returned them would reduce the amount by thirty pounds and more.", "\"여기 도버의 명세서요. 보다시피 내가 몇몇 품목에 표시를 해 두었는데, 이것들을 돌려주면 액수가 서른 파운드 넘게 줄어들 거요."], ["I have not marked any of the jewellery.”", "보석류에는 아무것도 표시하지 않았소.\""], ["Lydgate had really felt this point of the jewellery very bitter to himself; but he had overcome the feeling by severe argument.", "리드게이트는 사실 이 보석 문제가 자기에게 무척 쓰라리게 느껴졌지만, 엄정한 논리로 그 감정을 이겨 낸 터였다."], ["He could not propose to Rosamond that she should return any particular present of his, but he had told himself that he was bound to put Dover’s offer before her, and her inward prompting might make the affair easy.", "그는 로저먼드에게 자기가 준 특정 선물을 돌려 달라고 제안할 수는 없었다. 하지만 그는 도버의 제안을 그녀 앞에 내놓는 것이 자기의 도리라고 스스로에게 일렀고, 그녀의 내적인 움직임이 그 일을 수월하게 해 줄지도 모른다고 여겼다."]], [["“It is useless for me to look, Tertius,”", "\"제가 봐야 소용없어요, 터셔스.\""], ["said Rosamond, calmly; “you will return what you please.”", "로저먼드가 차분하게 말했다. \"당신이 원하는 걸 돌려주시면 되죠.\""], ["She would not turn her eyes on the paper, and Lydgate, flushing up to the roots of his hair, drew it back and let it fall on his knee.", "그녀는 그 종이로 눈을 돌리려 하지 않았고, 리드게이트는 머리카락 뿌리까지 붉어진 채 그것을 도로 거두어 무릎 위에 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Meanwhile Rosamond quietly went out of the room, leaving Lydgate helpless and wondering.", "그러는 사이에 로저먼드는 리드게이트를 무력하게, 어리둥절하게 내버려 둔 채 조용히 방을 나갔다."], ["Was she not coming back?", "그녀는 돌아오지 않으려는 걸까?"], ["It seemed that she had no more identified herself with him than if they had been creatures of different species and opposing interests.", "마치 그들이 서로 다른 종(種)에다 이해관계가 어긋나는 생물이기라도 한 듯, 그녀가 그와 자신을 동일시한 것이 더 이상 아닌 듯 보였다."], ["He tossed his head and thrust his hands deep into his pockets with a sort of vengeance.", "그는 머리를 홱 젖히고 일종의 분풀이로 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣었다."], ["There was still science—there were still good objects to work for.", "그래도 과학은 여전히 있었다—여전히 힘써 일할 만한 좋은 목표들이 있었다."], ["He must give a tug still—all the stronger because other satisfactions were going.", "그는 아직 한번 더 힘껏 잡아당겨야 했다—다른 만족들이 사라져 가고 있는 만큼 더욱 힘차게."]], [["But the door opened and Rosamond re-entered.", "그러나 문이 열리고 로저먼드가 다시 들어왔다."], ["She carried the leather box containing the amethysts, and a tiny ornamental basket which contained other boxes, and laying them on the chair where she had been sitting, she said, with perfect propriety in her air—", "그녀는 자수정이 담긴 가죽 상자와, 다른 상자들이 들어 있는 자그마한 장식 바구니를 들고 있었고, 그것들을 자기가 앉았던 의자 위에 내려놓으며 흠잡을 데 없이 단정한 분위기로 말했다."]], [["“This is all the jewellery you ever gave me.", "\"이게 당신이 제게 주신 보석 전부예요."], ["You can return what you like of it, and of the plate also.", "이 가운데 원하는 걸 돌려주시면 되고, 은제 식기도 마찬가지예요."], ["You will not, of course, expect me to stay at home to-morrow.", "물론 당신은 제가 내일 집에 있으리라 기대하지는 않으시겠죠."], ["I shall go to papa’s.”", "저는 아버지 댁에 가 있을 거예요.\""]], [["To many women the look Lydgate cast at her would have been more terrible than one of anger: it had in it a despairing acceptance of the distance she was placing between them.", "리드게이트가 그녀에게 던진 눈길은 많은 여자들에게는 성난 눈길보다 더 무서웠을 것이다. 그 눈길에는 그녀가 둘 사이에 두고 있는 거리를 절망적으로 받아들이는 빛이 담겨 있었다."]], [["“And when shall you come back again?”", "\"그러면 언제 다시 돌아오겠소?\""], ["he said, with a bitter edge on his accent.", "그가 어조에 쓰라린 날을 세우며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, in the evening.", "\"아, 저녁에요."], ["Of course I shall not mention the subject to mamma.”", "물론 어머니께는 이 일을 입에 올리지 않을 거예요.\""], ["Rosamond was convinced that no woman could behave more irreproachably than she was behaving; and she went to sit down at her work-table.", "로저먼드는 어떤 여자도 자기보다 더 나무랄 데 없이 처신할 수는 없으리라고 확신했다. 그러고는 자기 작업대로 가서 앉았다."], ["Lydgate sat meditating a minute or two, and the result was that he said, with some of the old emotion in his tone—", "리드게이트는 잠시 한두 순간 깊이 생각하며 앉아 있었고, 그 결과 어조에 예전의 감정을 얼마간 실어 말했다."]], [["“Now we have been united, Rosy, you should not leave me to myself in the first trouble that has come.”", "\"이제 우리는 부부가 되었으니, 로지, 첫 곤경이 닥쳤다고 나를 혼자 내버려 둬서는 안 되오.\""]], [["“Certainly not,”", "\"물론 그러지 않을 거예요.\""], ["said Rosamond; “I shall do everything it becomes me to do.”", "로저먼드가 말했다. \"저는 제가 해야 할 모든 일을 할 거예요.\""]], [["“It is not right that the thing should be left to servants, or that I should have to speak to them about it.", "\"이 일을 하인들에게 맡겨 두거나, 내가 그들에게 이 일을 말해야 하는 건 옳지 않소."], ["And I shall be obliged to go out—I don’t know how early.", "게다가 나는 부득이 외출해야 하오—얼마나 일찍일지 모르겠소."], ["I understand your shrinking from the humiliation of these money affairs.", "이런 돈 문제의 굴욕에서 당신이 움츠러드는 건 나도 이해하오."], ["But, my dear Rosamond, as a question of pride, which I feel just as much as you can, it is surely better to manage the thing ourselves, and let the servants see as little of it as possible; and since you are my wife, there is no hindering your share in my disgraces—if there were disgraces.”", "하지만 여보 로저먼드, 당신 못지않게 나도 절감하는 자존심의 문제로 따지자면, 이 일을 우리가 직접 처리하고 하인들에게는 되도록 적게 보이게 하는 편이 분명 더 나으오. 그리고 당신은 내 아내이니, 내 치욕에 당신이 한몫을 지는 걸—치욕이 있다면 말이지만—막을 길은 없소.\""]], [["Rosamond did not answer immediately, but at last she said, “Very well, I will stay at home.”", "로저먼드는 곧바로 대답하지 않았으나, 마침내 말했다. \"좋아요, 집에 있을게요.\""]], [["“I shall not touch these jewels, Rosy.", "\"이 보석들에는 손대지 않겠소, 로지."], ["Take them away again.", "도로 가져가요."], ["But I will write out a list of plate that we may return, and that can be packed up and sent at once.”", "하지만 우리가 돌려줄 수 있는 은제 식기 목록을 적어 두겠소. 그러면 그건 곧바로 꾸려서 보낼 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“The servants will know that,”", "\"하인들이 그건 알게 되겠네요.\""], ["said Rosamond, with the slightest touch of sarcasm.", "로저먼드가 아주 살짝 비꼬는 기색을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Well, we must meet some disagreeables as necessities.", "\"뭐, 우리는 어쩔 수 없는 일로 불쾌한 것들을 좀 마주해야 하오."], ["Where is the ink, I wonder?”", "잉크가 어디 있더라?\""], ["said Lydgate, rising, and throwing the account on the larger table where he meant to write.", "리드게이트가 일어서서, 자기가 글을 쓰려는 더 큰 탁자 위로 명세서를 던지며 말했다."]], [["Rosamond went to reach the inkstand, and after setting it on the table was going to turn away, when Lydgate, who was standing close by, put his arm round her and drew her towards him, saying—", "로저먼드는 잉크병을 가지러 갔고, 그것을 탁자에 내려놓은 뒤 돌아서려는데, 바로 곁에 서 있던 리드게이트가 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다."]], [["“Come, darling, let us make the best of things.", "\"자, 여보, 형편이 닿는 데까지 최선을 다해 봅시다."], ["It will only be for a time, I hope, that we shall have to be stingy and particular.", "우리가 인색하게 굴고 일일이 따져야 하는 건 그저 한동안일 거요."], ["Kiss me.”", "키스해 줘요.\""]], [["His native warm-heartedness took a great deal of quenching, and it is a part of manliness for a husband to feel keenly the fact that an inexperienced girl has got into trouble by marrying him.", "그가 타고난 따스한 마음은 좀처럼 꺼지지 않았다. 그리고 경험 없는 처녀가 자기와 결혼함으로써 곤경에 빠졌다는 사실을 절절히 느끼는 것은 남편으로서의 사내다움의 일부이다."], ["She received his kiss and returned it faintly, and in this way an appearance of accord was recovered for the time.", "그녀는 그의 키스를 받고 희미하게 그것을 되돌려 주었으며, 이런 식으로 한동안 화합의 외양이 회복되었다."], ["But Lydgate could not help looking forward with dread to the inevitable future discussions about expenditure and the necessity for a complete change in their way of living.", "하지만 리드게이트는, 지출에 관한, 또 생활 방식을 완전히 바꿔야 할 필요에 관한 피할 수 없는 앞으로의 의논을 두려움으로 내다보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LIX.", "# CHAPTER LIX."]], [["“They said of old the Soul had human shape, But smaller, subtler than the fleshly self, So wandered forth for airing when it pleased. And see!", "마음 내키면 바람을 쐬러 밖으로 떠도노라고."], ["beside her cherub-face there floats A pale-lipped form aerial whispering Its promptings in that little shell her ear.”", "보라! 그녀의 천사 같은 얼굴 곁에 떠도는"]], [["News is often dispersed as thoughtlessly and effectively as that pollen which the bees carry off (having no idea how powdery they are) when they are buzzing in search of their particular nectar. This fine comparison has reference to Fred Vincy, who on that evening at Lowick Parsonage heard a lively discussion among the ladies on the news which their old servant had got from Tantripp concerning Mr. Casaubon’s strange mention of Mr. Ladislaw in a codicil to his will made not long before his death. Miss Winifred was astounded to find that her brother had known the fact before, and observed that Camden was the most wonderful man for knowing things and not telling them; whereupon Mary Garth said that the codicil had perhaps got mixed up with the habits of spiders, which Miss Winifred never would listen to. Mrs. Farebrother considered that the news had something to do with their having only once seen Mr. Ladislaw at Lowick, and Miss Noble made many small compassionate mewings.", "창백한 입술의 공기 같은 형체가 속삭이며\n그 작은 조가비, 그녀의 귓속에 부추김을 흘려 넣는다."]], [["Fred knew little and cared less about Ladislaw and the Casaubons, and his mind never recurred to that discussion till one day calling on Rosamond at his mother’s request to deliver a message as he passed, he happened to see Ladislaw going away.", "소식이라는 것은 흔히, 꿀벌이 제 좋아하는 꽃의 꿀을 찾아 윙윙거리며 날아다니다가 (제 몸에 얼마나 가루가 묻었는지도 모른 채) 옮겨 나르는 그 꽃가루처럼 무심하면서도 효과적으로 퍼져 나간다. 이 멋들어진 비유는 프레드 빈시와 관련된 것인데, 그는 그날 저녁 로윅 사제관에서 부인들이 한 가지 소식을 두고 활기차게 토론하는 것을 들었다."], ["Fred and Rosamond had little to say to each other now that marriage had removed her from collision with the unpleasantness of brothers, and especially now that he had taken what she held the stupid and even reprehensible step of giving up the Church to take to such a business as Mr.", "그 집의 늙은 하녀가 탠트립에게서 들은 소식으로, 캐소본 씨가 죽기 얼마 전 작성한 유언 추가 조항에서 래디슬로 씨를 기묘하게 언급했다는 내용이었다. 위니프리드 양은 자기 오빠가 그 사실을 이미 알고 있었다는 데 깜짝 놀라며, 캠든은 어떤 일을 알면서도 말하지 않는 데서는 가장 놀라운 사람이라고 했다."], ["Garth’s. Hence Fred talked by preference of what he considered indifferent news, and “a propos of that young Ladislaw”", "그러자 메리 가스는 그 추가 조항이 어쩌면 거미의 습성에 관한 이야기와 뒤섞였을 거라고 했는데, 위니프리드 양은 그 말을 도무지 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["mentioned what he had heard at Lowick Parsonage.", "페어브러더 부인은 그 소식이 그들이 래디슬로 씨를 로윅에서 단 한 번밖에 보지 못한 일과 무언가 관계가 있을 거라고 생각했고, 노블 양은 가엾다는 듯 작게 야옹대는 소리를 여러 번 냈다."]], [["Now Lydgate, like Mr.", "프레드는 래디슬로나 캐소본 집안에 대해 아는 것도 거의 없고 관심도 더더욱 없었으므로, 그 토론이 그의 머릿속에 다시 떠오른 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["Farebrother, knew a great deal more than he told, and when he had once been set thinking about the relation between Will and Dorothea his conjectures had gone beyond the fact.", "그러던 어느 날, 어머니의 부탁으로 지나는 길에 전갈을 전하려고 로저먼드를 찾아갔다가, 마침 래디슬로가 돌아가는 것을 보게 되었다. 결혼이 로저먼드를 오빠들과 부딪치는 불쾌함에서 떼어 놓은 지금, 그리고 특히 프레드가 그녀로서는 어리석을뿐더러 비난받아 마땅하다고 여기는 행보, 즉 성직의 길을 포기하고 가스 씨 같은 사람이 하는 일에 뛰어든 지금, 두 사람은 서로 나눌 말이 별로 없었다."], ["He imagined that there was a passionate attachment on both sides, and this struck him as much too serious to gossip about.", "그래서 프레드는 그가 대수롭지 않다고 여기는 소식을 즐겨 화제로 삼았고, \"그 젊은 래디슬로 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데\" 하며 로윅 사제관에서 들은 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["He remembered Will’s irritability when he had mentioned Mrs.", "그런데 리드게이트는 페어브러더 씨처럼 말하는 것보다 훨씬 많이 알고 있었고, 한번 윌과 도러시아의 관계에 대해 생각하기 시작하자 그의 추측은 사실을 넘어서까지 뻗어 나갔다."], ["Casaubon, and was the more circumspect.", "그는 양쪽 모두에 열정적인 애착이 있다고 상상했고, 이것은 가십거리로 삼기에는 너무도 심각한 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["On the whole his surmises, in addition to what he knew of the fact, increased his friendliness and tolerance towards Ladislaw, and made him understand the vacillation which kept him at Middlemarch after he had said that he should go away.", "그는 캐소본 부인을 언급했을 때 윌이 보였던 짜증을 기억했고, 그래서 더욱 조심스러웠다. 대체로 그의 추측은, 그가 알고 있던 사실에 더해져, 래디슬로에 대한 그의 호의와 관용을 키웠으며, 떠나겠다고 말해 놓고도 미들마치에 머물게 한 그 우유부단함을 이해하게 해 주었다."], ["It was significant of the separateness between Lydgate’s mind and Rosamond’s that he had no impulse to speak to her on the subject; indeed, he did not quite trust her reticence towards Will.", "그가 이 문제에 대해 그녀에게 말하고 싶은 충동을 전혀 느끼지 않았다는 것은 리드게이트의 마음과 로저먼드의 마음 사이에 놓인 거리를 의미하는 일이었다. 사실 그는 윌에 대한 그녀의 입조심을 그다지 신뢰하지 않았다."], ["And he was right there; though he had no vision of the way in which her mind would act in urging her to speak.", "그리고 그 점에서 그는 옳았다. 다만 그녀의 마음이 어떤 식으로 작동하여 그녀로 하여금 말하도록 부추길지는 그도 짐작하지 못했다."]], [["When she repeated Fred’s news to Lydgate, he said, “Take care you don’t drop the faintest hint to Ladislaw, Rosy.", "그녀가 프레드의 소식을 리드게이트에게 옮기자, 그가 말했다. \"로지, 래디슬로한테 아주 희미한 암시조차도 흘리지 않도록 조심해요."], ["He is likely to fly out as if you insulted him.", "그 사람은 당신이 모욕이라도 한 것처럼 발끈할 가능성이 높으니까."], ["Of course it is a painful affair.”", "물론 괴로운 일이지.\""]], [["Rosamond turned her neck and patted her hair, looking the image of placid indifference.", "로저먼드는 목을 돌리고 머리를 매만지며, 평온한 무관심의 화신 같은 표정을 지었다."], ["But the next time Will came when Lydgate was away, she spoke archly about his not going to London as he had threatened.", "그러나 다음번에 리드게이트가 자리를 비웠을 때 윌이 찾아오자, 그녀는 그가 으름장 놓던 대로 런던에 가지 않은 것을 두고 짓궂게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“I know all about it.", "\"저는 다 알고 있어요."], ["I have a confidential little bird,”", "제겐 비밀을 일러 주는 작은 새가 한 마리 있거든요.\""], ["said she, showing very pretty airs of her head over the bit of work held high between her active fingers.", "그녀는 부지런히 놀리는 손가락 사이로 바느질감을 높이 들어 올린 채, 머리를 매우 예쁘게 갸웃거리며 말했다."], ["“There is a powerful magnet in this neighborhood.”", "\"이 동네에 강력한 자석이 하나 있더군요.\""]], [["“To be sure there is.", "\"물론 있고말고요."], ["Nobody knows that better than you,”", "그건 당신만큼 잘 아는 사람이 없지요.\""], ["said Will, with light gallantry, but inwardly prepared to be angry.", "윌은 가볍게 정중한 척하며 말했지만, 속으로는 화낼 준비를 하고 있었다."]], [["“It is really the most charming romance: Mr.", "\"정말이지 더없이 매력적인 연애담이에요."], ["Casaubon jealous, and foreseeing that there was no one else whom Mrs.", "캐소본 씨는 질투를 했고, 캐소본 부인이 그토록 결혼하고 싶어 할 사람이, 또 그분과 그토록 결혼하고 싶어 할 어떤 신사가 달리 없으리라는 걸 내다보았죠."], ["Casaubon would so much like to marry, and no one who would so much like to marry her as a certain gentleman; and then laying a plan to spoil all by making her forfeit her property if she did marry that gentleman—and then—and then—and then—oh, I have no doubt the end will be thoroughly romantic.”", "그러고는 그분이 만약 그 신사와 결혼하면 재산을 몰수당하게 만들어 모든 걸 망쳐 놓을 계획을 세웠고요. 그리고 그다음엔, 그다음엔, 그다음엔, 아, 결말이 틀림없이 더할 나위 없이 낭만적일 거예요.\""]], [["“Great God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what do you mean?”", "그게 무슨 소리예요?\""], ["said Will, flushing over face and ears, his features seeming to change as if he had had a violent shake.", "윌은 얼굴과 귀까지 벌게진 채 말했고, 격렬하게 흔들리기라도 한 듯 그의 이목구비가 일그러지는 것 같았다."], ["“Don’t joke; tell me what you mean.”", "\"농담은 그만두고, 무슨 뜻인지 말해 봐요.\""]], [["“You don’t really know?”", "\"정말로 모르세요?\""], ["said Rosamond, no longer playful, and desiring nothing better than to tell in order that she might evoke effects.", "로저먼드는 이제 더는 장난스럽지 않은 태도로, 효과를 자아내려고 그저 말해 주고 싶은 마음뿐이었다."]], [["“No!”", "\"몰라요!\""], ["he returned, impatiently.", "그가 조바심을 내며 대답했다."]], [["“Don’t know that Mr. Casaubon has left it in his will that if Mrs. Casaubon marries you she is to forfeit all her property?”", "\"캐소본 씨가 유언장에, 만약 캐소본 부인이 당신과 결혼하면 부인의 재산을 전부 몰수당하게 된다고 남겨 놓았다는 걸 모르세요?\""]], [["“How do you know that it is true?”", "\"그게 사실인 걸 어떻게 알죠?\""], ["said Will, eagerly.", "윌이 다급히 물었다."]], [["“My brother Fred heard it from the Farebrothers.”", "\"제 오빠 프레드가 페어브러더 댁에서 들었어요.\""], ["Will started up from his chair and reached his hat.", "윌은 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 모자를 집어 들었다."]], [["“I dare say she likes you better than the property,”", "\"제 생각엔 부인이 재산보다 당신을 더 좋아할 거예요.\""], ["said Rosamond, looking at him from a distance.", "로저먼드가 멀찍이서 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Pray don’t say any more about it,”", "\"제발 그 일에 대해선 더 말하지 말아요.\""], ["said Will, in a hoarse undertone extremely unlike his usual light voice.", "윌이 평소의 가벼운 목소리와는 전혀 다른, 쉰 듯한 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“It is a foul insult to her and to me.”", "\"그건 그분에게도 나에게도 더러운 모욕이에요.\""], ["Then he sat down absently, looking before him, but seeing nothing.", "그러고는 멍하니 앉아 앞을 바라보았지만, 아무것도 보고 있지 않았다."]], [["“Now you are angry with me,”", "\"이제 저한테 화가 나셨군요.\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“It is too bad to bear me malice.", "\"저한테 악감정을 품으시다니 너무하세요."], ["You ought to be obliged to me for telling you.”", "말해 드렸으니 저한테 고마워하셔야죠.\""]], [["“So I am,”", "\"고마워하고 있어요.\""], ["said Will, abruptly, speaking with that kind of double soul which belongs to dreamers who answer questions.", "윌은 질문에 대답하는 몽상가 특유의, 그 두 갈래로 갈린 영혼으로 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“I expect to hear of the marriage,”", "\"저는 그 결혼 소식을 듣게 되리라 기대하고 있어요.\""], ["said Rosamond, playfully.", "로저먼드가 장난스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Never!", "\"절대로!"], ["You will never hear of the marriage!”", "당신은 절대로 그 결혼 소식을 듣지 못할 거요!\""]], [["With those words uttered impetuously, Will rose, put out his hand to Rosamond, still with the air of a somnambulist, and went away.", "그 말을 격하게 내뱉고서 윌은 일어나, 여전히 몽유병자 같은 태도로 로저먼드에게 손을 내밀고는 떠나 버렸다."]], [["When he was gone, Rosamond left her chair and walked to the other end of the room, leaning when she got there against a chiffonniere, and looking out of the window wearily.", "그가 가 버리자, 로저먼드는 의자에서 일어나 방 저쪽 끝으로 걸어가더니, 그곳에 다다라 작은 장식장에 몸을 기댄 채 지친 듯 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["She was oppressed by ennui, and by that dissatisfaction which in women’s minds is continually turning into a trivial jealousy, referring to no real claims, springing from no deeper passion than the vague exactingness of egoism, and yet capable of impelling action as well as speech.", "그녀는 권태에 짓눌려 있었고, 또 여자들의 마음속에서 끊임없이 하찮은 질투로 변해 가는 그 불만에 짓눌려 있었다. 그것은 어떤 실제적인 권리에서 비롯된 것도 아니요, 자기중심주의의 막연한 까다로움보다 더 깊은 정념에서 솟아난 것도 아니었으나, 그러면서도 말뿐만 아니라 행동까지 부추길 수 있는 것이었다."], ["“There really is nothing to care for much,”", "\"정말이지 그다지 마음 쓸 만한 일이 없어.\""], ["said poor Rosamond inwardly, thinking of the family at Quallingham, who did not write to her; and that perhaps Tertius when he came home would tease her about expenses.", "가엾은 로저먼드는 속으로 그렇게 생각하며, 자기에게 편지 한 통 보내지 않는 콸링엄의 식구들을 떠올렸다. 그리고 어쩌면 테르티우스가 집에 돌아오면 지출 문제로 자기를 들볶을 거라는 생각도 했다."], ["She had already secretly disobeyed him by asking her father to help them, and he had ended decisively by saying, “I am more likely to want help myself.”", "그녀는 이미 그를 거역하고 몰래 아버지에게 도움을 청한 적이 있었는데, 아버지는 \"내가 오히려 도움이 필요할 판이다\"라는 말로 단호하게 끝맺어 버렸던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER LX.", "# CHAPTER LX."]], [["Good phrases are surely, and ever were, very commendable.", "좋은 표현은 분명, 그리고 언제나, 매우 칭찬할 만한 것이었다."], ["—Justice Shallow.", "—섈로 판사*"]], [["A few days afterwards—it was already the end of August—there was an occasion which caused some excitement in Middlemarch: the public, if it chose, was to have the advantage of buying, under the distinguished auspices of Mr.", "* 셰익스피어의 『헨리 4세 2부』에 등장하는 인물. 며칠 뒤—이미 팔월 말이었다—미들마치에 적잖은 흥분을 불러일으킨 사건이 하나 있었다."], ["Borthrop Trumbull, the furniture, books, and pictures which anybody might see by the handbills to be the best in every kind, belonging to Edwin Larcher, Esq.", "사람들은 원한다면 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 명망 높은 주관 아래, 가구와 책과 그림을 살 수 있는 이점을 누리게 되었으니, 그것들은 광고지에서 누구나 보았듯이 종류마다 최고의 물품으로 에드윈 라처 씨의 소유였다."], ["This was not one of the sales indicating the depression of trade; on the contrary, it was due to Mr.", "이것은 사업 부진을 나타내는 매각이 아니었다."], ["Larcher’s great success in the carrying business, which warranted his purchase of a mansion near Riverston already furnished in high style by an illustrious Spa physician—furnished indeed with such large framefuls of expensive flesh-painting in the dining-room, that Mrs. Larcher was nervous until reassured by finding the subjects to be Scriptural.", "오히려 라처 씨가 운수업에서 크게 성공한 덕분이었으니, 그 성공으로 그는 어느 저명한 온천 의사가 이미 고급스럽게 꾸며 놓은 리버스턴 근처의 저택을 사들일 수 있었다—식당에는 값비싼 살빛 그림이 큼직한 액자에 가득 담겨 걸려 있어 라처 부인이 불안해할 정도였으나, 그 주제가 성서적인 것임을 알고서야 안심했다."], ["Hence the fine opportunity to purchasers which was well pointed out in the handbills of Mr.", "그리하여 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 광고지에 잘 명시된, 구매자들을 위한 절호의 기회가 생긴 것이다."], ["Borthrop Trumbull, whose acquaintance with the history of art enabled him to state that the hall furniture, to be sold without reserve, comprised a piece of carving by a contemporary of Gibbons.", "그는 미술사에 대한 지식 덕분에, 유보 없이 매각될 현관 가구에는 기번스와 동시대 인물이 만든 조각 작품이 포함되어 있다고 밝힐 수 있었다."]], [["At Middlemarch in those times a large sale was regarded as a kind of festival.", "당시 미들마치에서 대규모 경매는 일종의 축제로 여겨졌다."], ["There was a table spread with the best cold eatables, as at a superior funeral; and facilities were offered for that generous-drinking of cheerful glasses which might lead to generous and cheerful bidding for undesirable articles. Mr.", "격조 높은 장례식에서처럼 최고급 찬 음식이 차려진 식탁이 마련되었고, 흥겨운 잔을 후하게 들이켜기에 좋은 편의가 제공되었으니, 그 술기운은 별로 탐나지 않는 물건에도 후하고 명랑하게 값을 부르도록 이끌 법했다."], ["Larcher’s sale was the more attractive in the fine weather because the house stood just at the end of the town, with a garden and stables attached, in that pleasant issue from Middlemarch called the London Road, which was also the road to the New Hospital and to Mr.", "라처 씨의 경매는 날씨가 좋아 더욱 매력적이었는데, 집이 마침 마을 끝자락에 정원과 마구간을 거느리고 서 있었기 때문이다. 미들마치에서 빠져나가는 그 기분 좋은 길은 런던 가도라 불렸으니, 신축 병원과 더 슈럽스라 알려진 불스트로드 씨의 은거지로 가는 길이기도 했다."], ["Bulstrode’s retired residence, known as the Shrubs. In short, the auction was as good as a fair, and drew all classes with leisure at command: to some, who risked making bids in order simply to raise prices, it was almost equal to betting at the races.", "요컨대 그 경매는 장날만큼이나 좋은 구경거리였고, 여유 있는 모든 계층의 사람들을 끌어모았다."], ["The second day, when the best furniture was to be sold, “everybody” was there; even Mr.", "단지 값을 올리려고 값을 부르는 모험을 하는 이들에게는 경마장에서 거는 내기나 다름없었다."], ["Thesiger, the rector of St. Peter’s, had looked in for a short time, wishing to buy the carved table, and had rubbed elbows with Mr.", "둘째 날, 가장 좋은 가구가 매각되는 날에는 \"누구나\""], ["Bambridge and Mr.", "그곳에 있었다."], ["Horrock. There was a wreath of Middlemarch ladies accommodated with seats round the large table in the dining-room, where Mr.", "세인트피터스 교구의 주임 사제인 시지어 씨조차 조각 탁자를 사고 싶어 잠깐 들렀고, 뱀브리지 씨와 호록 씨와 어깨를 부딪쳤다."], ["Borthrop Trumbull was mounted with desk and hammer; but the rows chiefly of masculine faces behind were often varied by incomings and outgoings both from the door and the large bow-window opening on to the lawn.", "식당의 큰 탁자 둘레에는 미들마치 부인들이 화환처럼 둘러앉아 자리를 차지했고, 그 한가운데에 보스럽 트럼불 씨가 책상과 망치를 갖추고 올라앉았다. 하지만 그 뒤로 주로 남자들의 얼굴이 늘어선 줄은, 문과 잔디밭으로 열린 큰 활 모양 창문을 통해 드나드는 사람들로 자주 흐트러졌다."]], [["“Everybody” that day did not include Mr. Bulstrode, whose health could not well endure crowds and draughts. But Mrs. Bulstrode had particularly wished to have a certain picture—a “Supper at Emmaus,” attributed in the catalogue to Guido; and at the last moment before the day of the sale Mr. Bulstrode had called at the office of the “Pioneer,” of which he was now one of the proprietors, to beg of Mr. Ladislaw as a great favor that he would obligingly use his remarkable knowledge of pictures on behalf of Mrs. Bulstrode, and judge of the value of this particular painting—“if,” added the scrupulously polite banker, “attendance at the sale would not interfere with the arrangements for your departure, which I know is imminent.”", "그날 \"누구나\"에 불스트로드 씨는 포함되지 않았으니, 그의 건강은 군중과 외풍을 잘 견디지 못했던 것이다. 하지만 불스트로드 부인은 어떤 그림 하나를 특히 갖고 싶어 했다—목록에는 귀도의 작품으로 적힌 「엠마오의 만찬」이었다. 경매 당일 마지막 순간에 불스트로드 씨는, 이제 자신도 그 일부 소유주가 된 『파이어니어』의 사무실에 들러, 래디슬로 씨에게 큰 호의를 베푸는 셈치고 그의 뛰어난 그림 식견을 불스트로드 부인을 위해 발휘하여 이 특정 그림의 가치를 판단해 달라고 부탁했다—\"다만,\" 그 꼼꼼하고 정중한 은행가는 덧붙였다, \"경매 참석이 임박해 있다고 알고 있는 당신의 출발 준비에 지장을 주지 않는다면 말입니다.\""]], [["This proviso might have sounded rather satirically in Will’s ear if he had been in a mood to care about such satire.", "이 단서는, 윌이 그런 풍자에 마음 쓸 기분이었다면, 다소 비꼬는 소리로 들렸을지도 모른다."], ["It referred to an understanding entered into many weeks before with the proprietors of the paper, that he should be at liberty any day he pleased to hand over the management to the subeditor whom he had been training; since he wished finally to quit Middlemarch.", "그것은 여러 주 전에 신문사 소유주들과 맺은 합의를 가리키는 것이었으니, 그가 원하는 날 언제든 자신이 훈련시켜 온 부편집인에게 경영을 넘기고 자유로이 떠나도 좋다는 내용이었다. 그가 마침내 미들마치를 떠나고 싶어 했기 때문이다."], ["But indefinite visions of ambition are weak against the ease of doing what is habitual or beguilingly agreeable; and we all know the difficulty of carrying out a resolve when we secretly long that it may turn out to be unnecessary.", "하지만 야망에 대한 막연한 환상은 익숙하거나 그럴듯하게 기분 좋은 일을 하는 편안함 앞에서 무력하다. 그리고 우리는 결심이 불필요한 것으로 드러나기를 은근히 바랄 때, 그것을 실행에 옮기기가 얼마나 어려운지 잘 안다."], ["In such states of mind the most incredulous person has a private leaning towards miracle: impossible to conceive how our wish could be fulfilled, still—very wonderful things have happened!", "그런 마음 상태에서는 가장 회의적인 사람조차 기적 쪽으로 은밀히 기울게 마련이다. 우리의 소망이 어떻게 이루어질 수 있을지 상상조차 할 수 없지만, 그래도—참으로 놀라운 일들이 일어나 왔으니!"], ["Will did not confess this weakness to himself, but he lingered.", "윌은 이 약점을 스스로 인정하지 않았으나, 머뭇거렸다."], ["What was the use of going to London at that time of the year?", "일 년 중 그런 시기에 런던에 가서 무슨 소용이 있겠는가?"], ["The Rugby men who would remember him were not there; and so far as political writing was concerned, he would rather for a few weeks go on with the “Pioneer.”", "그를 기억해 줄 럭비 학교 친구들은 그곳에 없었다. 정치 평론에 관한 한, 그는 차라리 몇 주 더 『파이어니어』 일을 계속하고 싶었다."], ["At the present moment, however, when Mr. Bulstrode was speaking to him, he had both a strengthened resolve to go and an equally strong resolve not to go till he had once more seen Dorothea.", "그러나 지금 이 순간, 불스트로드 씨가 그에게 말을 걸고 있을 때, 그에게는 떠나겠다는 굳어진 결심과 더불어, 도러시아를 한 번 더 보기 전까지는 떠나지 않겠다는 똑같이 강한 결심이 함께 있었다."], ["Hence he replied that he had reasons for deferring his departure a little, and would be happy to go to the sale.", "그리하여 그는 출발을 조금 미룰 이유가 있으며, 기꺼이 경매에 가겠다고 대답했다."]], [["Will was in a defiant mood, his consciousness being deeply stung with the thought that the people who looked at him probably knew a fact tantamount to an accusation against him as a fellow with low designs which were to be frustrated by a disposal of property.", "윌은 반항적인 기분이었으니, 자신을 쳐다보는 사람들이 아마도 그를 향한 고발이나 다름없는 사실, 곧 그가 재산 처분으로 좌절될 비열한 속셈을 품은 작자라는 사실을 알고 있으리라는 생각이 그의 의식을 깊이 찔렀던 것이다."], ["Like most people who assert their freedom with regard to conventional distinction, he was prepared to be sudden and quick at quarrel with any one who might hint that he had personal reasons for that assertion—that there was anything in his blood, his bearing, or his character to which he gave the mask of an opinion.", "관습적인 신분 구별에 맞서 자신의 자유를 주장하는 대부분의 사람들이 그러하듯, 그는 자신이 그런 주장을 펴는 데에 개인적인 이유가 있다고—자신의 혈통이나 처신이나 성품에 어떤 의견의 가면을 씌우고 있는 무언가가 있다고—넌지시 비치는 자가 있으면 누구와든 불쑥 빠르게 다투려고 벼르고 있었다."], ["When he was under an irritating impression of this kind he would go about for days with a defiant look, the color changing in his transparent skin as if he were on the qui vive, watching for something which he had to dart upon.", "그는 이런 종류의 자극적인 인상을 받으면 며칠 동안 반항적인 표정으로 돌아다녔고, 마치 경계 태세로 무언가를 노려 덮치려는 듯, 투명한 피부의 빛깔이 바뀌곤 했다."]], [["This expression was peculiarly noticeable in him at the sale, and those who had only seen him in his moods of gentle oddity or of bright enjoyment would have been struck with a contrast.", "이 표정은 경매에서 그에게서 유난히 두드러졌으니, 그의 부드러운 괴벽이나 밝은 즐거움의 기분만 보아 온 이들이라면 그 대조에 놀랐을 것이다."], ["He was not sorry to have this occasion for appearing in public before the Middlemarch tribes of Toller, Hackbutt, and the rest, who looked down on him as an adventurer, and were in a state of brutal ignorance about Dante—who sneered at his Polish blood, and were themselves of a breed very much in need of crossing.", "그는 톨러, 해크벗 등 미들마치 일족들 앞에 공개적으로 나설 이런 기회가 싫지 않았으니, 그들은 그를 모험가로 깔보았고 단테에 대해서는 야만적으로 무지한 자들이었다—그의 폴란드 혈통을 비웃었으나, 그들 자신이야말로 이종 교배가 몹시 필요한 혈통이었다. 그는 경매인에게서 멀지 않은 눈에 띄는 자리에, 양쪽 호주머니에 검지를 하나씩 찔러 넣고 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 서 있었다."], ["He stood in a conspicuous place not far from the auctioneer, with a fore-finger in each side-pocket and his head thrown backward, not caring to speak to anybody, though he had been cordially welcomed as a connoissure by Mr. Trumbull, who was enjoying the utmost activity of his great faculties.", "누구에게도 말을 걸 마음이 없었으나, 트럼불 씨는 그를 감식가로 따뜻이 맞아 주었고, 그 자신은 자신의 위대한 재능을 한껏 발휘하며 즐기고 있었다."]], [["And surely among all men whose vocation requires them to exhibit their powers of speech, the happiest is a prosperous provincial auctioneer keenly alive to his own jokes and sensible of his encyclopedic knowledge.", "그리고 분명 말솜씨를 발휘해야 하는 직업을 가진 모든 사람 중에서, 가장 행복한 자는 자기 농담에 예민하게 살아 있고 자신의 백과사전적 지식을 의식하는, 번창하는 지방 경매인이다. 침울하고 시큰둥한 성미의 어떤 이들은 장화 벗는 기구에서 \"베르헴\""], ["Some saturnine, sour-blooded persons might object to be constantly insisting on the merits of all articles from boot-jacks to “Berghems;” but Mr.", "그림에 이르기까지 모든 물건의 장점을 끊임없이 역설하는 일에 반감을 가질지도 모른다."], ["Borthrop Trumbull had a kindly liquid in his veins; he was an admirer by nature, and would have liked to have the universe under his hammer, feeling that it would go at a higher figure for his recommendation.", "하지만 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 핏줄에는 다정한 액체가 흘렀다. 그는 천성이 찬미자였고, 온 우주를 자신의 망치 아래 두고 싶어 했으니, 자신의 추천이라면 우주도 더 높은 값에 팔려 나가리라 여겼던 것이다."]], [["Meanwhile Mrs. Larcher’s drawing-room furniture was enough for him.", "한편 라처 부인의 응접실 가구만으로도 그에게는 충분했다."], ["When Will Ladislaw had come in, a second fender, said to have been forgotten in its right place, suddenly claimed the auctioneer’s enthusiasm, which he distributed on the equitable principle of praising those things most which were most in need of praise.", "윌 래디슬로가 들어왔을 때, 제자리에 놓는 것을 깜빡했다는 두 번째 난로 망이 느닷없이 경매인의 열정을 사로잡았으니, 그는 가장 칭찬이 필요한 물건을 가장 많이 칭찬한다는 공정한 원칙에 따라 그 열정을 배분했다."], ["The fender was of polished steel, with much lancet-shaped open-work and a sharp edge.", "그 난로 망은 광을 낸 강철로, 창끝 모양으로 뚫린 무늬가 많고 날이 날카로웠다."]], [["“Now, ladies,”", "\"자, 부인들,\""], ["said he, “I shall appeal to you.", "그가 말했다, \"여러분께 호소하겠습니다."], ["Here is a fender which at any other sale would hardly be offered with out reserve, being, as I may say, for quality of steel and quaintness of design, a kind of thing”—here Mr. Trumbull dropped his voice and became slightly nasal, trimming his outlines with his left finger—“that might not fall in with ordinary tastes.", "여기 이 난로 망은 다른 경매라면 유보 없이 내놓기 어려울 물건이니, 강철의 품질과 디자인의 기묘함으로 보아 이를테면 일종의\"—여기서 트럼불 씨는 목소리를 낮추고 약간 콧소리를 내며 왼손가락으로 윤곽을 다듬었다—\"보통의 취향에는 맞지 않을 법한 물건입니다."], ["Allow me to tell you that by-and-by this style of workmanship will be the only one in vogue—half-a-crown, you said?", "머지않아 이런 양식의 세공이 유일하게 유행하게 되리라는 점을 말씀드리지요—반 크라운이라 하셨습니까?"], ["thank you—going at half-a-crown, this characteristic fender; and I have particular information that the antique style is very much sought after in high quarters.", "감사합니다—반 크라운에 나갑니다, 이 개성 있는 난로 망이. 그리고 저는 이 고풍스러운 양식이 상류층에서 대단히 추구되고 있다는 특별한 정보를 가지고 있습니다."], ["Three shillings—three-and-sixpence—hold it well up, Joseph!", "삼 실링—삼 실링 육 펜스—잘 들어 올려라, 조지프!"], ["Look, ladies, at the chastity of the design—I have no doubt myself that it was turned out in the last century!", "보십시오, 부인들, 이 디자인의 정결함을—저는 이것이 지난 세기에 만들어졌다고 확신합니다!"], ["Four shillings, Mr. Mawmsey?—four shillings.”", "사 실링이십니까, 몸지 씨?—사 실링.\""]], [["“It’s not a thing I would put in my drawing-room,”", "\"저것은 제 응접실에 둘 만한 물건이 아니에요.\""], ["said Mrs. Mawmsey, audibly, for the warning of the rash husband.", "몸지 부인이, 경솔한 남편에게 경고하려는 듯 또렷이 말했다."], ["“I wonder at Mrs. Larcher.", "\"라처 부인은 어쩌자고."], ["Every blessed child’s head that fell against it would be cut in two.", "부딪치는 아이마다 머리가 둘로 갈라질 거예요."], ["The edge is like a knife.”", "날이 칼 같잖아요.\""]], [["“Quite true,” rejoined Mr.", "\"지당하신 말씀입니다.\""], ["Trumbull, quickly, “and most uncommonly useful to have a fender at hand that will cut, if you have a leather shoe-tie or a bit of string that wants cutting and no knife at hand: many a man has been left hanging because there was no knife to cut him down.", "트럼불 씨가 재빨리 응수했다. \"그리고 가까이 둘 수 있는, 자를 수 있는 난로 망이란 더없이 유용하지요."], ["Gentlemen, here’s a fender that if you had the misfortune to hang yourselves would cut you down in no time—with astonishing celerity—four-and-sixpence—five—five-and-sixpence—an appropriate thing for a spare bedroom where there was a four-poster and a guest a little out of his mind—six shillings—thank you, Mr.", "가죽 신발 끈이나 끊어야 할 노끈은 있는데 칼이 없을 때 말입니다."], ["Clintup—going at six shillings—going—gone!”", "끊어 줄 칼이 없어서 매달린 채 남겨진 사람이 한둘이 아닙니다."], ["The auctioneer’s glance, which had been searching round him with a preternatural susceptibility to all signs of bidding, here dropped on the paper before him, and his voice too dropped into a tone of indifferent despatch as he said, “Mr.", "신사 여러분, 여기 이 난로 망은, 만약 불행히도 목을 매신다면 눈 깜짝할 사이에 끊어 내려 줄 것입니다—놀라운 신속함으로요—사 실링 육 펜스—오 실링—오 실링 육 펜스—사주식 침대가 놓이고 손님이 다소 정신이 나간 손님방에 안성맞춤이지요—육 실링—감사합니다, 클린텁 씨—육 실링에 나갑니다—나갑니다—낙찰!\" 모든 호가의 기미에 초자연적인 예민함으로 주위를 살피던 경매인의 시선이 여기서 앞에 놓인 종이로 떨어졌고, 그의 목소리도 무심하게 처리하는 어조로 가라앉으며 그는 말했다, \"클린텁 씨."], ["Clintup. Be handy, Joseph.”", "잽싸게 해라, 조지프.\""]], [["“It was worth six shillings to have a fender you could always tell that joke on,” said Mr.", "\"저런 농담을 늘 곁들일 수 있는 난로 망이라면 육 실링 값은 하지.\""], ["Clintup, laughing low and apologetically to his next neighbor.", "클린텁 씨가 옆 사람에게 변명하듯 낮게 웃으며 말했다."], ["He was a diffident though distinguished nurseryman, and feared that the audience might regard his bid as a foolish one.", "그는 수줍지만 명망 있는 묘목 상인이었고, 청중이 자신의 호가를 어리석은 것으로 여길까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["Meanwhile Joseph had brought a trayful of small articles.", "그사이 조지프가 자잘한 물건이 가득 담긴 쟁반을 가져왔다."], ["“Now, ladies,”", "\"자, 부인들,\""], ["said Mr. Trumbull, taking up one of the articles, “this tray contains a very recherchy lot—a collection of trifles for the drawing-room table—and trifles make the sum of human things—nothing more important than trifles—(yes, Mr.", "트럼불 씨가 물건 하나를 집어 들며 말했다, \"이 쟁반에는 아주 별난 한 묶음이 담겨 있습니다—응접실 탁자를 위한 자잘한 소품 모음이지요—그리고 자잘한 것들이 인간 만사의 총합을 이룹니다—자잘한 것보다 더 중요한 것은 없지요—(예, 래디슬로 씨, 예, 곧 하겠습니다)—하지만 쟁반을 돌려라, 조지프—이 보석들은 살펴보셔야 합니다, 부인들."], ["Ladislaw, yes, by-and-by)—but pass the tray round, Joseph—these bijoux must be examined, ladies.", "제 손에 든 이것은 기발한 고안품입니다—말하자면 일종의 실용적인 그림 수수께끼라 할 수 있지요."], ["This I have in my hand is an ingenious contrivance—a sort of practical rebus, I may call it: here, you see, it looks like an elegant heart-shaped box, portable—for the pocket; there, again, it becomes like a splendid double flower—an ornament for the table; and now”—Mr.", "보십시오, 여기서는 우아한 하트 모양 상자처럼 보이고—휴대용으로, 주머니에 넣을 수 있습니다. 또 저기서는 화려한 겹꽃처럼 변합니다—탁자 장식품이지요."], ["Trumbull allowed the flower to fall alarmingly into strings of heart-shaped leaves—“a book of riddles!", "그리고 이제는\"—트럼불 씨는 그 꽃을 하트 모양 잎사귀들의 줄로 아슬아슬하게 늘어뜨렸다—\"수수께끼 책입니다!"], ["No less than five hundred printed in a beautiful red.", "무려 오백 개가 아름다운 붉은 글씨로 인쇄되어 있지요."], ["Gentlemen, if I had less of a conscience, I should not wish you to bid high for this lot—I have a longing for it myself.", "신사 여러분, 제가 양심이 조금만 덜했다면 여러분께서 이 묶음에 높은 값을 부르시기를 바라지 않았을 것입니다—저 자신이 이것을 탐내고 있으니까요."], ["What can promote innocent mirth, and I may say virtue, more than a good riddle?—it hinders profane language, and attaches a man to the society of refined females.", "좋은 수수께끼만큼 순수한 즐거움과, 감히 말하건대 미덕을 북돋우는 것이 또 있겠습니까?—그것은 상스러운 말을 막아 주고, 사람을 교양 있는 여성들의 모임에 붙들어 둡니다."], ["This ingenious article itself, without the elegant domino-box, card-basket, &c., ought alone to give a high price to the lot.", "이 기발한 물건 하나만으로도, 우아한 도미노 상자며 카드 바구니 따위는 빼고도, 이 묶음에 높은 값을 매겨 마땅합니다."], ["Carried in the pocket it might make an individual welcome in any society.", "주머니에 넣고 다니면 어떤 모임에서든 환영받는 사람이 될 수 있지요."], ["Four shillings, sir?—four shillings for this remarkable collection of riddles with the et caeteras.", "사 실링이십니까, 선생님?—이 놀라운 수수께끼 모음에 기타 등등을 더해 사 실링. 여기 견본이 하나 있습니다."], ["Here is a sample: ‘How must you spell honey to make it catch lady-birds?", "'꿀로 무당벌레를 잡으려면 꿀을 어떻게 써야 하는가? 답—돈이다.'* 들으셨습니까?—무당벌레—꿀과 돈."], ["Answer—money.’ You hear?—lady-birds—honey money.", "이것은 지력을 날카롭게 하는 오락입니다."], ["This is an amusement to sharpen the intellect; it has a sting—it has what we call satire, and wit without indecency.", "가시가 있고—우리가 풍자라 부르는 것이 있으며, 음란함 없는 재치가 있지요. 사 실링 육 펜스—오 실링.\""], ["Four-and-sixpence—five shillings.”", "* 영어 honey와 money의 발음 유사성을 이용한 말장난."]], [["The bidding ran on with warming rivalry.", "호가는 점점 달아오르는 경쟁 속에 이어졌다."], ["Mr. Bowyer was a bidder, and this was too exasperating.", "보이어 씨도 값을 불렀는데, 이것은 너무나 약 오르는 일이었다."], ["Bowyer couldn’t afford it, and only wanted to hinder every other man from making a figure. The current carried even Mr.", "보이어는 그것을 살 형편도 아니면서, 그저 다른 모든 사람이 잘난 척하는 것을 방해하고 싶을 뿐이었다."], ["Horrock with it, but this committal of himself to an opinion fell from him with so little sacrifice of his neutral expression, that the bid might not have been detected as his but for the friendly oaths of Mr.", "그 흐름은 호록 씨마저 휩쓸어 갔으나, 의견에 대한 이 같은 그의 가담은 중립적인 표정을 거의 희생하지 않고 흘러나온지라, 뱀브리지 씨의 다정한 욕설이 아니었던들 그것이 그의 호가임을 아무도 알아채지 못했을 것이다."], ["Bambridge, who wanted to know what Horrock would do with blasted stuff only fit for haberdashers given over to that state of perdition which the horse-dealer so cordially recognized in the majority of earthly existences.", "뱀브리지 씨는 호록이, 이 말 장수가 지상 존재 대다수에게서 그토록 흔쾌히 인정하는 그 파멸 상태에 빠진 잡화상에게나 어울릴 빌어먹을 물건을 가지고 무엇을 하려는지 알고 싶어 했다. 그 묶음은 결국 일 기니에 스필킨스 씨에게 낙찰되었으니, 그는 동네의 젊은 슬렌더*로, 용돈을 함부로 써 대는 데다 수수께끼에 대한 기억력이 모자란다고 느끼는 사람이었다."], ["The lot was finally knocked down at a guinea to Mr. Spilkins, a young Slender of the neighborhood, who was reckless with his pocket-money and felt his want of memory for riddles.", "* 셰익스피어의 『윈저의 즐거운 아낙네들』에 나오는 어리석은 젊은이."]], [["“Come, Trumbull, this is too bad—you’ve been putting some old maid’s rubbish into the sale,”", "\"이봐, 트럼불, 이건 너무하잖나—노처녀의 잡동사니를 경매에 끼워 넣다니.\""], ["murmured Mr. Toller, getting close to the auctioneer.", "톨러 씨가 경매인에게 바싹 다가서며 중얼거렸다."], ["“I want to see how the prints go, and I must be off soon.”", "\"판화가 어떻게 나가는지 보고 싶은데, 곧 가 봐야 한단 말일세.\""]], [["“Immediately, Mr. Toller.", "\"즉시 하겠습니다, 톨러 씨."], ["It was only an act of benevolence which your noble heart would approve.", "그것은 그저 당신의 고결한 마음도 인정하실 자선의 행위였을 뿐입니다."], ["Joseph!", "조지프!"], ["quick with the prints—Lot 235.", "판화를 어서—235번 묶음입니다. 자, 감식안을 갖추신 신사 여러분, 이제 진수성찬을 맛보시게 됩니다."], ["Now, gentlemen, you who are connoissures, you are going to have a treat.", "여기 워털루 전장에서 참모들에게 둘러싸인 웰링턴 공작의 판화가 있습니다. 그리고 최근 이를테면 우리의 위대한 영웅을 구름 속에 가려 버린 사건들이 있었음에도—제 직업을 가진 사람은 정치적 바람에 흔들려서는 안 되니까요—저는 감히 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Here is an engraving of the Duke of Wellington surrounded by his staff on the Field of Waterloo; and notwithstanding recent events which have, as it were, enveloped our great Hero in a cloud, I will be bold to say—for a man in my line must not be blown about by political winds—that a finer subject—of the modern order, belonging to our own time and epoch—the understanding of man could hardly conceive: angels might, perhaps, but not men, sirs, not men.”", "이보다 더 훌륭한 주제는—우리 자신의 시대와 시기에 속한 현대적 종류의 주제는—인간의 지력으로 거의 떠올릴 수 없을 것이라고요. 천사라면 혹 모르나, 인간은 아닙니다, 여러분, 인간은 아니지요.\""]], [["“Who painted it?”", "\"누가 그렸소?\""], ["said Mr. Powderell, much impressed.", "깊이 감동한 파우더렐 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It is a proof before the letter, Mr. Powderell—the painter is not known,”", "\"이것은 글자가 새겨지기 전의 시쇄본입니다, 파우더렐 씨—화가는 알려져 있지 않지요.\""], ["answered Trumbull, with a certain gaspingness in his last words, after which he pursed up his lips and stared round him.", "트럼불이 마지막 말에 숨을 약간 헐떡이며 대답하고는, 입술을 오므린 채 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“I’ll bid a pound!” said Mr.", "\"일 파운드 부르겠소!\""], ["Powderell, in a tone of resolved emotion, as of a man ready to put himself in the breach.", "파우더렐 씨가, 무너진 성벽을 메우려 몸을 던지는 사람처럼 결연한 감정의 어조로 말했다."], ["Whether from awe or pity, nobody raised the price on him.", "경외 때문인지 연민 때문인지, 아무도 그에게 값을 더 올려 부르지 않았다."]], [["Next came two Dutch prints which Mr. Toller had been eager for, and after he had secured them he went away.", "다음으로 톨러 씨가 간절히 원하던 네덜란드 판화 두 점이 나왔고, 그것을 손에 넣은 뒤 그는 떠났다."], ["Other prints, and afterwards some paintings, were sold to leading Middlemarchers who had come with a special desire for them, and there was a more active movement of the audience in and out; some, who had bought what they wanted, going away, others coming in either quite newly or from a temporary visit to the refreshments which were spread under the marquee on the lawn.", "다른 판화들, 그리고 그 뒤로 몇몇 그림이, 특별히 그것들을 원해서 온 미들마치의 유력 인사들에게 팔렸고, 청중의 출입이 더욱 활발해졌다. 원하던 것을 산 이들은 떠나고, 다른 이들은 아주 새로 들어오거나 잔디밭 천막 아래 차려진 다과를 잠시 즐기고 들어왔다."], ["It was this marquee that Mr. Bambridge was bent on buying, and he appeared to like looking inside it frequently, as a foretaste of its possession.", "뱀브리지 씨가 사려고 작정한 것이 바로 이 천막이었는데, 그는 소유의 전조를 맛보기라도 하듯 자주 그 안을 들여다보기를 좋아하는 것 같았다."], ["On the last occasion of his return from it he was observed to bring with him a new companion, a stranger to Mr.", "마지막으로 거기서 돌아왔을 때, 그가 새로운 동행을 데려오는 것이 눈에 띄었으니, 트럼불 씨를 비롯한 모두에게 낯선 사람이었다."], ["Trumbull and every one else, whose appearance, however, led to the supposition that he might be a relative of the horse-dealer’s—also “given to indulgence.”", "다만 그 외모로 보아 말 장수의 친척이 아닐까—역시 \"탐닉에 빠진\" 자가 아닐까 짐작되었다."], ["His large whiskers, imposing swagger, and swing of the leg, made him a striking figure; but his suit of black, rather shabby at the edges, caused the prejudicial inference that he was not able to afford himself as much indulgence as he liked.", "큼직한 구레나룻과 위세 있는 거들먹거림, 다리를 휘젓는 걸음걸이가 그를 눈에 띄는 인물로 만들었다. 하지만 가장자리가 다소 해진 검은 양복은, 그가 원하는 만큼 탐닉할 형편은 못 된다는 불리한 추측을 불러일으켰다."]], [["“Who is it you’ve picked up, Bam?”", "\"자네가 데려온 사람은 누구야, 뱀?\""], ["said Mr. Horrock, aside.", "호록 씨가 곁에서 물었다."]], [["“Ask him yourself,”", "\"직접 물어봐.\""], ["returned Mr. Bambridge.", "뱀브리지 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["“He said he’d just turned in from the road.”", "\"길에서 막 들어왔다더군.\""]], [["Mr. Horrock eyed the stranger, who was leaning back against his stick with one hand, using his toothpick with the other, and looking about him with a certain restlessness apparently under the silence imposed on him by circumstances.", "호록 씨는 그 낯선 사람을 살펴보았는데, 그는 한 손으로 지팡이에 몸을 기댄 채 다른 손으로 이쑤시개를 쓰며, 상황이 강요한 침묵 속에서 어떤 안절부절못함을 띤 채 주위를 둘러보고 있었다."]], [["At length the “Supper at Emmaus”", "마침내 「엠마오의 만찬」이 앞으로 나왔으니, 윌로서는 더없는 안도였다."], ["was brought forward, to Will’s immense relief, for he was getting so tired of the proceedings that he had drawn back a little and leaned his shoulder against the wall just behind the auctioneer.", "그는 진행에 너무나 싫증이 나서 조금 물러나 경매인 바로 뒤의 벽에 어깨를 기대고 있었던 것이다. 이제 그는 다시 앞으로 나섰는데, 그 눈에 눈에 띄는 그 낯선 사람이 들어왔다."], ["He now came forward again, and his eye caught the conspicuous stranger, who, rather to his surprise, was staring at him markedly.", "다소 놀랍게도, 그자는 그를 노골적으로 빤히 쳐다보고 있었다."], ["But Will was immediately appealed to by Mr. Trumbull.", "하지만 윌은 곧바로 트럼불 씨의 호소를 받았다."]], [["“Yes, Mr.", "\"예, 래디슬로 씨, 예."], ["Ladislaw, yes; this interests you as a connoissure, I think. It is some pleasure,”", "감식가이신 당신께서는 이것에 관심이 있으실 줄 압니다."], ["the auctioneer went on with a rising fervor, “to have a picture like this to show to a company of ladies and gentlemen—a picture worth any sum to an individual whose means were on a level with his judgment.", "이런 그림을 신사 숙녀 여러분께 보여 드릴 수 있다는 것은,\" 경매인이 고조되는 열정으로 말을 이었다, \"여간한 기쁨이 아닙니다—식견에 걸맞은 재력을 갖춘 분에게는 그 값을 매길 수 없는 그림이지요."], ["It is a painting of the Italian school—by the celebrated Guydo, the greatest painter in the world, the chief of the Old Masters, as they are called—I take it, because they were up to a thing or two beyond most of us—in possession of secrets now lost to the bulk of mankind.", "이것은 이탈리아 화파의 그림으로—세계 최고의 화가이자 이른바 옛 거장들의 우두머리인 저 유명한 귀도의 작품입니다—제 생각에 그들을 옛 거장이라 부르는 까닭은, 그들이 우리 대부분을 넘어서는 한두 가지 비결을, 이제는 인류 대다수에게 사라져 버린 비밀을 알고 있었기 때문이지요."], ["Let me tell you, gentlemen, I have seen a great many pictures by the Old Masters, and they are not all up to this mark—some of them are darker than you might like and not family subjects.", "말씀드리건대, 신사 여러분, 저는 옛 거장들의 그림을 매우 많이 보았으나, 그것들이 다 이 수준에 이르는 것은 아닙니다—그중에는 여러분 마음에 들기에 너무 어둡고 가족용 주제가 아닌 것도 있지요."], ["But here is a Guydo—the frame alone is worth pounds—which any lady might be proud to hang up—a suitable thing for what we call a refectory in a charitable institution, if any gentleman of the Corporation wished to show his munificence.", "하지만 여기 이것은 귀도입니다—액자만으로도 여러 파운드 값어치가 있는—어떤 부인이든 걸어 두고 자랑스러워할 그림이지요—만약 시 참사회의 어느 신사분께서 자신의 후덕함을 보이고 싶으시다면, 자선 기관의 이른바 식당에 어울리는 물건입니다."], ["Turn it a little, sir? yes.", "조금 돌려 보겠습니까, 선생님?"], ["Joseph, turn it a little towards Mr.", "그래."], ["Ladislaw—Mr. Ladislaw, having been abroad, understands the merit of these things, you observe.”", "조지프, 래디슬로 씨 쪽으로 조금 돌려라—보십시오, 래디슬로 씨께서는 외국에 다녀오신 분이라 이런 것들의 가치를 아십니다.\""]], [["All eyes were for a moment turned towards Will, who said, coolly, “Five pounds.”", "잠시 모든 시선이 윌에게로 향했고, 그는 냉담하게 말했다, \"오 파운드.\""], ["The auctioneer burst out in deep remonstrance.", "경매인이 깊은 항의를 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Mr. Ladislaw!", "래디슬로 씨!"], ["the frame alone is worth that.", "액자만으로도 그만한 값어치가 있습니다."], ["Ladies and gentlemen, for the credit of the town!", "신사 숙녀 여러분, 이 마을의 명예를 위해서라도!"], ["Suppose it should be discovered hereafter that a gem of art has been amongst us in this town, and nobody in Middlemarch awake to it.", "훗날 이 마을에 예술의 보석이 우리 가운데 있었는데, 미들마치의 그 누구도 그것을 알아보지 못했다는 사실이 밝혀진다면 어쩌시겠습니까. 오 기니—오 기니 칠 실링 육 펜스—오 기니 십 실링."], ["Five guineas—five seven-six—five ten.", "그래도, 부인들, 그래도요! 이것은 보석입니다."], ["Still, ladies, still!", "시인이 말했듯 '수많은 보석이'"], ["It is a gem, and ‘Full many a gem,’ as the poet says, has been allowed to go at a nominal price because the public knew no better, because it was offered in circles where there was—I was going to say a low feeling, but no!—Six pounds—six guineas—a Guydo of the first order going at six guineas—it is an insult to religion, ladies; it touches us all as Christians, gentlemen, that a subject like this should go at such a low figure—six pounds ten—seven—”", "헐값에 팔려 나가도록 방치되어 왔으니, 이는 대중이 알아보지 못했기 때문이요, 그것이—저열한 감정이라 말하려다 그만두겠습니다만—아무튼 그런 분위기의 자리에서 내놓였기 때문입니다—육 파운드—육 기니—일급 귀도가 육 기니에 나갑니다—이것은 종교에 대한 모독입니다, 부인들. 이런 주제가 그토록 헐값에 나가다니, 기독교도로서 우리 모두를 건드리는 일입니다, 신사 여러분—육 파운드 십 실링—칠—\""]], [["The bidding was brisk, and Will continued to share in it, remembering that Mrs. Bulstrode had a strong wish for the picture, and thinking that he might stretch the price to twelve pounds.", "호가는 활발했고, 윌은 불스트로드 부인이 그 그림을 간절히 바란다는 것을 떠올리며 계속 거기에 동참했으니, 십이 파운드까지는 값을 올릴 수 있으리라 생각했다."], ["But it was knocked down to him at ten guineas, whereupon he pushed his way towards the bow-window and went out.", "하지만 그것은 십 기니에 그에게 낙찰되었고, 그러자 그는 활 모양 창문 쪽으로 헤치고 나가 밖으로 나왔다."], ["He chose to go under the marquee to get a glass of water, being hot and thirsty: it was empty of other visitors, and he asked the woman in attendance to fetch him some fresh water; but before she was well gone he was annoyed to see entering the florid stranger who had stared at him.", "그는 덥고 목이 말라 물 한 잔을 마시러 천막 안으로 들어가기로 했다. 천막에는 다른 방문객이 없었고, 그는 시중드는 여자에게 신선한 물을 좀 가져다 달라고 청했다."], ["It struck Will at this moment that the man might be one of those political parasitic insects of the bloated kind who had once or twice claimed acquaintance with him as having heard him speak on the Reform question, and who might think of getting a shilling by news.", "하지만 그녀가 미처 나가기도 전에, 자신을 빤히 쳐다보던 그 혈색 좋은 낯선 사람이 들어오는 것이 보여 짜증이 났다. 그 순간 윌은 그자가, 자신이 개혁 문제에 관해 연설하는 것을 들었다며 한두 번 안면을 주장하고는, 소식거리로 한 실링이나 벌어 볼까 궁리하는, 그 부풀어 오른 종류의 정치적 기생충 같은 자들 중 하나일지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["In this light his person, already rather heating to behold on a summer’s day, appeared the more disagreeable; and Will, half-seated on the elbow of a garden-chair, turned his eyes carefully away from the comer.", "그렇게 보니, 여름날 보기만 해도 이미 다소 더워지는 그자의 모습이 더욱 불쾌하게 느껴졌다. 윌은 정원 의자 팔걸이에 반쯤 걸터앉은 채, 그 모퉁이에서 조심스레 눈을 돌렸다."], ["But this signified little to our acquaintance Mr. Raffles, who never hesitated to thrust himself on unwilling observation, if it suited his purpose to do so.", "하지만 그것은 우리의 지인 래플스 씨에게는 별 의미가 없었으니, 그는 자기 목적에 맞기만 하면 내키지 않아 하는 시선 앞에 자신을 들이미는 데 결코 주저하는 법이 없었다."], ["He moved a step or two till he was in front of Will, and said with full-mouthed haste, “Excuse me, Mr. Ladislaw—was your mother’s name Sarah Dunkirk?”", "그는 한두 걸음 옮겨 윌 앞에 서서, 입을 가득 채운 듯한 성급함으로 말했다, \"실례합니다, 래디슬로 씨—어머님 성함이 세라 던커크 아니었습니까?\""]], [["Will, starting to his feet, moved backward a step, frowning, and saying with some fierceness, “Yes, sir, it was.", "윌은 벌떡 일어나 한 걸음 물러서며, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 다소 사납게 말했다, \"그렇소, 선생. 그랬소."], ["And what is that to you?”", "그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오?\""]], [["It was in Will’s nature that the first spark it threw out was a direct answer of the question and a challenge of the consequences.", "처음 튀어나온 불꽃이 질문에 대한 직접적인 대답이자 그 결과에 대한 도전이라는 것이 윌의 천성이었다."], ["To have said, “What is that to you?” in the first instance, would have seemed like shuffling—as if he minded who knew anything about his origin!", "대뜸 \"그게 당신과 무슨 상관이오?\"라고 했다면, 회피하는 것처럼—마치 자신의 출신에 대해 누가 무엇을 알든 신경 쓰는 것처럼 보였을 테니까!"]], [["Raffles on his side had not the same eagerness for a collision which was implied in Ladislaw’s threatening air.", "래플스로 말하자면, 래디슬로의 위협적인 태도가 암시하는 충돌을 그만큼 바라지는 않았다."], ["The slim young fellow with his girl’s complexion looked like a tiger-cat ready to spring on him.", "소녀 같은 살결의 그 호리호리한 젊은이가 당장이라도 덮칠 듯한 살쾡이처럼 보였던 것이다."], ["Under such circumstances Mr. Raffles’s pleasure in annoying his company was kept in abeyance.", "이런 상황에서 일행을 괴롭히는 래플스 씨의 즐거움은 잠시 보류되었다."]], [["“No offence, my good sir, no offence!", "\"악의는 없습니다, 선생, 악의는 없어요!"], ["I only remember your mother—knew her when she was a girl.", "저는 그저 어머님을 기억할 뿐입니다—소녀 시절에 알았지요."], ["But it is your father that you feature, sir.", "하지만 당신이 닮은 것은 아버님입니다, 선생."], ["I had the pleasure of seeing your father too.", "저는 아버님을 뵙는 기쁨도 누렸지요."], ["Parents alive, Mr. Ladislaw?”", "부모님은 살아 계십니까, 래디슬로 씨?\""]], [["“No!”", "\"아니!\""], ["thundered Will, in the same attitude as before.", "윌이 아까와 같은 자세로 우레처럼 내뱉었다."]], [["“Should be glad to do you a service, Mr. Ladislaw—by Jove, I should!", "\"기꺼이 도움을 드리고 싶습니다, 래디슬로 씨—맹세코 그렇고말고요!"], ["Hope to meet again.”", "다시 뵙기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Hereupon Raffles, who had lifted his hat with the last words, turned himself round with a swing of his leg and walked away.", "이 말과 함께 래플스는 마지막 말을 하며 모자를 들어 올렸다가, 다리를 휘저으며 돌아서서 걸어갔다."], ["Will looked after him a moment, and could see that he did not re-enter the auction-room, but appeared to be walking towards the road.", "윌은 잠시 그의 뒷모습을 바라보았는데, 그가 경매장으로 다시 들어가지 않고 길 쪽으로 걸어가는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["For an instant he thought that he had been foolish not to let the man go on talking;—but no!", "순간 그는 그자가 계속 말하도록 내버려 두지 않은 것이 어리석었다는 생각이 들었다—하지만 아니!"], ["on the whole he preferred doing without knowledge from that source.", "대체로 그는 그런 출처에서 나온 지식 없이 지내는 편이 나았다."]], [["Later in the evening, however, Raffles overtook him in the street, and appearing either to have forgotten the roughness of his former reception or to intend avenging it by a forgiving familiarity, greeted him jovially and walked by his side, remarking at first on the pleasantness of the town and neighborhood.", "그러나 저녁 늦게 래플스가 거리에서 그를 따라잡았으니, 앞서 받은 거친 대접을 잊은 듯도 하고 너그러운 친근함으로 그것을 갚으려는 듯도 하게, 그를 쾌활하게 맞이하며 곁에서 나란히 걸었다. 그는 처음에는 마을과 그 일대가 쾌적하다는 말을 했다."], ["Will suspected that the man had been drinking and was considering how to shake him off when Raffles said—", "윌은 그자가 술을 마신 것 같다고 의심하며 어떻게 떼어 낼지 궁리하고 있는데, 래플스가 말했다—"]], [["“I’ve been abroad myself, Mr. Ladislaw—I’ve seen the world—used to parley-vous a little.", "\"저도 외국에 다녀왔지요, 래디슬로 씨—세상 구경을 했답니다—프랑스 말도 좀 했고요."], ["It was at Boulogne I saw your father—a most uncommon likeness you are of him, by Jove!", "제가 아버님을 뵌 것은 불로뉴에서였습니다—맹세코, 아버님을 어쩌면 그리도 빼닮으셨는지!"], ["mouth—nose—eyes—hair turned off your brow just like his—a little in the foreign style.", "입—코—눈—이마에서 넘긴 머리까지 꼭 그분 같군요—약간 외국식으로요."], ["John Bull doesn’t do much of that.", "영국 사람들은 그렇게 잘 안 하지요."], ["But your father was very ill when I saw him.", "하지만 제가 뵈었을 때 아버님은 몹시 편찮으셨습니다."], ["Lord, lord!", "저런, 저런!"], ["hands you might see through.", "손이 비칠 정도였지요."], ["You were a small youngster then.", "그때 당신은 어린 꼬마였습니다."], ["Did he get well?”", "그분은 회복되셨습니까?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니오.\""], ["said Will, curtly.", "윌이 퉁명스레 말했다."]], [["“Ah! Well!", "\"아!"], ["I’ve often wondered what became of your mother.", "그렇군요! 저는 어머님이 어찌 되셨는지 종종 궁금했습니다."], ["She ran away from her friends when she was a young lass—a proud-spirited lass, and pretty, by Jove!", "젊은 처녀 시절에 식구들에게서 도망쳤지요—기상이 높은 처녀였고, 맹세코, 예뻤습니다!"], ["I knew the reason why she ran away,”", "저는 그분이 왜 도망쳤는지 그 이유를 알지요.\""], ["said Raffles, winking slowly as he looked sideways at Will.", "래플스가 윌을 곁눈질하며 천천히 눈을 찡긋했다."]], [["“You know nothing dishonorable of her, sir,”", "\"당신은 어머니에 대해 떳떳치 못한 어떤 것도 알지 못하오, 선생.\""], ["said Will, turning on him rather savagely.", "윌이 다소 사납게 그에게 돌아서며 말했다."], ["But Mr. Raffles just now was not sensitive to shades of manner.", "하지만 래플스 씨는 지금 태도의 미묘한 차이에 둔감했다."]], [["“Not a bit!”", "\"천만에요!\""], ["said he, tossing his head decisively.", "그가 단호하게 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["“She was a little too honorable to like her friends—that was it!”", "\"그분은 식구들을 좋아하기엔 좀 지나치게 떳떳한 분이었지요—그게 다입니다!\""], ["Here Raffles again winked slowly.", "여기서 래플스가 다시 천천히 눈을 찡긋했다."], ["“Lord bless you, I knew all about ’em—a little in what you may call the respectable thieving line—the high style of receiving-house—none of your holes and corners—first-rate.", "\"이거 참, 저는 그들에 대해 다 알고 있었습니다—이를테면 점잖은 도둑질이라 할 만한, 고급 장물 취급소였지요—구질구질한 뒷골목 따위가 아니라—일류였습니다."], ["Slap-up shop, high profits and no mistake.", "으리으리한 가게에, 이문도 두둑하고 틀림없었지요."], ["But Lord!", "하지만 이거 참!"], ["Sarah would have known nothing about it—a dashing young lady she was—fine boarding-school—fit for a lord’s wife—only Archie Duncan threw it at her out of spite, because she would have nothing to do with him.", "세라는 그런 건 아무것도 몰랐을 겁니다—멋들어진 젊은 아가씨였고—훌륭한 기숙 학교를 다녔으며—귀족의 아내가 되기에도 손색이 없었지요—다만 아치 던컨이 앙심을 품고 그 사실을 그분에게 들이댄 겁니다. 그분이 자기를 상대해 주지 않았으니까요."], ["And so she ran away from the whole concern.", "그래서 그분은 그 모든 일에서 도망쳤습니다."], ["I travelled for ’em, sir, in a gentlemanly way—at a high salary.", "저는 그들을 위해 신사답게 외판을 다녔지요, 선생—높은 봉급을 받으며."], ["They didn’t mind her running away at first—godly folks, sir, very godly—and she was for the stage.", "그들은 처음엔 그분이 도망친 것을 개의치 않았습니다—독실한 사람들이었거든요, 선생, 아주 독실했지요—그리고 그분은 무대에 서고자 했습니다."], ["The son was alive then, and the daughter was at a discount.", "그때는 아들이 살아 있었고, 딸은 푸대접을 받았지요."], ["Hallo!", "어이쿠!"], ["here we are at the Blue Bull.", "다 왔군요, 블루 불입니다."], ["What do you say, Mr. Ladislaw?—shall we turn in and have a glass?”", "어떻습니까, 래디슬로 씨?—들어가서 한잔하실까요?\""]], [["“No, I must say good evening,”", "\"아니, 그만 작별 인사를 해야겠소.\""], ["said Will, dashing up a passage which led into Lowick Gate, and almost running to get out of Raffles’s reach.", "윌이 로윅 게이트로 통하는 골목길로 휙 들어서며, 래플스의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 벗어나려 거의 뛰다시피 하며 말했다."]], [["He walked a long while on the Lowick road away from the town, glad of the starlit darkness when it came.", "그는 마을에서 멀어지는 로윅 가도를 오래도록 걸었고, 이윽고 찾아온 별빛 어린 어둠이 반가웠다."], ["He felt as if he had had dirt cast on him amidst shouts of scorn.", "그는 마치 조롱의 외침 속에서 자기 몸에 오물이 끼얹어진 것 같은 느낌이었다."], ["There was this to confirm the fellow’s statement—that his mother never would tell him the reason why she had run away from her family.", "그자의 말을 뒷받침하는 것이 하나 있었으니—어머니가 자기 가족에게서 왜 도망쳤는지 그 이유를 결코 그에게 말해 주지 않았다는 사실이었다."]], [["Well!", "좋다!"], ["what was he, Will Ladislaw, the worse, supposing the truth about that family to be the ugliest?", "그 가족에 대한 진실이 가장 추악한 것이라 한들, 그, 윌 래디슬로가 그것으로 무엇이 더 나빠진단 말인가?"], ["His mother had braved hardship in order to separate herself from it.", "어머니는 그것에서 자신을 떼어 놓기 위해 고난을 무릅썼다."], ["But if Dorothea’s friends had known this story—if the Chettams had known it—they would have had a fine color to give their suspicions a welcome ground for thinking him unfit to come near her.", "하지만 도러시아의 친지들이 이 이야기를 알았더라면—체텀 집안이 알았더라면—그들은 자신들의 의심에 그럴듯한 빛깔을 입혀, 그가 그녀에게 가까이 갈 자격이 없다고 여길 반가운 근거로 삼았을 것이다."], ["However, let them suspect what they pleased, they would find themselves in the wrong.", "그러나 그들이야 좋을 대로 의심하라지, 그들은 자신들이 틀렸음을 알게 될 터였다."], ["They would find out that the blood in his veins was as free from the taint of meanness as theirs.", "그들은 그의 핏줄에 흐르는 피가 자신들의 것만큼이나 비열함의 오점에서 자유롭다는 것을 알게 될 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXI.", "# CHAPTER LXI."]], [["“Inconsistencies,”", "“모순은,”"], ["answered Imlac, “cannot both be right, but imputed to man they may both be true.”—Rasselas.", "임락이 대답했다, “둘 다 옳을 수는 없지만, 인간에게 돌린다면 둘 다 참일 수는 있다네.”—『라셀라스』."]], [["The same night, when Mr.", "* 『라셀라스』: 새뮤얼 존슨의 철학 소설 『아비시니아의 왕자 라셀라스 이야기』(1759)."], ["Bulstrode returned from a journey to Brassing on business, his good wife met him in the entrance-hall and drew him into his private sitting-room.", "같은 날 밤, 불스트로드 씨가 사업차 브래싱에 다녀와 돌아왔을 때, 그의 착한 아내가 현관 홀에서 그를 맞이하여 그의 사적인 거실로 데리고 들어갔다."]], [["“Nicholas,”", "“니컬러스,” 그녀는 정직한 눈으로 그를 근심스럽게 바라보며 말했다."], ["she said, fixing her honest eyes upon him anxiously, “there has been such a disagreeable man here asking for you—it has made me quite uncomfortable.”", "“여기 어떤 아주 불쾌한 남자가 와서 당신을 찾았어요. 그래서 제가 무척 불편했답니다.”"]], [["“What kind of man, my dear,” said Mr.", "“어떤 부류의 사람이었소, 여보,”"], ["Bulstrode, dreadfully certain of the answer.", "불스트로드 씨는 끔찍하게도 대답을 확신하며 말했다."]], [["“A red-faced man with large whiskers, and most impudent in his manner.", "“얼굴이 벌겋고 구레나룻이 큰 사람인데, 태도가 더없이 무례했어요."], ["He declared he was an old friend of yours, and said you would be sorry not to see him.", "자기가 당신의 오랜 친구라고 주장하면서, 자기를 만나지 못하면 당신이 섭섭해할 거라고 했어요."], ["He wanted to wait for you here, but I told him he could see you at the Bank to-morrow morning.", "여기서 당신을 기다리겠다고 하길래, 내일 아침 은행에서 만날 수 있을 거라고 제가 말해 줬지요. 어찌나 무례하던지!—저를 빤히 쳐다보면서, 자기 친구 닉은 아내 복이 있다고 하지 뭐예요."], ["Most impudent he was!—stared at me, and said his friend Nick had luck in wives.", "마침 블뤼허가 사슬을 끊고 자갈밭으로 달려 나오지 않았더라면 그 사람은 가지 않았을 거예요—제가 정원에 있었거든요."], ["I don’t believe he would have gone away, if Blucher had not happened to break his chain and come running round on the gravel—for I was in the garden; so I said, ‘You’d better go away—the dog is very fierce, and I can’t hold him.’ Do you really know anything of such a man?”", "그래서 제가 ‘가시는 게 좋겠어요—개가 아주 사나운데, 제가 붙잡을 수가 없네요’라고 했지요. 정말로 그런 사람을 아세요?”"]], [["“I believe I know who he is, my dear,” said Mr.", "“그가 누군지 알 것 같소, 여보,”"], ["Bulstrode, in his usual subdued voice, “an unfortunate dissolute wretch, whom I helped too much in days gone by.", "불스트로드 씨가 평소의 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다. “지난날 내가 너무 많이 도와주었던 불운하고 방탕한 가련한 자라오."], ["However, I presume you will not be troubled by him again.", "하지만 다시는 그자가 당신을 괴롭히지 않을 거요."], ["He will probably come to the Bank—to beg, doubtless.”", "아마 은행으로 올 테지—구걸하러, 틀림없이.”"]], [["No more was said on the subject until the next day, when Mr. Bulstrode had returned from the town and was dressing for dinner.", "이 일에 관해서는 다음 날까지 더 이상 말이 오가지 않았는데, 그날 불스트로드 씨가 읍내에서 돌아와 만찬을 위해 옷을 갈아입던 참이었다."], ["His wife, not sure that he was come home, looked into his dressing-room and saw him with his coat and cravat off, leaning one arm on a chest of drawers and staring absently at the ground.", "그의 아내는 그가 집에 돌아왔는지 확신하지 못하고 그의 화장실을 들여다보았다가, 그가 윗도리와 크라바트를 벗은 채 한쪽 팔을 서랍장에 기대고 멍하니 바닥을 응시하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He started nervously and looked up as she entered.", "그녀가 들어서자 그는 신경질적으로 흠칫 놀라며 고개를 들었다."]], [["“You look very ill, Nicholas.", "“무척 안 좋아 보여요, 니컬러스."], ["Is there anything the matter?”", "무슨 일이라도 있어요?”"]], [["“I have a good deal of pain in my head,”", "“머리가 몹시 아프오,” 불스트로드 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Bulstrode, who was so frequently ailing that his wife was always ready to believe in this cause of depression.", "그가 워낙 자주 앓았기에 그의 아내는 이런 우울의 원인을 늘 믿을 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["“Sit down and let me sponge it with vinegar.”", "“앉으세요, 식초로 좀 닦아 드릴게요.”"]], [["Physically Mr. Bulstrode did not want the vinegar, but morally the affectionate attention soothed him.", "육체적으로는 불스트로드 씨가 식초를 원하지 않았으나, 정신적으로는 그 애정 어린 보살핌이 그를 달래 주었다."], ["Though always polite, it was his habit to receive such services with marital coolness, as his wife’s duty.", "늘 정중하기는 했으되, 그런 봉사를 부부간의 무덤덤함으로, 아내의 의무로 받아들이는 것이 그의 습관이었다."], ["But to-day, while she was bending over him, he said, “You are very good, Harriet,”", "그러나 오늘은, 그녀가 자기 위로 몸을 굽히고 있을 때, 그가 “당신은 참 착하구려, 해리엇,”"], ["in a tone which had something new in it to her ear; she did not know exactly what the novelty was, but her woman’s solicitude shaped itself into a darting thought that he might be going to have an illness.", "하고 말했는데, 그 어조에는 그녀의 귀에 무언가 새로운 것이 담겨 있었다. 그녀는 그 새로움이 정확히 무엇인지 알지 못했지만, 그녀의 여성다운 염려가 그가 병이 나려는 것일지도 모른다는 화살 같은 생각으로 빚어졌다."]], [["“Has anything worried you?”", "“무슨 걱정거리라도 있었어요?”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Did that man come to you at the Bank?”", "“그 사람이 은행으로 당신을 찾아왔어요?”"]], [["“Yes; it was as I had supposed.", "“그렇소. 내가 짐작했던 그대로였소."], ["He is a man who at one time might have done better.", "한때는 더 나은 사람이 될 수도 있었을 자였소."], ["But he has sunk into a drunken debauched creature.”", "하지만 술에 절은 방탕한 자로 전락하고 말았소.”"]], [["“Is he quite gone away?” said Mrs.", "“완전히 떠나갔나요?”"], ["Bulstrode, anxiously; but for certain reasons she refrained from adding, “It was very disagreeable to hear him calling himself a friend of yours.”", "불스트로드 부인이 근심스럽게 말했다. 하지만 어떤 이유에서 그녀는 “그가 자기를 당신의 친구라고 부르는 걸 듣기가 무척 불쾌했어요”라는 말을 덧붙이는 것을 삼갔다."], ["At that moment she would not have liked to say anything which implied her habitual consciousness that her husband’s earlier connections were not quite on a level with her own.", "그 순간 그녀는 남편의 옛 인연들이 자기 쪽 사람들과 그리 동등하지 않다는 자신의 습관적인 의식을 내비치는 어떤 말도 하고 싶지 않았던 것이다."], ["Not that she knew much about them.", "사실 그녀가 그 인연들에 관해 많이 아는 것도 아니었다."], ["That her husband had at first been employed in a bank, that he had afterwards entered into what he called city business and gained a fortune before he was three-and-thirty, that he had married a widow who was much older than himself—a Dissenter, and in other ways probably of that disadvantageous quality usually perceptible in a first wife if inquired into with the dispassionate judgment of a second—was almost as much as she had cared to learn beyond the glimpses which Mr. Bulstrode’s narrative occasionally gave of his early bent towards religion, his inclination to be a preacher, and his association with missionary and philanthropic efforts.", "남편이 처음에는 은행에서 일했다는 것, 그 후 그가 도시 사업이라고 부르는 일에 뛰어들어 서른세 살이 되기 전에 재산을 모았다는 것, 그가 자기보다 훨씬 나이 많은 과부와 결혼했다는 것—그 여자는 비국교도였고, 다른 면에서도 아마 두 번째 아내의 냉정한 판단으로 따져 보면 첫 번째 아내에게서 으레 감지되는 그 불리한 자질을 지녔으리라는 것—이 정도가, 불스트로드 씨의 이야기가 이따금 비춰 준 어린 시절의 종교적 성향, 설교자가 되고 싶어 했던 그의 기질, 그리고 선교와 자선 활동과의 연관에 대한 단편들 외에, 그녀가 알고자 한 거의 전부였다."], ["She believed in him as an excellent man whose piety carried a peculiar eminence in belonging to a layman, whose influence had turned her own mind toward seriousness, and whose share of perishable good had been the means of raising her own position.", "그녀는 그를 훌륭한 사람으로 믿었으니, 평신도로서 그가 지닌 경건함은 특별한 탁월함을 띠었고, 그의 영향력이 그녀 자신의 마음을 진지함으로 돌려놓았으며, 그가 지닌 덧없는 재물의 몫이 그녀 자신의 지위를 끌어올리는 수단이 되었던 것이다."], ["But she also liked to think that it was well in every sense for Mr. Bulstrode to have won the hand of Harriet Vincy; whose family was undeniable in a Middlemarch light—a better light surely than any thrown in London thoroughfares or dissenting chapel-yards.", "하지만 그녀는 또한 불스트로드 씨가 해리엇 빈시의 손을 얻은 것이 모든 의미에서 잘된 일이라고 생각하기를 좋아했으니, 그녀의 집안은 미들마치의 시각에서 보면 부인할 수 없이 떳떳했고—그것은 분명 런던의 큰길이나 비국교도 예배당 뜰에 드리워진 그 어떤 빛보다도 더 나은 빛이었다."], ["The unreformed provincial mind distrusted London; and while true religion was everywhere saving, honest Mrs. Bulstrode was convinced that to be saved in the Church was more respectable.", "개혁되지 않은 지방의 정신은 런던을 불신했고, 참된 종교는 어디서든 사람을 구원하는 것이지만, 정직한 불스트로드 부인은 국교회 안에서 구원받는 것이 더 점잖다고 확신했다."], ["She so much wished to ignore towards others that her husband had ever been a London Dissenter, that she liked to keep it out of sight even in talking to him.", "그녀는 남편이 한때 런던의 비국교도였다는 사실을 남들에게 알리지 않고 싶은 마음이 너무도 컸기에, 그에게 이야기할 때조차 그것을 시야 밖에 두기를 좋아했다."], ["He was quite aware of this; indeed in some respects he was rather afraid of this ingenuous wife, whose imitative piety and native worldliness were equally sincere, who had nothing to be ashamed of, and whom he had married out of a thorough inclination still subsisting.", "그도 이 점을 잘 알고 있었으니, 사실 어떤 면에서는 그가 이 순진한 아내를 다소 두려워하기까지 했던 것인데, 그녀의 모방적인 경건함과 타고난 세속성은 똑같이 진실했고, 그녀에게는 부끄러워할 것이 아무것도 없었으며, 그가 여전히 지속되는 진정한 애정에서 그녀와 결혼했던 것이다."], ["But his fears were such as belong to a man who cares to maintain his recognized supremacy: the loss of high consideration from his wife, as from every one else who did not clearly hate him out of enmity to the truth, would be as the beginning of death to him.", "하지만 그의 두려움은 자신이 인정받는 우위를 유지하고 싶어 하는 사람에게 속하는 그런 종류였으니, 진리에 대한 적의에서 그를 분명히 미워하는 사람이 아닌 모든 이로부터 받는 것과 마찬가지로 아내로부터 받는 높은 존경을 잃는다면, 그것은 그에게 죽음의 시작과도 같을 것이었다."], ["When she said—", "그녀가 이렇게 말했을 때—"]], [["“Is he quite gone away?”", "“완전히 떠나갔나요?”"]], [["“Oh, I trust so,”", "“오, 그러기를 바라오,”"], ["he answered, with an effort to throw as much sober unconcern into his tone as possible!", "그가 가능한 한 차분한 무관심을 어조에 담으려 애쓰며 대답했다!"]], [["But in truth Mr. Bulstrode was very far from a state of quiet trust.", "하지만 진실로 불스트로드 씨는 고요한 신뢰의 상태와는 거리가 멀었다."], ["In the interview at the Bank, Raffles had made it evident that his eagerness to torment was almost as strong in him as any other greed.", "은행에서의 면담에서 래플스는 자신의 괴롭히려는 열망이 다른 어떤 탐욕 못지않게 강하다는 것을 분명히 보여 주었다."], ["He had frankly said that he had turned out of the way to come to Middlemarch, just to look about him and see whether the neighborhood would suit him to live in.", "그는 그저 주위를 둘러보고 이 동네가 자기가 살기에 어울리는지 살펴보려고 일부러 길을 돌아 미들마치로 왔노라고 솔직하게 말했다."], ["He had certainly had a few debts to pay more than he expected, but the two hundred pounds were not gone yet: a cool five-and-twenty would suffice him to go away with for the present.", "분명 그는 예상보다 갚아야 할 빚이 좀 더 있었지만, 이백 파운드가 아직 다 사라진 것은 아니었다. 당장은 떠날 밑천으로 깔끔하게 이십오 파운드면 충분할 터였다."], ["What he had wanted chiefly was to see his friend Nick and family, and know all about the prosperity of a man to whom he was so much attached.", "그가 무엇보다 원했던 것은 친구 닉과 그 가족을 보는 것, 그리고 자기가 그토록 애정을 품은 사람의 번영에 관해 죄다 아는 것이었다."], ["By-and-by he might come back for a longer stay.", "머지않아 더 오래 머물기 위해 다시 올지도 모를 일이었다."], ["This time Raffles declined to be “seen off the premises,” as he expressed it—declined to quit Middlemarch under Bulstrode’s eyes.", "이번에 래플스는 자기 표현대로 “부지 밖으로 배웅받기”를 거부했다—불스트로드의 눈앞에서 미들마치를 떠나기를 거부한 것이다."], ["He meant to go by coach the next day—if he chose.", "그는 내키면 다음 날 마차로 떠날 작정이었다."]], [["Bulstrode felt himself helpless.", "불스트로드는 자신이 무력하다고 느꼈다."], ["Neither threats nor coaxing could avail: he could not count on any persistent fear nor on any promise.", "위협도 회유도 소용이 없었다. 그는 어떤 지속적인 두려움도, 어떤 약속도 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["On the contrary, he felt a cold certainty at his heart that Raffles—unless providence sent death to hinder him—would come back to Middlemarch before long.", "도리어 그는 래플스가—섭리가 죽음을 보내 그를 막지 않는 한—머지않아 미들마치로 돌아오리라는 차가운 확신을 가슴에 품었다."], ["And that certainty was a terror.", "그리고 그 확신은 공포였다."]], [["It was not that he was in danger of legal punishment or of beggary: he was in danger only of seeing disclosed to the judgment of his neighbors and the mournful perception of his wife certain facts of his past life which would render him an object of scorn and an opprobrium of the religion with which he had diligently associated himself. The terror of being judged sharpens the memory: it sends an inevitable glare over that long-unvisited past which has been habitually recalled only in general phrases. Even without memory, the life is bound into one by a zone of dependence in growth and decay; but intense memory forces a man to own his blameworthy past. With memory set smarting like a reopened wound, a man’s past is not simply a dead history, an outworn preparation of the present: it is not a repented error shaken loose from the life: it is a still quivering part of himself, bringing shudders and bitter flavors and the tinglings of a merited shame.", "그가 법적 처벌이나 거지 신세의 위험에 처한 것은 아니었다. 그가 처한 위험은 오직 자기 과거 삶의 어떤 사실들이 이웃들의 판단과 아내의 슬픈 인식 앞에 드러나는 것이었으니, 그것은 그를 경멸의 대상으로, 그가 부지런히 결부해 온 종교의 치욕으로 만들어 버릴 터였다. 심판받는 공포는 기억을 날카롭게 한다. 그것은 오랫동안 일반적인 어구로만 습관적으로 떠올려 온 그 오래도록 찾지 않던 과거 위로 피할 수 없는 섬광을 던진다. 기억이 없더라도, 삶은 성장과 쇠퇴 속에서 의존의 띠로 하나로 엮인다. 하지만 강렬한 기억은 인간으로 하여금 자신의 비난받을 만한 과거를 인정하게 만든다. 다시 벌어진 상처처럼 쓰라리게 자극받은 기억과 함께, 인간의 과거는 단순히 죽은 역사가, 현재의 낡아 빠진 준비 과정이 아니다. 그것은 삶에서 떨어져 나간, 뉘우친 과오가 아니다. 그것은 여전히 떨고 있는 자기 자신의 일부로서, 전율과 쓰디쓴 맛과 마땅히 받아야 할 수치심의 따끔거림을 불러일으킨다."]], [["Into this second life Bulstrode’s past had now risen, only the pleasures of it seeming to have lost their quality.", "이 두 번째 삶 속으로 이제 불스트로드의 과거가 솟아올랐는데, 다만 그 쾌락들만은 본래의 성질을 잃어버린 듯했다."], ["Night and day, without interruption save of brief sleep which only wove retrospect and fear into a fantastic present, he felt the scenes of his earlier life coming between him and everything else, as obstinately as when we look through the window from a lighted room, the objects we turn our backs on are still before us, instead of the grass and the trees.", "짧은 잠—그조차도 회고와 두려움을 환상적인 현재 속으로 짜 넣을 뿐이었다—으로 끊기는 것 말고는 밤낮으로 쉼 없이, 그는 자신의 옛 삶의 장면들이 자기와 다른 모든 것 사이를 가로막고 들어서는 것을 느꼈다. 마치 불 켜진 방에서 창문을 통해 내다볼 때, 우리가 등을 돌린 사물들이 풀과 나무 대신 여전히 우리 앞에 어른거리듯, 그처럼 집요하게."], ["The successive events inward and outward were there in one view: though each might be dwelt on in turn, the rest still kept their hold in the consciousness.", "안팎으로 잇따른 사건들이 한눈에 펼쳐졌으니, 비록 그것들을 차례로 곱씹을 수는 있었어도 나머지는 여전히 의식 속에서 그 자리를 지키고 있었다."]], [["Once more he saw himself the young banker’s clerk, with an agreeable person, as clever in figures as he was fluent in speech and fond of theological definition: an eminent though young member of a Calvinistic dissenting church at Highbury, having had striking experience in conviction of sin and sense of pardon.", "다시 한번 그는 자신이 호감 가는 풍채를 지닌 젊은 은행원이었던 시절을 보았다. 숫자에 영민한 만큼이나 말솜씨도 유창했고 신학적 정의를 즐겨 따지던, 죄에 대한 통회와 사면의 감각에서 놀라운 체험을 겪은, 하이베리의 한 칼뱅파 비국교회의 젊으면서도 저명한 회원이었던 자신을."], ["Again he heard himself called for as Brother Bulstrode in prayer meetings, speaking on religious platforms, preaching in private houses.", "다시 그는 기도 모임에서 ‘불스트로드 형제’로 불려 나가, 종교 단상에서 연설하고, 개인 가정집에서 설교하는 자신의 목소리를 들었다."], ["Again he felt himself thinking of the ministry as possibly his vocation, and inclined towards missionary labor.", "다시 그는 성직이 어쩌면 자신의 천직일지도 모른다고 생각하며 선교 사역에 마음이 기울던 자신을 느꼈다."], ["That was the happiest time of his life: that was the spot he would have chosen now to awake in and find the rest a dream.", "그때가 그의 생애에서 가장 행복한 시절이었으니, 이제라면 그가 깨어나 나머지가 한낱 꿈이었음을 발견하고 싶은 곳도 바로 그 지점이었다."], ["The people among whom Brother Bulstrode was distinguished were very few, but they were very near to him, and stirred his satisfaction the more; his power stretched through a narrow space, but he felt its effect the more intensely.", "불스트로드 형제가 두각을 나타내던 사람들은 아주 적었지만, 그들은 그에게 무척 가까웠고 그래서 그의 만족감을 더욱 자극했다. 그의 영향력은 좁은 공간에 미쳤지만, 그래서 그는 그 효과를 더욱 강렬하게 느꼈다."], ["He believed without effort in the peculiar work of grace within him, and in the signs that God intended him for special instrumentality.", "그는 자기 안에서 이루어지는 은총의 독특한 역사(役事)를, 그리고 하느님이 자기를 특별한 도구로 삼으려 하신다는 징표를 힘들이지 않고 믿었다."]], [["Then came the moment of transition; it was with the sense of promotion he had when he, an orphan educated at a commercial charity-school, was invited to a fine villa belonging to Mr.", "이윽고 전환의 순간이 왔다. 상업 자선 학교에서 교육받은 고아였던 그가 신도들 가운데 가장 부유한 사람인 던커크 씨 소유의 멋진 별장으로 초대받았을 때, 그는 승진의 감각을 맛보았다."], ["Dunkirk, the richest man in the congregation. Soon he became an intimate there, honored for his piety by the wife, marked out for his ability by the husband, whose wealth was due to a flourishing city and west-end trade.", "곧 그는 그곳의 친밀한 사람이 되어, 아내에게는 경건함으로 존경받고, 번성하는 도심과 웨스트엔드 무역으로 부를 일군 남편에게는 능력으로 점찍혔다."], ["That was the setting-in of a new current for his ambition, directing his prospects of “instrumentality” towards the uniting of distinguished religious gifts with successful business.", "그것이 그의 야망에 새로운 물줄기가 흘러들기 시작한 계기였으니, ‘도구가 되리라’는 그의 전망을 빼어난 종교적 재능과 성공적인 사업의 결합 쪽으로 향하게 했다."]], [["By-and-by came a decided external leading: a confidential subordinate partner died, and nobody seemed to the principal so well fitted to fill the severely felt vacancy as his young friend Bulstrode, if he would become confidential accountant.", "머지않아 분명한 외적 인도가 찾아왔다. 신임받던 부수적 동업자가 죽었는데, 주인이 보기에 절실히 느껴지는 그 공백을 메우기에 젊은 친구 불스트로드만큼 적합한 사람이 없어 보였던 것이니, 다만 그가 신임받는 회계사가 되어 준다면 말이었다."], ["The offer was accepted.", "제안은 받아들여졌다."], ["The business was a pawnbroker’s, of the most magnificent sort both in extent and profits; and on a short acquaintance with it Bulstrode became aware that one source of magnificent profit was the easy reception of any goods offered, without strict inquiry as to where they came from.", "그 사업은 전당포로서, 규모로 보나 수익으로 보나 더없이 호화로운 종류였다. 그리고 그것을 잠시 겪어 보고서 불스트로드는 그 호화로운 수익의 한 원천이, 물건이 어디서 왔는지 엄밀히 따지지 않고 제공되는 어떤 물건이든 손쉽게 받아들이는 데 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["But there was a branch house at the west end, and no pettiness or dinginess to give suggestions of shame.", "하지만 웨스트엔드에 지점이 있었으니, 수치심을 떠올리게 할 옹졸함이나 추레함은 전혀 없었다."]], [["He remembered his first moments of shrinking.", "그는 처음 움츠러들던 순간들을 기억했다."], ["They were private, and were filled with arguments; some of these taking the form of prayer.", "그것들은 은밀했고, 갖가지 논변으로 채워져 있었으니, 그중 일부는 기도의 형태를 띠었다."], ["The business was established and had old roots; is it not one thing to set up a new gin-palace and another to accept an investment in an old one?", "사업은 이미 자리 잡았고 오래된 뿌리를 두고 있었다. 새 진 술집을 차리는 것과 오래된 진 술집에 투자를 받아들이는 것은 다른 일이 아니던가?"], ["The profits made out of lost souls—where can the line be drawn at which they begin in human transactions?", "길 잃은 영혼들에게서 나온 수익—인간의 거래에서 그것이 시작되는 경계선을 어디에 그을 수 있단 말인가?"], ["Was it not even God’s way of saving His chosen?", "그것이야말로 하느님께서 당신의 선택받은 자들을 구원하시는 방식이 아니던가?"], ["“Thou knowest,”—the young Bulstrode had said then, as the older Bulstrode was saying now—“Thou knowest how loose my soul sits from these things—how I view them all as implements for tilling Thy garden rescued here and there from the wilderness.”", "“당신께서는 아시나이다”—젊은 불스트로드는 그때 이렇게 말했고, 나이 든 불스트로드도 지금 이렇게 말하고 있었다—“제 영혼이 이 모든 것들에서 얼마나 헐겁게 떨어져 앉아 있는지를, 제가 그것들을 모두 황무지에서 여기저기 구해 낸 당신의 정원을 일구는 도구로 여기고 있음을 당신께서는 아시나이다.”"]], [["Metaphors and precedents were not wanting; peculiar spiritual experiences were not wanting which at last made the retention of his position seem a service demanded of him: the vista of a fortune had already opened itself, and Bulstrode’s shrinking remained private.", "비유와 선례는 모자라지 않았고, 마침내 그 지위를 지키는 것이 자기에게 요구된 봉사처럼 보이게 만든 독특한 영적 체험들도 모자라지 않았다. 재산의 전망은 이미 펼쳐졌고, 불스트로드의 움츠러듦은 여전히 은밀한 것으로 남았다."], ["Mr. Dunkirk had never expected that there would be any shrinking at all: he had never conceived that trade had anything to do with the scheme of salvation.", "던커크 씨는 애초에 움츠러듦이라는 것이 조금이라도 있으리라고는 예상하지 못했으니, 그는 무역이 구원의 도식과 무슨 관계가 있다고는 결코 생각해 본 적이 없었다."], ["And it was true that Bulstrode found himself carrying on two distinct lives; his religious activity could not be incompatible with his business as soon as he had argued himself into not feeling it incompatible.", "그리고 불스트로드가 두 개의 뚜렷이 구별되는 삶을 영위하고 있다는 것도 사실이었다. 일단 그것이 양립할 수 없다고 느끼지 않도록 스스로를 논변으로 설득해 버리자, 그의 종교 활동은 그의 사업과 양립할 수 없는 것이 아니게 되었다."]], [["Mentally surrounded with that past again, Bulstrode had the same pleas—indeed, the years had been perpetually spinning them into intricate thickness, like masses of spider-web, padding the moral sensibility; nay, as age made egoism more eager but less enjoying, his soul had become more saturated with the belief that he did everything for God’s sake, being indifferent to it for his own.", "마음속으로 다시 그 과거에 둘러싸인 불스트로드는 똑같은 변명들을 가지고 있었다—실로 세월은 거미줄 뭉치처럼 그것들을 끊임없이 복잡하게 두껍도록 자아내, 도덕적 감수성에 두툼한 덧대기를 해 왔던 것이다. 아니, 나이가 자아를 더 갈급하되 덜 즐기게 만들수록, 그의 영혼은 자기가 모든 것을 하느님을 위해 행한다는, 자기 자신을 위해서는 그것에 무관심하다는 믿음으로 더욱 흠뻑 젖어 들었다."], ["And yet—if he could be back in that far-off spot with his youthful poverty—why, then he would choose to be a missionary.", "그러나—만약 그가 젊은 날의 가난과 함께 그 머나먼 지점으로 돌아갈 수 있다면—그렇다면, 그때는 그가 선교사가 되기를 택할 것이었다."]], [["But the train of causes in which he had locked himself went on.", "하지만 그가 스스로를 가두어 넣은 원인들의 사슬은 계속 이어졌다."], ["There was trouble in the fine villa at Highbury.", "하이베리의 멋진 별장에 우환이 생겼다."], ["Years before, the only daughter had run away, defied her parents, and gone on the stage; and now the only boy died, and after a short time Mr.", "여러 해 전, 외동딸이 부모에게 반항하며 달아나 무대에 섰고, 이제 외아들이 죽었으며, 얼마 후 던커크 씨마저 죽었다."], ["Dunkirk died also. The wife, a simple pious woman, left with all the wealth in and out of the magnificent trade, of which she never knew the precise nature, had come to believe in Bulstrode, and innocently adore him as women often adore their priest or “man-made”", "아내는 소박하고 경건한 여인으로, 그 정확한 성질을 결코 알지 못했던 그 호화로운 무역의 안팎에 든 온갖 재물과 함께 홀로 남겨졌는데, 그녀는 불스트로드를 신뢰하게 되었고, 여인들이 흔히 자기네 사제나 ‘사람이 세운’ 목사를 흠모하듯 순진하게 그를 흠모했다."], ["minister. It was natural that after a time marriage should have been thought of between them.", "얼마 후 두 사람 사이에 결혼이 거론되게 된 것은 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["But Mrs. Dunkirk had qualms and yearnings about her daughter, who had long been regarded as lost both to God and her parents.", "하지만 던커크 부인은 자기 딸에 대해 불안과 그리움을 품고 있었으니, 그 딸은 오래도록 하느님께도 부모에게도 잃어버린 자로 여겨져 왔다."], ["It was known that the daughter had married, but she was utterly gone out of sight.", "딸이 결혼했다는 것은 알려져 있었지만, 그녀는 완전히 종적을 감추었다."], ["The mother, having lost her boy, imagined a grandson, and wished in a double sense to reclaim her daughter.", "아들을 잃은 어머니는 손자를 상상하며, 이중의 의미로 딸을 되찾기를 바랐다."], ["If she were found, there would be a channel for property—perhaps a wide one—in the provision for several grandchildren.", "만약 딸을 찾는다면, 여러 손주들을 위한 마련으로—어쩌면 넓은—재산의 물길이 트일 터였다."], ["Efforts to find her must be made before Mrs. Dunkirk would marry again.", "던커크 부인이 재혼하기 전에 반드시 딸을 찾으려는 노력이 이루어져야 했다."], ["Bulstrode concurred; but after advertisement as well as other modes of inquiry had been tried, the mother believed that her daughter was not to be found, and consented to marry without reservation of property.", "불스트로드도 동의했다. 하지만 광고를 비롯한 다른 탐문 방법들이 시도된 끝에, 어머니는 딸을 찾을 수 없다고 믿게 되었고, 재산의 유보 없이 결혼하기로 승낙했다."]], [["The daughter had been found; but only one man besides Bulstrode knew it, and he was paid for keeping silence and carrying himself away.", "그 딸은 찾아진 상태였다. 다만 불스트로드 외에 단 한 사람만이 그것을 알았고, 그는 침묵을 지키고 멀리 떠나는 대가로 돈을 받았다."]], [["That was the bare fact which Bulstrode was now forced to see in the rigid outline with which acts present themselves to onlookers.", "그것이 이제 불스트로드가 보지 않을 수 없게 된 적나라한 사실이었으니, 행위가 구경꾼에게 제 모습을 드러낼 때 띠는 그 빳빳한 윤곽 그대로였다."], ["But for himself at that distant time, and even now in burning memory, the fact was broken into little sequences, each justified as it came by reasonings which seemed to prove it righteous.", "하지만 그 먼 시절의 그 자신에게는, 그리고 지금 불타는 기억 속에서조차도, 그 사실은 자잘한 토막들로 쪼개져 있었고, 토막마다 닥쳐올 때마다 그것이 의롭다고 입증하는 듯한 논리들로 정당화되었다."], ["Bulstrode’s course up to that time had, he thought, been sanctioned by remarkable providences, appearing to point the way for him to be the agent in making the best use of a large property and withdrawing it from perversion.", "그때까지 불스트로드가 걸어온 길은, 그가 생각하기에, 놀라운 섭리들로 재가받은 것이었으니, 그 섭리들은 큰 재산을 가장 잘 쓰이게 하고 그것을 그릇된 용도에서 거두어들이는 일에 그가 대리인이 되도록 길을 가리키는 듯했다."], ["Death and other striking dispositions, such as feminine trustfulness, had come; and Bulstrode would have adopted Cromwell’s words—“Do you call these bare events?", "죽음과 그 밖의 두드러진 처분들이, 이를테면 여성다운 신뢰 같은 것들이 찾아왔던 것이다. 불스트로드라면 크롬웰의 말을 받아들였을 것이었다—“이것들을 한낱 사건이라 부르는가?"], ["The Lord pity you!”", "주께서 그대를 가엾이 여기시기를!”"], ["The events were comparatively small, but the essential condition was there—namely, that they were in favor of his own ends.", "사건들은 비교적 작은 것이었지만, 본질적인 조건은 거기 있었으니—다시 말해, 그것들이 자기 자신의 목적에 유리했다는 것이다."], ["It was easy for him to settle what was due from him to others by inquiring what were God’s intentions with regard to himself.", "자기 자신에 관한 하느님의 뜻이 무엇인지 물어봄으로써 자기가 남들에게 갚아야 할 것이 무엇인지 정하는 일은 그에게 쉬웠다."], ["Could it be for God’s service that this fortune should in any considerable proportion go to a young woman and her husband who were given up to the lightest pursuits, and might scatter it abroad in triviality—people who seemed to lie outside the path of remarkable providences?", "가장 가벼운 일들에 몸을 내맡긴, 그것을 하찮은 데 흩뿌려 버릴지도 모를 한 젊은 여인과 그 남편에게—놀라운 섭리의 길 밖에 놓여 있는 듯한 사람들에게—이 재산이 상당한 비율로 돌아가는 것이 하느님을 섬기는 일일 수 있겠는가?"], ["Bulstrode had never said to himself beforehand, “The daughter shall not be found”—nevertheless when the moment came he kept her existence hidden; and when other moments followed, he soothed the mother with consolation in the probability that the unhappy young woman might be no more.", "불스트로드는 미리 “그 딸을 찾아내지 말지어다”라고 스스로에게 말한 적은 결코 없었다. 그럼에도 그 순간이 닥치자 그는 그녀의 존재를 숨겼고, 다른 순간들이 뒤따르자 그 불행한 젊은 여인이 더는 살아 있지 않을지도 모른다는 위안으로 어머니를 달랬다."]], [["There were hours in which Bulstrode felt that his action was unrighteous; but how could he go back?", "불스트로드가 자기 행위가 의롭지 않다고 느끼는 시간들도 있었다. 하지만 어떻게 되돌아갈 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He had mental exercises, called himself nought, laid hold on redemption, and went on in his course of instrumentality.", "그는 영적 수련을 했고, 스스로를 아무것도 아닌 자라 일컬었으며, 구속(救贖)을 붙들고, 도구가 되는 자기 길을 계속 걸었다."], ["And after five years Death again came to widen his path, by taking away his wife.", "그리고 오 년 후, 죽음이 다시 찾아와 그의 아내를 데려감으로써 그의 길을 넓혀 주었다."], ["He did gradually withdraw his capital, but he did not make the sacrifices requisite to put an end to the business, which was carried on for thirteen years afterwards before it finally collapsed.", "그는 점차 자기 자본을 빼냈지만, 사업을 끝내는 데 필요한 희생은 치르지 않았으니, 그 사업은 그 후로도 십삼 년 동안 이어지다가 마침내 무너졌다."], ["Meanwhile Nicholas Bulstrode had used his hundred thousand discreetly, and was become provincially, solidly important—a banker, a Churchman, a public benefactor; also a sleeping partner in trading concerns, in which his ability was directed to economy in the raw material, as in the case of the dyes which rotted Mr.", "그러는 동안 니컬러스 불스트로드는 자신의 십만 파운드를 신중하게 굴려, 지방에서 견실하게 중요한 인물이 되었다—은행가, 국교회 신자, 공공 자선가로. 또한 그는 여러 무역 사업의 익명 동업자이기도 했는데, 거기서 그의 능력은 원료의 비용 절감으로 향했으니, 빈시 씨의 비단을 썩게 만든 그 염료의 경우가 그러했다."], ["Vincy’s silk. And now, when this respectability had lasted undisturbed for nearly thirty years—when all that preceded it had long lain benumbed in the consciousness—that past had risen and immersed his thought as if with the terrible irruption of a new sense overburthening the feeble being.", "그리고 이제, 이 존경받는 처지가 거의 삼십 년 동안 흔들림 없이 지속되어 온 지금—그것에 앞섰던 모든 것이 오랫동안 의식 속에서 마비된 채 누워 있던 지금—그 과거가 솟아올라, 마치 연약한 존재를 짓누르는 새로운 감각의 끔찍한 침입이 일어난 것처럼 그의 생각을 잠기게 했다."]], [["Meanwhile, in his conversation with Raffles, he had learned something momentous, something which entered actively into the struggle of his longings and terrors.", "그러는 동안, 래플스와의 대화에서 그는 무언가 중대한 것을, 그의 갈망과 공포의 싸움 속으로 능동적으로 끼어드는 무언가를 알게 되었다."], ["There, he thought, lay an opening towards spiritual, perhaps towards material rescue.", "거기에, 그는 생각했다, 영적인—어쩌면 물질적인—구제로 향하는 길이 트여 있었다."]], [["The spiritual kind of rescue was a genuine need with him.", "영적인 종류의 구제는 그에게 진정한 필요였다."], ["There may be coarse hypocrites, who consciously affect beliefs and emotions for the sake of gulling the world, but Bulstrode was not one of them.", "세상을 속이기 위해 의식적으로 신앙과 감정을 꾸며 내는 조잡한 위선자들도 있을지 모르나, 불스트로드는 그런 사람이 아니었다."], ["He was simply a man whose desires had been stronger than his theoretic beliefs, and who had gradually explained the gratification of his desires into satisfactory agreement with those beliefs.", "그는 단지 자신의 욕망이 이론적 신념보다 더 강했던 사람, 그리고 자신의 욕망의 충족을 그 신념들과 흡족하게 일치하는 것으로 차츰 설명해 낸 사람이었을 뿐이다."], ["If this be hypocrisy, it is a process which shows itself occasionally in us all, to whatever confession we belong, and whether we believe in the future perfection of our race or in the nearest date fixed for the end of the world; whether we regard the earth as a putrefying nidus for a saved remnant, including ourselves, or have a passionate belief in the solidarity of mankind.", "이것이 위선이라면, 그것은 우리가 어떤 신앙 고백에 속하든, 우리 종족의 미래의 완성을 믿든 세상의 종말로 정해진 가장 가까운 날짜를 믿든, 우리 자신을 포함한 구원받을 소수의 잔존자를 위한 썩어 가는 둥지로 지구를 여기든 인류의 연대성에 열렬한 믿음을 품든, 우리 모두에게서 이따금 드러나는 과정이다."]], [["The service he could do to the cause of religion had been through life the ground he alleged to himself for his choice of action: it had been the motive which he had poured out in his prayers.", "종교의 대의에 그가 할 수 있는 봉사는 평생토록 그가 자신의 행동 선택의 근거로 스스로에게 내세운 것이었다. 그것은 그가 기도 속에 쏟아부은 동기였다."], ["Who would use money and position better than he meant to use them?", "그가 돈과 지위를 쓰려고 의도한 것보다 더 잘 쓸 사람이 누가 있겠는가?"], ["Who could surpass him in self-abhorrence and exaltation of God’s cause? And to Mr.", "자기혐오와 하느님의 대의를 드높이는 일에서 그를 능가할 자가 누가 있겠는가?"], ["Bulstrode God’s cause was something distinct from his own rectitude of conduct: it enforced a discrimination of God’s enemies, who were to be used merely as instruments, and whom it would be as well if possible to keep out of money and consequent influence.", "그리고 불스트로드 씨에게 하느님의 대의는 자기 자신의 행실의 올바름과는 구별되는 무언가였다. 그것은 하느님의 적들을 식별할 것을 요구했으니, 그들은 한낱 도구로만 쓰일 자들이었고, 가능하다면 돈과 그에 따르는 영향력에서 떼어 놓는 것이 좋을 자들이었다."], ["Also, profitable investments in trades where the power of the prince of this world showed its most active devices, became sanctified by a right application of the profits in the hands of God’s servant.", "또한, 이 세상 군주의 권능이 가장 활발한 술책을 부리는 무역에 수익성 있게 투자하는 일도, 하느님의 종의 손에서 그 수익을 올바르게 쓰면 거룩해지는 것이었다."]], [["This implicit reasoning is essentially no more peculiar to evangelical belief than the use of wide phrases for narrow motives is peculiar to Englishmen.", "이런 암묵적 추론은, 좁은 동기를 넓은 어구로 둘러대는 일이 영국인에게만 특유한 것이 아니듯, 본질적으로 복음주의 신앙에만 특유한 것이 아니다."], ["There is no general doctrine which is not capable of eating out our morality if unchecked by the deep-seated habit of direct fellow-feeling with individual fellow-men.", "개개인 동료 인간과 직접 공감하는 깊이 뿌리박힌 습관으로 제어되지 않는다면, 우리의 도덕성을 좀먹어 들어갈 수 없는 일반 교리란 없다."]], [["But a man who believes in something else than his own greed, has necessarily a conscience or standard to which he more or less adapts himself.", "하지만 자기 자신의 탐욕 외에 다른 무언가를 믿는 사람은 필연적으로 양심을, 혹은 그가 어느 정도 자신을 맞추어 가는 기준을 가진다."], ["Bulstrode’s standard had been his serviceableness to God’s cause: “I am sinful and nought—a vessel to be consecrated by use—but use me!”—had been the mould into which he had constrained his immense need of being something important and predominating.", "불스트로드의 기준은 하느님의 대의에 대한 자신의 쓸모였다. “저는 죄 많고 아무것도 아닌 자이옵니다—쓰임으로써 거룩해질 그릇이옵니다—하오나 저를 쓰소서!”—이것이 그가 중요하고 우월한 존재가 되고자 하는 자신의 거대한 욕구를 억지로 밀어 넣은 틀이었다."], ["And now had come a moment in which that mould seemed in danger of being broken and utterly cast away.", "그리고 이제 그 틀이 부서져 완전히 내던져질 위기에 처한 듯한 순간이 찾아온 것이다."]], [["What if the acts he had reconciled himself to because they made him a stronger instrument of the divine glory, were to become the pretext of the scoffer, and a darkening of that glory?", "만약 그가 자신을 신적 영광의 더 강한 도구로 만들어 준다는 이유로 스스로 받아들였던 그 행위들이, 도리어 조롱하는 자의 구실이 되고 그 영광을 어둡게 만들 것이라면?"], ["If this were to be the ruling of Providence, he was cast out from the temple as one who had brought unclean offerings.", "만약 이것이 섭리의 결정이라면, 그는 부정한 제물을 바친 자로서 성전에서 쫓겨난 셈이었다."]], [["He had long poured out utterances of repentance.", "그는 오랫동안 회개의 말들을 쏟아부어 왔다."], ["But today a repentance had come which was of a bitterer flavor, and a threatening Providence urged him to a kind of propitiation which was not simply a doctrinal transaction.", "하지만 오늘은 더 쓰디쓴 맛을 띤 회개가 찾아왔고, 위협하는 섭리가 그를 한낱 교리적 거래에 그치지 않는 어떤 속죄 행위로 몰아붙였다."], ["The divine tribunal had changed its aspect for him; self-prostration was no longer enough, and he must bring restitution in his hand.", "그에게는 신적 심판대의 양상이 달라져 있었으니, 자기 자신을 엎드리는 것만으로는 더 이상 충분치 않았고, 그는 손에 배상을 들고 와야 했다."], ["It was really before his God that Bulstrode was about to attempt such restitution as seemed possible: a great dread had seized his susceptible frame, and the scorching approach of shame wrought in him a new spiritual need.", "불스트로드가 가능해 보이는 그런 배상을 시도하려 한 것은 실로 자신의 하느님 앞에서였으니, 큰 두려움이 그의 예민한 육체를 사로잡았고, 수치심의 타는 듯한 다가옴이 그의 안에 새로운 영적 필요를 빚어냈다."], ["Night and day, while the resurgent threatening past was making a conscience within him, he was thinking by what means he could recover peace and trust—by what sacrifice he could stay the rod.", "밤낮으로, 다시 솟아올라 위협하는 과거가 그의 안에 양심을 만들어 내는 동안, 그는 어떤 수단으로 평화와 신뢰를 되찾을 수 있을지를—어떤 희생으로 회초리를 멈추게 할 수 있을지를 궁리했다."], ["His belief in these moments of dread was, that if he spontaneously did something right, God would save him from the consequences of wrong-doing.", "이 두려움의 순간들에 그가 품은 믿음은, 만약 자기가 자발적으로 무언가 올바른 일을 한다면 하느님께서 잘못된 행위의 결과로부터 자기를 구해 주시리라는 것이었다."], ["For religion can only change when the emotions which fill it are changed; and the religion of personal fear remains nearly at the level of the savage.", "종교란 그것을 채우는 감정이 바뀔 때에만 바뀔 수 있는 것이기에, 그리고 개인적 두려움의 종교는 미개인의 수준에서 거의 벗어나지 못하기에."]], [["He had seen Raffles actually going away on the Brassing coach, and this was a temporary relief; it removed the pressure of an immediate dread, but did not put an end to the spiritual conflict and the need to win protection.", "그는 래플스가 실제로 브래싱행 마차를 타고 떠나는 것을 보았고, 이것은 일시적인 위안이었다. 그것은 당장의 두려움의 압박을 덜어 주었지만, 영적 갈등과 보호를 얻어야 할 필요에 종지부를 찍지는 못했다."], ["At last he came to a difficult resolve, and wrote a letter to Will Ladislaw, begging him to be at the Shrubs that evening for a private interview at nine o’clock.", "마침내 그는 어려운 결심에 이르러, 윌 래디슬로에게 편지를 써서 그날 저녁 아홉 시에 슈러브스에서 사적인 면담을 갖도록 와 달라고 청했다."], ["Will had felt no particular surprise at the request, and connected it with some new notions about the “Pioneer;”", "윌은 그 요청에 별다른 놀라움을 느끼지 않았고, 그것을 『파이어니어』에 관한 어떤 새로운 구상과 연결 지어 생각했다."], ["but when he was shown into Mr. Bulstrode’s private room, he was struck with the painfully worn look on the banker’s face, and was going to say, “Are you ill?”", "하지만 불스트로드 씨의 사적인 방으로 안내되었을 때, 그는 그 은행가의 얼굴에 어린 고통스럽게 수척한 기색에 충격을 받아, “편찮으십니까?”"], ["when, checking himself in that abruptness, he only inquired after Mrs. Bulstrode, and her satisfaction with the picture bought for her.", "하고 말하려다가, 그런 무람없음을 스스로 억누르고는, 그저 불스트로드 부인과 그녀를 위해 사 준 그림에 대한 그녀의 만족을 물었을 뿐이다."]], [["“Thank you, she is quite satisfied; she has gone out with her daughters this evening.", "“고맙네, 그 사람은 아주 만족하고 있다네. 오늘 저녁엔 딸들과 함께 외출했지."], ["I begged you to come, Mr. Ladislaw, because I have a communication of a very private—indeed, I will say, of a sacredly confidential nature, which I desire to make to you.", "자네를 오라고 청한 것은, 래디슬로 군, 자네에게 전하고 싶은 매우 사적인—실로, 신성하리만치 은밀한 성질의 이야기가 있어서라네."], ["Nothing, I dare say, has been farther from your thoughts than that there had been important ties in the past which could connect your history with mine.”", "자네 생각에서 가장 멀리 떨어져 있었던 것이, 자네의 내력을 나의 내력과 이어 줄 수 있는 중요한 인연이 과거에 있었다는 사실이리라고 짐작하네.”"]], [["Will felt something like an electric shock.", "윌은 전기 충격 비슷한 무언가를 느꼈다."], ["He was already in a state of keen sensitiveness and hardly allayed agitation on the subject of ties in the past, and his presentiments were not agreeable.", "그는 이미 과거의 인연이라는 주제에 대해 예민한 감수성과 좀처럼 가라앉지 않은 동요의 상태에 있었고, 그의 예감은 유쾌하지 않았다."], ["It seemed like the fluctuations of a dream—as if the action begun by that loud bloated stranger were being carried on by this pale-eyed sickly looking piece of respectability, whose subdued tone and glib formality of speech were at this moment almost as repulsive to him as their remembered contrast.", "그것은 꿈의 출렁임 같았으니—마치 그 시끄럽고 부풀어 오른 낯선 자가 시작한 행위를, 이 핏기 없는 눈에 병약해 보이는 이 존경받는 인물이 이어 가고 있는 것 같았다. 그의 가라앉은 어조와 매끄러운 격식의 말투는 이 순간 그에게, 기억 속 그 둘의 대비 못지않게 거의 거슬렸다."], ["He answered, with a marked change of color—", "그는 얼굴빛이 뚜렷이 변하며 대답했다—"]], [["“No, indeed, nothing.”", "“네, 정말이지, 전혀 없습니다.”"]], [["“You see before you, Mr. Ladislaw, a man who is deeply stricken.", "“자네 앞에 있는 것은, 래디슬로 군, 깊은 타격을 입은 사람이라네."], ["But for the urgency of conscience and the knowledge that I am before the bar of One who seeth not as man seeth, I should be under no compulsion to make the disclosure which has been my object in asking you to come here to-night.", "양심의 절박함과, 내가 사람이 보는 것처럼 보지 않으시는 그분의 심판대 앞에 서 있다는 인식이 아니라면, 나는 오늘 밤 자네를 이리로 오라고 청한 나의 목적이었던 이 고백을 할 아무런 강제도 받지 않을 것이네."], ["So far as human laws go, you have no claim on me whatever.”", "인간의 법으로 따지자면, 자네는 나에게 그 어떤 권리도 없다네.”"]], [["Will was even more uncomfortable than wondering.", "윌은 의아하다기보다도 한층 더 불편했다."], ["Mr. Bulstrode had paused, leaning his head on his hand, and looking at the floor.", "불스트로드 씨는 한쪽 손에 머리를 기대고 바닥을 바라보며 말을 멈추었다."], ["But he now fixed his examining glance on Will and said—", "하지만 이제 그는 살피는 시선을 윌에게 고정하고 말했다—"]], [["“I am told that your mother’s name was Sarah Dunkirk, and that she ran away from her friends to go on the stage.", "“자네 어머니의 이름이 세라 던커크였고, 친지들에게서 달아나 무대에 섰다고 들었네."], ["Also, that your father was at one time much emaciated by illness.", "또한 자네 아버지가 한때 병으로 몹시 쇠약했었다고도."], ["May I ask if you can confirm these statements?”", "이 진술들을 확인해 줄 수 있는지 물어봐도 되겠나?”"]], [["“Yes, they are all true,”", "“네, 모두 사실입니다,”"], ["said Will, struck with the order in which an inquiry had come, that might have been expected to be preliminary to the banker’s previous hints. But Mr.", "윌이 말했는데, 그는 은행가의 앞선 암시들에 앞서는 서두가 되었으리라 예상할 법한 탐문이 들어온 순서에 놀랐다."], ["Bulstrode had to-night followed the order of his emotions; he entertained no doubt that the opportunity for restitution had come, and he had an overpowering impulse towards the penitential expression by which he was deprecating chastisement.", "하지만 불스트로드 씨는 오늘 밤 자신의 감정의 순서를 따랐던 것이다. 그는 배상의 기회가 왔다는 것을 추호도 의심하지 않았고, 자신이 징벌을 면하기를 간구하는 그 참회의 표현을 향한 압도적인 충동에 휩싸여 있었다."]], [["“Do you know any particulars of your mother’s family?”", "“자네 어머니의 집안에 대해 무슨 자세한 사정을 아는가?”"], ["he continued.", "그가 계속했다."]], [["“No; she never liked to speak of them.", "“아니요. 어머니는 그 이야기를 결코 하고 싶어 하지 않으셨습니다."], ["She was a very generous, honorable woman,”", "어머니는 매우 너그럽고 명예로운 분이셨습니다,”"], ["said Will, almost angrily.", "윌이 거의 화난 듯이 말했다."]], [["“I do not wish to allege anything against her.", "“나는 그분에 대해 무엇 하나 탓하려는 게 아니라네."], ["Did she never mention her mother to you at all?”", "어머니가 자네에게 자기 어머니에 대해 전혀 언급하신 적이 없는가?”"]], [["“I have heard her say that she thought her mother did not know the reason of her running away.", "“어머니가 당신의 어머니는 당신이 달아난 이유를 모르셨던 것 같다고 말씀하시는 걸 들은 적이 있습니다."], ["She said ‘poor mother’ in a pitying tone.”", "어머니는 가엾어하는 어조로 ‘불쌍한 어머니’라고 하셨지요.”"]], [["“That mother became my wife,”", "“그 어머니가 나의 아내가 되었네,”"], ["said Bulstrode, and then paused a moment before he added, “you have a claim on me, Mr.", "불스트로드가 말하고는, 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. “자네는 나에게 권리가 있다네, 래디슬로 군."], ["Ladislaw: as I said before, not a legal claim, but one which my conscience recognizes.", "앞서 말했듯이, 법적인 권리는 아니지만, 나의 양심이 인정하는 권리라네."], ["I was enriched by that marriage—a result which would probably not have taken place—certainly not to the same extent—if your grandmother could have discovered her daughter.", "나는 그 결혼으로 부유해졌네—만약 자네의 외할머니가 따님을 찾아낼 수 있었더라면 아마도 일어나지 않았을—분명 같은 정도로는 일어나지 않았을 결과였지."], ["That daughter, I gather, is no longer living!”", "그 따님은, 내가 알기로, 이제 살아 있지 않다네!”"]], [["“No,”", "“네,”"], ["said Will, feeling suspicion and repugnance rising so strongly within him, that without quite knowing what he did, he took his hat from the floor and stood up.", "윌이 말했는데, 의심과 혐오가 그의 안에서 너무도 강하게 솟구쳐, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지도 채 모른 채 바닥에서 모자를 집어 들고 일어섰다."], ["The impulse within him was to reject the disclosed connection.", "그의 안에 인 충동은 드러난 그 인연을 거부하라는 것이었다."]], [["“Pray be seated, Mr. Ladislaw,”", "“제발 앉게나, 래디슬로 군,”"], ["said Bulstrode, anxiously.", "불스트로드가 근심스럽게 말했다."], ["“Doubtless you are startled by the suddenness of this discovery.", "“이 발견의 갑작스러움에 자네가 놀란 것은 틀림없는 일이지."], ["But I entreat your patience with one who is already bowed down by inward trial.”", "하지만 이미 내적 시련에 짓눌려 있는 한 사람에게 인내를 베풀어 주기를 간청하네.”"]], [["Will reseated himself, feeling some pity which was half contempt for this voluntary self-abasement of an elderly man.", "윌은 다시 자리에 앉으며, 한 노인의 이 자발적인 자기 비하에 대해 절반은 경멸이 섞인 어떤 연민을 느꼈다."]], [["“It is my wish, Mr. Ladislaw, to make amends for the deprivation which befell your mother.", "“나의 바람은, 래디슬로 군, 자네 어머니가 당한 박탈에 대해 보상하는 것이라네."], ["I know that you are without fortune, and I wish to supply you adequately from a store which would have probably already been yours had your grandmother been certain of your mother’s existence and been able to find her.”", "나는 자네가 재산이 없다는 것을 알고 있고, 만약 자네의 외할머니가 자네 어머니의 존재를 확신하고 그분을 찾아낼 수 있었더라면 아마 이미 자네 것이 되었을 재물에서 자네에게 충분히 마련해 주고 싶네.”"]], [["Mr. Bulstrode paused.", "불스트로드 씨는 말을 멈추었다."], ["He felt that he was performing a striking piece of scrupulosity in the judgment of his auditor, and a penitential act in the eyes of God.", "그는 자신의 듣는 이의 판단으로 볼 때 자기가 두드러진 양심의 행위를 수행하고 있으며, 하느님의 눈으로 볼 때 참회의 행위를 하고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["He had no clew to the state of Will Ladislaw’s mind, smarting as it was from the clear hints of Raffles, and with its natural quickness in construction stimulated by the expectation of discoveries which he would have been glad to conjure back into darkness.", "그는 윌 래디슬로의 마음 상태에 대한 아무런 실마리도 갖지 못했으니, 그 마음은 래플스의 명백한 암시로 쓰라려하고 있었고, 그가 기꺼이 다시 어둠 속으로 불러들이고 싶어 했을 발견들에 대한 예상으로 추론하는 타고난 민첩함이 자극받은 상태였다."], ["Will made no answer for several moments, till Mr. Bulstrode, who at the end of his speech had cast his eyes on the floor, now raised them with an examining glance, which Will met fully, saying—", "윌은 몇 순간 동안 아무 대답이 없었는데, 말을 마치며 바닥으로 눈을 내리깔았던 불스트로드 씨가 이제 살피는 시선으로 눈을 들었고, 윌은 그 시선을 정면으로 마주하며 말했다—"]], [["“I suppose you did know of my mother’s existence, and knew where she might have been found.”", "“선생님은 제 어머니의 존재를 알고 계셨고, 그분이 어디 계실지도 알고 계셨던 것 같군요.”"]], [["Bulstrode shrank—there was a visible quivering in his face and hands.", "불스트로드는 움츠러들었다—그의 얼굴과 손에 눈에 띄는 떨림이 일었다."], ["He was totally unprepared to have his advances met in this way, or to find himself urged into more revelation than he had beforehand set down as needful.", "그는 자신의 제의가 이런 식으로 응대되리라고는, 혹은 자기가 미리 필요하다고 정해 둔 것보다 더 많은 폭로로 떠밀리리라고는 전혀 대비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["But at that moment he dared not tell a lie, and he felt suddenly uncertain of his ground which he had trodden with some confidence before.", "하지만 그 순간 그는 감히 거짓말을 할 수 없었고, 앞서 어느 정도 자신 있게 밟아 왔던 자신의 발판이 갑자기 불확실하게 느껴졌다."]], [["“I will not deny that you conjecture rightly,”", "“자네가 옳게 짐작했다는 것을 부인하지 않겠네,”"], ["he answered, with a faltering in his tone.", "그가 더듬거리는 어조로 대답했다."], ["“And I wish to make atonement to you as the one still remaining who has suffered a loss through me.", "“그래서 나는 나로 인해 손실을 입은, 아직 남아 있는 유일한 사람인 자네에게 속죄하고 싶은 것이라네."], ["You enter, I trust, into my purpose, Mr.", "자네는, 바라건대, 나의 목적을 이해해 주겠지, 래디슬로 군."], ["Ladislaw, which has a reference to higher than merely human claims, and as I have already said, is entirely independent of any legal compulsion.", "그 목적은 한낱 인간적 권리보다 더 높은 것과 관련이 있고, 이미 말했듯이, 그 어떤 법적 강제와도 전적으로 무관하다네."], ["I am ready to narrow my own resources and the prospects of my family by binding myself to allow you five hundred pounds yearly during my life, and to leave you a proportional capital at my death—nay, to do still more, if more should be definitely necessary to any laudable project on your part.” Mr.", "나는 내 평생 동안 자네에게 해마다 오백 파운드를 주기로 스스로를 묶고, 내가 죽을 때 그에 비례하는 자본을 자네에게 남겨 줌으로써 나 자신의 재력과 내 가족의 전망을 줄일 준비가 되어 있네—아니, 자네 쪽의 어떤 칭찬할 만한 계획에 더 많은 것이 분명히 필요하다면, 그 이상까지도 할 준비가 되어 있네.”"], ["Bulstrode had gone on to particulars in the expectation that these would work strongly on Ladislaw, and merge other feelings in grateful acceptance.", "불스트로드 씨는 이런 세부 사항들이 래디슬로에게 강하게 작용하여 다른 감정들을 감사에 찬 수락 속으로 가라앉히리라는 기대로 거기까지 나아갔던 것이다."]], [["But Will was looking as stubborn as possible, with his lip pouting and his fingers in his side-pockets.", "하지만 윌은 입술을 삐죽 내밀고 두 손을 옆 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 더없이 완강한 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["He was not in the least touched, and said firmly,—", "그는 조금도 마음이 움직이지 않았고, 단호하게 말했다—"]], [["“Before I make any reply to your proposition, Mr. Bulstrode, I must beg you to answer a question or two.", "“선생님의 제의에 어떤 대답이라도 하기 전에, 불스트로드 씨, 한두 가지 질문에 답해 주시기를 청해야겠습니다."], ["Were you connected with the business by which that fortune you speak of was originally made?”", "선생님께서 말씀하신 그 재산이 애초에 만들어진 그 사업과 관련이 있으셨습니까?”"]], [["Mr. Bulstrode’s thought was, “Raffles has told him.”", "불스트로드 씨의 생각은 ‘래플스가 그에게 말했구나’였다."], ["How could he refuse to answer when he had volunteered what drew forth the question?", "자기가 그 질문을 끌어낸 말을 자청해 놓고서 어떻게 대답을 거절할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He answered, “Yes.”", "그는 “그렇네”라고 대답했다."]], [["“And was that business—or was it not—a thoroughly dishonorable one—nay, one that, if its nature had been made public, might have ranked those concerned in it with thieves and convicts?”", "“그렇다면 그 사업은—혹은 그렇지 않았는지—철저히 떳떳치 못한 것이었습니까—아니, 그 성질이 공개되었더라면 거기 관련된 자들을 도둑이나 죄수와 같은 부류로 놓았을 만한 것이었습니까?”"]], [["Will’s tone had a cutting bitterness: he was moved to put his question as nakedly as he could.", "윌의 어조에는 에는 듯한 신랄함이 있었으니, 그는 자신의 질문을 할 수 있는 한 적나라하게 던지고 싶은 충동에 사로잡혔다."]], [["Bulstrode reddened with irrepressible anger.", "불스트로드는 억누를 수 없는 분노로 얼굴이 붉어졌다."], ["He had been prepared for a scene of self-abasement, but his intense pride and his habit of supremacy overpowered penitence, and even dread, when this young man, whom he had meant to benefit, turned on him with the air of a judge.", "그는 자기 비하의 장면에 대비하고 있었지만, 그가 은혜를 베풀려 했던 이 젊은이가 심판관 같은 태도로 자기에게 맞서자, 그의 격렬한 자존심과 우위에 익숙한 습관이 참회를, 심지어 두려움마저 압도해 버렸다."]], [["“The business was established before I became connected with it, sir; nor is it for you to institute an inquiry of that kind,”", "“그 사업은 내가 거기 관련되기 전에 이미 자리 잡혀 있었네, 군. 또한 그런 종류의 탐문을 시작하는 것은 자네가 할 일이 아니라네,”"], ["he answered, not raising his voice, but speaking with quick defiantness.", "그가 목소리를 높이지는 않았으되, 재빠른 반항기를 담아 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it is,”", "“아니요, 제가 할 일입니다,”"], ["said Will, starting up again with his hat in his hand.", "윌이 손에 모자를 들고 다시 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."], ["“It is eminently mine to ask such questions, when I have to decide whether I will have transactions with you and accept your money.", "“선생님과 거래를 할 것인지, 선생님의 돈을 받을 것인지를 제가 결정해야 할 때, 그런 질문을 하는 것은 지극히 제가 할 일입니다."], ["My unblemished honor is important to me.", "저의 흠 없는 명예는 제게 중요합니다."], ["It is important to me to have no stain on my birth and connections.", "저의 출생과 인연에 아무런 오점이 없는 것이 제게 중요합니다."], ["And now I find there is a stain which I can’t help.", "그런데 이제 저는 제가 어쩔 수 없는 오점이 있다는 것을 알게 되었군요."], ["My mother felt it, and tried to keep as clear of it as she could, and so will I.", "저의 어머니도 그것을 느끼셨고, 거기서 할 수 있는 한 멀리 떨어져 있으려 하셨습니다. 저도 그럴 것입니다."], ["You shall keep your ill-gotten money.", "선생님의 부정하게 얻은 돈은 선생님이 가지십시오."], ["If I had any fortune of my own, I would willingly pay it to any one who could disprove what you have told me.", "만약 저에게 제 재산이 조금이라도 있다면, 선생님이 제게 말씀하신 것을 반증할 수 있는 누구에게든 그것을 기꺼이 내놓겠습니다."], ["What I have to thank you for is that you kept the money till now, when I can refuse it.", "제가 선생님께 감사드릴 것은, 제가 그 돈을 거절할 수 있는 지금까지 선생님이 그 돈을 간직해 두신 것입니다."], ["It ought to lie with a man’s self that he is a gentleman.", "한 사람이 신사라는 것은 마땅히 그 자신에게 달려 있어야 합니다."], ["Good-night, sir.”", "안녕히 계십시오, 선생님.”"]], [["Bulstrode was going to speak, but Will, with determined quickness, was out of the room in an instant, and in another the hall-door had closed behind him.", "불스트로드는 막 말을 하려던 참이었으나, 윌은 단호한 민첩함으로 순식간에 방을 빠져나갔고, 다음 순간 현관문이 그의 등 뒤에서 닫혔다."], ["He was too strongly possessed with passionate rebellion against this inherited blot which had been thrust on his knowledge to reflect at present whether he had not been too hard on Bulstrode—too arrogantly merciless towards a man of sixty, who was making efforts at retrieval when time had rendered them vain.", "그는 자신의 인식 속으로 떠밀려 들어온 이 물려받은 오점에 대한 열정적인 반항에 너무도 강하게 사로잡혀 있어서, 자기가 불스트로드에게 너무 가혹했던 것은 아닌지—시간이 모든 것을 헛되게 만들어 버린 마당에 만회의 노력을 기울이고 있는 예순 살 노인에게 너무 오만하게 무자비했던 것은 아닌지—지금으로서는 돌이켜 볼 겨를이 없었다."]], [["No third person listening could have thoroughly understood the impetuosity of Will’s repulse or the bitterness of his words.", "제삼자가 귀 기울여 듣고 있었다 한들 윌의 거부가 지닌 격렬함이나 그 말들의 신랄함을 온전히 이해하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["No one but himself then knew how everything connected with the sentiment of his own dignity had an immediate bearing for him on his relation to Dorothea and to Mr. Casaubon’s treatment of him.", "자기 자신의 존엄이라는 감정과 결부된 모든 것이 그에게는 도러시아와의 관계, 그리고 캐소본 씨가 그를 대한 방식과 곧바로 연관된다는 것을, 그때 그 자신 말고는 아무도 알지 못했다."], ["And in the rush of impulses by which he flung back that offer of Bulstrode’s there was mingled the sense that it would have been impossible for him ever to tell Dorothea that he had accepted it.", "그리고 불스트로드의 그 제의를 내던지게 한 충동들의 격류 속에는, 자기가 그것을 받아들였노라고 도러시아에게 결코 말할 수 없으리라는 의식이 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["As for Bulstrode—when Will was gone he suffered a violent reaction, and wept like a woman.", "불스트로드로 말하자면—윌이 떠나자 그는 격렬한 반작용을 겪으며, 여인처럼 울었다."], ["It was the first time he had encountered an open expression of scorn from any man higher than Raffles; and with that scorn hurrying like venom through his system, there was no sensibility left to consolations.", "그가 래플스보다 더 지체 높은 누군가로부터 노골적인 경멸의 표현을 마주한 것은 그것이 처음이었다. 그리고 그 경멸이 독처럼 그의 온몸으로 서둘러 퍼지면서, 위안에 반응할 감수성은 조금도 남지 않았다."], ["But the relief of weeping had to be checked.", "하지만 우는 데서 오는 위안도 억눌러야 했다."], ["His wife and daughters soon came home from hearing the address of an Oriental missionary, and were full of regret that papa had not heard, in the first instance, the interesting things which they tried to repeat to him.", "그의 아내와 딸들이 곧 어느 동방 선교사의 강연을 듣고 집으로 돌아왔고, 그들이 그에게 들려주려 애쓰는 그 흥미로운 이야기들을 아버지가 애초에 듣지 못한 것을 못내 아쉬워했다."]], [["Perhaps, through all other hidden thoughts, the one that breathed most comfort was, that Will Ladislaw at least was not likely to publish what had taken place that evening.", "어쩌면, 다른 모든 숨겨진 생각들을 통틀어, 가장 큰 위안을 내쉬게 한 생각은, 적어도 윌 래디슬로는 그날 저녁 일어난 일을 공표하지 않으리라는 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXII.", "# CHAPTER LXII."]], [["He was a squyer of lowe degre, That loved the king’s daughter of Hungrie.", "헝가리 왕의 딸을 사랑했더라."], ["—Old Romance.", "—옛 로맨스"]], [["Will Ladislaw’s mind was now wholly bent on seeing Dorothea again, and forthwith quitting Middlemarch.", "윌 래디슬로의 마음은 이제 온통 도러시아를 다시 만나고 곧장 미들마치를 떠나는 일에 쏠려 있었다."], ["The morning after his agitating scene with Bulstrode he wrote a brief letter to her, saying that various causes had detained him in the neighborhood longer than he had expected, and asking her permission to call again at Lowick at some hour which she would mention on the earliest possible day, he being anxious to depart, but unwilling to do so until she had granted him an interview.", "벌스트로드와 마음을 뒤흔드는 장면을 겪은 다음 날 아침, 그는 그녀에게 짧은 편지를 썼다. 여러 사정으로 예상보다 오래 이 근방에 머물게 되었다는 말과 함께, 가능한 한 빠른 날, 그녀가 정해 주는 시간에 다시 로윅으로 찾아가도 좋겠느냐고 허락을 구하는 내용이었다."], ["He left the letter at the office, ordering the messenger to carry it to Lowick Manor, and wait for an answer.", "떠나고 싶은 마음은 간절하나, 그녀가 만남을 허락하기 전에는 떠날 수 없다고 적었다. 그는 그 편지를 사무소에 맡기고, 심부름꾼에게 로윅 저택으로 가져가 답을 기다리라고 일렀다."]], [["Ladislaw felt the awkwardness of asking for more last words.", "래디슬로는 다시 한번 마지막 작별의 말을 청하는 일이 어색하게 느껴졌다."], ["His former farewell had been made in the hearing of Sir James Chettam, and had been announced as final even to the butler.", "앞서의 작별은 제임스 체텀 경이 듣는 자리에서 이루어졌고, 집사에게까지 그것이 마지막이라고 공언된 터였다."], ["It is certainly trying to a man’s dignity to reappear when he is not expected to do so: a first farewell has pathos in it, but to come back for a second lends an opening to comedy, and it was possible even that there might be bitter sneers afloat about Will’s motives for lingering.", "다시 나타나리라 예상하지 않은 자리에 도로 나타나는 것은 분명 한 남자의 품위에 거슬리는 일이었다. 첫 작별에는 애틋함이 있으나, 두 번째 작별을 위해 돌아오는 일은 희극의 빌미를 제공하는 법이며, 윌이 머뭇거리는 동기를 두고 신랄한 비웃음이 떠돌 가능성마저 있었다."], ["Still it was on the whole more satisfactory to his feeling to take the directest means of seeing Dorothea, than to use any device which might give an air of chance to a meeting of which he wished her to understand that it was what he earnestly sought.", "그래도 전체적으로 보면, 도러시아에게 자신이 그 만남을 진정으로 갈구했음을 이해시키고 싶었던 만큼, 우연을 가장하는 어떤 술책을 쓰기보다 그녀를 만날 가장 직접적인 방법을 택하는 편이 그의 감정에 더 흡족했다."], ["When he had parted from her before, he had been in ignorance of facts which gave a new aspect to the relation between them, and made a more absolute severance than he had then believed in.", "전에 그녀와 헤어졌을 때 그는 두 사람 사이의 관계에 새로운 국면을 부여하고, 그가 당시 믿었던 것보다 더 절대적인 단절을 짓는 사실들을 알지 못했다."], ["He knew nothing of Dorothea’s private fortune, and being little used to reflect on such matters, took it for granted that according to Mr. Casaubon’s arrangement marriage to him, Will Ladislaw, would mean that she consented to be penniless.", "그는 도러시아의 개인적 재산에 관해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그런 문제를 곰곰이 따져 보는 데 익숙하지 않았기에, 캐소본 씨의 처분에 따르면 자신, 곧 윌 래디슬로와의 결혼은 그녀가 빈털터리가 되기를 동의하는 것을 의미한다고 당연히 여겼다."], ["That was not what he could wish for even in his secret heart, or even if she had been ready to meet such hard contrast for his sake.", "그것은 그의 은밀한 마음속에서조차 바랄 수 있는 일이 아니었으니, 설령 그녀가 그를 위해 그처럼 가혹한 처지를 기꺼이 감수할 준비가 되어 있었다 해도 마찬가지였다."], ["And then, too, there was the fresh smart of that disclosure about his mother’s family, which if known would be an added reason why Dorothea’s friends should look down upon him as utterly below her.", "게다가 어머니 가문에 관한 그 폭로의 새로운 아픔도 있었으니, 만약 그것이 알려진다면 도러시아의 친지들이 그를 그녀에게 도무지 어울리지 않는 천한 자로 깔보아야 할 또 하나의 이유가 될 터였다."], ["The secret hope that after some years he might come back with the sense that he had at least a personal value equal to her wealth, seemed now the dreamy continuation of a dream.", "몇 해가 지난 뒤 적어도 그녀의 재산에 맞먹는 인간적 가치를 갖추었다는 자각을 품고 돌아올 수 있으리라던 은밀한 희망은, 이제 한낱 꿈의 꿈결 같은 연장처럼 보였다."], ["This change would surely justify him in asking Dorothea to receive him once more.", "이 변화야말로 도러시아에게 한 번 더 만나 달라고 청하는 일을 정당화해 줄 것이 분명했다."]], [["But Dorothea on that morning was not at home to receive Will’s note.", "그러나 그날 아침 도러시아는 윌의 편지를 받기에는 집에 없었다."], ["In consequence of a letter from her uncle announcing his intention to be at home in a week, she had driven first to Freshitt to carry the news, meaning to go on to the Grange to deliver some orders with which her uncle had intrusted her—thinking, as he said, “a little mental occupation of this sort good for a widow.”", "일주일 안에 집으로 돌아오겠다는 삼촌의 편지를 받은 까닭에, 그녀는 그 소식을 전하러 먼저 프레시트로 마차를 몰았고, 그다음에는 삼촌이 맡긴 몇 가지 지시를 전하러 그레인지로 갈 작정이었다. 삼촌은 \"이런 자질구레한 정신적 소일거리가 과부에게 좋은 법\"이라고 말했던 것이다."]], [["If Will Ladislaw could have overheard some of the talk at Freshitt that morning, he would have felt all his suppositions confirmed as to the readiness of certain people to sneer at his lingering in the neighborhood.", "만약 윌 래디슬로가 그날 아침 프레시트에서 오간 이야기를 엿들을 수 있었다면, 그가 이 근방에 머무는 일을 두고 어떤 사람들이 기꺼이 비웃을 것이라던 자신의 모든 추측이 옳았음을 절감했을 것이다."], ["Sir James, indeed, though much relieved concerning Dorothea, had been on the watch to learn Ladislaw’s movements, and had an instructed informant in Mr. Standish, who was necessarily in his confidence on this matter.", "실제로 제임스 경은 도러시아에 관해서는 크게 안심했으나, 래디슬로의 동향을 알아내려 줄곧 주시하고 있었고, 이 일에 관해 필연적으로 비밀을 함께 나누고 있던 스탠디시 씨라는 정통한 정보원을 두고 있었다."], ["That Ladislaw had stayed in Middlemarch nearly two months after he had declared that he was going immediately, was a fact to embitter Sir James’s suspicions, or at least to justify his aversion to a “young fellow”", "래디슬로가 곧 떠나겠다고 공언하고도 거의 두 달이나 미들마치에 머물렀다는 사실은 제임스 경의 의심을 더 쓰라리게 만들거나, 적어도 그가 그 \"젊은 친구\"에게 품은 반감을 정당화하기에 충분했다."], ["whom he represented to himself as slight, volatile, and likely enough to show such recklessness as naturally went along with a position unriveted by family ties or a strict profession.", "그는 그 청년을 가볍고 변덕스러우며, 가문의 인연이나 엄격한 직업으로 단단히 매여 있지 않은 처지에 으레 따르기 마련인 무분별을 보일 법한 자로 여기고 있었다."], ["But he had just heard something from Standish which, while it justified these surmises about Will, offered a means of nullifying all danger with regard to Dorothea.", "그런데 그는 방금 스탠디시에게서 어떤 이야기를 들었는데, 그것은 윌에 관한 이런 억측을 정당화하는 한편, 도러시아와 관련된 모든 위험을 무화할 방도를 제시해 주는 것이었다."]], [["Unwonted circumstances may make us all rather unlike ourselves: there are conditions under which the most majestic person is obliged to sneeze, and our emotions are liable to be acted on in the same incongruous manner.", "뜻하지 않은 상황은 우리 모두를 다소 평소답지 않게 만들곤 한다. 아무리 위엄 있는 사람이라도 재채기를 해야만 하는 처지가 있고, 우리의 감정도 그와 똑같이 어울리지 않는 방식으로 좌우될 수 있다."], ["Good Sir James was this morning so far unlike himself that he was irritably anxious to say something to Dorothea on a subject which he usually avoided as if it had been a matter of shame to them both.", "선량한 제임스 경은 이날 아침 평소답지 않게도, 보통은 두 사람 모두에게 수치스러운 일이기라도 한 듯 피하던 어떤 화제에 관해 도러시아에게 무언가 말하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다."], ["He could not use Celia as a medium, because he did not choose that she should know the kind of gossip he had in his mind; and before Dorothea happened to arrive he had been trying to imagine how, with his shyness and unready tongue, he could ever manage to introduce his communication.", "그는 마음속에 품은 그런 종류의 소문을 실리아가 알기를 원치 않았으므로, 실리아를 매개로 삼을 수 없었다. 그래서 도러시아가 도착하기 전까지 그는, 수줍음 많고 말주변 없는 자신이 어떻게 그 이야기를 꺼낼 수 있을지 상상해 보려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Her unexpected presence brought him to utter hopelessness in his own power of saying anything unpleasant; but desperation suggested a resource; he sent the groom on an unsaddled horse across the park with a pencilled note to Mrs.", "그녀가 뜻밖에 나타나자 그는 불쾌한 말을 꺼낼 능력에 관해 완전히 절망에 빠졌으나, 그 절망이 한 가지 방책을 일러 주었다. 그는 마구간지기를 안장 없는 말에 태워 공원을 가로질러 캐드월래더 부인에게 연필로 쓴 쪽지를 보냈다."], ["Cadwallader, who already knew the gossip, and would think it no compromise of herself to repeat it as often as required.", "부인은 이미 그 소문을 알고 있었고, 필요한 만큼 몇 번이고 그것을 되풀이하는 일을 자기 체면을 손상하는 것으로 여기지 않을 사람이었다."]], [["Dorothea was detained on the good pretext that Mr.", "도러시아는 만나고 싶어 하던 가스 씨가 한 시간 안에 저택에 들르기로 되어 있다는 그럴듯한 핑계로 붙잡혀 있었고, 자갈길에서 아직 케일럽과 이야기를 나누는 중이었다."], ["Garth, whom she wanted to see, was expected at the hall within the hour, and she was still talking to Caleb on the gravel when Sir James, on the watch for the rector’s wife, saw her coming and met her with the needful hints.", "그때 목사 부인을 기다리며 주시하던 제임스 경이 그녀가 오는 것을 보고 마중 나가 필요한 귀띔을 했다."]], [["“Enough!", "\"됐어요!"], ["I understand,”—said Mrs.", "알겠습니다.\" 캐드월래더 부인이 말했다."], ["Cadwallader. “You shall be innocent.", "\"당신은 결백하실 거예요."], ["I am such a blackamoor that I cannot smirch myself.”", "저야 워낙 시커먼 사람이라 더 더러워질 것도 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I don’t mean that it’s of any consequence,”", "\"그게 무슨 중대한 일이라는 뜻은 아닙니다.\""], ["said Sir James, disliking that Mrs. Cadwallader should understand too much.", "제임스 경은 캐드월래더 부인이 너무 많이 알아차리는 것이 못마땅하여 말했다."], ["“Only it is desirable that Dorothea should know there are reasons why she should not receive him again; and I really can’t say so to her.", "\"다만 도러시아가 그를 다시 받아들여서는 안 될 이유가 있다는 걸 알았으면 하는 겁니다. 그런데 나로서는 도무지 그녀에게 그렇게 말할 수가 없어요."], ["It will come lightly from you.”", "당신 입에서라면 가볍게 나올 테고요.\""]], [["It came very lightly indeed.", "과연 그것은 아주 가볍게 흘러나왔다."], ["When Dorothea quitted Caleb and turned to meet them, it appeared that Mrs. Cadwallader had stepped across the park by the merest chance in the world, just to chat with Celia in a matronly way about the baby.", "도러시아가 케일럽 곁을 떠나 그들을 맞이하러 돌아섰을 때, 캐드월래더 부인은 세상에서 가장 우연한 발걸음으로 공원을 가로질러, 그저 실리아와 아기에 관해 부인네답게 한담이나 나누려 들른 듯이 보였다."], ["And so Mr. Brooke was coming back?", "그래서, 브룩 씨가 돌아오신다고요?"], ["Delightful!—coming back, it was to be hoped, quite cured of Parliamentary fever and pioneering.", "반가운 일이로군요! 돌아오시면, 부디 의회 열병과 개척 사업에서 말끔히 나으셨기를."], ["Apropos of the “Pioneer”—somebody had prophesied that it would soon be like a dying dolphin, and turn all colors for want of knowing how to help itself, because Mr.", "그 「개척자」지 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 누군가는 그 신문이 곧 죽어 가는 돌고래 꼴이 되어 제 한 몸 어쩔 줄도 모른 채 온갖 빛깔로 변할 거라고 예언했다지요."], ["Brooke’s protege, the brilliant young Ladislaw, was gone or going.", "브룩 씨가 후원하던 그 명민한 청년 래디슬로가 떠났거나 떠나려 하니 말이에요."], ["Had Sir James heard that?", "제임스 경은 그 이야기를 들으셨던가요?"]], [["The three were walking along the gravel slowly, and Sir James, turning aside to whip a shrub, said he had heard something of that sort.", "세 사람은 자갈길을 천천히 거닐고 있었는데, 제임스 경은 곁의 관목을 채찍으로 후려치려 몸을 돌리며 그런 비슷한 이야기를 들은 적이 있다고 말했다."]], [["“All false!”", "\"전부 거짓말이에요!\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월래더 부인이 말했다."], ["“He is not gone, or going, apparently; the ‘Pioneer’ keeps its color, and Mr.", "\"그는 떠나지도, 떠나려는 기색도 보이지 않아요."], ["Orlando Ladislaw is making a sad dark-blue scandal by warbling continually with your Mr. Lydgate’s wife, who they tell me is as pretty as pretty can be.", "「개척자」지는 여전히 제 빛깔을 지키고 있고, 올랜도 래디슬로 씨는 당신네 리드게이트 씨의 부인과 끊임없이 노래나 불러 대며 참으로 짙푸른 추문을 빚어내고 있답니다."], ["It seems nobody ever goes into the house without finding this young gentleman lying on the rug or warbling at the piano.", "듣자 하니 그 부인은 더없이 예쁘다더군요. 누구든 그 집에 들어가면 이 젊은 신사분이 양탄자에 누워 있거나 피아노 앞에서 노래하는 모습을 보지 않은 적이 없다지 뭐예요."], ["But the people in manufacturing towns are always disreputable.”", "하기야 공장 도시 사람들이란 늘 평판이 좋지 못한 법이지요.\""]], [["“You began by saying that one report was false, Mrs. Cadwallader, and I believe this is false too,”", "\"부인께서는 처음에 어떤 소문 하나가 거짓이라고 하셨고, 저는 이것도 거짓이라고 믿습니다.\""], ["said Dorothea, with indignant energy; “at least, I feel sure it is a misrepresentation.", "도러시아가 분개하는 기운을 띠고 말했다. \"적어도 잘못 전해진 이야기라고 확신해요."], ["I will not hear any evil spoken of Mr.", "저는 래디슬로 씨에 관해 어떤 험담도 듣지 않겠어요."], ["Ladislaw; he has already suffered too much injustice.”", "그분은 이미 너무 많은 부당함을 겪으셨으니까요.\""]], [["Dorothea when thoroughly moved cared little what any one thought of her feelings; and even if she had been able to reflect, she would have held it petty to keep silence at injurious words about Will from fear of being herself misunderstood.", "도러시아는 일단 진정으로 마음이 동하면 남이 자기 감정을 어떻게 생각하는지 거의 개의치 않았다. 설령 차분히 헤아릴 수 있었다 해도, 자신이 오해받을까 두려워 윌에 관한 해로운 말 앞에서 침묵을 지키는 일을 그녀는 옹졸하다고 여겼을 것이다."], ["Her face was flushed and her lip trembled.", "그녀의 얼굴은 붉게 달아올랐고 입술은 떨렸다."]], [["Sir James, glancing at her, repented of his stratagem; but Mrs.", "제임스 경은 그녀를 흘끗 보며 자신의 책략을 후회했다."], ["Cadwallader, equal to all occasions, spread the palms of her hands outward and said—“Heaven grant it, my dear!—I mean that all bad tales about anybody may be false.", "그러나 어떤 상황에도 능란한 캐드월래더 부인은 두 손바닥을 바깥으로 펼쳐 보이며 말했다."], ["But it is a pity that young Lydgate should have married one of these Middlemarch girls.", "\"부디 그러기를 바라요, 얘야! 누구에 관해서든 나쁜 이야기는 다 거짓이기를 바란다는 뜻이지."], ["Considering he’s a son of somebody, he might have got a woman with good blood in her veins, and not too young, who would have put up with his profession.", "하지만 젊은 리드게이트가 이런 미들마치 처녀들 가운데 하나와 결혼한 건 안타까운 일이야. 그래도 명색이 누군가의 아들인데, 핏줄에 좋은 피가 흐르고 너무 어리지도 않으며 그의 직업을 견뎌 줄 만한 여자를 얻을 수도 있었을 텐데."], ["There’s Clara Harfager, for instance, whose friends don’t know what to do with her; and she has a portion.", "이를테면 클라라 하파거 같은 여자 말이야. 친지들이 그 애를 어쩌지 못해 쩔쩔매는데, 게다가 지참금까지 있거든."], ["Then we might have had her among us.", "그랬다면 우리 곁에 그 애를 두었을 텐데."], ["However!—it’s no use being wise for other people.", "하지만 어쩌겠어! 남을 위해 지혜로워 봐야 소용없는 일이지."], ["Where is Celia?", "실리아는 어디 있나?"], ["Pray let us go in.”", "자, 어서 들어갑시다.\""]], [["“I am going on immediately to Tipton,”", "\"저는 곧장 팁턴으로 가야 해요.\""], ["said Dorothea, rather haughtily.", "도러시아가 다소 거만하게 말했다."], ["“Good-by.”", "\"안녕히 계세요.\""]], [["Sir James could say nothing as he accompanied her to the carriage.", "제임스 경은 그녀를 마차까지 배웅하면서 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."], ["He was altogether discontented with the result of a contrivance which had cost him some secret humiliation beforehand.", "그는 미리 은밀한 굴욕까지 치르며 꾸민 술책의 결과에 도무지 만족할 수 없었다."]], [["Dorothea drove along between the berried hedgerows and the shorn corn-fields, not seeing or hearing anything around.", "도러시아는 열매 맺힌 산울타리와 베어 낸 곡식 밭 사이로 마차를 달렸으나, 주위의 무엇도 보거나 듣지 못했다."], ["The tears came and rolled down her cheeks, but she did not know it.", "눈물이 솟아 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸지만 그녀는 그것을 알지 못했다."], ["The world, it seemed, was turning ugly and hateful, and there was no place for her trustfulness.", "세상이 추하고 가증스럽게 변해 가는 듯했고, 그녀의 믿음이 깃들 자리는 어디에도 없었다."], ["“It is not true—it is not true!”", "\"그건 사실이 아니야, 사실이 아니야!\""], ["was the voice within her that she listened to; but all the while a remembrance to which there had always clung a vague uneasiness would thrust itself on her attention—the remembrance of that day when she had found Will Ladislaw with Mrs.", "그녀가 귀 기울이는 것은 내면의 그 목소리였다. 그러나 그러는 내내 언제나 막연한 불안이 들러붙어 있던 어떤 기억이 자꾸만 그녀의 주의를 비집고 들어왔다."], ["Lydgate, and had heard his voice accompanied by the piano.", "윌 래디슬로가 리드게이트 부인과 함께 있던 그날, 피아노 반주에 맞춰 노래하는 그의 목소리를 들었던 그날의 기억이었다."]], [["“He said he would never do anything that I disapproved—I wish I could have told him that I disapproved of that,”", "\"그분은 내가 못마땅해하는 일은 결코 하지 않겠다고 했어. 그게 못마땅하다고 말해 줄 수 있었더라면.\""], ["said poor Dorothea, inwardly, feeling a strange alternation between anger with Will and the passionate defence of him.", "가엾은 도러시아는 윌에 대한 분노와 그를 열렬히 변호하려는 마음 사이를 묘하게 오가며 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["“They all try to blacken him before me; but I will care for no pain, if he is not to blame.", "\"다들 내 앞에서 그분을 헐뜯으려 하지만, 그분에게 잘못이 없다면 나는 어떤 아픔도 개의치 않겠어. 나는 늘 그분이 선한 사람이라고 믿었어.\""], ["I always believed he was good.”—These were her last thoughts before she felt that the carriage was passing under the archway of the lodge-gate at the Grange, when she hurriedly pressed her handkerchief to her face and began to think of her errands.", "마차가 그레인지 정문 관리소의 아치 밑을 지나고 있다는 것을 느낀 순간, 이것이 그녀의 마지막 생각이었다. 그녀는 황급히 손수건을 얼굴에 갖다 대고 자신이 할 일들을 떠올리기 시작했다."], ["The coachman begged leave to take out the horses for half an hour as there was something wrong with a shoe; and Dorothea, having the sense that she was going to rest, took off her gloves and bonnet, while she was leaning against a statue in the entrance-hall, and talking to the housekeeper.", "마부는 말굽 하나에 문제가 생겼으니 삼십 분쯤 말을 풀어 놓아도 되겠느냐고 청했다. 도러시아는 쉴 수 있겠다는 생각에, 현관 홀의 조각상에 기대어 가정부와 이야기를 나누며 장갑과 보닛을 벗었다."], ["At last she said—", "마침내 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I must stay here a little, Mrs. Kell.", "\"여기서 잠깐 머물러야겠어요, 켈 부인."], ["I will go into the library and write you some memoranda from my uncle’s letter, if you will open the shutters for me.”", "덧문을 열어 주시면 서재로 들어가 삼촌 편지에서 몇 가지 적어 드리겠어요.\""]], [["“The shutters are open, madam,”", "\"덧문은 열려 있습니다, 마님.\""], ["said Mrs. Kell, following Dorothea, who had walked along as she spoke.", "켈 부인이, 말하면서 이미 걸어가던 도러시아를 따라가며 말했다."], ["“Mr. Ladislaw is there, looking for something.”", "\"래디슬로 씨가 거기서 뭔가를 찾고 계십니다.\""]], [["(Will had come to fetch a portfolio of his own sketches which he had missed in the act of packing his movables, and did not choose to leave behind.)", "(윌은 짐을 꾸리던 중 빠뜨린 자기 그림이 든 화첩을 가지러 온 것이었고, 그것을 두고 떠나고 싶지 않았다.)"]], [["Dorothea’s heart seemed to turn over as if it had had a blow, but she was not perceptibly checked: in truth, the sense that Will was there was for the moment all-satisfying to her, like the sight of something precious that one has lost.", "도러시아의 심장은 한 대 얻어맞기라도 한 듯 뒤집히는 것 같았으나, 겉으로는 눈에 띄게 멈칫하지 않았다. 사실 윌이 그곳에 있다는 느낌은 그 순간 그녀에게 더없는 만족이었으니, 잃어버린 줄 알았던 소중한 것을 다시 본 것과도 같았다."], ["When she reached the door she said to Mrs. Kell—", "문 앞에 이르자 그녀가 켈 부인에게 말했다."]], [["“Go in first, and tell him that I am here.”", "\"먼저 들어가서, 제가 와 있다고 전해 주세요.\""]], [["Will had found his portfolio, and had laid it on the table at the far end of the room, to turn over the sketches and please himself by looking at the memorable piece of art which had a relation to nature too mysterious for Dorothea.", "윌은 화첩을 찾아내어 방 저쪽 끝 탁자 위에 올려놓고, 그림을 한 장씩 넘기며 도러시아에게는 너무도 신비로워 자연과의 관계를 알 수 없을, 그 잊지 못할 예술 작품을 바라보며 흐뭇해하던 참이었다."], ["He was smiling at it still, and shaking the sketches into order with the thought that he might find a letter from her awaiting him at Middlemarch, when Mrs.", "그는 여전히 그것을 보며 미소 짓고 있었고, 미들마치에 돌아가면 그녀의 편지가 기다리고 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 그림들을 가지런히 추스르고 있었다."], ["Kell close to his elbow said—", "그때 그의 팔꿈치 가까이에서 켈 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Mrs. Casaubon is coming in, sir.”", "\"캐소본 부인께서 들어오십니다, 나리.\""]], [["Will turned round quickly, and the next moment Dorothea was entering. As Mrs.", "윌은 재빨리 돌아섰고, 다음 순간 도러시아가 들어서고 있었다."], ["Kell closed the door behind her they met: each was looking at the other, and consciousness was overflowed by something that suppressed utterance.", "켈 부인이 그녀의 뒤로 문을 닫자 두 사람은 마주했다. 서로가 서로를 바라보았고, 의식은 말문을 막아 버리는 무언가에 휩쓸려 넘쳤다."], ["It was not confusion that kept them silent, for they both felt that parting was near, and there is no shamefacedness in a sad parting.", "그들을 침묵하게 한 것은 당혹스러움이 아니었으니, 두 사람 모두 작별이 가까웠음을 느꼈고, 슬픈 작별에는 부끄러움이란 없는 법이다."]], [["She moved automatically towards her uncle’s chair against the writing-table, and Will, after drawing it out a little for her, went a few paces off and stood opposite to her.", "그녀는 무심결에 글쓰기 탁자 옆 삼촌의 의자로 다가갔고, 윌은 그 의자를 조금 빼 준 다음 몇 걸음 물러나 그녀 맞은편에 섰다."]], [["“Pray sit down,”", "\"부디 앉으세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, crossing her hands on her lap; “I am very glad you were here.”", "도러시아가 무릎 위에 두 손을 포개며 말했다. \"당신이 여기 계셔서 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["Will thought that her face looked just as it did when she first shook hands with him in Rome; for her widow’s cap, fixed in her bonnet, had gone off with it, and he could see that she had lately been shedding tears.", "윌은 그녀의 얼굴이 로마에서 처음 자기와 악수하던 때와 꼭 같아 보인다고 생각했다. 보닛에 고정되어 있던 과부의 두건이 보닛과 함께 벗겨진 터라, 그는 그녀가 최근에 눈물을 흘렸다는 것을 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["But the mixture of anger in her agitation had vanished at the sight of him; she had been used, when they were face to face, always to feel confidence and the happy freedom which comes with mutual understanding, and how could other people’s words hinder that effect on a sudden?", "그러나 그녀의 동요에 섞여 있던 분노는 그를 본 순간 사라져 버렸다. 두 사람이 얼굴을 마주할 때면 그녀는 늘 신뢰와, 서로의 이해에서 오는 행복한 자유로움을 느끼는 데 익숙했으니, 남들의 말이 어찌 그 효과를 별안간 가로막을 수 있겠는가?"], ["Let the music which can take possession of our frame and fill the air with joy for us, sound once more—what does it signify that we heard it found fault with in its absence?", "우리의 온몸을 사로잡고 우리를 위해 공기를 기쁨으로 채우는 음악이 다시 한번 울려 퍼진다면, 그것이 들리지 않을 때 누군가 그 음악을 흠잡는 것을 들었다 한들 무슨 상관이겠는가?"]], [["“I have sent a letter to Lowick Manor to-day, asking leave to see you,”", "\"오늘 로윅 저택으로 편지를 보내, 당신을 뵙게 해 달라고 청했습니다.\""], ["said Will, seating himself opposite to her.", "윌이 그녀 맞은편에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“I am going away immediately, and I could not go without speaking to you again.”", "\"저는 곧 떠납니다. 그런데 당신과 다시 이야기를 나누지 않고는 떠날 수 없었어요.\""]], [["“I thought we had parted when you came to Lowick many weeks ago—you thought you were going then,”", "\"몇 주 전에 당신이 로윅에 오셨을 때 우리는 작별한 줄 알았어요. 그때 떠나신다고 하셨으니까요.\""], ["said Dorothea, her voice trembling a little.", "도러시아가 약간 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Yes; but I was in ignorance then of things which I know now—things which have altered my feelings about the future.", "\"네. 하지만 그때 저는 지금 알게 된 일들을 몰랐습니다."], ["When I saw you before, I was dreaming that I might come back some day.", "미래에 관한 제 감정을 바꿔 놓은 일들 말이에요. 전에 당신을 뵈었을 때, 저는 언젠가 다시 돌아올 수 있으리라는 꿈을 꾸고 있었습니다."], ["I don’t think I ever shall—now.”", "그런데 이제는 결코 그러지 못하리라는 생각이 듭니다.\""], ["Will paused here.", "여기서 윌은 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“You wished me to know the reasons?”", "\"제가 그 이유를 알기를 바라시나요?\""], ["said Dorothea, timidly.", "도러시아가 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Will, impetuously, shaking his head backward, and looking away from her with irritation in his face.", "윌은 고개를 뒤로 젖히며 얼굴에 짜증을 띤 채 그녀에게서 시선을 돌리고 격하게 말했다."], ["“Of course I must wish it.", "\"물론 그러기를 바라야지요."], ["I have been grossly insulted in your eyes and in the eyes of others.", "저는 당신의 눈에도, 다른 이들의 눈에도 심하게 모욕당했습니다."], ["There has been a mean implication against my character.", "제 인격에 대한 비열한 암시가 있었어요."], ["I wish you to know that under no circumstances would I have lowered myself by—under no circumstances would I have given men the chance of saying that I sought money under the pretext of seeking—something else.", "저는 당신이 알아주시길 바랍니다. 어떤 상황에서도 저는, 무언가 다른 것을 구한다는 핑계로 돈을 노렸다는 말을 사람들에게 할 빌미를 주는 식으로 결코 제 자신을 낮추지 않았으리라는 것을요."], ["There was no need of other safeguard against me—the safeguard of wealth was enough.”", "저를 막아 줄 다른 안전장치 따위는 필요 없었습니다. 재산이라는 안전장치만으로 충분했으니까요.\""]], [["Will rose from his chair with the last word and went—he hardly knew where; but it was to the projecting window nearest him, which had been open as now about the same season a year ago, when he and Dorothea had stood within it and talked together. Her whole heart was going out at this moment in sympathy with Will’s indignation: she only wanted to convince him that she had never done him injustice, and he seemed to have turned away from her as if she too had been part of the unfriendly world.", "윌은 마지막 말과 함께 의자에서 일어나, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 발길을 옮겼다. 그러나 그곳은 그에게서 가장 가까운, 돌출된 창가였다. 그 창은 일 년 전 거의 같은 계절에 지금처럼 열려 있었고, 그때 그와 도러시아는 그 안에 서서 함께 이야기를 나누었었다. 이 순간 그녀의 온 마음은 윌의 분개에 공감하며 쏟아져 나가고 있었다. 그녀는 다만 자신이 그에게 결코 부당하게 군 적이 없음을 그에게 납득시키고 싶었을 뿐인데, 그는 마치 그녀마저 그 매정한 세상의 일부이기라도 한 듯 그녀에게서 등을 돌린 듯했다."]], [["“It would be very unkind of you to suppose that I ever attributed any meanness to you,”", "\"제가 당신에게 어떤 비열함을 갖다 붙인 적이 있다고 생각하신다면, 그건 정말 무정한 일이에요.\""], ["she began.", "그녀가 입을 열었다."], ["Then in her ardent way, wanting to plead with him, she moved from her chair and went in front of him to her old place in the window, saying, “Do you suppose that I ever disbelieved in you?”", "그러더니 그녀 특유의 열렬한 태도로, 그에게 호소하고 싶은 마음에 의자에서 일어나 그의 앞을 지나 창가의 옛 자리로 가서 말했다. \"제가 당신을 단 한 번이라도 믿지 않은 적이 있다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["When Will saw her there, he gave a start and moved backward out of the window, without meeting her glance.", "윌은 거기 선 그녀를 보자 흠칫 놀라 그녀의 시선을 마주하지 않은 채 창에서 뒤로 물러났다."], ["Dorothea was hurt by this movement following up the previous anger of his tone.", "도러시아는 앞서의 성난 어조에 뒤이은 이 몸짓에 상처받았다."], ["She was ready to say that it was as hard on her as on him, and that she was helpless; but those strange particulars of their relation which neither of them could explicitly mention kept her always in dread of saying too much.", "그녀는 그것이 그에게 못지않게 자신에게도 괴로운 일이며, 자신은 어찌할 도리가 없다고 말하고 싶었다. 그러나 두 사람 다 명시적으로 입에 올릴 수 없는 그들 관계의 그 묘한 사정들 탓에, 그녀는 늘 말을 너무 많이 할까 두려워했다."], ["At this moment she had no belief that Will would in any case have wanted to marry her, and she feared using words which might imply such a belief.", "이 순간 그녀는 윌이 어떤 경우에도 자신과 결혼하고 싶어 했으리라고는 믿지 않았으므로, 그런 믿음을 암시할 만한 말을 쓰기가 두려웠다."], ["She only said earnestly, recurring to his last word—", "그녀는 다만 그의 마지막 말로 되돌아가 간곡히 말했다."]], [["“I am sure no safeguard was ever needed against you.”", "\"당신을 막을 안전장치 따위는 결코 필요하지 않았다고 저는 확신해요.\""]], [["Will did not answer.", "윌은 대답하지 않았다."], ["In the stormy fluctuation of his feelings these words of hers seemed to him cruelly neutral, and he looked pale and miserable after his angry outburst.", "격렬하게 출렁이는 감정 속에서 그녀의 이 말은 그에게 잔인할 만큼 무심하게 들렸고, 성난 폭발 끝에 그의 얼굴은 창백하고 비참해 보였다."], ["He went to the table and fastened up his portfolio, while Dorothea looked at him from the distance.", "그가 탁자로 가서 화첩을 동여매는 동안, 도러시아는 멀찍이서 그를 바라보았다."], ["They were wasting these last moments together in wretched silence.", "그들은 함께하는 이 마지막 순간들을 비참한 침묵 속에 허비하고 있었다."], ["What could he say, since what had got obstinately uppermost in his mind was the passionate love for her which he forbade himself to utter?", "그가 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그의 마음속에 완고하게 솟구쳐 오른 것은 차마 입 밖에 낼 수 없도록 스스로 금한, 그녀를 향한 열렬한 사랑이었으니."], ["What could she say, since she might offer him no help—since she was forced to keep the money that ought to have been his?—since to-day he seemed not to respond as he used to do to her thorough trust and liking?", "그녀는 또 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그녀는 그에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없었으니, 마땅히 그의 것이어야 했던 돈을 어쩔 수 없이 지켜야 하는 처지였으니, 게다가 오늘 그는 그녀의 온전한 신뢰와 호의에 예전처럼 응답하지 않는 듯했으니."]], [["But Will at last turned away from his portfolio and approached the window again.", "그러나 윌은 마침내 화첩에서 몸을 돌려 다시 창가로 다가왔다."]], [["“I must go,”", "\"저는 가야 합니다.\""], ["he said, with that peculiar look of the eyes which sometimes accompanies bitter feeling, as if they had been tired and burned with gazing too close at a light.", "그는 쓰라린 감정이 이따금 동반하곤 하는 그 특유의 눈빛으로 말했으니, 마치 빛을 너무 가까이서 응시하다 지치고 타 버린 듯한 눈빛이었다."]], [["“What shall you do in life?”", "\"인생에서 무엇을 하실 건가요?\""], ["said Dorothea, timidly.", "도러시아가 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“Have your intentions remained just the same as when we said good-by before?”", "\"전에 작별 인사를 나누었을 때와 계획이 그대로신가요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Will, in a tone that seemed to waive the subject as uninteresting.", "윌이 그 화제를 흥미롭지 않다는 듯 밀쳐 내는 어조로 말했다."], ["“I shall work away at the first thing that offers.", "\"처음 주어지는 일에 매달려 일할 겁니다."], ["I suppose one gets a habit of doing without happiness or hope.”", "행복도 희망도 없이 지내는 습관이 드는 모양이지요.\""]], [["“Oh, what sad words!”", "\"오, 너무 슬픈 말씀이에요!\""], ["said Dorothea, with a dangerous tendency to sob.", "도러시아가 위태롭게 흐느낌이 터질 듯한 기색으로 말했다."], ["Then trying to smile, she added, “We used to agree that we were alike in speaking too strongly.”", "그러더니 애써 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"우리는 둘 다 말을 너무 강하게 한다는 점에서 닮았다고 늘 의견이 일치했잖아요.\""]], [["“I have not spoken too strongly now,”", "\"지금은 말을 너무 강하게 한 게 아닙니다.\""], ["said Will, leaning back against the angle of the wall.", "윌이 벽 모서리에 등을 기대며 말했다."], ["“There are certain things which a man can only go through once in his life; and he must know some time or other that the best is over with him.", "\"한 인간이 평생 단 한 번밖에 겪을 수 없는 일들이 있지요. 그리고 그는 언젠가 자신에게 가장 좋은 것은 이미 끝났음을 알아야만 합니다."], ["This experience has happened to me while I am very young—that is all.", "이런 경험이 저에게는 아주 젊은 나이에 닥쳤다, 그뿐입니다."], ["What I care more for than I can ever care for anything else is absolutely forbidden to me—I don’t mean merely by being out of my reach, but forbidden me, even if it were within my reach, by my own pride and honor—by everything I respect myself for.", "제가 그 무엇보다도 더 마음 쓰는 것이 저에게는 절대적으로 금지되어 있습니다. 단지 제 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있어서가 아니라, 설령 제 손이 닿는 곳에 있다 해도 제 자존심과 명예가, 제가 스스로를 존중하는 그 모든 것이 저에게 금지하고 있다는 말입니다."], ["Of course I shall go on living as a man might do who had seen heaven in a trance.”", "물론 저는 황홀경 속에서 천국을 본 사람이 살아갈 법한 방식으로 계속 살아가겠지요.\""]], [["Will paused, imagining that it would be impossible for Dorothea to misunderstand this; indeed he felt that he was contradicting himself and offending against his self-approval in speaking to her so plainly; but still—it could not be fairly called wooing a woman to tell her that he would never woo her.", "윌은 도러시아가 이 말을 오해할 리 없으리라 여기며 말을 멈추었다. 사실 그는 이토록 솔직하게 그녀에게 말하는 것이 자기모순이며 스스로의 양심에 어긋나는 일이라고 느꼈다."], ["It must be admitted to be a ghostly kind of wooing.", "그래도, 결코 구애하지 않으리라고 한 여자에게 말하는 것을 공정하게 구애라 부를 수는 없었다. 그것은 유령 같은 구애라고 인정해야만 할 일이었다."]], [["But Dorothea’s mind was rapidly going over the past with quite another vision than his.", "그러나 도러시아의 마음은 그와는 전혀 다른 시각으로 빠르게 과거를 더듬고 있었다."], ["The thought that she herself might be what Will most cared for did throb through her an instant, but then came doubt: the memory of the little they had lived through together turned pale and shrank before the memory which suggested how much fuller might have been the intercourse between Will and some one else with whom he had had constant companionship.", "자신이야말로 윌이 가장 마음 쓰는 대상일지 모른다는 생각이 한순간 그녀를 관통하며 고동쳤으나, 곧 의혹이 뒤따랐다. 둘이 함께 겪은 그 짧은 시간의 기억은, 윌이 끊임없이 곁에 두고 지낸 다른 누군가와 얼마나 더 충만한 교제를 나누었을지를 일러 주는 기억 앞에서 창백해지며 움츠러들었다."], ["Everything he had said might refer to that other relation, and whatever had passed between him and herself was thoroughly explained by what she had always regarded as their simple friendship and the cruel obstruction thrust upon it by her husband’s injurious act.", "그가 한 말은 모두 그 다른 관계를 가리키는 것일 수 있었고, 그와 그녀 자신 사이에 오간 일은, 그녀가 늘 둘의 단순한 우정이라 여겨 온 것과 남편의 해로운 행위가 거기에 들이민 잔인한 방해로 충분히 설명되었다."], ["Dorothea stood silent, with her eyes cast down dreamily, while images crowded upon her which left the sickening certainty that Will was referring to Mrs.", "도러시아는 꿈결처럼 눈을 내리깐 채 말없이 서 있었고, 그러는 동안 윌이 리드게이트 부인을 두고 한 말이라는 메스꺼운 확신을 남기는 영상들이 그녀에게 밀려들었다."], ["Lydgate. But why sickening?", "그런데 어째서 메스꺼운가?"], ["He wanted her to know that here too his conduct should be above suspicion.", "그는 여기서도 자신의 처신이 의심받을 데가 없기를 그녀가 알아주길 바랐을 뿐인데."]], [["Will was not surprised at her silence.", "윌은 그녀의 침묵에 놀라지 않았다."], ["His mind also was tumultuously busy while he watched her, and he was feeling rather wildly that something must happen to hinder their parting—some miracle, clearly nothing in their own deliberate speech.", "그녀를 지켜보는 동안 그의 마음 역시 어지러이 분주했고, 두 사람의 작별을 가로막을 무언가가 일어나야 한다는 다소 격앙된 느낌에 사로잡혀 있었다. 어떤 기적, 분명 그들 자신의 신중한 말로는 이룰 수 없는 무엇이."], ["Yet, after all, had she any love for him?—he could not pretend to himself that he would rather believe her to be without that pain.", "하지만 결국, 그녀가 그를 조금이라도 사랑하기는 했을까? 그녀가 그런 아픔을 겪지 않기를 차라리 바란다고 그는 스스로에게도 가장할 수 없었다."], ["He could not deny that a secret longing for the assurance that she loved him was at the root of all his words.", "그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 확신에 대한 은밀한 갈망이 그의 모든 말 밑바닥에 깔려 있음을 그는 부정할 수 없었다."]], [["Neither of them knew how long they stood in that way.", "두 사람은 그렇게 얼마나 오래 서 있었는지 알지 못했다."], ["Dorothea was raising her eyes, and was about to speak, when the door opened and her footman came to say—", "도러시아가 눈을 들어 막 입을 열려는데 문이 열리고 하인이 들어와 말했다."]], [["“The horses are ready, madam, whenever you like to start.”", "\"말들이 준비되었습니다, 마님. 떠나고 싶으실 때 언제든지요.\""]], [["“Presently,”", "\"이따가요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["Then turning to Will, she said, “I have some memoranda to write for the housekeeper.”", "그러더니 윌에게로 돌아서서 말했다. \"가정부에게 적어 줄 메모가 좀 있어요.\""]], [["“I must go,”", "\"저는 가야 합니다.\""], ["said Will, when the door had closed again—advancing towards her.", "문이 다시 닫히자 윌이 그녀에게 다가서며 말했다."], ["“The day after to-morrow I shall leave Middlemarch.”", "\"모레 저는 미들마치를 떠납니다.\""]], [["“You have acted in every way rightly,”", "\"당신은 모든 면에서 옳게 처신하셨어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, in a low tone, feeling a pressure at her heart which made it difficult to speak.", "도러시아가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 가슴을 짓누르는 압박감에 말하기가 힘겨웠다."]], [["She put out her hand, and Will took it for an instant without speaking, for her words had seemed to him cruelly cold and unlike herself.", "그녀가 손을 내밀자 윌은 잠시 아무 말 없이 그 손을 잡았으니, 그녀의 말이 그에게는 잔인할 만큼 차갑고 그녀답지 않게 들렸기 때문이다."], ["Their eyes met, but there was discontent in his, and in hers there was only sadness.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤으나, 그의 눈에는 불만이, 그녀의 눈에는 오직 슬픔만이 어려 있었다."], ["He turned away and took his portfolio under his arm.", "그는 몸을 돌려 화첩을 옆구리에 끼었다."]], [["“I have never done you injustice.", "\"저는 당신에게 부당하게 군 적이 없어요."], ["Please remember me,”", "부디 저를 기억해 주세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, repressing a rising sob.", "도러시아가 솟구치는 흐느낌을 억누르며 말했다."]], [["“Why should you say that?”", "\"어째서 그런 말씀을 하시는 거예요?\""], ["said Will, with irritation.", "윌이 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["“As if I were not in danger of forgetting everything else.”", "\"마치 제가 다른 모든 걸 잊어버릴 위험에 처하지 않기라도 한 것처럼요.\""]], [["He had really a movement of anger against her at that moment, and it impelled him to go away without pause.", "그 순간 그는 정말로 그녀에 대한 분노가 일었고, 그것이 그를 망설임 없이 떠나도록 떠밀었다."], ["It was all one flash to Dorothea—his last words—his distant bow to her as he reached the door—the sense that he was no longer there.", "도러시아에게는 모든 것이 한순간의 섬광 같았다. 그의 마지막 말, 문에 이르러 그녀에게 한 멀찍한 인사, 그가 더는 그곳에 없다는 느낌."], ["She sank into the chair, and for a few moments sat like a statue, while images and emotions were hurrying upon her.", "그녀는 의자에 주저앉아, 영상과 감정이 그녀에게 밀려드는 가운데 잠시 조각상처럼 앉아 있었다."], ["Joy came first, in spite of the threatening train behind it—joy in the impression that it was really herself whom Will loved and was renouncing, that there was really no other love less permissible, more blameworthy, which honor was hurrying him away from.", "뒤따르는 위협적인 행렬에도 불구하고 먼저 찾아온 것은 기쁨이었다. 윌이 사랑하면서도 단념하는 대상이 정말로 그녀 자신이라는 인상에서 오는 기쁨, 명예가 그를 황급히 떼어 놓는, 더 용납될 수 없고 더 비난받을 다른 사랑 따위는 정말로 없었다는 인상에서 오는 기쁨이었다."], ["They were parted all the same, but—Dorothea drew a deep breath and felt her strength return—she could think of him unrestrainedly.", "그래도 두 사람은 헤어졌지만, 도러시아는 깊이 숨을 들이쉬며 힘이 돌아오는 것을 느꼈다. 이제 그녀는 거리낌 없이 그를 생각할 수 있었다."], ["At that moment the parting was easy to bear: the first sense of loving and being loved excluded sorrow.", "그 순간 작별은 견디기 쉬웠으니, 사랑하고 사랑받는다는 첫 자각이 슬픔을 몰아냈던 것이다."], ["It was as if some hard icy pressure had melted, and her consciousness had room to expand: her past was come back to her with larger interpretation.", "마치 어떤 단단하고 차가운 압박이 녹아내려 그녀의 의식이 펼쳐질 공간을 얻은 듯했다. 그녀의 과거가 더 큰 의미를 띠고 그녀에게 되돌아왔다."], ["The joy was not the less—perhaps it was the more complete just then—because of the irrevocable parting; for there was no reproach, no contemptuous wonder to imagine in any eye or from any lips.", "그 기쁨은 돌이킬 수 없는 작별 때문에 덜하지 않았으니, 어쩌면 바로 그렇기에 그 순간 더욱 온전했는지도 모른다. 어떤 눈에서도, 어떤 입술에서도 책망이나 경멸 어린 의아함을 상상할 필요가 없었기 때문이다."], ["He had acted so as to defy reproach, and make wonder respectful.", "그는 책망을 거부하고, 의아함을 경의로 바꾸어 놓는 식으로 처신했던 것이다."]], [["Any one watching her might have seen that there was a fortifying thought within her.", "누군가 그녀를 지켜보았다면 그녀 안에 마음을 굳건히 다지는 어떤 생각이 자리 잡았음을 알아보았을 것이다."], ["Just as when inventive power is working with glad ease some small claim on the attention is fully met as if it were only a cranny opened to the sunlight, it was easy now for Dorothea to write her memoranda.", "창조의 힘이 기쁜 수월함으로 작동할 때 사소한 주의의 요구쯤은, 마치 햇빛이 비쳐 드는 작은 틈에 지나지 않는 듯 충분히 채워지듯이, 이제 도러시아가 메모를 적는 일은 수월했다."], ["She spoke her last words to the housekeeper in cheerful tones, and when she seated herself in the carriage her eyes were bright and her cheeks blooming under the dismal bonnet.", "그녀는 가정부에게 명랑한 어조로 마지막 말을 건넸고, 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉았을 때 그녀의 눈은 빛났으며 음울한 보닛 아래 두 뺨은 발그레 피어났다."], ["She threw back the heavy “weepers,” and looked before her, wondering which road Will had taken.", "그녀는 무거운 \"상장(喪章) 베일\"을 뒤로 젖히고 앞을 바라보며, 윌이 어느 길로 갔을지 궁금해했다."], ["It was in her nature to be proud that he was blameless, and through all her feelings there ran this vein—“I was right to defend him.”", "그가 결백하다는 사실을 자랑스러워하는 것이 그녀의 천성이었고, 그녀의 모든 감정을 관통하며 이런 가락이 흘렀다. \"내가 그를 변호한 건 옳았어.\""]], [["The coachman was used to drive his grays at a good pace, Mr.", "마부는 회색 말들을 빠른 속도로 모는 데 익숙했다."], ["Casaubon being unenjoying and impatient in everything away from his desk, and wanting to get to the end of all journeys; and Dorothea was now bowled along quickly.", "캐소본 씨는 책상을 떠난 모든 일에서 즐거움을 느끼지 못하고 조급해하며 어떤 여정이든 끝에 다다르고 싶어 했기 때문이다. 그래서 도러시아는 이제 빠르게 실려 갔다."], ["Driving was pleasant, for rain in the night had laid the dust, and the blue sky looked far off, away from the region of the great clouds that sailed in masses.", "마차를 타는 것은 즐거웠으니, 밤사이 내린 비가 먼지를 가라앉혔고, 푸른 하늘은 무리 지어 떠가는 거대한 구름의 영역에서 멀리 떨어져 아득히 보였다."], ["The earth looked like a happy place under the vast heavens, and Dorothea was wishing that she might overtake Will and see him once more.", "광활한 하늘 아래 대지는 행복한 곳처럼 보였고, 도러시아는 윌을 따라잡아 한 번 더 그를 볼 수 있기를 바라고 있었다."]], [["After a turn of the road, there he was with the portfolio under his arm; but the next moment she was passing him while he raised his hat, and she felt a pang at being seated there in a sort of exaltation, leaving him behind. She could not look back at him.", "길모퉁이를 돌자 그가 화첩을 옆구리에 낀 채 거기 있었다."], ["It was as if a crowd of indifferent objects had thrust them asunder, and forced them along different paths, taking them farther and farther away from each other, and making it useless to look back.", "그러나 다음 순간 그녀는 그를 지나치고 있었고 그는 모자를 들어 인사했다. 그녀는 일종의 고양감에 젖어 거기 앉아 그를 뒤에 남겨 두고 떠난다는 데서 가슴이 저미는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She could no more make any sign that would seem to say, “Need we part?”", "그녀는 그를 돌아볼 수 없었다. 마치 무심한 사물들의 무리가 두 사람을 갈라놓고 서로 다른 길로 떠밀어, 점점 더 멀리 떼어 놓으며 돌아보는 일을 부질없게 만든 것 같았다."], ["than she could stop the carriage to wait for him.", "\"우리가 꼭 헤어져야 하나요?\""], ["Nay, what a world of reasons crowded upon her against any movement of her thought towards a future that might reverse the decision of this day!", "하고 말하는 듯한 어떤 신호도 그녀는 보낼 수 없었으니, 마차를 멈추고 그를 기다릴 수 없는 것과 마찬가지였다. 아니, 오늘의 결정을 뒤집을지도 모를 미래로 그녀의 생각이 조금이라도 움직이는 것을 막는 온갖 이유들이 그 얼마나 많이 그녀에게 밀려들었던가!"]], [["“I only wish I had known before—I wish he knew—then we could be quite happy in thinking of each other, though we are forever parted.", "\"진작 알았더라면. 그분도 알았더라면."], ["And if I could but have given him the money, and made things easier for him!”—were the longings that came back the most persistently.", "그랬다면 우리는 영영 헤어졌어도 서로를 생각하며 더없이 행복할 수 있을 텐데. 그리고 그분에게 그 돈을 드려서, 그분의 처지를 좀 더 편하게 해 드릴 수 있었더라면!\""], ["And yet, so heavily did the world weigh on her in spite of her independent energy, that with this idea of Will as in need of such help and at a disadvantage with the world, there came always the vision of that unfittingness of any closer relation between them which lay in the opinion of every one connected with her.", "가장 끈질기게 되돌아오는 것은 이런 갈망들이었다. 그러나 그녀의 독립적인 기개에도 불구하고 세상은 그녀를 무겁게 짓눌렀으므로, 윌이 그런 도움을 필요로 하며 세상에서 불리한 처지에 놓여 있다는 이 생각에는, 두 사람 사이의 어떤 더 가까운 관계도 어울리지 않는다는 영상이 늘 따라붙었다."], ["She felt to the full all the imperativeness of the motives which urged Will’s conduct.", "그것은 그녀와 관계된 모든 사람의 의견 속에 자리 잡고 있는 것이었다. 그녀는 윌의 처신을 떠민 동기들이 얼마나 절박한 것이었는지 온전히 느꼈다."], ["How could he dream of her defying the barrier that her husband had placed between them?—how could she ever say to herself that she would defy it?", "남편이 두 사람 사이에 놓아둔 장벽에 그녀가 맞설 것이라고 그가 어찌 꿈엔들 상상할 수 있었겠는가? 그녀가 그것에 맞서겠다고 자신에게 어찌 말할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Will’s certainty as the carriage grew smaller in the distance, had much more bitterness in it.", "마차가 멀리서 점점 작아져 가는 동안 윌이 품은 확신에는 훨씬 더 많은 쓰라림이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Very slight matters were enough to gall him in his sensitive mood, and the sight of Dorothea driving past him while he felt himself plodding along as a poor devil seeking a position in a world which in his present temper offered him little that he coveted, made his conduct seem a mere matter of necessity, and took away the sustainment of resolve.", "예민해진 그의 기분에는 아주 사소한 일들도 그를 괴롭히기에 충분했다. 도러시아가 마차를 타고 자신을 지나쳐 가는 광경, 그러는 동안 자신은 지금의 심정으로는 탐낼 만한 것을 거의 내주지 않는 세상에서 한 자리를 구하는 가련한 신세로 터덜터덜 걸어가고 있다는 느낌, 그것은 그의 처신을 한낱 어쩔 수 없는 일로 보이게 했고 결심을 떠받치던 버팀목을 앗아 갔다."], ["After all, he had no assurance that she loved him: could any man pretend that he was simply glad in such a case to have the suffering all on his own side?", "결국 그는 그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 확신이 없었다. 이런 처지에서 그 고통을 죄다 자기 몫으로만 떠안고도 단지 기쁘다고 가장할 수 있는 남자가 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["That evening Will spent with the Lydgates; the next evening he was gone.", "그날 저녁 윌은 리드게이트 부부와 함께 보냈고, 이튿날 저녁 그는 떠나고 없었다."]], [["These little things are great to little man.—GOLDSMITH.", "이런 사소한 일들이 사소한 인간에게는 위대하다. — 골드스미스"]], [["“Have you seen much of your scientific phoenix, Lydgate, lately?” said Mr.", "\"요즘 자네의 과학적 불사조인 리드게이트를 자주 보았나?\""], ["Toller at one of his Christmas dinner-parties, speaking to Mr. Farebrother on his right hand.", "토러 씨가 크리스마스 만찬 모임에서 오른편에 앉은 페어브러더 씨에게 말했다."]], [["“Not much, I am sorry to say,”", "\"안타깝지만 자주 보지는 못했습니다.\""], ["answered the Vicar, accustomed to parry Mr. Toller’s banter about his belief in the new medical light.", "새로운 의학적 빛에 대한 자신의 믿음을 두고 토러 씨가 던지는 농담을 받아넘기는 데 익숙한 부목사가 대답했다."], ["“I am out of the way and he is too busy.”", "\"저는 외진 곳에 있고 그는 너무 바쁘니까요.\""]], [["“Is he?", "\"그래요?"], ["I am glad to hear it,”", "그렇다니 다행입니다.\""], ["said Dr. Minchin, with mingled suavity and surprise.", "민친 박사가 정중함과 놀라움을 뒤섞어 말했다."]], [["“He gives a great deal of time to the New Hospital,” said Mr.", "\"그는 새 병원에 많은 시간을 쏟고 있습니다.\""], ["Farebrother, who had his reasons for continuing the subject: “I hear of that from my neighbor, Mrs.", "페어브러더 씨가 말했는데, 그가 이 화제를 이어 가는 데에는 나름의 이유가 있었다. \"그 이야기는 이웃인 캐소본 부인에게서 듣지요."], ["Casaubon, who goes there often.", "그분이 그곳에 자주 가시거든요."], ["She says Lydgate is indefatigable, and is making a fine thing of Bulstrode’s institution.", "부인 말씀으로는 리드게이트가 지칠 줄 모르고 일하며, 불스트로드의 시설을 훌륭하게 만들어 가고 있다더군요."], ["He is preparing a new ward in case of the cholera coming to us.”", "콜레라가 우리에게 닥칠 경우에 대비해 새 병동을 준비하고 있답니다.\""]], [["“And preparing theories of treatment to try on the patients, I suppose,”", "\"그리고 짐작건대 환자들에게 시험해 볼 치료 이론도 준비하고 있겠지요.\""], ["said Mr. Toller.", "토러 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Come, Toller, be candid,”", "\"자, 토러, 솔직해지십시오.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother.", "페어브러더 씨가 말했다."], ["“You are too clever not to see the good of a bold fresh mind in medicine, as well as in everything else; and as to cholera, I fancy, none of you are very sure what you ought to do.", "\"당신은 다른 모든 분야에서와 마찬가지로 의학에서도 대담하고 신선한 정신이 주는 이로움을 모를 만큼 어리석은 분이 아닙니다. 그리고 콜레라로 말하자면, 제 생각엔 여러분 누구도 마땅히 무엇을 해야 할지 확신하지 못하고 있어요."], ["If a man goes a little too far along a new road, it is usually himself that he harms more than any one else.”", "어떤 사람이 새 길을 따라 조금 멀리 나아간다 해도, 대개는 다른 누구보다 자기 자신에게 가장 큰 해를 끼치는 법이지요.\""]], [["“I am sure you and Wrench ought to be obliged to him,” said Dr.", "\"당신과 렌치는 분명 그에게 고마워해야 합니다.\""], ["Minchin, looking towards Toller, “for he has sent you the cream of Peacock’s patients.”", "민친 박사가 토러 쪽을 바라보며 말했다. \"그가 피콕의 환자 중에서도 알짜배기를 당신들에게 보내 주었으니까요.\""]], [["“Lydgate has been living at a great rate for a young beginner,”", "\"리드게이트는 풋내기 초년생치고는 무척 호화롭게 살고 있어요.\""], ["said Mr. Harry Toller, the brewer.", "양조업자인 해리 토러 씨가 말했다."], ["“I suppose his relations in the North back him up.”", "\"북부에 있는 친척들이 그를 뒷받침해 주는 모양이지요.\""]], [["“I hope so,” said Mr.", "\"그러길 바랍니다.\""], ["Chichely, “else he ought not to have married that nice girl we were all so fond of.", "치철리 씨가 말했다. \"그렇지 않다면 우리 모두가 그토록 아끼던 그 예쁜 아가씨와 결혼하지 말았어야지요."], ["Hang it, one has a grudge against a man who carries off the prettiest girl in the town.”", "제기랄, 동네에서 가장 예쁜 아가씨를 채 가는 남자에게는 원한이 생기는 법이거든요.\""]], [["“Ay, by God!", "\"맞아요, 정말로!"], ["and the best too,” said Mr.", "게다가 가장 착한 아가씨이기도 하지요.\""], ["Standish.", "스탠디시 씨가 말했다."]], [["“My friend Vincy didn’t half like the marriage, I know that,”", "\"내 친구 빈시는 그 결혼을 절반도 마음에 들어 하지 않았어요."], ["said Mr. Chichely.", "그건 내가 압니다.\""], ["“He wouldn’t do much.", "치철리 씨가 말했다."], ["How the relations on the other side may have come down I can’t say.”", "\"그 사람은 별로 해 주지 않을 거예요. 다른 쪽 친척들이 얼마나 보태 주었는지는 나도 알 수 없지만요.\""], ["There was an emphatic kind of reticence in Mr. Chichely’s manner of speaking.", "치철리 씨의 말투에는 어딘가 의미심장한 함구의 기색이 있었다."]], [["“Oh, I shouldn’t think Lydgate ever looked to practice for a living,” said Mr.", "\"오, 리드게이트가 생계를 위해 개업의 일에 기대를 걸었으리라고는 생각하지 않습니다.\""], ["Toller, with a slight touch of sarcasm, and there the subject was dropped.", "토러 씨가 가벼운 빈정거림을 담아 말했고, 그쯤에서 화제는 사그라들었다."]], [["This was not the first time that Mr.", "리드게이트의 씀씀이가 개업으로 감당하기에는 분명 지나치게 크다는 암시를 페어브러더 씨가 들은 것은 이번이 처음이 아니었다."], ["Farebrother had heard hints of Lydgate’s expenses being obviously too great to be met by his practice, but he thought it not unlikely that there were resources or expectations which excused the large outlay at the time of Lydgate’s marriage, and which might hinder any bad consequences from the disappointment in his practice.", "하지만 그는 리드게이트의 결혼 무렵 그 큰 지출을 정당화해 줄, 그리고 개업이 기대에 못 미치더라도 나쁜 결과를 막아 줄 만한 재력이나 전망이 있을 법하다고 생각했다. 어느 날 저녁 그가 옛날처럼 리드게이트와 한담을 나누려고 일부러 수고를 들여 미들마치까지 갔을 때, 그는 리드게이트에게서 평소의 모습과는 사뭇 다른, 흥분된 노력의 기색을 알아차렸다."], ["One evening, when he took the pains to go to Middlemarch on purpose to have a chat with Lydgate as of old, he noticed in him an air of excited effort quite unlike his usual easy way of keeping silence or breaking it with abrupt energy whenever he had anything to say.", "평소 리드게이트는 할 말이 있을 때면 침묵을 지키거나 불쑥 활기차게 그 침묵을 깨곤 했었다. 그들이 작업실에 있는 동안 리드게이트는 끈질기게 말을 이어, 어떤 생물학적 견해들의 개연성에 대한 찬반 논거를 늘어놓았다."], ["Lydgate talked persistently when they were in his work-room, putting arguments for and against the probability of certain biological views; but he had none of those definite things to say or to show which give the waymarks of a patient uninterrupted pursuit, such as he used himself to insist on, saying that “there must be a systole and diastole in all inquiry,”", "그러나 그에게는 끈기 있고 끊임없는 탐구의 이정표가 되어 줄 그런 명확한 이야기나 보여 줄 것이 전혀 없었다. 예전의 그라면 \"모든 탐구에는 수축기와 확장기가 있어야 한다\"고, \"사람의 정신은 인류 전체의 지평과 대물렌즈의 지평 사이에서 끊임없이 팽창하고 수축해야 한다\"고 주장하며 그런 것들을 고집했을 텐데 말이다."], ["and that “a man’s mind must be continually expanding and shrinking between the whole human horizon and the horizon of an object-glass.”", "그날 저녁 그는 어떤 개인적인 함의를 거부하기 위해 일부러 두루뭉술하게 말하는 듯 보였다."], ["That evening he seemed to be talking widely for the sake of resisting any personal bearing; and before long they went into the drawing room, where Lydgate, having asked Rosamond to give them music, sank back in his chair in silence, but with a strange light in his eyes.", "그리고 얼마 지나지 않아 그들은 응접실로 들어갔는데, 리드게이트는 로저먼드에게 음악을 들려 달라고 청한 뒤 말없이 의자에 깊숙이 몸을 기댔으나, 그의 눈에는 기이한 빛이 어려 있었다. \"혹시 아편제를 복용했나\""], ["“He may have been taking an opiate,” was a thought that crossed Mr.", "하는 생각이 페어브러더 씨의 머리를 스쳤다."], ["Farebrother’s mind—“tic-douloureux perhaps—or medical worries.”", "\"어쩌면 안면 신경통일지도 — 아니면 의료상의 근심거리이거나.\""]], [["It did not occur to him that Lydgate’s marriage was not delightful: he believed, as the rest did, that Rosamond was an amiable, docile creature, though he had always thought her rather uninteresting—a little too much the pattern-card of the finishing-school; and his mother could not forgive Rosamond because she never seemed to see that Henrietta Noble was in the room.", "리드게이트의 결혼 생활이 즐겁지 않으리라는 생각은 그에게 떠오르지 않았다. 그는 나머지 사람들과 마찬가지로 로저먼드가 상냥하고 고분고분한 사람이라고 믿었다."], ["“However, Lydgate fell in love with her,”", "다만 그는 늘 그녀가 다소 흥미롭지 못하다고 — 마무리 학교의 견본 카드를 좀 지나치게 닮았다고 여겨 왔고, 그의 어머니는 로저먼드가 헨리에타 노블이 방 안에 있다는 사실을 도무지 알아차리는 법이 없다는 이유로 그녀를 용서하지 못했다. \"하지만 어쨌든 리드게이트가 그녀와 사랑에 빠졌으니,\""], ["said the Vicar to himself, “and she must be to his taste.”", "부목사는 속으로 생각했다. \"그녀가 그의 취향에 맞는 사람일 테지.\""]], [["Mr. Farebrother was aware that Lydgate was a proud man, but having very little corresponding fibre in himself, and perhaps too little care about personal dignity, except the dignity of not being mean or foolish, he could hardly allow enough for the way in which Lydgate shrank, as from a burn, from the utterance of any word about his private affairs.", "페어브러더 씨는 리드게이트가 자존심이 강한 사람임을 알고 있었지만, 자신에게는 그에 상응하는 기질이 거의 없었고, 어쩌면 비열하거나 어리석지 않다는 품위 외에는 개인적 위엄에 대한 관심이 너무 적었던 탓에, 리드게이트가 자기 사사로운 일에 관한 말이라면 무엇이든 화상이라도 입은 듯 움츠러드는 그 방식을 충분히 헤아리기 어려웠다."], ["And soon after that conversation at Mr. Toller’s, the Vicar learned something which made him watch the more eagerly for an opportunity of indirectly letting Lydgate know that if he wanted to open himself about any difficulty there was a friendly ear ready.", "그리고 토러 씨 댁에서의 그 대화가 있고 얼마 지나지 않아, 부목사는 어떤 사실을 알게 되었는데, 그로 인해 그는 리드게이트가 어떤 어려움에 대해 마음을 털어놓고 싶어 할 경우 기꺼이 들어 줄 다정한 귀가 준비되어 있다는 것을 넌지시 알릴 기회를 더욱 간절히 엿보게 되었다."]], [["The opportunity came at Mr.", "그 기회는 빈시 씨 댁에서 찾아왔다."], ["Vincy’s, where, on New Year’s Day, there was a party, to which Mr. Farebrother was irresistibly invited, on the plea that he must not forsake his old friends on the first new year of his being a greater man, and Rector as well as Vicar.", "새해 첫날 그곳에서 모임이 열렸는데, 페어브러더 씨는 더 높은 인물이자 부목사인 동시에 교구 목사가 된 첫 새해에 옛 친구들을 저버려서는 안 된다는 명분으로 거절할 수 없게 초대받았다."], ["And this party was thoroughly friendly: all the ladies of the Farebrother family were present; the Vincy children all dined at the table, and Fred had persuaded his mother that if she did not invite Mary Garth, the Farebrothers would regard it as a slight to themselves, Mary being their particular friend.", "그리고 이 모임은 더할 나위 없이 정겨운 자리였다. 페어브러더 집안의 여인들이 모두 참석했고, 빈시가의 아이들은 전부 식탁에서 함께 저녁을 먹었으며, 프레드는 어머니에게 메리 가스를 초대하지 않으면 페어브러더 집안 사람들이 자기들에 대한 결례로 여길 것이라고 설득했다."], ["Mary came, and Fred was in high spirits, though his enjoyment was of a checkered kind—triumph that his mother should see Mary’s importance with the chief personages in the party being much streaked with jealousy when Mr.", "메리가 그들의 각별한 친구였기 때문이다. 메리가 왔고, 프레드는 기분이 무척 좋았으나, 그 즐거움에는 얼룩이 섞여 있었다 — 어머니가 메리가 모임의 주요 인사들에게 얼마나 중요한 존재인지 보게 되리라는 의기양양함은, 페어브러더 씨가 메리 곁에 앉자 질투로 잔뜩 줄이 그어졌다."], ["Farebrother sat down by her.", "프레드는 \"페어브러더에게 밀려날까\""], ["Fred used to be much more easy about his own accomplishments in the days when he had not begun to dread being “bowled out by Farebrother,”", "두려워하기 시작하기 전, 그러니까 자기 자질에 대해 훨씬 마음 편하던 시절이 있었는데, 이 공포는 여전히 그의 앞에 도사리고 있었다. 무르익은 부인의 자태를 한껏 뽐내는 빈시 부인은 메리의 자그마한 몸집과 거칠게 물결치는 머리카락, 백합도 장미도 없는 얼굴을 바라보며 의아해했다."], ["and this terror was still before him. Mrs.", "그녀는 메리가 혼례복을 입은 모습에 마음을 쓰는 자신을, 혹은 가스 집안을 \"빼닮은\""], ["Vincy, in her fullest matronly bloom, looked at Mary’s little figure, rough wavy hair, and visage quite without lilies and roses, and wondered; trying unsuccessfully to fancy herself caring about Mary’s appearance in wedding clothes, or feeling complacency in grandchildren who would “feature”", "손주들에게 흐뭇해하는 자신을 그려 보려 했으나 헛수고였다. 그래도 모임은 즐거웠고 메리는 유난히 환했다."], ["the Garths. However, the party was a merry one, and Mary was particularly bright; being glad, for Fred’s sake, that his friends were getting kinder to her, and being also quite willing that they should see how much she was valued by others whom they must admit to be judges.", "프레드를 위해 그의 친구들이 자기에게 점점 더 다정해지는 것이 기뻤고, 또한 그들이 인정할 수밖에 없는 안목 있는 다른 이들에게 자기가 얼마나 높이 평가받는지 그들이 보는 것도 전혀 마다하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Mr. Farebrother noticed that Lydgate seemed bored, and that Mr.", "페어브러더 씨는 리드게이트가 지루해하는 듯 보인다는 것과 빈시 씨가 사위에게 되도록 말을 적게 건넨다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Vincy spoke as little as possible to his son-in-law. Rosamond was perfectly graceful and calm, and only a subtle observation such as the Vicar had not been roused to bestow on her would have perceived the total absence of that interest in her husband’s presence which a loving wife is sure to betray, even if etiquette keeps her aloof from him.", "로저먼드는 더없이 우아하고 침착했으며, 부목사가 미처 그녀에게 기울이지 않은 종류의 섬세한 관찰력으로만 알아챌 수 있을, 남편의 존재에 대한 관심이 완전히 결여된 모습 — 사랑하는 아내라면 예의상 남편과 거리를 두더라도 반드시 드러내고야 마는 그 관심이 전혀 없다는 사실은 눈에 띄지 않았다."], ["When Lydgate was taking part in the conversation, she never looked towards him any more than if she had been a sculptured Psyche modelled to look another way: and when, after being called out for an hour or two, he re-entered the room, she seemed unconscious of the fact, which eighteen months before would have had the effect of a numeral before ciphers.", "리드게이트가 대화에 끼어들 때면 그녀는 마치 다른 곳을 바라보도록 빚어진 프시케 조각상이라도 된 듯 그를 한 번도 바라보지 않았다. 그리고 한두 시간 불려 나갔다가 그가 방으로 다시 들어왔을 때, 그녀는 그 사실을 의식조차 못 하는 듯했는데, 열여덟 달 전이라면 그의 등장은 영(零)들 앞에 놓인 숫자 같은 효과를 냈을 것이다."], ["In reality, however, she was intensely aware of Lydgate’s voice and movements; and her pretty good-tempered air of unconsciousness was a studied negation by which she satisfied her inward opposition to him without compromise of propriety.", "그러나 실제로 그녀는 리드게이트의 목소리와 움직임을 강렬하게 의식하고 있었다. 의식하지 못하는 척하는 그 예쁘고 온화한 태도는, 예법을 어기지 않으면서 그를 향한 내면의 반감을 충족시키는, 계산된 부정이었다."], ["When the ladies were in the drawing-room after Lydgate had been called away from the dessert, Mrs.", "디저트 자리에서 리드게이트가 불려 나간 뒤 부인들이 응접실에 모여 있을 때, 페어브러더 부인은 마침 로저먼드가 가까이 있자 이렇게 말했다."], ["Farebrother, when Rosamond happened to be near her, said—“You have to give up a great deal of your husband’s society, Mrs. Lydgate.”", "\"남편과 함께하는 시간을 많이 포기해야겠군요, 리드게이트 부인.\""]], [["“Yes, the life of a medical man is very arduous: especially when he is so devoted to his profession as Mr.", "\"네, 의사의 삶은 무척 고되지요. 특히 리드게이트 씨처럼 자기 직업에 헌신적일 때는요.\""], ["Lydgate is,” said Rosamond, who was standing, and moved easily away at the end of this correct little speech.", "서 있던 로저먼드는 이 반듯한 짧은 인사말을 끝내고는 가벼이 자리를 떴다."]], [["“It is dreadfully dull for her when there is no company,” said Mrs.", "\"손님이 없을 때 저 애는 끔찍이도 따분해한답니다.\""], ["Vincy, who was seated at the old lady’s side.", "노부인 곁에 앉아 있던 빈시 부인이 말했다."], ["“I am sure I thought so when Rosamond was ill, and I was staying with her.", "\"로저먼드가 아파서 제가 함께 머무는 동안 정말 그렇게 느꼈지요."], ["You know, Mrs. Farebrother, ours is a cheerful house.", "아시다시피, 페어브러더 부인, 우리 집은 활기찬 집이에요."], ["I am of a cheerful disposition myself, and Mr. Vincy always likes something to be going on.", "저 자신이 명랑한 기질이고, 빈시 씨도 늘 뭔가 일이 벌어지는 걸 좋아하지요."], ["That is what Rosamond has been used to.", "로저먼드는 그런 데 익숙해 있어요."], ["Very different from a husband out at odd hours, and never knowing when he will come home, and of a close, proud disposition, I think”—indiscreet Mrs.", "시도 때도 없이 밖에 나가 있고, 언제 집에 돌아올지 도무지 알 수 없고, 게다가 제 생각엔 속을 닫아 두는 자존심 센 기질인 남편하고는 아주 딴판이지요.\" 경솔한 빈시 부인은 이 곁들인 말을 할 때 목소리를 살짝 낮추긴 했다."], ["Vincy did lower her tone slightly with this parenthesis.", "\"하지만 로저먼드는 늘 천사 같은 성품이었답니다."], ["“But Rosamond always had an angel of a temper; her brothers used very often not to please her, but she was never the girl to show temper; from a baby she was always as good as good, and with a complexion beyond anything.", "오빠들이 그 애를 곧잘 못마땅하게 굴어도 그 애는 한 번도 성을 내는 법이 없었어요. 갓난아기 때부터 더없이 착했고, 살결도 이루 말할 수 없이 고왔지요."], ["But my children are all good-tempered, thank God.”", "하지만 우리 아이들은 모두 성품이 좋아요, 하느님 감사하게도.\""]], [["This was easily credible to any one looking at Mrs. Vincy as she threw back her broad cap-strings, and smiled towards her three little girls, aged from seven to eleven.", "빈시 부인이 넓은 모자 끈을 뒤로 젖히며 일곱 살에서 열한 살 사이의 어린 세 딸을 향해 미소 짓는 모습을 보는 누구에게나 이 말은 쉽사리 믿어질 만했다."], ["But in that smiling glance she was obliged to include Mary Garth, whom the three girls had got into a corner to make her tell them stories.", "그러나 미소 띤 그 눈길 속에 그녀는 메리 가스도 어쩔 수 없이 포함시켜야 했는데, 세 소녀가 메리를 구석으로 데려가 이야기를 해 달라고 졸랐기 때문이다. 메리는 마침 룸펠슈틸츠헨의 그 흥미진진한 이야기를 막 끝내 가던 참이었다."], ["Mary was just finishing the delicious tale of Rumpelstiltskin, which she had well by heart, because Letty was never tired of communicating it to her ignorant elders from a favorite red volume. Louisa, Mrs.", "레티가 자기가 좋아하는 빨간 책에서 그 이야기를 가져다 아무것도 모르는 어른들에게 들려주기를 결코 지겨워하지 않았던 덕에, 메리는 그 이야기를 줄줄 외우고 있었다."], ["Vincy’s darling, now ran to her with wide-eyed serious excitement, crying, “Oh mamma, mamma, the little man stamped so hard on the floor he couldn’t get his leg out again!”", "빈시 부인의 귀염둥이 루이자가 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고 진지한 흥분에 사로잡혀 어머니에게 달려와 외쳤다. \"오 엄마, 엄마, 그 난쟁이가 바닥을 어찌나 세게 굴렀던지 다리를 다시 빼낼 수가 없었대요!\""]], [["“Bless you, my cherub!”", "\"오냐, 내 천사야!\""], ["said mamma; “you shall tell me all about it to-morrow.", "어머니가 말했다. \"내일 나한테 그 이야기 다 들려주렴."], ["Go and listen!”", "가서 들어!\""], ["and then, as her eyes followed Louisa back towards the attractive corner, she thought that if Fred wished her to invite Mary again she would make no objection, the children being so pleased with her.", "그러고는 루이자가 그 매력적인 구석 자리로 돌아가는 모습을 눈으로 좇으며, 그녀는 프레드가 메리를 다시 초대하기를 바란다면 반대하지 않으리라고 생각했다. 아이들이 메리를 그토록 좋아하니 말이다."]], [["But presently the corner became still more animated, for Mr. Farebrother came in, and seating himself behind Louisa, took her on his lap; whereupon the girls all insisted that he must hear Rumpelstiltskin, and Mary must tell it over again.", "그러나 이내 그 구석은 한층 더 활기를 띠었으니, 페어브러더 씨가 들어와 루이자 뒤에 앉아 그 아이를 무릎에 앉혔기 때문이다."], ["He insisted too, and Mary, without fuss, began again in her neat fashion, with precisely the same words as before.", "그러자 소녀들은 모두 그가 룸펠슈틸츠헨 이야기를 꼭 들어야 한다고, 메리가 그 이야기를 다시 해야 한다고 졸랐다."], ["Fred, who had also seated himself near, would have felt unmixed triumph in Mary’s effectiveness if Mr.", "그도 졸랐고, 메리는 야단스럽지 않게 단정한 솜씨로 앞서와 정확히 똑같은 말로 다시 이야기를 시작했다."], ["Farebrother had not been looking at her with evident admiration, while he dramatized an intense interest in the tale to please the children.", "가까이 앉아 있던 프레드는, 페어브러더 씨가 아이들을 즐겁게 해 주려고 그 이야기에 깊은 흥미를 느끼는 양 연기하면서도 역력한 감탄의 눈길로 메리를 바라보지만 않았더라면, 메리의 솜씨에 순수한 의기양양함을 느꼈을 것이다."]], [["“You will never care any more about my one-eyed giant, Loo,”", "\"이제 너는 내 외눈박이 거인 이야기 따위는 더 이상 좋아하지 않겠구나, 루.\""], ["said Fred at the end.", "이야기가 끝나자 프레드가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I shall.", "\"아니야, 좋아할 거야."], ["Tell about him now,”", "지금 그 이야기 해 줘.\""], ["said Louisa.", "루이자가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I dare say; I am quite cut out.", "\"오, 그럴 테지. 난 완전히 밀려났어."], ["Ask Mr. Farebrother.”", "페어브러더 씨께 부탁드려.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["added Mary; “ask Mr.", "메리가 거들었다."], ["Farebrother to tell you about the ants whose beautiful house was knocked down by a giant named Tom, and he thought they didn’t mind because he couldn’t hear them cry, or see them use their pocket-handkerchiefs.”", "\"페어브러더 씨께 톰이라는 거인에게 아름다운 집이 부서진 개미들 이야기를 해 달라고 부탁드려 봐. 거인은 개미들이 우는 소리를 들을 수도, 손수건을 쓰는 모습을 볼 수도 없었기 때문에 개미들이 아무렇지 않은 줄 알았다는 이야기 말이야.\""]], [["“Please,”", "\"부탁이에요.\""], ["said Louisa, looking up at the Vicar.", "루이자가 부목사를 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["“No, no, I am a grave old parson.", "\"아니, 아니, 나는 근엄한 늙은 목사란다."], ["If I try to draw a story out of my bag a sermon comes instead.", "내가 보따리에서 이야기를 꺼내려 하면 그 대신 설교가 나오지."], ["Shall I preach you a sermon?”", "너희에게 설교를 한 자락 해 줄까?\""], ["said he, putting on his short-sighted glasses, and pursing up his lips.", "그가 근시용 안경을 쓰고 입을 오므리며 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Louisa, falteringly.", "루이자가 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Let me see, then.", "\"그럼 어디 보자."], ["Against cakes: how cakes are bad things, especially if they are sweet and have plums in them.”", "과자를 경계하라는 설교다. 과자가 얼마나 나쁜 것인지, 특히 달콤하고 그 안에 자두가 들어 있으면 더욱 그렇다는 이야기지.\""]], [["Louisa took the affair rather seriously, and got down from the Vicar’s knee to go to Fred.", "루이자는 그 일을 꽤 진지하게 받아들이고는 부목사의 무릎에서 내려와 프레드에게로 갔다."]], [["“Ah, I see it will not do to preach on New Year’s Day,”", "\"아, 새해 첫날에 설교는 통하지 않겠구나.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother, rising and walking away.", "페어브러더 씨가 일어나 자리를 옮기며 말했다."], ["He had discovered of late that Fred had become jealous of him, and also that he himself was not losing his preference for Mary above all other women.", "그는 최근 프레드가 자기를 질투하게 되었다는 것을, 또한 자기 자신도 다른 어떤 여자보다 메리를 더 좋아하는 마음을 잃지 않고 있다는 것을 깨달은 터였다."]], [["“A delightful young person is Miss Garth,” said Mrs.", "\"가스 양은 참 사랑스러운 아가씨예요.\""], ["Farebrother, who had been watching her son’s movements.", "아들의 거동을 지켜보던 페어브러더 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Mrs. Vincy, obliged to reply, as the old lady turned to her expectantly.", "노부인이 기대에 차 자기를 돌아보자 대답하지 않을 수 없었던 빈시 부인이 말했다."], ["“It is a pity she is not better-looking.”", "\"더 예쁘지 못한 게 안타깝지요.\""]], [["“I cannot say that,” said Mrs.", "\"저는 그렇게 말할 수 없군요.\""], ["Farebrother, decisively.", "페어브러더 부인이 단호하게 말했다."], ["“I like her countenance.", "\"저는 그 아이의 얼굴이 좋아요."], ["We must not always ask for beauty, when a good God has seen fit to make an excellent young woman without it.", "자비로우신 하느님께서 미모 없이도 훌륭한 처녀를 만드는 것이 마땅하다고 보셨을 때, 우리가 늘 미모를 요구해서는 안 되지요."], ["I put good manners first, and Miss Garth will know how to conduct herself in any station.”", "저는 좋은 몸가짐을 첫째로 꼽는데, 가스 양은 어떤 처지에서든 처신할 줄 알 거예요.\""]], [["The old lady was a little sharp in her tone, having a prospective reference to Mary’s becoming her daughter-in-law; for there was this inconvenience in Mary’s position with regard to Fred, that it was not suitable to be made public, and hence the three ladies at Lowick Parsonage were still hoping that Camden would choose Miss Garth.", "노부인의 어조는 다소 날카로웠는데, 메리가 자기 며느리가 되는 일을 내심 염두에 두고 있었기 때문이다. 프레드와 관련한 메리의 처지에는 이런 불편함이 있었으니, 그것을 공공연히 밝히는 것이 적절치 않다는 점이었다. 그래서 로윅 목사관의 세 여인은 여전히 캠던이 가스 양을 택하기를 바라고 있었다."]], [["New visitors entered, and the drawing-room was given up to music and games, while whist-tables were prepared in the quiet room on the other side of the hall.", "새 손님들이 들어왔고, 응접실은 음악과 놀이에 내맡겨졌으며, 그동안 홀 건너편 조용한 방에는 휘스트 탁자가 마련되었다."], ["Mr. Farebrother played a rubber to satisfy his mother, who regarded her occasional whist as a protest against scandal and novelty of opinion, in which light even a revoke had its dignity.", "페어브러더 씨는 어머니의 뜻을 채워 드리려고 한 판을 쳤는데, 어머니는 자신이 이따금 휘스트를 치는 것을 추문과 새로운 견해에 대한 항의로 여겼고, 그런 견지에서는 카드를 잘못 내는 실수조차 나름의 품위를 지녔다."], ["But at the end he got Mr. Chichely to take his place, and left the room.", "그러나 한 판이 끝나자 그는 치철리 씨에게 자기 자리를 넘기고 방을 나섰다."], ["As he crossed the hall, Lydgate had just come in and was taking off his great-coat.", "그가 홀을 가로지를 때, 리드게이트가 막 들어와 외투를 벗고 있었다."]], [["“You are the man I was going to look for,”", "\"마침 자네를 찾으러 가려던 참이었네.\""], ["said the Vicar; and instead of entering the drawing-room, they walked along the hall and stood against the fireplace, where the frosty air helped to make a glowing bank.", "부목사가 말했다. 그리고 그들은 응접실로 들어가는 대신 홀을 따라 걸어가 벽난로 앞에 섰는데, 서리 내린 찬 공기 덕에 난롯불이 한층 발갛게 타오르는 듯했다."], ["“You see, I can leave the whist-table easily enough,”", "\"보다시피 나는 이제 휘스트 탁자를 아주 쉽게 떠날 수 있다네.\""], ["he went on, smiling at Lydgate, “now I don’t play for money.", "그가 리드게이트에게 미소 지으며 말을 이었다. \"이제 돈을 걸고 치지 않으니까."], ["I owe that to you, Mrs. Casaubon says.”", "캐소본 부인 말씀으로는 그게 다 자네 덕이라더군.\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어째서요?\""], ["said Lydgate, coldly.", "리드게이트가 쌀쌀하게 말했다."]], [["“Ah, you didn’t mean me to know it; I call that ungenerous reticence.", "\"아, 자네는 내가 그걸 알기를 바라지 않았겠지. 나는 그런 걸 인색한 함구라고 부른다네."], ["You should let a man have the pleasure of feeling that you have done him a good turn.", "자네가 누군가에게 좋은 일을 해 주었다는 느낌을 그 사람도 누리게 해 줘야지."], ["I don’t enter into some people’s dislike of being under an obligation: upon my word, I prefer being under an obligation to everybody for behaving well to me.”", "나는 신세 지는 것을 싫어하는 어떤 사람들의 마음을 도무지 이해 못 하겠어. 정말이지 나는 나에게 잘 대해 준 모든 이에게 신세를 지는 편이 더 좋다네.\""]], [["“I can’t tell what you mean,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, “unless it is that I once spoke of you to Mrs. Casaubon.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["But I did not think that she would break her promise not to mention that I had done so,”", "\"제가 언젠가 캐소본 부인께 목사님에 대해 말씀드린 것을 두고 하시는 말씀이 아니라면 말이지요."], ["said Lydgate, leaning his back against the corner of the mantel-piece, and showing no radiance in his face.", "하지만 그 일을 발설하지 않겠다는 약속을 부인이 깨실 줄은 몰랐습니다.\" 리드게이트는 벽난로 선반 모서리에 등을 기댄 채 얼굴에 아무런 빛도 내비치지 않으며 말했다."]], [["“It was Brooke who let it out, only the other day.", "\"그걸 흘린 건 바로 얼마 전 브룩이었네. 그가 나에게 칭찬을 하더군."], ["He paid me the compliment of saying that he was very glad I had the living though you had come across his tactics, and had praised me up as a Ken and a Tillotson, and that sort of thing, till Mrs. Casaubon would hear of no one else.”", "자네가 자기 계산을 헝클어 놓고는, 캐소본 부인이 다른 누구의 이야기도 들으려 하지 않을 만큼 나를 켄이니 틸럿슨이니 하는 부류로 한껏 추켜세우긴 했지만, 그래도 내가 그 성직록을 얻게 되어 무척 기쁘다고 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh, Brooke is such a leaky-minded fool,”", "\"오, 브룩은 입이 가벼운 멍청이지요.\""], ["said Lydgate, contemptuously.", "리드게이트가 경멸하듯 말했다."]], [["“Well, I was glad of the leakiness then.", "\"글쎄, 그때만큼은 그 입 가벼움이 고마웠다네."], ["I don’t see why you shouldn’t like me to know that you wished to do me a service, my dear fellow.", "자네가 나에게 도움을 주고 싶어 했다는 것을 내가 아는 걸 자네가 어째서 싫어해야 하는지 나는 모르겠어, 이 사람아."], ["And you certainly have done me one.", "그리고 자네는 분명 나에게 도움을 주었네."], ["It’s rather a strong check to one’s self-complacency to find how much of one’s right doing depends on not being in want of money.", "자신의 올바른 행실이 얼마나 많이 돈에 궁하지 않은 데 달려 있는지를 깨닫고 나면 자기만족에 꽤 강한 제동이 걸리지."], ["A man will not be tempted to say the Lord’s Prayer backward to please the devil, if he doesn’t want the devil’s services.", "악마의 도움이 필요 없는 사람이라면, 악마를 기쁘게 하려고 주기도문을 거꾸로 외우라는 유혹에 빠지지 않을 거야."], ["I have no need to hang on the smiles of chance now.”", "나는 이제 우연의 미소에 매달릴 필요가 없다네.\""]], [["“I don’t see that there’s any money-getting without chance,”", "\"우연 없이 돈을 버는 길이 있다고는 생각하지 않습니다.\" 리드게이트가 말했다."], ["said Lydgate; “if a man gets it in a profession, it’s pretty sure to come by chance.”", "\"어떤 직업에서 돈을 번다 해도, 그것은 거의 틀림없이 우연으로 들어오는 것이니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Farebrother thought he could account for this speech, in striking contrast with Lydgate’s former way of talking, as the perversity which will often spring from the moodiness of a man ill at ease in his affairs.", "페어브러더 씨는 리드게이트의 예전 말투와 뚜렷이 대비되는 이 말을, 자기 일이 뜻대로 안 되는 사람의 침울함에서 흔히 솟아나는 심술로 이해할 수 있으리라 생각했다."], ["He answered in a tone of good-humored admission—", "그는 너그러운 수긍의 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Ah, there’s enormous patience wanted with the way of the world.", "\"아, 세상사 돌아가는 방식에는 엄청난 인내가 필요하지."], ["But it is the easier for a man to wait patiently when he has friends who love him, and ask for nothing better than to help him through, so far as it lies in their power.”", "하지만 자기를 사랑하고 힘닿는 데까지 도와주는 것 외에 더 바랄 게 없는 친구들이 있다면, 인내하며 기다리기가 그만큼 더 쉬운 법이라네.\""]], [["“Oh yes,”", "\"오 그렇지요.\""], ["said Lydgate, in a careless tone, changing his attitude and looking at his watch.", "리드게이트가 무심한 어조로 자세를 바꾸고 회중시계를 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“People make much more of their difficulties than they need to do.”", "\"사람들은 자기 어려움을 필요 이상으로 부풀리는 법이지요.\""]], [["He knew as distinctly as possible that this was an offer of help to himself from Mr. Farebrother, and he could not bear it.", "이것이 페어브러더 씨가 자기에게 건네는 도움의 제안이라는 것을 그는 더없이 또렷이 알고 있었고, 그것을 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["So strangely determined are we mortals, that, after having been long gratified with the sense that he had privately done the Vicar a service, the suggestion that the Vicar discerned his need of a service in return made him shrink into unconquerable reticence.", "우리 인간이란 참으로 기이하게도 고집스러운 존재여서, 오랫동안 자신이 남몰래 부목사에게 도움을 주었다는 느낌에 흐뭇해하던 그였건만, 부목사가 그 보답으로 도움이 필요한 자기 처지를 알아차렸다는 암시에 그는 도저히 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 함구 속으로 움츠러들었다. 게다가 그런 모든 제안의 뒤에 무엇이 따라와야 하겠는가?"], ["Besides, behind all making of such offers what else must come?—that he should “mention his case,”", "— 자기가 \"사정을 털어놓고\", 구체적으로 무언가가 필요하다는 것을 내비쳐야 한다는 것이었다."], ["imply that he wanted specific things. At that moment, suicide seemed easier.", "그 순간에는 차라리 자살하는 편이 더 쉬워 보였다."]], [["Mr. Farebrother was too keen a man not to know the meaning of that reply, and there was a certain massiveness in Lydgate’s manner and tone, corresponding with his physique, which if he repelled your advances in the first instance seemed to put persuasive devices out of question.", "페어브러더 씨는 그 대답의 의미를 모를 만큼 둔한 사람이 아니었고, 리드게이트의 태도와 어조에는 그의 체격에 어울리는 어떤 묵직함이 있어서, 그가 처음부터 상대의 접근을 물리치면 어떤 설득의 수단도 무용지물로 만들어 버리는 듯했다."]], [["“What time are you?”", "\"몇 시인가?\""], ["said the Vicar, devouring his wounded feeling.", "부목사가 상처 입은 마음을 삼키며 말했다."]], [["“After eleven,”", "\"열한 시가 넘었습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["And they went into the drawing-room.", "그리고 그들은 응접실로 들어갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXIV.", "# CHAPTER LXIV."]], [["1st Gent. Where lies the power, there let the blame lie too.", "제1신사. 권력이 있는 곳, 그곳에 책임 또한 있게 하라. 제2신사. 아니다, 권력이란 상대적인 것. 그대는 다가오는 역병을"]], [["2d Gent. Nay, power is relative; you cannot fright The coming pest with border fortresses, Or catch your carp with subtle argument. All force is twain in one: cause is not cause Unless effect be there; and action’s self Must needs contain a passive. So command Exists but with obedience.", "국경의 요새로 위협할 수 없고,\n교묘한 논변으로 잉어를 낚을 수도 없으니."]], [["Even if Lydgate had been inclined to be quite open about his affairs, he knew that it would have hardly been in Mr. Farebrother’s power to give him the help he immediately wanted.", "모든 힘은 하나 속에 둘이다."], ["With the year’s bills coming in from his tradesmen, with Dover’s threatening hold on his furniture, and with nothing to depend on but slow dribbling payments from patients who must not be offended—for the handsome fees he had had from Freshitt Hall and Lowick Manor had been easily absorbed—nothing less than a thousand pounds would have freed him from actual embarrassment, and left a residue which, according to the favorite phrase of hopefulness in such circumstances, would have given him “time to look about him.”", "결과가 없으면\n원인은 원인이 아니며, 행위 자체도"]], [["Naturally, the merry Christmas bringing the happy New Year, when fellow-citizens expect to be paid for the trouble and goods they have smilingly bestowed on their neighbors, had so tightened the pressure of sordid cares on Lydgate’s mind that it was hardly possible for him to think unbrokenly of any other subject, even the most habitual and soliciting. He was not an ill-tempered man; his intellectual activity, the ardent kindness of his heart, as well as his strong frame, would always, under tolerably easy conditions, have kept him above the petty uncontrolled susceptibilities which make bad temper. But he was now a prey to that worst irritation which arises not simply from annoyances, but from the second consciousness underlying those annoyances, of wasted energy and a degrading preoccupation, which was the reverse of all his former purposes. “This is what I am thinking of; and that is what I might have been thinking of,” was the bitter incessant murmur within him, making every difficulty a double goad to impatience.", "필연코 수동성을 품어야 한다. 그러므로 명령은\n오직 복종과 더불어서만 존재한다."]], [["Some gentlemen have made an amazing figure in literature by general discontent with the universe as a trap of dulness into which their great souls have fallen by mistake; but the sense of a stupendous self and an insignificant world may have its consolations.", "리드게이트가 자신의 형편에 대해 아주 솔직하게 털어놓을 마음이 있었다 하더라도, 페어브러더 씨가 그에게 당장 필요한 도움을 줄 만한 처지가 못 된다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 상인들로부터 한 해 치 청구서가 들어오고 있었고, 도버가 그의 가구에 대한 압류를 들이밀며 위협하고 있었으며, 비위를 거스를 수 없는 환자들로부터 찔끔찔끔 들어오는 더딘 진료비 말고는 의지할 데가 없었다—프레시트 저택과 로윅 영지에서 받은 두둑한 사례비는 어느새 흔적도 없이 사라져 버렸으니—천 파운드는 족히 있어야 실질적인 곤경에서 벗어나, 그러한 상황에서 흔히 입에 올리곤 하는 희망 섞인 표현을 빌리자면 “주위를 둘러볼 여유”를 줄 만한 잔액이 남을 터였다."], ["Lydgate’s discontent was much harder to bear: it was the sense that there was a grand existence in thought and effective action lying around him, while his self was being narrowed into the miserable isolation of egoistic fears, and vulgar anxieties for events that might allay such fears.", "당연하게도, 즐거운 성탄절이 행복한 새해를 데려오고, 시민들이 이웃에게 웃으며 베푼 수고와 물품에 대한 대가를 받기를 기대하는 이 시기는, 리드게이트의 마음을 짓누르는 옹색한 근심의 압박을 한층 죄어 놓았으니, 그로서는 다른 어떤 주제에 대해서도, 아무리 습관적이고 마음을 잡아끄는 것일지라도 끊김 없이 생각하기란 거의 불가능했다. 그는 성품이 고약한 사람이 아니었다."], ["His troubles will perhaps appear miserably sordid, and beneath the attention of lofty persons who can know nothing of debt except on a magnificent scale.", "그의 지적 활동, 마음에서 우러나는 열렬한 친절, 그리고 튼튼한 체격은, 그런대로 편안한 조건 아래서라면 언제든 그를 고약한 성미를 빚어내는 사소하고 통제되지 않은 예민함 위로 올려 두었을 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 단순한 골칫거리에서가 아니라, 그 골칫거리 밑에 깔린 두 번째 의식, 곧 헛되이 낭비된 정력과 그의 모든 지난날의 목표와는 정반대인 비루한 골몰에 대한 의식에서 비롯되는, 그 가장 고약한 짜증의 먹이가 되어 있었다."], ["Doubtless they were sordid; and for the majority, who are not lofty, there is no escape from sordidness but by being free from money-craving, with all its base hopes and temptations, its watching for death, its hinted requests, its horse-dealer’s desire to make bad work pass for good, its seeking for function which ought to be another’s, its compulsion often to long for Luck in the shape of a wide calamity.", "“지금 내가 골몰하는 것은 바로 이것이다. 그리고 내가 골몰할 수도 있었던 것은 저것이다.”—이것이 그의 내면에서 끊임없이 흐르는 쓰라린 중얼거림이었고, 그것이 모든 곤란을 조바심을 향한 이중의 채찍으로 만들었다."]], [["It was because Lydgate writhed under the idea of getting his neck beneath this vile yoke that he had fallen into a bitter moody state which was continually widening Rosamond’s alienation from him. After the first disclosure about the bill of sale, he had made many efforts to draw her into sympathy with him about possible measures for narrowing their expenses, and with the threatening approach of Christmas his propositions grew more and more definite. “We two can do with only one servant, and live on very little,” he said, “and I shall manage with one horse.” For Lydgate, as we have seen, had begun to reason, with a more distinct vision, about the expenses of living, and any share of pride he had given to appearances of that sort was meagre compared with the pride which made him revolt from exposure as a debtor, or from asking men to help him with their money.", "어떤 신사들은 자기네 위대한 영혼이 실수로 빠져든 권태의 덫으로 우주를 여기는 전반적인 불만을 토로함으로써 문학에서 놀라운 명성을 떨치기도 했다. 그러나 어마어마한 자아와 보잘것없는 세계에 대한 감각에는 나름의 위안이 따를 수도 있다. 리드게이트의 불만은 견디기가 훨씬 더 어려웠다. 그것은 사유와 효과적인 행동 속에 펼쳐진 웅대한 존재가 자기 둘레에 놓여 있는데도, 정작 자기 자신은 이기적인 두려움과 그러한 두려움을 가라앉혀 줄 사건에 대한 천박한 불안의 비참한 고립 속으로 좁혀져 들고 있다는 감각이었다. 그의 고민거리는 어쩌면 비참할 만큼 옹색하게, 거창한 규모가 아니면 빚이라는 것을 도무지 알지 못하는 고결한 사람들의 관심에는 미치지 못하는 것으로 비칠지도 모른다. 분명 그것은 옹색한 것이었다. 그리고 고결하지 못한 대다수의 사람에게, 옹색함에서 벗어나는 길이란 오직 돈에 대한 갈망에서, 그 온갖 저열한 희망과 유혹에서, 누군가의 죽음을 기다리는 마음에서, 넌지시 던지는 부탁에서, 나쁜 물건을 좋은 것으로 둔갑시키려는 말 장수의 욕심에서, 마땅히 남의 몫이어야 할 자리를 노리는 마음에서, 그리고 흔히 닥쳐오는 폭넓은 재난의 모습을 한 행운을 갈망하지 않을 수 없게 만드는 강박에서 자유로워지는 것뿐이다. 리드게이트가 쓰라리고 침울한 상태에 빠져 들어 로저먼드와의 소원함을 끊임없이 넓혀 가게 된 것은, 바로 이 비열한 멍에 아래로 목을 들이미는 일이라는 생각에 그가 몸부림쳤기 때문이었다. 매각 증서에 관한 첫 번째 고백이 있은 뒤로, 그는 지출을 줄일 만한 가능한 방책에 관해 아내가 자기에게 공감을 갖도록 끌어들이려 여러 차례 애를 썼고, 성탄절이 위협적으로 다가오면서 그의 제안은 점점 더 구체적으로 변해 갔다. “우리 둘은 하인 하나만으로도 꾸려 갈 수 있고, 아주 적은 돈으로 살 수 있어.” 그가 말했다. “그리고 나도 말 한 마리로 어떻게든 해 볼게.” 우리가 보아 온 대로, 리드게이트는 생활비에 관해 한층 또렷한 안목을 갖고 따져 보기 시작한 터였고, 그가 그런 외양에 들이던 자존심 따위는, 빚쟁이로 드러나는 일이나 사람들에게 돈을 빌려 달라고 청하는 일을 못 견뎌 하게 만드는 그 자존심에 비하면 보잘것없는 것이었다."]], [["“Of course you can dismiss the other two servants, if you like,”", "“물론 당신이 원하면 다른 하인 둘은 내보낼 수 있어요.”"], ["said Rosamond; “but I should have thought it would be very injurious to your position for us to live in a poor way.", "로저먼드가 말했다. “하지만 우리가 궁색하게 사는 건 당신 입지에 아주 해로울 거라고 생각했는데요."], ["You must expect your practice to be lowered.”", "진료가 줄어들 걸 각오하셔야 할 거예요.”"]], [["“My dear Rosamond, it is not a question of choice.", "“로저먼드, 여보, 이건 선택의 문제가 아니야."], ["We have begun too expensively.", "우리는 너무 사치스럽게 시작했어."], ["Peacock, you know, lived in a much smaller house than this.", "피콕도, 알다시피 이 집보다 훨씬 작은 집에서 살았잖아."], ["It is my fault: I ought to have known better, and I deserve a thrashing—if there were anybody who had a right to give it me—for bringing you into the necessity of living in a poorer way than you have been used to.", "내 잘못이야. 더 분별 있게 했어야 했고, 누군가 나를 매질할 권리가 있는 사람이 있다면 매를 맞아 마땅해—당신을 여태까지 익숙했던 것보다 더 궁색하게 살아야 하는 처지로 끌어들였으니."], ["But we married because we loved each other, I suppose.", "하지만 우리는 서로 사랑해서 결혼했잖아, 아마도."], ["And that may help us to pull along till things get better.", "그게 형편이 나아질 때까지 우리가 버텨 나가는 데 힘이 되어 줄 거야."], ["Come, dear, put down that work and come to me.”", "자, 여보, 그 일감은 내려놓고 이리 와.”"]], [["He was really in chill gloom about her at that moment, but he dreaded a future without affection, and was determined to resist the oncoming of division between them.", "그 순간 그는 정말로 아내에 대해 싸늘하고 음울한 심정이었지만, 애정 없는 미래가 두려웠고, 둘 사이에 다가오는 분열에 맞서기로 마음먹고 있었다."], ["Rosamond obeyed him, and he took her on his knee, but in her secret soul she was utterly aloof from him.", "로저먼드는 그의 말을 따랐고, 그는 아내를 무릎에 앉혔으나, 마음속 깊은 곳에서 그녀는 그에게서 완전히 멀어져 있었다."], ["The poor thing saw only that the world was not ordered to her liking, and Lydgate was part of that world.", "가엾은 그녀는 다만 세상이 자기 뜻대로 돌아가지 않는다는 것만을 보았고, 리드게이트는 그 세상의 일부였다."], ["But he held her waist with one hand and laid the other gently on both of hers; for this rather abrupt man had much tenderness in his manners towards women, seeming to have always present in his imagination the weakness of their frames and the delicate poise of their health both in body and mind.", "그러나 그는 한 손으로 아내의 허리를 감고 다른 손은 그녀의 두 손 위에 살며시 얹었다. 다소 퉁명스러운 이 남자는 여인을 대하는 태도에 상당한 다정함을 지니고 있었으니, 여인들의 연약한 몸이며 몸과 마음 양쪽으로 위태롭게 균형 잡힌 건강을 늘 머릿속에 떠올리고 있는 듯했다."], ["And he began again to speak persuasively.", "그리고 그는 다시 설득하듯 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“I find, now I look into things a little, Rosy, that it is wonderful what an amount of money slips away in our housekeeping.", "“이제 좀 들여다보니 알겠는데, 로지, 우리 살림에서 얼마나 많은 돈이 새어 나가는지 놀라울 정도야."], ["I suppose the servants are careless, and we have had a great many people coming.", "하인들이 부주의한 모양이고, 손님도 무척 많이 드나들었지."], ["But there must be many in our rank who manage with much less: they must do with commoner things, I suppose, and look after the scraps.", "하지만 우리와 같은 처지에서도 훨씬 적은 돈으로 꾸려 가는 사람이 분명 많을 거야. 아마 더 흔한 물건으로 살고, 남는 것을 잘 챙기겠지."], ["It seems, money goes but a little way in these matters, for Wrench has everything as plain as possible, and he has a very large practice.”", "이런 일에서 돈이 얼마 가지 못하는 모양이야. 렌치만 해도 모든 걸 더할 나위 없이 수수하게 하면서도 진료는 아주 많이 하잖아.”"]], [["“Oh, if you think of living as the Wrenches do!”", "“어머, 렌치 댁처럼 사는 걸 생각하신다면!”"], ["said Rosamond, with a little turn of her neck.", "로저먼드가 목을 살짝 비틀며 말했다."], ["“But I have heard you express your disgust at that way of living.”", "“하지만 당신이 그런 식의 생활에 진저리를 친다고 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있는데요.”"]], [["“Yes, they have bad taste in everything—they make economy look ugly.", "“그래, 그 집은 모든 게 취향이 형편없지—절약을 흉하게 보이게 만든단 말이야."], ["We needn’t do that.", "우리가 그럴 필요는 없어."], ["I only meant that they avoid expenses, although Wrench has a capital practice.”", "나는 다만 렌치는 진료가 아주 잘되는데도 지출을 아낀다는 걸 말하려던 거야.”"]], [["“Why should not you have a good practice, Tertius?", "“당신은 왜 진료가 잘되면 안 되죠, 터셔스?"], ["Mr. Peacock had.", "피콕 씨는 그랬잖아요."], ["You should be more careful not to offend people, and you should send out medicines as the others do.", "당신은 사람들 비위를 거스르지 않도록 더 조심해야 하고, 다른 사람들처럼 약을 지어 보내 줘야 해요."], ["I am sure you began well, and you got several good houses.", "당신은 분명 출발이 좋았고, 괜찮은 집안도 여럿 잡았잖아요."], ["It cannot answer to be eccentric; you should think what will be generally liked,”", "별나게 굴어서는 잘될 리가 없어요. 사람들이 대체로 좋아할 만한 게 무엇인지 생각해 봐야죠.”"], ["said Rosamond, in a decided little tone of admonition.", "로저먼드가 야무지고 단호하게 타이르는 어조로 말했다."]], [["Lydgate’s anger rose: he was prepared to be indulgent towards feminine weakness, but not towards feminine dictation.", "리드게이트는 화가 치밀어 올랐다. 그는 여성적 연약함에는 너그럽게 대할 마음의 준비가 되어 있었지만, 여성적 지시질에는 그렇지 않았다."], ["The shallowness of a waternixie’s soul may have a charm until she becomes didactic.", "물의 요정 같은 영혼의 얄팍함도 그녀가 훈계를 늘어놓기 전까지는 매력으로 남을 수 있는 법이다."], ["But he controlled himself, and only said, with a touch of despotic firmness—", "그러나 그는 스스로를 억누르며, 다만 다소 전제적인 단호함을 띤 어조로 말했다."]], [["“What I am to do in my practice, Rosy, it is for me to judge.", "“내 진료에서 내가 어떻게 해야 할지는, 로지, 내가 판단할 일이야."], ["That is not the question between us.", "그건 우리 사이의 문제가 아니야."], ["It is enough for you to know that our income is likely to be a very narrow one—hardly four hundred, perhaps less, for a long time to come, and we must try to re-arrange our lives in accordance with that fact.”", "당신은 우리 수입이 앞으로 오랫동안 아주 빠듯할 것 같다는 것만 알면 돼—기껏해야 사백 파운드, 어쩌면 그보다 적을 거야. 그리고 우리는 그 사실에 맞춰 살림을 다시 꾸리도록 애써야 해.”"]], [["Rosamond was silent for a moment or two, looking before her, and then said, “My uncle Bulstrode ought to allow you a salary for the time you give to the Hospital: it is not right that you should work for nothing.”", "로저먼드는 한두 순간 말없이 앞을 바라보다가 이윽고 말했다. “벌스트로드 외삼촌이 당신이 병원에 들이는 시간에 대해 봉급을 드려야 마땅해요. 당신이 거저 일한다는 건 옳지 않아요.”"]], [["“It was understood from the beginning that my services would be gratuitous.", "“처음부터 내 봉사는 무보수라고 양해된 일이야."], ["That, again, need not enter into our discussion.", "그것도 우리 논의에 끌어들일 필요가 없어."], ["I have pointed out what is the only probability,”", "나는 유일하게 있을 법한 가능성을 짚어 주었을 뿐이야.”"], ["said Lydgate, impatiently.", "리드게이트가 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["Then checking himself, he went on more quietly—", "그러다 스스로를 다잡고 한결 차분하게 말을 이었다."]], [["“I think I see one resource which would free us from a good deal of the present difficulty.", "“지금의 어려움을 상당 부분 덜어 줄 방도가 하나 보이는 것 같아."], ["I hear that young Ned Plymdale is going to be married to Miss Sophy Toller.", "듣자니 젊은 네드 플림데일이 소피 톨러 양과 결혼한다더군."], ["They are rich, and it is not often that a good house is vacant in Middlemarch.", "그 집은 부유하고, 미들마치에서 좋은 집이 비는 일은 흔치 않아."], ["I feel sure that they would be glad to take this house from us with most of our furniture, and they would be willing to pay handsomely for the lease.", "그들이 우리 집을 가구 대부분과 함께 기꺼이 넘겨받으려 할 거라고 확신해. 임차료도 두둑이 치를 의향이 있을 거야."], ["I can employ Trumbull to speak to Plymdale about it.”", "내가 트럼불을 시켜 플림데일에게 이야기를 꺼내게 할 수 있어.”"]], [["Rosamond left her husband’s knee and walked slowly to the other end of the room; when she turned round and walked towards him it was evident that the tears had come, and that she was biting her under-lip and clasping her hands to keep herself from crying.", "로저먼드는 남편의 무릎에서 일어나 방의 저쪽 끝으로 천천히 걸어갔다. 그러다 몸을 돌려 그를 향해 걸어올 때, 눈물이 차오른 것이 또렷이 보였고, 그녀가 아랫입술을 깨물며 울음을 참으려고 두 손을 맞잡고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Lydgate was wretched—shaken with anger and yet feeling that it would be unmanly to vent the anger just now.", "리드게이트는 비참했다—분노로 떨리면서도, 지금 그 분노를 터뜨리는 것은 사내답지 못한 일이라고 느끼고 있었다."]], [["“I am very sorry, Rosamond; I know this is painful.”", "“정말 미안해, 로저먼드. 이게 괴로운 일이라는 거 알아.”"]], [["“I thought, at least, when I had borne to send the plate back and have that man taking an inventory of the furniture—I should have thought that would suffice.”", "“적어도 은식기를 돌려보내고, 그 사람이 가구 목록을 작성하게 하는 걸 참고 견뎠으면—그것으로 충분할 줄 알았어요.”"]], [["“I explained it to you at the time, dear.", "“그건 그때 당신한테 설명했잖아, 여보."], ["That was only a security and behind that security there is a debt.", "그건 그저 담보였을 뿐이고, 그 담보 뒤에는 빚이 있어."], ["And that debt must be paid within the next few months, else we shall have our furniture sold.", "그리고 그 빚은 앞으로 몇 달 안에 갚아야 해. 그러지 않으면 우리 가구가 팔려 나갈 거야."], ["If young Plymdale will take our house and most of our furniture, we shall be able to pay that debt, and some others too, and we shall be quit of a place too expensive for us.", "젊은 플림데일이 우리 집과 가구 대부분을 넘겨받는다면, 우리는 그 빚을, 그리고 다른 빚도 얼마간 갚을 수 있고, 우리한테 너무 비싼 이 집에서도 벗어날 수 있어. 우리는 더 작은 집을 얻을 수 있어."], ["We might take a smaller house: Trumbull, I know, has a very decent one to let at thirty pounds a-year, and this is ninety.”", "내가 알기로 트럼불한테 일 년에 삼십 파운드면 빌릴 수 있는 꽤 괜찮은 집이 있어. 그런데 이 집은 구십 파운드야.”"], ["Lydgate uttered this speech in the curt hammering way with which we usually try to nail down a vague mind to imperative facts.", "리드게이트는, 막연한 마음을 거역할 수 없는 사실에 못 박아 두려 할 때 우리가 흔히 쓰는 그 짧고 두드리는 듯한 어조로 이 말을 내뱉었다."], ["Tears rolled silently down Rosamond’s cheeks; she just pressed her handkerchief against them, and stood looking at the large vase on the mantel-piece.", "눈물이 로저먼드의 뺨을 따라 소리 없이 흘러내렸다. 그녀는 손수건을 눈물에 가만히 갖다 대고는, 벽난로 선반 위의 커다란 꽃병을 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["It was a moment of more intense bitterness than she had ever felt before.", "그것은 그녀가 이제껏 느껴 본 적 없는, 더욱 사무치게 쓰라린 순간이었다."], ["At last she said, without hurry and with careful emphasis—", "마침내 그녀는 서두르지 않고 조심스레 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“I never could have believed that you would like to act in that way.”", "“당신이 그런 식으로 행동하고 싶어 할 줄은 도무지 믿을 수가 없었어요.”"]], [["“Like it?”", "“하고 싶다고?”"], ["burst out Lydgate, rising from his chair, thrusting his hands in his pockets and stalking away from the hearth; “it’s not a question of liking.", "리드게이트가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고 벽난로에서 성큼성큼 걸어 나가며 버럭 소리쳤다. “이건 하고 싶고 말고의 문제가 아니야."], ["Of course, I don’t like it; it’s the only thing I can do.”", "물론 나도 하고 싶지 않아. 그저 내가 할 수 있는 유일한 일일 뿐이야.”"], ["He wheeled round there, and turned towards her.", "그는 거기서 휙 돌아서서 그녀를 향했다."]], [["“I should have thought there were many other means than that,”", "“그것 말고도 다른 방도가 많이 있을 줄 알았어요.”"], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“Let us have a sale and leave Middlemarch altogether.”", "“집안 물건을 다 팔아 버리고 아예 미들마치를 떠나요.”"]], [["“To do what?", "“떠나서 뭘 하라고?"], ["What is the use of my leaving my work in Middlemarch to go where I have none?", "미들마치에서 하던 일을 버리고 일거리도 없는 곳으로 가는 게 무슨 소용이야?"], ["We should be just as penniless elsewhere as we are here,”", "다른 데 가도 여기 못지않게 한 푼도 없을 거야.”"], ["said Lydgate still more angrily.", "리드게이트가 한층 더 노여워하며 말했다."]], [["“If we are to be in that position it will be entirely your own doing, Tertius,”", "“우리가 그런 처지에 놓인다면 그건 전적으로 당신이 자초한 일이에요, 터셔스.”"], ["said Rosamond, turning round to speak with the fullest conviction.", "로저먼드가 돌아서서, 더없이 확신에 찬 어조로 말했다."], ["“You will not behave as you ought to do to your own family.", "“당신은 자기 가족에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 하지 않으려고 해요."], ["You offended Captain Lydgate.", "당신은 리드게이트 대위의 비위를 거슬렀어요."], ["Sir Godwin was very kind to me when we were at Quallingham, and I am sure if you showed proper regard to him and told him your affairs, he would do anything for you.", "우리가 콸링엄에 있을 때 고드윈 경께서 저한테 무척 친절하셨는데, 당신이 그분께 합당한 예를 갖추고 형편을 말씀드린다면, 분명 당신을 위해 무엇이든 해 주실 거예요."], ["But rather than that, you like giving up our house and furniture to Mr. Ned Plymdale.”", "그런데 당신은 그러기보다는 우리 집과 가구를 네드 플림데일 씨한테 넘겨주는 쪽이 좋다는 거잖아요.”"]], [["There was something like fierceness in Lydgate’s eyes, as he answered with new violence, “Well, then, if you will have it so, I do like it.", "리드게이트의 눈에는 사나운 기색 같은 것이 어렸고, 그는 새삼스러운 격렬함으로 대꾸했다. “좋아, 그렇다면, 정 그렇게 받아들이고 싶다면, 나도 그게 좋아."], ["I admit that I like it better than making a fool of myself by going to beg where it’s of no use.", "인정하지, 아무 소용도 없는 데 구걸하러 가서 바보 꼴이 되는 것보다는 그게 더 좋아."], ["Understand then, that it is what I like to do.”", "그러니 알아 둬, 그게 바로 내가 하고 싶은 일이야.”"]], [["There was a tone in the last sentence which was equivalent to the clutch of his strong hand on Rosamond’s delicate arm.", "마지막 문장에는 그의 억센 손이 로저먼드의 가녀린 팔을 움켜쥔 것과 맞먹는 어조가 실려 있었다."], ["But for all that, his will was not a whit stronger than hers.", "그러나 그럼에도 불구하고 그의 의지는 그녀의 의지보다 조금도 더 강하지 않았다."], ["She immediately walked out of the room in silence, but with an intense determination to hinder what Lydgate liked to do.", "그녀는 곧장 말없이 방을 나갔지만, 리드게이트가 하고 싶어 하는 일을 막겠다는 강렬한 결심을 품고 있었다."]], [["He went out of the house, but as his blood cooled he felt that the chief result of the discussion was a deposit of dread within him at the idea of opening with his wife in future subjects which might again urge him to violent speech.", "그는 집을 나섰으나, 피가 식어 가면서 그 논쟁의 가장 큰 결과란 앞으로 자기를 다시 격한 말로 몰아갈지 모를 주제를 아내에게 꺼내는 것에 대한, 마음속에 가라앉은 두려움임을 깨달았다."], ["It was as if a fracture in delicate crystal had begun, and he was afraid of any movement that might make it fatal.", "그것은 마치 섬세한 수정에 금이 가기 시작한 것 같았고, 그는 그 금을 돌이킬 수 없게 만들지도 모를 어떤 움직임도 두려웠다."], ["His marriage would be a mere piece of bitter irony if they could not go on loving each other.", "두 사람이 서로 사랑을 이어 갈 수 없다면, 그의 결혼은 한낱 쓰디쓴 아이러니의 조각에 지나지 않을 터였다."], ["He had long ago made up his mind to what he thought was her negative character—her want of sensibility, which showed itself in disregard both of his specific wishes and of his general aims.", "그는 오래전에 그녀의 소극적인 성격이라고 여긴 것에 대해—자기의 구체적인 바람도 전반적인 목표도 아랑곳하지 않는 데서 드러나는 그녀의 감수성 결여에 대해—이미 마음을 정해 둔 터였다."], ["The first great disappointment had been borne: the tender devotedness and docile adoration of the ideal wife must be renounced, and life must be taken up on a lower stage of expectation, as it is by men who have lost their limbs.", "첫 번째 큰 실망은 이미 견뎌 낸 셈이었다. 이상적인 아내가 보여 줄 다정한 헌신과 고분고분한 흠모는 단념해야 했고, 삶은, 팔다리를 잃은 사람들이 그러하듯, 더 낮은 단계의 기대 위에서 다시 떠맡아야 했다."], ["But the real wife had not only her claims, she had still a hold on his heart, and it was his intense desire that the hold should remain strong.", "그러나 현실의 아내에게는 마땅한 권리만 있는 것이 아니었다. 그녀는 여전히 그의 마음을 사로잡고 있었고, 그 사로잡음이 계속 강하게 남아 있기를 그는 간절히 바랐다."], ["In marriage, the certainty, “She will never love me much,” is easier to bear than the fear, “I shall love her no more.”", "결혼에서, “그녀는 결코 나를 깊이 사랑하지 않으리라”는 확신은 “나는 더 이상 그녀를 사랑하지 않으리라”는 두려움보다 견디기가 더 쉽다."], ["Hence, after that outburst, his inward effort was entirely to excuse her, and to blame the hard circumstances which were partly his fault.", "그리하여 그렇게 폭발한 뒤, 그의 내면의 노력은 오로지 그녀를 변호하고, 일부는 자기 탓인 그 고된 상황을 탓하는 데로 향했다."], ["He tried that evening, by petting her, to heal the wound he had made in the morning, and it was not in Rosamond’s nature to be repellent or sulky; indeed, she welcomed the signs that her husband loved her and was under control.", "그날 저녁 그는 아내를 어르며 아침에 자기가 낸 상처를 아물게 하려 애썼고, 쌀쌀맞게 굴거나 토라지는 것은 로저먼드의 천성에 없는 일이었다. 사실 그녀는 남편이 자기를 사랑하며 자제하고 있다는 표시를 반겼다."], ["But this was something quite distinct from loving him.", "그러나 이것은 그를 사랑하는 것과는 전혀 별개의 일이었다."], ["Lydgate would not have chosen soon to recur to the plan of parting with the house; he was resolved to carry it out, and say as little more about it as possible.", "리드게이트는 집을 내놓는 계획을 곧 다시 입에 올릴 마음은 없었다. 그는 그 일을 끝까지 밀고 나가되, 그것에 관해서는 가능한 한 더는 말을 아끼기로 작정했다."], ["But Rosamond herself touched on it at breakfast by saying, mildly—", "그런데 정작 로저먼드가 아침 식사 자리에서 부드럽게 그 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Have you spoken to Trumbull yet?”", "“트럼불한테 벌써 이야기했어요?”"]], [["“No,”", "“아니.”"], ["said Lydgate, “but I shall call on him as I go by this morning.", "리드게이트가 말했다. “하지만 오늘 아침에 지나는 길에 들를 거야."], ["No time must be lost.”", "한시도 지체해선 안 돼.”"], ["He took Rosamond’s question as a sign that she withdrew her inward opposition, and kissed her head caressingly when he got up to go away.", "그는 로저먼드의 물음을 그녀가 마음속 반대를 거두어들이는 표시로 받아들이고는, 떠나려고 일어서면서 그녀의 머리에 다정하게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["As soon as it was late enough to make a call, Rosamond went to Mrs. Plymdale, Mr.", "방문하기에 충분히 늦은 시각이 되자마자, 로저먼드는 네드 씨의 어머니인 플림데일 부인을 찾아가, 다가오는 결혼에 관한 화제로 들어가 곱게 축하의 말을 건넸다."], ["Ned’s mother, and entered with pretty congratulations into the subject of the coming marriage. Mrs.", "어머니다운 플림데일 부인의 생각은, 로저먼드가 이제 어쩌면 자기 자신의 어리석음을 되돌아볼지도 모른다는 것이었다."], ["Plymdale’s maternal view was, that Rosamond might possibly now have retrospective glimpses of her own folly; and feeling the advantages to be at present all on the side of her son, was too kind a woman not to behave graciously.", "그리고 지금으로서는 모든 유리함이 자기 아들 쪽에 있다고 느끼면서, 그녀는 우아하게 처신하지 않을 만큼 마음씨가 박한 여인은 아니었다."]], [["“Yes, Ned is most happy, I must say.", "“그럼요, 네드는 더없이 행복하답니다, 정말이지요."], ["And Sophy Toller is all I could desire in a daughter-in-law.", "그리고 소피 톨러는 며느릿감으로 제가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추었지요."], ["Of course her father is able to do something handsome for her—that is only what would be expected with a brewery like his.", "물론 그 애 아버지가 그 애를 위해 후하게 뭔가 해 줄 형편이고요—저런 양조장을 가진 집안이라면 그쯤은 으레 기대할 만한 일이죠."], ["And the connection is everything we should desire.", "그리고 그 혼맥은 우리가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것이고요."], ["But that is not what I look at.", "하지만 내가 보는 건 그게 아니에요."], ["She is such a very nice girl—no airs, no pretensions, though on a level with the first.", "그 애는 정말 참한 아가씨예요—거드름도, 허세도 없으면서도 으뜸가는 사람들과 어깨를 나란히 한다니까요."], ["I don’t mean with the titled aristocracy.", "작위를 가진 귀족 말고요."], ["I see very little good in people aiming out of their own sphere.", "나는 제 분수를 넘어서 위로 겨냥하는 사람들에게서 좋은 점이라곤 거의 보지 못했어요."], ["I mean that Sophy is equal to the best in the town, and she is contented with that.”", "내 말은, 소피가 이 고장의 가장 훌륭한 사람들과 견주어 손색이 없으면서도, 그것으로 만족할 줄 안다는 거죠.”"]], [["“I have always thought her very agreeable,”", "“저는 늘 그분이 무척 상냥하다고 생각했어요.”"], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."]], [["“I look upon it as a reward for Ned, who never held his head too high, that he should have got into the very best connection,”", "“나는 이걸 네드에게 주어진 보답이라고 봐요. 그 애는 도무지 콧대를 너무 높이 든 적이 없었으니, 가장 훌륭한 혼맥을 맺게 된 거죠.”"], ["continued Mrs.", "플림데일 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Plymdale, her native sharpness softened by a fervid sense that she was taking a correct view.", "자기가 올바른 견해를 취하고 있다는 뜨거운 자부심에 그녀의 타고난 날카로움이 누그러져 있었다."], ["“And such particular people as the Tollers are, they might have objected because some of our friends are not theirs.", "“게다가 톨러네처럼 까다로운 사람들이라면, 우리 친구들 중 몇몇이 자기네 친구가 아니라는 이유로 트집을 잡을 수도 있었어요."], ["It is well known that your aunt Bulstrode and I have been intimate from our youth, and Mr. Plymdale has been always on Mr.", "댁의 벌스트로드 외숙모와 내가 젊을 적부터 가깝게 지내 온 건 익히 알려진 일이고, 플림데일 씨는 늘 벌스트로드 씨 편을 들어 오셨지요."], ["Bulstrode’s side. And I myself prefer serious opinions.", "그리고 나 자신도 경건한 신앙을 더 좋아하고요."], ["But the Tollers have welcomed Ned all the same.”", "그런데도 톨러네는 변함없이 네드를 반갑게 맞아 주었답니다.”"]], [["“I am sure he is a very deserving, well-principled young man,”", "“그분은 분명 무척 마땅한 자격을 갖춘, 행실 반듯한 청년이세요.”"], ["said Rosamond, with a neat air of patronage in return for Mrs. Plymdale’s wholesome corrections.", "로저먼드가, 플림데일 부인의 유익한 훈계에 대한 답례로 점잖게 생색내는 태도를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, he has not the style of a captain in the army, or that sort of carriage as if everybody was beneath him, or that showy kind of talking, and singing, and intellectual talent.", "“오, 그 애는 육군 대위 같은 풍채라든가, 마치 세상 모두가 자기 아래인 양 거드름 피우는 태도라든가, 그런 번지르르한 말솜씨며 노래며 지적인 재주 같은 건 없어요."], ["But I am thankful he has not.", "하지만 나는 그 애가 그런 게 없는 게 고맙답니다."], ["It is a poor preparation both for here and Hereafter.”", "그런 건 이승을 위해서도 저승을 위해서도 변변찮은 준비니까요.”"]], [["“Oh dear, yes; appearances have very little to do with happiness,”", "“어머, 정말 그래요. 겉모습은 행복과 거의 관계가 없죠.”"], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“I think there is every prospect of their being a happy couple.", "“두 분이 행복한 부부가 될 가망이 충분하다고 봐요."], ["What house will they take?”", "두 분은 어떤 집을 얻으실까요?”"]], [["“Oh, as for that, they must put up with what they can get.", "“오, 그 일이라면 구할 수 있는 걸로 만족해야겠죠."], ["They have been looking at the house in St. Peter’s Place, next to Mr.", "그 애들은 해크벗 씨 댁 옆, 성 베드로 광장에 있는 집을 보고 있어요."], ["Hackbutt’s; it belongs to him, and he is putting it nicely in repair.", "그건 해크벗 씨 소유인데, 그가 말끔하게 손보고 있답니다."], ["I suppose they are not likely to hear of a better.", "더 나은 집 소식을 듣기는 어려울 것 같아요."], ["Indeed, I think Ned will decide the matter to-day.”", "사실 네드가 오늘 그 문제를 결정할 것 같네요.”"]], [["“I should think it is a nice house; I like St.", "“좋은 집일 것 같아요."], ["Peter’s Place.”", "저는 성 베드로 광장이 마음에 들어요.”"]], [["“Well, it is near the Church, and a genteel situation.", "“글쎄요, 교회와 가깝고 점잖은 자리이긴 하죠."], ["But the windows are narrow, and it is all ups and downs.", "하지만 창문이 좁고, 온통 오르락내리락이에요."], ["You don’t happen to know of any other that would be at liberty?”", "혹시 비어 있을 만한 다른 집을 알고 있진 않나요?”"], ["said Mrs. Plymdale, fixing her round black eyes on Rosamond with the animation of a sudden thought in them.", "플림데일 부인이 동그란 검은 눈을 로저먼드에게 고정한 채, 그 눈에 불현듯 떠오른 생각의 생기를 담아 물었다."]], [["“Oh no; I hear so little of those things.”", "“아뇨, 저는 그런 일은 거의 듣지 못해서요.”"]], [["Rosamond had not foreseen that question and answer in setting out to pay her visit; she had simply meant to gather any information which would help her to avert the parting with her own house under circumstances thoroughly disagreeable to her.", "로저먼드는 방문에 나설 때만 해도 이런 문답을 미리 내다보지는 못했다. 그저 자기에게 더없이 마뜩잖은 상황에서 자기 집을 넘기는 일을 막아 줄 정보라면 무엇이든 얻으려 했을 뿐이었다."], ["As to the untruth in her reply, she no more reflected on it than she did on the untruth there was in her saying that appearances had very little to do with happiness.", "자기 대답 속의 거짓말에 대해서는, 겉모습이 행복과 거의 관계가 없다고 말한 데 들어 있는 거짓말에 대해서와 마찬가지로 조금도 곱씹지 않았다. 자기 목적은 더할 나위 없이 정당하다고 그녀는 확신했다."], ["Her object, she was convinced, was thoroughly justifiable: it was Lydgate whose intention was inexcusable; and there was a plan in her mind which, when she had carried it out fully, would prove how very false a step it would have been for him to have descended from his position.", "용서받을 수 없는 의도를 품은 쪽은 리드게이트였다. 그리고 그녀의 마음속에는 한 가지 계획이 있었으니, 그것을 온전히 실행에 옮기고 나면, 그가 자기 입지에서 끌어내리는 것이 얼마나 그릇된 한 걸음이었을지를 증명해 줄 터였다."]], [["She returned home by Mr. Borthrop Trumbull’s office, meaning to call there.", "그녀는 보스럽 트럼불 씨의 사무소를 거쳐 집으로 돌아왔으니, 그곳에 들를 작정이었다."], ["It was the first time in her life that Rosamond had thought of doing anything in the form of business, but she felt equal to the occasion.", "로저먼드가 사무적인 형식을 띤 무언가를 해 보겠다고 생각한 것은 평생 처음이었지만, 그녀는 그 일을 능히 감당할 수 있다고 느꼈다."], ["That she should be obliged to do what she intensely disliked, was an idea which turned her quiet tenacity into active invention.", "자기가 몹시 싫어하는 일을 부득이 하지 않으면 안 되게 되었다는 생각은 그녀의 조용한 고집을 능동적인 궁리로 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["Here was a case in which it could not be enough simply to disobey and be serenely, placidly obstinate: she must act according to her judgment, and she said to herself that her judgment was right—“indeed, if it had not been, she would not have wished to act on it.”", "이번 경우는 단순히 거역하고 태연자약하게 천연덕스레 고집을 부리는 것만으로는 충분할 수 없는 일이었다. 그녀는 자기 판단에 따라 행동해야 했고, 자기 판단이 옳다고 스스로에게 일렀다—“하긴, 옳지 않았다면 그것에 따라 행동하고 싶지도 않았을 테니.”"]], [["Mr. Trumbull was in the back-room of his office, and received Rosamond with his finest manners, not only because he had much sensibility to her charms, but because the good-natured fibre in him was stirred by his certainty that Lydgate was in difficulties, and that this uncommonly pretty woman—this young lady with the highest personal attractions—was likely to feel the pinch of trouble—to find herself involved in circumstances beyond her control.", "트럼불 씨는 사무소 안쪽 방에 있었고, 더없이 세련된 태도로 로저먼드를 맞이했다."], ["He begged her to do him the honor to take a seat, and stood before her trimming and comporting himself with an eager solicitude, which was chiefly benevolent.", "그녀의 매력에 무척 민감했기 때문만이 아니라, 리드게이트가 곤경에 처해 있고, 이 보기 드물게 예쁜 여인—더없이 빼어난 매력을 지닌 이 젊은 부인—이 곤궁의 쓴맛을 보게 되고, 자기 힘으로는 어쩔 수 없는 상황에 휘말리게 될 것 같다는 확신에 그의 내면에 깃든 마음씨 좋은 결이 움직였기 때문이기도 했다. 그는 부디 자리에 앉는 영광을 베풀어 달라고 청한 뒤, 그녀 앞에 서서 매무새를 다듬고 몸가짐을 가다듬으며, 주로 호의에서 우러나는 열렬한 정성을 보였다."], ["Rosamond’s first question was, whether her husband had called on Mr. Trumbull that morning, to speak about disposing of their house.", "로저먼드의 첫 질문은, 자기 남편이 그날 아침 집을 처분하는 일에 관해 이야기하러 트럼불 씨를 찾아왔느냐는 것이었다."]], [["“Yes, ma’am, yes, he did; he did so,”", "“예, 부인, 예, 그러셨습니다. 그러셨고말고요.”"], ["said the good auctioneer, trying to throw something soothing into his iteration.", "사람 좋은 경매인이 자기 되풀이하는 말에 무언가 달래는 기색을 담으려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“I was about to fulfil his order, if possible, this afternoon.", "“저는 가능하면 오늘 오후에 그분의 지시를 이행하려던 참이었습니다."], ["He wished me not to procrastinate.”", "그분께서 미루지 말라고 하셨으니까요.”"]], [["“I called to tell you not to go any further, Mr.", "“더 진행하지 마시라고 말씀드리러 들렀어요, 트럼불 씨."], ["Trumbull; and I beg of you not to mention what has been said on the subject.", "그리고 이 일에 관해 오간 이야기는 부디 언급하지 말아 주세요."], ["Will you oblige me?”", "제 부탁을 들어주시겠어요?”"]], [["“Certainly I will, Mrs. Lydgate, certainly.", "“물론이고말고요, 리드게이트 부인, 물론입니다."], ["Confidence is sacred with me on business or any other topic.", "사업에 관해서든 다른 어떤 화제에 관해서든, 신뢰는 저에게 신성한 것입니다."], ["I am then to consider the commission withdrawn?” said Mr.", "그러면 그 위임은 철회된 것으로 여기면 되겠습니까?”"], ["Trumbull, adjusting the long ends of his blue cravat with both hands, and looking at Rosamond deferentially.", "트럼불 씨가 두 손으로 푸른 크라바트의 긴 끝자락을 매만지며, 공손하게 로저먼드를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, if you please. I find that Mr.", "“네, 그래 주시면 좋겠어요."], ["Ned Plymdale has taken a house—the one in St. Peter’s Place next to Mr.", "알고 보니 네드 플림데일 씨가 집을 얻었더군요—해크벗 씨 댁 옆, 성 베드로 광장에 있는 집이요."], ["Hackbutt’s. Mr.", "리드게이트 씨도 자기 지시가 쓸데없이 이행되면 언짢아할 거예요."], ["Lydgate would be annoyed that his orders should be fulfilled uselessly. And besides that, there are other circumstances which render the proposal unnecessary.”", "게다가 그 제안을 불필요하게 만드는 다른 사정도 있고요.”"]], [["“Very good, Mrs. Lydgate, very good.", "“아주 좋습니다, 리드게이트 부인, 아주 좋아요."], ["I am at your commands, whenever you require any service of me,”", "무슨 일이든 저의 도움이 필요하실 때면 언제든 분부만 내려 주십시오.”"], ["said Mr. Trumbull, who felt pleasure in conjecturing that some new resources had been opened.", "트럼불 씨가 무언가 새로운 재원이 열린 모양이라고 짐작하며 즐거움을 느끼고 말했다."], ["“Rely on me, I beg.", "“저를 믿어 주십시오, 부디."], ["The affair shall go no further.”", "이 일은 더 이상 진행되지 않을 것입니다.”"]], [["That evening Lydgate was a little comforted by observing that Rosamond was more lively than she had usually been of late, and even seemed interested in doing what would please him without being asked.", "그날 저녁 리드게이트는 로저먼드가 요즘 들어 평소보다 더 활기차고, 시키지 않아도 자기를 기쁘게 할 일을 하는 데 관심까지 보이는 것을 알아채고 조금 위안을 얻었다. 그는 생각했다."], ["He thought, “If she will be happy and I can rub through, what does it all signify?", "“그녀가 행복하고 내가 그럭저럭 헤쳐 나갈 수만 있다면, 그게 다 무슨 대수겠는가?"], ["It is only a narrow swamp that we have to pass in a long journey.", "긴 여정에서 우리가 건너야 할 좁은 늪에 지나지 않아."], ["If I can get my mind clear again, I shall do.”", "머릿속을 다시 맑게 가다듬을 수만 있다면 잘 풀릴 거야.”"]], [["He was so much cheered that he began to search for an account of experiments which he had long ago meant to look up, and had neglected out of that creeping self-despair which comes in the train of petty anxieties.", "그는 어찌나 기운이 났던지, 오래전부터 찾아보려다가 사소한 근심들에 뒤따르는 슬금슬금 기어드는 자기 절망 때문에 미뤄 두었던 어떤 실험 기록을 다시 뒤지기 시작했다."], ["He felt again some of the old delightful absorption in a far-reaching inquiry, while Rosamond played the quiet music which was as helpful to his meditation as the plash of an oar on the evening lake.", "그는 멀리까지 뻗어 나가는 탐구에 대한 옛날의 그 황홀한 몰입을 얼마간 다시 느꼈고, 그동안 로저먼드는 저녁 호숫가의 노 젓는 물소리만큼이나 그의 사색에 도움이 되는 잔잔한 음악을 연주했다."], ["It was rather late; he had pushed away all the books, and was looking at the fire with his hands clasped behind his head in forgetfulness of everything except the construction of a new controlling experiment, when Rosamond, who had left the piano and was leaning back in her chair watching him, said—", "꽤 늦은 시각이었다. 그는 책을 죄다 옆으로 밀쳐 놓고, 두 손을 머리 뒤로 깍지 낀 채 불을 바라보며, 새로운 대조 실험을 구상하는 일 말고는 모든 것을 잊고 있었는데, 그때 피아노를 떠나 의자에 기대어 그를 지켜보고 있던 로저먼드가 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Ned Plymdale has taken a house already.”", "“네드 플림데일 씨가 벌써 집을 얻었어요.”"]], [["Lydgate, startled and jarred, looked up in silence for a moment, like a man who has been disturbed in his sleep.", "리드게이트는 흠칫 놀라고 신경이 거슬려, 마치 잠을 방해받은 사람처럼 잠시 말없이 고개를 들었다."], ["Then flushing with an unpleasant consciousness, he asked—", "그러다 언짢은 의식에 얼굴을 붉히며 물었다."]], [["“How do you know?”", "“그걸 어떻게 알아?”"]], [["“I called at Mrs. Plymdale’s this morning, and she told me that he had taken the house in St. Peter’s Place, next to Mr. Hackbutt’s.”", "“오늘 아침 플림데일 부인을 찾아갔는데, 그분이 그가 해크벗 씨 댁 옆, 성 베드로 광장에 있는 집을 얻었다고 하시더군요.”"]], [["Lydgate was silent.", "리드게이트는 잠자코 있었다."], ["He drew his hands from behind his head and pressed them against the hair which was hanging, as it was apt to do, in a mass on his forehead, while he rested his elbows on his knees.", "그는 머리 뒤에서 두 손을 끌어내려, 늘 그러기 십상이듯 이마 위로 한 무더기 늘어진 머리카락을 짓누르고, 두 팔꿈치를 무릎에 괴었다."], ["He was feeling bitter disappointment, as if he had opened a door out of a suffocating place and had found it walled up; but he also felt sure that Rosamond was pleased with the cause of his disappointment.", "그는 마치 숨 막히는 곳에서 빠져나갈 문을 열었다가 그 문이 벽으로 막혀 있는 것을 발견한 사람처럼 쓰라린 실망을 느끼고 있었다. 그러나 동시에 로저먼드가 자기 실망의 원인을 기뻐하고 있다는 것도 분명히 느꼈다."], ["He preferred not looking at her and not speaking, until he had got over the first spasm of vexation.", "그는 처음의 분한 경련이 가라앉을 때까지는 그녀를 보지도 말하지도 않는 편이 낫다고 여겼다."], ["After all, he said in his bitterness, what can a woman care about so much as house and furniture?", "따지고 보면, 그가 쓰라림 속에서 중얼거리길, 여자가 집과 가구만큼 마음 쓰는 게 뭐가 있겠는가?"], ["a husband without them is an absurdity.", "그것들 없는 남편이란 한낱 어이없는 존재일 뿐인 것을."], ["When he looked up and pushed his hair aside, his dark eyes had a miserable blank non-expectance of sympathy in them, but he only said, coolly—", "그가 고개를 들고 머리카락을 옆으로 밀어 넘겼을 때, 그의 검은 눈에는 공감을 도무지 기대하지 않는 비참한 공백이 어려 있었으나, 그는 다만 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps some one else may turn up.", "“어쩌면 다른 누군가가 나타날지도 모르지."], ["I told Trumbull to be on the look-out if he failed with Plymdale.”", "플림데일과 잘 안 되면 계속 알아보라고 트럼불한테 일러 두었으니까.”"]], [["Rosamond made no remark.", "로저먼드는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She trusted to the chance that nothing more would pass between her husband and the auctioneer until some issue should have justified her interference; at any rate, she had hindered the event which she immediately dreaded.", "그녀는 어떤 결말이 자기의 개입을 정당화해 줄 때까지는 남편과 경매인 사이에 더 이상 아무 일도 오가지 않으리라는 가능성에 기대를 걸었다. 어쨌거나 그녀는 당장 두려워하던 사태를 막아 낸 셈이었다."], ["After a pause, she said—", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“How much money is it that those disagreeable people want?”", "“그 못마땅한 사람들이 원하는 돈이 얼마나 되는데요?”"]], [["“What disagreeable people?”", "“못마땅한 사람들이라니?”"]], [["“Those who took the list—and the others.", "“목록을 작성한 그 사람들—그리고 다른 사람들이요."], ["I mean, how much money would satisfy them so that you need not be troubled any more?”", "그러니까, 당신이 더 이상 시달리지 않으려면 그들을 얼마면 만족시킬 수 있느냐는 거예요.”"]], [["Lydgate surveyed her for a moment, as if he were looking for symptoms, and then said, “Oh, if I could have got six hundred from Plymdale for furniture and as premium, I might have managed.", "리드게이트는 마치 증세를 살피려는 듯 잠시 그녀를 찬찬히 보다가 말했다. “오, 가구 값과 권리금으로 플림데일한테서 육백 파운드를 받을 수 있었다면 어떻게든 꾸려 갔을 거야."], ["I could have paid off Dover, and given enough on account to the others to make them wait patiently, if we contracted our expenses.”", "도버한테 빚을 청산하고, 나머지 사람들한테도 인내심을 갖고 기다릴 만큼 얼마간 갚아 두었겠지. 우리가 지출을 줄였다면 말이야.”"]], [["“But I mean how much should you want if we stayed in this house?”", "“하지만 우리가 이 집에 그대로 산다면 얼마나 필요하느냐는 거예요.”"]], [["“More than I am likely to get anywhere,”", "“내가 어디서든 마련할 수 있을 것보다 더 많이.”"], ["said Lydgate, with rather a grating sarcasm in his tone.", "리드게이트가 다소 거슬리는 빈정거림을 어조에 담아 말했다."], ["It angered him to perceive that Rosamond’s mind was wandering over impracticable wishes instead of facing possible efforts.", "로저먼드의 마음이 가능한 노력을 마주하는 대신 실현 불가능한 바람 위를 떠돌고 있다는 것을 알아챈 것이 그를 화나게 했다."]], [["“Why should you not mention the sum?”", "“금액을 말하면 안 되나요?”"], ["said Rosamond, with a mild indication that she did not like his manners.", "로저먼드가, 그의 태도가 마음에 들지 않는다는 것을 은근히 내비치며 말했다."]], [["“Well,”", "“글쎄,”"], ["said Lydgate in a guessing tone, “it would take at least a thousand to set me at ease.", "리드게이트가 어림짐작하는 어조로 말했다. “마음을 놓으려면 적어도 천 파운드는 있어야 해."], ["But,”", "하지만,”"], ["he added, incisively, “I have to consider what I shall do without it, not with it.”", "그가 날카롭게 덧붙였다. “나는 그게 있을 때가 아니라 없을 때 어떻게 할지를 궁리해야 하는 처지야.”"]], [["Rosamond said no more.", "로저먼드는 더 이상 말하지 않았다."]], [["But the next day she carried out her plan of writing to Sir Godwin Lydgate.", "그러나 이튿날 그녀는 고드윈 리드게이트 경에게 편지를 쓰겠다는 계획을 실행에 옮겼다."], ["Since the Captain’s visit, she had received a letter from him, and also one from Mrs. Mengan, his married sister, condoling with her on the loss of her baby, and expressing vaguely the hope that they should see her again at Quallingham.", "대위가 다녀간 뒤로, 그녀는 그에게서 편지를 받았고, 또 그의 출가한 누이인 멩건 부인에게서도 한 통을 받았는데, 아기를 잃은 일을 위로하면서, 콸링엄에서 다시 그녀를 보게 되기를 바란다는 막연한 뜻을 담은 편지였다."], ["Lydgate had told her that this politeness meant nothing; but she was secretly convinced that any backwardness in Lydgate’s family towards him was due to his cold and contemptuous behavior, and she had answered the letters in her most charming manner, feeling some confidence that a specific invitation would follow.", "리드게이트는 그녀에게 그런 예의는 별 뜻이 없다고 말했지만, 그녀는 리드게이트의 가족이 그에게 보이는 미온적인 태도가 모두 그의 차갑고 멸시하는 듯한 행실 탓이라고 속으로 굳게 믿고 있었고, 그래서 그 편지들에 더없이 매력적인 태도로 답장을 보내면서, 구체적인 초대가 뒤따르리라는 어느 정도의 확신을 품고 있었다."], ["But there had been total silence.", "그러나 완전한 침묵이 이어졌다."], ["The Captain evidently was not a great penman, and Rosamond reflected that the sisters might have been abroad.", "대위는 분명 펜을 잘 놀리는 사람이 아니었고, 로저먼드는 누이들이 외국에 나가 있었을지도 모른다고 생각했다."], ["However, the season was come for thinking of friends at home, and at any rate Sir Godwin, who had chucked her under the chin, and pronounced her to be like the celebrated beauty, Mrs. Croly, who had made a conquest of him in 1790, would be touched by any appeal from her, and would find it pleasant for her sake to behave as he ought to do towards his nephew.", "어쨌거나 고향의 벗들을 생각하는 계절이 돌아왔고, 적어도 그녀의 턱을 어루만지며 그녀가 1790년에 자기를 사로잡았던 그 이름난 미인 크롤리 부인을 닮았다고 단언했던 고드윈 경이라면, 그녀의 어떤 호소에도 마음이 움직일 테고, 그녀를 위해서라도 조카에게 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 다하는 것을 흐뭇하게 여길 터였다."], ["Rosamond was naively convinced of what an old gentleman ought to do to prevent her from suffering annoyance.", "로저먼드는 자기가 곤란을 겪지 않도록 노신사가 마땅히 해야 할 일이 무엇인지를 천진하게 확신하고 있었다."], ["And she wrote what she considered the most judicious letter possible—one which would strike Sir Godwin as a proof of her excellent sense—pointing out how desirable it was that Tertius should quit such a place as Middlemarch for one more fitted to his talents, how the unpleasant character of the inhabitants had hindered his professional success, and how in consequence he was in money difficulties, from which it would require a thousand pounds thoroughly to extricate him.", "그래서 그녀는 자기 딴에 가장 분별 있다고 여긴 편지를—고드윈 경에게 자기의 뛰어난 분별력을 증명해 줄 만한 편지를—썼으니, 터셔스가 미들마치 같은 곳을 떠나 자기 재능에 더 어울리는 곳으로 옮겨 가는 것이 얼마나 바람직한지, 그곳 주민들의 불쾌한 성정이 어떻게 그의 직업적 성공을 가로막았는지, 그리고 그 결과 그가 어떻게 금전적 곤경에 처했으며, 그를 완전히 거기서 빼내려면 천 파운드가 필요하다는 것을 짚어 보였다."], ["She did not say that Tertius was unaware of her intention to write; for she had the idea that his supposed sanction of her letter would be in accordance with what she did say of his great regard for his uncle Godwin as the relative who had always been his best friend.", "그녀는 자기가 편지를 쓰려 한다는 것을 터셔스가 모르고 있다는 말은 하지 않았다. 자기 편지를 그가 묵인했다고 가정하는 것이, 그가 늘 자기의 가장 좋은 벗이었던 친척인 고드윈 외삼촌을 크게 존경한다고 자기가 한 말과 부합하리라고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Such was the force of Poor Rosamond’s tactics now she applied them to affairs.", "가엾은 로저먼드의 술책이 이제 실무에 적용될 때 발휘되는 위력이 그러했다."]], [["This had happened before the party on New Year’s Day, and no answer had yet come from Sir Godwin.", "이 일은 새해 첫날의 만찬 모임 이전에 있었던 것이며, 고드윈 경에게서는 아직 아무 답장도 오지 않았다."], ["But on the morning of that day Lydgate had to learn that Rosamond had revoked his order to Borthrop Trumbull.", "그런데 그날 아침 리드게이트는 로저먼드가 보스럽 트럼불에게 내린 자기 지시를 취소했다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Feeling it necessary that she should be gradually accustomed to the idea of their quitting the house in Lowick Gate, he overcame his reluctance to speak to her again on the subject, and when they were breakfasting said—", "그녀가 로윅 게이트의 집을 떠나야 한다는 생각에 차츰 익숙해질 필요가 있다고 느낀 그는, 그 일에 관해 다시 그녀에게 말하기를 꺼리는 마음을 누르고, 아침 식사를 하면서 말했다."]], [["“I shall try to see Trumbull this morning, and tell him to advertise the house in the ‘Pioneer’ and the ‘Trumpet.’ If the thing were advertised, some one might be inclined to take it who would not otherwise have thought of a change.", "“오늘 아침에 트럼불을 만나서, 집을 ‘파이어니어’와 ‘트럼펫’에 광고로 내달라고 일러 둘 참이야. 광고를 내면, 그러지 않았더라면 이사를 생각도 안 했을 누군가가 마음이 끌릴지도 몰라."], ["In these country places many people go on in their old houses when their families are too large for them, for want of knowing where they can find another.", "이런 시골에서는 식구가 너무 늘어 집이 좁아져도, 다른 집을 어디서 구할 수 있는지 몰라서 옛집에 그대로 눌러 사는 사람이 많거든."], ["And Trumbull seems to have got no bite at all.”", "그런데 트럼불은 도무지 입질 하나 받지 못한 모양이야.”"]], [["Rosamond knew that the inevitable moment was come.", "로저먼드는 피할 수 없는 순간이 왔음을 알았다."], ["“I ordered Trumbull not to inquire further,”", "“트럼불한테 더는 알아보지 말라고 일러 두었어요.”"], ["she said, with a careful calmness which was evidently defensive.", "그녀가, 누가 봐도 방어적인, 조심스러운 침착함으로 말했다."]], [["Lydgate stared at her in mute amazement.", "리드게이트는 말문이 막힌 채 그녀를 멍하니 쳐다보았다."], ["Only half an hour before he had been fastening up her plaits for her, and talking the “little language” of affection, which Rosamond, though not returning it, accepted as if she had been a serene and lovely image, now and then miraculously dimpling towards her votary.", "불과 반 시간 전만 해도 그는 그녀의 땋은 머리를 매만져 주며 애정의 “어린아이 말투”를 건네던 참이었고, 로저먼드는 그것을 되돌려 주지는 않으면서도, 마치 자기가 고요하고 사랑스러운 우상이라도 되는 양, 이따금 자기를 떠받드는 신도를 향해 신비롭게 보조개를 지어 보이며 받아들이곤 했다."], ["With such fibres still astir in him, the shock he received could not at once be distinctly anger; it was confused pain.", "그러한 애정의 결이 그의 안에서 아직 가시지 않은 채 떨고 있던 터라, 그가 받은 충격은 곧바로 또렷한 분노일 수는 없었다."], ["He laid down the knife and fork with which he was carving, and throwing himself back in his chair, said at last, with a cool irony in his tone—", "그것은 어수선한 아픔이었다. 그는 고기를 썰던 칼과 포크를 내려놓고, 의자에 몸을 뒤로 젖히며, 마침내 차가운 빈정거림을 어조에 담아 말했다."]], [["“May I ask when and why you did so?”", "“언제, 왜 그랬는지 물어봐도 될까?”"]], [["“When I knew that the Plymdales had taken a house, I called to tell him not to mention ours to them; and at the same time I told him not to let the affair go on any further.", "“플림데일네가 집을 얻은 걸 알았을 때, 그 집에 대해 그들에게 우리 집 이야기를 꺼내지 말라고 일러 두러 갔어요. 그리고 그 자리에서, 그 일을 더는 진행하지 말라고 했고요."], ["I knew that it would be very injurious to you if it were known that you wished to part with your house and furniture, and I had a very strong objection to it.", "당신이 집과 가구를 처분하고 싶어 한다는 게 알려지면 당신한테 무척 해로울 거라는 걸 알았고, 저는 그게 몹시 못마땅했거든요."], ["I think that was reason enough.”", "그것으로 충분한 이유가 된다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“It was of no consequence then that I had told you imperative reasons of another kind; of no consequence that I had come to a different conclusion, and given an order accordingly?”", "“그럼 내가 당신한테 그것과는 다른 종류의 절박한 이유를 말한 건 아무 상관도 없었다는 거군. 내가 다른 결론에 이르렀고, 그에 따라 지시를 내렸다는 것도 아무 상관 없었다는 거야?”"], ["said Lydgate, bitingly, the thunder and lightning gathering about his brow and eyes.", "리드게이트가 매섭게 말했고, 천둥과 번개가 그의 이마와 눈가에 모여들고 있었다."]], [["The effect of any one’s anger on Rosamond had always been to make her shrink in cold dislike, and to become all the more calmly correct, in the conviction that she was not the person to misbehave whatever others might do.", "누군가의 분노가 로저먼드에게 미치는 영향은 늘, 그녀를 싸늘한 혐오 속에 움츠러들게 하고, 남들이야 어떻게 굴든 자기는 그릇되게 행동할 사람이 아니라는 확신 속에서 한층 더 차분하게 흠잡을 데 없는 태도를 갖게 만드는 것이었다."], ["She replied—", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“I think I had a perfect right to speak on a subject which concerns me at least as much as you.”", "“적어도 당신만큼이나 저와 관계되는 일에 대해 말할 권리가 저한테 충분히 있다고 생각해요.”"]], [["“Clearly—you had a right to speak, but only to me.", "“분명하지—당신은 말할 권리가 있었어, 하지만 오직 나한테만이지."], ["You had no right to contradict my orders secretly, and treat me as if I were a fool,”", "당신한테는 내 지시를 몰래 거스를 권리도, 나를 바보 취급할 권리도 없었어.”"], ["said Lydgate, in the same tone as before.", "리드게이트가 앞서와 똑같은 어조로 말했다."], ["Then with some added scorn, “Is it possible to make you understand what the consequences will be?", "그러더니 얼마간 경멸을 더해 말했다. “그 결과가 어떻게 될지 당신한테 이해시키는 게 가능하기는 할까?"], ["Is it of any use for me to tell you again why we must try to part with the house?”", "우리가 왜 이 집을 처분하려고 애써야 하는지를 다시 말해 주는 게 무슨 소용이 있기는 할까?”"]], [["“It is not necessary for you to tell me again,”", "“다시 말해 줄 필요는 없어요.”"], ["said Rosamond, in a voice that fell and trickled like cold water-drops.", "로저먼드가, 차가운 물방울처럼 낮게 떨어져 흘러내리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I remembered what you said.", "“당신이 한 말을 기억하고 있어요."], ["You spoke just as violently as you do now.", "지금처럼 그때도 똑같이 격하게 말했죠."], ["But that does not alter my opinion that you ought to try every other means rather than take a step which is so painful to me.", "하지만 그렇다고, 저한테 그토록 괴로운 그 한 걸음을 내딛기 전에 다른 온갖 방도를 다 시도해 봐야 한다는 제 생각이 바뀌지는 않아요."], ["And as to advertising the house, I think it would be perfectly degrading to you.”", "그리고 집을 광고로 내는 것에 대해 말하자면, 그건 당신한테 더없이 수치스러운 일이라고 생각해요.”"]], [["“And suppose I disregard your opinion as you disregard mine?”", "“그럼 당신이 내 생각을 무시하듯 내가 당신 생각을 무시한다면?”"]], [["“You can do so, of course.", "“물론 그러실 수 있죠."], ["But I think you ought to have told me before we were married that you would place me in the worst position, rather than give up your own will.”", "하지만 자기 고집을 꺾느니 차라리 저를 최악의 처지에 빠뜨리겠다는 거라면, 결혼하기 전에 미리 말해 줬어야 한다고 생각해요.”"]], [["Lydgate did not speak, but tossed his head on one side, and twitched the corners of his mouth in despair.", "리드게이트는 말이 없었다. 다만 고개를 한쪽으로 홱 젖히고, 절망에 입가를 씰룩였다."], ["Rosamond, seeing that he was not looking at her, rose and set his cup of coffee before him; but he took no notice of it, and went on with an inward drama and argument, occasionally moving in his seat, resting one arm on the table, and rubbing his hand against his hair.", "로저먼드는 그가 자기를 보고 있지 않은 것을 알고 일어나 그의 앞에 커피잔을 놓아 주었다. 그러나 그는 그것에 아랑곳하지 않고, 이따금 의자에서 몸을 움직이며, 한 팔을 탁자에 괴고, 손으로 머리카락을 비비면서 내면의 극과 논쟁을 이어 갔다."], ["There was a conflux of emotions and thoughts in him that would not let him either give thorough way to his anger or persevere with simple rigidity of resolve.", "그의 안에는 갖가지 감정과 생각이 한데 모여들어, 그가 분노에 온전히 자신을 내맡기지도, 단순한 결심의 완고함을 끝까지 밀고 나가지도 못하게 했다."], ["Rosamond took advantage of his silence.", "로저먼드는 그의 침묵을 틈탔다."]], [["“When we were married everyone felt that your position was very high.", "“우리가 결혼했을 때 모두가 당신 입지가 아주 높다고 여겼어요."], ["I could not have imagined then that you would want to sell our furniture, and take a house in Bride Street, where the rooms are like cages.", "그때는 당신이 우리 가구를 팔고, 방들이 새장 같은 브라이드 가의 집을 얻고 싶어 하리라곤 상상도 못 했어요."], ["If we are to live in that way let us at least leave Middlemarch.”", "우리가 그렇게 살아야 한다면, 적어도 미들마치는 떠나요.”"]], [["“These would be very strong considerations,”", "“그건 아주 강력한 고려 사항이겠지.”"], ["said Lydgate, half ironically—still there was a withered paleness about his lips as he looked at his coffee, and did not drink—“these would be very strong considerations if I did not happen to be in debt.”", "리드게이트가 반쯤 빈정대며 말했다—그래도 커피를 바라보며 입에 대지 않는 그의 입술 언저리에는 핏기 가신 시든 빛이 감돌았다—“내가 마침 빚을 지고 있지만 않다면, 그건 아주 강력한 고려 사항이겠지.”"]], [["“Many persons must have been in debt in the same way, but if they are respectable, people trust them.", "“같은 식으로 빚을 진 사람이 분명 많았을 텐데, 그래도 그들이 점잖은 사람들이면 사람들이 믿어 주잖아요."], ["I am sure I have heard papa say that the Torbits were in debt, and they went on very well.", "아빠가 토빗네도 빚을 졌었지만 아주 잘 지냈다고 말하는 걸 분명히 들었어요."], ["It cannot be good to act rashly,”", "경솔하게 행동하는 건 좋을 리가 없어요.”"], ["said Rosamond, with serene wisdom.", "로저먼드가 태연자약한 지혜로 말했다."]], [["Lydgate sat paralyzed by opposing impulses: since no reasoning he could apply to Rosamond seemed likely to conquer her assent, he wanted to smash and grind some object on which he could at least produce an impression, or else to tell her brutally that he was master, and she must obey.", "리드게이트는 서로 맞부딪치는 충동들에 마비된 채 앉아 있었다. 로저먼드에게 아무리 이치를 들이대도 그녀의 동의를 끌어낼 가망이 없어 보였으므로, 그는 적어도 어떤 자국이라도 남길 수 있는 무언가를 박살 내고 짓이기든가, 아니면 자기가 가장이고 그녀는 따라야 한다고 거칠게 쏘아붙이고 싶었다."], ["But he not only dreaded the effect of such extremities on their mutual life—he had a growing dread of Rosamond’s quiet elusive obstinacy, which would not allow any assertion of power to be final; and again, she had touched him in a spot of keenest feeling by implying that she had been deluded with a false vision of happiness in marrying him.", "그러나 그는 그런 극단적인 행동이 두 사람의 부부 생활에 미칠 영향을 두려워했을 뿐 아니라—어떤 권위의 행사도 끝내 매듭짓게 두지 않는 로저먼드의 조용하고 종잡을 수 없는 고집에 대한 두려움이 점점 커져 가고 있었다. 게다가 그녀는, 그와 결혼하면서 행복에 대한 거짓 환상에 속았다는 것을 넌지시 내비침으로써, 그의 가장 예민한 곳을 건드린 터였다."], ["As to saying that he was master, it was not the fact.", "자기가 가장이라고 말하는 것으로 말하자면, 그것은 사실이 아니었다."], ["The very resolution to which he had wrought himself by dint of logic and honorable pride was beginning to relax under her torpedo contact.", "논리와 명예로운 자존심의 힘으로 애써 다잡아 온 바로 그 결심이, 그녀의 전기뱀장어 같은 접촉 아래 풀어지기 시작하고 있었다."], ["He swallowed half his cup of coffee, and then rose to go.", "그는 커피를 반쯤 들이켜고는 일어나서 나가려 했다."]], [["“I may at least request that you will not go to Trumbull at present—until it has been seen that there are no other means,”", "“적어도, 다른 방도가 전혀 없다는 게 드러날 때까지는—당분간 트럼불한테 가지 말아 주시기를 청해도 될까요.”"], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["Although she was not subject to much fear, she felt it safer not to betray that she had written to Sir Godwin.", "그녀는 별로 겁이 많은 편은 아니었지만, 고드윈 경에게 편지를 썼다는 것을 드러내지 않는 편이 안전하다고 느꼈다."], ["“Promise me that you will not go to him for a few weeks, or without telling me.”", "“몇 주만이라도, 아니면 저한테 말하지 않고는 그에게 가지 않겠다고 약속해 줘요.”"]], [["Lydgate gave a short laugh.", "리드게이트는 짧게 웃었다."], ["“I think it is I who should exact a promise that you will do nothing without telling me,”", "“약속을 요구해야 할 사람은 오히려 나일 것 같은데, 당신이 나한테 말하지 않고는 아무 일도 하지 않겠다는 약속 말이야.”"], ["he said, turning his eyes sharply upon her, and then moving to the door.", "그가 날카로운 눈길을 그녀에게 돌리며 말하고는, 문 쪽으로 발길을 옮겼다."]], [["“You remember that we are going to dine at papa’s,”", "“우리 아빠 댁에서 저녁 먹기로 한 거 기억하죠.”"], ["said Rosamond, wishing that he should turn and make a more thorough concession to her.", "로저먼드가, 그가 돌아서서 자기에게 좀 더 온전한 양보를 해 주기를 바라며 말했다."], ["But he only said “Oh yes,” impatiently, and went away.", "그러나 그는 다만 짜증스럽게 “아, 그래.”라고만 하고는 나가 버렸다."], ["She held it to be very odious in him that he did not think the painful propositions he had had to make to her were enough, without showing so unpleasant a temper.", "그녀는, 자기한테 그토록 괴로운 제안들을 해야 했던 것으로도 모자라, 그렇게 불쾌한 성미까지 부리는 것이 그에게서 참으로 가증스러운 일이라고 여겼다."], ["And when she put the moderate request that he would defer going to Trumbull again, it was cruel in him not to assure her of what he meant to do.", "그리고 그녀가 트럼불한테 다시 가는 것을 미뤄 달라는 온건한 부탁을 했을 때, 자기가 어떻게 할 작정인지를 그녀에게 분명히 일러 주지 않은 것은 그의 잔인함이었다."], ["She was convinced of her having acted in every way for the best; and each grating or angry speech of Lydgate’s served only as an addition to the register of offences in her mind.", "그녀는 자기가 모든 면에서 최선을 다해 행동했다고 확신했다. 그리고 리드게이트의 거슬리거나 노여운 말 한마디 한마디는 다만 그녀의 마음속 잘못의 명부에 한 항목씩 더해질 뿐이었다."], ["Poor Rosamond for months had begun to associate her husband with feelings of disappointment, and the terribly inflexible relation of marriage had lost its charm of encouraging delightful dreams.", "가엾은 로저먼드는 몇 달째 남편을 실망의 감정과 결부 지어 생각하기 시작했고, 결혼이라는 그 무섭도록 융통성 없는 관계는 즐거운 꿈을 북돋우는 매력을 잃어버렸다. 그것은 그녀를 친정의 갖가지 불편함에서 벗어나게 해 주었지만, 그녀가 바라고 기대했던 모든 것을 주지는 못했다."], ["It had freed her from the disagreeables of her father’s house, but it had not given her everything that she had wished and hoped.", "그녀가 사랑에 빠졌던 그 리드게이트는, 그녀에게는 한 무더기의 공허한 조건들이었으니, 그 대부분은 이미 사라져 버린 반면, 그 자리는 시시각각 천천히 살아 내야 할 일상의 자질구레한 일들로 채워졌다."], ["The Lydgate with whom she had been in love had been a group of airy conditions for her, most of which had disappeared, while their place had been taken by every-day details which must be lived through slowly from hour to hour, not floated through with a rapid selection of favorable aspects.", "그것은 유리한 면들만 재빨리 골라내 미끄러지듯 지나칠 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 리드게이트의 직업적 습관, 거의 병적인 흡혈귀의 기호처럼 그녀에게 비친, 과학적 주제에 대한 그의 집안에서의 골몰, 구애의 대화에는 한 번도 끼어든 적 없던 사물에 대한 그의 독특한 견해—이 모든 끊임없이 멀어지게 하는 영향들은, 그가 이 고장에서 스스로를 불리한 처지에 놓았다는 사실이 없었더라도, 또 도버의 빚에 관한 그 첫 폭로의 충격이 없었더라도, 그의 존재를 그녀에게 따분한 것으로 만들었을 것이다."], ["The habits of Lydgate’s profession, his home preoccupation with scientific subjects, which seemed to her almost like a morbid vampire’s taste, his peculiar views of things which had never entered into the dialogue of courtship—all these continually alienating influences, even without the fact of his having placed himself at a disadvantage in the town, and without that first shock of revelation about Dover’s debt, would have made his presence dull to her.", "그녀의 결혼 초기부터 넉 달 전까지 줄곧 유쾌한 설렘이 되어 주던 또 다른 존재가 있었으나, 그것은 이제 사라지고 없었다. 로저먼드는 그로 인한 공백이 자기의 사무친 권태와 얼마나 깊이 관련되어 있는지를 스스로에게 인정하려 하지 않았다."], ["There was another presence which ever since the early days of her marriage, until four months ago, had been an agreeable excitement, but that was gone: Rosamond would not confess to herself how much the consequent blank had to do with her utter ennui; and it seemed to her (perhaps she was right) that an invitation to Quallingham, and an opening for Lydgate to settle elsewhere than in Middlemarch—in London, or somewhere likely to be free from unpleasantness—would satisfy her quite well, and make her indifferent to the absence of Will Ladislaw, towards whom she felt some resentment for his exaltation of Mrs. Casaubon.", "그리고 그녀에게는 (어쩌면 그녀가 옳았는지도 모른다) 콸링엄으로의 초대와, 리드게이트가 미들마치 아닌 다른 곳에—런던이나, 불쾌한 일에서 자유로울 만한 어딘가에 자리 잡을 길이 열린다면, 그것으로 충분히 만족스러울 것이고, 캐소본 부인을 떠받든 일로 어느 정도 원망을 품고 있던 윌 래디슬로의 부재에도 무심해질 수 있을 것 같았다."]], [["That was the state of things with Lydgate and Rosamond on the New Year’s Day when they dined at her father’s, she looking mildly neutral towards him in remembrance of his ill-tempered behavior at breakfast, and he carrying a much deeper effect from the inward conflict in which that morning scene was only one of many epochs.", "새해 첫날, 친정아버지 댁에서 저녁을 먹던 그날 리드게이트와 로저먼드의 사정이 그러했다. 그녀는 아침 식사 자리에서의 그의 고약한 처신을 떠올리며 그에게 온화하게 무관심한 태도를 보였고, 그는 그 아침의 장면이 여러 국면 가운데 하나에 지나지 않았던 그 내면의 갈등에서 훨씬 더 깊은 영향을 받은 채였다."], ["His flushed effort while talking to Mr. Farebrother—his effort after the cynical pretence that all ways of getting money are essentially the same, and that chance has an empire which reduces choice to a fool’s illusion—was but the symptom of a wavering resolve, a benumbed response to the old stimuli of enthusiasm.", "페어브러더 씨와 이야기하면서 그가 보인 상기된 안간힘—돈을 버는 모든 방법이 본질적으로 다 똑같으며, 우연이라는 것이 선택을 한낱 바보의 환상으로 깎아내리는 제국을 거느린다는, 그 냉소적인 가식 뒤의 안간힘—은 다만 흔들리는 결심의 한 증상이요, 열정의 옛 자극에 대한 둔감해진 반응일 뿐이었다."]], [["What was he to do?", "그는 어떻게 해야 했을까?"], ["He saw even more keenly than Rosamond did the dreariness of taking her into the small house in Bride Street, where she would have scanty furniture around her and discontent within: a life of privation and life with Rosamond were two images which had become more and more irreconcilable ever since the threat of privation had disclosed itself.", "그녀를 브라이드 가의 그 작은 집으로 데려가는 일의 황량함을, 그는 로저먼드보다도 한층 더 절실히 보았다. 그곳에서 그녀는 빈약한 가구에 둘러싸인 채 마음속에는 불만을 품게 될 터였다."], ["But even if his resolves had forced the two images into combination, the useful preliminaries to that hard change were not visibly within reach.", "궁핍한 삶과 로저먼드와 함께하는 삶이라는 두 이미지는, 궁핍의 위협이 모습을 드러낸 이래로 점점 더 서로 화해할 수 없는 것이 되어 갔다."], ["And though he had not given the promise which his wife had asked for, he did not go again to Trumbull. He even began to think of taking a rapid journey to the North and seeing Sir Godwin.", "그러나 설령 그의 결심이 그 두 이미지를 억지로 결합시켰다 한들, 그 고된 변화로 가는 데 필요한 유용한 사전 절차들은 손에 잡히는 데 있어 보이지 않았다."], ["He had once believed that nothing would urge him into making an application for money to his uncle, but he had not then known the full pressure of alternatives yet more disagreeable.", "그리고 아내가 청한 약속을 해 주지는 않았으나, 그는 다시 트럼불에게 가지 않았다. 그는 심지어 서둘러 북쪽으로 길을 떠나 고드윈 경을 만나 볼까 하는 생각까지 하기 시작했다."], ["He could not depend on the effect of a letter; it was only in an interview, however disagreeable this might be to himself, that he could give a thorough explanation and could test the effectiveness of kinship.", "한때 그는 무슨 일이 있어도 삼촌에게 돈을 청하는 일만큼은 결코 하지 않으리라 믿었지만, 그때는 그보다 한층 더 못마땅한 대안들이 주는 압박의 무게를 온전히 알지 못했던 것이다. 그는 편지의 효과에 기댈 수가 없었다."], ["No sooner had Lydgate begun to represent this step to himself as the easiest than there was a reaction of anger that he—he who had long ago determined to live aloof from such abject calculations, such self-interested anxiety about the inclinations and the pockets of men with whom he had been proud to have no aims in common—should have fallen not simply to their level, but to the level of soliciting them.", "아무리 그 자신에게 못마땅한 일일지라도, 직접 만나는 자리에서만 철저히 사정을 설명하고 혈연의 효력을 시험해 볼 수 있었다. 그런데 이 한 걸음을 가장 손쉬운 방도로 머릿속에 그리기 시작하자마자, 곧 분노의 반작용이 일었다—오래전에 그토록 비루한 타산에서, 함께할 목표라곤 없음을 자랑스러워했던 사람들의 성향이며 호주머니에 대한 그런 이기적인 불안에서 멀찍이 떨어져 살기로 작정했던 자기가—그들의 수준으로 떨어진 것도 모자라, 그들에게 구걸하는 수준으로까지 떨어졌다는 데 대한 분노였다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXV.", "# CHAPTER LXV."]], [["One of us two must bowen douteless, And, sith a man is more reasonable Than woman is, ye [men] moste be suffrable.", "그대들 [남자들]은 마땅히 참아야 하리라."], ["—CHAUCER: Canterbury Tales.", "—초서: 『캔터베리 이야기』"]], [["The bias of human nature to be slow in correspondence triumphs even over the present quickening in the general pace of things: what wonder then that in 1832 old Sir Godwin Lydgate was slow to write a letter which was of consequence to others rather than to himself?", "무슨 일에든 응답이 더딘 것은 인간 본성의 한 경향이어서, 그것은 만사의 속도가 전반적으로 빨라진 오늘날에도 끝내 승리를 거두는 법이다. 그러니 1832년에 늙은 고드윈 리드게이트 경이 자기 자신보다는 남에게 더 중요한 편지를 쓰는 데 느렸다 한들 무엇이 놀랍겠는가?"], ["Nearly three weeks of the new year were gone, and Rosamond, awaiting an answer to her winning appeal, was every day disappointed.", "새해가 시작된 지 거의 삼 주가 지났고, 로저먼드는 자신의 매력적인 호소에 대한 답장을 기다리며 날마다 실망했다."], ["Lydgate, in total ignorance of her expectations, was seeing the bills come in, and feeling that Dover’s use of his advantage over other creditors was imminent.", "리드게이트는 아내의 기대를 전혀 알지 못한 채, 청구서들이 들어오는 것을 지켜보며 도버가 다른 채권자들보다 우위에 선 자기 권리를 곧 행사하리라는 것을 느끼고 있었다."], ["He had never mentioned to Rosamond his brooding purpose of going to Quallingham: he did not want to admit what would appear to her a concession to her wishes after indignant refusal, until the last moment; but he was really expecting to set off soon.", "그는 콸링엄으로 갈 생각을 마음속에 품고 있으면서도 그것을 로저먼드에게 한 번도 말하지 않았다. 분개하며 거절한 뒤에 그녀의 바람에 양보하는 것처럼 보일 일을 마지막 순간까지는 인정하고 싶지 않았던 것이다."], ["A slice of the railway would enable him to manage the whole journey and back in four days.", "하지만 그는 사실 곧 떠날 작정이었다. 철도의 한 구간을 이용하면 왕복 여행 전체를 나흘 만에 해낼 수 있을 터였다."]], [["But one morning after Lydgate had gone out, a letter came addressed to him, which Rosamond saw clearly to be from Sir Godwin.", "그런데 어느 날 아침 리드게이트가 외출한 뒤에 그에게 온 편지 한 통이 도착했고, 로저먼드는 그것이 고드윈 경에게서 온 것임을 분명히 알아보았다."], ["She was full of hope.", "그녀는 희망으로 가득 찼다."], ["Perhaps there might be a particular note to her enclosed; but Lydgate was naturally addressed on the question of money or other aid, and the fact that he was written to, nay, the very delay in writing at all, seemed to certify that the answer was thoroughly compliant.", "어쩌면 그 안에 자신에게 보내는 특별한 쪽지가 동봉되어 있을지도 몰랐다. 하지만 돈이나 그 밖의 도움 문제라면 당연히 리드게이트에게 편지가 가게 마련이었고, 그에게 답장이 왔다는 사실, 아니 답장이 그토록 늦었다는 사실 자체가 그 답이 완전히 호의적이라는 것을 증명하는 듯했다."], ["She was too much excited by these thoughts to do anything but light stitching in a warm corner of the dining-room, with the outside of this momentous letter lying on the table before her.", "그녀는 이런 생각에 너무 들떠서, 식당의 따뜻한 구석에서 가벼운 바느질이나 할 뿐이었고, 그 중대한 편지의 겉봉을 앞 탁자 위에 놓아두었다. 열두 시쯤 복도에서 남편의 발소리가 들리자 그녀는 종종걸음으로 문을 열러 가며 더없이 가벼운 목소리로 말했다."], ["About twelve she heard her husband’s step in the passage, and tripping to open the door, she said in her lightest tones, “Tertius, come in here—here is a letter for you.”", "\"터셔스, 이리 들어와요. 당신한테 편지가 왔어요.\""]], [["“Ah?”", "\"아?\""], ["he said, not taking off his hat, but just turning her round within his arm to walk towards the spot where the letter lay.", "그가 모자도 벗지 않고 다만 그녀를 팔로 감싸 돌려세워 편지가 놓인 곳을 향해 걸으며 말했다."], ["“My uncle Godwin!”", "\"고드윈 삼촌이군!\""], ["he exclaimed, while Rosamond reseated herself, and watched him as he opened the letter.", "그가 소리쳤고, 로저먼드는 다시 자리에 앉아 그가 편지를 뜯는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["She had expected him to be surprised.", "그녀는 그가 놀라리라 예상했다."]], [["While Lydgate’s eyes glanced rapidly over the brief letter, she saw his face, usually of a pale brown, taking on a dry whiteness; with nostrils and lips quivering he tossed down the letter before her, and said violently—", "리드게이트의 눈이 그 짧은 편지를 빠르게 훑는 동안, 그녀는 평소 엷은 갈색이던 그의 얼굴이 마른 듯한 흰빛으로 변하는 것을 보았다. 콧방울과 입술을 떨면서 그는 편지를 그녀 앞에 내던지고는 격렬하게 말했다."]], [["“It will be impossible to endure life with you, if you will always be acting secretly—acting in opposition to me and hiding your actions.”", "\"당신이 늘 이렇게 몰래 행동한다면, 나에게 맞서 행동하고 자기 행동을 숨긴다면, 당신과 함께 사는 삶을 견디기란 불가능할 거요.\""]], [["He checked his speech and turned his back on her—then wheeled round and walked about, sat down, and got up again restlessly, grasping hard the objects deep down in his pockets.", "그는 말을 멈추고 그녀에게 등을 돌렸다. 그러다 휙 돌아서서 이리저리 거닐다가 앉았다가 다시 안절부절못하며 일어나, 주머니 깊숙이 든 물건들을 단단히 움켜쥐었다."], ["He was afraid of saying something irremediably cruel.", "그는 돌이킬 수 없이 잔인한 말을 할까 봐 두려웠다."]], [["Rosamond too had changed color as she read.", "로저먼드 역시 편지를 읽으면서 안색이 변했다."], ["The letter ran in this way:—", "편지는 이런 내용이었다."]], [["“DEAR TERTIUS,—Don’t set your wife to write to me when you have anything to ask.", "\"친애하는 터셔스에게. 무언가 부탁할 일이 있거든 자네 아내를 시켜 나에게 편지를 쓰게 하지 말게."], ["It is a roundabout wheedling sort of thing which I should not have credited you with.", "그것은 빙 둘러 알랑거리는 짓으로, 자네가 그럴 사람이라고는 생각지 못했네."], ["I never choose to write to a woman on matters of business.", "나는 사업 문제로 여자에게 편지를 쓰는 일을 결코 택하지 않네."], ["As to my supplying you with a thousand pounds, or only half that sum, I can do nothing of the sort.", "자네에게 천 파운드를, 아니 그 절반이라도 마련해 주는 일이라면, 나로서는 도저히 할 수 없네."], ["My own family drains me to the last penny.", "내 가족만으로도 한 푼 남김없이 들어가니 말일세."], ["With two younger sons and three daughters, I am not likely to have cash to spare.", "아들 둘에 딸 셋을 두었으니, 남는 돈이 있을 리 없지 않은가."], ["You seem to have got through your own money pretty quickly, and to have made a mess where you are; the sooner you go somewhere else the better.", "자네는 제 돈을 꽤나 빨리 다 써 버린 모양이고, 지금 있는 곳에서 엉망을 만들어 놓은 듯하네. 그러니 빨리 다른 데로 가는 편이 낫겠네."], ["But I have nothing to do with men of your profession, and can’t help you there.", "하지만 나는 자네 직업의 사람들과는 아무 상관이 없으니, 그 일에서는 자네를 도울 수가 없네."], ["I did the best I could for you as guardian, and let you have your own way in taking to medicine.", "후견인으로서 나는 자네를 위해 최선을 다했고, 자네가 의술을 택하겠다 했을 때도 자네 뜻대로 하게 두었네."], ["You might have gone into the army or the Church.", "자네는 군대나 교회로 갈 수도 있었어."], ["Your money would have held out for that, and there would have been a surer ladder before you.", "그랬다면 자네 돈으로 충분했을 것이고, 자네 앞에는 더 확실한 사다리가 놓였을 걸세."], ["Your uncle Charles has had a grudge against you for not going into his profession, but not I.", "자네 삼촌 찰스는 자네가 자기 직업을 택하지 않았다고 자네에게 앙심을 품었지만, 나는 그렇지 않네."], ["I have always wished you well, but you must consider yourself on your own legs entirely now.", "나는 늘 자네가 잘되기를 바랐네만, 이제 자네는 전적으로 제 발로 서야 한다고 여겨야 하네."]], [["Your affectionate uncle, GODWIN LYDGATE.”", "자네를 아끼는 삼촌,\n고드윈 리드게이트.\""]], [["When Rosamond had finished reading the letter she sat quite still, with her hands folded before her, restraining any show of her keen disappointment, and intrenching herself in quiet passivity under her husband’s wrath.", "편지를 다 읽고 나서 로저먼드는 두 손을 앞에 모은 채 아주 가만히 앉아, 날카로운 실망의 기색을 조금도 드러내지 않고, 남편의 분노 아래 조용한 수동성으로 자신을 에워쌌다."], ["Lydgate paused in his movements, looked at her again, and said, with biting severity—", "리드게이트는 거닐던 걸음을 멈추고 다시 그녀를 바라보며 신랄하게 매서운 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Will this be enough to convince you of the harm you may do by secret meddling?", "\"이것으로도 당신이 몰래 참견함으로써 끼칠 수 있는 해악을 깨닫기에 충분하지 않소?"], ["Have you sense enough to recognize now your incompetence to judge and act for me—to interfere with your ignorance in affairs which it belongs to me to decide on?”", "이제 당신이 나를 위해 판단하고 행동할 능력이 없다는 것을, 마땅히 내가 결정할 일에 무지한 채로 끼어든다는 것을 알아차릴 분별력은 있소?\""]], [["The words were hard; but this was not the first time that Lydgate had been frustrated by her.", "모진 말이었다. 하지만 리드게이트가 그녀 때문에 좌절한 것은 이번이 처음이 아니었다."], ["She did not look at him, and made no reply.", "그녀는 그를 쳐다보지 않았고 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“I had nearly resolved on going to Quallingham.", "\"나는 콸링엄으로 갈 결심을 거의 굳혔었소."], ["It would have cost me pain enough to do it, yet it might have been of some use.", "그렇게 하려면 나로서는 충분히 괴로운 일이었겠지만, 그래도 얼마쯤 도움이 되었을지도 모르오."], ["But it has been of no use for me to think of anything.", "하지만 내가 무엇을 생각하든 다 소용없는 일이었군."], ["You have always been counteracting me secretly.", "당신은 늘 몰래 나를 방해해 왔으니까."], ["You delude me with a false assent, and then I am at the mercy of your devices.", "당신은 거짓 동의로 나를 속이고, 그러고 나면 나는 당신의 술책에 휘둘리는 신세가 되지."], ["If you mean to resist every wish I express, say so and defy me.", "내가 표하는 모든 바람에 맞설 작정이라면, 그렇다고 말하고 나에게 대놓고 맞서시오."], ["I shall at least know what I am doing then.”", "그러면 적어도 내가 무엇을 하고 있는지는 알 테니.\""]], [["It is a terrible moment in young lives when the closeness of love’s bond has turned to this power of galling.", "사랑의 끈이 맺어 준 친밀함이 이처럼 서로를 쓰라리게 하는 힘으로 변하는 것은 젊은 삶에서 끔찍한 순간이다."], ["In spite of Rosamond’s self-control a tear fell silently and rolled over her lips.", "로저먼드의 자제력에도 불구하고 눈물 한 방울이 소리 없이 흘러 그녀의 입술을 타고 굴러떨어졌다. 그녀는 여전히 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She still said nothing; but under that quietude was hidden an intense effect: she was in such entire disgust with her husband that she wished she had never seen him.", "그러나 그 고요함 아래에는 강렬한 영향이 숨겨져 있었다. 그녀는 남편이 너무도 역겨워서, 차라리 그를 만나지 않았더라면 좋았으리라고 생각했다."], ["Sir Godwin’s rudeness towards her and utter want of feeling ranged him with Dover and all other creditors—disagreeable people who only thought of themselves, and did not mind how annoying they were to her.", "그녀를 향한 고드윈 경의 무례함과 완전한 몰인정함은 그를 도버를 비롯한 다른 모든 채권자들과 한 부류로 만들었다. 오직 자기 생각만 하고 그녀에게 얼마나 성가신지는 아랑곳하지 않는 불쾌한 사람들 말이다."], ["Even her father was unkind, and might have done more for them.", "그녀의 아버지조차 매정했고, 그들을 위해 더 해 줄 수 있었을 것이다."], ["In fact there was but one person in Rosamond’s world whom she did not regard as blameworthy, and that was the graceful creature with blond plaits and with little hands crossed before her, who had never expressed herself unbecomingly, and had always acted for the best—the best naturally being what she best liked.", "사실 로저먼드의 세계에서 비난받을 만하다고 여겨지지 않는 사람은 단 한 명뿐이었으니, 바로 금발을 땋아 내리고 작은 두 손을 앞에 포갠 우아한 존재, 단 한 번도 어울리지 않게 자신을 드러낸 적 없고 늘 최선을 다해 행동해 온—물론 그 최선이란 으레 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 것이었다—그녀 자신이었다."]], [["Lydgate pausing and looking at her began to feel that half-maddening sense of helplessness which comes over passionate people when their passion is met by an innocent-looking silence whose meek victimized air seems to put them in the wrong, and at last infects even the justest indignation with a doubt of its justice.", "리드게이트는 걸음을 멈추고 그녀를 바라보다가, 격정적인 사람이 자신의 격정과 마주한 것이 천진해 보이는 침묵일 때, 그 온순하게 희생당하는 듯한 태도가 도리어 자기를 잘못한 사람으로 만들고, 마침내 가장 정당한 분노마저 그 정당함을 의심하게 만들 때 밀려드는, 그 반쯤 미칠 것 같은 무력감을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He needed to recover the full sense that he was in the right by moderating his words.", "그는 자기 말을 누그러뜨림으로써 자신이 옳다는 온전한 감각을 되찾아야 했다."]], [["“Can you not see, Rosamond,”", "\"로저먼드,\" 그가 다시 입을 열었다."], ["he began again, trying to be simply grave and not bitter, “that nothing can be so fatal as a want of openness and confidence between us?", "신랄해지지 않고 그저 진지해지려 애쓰며. \"우리 사이에 솔직함과 신뢰가 없는 것만큼 치명적인 일은 없다는 걸 모르겠소?"], ["It has happened again and again that I have expressed a decided wish, and you have seemed to assent, yet after that you have secretly disobeyed my wish.", "나는 거듭거듭 분명한 바람을 표했고, 당신은 동의하는 듯 보였는데, 그러고 나서 당신은 몰래 내 바람을 어겼소."], ["In that way I can never know what I have to trust to.", "그런 식이라면 나는 무엇에 의지해야 할지 결코 알 수 없소."], ["There would be some hope for us if you would admit this.", "당신이 이것을 인정해 준다면 우리에게도 얼마쯤 희망이 있을 거요."], ["Am I such an unreasonable, furious brute?", "내가 그렇게도 이치에 어긋나고 사납기만 한 짐승이오?"], ["Why should you not be open with me?”", "어째서 나에게 솔직하지 못한 거요?\""], ["Still silence.", "그래도 침묵뿐이었다."]], [["“Will you only say that you have been mistaken, and that I may depend on your not acting secretly in future?”", "\"당신이 잘못 생각했다고, 그리고 앞으로는 몰래 행동하지 않으리라고 내가 믿어도 된다고, 그 말만이라도 해 주겠소?\""], ["said Lydgate, urgently, but with something of request in his tone which Rosamond was quick to perceive.", "리드게이트가 다급하게 말했지만, 그 어조에는 로저먼드가 재빨리 알아챈 간청 같은 것이 담겨 있었다."], ["She spoke with coolness.", "그녀는 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“I cannot possibly make admissions or promises in answer to such words as you have used towards me.", "\"당신이 나에게 쓴 그런 말들에 답하여 인정이나 약속을 할 수는 도저히 없어요."], ["I have not been accustomed to language of that kind.", "나는 그런 종류의 말에 익숙하지 않아요."], ["You have spoken of my ‘secret meddling,’ and my ‘interfering ignorance,’ and my ‘false assent.’ I have never expressed myself in that way to you, and I think that you ought to apologize.", "당신은 내 '몰래 참견함'이니, '무지한 끼어듦'이니, '거짓 동의'니 하는 말을 하셨죠. 나는 당신에게 한 번도 그런 식으로 말한 적이 없으니, 당신이 사과해야 한다고 생각해요."], ["You spoke of its being impossible to live with me.", "당신은 나와 함께 사는 게 불가능하다고 했지요."], ["Certainly you have not made my life pleasant to me of late.", "분명 당신은 요즘 내 삶을 즐겁게 해 주지 못했어요."], ["I think it was to be expected that I should try to avert some of the hardships which our marriage has brought on me.”", "우리 결혼이 나에게 가져온 고난을 내가 얼마라도 막아 보려 한 것은 당연한 일이라고 생각해요.\""], ["Another tear fell as Rosamond ceased speaking, and she pressed it away as quietly as the first.", "로저먼드는 말을 마치며 또 한 방울 눈물을 흘렸고, 처음 것처럼 조용히 닦아 냈다."]], [["Lydgate flung himself into a chair, feeling checkmated.", "리드게이트는 외통수에 몰린 기분으로 의자에 몸을 던졌다."], ["What place was there in her mind for a remonstrance to lodge in?", "항변이 깃들 자리가 그녀의 마음 어디에 있단 말인가?"], ["He laid down his hat, flung an arm over the back of his chair, and looked down for some moments without speaking.", "그는 모자를 내려놓고 의자 등받이에 한 팔을 걸친 채, 한참 동안 아무 말 없이 아래를 내려다보았다."], ["Rosamond had the double purchase over him of insensibility to the point of justice in his reproach, and of sensibility to the undeniable hardships now present in her married life.", "로저먼드는 그를 상대로 이중의 우위를 쥐고 있었다. 그의 책망에 담긴 정당함의 핵심에는 무감각했고, 지금 결혼 생활에 닥친 부인할 수 없는 고난에는 예민했던 것이다."], ["Although her duplicity in the affair of the house had exceeded what he knew, and had really hindered the Plymdales from knowing of it, she had no consciousness that her action could rightly be called false.", "집 문제에서 그녀가 부린 표리부동함은 그가 아는 것을 넘어섰고, 실제로 플림데일 집안이 그 일을 알지 못하게 막기까지 했지만, 그녀는 자기 행동이 거짓이라 불릴 만하다는 의식이 전혀 없었다."], ["We are not obliged to identify our own acts according to a strict classification, any more than the materials of our grocery and clothes.", "우리는 우리의 식료품이나 옷감을 엄격히 분류하지 않아도 되는 것처럼, 자신의 행위를 엄밀한 분류에 따라 규정해야 할 의무도 없는 법이다."], ["Rosamond felt that she was aggrieved, and that this was what Lydgate had to recognize.", "로저먼드는 자신이 부당한 일을 당했다고 느꼈고, 바로 이것을 리드게이트가 인정해야 한다고 여겼다."]], [["As for him, the need of accommodating himself to her nature, which was inflexible in proportion to its negations, held him as with pincers.", "그로서는, 부정의 정도에 비례해 완고해지는 그녀의 본성에 자신을 맞춰야 한다는 필요가 집게로 죄듯 그를 옭아맸다."], ["He had begun to have an alarmed foresight of her irrevocable loss of love for him, and the consequent dreariness of their life.", "그는 그녀가 자기를 향한 사랑을 돌이킬 수 없이 잃어 가고, 그 결과 둘의 삶이 황량해지리라는 것을 불안하게 예감하기 시작했다."], ["The ready fulness of his emotions made this dread alternate quickly with the first violent movements of his anger.", "감정이 쉬이 차오르는 그의 성정 탓에, 이 두려움은 분노의 첫 격렬한 동요와 빠르게 번갈아 들었다."], ["It would assuredly have been a vain boast in him to say that he was her master.", "자기가 그녀의 주인이라고 말한다면, 그것은 분명 그에게 헛된 호언에 지나지 않았을 것이다."]], [["“You have not made my life pleasant to me of late”—“the hardships which our marriage has brought on me”—these words were stinging his imagination as a pain makes an exaggerated dream.", "\"당신은 요즘 내 삶을 즐겁게 해 주지 못했어요\"—\"우리 결혼이 나에게 가져온 고난\"—이 말들이, 고통이 꿈을 부풀리듯 그의 상상을 쏘아 댔다."], ["If he were not only to sink from his highest resolve, but to sink into the hideous fettering of domestic hate?", "만약 자신이 가장 드높은 결심에서 가라앉기만 하는 것이 아니라, 가정 내 증오라는 그 흉측한 족쇄 속으로 가라앉아 버린다면?"]], [["“Rosamond,”", "\"로저먼드,\""], ["he said, turning his eyes on her with a melancholy look, “you should allow for a man’s words when he is disappointed and provoked.", "그가 우울한 눈길을 그녀에게 돌리며 말했다. \"남자가 실망하고 화가 났을 때 하는 말은 좀 헤아려 줘야 하오."], ["You and I cannot have opposite interests.", "당신과 나는 서로 반대되는 이해관계를 가질 수가 없소."], ["I cannot part my happiness from yours.", "나는 내 행복을 당신의 행복과 떼어 놓을 수 없으니까."], ["If I am angry with you, it is that you seem not to see how any concealment divides us.", "내가 당신에게 화를 내는 건, 어떤 숨김이 우리를 갈라놓는지 당신이 보지 못하는 것 같아서요."], ["How could I wish to make anything hard to you either by my words or conduct?", "어떻게 내가 말이나 행동으로 당신에게 무언가를 힘들게 하고 싶겠소?"], ["When I hurt you, I hurt part of my own life.", "당신을 아프게 하면, 나는 내 삶의 일부를 아프게 하는 거요."], ["I should never be angry with you if you would be quite open with me.”", "당신이 나에게 아주 솔직하기만 하다면 나는 결코 당신에게 화내지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“I have only wished to prevent you from hurrying us into wretchedness without any necessity,”", "\"나는 그저 당신이 아무런 필요도 없이 우리를 비참함 속으로 몰아넣지 않게 막으려 했을 뿐이에요.\""], ["said Rosamond, the tears coming again from a softened feeling now that her husband had softened.", "로저먼드가 말했다. 남편이 누그러지자 마음이 풀려 다시 눈물이 솟았다."], ["“It is so very hard to be disgraced here among all the people we know, and to live in such a miserable way.", "\"우리가 아는 사람들 사이에서 여기서 망신을 당하고, 이렇게 비참하게 사는 것은 너무도 견디기 힘든 일이에요."], ["I wish I had died with the baby.”", "차라리 아기와 함께 죽었더라면 좋았을 거예요.\""]], [["She spoke and wept with that gentleness which makes such words and tears omnipotent over a loving-hearted man.", "그녀는 그렇게 부드러운 어조로 말하고 울었으니, 그런 말과 눈물은 사랑이 가득한 마음을 지닌 남자에게는 전능한 힘을 발휘하는 법이었다."], ["Lydgate drew his chair near to hers and pressed her delicate head against his cheek with his powerful tender hand.", "리드게이트는 의자를 그녀 가까이 끌어당겨, 강하면서도 다정한 손으로 그녀의 가냘픈 머리를 자기 뺨에 가져다 눌렀다."], ["He only caressed her; he did not say anything; for what was there to say?", "그는 그저 그녀를 어루만질 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 무슨 할 말이 있었겠는가?"], ["He could not promise to shield her from the dreaded wretchedness, for he could see no sure means of doing so.", "그는 그녀를 그 두려운 비참함으로부터 지켜 주겠다고 약속할 수 없었으니, 그렇게 할 확실한 방도가 보이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["When he left her to go out again, he told himself that it was ten times harder for her than for him: he had a life away from home, and constant appeals to his activity on behalf of others.", "다시 외출하려고 그녀를 떠날 때, 그는 이 일이 자기보다 그녀에게 열 배는 더 힘든 일이라고 스스로에게 말했다. 그에게는 집 밖의 삶이 있었고, 다른 이들을 위해 그의 활동을 끊임없이 부르는 일들이 있었으니까."], ["He wished to excuse everything in her if he could—but it was inevitable that in that excusing mood he should think of her as if she were an animal of another and feebler species.", "그는 할 수만 있다면 그녀의 모든 것을 너그러이 보아 주고 싶었다. 하지만 그렇게 너그러워지는 마음으로 그가 그녀를 마치 다른, 더 연약한 종에 속한 동물처럼 여기게 되는 것은 어쩔 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Nevertheless she had mastered him.", "그럼에도 그녀는 그를 굴복시켰다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXVI.", "# CHAPTER LXVI."]], [["’Tis one thing to be tempted, Escalus, Another thing to fall. —Measure for Measure.", "리드게이트가 자신의 진료가 개인적인 근심을 상쇄하는 데 도움을 준다고 곱씹은 데에는 분명 그럴 만한 이유가 있었다."], ["Lydgate certainly had good reason to reflect on the service his practice did him in counteracting his personal cares. He had no longer free energy enough for spontaneous research and speculative thinking, but by the bedside of patients, the direct external calls on his judgment and sympathies brought the added impulse needed to draw him out of himself.", "그에게는 더 이상 자발적인 연구나 사색적인 사유에 쏟을 자유로운 에너지가 남아 있지 않았으나, 환자의 머리맡에서 그의 판단과 동정심을 향해 직접 가해지는 외부의 요청은 그를 자기 자신으로부터 끌어내는 데 필요한 추가적인 자극이 되어 주었다."], ["It was not simply that beneficent harness of routine which enables silly men to live respectably and unhappy men to live calmly—it was a perpetual claim on the immediate fresh application of thought, and on the consideration of another’s need and trial.", "그것은 단순히 어리석은 사람들로 하여금 점잖게 살게 하고 불행한 사람들로 하여금 평온하게 살게 해 주는 그 자비로운 일상의 굴레만은 아니었다. 그것은 즉각적이고 신선한 사고의 적용을, 그리고 타인의 필요와 시련에 대한 배려를 끊임없이 요구하는 것이었다."], ["Many of us looking back through life would say that the kindest man we have ever known has been a medical man, or perhaps that surgeon whose fine tact, directed by deeply informed perception, has come to us in our need with a more sublime beneficence than that of miracle-workers.", "삶을 돌아보는 우리들 중 많은 이는 우리가 알아 온 가장 친절한 사람이 어느 의사였다고, 혹은 깊은 식견에서 우러난 통찰에 의해 인도된 그 섬세한 솜씨로 우리가 곤경에 처했을 때 기적을 행하는 자들의 그것보다 더 숭고한 자애로움을 가지고 우리에게 다가와 준 어느 외과 의사였다고 말할 것이다."], ["Some of that twice-blessed mercy was always with Lydgate in his work at the Hospital or in private houses, serving better than any opiate to quiet and sustain him under his anxieties and his sense of mental degeneracy.", "그 두 배로 축복받은 자비의 얼마쯤은 언제나 리드게이트가 병원에서나 개인 가정에서 일할 때 그와 함께하며, 어떤 아편제보다도 더 잘 그를 진정시키고 그의 불안과 정신적 퇴화의 감각 아래에서 그를 지탱해 주었다."]], [["Mr. Farebrother’s suspicion as to the opiate was true, however.", "그러나 아편제에 관한 페어브러더 씨의 의심은 사실이었다."], ["Under the first galling pressure of foreseen difficulties, and the first perception that his marriage, if it were not to be a yoked loneliness, must be a state of effort to go on loving without too much care about being loved, he had once or twice tried a dose of opium.", "예견된 어려움이 처음으로 그를 쓰라리게 짓누르고, 그의 결혼이 멍에 씌워진 외로움이 아니려면 사랑받는 것에 너무 마음 쓰지 않으면서 계속 사랑하려 애쓰는 상태가 되어야 한다는 것을 처음으로 깨달았을 때, 그는 한두 번 아편을 한 차례 복용해 본 적이 있었다."], ["But he had no hereditary constitutional craving after such transient escapes from the hauntings of misery.", "그러나 그에게는 비참함의 망령으로부터 그런 일시적인 도피를 갈망하는 유전적이고 체질적인 욕구는 없었다."], ["He was strong, could drink a great deal of wine, but did not care about it; and when the men round him were drinking spirits, he took sugar and water, having a contemptuous pity even for the earliest stages of excitement from drink.", "그는 건강했고, 포도주를 많이 마실 수 있었으나 그것에 마음을 두지 않았다. 주위 남자들이 독주를 마실 때 그는 설탕물을 들었으니, 술기운에 흥분하는 가장 초기 단계조차 경멸 어린 연민으로 바라보았던 것이다."], ["It was the same with gambling.", "도박에 대해서도 마찬가지였다."], ["He had looked on at a great deal of gambling in Paris, watching it as if it had been a disease.", "그는 파리에서 수많은 도박을 지켜본 적이 있었는데, 마치 그것이 하나의 질병이라도 되는 양 관찰했다."], ["He was no more tempted by such winning than he was by drink.", "그는 술에 끌리지 않은 것과 마찬가지로 그런 승리에도 끌리지 않았다."], ["He had said to himself that the only winning he cared for must be attained by a conscious process of high, difficult combination tending towards a beneficent result.", "그는 자신이 마음을 둘 수 있는 유일한 승리란, 자애로운 결과를 지향하는 높고도 어려운 조합의 의식적인 과정을 통해서만 얻어져야 한다고 스스로에게 말해 왔다."], ["The power he longed for could not be represented by agitated fingers clutching a heap of coin, or by the half-barbarous, half-idiotic triumph in the eyes of a man who sweeps within his arms the ventures of twenty chapfallen companions.", "그가 갈망한 권능은 한 무더기의 동전을 움켜쥐는 떨리는 손가락으로, 혹은 풀이 죽은 스무 명의 동료가 건 판돈을 두 팔로 쓸어 담는 한 남자의 눈에 어린 반쯤은 야만적이고 반쯤은 백치 같은 승리감으로는 결코 표현될 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["But just as he had tried opium, so his thought now began to turn upon gambling—not with appetite for its excitement, but with a sort of wistful inward gaze after that easy way of getting money, which implied no asking and brought no responsibility.", "그러나 그가 아편을 시도해 본 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그의 생각은 이제 도박 쪽으로 향하기 시작했다. 그 흥분에 대한 욕구 때문이 아니라, 어떤 청탁도 필요 없고 어떤 책임도 따르지 않는, 돈을 손쉽게 얻는 그 방법을 향한 일종의 갈망 어린 내면의 응시로 인한 것이었다."], ["If he had been in London or Paris at that time, it is probable that such thoughts, seconded by opportunity, would have taken him into a gambling-house, no longer to watch the gamblers, but to watch with them in kindred eagerness.", "만약 그가 그 무렵 런던이나 파리에 있었더라면, 기회까지 더해진 그런 생각들은 아마도 그를 도박장으로 이끌었을 것이다. 더 이상 도박꾼들을 구경하기 위해서가 아니라, 그들과 한 마음의 열망을 품고 함께 도박을 지켜보기 위해서였을 것이다."], ["Repugnance would have been surmounted by the immense need to win, if chance would be kind enough to let him.", "만약 운이 그에게 친절을 베풀어 주기만 한다면, 이기고 싶다는 엄청난 욕구가 혐오감을 압도해 버렸을 것이다."], ["An incident which happened not very long after that airy notion of getting aid from his uncle had been excluded, was a strong sign of the effect that might have followed any extant opportunity of gambling.", "삼촌에게서 도움을 받겠다는 그 허황된 생각이 배제된 지 그리 오래되지 않아 일어난 한 사건은, 도박할 어떤 기회라도 있었다면 뒤따랐을 법한 결과를 강력하게 보여 주는 징표였다."]], [["The billiard-room at the Green Dragon was the constant resort of a certain set, most of whom, like our acquaintance Mr. Bambridge, were regarded as men of pleasure.", "그린 드래건의 당구장은 어떤 무리가 늘 모여드는 곳이었는데, 그들 대부분은 우리가 아는 뱀브리지 씨처럼 한량으로 여겨지는 사람들이었다."], ["It was here that poor Fred Vincy had made part of his memorable debt, having lost money in betting, and been obliged to borrow of that gay companion.", "가엾은 프레드 빈시가 그 잊지 못할 빚의 일부를 진 곳도 바로 여기였으니, 내기에서 돈을 잃고 그 유쾌한 동료에게 빌리지 않을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["It was generally known in Middlemarch that a good deal of money was lost and won in this way; and the consequent repute of the Green Dragon as a place of dissipation naturally heightened in some quarters the temptation to go there.", "이런 식으로 많은 돈을 잃고 따고 한다는 것은 미들마치에 널리 알려진 사실이었고, 그 결과 그린 드래건이 방탕한 장소로 얻은 평판은 자연히 어떤 부류 사람들에게는 그곳에 가고 싶은 유혹을 더욱 부추겼다."], ["Probably its regular visitants, like the initiates of freemasonry, wished that there were something a little more tremendous to keep to themselves concerning it; but they were not a closed community, and many decent seniors as well as juniors occasionally turned into the billiard-room to see what was going on.", "아마도 그곳의 단골손님들은, 프리메이슨의 비밀 입회자들처럼, 자기들끼리만 간직할 그곳에 관한 무언가 좀 더 굉장한 것이 있었으면 하고 바랐을 것이다. 그러나 그들은 폐쇄된 집단이 아니었고, 젊은이들뿐 아니라 점잖은 연장자들도 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보려고 이따금 당구장에 들렀다."], ["Lydgate, who had the muscular aptitude for billiards, and was fond of the game, had once or twice in the early days after his arrival in Middlemarch taken his turn with the cue at the Green Dragon; but afterwards he had no leisure for the game, and no inclination for the socialities there.", "당구에 근육적 소질이 있고 그 게임을 좋아했던 리드게이트도 미들마치에 도착한 초기 무렵에는 한두 번 그린 드래건에서 큐를 잡고 차례를 가져 본 적이 있었다. 그러나 그 후로는 게임을 할 여가도 없었고 그곳의 사교에 대한 흥미도 없었다."], ["One evening, however, he had occasion to seek Mr. Bambridge at that resort.", "그런데 어느 날 저녁 그는 그 모임 장소에서 뱀브리지 씨를 찾아야 할 일이 생겼다."], ["The horsedealer had engaged to get him a customer for his remaining good horse, for which Lydgate had determined to substitute a cheap hack, hoping by this reduction of style to get perhaps twenty pounds; and he cared now for every small sum, as a help towards feeding the patience of his tradesmen.", "이 말 장수가 그에게 남은 좋은 말 한 필을 사 갈 손님을 구해 주기로 약속했던 것인데, 리드게이트는 그 말 대신 값싼 승용마로 바꾸기로 작정한 터였다. 이렇게 격을 낮춤으로써 어쩌면 이십 파운드쯤 얻기를 바랐던 것이다."], ["To run up to the billiard-room, as he was passing, would save time.", "그리고 그는 이제 거래처 상인들의 인내심을 먹여 살리는 데 보탬이 될 만한 푼돈 하나하나에까지 마음을 쓰고 있었다. 지나가는 길에 당구장으로 잠깐 올라가면 시간을 절약할 수 있을 터였다."]], [["Mr. Bambridge was not yet come, but would be sure to arrive by-and-by, said his friend Mr.", "뱀브리지 씨는 아직 오지 않았으나 곧 틀림없이 도착할 거라고 그의 친구 호록 씨가 말했다."], ["Horrock; and Lydgate stayed, playing a game for the sake of passing the time.", "그래서 리드게이트는 시간을 보낼 겸 한 게임을 하며 머물렀다."], ["That evening he had the peculiar light in the eyes and the unusual vivacity which had been once noticed in him by Mr. Farebrother.", "그날 저녁 그의 눈에는 페어브러더 씨가 언젠가 그에게서 알아챈 적이 있던 그 독특한 빛과 평소답지 않은 생기가 어려 있었다."], ["The exceptional fact of his presence was much noticed in the room, where there was a good deal of Middlemarch company; and several lookers-on, as well as some of the players, were betting with animation.", "그가 그곳에 있다는 이례적인 사실은, 미들마치 사람들이 꽤 많이 모인 그 방에서 큰 주목을 받았다. 그리고 몇몇 구경꾼들은 물론 게임을 하던 사람들 중 일부도 활기차게 내기를 걸고 있었다."], ["Lydgate was playing well, and felt confident; the bets were dropping round him, and with a swift glancing thought of the probable gain which might double the sum he was saving from his horse, he began to bet on his own play, and won again and again.", "리드게이트는 게임을 잘하고 있었고 자신감을 느꼈다. 그의 주위로 내기 돈이 떨어졌고, 말을 팔아 절약하는 금액을 두 배로 불려 줄지도 모를 예상 수익을 잽싸게 떠올리며, 그는 자기 게임에 내기를 걸기 시작했고 거듭 이겼다."], ["Mr. Bambridge had come in, but Lydgate did not notice him.", "뱀브리지 씨가 들어왔으나 리드게이트는 그를 알아채지 못했다."], ["He was not only excited with his play, but visions were gleaming on him of going the next day to Brassing, where there was gambling on a grander scale to be had, and where, by one powerful snatch at the devil’s bait, he might carry it off without the hook, and buy his rescue from his daily solicitings.", "그는 게임에 흥분해 있었을 뿐 아니라, 이튿날 더 큰 규모의 도박을 할 수 있는 브래싱으로 가는 환영이 눈앞에 어른거리고 있었다. 그곳에서라면 악마의 미끼를 한 번 강하게 낚아챔으로써 바늘은 피한 채 미끼만 가지고 달아나, 날마다 시달리는 빚 독촉으로부터의 구출을 사들일 수 있을지도 몰랐다."]], [["He was still winning when two new visitors entered.", "두 명의 새로운 방문객이 들어왔을 때에도 그는 여전히 이기고 있었다."], ["One of them was a young Hawley, just come from his law studies in town, and the other was Fred Vincy, who had spent several evenings of late at this old haunt of his.", "그중 한 사람은 시내에서 법학 공부를 막 마치고 돌아온 젊은 홀리였고, 다른 한 사람은 요즈음 자신의 옛 단골 장소에서 여러 저녁을 보내곤 했던 프레드 빈시였다."], ["Young Hawley, an accomplished billiard-player, brought a cool fresh hand to the cue.", "능숙한 당구 선수인 젊은 홀리는 침착하고 산뜻한 솜씨로 큐를 잡았다."], ["But Fred Vincy, startled at seeing Lydgate, and astonished to see him betting with an excited air, stood aside, and kept out of the circle round the table.", "그러나 프레드 빈시는 리드게이트를 보고 깜짝 놀랐고, 흥분한 기색으로 내기를 거는 그를 보고 어안이 벙벙해져, 한쪽으로 비켜서서 테이블 주위의 원 밖에 머물러 있었다."]], [["Fred had been rewarding resolution by a little laxity of late. He had been working heartily for six months at all outdoor occupations under Mr.", "프레드는 요즈음 결심에 대한 보상으로 약간의 방종을 즐기고 있던 참이었다."], ["Garth, and by dint of severe practice had nearly mastered the defects of his handwriting, this practice being, perhaps, a little the less severe that it was often carried on in the evening at Mr.", "그는 여섯 달 동안 가스 씨 밑에서 온갖 야외 작업에 진심으로 매달려 왔고, 혹독한 연습 끝에 자신의 형편없는 글씨체의 결함을 거의 극복한 터였다."], ["Garth’s under the eyes of Mary. But the last fortnight Mary had been staying at Lowick Parsonage with the ladies there, during Mr.", "다만 이 연습은 종종 가스 씨 댁에서 저녁에 메리의 눈길 아래 이루어졌던 만큼 어쩌면 그다지 혹독하지는 않았을지도 모른다."], ["Farebrother’s residence in Middlemarch, where he was carrying out some parochial plans; and Fred, not seeing anything more agreeable to do, had turned into the Green Dragon, partly to play at billiards, partly to taste the old flavor of discourse about horses, sport, and things in general, considered from a point of view which was not strenuously correct.", "그러나 지난 두 주 동안 메리는, 페어브러더 씨가 교구 사업 몇 가지를 추진하느라 미들마치에 머무는 동안, 로윅 목사관에서 그곳 부인들과 함께 지내고 있었다. 그래서 프레드는 달리 더 마음에 드는 할 일을 찾지 못한 채 그린 드래건에 들렀던 것이니, 한편으로는 당구를 치기 위해서였고 한편으로는 말과 스포츠, 그리고 그다지 엄격하게 올바르지만은 않은 관점에서 바라본 이런저런 것들에 관한 담소의 옛 맛을 보기 위해서였다."], ["He had not been out hunting once this season, had had no horse of his own to ride, and had gone from place to place chiefly with Mr. Garth in his gig, or on the sober cob which Mr.", "그는 이번 시즌에 사냥을 한 번도 나가지 못했고, 탈 자기 말도 없었으며, 주로 가스 씨와 함께 그의 이륜마차를 타거나 가스 씨가 빌려줄 수 있는 그 점잖은 조랑말을 타고 이곳저곳을 다녔다."], ["Garth could lend him. It was a little too bad, Fred began to think, that he should be kept in the traces with more severity than if he had been a clergyman.", "프레드는 자신이 성직자였을 경우보다 더 엄격하게 고삐에 매여 지내는 것은 좀 너무하다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["“I will tell you what, Mistress Mary—it will be rather harder work to learn surveying and drawing plans than it would have been to write sermons,”", "\"이거 봐요, 메리 아가씨, 측량을 배우고 도면을 그리는 일은 설교를 쓰는 것보다 오히려 더 힘든 일일 거예요.\""], ["he had said, wishing her to appreciate what he went through for her sake; “and as to Hercules and Theseus, they were nothing to me.", "그는 그녀를 위해 자신이 어떤 고생을 겪고 있는지 그녀가 알아주기를 바라며 그렇게 말했었다. \"그리고 헤라클레스니 테세우스니 하는 자들은 나에 비하면 아무것도 아니죠."], ["They had sport, and never learned to write a bookkeeping hand.”", "그들은 운동이나 했지, 부기용 글씨체를 쓰는 법은 한 번도 배우지 않았으니까요.\""], ["And now, Mary being out of the way for a little while, Fred, like any other strong dog who cannot slip his collar, had pulled up the staple of his chain and made a small escape, not of course meaning to go fast or far.", "그리고 이제 메리가 잠시 자리를 비우자, 프레드는 목걸이를 벗어 던질 수 없는 다른 어떤 힘센 개와 마찬가지로 사슬의 말뚝을 뽑아내어 작은 탈출을 감행했던 것이니, 물론 빨리 혹은 멀리 갈 생각은 없었다."], ["There could be no reason why he should not play at billiards, but he was determined not to bet.", "그가 당구를 치지 못할 이유는 없었으나, 그는 내기는 하지 않기로 굳게 마음먹고 있었다."], ["As to money just now, Fred had in his mind the heroic project of saving almost all of the eighty pounds that Mr. Garth offered him, and returning it, which he could easily do by giving up all futile money-spending, since he had a superfluous stock of clothes, and no expense in his board.", "당장의 돈 문제로 말하자면, 프레드는 가스 씨가 그에게 준 팔십 파운드 가운데 거의 전부를 저축해 돌려주겠다는 영웅적인 계획을 마음에 품고 있었으니, 그는 옷이 남아돌 만큼 있었고 식비도 들지 않았으므로 쓸데없는 돈 낭비를 모두 그만두면 이 일을 쉽게 해낼 수 있었다."], ["In that way he could, in one year, go a good way towards repaying the ninety pounds of which he had deprived Mrs. Garth, unhappily at a time when she needed that sum more than she did now.", "그렇게 하면 그는 일 년 안에, 불행히도 가스 부인이 지금보다 그 돈을 더 절실히 필요로 하던 시기에 그녀에게서 빼앗았던 구십 파운드를 갚는 일에 상당히 진전을 볼 수 있을 터였다."], ["Nevertheless, it must be acknowledged that on this evening, which was the fifth of his recent visits to the billiard-room, Fred had, not in his pocket, but in his mind, the ten pounds which he meant to reserve for himself from his half-year’s salary (having before him the pleasure of carrying thirty to Mrs.", "그럼에도 인정해야 할 것은, 최근 당구장을 다섯 번째로 찾은 이날 저녁, 프레드의 주머니가 아니라 마음속에는, 반년치 봉급에서 자기 몫으로 떼어 두려 했던 십 파운드가 있었다는 점이다(메리가 다시 집에 돌아올 무렵 가스 부인에게 삼십 파운드를 가져다드리는 기쁨을 앞두고 있었던 것이다)."], ["Garth when Mary was likely to be come home again)—he had those ten pounds in his mind as a fund from which he might risk something, if there were a chance of a good bet.", "그는 좋은 내기의 기회가 생긴다면 무언가를 걸어 볼 만한 밑천으로서 그 십 파운드를 마음속에 품고 있었다."], ["Why?", "왜?"], ["Well, when sovereigns were flying about, why shouldn’t he catch a few?", "글쎄, 금화가 이리저리 날아다니는데 어찌 몇 닢쯤 잡아채지 않을 수 있겠는가? 그는 결코 다시는 그 길을 멀리 가지 않을 터였다."], ["He would never go far along that road again; but a man likes to assure himself, and men of pleasure generally, what he could do in the way of mischief if he chose, and that if he abstains from making himself ill, or beggaring himself, or talking with the utmost looseness which the narrow limits of human capacity will allow, it is not because he is a spooney.", "그러나 사람이란, 특히 한량들이란 대개, 마음만 먹으면 자기가 못된 짓을 어디까지 할 수 있는지를 스스로 확인하고 싶어 하는 법이며, 또 자기가 병이 들도록 마시거나, 거지가 되거나, 인간의 능력이 허용하는 좁은 한계 안에서 최대한 헤프게 떠벌리는 짓을 삼간다면 그것은 자기가 얼간이여서가 아니라는 것을 확인하고 싶어 하는 법이다. 프레드는 정식 논리를 따져 본 것은 아니었으니, 그런 논리란 옛 습관이 짜릿하게 되돌아오는 것이며 젊은 혈기의 변덕을 표현하기에는 매우 인위적이고 부정확한 방식이다."], ["Fred did not enter into formal reasons, which are a very artificial, inexact way of representing the tingling returns of old habit, and the caprices of young blood: but there was lurking in him a prophetic sense that evening, that when he began to play he should also begin to bet—that he should enjoy some punch-drinking, and in general prepare himself for feeling “rather seedy”", "그러나 그날 저녁 그의 안에는, 게임을 시작하면 내기도 시작하게 되리라는, 펀치를 좀 마시며 즐기고, 대체로 아침이면 \"다소 찌뿌둥한\" 기분을 느낄 채비를 하게 되리라는 예언적인 예감이 도사리고 있었다."], ["in the morning. It is in such indefinable movements that action often begins.", "행동이란 흔히 이처럼 규정하기 어려운 움직임 속에서 시작되는 법이다."]], [["But the last thing likely to have entered Fred’s expectation was that he should see his brother-in-law Lydgate—of whom he had never quite dropped the old opinion that he was a prig, and tremendously conscious of his superiority—looking excited and betting, just as he himself might have done.", "그러나 프레드의 예상에 가장 들어올 법하지 않은 일은, 자신의 매형 리드게이트가, 그가 잘난 체하는 인간이며 자신의 우월함을 지독히 의식하고 있다는 옛 견해를 결코 완전히 버린 적 없던 그 매형이, 마치 자기가 그랬을지도 모르는 것처럼 흥분한 채 내기를 거는 모습을 보게 되는 것이었다. 프레드는, 리드게이트가 빚을 졌고 그의 아버지가 그를 돕기를 거절했다는 막연한 사실만으로는 도저히 다 설명할 수 없는 큰 충격을 느꼈다."], ["Fred felt a shock greater than he could quite account for by the vague knowledge that Lydgate was in debt, and that his father had refused to help him; and his own inclination to enter into the play was suddenly checked.", "그리고 게임에 끼어들고 싶던 그 자신의 마음도 별안간 억제되었다. 그것은 묘하게 뒤바뀐 태도였다."], ["It was a strange reversal of attitudes: Fred’s blond face and blue eyes, usually bright and careless, ready to give attention to anything that held out a promise of amusement, looking involuntarily grave and almost embarrassed as if by the sight of something unfitting; while Lydgate, who had habitually an air of self-possessed strength, and a certain meditativeness that seemed to lie behind his most observant attention, was acting, watching, speaking with that excited narrow consciousness which reminds one of an animal with fierce eyes and retractile claws.", "평소에는 밝고 무심하며 무엇이든 재미를 약속하는 것에는 기꺼이 주의를 기울이던 프레드의 금발 얼굴과 푸른 눈은, 무언가 어울리지 않는 광경을 본 듯이 자기도 모르게 심각하고 거의 당황한 빛을 띠고 있었다. 반면 평소에는 침착하고 강인한 태도와, 가장 주의 깊은 관찰의 이면에 깔려 있는 듯한 어떤 사색적인 면모를 지녔던 리드게이트는, 사나운 눈빛과 오므렸다 폈다 하는 발톱을 지닌 짐승을 연상시키는 그 흥분되고 좁아진 의식으로 행동하고, 지켜보고, 말하고 있었다."]], [["Lydgate, by betting on his own strokes, had won sixteen pounds; but young Hawley’s arrival had changed the poise of things.", "리드게이트는 자기가 친 공에 내기를 걸어 십육 파운드를 땄으나, 젊은 홀리의 등장이 형세의 균형을 바꿔 놓았다."], ["He made first-rate strokes himself, and began to bet against Lydgate’s strokes, the strain of whose nerves was thus changed from simple confidence in his own movements to defying another person’s doubt in them.", "홀리 자신도 일류의 솜씨로 공을 쳤고, 리드게이트가 친 공에 반대로 내기를 걸기 시작했다. 그리하여 리드게이트의 신경에 가해지는 긴장은 자기 동작에 대한 단순한 자신감에서, 그 동작에 대한 타인의 의심에 맞서는 것으로 바뀌었다."], ["The defiance was more exciting than the confidence, but it was less sure.", "그 도전은 자신감보다 더 흥분되는 것이었으나 덜 확실했다."], ["He continued to bet on his own play, but began often to fail.", "그는 계속 자기 게임에 내기를 걸었으나, 자주 실패하기 시작했다."], ["Still he went on, for his mind was as utterly narrowed into that precipitous crevice of play as if he had been the most ignorant lounger there.", "그래도 그는 계속했으니, 그의 마음은 그가 그곳의 가장 무지한 빈둥거리는 자였더라도 그러했을 만큼 그 게임이라는 가파른 틈새로 완전히 좁아들어 있었기 때문이다."], ["Fred observed that Lydgate was losing fast, and found himself in the new situation of puzzling his brains to think of some device by which, without being offensive, he could withdraw Lydgate’s attention, and perhaps suggest to him a reason for quitting the room.", "프레드는 리드게이트가 빠르게 잃고 있는 것을 보고, 무례하게 굴지 않으면서 리드게이트의 주의를 돌려놓을, 그리고 어쩌면 그가 이 방을 떠날 만한 구실을 넌지시 일러 줄 어떤 방책을 짜내느라 머리를 쥐어짜는 새로운 처지에 놓이게 되었다."], ["He saw that others were observing Lydgate’s strange unlikeness to himself, and it occurred to him that merely to touch his elbow and call him aside for a moment might rouse him from his absorption.", "그는 다른 사람들도 리드게이트가 평소의 그답지 않게 이상하다는 것을 눈여겨보고 있음을 알아챘고, 그저 그의 팔꿈치를 건드려 잠시 한쪽으로 불러내면 그를 몰입 상태에서 깨어나게 할 수 있으리라는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그가 생각해 낼 수 있는 것이라곤, 로지를 보고 싶은데 오늘 저녁 그녀가 집에 있는지 알고 싶다고 말하는, 그 대담하고 그럴듯하지 않은 핑계뿐이었다."], ["He could think of nothing cleverer than the daring improbability of saying that he wanted to see Rosy, and wished to know if she were at home this evening; and he was going desperately to carry out this weak device, when a waiter came up to him with a message, saying that Mr. Farebrother was below, and begged to speak with him.", "그가 막 자포자기하는 심정으로 이 옹색한 방책을 실행에 옮기려는 참에, 한 종업원이 그에게 다가와 페어브러더 씨가 아래층에 와 계시며 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다는 전갈을 전했다."]], [["Fred was surprised, not quite comfortably, but sending word that he would be down immediately, he went with a new impulse up to Lydgate, said, “Can I speak to you a moment?”", "프레드는 그리 편치 않게 놀랐으나, 곧 내려가겠다는 말을 전하고 나서, 새로운 충동에 이끌려 리드게이트에게 다가가 \"잠깐 얘기 좀 할 수 있을까요?\""], ["and drew him aside.", "하고 말하며 그를 한쪽으로 끌어냈다."]], [["“Farebrother has just sent up a message to say that he wants to speak to me.", "\"페어브러더 씨가 방금 저에게 할 얘기가 있다는 전갈을 보냈어요. 아래층에 와 계시고요."], ["He is below. I thought you might like to know he was there, if you had anything to say to him.”", "그분에게 하실 말씀이 있으시면 그분이 와 계신 걸 알고 싶어 하실 것 같아서요.\""]], [["Fred had simply snatched up this pretext for speaking, because he could not say, “You are losing confoundedly, and are making everybody stare at you; you had better come away.”", "프레드는 그저 말을 꺼낼 핑계로 이것을 붙잡았던 것이니, \"형님이 지독하게 잃고 있고, 모두가 형님을 빤히 쳐다보게 만들고 있어요. 그만 자리를 뜨시는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["But inspiration could hardly have served him better.", "하고 말할 수는 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 영감인들 이보다 더 그를 도울 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["Lydgate had not before seen that Fred was present, and his sudden appearance with an announcement of Mr. Farebrother had the effect of a sharp concussion.", "리드게이트는 그때까지 프레드가 거기 있는 줄 몰랐던 터라, 페어브러더 씨를 알리는 그의 갑작스러운 출현은 날카로운 충격 같은 효과를 냈다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야.\""], ["said Lydgate; “I have nothing particular to say to him.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"그분에게 특별히 할 얘기는 없어."], ["But—the game is up—I must be going—I came in just to see Bambridge.”", "하지만, 게임은 끝났으니, 나는 가 봐야겠다. 뱀브리지를 좀 보려고 들렀던 거니까.\""]], [["“Bambridge is over there, but he is making a row—I don’t think he’s ready for business.", "\"뱀브리지는 저쪽에 있긴 한데, 소란을 피우고 있어서요. 거래할 정신은 아닌 것 같아요."], ["Come down with me to Farebrother.", "저랑 같이 내려가서 페어브러더 씨를 만나요."], ["I expect he is going to blow me up, and you will shield me,”", "그분이 저를 호되게 야단치실 것 같은데, 형님이 저를 좀 막아 주세요.\""], ["said Fred, with some adroitness.", "프레드가 약간의 재치를 부려 말했다."]], [["Lydgate felt shame, but could not bear to act as if he felt it, by refusing to see Mr.", "리드게이트는 부끄러움을 느꼈으나, 페어브러더 씨 만나기를 거절함으로써 그 부끄러움을 느끼는 듯 행동할 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["Farebrother; and he went down.", "그래서 그는 내려갔다."], ["They merely shook hands, however, and spoke of the frost; and when all three had turned into the street, the Vicar seemed quite willing to say good-by to Lydgate.", "그러나 그들은 그저 악수를 나누고 서리에 관해 이야기했을 뿐이다. 그리고 세 사람이 거리로 나서자, 목사는 기꺼이 리드게이트에게 작별을 고하고 싶어 하는 듯했다."], ["His present purpose was clearly to talk with Fred alone, and he said, kindly, “I disturbed you, young gentleman, because I have some pressing business with you.", "그의 지금 목적은 분명히 프레드와 단둘이 이야기하는 것이었으니, 그는 친절하게 말했다. \"젊은이, 자네를 방해해서 미안하네만, 자네에게 긴히 할 이야기가 있어서 말이야."], ["Walk with me to St. Botolph’s, will you?”", "세인트 보톨프 교회까지 나와 함께 걸어 주겠나?\""]], [["It was a fine night, the sky thick with stars, and Mr.", "화창한 밤이었고, 하늘에는 별이 빽빽했다."], ["Farebrother proposed that they should make a circuit to the old church by the London road.", "그래서 페어브러더 씨는 런던 가도를 따라 그 오래된 교회까지 빙 돌아가자고 제안했다."], ["The next thing he said was—", "그가 그다음에 한 말은 이러했다."]], [["“I thought Lydgate never went to the Green Dragon?”", "\"리드게이트는 그린 드래건에 절대 가지 않는 사람이라고 생각했는데?\""]], [["“So did I,” said Fred.", "\"저도 그렇게 생각했어요.\""], ["“But he said that he went to see Bambridge.”", "프레드가 말했다. \"그런데 뱀브리지를 만나러 갔다고 하더군요.\""]], [["“He was not playing, then?”", "\"그럼 게임을 하고 있지는 않았던 거고?\""]], [["Fred had not meant to tell this, but he was obliged now to say, “Yes, he was.", "프레드는 이 얘기를 할 생각이 없었으나, 이제는 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"네, 하고 있었어요."], ["But I suppose it was an accidental thing.", "하지만 우연한 일이었던 것 같아요."], ["I have never seen him there before.”", "전에는 거기서 그분을 본 적이 한 번도 없거든요.\""]], [["“You have been going often yourself, then, lately?”", "\"그럼 자네는 요즈음 자주 가곤 했다는 거로군?\""]], [["“Oh, about five or six times.”", "\"아, 다섯 번이나 여섯 번쯤이요.\""]], [["“I think you had some good reason for giving up the habit of going there?”", "\"내 생각에 자네는 거기 다니는 버릇을 끊을 만한 어떤 그럴 만한 이유가 있었던 것 같은데?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["You know all about it,”", "그건 다 아시잖아요.\""], ["said Fred, not liking to be catechised in this way.", "이런 식으로 추궁당하는 게 달갑지 않아 프레드가 말했다."], ["“I made a clean breast to you.”", "\"제가 다 솔직히 털어놓았으니까요.\""]], [["“I suppose that gives me a warrant to speak about the matter now.", "\"그러니 내가 지금 이 문제에 관해 이야기할 자격이 있는 셈이지. 우리 사이엔 그렇게 통하는 거 아닌가?"], ["It is understood between us, is it not?—that we are on a footing of open friendship: I have listened to you, and you will be willing to listen to me.", "우리가 터놓고 지내는 사이라는 거 말일세. 내가 자네 이야기를 들어 주었으니, 자네도 기꺼이 내 이야기를 들어 주겠지."], ["I may take my turn in talking a little about myself?”", "이번엔 내가 내 얘기를 좀 할 차례를 가져도 되겠나?\""]], [["“I am under the deepest obligation to you, Mr. Farebrother,”", "\"저는 목사님께 더없이 깊은 은혜를 입고 있는걸요, 페어브러더 씨.\""], ["said Fred, in a state of uncomfortable surmise.", "프레드가 불안한 추측에 사로잡힌 채 말했다."]], [["“I will not affect to deny that you are under some obligation to me.", "\"자네가 나에게 어느 정도 신세를 지고 있다는 걸 부인하는 척하지는 않겠네. 하지만 프레드, 자네에게 고백하건대, 방금 전에 나는 자네에게 입을 다묾으로써 그 모든 것을 뒤집어 버리고 싶은 유혹을 받았다네."], ["But I am going to confess to you, Fred, that I have been tempted to reverse all that by keeping silence with you just now.", "누군가가 나에게 '빈시 가의 젊은이가 또 매일 밤 당구대에 붙어살기 시작했어요. 그 친구 고삐를 오래 견디지 못할 겁니다.'"], ["When somebody said to me, ‘Young Vincy has taken to being at the billiard-table every night again—he won’t bear the curb long;’ I was tempted to do the opposite of what I am doing—to hold my tongue and wait while you went down the ladder again, betting first and then—”", "하고 말했을 때, 나는 지금 내가 하고 있는 것과 정반대의 일을 하고 싶은 유혹을 받았네. 입을 다물고, 자네가 다시 그 사다리를 내려가는 동안, 처음엔 내기를 걸고 그다음엔—\""]], [["“I have not made any bets,”", "\"저는 내기를 하나도 하지 않았어요.\""], ["said Fred, hastily.", "프레드가 황급히 말했다."]], [["“Glad to hear it.", "\"그 말을 들으니 기쁘군."], ["But I say, my prompting was to look on and see you take the wrong turning, wear out Garth’s patience, and lose the best opportunity of your life—the opportunity which you made some rather difficult effort to secure.", "하지만 내 말은, 나의 충동은 가만히 지켜보면서 자네가 잘못된 길로 접어들어, 가스의 인내심을 닳게 하고, 자네 인생 최고의 기회를—자네가 꽤나 힘든 노력을 들여 확보한 그 기회를—잃어버리는 것을 보고 싶다는 것이었어."], ["You can guess the feeling which raised that temptation in me—I am sure you know it.", "자네는 내 안에 그런 유혹을 일으킨 그 감정을 짐작할 수 있을 걸세. 자네도 분명히 알 거야."], ["I am sure you know that the satisfaction of your affections stands in the way of mine.”", "자네의 애정이 충족되는 것이 내 애정이 충족되는 길을 가로막고 있다는 것을 자네도 분명히 알 거라고 말일세.\""]], [["There was a pause. Mr.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Farebrother seemed to wait for a recognition of the fact; and the emotion perceptible in the tones of his fine voice gave solemnity to his words.", "페어브러더 씨는 그 사실을 인정하기를 기다리는 듯했고, 그 고운 목소리의 어조에 감지되는 감정은 그의 말에 엄숙함을 더해 주었다."], ["But no feeling could quell Fred’s alarm.", "그러나 어떤 감정도 프레드의 경악을 가라앉힐 수는 없었다."]], [["“I could not be expected to give her up,”", "\"제가 그녀를 포기하리라고 기대하실 수는 없잖아요.\""], ["he said, after a moment’s hesitation: it was not a case for any pretence of generosity.", "그는 잠시 머뭇거린 끝에 말했다. 이것은 너그러운 척할 만한 경우가 아니었다."]], [["“Clearly not, when her affection met yours.", "\"물론 그렇지, 그녀의 애정이 자네의 애정과 만났으니 말일세."], ["But relations of this sort, even when they are of long standing, are always liable to change.", "하지만 이런 종류의 관계는, 아무리 오래 이어진 것이라도, 언제든 변할 수 있는 법이야. 나는 자네가 그녀가 자네에게 느끼는 유대를 느슨하게 만드는 식으로 행동할 수도 있다는 것을 쉽게 상상할 수 있네."], ["I can easily conceive that you might act in a way to loosen the tie she feels towards you—it must be remembered that she is only conditionally bound to you—and that in that case, another man, who may flatter himself that he has a hold on her regard, might succeed in winning that firm place in her love as well as respect which you had let slip.", "그녀가 자네에게 단지 조건부로 매여 있을 뿐이라는 것을 잊어선 안 되지. 그리고 그런 경우에, 자기가 그녀의 호의에 대해 영향력을 지녔다고 우쭐댈 만한 또 다른 남자가, 자네가 놓쳐 버린 그 존경뿐 아니라 사랑 속의 확고한 자리까지 차지하는 데 성공할 수도 있다는 것을 말일세."], ["I can easily conceive such a result,”", "나는 그런 결과를 쉽게 상상할 수 있어.\""], ["repeated Mr. Farebrother, emphatically.", "페어브러더 씨가 힘주어 거듭 말했다."], ["“There is a companionship of ready sympathy, which might get the advantage even over the longest associations.”", "\"기꺼이 마음을 헤아려 주는 동반자 관계라는 것이 있어서, 그것은 아무리 오래된 관계라도 능가할 수 있는 법이지.\""], ["It seemed to Fred that if Mr. Farebrother had had a beak and talons instead of his very capable tongue, his mode of attack could hardly be more cruel.", "프레드에게는 만약 페어브러더 씨가 그 유능한 혀 대신 부리와 발톱을 지녔더라도 그 공격 방식이 이보다 더 잔인할 수는 없을 것 같았다."], ["He had a horrible conviction that behind all this hypothetic statement there was a knowledge of some actual change in Mary’s feeling.", "그는 이 모든 가정적인 진술의 이면에, 메리의 감정에 일어난 어떤 실제 변화에 대한 앎이 있다는 끔찍한 확신을 느꼈다."]], [["“Of course I know it might easily be all up with me,”", "\"물론 저는 저에 관한 모든 게 쉽게 끝장날 수 있다는 걸 알아요.\""], ["he said, in a troubled voice.", "그가 괴로운 목소리로 말했다."], ["“If she is beginning to compare—”", "\"만약 그녀가 비교하기 시작했다면—\""], ["He broke off, not liking to betray all he felt, and then said, by the help of a little bitterness, “But I thought you were friendly to me.”", "그는 자신이 느끼는 모든 것을 드러내고 싶지 않아 말을 끊었다가, 약간의 쓰라림에 기대어 말했다. \"하지만 저는 목사님이 저에게 우호적이신 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["“So I am; that is why we are here.", "\"그렇다네. 그래서 우리가 이렇게 함께 있는 거지."], ["But I have had a strong disposition to be otherwise.", "하지만 나는 그렇지 않은 쪽으로 강하게 기울었던 적이 있어. 나는 스스로에게 이렇게 말했네."], ["I have said to myself, ‘If there is a likelihood of that youngster doing himself harm, why should you interfere?", "'저 젊은이가 제 신세를 망칠 가능성이 있다면, 왜 네가 끼어드느냐? 너도 그 친구만큼 가치 있는 사람 아니냐."], ["Aren’t you worth as much as he is, and don’t your sixteen years over and above his, in which you have gone rather hungry, give you more right to satisfaction than he has?", "그리고 네가 그 친구보다 열여섯 해를 더 살았고, 그동안 꽤나 배를 곯아 가며 지냈으니, 그 세월이 그 친구보다 너에게 더 만족을 누릴 권리를 주는 것 아니냐. 그 친구가 파멸로 치달을 가능성이 있다면, 그냥 내버려 두어라."], ["If there’s a chance of his going to the dogs, let him—perhaps you could nohow hinder it—and do you take the benefit.’”", "어쩌면 네가 어떻게 해도 그것을 막을 수 없을지도 모른다. 그러니 너는 그 덕을 보아라.'\""]], [["There was a pause, in which Fred was seized by a most uncomfortable chill.", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그 사이 프레드는 더없이 불편한 한기에 사로잡혔다."], ["What was coming next?", "다음엔 무슨 말이 나올까?"], ["He dreaded to hear that something had been said to Mary—he felt as if he were listening to a threat rather than a warning.", "그는 메리에게 무슨 말이 전해졌다는 것을 듣게 될까 두려웠다. 그는 마치 경고가 아니라 위협을 듣고 있는 것 같은 기분이었다."], ["When the Vicar began again there was a change in his tone like the encouraging transition to a major key.", "목사가 다시 입을 열었을 때 그의 어조에는, 장조로 옮겨 가는 격려의 전환과 같은 변화가 있었다."]], [["“But I had once meant better than that, and I am come back to my old intention.", "\"하지만 나는 한때 그보다 더 나은 뜻을 품었었고, 이제 그 옛 의도로 돌아왔다네."], ["I thought that I could hardly secure myself in it better, Fred, than by telling you just what had gone on in me.", "프레드, 내 안에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 자네에게 그대로 말해 주는 것보다 그 의도를 더 단단히 지킬 방법은 거의 없으리라고 생각했어."], ["And now, do you understand me?", "그래, 이제 내 말을 알아듣겠나?"], ["I want you to make the happiness of her life and your own, and if there is any chance that a word of warning from me may turn aside any risk to the contrary—well, I have uttered it.”", "나는 자네가 그녀의 삶과 자네 자신의 삶을 행복하게 만들기를 바라네. 그리고 나의 경고 한마디가 그에 어긋날 어떤 위험을 비껴가게 할 가능성이 조금이라도 있다면, 그래, 나는 그 말을 한 셈이지.\""]], [["There was a drop in the Vicar’s voice when he spoke the last words.", "목사가 그 마지막 말을 할 때 그의 목소리는 한 단계 가라앉았다. 그는 말을 멈추었다."], ["He paused—they were standing on a patch of green where the road diverged towards St. Botolph’s, and he put out his hand, as if to imply that the conversation was closed.", "그들은 길이 세인트 보톨프 쪽으로 갈라지는 푸른 풀밭에 서 있었는데, 그는 마치 대화가 끝났음을 넌지시 알리려는 듯 손을 내밀었다."], ["Fred was moved quite newly.", "프레드는 아주 새로운 감동을 받았다."], ["Some one highly susceptible to the contemplation of a fine act has said, that it produces a sort of regenerating shudder through the frame, and makes one feel ready to begin a new life.", "훌륭한 행위를 바라보는 데 몹시 예민한 어떤 이가 말하기를, 그런 행위는 일종의 거듭나게 하는 전율을 온몸에 일으키며, 새 삶을 시작할 채비가 된 듯한 기분을 느끼게 한다고 했다."], ["A good degree of that effect was just then present in Fred Vincy.", "바로 그때 프레드 빈시에게도 그런 효과가 상당히 나타나고 있었다."]], [["“I will try to be worthy,”", "\"저는 합당한 사람이 되려고 애쓰겠어요.\""], ["he said, breaking off before he could say “of you as well as of her.”", "그는 \"그녀에게뿐 아니라 목사님께도\"라고 말하려다 말고 끊었다."], ["And meanwhile Mr. Farebrother had gathered the impulse to say something more.", "그러는 동안 페어브러더 씨는 무언가를 더 말하고 싶은 충동을 모으고 있었다."]], [["“You must not imagine that I believe there is at present any decline in her preference of you, Fred.", "\"내가 지금 그녀가 자네를 선호하는 마음이 식고 있다고 믿는다고 생각해선 안 되네, 프레드."], ["Set your heart at rest, that if you keep right, other things will keep right.”", "자네가 바르게 처신하면 다른 것들도 다 바르게 될 테니, 마음을 푹 놓게나.\""]], [["“I shall never forget what you have done,”", "\"목사님이 해 주신 일을 결코 잊지 않겠어요.\""], ["Fred answered.", "프레드가 대답했다."], ["“I can’t say anything that seems worth saying—only I will try that your goodness shall not be thrown away.”", "\"뭔가 할 만한 말이 떠오르지 않습니다. 다만 목사님의 선의가 헛되이 버려지지 않도록 애쓰겠어요.\""]], [["“That’s enough.", "\"그거면 충분하네."], ["Good-by, and God bless you.”", "잘 가게, 그리고 신의 가호가 있기를.\""]], [["In that way they parted.", "그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다."], ["But both of them walked about a long while before they went out of the starlight.", "그러나 두 사람 다 별빛 속에서 벗어나기 전에 오래도록 이리저리 거닐었다. 프레드의 골똘한 생각은 대체로 이런 말로 요약될 수 있을 것이다."], ["Much of Fred’s rumination might be summed up in the words, “It certainly would have been a fine thing for her to marry Farebrother—but if she loves me best and I am a good husband?”", "\"그녀가 페어브러더와 결혼하는 게 분명 멋진 일이었겠지. 하지만 그녀가 나를 가장 사랑하고 내가 좋은 남편이 된다면야?\""]], [["Perhaps Mr. Farebrother’s might be concentrated into a single shrug and one little speech.", "어쩌면 페어브러더 씨의 생각은 한 번의 어깨짓과 한마디 짧은 말로 응축될 수 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["“To think of the part one little woman can play in the life of a man, so that to renounce her may be a very good imitation of heroism, and to win her may be a discipline!”", "\"한 자그마한 여인이 한 남자의 삶에서 어떤 역할을 할 수 있는지를 생각하면! 그녀를 단념하는 것은 영웅적 행위의 매우 그럴듯한 흉내일 수 있고, 그녀를 얻는 것은 하나의 수양일 수 있으니!\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXVII.", "# 제63장"]], [["Now is there civil war within the soul: Resolve is thrust from off the sacred throne By clamorous Needs, and Pride the grand-vizier Makes humble compact, plays the supple part Of envoy and deft-tongued apologist For hungry rebels.", "이제 영혼 안에서 내전이 벌어진다. 결의는 신성한 옥좌에서 밀려나고"]], [["Happily Lydgate had ended by losing in the billiard-room, and brought away no encouragement to make a raid on luck. On the contrary, he felt unmixed disgust with himself the next day when he had to pay four or five pounds over and above his gains, and he carried about with him a most unpleasant vision of the figure he had made, not only rubbing elbows with the men at the Green Dragon but behaving just as they did. A philosopher fallen to betting is hardly distinguishable from a Philistine under the same circumstances: the difference will chiefly be found in his subsequent reflections, and Lydgate chewed a very disagreeable cud in that way. His reason told him how the affair might have been magnified into ruin by a slight change of scenery—if it had been a gambling-house that he had turned into, where chance could be clutched with both hands instead of being picked up with thumb and fore-finger. Nevertheless, though reason strangled the desire to gamble, there remained the feeling that, with an assurance of luck to the needful amount, he would have liked to gamble, rather than take the alternative which was beginning to urge itself as inevitable.", "아우성치는 욕구들에 의해, 그리고 대재상인 자존심은\n비굴한 협약을 맺으며, 굶주린 반역자들을 위해"]], [["That alternative was to apply to Mr. Bulstrode. Lydgate had so many times boasted both to himself and others that he was totally independent of Bulstrode, to whose plans he had lent himself solely because they enabled him to carry out his own ideas of professional work and public benefit—he had so constantly in their personal intercourse had his pride sustained by the sense that he was making a good social use of this predominating banker, whose opinions he thought contemptible and whose motives often seemed to him an absurd mixture of contradictory impressions—that he had been creating for himself strong ideal obstacles to the proffering of any considerable request to him on his own account.", "사절이자 능란한 혀를 놀리는 변호인의\n유연한 역할을 떠맡는다."]], [["Still, early in March his affairs were at that pass in which men begin to say that their oaths were delivered in ignorance, and to perceive that the act which they had called impossible to them is becoming manifestly possible.", "다행히 리드게이트는 당구장에서 결국 잃은 것으로 끝나, 운에 기대어 한판 벌이려는 부추김을 안고 돌아오지는 않았다. 오히려 이튿날 그는 딴 돈 외에 사오 파운드를 더 물어내야 했을 때 자신에 대한 순수한 혐오를 느꼈고, 자기가 그려 낸 꼴—그린 드래건의 사내들과 팔꿈치를 부비며 어울렸을 뿐 아니라 그들과 똑같이 처신한 모습—에 대한 가장 불쾌한 영상을 떨치지 못한 채 지니고 다녔다."], ["With Dover’s ugly security soon to be put in force, with the proceeds of his practice immediately absorbed in paying back debts, and with the chance, if the worst were known, of daily supplies being refused on credit, above all with the vision of Rosamond’s hopeless discontent continually haunting him, Lydgate had begun to see that he should inevitably bend himself to ask help from somebody or other.", "도박에 빠진 철학자는 같은 처지에 놓인 속물과 거의 구별되지 않는다. 그 차이는 주로 그 뒤에 이어지는 성찰에서 발견될 터인데, 리드게이트는 바로 그런 식으로 매우 떨떠름한 되새김질을 했다."], ["At first he had considered whether he should write to Mr.", "그의 이성은 무대 배경이 조금만 달랐더라면 그 일이 어떻게 파멸로 부풀려질 수 있었을지를 일러 주었다—만일 그가 들어선 곳이, 엄지와 검지로 운을 집어 올리는 대신 두 손으로 움켜쥘 수 있는 도박장이었더라면."], ["Vincy; but on questioning Rosamond he found that, as he had suspected, she had already applied twice to her father, the last time being since the disappointment from Sir Godwin; and papa had said that Lydgate must look out for himself.", "그럼에도 이성이 도박하려는 욕망을 목 졸랐으나, 필요한 만큼의 운이 보장되기만 한다면, 불가피하게 다가오기 시작한 그 대안을 택하느니 차라리 도박을 하고 싶었으리라는 느낌은 여전히 남아 있었다. 그 대안이란 불스트로드 씨에게 부탁하는 것이었다."], ["“Papa said he had come, with one bad year after another, to trade more and more on borrowed capital, and had had to give up many indulgences; he could not spare a single hundred from the charges of his family. He said, let Lydgate ask Bulstrode: they have always been hand and glove.”", "리드게이트는 자신이 불스트로드로부터 전적으로 독립해 있으며, 자신의 직업적 활동과 공공의 이익에 관한 자기 생각을 실현하게 해 주었기 때문에 오로지 그 이유로 그의 계획에 협력했을 뿐이라고 스스로에게도 남들에게도 숱하게 자랑해 왔고—두 사람의 개인적 교류에서도, 자기가 경멸할 만하다고 여기는 의견을 지녔으며 종종 모순된 인상이 우스꽝스럽게 뒤섞인 듯 보이는 동기를 가진 이 우세한 은행가를 사회적으로 유익하게 활용하고 있다는 의식으로 자존심을 떠받쳐 왔던지라—그리하여 자기 자신을 위해 그에게 상당한 청을 내미는 일에 대해 스스로 강력한 관념적 장벽을 쌓아 온 셈이었다."]], [["Indeed, Lydgate himself had come to the conclusion that if he must end by asking for a free loan, his relations with Bulstrode, more at least than with any other man, might take the shape of a claim which was not purely personal. Bulstrode had indirectly helped to cause the failure of his practice, and had also been highly gratified by getting a medical partner in his plans:—but who among us ever reduced himself to the sort of dependence in which Lydgate now stood, without trying to believe that he had claims which diminished the humiliation of asking? It was true that of late there had seemed to be a new languor of interest in Bulstrode about the Hospital; but his health had got worse, and showed signs of a deep-seated nervous affection. In other respects he did not appear to be changed: he had always been highly polite, but Lydgate had observed in him from the first a marked coldness about his marriage and other private circumstances, a coldness which he had hitherto preferred to any warmth of familiarity between them. He deferred the intention from day to day, his habit of acting on his conclusions being made infirm by his repugnance to every possible conclusion and its consequent act. He saw Mr. Bulstrode often, but he did not try to use any occasion for his private purpose. At one moment he thought, “I will write a letter: I prefer that to any circuitous talk;” at another he thought, “No; if I were talking to him, I could make a retreat before any signs of disinclination.”", "그래도 3월 초에 그의 형편은, 사람들이 자신의 맹세는 무지한 상태에서 내뱉은 것이라고 말하기 시작하고, 자신에게는 불가능하다고 일컬었던 행위가 명백히 가능해지고 있음을 깨닫게 되는 그런 막바지에 이르러 있었다. 도버의 흉한 담보가 곧 집행될 참이고, 개업 수입은 빚을 갚느라 곧장 빨려 들어가고, 최악의 사정이 알려진다면 일용품을 외상으로 받지 못할 가능성이 있고, 무엇보다 로저먼드의 가망 없는 불만에 찬 모습이 끊임없이 그를 따라다니는 가운데, 리드게이트는 자신이 불가피하게 누군가에게든 도움을 청하는 쪽으로 굽힐 수밖에 없으리라는 것을 알기 시작했다. 처음에 그는 빈시 씨에게 편지를 써야 할지 고민했다. 하지만 로저먼드에게 물어보니, 그가 짐작했던 대로 그녀는 이미 두 차례나 아버지에게 부탁했었고, 마지막은 고드윈 경으로부터 실망스러운 소식을 들은 뒤였으며, 아버지는 리드게이트가 알아서 해야 한다고 말했다는 것이었다. \"아버지 말씀이, 흉년이 해마다 이어지는 통에 점점 더 빌린 자본으로 장사를 하게 되었고, 여러 가지 즐거움을 포기해야 했다고 하셨어요. 가족을 부양하는 비용에서 백 파운드 한 장도 떼어 줄 수 없다고요. 리드게이트더러 불스트로드에게 부탁하라고 하셨어요. 둘은 늘 죽이 잘 맞는 사이였으니까.\" 사실 리드게이트 자신도, 만일 결국 무담보 대출을 청해야 한다면, 적어도 다른 누구와의 관계보다는 불스트로드와의 관계가 순전히 개인적인 것만은 아닌 청구의 형태를 띨 수 있으리라는 결론에 이르러 있었다. 불스트로드는 간접적으로 그의 개업이 실패하는 데 원인을 보탰고, 또한 자기 계획에 의료 동업자를 얻은 것에 크게 만족했었다. 그러나 우리 가운데 누가, 지금 리드게이트가 처한 그런 종류의 의존 상태로 자신을 떨어뜨리면서도, 부탁의 굴욕을 덜어 주는 모종의 청구권을 자기가 지녔다고 믿으려 애쓰지 않았겠는가? 최근 들어 불스트로드가 병원에 대한 관심에서 새로운 무기력을 보이는 듯했던 것은 사실이었다. 하지만 그의 건강은 더 나빠졌고, 뿌리 깊은 신경성 질환의 징후를 드러내고 있었다. 다른 면에서 그는 달라진 듯 보이지 않았다. 그는 늘 대단히 정중했으나, 리드게이트는 처음부터 그에게서 자기 결혼이나 다른 사적인 사정에 관한 두드러진 냉담함을 눈여겨보았는데, 그 냉담함을 그는 지금껏 두 사람 사이의 어떤 친근한 온기보다 더 선호해 왔다. 그는 그 의도를 하루하루 미루었는데, 가능한 온갖 결론과 그에 따르는 행동에 대한 거부감 탓에 결론에 따라 행동하던 그의 습관이 흔들렸기 때문이다. 그는 불스트로드 씨를 자주 만났지만, 어떤 기회도 자신의 사적인 목적에 이용하려 들지 않았다. 어느 순간에는 \"편지를 써야지. 빙빙 둘러 가는 말보다는 그게 낫겠어\"라고 생각했고, 또 어느 순간에는 \"아니, 직접 이야기를 한다면 내키지 않아 하는 기색이 보이는 즉시 물러설 수 있을 거야\"라고 생각했다."]], [["Still the days passed and no letter was written, no special interview sought.", "그래도 날들은 흘러갔고 편지는 쓰이지 않았으며, 특별한 면담도 청해지지 않았다."], ["In his shrinking from the humiliation of a dependent attitude towards Bulstrode, he began to familiarize his imagination with another step even more unlike his remembered self.", "불스트로드에게 의존적인 태도를 취해야 하는 굴욕에서 움츠러들면서, 그는 자신이 기억하는 자기 모습과는 더더욱 동떨어진 또 다른 조치에 상상을 길들이기 시작했다."], ["He began spontaneously to consider whether it would be possible to carry out that puerile notion of Rosamond’s which had often made him angry, namely, that they should quit Middlemarch without seeing anything beyond that preface.", "그는 종종 자신을 화나게 했던 로저먼드의 그 유치한 발상, 곧 미들마치를 그 서막 너머의 아무것도 보지 못한 채 떠나 버리자는 생각을 실행에 옮길 수 있을지 자발적으로 헤아려 보기 시작했다."], ["The question came—“Would any man buy the practice of me even now, for as little as it is worth?", "이런 의문이 떠올랐다—\"지금이라도, 이 헐값에라도 누가 내게서 개업권을 사 줄까?"], ["Then the sale might happen as a necessary preparation for going away.”", "그렇다면 매각은 떠나기 위한 필수적인 준비로서 이루어질 수도 있겠지.\""]], [["But against his taking this step, which he still felt to be a contemptible relinquishment of present work, a guilty turning aside from what was a real and might be a widening channel for worthy activity, to start again without any justified destination, there was this obstacle, that the purchaser, if procurable at all, might not be quickly forthcoming.", "하지만 이런 조치를 취하는 것에 대해—그는 여전히 그것을 현재의 일을 천하게 내팽개치는 짓이요, 가치 있는 활동을 위한 진짜 통로이자 넓어질 수도 있는 물길에서 죄스럽게 비켜서는 짓이며, 정당한 목적지도 없이 다시 시작하는 짓이라고 느꼈다—이런 장애가 있었으니, 곧 매수자를 구할 수 있다 해도 빨리 나타나지 않을지도 모른다는 것이었다."], ["And afterwards?", "그리고 그다음에는?"], ["Rosamond in a poor lodging, though in the largest city or most distant town, would not find the life that could save her from gloom, and save him from the reproach of having plunged her into it.", "가장 큰 도시든 가장 먼 읍이든, 로저먼드가 초라한 셋방에서 산다면 그녀를 음울함에서 구해 주고 그를 그녀를 그 속에 빠뜨렸다는 비난에서 구해 줄 만한 삶을 찾지 못할 것이었다."], ["For when a man is at the foot of the hill in his fortunes, he may stay a long while there in spite of professional accomplishment.", "사람이 운수의 비탈 밑바닥에 이르면, 직업적 성취가 있더라도 거기에 오래 머물러 있을 수 있기 때문이다."], ["In the British climate there is no incompatibility between scientific insight and furnished lodgings: the incompatibility is chiefly between scientific ambition and a wife who objects to that kind of residence.", "영국의 풍토에서는 과학적 통찰과 셋방살이 사이에 양립 불가능이란 없다. 양립 불가능은 주로 과학적 야망과, 그런 종류의 거처에 반대하는 아내 사이에 있는 법이다."]], [["But in the midst of his hesitation, opportunity came to decide him.", "그러나 그가 망설이는 와중에, 그를 결단하게 만들 기회가 찾아왔다."], ["A note from Mr. Bulstrode requested Lydgate to call on him at the Bank.", "불스트로드 씨가 보낸 쪽지가 리드게이트에게 은행으로 자기를 찾아와 달라고 청했다."], ["A hypochondriacal tendency had shown itself in the banker’s constitution of late; and a lack of sleep, which was really only a slight exaggeration of an habitual dyspeptic symptom, had been dwelt on by him as a sign of threatening insanity.", "근래 그 은행가의 체질에 건강염려증 경향이 나타나고 있었다. 실은 습관적인 소화불량 증상이 조금 과장된 데 지나지 않는 불면을, 그는 광기가 닥쳐온다는 징조라고 곱씹고 있었다."], ["He wanted to consult Lydgate without delay on that particular morning, although he had nothing to tell beyond what he had told before.", "그는 전에 했던 이야기 외에 달리 할 말이 없으면서도, 바로 그날 아침에 지체 없이 리드게이트와 상의하고 싶어 했다."], ["He listened eagerly to what Lydgate had to say in dissipation of his fears, though this too was only repetition; and this moment in which Bulstrode was receiving a medical opinion with a sense of comfort, seemed to make the communication of a personal need to him easier than it had been in Lydgate’s contemplation beforehand.", "그는 자신의 두려움을 떨쳐 내려는 리드게이트의 말에 열심히 귀를 기울였는데, 이 역시 되풀이에 지나지 않았다. 그리고 불스트로드가 안도감을 느끼며 의학적 소견을 받아들이고 있는 이 순간이, 리드게이트가 미리 헤아렸던 것보다 그에게 개인적 필요를 전하는 일을 더 쉽게 만들어 주는 듯했다."], ["He had been insisting that it would be well for Mr. Bulstrode to relax his attention to business.", "그는 불스트로드 씨가 사업에 쏟는 주의를 늦추는 편이 좋겠다고 역설하던 참이었다."]], [["“One sees how any mental strain, however slight, may affect a delicate frame,”", "\"아무리 가벼운 정신적 긴장도 섬약한 체질에 어떤 영향을 미칠 수 있는지는,\""], ["said Lydgate at that stage of the consultation when the remarks tend to pass from the personal to the general, “by the deep stamp which anxiety will make for a time even on the young and vigorous.", "진찰이, 개인적인 화제에서 일반적인 화제로 옮겨 가는 단계에 이르렀을 때 리드게이트가 말했다. \"젊고 정력적인 사람에게조차 불안이 한동안 깊은 자국을 새기는 것을 보면 알 수 있지요."], ["I am naturally very strong; yet I have been thoroughly shaken lately by an accumulation of trouble.”", "저는 천성이 매우 튼튼합니다. 그런데도 최근 누적된 곤경 탓에 완전히 흔들리고 말았어요.\""]], [["“I presume that a constitution in the susceptible state in which mine at present is, would be especially liable to fall a victim to cholera, if it visited our district.", "\"제 체질이 지금처럼 민감한 상태에 있다면, 만일 콜레라가 우리 지역을 찾아올 경우 특히나 그 희생자가 되기 쉬우리라 생각됩니다."], ["And since its appearance near London, we may well besiege the Mercy-seat for our protection,” said Mr.", "런던 부근에 그것이 나타난 마당이니, 우리는 마땅히 보호를 구하며 은혜의 보좌를 둘러싸고 매달려야겠지요.\""], ["Bulstrode, not intending to evade Lydgate’s allusion, but really preoccupied with alarms about himself.", "불스트로드 씨가 말했는데, 리드게이트의 암시를 회피하려는 것이 아니라 정말로 자신에 대한 불안에 사로잡혀 있었던 것이다."]], [["“You have at all events taken your share in using good practical precautions for the town, and that is the best mode of asking for protection,”", "\"어쨌든 선생께서는 이 읍을 위해 훌륭한 실질적 예방책을 강구하는 데 한몫하셨고, 그것이야말로 보호를 구하는 최선의 방법입니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, with a strong distaste for the broken metaphor and bad logic of the banker’s religion, somewhat increased by the apparent deafness of his sympathy.", "리드게이트가 말했는데, 그 은행가가 지닌 종교의 뒤죽박죽인 비유와 어설픈 논리에 대한 강한 거부감이, 동정을 호소하는 그의 명백한 귀먹음 탓에 다소 커졌다."], ["But his mind had taken up its long-prepared movement towards getting help, and was not yet arrested.", "하지만 그의 마음은 도움을 얻는 쪽으로 오래 준비해 온 움직임을 시작했고, 아직 멈추지 않은 상태였다."], ["He added, “The town has done well in the way of cleansing, and finding appliances; and I think that if the cholera should come, even our enemies will admit that the arrangements in the Hospital are a public good.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"이 읍은 청결과 설비 마련 면에서 잘해 왔고, 제 생각엔 만일 콜레라가 닥친다면 우리의 적들조차 병원의 시설이 공공의 이익이라는 점을 인정할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Truly,” said Mr.", "\"정말이지,\""], ["Bulstrode, with some coldness.", "불스트로드 씨가 다소 냉담하게 말했다."], ["“With regard to what you say, Mr. Lydgate, about the relaxation of my mental labor, I have for some time been entertaining a purpose to that effect—a purpose of a very decided character.", "\"제 정신적 노고를 늦추는 일에 관해 선생께서 말씀하시는 바에 대해 말하자면, 저는 얼마 전부터 그 방향으로 작정을 품고 있었습니다—매우 단호한 성격의 작정을요."], ["I contemplate at least a temporary withdrawal from the management of much business, whether benevolent or commercial.", "저는 자선적인 것이든 상업적인 것이든 많은 사업의 운영에서 적어도 한동안 물러날 생각을 하고 있습니다."], ["Also I think of changing my residence for a time: probably I shall close or let ‘The Shrubs,’ and take some place near the coast—under advice of course as to salubrity.", "또한 한동안 거처를 옮길 생각도 합니다. 아마도 '관목원'을 닫거나 세를 놓고, 해안 가까운 어딘가에 자리를 잡을 듯합니다—물론 건강에 좋은지에 관한 조언을 따라서요."], ["That would be a measure which you would recommend?”", "그것은 선생께서 권하실 만한 조치겠지요?\""]], [["“Oh yes,”", "\"아, 그럼요.\""], ["said Lydgate, falling backward in his chair, with ill-repressed impatience under the banker’s pale earnest eyes and intense preoccupation with himself.", "리드게이트가 의자에 등을 기대며 말했는데, 그 은행가의 창백하고 진지한 눈길과 자기 자신에 대한 강렬한 몰두 앞에서 제대로 억누르지 못한 초조함이 배어 있었다."]], [["“I have for some time felt that I should open this subject with you in relation to our Hospital,” continued Bulstrode.", "\"얼마 전부터 저는 우리 병원과 관련해 이 문제를 선생과 터놓고 이야기해야겠다고 느껴 왔습니다.\""], ["“Under the circumstances I have indicated, of course I must cease to have any personal share in the management, and it is contrary to my views of responsibility to continue a large application of means to an institution which I cannot watch over and to some extent regulate.", "불스트로드가 말을 이었다. \"제가 말씀드린 사정 아래에서는, 당연히 저는 운영에 개인적으로 관여하는 일을 그만두어야 하고, 제가 감독하고 어느 정도 통제할 수 없는 기관에 막대한 재원을 계속 쏟아붓는 것은 책임에 관한 제 견해에 어긋납니다."], ["I shall therefore, in case of my ultimate decision to leave Middlemarch, consider that I withdraw other support to the New Hospital than that which will subsist in the fact that I chiefly supplied the expenses of building it, and have contributed further large sums to its successful working.”", "그러므로 만일 제가 끝내 미들마치를 떠나기로 결정한다면, 새 병원에 대해, 제가 건축비를 주로 댔고 그 성공적인 운영을 위해 추가로 거액을 기부했다는 사실에 깃들어 있는 지원 외의 다른 지원은 거두어들이는 것으로 여기겠습니다.\""]], [["Lydgate’s thought, when Bulstrode paused according to his wont, was, “He has perhaps been losing a good deal of money.”", "불스트로드가 늘 하던 대로 말을 멈추자 리드게이트의 머릿속에 떠오른 생각은 이러했다. \"이 사람, 어쩌면 돈을 꽤 많이 잃은 모양이군.\""], ["This was the most plausible explanation of a speech which had caused rather a startling change in his expectations.", "이것이 그의 기대에 사뭇 놀라운 변화를 가져온 그 말에 대한 가장 그럴듯한 설명이었다."], ["He said in reply—", "그가 대답했다—"]], [["“The loss to the Hospital can hardly be made up, I fear.”", "\"병원이 입는 손실은 메우기 어려울 듯합니다.\""]], [["“Hardly,”", "\"어렵지요.\""], ["returned Bulstrode, in the same deliberate, silvery tone; “except by some changes of plan.", "불스트로드가 똑같이 신중하고 은쟁반 같은 어조로 대꾸했다. \"계획을 얼마간 바꾸지 않는 한 말입니다."], ["The only person who may be certainly counted on as willing to increase her contributions is Mrs.", "기꺼이 기부를 늘려 줄 것으로 확실히 기대할 수 있는 유일한 분은 캐소본 부인입니다."], ["Casaubon. I have had an interview with her on the subject, and I have pointed out to her, as I am about to do to you, that it will be desirable to win a more general support to the New Hospital by a change of system.”", "저는 그 문제로 그분과 면담을 가졌고, 지금 선생께 말씀드리려는 것처럼, 제도를 바꾸어 새 병원에 더 폭넓은 지원을 얻는 것이 바람직하다고 그분께 일러 드렸습니다.\""], ["Another pause, but Lydgate did not speak.", "또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀으나, 리드게이트는 말이 없었다."]], [["“The change I mean is an amalgamation with the Infirmary, so that the New Hospital shall be regarded as a special addition to the elder institution, having the same directing board.", "\"제가 말하는 변화란 진료소와의 통합인데, 그리하여 새 병원이 같은 운영 이사회를 둔, 더 오래된 기관에 덧붙은 특수 부속물로 여겨지도록 하는 것입니다."], ["It will be necessary, also, that the medical management of the two shall be combined.", "또한 두 곳의 의료 운영이 합쳐질 필요가 있겠지요."], ["In this way any difficulty as to the adequate maintenance of our new establishment will be removed; the benevolent interests of the town will cease to be divided.”", "이렇게 하면 우리 새 시설을 충분히 유지하는 데 따르는 어려움이 모두 해소되고, 이 읍의 자선적 관심사가 분열되는 일도 그치게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Bulstrode had lowered his eyes from Lydgate’s face to the buttons of his coat as he again paused.", "불스트로드 씨는 다시 말을 멈추며 리드게이트의 얼굴에서 그의 외투 단추로 시선을 떨구었다."]], [["“No doubt that is a good device as to ways and means,”", "\"방도와 재원에 관한 한 그것이 좋은 방책이라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, with an edge of irony in his tone.", "리드게이트가 어조에 빈정거림을 실어 말했다."], ["“But I can’t be expected to rejoice in it at once, since one of the first results will be that the other medical men will upset or interrupt my methods, if it were only because they are mine.”", "\"하지만 그 첫 결과 가운데 하나가, 다른 의사들이—그게 그저 제 방식이라는 이유만으로라도—제 방식을 뒤엎거나 방해하리라는 것일 텐데, 그걸 제가 대뜸 기뻐하리라고 기대하실 수는 없겠지요.\""]], [["“I myself, as you know, Mr. Lydgate, highly valued the opportunity of new and independent procedure which you have diligently employed: the original plan, I confess, was one which I had much at heart, under submission to the Divine Will.", "\"리드게이트 선생, 아시다시피 저 자신도 선생께서 부지런히 활용해 오신 새롭고 독자적인 진료의 기회를 매우 소중히 여겼습니다."], ["But since providential indications demand a renunciation from me, I renounce.”", "솔직히 말씀드려, 그 본래의 계획은 제가 신의 뜻에 순종하는 가운데 마음 깊이 품어 온 것이었지요. 그러나 섭리의 징조가 제게 단념을 요구하므로, 저는 단념하렵니다.\""]], [["Bulstrode showed a rather exasperating ability in this conversation.", "이 대화에서 불스트로드는 다소 사람 속을 끓게 하는 능란함을 보였다."], ["The broken metaphor and bad logic of motive which had stirred his hearer’s contempt were quite consistent with a mode of putting the facts which made it difficult for Lydgate to vent his own indignation and disappointment.", "듣는 이의 경멸을 불러일으켰던 그 뒤죽박죽인 비유와 동기의 어설픈 논리는, 리드게이트가 자신의 분노와 실망을 토로하기 어렵게 만드는 사실 제시 방식과 완벽하게 들어맞았다."], ["After some rapid reflection, he only asked—", "잠시 빠르게 생각을 굴린 뒤, 그는 그저 이렇게 물었다—"]], [["“What did Mrs. Casaubon say?”", "\"캐소본 부인은 뭐라고 하시던가요?\""]], [["“That was the further statement which I wished to make to you,”", "\"그것이 바로 제가 선생께 덧붙여 말씀드리고 싶었던 바입니다.\""], ["said Bulstrode, who had thoroughly prepared his ministerial explanation.", "불스트로드가 말했는데, 그는 자신의 설교조 해명을 빈틈없이 준비해 두고 있었다."], ["“She is, you are aware, a woman of most munificent disposition, and happily in possession—not I presume of great wealth, but of funds which she can well spare.", "\"그분은 선생도 아시다시피 더없이 후덕한 성품을 지닌 여인이며, 다행히도—대단한 부를 가졌다기보다는, 충분히 떼어 쓸 수 있는 자금을 보유하고 계십니다."], ["She has informed me that though she has destined the chief part of those funds to another purpose, she is willing to consider whether she cannot fully take my place in relation to the Hospital.", "그분께서 제게 알리시기를, 그 자금의 대부분을 다른 목적에 쓰기로 정해 두긴 했으나, 병원과 관련해 제 자리를 온전히 대신할 수 있을지 고려해 보겠노라고 하셨습니다."], ["But she wishes for ample time to mature her thoughts on the subject, and I have told her that there is no need for haste—that, in fact, my own plans are not yet absolute.”", "다만 그 문제에 관해 생각을 무르익힐 충분한 시간을 원하셔서, 저는 서두를 필요가 없다고—실은 제 계획도 아직 확정된 것은 아니라고 말씀드렸습니다.\""]], [["Lydgate was ready to say, “If Mrs. Casaubon would take your place, there would be gain, instead of loss.”", "리드게이트는 \"캐소본 부인이 선생 자리를 대신해 주신다면 손실이 아니라 이득이 되겠지요\"라고 말할 참이었다."], ["But there was still a weight on his mind which arrested this cheerful candor.", "하지만 이 명랑한 솔직함을 가로막는 무게가 여전히 그의 마음을 짓누르고 있었다. 그가 대답했다."], ["He replied, “I suppose, then, that I may enter into the subject with Mrs. Casaubon.”", "\"그럼 제가 캐소본 부인과 그 문제를 의논해도 되겠군요.\""]], [["“Precisely; that is what she expressly desires.", "\"바로 그렇습니다. 그것이 바로 그분이 분명히 바라시는 바예요."], ["Her decision, she says, will much depend on what you can tell her.", "그분 말씀으로는, 결정은 선생께서 무엇을 말씀해 주실 수 있느냐에 크게 달려 있다고 합니다."], ["But not at present: she is, I believe, just setting out on a journey.", "다만 지금 당장은 아니고요. 그분은 막 여행을 떠나시는 참인 듯합니다."], ["I have her letter here,” said Mr.", "여기 그분의 편지가 있습니다.\""], ["Bulstrode, drawing it out, and reading from it.", "불스트로드 씨가 편지를 꺼내 읽으며 말했다."], ["“‘I am immediately otherwise engaged,’ she says.", "\"'저는 당장 다른 일에 매여 있습니다'라고 쓰셨군요."], ["‘I am going into Yorkshire with Sir James and Lady Chettam; and the conclusions I come to about some land which I am to see there may affect my power of contributing to the Hospital.’ Thus, Mr.", "'저는 제임스 경, 그리고 체텀 부인과 함께 요크셔로 갈 예정인데, 거기서 보게 될 어떤 토지에 관해 제가 내리는 결론이 병원에 기부할 수 있는 제 능력에 영향을 미칠지도 모릅니다.'"], ["Lydgate, there is no haste necessary in this matter; but I wished to apprise you beforehand of what may possibly occur.”", "그러니 리드게이트 선생, 이 일에는 서두를 필요가 없습니다. 다만 일어날 수도 있는 일을 미리 알려 드리고 싶었던 것이지요.\""]], [["Mr. Bulstrode returned the letter to his side-pocket, and changed his attitude as if his business were closed.", "불스트로드 씨는 편지를 옆주머니에 도로 넣고는, 마치 용무가 끝났다는 듯 자세를 바꾸었다."], ["Lydgate, whose renewed hope about the Hospital only made him more conscious of the facts which poisoned his hope, felt that his effort after help, if made at all, must be made now and vigorously.", "병원에 관해 되살아난 희망이 도리어 그 희망에 독을 풀어 넣는 사실들을 더 의식하게 만든 리드게이트는, 도움을 얻으려는 노력을 한다면 그것은 지금, 그것도 힘차게 해야만 한다고 느꼈다."]], [["“I am much obliged to you for giving me full notice,”", "\"충분히 미리 알려 주셔서 대단히 감사합니다.\""], ["he said, with a firm intention in his tone, yet with an interruptedness in his delivery which showed that he spoke unwillingly.", "그가 단호한 의도를 어조에 담아, 그러나 마지못해 말하고 있음을 드러내는 끊긴 어투로 말했다."], ["“The highest object to me is my profession, and I had identified the Hospital with the best use I can at present make of my profession.", "\"제게 가장 높은 목표는 제 직업이고, 저는 병원을 현재 제 직업을 가장 잘 쓸 수 있는 방도와 동일시해 왔습니다."], ["But the best use is not always the same with monetary success.", "하지만 가장 잘 쓰는 것이 늘 금전적 성공과 같은 것은 아니지요."], ["Everything which has made the Hospital unpopular has helped with other causes—I think they are all connected with my professional zeal—to make me unpopular as a practitioner.", "병원을 인기 없게 만든 모든 것이—다른 원인들과 더불어, 제 생각엔 그것들이 다 제 직업적 열의와 연결되어 있는데—저를 인기 없는 개업의로 만드는 데 한몫했습니다."], ["I get chiefly patients who can’t pay me.", "저를 찾아오는 환자는 주로 진료비를 낼 수 없는 사람들입니다."], ["I should like them best, if I had nobody to pay on my own side.”", "제 쪽에서 갚아야 할 사람만 없다면, 저는 그런 환자들이 가장 좋을 텐데요.\""], ["Lydgate waited a little, but Bulstrode only bowed, looking at him fixedly, and he went on with the same interrupted enunciation—as if he were biting an objectional leek.", "리드게이트는 잠시 기다렸으나 불스트로드는 그를 뚫어지게 바라보며 고개만 까딱일 뿐이었고, 그는 마치 거슬리는 부추를 깨물고 있는 듯이 여전히 끊기는 발음으로 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have slipped into money difficulties which I can see no way out of, unless some one who trusts me and my future will advance me a sum without other security.", "\"저는 빠져나갈 길이 도무지 보이지 않는 금전적 곤경에 빠지고 말았는데, 저와 제 장래를 믿어 주는 누군가가 다른 담보 없이 일정한 액수를 선뜻 빌려주지 않는 한 말입니다."], ["I had very little fortune left when I came here.", "이곳에 왔을 때 제게 남은 재산은 거의 없었습니다."], ["I have no prospects of money from my own family.", "제 집안으로부터 돈을 받을 가망도 없고요."], ["My expenses, in consequence of my marriage, have been very much greater than I had expected.", "결혼 탓에 제 지출은 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 더 커졌습니다."], ["The result at this moment is that it would take a thousand pounds to clear me.", "지금 이 순간의 결과는, 저를 깨끗하게 정리하는 데 천 파운드가 들리라는 것입니다."], ["I mean, to free me from the risk of having all my goods sold in security of my largest debt—as well as to pay my other debts—and leave anything to keep us a little beforehand with our small income.", "다시 말해, 제 가장 큰 빚의 담보로 제 모든 가재도구가 팔려 나갈 위험에서 저를 벗어나게 하고—다른 빚들도 갚고—얼마 안 되는 수입으로 조금이나마 앞서 살아갈 무언가를 남기는 데 말입니다."], ["I find that it is out of the question that my wife’s father should make such an advance.", "제 아내의 아버지가 그런 거금을 빌려준다는 것은 어림없는 일임을 알았습니다."], ["That is why I mention my position to—to the only other man who may be held to have some personal connection with my prosperity or ruin.”", "그래서 제 처지를—제 번영이나 파멸에 어떤 개인적 연관이 있다고 여겨질 만한, 유일하게 남은 또 한 분께 말씀드리는 것입니다.\""]], [["Lydgate hated to hear himself.", "리드게이트는 자기 자신의 말을 듣는 것이 싫었다."], ["But he had spoken now, and had spoken with unmistakable directness.", "하지만 그는 이제 말을 해 버렸고, 틀림없이 단도직입적으로 말한 것이었다."], ["Mr. Bulstrode replied without haste, but also without hesitation.", "불스트로드 씨는 서두르지 않으면서도 망설임 없이 대답했다."]], [["“I am grieved, though, I confess, not surprised by this information, Mr. Lydgate.", "\"솔직히 말씀드려 놀라지는 않았으나, 이 소식에 마음이 아픕니다, 리드게이트 선생."], ["For my own part, I regretted your alliance with my brother-in-law’s family, which has always been of prodigal habits, and which has already been much indebted to me for sustainment in its present position.", "저 개인으로서는, 선생께서 제 처남 집안과 인연을 맺으신 것을 유감스럽게 여겨 왔습니다. 그 집안은 늘 헤픈 습관을 지녀 왔고, 지금의 지위를 유지하는 데에도 이미 제게 많은 신세를 졌지요."], ["My advice to you, Mr. Lydgate, would be, that instead of involving yourself in further obligations, and continuing a doubtful struggle, you should simply become a bankrupt.”", "리드게이트 선생, 제 조언은, 선생께서 더 많은 채무에 얽혀들며 미덥지 못한 분투를 계속하느니 그냥 파산하시는 편이 낫다는 것입니다.\""]], [["“That would not improve my prospect,”", "\"그건 제 전망을 나아지게 하지 못할 겁니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, rising and speaking bitterly, “even if it were a more agreeable thing in itself.”", "리드게이트가 일어서며 씁쓸하게 말했다. \"설령 그 자체로는 더 받아들일 만한 일이라 해도 말입니다.\""]], [["“It is always a trial,” said Mr.", "\"그것은 언제나 시련이지요.\""], ["Bulstrode; “but trial, my dear sir, is our portion here, and is a needed corrective.", "불스트로드 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 시련이란, 선생, 이 세상에서 우리에게 주어진 몫이며, 꼭 필요한 교정 수단입니다."], ["I recommend you to weigh the advice I have given.”", "제가 드린 조언을 잘 헤아려 보시기를 권합니다.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"감사합니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, not quite knowing what he said.", "리드게이트가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 말했다."], ["“I have occupied you too long.", "\"너무 오래 시간을 빼앗았군요."], ["Good-day.”", "안녕히 계십시오.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXVIII.", "# CHAPTER LXVIII."]], [["What suit of grace hath Virtue to put on If Vice shall wear as good, and do as well? If Wrong, if Craft, if Indiscretion Act as fair parts with ends as laudable? Which all this mighty volume of events The world, the universal map of deeds, Strongly controls, and proves from all descents, That the directest course still best succeeds. For should not grave and learn’d Experience That looks with the eyes of all the world beside, And with all ages holds intelligence, Go safer than Deceit without a guide! —DANIEL: Musophilus.", "악덕이 그만큼 좋은 옷을 걸치고 그만큼 잘 처신한다면? 그릇됨이, 간계가, 무분별이"]], [["That change of plan and shifting of interest which Bulstrode stated or betrayed in his conversation with Lydgate, had been determined in him by some severe experience which he had gone through since the epoch of Mr.", "똑같이 그럴듯한 역할을 칭송받을 만한 목적으로 연기한다면?"], ["Larcher’s sale, when Raffles had recognized Will Ladislaw, and when the banker had in vain attempted an act of restitution which might move Divine Providence to arrest painful consequences.", "이 모든 사건의 거대한 책,"]], [["His certainty that Raffles, unless he were dead, would return to Middlemarch before long, had been justified. On Christmas Eve he had reappeared at The Shrubs. Bulstrode was at home to receive him, and hinder his communication with the rest of the family, but he could not altogether hinder the circumstances of the visit from compromising himself and alarming his wife. Raffles proved more unmanageable than he had shown himself to be in his former appearances, his chronic state of mental restlessness, the growing effect of habitual intemperance, quickly shaking off every impression from what was said to him. He insisted on staying in the house, and Bulstrode, weighing two sets of evils, felt that this was at least not a worse alternative than his going into the town. He kept him in his own room for the evening and saw him to bed, Raffles all the while amusing himself with the annoyance he was causing this decent and highly prosperous fellow-sinner, an amusement which he facetiously expressed as sympathy with his friend’s pleasure in entertaining a man who had been serviceable to him, and who had not had all his earnings. There was a cunning calculation under this noisy joking—a cool resolve to extract something the handsomer from Bulstrode as payment for release from this new application of torture. But his cunning had a little overcast its mark.", "세계, 행위의 보편적 지도가\n이 모든 것을 강력히 다스리며, 모든 내력으로부터 증명하나니,"]], [["Bulstrode was indeed more tortured than the coarse fibre of Raffles could enable him to imagine. He had told his wife that he was simply taking care of this wretched creature, the victim of vice, who might otherwise injure himself; he implied, without the direct form of falsehood, that there was a family tie which bound him to this care, and that there were signs of mental alienation in Raffles which urged caution. He would himself drive the unfortunate being away the next morning. In these hints he felt that he was supplying Mrs. Bulstrode with precautionary information for his daughters and servants, and accounting for his allowing no one but himself to enter the room even with food and drink. But he sat in an agony of fear lest Raffles should be overheard in his loud and plain references to past facts—lest Mrs. Bulstrode should be even tempted to listen at the door. How could he hinder her, how betray his terror by opening the door to detect her? She was a woman of honest direct habits, and little likely to take so low a course in order to arrive at painful knowledge; but fear was stronger than the calculation of probabilities.", "가장 곧은 길이 언제나 가장 잘 성공한다는 것을. 세상의 모든 눈으로 바라보고,"]], [["In this way Raffles had pushed the torture too far, and produced an effect which had not been in his plan. By showing himself hopelessly unmanageable he had made Bulstrode feel that a strong defiance was the only resource left. After taking Raffles to bed that night the banker ordered his closed carriage to be ready at half-past seven the next morning. At six o’clock he had already been long dressed, and had spent some of his wretchedness in prayer, pleading his motives for averting the worst evil if in anything he had used falsity and spoken what was not true before God. For Bulstrode shrank from a direct lie with an intensity disproportionate to the number of his more indirect misdeeds. But many of these misdeeds were like the subtle muscular movements which are not taken account of in the consciousness, though they bring about the end that we fix our mind on and desire. And it is only what we are vividly conscious of that we can vividly imagine to be seen by Omniscience.", "모든 시대와 더불어 슬기를 나누는\n저 진중하고 학식 있는 경험이,"]], [["Bulstrode carried his candle to the bedside of Raffles, who was apparently in a painful dream. He stood silent, hoping that the presence of the light would serve to waken the sleeper gradually and gently, for he feared some noise as the consequence of a too sudden awakening.", "길잡이 없는 기만보다 더 안전히 나아가지 않을 수 있겠는가!"], ["He had watched for a couple of minutes or more the shudderings and pantings which seemed likely to end in waking, when Raffles, with a long half-stifled moan, started up and stared round him in terror, trembling and gasping. But he made no further noise, and Bulstrode, setting down the candle, awaited his recovery.", "—대니얼, 『무소필루스』."]], [["It was a quarter of an hour later before Bulstrode, with a cold peremptoriness of manner which he had not before shown, said, “I came to call you thus early, Mr.", "불스트로드가 리드게이트와 나눈 대화에서 드러냈거나 무심코 누설했던 그 계획의 변경과 관심의 이동은, 라처 씨의 경매가 있던 시기 이래로 그가 겪어 온 어떤 가혹한 경험에 의해 그의 마음속에서 결정된 것이었다."], ["Raffles, because I have ordered the carriage to be ready at half-past seven, and intend myself to conduct you as far as Ilsely, where you can either take the railway or await a coach.”", "그때 래플스가 윌 래디슬로를 알아보았고, 은행가는 고통스러운 결과를 막도록 신의 섭리를 움직일 수 있을지 모를 보상의 행위를 헛되이 시도했던 것이다. 죽지 않았다면 래플스가 머지않아 미들마치로 돌아오리라던 그의 확신은 사실로 입증되었다."], ["Raffles was about to speak, but Bulstrode anticipated him imperiously with the words, “Be silent, sir, and hear what I have to say.", "크리스마스이브에 그는 더 슈럽스에 다시 나타났다. 불스트로드는 그를 맞이하고 그가 나머지 가족과 접촉하는 것을 막으려고 집에 있었지만, 그 방문의 정황이 자신을 위태롭게 하고 아내를 놀라게 하는 것까지 완전히 막을 수는 없었다."], ["I shall supply you with money now, and I will furnish you with a reasonable sum from time to time, on your application to me by letter; but if you choose to present yourself here again, if you return to Middlemarch, if you use your tongue in a manner injurious to me, you will have to live on such fruits as your malice can bring you, without help from me.", "래플스는 이전에 나타났을 때보다 더 다루기 힘든 모습을 보였으니, 그의 만성적인 정신적 불안정과 습관적인 무절제가 점점 더 깊이 미친 영향 탓에, 그에게 무슨 말을 하든 그 인상이 금세 그에게서 떨어져 나갔다. 그는 집에 머물겠다고 고집했고, 불스트로드는 두 가지 해악을 저울질해 보고는 이것이 적어도 그가 시내로 들어가는 것보다 더 나쁜 대안은 아니라고 느꼈다."], ["Nobody will pay you well for blasting my name: I know the worst you can do against me, and I shall brave it if you dare to thrust yourself upon me again.", "그는 저녁 내내 래플스를 자기 방에 붙들어 두고 잠자리에 들 때까지 지켜보았는데, 그동안 래플스는 점잖고 대단히 번창한 동료 죄인에게 폐를 끼치는 것을 즐기면서, 그 즐거움을 자기에게 도움을 주었던, 그리고 마땅히 받을 몫을 다 받지 못했던 사람을 대접하는 친구의 기쁨에 대한 공감이라며 익살스럽게 표현했다."], ["Get up, sir, and do as I order you, without noise, or I will send for a policeman to take you off my premises, and you may carry your stories into every pothouse in the town, but you shall have no sixpence from me to pay your expenses there.”", "이 시끄러운 농담 아래에는 교활한 계산이 깔려 있었으니—이 새로운 고문에서 풀려나는 대가로 불스트로드에게서 무언가 더 두둑한 것을 뽑아내려는 냉정한 결심이었다. 그러나 그의 교활함은 그 표적을 조금 빗나가게 만들었다."]], [["Bulstrode had rarely in his life spoken with such nervous energy: he had been deliberating on this speech and its probable effects through a large part of the night; and though he did not trust to its ultimately saving him from any return of Raffles, he had concluded that it was the best throw he could make. It succeeded in enforcing submission from the jaded man this morning: his empoisoned system at this moment quailed before Bulstrode’s cold, resolute bearing, and he was taken off quietly in the carriage before the family breakfast time. The servants imagined him to be a poor relation, and were not surprised that a strict man like their master, who held his head high in the world, should be ashamed of such a cousin and want to get rid of him. The banker’s drive of ten miles with his hated companion was a dreary beginning of the Christmas day; but at the end of the drive, Raffles had recovered his spirits, and parted in a contentment for which there was the good reason that the banker had given him a hundred pounds. Various motives urged Bulstrode to this open-handedness, but he did not himself inquire closely into all of them. As he had stood watching Raffles in his uneasy sleep, it had certainly entered his mind that the man had been much shattered since the first gift of two hundred pounds.", "불스트로드는 사실 래플스의 거친 감성이 상상할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 고통을 받고 있었다. 그는 아내에게, 자신은 그저 이 가련한 자, 악덕의 희생자를 보살피고 있을 뿐이며, 그러지 않으면 그가 스스로를 해칠 수도 있다고 말했다. 그는 직접적인 거짓말의 형태를 취하지는 않으면서, 자신을 이 보살핌으로 묶어 두는 어떤 가족적 인연이 있으며, 래플스에게 조심을 요하는 정신 착란의 징후가 보인다는 것을 넌지시 암시했다. 다음 날 아침이면 자신이 직접 그 불운한 자를 멀리 데려다줄 것이라고 했다. 이런 암시 속에서 그는 불스트로드 부인에게 딸들과 하인들을 위한 예방적 정보를 주고 있으며, 음식과 음료를 가지고 가더라도 자신 외에는 아무도 그 방에 들어가지 못하게 하는 이유를 설명하고 있다고 느꼈다. 그러나 그는 래플스가 과거의 사실을 큰 소리로 노골적으로 언급하는 것을 누가 엿들을까 봐—불스트로드 부인이 행여 문 앞에서 엿듣고 싶은 유혹에 빠질까 봐 두려움에 사로잡혀 앉아 있었다. 어떻게 그녀를 막을 수 있단 말인가, 어떻게 그녀를 적발하려고 문을 열어 자신의 공포를 드러낼 수 있단 말인가? 그녀는 정직하고 솔직한 습관을 지닌 여인이었고, 고통스러운 사실을 알아내려고 그토록 비천한 방법을 취할 가능성은 거의 없었지만, 두려움은 개연성의 계산보다 더 강했다. 이런 식으로 래플스는 고문을 너무 멀리까지 밀어붙였고, 자기 계획에 없던 효과를 만들어 냈다. 자신이 도저히 다룰 수 없는 자임을 드러냄으로써, 그는 불스트로드로 하여금 강력한 저항만이 유일하게 남은 방책이라고 느끼게 만들었다. 그날 밤 래플스를 잠자리에 들게 한 뒤, 은행가는 다음 날 아침 일곱 시 반에 자신의 사륜마차를 준비해 두라고 지시했다. 여섯 시에 그는 이미 오래전에 옷을 다 차려입고, 비참함의 일부를 기도로 풀어내며, 신 앞에서 무엇인가에 거짓을 쓰고 사실이 아닌 것을 말했다면 그것은 최악의 해악을 막기 위함이었다고 자신의 동기를 호소했다. 불스트로드는 더 간접적인 비행의 수에 비하면 어울리지 않을 만큼 강렬하게 직접적인 거짓말 앞에서 움츠러들었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 비행들 가운데 많은 것은 의식 속에서는 헤아려지지 않으면서도 우리가 마음을 두고 바라는 목적을 이루어 내는, 저 미묘한 근육의 움직임과도 같았다. 그리고 우리가 전지자의 눈에 보인다고 생생히 상상할 수 있는 것은 오직 우리가 생생히 의식하는 것뿐이다."]], [["He had taken care to repeat the incisive statement of his resolve not to be played on any more; and had tried to penetrate Raffles with the fact that he had shown the risks of bribing him to be quite equal to the risks of defying him.", "불스트로드는 촛불을 들고 래플스의 침대 곁으로 갔는데, 그는 분명 괴로운 꿈을 꾸고 있는 듯했다."], ["But when, freed from his repulsive presence, Bulstrode returned to his quiet home, he brought with him no confidence that he had secured more than a respite.", "그는 너무 갑작스러운 깨어남의 결과로 어떤 소음이 일어날까 두려웠던 터라, 불빛의 존재가 잠든 자를 서서히 부드럽게 깨우는 데 도움이 되기를 바라며 말없이 서 있었다."], ["It was as if he had had a loathsome dream, and could not shake off its images with their hateful kindred of sensations—as if on all the pleasant surroundings of his life a dangerous reptile had left his slimy traces.", "그가 깨어남으로 끝날 듯한 그 떨림과 헐떡임을 이삼 분쯤 지켜보았을 때, 래플스가 반쯤 억눌린 긴 신음과 함께 벌떡 일어나 공포에 질려 주위를 둘러보며 떨고 헐떡였다."], ["Who can know how much of his most inward life is made up of the thoughts he believes other men to have about him, until that fabric of opinion is threatened with ruin?", "그러나 그는 더 이상 소리를 내지 않았고, 불스트로드는 촛불을 내려놓고 그가 진정되기를 기다렸다."]], [["Bulstrode was only the more conscious that there was a deposit of uneasy presentiment in his wife’s mind, because she carefully avoided any allusion to it. He had been used every day to taste the flavor of supremacy and the tribute of complete deference: and the certainty that he was watched or measured with a hidden suspicion of his having some discreditable secret, made his voice totter when he was speaking to edification. Foreseeing, to men of Bulstrode’s anxious temperament, is often worse than seeing; and his imagination continually heightened the anguish of an imminent disgrace. Yes, imminent; for if his defiance of Raffles did not keep the man away—and though he prayed for this result he hardly hoped for it—the disgrace was certain. In vain he said to himself that, if permitted, it would be a divine visitation, a chastisement, a preparation; he recoiled from the imagined burning; and he judged that it must be more for the Divine glory that he should escape dishonor. That recoil had at last urged him to make preparations for quitting Middlemarch. If evil truth must be reported of him, he would then be at a less scorching distance from the contempt of his old neighbors; and in a new scene, where his life would not have gathered the same wide sensibility, the tormentor, if he pursued him, would be less formidable. To leave the place finally would, he knew, be extremely painful to his wife, and on other grounds he would have preferred to stay where he had struck root. Hence he made his preparations at first in a conditional way, wishing to leave on all sides an opening for his return after brief absence, if any favorable intervention of Providence should dissipate his fears. He was preparing to transfer his management of the Bank, and to give up any active control of other commercial affairs in the neighborhood, on the ground of his failing health, but without excluding his future resumption of such work. The measure would cause him some added expense and some diminution of income beyond what he had already undergone from the general depression of trade; and the Hospital presented itself as a principal object of outlay on which he could fairly economize.", "십오 분쯤 지난 뒤에야 불스트로드는 전에는 보인 적 없는 차갑고 단호한 태도로 입을 열었다. \"이렇게 일찍 깨우러 온 것은, 래플스 씨, 마차를 일곱 시 반에 준비해 두라고 일러 두었기 때문이오. 내가 직접 일슬리까지 당신을 데려다줄 작정이오. 거기서 철도를 타든 역마차를 기다리든 하면 되오.\" 래플스가 막 말을 하려는데, 불스트로드가 위압적으로 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"조용히 하시오, 여보시오. 그리고 내가 할 말을 들으시오. 지금 돈을 주겠소. 그리고 당신이 편지로 내게 청한다면 때때로 적당한 액수를 보내 주겠소. 그러나 만약 당신이 다시 여기에 모습을 드러내겠다면, 만약 미들마치로 돌아온다면, 만약 나를 해치는 식으로 혀를 놀린다면, 당신은 나의 도움 없이 당신의 악의가 가져다줄 수 있는 그런 열매로 살아가야 할 것이오. 아무도 내 이름을 더럽힌 대가로 당신에게 두둑이 지불하지 않을 것이오. 당신이 나를 해코지해 할 수 있는 최악의 것을 나는 알고 있고, 당신이 감히 다시 내게 들이닥친다면 나는 그것에 맞설 것이오. 일어나시오, 여보시오. 그리고 소란 피우지 말고 내가 명하는 대로 하시오. 그러지 않으면 경관을 불러 당신을 내 집에서 끌어내게 하겠소. 그러면 당신은 시내의 모든 선술집에 당신 이야기를 떠들고 다녀도 좋지만, 그곳에서 쓸 경비로 나한테서는 단 한 푼도 받지 못할 것이오.\" 불스트로드가 평생에 이토록 신경을 곤두세운 격렬함으로 말한 적은 거의 없었다. 그는 밤의 상당 부분을 이 연설과 그 있을 법한 효과에 대해 곰곰이 따져 보았다. 그리고 그것이 끝내 래플스의 어떤 재방문으로부터도 자신을 구해 주리라고 믿지는 않았지만, 그것이 자신이 던질 수 있는 최선의 패라고 결론지었다. 그것은 오늘 아침 지친 사내에게서 복종을 끌어내는 데 성공했다. 그 순간 독에 찌든 그의 몸은 불스트로드의 차갑고 단호한 태도 앞에서 위축되었고, 그는 가족의 아침 식사 시간 전에 조용히 마차에 실려 떠났다. 하인들은 그를 가난한 친척쯤으로 여겼고, 세상에서 고개를 높이 들고 사는 자기네 주인 같은 엄격한 사람이 그런 사촌을 부끄러워하며 떼어 버리고 싶어 하는 것을 이상하게 여기지 않았다. 그 미운 동행자와 함께한 은행가의 십 마일 마차 길은 크리스마스 날의 음울한 시작이었다. 그러나 길의 끝에 이르렀을 때 래플스는 기운을 되찾았고, 흡족한 기분으로 작별했는데, 거기에는 은행가가 그에게 백 파운드를 준 충분한 이유가 있었다. 여러 가지 동기가 불스트로드를 이런 후한 베풂으로 몰아갔으나, 그 자신은 그 모든 동기를 면밀히 캐묻지 않았다. 래플스가 불안한 잠에 빠진 모습을 지켜보고 서 있을 때, 처음 이백 파운드를 준 이후로 그 사내가 무척 망가졌다는 생각이 그의 마음에 분명 떠올랐던 것이다."]], [["This was the experience which had determined his conversation with Lydgate.", "그는 더 이상 농락당하지 않겠다는 자신의 결심을 날카롭게 거듭 천명하는 데 신경을 썼고, 그를 매수하는 위험이 그에게 맞서는 위험과 똑같다는 것을 자신이 보여 주었다는 사실을 래플스에게 각인시키려 했다."], ["But at this time his arrangements had most of them gone no farther than a stage at which he could recall them if they proved to be unnecessary.", "그러나 그 혐오스러운 존재에게서 풀려나 조용한 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 불스트로드는 자신이 한낱 유예 이상의 무엇을 확보했다는 자신감을 가지고 오지 못했다."], ["He continually deferred the final steps; in the midst of his fears, like many a man who is in danger of shipwreck or of being dashed from his carriage by runaway horses, he had a clinging impression that something would happen to hinder the worst, and that to spoil his life by a late transplantation might be over-hasty—especially since it was difficult to account satisfactorily to his wife for the project of their indefinite exile from the only place where she would like to live.", "마치 역겨운 꿈을 꾸고서 그 미운 감각의 무리와 함께 그 영상들을 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 것 같았고—마치 자기 삶의 그 모든 즐거운 주변에 위험한 파충류가 끈적끈적한 흔적을 남기고 간 것 같았다. 자신의 가장 내밀한 삶이 다른 사람들이 자기에 대해 품고 있으리라 믿는 생각들로 얼마나 많이 이루어져 있는지, 그 의견의 직물이 파멸의 위협을 받기 전까지 누가 알 수 있겠는가?"]], [["Among the affairs Bulstrode had to care for, was the management of the farm at Stone Court in case of his absence; and on this as well as on all other matters connected with any houses and land he possessed in or about Middlemarch, he had consulted Caleb Garth. Like every one else who had business of that sort, he wanted to get the agent who was more anxious for his employer’s interests than his own. With regard to Stone Court, since Bulstrode wished to retain his hold on the stock, and to have an arrangement by which he himself could, if he chose, resume his favorite recreation of superintendence, Caleb had advised him not to trust to a mere bailiff, but to let the land, stock, and implements yearly, and take a proportionate share of the proceeds.", "불스트로드는 아내의 마음속에 불안한 예감의 침전물이 있다는 것을 더욱 절실히 의식하게 되었으니, 그녀가 그것에 대한 어떤 언급도 조심스럽게 피했기 때문이다. 그는 날마다 우월함의 맛과 완전한 공경의 헌사를 음미하는 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그런데 자신이 어떤 떳떳지 못한 비밀을 가지고 있다는 숨은 의심을 품은 채 누군가에게 감시당하거나 가늠당하고 있다는 확신은, 교화의 말을 할 때 그의 목소리를 떨리게 만들었다. 불스트로드처럼 불안한 기질을 지닌 사람들에게는 예견하는 것이 흔히 직접 보는 것보다 더 나쁜 법이다. 그의 상상력은 임박한 치욕의 고뇌를 끊임없이 키웠다. 그렇다, 임박했다. 래플스에 대한 그의 저항이 그 사내를 멀리 떼어 놓지 못한다면—그는 그런 결과를 기도했지만 거의 바라지는 못했으니—치욕은 확실했다. 만약 그것이 허락된다면 그것은 신의 징벌, 응징, 준비라고 스스로에게 말해 보았으나 헛일이었다. 그는 상상 속의 그 불길로부터 움츠러들었고, 자신이 치욕을 피하는 편이 신의 영광을 위해 더 나으리라고 판단했다. 그 움츠러듦이 마침내 그로 하여금 미들마치를 떠날 준비를 하도록 몰아갔다. 만약 자신에 관한 악한 진실이 폭로되어야만 한다면, 그때 그는 오랜 이웃들의 경멸로부터 덜 살을 태우는 거리에 있게 될 것이다. 그리고 자기 삶이 같은 광범위한 민감성을 쌓지 못한 새로운 무대에서라면, 그 고문자가 자신을 쫓아온다 해도 덜 무서울 것이었다. 이곳을 영영 떠나는 것이 아내에게 극심한 고통이 되리라는 것을 그는 알았고, 다른 이유들로도 자신이 뿌리내린 이곳에 머무는 편을 더 좋아했을 것이다. 그래서 그는 처음에는 조건부의 방식으로 준비를 했으니, 섭리의 어떤 호의적인 개입이 그의 두려움을 흩어 준다면 잠시 자리를 비운 뒤 돌아올 여지를 사방에 남겨 두고자 했다. 그는 건강이 쇠약해졌다는 이유로 은행 경영을 넘기고 인근의 다른 상업적 일에 대한 어떤 적극적 통제도 포기할 준비를 했지만, 장차 그런 일을 다시 맡을 가능성을 배제하지는 않았다. 그 조치는 그에게 이미 전반적인 거래의 침체로 겪은 것을 넘어선 약간의 추가 비용과 약간의 수입 감소를 가져올 것이었다. 그리고 병원이 그가 정당하게 절약할 수 있는 주된 지출 대상으로 떠올랐다."]], [["“May I trust to you to find me a tenant on these terms, Mr. Garth?”", "이것이 그가 리드게이트와 나눈 대화를 결정한 경험이었다."], ["said Bulstrode. “And will you mention to me the yearly sum which would repay you for managing these affairs which we have discussed together?”", "그러나 이 시점에 그의 준비는 대부분 불필요한 것으로 드러날 경우 철회할 수 있는 단계를 넘어서지 않았다."], ["“I’ll think about it,”", "그는 마지막 조치를 끊임없이 미루었다."], ["said Caleb, in his blunt way. “I’ll see how I can make it out.”", "두려움 한가운데서 그는, 난파의 위험에 처했거나 날뛰는 말들에 의해 마차에서 내동댕이쳐질 위험에 처한 많은 사람들처럼, 무언가가 일어나 최악을 막아 주리라는, 그리고 뒤늦은 이식으로 자기 삶을 망치는 것은 지나치게 성급한 일일지 모른다는 끈질긴 인상에 매달렸다—더구나 아내가 살고 싶어 하는 유일한 곳에서 무기한 추방되는 계획을 그녀에게 만족스럽게 설명하기란 어려운 일이었으니 더욱 그러했다."]], [["If it had not been that he had to consider Fred Vincy’s future, Mr.", "불스트로드가 처리해야 할 일들 가운데에는 자신이 부재할 경우 스톤 코트 농장을 관리하는 문제가 있었다."], ["Garth would not probably have been glad of any addition to his work, of which his wife was always fearing an excess for him as he grew older.", "그리고 이 일에 관해서는 물론, 그가 미들마치 안팎에 소유한 가옥과 토지에 관련된 다른 모든 문제에 관해서도, 그는 케일럽 가스와 상의했다."], ["But on quitting Bulstrode after that conversation, a very alluring idea occurred to him about this said letting of Stone Court.", "그런 종류의 일이 있는 다른 모든 사람과 마찬가지로, 그는 자기 자신의 이익보다 고용주의 이익을 더 염려하는 대리인을 얻고 싶었다."], ["What if Bulstrode would agree to his placing Fred Vincy there on the understanding that he, Caleb Garth, should be responsible for the management? It would be an excellent schooling for Fred; he might make a modest income there, and still have time left to get knowledge by helping in other business.", "스톤 코트에 관해서는, 불스트로드가 가축에 대한 자기 소유권을 유지하고 싶어 했고, 또 원한다면 자신이 좋아하는 감독이라는 소일거리를 다시 맡을 수 있는 방식을 마련하고 싶어 했으므로, 케일럽은 단순한 관리인에게 맡기지 말고 토지와 가축과 농기구를 연 단위로 임대하여 수익의 비례 몫을 받으라고 그에게 조언했다."], ["He mentioned his notion to Mrs.", "\"이런 조건으로 소작인을 찾는 일을 당신에게 맡겨도 되겠소, 가스 씨?\""], ["Garth with such evident delight that she could not bear to chill his pleasure by expressing her constant fear of his undertaking too much.", "불스트로드가 말했다. \"그리고 우리가 함께 논의한 이 일들을 관리하는 데 대한 보수로 연간 얼마를 드리면 되는지 말씀해 주시겠소?\""]], [["“The lad would be as happy as two,”", "\"생각해 보겠습니다.\" 케일럽이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["he said, throwing himself back in his chair, and looking radiant, “if I could tell him it was all settled.", "\"어떻게 셈을 맞출 수 있을지 알아보지요.\" 프레드 빈시의 장래를 고려해야 하는 처지만 아니었다면, 가스 씨는 아마 일이 더 늘어나는 것을 반기지 않았을 것이다."], ["Think; Susan! His mind had been running on that place for years before old Featherstone died.", "그의 아내는 그가 나이를 먹어 감에 따라 일을 지나치게 떠맡지나 않을까 늘 걱정했으니 말이다."], ["And it would be as pretty a turn of things as could be that he should hold the place in a good industrious way after all—by his taking to business.", "그러나 그 대화를 마치고 불스트로드와 헤어진 뒤, 그 스톤 코트의 임대라는 것에 관해 매우 솔깃한 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다."], ["For it’s likely enough Bulstrode might let him go on, and gradually buy the stock.", "만약 불스트로드가 케일럽 가스 자신이 관리를 책임진다는 조건으로 프레드 빈시를 그곳에 들이는 데 동의해 준다면 어떨까?"], ["He hasn’t made up his mind, I can see, whether or not he shall settle somewhere else as a lasting thing.", "그것은 프레드에게 훌륭한 수업이 될 터였다. 그는 거기서 변변한 수입을 올리면서도 다른 사업을 거들며 지식을 얻을 시간을 여전히 남겨 둘 수 있을 것이었다."], ["I never was better pleased with a notion in my life. And then the children might be married by-and-by, Susan.”", "그가 너무도 기뻐하며 이 생각을 가스 부인에게 이야기했기에, 그녀는 그가 일을 지나치게 떠맡는 것에 대한 자신의 변함없는 두려움을 표현하여 그의 기쁨에 찬물을 끼얹을 수가 없었다."]], [["“You will not give any hint of the plan to Fred, until you are sure that Bulstrode would agree to the plan?” said Mrs. Garth, in a tone of gentle caution. “And as to marriage, Caleb, we old people need not help to hasten it.”", "\"그 녀석은 더없이 기뻐할 거요.\" 그가 의자에 등을 기대고 환히 빛나는 얼굴로 말했다. \"다 정해졌다고 말해 줄 수만 있다면 말이오. 생각해 봐요, 수전! 페더스톤 영감이 죽기 여러 해 전부터 그 애 마음은 그 자리에 가 있었지. 그리고 결국 그 애가 사업에 뛰어들어 부지런하고 착실하게 그곳을 지킨다면, 그보다 더 멋진 일의 전환이 있을 수 없을 거요. 불스트로드가 그 애를 계속하게 두고서 점차 가축을 사들이게 할 가능성도 충분하니까. 그가 다른 어딘가에 영구히 정착할지 어떨지는 아직 마음을 정하지 못한 것 같소. 내 평생 이만큼 마음에 드는 생각은 없었소. 그러면 머지않아 아이들이 결혼할 수도 있겠지, 수전.\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t know,” said Caleb, swinging his head aside.", "\"불스트로드가 그 계획에 동의하리라는 확신이 서기 전까지는, 프레드에게 그 계획에 대해 어떤 낌새도 비치지 않으시겠지요?\""], ["“Marriage is a taming thing. Fred would want less of my bit and bridle.", "가스 부인이 부드럽게 조심스러운 어조로 말했다."], ["However, I shall say nothing till I know the ground I’m treading on. I shall speak to Bulstrode again.”", "\"그리고 결혼에 관해서라면, 케일럽, 우리 늙은 사람들이 그것을 서두르도록 거들 필요는 없어요.\""]], [["He took his earliest opportunity of doing so.", "\"오, 글쎄 어떨지.\" 케일럽이 고개를 옆으로 갸웃하며 말했다."], ["Bulstrode had anything but a warm interest in his nephew Fred Vincy, but he had a strong wish to secure Mr.", "\"결혼이란 사람을 길들이는 거요. 프레드는 내 재갈과 고삐가 덜 필요해질 거고."], ["Garth’s services on many scattered points of business at which he was sure to be a considerable loser, if they were under less conscientious management.", "어쨌든 내가 디디고 선 땅을 알기 전까지는 아무 말도 안 하겠소. 불스트로드와 다시 이야기해 보지.\""], ["On that ground he made no objection to Mr.", "그는 그렇게 할 가장 이른 기회를 잡았다."], ["Garth’s proposal; and there was also another reason why he was not sorry to give a consent which was to benefit one of the Vincy family.", "불스트로드는 조카 프레드 빈시에게 따뜻한 관심이라고는 도무지 없었지만, 더 양심적이지 못한 관리에 맡겼다가는 분명 상당한 손해를 볼 여러 산재한 사업 문제에서 가스 씨의 도움을 확보하고 싶은 강한 바람이 있었다."], ["It was that Mrs.", "그런 이유로 그는 가스 씨의 제안에 이의를 달지 않았다."], ["Bulstrode, having heard of Lydgate’s debts, had been anxious to know whether her husband could not do something for poor Rosamond, and had been much troubled on learning from him that Lydgate’s affairs were not easily remediable, and that the wisest plan was to let them “take their course.”", "그리고 빈시 집안 사람 가운데 하나에게 이익이 될 동의를 해 주는 것을 그가 유감스러워하지 않은 또 다른 이유도 있었다. 그것은 불스트로드 부인이 리드게이트의 빚에 관해 듣고서 남편이 가엾은 로저먼드를 위해 무언가 해 줄 수 없는지 알고 싶어 애를 태웠고, 리드게이트의 형편이 쉽게 구제될 수 없으며 가장 현명한 방책은 일을 \"되어 가는 대로 내버려 두는\""], ["Mrs. Bulstrode had then said for the first time, “I think you are always a little hard towards my family, Nicholas.", "것이라는 말을 남편에게 듣고 무척 괴로워했기 때문이었다."], ["And I am sure I have no reason to deny any of my relatives.", "그때 불스트로드 부인은 처음으로 이렇게 말했다. \"당신은 늘 제 식구들에게 좀 모질게 구는 것 같아요, 니컬러스."], ["Too worldly they may be, but no one ever had to say that they were not respectable.”", "그리고 저는 제 친척 누구도 부정할 이유가 없다고 분명히 말하겠어요. 그들이 너무 세속적일지는 몰라도, 그들이 존경받을 만하지 않다고 말한 사람은 아무도 없었어요.\""]], [["“My dear Harriet,” said Mr.", "\"여보, 해리엇.\""], ["Bulstrode, wincing under his wife’s eyes, which were filling with tears, “I have supplied your brother with a great deal of capital.", "불스트로드 씨는 눈물이 차오르는 아내의 눈길에 움찔하며 말했다. \"나는 당신 오라버니에게 막대한 자본을 대 주었소."], ["I cannot be expected to take care of his married children.”", "내가 그의 결혼한 자식들까지 돌봐 줄 거라고 기대해서는 안 되오.\""]], [["That seemed to be true, and Mrs.", "그것은 사실인 듯했고, 불스트로드 부인의 항변은 가엾은 로저먼드에 대한 연민으로 가라앉았다."], ["Bulstrode’s remonstrance subsided into pity for poor Rosamond, whose extravagant education she had always foreseen the fruits of.", "그녀는 그 애의 사치스러운 교육이 맺을 열매를 늘 예견해 왔던 터였다."]], [["But remembering that dialogue, Mr. Bulstrode felt that when he had to talk to his wife fully about his plan of quitting Middlemarch, he should be glad to tell her that he had made an arrangement which might be for the good of her nephew Fred.", "그러나 그 대화를 떠올리며, 불스트로드 씨는 미들마치를 떠나려는 자신의 계획에 대해 아내와 충분히 이야기해야 할 때, 그녀의 조카 프레드에게 이로울 수도 있는 어떤 조치를 마련했다고 그녀에게 말할 수 있어 다행이라고 느꼈다."], ["At present he had merely mentioned to her that he thought of shutting up The Shrubs for a few months, and taking a house on the Southern Coast.", "지금으로서는 더 슈럽스를 몇 달간 닫아 두고 남부 해안에 집을 한 채 얻을까 생각 중이라고만 그녀에게 말해 둔 터였다."]], [["Hence Mr. Garth got the assurance he desired, namely, that in case of Bulstrode’s departure from Middlemarch for an indefinite time, Fred Vincy should be allowed to have the tenancy of Stone Court on the terms proposed.", "그리하여 가스 씨는 자신이 바라던 확약을 얻었으니, 곧 불스트로드가 무기한 미들마치를 떠날 경우 프레드 빈시가 제안된 조건으로 스톤 코트의 소작권을 가질 수 있도록 허락한다는 것이었다."]], [["Caleb was so elated with his hope of this “neat turn” being given to things, that if his self-control had not been braced by a little affectionate wifely scolding, he would have betrayed everything to Mary, wanting “to give the child comfort.”", "케일럽은 일이 이렇게 \"깔끔한 전환\"을 맞으리라는 희망에 너무도 들떠서, 만약 애정 어린 아내의 약간의 잔소리로 자제력이 다잡아지지 않았더라면, \"그 애에게 위안을 주려고\""], ["However, he restrained himself, and kept in strict privacy from Fred certain visits which he was making to Stone Court, in order to look more thoroughly into the state of the land and stock, and take a preliminary estimate.", "메리에게 모든 것을 누설하고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그는 자신을 억눌렀고, 토지와 가축의 상태를 더 철저히 살펴보고 예비적인 견적을 내기 위해 스톤 코트를 몇 차례 다녀온 일을 프레드에게는 엄격히 비밀로 부쳤다."], ["He was certainly more eager in these visits than the probable speed of events required him to be; but he was stimulated by a fatherly delight in occupying his mind with this bit of probable happiness which he held in store like a hidden birthday gift for Fred and Mary.", "그가 이런 방문에 들인 열의는 분명 사태가 진행될 만한 속도가 요구하는 것보다 더 컸다. 그러나 그는 프레드와 메리를 위해 감춰 둔 생일 선물처럼 간직하고 있던 이 한 조각의 있을 법한 행복으로 마음을 채우는 데서 오는 아버지다운 기쁨에 자극받았던 것이다."]], [["“But suppose the whole scheme should turn out to be a castle in the air?”", "\"하지만 그 계획 전체가 한낱 뜬구름 잡는 일로 드러나면 어쩌지요?\""], ["said Mrs. Garth.", "가스 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"뭐, 괜찮소.\""], ["replied Caleb; “the castle will tumble about nobody’s head.”", "케일럽이 대답했다. \"그 누각이 무너진들 누구 머리 위로 떨어질 것도 아니니까.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXIX.", "# CHAPTER LXIX."]], [["“If thou hast heard a word, let it die with thee.”", "“네가 한 마디를 들었거든, 그것이 너와 함께 사라지게 하라.”"], ["—Ecclesiasticus.", "— 「집회서」."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode was still seated in his manager’s room at the Bank, about three o’clock of the same day on which he had received Lydgate there, when the clerk entered to say that his horse was waiting, and also that Mr. Garth was outside and begged to speak with him.", "벌스트로드 씨는 바로 그날 — 리드게이트를 그곳에서 맞이했던 그날 — 오후 세 시쯤, 여전히 은행의 지배인 사무실에 앉아 있었는데, 그때 서기가 들어와 말이 기다리고 있다는 것과 가스 씨가 밖에서 그와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 한다는 것을 알렸다."]], [["“By all means,”", "“얼마든지,”"], ["said Bulstrode; and Caleb entered.", "벌스트로드가 말했다. 그러자 케일럽이 들어왔다."], ["“Pray sit down, Mr. Garth,”", "“부디 앉으시지요, 가스 씨,”"], ["continued the banker, in his suavest tone.", "은행가는 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말을 이었다."]], [["“I am glad that you arrived just in time to find me here.", "“마침 제때 도착하셔서 저를 여기에서 만나시게 되니 다행입니다."], ["I know you count your minutes.”", "시간을 분 단위로 아끼시는 분이라는 걸 압니다.”"]], [["“Oh,”", "“아,”"], ["said Caleb, gently, with a slow swing of his head on one side, as he seated himself and laid his hat on the floor.", "케일럽이 부드럽게 말하며 고개를 한쪽으로 천천히 기울이고는, 자리에 앉아 모자를 바닥에 내려놓았다."]], [["He looked at the ground, leaning forward and letting his long fingers droop between his legs, while each finger moved in succession, as if it were sharing some thought which filled his large quiet brow.", "그는 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채 긴 손가락을 두 다리 사이로 늘어뜨리고 바닥을 바라보았는데, 손가락 하나하나가 차례로 움직이는 모습이, 마치 그의 넓고 고요한 이마를 채운 어떤 생각을 함께 나누고 있는 듯했다."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode, like every one else who knew Caleb, was used to his slowness in beginning to speak on any topic which he felt to be important, and rather expected that he was about to recur to the buying of some houses in Blindman’s Court, for the sake of pulling them down, as a sacrifice of property which would be well repaid by the influx of air and light on that spot.", "케일럽을 아는 다른 모든 사람처럼, 벌스트로드 씨도 그가 중요하다고 느끼는 어떤 주제든 말을 꺼낼 때면 늘 더디다는 것을 알고 있었기에, 그가 다시 블라인드맨스 코트의 집 몇 채를 사들이는 이야기를 꺼내려는 거라고 짐작했다."], ["It was by propositions of this kind that Caleb was sometimes troublesome to his employers; but he had usually found Bulstrode ready to meet him in projects of improvement, and they had got on well together.", "그 집들을 헐어버리려고 사들이자는 것이었으니, 그 자리에 공기와 빛이 흘러들어옴으로써 충분히 보상받을, 재산의 희생인 셈이었다. 케일럽은 때때로 이런 종류의 제안으로 고용주들을 곤란하게 했지만, 그는 대개 벌스트로드가 개선 사업이라면 기꺼이 응하는 것을 보았고, 둘은 사이좋게 일해 왔다."], ["When he spoke again, however, it was to say, in rather a subdued voice—", "그러나 그가 다시 입을 열었을 때, 그것은 다소 가라앉은 목소리로 한 말이었다."]], [["“I have just come away from Stone Court, Mr. Bulstrode.”", "“방금 스톤 코트에서 오는 길입니다, 벌스트로드 씨.”"]], [["“You found nothing wrong there, I hope,”", "“거기서 별일 없으셨기를 바랍니다,”"], ["said the banker; “I was there myself yesterday.", "은행가가 말했다. “어제는 제가 직접 다녀왔지요."], ["Abel has done well with the lambs this year.”", "에이블이 올해 새끼 양들을 잘 길렀더군요.”"]], [["“Why, yes,”", "“글쎄요, 그렇긴 합니다,”"], ["said Caleb, looking up gravely, “there is something wrong—a stranger, who is very ill, I think.", "케일럽이 진지하게 고개를 들며 말했다. “별일이 좀 있습니다 — 어떤 낯선 사람이 있는데, 제 생각엔 몹시 아픈 것 같습니다."], ["He wants a doctor, and I came to tell you of that.", "의사가 필요해서요, 그걸 알려드리러 왔습니다."], ["His name is Raffles.”", "그 사람 이름은 래플스입니다.”"]], [["He saw the shock of his words passing through Bulstrode’s frame.", "그는 자기 말이 벌스트로드의 온몸을 관통하는 충격을 보았다."], ["On this subject the banker had thought that his fears were too constantly on the watch to be taken by surprise; but he had been mistaken.", "이 문제에 관해서라면 은행가는 자신의 두려움이 늘 빈틈없이 경계하고 있어서 불시에 당할 일은 없으리라 여겼지만, 그것은 착각이었다."]], [["“Poor wretch!”", "“가엾은 친구!”"], ["he said in a compassionate tone, though his lips trembled a little.", "그가 동정 어린 어조로 말했으나, 입술은 조금 떨렸다."], ["“Do you know how he came there?”", "“그가 어떻게 거기까지 왔는지 아십니까?”"]], [["“I took him myself,”", "“제가 직접 데려왔습니다,”"], ["said Caleb, quietly—“took him up in my gig.", "케일럽이 조용히 말했다. “제 이륜마차에 태웠지요."], ["He had got down from the coach, and was walking a little beyond the turning from the toll-house, and I overtook him.", "그가 역마차에서 내려서 통행료 징수소 모퉁이를 지나 조금 걸어가고 있었는데, 제가 따라잡았습니다."], ["He remembered seeing me with you once before, at Stone Court, and he asked me to take him on.", "그가 예전에 한 번 스톤 코트에서 당신과 함께 있는 저를 본 걸 기억하고는, 태워달라고 청하더군요."], ["I saw he was ill: it seemed to me the right thing to do, to carry him under shelter.", "보니 아픈 사람이었습니다. 그를 지붕 아래로 데려가는 것이 옳은 일이라고 여겨졌지요."], ["And now I think you should lose no time in getting advice for him.”", "그러니 이제 지체 없이 그를 위해 의사의 진찰을 받게 하셔야 할 것 같습니다.”"], ["Caleb took up his hat from the floor as he ended, and rose slowly from his seat.", "케일럽은 말을 마치며 바닥에서 모자를 집어 들고, 천천히 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“Certainly,”", "“물론이지요,”"], ["said Bulstrode, whose mind was very active at this moment.", "이 순간 머리가 매우 분주하게 돌아가던 벌스트로드가 말했다."], ["“Perhaps you will yourself oblige me, Mr. Garth, by calling at Mr.", "“가스 씨, 어쩌면 지나가시는 길에 리드게이트 씨 댁에 들러 주신다면 제게 큰 도움이 되겠습니다 — 아니, 잠깐만요!"], ["Lydgate’s as you pass—or stay! he may at this hour probably be at the Hospital.", "이 시간이면 아마 그는 병원에 있을 겁니다."], ["I will first send my man on the horse there with a note this instant, and then I will myself ride to Stone Court.”", "우선 지금 즉시 말을 타고 가는 제 하인 편에 쪽지를 보내고, 그런 다음 제가 직접 스톤 코트로 가겠습니다.”"]], [["Bulstrode quickly wrote a note, and went out himself to give the commission to his man.", "벌스트로드는 재빨리 쪽지를 써서, 손수 밖으로 나가 하인에게 심부름을 시켰다."], ["When he returned, Caleb was standing as before with one hand on the back of the chair, holding his hat with the other.", "그가 돌아왔을 때 케일럽은 여전히 한 손은 의자 등받이에, 다른 손으로는 모자를 든 채 아까처럼 서 있었다."], ["In Bulstrode’s mind the dominant thought was, “Perhaps Raffles only spoke to Garth of his illness.", "벌스트로드의 마음속에서 가장 큰 생각은 이러했다. “어쩌면 래플스는 가스에게 자기 병에 대해서만 말했을지도 모른다."], ["Garth may wonder, as he must have done before, at this disreputable fellow’s claiming intimacy with me; but he will know nothing.", "가스는, 분명 전에도 그랬겠지만, 이 추잡한 작자가 나와 친분이 있는 척하는 것을 의아해할 테지. 하지만 그는 아무것도 모를 것이다."], ["And he is friendly to me—I can be of use to him.”", "게다가 그는 내게 우호적이지 않은가 — 나는 그에게 쓸모 있는 사람이 될 수 있다.”"]], [["He longed for some confirmation of this hopeful conjecture, but to have asked any question as to what Raffles had said or done would have been to betray fear.", "그는 이 희망적인 추측을 확증해 줄 무언가를 간절히 바랐지만, 래플스가 무슨 말을 했고 무슨 짓을 했는지 묻는다면 그것은 곧 두려움을 드러내는 일이 될 터였다."]], [["“I am exceedingly obliged to you, Mr.", "“대단히 감사합니다, 가스 씨,”"], ["Garth,” he said, in his usual tone of politeness.", "그가 평소의 정중한 어조로 말했다."], ["“My servant will be back in a few minutes, and I shall then go myself to see what can be done for this unfortunate man.", "“제 하인이 몇 분 안에 돌아올 테고, 그러면 제가 직접 가서 이 불운한 사람을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 살펴보겠습니다."], ["Perhaps you had some other business with me?", "혹시 제게 다른 볼일이 있으셨습니까?"], ["If so, pray be seated.”", "그러시다면, 부디 앉으시지요.”"]], [["“Thank you,”", "“고맙습니다,”"], ["said Caleb, making a slight gesture with his right hand to waive the invitation. “I wish to say, Mr.", "케일럽이 오른손으로 그 권유를 사양하는 가벼운 몸짓을 하며 말했다."], ["Bulstrode, that I must request you to put your business into some other hands than mine.", "“벌스트로드 씨, 드릴 말씀이 있습니다. 제 일을 저 말고 다른 사람의 손에 맡기시기를 청해야겠습니다."], ["I am obliged to you for your handsome way of meeting me—about the letting of Stone Court, and all other business.", "스톤 코트 임대 건이며 그 밖의 모든 일에서 저를 후하게 대해 주신 점은 감사하게 여깁니다."], ["But I must give it up.”", "하지만 저는 그 일을 그만두어야겠습니다.”"], ["A sharp certainty entered like a stab into Bulstrode’s soul.", "날카로운 확신이 칼날처럼 벌스트로드의 영혼을 찔러 들어왔다."]], [["“This is sudden, Mr. Garth,”", "“이거 너무 갑작스럽군요, 가스 씨,”"], ["was all he could say at first.", "그가 처음에 할 수 있었던 말은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["“It is,”", "“그렇습니다,”"], ["said Caleb; “but it is quite fixed.", "케일럽이 말했다. “하지만 이미 굳게 정한 일입니다."], ["I must give it up.”", "저는 그만두어야겠습니다.”"]], [["He spoke with a firmness which was very gentle, and yet he could see that Bulstrode seemed to cower under that gentleness, his face looking dried and his eyes swerving away from the glance which rested on him.", "그는 매우 온화하면서도 단호하게 말했지만, 벌스트로드가 그 온화함 앞에서 움츠러드는 듯한 모습을, 그의 얼굴이 메마르게 보이고 자신에게 머무는 시선을 피해 두 눈을 돌리는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Caleb felt a deep pity for him, but he could have used no pretexts to account for his resolve, even if they would have been of any use.", "케일럽은 그에게 깊은 연민을 느꼈으나, 자신의 결심을 변명할 어떤 핑계도 — 설령 그것이 무슨 소용이 있다 한들 — 댈 수는 없었다."]], [["“You have been led to this, I apprehend, by some slanders concerning me uttered by that unhappy creature,”", "“제 짐작에는, 저 불행한 작자가 저에 관해 늘어놓은 어떤 중상모략 때문에 이런 결심을 하게 되신 듯합니다만,”"], ["said Bulstrode, anxious now to know the utmost.", "이제 가장 나쁜 사실까지 알고 싶어진 벌스트로드가 말했다."]], [["“That is true.", "“사실입니다."], ["I can’t deny that I act upon what I heard from him.”", "그에게서 들은 것에 따라 행동한다는 걸 부인하지 않겠습니다.”"]], [["“You are a conscientious man, Mr. Garth—a man, I trust, who feels himself accountable to God.", "“가스 씨, 당신은 양심적인 사람입니다 — 제가 믿건대, 하느님 앞에 책임을 진다고 느끼는 사람이지요."], ["You would not wish to injure me by being too ready to believe a slander,”", "중상모략을 너무 쉽사리 믿어 저를 해치고 싶지는 않으실 겁니다,”"], ["said Bulstrode, casting about for pleas that might be adapted to his hearer’s mind.", "벌스트로드는 듣는 이의 마음에 맞을 만한 호소거리를 더듬어 찾으며 말했다."], ["“That is a poor reason for giving up a connection which I think I may say will be mutually beneficial.”", "“그것은, 제가 감히 말하건대 서로에게 유익할 거래를 그만둘 빈약한 이유입니다.”"]], [["“I would injure no man if I could help it,”", "“저는 할 수만 있다면 누구도 해치고 싶지 않습니다,”"], ["said Caleb; “even if I thought God winked at it.", "케일럽이 말했다. “설령 하느님이 눈감아 주신다고 여겨진다 해도요."], ["I hope I should have a feeling for my fellow-creature.", "제 동료 인간에 대한 마음은 지니고 싶습니다."], ["But, sir—I am obliged to believe that this Raffles has told me the truth.", "하지만, 선생 — 저는 이 래플스가 제게 진실을 말했다고 믿지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["And I can’t be happy in working with you, or profiting by you.", "그리고 당신과 함께 일하거나 당신 덕에 이득을 보면서는 마음 편할 수가 없습니다."], ["It hurts my mind.", "마음이 괴롭습니다."], ["I must beg you to seek another agent.”", "부디 다른 대리인을 구하시기를 청합니다.”"]], [["“Very well, Mr. Garth.", "“좋습니다, 가스 씨."], ["But I must at least claim to know the worst that he has told you.", "하지만 적어도 그가 당신에게 한 가장 나쁜 말이 무엇인지는 알아야겠습니다."], ["I must know what is the foul speech that I am liable to be the victim of,”", "제가 희생자가 될 수도 있는 그 추악한 험담이 무엇인지 알아야겠습니다,”"], ["said Bulstrode, a certain amount of anger beginning to mingle with his humiliation before this quiet man who renounced his benefits.", "벌스트로드가 말했는데, 자기에게 이익을 베풀던 자리를 마다하는 이 조용한 사내 앞에서 느끼는 굴욕에 어느 정도의 분노가 섞이기 시작했다."]], [["“That’s needless,”", "“그러실 필요 없습니다,”"], ["said Caleb, waving his hand, bowing his head slightly, and not swerving from the tone which had in it the merciful intention to spare this pitiable man.", "케일럽이 손을 내저으며, 고개를 살짝 숙이고는, 이 가련한 사내를 아껴 주려는 자비로운 뜻이 담긴 그 어조에서 조금도 벗어나지 않은 채 말했다. “그가 제게 한 말은, 지금 알지 못하는 어떤 일이 그것을 제 입에서 강제로 끌어내지 않는 한, 결코 제 입 밖으로 나오지 않을 겁니다."], ["“What he has said to me will never pass from my lips, unless something now unknown forces it from me.", "만일 당신이 이익을 위해 남을 해치는 삶을 살았고, 더 많은 것을 차지하려고 속임수로 남의 권리를 빼앗았다면, 감히 말하건대 후회하고 계실 겁니다 — 돌이키고 싶지만 그럴 수 없으시겠지요."], ["If you led a harmful life for gain, and kept others out of their rights by deceit, to get the more for yourself, I dare say you repent—you would like to go back, and can’t: that must be a bitter thing”—Caleb paused a moment and shook his head—“it is not for me to make your life harder to you.”", "그건 분명 쓰라린 일일 겁니다” — 케일럽은 잠시 말을 멈추고 고개를 가로저었다 — “제가 당신의 삶을 더 힘들게 만들 일은 아닙니다.”"]], [["“But you do—you do make it harder to me,”", "“하지만 당신은 그러고 있습니다 — 당신은 내 삶을 더 힘들게 만들고 있어요,”"], ["said Bulstrode constrained into a genuine, pleading cry.", "벌스트로드가 진심 어린 애원의 외침을 어쩔 수 없이 토해내며 말했다."], ["“You make it harder to me by turning your back on me.”", "“당신은 내게 등을 돌림으로써 내 삶을 더 힘들게 하고 있습니다.”"]], [["“That I’m forced to do,”", "“그건 제가 어쩔 수 없이 하는 일입니다,”"], ["said Caleb, still more gently, lifting up his hand.", "케일럽이 손을 들어 올리며, 한층 더 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“I am sorry.", "“죄송합니다."], ["I don’t judge you and say, he is wicked, and I am righteous.", "저는 당신을 심판하여, 저자는 악하고 나는 의롭다고 말하지 않습니다."], ["God forbid. I don’t know everything.", "하느님께서 그런 일은 막아 주시기를."], ["A man may do wrong, and his will may rise clear out of it, though he can’t get his life clear.", "저는 모든 것을 알지 못합니다. 사람이 그릇된 일을 저질러도 그 의지는 그 죄에서 맑게 솟아오를 수 있습니다, 비록 자기 삶을 깨끗이 추스를 수는 없을지라도요."], ["That’s a bad punishment.", "그건 가혹한 벌이지요."], ["If it is so with you,—well, I’m very sorry for you.", "만일 당신이 그런 처지라면 — 글쎄요, 저는 당신이 무척 안됐습니다."], ["But I have that feeling inside me, that I can’t go on working with you.", "하지만 제 안에는, 당신과 함께 일을 계속할 수 없다는 그런 느낌이 있습니다."], ["That’s all, Mr. Bulstrode.", "그게 전부입니다, 벌스트로드 씨."], ["Everything else is buried, so far as my will goes.", "그 밖의 모든 것은, 제 의지가 닿는 한 묻어 두겠습니다."], ["And I wish you good-day.”", "그럼 안녕히 계십시오.”"]], [["“One moment, Mr. Garth!”", "“잠깐만요, 가스 씨!”"], ["said Bulstrode, hurriedly.", "벌스트로드가 황급히 말했다."], ["“I may trust then to your solemn assurance that you will not repeat either to man or woman what—even if it have any degree of truth in it—is yet a malicious representation?”", "“그렇다면 당신의 엄숙한 다짐을 믿어도 되겠지요, 즉 — 설령 그 안에 어느 정도 진실이 담겨 있다 해도 — 어쨌든 악의적인 진술인 그것을, 남자든 여자든 누구에게도 되풀이하지 않으리라는 것을요?”"], ["Caleb’s wrath was stirred, and he said, indignantly—", "케일럽의 분노가 일었고, 그는 화를 내며 말했다."]], [["“Why should I have said it if I didn’t mean it?", "“제가 그럴 뜻이 없었다면 무엇 하러 그 말을 했겠습니까?"], ["I am in no fear of you.", "저는 당신이 두렵지 않습니다."], ["Such tales as that will never tempt my tongue.”", "그런 따위의 이야기는 결코 제 혀를 유혹하지 못할 겁니다.”"]], [["“Excuse me—I am agitated—I am the victim of this abandoned man.”", "“미안합니다 — 제가 흥분했군요 — 저는 이 타락한 작자의 희생자입니다.”"]], [["“Stop a bit!", "“잠깐 멈추시지요!"], ["you have got to consider whether you didn’t help to make him worse, when you profited by his vices.”", "당신이 그의 악행으로 이득을 보았을 때, 당신이 그를 더 나쁘게 만드는 데 한몫하지 않았는지를 따져 보셔야 할 겁니다.”"]], [["“You are wronging me by too readily believing him,”", "“당신은 그를 너무 쉽게 믿어 저를 부당하게 대하고 있습니다,”"], ["said Bulstrode, oppressed, as by a nightmare, with the inability to deny flatly what Raffles might have said; and yet feeling it an escape that Caleb had not so stated it to him as to ask for that flat denial.", "벌스트로드가 말했는데, 래플스가 했을지도 모를 말을 단호히 부인할 수 없다는 무력감에 악몽에 짓눌리듯 짓눌리면서도, 케일럽이 그 말을 자기에게 들이대어 단호한 부인을 요구하는 식으로 진술하지는 않았다는 점에서 한 줄기 도피처를 느끼고 있었다."]], [["“No,”", "“아닙니다,”"], ["said Caleb, lifting his hand deprecatingly; “I am ready to believe better, when better is proved.", "케일럽이 만류하듯 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. “저는 더 나은 사실이 입증되면 기꺼이 더 좋게 믿을 사람입니다."], ["I rob you of no good chance.", "저는 당신에게서 그 어떤 좋은 기회도 앗아 가지 않습니다."], ["As to speaking, I hold it a crime to expose a man’s sin unless I’m clear it must be done to save the innocent.", "말을 옮기는 것에 관해서라면, 무고한 이를 구하기 위해 꼭 해야 한다는 확신이 서지 않는 한, 남의 죄를 들추는 것을 저는 죄악으로 여깁니다."], ["That is my way of thinking, Mr.", "그게 제 생각입니다, 벌스트로드 씨."], ["Bulstrode, and what I say, I’ve no need to swear.", "그리고 제가 한 말은 굳이 맹세할 필요도 없습니다."], ["I wish you good-day.”", "그럼 안녕히 계십시오.”"]], [["Some hours later, when he was at home, Caleb said to his wife, incidentally, that he had had some little differences with Bulstrode, and that in consequence, he had given up all notion of taking Stone Court, and indeed had resigned doing further business for him.", "몇 시간 뒤, 집에 돌아온 케일럽은 아내에게 지나가는 말로, 벌스트로드와 약간의 의견 차이가 있었으며, 그 결과 스톤 코트를 맡으려던 생각을 모두 접었고, 실은 그를 위해 더는 일을 하지 않기로 그만두었다고 말했다."]], [["“He was disposed to interfere too much, was he?”", "“그가 너무 간섭하려 들던가요?”"], ["said Mrs. Garth, imagining that her husband had been touched on his sensitive point, and not been allowed to do what he thought right as to materials and modes of work.", "가스 부인이 말했는데, 남편이 그의 예민한 부분을 건드려서, 일감과 작업 방식에 관해 옳다고 여기는 대로 하지 못하게 막힌 모양이라고 짐작한 것이었다."]], [["“Oh,”", "“아,”"], ["said Caleb, bowing his head and waving his hand gravely.", "케일럽이 고개를 숙이며 진지하게 손을 내저었다."], ["And Mrs. Garth knew that this was a sign of his not intending to speak further on the subject.", "가스 부인은 이것이 그가 이 문제에 관해 더는 말할 뜻이 없다는 신호임을 알았다."]], [["As for Bulstrode, he had almost immediately mounted his horse and set off for Stone Court, being anxious to arrive there before Lydgate.", "벌스트로드로 말하자면, 그는 거의 곧바로 말에 올라 스톤 코트로 출발했으니, 리드게이트보다 먼저 그곳에 도착하고 싶어 안달이었던 것이다."]], [["His mind was crowded with images and conjectures, which were a language to his hopes and fears, just as we hear tones from the vibrations which shake our whole system.", "그의 마음은 갖가지 형상과 추측으로 가득 차 있었는데, 그것들은 그의 희망과 두려움에게 하나의 언어와도 같았다, 마치 우리가 온몸을 뒤흔드는 진동에서 어떤 음조를 듣게 되는 것처럼."], ["The deep humiliation with which he had winced under Caleb Garth’s knowledge of his past and rejection of his patronage, alternated with and almost gave way to the sense of safety in the fact that Garth, and no other, had been the man to whom Raffles had spoken.", "케일럽 가스가 자기 과거를 알고 그 후원을 거절한 일 앞에서 그가 움찔하며 느꼈던 깊은 굴욕감은, 래플스가 입을 연 상대가 다른 누구도 아닌 가스였다는 사실에서 오는 안도감과 번갈아 들었고, 거의 그 안도감에 자리를 내주었다."], ["It seemed to him a sort of earnest that Providence intended his rescue from worse consequences; the way being thus left open for the hope of secrecy.", "그에게는 그것이, 섭리가 그를 더 나쁜 결과에서 구해 내려 한다는 일종의 보증처럼 여겨졌다. 이로써 비밀이 지켜지리라는 희망의 길이 열린 셈이었다."], ["That Raffles should be afflicted with illness, that he should have been led to Stone Court rather than elsewhere—Bulstrode’s heart fluttered at the vision of probabilities which these events conjured up.", "래플스가 병에 시달리게 된 것, 그가 다른 곳도 아닌 스톤 코트로 인도된 것 — 이러한 사건들이 불러일으킨 갖가지 개연성의 환영 앞에서 벌스트로드의 심장은 두근거렸다."], ["If it should turn out that he was freed from all danger of disgrace—if he could breathe in perfect liberty—his life should be more consecrated than it had ever been before.", "만일 그가 모든 치욕의 위험에서 풀려나는 것으로 밝혀진다면 — 만일 그가 완전한 자유 속에서 숨 쉴 수 있다면 — 그의 삶은 이제껏 어느 때보다도 더 거룩하게 바쳐지리라. 그는 마음속으로 이 맹세를 들어 올렸으니, 마치 그것이 자기가 간절히 바라는 결과를 재촉해 주기라도 할 것처럼."], ["He mentally lifted up this vow as if it would urge the result he longed for—he tried to believe in the potency of that prayerful resolution—its potency to determine death.", "그는 그 기도 어린 결심의 효력을 — 죽음을 결정짓는 그 효력을 — 믿어 보려 애썼다. 그는 자신이 “당신의 뜻이 이루어지소서”"], ["He knew that he ought to say, “Thy will be done;”", "하고 말해야 함을 알았고, 또 자주 그렇게 말했다."], ["and he said it often. But the intense desire remained that the will of God might be the death of that hated man.", "그러나 하느님의 뜻이 그 미운 사내의 죽음이기를 바라는 격렬한 욕망은 여전히 남아 있었다."]], [["Yet when he arrived at Stone Court he could not see the change in Raffles without a shock.", "그러나 스톤 코트에 도착했을 때, 그는 충격 없이는 래플스의 변화를 볼 수 없었다."], ["But for his pallor and feebleness, Bulstrode would have called the change in him entirely mental.", "만일 그 창백함과 쇠약함만 아니었다면, 벌스트로드는 그에게 일어난 변화를 전적으로 정신적인 것이라 불렀을 것이다."], ["Instead of his loud tormenting mood, he showed an intense, vague terror, and seemed to deprecate Bulstrode’s anger, because the money was all gone—he had been robbed—it had half of it been taken from him.", "시끄럽게 사람을 괴롭히던 기색 대신, 그는 격렬하고도 막연한 공포를 드러냈고, 돈이 다 사라졌다며 — 그는 강도를 당했고 — 그 절반을 빼앗겼다며, 벌스트로드의 노여움을 달래려는 듯했다."], ["He had only come here because he was ill and somebody was hunting him—somebody was after him, he had told nobody anything, he had kept his mouth shut.", "그가 여기 온 것은 단지 아프기 때문이고 누군가 자기를 쫓고 있기 때문이라고 했다 — 누군가 자기 뒤를 밟고 있다고, 자기는 아무에게도 아무 말도 하지 않았다고, 입을 꾹 다물고 있었다고."], ["Bulstrode, not knowing the significance of these symptoms, interpreted this new nervous susceptibility into a means of alarming Raffles into true confessions, and taxed him with falsehood in saying that he had not told anything, since he had just told the man who took him up in his gig and brought him to Stone Court.", "이런 증상들의 의미를 알지 못한 벌스트로드는, 이 새로운 신경 과민을 래플스를 겁주어 진실을 자백하게 만들 수단으로 해석하고는, 아무 말도 하지 않았다는 그의 주장이 거짓이라고 추궁했다. 방금 그를 이륜마차에 태워 스톤 코트로 데려온 사람에게 다 말해 놓고서 무슨 소리냐는 것이었다."], ["Raffles denied this with solemn adjurations; the fact being that the links of consciousness were interrupted in him, and that his minute terror-stricken narrative to Caleb Garth had been delivered under a set of visionary impulses which had dropped back into darkness.", "래플스는 엄숙히 맹세하며 이를 부인했는데, 사실인즉 그의 의식의 연결고리가 끊겨 있었던 데다, 케일럽 가스에게 한 그 자질구레하고도 공포에 질린 이야기는 어둠 속으로 도로 가라앉아 버린 일련의 환각적 충동에 휩쓸려 나온 것이었다."]], [["Bulstrode’s heart sank again at this sign that he could get no grasp over the wretched man’s mind, and that no word of Raffles could be trusted as to the fact which he most wanted to know, namely, whether or not he had really kept silence to every one in the neighborhood except Caleb Garth. The housekeeper had told him without the least constraint of manner that since Mr.", "이 가련한 사내의 마음을 도무지 붙들 수 없으며, 그가 가장 알고 싶어 하는 사실 — 즉 그가 케일럽 가스를 제외한 인근의 모든 사람에게 정말로 침묵을 지켰는가 아닌가 — 에 관해서는 래플스의 어떤 말도 믿을 수 없다는 이 징후 앞에서, 벌스트로드의 마음은 다시 가라앉았다."], ["Garth left, Raffles had asked her for beer, and after that had not spoken, seeming very ill. On that side it might be concluded that there had been no betrayal.", "가정부는 조금도 거리낌 없는 태도로, 가스 씨가 떠난 뒤 래플스가 맥주를 청했고 그 뒤로는 말이 없으며 몹시 아파 보인다고 말했다."], ["Mrs. Abel thought, like the servants at The Shrubs, that the strange man belonged to the unpleasant “kin”", "그 점에서는 아무런 누설도 없었다고 결론지을 수 있었다."], ["who are among the troubles of the rich; she had at first referred the kinship to Mr. Rigg, and where there was property left, the buzzing presence of such large blue-bottles seemed natural enough.", "에이블 부인은, 더 슈럽스의 하인들이 그러했듯, 그 낯선 사내를 부자들의 골칫거리 가운데 하나인 그 달갑잖은 “친척”"], ["How he could be “kin”", "부류로 여겼다."], ["to Bulstrode as well was not so clear, but Mrs. Abel agreed with her husband that there was “no knowing,”", "그녀는 처음에 그 친척 관계를 리그 씨에게 결부시켰는데, 물려받을 재산이 있는 곳이라면 그처럼 큼직한 쉬파리들이 윙윙거리며 모여드는 것이 자연스러워 보였던 것이다."], ["a proposition which had a great deal of mental food for her, so that she shook her head over it without further speculation.", "어떻게 그가 벌스트로드와도 “친척”일 수 있는지는 그리 명확하지 않았으나, 에이블 부인은 남편과 의견을 같이하여 “알 수 없는 일”이라 여겼으니, 이 명제는 그녀에게 적잖은 사색거리가 되었기에, 그녀는 더 깊이 헤아리지 않은 채 그저 고개만 갸웃거렸다."]], [["In less than an hour Lydgate arrived.", "한 시간이 채 못 되어 리드게이트가 도착했다."], ["Bulstrode met him outside the wainscoted parlor, where Raffles was, and said—", "벌스트로드는 래플스가 있는, 벽판을 두른 응접실 밖에서 그를 맞으며 말했다."]], [["“I have called you in, Mr. Lydgate, to an unfortunate man who was once in my employment, many years ago.", "“리드게이트 씨, 여러 해 전에 한때 제 밑에서 일했던 불운한 사람 때문에 당신을 모셨습니다."], ["Afterwards he went to America, and returned I fear to an idle dissolute life.", "그 후 그는 미국으로 건너갔다가, 안타깝게도 게으르고 방탕한 생활로 돌아온 모양입니다."], ["Being destitute, he has a claim on me.", "무일푼이 된 그는 제게 도움을 청할 처지지요."], ["He was slightly connected with Rigg, the former owner of this place, and in consequence found his way here.", "그는 이곳의 옛 주인이던 리그와 약간의 연고가 있어서, 그 때문에 여기까지 흘러들어 왔습니다."], ["I believe he is seriously ill: apparently his mind is affected.", "제가 보기에 그는 심각하게 아프고, 정신도 영향을 받은 것 같습니다."], ["I feel bound to do the utmost for him.”", "저는 그를 위해 최선을 다해야 한다고 느낍니다.”"]], [["Lydgate, who had the remembrance of his last conversation with Bulstrode strongly upon him, was not disposed to say an unnecessary word to him, and bowed slightly in answer to this account; but just before entering the room he turned automatically and said, “What is his name?”—to know names being as much a part of the medical man’s accomplishment as of the practical politician’s.", "벌스트로드와 나눈 지난번 대화가 강하게 마음에 남아 있던 리드게이트는, 그에게 불필요한 말을 한마디도 하고 싶지 않아, 이 설명에 답하여 가볍게 고개만 숙였다. 그러나 방으로 들어서기 직전, 그는 무심결에 돌아서서 말했다. “이름이 무엇입니까?” — 이름을 아는 것은 실무 정치가에게나 마찬가지로 의사에게도 필수적인 소양이었던 것이다."]], [["“Raffles, John Raffles,”", "“래플스, 존 래플스입니다,”"], ["said Bulstrode, who hoped that whatever became of Raffles, Lydgate would never know any more of him.", "벌스트로드가 말했는데, 그는 래플스가 어떻게 되든 리드게이트가 그에 관해 더는 아무것도 알지 못하기를 바랐다."]], [["When he had thoroughly examined and considered the patient, Lydgate ordered that he should go to bed, and be kept there in as complete quiet as possible, and then went with Bulstrode into another room.", "환자를 철저히 진찰하고 살펴본 뒤, 리드게이트는 그를 침상에 눕히고 되도록 완전한 안정 속에 두라고 지시한 다음, 벌스트로드와 함께 다른 방으로 갔다."]], [["“It is a serious case, I apprehend,”", "“제 생각에는 심각한 경우인 것 같습니다만,”"], ["said the banker, before Lydgate began to speak.", "리드게이트가 말을 꺼내기도 전에 은행가가 말했다."]], [["“No—and yes,” said Lydgate, half dubiously.", "“아니요 — 그렇기도 합니다,”"], ["“It is difficult to decide as to the possible effect of long-standing complications; but the man had a robust constitution to begin with.", "리드게이트가 반쯤 미심쩍게 말했다. “오래 묵은 합병증이 어떤 영향을 미칠지는 판단하기 어렵습니다."], ["I should not expect this attack to be fatal, though of course the system is in a ticklish state.", "그렇지만 이 사람은 본래 튼튼한 체질이었습니다. 이번 발작이 치명적일 거라고는 보지 않습니다, 물론 몸 상태가 위태로운 지경이긴 하지만요."], ["He should be well watched and attended to.”", "잘 지켜보고 보살펴야 합니다.”"]], [["“I will remain here myself,”", "“제가 직접 여기 남겠습니다,”"], ["said Bulstrode. “Mrs.", "벌스트로드가 말했다."], ["Abel and her husband are inexperienced.", "“에이블 부인과 그 남편은 경험이 없으니까요."], ["I can easily remain here for the night, if you will oblige me by taking a note for Mrs. Bulstrode.”", "벌스트로드 부인에게 쪽지를 전해 주시는 수고만 해 주신다면, 저는 얼마든지 여기서 밤을 새울 수 있습니다.”"]], [["“I should think that is hardly necessary,”", "“그럴 필요까진 거의 없을 듯합니다,”"], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“He seems tame and terrified enough.", "“그는 충분히 온순하고 겁에 질려 있어 보입니다."], ["He might become more unmanageable.", "더 다루기 어려워질 수도 있겠지요."], ["But there is a man here—is there not?”", "하지만 여기 일하는 사람이 있지 않습니까?”"]], [["“I have more than once stayed here a few nights for the sake of seclusion,”", "“저는 한적함을 위해 여기서 며칠 밤을 묵은 적이 한두 번이 아닙니다,”"], ["said Bulstrode, indifferently; “I am quite disposed to do so now.", "벌스트로드가 대수롭지 않다는 듯 말했다. “지금도 얼마든지 그럴 마음이 있습니다."], ["Mrs. Abel and her husband can relieve or aid me, if necessary.”", "필요하면 에이블 부인과 그 남편이 저를 교대해 주거나 거들어 줄 수 있고요.”"]], [["“Very well.", "“좋습니다."], ["Then I need give my directions only to you,”", "그렇다면 제 지시는 당신에게만 하면 되겠군요,”"], ["said Lydgate, not feeling surprised at a little peculiarity in Bulstrode.", "리드게이트가, 벌스트로드의 약간 별난 점에 그리 놀라지 않으며 말했다."]], [["“You think, then, that the case is hopeful?”", "“그렇다면, 가망이 있다고 보십니까?”"], ["said Bulstrode, when Lydgate had ended giving his orders.", "리드게이트가 지시를 마치자 벌스트로드가 말했다."]], [["“Unless there turn out to be further complications, such as I have not at present detected—yes,”", "“현재 제가 발견하지 못한 어떤 추가 합병증이 드러나지 않는 한 — 예, 있습니다,”"], ["said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["“He may pass on to a worse stage; but I should not wonder if he got better in a few days, by adhering to the treatment I have prescribed.", "“더 나쁜 단계로 넘어갈 수도 있지만, 제가 처방한 치료를 잘 지키면 며칠 안에 좋아진다 해도 놀랍지 않을 겁니다."], ["There must be firmness.", "단호함이 있어야 합니다."], ["Remember, if he calls for liquors of any sort, not to give them to him.", "명심하십시오, 그가 어떤 종류든 술을 찾거든 절대로 주지 마십시오."], ["In my opinion, men in his condition are oftener killed by treatment than by the disease.", "제 견해로는, 이런 상태의 사람들은 병보다 치료로 죽는 경우가 더 많습니다."], ["Still, new symptoms may arise.", "그래도 새로운 증상이 나타날 수는 있습니다."], ["I shall come again to-morrow morning.”", "내일 아침에 다시 오겠습니다.”"]], [["After waiting for the note to be carried to Mrs. Bulstrode, Lydgate rode away, forming no conjectures, in the first instance, about the history of Raffles, but rehearsing the whole argument, which had lately been much stirred by the publication of Dr.", "쪽지가 벌스트로드 부인에게 전달되도록 기다린 뒤, 리드게이트는 말을 타고 떠났는데, 처음에는 래플스의 내력에 관해 아무런 추측도 하지 않은 채, 다만 이런 알코올 중독 사례를 다루는 올바른 방법에 관한 논쟁 전체를 머릿속으로 되짚어 보았으니, 그 논쟁은 미국에서 웨어 박사가 쌓은 풍부한 경험이 발표되면서 근래 크게 들끓던 것이었다."], ["Ware’s abundant experience in America, as to the right way of treating cases of alcoholic poisoning such as this.", "리드게이트는 외국에 있을 때 이미 이 문제에 관심을 가졌었다."], ["Lydgate, when abroad, had already been interested in this question: he was strongly convinced against the prevalent practice of allowing alcohol and persistently administering large doses of opium; and he had repeatedly acted on this conviction with a favorable result.", "그는 알코올을 허용하고 다량의 아편을 끈질기게 투여하는 통상적인 관행에 강하게 반대하는 신념을 지녔고, 이 신념에 따라 여러 차례 행동하여 좋은 결과를 얻었던 것이다."]], [["“The man is in a diseased state,”", "“이 사람은 병든 상태이긴 하지만,”"], ["he thought, “but there’s a good deal of wear in him still.", "그는 생각했다. “아직 버틸 힘이 꽤 남아 있어."], ["I suppose he is an object of charity to Bulstrode.", "짐작건대 벌스트로드의 자선 대상이겠지."], ["It is curious what patches of hardness and tenderness lie side by side in men’s dispositions.", "사람의 성품 속에 어찌 그리 모진 구석과 다정한 구석이 나란히 자리하는지 신기한 일이야."], ["Bulstrode seems the most unsympathetic fellow I ever saw about some people, and yet he has taken no end of trouble, and spent a great deal of money, on benevolent objects.", "벌스트로드는 어떤 사람들에게는 내가 본 가장 몰인정한 작자 같은데도, 자선 사업에는 한없는 수고를 들이고 막대한 돈을 써 왔으니 말이지."], ["I suppose he has some test by which he finds out whom Heaven cares for—he has made up his mind that it doesn’t care for me.”", "짐작건대 그에게는 하늘이 누구를 보살피는지 가려내는 어떤 시금석이 있는 모양인데 — 하늘이 나는 보살피지 않는다고 그는 마음을 정해 둔 거지.”"]], [["This streak of bitterness came from a plenteous source, and kept widening in the current of his thought as he neared Lowick Gate.", "이 한 줄기 쓰라림은 풍부한 원천에서 솟아난 것으로, 그가 로윅 게이트에 가까워질수록 생각의 물줄기 속에서 점점 더 넓게 퍼져 갔다."], ["He had not been there since his first interview with Bulstrode in the morning, having been found at the Hospital by the banker’s messenger; and for the first time he was returning to his home without the vision of any expedient in the background which left him a hope of raising money enough to deliver him from the coming destitution of everything which made his married life tolerable—everything which saved him and Rosamond from that bare isolation in which they would be forced to recognize how little of a comfort they could be to each other.", "그는 그날 아침 벌스트로드와의 첫 면담 이후로 그곳에 가지 못했으니, 은행가의 심부름꾼이 그를 병원에서 찾아냈던 것이다. 그리고 그는 난생처음으로, 닥쳐오는 궁핍 — 결혼 생활을 견딜 만하게 해 주던 모든 것의, 그와 로저먼드를 서로에게 얼마나 보잘것없는 위안일 수밖에 없는지 깨닫지 않을 수 없는 그 헐벗은 고립으로부터 그들을 지켜 주던 모든 것의 궁핍 — 에서 자신을 구해 줄 만큼의 돈을 마련할 수 있으리라는 희망을 남겨 주는 어떤 방책의 환영도 마음 한구석에 두지 못한 채 집으로 돌아가는 길이었다."], ["It was more bearable to do without tenderness for himself than to see that his own tenderness could make no amends for the lack of other things to her.", "자기 자신을 위한 다정함 없이 지내는 편이, 자신의 다정함이 그녀에게 다른 것들의 결핍을 결코 메워 줄 수 없음을 보는 것보다 차라리 견딜 만했다."], ["The sufferings of his own pride from humiliations past and to come were keen enough, yet they were hardly distinguishable to himself from that more acute pain which dominated them—the pain of foreseeing that Rosamond would come to regard him chiefly as the cause of disappointment and unhappiness to her.", "지나갔거나 앞으로 닥칠 굴욕으로 인한 그 자신의 자존심의 고통은 충분히 날카로웠으나, 그것은 그 위에 군림하는 한층 더 예리한 고통과 그 자신조차 거의 구별할 수 없었으니 — 로저먼드가 자기를 주로 자기에게 실망과 불행을 안긴 원인으로 여기게 되리라는 것을 예견하는 고통이었다."], ["He had never liked the makeshifts of poverty, and they had never before entered into his prospects for himself; but he was beginning now to imagine how two creatures who loved each other, and had a stock of thoughts in common, might laugh over their shabby furniture, and their calculations how far they could afford butter and eggs.", "그는 가난의 임시변통을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없었고, 그것이 자신의 앞날에 끼어든 적도 이제껏 없었다. 그러나 이제 그는, 서로 사랑하고 생각의 밑천을 공유하는 두 사람이라면 초라한 가구를 두고도, 버터와 달걀을 얼마나 살 수 있을지 셈하면서도 함께 웃을 수 있으리라는 것을 그려 보기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["But the glimpse of that poetry seemed as far off from him as the carelessness of the golden age; in poor Rosamond’s mind there was not room enough for luxuries to look small in.", "그러나 그 시정(詩情)의 한 자락은 그에게 황금시대의 무사태평만큼이나 까마득해 보였다. 가엾은 로저먼드의 마음속에는 사치품들이 하찮게 보일 만큼의 여유 공간이 없었다."], ["He got down from his horse in a very sad mood, and went into the house, not expecting to be cheered except by his dinner, and reflecting that before the evening closed it would be wise to tell Rosamond of his application to Bulstrode and its failure.", "그는 매우 침울한 기분으로 말에서 내려 집 안으로 들어갔으니, 저녁 식사 말고는 무엇으로도 기운이 나리라 기대하지 못한 채, 날이 저물기 전에 자신이 벌스트로드에게 청을 넣었다가 실패한 일을 로저먼드에게 알리는 것이 현명하리라 생각하면서였다."], ["It would be well not to lose time in preparing her for the worst.", "최악의 상황에 대비시키는 데 지체하지 않는 편이 좋을 터였다."]], [["But his dinner waited long for him before he was able to eat it.", "그러나 그의 저녁 식사는, 그가 그것을 먹을 수 있게 되기까지 오래도록 그를 기다려야 했다."], ["For on entering he found that Dover’s agent had already put a man in the house, and when he asked where Mrs. Lydgate was, he was told that she was in her bedroom.", "들어서면서 그는 도버의 대리인이 이미 집 안에 사람을 들여놓은 것을 발견했고, 리드게이트 부인이 어디 있느냐고 묻자, 그녀가 침실에 있다는 대답을 들었던 것이다."], ["He went up and found her stretched on the bed pale and silent, without an answer even in her face to any word or look of his.", "그가 올라가 보니 그녀는 창백하고 말없이 침대에 뻗어 누워, 그의 어떤 말이나 눈길에도 얼굴로조차 아무 응답이 없었다."], ["He sat down by the bed and leaning over her said with almost a cry of prayer—", "그는 침대 곁에 앉아 그녀에게 몸을 숙이고는, 거의 기도의 외침과도 같이 말했다."]], [["“Forgive me for this misery, my poor Rosamond!", "“이 비참함을 용서해 주오, 가엾은 로저먼드!"], ["Let us only love one another.”", "우리 그저 서로 사랑하기만 합시다.”"]], [["She looked at him silently, still with the blank despair on her face; but then the tears began to fill her blue eyes, and her lip trembled.", "그녀는 여전히 얼굴에 공허한 절망을 띤 채 말없이 그를 바라보았다. 그러나 이윽고 그녀의 푸른 눈에 눈물이 차오르기 시작했고, 입술이 떨렸다."], ["The strong man had had too much to bear that day.", "그 강인한 사내는 그날 너무 많은 것을 견뎌야 했다."], ["He let his head fall beside hers and sobbed.", "그는 머리를 그녀의 머리 곁에 떨구고는 흐느꼈다."]], [["He did not hinder her from going to her father early in the morning—it seemed now that he ought not to hinder her from doing as she pleased.", "그는 그녀가 이튿날 아침 일찍 친정아버지에게 가는 것을 막지 않았으니 — 이제는 그녀가 하고 싶은 대로 하는 것을 막아서는 안 된다는 생각이 들었던 것이다."], ["In half an hour she came back, and said that papa and mamma wished her to go and stay with them while things were in this miserable state.", "반 시간쯤 뒤 그녀가 돌아와 말하기를, 이렇게 비참한 형편이 이어지는 동안 아빠와 엄마가 자기더러 친정에 와서 지내라고 하셨다는 것이었다."], ["Papa said he could do nothing about the debt—if he paid this, there would be half-a-dozen more.", "아빠가 말하기를, 빚에 대해서는 자기로서는 어쩔 도리가 없으며 — 이것을 갚아 준다 한들 또 대여섯 건이 더 생길 거라고."], ["She had better come back home again till Lydgate had got a comfortable home for her.", "리드게이트가 그녀에게 편안한 집을 마련해 줄 때까지는 다시 친정으로 돌아오는 편이 낫겠다고."], ["“Do you object, Tertius?”", "“당신 반대하나요, 터셔스?”"]], [["“Do as you like,” said Lydgate.", "“당신 좋을 대로 해요,”"], ["“But things are not coming to a crisis immediately.", "리드게이트가 말했다. “하지만 당장 위기가 닥치는 건 아니오."], ["There is no hurry.”", "서두를 것 없소.”"]], [["“I should not go till to-morrow,”", "“저는 내일까지는 안 가겠어요,”"], ["said Rosamond; “I shall want to pack my clothes.”", "로저먼드가 말했다. “옷가지를 챙겨야 하니까요.”"]], [["“Oh, I would wait a little longer than to-morrow—there is no knowing what may happen,”", "“아, 나라면 내일보다 좀 더 기다리겠소 — 무슨 일이 일어날지 알 수 없으니 말이오,”"], ["said Lydgate, with bitter irony.", "리드게이트가 쓰라린 반어를 담아 말했다."], ["“I may get my neck broken, and that may make things easier to you.”", "“내가 목이라도 부러지면, 당신은 한결 수월해질지도 모르지.”"]], [["It was Lydgate’s misfortune and Rosamond’s too, that his tenderness towards her, which was both an emotional prompting and a well-considered resolve, was inevitably interrupted by these outbursts of indignation either ironical or remonstrant.", "그의 그녀에 대한 다정함은 감정의 충동이자 신중히 다진 결심이기도 했는데, 그것이 이런 반어적이거나 항의 어린 분노의 폭발로 어쩔 수 없이 끊기곤 한다는 것은, 리드게이트의 불행이자 로저먼드의 불행이기도 했다."], ["She thought them totally unwarranted, and the repulsion which this exceptional severity excited in her was in danger of making the more persistent tenderness unacceptable.", "그녀는 그것을 전혀 정당하지 못하다고 여겼고, 이 유별난 가혹함이 그녀 안에 불러일으킨 반감은 그 더 끈질긴 다정함마저 받아들일 수 없게 만들 위험이 있었다."]], [["“I see you do not wish me to go,”", "“당신은 제가 가는 걸 원치 않으시는군요,”"], ["she said, with chill mildness; “why can you not say so, without that kind of violence?", "그녀가 쌀쌀한 부드러움으로 말했다. “그런데 어째서 그런 식의 난폭함 없이 그렇게 말씀하시지 못하나요?"], ["I shall stay until you request me to do otherwise.”", "당신이 다르게 하라고 청하실 때까지 저는 여기 있겠어요.”"]], [["Lydgate said no more, but went out on his rounds.", "리드게이트는 더 말하지 않고 왕진을 나섰다."], ["He felt bruised and shattered, and there was a dark line under his eyes which Rosamond had not seen before.", "그는 멍들고 부서진 듯한 느낌이었고, 두 눈 밑에는 로저먼드가 전에 본 적 없는 거뭇한 줄이 패어 있었다."], ["She could not bear to look at him.", "그녀는 차마 그를 바라볼 수가 없었다."], ["Tertius had a way of taking things which made them a great deal worse for her.", "터셔스에게는 어떤 일이든 그녀에게 한층 더 나쁜 것으로 만들어 버리는 방식이 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXX.", "# CHAPTER LXX."]], [["“Our deeds still travel with us from afar, And what we have been makes us what we are.”", "우리의 행위는 여전히 먼 곳에서 우리와 함께 여행하고, 우리가 어떠했는가가 지금의 우리를 만든다."]], [["Bulstrode’s first object after Lydgate had left Stone Court was to examine Raffles’s pockets, which he imagined were sure to carry signs in the shape of hotel-bills of the places he had stopped in, if he had not told the truth in saying that he had come straight from Liverpool because he was ill and had no money.", "라이드게이트가 스톤 코트를 떠난 뒤 불스트로드가 가장 먼저 한 일은 래플스의 주머니를 뒤지는 것이었다. 자신이 병들고 돈도 없어 리버풀에서 곧장 왔다는 말이 거짓이 아니라면, 그가 묵었던 곳들의 호텔 청구서 같은 흔적이 분명 주머니에서 나오리라고 생각했기 때문이다."], ["There were various bills crammed into his pocketbook, but none of a later date than Christmas at any other place, except one, which bore date that morning.", "그의 수첩 안에는 여러 장의 청구서가 구겨진 채 쑤셔 넣어져 있었으나, 그날 아침 날짜가 찍힌 한 장을 제외하고는 어느 곳에서도 크리스마스 이후로 날짜가 더 늦은 것은 없었다."], ["This was crumpled up with a hand-bill about a horse-fair in one of his tail-pockets, and represented the cost of three days’ stay at an inn at Bilkley, where the fair was held—a town at least forty miles from Middlemarch.", "그날 아침 날짜의 청구서는 그의 뒷주머니 한쪽에서 어느 마시(馬市)에 관한 전단과 함께 구겨져 있었는데, 그 시장이 열리는 빌클리의 한 여인숙에서 사흘간 묵은 비용을 나타내고 있었다. 빌클리는 미들마치에서 적어도 사십 마일은 떨어진 마을이었다."], ["The bill was heavy, and since Raffles had no luggage with him, it seemed probable that he had left his portmanteau behind in payment, in order to save money for his travelling fare; for his purse was empty, and he had only a couple of sixpences and some loose pence in his pockets.", "청구액이 상당한 데다 래플스가 짐 하나 지니고 있지 않은 것으로 보아, 여비를 아끼기 위해 여행 가방을 숙박비 대신 맡기고 떠난 듯했다. 그의 지갑은 비어 있었고, 주머니에는 육 펜스짜리 동전 두어 개와 약간의 잔돈만 들어 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Bulstrode gathered a sense of safety from these indications that Raffles had really kept at a distance from Middlemarch since his memorable visit at Christmas.", "이런 정황들로부터 불스트로드는 래플스가 크리스마스의 그 잊지 못할 방문 이후 정말로 미들마치에서 멀리 떨어져 지냈다는 안도감을 얻었다."], ["At a distance and among people who were strangers to Bulstrode, what satisfaction could there be to Raffles’s tormenting, self-magnifying vein in telling old scandalous stories about a Middlemarch banker?", "멀리 떨어진 곳에서, 불스트로드와는 일면식도 없는 사람들 틈에 있었다면, 미들마치의 한 은행가에 관한 케케묵은 추문을 떠들어 댄다 한들 래플스의 그 괴롭히기 좋아하고 자기를 과시하려는 기질에 무슨 만족이 있겠는가?"], ["And what harm if he did talk?", "또 그가 떠든다 해서 무슨 해가 있겠는가?"], ["The chief point now was to keep watch over him as long as there was any danger of that intelligible raving, that unaccountable impulse to tell, which seemed to have acted towards Caleb Garth; and Bulstrode felt much anxiety lest some such impulse should come over him at the sight of Lydgate.", "이제 중요한 점은, 케일럽 가스에게 작용한 듯 보였던 그 알아들을 만한 헛소리, 그 설명할 길 없는 폭로 충동의 위험이 있는 한 그를 지켜보는 일이었다. 그리고 불스트로드는 라이드게이트를 보면 혹 그런 충동이 그에게 일지 않을까 몹시 불안했다."], ["He sat up alone with him through the night, only ordering the housekeeper to lie down in her clothes, so as to be ready when he called her, alleging his own indisposition to sleep, and his anxiety to carry out the doctor’s orders.", "그는 밤새도록 홀로 그의 곁을 지켰고, 가정부에게는 자신이 부르면 곧장 올 수 있도록 옷을 입은 채 누워 있으라고만 일렀다. 자신이 잠을 이루지 못하는 데다 의사의 지시를 충실히 따르고 싶어서라는 핑계를 댔다."], ["He did carry them out faithfully, although Raffles was incessantly asking for brandy, and declaring that he was sinking away—that the earth was sinking away from under him.", "래플스가 끊임없이 브랜디를 달라고 하며 자신이 가라앉고 있다고, 발밑에서 땅이 꺼져 내려가고 있다고 외쳐 댔지만, 그는 충실히 지시를 이행했다."], ["He was restless and sleepless, but still quailing and manageable.", "래플스는 잠도 못 자고 안절부절못했으나, 여전히 겁에 질려 다루기 쉬운 상태였다."], ["On the offer of the food ordered by Lydgate, which he refused, and the denial of other things which he demanded, he seemed to concentrate all his terror on Bulstrode, imploringly deprecating his anger, his revenge on him by starvation, and declaring with strong oaths that he had never told any mortal a word against him.", "라이드게이트가 지시한 음식을 권하면 거절하고 자기가 요구한 다른 것들이 거부되자, 그는 자신의 모든 공포를 불스트로드에게 집중하는 듯했다. 그의 분노를, 굶겨 죽임으로써 자신에게 복수하려는 마음을 애원하듯 빌며 누그러뜨리려 했고, 누구에게도 그에 관해 한마디도 한 적이 없다고 거센 맹세로 단언했다."], ["Even this Bulstrode felt that he would not have liked Lydgate to hear; but a more alarming sign of fitful alternation in his delirium was, that in-the morning twilight Raffles suddenly seemed to imagine a doctor present, addressing him and declaring that Bulstrode wanted to starve him to death out of revenge for telling, when he never had told.", "이런 말조차도 불스트로드는 라이드게이트가 듣지 않기를 바랐다. 그러나 그의 섬망이 변덕스럽게 오락가락한다는 더 놀라운 징후는, 동틀 무렵의 어스름 속에서 래플스가 갑자기 의사가 곁에 있다고 상상하며, 그에게 말을 걸어 불스트로드가 자기가 발설했다는 이유로 복수하여 굶겨 죽이려 한다고, 사실은 발설한 적이 결코 없는데도 그렇게 단언하는 것이었다."]], [["Bulstrode’s native imperiousness and strength of determination served him well.", "불스트로드의 타고난 위압적인 기질과 강한 결단력이 그를 잘 도왔다."], ["This delicate-looking man, himself nervously perturbed, found the needed stimulus in his strenuous circumstances, and through that difficult night and morning, while he had the air of an animated corpse returned to movement without warmth, holding the mastery by its chill impassibility, his mind was intensely at work thinking of what he had to guard against and what would win him security.", "이 섬세해 보이는 남자는 그 자신도 신경이 곤두서 있었으나, 절박한 상황 속에서 필요한 자극을 발견했다. 그 힘겨운 밤과 아침 내내, 온기 없이 움직임만 되찾은 살아 있는 시체 같은 모습으로, 그 차가운 무표정으로 사태를 장악하고 있는 동안, 그의 마음은 무엇을 경계해야 하며 무엇이 자신에게 안전을 가져다줄지를 골똘히 생각하느라 격렬하게 움직이고 있었다."], ["Whatever prayers he might lift up, whatever statements he might inwardly make of this man’s wretched spiritual condition, and the duty he himself was under to submit to the punishment divinely appointed for him rather than to wish for evil to another—through all this effort to condense words into a solid mental state, there pierced and spread with irresistible vividness the images of the events he desired.", "그가 어떤 기도를 올리든, 이 사람의 비참한 영적 상태와, 타인에게 악이 닥치기를 바라기보다는 신께서 정하신 벌을 자신이 감수해야 할 의무에 관해 마음속으로 어떤 진술을 하든 간에 — 말을 견고한 정신 상태로 응축하려는 이 모든 노력을 뚫고서, 그가 바라는 사건들의 영상이 저항할 수 없는 생생함으로 파고들어 퍼져 나갔다."], ["And in the train of those images came their apology.", "그리고 그 영상들의 행렬을 따라 그 영상들을 변호하는 논리도 따라왔다."], ["He could not but see the death of Raffles, and see in it his own deliverance.", "그는 래플스의 죽음을 보지 않을 수 없었고, 그 속에서 자신의 구원을 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["What was the removal of this wretched creature?", "이 비참한 인간이 사라진다는 것이 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["He was impenitent—but were not public criminals impenitent?—yet the law decided on their fate.", "그는 뉘우치지 않았다 — 하지만 공공의 범죄자들도 뉘우치지 않는 자들이 아니던가? 그래도 법은 그들의 운명을 결정했다."], ["Should Providence in this case award death, there was no sin in contemplating death as the desirable issue—if he kept his hands from hastening it—if he scrupulously did what was prescribed.", "만약 섭리가 이 경우에 죽음을 내린다면, 죽음을 바람직한 결말로 여기는 데에는 죄가 없었다 — 그가 그것을 앞당기는 데 손을 대지 않는다면, 처방된 바를 양심껏 따른다면 말이다. 여기에도 잘못이 있을 수 있었다."], ["Even here there might be a mistake: human prescriptions were fallible things: Lydgate had said that treatment had hastened death,—why not his own method of treatment?", "인간의 처방이란 오류를 범할 수 있는 것이었다. 라이드게이트도 치료가 죽음을 앞당겼다고 하지 않았던가 — 자신의 치료법이라고 그러지 말란 법이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["But of course intention was everything in the question of right and wrong.", "하지만 물론 옳고 그름의 문제에서는 의도가 전부였다."]], [["And Bulstrode set himself to keep his intention separate from his desire.", "그리하여 불스트로드는 자신의 의도를 자신의 욕망과 분리해 두려고 애썼다."], ["He inwardly declared that he intended to obey orders.", "그는 마음속으로 지시에 따를 작정이라고 선언했다."], ["Why should he have got into any argument about the validity of these orders?", "무엇 때문에 그가 그 지시의 타당성을 두고 논쟁에 빠져야 한단 말인가?"], ["It was only the common trick of desire—which avails itself of any irrelevant scepticism, finding larger room for itself in all uncertainty about effects, in every obscurity that looks like the absence of law.", "그것은 욕망이 흔히 부리는 술책에 지나지 않았다 — 욕망은 무관한 회의(懷疑)라면 무엇이든 끌어들이며, 결과에 대한 온갖 불확실성 속에서, 법의 부재처럼 보이는 온갖 모호함 속에서 제 자리를 더 넓게 마련하는 법이다."], ["Still, he did obey the orders.", "그래도 그는 지시에 따랐다."]], [["His anxieties continually glanced towards Lydgate, and his remembrance of what had taken place between them the morning before was accompanied with sensibilities which had not been roused at all during the actual scene.", "그의 불안은 끊임없이 라이드게이트를 향해 곁눈질했고, 전날 아침 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일에 대한 기억에는 그 실제 장면 동안에는 전혀 일지 않았던 감정들이 따라붙었다."], ["He had then cared but little about Lydgate’s painful impressions with regard to the suggested change in the Hospital, or about the disposition towards himself which what he held to be his justifiable refusal of a rather exorbitant request might call forth.", "그때 그는 병원에 제안된 변화에 대한 라이드게이트의 괴로운 심정에 대해서도, 다소 과도한 요청이라고 여겨 거절한 것이 — 그로서는 정당한 거절이라 여겼지만 — 자신에 대해 어떤 마음을 불러일으킬지에 대해서도 거의 신경 쓰지 않았다."], ["He recurred to the scene now with a perception that he had probably made Lydgate his enemy, and with an awakened desire to propitiate him, or rather to create in him a strong sense of personal obligation.", "그러나 이제 그는 그 장면을 되새기면서, 자신이 아마도 라이드게이트를 적으로 만들었으리라는 인식과 함께, 그를 달래고 싶은, 아니 차라리 그에게 개인적인 은혜를 입었다는 강한 느낌을 심어 주고 싶은 욕구가 깨어났다."], ["He regretted that he had not at once made even an unreasonable money-sacrifice.", "그는 즉시, 비록 분에 넘치는 것일지라도 금전적 희생을 치르지 않은 것을 후회했다."], ["For in case of unpleasant suspicions, or even knowledge gathered from the raving of Raffles, Bulstrode would have felt that he had a defence in Lydgate’s mind by having conferred a momentous benefit on him.", "불쾌한 의심이 생기거나, 심지어 래플스의 헛소리에서 사실을 알게 되는 경우라도, 불스트로드는 중대한 은혜를 베풂으로써 라이드게이트의 마음속에 자신의 방어막을 마련해 두었다고 느꼈을 터였다."], ["But the regret had perhaps come too late.", "그러나 그 후회는 어쩌면 너무 늦게 찾아온 것인지도 몰랐다."]], [["Strange, piteous conflict in the soul of this unhappy man, who had longed for years to be better than he was—who had taken his selfish passions into discipline and clad them in severe robes, so that he had walked with them as a devout choir, till now that a terror had risen among them, and they could chant no longer, but threw out their common cries for safety.", "이 불행한 남자의 영혼 속에서 벌어지는 기이하고도 가련한 갈등이여. 그는 여러 해 동안 지금의 자신보다 더 나은 사람이 되기를 갈망했고 — 자신의 이기적인 정념들을 훈련시켜 엄숙한 예복을 입혀 두었기에, 그것들과 함께 경건한 성가대처럼 걸어왔건만, 이제 그 가운데 공포가 일어 그들은 더 이상 성가를 부르지 못하고 안전을 구하는 공통의 비명만을 토해 내고 있었다."]], [["It was nearly the middle of the day before Lydgate arrived: he had meant to come earlier, but had been detained, he said; and his shattered looks were noticed by Balstrode.", "라이드게이트가 도착했을 때는 거의 정오 무렵이었다. 더 일찍 오려 했으나 일이 있어 붙들렸다고 그는 말했다."], ["But he immediately threw himself into the consideration of the patient, and inquired strictly into all that had occurred.", "그의 초췌한 모습을 불스트로드는 알아챘다. 하지만 그는 곧바로 환자를 살피는 데 몰두하여 그동안 일어난 일을 모조리 꼼꼼히 물었다."], ["Raffles was worse, would take hardly any food, was persistently wakeful and restlessly raving; but still not violent.", "래플스는 악화되어 음식을 거의 들지 못했고, 줄곧 잠을 못 이루며 불안하게 헛소리를 해 댔다. 그러나 여전히 난폭하지는 않았다."], ["Contrary to Bulstrode’s alarmed expectation, he took little notice of Lydgate’s presence, and continued to talk or murmur incoherently.", "불스트로드가 불안하게 예상했던 것과는 달리, 그는 라이드게이트가 있는 것을 거의 알아채지 못한 채 계속 두서없이 떠들거나 중얼거렸다."]], [["“What do you think of him?”", "\"그를 어떻게 보십니까?\""], ["said Bulstrode, in private.", "불스트로드가 단둘이 있을 때 물었다."]], [["“The symptoms are worse.”", "\"증상이 더 나빠졌습니다.\""]], [["“You are less hopeful?”", "\"희망이 줄어든 겁니까?\""]], [["“No; I still think he may come round.", "\"아니요. 그래도 회복될 수 있다고 봅니다."], ["Are you going to stay here yourself?”", "선생께서는 여기 머무르실 작정입니까?\""], ["said Lydgate, looking at Bulstrode with an abrupt question, which made him uneasy, though in reality it was not due to any suspicious conjecture.", "라이드게이트가 불스트로드를 바라보며 불쑥 물었는데, 그 질문이 그를 불안하게 했다. 사실 그것은 의심 어린 추측에서 나온 것이 결코 아니었는데도 그러했다."]], [["“Yes, I think so,”", "\"네, 그럴 생각입니다.\""], ["said Bulstrode, governing himself and speaking with deliberation.", "불스트로드가 자신을 다스리며 신중하게 말했다."], ["“Mrs. Bulstrode is advised of the reasons which detain me.", "\"아내에게는 제가 붙들려 있는 사정을 알려 두었습니다."], ["Mrs. Abel and her husband are not experienced enough to be left quite alone, and this kind of responsibility is scarcely included in their service of me.", "에이블 부인과 그 남편은 완전히 맡겨 두기에는 경험이 부족하고, 이런 종류의 책임은 그들이 제게 하는 일에 포함되어 있다고 보기도 어렵습니다."], ["You have some fresh instructions, I presume.”", "새로 지시할 것이 있으시겠지요.\""]], [["The chief new instruction that Lydgate had to give was on the administration of extremely moderate doses of opium, in case of the sleeplessness continuing after several hours.", "라이드게이트가 새로 일러 줄 주된 지시는, 몇 시간이 지나도 불면이 계속될 경우 극히 적은 양의 아편을 투여하라는 것이었다."], ["He had taken the precaution of bringing opium in his pocket, and he gave minute directions to Bulstrode as to the doses, and the point at which they should cease.", "그는 만일을 대비해 아편을 주머니에 넣어 왔고, 투여량과 멈춰야 할 시점에 관해 불스트로드에게 세세히 일러 주었다."], ["He insisted on the risk of not ceasing; and repeated his order that no alcohol should be given.", "그는 멈추지 않을 경우의 위험을 강조했고, 어떤 알코올도 주어서는 안 된다는 지시를 거듭 강조했다."]], [["“From what I see of the case,”", "\"제가 이 환자를 본 바로는,\""], ["he ended, “narcotism is the only thing I should be much afraid of.", "그가 말을 맺었다. \"제가 가장 두려워할 만한 것은 마취 작용뿐입니다."], ["He may wear through even without much food.", "음식을 많이 들지 못하더라도 그는 견뎌 낼 수도 있습니다."], ["There’s a good deal of strength in him.”", "그에게는 상당한 체력이 있어요.\""]], [["“You look ill yourself, Mr. Lydgate—a most unusual, I may say unprecedented thing in my knowledge of you,”", "\"라이드게이트 선생, 선생께서도 안색이 안 좋아 보입니다 — 제가 선생을 알아 온 바로는 매우 드문, 아니 전례가 없는 일이라 해야겠지요.\""], ["said Bulstrode, showing a solicitude as unlike his indifference the day before, as his present recklessness about his own fatigue was unlike his habitual self-cherishing anxiety.", "불스트로드가 전날의 무관심과는 너무도 다른 염려를 내보이며 말했다. 그것은 마치 자신의 피로에 대한 지금의 무심함이 평소의 자기 보전적인 조심성과 너무도 다른 것과 마찬가지였다."], ["“I fear you are harassed.”", "\"선생께서 시달리고 계신 듯하여 걱정입니다.\""]], [["“Yes, I am,”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, brusquely, holding his hat, and ready to go.", "라이드게이트가 모자를 들고 떠날 채비를 하며 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Something new, I fear,”", "\"무언가 새로운 일인 듯하여 걱정입니다.\""], ["said Bulstrode, inquiringly.", "불스트로드가 캐묻듯이 말했다."], ["“Pray be seated.”", "\"부디 앉으시지요.\""]], [["“No, thank you,” said Lydgate, with some hauteur.", "\"아닙니다, 괜찮습니다.\""], ["“I mentioned to you yesterday what was the state of my affairs.", "라이드게이트가 다소 거만하게 말했다. \"어제 제 형편이 어떤지 말씀드렸지요."], ["There is nothing to add, except that the execution has since then been actually put into my house.", "더 보탤 것은 없습니다. 다만 그 후로 실제로 제 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 것뿐입니다."], ["One can tell a good deal of trouble in a short sentence.", "한 문장으로도 꽤 많은 곤경을 전할 수 있군요."], ["I will say good morning.”", "이만 가 보겠습니다.\""]], [["“Stay, Mr. Lydgate, stay,”", "\"잠깐만, 라이드게이트 선생, 잠깐만.\""], ["said Bulstrode; “I have been reconsidering this subject.", "불스트로드가 말했다. \"이 문제를 다시 생각해 보았습니다."], ["I was yesterday taken by surprise, and saw it superficially.", "어제는 갑작스러워서 피상적으로만 보았던 것이지요."], ["Mrs. Bulstrode is anxious for her niece, and I myself should grieve at a calamitous change in your position.", "아내가 자기 조카를 걱정하고, 저 자신도 선생의 처지가 비참하게 바뀌는 것을 슬퍼할 것입니다."], ["Claims on me are numerous, but on reconsideration, I esteem it right that I should incur a small sacrifice rather than leave you unaided.", "제게 청구되는 것들이 많기는 하나, 다시 생각해 보니 선생을 돕지 않은 채 두기보다는 제가 작은 희생을 감수하는 편이 옳다고 여겨집니다."], ["You said, I think, that a thousand pounds would suffice entirely to free you from your burthens, and enable you to recover a firm stand?”", "천 파운드면 선생의 부담에서 완전히 벗어나 다시 굳건히 설 수 있다고 하셨던 것 같은데요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Lydgate, a great leap of joy within him surmounting every other feeling; “that would pay all my debts, and leave me a little on hand.", "라이드게이트가 말했는데, 그의 내면에서 솟구치는 거대한 기쁨의 도약이 다른 모든 감정을 압도했다. \"그러면 제 빚을 모두 갚고도 약간이 손에 남을 겁니다."], ["I could set about economizing in our way of living.", "우리 살림을 절약하는 방향으로 꾸려 갈 수 있겠지요."], ["And by-and-by my practice might look up.”", "그리고 머지않아 제 진료도 나아질 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“If you will wait a moment, Mr. Lydgate, I will draw a check to that amount.", "\"잠시 기다려 주신다면, 라이드게이트 선생, 그 금액으로 수표를 끊어 드리지요."], ["I am aware that help, to be effectual in these cases, should be thorough.”", "이런 경우의 도움이 실효를 거두려면 철저해야 한다는 것을 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["While Bulstrode wrote, Lydgate turned to the window thinking of his home—thinking of his life with its good start saved from frustration, its good purposes still unbroken.", "불스트로드가 수표를 쓰는 동안 라이드게이트는 창가로 돌아서서 자신의 집을 생각했다 — 좌절에서 구원받은 좋은 출발과, 아직 꺾이지 않은 선한 목적을 지닌 자신의 삶을 생각했다."]], [["“You can give me a note of hand for this, Mr. Lydgate,”", "\"이것에 대해 차용증서 한 장을 써 주시면 됩니다, 라이드게이트 선생.\""], ["said the banker, advancing towards him with the check.", "은행가가 수표를 들고 그에게 다가서며 말했다."], ["“And by-and-by, I hope, you may be in circumstances gradually to repay me.", "\"그리고 머지않아 선생께서 차차 형편이 되어 제게 갚을 수 있게 되기를 바랍니다."], ["Meanwhile, I have pleasure in thinking that you will be released from further difficulty.”", "그동안에는 선생께서 더 이상의 곤경에서 벗어나리라 생각하니 기쁩니다.\""]], [["“I am deeply obliged to you,”", "\"깊이 감사드립니다.\""], ["said Lydgate.", "라이드게이트가 말했다."], ["“You have restored to me the prospect of working with some happiness and some chance of good.”", "\"선생께서는 제게 얼마간의 행복과 선을 이룰 얼마간의 기회를 가지고 일할 전망을 되찾아 주셨습니다.\""]], [["It appeared to him a very natural movement in Bulstrode that he should have reconsidered his refusal: it corresponded with the more munificent side of his character.", "불스트로드가 거절을 다시 생각한 것이 그에게는 매우 자연스러운 처사로 보였다. 그것은 그의 성품 가운데 더 너그러운 면과 일치하는 것이었다."], ["But as he put his hack into a canter, that he might get the sooner home, and tell the good news to Rosamond, and get cash at the bank to pay over to Dover’s agent, there crossed his mind, with an unpleasant impression, as from a dark-winged flight of evil augury across his vision, the thought of that contrast in himself which a few months had brought—that he should be overjoyed at being under a strong personal obligation—that he should be overjoyed at getting money for himself from Bulstrode.", "그러나 한시바삐 집에 돌아가 로저먼드에게 좋은 소식을 전하고, 은행에서 현금을 찾아 도버의 대리인에게 건네주려고 말을 빠른 속보로 몰면서, 마치 어두운 날개를 단 흉조의 비행이 시야를 가로지르듯, 불쾌한 인상과 함께 한 가지 생각이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤다 — 몇 달 사이에 자신에게 일어난 그 대조에 대한 생각이었다. 강한 개인적 은혜를 입게 된 것에 자신이 더없이 기뻐하고 있다는 것 — 불스트로드에게서 자신을 위한 돈을 얻게 된 것에 자신이 더없이 기뻐하고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["The banker felt that he had done something to nullify one cause of uneasiness, and yet he was scarcely the easier.", "은행가는 불안의 한 원인을 무효화하는 무언가를 했다고 느꼈으나, 그럼에도 마음이 그다지 편해지지는 않았다."], ["He did not measure the quantity of diseased motive which had made him wish for Lydgate’s good-will, but the quantity was none the less actively there, like an irritating agent in his blood.", "그는 라이드게이트의 호의를 바라게 만든 병든 동기의 양을 가늠하지는 않았으나, 그 양은 그럼에도 여전히 그의 핏속에서 자극제처럼 활발하게 작용하고 있었다."], ["A man vows, and yet will not cast away the means of breaking his vow.", "사람은 맹세를 하면서도 그 맹세를 깨뜨릴 수단을 내던지지 않는다."], ["Is it that he distinctly means to break it?", "그것이 그가 명백히 그 맹세를 깨뜨릴 작정이기 때문일까?"], ["Not at all; but the desires which tend to break it are at work in him dimly, and make their way into his imagination, and relax his muscles in the very moments when he is telling himself over again the reasons for his vow.", "결코 그렇지 않다. 다만 그 맹세를 깨뜨리는 쪽으로 기우는 욕망들이 그의 안에서 어렴풋이 작동하며 그의 상상 속으로 파고들어, 그가 그 맹세의 이유를 스스로에게 거듭 되뇌고 있는 바로 그 순간에 그의 근육을 풀어 놓는 것이다."], ["Raffles, recovering quickly, returning to the free use of his odious powers—how could Bulstrode wish for that?", "래플스가 빠르게 회복하여 그 가증스러운 능력을 다시 마음껏 휘두르게 되는 것 — 불스트로드가 어떻게 그것을 바랄 수 있겠는가?"], ["Raffles dead was the image that brought release, and indirectly he prayed for that way of release, beseeching that, if it were possible, the rest of his days here below might be freed from the threat of an ignominy which would break him utterly as an instrument of God’s service.", "죽은 래플스야말로 해방을 가져다주는 영상이었고, 그는 간접적으로 그 해방의 길을 위해 기도했다. 만약 가능하다면, 이 지상에 남은 자신의 여생이, 신을 섬기는 도구로서의 자신을 완전히 무너뜨릴 그 치욕의 위협에서 벗어나게 해 달라고 간청했다."], ["Lydgate’s opinion was not on the side of promise that this prayer would be fulfilled; and as the day advanced, Bulstrode felt himself getting irritated at the persistent life in this man, whom he would fain have seen sinking into the silence of death: imperious will stirred murderous impulses towards this brute life, over which will, by itself, had no power.", "라이드게이트의 견해는 이 기도가 이루어지리라는 약속의 편에 서 있지 않았다. 그리고 하루가 저물어 가면서 불스트로드는 이 남자의 끈질긴 생명력에 점점 짜증이 나는 것을 느꼈으니, 그는 이 남자가 죽음의 침묵 속으로 가라앉는 것을 보고 싶어 했다."], ["He said inwardly that he was getting too much worn; he would not sit up with the patient to-night, but leave him to Mrs.", "위압적인 의지가 이 짐승 같은 생명에 대한 살의를 부추겼으나, 의지만으로는 그 생명에 아무런 힘을 미치지 못했다. 그는 마음속으로 자신이 너무 지쳤다고 말했다."], ["Abel, who, if necessary, could call her husband.", "오늘 밤은 환자 곁을 지키지 않고, 필요하면 남편을 부를 수 있는 에이블 부인에게 맡길 작정이었다."]], [["At six o’clock, Raffles, having had only fitful perturbed snatches of sleep, from which he waked with fresh restlessness and perpetual cries that he was sinking away, Bulstrode began to administer the opium according to Lydgate’s directions.", "여섯 시 무렵, 래플스가 발작적으로 어수선한 토막잠밖에 자지 못하고 그때마다 깨어나 새로운 불안과 끊임없는 비명으로 자신이 가라앉고 있다고 외쳐 댔으므로, 불스트로드는 라이드게이트의 지시에 따라 아편을 투여하기 시작했다."], ["At the end of half an hour or more he called Mrs. Abel and told her that he found himself unfit for further watching.", "삼십 분쯤 지났을 때, 그는 에이블 부인을 불러 자신이 더 이상 곁을 지킬 수 없는 상태라고 말했다."], ["He must now consign the patient to her care; and he proceeded to repeat to her Lydgate’s directions as to the quantity of each dose. Mrs.", "이제 환자를 그녀의 보살핌에 맡겨야 한다며, 각 회분의 양에 관한 라이드게이트의 지시를 그녀에게 일러 주었다."], ["Abel had not before known anything of Lydgate’s prescriptions; she had simply prepared and brought whatever Bulstrode ordered, and had done what he pointed out to her.", "에이블 부인은 그전까지 라이드게이트의 처방에 관해 아무것도 알지 못했다. 그녀는 그저 불스트로드가 지시하는 것을 준비해 가져왔고, 그가 일러 주는 대로 했을 뿐이었다."], ["She began now to ask what else she should do besides administering the opium.", "그녀는 이제 아편을 투여하는 것 외에 또 무엇을 해야 하는지 묻기 시작했다."]], [["“Nothing at present, except the offer of the soup or the soda-water: you can come to me for further directions.", "\"지금으로서는 수프나 소다수를 권하는 것 말고는 없습니다. 더 지시할 것이 있으면 제게 오시오."], ["Unless there is any important change, I shall not come into the room again to-night.", "무언가 중대한 변화가 없는 한, 오늘 밤은 다시 이 방에 들어오지 않겠습니다."], ["You will ask your husband for help if necessary.", "필요하면 남편에게 도움을 청하시오."], ["I must go to bed early.”", "나는 일찍 자야겠소.\""]], [["“You’ve much need, sir, I’m sure,” said Mrs.", "\"정말이지 그러셔야지요, 나리.\""], ["Abel, “and to take something more strengthening than what you’ve done.”", "에이블 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 지금까지 드신 것보다 더 기운 나는 걸 좀 드셔야겠어요.\""]], [["Bulstrode went away now without anxiety as to what Raffles might say in his raving, which had taken on a muttering incoherence not likely to create any dangerous belief.", "이제 불스트로드는 래플스가 헛소리로 무슨 말을 할지 걱정하지 않고 자리를 떴다. 그의 헛소리는 위험한 믿음을 만들어 낼 것 같지 않은 두서없는 중얼거림으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["At any rate he must risk this.", "어쨌든 이 정도는 감수해야 했다."], ["He went down into the wainscoted parlor first, and began to consider whether he would not have his horse saddled and go home by the moonlight, and give up caring for earthly consequences.", "그는 먼저 판벽을 두른 응접실로 내려가, 말에 안장을 얹게 하여 달빛을 받으며 집으로 돌아가, 이 세상의 결과에 대한 근심을 내려놓아 버릴까 생각하기 시작했다."], ["Then, he wished that he had begged Lydgate to come again that evening.", "그러다 라이드게이트에게 그날 저녁 다시 와 달라고 청했더라면 좋았을 것이라고 생각했다."], ["Perhaps he might deliver a different opinion, and think that Raffles was getting into a less hopeful state.", "어쩌면 그가 다른 견해를 내놓아, 래플스가 희망이 덜한 상태로 접어들고 있다고 여길지도 몰랐다."], ["Should he send for Lydgate?", "라이드게이트를 부르러 보내야 할까?"], ["If Raffles were really getting worse, and slowly dying, Bulstrode felt that he could go to bed and sleep in gratitude to Providence. But was he worse?", "만약 래플스가 정말로 악화되어 서서히 죽어 가고 있다면, 불스트로드는 섭리에 감사하며 잠자리에 들어 잠들 수 있으리라 느꼈다."], ["Lydgate might come and simply say that he was going on as he expected, and predict that he would by-and-by fall into a good sleep, and get well.", "그러나 그가 정말 더 나빠졌을까? 라이드게이트가 와서, 그저 예상대로 진행되고 있다고만 말하며, 머지않아 깊은 잠에 빠져 회복할 것이라고 예언할지도 몰랐다."], ["What was the use of sending for him?", "그를 부르러 보낸들 무슨 소용인가?"], ["Bulstrode shrank from that result.", "불스트로드는 그런 결과 앞에서 움츠러들었다."], ["No ideas or opinions could hinder him from seeing the one probability to be, that Raffles recovered would be just the same man as before, with his strength as a tormentor renewed, obliging him to drag away his wife to spend her years apart from her friends and native place, carrying an alienating suspicion against him in her heart.", "어떤 관념이나 견해도 그가 단 하나의 개연성을 보는 것을 막을 수 없었으니, 그것은 곧 래플스가 회복되면 예전과 똑같은 사람이 되어, 괴롭히는 자로서의 힘을 새로이 갖추고서, 그로 하여금 아내를 친구들과 고향에서 떼어 내어 객지에서 세월을 보내게 하고, 그녀의 마음속에 그를 멀어지게 하는 의혹을 품게 만들리라는 것이었다."]], [["He had sat an hour and a half in this conflict by the firelight only, when a sudden thought made him rise and light the bed-candle, which he had brought down with him.", "그는 오직 벽난로 불빛만 받으며 한 시간 반 동안 이 갈등에 잠겨 앉아 있다가, 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올라 자리에서 일어나 들고 내려왔던 머리맡 촛불에 불을 붙였다."], ["The thought was, that he had not told Mrs. Abel when the doses of opium must cease.", "그 생각이란, 아편 투여를 언제 멈춰야 하는지 에이블 부인에게 말해 두지 않았다는 것이었다."]], [["He took hold of the candlestick, but stood motionless for a long while.", "그는 촛대를 집어 들었으나, 한참 동안 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["She might already have given him more than Lydgate had prescribed.", "그녀는 이미 라이드게이트가 처방한 것보다 더 많이 그에게 주었을지도 몰랐다."], ["But it was excusable in him, that he should forget part of an order, in his present wearied condition.", "하지만 지금처럼 지친 상태에서 지시의 일부를 잊는 것은 용서받을 만한 일이었다."], ["He walked up-stairs, candle in hand, not knowing whether he should straightway enter his own room and go to bed, or turn to the patient’s room and rectify his omission.", "그는 촛불을 손에 들고 위층으로 올라가면서도, 곧장 자기 방으로 들어가 잠자리에 들어야 할지, 아니면 환자의 방으로 가서 자신의 누락을 바로잡아야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["He paused in the passage, with his face turned towards Raffles’s room, and he could hear him moaning and murmuring.", "그는 래플스의 방 쪽으로 얼굴을 향한 채 복도에서 멈춰 섰고, 그가 신음하며 중얼거리는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["He was not asleep, then.", "그렇다면 그는 잠들지 않은 것이었다."], ["Who could know that Lydgate’s prescription would not be better disobeyed than followed, since there was still no sleep?", "아직도 잠이 오지 않는다면, 라이드게이트의 처방을 따르기보다 어기는 편이 더 낫지 않으리라고 누가 장담할 수 있겠는가?"]], [["He turned into his own room.", "그는 자기 방으로 들어섰다."], ["Before he had quite undressed, Mrs.", "옷을 채 다 벗기도 전에 에이블 부인이 문을 두드렸다."], ["Abel rapped at the door; he opened it an inch, so that he could hear her speak low.", "그는 그녀가 낮은 목소리로 말하는 것을 들을 수 있을 만큼만 문을 한 치쯤 열었다."]], [["“If you please, sir, should I have no brandy nor nothing to give the poor creetur?", "\"죄송합니다만, 나리, 저 가엾은 사람에게 브랜디나 뭐라도 줄 게 없을까요?"], ["He feels sinking away, and nothing else will he swaller—and but little strength in it, if he did—only the opium.", "자꾸 가라앉는다고 하면서, 다른 건 아무것도 삼키질 못해요 — 삼킨다 해도 기운이 될 만한 게 없고요 — 아편뿐인걸요."], ["And he says more and more he’s sinking down through the earth.”", "그리고 점점 더 자기가 땅속으로 꺼져 들어간다고 하네요.\""]], [["To her surprise, Mr. Bulstrode did not answer.", "그녀가 놀랍게도 불스트로드 씨는 대답하지 않았다."], ["A struggle was going on within him.", "그의 내면에서 갈등이 벌어지고 있었다."]], [["“I think he must die for want o’ support, if he goes on in that way.", "\"이대로 가다간 기운을 못 차려서 죽고 말 것 같아요."], ["When I nursed my poor master, Mr. Robisson, I had to give him port-wine and brandy constant, and a big glass at a time,”", "제가 가엾은 옛 주인 로비슨 나리를 간호할 때는 포트와인이랑 브랜디를 끊임없이, 한 번에 큰 잔으로 드려야 했답니다.\""], ["added Mrs. Abel, with a touch of remonstrance in her tone.", "에이블 부인이 다소 항의하는 어조로 덧붙였다."]], [["But again Mr. Bulstrode did not answer immediately, and she continued, “It’s not a time to spare when people are at death’s door, nor would you wish it, sir, I’m sure.", "그러나 또다시 불스트로드 씨가 곧바로 대답하지 않자, 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["Else I should give him our own bottle o’ rum as we keep by us.", "\"사람이 죽음의 문턱에 있을 때는 아낄 때가 아니지요, 나리께서도 분명 그러길 바라지 않으실 거예요."], ["But a sitter-up so as you’ve been, and doing everything as laid in your power—”", "안 그러면 제가 우리가 두고 쓰는 럼주 병이라도 그에게 줄 텐데요. 하지만 나리처럼 밤새 곁을 지키시고, 할 수 있는 일은 다 하신 분이—\""]], [["Here a key was thrust through the inch of doorway, and Mr.", "이때 문틈으로 열쇠 하나가 쑥 내밀어졌고, 불스트로드 씨가 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["Bulstrode said huskily, “That is the key of the wine-cooler.", "\"이것이 술 보관함 열쇠요."], ["You will find plenty of brandy there.”", "거기 브랜디가 많이 있을 거요.\""]], [["Early in the morning—about six—Mr. Bulstrode rose and spent some time in prayer.", "이튿날 이른 아침 — 여섯 시쯤 — 불스트로드 씨는 일어나 한동안 기도하며 시간을 보냈다."], ["Does any one suppose that private prayer is necessarily candid—necessarily goes to the roots of action?", "사사로운 기도가 반드시 진솔하다고, 반드시 행위의 뿌리까지 닿는다고 누가 생각하겠는가?"], ["Private prayer is inaudible speech, and speech is representative: who can represent himself just as he is, even in his own reflections?", "사사로운 기도란 소리 없는 말이며, 말이란 무언가를 대표하는 것이다. 자기 자신의 성찰 속에서조차, 누가 있는 그대로의 자신을 대표할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Bulstrode had not yet unravelled in his thought the confused promptings of the last four-and-twenty hours.", "불스트로드는 아직 지난 스물네 시간의 어지러운 충동들을 마음속에서 풀어내지 못한 상태였다."]], [["He listened in the passage, and could hear hard stertorous breathing.", "그는 복도에서 귀를 기울였고, 거칠게 코를 고는 듯한 숨소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["Then he walked out in the garden, and looked at the early rime on the grass and fresh spring leaves.", "그러고는 정원으로 걸어 나가, 풀잎과 싱그러운 봄 새잎에 내린 이른 서리를 바라보았다."], ["When he re-entered the house, he felt startled at the sight of Mrs. Abel.", "다시 집 안으로 들어섰을 때, 그는 에이블 부인의 모습을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["“How is your patient—asleep, I think?”", "\"환자는 좀 어떻소 — 자고 있는 것 같은데?\""], ["he said, with an attempt at cheerfulness in his tone.", "그가 짐짓 밝은 어조를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“He’s gone very deep, sir,”", "\"아주 깊이 들었어요, 나리.\""], ["said Mrs. Abel.", "에이블 부인이 말했다."], ["“He went off gradual between three and four o’clock.", "\"세 시에서 네 시 사이에 차츰차츰 잠들었답니다."], ["Would you please to go and look at him?", "가서 좀 보시겠어요?"], ["I thought it no harm to leave him.", "그를 두고 와도 괜찮겠다 싶어서요."], ["My man’s gone afield, and the little girl’s seeing to the kettles.”", "제 남편은 들에 나갔고, 어린 계집아이가 주전자를 보고 있거든요.\""]], [["Bulstrode went up.", "불스트로드는 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["At a glance he knew that Raffles was not in the sleep which brings revival, but in the sleep which streams deeper and deeper into the gulf of death.", "한눈에 그는 래플스가 소생을 가져오는 잠이 아니라, 죽음의 심연 속으로 점점 더 깊이 흘러 내려가는 잠에 빠져 있음을 알아보았다."]], [["He looked round the room and saw a bottle with some brandy in it, and the almost empty opium phial.", "그는 방을 둘러보다가 브랜디가 조금 남은 병과 거의 빈 아편 약병을 보았다."], ["He put the phial out of sight, and carried the brandy-bottle down-stairs with him, locking it again in the wine-cooler.", "그는 약병을 보이지 않는 곳에 치우고, 브랜디 병을 들고 아래층으로 내려가 다시 술 보관함에 넣고 잠갔다."]], [["While breakfasting he considered whether he should ride to Middlemarch at once, or wait for Lydgate’s arrival.", "아침을 먹으면서 그는 곧장 미들마치로 말을 타고 갈지, 아니면 라이드게이트가 오기를 기다릴지 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["He decided to wait, and told Mrs. Abel that she might go about her work—he could watch in the bed-chamber.", "그는 기다리기로 했고, 에이블 부인에게 일을 보러 가도 좋다고 말했다 — 자신이 침실에서 지켜볼 수 있다는 것이었다."]], [["As he sat there and beheld the enemy of his peace going irrevocably into silence, he felt more at rest than he had done for many months.", "그가 그곳에 앉아 자신의 평안을 위협하던 적이 돌이킬 수 없이 침묵 속으로 들어가는 것을 지켜보면서, 그는 여러 달 동안 느껴 보지 못한 평온을 느꼈다."], ["His conscience was soothed by the enfolding wing of secrecy, which seemed just then like an angel sent down for his relief.", "비밀이라는 감싸 안는 날개가 그의 양심을 달래 주었으니, 그것은 바로 그 순간 그를 구원하기 위해 내려온 천사처럼 여겨졌다."], ["He drew out his pocket-book to review various memoranda there as to the arrangements he had projected and partly carried out in the prospect of quitting Middlemarch, and considered how far he would let them stand or recall them, now that his absence would be brief.", "그는 수첩을 꺼내, 미들마치를 떠날 것을 예상하고 계획하여 일부 실행에 옮겼던 여러 사항에 관한 메모들을 검토했고, 이제 자신의 부재가 잠시뿐이게 되었으니 그것들을 그대로 둘지 아니면 거둬들일지를 어디까지 정해야 할지 헤아렸다."], ["Some economies which he felt desirable might still find a suitable occasion in his temporary withdrawal from management, and he hoped still that Mrs. Casaubon would take a large share in the expenses of the Hospital.", "그가 바람직하다고 여기는 몇 가지 절약 방안은, 그가 잠시 경영에서 물러나 있는 동안에도 여전히 적당한 기회를 찾을 수 있을 터였고, 그는 여전히 캐소본 부인이 병원 비용의 상당 부분을 떠맡아 주기를 바랐다."], ["In that way the moments passed, until a change in the stertorous breathing was marked enough to draw his attention wholly to the bed, and forced him to think of the departing life, which had once been subservient to his own—which he had once been glad to find base enough for him to act on as he would.", "그렇게 시간이 흘러가다가, 코 고는 듯한 숨소리의 변화가 그의 주의를 온통 침대로 끌어당길 만큼 뚜렷해졌고, 그로 하여금 떠나가는 그 생명을 생각하지 않을 수 없게 만들었다. 한때 자신에게 종속되어 있던 생명 — 한때는 자신이 마음대로 이용할 수 있을 만큼 비천하다는 것을 발견하고 기뻐했던 생명이었다."], ["It was his gladness then which impelled him now to be glad that the life was at an end.", "그때의 그 기쁨이야말로 이제 그로 하여금 그 생명이 끝나가는 것을 기뻐하게 만든 것이었다."]], [["And who could say that the death of Raffles had been hastened?", "그리고 래플스의 죽음이 앞당겨졌다고 누가 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Who knew what would have saved him?", "무엇이 그를 살릴 수 있었을지 누가 알겠는가?"]], [["Lydgate arrived at half-past ten, in time to witness the final pause of the breath.", "라이드게이트는 열 시 반에 도착하여, 마지막 숨이 멎는 순간을 목격하기에 알맞은 때였다."], ["When he entered the room Bulstrode observed a sudden expression in his face, which was not so much surprise as a recognition that he had not judged correctly.", "그가 방에 들어섰을 때 불스트로드는 그의 얼굴에 떠오르는 갑작스러운 표정을 보았는데, 그것은 놀라움이라기보다 자신이 옳게 판단하지 못했음을 깨닫는 표정이었다."], ["He stood by the bed in silence for some time, with his eyes turned on the dying man, but with that subdued activity of expression which showed that he was carrying on an inward debate.", "그는 한동안 죽어 가는 사람에게 눈길을 둔 채 말없이 침대 곁에 서 있었으나, 그 표정에는 내면의 논쟁을 이어 가고 있음을 보여 주는 억눌린 활기가 어려 있었다."]], [["“When did this change begin?”", "\"이 변화가 언제 시작되었습니까?\""], ["said he, looking at Bulstrode.", "그가 불스트로드를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I did not watch by him last night,”", "\"어젯밤에는 제가 곁을 지키지 않았습니다.\""], ["said Bulstrode.", "불스트로드가 말했다."], ["“I was over-worn, and left him under Mrs.", "\"너무 지쳐서 에이블 부인에게 맡겼지요."], ["Abel’s care. She said that he sank into sleep between three and four o’clock.", "그녀 말로는 세 시에서 네 시 사이에 그가 잠에 빠져들었다고 합니다."], ["When I came in before eight he was nearly in this condition.”", "제가 여덟 시 전에 들어와 보니 거의 이런 상태였습니다.\""]], [["Lydgate did not ask another question, but watched in silence until he said, “It’s all over.”", "라이드게이트는 더 이상 묻지 않고, 말없이 지켜보다가 입을 열었다. \"다 끝났습니다.\""]], [["This morning Lydgate was in a state of recovered hope and freedom.", "그날 아침 라이드게이트는 희망과 자유를 되찾은 상태였다."], ["He had set out on his work with all his old animation, and felt himself strong enough to bear all the deficiencies of his married life.", "그는 예전의 그 모든 활기를 띠고 자신의 일에 나섰고, 결혼 생활의 온갖 결핍을 견뎌 낼 만큼 자신이 강하다고 느꼈다."], ["And he was conscious that Bulstrode had been a benefactor to him.", "그리고 그는 불스트로드가 자신에게 은인이 되었다는 것을 의식하고 있었다."], ["But he was uneasy about this case.", "그러나 그는 이 환자의 일이 마음에 걸렸다."], ["He had not expected it to terminate as it had done.", "그는 일이 이렇게 끝나리라고는 예상하지 못했다."], ["Yet he hardly knew how to put a question on the subject to Bulstrode without appearing to insult him; and if he examined the housekeeper—why, the man was dead.", "그러나 그는 불스트로드에게 그를 모욕하는 것처럼 보이지 않으면서 이 일에 관해 어떻게 질문을 던져야 할지 알 수 없었다. 또 가정부를 캐묻는다 한들 — 그 남자는 이미 죽었으니."], ["There seemed to be no use in implying that somebody’s ignorance or imprudence had killed him.", "누군가의 무지나 경솔함이 그를 죽게 했다고 넌지시 비치는 것은 아무 소용이 없을 듯했다."], ["And after all, he himself might be wrong.", "게다가 결국 자기 자신이 틀렸을 수도 있었다."]], [["He and Bulstrode rode back to Middlemarch together, talking of many things—chiefly cholera and the chances of the Reform Bill in the House of Lords, and the firm resolve of the political Unions.", "그와 불스트로드는 함께 미들마치로 말을 타고 돌아오면서 여러 가지 이야기를 나누었다 — 주로 콜레라와 상원에서 개혁 법안이 통과될 가망성, 그리고 정치 연맹들의 굳은 결의에 관한 것이었다."], ["Nothing was said about Raffles, except that Bulstrode mentioned the necessity of having a grave for him in Lowick churchyard, and observed that, so far as he knew, the poor man had no connections, except Rigg, whom he had stated to be unfriendly towards him.", "래플스에 관해서는 아무 말도 나오지 않았으나, 다만 불스트로드가 그를 로익 교회 묘지에 매장할 무덤이 필요하다는 것을 언급하며, 자신이 아는 한 그 가엾은 사람은 그에게 적대적이라고 그가 진술한 바 있는 릭을 제외하고는 친척이 없었다고 말했다."]], [["On returning home Lydgate had a visit from Mr. Farebrother.", "집에 돌아온 라이드게이트는 페어브러더 씨의 방문을 받았다."], ["The Vicar had not been in the town the day before, but the news that there was an execution in Lydgate’s house had got to Lowick by the evening, having been carried by Mr.", "목사는 전날 읍내에 나오지 않았으나, 라이드게이트의 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 소식이 저녁 무렵 로익까지 전해졌다."], ["Spicer, shoemaker and parish-clerk, who had it from his brother, the respectable bell-hanger in Lowick Gate.", "구두장이이자 교구 서기인 스파이서 씨가 그 소식을 옮긴 것이었는데, 그는 로익 게이트에서 종을 다는 점잖은 직공인 자기 형에게서 그 소식을 들었다."], ["Since that evening when Lydgate had come down from the billiard room with Fred Vincy, Mr. Farebrother’s thoughts about him had been rather gloomy.", "라이드게이트가 프레드 빈시와 함께 당구장에서 내려오던 그날 저녁 이후로, 페어브러더 씨가 그에 관해 품은 생각은 다소 어두웠다."], ["Playing at the Green Dragon once or oftener might have been a trifle in another man; but in Lydgate it was one of several signs that he was getting unlike his former self.", "그린 드래건에서 한두 번 노름을 한 것은 다른 사람에게는 사소한 일일 수도 있었으나, 라이드게이트에게는 그가 예전의 자신과 달라지고 있다는 여러 징후 가운데 하나였다."], ["He was beginning to do things for which he had formerly even an excessive scorn.", "그는 예전 같았으면 지나칠 만큼 경멸했을 일들을 하기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Whatever certain dissatisfactions in marriage, which some silly tinklings of gossip had given him hints of, might have to do with this change, Mr. Farebrother felt sure that it was chiefly connected with the debts which were being more and more distinctly reported, and he began to fear that any notion of Lydgate’s having resources or friends in the background must be quite illusory.", "어리석은 소문의 짤랑거림이 그에게 슬쩍 귀띔해 준, 결혼 생활의 어떤 불만들이 이 변화와 어떤 관련이 있든 간에, 페어브러더 씨는 그것이 주로 점점 더 분명하게 떠도는 빚과 연관되어 있음을 확신했고, 라이드게이트가 뒤에 어떤 재력이나 친구를 두고 있으리라는 어떤 생각도 전혀 허황된 것일지 모른다는 두려움이 들기 시작했다."], ["The rebuff he had met with in his first attempt to win Lydgate’s confidence, disinclined him to a second; but this news of the execution being actually in the house, determined the Vicar to overcome his reluctance.", "라이드게이트의 신뢰를 얻으려던 첫 시도에서 퇴짜를 맞은 일이 그로 하여금 두 번째 시도를 꺼리게 만들었으나, 실제로 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 이 소식이 목사로 하여금 자신의 망설임을 극복하게 했다."]], [["Lydgate had just dismissed a poor patient, in whom he was much interested, and he came forward to put out his hand—with an open cheerfulness which surprised Mr. Farebrother.", "라이드게이트는 마침 자신이 무척 관심을 두고 있던 한 가난한 환자를 막 돌려보낸 참이었고, 페어브러더 씨를 놀라게 할 만큼 거리낌 없는 명랑함으로 손을 내밀러 나왔다."], ["Could this too be a proud rejection of sympathy and help?", "이것 역시 동정과 도움에 대한 오만한 거절일까?"], ["Never mind; the sympathy and help should be offered.", "상관없었다. 동정과 도움은 베풀어져야 했다."]], [["“How are you, Lydgate?", "\"잘 지내셨소, 라이드게이트?"], ["I came to see you because I had heard something which made me anxious about you,”", "선생에 관해 걱정스러운 이야기를 들어서 보러 왔소.\""], ["said the Vicar, in the tone of a good brother, only that there was no reproach in it.", "목사가 좋은 형 같은 어조로 말했는데, 다만 거기에는 책망이 담겨 있지 않았다."], ["They were both seated by this time, and Lydgate answered immediately—", "이때쯤 두 사람은 다 자리에 앉아 있었고, 라이드게이트가 곧바로 대답했다 —"]], [["“I think I know what you mean.", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 알 것 같습니다."], ["You had heard that there was an execution in the house?”", "제 집에 차압이 들어왔다는 이야기를 들으셨군요?\""]], [["“Yes; is it true?”", "\"그렇소. 사실이오?\""]], [["“It was true,”", "\"사실이었습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, with an air of freedom, as if he did not mind talking about the affair now.", "라이드게이트가, 이제는 그 일을 이야기해도 개의치 않는다는 듯 거리낌 없는 태도로 말했다."], ["“But the danger is over; the debt is paid.", "\"하지만 위기는 지나갔습니다. 빚은 갚았어요."], ["I am out of my difficulties now: I shall be freed from debts, and able, I hope, to start afresh on a better plan.”", "이제 곤경에서 벗어났습니다. 빚에서 풀려나, 바라건대 더 나은 계획으로 새로 시작할 수 있게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“I am very thankful to hear it,”", "\"그 말을 들으니 정말 다행이오.\""], ["said the Vicar, falling back in his chair, and speaking with that low-toned quickness which often follows the removal of a load.", "목사가 의자에 등을 기대며, 짐을 내려놓은 뒤에 흔히 뒤따르는 그 낮고 빠른 어조로 말했다."], ["“I like that better than all the news in the ‘Times.’ I confess I came to you with a heavy heart.”", "\"「타임스」의 그 어떤 소식보다 그 말이 더 좋소. 솔직히 말하면 무거운 마음으로 선생을 찾아왔다오.\""]], [["“Thank you for coming,”", "\"와 주셔서 감사합니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, cordially.", "라이드게이트가 진심으로 말했다."], ["“I can enjoy the kindness all the more because I am happier.", "\"제가 더 행복해졌기에 그 친절을 더욱 만끽할 수 있습니다."], ["I have certainly been a good deal crushed.", "분명 저는 적잖이 짓눌렸지요."], ["I’m afraid I shall find the bruises still painful by-and by,”", "그 멍이 머지않아 여전히 쓰라리게 느껴질까 봐 걱정입니다.\""], ["he added, smiling rather sadly; “but just now I can only feel that the torture-screw is off.”", "그가 다소 서글프게 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"하지만 지금 이 순간은 그저 고문하던 나사가 풀렸다는 것만 느껴질 뿐입니다.\""]], [["Mr. Farebrother was silent for a moment, and then said earnestly, “My dear fellow, let me ask you one question.", "페어브러더 씨는 잠시 말이 없다가 진지하게 입을 열었다."], ["Forgive me if I take a liberty.”", "\"이보게, 한 가지만 물어봅시다. 결례를 범하더라도 용서해 주오.\""]], [["“I don’t believe you will ask anything that ought to offend me.”", "\"제 마음을 상하게 할 만한 것은 아무것도 묻지 않으시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["“Then—this is necessary to set my heart quite at rest—you have not—have you?—in order to pay your debts, incurred another debt which may harass you worse hereafter?”", "\"그러면 — 이건 내 마음을 완전히 놓기 위해 필요한 것이오 — 선생은 혹시 — 그러지 않았소? — 빚을 갚기 위해, 나중에 더 심하게 선생을 괴롭힐지도 모를 또 다른 빚을 지지는 않았소?\""]], [["“No,” said Lydgate, coloring slightly.", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["“There is no reason why I should not tell you—since the fact is so—that the person to whom I am indebted is Bulstrode.", "라이드게이트가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"사실이 그러하니 말씀드리지 못할 이유가 없겠지요 — 제가 빚을 진 사람은 불스트로드입니다."], ["He has made me a very handsome advance—a thousand pounds—and he can afford to wait for repayment.”", "그가 제게 아주 후한 선금을 빌려주었어요 — 천 파운드를요 — 그리고 그는 상환을 느긋이 기다릴 여유가 있는 사람입니다.\""]], [["“Well, that is generous,” said Mr.", "\"음, 그건 너그러운 처사로군.\""], ["Farebrother, compelling himself to approve of the man whom he disliked.", "페어브러더 씨가, 자신이 싫어하는 그 남자를 억지로 인정하며 말했다."], ["His delicate feeling shrank from dwelling even in his thought on the fact that he had always urged Lydgate to avoid any personal entanglement with Bulstrode.", "그의 섬세한 감정은 자신이 늘 라이드게이트에게 불스트로드와의 어떤 개인적 얽힘도 피하라고 권했다는 사실을 생각 속에서조차 곱씹기를 꺼렸다. 그는 곧바로 덧붙였다."], ["He added immediately, “And Bulstrode must naturally feel an interest in your welfare, after you have worked with him in a way which has probably reduced your income instead of adding to it.", "\"그리고 불스트로드로서는 당연히 선생의 안녕에 관심을 느낄 만하지. 선생이 그와 함께 일하면서, 아마도 수입을 늘리기는커녕 오히려 줄였을 테니 말이오."], ["I am glad to think that he has acted accordingly.”", "그가 그에 걸맞게 행동했다고 생각하니 기쁘오.\""]], [["Lydgate felt uncomfortable under these kindly suppositions.", "라이드게이트는 이런 친절한 추측들 앞에서 불편함을 느꼈다."], ["They made more distinct within him the uneasy consciousness which had shown its first dim stirrings only a few hours before, that Bulstrode’s motives for his sudden beneficence following close upon the chillest indifference might be merely selfish.", "그것들은 불과 몇 시간 전에야 처음으로 어렴풋이 꿈틀거리기 시작했던 그 불안한 의식을, 곧 불스트로드가 더없이 냉랭한 무관심을 보인 직후에 갑작스레 베푼 호의의 동기가 어쩌면 한낱 이기적인 것일지도 모른다는 의식을 그의 안에서 더욱 또렷하게 만들었다."], ["He let the kindly suppositions pass.", "그는 그 친절한 추측들이 그냥 지나가도록 두었다."], ["He could not tell the history of the loan, but it was more vividly present with him than ever, as well as the fact which the Vicar delicately ignored—that this relation of personal indebtedness to Bulstrode was what he had once been most resolved to avoid.", "그는 그 대출의 내력을 이야기할 수는 없었으나, 그것은 그 어느 때보다 생생하게 그의 마음에 떠올라 있었고, 목사가 짐짓 모른 체한 그 사실 — 불스트로드에게 개인적으로 빚을 지는 이 관계야말로 그가 한때 가장 단호히 피하고자 했던 것이라는 사실 또한 그러했다."]], [["He began, instead of answering, to speak of his projected economies, and of his having come to look at his life from a different point of view.", "그는 대답하는 대신, 자신이 계획한 절약 방안에 관해, 그리고 자신이 삶을 다른 관점에서 바라보게 되었다는 것에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“I shall set up a surgery,”", "\"진료실을 차릴 작정입니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I really think I made a mistaken effort in that respect.", "\"사실 그 점에서는 제가 잘못된 노력을 했던 것 같습니다."], ["And if Rosamond will not mind, I shall take an apprentice.", "그리고 로저먼드가 꺼리지 않는다면, 견습생을 하나 둘 생각입니다."], ["I don’t like these things, but if one carries them out faithfully they are not really lowering.", "이런 일들이 마음에 들지는 않지만, 충실히 해낸다면 정말로 품위를 떨어뜨리는 것은 아니지요."], ["I have had a severe galling to begin with: that will make the small rubs seem easy.”", "시작부터 호된 시련을 겪었으니, 그 덕에 작은 마찰들은 쉬워 보일 겁니다.\""]], [["Poor Lydgate! the “if Rosamond will not mind,”", "가엾은 라이드게이트여!"], ["which had fallen from him involuntarily as part of his thought, was a significant mark of the yoke he bore. But Mr.", "그의 생각의 일부로서 무심코 흘러나온 그 \"로저먼드가 꺼리지 않는다면\"이라는 말은 그가 짊어진 멍에를 보여 주는 의미심장한 표시였다."], ["Farebrother, whose hopes entered strongly into the same current with Lydgate’s, and who knew nothing about him that could now raise a melancholy presentiment, left him with affectionate congratulation.", "그러나 자신의 희망이 라이드게이트의 희망과 강하게 같은 흐름을 타고 있던 페어브러더 씨는, 이제 우울한 예감을 불러일으킬 만한 어떤 것도 그에 관해 알지 못했기에, 다정한 축하의 말을 남기고 그를 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXI.", "# CHAPTER LXXI."]], [["Clown. . . . ’Twas in the Bunch of Grapes, where, indeed, you have a delight to sit, have you not? Froth. I have so: because it is an open room, and good for winter. Clo. Why, very well then: I hope here be truths. —Measure for Measure.", "어릿광대. ……그게 포도송이 술집에서였지요, 거기 정말이지\n당신이 즐겨 앉는 자리가 있잖소, 안 그렇소?"]], [["Five days after the death of Raffles, Mr. Bambridge was standing at his leisure under the large archway leading into the yard of the Green Dragon. He was not fond of solitary contemplation, but he had only just come out of the house, and any human figure standing at ease under the archway in the early afternoon was as certain to attract companionship as a pigeon which has found something worth pecking at. In this case there was no material object to feed upon, but the eye of reason saw a probability of mental sustenance in the shape of gossip. Mr. Hopkins, the meek-mannered draper opposite, was the first to act on this inward vision, being the more ambitious of a little masculine talk because his customers were chiefly women. Mr. Bambridge was rather curt to the draper, feeling that Hopkins was of course glad to talk to him, but that he was not going to waste much of his talk on Hopkins. Soon, however, there was a small cluster of more important listeners, who were either deposited from the passers-by, or had sauntered to the spot expressly to see if there were anything going on at the Green Dragon; and Mr. Bambridge was finding it worth his while to say many impressive things about the fine studs he had been seeing and the purchases he had made on a journey in the north from which he had just returned. Gentlemen present were assured that when they could show him anything to cut out a blood mare, a bay, rising four, which was to be seen at Doncaster if they chose to go and look at it, Mr. Bambridge would gratify them by being shot “from here to Hereford.” Also, a pair of blacks which he was going to put into the break recalled vividly to his mind a pair which he had sold to Faulkner in ’19, for a hundred guineas, and which Faulkner had sold for a hundred and sixty two months later—any gent who could disprove this statement being offered the privilege of calling Mr. Bambridge by a very ugly name until the exercise made his throat dry.", "거품. 그렇소이다. 거긴 트인 방이라 겨울에 좋으니까요. 어릿광대. 아, 그렇다면 아주 좋소. 여기서 진실이 밝혀지길 바라오."]], [["When the discourse was at this point of animation, came up Mr. Frank Hawley. He was not a man to compromise his dignity by lounging at the Green Dragon, but happening to pass along the High Street and seeing Bambridge on the other side, he took some of his long strides across to ask the horsedealer whether he had found the first-rate gig-horse which he had engaged to look for. Mr. Hawley was requested to wait until he had seen a gray selected at Bilkley: if that did not meet his wishes to a hair, Bambridge did not know a horse when he saw it, which seemed to be the highest conceivable unlikelihood. Mr. Hawley, standing with his back to the street, was fixing a time for looking at the gray and seeing it tried, when a horseman passed slowly by.", "―『자에는 자로』\n* 『자에는 자로』: 셰익스피어의 희극."]], [["“Bulstrode!” said two or three voices at once in a low tone, one of them, which was the draper’s, respectfully prefixing the “Mr.;” but nobody having more intention in this interjectural naming than if they had said “the Riverston coach” when that vehicle appeared in the distance. Mr. Hawley gave a careless glance round at Bulstrode’s back, but as Bambridge’s eyes followed it he made a sarcastic grimace.", "래플스가 죽고 닷새 뒤, 뱀브리지 씨는 그린 드래건 마구간 마당으로 통하는 커다란 아치문 아래 한가로이 서 있었다. 그는 홀로 사색에 잠기는 것을 즐기는 사람이 아니었으나, 방금 집에서 나온 참이었고, 이른 오후 아치문 아래 편안히 서 있는 사람이라면 누구든, 쪼아 먹을 만한 무언가를 발견한 비둘기처럼 어김없이 동행을 끌어모으게 마련이었다. 이 경우에는 양식이 될 만한 실체적인 대상은 없었으나, 이성의 눈은 잡담이라는 형태로 정신의 양식을 얻을 가능성을 알아보았다. 맞은편의 온순한 포목상 홉킨스 씨가 이 내적 통찰에 가장 먼저 따라 움직였으니, 그의 손님이 주로 여자들인 까닭에 사내들끼리의 대화를 더욱 갈망했던 것이다. 뱀브리지 씨는 포목상에게 다소 퉁명스러웠는데, 홉킨스가 자기와 이야기하게 되어 당연히 기뻐하리라고 느끼면서도, 자신은 홉킨스에게 말을 많이 허비할 생각이 없었던 것이다. 그러나 곧 더 중요한 청중이 작은 무리를 이루었으니, 지나가던 행인들 가운데 떨어져 나온 이들이거나, 그린 드래건에 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 일부러 보러 어슬렁어슬렁 다가온 이들이었다. 그리고 뱀브리지 씨는, 자신이 보고 온 훌륭한 종마들과 방금 돌아온 북부 여행에서 사들인 말들에 관해 인상적인 이야기를 늘어놓을 만한 가치가 있음을 깨닫고 있었다. 그 자리에 있던 신사들은 만약 누군가가 마음만 먹으면 돈캐스터에서 볼 수 있는, 곧 네 살이 되는 적갈색 순종 암말을 능가할 말을 그에게 보여 줄 수 있다면 자기가 \"여기서 헤리퍼드까지\" 총에 맞아도 좋다고 그들을 흡족하게 해 주겠다는 다짐을 들었다. 또한 그가 사륜마차에 매려는 검은 말 한 쌍은, 19년에 자기가 포크너에게 백 기니를 받고 팔았던, 그리고 포크너가 두 달 뒤에 백육십 기니에 되팔았던 한 쌍을 그의 머릿속에 생생하게 떠올리게 했으니―이 말을 반증할 수 있는 신사가 있다면, 목이 마를 때까지 마음껏 뱀브리지 씨를 아주 흉한 이름으로 불러도 좋다는 특권을 주겠노라 했다. 이야기가 이렇게 한창 무르익었을 때, 프랭크 홀리 씨가 다가왔다. 그는 그린 드래건에서 빈둥거리며 자기 품위를 떨어뜨릴 사람은 아니었으나, 마침 하이 스트리트를 지나다가 길 건너편의 뱀브리지를 보고는, 자기가 찾아봐 달라고 부탁한 일급 이륜마차용 말을 구했는지 물어보려고 성큼성큼 길을 건너왔다. 홀리 씨는 빌클리에서 골라 놓은 회색 말을 볼 때까지 기다려 달라는 청을 받았다. 만약 그 말이 그의 바람에 털끝만큼이라도 맞지 않는다면, 뱀브리지는 말을 보고도 말을 알아보지 못하는 사람이라는 것인데, 그것이야말로 상상할 수 있는 가장 있을 법하지 않은 일로 보였다. 길을 등지고 선 홀리 씨가 그 회색 말을 보고 시승해 볼 시간을 정하고 있을 때, 말을 탄 사람 하나가 천천히 지나갔다."]], [["“By jingo!", "\"불스트로드!\""], ["that reminds me,”", "두세 사람이 동시에 나직한 목소리로 말했는데, 그중 포목상의 목소리는 공손하게 \"씨\"를 앞에 붙였다."], ["he began, lowering his voice a little, “I picked up something else at Bilkley besides your gig-horse, Mr.", "그러나 이렇게 외쳐 이름을 부른 데에는, 멀리서 마차가 나타났을 때 \"리버스턴 마차\"라고 말하는 것 이상의 의도는 누구에게도 없었다. 홀리 씨는 불스트로드의 등 쪽을 무심하게 한 번 둘러보았으나, 뱀브리지의 눈길이 그것을 좇으면서 그는 비꼬는 듯한 표정을 지었다."], ["Hawley.", "\"옳거니!"], ["I picked up a fine story about Bulstrode.", "생각났군.\" 그가 목소리를 조금 낮추며 말을 꺼냈다."], ["Do you know how he came by his fortune?", "\"빌클리에서 당신 이륜마차용 말 말고도 다른 걸 하나 주워 왔지요, 홀리 씨."], ["Any gentleman wanting a bit of curious information, I can give it him free of expense.", "불스트로드에 관한 근사한 이야기를 하나 주워 왔단 말입니다. 그가 어떻게 재산을 모았는지 아시오?"], ["If everybody got their deserts, Bulstrode might have had to say his prayers at Botany Bay.”", "진기한 정보가 필요한 신사분께는, 내가 공짜로 알려 드리리다. 모두가 제 응보를 받는다면, 불스트로드는 보터니 만에서 기도를 올리고 있어야 할지도 모르오.\""]], [["“What do you mean?” said Mr.", "* 보터니 만: 오스트레일리아의 유형지."], ["Hawley, thrusting his hands into his pockets, and pushing a little forward under the archway.", "\"무슨 말이오?\" 홀리 씨가 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 아치문 아래로 조금 다가서며 말했다."], ["If Bulstrode should turn out to be a rascal, Frank Hawley had a prophetic soul.", "만약 불스트로드가 악당으로 드러난다면, 프랭크 홀리는 그것을 예언한 셈이었다."]], [["“I had it from a party who was an old chum of Bulstrode’s.", "\"불스트로드의 옛 친구였던 작자한테서 들은 얘깁니다."], ["I’ll tell you where I first picked him up,”", "그자를 어디서 처음 마주쳤는지 말씀드리지요.\""], ["said Bambridge, with a sudden gesture of his fore-finger.", "뱀브리지가 갑자기 집게손가락을 까딱이며 말했다."], ["“He was at Larcher’s sale, but I knew nothing of him then—he slipped through my fingers—was after Bulstrode, no doubt.", "\"그자는 라처 경매장에 있었는데, 그때는 내가 그를 전혀 몰랐소이다―손가락 사이로 빠져나갔지요―필시 불스트로드를 노리고 있었던 게요."], ["He tells me he can tap Bulstrode to any amount, knows all his secrets.", "그자 말로는 불스트로드한테서 얼마든지 돈을 우려낼 수 있고, 그의 비밀을 죄다 안다는 거요."], ["However, he blabbed to me at Bilkley: he takes a stiff glass.", "어쨌든 그자가 빌클리에서 내게 죄다 털어놓았소. 술을 독하게 마시는 작자라."], ["Damme if I think he meant to turn king’s evidence; but he’s that sort of bragging fellow, the bragging runs over hedge and ditch with him, till he’d brag of a spavin as if it ’ud fetch money.", "빌어먹을, 그가 정말 밀고할 작정이었다고는 생각하지 않소만, 워낙 허풍 떠는 부류라, 그 허풍이 울타리고 도랑이고 넘어 다니는 통에, 마치 돈이라도 되는 양 말의 다리 종양까지 자랑할 위인이오."], ["A man should know when to pull up.” Mr.", "사람이라면 멈출 때를 알아야 하는 법인데.\""], ["Bambridge made this remark with an air of disgust, satisfied that his own bragging showed a fine sense of the marketable.", "뱀브리지 씨는 못마땅하다는 투로 이 말을 했으니, 자기 자신의 허풍은 상품 가치를 보는 훌륭한 안목을 드러낸다고 흡족해하던 터였다."]], [["“What’s the man’s name?", "\"그자 이름이 뭐요?"], ["Where can he be found?”", "어디 가면 만날 수 있소?\""], ["said Mr. Hawley.", "홀리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“As to where he is to be found, I left him to it at the Saracen’s Head; but his name is Raffles.”", "\"어디 가면 만날 수 있느냐 하면, 새러슨스 헤드에 그자를 두고 왔소이다. 한데 이름은 래플스라 하오.\""]], [["“Raffles!”", "\"래플스라니!\""], ["exclaimed Mr. Hopkins.", "홉킨스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“I furnished his funeral yesterday.", "\"내가 어제 그의 장례 물품을 댔소이다."], ["He was buried at Lowick.", "그는 로윅에 묻혔지요."], ["Mr. Bulstrode followed him.", "불스트로드 씨가 상여를 따라갔고요."], ["A very decent funeral.”", "아주 점잖은 장례였소.\""], ["There was a strong sensation among the listeners. Mr.", "청중들 사이에 강한 동요가 일었다."], ["Bambridge gave an ejaculation in which “brimstone” was the mildest word, and Mr.", "뱀브리지 씨는 \"지옥불\"이 그중 가장 점잖은 말일 만한 욕설을 내뱉었고, 홀리 씨는 눈썹을 찌푸리고 고개를 앞으로 숙이며 외쳤다."], ["Hawley, knitting his brows and bending his head forward, exclaimed, “What?—where did the man die?”", "\"뭐라고?―그자가 어디서 죽었다고?\""]], [["“At Stone Court,”", "\"스톤 코트에서요.\""], ["said the draper.", "포목상이 말했다."], ["“The housekeeper said he was a relation of the master’s.", "\"가정부 말로는 그가 주인 어른의 친척이랍디다."], ["He came there ill on Friday.”", "금요일에 병이 들어 거기 왔다는군요.\""]], [["“Why, it was on Wednesday I took a glass with him,”", "\"아니, 내가 그자와 술 한잔 한 게 수요일이었는데.\""], ["interposed Bambridge.", "뱀브리지가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Did any doctor attend him?”", "\"의사가 그를 진찰했소?\""], ["said Mr. Hawley", "홀리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Yes. Mr.", "\"그렇소."], ["Lydgate. Mr.", "리드게이트 씨가요."], ["Bulstrode sat up with him one night.", "불스트로드 씨가 하룻밤 곁을 지켰고요."], ["He died the third morning.”", "그자는 사흘째 되는 날 아침에 죽었소이다.\""]], [["“Go on, Bambridge,”", "\"계속하시오, 뱀브리지.\""], ["said Mr. Hawley, insistently.", "홀리 씨가 다그치며 말했다."], ["“What did this fellow say about Bulstrode?”", "\"이 작자가 불스트로드에 관해 뭐라고 했소?\""]], [["The group had already become larger, the town-clerk’s presence being a guarantee that something worth listening to was going on there; and Mr.", "무리는 어느새 더 커져 있었으니, 읍사무소 서기의 존재 자체가 거기서 들을 만한 무언가가 벌어지고 있다는 보증이었다. 그리하여 뱀브리지 씨는 일곱 사람이 듣는 가운데 자기 이야기를 풀어놓았다."], ["Bambridge delivered his narrative in the hearing of seven.", "그것은 대체로 우리가 아는 내용이었으니, 윌 래디슬로에 관한 사실도 포함되어 있었고, 거기에 약간의 지방색과 정황이 덧붙여졌다."], ["It was mainly what we know, including the fact about Will Ladislaw, with some local color and circumstance added: it was what Bulstrode had dreaded the betrayal of—and hoped to have buried forever with the corpse of Raffles—it was that haunting ghost of his earlier life which as he rode past the archway of the Green Dragon he was trusting that Providence had delivered him from.", "그것은 불스트로드가 폭로될까 두려워하던 바였고―래플스의 시체와 함께 영원히 묻어 버리기를 바라던 바였다. 그것은 그의 지난 삶에 들러붙은 유령이었으니, 그가 그린 드래건의 아치문을 지나 말을 타고 가면서, 섭리가 자신을 거기서 구해 주었다고 믿고 있던 바로 그 유령이었다."], ["Yes, Providence.", "그렇다, 섭리."], ["He had not confessed to himself yet that he had done anything in the way of contrivance to this end; he had accepted what seemed to have been offered.", "그는 이 목적을 위해 자신이 무슨 책략 같은 짓을 했다고 아직 스스로 고백한 적이 없었다. 그는 제공된 듯이 보이는 것을 받아들였을 뿐이었다."], ["It was impossible to prove that he had done anything which hastened the departure of that man’s soul.", "그가 그 사내의 영혼이 떠나는 것을 앞당기는 무슨 짓을 했다고 입증하기란 불가능했다."]], [["But this gossip about Bulstrode spread through Middlemarch like the smell of fire. Mr.", "그러나 불스트로드에 관한 이 잡담은 불 냄새처럼 미들마치 전역으로 퍼져 나갔다."], ["Frank Hawley followed up his information by sending a clerk whom he could trust to Stone Court on a pretext of inquiring about hay, but really to gather all that could be learned about Raffles and his illness from Mrs. Abel.", "프랭크 홀리 씨는 자기가 신뢰할 수 있는 서기를 건초에 관해 물어본다는 구실로 스톤 코트에 보냈으니, 실은 에이블 부인에게서 래플스와 그의 병에 관해 알아낼 수 있는 모든 것을 캐내려는 것이었다."], ["In this way it came to his knowledge that Mr. Garth had carried the man to Stone Court in his gig; and Mr.", "이런 식으로 그는 가스 씨가 그 사내를 자기 이륜마차에 태워 스톤 코트로 데려갔다는 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["Hawley in consequence took an opportunity of seeing Caleb, calling at his office to ask whether he had time to undertake an arbitration if it were required, and then asking him incidentally about Raffles.", "그 결과 홀리 씨는 케일럽을 만날 기회를 잡았으니, 그의 사무실에 들러 필요하다면 중재를 맡아 줄 시간이 있는지 묻고 나서, 지나가는 말처럼 래플스에 관해 물었다."], ["Caleb was betrayed into no word injurious to Bulstrode beyond the fact which he was forced to admit, that he had given up acting for him within the last week.", "케일럽은 지난 한 주 사이에 불스트로드를 위한 일을 그만두었다는, 인정하지 않을 수 없었던 사실 외에는 불스트로드에게 해가 될 만한 말은 한마디도 내비치지 않았다."], ["Mr Hawley drew his inferences, and feeling convinced that Raffles had told his story to Garth, and that Garth had given up Bulstrode’s affairs in consequence, said so a few hours later to Mr. Toller.", "홀리 씨는 나름의 추론을 끌어냈고, 래플스가 가스에게 자기 이야기를 했으며 그 결과 가스가 불스트로드의 일을 그만두었다고 확신하여, 몇 시간 뒤 톨러 씨에게 그렇게 말했다."], ["The statement was passed on until it had quite lost the stamp of an inference, and was taken as information coming straight from Garth, so that even a diligent historian might have concluded Caleb to be the chief publisher of Bulstrode’s misdemeanors.", "그 진술은 이 사람 저 사람을 거치며 추론의 자취를 완전히 잃어버린 채, 가스에게서 곧장 나온 정보로 받아들여졌으니, 그리하여 부지런한 역사가라 해도 케일럽이 불스트로드의 비행을 퍼뜨린 주범이라고 결론지을 만했다."]], [["Mr. Hawley was not slow to perceive that there was no handle for the law either in the revelations made by Raffles or in the circumstances of his death.", "홀리 씨는 래플스가 폭로한 내용에서든 그의 죽음을 둘러싼 정황에서든 법으로 걸고넘어질 만한 구실이 없다는 것을 알아채는 데 더디지 않았다."], ["He had himself ridden to Lowick village that he might look at the register and talk over the whole matter with Mr. Farebrother, who was not more surprised than the lawyer that an ugly secret should have come to light about Bulstrode, though he had always had justice enough in him to hinder his antipathy from turning into conclusions.", "그는 등기부를 확인하고 이 일 전체를 페어브러더 씨와 의논하려고 직접 로윅 마을까지 말을 타고 갔으니, 페어브러더 씨는 불스트로드에 관해 흉한 비밀이 드러났다는 사실에 이 변호사 못지않게 놀라기는 했으나, 자신의 반감이 결론으로 굳어지는 것을 막을 만한 공정함은 늘 지니고 있었던 터였다."], ["But while they were talking another combination was silently going forward in Mr.", "그러나 그들이 이야기를 나누는 동안, 페어브러더 씨의 마음속에서는 또 다른 조합이 조용히 진행되고 있었으니, 그것은 머지않아 미들마치에서 필연적으로 \"둘에 둘을 더하는\""], ["Farebrother’s mind, which foreshadowed what was soon to be loudly spoken of in Middlemarch as a necessary “putting of two and two together.”", "일이라고 시끄럽게 거론될 바를 예고하고 있었다."], ["With the reasons which kept Bulstrode in dread of Raffles there flashed the thought that the dread might have something to do with his munificence towards his medical man; and though he resisted the suggestion that it had been consciously accepted in any way as a bribe, he had a foreboding that this complication of things might be of malignant effect on Lydgate’s reputation.", "불스트로드가 래플스를 두려워하게 만든 이유들과 더불어, 그 두려움이 그가 주치의에게 베푼 후한 인심과 무슨 관련이 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 번쩍 스쳐 갔다. 그리고 그것이 어떤 식으로든 의식적으로 뇌물로 받아들여졌다는 추측에는 저항했지만, 이렇게 얽힌 정황이 리드게이트의 평판에 악의적인 영향을 미칠지도 모른다는 불길한 예감이 들었다."], ["He perceived that Mr. Hawley knew nothing at present of the sudden relief from debt, and he himself was careful to glide away from all approaches towards the subject.", "그는 홀리 씨가 갑작스러운 빚 청산에 관해서는 현재 아무것도 모른다는 것을 알아챘고, 자신은 그 주제로 다가가는 모든 접근을 조심스럽게 비껴 갔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["he said, with a deep breath, wanting to wind up the illimitable discussion of what might have been, though nothing could be legally proven, “it is a strange story.", "그는 법적으로는 아무것도 입증될 수 없으나 일어났을지도 모르는 일에 관한 끝없는 논의를 마무리 짓고 싶어서, 깊은 숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"참 묘한 이야기로군."], ["So our mercurial Ladislaw has a queer genealogy!", "우리의 변덕스러운 래디슬로가 그렇게 기묘한 혈통을 지녔다니!"], ["A high-spirited young lady and a musical Polish patriot made a likely enough stock for him to spring from, but I should never have suspected a grafting of the Jew pawnbroker.", "기개 넘치는 아가씨와 음악가인 폴란드 애국지사라면 그가 나올 만한 그럴듯한 혈통이지만, 유대인 전당포 주인이 접목되어 있을 줄은 결코 의심하지 못했겠지요."], ["However, there’s no knowing what a mixture will turn out beforehand.", "하지만 섞인 것이 어떻게 나올지는 미리 알 도리가 없는 법."], ["Some sorts of dirt serve to clarify.”", "어떤 종류의 흙은 오히려 정화하는 데 쓰이기도 하지요.\""]], [["“It’s just what I should have expected,”", "\"바로 내가 예상했던 바요.\""], ["said Mr. Hawley, mounting his horse.", "홀리 씨가 말에 오르며 말했다."], ["“Any cursed alien blood, Jew, Corsican, or Gypsy.”", "\"유대인이든 코르시카인이든 집시든, 빌어먹을 이방인의 피란 다 그렇소.\""]], [["“I know he’s one of your black sheep, Hawley.", "\"그가 당신네 검은 양 가운데 하나인 건 알지요, 홀리."], ["But he is really a disinterested, unworldly fellow,”", "하지만 그는 정말이지 사심 없고 세속을 모르는 친구요.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother, smiling.", "페어브러더 씨가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Ay, ay, that is your Whiggish twist,” said Mr.", "\"그래, 그래, 그게 당신의 휘그당식 비틀린 생각이오.\""], ["Hawley, who had been in the habit of saying apologetically that Farebrother was such a damned pleasant good-hearted fellow you would mistake him for a Tory.", "홀리 씨가 말했는데, 그는 페어브러더가 어찌나 빌어먹게 유쾌하고 마음씨 좋은 친구인지 토리당원으로 착각할 지경이라고 변명하듯 말하곤 했던 터였다."]], [["Mr. Hawley rode home without thinking of Lydgate’s attendance on Raffles in any other light than as a piece of evidence on the side of Bulstrode.", "홀리 씨는 리드게이트가 래플스를 돌본 일을 불스트로드에게 불리한 한 가지 증거로밖에는 달리 생각하지 않은 채 집으로 말을 몰았다."], ["But the news that Lydgate had all at once become able not only to get rid of the execution in his house but to pay all his debts in Middlemarch was spreading fast, gathering round it conjectures and comments which gave it new body and impetus, and soon filling the ears of other persons besides Mr.", "그러나 리드게이트가 별안간 자기 집에 들어왔던 압류를 면했을 뿐 아니라 미들마치의 빚을 모두 갚을 수 있게 되었다는 소식이 빠르게 퍼져 나가면서, 그 주위로 갖가지 억측과 논평이 모여들어 새로운 살이 붙고 추진력을 얻었으니, 곧 홀리 씨 외의 다른 사람들의 귀에도 가득 차게 되었고, 그들은 이 갑작스러운 자금 동원력과 래플스에 관한 추문을 잠재우려는 불스트로드의 욕망 사이에 의미심장한 연관이 있음을 알아채는 데 더디지 않았다. 그 돈이 불스트로드에게서 나왔다는 것은, 설령 그것을 직접 증명할 증거가 없었더라도 어김없이 짐작되었을 것이다."], ["Hawley, who were not slow to see a significant relation between this sudden command of money and Bulstrode’s desire to stifle the scandal of Raffles.", "그의 장인도 그 자신의 가족도 그를 위해 아무것도 해 주지 않으리라는 사실이 리드게이트의 형편에 관한 잡담에 이미 들어 있었기 때문이다."], ["That the money came from Bulstrode would infallibly have been guessed even if there had been no direct evidence of it; for it had beforehand entered into the gossip about Lydgate’s affairs, that neither his father-in-law nor his own family would do anything for him, and direct evidence was furnished not only by a clerk at the Bank, but by innocent Mrs.", "게다가 직접적인 증거가 은행 서기뿐 아니라 순진한 불스트로드 부인 본인에게서도 나왔으니, 그녀가 그 대출 이야기를 플림데일 부인에게 했고, 플림데일 부인은 그것을 톨러 가문에 시집간 며느리에게 했으며, 그 며느리는 그것을 두루 퍼뜨렸던 것이다. 이 일은 워낙 공공연하고 중대하게 여겨졌기에 그것을 떠받칠 만찬이 필요했고, 마침 그 무렵 불스트로드와 리드게이트에 관한 이 추문을 발판으로 숱한 초대장이 발송되고 수락되었다."], ["Bulstrode herself, who mentioned the loan to Mrs. Plymdale, who mentioned it to her daughter-in-law of the house of Toller, who mentioned it generally.", "부인들과 과부들과 미혼 여성들이 평소보다 더 자주 일감을 들고 차를 마시러 나갔다."], ["The business was felt to be so public and important that it required dinners to feed it, and many invitations were just then issued and accepted on the strength of this scandal concerning Bulstrode and Lydgate; wives, widows, and single ladies took their work and went out to tea oftener than usual; and all public conviviality, from the Green Dragon to Dollop’s, gathered a zest which could not be won from the question whether the Lords would throw out the Reform Bill.", "그리고 그린 드래건에서 돌롭스 술집에 이르기까지 모든 공공의 흥겨운 자리는, 상원이 개혁 법안을 부결시킬 것인가 하는 문제로는 결코 얻을 수 없는 활기를 띠게 되었다. * 개혁 법안: 1832년 선거법 개정안."]], [["For hardly anybody doubted that some scandalous reason or other was at the bottom of Bulstrode’s liberality to Lydgate.", "불스트로드가 리드게이트에게 베푼 후한 인심의 밑바닥에 추잡한 이유가 있다는 것을 의심하는 사람은 거의 없었으니 말이다."], ["Mr. Hawley indeed, in the first instance, invited a select party, including the two physicians, with Mr Toller and Mr.", "실제로 홀리 씨는 우선 두 내과 의사와 톨러 씨, 렌치 씨를 포함한 엄선된 일행을 초대했으니, 래플스의 병에 관한 개연성을 면밀히 따지자는 명백한 취지에서였다."], ["Wrench, expressly to hold a close discussion as to the probabilities of Raffles’s illness, reciting to them all the particulars which had been gathered from Mrs.", "그는 리드게이트의 진단서―사인이 진전 섬망이라는―와 관련하여 에이블 부인에게서 수집한 세부 사항을 그들에게 죄다 들려주었다."], ["Abel in connection with Lydgate’s certificate, that the death was due to delirium tremens; and the medical gentlemen, who all stood undisturbedly on the old paths in relation to this disease, declared that they could see nothing in these particulars which could be transformed into a positive ground of suspicion.", "그러나 이 병에 관해 모두 옛 방식을 흔들림 없이 고수하는 의료계 신사들은, 이 세부 사항 가운데 명확한 의심의 근거로 바뀔 만한 것은 아무것도 찾을 수 없다고 단언했다. 그러나 도덕적인 의심의 근거는 남아 있었다."], ["But the moral grounds of suspicion remained: the strong motives Bulstrode clearly had for wishing to be rid of Raffles, and the fact that at this critical moment he had given Lydgate the help which he must for some time have known the need for; the disposition, moreover, to believe that Bulstrode would be unscrupulous, and the absence of any indisposition to believe that Lydgate might be as easily bribed as other haughty-minded men when they have found themselves in want of money.", "불스트로드가 래플스를 떼어 버리고 싶어 할 강력한 동기가 분명히 있었다는 점, 그리고 이 결정적인 순간에 그가 리드게이트에게, 그가 얼마간 전부터 필요로 한다는 것을 분명 알고 있었을 도움을 주었다는 사실, 게다가 불스트로드라면 양심의 거리낌이 없으리라고 믿으려는 성향, 또한 리드게이트라 해도 다른 거만한 사내들이 돈에 궁할 때 그러하듯 쉽사리 매수당할 수 있으리라고 믿는 데 아무런 거리낌이 없다는 점이 그것이었다."], ["Even if the money had been given merely to make him hold his tongue about the scandal of Bulstrode’s earlier life, the fact threw an odious light on Lydgate, who had long been sneered at as making himself subservient to the banker for the sake of working himself into predominance, and discrediting the elder members of his profession.", "설령 그 돈이 그저 불스트로드의 지난 삶에 관한 추문에 대해 입을 다물게 하려고 주어진 것이라 해도, 그 사실은 리드게이트에게 추악한 빛을 드리웠으니, 그는 스스로 우위를 차지하고 동업계의 연장자들을 깎아내리려는 속셈으로 은행가에게 굽실거린다고 오래도록 비웃음을 사 왔던 터였다."], ["Hence, in spite of the negative as to any direct sign of guilt in relation to the death at Stone Court, Mr. Hawley’s select party broke up with the sense that the affair had “an ugly look.”", "그리하여 스톤 코트의 죽음과 관련하여 죄를 입증할 직접적인 흔적이 전혀 없다는 결론에도 불구하고, 홀리 씨가 엄선한 일행은 이 일에 \"흉한 구석\"이 있다는 느낌을 품은 채 헤어졌다."]], [["But this vague conviction of indeterminable guilt, which was enough to keep up much head-shaking and biting innuendo even among substantial professional seniors, had for the general mind all the superior power of mystery over fact.", "그러나 이렇게 막연하면서도 단정할 수 없는 죄의 확신은, 점잖은 직업계 원로들 사이에서조차 숱한 고갯짓과 신랄한 암시를 계속 불러일으키기에 충분했거니와, 일반 사람들의 마음에는 사실에 대한 신비의 온갖 우월한 위력을 발휘했다."], ["Everybody liked better to conjecture how the thing was, than simply to know it; for conjecture soon became more confident than knowledge, and had a more liberal allowance for the incompatible.", "사람들은 그 일이 어떠했는지 그저 아는 것보다 추측하는 것을 누구나 더 좋아했다. 추측은 곧 지식보다 더 자신만만해졌고, 양립할 수 없는 것에 대해서도 더 너그러운 여지를 베풀었기 때문이다."], ["Even the more definite scandal concerning Bulstrode’s earlier life was, for some minds, melted into the mass of mystery, as so much lively metal to be poured out in dialogue, and to take such fantastic shapes as heaven pleased.", "불스트로드의 지난 삶에 관한 더 분명한 추문조차도, 어떤 이들의 마음속에서는 신비의 덩어리 속으로 녹아들었으니, 마치 대화 속에 부어져 하늘이 빚고 싶은 대로 갖가지 환상적인 형상을 띠게 될, 그만큼 활기 넘치는 쇳물 같았다."]], [["This was the tone of thought chiefly sanctioned by Mrs. Dollop, the spirited landlady of the Tankard in Slaughter Lane, who had often to resist the shallow pragmatism of customers disposed to think that their reports from the outer world were of equal force with what had “come up”", "이것이 슬로터 골목에 있는 탱커드 술집의 기개 넘치는 안주인 돌롭 부인이 주로 옹호하는 사고방식이었으니, 그녀는 바깥세상에서 들어온 자기들의 소식이 그녀 머릿속에 \"떠오른\""], ["in her mind. How it had been brought to her she didn’t know, but it was there before her as if it had been “scored with the chalk on the chimney-board—”", "것과 똑같은 위력을 지닌다고 여기려 드는 손님들의 얄팍한 실용주의에 자주 맞서야 했다."], ["as Bulstrode should say, “his inside was that black as if the hairs of his head knowed the thoughts of his heart, he’d tear ’em up by the roots.”", "그것이 어떻게 자기에게 다가왔는지 그녀는 알지 못했으나, 그것은 마치 \"굴뚝 판자에 분필로 적힌\" 것처럼 그녀 앞에 떡하니 있었다―불스트로드로 말하자면, \"그 속이 어찌나 시커먼지 제 머리카락이 제 마음속 생각을 알기라도 한다면 뿌리째 다 뽑아 버릴 위인\"이라는 것이었다."]], [["“That’s odd,” said Mr.", "\"그것참 묘하군요.\""], ["Limp, a meditative shoemaker, with weak eyes and a piping voice.", "약한 눈에 가느다란 목소리를 지닌, 사색적인 구두장이 림프 씨가 말했다."], ["“Why, I read in the ‘Trumpet’ that was what the Duke of Wellington said when he turned his coat and went over to the Romans.”", "\"아니, 내가 『트럼펫』에서 읽기로는, 웰링턴 공작이 변절해서 로마 가톨릭 쪽으로 넘어갔을 때 한 말이 바로 그거였는데.\""]], [["“Very like,”", "\"그럴 만도 하지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Dollop.", "돌롭 부인이 말했다."], ["“If one raskill said it, it’s more reason why another should.", "\"악당 하나가 그런 말을 했다면, 다른 악당이 그러는 게 더 당연하지 않겠소."], ["But hypocrite as he’s been, and holding things with that high hand, as there was no parson i’ the country good enough for him, he was forced to take Old Harry into his counsel, and Old Harry’s been too many for him.”", "하지만 그자가 위선자 노릇을 하면서, 나라 안에 자기한테 어울릴 만큼 훌륭한 목사가 없다는 듯 그렇게 거만하게 굴더니, 결국 늙은 마왕을 제 모사로 끌어들일 수밖에 없었던 게요. 한데 그 늙은 마왕이 그자보다 한 수 위였던 거지.\""]], [["“Ay, ay, he’s a ’complice you can’t send out o’ the country,”", "\"그래요, 그래, 그놈은 이 나라 밖으로 내쫓을 수 없는 공범이지요.\""], ["said Mr. Crabbe, the glazier, who gathered much news and groped among it dimly.", "유리장이 크래브 씨가 말했는데, 그는 온갖 소식을 그러모으되 그 속을 어렴풋이 더듬을 뿐인 사람이었다."], ["“But by what I can make out, there’s them says Bulstrode was for running away, for fear o’ being found out, before now.”", "\"하지만 내가 알아낸 바로는, 불스트로드가 진작부터 들통날까 봐 도망치려 했다고 말하는 이들이 있다더군요.\""]], [["“He’ll be drove away, whether or no,”", "\"내쫓기게 될 거요, 그러든 안 그러든.\" 방금 들어선 이발사 딜 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Dill, the barber, who had just dropped in.", "\"오늘 아침에 홀리 씨네 서기인 플레처를 면도해 줬는데―손가락을 다쳤더군요―그 사람 말이, 다들 한마음으로 불스트로드를 쫓아낼 작정이랍디다."], ["“I shaved Fletcher, Hawley’s clerk, this morning—he’s got a bad finger—and he says they’re all of one mind to get rid of Bulstrode.", "세시저 씨도 그자에게 등을 돌려서 교구에서 내보내고 싶어 한다는 거예요. 또 이 읍내에 죄수선에서 나온 작자랑 차라리 함께 식사하겠다는 신사들이 있다더군요."], ["Mr. Thesiger is turned against him, and wants him out o’ the parish.", "'나라면 차라리 그게 훨씬 낫겠소.'"], ["And there’s gentlemen in this town says they’d as soon dine with a fellow from the hulks.", "플레처가 그러더라고요. '제 종교로 사람을 불편하게 만들면서, 자기한테는 십계명만으로는 부족하다고 떠벌리는 작자보다 더 비위 상하는 게 어디 있겠소?"], ["‘And a deal sooner I would,’ says Fletcher; ‘for what’s more against one’s stomach than a man coming and making himself bad company with his religion, and giving out as the Ten Commandments are not enough for him, and all the while he’s worse than half the men at the tread-mill?’ Fletcher said so himself.”", "게다가 그자는 그러는 내내 디딜방아 죄수의 절반보다도 더 나쁜 놈인데 말이오.' 플레처가 직접 그렇게 말했소이다.\""]], [["“It’ll be a bad thing for the town though, if Bulstrode’s money goes out of it,”", "\"그래도 불스트로드의 돈이 이 읍내를 빠져나가면, 이 동네엔 나쁜 일이 될 텐데요.\""], ["said Mr. Limp, quaveringly.", "림프 씨가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Ah, there’s better folks spend their money worse,”", "\"아, 더 훌륭한 사람들도 제 돈을 더 나쁘게 쓰는 법이지요.\""], ["said a firm-voiced dyer, whose crimson hands looked out of keeping with his good-natured face.", "진홍빛 손이 사람 좋아 보이는 얼굴과 어울리지 않는 한 염색공이 단호한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“But he won’t keep his money, by what I can make out,”", "\"하지만 내가 알아낸 바로는, 그자는 제 돈을 지키지 못할 거요.\""], ["said the glazier.", "유리장이가 말했다."], ["“Don’t they say as there’s somebody can strip it off him?", "\"그자한테서 돈을 다 벗겨 낼 수 있는 누군가가 있다고들 하지 않소?"], ["By what I can understan’, they could take every penny off him, if they went to lawing.”", "내가 알기로는, 소송을 건다면 그자한테서 한 푼도 남김없이 빼앗을 수 있다던데.\""]], [["“No such thing!”", "\"그런 일은 없소!\""], ["said the barber, who felt himself a little above his company at Dollop’s, but liked it none the worse.", "이발사가 말했는데, 그는 돌롭스에서 자기가 좌중보다 조금 격이 높다고 느끼면서도 그것을 싫어하지는 않았다."], ["“Fletcher says it’s no such thing.", "\"플레처 말로는 그런 일은 없답디다."], ["He says they might prove over and over again whose child this young Ladislaw was, and they’d do no more than if they proved I came out of the Fens—he couldn’t touch a penny.”", "이 젊은 래디슬로가 누구 자식인지 몇 번이고 증명할 수 있다 해도, 내가 펜스 늪지대 출신이라는 걸 증명하는 것 이상은 못 한다는 거예요―한 푼도 건드릴 수 없다는 거지요.\""]], [["“Look you there now!”", "\"거 보시오!\""], ["said Mrs. Dollop, indignantly.", "돌롭 부인이 분개하여 말했다."], ["“I thank the Lord he took my children to Himself, if that’s all the law can do for the motherless.", "\"어미 없는 자식에게 법이 해 줄 수 있는 게 고작 그거라면, 내 아이들을 하느님께서 데려가신 것을 감사할 따름이오."], ["Then by that, it’s o’ no use who your father and mother is.", "그렇다면 그 말대로라면, 네 아비가 누구고 어미가 누구인지는 아무 소용이 없다는 거잖소."], ["But as to listening to what one lawyer says without asking another—I wonder at a man o’ your cleverness, Mr. Dill.", "한데 변호사 하나가 하는 말을 다른 변호사한테 물어보지도 않고 그냥 곧이듣다니―당신처럼 영리한 양반이 그러다니 놀랍군요, 딜 씨."], ["It’s well known there’s always two sides, if no more; else who’d go to law, I should like to know?", "무슨 일이든 늘 양쪽이 있다는 건, 더 많지는 않더라도, 누구나 다 아는 사실이오. 그렇지 않다면 대체 누가 소송을 걸겠소, 내 묻고 싶구려?"], ["It’s a poor tale, with all the law as there is up and down, if it’s no use proving whose child you are.", "이러쿵저러쿵 법이란 게 그렇게 많은데, 네가 누구 자식인지 증명하는 게 아무 소용이 없다면 그거야말로 한심한 노릇이지."], ["Fletcher may say that if he likes, but I say, don’t Fletcher me!”", "플레처가 그러고 싶으면 그러라지요. 하지만 나한테는 플레처고 뭐고 들먹이지 마시오!\""]], [["Mr. Dill affected to laugh in a complimentary way at Mrs. Dollop, as a woman who was more than a match for the lawyers; being disposed to submit to much twitting from a landlady who had a long score against him.", "딜 씨는 돌롭 부인이 변호사들도 능히 당해 낼 만한 여자라며 추켜세우는 척 웃었으니, 자기에게 오래 쌓인 외상값을 가진 안주인에게서 어지간한 놀림은 감수하려는 마음에서였다."]], [["“If they come to lawing, and it’s all true as folks say, there’s more to be looked to nor money,”", "\"그자들이 소송에 들어가고, 사람들 말이 죄다 사실이라면, 돈보다 더 따져 봐야 할 게 있소.\""], ["said the glazier.", "유리장이가 말했다."], ["“There’s this poor creetur as is dead and gone; by what I can make out, he’d seen the day when he was a deal finer gentleman nor Bulstrode.”", "\"이렇게 죽어 없어진 가엾은 작자 말이오. 내가 알아낸 바로는, 그 사람도 한때는 불스트로드보다 훨씬 더 훌륭한 신사였던 시절이 있었다는데.\""]], [["“Finer gentleman!", "\"훨씬 더 훌륭한 신사였다고요!"], ["I’ll warrant him,”", "그야 틀림없지요.\""], ["said Mrs.", "돌롭 부인이 말했다."], ["Dollop; “and a far personabler man, by what I can hear. As I said when Mr.", "\"내가 들은 바로는 풍채도 훨씬 더 그럴듯한 사람이었다는데."], ["Baldwin, the tax-gatherer, comes in, a-standing where you sit, and says, ‘Bulstrode got all his money as he brought into this town by thieving and swindling,’—I said, ‘You don’t make me no wiser, Mr.", "세리 볼드윈 씨가 지금 당신이 앉은 자리에 떡 버티고 서서 '불스트로드는 이 읍내에 가져온 돈을 죄다 도둑질하고 사기 쳐서 모았다'고 했을 때, 내가 그랬소이다. '당신 덕에 더 알게 된 건 없소, 볼드윈 씨."], ["Baldwin: it’s set my blood a-creeping to look at him ever sin’ here he came into Slaughter Lane a-wanting to buy the house over my head: folks don’t look the color o’ the dough-tub and stare at you as if they wanted to see into your backbone for nothingk.’ That was what I said, and Mr.", "그자가 여기 슬로터 골목에 들어와 내 머리 위의 이 집을 사려고 했던 그때부터, 그자만 봤다 하면 내 피가 다 오싹해진단 말이오. 멀쩡한 사람이 빵 반죽 통 같은 낯빛을 하고서, 마치 당신 등뼈 속까지 들여다보고 싶다는 듯 공연히 빤히 쳐다보지는 않는 법이거든.'"], ["Baldwin can bear me witness.”", "내가 그렇게 말했고, 볼드윈 씨가 증인이 되어 줄 게요.\""]], [["“And in the rights of it too,”", "\"그것도 옳은 말씀이오.\""], ["said Mr. Crabbe.", "크래브 씨가 말했다."], ["“For by what I can make out, this Raffles, as they call him, was a lusty, fresh-colored man as you’d wish to see, and the best o’ company—though dead he lies in Lowick churchyard sure enough; and by what I can understan’, there’s them knows more than they should know about how he got there.”", "\"내가 알아낸 바로는, 사람들이 래플스라 부르는 이 사내는 보기에 좋을 만큼 혈색 좋고 우람한 사람이었고, 더없이 좋은 술친구였답디다―물론 지금이야 로윅 교회 묘지에 분명히 죽어 누워 있지만요. 그리고 내가 알기로는, 그자가 어떻게 거기 묻히게 됐는지를 마땅히 알아야 할 것보다 더 많이 아는 이들이 있다는 거요.\""]], [["“I’ll believe you!” said Mrs.", "\"누가 아니래요!\""], ["Dollop, with a touch of scorn at Mr. Crabbe’s apparent dimness.", "돌롭 부인이 크래브 씨의 빤한 둔함을 약간 비웃으며 말했다."], ["“When a man’s been ’ticed to a lone house, and there’s them can pay for hospitals and nurses for half the country-side choose to be sitters-up night and day, and nobody to come near but a doctor as is known to stick at nothingk, and as poor as he can hang together, and after that so flush o’ money as he can pay off Mr. Byles the butcher as his bill has been running on for the best o’ joints since last Michaelmas was a twelvemonth—I don’t want anybody to come and tell me as there’s been more going on nor the Prayer-book’s got a service for—I don’t want to stand winking and blinking and thinking.”", "\"사내 하나가 외딴집으로 꾀여 들어갔는데, 이 동네 절반의 병원이며 간호사 값을 다 댈 수 있는 이들이 밤낮으로 곁을 지키겠다고 나서고, 가까이 오는 이라곤 무슨 짓이든 서슴지 않기로 소문난, 게다가 빌어먹게 가난뱅이인 의사 하나뿐이더니, 그 일이 있고 나서는 그자가 어찌나 돈이 넉넉해졌는지 작년 미카엘 축일 한 해 전부터 좋은 고기 외상값이 쌓인 정육점 주인 바일스 씨한테 그걸 죄다 갚을 수 있게 됐다 하면―기도서에 그에 맞는 예식이 있는 것 이상의 무슨 일이 벌어졌다고, 나한테 누가 와서 일러 주지 않아도 다 안단 말이오―나는 눈을 끔벅끔벅하며 생각에 잠겨 있을 사람이 아니거든.\""]], [["Mrs. Dollop looked round with the air of a landlady accustomed to dominate her company.", "* 미카엘 축일: 9월 29일."], ["There was a chorus of adhesion from the more courageous; but Mr.", "돌롭 부인은 좌중을 휘어잡는 데 익숙한 안주인다운 태도로 둘러보았다. 더 용감한 이들에게서 동조의 합창이 일었다."], ["Limp, after taking a draught, placed his flat hands together and pressed them hard between his knees, looking down at them with blear-eyed contemplation, as if the scorching power of Mrs. Dollop’s speech had quite dried up and nullified his wits until they could be brought round again by further moisture.", "그러나 림프 씨는 한 모금 들이켜고 나서, 두 손바닥을 마주 모아 무릎 사이에 꽉 끼우고는, 마치 돌롭 부인의 이글거리는 말솜씨가 그의 재치를 바싹 말려 무력하게 만들어, 더 많은 수분으로 다시 적셔질 때까지는 회복되지 못하리라는 듯, 흐릿한 눈으로 그 손을 내려다보았다."]], [["“Why shouldn’t they dig the man up and have the Crowner?”", "\"그자를 파내서 검시관을 부르면 안 될 게 뭐요?\""], ["said the dyer.", "염색공이 말했다."], ["“It’s been done many and many’s the time.", "\"그런 일은 숱하게 있었잖소."], ["If there’s been foul play they might find it out.”", "부정한 짓이 있었다면 밝혀낼 수도 있을 거요.\""]], [["“Not they, Mr. Jonas!”", "\"못 밝혀내요, 조너스 씨!\""], ["said Mrs Dollop, emphatically.", "돌롭 부인이 힘주어 말했다."], ["“I know what doctors are.", "\"내가 의사들이 어떤 작자들인지 잘 아오."], ["They’re a deal too cunning to be found out.", "밝혀내기엔 너무도 교활한 자들이지요."], ["And this Doctor Lydgate that’s been for cutting up everybody before the breath was well out o’ their body—it’s plain enough what use he wanted to make o’ looking into respectable people’s insides.", "게다가 이 리드게이트 박사라는 자는 숨이 채 끊어지기도 전에 사람들을 죄다 갈라 보려고 안달이었으니―점잖은 사람들의 속을 들여다보려는 게 무슨 속셈이었는지 뻔하지 않소."], ["He knows drugs, you may be sure, as you can neither smell nor see, neither before they’re swallowed nor after.", "그자는 약을 잘 알지요, 틀림없이, 삼키기 전에도 후에도 냄새도 안 나고 보이지도 않는 약을 말이오."], ["Why, I’ve seen drops myself ordered by Doctor Gambit, as is our club doctor and a good charikter, and has brought more live children into the world nor ever another i’ Middlemarch—I say I’ve seen drops myself as made no difference whether they was in the glass or out, and yet have griped you the next day.", "아니, 나도 우리 공제회 의사인 갬빗 박사가―평판도 좋고 미들마치의 그 누구보다 더 많은 아이를 살려서 세상에 데려온 분이지만―처방한 물약을 직접 본 적이 있는데, 글쎄, 잔에 들었든 안 들었든 아무 차이도 없는 물약이 이튿날 사람 배를 쥐어뜯더란 말이오. 그러니 당신네 분별에 맡겨 판단하시구려."], ["So I’ll leave your own sense to judge.", "나한테 이러쿵저러쿵 마시오!"], ["Don’t tell me! All I say is, it’s a mercy they didn’t take this Doctor Lydgate on to our club.", "내가 하고 싶은 말은, 이 리드게이트 박사를 우리 공제회에 들이지 않은 게 천만다행이라는 거요."], ["There’s many a mother’s child might ha’ rued it.”", "안 그랬으면 어느 어미의 자식이 후회할 뻔했지.\""]], [["The heads of this discussion at “Dollop’s” had been the common theme among all classes in the town, had been carried to Lowick Parsonage on one side and to Tipton Grange on the other, had come fully to the ears of the Vincy family, and had been discussed with sad reference to “poor Harriet”", "\"돌롭스\"에서 이렇게 오간 논의의 골자는 읍내 모든 계층의 공통된 화제가 되어, 한쪽으로는 로윅 목사관까지, 다른 쪽으로는 팁턴 그레인지까지 실려 갔고, 빈시 가족의 귀에도 죄다 들어갔으며, 불스트로드 부인의 친구들은 모두 \"가엾은 해리엇\"을 들먹이며 슬픈 마음으로 그것을 입에 올렸으니, 리드게이트는 사람들이 왜 자기를 이상한 눈으로 쳐다보는지 분명히 알기 전이었고, 불스트로드 자신도 자기 비밀이 누설된 것을 의심하기 전이었다."], ["by all Mrs. Bulstrode’s friends, before Lydgate knew distinctly why people were looking strangely at him, and before Bulstrode himself suspected the betrayal of his secrets.", "그는 이웃들과 그다지 다정한 관계를 맺어 온 적이 없었으니, 그래서 다정함의 신호가 사라진 것을 알아챌 수가 없었다."], ["He had not been accustomed to very cordial relations with his neighbors, and hence he could not miss the signs of cordiality; moreover, he had been taking journeys on business of various kinds, having now made up his mind that he need not quit Middlemarch, and feeling able consequently to determine on matters which he had before left in suspense.", "게다가 그는 이제 미들마치를 떠날 필요가 없다고 마음을 굳혔던 터라 여러 가지 용무로 여행을 다니던 참이었고, 그리하여 전에는 미결로 남겨 두었던 문제들을 결정할 수 있다고 느끼고 있었다."]], [["“We will make a journey to Cheltenham in the course of a month or two,”", "\"한두 달 안으로 첼튼엄에 다녀옵시다.\""], ["he had said to his wife.", "그는 아내에게 말했다."], ["“There are great spiritual advantages to be had in that town along with the air and the waters, and six weeks there will be eminently refreshing to us.”", "\"그 읍내는 공기며 광천수와 더불어 영적으로도 크게 얻을 것이 있는 곳이니, 거기서 여섯 주를 지내면 우리에게 더없이 상쾌한 휴식이 될 거요.\""]], [["He really believed in the spiritual advantages, and meant that his life henceforth should be the more devoted because of those later sins which he represented to himself as hypothetic, praying hypothetically for their pardon:—“if I have herein transgressed.”", "그는 정말로 그 영적인 이로움을 믿었고, 앞으로 자기 삶이 더욱 헌신적이어야 하리라 작정했으니, 가정형으로 그 용서를 빌며―\"만약 내가 이 일에 죄를 지었다면\"―스스로 가정적인 것이라 여기던 그 후일의 죄들 때문이었다."]], [["As to the Hospital, he avoided saying anything further to Lydgate, fearing to manifest a too sudden change of plans immediately on the death of Raffles.", "병원에 관해서는, 래플스가 죽자마자 너무 갑작스럽게 계획을 바꾸는 것처럼 보일까 두려워, 리드게이트에게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["In his secret soul he believed that Lydgate suspected his orders to have been intentionally disobeyed, and suspecting this he must also suspect a motive.", "그는 마음속 깊은 곳에서, 리드게이트가 자기 지시가 고의로 지켜지지 않았음을 의심하고 있으며, 이를 의심한다면 그 동기까지도 의심하리라고 믿었다."], ["But nothing had been betrayed to him as to the history of Raffles, and Bulstrode was anxious not to do anything which would give emphasis to his undefined suspicions.", "그러나 래플스의 내력에 관해 그에게 누설된 것은 아무것도 없었고, 불스트로드는 자기의 막연한 의심에 무게를 실어 줄 만한 일은 어떤 것도 하지 않으려고 노심초사했다."], ["As to any certainty that a particular method of treatment would either save or kill, Lydgate himself was constantly arguing against such dogmatism; he had no right to speak, and he had every motive for being silent.", "어떤 특정한 치료법이 사람을 살리거나 죽이리라는 그 어떤 확실성에 대해서도, 리드게이트 자신이 늘 그런 독단에 반대하는 논리를 펴 왔으니, 그는 말할 자격이 없었고, 또 침묵해야 할 온갖 동기가 있었다."], ["Hence Bulstrode felt himself providentially secured.", "그리하여 불스트로드는 자신이 섭리에 의해 보호받고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["The only incident he had strongly winced under had been an occasional encounter with Caleb Garth, who, however, had raised his hat with mild gravity.", "그가 강하게 움찔했던 유일한 사건은 이따금 케일럽 가스와 마주친 일이었는데, 그러나 케일럽은 온화하고 진중하게 모자를 들어 올려 인사했을 뿐이었다."]], [["Meanwhile, on the part of the principal townsmen a strong determination was growing against him.", "한편 읍내의 주요 인사들 사이에서는 그에 대한 강력한 결의가 자라나고 있었다."]], [["A meeting was to be held in the Town-Hall on a sanitary question which had risen into pressing importance by the occurrence of a cholera case in the town.", "읍내에 콜레라 환자가 발생하여 급박하게 중요해진 위생 문제를 두고 읍사무소에서 회의가 열릴 예정이었다."], ["Since the Act of Parliament, which had been hurriedly passed, authorizing assessments for sanitary measures, there had been a Board for the superintendence of such measures appointed in Middlemarch, and much cleansing and preparation had been concurred in by Whigs and Tories.", "위생 조치를 위한 부과금을 승인하는 의회 법안이 서둘러 통과된 이래, 미들마치에는 그러한 조치를 감독하는 위원회가 임명되었고, 휘그당과 토리당이 의견을 모아 많은 청소와 준비 작업이 이루어졌다."], ["The question now was, whether a piece of ground outside the town should be secured as a burial-ground by means of assessment or by private subscription.", "이제 문제는 읍내 외곽의 한 부지를 부과금으로 확보하여 묘지로 쓸 것인가, 아니면 개인의 기부로 할 것인가 하는 것이었다."], ["The meeting was to be open, and almost everybody of importance in the town was expected to be there.", "회의는 공개로 열릴 예정이었고, 읍내에서 중요한 인물이라면 거의 모두가 거기 참석하리라 예상되었다."]], [["Mr. Bulstrode was a member of the Board, and just before twelve o’clock he started from the Bank with the intention of urging the plan of private subscription.", "불스트로드 씨는 위원회의 일원이었으니, 정오가 되기 직전에 개인 기부 방안을 강력히 주장할 작정으로 은행을 나섰다."], ["Under the hesitation of his projects, he had for some time kept himself in the background, and he felt that he should this morning resume his old position as a man of action and influence in the public affairs of the town where he expected to end his days.", "그동안 자기 계획이 흔들리는 통에 한동안 뒤로 물러나 있었던 그는, 자신이 여생을 마치리라 기대하는 이 읍내의 공적인 일에서, 오늘 아침에는 행동력과 영향력을 갖춘 사람으로서 옛 지위를 되찾으리라 느꼈다."], ["Among the various persons going in the same direction, he saw Lydgate; they joined, talked over the object of the meeting, and entered it together.", "같은 방향으로 가던 여러 사람들 가운데서 그는 리드게이트를 보았다. 두 사람은 합류하여, 회의의 목적을 두고 이야기를 나누며 함께 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["It seemed that everybody of mark had been earlier than they.", "내로라하는 사람들은 모두 그들보다 먼저 온 듯했다."], ["But there were still spaces left near the head of the large central table, and they made their way thither.", "그러나 커다란 중앙 탁자의 상석 가까이에는 아직 빈자리가 남아 있어서, 그들은 그쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["Mr. Farebrother sat opposite, not far from Mr.", "페어브러더 씨는 맞은편, 홀리 씨에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 앉아 있었다."], ["Hawley; all the medical men were there; Mr.", "의료계 인사들은 모두 와 있었다."], ["Thesiger was in the chair, and Mr. Brooke of Tipton was on his right hand.", "세시저 씨가 의장석에 앉았고, 팁턴의 브룩 씨가 그의 오른편에 있었다."]], [["Lydgate noticed a peculiar interchange of glances when he and Bulstrode took their seats.", "리드게이트는 자기와 불스트로드가 자리에 앉자 묘한 눈길이 오가는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["After the business had been fully opened by the chairman, who pointed out the advantages of purchasing by subscription a piece of ground large enough to be ultimately used as a general cemetery, Mr. Bulstrode, whose rather high-pitched but subdued and fluent voice the town was used to at meetings of this sort, rose and asked leave to deliver his opinion.", "의장이 궁극적으로 공동묘지로 쓸 만큼 넓은 부지를 기부로 매입하는 것의 이점을 지적하며 안건을 충분히 개진한 뒤, 이런 종류의 회의에서 다소 높지만 차분하고 유창한 그의 목소리에 읍내가 익숙해져 있던 불스트로드 씨가 일어나 자기 의견을 말할 수 있도록 허락을 청했다."], ["Lydgate could see again the peculiar interchange of glances before Mr. Hawley started up, and said in his firm resonant voice, “Mr.", "홀리 씨가 벌떡 일어나 단호하고 우렁찬 목소리로 말하기 전에, 리드게이트는 다시 한 번 그 묘한 눈길이 오가는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Chairman, I request that before any one delivers his opinion on this point I may be permitted to speak on a question of public feeling, which not only by myself, but by many gentlemen present, is regarded as preliminary.”", "\"의장님, 이 문제에 관해 누가 의견을 말하기에 앞서, 저뿐 아니라 여기 참석한 여러 신사분들이 선결 사항으로 여기는 공적 정서의 문제에 관해 제가 말할 수 있도록 허락해 주시기를 청합니다.\""]], [["Mr. Hawley’s mode of speech, even when public decorum repressed his “awful language,” was formidable in its curtness and self-possession. Mr. Thesiger sanctioned the request, Mr. Bulstrode sat down, and Mr. Hawley continued.", "홀리 씨의 말투는, 공적인 예의가 그의 \"끔찍한 언사\"를 억누를 때조차도, 그 간결함과 침착함이 만만치 않았다. 세시저 씨가 그 청을 받아들였고, 불스트로드 씨는 자리에 앉았으며, 홀리 씨는 말을 이었다."]], [["“In what I have to say, Mr.", "\"제가 드릴 말씀은, 의장님, 단지 저 자신을 위해 드리는 것이 아닙니다."], ["Chairman, I am not speaking simply on my own behalf: I am speaking with the concurrence and at the express request of no fewer than eight of my fellow-townsmen, who are immediately around us.", "저는 바로 우리 주위에 있는, 적어도 여덟 명에 이르는 동료 읍민들의 동의를 얻어, 또 그들의 명백한 요청에 따라 말씀드리는 것입니다. 불스트로드 씨가 단지 납세자로서가 아니라 신사들 가운데 한 신사로서 맡고 있는 공직에서 물러날 것을 요구해야 한다는 것이 우리의 한결같은 정서입니다."], ["It is our united sentiment that Mr.", "그리고 저는 지금 그에게 그것을 요구하는 바입니다."], ["Bulstrode should be called upon—and I do now call upon him—to resign public positions which he holds not simply as a tax-payer, but as a gentleman among gentlemen.", "정황상 법이 단죄할 수는 없으나, 법적으로 처벌 가능한 많은 일들보다 더 나쁠 수도 있는 관행과 행위가 있습니다."], ["There are practices and there are acts which, owing to circumstances, the law cannot visit, though they may be worse than many things which are legally punishable.", "정직한 사람들과 신사들은, 그런 행위를 저지르는 자들과 어울리기를 원치 않는다면, 스스로 할 수 있는 최선을 다해 자신을 지켜야 합니다."], ["Honest men and gentlemen, if they don’t want the company of people who perpetrate such acts, have got to defend themselves as they best can, and that is what I and the friends whom I may call my clients in this affair are determined to do.", "그리고 그것이야말로 저와, 이 일에서 제가 의뢰인이라 부를 만한 친구들이 하기로 결심한 바입니다. 저는 불스트로드 씨가 수치스러운 행위를 저질렀다고 말하는 것이 아닙니다."], ["I don’t say that Mr. Bulstrode has been guilty of shameful acts, but I call upon him either publicly to deny and confute the scandalous statements made against him by a man now dead, and who died in his house—the statement that he was for many years engaged in nefarious practices, and that he won his fortune by dishonest procedures—or else to withdraw from positions which could only have been allowed him as a gentleman among gentlemen.”", "다만 그에게 요구하건대, 지금은 죽었고 그의 집에서 죽은 한 사내가 그에게 퍼부은 추잡한 진술―그가 여러 해 동안 사악한 짓을 저질렀으며 부정한 수단으로 재산을 모았다는 진술―을 공개적으로 부인하고 논박하든지, 아니면 신사들 가운데 한 신사로서가 아니고는 결코 허락될 수 없었던 그 지위에서 물러나든지 하라는 것입니다.\""]], [["All eyes in the room were turned on Mr. Bulstrode, who, since the first mention of his name, had been going through a crisis of feeling almost too violent for his delicate frame to support.", "방 안의 모든 눈길이 불스트로드 씨에게 쏠렸으니, 그는 자기 이름이 처음 거론된 순간부터, 그 가냘픈 몸으로는 거의 견디기 힘들 만큼 격렬한 감정의 위기를 겪고 있었다."], ["Lydgate, who himself was undergoing a shock as from the terrible practical interpretation of some faint augury, felt, nevertheless, that his own movement of resentful hatred was checked by that instinct of the Healer which thinks first of bringing rescue or relief to the sufferer, when he looked at the shrunken misery of Bulstrode’s livid face.", "어떤 희미한 전조의 무시무시한 실제적 해석을 마주한 듯 충격을 받고 있던 리드게이트조차도, 불스트로드의 핏기 없는 얼굴에 어린 쪼그라든 비참함을 바라보았을 때, 자기 안에서 일던 분노에 찬 증오의 움직임이, 무엇보다 먼저 고통받는 자에게 구원이나 위안을 가져다줄 것을 생각하는 치유자의 본능에 의해 제지당하는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["The quick vision that his life was after all a failure, that he was a dishonored man, and must quail before the glance of those towards whom he had habitually assumed the attitude of a reprover—that God had disowned him before men and left him unscreened to the triumphant scorn of those who were glad to have their hatred justified—the sense of utter futility in that equivocation with his conscience in dealing with the life of his accomplice, an equivocation which now turned venomously upon him with the full-grown fang of a discovered lie:—all this rushed through him like the agony of terror which fails to kill, and leaves the ears still open to the returning wave of execration.", "자기 삶이 결국 실패였다는 것, 자신이 불명예스러운 사람이며, 늘 책망하는 태도를 취해 왔던 그 사람들의 눈길 앞에서 움츠러들어야 한다는 것―하느님이 사람들 앞에서 그를 부인하고, 자기들의 증오가 정당화되어 기뻐하는 자들의 의기양양한 멸시에 그를 가린 것 없이 내버려 두었다는 것―공범의 목숨을 다루면서 양심을 속였던 그 기만이 부질없었다는 자각, 이제 드러난 거짓말의 다 자란 송곳니를 드러내며 독을 품고 그에게 덤벼드는 그 기만―이 모든 것이, 죽이지는 못한 채 귀만은 열어 둔 채로 되돌아오는 저주의 물결을 듣게 하는 공포의 고통처럼 그를 휩쓸고 지나갔다."], ["The sudden sense of exposure after the re-established sense of safety came—not to the coarse organization of a criminal, but to the susceptible nerve of a man whose intensest being lay in such mastery and predominance as the conditions of his life had shaped for him.", "안전하다는 감각을 다시 확립한 뒤에 찾아온 갑작스러운 노출의 감각은, 범죄자의 거친 신경이 아니라, 자기 삶의 조건이 빚어낸 그러한 지배와 우위에 가장 강렬한 존재 의의를 두었던 한 사내의 예민한 신경에 들이닥쳤다."]], [["But in that intense being lay the strength of reaction.", "그러나 바로 그 강렬한 존재 의의 속에 반작용의 힘이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Through all his bodily infirmity there ran a tenacious nerve of ambitious self-preserving will, which had continually leaped out like a flame, scattering all doctrinal fears, and which, even while he sat an object of compassion for the merciful, was beginning to stir and glow under his ashy paleness.", "그의 온갖 육체적 허약함을 관통하여 야심에 찬 자기 보존의 의지가 질긴 신경처럼 흐르고 있었으니, 그것은 온갖 교리상의 두려움을 흩어 버리며 끊임없이 불꽃처럼 솟구쳐 올랐고, 자비로운 이들에게 동정의 대상이 되어 앉아 있는 동안에도 그의 잿빛 창백함 아래서 꿈틀거리며 타오르기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Before the last words were out of Mr. Hawley’s mouth, Bulstrode felt that he should answer, and that his answer would be a retort.", "홀리 씨의 마지막 말이 입에서 채 떨어지기도 전에, 불스트로드는 자기가 대답하리라는 것을, 그리고 그 대답이 반박이 되리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He dared not get up and say, “I am not guilty, the whole story is false”—even if he had dared this, it would have seemed to him, under his present keen sense of betrayal, as vain as to pull, for covering to his nakedness, a frail rag which would rend at every little strain.", "그는 감히 일어나서 \"나는 죄가 없으며, 그 이야기는 죄다 거짓이오\"라고 말하지는 못했다―설령 그렇게 할 용기가 있었다 해도, 지금처럼 배신감을 예리하게 느끼는 그에게는, 벌거벗은 몸을 가리려고, 조금만 힘을 주어도 찢어질 연약한 누더기를 끌어당기는 것만큼이나 부질없게 여겨졌을 것이다."]], [["For a few moments there was total silence, while every man in the room was looking at Bulstrode.", "잠시 동안 완전한 침묵이 흘렀고, 방 안의 모든 사람이 불스트로드를 바라보았다."], ["He sat perfectly still, leaning hard against the back of his chair; he could not venture to rise, and when he began to speak he pressed his hands upon the seat on each side of him.", "그는 의자 등받이에 몸을 힘껏 기댄 채 완전히 가만히 앉아 있었다. 그는 감히 일어날 엄두를 내지 못했고, 입을 열기 시작했을 때는 양옆의 의자 좌석을 두 손으로 눌렀다."], ["But his voice was perfectly audible, though hoarser than usual, and his words were distinctly pronounced, though he paused between sentence as if short of breath. He said, turning first toward Mr.", "그러나 그의 목소리는 평소보다 쉬기는 했으나 또렷이 들렸고, 마치 숨이 가쁜 듯 문장 사이사이에 멈추기는 했으나 그의 말은 분명하게 발음되었다."], ["Thesiger, and then looking at Mr. Hawley—", "그는 먼저 세시저 씨 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다가 홀리 씨를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I protest before you, sir, as a Christian minister, against the sanction of proceedings towards me which are dictated by virulent hatred.", "\"기독교 목사이신 당신 앞에서, 저는 항의합니다. 악독한 증오에 휘둘린 저에 대한 이 절차를 용인하는 데 항의하는 바입니다."], ["Those who are hostile to me are glad to believe any libel uttered by a loose tongue against me.", "저에게 적의를 품은 자들은 함부로 놀리는 혀가 저에 대해 내뱉은 어떤 비방이든 기꺼이 믿으려 합니다."], ["And their consciences become strict against me.", "그리고 그들의 양심은 유독 저에게만 엄격해집니다."], ["Say that the evil-speaking of which I am to be made the victim accuses me of malpractices—”", "제가 그 희생양이 될 그 험담이 저를 비행으로 고발한다고 칩시다―\""], ["here Bulstrode’s voice rose and took on a more biting accent, till it seemed a low cry—“who shall be my accuser?", "여기서 불스트로드의 목소리가 높아지며 더 신랄한 어조를 띠어, 마침내 낮은 절규처럼 들렸다. \"누가 저의 고발자가 되겠습니까?"], ["Not men whose own lives are unchristian, nay, scandalous—not men who themselves use low instruments to carry out their ends—whose profession is a tissue of chicanery—who have been spending their income on their own sensual enjoyments, while I have been devoting mine to advance the best objects with regard to this life and the next.”", "제 삶이 비기독교적이고, 아니 추문투성이인 자들은 아닐 테지요―제 목적을 이루려고 비열한 수단을 동원하는 자들도 아닐 테지요―그 직업이 협잡으로 짜인 자들도 아닐 테지요―제 수입을 제 육욕의 향락에 탕진해 온 자들도 아닐 테지요. 그러는 동안 저는 제 수입을 이승과 저승에 관한 최선의 목적을 위해 바쳐 왔습니다.\""]], [["After the word chicanery there was a growing noise, half of murmurs and half of hisses, while four persons started up at once—Mr. Hawley, Mr. Toller, Mr. Chichely, and Mr. Hackbutt; but Mr. Hawley’s outburst was instantaneous, and left the others behind in silence.", "협잡이라는 말이 나오자, 절반은 웅성거림이요 절반은 야유인 소음이 점점 커졌고, 동시에 네 사람이 벌떡 일어났다―홀리 씨, 톨러 씨, 치철리 씨, 그리고 해크벗 씨가. 그러나 홀리 씨의 폭발은 즉각적이어서, 나머지 사람들을 침묵 속에 뒤처지게 했다."]], [["“If you mean me, sir, I call you and every one else to the inspection of my professional life.", "\"저를 두고 하는 말이라면, 선생, 저는 당신을 비롯한 누구든 제 직업적 삶을 검증해 보라고 촉구하는 바입니다."], ["As to Christian or unchristian, I repudiate your canting palavering Christianity; and as to the way in which I spend my income, it is not my principle to maintain thieves and cheat offspring of their due inheritance in order to support religion and set myself up as a saintly Killjoy.", "기독교적이냐 비기독교적이냐 하는 데 대해서는, 저는 당신의 위선적이고 입에 발린 기독교를 거부합니다. 그리고 제가 수입을 쓰는 방식으로 말하자면, 종교를 떠받치고 저 자신을 성스러운 흥취 깨는 자로 내세우려고 도둑들을 거두어 먹이고 자식들에게서 정당한 상속을 가로채는 것은 제 원칙이 아닙니다."], ["I affect no niceness of conscience—I have not found any nice standards necessary yet to measure your actions by, sir.", "저는 양심의 결벽 따위는 가장하지 않습니다―저는 당신의 행위를 재기 위해 무슨 까다로운 잣대가 필요하다고 아직 여겨 본 적이 없소이다, 선생."], ["And I again call upon you to enter into satisfactory explanations concerning the scandals against you, or else to withdraw from posts in which we at any rate decline you as a colleague.", "그리고 다시 한 번 당신에게 촉구하건대, 당신에 대한 추문에 관해 만족스러운 해명을 내놓든지, 아니면 우리가 어쨌든 동료로 받아들이기를 거부하는 그 지위에서 물러나든지 하시오."], ["I say, sir, we decline to co-operate with a man whose character is not cleared from infamous lights cast upon it, not only by reports but by recent actions.”", "선생, 저는 말하건대, 우리는 소문뿐 아니라 최근의 행위로도 추악한 빛이 드리워진, 그 인격이 결백하게 밝혀지지 않은 사람과는 협력하기를 거부합니다.\""]], [["“Allow me, Mr. Hawley,”", "\"잠깐만요, 홀리 씨.\""], ["said the chairman; and Mr. Hawley, still fuming, bowed half impatiently, and sat down with his hands thrust deep in his pockets.", "의장이 말하자, 홀리 씨는 여전히 분을 삭이지 못한 채 다소 참을성 없이 고개를 숙이고는, 두 손을 주머니 깊숙이 찔러 넣은 채 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Mr. Bulstrode, it is not desirable, I think, to prolong the present discussion,”", "\"불스트로드 씨, 지금의 논의를 길게 끄는 것은 바람직하지 않다고 생각합니다.\""], ["said Mr. Thesiger, turning to the pallid trembling man; “I must so far concur with what has fallen from Mr.", "세시저 씨가 창백하게 떨고 있는 사내를 향해 몸을 돌리며 말했다."], ["Hawley in expression of a general feeling, as to think it due to your Christian profession that you should clear yourself, if possible, from unhappy aspersions.", "\"전반적인 정서를 표현한 홀리 씨의 말에, 저도 이만큼은 동의할 수밖에 없습니다. 즉, 당신의 기독교 신앙고백을 보아서라도, 가능하다면 이 불행한 중상으로부터 스스로를 결백하게 밝히는 것이 마땅하다고 생각합니다."], ["I for my part should be willing to give you full opportunity and hearing.", "저로서는 기꺼이 당신에게 충분한 기회와 청문의 자리를 드리겠습니다."], ["But I must say that your present attitude is painfully inconsistent with those principles which you have sought to identify yourself with, and for the honor of which I am bound to care.", "그러나 분명히 말씀드리건대, 지금 당신의 태도는 당신이 스스로 일치시키고자 애써 온, 그리고 제가 그 명예를 마땅히 지켜야 할 그 원칙들과 고통스러울 만큼 어긋납니다."], ["I recommend you at present, as your clergyman, and one who hopes for your reinstatement in respect, to quit the room, and avoid further hindrance to business.”", "저는 지금, 당신의 성직자로서, 또 당신이 다시 존경받는 자리로 회복되기를 바라는 한 사람으로서, 이 방을 떠나 더 이상 업무에 지장을 주지 마시기를 권합니다.\""]], [["Bulstrode, after a moment’s hesitation, took his hat from the floor and slowly rose, but he grasped the corner of the chair so totteringly that Lydgate felt sure there was not strength enough in him to walk away without support.", "불스트로드는 잠시 망설인 뒤, 바닥에서 모자를 집어 들고 천천히 일어났으나, 의자 모서리를 어찌나 위태롭게 부여잡았는지 리드게이트는 그에게 부축 없이 걸어 나갈 만한 힘이 남아 있지 않다고 확신했다. 그가 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["What could he do? He could not see a man sink close to him for want of help.", "그는 한 사람이 도움이 없어 자기 바로 곁에서 쓰러지는 것을 보고만 있을 수는 없었다."], ["He rose and gave his arm to Bulstrode, and in that way led him out of the room; yet this act, which might have been one of gentle duty and pure compassion, was at this moment unspeakably bitter to him.", "그는 일어나서 불스트로드에게 팔을 내주었고, 그렇게 그를 부축하여 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다. 그러나 부드러운 의무이자 순수한 연민의 행위가 될 수도 있었을 이 행동이, 이 순간 그에게는 이루 말할 수 없이 쓰라렸다."], ["It seemed as if he were putting his sign-manual to that association of himself with Bulstrode, of which he now saw the full meaning as it must have presented itself to other minds.", "마치 자기와 불스트로드를 한데 엮는 그 연관에 친필 서명을 하는 것만 같았으니, 그는 이제 그것이 다른 사람들의 마음에 어떻게 비쳤을지, 그 온전한 의미를 깨달았던 것이다."], ["He now felt the conviction that this man who was leaning tremblingly on his arm, had given him the thousand pounds as a bribe, and that somehow the treatment of Raffles had been tampered with from an evil motive.", "그는 이제 자기 팔에 떨며 기댄 이 사내가 자기에게 천 파운드를 뇌물로 주었으며, 어떤 사악한 동기에서 래플스의 치료가 어떤 식으로든 조작되었다는 확신을 느꼈다."], ["The inferences were closely linked enough; the town knew of the loan, believed it to be a bribe, and believed that he took it as a bribe.", "그 추론들은 충분히 긴밀하게 연결되어 있었으니, 읍내는 그 대출을 알았고, 그것을 뇌물이라 믿었으며, 그가 그것을 뇌물로 받았다고 믿었던 것이다."]], [["Poor Lydgate, his mind struggling under the terrible clutch of this revelation, was all the while morally forced to take Mr. Bulstrode to the Bank, send a man off for his carriage, and wait to accompany him home.", "가엾은 리드게이트는, 이 폭로의 무시무시한 손아귀 아래 마음이 몸부림치는 내내, 도덕적으로 불스트로드 씨를 은행으로 데려가, 사람을 보내 그의 마차를 부르고, 그를 집까지 바래다주려고 기다릴 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Meanwhile the business of the meeting was despatched, and fringed off into eager discussion among various groups concerning this affair of Bulstrode—and Lydgate.", "한편 회의의 업무는 처리되었고, 불스트로드와―그리고 리드게이트에 관한 이 일을 두고 여러 무리 사이에서 벌어진 열띤 논의로 가닥가닥 흩어졌다."]], [["Mr. Brooke, who had before heard only imperfect hints of it, and was very uneasy that he had “gone a little too far” in countenancing Bulstrode, now got himself fully informed, and felt some benevolent sadness in talking to Mr. Farebrother about the ugly light in which Lydgate had come to be regarded. Mr. Farebrother was going to walk back to Lowick.", "전에는 이 일에 관해 불완전한 암시만 들었을 뿐이며, 자기가 불스트로드를 두둔하는 데 \"조금 지나쳤다\"는 것이 몹시 불안했던 브룩 씨는, 이제 자초지종을 죄다 알게 되었고, 리드게이트가 그토록 추한 시선을 받게 된 처지를 두고 페어브러더 씨와 이야기하면서 어떤 자비로운 슬픔을 느꼈다. 페어브러더 씨는 로윅까지 걸어서 돌아가려던 참이었다."]], [["“Step into my carriage,”", "\"내 마차에 타시지요.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“I am going round to see Mrs. Casaubon.", "\"나는 캐소본 부인을 보러 돌아가는 길이오."], ["She was to come back from Yorkshire last night.", "그 애가 어젯밤 요크셔에서 돌아오기로 되어 있었거든."], ["She will like to see me, you know.”", "그 애가 나를 보면 좋아할 거요, 알다시피.\""]], [["So they drove along, Mr.", "그리하여 그들은 마차를 몰고 갔으니, 브룩 씨는 리드게이트의 행실에 정말로 시커먼 구석이 있었던 것은 아니리라는 마음씨 좋은 희망을 품고 이야기를 늘어놓았다―그가 자기 숙부 고드윈 경의 편지를 가져왔을 때, 브룩 씨는 그 젊은이가 여느 사람보다 훨씬 뛰어나다는 것을 알아보았던 터였다."], ["Brooke chatting with good-natured hope that there had not really been anything black in Lydgate’s behavior—a young fellow whom he had seen to be quite above the common mark, when he brought a letter from his uncle Sir Godwin. Mr.", "페어브러더 씨는 말을 거의 하지 않았다."], ["Farebrother said little: he was deeply mournful: with a keen perception of human weakness, he could not be confident that under the pressure of humiliating needs Lydgate had not fallen below himself.", "그는 깊이 침울했다. 인간의 약점을 예리하게 통찰하는 그로서는, 굴욕적인 궁핍의 압박 아래서 리드게이트가 자기 자신보다 못한 데로 떨어지지 않았으리라고 확신할 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["When the carriage drove up to the gate of the Manor, Dorothea was out on the gravel, and came to greet them.", "마차가 저택의 문 앞에 다다랐을 때, 도러시아는 자갈길에 나와 있다가 그들을 맞으러 다가왔다."]], [["“Well, my dear,” said Mr.", "\"음, 얘야,\""], ["Brooke, “we have just come from a meeting—a sanitary meeting, you know.”", "브룩 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 방금 회의에서 오는 길이란다―위생 회의 말이다, 알다시피.\""]], [["“Was Mr. Lydgate there?”", "\"리드게이트 씨도 거기 계셨나요?\""], ["said Dorothea, who looked full of health and animation, and stood with her head bare under the gleaming April lights.", "도러시아가 말했는데, 그녀는 건강과 생기가 넘치는 모습으로, 반짝이는 4월의 햇살 아래 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 서 있었다."], ["“I want to see him and have a great consultation with him about the Hospital.", "\"그분을 만나서 병원에 관해 크게 의논하고 싶어요."], ["I have engaged with Mr. Bulstrode to do so.”", "그러기로 불스트로드 씨와 약속했거든요.\""]], [["“Oh, my dear,”", "\"오, 얘야,\""], ["said Mr.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["Brooke, “we have been hearing bad news—bad news, you know.”", "\"우리가 나쁜 소식을 들었단다―나쁜 소식 말이다, 알다시피.\""]], [["They walked through the garden towards the churchyard gate, Mr.", "그들은 정원을 가로질러 교회 묘지 문 쪽으로 걸어갔으니, 페어브러더 씨가 목사관까지 계속 가려던 참이었다."], ["Farebrother wanting to go on to the parsonage; and Dorothea heard the whole sad story.", "그리고 도러시아는 그 슬픈 이야기를 죄다 들었다."]], [["She listened with deep interest, and begged to hear twice over the facts and impressions concerning Lydgate.", "그녀는 깊은 관심을 가지고 귀를 기울였고, 리드게이트에 관한 사실과 인상들을 두 번이나 들려 달라고 청했다."], ["After a short silence, pausing at the churchyard gate, and addressing Mr. Farebrother, she said energetically—", "잠시 침묵한 뒤, 교회 묘지 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 페어브러더 씨를 향해, 그녀는 힘차게 말했다."]], [["“You don’t believe that Mr. Lydgate is guilty of anything base?", "\"리드게이트 씨가 무슨 비열한 짓을 저질렀다고는 믿지 않으시지요?"], ["I will not believe it.", "저는 믿지 않겠어요."], ["Let us find out the truth and clear him!”", "진실을 밝혀내어 그분의 결백을 입증합시다!\""]], [["# BOOK VIII. SUNSET AND SUNRISE. CHAPTER LXXII.", "# BOOK VIII. SUNSET AND SUNRISE. CHAPTER LXXII."]], [["Full souls are double mirrors, making still An endless vista of fair things before, Repeating things behind.", "뒤로는 지나간 것들을 되비춘다."]], [["Dorothea’s impetuous generosity, which would have leaped at once to the vindication of Lydgate from the suspicion of having accepted money as a bribe, underwent a melancholy check when she came to consider all the circumstances of the case by the light of Mr. Farebrother’s experience.", "리드게이트가 뇌물로 돈을 받았다는 의혹에서 그를 변호하려 단숨에 달려들었을 도로시아의 격정적인 관대함은, 페어브러더 씨의 경험에 비추어 이 사건의 모든 정황을 헤아리게 되자 우울한 제동에 부딪혔다."]], [["“It is a delicate matter to touch,”", "\"이건 건드리기에 미묘한 문제입니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“How can we begin to inquire into it?", "\"어떻게 조사를 시작할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["It must be either publicly by setting the magistrate and coroner to work, or privately by questioning Lydgate.", "공개적으로 치안 판사와 검시관을 움직이게 하든가, 아니면 사적으로 리드게이트에게 캐묻든가 둘 중 하나여야 합니다. 첫 번째 방법은 근거로 삼을 확실한 토대가 없습니다."], ["As to the first proceeding there is no solid ground to go upon, else Hawley would have adopted it; and as to opening the subject with Lydgate, I confess I should shrink from it.", "그게 있었다면 홀리가 진작 그렇게 했겠지요. 그리고 리드게이트에게 그 화제를 꺼내는 일이라면, 솔직히 저는 망설여집니다."], ["He would probably take it as a deadly insult.", "그는 아마 그것을 치명적인 모욕으로 받아들일 겁니다."], ["I have more than once experienced the difficulty of speaking to him on personal matters.", "개인적인 문제로 그에게 말을 거는 어려움을 저는 한두 번 겪은 게 아닙니다."], ["And—one should know the truth about his conduct beforehand, to feel very confident of a good result.”", "그리고 좋은 결과를 확신하려면, 그의 행적에 대한 진실을 미리 알고 있어야 하지요.\""]], [["“I feel convinced that his conduct has not been guilty: I believe that people are almost always better than their neighbors think they are,”", "\"저는 그의 행동에 죄가 없다고 확신합니다. 사람은 거의 언제나 이웃이 생각하는 것보다 더 나은 존재라고 믿어요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도로시아가 말했다."], ["Some of her intensest experience in the last two years had set her mind strongly in opposition to any unfavorable construction of others; and for the first time she felt rather discontented with Mr.", "지난 두 해 동안 겪은 가장 강렬한 경험들이 그녀의 마음을 타인에 대한 어떤 불리한 해석에도 강하게 맞서도록 굳혀 놓았다. 그리고 그녀는 처음으로 페어브러더 씨에게 다소 불만을 느꼈다."], ["Farebrother. She disliked this cautious weighing of consequences, instead of an ardent faith in efforts of justice and mercy, which would conquer by their emotional force.", "그녀는 정의와 자비를 위한 노력에 대한 뜨거운 신념 대신 결과를 이렇게 신중히 저울질하는 태도가 못마땅했으니, 그 노력이야말로 감정의 힘으로 승리할 것이었기 때문이다."], ["Two days afterwards, he was dining at the Manor with her uncle and the Chettams, and when the dessert was standing uneaten, the servants were out of the room, and Mr.", "이틀 뒤, 그는 영주관에서 그녀의 삼촌과 체텀 부부와 함께 식사를 하고 있었다."], ["Brooke was nodding in a nap, she returned to the subject with renewed vivacity.", "디저트가 손도 대지 않은 채 놓여 있고 하인들은 방에서 나가 있었으며 브룩 씨는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있을 때, 그녀는 새로운 활기를 띠고 그 화제로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Mr. Lydgate would understand that if his friends hear a calumny about him their first wish must be to justify him.", "\"리드게이트 씨도 자기 친구들이 자신에 관한 중상을 들으면 가장 먼저 그를 변호하고 싶어 하리라는 것을 이해할 거예요."], ["What do we live for, if it is not to make life less difficult to each other?", "우리가 무엇을 위해 사나요, 서로의 삶을 덜 힘들게 해 주기 위해서가 아니라면?"], ["I cannot be indifferent to the troubles of a man who advised me in my trouble, and attended me in my illness.”", "제가 곤경에 처했을 때 조언해 주고 제가 병들었을 때 돌봐 준 사람의 어려움에 저는 무관심할 수 없어요.\""]], [["Dorothea’s tone and manner were not more energetic than they had been when she was at the head of her uncle’s table nearly three years before, and her experience since had given her more right to express a decided opinion.", "도로시아의 어조와 태도는 거의 삼 년 전 삼촌의 식탁 상석에 앉아 있을 때보다 더 격렬할 것도 없었으나, 그동안의 경험은 그녀에게 단호한 의견을 표명할 더 많은 자격을 주었다."], ["But Sir James Chettam was no longer the diffident and acquiescent suitor: he was the anxious brother-in-law, with a devout admiration for his sister, but with a constant alarm lest she should fall under some new illusion almost as bad as marrying Casaubon.", "그러나 제임스 체텀 경은 더 이상 소심하고 순종적인 구혼자가 아니었다. 그는 이제 노심초사하는 처형의 매제로서, 처형을 향한 경건한 흠모를 품으면서도 그녀가 캐소본과 결혼한 것에 못지않게 나쁜 어떤 새로운 환상에 빠지지나 않을까 끊임없이 노심초사했다."], ["He smiled much less; when he said “Exactly”", "그는 훨씬 덜 웃었다."], ["it was more often an introduction to a dissentient opinion than in those submissive bachelor days; and Dorothea found to her surprise that she had to resolve not to be afraid of him—all the more because he was really her best friend.", "그가 \"그렇지요\"라고 말할 때면 순종적인 독신 시절보다 반대 의견을 꺼내는 서두인 경우가 더 잦았다. 그래서 도로시아는 그를 두려워하지 않기로 마음먹어야 한다는 사실을 놀랍게도 깨달았으니, 그가 정말로 그녀의 가장 좋은 친구였기에 더욱 그러했다."], ["He disagreed with her now.", "지금 그는 그녀의 의견에 반대했다."]], [["“But, Dorothea,”", "\"하지만 도로시아.\""], ["he said, remonstrantly, “you can’t undertake to manage a man’s life for him in that way.", "그가 타이르듯 말했다. \"그런 식으로 남의 인생을 대신 처리해 주겠다고 나설 수는 없어요."], ["Lydgate must know—at least he will soon come to know how he stands.", "리드게이트도 자기가 어떤 처지인지 알 거예요. 적어도 곧 알게 되겠지요."], ["If he can clear himself, he will.", "자기 결백을 밝힐 수 있다면 그가 밝힐 겁니다."], ["He must act for himself.”", "그는 스스로 행동해야 해요.\""]], [["“I think his friends must wait till they find an opportunity,”", "\"그의 친구들은 기회를 찾을 때까지 기다려야 한다고 봅니다.\" 페어브러더 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["added Mr. Farebrother.", "\"이런 일도 가능하지요."], ["“It is possible—I have often felt so much weakness in myself that I can conceive even a man of honorable disposition, such as I have always believed Lydgate to be, succumbing to such a temptation as that of accepting money which was offered more or less indirectly as a bribe to insure his silence about scandalous facts long gone by.", "저 자신에게서 그토록 많은 약점을 느껴 온 터라, 제가 늘 명예로운 기질의 사람이라 믿어 온 리드게이트 같은 사람조차도 그런 유혹에 굴복할 수 있다고 상상할 수 있습니다. 오래전에 지나간 추문에 대해 그가 침묵을 지키도록 보증하는 뇌물로, 다소 간접적으로 건네진 돈을 받는 유혹 말입니다."], ["I say, I can conceive this, if he were under the pressure of hard circumstances—if he had been harassed as I feel sure Lydgate has been.", "다시 말하지만, 그가 가혹한 처지의 압박 아래 있었다면, 제가 분명 그러했으리라 확신하듯 그가 시달려 왔다면, 저는 이런 일을 상상할 수 있습니다."], ["I would not believe anything worse of him except under stringent proof.", "엄격한 증거가 없는 한 저는 그에 대해 그보다 더 나쁜 어떤 것도 믿지 않겠습니다."], ["But there is the terrible Nemesis following on some errors, that it is always possible for those who like it to interpret them into a crime: there is no proof in favor of the man outside his own consciousness and assertion.”", "하지만 어떤 과오에는 무서운 응보가 따르기 마련이니, 그것을 죄로 해석하고 싶어 하는 자들에게는 언제나 그렇게 해석할 여지가 있다는 점입니다. 그 사람 자신의 양심과 주장 외에는 그에게 유리한 증거가 전혀 없는 거지요.\""]], [["“Oh, how cruel!”", "\"아, 어쩌면 그렇게 잔인할까요!\""], ["said Dorothea, clasping her hands.", "도로시아가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."], ["“And would you not like to be the one person who believed in that man’s innocence, if the rest of the world belied him?", "\"그리고 세상 모두가 그 사람을 거짓되이 헐뜯는다 해도, 그의 결백을 믿어 주는 단 한 사람이 되고 싶지 않으신가요?"], ["Besides, there is a man’s character beforehand to speak for him.”", "게다가 그를 위해 미리 변호해 줄 그 사람의 인격이 있잖아요.\""]], [["“But, my dear Mrs.", "\"하지만, 친애하는 캐소본 부인.\""], ["Casaubon,” said Mr.", "페어브러더 씨가 그녀의 열정에 부드럽게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Farebrother, smiling gently at her ardor, “character is not cut in marble—it is not something solid and unalterable.", "\"인격이란 대리석에 새겨진 것이 아닙니다. 단단하고 변치 않는 무엇이 아니에요."], ["It is something living and changing, and may become diseased as our bodies do.”", "그것은 살아 움직이고 변하는 것이며, 우리 육신이 그러하듯 병들 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["“Then it may be rescued and healed,”", "\"그렇다면 구원되고 치유될 수도 있겠지요.\" 도로시아가 말했다."], ["said Dorothea “I should not be afraid of asking Mr. Lydgate to tell me the truth, that I might help him.", "\"저는 리드게이트 씨에게 진실을 말해 달라고 청하는 게 두렵지 않을 거예요, 그를 도울 수 있도록 말이지요."], ["Why should I be afraid?", "제가 왜 두려워해야 하나요?"], ["Now that I am not to have the land, James, I might do as Mr. Bulstrode proposed, and take his place in providing for the Hospital; and I have to consult Mr.", "이제 그 땅을 갖지 않게 되었으니, 제임스, 저는 불스트로드 씨가 제안한 대로 할 수 있고, 병원을 후원하는 일에서 그의 자리를 대신할 수도 있어요."], ["Lydgate, to know thoroughly what are the prospects of doing good by keeping up the present plans.", "그러려면 리드게이트 씨와 상의해서, 현재의 계획을 유지함으로써 좋은 일을 할 가망이 어떤지 철저히 알아봐야 해요."], ["There is the best opportunity in the world for me to ask for his confidence; and he would be able to tell me things which might make all the circumstances clear.", "그러니 제가 그에게 속내를 털어놓아 달라고 청할 더없이 좋은 기회인 셈이지요. 그러면 그는 모든 정황을 분명히 밝혀 줄 만한 이야기들을 제게 해 줄 수 있을 거예요."], ["Then we would all stand by him and bring him out of his trouble.", "그러고 나면 우리 모두가 그의 편에 서서 그를 곤경에서 끌어내 줄 수 있겠지요."], ["People glorify all sorts of bravery except the bravery they might show on behalf of their nearest neighbors.”", "사람들은 온갖 종류의 용기를 찬양하면서도, 가장 가까운 이웃을 위해 보일 수 있는 용기만은 빼놓는답니다.\""], ["Dorothea’s eyes had a moist brightness in them, and the changed tones of her voice roused her uncle, who began to listen.", "도로시아의 눈에는 촉촉한 빛이 어렸고, 달라진 그녀의 목소리에 삼촌이 정신을 차리고 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."]], [["“It is true that a woman may venture on some efforts of sympathy which would hardly succeed if we men undertook them,”", "\"우리 남자들이 나섰다가는 좀처럼 성공하지 못할 어떤 공감의 노력을, 여자라면 감히 시도해 볼 수 있다는 것은 사실입니다.\""], ["said Mr. Farebrother, almost converted by Dorothea’s ardor.", "도로시아의 열정에 거의 설득당한 페어브러더 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Surely, a woman is bound to be cautious and listen to those who know the world better than she does.”", "\"여자라면 분명 신중해야 하고 자기보다 세상을 더 잘 아는 이들의 말에 귀를 기울여야지요.\""], ["said Sir James, with his little frown.", "제임스 경이 살짝 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“Whatever you do in the end, Dorothea, you should really keep back at present, and not volunteer any meddling with this Bulstrode business.", "\"결국 무엇을 하든, 도로시아, 지금은 정말로 자제하고 이 불스트로드 사건에 자진해서 끼어들지 말아야 해요."], ["We don’t know yet what may turn up.", "우리는 아직 무슨 일이 드러날지 몰라요."], ["You must agree with me?”", "제 말에 동의하시지요?\""], ["he ended, looking at Mr. Farebrother.", "그가 페어브러더 씨를 바라보며 말을 맺었다."]], [["“I do think it would be better to wait,”", "\"기다리는 편이 나으리라고 저도 생각합니다.\""], ["said the latter.", "후자가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, my dear,” said Mr.", "\"그래, 그래, 얘야.\""], ["Brooke, not quite knowing at what point the discussion had arrived, but coming up to it with a contribution which was generally appropriate.", "브룩 씨가 논의가 어느 지점에 이르렀는지 정확히는 모르면서도, 대체로 적절한 한마디를 보태며 끼어들었다."], ["“It is easy to go too far, you know.", "\"도가 지나치기는 쉬운 법이지, 알다시피."], ["You must not let your ideas run away with you.", "네 생각에 끌려가서는 안 돼."], ["And as to being in a hurry to put money into schemes—it won’t do, you know.", "그리고 서둘러 무슨 사업에 돈을 넣는 것도 말이지, 그건 안 될 일이야, 알겠니."], ["Garth has drawn me in uncommonly with repairs, draining, that sort of thing: I’m uncommonly out of pocket with one thing or another.", "가스가 수리니 배수니 그런 일로 나를 어지간히 끌어들였단다. 이것저것으로 주머니가 어지간히 비었지."], ["I must pull up.", "나는 고삐를 당겨야 해."], ["As for you, Chettam, you are spending a fortune on those oak fences round your demesne.”", "너로 말하자면, 체텀, 자네는 그 영지를 두른 떡갈나무 울타리에 거금을 쏟아붓고 있더군.\""]], [["Dorothea, submitting uneasily to this discouragement, went with Celia into the library, which was her usual drawing-room.", "이 만류에 불편하게 따른 도로시아는 실리아와 함께 서재로 갔는데, 그곳은 그녀가 평소 거실로 쓰는 방이었다."]], [["“Now, Dodo, do listen to what James says,”", "\"자, 도도, 제발 제임스가 하는 말을 좀 들어.\""], ["said Celia, “else you will be getting into a scrape.", "실리아가 말했다. \"안 그러면 곤경에 빠질 거야."], ["You always did, and you always will, when you set about doing as you please.", "언니는 늘 그랬고, 마음 내키는 대로 하려 들면 앞으로도 늘 그럴 거야."], ["And I think it is a mercy now after all that you have got James to think for you.", "그래도 이젠 언니를 위해 생각해 줄 제임스가 있으니 그나마 다행이라고 난 생각해."], ["He lets you have your plans, only he hinders you from being taken in.", "그이는 언니가 계획을 세우게 두면서도, 언니가 속아 넘어가는 것만은 막아 주잖아."], ["And that is the good of having a brother instead of a husband.", "그게 바로 남편 대신 오라버니가 있어서 좋은 점이지."], ["A husband would not let you have your plans.”", "남편이라면 언니가 계획을 세우게 두지 않을 테니까.\""]], [["“As if I wanted a husband!”", "\"내가 남편을 원하기라도 한다는 듯이!\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도로시아가 말했다."], ["“I only want not to have my feelings checked at every turn.”", "\"난 그저 사사건건 내 감정이 가로막히지 않기를 바랄 뿐이야.\""], ["Mrs. Casaubon was still undisciplined enough to burst into angry tears.", "캐소본 부인은 아직도 분에 차서 눈물을 터뜨릴 만큼 다스려지지 않은 상태였다."]], [["“Now, really, Dodo,”", "\"정말이지, 도도.\""], ["said Celia, with rather a deeper guttural than usual, “you are contradictory: first one thing and then another.", "실리아가 평소보다 다소 깊은 목쉰 소리로 말했다. \"언니는 모순적이야."], ["You used to submit to Mr.", "처음엔 이 말 했다가 다음엔 저 말 했다가."], ["Casaubon quite shamefully: I think you would have given up ever coming to see me if he had asked you.”", "언니는 캐소본 씨한테는 정말 창피할 만큼 순종했잖아. 그이가 부탁했다면 나를 보러 오는 것조차 그만뒀을걸.\""]], [["“Of course I submitted to him, because it was my duty; it was my feeling for him,”", "\"물론 나는 그분께 순종했지, 그게 내 의무였으니까. 그건 그분을 향한 내 마음이었어.\""], ["said Dorothea, looking through the prism of her tears.", "도로시아가 눈물의 프리즘을 통해 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Then why can’t you think it your duty to submit a little to what James wishes?”", "\"그럼 제임스가 바라는 바에 조금 순종하는 것도 언니의 의무라고 생각할 수는 없어?\""], ["said Celia, with a sense of stringency in her argument.", "자기 논리에 빈틈이 없다는 느낌으로 실리아가 말했다."], ["“Because he only wishes what is for your own good.", "\"그이는 오직 언니에게 이로운 것만 바라니까."], ["And, of course, men know best about everything, except what women know better.”", "그리고 물론, 남자들은 모든 것에 대해 가장 잘 알아, 여자들이 더 잘 아는 것만 빼고는.\""], ["Dorothea laughed and forgot her tears.", "도로시아는 웃음을 터뜨리며 눈물을 잊었다."]], [["“Well, I mean about babies and those things,”", "\"음, 내 말은 아기들이랑 그런 것들에 관해서야.\""], ["explained Celia.", "실리아가 설명했다."], ["“I should not give up to James when I knew he was wrong, as you used to do to Mr. Casaubon.”", "\"나라면 제임스가 틀렸다는 걸 알면서, 언니가 캐소본 씨한테 그러던 것처럼 그이한테 굽히지는 않을 거야.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXXIII.", "# CHAPTER LXXIII."]], [["Pity the laden one; this wandering woe May visit you and me.", "그대와 나를 찾아올지도 모르니."]], [["When Lydgate had allayed Mrs. Bulstrode’s anxiety by telling her that her husband had been seized with faintness at the meeting, but that he trusted soon to see him better and would call again the next day, unless she sent for him earlier, he went directly home, got on his horse, and rode three miles out of the town for the sake of being out of reach.", "리드게이트는 불스트로드 부인에게, 남편이 회의 도중 졸도하였으나 곧 차도가 있으리라 믿으며, 만약 그녀가 더 일찍 사람을 보내지 않는다면 이튿날 다시 들르겠다고 말하여 그녀의 불안을 가라앉힌 뒤, 곧장 집으로 가서 말에 올라타 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있기 위해 읍에서 삼 마일 밖으로 말을 달렸다."]], [["He felt himself becoming violent and unreasonable as if raging under the pain of stings: he was ready to curse the day on which he had come to Middlemarch.", "그는 마치 가시에 찔리는 고통 아래서 격분하기라도 하는 것처럼, 자신이 난폭하고 분별없어지는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 미들마치에 온 그날을 저주할 지경이었다."], ["Everything that had happened to him there seemed a mere preparation for this hateful fatality, which had come as a blight on his honorable ambition, and must make even people who had only vulgar standards regard his reputation as irrevocably damaged.", "그곳에서 그에게 일어난 모든 일이 이 가증스러운 운명을 위한 한낱 준비에 불과한 듯했다. 그 운명은 그의 명예로운 야망에 마름병처럼 들이닥쳐, 천박한 기준밖에 지니지 못한 사람들조차 그의 평판이 돌이킬 수 없이 손상되었다고 여기게 만들 것이었다."], ["In such moments a man can hardly escape being unloving.", "그런 순간에 인간은 애정 없는 마음에서 벗어나기 어려운 법이다."], ["Lydgate thought of himself as the sufferer, and of others as the agents who had injured his lot.", "리드게이트는 스스로를 고통받는 자로, 다른 이들을 자기 운명을 해친 가해자로 생각했다."], ["He had meant everything to turn out differently; and others had thrust themselves into his life and thwarted his purposes.", "그는 모든 것이 다르게 풀려 가기를 바랐건만, 다른 이들이 그의 삶에 비집고 들어와 그의 목적을 가로막았다."], ["His marriage seemed an unmitigated calamity; and he was afraid of going to Rosamond before he had vented himself in this solitary rage, lest the mere sight of her should exasperate him and make him behave unwarrantably.", "그의 결혼은 완화될 길 없는 재앙처럼 보였다. 그래서 그는 이 고독한 분노를 다 쏟아 내기 전에는 로저먼드에게 가기가 두려웠으니, 그녀를 보는 것만으로도 자신이 격앙되어 부당하게 행동할까 봐 걱정되었기 때문이다."], ["There are episodes in most men’s lives in which their highest qualities can only cast a deterring shadow over the objects that fill their inward vision: Lydgate’s tenderheartedness was present just then only as a dread lest he should offend against it, not as an emotion that swayed him to tenderness.", "대부분의 사람의 생애에는 그들의 가장 고결한 자질이 내면의 시야를 채운 대상들 위에 한낱 가로막는 그림자만을 드리우는 순간들이 있다. 리드게이트의 다정한 마음은 바로 그때 그가 그것을 거스를까 두려워하는 형태로만 존재했을 뿐, 그를 부드러움으로 이끄는 감정으로는 존재하지 않았다."], ["For he was very miserable.", "그는 무척이나 비참했기 때문이다."], ["Only those who know the supremacy of the intellectual life—the life which has a seed of ennobling thought and purpose within it—can understand the grief of one who falls from that serene activity into the absorbing soul-wasting struggle with worldly annoyances.", "지성적인 삶의 지고함을—고결한 사유와 목적의 씨앗을 품은 그 삶을—아는 사람만이, 그 평온한 활동에서 떨어져 세속적인 골칫거리와 영혼을 좀먹는 몰입의 투쟁으로 추락한 자의 비탄을 이해할 수 있다."]], [["How was he to live on without vindicating himself among people who suspected him of baseness?", "자신을 비열하다고 의심하는 사람들 사이에서 결백을 입증하지 못한 채 어떻게 살아갈 것인가?"], ["How could he go silently away from Middlemarch as if he were retreating before a just condemnation?", "정당한 단죄 앞에서 물러서는 것처럼 어떻게 미들마치를 말없이 떠날 수 있겠는가?"], ["And yet how was he to set about vindicating himself?", "그러나 또한 어떻게 자기 결백을 입증하는 일에 착수할 것인가?"]], [["For that scene at the meeting, which he had just witnessed, although it had told him no particulars, had been enough to make his own situation thoroughly clear to him.", "방금 그가 목격한 회의에서의 그 장면은, 비록 그에게 구체적인 내막을 일러 주지는 않았으나, 자신의 처지를 철저히 명확하게 깨닫게 하기에는 충분했기 때문이다. 불스트로드는 래플스 쪽에서 추문이 폭로될까 두려워하고 있었다."], ["Bulstrode had been in dread of scandalous disclosures on the part of Raffles.", "이제 리드게이트는 이 사건의 모든 개연성을 짜 맞출 수 있었다."], ["Lydgate could now construct all the probabilities of the case.", "\"그는 내가 듣는 데서 무언가 폭로될까 두려워했어."], ["“He was afraid of some betrayal in my hearing: all he wanted was to bind me to him by a strong obligation: that was why he passed on a sudden from hardness to liberality.", "그가 원한 것은 오로지 강한 의무로 나를 자신에게 묶어 두는 것이었지. 그래서 그가 갑작스레 냉정함에서 관대함으로 돌아선 거야."], ["And he may have tampered with the patient—he may have disobeyed my orders.", "그리고 그는 환자를 함부로 다루었을지도 몰라. 내 지시를 어겼을지도 모르지."], ["I fear he did.", "그랬을 거라는 생각이 들어."], ["But whether he did or not, the world believes that he somehow or other poisoned the man and that I winked at the crime, if I didn’t help in it.", "하지만 그가 그랬든 안 그랬든, 세상은 그가 어떻게든 그 사람을 독살했고 내가 범행을 거들지는 않았더라도 못 본 척 눈감아 주었다고 믿고 있어."], ["And yet—and yet he may not be guilty of the last offence; and it is just possible that the change towards me may have been a genuine relenting—the effect of second thoughts such as he alleged.", "그렇지만—그렇지만 그가 그 마지막 죄를 짓지 않았을 수도 있어. 그리고 나를 향한 그 태도의 변화가 진정한 마음의 누그러짐이었을 가능성도 충분히 있어—그가 주장했던 것 같은 재고의 결과였을 수도 있지."], ["What we call the ‘just possible’ is sometimes true and the thing we find it easier to believe is grossly false.", "우리가 '충분히 가능한 일'이라고 부르는 것이 때로는 진실이고, 우리가 믿기 더 쉽다고 여기는 것이 터무니없이 거짓일 때가 있어."], ["In his last dealings with this man Bulstrode may have kept his hands pure, in spite of my suspicion to the contrary.”", "이 사람과의 마지막 거래에서 불스트로드는, 그렇지 않으리라는 내 의심에도 불구하고, 손을 깨끗이 지켰을지도 몰라.\""]], [["There was a benumbing cruelty in his position.", "그의 처지에는 사람을 마비시키는 잔혹함이 있었다."], ["Even if he renounced every other consideration than that of justifying himself—if he met shrugs, cold glances, and avoidance as an accusation, and made a public statement of all the facts as he knew them, who would be convinced?", "설령 그가 자기 결백을 밝히는 것 말고 다른 모든 고려를 포기한다 하더라도—어깨를 으쓱하는 몸짓, 차가운 눈길, 그리고 회피를 하나의 고발로 받아들이고 자신이 아는 모든 사실을 공개적으로 진술한다 하더라도, 누가 납득하겠는가?"], ["It would be playing the part of a fool to offer his own testimony on behalf of himself, and say, “I did not take the money as a bribe.”", "자기 자신을 위하여 스스로의 증언을 내놓으며 \"나는 그 돈을 뇌물로 받지 않았다\"라고 말하는 것은 바보 짓을 하는 격일 것이었다."], ["The circumstances would always be stronger than his assertion.", "정황은 언제나 그의 주장보다 더 강력할 터였다."], ["And besides, to come forward and tell everything about himself must include declarations about Bulstrode which would darken the suspicions of others against him.", "게다가 나서서 자신에 관한 모든 것을 털어놓으려면 불스트로드에 관한 진술도 포함해야 했고, 그것은 그를 향한 다른 이들의 의심을 더욱 짙게 만들 것이었다."], ["He must tell that he had not known of Raffles’s existence when he first mentioned his pressing need of money to Bulstrode, and that he took the money innocently as a result of that communication, not knowing that a new motive for the loan might have arisen on his being called in to this man.", "그는 처음 불스트로드에게 절박한 돈의 필요를 언급했을 때 래플스라는 사람의 존재를 알지 못했으며, 그 이야기의 결과로 순진하게 그 돈을 받았을 뿐, 자신이 이 사람의 진료에 불려 가게 됨으로써 대출에 새로운 동기가 생겼을 수 있다는 것은 몰랐다고 말해야 했다."], ["And after all, the suspicion of Bulstrode’s motives might be unjust.", "그리고 결국 불스트로드의 동기에 대한 의심도 부당한 것일 수 있었다."]], [["But then came the question whether he should have acted in precisely the same way if he had not taken the money?", "하지만 그렇다면, 만약 그가 그 돈을 받지 않았더라면 정확히 똑같은 방식으로 행동했을 것인가 하는 물음이 떠올랐다."], ["Certainly, if Raffles had continued alive and susceptible of further treatment when he arrived, and he had then imagined any disobedience to his orders on the part of Bulstrode, he would have made a strict inquiry, and if his conjecture had been verified he would have thrown up the case, in spite of his recent heavy obligation.", "분명, 만약 그가 도착했을 때 래플스가 여전히 살아 있어 더 치료할 수 있는 상태였고, 그때 불스트로드 쪽에서 자기 지시에 무언가 불복종한 정황을 알아챘더라면, 그는 엄정한 조사를 했을 것이고, 만약 그 추측이 입증되었더라면 최근의 무거운 신세에도 불구하고 그 사건을 손에서 놓아 버렸을 것이다."], ["But if he had not received any money—if Bulstrode had never revoked his cold recommendation of bankruptcy—would he, Lydgate, have abstained from all inquiry even on finding the man dead?—would the shrinking from an insult to Bulstrode—would the dubiousness of all medical treatment and the argument that his own treatment would pass for the wrong with most members of his profession—have had just the same force or significance with him?", "그러나 만약 그가 아무 돈도 받지 않았더라면—만약 불스트로드가 파산을 권하던 냉정한 조언을 결코 철회하지 않았더라면—그, 리드게이트는 그 사람이 죽은 것을 발견하고서도 모든 조사를 삼갔을 것인가?—불스트로드를 모욕하는 것에 대한 움츠림이—모든 의학적 치료의 불확실성과, 자신의 치료법이 그 직종의 대다수 사람들에게 잘못된 것으로 통할 것이라는 논리가—그에게 똑같은 무게나 의미를 지녔을 것인가?"]], [["That was the uneasy corner of Lydgate’s consciousness while he was reviewing the facts and resisting all reproach.", "바로 그것이, 그가 사실들을 되짚으며 모든 자책을 물리치는 동안 리드게이트의 의식 속에 자리한 불안한 구석이었다."], ["If he had been independent, this matter of a patient’s treatment and the distinct rule that he must do or see done that which he believed best for the life committed to him, would have been the point on which he would have been the sturdiest.", "만약 그가 독립된 처지였더라면, 환자의 치료라는 이 문제와, 자신에게 맡겨진 생명을 위해 최선이라 믿는 바를 행하거나 행하게 해야 한다는 분명한 원칙이야말로 그가 가장 꿋꿋했을 지점이었으리라."], ["As it was, he had rested in the consideration that disobedience to his orders, however it might have arisen, could not be considered a crime, that in the dominant opinion obedience to his orders was just as likely to be fatal, and that the affair was simply one of etiquette.", "그러나 실상 그는, 자기 지시에 대한 불복종이 어떻게 일어났든 범죄로 간주될 수는 없다는 것, 지배적인 견해로는 자기 지시에 복종하는 것 역시 치명적이었을 가능성이 똑같이 있다는 것, 그리고 그 일이 단순히 예법의 문제일 뿐이라는 생각에 안주하고 있었다. 반면, 자유로웠던 시절에 그는 거듭거듭 병리학적 의심을 도덕적 의심으로 왜곡하는 것을 규탄하며 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["Whereas, again and again, in his time of freedom, he had denounced the perversion of pathological doubt into moral doubt and had said—“the purest experiment in treatment may still be conscientious: my business is to take care of life, and to do the best I can think of for it.", "\"가장 순수한 치료 실험도 여전히 양심적일 수 있다. 내 일은 생명을 돌보고 그것을 위해 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 최선을 다하는 것이다."], ["Science is properly more scrupulous than dogma.", "과학은 본래 교리보다 더 세심하다."], ["Dogma gives a charter to mistake, but the very breath of science is a contest with mistake, and must keep the conscience alive.”", "교리는 오류에 면허를 내주지만, 과학의 숨결 그 자체는 오류와의 다툼이며, 양심을 살아 있게 지켜야 한다.\""], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["the scientific conscience had got into the debasing company of money obligation and selfish respects.", "그 과학적 양심이 금전적 의무와 이기적인 체면이라는 타락한 무리 속으로 들어와 버린 것이다."]], [["“Is there a medical man of them all in Middlemarch who would question himself as I do?”", "\"미들마치의 그 모든 의료인 가운데 나처럼 스스로를 추궁할 사람이 하나라도 있을까?\""], ["said poor Lydgate, with a renewed outburst of rebellion against the oppression of his lot.", "가엾은 리드게이트는 자기 운명의 압제에 대한 반항심이 새로이 솟구치는 가운데 이렇게 말했다."], ["“And yet they will all feel warranted in making a wide space between me and them, as if I were a leper!", "\"그러면서도 그들은 모두, 내가 나병 환자라도 되는 양 나와 자기들 사이에 넓은 간격을 두는 것이 정당하다고 느낄 테지!"], ["My practice and my reputation are utterly damned—I can see that.", "내 진료와 내 평판은 완전히 끝장났어—나는 그걸 알 수 있어."], ["Even if I could be cleared by valid evidence, it would make little difference to the blessed world here.", "설령 내가 타당한 증거로 결백을 밝힐 수 있다 해도, 이곳의 그 복된 세상에는 별 차이가 없을 거야."], ["I have been set down as tainted and should be cheapened to them all the same.”", "나는 이미 오염된 자로 낙인찍혔으니, 어찌 됐든 그들 모두에게 값싼 존재로 떨어질 테지.\""]], [["Already there had been abundant signs which had hitherto puzzled him, that just when he had been paying off his debts and getting cheerfully on his feet, the townsmen were avoiding him or looking strangely at him, and in two instances it came to his knowledge that patients of his had called in another practitioner.", "그가 빚을 갚아 나가며 기운차게 자립해 가던 바로 그때, 읍 사람들이 그를 피하거나 이상한 눈으로 바라보았다는, 지금까지 그를 어리둥절하게 했던 징후들이 이미 숱하게 있었다. 그리고 두 번에 걸쳐 그의 환자들이 다른 의사를 불렀다는 사실을 그는 알게 되었다."], ["The reasons were too plain now.", "이제 그 이유는 너무도 명백했다."], ["The general black-balling had begun.", "모두가 그를 따돌리기 시작한 것이다."]], [["No wonder that in Lydgate’s energetic nature the sense of a hopeless misconstruction easily turned into a dogged resistance.", "리드게이트의 활기찬 천성에서, 가망 없는 오해를 받고 있다는 의식이 쉽사리 완강한 저항으로 바뀐 것도 무리는 아니었다."], ["The scowl which occasionally showed itself on his square brow was not a meaningless accident.", "그의 각진 이마에 이따금 떠오르는 찌푸린 표정은 의미 없는 우연이 아니었다."], ["Already when he was re-entering the town after that ride taken in the first hours of stinging pain, he was setting his mind on remaining in Middlemarch in spite of the worst that could be done against him.", "찌르는 듯한 고통의 첫 시간에 말을 달렸다가 읍으로 다시 들어설 때, 그는 이미 자신에게 가해질 수 있는 최악의 일에도 불구하고 미들마치에 남아 있기로 마음을 다잡고 있었다."], ["He would not retreat before calumny, as if he submitted to it.", "그는 중상모략에 굴복하는 것처럼 그 앞에서 물러서지 않을 것이었다."], ["He would face it to the utmost, and no act of his should show that he was afraid.", "그는 그것을 끝까지 마주할 것이며, 자신의 어떤 행동도 두려워한다는 것을 드러내지 않을 것이었다."], ["It belonged to the generosity as well as defiant force of his nature that he resolved not to shrink from showing to the full his sense of obligation to Bulstrode.", "불스트로드에 대한 신세를 남김없이 드러내겠다고 결심한 것은 그의 천성에 깃든 관대함뿐 아니라 도전적인 기개에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["It was true that the association with this man had been fatal to him—true that if he had had the thousand pounds still in his hands with all his debts unpaid he would have returned the money to Bulstrode, and taken beggary rather than the rescue which had been sullied with the suspicion of a bribe (for, remember, he was one of the proudest among the sons of men)—nevertheless, he would not turn away from this crushed fellow-mortal whose aid he had used, and make a pitiful effort to get acquittal for himself by howling against another.", "이 사람과의 관계가 그에게 치명적이었다는 것은 사실이었고—만약 그 천 파운드가 여전히 그의 손안에 있고 빚도 모두 갚지 못한 상태였더라면 그는 그 돈을 불스트로드에게 돌려주고, 뇌물의 의심으로 더럽혀진 구원을 받느니 차라리 거지 신세를 택했으리라는 것도 사실이었다(기억하라, 그는 인간의 아들들 가운데 가장 자존심 강한 축에 드는 사람이었다)—그럼에도 그는, 자신이 그 도움을 이용했던 이 짓밟힌 동료 인간에게서 등을 돌려, 다른 사람을 향해 울부짖음으로써 자신의 무죄를 얻어 내려는 가련한 짓을 하지는 않을 것이었다."], ["“I shall do as I think right, and explain to nobody.", "\"나는 옳다고 생각하는 대로 할 것이고, 누구에게도 해명하지 않겠어."], ["They will try to starve me out, but—”", "그들은 나를 굶겨 내쫓으려 하겠지만—\""], ["he was going on with an obstinate resolve, but he was getting near home, and the thought of Rosamond urged itself again into that chief place from which it had been thrust by the agonized struggles of wounded honor and pride.", "그는 완고한 결의로 생각을 이어 가고 있었으나, 어느새 집 가까이에 이르렀고, 로저먼드에 대한 생각이, 상처 입은 명예와 자존심의 고뇌에 찬 투쟁에 밀려나 있던 그 으뜸 자리로 다시금 비집고 들어왔다."]], [["How would Rosamond take it all?", "로저먼드는 이 모든 것을 어떻게 받아들일까?"], ["Here was another weight of chain to drag, and poor Lydgate was in a bad mood for bearing her dumb mastery.", "여기 끌고 가야 할 또 하나의 사슬의 무게가 있었으니, 가엾은 리드게이트는 그녀의 말 없는 지배를 견디기에 좋지 못한 기분이었다."], ["He had no impulse to tell her the trouble which must soon be common to them both.", "그는 머지않아 두 사람 모두의 것이 될 수밖에 없는 그 곤경을 그녀에게 말해 주고 싶은 충동이 일지 않았다."], ["He preferred waiting for the incidental disclosure which events must soon bring about.", "그는 사태가 머지않아 불러올 우연한 폭로를 기다리는 편을 택했다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXIV.", "# CHAPTER LXXIV."]], [["“Mercifully grant that we may grow aged together.”", "크나큰 자비로 우리가 함께 늙어 갈 수 있도록 허락하소서."], ["—BOOK OF TOBIT: Marriage Prayer.", "— 토비트서: 결혼 기도"]], [["In Middlemarch a wife could not long remain ignorant that the town held a bad opinion of her husband.", "미들마치에서 아내가 자기 남편에 대한 마을의 평판이 나쁘다는 사실을 오래 모르고 지낼 수는 없었다."], ["No feminine intimate might carry her friendship so far as to make a plain statement to the wife of the unpleasant fact known or believed about her husband; but when a woman with her thoughts much at leisure got them suddenly employed on something grievously disadvantageous to her neighbors, various moral impulses were called into play which tended to stimulate utterance.", "어떤 여자 친구도 우정을 너무 멀리 밀고 나가, 남편에 관해 알려졌거나 그렇게 믿어지는 불쾌한 사실을 아내에게 대놓고 말해 주지는 않았다. 하지만 평소 생각할 거리가 한가하던 여자가 이웃에게 몹시 불리한 일에 갑자기 생각을 골몰하게 되면, 발설을 부추기는 여러 가지 도덕적 충동이 작동하기 마련이었다."], ["Candor was one.", "그중 하나가 솔직함이었다."], ["To be candid, in Middlemarch phraseology, meant, to use an early opportunity of letting your friends know that you did not take a cheerful view of their capacity, their conduct, or their position; and a robust candor never waited to be asked for its opinion.", "미들마치 어법으로 솔직하다는 것은, 당신의 친구들에게 그들의 능력이나 처신이나 처지를 그다지 밝게 보지 않는다는 것을 일찌감치 기회 닿는 대로 알려 준다는 뜻이었다. 그리고 건강하고 강건한 솔직함은 의견을 청해 올 때까지 기다리는 법이 없었다."], ["Then, again, there was the love of truth—a wide phrase, but meaning in this relation, a lively objection to seeing a wife look happier than her husband’s character warranted, or manifest too much satisfaction in her lot—the poor thing should have some hint given her that if she knew the truth she would have less complacency in her bonnet, and in light dishes for a supper-party.", "그다음으로는 진실에 대한 사랑이 있었다. 이것은 폭넓은 표현이지만 이런 관계에서는, 한 아내가 자기 남편의 인품이 허락하는 것보다 더 행복해 보이거나 제 처지에 너무 흡족해하는 모습을 보면 활발하게 반발한다는 뜻이었다."], ["Stronger than all, there was the regard for a friend’s moral improvement, sometimes called her soul, which was likely to be benefited by remarks tending to gloom, uttered with the accompaniment of pensive staring at the furniture and a manner implying that the speaker would not tell what was on her mind, from regard to the feelings of her hearer.", "그 가엾은 여자는, 진실을 안다면 자기 보닛이나 야식 모임용 가벼운 요리들에 그토록 만족하지 못하리라는 것을 어느 정도 귀띔받아야 한다는 것이다. 무엇보다도 강력한 것은 친구의 도덕적 향상에 대한, 때로는 그녀의 영혼이라고도 불리는 것에 대한 관심이었다."], ["On the whole, one might say that an ardent charity was at work setting the virtuous mind to make a neighbor unhappy for her good.", "그것은 가구를 수심에 잠겨 바라보며, 듣는 이의 감정을 배려해서 자기 속에 있는 말을 차마 다 하지 못한다는 듯한 태도로 우울함을 자아내는 발언을 던질 때 향상될 법한 것이었다. 대체로 보아, 열렬한 자비심이 작동하여 덕망 있는 마음으로 하여금 이웃을 그녀 자신을 위한답시고 불행하게 만들도록 부추긴다고 말할 수 있을 터였다."]], [["There were hardly any wives in Middlemarch whose matrimonial misfortunes would in different ways be likely to call forth more of this moral activity than Rosamond and her aunt Bulstrode.", "미들마치에서 이런 도덕적 활동을 여러 방식으로 가장 많이 불러일으킬 만한 결혼 생활의 불운을 지닌 아내는, 로저먼드와 그녀의 숙모 벌스트로드 부인을 빼면 거의 없었다."], ["Mrs. Bulstrode was not an object of dislike, and had never consciously injured any human being.", "벌스트로드 부인은 미움의 대상이 아니었고, 어떤 인간도 의식적으로 해친 적이 없었다."], ["Men had always thought her a handsome comfortable woman, and had reckoned it among the signs of Bulstrode’s hypocrisy that he had chosen a red-blooded Vincy, instead of a ghastly and melancholy person suited to his low esteem for earthly pleasure.", "남자들은 늘 그녀를 잘생기고 편안한 여인이라 여겼고, 벌스트로드가 지상의 쾌락을 낮춰 보는 그의 태도에 어울릴 만한 으스스하고 우울한 사람 대신 혈색 좋은 빈시 집안 여자를 택했다는 것을 그의 위선의 징표 가운데 하나로 꼽았다."], ["When the scandal about her husband was disclosed they remarked of her—“Ah, poor woman!", "그녀의 남편에 관한 추문이 폭로되자 사람들은 그녀를 두고 말했다."], ["She’s as honest as the day—she never suspected anything wrong in him, you may depend on it.”", "\"아, 가엾은 여자! 더없이 정직한 사람이지."], ["Women, who were intimate with her, talked together much of “poor Harriet,”", "남편에게 뭔가 잘못이 있으리라고는 의심도 못 했을 거요. 틀림없어.\""], ["imagined what her feelings must be when she came to know everything, and conjectured how much she had already come to know.", "그녀와 친한 여자들은 \"가엾은 해리엇\"에 관해 많은 이야기를 나누며, 그녀가 모든 것을 알게 되었을 때 어떤 심정일지 상상했고, 이미 얼마나 알게 되었을지 추측했다."], ["There was no spiteful disposition towards her; rather, there was a busy benevolence anxious to ascertain what it would be well for her to feel and do under the circumstances, which of course kept the imagination occupied with her character and history from the times when she was Harriet Vincy till now.", "그녀에 대한 악의는 전혀 없었다. 오히려 이런 상황에서 그녀가 무엇을 느끼고 무엇을 하는 것이 좋을지 알아내려고 안달하는 분주한 선의가 있었으니, 이는 물론 그녀가 해리엇 빈시였던 시절부터 지금까지의 성품과 내력에 상상력을 골몰하게 만들었다."], ["With the review of Mrs. Bulstrode and her position it was inevitable to associate Rosamond, whose prospects were under the same blight with her aunt’s.", "벌스트로드 부인과 그녀의 처지를 되짚다 보면, 숙모와 똑같은 먹구름 아래 놓인 앞날을 지닌 로저먼드를 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Rosamond was more severely criticised and less pitied, though she too, as one of the good old Vincy family who had always been known in Middlemarch, was regarded as a victim to marriage with an interloper.", "로저먼드는 더 호되게 비판받고 덜 동정받았으나, 그녀 역시 미들마치에서 늘 알려져 온 유서 깊은 빈시 집안의 일원으로서 외부에서 끼어든 사내와의 결혼에 희생된 사람으로 여겨졌다."], ["The Vincys had their weaknesses, but then they lay on the surface: there was never anything bad to be “found out”", "빈시 집안 사람들에게도 약점은 있었지만, 그 약점은 표면에 드러나 있었다. 그들에 관해서는 \"들춰낼\""], ["concerning them. Mrs.", "만한 나쁜 일이 결코 없었다."], ["Bulstrode was vindicated from any resemblance to her husband.", "벌스트로드 부인은 자기 남편과 닮은 점이 전혀 없다는 변호를 받았다."], ["Harriet’s faults were her own.", "해리엇의 결점은 그녀 자신의 것이었다."]], [["“She has always been showy,”", "\"그 사람은 늘 겉치레가 심했어요.\""], ["said Mrs.", "핵벗 부인이 작은 모임을 위해 차를 준비하며 말했다."], ["Hackbutt, making tea for a small party, “though she has got into the way of putting her religion forward, to conform to her husband; she has tried to hold her head up above Middlemarch by making it known that she invites clergymen and heaven-knows-who from Riverston and those places.”", "\"물론 남편에게 맞추느라 신앙을 앞세우는 버릇이 들기는 했지만요. 리버스턴이며 그런 곳에서 성직자들이며 누군지도 모를 사람들을 초대한다는 걸 알려서 미들마치 위로 고개를 쳐들려고 했지요.\""]], [["“We can hardly blame her for that,” said Mrs.", "\"그 일로 그분을 탓하기는 어려워요.\""], ["Sprague; “because few of the best people in the town cared to associate with Bulstrode, and she must have somebody to sit down at her table.”", "스프레이그 부인이 말했다. \"이 마을의 점잖은 사람들 중에 벌스트로드와 어울리려는 이가 거의 없었으니, 자기 식탁에 앉힐 누군가는 있어야 했을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Mr. Thesiger has always countenanced him,”", "\"세시저 목사님은 늘 그를 두둔하셨어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hackbutt.", "핵벗 부인이 말했다."], ["“I think he must be sorry now.”", "\"지금쯤은 후회하고 계실 것 같네요.\""]], [["“But he was never fond of him in his heart—that every one knows,”", "\"하지만 그 양반은 속으로는 그를 좋아한 적이 없어요."], ["said Mrs. Tom Toller.", "다들 아는 사실이지요.\""], ["“Mr. Thesiger never goes into extremes.", "톰 톨러 부인이 말했다."], ["He keeps to the truth in what is evangelical.", "\"세시저 목사님은 결코 극단으로 치우치지 않으시거든요."], ["It is only clergymen like Mr.", "복음주의에 관한 한 진실을 지키는 분이에요."], ["Tyke, who want to use Dissenting hymn-books and that low kind of religion, who ever found Bulstrode to their taste.”", "비국교도 찬송가집이며 그런 저급한 신앙을 쓰고 싶어 하는 타이크 목사님 같은 성직자들이나 벌스트로드를 취향에 맞다고 여겼지요.\""]], [["“I understand, Mr. Tyke is in great distress about him,”", "\"제가 듣기로 타이크 목사님은 그 사람 일로 크게 상심하셨다더군요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hackbutt.", "핵벗 부인이 말했다."], ["“And well he may be: they say the Bulstrodes have half kept the Tyke family.”", "\"그럴 만도 하지요. 사람들 말로는 벌스트로드네가 타이크 집안을 절반은 먹여 살렸다니까요.\""]], [["“And of course it is a discredit to his doctrines,” said Mrs.", "\"그러니 당연히 그분 교리에도 먹칠이 되는 셈이지요.\""], ["Sprague, who was elderly, and old-fashioned in her opinions.", "나이가 지긋하고 견해가 구식인 스프레이그 부인이 말했다."]], [["“People will not make a boast of being methodistical in Middlemarch for a good while to come.”", "\"앞으로 한동안 미들마치에서 자기가 감리교 신자라고 자랑하는 사람은 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“I think we must not set down people’s bad actions to their religion,”", "\"사람들의 나쁜 행실을 그들의 신앙 탓으로 돌려서는 안 된다고 봐요.\""], ["said falcon-faced Mrs. Plymdale, who had been listening hitherto.", "그때까지 잠자코 듣고 있던 매 같은 얼굴의 플림데일 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, my dear, we are forgetting,”", "\"아, 이런, 우리가 깜빡했네요.\""], ["said Mrs. Sprague.", "스프레이그 부인이 말했다."], ["“We ought not to be talking of this before you.”", "\"댁 앞에서 이런 이야기를 하면 안 되는 건데.\""]], [["“I am sure I have no reason to be partial,” said Mrs.", "\"제가 편을 들 이유는 전혀 없답니다.\""], ["Plymdale, coloring. “It’s true Mr.", "플림데일 부인이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Plymdale has always been on good terms with Mr. Bulstrode, and Harriet Vincy was my friend long before she married him.", "\"물론 우리 플림데일 씨가 벌스트로드 씨와 늘 사이가 좋았던 건 사실이고, 해리엇 빈시는 그 사람과 결혼하기 훨씬 전부터 제 친구였지요."], ["But I have always kept my own opinions and told her where she was wrong, poor thing.", "하지만 저는 늘 제 의견을 지켜 왔고, 그 가엾은 사람에게 어디가 잘못됐는지 말해 주곤 했어요."], ["Still, in point of religion, I must say, Mr. Bulstrode might have done what he has, and worse, and yet have been a man of no religion.", "그래도 신앙이라는 점에서 말하자면, 벌스트로드 씨가 한 짓을, 아니 그보다 더한 짓을 했다 해도 신앙이라곤 전혀 없는 사람일 수도 있는 거예요."], ["I don’t say that there has not been a little too much of that—I like moderation myself.", "그게 좀 지나쳤다는 것을 부정하는 건 아니에요. 저야 절제를 좋아하니까요."], ["But truth is truth.", "하지만 진실은 진실이지요."], ["The men tried at the assizes are not all over-religious, I suppose.”", "순회 재판에서 재판받는 사람들이 다들 지나치게 독실하지는 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Well,” said Mrs.", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["Hackbutt, wheeling adroitly, “all I can say is, that I think she ought to separate from him.”", "핵벗 부인이 능란하게 화제를 돌리며 말했다. \"제가 말할 수 있는 건, 그 사람이 남편과 헤어져야 한다고 생각한다는 거예요.\""]], [["“I can’t say that,”", "\"그건 모르겠네요.\""], ["said Mrs. Sprague.", "스프레이그 부인이 말했다."], ["“She took him for better or worse, you know.”", "\"아시다시피 좋든 나쁘든 함께하겠다고 그를 받아들였잖아요.\""]], [["“But ‘worse’ can never mean finding out that your husband is fit for Newgate,”", "\"하지만 '나쁘든'이라는 게 남편이 뉴게이트 감옥에나 어울리는 사람임을 알게 되는 걸 뜻할 수는 없지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hackbutt.", "핵벗 부인이 말했다."], ["“Fancy living with such a man!", "\"그런 남자와 함께 산다고 생각해 봐요!"], ["I should expect to be poisoned.”", "저라면 독살이라도 당할까 봐 겁날 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, I think myself it is an encouragement to crime if such men are to be taken care of and waited on by good wives,”", "* 뉴게이트: 런던의 악명 높은 감옥으로, 중범죄자가 수감되던 곳. \"네, 저도 그런 남자들이 착한 아내들의 보살핌과 시중을 받게 된다면 그게 범죄를 부추기는 일이라고 생각해요.\""], ["said Mrs. Tom Toller.", "톰 톨러 부인이 말했다."]], [["“And a good wife poor Harriet has been,”", "\"그리고 가엾은 해리엇은 정말 좋은 아내였어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Plymdale.", "플림데일 부인이 말했다."], ["“She thinks her husband the first of men.", "\"그 사람은 자기 남편을 세상 으뜸가는 남자로 여기지요."], ["It’s true he has never denied her anything.”", "하기야 그가 그 사람에게 뭐든 안 된다고 한 적이 없긴 해요.\""]], [["“Well, we shall see what she will do,”", "\"음, 그 사람이 어떻게 하는지 두고 봐야겠지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hackbutt.", "핵벗 부인이 말했다."], ["“I suppose she knows nothing yet, poor creature.", "\"아직 아무것도 모르나 봐요, 가엾은 것."], ["I do hope and trust I shall not see her, for I should be frightened to death lest I should say anything about her husband.", "정말이지 그 사람을 만나지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 행여 남편 이야기를 입에 올릴까 봐 겁이 나서 죽을 지경일 테니까요."], ["Do you think any hint has reached her?”", "어떤 낌새라도 그 사람 귀에 들어갔을까요?\""]], [["“I should hardly think so,”", "\"그럴 것 같진 않아요.\""], ["said Mrs. Tom Toller.", "톰 톨러 부인이 말했다."], ["“We hear that he is ill, and has never stirred out of the house since the meeting on Thursday; but she was with her girls at church yesterday, and they had new Tuscan bonnets.", "\"그 사람 남편이 아프고 목요일 모임 이후로 집 밖에 한 발짝도 안 나왔다고 들었어요. 하지만 그 사람은 어제 딸들과 함께 교회에 왔고, 다들 새 투스카니 보닛을 쓰고 있더군요."], ["Her own had a feather in it.", "자기 보닛에는 깃털까지 달려 있었고요."], ["I have never seen that her religion made any difference in her dress.”", "그 사람 신앙이 옷차림에 무슨 변화를 가져온 건 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“She wears very neat patterns always,”", "\"그 사람은 늘 아주 단정한 무늬를 입는걸요.\""], ["said Mrs. Plymdale, a little stung.", "플림데일 부인이 살짝 발끈하여 말했다."], ["“And that feather I know she got dyed a pale lavender on purpose to be consistent.", "\"그리고 그 깃털은 일부러 연한 라벤더색으로 염색한 거예요. 신앙에 어울리게 하려고요."], ["I must say it of Harriet that she wishes to do right.”", "해리엇에 대해 이 말은 해 두어야겠어요. 그 사람은 바르게 살고 싶어 한다고요.\""]], [["“As to her knowing what has happened, it can’t be kept from her long,”", "\"그 사람이 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알게 되는 거야, 오래 숨길 수는 없겠지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hackbutt.", "핵벗 부인이 말했다."], ["“The Vincys know, for Mr.", "\"빈시 집안은 알고 있어요."], ["Vincy was at the meeting.", "빈시 씨가 그 모임에 있었으니까요."], ["It will be a great blow to him.", "그분에게 큰 타격일 거예요."], ["There is his daughter as well as his sister.”", "누이뿐 아니라 딸까지 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, indeed,” said Mrs.", "\"네, 정말 그래요.\""], ["Sprague. “Nobody supposes that Mr.", "스프레이그 부인이 말했다."], ["Lydgate can go on holding up his head in Middlemarch, things look so black about the thousand pounds he took just at that man’s death.", "\"리드게이트 씨가 미들마치에서 계속 고개를 들고 다닐 수 있으리라고 생각하는 사람은 아무도 없어요. 그 사람이 죽은 바로 그 무렵에 천 파운드를 받았다니 모든 게 너무 수상쩍거든요."], ["It really makes one shudder.”", "정말이지 소름이 끼친다니까요.\""]], [["“Pride must have a fall,”", "\"교만한 자는 넘어지기 마련이지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hackbutt.", "핵벗 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I am not so sorry for Rosamond Vincy that was as I am for her aunt,”", "\"저는 한때 로저먼드 빈시였던 그 사람보다 그 숙모가 더 안됐어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Plymdale.", "플림데일 부인이 말했다."], ["“She needed a lesson.”", "\"그 애는 따끔한 교훈이 필요했어요.\""]], [["“I suppose the Bulstrodes will go and live abroad somewhere,”", "\"벌스트로드네는 어디 외국에 가서 살게 될 거예요.\""], ["said Mrs. Sprague.", "스프레이그 부인이 말했다."], ["“That is what is generally done when there is anything disgraceful in a family.”", "\"한 집안에 망신스러운 일이 생기면 보통 그렇게들 하니까요.\""]], [["“And a most deadly blow it will be to Harriet,”", "\"그리고 그건 해리엇에게 더없이 치명적인 타격이 될 거예요.\""], ["said Mrs. Plymdale.", "플림데일 부인이 말했다."], ["“If ever a woman was crushed, she will be.", "\"한 여자가 짓밟히는 일이 있다면, 그 사람이 그럴 거예요."], ["I pity her from my heart.", "진심으로 가엾게 생각해요."], ["And with all her faults, few women are better.", "온갖 결점에도 불구하고 그 사람보다 나은 여자는 드물어요."], ["From a girl she had the neatest ways, and was always good-hearted, and as open as the day.", "처녀 시절부터 더없이 깔끔했고, 늘 마음씨가 곱고 대낮처럼 환히 트인 사람이었지요."], ["You might look into her drawers when you would—always the same.", "그 사람 서랍은 언제 열어 봐도 한결같았어요."], ["And so she has brought up Kate and Ellen.", "그렇게 케이트와 엘런도 길러 냈고요."], ["You may think how hard it will be for her to go among foreigners.”", "그러니 외국인들 틈에서 살아가는 게 그 사람에게 얼마나 힘들지 짐작이 가시겠지요.\""]], [["“The doctor says that is what he should recommend the Lydgates to do,”", "\"의사 말로는 리드게이트네에게도 그렇게 하라고 권하겠다더군요.\""], ["said Mrs. Sprague.", "스프레이그 부인이 말했다."], ["“He says Lydgate ought to have kept among the French.”", "\"리드게이트는 프랑스 사람들 틈에 있었어야 했다나요.\""]], [["“That would suit her well enough, I dare say,” said Mrs.", "\"그거야 그 사람한테는 충분히 어울릴 거예요.\""], ["Plymdale; “there is that kind of lightness about her.", "플림데일 부인이 말했다. \"그 사람한테는 그런 경박한 구석이 있으니까요."], ["But she got that from her mother; she never got it from her aunt Bulstrode, who always gave her good advice, and to my knowledge would rather have had her marry elsewhere.”", "하지만 그건 친정어머니한테서 물려받은 거예요. 늘 좋은 충고를 해 주었고 내가 알기로는 차라리 다른 데로 시집보내고 싶어 했던 벌스트로드 숙모한테서 물려받은 게 아니라요.\""]], [["Mrs. Plymdale was in a situation which caused her some complication of feeling.", "플림데일 부인은 다소 복잡한 심경을 일으키는 처지에 놓여 있었다."], ["There had been not only her intimacy with Mrs. Bulstrode, but also a profitable business relation of the great Plymdale dyeing house with Mr.", "벌스트로드 부인과의 친분뿐 아니라, 명망 높은 플림데일 염색 회사가 벌스트로드 씨와 맺어 온 짭짤한 사업 관계도 있었던 것이다."], ["Bulstrode, which on the one hand would have inclined her to desire that the mildest view of his character should be the true one, but on the other, made her the more afraid of seeming to palliate his culpability.", "이 때문에 한편으로는 그의 인품에 대한 가장 너그러운 해석이 진실이기를 바라는 마음으로 기울었지만, 다른 한편으로는 그의 죄과를 두둔하는 것처럼 보일까 봐 더욱 두려웠다."], ["Again, the late alliance of her family with the Tollers had brought her in connection with the best circle, which gratified her in every direction except in the inclination to those serious views which she believed to be the best in another sense.", "또한 근래에 그녀 집안이 톨러 가문과 맺은 인척 관계 덕분에 최상류 계층과 인연이 닿게 되었으니, 이는 또 다른 의미에서 최선이라고 그녀가 믿는 그 진지한 견해들로 기우는 성향만 빼고는 모든 방면에서 그녀를 흡족하게 했다."], ["The sharp little woman’s conscience was somewhat troubled in the adjustment of these opposing “bests,”", "이 영악하고 자그마한 여인의 양심은, 서로 어긋나는 이 \"최선\"들을 조정하면서, 또한 근래의 사건들이 일으킨 자신의 슬픔과 만족을 조정하면서 다소 괴로웠다."], ["and of her griefs and satisfactions under late events, which were likely to humble those who needed humbling, but also to fall heavily on her old friend whose faults she would have preferred seeing on a background of prosperity.", "그 사건들은 응당 겸허해져야 할 이들을 겸허하게 만들 법했으나, 차라리 번영을 배경으로 그 결점을 보고 싶었던 그녀의 옛 친구에게도 무겁게 떨어질 것이었다."]], [["Poor Mrs. Bulstrode, meanwhile, had been no further shaken by the oncoming tread of calamity than in the busier stirring of that secret uneasiness which had always been present in her since the last visit of Raffles to The Shrubs.", "한편, 가엾은 벌스트로드 부인은 다가오는 재앙의 발소리에도, 래플스가 슈러브스에 마지막으로 다녀간 이후로 늘 그녀 안에 자리하고 있던 그 은밀한 불안이 한층 분주하게 술렁이는 것 말고는 별다른 동요를 겪지 않았다."], ["That the hateful man had come ill to Stone Court, and that her husband had chosen to remain there and watch over him, she allowed to be explained by the fact that Raffles had been employed and aided in earlier-days, and that this made a tie of benevolence towards him in his degraded helplessness; and she had been since then innocently cheered by her husband’s more hopeful speech about his own health and ability to continue his attention to business.", "그 가증스러운 사내가 병들어 스톤 코트에 왔고 남편이 그곳에 머무르며 그를 돌보기로 한 일은, 래플스가 예전에 일자리를 얻고 도움을 받았던 사람이라 그가 영락하여 무력해진 상황에서 그에 대한 선의의 의리가 생긴 것이라고 설명되는 것을 그녀는 받아들였다. 그리고 그 이후로 남편이 자신의 건강과 계속 사업에 전념할 수 있는 능력에 관해 더 희망찬 말을 하는 것에 그녀는 천진하게 마음이 놓였다."], ["The calm was disturbed when Lydgate had brought him home ill from the meeting, and in spite of comforting assurances during the next few days, she cried in private from the conviction that her husband was not suffering from bodily illness merely, but from something that afflicted his mind.", "그 평온은 리드게이트가 모임에서 병든 남편을 집으로 데려왔을 때 흔들렸고, 이후 며칠간 위로가 되는 다짐들이 이어졌음에도, 그녀는 남편이 단지 육체의 병만이 아니라 마음을 괴롭히는 무언가로 고통받고 있다는 확신에 남몰래 울었다."], ["He would not allow her to read to him, and scarcely to sit with him, alleging nervous susceptibility to sounds and movements; yet she suspected that in shutting himself up in his private room he wanted to be busy with his papers.", "그는 소리와 움직임에 신경이 예민해졌다는 핑계로, 그녀가 책을 읽어 주는 것은 물론 곁에 앉아 있는 것조차 허락하지 않았다. 하지만 그녀는 그가 자기 방에 틀어박혀 서류를 들여다보며 무언가 분주히 하고 싶어 한다고 의심했다."], ["Something, she felt sure, had happened.", "무슨 일이 일어난 게 틀림없다고 그녀는 확신했다."], ["Perhaps it was some great loss of money; and she was kept in the dark.", "어쩌면 큰 금전적 손실일지도 몰랐다. 그런데 그녀만 어둠 속에 갇혀 있었다."], ["Not daring to question her husband, she said to Lydgate, on the fifth day after the meeting, when she had not left home except to go to church—", "감히 남편에게 물어보지 못한 채, 모임이 있은 지 닷새째 되던 날, 교회에 가는 것 말고는 집을 나선 적이 없던 그녀가 리드게이트에게 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Lydgate, pray be open with me: I like to know the truth.", "\"리드게이트 씨, 부디 제게 솔직히 말씀해 주세요."], ["Has anything happened to Mr.", "저는 진실을 알고 싶어요."], ["Bulstrode?”", "벌스트로드 씨에게 무슨 일이 생긴 건가요?\""]], [["“Some little nervous shock,”", "\"가벼운 신경성 충격입니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, evasively.", "리드게이트가 얼버무리며 말했다."], ["He felt that it was not for him to make the painful revelation.", "그 괴로운 사실을 밝히는 것이 자기 몫은 아니라고 그는 느꼈다."]], [["“But what brought it on?” said Mrs.", "\"그런데 무엇 때문에 그렇게 된 거지요?\""], ["Bulstrode, looking directly at him with her large dark eyes.", "벌스트로드 부인이 크고 검은 눈으로 그를 똑바로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“There is often something poisonous in the air of public rooms,” said Lydgate.", "\"공공장소의 공기에는 흔히 해로운 무언가가 있습니다.\""], ["“Strong men can stand it, but it tells on people in proportion to the delicacy of their systems.", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"건강한 사람은 견디지만, 신체가 섬세한 사람일수록 그 영향을 받지요."], ["It is often impossible to account for the precise moment of an attack—or rather, to say why the strength gives way at a particular moment.”", "발작이 일어나는 정확한 순간을 설명하기란 흔히 불가능합니다. 아니, 어째서 하필 그 순간에 기력이 무너지는지를 말하기란 말입니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Bulstrode was not satisfied with this answer.", "벌스트로드 부인은 이 대답에 만족하지 못했다."], ["There remained in her the belief that some calamity had befallen her husband, of which she was to be kept in ignorance; and it was in her nature strongly to object to such concealment.", "그녀에게는 남편에게 어떤 재앙이 닥쳤고 자기만 그 사실을 모르도록 가려져 있다는 믿음이 남아 있었다. 그리고 그런 은폐를 강하게 거부하는 것이 그녀의 천성이었다."], ["She begged leave for her daughters to sit with their father, and drove into the town to pay some visits, conjecturing that if anything were known to have gone wrong in Mr.", "그녀는 딸들에게 아버지 곁에 앉아 있으라고 청하고는, 몇 군데 방문을 하러 마차를 타고 마을로 나갔다."], ["Bulstrode’s affairs, she should see or hear some sign of it.", "벌스트로드 씨의 일에 무언가 잘못된 것이 알려져 있다면, 그 어떤 징후라도 보거나 듣게 되리라 짐작하면서였다."]], [["She called on Mrs. Thesiger, who was not at home, and then drove to Mrs.", "그녀는 세시저 부인을 찾아갔으나 부인은 집에 없었고, 그래서 교회 묘지 건너편에 있는 핵벗 부인의 집으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["Hackbutt’s on the other side of the churchyard. Mrs.", "핵벗 부인은 위층 창문에서 그녀가 오는 것을 보았고, 행여 벌스트로드 부인과 마주칠까 봐 두려워하던 예전의 마음을 떠올리며 일관성을 지키자면 집에 없다고 전갈을 보내야 마땅하다고 느꼈다."], ["Hackbutt saw her coming from an up-stairs window, and remembering her former alarm lest she should meet Mrs. Bulstrode, felt almost bound in consistency to send word that she was not at home; but against that, there was a sudden strong desire within her for the excitement of an interview in which she was quite determined not to make the slightest allusion to what was in her mind.", "하지만 그에 맞서, 자기 속에 있는 것은 추호도 내비치지 않겠다고 단단히 결심한 채로 면담의 흥분을 맛보고 싶은 강한 욕망이 불현듯 솟구쳤다."]], [["Hence Mrs. Bulstrode was shown into the drawing-room, and Mrs.", "그리하여 벌스트로드 부인은 응접실로 안내되었고, 핵벗 부인은 평소보다 더 입술을 꼭 다물고 두 손을 비비며 그녀에게 다가갔다."], ["Hackbutt went to her, with more tightness of lip and rubbing of her hands than was usually observable in her, these being precautions adopted against freedom of speech.", "이는 말이 헤퍼지는 것을 막으려고 취한 조심성이었다."], ["She was resolved not to ask how Mr. Bulstrode was.", "그녀는 벌스트로드 씨가 어떠냐고 묻지 않기로 단단히 마음먹었다."]], [["“I have not been anywhere except to church for nearly a week,”", "\"거의 일주일 동안 교회 말고는 어디에도 가지 못했어요.\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, after a few introductory remarks.", "몇 마디 인사를 나눈 뒤 벌스트로드 부인이 말했다."], ["“But Mr. Bulstrode was taken so ill at the meeting on Thursday that I have not liked to leave the house.”", "\"하지만 벌스트로드 씨가 목요일 모임에서 너무 심하게 앓아눕는 바람에 집을 비우고 싶지 않았거든요.\""]], [["Mrs. Hackbutt rubbed the back of one hand with the palm of the other held against her chest, and let her eyes ramble over the pattern on the rug.", "핵벗 부인은 가슴에 댄 한 손의 손등을 다른 손바닥으로 문지르며, 깔개의 무늬 위로 눈길을 헤매게 두었다."]], [["“Was Mr. Hackbutt at the meeting?”", "\"핵벗 씨도 그 모임에 계셨나요?\""], ["persevered Mrs. Bulstrode.", "벌스트로드 부인이 끈질기게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, he was,” said Mrs.", "\"네, 계셨어요.\""], ["Hackbutt, with the same attitude.", "핵벗 부인이 똑같은 자세로 말했다."], ["“The land is to be bought by subscription, I believe.”", "\"그 땅은 기부금으로 사들이게 될 거라더군요.\""]], [["“Let us hope that there will be no more cases of cholera to be buried in it,”", "\"거기 콜레라로 죽은 사람들을 더는 묻을 일이 없기를 바라야지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode.", "벌스트로드 부인이 말했다."], ["“It is an awful visitation.", "\"무서운 재앙이에요."], ["But I always think Middlemarch a very healthy spot.", "하지만 저는 늘 미들마치가 아주 건강한 곳이라고 생각해요."], ["I suppose it is being used to it from a child; but I never saw the town I should like to live at better, and especially our end.”", "어릴 적부터 익숙해서 그렇겠지만, 여기보다 더 살고 싶은 마을은 본 적이 없어요. 특히 우리 동네 쪽은요.\""]], [["“I am sure I should be glad that you always should live at Middlemarch, Mrs. Bulstrode,”", "\"벌스트로드 부인, 부인이 늘 미들마치에 사신다면 저야 정말 기쁘지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Hackbutt, with a slight sigh.", "핵벗 부인이 가벼운 한숨과 함께 말했다."], ["“Still, we must learn to resign ourselves, wherever our lot may be cast.", "\"그래도 우리의 운명이 어디로 던져지든, 우리는 체념하는 법을 배워야 하지요."], ["Though I am sure there will always be people in this town who will wish you well.”", "물론 이 마을에는 늘 부인이 잘되기를 바라는 사람들이 있을 테지만요.\""]], [["Mrs. Hackbutt longed to say, “if you take my advice you will part from your husband,”", "핵벗 부인은 \"제 충고를 받아들이신다면 남편과 헤어지셔야 해요\"라고 말하고 싶어 견딜 수 없었지만, 이 가엾은 여인이 제 머리 위로 터지려는 천둥에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 것이 분명해 보였다."], ["but it seemed clear to her that the poor woman knew nothing of the thunder ready to bolt on her head, and she herself could do no more than prepare her a little. Mrs.", "그리고 그녀 자신이 할 수 있는 일이라고는 마음의 준비를 조금 시켜 주는 것뿐이었다."], ["Bulstrode felt suddenly rather chill and trembling: there was evidently something unusual behind this speech of Mrs.", "벌스트로드 부인은 갑자기 다소 오싹하고 몸이 떨렸다. 핵벗 부인의 이 말 뒤에는 분명 무언가 심상치 않은 것이 있었다."], ["Hackbutt’s; but though she had set out with the desire to be fully informed, she found herself unable now to pursue her brave purpose, and turning the conversation by an inquiry about the young Hackbutts, she soon took her leave saying that she was going to see Mrs. Plymdale.", "하지만 충분히 알아내겠다는 마음으로 나섰음에도, 이제 그녀는 그 용감한 의도를 밀고 나갈 수 없음을 깨달았다."], ["On her way thither she tried to imagine that there might have been some unusually warm sparring at the meeting between Mr.", "그래서 핵벗네 아이들의 안부를 물어 화제를 돌리고는, 플림데일 부인을 만나러 가는 길이라고 말하며 곧 자리를 떴다."], ["Bulstrode and some of his frequent opponents—perhaps Mr. Hackbutt might have been one of them.", "그곳으로 가는 길에 그녀는 모임에서 벌스트로드 씨와 그의 잦은 적수들 가운데 누군가가, 어쩌면 핵벗 씨가 그중 하나였을지 모르나, 유난히 격렬한 언쟁을 벌인 것이 아닐까 상상해 보려 애썼다."], ["That would account for everything.", "그렇다면 모든 것이 설명될 터였다."]], [["But when she was in conversation with Mrs.", "하지만 플림데일 부인과 이야기를 나누자, 그 위로가 되는 설명은 더 이상 지탱되지 않을 것 같았다."], ["Plymdale that comforting explanation seemed no longer tenable. “Selina”", "\"셀리나\"는 애처로운 다정함으로 그녀를 맞이하며, 더없이 평범한 화제에도 교화적인 대답을 하려는 성향을 보였다."], ["received her with a pathetic affectionateness and a disposition to give edifying answers on the commonest topics, which could hardly have reference to an ordinary quarrel of which the most important consequence was a perturbation of Mr. Bulstrode’s health.", "그것은 기껏해야 벌스트로드 씨의 건강 악화가 가장 중대한 결과인 평범한 다툼과는 도무지 관련이 없어 보였다."], ["Beforehand Mrs. Bulstrode had thought that she would sooner question Mrs.", "미리부터 벌스트로드 부인은 다른 누구보다도 플림데일 부인에게 더 먼저 물어보리라 생각했었다."], ["Plymdale than any one else; but she found to her surprise that an old friend is not always the person whom it is easiest to make a confidant of: there was the barrier of remembered communication under other circumstances—there was the dislike of being pitied and informed by one who had been long wont to allow her the superiority.", "하지만 놀랍게도 그녀는, 오랜 친구가 언제나 가장 속을 털어놓기 쉬운 사람은 아니라는 것을 깨달았다. 다른 상황에서 주고받은 이야기들이 기억의 장벽으로 남아 있었고, 오래도록 그녀에게 우위를 인정해 온 사람에게서 동정과 정보를 받기 싫은 마음이 있었던 것이다."], ["For certain words of mysterious appropriateness that Mrs. Plymdale let fall about her resolution never to turn her back on her friends, convinced Mrs.", "플림데일 부인이 결코 친구들에게 등을 돌리지 않겠다는 결심에 대해 신비롭게 들어맞는 어떤 말들을 흘렸기 때문에, 벌스트로드 부인은 일어난 일이 어떤 종류의 불행임에 틀림없다고 확신하게 되었다."], ["Bulstrode that what had happened must be some kind of misfortune, and instead of being able to say with her native directness, “What is it that you have in your mind?”", "그래서 타고난 직설로 \"당신 마음속에 있는 게 뭔가요?\"라고 묻기는커녕, 더 분명한 무언가를 듣기 전에 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["she found herself anxious to get away before she had heard anything more explicit.", "그녀는 그 불행이 단순한 금전적 손실 이상의 무엇이라는 동요스러운 확신을 갖기 시작했다."], ["She began to have an agitating certainty that the misfortune was something more than the mere loss of money, being keenly sensitive to the fact that Selina now, just as Mrs. Hackbutt had done before, avoided noticing what she said about her husband, as they would have avoided noticing a personal blemish.", "셀리나 역시 조금 전 핵벗 부인이 그랬던 것과 똑같이, 마치 신체의 흠을 못 본 척하듯 그녀가 남편에 관해 하는 말을 못 들은 체한다는 사실을 그녀가 예민하게 알아차렸기 때문이다."]], [["She said good-by with nervous haste, and told the coachman to drive to Mr. Vincy’s warehouse.", "그녀는 안절부절못하며 황급히 작별 인사를 하고는, 마부에게 빈시 씨의 창고로 가자고 일렀다."], ["In that short drive her dread gathered so much force from the sense of darkness, that when she entered the private counting-house where her brother sat at his desk, her knees trembled and her usually florid face was deathly pale.", "그 짧은 마차 길에, 어둠에 휩싸였다는 느낌으로 그녀의 두려움은 무척 커져서, 오빠가 책상에 앉아 있는 개인 사무실로 들어섰을 때 그녀의 무릎은 떨렸고 평소 혈색 좋던 얼굴은 죽은 듯이 창백했다."], ["Something of the same effect was produced in him by the sight of her: he rose from his seat to meet her, took her by the hand, and said, with his impulsive rashness—", "그녀를 보는 순간 그에게도 같은 효과가 어느 정도 일어났다. 그는 그녀를 맞으러 자리에서 일어나 그녀의 손을 잡고, 충동적이고 경솔한 투로 말했다."]], [["“God help you, Harriet!", "\"하느님이 도우시기를, 해리엇!"], ["you know all.”", "너 다 알고 있구나.\""]], [["That moment was perhaps worse than any which came after.", "그 순간은 어쩌면 그 뒤에 닥친 어떤 순간보다 더 끔찍했다."], ["It contained that concentrated experience which in great crises of emotion reveals the bias of a nature, and is prophetic of the ultimate act which will end an intermediate struggle.", "그 순간에는, 감정의 큰 위기에서 한 사람의 천성이 어느 쪽으로 기우는지를 드러내고, 중간에 벌어지는 갈등을 끝맺을 궁극의 행위를 예언하는 그 응축된 경험이 담겨 있었다. 래플스에 대한 기억이 없었다면 그녀는 여전히 금전적 파산만을 생각했을지도 모른다."], ["Without that memory of Raffles she might still have thought only of monetary ruin, but now along with her brother’s look and words there darted into her mind the idea of some guilt in her husband—then, under the working of terror came the image of her husband exposed to disgrace—and then, after an instant of scorching shame in which she felt only the eyes of the world, with one leap of her heart she was at his side in mournful but unreproaching fellowship with shame and isolation.", "하지만 이제 오빠의 표정과 말과 더불어, 남편에게 어떤 죄가 있으리라는 생각이 그녀의 마음속으로 화살처럼 꽂혔다. 이어서 공포가 작동하는 가운데 망신을 당하는 남편의 모습이 떠올랐고, 그러고는 세상의 눈만이 느껴지던 살을 에는 수치심의 한순간이 지난 뒤, 그녀의 심장은 단번에 솟구쳐 그의 곁으로 가, 수치와 고립을 함께 나누는 슬프지만 책망하지 않는 동반자가 되었다."], ["All this went on within her in a mere flash of time—while she sank into the chair, and raised her eyes to her brother, who stood over her.", "이 모든 것이 그녀 안에서 한순간의 섬광처럼 지나갔다. 그녀가 의자에 주저앉으며, 자기 위로 우뚝 선 오빠에게 눈을 들어 올리는 사이에."], ["“I know nothing, Walter.", "\"저는 아무것도 몰라요, 월터."], ["What is it?”", "무슨 일이지요?\""], ["she said, faintly.", "그녀가 힘없이 말했다."]], [["He told her everything, very inartificially, in slow fragments, making her aware that the scandal went much beyond proof, especially as to the end of Raffles.", "그는 그녀에게 모든 것을, 아주 꾸밈없이, 더디게 토막토막 이야기해 주었다. 그리하여 그 추문이, 특히 래플스의 죽음에 관해서는, 증거를 훨씬 넘어선다는 것을 그녀가 알게 했다."]], [["“People will talk,”", "\"사람들은 입방아를 찧을 거다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Even if a man has been acquitted by a jury, they’ll talk, and nod and wink—and as far as the world goes, a man might often as well be guilty as not.", "\"배심원에게 무죄 판결을 받은 사람이라 해도, 사람들은 입방아를 찧고 고개를 끄덕이며 눈짓을 할 테지. 그러니 세상이 보는 한, 사람이란 죄가 있는 거나 없는 거나 매한가지일 때가 많아."], ["It’s a breakdown blow, and it damages Lydgate as much as Bulstrode.", "이건 파멸의 일격이고, 벌스트로드만큼이나 리드게이트에게도 타격이다."], ["I don’t pretend to say what is the truth.", "무엇이 진실인지 나는 함부로 말하지 않으마."], ["I only wish we had never heard the name of either Bulstrode or Lydgate.", "다만 우리가 벌스트로드든 리드게이트든 그 이름을 아예 들은 적이 없었더라면 싶을 뿐이야."], ["You’d better have been a Vincy all your life, and so had Rosamond.”", "너는 평생 빈시 집안 사람으로 남는 게 나았을 거다. 로저먼드도 그렇고.\""], ["Mrs. Bulstrode made no reply.", "벌스트로드 부인은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“But you must bear up as well as you can, Harriet.", "\"하지만 너는 할 수 있는 한 꿋꿋이 견뎌야 한다, 해리엇."], ["People don’t blame you.", "사람들이 너를 탓하지는 않아."], ["And I’ll stand by you whatever you make up your mind to do,”", "그리고 네가 무엇을 하기로 마음먹든 내가 네 곁을 지키마.\""], ["said the brother, with rough but well-meaning affectionateness.", "오빠가 투박하지만 선의 어린 다정함으로 말했다."]], [["“Give me your arm to the carriage, Walter,”", "\"마차까지 팔 좀 빌려주세요, 월터.\""], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode.", "벌스트로드 부인이 말했다."], ["“I feel very weak.”", "\"몸이 너무 약해진 것 같아요.\""]], [["And when she got home she was obliged to say to her daughter, “I am not well, my dear; I must go and lie down.", "그리고 집에 돌아오자 그녀는 딸에게 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"몸이 좋지 않구나, 얘야."], ["Attend to your papa.", "가서 좀 누워야겠다."], ["Leave me in quiet.", "아버지를 돌봐 드려라."], ["I shall take no dinner.”", "나는 조용히 있게 내버려 두렴. 저녁은 들지 않으마.\""]], [["She locked herself in her room.", "그녀는 방문을 잠갔다."], ["She needed time to get used to her maimed consciousness, her poor lopped life, before she could walk steadily to the place allotted her.", "그녀에게는 절단된 의식에, 가련하게 잘려 나간 삶에 익숙해질 시간이 필요했다. 자기에게 할당된 자리로 흔들림 없이 걸어가기 전에 말이다."], ["A new searching light had fallen on her husband’s character, and she could not judge him leniently: the twenty years in which she had believed in him and venerated him by virtue of his concealments came back with particulars that made them seem an odious deceit.", "새로이 파고드는 빛이 남편의 인품 위에 떨어졌고, 그녀는 그를 너그럽게 판단할 수 없었다. 그의 은폐 덕분에 그를 믿고 떠받들었던 이십 년 세월이, 그 모든 것을 가증스러운 기만으로 보이게 하는 세세한 일들과 함께 되살아났다."], ["He had married her with that bad past life hidden behind him, and she had no faith left to protest his innocence of the worst that was imputed to him.", "그는 그 추악한 과거를 등 뒤에 감춘 채 그녀와 결혼했고, 이제 그녀에게는 그에게 씌워진 최악의 혐의에 대해 그의 결백을 항변할 믿음이 남아 있지 않았다."], ["Her honest ostentatious nature made the sharing of a merited dishonor as bitter as it could be to any mortal.", "정직하고 과시적인 그녀의 천성은, 당연히 받아 마땅한 불명예를 함께 짊어지는 일을 어떤 인간에게도 그러할 만큼 쓰라리게 만들었다."]], [["But this imperfectly taught woman, whose phrases and habits were an odd patchwork, had a loyal spirit within her.", "하지만 그녀의 어법과 습관이 기묘한 누더기 조각보 같았던, 불완전하게 교육받은 이 여인에게는 그 안에 충직한 정신이 있었다."], ["The man whose prosperity she had shared through nearly half a life, and who had unvaryingly cherished her—now that punishment had befallen him it was not possible to her in any sense to forsake him.", "인생의 절반 가까이 번영을 함께 나누었고, 한결같이 그녀를 아껴 준 남자, 이제 그에게 벌이 닥쳤으니 어떤 의미에서도 그를 저버리는 일은 그녀로서는 불가능했다."], ["There is a forsaking which still sits at the same board and lies on the same couch with the forsaken soul, withering it the more by unloving proximity.", "버림받은 영혼과 여전히 같은 식탁에 앉고 같은 침상에 누우면서, 애정 없는 가까움으로 그 영혼을 더욱 시들게 하는, 그런 저버림도 있는 법이다."], ["She knew, when she locked her door, that she should unlock it ready to go down to her unhappy husband and espouse his sorrow, and say of his guilt, I will mourn and not reproach.", "문을 잠그면서 그녀는 알았다. 자기가 그 문을 다시 열고, 불행한 남편에게 내려가 그의 슬픔을 함께하고, 그의 죄에 대해 책망하지 않고 슬퍼하겠노라 말할 준비를 한 채 나서리라는 것을."], ["But she needed time to gather up her strength; she needed to sob out her farewell to all the gladness and pride of her life.", "하지만 그녀에게는 기운을 그러모을 시간이 필요했다. 자기 삶의 모든 기쁨과 자랑에 작별을 고하며 흐느껴 울 시간이 필요했다."], ["When she had resolved to go down, she prepared herself by some little acts which might seem mere folly to a hard onlooker; they were her way of expressing to all spectators visible or invisible that she had begun a new life in which she embraced humiliation.", "내려가기로 결심하자, 그녀는 매정한 구경꾼에게는 한낱 어리석은 짓으로 보일 법한 몇 가지 작은 행동으로 마음의 준비를 했다. 그것들은 보이든 보이지 않든 모든 구경꾼에게, 자기가 굴욕을 끌어안는 새로운 삶을 시작했음을 표현하는 그녀 나름의 방식이었다."], ["She took off all her ornaments and put on a plain black gown, and instead of wearing her much-adorned cap and large bows of hair, she brushed her hair down and put on a plain bonnet-cap, which made her look suddenly like an early Methodist.", "그녀는 장신구를 모두 떼어 내고 수수한 검은 가운을 입었으며, 잔뜩 꾸민 모자와 머리에 단 커다란 매듭 리본 대신 머리를 아래로 빗어 내리고 수수한 보닛 모자를 썼다. 그 모습은 그녀를 문득 초기 감리교 신자처럼 보이게 했다."]], [["Bulstrode, who knew that his wife had been out and had come in saying that she was not well, had spent the time in an agitation equal to hers.", "벌스트로드는 아내가 외출했다가 몸이 좋지 않다며 돌아왔다는 것을 알고 있었고, 그동안 그녀에 못지않은 동요 속에서 시간을 보냈다."], ["He had looked forward to her learning the truth from others, and had acquiesced in that probability, as something easier to him than any confession.", "그는 아내가 다른 사람들에게서 진실을 알게 되리라 예상해 왔고, 그것이 자신이 직접 고백하는 것보다 더 수월한 일이라며 그 가능성을 묵인했었다."], ["But now that he imagined the moment of her knowledge come, he awaited the result in anguish.", "하지만 이제 그녀가 진실을 알게 되는 순간이 왔다고 상상하자, 그는 고뇌 속에서 그 결과를 기다렸다."], ["His daughters had been obliged to consent to leave him, and though he had allowed some food to be brought to him, he had not touched it.", "딸들은 어쩔 수 없이 그의 곁을 떠나는 데 동의해야 했고, 그는 음식을 가져오게는 했으나 손도 대지 않았다."], ["He felt himself perishing slowly in unpitied misery.", "그는 아무도 동정해 주지 않는 비참함 속에서 서서히 죽어 가는 듯한 기분이었다."], ["Perhaps he should never see his wife’s face with affection in it again.", "어쩌면 그는 다시는 애정이 깃든 아내의 얼굴을 보지 못할지도 몰랐다."], ["And if he turned to God there seemed to be no answer but the pressure of retribution.", "그리고 그가 하느님께 돌아선다 해도, 응보의 압박 외에는 아무런 응답이 없는 것 같았다."]], [["It was eight o’clock in the evening before the door opened and his wife entered.", "문이 열리고 아내가 들어선 것은 저녁 여덟 시였다."], ["He dared not look up at her.", "그는 감히 그녀를 올려다보지 못했다."], ["He sat with his eyes bent down, and as she went towards him she thought he looked smaller—he seemed so withered and shrunken.", "그는 눈을 내리깔고 앉아 있었고, 그녀가 그에게 다가가면서 보니 그가 더 작아 보였다. 그토록 시들고 쪼그라든 듯했다."], ["A movement of new compassion and old tenderness went through her like a great wave, and putting one hand on his which rested on the arm of the chair, and the other on his shoulder, she said, solemnly but kindly—", "새로운 연민과 오래된 다정함이 큰 물결처럼 그녀를 관통했고, 의자 팔걸이에 놓인 그의 손 위에 한 손을, 그의 어깨 위에 다른 손을 얹으며 그녀는 엄숙하지만 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“Look up, Nicholas.”", "\"고개를 드세요, 니콜라스.\""]], [["He raised his eyes with a little start and looked at her half amazed for a moment: her pale face, her changed, mourning dress, the trembling about her mouth, all said, “I know;”", "그는 흠칫 놀라며 눈을 들어 잠시 반쯤 어리둥절한 채로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀의 창백한 얼굴, 달라진 상복, 입가의 떨림, 그 모든 것이 \"나는 알아요\"라고 말하고 있었다."], ["and her hands and eyes rested gently on him.", "그리고 그녀의 손과 눈길이 부드럽게 그에게 머물렀다."], ["He burst out crying and they cried together, she sitting at his side.", "그는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 그녀가 그의 곁에 앉아 두 사람은 함께 울었다."], ["They could not yet speak to each other of the shame which she was bearing with him, or of the acts which had brought it down on them.", "그들은 그녀가 그와 함께 짊어지고 있는 수치에 대해서도, 그 수치를 그들 위로 끌어내린 행위들에 대해서도, 아직 서로에게 말할 수 없었다."], ["His confession was silent, and her promise of faithfulness was silent.", "그의 고백은 침묵이었고, 그녀의 변치 않겠다는 약속도 침묵이었다."], ["Open-minded as she was, she nevertheless shrank from the words which would have expressed their mutual consciousness, as she would have shrunk from flakes of fire.", "마음이 트인 사람이었음에도 불구하고, 그녀는 그들의 공통된 의식을 표현해 줄 그 말들을 마치 불꽃을 피하듯 움츠리며 피했다."], ["She could not say, “How much is only slander and false suspicion?” and he did not say, “I am innocent.”", "그녀는 \"어디까지가 그저 중상과 거짓된 의심인가요?\"라고 말할 수 없었고, 그도 \"나는 결백하오\"라고 말하지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXV.", "# CHAPTER LXXV."]], [["“Le sentiment de la fausseté des plaisirs présents, et l’ignorance de la vanité des plaisirs absents causent l’inconstance.”—PASCAL.", "로사먼드는 위협적인 인물이 집에서 사라지고 그 모든 불쾌한 채권자들에게 빚을 갚고 나자 잠시 명랑함을 되찾는 듯한 빛을 보였다."], ["Rosamond had a gleam of returning cheerfulness when the house was freed from the threatening figure, and when all the disagreeable creditors were paid.", "하지만 그녀는 즐겁지 않았다. 결혼 생활은 그녀의 어떤 희망도 채워 주지 못했고, 그녀의 상상 속에서 완전히 망쳐진 것이었다."], ["But she was not joyous: her married life had fulfilled none of her hopes, and had been quite spoiled for her imagination.", "이 짧은 평온의 막간에, 리드게이트는 마음이 어지러울 때 자신이 종종 사나워졌던 것을 떠올리고 로사먼드가 견뎌야 했던 고통을 마음에 두며 그녀에게 조심스럽게 부드러이 대했다."], ["In this brief interval of calm, Lydgate, remembering that he had often been stormy in his hours of perturbation, and mindful of the pain Rosamond had had to bear, was carefully gentle towards her; but he, too, had lost some of his old spirit, and he still felt it necessary to refer to an economical change in their way of living as a matter of course, trying to reconcile her to it gradually, and repressing his anger when she answered by wishing that he would go to live in London.", "그러나 그 또한 예전의 활기를 어느 정도 잃었고, 살림살이를 절약해야 한다는 변화를 당연한 일로 그녀에게 거론할 필요를 여전히 느꼈으며, 그녀를 차츰 그 변화에 따르도록 설득하려 애쓰면서, 그녀가 차라리 런던에 가서 살았으면 좋겠다고 답할 때마다 치미는 분노를 억눌렀다. 그녀가 그런 대답을 하지 않을 때는 나른하게 귀를 기울이며, 자신에게 살아갈 가치가 있는 것이 무엇이나 있는지 의아해했다."], ["When she did not make this answer, she listened languidly, and wondered what she had that was worth living for.", "남편이 분노 속에서 내뱉은 모질고 경멸하는 말들은 그가 처음에는 능동적인 즐거움으로 일깨워 주었던 그 허영심을 깊이 상하게 했다."], ["The hard and contemptuous words which had fallen from her husband in his anger had deeply offended that vanity which he had at first called into active enjoyment; and what she regarded as his perverse way of looking at things, kept up a secret repulsion, which made her receive all his tenderness as a poor substitute for the happiness he had failed to give her.", "그리고 그녀가 사물을 바라보는 그의 비뚤어진 방식이라고 여긴 것은 은밀한 반발심을 부추겨, 그가 베푸는 모든 다정함을 그가 끝내 주지 못한 행복의 초라한 대체물로 받아들이게 만들었다. 그들은 이웃들 사이에서 불리한 처지에 놓였고, 콸링엄을 향한 전망도 더 이상 없었다."], ["They were at a disadvantage with their neighbors, and there was no longer any outlook towards Quallingham—there was no outlook anywhere except in an occasional letter from Will Ladislaw.", "윌 래디슬로에게서 이따금 오는 편지 말고는 어디에도 전망이 없었다. 그녀는 윌이 미들마치를 떠나기로 결심한 데 마음이 찔리고 실망했으니, 그가 도로테아를 흠모한다는 것을 알고 짐작했음에도, 그가 자신을 훨씬 더 흠모하고 있거나 반드시 그렇게 되리라는 믿음을 은밀히 품고 있었기 때문이다."], ["She had felt stung and disappointed by Will’s resolution to quit Middlemarch, for in spite of what she knew and guessed about his admiration for Dorothea, she secretly cherished the belief that he had, or would necessarily come to have, much more admiration for herself; Rosamond being one of those women who live much in the idea that each man they meet would have preferred them if the preference had not been hopeless. Mrs.", "로사먼드는 만나는 남자마다 자신을 더 좋아했을 텐데 다만 그 호감이 가망 없는 일이라 그러지 못했을 뿐이라는 생각 속에서 살아가는 부류의 여자였던 것이다."], ["Casaubon was all very well; but Will’s interest in her dated before he knew Mrs. Lydgate.", "캐소본 부인이야 더할 나위 없었지만, 윌이 그녀에게 품은 관심은 리드게이트 부인을 알기 전부터 시작된 것이었다."], ["Rosamond took his way of talking to herself, which was a mixture of playful fault-finding and hyperbolical gallantry, as the disguise of a deeper feeling; and in his presence she felt that agreeable titillation of vanity and sense of romantic drama which Lydgate’s presence had no longer the magic to create.", "로사먼드는 윌이 자기에게 말하는 방식을, 즉 장난스러운 흠잡기와 과장된 친절이 뒤섞인 그 방식을 더 깊은 감정의 위장으로 받아들였다. 그리고 그가 곁에 있으면 리드게이트의 존재가 더 이상 만들어 낼 마력을 잃어버린 그 허영심의 기분 좋은 간지러움과 낭만적인 연극 같은 감각을 느꼈다."], ["She even fancied—what will not men and women fancy in these matters?—that Will exaggerated his admiration for Mrs. Casaubon in order to pique herself.", "그녀는 심지어, 이런 문제에서 남녀가 무엇인들 상상하지 않겠는가만, 윌이 자기를 약 올리려고 캐소본 부인에 대한 흠모를 과장하는 것이라고까지 상상했다."], ["In this way poor Rosamond’s brain had been busy before Will’s departure.", "이런 식으로 가엾은 로사먼드의 머릿속은 윌이 떠나기 전부터 분주했다."], ["He would have made, she thought, a much more suitable husband for her than she had found in Lydgate.", "그녀가 보기에 윌은 리드게이트에게서 찾은 것보다 훨씬 더 어울리는 남편이 되었을 사람이었다."], ["No notion could have been falser than this, for Rosamond’s discontent in her marriage was due to the conditions of marriage itself, to its demand for self-suppression and tolerance, and not to the nature of her husband; but the easy conception of an unreal Better had a sentimental charm which diverted her ennui.", "이보다 더 그릇된 생각은 없었을 텐데, 로사먼드가 결혼 생활에서 느끼는 불만은 남편의 천성 때문이 아니라 자기 억제와 관용을 요구하는 결혼 자체의 조건 때문이었기 때문이다. 그러나 실재하지 않는 더 나은 것을 손쉽게 떠올리는 일에는 그녀의 권태를 달래 주는 감상적인 매력이 있었다."], ["She constructed a little romance which was to vary the flatness of her life: Will Ladislaw was always to be a bachelor and live near her, always to be at her command, and have an understood though never fully expressed passion for her, which would be sending out lambent flames every now and then in interesting scenes.", "그녀는 자기 삶의 단조로움을 변화시켜 줄 작은 연애담을 지어냈다. 윌 래디슬로는 언제까지나 독신으로 그녀 곁에 살면서 늘 그녀의 부름에 응하고, 결코 온전히 표현되지는 않으나 서로 이해되는 열정을 그녀에게 품고 있어, 이따금 흥미로운 장면들 속에서 그 열정이 가물거리는 불꽃을 내뿜으리라는 것이었다."], ["His departure had been a proportionate disappointment, and had sadly increased her weariness of Middlemarch; but at first she had the alternative dream of pleasures in store from her intercourse with the family at Quallingham.", "그가 떠난 것은 그만큼의 실망이었고, 미들마치에 대한 그녀의 권태를 슬프게도 더욱 키워 놓았다. 그러나 처음에는 콸링엄 가족과의 교류에서 누릴 즐거움이 기다리고 있으리라는 대체용 꿈이 있었다."], ["Since then the troubles of her married life had deepened, and the absence of other relief encouraged her regretful rumination over that thin romance which she had once fed on.", "그 후로 결혼 생활의 시련은 깊어졌고, 다른 위안이 없다 보니 한때 마음의 양식으로 삼았던 그 빈약한 연애담을 아쉬워하며 곱씹게 되었다. 남녀는 자기 자신의 징후에 대해 슬픈 착각을 저질러, 막연하고 불안한 갈망을 어떤 때는 천재성으로, 어떤 때는 신앙심으로, 그리고 더욱 흔하게는 위대한 사랑으로 여긴다."], ["Men and women make sad mistakes about their own symptoms, taking their vague uneasy longings, sometimes for genius, sometimes for religion, and oftener still for a mighty love.", "윌 래디슬로는 절반은 그녀에게, 절반은 리드게이트에게 보내는 식의 수다스러운 편지를 써 보냈고, 그녀는 답장을 했다. 그들의 이별이 최종적인 것은 아니리라고 그녀는 느꼈고, 이제 그녀가 가장 갈망하는 변화는 리드게이트가 런던에 가서 사는 것이었다."], ["Will Ladislaw had written chatty letters, half to her and half to Lydgate, and she had replied: their separation, she felt, was not likely to be final, and the change she now most longed for was that Lydgate should go to live in London; everything would be agreeable in London; and she had set to work with quiet determination to win this result, when there came a sudden, delightful promise which inspirited her.", "런던에서라면 모든 것이 즐거우리라. 그래서 그녀가 조용한 결의로 이 결과를 얻어 내기 위해 공을 들이기 시작했을 무렵, 그녀에게 활기를 불어넣는 갑작스럽고 반가운 약속이 찾아왔다."]], [["It came shortly before the memorable meeting at the town-hall, and was nothing less than a letter from Will Ladislaw to Lydgate, which turned indeed chiefly on his new interest in plans of colonization, but mentioned incidentally, that he might find it necessary to pay a visit to Middlemarch within the next few weeks—a very pleasant necessity, he said, almost as good as holidays to a schoolboy.", "그것은 시청에서 열린 그 잊지 못할 모임이 있기 직전에 온 것으로, 다름 아닌 윌 래디슬로가 리드게이트에게 보낸 편지였다. 편지는 사실 주로 그가 새로 관심을 갖게 된 식민 사업 계획에 관한 것이었으나, 앞으로 몇 주 안에 미들마치를 한번 찾아야 할지도 모르겠다고 지나가는 말처럼 언급하고 있었다."], ["He hoped there was his old place on the rug, and a great deal of music in store for him.", "그것은 학생에게 방학이나 다름없이 좋은, 아주 즐거운 일이라고 그는 말했다. 난로 앞 깔개 위의 자기 옛 자리가 그대로 있기를, 그리고 들을 음악이 잔뜩 마련되어 있기를 바란다고 했다."], ["But he was quite uncertain as to the time.", "다만 시기에 대해서는 도무지 확실치 않다고 했다."], ["While Lydgate was reading the letter to Rosamond, her face looked like a reviving flower—it grew prettier and more blooming.", "리드게이트가 로사먼드에게 편지를 읽어 주는 동안, 그녀의 얼굴은 되살아나는 꽃처럼 보였다. 더 예뻐지고 더 활짝 피어났다."], ["There was nothing unendurable now: the debts were paid, Mr.", "이제 견딜 수 없는 일은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Ladislaw was coming, and Lydgate would be persuaded to leave Middlemarch and settle in London, which was “so different from a provincial town.”", "빚은 갚았고, 래디슬로 씨가 올 것이며, 리드게이트는 미들마치를 떠나 \"지방 도시와는 너무도 다른\" 런던에 정착하도록 설득될 것이었다."]], [["That was a bright bit of morning.", "그것은 환한 아침의 한 조각이었다."], ["But soon the sky became black over poor Rosamond.", "그러나 곧 가엾은 로사먼드의 머리 위로 하늘이 시커멓게 변했다."], ["The presence of a new gloom in her husband, about which he was entirely reserved towards her—for he dreaded to expose his lacerated feeling to her neutrality and misconception—soon received a painfully strange explanation, alien to all her previous notions of what could affect her happiness.", "남편에게 새로운 우울이 깃들었으나 그는 그것에 관해 그녀에게 완전히 입을 다물었으니, 자신의 갈가리 찢긴 감정을 그녀의 무관심과 오해에 드러내기가 두려웠던 것이다. 그 우울은 곧 고통스럽고도 기이한 설명을 얻었는데, 그것은 자신의 행복에 영향을 미칠 수 있는 것에 관한 그녀의 종래 관념과는 전혀 동떨어진 것이었다."], ["In the new gayety of her spirits, thinking that Lydgate had merely a worse fit of moodiness than usual, causing him to leave her remarks unanswered, and evidently to keep out of her way as much as possible, she chose, a few days after the meeting, and without speaking to him on the subject, to send out notes of invitation for a small evening party, feeling convinced that this was a judicious step, since people seemed to have been keeping aloof from them, and wanted restoring to the old habit of intercourse.", "새로 명랑해진 기분 속에서, 그녀는 리드게이트가 그저 평소보다 더 심한 침울에 빠진 것뿐이어서 자기 말에 대꾸도 하지 않고 가능한 한 자신을 피하려는 것이 분명하다고 생각하고는, 모임이 있은 며칠 뒤에 그 일에 관해 남편에게 말하지도 않은 채 작은 저녁 모임의 초대장을 보내기로 했다. 사람들이 자기들을 멀리하는 듯하니 예전의 교류 습관을 되살릴 필요가 있다고 여겨, 이것이 분별 있는 조치라고 확신했던 것이다."], ["When the invitations had been accepted, she would tell Lydgate, and give him a wise admonition as to how a medical man should behave to his neighbors; for Rosamond had the gravest little airs possible about other people’s duties.", "초대가 받아들여지면 리드게이트에게 알리고, 의사라면 이웃들에게 어떻게 처신해야 하는지 현명하게 타이를 작정이었다. 로사먼드는 다른 사람들의 의무에 관해서라면 더할 나위 없이 진지하고 자그마한 태도를 지니고 있었던 것이다."], ["But all the invitations were declined, and the last answer came into Lydgate’s hands.", "그러나 초대는 모두 거절당했고, 마지막 답장이 리드게이트의 손에 들어왔다."]], [["“This is Chichely’s scratch.", "\"이건 치철리의 갈겨쓴 글씨로군."], ["What is he writing to you about?”", "당신한테 무슨 일로 편지를 쓴 거지?\""], ["said Lydgate, wonderingly, as he handed the note to her.", "리드게이트가 의아해하며 그녀에게 쪽지를 건네면서 말했다."], ["She was obliged to let him see it, and, looking at her severely, he said—", "그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 그에게 그것을 보여 줄 수밖에 없었고, 그는 그녀를 매섭게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Why on earth have you been sending out invitations without telling me, Rosamond?", "\"도대체 왜 나한테 말도 없이 초대장을 보낸 거요, 로사먼드?"], ["I beg, I insist that you will not invite any one to this house.", "부탁이오, 이 집에는 그 누구도 초대하지 말라고 강력히 요구하는 바이오."], ["I suppose you have been inviting others, and they have refused too.”", "다른 사람들도 초대한 모양인데, 그들도 거절했겠지.\""], ["She said nothing.", "그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Do you hear me?”", "\"내 말 듣고 있소?\""], ["thundered Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 우레같이 소리쳤다."]], [["“Yes, certainly I hear you,”", "\"네, 물론 듣고 있어요.\""], ["said Rosamond, turning her head aside with the movement of a graceful long-necked bird.", "로사먼드가 우아하게 목이 긴 새 같은 몸짓으로 고개를 돌리며 말했다."]], [["Lydgate tossed his head without any grace and walked out of the room, feeling himself dangerous.", "리드게이트는 조금도 우아하지 않게 머리를 홱 젖히고는 방을 나가 버렸으니, 자기 자신이 위험한 사람처럼 느껴졌던 것이다."], ["Rosamond’s thought was, that he was getting more and more unbearable—not that there was any new special reason for this peremptoriness.", "로사먼드의 생각은 그가 점점 더 견딜 수 없어진다는 것이었다. 이 단호함에 무슨 새로운 특별한 이유가 있어서가 아니었다."], ["His indisposition to tell her anything in which he was sure beforehand that she would not be interested was growing into an unreflecting habit, and she was in ignorance of everything connected with the thousand pounds except that the loan had come from her uncle Bulstrode.", "그녀가 흥미를 가질 리 없다고 미리 확신하는 일이라면 무엇도 그녀에게 말하지 않으려는 그의 성향은 무심한 습관으로 굳어 가고 있었고, 그녀는 그 천 파운드와 관련된 모든 일에 대해, 그 돈이 외삼촌 벌스트로드에게서 빌린 것이라는 사실 말고는 아무것도 알지 못했다."], ["Lydgate’s odious humors and their neighbors’ apparent avoidance of them had an unaccountable date for her in their relief from money difficulties.", "그녀에게 리드게이트의 고약한 기분과 이웃들이 자기들을 분명히 피하는 일은 돈 문제에서 벗어난 것과 더불어 도무지 설명할 수 없는 시점에 일어난 일이었다."], ["If the invitations had been accepted she would have gone to invite her mamma and the rest, whom she had seen nothing of for several days; and she now put on her bonnet to go and inquire what had become of them all, suddenly feeling as if there were a conspiracy to leave her in isolation with a husband disposed to offend everybody.", "초대가 받아들여졌더라면 그녀는 며칠째 보지 못한 어머니와 다른 가족들을 초대하러 갔을 것이다. 그런데 이제 그녀는 모든 이를 노하게 만들기로 작정한 남편과 함께 자신을 고립 속에 가두려는 음모라도 있는 듯 갑자기 느끼면서, 그들 모두가 어찌 된 일인지 알아보러 가려고 보닛을 썼다."], ["It was after the dinner hour, and she found her father and mother seated together alone in the drawing-room.", "만찬 시간이 지난 뒤였고, 그녀는 응접실에 아버지와 어머니만 함께 앉아 있는 것을 발견했다. 두 사람은 슬픈 표정으로 \"오냐, 얘야!\""], ["They greeted her with sad looks, saying “Well, my dear!” and no more.", "하고 그녀를 맞이할 뿐 더는 말이 없었다."], ["She had never seen her father look so downcast; and seating herself near him she said—", "그녀는 아버지가 그토록 풀이 죽은 모습을 본 적이 없었다. 아버지 곁에 앉으며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Is there anything the matter, papa?”", "\"무슨 일이 있나요, 아버지?\""]], [["He did not answer, but Mrs.", "그는 대답하지 않았으나, 빈시 부인이 말했다."], ["Vincy said, “Oh, my dear, have you heard nothing?", "\"오, 얘야, 아무 얘기도 못 들었니?"], ["It won’t be long before it reaches you.”", "머지않아 네 귀에도 들어갈 텐데.\""]], [["“Is it anything about Tertius?”", "\"테르티우스에 관한 일인가요?\""], ["said Rosamond, turning pale.", "로사먼드가 창백해지며 말했다."], ["The idea of trouble immediately connected itself with what had been unaccountable to her in him.", "시련이라는 생각은 곧바로 그에게서 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었던 일과 연결되었다."]], [["“Oh, my dear, yes.", "\"오, 얘야, 그래."], ["To think of your marrying into this trouble.", "네가 이런 시련 속으로 시집을 가다니."], ["Debt was bad enough, but this will be worse.”", "빚도 충분히 나빴지만, 이건 더 나쁠 거다.\""]], [["“Stay, stay, Lucy,”", "\"가만, 가만있어요, 루시.\""], ["said Mr. Vincy.", "빈시 씨가 말했다."], ["“Have you heard nothing about your uncle Bulstrode, Rosamond?”", "\"벌스트로드 외삼촌에 관한 얘기는 아무것도 못 들었니, 로사먼드?\""]], [["“No, papa,”", "\"못 들었어요, 아버지.\""], ["said the poor thing, feeling as if trouble were not anything she had before experienced, but some invisible power with an iron grasp that made her soul faint within her.", "가엾은 것이 말했으니, 시련이라는 것이 전에 겪어 본 그 무엇이 아니라 강철 같은 손아귀로 자기 영혼을 안에서 까무러치게 만드는 어떤 보이지 않는 힘처럼 느껴졌던 것이다."]], [["Her father told her everything, saying at the end, “It’s better for you to know, my dear.", "아버지는 그녀에게 모든 것을 이야기한 뒤 끝에 가서 말했다. \"네가 아는 편이 낫겠다, 얘야."], ["I think Lydgate must leave the town.", "리드게이트는 이 마을을 떠나야 할 게다."], ["Things have gone against him.", "일이 그에게 불리하게 돌아갔어."], ["I dare say he couldn’t help it.", "어쩔 수 없었으리라 싶다."], ["I don’t accuse him of any harm,”", "그를 무슨 잘못이 있다고 탓하는 건 아니다.\""], ["said Mr. Vincy.", "빈시 씨가 말했다."], ["He had always before been disposed to find the utmost fault with Lydgate.", "그는 전에는 늘 리드게이트의 흠을 최대한 잡으려 들었던 사람이었다."]], [["The shock to Rosamond was terrible.", "로사먼드가 받은 충격은 끔찍했다."], ["It seemed to her that no lot could be so cruelly hard as hers to have married a man who had become the centre of infamous suspicions.", "그녀가 보기에 치욕스러운 의심의 중심이 되어 버린 남자와 결혼한 자기 처지만큼 가혹하게 모진 운명은 없을 것 같았다."], ["In many cases it is inevitable that the shame is felt to be the worst part of crime; and it would have required a great deal of disentangling reflection, such as had never entered into Rosamond’s life, for her in these moments to feel that her trouble was less than if her husband had been certainly known to have done something criminal.", "많은 경우 수치심이 범죄의 가장 나쁜 부분으로 느껴지는 것은 어쩔 수 없는 일이다. 그리고 이런 순간에 자신의 시련이 남편이 분명히 무슨 범죄를 저질렀다고 알려진 경우보다 덜하다고 느끼려면, 로사먼드의 인생에 한 번도 들어와 본 적 없는 종류의, 실타래를 풀어 가는 깊은 성찰이 무척이나 필요했을 것이다."], ["All the shame seemed to be there.", "모든 수치가 거기 있는 것만 같았다."], ["And she had innocently married this man with the belief that he and his family were a glory to her!", "그런데 그녀는 그와 그의 가문이 자신에게 영광이 되리라 믿고 순진하게도 이 남자와 결혼했던 것이다!"], ["She showed her usual reticence to her parents, and only said, that if Lydgate had done as she wished he would have left Middlemarch long ago.", "그녀는 부모에게 늘 그렇듯 말을 아꼈고, 다만 리드게이트가 자기 바람대로만 했더라면 진작에 미들마치를 떠났을 거라고만 말했다."]], [["“She bears it beyond anything,”", "\"저 애는 정말 잘도 견디는군요.\""], ["said her mother when she was gone.", "그녀가 떠난 뒤 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, thank God!”", "\"아, 다행이오!\""], ["said Mr. Vincy, who was much broken down.", "몹시 무너져 내린 빈시 씨가 말했다."]], [["But Rosamond went home with a sense of justified repugnance towards her husband.", "그러나 로사먼드는 남편에 대해 정당한 혐오감을 품고 집으로 돌아갔다. 그가 정말로 무슨 짓을 했는가, 정말로 어떻게 행동했는가?"], ["What had he really done—how had he really acted?", "그녀는 알지 못했다. 왜 그는 자기에게 모든 것을 말해 주지 않았는가?"], ["She did not know. Why had he not told her everything?", "그는 그 일에 관해 그녀에게 말하지 않았고, 물론 그녀도 그에게 말할 수가 없었다."], ["He did not speak to her on the subject, and of course she could not speak to him.", "한번은 아버지에게 다시 친정으로 가서 살게 해 달라고 청해 볼까 하는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그 전망을 곰곰이 생각하니 그것은 더없는 황량함으로만 여겨졌다."], ["It came into her mind once that she would ask her father to let her go home again; but dwelling on that prospect made it seem utter dreariness to her: a married woman gone back to live with her parents—life seemed to have no meaning for her in such a position: she could not contemplate herself in it.", "결혼한 여자가 부모에게로 되돌아가 사는 것이라니, 그런 처지에서는 삶에 아무런 의미도 없을 것 같았다. 그녀는 그런 처지에 놓인 자신을 도저히 그려 볼 수 없었다."]], [["The next two days Lydgate observed a change in her, and believed that she had heard the bad news.", "그 후 이틀 동안 리드게이트는 그녀에게 일어난 변화를 알아챘고, 그녀가 그 나쁜 소식을 들었다고 믿었다."], ["Would she speak to him about it, or would she go on forever in the silence which seemed to imply that she believed him guilty?", "그녀는 그 일에 관해 자기에게 말을 할 것인가, 아니면 자기를 유죄라고 믿는 듯한 그 침묵을 영원히 이어 갈 것인가? 그가 거의 모든 접촉이 고통인 병적인 마음 상태에 있었음을 우리는 기억해야 한다."], ["We must remember that he was in a morbid state of mind, in which almost all contact was pain.", "분명 이 경우 로사먼드도 그가 입을 다물고 마음을 터놓지 않는 데 대해 똑같이 불평할 이유가 있었다."], ["Certainly Rosamond in this case had equal reason to complain of reserve and want of confidence on his part; but in the bitterness of his soul he excused himself;—was he not justified in shrinking from the task of telling her, since now she knew the truth she had no impulse to speak to him?", "그러나 영혼의 쓰라림 속에서 그는 자신을 변명했다. 이제 그녀가 진실을 알게 되었는데도 자기에게 말을 걸 마음이 없으니, 그녀에게 알리는 일에서 물러서는 것이 정당하지 않은가?"], ["But a deeper-lying consciousness that he was in fault made him restless, and the silence between them became intolerable to him; it was as if they were both adrift on one piece of wreck and looked away from each other.", "그러나 자신에게 잘못이 있다는 더 깊이 자리 잡은 의식이 그를 불안하게 했고, 둘 사이의 침묵은 그에게 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었다. 마치 두 사람이 한 조각 난파선에 몸을 싣고 떠다니면서 서로 외면하고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["He thought, “I am a fool.", "그는 생각했다. \"난 바보다."], ["Haven’t I given up expecting anything?", "무언가를 기대하는 걸 이미 포기하지 않았던가?"], ["I have married care, not help.”", "나는 도움이 아니라 근심거리와 결혼했어.\""], ["And that evening he said—", "그날 저녁 그가 말했다."]], [["“Rosamond, have you heard anything that distresses you?”", "\"로사먼드, 당신을 괴롭히는 무슨 얘기를 들었소?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she answered, laying down her work, which she had been carrying on with a languid semi-consciousness, most unlike her usual self.", "그녀가 평소의 자기 모습과는 전혀 다르게 나른하고 반쯤 넋이 나간 채로 해 오던 바느질감을 내려놓으며 대답했다."]], [["“What have you heard?”", "\"무슨 얘기를 들었소?\""]], [["“Everything, I suppose.", "\"전부 다요, 아마."], ["Papa told me.”", "아버지가 말씀해 주셨어요.\""]], [["“That people think me disgraced?”", "\"사람들이 나를 망신스럽다고 여긴다는 거?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said Rosamond, faintly, beginning to sew again automatically.", "로사먼드가 희미하게 말하며 다시 기계적으로 바느질을 시작했다."]], [["There was silence.", "침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Lydgate thought, “If she has any trust in me—any notion of what I am, she ought to speak now and say that she does not believe I have deserved disgrace.”", "리드게이트는 생각했다. \"그녀가 나를 조금이라도 믿는다면, 내가 어떤 사람인지 조금이라도 안다면, 지금 입을 열어 내가 망신을 당해 마땅하다고는 믿지 않는다고 말해야 마땅하다.\""]], [["But Rosamond on her side went on moving her fingers languidly.", "그러나 로사먼드는 로사먼드대로 나른하게 손가락만 계속 움직였다."], ["Whatever was to be said on the subject she expected to come from Tertius.", "이 일에 관해 할 말이 무엇이든 그것은 테르티우스에게서 나오리라고 그녀는 기대했다."], ["What did she know?", "자기가 무엇을 안단 말인가?"], ["And if he were innocent of any wrong, why did he not do something to clear himself?", "그리고 그가 아무 잘못도 저지르지 않았다면, 왜 자기 결백을 밝히기 위해 무언가를 하지 않는단 말인가?"]], [["This silence of hers brought a new rush of gall to that bitter mood in which Lydgate had been saying to himself that nobody believed in him—even Farebrother had not come forward.", "그녀의 이 침묵은 리드게이트가 아무도 자기를 믿어 주지 않는다고, 페어브러더조차 나서지 않았다고 혼잣말하던 그 쓰라린 기분에 새로운 쓸개즙을 한 줄기 끼얹었다."], ["He had begun to question her with the intent that their conversation should disperse the chill fog which had gathered between them, but he felt his resolution checked by despairing resentment.", "그는 둘 사이에 자욱이 낀 차가운 안개를 대화로 흩어 버리려는 뜻에서 그녀에게 묻기 시작했으나, 절망 어린 원망에 자신의 결심이 가로막히는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Even this trouble, like the rest, she seemed to regard as if it were hers alone.", "다른 모든 시련과 마찬가지로 이 시련조차 그녀는 마치 자기 혼자만의 것인 양 여기는 듯했다."], ["He was always to her a being apart, doing what she objected to.", "그는 그녀에게 언제나 동떨어진 존재, 그녀가 반대하는 일을 하는 존재였다."], ["He started from his chair with an angry impulse, and thrusting his hands in his pockets, walked up and down the room.", "그는 분노의 충동에 사로잡혀 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["There was an underlying consciousness all the while that he should have to master this anger, and tell her everything, and convince her of the facts.", "그러는 내내 이 분노를 다스리고 그녀에게 모든 것을 말하여 사실을 납득시켜야 한다는 의식이 밑바닥에 깔려 있었다."], ["For he had almost learned the lesson that he must bend himself to her nature, and that because she came short in her sympathy, he must give the more.", "자신이 그녀의 천성에 맞추어 굽혀야 한다는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 공감이 부족한 만큼 자신이 더 많이 베풀어야 한다는 것을 그는 거의 깨우친 터였기 때문이다."], ["Soon he recurred to his intention of opening himself: the occasion must not be lost.", "곧 그는 마음을 털어놓겠다는 자신의 뜻으로 되돌아왔다. 이 기회를 놓쳐서는 안 되었다."], ["If he could bring her to feel with some solemnity that here was a slander which must be met and not run away from, and that the whole trouble had come out of his desperate want of money, it would be a moment for urging powerfully on her that they should be one in the resolve to do with as little money as possible, so that they might weather the bad time and keep themselves independent.", "만약 여기 맞서 싸워야 하고 도망쳐서는 안 될 중상모략이 있다는 것을, 그리고 이 모든 시련이 절박한 돈 부족에서 비롯되었다는 것을 그녀가 어느 정도 엄숙하게 느끼게 할 수 있다면, 그때야말로 그들이 한마음이 되어 가능한 한 돈을 적게 쓰기로 결의함으로써 이 험한 시기를 헤쳐 나가고 자립을 지켜야 한다고 그녀에게 강력히 촉구할 순간이 될 것이었다."], ["He would mention the definite measures which he desired to take, and win her to a willing spirit.", "그는 자신이 취하고자 하는 구체적인 방책들을 언급하여 그녀를 기꺼운 마음으로 이끌어 낼 작정이었다. 그는 마땅히 이를 시도해야 했다."], ["He was bound to try this—and what else was there for him to do?", "그것 말고 그가 달리 할 수 있는 일이 무엇이 있었겠는가?"]], [["He did not know how long he had been walking uneasily backwards and forwards, but Rosamond felt that it was long, and wished that he would sit down.", "그는 자신이 얼마나 오래 불안하게 방 안을 오가고 있었는지 알지 못했으나, 로사먼드는 그것이 길다고 느껴 그가 좀 앉았으면 했다."], ["She too had begun to think this an opportunity for urging on Tertius what he ought to do.", "그녀 또한 이것을 테르티우스에게 그가 해야 할 일을 촉구할 기회라고 생각하기 시작했던 것이다."], ["Whatever might be the truth about all this misery, there was one dread which asserted itself.", "이 모든 비참한 일에 관한 진실이 무엇이든, 떨쳐 낼 수 없이 고개를 드는 한 가지 두려움이 있었다."]], [["Lydgate at last seated himself, not in his usual chair, but in one nearer to Rosamond, leaning aside in it towards her, and looking at her gravely before he reopened the sad subject.", "리드게이트는 마침내 자리에 앉았는데, 평소의 자기 의자가 아니라 로사먼드에게 더 가까운 의자였다. 그는 그쪽으로 비스듬히 몸을 기울인 채, 그 슬픈 화제를 다시 꺼내기 전에 그녀를 진지하게 바라보았다."], ["He had conquered himself so far, and was about to speak with a sense of solemnity, as on an occasion which was not to be repeated.", "그는 그만큼 자신을 이겨 낸 터였고, 다시는 되풀이되지 않을 일을 대하듯 엄숙한 마음으로 입을 떼려던 참이었다."], ["He had even opened his lips, when Rosamond, letting her hands fall, looked at him and said—", "그가 막 입술을 열었을 때, 로사먼드가 두 손을 떨어뜨리고는 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Surely, Tertius—”", "\"테르티우스, 분명히—\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"뭐요?\""]], [["“Surely now at last you have given up the idea of staying in Middlemarch.", "\"분명히 이제는 마침내 당신도 미들마치에 머무르겠다는 생각을 버리셨겠죠."], ["I cannot go on living here.", "저는 여기서 더 살 수 없어요."], ["Let us go to London.", "우리 런던으로 가요."], ["Papa, and every one else, says you had better go.", "아버지도, 다른 모든 사람도 당신이 떠나는 게 낫겠다고 말해요."], ["Whatever misery I have to put up with, it will be easier away from here.”", "제가 무슨 비참한 일을 견뎌야 하든, 여기를 떠나면 한결 견디기 쉬울 거예요.\""]], [["Lydgate felt miserably jarred.", "리드게이트는 비참하게 가슴이 어긋나는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Instead of that critical outpouring for which he had prepared himself with effort, here was the old round to be gone through again.", "애써 마음의 준비를 했던 그 결정적인 토로 대신, 또다시 거쳐야 할 그 해묵은 되풀이가 거기 있었던 것이다."], ["He could not bear it.", "그는 그것을 견딜 수 없었다."], ["With a quick change of countenance he rose and went out of the room.", "그는 안색이 홱 변하더니 일어나서 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["Perhaps if he had been strong enough to persist in his determination to be the more because she was less, that evening might have had a better issue.", "어쩌면 그가 그녀가 덜한 만큼 자기가 더하리라는 결심을 끝까지 밀고 나갈 만큼 강했더라면, 그날 저녁은 더 나은 결말을 맞았을지도 모른다."], ["If his energy could have borne down that check, he might still have wrought on Rosamond’s vision and will.", "그의 기력이 그 좌절을 눌러 이겨 낼 수 있었더라면, 그는 그래도 로사먼드의 시야와 의지에 영향을 미쳤을지도 모른다."], ["We cannot be sure that any natures, however inflexible or peculiar, will resist this effect from a more massive being than their own.", "아무리 굽힐 줄 모르고 별난 천성이라 해도 자기보다 더 묵직한 존재로부터 받는 이 영향에 저항하리라고는 우리는 장담할 수 없다."], ["They may be taken by storm and for the moment converted, becoming part of the soul which enwraps them in the ardor of its movement.", "그런 천성은 폭풍처럼 휘몰아치는 기세에 사로잡혀 한순간 돌아서서, 격렬한 움직임으로 자신을 감싸는 그 영혼의 일부가 될 수도 있다."], ["But poor Lydgate had a throbbing pain within him, and his energy had fallen short of its task.", "그러나 가엾은 리드게이트는 가슴 속에 욱신거리는 고통을 안고 있었고, 그의 기력은 그 과업에 미치지 못했다."]], [["The beginning of mutual understanding and resolve seemed as far off as ever; nay, it seemed blocked out by the sense of unsuccessful effort.", "서로를 이해하고 결의를 다지는 일의 첫걸음은 여전히 아득히 멀게만 보였다. 아니, 실패로 끝난 노력에 대한 의식에 가로막혀 버린 것만 같았다."], ["They lived on from day to day with their thoughts still apart, Lydgate going about what work he had in a mood of despair, and Rosamond feeling, with some justification, that he was behaving cruelly.", "두 사람은 여전히 생각을 따로 둔 채 하루하루를 살아갔고, 리드게이트는 자기에게 있는 일이 무엇이든 절망적인 기분으로 처리했으며, 로사먼드는 다소 근거 있게도 그가 잔인하게 굴고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["It was of no use to say anything to Tertius; but when Will Ladislaw came, she was determined to tell him everything.", "테르티우스에게 무슨 말을 해 봐야 소용없었다. 그러나 윌 래디슬로가 오면, 그에게 모든 것을 이야기하리라고 그녀는 마음먹었다."], ["In spite of her general reticence, she needed some one who would recognize her wrongs.", "평소의 그 과묵함에도 불구하고, 그녀는 자신이 부당한 일을 당했음을 알아줄 누군가가 필요했던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXVI.", "# 제72장"]], [["To mercy, pity, peace, and love All pray in their distress, And to these virtues of delight, Return their thankfulness.", "감사를 되돌려 바친다."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], [". .", "."], ["For Mercy has a human heart, Pity a human face; And Love, the human form divine; And Peace, the human dress.", "."], ["—WILLIAM BLAKE: Songs of Innocence.", ". ."]], [["Some days later, Lydgate was riding to Lowick Manor, in consequence of a summons from Dorothea. The summons had not been unexpected, since it had followed a letter from Mr. Bulstrode, in which he stated that he had resumed his arrangements for quitting Middlemarch, and must remind Lydgate of his previous communications about the Hospital, to the purport of which he still adhered. It had been his duty, before taking further steps, to reopen the subject with Mrs. Casaubon, who now wished, as before, to discuss the question with Lydgate. “Your views may possibly have undergone some change,” wrote Mr. Bulstrode; “but, in that case also, it is desirable that you should lay them before her.”", "자비는 인간의 심장을 지녔고,\n연민은 인간의 얼굴을 지녔으며,"]], [["Dorothea awaited his arrival with eager interest. Though, in deference to her masculine advisers, she had refrained from what Sir James had called “interfering in this Bulstrode business,” the hardship of Lydgate’s position was continually in her mind, and when Bulstrode applied to her again about the hospital, she felt that the opportunity was come to her which she had been hindered from hastening. In her luxurious home, wandering under the boughs of her own great trees, her thought was going out over the lot of others, and her emotions were imprisoned. The idea of some active good within her reach, “haunted her like a passion,” and another’s need having once come to her as a distinct image, preoccupied her desire with the yearning to give relief, and made her own ease tasteless. She was full of confident hope about this interview with Lydgate, never heeding what was said of his personal reserve; never heeding that she was a very young woman. Nothing could have seemed more irrelevant to Dorothea than insistence on her youth and sex when she was moved to show her human fellowship.", "사랑은 신성한 인간의 형상이요,\n평화는 인간의 옷차림이니."]], [["As she sat waiting in the library, she could do nothing but live through again all the past scenes which had brought Lydgate into her memories.", "―윌리엄 블레이크, 『순수의 노래』\n며칠 후 리드게이트는 도러시아의 부름을 받아 로윅 저택으로 말을 달리고 있었다. 그 부름은 뜻밖의 일이 아니었으니, 불스트로드 씨의 편지가 먼저 도착한 뒤에 이어진 것이었기 때문이다."], ["They all owed their significance to her marriage and its troubles—but no; there were two occasions in which the image of Lydgate had come painfully in connection with his wife and some one else.", "그 편지에서 그는 미들마치를 떠나기 위한 준비를 다시 진행하고 있으며, 병원에 관해 이전에 주고받은 이야기를 리드게이트에게 상기시켜야겠다고, 그리고 자신은 여전히 그 취지를 고수한다고 밝혔다. 더 나아가기 전에 캐소본 부인과 그 문제를 다시 의논하는 것이 자신의 의무였는데, 부인은 전과 마찬가지로 그 문제를 리드게이트와 논의하고 싶어 한다는 것이었다."], ["The pain had been allayed for Dorothea, but it had left in her an awakened conjecture as to what Lydgate’s marriage might be to him, a susceptibility to the slightest hint about Mrs.", "\"당신의 견해가 어쩌면 다소 달라졌을지도 모르겠습니다.\" 불스트로드 씨는 이렇게 썼다."], ["Lydgate. These thoughts were like a drama to her, and made her eyes bright, and gave an attitude of suspense to her whole frame, though she was only looking out from the brown library on to the turf and the bright green buds which stood in relief against the dark evergreens.", "\"하지만 그렇다 하더라도 그 견해를 부인께 말씀드리는 것이 바람직할 것입니다.\""]], [["When Lydgate came in, she was almost shocked at the change in his face, which was strikingly perceptible to her who had not seen him for two months. It was not the change of emaciation, but that effect which even young faces will very soon show from the persistent presence of resentment and despondency. Her cordial look, when she put out her hand to him, softened his expression, but only with melancholy.", "도러시아는 간절한 관심을 품고 그가 오기를 기다렸다. 남성 조언자들의 뜻을 존중하여 제임스 경이 \"이 불스트로드 일에 끼어드는 것\"이라 부른 행동은 삼갔지만, 리드게이트가 처한 곤경은 끊임없이 그녀의 마음에 걸렸다. 그리하여 불스트로드가 병원 문제로 다시 그녀에게 부탁을 해 왔을 때, 그녀는 그동안 서두르지 못하도록 막혀 있던 기회가 마침내 찾아왔다고 느꼈다. 호화로운 집에서 자기 소유의 큰 나무들이 드리운 가지 아래를 거닐 때면, 그녀의 생각은 다른 사람들의 처지로 뻗어 나갔고 그녀의 감정은 갇혀 있었다. 자신의 손이 닿는 곳에 어떤 능동적인 선행이 있다는 생각이 \"하나의 열정처럼 그녀를 사로잡았고\", 다른 사람의 절박함이 한번 또렷한 형상으로 그녀에게 다가오자, 그것은 도움을 베풀고자 하는 갈망으로 그녀의 욕망을 가득 채워 자기 자신의 안락을 무미건조하게 만들었다. 그녀는 리드게이트와의 이 면담에 대해 확신에 찬 희망으로 가득 차 있었으니, 그의 개인적인 과묵함에 관한 소문 따위는 조금도 개의치 않았고, 자신이 매우 젊은 여자라는 사실도 전혀 마음에 두지 않았다. 인간적 동료애를 보이고자 마음이 움직였을 때, 그녀의 젊음과 성별을 들먹이는 것만큼 도러시아에게 무관해 보이는 일은 없었을 것이다."]], [["“I have wished very much to see you for a long while, Mr. Lydgate,”", "서재에 앉아 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 리드게이트를 기억 속에 떠올리게 한 지난 모든 장면들을 다시 살아 보는 것밖에 할 수 없었다."], ["said Dorothea when they were seated opposite each other; “but I put off asking you to come until Mr.", "그 장면들은 모두 자신의 결혼과 그로 인한 고난에서 의미를 얻은 것이었다―아니, 그렇지 않았다."], ["Bulstrode applied to me again about the Hospital.", "리드게이트의 모습이 그의 아내와 또 다른 누군가와 연관되어 고통스럽게 떠오른 경우가 두 차례 있었다."], ["I know that the advantage of keeping the management of it separate from that of the Infirmary depends on you, or, at least, on the good which you are encouraged to hope for from having it under your control.", "그 고통은 도러시아에게 누그러졌지만, 리드게이트의 결혼이 그에게 어떤 것일까 하는 일깨워진 추측을, 리드게이트 부인에 관한 아주 사소한 암시에도 예민해지는 감수성을 그녀 안에 남겨 두었다."], ["And I am sure you will not refuse to tell me exactly what you think.”", "이런 생각들은 그녀에게 하나의 연극과도 같아서 두 눈을 빛나게 했고, 비록 갈색 서재에서 잔디밭과, 짙은 상록수를 배경으로 도드라진 밝은 초록빛 새순을 내다보고 있을 뿐이었지만, 그녀의 온몸에 긴장된 기대의 자세를 부여했다."]], [["“You want to decide whether you should give a generous support to the Hospital,” said Lydgate.", "리드게이트가 들어왔을 때, 그녀는 그의 얼굴이 변한 모습에 거의 충격을 받았는데, 두 달 동안 그를 보지 못한 그녀에게는 그 변화가 유난히 또렷이 느껴졌다."], ["“I cannot conscientiously advise you to do it in dependence on any activity of mine. I may be obliged to leave the town.”", "그것은 수척해진 변화가 아니라, 젊은 얼굴조차도 분노와 낙담이 끈질기게 자리 잡으면 머지않아 드러내고 마는 그런 변화였다."], ["He spoke curtly, feeling the ache of despair as to his being able to carry out any purpose that Rosamond had set her mind against.", "그녀가 손을 내밀며 보낸 다정한 눈빛은 그의 표정을 누그러뜨렸으나, 그 누그러짐은 오직 우수를 띠고 있었다."]], [["“Not because there is no one to believe in you?”", "\"오래전부터 당신을 정말 뵙고 싶었어요, 리드게이트 선생님.\" 서로 마주 보고 앉았을 때 도러시아가 말했다."], ["said Dorothea, pouring out her words in clearness from a full heart.", "\"하지만 불스트로드 씨가 병원 문제로 다시 제게 부탁할 때까지 와 주십사 청하는 것을 미루었어요."], ["“I know the unhappy mistakes about you. I knew them from the first moment to be mistakes.", "병원 운영을 진료소 운영과 분리해 두는 것의 이점이 선생님께 달려 있다는 것을, 적어도 선생님이 그것을 직접 관할함으로써 기대해도 좋다고 여기시는 그 좋은 결과에 달려 있다는 것을 저는 알아요."], ["You have never done anything vile. You would not do anything dishonorable.”", "그러니 선생님 생각을 정확히 말씀해 주시기를 거절하지 않으시리라 믿어요.\""]], [["It was the first assurance of belief in him that had fallen on Lydgate’s ears.", "\"부인께서는 병원에 후한 지원을 해야 할지 말지를 결정하고 싶으신 거지요.\""], ["He drew a deep breath, and said, “Thank you.”", "리드게이트가 말했다."], ["He could say no more: it was something very new and strange in his life that these few words of trust from a woman should be so much to him.", "\"양심상 저는 제 어떤 활동을 믿고 그렇게 하시라고 권할 수가 없습니다. 저는 어쩌면 이 도시를 떠나야 할지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“I beseech you to tell me how everything was,” said Dorothea, fearlessly. “I am sure that the truth would clear you.”", "그는 로저먼드가 반대하기로 마음먹은 어떤 목적도 자신이 이루어 낼 수 없으리라는 절망의 아픔을 느끼며 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["Lydgate started up from his chair and went towards the window, forgetting where he was. He had so often gone over in his mind the possibility of explaining everything without aggravating appearances that would tell, perhaps unfairly, against Bulstrode, and had so often decided against it—he had so often said to himself that his assertions would not change people’s impressions—that Dorothea’s words sounded like a temptation to do something which in his soberness he had pronounced to be unreasonable.", "\"선생님을 믿어 주는 사람이 아무도 없어서 그런 건 아니지요?\" 도러시아가 가득 찬 가슴에서 명료하게 말을 쏟아내며 물었다. \"선생님에 관한 그 불행한 오해들을 저는 알아요. 처음 순간부터 그것이 오해라는 것을 알았어요. 선생님은 결코 비열한 짓을 하지 않으셨어요. 명예롭지 못한 일은 하실 분이 아니지요.\" 그것은 리드게이트의 귀에 떨어진, 그를 믿어 준다는 첫 번째 확언이었다. 그는 깊은 숨을 들이쉬고 말했다. \"고맙습니다.\" 그는 더는 말할 수 없었다. 한 여자에게서 나온 이 몇 마디 신뢰의 말이 자신에게 이토록 큰 의미가 된다는 것은 그의 삶에서 무척 새롭고 낯선 일이었다."]], [["“Tell me, pray,” said Dorothea, with simple earnestness; “then we can consult together.", "\"모든 일이 어떻게 된 것인지 제게 말씀해 주시기를 간청해요.\""], ["It is wicked to let people think evil of any one falsely, when it can be hindered.”", "도러시아가 두려움 없이 말했다. \"진실이 선생님의 결백을 밝혀 주리라 확신해요.\""]], [["Lydgate turned, remembering where he was, and saw Dorothea’s face looking up at him with a sweet trustful gravity.", "리드게이트는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 자신이 어디에 있는지도 잊은 채 창문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["The presence of a noble nature, generous in its wishes, ardent in its charity, changes the lights for us: we begin to see things again in their larger, quieter masses, and to believe that we too can be seen and judged in the wholeness of our character.", "그는 어쩌면 부당하게도 불스트로드에게 불리하게 작용할 외관을 악화시키지 않으면서 모든 것을 설명할 수 있는 가능성을 마음속으로 여러 번 따져 보았고, 그때마다 그렇게 하지 않기로 결정했다. 자신이 주장한다 한들 사람들의 인상을 바꾸지는 못하리라고 여러 번 스스로에게 되뇌었기에, 도러시아의 말은 마치 냉정한 상태에서 자신이 비이성적이라고 단정해 버린 일을 하라는 유혹처럼 들렸다."], ["That influence was beginning to act on Lydgate, who had for many days been seeing all life as one who is dragged and struggling amid the throng.", "\"제발 말씀해 주세요.\" 도러시아가 꾸밈없는 진지함으로 말했다."], ["He sat down again, and felt that he was recovering his old self in the consciousness that he was with one who believed in it.", "\"그러면 함께 의논할 수 있어요. 막을 수 있는데도 사람들이 누군가를 거짓으로 나쁘게 생각하도록 내버려 두는 것은 사악한 일이에요.\""]], [["“I don’t want,” he said, “to bear hard on Bulstrode, who has lent me money of which I was in need—though I would rather have gone without it now.", "리드게이트는 자신이 어디에 있는지를 떠올리고 몸을 돌렸다가, 다정하고 신뢰에 찬 진지함으로 자신을 올려다보는 도러시아의 얼굴을 보았다."], ["He is hunted down and miserable, and has only a poor thread of life in him. But I should like to tell you everything.", "소망에 너그럽고 자애에 열렬한 고결한 천성이 곁에 있으면 우리에게 비치는 빛이 달라진다."], ["It will be a comfort to me to speak where belief has gone beforehand, and where I shall not seem to be offering assertions of my own honesty.", "우리는 다시 사물을 더 크고 고요한 덩어리로 보기 시작하며, 우리 또한 우리 인격 전체로서 보이고 판단받을 수 있다고 믿게 된다. 그 영향력이 리드게이트에게 작용하기 시작했으니, 그는 여러 날 동안 군중 속에서 떠밀리며 발버둥 치는 사람의 눈으로 온 삶을 바라보아 왔던 터였다."], ["You will feel what is fair to another, as you feel what is fair to me.”", "그는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 자신을 믿어 주는 사람과 함께 있다는 의식 속에서 옛날의 자신을 되찾고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["“Do trust me,”", "\"저는,\""], ["said Dorothea; “I will not repeat anything without your leave.", "그가 말했다. \"불스트로드를 가혹하게 몰아붙이고 싶지 않습니다."], ["But at the very least, I could say that you have made all the circumstances clear to me, and that I know you are not in any way guilty.", "그는 제게 필요했던 돈을 빌려준 사람이니까요―물론 이제 와서는 차라리 그 돈 없이 지냈더라면 싶지만요."], ["Mr. Farebrother would believe me, and my uncle, and Sir James Chettam.", "그는 쫓기는 신세에 비참하고, 몸에는 가느다란 생명의 실 한 가닥밖에 남지 않았습니다."], ["Nay, there are persons in Middlemarch to whom I could go; although they don’t know much of me, they would believe me.", "하지만 부인께는 모든 것을 말씀드리고 싶습니다."], ["They would know that I could have no other motive than truth and justice. I would take any pains to clear you.", "믿음이 미리 자리 잡고 있는 곳에서, 제 정직함을 스스로 주장하는 것처럼 보이지 않아도 되는 곳에서 이야기하는 것은 제게 위안이 될 겁니다."], ["I have very little to do. There is nothing better that I can do in the world.”", "부인께서는 제게 무엇이 공정한지를 느끼시듯, 다른 사람에게도 무엇이 공정한지를 느끼실 테니까요.\""]], [["Dorothea’s voice, as she made this childlike picture of what she would do, might have been almost taken as a proof that she could do it effectively. The searching tenderness of her woman’s tones seemed made for a defence against ready accusers. Lydgate did not stay to think that she was Quixotic: he gave himself up, for the first time in his life, to the exquisite sense of leaning entirely on a generous sympathy, without any check of proud reserve. And he told her everything, from the time when, under the pressure of his difficulties, he unwillingly made his first application to Bulstrode; gradually, in the relief of speaking, getting into a more thorough utterance of what had gone on in his mind—entering fully into the fact that his treatment of the patient was opposed to the dominant practice, into his doubts at the last, his ideal of medical duty, and his uneasy consciousness that the acceptance of the money had made some difference in his private inclination and professional behavior, though not in his fulfilment of any publicly recognized obligation.", "\"부디 저를 믿어 주세요.\" 도러시아가 말했다. \"선생님의 허락 없이는 어떤 것도 옮기지 않겠어요. 하지만 적어도 선생님이 제게 모든 상황을 분명히 밝혀 주셨고, 선생님이 어떤 식으로도 죄가 없다는 것을 제가 안다는 말은 할 수 있어요. 페어브러더 씨도 제 말을 믿어 줄 거예요. 제 삼촌도, 제임스 체텀 경도요. 아니, 미들마치에는 제가 찾아갈 수 있는 사람들이 있어요. 그들이 저를 잘 알지는 못해도 제 말을 믿어 줄 거예요. 제게 진실과 정의 외에 다른 동기가 있을 리 없다는 것을 그들도 알 테니까요. 선생님의 결백을 밝히기 위해서라면 어떤 수고도 마다하지 않겠어요. 저는 할 일이 거의 없어요. 이 세상에서 제가 할 수 있는 일 가운데 이보다 더 나은 것은 없어요.\" 도러시아가 자신이 하겠다는 이 아이 같은 그림을 그릴 때 그녀의 목소리는, 그녀가 그 일을 실제로 효과적으로 해낼 수 있으리라는 증거로 받아들여질 만했다. 파고드는 듯한 그녀의 여성스러운 음색의 부드러움은 성급한 고발자들에 맞서는 방패로 만들어진 듯했다. 리드게이트는 그녀가 돈키호테 같다고 생각하느라 머뭇거리지 않았다. 그는 평생 처음으로 자존심 어린 과묵함의 제어도 없이, 너그러운 공감에 온전히 기대는 그 더없이 절묘한 느낌에 자신을 내맡겼다. 그리하여 그는 그녀에게 모든 것을 이야기했다. 곤경에 짓눌려 마지못해 처음 불스트로드에게 부탁했던 때부터 시작하여, 이야기를 털어놓는 가운데 점차 마음의 안도를 얻으며 자기 마음속에서 일어났던 일들을 더 철저하게 말하게 되었다. 환자를 치료한 자신의 방식이 지배적인 관행에 어긋났다는 사실, 마지막 순간에 품었던 의심, 의술의 의무에 대한 자신의 이상, 그리고 비록 공적으로 인정된 어떤 의무를 이행하는 데는 차이가 없었지만 그 돈을 받은 것이 자신의 사적인 성향과 직업적 처신에 어떤 차이를 만들어 냈다는 불편한 자각까지 낱낱이 들어가서 이야기했다."]], [["“It has come to my knowledge since,”", "\"그 후에 제가 알게 된 사실인데요,\""], ["he added, “that Hawley sent some one to examine the housekeeper at Stone Court, and she said that she gave the patient all the opium in the phial I left, as well as a good deal of brandy.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"홀리가 스톤 코트의 가정부를 캐묻도록 누군가를 보냈고, 그녀는 제가 남기고 온 약병에 든 아편을 전부, 그리고 상당량의 브랜디까지 환자에게 주었다고 말했다더군요."], ["But that would not have been opposed to ordinary prescriptions, even of first-rate men.", "하지만 그것은 일류 의사들의 평범한 처방에도 어긋나는 일은 아니었을 겁니다."], ["The suspicions against me had no hold there: they are grounded on the knowledge that I took money, that Bulstrode had strong motives for wishing the man to die, and that he gave me the money as a bribe to concur in some malpractices or other against the patient—that in any case I accepted a bribe to hold my tongue.", "저를 향한 의심은 거기서는 근거를 잃습니다. 그 의심들은 제가 돈을 받았다는 것, 불스트로드에게는 그 사람이 죽기를 바랄 강력한 동기가 있었다는 것, 그가 환자에게 행해진 이런저런 부정한 처치에 가담하라고 제게 뇌물로 그 돈을 주었다는 것―어쨌든 제가 입을 다물도록 뇌물을 받았다는 것에 근거를 두고 있습니다."], ["They are just the suspicions that cling the most obstinately, because they lie in people’s inclination and can never be disproved.", "그것들은 가장 끈질기게 들러붙는 종류의 의심인데, 사람들의 성향 속에 자리 잡고 있어서 결코 반증할 수 없기 때문이지요. 제 지시가 어쩌다 어겨지게 되었는지는 제가 답을 알지 못하는 문제입니다."], ["How my orders came to be disobeyed is a question to which I don’t know the answer.", "불스트로드에게 어떤 범죄적 의도가 없었을 가능성도 여전히 있습니다."], ["It is still possible that Bulstrode was innocent of any criminal intention—even possible that he had nothing to do with the disobedience, and merely abstained from mentioning it.", "그가 그 불복종과 아무 관련이 없었고 단지 그것을 언급하지 않고 넘어갔을 가능성조차 있지요. 하지만 그 모든 것은 대중의 믿음과는 아무 상관이 없습니다."], ["But all that has nothing to do with the public belief.", "이것은 한 사람이 그 인격을 근거로 단죄당하는 그런 경우 가운데 하나입니다."], ["It is one of those cases on which a man is condemned on the ground of his character—it is believed that he has committed a crime in some undefined way, because he had the motive for doing it; and Bulstrode’s character has enveloped me, because I took his money.", "어떤 모호한 방식으로 죄를 저질렀으리라고 사람들이 믿는데, 그에게 그럴 동기가 있었기 때문이지요. 그리고 불스트로드의 인격이 저를 감싸 버린 것은, 제가 그의 돈을 받았기 때문입니다."], ["I am simply blighted—like a damaged ear of corn—the business is done and can’t be undone.”", "저는 그냥 시들어 버렸습니다―병든 옥수수 이삭처럼요―이미 끝난 일이고 되돌릴 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, it is hard!” said Dorothea.", "\"오, 정말 가혹한 일이군요!\""], ["“I understand the difficulty there is in your vindicating yourself.", "도러시아가 말했다. \"선생님이 스스로를 변호하기가 얼마나 어려운지 이해해요."], ["And that all this should have come to you who had meant to lead a higher life than the common, and to find out better ways—I cannot bear to rest in this as unchangeable. I know you meant that.", "그리고 이 모든 일이, 평범한 삶보다 더 높은 삶을 살고 더 나은 길을 찾아내려 했던 선생님께 닥쳤다는 것이―저는 이것을 바꿀 수 없는 일로 받아들이고만 있을 수가 없어요."], ["I remember what you said to me when you first spoke to me about the hospital.", "선생님이 그런 뜻을 품었다는 것을 알아요. 처음 병원 이야기를 제게 하셨을 때 하신 말씀을 기억해요."], ["There is no sorrow I have thought more about than that—to love what is great, and try to reach it, and yet to fail.”", "위대한 것을 사랑하고, 거기에 다다르려 애쓰지만 끝내 실패하는 것―이보다 더 깊이 생각해 본 슬픔은 제게 없어요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, feeling that here he had found room for the full meaning of his grief.", "리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 여기서 비로소 자기 슬픔의 온전한 의미를 담을 자리를 찾은 듯 느꼈다."], ["“I had some ambition.", "\"제게는 약간의 야망이 있었습니다."], ["I meant everything to be different with me.", "저는 모든 것을 남들과 다르게 하고자 했어요."], ["I thought I had more strength and mastery.", "제게는 더 큰 힘과 자제력이 있다고 생각했지요."], ["But the most terrible obstacles are such as nobody can see except oneself.”", "하지만 가장 끔찍한 장애물은 자기 자신 말고는 아무도 볼 수 없는 그런 것입니다.\""]], [["“Suppose,”", "\"가령,\" 도러시아가 사색에 잠긴 듯 말했다."], ["said Dorothea, meditatively,—“suppose we kept on the Hospital according to the present plan, and you stayed here though only with the friendship and support of a few, the evil feeling towards you would gradually die out; there would come opportunities in which people would be forced to acknowledge that they had been unjust to you, because they would see that your purposes were pure.", "\"가령 우리가 지금 계획대로 병원을 계속 유지하고, 선생님이 비록 몇몇 사람의 우정과 지지뿐일지라도 이곳에 머무신다면, 선생님을 향한 나쁜 감정은 차차 사라질 거예요. 사람들이 자기들이 선생님께 부당하게 굴었음을 인정할 수밖에 없는 기회가 찾아올 거예요."], ["You may still win a great fame like the Louis and Laennec I have heard you speak of, and we shall all be proud of you,”", "선생님의 목적이 순수했음을 그들도 알게 될 테니까요. 선생님이 말씀하시던 그 루이나 라에네크처럼 여전히 큰 명성을 얻으실 수 있어요."], ["she ended, with a smile.", "그러면 우리 모두 선생님을 자랑스러워할 거예요.\" 그녀는 미소 지으며 말을 맺었다."]], [["“That might do if I had my old trust in myself,”", "\"제가 예전처럼 저 자신에 대한 믿음이 있다면 그럴 수도 있겠지요.\""], ["said Lydgate, mournfully.", "리드게이트가 서글프게 말했다."], ["“Nothing galls me more than the notion of turning round and running away before this slander, leaving it unchecked behind me.", "\"이 중상모략 앞에서 등을 돌리고 도망쳐, 그것을 제지하지 않은 채 뒤에 남겨 둔다는 생각만큼 저를 괴롭히는 것은 없습니다."], ["Still, I can’t ask any one to put a great deal of money into a plan which depends on me.”", "그렇지만 저는 누구에게도 저 자신에게 달린 계획에 큰돈을 쏟으라고 청할 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“It would be quite worth my while,”", "\"그건 제가 충분히 해 볼 만한 일이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea, simply.", "도러시아가 꾸밈없이 말했다."], ["“Only think.", "\"한번 생각해 보세요."], ["I am very uncomfortable with my money, because they tell me I have too little for any great scheme of the sort I like best, and yet I have too much.", "저는 제 돈 때문에 무척 곤란해요. 사람들은 제가 가장 좋아하는 그런 종류의 큰 계획을 펼치기에는 돈이 너무 적다고 하는데, 그렇다고 또 너무 많기도 하거든요."], ["I don’t know what to do.", "어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["I have seven hundred a-year of my own fortune, and nineteen hundred a-year that Mr. Casaubon left me, and between three and four thousand of ready money in the bank.", "제 몫의 재산에서 나오는 연 칠백 파운드와, 캐소본 씨가 제게 남긴 연 천구백 파운드, 그리고 은행에 든 현금 삼사천 파운드가 있어요."], ["I wished to raise money and pay it off gradually out of my income which I don’t want, to buy land with and found a village which should be a school of industry; but Sir James and my uncle have convinced me that the risk would be too great.", "저는 돈을 마련해서, 제게 필요하지도 않은 수입으로 차차 갚아 나가며 땅을 사고, 근면의 학교가 될 마을을 세우고 싶었어요. 하지만 제임스 경과 삼촌은 위험이 너무 크다고 저를 설득했지요."], ["So you see that what I should most rejoice at would be to have something good to do with my money: I should like it to make other people’s lives better to them.", "그러니 아시겠지요, 제가 가장 기뻐할 일은 제 돈으로 무언가 좋은 일을 하는 것이에요. 그 돈으로 다른 사람들의 삶이 더 나아졌으면 좋겠어요."], ["It makes me very uneasy—coming all to me who don’t want it.”", "그게 필요하지도 않은 제게 다 굴러들어 온다는 게 저를 무척 불편하게 해요.\""]], [["A smile broke through the gloom of Lydgate’s face.", "리드게이트의 얼굴을 덮고 있던 음울함 사이로 미소가 비어져 나왔다."], ["The childlike grave-eyed earnestness with which Dorothea said all this was irresistible—blent into an adorable whole with her ready understanding of high experience.", "도러시아가 이 모든 것을 말할 때의 그 아이같이 진지한 눈빛은 거역할 수 없는 것이었다―고매한 경험에 대한 그녀의 명민한 이해와 어우러져 사랑스러운 하나의 전체를 이루고 있었다."], ["(Of lower experience such as plays a great part in the world, poor Mrs. Casaubon had a very blurred shortsighted knowledge, little helped by her imagination.) But she took the smile as encouragement of her plan.", "(세상에서 큰 몫을 차지하는 그런 비속한 경험에 대해서는, 가엾은 캐소본 부인은 무척 흐릿하고 근시안적인 앎밖에 없었고 상상력의 도움도 거의 받지 못했다.) 하지만 그녀는 그 미소를 자기 계획에 대한 격려로 받아들였다."]], [["“I think you see now that you spoke too scrupulously,”", "\"이제 선생님도 너무 지나치게 신중하게 말씀하셨다는 것을 아시겠지요.\""], ["she said, in a tone of persuasion.", "그녀가 설득하는 어조로 말했다."], ["“The hospital would be one good; and making your life quite whole and well again would be another.”", "\"병원도 하나의 좋은 일이고, 선생님의 삶을 다시 온전하고 건강하게 만드는 것도 또 하나의 좋은 일이에요.\""]], [["Lydgate’s smile had died away.", "리드게이트의 미소는 사라져 버렸다."], ["“You have the goodness as well as the money to do all that; if it could be done,”", "\"부인께는 그 모든 것을 하실 만한 선의도, 돈도 있으십니다. 그것이 가능하다면 말이지요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But—”", "\"하지만―\""]], [["He hesitated a little while, looking vaguely towards the window; and she sat in silent expectation.", "그는 잠시 머뭇거리며 창문 쪽을 막연히 바라보았고, 그녀는 말없이 기대하며 앉아 있었다."], ["At last he turned towards her and said impetuously—", "마침내 그는 그녀를 향해 돌아서더니 격하게 말했다."]], [["“Why should I not tell you?—you know what sort of bond marriage is.", "\"제가 부인께 말씀드리지 못할 까닭이 무엇이겠습니까?―부인께서는 결혼이라는 것이 어떤 속박인지 아시지요."], ["You will understand everything.”", "모든 것을 이해하실 겁니다.\""]], [["Dorothea felt her heart beginning to beat faster.", "도러시아는 가슴이 더 빠르게 뛰기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Had he that sorrow too?", "이분에게도 그런 슬픔이 있었던 것일까?"], ["But she feared to say any word, and he went on immediately.", "하지만 그녀는 한마디라도 꺼내기가 두려웠고, 그는 곧바로 말을 이었다."]], [["“It is impossible for me now to do anything—to take any step without considering my wife’s happiness.", "\"이제 저는 아내의 행복을 고려하지 않고서는 아무것도 할 수 없습니다―어떤 발걸음도 내디딜 수가 없어요."], ["The thing that I might like to do if I were alone, is become impossible to me.", "혼자였더라면 하고 싶었을 법한 일이, 제게는 불가능한 일이 되고 맙니다."], ["I can’t see her miserable.", "저는 그녀가 비참해하는 모습을 볼 수가 없어요."], ["She married me without knowing what she was going into, and it might have been better for her if she had not married me.”", "그녀는 자기가 무슨 길로 들어서는지도 모른 채 저와 결혼했고, 어쩌면 저와 결혼하지 않았더라면 그녀에게 더 나았을지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["“I know, I know—you could not give her pain, if you were not obliged to do it,”", "\"알아요, 알아요―어쩔 수 없는 경우가 아니라면 선생님은 그녀에게 고통을 주지 못하실 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea, with keen memory of her own life.", "자신의 삶을 절절히 떠올리며 도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“And she has set her mind against staying.", "\"그리고 그녀는 머무르지 않겠다고 마음을 굳혔습니다."], ["She wishes to go.", "떠나기를 바라지요."], ["The troubles she has had here have wearied her,”", "여기서 겪은 시련들이 그녀를 지치게 했어요.\""], ["said Lydgate, breaking off again, lest he should say too much.", "리드게이트는 너무 많은 말을 할까 봐 또 말을 끊었다."]], [["“But when she saw the good that might come of staying—”", "\"하지만 머무름으로써 생길 수 있는 좋은 결과를 그녀가 본다면―\""], ["said Dorothea, remonstrantly, looking at Lydgate as if he had forgotten the reasons which had just been considered.", "도러시아가 항변하듯 말하며, 마치 그가 방금 함께 고려했던 이유들을 잊어버리기라도 한 것처럼 리드게이트를 바라보았다."], ["He did not speak immediately.", "그는 곧바로 말하지 않았다."]], [["“She would not see it,”", "\"그녀는 그것을 보려 하지 않을 겁니다.\""], ["he said at last, curtly, feeling at first that this statement must do without explanation.", "마침내 그가 퉁명스럽게 말했는데, 처음에는 이 말이 설명 없이도 통하리라 느꼈다."], ["“And, indeed, I have lost all spirit about carrying on my life here.”", "\"그리고 사실, 저 자신도 이곳에서 제 삶을 계속 꾸려 갈 의욕을 모두 잃었습니다.\""], ["He paused a moment and then, following the impulse to let Dorothea see deeper into the difficulty of his life, he said, “The fact is, this trouble has come upon her confusedly.", "그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가, 도러시아가 자기 삶의 어려움을 더 깊이 들여다보게 하고 싶은 충동에 이끌려 말했다. \"사실은, 이 시련이 그녀에게 혼란스러운 형태로 닥쳤습니다."], ["We have not been able to speak to each other about it.", "우리는 그것에 대해 서로 이야기를 나누지 못했어요."], ["I am not sure what is in her mind about it: she may fear that I have really done something base.", "그것에 관해 그녀의 마음속에 무엇이 들어 있는지 저는 확신할 수 없습니다. 어쩌면 제가 정말로 무언가 비열한 짓을 저질렀을까 봐 두려워하는지도 모르지요."], ["It is my fault; I ought to be more open.", "제 잘못입니다. 좀 더 터놓았어야 했어요."], ["But I have been suffering cruelly.”", "하지만 저는 잔인하리만치 괴로워하고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“May I go and see her?”", "\"제가 가서 그녀를 만나 봐도 될까요?\""], ["said Dorothea, eagerly.", "도러시아가 간절하게 말했다."], ["“Would she accept my sympathy?", "\"그녀가 제 공감을 받아 줄까요?"], ["I would tell her that you have not been blamable before any one’s judgment but your own.", "제가 그녀에게, 선생님은 선생님 자신의 판단 외에 그 누구의 판단으로도 비난받을 일이 없다고 말해 주겠어요."], ["I would tell her that you shall be cleared in every fair mind.", "공정한 마음을 가진 사람이라면 누구에게나 선생님의 결백이 밝혀질 거라고 말해 주겠어요."], ["I would cheer her heart.", "그녀의 마음을 북돋아 주겠어요."], ["Will you ask her if I may go to see her?", "제가 그녀를 만나러 가도 되는지 그녀에게 물어봐 주시겠어요?"], ["I did see her once.”", "전에 한 번 만난 적이 있어요.\""]], [["“I am sure you may,”", "\"물론 되고말고요.\""], ["said Lydgate, seizing the proposition with some hope.", "리드게이트가 약간의 희망을 품고 그 제안을 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["“She would feel honored—cheered, I think, by the proof that you at least have some respect for me.", "\"그녀는 영광스럽게 여길 겁니다―적어도 부인께서 저를 어느 정도 존중하신다는 증거에 마음이 북돋아질 것 같습니다."], ["I will not speak to her about your coming—that she may not connect it with my wishes at all.", "부인이 오신다는 것을 제가 그녀에게 말하지는 않겠습니다―그래야 그녀가 그것을 제 바람과 전혀 연결 짓지 않을 테니까요."], ["I know very well that I ought not to have left anything to be told her by others, but—”", "다른 사람을 통해 그녀에게 전해질 일을 남겨 두지 말았어야 한다는 것을 저도 잘 압니다만, 그래도―\""]], [["He broke off, and there was a moment’s silence.", "그는 말을 멈추었고, 잠시 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Dorothea refrained from saying what was in her mind—how well she knew that there might be invisible barriers to speech between husband and wife.", "도러시아는 자기 마음속에 있던 말을―남편과 아내 사이에 말로는 넘을 수 없는 보이지 않는 장벽이 있을 수 있다는 것을 자신이 얼마나 잘 아는지를 입 밖에 내지 않았다."], ["This was a point on which even sympathy might make a wound.", "이것은 공감조차도 상처를 줄 수 있는 지점이었다."], ["She returned to the more outward aspect of Lydgate’s position, saying cheerfully—", "그녀는 리드게이트가 처한 상황의 좀 더 겉으로 드러난 측면으로 화제를 돌려, 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["“And if Mrs. Lydgate knew that there were friends who would believe in you and support you, she might then be glad that you should stay in your place and recover your hopes—and do what you meant to do.", "\"그리고 만약 리드게이트 부인이 선생님을 믿고 지지해 줄 친구들이 있다는 것을 안다면, 선생님이 자리에 머물러 희망을 되찾고―뜻하셨던 일을 하시는 것을 그분도 기뻐할지 몰라요."], ["Perhaps then you would see that it was right to agree with what I proposed about your continuing at the Hospital.", "그러면 어쩌면 선생님도, 병원에 계속 머무르시는 것에 관한 제 제안에 동의하는 것이 옳다고 보시게 될 거예요."], ["Surely you would, if you still have faith in it as a means of making your knowledge useful?”", "선생님이 그것을 여전히 선생님의 지식을 유용하게 쓸 수단으로 믿고 계신다면, 분명 그러시겠지요?\""]], [["Lydgate did not answer, and she saw that he was debating with himself.", "리드게이트는 대답하지 않았고, 그녀는 그가 스스로와 씨름하고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“You need not decide immediately,”", "\"당장 결정하실 필요는 없어요.\""], ["she said, gently.", "그녀가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“A few days hence it will be early enough for me to send my answer to Mr. Bulstrode.”", "\"지금부터 며칠 후에 제 답을 불스트로드 씨에게 보내도 충분히 이를 거예요.\""]], [["Lydgate still waited, but at last turned to speak in his most decisive tones.", "리드게이트는 여전히 망설였으나, 마침내 더없이 단호한 어조로 말하려 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“No; I prefer that there should be no interval left for wavering.", "\"아닙니다. 흔들릴 여지를 남겨 두지 않는 편이 좋겠습니다."], ["I am no longer sure enough of myself—I mean of what it would be possible for me to do under the changed circumstances of my life.", "저는 더 이상 저 자신을 충분히 확신하지 못합니다―제 삶이 처한 달라진 상황 아래서 제가 무엇을 할 수 있을지 말입니다."], ["It would be dishonorable to let others engage themselves to anything serious in dependence on me.", "다른 사람들이 저를 믿고 무언가 중대한 일에 발을 들이게 하는 것은 명예롭지 못한 일일 겁니다."], ["I might be obliged to go away after all; I see little chance of anything else.", "저는 결국 떠나야 할지도 모르고요. 그 외에 다른 가능성은 거의 보이지 않습니다."], ["The whole thing is too problematic; I cannot consent to be the cause of your goodness being wasted.", "모든 것이 너무 불확실합니다. 부인의 선의가 헛되이 쓰이는 원인이 되는 것에 저는 동의할 수 없습니다."], ["No—let the new Hospital be joined with the old Infirmary, and everything go on as it might have done if I had never come.", "아닙니다―새 병원을 옛 진료소와 합치고, 마치 제가 애초에 오지 않았더라면 그랬을 법한 대로 모든 것이 흘러가게 두십시오."], ["I have kept a valuable register since I have been there; I shall send it to a man who will make use of it,”", "그곳에 있는 동안 저는 귀중한 진료 기록을 작성해 두었습니다. 그것을 활용할 사람에게 보내겠습니다.\""], ["he ended bitterly.", "그가 쓰라리게 말을 맺었다."], ["“I can think of nothing for a long while but getting an income.”", "\"한동안은 수입을 얻는 것 말고는 아무것도 생각할 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“It hurts me very much to hear you speak so hopelessly,”", "\"선생님이 그렇게 절망적으로 말씀하시는 것을 듣자니 무척 마음이 아파요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“It would be a happiness to your friends, who believe in your future, in your power to do great things, if you would let them save you from that.", "\"선생님의 미래를, 위대한 일을 해낼 선생님의 힘을 믿는 친구들에게는, 선생님이 그들로 하여금 선생님을 그 처지에서 구하게 해 주신다면 그것이 곧 행복일 거예요."], ["Think how much money I have; it would be like taking a burthen from me if you took some of it every year till you got free from this fettering want of income.", "제게 돈이 얼마나 많은지 생각해 보세요. 선생님이 이 옥죄는 수입 부족에서 벗어나실 때까지 해마다 그 돈을 조금씩 가져가신다면, 그것은 제게서 짐을 덜어 주는 것이나 마찬가지일 거예요."], ["Why should not people do these things?", "어째서 사람들이 이런 일을 하면 안 되는 걸까요?"], ["It is so difficult to make shares at all even.", "무엇이든 고르게 나누기란 정말 어려운 일이에요."], ["This is one way.”", "이것이 하나의 방법이에요.\""]], [["“God bless you, Mrs. Casaubon!”", "\"부인께 신의 축복이 있기를, 캐소본 부인!\""], ["said Lydgate, rising as if with the same impulse that made his words energetic, and resting his arm on the back of the great leather chair he had been sitting in.", "리드게이트가, 그의 말에 힘을 실어 준 것과 같은 충동에 이끌리듯 일어서서, 자신이 앉아 있던 커다란 가죽 의자의 등받이에 팔을 기대며 말했다."], ["“It is good that you should have such feelings.", "\"부인께서 그런 마음을 가지셨다는 것은 좋은 일입니다."], ["But I am not the man who ought to allow himself to benefit by them.", "하지만 저는 그 마음의 덕을 보도록 제 자신을 허락해서는 안 될 사람입니다."], ["I have not given guarantees enough.", "저는 충분한 보증을 내놓지 못했어요."], ["I must not at least sink into the degradation of being pensioned for work that I never achieved.", "적어도 저는, 제가 결코 이루지 못한 일에 대해 연금을 받는 그런 타락에 빠져들어서는 안 됩니다."], ["It is very clear to me that I must not count on anything else than getting away from Middlemarch as soon as I can manage it.", "제가 할 수 있는 한 빨리 미들마치를 떠나는 것 외에는 어떤 것도 기대해서는 안 된다는 것이 제게는 아주 분명합니다."], ["I should not be able for a long while, at the very best, to get an income here, and—and it is easier to make necessary changes in a new place.", "아무리 잘되어도 한동안은 이곳에서 수입을 얻을 수 없을 테고―그리고―낯선 곳에서 필요한 변화를 만들어 가는 것이 더 쉽지요. 저도 다른 사람들이 하는 대로 해야 합니다."], ["I must do as other men do, and think what will please the world and bring in money; look for a little opening in the London crowd, and push myself; set up in a watering-place, or go to some southern town where there are plenty of idle English, and get myself puffed,—that is the sort of shell I must creep into and try to keep my soul alive in.”", "세상의 비위를 맞추고 돈벌이가 될 일을 궁리하고, 런던의 인파 속에서 작은 틈새를 찾아 비집고 들어가야지요. 온천 휴양지에 개업하거나, 한가한 영국인들이 잔뜩 있는 어느 남부 도시로 가서 제 이름을 떠벌리게 만들어야 합니다―그것이 제가 기어들어 가 그 안에서 영혼을 겨우 살려 두려 애써야 할 그런 껍데기입니다.\""]], [["“Now that is not brave,”", "\"그건 용감하지 못한 일이에요.\""], ["said Dorothea,—“to give up the fight.”", "도러시아가 말했다. \"싸움을 포기하다니요.\""]], [["“No, it is not brave,”", "\"그렇지요, 용감하지 못한 일입니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, “but if a man is afraid of creeping paralysis?”", "리드게이트가 말했다. \"하지만 사람이 서서히 다가오는 마비를 두려워한다면 어쩌겠습니까?\""], ["Then, in another tone, “Yet you have made a great difference in my courage by believing in me.", "그러더니 다른 어조로 말했다. \"그래도 부인께서 저를 믿어 주신 덕분에 제 용기에 큰 변화가 생겼습니다."], ["Everything seems more bearable since I have talked to you; and if you can clear me in a few other minds, especially in Farebrother’s, I shall be deeply grateful.", "부인과 이야기를 나눈 뒤로 모든 것이 더 견딜 만하게 느껴집니다. 그리고 부인께서 몇몇 다른 사람들의 마음속에서, 특히 페어브러더의 마음속에서 제 결백을 밝혀 주신다면 저는 깊이 감사하겠습니다."], ["The point I wish you not to mention is the fact of disobedience to my orders.", "부인께서 언급하지 않으셨으면 하는 점은, 제 지시가 어겨졌다는 사실입니다."], ["That would soon get distorted.", "그것은 금세 왜곡될 테니까요."], ["After all, there is no evidence for me but people’s opinion of me beforehand.", "결국, 저를 위한 증거라고는 사람들이 미리부터 저에 대해 품고 있는 평판밖에 없습니다."], ["You can only repeat my own report of myself.”", "부인께서는 저 자신에 대한 제 보고를 그대로 옮기실 수 있을 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“Mr. Farebrother will believe—others will believe,”", "\"페어브러더 씨는 믿어 줄 거예요―다른 사람들도 믿어 줄 거예요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."], ["“I can say of you what will make it stupidity to suppose that you would be bribed to do a wickedness.”", "\"선생님이 사악한 짓을 저지르라고 매수당하리라 짐작하는 것이 어리석은 일이 되게 만들, 그런 말을 제가 선생님에 관해 할 수 있어요.\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠습니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, with something like a groan in his voice.", "리드게이트가 목소리에 신음 같은 것을 담아 말했다."], ["“I have not taken a bribe yet.", "\"저는 아직 뇌물을 받은 적은 없습니다."], ["But there is a pale shade of bribery which is sometimes called prosperity.", "하지만 때때로 번영이라 불리는, 뇌물의 희미한 그림자라는 것이 있지요."], ["You will do me another great kindness, then, and come to see my wife?”", "그러면 부인께서 제게 또 하나의 큰 친절을 베푸셔서, 제 아내를 보러 와 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, I will.", "\"네, 그러겠어요."], ["I remember how pretty she is,”", "그분이 얼마나 예쁜지 기억해요.\""], ["said Dorothea, into whose mind every impression about Rosamond had cut deep.", "도러시아가 말했다. 로저먼드에 관한 모든 인상은 그녀의 마음에 깊이 새겨져 있었다."], ["“I hope she will like me.”", "\"그분이 저를 좋아해 주면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["As Lydgate rode away, he thought, “This young creature has a heart large enough for the Virgin Mary.", "리드게이트는 말을 타고 떠나며 생각했다. \"이 젊은 사람은 성모 마리아만큼이나 큰 가슴을 지녔구나."], ["She evidently thinks nothing of her own future, and would pledge away half her income at once, as if she wanted nothing for herself but a chair to sit in from which she can look down with those clear eyes at the poor mortals who pray to her.", "분명 자기 자신의 미래는 조금도 생각하지 않고, 마치 자기에게는 의자 하나만 있으면 된다는 듯이―그 맑은 눈으로 자기에게 기도하는 가엾은 인간들을 내려다볼 수 있는 의자 하나만 있으면 된다는 듯이, 당장이라도 수입의 절반을 내어 줄 사람이야."], ["She seems to have what I never saw in any woman before—a fountain of friendship towards men—a man can make a friend of her.", "저 사람에게는 내가 여태껏 어떤 여자에게서도 본 적 없는 것이 있어―남자들을 향한 우정의 샘이 말이야―남자가 저 사람을 친구로 삼을 수 있어."], ["Casaubon must have raised some heroic hallucination in her.", "캐소본이 저 사람에게 어떤 영웅적인 환상을 불어넣었던 게 틀림없어."], ["I wonder if she could have any other sort of passion for a man?", "저 사람이 한 남자에게 다른 종류의 열정을 품을 수도 있었을까?"], ["Ladislaw?—there was certainly an unusual feeling between them.", "래디슬로 말인가?―확실히 두 사람 사이에는 예사롭지 않은 감정이 있었지."], ["And Casaubon must have had a notion of it.", "그리고 캐소본도 그것을 눈치챘을 게 분명해."], ["Well—her love might help a man more than her money.”", "흠―저 사람의 사랑은 돈보다 한 남자에게 더 큰 도움이 될지도 모르지.\""]], [["Dorothea on her side had immediately formed a plan of relieving Lydgate from his obligation to Bulstrode, which she felt sure was a part, though small, of the galling pressure he had to bear.", "도러시아는 도러시아대로, 리드게이트를 불스트로드에 대한 빚에서 벗어나게 해 줄 계획을 곧바로 세웠는데, 그 빚이 비록 작은 것이긴 해도 그가 견뎌야 하는 그 옥죄는 압박의 한 부분이라는 것을 그녀는 확신했다."], ["She sat down at once under the inspiration of their interview, and wrote a brief note, in which she pleaded that she had more claim than Mr.", "그녀는 두 사람의 면담에서 받은 영감을 안고 곧장 자리에 앉아 짧은 편지를 썼다."], ["Bulstrode had to the satisfaction of providing the money which had been serviceable to Lydgate—that it would be unkind in Lydgate not to grant her the position of being his helper in this small matter, the favor being entirely to her who had so little that was plainly marked out for her to do with her superfluous money.", "그 편지에서 그녀는, 리드게이트에게 도움이 되었던 그 돈을 마련하는 만족을 누릴 권리가 불스트로드 씨보다 자신에게 더 많다고, 이 작은 일에서 자신이 그의 조력자가 되는 위치를 그가 허락하지 않는다면 그것은 리드게이트의 박정한 처사가 될 것이며, 그 호의는 전적으로 자기 같은 사람에게 베푸는 것이라고―남아도는 돈으로 할 만한, 자기에게 분명하게 정해진 일이 거의 없는 자기 같은 사람에게 베푸는 것이라고 호소했다."], ["He might call her a creditor or by any other name if it did but imply that he granted her request.", "그가 자신의 청을 들어준다는 뜻이기만 하다면, 그는 그녀를 채권자라 부르든 다른 어떤 이름으로 부르든 상관없다고 했다."], ["She enclosed a check for a thousand pounds, and determined to take the letter with her the next day when she went to see Rosamond.", "그녀는 천 파운드짜리 수표를 동봉했고, 이튿날 로저먼드를 만나러 갈 때 그 편지를 가지고 가기로 마음먹었다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXVII.", "# CHAPTER LXXVII."]], [["“And thus thy fall hath left a kind of blot, To mark the full-fraught man and best indued With some suspicion.”", "이튿날 리드게이트는 브래싱에 가야 했으므로, 로저먼드에게 저녁까지 돌아오지 못할 것이라고 말했다. 근래 그녀는 교회에 갈 때와, 한 번 아빠를 뵈러 갈 때를 제외하고는 자기 집과 정원 밖으로 나간 적이 없었다."], ["—Henry V.", "그녀는 아빠에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["The next day Lydgate had to go to Brassing, and told Rosamond that he should be away until the evening.", "\"터셔스가 떠나면 우리가 이사하는 걸 도와주실 거죠, 아빠? 우리에겐 돈이 거의 없을 것 같아요."], ["Of late she had never gone beyond her own house and garden, except to church, and once to see her papa, to whom she said, “If Tertius goes away, you will help us to move, will you not, papa?", "누군가 우리를 도와주었으면 정말 좋겠어요.\" 그러자 빈시 씨는 이렇게 대답했다."], ["I suppose we shall have very little money.", "\"그럼, 얘야, 백 파운드나 이백 파운드쯤은 괜찮다."], ["I am sure I hope some one will help us.” And Mr.", "그 정도는 감당할 수 있어.\""], ["Vincy had said, “Yes, child, I don’t mind a hundred or two. I can see the end of that.”", "이런 예외를 빼면 그녀는 집에 앉아 나른한 우울과 불안에 잠긴 채, 윌 래디슬로가 오는 것을 유일한 희망과 관심의 한 점으로 마음에 새기고 있었다."], ["With these exceptions she had sat at home in languid melancholy and suspense, fixing her mind on Will Ladislaw’s coming as the one point of hope and interest, and associating this with some new urgency on Lydgate to make immediate arrangements for leaving Middlemarch and going to London, till she felt assured that the coming would be a potent cause of the going, without at all seeing how.", "그리고 이를 리드게이트가 미들마치를 떠나 런던으로 가는 일을 즉시 추진하도록 새롭게 다그치는 일과 결부시켰으니, 마침내 그녀는 그가 오는 것이 떠나는 일의 강력한 원인이 되리라고 확신하게 되었다. 어떻게 그렇게 될지는 전혀 알지 못한 채로."], ["This way of establishing sequences is too common to be fairly regarded as a peculiar folly in Rosamond.", "이렇게 인과를 세우는 방식은 너무나 흔한 일이라 로저먼드만의 특이한 어리석음이라고 보기는 어렵다. 그리고 이런 종류의 인과야말로 그것이 끊어질 때 가장 큰 충격을 안겨 준다."], ["And it is precisely this sort of sequence which causes the greatest shock when it is sundered: for to see how an effect may be produced is often to see possible missings and checks; but to see nothing except the desirable cause, and close upon it the desirable effect, rids us of doubt and makes our minds strongly intuitive.", "어떤 결과가 어떻게 생겨날 수 있는지 보는 것은 흔히 빗나갈 가능성과 가로막힘을 보는 일이지만, 바라는 원인과 그에 바짝 잇닿은 바라는 결과 외에는 아무것도 보지 않을 때 우리는 의심에서 벗어나고 우리의 마음은 강렬하게 직관적인 것이 되기 때문이다. 가엾은 로저먼드의 내면에서 진행되던 것이 바로 그런 과정이었다."], ["That was the process going on in poor Rosamond, while she arranged all objects around her with the same nicety as ever, only with more slowness—or sat down to the piano, meaning to play, and then desisting, yet lingering on the music stool with her white fingers suspended on the wooden front, and looking before her in dreamy ennui.", "그러는 동안에도 그녀는 주위의 온갖 물건을 여전히 똑같이 꼼꼼하게 정돈했으니, 다만 좀 더 굼떴을 뿐이었다. 아니면 피아노 앞에 앉아 연주하려다가 그만두고, 그러면서도 흰 손가락을 나무로 된 앞판에 멈춘 채 피아노 의자에 머뭇거리며 꿈꾸는 듯한 권태 속에 앞을 바라보곤 했다."], ["Her melancholy had become so marked that Lydgate felt a strange timidity before it, as a perpetual silent reproach, and the strong man, mastered by his keen sensibilities towards this fair fragile creature whose life he seemed somehow to have bruised, shrank from her look, and sometimes started at her approach, fear of her and fear for her rushing in only the more forcibly after it had been momentarily expelled by exasperation.", "그녀의 우울이 너무나 또렷해진 나머지, 리드게이트는 그것을 끊임없는 무언의 책망처럼 느끼며 그 앞에서 묘한 소심함을 느꼈다. 그리고 어쩐지 자신이 그 삶에 멍을 들인 듯한 이 곱고 연약한 존재를 향한 예리한 감수성에 사로잡힌 그 강한 남자는, 그녀의 시선 앞에서 움츠러들고 때로는 그녀가 다가오면 흠칫 놀랐으니, 그녀에 대한 두려움과 그녀를 위한 두려움이 짜증으로 잠시 쫓겨난 뒤에 오히려 더 거세게 밀려들었기 때문이다."]], [["But this morning Rosamond descended from her room upstairs—where she sometimes sat the whole day when Lydgate was out—equipped for a walk in the town.", "그러나 이날 아침 로저먼드는 위층 자기 방에서 — 리드게이트가 나가고 없을 때면 그녀는 가끔 하루 종일 그곳에 앉아 있었다 — 시내로 산책을 나갈 채비를 하고 내려왔다."], ["She had a letter to post—a letter addressed to Mr. Ladislaw and written with charming discretion, but intended to hasten his arrival by a hint of trouble.", "그녀에게는 부쳐야 할 편지가 한 통 있었는데, 래디슬로 씨 앞으로 쓴 그 편지는 자못 매력적인 신중함으로 쓰여 있었으나, 곤란한 사정을 넌지시 비쳐 그의 도착을 재촉하려는 의도가 담겨 있었다."], ["The servant-maid, their sole house-servant now, noticed her coming down-stairs in her walking dress, and thought “there never did anybody look so pretty in a bonnet poor thing.”", "이제 그 집의 유일한 하인이 된 하녀는 그녀가 외출복 차림으로 계단을 내려오는 것을 보고 이렇게 생각했다. \"보닛을 쓴 모습이 저렇게 예쁜 사람은 본 적이 없어, 가엾기도 하지.\""]], [["Meanwhile Dorothea’s mind was filled with her project of going to Rosamond, and with the many thoughts, both of the past and the probable future, which gathered round the idea of that visit.", "한편 도러시아의 마음은 로저먼드를 찾아가려는 계획과, 그 방문이라는 생각을 둘러싸고 모여드는 과거와 있음직한 미래에 관한 갖가지 상념으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["Until yesterday when Lydgate had opened to her a glimpse of some trouble in his married life, the image of Mrs. Lydgate had always been associated for her with that of Will Ladislaw.", "어제 리드게이트가 자기 결혼 생활의 어떤 어려움을 그녀에게 살짝 내비치기 전까지, 그녀에게 리드게이트 부인의 모습은 늘 윌 래디슬로의 모습과 결부되어 있었다."], ["Even in her most uneasy moments—even when she had been agitated by Mrs. Cadwallader’s painfully graphic report of gossip—her effort, nay, her strongest impulsive prompting, had been towards the vindication of Will from any sullying surmises; and when, in her meeting with him afterwards, she had at first interpreted his words as a probable allusion to a feeling towards Mrs.", "가장 불안했던 순간에도 — 캐드월러더 부인이 고통스러우리만치 생생하게 전한 소문 이야기에 동요했을 때조차 — 그녀의 노력은, 아니 그녀의 가장 강한 충동적 끌림은 윌을 더럽히는 어떤 억측으로부터도 그를 변호하려는 쪽이었다."], ["Lydgate which he was determined to cut himself off from indulging, she had had a quick, sad, excusing vision of the charm there might be in his constant opportunities of companionship with that fair creature, who most likely shared his other tastes as she evidently did his delight in music.", "그리고 그 후 그를 만났을 때, 그녀는 처음에 그의 말을 리드게이트 부인을 향한, 그러나 결코 빠져들지 않겠다고 다짐한 어떤 감정에 대한 암시로 해석했고, 그러자 그가 그 고운 존재와 늘 함께할 기회 속에 있을지도 모르는 매력에 대해 재빠르고 슬프고 너그러운 환영을 떠올렸으니, 그녀가 분명 그의 음악적 기쁨을 나누었던 것처럼 그의 다른 취향도 함께 나누었으리라 짐작했던 것이다."], ["But there had followed his parting words—the few passionate words in which he had implied that she herself was the object of whom his love held him in dread, that it was his love for her only which he was resolved not to declare but to carry away into banishment.", "그러나 그 뒤로 그의 작별의 말이 이어졌다 — 그가 자기 사랑이 두려워하는 대상이 바로 그녀 자신이며, 자신이 결코 고백하지 않고 추방 속으로 안고 가기로 결심한 것이 오로지 그녀를 향한 사랑임을 넌지시 내비친 그 몇 마디 열정적인 말이었다."], ["From the time of that parting, Dorothea, believing in Will’s love for her, believing with a proud delight in his delicate sense of honor and his determination that no one should impeach him justly, felt her heart quite at rest as to the regard he might have for Mrs.", "그 이별 이후로 도러시아는 윌이 자신을 사랑한다는 것을 믿었고, 그의 섬세한 명예심과 누구도 정당하게 그를 비난하지 못하게 하리라는 그의 결심을 자랑스러운 기쁨으로 믿으면서, 그가 리드게이트 부인에게 품었을지도 모르는 마음에 대해서는 자기 가슴이 아주 평온하다고 느꼈다."], ["Lydgate. She was sure that the regard was blameless.", "그녀는 그 마음이 흠 없는 것이라고 확신했다."]], [["There are natures in which, if they love us, we are conscious of having a sort of baptism and consecration: they bind us over to rectitude and purity by their pure belief about us; and our sins become that worst kind of sacrilege which tears down the invisible altar of trust.", "어떤 천성을 지닌 사람들이 우리를 사랑하면, 우리는 일종의 세례와 봉헌을 받은 것 같은 의식을 갖게 된다. 그들은 우리에 대한 순수한 믿음으로 우리를 올곧음과 순결함에 묶어 두고, 그리하여 우리의 죄는 보이지 않는 신뢰의 제단을 허무는 가장 나쁜 종류의 신성 모독이 된다."], ["“If you are not good, none is good”—those little words may give a terrific meaning to responsibility, may hold a vitriolic intensity for remorse.", "\"당신이 선하지 않다면 누구도 선하지 않다\" — 이 짧은 말은 책임에 무시무시한 의미를 부여할 수 있고, 회한에 황산 같은 강렬함을 담을 수 있다."]], [["Dorothea’s nature was of that kind: her own passionate faults lay along the easily counted open channels of her ardent character; and while she was full of pity for the visible mistakes of others, she had not yet any material within her experience for subtle constructions and suspicions of hidden wrong.", "도러시아의 천성이 바로 그런 종류였다. 그녀 자신의 열정적인 결점들은 그녀의 열렬한 성격에서 쉽게 헤아릴 수 있는 트인 물길을 따라 놓여 있었다."], ["But that simplicity of hers, holding up an ideal for others in her believing conception of them, was one of the great powers of her womanhood.", "그리고 그녀는 남들의 눈에 보이는 실수에 대해서는 연민으로 가득했으나, 아직 그녀의 경험 안에는 숨겨진 잘못을 미묘하게 구성하고 의심할 만한 재료가 없었다. 그러나 그녀의 그 단순함은, 남들에 대한 자신의 믿음 어린 관념 속에서 그들에게 하나의 이상을 들어 보였으니, 그것이야말로 그녀의 여성다움의 위대한 힘 가운데 하나였다."], ["And it had from the first acted strongly on Will Ladislaw.", "그리고 그것은 처음부터 윌 래디슬로에게 강하게 작용했다. 그녀와 헤어질 때 그는 느꼈다."], ["He felt, when he parted from her, that the brief words by which he had tried to convey to her his feeling about herself and the division which her fortune made between them, would only profit by their brevity when Dorothea had to interpret them: he felt that in her mind he had found his highest estimate.", "그녀 자신에 대한 자기 감정과 그녀의 재산이 둘 사이에 만들어 놓은 간극을 그녀에게 전하려 했던 그 짧은 말들이, 도러시아가 그것을 해석해야 할 때 오로지 그 짧음 덕분에 빛을 발하리라는 것을. 그는 그녀의 마음속에서 자신에 대한 가장 높은 평가를 발견했다고 느꼈다."]], [["And he was right there.", "그리고 그 점에서 그는 옳았다."], ["In the months since their parting Dorothea had felt a delicious though sad repose in their relation to each other, as one which was inwardly whole and without blemish.", "헤어진 이후 여러 달 동안 도러시아는 서로의 관계 속에서 슬프지만 감미로운 안식을 느껴 왔으니, 그것은 내면적으로 온전하고 흠 없는 관계였다."], ["She had an active force of antagonism within her, when the antagonism turned on the defence either of plans or persons that she believed in; and the wrongs which she felt that Will had received from her husband, and the external conditions which to others were grounds for slighting him, only gave the more tenacity to her affection and admiring judgment.", "그녀는 자기 안에 적극적인 대항의 힘을 지니고 있었는데, 그 대항이 자기가 믿는 계획이나 사람을 옹호하는 쪽으로 향할 때면 더욱 그러했다. 윌이 자기 남편에게서 받았다고 느낀 부당한 처사와, 다른 사람들에게는 그를 깔보는 근거가 되는 외적인 조건들은, 오히려 그녀의 애정과 찬탄 어린 판단을 더욱 끈질기게 만들 뿐이었다."], ["And now with the disclosures about Bulstrode had come another fact affecting Will’s social position, which roused afresh Dorothea’s inward resistance to what was said about him in that part of her world which lay within park palings.", "그리고 이제 불스트로드에 관한 폭로와 함께 윌의 사회적 지위에 영향을 미치는 또 하나의 사실이 드러나, 공원 울타리 안쪽에 자리한 그녀 세계의 그 부분에서 그에 대해 오가는 말에 맞서는 도러시아의 내적 저항을 새삼 일깨웠다."]], [["“Young Ladislaw the grandson of a thieving Jew pawnbroker” was a phrase which had entered emphatically into the dialogues about the Bulstrode business, at Lowick, Tipton, and Freshitt, and was a worse kind of placard on poor Will’s back than the “Italian with white mice.”", "\"도둑질하는 유대인 전당포 주인의 손자인 젊은 래디슬로\"라는 말은 로윅과 티프턴과 프레시트에서 불스트로드 사건을 두고 오간 대화 속에 힘주어 끼어들었으니, 그것은 \"흰 생쥐를 데리고 다니는 이탈리아 사람\"보다 더 고약하게 가엾은 윌의 등에 붙은 표찰이었다."], ["Upright Sir James Chettam was convinced that his own satisfaction was righteous when he thought with some complacency that here was an added league to that mountainous distance between Ladislaw and Dorothea, which enabled him to dismiss any anxiety in that direction as too absurd.", "올곧은 제임스 체텀 경은, 이로써 래디슬로와 도러시아 사이의 그 산처럼 까마득한 거리에 또 하나의 막대한 거리가 더해졌으며, 그 덕분에 그쪽으로의 어떤 근심도 너무 터무니없는 것으로 떨쳐 버릴 수 있게 되었다고 다소 흐뭇하게 생각하면서, 자기 만족이 정의로운 것이라고 확신했다."], ["And perhaps there had been some pleasure in pointing Mr. Brooke’s attention to this ugly bit of Ladislaw’s genealogy, as a fresh candle for him to see his own folly by.", "그리고 어쩌면 래디슬로 가계의 이 추한 한 토막을 브룩 씨의 주의에 일깨워 주는 데에, 그가 자기 자신의 어리석음을 비추어 볼 새로운 촛불로 삼게 하는 데에 얼마간의 즐거움이 있었을지도 모른다."], ["Dorothea had observed the animus with which Will’s part in the painful story had been recalled more than once; but she had uttered no word, being checked now, as she had not been formerly in speaking of Will, by the consciousness of a deeper relation between them which must always remain in consecrated secrecy.", "도러시아는 윌이 그 고통스러운 이야기에서 맡은 역할을 한 번 이상 떠올릴 때 깃들던 적의를 알아차렸다. 그러나 그녀는 한마디도 입 밖에 내지 않았으니, 예전에 윌에 관해 이야기할 때와는 달리, 이제는 두 사람 사이에 언제까지나 봉헌된 비밀로 남아야 할 더 깊은 관계가 있다는 의식이 그녀를 가로막았던 것이다."], ["But her silence shrouded her resistant emotion into a more thorough glow; and this misfortune in Will’s lot which, it seemed, others were wishing to fling at his back as an opprobrium, only gave something more of enthusiasm to her clinging thought.", "그러나 그 침묵은 그녀의 저항하는 감정을 더욱 철저한 열기로 감싸 들였다. 그리고 남들이 치욕으로 그의 등에 내던지고 싶어 하는 듯한 윌의 운명 속 이 불행은, 그녀가 매달리는 생각에 오히려 더한 열정을 더해 줄 뿐이었다."]], [["She entertained no visions of their ever coming into nearer union, and yet she had taken no posture of renunciation.", "그녀는 두 사람이 언젠가 더 가까운 결합에 이르리라는 어떤 환상도 품지 않았으나, 그렇다고 단념의 자세를 취하지도 않았다."], ["She had accepted her whole relation to Will very simply as part of her marriage sorrows, and would have thought it very sinful in her to keep up an inward wail because she was not completely happy, being rather disposed to dwell on the superfluities of her lot.", "그녀는 윌과의 모든 관계를 아주 담담하게 자기 결혼 생활의 슬픔의 일부로 받아들였고, 완전히 행복하지 않다는 이유로 마음속 비탄을 이어 간다면 그것이야말로 매우 죄스러운 일이라고 여겼을 것이니, 오히려 자기 운명의 넘치는 것들에 마음을 두려는 편이었다."], ["She could bear that the chief pleasures of her tenderness should lie in memory, and the idea of marriage came to her solely as a repulsive proposition from some suitor of whom she at present knew nothing, but whose merits, as seen by her friends, would be a source of torment to her:—“somebody who will manage your property for you, my dear,”", "그녀는 자기 애정의 주된 기쁨이 추억 속에 놓여 있어도 견딜 수 있었으며, 결혼이라는 생각은 오로지 지금으로서는 전혀 알지 못하지만 친구들 눈에 비친 그 장점이 그녀에게 고통의 원천이 될 어떤 구혼자가 내미는 역겨운 제안으로만 다가왔다."], ["was Mr. Brooke’s attractive suggestion of suitable characteristics.", "\"네 재산을 대신 관리해 줄 사람 말이다, 얘야\"라는 것이 어울리는 자질에 대한 브룩 씨의 매력적인 제안이었다."], ["“I should like to manage it myself, if I knew what to do with it,” said Dorothea.", "\"어떻게 쓸지만 안다면 제가 직접 관리하고 싶어요\"라고 도러시아는 말했다."], ["No—she adhered to her declaration that she would never be married again, and in the long valley of her life which looked so flat and empty of waymarks, guidance would come as she walked along the road, and saw her fellow-passengers by the way.", "아니, 그녀는 다시는 결혼하지 않겠다는 자신의 선언을 고수했으며, 이정표 하나 없이 그토록 평평하고 텅 비어 보이는 자기 인생의 긴 골짜기에서, 길을 걸어가며 길동무들을 만나는 동안 인도가 찾아오리라 여겼다."]], [["This habitual state of feeling about Will Ladislaw had been strong in all her waking hours since she had proposed to pay a visit to Mrs. Lydgate, making a sort of background against which she saw Rosamond’s figure presented to her without hindrances to her interest and compassion.", "윌 래디슬로에 대한 이런 습관적인 감정 상태는, 리드게이트 부인을 방문하겠다고 마음먹은 이래로 깨어 있는 모든 시간 동안 그녀 안에서 강하게 자리 잡아, 일종의 배경을 이루었으며, 그 배경을 바탕으로 그녀는 자신의 관심과 연민을 가로막는 것 없이 로저먼드의 모습을 마주 보았다."], ["There was evidently some mental separation, some barrier to complete confidence which had arisen between this wife and the husband who had yet made her happiness a law to him.", "자신의 행복을 하나의 법으로 삼아 온 남편과 이 아내 사이에는 분명 어떤 정신적인 단절이, 온전한 신뢰를 가로막는 어떤 장벽이 생겨난 것 같았다."], ["That was a trouble which no third person must directly touch.", "그것은 제삼자가 직접 건드려서는 안 되는 어려움이었다."], ["But Dorothea thought with deep pity of the loneliness which must have come upon Rosamond from the suspicions cast on her husband; and there would surely be help in the manifestation of respect for Lydgate and sympathy with her.", "그러나 도러시아는 남편에게 던져진 의혹으로 인해 로저먼드에게 닥쳤을 외로움을 깊은 연민으로 생각했으니, 리드게이트에 대한 존경과 그녀에 대한 공감을 드러내는 데에는 분명 도움이 되는 무언가가 있으리라 여겼다."]], [["“I shall talk to her about her husband,”", "\"그 부인에게 남편에 관해 이야기해야겠어.\""], ["thought Dorothea, as she was being driven towards the town.", "도러시아는 시내로 마차를 타고 가면서 그렇게 생각했다."], ["The clear spring morning, the scent of the moist earth, the fresh leaves just showing their creased-up wealth of greenery from out their half-opened sheaths, seemed part of the cheerfulness she was feeling from a long conversation with Mr.", "맑은 봄날 아침과, 촉촉한 흙냄새와, 반쯤 벌어진 깍지에서 갓 돋아 주름진 풍성한 초록빛을 내보이는 싱그러운 잎들은, 리드게이트의 처신을 변호하는 설명을 기쁘게 받아들인 페어브러더 씨와 오래 나눈 대화에서 그녀가 느끼던 명랑함의 일부인 듯했다."], ["Farebrother, who had joyfully accepted the justifying explanation of Lydgate’s conduct. “I shall take Mrs.", "\"리드게이트 부인에게 좋은 소식을 가져가야지."], ["Lydgate good news, and perhaps she will like to talk to me and make a friend of me.”", "그러면 그분도 나와 이야기 나누고 나를 친구로 삼고 싶어 할지도 몰라.\""]], [["Dorothea had another errand in Lowick Gate: it was about a new fine-toned bell for the school-house, and as she had to get out of her carriage very near to Lydgate’s, she walked thither across the street, having told the coachman to wait for some packages.", "도러시아에게는 로윅 게이트에서 볼일이 또 하나 있었다. 학교 건물에 놓을 음색 좋은 새 종에 관한 일이었는데, 마차에서 내려야 할 곳이 리드게이트의 집 바로 근처였으므로, 그녀는 마부에게 짐 꾸러미 몇 개를 기다리라고 일러 두고 길을 건너 그쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["The street door was open, and the servant was taking the opportunity of looking out at the carriage which was pausing within sight when it became apparent to her that the lady who “belonged to it” was coming towards her.", "길로 난 문은 열려 있었고, 하녀는 마침 시야 안에 멈춰 선 마차를 내다보던 참이었는데, 그 \"마차의 주인\"인 부인이 자기 쪽으로 다가오고 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Is Mrs. Lydgate at home?”", "\"리드게이트 부인 댁에 계신가요?\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“I’m not sure, my lady; I’ll see, if you’ll please to walk in,”", "\"잘 모르겠습니다, 마님. 들어와 계시면 제가 보고 오겠습니다.\""], ["said Martha, a little confused on the score of her kitchen apron, but collected enough to be sure that “mum” was not the right title for this queenly young widow with a carriage and pair.", "마사는 부엌 앞치마 차림이 신경 쓰여 다소 당황했으나, 한 쌍의 말이 끄는 마차를 거느린 이 여왕 같은 젊은 미망인에게 \"사모님\"이라는 호칭은 어울리지 않는다는 것쯤은 알아챌 만큼 정신을 차리고 있었다."], ["“Will you please to walk in, and I’ll go and see.”", "\"들어와 계시면 제가 가서 보고 오겠습니다.\""]], [["“Say that I am Mrs. Casaubon,”", "\"캐소본 부인이 왔다고 전해 주세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, as Martha moved forward intending to show her into the drawing-room and then to go up-stairs to see if Rosamond had returned from her walk.", "마사가 그녀를 응접실로 안내한 다음 위층으로 올라가 로저먼드가 산책에서 돌아왔는지 보려고 앞으로 나아갈 때 도러시아가 말했다."]], [["They crossed the broader part of the entrance-hall, and turned up the passage which led to the garden.", "그들은 현관 홀의 넓은 부분을 가로질러, 정원으로 통하는 복도 쪽으로 꺾어 들어갔다."], ["The drawing-room door was unlatched, and Martha, pushing it without looking into the room, waited for Mrs. Casaubon to enter and then turned away, the door having swung open and swung back again without noise.", "응접실 문은 빗장이 걸려 있지 않았고, 마사는 방 안을 들여다보지 않은 채 문을 밀고 나서 캐소본 부인이 들어가기를 기다렸다가 돌아섰는데, 문은 소리 없이 활짝 열렸다가 다시 닫혔다."]], [["Dorothea had less of outward vision than usual this morning, being filled with images of things as they had been and were going to be.", "이날 아침 도러시아는 지난날의 일들과 앞으로 닥칠 일들의 영상으로 가득 차 있어 평소보다 바깥을 보는 눈이 무뎠다."], ["She found herself on the other side of the door without seeing anything remarkable, but immediately she heard a voice speaking in low tones which startled her as with a sense of dreaming in daylight, and advancing unconsciously a step or two beyond the projecting slab of a bookcase, she saw, in the terrible illumination of a certainty which filled up all outlines, something which made her pause, motionless, without self-possession enough to speak.", "그녀는 별다른 것을 보지 못한 채 문 안쪽에 들어서 있었으나, 곧 낮은 어조로 말하는 어떤 목소리가 들려와, 마치 대낮에 꿈을 꾸는 듯한 느낌으로 그녀를 흠칫 놀라게 했다. 그리고 무심코 책장의 튀어나온 판 너머로 한두 걸음 나아간 그녀는, 모든 윤곽을 또렷이 메워 버리는 어떤 확신의 무서운 조명 속에서, 그 자리에 멈춰 서게 만드는 무언가를, 입을 열 만한 침착함조차 없게 만드는 무언가를 보았다."]], [["Seated with his back towards her on a sofa which stood against the wall on a line with the door by which she had entered, she saw Will Ladislaw: close by him and turned towards him with a flushed tearfulness which gave a new brilliancy to her face sat Rosamond, her bonnet hanging back, while Will leaning towards her clasped both her upraised hands in his and spoke with low-toned fervor.", "그녀가 들어온 문과 일직선으로 벽에 붙어 놓인 소파에 등을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리고 앉은 윌 래디슬로가 보였다. 그 바로 곁에는 로저먼드가 그를 향해 돌아앉아 있었는데, 발갛게 상기된 눈물기가 그녀의 얼굴에 새로운 광채를 더했고, 보닛은 뒤로 젖혀져 있었으며, 한편 윌은 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 기울인 채 그녀가 치켜든 두 손을 자기 두 손으로 꼭 감싸 쥐고 낮은 어조의 열정으로 말하고 있었다."]], [["Rosamond in her agitated absorption had not noticed the silently advancing figure; but when Dorothea, after the first immeasurable instant of this vision, moved confusedly backward and found herself impeded by some piece of furniture, Rosamond was suddenly aware of her presence, and with a spasmodic movement snatched away her hands and rose, looking at Dorothea who was necessarily arrested.", "흥분에 사로잡혀 있던 로저먼드는 소리 없이 다가오는 모습을 알아채지 못했다. 그러나 도러시아가 이 광경의 헤아릴 수 없는 첫 순간이 지난 뒤 어쩔 줄 몰라 뒷걸음치다가 무슨 가구에 가로막히자, 로저먼드는 문득 그녀가 있음을 알아차리고는 발작하듯 두 손을 홱 빼더니 일어서서, 어쩔 수 없이 멈춰 선 도러시아를 바라보았다."], ["Will Ladislaw, starting up, looked round also, and meeting Dorothea’s eyes with a new lightning in them, seemed changing to marble.", "윌 래디슬로도 벌떡 일어나 주위를 둘러보았고, 새로운 번갯불이 어린 도러시아의 두 눈과 마주치자 대리석으로 변해 가는 것 같았다."], ["But she immediately turned them away from him to Rosamond and said in a firm voice—", "그러나 그녀는 곧장 그에게서 눈을 돌려 로저먼드를 향하고는 단호한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Excuse me, Mrs.", "\"실례했습니다, 리드게이트 부인."], ["Lydgate, the servant did not know that you were here.", "하녀가 부인이 여기 계신 줄 몰랐던 모양이에요."], ["I called to deliver an important letter for Mr. Lydgate, which I wished to put into your own hands.”", "리드게이트 씨에게 전할 중요한 편지가 있어 들렀는데, 부인 손에 직접 건네고 싶었습니다.\""]], [["She laid down the letter on the small table which had checked her retreat, and then including Rosamond and Will in one distant glance and bow, she went quickly out of the room, meeting in the passage the surprised Martha, who said she was sorry the mistress was not at home, and then showed the strange lady out with an inward reflection that grand people were probably more impatient than others.", "그녀는 자신의 뒷걸음을 막았던 작은 탁자 위에 편지를 내려놓고는, 로저먼드와 윌을 한데 묶어 멀찍이 한 번 눈길을 주고 가볍게 인사한 뒤 빠르게 방을 나섰다. 복도에서 마주친 놀란 마사가 사모님이 안 계셔서 죄송하다고 말한 다음, 신분 높은 사람들은 아마 다른 이들보다 더 참을성이 없으리라고 속으로 생각하며 그 낯선 부인을 배웅했다."]], [["Dorothea walked across the street with her most elastic step and was quickly in her carriage again.", "도러시아는 더없이 탄력 있는 걸음으로 길을 건너 이내 다시 마차에 올랐다."]], [["“Drive on to Freshitt Hall,”", "\"프레시트 홀로 가요.\""], ["she said to the coachman, and any one looking at her might have thought that though she was paler than usual she was never animated by a more self-possessed energy.", "그녀가 마부에게 말했다. 그녀를 바라보는 사람이라면 누구나, 그녀의 얼굴이 평소보다 창백하긴 했어도 이토록 침착한 활력에 차 있었던 적은 없었으리라고 생각했을 것이다."], ["And that was really her experience.", "그리고 그것은 정말로 그녀가 느끼던 바였다."], ["It was as if she had drunk a great draught of scorn that stimulated her beyond the susceptibility to other feelings.", "마치 다른 감정에 대한 감수성을 넘어서까지 그녀를 자극하는 경멸의 술을 한 모금 크게 들이켠 것 같았다."], ["She had seen something so far below her belief, that her emotions rushed back from it and made an excited throng without an object.", "그녀는 자신의 믿음에 한참이나 못 미치는 무언가를 보았으니, 그녀의 감정은 거기에서 도로 밀려 나와 아무런 대상 없이 흥분한 무리를 이루었다."], ["She needed something active to turn her excitement out upon.", "그녀에게는 그 흥분을 쏟아부을 어떤 활동적인 것이 필요했다."], ["She felt power to walk and work for a day, without meat or drink.", "그녀는 음식이나 물 없이도 하루 종일 걷고 일할 힘을 느꼈다."], ["And she would carry out the purpose with which she had started in the morning, of going to Freshitt and Tipton to tell Sir James and her uncle all that she wished them to know about Lydgate, whose married loneliness under his trial now presented itself to her with new significance, and made her more ardent in readiness to be his champion.", "그래서 아침에 길을 나설 때 품었던 목적, 곧 프레시트와 티프턴에 가서 제임스 경과 삼촌에게 리드게이트에 관해 자기가 알리고 싶은 모든 것을 전하겠다는 그 목적을 실행하기로 했다. 시련 속에서 리드게이트가 겪는 결혼 생활의 외로움이 이제 그녀에게 새로운 의미로 다가왔고, 그래서 그녀는 더욱 열렬히 그의 옹호자가 될 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["She had never felt anything like this triumphant power of indignation in the struggle of her married life, in which there had always been a quickly subduing pang; and she took it as a sign of new strength.", "그녀는 자기 결혼 생활의 분투 속에서 — 거기에는 늘 재빨리 가라앉는 아픔이 있었으나 — 이처럼 의기양양한 분노의 힘 같은 것을 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 그것을 새로운 힘의 표시로 받아들였다."]], [["“Dodo, how very bright your eyes are!”", "\"도도, 눈이 어쩜 그렇게 반짝이니!\""], ["said Celia, when Sir James was gone out of the room.", "제임스 경이 방에서 나가자 실리아가 말했다."], ["“And you don’t see anything you look at, Arthur or anything.", "\"그리고 언니는 보고 있는 걸 하나도 보지 못해, 아서도 아무것도 말이야."], ["You are going to do something uncomfortable, I know.", "언니 분명 뭔가 마음 불편한 일을 벌이려는 거지."], ["Is it all about Mr. Lydgate, or has something else happened?”", "다 리드게이트 씨 일 때문이야, 아니면 또 무슨 일이 있었던 거야?\""], ["Celia had been used to watch her sister with expectation.", "실리아는 언니를 기대에 차서 지켜보는 데 익숙했다."]], [["“Yes, dear, a great many things have happened,”", "\"그래, 얘야, 아주 많은 일이 있었어.\""], ["said Dodo, in her full tones.", "도도가 충만한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“I wonder what,”", "\"무슨 일인지 궁금하네.\""], ["said Celia, folding her arms cozily and leaning forward upon them.", "실리아가 팔짱을 아늑하게 끼고 그 위에 몸을 기울이며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, all the troubles of all people on the face of the earth,”", "\"오, 이 땅 위 모든 사람의 온갖 괴로움이지.\""], ["said Dorothea, lifting her arms to the back of her head.", "도러시아가 두 팔을 들어 뒤통수에 갖다 대며 말했다."]], [["“Dear me, Dodo, are you going to have a scheme for them?”", "\"어머나, 도도, 언니 그걸 위해 무슨 계획이라도 세우려는 거야?\""], ["said Celia, a little uneasy at this Hamlet-like raving.", "실리아가 이 햄릿 같은 헛소리에 다소 불안해하며 말했다."]], [["But Sir James came in again, ready to accompany Dorothea to the Grange, and she finished her expedition well, not swerving in her resolution until she descended at her own door.", "그러나 제임스 경이 도러시아를 그레인지까지 데려다줄 채비를 하고 다시 들어왔고, 그녀는 자기 집 문 앞에 내릴 때까지 결심을 굽히지 않은 채 그 여정을 무사히 마쳤다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXVIII.", "# CHAPTER LXXVIII."]], [["“Would it were yesterday and I i’ the grave, With her sweet faith above for monument.”", "어제였으면, 그래서 내가 무덤 속에 있다면, 그녀의 다정한 신의가 비석처럼 그 위에 놓인다면."]], [["Rosamond and Will stood motionless—they did not know how long—he looking towards the spot where Dorothea had stood, and she looking towards him with doubt.", "로저먼드와 윌은 꼼짝도 하지 않고 서 있었다. 얼마나 오래 그렇게 있었는지 그들은 알지 못했다."], ["It seemed an endless time to Rosamond, in whose inmost soul there was hardly so much annoyance as gratification from what had just happened.", "그는 도러시아가 서 있던 자리를 바라보았고, 그녀는 의혹에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 로저먼드에게는 그것이 끝없는 시간처럼 느껴졌으나, 그녀의 가장 깊은 영혼 속에는 방금 일어난 일에 대한 짜증보다 만족이 더 컸다."], ["Shallow natures dream of an easy sway over the emotions of others, trusting implicitly in their own petty magic to turn the deepest streams, and confident, by pretty gestures and remarks, of making the thing that is not as though it were.", "천박한 본성은 다른 사람의 감정을 손쉽게 휘두를 수 있으리라 꿈꾸며, 가장 깊은 물줄기조차 돌릴 수 있다는 제 보잘것없는 마술을 무턱대고 믿고, 예쁜 몸짓과 말 몇 마디로 있지 않은 것을 마치 있는 것처럼 만들 수 있다고 자신한다."], ["She knew that Will had received a severe blow, but she had been little used to imagining other people’s states of mind except as a material cut into shape by her own wishes; and she believed in her own power to soothe or subdue.", "그녀는 윌이 심한 타격을 받았다는 것을 알았지만, 자기 자신의 소망에 맞추어 빚어낼 재료가 아닌 한 다른 사람의 마음 상태를 상상하는 데 익숙하지 않았다. 그래서 그녀는 자신에게 누군가를 달래거나 굴복시킬 힘이 있다고 믿었다."], ["Even Tertius, that most perverse of men, was always subdued in the long-run: events had been obstinate, but still Rosamond would have said now, as she did before her marriage, that she never gave up what she had set her mind on.", "그 누구보다 비뚤어진 남자인 터티어스조차 결국에는 늘 누그러지지 않았던가. 사건들은 완강했지만, 그래도 로저먼드는 결혼 전에 그랬듯이 지금도 마음먹은 일은 결코 포기하지 않는다고 말했을 것이다."]], [["She put out her arm and laid the tips of her fingers on Will’s coat-sleeve.", "그녀는 팔을 내밀어 손가락 끝을 윌의 윗옷 소매에 얹었다."]], [["“Don’t touch me!”", "\"나를 건드리지 마시오!\""], ["he said, with an utterance like the cut of a lash, darting from her, and changing from pink to white and back again, as if his whole frame were tingling with the pain of the sting.", "그가 채찍을 후려치듯 내뱉으며 그녀에게서 홱 물러섰다. 온몸이 독침의 고통으로 얼얼한 듯, 그의 얼굴은 붉어졌다가 하얘졌다가 다시 붉어졌다."], ["He wheeled round to the other side of the room and stood opposite to her, with the tips of his fingers in his pockets and his head thrown back, looking fiercely not at Rosamond but at a point a few inches away from her.", "그는 방의 반대편으로 돌아서서 그녀와 마주 보고 섰는데, 손가락 끝을 호주머니에 꽂고 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 로저먼드가 아니라 그녀에게서 몇 인치 떨어진 한 점을 사납게 노려보고 있었다."]], [["She was keenly offended, but the signs she made of this were such as only Lydgate was used to interpret.", "그녀는 몹시 모욕감을 느꼈지만, 그녀가 그것을 드러내는 신호는 오직 리드게이트만이 해석하는 데 익숙한 것이었다."], ["She became suddenly quiet and seated herself, untying her hanging bonnet and laying it down with her shawl.", "그녀는 갑자기 조용해지더니 자리에 앉아, 늘어진 보닛의 끈을 풀어 숄과 함께 내려놓았다."], ["Her little hands which she folded before her were very cold.", "앞으로 모아 쥔 그녀의 작은 손은 무척 차가웠다."]], [["It would have been safer for Will in the first instance to have taken up his hat and gone away; but he had felt no impulse to do this; on the contrary, he had a horrible inclination to stay and shatter Rosamond with his anger.", "처음부터 윌이 모자를 집어 들고 떠나 버렸다면 더 안전했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그렇게 할 충동을 조금도 느끼지 못했다."], ["It seemed as impossible to bear the fatality she had drawn down on him without venting his fury as it would be to a panther to bear the javelin-wound without springing and biting.", "오히려 그는 머물러서 분노로 로저먼드를 박살 내고 싶은 끔찍한 욕구를 느꼈다. 표범이 창에 찔린 상처를 입고도 뛰어올라 물어뜯지 않고는 견딜 수 없듯이, 그녀가 자신에게 끌어내린 그 운명을 분노를 쏟아내지 않고는 견딜 수 없을 것만 같았다."], ["And yet—how could he tell a woman that he was ready to curse her?", "하지만, 한 여자에게 당신을 저주하고 싶다고 어떻게 말할 수 있겠는가?"], ["He was fuming under a repressive law which he was forced to acknowledge: he was dangerously poised, and Rosamond’s voice now brought the decisive vibration.", "그는 인정하지 않을 수 없는 억제의 법칙 아래서 부글부글 끓고 있었다. 그는 위태롭게 균형을 잡고 있었는데, 이제 로저먼드의 목소리가 결정적인 진동을 가져왔다."], ["In flute-like tones of sarcasm she said—", "그녀는 피리 같은 비아냥의 어조로 말했다."]], [["“You can easily go after Mrs. Casaubon and explain your preference.”", "\"카소봉 부인을 쫓아가서 당신이 그분을 더 좋아한다고 설명하면 되겠네요.\""]], [["“Go after her!”", "\"쫓아가라고!\""], ["he burst out, with a sharp edge in his voice.", "그가 목소리에 날카로운 칼날을 세우며 터뜨렸다."], ["“Do you think she would turn to look at me, or value any word I ever uttered to her again at more than a dirty feather?—Explain!", "\"그녀가 돌아서서 나를 봐 주기라도 할 것 같소? 내가 그녀에게 한 어떤 말이라도 더러운 깃털 한 조각 이상으로 여겨 줄 것 같소?"], ["How can a man explain at the expense of a woman?”", "설명이라니! 한 남자가 어찌 한 여자를 희생시키면서 설명할 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“You can tell her what you please,”", "\"좋으실 대로 말씀하시면 되죠.\""], ["said Rosamond with more tremor.", "로저먼드가 좀 더 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Do you suppose she would like me better for sacrificing you?", "\"내가 당신을 희생시킨다고 그녀가 나를 더 좋아하리라 생각하오?"], ["She is not a woman to be flattered because I made myself despicable—to believe that I must be true to her because I was a dastard to you.”", "그녀는 내가 비열해졌다고 해서 우쭐해할 여자가 아니오. 내가 당신에게 비겁한 자였으니 그녀에게는 진실하리라고 믿을 여자가 아니란 말이오.\""]], [["He began to move about with the restlessness of a wild animal that sees prey but cannot reach it.", "그는 먹이를 보고도 잡을 수 없는 야생 동물처럼 안절부절못하며 서성이기 시작했다."], ["Presently he burst out again—", "이윽고 그가 다시 터뜨렸다."]], [["“I had no hope before—not much—of anything better to come.", "\"전에도 나는 희망이 없었소. 더 나은 무언가가 오리라는 희망이 거의 없었소."], ["But I had one certainty—that she believed in me.", "하지만 한 가지 확신은 있었지. 그녀가 나를 믿어 준다는 것 말이오."], ["Whatever people had said or done about me, she believed in me.—That’s gone!", "사람들이 나에 대해 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 하든, 그녀는 나를 믿어 주었소. 그게 사라졌소!"], ["She’ll never again think me anything but a paltry pretence—too nice to take heaven except upon flattering conditions, and yet selling myself for any devil’s change by the sly.", "이제 그녀는 나를 보잘것없는 위선자 이상으로는 결코 여기지 않을 거요. 아첨하는 조건이 아니고서는 천국조차 받아들이지 않을 만큼 까다로운 척하면서, 정작 슬그머니 어떤 악마의 잔돈에라도 자신을 팔아넘기는 자라고 말이오."], ["She’ll think of me as an incarnate insult to her, from the first moment we—”", "그녀는 나를, 우리가 처음 만난 순간부터, 자기에게 가한 살아 있는 모욕이라고 생각할 거요.\""]], [["Will stopped as if he had found himself grasping something that must not be thrown and shattered.", "윌은 던져서는 안 될 무언가, 던졌다가는 산산조각 날 무언가를 쥐고 있음을 깨달은 듯 말을 멈추었다."], ["He found another vent for his rage by snatching up Rosamond’s words again, as if they were reptiles to be throttled and flung off.", "그는 로저먼드의 말을 다시 낚아채는 데서 분노의 또 다른 배출구를 찾았다. 마치 그것이 목 졸라 내던져야 할 파충류라도 되는 듯이."]], [["“Explain!", "\"설명이라니!"], ["Tell a man to explain how he dropped into hell! Explain my preference!", "한 남자에게 자기가 어떻게 지옥에 떨어졌는지 설명하라니!"], ["I never had a preference for her, any more than I have a preference for breathing.", "내가 그녀를 더 좋아한다는 걸 설명하라고! 나는 그녀를 좋아한 적이 없소."], ["No other woman exists by the side of her.", "숨 쉬는 것을 좋아한다고 할 수 없듯이 말이오."], ["I would rather touch her hand if it were dead, than I would touch any other woman’s living.”", "그녀 곁에는 다른 어떤 여자도 존재하지 않소. 나는 다른 여자의 산 손을 만지느니 차라리 그녀의 죽은 손을 만지겠소.\""]], [["Rosamond, while these poisoned weapons were being hurled at her, was almost losing the sense of her identity, and seemed to be waking into some new terrible existence.", "이 독 묻은 무기들이 자신에게 날아드는 동안, 로저먼드는 거의 자기 정체성의 감각을 잃어 가고 있었으며, 어떤 새롭고 끔찍한 존재로 깨어나는 것만 같았다."], ["She had no sense of chill resolute repulsion, of reticent self-justification such as she had known under Lydgate’s most stormy displeasure: all her sensibility was turned into a bewildering novelty of pain; she felt a new terrified recoil under a lash never experienced before.", "리드게이트의 가장 격렬한 노여움 아래서 알았던 차갑고 단호한 반발이나 말없는 자기 정당화의 감각은 전혀 없었다. 그녀의 모든 감수성은 어리둥절한 새로운 고통으로 변해 버렸으니, 그녀는 한 번도 겪어 보지 못한 채찍질 아래서 새삼 겁에 질려 움츠러드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["What another nature felt in opposition to her own was being burnt and bitten into her consciousness.", "자신의 본성에 맞서는 또 다른 본성이 느끼는 바가 그녀의 의식 속으로 불태워지고 물어뜯기듯 새겨지고 있었다."], ["When Will had ceased to speak she had become an image of sickened misery: her lips were pale, and her eyes had a tearless dismay in them.", "윌이 말을 멈추었을 때 그녀는 병든 비참함의 형상이 되어 있었다. 입술은 창백했고, 눈에는 눈물 없는 절망이 어려 있었다."], ["If it had been Tertius who stood opposite to her, that look of misery would have been a pang to him, and he would have sunk by her side to comfort her, with that strong-armed comfort which she had often held very cheap.", "만약 그녀와 마주 선 사람이 터티어스였다면, 그 비참한 표정은 그에게 고통이 되었을 테고, 그는 그녀 곁에 주저앉아, 그녀가 흔히 하찮게 여기던 그 억센 팔의 위로로 그녀를 다독였을 것이다."]], [["Let it be forgiven to Will that he had no such movement of pity.", "윌에게 그런 연민의 움직임이 없었던 것은 용서받아도 좋으리라."], ["He had felt no bond beforehand to this woman who had spoiled the ideal treasure of his life, and he held himself blameless.", "그는 자기 삶의 이상적인 보물을 망쳐 버린 이 여자에게 미리 어떤 유대도 느낀 적이 없었으며, 자신에게는 잘못이 없다고 여겼다."], ["He knew that he was cruel, but he had no relenting in him yet.", "그는 자신이 잔인하다는 것을 알았지만, 아직 마음이 누그러들지 않았다."]], [["After he had done speaking, he still moved about, half in absence of mind, and Rosamond sat perfectly still.", "말을 마친 뒤에도 그는 반쯤 정신이 나간 채 계속 서성거렸고, 로저먼드는 완전히 가만히 앉아 있었다."], ["At length Will, seeming to bethink himself, took up his hat, yet stood some moments irresolute.", "마침내 윌은 정신을 차린 듯 모자를 집어 들었지만, 그래도 한동안 결단을 내리지 못한 채 서 있었다. 그는 그녀에게 흔한 예의의 말 한마디조차 꺼내기 어려운 방식으로 말을 해 버렸다."], ["He had spoken to her in a way that made a phrase of common politeness difficult to utter; and yet, now that he had come to the point of going away from her without further speech, he shrank from it as a brutality; he felt checked and stultified in his anger.", "그런데 막상 더 이상 아무 말 없이 그녀를 떠나는 순간에 이르자, 그는 그것이 잔인한 짓인 듯 머뭇거렸다. 그는 자신의 분노가 가로막히고 무력해진 것을 느꼈다."], ["He walked towards the mantel-piece and leaned his arm on it, and waited in silence for—he hardly knew what.", "그는 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 걸어가 팔을 그 위에 기댄 채, 무엇을 기다리는지도 잘 모르면서 잠자코 기다렸다. 복수심의 불길은 여전히 그의 안에서 타오르고 있어서 그는 철회의 말 한마디도 꺼낼 수 없었다."], ["The vindictive fire was still burning in him, and he could utter no word of retractation; but it was nevertheless in his mind that having come back to this hearth where he had enjoyed a caressing friendship he had found calamity seated there—he had had suddenly revealed to him a trouble that lay outside the home as well as within it.", "그렇지만 그의 마음속에는 이런 생각이 자리 잡고 있었다. 다정한 우정을 누렸던 이 화롯가로 돌아와 보니 그곳에 재앙이 앉아 있었고, 집 안에서뿐만 아니라 집 밖에도 놓여 있는 고난이 그에게 불현듯 드러났다는 것이다."], ["And what seemed a foreboding was pressing upon him as with slow pincers:—that his life might come to be enslaved by this helpless woman who had thrown herself upon him in the dreary sadness of her heart.", "그리고 어떤 예감 같은 것이 느린 집게처럼 그를 짓눌렀다. 마음의 음울한 슬픔 속에서 그에게 자신을 내던진 이 무력한 여자에게 그의 삶이 예속되어 버릴지도 모른다는 예감이었다."], ["But he was in gloomy rebellion against the fact that his quick apprehensiveness foreshadowed to him, and when his eyes fell on Rosamond’s blighted face it seemed to him that he was the more pitiable of the two; for pain must enter into its glorified life of memory before it can turn into compassion.", "그러나 그는 자신의 예민한 직감이 미리 보여 주는 그 사실에 음울하게 반항하고 있었으니, 그의 눈길이 로저먼드의 시들어 버린 얼굴에 떨어졌을 때 그에게는 자신이 둘 중 더 가련한 쪽인 것처럼 여겨졌다. 고통이 연민으로 바뀌려면 먼저 기억의 영광된 삶 속으로 들어가야 하는 법이니까."]], [["And so they remained for many minutes, opposite each other, far apart, in silence; Will’s face still possessed by a mute rage, and Rosamond’s by a mute misery. The poor thing had no force to fling out any passion in return; the terrible collapse of the illusion towards which all her hope had been strained was a stroke which had too thoroughly shaken her: her little world was in ruins, and she felt herself tottering in the midst as a lonely bewildered consciousness.", "그리하여 그들은 여러 분 동안 서로 마주 본 채, 멀리 떨어져, 침묵 속에 남아 있었다. 윌의 얼굴은 여전히 말 없는 분노에 사로잡혀 있었고, 로저먼드의 얼굴은 말 없는 비참함에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그 가엾은 여인에게는 어떤 격정도 되받아 쏟아낼 힘이 없었다. 그녀의 모든 희망이 향해 팽팽히 당겨져 있던 환상의 끔찍한 붕괴는 그녀를 너무도 철저하게 뒤흔든 일격이었다. 그녀의 작은 세계는 폐허가 되었고, 그녀는 그 한가운데서 외롭고 어리둥절한 의식으로 비틀거리고 있는 자신을 느꼈다."]], [["Will wished that she would speak and bring some mitigating shadow across his own cruel speech, which seemed to stand staring at them both in mockery of any attempt at revived fellowship.", "윌은 그녀가 무슨 말이라도 해서, 되살아난 친교의 어떤 시도도 비웃듯 두 사람을 뚫어지게 노려보고 서 있는 것만 같은 자신의 잔인한 말에 누그러뜨릴 그늘을 드리워 주기를 바랐다."], ["But she said nothing, and at last with a desperate effort over himself, he asked, “Shall I come in and see Lydgate this evening?”", "그러나 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 마침내 그는 필사적으로 자신을 다잡으며 물었다. \"오늘 저녁에 들러서 리드게이트를 봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["“If you like,”", "\"좋으실 대로요.\""], ["Rosamond answered, just audibly.", "로저먼드가 겨우 들릴 만큼 대답했다."]], [["And then Will went out of the house, Martha never knowing that he had been in.", "그러고 나서 윌은 집을 나섰는데, 마사는 그가 안에 들어와 있었던 줄도 알지 못했다."]], [["After he was gone, Rosamond tried to get up from her seat, but fell back fainting.", "그가 가 버린 뒤, 로저먼드는 자리에서 일어서려 했으나 정신을 잃고 도로 주저앉았다."], ["When she came to herself again, she felt too ill to make the exertion of rising to ring the bell, and she remained helpless until the girl, surprised at her long absence, thought for the first time of looking for her in all the down-stairs rooms.", "다시 정신이 들었을 때는 너무 아파서 일어나 종을 울릴 기력도 없었고, 그녀가 오래 보이지 않는 것을 이상히 여긴 하녀가 처음으로 아래층 방들을 모두 뒤져 그녀를 찾을 생각을 할 때까지 그녀는 속수무책으로 그대로 있었다."], ["Rosamond said that she had felt suddenly sick and faint, and wanted to be helped up-stairs.", "로저먼드는 갑자기 속이 메스껍고 어지러워졌다며 위층으로 부축해 달라고 했다."], ["When there she threw herself on the bed with her clothes on, and lay in apparent torpor, as she had done once before on a memorable day of grief.", "위층에 이르자 그녀는 옷을 입은 채 침대에 몸을 던지고는, 언젠가 잊지 못할 슬픔의 날에 한 번 그랬던 것처럼 무기력에 빠진 듯 누워 있었다."]], [["Lydgate came home earlier than he had expected, about half-past five, and found her there.", "리드게이트는 예상보다 일찍, 다섯 시 반쯤 집에 돌아와 그녀가 그렇게 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The perception that she was ill threw every other thought into the background.", "그녀가 아프다는 것을 알아차리자 다른 모든 생각이 뒤로 물러났다."], ["When he felt her pulse, her eyes rested on him with more persistence than they had done for a long while, as if she felt some content that he was there.", "그가 그녀의 맥을 짚었을 때, 그녀의 눈은 오랫동안 보이지 않던 끈질김으로 그에게 머물렀는데, 마치 그가 거기 있다는 사실에 어떤 만족을 느끼는 것 같았다."], ["He perceived the difference in a moment, and seating himself by her put his arm gently under her, and bending over her said, “My poor Rosamond!", "그는 그 차이를 단번에 알아차렸고, 그녀 곁에 앉아 팔을 그녀의 몸 아래에 살며시 넣고는 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히며 말했다. \"가엾은 내 로저먼드!"], ["has something agitated you?”", "무슨 일로 마음이 흔들렸소?\""], ["Clinging to him she fell into hysterical sobbings and cries, and for the next hour he did nothing but soothe and tend her.", "그에게 매달린 채 그녀는 발작적인 흐느낌과 울음에 빠져들었고, 이후 한 시간 동안 그는 그녀를 달래고 보살피는 일밖에 하지 않았다."], ["He imagined that Dorothea had been to see her, and that all this effect on her nervous system, which evidently involved some new turning towards himself, was due to the excitement of the new impressions which that visit had raised.", "그는 도러시아가 그녀를 보러 왔었으며, 분명 자신을 향한 어떤 새로운 마음의 변화를 담고 있는 그녀의 신경계에 미친 이 모든 영향이 그 방문이 불러일으킨 새로운 인상의 흥분에서 비롯된 것이라고 짐작했다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXIX.", "# CHAPTER LXXIX."]], [["“Now, I saw in my dream, that just as they had ended their talk, they drew nigh to a very miry slough, that was in the midst of the plain; and they, being heedless, did both fall suddenly into the bog.", "이제 나는 꿈속에서 보았다. 그들이 이야기를 막 끝냈을 무렵, 들판 한가운데 있는 몹시 질척한 늪에 가까이 이르렀으니, 그들은 부주의하여 둘 다 갑자기 그 수렁에 빠지고 말았다."], ["The name of the slough was Despond.”—BUNYAN.", "그 늪의 이름은 절망이었다.—번연."]], [["When Rosamond was quiet, and Lydgate had left her, hoping that she might soon sleep under the effect of an anodyne, he went into the drawing-room to fetch a book which he had left there, meaning to spend the evening in his work-room, and he saw on the table Dorothea’s letter addressed to him.", "로저먼드가 안정을 되찾고, 진통제의 효력으로 곧 잠들 수 있기를 바라며 리드게이트가 그녀의 곁을 떠난 뒤, 그는 거실에 두고 온 책을 가지러 갔다. 저녁 시간을 작업실에서 보낼 작정이었던 것이다."], ["He had not ventured to ask Rosamond if Mrs.", "그러다 그는 탁자 위에서 자기 앞으로 온 도러시아의 편지를 보았다."], ["Casaubon had called, but the reading of this letter assured him of the fact, for Dorothea mentioned that it was to be carried by herself.", "그는 캐소번 부인이 다녀갔는지 로저먼드에게 감히 묻지 못했지만, 이 편지를 읽고서 그 사실을 확인할 수 있었다. 도러시아가 자기 손으로 직접 이 편지를 전하겠노라고 적어 두었기 때문이다."]], [["When Will Ladislaw came in a little later Lydgate met him with a surprise which made it clear that he had not been told of the earlier visit, and Will could not say, “Did not Mrs.", "조금 뒤에 윌 래디슬로가 들어왔을 때, 리드게이트는 놀란 기색으로 그를 맞이했는데, 그 모습으로 보아 그가 앞서 있었던 방문에 대해 듣지 못했음이 분명했다."], ["Lydgate tell you that I came this morning?”", "그래서 윌은 \"리드게이트 부인이 오늘 아침 제가 왔다고 말씀드리지 않던가요?\"라고 말할 수 없었다."]], [["“Poor Rosamond is ill,”", "\"가엾게도 로저먼드가 아프다네.\""], ["Lydgate added immediately on his greeting.", "리드게이트가 인사를 건네자마자 덧붙였다."]], [["“Not seriously, I hope,”", "\"심각한 것은 아니겠지요.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."]], [["“No—only a slight nervous shock—the effect of some agitation.", "\"아닐세—그저 가벼운 신경성 충격일 뿐이야—어떤 동요의 여파지."], ["She has been overwrought lately.", "요즘 너무 무리를 했거든."], ["The truth is, Ladislaw, I am an unlucky devil.", "사실대로 말하면, 래디슬로, 나는 운 없는 가련한 놈이라네."], ["We have gone through several rounds of purgatory since you left, and I have lately got on to a worse ledge of it than ever.", "자네가 떠난 뒤로 우리는 연옥을 몇 바퀴나 돌았고, 나는 요즘 그 어느 때보다 더 험한 벼랑에 올라서고 말았네."], ["I suppose you are only just come down—you look rather battered—you have not been long enough in the town to hear anything?”", "자네는 이제 막 내려온 모양이군—꽤 지쳐 보이는걸—이 마을에 온 지 얼마 안 되어 아무 소식도 듣지 못했겠지?\""]], [["“I travelled all night and got to the White Hart at eight o’clock this morning.", "\"밤새 여행해서 오늘 아침 여덟 시에 화이트 하트에 닿았습니다."], ["I have been shutting myself up and resting,”", "그 뒤로 들어앉아 쉬고 있었지요.\" 윌이 말했다."], ["said Will, feeling himself a sneak, but seeing no alternative to this evasion.", "자신이 비겁자처럼 느껴졌지만, 이렇게 둘러대는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다."]], [["And then he heard Lydgate’s account of the troubles which Rosamond had already depicted to him in her way.", "이어서 그는 로저먼드가 이미 그녀 나름의 방식으로 그에게 들려준 그 곤경에 대한 리드게이트의 설명을 들었다."], ["She had not mentioned the fact of Will’s name being connected with the public story—this detail not immediately affecting her—and he now heard it for the first time.", "그녀는 윌의 이름이 세간의 이야기와 얽혀 있다는 사실은 언급하지 않았으니—이 세부 사항은 그녀에게 당장 영향을 미치는 것이 아니었기에—그는 이제야 처음으로 그것을 듣게 되었다."]], [["“I thought it better to tell you that your name is mixed up with the disclosures,”", "\"자네 이름이 이번에 폭로된 일들과 뒤섞여 있다는 걸 말해 두는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했네.\""], ["said Lydgate, who could understand better than most men how Ladislaw might be stung by the revelation.", "리드게이트가 말했다. 그는 그 폭로가 래디슬로에게 어떤 아픔을 줄 수 있을지를 대다수 사람들보다 더 잘 헤아릴 수 있었다."], ["“You will be sure to hear it as soon as you turn out into the town.", "\"이 마을로 나가는 순간 틀림없이 듣게 될 테니까."], ["I suppose it is true that Raffles spoke to you.”", "래플스가 자네에게 말을 걸었다는 게 사실인 모양이지.\""]], [["“Yes,” said Will, sardonically.", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["“I shall be fortunate if gossip does not make me the most disreputable person in the whole affair.", "윌이 빈정거리듯 말했다. \"소문이 저를 이번 사건 전체에서 가장 평판 나쁜 인물로 만들지 않는다면 다행이겠지요."], ["I should think the latest version must be, that I plotted with Raffles to murder Bulstrode, and ran away from Middlemarch for the purpose.”", "아마 최신판 이야기는 제가 래플스와 공모해 불스트로드를 살해하려 했고, 그 목적으로 미들마치에서 달아났다는 식이겠지요.\""]], [["He was thinking “Here is a new ring in the sound of my name to recommend it in her hearing; however—what does it signify now?”", "그는 이렇게 생각하고 있었다. \"그녀가 듣는 자리에서 내 이름을 추천해 줄, 또 하나의 새로운 울림이 생긴 셈이군. 하지만—이제 와서 그게 무슨 의미가 있겠는가?\""]], [["But he said nothing of Bulstrode’s offer to him.", "그러나 그는 불스트로드가 자신에게 제안했던 일에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Will was very open and careless about his personal affairs, but it was among the more exquisite touches in nature’s modelling of him that he had a delicate generosity which warned him into reticence here.", "윌은 자기 개인적인 일에 대해서는 매우 거리낌 없고 무심한 편이었지만, 그를 빚어낸 자연의 섬세한 손길 가운데서도 더없이 절묘한 한 가지는 그가 여기서 침묵을 지키도록 일러 주는 섬세한 너그러움을 지녔다는 점이었다."], ["He shrank from saying that he had rejected Bulstrode’s money, in the moment when he was learning that it was Lydgate’s misfortune to have accepted it.", "그 돈을 받아들인 것이 리드게이트의 불운이었음을 알게 된 바로 그 순간에, 자신은 불스트로드의 돈을 거절했노라고 말하기가 그는 꺼려졌다."]], [["Lydgate too was reticent in the midst of his confidence.", "리드게이트 역시 속을 털어놓는 와중에도 말을 아꼈다."], ["He made no allusion to Rosamond’s feeling under their trouble, and of Dorothea he only said, “Mrs.", "그는 이 곤경 앞에서 로저먼드가 느끼는 심정에 대해서는 전혀 내비치지 않았고, 도러시아에 대해서도 그저 이렇게만 말했다."], ["Casaubon has been the one person to come forward and say that she had no belief in any of the suspicions against me.”", "\"나를 향한 그 어떤 의혹도 믿지 않는다고 나서서 말해 준 단 한 사람이 바로 캐소번 부인이라네.\""], ["Observing a change in Will’s face, he avoided any further mention of her, feeling himself too ignorant of their relation to each other not to fear that his words might have some hidden painful bearing on it.", "윌의 표정에 변화가 이는 것을 알아챈 그는, 두 사람의 관계를 너무도 모르는 까닭에 자기 말이 거기에 어떤 숨겨진 아픔을 건드릴까 봐 두려워, 더는 그녀를 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["And it occurred to him that Dorothea was the real cause of the present visit to Middlemarch.", "그리고 도러시아야말로 윌이 지금 미들마치를 찾아온 진짜 이유가 아닐까 하는 생각이 그에게 떠올랐다."]], [["The two men were pitying each other, but it was only Will who guessed the extent of his companion’s trouble.", "두 사람은 서로를 가엾이 여기고 있었지만, 상대의 곤경이 얼마나 깊은지를 짐작한 것은 윌뿐이었다."], ["When Lydgate spoke with desperate resignation of going to settle in London, and said with a faint smile, “We shall have you again, old fellow,”", "리드게이트가 절망적인 체념에 잠겨 런던에 가서 정착하겠노라 말하며 희미하게 미소 띤 채 \"우리는 또 자넬 보게 되겠지, 이 친구야\"라고 했을 때, 윌은 이루 말할 수 없는 슬픔을 느끼며 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Will felt inexpressibly mournful, and said nothing.", "그날 아침 로저먼드는 그에게 리드게이트에게 이 결단을 재촉해 달라고 간청했었다."], ["Rosamond had that morning entreated him to urge this step on Lydgate; and it seemed to him as if he were beholding in a magic panorama a future where he himself was sliding into that pleasureless yielding to the small solicitations of circumstance, which is a commoner history of perdition than any single momentous bargain.", "그리고 그는 마치 어떤 마법의 파노라마 속에서, 자기 자신이 상황의 사소한 유혹들에 즐거움 없이 굴복해 미끄러져 들어가는 미래를 바라보는 듯한 기분이 들었다. 그것은 어떤 단 한 번의 중대한 거래보다도 더 흔한 파멸의 역사인 것이다."]], [["We are on a perilous margin when we begin to look passively at our future selves, and see our own figures led with dull consent into insipid misdoing and shabby achievement.", "우리가 우리의 미래 자아를 수동적으로 바라보기 시작할 때, 그리고 우리 자신의 모습이 무미건조한 잘못과 초라한 성취로 무딘 동의 속에 끌려가는 것을 지켜볼 때, 우리는 위태로운 벼랑 끝에 서 있는 셈이다. 가엾은 리드게이트는 그 벼랑 끝에서 속으로 신음하고 있었고, 윌은 이제 막 그곳에 다다르고 있었다."], ["Poor Lydgate was inwardly groaning on that margin, and Will was arriving at it.", "그날 저녁 그에게는, 로저먼드에게 모질게 터뜨린 그 격정이 자신에게 하나의 의무를 지운 듯 여겨졌고, 그는 그 의무가 두려웠다. 아무것도 의심하지 않는 리드게이트의 선의가 두려웠고, 망쳐 버린 자신의 삶에 대한 자신의 혐오가 두려웠으니, 그 혐오는 그를 아무런 동기도 없는 경박함 속에 내버려 둘 것이었다."], ["It seemed to him this evening as if the cruelty of his outburst to Rosamond had made an obligation for him, and he dreaded the obligation: he dreaded Lydgate’s unsuspecting good-will: he dreaded his own distaste for his spoiled life, which would leave him in motiveless levity.", "* 번연: 존 번연(1628~1688)의 『천로역정』에서 인용한 구절. '절망의 늪'은 작품에 등장하는 상징적 장소다."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXX.", "# CHAPTER LXXX."]], [["Stern lawgiver! yet thou dost wear The Godhead’s most benignant grace; Nor know we anything so fair As is the smile upon thy face; Flowers laugh before thee on their beds, And fragrance in thy footing treads; Thou dost preserve the Stars from wrong; And the most ancient Heavens, through thee, are fresh and strong.", "그대의 얼굴에 어린 미소만큼 아름답지 않도다."], ["—WORDSWORTH: Ode to Duty.", "꽃들은 제 화단에서 그대 앞에 웃음 짓고,"]], [["When Dorothea had seen Mr. Farebrother in the morning, she had promised to go and dine at the parsonage on her return from Freshitt. There was a frequent interchange of visits between her and the Farebrother family, which enabled her to say that she was not at all lonely at the Manor, and to resist for the present the severe prescription of a lady companion. When she reached home and remembered her engagement, she was glad of it; and finding that she had still an hour before she could dress for dinner, she walked straight to the schoolhouse and entered into a conversation with the master and mistress about the new bell, giving eager attention to their small details and repetitions, and getting up a dramatic sense that her life was very busy. She paused on her way back to talk to old Master Bunney who was putting in some garden-seeds, and discoursed wisely with that rural sage about the crops that would make the most return on a perch of ground, and the result of sixty years’ experience as to soils—namely, that if your soil was pretty mellow it would do, but if there came wet, wet, wet to make it all of a mummy, why then—", "향기는 그대의 발걸음을 따라 퍼지노라."]], [["Finding that the social spirit had beguiled her into being rather late, she dressed hastily and went over to the parsonage rather earlier than was necessary. That house was never dull, Mr. Farebrother, like another White of Selborne, having continually something new to tell of his inarticulate guests and proteges, whom he was teaching the boys not to torment; and he had just set up a pair of beautiful goats to be pets of the village in general, and to walk at large as sacred animals. The evening went by cheerfully till after tea, Dorothea talking more than usual and dilating with Mr. Farebrother on the possible histories of creatures that converse compendiously with their antennae, and for aught we know may hold reformed parliaments; when suddenly some inarticulate little sounds were heard which called everybody’s attention.", "그대는 별들을 그릇됨으로부터 지켜 주고,"]], [["“Henrietta Noble,” said Mrs.", "가장 오래된 하늘도 그대를 통해 새롭고 굳건하도다."], ["Farebrother, seeing her small sister moving about the furniture-legs distressfully, “what is the matter?”", "— 워즈워스, 「의무에 부치는 송시」"]], [["“I have lost my tortoise-shell lozenge-box.", "도러시아는 아침에 페어브러더 씨를 만났을 때, 프레시트에서 돌아오는 길에 목사관에 들러 저녁을 함께하겠다고 약속했었다."], ["I fear the kitten has rolled it away,”", "그녀와 페어브러더 가족 사이에는 잦은 왕래가 있었고, 그 덕분에 그녀는 저택에서 전혀 외롭지 않다고 말할 수 있었으며, 당분간은 말동무가 될 부인을 들이라는 엄격한 처방을 물리칠 수 있었다. 집에 도착해 약속을 떠올렸을 때, 그녀는 그 약속이 반가웠다."], ["said the tiny old lady, involuntarily continuing her beaver-like notes. “Is it a great treasure, aunt?”", "그리고 저녁 식사를 위해 옷을 갈아입기까지 아직 한 시간이 남았음을 알자, 곧장 학교 건물로 걸어가 교장 부부와 새 종에 관해 이야기를 나누었으며, 그들의 자질구레한 세부 사항과 거듭되는 말에 열심히 귀를 기울이면서, 자기 삶이 매우 바쁘다는 극적인 느낌을 자아냈다."], ["said Mr. Farebrother, putting up his glasses and looking at the carpet.", "그녀는 돌아오는 길에 발걸음을 멈추고, 텃밭에 씨앗을 심고 있던 늙은 버니 영감과 이야기를 나누었으며, 그 시골의 현자와 함께 한 평의 땅에서 가장 많은 수확을 올릴 수 있는 작물에 관해, 그리고 육십 년 경험으로 얻은 토양에 관한 결론—즉 흙이 어지간히 부드러우면 괜찮지만, 비가 오고 또 오고 또 와서 온통 미라처럼 굳어 버리면, 그땐 이제—에 관해 슬기롭게 담론을 펼쳤다."]], [["“Mr. Ladislaw gave it me,”", "사교적인 기질에 이끌려 다소 늦어졌음을 깨닫고, 그녀는 서둘러 옷을 갈아입고 필요 이상으로 일찍 목사관으로 건너갔다."], ["said Miss Noble. “A German box—very pretty, but if it falls it always spins away as far as it can.”", "그 집은 결코 따분하지 않았으니, 페어브러더 씨는 또 한 사람의 셀번의 화이트처럼, 말 못 하는 손님들이자 자기가 돌보는 피보호자들—소년들에게 괴롭히지 말라고 가르치던 그 생물들—에 관해 늘 들려줄 새로운 이야깃거리를 가지고 있었기 때문이다."], ["“Oh, if it is Ladislaw’s present,” said Mr.", "그는 마침 마을 전체의 귀염둥이가 되어 신성한 동물처럼 마음대로 돌아다니게끔 아름다운 염소 한 쌍을 새로 길들이던 참이었다."], ["Farebrother, in a deep tone of comprehension, getting up and hunting.", "저녁 시간은 차를 마신 뒤까지 유쾌하게 흘러갔고, 도러시아는 평소보다 말을 많이 하며 더듬이로 간략하게 대화를 나누는, 어쩌면 우리가 모르는 사이에 개혁된 의회를 열고 있을지도 모를 생물들의 있을 법한 역사에 관해 페어브러더 씨와 더불어 이야기를 부풀려 나갔다."], ["The box was found at last under a chiffonier, and Miss Noble grasped it with delight, saying, “it was under a fender the last time.”", "그때 갑자기 모두의 주의를 끄는 알아들을 수 없는 작은 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“That is an affair of the heart with my aunt,” said Mr.", "\"헨리에타 노블,\""], ["Farebrother, smiling at Dorothea, as he reseated himself.", "페어브러더 부인이, 가구 다리 사이를 안절부절 헤매고 다니는 자기 작은 여동생을 보며 말했다. \"무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["“If Henrietta Noble forms an attachment to any one, Mrs. Casaubon,”", "\"내 거북 등딱지 사탕갑을 잃어버렸어."], ["said his mother, emphatically,—“she is like a dog—she would take their shoes for a pillow and sleep the better.”", "새끼 고양이가 굴려 버린 게 아닌가 싶구나.\" 그 자그마한 노부인이, 비버처럼 부산스러운 소리를 무심코 계속 내며 말했다."]], [["“Mr. Ladislaw’s shoes, I would,”", "\"그게 그렇게 귀한 보물인가요, 이모?\""], ["said Henrietta Noble.", "페어브러더 씨가 안경을 추켜올리고 카펫을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["Dorothea made an attempt at smiling in return.", "\"래디슬로 씨가 내게 준 거야,\""], ["She was surprised and annoyed to find that her heart was palpitating violently, and that it was quite useless to try after a recovery of her former animation.", "노블 양이 말했다. \"독일제 갑인데—아주 예쁘단다."], ["Alarmed at herself—fearing some further betrayal of a change so marked in its occasion, she rose and said in a low voice with undisguised anxiety, “I must go; I have overtired myself.”", "하지만 떨어지면 늘 갈 수 있는 데까지 빙글빙글 굴러가 버리거든.\""]], [["Mr.", "\"아, 래디슬로의 선물이라면야,\""], ["Farebrother, quick in perception, rose and said, “It is true; you must have half-exhausted yourself in talking about Lydgate.", "페어브러더 씨가 깊이 이해한다는 어조로 말하며, 일어서서 찾기 시작했다. 마침내 그 갑은 작은 장식장 아래에서 발견되었고, 노블 양은 기쁨에 차 그것을 움켜쥐며 말했다."], ["That sort of work tells upon one after the excitement is over.”", "\"지난번엔 난로 가리개 아래 있었지.\""]], [["He gave her his arm back to the Manor, but Dorothea did not attempt to speak, even when he said good-night.", "\"이건 우리 이모님에게는 마음의 문제랍니다,\" 페어브러더 씨가 다시 자리에 앉으며 도러시아를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["The limit of resistance was reached, and she had sunk back helpless within the clutch of inescapable anguish. Dismissing Tantripp with a few faint words, she locked her door, and turning away from it towards the vacant room she pressed her hands hard on the top of her head, and moaned out—", "\"헨리에타 노블이 누군가에게 애착을 품게 되면요, 커소번 부인,\" 그의 어머니가 힘주어 말했다. \"꼭 개와 같답니다. 그 사람 신발을 베개 삼아 베고 더 달게 잠을 잘 거예요.\" \"래디슬로 씨 신발이라면, 그럴 거예요,\" 헨리에타 노블이 말했다."]], [["Then came the hour in which the waves of suffering shook her too thoroughly to leave any power of thought. She could only cry in loud whispers, between her sobs, after her lost belief which she had planted and kept alive from a very little seed since the days in Rome—after her lost joy of clinging with silent love and faith to one who, misprized by others, was worthy in her thought—after her lost woman’s pride of reigning in his memory—after her sweet dim perspective of hope, that along some pathway they should meet with unchanged recognition and take up the backward years as a yesterday.", "도러시아는 화답하여 미소 지으려 애썼다. 그녀는 자기 심장이 격렬하게 두근거리고 있음을, 그리고 예전의 생기를 되찾으려 애써 보아야 아무 소용이 없음을 깨닫고 놀라며 당황했다. 자기 자신에게 경각심을 느끼고—그 변화가 일어난 계기가 그토록 뚜렷한 만큼 더 이상의 누설이 있을까 두려워하며, 그녀는 일어서서 낮은 목소리로 감추지 못한 불안을 담아 말했다. \"이만 가 봐야겠어요. 제가 너무 무리를 했나 봐요.\" 지각이 빠른 페어브러더 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"그렇지요. 리드게이트 이야기를 하느라 기력을 절반은 소진하셨을 겁니다. 그런 일은 흥분이 가라앉고 나면 사람을 지치게 하지요.\""]], [["In that hour she repeated what the merciful eyes of solitude have looked on for ages in the spiritual struggles of man—she besought hardness and coldness and aching weariness to bring her relief from the mysterious incorporeal might of her anguish: she lay on the bare floor and let the night grow cold around her; while her grand woman’s frame was shaken by sobs as if she had been a despairing child.", "그는 팔을 내밀어 그녀를 저택까지 바래다주었지만, 도러시아는 그가 안녕히 주무시라고 인사할 때조차 말을 꺼내려 하지 않았다. 저항의 한계에 다다랐고, 그녀는 피할 길 없는 고뇌의 손아귀에 사로잡힌 채 맥없이 주저앉았다. 몇 마디 힘없는 말로 탠트립을 물린 뒤, 그녀는 방문을 잠그고, 문에서 돌아서서 텅 빈 방 쪽을 향해 두 손으로 머리 꼭대기를 세게 누르며 신음하듯 토해 냈다—"]], [["There were two images—two living forms that tore her heart in two, as if it had been the heart of a mother who seems to see her child divided by the sword, and presses one bleeding half to her breast while her gaze goes forth in agony towards the half which is carried away by the lying woman that has never known the mother’s pang.", "\"오, 나는 정말 그를 사랑했어!\" 그러고 나서 고통의 물결이 그녀를 너무도 철저히 뒤흔들어 사고할 힘조차 남기지 않는 시간이 찾아왔다. 그녀는 흐느낌 사이로 큰 속삭임으로 울부짖을 수밖에 없었다. 로마 시절 이래 아주 작은 씨앗에서부터 심어 살려 온 잃어버린 믿음을 향해—남들에게 멸시받았으나 그녀의 생각으로는 가치 있던 한 사람에게 말없는 사랑과 신뢰로 매달리던, 그 잃어버린 기쁨을 향해—그의 기억 속에서 군림하던, 그 잃어버린 여인으로서의 자긍심을 향해—어느 길 위에서 그들이 변치 않은 알아봄으로 다시 만나 지나간 세월을 어제처럼 이어 가리라던, 그 잃어버린 희망의 감미롭고 아련한 전망을 향해."]], [["Here, with the nearness of an answering smile, here within the vibrating bond of mutual speech, was the bright creature whom she had trusted—who had come to her like the spirit of morning visiting the dim vault where she sat as the bride of a worn-out life; and now, with a full consciousness which had never awakened before, she stretched out her arms towards him and cried with bitter cries that their nearness was a parting vision: she discovered her passion to herself in the unshrinking utterance of despair.", "그 시간에 그녀는 인류의 영적 투쟁 속에서 고독의 자비로운 눈이 오랜 세월 지켜보아 온 일을 되풀이했다—그녀는 신비롭고 형체 없는 고뇌의 위력으로부터 자신을 구해 달라고, 모진 마음과 차가움과 쑤시는 듯한 피로에 간청했다. 그녀는 맨바닥에 누워 밤이 자기 주위로 차갑게 깊어 가도록 내버려 두었고, 그러는 동안 그 의젓한 여인의 몸은 절망에 빠진 어린아이라도 된 듯 흐느낌으로 떨렸다. 거기에는 두 개의 형상—그녀의 심장을 둘로 찢어 놓는 두 개의 살아 있는 모습이 있었으니, 마치 자기 아이가 칼에 두 동강 나는 것을 보는 어머니가, 피 흐르는 한쪽을 가슴에 끌어안으면서도, 어머니의 진통을 결코 알지 못하는 거짓된 여인의 손에 실려 가는 다른 한쪽을 향해 고뇌에 차 시선을 보내는 것과도 같았다."]], [["And there, aloof, yet persistently with her, moving wherever she moved, was the Will Ladislaw who was a changed belief exhausted of hope, a detected illusion—no, a living man towards whom there could not yet struggle any wail of regretful pity, from the midst of scorn and indignation and jealous offended pride.", "여기, 화답하는 미소처럼 가까이, 여기, 서로 주고받는 말의 떨리는 끈 안에, 그녀가 믿었던 그 빛나는 존재가 있었다—다 닳아 버린 삶의 신부로 앉아 있던 그 어슴푸레한 지하 무덤을, 아침의 정령처럼 찾아와 준 그 존재가. 그리고 이제, 한 번도 깨어난 적 없던 충만한 자각과 더불어, 그녀는 그를 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 그들의 가까움이 한낱 이별의 환영일 뿐임을 쓰라린 울음으로 부르짖었다."], ["The fire of Dorothea’s anger was not easily spent, and it flamed out in fitful returns of spurning reproach.", "그녀는 절망의 움츠림 없는 토로 속에서 자신의 열정을 스스로에게 드러냈다."], ["Why had he come obtruding his life into hers, hers that might have been whole enough without him?", "그리고 저편에, 멀리 떨어져 있으면서도 끈질기게 그녀와 함께, 그녀가 움직이는 곳마다 따라 움직이는, 또 한 사람의 윌 래디슬로가 있었으니, 그는 희망이 다 빠져나간 변질된 믿음이요, 들통난 환상이었다—아니, 멸시와 분노와 상처 입은 질투의 자존심 한가운데서, 아직은 그 어떤 회한 어린 연민의 통곡도 솟아날 수 없는, 살아 있는 한 남자였다."], ["Why had he brought his cheap regard and his lip-born words to her who had nothing paltry to give in exchange?", "도러시아의 분노의 불길은 쉬이 사그라지지 않아, 발작처럼 되돌아오는 멸시 어린 비난으로 타올랐다. 어째서 그는 그녀의 삶에—그가 없었어도 충분히 온전했을지 모를 그녀의 삶에—제 삶을 끼워 넣으며 비집고 들어왔던가?"], ["He knew that he was deluding her—wished, in the very moment of farewell, to make her believe that he gave her the whole price of her heart, and knew that he had spent it half before.", "어째서 그는, 대가로 내놓을 변변찮은 것이라곤 아무것도 없던 그녀에게, 자기의 값싼 호의와 입에서만 나온 말들을 가져왔던가? 그는 자기가 그녀를 속이고 있음을 알았다—바로 그 작별의 순간에, 그녀의 마음에 대한 값을 온전히 다 치르는 척하면서, 사실은 그 절반을 이미 써 버렸음을 알고 있었으면서도, 그녀가 그 말을 믿게 하고 싶어 했다."], ["Why had he not stayed among the crowd of whom she asked nothing—but only prayed that they might be less contemptible?", "어째서 그는, 그녀가 아무것도 바라지 않은—다만 그들이 좀 덜 경멸스러운 존재이기를 기도했을 뿐인 그 무리 사이에 그냥 머물러 있지 않았던가?"]], [["But she lost energy at last even for her loud-whispered cries and moans: she subsided into helpless sobs, and on the cold floor she sobbed herself to sleep.", "그러나 마침내 그녀는 큰 속삭임의 울부짖음과 신음을 이어 갈 기력조차 잃었다. 그녀는 맥없는 흐느낌으로 가라앉았고, 차가운 바닥 위에서 흐느끼다가 잠이 들었다."]], [["In the chill hours of the morning twilight, when all was dim around her, she awoke—not with any amazed wondering where she was or what had happened, but with the clearest consciousness that she was looking into the eyes of sorrow.", "새벽 여명의 쌀쌀한 시각, 주위가 온통 어슴푸레할 때, 그녀는 잠에서 깨어났다—여기가 어디인지, 무슨 일이 있었는지 어리둥절해하며 놀란 것이 아니라, 자기가 슬픔의 두 눈을 들여다보고 있다는 더없이 또렷한 자각과 더불어. 그녀는 일어나 따뜻한 것들로 몸을 감싸고, 예전에도 자주 무언가를 지켜보곤 했던 커다란 안락의자에 앉았다."], ["She rose, and wrapped warm things around her, and seated herself in a great chair where she had often watched before.", "그녀는 그 모진 밤을 견디고도 몸이 아프지 않을 만큼 정정했으니, 다소의 쑤심과 피로 말고는 별다른 탈이 없었다. 하지만 그녀는 새로운 상태로 깨어나 있었다."], ["She was vigorous enough to have borne that hard night without feeling ill in body, beyond some aching and fatigue; but she had waked to a new condition: she felt as if her soul had been liberated from its terrible conflict; she was no longer wrestling with her grief, but could sit down with it as a lasting companion and make it a sharer in her thoughts.", "자기 영혼이 그 끔찍한 갈등으로부터 해방된 것 같은 느낌이었다. 더 이상 슬픔과 씨름하는 것이 아니라, 그것을 오래갈 동반자로 곁에 앉히고 자기 생각을 함께 나눌 수 있었다."], ["For now the thoughts came thickly.", "이제 생각들이 빽빽이 밀려들었기 때문이다."], ["It was not in Dorothea’s nature, for longer than the duration of a paroxysm, to sit in the narrow cell of her calamity, in the besotted misery of a consciousness that only sees another’s lot as an accident of its own.", "발작이 지속되는 동안 말고는, 자기 재앙의 비좁은 감방에 틀어박혀, 남의 처지를 한낱 자기 처지의 부수적 사건으로만 바라보는 그 얼빠진 비참함에 잠겨 있는 것은, 도러시아의 천성이 아니었다."]], [["She began now to live through that yesterday morning deliberately again, forcing herself to dwell on every detail and its possible meaning.", "그녀는 이제 그 어제 아침을 다시금 신중하게 처음부터 끝까지 되살려 보기 시작하며, 모든 세부와 그것이 지닐 법한 의미 하나하나에 일부러 머물렀다."], ["Was she alone in that scene? Was it her event only?", "그 장면에 있던 사람이 그녀 혼자였던가?"], ["She forced herself to think of it as bound up with another woman’s life—a woman towards whom she had set out with a longing to carry some clearness and comfort into her beclouded youth.", "그것이 오로지 그녀만의 사건이었던가? 그녀는 그 일을 또 다른 여인의 삶과 얽힌 것으로—흐려진 젊음에 얼마간의 밝음과 위안을 가져다주고 싶다는 갈망을 품고 찾아갔던 그 여인의 삶과 얽힌 것으로 생각하려 애썼다."], ["In her first outleap of jealous indignation and disgust, when quitting the hateful room, she had flung away all the mercy with which she had undertaken that visit.", "질투 어린 분노와 혐오가 처음 솟구쳤을 때, 그 끔찍한 방을 나서며 그녀는 그 방문을 시작하게 했던 모든 자비를 던져 버렸었다."], ["She had enveloped both Will and Rosamond in her burning scorn, and it seemed to her as if Rosamond were burned out of her sight forever.", "그녀는 윌과 로저먼드 둘 다를 자신의 불타는 멸시로 휘감았고, 그녀에게는 로저먼드가 영영 시야에서 타 버린 듯이 여겨졌다."], ["But that base prompting which makes a women more cruel to a rival than to a faithless lover, could have no strength of recurrence in Dorothea when the dominant spirit of justice within her had once overcome the tumult and had once shown her the truer measure of things.", "하지만 여인으로 하여금 변심한 연인보다 연적에게 더 잔인하게 굴게 만드는 그 비열한 충동은, 그녀 안의 지배적인 정의의 정신이 일단 혼란을 이겨 내고 사물의 더 참된 척도를 그녀에게 보여 주고 나자, 도러시아 안에서 다시 되살아날 힘을 가질 수 없었다."], ["All the active thought with which she had before been representing to herself the trials of Lydgate’s lot, and this young marriage union which, like her own, seemed to have its hidden as well as evident troubles—all this vivid sympathetic experience returned to her now as a power: it asserted itself as acquired knowledge asserts itself and will not let us see as we saw in the day of our ignorance.", "전에 그녀가 리드게이트의 처지의 시련을, 그리고 자기 결혼처럼 명백한 고통뿐 아니라 감추어진 고통까지 안고 있는 듯한 그 젊은 부부의 결합을 스스로에게 그려 보며 기울였던 그 모든 능동적인 사유—그 모든 생생하고 공감 어린 경험이 이제 하나의 힘이 되어 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 그것은, 한번 얻은 지식이 스스로를 주장하며 우리가 무지하던 시절에 보던 대로 다시는 보지 못하게 하듯이, 스스로를 주장했다."], ["She said to her own irremediable grief, that it should make her more helpful, instead of driving her back from effort.", "그녀는 돌이킬 수 없는 자신의 슬픔에게, 그 슬픔이 자기를 노력에서 물러나게 몰아붙이는 대신 더욱 남을 도울 수 있게 만들어야 한다고 말했다."]], [["And what sort of crisis might not this be in three lives whose contact with hers laid an obligation on her as if they had been suppliants bearing the sacred branch?", "그리고 그녀와 닿은 인연이, 마치 그들이 신성한 가지를 든 탄원자들이라도 되는 양 그녀에게 의무를 지운 그 세 사람의 삶에, 이것이 어떤 위기가 되지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠는가?"], ["The objects of her rescue were not to be sought out by her fancy: they were chosen for her.", "그녀가 구해야 할 대상들은 그녀의 공상으로 찾아낼 것이 아니었다. 그들은 이미 그녀를 위해 선택되어 있었다."], ["She yearned towards the perfect Right, that it might make a throne within her, and rule her errant will.", "그녀는 완전한 정의를 갈망했으니, 그것이 그녀 안에 옥좌를 세우고 그녀의 길 잃은 의지를 다스려 주기를 바랐다."], ["“What should I do—how should I act now, this very day, if I could clutch my own pain, and compel it to silence, and think of those three?”", "\"내가 만약 나 자신의 고통을 움켜잡아 침묵하게 만들고 저 세 사람을 생각할 수 있다면, 나는 무엇을 해야 할까—오늘, 바로 이날, 어떻게 행동해야 할까?\""]], [["It had taken long for her to come to that question, and there was light piercing into the room.", "그 물음에 이르기까지 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 방 안으로 빛이 뚫고 들어오고 있었다."], ["She opened her curtains, and looked out towards the bit of road that lay in view, with fields beyond outside the entrance-gates.", "그녀는 커튼을 열고, 시야에 들어오는 한 자락 길을, 그 너머 정문 밖의 들판을 바라보았다."], ["On the road there was a man with a bundle on his back and a woman carrying her baby; in the field she could see figures moving—perhaps the shepherd with his dog.", "길 위에는 등에 보따리를 진 남자와 아기를 안은 여자가 있었고, 들판에서는 움직이는 형체들이 보였으니—아마도 개를 데리고 다니는 양치기였으리라."], ["Far off in the bending sky was the pearly light; and she felt the largeness of the world and the manifold wakings of men to labor and endurance.", "멀리 굽은 하늘에는 진주빛 광채가 어려 있었고, 그녀는 세상의 광대함과, 노동과 인내를 향한 사람들의 갖가지 깨어남을 느꼈다."], ["She was a part of that involuntary, palpitating life, and could neither look out on it from her luxurious shelter as a mere spectator, nor hide her eyes in selfish complaining.", "그녀도 그 자기도 모르게 고동치는 삶의 일부였으니, 호화로운 안식처에서 한낱 구경꾼으로 그것을 내다볼 수도, 이기적인 한탄 속에 두 눈을 감출 수도 없었다."]], [["What she would resolve to do that day did not yet seem quite clear, but something that she could achieve stirred her as with an approaching murmur which would soon gather distinctness.", "그날 무엇을 하기로 결심할지는 아직 그리 분명해 보이지 않았으나, 그녀가 이룰 수 있는 어떤 것이, 곧 또렷해질 다가오는 웅성거림처럼 그녀를 움직였다."], ["She took off the clothes which seemed to have some of the weariness of a hard watching in them, and began to make her toilet.", "그녀는 모진 밤샘의 피로가 얼마간 배어든 듯한 옷을 벗고 몸단장을 시작했다."], ["Presently she rang for Tantripp, who came in her dressing-gown.", "이윽고 그녀가 탠트립을 부르는 종을 울리자, 탠트립이 실내복 차림으로 들어왔다."]], [["“Why, madam, you’ve never been in bed this blessed night,”", "\"아니, 마님, 이 좋은 밤을 침대에 들지도 않으셨네요,\""], ["burst out Tantripp, looking first at the bed and then at Dorothea’s face, which in spite of bathing had the pale cheeks and pink eyelids of a mater dolorosa.", "탠트립이 먼저 침대를, 그다음 도러시아의 얼굴을 바라보며 불쑥 말했다. 그 얼굴은 씻었는데도 슬픔의 성모처럼 창백한 두 뺨과 발그레한 눈꺼풀을 하고 있었다."], ["“You’ll kill yourself, you will.", "\"이러다 몸 상하세요, 정말요."], ["Anybody might think now you had a right to give yourself a little comfort.”", "지금쯤은 누구라도 마님께서 스스로에게 자그마한 위안 하나쯤 누리실 자격이 있다고 여길 텐데요.\""]], [["“Don’t be alarmed, Tantripp,”", "\"걱정 말아요, 탠트립,\""], ["said Dorothea, smiling.", "도러시아가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I have slept; I am not ill.", "\"나 잤어요. 아프지 않아요."], ["I shall be glad of a cup of coffee as soon as possible.", "되도록 빨리 커피 한 잔 마실 수 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["And I want you to bring me my new dress; and most likely I shall want my new bonnet to-day.”", "그리고 새 드레스를 가져다줘요. 오늘은 아마 새 보닛도 필요할 것 같아요.\""]], [["“They’ve lain there a month and more ready for you, madam, and most thankful I shall be to see you with a couple o’ pounds’ worth less of crape,”", "\"한 달 넘게 마님을 위해 마련되어 그대로 놓여 있는 것들인데요, 마님. 마님께서 두어 파운드어치 크레이프를 덜 두르신 모습을 뵙게 되다니 더없이 감사할 따름이에요,\""], ["said Tantripp, stooping to light the fire.", "탠트립이 불을 지피려 몸을 굽히며 말했다."], ["“There’s a reason in mourning, as I’ve always said; and three folds at the bottom of your skirt and a plain quilling in your bonnet—and if ever anybody looked like an angel, it’s you in a net quilling—is what’s consistent for a second year.", "\"상복에도 분별이 있어야 하는 법이라고 저는 늘 말해 왔지요. 치맛자락 아래 주름 세 단에 보닛에는 수수한 주름 장식이면—그리고 누가 천사처럼 보인 적이 있다면, 그건 망사 주름 장식을 두르신 마님이세요—두 해째에는 그게 격에 맞는 거예요."], ["At least, that’s my thinking,”", "적어도 제 생각엔 그래요,\""], ["ended Tantripp, looking anxiously at the fire; “and if anybody was to marry me flattering himself I should wear those hijeous weepers two years for him, he’d be deceived by his own vanity, that’s all.”", "탠트립이 불을 근심스레 바라보며 말을 맺었다. \"그리고 누가 저랑 결혼하면서 제가 그 흉측한 상복용 소맷동을 자기를 위해 두 해나 두르리라고 우쭐댄다면, 그건 제 허영에 제가 속는 거예요, 그뿐이지요.\""]], [["“The fire will do, my good Tan,”", "\"불은 그만하면 됐어요, 착한 탠,\""], ["said Dorothea, speaking as she used to do in the old Lausanne days, only with a very low voice; “get me the coffee.”", "도러시아가 옛 로잔 시절에 그러던 것처럼, 다만 아주 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"커피나 갖다줘요.\""]], [["She folded herself in the large chair, and leaned her head against it in fatigued quiescence, while Tantripp went away wondering at this strange contrariness in her young mistress—that just the morning when she had more of a widow’s face than ever, she should have asked for her lighter mourning which she had waived before.", "그녀는 커다란 안락의자에 몸을 파묻고, 지친 정적 속에 머리를 등받이에 기댔다. 그러는 동안 탠트립은 자기 젊은 안주인의 이 이상한 모순됨에—그 어느 때보다 더 미망인다운 얼굴을 한 바로 그날 아침에, 전에는 마다했던 더 가벼운 상복을 청하다니—의아해하며 물러갔다."], ["Tantripp would never have found the clew to this mystery.", "탠트립으로서는 이 수수께끼의 실마리를 결코 찾지 못했으리라."], ["Dorothea wished to acknowledge that she had not the less an active life before her because she had buried a private joy; and the tradition that fresh garments belonged to all initiation, haunting her mind, made her grasp after even that slight outward help towards calm resolve.", "도러시아는 은밀한 기쁨 하나를 묻었다고 해서 자기 앞에 놓인 능동적인 삶이 줄어든 것은 아님을 스스로 인정하고 싶었다. 그리고 새로운 옷이 모든 입문에 따르는 것이라는 그 전통이 그녀의 마음에 어른거렸기에, 그녀는 차분한 결심을 향한 그 사소한 외적 도움이나마 붙잡으려 했다."], ["For the resolve was not easy.", "그 결심은 쉽지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["Nevertheless at eleven o’clock she was walking towards Middlemarch, having made up her mind that she would make as quietly and unnoticeably as possible her second attempt to see and save Rosamond.", "그럼에도 열한 시에 그녀는 미들마치를 향해 걷고 있었으니, 되도록 조용히, 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 로저먼드를 만나 구하려는 두 번째 시도를 하기로 마음을 굳힌 뒤였다. * 슬픔의 성모: 원문 'mater dolorosa'. 그리스도의 수난을 슬퍼하는 성모 마리아를 가리키는 라틴어 표현."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXXI.", "# CHAPTER LXXXI."]], [["Du Erde warst auch diese Nacht beständig, Und athmest neu erquickt zu meinen Füssen, Beginnest schon mit Lust mich zu umgeben, Du regst und rührst ein kräftiges Beschliessen Zum höchsten Dasein immerfort zu streben.", "분투하려는 굳센 결의를 일깨우고 흔드는구나."], ["—Faust: 2r Theil.", "—『파우스트』 제2부."]], [["When Dorothea was again at Lydgate’s door speaking to Martha, he was in the room close by with the door ajar, preparing to go out.", "도러시아가 다시 리드게이트의 집 문 앞에서 마사에게 말을 건네고 있을 때, 그는 문이 빠끔히 열린 바로 옆방에서 외출 채비를 하고 있었다."], ["He heard her voice, and immediately came to her.", "그녀의 목소리를 듣자 그는 곧바로 그녀에게로 나왔다."]], [["“Do you think that Mrs. Lydgate can receive me this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침에 리드게이트 부인이 저를 만나 주실 수 있을까요?\""], ["she said, having reflected that it would be better to leave out all allusion to her previous visit.", "그녀가 말했다. 어제 방문에 관한 언급은 일절 하지 않는 편이 좋겠다고 생각한 터였다."]], [["“I have no doubt she will,”", "\"틀림없이 만날 겁니다.\""], ["said Lydgate, suppressing his thought about Dorothea’s looks, which were as much changed as Rosamond’s, “if you will be kind enough to come in and let me tell her that you are here.", "리드게이트가 말했다. 도러시아의 안색도 로저먼드만큼이나 달라졌다는 생각이 떠올랐으나 그는 그것을 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["She has not been very well since you were here yesterday, but she is better this morning, and I think it is very likely that she will be cheered by seeing you again.”", "\"안으로 들어오셔서 제가 아내에게 부인이 오셨다는 말을 전하게 해 주신다면요. 어제 다녀가신 뒤로 아내가 그리 좋지 않았습니다만, 오늘 아침에는 한결 나아졌으니, 다시 부인을 뵈면 기운을 차릴 가능성이 아주 높을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["It was plain that Lydgate, as Dorothea had expected, knew nothing about the circumstances of her yesterday’s visit; nay, he appeared to imagine that she had carried it out according to her intention.", "도러시아가 예상했던 대로 리드게이트는 어제 그녀가 방문했던 정황에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것이 분명했다. 아니, 그는 그녀가 본래 의도했던 대로 일을 마치고 갔으리라 짐작하는 듯 보였다."], ["She had prepared a little note asking Rosamond to see her, which she would have given to the servant if he had not been in the way, but now she was in much anxiety as to the result of his announcement.", "그녀는 로저먼드에게 만나 달라고 청하는 짧은 쪽지를 미리 준비해 두었고, 그가 거기 없었더라면 하인에게 그것을 건넸을 터였지만, 이제는 그가 자기 방문을 알린 결과가 어찌 될지 몹시 불안했다."]], [["After leading her into the drawing-room, he paused to take a letter from his pocket and put it into her hands, saying, “I wrote this last night, and was going to carry it to Lowick in my ride.", "그녀를 응접실로 안내한 뒤 그는 잠시 멈춰 호주머니에서 편지 한 통을 꺼내 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"어젯밤에 이것을 썼는데, 말을 타고 나가는 길에 로윅으로 가져갈 참이었습니다."], ["When one is grateful for something too good for common thanks, writing is less unsatisfactory than speech—one does not at least hear how inadequate the words are.”", "흔한 감사의 말로는 다 갚을 수 없을 만큼 너무도 과분한 것에 고마워할 때는, 말보다 글이 덜 답답한 법이지요. 적어도 그 말이 얼마나 모자란지를 귀로 듣지는 않아도 되니까요.\""]], [["Dorothea’s face brightened.", "도러시아의 얼굴이 밝아졌다."], ["“It is I who have most to thank for, since you have let me take that place.", "\"정작 고마워해야 할 사람은 저예요. 제게 그 역할을 맡게 해 주셨으니까요."], ["You have consented?”", "정말 승낙하신 거지요?\""], ["she said, suddenly doubting.", "그녀가 갑자기 의심이 들어 물었다."]], [["“Yes, the check is going to Bulstrode to-day.”", "\"네, 오늘 수표가 불스트로드에게 갈 겁니다.\""]], [["He said no more, but went up-stairs to Rosamond, who had but lately finished dressing herself, and sat languidly wondering what she should do next, her habitual industry in small things, even in the days of her sadness, prompting her to begin some kind of occupation, which she dragged through slowly or paused in from lack of interest.", "그는 더는 말하지 않고 위층의 로저먼드에게로 올라갔다. 그녀는 막 옷차림을 끝내고 나른하게 앉아 다음으로 무엇을 할까 궁리하고 있었다."], ["She looked ill, but had recovered her usual quietude of manner, and Lydgate had feared to disturb her by any questions.", "슬픔에 잠긴 나날에도 사소한 일들에 부지런히 손을 놀리는 평소의 습성 탓에 무언가 일거리를 시작하긴 했으나, 흥미가 일지 않아 더디게 끌고 가거나 도중에 멈춰 버리곤 했다. 그녀는 아파 보였지만 평소의 차분한 태도는 되찾은 상태였고, 리드게이트는 어떤 질문으로도 그녀의 마음을 어지럽힐까 두려워했다."], ["He had told her of Dorothea’s letter containing the check, and afterwards he had said, “Ladislaw is come, Rosy; he sat with me last night; I dare say he will be here again to-day.", "그는 그녀에게 수표가 든 도러시아의 편지에 관해 이야기했고, 그러고 나서 이렇게 말했다. \"래디슬로가 돌아왔어, 로지."], ["I thought he looked rather battered and depressed.”", "어젯밤에 나와 함께 앉아 있었지. 아마 오늘 또 들를 거야."], ["And Rosamond had made no reply.", "좀 지치고 풀이 죽어 보이더군.\" 그러나 로저먼드는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["Now, when he came up, he said to her very gently, “Rosy, dear, Mrs. Casaubon is come to see you again; you would like to see her, would you not?” That she colored and gave rather a startled movement did not surprise him after the agitation produced by the interview yesterday—a beneficent agitation, he thought, since it seemed to have made her turn to him again.", "이제 위층으로 올라온 그는 그녀에게 아주 다정하게 말했다. \"로지, 캐소본 부인이 당신을 또 보러 오셨어. 만나 보고 싶지 않아?\" 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히며 다소 놀란 듯 움찔하는 것을 보고도 그는 놀라지 않았다. 어제의 면담이 일으킨 동요 뒤끝이려니 여긴 것이다. 그것은 이로운 동요라고, 그는 생각했다. 그 덕분에 그녀가 다시 자기에게로 마음을 돌린 듯했으니까."]], [["Rosamond dared not say no.", "로저먼드는 감히 싫다고 말하지 못했다."], ["She dared not with a tone of her voice touch the facts of yesterday.", "목소리의 한 가락으로라도 어제의 사실을 건드릴 엄두가 나지 않았다."], ["Why had Mrs. Casaubon come again?", "캐소본 부인은 어찌하여 또 온 것일까?"], ["The answer was a blank which Rosamond could only fill up with dread, for Will Ladislaw’s lacerating words had made every thought of Dorothea a fresh smart to her.", "그 답은 텅 빈 공백이었고, 로저먼드는 그것을 오직 두려움으로만 채울 수 있었다. 윌 래디슬로의 가슴을 찢는 말들이 도러시아에 관한 온갖 생각을 새로운 아픔으로 바꿔 놓았기 때문이다."], ["Nevertheless, in her new humiliating uncertainty she dared do nothing but comply.", "그럼에도 굴욕에 찬 이 새로운 불확실함 속에서 그녀는 따르는 것 말고는 감히 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["She did not say yes, but she rose and let Lydgate put a light shawl over her shoulders, while he said, “I am going out immediately.”", "그녀는 그러겠다고 말하진 않았지만 자리에서 일어나 리드게이트가 어깨에 가벼운 숄을 둘러 주도록 내버려 두었고, 그러는 동안 그는 \"난 곧 나갈 거야\"라고 말했다."], ["Then something crossed her mind which prompted her to say, “Pray tell Martha not to bring any one else into the drawing-room.”", "그때 무언가가 그녀의 머리를 스쳐, 그녀는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다. \"마사에게 응접실로는 아무도 들이지 말라고 일러 줘요.\""], ["And Lydgate assented, thinking that he fully understood this wish.", "리드게이트는 이 바람을 충분히 이해한다고 여기며 그러마고 했다."], ["He led her down to the drawing-room door, and then turned away, observing to himself that he was rather a blundering husband to be dependent for his wife’s trust in him on the influence of another woman.", "그는 그녀를 응접실 문까지 데려다주고는 발길을 돌리며, 아내가 자기를 신뢰하도록 만드는 일을 다른 여자의 영향력에 기대야 하는 자신은 꽤나 서툰 남편이라고 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["Rosamond, wrapping her soft shawl around her as she walked towards Dorothea, was inwardly wrapping her soul in cold reserve.", "로저먼드는 도러시아를 향해 걸어가며 부드러운 숄을 몸에 두르는 한편, 마음속으로는 자신의 영혼을 차가운 자제로 감싸고 있었다."], ["Had Mrs. Casaubon come to say anything to her about Will?", "캐소본 부인이 윌에 관해 무슨 말을 하러 온 것일까?"], ["If so, it was a liberty that Rosamond resented; and she prepared herself to meet every word with polite impassibility.", "만약 그렇다면 그것은 로저먼드가 분개할 만한 무례였다. 그래서 그녀는 어떤 말에도 정중한 무감각으로 응대하리라 마음을 다잡았다."], ["Will had bruised her pride too sorely for her to feel any compunction towards him and Dorothea: her own injury seemed much the greater.", "윌이 그녀의 자존심을 너무도 모질게 짓밟아 놓아, 그녀는 그와 도러시아에게 어떤 가책도 느낄 수 없었다."], ["Dorothea was not only the “preferred”", "자신이 입은 상처가 훨씬 더 큰 것만 같았다."], ["woman, but had also a formidable advantage in being Lydgate’s benefactor; and to poor Rosamond’s pained confused vision it seemed that this Mrs.", "도러시아는 단지 \"선택받은\" 여자일 뿐 아니라, 리드게이트의 은인이라는 무시무시한 이점까지 지니고 있었다."], ["Casaubon—this woman who predominated in all things concerning her—must have come now with the sense of having the advantage, and with animosity prompting her to use it.", "가엾은 로저먼드의 고통스럽고 혼란스러운 눈에는, 자신과 관련된 모든 일에서 우위를 점하는 이 캐소본 부인이라는 여자가, 이제 유리한 처지에 있다는 자각과 그것을 이용하라 부추기는 적의를 품고 찾아온 것만 같았다."], ["Indeed, not Rosamond only, but any one else, knowing the outer facts of the case, and not the simple inspiration on which Dorothea acted, might well have wondered why she came.", "사실 로저먼드뿐 아니라, 이 사건의 겉으로 드러난 사실들만 알고 도러시아가 따른 그 단순한 마음의 충동을 알지 못하는 사람이라면 누구라도, 그녀가 왜 왔는지 의아해할 만했다."]], [["Looking like the lovely ghost of herself, her graceful slimness wrapped in her soft white shawl, the rounded infantine mouth and cheek inevitably suggesting mildness and innocence, Rosamond paused at three yards’ distance from her visitor and bowed.", "마치 제 모습의 사랑스러운 유령 같은 모습으로, 우아하고 가냘픈 몸을 부드러운 흰 숄로 감싸고, 둥글고 앳된 입매와 뺨이 어쩔 수 없이 온순함과 순결함을 떠올리게 하는 채로, 로저먼드는 손님으로부터 세 야드쯤 떨어진 자리에 멈춰 서서 고개를 숙였다."], ["But Dorothea, who had taken off her gloves, from an impulse which she could never resist when she wanted a sense of freedom, came forward, and with her face full of a sad yet sweet openness, put out her hand.", "그러나 자유로움을 느끼고 싶을 때면 결코 억누를 수 없는 충동에서 장갑을 벗어 둔 도러시아는, 슬프면서도 다정한 솔직함이 가득한 얼굴로 앞으로 나서며 손을 내밀었다. 로저먼드는 그 눈길을 마주하지 않을 수 없었고, 자신의 작은 손을 도러시아의 손에 맡기지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 손은 부드러운 모성으로 그것을 감싸 쥐었다."], ["Rosamond could not avoid meeting her glance, could not avoid putting her small hand into Dorothea’s, which clasped it with gentle motherliness; and immediately a doubt of her own prepossessions began to stir within her.", "그러자 곧바로 자기 선입견에 대한 의구심이 그녀의 내면에서 꿈틀대기 시작했다. 로저먼드는 얼굴을 살피는 데 빠른 눈을 지녔던지라, 캐소본 부인의 얼굴이 어제보다 창백하고 달라졌으면서도 부드러운 것이, 그 손의 굳건한 온화함과 닮았다는 것을 알아보았다."], ["Rosamond’s eye was quick for faces; she saw that Mrs.", "그러나 도러시아는 자신의 기력을 조금 과신했던 터였다."], ["Casaubon’s face looked pale and changed since yesterday, yet gentle, and like the firm softness of her hand.", "그날 아침 그녀의 정신 작용이 보인 명료함과 강렬함은, 그녀의 몸을 가장 고운 베네치아 수정처럼 위태로울 만큼 예민하게 만든 신경의 고양이 이어진 것이었다."], ["But Dorothea had counted a little too much on her own strength: the clearness and intensity of her mental action this morning were the continuance of a nervous exaltation which made her frame as dangerously responsive as a bit of finest Venetian crystal; and in looking at Rosamond, she suddenly found her heart swelling, and was unable to speak—all her effort was required to keep back tears.", "그리하여 로저먼드를 바라보던 그녀는 갑자기 가슴이 북받쳐 오르는 것을 느끼며 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["She succeeded in that, and the emotion only passed over her face like the spirit of a sob; but it added to Rosamond’s impression that Mrs.", "눈물을 참는 데 온 힘이 필요했다. 그녀는 가까스로 그 일에 성공했고, 그 감정은 흐느낌의 혼령처럼 얼굴 위로 스쳐 갔을 뿐이었다."], ["Casaubon’s state of mind must be something quite different from what she had imagined.", "하지만 그것은 캐소본 부인의 심경이 자신이 상상했던 것과는 사뭇 다른 무언가일 것이라는 로저먼드의 인상을 한층 더 굳혀 주었다."]], [["So they sat down without a word of preface on the two chairs that happened to be nearest, and happened also to be close together; though Rosamond’s notion when she first bowed was that she should stay a long way off from Mrs.", "그리하여 두 사람은 한마디 서두도 없이 마침 가장 가까이에 있던, 그리고 마침 서로 바짝 붙어 있던 두 의자에 앉았다. 처음 고개를 숙였을 때 로저먼드는 캐소본 부인에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 있어야겠다고 생각했었는데도 말이다."], ["Casaubon. But she ceased thinking how anything would turn out—merely wondering what would come.", "그러나 그녀는 어떤 일이 어찌 될지 따지기를 그치고, 그저 무슨 일이 닥칠지 궁금해하기만 했다."], ["And Dorothea began to speak quite simply, gathering firmness as she went on.", "그리고 도러시아는 아주 담담하게 말문을 열어, 이야기를 이어 갈수록 단단해져 갔다."]], [["“I had an errand yesterday which I did not finish; that is why I am here again so soon.", "\"어제 다 마치지 못한 볼일이 있었어요. 그래서 이렇게 곧바로 다시 찾아온 거예요."], ["You will not think me too troublesome when I tell you that I came to talk to you about the injustice that has been shown towards Mr.", "제가 부인을 너무 성가시게 한다고 여기지 않으시겠지요. 리드게이트 씨에게 가해진 부당한 처사에 관해 부인과 이야기하러 왔다고 말씀드리면요."], ["Lydgate.", "부인께 위안이 되지 않을까요?"], ["It will cheer you—will it not?—to know a great deal about him, that he may not like to speak about himself just because it is in his own vindication and to his own honor.", "그분에 관해 많은 것을 아시는 게요. 정작 자기 변호가 되고 자기 명예가 걸린 일이라는 바로 그 이유로 그분 자신은 입에 올리고 싶어 하지 않을 그런 것들 말이에요."], ["You will like to know that your husband has warm friends, who have not left off believing in his high character?", "부군에게는 그분의 고결한 인품에 대한 믿음을 저버리지 않은 따뜻한 벗들이 있다는 것을 아시면 기쁘지 않으시겠어요?"], ["You will let me speak of this without thinking that I take a liberty?”", "제가 주제넘게 군다고 여기지 마시고 이 이야기를 하게 해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["The cordial, pleading tones which seemed to flow with generous heedlessness above all the facts which had filled Rosamond’s mind as grounds of obstruction and hatred between her and this woman, came as soothingly as a warm stream over her shrinking fears. Of course Mrs.", "로저먼드의 마음을, 자신과 이 여자 사이를 가로막고 미움을 부추기는 근거로 가득 채웠던 그 모든 사실들 위로, 너그러운 무심함과 함께 흘러나오는 듯한 그 다정하고 간곡한 어조는, 움츠러든 그녀의 두려움 위로 따뜻한 물줄기처럼 어루만지듯 다가왔다."], ["Casaubon had the facts in her mind, but she was not going to speak of anything connected with them.", "물론 캐소본 부인은 그 사실들을 마음에 두고 있었으나, 그와 관련된 어떤 것도 말하려 하지 않았다."], ["That relief was too great for Rosamond to feel much else at the moment.", "그 안도감이 너무 커서 로저먼드는 그 순간 다른 것을 거의 느낄 수 없었다."], ["She answered prettily, in the new ease of her soul—", "그녀는 마음이 새롭게 편안해진 가운데 곱게 대답했다."]], [["“I know you have been very good.", "\"부인이 무척 친절하셨다는 것을 알아요."], ["I shall like to hear anything you will say to me about Tertius.”", "터셔스에 관해 제게 해 주실 말씀이라면 무엇이든 듣고 싶어요.\""]], [["“The day before yesterday,”", "\"그저께,\" 도러시아가 말했다."], ["said Dorothea, “when I had asked him to come to Lowick to give me his opinion on the affairs of the Hospital, he told me everything about his conduct and feelings in this sad event which has made ignorant people cast suspicions on him.", "\"제가 병원 일에 관한 의견을 듣고 싶어 그분께 로윅으로 와 달라고 청했을 때, 그분은 무지한 사람들이 그분에게 의혹을 던지게 만든 이 슬픈 사건에서 자신이 어떻게 처신했고 어떤 심정이었는지를 제게 죄다 이야기해 주셨어요."], ["The reason he told me was because I was very bold and asked him.", "그분이 제게 말씀하신 까닭은 제가 아주 당돌하게 여쭈었기 때문이에요."], ["I believed that he had never acted dishonorably, and I begged him to tell me the history.", "저는 그분이 결코 부정한 행동을 한 적이 없다고 믿었고, 그래서 그분께 그 자초지종을 들려 달라고 청했어요."], ["He confessed to me that he had never told it before, not even to you, because he had a great dislike to say, ‘I was not wrong,’ as if that were proof, when there are guilty people who will say so.", "그분은 전에는 한 번도, 심지어 부인께도 그 이야기를 한 적이 없노라고 제게 털어놓으셨어요. 죄 지은 사람들도 그렇게 말하는 마당에, 마치 그것이 증거라도 되는 양 '나는 잘못이 없다'고 말하기가 무척 싫었기 때문이래요."], ["The truth is, he knew nothing of this man Raffles, or that there were any bad secrets about him; and he thought that Mr.", "사실인즉슨, 그분은 이 래플스라는 사람에 관해 아무것도, 그에게 어떤 나쁜 비밀이 있는지도 전혀 몰랐어요."], ["Bulstrode offered him the money because he repented, out of kindness, of having refused it before.", "그리고 불스트로드 씨가 자기에게 돈을 내준 것은 전에 그것을 거절했던 일을 뉘우치고 호의에서 그러는 것이라고 여겼지요."], ["All his anxiety about his patient was to treat him rightly, and he was a little uncomfortable that the case did not end as he had expected; but he thought then and still thinks that there may have been no wrong in it on any one’s part.", "환자에 관한 그분의 온 관심은 그를 올바르게 치료하는 데 있었고, 그 사례가 자신이 예상했던 대로 끝나지 않은 데 다소 마음이 편치 않았어요. 하지만 그분은 그때도, 그리고 지금도, 거기에 누구의 잘못도 없었을 수 있다고 생각하세요."], ["And I have told Mr. Farebrother, and Mr.", "그리고 저는 페어브러더 씨와 브룩 씨와 제임스 체텀 경에게 이야기했어요."], ["Brooke, and Sir James Chettam: they all believe in your husband.", "그들 모두 부군을 믿고 있어요."], ["That will cheer you, will it not?", "그러면 부인께 위안이 되지 않겠어요?"], ["That will give you courage?”", "그러면 부인께 용기가 생기지 않겠어요?\""]], [["Dorothea’s face had become animated, and as it beamed on Rosamond very close to her, she felt something like bashful timidity before a superior, in the presence of this self-forgetful ardor.", "도러시아의 얼굴은 생기를 띠었고, 그것이 아주 가까이에서 로저먼드를 향해 빛나자, 그녀는 이 자기 망각의 열정 앞에서 윗사람을 대하는 듯한 수줍은 머뭇거림 비슷한 것을 느꼈다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 어색하게 말했다."], ["She said, with blushing embarrassment, “Thank you: you are very kind.”", "\"고맙습니다. 무척 친절하시네요.\""]], [["“And he felt that he had been so wrong not to pour out everything about this to you.", "\"그리고 그분은 이 일에 관해 모든 것을 부인께 쏟아 내지 않은 것이 너무도 잘못이었다고 느끼셨어요."], ["But you will forgive him.", "하지만 부인은 그분을 용서하실 거예요."], ["It was because he feels so much more about your happiness than anything else—he feels his life bound into one with yours, and it hurts him more than anything, that his misfortunes must hurt you.", "그것은 그분이 다른 무엇보다 부인의 행복을 훨씬 더 마음에 두기 때문이었어요. 그분은 자기 삶이 부인의 삶과 하나로 묶여 있다고 느끼시고, 자신의 불운이 부인을 아프게 할 수밖에 없다는 것을 무엇보다 가슴 아파하세요."], ["He could speak to me because I am an indifferent person.", "그분이 제게는 말씀하실 수 있었던 것은 제가 아무 상관 없는 사람이기 때문이에요."], ["And then I asked him if I might come to see you; because I felt so much for his trouble and yours.", "그러고 나서 저는 그분께 부인을 뵈러 와도 되겠느냐고 여쭈었어요. 그분의 괴로움과 부인의 괴로움이 너무도 마음에 사무쳤거든요."], ["That is why I came yesterday, and why I am come to-day.", "그래서 제가 어제 찾아온 것이고, 오늘 또 온 거예요."], ["Trouble is so hard to bear, is it not?— How can we live and think that any one has trouble—piercing trouble—and we could help them, and never try?”", "괴로움이란 견디기가 참 힘든 것이지요, 그렇지 않나요? 누군가가 괴로움을, 가슴을 에는 괴로움을 겪고 있는데 우리가 도울 수 있으면서도 결코 애쓰지 않는다는 것을, 어찌 알면서 살아갈 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["Dorothea, completely swayed by the feeling that she was uttering, forgot everything but that she was speaking from out the heart of her own trial to Rosamond’s.", "자신이 토해 내는 그 감정에 온전히 사로잡힌 도러시아는, 자기 자신의 시련의 한복판에서 로저먼드의 시련을 향해 이야기하고 있다는 것 말고는 모든 것을 잊어버렸다."], ["The emotion had wrought itself more and more into her utterance, till the tones might have gone to one’s very marrow, like a low cry from some suffering creature in the darkness.", "그 격정이 점점 더 그녀의 말에 스며들어, 이윽고 그 어조는 어둠 속에서 고통받는 어떤 생명의 나직한 울부짖음처럼 듣는 이의 골수에까지 사무칠 만했다."], ["And she had unconsciously laid her hand again on the little hand that she had pressed before.", "그리고 그녀는 자기도 모르게, 앞서 꼭 쥐었던 그 작은 손 위에 다시 손을 얹고 있었다."]], [["Rosamond, with an overmastering pang, as if a wound within her had been probed, burst into hysterical crying as she had done the day before when she clung to her husband.", "로저먼드는, 마치 내면의 상처를 후벼 판 것 같은 사무치는 아픔에 사로잡혀, 어제 남편에게 매달렸을 때와 똑같이 발작적인 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Poor Dorothea was feeling a great wave of her own sorrow returning over her—her thought being drawn to the possible share that Will Ladislaw might have in Rosamond’s mental tumult.", "가엾은 도러시아는 자기 자신의 슬픔의 거대한 물결이 다시 자기 위로 밀려드는 것을 느끼고 있었다. 그녀의 생각이 로저먼드의 심란함에 윌 래디슬로가 어쩌면 한몫 했을지도 모른다는 데로 이끌렸던 것이다."], ["She was beginning to fear that she should not be able to suppress herself enough to the end of this meeting, and while her hand was still resting on Rosamond’s lap, though the hand underneath it was withdrawn, she was struggling against her own rising sobs.", "그녀는 이 만남이 끝날 때까지 자신을 충분히 억누를 수 없을까 봐 두려워지기 시작했고, 그녀의 손은 여전히 로저먼드의 무릎 위에 놓여 있었으나—그 밑에 있던 손은 빠져나가고 없었다—치밀어 오르는 자신의 흐느낌과 맞서 싸우고 있었다."], ["She tried to master herself with the thought that this might be a turning-point in three lives—not in her own; no, there the irrevocable had happened, but—in those three lives which were touching hers with the solemn neighborhood of danger and distress.", "그녀는 이것이 세 사람의 인생에서, 자기 자신의 인생이 아니라—아니, 자기 인생에서는 이미 돌이킬 수 없는 일이 일어났으니—위험과 고통의 엄숙한 이웃으로 자기 삶을 스치고 있는 그 세 사람의 인생에서 하나의 전환점일지도 모른다는 생각으로 자신을 다스리려 애썼다. 자기 곁에서 울고 있는 이 연약한 존재를, 어긋나고 어울리지 않는 결속의 비참함에서 구해 낼 시간이 어쩌면 아직 남아 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["The fragile creature who was crying close to her—there might still be time to rescue her from the misery of false incompatible bonds; and this moment was unlike any other: she and Rosamond could never be together again with the same thrilling consciousness of yesterday within them both.", "그리고 이 순간은 다른 어떤 순간과도 달랐다. 그녀와 로저먼드가 두 사람 모두 어제의 그 짜릿한 자각을 안은 채 다시 함께 있게 되는 일은 결코 없을 터였다."], ["She felt the relation between them to be peculiar enough to give her a peculiar influence, though she had no conception that the way in which her own feelings were involved was fully known to Mrs. Lydgate.", "그녀는 둘 사이의 관계가, 비록 자신의 감정이 어떤 식으로 얽혀 있는지를 리드게이트 부인이 죄다 알고 있다고는 짐작조차 못 했지만, 자신에게 특별한 영향력을 줄 만큼 충분히 특이하다고 느꼈다."]], [["It was a newer crisis in Rosamond’s experience than even Dorothea could imagine: she was under the first great shock that had shattered her dream-world in which she had been easily confident of herself and critical of others; and this strange unexpected manifestation of feeling in a woman whom she had approached with a shrinking aversion and dread, as one who must necessarily have a jealous hatred towards her, made her soul totter all the more with a sense that she had been walking in an unknown world which had just broken in upon her.", "그것은 도러시아가 상상할 수 있는 것보다도 더 새로운 위기였다. 로저먼드는 자기 자신을 손쉽게 자신하고 남들을 비판하며 지내던 자신의 꿈의 세계를 산산이 부순 첫 번째 큰 충격을 겪고 있었다. 그리고 자기에게 필연코 질투에 찬 미움을 품고 있으리라 여겨 움츠러드는 혐오와 두려움으로 다가갔던 한 여자에게서 느껴지는 이 기이하고 뜻밖의 감정의 발로는, 자신이 이제 막 들이닥친 미지의 세계를 거닐고 있었다는 자각과 더불어 그녀의 영혼을 한층 더 휘청거리게 만들었다."]], [["When Rosamond’s convulsed throat was subsiding into calm, and she withdrew the handkerchief with which she had been hiding her face, her eyes met Dorothea’s as helplessly as if they had been blue flowers.", "로저먼드의 경련하던 목이 가라앉아 잠잠해지고, 얼굴을 가리고 있던 손수건을 거두었을 때, 그녀의 눈은 마치 푸른 꽃송이라도 되는 양 속절없이 도러시아의 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["What was the use of thinking about behavior after this crying?", "이렇게 울고 난 뒤에 처신을 따져 무엇하겠는가?"], ["And Dorothea looked almost as childish, with the neglected trace of a silent tear.", "그리고 도러시아도 소리 없이 흘린 눈물 자국을 미처 지우지 못한 채, 거의 그만큼이나 어린아이 같아 보였다."], ["Pride was broken down between these two.", "이 두 사람 사이에서 자존심은 허물어져 있었다."]], [["“We were talking about your husband,”", "\"우리는 부군 이야기를 하고 있었지요.\""], ["Dorothea said, with some timidity.", "도러시아가 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“I thought his looks were sadly changed with suffering the other day.", "\"요전 날 그분의 모습이 괴로움으로 몹시 달라져 보였어요."], ["I had not seen him for many weeks before.", "그 전 여러 주 동안 그분을 뵙지 못했었거든요."], ["He said he had been feeling very lonely in his trial; but I think he would have borne it all better if he had been able to be quite open with you.”", "그분은 이 시련 속에서 무척 외로움을 느껴 왔다고 하셨어요. 하지만 부인께 모든 것을 활짝 털어놓을 수 있었더라면 이 모든 것을 한결 잘 견뎌 내셨으리라 생각해요.\""]], [["“Tertius is so angry and impatient if I say anything,”", "\"터셔스는 제가 무슨 말이라도 하면 어찌나 화를 내고 못 참아 하는지요.\""], ["said Rosamond, imagining that he had been complaining of her to Dorothea.", "로저먼드가, 그가 도러시아에게 자기 험담을 했으리라 짐작하며 말했다."], ["“He ought not to wonder that I object to speak to him on painful subjects.”", "\"제가 그이에게 괴로운 이야기를 하기 싫어하는 걸 의아해하면 안 되지요.\""]], [["“It was himself he blamed for not speaking,”", "\"그분은 말을 하지 않은 자기 자신을 탓하셨어요.\" 도러시아가 말했다."], ["said Dorothea.", "\"부인에 관해 하신 말씀은, 부인을 불행하게 만드는 일은 무엇이든 자신은 그것을 하면서 행복할 수 없다는 것, 자신의 결혼은 당연히 모든 일에 관한 자기 선택에 영향을 미칠 수밖에 없는 결속이라는 것이었어요. 바로 그 이유로 그분은 병원에서 자리를 지키시라는 제 제안을 거절하셨어요."], ["“What he said of you was, that he could not be happy in doing anything which made you unhappy—that his marriage was of course a bond which must affect his choice about everything; and for that reason he refused my proposal that he should keep his position at the Hospital, because that would bind him to stay in Middlemarch, and he would not undertake to do anything which would be painful to you.", "그러면 미들마치에 머물러야 하는데, 부인께 괴로움이 될 일은 어떤 것도 하지 않으려 하셨거든요. 그분이 제게 그런 말씀을 하실 수 있었던 것은, 제가 결혼 생활에서, 남편의 병환으로 인해 많은 시련을 겪었다는 것을 아시기 때문이에요."], ["He could say that to me, because he knows that I had much trial in my marriage, from my husband’s illness, which hindered his plans and saddened him; and he knows that I have felt how hard it is to walk always in fear of hurting another who is tied to us.”", "그 병환이 남편의 계획을 가로막고 그분을 슬프게 했지요. 그리고 우리에게 매여 있는 다른 사람을 아프게 할까 봐 늘 두려워하며 살아가는 것이 얼마나 힘든지를 제가 절감했다는 것도 아시고요.\""]], [["Dorothea waited a little; she had discerned a faint pleasure stealing over Rosamond’s face.", "도러시아는 잠시 기다렸다. 로저먼드의 얼굴 위로 희미한 기쁨이 살며시 번지는 것을 알아챈 것이다."], ["But there was no answer, and she went on, with a gathering tremor, “Marriage is so unlike everything else.", "그러나 대답이 없자, 그녀는 점점 떨려 오는 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"결혼이란 다른 무엇과도 너무 달라요."], ["There is something even awful in the nearness it brings.", "그것이 가져다주는 가까움에는 어쩐지 두렵기까지 한 무언가가 있어요. 설령 우리가 결혼한 사람보다—그 사람보다—다른 누군가를 더 사랑한다 해도, 그것은 아무 소용이 없을 거예요.\""], ["Even if we loved some one else better than—than those we were married to, it would be no use”—poor Dorothea, in her palpitating anxiety, could only seize her language brokenly—“I mean, marriage drinks up all our power of giving or getting any blessedness in that sort of love.", "가엾은 도러시아는, 가슴이 두근거리는 불안 속에서 더듬더듬 말을 붙들 수밖에 없었다. \"제 말은, 결혼은 그런 종류의 사랑 속에서 어떤 축복을 주거나 받을 수 있는 우리의 모든 힘을 빨아들여 버린다는 거예요."], ["I know it may be very dear—but it murders our marriage—and then the marriage stays with us like a murder—and everything else is gone.", "그 사랑이 무척 소중할 수도 있다는 걸 알아요. 하지만 그것은 우리의 결혼을 죽여 버려요."], ["And then our husband—if he loved and trusted us, and we have not helped him, but made a curse in his life—”", "그러면 그 결혼은 살인처럼 우리 곁에 남고, 다른 모든 것은 사라져 버리지요. 그리고 우리 남편이—만약 그이가 우리를 사랑하고 믿었는데, 우리가 그이를 돕기는커녕 그이의 삶에 저주가 되고 말았다면—\""]], [["Her voice had sunk very low: there was a dread upon her of presuming too far, and of speaking as if she herself were perfection addressing error. She was too much preoccupied with her own anxiety, to be aware that Rosamond was trembling too; and filled with the need to express pitying fellowship rather than rebuke, she put her hands on Rosamond’s, and said with more agitated rapidity,—“I know, I know that the feeling may be very dear—it has taken hold of us unawares—it is so hard, it may seem like death to part with it—and we are weak—I am weak—”", "그녀의 목소리는 아주 낮게 가라앉았다. 너무 주제넘게 구는 것은 아닐까, 마치 자기 자신은 완전무결한데 잘못을 향해 말하는 것처럼 들리지는 않을까 하는 두려움이 그녀를 짓눌렀던 것이다. 그녀는 자신의 불안에 너무 골몰한 나머지 로저먼드도 떨고 있다는 것을 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 질책하기보다는 가엾이 여기는 동류의 정을 표하고 싶은 마음이 가득하여, 그녀는 로저먼드의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹고 한층 더 격하게 빠른 어조로 말했다. \"알아요, 그 감정이 무척 소중할 수 있다는 것을 알아요. 그것은 우리도 모르는 사이에 우리를 사로잡았지요. 그것은 너무도 힘겨워서, 그것과 헤어지는 일이 죽음처럼 느껴질지도 몰라요. 그리고 우리는 약해요. 저도 약하답니다.\""]], [["The waves of her own sorrow, from out of which she was struggling to save another, rushed over Dorothea with conquering force.", "다른 사람을 구해 내려 안간힘을 쓰던 그 한복판에서 솟구친 자기 자신의 슬픔의 물결이, 도러시아를 압도하는 힘으로 덮쳐 왔다."], ["She stopped in speechless agitation, not crying, but feeling as if she were being inwardly grappled.", "그녀는 말을 잃은 채 동요에 휩싸여 멈춰 섰다. 울지는 않았으나 속으로 무언가에 붙들려 씨름하는 듯한 느낌이었다."], ["Her face had become of a deathlier paleness, her lips trembled, and she pressed her hands helplessly on the hands that lay under them.", "그녀의 얼굴은 한층 죽은 듯한 창백함을 띠었고, 입술은 떨렸으며, 그녀는 자기 손 밑에 놓인 손을 속절없이 눌렀다."]], [["Rosamond, taken hold of by an emotion stronger than her own—hurried along in a new movement which gave all things some new, awful, undefined aspect—could find no words, but involuntarily she put her lips to Dorothea’s forehead which was very near her, and then for a minute the two women clasped each other as if they had been in a shipwreck.", "자기 것보다 더 강한 감정에 사로잡혀—모든 것에 새롭고 무시무시하며 형언할 수 없는 양상을 입히는 어떤 새로운 움직임에 떠밀려 가던—로저먼드는 아무 말도 찾지 못했으나, 저도 모르게 아주 가까이 있던 도러시아의 이마에 입술을 댔다. 그러고는 잠시 동안 두 여자는 마치 난파를 당한 사람들처럼 서로를 부둥켜안았다."]], [["“You are thinking what is not true,”", "\"부인은 사실이 아닌 것을 생각하고 계세요.\""], ["said Rosamond, in an eager half-whisper, while she was still feeling Dorothea’s arms round her—urged by a mysterious necessity to free herself from something that oppressed her as if it were blood guiltiness.", "로저먼드가, 여전히 도러시아의 팔이 자신을 감싸고 있는 것을 느끼며 다급한 반쯤의 속삭임으로 말했다. 마치 핏빛 죄책감이라도 되는 양 자신을 짓누르는 무언가로부터 벗어나려는, 알 수 없는 절박함에 떠밀린 것이었다."]], [["They moved apart, looking at each other.", "두 사람은 서로를 바라보며 떨어졌다."]], [["“When you came in yesterday—it was not as you thought,”", "\"어제 부인이 들어오셨을 때, 그것은 부인이 생각하신 것과 달랐어요.\""], ["said Rosamond in the same tone.", "로저먼드가 같은 어조로 말했다."]], [["There was a movement of surprised attention in Dorothea.", "도러시아에게서 놀라 귀 기울이는 움직임이 일었다."], ["She expected a vindication of Rosamond herself.", "그녀는 로저먼드 자신에 대한 변명을 예상했다."]], [["“He was telling me how he loved another woman, that I might know he could never love me,”", "\"그이는 제게 다른 여자를 얼마나 사랑하는지를 말하고 있었어요. 자기는 결코 저를 사랑할 수 없다는 것을 제가 알게 하려고요.\""], ["said Rosamond, getting more and more hurried as she went on.", "로저먼드가, 말을 이어 갈수록 점점 더 다급해지며 말했다."], ["“And now I think he hates me because—because you mistook him yesterday.", "\"그리고 이제 저는 그이가 저를 미워한다고 생각해요. 왜냐하면—왜냐하면 부인이 어제 그이를 오해하셨으니까요."], ["He says it is through me that you will think ill of him—think that he is a false person.", "그이는 저 때문에 부인이 자기를 나쁘게 여기게 될 거라고, 자기가 거짓된 사람이라고 여기게 될 거라고 말해요. 하지만 저 때문에 그리되지는 않을 거예요."], ["But it shall not be through me.", "그이는 저를 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없어요."], ["He has never had any love for me—I know he has not—he has always thought slightly of me.", "제가 알아요, 그런 적이 없다는 걸요. 그이는 늘 저를 대수롭지 않게 여겨 왔어요."], ["He said yesterday that no other woman existed for him beside you.", "어제 그이는 부인 말고는 자기에게 다른 어떤 여자도 존재하지 않는다고 말했어요."], ["The blame of what happened is entirely mine.", "일어난 일의 책임은 온전히 저에게 있어요."], ["He said he could never explain to you—because of me.", "그이는 부인께 결코 해명할 수 없다고 말했어요. 저 때문에요."], ["He said you could never think well of him again.", "그이는 부인이 다시는 자기를 좋게 여길 수 없을 거라고 말했어요."], ["But now I have told you, and he cannot reproach me any more.”", "하지만 이제 제가 부인께 말씀드렸으니, 그이는 더 이상 저를 책망할 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["Rosamond had delivered her soul under impulses which she had not known before.", "로저먼드는 전에는 알지 못했던 충동에 떠밀려 자기 영혼을 털어놓았다."], ["She had begun her confession under the subduing influence of Dorothea’s emotion; and as she went on she had gathered the sense that she was repelling Will’s reproaches, which were still like a knife-wound within her.", "그녀는 도러시아의 격정이 자신을 가라앉히는 영향 아래 고백을 시작했고, 말을 이어 갈수록, 여전히 내면에서 칼에 베인 상처처럼 사무치는 윌의 책망을 자신이 물리치고 있다는 자각을 얻게 되었다."]], [["The revulsion of feeling in Dorothea was too strong to be called joy.", "도러시아 안에서 일어난 감정의 격변은 기쁨이라 부르기에는 너무도 격렬했다."], ["It was a tumult in which the terrible strain of the night and morning made a resistant pain:—she could only perceive that this would be joy when she had recovered her power of feeling it.", "그것은 하나의 소용돌이였고, 그 속에서 밤과 아침의 끔찍한 긴장이 버티는 아픔을 빚어냈다. 그녀는 다만 자신이 그것을 느낄 힘을 되찾았을 때 이것이 기쁨이 되리라는 것만을 어렴풋이 알 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Her immediate consciousness was one of immense sympathy without check; she cared for Rosamond without struggle now, and responded earnestly to her last words—", "그 순간 그녀의 의식은 거침없는 무한한 연민의 의식이었다. 이제 그녀는 아무런 갈등 없이 로저먼드를 가엾이 여겼고, 그녀의 마지막 말에 진심으로 응답했다."]], [["“No, he cannot reproach you any more.”", "\"그래요, 그분은 더 이상 부인을 책망할 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["With her usual tendency to over-estimate the good in others, she felt a great outgoing of her heart towards Rosamond, for the generous effort which had redeemed her from suffering, not counting that the effort was a reflex of her own energy.", "남에게서 좋은 점을 과대평가하는 평소의 성향대로, 그녀는 자신을 고통에서 구해 준 그 너그러운 노력에 대해 로저먼드를 향한 큰 정이 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다. 그 노력이 실은 자기 자신의 기운이 빚어낸 반향이라는 것은 미처 헤아리지 못한 채로."], ["After they had been silent a little, she said—", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“You are not sorry that I came this morning?”", "\"제가 오늘 아침에 찾아온 게 못마땅하지 않으시죠?\""]], [["“No, you have been very good to me,”", "\"아니요, 부인은 제게 무척 잘해 주셨어요.\""], ["said Rosamond.", "로저먼드가 말했다."], ["“I did not think that you would be so good.", "\"부인이 그렇게 잘해 주시리라고는 생각지 못했어요."], ["I was very unhappy.", "저는 무척 불행했어요."], ["I am not happy now.", "지금도 행복하진 않아요."], ["Everything is so sad.”", "모든 것이 너무 슬퍼요.\""]], [["“But better days will come.", "\"하지만 더 나은 날들이 올 거예요."], ["Your husband will be rightly valued.", "부군은 정당하게 평가받으실 거예요."], ["And he depends on you for comfort.", "그리고 그분은 위안을 부인께 기대고 계세요."], ["He loves you best.", "그분이 가장 사랑하는 사람은 부인이에요."], ["The worst loss would be to lose that—and you have not lost it,”", "가장 끔찍한 상실은 그것을 잃는 것일 텐데, 부인은 그것을 잃지 않으셨어요.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["She tried to thrust away the too overpowering thought of her own relief, lest she should fail to win some sign that Rosamond’s affection was yearning back towards her husband.", "그녀는 로저먼드의 애정이 다시 남편에게로 향해 가고 있다는 어떤 표시를 얻어 내지 못할까 봐, 자기 자신의 안도라는 너무도 압도적인 생각을 밀어내려 애썼다."]], [["“Tertius did not find fault with me, then?”", "\"그러면 터셔스는 저를 탓하지 않았던 거군요?\" 로저먼드가 말했다."], ["said Rosamond, understanding now that Lydgate might have said anything to Mrs. Casaubon, and that she certainly was different from other women.", "이제 그녀는 리드게이트가 캐소본 부인에게 무슨 말이든 했을 수 있다는 것, 그리고 이 부인이 다른 여자들과는 분명히 다르다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Perhaps there was a faint taste of jealousy in the question.", "어쩌면 그 물음에는 희미한 질투의 맛이 배어 있었는지도 모른다."], ["A smile began to play over Dorothea’s face as she said—", "도러시아의 얼굴 위로 미소가 번지기 시작했고, 그녀는 말했다."]], [["“No, indeed!", "\"전혀요!"], ["How could you imagine it?”", "어떻게 그런 생각을 하실 수 있어요?\""], ["But here the door opened, and Lydgate entered.", "그러나 바로 그때 문이 열리며 리드게이트가 들어왔다."]], [["“I am come back in my quality of doctor,”", "\"의사 자격으로 다시 돌아왔습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“After I went away, I was haunted by two pale faces: Mrs.", "\"나간 뒤에 창백한 두 얼굴이 자꾸 눈앞에 어른거리더군요."], ["Casaubon looked as much in need of care as you, Rosy.", "캐소본 부인도 당신만큼이나 보살핌이 필요해 보였어, 로지."], ["And I thought that I had not done my duty in leaving you together; so when I had been to Coleman’s I came home again.", "그리고 두 분을 함께 두고 나온 것이 제 도리를 다하지 못한 것 같았습니다."], ["I noticed that you were walking, Mrs.", "그래서 콜먼네에 다녀온 뒤 다시 집으로 온 거예요."], ["Casaubon, and the sky has changed—I think we may have rain.", "캐소본 부인, 걸어오신 걸 봤습니다만, 하늘이 변했네요. 비가 올 것 같아요."], ["May I send some one to order your carriage to come for you?”", "사람을 보내 부인을 모셔 갈 마차를 부르라고 일러도 될까요?\""]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"오, 아니에요!"], ["I am strong: I need the walk,”", "저는 튼튼해요. 걷는 게 필요하답니다.\""], ["said Dorothea, rising with animation in her face.", "도러시아가 얼굴에 생기를 띠며 일어섰다."], ["“Mrs. Lydgate and I have chatted a great deal, and it is time for me to go.", "\"리드게이트 부인과 이야기를 많이 나누었으니, 이제 갈 때가 되었네요."], ["I have always been accused of being immoderate and saying too much.”", "저는 늘 도가 지나치고 말이 너무 많다고 핀잔을 들어 왔거든요.\""]], [["She put out her hand to Rosamond, and they said an earnest, quiet good-by without kiss or other show of effusion: there had been between them too much serious emotion for them to use the signs of it superficially.", "그녀는 로저먼드에게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람은 입맞춤도 다른 어떤 격정의 표시도 없이 진심 어린 조용한 작별을 나누었다. 그것을 겉으로 드러내는 표시를 피상적으로 쓰기에는, 두 사람 사이에 진지한 감정이 너무도 깊이 오갔던 것이다."]], [["As Lydgate took her to the door she said nothing of Rosamond, but told him of Mr. Farebrother and the other friends who had listened with belief to his story.", "리드게이트가 그녀를 문까지 배웅하는 동안 그녀는 로저먼드에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 그의 이야기를 믿음으로 귀 기울여 들어 준 페어브러더 씨와 다른 벗들에 관해 그에게 들려주었다."]], [["When he came back to Rosamond, she had already thrown herself on the sofa, in resigned fatigue.", "그가 로저먼드에게로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 이미 체념한 듯한 피로에 잠겨 소파에 몸을 던지고 있었다."]], [["“Well, Rosy,”", "\"자, 로지.\""], ["he said, standing over her, and touching her hair, “what do you think of Mrs.", "그가 그녀 위로 몸을 숙이고 머리카락을 쓰다듬으며 말했다."], ["Casaubon now you have seen so much of her?”", "\"이렇게 많이 겪어 보고 나니 이제 캐소본 부인을 어떻게 생각해?\""]], [["“I think she must be better than any one,”", "\"그분은 누구보다도 훌륭하신 분이 틀림없어요.\""], ["said Rosamond, “and she is very beautiful.", "로저먼드가 말했다. \"그리고 무척 아름다우시고요."], ["If you go to talk to her so often, you will be more discontented with me than ever!”", "당신이 그렇게 자주 그분과 이야기하러 다니면, 저한테는 더더욱 불만이 쌓이겠지요!\""]], [["Lydgate laughed at the “so often.”", "리드게이트는 \"그렇게 자주\"라는 말에 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“But has she made you any less discontented with me?”", "\"그런데 그분 덕분에 나에 대한 당신 불만은 좀 줄어든 거야?\""]], [["“I think she has,”", "\"줄어든 것 같아요.\""], ["said Rosamond, looking up in his face.", "로저먼드가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."], ["“How heavy your eyes are, Tertius—and do push your hair back.”", "\"당신 눈이 어쩜 그렇게 무거워 보여요, 터셔스. 그리고 머리 좀 뒤로 넘겨요.\""], ["He lifted up his large white hand to obey her, and felt thankful for this little mark of interest in him.", "그는 그녀의 말에 따르려고 크고 흰 손을 들어 올렸고, 자신에 대한 이 작은 관심의 표시에 고마움을 느꼈다."], ["Poor Rosamond’s vagrant fancy had come back terribly scourged—meek enough to nestle under the old despised shelter.", "가엾은 로저먼드의 방황하던 공상은 끔찍하게 채찍질당한 채 돌아와 있었다. 한때 업신여기던 옛 그늘 아래로 파고들 만큼 충분히 온순해진 채로."], ["And the shelter was still there: Lydgate had accepted his narrowed lot with sad resignation.", "그리고 그 그늘은 여전히 거기 있었다. 리드게이트는 좁아진 자신의 운명을 슬픈 체념으로 받아들였던 것이다."], ["He had chosen this fragile creature, and had taken the burthen of her life upon his arms.", "그는 이 연약한 존재를 택했고, 그녀의 삶의 짐을 자기 팔에 떠안았다."], ["He must walk as he could, carrying that burthen pitifully.", "그는 할 수 있는 대로 걸어가야 했다. 그 짐을 가엾이 여기며 짊어진 채로."]], [["# CHAPTER LXXXII.", "# CHAPTER LXXXII."]], [["“My grief lies onward and my joy behind.” —SHAKESPEARE: Sonnets.", "인간의 역사를 살펴보면 추방당한 자들이 희망을 양식으로 삼는 일은 익히 알려진 사실이며, 그들은 어쩔 수 없는 처지가 아니라면 좀처럼 유배지에 머무르지 않는다."], ["Exiles notoriously feed much on hopes, and are unlikely to stay in banishment unless they are obliged.", "윌 래디슬로가 스스로를 미들마치에서 추방했을 때, 그가 자신의 귀환에 세워 둔 장애물이라곤 자기 자신의 결심뿐이었으니, 그것은 결코 무쇠 빗장 같은 것이 아니라 단지 다른 마음 상태와 어우러져 미뉴에트를 추듯 녹아들고, 정중한 손쉬움으로 절하고 미소 지으며 자리를 내어 주기 일쑤인 마음의 한 상태에 지나지 않았다."], ["When Will Ladislaw exiled himself from Middlemarch he had placed no stronger obstacle to his return than his own resolve, which was by no means an iron barrier, but simply a state of mind liable to melt into a minuet with other states of mind, and to find itself bowing, smiling, and giving place with polite facility.", "달이 거듭될수록 그는 미들마치로 잠깐 내려가서는 안 될 이유가 무엇인지를 점점 말하기 어렵게 느꼈다. 그저 도러시아에 관한 소식을 좀 듣기 위해서일 뿐이었으니까."], ["As the months went on, it had seemed more and more difficult to him to say why he should not run down to Middlemarch—merely for the sake of hearing something about Dorothea; and if on such a flying visit he should chance by some strange coincidence to meet with her, there was no reason for him to be ashamed of having taken an innocent journey which he had beforehand supposed that he should not take.", "그리고 그렇게 잠깐 들른 길에 어떤 기이한 우연으로 그녀와 마주치게 된다 하더라도, 미리 가지 않으리라 짐작했던 결백한 여행을 했다는 것을 두고 부끄러워할 이유는 없었다. 이미 그녀와는 절망적으로 갈라선 처지였으니, 그녀가 사는 동네 근처로 가 보는 것쯤은 분명 감행해도 좋을 일이었다."], ["Since he was hopelessly divided from her, he might surely venture into her neighborhood; and as to the suspicious friends who kept a dragon watch over her—their opinions seemed less and less important with time and change of air.", "그리고 용처럼 그녀를 감시하는 의심 많은 친구들로 말하자면, 그들의 견해는 시간이 흐르고 환경이 바뀌면서 점점 덜 중요해 보였다."]], [["And there had come a reason quite irrespective of Dorothea, which seemed to make a journey to Middlemarch a sort of philanthropic duty.", "그런데 도러시아와는 전혀 무관한 어떤 이유 하나가 생겨났고, 그것은 미들마치로의 여행을 일종의 박애적 의무처럼 보이게 만들었다."], ["Will had given a disinterested attention to an intended settlement on a new plan in the Far West, and the need for funds in order to carry out a good design had set him on debating with himself whether it would not be a laudable use to make of his claim on Bulstrode, to urge the application of that money which had been offered to himself as a means of carrying out a scheme likely to be largely beneficial.", "윌은 머나먼 서부에 새로운 방식으로 정착촌을 세우려는 계획에 사심 없는 관심을 기울여 왔는데, 훌륭한 구상을 실행하는 데 필요한 자금 문제가 그로 하여금 자문하게 만들었던 것이다. 자신에게 제안되었던 그 돈을, 널리 이로울 법한 사업을 실현하는 수단으로 쓰도록 권유하는 데 자신이 벌스트로드에게 가진 권리를 활용하는 것이야말로 칭찬받을 만한 용도가 아니겠는가."], ["The question seemed a very dubious one to Will, and his repugnance to again entering into any relation with the banker might have made him dismiss it quickly, if there had not arisen in his imagination the probability that his judgment might be more safely determined by a visit to Middlemarch.", "그 문제는 윌에게 무척 미심쩍게 여겨졌고, 그 은행가와 다시 어떤 관계를 맺는다는 데 대한 혐오감 탓에 그는 그것을 어서 떨쳐 버렸을 수도 있었다. 미들마치를 한번 방문하면 자신의 판단을 더 안전하게 정할 수 있으리라는 가능성이 그의 상상 속에 떠오르지만 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["That was the object which Will stated to himself as a reason for coming down.", "그것이 윌이 내려갈 이유랍시고 스스로에게 내세운 명분이었다. 그는 리드게이트에게 속내를 털어놓고 그와 돈 문제를 의논할 작정이었으며, 머무는 며칠 저녁을 아리따운 로저먼드와 음악을 잔뜩 즐기고 농담을 주고받으며 즐겁게 보낼 작정이었다."], ["He had meant to confide in Lydgate, and discuss the money question with him, and he had meant to amuse himself for the few evenings of his stay by having a great deal of music and badinage with fair Rosamond, without neglecting his friends at Lowick Parsonage:—if the Parsonage was close to the Manor, that was no fault of his.", "물론 로윅 목사관의 친구들을 소홀히 하지 않으면서 말이다. 목사관이 영주관과 가까이 있다 한들 그것은 그의 잘못이 아니었다."], ["He had neglected the Farebrothers before his departure, from a proud resistance to the possible accusation of indirectly seeking interviews with Dorothea; but hunger tames us, and Will had become very hungry for the vision of a certain form and the sound of a certain voice.", "그는 떠나기 전 페어브러더 가족을 소홀히 했었는데, 도러시아와의 만남을 에둘러 꾀한다는 혐의를 받을지 모른다는 데 대한 자존심 어린 저항 때문이었다. 하지만 굶주림은 우리를 길들이는 법이라, 윌은 어떤 모습을 보고 싶고 어떤 목소리를 듣고 싶은 마음에 몹시 굶주려 있었다."], ["Nothing had done instead—not the opera, or the converse of zealous politicians, or the flattering reception (in dim corners) of his new hand in leading articles.", "그 무엇도 그것을 대신해 주지 못했다. 오페라도, 열성적인 정치가들과의 담론도, 사설란에서 새로 발휘한 자기 솜씨에 대한 (어둑한 구석에서의) 우쭐한 환대도."]], [["Thus he had come down, foreseeing with confidence how almost everything would be in his familiar little world; fearing, indeed, that there would be no surprises in his visit.", "그리하여 그는 내려왔으니, 자신의 익숙하고 자그마한 세계에서 거의 모든 것이 어떠하리라는 것을 자신만만하게 내다보았고, 사실 이번 방문에 아무 놀랄 일도 없으리라는 것을 오히려 두려워하기까지 했다."], ["But he had found that humdrum world in a terribly dynamic condition, in which even badinage and lyrism had turned explosive; and the first day of this visit had become the most fatal epoch of his life.", "그러나 그는 그 단조로운 세계가 끔찍하게 격동하는 상태에 빠져 있음을 발견했으니, 그곳에서는 농담과 서정조차 폭발성을 띠게 되어 있었다. 그리고 이 방문의 첫날은 그의 생애에서 가장 운명적인 시기가 되고 말았다."], ["The next morning he felt so harassed with the nightmare of consequences—he dreaded so much the immediate issues before him—that seeing while he breakfasted the arrival of the Riverston coach, he went out hurriedly and took his place on it, that he might be relieved, at least for a day, from the necessity of doing or saying anything in Middlemarch.", "이튿날 아침 그는 그 결과들의 악몽에 너무도 시달린 나머지, 또 자기 앞에 닥친 당장의 문제들을 너무도 두려워한 나머지, 아침을 먹다가 리버스턴행 마차가 도착하는 것을 보고는 황급히 나가 그 마차에 자리를 잡았다. 적어도 하루만이라도 미들마치에서 무언가를 하거나 말해야 할 필요에서 벗어나기 위해서였다."], ["Will Ladislaw was in one of those tangled crises which are commoner in experience than one might imagine, from the shallow absoluteness of men’s judgments.", "윌 래디슬로는 그런 얽히고설킨 위기들 중 하나에 처해 있었는데, 그런 위기는 사람들의 판단이 지닌 천박한 단정성에서 짐작할 법한 것보다 실제 경험에서는 훨씬 흔한 것이다."], ["He had found Lydgate, for whom he had the sincerest respect, under circumstances which claimed his thorough and frankly declared sympathy; and the reason why, in spite of that claim, it would have been better for Will to have avoided all further intimacy, or even contact, with Lydgate, was precisely of the kind to make such a course appear impossible.", "그는 진심으로 존경하던 리드게이트가, 자신의 철저하고 솔직히 표명된 동정을 마땅히 받아야 할 처지에 놓인 것을 보았다. 그런데 그러한 동정의 당위에도 불구하고 윌이 리드게이트와 더 이상의 친밀한 교류를, 아니 접촉마저도 피하는 편이 더 나았을 그 이유야말로, 그런 방도를 도무지 불가능해 보이게 만드는 종류의 것이었다."], ["To a creature of Will’s susceptible temperament—without any neutral region of indifference in his nature, ready to turn everything that befell him into the collisions of a passionate drama—the revelation that Rosamond had made her happiness in any way dependent on him was a difficulty which his outburst of rage towards her had immeasurably increased for him.", "윌처럼 민감한 기질을 지닌 존재에게는, 자기 본성 안에 무관심이라는 중립지대가 전혀 없어서 자신에게 닥치는 모든 일을 격정적인 드라마의 충돌로 바꾸어 버릴 태세가 되어 있는 존재에게는, 로저먼드가 자신의 행복을 어떤 식으로든 그에게 의존하게 만들었다는 깨달음은 하나의 곤경이었으며, 그녀를 향해 터뜨린 분노가 그 곤경을 그에게 한없이 키워 놓았던 것이다. 그는 자신의 잔인함을 미워했으나, 한편으로는 누그러진 마음을 온전히 드러내기를 두려워했다."], ["He hated his own cruelty, and yet he dreaded to show the fulness of his relenting: he must go to her again; the friendship could not be put to a sudden end; and her unhappiness was a power which he dreaded.", "그는 그녀에게 다시 가야 했다. 그 우정을 느닷없이 끝낼 수는 없었으니까."], ["And all the while there was no more foretaste of enjoyment in the life before him than if his limbs had been lopped off and he was making his fresh start on crutches.", "그리고 그녀의 불행은 그가 두려워하는 하나의 힘이었다. 그러는 내내 그의 앞에 놓인 삶에는 즐거움의 예감이라곤 조금도 없었으니, 마치 사지가 잘려 나간 채 목발을 짚고 새로 첫발을 내딛는 것이나 다름없었다."], ["In the night he had debated whether he should not get on the coach, not for Riverston, but for London, leaving a note to Lydgate which would give a makeshift reason for his retreat.", "밤중에 그는 리버스턴이 아니라 런던으로 가는 마차에 올라타, 리드게이트에게 자신의 퇴각에 대한 임시변통의 이유를 적은 쪽지를 남겨 두고 떠나 버릴까를 고심했다."], ["But there were strong cords pulling him back from that abrupt departure: the blight on his happiness in thinking of Dorothea, the crushing of that chief hope which had remained in spite of the acknowledged necessity for renunciation, was too fresh a misery for him to resign himself to it and go straightway into a distance which was also despair.", "그러나 그 갑작스러운 떠남에서 그를 끌어당겨 막는 질긴 밧줄들이 있었다. 도러시아를 생각할 때 그의 행복에 드리워진 병충해, 단념해야 한다는 것을 인정했음에도 그래도 남아 있던 그 으뜸가는 희망이 짓밟히는 일은, 너무도 생생한 비참함이어서 그로서는 그것을 체념하고 곧장 떠나 절망이기도 한 먼 곳으로 가 버릴 수가 없었다."]], [["Thus he did nothing more decided than taking the Riverston coach.", "그리하여 그는 리버스턴행 마차를 타는 것 이상으로 결단력 있는 일은 아무것도 하지 못했다."], ["He came back again by it while it was still daylight, having made up his mind that he must go to Lydgate’s that evening.", "그는 그날 저녁 리드게이트의 집에 가야 한다고 마음을 정한 채, 아직 환할 때 그 마차로 다시 돌아왔다."], ["The Rubicon, we know, was a very insignificant stream to look at; its significance lay entirely in certain invisible conditions.", "우리가 알다시피 루비콘 강은 보기에 아주 보잘것없는 시내였다. 그 의미는 전적으로 어떤 보이지 않는 조건들에 있었다."], ["Will felt as if he were forced to cross his small boundary ditch, and what he saw beyond it was not empire, but discontented subjection.", "윌은 자신의 그 자그마한 경계의 도랑을 억지로 건너야 하는 것처럼 느꼈고, 그 너머에 보이는 것은 제국이 아니라 불만에 찬 예속이었다."]], [["But it is given to us sometimes even in our every-day life to witness the saving influence of a noble nature, the divine efficacy of rescue that may lie in a self-subduing act of fellowship.", "그러나 우리에게는 때때로 일상생활 속에서조차 고귀한 본성이 발휘하는 구원의 영향력을, 즉 자기를 억누르는 동료애의 행위 속에 깃들 수 있는 신성한 구제의 효력을 목격할 기회가 주어진다."], ["If Dorothea, after her night’s anguish, had not taken that walk to Rosamond—why, she perhaps would have been a woman who gained a higher character for discretion, but it would certainly not have been as well for those three who were on one hearth in Lydgate’s house at half-past seven that evening.", "만약 도러시아가 그 밤의 고뇌를 겪은 뒤에 로저먼드에게로 그 발걸음을 옮기지 않았더라면, 그야 아마 그녀는 신중함으로 더 높은 평판을 얻은 여인이 되었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그날 저녁 일곱 시 반에 리드게이트 집의 한 난롯가에 모여 있던 그 세 사람에게는 분명 그렇게 좋은 일이 되지 못했으리라."]], [["Rosamond had been prepared for Will’s visit, and she received him with a languid coldness which Lydgate accounted for by her nervous exhaustion, of which he could not suppose that it had any relation to Will.", "로저먼드는 윌의 방문에 대비하고 있었고, 나른한 냉담함으로 그를 맞이했는데, 리드게이트는 그것을 그녀의 신경 쇠약 탓으로 돌렸을 뿐, 그것이 윌과 어떤 관련이 있으리라고는 상상도 할 수 없었다."], ["And when she sat in silence bending over a bit of work, he innocently apologized for her in an indirect way by begging her to lean backward and rest.", "그리고 그녀가 바느질감을 들여다보며 말없이 앉아 있자, 그는 순진하게도 그녀에게 등을 기대고 쉬라고 권하면서 에둘러 그녀를 변호했다."], ["Will was miserable in the necessity for playing the part of a friend who was making his first appearance and greeting to Rosamond, while his thoughts were busy about her feeling since that scene of yesterday, which seemed still inexorably to enclose them both, like the painful vision of a double madness.", "윌은 처음으로 모습을 드러내며 로저먼드에게 인사를 건네는 친구의 역할을 연기해야 하는 처지가 비참했으니, 그러는 동안 그의 생각은 어제의 그 장면 이후 그녀의 심정을 두고 분주히 맴돌았던 것이다. 그 장면은 마치 이중의 광기가 빚어낸 고통스러운 환영처럼 여전히 가차 없이 두 사람을 에워싸고 있는 듯했다."], ["It happened that nothing called Lydgate out of the room; but when Rosamond poured out the tea, and Will came near to fetch it, she placed a tiny bit of folded paper in his saucer.", "마침 리드게이트를 방 밖으로 불러내는 일은 아무것도 없었다. 그러나 로저먼드가 차를 따르고 윌이 그것을 가지러 가까이 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 그의 찻잔 받침에 자그맣게 접은 종이쪽지 하나를 놓았다."], ["He saw it and secured it quickly, but as he went back to his inn he had no eagerness to unfold the paper.", "그는 그것을 보고 재빨리 챙겼으나, 여관으로 돌아가는 길에도 그 쪽지를 펼쳐 보고 싶은 열의는 없었다."], ["What Rosamond had written to him would probably deepen the painful impressions of the evening.", "로저먼드가 그에게 써 보낸 것은 십중팔구 그날 저녁의 고통스러운 인상을 더 깊게 만들 것이었다."], ["Still, he opened and read it by his bed-candle.", "그래도 그는 침대맡 촛불 곁에서 그것을 펼쳐 읽었다."], ["There were only these few words in her neatly flowing hand:—", "단정하게 흘려 쓴 그녀의 글씨로 이런 몇 마디만 적혀 있었다."]], [["“I have told Mrs. Casaubon.", "\"커소번 부인께 말씀드렸어요."], ["She is not under any mistake about you.", "그분은 당신에 대해 아무 오해도 하고 계시지 않아요."], ["I told her because she came to see me and was very kind.", "저를 보러 오셔서 무척 다정하게 대해 주셨기에 말씀드렸습니다."], ["You will have nothing to reproach me with now.", "이제 저를 두고 책망하실 일은 없으실 거예요."], ["I shall not have made any difference to you.”", "저는 당신에게 아무런 차이도 만들지 않은 셈이 될 테니까요.\""]], [["The effect of these words was not quite all gladness.", "이 말이 자아낸 효과가 온통 기쁨만은 아니었다."], ["As Will dwelt on them with excited imagination, he felt his cheeks and ears burning at the thought of what had occurred between Dorothea and Rosamond—at the uncertainty how far Dorothea might still feel her dignity wounded in having an explanation of his conduct offered to her.", "윌이 흥분한 상상으로 그 말을 곱씹는 동안, 도러시아와 로저먼드 사이에 오간 일을 생각하니 그의 두 뺨과 귀가 화끈 달아올랐다. 자신의 행동에 대한 해명을 들은 일로 도러시아가 여전히 얼마만큼이나 자존심에 상처를 입었다고 느낄지 알 수 없었던 것이다."], ["There might still remain in her mind a changed association with him which made an irremediable difference—a lasting flaw.", "그녀의 마음속에는 여전히 그에 대한 달라진 인상이 남아 있어, 돌이킬 수 없는 차이를, 영구한 흠집을 만들었을지도 몰랐다."], ["With active fancy he wrought himself into a state of doubt little more easy than that of the man who has escaped from wreck by night and stands on unknown ground in the darkness.", "활발한 공상으로 그는 스스로를, 밤중에 난파에서 빠져나와 어둠 속 알 수 없는 땅 위에 서 있는 사람의 처지보다 별로 나을 것 없는 의심의 상태로 몰아넣었다."], ["Until that wretched yesterday—except the moment of vexation long ago in the very same room and in the very same presence—all their vision, all their thought of each other, had been as in a world apart, where the sunshine fell on tall white lilies, where no evil lurked, and no other soul entered.", "그 비참한 어제까지는, 오래전 바로 그 같은 방에서 바로 그 같은 사람을 앞에 두고 느꼈던 짜증의 순간만 빼면, 두 사람의 모든 환상, 서로에 대한 모든 생각은 마치 동떨어진 세계 속에 있는 것 같았다. 거기서는 햇살이 키 큰 흰 백합 위로 쏟아졌고, 어떤 악도 도사리지 않았으며, 다른 어떤 영혼도 들어오지 않았다."], ["But now—would Dorothea meet him in that world again?", "그러나 이제, 도러시아가 그 세계에서 그를 다시 만나 줄까?"]], [["# CHAPTER LXXXIII.", "# CHAPTER LXXXIII."]], [["“And now good-morrow to our waking souls Which watch not one another out of fear; For love all love of other sights controls, And makes one little room, an everywhere.” —DR. DONNE.", "이제 우리의 깨어 있는 영혼들에게 좋은 아침을 빌어 주자\n서로를 두려움으로 살피지 않는 영혼들에게"]], [["On the second morning after Dorothea’s visit to Rosamond, she had had two nights of sound sleep, and had not only lost all traces of fatigue, but felt as if she had a great deal of superfluous strength—that is to say, more strength than she could manage to concentrate on any occupation. The day before, she had taken long walks outside the grounds, and had paid two visits to the Parsonage; but she never in her life told any one the reason why she spent her time in that fruitless manner, and this morning she was rather angry with herself for her childish restlessness. To-day was to be spent quite differently. What was there to be done in the village? Oh dear! nothing. Everybody was well and had flannel; nobody’s pig had died; and it was Saturday morning, when there was a general scrubbing of doors and door-stones, and when it was useless to go into the school. But there were various subjects that Dorothea was trying to get clear upon, and she resolved to throw herself energetically into the gravest of all. She sat down in the library before her particular little heap of books on political economy and kindred matters, out of which she was trying to get light as to the best way of spending money so as not to injure one’s neighbors, or—what comes to the same thing—so as to do them the most good. Here was a weighty subject which, if she could but lay hold of it, would certainly keep her mind steady. Unhappily her mind slipped off it for a whole hour; and at the end she found herself reading sentences twice over with an intense consciousness of many things, but not of any one thing contained in the text. This was hopeless. Should she order the carriage and drive to Tipton? No; for some reason or other she preferred staying at Lowick. But her vagrant mind must be reduced to order: there was an art in self-discipline; and she walked round and round the brown library considering by what sort of manoeuvre she could arrest her wandering thoughts. Perhaps a mere task was the best means—something to which she must go doggedly. Was there not the geography of Asia Minor, in which her slackness had often been rebuked by Mr. Casaubon? She went to the cabinet of maps and unrolled one: this morning she might make herself finally sure that Paphlagonia was not on the Levantine coast, and fix her total darkness about the Chalybes firmly on the shores of the Euxine. A map was a fine thing to study when you were disposed to think of something else, being made up of names that would turn into a chime if you went back upon them. Dorothea set earnestly to work, bending close to her map, and uttering the names in an audible, subdued tone, which often got into a chime. She looked amusingly girlish after all her deep experience—nodding her head and marking the names off on her fingers, with a little pursing of her lip, and now and then breaking off to put her hands on each side of her face and say, “Oh dear! oh dear!”", "사랑은 다른 모든 풍경의 사랑을 다스려"]], [["There was no reason why this should end any more than a merry-go-round; but it was at last interrupted by the opening of the door and the announcement of Miss Noble.", "작은 방 하나를 어디에나 있는 곳으로 만드나니. —던 박사*"]], [["The little old lady, whose bonnet hardly reached Dorothea’s shoulder, was warmly welcomed, but while her hand was being pressed she made many of her beaver-like noises, as if she had something difficult to say.", "* 존 던: 17세기 영국의 형이상학파 시인."]], [["“Do sit down,” said Dorothea, rolling a chair forward. “Am I wanted for anything? I shall be so glad if I can do anything.” “I will not stay,” said Miss Noble, putting her hand into her small basket, and holding some article inside it nervously; “I have left a friend in the churchyard.” She lapsed into her inarticulate sounds, and unconsciously drew forth the article which she was fingering. It was the tortoise-shell lozenge-box, and Dorothea felt the color mounting to her cheeks.", "도러시아가 로저먼드를 방문한 지 이틀째 되는 날 아침, 그녀는 이틀 밤을 푹 자고 나서 피로의 흔적을 모조리 떨쳐 냈을 뿐 아니라 넘쳐 나는 기운을 주체하지 못할 것 같은 느낌이 들었다. 다시 말해 어떤 일에 집중할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 기운이 남아돈다는 뜻이었다. 그 전날 그녀는 저택의 부지 밖으로 오랫동안 산책을 나갔고 목사관에도 두 번 들렀다. 하지만 자신이 왜 그토록 보람 없이 시간을 보냈는지 그 이유를 평생 누구에게도 말하지 않았으며, 이날 아침 그녀는 자신의 유치한 안절부절못함에 다소 화가 났다. 오늘은 아주 다르게 보낼 작정이었다. 마을에서 할 일이 무엇이 있을까? 아아! 아무것도 없었다. 모두 건강했고 플란넬 옷도 충분했으며, 돼지가 죽은 집도 없었다. 게다가 토요일 아침이라 집집마다 문과 댓돌을 닦아 대는 날이었으니, 학교에 가 봐야 소용없었다. 그러나 도러시아가 분명히 이해해 보려고 애쓰는 여러 가지 주제가 있었기에, 그녀는 그중에서도 가장 진지한 문제에 정력적으로 몰두해 보기로 결심했다. 그녀는 서재에서 정치 경제학과 그와 관련된 문제들을 다룬 자신만의 작은 책 더미 앞에 앉았는데, 그 책들에서 이웃에게 해를 끼치지 않으면서 돈을 쓰는 최선의 방법, 다시 말해 같은 말이지만 이웃에게 가장 큰 도움을 주는 방법에 관해 어떤 깨우침을 얻으려는 것이었다. 여기에 그녀가 붙잡기만 한다면 분명 마음을 한결같이 지켜 줄 묵직한 주제가 있었다. 불행히도 그녀의 마음은 꼬박 한 시간 동안 그 주제에서 미끄러져 나갔고, 결국 그녀는 여러 가지 일을 강렬하게 의식하면서도 정작 본문에 담긴 어느 한 가지도 의식하지 못한 채 문장을 두 번씩 거듭 읽고 있는 자신을 발견했다. 이건 가망이 없었다. 마차를 불러 팁턴으로 갈까? 아니다. 무슨 까닭에선지 그녀는 로윅에 머무는 편이 더 좋았다. 하지만 이 방황하는 마음은 질서를 되찾아야만 했다. 자기 수양에도 기술이 있는 법이었다. 그래서 그녀는 갈색 서재를 빙글빙글 돌면서 어떤 식의 책략을 써야 떠도는 생각을 붙잡을 수 있을지 궁리했다. 어쩌면 그저 어떤 과제 하나가 최선의 방편일지도 몰랐다. 끈질기게 매달려야 하는 무엇 말이다. 소아시아의 지리가 있지 않은가, 캐소본 씨가 그녀의 게으름을 종종 나무라던 그 분야가? 그녀는 지도가 든 진열장으로 가서 한 장을 펼쳤다. 오늘 아침에는 파플라고니아가 레반트 해안에 있지 않다는 것을 마침내 확실히 해 두고, 칼리베스족에 관한 자신의 완전한 무지를 흑해 연안에 단단히 고정시켜 둘 수 있으리라. 지도란 다른 무언가를 생각하고 싶을 때 공부하기에 좋은 것이었으니, 거슬러 되짚어 보면 하나의 종소리처럼 어우러지는 이름들로 이루어져 있기 때문이었다. 도러시아는 지도에 바싹 몸을 숙이고 진지하게 작업에 착수해, 들릴락 말락 한 낮은 목소리로 이름들을 외워 나갔는데, 그 소리는 종종 하나의 가락처럼 이어졌다. 그토록 깊은 경험을 다 겪고 나서도 그녀는 우습게도 소녀 같아 보였다. 고개를 끄덕이고 손가락으로 이름을 하나씩 짚어 가며, 입술을 살짝 오므렸다가, 이따금 손을 멈추고 두 손을 양 볼에 대고는 \"아아! 아아!\" 하고 탄식하곤 했다."]], [["“Mr. Ladislaw,”", "이것이 회전목마보다 더 빨리 끝날 까닭은 없었다."], ["continued the timid little woman.", "그러나 마침내 문이 열리고 노블 양이 왔다는 전갈이 들리면서 중단되었다."], ["“He fears he has offended you, and has begged me to ask if you will see him for a few minutes.”", "보닛을 쓴 키가 도러시아의 어깨에도 채 닿지 않는 그 자그마한 노부인은 따뜻한 환대를 받았으나, 손을 맞잡은 채로 마치 무언가 말하기 어려운 것이 있는 듯 비버 같은 소리를 잔뜩 냈다."]], [["Dorothea did not answer on the instant: it was crossing her mind that she could not receive him in this library, where her husband’s prohibition seemed to dwell.", "\"앉으세요.\" 도러시아가 의자를 앞으로 밀며 말했다."], ["She looked towards the window.", "\"제게 무슨 볼일이라도 있으신가요? 제가 도울 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 기쁘게 하겠어요.\""], ["Could she go out and meet him in the grounds?", "\"오래 있지는 않을 거예요.\" 노블 양이 작은 바구니에 손을 넣어 그 안의 무언가를 초조하게 만지작거리며 말했다."], ["The sky was heavy, and the trees had begun to shiver as at a coming storm.", "\"교회 묘지에 친구 한 사람을 두고 왔거든요.\" 그녀는 다시 알아들을 수 없는 소리로 빠져들더니, 무심결에 만지작거리던 그 물건을 꺼냈다."], ["Besides, she shrank from going out to him.", "그것은 거북 등딱지로 만든 마름모꼴 사탕 갑이었고, 도러시아는 얼굴로 붉은빛이 번져 오르는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Do see him, Mrs. Casaubon,”", "\"래디슬로 씨예요.\""], ["said Miss Noble, pathetically; “else I must go back and say No, and that will hurt him.” “Yes, I will see him,”", "소심한 자그마한 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["said Dorothea. “Pray tell him to come.”", "\"당신의 기분을 상하게 한 것은 아닐까 걱정하면서, 잠깐만이라도 만나 주실 수 있는지 여쭤 봐 달라고 제게 간청했답니다.\""]], [["What else was there to be done? There was nothing that she longed for at that moment except to see Will: the possibility of seeing him had thrust itself insistently between her and every other object; and yet she had a throbbing excitement like an alarm upon her—a sense that she was doing something daringly defiant for his sake.", "도러시아는 곧바로 대답하지 못했다. 죽은 남편의 금령이 깃들어 있는 듯한 이 서재에서는 그를 맞이할 수 없다는 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤기 때문이다. 그녀는 창 쪽을 바라보았다. 밖으로 나가 정원에서 그를 만날 수 있을까? 하늘은 무겁게 내려앉았고, 나무들은 다가오는 폭풍을 예감한 듯 떨기 시작했다. 게다가 그녀는 그에게로 나가는 것이 꺼려졌다. \"부디 만나 주세요, 캐소본 부인.\" 노블 양이 애처롭게 말했다. \"그러지 않으면 저는 돌아가서 안 된다고 말해야 하고, 그러면 그 사람이 상심할 거예요.\""]], [["When the little lady had trotted away on her mission, Dorothea stood in the middle of the library with her hands falling clasped before her, making no attempt to compose herself in an attitude of dignified unconsciousness.", "\"네, 만나겠어요.\""], ["What she was least conscious of just then was her own body: she was thinking of what was likely to be in Will’s mind, and of the hard feelings that others had had about him.", "도러시아가 말했다. \"부디 오시라고 전해 주세요.\""], ["How could any duty bind her to hardness?", "달리 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["Resistance to unjust dispraise had mingled with her feeling for him from the very first, and now in the rebound of her heart after her anguish the resistance was stronger than ever.", "그 순간 그녀가 간절히 바라는 것은 윌을 만나는 것 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 그를 만날 수 있다는 가능성이 그녀와 다른 모든 대상 사이에 집요하게 끼어들었다."], ["“If I love him too much it is because he has been used so ill:”—there was a voice within her saying this to some imagined audience in the library, when the door was opened, and she saw Will before her.", "그러면서도 그녀에게는 경보처럼 두근거리는 흥분이 일었다. 그를 위해 무언가 대담하게 도전하는 일을 하고 있다는 느낌이었다."]], [["She did not move, and he came towards her with more doubt and timidity in his face than she had ever seen before. He was in a state of uncertainty which made him afraid lest some look or word of his should condemn him to a new distance from her; and Dorothea was afraid of her own emotion. She looked as if there were a spell upon her, keeping her motionless and hindering her from unclasping her hands, while some intense, grave yearning was imprisoned within her eyes. Seeing that she did not put out her hand as usual, Will paused a yard from her and said with embarrassment, “I am so grateful to you for seeing me.”", "자그마한 부인이 자신의 임무를 띠고 종종걸음으로 나가자, 도러시아는 서재 한가운데 서서 두 손을 앞으로 모은 채 늘어뜨리고는, 위엄 있는 무심함의 자세로 마음을 가다듬으려는 시도조차 하지 않았다. 그때 그녀가 가장 의식하지 못한 것은 바로 자기 자신의 몸이었다. 그녀는 윌의 마음속에 무엇이 있을지, 그리고 다른 사람들이 그에 대해 품었던 모진 감정들을 생각하고 있었다. 어떤 의무가 그녀를 매정함에 옭아맬 수 있겠는가? 부당한 비난에 맞서려는 마음은 처음부터 그를 향한 감정과 뒤섞여 있었고, 이제 고뇌를 겪고 난 마음의 반동 속에서 그 저항은 어느 때보다 강해졌다. \"내가 그를 너무 사랑한다면, 그건 그가 그토록 부당한 대우를 받아 왔기 때문이야.\" 서재 안의 어떤 상상 속 청중을 향해 그녀의 내면에서 이렇게 말하는 목소리가 있었는데, 그때 문이 열리고 그녀는 눈앞에 윌을 보았다. 그녀는 움직이지 않았고, 그는 일찍이 본 적 없는 의심과 소심함이 어린 얼굴로 그녀에게 다가왔다. 그는 자신의 어떤 눈빛이나 말 한마디가 그녀로부터 새로이 멀어지는 형벌을 받게 하지나 않을까 두려워하는 불확실한 상태에 있었다. 그리고 도러시아는 자신의 감정이 두려웠다. 그녀는 마치 주문에라도 걸린 듯 미동도 없이, 모아 쥔 두 손을 풀지 못한 채, 강렬하고 진지한 어떤 갈망을 눈 속에 가둬 두고 있는 것 같았다. 그녀가 여느 때처럼 손을 내밀지 않는 것을 보고, 윌은 그녀에게서 한 발짝쯤 떨어진 곳에 멈춰 서서 당혹스럽게 말했다. \"만나 주셔서 정말 감사합니다.\""]], [["“I wanted to see you,”", "\"저도 당신을 만나고 싶었어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, having no other words at command.", "달리 할 말이 떠오르지 않은 도러시아가 말했다."], ["It did not occur to her to sit down, and Will did not give a cheerful interpretation to this queenly way of receiving him; but he went on to say what he had made up his mind to say.", "그녀는 앉을 생각이 미처 들지 않았고, 윌은 자신을 맞이하는 이 여왕 같은 태도를 밝게 해석하지 않았다. 그래도 그는 마음먹고 온 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“I fear you think me foolish and perhaps wrong for coming back so soon.", "\"이렇게 빨리 돌아온 제가 어리석고, 어쩌면 잘못했다고 여기실까 봐 두렵습니다."], ["I have been punished for my impatience.", "제 조급함에 대한 벌을 받았지요."], ["You know—every one knows now—a painful story about my parentage.", "아시겠지만, 이제는 누구나 알고 있어요. 제 출생에 관한 고통스러운 이야기를요."], ["I knew of it before I went away, and I always meant to tell you of it if—if we ever met again.”", "저는 떠나기 전에 그 일을 알고 있었고, 만약 우리가 다시 만나게 된다면 그 이야기를 꼭 당신께 하려고 늘 마음먹고 있었습니다.\""]], [["There was a slight movement in Dorothea, and she unclasped her hands, but immediately folded them over each other.", "도러시아에게서 가벼운 움직임이 일더니, 그녀는 모았던 두 손을 풀었다가 곧바로 다시 포개어 쥐었다."]], [["“But the affair is matter of gossip now,”", "\"하지만 이제 그 일은 사람들의 입에 오르내리는 가십거리가 되었지요.\""], ["Will continued.", "윌이 말을 이었다."], ["“I wished you to know that something connected with it—something which happened before I went away, helped to bring me down here again.", "\"당신께 알리고 싶었던 것은, 그 일과 연관된 어떤 것, 제가 떠나기 전에 일어난 어떤 일이 저를 이곳으로 다시 끌어 내려오게 하는 데 한몫했다는 점입니다. 적어도 그것이 제가 온 것을 변명해 준다고 생각했어요."], ["At least I thought it excused my coming.", "벌스트로드에게 어떤 돈을 공적인 목적에 쓰도록 하려는 생각이었지요."], ["It was the idea of getting Bulstrode to apply some money to a public purpose—some money which he had thought of giving me.", "그가 제게 주려고 마음먹었던 어떤 돈을요. 어쩌면 그가 옛 잘못에 대해 제게 은밀히 보상을 제안한 것은 다소 그의 명예가 될지도 모르겠습니다."], ["Perhaps it is rather to Bulstrode’s credit that he privately offered me compensation for an old injury: he offered to give me a good income to make amends; but I suppose you know the disagreeable story?”", "그는 그 잘못을 갚으려고 제게 넉넉한 수입을 주겠다고 제안했어요. 하지만 그 불쾌한 이야기는 당신도 아시겠지요?\""]], [["Will looked doubtfully at Dorothea, but his manner was gathering some of the defiant courage with which he always thought of this fact in his destiny.", "윌은 의심스러운 눈으로 도러시아를 바라보았지만, 그의 태도에는 자신의 운명에 얽힌 이 사실을 떠올릴 때면 늘 따라붙던 반항적인 용기가 다소 깃들고 있었다. 그는 덧붙였다."], ["He added, “You know that it must be altogether painful to me.”", "\"제게는 그것이 전적으로 고통스러운 일이라는 걸 아시겠지요.\""]], [["“Yes—yes—I know,”", "\"네, 네, 알아요.\""], ["said Dorothea, hastily.", "도러시아가 황급히 말했다."]], [["“I did not choose to accept an income from such a source.", "\"저는 그런 출처에서 나온 수입을 받아들이기로 선택하지 않았습니다."], ["I was sure that you would not think well of me if I did so,”", "제가 그렇게 한다면 당신이 저를 좋게 여기지 않으리라고 확신했거든요.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["Why should he mind saying anything of that sort to her now?", "이제 그녀에게 그런 종류의 이야기를 하는 것을 그가 왜 꺼리겠는가?"], ["She knew that he had avowed his love for her.", "그녀는 그가 자신에 대한 사랑을 고백했다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["“I felt that”—he broke off, nevertheless.", "\"저는 그렇게 느꼈습니다.\" 그러나 그는 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“You acted as I should have expected you to act,”", "\"제가 예상한 대로 행동하셨군요.\" 도러시아가 말했다."], ["said Dorothea, her face brightening and her head becoming a little more erect on its beautiful stem.", "그녀의 얼굴이 환해지며, 아름다운 줄기 같은 목 위에서 머리가 조금 더 꼿꼿이 들렸다."]], [["“I did not believe that you would let any circumstance of my birth create a prejudice in you against me, though it was sure to do so in others,”", "\"제 출생의 어떤 정황이 다른 사람들에게는 분명히 저에 대한 편견을 심어 줄지언정, 당신만은 그것으로 인해 저에 대한 편견을 품지 않으리라 믿었습니다.\""], ["said Will, shaking his head backward in his old way, and looking with a grave appeal into her eyes.", "윌이 예전 버릇대로 고개를 뒤로 젖히며, 진지한 호소를 담아 그녀의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“If it were a new hardship it would be a new reason for me to cling to you,”", "\"그것이 새로운 시련이라면, 그것은 오히려 제가 당신께 매달릴 새로운 이유가 될 거예요.\" 도러시아가 열렬히 말했다."], ["said Dorothea, fervidly.", "\"저를 변하게 할 수 있는 것은 아무것도 없었어요."], ["“Nothing could have changed me but—”", "다만……\" 그녀의 가슴이 벅차올라 말을 잇기가 어려웠다."], ["her heart was swelling, and it was difficult to go on; she made a great effort over herself to say in a low tremulous voice, “but thinking that you were different—not so good as I had believed you to be.”", "그녀는 안간힘을 써서 떨리는 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"다만 당신이 제가 믿었던 모습과 다르다고, 제가 믿었던 만큼 훌륭하지 않다고 생각하는 것 말고는요.\""]], [["“You are sure to believe me better than I am in everything but one,”", "\"당신은 한 가지만 빼면 모든 면에서 저를 실제 모습보다 더 낫게 믿어 주실 게 분명합니다.\""], ["said Will, giving way to his own feeling in the evidence of hers.", "윌이 그녀의 감정이 드러나는 모습에 자신의 감정도 내맡기며 말했다."], ["“I mean, in my truth to you.", "\"그 한 가지란, 당신을 향한 제 진심을 두고 하는 말입니다."], ["When I thought you doubted of that, I didn’t care about anything that was left.", "당신이 그것을 의심한다고 생각했을 때, 저는 남아 있는 그 무엇에도 마음이 가지 않았어요."], ["I thought it was all over with me, and there was nothing to try for—only things to endure.”", "제게는 모든 것이 끝났고, 이루려고 애쓸 것은 아무것도 없으며, 그저 견뎌야 할 일들만 남았다고 생각했지요.\""]], [["“I don’t doubt you any longer,”", "\"이제는 당신을 더 이상 의심하지 않아요.\""], ["said Dorothea, putting out her hand; a vague fear for him impelling her unutterable affection.", "도러시아가 손을 내밀며 말했다. 그를 향한 막연한 두려움이 형언할 수 없는 애정을 부추기고 있었다."]], [["He took her hand and raised it to his lips with something like a sob.", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 흐느낌 같은 무언가와 함께 입술로 가져갔다."], ["But he stood with his hat and gloves in the other hand, and might have done for the portrait of a Royalist.", "그러나 그는 다른 손에 모자와 장갑을 든 채 서 있었으니, 어느 왕당파의 초상화 속 인물로 보일 만했다."], ["Still it was difficult to loose the hand, and Dorothea, withdrawing it in a confusion that distressed her, looked and moved away.", "그래도 그 손을 놓기가 어려웠고, 도러시아는 자신을 괴롭게 하는 혼란 속에서 손을 빼며, 시선을 돌리고 자리를 옮겼다."]], [["“See how dark the clouds have become, and how the trees are tossed,”", "\"보세요, 구름이 얼마나 어두워졌는지, 나무들이 얼마나 휘청대는지.\""], ["she said, walking towards the window, yet speaking and moving with only a dim sense of what she was doing.", "그녀가 창 쪽으로 걸어가며 말했다. 그러나 말하고 움직이면서도 자신이 무엇을 하고 있는지는 어렴풋이만 의식할 뿐이었다."]], [["Will followed her at a little distance, and leaned against the tall back of a leather chair, on which he ventured now to lay his hat and gloves, and free himself from the intolerable durance of formality to which he had been for the first time condemned in Dorothea’s presence.", "윌은 조금 떨어져 그녀를 뒤따라가, 가죽 의자의 높은 등받이에 기댔는데, 이제는 그 위에 모자와 장갑을 올려놓고서, 도러시아 앞에서 난생처음 떠안았던 그 견딜 수 없는 격식의 속박에서 자신을 풀어놓을 만큼의 여유를 부려 보았다."], ["It must be confessed that he felt very happy at that moment leaning on the chair.", "고백하건대 그는 그 순간 의자에 기댄 채 무척 행복했다."], ["He was not much afraid of anything that she might feel now.", "그는 이제 그녀가 무엇을 느끼든 별로 두렵지 않았다."]], [["They stood silent, not looking at each other, but looking at the evergreens which were being tossed, and were showing the pale underside of their leaves against the blackening sky.", "두 사람은 말없이 서서 서로를 바라보지 않고, 휘청대는 상록수들을, 검게 물들어 가는 하늘을 배경으로 잎의 창백한 뒷면을 드러내는 그 나무들을 바라보았다."], ["Will never enjoyed the prospect of a storm so much: it delivered him from the necessity of going away.", "윌은 폭풍이 다가오는 풍경을 그토록 즐겁게 여긴 적이 없었다. 그것이 그를 떠나야 하는 처지에서 풀어 주었기 때문이다."], ["Leaves and little branches were hurled about, and the thunder was getting nearer.", "나뭇잎과 잔가지들이 사방으로 내던져졌고, 천둥소리가 점점 가까워졌다."], ["The light was more and more sombre, but there came a flash of lightning which made them start and look at each other, and then smile.", "빛은 점점 더 음울해졌으나, 번쩍하고 번개가 치자 두 사람은 흠칫 놀라 서로를 바라보았고, 그러고는 미소 지었다."], ["Dorothea began to say what she had been thinking of.", "도러시아가 생각하고 있던 것을 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“That was a wrong thing for you to say, that you would have had nothing to try for.", "\"이루려고 애쓸 것이 아무것도 없었다고 하신 건 잘못된 말이에요."], ["If we had lost our own chief good, other people’s good would remain, and that is worth trying for.", "우리가 우리 자신의 가장 큰 행복을 잃는다 해도, 다른 사람들의 행복은 남아 있을 테고, 그것은 애써 볼 만한 가치가 있는 일이에요."], ["Some can be happy.", "어떤 이들은 행복할 수 있으니까요."], ["I seemed to see that more clearly than ever, when I was the most wretched.", "저는 가장 비참했을 때 오히려 그것을 그 어느 때보다 또렷이 알아본 것 같았어요."], ["I can hardly think how I could have borne the trouble, if that feeling had not come to me to make strength.”", "그런 느낌이 제게 와서 힘을 주지 않았다면, 제가 그 고난을 어떻게 견뎌 냈을지 짐작도 못 하겠어요.\""]], [["“You have never felt the sort of misery I felt,”", "\"당신은 제가 느낀 그런 비참함을 결코 느껴 보지 못했을 겁니다.\""], ["said Will; “the misery of knowing that you must despise me.”", "윌이 말했다. \"당신이 저를 경멸할 수밖에 없다는 것을 아는 비참함을요.\""]], [["“But I have felt worse—it was worse to think ill—”", "\"하지만 저는 더한 것을 느꼈어요. 나쁘게 생각하는 게 더 괴로웠어요…….\""], ["Dorothea had begun impetuously, but broke off.", "도러시아가 격하게 말을 꺼냈다가 멈추었다."]], [["Will colored.", "윌은 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["He had the sense that whatever she said was uttered in the vision of a fatality that kept them apart.", "그녀가 무슨 말을 하든 그것이 두 사람을 갈라놓는 어떤 숙명을 내다보며 내뱉는 말이라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["He was silent a moment, and then said passionately—", "그는 잠시 침묵하다가, 격정적으로 말했다."]], [["“We may at least have the comfort of speaking to each other without disguise.", "\"적어도 우리는 서로에게 거짓 없이 이야기하는 위안만은 누릴 수 있겠지요. 저는 떠나야만 하니까요."], ["Since I must go away—since we must always be divided—you may think of me as one on the brink of the grave.”", "우리는 언제까지나 갈라져 있어야 하니까요. 그러니 당신은 저를 무덤가에 선 사람으로 여기셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["While he was speaking there came a vivid flash of lightning which lit each of them up for the other—and the light seemed to be the terror of a hopeless love.", "그가 말하는 동안 선명한 번개가 번쩍이며 두 사람을 서로에게 환히 비추었는데, 그 빛은 마치 가망 없는 사랑의 공포처럼 보였다."], ["Dorothea darted instantaneously from the window; Will followed her, seizing her hand with a spasmodic movement; and so they stood, with their hands clasped, like two children, looking out on the storm, while the thunder gave a tremendous crack and roll above them, and the rain began to pour down.", "도러시아가 순식간에 창가에서 물러섰고, 윌이 그녀를 따라가 발작적인 동작으로 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다. 그렇게 두 사람은 손을 맞잡고 두 아이처럼 서서 폭풍을 내다보았으며, 그동안 머리 위에서 천둥이 엄청난 굉음과 함께 우르릉 울렸고 비가 쏟아지기 시작했다."], ["Then they turned their faces towards each other, with the memory of his last words in them, and they did not loose each other’s hands.", "그러더니 그들은 그의 마지막 말을 마음에 새긴 채 서로를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸고, 맞잡은 손을 놓지 않았다."]], [["“There is no hope for me,”", "\"저에게는 희망이 없습니다.\""], ["said Will.", "윌이 말했다."], ["“Even if you loved me as well as I love you—even if I were everything to you—I shall most likely always be very poor: on a sober calculation, one can count on nothing but a creeping lot.", "\"설령 당신이 제가 당신을 사랑하는 만큼 저를 사랑한다 해도, 설령 제가 당신에게 전부라 해도, 저는 십중팔구 언제까지나 무척 가난할 거예요. 냉정하게 따져 보면 비루하게 기어가는 운명 말고는 기대할 게 아무것도 없습니다."], ["It is impossible for us ever to belong to each other.", "우리가 서로의 사람이 된다는 건 결코 불가능한 일이에요."], ["It is perhaps base of me to have asked for a word from you.", "당신에게서 한마디를 청한 것이 어쩌면 비열한 짓이었는지도 모르지요."], ["I meant to go away into silence, but I have not been able to do what I meant.”", "저는 침묵 속으로 떠나려 했는데, 마음먹은 대로 하지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“Don’t be sorry,”", "\"미안해하지 마세요.\""], ["said Dorothea, in her clear tender tones.", "도러시아가 맑고 다정한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I would rather share all the trouble of our parting.”", "\"차라리 저는 우리 이별의 모든 괴로움을 함께 나누고 싶어요.\""]], [["Her lips trembled, and so did his.", "그녀의 입술이 떨렸고, 그의 입술도 떨렸다."], ["It was never known which lips were the first to move towards the other lips; but they kissed tremblingly, and then they moved apart.", "어느 쪽 입술이 먼저 다른 입술을 향해 움직였는지는 끝내 알 수 없었으나, 두 사람은 떨면서 입을 맞추었고, 그러고는 떨어졌다."]], [["The rain was dashing against the window-panes as if an angry spirit were within it, and behind it was the great swoop of the wind; it was one of those moments in which both the busy and the idle pause with a certain awe.", "빗줄기가 마치 그 안에 성난 영혼이라도 깃든 듯 창유리를 후려쳤고, 그 뒤로는 바람의 거센 휘몰아침이 있었다. 분주한 사람도 한가로운 사람도 한결같이 어떤 경외감에 사로잡혀 멈춰 서게 되는 그런 순간 가운데 하나였다."]], [["Dorothea sat down on the seat nearest to her, a long low ottoman in the middle of the room, and with her hands folded over each other on her lap, looked at the drear outer world.", "도러시아는 가장 가까운 자리, 방 한가운데 놓인 길고 낮은 오토만에 앉아, 두 손을 무릎 위에 포개어 놓고 황량한 바깥 세계를 바라보았다."], ["Will stood still an instant looking at her, then seated himself beside her, and laid his hand on hers, which turned itself upward to be clasped.", "윌은 잠시 가만히 선 채 그녀를 바라보다가, 그녀 곁에 자리를 잡고 앉아 그녀의 손 위에 자신의 손을 얹었는데, 그 손은 맞잡히려는 듯 위로 돌아 펴졌다."], ["They sat in that way without looking at each other, until the rain abated and began to fall in stillness.", "두 사람은 그렇게 서로를 바라보지 않은 채, 비가 잦아들어 고요히 내리기 시작할 때까지 앉아 있었다."], ["Each had been full of thoughts which neither of them could begin to utter.", "둘 다 어느 쪽도 차마 입 밖에 낼 수 없는 생각들로 가득 차 있었다."]], [["But when the rain was quiet, Dorothea turned to look at Will.", "그러나 빗소리가 잠잠해지자 도러시아가 윌을 보려고 돌아앉았다."], ["With passionate exclamation, as if some torture screw were threatening him, he started up and said, “It is impossible!”", "마치 무슨 고문 나사가 그를 위협하기라도 하는 듯, 그는 격정에 찬 탄성을 지르며 벌떡 일어나 말했다. \"불가능해요!\""]], [["He went and leaned on the back of the chair again, and seemed to be battling with his own anger, while she looked towards him sadly.", "그는 다시 의자 등받이로 가서 기대고는 자신의 분노와 씨름하는 것 같았고, 그동안 그녀는 슬픈 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“It is as fatal as a murder or any other horror that divides people,”", "\"이건 살인이나, 사람들을 갈라놓는 그 어떤 끔찍한 일만큼이나 치명적이에요.\" 그가 다시 터뜨렸다."], ["he burst out again; “it is more intolerable—to have our life maimed by petty accidents.”", "\"아니, 더 견딜 수 없는 일이지요. 우리의 삶이 하찮은 우연들 때문에 불구가 되어 버린다는 게.\""]], [["“No—don’t say that—your life need not be maimed,”", "\"아니에요, 그런 말 하지 마세요. 당신의 삶이 불구가 될 필요는 없어요.\""], ["said Dorothea, gently.", "도러시아가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it must,”", "\"아니요, 그럴 수밖에 없어요.\""], ["said Will, angrily.", "윌이 화가 나서 말했다."], ["“It is cruel of you to speak in that way—as if there were any comfort.", "\"그런 식으로 말하다니, 마치 무슨 위안거리라도 있는 것처럼 말하다니 당신은 잔인하군요."], ["You may see beyond the misery of it, but I don’t.", "당신은 그 비참함 너머를 볼 수 있을지 몰라도, 저는 못 봅니다."], ["It is unkind—it is throwing back my love for you as if it were a trifle, to speak in that way in the face of the fact.", "그건 매정한 일이에요. 이런 엄연한 사실 앞에서 그렇게 말하는 것은, 당신을 향한 제 사랑을 마치 하찮은 것인 양 도로 내던져 버리는 거예요."], ["We can never be married.”", "우리는 결코 결혼할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Some time—we might,”", "\"언젠가는, 우리도 할 수 있을지 몰라요.\""], ["said Dorothea, in a trembling voice.", "도러시아가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“When?”", "\"언제요?\""], ["said Will, bitterly.", "윌이 씁쓸하게 말했다."], ["“What is the use of counting on any success of mine?", "\"제가 무슨 성공이라도 거두리라 기대하는 게 무슨 소용입니까?"], ["It is a mere toss up whether I shall ever do more than keep myself decently, unless I choose to sell myself as a mere pen and a mouthpiece.", "제가 한낱 펜이며 대변자로 저 자신을 팔아넘기기를 택하지 않는 한, 제가 그저 그럭저럭 제 한 몸 건사하는 것 이상을 해낼 수 있을지는 순전히 운에 맡길 일이에요."], ["I can see that clearly enough.", "그건 제 눈에도 충분히 똑똑히 보입니다."], ["I could not offer myself to any woman, even if she had no luxuries to renounce.”", "저는 어떤 여자에게도, 설령 그 여자가 포기할 사치라곤 하나도 없다 해도, 제 자신을 내밀 수 없어요.\""]], [["There was silence.", "침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Dorothea’s heart was full of something that she wanted to say, and yet the words were too difficult.", "도러시아의 가슴은 말하고 싶은 무언가로 가득했지만, 그 말은 너무도 꺼내기 어려웠다."], ["She was wholly possessed by them: at that moment debate was mute within her.", "그녀는 온통 그 말에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그 순간 그녀의 내면에서는 어떤 갈등도 입을 다물고 있었다."], ["And it was very hard that she could not say what she wanted to say.", "그리고 하고 싶은 말을 할 수 없다는 것이 무척이나 괴로웠다."], ["Will was looking out of the window angrily.", "윌은 화난 얼굴로 창밖을 내다보고 있었다."], ["If he would have looked at her and not gone away from her side, she thought everything would have been easier.", "그가 그녀를 바라보며 곁을 떠나지만 않는다면 모든 것이 더 수월하리라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["At last he turned, still resting against the chair, and stretching his hand automatically towards his hat, said with a sort of exasperation, “Good-by.”", "마침내 그는 여전히 의자에 몸을 기댄 채 돌아서서, 무의식적으로 모자를 향해 손을 뻗으며 일종의 격앙된 어조로 말했다. \"그럼, 안녕히.\""]], [["“Oh, I cannot bear it—my heart will break,” said Dorothea, starting from her seat, the flood of her young passion bearing down all the obstructions which had kept her silent—the great tears rising and falling in an instant: “I don’t mind about poverty—I hate my wealth.”", "\"오, 저는 견딜 수 없어요. 제 심장이 터질 거예요.\" 도러시아가 자리에서 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. 그녀의 젊은 열정의 물결이 그녀를 침묵하게 했던 온갖 장애물을 한꺼번에 무너뜨렸고, 굵은 눈물방울이 순식간에 차올라 흘러내렸다. \"저는 가난 따위 상관없어요. 제 재산이 미워요.\""]], [["In an instant Will was close to her and had his arms round her, but she drew her head back and held his away gently that she might go on speaking, her large tear-filled eyes looking at his very simply, while she said in a sobbing childlike way, “We could live quite well on my own fortune—it is too much—seven hundred a-year—I want so little—no new clothes—and I will learn what everything costs.”", "순식간에 윌이 그녀에게 바싹 다가가 두 팔로 그녀를 감쌌으나, 그녀는 말을 계속하려고 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 그의 머리를 부드럽게 밀어냈다. 눈물이 그렁그렁 고인 커다란 눈으로 너무도 천진하게 그를 바라보며, 흐느끼는 어린아이처럼 말했다. \"우리는 제 재산만으로도 아주 잘 살 수 있어요. 너무 많거든요. 연 칠백 파운드나 돼요. 저는 정말 적게 들어요. 새 옷도 필요 없고요. 그리고 모든 것이 얼마나 드는지 배울게요.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXXXIV.", "# CHAPTER LXXXIV."]], [["“Though it be songe of old and yonge, That I sholde be to blame, Theyrs be the charge, that spoke so large In hurtynge of my name.” —The Not-Browne Mayde.", "상원이 개혁 법안을 막 부결시킨 직후의 일이었다."], ["It was just after the Lords had thrown out the Reform Bill: that explains how Mr. Cadwallader came to be walking on the slope of the lawn near the great conservatory at Freshitt Hall, holding the “Times”", "그것이 바로 캐드월러더 씨가 프레시트 홀의 큰 온실 근처 잔디밭 비탈을 거닐며 등 뒤로 「타임스」를 든 채, 송어 낚시꾼다운 초연함으로 제임스 체텀 경에게 나라의 앞날을 이야기하게 된 까닭이었다."], ["in his hands behind him, while he talked with a trout-fisher’s dispassionateness about the prospects of the country to Sir James Chettam. Mrs.", "캐드월러더 부인과 노부인 체텀 부인, 그리고 실리아는 때로 정원 의자에 앉아 있기도 하고, 때로는 어린 아서를 맞으러 걸어가기도 했다."], ["Cadwallader, the Dowager Lady Chettam, and Celia were sometimes seated on garden-chairs, sometimes walking to meet little Arthur, who was being drawn in his chariot, and, as became the infantine Bouddha, was sheltered by his sacred umbrella with handsome silken fringe.", "아서는 마차에 실려 끌려오고 있었는데, 어린 부처에게 어울리게 아름다운 비단 술이 달린 신성한 양산 아래 그늘져 있었다."]], [["The ladies also talked politics, though more fitfully. Mrs.", "부인들도 정치 이야기를 했지만, 좀 더 띄엄띄엄했다."], ["Cadwallader was strong on the intended creation of peers: she had it for certain from her cousin that Truberry had gone over to the other side entirely at the instigation of his wife, who had scented peerages in the air from the very first introduction of the Reform question, and would sign her soul away to take precedence of her younger sister, who had married a baronet.", "캐드월러더 부인은 작위 수여 계획에 관해 단호했다. 그녀는 사촌에게서 확실히 들은 바, 트루베리가 그의 아내가 부추긴 탓에 완전히 반대편으로 넘어갔다는 것이었다."], ["Lady Chettam thought that such conduct was very reprehensible, and remembered that Mrs.", "그 아내는 개혁 문제가 처음 제기된 바로 그 순간부터 작위의 냄새를 맡았고, 준남작과 결혼한 동생보다 윗자리를 차지할 수만 있다면 영혼이라도 팔아넘길 여자였다."], ["Truberry’s mother was a Miss Walsingham of Melspring.", "체텀 부인은 그런 행동이 참으로 비난받아 마땅하다고 여겼으며, 트루베리 부인의 어머니가 멜스프링의 월싱엄 양이었던 것을 기억해 냈다."], ["Celia confessed it was nicer to be “Lady” than “Mrs.,”", "실리아는 \"부인\"보다 \"귀부인\"이 더 좋다는 점은 인정하면서도, 도도는 제 뜻대로 할 수만 있다면 윗자리 따위는 전혀 신경 쓰지 않는다고 했다."], ["and that Dodo never minded about precedence if she could have her own way. Mrs.", "캐드월러더 부인은 주위 모든 사람이 당신의 핏줄에 좋은 피라곤 한 방울도 없음을 아는데 윗자리를 차지한들 그건 보잘것없는 만족이라고 했다."], ["Cadwallader held that it was a poor satisfaction to take precedence when everybody about you knew that you had not a drop of good blood in your veins; and Celia again, stopping to look at Arthur, said, “It would be very nice, though, if he were a Viscount—and his lordship’s little tooth coming through!", "그러자 실리아는 다시 아서를 보려고 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"그래도 이 아이가 자작이라면 참 좋을 텐데요."], ["He might have been, if James had been an Earl.”", "자작님의 작은 이가 막 나오는데 말이에요! 제임스가 백작이었다면 이 아이도 자작이 되었을 텐데.\""]], [["“My dear Celia,”", "\"실리아야,\""], ["said the Dowager, “James’s title is worth far more than any new earldom.", "노부인이 말했다. \"제임스의 작위는 어떤 새 백작 작위보다도 훨씬 값진 거란다."], ["I never wished his father to be anything else than Sir James.”", "나는 제임스의 아버지가 제임스 경 외에 다른 무엇이기를 바란 적이 한 번도 없어.\""]], [["“Oh, I only meant about Arthur’s little tooth,”", "\"아, 저는 그저 아서의 작은 이를 두고 한 말이에요.\""], ["said Celia, comfortably.", "실리아가 편안하게 말했다."], ["“But see, here is my uncle coming.”", "\"그런데 보세요, 저기 외삼촌이 오시네요.\""]], [["She tripped off to meet her uncle, while Sir James and Mr.", "실리아는 종종걸음으로 외삼촌을 맞으러 갔고, 제임스 경과 캐드월러더 씨는 부인들과 한데 모이려고 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["Cadwallader came forward to make one group with the ladies.", "실리아는 외삼촌의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼웠고, 그는 다소 우울하게 \"그래, 얘야!\""], ["Celia had slipped her arm through her uncle’s, and he patted her hand with a rather melancholy “Well, my dear!”", "하면서 그녀의 손을 토닥였다. 그들이 다가오자 브룩 씨가 풀이 죽어 보인다는 게 분명했지만, 이는 정치 상황으로 충분히 설명될 만한 일이었다."], ["As they approached, it was evident that Mr.", "그가 \"음, 다들 여기 모였군그래\""], ["Brooke was looking dejected, but this was fully accounted for by the state of politics; and as he was shaking hands all round without more greeting than a “Well, you’re all here, you know,” the Rector said, laughingly—", "하는 인사 외엔 별다른 말도 없이 두루 악수를 나누자, 교구 목사가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t take the throwing out of the Bill so much to heart, Brooke; you’ve got all the riff-raff of the country on your side.”", "\"법안 부결을 그렇게 마음에 담아 두지 마시오, 브룩. 당신 편엔 이 나라의 온갖 잡것들이 다 붙어 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["“The Bill, eh? ah!”", "\"법안 말이오, 응?"], ["said Mr.", "아!\""], ["Brooke, with a mild distractedness of manner.", "브룩 씨가 온화하면서도 멍한 태도로 말했다."], ["“Thrown out, you know, eh?", "\"부결됐지, 응?"], ["The Lords are going too far, though.", "하지만 상원이 너무 지나치게 나가고 있어요."], ["They’ll have to pull up.", "결국 고삐를 죄어야 할 거요."], ["Sad news, you know.", "슬픈 소식이오."], ["I mean, here at home—sad news.", "그러니까, 여기 집안에서 말이오."], ["But you must not blame me, Chettam.”", "슬픈 소식이오. 하지만 나를 탓해선 안 되네, 체텀.\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일입니까?\""], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."], ["“Not another gamekeeper shot, I hope?", "\"또 사냥터지기가 총에 맞은 건 아니겠죠?"], ["It’s what I should expect, when a fellow like Trapping Bass is let off so easily.”", "트래핑 배스 같은 놈을 그렇게 쉽게 풀어 주니, 그럴 만도 하지요.\""]], [["“Gamekeeper?", "\"사냥터지기?"], ["No.", "아니야."], ["Let us go in; I can tell you all in the house, you know,”", "안으로 들어가세. 집 안에서 다 말해 줄 테니, 알겠나.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, nodding at the Cadwalladers, to show that he included them in his confidence.", "브룩 씨가 캐드월러더 부부를 향해 고개를 끄덕여, 그들도 자신의 비밀에 끼워 준다는 뜻을 내비치며 말했다."], ["“As to poachers like Trapping Bass, you know, Chettam,”", "\"트래핑 배스 같은 밀렵꾼들 말인데, 체텀,\""], ["he continued, as they were entering, “when you are a magistrate, you’ll not find it so easy to commit.", "그가 안으로 들어서며 말을 이었다. \"자네도 치안 판사가 되어 보면 사람을 구금하기가 그리 쉽지 않다는 걸 알게 될 거야."], ["Severity is all very well, but it’s a great deal easier when you’ve got somebody to do it for you.", "엄격함도 좋지만, 그걸 대신 해 줄 누군가가 있을 때 훨씬 더 쉬운 법이지."], ["You have a soft place in your heart yourself, you know—you’re not a Draco, a Jeffreys, that sort of thing.”", "자네도 마음 한구석은 무른 데가 있잖나. 자네는 드라코나 제프리스, 그런 부류는 아니니까 말이야.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke was evidently in a state of nervous perturbation.", "브룩 씨는 분명 신경이 곤두선 상태였다."], ["When he had something painful to tell, it was usually his way to introduce it among a number of disjointed particulars, as if it were a medicine that would get a milder flavor by mixing.", "무언가 괴로운 이야기를 전해야 할 때면, 그는 흔히 그것을 여러 두서없는 자질구레한 이야기들 사이에 끼워 넣어 꺼내는 버릇이 있었으니, 마치 다른 것과 섞으면 맛이 좀 순해지는 약이라도 되는 듯했다."], ["He continued his chat with Sir James about the poachers until they were all seated, and Mrs. Cadwallader, impatient of this drivelling, said—", "그는 모두가 자리에 앉을 때까지 제임스 경과 밀렵꾼 이야기를 계속했고, 마침내 이런 군소리를 못 견딘 캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I’m dying to know the sad news.", "\"슬픈 소식이 뭔지 궁금해 죽겠네요."], ["The gamekeeper is not shot: that is settled.", "사냥터지기가 총에 맞은 건 아니다, 그건 정해졌고요."], ["What is it, then?”", "그럼 대체 뭐예요?\""]], [["“Well, it’s a very trying thing, you know,”", "\"음, 참으로 괴로운 일이오.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“I’m glad you and the Rector are here; it’s a family matter—but you will help us all to bear it, Cadwallader.", "\"당신과 목사님이 여기 계셔서 다행이오. 집안일이지만, 당신들이 우리 모두가 이걸 견디도록 도와줄 테니까, 캐드월러더."], ["I’ve got to break it to you, my dear.” Here Mr.", "너에게 알려 줘야겠구나, 얘야.\""], ["Brooke looked at Celia—“You’ve no notion what it is, you know.", "여기서 브룩 씨는 실리아를 바라보았다. \"무슨 일인지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야."], ["And, Chettam, it will annoy you uncommonly—but, you see, you have not been able to hinder it, any more than I have.", "그리고 체텀, 자네는 무척 언짢을 걸세. 하지만 알다시피 자네도 나처럼 이걸 막을 수가 없었어."], ["There’s something singular in things: they come round, you know.”", "세상일엔 묘한 데가 있어. 결국 돌고 도는 법이거든.\""]], [["“It must be about Dodo,”", "\"도도 언니 일이 분명해요.\""], ["said Celia, who had been used to think of her sister as the dangerous part of the family machinery.", "실리아가 말했다. 그녀는 늘 언니를 집안이라는 기계 장치 가운데 위험한 부품으로 여기는 데 익숙했다."], ["She had seated herself on a low stool against her husband’s knee.", "그녀는 남편의 무릎에 기대어 낮은 발판에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“For God’s sake let us hear what it is!”", "\"제발 무슨 일인지 들어 봅시다!\""], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“Well, you know, Chettam, I couldn’t help Casaubon’s will: it was a sort of will to make things worse.”", "\"음, 알다시피 체텀, 나는 캐소본의 유언장을 어쩔 수가 없었네. 일을 더 나쁘게 만드는 그런 유언장이었지.\""]], [["“Exactly,”", "\"바로 그겁니다.\""], ["said Sir James, hastily.", "제임스 경이 다급하게 말했다."], ["“But what is worse?”", "\"그런데 무엇이 더 나빠졌단 말입니까?\""]], [["“Dorothea is going to be married again, you know,”", "\"도러시아가 다시 결혼한다네, 알겠나.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, nodding towards Celia, who immediately looked up at her husband with a frightened glance, and put her hand on his knee.", "브룩 씨가 실리아를 향해 고개를 끄덕이며 말하자, 실리아는 즉시 겁먹은 눈빛으로 남편을 올려다보며 그의 무릎에 손을 얹었다."], ["Sir James was almost white with anger, but he did not speak.", "제임스 경은 분노로 얼굴이 거의 하얗게 질렸지만, 입을 열지는 않았다."]], [["“Merciful heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“Not to young Ladislaw?”", "\"설마 그 젊은 래디슬로한테는 아니겠지요?\""]], [["Mr. Brooke nodded, saying, “Yes; to Ladislaw,” and then fell into a prudential silence.", "브룩 씨는 고개를 끄덕이며 \"그래요, 래디슬로한테요\"라고 말하고는, 신중을 기해 침묵에 빠졌다."]], [["“You see, Humphrey!”", "\"보세요, 험프리!\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader, waving her arm towards her husband.", "캐드월러더 부인이 남편을 향해 팔을 휘저으며 말했다."], ["“Another time you will admit that I have some foresight; or rather you will contradict me and be just as blind as ever.", "\"다음번엔 내게도 어느 정도 선견지명이 있다는 걸 인정하시겠죠. 아니면 또 반박하면서 여느 때처럼 눈먼 소리나 하시겠지요."], ["You supposed that the young gentleman was gone out of the country.”", "당신은 그 젊은이가 나라를 떠났다고 여겼잖아요.\""]], [["“So he might be, and yet come back,”", "\"떠났을 수도 있지, 그러고도 돌아왔을 수도 있고.\""], ["said the Rector, quietly.", "목사가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“When did you learn this?”", "\"이걸 언제 아셨습니까?\""], ["said Sir James, not liking to hear any one else speak, though finding it difficult to speak himself.", "제임스 경이 말했다. 다른 누가 말하는 걸 듣기 싫었지만, 정작 자신이 말하기는 힘들었던 것이다."]], [["“Yesterday,”", "\"어제였네.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, meekly.", "브룩 씨가 풀죽어 말했다."], ["“I went to Lowick.", "\"로윅에 갔었지."], ["Dorothea sent for me, you know.", "도러시아가 나를 불렀거든, 알겠나."], ["It had come about quite suddenly—neither of them had any idea two days ago—not any idea, you know.", "아주 갑작스레 벌어진 일이야. 이틀 전만 해도 두 사람 다 전혀 생각이 없었어."], ["There’s something singular in things.", "전혀 말이야, 알겠나. 세상일엔 묘한 데가 있어."], ["But Dorothea is quite determined—it is no use opposing.", "하지만 도러시아는 단단히 결심했어. 반대해 봐야 소용없네."], ["I put it strongly to her.", "나는 강하게 말했어."], ["I did my duty, Chettam.", "내 도리는 다했네, 체텀."], ["But she can act as she likes, you know.”", "하지만 그 애는 제 좋을 대로 할 수 있는 거니까, 알겠나.\""]], [["“It would have been better if I had called him out and shot him a year ago,”", "\"일 년 전에 그자에게 결투를 신청해서 쏘아 버렸더라면 좋았을 것을.\""], ["said Sir James, not from bloody-mindedness, but because he needed something strong to say.", "제임스 경이 말했다. 잔인해서가 아니라, 무언가 강한 말이 필요했기 때문이었다."]], [["“Really, James, that would have been very disagreeable,”", "\"정말, 제임스, 그건 아주 불쾌한 일이었을 거예요.\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."]], [["“Be reasonable, Chettam.", "\"진정하게, 체텀."], ["Look at the affair more quietly,” said Mr.", "좀 더 차분하게 이 일을 보게나.\""], ["Cadwallader, sorry to see his good-natured friend so overmastered by anger.", "캐드월러더 씨가 말했다. 그는 마음씨 좋은 친구가 분노에 그토록 휘둘리는 모습을 보는 게 안타까웠다."]], [["“That is not so very easy for a man of any dignity—with any sense of right—when the affair happens to be in his own family,”", "\"품위라곤, 옳고 그름의 분별이라곤 조금이라도 있는 사람이라면, 그것도 그 일이 자기 집안에서 일어났을 때는, 그게 그리 쉬운 일이 아닙니다.\""], ["said Sir James, still in his white indignation.", "제임스 경이 여전히 하얗게 질린 분노 속에서 말했다."], ["“It is perfectly scandalous.", "\"그건 정말 추잡한 일입니다."], ["If Ladislaw had had a spark of honor he would have gone out of the country at once, and never shown his face in it again.", "래디슬로에게 명예심이라곤 한 줌이라도 있었다면, 당장 나라를 떠나 다시는 이곳에 얼굴을 내밀지 않았을 겁니다."], ["However, I am not surprised.", "하지만 나는 놀라지 않습니다."], ["The day after Casaubon’s funeral I said what ought to be done.", "캐소본의 장례식 다음 날 나는 어떻게 해야 하는지 말했어요."], ["But I was not listened to.”", "하지만 아무도 내 말을 듣지 않았지요.\""]], [["“You wanted what was impossible, you know, Chettam,”", "\"자네는 불가능한 걸 원했어, 알겠나, 체텀.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke.", "브룩 씨가 말했다."], ["“You wanted him shipped off.", "\"그자를 배에 태워 보내길 바랐지."], ["I told you Ladislaw was not to be done as we liked with: he had his ideas.", "나는 래디슬로를 우리 마음대로 할 수 없다고 말했네. 그자에겐 그자만의 생각이 있었어."], ["He was a remarkable fellow—I always said he was a remarkable fellow.”", "비범한 친구였지. 나는 늘 그가 비범한 친구라고 말했었네.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["said Sir James, unable to repress a retort, “it is rather a pity you formed that high opinion of him.", "제임스 경이 받아치지 않고는 못 배기겠다는 듯 말했다. \"삼촌이 그자를 그렇게 높이 평가하신 게 다소 유감입니다."], ["We are indebted to that for his being lodged in this neighborhood.", "그자가 이 동네에 자리 잡게 된 건 다 그 덕이니까요."], ["We are indebted to that for seeing a woman like Dorothea degrading herself by marrying him.”", "도러시아 같은 여인이 그자와 결혼해 스스로를 깎아내리는 꼴을 보게 된 것도 다 그 덕이고요.\""], ["Sir James made little stoppages between his clauses, the words not coming easily.", "제임스 경은 말이 쉽게 나오지 않아 구절 사이마다 조금씩 끊어 말했다."], ["“A man so marked out by her husband’s will, that delicacy ought to have forbidden her from seeing him again—who takes her out of her proper rank—into poverty—has the meanness to accept such a sacrifice—has always had an objectionable position—a bad origin—and, I believe, is a man of little principle and light character.", "\"그 여인의 남편이 유언으로 콕 집어 둔 자라, 다시는 만나지 않는 게 도리였을 자입니다. 그 여인을 제 신분에서 끌어내려 가난 속으로 몰아넣는 자, 그런 희생을 받아들이는 비열함을 가진 자, 늘 떳떳지 못한 처지에 있었던 자, 출신이 나쁜 자, 게다가 내가 보기엔 원칙도 없고 가벼운 인간입니다."], ["That is my opinion.”", "그것이 내 생각입니다.\""], ["Sir James ended emphatically, turning aside and crossing his leg.", "제임스 경은 단호하게 말을 맺고는 몸을 돌려 다리를 꼬았다."]], [["“I pointed everything out to her,” said Mr.", "\"나는 그 애에게 모든 걸 짚어 줬네.\""], ["Brooke, apologetically—“I mean the poverty, and abandoning her position.", "브룩 씨가 변명하듯 말했다. \"그러니까 가난과 신분을 버리는 일 말이야."], ["I said, ‘My dear, you don’t know what it is to live on seven hundred a-year, and have no carriage, and that kind of thing, and go amongst people who don’t know who you are.’ I put it strongly to her.", "'얘야, 너는 연 칠백 파운드로 사는 게, 마차도 없고 그런 것도 없이, 네가 누군지도 모르는 사람들 틈에서 사는 게 어떤 건지 모른다'고 말했지. 강하게 말했어."], ["But I advise you to talk to Dorothea herself.", "하지만 도러시아한테 직접 이야기해 보길 권하네."], ["The fact is, she has a dislike to Casaubon’s property.", "사실은, 그 애가 캐소본의 재산을 싫어해."], ["You will hear what she says, you know.”", "그 애 말을 들어 보면 알 걸세.\""]], [["“No—excuse me—I shall not,”", "\"아니요, 죄송하지만 그러지 않겠습니다.\""], ["said Sir James, with more coolness.", "제임스 경이 좀 더 차분하게 말했다."], ["“I cannot bear to see her again; it is too painful.", "\"그녀를 다시 볼 수가 없습니다. 너무 괴로워서요."], ["It hurts me too much that a woman like Dorothea should have done what is wrong.”", "도러시아 같은 여인이 잘못된 일을 저질렀다는 게 너무나 마음 아픕니다.\""]], [["“Be just, Chettam,”", "\"공정하게 봅시다, 체텀.\""], ["said the easy, large-lipped Rector, who objected to all this unnecessary discomfort. “Mrs.", "느긋하고 입술이 두툼한 목사가 이 모든 불필요한 불편을 못마땅해하며 말했다."], ["Casaubon may be acting imprudently: she is giving up a fortune for the sake of a man, and we men have so poor an opinion of each other that we can hardly call a woman wise who does that.", "\"캐소본 부인이 경솔하게 행동하는 건지도 모르오. 한 남자를 위해 재산을 포기하고 있으니 말이오."], ["But I think you should not condemn it as a wrong action, in the strict sense of the word.”", "우리 사내들은 서로를 워낙 하찮게 보는지라, 그런 짓을 하는 여인을 두고 현명하다고 하기는 어렵소. 하지만 엄밀한 의미에서 그걸 잘못된 행동이라고 단죄해서는 안 될 것 같소.\""]], [["“Yes, I do,”", "\"네, 그렇게 봅니다.\""], ["answered Sir James.", "제임스 경이 대답했다."], ["“I think that Dorothea commits a wrong action in marrying Ladislaw.”", "\"도러시아가 래디슬로와 결혼하는 건 잘못된 행동이라고 봅니다.\""]], [["“My dear fellow, we are rather apt to consider an act wrong because it is unpleasant to us,”", "\"이보게, 우리는 어떤 행동이 우리에게 불쾌하다고 해서 그걸 잘못이라 여기기 쉽다네.\""], ["said the Rector, quietly.", "목사가 조용히 말했다."], ["Like many men who take life easily, he had the knack of saying a home truth occasionally to those who felt themselves virtuously out of temper.", "인생을 느긋하게 사는 많은 이들이 그렇듯, 그는 스스로 의로운 분노에 휩싸였다고 여기는 이들에게 이따금 뼈아픈 진실을 한마디 던지는 재주가 있었다."], ["Sir James took out his handkerchief and began to bite the corner.", "제임스 경은 손수건을 꺼내 그 귀퉁이를 깨물기 시작했다."]], [["“It is very dreadful of Dodo, though,”", "\"그래도 도도 언니가 너무한 거예요.\""], ["said Celia, wishing to justify her husband.", "실리아가 남편을 두둔하고 싶어 말했다."], ["“She said she never would marry again—not anybody at all.”", "\"언니는 절대로 다시 결혼하지 않겠다고 했어요. 그 누구와도요.\""]], [["“I heard her say the same thing myself,”", "\"나도 그 애가 똑같은 말을 하는 걸 직접 들었지.\""], ["said Lady Chettam, majestically, as if this were royal evidence.", "체텀 부인이 마치 이것이 왕실의 증언이라도 되는 양 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, there is usually a silent exception in such cases,”", "\"오, 그런 경우엔 으레 입 밖에 내지 않은 예외가 하나 있는 법이지요.\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“The only wonder to me is, that any of you are surprised.", "\"나한테 유일하게 놀라운 건, 당신들 중 누구라도 놀란다는 거예요."], ["You did nothing to hinder it.", "당신들은 이걸 막으려고 아무것도 하지 않았어요."], ["If you would have had Lord Triton down here to woo her with his philanthropy, he might have carried her off before the year was over.", "트라이턴 경을 이리로 불러 그의 박애주의로 구애하게 했더라면, 그가 일 년이 가기 전에 그녀를 채 갔을 텐데요."], ["There was no safety in anything else. Mr.", "그 외엔 어디에도 안전이란 게 없었지요."], ["Casaubon had prepared all this as beautifully as possible.", "캐소본 씨는 이 모든 걸 더없이 아름답게 준비해 두었어요. 스스로 불쾌한 사람이 됐지요."], ["He made himself disagreeable—or it pleased God to make him so—and then he dared her to contradict him.", "아니면 하느님께서 그를 그렇게 만드신 거고요. 그러고는 감히 그녀에게 거역할 테면 해 보라고 했지요."], ["It’s the way to make any trumpery tempting, to ticket it at a high price in that way.”", "어떤 하찮은 물건이라도 솔깃하게 만드는 비결은 바로 그렇게 비싼 값을 매겨 두는 거랍니다.\""]], [["“I don’t know what you mean by wrong, Cadwallader,”", "\"잘못이라는 게 무슨 뜻인지 모르겠소, 캐드월러더.\""], ["said Sir James, still feeling a little stung, and turning round in his chair towards the Rector.", "제임스 경이 여전히 다소 찔린 기분으로 의자에 앉은 채 목사 쪽으로 돌아앉으며 말했다."], ["“He’s not a man we can take into the family.", "\"그는 우리 집안에 들일 수 있는 사람이 아니오."], ["At least, I must speak for myself,”", "적어도, 나로서는 그렇다는 말이오.\""], ["he continued, carefully keeping his eyes off Mr. Brooke.", "그는 브룩 씨에게 시선을 주지 않으려 조심하며 말을 이었다."], ["“I suppose others will find his society too pleasant to care about the propriety of the thing.”", "\"다른 분들은 그자와 어울리는 게 너무 즐거워서 이 일의 온당함 따위는 개의치 않으시겠지만요.\""]], [["“Well, you know, Chettam,” said Mr.", "\"음, 알다시피, 체텀,\""], ["Brooke, good-humoredly, nursing his leg, “I can’t turn my back on Dorothea.", "브룩 씨가 다리를 어루만지며 너그럽게 말했다. \"나는 도러시아에게 등을 돌릴 수가 없어."], ["I must be a father to her up to a certain point.", "어느 선까지는 그 애의 아버지 노릇을 해 줘야지."], ["I said, ‘My dear, I won’t refuse to give you away.’ I had spoken strongly before.", "나는 '얘야, 내가 너를 넘겨주는 일을 거절하진 않으마'"], ["But I can cut off the entail, you know.", "하고 말했네. 그전엔 강하게 말했지만 말이야."], ["It will cost money and be troublesome; but I can do it, you know.”", "하지만 한정 상속을 끊어 줄 수는 있네, 알겠나. 돈도 들고 번거롭겠지만, 그렇게 할 수 있어, 알겠나.\""]], [["Mr. Brooke nodded at Sir James, and felt that he was both showing his own force of resolution and propitiating what was just in the Baronet’s vexation.", "* 한정 상속(entail): 토지를 특정 상속인 계열에게만 물려주도록 묶어 두는 제도."], ["He had hit on a more ingenious mode of parrying than he was aware of.", "브룩 씨는 제임스 경에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였고, 자신이 결단력을 보여 주는 동시에 준남작의 짜증 가운데 정당한 부분을 달래 주고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["He had touched a motive of which Sir James was ashamed.", "그는 자신도 미처 깨닫지 못한 한층 교묘한 받아치기 방식을 찾아낸 것이었다."], ["The mass of his feeling about Dorothea’s marriage to Ladislaw was due partly to excusable prejudice, or even justifiable opinion, partly to a jealous repugnance hardly less in Ladislaw’s case than in Casaubon’s.", "그는 제임스 경이 부끄러워하는 어떤 동기를 건드렸다. 도러시아가 래디슬로와 결혼하는 데 대한 그의 감정은, 한편으로는 용서받을 만한 편견이나 심지어 정당한 견해에서 비롯된 것이었고, 다른 한편으로는 캐소본의 경우 못지않게 래디슬로의 경우에도 강한 질투 어린 혐오감에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["He was convinced that the marriage was a fatal one for Dorothea.", "그는 이 결혼이 도러시아에게 치명적인 결합이라고 확신했다."], ["But amid that mass ran a vein of which he was too good and honorable a man to like the avowal even to himself: it was undeniable that the union of the two estates—Tipton and Freshitt—lying charmingly within a ring-fence, was a prospect that flattered him for his son and heir.", "하지만 그 감정의 덩어리 가운데에는 그가 너무 선량하고 명예로운 사람인지라 스스로에게조차 인정하기 꺼리는 한 가닥 줄기가 흐르고 있었다. 부정할 수 없는 사실은, 울타리 하나 안에 매력적으로 자리한 두 영지, 즉 티프턴과 프레시트가 하나로 합쳐지는 전망이 그의 아들이자 상속인을 위해 그의 마음을 흡족하게 했다는 것이다."], ["Hence when Mr. Brooke noddingly appealed to that motive, Sir James felt a sudden embarrassment; there was a stoppage in his throat; he even blushed.", "그래서 브룩 씨가 고개를 끄덕여 그 동기를 건드리자, 제임스 경은 문득 당혹스러움을 느꼈다."], ["He had found more words than usual in the first jet of his anger, but Mr.", "목구멍이 막히는 것 같았고, 심지어 얼굴까지 붉어졌다."], ["Brooke’s propitiation was more clogging to his tongue than Mr. Cadwallader’s caustic hint.", "분노가 처음 솟구쳤을 때는 평소보다 말이 많이 나왔지만, 브룩 씨의 달램은 캐드월러더 씨의 신랄한 암시보다 그의 혀를 더 단단히 묶어 버렸다."]], [["But Celia was glad to have room for speech after her uncle’s suggestion of the marriage ceremony, and she said, though with as little eagerness of manner as if the question had turned on an invitation to dinner, “Do you mean that Dodo is going to be married directly, uncle?”", "하지만 실리아는 외삼촌이 결혼식 이야기를 꺼낸 덕에 말할 틈이 생긴 게 반가워, 마치 저녁 식사 초대에 관한 문제라도 되는 양 별 열의 없는 태도로 말했다. \"외삼촌, 도도 언니가 곧바로 결혼한다는 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“In three weeks, you know,”", "\"삼 주 후래, 알겠니.\""], ["said Mr. Brooke, helplessly.", "브룩 씨가 어쩔 수 없다는 듯 말했다."], ["“I can do nothing to hinder it, Cadwallader,”", "\"나는 막을 도리가 없네, 캐드월러더.\""], ["he added, turning for a little countenance toward the Rector, who said—", "그는 약간의 지지를 구하듯 목사 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며 덧붙였고, 목사가 말했다."]], [["“I should not make any fuss about it.", "\"나라면 이 일로 야단법석을 떨지 않겠소."], ["If she likes to be poor, that is her affair.", "그녀가 가난하게 살고 싶다면, 그건 그녀의 문제요."], ["Nobody would have said anything if she had married the young fellow because he was rich.", "그녀가 그 젊은이가 부자라서 결혼한다면 아무도 한마디 안 했을 거요."], ["Plenty of beneficed clergy are poorer than they will be.", "성직록을 받는 성직자들 중에도 그들이 살게 될 것보다 더 가난한 사람이 수두룩하오. 여기 엘리너만 봐도,\""], ["Here is Elinor,”", "짓궂은 남편이 말을 이었다."], ["continued the provoking husband; “she vexed her friends by me: I had hardly a thousand a-year—I was a lout—nobody could see anything in me—my shoes were not the right cut—all the men wondered how a woman could like me.", "\"이 사람은 나 때문에 친구들 속을 썩였소. 나는 연 천 파운드도 못 벌었고, 촌뜨기였고, 아무도 내게서 뭐 볼 만한 걸 찾지 못했고, 내 신발은 모양새가 그르고, 모든 사내가 어떻게 여자가 나를 좋아할 수 있는지 의아해했소."], ["Upon my word, I must take Ladislaw’s part until I hear more harm of him.”", "정말이지, 래디슬로에 대해 더 나쁜 소리를 듣기 전까지는 그의 편을 들어야겠소.\""]], [["“Humphrey, that is all sophistry, and you know it,”", "\"험프리, 그건 다 궤변이고, 당신도 알잖아요.\""], ["said his wife.", "그의 아내가 말했다."], ["“Everything is all one—that is the beginning and end with you.", "\"당신에겐 모든 게 다 똑같지요. 그게 처음이자 끝이에요."], ["As if you had not been a Cadwallader!", "마치 당신이 캐드월러더가 아니었던 것처럼!"], ["Does any one suppose that I would have taken such a monster as you by any other name?”", "당신이 다른 이름이었다면 내가 당신 같은 괴물을 받아들였으리라고 누가 생각이나 하겠어요?\""]], [["“And a clergyman too,”", "\"게다가 성직자이기도 하고요.\""], ["observed Lady Chettam with approbation.", "체텀 부인이 흡족하게 한마디 했다."], ["“Elinor cannot be said to have descended below her rank.", "\"엘리너가 제 신분 아래로 내려갔다고는 할 수 없지요."], ["It is difficult to say what Mr. Ladislaw is, eh, James?”", "래디슬로 씨가 무엇이라고 말하기는 어렵겠지만 말이에요, 안 그러냐, 제임스?\""]], [["Sir James gave a small grunt, which was less respectful than his usual mode of answering his mother.", "제임스 경은 짧게 끙 소리를 냈는데, 어머니에게 답하는 평소의 방식보다 덜 공손했다."], ["Celia looked up at him like a thoughtful kitten.", "실리아는 생각에 잠긴 새끼 고양이처럼 그를 올려다보았다."]], [["“It must be admitted that his blood is a frightful mixture!”", "\"그자의 핏줄이 끔찍한 혼합물이라는 건 인정해야겠지요!\""], ["said Mrs. Cadwallader.", "캐드월러더 부인이 말했다."], ["“The Casaubon cuttle-fish fluid to begin with, and then a rebellious Polish fiddler or dancing-master, was it?—and then an old clo—”", "\"우선 캐소본 집안의 오징어 먹물이 있고, 그다음엔 반항적인 폴란드 바이올린 연주자인가 무용 선생인가, 그랬던가요? 그러고는 헌 옷 장—\""]], [["“Nonsense, Elinor,”", "\"실없는 소리 말아요, 엘리너.\""], ["said the Rector, rising.", "목사가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“It is time for us to go.”", "\"이제 우리가 갈 시간이오.\""]], [["“After all, he is a pretty sprig,” said Mrs.", "\"하지만 그래도 그 젊은이는 잘생긴 도련님이지요.\""], ["Cadwallader, rising too, and wishing to make amends.", "캐드월러더 부인도 일어서며, 만회하고 싶은 마음에 말했다."], ["“He is like the fine old Crichley portraits before the idiots came in.”", "\"그는 바보들이 끼어들기 전의 저 멋진 옛 크리츨리 가문 초상화들을 닮았어요.\""]], [["“I’ll go with you,” said Mr.", "\"나도 함께 가겠소.\""], ["Brooke, starting up with alacrity.", "브룩 씨가 부랴부랴 일어서며 말했다."], ["“You must all come and dine with me to-morrow, you know—eh, Celia, my dear?”", "\"다들 내일 우리 집에 와서 식사해야 하오, 알겠소. 응, 실리아야?\""]], [["“You will, James—won’t you?”", "\"그렇게 할 거죠, 제임스, 그렇죠?\""], ["said Celia, taking her husband’s hand.", "실리아가 남편의 손을 잡으며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, of course, if you like,”", "\"오, 물론이지, 당신이 원한다면.\""], ["said Sir James, pulling down his waistcoat, but unable yet to adjust his face good-humoredly.", "제임스 경이 조끼를 끌어내리며 말했지만, 아직 표정을 너그럽게 가다듬지는 못했다."], ["“That is to say, if it is not to meet anybody else.”", "\"그러니까, 다른 누구를 만나는 자리만 아니라면 말이오.\""]], [["“No, no, no,” said Mr.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니야.\""], ["Brooke, understanding the condition.", "브룩 씨가 그 조건을 알아듣고 말했다."], ["“Dorothea would not come, you know, unless you had been to see her.”", "\"도러시아는 자네가 자기를 보러 오지 않는 한 오지 않을 테니까, 알겠나.\""]], [["When Sir James and Celia were alone, she said, “Do you mind about my having the carriage to go to Lowick, James?”", "제임스 경과 실리아만 남게 되자, 그녀가 말했다. \"제임스, 로윅에 가게 마차를 좀 써도 괜찮을까요?\""]], [["“What, now, directly?”", "\"뭐라고, 지금, 당장?\""], ["he answered, with some surprise.", "그가 다소 놀라며 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, it is very important,”", "\"네, 아주 중요한 일이에요.\""], ["said Celia.", "실리아가 말했다."]], [["“Remember, Celia, I cannot see her,”", "\"기억해 둬, 실리아, 나는 그녀를 볼 수 없소.\""], ["said Sir James.", "제임스 경이 말했다."]], [["“Not if she gave up marrying?”", "\"언니가 결혼을 그만둔다 해도요?\""]], [["“What is the use of saying that?—however, I’m going to the stables.", "\"그런 말을 해서 뭐 하겠소? 어쨌든 나는 마구간에 가겠소."], ["I’ll tell Briggs to bring the carriage round.”", "브리그스에게 마차를 대령하라고 이르지.\""]], [["Celia thought it was of great use, if not to say that, at least to take a journey to Lowick in order to influence Dorothea’s mind.", "실리아는, 그 말은 못 하더라도 적어도 도러시아의 마음을 움직이러 로윅까지 가는 것은 큰 도움이 되리라고 생각했다."], ["All through their girlhood she had felt that she could act on her sister by a word judiciously placed—by opening a little window for the daylight of her own understanding to enter among the strange colored lamps by which Dodo habitually saw.", "소녀 시절 내내 그녀는 적절히 놓인 한마디로, 도도가 늘 보고 살아온 이상한 색색의 등불들 사이로 자기 자신의 이해라는 햇빛이 들어갈 작은 창을 열어 줌으로써 언니에게 영향을 미칠 수 있다고 느껴 왔다."], ["And Celia the matron naturally felt more able to advise her childless sister.", "그리고 이제 부인이 된 실리아는 자식 없는 언니에게 충고할 자격이 더 있다고 자연스레 느꼈다."], ["How could any one understand Dodo so well as Celia did or love her so tenderly?", "그 누가 실리아만큼 도도를 잘 이해하고, 그토록 다정하게 사랑할 수 있겠는가?"]], [["Dorothea, busy in her boudoir, felt a glow of pleasure at the sight of her sister so soon after the revelation of her intended marriage.", "화장방에서 분주하던 도러시아는, 결혼 계획을 밝힌 지 이토록 얼마 되지 않아 동생이 찾아온 모습을 보고 기쁨의 온기가 차오르는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She had prefigured to herself, even with exaggeration, the disgust of her friends, and she had even feared that Celia might be kept aloof from her.", "그녀는 친구들의 반감을 미리, 그것도 과장되게 그려 보았고, 실리아마저 자신에게서 멀어질지도 모른다고 두려워하기까지 했던 것이다."]], [["“O Kitty, I am delighted to see you!”", "\"오, 키티, 와 줘서 정말 기뻐!\""], ["said Dorothea, putting her hands on Celia’s shoulders, and beaming on her.", "도러시아가 실리아의 어깨에 두 손을 얹고 환하게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I almost thought you would not come to me.”", "\"나는 네가 나를 보러 오지 않을 거라고 거의 생각했었어.\""]], [["“I have not brought Arthur, because I was in a hurry,”", "\"서두르느라 아서는 데려오지 않았어요.\""], ["said Celia, and they sat down on two small chairs opposite each other, with their knees touching.", "실리아가 말했고, 두 사람은 마주 보는 작은 의자 두 개에 무릎을 맞대고 앉았다."]], [["“You know, Dodo, it is very bad,”", "\"있잖아요, 도도 언니, 이건 정말 나빠요.\""], ["said Celia, in her placid guttural, looking as prettily free from humors as possible.", "실리아가 평온한 목쉰 소리로, 더없이 까탈스럽지 않은 예쁜 표정으로 말했다."], ["“You have disappointed us all so.", "\"언니는 우리 모두를 너무나 실망시켰어요."], ["And I can’t think that it ever will be—you never can go and live in that way.", "그리고 나는 그 일이 정말로 이루어지리라곤 생각할 수가 없어요. 언니는 절대로 그런 식으로 살아갈 수 없어요."], ["And then there are all your plans!", "게다가 언니의 그 모든 계획들은요!"], ["You never can have thought of that.", "언니는 그건 생각도 안 했을 거예요."], ["James would have taken any trouble for you, and you might have gone on all your life doing what you liked.”", "제임스는 언니를 위해서라면 어떤 수고도 마다하지 않았을 텐데, 언니는 평생 하고 싶은 일을 하며 살아갈 수도 있었어요.\""]], [["“On the contrary, dear,”", "\"오히려 그 반대란다, 얘야.\""], ["said Dorothea, “I never could do anything that I liked.", "도러시아가 말했다. \"나는 내가 하고 싶은 일을 단 한 번도 할 수가 없었어."], ["I have never carried out any plan yet.”", "아직 어떤 계획도 이루어 내지 못했지.\""]], [["“Because you always wanted things that wouldn’t do.", "\"그건 언니가 늘 될 수 없는 것들만 원했기 때문이에요."], ["But other plans would have come.", "하지만 다른 계획들이 생겼을 거예요."], ["And how can you marry Mr. Ladislaw, that we none of us ever thought you could marry?", "그리고 우리 가운데 누구도 언니가 결혼할 수 있으리라고 생각해 본 적 없는 래디슬로 씨와 어떻게 결혼할 수 있어요?"], ["It shocks James so dreadfully.", "제임스는 그 일로 너무나 충격을 받았어요."], ["And then it is all so different from what you have always been.", "게다가 그건 언니가 늘 살아온 것과 너무나 달라요."], ["You would have Mr. Casaubon because he had such a great soul, and was so old and dismal and learned; and now, to think of marrying Mr.", "언니는 캐소본 씨가 그토록 위대한 영혼을 지녔고, 또 그렇게 늙고 음울하고 학식이 많아서 그분과 결혼했잖아요."], ["Ladislaw, who has got no estate or anything.", "그런데 이제 영지도 아무것도 없는 래디슬로 씨와 결혼한다니."], ["I suppose it is because you must be making yourself uncomfortable in some way or other.”", "내 생각엔 언니가 어떻게든 스스로를 불편하게 만들고 싶어 하기 때문인 것 같아요.\""]], [["Dorothea laughed.", "도러시아가 웃었다."]], [["“Well, it is very serious, Dodo,”", "\"정말, 이건 아주 심각한 일이에요, 도도 언니.\""], ["said Celia, becoming more impressive.", "실리아가 더욱 진지해지며 말했다."], ["“How will you live?", "\"어떻게 살아갈 거예요?"], ["and you will go away among queer people.", "그리고 언니는 이상한 사람들 틈으로 떠나 버리겠죠. 나는 언니를 영영 보지 못할 거고요."], ["And I shall never see you—and you won’t mind about little Arthur—and I thought you always would—”", "언니는 어린 아서한테도 관심을 두지 않을 거예요. 나는 늘 언니가 그럴 거라고 생각했는데—\""]], [["Celia’s rare tears had got into her eyes, and the corners of her mouth were agitated.", "실리아의 좀처럼 보기 힘든 눈물이 눈에 고였고, 입가가 떨렸다."]], [["“Dear Celia,”", "\"실리아야,\""], ["said Dorothea, with tender gravity, “if you don’t ever see me, it will not be my fault.”", "도러시아가 다정하면서도 진지하게 말했다. \"네가 나를 영영 보지 못한다면, 그건 내 잘못이 아닐 거야.\""]], [["“Yes, it will,”", "\"아니에요, 언니 잘못이 될 거예요.\""], ["said Celia, with the same touching distortion of her small features.", "실리아가 작은 이목구비를 또 그렇게 가슴 아프게 일그러뜨리며 말했다. \"제임스가 견디지 못하는데 내가 어떻게 언니를 보러 가거나 언니를 우리 집에 둘 수 있겠어요?"], ["“How can I come to you or have you with me when James can’t bear it?—that is because he thinks it is not right—he thinks you are so wrong, Dodo.", "그건 그이가 이 일이 옳지 않다고 여기기 때문이에요. 그이는 언니가 너무 잘못됐다고 생각해요, 도도 언니."], ["But you always were wrong: only I can’t help loving you.", "하지만 언니는 늘 잘못됐었어요. 그래도 나는 언니를 사랑하지 않을 수가 없어요."], ["And nobody can think where you will live: where can you go?”", "그리고 언니가 어디서 살게 될지 아무도 짐작할 수 없어요. 대체 어디로 갈 거예요?\""]], [["“I am going to London,”", "\"나는 런던으로 갈 거야.\""], ["said Dorothea.", "도러시아가 말했다."]], [["“How can you always live in a street?", "\"어떻게 늘 길거리에서 살 수 있어요?"], ["And you will be so poor.", "게다가 그렇게 가난해질 텐데."], ["I could give you half my things, only how can I, when I never see you?”", "내 물건을 절반은 줄 수도 있겠지만, 언니를 통 보지 못하는데 어떻게 그러겠어요?\""]], [["“Bless you, Kitty,”", "\"고맙구나, 키티.\""], ["said Dorothea, with gentle warmth.", "도러시아가 부드러운 온정으로 말했다."], ["“Take comfort: perhaps James will forgive me some time.”", "\"마음 편히 가지렴. 어쩌면 제임스가 언젠가는 나를 용서해 줄지도 모르잖니.\""]], [["“But it would be much better if you would not be married,”", "\"하지만 언니가 결혼만 하지 않는다면 훨씬 더 좋을 거예요.\""], ["said Celia, drying her eyes, and returning to her argument; “then there would be nothing uncomfortable.", "실리아가 눈물을 닦으며 다시 자기 주장으로 돌아와 말했다. \"그러면 불편한 일이라곤 하나도 없을 거예요."], ["And you would not do what nobody thought you could do.", "그리고 아무도 언니가 할 수 있으리라 생각지 않은 일을 하지도 않을 거고요."], ["James always said you ought to be a queen; but this is not at all being like a queen.", "제임스는 늘 언니가 여왕이 되어야 한다고 했는데, 이건 도무지 여왕다운 게 아니에요."], ["You know what mistakes you have always been making, Dodo, and this is another.", "언니, 언니가 늘 어떤 실수들을 해 왔는지 알잖아요. 그리고 이건 또 하나의 실수예요."], ["Nobody thinks Mr. Ladislaw a proper husband for you.", "아무도 래디슬로 씨를 언니한테 어울리는 남편이라고 생각하지 않아요."], ["And you said you would never be married again.”", "게다가 언니는 절대 다시 결혼하지 않겠다고 말했었잖아요.\""]], [["“It is quite true that I might be a wiser person, Celia,”", "\"내가 더 현명한 사람일 수도 있었다는 건 정말 맞는 말이야, 실리아.\""], ["said Dorothea, “and that I might have done something better, if I had been better.", "도러시아가 말했다. \"그리고 내가 더 나은 사람이었다면 더 나은 일을 할 수 있었을지도 모르지."], ["But this is what I am going to do.", "하지만 이게 내가 하려는 일이야."], ["I have promised to marry Mr. Ladislaw; and I am going to marry him.”", "나는 래디슬로 씨와 결혼하기로 약속했고, 그와 결혼할 거야.\""]], [["The tone in which Dorothea said this was a note that Celia had long learned to recognize.", "도러시아가 이 말을 하는 어조는 실리아가 오래전부터 알아차리도록 익혀 온 곡조였다."], ["She was silent a few moments, and then said, as if she had dismissed all contest, “Is he very fond of you, Dodo?”", "그녀는 잠시 말이 없다가, 모든 다툼을 접은 듯이 말했다. \"그 사람이 언니를 무척 좋아해요, 도도 언니?\""]], [["“I hope so.", "\"그러길 바라."], ["I am very fond of him.”", "나는 그를 무척 좋아해.\""]], [["“That is nice,”", "\"그건 좋네요.\""], ["said Celia, comfortably.", "실리아가 편안하게 말했다."], ["“Only I would rather you had such a sort of husband as James is, with a place very near, that I could drive to.”", "\"다만 나는 언니가 제임스 같은 남편을 두었으면 했어요. 집도 아주 가까워서 내가 마차로 갈 수 있는 그런 사람 말이에요.\""]], [["Dorothea smiled, and Celia looked rather meditative.", "도러시아가 미소 지었고, 실리아는 다소 생각에 잠긴 표정이 되었다."], ["Presently she said, “I cannot think how it all came about.”", "이윽고 그녀가 말했다. \"이 모든 일이 어떻게 일어났는지 도무지 모르겠어요.\""], ["Celia thought it would be pleasant to hear the story.", "실리아는 그 이야기를 듣는 게 즐거우리라고 생각했다."]], [["“I dare say not,”", "\"그렇겠지.\""], ["said Dorothea, pinching her sister’s chin.", "도러시아가 동생의 턱을 꼬집으며 말했다."], ["“If you knew how it came about, it would not seem wonderful to you.”", "\"그게 어떻게 일어났는지 안다면, 너한테는 그리 놀랍지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“Can’t you tell me?”", "\"내게 말해 줄 수 없어요?\""], ["said Celia, settling her arms cozily.", "실리아가 두 팔을 아늑하게 모으며 말했다."]], [["“No, dear, you would have to feel with me, else you would never know.”", "\"안 돼, 얘야, 너도 나와 함께 느껴 봐야 할 거야. 그러지 않으면 결코 알 수 없을 테니까.\""]], [["# CHAPTER LXXXV.", "# CHAPTER LXXXV."]], [["“Then went the jury out whose names were Mr. Blindman, Mr.", "“그러자 배심원들이 나갔으니, 그들의 이름은 눈먼 자 씨, 쓸모없는 자 씨, 악의 씨, 음욕 씨, 방탕 씨, 무모한 자 씨, 거만한 자 씨, 적의 씨, 거짓말쟁이 씨, 잔혹 씨, 빛을 미워하는 자 씨, 그리고 무자비한 자 씨였다."], ["No-good, Mr. Malice, Mr.", "이들은 저마다 자기들끼리 그에게 불리한 사사로운 평결을 내렸고, 그런 다음 만장일치로 그를 재판관 앞에서 유죄로 끌어들이기로 결론지었다."], ["Love-lust, Mr. Live-loose, Mr.", "그리하여 우선 자기들끼리, 배심장인 눈먼 자 씨가 말했다."], ["Heady, Mr. High-mind, Mr.", "이 사람이 이단임을 나는 분명히 안다."], ["Enmity, Mr. Liar, Mr.", "그러자 쓸모없는 자 씨가 말했다."], ["Cruelty, Mr. Hate-light, Mr.", "이런 자는 이 땅에서 없애 버려라!"], ["Implacable, who every one gave in his private verdict against him among themselves, and afterwards unanimously concluded to bring him in guilty before the judge.", "옳거니, 악의 씨가 말했다."], ["And first among themselves, Mr.", "나는 저자의 꼴조차 보기 싫으니까."], ["Blindman, the foreman, said, I see clearly that this man is a heretic. Then said Mr.", "그러자 음욕 씨가 말했다."], ["No-good, Away with such a fellow from the earth! Ay, said Mr.", "나는 도저히 저자를 견딜 수 없었다."], ["Malice, for I hate the very look of him. Then said Mr.", "나도 마찬가지요, 방탕 씨가 말했다."], ["Love-lust, I could never endure him. Nor I, said Mr.", "저자는 늘 내 방식을 비난하려 들었으니."], ["Live-loose; for he would be always condemning my way. Hang him, hang him, said Mr.", "목매달아라, 목매달아라, 무모한 자 씨가 말했다."], ["Heady. A sorry scrub, said Mr.", "한심한 비렁뱅이로다, 거만한 자 씨가 말했다."], ["High-mind. My heart riseth against him, said Mr.", "내 마음이 저자에게 들고일어나는구나, 적의 씨가 말했다."], ["Enmity. He is a rogue, said Mr.", "저자는 악당이오, 거짓말쟁이 씨가 말했다."], ["Liar. Hanging is too good for him, said Mr.", "목매다는 것도 저자에겐 과분하오, 잔혹 씨가 말했다."], ["Cruelty. Let us despatch him out of the way said Mr.", "저자를 어서 처치해 버립시다, 빛을 미워하는 자 씨가 말했다."], ["Hate-light. Then said Mr.", "그러자 무자비한 자 씨가 말했다."], ["Implacable, Might I have all the world given me, I could not be reconciled to him; therefore let us forthwith bring him in guilty of death.”—Pilgrim’s Progress.", "온 세상을 다 준다 해도 나는 저자와 화해할 수 없으니, 그러니 당장 저자를 사형에 처할 죄인으로 끌어들입시다.” — 『천로역정』."]], [["When immortal Bunyan makes his picture of the persecuting passions bringing in their verdict of guilty, who pities Faithful?", "불멸의 버니언이 박해의 정념들이 유죄 평결을 내리는 장면을 그릴 때, 누가 페이스풀을 가엾게 여기겠는가?"], ["That is a rare and blessed lot which some greatest men have not attained, to know ourselves guiltless before a condemning crowd—to be sure that what we are denounced for is solely the good in us.", "단죄하는 군중 앞에서 자신이 무죄임을 아는 것, 우리가 비난받는 까닭이 오직 우리 안의 선함 때문임을 확신하는 것은 가장 위대한 사람들조차 이르지 못한 드물고도 복된 운명이다."], ["The pitiable lot is that of the man who could not call himself a martyr even though he were to persuade himself that the men who stoned him were but ugly passions incarnate—who knows that he is stoned, not for professing the Right, but for not being the man he professed to be.", "가엾은 운명이란, 자신에게 돌을 던지는 자들이 추악한 정념의 화신에 지나지 않는다고 스스로를 설득한다 해도 자신을 순교자라 부를 수 없는 사람의 운명, 자신이 정의를 표방했기 때문이 아니라 표방하던 그 사람이 되지 못했기 때문에 돌을 맞고 있음을 아는 사람의 운명이다."]], [["This was the consciousness that Bulstrode was withering under while he made his preparations for departing from Middlemarch, and going to end his stricken life in that sad refuge, the indifference of new faces.", "이것이야말로 벌스트로드가 미들마치를 떠날 채비를 하면서, 그리고 상처받은 자신의 삶을 새로운 얼굴들의 무관심이라는 그 서글픈 도피처에서 끝맺으러 가면서, 그 아래 시들어 가던 의식이었다."], ["The duteous merciful constancy of his wife had delivered him from one dread, but it could not hinder her presence from being still a tribunal before which he shrank from confession and desired advocacy.", "아내의 의무에 충실하고 자비로운 한결같음이 그를 한 가지 공포에서 구해 주었으나, 그녀의 존재가 여전히 하나의 법정으로 남아 있는 것까지 막을 수는 없었으니, 그는 그 앞에서 고백을 움츠리면서도 변호를 갈망했다."], ["His equivocations with himself about the death of Raffles had sustained the conception of an Omniscience whom he prayed to, yet he had a terror upon him which would not let him expose them to judgment by a full confession to his wife: the acts which he had washed and diluted with inward argument and motive, and for which it seemed comparatively easy to win invisible pardon—what name would she call them by?", "래플스의 죽음에 관해 자기 자신에게 둘러대 온 변명들은 그가 기도드리는 전지전능한 존재의 관념을 떠받쳐 주었지만, 그럼에도 그에게는 그 변명들을 아내에게 온전히 고백함으로써 심판대에 드러내지 못하게 막는 공포가 있었다. 내면의 논리와 동기로 씻고 희석한 행위들, 보이지 않는 용서를 비교적 쉽게 얻어 낼 수 있을 것 같던 그 행위들을 아내는 무어라 이름 붙일 것인가?"], ["That she should ever silently call his acts Murder was what he could not bear.", "그녀가 언젠가 말없이 그의 행위를 살인이라 부른다는 것, 그것이야말로 그가 견딜 수 없는 일이었다."], ["He felt shrouded by her doubt: he got strength to face her from the sense that she could not yet feel warranted in pronouncing that worst condemnation on him.", "그는 그녀의 의심에 둘러싸인 듯 느꼈다. 그녀가 아직은 그에게 그 최악의 단죄를 선고할 자격이 있다고 느끼지 못하리라는 데서 그는 그녀를 마주할 힘을 얻었다."], ["Some time, perhaps—when he was dying—he would tell her all: in the deep shadow of that time, when she held his hand in the gathering darkness, she might listen without recoiling from his touch.", "언젠가는, 어쩌면 — 죽어 갈 때에는 — 그녀에게 모든 것을 말하리라. 그 시간의 깊은 그늘 속에서, 짙어 가는 어둠 가운데 그녀가 그의 손을 잡고 있을 때면, 그녀는 그의 손길에서 움츠러들지 않고 들어 줄지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps: but concealment had been the habit of his life, and the impulse to confession had no power against the dread of a deeper humiliation.", "어쩌면. 하지만 은폐는 그의 평생의 습관이었고, 고백하려는 충동은 더 깊은 굴욕에 대한 두려움 앞에서 아무런 힘도 없었다."]], [["He was full of timid care for his wife, not only because he deprecated any harshness of judgment from her, but because he felt a deep distress at the sight of her suffering.", "그는 아내를 향한 소심한 염려로 가득 차 있었으니, 그녀에게서 어떤 가혹한 판단이 나올까 두려워서만이 아니라 그녀가 고통받는 모습을 보며 깊은 비탄을 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["She had sent her daughters away to board at a school on the coast, that this crisis might be hidden from them as far as possible.", "그녀는 이 위기를 가능한 한 딸들에게 숨기려고 그들을 해안가의 어느 기숙학교로 보내 버렸다."], ["Set free by their absence from the intolerable necessity of accounting for her grief or of beholding their frightened wonder, she could live unconstrainedly with the sorrow that was every day streaking her hair with whiteness and making her eyelids languid.", "딸들이 없는 덕분에 자신의 슬픔을 해명해야 하는 견딜 수 없는 필요나 그들의 겁먹은 놀라움을 마주해야 하는 부담에서 풀려난 그녀는, 날마다 머리카락에 흰빛을 줄긋고 눈꺼풀을 무겁게 만드는 슬픔과 거리낌 없이 함께 지낼 수 있었다."]], [["“Tell me anything that you would like to have me do, Harriet,”", "“당신이 내게 바라는 일이 있으면 무엇이든 말해 주오, 해리엇.”"], ["Bulstrode had said to her; “I mean with regard to arrangements of property.", "벌스트로드가 그녀에게 말했다. “재산 정리에 관한 일 말이오."], ["It is my intention not to sell the land I possess in this neighborhood, but to leave it to you as a safe provision.", "나는 이 근방에 가진 땅을 팔지 않고 당신에게 안전한 생활 대책으로 남겨 줄 생각이오."], ["If you have any wish on such subjects, do not conceal it from me.”", "그런 문제에 무슨 바람이 있거든 내게 숨기지 마오.”"]], [["A few days afterwards, when she had returned from a visit to her brother’s, she began to speak to her husband on a subject which had for some time been in her mind.", "며칠 뒤, 오라비를 찾아갔다가 돌아온 그녀는 한동안 마음에 품어 온 화제를 두고 남편에게 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“I should like to do something for my brother’s family, Nicholas; and I think we are bound to make some amends to Rosamond and her husband.", "“나는 정말이지 오라버니 가족을 위해 무언가 해 주고 싶어요, 니컬러스. 그리고 우리는 로저먼드와 그 사람 남편에게도 얼마쯤 보상을 해야 할 의무가 있다고 생각해요."], ["Walter says Mr. Lydgate must leave the town, and his practice is almost good for nothing, and they have very little left to settle anywhere with.", "월터 말로는 리드게이트 씨가 이 도시를 떠나야 한다는데, 그분 진료는 거의 아무 쓸모가 없게 되었고, 어디에 정착하기에도 가진 게 거의 남지 않았다고 해요."], ["I would rather do without something for ourselves, to make some amends to my poor brother’s family.”", "가엾은 오라버니 가족에게 얼마쯤 보상을 해 줄 수 있다면, 차라리 우리 자신을 위한 것을 줄이는 편이 낫겠어요.”"]], [["Mrs. Bulstrode did not wish to go nearer to the facts than in the phrase “make some amends;”", "벌스트로드 부인은 “얼마쯤 보상을 해 준다”라는 말 이상으로 사실에 다가가고 싶지 않았으니, 남편이 자기 뜻을 알아들으리라 여겼기 때문이다."], ["knowing that her husband must understand her. He had a particular reason, which she was not aware of, for wincing under her suggestion.", "그에게는 그녀가 알지 못하는 특별한 까닭이 있어 그녀의 제안에 움찔했다."], ["He hesitated before he said—", "그는 머뭇거리다가 입을 열었다."]], [["“It is not possible to carry out your wish in the way you propose, my dear.", "“당신이 제안하는 방식으로는 당신의 바람을 이룰 수가 없소, 여보."], ["Mr. Lydgate has virtually rejected any further service from me.", "리드게이트 씨는 사실상 나에게서 더 이상 어떤 도움도 받기를 거절했소."], ["He has returned the thousand pounds which I lent him.", "내가 빌려 준 천 파운드를 돌려보냈으니까."], ["Mrs. Casaubon advanced him the sum for that purpose.", "캐소본 부인이 그 목적으로 그에게 그 돈을 미리 내어 주었소."], ["Here is his letter.”", "여기 그의 편지가 있소.”"]], [["The letter seemed to cut Mrs. Bulstrode severely.", "그 편지는 벌스트로드 부인에게 모질게 칼을 대는 듯했다."], ["The mention of Mrs. Casaubon’s loan seemed a reflection of that public feeling which held it a matter of course that every one would avoid a connection with her husband.", "캐소본 부인의 대부금에 관한 언급은, 누구든 자기 남편과 엮이는 일을 피하는 게 당연하다고 여기는 그 세간의 감정을 비추는 것만 같았다."], ["She was silent for some time; and the tears fell one after the other, her chin trembling as she wiped them away.", "그녀는 한동안 말이 없었다. 눈물이 하나둘 떨어졌고, 그것을 닦아 내는 동안 그녀의 턱이 떨렸다."], ["Bulstrode, sitting opposite to her, ached at the sight of that grief-worn face, which two months before had been bright and blooming.", "맞은편에 앉아 있던 벌스트로드는 두 달 전만 해도 환하게 피어나 있던 그 슬픔에 지친 얼굴을 보며 마음이 아팠다."], ["It had aged to keep sad company with his own withered features.", "그 얼굴은 시들어 버린 자신의 이목구비와 슬픔의 짝을 이루느라 늙어 버렸다."], ["Urged into some effort at comforting her, he said—", "그녀를 위로하려는 어떤 노력에 떠밀려 그가 말했다."]], [["“There is another means, Harriet, by which I might do a service to your brother’s family, if you like to act in it.", "“또 다른 방법이 있소, 해리엇. 당신이 거기 나서고 싶다면, 그것으로 내가 당신 오라버니 가족에게 도움을 줄 수 있을 거요."], ["And it would, I think, be beneficial to you: it would be an advantageous way of managing the land which I mean to be yours.”", "그리고 그것은, 내 생각에, 당신에게도 이로울 것이오. 당신 것이 되도록 정해 둔 그 땅을 관리하는 데에도 유리한 방법이 될 테니 말이오.”"]], [["She looked attentive.", "그녀는 귀를 기울이는 표정이었다."]], [["“Garth once thought of undertaking the management of Stone Court in order to place your nephew Fred there.", "“가스가 한때 스톤 코트의 관리를 맡아 당신 조카 프레드를 거기 앉힐 생각을 한 적이 있소."], ["The stock was to remain as it is, and they were to pay a certain share of the profits instead of an ordinary rent.", "가축은 지금 그대로 두고, 보통의 지대 대신에 수익의 일정 몫을 내는 방식이었지."], ["That would be a desirable beginning for the young man, in conjunction with his employment under Garth.", "그 젊은이가 가스 밑에서 일하는 것과 더불어 그렇게 시작한다면 바람직할 거요."], ["Would it be a satisfaction to you?”", "당신에게 만족스러운 일이 되겠소?”"]], [["“Yes, it would,”", "“네, 그럴 거예요.”"], ["said Mrs. Bulstrode, with some return of energy.", "벌스트로드 부인이 얼마간 기운을 되찾으며 말했다."], ["“Poor Walter is so cast down; I would try anything in my power to do him some good before I go away.", "“가엾은 월터가 너무 풀이 죽어 있어요. 떠나기 전에 오라버니에게 조금이라도 도움이 될 일이라면 내 힘닿는 데까지 무엇이든 해 보고 싶어요."], ["We have always been brother and sister.”", "우리는 늘 다정한 남매였으니까요.”"]], [["“You must make the proposal to Garth yourself, Harriet,” said Mr.", "“그 제안은 당신이 직접 가스에게 해야 하오, 해리엇.”"], ["Bulstrode, not liking what he had to say, but desiring the end he had in view, for other reasons besides the consolation of his wife.", "벌스트로드 씨가 말했다. 해야 할 말이 마음에 들지는 않았지만, 아내를 위로하는 것 말고도 다른 이유들로 그가 마음에 둔 결말을 바랐던 것이다."], ["“You must state to him that the land is virtually yours, and that he need have no transactions with me.", "“그 땅이 사실상 당신 것이며 그가 나와는 어떤 거래도 할 필요가 없다고 그에게 분명히 말해야 하오."], ["Communications can be made through Standish.", "연락은 스탠디시를 통해 주고받을 수 있소."], ["I mention this, because Garth gave up being my agent.", "이 말을 하는 까닭은 가스가 내 대리인 노릇을 그만두었기 때문이오."], ["I can put into your hands a paper which he himself drew up, stating conditions; and you can propose his renewed acceptance of them.", "그가 직접 작성해 조건을 적어 둔 서류를 당신 손에 쥐여 줄 수 있으니, 당신이 그 조건들을 그가 다시 받아들이도록 제안하면 되오."], ["I think it is not unlikely that he will accept when you propose the thing for the sake of your nephew.”", "조카를 위해서라는 명목으로 당신이 그 일을 제안한다면, 그가 받아들일 가능성이 적지 않다고 생각하오.”"]], [["# CHAPTER LXXXVI.", "# CHAPTER LXXXVI."]], [["“Le cœur se sature d’amour comme d’un sel divin qui le conserve; de là l’incorruptible adhérence de ceux qui se sont aimés dès l’aube de la vie, et la fraîcheur des vielles amours prolongées.", "“마음은 사랑으로 포화되니, 마치 자신을 보존하는 신성한 소금에 절여지듯 하다. 그리하여 생의 새벽부터 서로를 사랑한 이들의 부패하지 않는 결속이, 그리고 오래 이어진 옛사랑의 싱그러움이 있다."], ["Il existe un embaumement d’amour.", "사랑의 방부(防腐)가 존재하는 것이다."], ["C’est de Daphnis et Chloé que sont faits Philémon et Baucis.", "필레몬과 바우키스는 다프니스와 클로에로 빚어졌다."], ["Cette vieillesse-là, ressemblance du soir avec l’aurore.”—VICTOR HUGO: L’homme qui rit.", "그러한 노년이란, 저녁이 새벽을 닮은 모습이다.” — 빅토르 위고, 『웃는 남자』"]], [["Mrs. Garth, hearing Caleb enter the passage about tea-time, opened the parlor-door and said, “There you are, Caleb.", "개스 부인은 차 마실 무렵 케일럽이 복도로 들어서는 소리를 듣고 거실 문을 열며 말했다."], ["Have you had your dinner?” (Mr.", "“이제 오셨군요, 케일럽."], ["Garth’s meals were much subordinated to “business.”)", "식사는 하셨어요?” (개스 씨의 식사는 늘 “일”에 밀려 뒷전이었다.)"]], [["“Oh yes, a good dinner—cold mutton and I don’t know what.", "“오, 했지, 잘 먹었어. 식은 양고기에다 뭐였는지 모를 것들."], ["Where is Mary?”", "메리는 어디 있소?”"]], [["“In the garden with Letty, I think.”", "“레티하고 정원에 있을 거예요.”"]], [["“Fred is not come yet?”", "“프레드는 아직 안 왔소?”"]], [["“No.", "“네."], ["Are you going out again without taking tea, Caleb?” said Mrs.", "차도 안 드시고 또 나가시려고요, 케일럽?”"], ["Garth, seeing that her absent-minded husband was putting on again the hat which he had just taken off.", "개스 부인이 말했다. 정신이 딴 데 팔린 남편이 방금 벗어 놓은 모자를 다시 쓰는 것을 보았던 것이다."]], [["“No, no; I’m only going to Mary a minute.”", "“아니, 아니야. 메리에게 잠깐 다녀오려는 것뿐이오.”"]], [["Mary was in a grassy corner of the garden, where there was a swing loftily hung between two pear-trees.", "메리는 정원의 풀밭 한구석에 있었는데, 그곳에는 배나무 두 그루 사이에 그네가 높다랗게 매달려 있었다."], ["She had a pink kerchief tied over her head, making a little poke to shade her eyes from the level sunbeams, while she was giving a glorious swing to Letty, who laughed and screamed wildly.", "그녀는 분홍 손수건을 머리에 둘러 작은 차양처럼 만들어 비스듬히 비치는 햇살로부터 눈을 가리고 있었고, 그러면서 레티를 한껏 힘차게 밀어 주고 있었다. 레티는 마구 웃으며 소리를 질러 댔다."]], [["Seeing her father, Mary left the swing and went to meet him, pushing back the pink kerchief and smiling afar off at him with the involuntary smile of loving pleasure.", "아버지를 보자 메리는 그네에서 떨어져 그를 맞으러 갔고, 분홍 손수건을 뒤로 젖히며 멀리서부터 사랑스러운 기쁨이 절로 배어나는 미소를 지어 보였다."]], [["“I came to look for you, Mary,”", "“너를 찾으러 왔단다, 메리.”"], ["said Mr. Garth.", "개스 씨가 말했다."], ["“Let us walk about a bit.”", "“잠깐 좀 걷자꾸나.”"]], [["Mary knew quite well that her father had something particular to say: his eyebrows made their pathetic angle, and there was a tender gravity in his voice: these things had been signs to her when she was Letty’s age.", "메리는 아버지가 무언가 특별히 할 말이 있다는 것을 잘 알았다. 그의 눈썹이 그 애처로운 각도를 이루고 있었고, 목소리에는 부드러운 진중함이 어려 있었다."], ["She put her arm within his, and they turned by the row of nut-trees.", "이런 것들은 메리가 레티만 한 나이였을 때부터 그녀에게 신호였다. 그녀는 아버지의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼었고, 두 사람은 호두나무가 늘어선 줄을 따라 돌아갔다."]], [["“It will be a sad while before you can be married, Mary,”", "“네가 결혼하려면 한참을 기다려야 할 게다, 메리."], ["said her father, not looking at her, but at the end of the stick which he held in his other hand.", "마음이 좀 안됐구나.” 아버지가 그녀를 보지 않고 다른 손에 쥔 지팡이 끝을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Not a sad while, father—I mean to be merry,”", "“안됐을 게 뭐 있어요, 아버지. 저는 즐겁게 지낼 작정인걸요.”"], ["said Mary, laughingly.", "메리가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I have been single and merry for four-and-twenty years and more: I suppose it will not be quite as long again as that.”", "“저는 스물네 해도 넘게 혼자서 즐겁게 지냈는걸요. 다음번엔 그렇게까지 오래 걸리지는 않을 거예요.”"], ["Then, after a little pause, she said, more gravely, bending her face before her father’s, “If you are contented with Fred?”", "그러고는 잠깐 말을 멈추었다가 더 진지하게, 얼굴을 아버지 쪽으로 숙이며 말했다. “아버지는 프레드가 마음에 드세요?”"]], [["Caleb screwed up his mouth and turned his head aside wisely.", "케일럽은 입을 오므리고 슬쩍 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“Now, father, you did praise him last Wednesday.", "“아니, 아버지, 지난 수요일에는 그 사람을 칭찬하셨잖아요."], ["You said he had an uncommon notion of stock, and a good eye for things.”", "가축에 대한 감각이 남다르고, 보는 눈이 좋다고 하셨죠.”"]], [["“Did I?”", "“내가 그랬더냐?”"], ["said Caleb, rather slyly.", "케일럽이 다소 능청스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I put it all down, and the date, anno Domini, and everything,”", "“네, 제가 전부 적어 두었는걸요, 날짜에 서기 연도까지, 죄다요.”"], ["said Mary.", "메리가 말했다."], ["“You like things to be neatly booked.", "“아버지는 뭐든 깔끔하게 장부에 적어 두는 걸 좋아하시잖아요. 그리고 아버지를 대하는 그 사람 태도도 정말 좋아요."], ["And then his behavior to you, father, is really good; he has a deep respect for you; and it is impossible to have a better temper than Fred has.”", "아버지를 깊이 존경하고요. 게다가 프레드보다 성품이 더 좋은 사람은 없을 거예요.”"]], [["“Ay, ay; you want to coax me into thinking him a fine match.”", "“그래, 그래. 그 사람이 훌륭한 신랑감이라고 나를 구슬려 믿게 하려는 게로구나.”"]], [["“No, indeed, father.", "“정말 아니에요, 아버지."], ["I don’t love him because he is a fine match.”", "저는 그 사람이 훌륭한 신랑감이라서 사랑하는 게 아니에요.”"]], [["“What for, then?”", "“그럼 무엇 때문이냐?”"]], [["“Oh, dear, because I have always loved him.", "“아유, 그야 제가 늘 그 사람을 사랑해 왔기 때문이죠."], ["I should never like scolding any one else so well; and that is a point to be thought of in a husband.”", "다른 누구를 그렇게 마음껏 야단치는 건 결코 좋아하지 못할 거예요. 그건 남편감에게서 생각해 볼 점이잖아요.”"]], [["“Your mind is quite settled, then, Mary?”", "“그럼 네 마음은 완전히 정해진 게냐, 메리?”"], ["said Caleb, returning to his first tone.", "케일럽이 처음의 어조로 돌아와 말했다."], ["“There’s no other wish come into it since things have been going on as they have been of late?”", "“요즘처럼 일이 흘러온 동안 다른 바람이 마음에 들어선 건 아니고?”"], ["(Caleb meant a great deal in that vague phrase;) “because, better late than never.", "(케일럽은 그 막연한 말 속에 많은 뜻을 담았다.) “늦더라도 안 하는 것보다는 나으니 말이다."], ["A woman must not force her heart—she’ll do a man no good by that.”", "여자는 제 마음을 억지로 다잡아선 안 돼. 그런 식으로는 남자에게 아무 도움도 못 줄 게다.”"]], [["“My feelings have not changed, father,”", "“제 마음은 변하지 않았어요, 아버지.”"], ["said Mary, calmly.", "메리가 차분하게 말했다."], ["“I shall be constant to Fred as long as he is constant to me.", "“프레드가 저에게 한결같은 한 저도 그에게 한결같을 거예요."], ["I don’t think either of us could spare the other, or like any one else better, however much we might admire them.", "우리 둘 중 누구도 상대 없이는 견디지 못할 거고, 아무리 다른 사람을 흠모하게 되더라도 그 사람을 더 좋아할 수는 없을 거예요."], ["It would make too great a difference to us—like seeing all the old places altered, and changing the name for everything.", "그렇게 되면 우리에겐 너무 큰 차이가 생기는걸요. 정든 옛 장소들이 전부 바뀌고, 모든 것의 이름이 달라지는 걸 보는 것처럼요."], ["We must wait for each other a long while; but Fred knows that.”", "우리는 오래 서로를 기다려야 해요. 하지만 프레드도 그걸 알아요.”"]], [["Instead of speaking immediately, Caleb stood still and screwed his stick on the grassy walk.", "케일럽은 곧바로 대답하지 않고 가만히 서서 풀 깔린 길에 지팡이를 비벼 댔다."], ["Then he said, with emotion in his voice, “Well, I’ve got a bit of news.", "그러다 목소리에 감정을 실어 말했다. “음, 소식이 좀 있단다."], ["What do you think of Fred going to live at Stone Court, and managing the land there?”", "프레드가 스톤코트에 가서 살면서 그곳 땅을 관리한다면 어떻겠느냐?”"]], [["“How can that ever be, father?”", "“그게 어떻게 가능해요, 아버지?”"], ["said Mary, wonderingly.", "메리가 의아해하며 말했다."]], [["“He would manage it for his aunt Bulstrode.", "“그 사람 숙모인 벌스트로드 부인을 위해 관리해 주는 거지."], ["The poor woman has been to me begging and praying.", "그 가엾은 부인이 나한테 와서 사정하며 간곡히 부탁하더구나."], ["She wants to do the lad good, and it might be a fine thing for him.", "그 청년에게 도움이 되어 주고 싶다는 거야. 그 사람한테는 좋은 일이 될지도 모르지."], ["With saving, he might gradually buy the stock, and he has a turn for farming.”", "절약하면 차차 가축을 사들일 수 있을 테고, 그 사람은 농사에 소질이 있으니까.”"]], [["“Oh, Fred would be so happy!", "“오, 프레드가 정말 기뻐하겠어요!"], ["It is too good to believe.”", "믿어지지 않을 만큼 좋은 일이에요.”"]], [["“Ah, but mind you,”", "“아, 하지만 명심하거라.” 케일럽이 경고하듯 고개를 돌리며 말했다."], ["said Caleb, turning his head warningly, “I must take it on my shoulders, and be responsible, and see after everything; and that will grieve your mother a bit, though she mayn’t say so.", "“이 일은 내가 직접 짊어지고 책임지면서 모든 걸 살펴야 하는 거다. 그러면 네 어머니가 좀 마음 상해할 게야, 입 밖에 내진 않더라도 말이지."], ["Fred had need be careful.”", "프레드는 신중해야 할 거다.”"]], [["“Perhaps it is too much, father,”", "“아버지, 어쩌면 너무 무리예요.”"], ["said Mary, checked in her joy.", "메리가 기쁨을 누그러뜨리며 말했다."], ["“There would be no happiness in bringing you any fresh trouble.”", "“아버지께 새로운 골칫거리를 안겨 드린다면 거기엔 아무 기쁨도 없을 거예요.”"]], [["“Nay, nay; work is my delight, child, when it doesn’t vex your mother.", "“아니다, 아니야. 일은 내 낙이란다, 얘야, 네 어머니를 속상하게만 하지 않는다면 말이지."], ["And then, if you and Fred get married,”", "그리고 너랑 프레드가 결혼하면,” 여기서 케일럽의 목소리가 알아챌 듯 말 듯 떨렸다."], ["here Caleb’s voice shook just perceptibly, “he’ll be steady and saving; and you’ve got your mother’s cleverness, and mine too, in a woman’s sort of way; and you’ll keep him in order.", "“그 사람은 착실하고 알뜰해질 게다. 게다가 너는 여자다운 방식으로나마 네 어머니의 영민함을, 그리고 내 영민함도 물려받았으니, 그 사람을 잘 다잡아 줄 게야."], ["He’ll be coming by-and-by, so I wanted to tell you first, because I think you’d like to tell him by yourself.", "그 사람이 조금 있으면 올 테니, 너에게 먼저 말해 주고 싶었던 거란다. 네가 직접 그에게 알려 주고 싶을 것 같아서 말이지."], ["After that, I could talk it well over with him, and we could go into business and the nature of things.”", "그다음에 내가 그 사람과 잘 의논해서, 일이며 이런저런 사정들을 짚어 볼 수 있을 게다.”"]], [["“Oh, you dear good father!”", "“오, 다정하고 좋으신 아버지!”"], ["cried Mary, putting her hands round her father’s neck, while he bent his head placidly, willing to be caressed.", "메리가 외치며 두 손으로 아버지의 목을 끌어안았고, 아버지는 어루만져지는 것을 기꺼이 받아들이며 차분하게 고개를 숙였다."], ["“I wonder if any other girl thinks her father the best man in the world!”", "“다른 여자애들도 자기 아버지가 세상에서 제일 훌륭한 사람이라고 생각할까요!”"]], [["“Nonsense, child; you’ll think your husband better.”", "“말도 안 되는 소리, 얘야. 너는 네 남편이 더 낫다고 생각하게 될 거다.”"]], [["“Impossible,”", "“그럴 리가 없어요.”"], ["said Mary, relapsing into her usual tone; “husbands are an inferior class of men, who require keeping in order.”", "메리가 평소의 어조로 돌아오며 말했다. “남편들이란 다잡아 줘야 하는 열등한 부류의 인간들인걸요.”"]], [["When they were entering the house with Letty, who had run to join them, Mary saw Fred at the orchard-gate, and went to meet him.", "레티가 달려와 합류하여 함께 집으로 들어서던 때, 메리는 과수원 문가에 있는 프레드를 보고 그를 맞으러 갔다."]], [["“What fine clothes you wear, you extravagant youth!”", "“어쩜 그리 좋은 옷을 입었어요, 사치스러운 청년 같으니!”"], ["said Mary, as Fred stood still and raised his hat to her with playful formality.", "메리가 말했다. 프레드는 가만히 서서 장난스레 격식을 차리며 그녀에게 모자를 들어 올렸다."], ["“You are not learning economy.”", "“절약은 안 배우는군요.”"]], [["“Now that is too bad, Mary,”", "“이거 너무하잖아, 메리.”"], ["said Fred.", "프레드가 말했다."], ["“Just look at the edges of these coat-cuffs!", "“이 외투 소맷부리 끝을 좀 봐!"], ["It is only by dint of good brushing that I look respectable.", "부지런히 솔질을 해서 겨우 멀쩡해 보이는 거라고."], ["I am saving up three suits—one for a wedding-suit.”", "양복 세 벌을 아껴 두고 있어. 한 벌은 결혼 예복으로 말이야.”"]], [["“How very droll you will look!—like a gentleman in an old fashion-book.”", "“정말 우스워 보이겠네요! 낡은 유행 책에 나오는 신사처럼요.”"]], [["“Oh no, they will keep two years.”", "“오, 아니야, 두 해는 갈 거야.”"]], [["“Two years!", "“두 해라니!"], ["be reasonable, Fred,”", "좀 현실적으로 생각해요, 프레드.”"], ["said Mary, turning to walk.", "메리가 걸음을 돌리며 말했다."], ["“Don’t encourage flattering expectations.”", "“듣기 좋은 기대는 부추기지 마세요.”"]], [["“Why not?", "“왜 안 돼?"], ["One lives on them better than on unflattering ones.", "듣기 좋지 않은 기대보다는 듣기 좋은 기대로 사는 게 낫지."], ["If we can’t be married in two years, the truth will be quite bad enough when it comes.”", "우리가 두 해 안에 결혼할 수 없다면, 그 진실이 닥칠 때 충분히 나쁠 테니까.”"]], [["“I have heard a story of a young gentleman who once encouraged flattering expectations, and they did him harm.”", "“듣기 좋은 기대를 부추겼다가 그 때문에 해를 입은 어느 젊은 신사 이야기를 들은 적이 있어요.”"]], [["“Mary, if you’ve got something discouraging to tell me, I shall bolt; I shall go into the house to Mr.", "“메리, 만약 나를 낙담시킬 이야기를 하려는 거라면 나는 달아날 거야. 집으로 개스 씨께 가 버릴 테야."], ["Garth. I am out of spirits.", "나는 기운이 없어."], ["My father is so cut up—home is not like itself.", "아버지께서 너무 풀이 죽으셔서 집안이 예전 같지가 않아."], ["I can’t bear any more bad news.”", "더는 나쁜 소식을 견딜 수가 없어.”"]], [["“Should you call it bad news to be told that you were to live at Stone Court, and manage the farm, and be remarkably prudent, and save money every year till all the stock and furniture were your own, and you were a distinguished agricultural character, as Mr.", "“당신이 스톤코트에 살면서 농장을 관리하고, 놀라울 만큼 신중하게 굴며 해마다 돈을 모아 마침내 가축과 가구가 전부 당신 것이 되고, 보스럽 트럼불 씨 말마따나 명망 있는 농업가가 된다는 걸 나쁜 소식이라 부르겠어요?"], ["Borthrop Trumbull says—rather stout, I fear, and with the Greek and Latin sadly weather-worn?”", "안됐지만 좀 통통해지고, 그리스어와 라틴어는 슬프게도 비바람에 닳아 버린 채로 말이에요.”"]], [["“You don’t mean anything except nonsense, Mary?”", "“그냥 농담하는 거지, 메리?”"], ["said Fred, coloring slightly nevertheless.", "그렇게 말하면서도 프레드는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“That is what my father has just told me of as what may happen, and he never talks nonsense,”", "“그게 바로 방금 아버지께서 일어날 수도 있다고 말씀하신 일이에요, 아버지는 결코 허튼소리를 하시지 않잖아요.”"], ["said Mary, looking up at Fred now, while he grasped her hand as they walked, till it rather hurt her; but she would not complain.", "메리가 이제 프레드를 올려다보며 말했고, 프레드는 함께 걸으며 그녀의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐어 아플 지경이었지만, 그녀는 불평하지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, I could be a tremendously good fellow then, Mary, and we could be married directly.”", "“오, 그럼 나는 엄청나게 착한 사람이 될 거야, 메리, 그리고 우리는 당장 결혼할 수 있어.”"]], [["“Not so fast, sir; how do you know that I would not rather defer our marriage for some years?", "“그렇게 서두르지 마세요, 신사 양반. 내가 우리 결혼을 몇 해쯤 미루는 편을 더 좋아할지도 모른다는 걸 어떻게 알아요?"], ["That would leave you time to misbehave, and then if I liked some one else better, I should have an excuse for jilting you.”", "그러면 당신이 못되게 굴 시간이 생길 테고, 그러다 내가 다른 누군가를 더 좋아하게 되면, 당신을 차 버릴 핑계가 생기는 거죠.”"]], [["“Pray don’t joke, Mary,”", "“제발 농담하지 마, 메리.”"], ["said Fred, with strong feeling.", "프레드가 격한 감정에 차서 말했다."], ["“Tell me seriously that all this is true, and that you are happy because of it—because you love me best.”", "“이게 다 사실이라고, 그래서 네가 행복하다고 진지하게 말해 줘. 나를 제일 사랑하니까 말이야.”"]], [["“It is all true, Fred, and I am happy because of it—because I love you best,”", "“이게 다 사실이에요, 프레드, 그래서 저는 행복해요. 당신을 제일 사랑하니까요.”"], ["said Mary, in a tone of obedient recitation.", "메리가 시키는 대로 외워 읊는 듯한 어조로 말했다."]], [["They lingered on the door-step under the steep-roofed porch, and Fred almost in a whisper said—", "두 사람은 가파른 지붕의 현관 아래 문간 계단에서 머뭇거렸고, 프레드는 거의 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["“When we were first engaged, with the umbrella-ring, Mary, you used to—”", "“우리가 처음 약혼했을 때, 그 우산 고리로 말이야, 메리, 너는…….”"]], [["The spirit of joy began to laugh more decidedly in Mary’s eyes, but the fatal Ben came running to the door with Brownie yapping behind him, and, bouncing against them, said—", "기쁨의 정령이 메리의 눈 속에서 한층 또렷이 웃기 시작했지만, 그 야속한 벤이 문간으로 달려왔고 그 뒤로 브라우니가 컹컹 짖어 댔다. 벤은 두 사람에게 폴짝 부딪치며 말했다."]], [["“Fred and Mary!", "“프레드 형, 메리 누나!"], ["are you ever coming in?—or may I eat your cake?”", "도대체 안 들어올 거예요? 아니면 누나 케이크 내가 먹어도 돼요?”"]], [["Every limit is a beginning as well as an ending.", "피날레. 모든 끝은 끝인 동시에 시작이기도 하다."], ["Who can quit young lives after being long in company with them, and not desire to know what befell them in their after-years?", "젊은 생애들과 오랫동안 함께한 끝에, 그들이 훗날의 세월에 어떤 일을 겪었는지 알고 싶지 않을 사람이 누가 있겠는가?"], ["For the fragment of a life, however typical, is not the sample of an even web: promises may not be kept, and an ardent outset may be followed by declension; latent powers may find their long-waited opportunity; a past error may urge a grand retrieval.", "한 생의 단편이란, 아무리 전형적이라 해도, 고른 직물에서 떼어 낸 견본이 아니기 때문이다. 약속은 지켜지지 않을 수도 있고, 열렬한 출발 뒤에 쇠퇴가 따라올 수도 있으며, 잠재된 힘이 오래 기다리던 기회를 찾을 수도 있고, 지난날의 잘못이 위대한 만회를 재촉할 수도 있다."]], [["Marriage, which has been the bourne of so many narratives, is still a great beginning, as it was to Adam and Eve, who kept their honeymoon in Eden, but had their first little one among the thorns and thistles of the wilderness.", "수많은 이야기의 종착지였던 결혼은, 여전히 하나의 위대한 시작이다. 에덴에서 신혼을 보냈으나 광야의 가시덤불과 엉겅퀴 사이에서 첫아이를 얻은 아담과 이브에게 그러했듯이 말이다."], ["It is still the beginning of the home epic—the gradual conquest or irremediable loss of that complete union which makes the advancing years a climax, and age the harvest of sweet memories in common.", "결혼은 여전히 가정 서사시의 시작이다. 흘러가는 세월을 정점으로, 노년을 함께 나눈 달콤한 기억의 수확으로 만들어 주는 그 완전한 결합을 점차 쟁취하느냐, 아니면 돌이킬 수 없이 잃어버리느냐 하는 서사시의 시작인 것이다."]], [["Some set out, like Crusaders of old, with a glorious equipment of hope and enthusiasm and get broken by the way, wanting patience with each other and the world.", "어떤 이들은 옛 십자군처럼 희망과 열정의 찬란한 장비를 갖추고 출발하지만, 서로에게 그리고 세상에 인내심이 부족해 도중에 꺾여 버리고 만다."]], [["All who have cared for Fred Vincy and Mary Garth will like to know that these two made no such failure, but achieved a solid mutual happiness.", "프레드 빈시와 메리 개스에게 마음을 써 온 모든 이들은, 이 두 사람이 그런 실패를 겪지 않고 굳건한 상호 행복을 이루었음을 알면 기뻐할 것이다."], ["Fred surprised his neighbors in various ways.", "프레드는 여러 면에서 이웃들을 놀라게 했다."], ["He became rather distinguished in his side of the county as a theoretic and practical farmer, and produced a work on the “Cultivation of Green Crops and the Economy of Cattle-Feeding”", "그는 자기가 사는 주(州) 일대에서 이론과 실무를 겸비한 농부로 제법 이름을 날렸고, 『풋작물 재배와 가축 사육의 경제』라는 저작을 펴내어 농업 모임에서 큰 축하를 받았다."], ["which won him high congratulations at agricultural meetings.", "미들마치에서는 칭찬이 좀 더 인색했다."], ["In Middlemarch admiration was more reserved: most persons there were inclined to believe that the merit of Fred’s authorship was due to his wife, since they had never expected Fred Vincy to write on turnips and mangel-wurzel.", "그곳 사람들 대부분은 프레드 빈시가 순무나 사료용 사탕무에 관해 글을 쓰리라고는 전혀 기대하지 않았던 터라, 프레드가 거둔 저술의 공로가 그의 아내 덕분일 거라고 믿는 쪽으로 기울었다."]], [["But when Mary wrote a little book for her boys, called “Stories of Great Men, taken from Plutarch,” and had it printed and published by Gripp & Co., Middlemarch, every one in the town was willing to give the credit of this work to Fred, observing that he had been to the University, “where the ancients were studied,”", "그러나 메리가 자기 아들들을 위해 『플루타르코스에서 가려 뽑은 위인 이야기』라는 작은 책을 써서 미들마치의 그립 출판사에서 인쇄해 펴냈을 때는, 마을의 모든 사람이 이 책의 공로를 프레드에게 돌리려 했다."], ["and might have been a clergyman if he had chosen.", "프레드가 “고대 문헌을 공부하는” 대학에 다녔으며 마음만 먹었다면 성직자가 될 수도 있었으리라는 것이었다."]], [["In this way it was made clear that Middlemarch had never been deceived, and that there was no need to praise anybody for writing a book, since it was always done by somebody else.", "이런 식으로, 미들마치는 결코 속은 적이 없으며, 책을 썼다고 누군가를 칭찬할 필요는 없다는 것이 분명해졌다. 책이란 늘 다른 누군가가 쓴 것이었으니 말이다."]], [["Moreover, Fred remained unswervingly steady. Some years after his marriage he told Mary that his happiness was half owing to Farebrother, who gave him a strong pull-up at the right moment. I cannot say that he was never again misled by his hopefulness: the yield of crops or the profits of a cattle sale usually fell below his estimate; and he was always prone to believe that he could make money by the purchase of a horse which turned out badly—though this, Mary observed, was of course the fault of the horse, not of Fred’s judgment. He kept his love of horsemanship, but he rarely allowed himself a day’s hunting; and when he did so, it was remarkable that he submitted to be laughed at for cowardliness at the fences, seeming to see Mary and the boys sitting on the five-barred gate, or showing their curly heads between hedge and ditch.", "게다가 프레드는 흔들림 없이 착실했다. 결혼한 지 몇 해 뒤, 그는 메리에게 자신의 행복이 절반은 페어브러더 덕분이라고 말했다. 적절한 순간에 그를 단단히 잡아 일으켜 준 사람이 바로 그였던 것이다. 프레드가 다시는 자신의 낙천성에 현혹되지 않았다고 말할 수는 없다. 작물 수확량이나 가축 매매의 수익은 대개 그의 예상에 못 미쳤고, 그는 늘 결과가 나쁘게 드러난 말을 사들여 돈을 벌 수 있으리라 믿는 버릇이 있었다. 물론, 메리의 말에 따르면, 그건 프레드의 판단 탓이 아니라 말 탓이었지만 말이다. 그는 승마에 대한 애착을 간직했으나, 하루를 사냥에 내어 주는 일은 좀처럼 없었다. 그리고 그렇게 사냥에 나설 때면, 울타리 앞에서 겁쟁이라 놀림받는 것을 순순히 받아들이는 점이 눈에 띄었다. 마치 메리와 아이들이 다섯 가로대 문에 걸터앉아 있거나, 산울타리와 도랑 사이로 곱슬머리를 내밀고 있는 모습이 보이기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["There were three boys: Mary was not discontented that she brought forth men-children only; and when Fred wished to have a girl like her, she said, laughingly, “that would be too great a trial to your mother.”", "아들이 셋이었다."], ["Mrs.", "메리는 사내아이만 낳은 것을 불만스러워하지 않았다."], ["Vincy in her declining years, and in the diminished lustre of her housekeeping, was much comforted by her perception that two at least of Fred’s boys were real Vincys, and did not “feature the Garths.”", "프레드가 메리를 닮은 딸을 갖고 싶다고 하자, 그녀는 웃으며 “그러면 당신 어머니께 너무 큰 시련이 될 거예요”라고 말했다. 빈시 부인은 노년에 접어들어 살림의 광채가 빛바랜 가운데서도, 프레드의 아들들 가운데 적어도 둘은 진짜 빈시 집안 아이들이고 “개스 집안을 닮지”"], ["But Mary secretly rejoiced that the youngest of the three was very much what her father must have been when he wore a round jacket, and showed a marvellous nicety of aim in playing at marbles, or in throwing stones to bring down the mellow pears.", "않았다는 것을 알아차리고 크게 위안을 받았다. 그러나 메리는 셋 중 막내가 짧은 윗도리를 입던 시절의 자기 아버지 모습과 무척이나 닮았다는 것에, 또 구슬치기를 하거나 잘 익은 배를 떨어뜨리려 돌을 던질 때 놀라울 만큼 정확한 솜씨를 보인다는 것에 남몰래 기뻐했다."]], [["Ben and Letty Garth, who were uncle and aunt before they were well in their teens, disputed much as to whether nephews or nieces were more desirable; Ben contending that it was clear girls were good for less than boys, else they would not be always in petticoats, which showed how little they were meant for; whereupon Letty, who argued much from books, got angry in replying that God made coats of skins for both Adam and Eve alike—also it occurred to her that in the East the men too wore petticoats.", "벤과 레티 개스는 제대로 십대에 접어들기도 전에 삼촌과 이모가 되었는데, 조카로는 사내아이가 나은지 계집아이가 나은지를 두고 한참 다투었다. 벤은 계집아이가 사내아이보다 쓸모가 덜한 게 분명하다고 우겼으니, 그렇지 않다면 늘 치마만 입고 있을 리가 없으며, 그건 계집아이가 별 쓸모가 없도록 만들어졌다는 증거라는 것이었다."], ["But this latter argument, obscuring the majesty of the former, was one too many, for Ben answered contemptuously, “The more spooneys they!”", "그러자 책에서 근거를 곧잘 끌어오는 레티가 발끈하며, 하느님은 아담과 이브 모두에게 똑같이 가죽옷을 지어 주셨다고 받아쳤다."], ["and immediately appealed to his mother whether boys were not better than girls. Mrs.", "게다가 동방에서는 남자들도 치마를 입었다는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Garth pronounced that both were alike naughty, but that boys were undoubtedly stronger, could run faster, and throw with more precision to a greater distance.", "그러나 이 뒤엣것은 앞엣것의 위엄을 흐려 놓는, 군더더기 같은 논거였으니, 벤은 경멸하듯 “그래서 걔들이 그렇게 맹꽁이지!”라고 대꾸하고는 곧장 어머니에게 사내아이가 계집아이보다 낫지 않으냐고 호소했다. 개스 부인은 둘 다 똑같이 말썽꾸러기지만, 사내아이가 더 힘이 센 것은 의심할 여지가 없고, 더 빨리 달릴 수 있으며, 더 멀리까지 더 정확하게 던질 수 있다고 선언했다."], ["With this oracular sentence Ben was well satisfied, not minding the naughtiness; but Letty took it ill, her feeling of superiority being stronger than her muscles.", "이 신탁 같은 판결에 벤은 말썽꾸러기라는 말은 개의치 않고 흡족해했다. 그러나 레티는 그 말을 언짢게 받아들였으니, 그녀의 우월감은 근육보다 강했던 것이다."]], [["Fred never became rich—his hopefulness had not led him to expect that; but he gradually saved enough to become owner of the stock and furniture at Stone Court, and the work which Mr.", "프레드는 결코 부자가 되지 못했다."], ["Garth put into his hands carried him in plenty through those “bad times”", "그의 낙천성도 부자가 되리라는 기대까지 품게 하지는 않았다."], ["which are always present with farmers. Mary, in her matronly days, became as solid in figure as her mother; but, unlike her, gave the boys little formal teaching, so that Mrs.", "그러나 그는 차차 돈을 모아 스톤코트의 가축과 가구를 소유하게 되었고, 개스 씨가 그에게 맡긴 일 덕분에 농부들에게 늘 따라붙는 그 “어려운 시절”을 넉넉히 헤쳐 나갈 수 있었다."], ["Garth was alarmed lest they should never be well grounded in grammar and geography.", "메리는 한 집안의 안주인이 된 시절에 이르러 어머니만큼이나 다부진 몸매가 되었다."], ["Nevertheless, they were found quite forward enough when they went to school; perhaps, because they had liked nothing so well as being with their mother.", "그러나 어머니와 달리 아이들에게 정식 교육을 거의 시키지 않아서, 개스 부인은 아이들이 문법과 지리의 기초를 제대로 다지지 못할까 봐 걱정했다. 그럼에도 아이들은 학교에 갔을 때 충분히 앞서 있는 것으로 드러났는데, 어쩌면 그들이 어머니와 함께 있는 것만큼 좋아하는 일이 없었기 때문이었으리라."], ["When Fred was riding home on winter evenings he had a pleasant vision beforehand of the bright hearth in the wainscoted parlor, and was sorry for other men who could not have Mary for their wife; especially for Mr.", "프레드는 겨울 저녁에 말을 타고 집으로 돌아오면서, 미리부터 판벽을 두른 거실의 환한 난롯가를 즐겁게 떠올렸고, 메리를 아내로 맞지 못한 다른 남자들을 안쓰럽게 여겼다. 특히 페어브러더 씨를 말이다."], ["Farebrother. “He was ten times worthier of you than I was,”", "“그분은 나보다 열 배는 더 너에게 어울리는 분이었어.”"], ["Fred could now say to her, magnanimously.", "이제 프레드는 너그럽게 그녀에게 그렇게 말할 수 있었다."], ["“To be sure he was,”", "“물론 그랬죠.”"], ["Mary answered; “and for that reason he could do better without me.", "메리가 대답했다. “바로 그 때문에 그분은 나 없이도 더 잘 지낼 수 있었던 거예요."], ["But you—I shudder to think what you would have been—a curate in debt for horse-hire and cambric pocket-handkerchiefs!”", "하지만 당신은요, 당신이 어떻게 됐을지 생각하면 몸서리가 쳐져요. 말 빌린 값과 고운 삼베 손수건 값에 빚을 진 부목사가 됐겠죠!”"]], [["On inquiry it might possibly be found that Fred and Mary still inhabit Stone Court—that the creeping plants still cast the foam of their blossoms over the fine stone-wall into the field where the walnut-trees stand in stately row—and that on sunny days the two lovers who were first engaged with the umbrella-ring may be seen in white-haired placidity at the open window from which Mary Garth, in the days of old Peter Featherstone, had often been ordered to look out for Mr.", "혹 알아본다면, 프레드와 메리가 아직도 스톤코트에 살고 있음을 발견하게 될지도 모른다."], ["Lydgate.", "덩굴식물이 여전히 그 거품 같은 꽃송이를 멋진 돌담 너머로, 호두나무들이 위풍당당하게 줄지어 선 들판으로 흘려보내고 있음을, 그리고 햇살 좋은 날이면 우산 고리로 처음 약혼했던 그 두 연인이, 옛 피터 페더스톤 시절에 메리 개스가 리드게이트 씨가 오는지 내다보라는 분부를 자주 받았던 그 열린 창가에서 백발의 평온함에 잠겨 앉아 있는 모습을 볼 수 있을지도 모른다."]], [["Lydgate’s hair never became white.", "리드게이트의 머리카락은 끝내 희어지지 않았다."], ["He died when he was only fifty, leaving his wife and children provided for by a heavy insurance on his life.", "그는 겨우 쉰 살에 죽으면서, 생명보험에 거액을 들어 둔 덕에 아내와 자식들의 생계를 마련해 주었다."], ["He had gained an excellent practice, alternating, according to the season, between London and a Continental bathing-place; having written a treatise on Gout, a disease which has a good deal of wealth on its side.", "그는 훌륭한 개업의로 자리를 잡아 계절에 따라 런던과 대륙의 어느 온천 휴양지를 오가며 지냈고, 통풍에 관한 논문을 썼는데, 통풍이란 부유한 이들이 많이 걸리는 병이었다."], ["His skill was relied on by many paying patients, but he always regarded himself as a failure: he had not done what he once meant to do.", "돈을 내는 많은 환자가 그의 의술에 기댔으나, 그는 늘 자신을 실패자로 여겼다. 한때 하려고 마음먹었던 일을 이루지 못했던 것이다."], ["His acquaintances thought him enviable to have so charming a wife, and nothing happened to shake their opinion.", "지인들은 그가 그토록 매력적인 아내를 둔 것을 부러워했고, 그 생각을 흔들 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Rosamond never committed a second compromising indiscretion.", "로저먼드는 두 번 다시 명예를 더럽힐 경솔한 행동을 저지르지 않았다."], ["She simply continued to be mild in her temper, inflexible in her judgment, disposed to admonish her husband, and able to frustrate him by stratagem.", "그녀는 그저 변함없이 성품이 온화하고, 판단에 있어서는 완강하며, 남편을 타이르기를 좋아하고, 술책으로 그를 좌절시킬 줄 아는 채로 지냈다."], ["As the years went on he opposed her less and less, whence Rosamond concluded that he had learned the value of her opinion; on the other hand, she had a more thorough conviction of his talents now that he gained a good income, and instead of the threatened cage in Bride Street provided one all flowers and gilding, fit for the bird of paradise that she resembled.", "세월이 흐르면서 그는 점점 더 그녀에게 맞서지 않게 되었는데, 그로부터 로저먼드는 그가 자기 의견의 가치를 깨달았다고 결론지었다. 한편 그녀는 그가 좋은 수입을 올리게 되자 그의 재능을 한층 철저히 확신하게 되었고, 그 위협받던 브라이드 거리의 새장 대신 온통 꽃과 금박으로 꾸민, 자신이 닮은 극락조에게 어울릴 새장을 마련했다."], ["In brief, Lydgate was what is called a successful man.", "요컨대 리드게이트는 이른바 성공한 남자였다."], ["But he died prematurely of diphtheria, and Rosamond afterwards married an elderly and wealthy physician, who took kindly to her four children.", "그러나 그는 디프테리아로 일찍 세상을 떴고, 로저먼드는 그 후 나이 지긋하고 부유한 의사와 재혼했는데, 그는 그녀의 네 아이를 다정하게 대해 주었다. 그녀는 딸들을 데리고 마차를 몰고 다니며 무척 어여쁜 자태를 뽐냈고, 자신의 행복을 “보답”이라고 자주 일컬었다."], ["She made a very pretty show with her daughters, driving out in her carriage, and often spoke of her happiness as “a reward”—she did not say for what, but probably she meant that it was a reward for her patience with Tertius, whose temper never became faultless, and to the last occasionally let slip a bitter speech which was more memorable than the signs he made of his repentance.", "무엇에 대한 보답인지는 말하지 않았으나, 아마도 테르티우스에 대한 인내의 보답이라는 뜻이었으리라. 그의 성품은 끝내 흠 없이 다듬어지지 않아서, 죽는 순간까지도 이따금 쓰디쓴 말을 불쑥 내뱉었는데, 그것은 그가 내보인 후회의 기색보다 더 오래 기억에 남았다."], ["He once called her his basil plant; and when she asked for an explanation, said that basil was a plant which had flourished wonderfully on a murdered man’s brains.", "한번은 그가 그녀를 자신의 바질 화초라고 불렀고, 그녀가 무슨 뜻이냐고 묻자, 바질이란 살해된 사람의 뇌수에서 놀라울 만큼 무성하게 자란 식물이라고 대답했다."], ["Rosamond had a placid but strong answer to such speeches.", "로저먼드에게는 그런 말에 대해 평온하지만 강한 대꾸가 준비되어 있었다."], ["Why then had he chosen her?", "그렇다면 어째서 그는 자기를 택했단 말인가?"], ["It was a pity he had not had Mrs. Ladislaw, whom he was always praising and placing above her.", "그가 늘 칭찬하며 자기보다 위에 두던 래디슬로 부인을 얻지 못한 것이 안타까운 일이라고."], ["And thus the conversation ended with the advantage on Rosamond’s side.", "그리하여 대화는 로저먼드에게 유리한 쪽으로 끝맺어졌다."], ["But it would be unjust not to tell, that she never uttered a word in depreciation of Dorothea, keeping in religious remembrance the generosity which had come to her aid in the sharpest crisis of her life.", "그러나 그녀가 도러시아를 깎아내리는 말은 한마디도 입에 올린 적이 없으며, 생애 가장 모진 위기에 자기를 도우러 와 준 그 너그러움을 경건한 마음으로 기억했다는 것을 전하지 않는다면 공정하지 못한 일이리라."]], [["Dorothea herself had no dreams of being praised above other women, feeling that there was always something better which she might have done, if she had only been better and known better.", "도러시아 자신은 다른 여인들보다 칭송받기를 꿈꾸지 않았으니, 자신이 좀 더 나은 사람이었고 좀 더 잘 알았더라면 언제든 더 잘할 수 있는 무언가가 있었으리라고 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["Still, she never repented that she had given up position and fortune to marry Will Ladislaw, and he would have held it the greatest shame as well as sorrow to him if she had repented.", "그래도 그녀는 윌 래디슬로와 결혼하기 위해 지위와 재산을 포기한 것을 결코 후회하지 않았고, 만약 그녀가 후회했다면 윌은 그것을 자신에게 더없는 수치이자 슬픔으로 여겼을 것이다."], ["They were bound to each other by a love stronger than any impulses which could have marred it.", "두 사람은 그것을 해칠 수 있는 어떤 충동보다도 강한 사랑으로 서로 묶여 있었다."], ["No life would have been possible to Dorothea which was not filled with emotion, and she had now a life filled also with a beneficent activity which she had not the doubtful pains of discovering and marking out for herself.", "도러시아에게는 감정으로 채워지지 않은 삶이란 불가능했을 텐데, 이제 그녀의 삶은 자신을 위해 애써 찾아내고 표시해 두어야 하는 미심쩍은 수고 없이도 자비로운 활동으로도 채워졌다."], ["Will became an ardent public man, working well in those times when reforms were begun with a young hopefulness of immediate good which has been much checked in our days, and getting at last returned to Parliament by a constituency who paid his expenses.", "윌은 열렬한 공인(公人)이 되어, 즉각적인 선(善)에 대한 젊은 낙관과 더불어 개혁이 시작되던—우리 시대에는 그런 낙관이 크게 억눌렸지만—그런 시절에 잘 활약했고, 마침내 그의 경비를 대 준 한 선거구에 의해 의회로 진출하게 되었다."], ["Dorothea could have liked nothing better, since wrongs existed, than that her husband should be in the thick of a struggle against them, and that she should give him wifely help.", "도러시아로서는, 부조리가 존재하는 한, 남편이 그것과 맞서 싸우는 한복판에 있고 자신은 그에게 아내로서 도움을 주는 것보다 더 좋아할 일이 없었다."], ["Many who knew her, thought it a pity that so substantive and rare a creature should have been absorbed into the life of another, and be only known in a certain circle as a wife and mother.", "그녀를 아는 많은 이들은, 그토록 실질적이고 보기 드문 존재가 다른 사람의 삶 속으로 흡수되어 어떤 한정된 무리 안에서 한낱 아내이자 어머니로만 알려진 것을 안타깝게 여겼다."], ["But no one stated exactly what else that was in her power she ought rather to have done—not even Sir James Chettam, who went no further than the negative prescription that she ought not to have married Will Ladislaw.", "그러나 그녀가 가진 능력으로 그 대신 정확히 무엇을 했어야 했는지를 말하는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 윌 래디슬로와 결혼하지 말았어야 했다는 부정적인 처방에서 더 나아가지 못한 제임스 체텀 경조차도 그러했다."]], [["But this opinion of his did not cause a lasting alienation; and the way in which the family was made whole again was characteristic of all concerned.", "그러나 그의 이런 견해가 영구적인 소원함을 낳지는 않았다. 그리고 그 가족이 다시 온전해진 방식은 관련된 모든 이의 성격을 그대로 드러내는 것이었다."], ["Mr. Brooke could not resist the pleasure of corresponding with Will and Dorothea; and one morning when his pen had been remarkably fluent on the prospects of Municipal Reform, it ran off into an invitation to the Grange, which, once written, could not be done away with at less cost than the sacrifice (hardly to be conceived) of the whole valuable letter.", "브룩 씨는 윌과 도러시아와 편지를 주고받는 즐거움을 떨치지 못했다."], ["During the months of this correspondence Mr.", "그러던 어느 날 아침, 그의 펜이 시정(市政) 개혁의 전망에 관해 유난히 막힘없이 미끄러지다가, 그랜지로 초대하는 글로 흘러가 버렸고, 일단 써 놓고 보니 그 귀중한 편지 전체를 (도무지 생각조차 할 수 없는) 희생하는 것보다 적은 대가로는 그 초대를 없던 일로 할 수가 없었다."], ["Brooke had continually, in his talk with Sir James Chettam, been presupposing or hinting that the intention of cutting off the entail was still maintained; and the day on which his pen gave the daring invitation, he went to Freshitt expressly to intimate that he had a stronger sense than ever of the reasons for taking that energetic step as a precaution against any mixture of low blood in the heir of the Brookes.", "이 서신 왕래가 오가던 몇 달 동안, 브룩 씨는 제임스 체텀 경과 이야기를 나누면서 한정상속을 끊으려는 의도가 여전히 유지되고 있다고 줄곧 전제하거나 암시해 왔다. 그리고 그의 펜이 그 대담한 초대장을 써 버린 바로 그날, 그는 브룩 집안 상속자에게 천한 피가 섞이는 일을 막기 위한 예방책으로 그 단호한 조치를 취해야 할 이유를 어느 때보다 강하게 느낀다는 뜻을 알리려고, 일부러 프레시트로 향했다."]], [["But that morning something exciting had happened at the Hall.", "그러나 그날 아침 홀에서는 무언가 흥분되는 일이 벌어진 참이었다."], ["A letter had come to Celia which made her cry silently as she read it; and when Sir James, unused to see her in tears, asked anxiously what was the matter, she burst out in a wail such as he had never heard from her before.", "실리아에게 편지 한 통이 와 있었는데, 그녀는 그것을 읽으며 소리 없이 눈물을 흘렸다. 그녀가 우는 모습에 익숙하지 않던 제임스 경이 무슨 일이냐고 걱정스레 묻자, 그녀는 그가 여태 들어 본 적 없는 통곡을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Dorothea has a little boy.", "“도러시아가 사내아이를 낳았어요."], ["And you will not let me go and see her.", "그런데 당신은 내가 가서 그 애를 보지도 못하게 하잖아요."], ["And I am sure she wants to see me.", "분명 그 애도 나를 보고 싶어 할 거예요."], ["And she will not know what to do with the baby—she will do wrong things with it.", "게다가 그 애는 아기를 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 모를 거예요. 잘못된 방식으로 다룰 거라고요."], ["And they thought she would die.", "다들 그 애가 죽을 줄 알았대요."], ["It is very dreadful!", "정말 끔찍해요!"], ["Suppose it had been me and little Arthur, and Dodo had been hindered from coming to see me!", "만약 그게 나하고 어린 아서였는데, 도도가 나를 보러 오는 걸 막았다고 생각해 봐요!"], ["I wish you would be less unkind, James!”", "당신이 좀 덜 매정했으면 좋겠어요, 제임스!”"]], [["“Good heavens, Celia!”", "“맙소사, 실리아!”"], ["said Sir James, much wrought upon, “what do you wish?", "몹시 마음이 동요된 제임스 경이 말했다. “당신은 무얼 바라는 거요?"], ["I will do anything you like.", "당신이 원하는 건 뭐든 하리다."], ["I will take you to town to-morrow if you wish it.”", "당신이 원한다면 내일 당장 시내로 데려가겠소.”"], ["And Celia did wish it.", "그리고 실리아는 정말로 그것을 원했다."]], [["It was after this that Mr. Brooke came, and meeting the Baronet in the grounds, began to chat with him in ignorance of the news, which Sir James for some reason did not care to tell him immediately.", "브룩 씨가 온 것은 그다음의 일이었는데, 정원에서 준남작을 만난 그는 그 소식을 모른 채 그와 잡담을 나누기 시작했다."], ["But when the entail was touched on in the usual way, he said, “My dear sir, it is not for me to dictate to you, but for my part I would let that alone.", "무슨 까닭에서인지 제임스 경은 그 소식을 즉시 알리고 싶어 하지 않았다. 그러나 으레 그러듯 한정상속 이야기가 나오자, 그는 말했다."], ["I would let things remain as they are.”", "“이보세요, 제가 어른께 이래라저래라 할 입장은 아닙니다만, 제 생각으로는 그 일은 그냥 내버려 두겠습니다. 일이 지금 이대로 있게 두겠어요.”"]], [["Mr. Brooke felt so much surprised that he did not at once find out how much he was relieved by the sense that he was not expected to do anything in particular.", "브룩 씨는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 자신이 특별히 무언가를 해야 하리라는 기대를 받지 않게 되었다는 사실에 얼마나 안도하고 있는지를 단번에는 깨닫지 못했다."]], [["Such being the bent of Celia’s heart, it was inevitable that Sir James should consent to a reconciliation with Dorothea and her husband.", "실리아의 마음이 그러한 쪽으로 기울어 있었으니, 제임스 경이 도러시아와 그 남편과의 화해에 동의하는 것은 필연적인 일이었다."], ["Where women love each other, men learn to smother their mutual dislike.", "여인들끼리 서로 사랑하면, 남자들은 서로에 대한 반감을 억누르는 법을 배우게 된다."], ["Sir James never liked Ladislaw, and Will always preferred to have Sir James’s company mixed with another kind: they were on a footing of reciprocal tolerance which was made quite easy only when Dorothea and Celia were present.", "제임스 경은 결코 래디슬로를 좋아하지 않았고, 윌은 늘 제임스 경과 함께 있을 때면 다른 부류의 사람도 섞여 있기를 원했다. 두 사람은 서로를 너그러이 참아 주는 사이였는데, 도러시아와 실리아가 함께 있을 때에야 비로소 그 관계가 아주 수월해졌다."]], [["It became an understood thing that Mr. and Mrs. Ladislaw should pay at least two visits during the year to the Grange, and there came gradually a small row of cousins at Freshitt who enjoyed playing with the two cousins visiting Tipton as much as if the blood of these cousins had been less dubiously mixed.", "래디슬로 부부가 한 해에 적어도 두 번은 그랜지를 방문한다는 것이 당연한 일로 여겨지게 되었고, 프레시트에는 차차 사촌들이 한 줄로 늘어나, 그들은 팁턴을 방문하는 두 사촌과 노는 것을, 마치 그 사촌들의 피가 그리 미심쩍게 섞이지 않은 것처럼 즐거워했다."]], [["Mr.", "브룩 씨는 천수를 누리며 살았고, 그의 사유지는 도러시아의 아들이 물려받았다."], ["Brooke lived to a good old age, and his estate was inherited by Dorothea’s son, who might have represented Middlemarch, but declined, thinking that his opinions had less chance of being stifled if he remained out of doors.", "그 아들은 미들마치를 대표하는 의원이 될 수도 있었으나, 자신이 바깥에 남아 있어야 자기 견해가 짓눌릴 가능성이 줄어든다고 여겨 사양했다."]], [["Sir James never ceased to regard Dorothea’s second marriage as a mistake; and indeed this remained the tradition concerning it in Middlemarch, where she was spoken of to a younger generation as a fine girl who married a sickly clergyman, old enough to be her father, and in little more than a year after his death gave up her estate to marry his cousin—young enough to have been his son, with no property, and not well-born.", "제임스 경은 도러시아의 두 번째 결혼을 잘못된 일로 여기기를 결코 그치지 않았다. 그리고 사실 그것은 미들마치에서 그 결혼에 관한 통설로 남았으니, 그곳에서 도러시아는 다음 세대 사람들에게 이렇게 이야기되었다."], ["Those who had not seen anything of Dorothea usually observed that she could not have been “a nice woman,”", "자기 아버지뻘로 늙은 병약한 성직자와 결혼했던 어여쁜 처녀, 그가 죽은 지 일 년 남짓 만에 사유지를 포기하고 그의 사촌과 결혼해 버린 처녀라고. 그 사촌이란 그의 아들뻘로 젊고, 재산도 없으며, 가문도 변변치 않은 사람이었다."], ["else she would not have married either the one or the other.", "도러시아를 전혀 본 적 없는 이들은 대개, 그녀가 “괜찮은 여자”였을 리 없다고들 말했다. 그렇지 않고서야 둘 중 어느 쪽과도 결혼하지 않았으리라는 것이었다."]], [["Certainly those determining acts of her life were not ideally beautiful.", "분명 그녀 생애의 그 결정적인 행위들은 이상적으로 아름답지는 않았다."], ["They were the mixed result of young and noble impulse struggling amidst the conditions of an imperfect social state, in which great feelings will often take the aspect of error, and great faith the aspect of illusion.", "그것들은 불완전한 사회 상태의 조건들 한가운데서 분투하던 젊고 고결한 충동이 빚어낸 혼합된 결과였으니, 그러한 사회에서는 위대한 감정이 흔히 잘못의 모습을 띠고, 위대한 신앙이 환상의 모습을 띠곤 한다."], ["For there is no creature whose inward being is so strong that it is not greatly determined by what lies outside it.", "내면의 존재가 너무도 강해서 자기 바깥에 놓인 것에 크게 좌우되지 않을 만한 피조물은 없기 때문이다."], ["A new Theresa will hardly have the opportunity of reforming a conventual life, any more than a new Antigone will spend her heroic piety in daring all for the sake of a brother’s burial: the medium in which their ardent deeds took shape is forever gone.", "새로운 테레사가 수도원 생활을 개혁할 기회를 얻기는 어려울 것이고, 새로운 안티고네가 오라비의 매장을 위해 모든 것을 무릅쓰며 그 영웅적 신심을 다 바칠 일도 없을 것이다. 그들의 열렬한 행위가 형태를 빚어내던 그 매질(媒質)은 영영 사라져 버렸기 때문이다."], ["But we insignificant people with our daily words and acts are preparing the lives of many Dorotheas, some of which may present a far sadder sacrifice than that of the Dorothea whose story we know.", "그러나 날마다의 말과 행위를 지닌 보잘것없는 우리는 수많은 도러시아의 삶을 마련해 가고 있으니, 그 가운데 어떤 삶은 우리가 그 이야기를 아는 도러시아의 희생보다 훨씬 더 슬픈 희생을 보여 줄지도 모른다."]], [["Her finely touched spirit had still its fine issues, though they were not widely visible.", "섬세하게 다듬어진 그녀의 정신은, 비록 널리 드러나지는 않았으나, 여전히 섬세한 결실을 맺었다."], ["Her full nature, like that river of which Cyrus broke the strength, spent itself in channels which had no great name on the earth.", "키루스가 그 위세를 꺾어 놓았던 그 강물처럼, 그녀의 충만한 본성은 이 땅에서 이렇다 할 이름을 갖지 못한 물줄기들 속으로 자신을 쏟아부었다. 그러나 그녀의 존재가 주위 사람들에게 미친 영향은 헤아릴 수 없이 널리 퍼져 나갔다."], ["But the effect of her being on those around her was incalculably diffusive: for the growing good of the world is partly dependent on unhistoric acts; and that things are not so ill with you and me as they might have been, is half owing to the number who lived faithfully a hidden life, and rest in unvisited tombs.", "세상이 점점 더 나아지는 것은 부분적으로 역사에 남지 않은 행위들에 달려 있으며, 당신과 내가 처할 수도 있었던 것만큼 형편이 나쁘지 않은 것은, 절반은 충실하게 숨은 삶을 살다가 찾는 이 없는 무덤에 잠든 수많은 이들의 덕분이기 때문이다. 끝"]]];
